if(typeof BODIES_ORIG!=="undefined") BODIES_ORIG["gb-1400"]="My father’s family name being Pirrip, and my Christian name Philip, my infant tongue could make of both names nothing longer or more explicit than Pip. So, I called myself Pip, and came to be called Pip.\n\nI give Pirrip as my father’s family name, on the authority of his tombstone and my sister,—Mrs. Joe Gargery, who married the blacksmith. As I never saw my father or my mother, and never saw any likeness of either of them (for their days were long before the days of photographs), my first fancies regarding what they were like were unreasonably derived from their tombstones. The shape of the letters on my father’s, gave me an odd idea that he was a square, stout, dark man, with curly black hair. From the character and turn of the inscription, “Also Georgiana Wife of the Above,” I drew a childish conclusion that my mother was freckled and sickly. To five little stone lozenges, each about a foot and a half long, which were arranged in a neat row beside their grave, and were sacred to the memory of five little brothers of mine,—who gave up trying to get a living, exceedingly early in that universal struggle,—I am indebted for a belief I religiously entertained that they had all been born on their backs with their hands in their trousers-pockets, and had never taken them out in this state of existence.\n\nOurs was the marsh country, down by the river, within, as the river wound, twenty miles of the sea. My first most vivid and broad impression of the identity of things seems to me to have been gained on a memorable raw afternoon towards evening. At such a time I found out for certain that this bleak place overgrown with nettles was the churchyard; and that Philip Pirrip, late of this parish, and also Georgiana wife of the above, were dead and buried; and that Alexander, Bartholomew, Abraham, Tobias, and Roger, infant children of the aforesaid, were also dead and buried; and that the dark flat wilderness beyond the churchyard, intersected with dikes and mounds and gates, with scattered cattle feeding on it, was the marshes; and that the low leaden line beyond was the river; and that the distant savage lair from which the wind was rushing was the sea; and that the small bundle of shivers growing afraid of it all and beginning to cry, was Pip.\n\n“Hold your noise!” cried a terrible voice, as a man started up from among the graves at the side of the church porch. “Keep still, you little devil, or I’ll cut your throat!”\n\nA fearful man, all in coarse grey, with a great iron on his leg. A man with no hat, and with broken shoes, and with an old rag tied round his head. A man who had been soaked in water, and smothered in mud, and lamed by stones, and cut by flints, and stung by nettles, and torn by briars; who limped, and shivered, and glared, and growled; and whose teeth chattered in his head as he seized me by the chin.\n\n“Oh! Don’t cut my throat, sir,” I pleaded in terror. “Pray don’t do it, sir.”\n\n“Tell us your name!” said the man. “Quick!”\n\n“Pip, sir.”\n\n“Once more,” said the man, staring at me. “Give it mouth!”\n\n“Pip. Pip, sir.”\n\n“Show us where you live,” said the man. “Pint out the place!”\n\nI pointed to where our village lay, on the flat in-shore among the alder-trees and pollards, a mile or more from the church.\n\nThe man, after looking at me for a moment, turned me upside down, and emptied my pockets. There was nothing in them but a piece of bread. When the church came to itself,—for he was so sudden and strong that he made it go head over heels before me, and I saw the steeple under my feet,—when the church came to itself, I say, I was seated on a high tombstone, trembling while he ate the bread ravenously.\n\n“You young dog,” said the man, licking his lips, “what fat cheeks you ha’ got.”\n\nI believe they were fat, though I was at that time undersized for my years, and not strong.\n\n“Darn me if I couldn’t eat ’em,” said the man, with a threatening shake of his head, “and if I han’t half a mind to’t!”\n\nI earnestly expressed my hope that he wouldn’t, and held tighter to the tombstone on which he had put me; partly, to keep myself upon it; partly, to keep myself from crying.\n\n“Now lookee here!” said the man. “Where’s your mother?”\n\n“There, sir!” said I.\n\nHe started, made a short run, and stopped and looked over his shoulder.\n\n“There, sir!” I timidly explained. “Also Georgiana. That’s my mother.”\n\n“Oh!” said he, coming back. “And is that your father alonger your mother?”\n\n“Yes, sir,” said I; “him too; late of this parish.”\n\n“Ha!” he muttered then, considering. “Who d’ye live with,—supposin’ you’re kindly let to live, which I han’t made up my mind about?”\n\n“My sister, sir,—Mrs. Joe Gargery,—wife of Joe Gargery, the blacksmith, sir.”\n\n“Blacksmith, eh?” said he. And looked down at his leg.\n\nAfter darkly looking at his leg and me several times, he came closer to my tombstone, took me by both arms, and tilted me back as far as he could hold me; so that his eyes looked most powerfully down into mine, and mine looked most helplessly up into his.\n\n“Now lookee here,” he said, “the question being whether you’re to be let to live. You know what a file is?”\n\n“Yes, sir.”\n\n“And you know what wittles is?”\n\n“Yes, sir.”\n\nAfter each question he tilted me over a little more, so as to give me a greater sense of helplessness and danger.\n\n“You get me a file.” He tilted me again. “And you get me wittles.” He tilted me again. “You bring ’em both to me.” He tilted me again. “Or I’ll have your heart and liver out.” He tilted me again.\n\nI was dreadfully frightened, and so giddy that I clung to him with both hands, and said, “If you would kindly please to let me keep upright, sir, perhaps I shouldn’t be sick, and perhaps I could attend more.”\n\nHe gave me a most tremendous dip and roll, so that the church jumped over its own weathercock. Then, he held me by the arms, in an upright position on the top of the stone, and went on in these fearful terms:—\n\n“You bring me, to-morrow morning early, that file and them wittles. You bring the lot to me, at that old Battery over yonder. You do it, and you never dare to say a word or dare to make a sign concerning your having seen such a person as me, or any person sumever, and you shall be let to live. You fail, or you go from my words in any partickler, no matter how small it is, and your heart and your liver shall be tore out, roasted, and ate. Now, I ain’t alone, as you may think I am. There’s a young man hid with me, in comparison with which young man I am a Angel. That young man hears the words I speak. That young man has a secret way pecooliar to himself, of getting at a boy, and at his heart, and at his liver. It is in wain for a boy to attempt to hide himself from that young man. A boy may lock his door, may be warm in bed, may tuck himself up, may draw the clothes over his head, may think himself comfortable and safe, but that young man will softly creep and creep his way to him and tear him open. I am a keeping that young man from harming of you at the present moment, with great difficulty. I find it wery hard to hold that young man off of your inside. Now, what do you say?”\n\nI said that I would get him the file, and I would get him what broken bits of food I could, and I would come to him at the Battery, early in the morning.\n\n“Say Lord strike you dead if you don’t!” said the man.\n\nI said so, and he took me down.\n\n“Now,” he pursued, “you remember what you’ve undertook, and you remember that young man, and you get home!”\n\n“Goo-good night, sir,” I faltered.\n\n“Much of that!” said he, glancing about him over the cold wet flat. “I wish I was a frog. Or a eel!”\n\nAt the same time, he hugged his shuddering body in both his arms,—clasping himself, as if to hold himself together,—and limped towards the low church wall. As I saw him go, picking his way among the nettles, and among the brambles that bound the green mounds, he looked in my young eyes as if he were eluding the hands of the dead people, stretching up cautiously out of their graves, to get a twist upon his ankle and pull him in.\n\nWhen he came to the low church wall, he got over it, like a man whose legs were numbed and stiff, and then turned round to look for me. When I saw him turning, I set my face towards home, and made the best use of my legs. But presently I looked over my shoulder, and saw him going on again towards the river, still hugging himself in both arms, and picking his way with his sore feet among the great stones dropped into the marshes here and there, for stepping-places when the rains were heavy or the tide was in.\n\nThe marshes were just a long black horizontal line then, as I stopped to look after him; and the river was just another horizontal line, not nearly so broad nor yet so black; and the sky was just a row of long angry red lines and dense black lines intermixed. On the edge of the river I could faintly make out the only two black things in all the prospect that seemed to be standing upright; one of these was the beacon by which the sailors steered,—like an unhooped cask upon a pole,—an ugly thing when you were near it; the other, a gibbet, with some chains hanging to it which had once held a pirate. The man was limping on towards this latter, as if he were the pirate come to life, and come down, and going back to hook himself up again. It gave me a terrible turn when I thought so; and as I saw the cattle lifting their heads to gaze after him, I wondered whether they thought so too. I looked all round for the horrible young man, and could see no signs of him. But now I was frightened again, and ran home without stopping.\n\n# Chapter II.\n\nMy sister, Mrs. Joe Gargery, was more than twenty years older than I, and had established a great reputation with herself and the neighbours because she had brought me up “by hand.” Having at that time to find out for myself what the expression meant, and knowing her to have a hard and heavy hand, and to be much in the habit of laying it upon her husband as well as upon me, I supposed that Joe Gargery and I were both brought up by hand.\n\nShe was not a good-looking woman, my sister; and I had a general impression that she must have made Joe Gargery marry her by hand. Joe was a fair man, with curls of flaxen hair on each side of his smooth face, and with eyes of such a very undecided blue that they seemed to have somehow got mixed with their own whites. He was a mild, good-natured, sweet-tempered, easy-going, foolish, dear fellow,—a sort of Hercules in strength, and also in weakness.\n\nMy sister, Mrs. Joe, with black hair and eyes, had such a prevailing redness of skin that I sometimes used to wonder whether it was possible she washed herself with a nutmeg-grater instead of soap. She was tall and bony, and almost always wore a coarse apron, fastened over her figure behind with two loops, and having a square impregnable bib in front, that was stuck full of pins and needles. She made it a powerful merit in herself, and a strong reproach against Joe, that she wore this apron so much. Though I really see no reason why she should have worn it at all; or why, if she did wear it at all, she should not have taken it off, every day of her life.\n\nJoe’s forge adjoined our house, which was a wooden house, as many of the dwellings in our country were,—most of them, at that time. When I ran home from the churchyard, the forge was shut up, and Joe was sitting alone in the kitchen. Joe and I being fellow-sufferers, and having confidences as such, Joe imparted a confidence to me, the moment I raised the latch of the door and peeped in at him opposite to it, sitting in the chimney corner.\n\n“Mrs. Joe has been out a dozen times, looking for you, Pip. And she’s out now, making it a baker’s dozen.”\n\n“Is she?”\n\n“Yes, Pip,” said Joe; “and what’s worse, she’s got Tickler with her.”\n\nAt this dismal intelligence, I twisted the only button on my waistcoat round and round, and looked in great depression at the fire. Tickler was a wax-ended piece of cane, worn smooth by collision with my tickled frame.\n\n“She sot down,” said Joe, “and she got up, and she made a grab at Tickler, and she Ram-paged out. That’s what she did,” said Joe, slowly clearing the fire between the lower bars with the poker, and looking at it; “she Ram-paged out, Pip.”\n\n“Has she been gone long, Joe?” I always treated him as a larger species of child, and as no more than my equal.\n\n“Well,” said Joe, glancing up at the Dutch clock, “she’s been on the Ram-page, this last spell, about five minutes, Pip. She’s a-coming! Get behind the door, old chap, and have the jack-towel betwixt you.”\n\nI took the advice. My sister, Mrs. Joe, throwing the door wide open, and finding an obstruction behind it, immediately divined the cause, and applied Tickler to its further investigation. She concluded by throwing me—I often served as a connubial missile—at Joe, who, glad to get hold of me on any terms, passed me on into the chimney and quietly fenced me up there with his great leg.\n\n“Where have you been, you young monkey?” said Mrs. Joe, stamping her foot. “Tell me directly what you’ve been doing to wear me away with fret and fright and worrit, or I’d have you out of that corner if you was fifty Pips, and he was five hundred Gargerys.”\n\n“I have only been to the churchyard,” said I, from my stool, crying and rubbing myself.\n\n“Churchyard!” repeated my sister. “If it warn’t for me you’d have been to the churchyard long ago, and stayed there. Who brought you up by hand?”\n\n“You did,” said I.\n\n“And why did I do it, I should like to know?” exclaimed my sister.\n\nI whimpered, “I don’t know.”\n\n“I don’t!” said my sister. “I’d never do it again! I know that. I may truly say I’ve never had this apron of mine off since born you were. It’s bad enough to be a blacksmith’s wife (and him a Gargery) without being your mother.”\n\nMy thoughts strayed from that question as I looked disconsolately at the fire. For the fugitive out on the marshes with the ironed leg, the mysterious young man, the file, the food, and the dreadful pledge I was under to commit a larceny on those sheltering premises, rose before me in the avenging coals.\n\n“Hah!” said Mrs. Joe, restoring Tickler to his station. “Churchyard, indeed! You may well say churchyard, you two.” One of us, by the by, had not said it at all. “You’ll drive me to the churchyard betwixt you, one of these days, and O, a pr-r-recious pair you’d be without me!”\n\nAs she applied herself to set the tea-things, Joe peeped down at me over his leg, as if he were mentally casting me and himself up, and calculating what kind of pair we practically should make, under the grievous circumstances foreshadowed. After that, he sat feeling his right-side flaxen curls and whisker, and following Mrs. Joe about with his blue eyes, as his manner always was at squally times.\n\nMy sister had a trenchant way of cutting our bread and butter for us, that never varied. First, with her left hand she jammed the loaf hard and fast against her bib,—where it sometimes got a pin into it, and sometimes a needle, which we afterwards got into our mouths. Then she took some butter (not too much) on a knife and spread it on the loaf, in an apothecary kind of way, as if she were making a plaster,—using both sides of the knife with a slapping dexterity, and trimming and moulding the butter off round the crust. Then, she gave the knife a final smart wipe on the edge of the plaster, and then sawed a very thick round off the loaf: which she finally, before separating from the loaf, hewed into two halves, of which Joe got one, and I the other.\n\nOn the present occasion, though I was hungry, I dared not eat my slice. I felt that I must have something in reserve for my dreadful acquaintance, and his ally the still more dreadful young man. I knew Mrs. Joe’s housekeeping to be of the strictest kind, and that my larcenous researches might find nothing available in the safe. Therefore I resolved to put my hunk of bread and butter down the leg of my trousers.\n\nThe effort of resolution necessary to the achievement of this purpose I found to be quite awful. It was as if I had to make up my mind to leap from the top of a high house, or plunge into a great depth of water. And it was made the more difficult by the unconscious Joe. In our already-mentioned freemasonry as fellow-sufferers, and in his good-natured companionship with me, it was our evening habit to compare the way we bit through our slices, by silently holding them up to each other’s admiration now and then,—which stimulated us to new exertions. To-night, Joe several times invited me, by the display of his fast diminishing slice, to enter upon our usual friendly competition; but he found me, each time, with my yellow mug of tea on one knee, and my untouched bread and butter on the other. At last, I desperately considered that the thing I contemplated must be done, and that it had best be done in the least improbable manner consistent with the circumstances. I took advantage of a moment when Joe had just looked at me, and got my bread and butter down my leg.\n\nJoe was evidently made uncomfortable by what he supposed to be my loss of appetite, and took a thoughtful bite out of his slice, which he didn’t seem to enjoy. He turned it about in his mouth much longer than usual, pondering over it a good deal, and after all gulped it down like a pill. He was about to take another bite, and had just got his head on one side for a good purchase on it, when his eye fell on me, and he saw that my bread and butter was gone.\n\nThe wonder and consternation with which Joe stopped on the threshold of his bite and stared at me, were too evident to escape my sister’s observation.\n\n“What’s the matter now?” said she, smartly, as she put down her cup.\n\n“I say, you know!” muttered Joe, shaking his head at me in very serious remonstrance. “Pip, old chap! You’ll do yourself a mischief. It’ll stick somewhere. You can’t have chawed it, Pip.”\n\n“What’s the matter now?” repeated my sister, more sharply than before.\n\n“If you can cough any trifle on it up, Pip, I’d recommend you to do it,” said Joe, all aghast. “Manners is manners, but still your elth’s your elth.”\n\nBy this time, my sister was quite desperate, so she pounced on Joe, and, taking him by the two whiskers, knocked his head for a little while against the wall behind him, while I sat in the corner, looking guiltily on.\n\n“Now, perhaps you’ll mention what’s the matter,” said my sister, out of breath, “you staring great stuck pig.”\n\nJoe looked at her in a helpless way, then took a helpless bite, and looked at me again.\n\n“You know, Pip,” said Joe, solemnly, with his last bite in his cheek, and speaking in a confidential voice, as if we two were quite alone, “you and me is always friends, and I’d be the last to tell upon you, any time. But such a—” he moved his chair and looked about the floor between us, and then again at me—“such a most oncommon Bolt as that!”\n\n“Been bolting his food, has he?” cried my sister.\n\n“You know, old chap,” said Joe, looking at me, and not at Mrs. Joe, with his bite still in his cheek, “I Bolted, myself, when I was your age—frequent—and as a boy I’ve been among a many Bolters; but I never see your Bolting equal yet, Pip, and it’s a mercy you ain’t Bolted dead.”\n\nMy sister made a dive at me, and fished me up by the hair, saying nothing more than the awful words, “You come along and be dosed.”\n\nSome medical beast had revived Tar-water in those days as a fine medicine, and Mrs. Joe always kept a supply of it in the cupboard; having a belief in its virtues correspondent to its nastiness. At the best of times, so much of this elixir was administered to me as a choice restorative, that I was conscious of going about, smelling like a new fence. On this particular evening the urgency of my case demanded a pint of this mixture, which was poured down my throat, for my greater comfort, while Mrs. Joe held my head under her arm, as a boot would be held in a bootjack. Joe got off with half a pint; but was made to swallow that (much to his disturbance, as he sat slowly munching and meditating before the fire), “because he had had a turn.” Judging from myself, I should say he certainly had a turn afterwards, if he had had none before.\n\nConscience is a dreadful thing when it accuses man or boy; but when, in the case of a boy, that secret burden co-operates with another secret burden down the leg of his trousers, it is (as I can testify) a great punishment. The guilty knowledge that I was going to rob Mrs. Joe—I never thought I was going to rob Joe, for I never thought of any of the housekeeping property as his—united to the necessity of always keeping one hand on my bread and butter as I sat, or when I was ordered about the kitchen on any small errand, almost drove me out of my mind. Then, as the marsh winds made the fire glow and flare, I thought I heard the voice outside, of the man with the iron on his leg who had sworn me to secrecy, declaring that he couldn’t and wouldn’t starve until to-morrow, but must be fed now. At other times, I thought, What if the young man who was with so much difficulty restrained from imbruing his hands in me should yield to a constitutional impatience, or should mistake the time, and should think himself accredited to my heart and liver to-night, instead of to-morrow! If ever anybody’s hair stood on end with terror, mine must have done so then. But, perhaps, nobody’s ever did?\n\nIt was Christmas Eve, and I had to stir the pudding for next day, with a copper-stick, from seven to eight by the Dutch clock. I tried it with the load upon my leg (and that made me think afresh of the man with the load on his leg), and found the tendency of exercise to bring the bread and butter out at my ankle, quite unmanageable. Happily I slipped away, and deposited that part of my conscience in my garret bedroom.\n\n“Hark!” said I, when I had done my stirring, and was taking a final warm in the chimney corner before being sent up to bed; “was that great guns, Joe?”\n\n“Ah!” said Joe. “There’s another conwict off.”\n\n“What does that mean, Joe?” said I.\n\nMrs. Joe, who always took explanations upon herself, said, snappishly, “Escaped. Escaped.” Administering the definition like Tar-water.\n\nWhile Mrs. Joe sat with her head bending over her needlework, I put my mouth into the forms of saying to Joe, “What’s a convict?” Joe put his mouth into the forms of returning such a highly elaborate answer, that I could make out nothing of it but the single word “Pip.”\n\n“There was a conwict off last night,” said Joe, aloud, “after sunset-gun. And they fired warning of him. And now it appears they’re firing warning of another.”\n\n“Who’s firing?” said I.\n\n“Drat that boy,” interposed my sister, frowning at me over her work, “what a questioner he is. Ask no questions, and you’ll be told no lies.”\n\nIt was not very polite to herself, I thought, to imply that I should be told lies by her even if I did ask questions. But she never was polite unless there was company.\n\nAt this point Joe greatly augmented my curiosity by taking the utmost pains to open his mouth very wide, and to put it into the form of a word that looked to me like “sulks.” Therefore, I naturally pointed to Mrs. Joe, and put my mouth into the form of saying, “her?” But Joe wouldn’t hear of that, at all, and again opened his mouth very wide, and shook the form of a most emphatic word out of it. But I could make nothing of the word.\n\n“Mrs. Joe,” said I, as a last resort, “I should like to know—if you wouldn’t much mind—where the firing comes from?”\n\n“Lord bless the boy!” exclaimed my sister, as if she didn’t quite mean that but rather the contrary. “From the Hulks!”\n\n“Oh-h!” said I, looking at Joe. “Hulks!”\n\nJoe gave a reproachful cough, as much as to say, “Well, I told you so.”\n\n“And please, what’s Hulks?” said I.\n\n“That’s the way with this boy!” exclaimed my sister, pointing me out with her needle and thread, and shaking her head at me. “Answer him one question, and he’ll ask you a dozen directly. Hulks are prison-ships, right ’cross th’ meshes.” We always used that name for marshes, in our country.\n\n“I wonder who’s put into prison-ships, and why they’re put there?” said I, in a general way, and with quiet desperation.\n\nIt was too much for Mrs. Joe, who immediately rose. “I tell you what, young fellow,” said she, “I didn’t bring you up by hand to badger people’s lives out. It would be blame to me and not praise, if I had. People are put in the Hulks because they murder, and because they rob, and forge, and do all sorts of bad; and they always begin by asking questions. Now, you get along to bed!”\n\nI was never allowed a candle to light me to bed, and, as I went upstairs in the dark, with my head tingling,—from Mrs. Joe’s thimble having played the tambourine upon it, to accompany her last words,—I felt fearfully sensible of the great convenience that the hulks were handy for me. I was clearly on my way there. I had begun by asking questions, and I was going to rob Mrs. Joe.\n\nSince that time, which is far enough away now, I have often thought that few people know what secrecy there is in the young under terror. No matter how unreasonable the terror, so that it be terror. I was in mortal terror of the young man who wanted my heart and liver; I was in mortal terror of my interlocutor with the iron leg; I was in mortal terror of myself, from whom an awful promise had been extracted; I had no hope of deliverance through my all-powerful sister, who repulsed me at every turn; I am afraid to think of what I might have done on requirement, in the secrecy of my terror.\n\nIf I slept at all that night, it was only to imagine myself drifting down the river on a strong spring-tide, to the Hulks; a ghostly pirate calling out to me through a speaking-trumpet, as I passed the gibbet-station, that I had better come ashore and be hanged there at once, and not put it off. I was afraid to sleep, even if I had been inclined, for I knew that at the first faint dawn of morning I must rob the pantry. There was no doing it in the night, for there was no getting a light by easy friction then; to have got one I must have struck it out of flint and steel, and have made a noise like the very pirate himself rattling his chains.\n\nAs soon as the great black velvet pall outside my little window was shot with grey, I got up and went downstairs; every board upon the way, and every crack in every board calling after me, “Stop thief!” and “Get up, Mrs. Joe!” In the pantry, which was far more abundantly supplied than usual, owing to the season, I was very much alarmed by a hare hanging up by the heels, whom I rather thought I caught, when my back was half turned, winking. I had no time for verification, no time for selection, no time for anything, for I had no time to spare. I stole some bread, some rind of cheese, about half a jar of mincemeat (which I tied up in my pocket-handkerchief with my last night’s slice), some brandy from a stone bottle (which I decanted into a glass bottle I had secretly used for making that intoxicating fluid, Spanish-liquorice-water, up in my room: diluting the stone bottle from a jug in the kitchen cupboard), a meat bone with very little on it, and a beautiful round compact pork pie. I was nearly going away without the pie, but I was tempted to mount upon a shelf, to look what it was that was put away so carefully in a covered earthenware dish in a corner, and I found it was the pie, and I took it in the hope that it was not intended for early use, and would not be missed for some time.\n\nThere was a door in the kitchen, communicating with the forge; I unlocked and unbolted that door, and got a file from among Joe’s tools. Then I put the fastenings as I had found them, opened the door at which I had entered when I ran home last night, shut it, and ran for the misty marshes.\n\n# Chapter III.\n\nIt was a rimy morning, and very damp. I had seen the damp lying on the outside of my little window, as if some goblin had been crying there all night, and using the window for a pocket-handkerchief. Now, I saw the damp lying on the bare hedges and spare grass, like a coarser sort of spiders’ webs; hanging itself from twig to twig and blade to blade. On every rail and gate, wet lay clammy, and the marsh mist was so thick, that the wooden finger on the post directing people to our village—a direction which they never accepted, for they never came there—was invisible to me until I was quite close under it. Then, as I looked up at it, while it dripped, it seemed to my oppressed conscience like a phantom devoting me to the Hulks.\n\nThe mist was heavier yet when I got out upon the marshes, so that instead of my running at everything, everything seemed to run at me. This was very disagreeable to a guilty mind. The gates and dikes and banks came bursting at me through the mist, as if they cried as plainly as could be, “A boy with somebody else’s pork pie! Stop him!” The cattle came upon me with like suddenness, staring out of their eyes, and steaming out of their nostrils, “Halloa, young thief!” One black ox, with a white cravat on,—who even had to my awakened conscience something of a clerical air,—fixed me so obstinately with his eyes, and moved his blunt head round in such an accusatory manner as I moved round, that I blubbered out to him, “I couldn’t help it, sir! It wasn’t for myself I took it!” Upon which he put down his head, blew a cloud of smoke out of his nose, and vanished with a kick-up of his hind-legs and a flourish of his tail.\n\nAll this time, I was getting on towards the river; but however fast I went, I couldn’t warm my feet, to which the damp cold seemed riveted, as the iron was riveted to the leg of the man I was running to meet. I knew my way to the Battery, pretty straight, for I had been down there on a Sunday with Joe, and Joe, sitting on an old gun, had told me that when I was ’prentice to him, regularly bound, we would have such Larks there! However, in the confusion of the mist, I found myself at last too far to the right, and consequently had to try back along the river-side, on the bank of loose stones above the mud and the stakes that staked the tide out. Making my way along here with all despatch, I had just crossed a ditch which I knew to be very near the Battery, and had just scrambled up the mound beyond the ditch, when I saw the man sitting before me. His back was towards me, and he had his arms folded, and was nodding forward, heavy with sleep.\n\nI thought he would be more glad if I came upon him with his breakfast, in that unexpected manner, so I went forward softly and touched him on the shoulder. He instantly jumped up, and it was not the same man, but another man!\n\nAnd yet this man was dressed in coarse grey, too, and had a great iron on his leg, and was lame, and hoarse, and cold, and was everything that the other man was; except that he had not the same face, and had a flat broad-brimmed low-crowned felt hat on. All this I saw in a moment, for I had only a moment to see it in: he swore an oath at me, made a hit at me,—it was a round weak blow that missed me and almost knocked himself down, for it made him stumble,—and then he ran into the mist, stumbling twice as he went, and I lost him.\n\n“It’s the young man!” I thought, feeling my heart shoot as I identified him. I dare say I should have felt a pain in my liver, too, if I had known where it was.\n\nI was soon at the Battery after that, and there was the right man,—hugging himself and limping to and fro, as if he had never all night left off hugging and limping,—waiting for me. He was awfully cold, to be sure. I half expected to see him drop down before my face and die of deadly cold. His eyes looked so awfully hungry too, that when I handed him the file and he laid it down on the grass, it occurred to me he would have tried to eat it, if he had not seen my bundle. He did not turn me upside down this time to get at what I had, but left me right side upwards while I opened the bundle and emptied my pockets.\n\n“What’s in the bottle, boy?” said he.\n\n“Brandy,” said I.\n\nHe was already handing mincemeat down his throat in the most curious manner,—more like a man who was putting it away somewhere in a violent hurry, than a man who was eating it,—but he left off to take some of the liquor. He shivered all the while so violently, that it was quite as much as he could do to keep the neck of the bottle between his teeth, without biting it off.\n\n“I think you have got the ague,” said I.\n\n“I’m much of your opinion, boy,” said he.\n\n“It’s bad about here,” I told him. “You’ve been lying out on the meshes, and they’re dreadful aguish. Rheumatic too.”\n\n“I’ll eat my breakfast afore they’re the death of me,” said he. “I’d do that, if I was going to be strung up to that there gallows as there is over there, directly afterwards. I’ll beat the shivers so far, I’ll bet you.”\n\nHe was gobbling mincemeat, meatbone, bread, cheese, and pork pie, all at once: staring distrustfully while he did so at the mist all round us, and often stopping—even stopping his jaws—to listen. Some real or fancied sound, some clink upon the river or breathing of beast upon the marsh, now gave him a start, and he said, suddenly,—\n\n“You’re not a deceiving imp? You brought no one with you?”\n\n“No, sir! No!”\n\n“Nor giv’ no one the office to follow you?”\n“No!”\n\n“Well,” said he, “I believe you. You’d be but a fierce young hound indeed, if at your time of life you could help to hunt a wretched warmint hunted as near death and dunghill as this poor wretched warmint is!”\n\nSomething clicked in his throat as if he had works in him like a clock, and was going to strike. And he smeared his ragged rough sleeve over his eyes.\n\nPitying his desolation, and watching him as he gradually settled down upon the pie, I made bold to say, “I am glad you enjoy it.”\n\n“Did you speak?”\n\n“I said I was glad you enjoyed it.”\n\n“Thankee, my boy. I do.”\n\nI had often watched a large dog of ours eating his food; and I now noticed a decided similarity between the dog’s way of eating, and the man’s. The man took strong sharp sudden bites, just like the dog. He swallowed, or rather snapped up, every mouthful, too soon and too fast; and he looked sideways here and there while he ate, as if he thought there was danger in every direction of somebody’s coming to take the pie away. He was altogether too unsettled in his mind over it, to appreciate it comfortably I thought, or to have anybody to dine with him, without making a chop with his jaws at the visitor. In all of which particulars he was very like the dog.\n\n“I am afraid you won’t leave any of it for him,” said I, timidly; after a silence during which I had hesitated as to the politeness of making the remark. “There’s no more to be got where that came from.” It was the certainty of this fact that impelled me to offer the hint.\n\n“Leave any for him? Who’s him?” said my friend, stopping in his crunching of pie-crust.\n\n“The young man. That you spoke of. That was hid with you.”\n\n“Oh ah!” he returned, with something like a gruff laugh. “Him? Yes, yes! He don’t want no wittles.”\n\n“I thought he looked as if he did,” said I.\n\nThe man stopped eating, and regarded me with the keenest scrutiny and the greatest surprise.\n\n“Looked? When?”\n\n“Just now.”\n\n“Where?”\n\n“Yonder,” said I, pointing; “over there, where I found him nodding asleep, and thought it was you.”\n\nHe held me by the collar and stared at me so, that I began to think his first idea about cutting my throat had revived.\n\n“Dressed like you, you know, only with a hat,” I explained, trembling; “and—and”—I was very anxious to put this delicately—“and with—the same reason for wanting to borrow a file. Didn’t you hear the cannon last night?”\n\n“Then there was firing!” he said to himself.\n\n“I wonder you shouldn’t have been sure of that,” I returned, “for we heard it up at home, and that’s farther away, and we were shut in besides.”\n\n“Why, see now!” said he. “When a man’s alone on these flats, with a light head and a light stomach, perishing of cold and want, he hears nothin’ all night, but guns firing, and voices calling. Hears? He sees the soldiers, with their red coats lighted up by the torches carried afore, closing in round him. Hears his number called, hears himself challenged, hears the rattle of the muskets, hears the orders ‘Make ready! Present! Cover him steady, men!’ and is laid hands on—and there’s nothin’! Why, if I see one pursuing party last night—coming up in order, Damn ’em, with their tramp, tramp—I see a hundred. And as to firing! Why, I see the mist shake with the cannon, arter it was broad day,—But this man”; he had said all the rest, as if he had forgotten my being there; “did you notice anything in him?”\n\n“He had a badly bruised face,” said I, recalling what I hardly knew I knew.\n\n“Not here?” exclaimed the man, striking his left cheek mercilessly, with the flat of his hand.\n\n“Yes, there!”\n\n“Where is he?” He crammed what little food was left, into the breast of his grey jacket. “Show me the way he went. I’ll pull him down, like a bloodhound. Curse this iron on my sore leg! Give us hold of the file, boy.”\n\nI indicated in what direction the mist had shrouded the other man, and he looked up at it for an instant. But he was down on the rank wet grass, filing at his iron like a madman, and not minding me or minding his own leg, which had an old chafe upon it and was bloody, but which he handled as roughly as if it had no more feeling in it than the file. I was very much afraid of him again, now that he had worked himself into this fierce hurry, and I was likewise very much afraid of keeping away from home any longer. I told him I must go, but he took no notice, so I thought the best thing I could do was to slip off. The last I saw of him, his head was bent over his knee and he was working hard at his fetter, muttering impatient imprecations at it and at his leg. The last I heard of him, I stopped in the mist to listen, and the file was still going.\n\n# Chapter IV.\n\nI fully expected to find a Constable in the kitchen, waiting to take me up. But not only was there no Constable there, but no discovery had yet been made of the robbery. Mrs. Joe was prodigiously busy in getting the house ready for the festivities of the day, and Joe had been put upon the kitchen doorstep to keep him out of the dust-pan,—an article into which his destiny always led him, sooner or later, when my sister was vigorously reaping the floors of her establishment.\n\n“And where the deuce ha’ you been?” was Mrs. Joe’s Christmas salutation, when I and my conscience showed ourselves.\n\nI said I had been down to hear the Carols. “Ah! well!” observed Mrs. Joe. “You might ha’ done worse.” Not a doubt of that I thought.\n\n“Perhaps if I warn’t a blacksmith’s wife, and (what’s the same thing) a slave with her apron never off, I should have been to hear the Carols,” said Mrs. Joe. “I’m rather partial to Carols, myself, and that’s the best of reasons for my never hearing any.”\n\nJoe, who had ventured into the kitchen after me as the dustpan had retired before us, drew the back of his hand across his nose with a conciliatory air, when Mrs. Joe darted a look at him, and, when her eyes were withdrawn, secretly crossed his two forefingers, and exhibited them to me, as our token that Mrs. Joe was in a cross temper. This was so much her normal state, that Joe and I would often, for weeks together, be, as to our fingers, like monumental Crusaders as to their legs.\n\nWe were to have a superb dinner, consisting of a leg of pickled pork and greens, and a pair of roast stuffed fowls. A handsome mince-pie had been made yesterday morning (which accounted for the mincemeat not being missed), and the pudding was already on the boil. These extensive arrangements occasioned us to be cut off unceremoniously in respect of breakfast; “for I ain’t,” said Mrs. Joe,—“I ain’t a-going to have no formal cramming and busting and washing up now, with what I’ve got before me, I promise you!”\n\nSo, we had our slices served out, as if we were two thousand troops on a forced march instead of a man and boy at home; and we took gulps of milk and water, with apologetic countenances, from a jug on the dresser. In the meantime, Mrs. Joe put clean white curtains up, and tacked a new flowered flounce across the wide chimney to replace the old one, and uncovered the little state parlour across the passage, which was never uncovered at any other time, but passed the rest of the year in a cool haze of silver paper, which even extended to the four little white crockery poodles on the mantel-shelf, each with a black nose and a basket of flowers in his mouth, and each the counterpart of the other. Mrs. Joe was a very clean housekeeper, but had an exquisite art of making her cleanliness more uncomfortable and unacceptable than dirt itself. Cleanliness is next to Godliness, and some people do the same by their religion.\n\nMy sister, having so much to do, was going to church vicariously, that is to say, Joe and I were going. In his working-clothes, Joe was a well-knit characteristic-looking blacksmith; in his holiday clothes, he was more like a scarecrow in good circumstances, than anything else. Nothing that he wore then fitted him or seemed to belong to him; and everything that he wore then grazed him. On the present festive occasion he emerged from his room, when the blithe bells were going, the picture of misery, in a full suit of Sunday penitentials. As to me, I think my sister must have had some general idea that I was a young offender whom an Accoucheur Policeman had taken up (on my birthday) and delivered over to her, to be dealt with according to the outraged majesty of the law. I was always treated as if I had insisted on being born in opposition to the dictates of reason, religion, and morality, and against the dissuading arguments of my best friends. Even when I was taken to have a new suit of clothes, the tailor had orders to make them like a kind of Reformatory, and on no account to let me have the free use of my limbs.\n\nJoe and I going to church, therefore, must have been a moving spectacle for compassionate minds. Yet, what I suffered outside was nothing to what I underwent within. The terrors that had assailed me whenever Mrs. Joe had gone near the pantry, or out of the room, were only to be equalled by the remorse with which my mind dwelt on what my hands had done. Under the weight of my wicked secret, I pondered whether the Church would be powerful enough to shield me from the vengeance of the terrible young man, if I divulged to that establishment. I conceived the idea that the time when the banns were read and when the clergyman said, “Ye are now to declare it!” would be the time for me to rise and propose a private conference in the vestry. I am far from being sure that I might not have astonished our small congregation by resorting to this extreme measure, but for its being Christmas Day and no Sunday.\n\nMr. Wopsle, the clerk at church, was to dine with us; and Mr. Hubble the wheelwright and Mrs. Hubble; and Uncle Pumblechook (Joe’s uncle, but Mrs. Joe appropriated him), who was a well-to-do cornchandler in the nearest town, and drove his own chaise-cart. The dinner hour was half-past one. When Joe and I got home, we found the table laid, and Mrs. Joe dressed, and the dinner dressing, and the front door unlocked (it never was at any other time) for the company to enter by, and everything most splendid. And still, not a word of the robbery.\n\nThe time came, without bringing with it any relief to my feelings, and the company came. Mr. Wopsle, united to a Roman nose and a large shining bald forehead, had a deep voice which he was uncommonly proud of; indeed it was understood among his acquaintance that if you could only give him his head, he would read the clergyman into fits; he himself confessed that if the Church was “thrown open,” meaning to competition, he would not despair of making his mark in it. The Church not being “thrown open,” he was, as I have said, our clerk. But he punished the Amens tremendously; and when he gave out the psalm,—always giving the whole verse,—he looked all round the congregation first, as much as to say, “You have heard my friend overhead; oblige me with your opinion of this style!”\n\nI opened the door to the company,—making believe that it was a habit of ours to open that door,—and I opened it first to Mr. Wopsle, next to Mr. and Mrs. Hubble, and last of all to Uncle Pumblechook. N.B. I was not allowed to call him uncle, under the severest penalties.\n\n“Mrs. Joe,” said Uncle Pumblechook, a large hard-breathing middle-aged slow man, with a mouth like a fish, dull staring eyes, and sandy hair standing upright on his head, so that he looked as if he had just been all but choked, and had that moment come to, “I have brought you as the compliments of the season—I have brought you, Mum, a bottle of sherry wine—and I have brought you, Mum, a bottle of port wine.”\n\nEvery Christmas Day he presented himself, as a profound novelty, with exactly the same words, and carrying the two bottles like dumb-bells. Every Christmas Day, Mrs. Joe replied, as she now replied, “O, Un—cle Pum-ble—chook! This is kind!” Every Christmas Day, he retorted, as he now retorted, “It’s no more than your merits. And now are you all bobbish, and how’s Sixpennorth of halfpence?” meaning me.\n\nWe dined on these occasions in the kitchen, and adjourned, for the nuts and oranges and apples to the parlour; which was a change very like Joe’s change from his working-clothes to his Sunday dress. My sister was uncommonly lively on the present occasion, and indeed was generally more gracious in the society of Mrs. Hubble than in other company. I remember Mrs. Hubble as a little curly sharp-edged person in sky-blue, who held a conventionally juvenile position, because she had married Mr. Hubble,—I don’t know at what remote period,—when she was much younger than he. I remember Mr Hubble as a tough, high-shouldered, stooping old man, of a sawdusty fragrance, with his legs extraordinarily wide apart: so that in my short days I always saw some miles of open country between them when I met him coming up the lane.\n\nAmong this good company I should have felt myself, even if I hadn’t robbed the pantry, in a false position. Not because I was squeezed in at an acute angle of the tablecloth, with the table in my chest, and the Pumblechookian elbow in my eye, nor because I was not allowed to speak (I didn’t want to speak), nor because I was regaled with the scaly tips of the drumsticks of the fowls, and with those obscure corners of pork of which the pig, when living, had had the least reason to be vain. No; I should not have minded that, if they would only have left me alone. But they wouldn’t leave me alone. They seemed to think the opportunity lost, if they failed to point the conversation at me, every now and then, and stick the point into me. I might have been an unfortunate little bull in a Spanish arena, I got so smartingly touched up by these moral goads.\n\nIt began the moment we sat down to dinner. Mr. Wopsle said grace with theatrical declamation,—as it now appears to me, something like a religious cross of the Ghost in Hamlet with Richard the Third,—and ended with the very proper aspiration that we might be truly grateful. Upon which my sister fixed me with her eye, and said, in a low reproachful voice, “Do you hear that? Be grateful.”\n\n“Especially,” said Mr. Pumblechook, “be grateful, boy, to them which brought you up by hand.”\n\nMrs. Hubble shook her head, and contemplating me with a mournful presentiment that I should come to no good, asked, “Why is it that the young are never grateful?” This moral mystery seemed too much for the company until Mr. Hubble tersely solved it by saying, “Naterally wicious.” Everybody then murmured “True!” and looked at me in a particularly unpleasant and personal manner.\n\nJoe’s station and influence were something feebler (if possible) when there was company than when there was none. But he always aided and comforted me when he could, in some way of his own, and he always did so at dinner-time by giving me gravy, if there were any. There being plenty of gravy to-day, Joe spooned into my plate, at this point, about half a pint.\n\nA little later on in the dinner, Mr. Wopsle reviewed the sermon with some severity, and intimated—in the usual hypothetical case of the Church being “thrown open”—what kind of sermon he would have given them. After favouring them with some heads of that discourse, he remarked that he considered the subject of the day’s homily, ill chosen; which was the less excusable, he added, when there were so many subjects “going about.”\n\n“True again,” said Uncle Pumblechook. “You’ve hit it, sir! Plenty of subjects going about, for them that know how to put salt upon their tails. That’s what’s wanted. A man needn’t go far to find a subject, if he’s ready with his salt-box.” Mr. Pumblechook added, after a short interval of reflection, “Look at Pork alone. There’s a subject! If you want a subject, look at Pork!”\n\n“True, sir. Many a moral for the young,” returned Mr. Wopsle,—and I knew he was going to lug me in, before he said it; “might be deduced from that text.”\n\n(“You listen to this,” said my sister to me, in a severe parenthesis.)\n\nJoe gave me some more gravy.\n\n“Swine,” pursued Mr. Wopsle, in his deepest voice, and pointing his fork at my blushes, as if he were mentioning my Christian name,—“swine were the companions of the prodigal. The gluttony of Swine is put before us, as an example to the young.” (I thought this pretty well in him who had been praising up the pork for being so plump and juicy.) “What is detestable in a pig is more detestable in a boy.”\n\n“Or girl,” suggested Mr. Hubble.\n\n“Of course, or girl, Mr. Hubble,” assented Mr. Wopsle, rather irritably, “but there is no girl present.”\n\n“Besides,” said Mr. Pumblechook, turning sharp on me, “think what you’ve got to be grateful for. If you’d been born a Squeaker—”\n\n“He was, if ever a child was,” said my sister, most emphatically.\n\nJoe gave me some more gravy.\n\n“Well, but I mean a four-footed Squeaker,” said Mr. Pumblechook. “If you had been born such, would you have been here now? Not you—”\n\n“Unless in that form,” said Mr. Wopsle, nodding towards the dish.\n\n“But I don’t mean in that form, sir,” returned Mr. Pumblechook, who had an objection to being interrupted; “I mean, enjoying himself with his elders and betters, and improving himself with their conversation, and rolling in the lap of luxury. Would he have been doing that? No, he wouldn’t. And what would have been your destination?” turning on me again. “You would have been disposed of for so many shillings according to the market price of the article, and Dunstable the butcher would have come up to you as you lay in your straw, and he would have whipped you under his left arm, and with his right he would have tucked up his frock to get a penknife from out of his waistcoat-pocket, and he would have shed your blood and had your life. No bringing up by hand then. Not a bit of it!”\n\nJoe offered me more gravy, which I was afraid to take.\n\n“He was a world of trouble to you, ma’am,” said Mrs. Hubble, commiserating my sister.\n\n“Trouble?” echoed my sister; “trouble?” and then entered on a fearful catalogue of all the illnesses I had been guilty of, and all the acts of sleeplessness I had committed, and all the high places I had tumbled from, and all the low places I had tumbled into, and all the injuries I had done myself, and all the times she had wished me in my grave, and I had contumaciously refused to go there.\n\nI think the Romans must have aggravated one another very much, with their noses. Perhaps, they became the restless people they were, in consequence. Anyhow, Mr. Wopsle’s Roman nose so aggravated me, during the recital of my misdemeanours, that I should have liked to pull it until he howled. But, all I had endured up to this time was nothing in comparison with the awful feelings that took possession of me when the pause was broken which ensued upon my sister’s recital, and in which pause everybody had looked at me (as I felt painfully conscious) with indignation and abhorrence.\n\n“Yet,” said Mr. Pumblechook, leading the company gently back to the theme from which they had strayed, “Pork—regarded as biled—is rich, too; ain’t it?”\n\n“Have a little brandy, uncle,” said my sister.\n\nO Heavens, it had come at last! He would find it was weak, he would say it was weak, and I was lost! I held tight to the leg of the table under the cloth, with both hands, and awaited my fate.\n\nMy sister went for the stone bottle, came back with the stone bottle, and poured his brandy out: no one else taking any. The wretched man trifled with his glass,—took it up, looked at it through the light, put it down,—prolonged my misery. All this time Mrs. Joe and Joe were briskly clearing the table for the pie and pudding.\n\nI couldn’t keep my eyes off him. Always holding tight by the leg of the table with my hands and feet, I saw the miserable creature finger his glass playfully, take it up, smile, throw his head back, and drink the brandy off. Instantly afterwards, the company were seized with unspeakable consternation, owing to his springing to his feet, turning round several times in an appalling spasmodic whooping-cough dance, and rushing out at the door; he then became visible through the window, violently plunging and expectorating, making the most hideous faces, and apparently out of his mind.\n\nI held on tight, while Mrs. Joe and Joe ran to him. I didn’t know how I had done it, but I had no doubt I had murdered him somehow. In my dreadful situation, it was a relief when he was brought back, and surveying the company all round as if they had disagreed with him, sank down into his chair with the one significant gasp, “Tar!”\n\nI had filled up the bottle from the tar-water jug. I knew he would be worse by and by. I moved the table, like a Medium of the present day, by the vigor of my unseen hold upon it.\n\n“Tar!” cried my sister, in amazement. “Why, how ever could Tar come there?”\n\nBut, Uncle Pumblechook, who was omnipotent in that kitchen, wouldn’t hear the word, wouldn’t hear of the subject, imperiously waved it all away with his hand, and asked for hot gin and water. My sister, who had begun to be alarmingly meditative, had to employ herself actively in getting the gin, the hot water, the sugar, and the lemon-peel, and mixing them. For the time being at least, I was saved. I still held on to the leg of the table, but clutched it now with the fervor of gratitude.\n\nBy degrees, I became calm enough to release my grasp and partake of pudding. Mr. Pumblechook partook of pudding. All partook of pudding. The course terminated, and Mr. Pumblechook had begun to beam under the genial influence of gin and water. I began to think I should get over the day, when my sister said to Joe, “Clean plates,—cold.”\n\nI clutched the leg of the table again immediately, and pressed it to my bosom as if it had been the companion of my youth and friend of my soul. I foresaw what was coming, and I felt that this time I really was gone.\n\n“You must taste,” said my sister, addressing the guests with her best grace—“you must taste, to finish with, such a delightful and delicious present of Uncle Pumblechook’s!”\n\nMust they! Let them not hope to taste it!\n\n“You must know,” said my sister, rising, “it’s a pie; a savory pork pie.”\n\nThe company murmured their compliments. Uncle Pumblechook, sensible of having deserved well of his fellow-creatures, said,—quite vivaciously, all things considered,—“Well, Mrs. Joe, we’ll do our best endeavours; let us have a cut at this same pie.”\n\nMy sister went out to get it. I heard her steps proceed to the pantry. I saw Mr. Pumblechook balance his knife. I saw reawakening appetite in the Roman nostrils of Mr. Wopsle. I heard Mr. Hubble remark that “a bit of savory pork pie would lay atop of anything you could mention, and do no harm,” and I heard Joe say, “You shall have some, Pip.” I have never been absolutely certain whether I uttered a shrill yell of terror, merely in spirit, or in the bodily hearing of the company. I felt that I could bear no more, and that I must run away. I released the leg of the table, and ran for my life.\n\nBut I ran no farther than the house door, for there I ran head-foremost into a party of soldiers with their muskets, one of whom held out a pair of handcuffs to me, saying, “Here you are, look sharp, come on!”\n\n# Chapter V.\n\nThe apparition of a file of soldiers ringing down the but-ends of their loaded muskets on our door-step, caused the dinner-party to rise from table in confusion, and caused Mrs. Joe re-entering the kitchen empty-handed, to stop short and stare, in her wondering lament of “Gracious goodness gracious me, what’s gone—with the—pie!”\n\nThe sergeant and I were in the kitchen when Mrs. Joe stood staring; at which crisis I partially recovered the use of my senses. It was the sergeant who had spoken to me, and he was now looking round at the company, with his handcuffs invitingly extended towards them in his right hand, and his left on my shoulder.\n\n“Excuse me, ladies and gentleman,” said the sergeant, “but as I have mentioned at the door to this smart young shaver,” (which he hadn’t), “I am on a chase in the name of the king, and I want the blacksmith.”\n\n“And pray what might you want with him?” retorted my sister, quick to resent his being wanted at all.\n\n“Missis,” returned the gallant sergeant, “speaking for myself, I should reply, the honour and pleasure of his fine wife’s acquaintance; speaking for the king, I answer, a little job done.”\n\nThis was received as rather neat in the sergeant; insomuch that Mr. Pumblechook cried audibly, “Good again!”\n\n“You see, blacksmith,” said the sergeant, who had by this time picked out Joe with his eye, “we have had an accident with these, and I find the lock of one of ’em goes wrong, and the coupling don’t act pretty. As they are wanted for immediate service, will you throw your eye over them?”\n\nJoe threw his eye over them, and pronounced that the job would necessitate the lighting of his forge fire, and would take nearer two hours than one. “Will it? Then will you set about it at once, blacksmith?” said the off-hand sergeant, “as it’s on his Majesty’s service. And if my men can bear a hand anywhere, they’ll make themselves useful.” With that, he called to his men, who came trooping into the kitchen one after another, and piled their arms in a corner. And then they stood about, as soldiers do; now, with their hands loosely clasped before them; now, resting a knee or a shoulder; now, easing a belt or a pouch; now, opening the door to spit stiffly over their high stocks, out into the yard.\n\nAll these things I saw without then knowing that I saw them, for I was in an agony of apprehension. But beginning to perceive that the handcuffs were not for me, and that the military had so far got the better of the pie as to put it in the background, I collected a little more of my scattered wits.\n\n“Would you give me the time?” said the sergeant, addressing himself to Mr. Pumblechook, as to a man whose appreciative powers justified the inference that he was equal to the time.\n\n“It’s just gone half past two.”\n\n“That’s not so bad,” said the sergeant, reflecting; “even if I was forced to halt here nigh two hours, that’ll do. How far might you call yourselves from the marshes, hereabouts? Not above a mile, I reckon?”\n\n“Just a mile,” said Mrs. Joe.\n\n“That’ll do. We begin to close in upon ’em about dusk. A little before dusk, my orders are. That’ll do.”\n\n“Convicts, sergeant?” asked Mr. Wopsle, in a matter-of-course way.\n\n“Ay!” returned the sergeant, “two. They’re pretty well known to be out on the marshes still, and they won’t try to get clear of ’em before dusk. Anybody here seen anything of any such game?”\n\nEverybody, myself excepted, said no, with confidence. Nobody thought of me.\n\n“Well!” said the sergeant, “they’ll find themselves trapped in a circle, I expect, sooner than they count on. Now, blacksmith! If you’re ready, his Majesty the King is.”\n\nJoe had got his coat and waistcoat and cravat off, and his leather apron on, and passed into the forge. One of the soldiers opened its wooden windows, another lighted the fire, another turned to at the bellows, the rest stood round the blaze, which was soon roaring. Then Joe began to hammer and clink, hammer and clink, and we all looked on.\n\nThe interest of the impending pursuit not only absorbed the general attention, but even made my sister liberal. She drew a pitcher of beer from the cask for the soldiers, and invited the sergeant to take a glass of brandy. But Mr. Pumblechook said, sharply, “Give him wine, Mum. I’ll engage there’s no tar in that:” so, the sergeant thanked him and said that as he preferred his drink without tar, he would take wine, if it was equally convenient. When it was given him, he drank his Majesty’s health and compliments of the season, and took it all at a mouthful and smacked his lips.\n\n“Good stuff, eh, sergeant?” said Mr. Pumblechook.\n\n“I’ll tell you something,” returned the sergeant; “I suspect that stuff’s of your providing.”\n\nMr. Pumblechook, with a fat sort of laugh, said, “Ay, ay? Why?”\n\n“Because,” returned the sergeant, clapping him on the shoulder, “you’re a man that knows what’s what.”\n\n“D’ye think so?” said Mr. Pumblechook, with his former laugh. “Have another glass!”\n\n“With you. Hob and nob,” returned the sergeant. “The top of mine to the foot of yours,—the foot of yours to the top of mine,—Ring once, ring twice,—the best tune on the Musical Glasses! Your health. May you live a thousand years, and never be a worse judge of the right sort than you are at the present moment of your life!”\n\nThe sergeant tossed off his glass again and seemed quite ready for another glass. I noticed that Mr. Pumblechook in his hospitality appeared to forget that he had made a present of the wine, but took the bottle from Mrs. Joe and had all the credit of handing it about in a gush of joviality. Even I got some. And he was so very free of the wine that he even called for the other bottle, and handed that about with the same liberality, when the first was gone.\n\nAs I watched them while they all stood clustering about the forge, enjoying themselves so much, I thought what terrible good sauce for a dinner my fugitive friend on the marshes was. They had not enjoyed themselves a quarter so much, before the entertainment was brightened with the excitement he furnished. And now, when they were all in lively anticipation of “the two villains” being taken, and when the bellows seemed to roar for the fugitives, the fire to flare for them, the smoke to hurry away in pursuit of them, Joe to hammer and clink for them, and all the murky shadows on the wall to shake at them in menace as the blaze rose and sank, and the red-hot sparks dropped and died, the pale afternoon outside almost seemed in my pitying young fancy to have turned pale on their account, poor wretches.\n\nAt last, Joe’s job was done, and the ringing and roaring stopped. As Joe got on his coat, he mustered courage to propose that some of us should go down with the soldiers and see what came of the hunt. Mr. Pumblechook and Mr. Hubble declined, on the plea of a pipe and ladies’ society; but Mr. Wopsle said he would go, if Joe would. Joe said he was agreeable, and would take me, if Mrs. Joe approved. We never should have got leave to go, I am sure, but for Mrs. Joe’s curiosity to know all about it and how it ended. As it was, she merely stipulated, “If you bring the boy back with his head blown to bits by a musket, don’t look to me to put it together again.”\n\nThe sergeant took a polite leave of the ladies, and parted from Mr. Pumblechook as from a comrade; though I doubt if he were quite as fully sensible of that gentleman’s merits under arid conditions, as when something moist was going. His men resumed their muskets and fell in. Mr. Wopsle, Joe, and I, received strict charge to keep in the rear, and to speak no word after we reached the marshes. When we were all out in the raw air and were steadily moving towards our business, I treasonably whispered to Joe, “I hope, Joe, we shan’t find them.” and Joe whispered to me, “I’d give a shilling if they had cut and run, Pip.”\n\nWe were joined by no stragglers from the village, for the weather was cold and threatening, the way dreary, the footing bad, darkness coming on, and the people had good fires in-doors and were keeping the day. A few faces hurried to glowing windows and looked after us, but none came out. We passed the finger-post, and held straight on to the churchyard. There we were stopped a few minutes by a signal from the sergeant’s hand, while two or three of his men dispersed themselves among the graves, and also examined the porch. They came in again without finding anything, and then we struck out on the open marshes, through the gate at the side of the churchyard. A bitter sleet came rattling against us here on the east wind, and Joe took me on his back.\n\nNow that we were out upon the dismal wilderness where they little thought I had been within eight or nine hours and had seen both men hiding, I considered for the first time, with great dread, if we should come upon them, would my particular convict suppose that it was I who had brought the soldiers there? He had asked me if I was a deceiving imp, and he had said I should be a fierce young hound if I joined the hunt against him. Would he believe that I was both imp and hound in treacherous earnest, and had betrayed him?\n\nIt was of no use asking myself this question now. There I was, on Joe’s back, and there was Joe beneath me, charging at the ditches like a hunter, and stimulating Mr. Wopsle not to tumble on his Roman nose, and to keep up with us. The soldiers were in front of us, extending into a pretty wide line with an interval between man and man. We were taking the course I had begun with, and from which I had diverged in the mist. Either the mist was not out again yet, or the wind had dispelled it. Under the low red glare of sunset, the beacon, and the gibbet, and the mound of the Battery, and the opposite shore of the river, were plain, though all of a watery lead colour.\n\nWith my heart thumping like a blacksmith at Joe’s broad shoulder, I looked all about for any sign of the convicts. I could see none, I could hear none. Mr. Wopsle had greatly alarmed me more than once, by his blowing and hard breathing; but I knew the sounds by this time, and could dissociate them from the object of pursuit. I got a dreadful start, when I thought I heard the file still going; but it was only a sheep-bell. The sheep stopped in their eating and looked timidly at us; and the cattle, their heads turned from the wind and sleet, stared angrily as if they held us responsible for both annoyances; but, except these things, and the shudder of the dying day in every blade of grass, there was no break in the bleak stillness of the marshes.\n\nThe soldiers were moving on in the direction of the old Battery, and we were moving on a little way behind them, when, all of a sudden, we all stopped. For there had reached us on the wings of the wind and rain, a long shout. It was repeated. It was at a distance towards the east, but it was long and loud. Nay, there seemed to be two or more shouts raised together,—if one might judge from a confusion in the sound.\n\nTo this effect the sergeant and the nearest men were speaking under their breath, when Joe and I came up. After another moment’s listening, Joe (who was a good judge) agreed, and Mr. Wopsle (who was a bad judge) agreed. The sergeant, a decisive man, ordered that the sound should not be answered, but that the course should be changed, and that his men should make towards it “at the double.” So we slanted to the right (where the East was), and Joe pounded away so wonderfully, that I had to hold on tight to keep my seat.\n\nIt was a run indeed now, and what Joe called, in the only two words he spoke all the time, “a Winder.” Down banks and up banks, and over gates, and splashing into dikes, and breaking among coarse rushes: no man cared where he went. As we came nearer to the shouting, it became more and more apparent that it was made by more than one voice. Sometimes, it seemed to stop altogether, and then the soldiers stopped. When it broke out again, the soldiers made for it at a greater rate than ever, and we after them. After a while, we had so run it down, that we could hear one voice calling “Murder!” and another voice, “Convicts! Runaways! Guard! This way for the runaway convicts!” Then both voices would seem to be stifled in a struggle, and then would break out again. And when it had come to this, the soldiers ran like deer, and Joe too.\n\nThe sergeant ran in first, when we had run the noise quite down, and two of his men ran in close upon him. Their pieces were cocked and levelled when we all ran in.\n\n“Here are both men!” panted the sergeant, struggling at the bottom of a ditch. “Surrender, you two! and confound you for two wild beasts! Come asunder!”\n\nWater was splashing, and mud was flying, and oaths were being sworn, and blows were being struck, when some more men went down into the ditch to help the sergeant, and dragged out, separately, my convict and the other one. Both were bleeding and panting and execrating and struggling; but of course I knew them both directly.\n\n“Mind!” said my convict, wiping blood from his face with his ragged sleeves, and shaking torn hair from his fingers: “I took him! I give him up to you! Mind that!”\n\n“It’s not much to be particular about,” said the sergeant; “it’ll do you small good, my man, being in the same plight yourself. Handcuffs there!”\n\n“I don’t expect it to do me any good. I don’t want it to do me more good than it does now,” said my convict, with a greedy laugh. “I took him. He knows it. That’s enough for me.”\n\nThe other convict was livid to look at, and, in addition to the old bruised left side of his face, seemed to be bruised and torn all over. He could not so much as get his breath to speak, until they were both separately handcuffed, but leaned upon a soldier to keep himself from falling.\n\n“Take notice, guard,—he tried to murder me,” were his first words.\n\n“Tried to murder him?” said my convict, disdainfully. “Try, and not do it? I took him, and giv’ him up; that’s what I done. I not only prevented him getting off the marshes, but I dragged him here,—dragged him this far on his way back. He’s a gentleman, if you please, this villain. Now, the Hulks has got its gentleman again, through me. Murder him? Worth my while, too, to murder him, when I could do worse and drag him back!”\n\nThe other one still gasped, “He tried—he tried-to—murder me. Bear—bear witness.”\n\n“Lookee here!” said my convict to the sergeant. “Single-handed I got clear of the prison-ship; I made a dash and I done it. I could ha’ got clear of these death-cold flats likewise—look at my leg: you won’t find much iron on it—if I hadn’t made the discovery that he was here. Let him go free? Let him profit by the means as I found out? Let him make a tool of me afresh and again? Once more? No, no, no. If I had died at the bottom there,” and he made an emphatic swing at the ditch with his manacled hands, “I’d have held to him with that grip, that you should have been safe to find him in my hold.”\n\nThe other fugitive, who was evidently in extreme horror of his companion, repeated, “He tried to murder me. I should have been a dead man if you had not come up.”\n\n“He lies!” said my convict, with fierce energy. “He’s a liar born, and he’ll die a liar. Look at his face; ain’t it written there? Let him turn those eyes of his on me. I defy him to do it.”\n\nThe other, with an effort at a scornful smile, which could not, however, collect the nervous working of his mouth into any set expression, looked at the soldiers, and looked about at the marshes and at the sky, but certainly did not look at the speaker.\n\n“Do you see him?” pursued my convict. “Do you see what a villain he is? Do you see those grovelling and wandering eyes? That’s how he looked when we were tried together. He never looked at me.”\n\nThe other, always working and working his dry lips and turning his eyes restlessly about him far and near, did at last turn them for a moment on the speaker, with the words, “You are not much to look at,” and with a half-taunting glance at the bound hands. At that point, my convict became so frantically exasperated, that he would have rushed upon him but for the interposition of the soldiers. “Didn’t I tell you,” said the other convict then, “that he would murder me, if he could?” And any one could see that he shook with fear, and that there broke out upon his lips curious white flakes, like thin snow.\n\n“Enough of this parley,” said the sergeant. “Light those torches.”\n\nAs one of the soldiers, who carried a basket in lieu of a gun, went down on his knee to open it, my convict looked round him for the first time, and saw me. I had alighted from Joe’s back on the brink of the ditch when we came up, and had not moved since. I looked at him eagerly when he looked at me, and slightly moved my hands and shook my head. I had been waiting for him to see me that I might try to assure him of my innocence. It was not at all expressed to me that he even comprehended my intention, for he gave me a look that I did not understand, and it all passed in a moment. But if he had looked at me for an hour or for a day, I could not have remembered his face ever afterwards, as having been more attentive.\n\nThe soldier with the basket soon got a light, and lighted three or four torches, and took one himself and distributed the others. It had been almost dark before, but now it seemed quite dark, and soon afterwards very dark. Before we departed from that spot, four soldiers standing in a ring, fired twice into the air. Presently we saw other torches kindled at some distance behind us, and others on the marshes on the opposite bank of the river. “All right,” said the sergeant. “March.”\n\nWe had not gone far when three cannon were fired ahead of us with a sound that seemed to burst something inside my ear. “You are expected on board,” said the sergeant to my convict; “they know you are coming. Don’t straggle, my man. Close up here.”\n\nThe two were kept apart, and each walked surrounded by a separate guard. I had hold of Joe’s hand now, and Joe carried one of the torches. Mr. Wopsle had been for going back, but Joe was resolved to see it out, so we went on with the party. There was a reasonably good path now, mostly on the edge of the river, with a divergence here and there where a dike came, with a miniature windmill on it and a muddy sluice-gate. When I looked round, I could see the other lights coming in after us. The torches we carried dropped great blotches of fire upon the track, and I could see those, too, lying smoking and flaring. I could see nothing else but black darkness. Our lights warmed the air about us with their pitchy blaze, and the two prisoners seemed rather to like that, as they limped along in the midst of the muskets. We could not go fast, because of their lameness; and they were so spent, that two or three times we had to halt while they rested.\n\nAfter an hour or so of this travelling, we came to a rough wooden hut and a landing-place. There was a guard in the hut, and they challenged, and the sergeant answered. Then, we went into the hut, where there was a smell of tobacco and whitewash, and a bright fire, and a lamp, and a stand of muskets, and a drum, and a low wooden bedstead, like an overgrown mangle without the machinery, capable of holding about a dozen soldiers all at once. Three or four soldiers who lay upon it in their great-coats were not much interested in us, but just lifted their heads and took a sleepy stare, and then lay down again. The sergeant made some kind of report, and some entry in a book, and then the convict whom I call the other convict was drafted off with his guard, to go on board first.\n\nMy convict never looked at me, except that once. While we stood in the hut, he stood before the fire looking thoughtfully at it, or putting up his feet by turns upon the hob, and looking thoughtfully at them as if he pitied them for their recent adventures. Suddenly, he turned to the sergeant, and remarked,—\n\n“I wish to say something respecting this escape. It may prevent some persons laying under suspicion alonger me.”\n\n“You can say what you like,” returned the sergeant, standing coolly looking at him with his arms folded, “but you have no call to say it here. You’ll have opportunity enough to say about it, and hear about it, before it’s done with, you know.”\n\n“I know, but this is another pint, a separate matter. A man can’t starve; at least I can’t. I took some wittles, up at the willage over yonder,—where the church stands a’most out on the marshes.”\n\n“You mean stole,” said the sergeant.\n\n“And I’ll tell you where from. From the blacksmith’s.”\n\n“Halloa!” said the sergeant, staring at Joe.\n\n“Halloa, Pip!” said Joe, staring at me.\n\n“It was some broken wittles—that’s what it was—and a dram of liquor, and a pie.”\n\n“Have you happened to miss such an article as a pie, blacksmith?” asked the sergeant, confidentially.\n\n“My wife did, at the very moment when you came in. Don’t you know, Pip?”\n\n“So,” said my convict, turning his eyes on Joe in a moody manner, and without the least glance at me,—“so you’re the blacksmith, are you? Than I’m sorry to say, I’ve eat your pie.”\n\n“God knows you’re welcome to it,—so far as it was ever mine,” returned Joe, with a saving remembrance of Mrs. Joe. “We don’t know what you have done, but we wouldn’t have you starved to death for it, poor miserable fellow-creatur.—Would us, Pip?”\n\nThe something that I had noticed before, clicked in the man’s throat again, and he turned his back. The boat had returned, and his guard were ready, so we followed him to the landing-place made of rough stakes and stones, and saw him put into the boat, which was rowed by a crew of convicts like himself. No one seemed surprised to see him, or interested in seeing him, or glad to see him, or sorry to see him, or spoke a word, except that somebody in the boat growled as if to dogs, “Give way, you!” which was the signal for the dip of the oars. By the light of the torches, we saw the black Hulk lying out a little way from the mud of the shore, like a wicked Noah’s ark. Cribbed and barred and moored by massive rusty chains, the prison-ship seemed in my young eyes to be ironed like the prisoners. We saw the boat go alongside, and we saw him taken up the side and disappear. Then, the ends of the torches were flung hissing into the water, and went out, as if it were all over with him.\n\n# Chapter VI.\n\nMy state of mind regarding the pilfering from which I had been so unexpectedly exonerated did not impel me to frank disclosure; but I hope it had some dregs of good at the bottom of it.\n\nI do not recall that I felt any tenderness of conscience in reference to Mrs. Joe, when the fear of being found out was lifted off me. But I loved Joe,—perhaps for no better reason in those early days than because the dear fellow let me love him,—and, as to him, my inner self was not so easily composed. It was much upon my mind (particularly when I first saw him looking about for his file) that I ought to tell Joe the whole truth. Yet I did not, and for the reason that I mistrusted that if I did, he would think me worse than I was. The fear of losing Joe’s confidence, and of thenceforth sitting in the chimney corner at night staring drearily at my forever lost companion and friend, tied up my tongue. I morbidly represented to myself that if Joe knew it, I never afterwards could see him at the fireside feeling his fair whisker, without thinking that he was meditating on it. That, if Joe knew it, I never afterwards could see him glance, however casually, at yesterday’s meat or pudding when it came on to-day’s table, without thinking that he was debating whether I had been in the pantry. That, if Joe knew it, and at any subsequent period of our joint domestic life remarked that his beer was flat or thick, the conviction that he suspected tar in it, would bring a rush of blood to my face. In a word, I was too cowardly to do what I knew to be right, as I had been too cowardly to avoid doing what I knew to be wrong. I had had no intercourse with the world at that time, and I imitated none of its many inhabitants who act in this manner. Quite an untaught genius, I made the discovery of the line of action for myself.\n\nAs I was sleepy before we were far away from the prison-ship, Joe took me on his back again and carried me home. He must have had a tiresome journey of it, for Mr. Wopsle, being knocked up, was in such a very bad temper that if the Church had been thrown open, he would probably have excommunicated the whole expedition, beginning with Joe and myself. In his lay capacity, he persisted in sitting down in the damp to such an insane extent, that when his coat was taken off to be dried at the kitchen fire, the circumstantial evidence on his trousers would have hanged him, if it had been a capital offence.\n\nBy that time, I was staggering on the kitchen floor like a little drunkard, through having been newly set upon my feet, and through having been fast asleep, and through waking in the heat and lights and noise of tongues. As I came to myself (with the aid of a heavy thump between the shoulders, and the restorative exclamation “Yah! Was there ever such a boy as this!” from my sister,) I found Joe telling them about the convict’s confession, and all the visitors suggesting different ways by which he had got into the pantry. Mr. Pumblechook made out, after carefully surveying the premises, that he had first got upon the roof of the forge, and had then got upon the roof of the house, and had then let himself down the kitchen chimney by a rope made of his bedding cut into strips; and as Mr. Pumblechook was very positive and drove his own chaise-cart—over everybody—it was agreed that it must be so. Mr. Wopsle, indeed, wildly cried out, “No!” with the feeble malice of a tired man; but, as he had no theory, and no coat on, he was unanimously set at naught,—not to mention his smoking hard behind, as he stood with his back to the kitchen fire to draw the damp out: which was not calculated to inspire confidence.\n\nThis was all I heard that night before my sister clutched me, as a slumberous offence to the company’s eyesight, and assisted me up to bed with such a strong hand that I seemed to have fifty boots on, and to be dangling them all against the edges of the stairs. My state of mind, as I have described it, began before I was up in the morning, and lasted long after the subject had died out, and had ceased to be mentioned saving on exceptional occasions.\n\n# Chapter VII.\n\nAt the time when I stood in the churchyard reading the family tombstones, I had just enough learning to be able to spell them out. My construction even of their simple meaning was not very correct, for I read “wife of the Above” as a complimentary reference to my father’s exaltation to a better world; and if any one of my deceased relations had been referred to as “Below,” I have no doubt I should have formed the worst opinions of that member of the family. Neither were my notions of the theological positions to which my Catechism bound me, at all accurate; for, I have a lively remembrance that I supposed my declaration that I was to “walk in the same all the days of my life,” laid me under an obligation always to go through the village from our house in one particular direction, and never to vary it by turning down by the wheelwright’s or up by the mill.\n\nWhen I was old enough, I was to be apprenticed to Joe, and until I could assume that dignity I was not to be what Mrs. Joe called “Pompeyed,” or (as I render it) pampered. Therefore, I was not only odd-boy about the forge, but if any neighbour happened to want an extra boy to frighten birds, or pick up stones, or do any such job, I was favoured with the employment. In order, however, that our superior position might not be compromised thereby, a money-box was kept on the kitchen mantel-shelf, into which it was publicly made known that all my earnings were dropped. I have an impression that they were to be contributed eventually towards the liquidation of the National Debt, but I know I had no hope of any personal participation in the treasure.\n\nMr. Wopsle’s great-aunt kept an evening school in the village; that is to say, she was a ridiculous old woman of limited means and unlimited infirmity, who used to go to sleep from six to seven every evening, in the society of youth who paid two pence per week each, for the improving opportunity of seeing her do it. She rented a small cottage, and Mr. Wopsle had the room upstairs, where we students used to overhear him reading aloud in a most dignified and terrific manner, and occasionally bumping on the ceiling. There was a fiction that Mr. Wopsle “examined” the scholars once a quarter. What he did on those occasions was to turn up his cuffs, stick up his hair, and give us Mark Antony’s oration over the body of Caesar. This was always followed by Collins’s Ode on the Passions, wherein I particularly venerated Mr. Wopsle as Revenge throwing his blood-stained sword in thunder down, and taking the War-denouncing trumpet with a withering look. It was not with me then, as it was in later life, when I fell into the society of the Passions, and compared them with Collins and Wopsle, rather to the disadvantage of both gentlemen.\n\nMr. Wopsle’s great-aunt, besides keeping this Educational Institution, kept in the same room—a little general shop. She had no idea what stock she had, or what the price of anything in it was; but there was a little greasy memorandum-book kept in a drawer, which served as a Catalogue of Prices, and by this oracle Biddy arranged all the shop transactions. Biddy was Mr. Wopsle’s great-aunt’s granddaughter; I confess myself quite unequal to the working out of the problem, what relation she was to Mr. Wopsle. She was an orphan like myself; like me, too, had been brought up by hand. She was most noticeable, I thought, in respect of her extremities; for, her hair always wanted brushing, her hands always wanted washing, and her shoes always wanted mending and pulling up at heel. This description must be received with a week-day limitation. On Sundays, she went to church elaborated.\n\nMuch of my unassisted self, and more by the help of Biddy than of Mr. Wopsle’s great-aunt, I struggled through the alphabet as if it had been a bramble-bush; getting considerably worried and scratched by every letter. After that I fell among those thieves, the nine figures, who seemed every evening to do something new to disguise themselves and baffle recognition. But, at last I began, in a purblind groping way, to read, write, and cipher, on the very smallest scale.\n\nOne night I was sitting in the chimney corner with my slate, expending great efforts on the production of a letter to Joe. I think it must have been a full year after our hunt upon the marshes, for it was a long time after, and it was winter and a hard frost. With an alphabet on the hearth at my feet for reference, I contrived in an hour or two to print and smear this epistle:—\n\n“MI DEER JO i OPE U R KRWITE WELL i OPE i SHAL SON B HABELL 4 2 TEEDGE U JO AN THEN WE SHORL B SO GLODD AN WEN i M PRENGTD 2 U JO WOT LARX AN BLEVE ME INF XN PIP.”\n\nThere was no indispensable necessity for my communicating with Joe by letter, inasmuch as he sat beside me and we were alone. But I delivered this written communication (slate and all) with my own hand, and Joe received it as a miracle of erudition.\n\n“I say, Pip, old chap!” cried Joe, opening his blue eyes wide, “what a scholar you are! An’t you?”\n\n“I should like to be,” said I, glancing at the slate as he held it; with a misgiving that the writing was rather hilly.\n\n“Why, here’s a J,” said Joe, “and a O equal to anythink! Here’s a J and a O, Pip, and a J-O, Joe.”\n\nI had never heard Joe read aloud to any greater extent than this monosyllable, and I had observed at church last Sunday, when I accidentally held our Prayer-Book upside down, that it seemed to suit his convenience quite as well as if it had been all right. Wishing to embrace the present occasion of finding out whether in teaching Joe, I should have to begin quite at the beginning, I said, “Ah! But read the rest, Jo.”\n\n“The rest, eh, Pip?” said Joe, looking at it with a slow, searching eye, “One, two, three. Why, here’s three Js, and three Os, and three J-O, Joes in it, Pip!”\n\nI leaned over Joe, and, with the aid of my forefinger read him the whole letter.\n\n“Astonishing!” said Joe, when I had finished. “You ARE a scholar.”\n\n“How do you spell Gargery, Joe?” I asked him, with a modest patronage.\n\n“I don’t spell it at all,” said Joe.\n\n“But supposing you did?”\n\n“It can’t be supposed,” said Joe. “Tho’ I’m uncommon fond of reading, too.”\n\n“Are you, Joe?”\n\n“On-common. Give me,” said Joe, “a good book, or a good newspaper, and sit me down afore a good fire, and I ask no better. Lord!” he continued, after rubbing his knees a little, “when you do come to a J and a O, and says you, ‘Here, at last, is a J-O, Joe,’ how interesting reading is!”\n\nI derived from this, that Joe’s education, like Steam, was yet in its infancy. Pursuing the subject, I inquired,—\n\n“Didn’t you ever go to school, Joe, when you were as little as me?”\n\n“No, Pip.”\n\n“Why didn’t you ever go to school, Joe, when you were as little as me?”\n\n“Well, Pip,” said Joe, taking up the poker, and settling himself to his usual occupation when he was thoughtful, of slowly raking the fire between the lower bars; “I’ll tell you. My father, Pip, he were given to drink, and when he were overtook with drink, he hammered away at my mother, most onmerciful. It were a’most the only hammering he did, indeed, ’xcepting at myself. And he hammered at me with a wigor only to be equalled by the wigor with which he didn’t hammer at his anwil.—You’re a listening and understanding, Pip?”\n\n“Yes, Joe.”\n\n“Consequence, my mother and me we ran away from my father several times; and then my mother she’d go out to work, and she’d say, “Joe,” she’d say, “now, please God, you shall have some schooling, child,” and she’d put me to school. But my father were that good in his hart that he couldn’t abear to be without us. So, he’d come with a most tremenjous crowd and make such a row at the doors of the houses where we was, that they used to be obligated to have no more to do with us and to give us up to him. And then he took us home and hammered us. Which, you see, Pip,” said Joe, pausing in his meditative raking of the fire, and looking at me, “were a drawback on my learning.”\n\n“Certainly, poor Joe!”\n\n“Though mind you, Pip,” said Joe, with a judicial touch or two of the poker on the top bar, “rendering unto all their doo, and maintaining equal justice betwixt man and man, my father were that good in his hart, don’t you see?”\n\nI didn’t see; but I didn’t say so.\n\n“Well!” Joe pursued, “somebody must keep the pot a-biling, Pip, or the pot won’t bile, don’t you know?”\n\nI saw that, and said so.\n\n“Consequence, my father didn’t make objections to my going to work; so I went to work at my present calling, which were his too, if he would have followed it, and I worked tolerable hard, I assure you, Pip. In time I were able to keep him, and I kep him till he went off in a purple leptic fit. And it were my intentions to have had put upon his tombstone that, Whatsume’er the failings on his part, Remember reader he were that good in his heart.”\n\nJoe recited this couplet with such manifest pride and careful perspicuity, that I asked him if he had made it himself.\n\n“I made it,” said Joe, “my own self. I made it in a moment. It was like striking out a horseshoe complete, in a single blow. I never was so much surprised in all my life,—couldn’t credit my own ed,—to tell you the truth, hardly believed it were my own ed. As I was saying, Pip, it were my intentions to have had it cut over him; but poetry costs money, cut it how you will, small or large, and it were not done. Not to mention bearers, all the money that could be spared were wanted for my mother. She were in poor elth, and quite broke. She weren’t long of following, poor soul, and her share of peace come round at last.”\n\nJoe’s blue eyes turned a little watery; he rubbed first one of them, and then the other, in a most uncongenial and uncomfortable manner, with the round knob on the top of the poker.\n\n“It were but lonesome then,” said Joe, “living here alone, and I got acquainted with your sister. Now, Pip,”—Joe looked firmly at me as if he knew I was not going to agree with him;—“your sister is a fine figure of a woman.”\n\nI could not help looking at the fire, in an obvious state of doubt.\n\n“Whatever family opinions, or whatever the world’s opinions, on that subject may be, Pip, your sister is,” Joe tapped the top bar with the poker after every word following, “a-fine-figure—of—a—woman!”\n\nI could think of nothing better to say than “I am glad you think so, Joe.”\n\n“So am I,” returned Joe, catching me up. “I am glad I think so, Pip. A little redness or a little matter of Bone, here or there, what does it signify to Me?”\n\nI sagaciously observed, if it didn’t signify to him, to whom did it signify?\n\n“Certainly!” assented Joe. “That’s it. You’re right, old chap! When I got acquainted with your sister, it were the talk how she was bringing you up by hand. Very kind of her too, all the folks said, and I said, along with all the folks. As to you,” Joe pursued with a countenance expressive of seeing something very nasty indeed, “if you could have been aware how small and flabby and mean you was, dear me, you’d have formed the most contemptible opinion of yourself!”\n\nNot exactly relishing this, I said, “Never mind me, Joe.”\n\n“But I did mind you, Pip,” he returned with tender simplicity. “When I offered to your sister to keep company, and to be asked in church at such times as she was willing and ready to come to the forge, I said to her, ‘And bring the poor little child. God bless the poor little child,’ I said to your sister, ‘there’s room for him at the forge!’”\n\nI broke out crying and begging pardon, and hugged Joe round the neck: who dropped the poker to hug me, and to say, “Ever the best of friends; an’t us, Pip? Don’t cry, old chap!”\n\nWhen this little interruption was over, Joe resumed:—\n\n“Well, you see, Pip, and here we are! That’s about where it lights; here we are! Now, when you take me in hand in my learning, Pip (and I tell you beforehand I am awful dull, most awful dull), Mrs. Joe mustn’t see too much of what we’re up to. It must be done, as I may say, on the sly. And why on the sly? I’ll tell you why, Pip.”\n\nHe had taken up the poker again; without which, I doubt if he could have proceeded in his demonstration.\n\n“Your sister is given to government.”\n\n“Given to government, Joe?” I was startled, for I had some shadowy idea (and I am afraid I must add, hope) that Joe had divorced her in a favour of the Lords of the Admiralty, or Treasury.\n\n“Given to government,” said Joe. “Which I meantersay the government of you and myself.”\n\n“Oh!”\n\n“And she an’t over partial to having scholars on the premises,” Joe continued, “and in partickler would not be over partial to my being a scholar, for fear as I might rise. Like a sort of rebel, don’t you see?”\n\nI was going to retort with an inquiry, and had got as far as “Why—” when Joe stopped me.\n\n“Stay a bit. I know what you’re a-going to say, Pip; stay a bit! I don’t deny that your sister comes the Mo-gul over us, now and again. I don’t deny that she do throw us back-falls, and that she do drop down upon us heavy. At such times as when your sister is on the Ram-page, Pip,” Joe sank his voice to a whisper and glanced at the door, “candour compels fur to admit that she is a Buster.”\n\nJoe pronounced this word, as if it began with at least twelve capital Bs.\n\n“Why don’t I rise? That were your observation when I broke it off, Pip?”\n\n“Yes, Joe.”\n\n“Well,” said Joe, passing the poker into his left hand, that he might feel his whisker; and I had no hope of him whenever he took to that placid occupation; “your sister’s a master-mind. A master-mind.”\n\n“What’s that?” I asked, in some hope of bringing him to a stand. But Joe was readier with his definition than I had expected, and completely stopped me by arguing circularly, and answering with a fixed look, “Her.”\n\n“And I ain’t a master-mind,” Joe resumed, when he had unfixed his look, and got back to his whisker. “And last of all, Pip,—and this I want to say very serious to you, old chap,—I see so much in my poor mother, of a woman drudging and slaving and breaking her honest hart and never getting no peace in her mortal days, that I’m dead afeerd of going wrong in the way of not doing what’s right by a woman, and I’d fur rather of the two go wrong the t’other way, and be a little ill-conwenienced myself. I wish it was only me that got put out, Pip; I wish there warn’t no Tickler for you, old chap; I wish I could take it all on myself; but this is the up-and-down-and-straight on it, Pip, and I hope you’ll overlook shortcomings.”\n\nYoung as I was, I believe that I dated a new admiration of Joe from that night. We were equals afterwards, as we had been before; but, afterwards at quiet times when I sat looking at Joe and thinking about him, I had a new sensation of feeling conscious that I was looking up to Joe in my heart.\n\n“However,” said Joe, rising to replenish the fire; “here’s the Dutch-clock a-working himself up to being equal to strike Eight of ’em, and she’s not come home yet! I hope Uncle Pumblechook’s mare mayn’t have set a forefoot on a piece o’ ice, and gone down.”\n\nMrs. Joe made occasional trips with Uncle Pumblechook on market-days, to assist him in buying such household stuffs and goods as required a woman’s judgment; Uncle Pumblechook being a bachelor and reposing no confidences in his domestic servant. This was market-day, and Mrs. Joe was out on one of these expeditions.\n\nJoe made the fire and swept the hearth, and then we went to the door to listen for the chaise-cart. It was a dry cold night, and the wind blew keenly, and the frost was white and hard. A man would die to-night of lying out on the marshes, I thought. And then I looked at the stars, and considered how awful it would be for a man to turn his face up to them as he froze to death, and see no help or pity in all the glittering multitude.\n\n“Here comes the mare,” said Joe, “ringing like a peal of bells!”\n\nThe sound of her iron shoes upon the hard road was quite musical, as she came along at a much brisker trot than usual. We got a chair out, ready for Mrs. Joe’s alighting, and stirred up the fire that they might see a bright window, and took a final survey of the kitchen that nothing might be out of its place. When we had completed these preparations, they drove up, wrapped to the eyes. Mrs. Joe was soon landed, and Uncle Pumblechook was soon down too, covering the mare with a cloth, and we were soon all in the kitchen, carrying so much cold air in with us that it seemed to drive all the heat out of the fire.\n\n“Now,” said Mrs. Joe, unwrapping herself with haste and excitement, and throwing her bonnet back on her shoulders where it hung by the strings, “if this boy ain’t grateful this night, he never will be!”\n\nI looked as grateful as any boy possibly could, who was wholly uninformed why he ought to assume that expression.\n\n“It’s only to be hoped,” said my sister, “that he won’t be Pompeyed. But I have my fears.”\n\n“She ain’t in that line, Mum,” said Mr. Pumblechook. “She knows better.”\n\nShe? I looked at Joe, making the motion with my lips and eyebrows, “She?” Joe looked at me, making the motion with his lips and eyebrows, “She?” My sister catching him in the act, he drew the back of his hand across his nose with his usual conciliatory air on such occasions, and looked at her.\n\n“Well?” said my sister, in her snappish way. “What are you staring at? Is the house afire?”\n\n“—Which some individual,” Joe politely hinted, “mentioned—she.”\n\n“And she is a she, I suppose?” said my sister. “Unless you call Miss Havisham a he. And I doubt if even you’ll go so far as that.”\n\n“Miss Havisham, up town?” said Joe.\n\n“Is there any Miss Havisham down town?” returned my sister.\n“She wants this boy to go and play there. And of course he’s going. And he had better play there,” said my sister, shaking her head at me as an encouragement to be extremely light and sportive, “or I’ll work him.”\n\nI had heard of Miss Havisham up town,—everybody for miles round had heard of Miss Havisham up town,—as an immensely rich and grim lady who lived in a large and dismal house barricaded against robbers, and who led a life of seclusion.\n\n“Well to be sure!” said Joe, astounded. “I wonder how she come to know Pip!”\n\n“Noodle!” cried my sister. “Who said she knew him?”\n\n“—Which some individual,” Joe again politely hinted, “mentioned that she wanted him to go and play there.”\n\n“And couldn’t she ask Uncle Pumblechook if he knew of a boy to go and play there? Isn’t it just barely possible that Uncle Pumblechook may be a tenant of hers, and that he may sometimes—we won’t say quarterly or half-yearly, for that would be requiring too much of you—but sometimes—go there to pay his rent? And couldn’t she then ask Uncle Pumblechook if he knew of a boy to go and play there? And couldn’t Uncle Pumblechook, being always considerate and thoughtful for us—though you may not think it, Joseph,” in a tone of the deepest reproach, as if he were the most callous of nephews, “then mention this boy, standing Prancing here”—which I solemnly declare I was not doing—“that I have for ever been a willing slave to?”\n\n“Good again!” cried Uncle Pumblechook. “Well put! Prettily pointed! Good indeed! Now Joseph, you know the case.”\n\n“No, Joseph,” said my sister, still in a reproachful manner, while Joe apologetically drew the back of his hand across and across his nose, “you do not yet—though you may not think it—know the case. You may consider that you do, but you do not, Joseph. For you do not know that Uncle Pumblechook, being sensible that for anything we can tell, this boy’s fortune may be made by his going to Miss Havisham’s, has offered to take him into town to-night in his own chaise-cart, and to keep him to-night, and to take him with his own hands to Miss Havisham’s to-morrow morning. And Lor-a-mussy me!” cried my sister, casting off her bonnet in sudden desperation, “here I stand talking to mere Mooncalfs, with Uncle Pumblechook waiting, and the mare catching cold at the door, and the boy grimed with crock and dirt from the hair of his head to the sole of his foot!”\n\nWith that, she pounced upon me, like an eagle on a lamb, and my face was squeezed into wooden bowls in sinks, and my head was put under taps of water-butts, and I was soaped, and kneaded, and towelled, and thumped, and harrowed, and rasped, until I really was quite beside myself. (I may here remark that I suppose myself to be better acquainted than any living authority, with the ridgy effect of a wedding-ring, passing unsympathetically over the human countenance.)\n\nWhen my ablutions were completed, I was put into clean linen of the stiffest character, like a young penitent into sackcloth, and was trussed up in my tightest and fearfullest suit. I was then delivered over to Mr. Pumblechook, who formally received me as if he were the Sheriff, and who let off upon me the speech that I knew he had been dying to make all along: “Boy, be forever grateful to all friends, but especially unto them which brought you up by hand!”\n\n“Good-bye, Joe!”\n\n“God bless you, Pip, old chap!”\n\nI had never parted from him before, and what with my feelings and what with soapsuds, I could at first see no stars from the chaise-cart. But they twinkled out one by one, without throwing any light on the questions why on earth I was going to play at Miss Havisham’s, and what on earth I was expected to play at.\n\n# Chapter VIII.\n\nMr. Pumblechook’s premises in the High Street of the market town, were of a peppercorny and farinaceous character, as the premises of a cornchandler and seedsman should be. It appeared to me that he must be a very happy man indeed, to have so many little drawers in his shop; and I wondered when I peeped into one or two on the lower tiers, and saw the tied-up brown paper packets inside, whether the flower-seeds and bulbs ever wanted of a fine day to break out of those jails, and bloom.\n\nIt was in the early morning after my arrival that I entertained this speculation. On the previous night, I had been sent straight to bed in an attic with a sloping roof, which was so low in the corner where the bedstead was, that I calculated the tiles as being within a foot of my eyebrows. In the same early morning, I discovered a singular affinity between seeds and corduroys. Mr. Pumblechook wore corduroys, and so did his shopman; and somehow, there was a general air and flavour about the corduroys, so much in the nature of seeds, and a general air and flavour about the seeds, so much in the nature of corduroys, that I hardly knew which was which. The same opportunity served me for noticing that Mr. Pumblechook appeared to conduct his business by looking across the street at the saddler, who appeared to transact his business by keeping his eye on the coachmaker, who appeared to get on in life by putting his hands in his pockets and contemplating the baker, who in his turn folded his arms and stared at the grocer, who stood at his door and yawned at the chemist. The watchmaker, always poring over a little desk with a magnifying-glass at his eye, and always inspected by a group of smock-frocks poring over him through the glass of his shop-window, seemed to be about the only person in the High Street whose trade engaged his attention.\n\nMr. Pumblechook and I breakfasted at eight o’clock in the parlour behind the shop, while the shopman took his mug of tea and hunch of bread and butter on a sack of peas in the front premises. I considered Mr. Pumblechook wretched company. Besides being possessed by my sister’s idea that a mortifying and penitential character ought to be imparted to my diet,—besides giving me as much crumb as possible in combination with as little butter, and putting such a quantity of warm water into my milk that it would have been more candid to have left the milk out altogether,—his conversation consisted of nothing but arithmetic. On my politely bidding him Good-morning, he said, pompously, “Seven times nine, boy?” And how should I be able to answer, dodged in that way, in a strange place, on an empty stomach! I was hungry, but before I had swallowed a morsel, he began a running sum that lasted all through the breakfast. “Seven?” “And four?” “And eight?” “And six?” “And two?” “And ten?” And so on. And after each figure was disposed of, it was as much as I could do to get a bite or a sup, before the next came; while he sat at his ease guessing nothing, and eating bacon and hot roll, in (if I may be allowed the expression) a gorging and gormandizing manner.\n\nFor such reasons, I was very glad when ten o’clock came and we started for Miss Havisham’s; though I was not at all at my ease regarding the manner in which I should acquit myself under that lady’s roof. Within a quarter of an hour we came to Miss Havisham’s house, which was of old brick, and dismal, and had a great many iron bars to it. Some of the windows had been walled up; of those that remained, all the lower were rustily barred. There was a courtyard in front, and that was barred; so we had to wait, after ringing the bell, until some one should come to open it. While we waited at the gate, I peeped in (even then Mr. Pumblechook said, “And fourteen?” but I pretended not to hear him), and saw that at the side of the house there was a large brewery. No brewing was going on in it, and none seemed to have gone on for a long long time.\n\nA window was raised, and a clear voice demanded “What name?” To which my conductor replied, “Pumblechook.” The voice returned, “Quite right,” and the window was shut again, and a young lady came across the court-yard, with keys in her hand.\n\n“This,” said Mr. Pumblechook, “is Pip.”\n\n“This is Pip, is it?” returned the young lady, who was very pretty and seemed very proud; “come in, Pip.”\n\nMr. Pumblechook was coming in also, when she stopped him with the gate.\n\n“Oh!” she said. “Did you wish to see Miss Havisham?”\n\n“If Miss Havisham wished to see me,” returned Mr. Pumblechook, discomfited.\n\n“Ah!” said the girl; “but you see she don’t.”\n\nShe said it so finally, and in such an undiscussible way, that Mr. Pumblechook, though in a condition of ruffled dignity, could not protest. But he eyed me severely,—as if I had done anything to him!—and departed with the words reproachfully delivered: “Boy! Let your behaviour here be a credit unto them which brought you up by hand!” I was not free from apprehension that he would come back to propound through the gate, “And sixteen?” But he didn’t.\n\nMy young conductress locked the gate, and we went across the courtyard. It was paved and clean, but grass was growing in every crevice. The brewery buildings had a little lane of communication with it, and the wooden gates of that lane stood open, and all the brewery beyond stood open, away to the high enclosing wall; and all was empty and disused. The cold wind seemed to blow colder there than outside the gate; and it made a shrill noise in howling in and out at the open sides of the brewery, like the noise of wind in the rigging of a ship at sea.\n\nShe saw me looking at it, and she said, “You could drink without hurt all the strong beer that’s brewed there now, boy.”\n\n“I should think I could, miss,” said I, in a shy way.\n\n“Better not try to brew beer there now, or it would turn out sour, boy; don’t you think so?”\n\n“It looks like it, miss.”\n\n“Not that anybody means to try,” she added, “for that’s all done with, and the place will stand as idle as it is till it falls. As to strong beer, there’s enough of it in the cellars already, to drown the Manor House.”\n\n“Is that the name of this house, miss?”\n\n“One of its names, boy.”\n\n“It has more than one, then, miss?”\n\n“One more. Its other name was Satis; which is Greek, or Latin, or Hebrew, or all three—or all one to me—for enough.”\n\n“Enough House,” said I; “that’s a curious name, miss.”\n\n“Yes,” she replied; “but it meant more than it said. It meant, when it was given, that whoever had this house could want nothing else. They must have been easily satisfied in those days, I should think. But don’t loiter, boy.”\n\nThough she called me “boy” so often, and with a carelessness that was far from complimentary, she was of about my own age. She seemed much older than I, of course, being a girl, and beautiful and self-possessed; and she was as scornful of me as if she had been one-and-twenty, and a queen.\n\nWe went into the house by a side door, the great front entrance had two chains across it outside,—and the first thing I noticed was, that the passages were all dark, and that she had left a candle burning there. She took it up, and we went through more passages and up a staircase, and still it was all dark, and only the candle lighted us.\n\nAt last we came to the door of a room, and she said, “Go in.”\n\nI answered, more in shyness than politeness, “After you, miss.”\n\nTo this she returned: “Don’t be ridiculous, boy; I am not going in.” And scornfully walked away, and—what was worse—took the candle with her.\n\nThis was very uncomfortable, and I was half afraid. However, the only thing to be done being to knock at the door, I knocked, and was told from within to enter. I entered, therefore, and found myself in a pretty large room, well lighted with wax candles. No glimpse of daylight was to be seen in it. It was a dressing-room, as I supposed from the furniture, though much of it was of forms and uses then quite unknown to me. But prominent in it was a draped table with a gilded looking-glass, and that I made out at first sight to be a fine lady’s dressing-table.\n\nWhether I should have made out this object so soon if there had been no fine lady sitting at it, I cannot say. In an arm-chair, with an elbow resting on the table and her head leaning on that hand, sat the strangest lady I have ever seen, or shall ever see.\n\nShe was dressed in rich materials,—satins, and lace, and silks,—all of white. Her shoes were white. And she had a long white veil dependent from her hair, and she had bridal flowers in her hair, but her hair was white. Some bright jewels sparkled on her neck and on her hands, and some other jewels lay sparkling on the table. Dresses, less splendid than the dress she wore, and half-packed trunks, were scattered about. She had not quite finished dressing, for she had but one shoe on,—the other was on the table near her hand,—her veil was but half arranged, her watch and chain were not put on, and some lace for her bosom lay with those trinkets, and with her handkerchief, and gloves, and some flowers, and a Prayer-Book all confusedly heaped about the looking-glass.\n\nIt was not in the first few moments that I saw all these things, though I saw more of them in the first moments than might be supposed. But I saw that everything within my view which ought to be white, had been white long ago, and had lost its lustre and was faded and yellow. I saw that the bride within the bridal dress had withered like the dress, and like the flowers, and had no brightness left but the brightness of her sunken eyes. I saw that the dress had been put upon the rounded figure of a young woman, and that the figure upon which it now hung loose had shrunk to skin and bone. Once, I had been taken to see some ghastly waxwork at the Fair, representing I know not what impossible personage lying in state. Once, I had been taken to one of our old marsh churches to see a skeleton in the ashes of a rich dress that had been dug out of a vault under the church pavement. Now, waxwork and skeleton seemed to have dark eyes that moved and looked at me. I should have cried out, if I could.\n\n“Who is it?” said the lady at the table.\n\n“Pip, ma’am.”\n\n“Pip?”\n\n“Mr. Pumblechook’s boy, ma’am. Come—to play.”\n\n“Come nearer; let me look at you. Come close.”\n\nIt was when I stood before her, avoiding her eyes, that I took note of the surrounding objects in detail, and saw that her watch had stopped at twenty minutes to nine, and that a clock in the room had stopped at twenty minutes to nine.\n\n“Look at me,” said Miss Havisham. “You are not afraid of a woman who has never seen the sun since you were born?”\n\nI regret to state that I was not afraid of telling the enormous lie comprehended in the answer “No.”\n\n“Do you know what I touch here?” she said, laying her hands, one upon the other, on her left side.\n\n“Yes, ma’am.” (It made me think of the young man.)\n\n“What do I touch?”\n\n“Your heart.”\n\n“Broken!”\n\nShe uttered the word with an eager look, and with strong emphasis, and with a weird smile that had a kind of boast in it. Afterwards she kept her hands there for a little while, and slowly took them away as if they were heavy.\n\n“I am tired,” said Miss Havisham. “I want diversion, and I have done with men and women. Play.”\n\nI think it will be conceded by my most disputatious reader, that she could hardly have directed an unfortunate boy to do anything in the wide world more difficult to be done under the circumstances.\n\n“I sometimes have sick fancies,” she went on, “and I have a sick fancy that I want to see some play. There, there!” with an impatient movement of the fingers of her right hand; “play, play, play!”\n\nFor a moment, with the fear of my sister’s working me before my eyes, I had a desperate idea of starting round the room in the assumed character of Mr. Pumblechook’s chaise-cart. But I felt myself so unequal to the performance that I gave it up, and stood looking at Miss Havisham in what I suppose she took for a dogged manner, inasmuch as she said, when we had taken a good look at each other,—\n\n“Are you sullen and obstinate?”\n\n“No, ma’am, I am very sorry for you, and very sorry I can’t play just now. If you complain of me I shall get into trouble with my sister, so I would do it if I could; but it’s so new here, and so strange, and so fine,—and melancholy—.” I stopped, fearing I might say too much, or had already said it, and we took another look at each other.\n\nBefore she spoke again, she turned her eyes from me, and looked at the dress she wore, and at the dressing-table, and finally at herself in the looking-glass.\n\n“So new to him,” she muttered, “so old to me; so strange to him, so familiar to me; so melancholy to both of us! Call Estella.”\n\nAs she was still looking at the reflection of herself, I thought she was still talking to herself, and kept quiet.\n\n“Call Estella,” she repeated, flashing a look at me. “You can do that. Call Estella. At the door.”\n\nTo stand in the dark in a mysterious passage of an unknown house, bawling Estella to a scornful young lady neither visible nor responsive, and feeling it a dreadful liberty so to roar out her name, was almost as bad as playing to order. But she answered at last, and her light came along the dark passage like a star.\n\nMiss Havisham beckoned her to come close, and took up a jewel from the table, and tried its effect upon her fair young bosom and against her pretty brown hair. “Your own, one day, my dear, and you will use it well. Let me see you play cards with this boy.”\n\n“With this boy? Why, he is a common labouring-boy!”\n\nI thought I overheard Miss Havisham answer,—only it seemed so unlikely,—“Well? You can break his heart.”\n\n“What do you play, boy?” asked Estella of myself, with the greatest disdain.\n\n“Nothing but beggar my neighbour, miss.”\n\n“Beggar him,” said Miss Havisham to Estella. So we sat down to cards.\n\nIt was then I began to understand that everything in the room had stopped, like the watch and the clock, a long time ago. I noticed that Miss Havisham put down the jewel exactly on the spot from which she had taken it up. As Estella dealt the cards, I glanced at the dressing-table again, and saw that the shoe upon it, once white, now yellow, had never been worn. I glanced down at the foot from which the shoe was absent, and saw that the silk stocking on it, once white, now yellow, had been trodden ragged. Without this arrest of everything, this standing still of all the pale decayed objects, not even the withered bridal dress on the collapsed form could have looked so like grave-clothes, or the long veil so like a shroud.\n\nSo she sat, corpse-like, as we played at cards; the frillings and trimmings on her bridal dress, looking like earthy paper. I knew nothing then of the discoveries that are occasionally made of bodies buried in ancient times, which fall to powder in the moment of being distinctly seen; but, I have often thought since, that she must have looked as if the admission of the natural light of day would have struck her to dust.\n\n“He calls the knaves Jacks, this boy!” said Estella with disdain, before our first game was out. “And what coarse hands he has! And what thick boots!”\n\nI had never thought of being ashamed of my hands before; but I began to consider them a very indifferent pair. Her contempt for me was so strong, that it became infectious, and I caught it.\n\nShe won the game, and I dealt. I misdealt, as was only natural, when I knew she was lying in wait for me to do wrong; and she denounced me for a stupid, clumsy labouring-boy.\n\n“You say nothing of her,” remarked Miss Havisham to me, as she looked on. “She says many hard things of you, but you say nothing of her. What do you think of her?”\n\n“I don’t like to say,” I stammered.\n\n“Tell me in my ear,” said Miss Havisham, bending down.\n\n“I think she is very proud,” I replied, in a whisper.\n\n“Anything else?”\n\n“I think she is very pretty.”\n\n“Anything else?”\n\n“I think she is very insulting.” (She was looking at me then with a look of supreme aversion.)\n\n“Anything else?”\n\n“I think I should like to go home.”\n\n“And never see her again, though she is so pretty?”\n\n“I am not sure that I shouldn’t like to see her again, but I should like to go home now.”\n\n“You shall go soon,” said Miss Havisham, aloud. “Play the game out.”\n\nSaving for the one weird smile at first, I should have felt almost sure that Miss Havisham’s face could not smile. It had dropped into a watchful and brooding expression,—most likely when all the things about her had become transfixed,—and it looked as if nothing could ever lift it up again. Her chest had dropped, so that she stooped; and her voice had dropped, so that she spoke low, and with a dead lull upon her; altogether, she had the appearance of having dropped body and soul, within and without, under the weight of a crushing blow.\n\nI played the game to an end with Estella, and she beggared me. She threw the cards down on the table when she had won them all, as if she despised them for having been won of me.\n\n“When shall I have you here again?” said Miss Havisham. “Let me think.”\n\nI was beginning to remind her that to-day was Wednesday, when she checked me with her former impatient movement of the fingers of her right hand.\n\n“There, there! I know nothing of days of the week; I know nothing of weeks of the year. Come again after six days. You hear?”\n\n“Yes, ma’am.”\n\n“Estella, take him down. Let him have something to eat, and let him roam and look about him while he eats. Go, Pip.”\n\nI followed the candle down, as I had followed the candle up, and she stood it in the place where we had found it. Until she opened the side entrance, I had fancied, without thinking about it, that it must necessarily be night-time. The rush of the daylight quite confounded me, and made me feel as if I had been in the candlelight of the strange room many hours.\n\n“You are to wait here, you boy,” said Estella; and disappeared and closed the door.\n\nI took the opportunity of being alone in the courtyard to look at my coarse hands and my common boots. My opinion of those accessories was not favourable. They had never troubled me before, but they troubled me now, as vulgar appendages. I determined to ask Joe why he had ever taught me to call those picture-cards Jacks, which ought to be called knaves. I wished Joe had been rather more genteelly brought up, and then I should have been so too.\n\nShe came back, with some bread and meat and a little mug of beer. She put the mug down on the stones of the yard, and gave me the bread and meat without looking at me, as insolently as if I were a dog in disgrace. I was so humiliated, hurt, spurned, offended, angry, sorry,—I cannot hit upon the right name for the smart—God knows what its name was,—that tears started to my eyes. The moment they sprang there, the girl looked at me with a quick delight in having been the cause of them. This gave me power to keep them back and to look at her: so, she gave a contemptuous toss—but with a sense, I thought, of having made too sure that I was so wounded—and left me.\n\nBut when she was gone, I looked about me for a place to hide my face in, and got behind one of the gates in the brewery-lane, and leaned my sleeve against the wall there, and leaned my forehead on it and cried. As I cried, I kicked the wall, and took a hard twist at my hair; so bitter were my feelings, and so sharp was the smart without a name, that needed counteraction.\n\nMy sister’s bringing up had made me sensitive. In the little world in which children have their existence whosoever brings them up, there is nothing so finely perceived and so finely felt as injustice. It may be only small injustice that the child can be exposed to; but the child is small, and its world is small, and its rocking-horse stands as many hands high, according to scale, as a big-boned Irish hunter. Within myself, I had sustained, from my babyhood, a perpetual conflict with injustice. I had known, from the time when I could speak, that my sister, in her capricious and violent coercion, was unjust to me. I had cherished a profound conviction that her bringing me up by hand gave her no right to bring me up by jerks. Through all my punishments, disgraces, fasts, and vigils, and other penitential performances, I had nursed this assurance; and to my communing so much with it, in a solitary and unprotected way, I in great part refer the fact that I was morally timid and very sensitive.\n\nI got rid of my injured feelings for the time by kicking them into the brewery wall, and twisting them out of my hair, and then I smoothed my face with my sleeve, and came from behind the gate. The bread and meat were acceptable, and the beer was warming and tingling, and I was soon in spirits to look about me.\n\nTo be sure, it was a deserted place, down to the pigeon-house in the brewery-yard, which had been blown crooked on its pole by some high wind, and would have made the pigeons think themselves at sea, if there had been any pigeons there to be rocked by it. But there were no pigeons in the dove-cot, no horses in the stable, no pigs in the sty, no malt in the storehouse, no smells of grains and beer in the copper or the vat. All the uses and scents of the brewery might have evaporated with its last reek of smoke. In a by-yard, there was a wilderness of empty casks, which had a certain sour remembrance of better days lingering about them; but it was too sour to be accepted as a sample of the beer that was gone,—and in this respect I remember those recluses as being like most others.\n\nBehind the furthest end of the brewery, was a rank garden with an old wall; not so high but that I could struggle up and hold on long enough to look over it, and see that the rank garden was the garden of the house, and that it was overgrown with tangled weeds, but that there was a track upon the green and yellow paths, as if some one sometimes walked there, and that Estella was walking away from me even then. But she seemed to be everywhere. For when I yielded to the temptation presented by the casks, and began to walk on them, I saw her walking on them at the end of the yard of casks. She had her back towards me, and held her pretty brown hair spread out in her two hands, and never looked round, and passed out of my view directly. So, in the brewery itself,—by which I mean the large paved lofty place in which they used to make the beer, and where the brewing utensils still were. When I first went into it, and, rather oppressed by its gloom, stood near the door looking about me, I saw her pass among the extinguished fires, and ascend some light iron stairs, and go out by a gallery high overhead, as if she were going out into the sky.\n\nIt was in this place, and at this moment, that a strange thing happened to my fancy. I thought it a strange thing then, and I thought it a stranger thing long afterwards. I turned my eyes—a little dimmed by looking up at the frosty light—towards a great wooden beam in a low nook of the building near me on my right hand, and I saw a figure hanging there by the neck. A figure all in yellow white, with but one shoe to the feet; and it hung so, that I could see that the faded trimmings of the dress were like earthy paper, and that the face was Miss Havisham’s, with a movement going over the whole countenance as if she were trying to call to me. In the terror of seeing the figure, and in the terror of being certain that it had not been there a moment before, I at first ran from it, and then ran towards it. And my terror was greatest of all when I found no figure there.\n\nNothing less than the frosty light of the cheerful sky, the sight of people passing beyond the bars of the court-yard gate, and the reviving influence of the rest of the bread and meat and beer, would have brought me round. Even with those aids, I might not have come to myself as soon as I did, but that I saw Estella approaching with the keys, to let me out. She would have some fair reason for looking down upon me, I thought, if she saw me frightened; and she would have no fair reason.\n\nShe gave me a triumphant glance in passing me, as if she rejoiced that my hands were so coarse and my boots were so thick, and she opened the gate, and stood holding it. I was passing out without looking at her, when she touched me with a taunting hand.\n\n“Why don’t you cry?”\n\n“Because I don’t want to.”\n\n“You do,” said she. “You have been crying till you are half blind, and you are near crying again now.”\n\nShe laughed contemptuously, pushed me out, and locked the gate upon me. I went straight to Mr. Pumblechook’s, and was immensely relieved to find him not at home. So, leaving word with the shopman on what day I was wanted at Miss Havisham’s again, I set off on the four-mile walk to our forge; pondering, as I went along, on all I had seen, and deeply revolving that I was a common labouring-boy; that my hands were coarse; that my boots were thick; that I had fallen into a despicable habit of calling knaves Jacks; that I was much more ignorant than I had considered myself last night, and generally that I was in a low-lived bad way.\n\n# Chapter IX.\n\nWhen I reached home, my sister was very curious to know all about Miss Havisham’s, and asked a number of questions. And I soon found myself getting heavily bumped from behind in the nape of the neck and the small of the back, and having my face ignominiously shoved against the kitchen wall, because I did not answer those questions at sufficient length.\n\nIf a dread of not being understood be hidden in the breasts of other young people to anything like the extent to which it used to be hidden in mine,—which I consider probable, as I have no particular reason to suspect myself of having been a monstrosity,—it is the key to many reservations. I felt convinced that if I described Miss Havisham’s as my eyes had seen it, I should not be understood. Not only that, but I felt convinced that Miss Havisham too would not be understood; and although she was perfectly incomprehensible to me, I entertained an impression that there would be something coarse and treacherous in my dragging her as she really was (to say nothing of Miss Estella) before the contemplation of Mrs. Joe. Consequently, I said as little as I could, and had my face shoved against the kitchen wall.\n\nThe worst of it was that that bullying old Pumblechook, preyed upon by a devouring curiosity to be informed of all I had seen and heard, came gaping over in his chaise-cart at tea-time, to have the details divulged to him. And the mere sight of the torment, with his fishy eyes and mouth open, his sandy hair inquisitively on end, and his waistcoat heaving with windy arithmetic, made me vicious in my reticence.\n\n“Well, boy,” Uncle Pumblechook began, as soon as he was seated in the chair of honour by the fire. “How did you get on up town?”\n\nI answered, “Pretty well, sir,” and my sister shook her fist at me.\n\n“Pretty well?” Mr. Pumblechook repeated. “Pretty well is no answer. Tell us what you mean by pretty well, boy?”\n\nWhitewash on the forehead hardens the brain into a state of obstinacy perhaps. Anyhow, with whitewash from the wall on my forehead, my obstinacy was adamantine. I reflected for some time, and then answered as if I had discovered a new idea, “I mean pretty well.”\n\nMy sister with an exclamation of impatience was going to fly at me,—I had no shadow of defence, for Joe was busy in the forge,—when Mr. Pumblechook interposed with “No! Don’t lose your temper. Leave this lad to me, ma’am; leave this lad to me.” Mr. Pumblechook then turned me towards him, as if he were going to cut my hair, and said,—\n\n“First (to get our thoughts in order): Forty-three pence?”\n\nI calculated the consequences of replying “Four Hundred Pound,” and finding them against me, went as near the answer as I could—which was somewhere about eightpence off. Mr. Pumblechook then put me through my pence-table from “twelve pence make one shilling,” up to “forty pence make three and fourpence,” and then triumphantly demanded, as if he had done for me, “Now! How much is forty-three pence?” To which I replied, after a long interval of reflection, “I don’t know.” And I was so aggravated that I almost doubt if I did know.\n\nMr. Pumblechook worked his head like a screw to screw it out of me, and said, “Is forty-three pence seven and sixpence three fardens, for instance?”\n\n“Yes!” said I. And although my sister instantly boxed my ears, it was highly gratifying to me to see that the answer spoilt his joke, and brought him to a dead stop.\n\n“Boy! What like is Miss Havisham?” Mr. Pumblechook began again when he had recovered; folding his arms tight on his chest and applying the screw.\n\n“Very tall and dark,” I told him.\n\n“Is she, uncle?” asked my sister.\n\nMr. Pumblechook winked assent; from which I at once inferred that he had never seen Miss Havisham, for she was nothing of the kind.\n\n“Good!” said Mr. Pumblechook conceitedly. (“This is the way to have him! We are beginning to hold our own, I think, Mum?”)\n\n“I am sure, uncle,” returned Mrs. Joe, “I wish you had him always; you know so well how to deal with him.”\n\n“Now, boy! What was she a-doing of, when you went in today?” asked Mr. Pumblechook.\n\n“She was sitting,” I answered, “in a black velvet coach.”\n\nMr. Pumblechook and Mrs. Joe stared at one another—as they well might—and both repeated, “In a black velvet coach?”\n\n“Yes,” said I. “And Miss Estella—that’s her niece, I think—handed her in cake and wine at the coach-window, on a gold plate. And we all had cake and wine on gold plates. And I got up behind the coach to eat mine, because she told me to.”\n\n“Was anybody else there?” asked Mr. Pumblechook.\n\n“Four dogs,” said I.\n\n“Large or small?”\n\n“Immense,” said I. “And they fought for veal-cutlets out of a silver basket.”\n\nMr. Pumblechook and Mrs. Joe stared at one another again, in utter amazement. I was perfectly frantic,—a reckless witness under the torture,—and would have told them anything.\n\n“Where was this coach, in the name of gracious?” asked my sister.\n\n“In Miss Havisham’s room.” They stared again. “But there weren’t any horses to it.” I added this saving clause, in the moment of rejecting four richly caparisoned coursers which I had had wild thoughts of harnessing.\n\n“Can this be possible, uncle?” asked Mrs. Joe. “What can the boy mean?”\n\n“I’ll tell you, Mum,” said Mr. Pumblechook. “My opinion is, it’s a sedan-chair. She’s flighty, you know,—very flighty,—quite flighty enough to pass her days in a sedan-chair.”\n\n“Did you ever see her in it, uncle?” asked Mrs. Joe.\n\n“How could I,” he returned, forced to the admission, “when I never see her in my life? Never clapped eyes upon her!”\n\n“Goodness, uncle! And yet you have spoken to her?”\n\n“Why, don’t you know,” said Mr. Pumblechook, testily, “that when I have been there, I have been took up to the outside of her door, and the door has stood ajar, and she has spoke to me that way. Don’t say you don’t know that, Mum. Howsever, the boy went there to play. What did you play at, boy?”\n\n“We played with flags,” I said. (I beg to observe that I think of myself with amazement, when I recall the lies I told on this occasion.)\n\n“Flags!” echoed my sister.\n\n“Yes,” said I. “Estella waved a blue flag, and I waved a red one, and Miss Havisham waved one sprinkled all over with little gold stars, out at the coach-window. And then we all waved our swords and hurrahed.”\n\n“Swords!” repeated my sister. “Where did you get swords from?”\n\n“Out of a cupboard,” said I. “And I saw pistols in it,—and jam,—and pills. And there was no daylight in the room, but it was all lighted up with candles.”\n\n“That’s true, Mum,” said Mr. Pumblechook, with a grave nod. “That’s the state of the case, for that much I’ve seen myself.” And then they both stared at me, and I, with an obtrusive show of artlessness on my countenance, stared at them, and plaited the right leg of my trousers with my right hand.\n\nIf they had asked me any more questions, I should undoubtedly have betrayed myself, for I was even then on the point of mentioning that there was a balloon in the yard, and should have hazarded the statement but for my invention being divided between that phenomenon and a bear in the brewery. They were so much occupied, however, in discussing the marvels I had already presented for their consideration, that I escaped. The subject still held them when Joe came in from his work to have a cup of tea. To whom my sister, more for the relief of her own mind than for the gratification of his, related my pretended experiences.\n\nNow, when I saw Joe open his blue eyes and roll them all round the kitchen in helpless amazement, I was overtaken by penitence; but only as regarded him,—not in the least as regarded the other two. Towards Joe, and Joe only, I considered myself a young monster, while they sat debating what results would come to me from Miss Havisham’s acquaintance and favour. They had no doubt that Miss Havisham would “do something” for me; their doubts related to the form that something would take. My sister stood out for “property.” Mr. Pumblechook was in favour of a handsome premium for binding me apprentice to some genteel trade,—say, the corn and seed trade, for instance. Joe fell into the deepest disgrace with both, for offering the bright suggestion that I might only be presented with one of the dogs who had fought for the veal-cutlets. “If a fool’s head can’t express better opinions than that,” said my sister, “and you have got any work to do, you had better go and do it.” So he went.\n\nAfter Mr. Pumblechook had driven off, and when my sister was washing up, I stole into the forge to Joe, and remained by him until he had done for the night. Then I said, “Before the fire goes out, Joe, I should like to tell you something.”\n\n“Should you, Pip?” said Joe, drawing his shoeing-stool near the forge. “Then tell us. What is it, Pip?”\n\n“Joe,” said I, taking hold of his rolled-up shirt sleeve, and twisting it between my finger and thumb, “you remember all that about Miss Havisham’s?”\n\n“Remember?” said Joe. “I believe you! Wonderful!”\n\n“It’s a terrible thing, Joe; it ain’t true.”\n\n“What are you telling of, Pip?” cried Joe, falling back in the greatest amazement. “You don’t mean to say it’s—”\n\n“Yes I do; it’s lies, Joe.”\n\n“But not all of it? Why sure you don’t mean to say, Pip, that there was no black welwet co—eh?” For, I stood shaking my head. “But at least there was dogs, Pip? Come, Pip,” said Joe, persuasively, “if there warn’t no weal-cutlets, at least there was dogs?”\n\n“No, Joe.”\n“A dog?” said Joe. “A puppy? Come?”\n\n“No, Joe, there was nothing at all of the kind.”\n\nAs I fixed my eyes hopelessly on Joe, Joe contemplated me in dismay. “Pip, old chap! This won’t do, old fellow! I say! Where do you expect to go to?”\n\n“It’s terrible, Joe; ain’t it?”\n\n“Terrible?” cried Joe. “Awful! What possessed you?”\n\n“I don’t know what possessed me, Joe,” I replied, letting his shirt sleeve go, and sitting down in the ashes at his feet, hanging my head; “but I wish you hadn’t taught me to call Knaves at cards Jacks; and I wish my boots weren’t so thick nor my hands so coarse.”\n\nAnd then I told Joe that I felt very miserable, and that I hadn’t been able to explain myself to Mrs. Joe and Pumblechook, who were so rude to me, and that there had been a beautiful young lady at Miss Havisham’s who was dreadfully proud, and that she had said I was common, and that I knew I was common, and that I wished I was not common, and that the lies had come of it somehow, though I didn’t know how.\n\nThis was a case of metaphysics, at least as difficult for Joe to deal with as for me. But Joe took the case altogether out of the region of metaphysics, and by that means vanquished it.\n\n“There’s one thing you may be sure of, Pip,” said Joe, after some rumination, “namely, that lies is lies. Howsever they come, they didn’t ought to come, and they come from the father of lies, and work round to the same. Don’t you tell no more of ’em, Pip. That ain’t the way to get out of being common, old chap. And as to being common, I don’t make it out at all clear. You are oncommon in some things. You’re oncommon small. Likewise you’re a oncommon scholar.”\n\n“No, I am ignorant and backward, Joe.”\n\n“Why, see what a letter you wrote last night! Wrote in print even! I’ve seen letters—Ah! and from gentlefolks!—that I’ll swear weren’t wrote in print,” said Joe.\n\n“I have learnt next to nothing, Joe. You think much of me. It’s only that.”\n\n“Well, Pip,” said Joe, “be it so or be it son’t, you must be a common scholar afore you can be a oncommon one, I should hope! The king upon his throne, with his crown upon his ed, can’t sit and write his acts of Parliament in print, without having begun, when he were a unpromoted Prince, with the alphabet.—Ah!” added Joe, with a shake of the head that was full of meaning, “and begun at A too, and worked his way to Z. And I know what that is to do, though I can’t say I’ve exactly done it.”\n\nThere was some hope in this piece of wisdom, and it rather encouraged me.\n\n“Whether common ones as to callings and earnings,” pursued Joe, reflectively, “mightn’t be the better of continuing for to keep company with common ones, instead of going out to play with oncommon ones,—which reminds me to hope that there were a flag, perhaps?”\n\n“No, Joe.”\n\n“(I’m sorry there weren’t a flag, Pip). Whether that might be or mightn’t be, is a thing as can’t be looked into now, without putting your sister on the Rampage; and that’s a thing not to be thought of as being done intentional. Lookee here, Pip, at what is said to you by a true friend. Which this to you the true friend say. If you can’t get to be oncommon through going straight, you’ll never get to do it through going crooked. So don’t tell no more on ’em, Pip, and live well and die happy.”\n\n“You are not angry with me, Joe?”\n\n“No, old chap. But bearing in mind that them were which I meantersay of a stunning and outdacious sort,—alluding to them which bordered on weal-cutlets and dog-fighting,—a sincere well-wisher would adwise, Pip, their being dropped into your meditations, when you go upstairs to bed. That’s all, old chap, and don’t never do it no more.”\n\nWhen I got up to my little room and said my prayers, I did not forget Joe’s recommendation, and yet my young mind was in that disturbed and unthankful state, that I thought long after I laid me down, how common Estella would consider Joe, a mere blacksmith; how thick his boots, and how coarse his hands. I thought how Joe and my sister were then sitting in the kitchen, and how I had come up to bed from the kitchen, and how Miss Havisham and Estella never sat in a kitchen, but were far above the level of such common doings. I fell asleep recalling what I “used to do” when I was at Miss Havisham’s; as though I had been there weeks or months, instead of hours; and as though it were quite an old subject of remembrance, instead of one that had arisen only that day.\n\nThat was a memorable day to me, for it made great changes in me. But it is the same with any life. Imagine one selected day struck out of it, and think how different its course would have been. Pause you who read this, and think for a moment of the long chain of iron or gold, of thorns or flowers, that would never have bound you, but for the formation of the first link on one memorable day.\n\n# Chapter X.\n\nThe felicitous idea occurred to me a morning or two later when I woke, that the best step I could take towards making myself uncommon was to get out of Biddy everything she knew. In pursuance of this luminous conception I mentioned to Biddy when I went to Mr. Wopsle’s great-aunt’s at night, that I had a particular reason for wishing to get on in life, and that I should feel very much obliged to her if she would impart all her learning to me. Biddy, who was the most obliging of girls, immediately said she would, and indeed began to carry out her promise within five minutes.\n\nThe Educational scheme or Course established by Mr. Wopsle’s great-aunt may be resolved into the following synopsis. The pupils ate apples and put straws down one another’s backs, until Mr. Wopsle’s great-aunt collected her energies, and made an indiscriminate totter at them with a birch-rod. After receiving the charge with every mark of derision, the pupils formed in line and buzzingly passed a ragged book from hand to hand. The book had an alphabet in it, some figures and tables, and a little spelling,—that is to say, it had had once. As soon as this volume began to circulate, Mr. Wopsle’s great-aunt fell into a state of coma, arising either from sleep or a rheumatic paroxysm. The pupils then entered among themselves upon a competitive examination on the subject of Boots, with the view of ascertaining who could tread the hardest upon whose toes. This mental exercise lasted until Biddy made a rush at them and distributed three defaced Bibles (shaped as if they had been unskilfully cut off the chump end of something), more illegibly printed at the best than any curiosities of literature I have since met with, speckled all over with ironmould, and having various specimens of the insect world smashed between their leaves. This part of the Course was usually lightened by several single combats between Biddy and refractory students. When the fights were over, Biddy gave out the number of a page, and then we all read aloud what we could,—or what we couldn’t—in a frightful chorus; Biddy leading with a high, shrill, monotonous voice, and none of us having the least notion of, or reverence for, what we were reading about. When this horrible din had lasted a certain time, it mechanically awoke Mr. Wopsle’s great-aunt, who staggered at a boy fortuitously, and pulled his ears. This was understood to terminate the Course for the evening, and we emerged into the air with shrieks of intellectual victory. It is fair to remark that there was no prohibition against any pupil’s entertaining himself with a slate or even with the ink (when there was any), but that it was not easy to pursue that branch of study in the winter season, on account of the little general shop in which the classes were holden—and which was also Mr. Wopsle’s great-aunt’s sitting-room and bedchamber—being but faintly illuminated through the agency of one low-spirited dip-candle and no snuffers.\n\nIt appeared to me that it would take time to become uncommon, under these circumstances: nevertheless, I resolved to try it, and that very evening Biddy entered on our special agreement, by imparting some information from her little catalogue of Prices, under the head of moist sugar, and lending me, to copy at home, a large old English D which she had imitated from the heading of some newspaper, and which I supposed, until she told me what it was, to be a design for a buckle.\n\nOf course there was a public-house in the village, and of course Joe liked sometimes to smoke his pipe there. I had received strict orders from my sister to call for him at the Three Jolly Bargemen, that evening, on my way from school, and bring him home at my peril. To the Three Jolly Bargemen, therefore, I directed my steps.\n\nThere was a bar at the Jolly Bargemen, with some alarmingly long chalk scores in it on the wall at the side of the door, which seemed to me to be never paid off. They had been there ever since I could remember, and had grown more than I had. But there was a quantity of chalk about our country, and perhaps the people neglected no opportunity of turning it to account.\n\nIt being Saturday night, I found the landlord looking rather grimly at these records; but as my business was with Joe and not with him, I merely wished him good evening, and passed into the common room at the end of the passage, where there was a bright large kitchen fire, and where Joe was smoking his pipe in company with Mr. Wopsle and a stranger. Joe greeted me as usual with “Halloa, Pip, old chap!” and the moment he said that, the stranger turned his head and looked at me.\n\nHe was a secret-looking man whom I had never seen before. His head was all on one side, and one of his eyes was half shut up, as if he were taking aim at something with an invisible gun. He had a pipe in his mouth, and he took it out, and, after slowly blowing all his smoke away and looking hard at me all the time, nodded. So, I nodded, and then he nodded again, and made room on the settle beside him that I might sit down there.\n\nBut as I was used to sit beside Joe whenever I entered that place of resort, I said “No, thank you, sir,” and fell into the space Joe made for me on the opposite settle. The strange man, after glancing at Joe, and seeing that his attention was otherwise engaged, nodded to me again when I had taken my seat, and then rubbed his leg—in a very odd way, as it struck me.\n\n“You was saying,” said the strange man, turning to Joe, “that you was a blacksmith.”\n\n“Yes. I said it, you know,” said Joe.\n\n“What’ll you drink, Mr.—? You didn’t mention your name, by the bye.”\n\nJoe mentioned it now, and the strange man called him by it. “What’ll you drink, Mr. Gargery? At my expense? To top up with?”\n\n“Well,” said Joe, “to tell you the truth, I ain’t much in the habit of drinking at anybody’s expense but my own.”\n\n“Habit? No,” returned the stranger, “but once and away, and on a Saturday night too. Come! Put a name to it, Mr. Gargery.”\n\n“I wouldn’t wish to be stiff company,” said Joe. “Rum.”\n\n“Rum,” repeated the stranger. “And will the other gentleman originate a sentiment.”\n\n“Rum,” said Mr. Wopsle.\n\n“Three Rums!” cried the stranger, calling to the landlord. “Glasses round!”\n\n“This other gentleman,” observed Joe, by way of introducing Mr. Wopsle, “is a gentleman that you would like to hear give it out. Our clerk at church.”\n\n“Aha!” said the stranger, quickly, and cocking his eye at me. “The lonely church, right out on the marshes, with graves round it!”\n\n“That’s it,” said Joe.\n\nThe stranger, with a comfortable kind of grunt over his pipe, put his legs up on the settle that he had to himself. He wore a flapping broad-brimmed traveller’s hat, and under it a handkerchief tied over his head in the manner of a cap: so that he showed no hair. As he looked at the fire, I thought I saw a cunning expression, followed by a half-laugh, come into his face.\n\n“I am not acquainted with this country, gentlemen, but it seems a solitary country towards the river.”\n\n“Most marshes is solitary,” said Joe.\n\n“No doubt, no doubt. Do you find any gypsies, now, or tramps, or vagrants of any sort, out there?”\n\n“No,” said Joe; “none but a runaway convict now and then. And we don’t find them, easy. Eh, Mr. Wopsle?”\n\nMr. Wopsle, with a majestic remembrance of old discomfiture, assented; but not warmly.\n\n“Seems you have been out after such?” asked the stranger.\n\n“Once,” returned Joe. “Not that we wanted to take them, you understand; we went out as lookers on; me, and Mr. Wopsle, and Pip. Didn’t us, Pip?”\n\n“Yes, Joe.”\n\nThe stranger looked at me again,—still cocking his eye, as if he were expressly taking aim at me with his invisible gun,—and said, “He’s a likely young parcel of bones that. What is it you call him?”\n\n“Pip,” said Joe.\n\n“Christened Pip?”\n\n“No, not christened Pip.”\n\n“Surname Pip?”\n\n“No,” said Joe, “it’s a kind of family name what he gave himself when a infant, and is called by.”\n\n“Son of yours?”\n\n“Well,” said Joe, meditatively, not, of course, that it could be in anywise necessary to consider about it, but because it was the way at the Jolly Bargemen to seem to consider deeply about everything that was discussed over pipes,—“well—no. No, he ain’t.”\n\n“Nevvy?” said the strange man.\n\n“Well,” said Joe, with the same appearance of profound cogitation, “he is not—no, not to deceive you, he is not—my nevvy.”\n\n“What the Blue Blazes is he?” asked the stranger. Which appeared to me to be an inquiry of unnecessary strength.\n\nMr. Wopsle struck in upon that; as one who knew all about relationships, having professional occasion to bear in mind what female relations a man might not marry; and expounded the ties between me and Joe. Having his hand in, Mr. Wopsle finished off with a most terrifically snarling passage from Richard the Third, and seemed to think he had done quite enough to account for it when he added, “—as the poet says.”\n\nAnd here I may remark that when Mr. Wopsle referred to me, he considered it a necessary part of such reference to rumple my hair and poke it into my eyes. I cannot conceive why everybody of his standing who visited at our house should always have put me through the same inflammatory process under similar circumstances. Yet I do not call to mind that I was ever in my earlier youth the subject of remark in our social family circle, but some large-handed person took some such ophthalmic steps to patronise me.\n\nAll this while, the strange man looked at nobody but me, and looked at me as if he were determined to have a shot at me at last, and bring me down. But he said nothing after offering his Blue Blazes observation, until the glasses of rum and water were brought; and then he made his shot, and a most extraordinary shot it was.\n\nIt was not a verbal remark, but a proceeding in dumb-show, and was pointedly addressed to me. He stirred his rum and water pointedly at me, and he tasted his rum and water pointedly at me. And he stirred it and he tasted it; not with a spoon that was brought to him, but with a file.\n\nHe did this so that nobody but I saw the file; and when he had done it he wiped the file and put it in a breast-pocket. I knew it to be Joe’s file, and I knew that he knew my convict, the moment I saw the instrument. I sat gazing at him, spell-bound. But he now reclined on his settle, taking very little notice of me, and talking principally about turnips.\n\nThere was a delicious sense of cleaning-up and making a quiet pause before going on in life afresh, in our village on Saturday nights, which stimulated Joe to dare to stay out half an hour longer on Saturdays than at other times. The half-hour and the rum and water running out together, Joe got up to go, and took me by the hand.\n\n“Stop half a moment, Mr. Gargery,” said the strange man. “I think I’ve got a bright new shilling somewhere in my pocket, and if I have, the boy shall have it.”\n\nHe looked it out from a handful of small change, folded it in some crumpled paper, and gave it to me. “Yours!” said he. “Mind! Your own.”\n\nI thanked him, staring at him far beyond the bounds of good manners, and holding tight to Joe. He gave Joe good-night, and he gave Mr. Wopsle good-night (who went out with us), and he gave me only a look with his aiming eye,—no, not a look, for he shut it up, but wonders may be done with an eye by hiding it.\n\nOn the way home, if I had been in a humour for talking, the talk must have been all on my side, for Mr. Wopsle parted from us at the door of the Jolly Bargemen, and Joe went all the way home with his mouth wide open, to rinse the rum out with as much air as possible. But I was in a manner stupefied by this turning up of my old misdeed and old acquaintance, and could think of nothing else.\n\nMy sister was not in a very bad temper when we presented ourselves in the kitchen, and Joe was encouraged by that unusual circumstance to tell her about the bright shilling. “A bad un, I’ll be bound,” said Mrs. Joe triumphantly, “or he wouldn’t have given it to the boy! Let’s look at it.”\n\nI took it out of the paper, and it proved to be a good one. “But what’s this?” said Mrs. Joe, throwing down the shilling and catching up the paper. “Two One-Pound notes?”\n\nNothing less than two fat sweltering one-pound notes that seemed to have been on terms of the warmest intimacy with all the cattle-markets in the county. Joe caught up his hat again, and ran with them to the Jolly Bargemen to restore them to their owner. While he was gone, I sat down on my usual stool and looked vacantly at my sister, feeling pretty sure that the man would not be there.\n\nPresently, Joe came back, saying that the man was gone, but that he, Joe, had left word at the Three Jolly Bargemen concerning the notes. Then my sister sealed them up in a piece of paper, and put them under some dried rose-leaves in an ornamental teapot on the top of a press in the state parlour. There they remained, a nightmare to me, many and many a night and day.\n\nI had sadly broken sleep when I got to bed, through thinking of the strange man taking aim at me with his invisible gun, and of the guiltily coarse and common thing it was, to be on secret terms of conspiracy with convicts,—a feature in my low career that I had previously forgotten. I was haunted by the file too. A dread possessed me that when I least expected it, the file would reappear. I coaxed myself to sleep by thinking of Miss Havisham’s, next Wednesday; and in my sleep I saw the file coming at me out of a door, without seeing who held it, and I screamed myself awake.\n\n# Chapter XI.\n\nAt the appointed time I returned to Miss Havisham’s, and my hesitating ring at the gate brought out Estella. She locked it after admitting me, as she had done before, and again preceded me into the dark passage where her candle stood. She took no notice of me until she had the candle in her hand, when she looked over her shoulder, superciliously saying, “You are to come this way to-day,” and took me to quite another part of the house.\n\nThe passage was a long one, and seemed to pervade the whole square basement of the Manor House. We traversed but one side of the square, however, and at the end of it she stopped, and put her candle down and opened a door. Here, the daylight reappeared, and I found myself in a small paved courtyard, the opposite side of which was formed by a detached dwelling-house, that looked as if it had once belonged to the manager or head clerk of the extinct brewery. There was a clock in the outer wall of this house. Like the clock in Miss Havisham’s room, and like Miss Havisham’s watch, it had stopped at twenty minutes to nine.\n\nWe went in at the door, which stood open, and into a gloomy room with a low ceiling, on the ground-floor at the back. There was some company in the room, and Estella said to me as she joined it, “You are to go and stand there boy, till you are wanted.” “There”, being the window, I crossed to it, and stood “there,” in a very uncomfortable state of mind, looking out.\n\nIt opened to the ground, and looked into a most miserable corner of the neglected garden, upon a rank ruin of cabbage-stalks, and one box-tree that had been clipped round long ago, like a pudding, and had a new growth at the top of it, out of shape and of a different colour, as if that part of the pudding had stuck to the saucepan and got burnt. This was my homely thought, as I contemplated the box-tree. There had been some light snow, overnight, and it lay nowhere else to my knowledge; but, it had not quite melted from the cold shadow of this bit of garden, and the wind caught it up in little eddies and threw it at the window, as if it pelted me for coming there.\n\nI divined that my coming had stopped conversation in the room, and that its other occupants were looking at me. I could see nothing of the room except the shining of the fire in the window-glass, but I stiffened in all my joints with the consciousness that I was under close inspection.\n\nThere were three ladies in the room and one gentleman. Before I had been standing at the window five minutes, they somehow conveyed to me that they were all toadies and humbugs, but that each of them pretended not to know that the others were toadies and humbugs: because the admission that he or she did know it, would have made him or her out to be a toady and humbug.\n\nThey all had a listless and dreary air of waiting somebody’s pleasure, and the most talkative of the ladies had to speak quite rigidly to repress a yawn. This lady, whose name was Camilla, very much reminded me of my sister, with the difference that she was older, and (as I found when I caught sight of her) of a blunter cast of features. Indeed, when I knew her better I began to think it was a Mercy she had any features at all, so very blank and high was the dead wall of her face.\n\n“Poor dear soul!” said this lady, with an abruptness of manner quite my sister’s. “Nobody’s enemy but his own!”\n\n“It would be much more commendable to be somebody else’s enemy,” said the gentleman; “far more natural.”\n\n“Cousin Raymond,” observed another lady, “we are to love our neighbour.”\n\n“Sarah Pocket,” returned Cousin Raymond, “if a man is not his own neighbour, who is?”\n\nMiss Pocket laughed, and Camilla laughed and said (checking a yawn), “The idea!” But I thought they seemed to think it rather a good idea too. The other lady, who had not spoken yet, said gravely and emphatically, “Very true!”\n\n“Poor soul!” Camilla presently went on (I knew they had all been looking at me in the mean time), “he is so very strange! Would anyone believe that when Tom’s wife died, he actually could not be induced to see the importance of the children’s having the deepest of trimmings to their mourning? ‘Good Lord!’ says he, ‘Camilla, what can it signify so long as the poor bereaved little things are in black?’ So like Matthew! The idea!”\n\n“Good points in him, good points in him,” said Cousin Raymond; “Heaven forbid I should deny good points in him; but he never had, and he never will have, any sense of the proprieties.”\n\n“You know I was obliged,” said Camilla,—“I was obliged to be firm. I said, ‘It WILL NOT DO, for the credit of the family.’ I told him that, without deep trimmings, the family was disgraced. I cried about it from breakfast till dinner. I injured my digestion. And at last he flung out in his violent way, and said, with a D, ‘Then do as you like.’ Thank Goodness it will always be a consolation to me to know that I instantly went out in a pouring rain and bought the things.”\n\n“He paid for them, did he not?” asked Estella.\n\n“It’s not the question, my dear child, who paid for them,” returned Camilla. “I bought them. And I shall often think of that with peace, when I wake up in the night.”\n\nThe ringing of a distant bell, combined with the echoing of some cry or call along the passage by which I had come, interrupted the conversation and caused Estella to say to me, “Now, boy!” On my turning round, they all looked at me with the utmost contempt, and, as I went out, I heard Sarah Pocket say, “Well I am sure! What next!” and Camilla add, with indignation, “Was there ever such a fancy! The i-de-a!”\n\nAs we were going with our candle along the dark passage, Estella stopped all of a sudden, and, facing round, said in her taunting manner, with her face quite close to mine,—\n\n“Well?”\n\n“Well, miss?” I answered, almost falling over her and checking myself.\n\nShe stood looking at me, and, of course, I stood looking at her.\n\n“Am I pretty?”\n\n“Yes; I think you are very pretty.”\n\n“Am I insulting?”\n\n“Not so much so as you were last time,” said I.\n\n“Not so much so?”\n\n“No.”\n\nShe fired when she asked the last question, and she slapped my face with such force as she had, when I answered it.\n\n“Now?” said she. “You little coarse monster, what do you think of me now?”\n\n“I shall not tell you.”\n\n“Because you are going to tell upstairs. Is that it?”\n\n“No,” said I, “that’s not it.”\n\n“Why don’t you cry again, you little wretch?”\n\n“Because I’ll never cry for you again,” said I. Which was, I suppose, as false a declaration as ever was made; for I was inwardly crying for her then, and I know what I know of the pain she cost me afterwards.\n\nWe went on our way upstairs after this episode; and, as we were going up, we met a gentleman groping his way down.\n\n“Whom have we here?” asked the gentleman, stopping and looking at me.\n\n“A boy,” said Estella.\n\nHe was a burly man of an exceedingly dark complexion, with an exceedingly large head, and a corresponding large hand. He took my chin in his large hand and turned up my face to have a look at me by the light of the candle. He was prematurely bald on the top of his head, and had bushy black eyebrows that wouldn’t lie down but stood up bristling. His eyes were set very deep in his head, and were disagreeably sharp and suspicious. He had a large watch-chain, and strong black dots where his beard and whiskers would have been if he had let them. He was nothing to me, and I could have had no foresight then, that he ever would be anything to me, but it happened that I had this opportunity of observing him well.\n\n“Boy of the neighbourhood? Hey?” said he.\n\n“Yes, sir,” said I.\n\n“How do you come here?”\n\n“Miss Havisham sent for me, sir,” I explained.\n\n“Well! Behave yourself. I have a pretty large experience of boys, and you’re a bad set of fellows. Now mind!” said he, biting the side of his great forefinger as he frowned at me, “you behave yourself!”\n\nWith those words, he released me—which I was glad of, for his hand smelt of scented soap—and went his way downstairs. I wondered whether he could be a doctor; but no, I thought; he couldn’t be a doctor, or he would have a quieter and more persuasive manner. There was not much time to consider the subject, for we were soon in Miss Havisham’s room, where she and everything else were just as I had left them. Estella left me standing near the door, and I stood there until Miss Havisham cast her eyes upon me from the dressing-table.\n\n“So!” she said, without being startled or surprised: “the days have worn away, have they?”\n\n“Yes, ma’am. To-day is—”\n\n“There, there, there!” with the impatient movement of her fingers. “I don’t want to know. Are you ready to play?”\n\nI was obliged to answer in some confusion, “I don’t think I am, ma’am.”\n\n“Not at cards again?” she demanded, with a searching look.\n\n“Yes, ma’am; I could do that, if I was wanted.”\n\n“Since this house strikes you old and grave, boy,” said Miss Havisham, impatiently, “and you are unwilling to play, are you willing to work?”\n\nI could answer this inquiry with a better heart than I had been able to find for the other question, and I said I was quite willing.\n\n“Then go into that opposite room,” said she, pointing at the door behind me with her withered hand, “and wait there till I come.”\n\nI crossed the staircase landing, and entered the room she indicated. From that room, too, the daylight was completely excluded, and it had an airless smell that was oppressive. A fire had been lately kindled in the damp old-fashioned grate, and it was more disposed to go out than to burn up, and the reluctant smoke which hung in the room seemed colder than the clearer air,—like our own marsh mist. Certain wintry branches of candles on the high chimney-piece faintly lighted the chamber; or it would be more expressive to say, faintly troubled its darkness. It was spacious, and I dare say had once been handsome, but every discernible thing in it was covered with dust and mould, and dropping to pieces. The most prominent object was a long table with a tablecloth spread on it, as if a feast had been in preparation when the house and the clocks all stopped together. An epergne or centre-piece of some kind was in the middle of this cloth; it was so heavily overhung with cobwebs that its form was quite undistinguishable; and, as I looked along the yellow expanse out of which I remember its seeming to grow, like a black fungus, I saw speckle-legged spiders with blotchy bodies running home to it, and running out from it, as if some circumstances of the greatest public importance had just transpired in the spider community.\n\nI heard the mice too, rattling behind the panels, as if the same occurrence were important to their interests. But the black beetles took no notice of the agitation, and groped about the hearth in a ponderous elderly way, as if they were short-sighted and hard of hearing, and not on terms with one another.\n\nThese crawling things had fascinated my attention, and I was watching them from a distance, when Miss Havisham laid a hand upon my shoulder. In her other hand she had a crutch-headed stick on which she leaned, and she looked like the Witch of the place.\n\n“This,” said she, pointing to the long table with her stick, “is where I will be laid when I am dead. They shall come and look at me here.”\n\nWith some vague misgiving that she might get upon the table then and there and die at once, the complete realisation of the ghastly waxwork at the Fair, I shrank under her touch.\n\n“What do you think that is?” she asked me, again pointing with her stick; “that, where those cobwebs are?”\n\n“I can’t guess what it is, ma’am.”\n\n“It’s a great cake. A bride-cake. Mine!”\n\nShe looked all round the room in a glaring manner, and then said, leaning on me while her hand twitched my shoulder, “Come, come, come! Walk me, walk me!”\n\nI made out from this, that the work I had to do, was to walk Miss Havisham round and round the room. Accordingly, I started at once, and she leaned upon my shoulder, and we went away at a pace that might have been an imitation (founded on my first impulse under that roof) of Mr. Pumblechook’s chaise-cart.\n\nShe was not physically strong, and after a little time said, “Slower!” Still, we went at an impatient fitful speed, and as we went, she twitched the hand upon my shoulder, and worked her mouth, and led me to believe that we were going fast because her thoughts went fast. After a while she said, “Call Estella!” so I went out on the landing and roared that name as I had done on the previous occasion. When her light appeared, I returned to Miss Havisham, and we started away again round and round the room.\n\nIf only Estella had come to be a spectator of our proceedings, I should have felt sufficiently discontented; but as she brought with her the three ladies and the gentleman whom I had seen below, I didn’t know what to do. In my politeness, I would have stopped; but Miss Havisham twitched my shoulder, and we posted on,—with a shame-faced consciousness on my part that they would think it was all my doing.\n\n“Dear Miss Havisham,” said Miss Sarah Pocket. “How well you look!”\n\n“I do not,” returned Miss Havisham. “I am yellow skin and bone.”\n\nCamilla brightened when Miss Pocket met with this rebuff; and she murmured, as she plaintively contemplated Miss Havisham, “Poor dear soul! Certainly not to be expected to look well, poor thing. The idea!”\n\n“And how are you?” said Miss Havisham to Camilla. As we were close to Camilla then, I would have stopped as a matter of course, only Miss Havisham wouldn’t stop. We swept on, and I felt that I was highly obnoxious to Camilla.\n\n“Thank you, Miss Havisham,” she returned, “I am as well as can be expected.”\n\n“Why, what’s the matter with you?” asked Miss Havisham, with exceeding sharpness.\n\n“Nothing worth mentioning,” replied Camilla. “I don’t wish to make a display of my feelings, but I have habitually thought of you more in the night than I am quite equal to.”\n\n“Then don’t think of me,” retorted Miss Havisham.\n\n“Very easily said!” remarked Camilla, amiably repressing a sob, while a hitch came into her upper lip, and her tears overflowed. “Raymond is a witness what ginger and sal volatile I am obliged to take in the night. Raymond is a witness what nervous jerkings I have in my legs. Chokings and nervous jerkings, however, are nothing new to me when I think with anxiety of those I love. If I could be less affectionate and sensitive, I should have a better digestion and an iron set of nerves. I am sure I wish it could be so. But as to not thinking of you in the night—The idea!” Here, a burst of tears.\n\nThe Raymond referred to, I understood to be the gentleman present, and him I understood to be Mr. Camilla. He came to the rescue at this point, and said in a consolatory and complimentary voice, “Camilla, my dear, it is well known that your family feelings are gradually undermining you to the extent of making one of your legs shorter than the other.”\n\n“I am not aware,” observed the grave lady whose voice I had heard but once, “that to think of any person is to make a great claim upon that person, my dear.”\n\nMiss Sarah Pocket, whom I now saw to be a little dry, brown, corrugated old woman, with a small face that might have been made of walnut-shells, and a large mouth like a cat’s without the whiskers, supported this position by saying, “No, indeed, my dear. Hem!”\n\n“Thinking is easy enough,” said the grave lady.\n\n“What is easier, you know?” assented Miss Sarah Pocket.\n\n“Oh, yes, yes!” cried Camilla, whose fermenting feelings appeared to rise from her legs to her bosom. “It’s all very true! It’s a weakness to be so affectionate, but I can’t help it. No doubt my health would be much better if it was otherwise, still I wouldn’t change my disposition if I could. It’s the cause of much suffering, but it’s a consolation to know I possess it, when I wake up in the night.” Here another burst of feeling.\n\nMiss Havisham and I had never stopped all this time, but kept going round and round the room; now brushing against the skirts of the visitors, now giving them the whole length of the dismal chamber.\n\n“There’s Matthew!” said Camilla. “Never mixing with any natural ties, never coming here to see how Miss Havisham is! I have taken to the sofa with my staylace cut, and have lain there hours insensible, with my head over the side, and my hair all down, and my feet I don’t know where—”\n\n(“Much higher than your head, my love,” said Mr. Camilla.)\n\n“I have gone off into that state, hours and hours, on account of Matthew’s strange and inexplicable conduct, and nobody has thanked me.”\n\n“Really I must say I should think not!” interposed the grave lady.\n\n“You see, my dear,” added Miss Sarah Pocket (a blandly vicious personage), “the question to put to yourself is, who did you expect to thank you, my love?”\n\n“Without expecting any thanks, or anything of the sort,” resumed Camilla, “I have remained in that state, hours and hours, and Raymond is a witness of the extent to which I have choked, and what the total inefficacy of ginger has been, and I have been heard at the piano-forte tuner’s across the street, where the poor mistaken children have even supposed it to be pigeons cooing at a distance,—and now to be told—” Here Camilla put her hand to her throat, and began to be quite chemical as to the formation of new combinations there.\n\nWhen this same Matthew was mentioned, Miss Havisham stopped me and herself, and stood looking at the speaker. This change had a great influence in bringing Camilla’s chemistry to a sudden end.\n\n“Matthew will come and see me at last,” said Miss Havisham, sternly, “when I am laid on that table. That will be his place,—there,” striking the table with her stick, “at my head! And yours will be there! And your husband’s there! And Sarah Pocket’s there! And Georgiana’s there! Now you all know where to take your stations when you come to feast upon me. And now go!”\n\nAt the mention of each name, she had struck the table with her stick in a new place. She now said, “Walk me, walk me!” and we went on again.\n\n“I suppose there’s nothing to be done,” exclaimed Camilla, “but comply and depart. It’s something to have seen the object of one’s love and duty for even so short a time. I shall think of it with a melancholy satisfaction when I wake up in the night. I wish Matthew could have that comfort, but he sets it at defiance. I am determined not to make a display of my feelings, but it’s very hard to be told one wants to feast on one’s relations,—as if one was a Giant,—and to be told to go. The bare idea!”\n\nMr. Camilla interposing, as Mrs. Camilla laid her hand upon her heaving bosom, that lady assumed an unnatural fortitude of manner which I supposed to be expressive of an intention to drop and choke when out of view, and kissing her hand to Miss Havisham, was escorted forth. Sarah Pocket and Georgiana contended who should remain last; but Sarah was too knowing to be outdone, and ambled round Georgiana with that artful slipperiness that the latter was obliged to take precedence. Sarah Pocket then made her separate effect of departing with, “Bless you, Miss Havisham dear!” and with a smile of forgiving pity on her walnut-shell countenance for the weaknesses of the rest.\n\nWhile Estella was away lighting them down, Miss Havisham still walked with her hand on my shoulder, but more and more slowly. At last she stopped before the fire, and said, after muttering and looking at it some seconds,—\n\n“This is my birthday, Pip.”\n\nI was going to wish her many happy returns, when she lifted her stick.\n\n“I don’t suffer it to be spoken of. I don’t suffer those who were here just now, or any one to speak of it. They come here on the day, but they dare not refer to it.”\n\nOf course I made no further effort to refer to it.\n\n“On this day of the year, long before you were born, this heap of decay,” stabbing with her crutched stick at the pile of cobwebs on the table, but not touching it, “was brought here. It and I have worn away together. The mice have gnawed at it, and sharper teeth than teeth of mice have gnawed at me.”\n\nShe held the head of her stick against her heart as she stood looking at the table; she in her once white dress, all yellow and withered; the once white cloth all yellow and withered; everything around in a state to crumble under a touch.\n\n“When the ruin is complete,” said she, with a ghastly look, “and when they lay me dead, in my bride’s dress on the bride’s table,—which shall be done, and which will be the finished curse upon him,—so much the better if it is done on this day!”\n\nShe stood looking at the table as if she stood looking at her own figure lying there. I remained quiet. Estella returned, and she too remained quiet. It seemed to me that we continued thus for a long time. In the heavy air of the room, and the heavy darkness that brooded in its remoter corners, I even had an alarming fancy that Estella and I might presently begin to decay.\n\nAt length, not coming out of her distraught state by degrees, but in an instant, Miss Havisham said, “Let me see you two play cards; why have you not begun?” With that, we returned to her room, and sat down as before; I was beggared, as before; and again, as before, Miss Havisham watched us all the time, directed my attention to Estella’s beauty, and made me notice it the more by trying her jewels on Estella’s breast and hair.\n\nEstella, for her part, likewise treated me as before, except that she did not condescend to speak. When we had played some half-dozen games, a day was appointed for my return, and I was taken down into the yard to be fed in the former dog-like manner. There, too, I was again left to wander about as I liked.\n\nIt is not much to the purpose whether a gate in that garden wall which I had scrambled up to peep over on the last occasion was, on that last occasion, open or shut. Enough that I saw no gate then, and that I saw one now. As it stood open, and as I knew that Estella had let the visitors out,—for she had returned with the keys in her hand,—I strolled into the garden, and strolled all over it. It was quite a wilderness, and there were old melon-frames and cucumber-frames in it, which seemed in their decline to have produced a spontaneous growth of weak attempts at pieces of old hats and boots, with now and then a weedy offshoot into the likeness of a battered saucepan.\n\nWhen I had exhausted the garden and a greenhouse with nothing in it but a fallen-down grape-vine and some bottles, I found myself in the dismal corner upon which I had looked out of the window. Never questioning for a moment that the house was now empty, I looked in at another window, and found myself, to my great surprise, exchanging a broad stare with a pale young gentleman with red eyelids and light hair.\n\nThis pale young gentleman quickly disappeared, and reappeared beside me. He had been at his books when I had found myself staring at him, and I now saw that he was inky.\n\n“Halloa!” said he, “young fellow!”\n\nHalloa being a general observation which I had usually observed to be best answered by itself, I said, “Halloa!” politely omitting young fellow.\n\n“Who let you in?” said he.\n\n“Miss Estella.”\n\n“Who gave you leave to prowl about?”\n\n“Miss Estella.”\n\n“Come and fight,” said the pale young gentleman.\n\nWhat could I do but follow him? I have often asked myself the question since; but what else could I do? His manner was so final, and I was so astonished, that I followed where he led, as if I had been under a spell.\n\n“Stop a minute, though,” he said, wheeling round before we had gone many paces. “I ought to give you a reason for fighting, too. There it is!” In a most irritating manner he instantly slapped his hands against one another, daintily flung one of his legs up behind him, pulled my hair, slapped his hands again, dipped his head, and butted it into my stomach.\n\nThe bull-like proceeding last mentioned, besides that it was unquestionably to be regarded in the light of a liberty, was particularly disagreeable just after bread and meat. I therefore hit out at him and was going to hit out again, when he said, “Aha! Would you?” and began dancing backwards and forwards in a manner quite unparalleled within my limited experience.\n\n“Laws of the game!” said he. Here, he skipped from his left leg on to his right. “Regular rules!” Here, he skipped from his right leg on to his left. “Come to the ground, and go through the preliminaries!” Here, he dodged backwards and forwards, and did all sorts of things while I looked helplessly at him.\n\nI was secretly afraid of him when I saw him so dexterous; but I felt morally and physically convinced that his light head of hair could have had no business in the pit of my stomach, and that I had a right to consider it irrelevant when so obtruded on my attention. Therefore, I followed him without a word, to a retired nook of the garden, formed by the junction of two walls and screened by some rubbish. On his asking me if I was satisfied with the ground, and on my replying Yes, he begged my leave to absent himself for a moment, and quickly returned with a bottle of water and a sponge dipped in vinegar. “Available for both,” he said, placing these against the wall. And then fell to pulling off, not only his jacket and waistcoat, but his shirt too, in a manner at once light-hearted, business-like, and bloodthirsty.\n\nAlthough he did not look very healthy,—having pimples on his face, and a breaking out at his mouth,—these dreadful preparations quite appalled me. I judged him to be about my own age, but he was much taller, and he had a way of spinning himself about that was full of appearance. For the rest, he was a young gentleman in a grey suit (when not denuded for battle), with his elbows, knees, wrists, and heels considerably in advance of the rest of him as to development.\n\nMy heart failed me when I saw him squaring at me with every demonstration of mechanical nicety, and eyeing my anatomy as if he were minutely choosing his bone. I never have been so surprised in my life, as I was when I let out the first blow, and saw him lying on his back, looking up at me with a bloody nose and his face exceedingly fore-shortened.\n\nBut, he was on his feet directly, and after sponging himself with a great show of dexterity began squaring again. The second greatest surprise I have ever had in my life was seeing him on his back again, looking up at me out of a black eye.\n\nHis spirit inspired me with great respect. He seemed to have no strength, and he never once hit me hard, and he was always knocked down; but he would be up again in a moment, sponging himself or drinking out of the water-bottle, with the greatest satisfaction in seconding himself according to form, and then came at me with an air and a show that made me believe he really was going to do for me at last. He got heavily bruised, for I am sorry to record that the more I hit him, the harder I hit him; but he came up again and again and again, until at last he got a bad fall with the back of his head against the wall. Even after that crisis in our affairs, he got up and turned round and round confusedly a few times, not knowing where I was; but finally went on his knees to his sponge and threw it up: at the same time panting out, “That means you have won.”\n\nHe seemed so brave and innocent, that although I had not proposed the contest, I felt but a gloomy satisfaction in my victory. Indeed, I go so far as to hope that I regarded myself while dressing as a species of savage young wolf or other wild beast. However, I got dressed, darkly wiping my sanguinary face at intervals, and I said, “Can I help you?” and he said “No thankee,” and I said “Good afternoon,” and he said “Same to you.”\n\nWhen I got into the courtyard, I found Estella waiting with the keys. But she neither asked me where I had been, nor why I had kept her waiting; and there was a bright flush upon her face, as though something had happened to delight her. Instead of going straight to the gate, too, she stepped back into the passage, and beckoned me.\n\n“Come here! You may kiss me, if you like.”\n\nI kissed her cheek as she turned it to me. I think I would have gone through a great deal to kiss her cheek. But I felt that the kiss was given to the coarse common boy as a piece of money might have been, and that it was worth nothing.\n\nWhat with the birthday visitors, and what with the cards, and what with the fight, my stay had lasted so long, that when I neared home the light on the spit of sand off the point on the marshes was gleaming against a black night-sky, and Joe’s furnace was flinging a path of fire across the road.\n\n# Chapter XII.\n\nMy mind grew very uneasy on the subject of the pale young gentleman. The more I thought of the fight, and recalled the pale young gentleman on his back in various stages of puffy and incrimsoned countenance, the more certain it appeared that something would be done to me. I felt that the pale young gentleman’s blood was on my head, and that the Law would avenge it. Without having any definite idea of the penalties I had incurred, it was clear to me that village boys could not go stalking about the country, ravaging the houses of gentlefolks and pitching into the studious youth of England, without laying themselves open to severe punishment. For some days, I even kept close at home, and looked out at the kitchen door with the greatest caution and trepidation before going on an errand, lest the officers of the County Jail should pounce upon me. The pale young gentleman’s nose had stained my trousers, and I tried to wash out that evidence of my guilt in the dead of night. I had cut my knuckles against the pale young gentleman’s teeth, and I twisted my imagination into a thousand tangles, as I devised incredible ways of accounting for that damnatory circumstance when I should be haled before the Judges.\n\nWhen the day came round for my return to the scene of the deed of violence, my terrors reached their height. Whether myrmidons of Justice, especially sent down from London, would be lying in ambush behind the gate;—whether Miss Havisham, preferring to take personal vengeance for an outrage done to her house, might rise in those grave-clothes of hers, draw a pistol, and shoot me dead:—whether suborned boys—a numerous band of mercenaries—might be engaged to fall upon me in the brewery, and cuff me until I was no more;—it was high testimony to my confidence in the spirit of the pale young gentleman, that I never imagined him accessory to these retaliations; they always came into my mind as the acts of injudicious relatives of his, goaded on by the state of his visage and an indignant sympathy with the family features.\n\nHowever, go to Miss Havisham’s I must, and go I did. And behold! nothing came of the late struggle. It was not alluded to in any way, and no pale young gentleman was to be discovered on the premises. I found the same gate open, and I explored the garden, and even looked in at the windows of the detached house; but my view was suddenly stopped by the closed shutters within, and all was lifeless. Only in the corner where the combat had taken place could I detect any evidence of the young gentleman’s existence. There were traces of his gore in that spot, and I covered them with garden-mould from the eye of man.\n\nOn the broad landing between Miss Havisham’s own room and that other room in which the long table was laid out, I saw a garden-chair,—a light chair on wheels, that you pushed from behind. It had been placed there since my last visit, and I entered, that same day, on a regular occupation of pushing Miss Havisham in this chair (when she was tired of walking with her hand upon my shoulder) round her own room, and across the landing, and round the other room. Over and over and over again, we would make these journeys, and sometimes they would last as long as three hours at a stretch. I insensibly fall into a general mention of these journeys as numerous, because it was at once settled that I should return every alternate day at noon for these purposes, and because I am now going to sum up a period of at least eight or ten months.\n\nAs we began to be more used to one another, Miss Havisham talked more to me, and asked me such questions as what had I learnt and what was I going to be? I told her I was going to be apprenticed to Joe, I believed; and I enlarged upon my knowing nothing and wanting to know everything, in the hope that she might offer some help towards that desirable end. But she did not; on the contrary, she seemed to prefer my being ignorant. Neither did she ever give me any money,—or anything but my daily dinner,—nor ever stipulate that I should be paid for my services.\n\nEstella was always about, and always let me in and out, but never told me I might kiss her again. Sometimes, she would coldly tolerate me; sometimes, she would condescend to me; sometimes, she would be quite familiar with me; sometimes, she would tell me energetically that she hated me. Miss Havisham would often ask me in a whisper, or when we were alone, “Does she grow prettier and prettier, Pip?” And when I said yes (for indeed she did), would seem to enjoy it greedily. Also, when we played at cards Miss Havisham would look on, with a miserly relish of Estella’s moods, whatever they were. And sometimes, when her moods were so many and so contradictory of one another that I was puzzled what to say or do, Miss Havisham would embrace her with lavish fondness, murmuring something in her ear that sounded like “Break their hearts my pride and hope, break their hearts and have no mercy!”\n\nThere was a song Joe used to hum fragments of at the forge, of which the burden was Old Clem. This was not a very ceremonious way of rendering homage to a patron saint, but I believe Old Clem stood in that relation towards smiths. It was a song that imitated the measure of beating upon iron, and was a mere lyrical excuse for the introduction of Old Clem’s respected name. Thus, you were to hammer boys round—Old Clem! With a thump and a sound—Old Clem! Beat it out, beat it out—Old Clem! With a clink for the stout—Old Clem! Blow the fire, blow the fire—Old Clem! Roaring dryer, soaring higher—Old Clem! One day soon after the appearance of the chair, Miss Havisham suddenly saying to me, with the impatient movement of her fingers, “There, there, there! Sing!” I was surprised into crooning this ditty as I pushed her over the floor. It happened so to catch her fancy that she took it up in a low brooding voice as if she were singing in her sleep. After that, it became customary with us to have it as we moved about, and Estella would often join in; though the whole strain was so subdued, even when there were three of us, that it made less noise in the grim old house than the lightest breath of wind.\n\nWhat could I become with these surroundings? How could my character fail to be influenced by them? Is it to be wondered at if my thoughts were dazed, as my eyes were, when I came out into the natural light from the misty yellow rooms?\n\nPerhaps I might have told Joe about the pale young gentleman, if I had not previously been betrayed into those enormous inventions to which I had confessed. Under the circumstances, I felt that Joe could hardly fail to discern in the pale young gentleman, an appropriate passenger to be put into the black velvet coach; therefore, I said nothing of him. Besides, that shrinking from having Miss Havisham and Estella discussed, which had come upon me in the beginning, grew much more potent as time went on. I reposed complete confidence in no one but Biddy; but I told poor Biddy everything. Why it came natural to me to do so, and why Biddy had a deep concern in everything I told her, I did not know then, though I think I know now.\n\nMeanwhile, councils went on in the kitchen at home, fraught with almost insupportable aggravation to my exasperated spirit. That ass, Pumblechook, used often to come over of a night for the purpose of discussing my prospects with my sister; and I really do believe (to this hour with less penitence than I ought to feel), that if these hands could have taken a linchpin out of his chaise-cart, they would have done it. The miserable man was a man of that confined stolidity of mind, that he could not discuss my prospects without having me before him,—as it were, to operate upon,—and he would drag me up from my stool (usually by the collar) where I was quiet in a corner, and, putting me before the fire as if I were going to be cooked, would begin by saying, “Now, Mum, here is this boy! Here is this boy which you brought up by hand. Hold up your head, boy, and be forever grateful unto them which so did do. Now, Mum, with respections to this boy!” And then he would rumple my hair the wrong way,—which from my earliest remembrance, as already hinted, I have in my soul denied the right of any fellow-creature to do,—and would hold me before him by the sleeve,—a spectacle of imbecility only to be equalled by himself.\n\nThen, he and my sister would pair off in such nonsensical speculations about Miss Havisham, and about what she would do with me and for me, that I used to want—quite painfully—to burst into spiteful tears, fly at Pumblechook, and pummel him all over. In these dialogues, my sister spoke to me as if she were morally wrenching one of my teeth out at every reference; while Pumblechook himself, self-constituted my patron, would sit supervising me with a depreciatory eye, like the architect of my fortunes who thought himself engaged on a very unremunerative job.\n\nIn these discussions, Joe bore no part. But he was often talked at, while they were in progress, by reason of Mrs. Joe’s perceiving that he was not favourable to my being taken from the forge. I was fully old enough now to be apprenticed to Joe; and when Joe sat with the poker on his knees thoughtfully raking out the ashes between the lower bars, my sister would so distinctly construe that innocent action into opposition on his part, that she would dive at him, take the poker out of his hands, shake him, and put it away. There was a most irritating end to every one of these debates. All in a moment, with nothing to lead up to it, my sister would stop herself in a yawn, and catching sight of me as it were incidentally, would swoop upon me with, “Come! there’s enough of you! You get along to bed; you’ve given trouble enough for one night, I hope!” As if I had besought them as a favour to bother my life out.\n\nWe went on in this way for a long time, and it seemed likely that we should continue to go on in this way for a long time, when one day Miss Havisham stopped short as she and I were walking, she leaning on my shoulder; and said with some displeasure,—\n\n“You are growing tall, Pip!”\n\nI thought it best to hint, through the medium of a meditative look, that this might be occasioned by circumstances over which I had no control.\n\nShe said no more at the time; but she presently stopped and looked at me again; and presently again; and after that, looked frowning and moody. On the next day of my attendance, when our usual exercise was over, and I had landed her at her dressing-table, she stayed me with a movement of her impatient fingers:—\n\n“Tell me the name again of that blacksmith of yours.”\n\n“Joe Gargery, ma’am.”\n\n“Meaning the master you were to be apprenticed to?”\n\n“Yes, Miss Havisham.”\n\n“You had better be apprenticed at once. Would Gargery come here with you, and bring your indentures, do you think?”\n\nI signified that I had no doubt he would take it as an honour to be asked.\n\n“Then let him come.”\n\n“At any particular time, Miss Havisham?”\n\n“There, there! I know nothing about times. Let him come soon, and come along with you.”\n\nWhen I got home at night, and delivered this message for Joe, my sister “went on the Rampage,” in a more alarming degree than at any previous period. She asked me and Joe whether we supposed she was door-mats under our feet, and how we dared to use her so, and what company we graciously thought she was fit for? When she had exhausted a torrent of such inquiries, she threw a candlestick at Joe, burst into a loud sobbing, got out the dustpan,—which was always a very bad sign,—put on her coarse apron, and began cleaning up to a terrible extent. Not satisfied with a dry cleaning, she took to a pail and scrubbing-brush, and cleaned us out of house and home, so that we stood shivering in the back-yard. It was ten o’clock at night before we ventured to creep in again, and then she asked Joe why he hadn’t married a Negress Slave at once? Joe offered no answer, poor fellow, but stood feeling his whisker and looking dejectedly at me, as if he thought it really might have been a better speculation.\n\n# Chapter XIII.\n\nIt was a trial to my feelings, on the next day but one, to see Joe arraying himself in his Sunday clothes to accompany me to Miss Havisham’s. However, as he thought his court-suit necessary to the occasion, it was not for me to tell him that he looked far better in his working-dress; the rather, because I knew he made himself so dreadfully uncomfortable, entirely on my account, and that it was for me he pulled up his shirt-collar so very high behind, that it made the hair on the crown of his head stand up like a tuft of feathers.\n\nAt breakfast-time my sister declared her intention of going to town with us, and being left at Uncle Pumblechook’s and called for “when we had done with our fine ladies”—a way of putting the case, from which Joe appeared inclined to augur the worst. The forge was shut up for the day, and Joe inscribed in chalk upon the door (as it was his custom to do on the very rare occasions when he was not at work) the monosyllable HOUT, accompanied by a sketch of an arrow supposed to be flying in the direction he had taken.\n\nWe walked to town, my sister leading the way in a very large beaver bonnet, and carrying a basket like the Great Seal of England in plaited Straw, a pair of pattens, a spare shawl, and an umbrella, though it was a fine bright day. I am not quite clear whether these articles were carried penitentially or ostentatiously; but I rather think they were displayed as articles of property,—much as Cleopatra or any other sovereign lady on the Rampage might exhibit her wealth in a pageant or procession.\n\nWhen we came to Pumblechook’s, my sister bounced in and left us. As it was almost noon, Joe and I held straight on to Miss Havisham’s house. Estella opened the gate as usual, and, the moment she appeared, Joe took his hat off and stood weighing it by the brim in both his hands; as if he had some urgent reason in his mind for being particular to half a quarter of an ounce.\n\nEstella took no notice of either of us, but led us the way that I knew so well. I followed next to her, and Joe came last. When I looked back at Joe in the long passage, he was still weighing his hat with the greatest care, and was coming after us in long strides on the tips of his toes.\n\nEstella told me we were both to go in, so I took Joe by the coat-cuff and conducted him into Miss Havisham’s presence. She was seated at her dressing-table, and looked round at us immediately.\n\n“Oh!” said she to Joe. “You are the husband of the sister of this boy?”\n\nI could hardly have imagined dear old Joe looking so unlike himself or so like some extraordinary bird; standing as he did speechless, with his tuft of feathers ruffled, and his mouth open as if he wanted a worm.\n\n“You are the husband,” repeated Miss Havisham, “of the sister of this boy?”\n\nIt was very aggravating; but, throughout the interview, Joe persisted in addressing Me instead of Miss Havisham.\n\n“Which I meantersay, Pip,” Joe now observed in a manner that was at once expressive of forcible argumentation, strict confidence, and great politeness, “as I hup and married your sister, and I were at the time what you might call (if you was anyways inclined) a single man.”\n\n“Well!” said Miss Havisham. “And you have reared the boy, with the intention of taking him for your apprentice; is that so, Mr. Gargery?”\n\n“You know, Pip,” replied Joe, “as you and me were ever friends, and it were looked for’ard to betwixt us, as being calc’lated to lead to larks. Not but what, Pip, if you had ever made objections to the business,—such as its being open to black and sut, or such-like,—not but what they would have been attended to, don’t you see?”\n\n“Has the boy,” said Miss Havisham, “ever made any objection? Does he like the trade?”\n\n“Which it is well beknown to yourself, Pip,” returned Joe, strengthening his former mixture of argumentation, confidence, and politeness, “that it were the wish of your own hart.” (I saw the idea suddenly break upon him that he would adapt his epitaph to the occasion, before he went on to say) “And there weren’t no objection on your part, and Pip it were the great wish of your hart!”\n\nIt was quite in vain for me to endeavour to make him sensible that he ought to speak to Miss Havisham. The more I made faces and gestures to him to do it, the more confidential, argumentative, and polite, he persisted in being to Me.\n\n“Have you brought his indentures with you?” asked Miss Havisham.\n\n“Well, Pip, you know,” replied Joe, as if that were a little unreasonable, “you yourself see me put ’em in my ’at, and therefore you know as they are here.” With which he took them out, and gave them, not to Miss Havisham, but to me. I am afraid I was ashamed of the dear good fellow,—I know I was ashamed of him,—when I saw that Estella stood at the back of Miss Havisham’s chair, and that her eyes laughed mischievously. I took the indentures out of his hand and gave them to Miss Havisham.\n\n“You expected,” said Miss Havisham, as she looked them over, “no premium with the boy?”\n\n“Joe!” I remonstrated, for he made no reply at all. “Why don’t you answer—”\n\n“Pip,” returned Joe, cutting me short as if he were hurt, “which I meantersay that were not a question requiring a answer betwixt yourself and me, and which you know the answer to be full well No. You know it to be No, Pip, and wherefore should I say it?”\n\nMiss Havisham glanced at him as if she understood what he really was better than I had thought possible, seeing what he was there; and took up a little bag from the table beside her.\n\n“Pip has earned a premium here,” she said, “and here it is. There are five-and-twenty guineas in this bag. Give it to your master, Pip.”\n\nAs if he were absolutely out of his mind with the wonder awakened in him by her strange figure and the strange room, Joe, even at this pass, persisted in addressing me.\n\n“This is wery liberal on your part, Pip,” said Joe, “and it is as such received and grateful welcome, though never looked for, far nor near, nor nowheres. And now, old chap,” said Joe, conveying to me a sensation, first of burning and then of freezing, for I felt as if that familiar expression were applied to Miss Havisham,—“and now, old chap, may we do our duty! May you and me do our duty, both on us, by one and another, and by them which your liberal present—have-conweyed—to be—for the satisfaction of mind-of—them as never—” here Joe showed that he felt he had fallen into frightful difficulties, until he triumphantly rescued himself with the words, “and from myself far be it!” These words had such a round and convincing sound for him that he said them twice.\n\n“Good-bye, Pip!” said Miss Havisham. “Let them out, Estella.”\n\n“Am I to come again, Miss Havisham?” I asked.\n\n“No. Gargery is your master now. Gargery! One word!”\n\nThus calling him back as I went out of the door, I heard her say to Joe in a distinct emphatic voice, “The boy has been a good boy here, and that is his reward. Of course, as an honest man, you will expect no other and no more.”\n\nHow Joe got out of the room, I have never been able to determine; but I know that when he did get out he was steadily proceeding upstairs instead of coming down, and was deaf to all remonstrances until I went after him and laid hold of him. In another minute we were outside the gate, and it was locked, and Estella was gone. When we stood in the daylight alone again, Joe backed up against a wall, and said to me, “Astonishing!” And there he remained so long saying, “Astonishing” at intervals, so often, that I began to think his senses were never coming back. At length he prolonged his remark into “Pip, I do assure you this is as-TON-ishing!” and so, by degrees, became conversational and able to walk away.\n\nI have reason to think that Joe’s intellects were brightened by the encounter they had passed through, and that on our way to Pumblechook’s he invented a subtle and deep design. My reason is to be found in what took place in Mr. Pumblechook’s parlour: where, on our presenting ourselves, my sister sat in conference with that detested seedsman.\n\n“Well?” cried my sister, addressing us both at once. “And what’s happened to you? I wonder you condescend to come back to such poor society as this, I am sure I do!”\n\n“Miss Havisham,” said Joe, with a fixed look at me, like an effort of remembrance, “made it wery partick’ler that we should give her—were it compliments or respects, Pip?”\n\n“Compliments,” I said.\n\n“Which that were my own belief,” answered Joe; “her compliments to Mrs. J. Gargery—”\n\n“Much good they’ll do me!” observed my sister; but rather gratified too.\n\n“And wishing,” pursued Joe, with another fixed look at me, like another effort of remembrance, “that the state of Miss Havisham’s elth were sitch as would have—allowed, were it, Pip?”\n\n“Of her having the pleasure,” I added.\n\n“Of ladies’ company,” said Joe. And drew a long breath.\n\n“Well!” cried my sister, with a mollified glance at Mr. Pumblechook. “She might have had the politeness to send that message at first, but it’s better late than never. And what did she give young Rantipole here?”\n\n“She giv’ him,” said Joe, “nothing.”\n\nMrs. Joe was going to break out, but Joe went on.\n\n“What she giv’,” said Joe, “she giv’ to his friends. ‘And by his friends,’ were her explanation, ‘I mean into the hands of his sister Mrs. J. Gargery.’ Them were her words; ‘Mrs. J. Gargery.’ She mayn’t have know’d,” added Joe, with an appearance of reflection, “whether it were Joe, or Jorge.”\n\nMy sister looked at Pumblechook: who smoothed the elbows of his wooden arm-chair, and nodded at her and at the fire, as if he had known all about it beforehand.\n\n“And how much have you got?” asked my sister, laughing. Positively laughing!\n\n“What would present company say to ten pound?” demanded Joe.\n\n“They’d say,” returned my sister, curtly, “pretty well. Not too much, but pretty well.”\n\n“It’s more than that, then,” said Joe.\n\nThat fearful Impostor, Pumblechook, immediately nodded, and said, as he rubbed the arms of his chair, “It’s more than that, Mum.”\n\n“Why, you don’t mean to say—” began my sister.\n\n“Yes I do, Mum,” said Pumblechook; “but wait a bit. Go on, Joseph. Good in you! Go on!”\n\n“What would present company say,” proceeded Joe, “to twenty pound?”\n\n“Handsome would be the word,” returned my sister.\n\n“Well, then,” said Joe, “It’s more than twenty pound.”\n\nThat abject hypocrite, Pumblechook, nodded again, and said, with a patronizing laugh, “It’s more than that, Mum. Good again! Follow her up, Joseph!”\n\n“Then to make an end of it,” said Joe, delightedly handing the bag to my sister; “it’s five-and-twenty pound.”\n\n“It’s five-and-twenty pound, Mum,” echoed that basest of swindlers, Pumblechook, rising to shake hands with her; “and it’s no more than your merits (as I said when my opinion was asked), and I wish you joy of the money!”\n\nIf the villain had stopped here, his case would have been sufficiently awful, but he blackened his guilt by proceeding to take me into custody, with a right of patronage that left all his former criminality far behind.\n\n“Now you see, Joseph and wife,” said Pumblechook, as he took me by the arm above the elbow, “I am one of them that always go right through with what they’ve begun. This boy must be bound, out of hand. That’s my way. Bound out of hand.”\n\n“Goodness knows, Uncle Pumblechook,” said my sister (grasping the money), “we’re deeply beholden to you.”\n\n“Never mind me, Mum,” returned that diabolical cornchandler. “A pleasure’s a pleasure all the world over. But this boy, you know; we must have him bound. I said I’d see to it—to tell you the truth.”\n\nThe Justices were sitting in the Town Hall near at hand, and we at once went over to have me bound apprentice to Joe in the Magisterial presence. I say we went over, but I was pushed over by Pumblechook, exactly as if I had that moment picked a pocket or fired a rick; indeed, it was the general impression in Court that I had been taken red-handed; for, as Pumblechook shoved me before him through the crowd, I heard some people say, “What’s he done?” and others, “He’s a young ’un, too, but looks bad, don’t he?” One person of mild and benevolent aspect even gave me a tract ornamented with a woodcut of a malevolent young man fitted up with a perfect sausage-shop of fetters, and entitled TO BE READ IN MY CELL.\n\nThe Hall was a queer place, I thought, with higher pews in it than a church,—and with people hanging over the pews looking on,—and with mighty Justices (one with a powdered head) leaning back in chairs, with folded arms, or taking snuff, or going to sleep, or writing, or reading the newspapers,—and with some shining black portraits on the walls, which my unartistic eye regarded as a composition of hardbake and sticking-plaster. Here, in a corner my indentures were duly signed and attested, and I was “bound”; Mr. Pumblechook holding me all the while as if we had looked in on our way to the scaffold, to have those little preliminaries disposed of.\n\nWhen we had come out again, and had got rid of the boys who had been put into great spirits by the expectation of seeing me publicly tortured, and who were much disappointed to find that my friends were merely rallying round me, we went back to Pumblechook’s. And there my sister became so excited by the twenty-five guineas, that nothing would serve her but we must have a dinner out of that windfall at the Blue Boar, and that Pumblechook must go over in his chaise-cart, and bring the Hubbles and Mr. Wopsle.\n\nIt was agreed to be done; and a most melancholy day I passed. For, it inscrutably appeared to stand to reason, in the minds of the whole company, that I was an excrescence on the entertainment. And to make it worse, they all asked me from time to time,—in short, whenever they had nothing else to do,—why I didn’t enjoy myself? And what could I possibly do then, but say I was enjoying myself,—when I wasn’t!\n\nHowever, they were grown up and had their own way, and they made the most of it. That swindling Pumblechook, exalted into the beneficent contriver of the whole occasion, actually took the top of the table; and, when he addressed them on the subject of my being bound, and had fiendishly congratulated them on my being liable to imprisonment if I played at cards, drank strong liquors, kept late hours or bad company, or indulged in other vagaries which the form of my indentures appeared to contemplate as next to inevitable, he placed me standing on a chair beside him to illustrate his remarks.\n\nMy only other remembrances of the great festival are, That they wouldn’t let me go to sleep, but whenever they saw me dropping off, woke me up and told me to enjoy myself. That, rather late in the evening Mr. Wopsle gave us Collins’s ode, and threw his bloodstained sword in thunder down, with such effect, that a waiter came in and said, “The Commercials underneath sent up their compliments, and it wasn’t the Tumblers’ Arms.” That, they were all in excellent spirits on the road home, and sang, O Lady Fair! Mr. Wopsle taking the bass, and asserting with a tremendously strong voice (in reply to the inquisitive bore who leads that piece of music in a most impertinent manner, by wanting to know all about everybody’s private affairs) that he was the man with his white locks flowing, and that he was upon the whole the weakest pilgrim going.\n\nFinally, I remember that when I got into my little bedroom, I was truly wretched, and had a strong conviction on me that I should never like Joe’s trade. I had liked it once, but once was not now.\n\n# Chapter XIV.\n\nIt is a most miserable thing to feel ashamed of home. There may be black ingratitude in the thing, and the punishment may be retributive and well deserved; but that it is a miserable thing, I can testify.\n\nHome had never been a very pleasant place to me, because of my sister’s temper. But, Joe had sanctified it, and I had believed in it. I had believed in the best parlour as a most elegant saloon; I had believed in the front door, as a mysterious portal of the Temple of State whose solemn opening was attended with a sacrifice of roast fowls; I had believed in the kitchen as a chaste though not magnificent apartment; I had believed in the forge as the glowing road to manhood and independence. Within a single year all this was changed. Now it was all coarse and common, and I would not have had Miss Havisham and Estella see it on any account.\n\nHow much of my ungracious condition of mind may have been my own fault, how much Miss Havisham’s, how much my sister’s, is now of no moment to me or to any one. The change was made in me; the thing was done. Well or ill done, excusably or inexcusably, it was done.\n\nOnce, it had seemed to me that when I should at last roll up my shirt-sleeves and go into the forge, Joe’s ’prentice, I should be distinguished and happy. Now the reality was in my hold, I only felt that I was dusty with the dust of small-coal, and that I had a weight upon my daily remembrance to which the anvil was a feather. There have been occasions in my later life (I suppose as in most lives) when I have felt for a time as if a thick curtain had fallen on all its interest and romance, to shut me out from anything save dull endurance any more. Never has that curtain dropped so heavy and blank, as when my way in life lay stretched out straight before me through the newly entered road of apprenticeship to Joe.\n\nI remember that at a later period of my “time,” I used to stand about the churchyard on Sunday evenings when night was falling, comparing my own perspective with the windy marsh view, and making out some likeness between them by thinking how flat and low both were, and how on both there came an unknown way and a dark mist and then the sea. I was quite as dejected on the first working-day of my apprenticeship as in that after-time; but I am glad to know that I never breathed a murmur to Joe while my indentures lasted. It is about the only thing I am glad to know of myself in that connection.\n\nFor, though it includes what I proceed to add, all the merit of what I proceed to add was Joe’s. It was not because I was faithful, but because Joe was faithful, that I never ran away and went for a soldier or a sailor. It was not because I had a strong sense of the virtue of industry, but because Joe had a strong sense of the virtue of industry, that I worked with tolerable zeal against the grain. It is not possible to know how far the influence of any amiable honest-hearted duty-doing man flies out into the world; but it is very possible to know how it has touched one’s self in going by, and I know right well that any good that intermixed itself with my apprenticeship came of plain contented Joe, and not of restlessly aspiring discontented me.\n\nWhat I wanted, who can say? How can I say, when I never knew? What I dreaded was, that in some unlucky hour I, being at my grimiest and commonest, should lift up my eyes and see Estella looking in at one of the wooden windows of the forge. I was haunted by the fear that she would, sooner or later, find me out, with a black face and hands, doing the coarsest part of my work, and would exult over me and despise me. Often after dark, when I was pulling the bellows for Joe, and we were singing Old Clem, and when the thought how we used to sing it at Miss Havisham’s would seem to show me Estella’s face in the fire, with her pretty hair fluttering in the wind and her eyes scorning me,—often at such a time I would look towards those panels of black night in the wall which the wooden windows then were, and would fancy that I saw her just drawing her face away, and would believe that she had come at last.\n\nAfter that, when we went in to supper, the place and the meal would have a more homely look than ever, and I would feel more ashamed of home than ever, in my own ungracious breast.\n\n# Chapter XV.\n\nAs I was getting too big for Mr. Wopsle’s great-aunt’s room, my education under that preposterous female terminated. Not, however, until Biddy had imparted to me everything she knew, from the little catalogue of prices, to a comic song she had once bought for a half-penny. Although the only coherent part of the latter piece of literature were the opening lines,\n\nWhen I went to Lunnon town sirs, Too rul loo rul Too rul loo rul Wasn’t I done very brown sirs? Too rul loo rul Too rul loo rul\n\n—still, in my desire to be wiser, I got this composition by heart with the utmost gravity; nor do I recollect that I questioned its merit, except that I thought (as I still do) the amount of Too rul somewhat in excess of the poetry. In my hunger for information, I made proposals to Mr. Wopsle to bestow some intellectual crumbs upon me, with which he kindly complied. As it turned out, however, that he only wanted me for a dramatic lay-figure, to be contradicted and embraced and wept over and bullied and clutched and stabbed and knocked about in a variety of ways, I soon declined that course of instruction; though not until Mr. Wopsle in his poetic fury had severely mauled me.\n\nWhatever I acquired, I tried to impart to Joe. This statement sounds so well, that I cannot in my conscience let it pass unexplained. I wanted to make Joe less ignorant and common, that he might be worthier of my society and less open to Estella’s reproach.\n\nThe old Battery out on the marshes was our place of study, and a broken slate and a short piece of slate-pencil were our educational implements: to which Joe always added a pipe of tobacco. I never knew Joe to remember anything from one Sunday to another, or to acquire, under my tuition, any piece of information whatever. Yet he would smoke his pipe at the Battery with a far more sagacious air than anywhere else,—even with a learned air,—as if he considered himself to be advancing immensely. Dear fellow, I hope he did.\n\nIt was pleasant and quiet, out there with the sails on the river passing beyond the earthwork, and sometimes, when the tide was low, looking as if they belonged to sunken ships that were still sailing on at the bottom of the water. Whenever I watched the vessels standing out to sea with their white sails spread, I somehow thought of Miss Havisham and Estella; and whenever the light struck aslant, afar off, upon a cloud or sail or green hillside or water-line, it was just the same.—Miss Havisham and Estella and the strange house and the strange life appeared to have something to do with everything that was picturesque.\n\nOne Sunday when Joe, greatly enjoying his pipe, had so plumed himself on being “most awful dull,” that I had given him up for the day, I lay on the earthwork for some time with my chin on my hand, descrying traces of Miss Havisham and Estella all over the prospect, in the sky and in the water, until at last I resolved to mention a thought concerning them that had been much in my head.\n\n“Joe,” said I; “don’t you think I ought to make Miss Havisham a visit?”\n\n“Well, Pip,” returned Joe, slowly considering. “What for?”\n\n“What for, Joe? What is any visit made for?”\n\n“There is some wisits p’r’aps,” said Joe, “as for ever remains open to the question, Pip. But in regard to wisiting Miss Havisham. She might think you wanted something,—expected something of her.”\n\n“Don’t you think I might say that I did not, Joe?”\n\n“You might, old chap,” said Joe. “And she might credit it. Similarly she mightn’t.”\n\nJoe felt, as I did, that he had made a point there, and he pulled hard at his pipe to keep himself from weakening it by repetition.\n\n“You see, Pip,” Joe pursued, as soon as he was past that danger, “Miss Havisham done the handsome thing by you. When Miss Havisham done the handsome thing by you, she called me back to say to me as that were all.”\n\n“Yes, Joe. I heard her.”\n\n“ALL,” Joe repeated, very emphatically.\n\n“Yes, Joe. I tell you, I heard her.”\n\n“Which I meantersay, Pip, it might be that her meaning were,—Make a end on it!—As you was!—Me to the North, and you to the South!—Keep in sunders!”\n\nI had thought of that too, and it was very far from comforting to me to find that he had thought of it; for it seemed to render it more probable.\n\n“But, Joe.”\n\n“Yes, old chap.”\n\n“Here am I, getting on in the first year of my time, and, since the day of my being bound, I have never thanked Miss Havisham, or asked after her, or shown that I remember her.”\n\n“That’s true, Pip; and unless you was to turn her out a set of shoes all four round,—and which I meantersay as even a set of shoes all four round might not be acceptable as a present, in a total wacancy of hoofs—”\n\n“I don’t mean that sort of remembrance, Joe; I don’t mean a present.”\n\nBut Joe had got the idea of a present in his head and must harp upon it. “Or even,” said he, “if you was helped to knocking her up a new chain for the front door,—or say a gross or two of shark-headed screws for general use,—or some light fancy article, such as a toasting-fork when she took her muffins,—or a gridiron when she took a sprat or such like—”\n\n“I don’t mean any present at all, Joe,” I interposed.\n\n“Well,” said Joe, still harping on it as though I had particularly pressed it, “if I was yourself, Pip, I wouldn’t. No, I would not. For what’s a door-chain when she’s got one always up? And shark-headers is open to misrepresentations. And if it was a toasting-fork, you’d go into brass and do yourself no credit. And the oncommonest workman can’t show himself oncommon in a gridiron,—for a gridiron IS a gridiron,” said Joe, steadfastly impressing it upon me, as if he were endeavouring to rouse me from a fixed delusion, “and you may haim at what you like, but a gridiron it will come out, either by your leave or again your leave, and you can’t help yourself—”\n\n“My dear Joe,” I cried, in desperation, taking hold of his coat, “don’t go on in that way. I never thought of making Miss Havisham any present.”\n\n“No, Pip,” Joe assented, as if he had been contending for that, all along; “and what I say to you is, you are right, Pip.”\n\n“Yes, Joe; but what I wanted to say, was, that as we are rather slack just now, if you would give me a half-holiday to-morrow, I think I would go uptown and make a call on Miss Est—Havisham.”\n\n“Which her name,” said Joe, gravely, “ain’t Estavisham, Pip, unless she have been rechris’ened.”\n\n“I know, Joe, I know. It was a slip of mine. What do you think of it, Joe?”\n\nIn brief, Joe thought that if I thought well of it, he thought well of it. But, he was particular in stipulating that if I were not received with cordiality, or if I were not encouraged to repeat my visit as a visit which had no ulterior object but was simply one of gratitude for a favour received, then this experimental trip should have no successor. By these conditions I promised to abide.\n\nNow, Joe kept a journeyman at weekly wages whose name was Orlick. He pretended that his Christian name was Dolge,—a clear Impossibility,—but he was a fellow of that obstinate disposition that I believe him to have been the prey of no delusion in this particular, but wilfully to have imposed that name upon the village as an affront to its understanding. He was a broadshouldered loose-limbed swarthy fellow of great strength, never in a hurry, and always slouching. He never even seemed to come to his work on purpose, but would slouch in as if by mere accident; and when he went to the Jolly Bargemen to eat his dinner, or went away at night, he would slouch out, like Cain or the Wandering Jew, as if he had no idea where he was going and no intention of ever coming back. He lodged at a sluice-keeper’s out on the marshes, and on working-days would come slouching from his hermitage, with his hands in his pockets and his dinner loosely tied in a bundle round his neck and dangling on his back. On Sundays he mostly lay all day on the sluice-gates, or stood against ricks and barns. He always slouched, locomotively, with his eyes on the ground; and, when accosted or otherwise required to raise them, he looked up in a half-resentful, half-puzzled way, as though the only thought he ever had was, that it was rather an odd and injurious fact that he should never be thinking.\n\nThis morose journeyman had no liking for me. When I was very small and timid, he gave me to understand that the Devil lived in a black corner of the forge, and that he knew the fiend very well: also that it was necessary to make up the fire, once in seven years, with a live boy, and that I might consider myself fuel. When I became Joe’s ’prentice, Orlick was perhaps confirmed in some suspicion that I should displace him; howbeit, he liked me still less. Not that he ever said anything, or did anything, openly importing hostility; I only noticed that he always beat his sparks in my direction, and that whenever I sang Old Clem, he came in out of time.\n\nDolge Orlick was at work and present, next day, when I reminded Joe of my half-holiday. He said nothing at the moment, for he and Joe had just got a piece of hot iron between them, and I was at the bellows; but by and by he said, leaning on his hammer,—\n\n“Now, master! Sure you’re not a-going to favour only one of us. If Young Pip has a half-holiday, do as much for Old Orlick.” I suppose he was about five-and-twenty, but he usually spoke of himself as an ancient person.\n\n“Why, what’ll you do with a half-holiday, if you get it?” said Joe.\n\n“What’ll I do with it! What’ll he do with it? I’ll do as much with it as him,” said Orlick.\n\n“As to Pip, he’s going up town,” said Joe.\n\n“Well then, as to Old Orlick, he’s a-going up town,” retorted that worthy. “Two can go up town. Tain’t only one wot can go up town.\n\n“Don’t lose your temper,” said Joe.\n\n“Shall if I like,” growled Orlick. “Some and their uptowning! Now, master! Come. No favouring in this shop. Be a man!”\n\nThe master refusing to entertain the subject until the journeyman was in a better temper, Orlick plunged at the furnace, drew out a red-hot bar, made at me with it as if he were going to run it through my body, whisked it round my head, laid it on the anvil, hammered it out,—as if it were I, I thought, and the sparks were my spirting blood,—and finally said, when he had hammered himself hot and the iron cold, and he again leaned on his hammer,—\n\n“Now, master!”\n\n“Are you all right now?” demanded Joe.\n\n“Ah! I am all right,” said gruff Old Orlick.\n\n“Then, as in general you stick to your work as well as most men,” said Joe, “let it be a half-holiday for all.”\n\nMy sister had been standing silent in the yard, within hearing,—she was a most unscrupulous spy and listener,—and she instantly looked in at one of the windows.\n\n“Like you, you fool!” said she to Joe, “giving holidays to great idle hulkers like that. You are a rich man, upon my life, to waste wages in that way. I wish I was his master!”\n\n“You’d be everybody’s master, if you durst,” retorted Orlick, with an ill-favoured grin.\n\n(“Let her alone,” said Joe.)\n\n“I’d be a match for all noodles and all rogues,” returned my sister, beginning to work herself into a mighty rage. “And I couldn’t be a match for the noodles, without being a match for your master, who’s the dunder-headed king of the noodles. And I couldn’t be a match for the rogues, without being a match for you, who are the blackest-looking and the worst rogue between this and France. Now!”\n\n“You’re a foul shrew, Mother Gargery,” growled the journeyman. “If that makes a judge of rogues, you ought to be a good’un.”\n\n(“Let her alone, will you?” said Joe.)\n\n“What did you say?” cried my sister, beginning to scream. “What did you say? What did that fellow Orlick say to me, Pip? What did he call me, with my husband standing by? Oh! oh! oh!” Each of these exclamations was a shriek; and I must remark of my sister, what is equally true of all the violent women I have ever seen, that passion was no excuse for her, because it is undeniable that instead of lapsing into passion, she consciously and deliberately took extraordinary pains to force herself into it, and became blindly furious by regular stages; “what was the name he gave me before the base man who swore to defend me? Oh! Hold me! Oh!”\n\n“Ah-h-h!” growled the journeyman, between his teeth, “I’d hold you, if you was my wife. I’d hold you under the pump, and choke it out of you.”\n\n(“I tell you, let her alone,” said Joe.)\n\n“Oh! To hear him!” cried my sister, with a clap of her hands and a scream together,—which was her next stage. “To hear the names he’s giving me! That Orlick! In my own house! Me, a married woman! With my husband standing by! Oh! Oh!” Here my sister, after a fit of clappings and screamings, beat her hands upon her bosom and upon her knees, and threw her cap off, and pulled her hair down,—which were the last stages on her road to frenzy. Being by this time a perfect Fury and a complete success, she made a dash at the door which I had fortunately locked.\n\nWhat could the wretched Joe do now, after his disregarded parenthetical interruptions, but stand up to his journeyman, and ask him what he meant by interfering betwixt himself and Mrs. Joe; and further whether he was man enough to come on? Old Orlick felt that the situation admitted of nothing less than coming on, and was on his defence straightway; so, without so much as pulling off their singed and burnt aprons, they went at one another, like two giants. But, if any man in that neighbourhood could stand uplong against Joe, I never saw the man. Orlick, as if he had been of no more account than the pale young gentleman, was very soon among the coal-dust, and in no hurry to come out of it. Then Joe unlocked the door and picked up my sister, who had dropped insensible at the window (but who had seen the fight first, I think), and who was carried into the house and laid down, and who was recommended to revive, and would do nothing but struggle and clench her hands in Joe’s hair. Then came that singular calm and silence which succeed all uproars; and then, with the vague sensation which I have always connected with such a lull,—namely, that it was Sunday, and somebody was dead,—I went upstairs to dress myself.\n\nWhen I came down again, I found Joe and Orlick sweeping up, without any other traces of discomposure than a slit in one of Orlick’s nostrils, which was neither expressive nor ornamental. A pot of beer had appeared from the Jolly Bargemen, and they were sharing it by turns in a peaceable manner. The lull had a sedative and philosophical influence on Joe, who followed me out into the road to say, as a parting observation that might do me good, “On the Rampage, Pip, and off the Rampage, Pip:—such is Life!”\n\nWith what absurd emotions (for we think the feelings that are very serious in a man quite comical in a boy) I found myself again going to Miss Havisham’s, matters little here. Nor, how I passed and repassed the gate many times before I could make up my mind to ring. Nor, how I debated whether I should go away without ringing; nor, how I should undoubtedly have gone, if my time had been my own, to come back.\n\nMiss Sarah Pocket came to the gate. No Estella.\n\n“How, then? You here again?” said Miss Pocket. “What do you want?”\n\nWhen I said that I only came to see how Miss Havisham was, Sarah evidently deliberated whether or no she should send me about my business. But unwilling to hazard the responsibility, she let me in, and presently brought the sharp message that I was to “come up.”\n\nEverything was unchanged, and Miss Havisham was alone.\n\n“Well?” said she, fixing her eyes upon me. “I hope you want nothing? You’ll get nothing.”\n\n“No indeed, Miss Havisham. I only wanted you to know that I am doing very well in my apprenticeship, and am always much obliged to you.”\n\n“There, there!” with the old restless fingers. “Come now and then; come on your birthday.—Ay!” she cried suddenly, turning herself and her chair towards me, “You are looking round for Estella? Hey?”\n\nI had been looking round,—in fact, for Estella,—and I stammered that I hoped she was well.\n\n“Abroad,” said Miss Havisham; “educating for a lady; far out of reach; prettier than ever; admired by all who see her. Do you feel that you have lost her?”\n\nThere was such a malignant enjoyment in her utterance of the last words, and she broke into such a disagreeable laugh, that I was at a loss what to say. She spared me the trouble of considering, by dismissing me. When the gate was closed upon me by Sarah of the walnut-shell countenance, I felt more than ever dissatisfied with my home and with my trade and with everything; and that was all I took by that motion.\n\nAs I was loitering along the High Street, looking in disconsolately at the shop windows, and thinking what I would buy if I were a gentleman, who should come out of the bookshop but Mr. Wopsle. Mr. Wopsle had in his hand the affecting tragedy of George Barnwell, in which he had that moment invested sixpence, with the view of heaping every word of it on the head of Pumblechook, with whom he was going to drink tea. No sooner did he see me, than he appeared to consider that a special Providence had put a ’prentice in his way to be read at; and he laid hold of me, and insisted on my accompanying him to the Pumblechookian parlour. As I knew it would be miserable at home, and as the nights were dark and the way was dreary, and almost any companionship on the road was better than none, I made no great resistance; consequently, we turned into Pumblechook’s just as the street and the shops were lighting up.\n\nAs I never assisted at any other representation of George Barnwell, I don’t know how long it may usually take; but I know very well that it took until half-past nine o’ clock that night, and that when Mr. Wopsle got into Newgate, I thought he never would go to the scaffold, he became so much slower than at any former period of his disgraceful career. I thought it a little too much that he should complain of being cut short in his flower after all, as if he had not been running to seed, leaf after leaf, ever since his course began. This, however, was a mere question of length and wearisomeness. What stung me, was the identification of the whole affair with my unoffending self. When Barnwell began to go wrong, I declare that I felt positively apologetic, Pumblechook’s indignant stare so taxed me with it. Wopsle, too, took pains to present me in the worst light. At once ferocious and maudlin, I was made to murder my uncle with no extenuating circumstances whatever; Millwood put me down in argument, on every occasion; it became sheer monomania in my master’s daughter to care a button for me; and all I can say for my gasping and procrastinating conduct on the fatal morning, is, that it was worthy of the general feebleness of my character. Even after I was happily hanged and Wopsle had closed the book, Pumblechook sat staring at me, and shaking his head, and saying, “Take warning, boy, take warning!” as if it were a well-known fact that I contemplated murdering a near relation, provided I could only induce one to have the weakness to become my benefactor.\n\nIt was a very dark night when it was all over, and when I set out with Mr. Wopsle on the walk home. Beyond town, we found a heavy mist out, and it fell wet and thick. The turnpike lamp was a blur, quite out of the lamp’s usual place apparently, and its rays looked solid substance on the fog. We were noticing this, and saying how that the mist rose with a change of wind from a certain quarter of our marshes, when we came upon a man, slouching under the lee of the turnpike house.\n\n“Halloa!” we said, stopping. “Orlick there?”\n\n“Ah!” he answered, slouching out. “I was standing by a minute, on the chance of company.”\n\n“You are late,” I remarked.\n\nOrlick not unnaturally answered, “Well? And you’re late.”\n\n“We have been,” said Mr. Wopsle, exalted with his late performance,—“we have been indulging, Mr. Orlick, in an intellectual evening.”\n\nOld Orlick growled, as if he had nothing to say about that, and we all went on together. I asked him presently whether he had been spending his half-holiday up and down town?\n\n“Yes,” said he, “all of it. I come in behind yourself. I didn’t see you, but I must have been pretty close behind you. By the by, the guns is going again.”\n\n“At the Hulks?” said I.\n\n“Ay! There’s some of the birds flown from the cages. The guns have been going since dark, about. You’ll hear one presently.”\n\nIn effect, we had not walked many yards further, when the well-remembered boom came towards us, deadened by the mist, and heavily rolled away along the low grounds by the river, as if it were pursuing and threatening the fugitives.\n\n“A good night for cutting off in,” said Orlick. “We’d be puzzled how to bring down a jail-bird on the wing, to-night.”\n\nThe subject was a suggestive one to me, and I thought about it in silence. Mr. Wopsle, as the ill-requited uncle of the evening’s tragedy, fell to meditating aloud in his garden at Camberwell. Orlick, with his hands in his pockets, slouched heavily at my side. It was very dark, very wet, very muddy, and so we splashed along. Now and then, the sound of the signal cannon broke upon us again, and again rolled sulkily along the course of the river. I kept myself to myself and my thoughts. Mr. Wopsle died amiably at Camberwell, and exceedingly game on Bosworth Field, and in the greatest agonies at Glastonbury. Orlick sometimes growled, “Beat it out, beat it out,—Old Clem! With a clink for the stout,—Old Clem!” I thought he had been drinking, but he was not drunk.\n\nThus, we came to the village. The way by which we approached it took us past the Three Jolly Bargemen, which we were surprised to find—it being eleven o’clock—in a state of commotion, with the door wide open, and unwonted lights that had been hastily caught up and put down scattered about. Mr. Wopsle dropped in to ask what was the matter (surmising that a convict had been taken), but came running out in a great hurry.\n\n“There’s something wrong,” said he, without stopping, “up at your place, Pip. Run all!”\n\n“What is it?” I asked, keeping up with him. So did Orlick, at my side.\n\n“I can’t quite understand. The house seems to have been violently entered when Joe Gargery was out. Supposed by convicts. Somebody has been attacked and hurt.”\n\nWe were running too fast to admit of more being said, and we made no stop until we got into our kitchen. It was full of people; the whole village was there, or in the yard; and there was a surgeon, and there was Joe, and there were a group of women, all on the floor in the midst of the kitchen. The unemployed bystanders drew back when they saw me, and so I became aware of my sister,—lying without sense or movement on the bare boards where she had been knocked down by a tremendous blow on the back of the head, dealt by some unknown hand when her face was turned towards the fire,—destined never to be on the Rampage again, while she was the wife of Joe.\n\n# Chapter XVI.\n\nWith my head full of George Barnwell, I was at first disposed to believe that I must have had some hand in the attack upon my sister, or at all events that as her near relation, popularly known to be under obligations to her, I was a more legitimate object of suspicion than any one else. But when, in the clearer light of next morning, I began to reconsider the matter and to hear it discussed around me on all sides, I took another view of the case, which was more reasonable.\n\nJoe had been at the Three Jolly Bargemen, smoking his pipe, from a quarter after eight o’clock to a quarter before ten. While he was there, my sister had been seen standing at the kitchen door, and had exchanged Good Night with a farm-labourer going home. The man could not be more particular as to the time at which he saw her (he got into dense confusion when he tried to be), than that it must have been before nine. When Joe went home at five minutes before ten, he found her struck down on the floor, and promptly called in assistance. The fire had not then burnt unusually low, nor was the snuff of the candle very long; the candle, however, had been blown out.\n\nNothing had been taken away from any part of the house. Neither, beyond the blowing out of the candle,—which stood on a table between the door and my sister, and was behind her when she stood facing the fire and was struck,—was there any disarrangement of the kitchen, excepting such as she herself had made, in falling and bleeding. But, there was one remarkable piece of evidence on the spot. She had been struck with something blunt and heavy, on the head and spine; after the blows were dealt, something heavy had been thrown down at her with considerable violence, as she lay on her face. And on the ground beside her, when Joe picked her up, was a convict’s leg-iron which had been filed asunder.\n\nNow, Joe, examining this iron with a smith’s eye, declared it to have been filed asunder some time ago. The hue and cry going off to the Hulks, and people coming thence to examine the iron, Joe’s opinion was corroborated. They did not undertake to say when it had left the prison-ships to which it undoubtedly had once belonged; but they claimed to know for certain that that particular manacle had not been worn by either of the two convicts who had escaped last night. Further, one of those two was already retaken, and had not freed himself of his iron.\n\nKnowing what I knew, I set up an inference of my own here. I believed the iron to be my convict’s iron,—the iron I had seen and heard him filing at, on the marshes,—but my mind did not accuse him of having put it to its latest use. For I believed one of two other persons to have become possessed of it, and to have turned it to this cruel account. Either Orlick, or the strange man who had shown me the file.\n\nNow, as to Orlick; he had gone to town exactly as he told us when we picked him up at the turnpike, he had been seen about town all the evening, he had been in divers companies in several public-houses, and he had come back with myself and Mr. Wopsle. There was nothing against him, save the quarrel; and my sister had quarrelled with him, and with everybody else about her, ten thousand times. As to the strange man; if he had come back for his two bank-notes there could have been no dispute about them, because my sister was fully prepared to restore them. Besides, there had been no altercation; the assailant had come in so silently and suddenly, that she had been felled before she could look round.\n\nIt was horrible to think that I had provided the weapon, however undesignedly, but I could hardly think otherwise. I suffered unspeakable trouble while I considered and reconsidered whether I should at last dissolve that spell of my childhood and tell Joe all the story. For months afterwards, I every day settled the question finally in the negative, and reopened and reargued it next morning. The contention came, after all, to this;—the secret was such an old one now, had so grown into me and become a part of myself, that I could not tear it away. In addition to the dread that, having led up to so much mischief, it would be now more likely than ever to alienate Joe from me if he believed it, I had a further restraining dread that he would not believe it, but would assort it with the fabulous dogs and veal-cutlets as a monstrous invention. However, I temporized with myself, of course—for, was I not wavering between right and wrong, when the thing is always done?—and resolved to make a full disclosure if I should see any such new occasion as a new chance of helping in the discovery of the assailant.\n\nThe Constables and the Bow Street men from London—for, this happened in the days of the extinct red-waistcoated police—were about the house for a week or two, and did pretty much what I have heard and read of like authorities doing in other such cases. They took up several obviously wrong people, and they ran their heads very hard against wrong ideas, and persisted in trying to fit the circumstances to the ideas, instead of trying to extract ideas from the circumstances. Also, they stood about the door of the Jolly Bargemen, with knowing and reserved looks that filled the whole neighbourhood with admiration; and they had a mysterious manner of taking their drink, that was almost as good as taking the culprit. But not quite, for they never did it.\n\nLong after these constitutional powers had dispersed, my sister lay very ill in bed. Her sight was disturbed, so that she saw objects multiplied, and grasped at visionary teacups and wineglasses instead of the realities; her hearing was greatly impaired; her memory also; and her speech was unintelligible. When, at last, she came round so far as to be helped downstairs, it was still necessary to keep my slate always by her, that she might indicate in writing what she could not indicate in speech. As she was (very bad handwriting apart) a more than indifferent speller, and as Joe was a more than indifferent reader, extraordinary complications arose between them which I was always called in to solve. The administration of mutton instead of medicine, the substitution of Tea for Joe, and the baker for bacon, were among the mildest of my own mistakes.\n\nHowever, her temper was greatly improved, and she was patient. A tremulous uncertainty of the action of all her limbs soon became a part of her regular state, and afterwards, at intervals of two or three months, she would often put her hands to her head, and would then remain for about a week at a time in some gloomy aberration of mind. We were at a loss to find a suitable attendant for her, until a circumstance happened conveniently to relieve us. Mr. Wopsle’s great-aunt conquered a confirmed habit of living into which she had fallen, and Biddy became a part of our establishment.\n\nIt may have been about a month after my sister’s reappearance in the kitchen, when Biddy came to us with a small speckled box containing the whole of her worldly effects, and became a blessing to the household. Above all, she was a blessing to Joe, for the dear old fellow was sadly cut up by the constant contemplation of the wreck of his wife, and had been accustomed, while attending on her of an evening, to turn to me every now and then and say, with his blue eyes moistened, “Such a fine figure of a woman as she once were, Pip!” Biddy instantly taking the cleverest charge of her as though she had studied her from infancy; Joe became able in some sort to appreciate the greater quiet of his life, and to get down to the Jolly Bargemen now and then for a change that did him good. It was characteristic of the police people that they had all more or less suspected poor Joe (though he never knew it), and that they had to a man concurred in regarding him as one of the deepest spirits they had ever encountered.\n\nBiddy’s first triumph in her new office, was to solve a difficulty that had completely vanquished me. I had tried hard at it, but had made nothing of it. Thus it was:—\n\nAgain and again and again, my sister had traced upon the slate, a character that looked like a curious T, and then with the utmost eagerness had called our attention to it as something she particularly wanted. I had in vain tried everything producible that began with a T, from tar to toast and tub. At length it had come into my head that the sign looked like a hammer, and on my lustily calling that word in my sister’s ear, she had begun to hammer on the table and had expressed a qualified assent. Thereupon, I had brought in all our hammers, one after another, but without avail. Then I bethought me of a crutch, the shape being much the same, and I borrowed one in the village, and displayed it to my sister with considerable confidence. But she shook her head to that extent when she was shown it, that we were terrified lest in her weak and shattered state she should dislocate her neck.\n\nWhen my sister found that Biddy was very quick to understand her, this mysterious sign reappeared on the slate. Biddy looked thoughtfully at it, heard my explanation, looked thoughtfully at my sister, looked thoughtfully at Joe (who was always represented on the slate by his initial letter), and ran into the forge, followed by Joe and me.\n\n“Why, of course!” cried Biddy, with an exultant face. “Don’t you see? It’s him!”\n\nOrlick, without a doubt! She had lost his name, and could only signify him by his hammer. We told him why we wanted him to come into the kitchen, and he slowly laid down his hammer, wiped his brow with his arm, took another wipe at it with his apron, and came slouching out, with a curious loose vagabond bend in the knees that strongly distinguished him.\n\nI confess that I expected to see my sister denounce him, and that I was disappointed by the different result. She manifested the greatest anxiety to be on good terms with him, was evidently much pleased by his being at length produced, and motioned that she would have him given something to drink. She watched his countenance as if she were particularly wishful to be assured that he took kindly to his reception, she showed every possible desire to conciliate him, and there was an air of humble propitiation in all she did, such as I have seen pervade the bearing of a child towards a hard master. After that day, a day rarely passed without her drawing the hammer on her slate, and without Orlick’s slouching in and standing doggedly before her, as if he knew no more than I did what to make of it.\n\n# Chapter XVII.\n\nI now fell into a regular routine of apprenticeship life, which was varied beyond the limits of the village and the marshes, by no more remarkable circumstance than the arrival of my birthday and my paying another visit to Miss Havisham. I found Miss Sarah Pocket still on duty at the gate; I found Miss Havisham just as I had left her, and she spoke of Estella in the very same way, if not in the very same words. The interview lasted but a few minutes, and she gave me a guinea when I was going, and told me to come again on my next birthday. I may mention at once that this became an annual custom. I tried to decline taking the guinea on the first occasion, but with no better effect than causing her to ask me very angrily, if I expected more? Then, and after that, I took it.\n\nSo unchanging was the dull old house, the yellow light in the darkened room, the faded spectre in the chair by the dressing-table glass, that I felt as if the stopping of the clocks had stopped Time in that mysterious place, and, while I and everything else outside it grew older, it stood still. Daylight never entered the house as to my thoughts and remembrances of it, any more than as to the actual fact. It bewildered me, and under its influence I continued at heart to hate my trade and to be ashamed of home.\n\nImperceptibly I became conscious of a change in Biddy, however. Her shoes came up at the heel, her hair grew bright and neat, her hands were always clean. She was not beautiful,—she was common, and could not be like Estella,—but she was pleasant and wholesome and sweet-tempered. She had not been with us more than a year (I remember her being newly out of mourning at the time it struck me), when I observed to myself one evening that she had curiously thoughtful and attentive eyes; eyes that were very pretty and very good.\n\nIt came of my lifting up my own eyes from a task I was poring at—writing some passages from a book, to improve myself in two ways at once by a sort of stratagem—and seeing Biddy observant of what I was about. I laid down my pen, and Biddy stopped in her needlework without laying it down.\n\n“Biddy,” said I, “how do you manage it? Either I am very stupid, or you are very clever.”\n\n“What is it that I manage? I don’t know,” returned Biddy, smiling.\n\nShe managed our whole domestic life, and wonderfully too; but I did not mean that, though that made what I did mean more surprising.\n\n“How do you manage, Biddy,” said I, “to learn everything that I learn, and always to keep up with me?” I was beginning to be rather vain of my knowledge, for I spent my birthday guineas on it, and set aside the greater part of my pocket-money for similar investment; though I have no doubt, now, that the little I knew was extremely dear at the price.\n\n“I might as well ask you,” said Biddy, “how you manage?”\n\n“No; because when I come in from the forge of a night, any one can see me turning to at it. But you never turn to at it, Biddy.”\n\n“I suppose I must catch it like a cough,” said Biddy, quietly; and went on with her sewing.\n\nPursuing my idea as I leaned back in my wooden chair, and looked at Biddy sewing away with her head on one side, I began to think her rather an extraordinary girl. For I called to mind now, that she was equally accomplished in the terms of our trade, and the names of our different sorts of work, and our various tools. In short, whatever I knew, Biddy knew. Theoretically, she was already as good a blacksmith as I, or better.\n\n“You are one of those, Biddy,” said I, “who make the most of every chance. You never had a chance before you came here, and see how improved you are!”\n\nBiddy looked at me for an instant, and went on with her sewing. “I was your first teacher though; wasn’t I?” said she, as she sewed.\n\n“Biddy!” I exclaimed, in amazement. “Why, you are crying!”\n\n“No I am not,” said Biddy, looking up and laughing. “What put that in your head?”\n\nWhat could have put it in my head but the glistening of a tear as it dropped on her work? I sat silent, recalling what a drudge she had been until Mr. Wopsle’s great-aunt successfully overcame that bad habit of living, so highly desirable to be got rid of by some people. I recalled the hopeless circumstances by which she had been surrounded in the miserable little shop and the miserable little noisy evening school, with that miserable old bundle of incompetence always to be dragged and shouldered. I reflected that even in those untoward times there must have been latent in Biddy what was now developing, for, in my first uneasiness and discontent I had turned to her for help, as a matter of course. Biddy sat quietly sewing, shedding no more tears, and while I looked at her and thought about it all, it occurred to me that perhaps I had not been sufficiently grateful to Biddy. I might have been too reserved, and should have patronised her more (though I did not use that precise word in my meditations) with my confidence.\n\n“Yes, Biddy,” I observed, when I had done turning it over, “you were my first teacher, and that at a time when we little thought of ever being together like this, in this kitchen.”\n\n“Ah, poor thing!” replied Biddy. It was like her self-forgetfulness to transfer the remark to my sister, and to get up and be busy about her, making her more comfortable; “that’s sadly true!”\n\n“Well!” said I, “we must talk together a little more, as we used to do. And I must consult you a little more, as I used to do. Let us have a quiet walk on the marshes next Sunday, Biddy, and a long chat.”\n\nMy sister was never left alone now; but Joe more than readily undertook the care of her on that Sunday afternoon, and Biddy and I went out together. It was summer-time, and lovely weather. When we had passed the village and the church and the churchyard, and were out on the marshes and began to see the sails of the ships as they sailed on, I began to combine Miss Havisham and Estella with the prospect, in my usual way. When we came to the river-side and sat down on the bank, with the water rippling at our feet, making it all more quiet than it would have been without that sound, I resolved that it was a good time and place for the admission of Biddy into my inner confidence.\n\n“Biddy,” said I, after binding her to secrecy, “I want to be a gentleman.”\n\n“O, I wouldn’t, if I was you!” she returned. “I don’t think it would answer.”\n\n“Biddy,” said I, with some severity, “I have particular reasons for wanting to be a gentleman.”\n\n“You know best, Pip; but don’t you think you are happier as you are?”\n\n“Biddy,” I exclaimed, impatiently, “I am not at all happy as I am. I am disgusted with my calling and with my life. I have never taken to either, since I was bound. Don’t be absurd.”\n\n“Was I absurd?” said Biddy, quietly raising her eyebrows; “I am sorry for that; I didn’t mean to be. I only want you to do well, and to be comfortable.”\n\n“Well, then, understand once for all that I never shall or can be comfortable—or anything but miserable—there, Biddy!—unless I can lead a very different sort of life from the life I lead now.”\n\n“That’s a pity!” said Biddy, shaking her head with a sorrowful air.\n\nNow, I too had so often thought it a pity, that, in the singular kind of quarrel with myself which I was always carrying on, I was half inclined to shed tears of vexation and distress when Biddy gave utterance to her sentiment and my own. I told her she was right, and I knew it was much to be regretted, but still it was not to be helped.\n\n“If I could have settled down,” I said to Biddy, plucking up the short grass within reach, much as I had once upon a time pulled my feelings out of my hair and kicked them into the brewery wall,—“if I could have settled down and been but half as fond of the forge as I was when I was little, I know it would have been much better for me. You and I and Joe would have wanted nothing then, and Joe and I would perhaps have gone partners when I was out of my time, and I might even have grown up to keep company with you, and we might have sat on this very bank on a fine Sunday, quite different people. I should have been good enough for you; shouldn’t I, Biddy?”\n\nBiddy sighed as she looked at the ships sailing on, and returned for answer, “Yes; I am not over-particular.” It scarcely sounded flattering, but I knew she meant well.\n\n“Instead of that,” said I, plucking up more grass and chewing a blade or two, “see how I am going on. Dissatisfied, and uncomfortable, and—what would it signify to me, being coarse and common, if nobody had told me so!”\n\nBiddy turned her face suddenly towards mine, and looked far more attentively at me than she had looked at the sailing ships.\n\n“It was neither a very true nor a very polite thing to say,” she remarked, directing her eyes to the ships again. “Who said it?”\n\nI was disconcerted, for I had broken away without quite seeing where I was going to. It was not to be shuffled off now, however, and I answered, “The beautiful young lady at Miss Havisham’s, and she’s more beautiful than anybody ever was, and I admire her dreadfully, and I want to be a gentleman on her account.” Having made this lunatic confession, I began to throw my torn-up grass into the river, as if I had some thoughts of following it.\n\n“Do you want to be a gentleman, to spite her or to gain her over?” Biddy quietly asked me, after a pause.\n\n“I don’t know,” I moodily answered.\n\n“Because, if it is to spite her,” Biddy pursued, “I should think—but you know best—that might be better and more independently done by caring nothing for her words. And if it is to gain her over, I should think—but you know best—she was not worth gaining over.”\n\nExactly what I myself had thought, many times. Exactly what was perfectly manifest to me at the moment. But how could I, a poor dazed village lad, avoid that wonderful inconsistency into which the best and wisest of men fall every day?\n\n“It may be all quite true,” said I to Biddy, “but I admire her dreadfully.”\n\nIn short, I turned over on my face when I came to that, and got a good grasp on the hair on each side of my head, and wrenched it well. All the while knowing the madness of my heart to be so very mad and misplaced, that I was quite conscious it would have served my face right, if I had lifted it up by my hair, and knocked it against the pebbles as a punishment for belonging to such an idiot.\n\nBiddy was the wisest of girls, and she tried to reason no more with me. She put her hand, which was a comfortable hand though roughened by work, upon my hands, one after another, and gently took them out of my hair. Then she softly patted my shoulder in a soothing way, while with my face upon my sleeve I cried a little,—exactly as I had done in the brewery yard,—and felt vaguely convinced that I was very much ill-used by somebody, or by everybody; I can’t say which.\n\n“I am glad of one thing,” said Biddy, “and that is, that you have felt you could give me your confidence, Pip. And I am glad of another thing, and that is, that of course you know you may depend upon my keeping it and always so far deserving it. If your first teacher (dear! such a poor one, and so much in need of being taught herself!) had been your teacher at the present time, she thinks she knows what lesson she would set. But it would be a hard one to learn, and you have got beyond her, and it’s of no use now.” So, with a quiet sigh for me, Biddy rose from the bank, and said, with a fresh and pleasant change of voice, “Shall we walk a little farther, or go home?”\n\n“Biddy,” I cried, getting up, putting my arm round her neck, and giving her a kiss, “I shall always tell you everything.”\n\n“Till you’re a gentleman,” said Biddy.\n\n“You know I never shall be, so that’s always. Not that I have any occasion to tell you anything, for you know everything I know,—as I told you at home the other night.”\n\n“Ah!” said Biddy, quite in a whisper, as she looked away at the ships. And then repeated, with her former pleasant change, “shall we walk a little farther, or go home?”\n\nI said to Biddy we would walk a little farther, and we did so, and the summer afternoon toned down into the summer evening, and it was very beautiful. I began to consider whether I was not more naturally and wholesomely situated, after all, in these circumstances, than playing beggar my neighbour by candle-light in the room with the stopped clocks, and being despised by Estella. I thought it would be very good for me if I could get her out of my head, with all the rest of those remembrances and fancies, and could go to work determined to relish what I had to do, and stick to it, and make the best of it. I asked myself the question whether I did not surely know that if Estella were beside me at that moment instead of Biddy, she would make me miserable? I was obliged to admit that I did know it for a certainty, and I said to myself, “Pip, what a fool you are!”\n\nWe talked a good deal as we walked, and all that Biddy said seemed right. Biddy was never insulting, or capricious, or Biddy to-day and somebody else to-morrow; she would have derived only pain, and no pleasure, from giving me pain; she would far rather have wounded her own breast than mine. How could it be, then, that I did not like her much the better of the two?\n\n“Biddy,” said I, when we were walking homeward, “I wish you could put me right.”\n\n“I wish I could!” said Biddy.\n\n“If I could only get myself to fall in love with you,—you don’t mind my speaking so openly to such an old acquaintance?”\n\n“Oh dear, not at all!” said Biddy. “Don’t mind me.”\n\n“If I could only get myself to do it, that would be the thing for me.”\n\n“But you never will, you see,” said Biddy.\n\nIt did not appear quite so unlikely to me that evening, as it would have done if we had discussed it a few hours before. I therefore observed I was not quite sure of that. But Biddy said she was, and she said it decisively. In my heart I believed her to be right; and yet I took it rather ill, too, that she should be so positive on the point.\n\nWhen we came near the churchyard, we had to cross an embankment, and get over a stile near a sluice-gate. There started up, from the gate, or from the rushes, or from the ooze (which was quite in his stagnant way), Old Orlick.\n\n“Halloa!” he growled, “where are you two going?”\n\n“Where should we be going, but home?”\n\n“Well, then,” said he, “I’m jiggered if I don’t see you home!”\n\nThis penalty of being jiggered was a favourite supposititious case of his. He attached no definite meaning to the word that I am aware of, but used it, like his own pretended Christian name, to affront mankind, and convey an idea of something savagely damaging. When I was younger, I had had a general belief that if he had jiggered me personally, he would have done it with a sharp and twisted hook.\n\nBiddy was much against his going with us, and said to me in a whisper, “Don’t let him come; I don’t like him.” As I did not like him either, I took the liberty of saying that we thanked him, but we didn’t want seeing home. He received that piece of information with a yell of laughter, and dropped back, but came slouching after us at a little distance.\n\nCurious to know whether Biddy suspected him of having had a hand in that murderous attack of which my sister had never been able to give any account, I asked her why she did not like him.\n\n“Oh!” she replied, glancing over her shoulder as he slouched after us, “because I—I am afraid he likes me.”\n\n“Did he ever tell you he liked you?” I asked indignantly.\n\n“No,” said Biddy, glancing over her shoulder again, “he never told me so; but he dances at me, whenever he can catch my eye.”\n\nHowever novel and peculiar this testimony of attachment, I did not doubt the accuracy of the interpretation. I was very hot indeed upon Old Orlick’s daring to admire her; as hot as if it were an outrage on myself.\n\n“But it makes no difference to you, you know,” said Biddy, calmly.\n\n“No, Biddy, it makes no difference to me; only I don’t like it; I don’t approve of it.”\n\n“Nor I neither,” said Biddy. “Though that makes no difference to you.”\n\n“Exactly,” said I; “but I must tell you I should have no opinion of you, Biddy, if he danced at you with your own consent.”\n\nI kept an eye on Orlick after that night, and, whenever circumstances were favourable to his dancing at Biddy, got before him to obscure that demonstration. He had struck root in Joe’s establishment, by reason of my sister’s sudden fancy for him, or I should have tried to get him dismissed. He quite understood and reciprocated my good intentions, as I had reason to know thereafter.\n\nAnd now, because my mind was not confused enough before, I complicated its confusion fifty thousand-fold, by having states and seasons when I was clear that Biddy was immeasurably better than Estella, and that the plain honest working life to which I was born had nothing in it to be ashamed of, but offered me sufficient means of self-respect and happiness. At those times, I would decide conclusively that my disaffection to dear old Joe and the forge was gone, and that I was growing up in a fair way to be partners with Joe and to keep company with Biddy,—when all in a moment some confounding remembrance of the Havisham days would fall upon me like a destructive missile, and scatter my wits again. Scattered wits take a long time picking up; and often before I had got them well together, they would be dispersed in all directions by one stray thought, that perhaps after all Miss Havisham was going to make my fortune when my time was out.\n\nIf my time had run out, it would have left me still at the height of my perplexities, I dare say. It never did run out, however, but was brought to a premature end, as I proceed to relate.\n\n# Chapter XVIII.\n\nIt was in the fourth year of my apprenticeship to Joe, and it was a Saturday night. There was a group assembled round the fire at the Three Jolly Bargemen, attentive to Mr. Wopsle as he read the newspaper aloud. Of that group I was one.\n\nA highly popular murder had been committed, and Mr. Wopsle was imbrued in blood to the eyebrows. He gloated over every abhorrent adjective in the description, and identified himself with every witness at the Inquest. He faintly moaned, “I am done for,” as the victim, and he barbarously bellowed, “I’ll serve you out,” as the murderer. He gave the medical testimony, in pointed imitation of our local practitioner; and he piped and shook, as the aged turnpike-keeper who had heard blows, to an extent so very paralytic as to suggest a doubt regarding the mental competency of that witness. The coroner, in Mr. Wopsle’s hands, became Timon of Athens; the beadle, Coriolanus. He enjoyed himself thoroughly, and we all enjoyed ourselves, and were delightfully comfortable. In this cosey state of mind we came to the verdict Wilful Murder.\n\nThen, and not sooner, I became aware of a strange gentleman leaning over the back of the settle opposite me, looking on. There was an expression of contempt on his face, and he bit the side of a great forefinger as he watched the group of faces.\n\n“Well!” said the stranger to Mr. Wopsle, when the reading was done, “you have settled it all to your own satisfaction, I have no doubt?”\n\nEverybody started and looked up, as if it were the murderer. He looked at everybody coldly and sarcastically.\n\n“Guilty, of course?” said he. “Out with it. Come!”\n\n“Sir,” returned Mr. Wopsle, “without having the honour of your acquaintance, I do say Guilty.” Upon this we all took courage to unite in a confirmatory murmur.\n\n“I know you do,” said the stranger; “I knew you would. I told you so. But now I’ll ask you a question. Do you know, or do you not know, that the law of England supposes every man to be innocent, until he is proved—proved—to be guilty?”\n\n“Sir,” Mr. Wopsle began to reply, “as an Englishman myself, I—”\n\n“Come!” said the stranger, biting his forefinger at him. “Don’t evade the question. Either you know it, or you don’t know it. Which is it to be?”\n\nHe stood with his head on one side and himself on one side, in a bullying, interrogative manner, and he threw his forefinger at Mr. Wopsle,—as it were to mark him out—before biting it again.\n\n“Now!” said he. “Do you know it, or don’t you know it?”\n\n“Certainly I know it,” replied Mr. Wopsle.\n\n“Certainly you know it. Then why didn’t you say so at first? Now, I’ll ask you another question,”—taking possession of Mr. Wopsle, as if he had a right to him,—“do you know that none of these witnesses have yet been cross-examined?”\n\nMr. Wopsle was beginning, “I can only say—” when the stranger stopped him.\n\n“What? You won’t answer the question, yes or no? Now, I’ll try you again.” Throwing his finger at him again. “Attend to me. Are you aware, or are you not aware, that none of these witnesses have yet been cross-examined? Come, I only want one word from you. Yes, or no?”\n\nMr. Wopsle hesitated, and we all began to conceive rather a poor opinion of him.\n\n“Come!” said the stranger, “I’ll help you. You don’t deserve help, but I’ll help you. Look at that paper you hold in your hand. What is it?”\n\n“What is it?” repeated Mr. Wopsle, eyeing it, much at a loss.\n\n“Is it,” pursued the stranger in his most sarcastic and suspicious manner, “the printed paper you have just been reading from?”\n\n“Undoubtedly.”\n\n“Undoubtedly. Now, turn to that paper, and tell me whether it distinctly states that the prisoner expressly said that his legal advisers instructed him altogether to reserve his defence?”\n\n“I read that just now,” Mr. Wopsle pleaded.\n\n“Never mind what you read just now, sir; I don’t ask you what you read just now. You may read the Lord’s Prayer backwards, if you like,—and, perhaps, have done it before to-day. Turn to the paper. No, no, no my friend; not to the top of the column; you know better than that; to the bottom, to the bottom.” (We all began to think Mr. Wopsle full of subterfuge.) “Well? Have you found it?”\n\n“Here it is,” said Mr. Wopsle.\n\n“Now, follow that passage with your eye, and tell me whether it distinctly states that the prisoner expressly said that he was instructed by his legal advisers wholly to reserve his defence? Come! Do you make that of it?”\n\nMr. Wopsle answered, “Those are not the exact words.”\n\n“Not the exact words!” repeated the gentleman bitterly. “Is that the exact substance?”\n\n“Yes,” said Mr. Wopsle.\n\n“Yes,” repeated the stranger, looking round at the rest of the company with his right hand extended towards the witness, Wopsle. “And now I ask you what you say to the conscience of that man who, with that passage before his eyes, can lay his head upon his pillow after having pronounced a fellow-creature guilty, unheard?”\n\nWe all began to suspect that Mr. Wopsle was not the man we had thought him, and that he was beginning to be found out.\n\n“And that same man, remember,” pursued the gentleman, throwing his finger at Mr. Wopsle heavily,—“that same man might be summoned as a juryman upon this very trial, and, having thus deeply committed himself, might return to the bosom of his family and lay his head upon his pillow, after deliberately swearing that he would well and truly try the issue joined between Our Sovereign Lord the King and the prisoner at the bar, and would a true verdict give according to the evidence, so help him God!”\n\nWe were all deeply persuaded that the unfortunate Wopsle had gone too far, and had better stop in his reckless career while there was yet time.\n\nThe strange gentleman, with an air of authority not to be disputed, and with a manner expressive of knowing something secret about every one of us that would effectually do for each individual if he chose to disclose it, left the back of the settle, and came into the space between the two settles, in front of the fire, where he remained standing, his left hand in his pocket, and he biting the forefinger of his right.\n\n“From information I have received,” said he, looking round at us as we all quailed before him, “I have reason to believe there is a blacksmith among you, by name Joseph—or Joe—Gargery. Which is the man?”\n\n“Here is the man,” said Joe.\n\nThe strange gentleman beckoned him out of his place, and Joe went.\n\n“You have an apprentice,” pursued the stranger, “commonly known as Pip? Is he here?”\n“I am here!” I cried.\n\nThe stranger did not recognise me, but I recognised him as the gentleman I had met on the stairs, on the occasion of my second visit to Miss Havisham. I had known him the moment I saw him looking over the settle, and now that I stood confronting him with his hand upon my shoulder, I checked off again in detail his large head, his dark complexion, his deep-set eyes, his bushy black eyebrows, his large watch-chain, his strong black dots of beard and whisker, and even the smell of scented soap on his great hand.\n\n“I wish to have a private conference with you two,” said he, when he had surveyed me at his leisure. “It will take a little time. Perhaps we had better go to your place of residence. I prefer not to anticipate my communication here; you will impart as much or as little of it as you please to your friends afterwards; I have nothing to do with that.”\n\nAmidst a wondering silence, we three walked out of the Jolly Bargemen, and in a wondering silence walked home. While going along, the strange gentleman occasionally looked at me, and occasionally bit the side of his finger. As we neared home, Joe vaguely acknowledging the occasion as an impressive and ceremonious one, went on ahead to open the front door. Our conference was held in the state parlour, which was feebly lighted by one candle.\n\nIt began with the strange gentleman’s sitting down at the table, drawing the candle to him, and looking over some entries in his pocket-book. He then put up the pocket-book and set the candle a little aside, after peering round it into the darkness at Joe and me, to ascertain which was which.\n\n“My name,” he said, “is Jaggers, and I am a lawyer in London. I am pretty well known. I have unusual business to transact with you, and I commence by explaining that it is not of my originating. If my advice had been asked, I should not have been here. It was not asked, and you see me here. What I have to do as the confidential agent of another, I do. No less, no more.”\n\nFinding that he could not see us very well from where he sat, he got up, and threw one leg over the back of a chair and leaned upon it; thus having one foot on the seat of the chair, and one foot on the ground.\n\n“Now, Joseph Gargery, I am the bearer of an offer to relieve you of this young fellow your apprentice. You would not object to cancel his indentures at his request and for his good? You would want nothing for so doing?”\n\n“Lord forbid that I should want anything for not standing in Pip’s way,” said Joe, staring.\n\n“Lord forbidding is pious, but not to the purpose,” returned Mr. Jaggers. “The question is, Would you want anything? Do you want anything?”\n\n“The answer is,” returned Joe, sternly, “No.”\n\nI thought Mr. Jaggers glanced at Joe, as if he considered him a fool for his disinterestedness. But I was too much bewildered between breathless curiosity and surprise, to be sure of it.\n\n“Very well,” said Mr. Jaggers. “Recollect the admission you have made, and don’t try to go from it presently.”\n\n“Who’s a-going to try?” retorted Joe.\n\n“I don’t say anybody is. Do you keep a dog?”\n\n“Yes, I do keep a dog.”\n\n“Bear in mind then, that Brag is a good dog, but Holdfast is a better. Bear that in mind, will you?” repeated Mr. Jaggers, shutting his eyes and nodding his head at Joe, as if he were forgiving him something. “Now, I return to this young fellow. And the communication I have got to make is, that he has great expectations.”\n\nJoe and I gasped, and looked at one another.\n\n“I am instructed to communicate to him,” said Mr. Jaggers, throwing his finger at me sideways, “that he will come into a handsome property. Further, that it is the desire of the present possessor of that property, that he be immediately removed from his present sphere of life and from this place, and be brought up as a gentleman,—in a word, as a young fellow of great expectations.”\n\nMy dream was out; my wild fancy was surpassed by sober reality; Miss Havisham was going to make my fortune on a grand scale.\n\n“Now, Mr. Pip,” pursued the lawyer, “I address the rest of what I have to say, to you. You are to understand, first, that it is the request of the person from whom I take my instructions that you always bear the name of Pip. You will have no objection, I dare say, to your great expectations being encumbered with that easy condition. But if you have any objection, this is the time to mention it.”\n\nMy heart was beating so fast, and there was such a singing in my ears, that I could scarcely stammer I had no objection.\n\n“I should think not! Now you are to understand, secondly, Mr. Pip, that the name of the person who is your liberal benefactor remains a profound secret, until the person chooses to reveal it. I am empowered to mention that it is the intention of the person to reveal it at first hand by word of mouth to yourself. When or where that intention may be carried out, I cannot say; no one can say. It may be years hence. Now, you are distinctly to understand that you are most positively prohibited from making any inquiry on this head, or any allusion or reference, however distant, to any individual whomsoever as the individual, in all the communications you may have with me. If you have a suspicion in your own breast, keep that suspicion in your own breast. It is not the least to the purpose what the reasons of this prohibition are; they may be the strongest and gravest reasons, or they may be mere whim. This is not for you to inquire into. The condition is laid down. Your acceptance of it, and your observance of it as binding, is the only remaining condition that I am charged with, by the person from whom I take my instructions, and for whom I am not otherwise responsible. That person is the person from whom you derive your expectations, and the secret is solely held by that person and by me. Again, not a very difficult condition with which to encumber such a rise in fortune; but if you have any objection to it, this is the time to mention it. Speak out.”\n\nOnce more, I stammered with difficulty that I had no objection.\n\n“I should think not! Now, Mr. Pip, I have done with stipulations.” Though he called me Mr. Pip, and began rather to make up to me, he still could not get rid of a certain air of bullying suspicion; and even now he occasionally shut his eyes and threw his finger at me while he spoke, as much as to express that he knew all kinds of things to my disparagement, if he only chose to mention them. “We come next, to mere details of arrangement. You must know that, although I have used the term ‘expectations’ more than once, you are not endowed with expectations only. There is already lodged in my hands a sum of money amply sufficient for your suitable education and maintenance. You will please consider me your guardian. Oh!” for I was going to thank him, “I tell you at once, I am paid for my services, or I shouldn’t render them. It is considered that you must be better educated, in accordance with your altered position, and that you will be alive to the importance and necessity of at once entering on that advantage.”\n\nI said I had always longed for it.\n\n“Never mind what you have always longed for, Mr. Pip,” he retorted; “keep to the record. If you long for it now, that’s enough. Am I answered that you are ready to be placed at once under some proper tutor? Is that it?”\n\nI stammered yes, that was it.\n\n“Good. Now, your inclinations are to be consulted. I don’t think that wise, mind, but it’s my trust. Have you ever heard of any tutor whom you would prefer to another?”\n\nI had never heard of any tutor but Biddy and Mr. Wopsle’s great-aunt; so, I replied in the negative.\n\n“There is a certain tutor, of whom I have some knowledge, who I think might suit the purpose,” said Mr. Jaggers. “I don’t recommend him, observe; because I never recommend anybody. The gentleman I speak of is one Mr. Matthew Pocket.”\n\nAh! I caught at the name directly. Miss Havisham’s relation. The Matthew whom Mr. and Mrs. Camilla had spoken of. The Matthew whose place was to be at Miss Havisham’s head, when she lay dead, in her bride’s dress on the bride’s table.\n\n“You know the name?” said Mr. Jaggers, looking shrewdly at me, and then shutting up his eyes while he waited for my answer.\n\nMy answer was, that I had heard of the name.\n\n“Oh!” said he. “You have heard of the name. But the question is, what do you say of it?”\n\nI said, or tried to say, that I was much obliged to him for his recommendation—\n\n“No, my young friend!” he interrupted, shaking his great head very slowly. “Recollect yourself!”\n\nNot recollecting myself, I began again that I was much obliged to him for his recommendation—\n\n“No, my young friend,” he interrupted, shaking his head and frowning and smiling both at once,—“no, no, no; it’s very well done, but it won’t do; you are too young to fix me with it. Recommendation is not the word, Mr. Pip. Try another.”\n\nCorrecting myself, I said that I was much obliged to him for his mention of Mr. Matthew Pocket—\n\n“That’s more like it!” cried Mr. Jaggers.—And (I added), I would gladly try that gentleman.\n\n“Good. You had better try him in his own house. The way shall be prepared for you, and you can see his son first, who is in London. When will you come to London?”\n\nI said (glancing at Joe, who stood looking on, motionless), that I supposed I could come directly.\n\n“First,” said Mr. Jaggers, “you should have some new clothes to come in, and they should not be working-clothes. Say this day week. You’ll want some money. Shall I leave you twenty guineas?”\n\nHe produced a long purse, with the greatest coolness, and counted them out on the table and pushed them over to me. This was the first time he had taken his leg from the chair. He sat astride of the chair when he had pushed the money over, and sat swinging his purse and eyeing Joe.\n\n“Well, Joseph Gargery? You look dumbfoundered?”\n\n“I am!” said Joe, in a very decided manner.\n\n“It was understood that you wanted nothing for yourself, remember?”\n\n“It were understood,” said Joe. “And it are understood. And it ever will be similar according.”\n\n“But what,” said Mr. Jaggers, swinging his purse,—“what if it was in my instructions to make you a present, as compensation?”\n\n“As compensation what for?” Joe demanded.\n\n“For the loss of his services.”\n\nJoe laid his hand upon my shoulder with the touch of a woman. I have often thought him since, like the steam-hammer that can crush a man or pat an egg-shell, in his combination of strength with gentleness. “Pip is that hearty welcome,” said Joe, “to go free with his services, to honour and fortun’, as no words can tell him. But if you think as Money can make compensation to me for the loss of the little child—what come to the forge—and ever the best of friends!—”\n\nO dear good Joe, whom I was so ready to leave and so unthankful to, I see you again, with your muscular blacksmith’s arm before your eyes, and your broad chest heaving, and your voice dying away. O dear good faithful tender Joe, I feel the loving tremble of your hand upon my arm, as solemnly this day as if it had been the rustle of an angel’s wing!\n\nBut I encouraged Joe at the time. I was lost in the mazes of my future fortunes, and could not retrace the by-paths we had trodden together. I begged Joe to be comforted, for (as he said) we had ever been the best of friends, and (as I said) we ever would be so. Joe scooped his eyes with his disengaged wrist, as if he were bent on gouging himself, but said not another word.\n\nMr. Jaggers had looked on at this, as one who recognised in Joe the village idiot, and in me his keeper. When it was over, he said, weighing in his hand the purse he had ceased to swing:—\n\n“Now, Joseph Gargery, I warn you this is your last chance. No half measures with me. If you mean to take a present that I have it in charge to make you, speak out, and you shall have it. If on the contrary you mean to say—” Here, to his great amazement, he was stopped by Joe’s suddenly working round him with every demonstration of a fell pugilistic purpose.\n\n“Which I meantersay,” cried Joe, “that if you come into my place bull-baiting and badgering me, come out! Which I meantersay as sech if you’re a man, come on! Which I meantersay that what I say, I meantersay and stand or fall by!”\n\nI drew Joe away, and he immediately became placable; merely stating to me, in an obliging manner and as a polite expostulatory notice to any one whom it might happen to concern, that he were not a-going to be bull-baited and badgered in his own place. Mr. Jaggers had risen when Joe demonstrated, and had backed near the door. Without evincing any inclination to come in again, he there delivered his valedictory remarks. They were these.\n\n“Well, Mr. Pip, I think the sooner you leave here—as you are to be a gentleman—the better. Let it stand for this day week, and you shall receive my printed address in the meantime. You can take a hackney-coach at the stage-coach office in London, and come straight to me. Understand, that I express no opinion, one way or other, on the trust I undertake. I am paid for undertaking it, and I do so. Now, understand that, finally. Understand that!”\n\nHe was throwing his finger at both of us, and I think would have gone on, but for his seeming to think Joe dangerous, and going off.\n\nSomething came into my head which induced me to run after him, as he was going down to the Jolly Bargemen, where he had left a hired carriage.\n\n“I beg your pardon, Mr. Jaggers.”\n\n“Halloa!” said he, facing round, “what’s the matter?”\n\n“I wish to be quite right, Mr. Jaggers, and to keep to your directions; so I thought I had better ask. Would there be any objection to my taking leave of any one I know, about here, before I go away?”\n\n“No,” said he, looking as if he hardly understood me.\n\n“I don’t mean in the village only, but up town?”\n\n“No,” said he. “No objection.”\n\nI thanked him and ran home again, and there I found that Joe had already locked the front door and vacated the state parlour, and was seated by the kitchen fire with a hand on each knee, gazing intently at the burning coals. I too sat down before the fire and gazed at the coals, and nothing was said for a long time.\n\nMy sister was in her cushioned chair in her corner, and Biddy sat at her needle-work before the fire, and Joe sat next Biddy, and I sat next Joe in the corner opposite my sister. The more I looked into the glowing coals, the more incapable I became of looking at Joe; the longer the silence lasted, the more unable I felt to speak.\n\nAt length I got out, “Joe, have you told Biddy?”\n\n“No, Pip,” returned Joe, still looking at the fire, and holding his knees tight, as if he had private information that they intended to make off somewhere, “which I left it to yourself, Pip.”\n\n“I would rather you told, Joe.”\n\n“Pip’s a gentleman of fortun’ then,” said Joe, “and God bless him in it!”\n\nBiddy dropped her work, and looked at me. Joe held his knees and looked at me. I looked at both of them. After a pause, they both heartily congratulated me; but there was a certain touch of sadness in their congratulations that I rather resented.\n\nI took it upon myself to impress Biddy (and through Biddy, Joe) with the grave obligation I considered my friends under, to know nothing and say nothing about the maker of my fortune. It would all come out in good time, I observed, and in the meanwhile nothing was to be said, save that I had come into great expectations from a mysterious patron. Biddy nodded her head thoughtfully at the fire as she took up her work again, and said she would be very particular; and Joe, still detaining his knees, said, “Ay, ay, I’ll be ekervally partickler, Pip;” and then they congratulated me again, and went on to express so much wonder at the notion of my being a gentleman that I didn’t half like it.\n\nInfinite pains were then taken by Biddy to convey to my sister some idea of what had happened. To the best of my belief, those efforts entirely failed. She laughed and nodded her head a great many times, and even repeated after Biddy, the words “Pip” and “Property.” But I doubt if they had more meaning in them than an election cry, and I cannot suggest a darker picture of her state of mind.\n\nI never could have believed it without experience, but as Joe and Biddy became more at their cheerful ease again, I became quite gloomy. Dissatisfied with my fortune, of course I could not be; but it is possible that I may have been, without quite knowing it, dissatisfied with myself.\n\nAnyhow, I sat with my elbow on my knee and my face upon my hand, looking into the fire, as those two talked about my going away, and about what they should do without me, and all that. And whenever I caught one of them looking at me, though never so pleasantly (and they often looked at me,—particularly Biddy), I felt offended: as if they were expressing some mistrust of me. Though Heaven knows they never did by word or sign.\n\nAt those times I would get up and look out at the door; for our kitchen door opened at once upon the night, and stood open on summer evenings to air the room. The very stars to which I then raised my eyes, I am afraid I took to be but poor and humble stars for glittering on the rustic objects among which I had passed my life.\n\n“Saturday night,” said I, when we sat at our supper of bread and cheese and beer. “Five more days, and then the day before the day! They’ll soon go.”\n\n“Yes, Pip,” observed Joe, whose voice sounded hollow in his beer-mug. “They’ll soon go.”\n\n“Soon, soon go,” said Biddy.\n\n“I have been thinking, Joe, that when I go down town on Monday, and order my new clothes, I shall tell the tailor that I’ll come and put them on there, or that I’ll have them sent to Mr. Pumblechook’s. It would be very disagreeable to be stared at by all the people here.”\n\n“Mr. and Mrs. Hubble might like to see you in your new gen-teel figure too, Pip,” said Joe, industriously cutting his bread, with his cheese on it, in the palm of his left hand, and glancing at my untasted supper as if he thought of the time when we used to compare slices. “So might Wopsle. And the Jolly Bargemen might take it as a compliment.”\n\n“That’s just what I don’t want, Joe. They would make such a business of it,—such a coarse and common business,—that I couldn’t bear myself.”\n\n“Ah, that indeed, Pip!” said Joe. “If you couldn’t abear yourself—”\n\nBiddy asked me here, as she sat holding my sister’s plate, “Have you thought about when you’ll show yourself to Mr. Gargery, and your sister and me? You will show yourself to us; won’t you?”\n\n“Biddy,” I returned with some resentment, “you are so exceedingly quick that it’s difficult to keep up with you.”\n\n(“She always were quick,” observed Joe.)\n\n“If you had waited another moment, Biddy, you would have heard me say that I shall bring my clothes here in a bundle one evening,—most likely on the evening before I go away.”\n\nBiddy said no more. Handsomely forgiving her, I soon exchanged an affectionate good night with her and Joe, and went up to bed. When I got into my little room, I sat down and took a long look at it, as a mean little room that I should soon be parted from and raised above, for ever. It was furnished with fresh young remembrances too, and even at the same moment I fell into much the same confused division of mind between it and the better rooms to which I was going, as I had been in so often between the forge and Miss Havisham’s, and Biddy and Estella.\n\nThe sun had been shining brightly all day on the roof of my attic, and the room was warm. As I put the window open and stood looking out, I saw Joe come slowly forth at the dark door, below, and take a turn or two in the air; and then I saw Biddy come, and bring him a pipe and light it for him. He never smoked so late, and it seemed to hint to me that he wanted comforting, for some reason or other.\n\nHe presently stood at the door immediately beneath me, smoking his pipe, and Biddy stood there too, quietly talking to him, and I knew that they talked of me, for I heard my name mentioned in an endearing tone by both of them more than once. I would not have listened for more, if I could have heard more; so I drew away from the window, and sat down in my one chair by the bedside, feeling it very sorrowful and strange that this first night of my bright fortunes should be the loneliest I had ever known.\n\nLooking towards the open window, I saw light wreaths from Joe’s pipe floating there, and I fancied it was like a blessing from Joe,—not obtruded on me or paraded before me, but pervading the air we shared together. I put my light out, and crept into bed; and it was an uneasy bed now, and I never slept the old sound sleep in it any more.\n\n# Chapter XIX.\n\nMorning made a considerable difference in my general prospect of Life, and brightened it so much that it scarcely seemed the same. What lay heaviest on my mind was, the consideration that six days intervened between me and the day of departure; for I could not divest myself of a misgiving that something might happen to London in the meanwhile, and that, when I got there, it would be either greatly deteriorated or clean gone.\n\nJoe and Biddy were very sympathetic and pleasant when I spoke of our approaching separation; but they only referred to it when I did. After breakfast, Joe brought out my indentures from the press in the best parlour, and we put them in the fire, and I felt that I was free. With all the novelty of my emancipation on me, I went to church with Joe, and thought perhaps the clergyman wouldn’t have read that about the rich man and the kingdom of Heaven, if he had known all.\n\nAfter our early dinner, I strolled out alone, purposing to finish off the marshes at once, and get them done with. As I passed the church, I felt (as I had felt during service in the morning) a sublime compassion for the poor creatures who were destined to go there, Sunday after Sunday, all their lives through, and to lie obscurely at last among the low green mounds. I promised myself that I would do something for them one of these days, and formed a plan in outline for bestowing a dinner of roast-beef and plum-pudding, a pint of ale, and a gallon of condescension, upon everybody in the village.\n\nIf I had often thought before, with something allied to shame, of my companionship with the fugitive whom I had once seen limping among those graves, what were my thoughts on this Sunday, when the place recalled the wretch, ragged and shivering, with his felon iron and badge! My comfort was, that it happened a long time ago, and that he had doubtless been transported a long way off, and that he was dead to me, and might be veritably dead into the bargain.\n\nNo more low, wet grounds, no more dikes and sluices, no more of these grazing cattle,—though they seemed, in their dull manner, to wear a more respectful air now, and to face round, in order that they might stare as long as possible at the possessor of such great expectations,—farewell, monotonous acquaintances of my childhood, henceforth I was for London and greatness; not for smith’s work in general, and for you! I made my exultant way to the old Battery, and, lying down there to consider the question whether Miss Havisham intended me for Estella, fell asleep.\n\nWhen I awoke, I was much surprised to find Joe sitting beside me, smoking his pipe. He greeted me with a cheerful smile on my opening my eyes, and said,—\n\n“As being the last time, Pip, I thought I’d foller.”\n\n“And Joe, I am very glad you did so.”\n\n“Thankee, Pip.”\n\n“You may be sure, dear Joe,” I went on, after we had shaken hands, “that I shall never forget you.”\n\n“No, no, Pip!” said Joe, in a comfortable tone, “I’m sure of that. Ay, ay, old chap! Bless you, it were only necessary to get it well round in a man’s mind, to be certain on it. But it took a bit of time to get it well round, the change come so oncommon plump; didn’t it?”\n\nSomehow, I was not best pleased with Joe’s being so mightily secure of me. I should have liked him to have betrayed emotion, or to have said, “It does you credit, Pip,” or something of that sort. Therefore, I made no remark on Joe’s first head; merely saying as to his second, that the tidings had indeed come suddenly, but that I had always wanted to be a gentleman, and had often and often speculated on what I would do, if I were one.\n\n“Have you though?” said Joe. “Astonishing!”\n\n“It’s a pity now, Joe,” said I, “that you did not get on a little more, when we had our lessons here; isn’t it?”\n\n“Well, I don’t know,” returned Joe. “I’m so awful dull. I’m only master of my own trade. It were always a pity as I was so awful dull; but it’s no more of a pity now, than it was—this day twelvemonth—don’t you see?”\n\nWhat I had meant was, that when I came into my property and was able to do something for Joe, it would have been much more agreeable if he had been better qualified for a rise in station. He was so perfectly innocent of my meaning, however, that I thought I would mention it to Biddy in preference.\n\nSo, when we had walked home and had had tea, I took Biddy into our little garden by the side of the lane, and, after throwing out in a general way for the elevation of her spirits, that I should never forget her, said I had a favour to ask of her.\n\n“And it is, Biddy,” said I, “that you will not omit any opportunity of helping Joe on, a little.”\n\n“How helping him on?” asked Biddy, with a steady sort of glance.\n\n“Well! Joe is a dear good fellow,—in fact, I think he is the dearest fellow that ever lived,—but he is rather backward in some things. For instance, Biddy, in his learning and his manners.”\n\nAlthough I was looking at Biddy as I spoke, and although she opened her eyes very wide when I had spoken, she did not look at me.\n\n“O, his manners! won’t his manners do then?” asked Biddy, plucking a black-currant leaf.\n\n“My dear Biddy, they do very well here—”\n\n“O! they do very well here?” interrupted Biddy, looking closely at the leaf in her hand.\n\n“Hear me out,—but if I were to remove Joe into a higher sphere, as I shall hope to remove him when I fully come into my property, they would hardly do him justice.”\n\n“And don’t you think he knows that?” asked Biddy.\n\nIt was such a very provoking question (for it had never in the most distant manner occurred to me), that I said, snappishly,—\n\n“Biddy, what do you mean?”\n\nBiddy, having rubbed the leaf to pieces between her hands,—and the smell of a black-currant bush has ever since recalled to me that evening in the little garden by the side of the lane,—said, “Have you never considered that he may be proud?”\n\n“Proud?” I repeated, with disdainful emphasis.\n\n“O! there are many kinds of pride,” said Biddy, looking full at me and shaking her head; “pride is not all of one kind—”\n\n“Well? What are you stopping for?” said I.\n\n“Not all of one kind,” resumed Biddy. “He may be too proud to let any one take him out of a place that he is competent to fill, and fills well and with respect. To tell you the truth, I think he is; though it sounds bold in me to say so, for you must know him far better than I do.”\n\n“Now, Biddy,” said I, “I am very sorry to see this in you. I did not expect to see this in you. You are envious, Biddy, and grudging. You are dissatisfied on account of my rise in fortune, and you can’t help showing it.”\n\n“If you have the heart to think so,” returned Biddy, “say so. Say so over and over again, if you have the heart to think so.”\n\n“If you have the heart to be so, you mean, Biddy,” said I, in a virtuous and superior tone; “don’t put it off upon me. I am very sorry to see it, and it’s a—it’s a bad side of human nature. I did intend to ask you to use any little opportunities you might have after I was gone, of improving dear Joe. But after this I ask you nothing. I am extremely sorry to see this in you, Biddy,” I repeated. “It’s a—it’s a bad side of human nature.”\n\n“Whether you scold me or approve of me,” returned poor Biddy, “you may equally depend upon my trying to do all that lies in my power, here, at all times. And whatever opinion you take away of me, shall make no difference in my remembrance of you. Yet a gentleman should not be unjust neither,” said Biddy, turning away her head.\n\nI again warmly repeated that it was a bad side of human nature (in which sentiment, waiving its application, I have since seen reason to think I was right), and I walked down the little path away from Biddy, and Biddy went into the house, and I went out at the garden gate and took a dejected stroll until supper-time; again feeling it very sorrowful and strange that this, the second night of my bright fortunes, should be as lonely and unsatisfactory as the first.\n\nBut, morning once more brightened my view, and I extended my clemency to Biddy, and we dropped the subject. Putting on the best clothes I had, I went into town as early as I could hope to find the shops open, and presented myself before Mr. Trabb, the tailor, who was having his breakfast in the parlour behind his shop, and who did not think it worth his while to come out to me, but called me in to him.\n\n“Well!” said Mr. Trabb, in a hail-fellow-well-met kind of way. “How are you, and what can I do for you?”\n\nMr. Trabb had sliced his hot roll into three feather-beds, and was slipping butter in between the blankets, and covering it up. He was a prosperous old bachelor, and his open window looked into a prosperous little garden and orchard, and there was a prosperous iron safe let into the wall at the side of his fireplace, and I did not doubt that heaps of his prosperity were put away in it in bags.\n\n“Mr. Trabb,” said I, “it’s an unpleasant thing to have to mention, because it looks like boasting; but I have come into a handsome property.”\n\nA change passed over Mr. Trabb. He forgot the butter in bed, got up from the bedside, and wiped his fingers on the tablecloth, exclaiming, “Lord bless my soul!”\n\n“I am going up to my guardian in London,” said I, casually drawing some guineas out of my pocket and looking at them; “and I want a fashionable suit of clothes to go in. I wish to pay for them,” I added—otherwise I thought he might only pretend to make them, “with ready money.”\n\n“My dear sir,” said Mr. Trabb, as he respectfully bent his body, opened his arms, and took the liberty of touching me on the outside of each elbow, “don’t hurt me by mentioning that. May I venture to congratulate you? Would you do me the favour of stepping into the shop?”\n\nMr. Trabb’s boy was the most audacious boy in all that country-side. When I had entered he was sweeping the shop, and he had sweetened his labours by sweeping over me. He was still sweeping when I came out into the shop with Mr. Trabb, and he knocked the broom against all possible corners and obstacles, to express (as I understood it) equality with any blacksmith, alive or dead.\n\n“Hold that noise,” said Mr. Trabb, with the greatest sternness, “or I’ll knock your head off!—Do me the favour to be seated, sir. Now, this,” said Mr. Trabb, taking down a roll of cloth, and tiding it out in a flowing manner over the counter, preparatory to getting his hand under it to show the gloss, “is a very sweet article. I can recommend it for your purpose, sir, because it really is extra super. But you shall see some others. Give me Number Four, you!” (To the boy, and with a dreadfully severe stare; foreseeing the danger of that miscreant’s brushing me with it, or making some other sign of familiarity.)\n\nMr. Trabb never removed his stern eye from the boy until he had deposited number four on the counter and was at a safe distance again. Then he commanded him to bring number five, and number eight. “And let me have none of your tricks here,” said Mr. Trabb, “or you shall repent it, you young scoundrel, the longest day you have to live.”\n\nMr. Trabb then bent over number four, and in a sort of deferential confidence recommended it to me as a light article for summer wear, an article much in vogue among the nobility and gentry, an article that it would ever be an honour to him to reflect upon a distinguished fellow-townsman’s (if he might claim me for a fellow-townsman) having worn. “Are you bringing numbers five and eight, you vagabond,” said Mr. Trabb to the boy after that, “or shall I kick you out of the shop and bring them myself?”\n\nI selected the materials for a suit, with the assistance of Mr. Trabb’s judgment, and re-entered the parlour to be measured. For although Mr. Trabb had my measure already, and had previously been quite contented with it, he said apologetically that it “wouldn’t do under existing circumstances, sir,—wouldn’t do at all.” So, Mr. Trabb measured and calculated me in the parlour, as if I were an estate and he the finest species of surveyor, and gave himself such a world of trouble that I felt that no suit of clothes could possibly remunerate him for his pains. When he had at last done and had appointed to send the articles to Mr. Pumblechook’s on the Thursday evening, he said, with his hand upon the parlour lock, “I know, sir, that London gentlemen cannot be expected to patronise local work, as a rule; but if you would give me a turn now and then in the quality of a townsman, I should greatly esteem it. Good-morning, sir, much obliged.—Door!”\n\nThe last word was flung at the boy, who had not the least notion what it meant. But I saw him collapse as his master rubbed me out with his hands, and my first decided experience of the stupendous power of money was, that it had morally laid upon his back Trabb’s boy.\n\nAfter this memorable event, I went to the hatter’s, and the bootmaker’s, and the hosier’s, and felt rather like Mother Hubbard’s dog whose outfit required the services of so many trades. I also went to the coach-office and took my place for seven o’clock on Saturday morning. It was not necessary to explain everywhere that I had come into a handsome property; but whenever I said anything to that effect, it followed that the officiating tradesman ceased to have his attention diverted through the window by the High Street, and concentrated his mind upon me. When I had ordered everything I wanted, I directed my steps towards Pumblechook’s, and, as I approached that gentleman’s place of business, I saw him standing at his door.\n\nHe was waiting for me with great impatience. He had been out early with the chaise-cart, and had called at the forge and heard the news. He had prepared a collation for me in the Barnwell parlour, and he too ordered his shopman to “come out of the gangway” as my sacred person passed.\n\n“My dear friend,” said Mr. Pumblechook, taking me by both hands, when he and I and the collation were alone, “I give you joy of your good fortune. Well deserved, well deserved!”\n\nThis was coming to the point, and I thought it a sensible way of expressing himself.\n\n“To think,” said Mr. Pumblechook, after snorting admiration at me for some moments, “that I should have been the humble instrument of leading up to this, is a proud reward.”\n\nI begged Mr. Pumblechook to remember that nothing was to be ever said or hinted, on that point.\n\n“My dear young friend,” said Mr. Pumblechook; “if you will allow me to call you so—”\n\nI murmured “Certainly,” and Mr. Pumblechook took me by both hands again, and communicated a movement to his waistcoat, which had an emotional appearance, though it was rather low down, “My dear young friend, rely upon my doing my little all in your absence, by keeping the fact before the mind of Joseph.—Joseph!” said Mr. Pumblechook, in the way of a compassionate adjuration. “Joseph!! Joseph!!!” Thereupon he shook his head and tapped it, expressing his sense of deficiency in Joseph.\n\n“But my dear young friend,” said Mr. Pumblechook, “you must be hungry, you must be exhausted. Be seated. Here is a chicken had round from the Boar, here is a tongue had round from the Boar, here’s one or two little things had round from the Boar, that I hope you may not despise. But do I,” said Mr. Pumblechook, getting up again the moment after he had sat down, “see afore me, him as I ever sported with in his times of happy infancy? And may I—may I—?”\n\nThis May I, meant might he shake hands? I consented, and he was fervent, and then sat down again.\n\n“Here is wine,” said Mr. Pumblechook. “Let us drink, Thanks to Fortune, and may she ever pick out her favourites with equal judgment! And yet I cannot,” said Mr. Pumblechook, getting up again, “see afore me One—and likewise drink to One—without again expressing—May I—may I—?”\n\nI said he might, and he shook hands with me again, and emptied his glass and turned it upside down. I did the same; and if I had turned myself upside down before drinking, the wine could not have gone more direct to my head.\n\nMr. Pumblechook helped me to the liver wing, and to the best slice of tongue (none of those out-of-the-way No Thoroughfares of Pork now), and took, comparatively speaking, no care of himself at all. “Ah! poultry, poultry! You little thought,” said Mr. Pumblechook, apostrophising the fowl in the dish, “when you was a young fledgling, what was in store for you. You little thought you was to be refreshment beneath this humble roof for one as—Call it a weakness, if you will,” said Mr. Pumblechook, getting up again, “but may I? may I—?”\n\nIt began to be unnecessary to repeat the form of saying he might, so he did it at once. How he ever did it so often without wounding himself with my knife, I don’t know.\n\n“And your sister,” he resumed, after a little steady eating, “which had the honour of bringing you up by hand! It’s a sad picter, to reflect that she’s no longer equal to fully understanding the honour. May—”\n\nI saw he was about to come at me again, and I stopped him.\n\n“We’ll drink her health,” said I.\n\n“Ah!” cried Mr. Pumblechook, leaning back in his chair, quite flaccid with admiration, “that’s the way you know ’em, sir!” (I don’t know who Sir was, but he certainly was not I, and there was no third person present); “that’s the way you know the noble-minded, sir! Ever forgiving and ever affable. It might,” said the servile Pumblechook, putting down his untasted glass in a hurry and getting up again, “to a common person, have the appearance of repeating—but may I—?”\n\nWhen he had done it, he resumed his seat and drank to my sister. “Let us never be blind,” said Mr. Pumblechook, “to her faults of temper, but it is to be hoped she meant well.”\n\nAt about this time, I began to observe that he was getting flushed in the face; as to myself, I felt all face, steeped in wine and smarting.\n\nI mentioned to Mr. Pumblechook that I wished to have my new clothes sent to his house, and he was ecstatic on my so distinguishing him. I mentioned my reason for desiring to avoid observation in the village, and he lauded it to the skies. There was nobody but himself, he intimated, worthy of my confidence, and—in short, might he? Then he asked me tenderly if I remembered our boyish games at sums, and how we had gone together to have me bound apprentice, and, in effect, how he had ever been my favourite fancy and my chosen friend? If I had taken ten times as many glasses of wine as I had, I should have known that he never had stood in that relation towards me, and should in my heart of hearts have repudiated the idea. Yet for all that, I remember feeling convinced that I had been much mistaken in him, and that he was a sensible, practical, good-hearted prime fellow.\n\nBy degrees he fell to reposing such great confidence in me, as to ask my advice in reference to his own affairs. He mentioned that there was an opportunity for a great amalgamation and monopoly of the corn and seed trade on those premises, if enlarged, such as had never occurred before in that or any other neighbourhood. What alone was wanting to the realisation of a vast fortune, he considered to be More Capital. Those were the two little words, more capital. Now it appeared to him (Pumblechook) that if that capital were got into the business, through a sleeping partner, sir,—which sleeping partner would have nothing to do but walk in, by self or deputy, whenever he pleased, and examine the books,—and walk in twice a year and take his profits away in his pocket, to the tune of fifty per cent,—it appeared to him that that might be an opening for a young gentleman of spirit combined with property, which would be worthy of his attention. But what did I think? He had great confidence in my opinion, and what did I think? I gave it as my opinion. “Wait a bit!” The united vastness and distinctness of this view so struck him, that he no longer asked if he might shake hands with me, but said he really must,—and did.\n\nWe drank all the wine, and Mr. Pumblechook pledged himself over and over again to keep Joseph up to the mark (I don’t know what mark), and to render me efficient and constant service (I don’t know what service). He also made known to me for the first time in my life, and certainly after having kept his secret wonderfully well, that he had always said of me, “That boy is no common boy, and mark me, his fortun’ will be no common fortun’.” He said with a tearful smile that it was a singular thing to think of now, and I said so too. Finally, I went out into the air, with a dim perception that there was something unwonted in the conduct of the sunshine, and found that I had slumberously got to the turnpike without having taken any account of the road.\n\nThere, I was roused by Mr. Pumblechook’s hailing me. He was a long way down the sunny street, and was making expressive gestures for me to stop. I stopped, and he came up breathless.\n\n“No, my dear friend,” said he, when he had recovered wind for speech. “Not if I can help it. This occasion shall not entirely pass without that affability on your part.—May I, as an old friend and well-wisher? May I?”\n\nWe shook hands for the hundredth time at least, and he ordered a young carter out of my way with the greatest indignation. Then, he blessed me and stood waving his hand to me until I had passed the crook in the road; and then I turned into a field and had a long nap under a hedge before I pursued my way home.\n\nI had scant luggage to take with me to London, for little of the little I possessed was adapted to my new station. But I began packing that same afternoon, and wildly packed up things that I knew I should want next morning, in a fiction that there was not a moment to be lost.\n\nSo, Tuesday, Wednesday, and Thursday, passed; and on Friday morning I went to Mr. Pumblechook’s, to put on my new clothes and pay my visit to Miss Havisham. Mr. Pumblechook’s own room was given up to me to dress in, and was decorated with clean towels expressly for the event. My clothes were rather a disappointment, of course. Probably every new and eagerly expected garment ever put on since clothes came in, fell a trifle short of the wearer’s expectation. But after I had had my new suit on some half an hour, and had gone through an immensity of posturing with Mr. Pumblechook’s very limited dressing-glass, in the futile endeavour to see my legs, it seemed to fit me better. It being market morning at a neighbouring town some ten miles off, Mr. Pumblechook was not at home. I had not told him exactly when I meant to leave, and was not likely to shake hands with him again before departing. This was all as it should be, and I went out in my new array, fearfully ashamed of having to pass the shopman, and suspicious after all that I was at a personal disadvantage, something like Joe’s in his Sunday suit.\n\nI went circuitously to Miss Havisham’s by all the back ways, and rang at the bell constrainedly, on account of the stiff long fingers of my gloves. Sarah Pocket came to the gate, and positively reeled back when she saw me so changed; her walnut-shell countenance likewise turned from brown to green and yellow.\n\n“You?” said she. “You? Good gracious! What do you want?”\n\n“I am going to London, Miss Pocket,” said I, “and want to say good-bye to Miss Havisham.”\n\nI was not expected, for she left me locked in the yard, while she went to ask if I were to be admitted. After a very short delay, she returned and took me up, staring at me all the way.\n\nMiss Havisham was taking exercise in the room with the long spread table, leaning on her crutch stick. The room was lighted as of yore, and at the sound of our entrance, she stopped and turned. She was then just abreast of the rotted bride-cake.\n\n“Don’t go, Sarah,” she said. “Well, Pip?”\n\n“I start for London, Miss Havisham, to-morrow,” I was exceedingly careful what I said, “and I thought you would kindly not mind my taking leave of you.”\n\n“This is a gay figure, Pip,” said she, making her crutch stick play round me, as if she, the fairy godmother who had changed me, were bestowing the finishing gift.\n\n“I have come into such good fortune since I saw you last, Miss Havisham,” I murmured. “And I am so grateful for it, Miss Havisham!”\n\n“Ay, ay!” said she, looking at the discomfited and envious Sarah, with delight. “I have seen Mr. Jaggers. I have heard about it, Pip. So you go to-morrow?”\n\n“Yes, Miss Havisham.”\n\n“And you are adopted by a rich person?”\n\n“Yes, Miss Havisham.”\n\n“Not named?”\n\n“No, Miss Havisham.”\n\n“And Mr. Jaggers is made your guardian?”\n\n“Yes, Miss Havisham.”\n\nShe quite gloated on these questions and answers, so keen was her enjoyment of Sarah Pocket’s jealous dismay. “Well!” she went on; “you have a promising career before you. Be good—deserve it—and abide by Mr. Jaggers’s instructions.” She looked at me, and looked at Sarah, and Sarah’s countenance wrung out of her watchful face a cruel smile. “Good-bye, Pip!—you will always keep the name of Pip, you know.”\n\n“Yes, Miss Havisham.”\n\n“Good-bye, Pip!”\n\nShe stretched out her hand, and I went down on my knee and put it to my lips. I had not considered how I should take leave of her; it came naturally to me at the moment to do this. She looked at Sarah Pocket with triumph in her weird eyes, and so I left my fairy godmother, with both her hands on her crutch stick, standing in the midst of the dimly lighted room beside the rotten bride-cake that was hidden in cobwebs.\n\nSarah Pocket conducted me down, as if I were a ghost who must be seen out. She could not get over my appearance, and was in the last degree confounded. I said “Good-bye, Miss Pocket;” but she merely stared, and did not seem collected enough to know that I had spoken. Clear of the house, I made the best of my way back to Pumblechook’s, took off my new clothes, made them into a bundle, and went back home in my older dress, carrying it—to speak the truth—much more at my ease too, though I had the bundle to carry.\n\nAnd now, those six days which were to have run out so slowly, had run out fast and were gone, and to-morrow looked me in the face more steadily than I could look at it. As the six evenings had dwindled away, to five, to four, to three, to two, I had become more and more appreciative of the society of Joe and Biddy. On this last evening, I dressed myself out in my new clothes for their delight, and sat in my splendour until bedtime. We had a hot supper on the occasion, graced by the inevitable roast fowl, and we had some flip to finish with. We were all very low, and none the higher for pretending to be in spirits.\n\nI was to leave our village at five in the morning, carrying my little hand-portmanteau, and I had told Joe that I wished to walk away all alone. I am afraid—sore afraid—that this purpose originated in my sense of the contrast there would be between me and Joe, if we went to the coach together. I had pretended with myself that there was nothing of this taint in the arrangement; but when I went up to my little room on this last night, I felt compelled to admit that it might be so, and had an impulse upon me to go down again and entreat Joe to walk with me in the morning. I did not.\n\nAll night there were coaches in my broken sleep, going to wrong places instead of to London, and having in the traces, now dogs, now cats, now pigs, now men,—never horses. Fantastic failures of journeys occupied me until the day dawned and the birds were singing. Then, I got up and partly dressed, and sat at the window to take a last look out, and in taking it fell asleep.\n\nBiddy was astir so early to get my breakfast, that, although I did not sleep at the window an hour, I smelt the smoke of the kitchen fire when I started up with a terrible idea that it must be late in the afternoon. But long after that, and long after I had heard the clinking of the teacups and was quite ready, I wanted the resolution to go downstairs. After all, I remained up there, repeatedly unlocking and unstrapping my small portmanteau and locking and strapping it up again, until Biddy called to me that I was late.\n\nIt was a hurried breakfast with no taste in it. I got up from the meal, saying with a sort of briskness, as if it had only just occurred to me, “Well! I suppose I must be off!” and then I kissed my sister who was laughing and nodding and shaking in her usual chair, and kissed Biddy, and threw my arms around Joe’s neck. Then I took up my little portmanteau and walked out. The last I saw of them was, when I presently heard a scuffle behind me, and looking back, saw Joe throwing an old shoe after me and Biddy throwing another old shoe. I stopped then, to wave my hat, and dear old Joe waved his strong right arm above his head, crying huskily “Hooroar!” and Biddy put her apron to her face.\n\nI walked away at a good pace, thinking it was easier to go than I had supposed it would be, and reflecting that it would never have done to have had an old shoe thrown after the coach, in sight of all the High Street. I whistled and made nothing of going. But the village was very peaceful and quiet, and the light mists were solemnly rising, as if to show me the world, and I had been so innocent and little there, and all beyond was so unknown and great, that in a moment with a strong heave and sob I broke into tears. It was by the finger-post at the end of the village, and I laid my hand upon it, and said, “Good-bye, O my dear, dear friend!”\n\nHeaven knows we need never be ashamed of our tears, for they are rain upon the blinding dust of earth, overlying our hard hearts. I was better after I had cried than before,—more sorry, more aware of my own ingratitude, more gentle. If I had cried before, I should have had Joe with me then.\n\nSo subdued I was by those tears, and by their breaking out again in the course of the quiet walk, that when I was on the coach, and it was clear of the town, I deliberated with an aching heart whether I would not get down when we changed horses and walk back, and have another evening at home, and a better parting. We changed, and I had not made up my mind, and still reflected for my comfort that it would be quite practicable to get down and walk back, when we changed again. And while I was occupied with these deliberations, I would fancy an exact resemblance to Joe in some man coming along the road towards us, and my heart would beat high.—As if he could possibly be there!\n\nWe changed again, and yet again, and it was now too late and too far to go back, and I went on. And the mists had all solemnly risen now, and the world lay spread before me.\n\n# Chapter XX.\n\nThe journey from our town to the metropolis was a journey of about five hours. It was a little past midday when the four-horse stage-coach by which I was a passenger, got into the ravel of traffic frayed out about the Cross Keys, Wood Street, Cheapside, London.\n\nWe Britons had at that time particularly settled that it was treasonable to doubt our having and our being the best of everything: otherwise, while I was scared by the immensity of London, I think I might have had some faint doubts whether it was not rather ugly, crooked, narrow, and dirty.\n\nMr. Jaggers had duly sent me his address; it was, Little Britain, and he had written after it on his card, “just out of Smithfield, and close by the coach-office.” Nevertheless, a hackney-coachman, who seemed to have as many capes to his greasy great-coat as he was years old, packed me up in his coach and hemmed me in with a folding and jingling barrier of steps, as if he were going to take me fifty miles. His getting on his box, which I remember to have been decorated with an old weather-stained pea-green hammercloth moth-eaten into rags, was quite a work of time. It was a wonderful equipage, with six great coronets outside, and ragged things behind for I don’t know how many footmen to hold on by, and a harrow below them, to prevent amateur footmen from yielding to the temptation.\n\nI had scarcely had time to enjoy the coach and to think how like a straw-yard it was, and yet how like a rag-shop, and to wonder why the horses’ nose-bags were kept inside, when I observed the coachman beginning to get down, as if we were going to stop presently. And stop we presently did, in a gloomy street, at certain offices with an open door, whereon was painted MR. JAGGERS.\n\n“How much?” I asked the coachman.\n\nThe coachman answered, “A shilling—unless you wish to make it more.”\n\nI naturally said I had no wish to make it more.\n\n“Then it must be a shilling,” observed the coachman. “I don’t want to get into trouble. I know him!” He darkly closed an eye at Mr. Jaggers’s name, and shook his head.\n\nWhen he had got his shilling, and had in course of time completed the ascent to his box, and had got away (which appeared to relieve his mind), I went into the front office with my little portmanteau in my hand and asked, Was Mr. Jaggers at home?\n\n“He is not,” returned the clerk. “He is in Court at present. Am I addressing Mr. Pip?”\n\nI signified that he was addressing Mr. Pip.\n\n“Mr. Jaggers left word, would you wait in his room. He couldn’t say how long he might be, having a case on. But it stands to reason, his time being valuable, that he won’t be longer than he can help.”\n\nWith those words, the clerk opened a door, and ushered me into an inner chamber at the back. Here, we found a gentleman with one eye, in a velveteen suit and knee-breeches, who wiped his nose with his sleeve on being interrupted in the perusal of the newspaper.\n\n“Go and wait outside, Mike,” said the clerk.\n\nI began to say that I hoped I was not interrupting, when the clerk shoved this gentleman out with as little ceremony as I ever saw used, and tossing his fur cap out after him, left me alone.\n\nMr. Jaggers’s room was lighted by a skylight only, and was a most dismal place; the skylight, eccentrically pitched like a broken head, and the distorted adjoining houses looking as if they had twisted themselves to peep down at me through it. There were not so many papers about, as I should have expected to see; and there were some odd objects about, that I should not have expected to see,—such as an old rusty pistol, a sword in a scabbard, several strange-looking boxes and packages, and two dreadful casts on a shelf, of faces peculiarly swollen, and twitchy about the nose. Mr. Jaggers’s own high-backed chair was of deadly black horsehair, with rows of brass nails round it, like a coffin; and I fancied I could see how he leaned back in it, and bit his forefinger at the clients. The room was but small, and the clients seemed to have had a habit of backing up against the wall; the wall, especially opposite to Mr. Jaggers’s chair, being greasy with shoulders. I recalled, too, that the one-eyed gentleman had shuffled forth against the wall when I was the innocent cause of his being turned out.\n\nI sat down in the cliental chair placed over against Mr. Jaggers’s chair, and became fascinated by the dismal atmosphere of the place. I called to mind that the clerk had the same air of knowing something to everybody else’s disadvantage, as his master had. I wondered how many other clerks there were upstairs, and whether they all claimed to have the same detrimental mastery of their fellow-creatures. I wondered what was the history of all the odd litter about the room, and how it came there. I wondered whether the two swollen faces were of Mr. Jaggers’s family, and, if he were so unfortunate as to have had a pair of such ill-looking relations, why he stuck them on that dusty perch for the blacks and flies to settle on, instead of giving them a place at home. Of course I had no experience of a London summer day, and my spirits may have been oppressed by the hot exhausted air, and by the dust and grit that lay thick on everything. But I sat wondering and waiting in Mr. Jaggers’s close room, until I really could not bear the two casts on the shelf above Mr. Jaggers’s chair, and got up and went out.\n\nWhen I told the clerk that I would take a turn in the air while I waited, he advised me to go round the corner and I should come into Smithfield. So I came into Smithfield; and the shameful place, being all asmear with filth and fat and blood and foam, seemed to stick to me. So, I rubbed it off with all possible speed by turning into a street where I saw the great black dome of Saint Paul’s bulging at me from behind a grim stone building which a bystander said was Newgate Prison. Following the wall of the jail, I found the roadway covered with straw to deaden the noise of passing vehicles; and from this, and from the quantity of people standing about smelling strongly of spirits and beer, I inferred that the trials were on.\n\nWhile I looked about me here, an exceedingly dirty and partially drunk minister of justice asked me if I would like to step in and hear a trial or so: informing me that he could give me a front place for half a crown, whence I should command a full view of the Lord Chief Justice in his wig and robes,—mentioning that awful personage like waxwork, and presently offering him at the reduced price of eighteen-pence. As I declined the proposal on the plea of an appointment, he was so good as to take me into a yard and show me where the gallows was kept, and also where people were publicly whipped, and then he showed me the Debtors’ Door, out of which culprits came to be hanged; heightening the interest of that dreadful portal by giving me to understand that “four on ’em” would come out at that door the day after to-morrow at eight in the morning, to be killed in a row. This was horrible, and gave me a sickening idea of London; the more so as the Lord Chief Justice’s proprietor wore (from his hat down to his boots and up again to his pocket-handkerchief inclusive) mildewed clothes which had evidently not belonged to him originally, and which I took it into my head he had bought cheap of the executioner. Under these circumstances I thought myself well rid of him for a shilling.\n\nI dropped into the office to ask if Mr. Jaggers had come in yet, and I found he had not, and I strolled out again. This time, I made the tour of Little Britain, and turned into Bartholomew Close; and now I became aware that other people were waiting about for Mr. Jaggers, as well as I. There were two men of secret appearance lounging in Bartholomew Close, and thoughtfully fitting their feet into the cracks of the pavement as they talked together, one of whom said to the other when they first passed me, that “Jaggers would do it if it was to be done.” There was a knot of three men and two women standing at a corner, and one of the women was crying on her dirty shawl, and the other comforted her by saying, as she pulled her own shawl over her shoulders, “Jaggers is for him, ’Melia, and what more could you have?” There was a red-eyed little Jew who came into the Close while I was loitering there, in company with a second little Jew whom he sent upon an errand; and while the messenger was gone, I remarked this Jew, who was of a highly excitable temperament, performing a jig of anxiety under a lamp-post and accompanying himself, in a kind of frenzy, with the words, “O Jaggerth, Jaggerth, Jaggerth! all otherth ith Cag-Maggerth, give me Jaggerth!” These testimonies to the popularity of my guardian made a deep impression on me, and I admired and wondered more than ever.\n\nAt length, as I was looking out at the iron gate of Bartholomew Close into Little Britain, I saw Mr. Jaggers coming across the road towards me. All the others who were waiting saw him at the same time, and there was quite a rush at him. Mr. Jaggers, putting a hand on my shoulder and walking me on at his side without saying anything to me, addressed himself to his followers.\n\nFirst, he took the two secret men.\n\n“Now, I have nothing to say to you,” said Mr. Jaggers, throwing his finger at them. “I want to know no more than I know. As to the result, it’s a toss-up. I told you from the first it was a toss-up. Have you paid Wemmick?”\n\n“We made the money up this morning, sir,” said one of the men, submissively, while the other perused Mr. Jaggers’s face.\n\n“I don’t ask you when you made it up, or where, or whether you made it up at all. Has Wemmick got it?”\n\n“Yes, sir,” said both the men together.\n\n“Very well; then you may go. Now, I won’t have it!” said Mr Jaggers, waving his hand at them to put them behind him. “If you say a word to me, I’ll throw up the case.”\n\n“We thought, Mr. Jaggers—” one of the men began, pulling off his hat.\n\n“That’s what I told you not to do,” said Mr. Jaggers. “You thought! I think for you; that’s enough for you. If I want you, I know where to find you; I don’t want you to find me. Now I won’t have it. I won’t hear a word.”\n\nThe two men looked at one another as Mr. Jaggers waved them behind again, and humbly fell back and were heard no more.\n\n“And now you!” said Mr. Jaggers, suddenly stopping, and turning on the two women with the shawls, from whom the three men had meekly separated,—“Oh! Amelia, is it?”\n\n“Yes, Mr. Jaggers.”\n\n“And do you remember,” retorted Mr. Jaggers, “that but for me you wouldn’t be here and couldn’t be here?”\n\n“O yes, sir!” exclaimed both women together. “Lord bless you, sir, well we knows that!”\n\n“Then why,” said Mr. Jaggers, “do you come here?”\n\n“My Bill, sir!” the crying woman pleaded.\n\n“Now, I tell you what!” said Mr. Jaggers. “Once for all. If you don’t know that your Bill’s in good hands, I know it. And if you come here bothering about your Bill, I’ll make an example of both your Bill and you, and let him slip through my fingers. Have you paid Wemmick?”\n\n“O yes, sir! Every farden.”\n\n“Very well. Then you have done all you have got to do. Say another word—one single word—and Wemmick shall give you your money back.”\n\nThis terrible threat caused the two women to fall off immediately. No one remained now but the excitable Jew, who had already raised the skirts of Mr. Jaggers’s coat to his lips several times.\n\n“I don’t know this man!” said Mr. Jaggers, in the same devastating strain: “What does this fellow want?”\n\n“Ma thear Mithter Jaggerth. Hown brother to Habraham Latharuth?”\n\n“Who’s he?” said Mr. Jaggers. “Let go of my coat.”\n\nThe suitor, kissing the hem of the garment again before relinquishing it, replied, “Habraham Latharuth, on thuthpithion of plate.”\n\n“You’re too late,” said Mr. Jaggers. “I am over the way.”\n\n“Holy father, Mithter Jaggerth!” cried my excitable acquaintance, turning white, “don’t thay you’re again Habraham Latharuth!”\n\n“I am,” said Mr. Jaggers, “and there’s an end of it. Get out of the way.”\n\n“Mithter Jaggerth! Half a moment! My hown cuthen’th gone to Mithter Wemmick at thith prethent minute, to hoffer him hany termth. Mithter Jaggerth! Half a quarter of a moment! If you’d have the condethenthun to be bought off from the t’other thide—at hany thuperior prithe!—money no object!—Mithter Jaggerth—Mithter—!”\n\nMy guardian threw his supplicant off with supreme indifference, and left him dancing on the pavement as if it were red hot. Without further interruption, we reached the front office, where we found the clerk and the man in velveteen with the fur cap.\n\n“Here’s Mike,” said the clerk, getting down from his stool, and approaching Mr. Jaggers confidentially.\n\n“Oh!” said Mr. Jaggers, turning to the man, who was pulling a lock of hair in the middle of his forehead, like the Bull in Cock Robin pulling at the bell-rope; “your man comes on this afternoon. Well?”\n\n“Well, Mas’r Jaggers,” returned Mike, in the voice of a sufferer from a constitutional cold; “arter a deal o’ trouble, I’ve found one, sir, as might do.”\n\n“What is he prepared to swear?”\n\n“Well, Mas’r Jaggers,” said Mike, wiping his nose on his fur cap this time; “in a general way, anythink.”\n\nMr. Jaggers suddenly became most irate. “Now, I warned you before,” said he, throwing his forefinger at the terrified client, “that if you ever presumed to talk in that way here, I’d make an example of you. You infernal scoundrel, how dare you tell ME that?”\n\nThe client looked scared, but bewildered too, as if he were unconscious what he had done.\n\n“Spooney!” said the clerk, in a low voice, giving him a stir with his elbow. “Soft Head! Need you say it face to face?”\n\n“Now, I ask you, you blundering booby,” said my guardian, very sternly, “once more and for the last time, what the man you have brought here is prepared to swear?”\n\nMike looked hard at my guardian, as if he were trying to learn a lesson from his face, and slowly replied, “Ayther to character, or to having been in his company and never left him all the night in question.”\n\n“Now, be careful. In what station of life is this man?”\n\nMike looked at his cap, and looked at the floor, and looked at the ceiling, and looked at the clerk, and even looked at me, before beginning to reply in a nervous manner, “We’ve dressed him up like—” when my guardian blustered out,—\n\n“What? You WILL, will you?”\n\n(“Spooney!” added the clerk again, with another stir.)\n\nAfter some helpless casting about, Mike brightened and began again:—\n\n“He is dressed like a ’spectable pieman. A sort of a pastry-cook.”\n\n“Is he here?” asked my guardian.\n\n“I left him,” said Mike, “a setting on some doorsteps round the corner.”\n\n“Take him past that window, and let me see him.”\n\nThe window indicated was the office window. We all three went to it, behind the wire blind, and presently saw the client go by in an accidental manner, with a murderous-looking tall individual, in a short suit of white linen and a paper cap. This guileless confectioner was not by any means sober, and had a black eye in the green stage of recovery, which was painted over.\n\n“Tell him to take his witness away directly,” said my guardian to the clerk, in extreme disgust, “and ask him what he means by bringing such a fellow as that.”\n\nMy guardian then took me into his own room, and while he lunched, standing, from a sandwich-box and a pocket-flask of sherry (he seemed to bully his very sandwich as he ate it), informed me what arrangements he had made for me. I was to go to “Barnard’s Inn,” to young Mr. Pocket’s rooms, where a bed had been sent in for my accommodation; I was to remain with young Mr. Pocket until Monday; on Monday I was to go with him to his father’s house on a visit, that I might try how I liked it. Also, I was told what my allowance was to be,—it was a very liberal one,—and had handed to me from one of my guardian’s drawers, the cards of certain tradesmen with whom I was to deal for all kinds of clothes, and such other things as I could in reason want. “You will find your credit good, Mr. Pip,” said my guardian, whose flask of sherry smelt like a whole caskful, as he hastily refreshed himself, “but I shall by this means be able to check your bills, and to pull you up if I find you outrunning the constable. Of course you’ll go wrong somehow, but that’s no fault of mine.”\n\nAfter I had pondered a little over this encouraging sentiment, I asked Mr. Jaggers if I could send for a coach? He said it was not worth while, I was so near my destination; Wemmick should walk round with me, if I pleased.\n\nI then found that Wemmick was the clerk in the next room. Another clerk was rung down from upstairs to take his place while he was out, and I accompanied him into the street, after shaking hands with my guardian. We found a new set of people lingering outside, but Wemmick made a way among them by saying coolly yet decisively, “I tell you it’s no use; he won’t have a word to say to one of you;” and we soon got clear of them, and went on side by side.\n\n# Chapter XXI.\n\nCasting my eyes on Mr. Wemmick as we went along, to see what he was like in the light of day, I found him to be a dry man, rather short in stature, with a square wooden face, whose expression seemed to have been imperfectly chipped out with a dull-edged chisel. There were some marks in it that might have been dimples, if the material had been softer and the instrument finer, but which, as it was, were only dints. The chisel had made three or four of these attempts at embellishment over his nose, but had given them up without an effort to smooth them off. I judged him to be a bachelor from the frayed condition of his linen, and he appeared to have sustained a good many bereavements; for he wore at least four mourning rings, besides a brooch representing a lady and a weeping willow at a tomb with an urn on it. I noticed, too, that several rings and seals hung at his watch-chain, as if he were quite laden with remembrances of departed friends. He had glittering eyes,—small, keen, and black,—and thin wide mottled lips. He had had them, to the best of my belief, from forty to fifty years.\n\n“So you were never in London before?” said Mr. Wemmick to me.\n\n“No,” said I.\n\n“I was new here once,” said Mr. Wemmick. “Rum to think of now!”\n\n“You are well acquainted with it now?”\n\n“Why, yes,” said Mr. Wemmick. “I know the moves of it.”\n\n“Is it a very wicked place?” I asked, more for the sake of saying something than for information.\n\n“You may get cheated, robbed, and murdered in London. But there are plenty of people anywhere, who’ll do that for you.”\n\n“If there is bad blood between you and them,” said I, to soften it off a little.\n\n“O! I don’t know about bad blood,” returned Mr. Wemmick; “there’s not much bad blood about. They’ll do it, if there’s anything to be got by it.”\n\n“That makes it worse.”\n\n“You think so?” returned Mr. Wemmick. “Much about the same, I should say.”\n\nHe wore his hat on the back of his head, and looked straight before him: walking in a self-contained way as if there were nothing in the streets to claim his attention. His mouth was such a post-office of a mouth that he had a mechanical appearance of smiling. We had got to the top of Holborn Hill before I knew that it was merely a mechanical appearance, and that he was not smiling at all.\n\n“Do you know where Mr. Matthew Pocket lives?” I asked Mr. Wemmick.\n\n“Yes,” said he, nodding in the direction. “At Hammersmith, west of London.”\n\n“Is that far?”\n\n“Well! Say five miles.”\n\n“Do you know him?”\n\n“Why, you’re a regular cross-examiner!” said Mr. Wemmick, looking at me with an approving air. “Yes, I know him. I know him!”\n\nThere was an air of toleration or depreciation about his utterance of these words that rather depressed me; and I was still looking sideways at his block of a face in search of any encouraging note to the text, when he said here we were at Barnard’s Inn. My depression was not alleviated by the announcement, for, I had supposed that establishment to be an hotel kept by Mr. Barnard, to which the Blue Boar in our town was a mere public-house. Whereas I now found Barnard to be a disembodied spirit, or a fiction, and his inn the dingiest collection of shabby buildings ever squeezed together in a rank corner as a club for Tom-cats.\n\nWe entered this haven through a wicket-gate, and were disgorged by an introductory passage into a melancholy little square that looked to me like a flat burying-ground. I thought it had the most dismal trees in it, and the most dismal sparrows, and the most dismal cats, and the most dismal houses (in number half a dozen or so), that I had ever seen. I thought the windows of the sets of chambers into which those houses were divided were in every stage of dilapidated blind and curtain, crippled flower-pot, cracked glass, dusty decay, and miserable makeshift; while To Let, To Let, To Let, glared at me from empty rooms, as if no new wretches ever came there, and the vengeance of the soul of Barnard were being slowly appeased by the gradual suicide of the present occupants and their unholy interment under the gravel. A frowzy mourning of soot and smoke attired this forlorn creation of Barnard, and it had strewn ashes on its head, and was undergoing penance and humiliation as a mere dust-hole. Thus far my sense of sight; while dry rot and wet rot and all the silent rots that rot in neglected roof and cellar,—rot of rat and mouse and bug and coaching-stables near at hand besides—addressed themselves faintly to my sense of smell, and moaned, “Try Barnard’s Mixture.”\n\nSo imperfect was this realisation of the first of my great expectations, that I looked in dismay at Mr. Wemmick. “Ah!” said he, mistaking me; “the retirement reminds you of the country. So it does me.”\n\nHe led me into a corner and conducted me up a flight of stairs,—which appeared to me to be slowly collapsing into sawdust, so that one of those days the upper lodgers would look out at their doors and find themselves without the means of coming down,—to a set of chambers on the top floor. MR. POCKET, JUN., was painted on the door, and there was a label on the letter-box, “Return shortly.”\n\n“He hardly thought you’d come so soon,” Mr. Wemmick explained. “You don’t want me any more?”\n\n“No, thank you,” said I.\n\n“As I keep the cash,” Mr. Wemmick observed, “we shall most likely meet pretty often. Good day.”\n\n“Good day.”\n\nI put out my hand, and Mr. Wemmick at first looked at it as if he thought I wanted something. Then he looked at me, and said, correcting himself,—\n\n“To be sure! Yes. You’re in the habit of shaking hands?”\n\nI was rather confused, thinking it must be out of the London fashion, but said yes.\n\n“I have got so out of it!” said Mr. Wemmick,—“except at last. Very glad, I’m sure, to make your acquaintance. Good day!”\n\nWhen we had shaken hands and he was gone, I opened the staircase window and had nearly beheaded myself, for, the lines had rotted away, and it came down like the guillotine. Happily it was so quick that I had not put my head out. After this escape, I was content to take a foggy view of the Inn through the window’s encrusting dirt, and to stand dolefully looking out, saying to myself that London was decidedly overrated.\n\nMr. Pocket, Junior’s, idea of Shortly was not mine, for I had nearly maddened myself with looking out for half an hour, and had written my name with my finger several times in the dirt of every pane in the window, before I heard footsteps on the stairs. Gradually there arose before me the hat, head, neckcloth, waistcoat, trousers, boots, of a member of society of about my own standing. He had a paper-bag under each arm and a pottle of strawberries in one hand, and was out of breath.\n\n“Mr. Pip?” said he.\n\n“Mr. Pocket?” said I.\n\n“Dear me!” he exclaimed. “I am extremely sorry; but I knew there was a coach from your part of the country at midday, and I thought you would come by that one. The fact is, I have been out on your account,—not that that is any excuse,—for I thought, coming from the country, you might like a little fruit after dinner, and I went to Covent Garden Market to get it good.”\n\nFor a reason that I had, I felt as if my eyes would start out of my head. I acknowledged his attention incoherently, and began to think this was a dream.\n\n“Dear me!” said Mr. Pocket, Junior. “This door sticks so!”\n\nAs he was fast making jam of his fruit by wrestling with the door while the paper-bags were under his arms, I begged him to allow me to hold them. He relinquished them with an agreeable smile, and combated with the door as if it were a wild beast. It yielded so suddenly at last, that he staggered back upon me, and I staggered back upon the opposite door, and we both laughed. But still I felt as if my eyes must start out of my head, and as if this must be a dream.\n\n“Pray come in,” said Mr. Pocket, Junior. “Allow me to lead the way. I am rather bare here, but I hope you’ll be able to make out tolerably well till Monday. My father thought you would get on more agreeably through to-morrow with me than with him, and might like to take a walk about London. I am sure I shall be very happy to show London to you. As to our table, you won’t find that bad, I hope, for it will be supplied from our coffee-house here, and (it is only right I should add) at your expense, such being Mr. Jaggers’s directions. As to our lodging, it’s not by any means splendid, because I have my own bread to earn, and my father hasn’t anything to give me, and I shouldn’t be willing to take it, if he had. This is our sitting-room,—just such chairs and tables and carpet and so forth, you see, as they could spare from home. You mustn’t give me credit for the tablecloth and spoons and castors, because they come for you from the coffee-house. This is my little bedroom; rather musty, but Barnard’s is musty. This is your bedroom; the furniture’s hired for the occasion, but I trust it will answer the purpose; if you should want anything, I’ll go and fetch it. The chambers are retired, and we shall be alone together, but we shan’t fight, I dare say. But dear me, I beg your pardon, you’re holding the fruit all this time. Pray let me take these bags from you. I am quite ashamed.”\n\nAs I stood opposite to Mr. Pocket, Junior, delivering him the bags, One, Two, I saw the starting appearance come into his own eyes that I knew to be in mine, and he said, falling back,—\n\n“Lord bless me, you’re the prowling boy!”\n\n“And you,” said I, “are the pale young gentleman!”\n\n# Chapter XXII.\n\nThe pale young gentleman and I stood contemplating one another in Barnard’s Inn, until we both burst out laughing. “The idea of its being you!” said he. “The idea of its being you!” said I. And then we contemplated one another afresh, and laughed again. “Well!” said the pale young gentleman, reaching out his hand good-humouredly, “it’s all over now, I hope, and it will be magnanimous in you if you’ll forgive me for having knocked you about so.”\n\nI derived from this speech that Mr. Herbert Pocket (for Herbert was the pale young gentleman’s name) still rather confounded his intention with his execution. But I made a modest reply, and we shook hands warmly.\n\n“You hadn’t come into your good fortune at that time?” said Herbert Pocket.\n\n“No,” said I.\n\n“No,” he acquiesced: “I heard it had happened very lately. I was rather on the lookout for good fortune then.”\n\n“Indeed?”\n\n“Yes. Miss Havisham had sent for me, to see if she could take a fancy to me. But she couldn’t,—at all events, she didn’t.”\n\nI thought it polite to remark that I was surprised to hear that.\n\n“Bad taste,” said Herbert, laughing, “but a fact. Yes, she had sent for me on a trial visit, and if I had come out of it successfully, I suppose I should have been provided for; perhaps I should have been what-you-may-called it to Estella.”\n\n“What’s that?” I asked, with sudden gravity.\n\nHe was arranging his fruit in plates while we talked, which divided his attention, and was the cause of his having made this lapse of a word. “Affianced,” he explained, still busy with the fruit. “Betrothed. Engaged. What’s-his-named. Any word of that sort.”\n\n“How did you bear your disappointment?” I asked.\n\n“Pooh!” said he, “I didn’t care much for it. She’s a Tartar.”\n\n“Miss Havisham?”\n\n“I don’t say no to that, but I meant Estella. That girl’s hard and haughty and capricious to the last degree, and has been brought up by Miss Havisham to wreak revenge on all the male sex.”\n\n“What relation is she to Miss Havisham?”\n\n“None,” said he. “Only adopted.”\n\n“Why should she wreak revenge on all the male sex? What revenge?”\n\n“Lord, Mr. Pip!” said he. “Don’t you know?”\n\n“No,” said I.\n\n“Dear me! It’s quite a story, and shall be saved till dinner-time. And now let me take the liberty of asking you a question. How did you come there, that day?”\n\nI told him, and he was attentive until I had finished, and then burst out laughing again, and asked me if I was sore afterwards? I didn’t ask him if he was, for my conviction on that point was perfectly established.\n\n“Mr. Jaggers is your guardian, I understand?” he went on.\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“You know he is Miss Havisham’s man of business and solicitor, and has her confidence when nobody else has?”\n\nThis was bringing me (I felt) towards dangerous ground. I answered with a constraint I made no attempt to disguise, that I had seen Mr. Jaggers in Miss Havisham’s house on the very day of our combat, but never at any other time, and that I believed he had no recollection of having ever seen me there.\n\n“He was so obliging as to suggest my father for your tutor, and he called on my father to propose it. Of course he knew about my father from his connection with Miss Havisham. My father is Miss Havisham’s cousin; not that that implies familiar intercourse between them, for he is a bad courtier and will not propitiate her.”\n\nHerbert Pocket had a frank and easy way with him that was very taking. I had never seen any one then, and I have never seen any one since, who more strongly expressed to me, in every look and tone, a natural incapacity to do anything secret and mean. There was something wonderfully hopeful about his general air, and something that at the same time whispered to me he would never be very successful or rich. I don’t know how this was. I became imbued with the notion on that first occasion before we sat down to dinner, but I cannot define by what means.\n\nHe was still a pale young gentleman, and had a certain conquered languor about him in the midst of his spirits and briskness, that did not seem indicative of natural strength. He had not a handsome face, but it was better than handsome: being extremely amiable and cheerful. His figure was a little ungainly, as in the days when my knuckles had taken such liberties with it, but it looked as if it would always be light and young. Whether Mr. Trabb’s local work would have sat more gracefully on him than on me, may be a question; but I am conscious that he carried off his rather old clothes much better than I carried off my new suit.\n\nAs he was so communicative, I felt that reserve on my part would be a bad return unsuited to our years. I therefore told him my small story, and laid stress on my being forbidden to inquire who my benefactor was. I further mentioned that as I had been brought up a blacksmith in a country place, and knew very little of the ways of politeness, I would take it as a great kindness in him if he would give me a hint whenever he saw me at a loss or going wrong.\n\n“With pleasure,” said he, “though I venture to prophesy that you’ll want very few hints. I dare say we shall be often together, and I should like to banish any needless restraint between us. Will you do me the favour to begin at once to call me by my Christian name, Herbert?”\n\nI thanked him and said I would. I informed him in exchange that my Christian name was Philip.\n\n“I don’t take to Philip,” said he, smiling, “for it sounds like a moral boy out of the spelling-book, who was so lazy that he fell into a pond, or so fat that he couldn’t see out of his eyes, or so avaricious that he locked up his cake till the mice ate it, or so determined to go a bird’s-nesting that he got himself eaten by bears who lived handy in the neighbourhood. I tell you what I should like. We are so harmonious, and you have been a blacksmith,—would you mind it?”\n\n“I shouldn’t mind anything that you propose,” I answered, “but I don’t understand you.”\n\n“Would you mind Handel for a familiar name? There’s a charming piece of music by Handel, called the Harmonious Blacksmith.”\n\n“I should like it very much.”\n\n“Then, my dear Handel,” said he, turning round as the door opened, “here is the dinner, and I must beg of you to take the top of the table, because the dinner is of your providing.”\n\nThis I would not hear of, so he took the top, and I faced him. It was a nice little dinner,—seemed to me then a very Lord Mayor’s Feast,—and it acquired additional relish from being eaten under those independent circumstances, with no old people by, and with London all around us. This again was heightened by a certain gypsy character that set the banquet off; for while the table was, as Mr. Pumblechook might have said, the lap of luxury,—being entirely furnished forth from the coffee-house,—the circumjacent region of sitting-room was of a comparatively pastureless and shifty character; imposing on the waiter the wandering habits of putting the covers on the floor (where he fell over them), the melted butter in the arm-chair, the bread on the bookshelves, the cheese in the coal-scuttle, and the boiled fowl into my bed in the next room,—where I found much of its parsley and butter in a state of congelation when I retired for the night. All this made the feast delightful, and when the waiter was not there to watch me, my pleasure was without alloy.\n\nWe had made some progress in the dinner, when I reminded Herbert of his promise to tell me about Miss Havisham.\n\n“True,” he replied. “I’ll redeem it at once. Let me introduce the topic, Handel, by mentioning that in London it is not the custom to put the knife in the mouth,—for fear of accidents,—and that while the fork is reserved for that use, it is not put further in than necessary. It is scarcely worth mentioning, only it’s as well to do as other people do. Also, the spoon is not generally used over-hand, but under. This has two advantages. You get at your mouth better (which after all is the object), and you save a good deal of the attitude of opening oysters, on the part of the right elbow.”\n\nHe offered these friendly suggestions in such a lively way, that we both laughed and I scarcely blushed.\n\n“Now,” he pursued, “concerning Miss Havisham. Miss Havisham, you must know, was a spoilt child. Her mother died when she was a baby, and her father denied her nothing. Her father was a country gentleman down in your part of the world, and was a brewer. I don’t know why it should be a crack thing to be a brewer; but it is indisputable that while you cannot possibly be genteel and bake, you may be as genteel as never was and brew. You see it every day.”\n\n“Yet a gentleman may not keep a public-house; may he?” said I.\n\n“Not on any account,” returned Herbert; “but a public-house may keep a gentleman. Well! Mr. Havisham was very rich and very proud. So was his daughter.”\n\n“Miss Havisham was an only child?” I hazarded.\n\n“Stop a moment, I am coming to that. No, she was not an only child; she had a half-brother. Her father privately married again—his cook, I rather think.”\n\n“I thought he was proud,” said I.\n\n“My good Handel, so he was. He married his second wife privately, because he was proud, and in course of time she died. When she was dead, I apprehend he first told his daughter what he had done, and then the son became a part of the family, residing in the house you are acquainted with. As the son grew a young man, he turned out riotous, extravagant, undutiful,—altogether bad. At last his father disinherited him; but he softened when he was dying, and left him well off, though not nearly so well off as Miss Havisham.—Take another glass of wine, and excuse my mentioning that society as a body does not expect one to be so strictly conscientious in emptying one’s glass, as to turn it bottom upwards with the rim on one’s nose.”\n\nI had been doing this, in an excess of attention to his recital. I thanked him, and apologised. He said, “Not at all,” and resumed.\n\n“Miss Havisham was now an heiress, and you may suppose was looked after as a great match. Her half-brother had now ample means again, but what with debts and what with new madness wasted them most fearfully again. There were stronger differences between him and her than there had been between him and his father, and it is suspected that he cherished a deep and mortal grudge against her as having influenced the father’s anger. Now, I come to the cruel part of the story,—merely breaking off, my dear Handel, to remark that a dinner-napkin will not go into a tumbler.”\n\nWhy I was trying to pack mine into my tumbler, I am wholly unable to say. I only know that I found myself, with a perseverance worthy of a much better cause, making the most strenuous exertions to compress it within those limits. Again I thanked him and apologised, and again he said in the cheerfullest manner, “Not at all, I am sure!” and resumed.\n\n“There appeared upon the scene—say at the races, or the public balls, or anywhere else you like—a certain man, who made love to Miss Havisham. I never saw him (for this happened five-and-twenty years ago, before you and I were, Handel), but I have heard my father mention that he was a showy man, and the kind of man for the purpose. But that he was not to be, without ignorance or prejudice, mistaken for a gentleman, my father most strongly asseverates; because it is a principle of his that no man who was not a true gentleman at heart ever was, since the world began, a true gentleman in manner. He says, no varnish can hide the grain of the wood; and that the more varnish you put on, the more the grain will express itself. Well! This man pursued Miss Havisham closely, and professed to be devoted to her. I believe she had not shown much susceptibility up to that time; but all the susceptibility she possessed certainly came out then, and she passionately loved him. There is no doubt that she perfectly idolized him. He practised on her affection in that systematic way, that he got great sums of money from her, and he induced her to buy her brother out of a share in the brewery (which had been weakly left him by his father) at an immense price, on the plea that when he was her husband he must hold and manage it all. Your guardian was not at that time in Miss Havisham’s counsels, and she was too haughty and too much in love to be advised by any one. Her relations were poor and scheming, with the exception of my father; he was poor enough, but not time-serving or jealous. The only independent one among them, he warned her that she was doing too much for this man, and was placing herself too unreservedly in his power. She took the first opportunity of angrily ordering my father out of the house, in his presence, and my father has never seen her since.”\n\nI thought of her having said, “Matthew will come and see me at last when I am laid dead upon that table;” and I asked Herbert whether his father was so inveterate against her?\n\n“It’s not that,” said he, “but she charged him, in the presence of her intended husband, with being disappointed in the hope of fawning upon her for his own advancement, and, if he were to go to her now, it would look true—even to him—and even to her. To return to the man and make an end of him. The marriage day was fixed, the wedding dresses were bought, the wedding tour was planned out, the wedding guests were invited. The day came, but not the bridegroom. He wrote her a letter—”\n\n“Which she received,” I struck in, “when she was dressing for her marriage? At twenty minutes to nine?”\n\n“At the hour and minute,” said Herbert, nodding, “at which she afterwards stopped all the clocks. What was in it, further than that it most heartlessly broke the marriage off, I can’t tell you, because I don’t know. When she recovered from a bad illness that she had, she laid the whole place waste, as you have seen it, and she has never since looked upon the light of day.”\n\n“Is that all the story?” I asked, after considering it.\n\n“All I know of it; and indeed I only know so much, through piecing it out for myself; for my father always avoids it, and, even when Miss Havisham invited me to go there, told me no more of it than it was absolutely requisite I should understand. But I have forgotten one thing. It has been supposed that the man to whom she gave her misplaced confidence acted throughout in concert with her half-brother; that it was a conspiracy between them; and that they shared the profits.”\n\n“I wonder he didn’t marry her and get all the property,” said I.\n\n“He may have been married already, and her cruel mortification may have been a part of her half-brother’s scheme,” said Herbert. “Mind! I don’t know that.”\n\n“What became of the two men?” I asked, after again considering the subject.\n\n“They fell into deeper shame and degradation—if there can be deeper—and ruin.”\n\n“Are they alive now?”\n\n“I don’t know.”\n\n“You said just now that Estella was not related to Miss Havisham, but adopted. When adopted?”\n\nHerbert shrugged his shoulders. “There has always been an Estella, since I have heard of a Miss Havisham. I know no more. And now, Handel,” said he, finally throwing off the story as it were, “there is a perfectly open understanding between us. All that I know about Miss Havisham, you know.”\n\n“And all that I know,” I retorted, “you know.”\n\n“I fully believe it. So there can be no competition or perplexity between you and me. And as to the condition on which you hold your advancement in life,—namely, that you are not to inquire or discuss to whom you owe it,—you may be very sure that it will never be encroached upon, or even approached, by me, or by any one belonging to me.”\n\nIn truth, he said this with so much delicacy, that I felt the subject done with, even though I should be under his father’s roof for years and years to come. Yet he said it with so much meaning, too, that I felt he as perfectly understood Miss Havisham to be my benefactress, as I understood the fact myself.\n\nIt had not occurred to me before, that he had led up to the theme for the purpose of clearing it out of our way; but we were so much the lighter and easier for having broached it, that I now perceived this to be the case. We were very gay and sociable, and I asked him, in the course of conversation, what he was? He replied, “A capitalist,—an Insurer of Ships.” I suppose he saw me glancing about the room in search of some tokens of Shipping, or capital, for he added, “In the City.”\n\nI had grand ideas of the wealth and importance of Insurers of Ships in the City, and I began to think with awe of having laid a young Insurer on his back, blackened his enterprising eye, and cut his responsible head open. But again there came upon me, for my relief, that odd impression that Herbert Pocket would never be very successful or rich.\n\n“I shall not rest satisfied with merely employing my capital in insuring ships. I shall buy up some good Life Assurance shares, and cut into the Direction. I shall also do a little in the mining way. None of these things will interfere with my chartering a few thousand tons on my own account. I think I shall trade,” said he, leaning back in his chair, “to the East Indies, for silks, shawls, spices, dyes, drugs, and precious woods. It’s an interesting trade.”\n\n“And the profits are large?” said I.\n\n“Tremendous!” said he.\n\nI wavered again, and began to think here were greater expectations than my own.\n\n“I think I shall trade, also,” said he, putting his thumbs in his waist-coat pockets, “to the West Indies, for sugar, tobacco, and rum. Also to Ceylon, especially for elephants’ tusks.”\n\n“You will want a good many ships,” said I.\n\n“A perfect fleet,” said he.\n\nQuite overpowered by the magnificence of these transactions, I asked him where the ships he insured mostly traded to at present?\n\n“I haven’t begun insuring yet,” he replied. “I am looking about me.”\n\nSomehow, that pursuit seemed more in keeping with Barnard’s Inn. I said (in a tone of conviction), “Ah-h!”\n\n“Yes. I am in a counting-house, and looking about me.”\n\n“Is a counting-house profitable?” I asked.\n\n“To—do you mean to the young fellow who’s in it?” he asked, in reply.\n\n“Yes; to you.”\n\n“Why, n-no; not to me.” He said this with the air of one carefully reckoning up and striking a balance. “Not directly profitable. That is, it doesn’t pay me anything, and I have to—keep myself.”\n\nThis certainly had not a profitable appearance, and I shook my head as if I would imply that it would be difficult to lay by much accumulative capital from such a source of income.\n\n“But the thing is,” said Herbert Pocket, “that you look about you. That’s the grand thing. You are in a counting-house, you know, and you look about you.”\n\nIt struck me as a singular implication that you couldn’t be out of a counting-house, you know, and look about you; but I silently deferred to his experience.\n\n“Then the time comes,” said Herbert, “when you see your opening. And you go in, and you swoop upon it and you make your capital, and then there you are! When you have once made your capital, you have nothing to do but employ it.”\n\nThis was very like his way of conducting that encounter in the garden; very like. His manner of bearing his poverty, too, exactly corresponded to his manner of bearing that defeat. It seemed to me that he took all blows and buffets now with just the same air as he had taken mine then. It was evident that he had nothing around him but the simplest necessaries, for everything that I remarked upon turned out to have been sent in on my account from the coffee-house or somewhere else.\n\nYet, having already made his fortune in his own mind, he was so unassuming with it that I felt quite grateful to him for not being puffed up. It was a pleasant addition to his naturally pleasant ways, and we got on famously. In the evening we went out for a walk in the streets, and went half-price to the Theatre; and next day we went to church at Westminster Abbey, and in the afternoon we walked in the Parks; and I wondered who shod all the horses there, and wished Joe did.\n\nOn a moderate computation, it was many months, that Sunday, since I had left Joe and Biddy. The space interposed between myself and them partook of that expansion, and our marshes were any distance off. That I could have been at our old church in my old church-going clothes, on the very last Sunday that ever was, seemed a combination of impossibilities, geographical and social, solar and lunar. Yet in the London streets so crowded with people and so brilliantly lighted in the dusk of evening, there were depressing hints of reproaches for that I had put the poor old kitchen at home so far away; and in the dead of night, the footsteps of some incapable impostor of a porter mooning about Barnard’s Inn, under pretence of watching it, fell hollow on my heart.\n\nOn the Monday morning at a quarter before nine, Herbert went to the counting-house to report himself,—to look about him, too, I suppose,—and I bore him company. He was to come away in an hour or two to attend me to Hammersmith, and I was to wait about for him. It appeared to me that the eggs from which young Insurers were hatched were incubated in dust and heat, like the eggs of ostriches, judging from the places to which those incipient giants repaired on a Monday morning. Nor did the counting-house where Herbert assisted, show in my eyes as at all a good Observatory; being a back second floor up a yard, of a grimy presence in all particulars, and with a look into another back second floor, rather than a look out.\n\nI waited about until it was noon, and I went upon ’Change, and I saw fluey men sitting there under the bills about shipping, whom I took to be great merchants, though I couldn’t understand why they should all be out of spirits. When Herbert came, we went and had lunch at a celebrated house which I then quite venerated, but now believe to have been the most abject superstition in Europe, and where I could not help noticing, even then, that there was much more gravy on the tablecloths and knives and waiters’ clothes, than in the steaks. This collation disposed of at a moderate price (considering the grease, which was not charged for), we went back to Barnard’s Inn and got my little portmanteau, and then took coach for Hammersmith. We arrived there at two or three o’clock in the afternoon, and had very little way to walk to Mr. Pocket’s house. Lifting the latch of a gate, we passed direct into a little garden overlooking the river, where Mr. Pocket’s children were playing about. And unless I deceive myself on a point where my interests or prepossessions are certainly not concerned, I saw that Mr. and Mrs. Pocket’s children were not growing up or being brought up, but were tumbling up.\n\nMrs. Pocket was sitting on a garden chair under a tree, reading, with her legs upon another garden chair; and Mrs. Pocket’s two nurse-maids were looking about them while the children played. “Mamma,” said Herbert, “this is young Mr. Pip.” Upon which Mrs. Pocket received me with an appearance of amiable dignity.\n\n“Master Alick and Miss Jane,” cried one of the nurses to two of the children, “if you go a bouncing up against them bushes you’ll fall over into the river and be drownded, and what’ll your pa say then?”\n\nAt the same time this nurse picked up Mrs. Pocket’s handkerchief, and said, “If that don’t make six times you’ve dropped it, Mum!” Upon which Mrs. Pocket laughed and said, “Thank you, Flopson,” and settling herself in one chair only, resumed her book. Her countenance immediately assumed a knitted and intent expression as if she had been reading for a week, but before she could have read half a dozen lines, she fixed her eyes upon me, and said, “I hope your mamma is quite well?” This unexpected inquiry put me into such a difficulty that I began saying in the absurdest way that if there had been any such person I had no doubt she would have been quite well and would have been very much obliged and would have sent her compliments, when the nurse came to my rescue.\n\n“Well!” she cried, picking up the pocket-handkerchief, “if that don’t make seven times! What ARE you a-doing of this afternoon, Mum!” Mrs. Pocket received her property, at first with a look of unutterable surprise as if she had never seen it before, and then with a laugh of recognition, and said, “Thank you, Flopson,” and forgot me, and went on reading.\n\nI found, now I had leisure to count them, that there were no fewer than six little Pockets present, in various stages of tumbling up. I had scarcely arrived at the total when a seventh was heard, as in the region of air, wailing dolefully.\n\n“If there ain’t Baby!” said Flopson, appearing to think it most surprising. “Make haste up, Millers.”\n\nMillers, who was the other nurse, retired into the house, and by degrees the child’s wailing was hushed and stopped, as if it were a young ventriloquist with something in its mouth. Mrs. Pocket read all the time, and I was curious to know what the book could be.\n\nWe were waiting, I supposed, for Mr. Pocket to come out to us; at any rate we waited there, and so I had an opportunity of observing the remarkable family phenomenon that whenever any of the children strayed near Mrs. Pocket in their play, they always tripped themselves up and tumbled over her,—always very much to her momentary astonishment, and their own more enduring lamentation. I was at a loss to account for this surprising circumstance, and could not help giving my mind to speculations about it, until by and by Millers came down with the baby, which baby was handed to Flopson, which Flopson was handing it to Mrs. Pocket, when she too went fairly head foremost over Mrs. Pocket, baby and all, and was caught by Herbert and myself.\n\n“Gracious me, Flopson!” said Mrs. Pocket, looking off her book for a moment, “everybody’s tumbling!”\n\n“Gracious you, indeed, Mum!” returned Flopson, very red in the face; “what have you got there?”\n\n“I got here, Flopson?” asked Mrs. Pocket.\n\n“Why, if it ain’t your footstool!” cried Flopson. “And if you keep it under your skirts like that, who’s to help tumbling? Here! Take the baby, Mum, and give me your book.”\n\nMrs. Pocket acted on the advice, and inexpertly danced the infant a little in her lap, while the other children played about it. This had lasted but a very short time, when Mrs. Pocket issued summary orders that they were all to be taken into the house for a nap. Thus I made the second discovery on that first occasion, that the nurture of the little Pockets consisted of alternately tumbling up and lying down.\n\nUnder these circumstances, when Flopson and Millers had got the children into the house, like a little flock of sheep, and Mr. Pocket came out of it to make my acquaintance, I was not much surprised to find that Mr. Pocket was a gentleman with a rather perplexed expression of face, and with his very grey hair disordered on his head, as if he didn’t quite see his way to putting anything straight.\n\n# Chapter XXIII.\n\nMr. Pocket said he was glad to see me, and he hoped I was not sorry to see him. “For, I really am not,” he added, with his son’s smile, “an alarming personage.” He was a young-looking man, in spite of his perplexities and his very grey hair, and his manner seemed quite natural. I use the word natural, in the sense of its being unaffected; there was something comic in his distraught way, as though it would have been downright ludicrous but for his own perception that it was very near being so. When he had talked with me a little, he said to Mrs. Pocket, with a rather anxious contraction of his eyebrows, which were black and handsome, “Belinda, I hope you have welcomed Mr. Pip?” And she looked up from her book, and said, “Yes.” She then smiled upon me in an absent state of mind, and asked me if I liked the taste of orange-flower water? As the question had no bearing, near or remote, on any foregone or subsequent transaction, I consider it to have been thrown out, like her previous approaches, in general conversational condescension.\n\nI found out within a few hours, and may mention at once, that Mrs. Pocket was the only daughter of a certain quite accidental deceased Knight, who had invented for himself a conviction that his deceased father would have been made a Baronet but for somebody’s determined opposition arising out of entirely personal motives,—I forget whose, if I ever knew,—the Sovereign’s, the Prime Minister’s, the Lord Chancellor’s, the Archbishop of Canterbury’s, anybody’s,—and had tacked himself on to the nobles of the earth in right of this quite supposititious fact. I believe he had been knighted himself for storming the English grammar at the point of the pen, in a desperate address engrossed on vellum, on the occasion of the laying of the first stone of some building or other, and for handing some Royal Personage either the trowel or the mortar. Be that as it may, he had directed Mrs. Pocket to be brought up from her cradle as one who in the nature of things must marry a title, and who was to be guarded from the acquisition of plebeian domestic knowledge.\n\nSo successful a watch and ward had been established over the young lady by this judicious parent, that she had grown up highly ornamental, but perfectly helpless and useless. With her character thus happily formed, in the first bloom of her youth she had encountered Mr. Pocket: who was also in the first bloom of youth, and not quite decided whether to mount to the Woolsack, or to roof himself in with a mitre. As his doing the one or the other was a mere question of time, he and Mrs. Pocket had taken Time by the forelock (when, to judge from its length, it would seem to have wanted cutting), and had married without the knowledge of the judicious parent. The judicious parent, having nothing to bestow or withhold but his blessing, had handsomely settled that dower upon them after a short struggle, and had informed Mr. Pocket that his wife was “a treasure for a Prince.” Mr. Pocket had invested the Prince’s treasure in the ways of the world ever since, and it was supposed to have brought him in but indifferent interest. Still, Mrs. Pocket was in general the object of a queer sort of respectful pity, because she had not married a title; while Mr. Pocket was the object of a queer sort of forgiving reproach, because he had never got one.\n\nMr. Pocket took me into the house and showed me my room: which was a pleasant one, and so furnished as that I could use it with comfort for my own private sitting-room. He then knocked at the doors of two other similar rooms, and introduced me to their occupants, by name Drummle and Startop. Drummle, an old-looking young man of a heavy order of architecture, was whistling. Startop, younger in years and appearance, was reading and holding his head, as if he thought himself in danger of exploding it with too strong a charge of knowledge.\n\nBoth Mr. and Mrs. Pocket had such a noticeable air of being in somebody else’s hands, that I wondered who really was in possession of the house and let them live there, until I found this unknown power to be the servants. It was a smooth way of going on, perhaps, in respect of saving trouble; but it had the appearance of being expensive, for the servants felt it a duty they owed to themselves to be nice in their eating and drinking, and to keep a deal of company downstairs. They allowed a very liberal table to Mr. and Mrs. Pocket, yet it always appeared to me that by far the best part of the house to have boarded in would have been the kitchen,—always supposing the boarder capable of self-defence, for, before I had been there a week, a neighbouring lady with whom the family were personally unacquainted, wrote in to say that she had seen Millers slapping the baby. This greatly distressed Mrs. Pocket, who burst into tears on receiving the note, and said that it was an extraordinary thing that the neighbours couldn’t mind their own business.\n\nBy degrees I learnt, and chiefly from Herbert, that Mr. Pocket had been educated at Harrow and at Cambridge, where he had distinguished himself; but that when he had had the happiness of marrying Mrs. Pocket very early in life, he had impaired his prospects and taken up the calling of a Grinder. After grinding a number of dull blades,—of whom it was remarkable that their fathers, when influential, were always going to help him to preferment, but always forgot to do it when the blades had left the Grindstone,—he had wearied of that poor work and had come to London. Here, after gradually failing in loftier hopes, he had “read” with divers who had lacked opportunities or neglected them, and had refurbished divers others for special occasions, and had turned his acquirements to the account of literary compilation and correction, and on such means, added to some very moderate private resources, still maintained the house I saw.\n\nMr. and Mrs. Pocket had a toady neighbour; a widow lady of that highly sympathetic nature that she agreed with everybody, blessed everybody, and shed smiles and tears on everybody, according to circumstances. This lady’s name was Mrs. Coiler, and I had the honour of taking her down to dinner on the day of my installation. She gave me to understand on the stairs, that it was a blow to dear Mrs. Pocket that dear Mr. Pocket should be under the necessity of receiving gentlemen to read with him. That did not extend to me, she told me in a gush of love and confidence (at that time, I had known her something less than five minutes); if they were all like Me, it would be quite another thing.\n\n“But dear Mrs. Pocket,” said Mrs. Coiler, “after her early disappointment (not that dear Mr. Pocket was to blame in that), requires so much luxury and elegance—”\n\n“Yes, ma’am,” I said, to stop her, for I was afraid she was going to cry.\n\n“And she is of so aristocratic a disposition—”\n\n“Yes, ma’am,” I said again, with the same object as before.\n\n“—That it is hard,” said Mrs. Coiler, “to have dear Mr. Pocket’s time and attention diverted from dear Mrs. Pocket.”\n\nI could not help thinking that it might be harder if the butcher’s time and attention were diverted from dear Mrs. Pocket; but I said nothing, and indeed had enough to do in keeping a bashful watch upon my company manners.\n\nIt came to my knowledge, through what passed between Mrs. Pocket and Drummle while I was attentive to my knife and fork, spoon, glasses, and other instruments of self-destruction, that Drummle, whose Christian name was Bentley, was actually the next heir but one to a baronetcy. It further appeared that the book I had seen Mrs. Pocket reading in the garden was all about titles, and that she knew the exact date at which her grandpapa would have come into the book, if he ever had come at all. Drummle didn’t say much, but in his limited way (he struck me as a sulky kind of fellow) he spoke as one of the elect, and recognised Mrs. Pocket as a woman and a sister. No one but themselves and Mrs. Coiler the toady neighbour showed any interest in this part of the conversation, and it appeared to me that it was painful to Herbert; but it promised to last a long time, when the page came in with the announcement of a domestic affliction. It was, in effect, that the cook had mislaid the beef. To my unutterable amazement, I now, for the first time, saw Mr. Pocket relieve his mind by going through a performance that struck me as very extraordinary, but which made no impression on anybody else, and with which I soon became as familiar as the rest. He laid down the carving-knife and fork,—being engaged in carving, at the moment,—put his two hands into his disturbed hair, and appeared to make an extraordinary effort to lift himself up by it. When he had done this, and had not lifted himself up at all, he quietly went on with what he was about.\n\nMrs. Coiler then changed the subject and began to flatter me. I liked it for a few moments, but she flattered me so very grossly that the pleasure was soon over. She had a serpentine way of coming close at me when she pretended to be vitally interested in the friends and localities I had left, which was altogether snaky and fork-tongued; and when she made an occasional bounce upon Startop (who said very little to her), or upon Drummle (who said less), I rather envied them for being on the opposite side of the table.\n\nAfter dinner the children were introduced, and Mrs. Coiler made admiring comments on their eyes, noses, and legs,—a sagacious way of improving their minds. There were four little girls, and two little boys, besides the baby who might have been either, and the baby’s next successor who was as yet neither. They were brought in by Flopson and Millers, much as though those two non-commissioned officers had been recruiting somewhere for children and had enlisted these, while Mrs. Pocket looked at the young Nobles that ought to have been as if she rather thought she had had the pleasure of inspecting them before, but didn’t quite know what to make of them.\n\n“Here! Give me your fork, Mum, and take the baby,” said Flopson. “Don’t take it that way, or you’ll get its head under the table.”\n\nThus advised, Mrs. Pocket took it the other way, and got its head upon the table; which was announced to all present by a prodigious concussion.\n\n“Dear, dear! Give it me back, Mum,” said Flopson; “and Miss Jane, come and dance to baby, do!”\n\nOne of the little girls, a mere mite who seemed to have prematurely taken upon herself some charge of the others, stepped out of her place by me, and danced to and from the baby until it left off crying, and laughed. Then, all the children laughed, and Mr. Pocket (who in the meantime had twice endeavoured to lift himself up by the hair) laughed, and we all laughed and were glad.\n\nFlopson, by dint of doubling the baby at the joints like a Dutch doll, then got it safely into Mrs. Pocket’s lap, and gave it the nut-crackers to play with; at the same time recommending Mrs. Pocket to take notice that the handles of that instrument were not likely to agree with its eyes, and sharply charging Miss Jane to look after the same. Then, the two nurses left the room, and had a lively scuffle on the staircase with a dissipated page who had waited at dinner, and who had clearly lost half his buttons at the gaming-table.\n\nI was made very uneasy in my mind by Mrs. Pocket’s falling into a discussion with Drummle respecting two baronetcies, while she ate a sliced orange steeped in sugar and wine, and, forgetting all about the baby on her lap, who did most appalling things with the nut-crackers. At length little Jane, perceiving its young brains to be imperilled, softly left her place, and with many small artifices coaxed the dangerous weapon away. Mrs. Pocket finishing her orange at about the same time, and not approving of this, said to Jane,—\n\n“You naughty child, how dare you? Go and sit down this instant!”\n\n“Mamma dear,” lisped the little girl, “baby ood have put hith eyeth out.”\n\n“How dare you tell me so?” retorted Mrs. Pocket. “Go and sit down in your chair this moment!”\n\nMrs. Pocket’s dignity was so crushing, that I felt quite abashed, as if I myself had done something to rouse it.\n\n“Belinda,” remonstrated Mr. Pocket, from the other end of the table, “how can you be so unreasonable? Jane only interfered for the protection of baby.”\n\n“I will not allow anybody to interfere,” said Mrs. Pocket. “I am surprised, Matthew, that you should expose me to the affront of interference.”\n\n“Good God!” cried Mr. Pocket, in an outbreak of desolate desperation. “Are infants to be nut-crackered into their tombs, and is nobody to save them?”\n\n“I will not be interfered with by Jane,” said Mrs. Pocket, with a majestic glance at that innocent little offender. “I hope I know my poor grandpapa’s position. Jane, indeed!”\n\nMr. Pocket got his hands in his hair again, and this time really did lift himself some inches out of his chair. “Hear this!” he helplessly exclaimed to the elements. “Babies are to be nut-crackered dead, for people’s poor grandpapa’s positions!” Then he let himself down again, and became silent.\n\nWe all looked awkwardly at the tablecloth while this was going on. A pause succeeded, during which the honest and irrepressible baby made a series of leaps and crows at little Jane, who appeared to me to be the only member of the family (irrespective of servants) with whom it had any decided acquaintance.\n\n“Mr. Drummle,” said Mrs. Pocket, “will you ring for Flopson? Jane, you undutiful little thing, go and lie down. Now, baby darling, come with ma!”\n\nThe baby was the soul of honour, and protested with all its might. It doubled itself up the wrong way over Mrs. Pocket’s arm, exhibited a pair of knitted shoes and dimpled ankles to the company in lieu of its soft face, and was carried out in the highest state of mutiny. And it gained its point after all, for I saw it through the window within a few minutes, being nursed by little Jane.\n\nIt happened that the other five children were left behind at the dinner-table, through Flopson’s having some private engagement, and their not being anybody else’s business. I thus became aware of the mutual relations between them and Mr. Pocket, which were exemplified in the following manner. Mr. Pocket, with the normal perplexity of his face heightened and his hair rumpled, looked at them for some minutes, as if he couldn’t make out how they came to be boarding and lodging in that establishment, and why they hadn’t been billeted by Nature on somebody else. Then, in a distant Missionary way he asked them certain questions,—as why little Joe had that hole in his frill, who said, Pa, Flopson was going to mend it when she had time,—and how little Fanny came by that whitlow, who said, Pa, Millers was going to poultice it when she didn’t forget. Then, he melted into parental tenderness, and gave them a shilling apiece and told them to go and play; and then as they went out, with one very strong effort to lift himself up by the hair he dismissed the hopeless subject.\n\nIn the evening there was rowing on the river. As Drummle and Startop had each a boat, I resolved to set up mine, and to cut them both out. I was pretty good at most exercises in which country boys are adepts, but as I was conscious of wanting elegance of style for the Thames,—not to say for other waters,—I at once engaged to place myself under the tuition of the winner of a prize-wherry who plied at our stairs, and to whom I was introduced by my new allies. This practical authority confused me very much by saying I had the arm of a blacksmith. If he could have known how nearly the compliment lost him his pupil, I doubt if he would have paid it.\n\nThere was a supper-tray after we got home at night, and I think we should all have enjoyed ourselves, but for a rather disagreeable domestic occurrence. Mr. Pocket was in good spirits, when a housemaid came in, and said, “If you please, sir, I should wish to speak to you.”\n\n“Speak to your master?” said Mrs. Pocket, whose dignity was roused again. “How can you think of such a thing? Go and speak to Flopson. Or speak to me—at some other time.”\n\n“Begging your pardon, ma’am,” returned the housemaid, “I should wish to speak at once, and to speak to master.”\n\nHereupon, Mr. Pocket went out of the room, and we made the best of ourselves until he came back.\n\n“This is a pretty thing, Belinda!” said Mr. Pocket, returning with a countenance expressive of grief and despair. “Here’s the cook lying insensibly drunk on the kitchen floor, with a large bundle of fresh butter made up in the cupboard ready to sell for grease!”\n\nMrs. Pocket instantly showed much amiable emotion, and said, “This is that odious Sophia’s doing!”\n\n“What do you mean, Belinda?” demanded Mr. Pocket.\n\n“Sophia has told you,” said Mrs. Pocket. “Did I not see her with my own eyes and hear her with my own ears, come into the room just now and ask to speak to you?”\n\n“But has she not taken me downstairs, Belinda,” returned Mr. Pocket, “and shown me the woman, and the bundle too?”\n\n“And do you defend her, Matthew,” said Mrs. Pocket, “for making mischief?”\n\nMr. Pocket uttered a dismal groan.\n\n“Am I, grandpapa’s granddaughter, to be nothing in the house?” said Mrs. Pocket. “Besides, the cook has always been a very nice respectful woman, and said in the most natural manner when she came to look after the situation, that she felt I was born to be a Duchess.”\n\nThere was a sofa where Mr. Pocket stood, and he dropped upon it in the attitude of the Dying Gladiator. Still in that attitude he said, with a hollow voice, “Good night, Mr. Pip,” when I deemed it advisable to go to bed and leave him.\n\n# Chapter XXIV.\n\nAfter two or three days, when I had established myself in my room and had gone backwards and forwards to London several times, and had ordered all I wanted of my tradesmen, Mr. Pocket and I had a long talk together. He knew more of my intended career than I knew myself, for he referred to his having been told by Mr. Jaggers that I was not designed for any profession, and that I should be well enough educated for my destiny if I could “hold my own” with the average of young men in prosperous circumstances. I acquiesced, of course, knowing nothing to the contrary.\n\nHe advised my attending certain places in London, for the acquisition of such mere rudiments as I wanted, and my investing him with the functions of explainer and director of all my studies. He hoped that with intelligent assistance I should meet with little to discourage me, and should soon be able to dispense with any aid but his. Through his way of saying this, and much more to similar purpose, he placed himself on confidential terms with me in an admirable manner; and I may state at once that he was always so zealous and honourable in fulfilling his compact with me, that he made me zealous and honourable in fulfilling mine with him. If he had shown indifference as a master, I have no doubt I should have returned the compliment as a pupil; he gave me no such excuse, and each of us did the other justice. Nor did I ever regard him as having anything ludicrous about him—or anything but what was serious, honest, and good—in his tutor communication with me.\n\nWhen these points were settled, and so far carried out as that I had begun to work in earnest, it occurred to me that if I could retain my bedroom in Barnard’s Inn, my life would be agreeably varied, while my manners would be none the worse for Herbert’s society. Mr. Pocket did not object to this arrangement, but urged that before any step could possibly be taken in it, it must be submitted to my guardian. I felt that this delicacy arose out of the consideration that the plan would save Herbert some expense, so I went off to Little Britain and imparted my wish to Mr. Jaggers.\n\n“If I could buy the furniture now hired for me,” said I, “and one or two other little things, I should be quite at home there.”\n\n“Go it!” said Mr. Jaggers, with a short laugh. “I told you you’d get on. Well! How much do you want?”\n\nI said I didn’t know how much.\n\n“Come!” retorted Mr. Jaggers. “How much? Fifty pounds?”\n\n“O, not nearly so much.”\n\n“Five pounds?” said Mr. Jaggers.\n\nThis was such a great fall, that I said in discomfiture, “O, more than that.”\n\n“More than that, eh!” retorted Mr. Jaggers, lying in wait for me, with his hands in his pockets, his head on one side, and his eyes on the wall behind me; “how much more?”\n\n“It is so difficult to fix a sum,” said I, hesitating.\n\n“Come!” said Mr. Jaggers. “Let’s get at it. Twice five; will that do? Three times five; will that do? Four times five; will that do?”\n\nI said I thought that would do handsomely.\n\n“Four times five will do handsomely, will it?” said Mr. Jaggers, knitting his brows. “Now, what do you make of four times five?”\n\n“What do I make of it?”\n\n“Ah!” said Mr. Jaggers; “how much?”\n\n“I suppose you make it twenty pounds,” said I, smiling.\n\n“Never mind what I make it, my friend,” observed Mr. Jaggers, with a knowing and contradictory toss of his head. “I want to know what you make it.”\n\n“Twenty pounds, of course.”\n\n“Wemmick!” said Mr. Jaggers, opening his office door. “Take Mr. Pip’s written order, and pay him twenty pounds.”\n\nThis strongly marked way of doing business made a strongly marked impression on me, and that not of an agreeable kind. Mr. Jaggers never laughed; but he wore great bright creaking boots, and, in poising himself on these boots, with his large head bent down and his eyebrows joined together, awaiting an answer, he sometimes caused the boots to creak, as if they laughed in a dry and suspicious way. As he happened to go out now, and as Wemmick was brisk and talkative, I said to Wemmick that I hardly knew what to make of Mr. Jaggers’s manner.\n\n“Tell him that, and he’ll take it as a compliment,” answered Wemmick; “he don’t mean that you should know what to make of it.—Oh!” for I looked surprised, “it’s not personal; it’s professional: only professional.”\n\nWemmick was at his desk, lunching—and crunching—on a dry hard biscuit; pieces of which he threw from time to time into his slit of a mouth, as if he were posting them.\n\n“Always seems to me,” said Wemmick, “as if he had set a man-trap and was watching it. Suddenly—click—you’re caught!”\n\nWithout remarking that man-traps were not among the amenities of life, I said I supposed he was very skilful?\n\n“Deep,” said Wemmick, “as Australia.” Pointing with his pen at the office floor, to express that Australia was understood, for the purposes of the figure, to be symmetrically on the opposite spot of the globe. “If there was anything deeper,” added Wemmick, bringing his pen to paper, “he’d be it.”\n\nThen, I said I supposed he had a fine business, and Wemmick said, “Ca-pi-tal!” Then I asked if there were many clerks? to which he replied,—\n\n“We don’t run much into clerks, because there’s only one Jaggers, and people won’t have him at second hand. There are only four of us. Would you like to see ’em? You are one of us, as I may say.”\n\nI accepted the offer. When Mr. Wemmick had put all the biscuit into the post, and had paid me my money from a cash-box in a safe, the key of which safe he kept somewhere down his back and produced from his coat-collar like an iron-pigtail, we went upstairs. The house was dark and shabby, and the greasy shoulders that had left their mark in Mr. Jaggers’s room seemed to have been shuffling up and down the staircase for years. In the front first floor, a clerk who looked something between a publican and a rat-catcher—a large pale, puffed, swollen man—was attentively engaged with three or four people of shabby appearance, whom he treated as unceremoniously as everybody seemed to be treated who contributed to Mr. Jaggers’s coffers. “Getting evidence together,” said Mr. Wemmick, as we came out, “for the Bailey.” In the room over that, a little flabby terrier of a clerk with dangling hair (his cropping seemed to have been forgotten when he was a puppy) was similarly engaged with a man with weak eyes, whom Mr. Wemmick presented to me as a smelter who kept his pot always boiling, and who would melt me anything I pleased,—and who was in an excessive white-perspiration, as if he had been trying his art on himself. In a back room, a high-shouldered man with a face-ache tied up in dirty flannel, who was dressed in old black clothes that bore the appearance of having been waxed, was stooping over his work of making fair copies of the notes of the other two gentlemen, for Mr. Jaggers’s own use.\n\nThis was all the establishment. When we went downstairs again, Wemmick led me into my guardian’s room, and said, “This you’ve seen already.”\n\n“Pray,” said I, as the two odious casts with the twitchy leer upon them caught my sight again, “whose likenesses are those?”\n\n“These?” said Wemmick, getting upon a chair, and blowing the dust off the horrible heads before bringing them down. “These are two celebrated ones. Famous clients of ours that got us a world of credit. This chap (why you must have come down in the night and been peeping into the inkstand, to get this blot upon your eyebrow, you old rascal!) murdered his master, and, considering that he wasn’t brought up to evidence, didn’t plan it badly.”\n\n“Is it like him?” I asked, recoiling from the brute, as Wemmick spat upon his eyebrow and gave it a rub with his sleeve.\n\n“Like him? It’s himself, you know. The cast was made in Newgate, directly after he was taken down. You had a particular fancy for me, hadn’t you, Old Artful?” said Wemmick. He then explained this affectionate apostrophe, by touching his brooch representing the lady and the weeping willow at the tomb with the urn upon it, and saying, “Had it made for me, express!”\n\n“Is the lady anybody?” said I.\n\n“No,” returned Wemmick. “Only his game. (You liked your bit of game, didn’t you?) No; deuce a bit of a lady in the case, Mr. Pip, except one,—and she wasn’t of this slender lady-like sort, and you wouldn’t have caught her looking after this urn, unless there was something to drink in it.” Wemmick’s attention being thus directed to his brooch, he put down the cast, and polished the brooch with his pocket-handkerchief.\n\n“Did that other creature come to the same end?” I asked. “He has the same look.”\n\n“You’re right,” said Wemmick; “it’s the genuine look. Much as if one nostril was caught up with a horse-hair and a little fish-hook. Yes, he came to the same end; quite the natural end here, I assure you. He forged wills, this blade did, if he didn’t also put the supposed testators to sleep too. You were a gentlemanly Cove, though” (Mr. Wemmick was again apostrophising), “and you said you could write Greek. Yah, Bounceable! What a liar you were! I never met such a liar as you!” Before putting his late friend on his shelf again, Wemmick touched the largest of his mourning rings and said, “Sent out to buy it for me, only the day before.”\n\nWhile he was putting up the other cast and coming down from the chair, the thought crossed my mind that all his personal jewelry was derived from like sources. As he had shown no diffidence on the subject, I ventured on the liberty of asking him the question, when he stood before me, dusting his hands.\n\n“O yes,” he returned, “these are all gifts of that kind. One brings another, you see; that’s the way of it. I always take ’em. They’re curiosities. And they’re property. They may not be worth much, but, after all, they’re property and portable. It don’t signify to you with your brilliant lookout, but as to myself, my guiding-star always is, ‘Get hold of portable property’.”\n\nWhen I had rendered homage to this light, he went on to say, in a friendly manner:—\n\n“If at any odd time when you have nothing better to do, you wouldn’t mind coming over to see me at Walworth, I could offer you a bed, and I should consider it an honour. I have not much to show you; but such two or three curiosities as I have got you might like to look over; and I am fond of a bit of garden and a summer-house.”\n\nI said I should be delighted to accept his hospitality.\n\n“Thankee,” said he; “then we’ll consider that it’s to come off, when convenient to you. Have you dined with Mr. Jaggers yet?”\n\n“Not yet.”\n\n“Well,” said Wemmick, “he’ll give you wine, and good wine. I’ll give you punch, and not bad punch. And now I’ll tell you something. When you go to dine with Mr. Jaggers, look at his housekeeper.”\n\n“Shall I see something very uncommon?”\n\n“Well,” said Wemmick, “you’ll see a wild beast tamed. Not so very uncommon, you’ll tell me. I reply, that depends on the original wildness of the beast, and the amount of taming. It won’t lower your opinion of Mr. Jaggers’s powers. Keep your eye on it.”\n\nI told him I would do so, with all the interest and curiosity that his preparation awakened. As I was taking my departure, he asked me if I would like to devote five minutes to seeing Mr. Jaggers “at it?”\n\nFor several reasons, and not least because I didn’t clearly know what Mr. Jaggers would be found to be “at,” I replied in the affirmative. We dived into the City, and came up in a crowded police-court, where a blood-relation (in the murderous sense) of the deceased, with the fanciful taste in brooches, was standing at the bar, uncomfortably chewing something; while my guardian had a woman under examination or cross-examination,—I don’t know which,—and was striking her, and the bench, and everybody present, with awe. If anybody, of whatsoever degree, said a word that he didn’t approve of, he instantly required to have it “taken down.” If anybody wouldn’t make an admission, he said, “I’ll have it out of you!” and if anybody made an admission, he said, “Now I have got you!” The magistrates shivered under a single bite of his finger. Thieves and thief-takers hung in dread rapture on his words, and shrank when a hair of his eyebrows turned in their direction. Which side he was on I couldn’t make out, for he seemed to me to be grinding the whole place in a mill; I only know that when I stole out on tiptoe, he was not on the side of the bench; for, he was making the legs of the old gentleman who presided, quite convulsive under the table, by his denunciations of his conduct as the representative of British law and justice in that chair that day.\n\n# Chapter XXV.\n\nBentley Drummle, who was so sulky a fellow that he even took up a book as if its writer had done him an injury, did not take up an acquaintance in a more agreeable spirit. Heavy in figure, movement, and comprehension,—in the sluggish complexion of his face, and in the large, awkward tongue that seemed to loll about in his mouth as he himself lolled about in a room,—he was idle, proud, niggardly, reserved, and suspicious. He came of rich people down in Somersetshire, who had nursed this combination of qualities until they made the discovery that it was just of age and a blockhead. Thus, Bentley Drummle had come to Mr. Pocket when he was a head taller than that gentleman, and half a dozen heads thicker than most gentlemen.\n\nStartop had been spoilt by a weak mother and kept at home when he ought to have been at school, but he was devotedly attached to her, and admired her beyond measure. He had a woman’s delicacy of feature, and was—“as you may see, though you never saw her,” said Herbert to me—“exactly like his mother.” It was but natural that I should take to him much more kindly than to Drummle, and that, even in the earliest evenings of our boating, he and I should pull homeward abreast of one another, conversing from boat to boat, while Bentley Drummle came up in our wake alone, under the overhanging banks and among the rushes. He would always creep in-shore like some uncomfortable amphibious creature, even when the tide would have sent him fast upon his way; and I always think of him as coming after us in the dark or by the back-water, when our own two boats were breaking the sunset or the moonlight in mid-stream.\n\nHerbert was my intimate companion and friend. I presented him with a half-share in my boat, which was the occasion of his often coming down to Hammersmith; and my possession of a half-share in his chambers often took me up to London. We used to walk between the two places at all hours. I have an affection for the road yet (though it is not so pleasant a road as it was then), formed in the impressibility of untried youth and hope.\n\nWhen I had been in Mr. Pocket’s family a month or two, Mr. and Mrs. Camilla turned up. Camilla was Mr. Pocket’s sister. Georgiana, whom I had seen at Miss Havisham’s on the same occasion, also turned up. She was a cousin,—an indigestive single woman, who called her rigidity religion, and her liver love. These people hated me with the hatred of cupidity and disappointment. As a matter of course, they fawned upon me in my prosperity with the basest meanness. Towards Mr. Pocket, as a grown-up infant with no notion of his own interests, they showed the complacent forbearance I had heard them express. Mrs. Pocket they held in contempt; but they allowed the poor soul to have been heavily disappointed in life, because that shed a feeble reflected light upon themselves.\n\nThese were the surroundings among which I settled down, and applied myself to my education. I soon contracted expensive habits, and began to spend an amount of money that within a few short months I should have thought almost fabulous; but through good and evil I stuck to my books. There was no other merit in this, than my having sense enough to feel my deficiencies. Between Mr. Pocket and Herbert I got on fast; and, with one or the other always at my elbow to give me the start I wanted, and clear obstructions out of my road, I must have been as great a dolt as Drummle if I had done less.\n\nI had not seen Mr. Wemmick for some weeks, when I thought I would write him a note and propose to go home with him on a certain evening. He replied that it would give him much pleasure, and that he would expect me at the office at six o’clock. Thither I went, and there I found him, putting the key of his safe down his back as the clock struck.\n\n“Did you think of walking down to Walworth?” said he.\n\n“Certainly,” said I, “if you approve.”\n\n“Very much,” was Wemmick’s reply, “for I have had my legs under the desk all day, and shall be glad to stretch them. Now, I’ll tell you what I have got for supper, Mr. Pip. I have got a stewed steak,—which is of home preparation,—and a cold roast fowl,—which is from the cook’s-shop. I think it’s tender, because the master of the shop was a Juryman in some cases of ours the other day, and we let him down easy. I reminded him of it when I bought the fowl, and I said, “Pick us out a good one, old Briton, because if we had chosen to keep you in the box another day or two, we could easily have done it.” He said to that, “Let me make you a present of the best fowl in the shop.” I let him, of course. As far as it goes, it’s property and portable. You don’t object to an aged parent, I hope?”\n\nI really thought he was still speaking of the fowl, until he added, “Because I have got an aged parent at my place.” I then said what politeness required.\n\n“So, you haven’t dined with Mr. Jaggers yet?” he pursued, as we walked along.\n\n“Not yet.”\n\n“He told me so this afternoon when he heard you were coming. I expect you’ll have an invitation to-morrow. He’s going to ask your pals, too. Three of ’em; ain’t there?”\n\nAlthough I was not in the habit of counting Drummle as one of my intimate associates, I answered, “Yes.”\n\n“Well, he’s going to ask the whole gang,”—I hardly felt complimented by the word,—“and whatever he gives you, he’ll give you good. Don’t look forward to variety, but you’ll have excellence. And there’s another rum thing in his house,” proceeded Wemmick, after a moment’s pause, as if the remark followed on the housekeeper understood; “he never lets a door or window be fastened at night.”\n\n“Is he never robbed?”\n\n“That’s it!” returned Wemmick. “He says, and gives it out publicly, “I want to see the man who’ll rob me.” Lord bless you, I have heard him, a hundred times, if I have heard him once, say to regular cracksmen in our front office, “You know where I live; now, no bolt is ever drawn there; why don’t you do a stroke of business with me? Come; can’t I tempt you?” Not a man of them, sir, would be bold enough to try it on, for love or money.”\n\n“They dread him so much?” said I.\n\n“Dread him,” said Wemmick. “I believe you they dread him. Not but what he’s artful, even in his defiance of them. No silver, sir. Britannia metal, every spoon.”\n\n“So they wouldn’t have much,” I observed, “even if they—”\n\n“Ah! But he would have much,” said Wemmick, cutting me short, “and they know it. He’d have their lives, and the lives of scores of ’em. He’d have all he could get. And it’s impossible to say what he couldn’t get, if he gave his mind to it.”\n\nI was falling into meditation on my guardian’s greatness, when Wemmick remarked:—\n\n“As to the absence of plate, that’s only his natural depth, you know. A river’s its natural depth, and he’s his natural depth. Look at his watch-chain. That’s real enough.”\n\n“It’s very massive,” said I.\n\n“Massive?” repeated Wemmick. “I think so. And his watch is a gold repeater, and worth a hundred pound if it’s worth a penny. Mr. Pip, there are about seven hundred thieves in this town who know all about that watch; there’s not a man, a woman, or a child, among them, who wouldn’t identify the smallest link in that chain, and drop it as if it was red hot, if inveigled into touching it.”\n\nAt first with such discourse, and afterwards with conversation of a more general nature, did Mr. Wemmick and I beguile the time and the road, until he gave me to understand that we had arrived in the district of Walworth.\n\nIt appeared to be a collection of back lanes, ditches, and little gardens, and to present the aspect of a rather dull retirement. Wemmick’s house was a little wooden cottage in the midst of plots of garden, and the top of it was cut out and painted like a battery mounted with guns.\n\n“My own doing,” said Wemmick. “Looks pretty; don’t it?”\n\nI highly commended it, I think it was the smallest house I ever saw; with the queerest gothic windows (by far the greater part of them sham), and a gothic door almost too small to get in at.\n\n“That’s a real flagstaff, you see,” said Wemmick, “and on Sundays I run up a real flag. Then look here. After I have crossed this bridge, I hoist it up—so—and cut off the communication.”\n\nThe bridge was a plank, and it crossed a chasm about four feet wide and two deep. But it was very pleasant to see the pride with which he hoisted it up and made it fast; smiling as he did so, with a relish and not merely mechanically.\n\n“At nine o’clock every night, Greenwich time,” said Wemmick, “the gun fires. There he is, you see! And when you hear him go, I think you’ll say he’s a Stinger.”\n\nThe piece of ordnance referred to, was mounted in a separate fortress, constructed of lattice-work. It was protected from the weather by an ingenious little tarpaulin contrivance in the nature of an umbrella.\n\n“Then, at the back,” said Wemmick, “out of sight, so as not to impede the idea of fortifications,—for it’s a principle with me, if you have an idea, carry it out and keep it up,—I don’t know whether that’s your opinion—”\n\nI said, decidedly.\n\n“—At the back, there’s a pig, and there are fowls and rabbits; then, I knock together my own little frame, you see, and grow cucumbers; and you’ll judge at supper what sort of a salad I can raise. So, sir,” said Wemmick, smiling again, but seriously too, as he shook his head, “if you can suppose the little place besieged, it would hold out a devil of a time in point of provisions.”\n\nThen, he conducted me to a bower about a dozen yards off, but which was approached by such ingenious twists of path that it took quite a long time to get at; and in this retreat our glasses were already set forth. Our punch was cooling in an ornamental lake, on whose margin the bower was raised. This piece of water (with an island in the middle which might have been the salad for supper) was of a circular form, and he had constructed a fountain in it, which, when you set a little mill going and took a cork out of a pipe, played to that powerful extent that it made the back of your hand quite wet.\n\n“I am my own engineer, and my own carpenter, and my own plumber, and my own gardener, and my own Jack of all Trades,” said Wemmick, in acknowledging my compliments. “Well; it’s a good thing, you know. It brushes the Newgate cobwebs away, and pleases the Aged. You wouldn’t mind being at once introduced to the Aged, would you? It wouldn’t put you out?”\n\nI expressed the readiness I felt, and we went into the castle. There we found, sitting by a fire, a very old man in a flannel coat: clean, cheerful, comfortable, and well cared for, but intensely deaf.\n\n“Well aged parent,” said Wemmick, shaking hands with him in a cordial and jocose way, “how am you?”\n\n“All right, John; all right!” replied the old man.\n\n“Here’s Mr. Pip, aged parent,” said Wemmick, “and I wish you could hear his name. Nod away at him, Mr. Pip; that’s what he likes. Nod away at him, if you please, like winking!”\n\n“This is a fine place of my son’s, sir,” cried the old man, while I nodded as hard as I possibly could. “This is a pretty pleasure-ground, sir. This spot and these beautiful works upon it ought to be kept together by the Nation, after my son’s time, for the people’s enjoyment.”\n\n“You’re as proud of it as Punch; ain’t you, Aged?” said Wemmick, contemplating the old man, with his hard face really softened; “there’s a nod for you;” giving him a tremendous one; “there’s another for you;” giving him a still more tremendous one; “you like that, don’t you? If you’re not tired, Mr. Pip—though I know it’s tiring to strangers—will you tip him one more? You can’t think how it pleases him.”\n\nI tipped him several more, and he was in great spirits. We left him bestirring himself to feed the fowls, and we sat down to our punch in the arbour; where Wemmick told me, as he smoked a pipe, that it had taken him a good many years to bring the property up to its present pitch of perfection.\n\n“Is it your own, Mr. Wemmick?”\n\n“O yes,” said Wemmick, “I have got hold of it, a bit at a time. It’s a freehold, by George!”\n\n“Is it indeed? I hope Mr. Jaggers admires it?”\n\n“Never seen it,” said Wemmick. “Never heard of it. Never seen the Aged. Never heard of him. No; the office is one thing, and private life is another. When I go into the office, I leave the Castle behind me, and when I come into the Castle, I leave the office behind me. If it’s not in any way disagreeable to you, you’ll oblige me by doing the same. I don’t wish it professionally spoken about.”\n\nOf course I felt my good faith involved in the observance of his request. The punch being very nice, we sat there drinking it and talking, until it was almost nine o’clock. “Getting near gun-fire,” said Wemmick then, as he laid down his pipe; “it’s the Aged’s treat.”\n\nProceeding into the Castle again, we found the Aged heating the poker, with expectant eyes, as a preliminary to the performance of this great nightly ceremony. Wemmick stood with his watch in his hand until the moment was come for him to take the red-hot poker from the Aged, and repair to the battery. He took it, and went out, and presently the Stinger went off with a Bang that shook the crazy little box of a cottage as if it must fall to pieces, and made every glass and teacup in it ring. Upon this, the Aged—who I believe would have been blown out of his arm-chair but for holding on by the elbows—cried out exultingly, “He’s fired! I heerd him!” and I nodded at the old gentleman until it is no figure of speech to declare that I absolutely could not see him.\n\nThe interval between that time and supper Wemmick devoted to showing me his collection of curiosities. They were mostly of a felonious character; comprising the pen with which a celebrated forgery had been committed, a distinguished razor or two, some locks of hair, and several manuscript confessions written under condemnation,—upon which Mr. Wemmick set particular value as being, to use his own words, “every one of ’em Lies, sir.” These were agreeably dispersed among small specimens of china and glass, various neat trifles made by the proprietor of the museum, and some tobacco-stoppers carved by the Aged. They were all displayed in that chamber of the Castle into which I had been first inducted, and which served, not only as the general sitting-room but as the kitchen too, if I might judge from a saucepan on the hob, and a brazen bijou over the fireplace designed for the suspension of a roasting-jack.\n\nThere was a neat little girl in attendance, who looked after the Aged in the day. When she had laid the supper-cloth, the bridge was lowered to give her means of egress, and she withdrew for the night. The supper was excellent; and though the Castle was rather subject to dry-rot insomuch that it tasted like a bad nut, and though the pig might have been farther off, I was heartily pleased with my whole entertainment. Nor was there any drawback on my little turret bedroom, beyond there being such a very thin ceiling between me and the flagstaff, that when I lay down on my back in bed, it seemed as if I had to balance that pole on my forehead all night.\n\nWemmick was up early in the morning, and I am afraid I heard him cleaning my boots. After that, he fell to gardening, and I saw him from my gothic window pretending to employ the Aged, and nodding at him in a most devoted manner. Our breakfast was as good as the supper, and at half-past eight precisely we started for Little Britain. By degrees, Wemmick got dryer and harder as we went along, and his mouth tightened into a post-office again. At last, when we got to his place of business and he pulled out his key from his coat-collar, he looked as unconscious of his Walworth property as if the Castle and the drawbridge and the arbour and the lake and the fountain and the Aged, had all been blown into space together by the last discharge of the Stinger.\n\n# Chapter XXVI.\n\nIt fell out as Wemmick had told me it would, that I had an early opportunity of comparing my guardian’s establishment with that of his cashier and clerk. My guardian was in his room, washing his hands with his scented soap, when I went into the office from Walworth; and he called me to him, and gave me the invitation for myself and friends which Wemmick had prepared me to receive. “No ceremony,” he stipulated, “and no dinner dress, and say to-morrow.” I asked him where we should come to (for I had no idea where he lived), and I believe it was in his general objection to make anything like an admission, that he replied, “Come here, and I’ll take you home with me.” I embrace this opportunity of remarking that he washed his clients off, as if he were a surgeon or a dentist. He had a closet in his room, fitted up for the purpose, which smelt of the scented soap like a perfumer’s shop. It had an unusually large jack-towel on a roller inside the door, and he would wash his hands, and wipe them and dry them all over this towel, whenever he came in from a police court or dismissed a client from his room. When I and my friends repaired to him at six o’clock next day, he seemed to have been engaged on a case of a darker complexion than usual, for we found him with his head butted into this closet, not only washing his hands, but laving his face and gargling his throat. And even when he had done all that, and had gone all round the jack-towel, he took out his penknife and scraped the case out of his nails before he put his coat on.\n\nThere were some people slinking about as usual when we passed out into the street, who were evidently anxious to speak with him; but there was something so conclusive in the halo of scented soap which encircled his presence, that they gave it up for that day. As we walked along westward, he was recognised ever and again by some face in the crowd of the streets, and whenever that happened he talked louder to me; but he never otherwise recognised anybody, or took notice that anybody recognised him.\n\nHe conducted us to Gerrard Street, Soho, to a house on the south side of that street. Rather a stately house of its kind, but dolefully in want of painting, and with dirty windows. He took out his key and opened the door, and we all went into a stone hall, bare, gloomy, and little used. So, up a dark brown staircase into a series of three dark brown rooms on the first floor. There were carved garlands on the panelled walls, and as he stood among them giving us welcome, I know what kind of loops I thought they looked like.\n\nDinner was laid in the best of these rooms; the second was his dressing-room; the third, his bedroom. He told us that he held the whole house, but rarely used more of it than we saw. The table was comfortably laid—no silver in the service, of course—and at the side of his chair was a capacious dumb-waiter, with a variety of bottles and decanters on it, and four dishes of fruit for dessert. I noticed throughout, that he kept everything under his own hand, and distributed everything himself.\n\nThere was a bookcase in the room; I saw from the backs of the books, that they were about evidence, criminal law, criminal biography, trials, acts of Parliament, and such things. The furniture was all very solid and good, like his watch-chain. It had an official look, however, and there was nothing merely ornamental to be seen. In a corner was a little table of papers with a shaded lamp: so that he seemed to bring the office home with him in that respect too, and to wheel it out of an evening and fall to work.\n\nAs he had scarcely seen my three companions until now,—for he and I had walked together,—he stood on the hearth-rug, after ringing the bell, and took a searching look at them. To my surprise, he seemed at once to be principally if not solely interested in Drummle.\n\n“Pip,” said he, putting his large hand on my shoulder and moving me to the window, “I don’t know one from the other. Who’s the Spider?”\n\n“The spider?” said I.\n\n“The blotchy, sprawly, sulky fellow.”\n\n“That’s Bentley Drummle,” I replied; “the one with the delicate face is Startop.”\n\nNot making the least account of “the one with the delicate face,” he returned, “Bentley Drummle is his name, is it? I like the look of that fellow.”\n\nHe immediately began to talk to Drummle: not at all deterred by his replying in his heavy reticent way, but apparently led on by it to screw discourse out of him. I was looking at the two, when there came between me and them the housekeeper, with the first dish for the table.\n\nShe was a woman of about forty, I supposed,—but I may have thought her younger than she was. Rather tall, of a lithe nimble figure, extremely pale, with large faded eyes, and a quantity of streaming hair. I cannot say whether any diseased affection of the heart caused her lips to be parted as if she were panting, and her face to bear a curious expression of suddenness and flutter; but I know that I had been to see Macbeth at the theatre, a night or two before, and that her face looked to me as if it were all disturbed by fiery air, like the faces I had seen rise out of the Witches’ caldron.\n\nShe set the dish on, touched my guardian quietly on the arm with a finger to notify that dinner was ready, and vanished. We took our seats at the round table, and my guardian kept Drummle on one side of him, while Startop sat on the other. It was a noble dish of fish that the housekeeper had put on table, and we had a joint of equally choice mutton afterwards, and then an equally choice bird. Sauces, wines, all the accessories we wanted, and all of the best, were given out by our host from his dumb-waiter; and when they had made the circuit of the table, he always put them back again. Similarly, he dealt us clean plates and knives and forks, for each course, and dropped those just disused into two baskets on the ground by his chair. No other attendant than the housekeeper appeared. She set on every dish; and I always saw in her face, a face rising out of the caldron. Years afterwards, I made a dreadful likeness of that woman, by causing a face that had no other natural resemblance to it than it derived from flowing hair to pass behind a bowl of flaming spirits in a dark room.\n\nInduced to take particular notice of the housekeeper, both by her own striking appearance and by Wemmick’s preparation, I observed that whenever she was in the room she kept her eyes attentively on my guardian, and that she would remove her hands from any dish she put before him, hesitatingly, as if she dreaded his calling her back, and wanted him to speak when she was nigh, if he had anything to say. I fancied that I could detect in his manner a consciousness of this, and a purpose of always holding her in suspense.\n\nDinner went off gayly, and although my guardian seemed to follow rather than originate subjects, I knew that he wrenched the weakest part of our dispositions out of us. For myself, I found that I was expressing my tendency to lavish expenditure, and to patronise Herbert, and to boast of my great prospects, before I quite knew that I had opened my lips. It was so with all of us, but with no one more than Drummle: the development of whose inclination to gird in a grudging and suspicious way at the rest, was screwed out of him before the fish was taken off.\n\nIt was not then, but when we had got to the cheese, that our conversation turned upon our rowing feats, and that Drummle was rallied for coming up behind of a night in that slow amphibious way of his. Drummle upon this, informed our host that he much preferred our room to our company, and that as to skill he was more than our master, and that as to strength he could scatter us like chaff. By some invisible agency, my guardian wound him up to a pitch little short of ferocity about this trifle; and he fell to baring and spanning his arm to show how muscular it was, and we all fell to baring and spanning our arms in a ridiculous manner.\n\nNow the housekeeper was at that time clearing the table; my guardian, taking no heed of her, but with the side of his face turned from her, was leaning back in his chair biting the side of his forefinger and showing an interest in Drummle, that, to me, was quite inexplicable. Suddenly, he clapped his large hand on the housekeeper’s, like a trap, as she stretched it across the table. So suddenly and smartly did he do this, that we all stopped in our foolish contention.\n\n“If you talk of strength,” said Mr. Jaggers, “I’ll show you a wrist. Molly, let them see your wrist.”\n\nHer entrapped hand was on the table, but she had already put her other hand behind her waist. “Master,” she said, in a low voice, with her eyes attentively and entreatingly fixed upon him. “Don’t.”\n\n“I’ll show you a wrist,” repeated Mr. Jaggers, with an immovable determination to show it. “Molly, let them see your wrist.”\n\n“Master,” she again murmured. “Please!”\n\n“Molly,” said Mr. Jaggers, not looking at her, but obstinately looking at the opposite side of the room, “let them see both your wrists. Show them. Come!”\n\nHe took his hand from hers, and turned that wrist up on the table. She brought her other hand from behind her, and held the two out side by side. The last wrist was much disfigured,—deeply scarred and scarred across and across. When she held her hands out she took her eyes from Mr. Jaggers, and turned them watchfully on every one of the rest of us in succession.\n\n“There’s power here,” said Mr. Jaggers, coolly tracing out the sinews with his forefinger. “Very few men have the power of wrist that this woman has. It’s remarkable what mere force of grip there is in these hands. I have had occasion to notice many hands; but I never saw stronger in that respect, man’s or woman’s, than these.”\n\nWhile he said these words in a leisurely, critical style, she continued to look at every one of us in regular succession as we sat. The moment he ceased, she looked at him again. “That’ll do, Molly,” said Mr. Jaggers, giving her a slight nod; “you have been admired, and can go.” She withdrew her hands and went out of the room, and Mr. Jaggers, putting the decanters on from his dumb-waiter, filled his glass and passed round the wine.\n\n“At half-past nine, gentlemen,” said he, “we must break up. Pray make the best use of your time. I am glad to see you all. Mr. Drummle, I drink to you.”\n\nIf his object in singling out Drummle were to bring him out still more, it perfectly succeeded. In a sulky triumph, Drummle showed his morose depreciation of the rest of us, in a more and more offensive degree, until he became downright intolerable. Through all his stages, Mr. Jaggers followed him with the same strange interest. He actually seemed to serve as a zest to Mr. Jaggers’s wine.\n\nIn our boyish want of discretion I dare say we took too much to drink, and I know we talked too much. We became particularly hot upon some boorish sneer of Drummle’s, to the effect that we were too free with our money. It led to my remarking, with more zeal than discretion, that it came with a bad grace from him, to whom Startop had lent money in my presence but a week or so before.\n\n“Well,” retorted Drummle; “he’ll be paid.”\n\n“I don’t mean to imply that he won’t,” said I, “but it might make you hold your tongue about us and our money, I should think.”\n\n“You should think!” retorted Drummle. “Oh Lord!”\n\n“I dare say,” I went on, meaning to be very severe, “that you wouldn’t lend money to any of us if we wanted it.”\n\n“You are right,” said Drummle. “I wouldn’t lend one of you a sixpence. I wouldn’t lend anybody a sixpence.”\n\n“Rather mean to borrow under those circumstances, I should say.”\n\n“You should say,” repeated Drummle. “Oh Lord!”\n\nThis was so very aggravating—the more especially as I found myself making no way against his surly obtuseness—that I said, disregarding Herbert’s efforts to check me,—\n\n“Come, Mr. Drummle, since we are on the subject, I’ll tell you what passed between Herbert here and me, when you borrowed that money.”\n\n“I don’t want to know what passed between Herbert there and you,” growled Drummle. And I think he added in a lower growl, that we might both go to the devil and shake ourselves.\n\n“I’ll tell you, however,” said I, “whether you want to know or not. We said that as you put it in your pocket very glad to get it, you seemed to be immensely amused at his being so weak as to lend it.”\n\nDrummle laughed outright, and sat laughing in our faces, with his hands in his pockets and his round shoulders raised; plainly signifying that it was quite true, and that he despised us as asses all.\n\nHereupon Startop took him in hand, though with a much better grace than I had shown, and exhorted him to be a little more agreeable. Startop, being a lively, bright young fellow, and Drummle being the exact opposite, the latter was always disposed to resent him as a direct personal affront. He now retorted in a coarse, lumpish way, and Startop tried to turn the discussion aside with some small pleasantry that made us all laugh. Resenting this little success more than anything, Drummle, without any threat or warning, pulled his hands out of his pockets, dropped his round shoulders, swore, took up a large glass, and would have flung it at his adversary’s head, but for our entertainer’s dexterously seizing it at the instant when it was raised for that purpose.\n\n“Gentlemen,” said Mr. Jaggers, deliberately putting down the glass, and hauling out his gold repeater by its massive chain, “I am exceedingly sorry to announce that it’s half past nine.”\n\nOn this hint we all rose to depart. Before we got to the street door, Startop was cheerily calling Drummle “old boy,” as if nothing had happened. But the old boy was so far from responding, that he would not even walk to Hammersmith on the same side of the way; so Herbert and I, who remained in town, saw them going down the street on opposite sides; Startop leading, and Drummle lagging behind in the shadow of the houses, much as he was wont to follow in his boat.\n\nAs the door was not yet shut, I thought I would leave Herbert there for a moment, and run upstairs again to say a word to my guardian. I found him in his dressing-room surrounded by his stock of boots, already hard at it, washing his hands of us.\n\nI told him I had come up again to say how sorry I was that anything disagreeable should have occurred, and that I hoped he would not blame me much.\n\n“Pooh!” said he, sluicing his face, and speaking through the water-drops; “it’s nothing, Pip. I like that Spider though.”\n\nHe had turned towards me now, and was shaking his head, and blowing, and towelling himself.\n\n“I am glad you like him, sir,” said I—“but I don’t.”\n\n“No, no,” my guardian assented; “don’t have too much to do with him. Keep as clear of him as you can. But I like the fellow, Pip; he is one of the true sort. Why, if I was a fortune-teller—”\n\nLooking out of the towel, he caught my eye.\n\n“But I am not a fortune-teller,” he said, letting his head drop into a festoon of towel, and towelling away at his two ears. “You know what I am, don’t you? Good night, Pip.”\n\n“Good night, sir.”\n\nIn about a month after that, the Spider’s time with Mr. Pocket was up for good, and, to the great relief of all the house but Mrs. Pocket, he went home to the family hole.\n\n“I write this by request of Mr. Gargery, for to let you know that he is going to London in company with Mr. Wopsle and would be glad if agreeable to be allowed to see you. He would call at Barnard’s Hotel Tuesday morning at nine o’clock, when if not agreeable please leave word. Your poor sister is much the same as when you left. We talk of you in the kitchen every night, and wonder what you are saying and doing. If now considered in the light of a liberty, excuse it for the love of poor old days. No more, dear Mr. Pip, from\n\n“Your ever obliged, and affectionate servant, “BIDDY.”\n\n“P.S. He wishes me most particular to write what larks. He says you will understand. I hope and do not doubt it will be agreeable to see him, even though a gentleman, for you had ever a good heart, and he is a worthy, worthy man. I have read him all, excepting only the last little sentence, and he wishes me most particular to write again what larks.”\n\nI received this letter by the post on Monday morning, and therefore its appointment was for next day. Let me confess exactly with what feelings I looked forward to Joe’s coming.\n\nNot with pleasure, though I was bound to him by so many ties; no; with considerable disturbance, some mortification, and a keen sense of incongruity. If I could have kept him away by paying money, I certainly would have paid money. My greatest reassurance was that he was coming to Barnard’s Inn, not to Hammersmith, and consequently would not fall in Bentley Drummle’s way. I had little objection to his being seen by Herbert or his father, for both of whom I had a respect; but I had the sharpest sensitiveness as to his being seen by Drummle, whom I held in contempt. So, throughout life, our worst weaknesses and meannesses are usually committed for the sake of the people whom we most despise.\n\nI had begun to be always decorating the chambers in some quite unnecessary and inappropriate way or other, and very expensive those wrestles with Barnard proved to be. By this time, the rooms were vastly different from what I had found them, and I enjoyed the honour of occupying a few prominent pages in the books of a neighbouring upholsterer. I had got on so fast of late, that I had even started a boy in boots,—top boots,—in bondage and slavery to whom I might have been said to pass my days. For, after I had made the monster (out of the refuse of my washerwoman’s family), and had clothed him with a blue coat, canary waistcoat, white cravat, creamy breeches, and the boots already mentioned, I had to find him a little to do and a great deal to eat; and with both of those horrible requirements he haunted my existence.\n\nThis avenging phantom was ordered to be on duty at eight on Tuesday morning in the hall, (it was two feet square, as charged for floorcloth,) and Herbert suggested certain things for breakfast that he thought Joe would like. While I felt sincerely obliged to him for being so interested and considerate, I had an odd half-provoked sense of suspicion upon me, that if Joe had been coming to see him, he wouldn’t have been quite so brisk about it.\n\nHowever, I came into town on the Monday night to be ready for Joe, and I got up early in the morning, and caused the sitting-room and breakfast-table to assume their most splendid appearance. Unfortunately the morning was drizzly, and an angel could not have concealed the fact that Barnard was shedding sooty tears outside the window, like some weak giant of a Sweep.\n\nAs the time approached I should have liked to run away, but the Avenger pursuant to orders was in the hall, and presently I heard Joe on the staircase. I knew it was Joe, by his clumsy manner of coming upstairs,—his state boots being always too big for him,—and by the time it took him to read the names on the other floors in the course of his ascent. When at last he stopped outside our door, I could hear his finger tracing over the painted letters of my name, and I afterwards distinctly heard him breathing in at the keyhole. Finally he gave a faint single rap, and Pepper—such was the compromising name of the avenging boy—announced “Mr. Gargery!” I thought he never would have done wiping his feet, and that I must have gone out to lift him off the mat, but at last he came in.\n\n“Joe, how are you, Joe?”\n\n“Pip, how AIR you, Pip?”\n\nWith his good honest face all glowing and shining, and his hat put down on the floor between us, he caught both my hands and worked them straight up and down, as if I had been the last-patented Pump.\n\n“I am glad to see you, Joe. Give me your hat.”\n\nBut Joe, taking it up carefully with both hands, like a bird’s-nest with eggs in it, wouldn’t hear of parting with that piece of property, and persisted in standing talking over it in a most uncomfortable way.\n\n“Which you have that growed,” said Joe, “and that swelled, and that gentle-folked;” Joe considered a little before he discovered this word; “as to be sure you are a honour to your king and country.”\n\n“And you, Joe, look wonderfully well.”\n\n“Thank God,” said Joe, “I’m ekerval to most. And your sister, she’s no worse than she were. And Biddy, she’s ever right and ready. And all friends is no backerder, if not no forarder. ’Ceptin Wopsle; he’s had a drop.”\n\nAll this time (still with both hands taking great care of the bird’s-nest), Joe was rolling his eyes round and round the room, and round and round the flowered pattern of my dressing-gown.\n\n“Had a drop, Joe?”\n\n“Why yes,” said Joe, lowering his voice, “he’s left the Church and went into the playacting. Which the playacting have likeways brought him to London along with me. And his wish were,” said Joe, getting the bird’s-nest under his left arm for the moment, and groping in it for an egg with his right; “if no offence, as I would ’and you that.”\n\nI took what Joe gave me, and found it to be the crumpled play-bill of a small metropolitan theatre, announcing the first appearance, in that very week, of “the celebrated Provincial Amateur of Roscian renown, whose unique performance in the highest tragic walk of our National Bard has lately occasioned so great a sensation in local dramatic circles.”\n\n“Were you at his performance, Joe?” I inquired.\n\n“I were,” said Joe, with emphasis and solemnity.\n\n“Was there a great sensation?”\n\n“Why,” said Joe, “yes, there certainly were a peck of orange-peel. Partickler when he see the ghost. Though I put it to yourself, sir, whether it were calc’lated to keep a man up to his work with a good hart, to be continiwally cutting in betwixt him and the Ghost with “Amen!” A man may have had a misfortun’ and been in the Church,” said Joe, lowering his voice to an argumentative and feeling tone, “but that is no reason why you should put him out at such a time. Which I meantersay, if the ghost of a man’s own father cannot be allowed to claim his attention, what can, Sir? Still more, when his mourning ’at is unfortunately made so small as that the weight of the black feathers brings it off, try to keep it on how you may.”\n\nA ghost-seeing effect in Joe’s own countenance informed me that Herbert had entered the room. So, I presented Joe to Herbert, who held out his hand; but Joe backed from it, and held on by the bird’s-nest.\n\n“Your servant, Sir,” said Joe, “which I hope as you and Pip”—here his eye fell on the Avenger, who was putting some toast on table, and so plainly denoted an intention to make that young gentleman one of the family, that I frowned it down and confused him more—“I meantersay, you two gentlemen,—which I hope as you get your elths in this close spot? For the present may be a werry good inn, according to London opinions,” said Joe, confidentially, “and I believe its character do stand it; but I wouldn’t keep a pig in it myself,—not in the case that I wished him to fatten wholesome and to eat with a meller flavour on him.”\n\nHaving borne this flattering testimony to the merits of our dwelling-place, and having incidentally shown this tendency to call me “sir,” Joe, being invited to sit down to table, looked all round the room for a suitable spot on which to deposit his hat,—as if it were only on some very few rare substances in nature that it could find a resting place,—and ultimately stood it on an extreme corner of the chimney-piece, from which it ever afterwards fell off at intervals.\n\n“Do you take tea, or coffee, Mr. Gargery?” asked Herbert, who always presided of a morning.\n\n“Thankee, Sir,” said Joe, stiff from head to foot, “I’ll take whichever is most agreeable to yourself.”\n\n“What do you say to coffee?”\n\n“Thankee, Sir,” returned Joe, evidently dispirited by the proposal, “since you are so kind as make chice of coffee, I will not run contrairy to your own opinions. But don’t you never find it a little ’eating?”\n\n“Say tea then,” said Herbert, pouring it out.\n\nHere Joe’s hat tumbled off the mantel-piece, and he started out of his chair and picked it up, and fitted it to the same exact spot. As if it were an absolute point of good breeding that it should tumble off again soon.\n\n“When did you come to town, Mr. Gargery?”\n\n“Were it yesterday afternoon?” said Joe, after coughing behind his hand, as if he had had time to catch the whooping-cough since he came. “No it were not. Yes it were. Yes. It were yesterday afternoon” (with an appearance of mingled wisdom, relief, and strict impartiality).\n\n“Have you seen anything of London yet?”\n\n“Why, yes, Sir,” said Joe, “me and Wopsle went off straight to look at the Blacking Ware’us. But we didn’t find that it come up to its likeness in the red bills at the shop doors; which I meantersay,” added Joe, in an explanatory manner, “as it is there drawd too architectooralooral.”\n\nI really believe Joe would have prolonged this word (mightily expressive to my mind of some architecture that I know) into a perfect Chorus, but for his attention being providentially attracted by his hat, which was toppling. Indeed, it demanded from him a constant attention, and a quickness of eye and hand, very like that exacted by wicket-keeping. He made extraordinary play with it, and showed the greatest skill; now, rushing at it and catching it neatly as it dropped; now, merely stopping it midway, beating it up, and humouring it in various parts of the room and against a good deal of the pattern of the paper on the wall, before he felt it safe to close with it; finally splashing it into the slop-basin, where I took the liberty of laying hands upon it.\n\nAs to his shirt-collar, and his coat-collar, they were perplexing to reflect upon,—insoluble mysteries both. Why should a man scrape himself to that extent, before he could consider himself full dressed? Why should he suppose it necessary to be purified by suffering for his holiday clothes? Then he fell into such unaccountable fits of meditation, with his fork midway between his plate and his mouth; had his eyes attracted in such strange directions; was afflicted with such remarkable coughs; sat so far from the table, and dropped so much more than he ate, and pretended that he hadn’t dropped it; that I was heartily glad when Herbert left us for the City.\n\nI had neither the good sense nor the good feeling to know that this was all my fault, and that if I had been easier with Joe, Joe would have been easier with me. I felt impatient of him and out of temper with him; in which condition he heaped coals of fire on my head.\n\n“Us two being now alone, sir,”—began Joe.\n\n“Joe,” I interrupted, pettishly, “how can you call me, sir?”\n\nJoe looked at me for a single instant with something faintly like reproach. Utterly preposterous as his cravat was, and as his collars were, I was conscious of a sort of dignity in the look.\n\n“Us two being now alone,” resumed Joe, “and me having the intentions and abilities to stay not many minutes more, I will now conclude—leastways begin—to mention what have led to my having had the present honour. For was it not,” said Joe, with his old air of lucid exposition, “that my only wish were to be useful to you, I should not have had the honour of breaking wittles in the company and abode of gentlemen.”\n\nI was so unwilling to see the look again, that I made no remonstrance against this tone.\n\n“Well, sir,” pursued Joe, “this is how it were. I were at the Bargemen t’other night, Pip;”—whenever he subsided into affection, he called me Pip, and whenever he relapsed into politeness he called me sir; “when there come up in his shay-cart, Pumblechook. Which that same identical,” said Joe, going down a new track, “do comb my ’air the wrong way sometimes, awful, by giving out up and down town as it were him which ever had your infant companionation and were looked upon as a playfellow by yourself.”\n\n“Nonsense. It was you, Joe.”\n\n“Which I fully believed it were, Pip,” said Joe, slightly tossing his head, “though it signify little now, sir. Well, Pip; this same identical, which his manners is given to blusterous, come to me at the Bargemen (wot a pipe and a pint of beer do give refreshment to the workingman, sir, and do not over stimilate), and his word were, ‘Joseph, Miss Havisham she wish to speak to you.’”\n\n“Miss Havisham, Joe?”\n\n“‘She wish,’ were Pumblechook’s word, ‘to speak to you.’” Joe sat and rolled his eyes at the ceiling.\n\n“Yes, Joe? Go on, please.”\n\n“Next day, sir,” said Joe, looking at me as if I were a long way off, “having cleaned myself, I go and I see Miss A.”\n\n“Miss A., Joe? Miss Havisham?”\n\n“Which I say, sir,” replied Joe, with an air of legal formality, as if he were making his will, “Miss A., or otherways Havisham. Her expression air then as follering: ‘Mr. Gargery. You air in correspondence with Mr. Pip?’ Having had a letter from you, I were able to say ‘I am.’ (When I married your sister, sir, I said ‘I will;’ and when I answered your friend, Pip, I said ‘I am.’) ‘Would you tell him, then,’ said she, ‘that which Estella has come home and would be glad to see him.’”\n\nI felt my face fire up as I looked at Joe. I hope one remote cause of its firing may have been my consciousness that if I had known his errand, I should have given him more encouragement.\n\n“Biddy,” pursued Joe, “when I got home and asked her fur to write the message to you, a little hung back. Biddy says, ‘I know he will be very glad to have it by word of mouth, it is holiday time, you want to see him, go!’ I have now concluded, sir,” said Joe, rising from his chair, “and, Pip, I wish you ever well and ever prospering to a greater and a greater height.”\n\n“But you are not going now, Joe?”\n\n“Yes I am,” said Joe.\n\n“But you are coming back to dinner, Joe?”\n\n“No I am not,” said Joe.\n\nOur eyes met, and all the “Sir” melted out of that manly heart as he gave me his hand.\n\n“Pip, dear old chap, life is made of ever so many partings welded together, as I may say, and one man’s a blacksmith, and one’s a whitesmith, and one’s a goldsmith, and one’s a coppersmith. Diwisions among such must come, and must be met as they come. If there’s been any fault at all to-day, it’s mine. You and me is not two figures to be together in London; nor yet anywheres else but what is private, and beknown, and understood among friends. It ain’t that I am proud, but that I want to be right, as you shall never see me no more in these clothes. I’m wrong in these clothes. I’m wrong out of the forge, the kitchen, or off th’ meshes. You won’t find half so much fault in me if you think of me in my forge dress, with my hammer in my hand, or even my pipe. You won’t find half so much fault in me if, supposing as you should ever wish to see me, you come and put your head in at the forge window and see Joe the blacksmith, there, at the old anvil, in the old burnt apron, sticking to the old work. I’m awful dull, but I hope I’ve beat out something nigh the rights of this at last. And so GOD bless you, dear old Pip, old chap, GOD bless you!”\n\nI had not been mistaken in my fancy that there was a simple dignity in him. The fashion of his dress could no more come in its way when he spoke these words than it could come in its way in Heaven. He touched me gently on the forehead, and went out. As soon as I could recover myself sufficiently, I hurried out after him and looked for him in the neighbouring streets; but he was gone.\n\n# Chapter XXVIII.\n\nIt was clear that I must repair to our town next day, and in the first flow of my repentance, it was equally clear that I must stay at Joe’s. But, when I had secured my box-place by to-morrow’s coach, and had been down to Mr. Pocket’s and back, I was not by any means convinced on the last point, and began to invent reasons and make excuses for putting up at the Blue Boar. I should be an inconvenience at Joe’s; I was not expected, and my bed would not be ready; I should be too far from Miss Havisham’s, and she was exacting and mightn’t like it. All other swindlers upon earth are nothing to the self-swindlers, and with such pretences did I cheat myself. Surely a curious thing. That I should innocently take a bad half-crown of somebody else’s manufacture is reasonable enough; but that I should knowingly reckon the spurious coin of my own make as good money! An obliging stranger, under pretence of compactly folding up my bank-notes for security’s sake, abstracts the notes and gives me nutshells; but what is his sleight of hand to mine, when I fold up my own nutshells and pass them on myself as notes!\n\nHaving settled that I must go to the Blue Boar, my mind was much disturbed by indecision whether or not to take the Avenger. It was tempting to think of that expensive Mercenary publicly airing his boots in the archway of the Blue Boar’s posting-yard; it was almost solemn to imagine him casually produced in the tailor’s shop, and confounding the disrespectful senses of Trabb’s boy. On the other hand, Trabb’s boy might worm himself into his intimacy and tell him things; or, reckless and desperate wretch as I knew he could be, might hoot him in the High Street. My patroness, too, might hear of him, and not approve. On the whole, I resolved to leave the Avenger behind.\n\nIt was the afternoon coach by which I had taken my place, and, as winter had now come round, I should not arrive at my destination until two or three hours after dark. Our time of starting from the Cross Keys was two o’clock. I arrived on the ground with a quarter of an hour to spare, attended by the Avenger,—if I may connect that expression with one who never attended on me if he could possibly help it.\n\nAt that time it was customary to carry Convicts down to the dock-yards by stage-coach. As I had often heard of them in the capacity of outside passengers, and had more than once seen them on the high road dangling their ironed legs over the coach roof, I had no cause to be surprised when Herbert, meeting me in the yard, came up and told me there were two convicts going down with me. But I had a reason that was an old reason now for constitutionally faltering whenever I heard the word “convict.”\n\n“You don’t mind them, Handel?” said Herbert.\n\n“O no!”\n\n“I thought you seemed as if you didn’t like them?”\n\n“I can’t pretend that I do like them, and I suppose you don’t particularly. But I don’t mind them.”\n\n“See! There they are,” said Herbert, “coming out of the Tap. What a degraded and vile sight it is!”\n\nThey had been treating their guard, I suppose, for they had a gaoler with them, and all three came out wiping their mouths on their hands. The two convicts were handcuffed together, and had irons on their legs,—irons of a pattern that I knew well. They wore the dress that I likewise knew well. Their keeper had a brace of pistols, and carried a thick-knobbed bludgeon under his arm; but he was on terms of good understanding with them, and stood with them beside him, looking on at the putting-to of the horses, rather with an air as if the convicts were an interesting Exhibition not formally open at the moment, and he the Curator. One was a taller and stouter man than the other, and appeared as a matter of course, according to the mysterious ways of the world, both convict and free, to have had allotted to him the smaller suit of clothes. His arms and legs were like great pincushions of those shapes, and his attire disguised him absurdly; but I knew his half-closed eye at one glance. There stood the man whom I had seen on the settle at the Three Jolly Bargemen on a Saturday night, and who had brought me down with his invisible gun!\n\nIt was easy to make sure that as yet he knew me no more than if he had never seen me in his life. He looked across at me, and his eye appraised my watch-chain, and then he incidentally spat and said something to the other convict, and they laughed and slued themselves round with a clink of their coupling manacle, and looked at something else. The great numbers on their backs, as if they were street doors; their coarse mangy ungainly outer surface, as if they were lower animals; their ironed legs, apologetically garlanded with pocket-handkerchiefs; and the way in which all present looked at them and kept from them; made them (as Herbert had said) a most disagreeable and degraded spectacle.\n\nBut this was not the worst of it. It came out that the whole of the back of the coach had been taken by a family removing from London, and that there were no places for the two prisoners but on the seat in front behind the coachman. Hereupon, a choleric gentleman, who had taken the fourth place on that seat, flew into a most violent passion, and said that it was a breach of contract to mix him up with such villainous company, and that it was poisonous, and pernicious, and infamous, and shameful, and I don’t know what else. At this time the coach was ready and the coachman impatient, and we were all preparing to get up, and the prisoners had come over with their keeper,—bringing with them that curious flavour of bread-poultice, baize, rope-yarn, and hearthstone, which attends the convict presence.\n\n“Don’t take it so much amiss, sir,” pleaded the keeper to the angry passenger; “I’ll sit next you myself. I’ll put ’em on the outside of the row. They won’t interfere with you, sir. You needn’t know they’re there.”\n\n“And don’t blame me,” growled the convict I had recognised. “I don’t want to go. I am quite ready to stay behind. As fur as I am concerned any one’s welcome to my place.”\n\n“Or mine,” said the other, gruffly. “I wouldn’t have incommoded none of you, if I’d had my way.” Then they both laughed, and began cracking nuts, and spitting the shells about.—As I really think I should have liked to do myself, if I had been in their place and so despised.\n\nAt length, it was voted that there was no help for the angry gentleman, and that he must either go in his chance company or remain behind. So he got into his place, still making complaints, and the keeper got into the place next him, and the convicts hauled themselves up as well as they could, and the convict I had recognised sat behind me with his breath on the hair of my head.\n\n“Good-bye, Handel!” Herbert called out as we started. I thought what a blessed fortune it was, that he had found another name for me than Pip.\n\nIt is impossible to express with what acuteness I felt the convict’s breathing, not only on the back of my head, but all along my spine. The sensation was like being touched in the marrow with some pungent and searching acid, it set my very teeth on edge. He seemed to have more breathing business to do than another man, and to make more noise in doing it; and I was conscious of growing high-shouldered on one side, in my shrinking endeavours to fend him off.\n\nThe weather was miserably raw, and the two cursed the cold. It made us all lethargic before we had gone far, and when we had left the Half-way House behind, we habitually dozed and shivered and were silent. I dozed off, myself, in considering the question whether I ought to restore a couple of pounds sterling to this creature before losing sight of him, and how it could best be done. In the act of dipping forward as if I were going to bathe among the horses, I woke in a fright and took the question up again.\n\nBut I must have lost it longer than I had thought, since, although I could recognise nothing in the darkness and the fitful lights and shadows of our lamps, I traced marsh country in the cold damp wind that blew at us. Cowering forward for warmth and to make me a screen against the wind, the convicts were closer to me than before. The very first words I heard them interchange as I became conscious, were the words of my own thought, “Two One Pound notes.”\n\n“How did he get ’em?” said the convict I had never seen.\n\n“How should I know?” returned the other. “He had ’em stowed away somehows. Giv him by friends, I expect.”\n\n“I wish,” said the other, with a bitter curse upon the cold, “that I had ’em here.”\n\n“Two one pound notes, or friends?”\n\n“Two one pound notes. I’d sell all the friends I ever had for one, and think it a blessed good bargain. Well? So he says—?”\n\n“So he says,” resumed the convict I had recognised,—“it was all said and done in half a minute, behind a pile of timber in the Dock-yard,—‘You’re a-going to be discharged?’ Yes, I was. Would I find out that boy that had fed him and kep his secret, and give him them two one pound notes? Yes, I would. And I did.”\n\n“More fool you,” growled the other. “I’d have spent ’em on a Man, in wittles and drink. He must have been a green one. Mean to say he knowed nothing of you?”\n\n“Not a ha’porth. Different gangs and different ships. He was tried again for prison breaking, and got made a Lifer.”\n\n“And was that—Honour!—the only time you worked out, in this part of the country?”\n\n“The only time.”\n\n“What might have been your opinion of the place?”\n\n“A most beastly place. Mudbank, mist, swamp, and work; work, swamp, mist, and mudbank.”\n\nThey both execrated the place in very strong language, and gradually growled themselves out, and had nothing left to say.\n\nAfter overhearing this dialogue, I should assuredly have got down and been left in the solitude and darkness of the highway, but for feeling certain that the man had no suspicion of my identity. Indeed, I was not only so changed in the course of nature, but so differently dressed and so differently circumstanced, that it was not at all likely he could have known me without accidental help. Still, the coincidence of our being together on the coach, was sufficiently strange to fill me with a dread that some other coincidence might at any moment connect me, in his hearing, with my name. For this reason, I resolved to alight as soon as we touched the town, and put myself out of his hearing. This device I executed successfully. My little portmanteau was in the boot under my feet; I had but to turn a hinge to get it out; I threw it down before me, got down after it, and was left at the first lamp on the first stones of the town pavement. As to the convicts, they went their way with the coach, and I knew at what point they would be spirited off to the river. In my fancy, I saw the boat with its convict crew waiting for them at the slime-washed stairs,—again heard the gruff “Give way, you!” like an order to dogs,—again saw the wicked Noah’s Ark lying out on the black water.\n\nI could not have said what I was afraid of, for my fear was altogether undefined and vague, but there was great fear upon me. As I walked on to the hotel, I felt that a dread, much exceeding the mere apprehension of a painful or disagreeable recognition, made me tremble. I am confident that it took no distinctness of shape, and that it was the revival for a few minutes of the terror of childhood.\n\nThe coffee-room at the Blue Boar was empty, and I had not only ordered my dinner there, but had sat down to it, before the waiter knew me. As soon as he had apologised for the remissness of his memory, he asked me if he should send Boots for Mr. Pumblechook?\n\n“No,” said I, “certainly not.”\n\nThe waiter (it was he who had brought up the Great Remonstrance from the Commercials, on the day when I was bound) appeared surprised, and took the earliest opportunity of putting a dirty old copy of a local newspaper so directly in my way, that I took it up and read this paragraph:—\n\nOur readers will learn, not altogether without interest, in reference to the recent romantic rise in fortune of a young artificer in iron of this neighbourhood (what a theme, by the way, for the magic pen of our as yet not universally acknowledged townsman TOOBY, the poet of our columns!) that the youth’s earliest patron, companion, and friend, was a highly respected individual not entirely unconnected with the corn and seed trade, and whose eminently convenient and commodious business premises are situate within a hundred miles of the High Street. It is not wholly irrespective of our personal feelings that we record HIM as the Mentor of our young Telemachus, for it is good to know that our town produced the founder of the latter’s fortunes. Does the thought-contracted brow of the local Sage or the lustrous eye of local Beauty inquire whose fortunes? We believe that Quintin Matsys was the BLACKSMITH of Antwerp. VERB. SAP.\n\nI entertain a conviction, based upon large experience, that if in the days of my prosperity I had gone to the North Pole, I should have met somebody there, wandering Esquimaux or civilized man, who would have told me that Pumblechook was my earliest patron and the founder of my fortunes.\n\n# Chapter XXIX.\n\nBetimes in the morning I was up and out. It was too early yet to go to Miss Havisham’s, so I loitered into the country on Miss Havisham’s side of town,—which was not Joe’s side; I could go there to-morrow,—thinking about my patroness, and painting brilliant pictures of her plans for me.\n\nShe had adopted Estella, she had as good as adopted me, and it could not fail to be her intention to bring us together. She reserved it for me to restore the desolate house, admit the sunshine into the dark rooms, set the clocks a-going and the cold hearths a-blazing, tear down the cobwebs, destroy the vermin,—in short, do all the shining deeds of the young Knight of romance, and marry the Princess. I had stopped to look at the house as I passed; and its seared red brick walls, blocked windows, and strong green ivy clasping even the stacks of chimneys with its twigs and tendons, as if with sinewy old arms, had made up a rich attractive mystery, of which I was the hero. Estella was the inspiration of it, and the heart of it, of course. But, though she had taken such strong possession of me, though my fancy and my hope were so set upon her, though her influence on my boyish life and character had been all-powerful, I did not, even that romantic morning, invest her with any attributes save those she possessed. I mention this in this place, of a fixed purpose, because it is the clue by which I am to be followed into my poor labyrinth. According to my experience, the conventional notion of a lover cannot be always true. The unqualified truth is, that when I loved Estella with the love of a man, I loved her simply because I found her irresistible. Once for all; I knew to my sorrow, often and often, if not always, that I loved her against reason, against promise, against peace, against hope, against happiness, against all discouragement that could be. Once for all; I loved her none the less because I knew it, and it had no more influence in restraining me than if I had devoutly believed her to be human perfection.\n\nI so shaped out my walk as to arrive at the gate at my old time. When I had rung at the bell with an unsteady hand, I turned my back upon the gate, while I tried to get my breath and keep the beating of my heart moderately quiet. I heard the side-door open, and steps come across the courtyard; but I pretended not to hear, even when the gate swung on its rusty hinges.\n\nBeing at last touched on the shoulder, I started and turned. I started much more naturally then, to find myself confronted by a man in a sober grey dress. The last man I should have expected to see in that place of porter at Miss Havisham’s door.\n\n“Orlick!”\n\n“Ah, young master, there’s more changes than yours. But come in, come in. It’s opposed to my orders to hold the gate open.”\n\nI entered and he swung it, and locked it, and took the key out. “Yes!” said he, facing round, after doggedly preceding me a few steps towards the house. “Here I am!”\n\n“How did you come here?”\n\n“I come here,” he retorted, “on my legs. I had my box brought alongside me in a barrow.”\n\n“Are you here for good?”\n\n“I ain’t here for harm, young master, I suppose?”\n\nI was not so sure of that. I had leisure to entertain the retort in my mind, while he slowly lifted his heavy glance from the pavement, up my legs and arms, to my face.\n\n“Then you have left the forge?” I said.\n\n“Do this look like a forge?” replied Orlick, sending his glance all round him with an air of injury. “Now, do it look like it?”\n\nI asked him how long he had left Gargery’s forge?\n\n“One day is so like another here,” he replied, “that I don’t know without casting it up. However, I come here some time since you left.”\n\n“I could have told you that, Orlick.”\n\n“Ah!” said he, dryly. “But then you’ve got to be a scholar.”\n\nBy this time we had come to the house, where I found his room to be one just within the side-door, with a little window in it looking on the courtyard. In its small proportions, it was not unlike the kind of place usually assigned to a gate-porter in Paris. Certain keys were hanging on the wall, to which he now added the gate key; and his patchwork-covered bed was in a little inner division or recess. The whole had a slovenly, confined, and sleepy look, like a cage for a human dormouse; while he, looming dark and heavy in the shadow of a corner by the window, looked like the human dormouse for whom it was fitted up,—as indeed he was.\n\n“I never saw this room before,” I remarked; “but there used to be no Porter here.”\n\n“No,” said he; “not till it got about that there was no protection on the premises, and it come to be considered dangerous, with convicts and Tag and Rag and Bobtail going up and down. And then I was recommended to the place as a man who could give another man as good as he brought, and I took it. It’s easier than bellowsing and hammering.—That’s loaded, that is.”\n\nMy eye had been caught by a gun with a brass-bound stock over the chimney-piece, and his eye had followed mine.\n\n“Well,” said I, not desirous of more conversation, “shall I go up to Miss Havisham?”\n\n“Burn me, if I know!” he retorted, first stretching himself and then shaking himself; “my orders ends here, young master. I give this here bell a rap with this here hammer, and you go on along the passage till you meet somebody.”\n\n“I am expected, I believe?”\n\n“Burn me twice over, if I can say!” said he.\n\nUpon that, I turned down the long passage which I had first trodden in my thick boots, and he made his bell sound. At the end of the passage, while the bell was still reverberating, I found Sarah Pocket, who appeared to have now become constitutionally green and yellow by reason of me.\n\n“Oh!” said she. “You, is it, Mr. Pip?”\n\n“It is, Miss Pocket. I am glad to tell you that Mr. Pocket and family are all well.”\n\n“Are they any wiser?” said Sarah, with a dismal shake of the head; “they had better be wiser, than well. Ah, Matthew, Matthew! You know your way, sir?”\n\nTolerably, for I had gone up the staircase in the dark, many a time. I ascended it now, in lighter boots than of yore, and tapped in my old way at the door of Miss Havisham’s room. “Pip’s rap,” I heard her say, immediately; “come in, Pip.”\n\nShe was in her chair near the old table, in the old dress, with her two hands crossed on her stick, her chin resting on them, and her eyes on the fire. Sitting near her, with the white shoe, that had never been worn, in her hand, and her head bent as she looked at it, was an elegant lady whom I had never seen.\n\n“Come in, Pip,” Miss Havisham continued to mutter, without looking round or up; “come in, Pip, how do you do, Pip? so you kiss my hand as if I were a queen, eh?—Well?”\n\nShe looked up at me suddenly, only moving her eyes, and repeated in a grimly playful manner,—\n\n“Well?”\n\n“I heard, Miss Havisham,” said I, rather at a loss, “that you were so kind as to wish me to come and see you, and I came directly.”\n\n“Well?”\n\nThe lady whom I had never seen before, lifted up her eyes and looked archly at me, and then I saw that the eyes were Estella’s eyes. But she was so much changed, was so much more beautiful, so much more womanly, in all things winning admiration, had made such wonderful advance, that I seemed to have made none. I fancied, as I looked at her, that I slipped hopelessly back into the coarse and common boy again. O the sense of distance and disparity that came upon me, and the inaccessibility that came about her!\n\nShe gave me her hand. I stammered something about the pleasure I felt in seeing her again, and about my having looked forward to it, for a long, long time.\n\n“Do you find her much changed, Pip?” asked Miss Havisham, with her greedy look, and striking her stick upon a chair that stood between them, as a sign to me to sit down there.\n\n“When I came in, Miss Havisham, I thought there was nothing of Estella in the face or figure; but now it all settles down so curiously into the old—”\n\n“What? You are not going to say into the old Estella?” Miss Havisham interrupted. “She was proud and insulting, and you wanted to go away from her. Don’t you remember?”\n\nI said confusedly that that was long ago, and that I knew no better then, and the like. Estella smiled with perfect composure, and said she had no doubt of my having been quite right, and of her having been very disagreeable.\n\n“Is he changed?” Miss Havisham asked her.\n\n“Very much,” said Estella, looking at me.\n\n“Less coarse and common?” said Miss Havisham, playing with Estella’s hair.\n\nEstella laughed, and looked at the shoe in her hand, and laughed again, and looked at me, and put the shoe down. She treated me as a boy still, but she lured me on.\n\nWe sat in the dreamy room among the old strange influences which had so wrought upon me, and I learnt that she had but just come home from France, and that she was going to London. Proud and wilful as of old, she had brought those qualities into such subjection to her beauty that it was impossible and out of nature—or I thought so—to separate them from her beauty. Truly it was impossible to dissociate her presence from all those wretched hankerings after money and gentility that had disturbed my boyhood,—from all those ill-regulated aspirations that had first made me ashamed of home and Joe,—from all those visions that had raised her face in the glowing fire, struck it out of the iron on the anvil, extracted it from the darkness of night to look in at the wooden window of the forge, and flit away. In a word, it was impossible for me to separate her, in the past or in the present, from the innermost life of my life.\n\nIt was settled that I should stay there all the rest of the day, and return to the hotel at night, and to London to-morrow. When we had conversed for a while, Miss Havisham sent us two out to walk in the neglected garden: on our coming in by and by, she said, I should wheel her about a little, as in times of yore.\n\nSo, Estella and I went out into the garden by the gate through which I had strayed to my encounter with the pale young gentleman, now Herbert; I, trembling in spirit and worshipping the very hem of her dress; she, quite composed and most decidedly not worshipping the hem of mine. As we drew near to the place of encounter, she stopped and said,—\n\n“I must have been a singular little creature to hide and see that fight that day; but I did, and I enjoyed it very much.”\n\n“You rewarded me very much.”\n\n“Did I?” she replied, in an incidental and forgetful way. “I remember I entertained a great objection to your adversary, because I took it ill that he should be brought here to pester me with his company.”\n\n“He and I are great friends now.”\n\n“Are you? I think I recollect though, that you read with his father?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\nI made the admission with reluctance, for it seemed to have a boyish look, and she already treated me more than enough like a boy.\n\n“Since your change of fortune and prospects, you have changed your companions,” said Estella.\n\n“Naturally,” said I.\n\n“And necessarily,” she added, in a haughty tone; “what was fit company for you once, would be quite unfit company for you now.”\n\nIn my conscience, I doubt very much whether I had any lingering intention left of going to see Joe; but if I had, this observation put it to flight.\n\n“You had no idea of your impending good fortune, in those times?” said Estella, with a slight wave of her hand, signifying in the fighting times.\n\n“Not the least.”\n\nThe air of completeness and superiority with which she walked at my side, and the air of youthfulness and submission with which I walked at hers, made a contrast that I strongly felt. It would have rankled in me more than it did, if I had not regarded myself as eliciting it by being so set apart for her and assigned to her.\n\nThe garden was too overgrown and rank for walking in with ease, and after we had made the round of it twice or thrice, we came out again into the brewery yard. I showed her to a nicety where I had seen her walking on the casks, that first old day, and she said, with a cold and careless look in that direction, “Did I?” I reminded her where she had come out of the house and given me my meat and drink, and she said, “I don’t remember.” “Not remember that you made me cry?” said I. “No,” said she, and shook her head and looked about her. I verily believe that her not remembering and not minding in the least, made me cry again, inwardly,—and that is the sharpest crying of all.\n\n“You must know,” said Estella, condescending to me as a brilliant and beautiful woman might, “that I have no heart,—if that has anything to do with my memory.”\n\nI got through some jargon to the effect that I took the liberty of doubting that. That I knew better. That there could be no such beauty without it.\n\n“Oh! I have a heart to be stabbed in or shot in, I have no doubt,” said Estella, “and of course if it ceased to beat I should cease to be. But you know what I mean. I have no softness there, no—sympathy—sentiment—nonsense.”\n\nWhat was it that was borne in upon my mind when she stood still and looked attentively at me? Anything that I had seen in Miss Havisham? No. In some of her looks and gestures there was that tinge of resemblance to Miss Havisham which may often be noticed to have been acquired by children, from grown person with whom they have been much associated and secluded, and which, when childhood is passed, will produce a remarkable occasional likeness of expression between faces that are otherwise quite different. And yet I could not trace this to Miss Havisham. I looked again, and though she was still looking at me, the suggestion was gone.\n\nWhat was it?\n“I am serious,” said Estella, not so much with a frown (for her brow was smooth) as with a darkening of her face; “if we are to be thrown much together, you had better believe it at once. No!” imperiously stopping me as I opened my lips. “I have not bestowed my tenderness anywhere. I have never had any such thing.”\n\nIn another moment we were in the brewery, so long disused, and she pointed to the high gallery where I had seen her going out on that same first day, and told me she remembered to have been up there, and to have seen me standing scared below. As my eyes followed her white hand, again the same dim suggestion that I could not possibly grasp crossed me. My involuntary start occasioned her to lay her hand upon my arm. Instantly the ghost passed once more and was gone.\nWhat was it?\n\n“What is the matter?” asked Estella. “Are you scared again?”\n\n“I should be, if I believed what you said just now,” I replied, to turn it off.\n\n“Then you don’t? Very well. It is said, at any rate. Miss Havisham will soon be expecting you at your old post, though I think that might be laid aside now, with other old belongings. Let us make one more round of the garden, and then go in. Come! You shall not shed tears for my cruelty to-day; you shall be my Page, and give me your shoulder.”\n\nHer handsome dress had trailed upon the ground. She held it in one hand now, and with the other lightly touched my shoulder as we walked. We walked round the ruined garden twice or thrice more, and it was all in bloom for me. If the green and yellow growth of weed in the chinks of the old wall had been the most precious flowers that ever blew, it could not have been more cherished in my remembrance.\n\nThere was no discrepancy of years between us to remove her far from me; we were of nearly the same age, though of course the age told for more in her case than in mine; but the air of inaccessibility which her beauty and her manner gave her, tormented me in the midst of my delight, and at the height of the assurance I felt that our patroness had chosen us for one another. Wretched boy!\n\nAt last we went back into the house, and there I heard, with surprise, that my guardian had come down to see Miss Havisham on business, and would come back to dinner. The old wintry branches of chandeliers in the room where the mouldering table was spread had been lighted while we were out, and Miss Havisham was in her chair and waiting for me.\n\nIt was like pushing the chair itself back into the past, when we began the old slow circuit round about the ashes of the bridal feast. But, in the funereal room, with that figure of the grave fallen back in the chair fixing its eyes upon her, Estella looked more bright and beautiful than before, and I was under stronger enchantment.\n\nThe time so melted away, that our early dinner-hour drew close at hand, and Estella left us to prepare herself. We had stopped near the centre of the long table, and Miss Havisham, with one of her withered arms stretched out of the chair, rested that clenched hand upon the yellow cloth. As Estella looked back over her shoulder before going out at the door, Miss Havisham kissed that hand to her, with a ravenous intensity that was of its kind quite dreadful.\n\nThen, Estella being gone and we two left alone, she turned to me, and said in a whisper,—\n\n“Is she beautiful, graceful, well-grown? Do you admire her?”\n\n“Everybody must who sees her, Miss Havisham.”\n\nShe drew an arm round my neck, and drew my head close down to hers as she sat in the chair. “Love her, love her, love her! How does she use you?”\n\nBefore I could answer (if I could have answered so difficult a question at all) she repeated, “Love her, love her, love her! If she favours you, love her. If she wounds you, love her. If she tears your heart to pieces,—and as it gets older and stronger it will tear deeper,—love her, love her, love her!”\n\nNever had I seen such passionate eagerness as was joined to her utterance of these words. I could feel the muscles of the thin arm round my neck swell with the vehemence that possessed her.\n\n“Hear me, Pip! I adopted her, to be loved. I bred her and educated her, to be loved. I developed her into what she is, that she might be loved. Love her!”\n\nShe said the word often enough, and there could be no doubt that she meant to say it; but if the often repeated word had been hate instead of love—despair—revenge—dire death—it could not have sounded from her lips more like a curse.\n\n“I’ll tell you,” said she, in the same hurried passionate whisper, “what real love is. It is blind devotion, unquestioning self-humiliation, utter submission, trust and belief against yourself and against the whole world, giving up your whole heart and soul to the smiter—as I did!”\n\nWhen she came to that, and to a wild cry that followed that, I caught her round the waist. For she rose up in the chair, in her shroud of a dress, and struck at the air as if she would as soon have struck herself against the wall and fallen dead.\n\nAll this passed in a few seconds. As I drew her down into her chair, I was conscious of a scent that I knew, and turning, saw my guardian in the room.\n\nHe always carried (I have not yet mentioned it, I think) a pocket-handkerchief of rich silk and of imposing proportions, which was of great value to him in his profession. I have seen him so terrify a client or a witness by ceremoniously unfolding this pocket-handkerchief as if he were immediately going to blow his nose, and then pausing, as if he knew he should not have time to do it before such client or witness committed himself, that the self-committal has followed directly, quite as a matter of course. When I saw him in the room he had this expressive pocket-handkerchief in both hands, and was looking at us. On meeting my eye, he said plainly, by a momentary and silent pause in that attitude, “Indeed? Singular!” and then put the handkerchief to its right use with wonderful effect.\n\nMiss Havisham had seen him as soon as I, and was (like everybody else) afraid of him. She made a strong attempt to compose herself, and stammered that he was as punctual as ever.\n\n“As punctual as ever,” he repeated, coming up to us. “(How do you do, Pip? Shall I give you a ride, Miss Havisham? Once round?) And so you are here, Pip?”\n\nI told him when I had arrived, and how Miss Havisham had wished me to come and see Estella. To which he replied, “Ah! Very fine young lady!” Then he pushed Miss Havisham in her chair before him, with one of his large hands, and put the other in his trousers-pocket as if the pocket were full of secrets.\n\n“Well, Pip! How often have you seen Miss Estella before?” said he, when he came to a stop.\n\n“How often?”\n\n“Ah! How many times? Ten thousand times?”\n\n“Oh! Certainly not so many.”\n\n“Twice?”\n\n“Jaggers,” interposed Miss Havisham, much to my relief, “leave my Pip alone, and go with him to your dinner.”\n\nHe complied, and we groped our way down the dark stairs together. While we were still on our way to those detached apartments across the paved yard at the back, he asked me how often I had seen Miss Havisham eat and drink; offering me a breadth of choice, as usual, between a hundred times and once.\n\nI considered, and said, “Never.”\n\n“And never will, Pip,” he retorted, with a frowning smile. “She has never allowed herself to be seen doing either, since she lived this present life of hers. She wanders about in the night, and then lays hands on such food as she takes.”\n\n“Pray, sir,” said I, “may I ask you a question?”\n\n“You may,” said he, “and I may decline to answer it. Put your question.”\n\n“Estella’s name. Is it Havisham or—?” I had nothing to add.\n\n“Or what?” said he.\n\n“Is it Havisham?”\n\n“It is Havisham.”\n\nThis brought us to the dinner-table, where she and Sarah Pocket awaited us. Mr. Jaggers presided, Estella sat opposite to him, I faced my green and yellow friend. We dined very well, and were waited on by a maid-servant whom I had never seen in all my comings and goings, but who, for anything I know, had been in that mysterious house the whole time. After dinner a bottle of choice old port was placed before my guardian (he was evidently well acquainted with the vintage), and the two ladies left us.\n\nAnything to equal the determined reticence of Mr. Jaggers under that roof I never saw elsewhere, even in him. He kept his very looks to himself, and scarcely directed his eyes to Estella’s face once during dinner. When she spoke to him, he listened, and in due course answered, but never looked at her, that I could see. On the other hand, she often looked at him, with interest and curiosity, if not distrust, but his face never showed the least consciousness. Throughout dinner he took a dry delight in making Sarah Pocket greener and yellower, by often referring in conversation with me to my expectations; but here, again, he showed no consciousness, and even made it appear that he extorted—and even did extort, though I don’t know how—those references out of my innocent self.\n\nAnd when he and I were left alone together, he sat with an air upon him of general lying by in consequence of information he possessed, that really was too much for me. He cross-examined his very wine when he had nothing else in hand. He held it between himself and the candle, tasted the port, rolled it in his mouth, swallowed it, looked at his glass again, smelt the port, tried it, drank it, filled again, and cross-examined the glass again, until I was as nervous as if I had known the wine to be telling him something to my disadvantage. Three or four times I feebly thought I would start conversation; but whenever he saw me going to ask him anything, he looked at me with his glass in his hand, and rolling his wine about in his mouth, as if requesting me to take notice that it was of no use, for he couldn’t answer.\n\nI think Miss Pocket was conscious that the sight of me involved her in the danger of being goaded to madness, and perhaps tearing off her cap,—which was a very hideous one, in the nature of a muslin mop,—and strewing the ground with her hair,—which assuredly had never grown on her head. She did not appear when we afterwards went up to Miss Havisham’s room, and we four played at whist. In the interval, Miss Havisham, in a fantastic way, had put some of the most beautiful jewels from her dressing-table into Estella’s hair, and about her bosom and arms; and I saw even my guardian look at her from under his thick eyebrows, and raise them a little, when her loveliness was before him, with those rich flushes of glitter and colour in it.\n\nOf the manner and extent to which he took our trumps into custody, and came out with mean little cards at the ends of hands, before which the glory of our Kings and Queens was utterly abased, I say nothing; nor, of the feeling that I had, respecting his looking upon us personally in the light of three very obvious and poor riddles that he had found out long ago. What I suffered from, was the incompatibility between his cold presence and my feelings towards Estella. It was not that I knew I could never bear to speak to him about her, that I knew I could never bear to hear him creak his boots at her, that I knew I could never bear to see him wash his hands of her; it was, that my admiration should be within a foot or two of him,—it was, that my feelings should be in the same place with him,—that, was the agonizing circumstance.\n\nWe played until nine o’clock, and then it was arranged that when Estella came to London I should be forewarned of her coming and should meet her at the coach; and then I took leave of her, and touched her and left her.\n\nMy guardian lay at the Boar in the next room to mine. Far into the night, Miss Havisham’s words, “Love her, love her, love her!” sounded in my ears. I adapted them for my own repetition, and said to my pillow, “I love her, I love her, I love her!” hundreds of times. Then, a burst of gratitude came upon me, that she should be destined for me, once the blacksmith’s boy. Then I thought if she were, as I feared, by no means rapturously grateful for that destiny yet, when would she begin to be interested in me? When should I awaken the heart within her that was mute and sleeping now?\n\nAh me! I thought those were high and great emotions. But I never thought there was anything low and small in my keeping away from Joe, because I knew she would be contemptuous of him. It was but a day gone, and Joe had brought the tears into my eyes; they had soon dried, God forgive me! soon dried.\n\n# Chapter XXX.\n\nAfter well considering the matter while I was dressing at the Blue Boar in the morning, I resolved to tell my guardian that I doubted Orlick’s being the right sort of man to fill a post of trust at Miss Havisham’s. “Why of course he is not the right sort of man, Pip,” said my guardian, comfortably satisfied beforehand on the general head, “because the man who fills the post of trust never is the right sort of man.” It seemed quite to put him into spirits to find that this particular post was not exceptionally held by the right sort of man, and he listened in a satisfied manner while I told him what knowledge I had of Orlick. “Very good, Pip,” he observed, when I had concluded, “I’ll go round presently, and pay our friend off.” Rather alarmed by this summary action, I was for a little delay, and even hinted that our friend himself might be difficult to deal with. “Oh no he won’t,” said my guardian, making his pocket-handkerchief-point, with perfect confidence; “I should like to see him argue the question with me.”\n\nAs we were going back together to London by the midday coach, and as I breakfasted under such terrors of Pumblechook that I could scarcely hold my cup, this gave me an opportunity of saying that I wanted a walk, and that I would go on along the London road while Mr. Jaggers was occupied, if he would let the coachman know that I would get into my place when overtaken. I was thus enabled to fly from the Blue Boar immediately after breakfast. By then making a loop of about a couple of miles into the open country at the back of Pumblechook’s premises, I got round into the High Street again, a little beyond that pitfall, and felt myself in comparative security.\n\nIt was interesting to be in the quiet old town once more, and it was not disagreeable to be here and there suddenly recognised and stared after. One or two of the tradespeople even darted out of their shops and went a little way down the street before me, that they might turn, as if they had forgotten something, and pass me face to face,—on which occasions I don’t know whether they or I made the worse pretence; they of not doing it, or I of not seeing it. Still my position was a distinguished one, and I was not at all dissatisfied with it, until Fate threw me in the way of that unlimited miscreant, Trabb’s boy.\n\nCasting my eyes along the street at a certain point of my progress, I beheld Trabb’s boy approaching, lashing himself with an empty blue bag. Deeming that a serene and unconscious contemplation of him would best beseem me, and would be most likely to quell his evil mind, I advanced with that expression of countenance, and was rather congratulating myself on my success, when suddenly the knees of Trabb’s boy smote together, his hair uprose, his cap fell off, he trembled violently in every limb, staggered out into the road, and crying to the populace, “Hold me! I’m so frightened!” feigned to be in a paroxysm of terror and contrition, occasioned by the dignity of my appearance. As I passed him, his teeth loudly chattered in his head, and with every mark of extreme humiliation, he prostrated himself in the dust.\n\nThis was a hard thing to bear, but this was nothing. I had not advanced another two hundred yards when, to my inexpressible terror, amazement, and indignation, I again beheld Trabb’s boy approaching. He was coming round a narrow corner. His blue bag was slung over his shoulder, honest industry beamed in his eyes, a determination to proceed to Trabb’s with cheerful briskness was indicated in his gait. With a shock he became aware of me, and was severely visited as before; but this time his motion was rotatory, and he staggered round and round me with knees more afflicted, and with uplifted hands as if beseeching for mercy. His sufferings were hailed with the greatest joy by a knot of spectators, and I felt utterly confounded.\n\nI had not got as much further down the street as the post-office, when I again beheld Trabb’s boy shooting round by a back way. This time, he was entirely changed. He wore the blue bag in the manner of my great-coat, and was strutting along the pavement towards me on the opposite side of the street, attended by a company of delighted young friends to whom he from time to time exclaimed, with a wave of his hand, “Don’t know yah!” Words cannot state the amount of aggravation and injury wreaked upon me by Trabb’s boy, when passing abreast of me, he pulled up his shirt-collar, twined his side-hair, stuck an arm akimbo, and smirked extravagantly by, wriggling his elbows and body, and drawling to his attendants, “Don’t know yah, don’t know yah, ’pon my soul don’t know yah!” The disgrace attendant on his immediately afterwards taking to crowing and pursuing me across the bridge with crows, as from an exceedingly dejected fowl who had known me when I was a blacksmith, culminated the disgrace with which I left the town, and was, so to speak, ejected by it into the open country.\n\nBut unless I had taken the life of Trabb’s boy on that occasion, I really do not even now see what I could have done save endure. To have struggled with him in the street, or to have exacted any lower recompense from him than his heart’s best blood, would have been futile and degrading. Moreover, he was a boy whom no man could hurt; an invulnerable and dodging serpent who, when chased into a corner, flew out again between his captor’s legs, scornfully yelping. I wrote, however, to Mr. Trabb by next day’s post, to say that Mr. Pip must decline to deal further with one who could so far forget what he owed to the best interests of society, as to employ a boy who excited Loathing in every respectable mind.\n\nThe coach, with Mr. Jaggers inside, came up in due time, and I took my box-seat again, and arrived in London safe,—but not sound, for my heart was gone. As soon as I arrived, I sent a penitential codfish and barrel of oysters to Joe (as reparation for not having gone myself), and then went on to Barnard’s Inn.\n\nI found Herbert dining on cold meat, and delighted to welcome me back. Having despatched The Avenger to the coffee-house for an addition to the dinner, I felt that I must open my breast that very evening to my friend and chum. As confidence was out of the question with The Avenger in the hall, which could merely be regarded in the light of an antechamber to the keyhole, I sent him to the Play. A better proof of the severity of my bondage to that taskmaster could scarcely be afforded, than the degrading shifts to which I was constantly driven to find him employment. So mean is extremity, that I sometimes sent him to Hyde Park corner to see what o’clock it was.\n\nDinner done and we sitting with our feet upon the fender, I said to Herbert, “My dear Herbert, I have something very particular to tell you.”\n\n“My dear Handel,” he returned, “I shall esteem and respect your confidence.”\n\n“It concerns myself, Herbert,” said I, “and one other person.”\n\nHerbert crossed his feet, looked at the fire with his head on one side, and having looked at it in vain for some time, looked at me because I didn’t go on.\n\n“Herbert,” said I, laying my hand upon his knee, “I love—I adore—Estella.”\n\nInstead of being transfixed, Herbert replied in an easy matter-of-course way, “Exactly. Well?”\n\n“Well, Herbert? Is that all you say? Well?”\n\n“What next, I mean?” said Herbert. “Of course I know that.”\n\n“How do you know it?” said I.\n\n“How do I know it, Handel? Why, from you.”\n“I never told you.”\n\n“Told me! You have never told me when you have got your hair cut, but I have had senses to perceive it. You have always adored her, ever since I have known you. You brought your adoration and your portmanteau here together. Told me! Why, you have always told me all day long. When you told me your own story, you told me plainly that you began adoring her the first time you saw her, when you were very young indeed.”\n\n“Very well, then,” said I, to whom this was a new and not unwelcome light, “I have never left off adoring her. And she has come back, a most beautiful and most elegant creature. And I saw her yesterday. And if I adored her before, I now doubly adore her.”\n\n“Lucky for you then, Handel,” said Herbert, “that you are picked out for her and allotted to her. Without encroaching on forbidden ground, we may venture to say that there can be no doubt between ourselves of that fact. Have you any idea yet, of Estella’s views on the adoration question?”\n\nI shook my head gloomily. “Oh! She is thousands of miles away, from me,” said I.\n\n“Patience, my dear Handel: time enough, time enough. But you have something more to say?”\n\n“I am ashamed to say it,” I returned, “and yet it’s no worse to say it than to think it. You call me a lucky fellow. Of course, I am. I was a blacksmith’s boy but yesterday; I am—what shall I say I am—to-day?”\n\n“Say a good fellow, if you want a phrase,” returned Herbert, smiling, and clapping his hand on the back of mine—“a good fellow, with impetuosity and hesitation, boldness and diffidence, action and dreaming, curiously mixed in him.”\n\nI stopped for a moment to consider whether there really was this mixture in my character. On the whole, I by no means recognised the analysis, but thought it not worth disputing.\n\n“When I ask what I am to call myself to-day, Herbert,” I went on, “I suggest what I have in my thoughts. You say I am lucky. I know I have done nothing to raise myself in life, and that Fortune alone has raised me; that is being very lucky. And yet, when I think of Estella—”\n\n(“And when don’t you, you know?” Herbert threw in, with his eyes on the fire; which I thought kind and sympathetic of him.)\n\n“—Then, my dear Herbert, I cannot tell you how dependent and uncertain I feel, and how exposed to hundreds of chances. Avoiding forbidden ground, as you did just now, I may still say that on the constancy of one person (naming no person) all my expectations depend. And at the best, how indefinite and unsatisfactory, only to know so vaguely what they are!” In saying this, I relieved my mind of what had always been there, more or less, though no doubt most since yesterday.\n\n“Now, Handel,” Herbert replied, in his gay, hopeful way, “it seems to me that in the despondency of the tender passion, we are looking into our gift-horse’s mouth with a magnifying-glass. Likewise, it seems to me that, concentrating our attention on the examination, we altogether overlook one of the best points of the animal. Didn’t you tell me that your guardian, Mr. Jaggers, told you in the beginning, that you were not endowed with expectations only? And even if he had not told you so,—though that is a very large If, I grant,—could you believe that of all men in London, Mr. Jaggers is the man to hold his present relations towards you unless he were sure of his ground?”\n\nI said I could not deny that this was a strong point. I said it (people often do so, in such cases) like a rather reluctant concession to truth and justice;—as if I wanted to deny it!\n\n“I should think it was a strong point,” said Herbert, “and I should think you would be puzzled to imagine a stronger; as to the rest, you must bide your guardian’s time, and he must bide his client’s time. You’ll be one-and-twenty before you know where you are, and then perhaps you’ll get some further enlightenment. At all events, you’ll be nearer getting it, for it must come at last.”\n\n“What a hopeful disposition you have!” said I, gratefully admiring his cheery ways.\n\n“I ought to have,” said Herbert, “for I have not much else. I must acknowledge, by the by, that the good sense of what I have just said is not my own, but my father’s. The only remark I ever heard him make on your story, was the final one, “The thing is settled and done, or Mr. Jaggers would not be in it.” And now before I say anything more about my father, or my father’s son, and repay confidence with confidence, I want to make myself seriously disagreeable to you for a moment,—positively repulsive.”\n\n“You won’t succeed,” said I.\n\n“O yes I shall!” said he. “One, two, three, and now I am in for it. Handel, my good fellow;”—though he spoke in this light tone, he was very much in earnest,—“I have been thinking since we have been talking with our feet on this fender, that Estella surely cannot be a condition of your inheritance, if she was never referred to by your guardian. Am I right in so understanding what you have told me, as that he never referred to her, directly or indirectly, in any way? Never even hinted, for instance, that your patron might have views as to your marriage ultimately?”\n\n“Never.”\n\n“Now, Handel, I am quite free from the flavour of sour grapes, upon my soul and honour! Not being bound to her, can you not detach yourself from her?—I told you I should be disagreeable.”\n\nI turned my head aside, for, with a rush and a sweep, like the old marsh winds coming up from the sea, a feeling like that which had subdued me on the morning when I left the forge, when the mists were solemnly rising, and when I laid my hand upon the village finger-post, smote upon my heart again. There was silence between us for a little while.\n\n“Yes; but my dear Handel,” Herbert went on, as if we had been talking, instead of silent, “its having been so strongly rooted in the breast of a boy whom nature and circumstances made so romantic, renders it very serious. Think of her bringing-up, and think of Miss Havisham. Think of what she is herself (now I am repulsive and you abominate me). This may lead to miserable things.”\n\n“I know it, Herbert,” said I, with my head still turned away, “but I can’t help it.”\n\n“You can’t detach yourself?”\n\n“No. Impossible!”\n\n“You can’t try, Handel?”\n\n“No. Impossible!”\n\n“Well!” said Herbert, getting up with a lively shake as if he had been asleep, and stirring the fire, “now I’ll endeavour to make myself agreeable again!”\n\nSo he went round the room and shook the curtains out, put the chairs in their places, tidied the books and so forth that were lying about, looked into the hall, peeped into the letter-box, shut the door, and came back to his chair by the fire: where he sat down, nursing his left leg in both arms.\n\n“I was going to say a word or two, Handel, concerning my father and my father’s son. I am afraid it is scarcely necessary for my father’s son to remark that my father’s establishment is not particularly brilliant in its housekeeping.”\n\n“There is always plenty, Herbert,” said I, to say something encouraging.\n\n“O yes! and so the dustman says, I believe, with the strongest approval, and so does the marine-store shop in the back street. Gravely, Handel, for the subject is grave enough, you know how it is as well as I do. I suppose there was a time once when my father had not given matters up; but if ever there was, the time is gone. May I ask you if you have ever had an opportunity of remarking, down in your part of the country, that the children of not exactly suitable marriages are always most particularly anxious to be married?”\n\nThis was such a singular question, that I asked him in return, “Is it so?”\n\n“I don’t know,” said Herbert, “that’s what I want to know. Because it is decidedly the case with us. My poor sister Charlotte, who was next me and died before she was fourteen, was a striking example. Little Jane is the same. In her desire to be matrimonially established, you might suppose her to have passed her short existence in the perpetual contemplation of domestic bliss. Little Alick in a frock has already made arrangements for his union with a suitable young person at Kew. And indeed, I think we are all engaged, except the baby.”\n“Then you are?” said I.\n\n“I am,” said Herbert; “but it’s a secret.”\n\nI assured him of my keeping the secret, and begged to be favoured with further particulars. He had spoken so sensibly and feelingly of my weakness that I wanted to know something about his strength.\n\n“May I ask the name?” I said.\n\n“Name of Clara,” said Herbert.\n\n“Live in London?”\n\n“Yes, perhaps I ought to mention,” said Herbert, who had become curiously crestfallen and meek, since we entered on the interesting theme, “that she is rather below my mother’s nonsensical family notions. Her father had to do with the victualling of passenger-ships. I think he was a species of purser.”\n\n“What is he now?” said I.\n\n“He’s an invalid now,” replied Herbert.\n\n“Living on—?”\n\n“On the first floor,” said Herbert. Which was not at all what I meant, for I had intended my question to apply to his means. “I have never seen him, for he has always kept his room overhead, since I have known Clara. But I have heard him constantly. He makes tremendous rows,—roars, and pegs at the floor with some frightful instrument.” In looking at me and then laughing heartily, Herbert for the time recovered his usual lively manner.\n\n“Don’t you expect to see him?” said I.\n\n“O yes, I constantly expect to see him,” returned Herbert, “because I never hear him, without expecting him to come tumbling through the ceiling. But I don’t know how long the rafters may hold.”\n\nWhen he had once more laughed heartily, he became meek again, and told me that the moment he began to realise Capital, it was his intention to marry this young lady. He added as a self-evident proposition, engendering low spirits, “But you can’t marry, you know, while you’re looking about you.”\n\nAs we contemplated the fire, and as I thought what a difficult vision to realise this same Capital sometimes was, I put my hands in my pockets. A folded piece of paper in one of them attracting my attention, I opened it and found it to be the play-bill I had received from Joe, relative to the celebrated provincial amateur of Roscian renown. “And bless my heart,” I involuntarily added aloud, “it’s to-night!”\n\nThis changed the subject in an instant, and made us hurriedly resolve to go to the play. So, when I had pledged myself to comfort and abet Herbert in the affair of his heart by all practicable and impracticable means, and when Herbert had told me that his affianced already knew me by reputation and that I should be presented to her, and when we had warmly shaken hands upon our mutual confidence, we blew out our candles, made up our fire, locked our door, and issued forth in quest of Mr. Wopsle and Denmark.\n\n# Chapter XXXI.\n\nOn our arrival in Denmark, we found the king and queen of that country elevated in two arm-chairs on a kitchen-table, holding a Court. The whole of the Danish nobility were in attendance; consisting of a noble boy in the wash-leather boots of a gigantic ancestor, a venerable Peer with a dirty face who seemed to have risen from the people late in life, and the Danish chivalry with a comb in its hair and a pair of white silk legs, and presenting on the whole a feminine appearance. My gifted townsman stood gloomily apart, with folded arms, and I could have wished that his curls and forehead had been more probable.\n\nSeveral curious little circumstances transpired as the action proceeded. The late king of the country not only appeared to have been troubled with a cough at the time of his decease, but to have taken it with him to the tomb, and to have brought it back. The royal phantom also carried a ghostly manuscript round its truncheon, to which it had the appearance of occasionally referring, and that too, with an air of anxiety and a tendency to lose the place of reference which were suggestive of a state of mortality. It was this, I conceive, which led to the Shade’s being advised by the gallery to “turn over!”—a recommendation which it took extremely ill. It was likewise to be noted of this majestic spirit, that whereas it always appeared with an air of having been out a long time and walked an immense distance, it perceptibly came from a closely contiguous wall. This occasioned its terrors to be received derisively. The Queen of Denmark, a very buxom lady, though no doubt historically brazen, was considered by the public to have too much brass about her; her chin being attached to her diadem by a broad band of that metal (as if she had a gorgeous toothache), her waist being encircled by another, and each of her arms by another, so that she was openly mentioned as “the kettle-drum.” The noble boy in the ancestral boots was inconsistent, representing himself, as it were in one breath, as an able seaman, a strolling actor, a grave-digger, a clergyman, and a person of the utmost importance at a Court fencing-match, on the authority of whose practised eye and nice discrimination the finest strokes were judged. This gradually led to a want of toleration for him, and even—on his being detected in holy orders, and declining to perform the funeral service—to the general indignation taking the form of nuts. Lastly, Ophelia was a prey to such slow musical madness, that when, in course of time, she had taken off her white muslin scarf, folded it up, and buried it, a sulky man who had been long cooling his impatient nose against an iron bar in the front row of the gallery, growled, “Now the baby’s put to bed let’s have supper!” Which, to say the least of it, was out of keeping.\n\nUpon my unfortunate townsman all these incidents accumulated with playful effect. Whenever that undecided Prince had to ask a question or state a doubt, the public helped him out with it. As for example; on the question whether ’twas nobler in the mind to suffer, some roared yes, and some no, and some inclining to both opinions said “Toss up for it;” and quite a Debating Society arose. When he asked what should such fellows as he do crawling between earth and heaven, he was encouraged with loud cries of “Hear, hear!” When he appeared with his stocking disordered (its disorder expressed, according to usage, by one very neat fold in the top, which I suppose to be always got up with a flat iron), a conversation took place in the gallery respecting the paleness of his leg, and whether it was occasioned by the turn the ghost had given him. On his taking the recorders,—very like a little black flute that had just been played in the orchestra and handed out at the door,—he was called upon unanimously for Rule Britannia. When he recommended the player not to saw the air thus, the sulky man said, “And don’t you do it, neither; you’re a deal worse than him!” And I grieve to add that peals of laughter greeted Mr. Wopsle on every one of these occasions.\n\nBut his greatest trials were in the churchyard, which had the appearance of a primeval forest, with a kind of small ecclesiastical wash-house on one side, and a turnpike gate on the other. Mr. Wopsle in a comprehensive black cloak, being descried entering at the turnpike, the gravedigger was admonished in a friendly way, “Look out! Here’s the undertaker a coming, to see how you’re a getting on with your work!” I believe it is well known in a constitutional country that Mr. Wopsle could not possibly have returned the skull, after moralizing over it, without dusting his fingers on a white napkin taken from his breast; but even that innocent and indispensable action did not pass without the comment, “Wai-ter!” The arrival of the body for interment (in an empty black box with the lid tumbling open), was the signal for a general joy, which was much enhanced by the discovery, among the bearers, of an individual obnoxious to identification. The joy attended Mr. Wopsle through his struggle with Laertes on the brink of the orchestra and the grave, and slackened no more until he had tumbled the king off the kitchen-table, and had died by inches from the ankles upward.\n\nWe had made some pale efforts in the beginning to applaud Mr. Wopsle; but they were too hopeless to be persisted in. Therefore we had sat, feeling keenly for him, but laughing, nevertheless, from ear to ear. I laughed in spite of myself all the time, the whole thing was so droll; and yet I had a latent impression that there was something decidedly fine in Mr. Wopsle’s elocution,—not for old associations’ sake, I am afraid, but because it was very slow, very dreary, very uphill and downhill, and very unlike any way in which any man in any natural circumstances of life or death ever expressed himself about anything. When the tragedy was over, and he had been called for and hooted, I said to Herbert, “Let us go at once, or perhaps we shall meet him.”\n\nWe made all the haste we could downstairs, but we were not quick enough either. Standing at the door was a Jewish man with an unnatural heavy smear of eyebrow, who caught my eyes as we advanced, and said, when we came up with him,—\n\n“Mr. Pip and friend?”\n\nIdentity of Mr. Pip and friend confessed.\n\n“Mr. Waldengarver,” said the man, “would be glad to have the honour.”\n\n“Waldengarver?” I repeated—when Herbert murmured in my ear, “Probably Wopsle.”\n\n“Oh!” said I. “Yes. Shall we follow you?”\n\n“A few steps, please.” When we were in a side alley, he turned and asked, “How did you think he looked?—I dressed him.”\n\nI don’t know what he had looked like, except a funeral; with the addition of a large Danish sun or star hanging round his neck by a blue ribbon, that had given him the appearance of being insured in some extraordinary Fire Office. But I said he had looked very nice.\n\n“When he come to the grave,” said our conductor, “he showed his cloak beautiful. But, judging from the wing, it looked to me that when he see the ghost in the queen’s apartment, he might have made more of his stockings.”\n\nI modestly assented, and we all fell through a little dirty swing door, into a sort of hot packing-case immediately behind it. Here Mr. Wopsle was divesting himself of his Danish garments, and here there was just room for us to look at him over one another’s shoulders, by keeping the packing-case door, or lid, wide open.\n\n“Gentlemen,” said Mr. Wopsle, “I am proud to see you. I hope, Mr. Pip, you will excuse my sending round. I had the happiness to know you in former times, and the Drama has ever had a claim which has ever been acknowledged, on the noble and the affluent.”\n\nMeanwhile, Mr. Waldengarver, in a frightful perspiration, was trying to get himself out of his princely sables.\n\n“Skin the stockings off Mr. Waldengarver,” said the owner of that property, “or you’ll bust ’em. Bust ’em, and you’ll bust five-and-thirty shillings. Shakspeare never was complimented with a finer pair. Keep quiet in your chair now, and leave ’em to me.”\n\nWith that, he went upon his knees, and began to flay his victim; who, on the first stocking coming off, would certainly have fallen over backward with his chair, but for there being no room to fall anyhow.\n\nI had been afraid until then to say a word about the play. But then, Mr. Waldengarver looked up at us complacently, and said,—\n\n“Gentlemen, how did it seem to you, to go, in front?”\n\nHerbert said from behind (at the same time poking me), “Capitally.” So I said “Capitally.”\n\n“How did you like my reading of the character, gentlemen?” said Mr. Waldengarver, almost, if not quite, with patronage.\n\nHerbert said from behind (again poking me), “Massive and concrete.” So I said boldly, as if I had originated it, and must beg to insist upon it, “Massive and concrete.”\n\n“I am glad to have your approbation, gentlemen,” said Mr. Waldengarver, with an air of dignity, in spite of his being ground against the wall at the time, and holding on by the seat of the chair.\n\n“But I’ll tell you one thing, Mr. Waldengarver,” said the man who was on his knees, “in which you’re out in your reading. Now mind! I don’t care who says contrairy; I tell you so. You’re out in your reading of Hamlet when you get your legs in profile. The last Hamlet as I dressed, made the same mistakes in his reading at rehearsal, till I got him to put a large red wafer on each of his shins, and then at that rehearsal (which was the last) I went in front, sir, to the back of the pit, and whenever his reading brought him into profile, I called out “I don’t see no wafers!” And at night his reading was lovely.”\n\nMr. Waldengarver smiled at me, as much as to say “a faithful Dependent—I overlook his folly;” and then said aloud, “My view is a little classic and thoughtful for them here; but they will improve, they will improve.”\n\nHerbert and I said together, O, no doubt they would improve.\n\n“Did you observe, gentlemen,” said Mr. Waldengarver, “that there was a man in the gallery who endeavoured to cast derision on the service,—I mean, the representation?”\n\nWe basely replied that we rather thought we had noticed such a man. I added, “He was drunk, no doubt.”\n\n“O dear no, sir,” said Mr. Wopsle, “not drunk. His employer would see to that, sir. His employer would not allow him to be drunk.”\n\n“You know his employer?” said I.\n\nMr. Wopsle shut his eyes, and opened them again; performing both ceremonies very slowly. “You must have observed, gentlemen,” said he, “an ignorant and a blatant ass, with a rasping throat and a countenance expressive of low malignity, who went through—I will not say sustained—the rôle (if I may use a French expression) of Claudius, King of Denmark. That is his employer, gentlemen. Such is the profession!”\n\nWithout distinctly knowing whether I should have been more sorry for Mr. Wopsle if he had been in despair, I was so sorry for him as it was, that I took the opportunity of his turning round to have his braces put on,—which jostled us out at the doorway,—to ask Herbert what he thought of having him home to supper? Herbert said he thought it would be kind to do so; therefore I invited him, and he went to Barnard’s with us, wrapped up to the eyes, and we did our best for him, and he sat until two o’clock in the morning, reviewing his success and developing his plans. I forget in detail what they were, but I have a general recollection that he was to begin with reviving the Drama, and to end with crushing it; inasmuch as his decease would leave it utterly bereft and without a chance or hope.\n\nMiserably I went to bed after all, and miserably thought of Estella, and miserably dreamed that my expectations were all cancelled, and that I had to give my hand in marriage to Herbert’s Clara, or play Hamlet to Miss Havisham’s Ghost, before twenty thousand people, without knowing twenty words of it.\n\n# Chapter XXXII.\n\nOne day when I was busy with my books and Mr. Pocket, I received a note by the post, the mere outside of which threw me into a great flutter; for, though I had never seen the handwriting in which it was addressed, I divined whose hand it was. It had no set beginning, as Dear Mr. Pip, or Dear Pip, or Dear Sir, or Dear Anything, but ran thus:—\n\n“I am to come to London the day after to-morrow by the midday coach. I believe it was settled you should meet me? At all events Miss Havisham has that impression, and I write in obedience to it. She sends you her regard.\n\n“Yours, ESTELLA.”\n\nIf there had been time, I should probably have ordered several suits of clothes for this occasion; but as there was not, I was fain to be content with those I had. My appetite vanished instantly, and I knew no peace or rest until the day arrived. Not that its arrival brought me either; for, then I was worse than ever, and began haunting the coach-office in Wood Street, Cheapside, before the coach had left the Blue Boar in our town. For all that I knew this perfectly well, I still felt as if it were not safe to let the coach-office be out of my sight longer than five minutes at a time; and in this condition of unreason I had performed the first half-hour of a watch of four or five hours, when Wemmick ran against me.\n\n“Halloa, Mr. Pip,” said he; “how do you do? I should hardly have thought this was your beat.”\n\nI explained that I was waiting to meet somebody who was coming up by coach, and I inquired after the Castle and the Aged.\n\n“Both flourishing thankye,” said Wemmick, “and particularly the Aged. He’s in wonderful feather. He’ll be eighty-two next birthday. I have a notion of firing eighty-two times, if the neighbourhood shouldn’t complain, and that cannon of mine should prove equal to the pressure. However, this is not London talk. Where do you think I am going to?”\n\n“To the office?” said I, for he was tending in that direction.\n\n“Next thing to it,” returned Wemmick, “I am going to Newgate. We are in a banker’s-parcel case just at present, and I have been down the road taking a squint at the scene of action, and thereupon must have a word or two with our client.”\n\n“Did your client commit the robbery?” I asked.\n\n“Bless your soul and body, no,” answered Wemmick, very drily. “But he is accused of it. So might you or I be. Either of us might be accused of it, you know.”\n\n“Only neither of us is,” I remarked.\n\n“Yah!” said Wemmick, touching me on the breast with his forefinger; “you’re a deep one, Mr. Pip! Would you like to have a look at Newgate? Have you time to spare?”\n\nI had so much time to spare, that the proposal came as a relief, notwithstanding its irreconcilability with my latent desire to keep my eye on the coach-office. Muttering that I would make the inquiry whether I had time to walk with him, I went into the office, and ascertained from the clerk with the nicest precision and much to the trying of his temper, the earliest moment at which the coach could be expected,—which I knew beforehand, quite as well as he. I then rejoined Mr. Wemmick, and affecting to consult my watch, and to be surprised by the information I had received, accepted his offer.\n\nWe were at Newgate in a few minutes, and we passed through the lodge where some fetters were hanging up on the bare walls among the prison rules, into the interior of the jail. At that time jails were much neglected, and the period of exaggerated reaction consequent on all public wrongdoing—and which is always its heaviest and longest punishment—was still far off. So, felons were not lodged and fed better than soldiers (to say nothing of paupers), and seldom set fire to their prisons with the excusable object of improving the flavour of their soup. It was visiting time when Wemmick took me in, and a potman was going his rounds with beer; and the prisoners, behind bars in yards, were buying beer, and talking to friends; and a frowzy, ugly, disorderly, depressing scene it was.\n\nIt struck me that Wemmick walked among the prisoners much as a gardener might walk among his plants. This was first put into my head by his seeing a shoot that had come up in the night, and saying, “What, Captain Tom? Are you there? Ah, indeed!” and also, “Is that Black Bill behind the cistern? Why I didn’t look for you these two months; how do you find yourself?” Equally in his stopping at the bars and attending to anxious whisperers,—always singly,—Wemmick with his post-office in an immovable state, looked at them while in conference, as if he were taking particular notice of the advance they had made, since last observed, towards coming out in full blow at their trial.\n\nHe was highly popular, and I found that he took the familiar department of Mr. Jaggers’s business; though something of the state of Mr. Jaggers hung about him too, forbidding approach beyond certain limits. His personal recognition of each successive client was comprised in a nod, and in his settling his hat a little easier on his head with both hands, and then tightening the post-office, and putting his hands in his pockets. In one or two instances there was a difficulty respecting the raising of fees, and then Mr. Wemmick, backing as far as possible from the insufficient money produced, said, “it’s no use, my boy. I’m only a subordinate. I can’t take it. Don’t go on in that way with a subordinate. If you are unable to make up your quantum, my boy, you had better address yourself to a principal; there are plenty of principals in the profession, you know, and what is not worth the while of one, may be worth the while of another; that’s my recommendation to you, speaking as a subordinate. Don’t try on useless measures. Why should you? Now, who’s next?”\n\nThus, we walked through Wemmick’s greenhouse, until he turned to me and said, “Notice the man I shall shake hands with.” I should have done so, without the preparation, as he had shaken hands with no one yet.\n\nAlmost as soon as he had spoken, a portly upright man (whom I can see now, as I write) in a well-worn olive-coloured frock-coat, with a peculiar pallor overspreading the red in his complexion, and eyes that went wandering about when he tried to fix them, came up to a corner of the bars, and put his hand to his hat—which had a greasy and fatty surface like cold broth—with a half-serious and half-jocose military salute.\n\n“Colonel, to you!” said Wemmick; “how are you, Colonel?”\n\n“All right, Mr. Wemmick.”\n\n“Everything was done that could be done, but the evidence was too strong for us, Colonel.”\n\n“Yes, it was too strong, sir,—but I don’t care.”\n\n“No, no,” said Wemmick, coolly, “you don’t care.” Then, turning to me, “Served His Majesty this man. Was a soldier in the line and bought his discharge.”\n\nI said, “Indeed?” and the man’s eyes looked at me, and then looked over my head, and then looked all round me, and then he drew his hand across his lips and laughed.\n\n“I think I shall be out of this on Monday, sir,” he said to Wemmick.\n\n“Perhaps,” returned my friend, “but there’s no knowing.”\n\n“I am glad to have the chance of bidding you good-bye, Mr. Wemmick,” said the man, stretching out his hand between two bars.\n\n“Thankye,” said Wemmick, shaking hands with him. “Same to you, Colonel.”\n\n“If what I had upon me when taken had been real, Mr. Wemmick,” said the man, unwilling to let his hand go, “I should have asked the favour of your wearing another ring—in acknowledgment of your attentions.”\n\n“I’ll accept the will for the deed,” said Wemmick. “By the by; you were quite a pigeon-fancier.” The man looked up at the sky. “I am told you had a remarkable breed of tumblers. Could you commission any friend of yours to bring me a pair, if you’ve no further use for ’em?”\n\n“It shall be done, sir.”\n\n“All right,” said Wemmick, “they shall be taken care of. Good-afternoon, Colonel. Good-bye!” They shook hands again, and as we walked away Wemmick said to me, “A Coiner, a very good workman. The Recorder’s report is made to-day, and he is sure to be executed on Monday. Still you see, as far as it goes, a pair of pigeons are portable property all the same.” With that, he looked back, and nodded at this dead plant, and then cast his eyes about him in walking out of the yard, as if he were considering what other pot would go best in its place.\n\nAs we came out of the prison through the lodge, I found that the great importance of my guardian was appreciated by the turnkeys, no less than by those whom they held in charge. “Well, Mr. Wemmick,” said the turnkey, who kept us between the two studded and spiked lodge gates, and who carefully locked one before he unlocked the other, “what’s Mr. Jaggers going to do with that water-side murder? Is he going to make it manslaughter, or what’s he going to make of it?”\n\n“Why don’t you ask him?” returned Wemmick.\n\n“O yes, I dare say!” said the turnkey.\n\n“Now, that’s the way with them here, Mr. Pip,” remarked Wemmick, turning to me with his post-office elongated. “They don’t mind what they ask of me, the subordinate; but you’ll never catch ’em asking any questions of my principal.”\n\n“Is this young gentleman one of the ’prentices or articled ones of your office?” asked the turnkey, with a grin at Mr. Wemmick’s humour.\n\n“There he goes again, you see!” cried Wemmick, “I told you so! Asks another question of the subordinate before his first is dry! Well, supposing Mr. Pip is one of them?”\n\n“Why then,” said the turnkey, grinning again, “he knows what Mr. Jaggers is.”\n\n“Yah!” cried Wemmick, suddenly hitting out at the turnkey in a facetious way, “you’re dumb as one of your own keys when you have to do with my principal, you know you are. Let us out, you old fox, or I’ll get him to bring an action against you for false imprisonment.”\n\nThe turnkey laughed, and gave us good day, and stood laughing at us over the spikes of the wicket when we descended the steps into the street.\n\n“Mind you, Mr. Pip,” said Wemmick, gravely in my ear, as he took my arm to be more confidential; “I don’t know that Mr. Jaggers does a better thing than the way in which he keeps himself so high. He’s always so high. His constant height is of a piece with his immense abilities. That Colonel durst no more take leave of him, than that turnkey durst ask him his intentions respecting a case. Then, between his height and them, he slips in his subordinate,—don’t you see?—and so he has ’em, soul and body.”\n\nI was very much impressed, and not for the first time, by my guardian’s subtlety. To confess the truth, I very heartily wished, and not for the first time, that I had had some other guardian of minor abilities.\n\nMr. Wemmick and I parted at the office in Little Britain, where suppliants for Mr. Jaggers’s notice were lingering about as usual, and I returned to my watch in the street of the coach-office, with some three hours on hand. I consumed the whole time in thinking how strange it was that I should be encompassed by all this taint of prison and crime; that, in my childhood out on our lonely marshes on a winter evening, I should have first encountered it; that, it should have reappeared on two occasions, starting out like a stain that was faded but not gone; that, it should in this new way pervade my fortune and advancement. While my mind was thus engaged, I thought of the beautiful young Estella, proud and refined, coming towards me, and I thought with absolute abhorrence of the contrast between the jail and her. I wished that Wemmick had not met me, or that I had not yielded to him and gone with him, so that, of all days in the year on this day, I might not have had Newgate in my breath and on my clothes. I beat the prison dust off my feet as I sauntered to and fro, and I shook it out of my dress, and I exhaled its air from my lungs. So contaminated did I feel, remembering who was coming, that the coach came quickly after all, and I was not yet free from the soiling consciousness of Mr. Wemmick’s conservatory, when I saw her face at the coach window and her hand waving to me.\n\nWhat was the nameless shadow which again in that one instant had passed?\n\n# Chapter XXXIII.\n\nIn her furred travelling-dress, Estella seemed more delicately beautiful than she had ever seemed yet, even in my eyes. Her manner was more winning than she had cared to let it be to me before, and I thought I saw Miss Havisham’s influence in the change.\n\nWe stood in the Inn Yard while she pointed out her luggage to me, and when it was all collected I remembered—having forgotten everything but herself in the meanwhile—that I knew nothing of her destination.\n\n“I am going to Richmond,” she told me. “Our lesson is, that there are two Richmonds, one in Surrey and one in Yorkshire, and that mine is the Surrey Richmond. The distance is ten miles. I am to have a carriage, and you are to take me. This is my purse, and you are to pay my charges out of it. O, you must take the purse! We have no choice, you and I, but to obey our instructions. We are not free to follow our own devices, you and I.”\n\nAs she looked at me in giving me the purse, I hoped there was an inner meaning in her words. She said them slightingly, but not with displeasure.\n\n“A carriage will have to be sent for, Estella. Will you rest here a little?”\n\n“Yes, I am to rest here a little, and I am to drink some tea, and you are to take care of me the while.”\n\nShe drew her arm through mine, as if it must be done, and I requested a waiter who had been staring at the coach like a man who had never seen such a thing in his life, to show us a private sitting-room. Upon that, he pulled out a napkin, as if it were a magic clue without which he couldn’t find the way upstairs, and led us to the black hole of the establishment, fitted up with a diminishing mirror (quite a superfluous article, considering the hole’s proportions), an anchovy sauce-cruet, and somebody’s pattens. On my objecting to this retreat, he took us into another room with a dinner-table for thirty, and in the grate a scorched leaf of a copy-book under a bushel of coal-dust. Having looked at this extinct conflagration and shaken his head, he took my order; which, proving to be merely, “Some tea for the lady,” sent him out of the room in a very low state of mind.\n\nI was, and I am, sensible that the air of this chamber, in its strong combination of stable with soup-stock, might have led one to infer that the coaching department was not doing well, and that the enterprising proprietor was boiling down the horses for the refreshment department. Yet the room was all in all to me, Estella being in it. I thought that with her I could have been happy there for life. (I was not at all happy there at the time, observe, and I knew it well.)\n\n“Where are you going to, at Richmond?” I asked Estella.\n\n“I am going to live,” said she, “at a great expense, with a lady there, who has the power—or says she has—of taking me about, and introducing me, and showing people to me and showing me to people.”\n\n“I suppose you will be glad of variety and admiration?”\n\n“Yes, I suppose so.”\n\nShe answered so carelessly, that I said, “You speak of yourself as if you were some one else.”\n\n“Where did you learn how I speak of others? Come, come,” said Estella, smiling delightfully, “you must not expect me to go to school to you; I must talk in my own way. How do you thrive with Mr. Pocket?”\n\n“I live quite pleasantly there; at least—” It appeared to me that I was losing a chance.\n\n“At least?” repeated Estella.\n\n“As pleasantly as I could anywhere, away from you.”\n\n“You silly boy,” said Estella, quite composedly, “how can you talk such nonsense? Your friend Mr. Matthew, I believe, is superior to the rest of his family?”\n\n“Very superior indeed. He is nobody’s enemy—”\n\n“Don’t add but his own,” interposed Estella, “for I hate that class of man. But he really is disinterested, and above small jealousy and spite, I have heard?”\n\n“I am sure I have every reason to say so.”\n\n“You have not every reason to say so of the rest of his people,” said Estella, nodding at me with an expression of face that was at once grave and rallying, “for they beset Miss Havisham with reports and insinuations to your disadvantage. They watch you, misrepresent you, write letters about you (anonymous sometimes), and you are the torment and the occupation of their lives. You can scarcely realise to yourself the hatred those people feel for you.”\n\n“They do me no harm, I hope?”\n\nInstead of answering, Estella burst out laughing. This was very singular to me, and I looked at her in considerable perplexity. When she left off—and she had not laughed languidly, but with real enjoyment—I said, in my diffident way with her,—\n\n“I hope I may suppose that you would not be amused if they did me any harm.”\n\n“No, no you may be sure of that,” said Estella. “You may be certain that I laugh because they fail. O, those people with Miss Havisham, and the tortures they undergo!” She laughed again, and even now when she had told me why, her laughter was very singular to me, for I could not doubt its being genuine, and yet it seemed too much for the occasion. I thought there must really be something more here than I knew; she saw the thought in my mind, and answered it.\n\n“It is not easy for even you.” said Estella, “to know what satisfaction it gives me to see those people thwarted, or what an enjoyable sense of the ridiculous I have when they are made ridiculous. For you were not brought up in that strange house from a mere baby. I was. You had not your little wits sharpened by their intriguing against you, suppressed and defenceless, under the mask of sympathy and pity and what not that is soft and soothing. I had. You did not gradually open your round childish eyes wider and wider to the discovery of that impostor of a woman who calculates her stores of peace of mind for when she wakes up in the night. I did.”\n\nIt was no laughing matter with Estella now, nor was she summoning these remembrances from any shallow place. I would not have been the cause of that look of hers for all my expectations in a heap.\n\n“Two things I can tell you,” said Estella. “First, notwithstanding the proverb that constant dropping will wear away a stone, you may set your mind at rest that these people never will—never would in a hundred years—impair your ground with Miss Havisham, in any particular, great or small. Second, I am beholden to you as the cause of their being so busy and so mean in vain, and there is my hand upon it.”\n\nAs she gave it to me playfully,—for her darker mood had been but momentary—I held it and put it to my lips. “You ridiculous boy,” said Estella, “will you never take warning? Or do you kiss my hand in the same spirit in which I once let you kiss my cheek?”\n\n“What spirit was that?” said I.\n\n“I must think a moment. A spirit of contempt for the fawners and plotters.”\n\n“If I say yes, may I kiss the cheek again?”\n\n“You should have asked before you touched the hand. But, yes, if you like.”\n\nI leaned down, and her calm face was like a statue’s. “Now,” said Estella, gliding away the instant I touched her cheek, “you are to take care that I have some tea, and you are to take me to Richmond.”\n\nHer reverting to this tone as if our association were forced upon us, and we were mere puppets, gave me pain; but everything in our intercourse did give me pain. Whatever her tone with me happened to be, I could put no trust in it, and build no hope on it; and yet I went on against trust and against hope. Why repeat it a thousand times? So it always was.\n\nI rang for the tea, and the waiter, reappearing with his magic clue, brought in by degrees some fifty adjuncts to that refreshment, but of tea not a glimpse. A teaboard, cups and saucers, plates, knives and forks (including carvers), spoons (various), salt-cellars, a meek little muffin confined with the utmost precaution under a strong iron cover, Moses in the bulrushes typified by a soft bit of butter in a quantity of parsley, a pale loaf with a powdered head, two proof impressions of the bars of the kitchen fireplace on triangular bits of bread, and ultimately a fat family urn; which the waiter staggered in with, expressing in his countenance burden and suffering. After a prolonged absence at this stage of the entertainment, he at length came back with a casket of precious appearance containing twigs. These I steeped in hot water, and so from the whole of these appliances extracted one cup of I don’t know what for Estella.\n\nThe bill paid, and the waiter remembered, and the ostler not forgotten, and the chambermaid taken into consideration,—in a word, the whole house bribed into a state of contempt and animosity, and Estella’s purse much lightened,—we got into our post-coach and drove away. Turning into Cheapside and rattling up Newgate Street, we were soon under the walls of which I was so ashamed.\n\n“What place is that?” Estella asked me.\n\nI made a foolish pretence of not at first recognising it, and then told her. As she looked at it, and drew in her head again, murmuring, “Wretches!” I would not have confessed to my visit for any consideration.\n\n“Mr. Jaggers,” said I, by way of putting it neatly on somebody else, “has the reputation of being more in the secrets of that dismal place than any man in London.”\n\n“He is more in the secrets of every place, I think,” said Estella, in a low voice.\n\n“You have been accustomed to see him often, I suppose?”\n\n“I have been accustomed to see him at uncertain intervals, ever since I can remember. But I know him no better now, than I did before I could speak plainly. What is your own experience of him? Do you advance with him?”\n\n“Once habituated to his distrustful manner,” said I, “I have done very well.”\n\n“Are you intimate?”\n\n“I have dined with him at his private house.”\n\n“I fancy,” said Estella, shrinking “that must be a curious place.”\n\n“It is a curious place.”\n\nI should have been chary of discussing my guardian too freely even with her; but I should have gone on with the subject so far as to describe the dinner in Gerrard Street, if we had not then come into a sudden glare of gas. It seemed, while it lasted, to be all alight and alive with that inexplicable feeling I had had before; and when we were out of it, I was as much dazed for a few moments as if I had been in lightning.\n\nSo we fell into other talk, and it was principally about the way by which we were travelling, and about what parts of London lay on this side of it, and what on that. The great city was almost new to her, she told me, for she had never left Miss Havisham’s neighbourhood until she had gone to France, and she had merely passed through London then in going and returning. I asked her if my guardian had any charge of her while she remained here? To that she emphatically said “God forbid!” and no more.\n\nIt was impossible for me to avoid seeing that she cared to attract me; that she made herself winning, and would have won me even if the task had needed pains. Yet this made me none the happier, for even if she had not taken that tone of our being disposed of by others, I should have felt that she held my heart in her hand because she wilfully chose to do it, and not because it would have wrung any tenderness in her to crush it and throw it away.\n\nWhen we passed through Hammersmith, I showed her where Mr. Matthew Pocket lived, and said it was no great way from Richmond, and that I hoped I should see her sometimes.\n\n“O yes, you are to see me; you are to come when you think proper; you are to be mentioned to the family; indeed you are already mentioned.”\n\nI inquired was it a large household she was going to be a member of?\n\n“No; there are only two; mother and daughter. The mother is a lady of some station, though not averse to increasing her income.”\n\n“I wonder Miss Havisham could part with you again so soon.”\n\n“It is a part of Miss Havisham’s plans for me, Pip,” said Estella, with a sigh, as if she were tired; “I am to write to her constantly and see her regularly and report how I go on,—I and the jewels,—for they are nearly all mine now.”\n\nIt was the first time she had ever called me by my name. Of course she did so purposely, and knew that I should treasure it up.\n\nWe came to Richmond all too soon, and our destination there was a house by the green,—a staid old house, where hoops and powder and patches, embroidered coats, rolled stockings, ruffles and swords, had had their court days many a time. Some ancient trees before the house were still cut into fashions as formal and unnatural as the hoops and wigs and stiff skirts; but their own allotted places in the great procession of the dead were not far off, and they would soon drop into them and go the silent way of the rest.\n\nA bell with an old voice—which I dare say in its time had often said to the house, Here is the green farthingale, Here is the diamond-hilted sword, Here are the shoes with red heels and the blue solitaire—sounded gravely in the moonlight, and two cherry-coloured maids came fluttering out to receive Estella. The doorway soon absorbed her boxes, and she gave me her hand and a smile, and said good-night, and was absorbed likewise. And still I stood looking at the house, thinking how happy I should be if I lived there with her, and knowing that I never was happy with her, but always miserable.\n\nI got into the carriage to be taken back to Hammersmith, and I got in with a bad heart-ache, and I got out with a worse heart-ache. At our own door, I found little Jane Pocket coming home from a little party escorted by her little lover; and I envied her little lover, in spite of his being subject to Flopson.\n\nMr. Pocket was out lecturing; for, he was a most delightful lecturer on domestic economy, and his treatises on the management of children and servants were considered the very best text-books on those themes. But Mrs. Pocket was at home, and was in a little difficulty, on account of the baby’s having been accommodated with a needle-case to keep him quiet during the unaccountable absence (with a relative in the Foot Guards) of Millers. And more needles were missing than it could be regarded as quite wholesome for a patient of such tender years either to apply externally or to take as a tonic.\n\nMr. Pocket being justly celebrated for giving most excellent practical advice, and for having a clear and sound perception of things and a highly judicious mind, I had some notion in my heart-ache of begging him to accept my confidence. But happening to look up at Mrs. Pocket as she sat reading her book of dignities after prescribing Bed as a sovereign remedy for baby, I thought—Well—No, I wouldn’t.\n\n# Chapter XXXIV.\n\nAs I had grown accustomed to my expectations, I had insensibly begun to notice their effect upon myself and those around me. Their influence on my own character I disguised from my recognition as much as possible, but I knew very well that it was not all good. I lived in a state of chronic uneasiness respecting my behaviour to Joe. My conscience was not by any means comfortable about Biddy. When I woke up in the night,—like Camilla,—I used to think, with a weariness on my spirits, that I should have been happier and better if I had never seen Miss Havisham’s face, and had risen to manhood content to be partners with Joe in the honest old forge. Many a time of an evening, when I sat alone looking at the fire, I thought, after all there was no fire like the forge fire and the kitchen fire at home.\n\nYet Estella was so inseparable from all my restlessness and disquiet of mind, that I really fell into confusion as to the limits of my own part in its production. That is to say, supposing I had had no expectations, and yet had had Estella to think of, I could not make out to my satisfaction that I should have done much better. Now, concerning the influence of my position on others, I was in no such difficulty, and so I perceived—though dimly enough perhaps—that it was not beneficial to anybody, and, above all, that it was not beneficial to Herbert. My lavish habits led his easy nature into expenses that he could not afford, corrupted the simplicity of his life, and disturbed his peace with anxieties and regrets. I was not at all remorseful for having unwittingly set those other branches of the Pocket family to the poor arts they practised; because such littlenesses were their natural bent, and would have been evoked by anybody else, if I had left them slumbering. But Herbert’s was a very different case, and it often caused me a twinge to think that I had done him evil service in crowding his sparely furnished chambers with incongruous upholstery work, and placing the Canary-breasted Avenger at his disposal.\n\nSo now, as an infallible way of making little ease great ease, I began to contract a quantity of debt. I could hardly begin but Herbert must begin too, so he soon followed. At Startop’s suggestion, we put ourselves down for election into a club called The Finches of the Grove: the object of which institution I have never divined, if it were not that the members should dine expensively once a fortnight, to quarrel among themselves as much as possible after dinner, and to cause six waiters to get drunk on the stairs. I know that these gratifying social ends were so invariably accomplished, that Herbert and I understood nothing else to be referred to in the first standing toast of the society: which ran “Gentlemen, may the present promotion of good feeling ever reign predominant among the Finches of the Grove.”\n\nThe Finches spent their money foolishly (the Hotel we dined at was in Covent Garden), and the first Finch I saw when I had the honour of joining the Grove was Bentley Drummle, at that time floundering about town in a cab of his own, and doing a great deal of damage to the posts at the street corners. Occasionally, he shot himself out of his equipage headforemost over the apron; and I saw him on one occasion deliver himself at the door of the Grove in this unintentional way—like coals. But here I anticipate a little, for I was not a Finch, and could not be, according to the sacred laws of the society, until I came of age.\n\nIn my confidence in my own resources, I would willingly have taken Herbert’s expenses on myself; but Herbert was proud, and I could make no such proposal to him. So he got into difficulties in every direction, and continued to look about him. When we gradually fell into keeping late hours and late company, I noticed that he looked about him with a desponding eye at breakfast-time; that he began to look about him more hopefully about midday; that he drooped when he came into dinner; that he seemed to descry Capital in the distance, rather clearly, after dinner; that he all but realised Capital towards midnight; and that at about two o’clock in the morning, he became so deeply despondent again as to talk of buying a rifle and going to America, with a general purpose of compelling buffaloes to make his fortune.\n\nI was usually at Hammersmith about half the week, and when I was at Hammersmith I haunted Richmond, whereof separately by and by. Herbert would often come to Hammersmith when I was there, and I think at those seasons his father would occasionally have some passing perception that the opening he was looking for, had not appeared yet. But in the general tumbling up of the family, his tumbling out in life somewhere, was a thing to transact itself somehow. In the meantime Mr. Pocket grew greyer, and tried oftener to lift himself out of his perplexities by the hair. While Mrs. Pocket tripped up the family with her footstool, read her book of dignities, lost her pocket-handkerchief, told us about her grandpapa, and taught the young idea how to shoot, by shooting it into bed whenever it attracted her notice.\n\nAs I am now generalising a period of my life with the object of clearing my way before me, I can scarcely do so better than by at once completing the description of our usual manners and customs at Barnard’s Inn.\n\nWe spent as much money as we could, and got as little for it as people could make up their minds to give us. We were always more or less miserable, and most of our acquaintance were in the same condition. There was a gay fiction among us that we were constantly enjoying ourselves, and a skeleton truth that we never did. To the best of my belief, our case was in the last aspect a rather common one.\n\nEvery morning, with an air ever new, Herbert went into the City to look about him. I often paid him a visit in the dark back-room in which he consorted with an ink-jar, a hat-peg, a coal-box, a string-box, an almanac, a desk and stool, and a ruler; and I do not remember that I ever saw him do anything else but look about him. If we all did what we undertake to do, as faithfully as Herbert did, we might live in a Republic of the Virtues. He had nothing else to do, poor fellow, except at a certain hour of every afternoon to “go to Lloyd’s”—in observance of a ceremony of seeing his principal, I think. He never did anything else in connection with Lloyd’s that I could find out, except come back again. When he felt his case unusually serious, and that he positively must find an opening, he would go on ’Change at a busy time, and walk in and out, in a kind of gloomy country dance figure, among the assembled magnates. “For,” says Herbert to me, coming home to dinner on one of those special occasions, “I find the truth to be, Handel, that an opening won’t come to one, but one must go to it,—so I have been.”\n\nIf we had been less attached to one another, I think we must have hated one another regularly every morning. I detested the chambers beyond expression at that period of repentance, and could not endure the sight of the Avenger’s livery; which had a more expensive and a less remunerative appearance then than at any other time in the four-and-twenty hours. As we got more and more into debt, breakfast became a hollower and hollower form, and, being on one occasion at breakfast-time threatened (by letter) with legal proceedings, “not unwholly unconnected,” as my local paper might put it, “with jewelery,” I went so far as to seize the Avenger by his blue collar and shake him off his feet,—so that he was actually in the air, like a booted Cupid,—for presuming to suppose that we wanted a roll.\n\nAt certain times—meaning at uncertain times, for they depended on our humour—I would say to Herbert, as if it were a remarkable discovery,—\n\n“My dear Herbert, we are getting on badly.”\n\n“My dear Handel,” Herbert would say to me, in all sincerity, “if you will believe me, those very words were on my lips, by a strange coincidence.”\n\n“Then, Herbert,” I would respond, “let us look into our affairs.”\n\nWe always derived profound satisfaction from making an appointment for this purpose. I always thought this was business, this was the way to confront the thing, this was the way to take the foe by the throat. And I know Herbert thought so too.\n\nWe ordered something rather special for dinner, with a bottle of something similarly out of the common way, in order that our minds might be fortified for the occasion, and we might come well up to the mark. Dinner over, we produced a bundle of pens, a copious supply of ink, and a goodly show of writing and blotting paper. For there was something very comfortable in having plenty of stationery.\n\nI would then take a sheet of paper, and write across the top of it, in a neat hand, the heading, “Memorandum of Pip’s debts”; with Barnard’s Inn and the date very carefully added. Herbert would also take a sheet of paper, and write across it with similar formalities, “Memorandum of Herbert’s debts.”\n\nEach of us would then refer to a confused heap of papers at his side, which had been thrown into drawers, worn into holes in pockets, half burnt in lighting candles, stuck for weeks into the looking-glass, and otherwise damaged. The sound of our pens going refreshed us exceedingly, insomuch that I sometimes found it difficult to distinguish between this edifying business proceeding and actually paying the money. In point of meritorious character, the two things seemed about equal.\n\nWhen we had written a little while, I would ask Herbert how he got on? Herbert probably would have been scratching his head in a most rueful manner at the sight of his accumulating figures.\n\n“They are mounting up, Handel,” Herbert would say; “upon my life, they are mounting up.”\n\n“Be firm, Herbert,” I would retort, plying my own pen with great assiduity. “Look the thing in the face. Look into your affairs. Stare them out of countenance.”\n\n“So I would, Handel, only they are staring me out of countenance.”\n\nHowever, my determined manner would have its effect, and Herbert would fall to work again. After a time he would give up once more, on the plea that he had not got Cobbs’s bill, or Lobbs’s, or Nobbs’s, as the case might be.\n\n“Then, Herbert, estimate; estimate it in round numbers, and put it down.”\n\n“What a fellow of resource you are!” my friend would reply, with admiration. “Really your business powers are very remarkable.”\n\nI thought so too. I established with myself, on these occasions, the reputation of a first-rate man of business,—prompt, decisive, energetic, clear, cool-headed. When I had got all my responsibilities down upon my list, I compared each with the bill, and ticked it off. My self-approval when I ticked an entry was quite a luxurious sensation. When I had no more ticks to make, I folded all my bills up uniformly, docketed each on the back, and tied the whole into a symmetrical bundle. Then I did the same for Herbert (who modestly said he had not my administrative genius), and felt that I had brought his affairs into a focus for him.\n\nMy business habits had one other bright feature, which I called “leaving a Margin.” For example; supposing Herbert’s debts to be one hundred and sixty-four pounds four-and-twopence, I would say, “Leave a margin, and put them down at two hundred.” Or, supposing my own to be four times as much, I would leave a margin, and put them down at seven hundred. I had the highest opinion of the wisdom of this same Margin, but I am bound to acknowledge that on looking back, I deem it to have been an expensive device. For, we always ran into new debt immediately, to the full extent of the margin, and sometimes, in the sense of freedom and solvency it imparted, got pretty far on into another margin.\n\nBut there was a calm, a rest, a virtuous hush, consequent on these examinations of our affairs that gave me, for the time, an admirable opinion of myself. Soothed by my exertions, my method, and Herbert’s compliments, I would sit with his symmetrical bundle and my own on the table before me among the stationery, and feel like a Bank of some sort, rather than a private individual.\n\nWe shut our outer door on these solemn occasions, in order that we might not be interrupted. I had fallen into my serene state one evening, when we heard a letter dropped through the slit in the said door, and fall on the ground. “It’s for you, Handel,” said Herbert, going out and coming back with it, “and I hope there is nothing the matter.” This was in allusion to its heavy black seal and border.\n\nThe letter was signed Trabb & Co., and its contents were simply, that I was an honoured sir, and that they begged to inform me that Mrs. J. Gargery had departed this life on Monday last at twenty minutes past six in the evening, and that my attendance was requested at the interment on Monday next at three o’clock in the afternoon.\n\n# Chapter XXXV.\n\nIt was the first time that a grave had opened in my road of life, and the gap it made in the smooth ground was wonderful. The figure of my sister in her chair by the kitchen fire, haunted me night and day. That the place could possibly be, without her, was something my mind seemed unable to compass; and whereas she had seldom or never been in my thoughts of late, I had now the strangest ideas that she was coming towards me in the street, or that she would presently knock at the door. In my rooms too, with which she had never been at all associated, there was at once the blankness of death and a perpetual suggestion of the sound of her voice or the turn of her face or figure, as if she were still alive and had been often there.\n\nWhatever my fortunes might have been, I could scarcely have recalled my sister with much tenderness. But I suppose there is a shock of regret which may exist without much tenderness. Under its influence (and perhaps to make up for the want of the softer feeling) I was seized with a violent indignation against the assailant from whom she had suffered so much; and I felt that on sufficient proof I could have revengefully pursued Orlick, or any one else, to the last extremity.\n\nHaving written to Joe, to offer him consolation, and to assure him that I would come to the funeral, I passed the intermediate days in the curious state of mind I have glanced at. I went down early in the morning, and alighted at the Blue Boar in good time to walk over to the forge.\n\nIt was fine summer weather again, and, as I walked along, the times when I was a little helpless creature, and my sister did not spare me, vividly returned. But they returned with a gentle tone upon them that softened even the edge of Tickler. For now, the very breath of the beans and clover whispered to my heart that the day must come when it would be well for my memory that others walking in the sunshine should be softened as they thought of me.\n\nAt last I came within sight of the house, and saw that Trabb and Co. had put in a funereal execution and taken possession. Two dismally absurd persons, each ostentatiously exhibiting a crutch done up in a black bandage,—as if that instrument could possibly communicate any comfort to anybody,—were posted at the front door; and in one of them I recognised a postboy discharged from the Boar for turning a young couple into a sawpit on their bridal morning, in consequence of intoxication rendering it necessary for him to ride his horse clasped round the neck with both arms. All the children of the village, and most of the women, were admiring these sable warders and the closed windows of the house and forge; and as I came up, one of the two warders (the postboy) knocked at the door,—implying that I was far too much exhausted by grief to have strength remaining to knock for myself.\n\nAnother sable warder (a carpenter, who had once eaten two geese for a wager) opened the door, and showed me into the best parlour. Here, Mr. Trabb had taken unto himself the best table, and had got all the leaves up, and was holding a kind of black Bazaar, with the aid of a quantity of black pins. At the moment of my arrival, he had just finished putting somebody’s hat into black long-clothes, like an African baby; so he held out his hand for mine. But I, misled by the action, and confused by the occasion, shook hands with him with every testimony of warm affection.\n\nPoor dear Joe, entangled in a little black cloak tied in a large bow under his chin, was seated apart at the upper end of the room; where, as chief mourner, he had evidently been stationed by Trabb. When I bent down and said to him, “Dear Joe, how are you?” he said, “Pip, old chap, you knowed her when she were a fine figure of a—” and clasped my hand and said no more.\n\nBiddy, looking very neat and modest in her black dress, went quietly here and there, and was very helpful. When I had spoken to Biddy, as I thought it not a time for talking I went and sat down near Joe, and there began to wonder in what part of the house it—she—my sister—was. The air of the parlour being faint with the smell of sweet-cake, I looked about for the table of refreshments; it was scarcely visible until one had got accustomed to the gloom, but there was a cut-up plum cake upon it, and there were cut-up oranges, and sandwiches, and biscuits, and two decanters that I knew very well as ornaments, but had never seen used in all my life; one full of port, and one of sherry. Standing at this table, I became conscious of the servile Pumblechook in a black cloak and several yards of hatband, who was alternately stuffing himself, and making obsequious movements to catch my attention. The moment he succeeded, he came over to me (breathing sherry and crumbs), and said in a subdued voice, “May I, dear sir?” and did. I then descried Mr. and Mrs. Hubble; the last-named in a decent speechless paroxysm in a corner. We were all going to “follow,” and were all in course of being tied up separately (by Trabb) into ridiculous bundles.\n\n“Which I meantersay, Pip,” Joe whispered me, as we were being what Mr. Trabb called “formed” in the parlour, two and two,—and it was dreadfully like a preparation for some grim kind of dance; “which I meantersay, sir, as I would in preference have carried her to the church myself, along with three or four friendly ones wot come to it with willing harts and arms, but it were considered wot the neighbours would look down on such and would be of opinions as it were wanting in respect.”\n\n“Pocket-handkerchiefs out, all!” cried Mr. Trabb at this point, in a depressed business-like voice. “Pocket-handkerchiefs out! We are ready!”\n\nSo we all put our pocket-handkerchiefs to our faces, as if our noses were bleeding, and filed out two and two; Joe and I; Biddy and Pumblechook; Mr. and Mrs. Hubble. The remains of my poor sister had been brought round by the kitchen door, and, it being a point of Undertaking ceremony that the six bearers must be stifled and blinded under a horrible black velvet housing with a white border, the whole looked like a blind monster with twelve human legs, shuffling and blundering along, under the guidance of two keepers,—the postboy and his comrade.\n\nThe neighbourhood, however, highly approved of these arrangements, and we were much admired as we went through the village; the more youthful and vigorous part of the community making dashes now and then to cut us off, and lying in wait to intercept us at points of vantage. At such times the more exuberant among them called out in an excited manner on our emergence round some corner of expectancy, “Here they come!” “Here they are!” and we were all but cheered. In this progress I was much annoyed by the abject Pumblechook, who, being behind me, persisted all the way as a delicate attention in arranging my streaming hatband, and smoothing my cloak. My thoughts were further distracted by the excessive pride of Mr. and Mrs. Hubble, who were surpassingly conceited and vainglorious in being members of so distinguished a procession.\n\nAnd now the range of marshes lay clear before us, with the sails of the ships on the river growing out of it; and we went into the churchyard, close to the graves of my unknown parents, Philip Pirrip, late of this parish, and Also Georgiana, Wife of the Above. And there, my sister was laid quietly in the earth, while the larks sang high above it, and the light wind strewed it with beautiful shadows of clouds and trees.\n\nOf the conduct of the worldly minded Pumblechook while this was doing, I desire to say no more than it was all addressed to me; and that even when those noble passages were read which remind humanity how it brought nothing into the world and can take nothing out, and how it fleeth like a shadow and never continueth long in one stay, I heard him cough a reservation of the case of a young gentleman who came unexpectedly into large property. When we got back, he had the hardihood to tell me that he wished my sister could have known I had done her so much honour, and to hint that she would have considered it reasonably purchased at the price of her death. After that, he drank all the rest of the sherry, and Mr. Hubble drank the port, and the two talked (which I have since observed to be customary in such cases) as if they were of quite another race from the deceased, and were notoriously immortal. Finally, he went away with Mr. and Mrs. Hubble,—to make an evening of it, I felt sure, and to tell the Jolly Bargemen that he was the founder of my fortunes and my earliest benefactor.\n\nWhen they were all gone, and when Trabb and his men—but not his Boy; I looked for him—had crammed their mummery into bags, and were gone too, the house felt wholesomer. Soon afterwards, Biddy, Joe, and I, had a cold dinner together; but we dined in the best parlour, not in the old kitchen, and Joe was so exceedingly particular what he did with his knife and fork and the saltcellar and what not, that there was great restraint upon us. But after dinner, when I made him take his pipe, and when I had loitered with him about the forge, and when we sat down together on the great block of stone outside it, we got on better. I noticed that after the funeral Joe changed his clothes so far, as to make a compromise between his Sunday dress and working dress; in which the dear fellow looked natural, and like the Man he was.\n\nHe was very much pleased by my asking if I might sleep in my own little room, and I was pleased too; for I felt that I had done rather a great thing in making the request. When the shadows of evening were closing in, I took an opportunity of getting into the garden with Biddy for a little talk.\n\n“Biddy,” said I, “I think you might have written to me about these sad matters.”\n\n“Do you, Mr. Pip?” said Biddy. “I should have written if I had thought that.”\n\n“Don’t suppose that I mean to be unkind, Biddy, when I say I consider that you ought to have thought that.”\n\n“Do you, Mr. Pip?”\n\nShe was so quiet, and had such an orderly, good, and pretty way with her, that I did not like the thought of making her cry again. After looking a little at her downcast eyes as she walked beside me, I gave up that point.\n\n“I suppose it will be difficult for you to remain here now, Biddy dear?”\n\n“Oh! I can’t do so, Mr. Pip,” said Biddy, in a tone of regret but still of quiet conviction. “I have been speaking to Mrs. Hubble, and I am going to her to-morrow. I hope we shall be able to take some care of Mr. Gargery, together, until he settles down.”\n\n“How are you going to live, Biddy? If you want any mo—”\n\n“How am I going to live?” repeated Biddy, striking in, with a momentary flush upon her face. “I’ll tell you, Mr. Pip. I am going to try to get the place of mistress in the new school nearly finished here. I can be well recommended by all the neighbours, and I hope I can be industrious and patient, and teach myself while I teach others. You know, Mr. Pip,” pursued Biddy, with a smile, as she raised her eyes to my face, “the new schools are not like the old, but I learnt a good deal from you after that time, and have had time since then to improve.”\n\n“I think you would always improve, Biddy, under any circumstances.”\n\n“Ah! Except in my bad side of human nature,” murmured Biddy.\n\nIt was not so much a reproach as an irresistible thinking aloud. Well! I thought I would give up that point too. So, I walked a little further with Biddy, looking silently at her downcast eyes.\n\n“I have not heard the particulars of my sister’s death, Biddy.”\n\n“They are very slight, poor thing. She had been in one of her bad states—though they had got better of late, rather than worse—for four days, when she came out of it in the evening, just at tea-time, and said quite plainly, ‘Joe.’ As she had never said any word for a long while, I ran and fetched in Mr. Gargery from the forge. She made signs to me that she wanted him to sit down close to her, and wanted me to put her arms round his neck. So I put them round his neck, and she laid her head down on his shoulder quite content and satisfied. And so she presently said ‘Joe’ again, and once ‘Pardon,’ and once ‘Pip.’ And so she never lifted her head up any more, and it was just an hour later when we laid it down on her own bed, because we found she was gone.”\n\nBiddy cried; the darkening garden, and the lane, and the stars that were coming out, were blurred in my own sight.\n\n“Nothing was ever discovered, Biddy?”\n\n“Nothing.”\n\n“Do you know what is become of Orlick?”\n\n“I should think from the colour of his clothes that he is working in the quarries.”\n\n“Of course you have seen him then?—Why are you looking at that dark tree in the lane?”\n\n“I saw him there, on the night she died.”\n\n“That was not the last time either, Biddy?”\n\n“No; I have seen him there, since we have been walking here.—It is of no use,” said Biddy, laying her hand upon my arm, as I was for running out, “you know I would not deceive you; he was not there a minute, and he is gone.”\n\nIt revived my utmost indignation to find that she was still pursued by this fellow, and I felt inveterate against him. I told her so, and told her that I would spend any money or take any pains to drive him out of that country. By degrees she led me into more temperate talk, and she told me how Joe loved me, and how Joe never complained of anything,—she didn’t say, of me; she had no need; I knew what she meant,—but ever did his duty in his way of life, with a strong hand, a quiet tongue, and a gentle heart.\n\n“Indeed, it would be hard to say too much for him,” said I; “and Biddy, we must often speak of these things, for of course I shall be often down here now. I am not going to leave poor Joe alone.”\n\nBiddy said never a single word.\n\n“Biddy, don’t you hear me?”\n\n“Yes, Mr. Pip.”\n\n“Not to mention your calling me Mr. Pip,—which appears to me to be in bad taste, Biddy,—what do you mean?”\n\n“What do I mean?” asked Biddy, timidly.\n\n“Biddy,” said I, in a virtuously self-asserting manner, “I must request to know what you mean by this?”\n\n“By this?” said Biddy.\n\n“Now, don’t echo,” I retorted. “You used not to echo, Biddy.”\n“Used not!” said Biddy. “O Mr. Pip! Used!”\n\nWell! I rather thought I would give up that point too. After another silent turn in the garden, I fell back on the main position.\n\n“Biddy,” said I, “I made a remark respecting my coming down here often, to see Joe, which you received with a marked silence. Have the goodness, Biddy, to tell me why.”\n\n“Are you quite sure, then, that you WILL come to see him often?” asked Biddy, stopping in the narrow garden walk, and looking at me under the stars with a clear and honest eye.\n\n“O dear me!” said I, as if I found myself compelled to give up Biddy in despair. “This really is a very bad side of human nature! Don’t say any more, if you please, Biddy. This shocks me very much.”\n\nFor which cogent reason I kept Biddy at a distance during supper, and when I went up to my own old little room, took as stately a leave of her as I could, in my murmuring soul, deem reconcilable with the churchyard and the event of the day. As often as I was restless in the night, and that was every quarter of an hour, I reflected what an unkindness, what an injury, what an injustice, Biddy had done me.\n\nEarly in the morning I was to go. Early in the morning I was out, and looking in, unseen, at one of the wooden windows of the forge. There I stood, for minutes, looking at Joe, already at work with a glow of health and strength upon his face that made it show as if the bright sun of the life in store for him were shining on it.\n\n“Good-bye, dear Joe!—No, don’t wipe it off—for God’s sake, give me your blackened hand!—I shall be down soon and often.”\n\n“Never too soon, sir,” said Joe, “and never too often, Pip!”\n\nBiddy was waiting for me at the kitchen door, with a mug of new milk and a crust of bread. “Biddy,” said I, when I gave her my hand at parting, “I am not angry, but I am hurt.”\n\n“No, don’t be hurt,” she pleaded quite pathetically; “let only me be hurt, if I have been ungenerous.”\n\nOnce more, the mists were rising as I walked away. If they disclosed to me, as I suspect they did, that I should not come back, and that Biddy was quite right, all I can say is,—they were quite right too.\n\n# Chapter XXXVI.\n\nHerbert and I went on from bad to worse, in the way of increasing our debts, looking into our affairs, leaving Margins, and the like exemplary transactions; and Time went on, whether or no, as he has a way of doing; and I came of age,—in fulfilment of Herbert’s prediction, that I should do so before I knew where I was.\n\nHerbert himself had come of age eight months before me. As he had nothing else than his majority to come into, the event did not make a profound sensation in Barnard’s Inn. But we had looked forward to my one-and-twentieth birthday, with a crowd of speculations and anticipations, for we had both considered that my guardian could hardly help saying something definite on that occasion.\n\nI had taken care to have it well understood in Little Britain when my birthday was. On the day before it, I received an official note from Wemmick, informing me that Mr. Jaggers would be glad if I would call upon him at five in the afternoon of the auspicious day. This convinced us that something great was to happen, and threw me into an unusual flutter when I repaired to my guardian’s office, a model of punctuality.\n\nIn the outer office Wemmick offered me his congratulations, and incidentally rubbed the side of his nose with a folded piece of tissue-paper that I liked the look of. But he said nothing respecting it, and motioned me with a nod into my guardian’s room. It was November, and my guardian was standing before his fire leaning his back against the chimney-piece, with his hands under his coattails.\n\n“Well, Pip,” said he, “I must call you Mr. Pip to-day. Congratulations, Mr. Pip.”\n\nWe shook hands,—he was always a remarkably short shaker,—and I thanked him.\n\n“Take a chair, Mr. Pip,” said my guardian.\n\nAs I sat down, and he preserved his attitude and bent his brows at his boots, I felt at a disadvantage, which reminded me of that old time when I had been put upon a tombstone. The two ghastly casts on the shelf were not far from him, and their expression was as if they were making a stupid apoplectic attempt to attend to the conversation.\n\n“Now my young friend,” my guardian began, as if I were a witness in the box, “I am going to have a word or two with you.”\n\n“If you please, sir.”\n\n“What do you suppose,” said Mr. Jaggers, bending forward to look at the ground, and then throwing his head back to look at the ceiling,—“what do you suppose you are living at the rate of?”\n\n“At the rate of, sir?”\n\n“At,” repeated Mr. Jaggers, still looking at the ceiling, “the—rate—of?” And then looked all round the room, and paused with his pocket-handkerchief in his hand, half-way to his nose.\n\nI had looked into my affairs so often, that I had thoroughly destroyed any slight notion I might ever have had of their bearings. Reluctantly, I confessed myself quite unable to answer the question. This reply seemed agreeable to Mr. Jaggers, who said, “I thought so!” and blew his nose with an air of satisfaction.\n\n“Now, I have asked you a question, my friend,” said Mr. Jaggers. “Have you anything to ask me?”\n\n“Of course it would be a great relief to me to ask you several questions, sir; but I remember your prohibition.”\n\n“Ask one,” said Mr. Jaggers.\n\n“Is my benefactor to be made known to me to-day?”\n\n“No. Ask another.”\n\n“Is that confidence to be imparted to me soon?”\n\n“Waive that, a moment,” said Mr. Jaggers, “and ask another.”\n\nI looked about me, but there appeared to be now no possible escape from the inquiry, “Have-I—anything to receive, sir?” On that, Mr. Jaggers said, triumphantly, “I thought we should come to it!” and called to Wemmick to give him that piece of paper. Wemmick appeared, handed it in, and disappeared.\n\n“Now, Mr. Pip,” said Mr. Jaggers, “attend, if you please. You have been drawing pretty freely here; your name occurs pretty often in Wemmick’s cash-book; but you are in debt, of course?”\n\n“I am afraid I must say yes, sir.”\n\n“You know you must say yes; don’t you?” said Mr. Jaggers.\n\n“Yes, sir.”\n\n“I don’t ask you what you owe, because you don’t know; and if you did know, you wouldn’t tell me; you would say less. Yes, yes, my friend,” cried Mr. Jaggers, waving his forefinger to stop me as I made a show of protesting: “it’s likely enough that you think you wouldn’t, but you would. You’ll excuse me, but I know better than you. Now, take this piece of paper in your hand. You have got it? Very good. Now, unfold it and tell me what it is.”\n\n“This is a bank-note,” said I, “for five hundred pounds.”\n\n“That is a bank-note,” repeated Mr. Jaggers, “for five hundred pounds. And a very handsome sum of money too, I think. You consider it so?”\n\n“How could I do otherwise!”\n\n“Ah! But answer the question,” said Mr. Jaggers.\n\n“You consider it, undoubtedly, a handsome sum of money. Now, that handsome sum of money, Pip, is your own. It is a present to you on this day, in earnest of your expectations. And at the rate of that handsome sum of money per annum, and at no higher rate, you are to live until the donor of the whole appears. That is to say, you will now take your money affairs entirely into your own hands, and you will draw from Wemmick one hundred and twenty-five pounds per quarter, until you are in communication with the fountain-head, and no longer with the mere agent. As I have told you before, I am the mere agent. I execute my instructions, and I am paid for doing so. I think them injudicious, but I am not paid for giving any opinion on their merits.”\n\nI was beginning to express my gratitude to my benefactor for the great liberality with which I was treated, when Mr. Jaggers stopped me. “I am not paid, Pip,” said he, coolly, “to carry your words to any one;” and then gathered up his coat-tails, as he had gathered up the subject, and stood frowning at his boots as if he suspected them of designs against him.\n\nAfter a pause, I hinted,—\n\n“There was a question just now, Mr. Jaggers, which you desired me to waive for a moment. I hope I am doing nothing wrong in asking it again?”\n\n“What is it?” said he.\n\nI might have known that he would never help me out; but it took me aback to have to shape the question afresh, as if it were quite new. “Is it likely,” I said, after hesitating, “that my patron, the fountain-head you have spoken of, Mr. Jaggers, will soon—” there I delicately stopped.\n\n“Will soon what?” asked Mr. Jaggers. “That’s no question as it stands, you know.”\n\n“Will soon come to London,” said I, after casting about for a precise form of words, “or summon me anywhere else?”\n\n“Now, here,” replied Mr. Jaggers, fixing me for the first time with his dark deep-set eyes, “we must revert to the evening when we first encountered one another in your village. What did I tell you then, Pip?”\n\n“You told me, Mr. Jaggers, that it might be years hence when that person appeared.”\n\n“Just so,” said Mr. Jaggers, “that’s my answer.”\n\nAs we looked full at one another, I felt my breath come quicker in my strong desire to get something out of him. And as I felt that it came quicker, and as I felt that he saw that it came quicker, I felt that I had less chance than ever of getting anything out of him.\n\n“Do you suppose it will still be years hence, Mr. Jaggers?”\n\nMr. Jaggers shook his head,—not in negativing the question, but in altogether negativing the notion that he could anyhow be got to answer it,—and the two horrible casts of the twitched faces looked, when my eyes strayed up to them, as if they had come to a crisis in their suspended attention, and were going to sneeze.\n\n“Come!” said Mr. Jaggers, warming the backs of his legs with the backs of his warmed hands, “I’ll be plain with you, my friend Pip. That’s a question I must not be asked. You’ll understand that better, when I tell you it’s a question that might compromise me. Come! I’ll go a little further with you; I’ll say something more.”\n\nHe bent down so low to frown at his boots, that he was able to rub the calves of his legs in the pause he made.\n\n“When that person discloses,” said Mr. Jaggers, straightening himself, “you and that person will settle your own affairs. When that person discloses, my part in this business will cease and determine. When that person discloses, it will not be necessary for me to know anything about it. And that’s all I have got to say.”\n\nWe looked at one another until I withdrew my eyes, and looked thoughtfully at the floor. From this last speech I derived the notion that Miss Havisham, for some reason or no reason, had not taken him into her confidence as to her designing me for Estella; that he resented this, and felt a jealousy about it; or that he really did object to that scheme, and would have nothing to do with it. When I raised my eyes again, I found that he had been shrewdly looking at me all the time, and was doing so still.\n\n“If that is all you have to say, sir,” I remarked, “there can be nothing left for me to say.”\n\nHe nodded assent, and pulled out his thief-dreaded watch, and asked me where I was going to dine? I replied at my own chambers, with Herbert. As a necessary sequence, I asked him if he would favour us with his company, and he promptly accepted the invitation. But he insisted on walking home with me, in order that I might make no extra preparation for him, and first he had a letter or two to write, and (of course) had his hands to wash. So I said I would go into the outer office and talk to Wemmick.\n\nThe fact was, that when the five hundred pounds had come into my pocket, a thought had come into my head which had been often there before; and it appeared to me that Wemmick was a good person to advise with concerning such thought.\n\nHe had already locked up his safe, and made preparations for going home. He had left his desk, brought out his two greasy office candlesticks and stood them in line with the snuffers on a slab near the door, ready to be extinguished; he had raked his fire low, put his hat and great-coat ready, and was beating himself all over the chest with his safe-key, as an athletic exercise after business.\n\n“Mr. Wemmick,” said I, “I want to ask your opinion. I am very desirous to serve a friend.”\n\nWemmick tightened his post-office and shook his head, as if his opinion were dead against any fatal weakness of that sort.\n\n“This friend,” I pursued, “is trying to get on in commercial life, but has no money, and finds it difficult and disheartening to make a beginning. Now I want somehow to help him to a beginning.”\n\n“With money down?” said Wemmick, in a tone drier than any sawdust.\n\n“With some money down,” I replied, for an uneasy remembrance shot across me of that symmetrical bundle of papers at home—“with some money down, and perhaps some anticipation of my expectations.”\n\n“Mr. Pip,” said Wemmick, “I should like just to run over with you on my fingers, if you please, the names of the various bridges up as high as Chelsea Reach. Let’s see; there’s London, one; Southwark, two; Blackfriars, three; Waterloo, four; Westminster, five; Vauxhall, six.” He had checked off each bridge in its turn, with the handle of his safe-key on the palm of his hand. “There’s as many as six, you see, to choose from.”\n\n“I don’t understand you,” said I.\n\n“Choose your bridge, Mr. Pip,” returned Wemmick, “and take a walk upon your bridge, and pitch your money into the Thames over the centre arch of your bridge, and you know the end of it. Serve a friend with it, and you may know the end of it too,—but it’s a less pleasant and profitable end.”\n\nI could have posted a newspaper in his mouth, he made it so wide after saying this.\n\n“This is very discouraging,” said I.\n\n“Meant to be so,” said Wemmick.\n\n“Then is it your opinion,” I inquired, with some little indignation, “that a man should never—”\n\n“—Invest portable property in a friend?” said Wemmick. “Certainly he should not. Unless he wants to get rid of the friend,—and then it becomes a question how much portable property it may be worth to get rid of him.”\n\n“And that,” said I, “is your deliberate opinion, Mr. Wemmick?”\n\n“That,” he returned, “is my deliberate opinion in this office.”\n\n“Ah!” said I, pressing him, for I thought I saw him near a loophole here; “but would that be your opinion at Walworth?”\n\n“Mr. Pip,” he replied, with gravity, “Walworth is one place, and this office is another. Much as the Aged is one person, and Mr. Jaggers is another. They must not be confounded together. My Walworth sentiments must be taken at Walworth; none but my official sentiments can be taken in this office.”\n\n“Very well,” said I, much relieved, “then I shall look you up at Walworth, you may depend upon it.”\n\n“Mr. Pip,” he returned, “you will be welcome there, in a private and personal capacity.”\n\nWe had held this conversation in a low voice, well knowing my guardian’s ears to be the sharpest of the sharp. As he now appeared in his doorway, towelling his hands, Wemmick got on his great-coat and stood by to snuff out the candles. We all three went into the street together, and from the door-step Wemmick turned his way, and Mr. Jaggers and I turned ours.\n\nI could not help wishing more than once that evening, that Mr. Jaggers had had an Aged in Gerrard Street, or a Stinger, or a Something, or a Somebody, to unbend his brows a little. It was an uncomfortable consideration on a twenty-first birthday, that coming of age at all seemed hardly worth while in such a guarded and suspicious world as he made of it. He was a thousand times better informed and cleverer than Wemmick, and yet I would a thousand times rather have had Wemmick to dinner. And Mr. Jaggers made not me alone intensely melancholy, because, after he was gone, Herbert said of himself, with his eyes fixed on the fire, that he thought he must have committed a felony and forgotten the details of it, he felt so dejected and guilty.\n\n# Chapter XXXVII.\n\nDeeming Sunday the best day for taking Mr. Wemmick’s Walworth sentiments, I devoted the next ensuing Sunday afternoon to a pilgrimage to the Castle. On arriving before the battlements, I found the Union Jack flying and the drawbridge up; but undeterred by this show of defiance and resistance, I rang at the gate, and was admitted in a most pacific manner by the Aged.\n\n“My son, sir,” said the old man, after securing the drawbridge, “rather had it in his mind that you might happen to drop in, and he left word that he would soon be home from his afternoon’s walk. He is very regular in his walks, is my son. Very regular in everything, is my son.”\n\nI nodded at the old gentleman as Wemmick himself might have nodded, and we went in and sat down by the fireside.\n\n“You made acquaintance with my son, sir,” said the old man, in his chirping way, while he warmed his hands at the blaze, “at his office, I expect?” I nodded. “Hah! I have heerd that my son is a wonderful hand at his business, sir?” I nodded hard. “Yes; so they tell me. His business is the Law?” I nodded harder. “Which makes it more surprising in my son,” said the old man, “for he was not brought up to the Law, but to the Wine-Coopering.”\n\nCurious to know how the old gentleman stood informed concerning the reputation of Mr. Jaggers, I roared that name at him. He threw me into the greatest confusion by laughing heartily and replying in a very sprightly manner, “No, to be sure; you’re right.” And to this hour I have not the faintest notion what he meant, or what joke he thought I had made.\n\nAs I could not sit there nodding at him perpetually, without making some other attempt to interest him, I shouted at inquiry whether his own calling in life had been “the Wine-Coopering.” By dint of straining that term out of myself several times and tapping the old gentleman on the chest to associate it with him, I at last succeeded in making my meaning understood.\n\n“No,” said the old gentleman; “the warehousing, the warehousing. First, over yonder;” he appeared to mean up the chimney, but I believe he intended to refer me to Liverpool; “and then in the City of London here. However, having an infirmity—for I am hard of hearing, sir—”\n\nI expressed in pantomime the greatest astonishment.\n\n“—Yes, hard of hearing; having that infirmity coming upon me, my son he went into the Law, and he took charge of me, and he by little and little made out this elegant and beautiful property. But returning to what you said, you know,” pursued the old man, again laughing heartily, “what I say is, No to be sure; you’re right.”\n\nI was modestly wondering whether my utmost ingenuity would have enabled me to say anything that would have amused him half as much as this imaginary pleasantry, when I was startled by a sudden click in the wall on one side of the chimney, and the ghostly tumbling open of a little wooden flap with “JOHN” upon it. The old man, following my eyes, cried with great triumph, “My son’s come home!” and we both went out to the drawbridge.\n\nIt was worth any money to see Wemmick waving a salute to me from the other side of the moat, when we might have shaken hands across it with the greatest ease. The Aged was so delighted to work the drawbridge, that I made no offer to assist him, but stood quiet until Wemmick had come across, and had presented me to Miss Skiffins; a lady by whom he was accompanied.\n\nMiss Skiffins was of a wooden appearance, and was, like her escort, in the post-office branch of the service. She might have been some two or three years younger than Wemmick, and I judged her to stand possessed of portable property. The cut of her dress from the waist upward, both before and behind, made her figure very like a boy’s kite; and I might have pronounced her gown a little too decidedly orange, and her gloves a little too intensely green. But she seemed to be a good sort of fellow, and showed a high regard for the Aged. I was not long in discovering that she was a frequent visitor at the Castle; for, on our going in, and my complimenting Wemmick on his ingenious contrivance for announcing himself to the Aged, he begged me to give my attention for a moment to the other side of the chimney, and disappeared. Presently another click came, and another little door tumbled open with “Miss Skiffins” on it; then Miss Skiffins shut up and John tumbled open; then Miss Skiffins and John both tumbled open together, and finally shut up together. On Wemmick’s return from working these mechanical appliances, I expressed the great admiration with which I regarded them, and he said, “Well, you know, they’re both pleasant and useful to the Aged. And by George, sir, it’s a thing worth mentioning, that of all the people who come to this gate, the secret of those pulls is only known to the Aged, Miss Skiffins, and me!”\n\n“And Mr. Wemmick made them,” added Miss Skiffins, “with his own hands out of his own head.”\n\nWhile Miss Skiffins was taking off her bonnet (she retained her green gloves during the evening as an outward and visible sign that there was company), Wemmick invited me to take a walk with him round the property, and see how the island looked in wintertime. Thinking that he did this to give me an opportunity of taking his Walworth sentiments, I seized the opportunity as soon as we were out of the Castle.\n\nHaving thought of the matter with care, I approached my subject as if I had never hinted at it before. I informed Wemmick that I was anxious in behalf of Herbert Pocket, and I told him how we had first met, and how we had fought. I glanced at Herbert’s home, and at his character, and at his having no means but such as he was dependent on his father for; those, uncertain and unpunctual. I alluded to the advantages I had derived in my first rawness and ignorance from his society, and I confessed that I feared I had but ill repaid them, and that he might have done better without me and my expectations. Keeping Miss Havisham in the background at a great distance, I still hinted at the possibility of my having competed with him in his prospects, and at the certainty of his possessing a generous soul, and being far above any mean distrusts, retaliations, or designs. For all these reasons (I told Wemmick), and because he was my young companion and friend, and I had a great affection for him, I wished my own good fortune to reflect some rays upon him, and therefore I sought advice from Wemmick’s experience and knowledge of men and affairs, how I could best try with my resources to help Herbert to some present income,—say of a hundred a year, to keep him in good hope and heart,—and gradually to buy him on to some small partnership. I begged Wemmick, in conclusion, to understand that my help must always be rendered without Herbert’s knowledge or suspicion, and that there was no one else in the world with whom I could advise. I wound up by laying my hand upon his shoulder, and saying, “I can’t help confiding in you, though I know it must be troublesome to you; but that is your fault, in having ever brought me here.”\n\nWemmick was silent for a little while, and then said with a kind of start, “Well you know, Mr. Pip, I must tell you one thing. This is devilish good of you.”\n\n“Say you’ll help me to be good then,” said I.\n\n“Ecod,” replied Wemmick, shaking his head, “that’s not my trade.”\n\n“Nor is this your trading-place,” said I.\n\n“You are right,” he returned. “You hit the nail on the head. Mr. Pip, I’ll put on my considering-cap, and I think all you want to do may be done by degrees. Skiffins (that’s her brother) is an accountant and agent. I’ll look him up and go to work for you.”\n\n“I thank you ten thousand times.”\n\n“On the contrary,” said he, “I thank you, for though we are strictly in our private and personal capacity, still it may be mentioned that there are Newgate cobwebs about, and it brushes them away.”\n\nAfter a little further conversation to the same effect, we returned into the Castle where we found Miss Skiffins preparing tea. The responsible duty of making the toast was delegated to the Aged, and that excellent old gentleman was so intent upon it that he seemed to me in some danger of melting his eyes. It was no nominal meal that we were going to make, but a vigorous reality. The Aged prepared such a hay-stack of buttered toast, that I could scarcely see him over it as it simmered on an iron stand hooked on to the top-bar; while Miss Skiffins brewed such a jorum of tea, that the pig in the back premises became strongly excited, and repeatedly expressed his desire to participate in the entertainment.\n\nThe flag had been struck, and the gun had been fired, at the right moment of time, and I felt as snugly cut off from the rest of Walworth as if the moat were thirty feet wide by as many deep. Nothing disturbed the tranquillity of the Castle, but the occasional tumbling open of John and Miss Skiffins: which little doors were a prey to some spasmodic infirmity that made me sympathetically uncomfortable until I got used to it. I inferred from the methodical nature of Miss Skiffins’s arrangements that she made tea there every Sunday night; and I rather suspected that a classic brooch she wore, representing the profile of an undesirable female with a very straight nose and a very new moon, was a piece of portable property that had been given her by Wemmick.\n\nWe ate the whole of the toast, and drank tea in proportion, and it was delightful to see how warm and greasy we all got after it. The Aged especially, might have passed for some clean old chief of a savage tribe, just oiled. After a short pause of repose, Miss Skiffins—in the absence of the little servant who, it seemed, retired to the bosom of her family on Sunday afternoons—washed up the tea-things, in a trifling lady-like amateur manner that compromised none of us. Then, she put on her gloves again, and we drew round the fire, and Wemmick said, “Now, Aged Parent, tip us the paper.”\n\nWemmick explained to me while the Aged got his spectacles out, that this was according to custom, and that it gave the old gentleman infinite satisfaction to read the news aloud. “I won’t offer an apology,” said Wemmick, “for he isn’t capable of many pleasures—are you, Aged P.?”\n\n“All right, John, all right,” returned the old man, seeing himself spoken to.\n\n“Only tip him a nod every now and then when he looks off his paper,” said Wemmick, “and he’ll be as happy as a king. We are all attention, Aged One.”\n\n“All right, John, all right!” returned the cheerful old man, so busy and so pleased, that it really was quite charming.\n\nThe Aged’s reading reminded me of the classes at Mr. Wopsle’s great-aunt’s, with the pleasanter peculiarity that it seemed to come through a keyhole. As he wanted the candles close to him, and as he was always on the verge of putting either his head or the newspaper into them, he required as much watching as a powder-mill. But Wemmick was equally untiring and gentle in his vigilance, and the Aged read on, quite unconscious of his many rescues. Whenever he looked at us, we all expressed the greatest interest and amazement, and nodded until he resumed again.\n\nAs Wemmick and Miss Skiffins sat side by side, and as I sat in a shadowy corner, I observed a slow and gradual elongation of Mr. Wemmick’s mouth, powerfully suggestive of his slowly and gradually stealing his arm round Miss Skiffins’s waist. In course of time I saw his hand appear on the other side of Miss Skiffins; but at that moment Miss Skiffins neatly stopped him with the green glove, unwound his arm again as if it were an article of dress, and with the greatest deliberation laid it on the table before her. Miss Skiffins’s composure while she did this was one of the most remarkable sights I have ever seen, and if I could have thought the act consistent with abstraction of mind, I should have deemed that Miss Skiffins performed it mechanically.\n\nBy and by, I noticed Wemmick’s arm beginning to disappear again, and gradually fading out of view. Shortly afterwards, his mouth began to widen again. After an interval of suspense on my part that was quite enthralling and almost painful, I saw his hand appear on the other side of Miss Skiffins. Instantly, Miss Skiffins stopped it with the neatness of a placid boxer, took off that girdle or cestus as before, and laid it on the table. Taking the table to represent the path of virtue, I am justified in stating that during the whole time of the Aged’s reading, Wemmick’s arm was straying from the path of virtue and being recalled to it by Miss Skiffins.\n\nAt last, the Aged read himself into a light slumber. This was the time for Wemmick to produce a little kettle, a tray of glasses, and a black bottle with a porcelain-topped cork, representing some clerical dignitary of a rubicund and social aspect. With the aid of these appliances we all had something warm to drink, including the Aged, who was soon awake again. Miss Skiffins mixed, and I observed that she and Wemmick drank out of one glass. Of course I knew better than to offer to see Miss Skiffins home, and under the circumstances I thought I had best go first; which I did, taking a cordial leave of the Aged, and having passed a pleasant evening.\n\nBefore a week was out, I received a note from Wemmick, dated Walworth, stating that he hoped he had made some advance in that matter appertaining to our private and personal capacities, and that he would be glad if I could come and see him again upon it. So, I went out to Walworth again, and yet again, and yet again, and I saw him by appointment in the City several times, but never held any communication with him on the subject in or near Little Britain. The upshot was, that we found a worthy young merchant or shipping-broker, not long established in business, who wanted intelligent help, and who wanted capital, and who in due course of time and receipt would want a partner. Between him and me, secret articles were signed of which Herbert was the subject, and I paid him half of my five hundred pounds down, and engaged for sundry other payments: some, to fall due at certain dates out of my income: some, contingent on my coming into my property. Miss Skiffins’s brother conducted the negotiation. Wemmick pervaded it throughout, but never appeared in it.\n\nThe whole business was so cleverly managed, that Herbert had not the least suspicion of my hand being in it. I never shall forget the radiant face with which he came home one afternoon, and told me, as a mighty piece of news, of his having fallen in with one Clarriker (the young merchant’s name), and of Clarriker’s having shown an extraordinary inclination towards him, and of his belief that the opening had come at last. Day by day as his hopes grew stronger and his face brighter, he must have thought me a more and more affectionate friend, for I had the greatest difficulty in restraining my tears of triumph when I saw him so happy. At length, the thing being done, and he having that day entered Clarriker’s House, and he having talked to me for a whole evening in a flush of pleasure and success, I did really cry in good earnest when I went to bed, to think that my expectations had done some good to somebody.\n\nA great event in my life, the turning point of my life, now opens on my view. But, before I proceed to narrate it, and before I pass on to all the changes it involved, I must give one chapter to Estella. It is not much to give to the theme that so long filled my heart.\n\n# Chapter XXXVIII.\n\nIf that staid old house near the Green at Richmond should ever come to be haunted when I am dead, it will be haunted, surely, by my ghost. O the many, many nights and days through which the unquiet spirit within me haunted that house when Estella lived there! Let my body be where it would, my spirit was always wandering, wandering, wandering, about that house.\n\nThe lady with whom Estella was placed, Mrs. Brandley by name, was a widow, with one daughter several years older than Estella. The mother looked young, and the daughter looked old; the mother’s complexion was pink, and the daughter’s was yellow; the mother set up for frivolity, and the daughter for theology. They were in what is called a good position, and visited, and were visited by, numbers of people. Little, if any, community of feeling subsisted between them and Estella, but the understanding was established that they were necessary to her, and that she was necessary to them. Mrs. Brandley had been a friend of Miss Havisham’s before the time of her seclusion.\n\nIn Mrs. Brandley’s house and out of Mrs. Brandley’s house, I suffered every kind and degree of torture that Estella could cause me. The nature of my relations with her, which placed me on terms of familiarity without placing me on terms of favour, conduced to my distraction. She made use of me to tease other admirers, and she turned the very familiarity between herself and me to the account of putting a constant slight on my devotion to her. If I had been her secretary, steward, half-brother, poor relation,—if I had been a younger brother of her appointed husband,—I could not have seemed to myself further from my hopes when I was nearest to her. The privilege of calling her by her name and hearing her call me by mine became, under the circumstances an aggravation of my trials; and while I think it likely that it almost maddened her other lovers, I know too certainly that it almost maddened me.\n\nShe had admirers without end. No doubt my jealousy made an admirer of every one who went near her; but there were more than enough of them without that.\n\nI saw her often at Richmond, I heard of her often in town, and I used often to take her and the Brandleys on the water; there were picnics, fête days, plays, operas, concerts, parties, all sorts of pleasures, through which I pursued her,—and they were all miseries to me. I never had one hour’s happiness in her society, and yet my mind all round the four-and-twenty hours was harping on the happiness of having her with me unto death.\n\nThroughout this part of our intercourse,—and it lasted, as will presently be seen, for what I then thought a long time,—she habitually reverted to that tone which expressed that our association was forced upon us. There were other times when she would come to a sudden check in this tone and in all her many tones, and would seem to pity me.\n\n“Pip, Pip,” she said one evening, coming to such a check, when we sat apart at a darkening window of the house in Richmond; “will you never take warning?”\n\n“Of what?”\n“Of me.”\n\n“Warning not to be attracted by you, do you mean, Estella?”\n\n“Do I mean! If you don’t know what I mean, you are blind.”\n\nI should have replied that Love was commonly reputed blind, but for the reason that I always was restrained—and this was not the least of my miseries—by a feeling that it was ungenerous to press myself upon her, when she knew that she could not choose but obey Miss Havisham. My dread always was, that this knowledge on her part laid me under a heavy disadvantage with her pride, and made me the subject of a rebellious struggle in her bosom.\n\n“At any rate,” said I, “I have no warning given me just now, for you wrote to me to come to you, this time.”\n\n“That’s true,” said Estella, with a cold careless smile that always chilled me.\n\nAfter looking at the twilight without, for a little while, she went on to say:—\n\n“The time has come round when Miss Havisham wishes to have me for a day at Satis. You are to take me there, and bring me back, if you will. She would rather I did not travel alone, and objects to receiving my maid, for she has a sensitive horror of being talked of by such people. Can you take me?”\n\n“Can I take you, Estella!”\n\n“You can then? The day after to-morrow, if you please. You are to pay all charges out of my purse. You hear the condition of your going?”\n\n“And must obey,” said I.\n\nThis was all the preparation I received for that visit, or for others like it; Miss Havisham never wrote to me, nor had I ever so much as seen her handwriting. We went down on the next day but one, and we found her in the room where I had first beheld her, and it is needless to add that there was no change in Satis House.\n\nShe was even more dreadfully fond of Estella than she had been when I last saw them together; I repeat the word advisedly, for there was something positively dreadful in the energy of her looks and embraces. She hung upon Estella’s beauty, hung upon her words, hung upon her gestures, and sat mumbling her own trembling fingers while she looked at her, as though she were devouring the beautiful creature she had reared.\n\nFrom Estella she looked at me, with a searching glance that seemed to pry into my heart and probe its wounds. “How does she use you, Pip; how does she use you?” she asked me again, with her witch-like eagerness, even in Estella’s hearing. But, when we sat by her flickering fire at night, she was most weird; for then, keeping Estella’s hand drawn through her arm and clutched in her own hand, she extorted from her, by dint of referring back to what Estella had told her in her regular letters, the names and conditions of the men whom she had fascinated; and as Miss Havisham dwelt upon this roll, with the intensity of a mind mortally hurt and diseased, she sat with her other hand on her crutch stick, and her chin on that, and her wan bright eyes glaring at me, a very spectre.\n\nI saw in this, wretched though it made me, and bitter the sense of dependence and even of degradation that it awakened,—I saw in this that Estella was set to wreak Miss Havisham’s revenge on men, and that she was not to be given to me until she had gratified it for a term. I saw in this, a reason for her being beforehand assigned to me. Sending her out to attract and torment and do mischief, Miss Havisham sent her with the malicious assurance that she was beyond the reach of all admirers, and that all who staked upon that cast were secured to lose. I saw in this that I, too, was tormented by a perversion of ingenuity, even while the prize was reserved for me. I saw in this the reason for my being staved off so long and the reason for my late guardian’s declining to commit himself to the formal knowledge of such a scheme. In a word, I saw in this Miss Havisham as I had her then and there before my eyes, and always had had her before my eyes; and I saw in this, the distinct shadow of the darkened and unhealthy house in which her life was hidden from the sun.\n\nThe candles that lighted that room of hers were placed in sconces on the wall. They were high from the ground, and they burnt with the steady dulness of artificial light in air that is seldom renewed. As I looked round at them, and at the pale gloom they made, and at the stopped clock, and at the withered articles of bridal dress upon the table and the ground, and at her own awful figure with its ghostly reflection thrown large by the fire upon the ceiling and the wall, I saw in everything the construction that my mind had come to, repeated and thrown back to me. My thoughts passed into the great room across the landing where the table was spread, and I saw it written, as it were, in the falls of the cobwebs from the centre-piece, in the crawlings of the spiders on the cloth, in the tracks of the mice as they betook their little quickened hearts behind the panels, and in the gropings and pausings of the beetles on the floor.\n\nIt happened on the occasion of this visit that some sharp words arose between Estella and Miss Havisham. It was the first time I had ever seen them opposed.\n\nWe were seated by the fire, as just now described, and Miss Havisham still had Estella’s arm drawn through her own, and still clutched Estella’s hand in hers, when Estella gradually began to detach herself. She had shown a proud impatience more than once before, and had rather endured that fierce affection than accepted or returned it.\n\n“What!” said Miss Havisham, flashing her eyes upon her, “are you tired of me?”\n\n“Only a little tired of myself,” replied Estella, disengaging her arm, and moving to the great chimney-piece, where she stood looking down at the fire.\n\n“Speak the truth, you ingrate!” cried Miss Havisham, passionately striking her stick upon the floor; “you are tired of me.”\n\nEstella looked at her with perfect composure, and again looked down at the fire. Her graceful figure and her beautiful face expressed a self-possessed indifference to the wild heat of the other, that was almost cruel.\n\n“You stock and stone!” exclaimed Miss Havisham. “You cold, cold heart!”\n\n“What?” said Estella, preserving her attitude of indifference as she leaned against the great chimney-piece and only moving her eyes; “do you reproach me for being cold? You?”\n\n“Are you not?” was the fierce retort.\n\n“You should know,” said Estella. “I am what you have made me. Take all the praise, take all the blame; take all the success, take all the failure; in short, take me.”\n\n“O, look at her, look at her!” cried Miss Havisham, bitterly; “Look at her so hard and thankless, on the hearth where she was reared! Where I took her into this wretched breast when it was first bleeding from its stabs, and where I have lavished years of tenderness upon her!”\n\n“At least I was no party to the compact,” said Estella, “for if I could walk and speak, when it was made, it was as much as I could do. But what would you have? You have been very good to me, and I owe everything to you. What would you have?”\n\n“Love,” replied the other.\n\n“You have it.”\n\n“I have not,” said Miss Havisham.\n\n“Mother by adoption,” retorted Estella, never departing from the easy grace of her attitude, never raising her voice as the other did, never yielding either to anger or tenderness,—“mother by adoption, I have said that I owe everything to you. All I possess is freely yours. All that you have given me, is at your command to have again. Beyond that, I have nothing. And if you ask me to give you, what you never gave me, my gratitude and duty cannot do impossibilities.”\n\n“Did I never give her love!” cried Miss Havisham, turning wildly to me. “Did I never give her a burning love, inseparable from jealousy at all times, and from sharp pain, while she speaks thus to me! Let her call me mad, let her call me mad!”\n\n“Why should I call you mad,” returned Estella, “I, of all people? Does any one live, who knows what set purposes you have, half as well as I do? Does any one live, who knows what a steady memory you have, half as well as I do? I who have sat on this same hearth on the little stool that is even now beside you there, learning your lessons and looking up into your face, when your face was strange and frightened me!”\n\n“Soon forgotten!” moaned Miss Havisham. “Times soon forgotten!”\n\n“No, not forgotten,” retorted Estella,—“not forgotten, but treasured up in my memory. When have you found me false to your teaching? When have you found me unmindful of your lessons? When have you found me giving admission here,” she touched her bosom with her hand, “to anything that you excluded? Be just to me.”\n\n“So proud, so proud!” moaned Miss Havisham, pushing away her grey hair with both her hands.\n\n“Who taught me to be proud?” returned Estella. “Who praised me when I learnt my lesson?”\n\n“So hard, so hard!” moaned Miss Havisham, with her former action.\n\n“Who taught me to be hard?” returned Estella. “Who praised me when I learnt my lesson?”\n\n“But to be proud and hard to me!” Miss Havisham quite shrieked, as she stretched out her arms. “Estella, Estella, Estella, to be proud and hard to me!”\n\nEstella looked at her for a moment with a kind of calm wonder, but was not otherwise disturbed; when the moment was past, she looked down at the fire again.\n\n“I cannot think,” said Estella, raising her eyes after a silence “why you should be so unreasonable when I come to see you after a separation. I have never forgotten your wrongs and their causes. I have never been unfaithful to you or your schooling. I have never shown any weakness that I can charge myself with.”\n\n“Would it be weakness to return my love?” exclaimed Miss Havisham. “But yes, yes, she would call it so!”\n\n“I begin to think,” said Estella, in a musing way, after another moment of calm wonder, “that I almost understand how this comes about. If you had brought up your adopted daughter wholly in the dark confinement of these rooms, and had never let her know that there was such a thing as the daylight by which she had never once seen your face,—if you had done that, and then, for a purpose had wanted her to understand the daylight and know all about it, you would have been disappointed and angry?”\n\nMiss Havisham, with her head in her hands, sat making a low moaning, and swaying herself on her chair, but gave no answer.\n\n“Or,” said Estella,—“which is a nearer case,—if you had taught her, from the dawn of her intelligence, with your utmost energy and might, that there was such a thing as daylight, but that it was made to be her enemy and destroyer, and she must always turn against it, for it had blighted you and would else blight her;—if you had done this, and then, for a purpose, had wanted her to take naturally to the daylight and she could not do it, you would have been disappointed and angry?”\n\nMiss Havisham sat listening (or it seemed so, for I could not see her face), but still made no answer.\n\n“So,” said Estella, “I must be taken as I have been made. The success is not mine, the failure is not mine, but the two together make me.”\n\nMiss Havisham had settled down, I hardly knew how, upon the floor, among the faded bridal relics with which it was strewn. I took advantage of the moment—I had sought one from the first—to leave the room, after beseeching Estella’s attention to her, with a movement of my hand. When I left, Estella was yet standing by the great chimney-piece, just as she had stood throughout. Miss Havisham’s grey hair was all adrift upon the ground, among the other bridal wrecks, and was a miserable sight to see.\n\nIt was with a depressed heart that I walked in the starlight for an hour and more, about the courtyard, and about the brewery, and about the ruined garden. When I at last took courage to return to the room, I found Estella sitting at Miss Havisham’s knee, taking up some stitches in one of those old articles of dress that were dropping to pieces, and of which I have often been reminded since by the faded tatters of old banners that I have seen hanging up in cathedrals. Afterwards, Estella and I played at cards, as of yore,—only we were skilful now, and played French games,—and so the evening wore away, and I went to bed.\n\nI lay in that separate building across the courtyard. It was the first time I had ever lain down to rest in Satis House, and sleep refused to come near me. A thousand Miss Havishams haunted me. She was on this side of my pillow, on that, at the head of the bed, at the foot, behind the half-opened door of the dressing-room, in the dressing-room, in the room overhead, in the room beneath,—everywhere. At last, when the night was slow to creep on towards two o’clock, I felt that I absolutely could no longer bear the place as a place to lie down in, and that I must get up. I therefore got up and put on my clothes, and went out across the yard into the long stone passage, designing to gain the outer courtyard and walk there for the relief of my mind. But I was no sooner in the passage than I extinguished my candle; for I saw Miss Havisham going along it in a ghostly manner, making a low cry. I followed her at a distance, and saw her go up the staircase. She carried a bare candle in her hand, which she had probably taken from one of the sconces in her own room, and was a most unearthly object by its light. Standing at the bottom of the staircase, I felt the mildewed air of the feast-chamber, without seeing her open the door, and I heard her walking there, and so across into her own room, and so across again into that, never ceasing the low cry. After a time, I tried in the dark both to get out, and to go back, but I could do neither until some streaks of day strayed in and showed me where to lay my hands. During the whole interval, whenever I went to the bottom of the staircase, I heard her footstep, saw her light pass above, and heard her ceaseless low cry.\n\nBefore we left next day, there was no revival of the difference between her and Estella, nor was it ever revived on any similar occasion; and there were four similar occasions, to the best of my remembrance. Nor, did Miss Havisham’s manner towards Estella in anywise change, except that I believed it to have something like fear infused among its former characteristics.\n\nIt is impossible to turn this leaf of my life, without putting Bentley Drummle’s name upon it; or I would, very gladly.\n\nOn a certain occasion when the Finches were assembled in force, and when good feeling was being promoted in the usual manner by nobody’s agreeing with anybody else, the presiding Finch called the Grove to order, forasmuch as Mr. Drummle had not yet toasted a lady; which, according to the solemn constitution of the society, it was the brute’s turn to do that day. I thought I saw him leer in an ugly way at me while the decanters were going round, but as there was no love lost between us, that might easily be. What was my indignant surprise when he called upon the company to pledge him to “Estella!”\n\n“Estella who?” said I.\n\n“Never you mind,” retorted Drummle.\n\n“Estella of where?” said I. “You are bound to say of where.” Which he was, as a Finch.\n\n“Of Richmond, gentlemen,” said Drummle, putting me out of the question, “and a peerless beauty.”\n\nMuch he knew about peerless beauties, a mean, miserable idiot! I whispered Herbert.\n\n“I know that lady,” said Herbert, across the table, when the toast had been honoured.\n\n“Do you?” said Drummle.\n\n“And so do I,” I added, with a scarlet face.\n\n“Do you?” said Drummle. “O, Lord!”\n\nThis was the only retort—except glass or crockery—that the heavy creature was capable of making; but, I became as highly incensed by it as if it had been barbed with wit, and I immediately rose in my place and said that I could not but regard it as being like the honourable Finch’s impudence to come down to that Grove,—we always talked about coming down to that Grove, as a neat Parliamentary turn of expression,—down to that Grove, proposing a lady of whom he knew nothing. Mr. Drummle, upon this, starting up, demanded what I meant by that? Whereupon I made him the extreme reply that I believed he knew where I was to be found.\n\nWhether it was possible in a Christian country to get on without blood, after this, was a question on which the Finches were divided. The debate upon it grew so lively, indeed, that at least six more honourable members told six more, during the discussion, that they believed they knew where they were to be found. However, it was decided at last (the Grove being a Court of Honour) that if Mr. Drummle would bring never so slight a certificate from the lady, importing that he had the honour of her acquaintance, Mr. Pip must express his regret, as a gentleman and a Finch, for “having been betrayed into a warmth which.” Next day was appointed for the production (lest our honour should take cold from delay), and next day Drummle appeared with a polite little avowal in Estella’s hand, that she had had the honour of dancing with him several times. This left me no course but to regret that I had been “betrayed into a warmth which,” and on the whole to repudiate, as untenable, the idea that I was to be found anywhere. Drummle and I then sat snorting at one another for an hour, while the Grove engaged in indiscriminate contradiction, and finally the promotion of good feeling was declared to have gone ahead at an amazing rate.\n\nI tell this lightly, but it was no light thing to me. For, I cannot adequately express what pain it gave me to think that Estella should show any favour to a contemptible, clumsy, sulky booby, so very far below the average. To the present moment, I believe it to have been referable to some pure fire of generosity and disinterestedness in my love for her, that I could not endure the thought of her stooping to that hound. No doubt I should have been miserable whomsoever she had favoured; but a worthier object would have caused me a different kind and degree of distress.\n\nIt was easy for me to find out, and I did soon find out, that Drummle had begun to follow her closely, and that she allowed him to do it. A little while, and he was always in pursuit of her, and he and I crossed one another every day. He held on, in a dull persistent way, and Estella held him on; now with encouragement, now with discouragement, now almost flattering him, now openly despising him, now knowing him very well, now scarcely remembering who he was.\n\nThe Spider, as Mr. Jaggers had called him, was used to lying in wait, however, and had the patience of his tribe. Added to that, he had a blockhead confidence in his money and in his family greatness, which sometimes did him good service,—almost taking the place of concentration and determined purpose. So, the Spider, doggedly watching Estella, outwatched many brighter insects, and would often uncoil himself and drop at the right nick of time.\n\nAt a certain Assembly Ball at Richmond (there used to be Assembly Balls at most places then), where Estella had outshone all other beauties, this blundering Drummle so hung about her, and with so much toleration on her part, that I resolved to speak to her concerning him. I took the next opportunity; which was when she was waiting for Mrs. Blandley to take her home, and was sitting apart among some flowers, ready to go. I was with her, for I almost always accompanied them to and from such places.\n\n“Are you tired, Estella?”\n\n“Rather, Pip.”\n\n“You should be.”\n\n“Say rather, I should not be; for I have my letter to Satis House to write, before I go to sleep.”\n\n“Recounting to-night’s triumph?” said I. “Surely a very poor one, Estella.”\n\n“What do you mean? I didn’t know there had been any.”\n\n“Estella,” said I, “do look at that fellow in the corner yonder, who is looking over here at us.”\n\n“Why should I look at him?” returned Estella, with her eyes on me instead. “What is there in that fellow in the corner yonder,—to use your words,—that I need look at?”\n\n“Indeed, that is the very question I want to ask you,” said I. “For he has been hovering about you all night.”\n\n“Moths, and all sorts of ugly creatures,” replied Estella, with a glance towards him, “hover about a lighted candle. Can the candle help it?”\n\n“No,” I returned; “but cannot the Estella help it?”\n\n“Well!” said she, laughing, after a moment, “perhaps. Yes. Anything you like.”\n\n“But, Estella, do hear me speak. It makes me wretched that you should encourage a man so generally despised as Drummle. You know he is despised.”\n\n“Well?” said she.\n\n“You know he is as ungainly within as without. A deficient, ill-tempered, lowering, stupid fellow.”\n\n“Well?” said she.\n\n“You know he has nothing to recommend him but money and a ridiculous roll of addle-headed predecessors; now, don’t you?”\n\n“Well?” said she again; and each time she said it, she opened her lovely eyes the wider.\n\nTo overcome the difficulty of getting past that monosyllable, I took it from her, and said, repeating it with emphasis, “Well! Then, that is why it makes me wretched.”\n\nNow, if I could have believed that she favoured Drummle with any idea of making me—me—wretched, I should have been in better heart about it; but in that habitual way of hers, she put me so entirely out of the question, that I could believe nothing of the kind.\n\n“Pip,” said Estella, casting her glance over the room, “don’t be foolish about its effect on you. It may have its effect on others, and may be meant to have. It’s not worth discussing.”\n\n“Yes it is,” said I, “because I cannot bear that people should say, ‘she throws away her graces and attractions on a mere boor, the lowest in the crowd.’”\n\n“I can bear it,” said Estella.\n\n“Oh! don’t be so proud, Estella, and so inflexible.”\n\n“Calls me proud and inflexible in this breath!” said Estella, opening her hands. “And in his last breath reproached me for stooping to a boor!”\n\n“There is no doubt you do,” said I, something hurriedly, “for I have seen you give him looks and smiles this very night, such as you never give to—me.”\n\n“Do you want me then,” said Estella, turning suddenly with a fixed and serious, if not angry, look, “to deceive and entrap you?”\n\n“Do you deceive and entrap him, Estella?”\n\n“Yes, and many others,—all of them but you. Here is Mrs. Brandley. I’ll say no more.”\n\nAnd now that I have given the one chapter to the theme that so filled my heart, and so often made it ache and ache again, I pass on unhindered, to the event that had impended over me longer yet; the event that had begun to be prepared for, before I knew that the world held Estella, and in the days when her baby intelligence was receiving its first distortions from Miss Havisham’s wasting hands.\n\nIn the Eastern story, the heavy slab that was to fall on the bed of state in the flush of conquest was slowly wrought out of the quarry, the tunnel for the rope to hold it in its place was slowly carried through the leagues of rock, the slab was slowly raised and fitted in the roof, the rope was rove to it and slowly taken through the miles of hollow to the great iron ring. All being made ready with much labour, and the hour come, the sultan was aroused in the dead of the night, and the sharpened axe that was to sever the rope from the great iron ring was put into his hand, and he struck with it, and the rope parted and rushed away, and the ceiling fell. So, in my case; all the work, near and afar, that tended to the end, had been accomplished; and in an instant the blow was struck, and the roof of my stronghold dropped upon me.\n\n# Chapter XXXIX.\n\nI was three-and-twenty years of age. Not another word had I heard to enlighten me on the subject of my expectations, and my twenty-third birthday was a week gone. We had left Barnard’s Inn more than a year, and lived in the Temple. Our chambers were in Garden-court, down by the river.\n\nMr. Pocket and I had for some time parted company as to our original relations, though we continued on the best terms. Notwithstanding my inability to settle to anything,—which I hope arose out of the restless and incomplete tenure on which I held my means,—I had a taste for reading, and read regularly so many hours a day. That matter of Herbert’s was still progressing, and everything with me was as I have brought it down to the close of the last preceding chapter.\n\nBusiness had taken Herbert on a journey to Marseilles. I was alone, and had a dull sense of being alone. Dispirited and anxious, long hoping that to-morrow or next week would clear my way, and long disappointed, I sadly missed the cheerful face and ready response of my friend.\n\nIt was wretched weather; stormy and wet, stormy and wet; and mud, mud, mud, deep in all the streets. Day after day, a vast heavy veil had been driving over London from the East, and it drove still, as if in the East there were an eternity of cloud and wind. So furious had been the gusts, that high buildings in town had had the lead stripped off their roofs; and in the country, trees had been torn up, and sails of windmills carried away; and gloomy accounts had come in from the coast, of shipwreck and death. Violent blasts of rain had accompanied these rages of wind, and the day just closed as I sat down to read had been the worst of all.\n\nAlterations have been made in that part of the Temple since that time, and it has not now so lonely a character as it had then, nor is it so exposed to the river. We lived at the top of the last house, and the wind rushing up the river shook the house that night, like discharges of cannon, or breakings of a sea. When the rain came with it and dashed against the windows, I thought, raising my eyes to them as they rocked, that I might have fancied myself in a storm-beaten lighthouse. Occasionally, the smoke came rolling down the chimney as though it could not bear to go out into such a night; and when I set the doors open and looked down the staircase, the staircase lamps were blown out; and when I shaded my face with my hands and looked through the black windows (opening them ever so little was out of the question in the teeth of such wind and rain), I saw that the lamps in the court were blown out, and that the lamps on the bridges and the shore were shuddering, and that the coal-fires in barges on the river were being carried away before the wind like red-hot splashes in the rain.\n\nI read with my watch upon the table, purposing to close my book at eleven o’clock. As I shut it, Saint Paul’s, and all the many church-clocks in the City—some leading, some accompanying, some following—struck that hour. The sound was curiously flawed by the wind; and I was listening, and thinking how the wind assailed and tore it, when I heard a footstep on the stair.\n\nWhat nervous folly made me start, and awfully connect it with the footstep of my dead sister, matters not. It was past in a moment, and I listened again, and heard the footstep stumble in coming on. Remembering then, that the staircase-lights were blown out, I took up my reading-lamp and went out to the stair-head. Whoever was below had stopped on seeing my lamp, for all was quiet.\n\n“There is some one down there, is there not?” I called out, looking down.\n\n“Yes,” said a voice from the darkness beneath.\n\n“What floor do you want?”\n\n“The top. Mr. Pip.”\n\n“That is my name.—There is nothing the matter?”\n\n“Nothing the matter,” returned the voice. And the man came on.\n\nI stood with my lamp held out over the stair-rail, and he came slowly within its light. It was a shaded lamp, to shine upon a book, and its circle of light was very contracted; so that he was in it for a mere instant, and then out of it. In the instant, I had seen a face that was strange to me, looking up with an incomprehensible air of being touched and pleased by the sight of me.\n\nMoving the lamp as the man moved, I made out that he was substantially dressed, but roughly, like a voyager by sea. That he had long iron-grey hair. That his age was about sixty. That he was a muscular man, strong on his legs, and that he was browned and hardened by exposure to weather. As he ascended the last stair or two, and the light of my lamp included us both, I saw, with a stupid kind of amazement, that he was holding out both his hands to me.\n\n“Pray what is your business?” I asked him.\n\n“My business?” he repeated, pausing. “Ah! Yes. I will explain my business, by your leave.”\n\n“Do you wish to come in?”\n\n“Yes,” he replied; “I wish to come in, master.”\n\nI had asked him the question inhospitably enough, for I resented the sort of bright and gratified recognition that still shone in his face. I resented it, because it seemed to imply that he expected me to respond to it. But I took him into the room I had just left, and, having set the lamp on the table, asked him as civilly as I could to explain himself.\n\nHe looked about him with the strangest air,—an air of wondering pleasure, as if he had some part in the things he admired,—and he pulled off a rough outer coat, and his hat. Then, I saw that his head was furrowed and bald, and that the long iron-grey hair grew only on its sides. But, I saw nothing that in the least explained him. On the contrary, I saw him next moment, once more holding out both his hands to me.\n\n“What do you mean?” said I, half suspecting him to be mad.\n\nHe stopped in his looking at me, and slowly rubbed his right hand over his head. “It’s disapinting to a man,” he said, in a coarse broken voice, “arter having looked for’ard so distant, and come so fur; but you’re not to blame for that,—neither on us is to blame for that. I’ll speak in half a minute. Give me half a minute, please.”\n\nHe sat down on a chair that stood before the fire, and covered his forehead with his large brown veinous hands. I looked at him attentively then, and recoiled a little from him; but I did not know him.\n\n“There’s no one nigh,” said he, looking over his shoulder; “is there?”\n\n“Why do you, a stranger coming into my rooms at this time of the night, ask that question?” said I.\n\n“You’re a game one,” he returned, shaking his head at me with a deliberate affection, at once most unintelligible and most exasperating; “I’m glad you’ve grow’d up, a game one! But don’t catch hold of me. You’d be sorry arterwards to have done it.”\n\nI relinquished the intention he had detected, for I knew him! Even yet I could not recall a single feature, but I knew him! If the wind and the rain had driven away the intervening years, had scattered all the intervening objects, had swept us to the churchyard where we first stood face to face on such different levels, I could not have known my convict more distinctly than I knew him now as he sat in the chair before the fire. No need to take a file from his pocket and show it to me; no need to take the handkerchief from his neck and twist it round his head; no need to hug himself with both his arms, and take a shivering turn across the room, looking back at me for recognition. I knew him before he gave me one of those aids, though, a moment before, I had not been conscious of remotely suspecting his identity.\n\nHe came back to where I stood, and again held out both his hands. Not knowing what to do,—for, in my astonishment I had lost my self-possession,—I reluctantly gave him my hands. He grasped them heartily, raised them to his lips, kissed them, and still held them.\n\n“You acted noble, my boy,” said he. “Noble, Pip! And I have never forgot it!”\n\nAt a change in his manner as if he were even going to embrace me, I laid a hand upon his breast and put him away.\n\n“Stay!” said I. “Keep off! If you are grateful to me for what I did when I was a little child, I hope you have shown your gratitude by mending your way of life. If you have come here to thank me, it was not necessary. Still, however you have found me out, there must be something good in the feeling that has brought you here, and I will not repulse you; but surely you must understand that—I—”\n\nMy attention was so attracted by the singularity of his fixed look at me, that the words died away on my tongue.\n\n“You was a-saying,” he observed, when we had confronted one another in silence, “that surely I must understand. What, surely must I understand?”\n\n“That I cannot wish to renew that chance intercourse with you of long ago, under these different circumstances. I am glad to believe you have repented and recovered yourself. I am glad to tell you so. I am glad that, thinking I deserve to be thanked, you have come to thank me. But our ways are different ways, none the less. You are wet, and you look weary. Will you drink something before you go?”\n\nHe had replaced his neckerchief loosely, and had stood, keenly observant of me, biting a long end of it. “I think,” he answered, still with the end at his mouth and still observant of me, “that I will drink (I thank you) afore I go.”\n\nThere was a tray ready on a side-table. I brought it to the table near the fire, and asked him what he would have? He touched one of the bottles without looking at it or speaking, and I made him some hot rum and water. I tried to keep my hand steady while I did so, but his look at me as he leaned back in his chair with the long draggled end of his neckerchief between his teeth—evidently forgotten—made my hand very difficult to master. When at last I put the glass to him, I saw with amazement that his eyes were full of tears.\n\nUp to this time I had remained standing, not to disguise that I wished him gone. But I was softened by the softened aspect of the man, and felt a touch of reproach. “I hope,” said I, hurriedly putting something into a glass for myself, and drawing a chair to the table, “that you will not think I spoke harshly to you just now. I had no intention of doing it, and I am sorry for it if I did. I wish you well and happy!”\n\nAs I put my glass to my lips, he glanced with surprise at the end of his neckerchief, dropping from his mouth when he opened it, and stretched out his hand. I gave him mine, and then he drank, and drew his sleeve across his eyes and forehead.\n\n“How are you living?” I asked him.\n\n“I’ve been a sheep-farmer, stock-breeder, other trades besides, away in the new world,” said he; “many a thousand mile of stormy water off from this.”\n\n“I hope you have done well?”\n\n“I’ve done wonderfully well. There’s others went out alonger me as has done well too, but no man has done nigh as well as me. I’m famous for it.”\n\n“I am glad to hear it.”\n\n“I hope to hear you say so, my dear boy.”\n\nWithout stopping to try to understand those words or the tone in which they were spoken, I turned off to a point that had just come into my mind.\n\n“Have you ever seen a messenger you once sent to me,” I inquired, “since he undertook that trust?”\n\n“Never set eyes upon him. I warn’t likely to it.”\n\n“He came faithfully, and he brought me the two one-pound notes. I was a poor boy then, as you know, and to a poor boy they were a little fortune. But, like you, I have done well since, and you must let me pay them back. You can put them to some other poor boy’s use.” I took out my purse.\n\nHe watched me as I laid my purse upon the table and opened it, and he watched me as I separated two one-pound notes from its contents. They were clean and new, and I spread them out and handed them over to him. Still watching me, he laid them one upon the other, folded them long-wise, gave them a twist, set fire to them at the lamp, and dropped the ashes into the tray.\n\n“May I make so bold,” he said then, with a smile that was like a frown, and with a frown that was like a smile, “as ask you how you have done well, since you and me was out on them lone shivering marshes?”\n\n“How?”\n“Ah!”\n\nHe emptied his glass, got up, and stood at the side of the fire, with his heavy brown hand on the mantel-shelf. He put a foot up to the bars, to dry and warm it, and the wet boot began to steam; but, he neither looked at it, nor at the fire, but steadily looked at me. It was only now that I began to tremble.\n\nWhen my lips had parted, and had shaped some words that were without sound, I forced myself to tell him (though I could not do it distinctly), that I had been chosen to succeed to some property.\n\n“Might a mere warmint ask what property?” said he.\n\nI faltered, “I don’t know.”\n\n“Might a mere warmint ask whose property?” said he.\n\nI faltered again, “I don’t know.”\n\n“Could I make a guess, I wonder,” said the Convict, “at your income since you come of age! As to the first figure now. Five?”\n\nWith my heart beating like a heavy hammer of disordered action, I rose out of my chair, and stood with my hand upon the back of it, looking wildly at him.\n\n“Concerning a guardian,” he went on. “There ought to have been some guardian, or such-like, whiles you was a minor. Some lawyer, maybe. As to the first letter of that lawyer’s name now. Would it be J?”\n\nAll the truth of my position came flashing on me; and its disappointments, dangers, disgraces, consequences of all kinds, rushed in in such a multitude that I was borne down by them and had to struggle for every breath I drew.\n\n“Put it,” he resumed, “as the employer of that lawyer whose name begun with a J, and might be Jaggers,—put it as he had come over sea to Portsmouth, and had landed there, and had wanted to come on to you. ‘However, you have found me out,’ you says just now. Well! However, did I find you out? Why, I wrote from Portsmouth to a person in London, for particulars of your address. That person’s name? Why, Wemmick.”\n\nI could not have spoken one word, though it had been to save my life. I stood, with a hand on the chair-back and a hand on my breast, where I seemed to be suffocating,—I stood so, looking wildly at him, until I grasped at the chair, when the room began to surge and turn. He caught me, drew me to the sofa, put me up against the cushions, and bent on one knee before me, bringing the face that I now well remembered, and that I shuddered at, very near to mine.\n\n“Yes, Pip, dear boy, I’ve made a gentleman on you! It’s me wot has done it! I swore that time, sure as ever I earned a guinea, that guinea should go to you. I swore arterwards, sure as ever I spec’lated and got rich, you should get rich. I lived rough, that you should live smooth; I worked hard, that you should be above work. What odds, dear boy? Do I tell it, fur you to feel a obligation? Not a bit. I tell it, fur you to know as that there hunted dunghill dog wot you kep life in, got his head so high that he could make a gentleman,—and, Pip, you’re him!”\n\nThe abhorrence in which I held the man, the dread I had of him, the repugnance with which I shrank from him, could not have been exceeded if he had been some terrible beast.\n\n“Look’ee here, Pip. I’m your second father. You’re my son,—more to me nor any son. I’ve put away money, only for you to spend. When I was a hired-out shepherd in a solitary hut, not seeing no faces but faces of sheep till I half forgot wot men’s and women’s faces wos like, I see yourn. I drops my knife many a time in that hut when I was a-eating my dinner or my supper, and I says, ‘Here’s the boy again, a looking at me whiles I eats and drinks!’ I see you there a many times, as plain as ever I see you on them misty marshes. ‘Lord strike me dead!’ I says each time,—and I goes out in the air to say it under the open heavens,—‘but wot, if I gets liberty and money, I’ll make that boy a gentleman!’ And I done it. Why, look at you, dear boy! Look at these here lodgings of yourn, fit for a lord! A lord? Ah! You shall show money with lords for wagers, and beat ’em!”\n\nIn his heat and triumph, and in his knowledge that I had been nearly fainting, he did not remark on my reception of all this. It was the one grain of relief I had.\n\n“Look’ee here!” he went on, taking my watch out of my pocket, and turning towards him a ring on my finger, while I recoiled from his touch as if he had been a snake, “a gold ’un and a beauty: that’s a gentleman’s, I hope! A diamond all set round with rubies; that’s a gentleman’s, I hope! Look at your linen; fine and beautiful! Look at your clothes; better ain’t to be got! And your books too,” turning his eyes round the room, “mounting up, on their shelves, by hundreds! And you read ’em; don’t you? I see you’d been a reading of ’em when I come in. Ha, ha, ha! You shall read ’em to me, dear boy! And if they’re in foreign languages wot I don’t understand, I shall be just as proud as if I did.”\n\nAgain he took both my hands and put them to his lips, while my blood ran cold within me.\n\n“Don’t you mind talking, Pip,” said he, after again drawing his sleeve over his eyes and forehead, as the click came in his throat which I well remembered,—and he was all the more horrible to me that he was so much in earnest; “you can’t do better nor keep quiet, dear boy. You ain’t looked slowly forward to this as I have; you wosn’t prepared for this as I wos. But didn’t you never think it might be me?”\n\n“O no, no, no,” I returned, “Never, never!”\n\n“Well, you see it wos me, and single-handed. Never a soul in it but my own self and Mr. Jaggers.”\n\n“Was there no one else?” I asked.\n\n“No,” said he, with a glance of surprise: “who else should there be? And, dear boy, how good looking you have growed! There’s bright eyes somewheres—eh? Isn’t there bright eyes somewheres, wot you love the thoughts on?”\n\nO Estella, Estella!\n\n“They shall be yourn, dear boy, if money can buy ’em. Not that a gentleman like you, so well set up as you, can’t win ’em off of his own game; but money shall back you! Let me finish wot I was a telling you, dear boy. From that there hut and that there hiring-out, I got money left me by my master (which died, and had been the same as me), and got my liberty and went for myself. In every single thing I went for, I went for you. ‘Lord strike a blight upon it,’ I says, wotever it was I went for, ‘if it ain’t for him!’ It all prospered wonderful. As I giv’ you to understand just now, I’m famous for it. It was the money left me, and the gains of the first few year wot I sent home to Mr. Jaggers—all for you—when he first come arter you, agreeable to my letter.”\n\nO that he had never come! That he had left me at the forge,—far from contented, yet, by comparison happy!\n\n“And then, dear boy, it was a recompense to me, look’ee here, to know in secret that I was making a gentleman. The blood horses of them colonists might fling up the dust over me as I was walking; what do I say? I says to myself, ‘I’m making a better gentleman nor ever you’ll be!’ When one of ’em says to another, ‘He was a convict, a few year ago, and is a ignorant common fellow now, for all he’s lucky,’ what do I say? I says to myself, ‘If I ain’t a gentleman, nor yet ain’t got no learning, I’m the owner of such. All on you owns stock and land; which on you owns a brought-up London gentleman?’ This way I kep myself a-going. And this way I held steady afore my mind that I would for certain come one day and see my boy, and make myself known to him, on his own ground.”\n\nHe laid his hand on my shoulder. I shuddered at the thought that for anything I knew, his hand might be stained with blood.\n\n“It warn’t easy, Pip, for me to leave them parts, nor yet it warn’t safe. But I held to it, and the harder it was, the stronger I held, for I was determined, and my mind firm made up. At last I done it. Dear boy, I done it!”\n\nI tried to collect my thoughts, but I was stunned. Throughout, I had seemed to myself to attend more to the wind and the rain than to him; even now, I could not separate his voice from those voices, though those were loud and his was silent.\n\n“Where will you put me?” he asked, presently. “I must be put somewheres, dear boy.”\n\n“To sleep?” said I.\n\n“Yes. And to sleep long and sound,” he answered; “for I’ve been sea-tossed and sea-washed, months and months.”\n\n“My friend and companion,” said I, rising from the sofa, “is absent; you must have his room.”\n\n“He won’t come back to-morrow; will he?”\n\n“No,” said I, answering almost mechanically, in spite of my utmost efforts; “not to-morrow.”\n\n“Because, look’ee here, dear boy,” he said, dropping his voice, and laying a long finger on my breast in an impressive manner, “caution is necessary.”\n\n“How do you mean? Caution?”\n\n“By G——, it’s Death!”\n“What’s death?”\n\n“I was sent for life. It’s death to come back. There’s been overmuch coming back of late years, and I should of a certainty be hanged if took.”\n\nNothing was needed but this; the wretched man, after loading wretched me with his gold and silver chains for years, had risked his life to come to me, and I held it there in my keeping! If I had loved him instead of abhorring him; if I had been attracted to him by the strongest admiration and affection, instead of shrinking from him with the strongest repugnance; it could have been no worse. On the contrary, it would have been better, for his preservation would then have naturally and tenderly addressed my heart.\n\nMy first care was to close the shutters, so that no light might be seen from without, and then to close and make fast the doors. While I did so, he stood at the table drinking rum and eating biscuit; and when I saw him thus engaged, I saw my convict on the marshes at his meal again. It almost seemed to me as if he must stoop down presently, to file at his leg.\n\nWhen I had gone into Herbert’s room, and had shut off any other communication between it and the staircase than through the room in which our conversation had been held, I asked him if he would go to bed? He said yes, but asked me for some of my “gentleman’s linen” to put on in the morning. I brought it out, and laid it ready for him, and my blood again ran cold when he again took me by both hands to give me good-night.\n\nI got away from him, without knowing how I did it, and mended the fire in the room where we had been together, and sat down by it, afraid to go to bed. For an hour or more, I remained too stunned to think; and it was not until I began to think, that I began fully to know how wrecked I was, and how the ship in which I had sailed was gone to pieces.\n\nMiss Havisham’s intentions towards me, all a mere dream; Estella not designed for me; I only suffered in Satis House as a convenience, a sting for the greedy relations, a model with a mechanical heart to practise on when no other practice was at hand; those were the first smarts I had. But, sharpest and deepest pain of all,—it was for the convict, guilty of I knew not what crimes, and liable to be taken out of those rooms where I sat thinking, and hanged at the Old Bailey door, that I had deserted Joe.\n\nI would not have gone back to Joe now, I would not have gone back to Biddy now, for any consideration; simply, I suppose, because my sense of my own worthless conduct to them was greater than every consideration. No wisdom on earth could have given me the comfort that I should have derived from their simplicity and fidelity; but I could never, never, undo what I had done.\n\nIn every rage of wind and rush of rain, I heard pursuers. Twice, I could have sworn there was a knocking and whispering at the outer door. With these fears upon me, I began either to imagine or recall that I had had mysterious warnings of this man’s approach. That, for weeks gone by, I had passed faces in the streets which I had thought like his. That these likenesses had grown more numerous, as he, coming over the sea, had drawn nearer. That his wicked spirit had somehow sent these messengers to mine, and that now on this stormy night he was as good as his word, and with me.\n\nCrowding up with these reflections came the reflection that I had seen him with my childish eyes to be a desperately violent man; that I had heard that other convict reiterate that he had tried to murder him; that I had seen him down in the ditch tearing and fighting like a wild beast. Out of such remembrances I brought into the light of the fire a half-formed terror that it might not be safe to be shut up there with him in the dead of the wild solitary night. This dilated until it filled the room, and impelled me to take a candle and go in and look at my dreadful burden.\n\nHe had rolled a handkerchief round his head, and his face was set and lowering in his sleep. But he was asleep, and quietly too, though he had a pistol lying on the pillow. Assured of this, I softly removed the key to the outside of his door, and turned it on him before I again sat down by the fire. Gradually I slipped from the chair and lay on the floor. When I awoke without having parted in my sleep with the perception of my wretchedness, the clocks of the Eastward churches were striking five, the candles were wasted out, the fire was dead, and the wind and rain intensified the thick black darkness.\n\n# Chapter XL.\n\nIt was fortunate for me that I had to take precautions to ensure (so far as I could) the safety of my dreaded visitor; for, this thought pressing on me when I awoke, held other thoughts in a confused concourse at a distance.\n\nThe impossibility of keeping him concealed in the chambers was self-evident. It could not be done, and the attempt to do it would inevitably engender suspicion. True, I had no Avenger in my service now, but I was looked after by an inflammatory old female, assisted by an animated rag-bag whom she called her niece, and to keep a room secret from them would be to invite curiosity and exaggeration. They both had weak eyes, which I had long attributed to their chronically looking in at keyholes, and they were always at hand when not wanted; indeed that was their only reliable quality besides larceny. Not to get up a mystery with these people, I resolved to announce in the morning that my uncle had unexpectedly come from the country.\n\nThis course I decided on while I was yet groping about in the darkness for the means of getting a light. Not stumbling on the means after all, I was fain to go out to the adjacent Lodge and get the watchman there to come with his lantern. Now, in groping my way down the black staircase I fell over something, and that something was a man crouching in a corner.\n\nAs the man made no answer when I asked him what he did there, but eluded my touch in silence, I ran to the Lodge and urged the watchman to come quickly; telling him of the incident on the way back. The wind being as fierce as ever, we did not care to endanger the light in the lantern by rekindling the extinguished lamps on the staircase, but we examined the staircase from the bottom to the top and found no one there. It then occurred to me as possible that the man might have slipped into my rooms; so, lighting my candle at the watchman’s, and leaving him standing at the door, I examined them carefully, including the room in which my dreaded guest lay asleep. All was quiet, and assuredly no other man was in those chambers.\n\nIt troubled me that there should have been a lurker on the stairs, on that night of all nights in the year, and I asked the watchman, on the chance of eliciting some hopeful explanation as I handed him a dram at the door, whether he had admitted at his gate any gentleman who had perceptibly been dining out? Yes, he said; at different times of the night, three. One lived in Fountain Court, and the other two lived in the Lane, and he had seen them all go home. Again, the only other man who dwelt in the house of which my chambers formed a part had been in the country for some weeks, and he certainly had not returned in the night, because we had seen his door with his seal on it as we came upstairs.\n\n“The night being so bad, sir,” said the watchman, as he gave me back my glass, “uncommon few have come in at my gate. Besides them three gentlemen that I have named, I don’t call to mind another since about eleven o’clock, when a stranger asked for you.”\n\n“My uncle,” I muttered. “Yes.”\n\n“You saw him, sir?”\n\n“Yes. Oh yes.”\n\n“Likewise the person with him?”\n\n“Person with him!” I repeated.\n“I judged the person to be with him,” returned the watchman. “The person stopped, when he stopped to make inquiry of me, and the person took this way when he took this way.”\n\n“What sort of person?”\n\nThe watchman had not particularly noticed; he should say a working person; to the best of his belief, he had a dust-coloured kind of clothes on, under a dark coat. The watchman made more light of the matter than I did, and naturally; not having my reason for attaching weight to it.\n\nWhen I had got rid of him, which I thought it well to do without prolonging explanations, my mind was much troubled by these two circumstances taken together. Whereas they were easy of innocent solution apart,—as, for instance, some diner out or diner at home, who had not gone near this watchman’s gate, might have strayed to my staircase and dropped asleep there,—and my nameless visitor might have brought some one with him to show him the way,—still, joined, they had an ugly look to one as prone to distrust and fear as the changes of a few hours had made me.\n\nI lighted my fire, which burnt with a raw pale flare at that time of the morning, and fell into a doze before it. I seemed to have been dozing a whole night when the clocks struck six. As there was full an hour and a half between me and daylight, I dozed again; now, waking up uneasily, with prolix conversations about nothing, in my ears; now, making thunder of the wind in the chimney; at length, falling off into a profound sleep from which the daylight woke me with a start.\n\nAll this time I had never been able to consider my own situation, nor could I do so yet. I had not the power to attend to it. I was greatly dejected and distressed, but in an incoherent wholesale sort of way. As to forming any plan for the future, I could as soon have formed an elephant. When I opened the shutters and looked out at the wet wild morning, all of a leaden hue; when I walked from room to room; when I sat down again shivering, before the fire, waiting for my laundress to appear; I thought how miserable I was, but hardly knew why, or how long I had been so, or on what day of the week I made the reflection, or even who I was that made it.\n\nAt last, the old woman and the niece came in,—the latter with a head not easily distinguishable from her dusty broom,—and testified surprise at sight of me and the fire. To whom I imparted how my uncle had come in the night and was then asleep, and how the breakfast preparations were to be modified accordingly. Then I washed and dressed while they knocked the furniture about and made a dust; and so, in a sort of dream or sleep-waking, I found myself sitting by the fire again, waiting for—Him—to come to breakfast.\n\nBy and by, his door opened and he came out. I could not bring myself to bear the sight of him, and I thought he had a worse look by daylight.\n\n“I do not even know,” said I, speaking low as he took his seat at the table, “by what name to call you. I have given out that you are my uncle.”\n\n“That’s it, dear boy! Call me uncle.”\n\n“You assumed some name, I suppose, on board ship?”\n\n“Yes, dear boy. I took the name of Provis.”\n\n“Do you mean to keep that name?”\n\n“Why, yes, dear boy, it’s as good as another,—unless you’d like another.”\n\n“What is your real name?” I asked him in a whisper.\n\n“Magwitch,” he answered, in the same tone; “chrisen’d Abel.”\n\n“What were you brought up to be?”\n\n“A warmint, dear boy.”\n\nHe answered quite seriously, and used the word as if it denoted some profession.\n\n“When you came into the Temple last night—” said I, pausing to wonder whether that could really have been last night, which seemed so long ago.\n\n“Yes, dear boy?”\n\n“When you came in at the gate and asked the watchman the way here, had you any one with you?”\n\n“With me? No, dear boy.”\n\n“But there was some one there?”\n\n“I didn’t take particular notice,” he said, dubiously, “not knowing the ways of the place. But I think there was a person, too, come in alonger me.”\n\n“Are you known in London?”\n\n“I hope not!” said he, giving his neck a jerk with his forefinger that made me turn hot and sick.\n\n“Were you known in London, once?”\n\n“Not over and above, dear boy. I was in the provinces mostly.”\n\n“Were you—tried—in London?”\n\n“Which time?” said he, with a sharp look.\n\n“The last time.”\n\nHe nodded. “First knowed Mr. Jaggers that way. Jaggers was for me.”\n\nIt was on my lips to ask him what he was tried for, but he took up a knife, gave it a flourish, and with the words, “And what I done is worked out and paid for!” fell to at his breakfast.\n\nHe ate in a ravenous way that was very disagreeable, and all his actions were uncouth, noisy, and greedy. Some of his teeth had failed him since I saw him eat on the marshes, and as he turned his food in his mouth, and turned his head sideways to bring his strongest fangs to bear upon it, he looked terribly like a hungry old dog. If I had begun with any appetite, he would have taken it away, and I should have sat much as I did,—repelled from him by an insurmountable aversion, and gloomily looking at the cloth.\n\n“I’m a heavy grubber, dear boy,” he said, as a polite kind of apology when he made an end of his meal, “but I always was. If it had been in my constitution to be a lighter grubber, I might ha’ got into lighter trouble. Similarly, I must have my smoke. When I was first hired out as shepherd t’other side the world, it’s my belief I should ha’ turned into a molloncolly-mad sheep myself, if I hadn’t a had my smoke.”\n\nAs he said so, he got up from table, and putting his hand into the breast of the pea-coat he wore, brought out a short black pipe, and a handful of loose tobacco of the kind that is called Negro-head. Having filled his pipe, he put the surplus tobacco back again, as if his pocket were a drawer. Then, he took a live coal from the fire with the tongs, and lighted his pipe at it, and then turned round on the hearth-rug with his back to the fire, and went through his favourite action of holding out both his hands for mine.\n\n“And this,” said he, dandling my hands up and down in his, as he puffed at his pipe,—“and this is the gentleman what I made! The real genuine One! It does me good fur to look at you, Pip. All I stip’late, is, to stand by and look at you, dear boy!”\n\nI released my hands as soon as I could, and found that I was beginning slowly to settle down to the contemplation of my condition. What I was chained to, and how heavily, became intelligible to me, as I heard his hoarse voice, and sat looking up at his furrowed bald head with its iron grey hair at the sides.\n\n“I mustn’t see my gentleman a footing it in the mire of the streets; there mustn’t be no mud on his boots. My gentleman must have horses, Pip! Horses to ride, and horses to drive, and horses for his servant to ride and drive as well. Shall colonists have their horses (and blood ’uns, if you please, good Lord!) and not my London gentleman? No, no. We’ll show ’em another pair of shoes than that, Pip; won’t us?”\n\nHe took out of his pocket a great thick pocket-book, bursting with papers, and tossed it on the table.\n\n“There’s something worth spending in that there book, dear boy. It’s yourn. All I’ve got ain’t mine; it’s yourn. Don’t you be afeerd on it. There’s more where that come from. I’ve come to the old country fur to see my gentleman spend his money like a gentleman. That’ll be my pleasure. My pleasure ’ull be fur to see him do it. And blast you all!” he wound up, looking round the room and snapping his fingers once with a loud snap, “blast you every one, from the judge in his wig, to the colonist a stirring up the dust, I’ll show a better gentleman than the whole kit on you put together!”\n\n“Stop!” said I, almost in a frenzy of fear and dislike, “I want to speak to you. I want to know what is to be done. I want to know how you are to be kept out of danger, how long you are going to stay, what projects you have.”\n\n“Look’ee here, Pip,” said he, laying his hand on my arm in a suddenly altered and subdued manner; “first of all, look’ee here. I forgot myself half a minute ago. What I said was low; that’s what it was; low. Look’ee here, Pip. Look over it. I ain’t a-going to be low.”\n\n“First,” I resumed, half groaning, “what precautions can be taken against your being recognised and seized?”\n\n“No, dear boy,” he said, in the same tone as before, “that don’t go first. Lowness goes first. I ain’t took so many year to make a gentleman, not without knowing what’s due to him. Look’ee here, Pip. I was low; that’s what I was; low. Look over it, dear boy.”\n\nSome sense of the grimly-ludicrous moved me to a fretful laugh, as I replied, “I have looked over it. In Heaven’s name, don’t harp upon it!”\n\n“Yes, but look’ee here,” he persisted. “Dear boy, I ain’t come so fur, not fur to be low. Now, go on, dear boy. You was a saying—”\n\n“How are you to be guarded from the danger you have incurred?”\n\n“Well, dear boy, the danger ain’t so great. Without I was informed agen, the danger ain’t so much to signify. There’s Jaggers, and there’s Wemmick, and there’s you. Who else is there to inform?”\n\n“Is there no chance person who might identify you in the street?” said I.\n\n“Well,” he returned, “there ain’t many. Nor yet I don’t intend to advertise myself in the newspapers by the name of A.M. come back from Botany Bay; and years have rolled away, and who’s to gain by it? Still, look’ee here, Pip. If the danger had been fifty times as great, I should ha’ come to see you, mind you, just the same.”\n\n“And how long do you remain?”\n\n“How long?” said he, taking his black pipe from his mouth, and dropping his jaw as he stared at me. “I’m not a-going back. I’ve come for good.”\n\n“Where are you to live?” said I. “What is to be done with you? Where will you be safe?”\n\n“Dear boy,” he returned, “there’s disguising wigs can be bought for money, and there’s hair powder, and spectacles, and black clothes,—shorts and what not. Others has done it safe afore, and what others has done afore, others can do agen. As to the where and how of living, dear boy, give me your own opinions on it.”\n\n“You take it smoothly now,” said I, “but you were very serious last night, when you swore it was Death.”\n\n“And so I swear it is Death,” said he, putting his pipe back in his mouth, “and Death by the rope, in the open street not fur from this, and it’s serious that you should fully understand it to be so. What then, when that’s once done? Here I am. To go back now ’ud be as bad as to stand ground—worse. Besides, Pip, I’m here, because I’ve meant it by you, years and years. As to what I dare, I’m a old bird now, as has dared all manner of traps since first he was fledged, and I’m not afeerd to perch upon a scarecrow. If there’s Death hid inside of it, there is, and let him come out, and I’ll face him, and then I’ll believe in him and not afore. And now let me have a look at my gentleman agen.”\n\nOnce more, he took me by both hands and surveyed me with an air of admiring proprietorship: smoking with great complacency all the while.\n\nIt appeared to me that I could do no better than secure him some quiet lodging hard by, of which he might take possession when Herbert returned: whom I expected in two or three days. That the secret must be confided to Herbert as a matter of unavoidable necessity, even if I could have put the immense relief I should derive from sharing it with him out of the question, was plain to me. But it was by no means so plain to Mr. Provis (I resolved to call him by that name), who reserved his consent to Herbert’s participation until he should have seen him and formed a favourable judgment of his physiognomy. “And even then, dear boy,” said he, pulling a greasy little clasped black Testament out of his pocket, “we’ll have him on his oath.”\n\nTo state that my terrible patron carried this little black book about the world solely to swear people on in cases of emergency, would be to state what I never quite established; but this I can say, that I never knew him put it to any other use. The book itself had the appearance of having been stolen from some court of justice, and perhaps his knowledge of its antecedents, combined with his own experience in that wise, gave him a reliance on its powers as a sort of legal spell or charm. On this first occasion of his producing it, I recalled how he had made me swear fidelity in the churchyard long ago, and how he had described himself last night as always swearing to his resolutions in his solitude.\n\nAs he was at present dressed in a seafaring slop suit, in which he looked as if he had some parrots and cigars to dispose of, I next discussed with him what dress he should wear. He cherished an extraordinary belief in the virtues of “shorts” as a disguise, and had in his own mind sketched a dress for himself that would have made him something between a dean and a dentist. It was with considerable difficulty that I won him over to the assumption of a dress more like a prosperous farmer’s; and we arranged that he should cut his hair close, and wear a little powder. Lastly, as he had not yet been seen by the laundress or her niece, he was to keep himself out of their view until his change of dress was made.\n\nIt would seem a simple matter to decide on these precautions; but in my dazed, not to say distracted, state, it took so long, that I did not get out to further them until two or three in the afternoon. He was to remain shut up in the chambers while I was gone, and was on no account to open the door.\n\nThere being to my knowledge a respectable lodging-house in Essex Street, the back of which looked into the Temple, and was almost within hail of my windows, I first of all repaired to that house, and was so fortunate as to secure the second floor for my uncle, Mr. Provis. I then went from shop to shop, making such purchases as were necessary to the change in his appearance. This business transacted, I turned my face, on my own account, to Little Britain. Mr. Jaggers was at his desk, but, seeing me enter, got up immediately and stood before his fire.\n\n“Now, Pip,” said he, “be careful.”\n\n“I will, sir,” I returned. For, coming along I had thought well of what I was going to say.\n\n“Don’t commit yourself,” said Mr. Jaggers, “and don’t commit any one. You understand—any one. Don’t tell me anything: I don’t want to know anything; I am not curious.”\n\nOf course I saw that he knew the man was come.\n\n“I merely want, Mr. Jaggers,” said I, “to assure myself that what I have been told is true. I have no hope of its being untrue, but at least I may verify it.”\n\nMr. Jaggers nodded. “But did you say ‘told’ or ‘informed’?” he asked me, with his head on one side, and not looking at me, but looking in a listening way at the floor. “Told would seem to imply verbal communication. You can’t have verbal communication with a man in New South Wales, you know.”\n\n“I will say, informed, Mr. Jaggers.”\n\n“Good.”\n\n“I have been informed by a person named Abel Magwitch, that he is the benefactor so long unknown to me.”\n\n“That is the man,” said Mr. Jaggers, “in New South Wales.”\n\n“And only he?” said I.\n“And only he,” said Mr. Jaggers.\n\n“I am not so unreasonable, sir, as to think you at all responsible for my mistakes and wrong conclusions; but I always supposed it was Miss Havisham.”\n\n“As you say, Pip,” returned Mr. Jaggers, turning his eyes upon me coolly, and taking a bite at his forefinger, “I am not at all responsible for that.”\n\n“And yet it looked so like it, sir,” I pleaded with a downcast heart.\n\n“Not a particle of evidence, Pip,” said Mr. Jaggers, shaking his head and gathering up his skirts. “Take nothing on its looks; take everything on evidence. There’s no better rule.”\n\n“I have no more to say,” said I, with a sigh, after standing silent for a little while. “I have verified my information, and there’s an end.”\n\n“And Magwitch—in New South Wales—having at last disclosed himself,” said Mr. Jaggers, “you will comprehend, Pip, how rigidly throughout my communication with you, I have always adhered to the strict line of fact. There has never been the least departure from the strict line of fact. You are quite aware of that?”\n\n“Quite, sir.”\n“I communicated to Magwitch—in New South Wales—when he first wrote to me—from New South Wales—the caution that he must not expect me ever to deviate from the strict line of fact. I also communicated to him another caution. He appeared to me to have obscurely hinted in his letter at some distant idea he had of seeing you in England here. I cautioned him that I must hear no more of that; that he was not at all likely to obtain a pardon; that he was expatriated for the term of his natural life; and that his presenting himself in this country would be an act of felony, rendering him liable to the extreme penalty of the law. I gave Magwitch that caution,” said Mr. Jaggers, looking hard at me; “I wrote it to New South Wales. He guided himself by it, no doubt.”\n\n“No doubt,” said I.\n\n“I have been informed by Wemmick,” pursued Mr. Jaggers, still looking hard at me, “that he has received a letter, under date Portsmouth, from a colonist of the name of Purvis, or—”\n\n“Or Provis,” I suggested.\n\n“Or Provis—thank you, Pip. Perhaps it is Provis? Perhaps you know it’s Provis?”\n\n“Yes,” said I.\n\n“You know it’s Provis. A letter, under date Portsmouth, from a colonist of the name of Provis, asking for the particulars of your address, on behalf of Magwitch. Wemmick sent him the particulars, I understand, by return of post. Probably it is through Provis that you have received the explanation of Magwitch—in New South Wales?”\n\n“It came through Provis,” I replied.\n\n“Good day, Pip,” said Mr. Jaggers, offering his hand; “glad to have seen you. In writing by post to Magwitch—in New South Wales—or in communicating with him through Provis, have the goodness to mention that the particulars and vouchers of our long account shall be sent to you, together with the balance; for there is still a balance remaining. Good-day, Pip!”\n\nWe shook hands, and he looked hard at me as long as he could see me. I turned at the door, and he was still looking hard at me, while the two vile casts on the shelf seemed to be trying to get their eyelids open, and to force out of their swollen throats, “O, what a man he is!”\n\nWemmick was out, and though he had been at his desk he could have done nothing for me. I went straight back to the Temple, where I found the terrible Provis drinking rum and water and smoking negro-head, in safety.\n\nNext day the clothes I had ordered all came home, and he put them on. Whatever he put on, became him less (it dismally seemed to me) than what he had worn before. To my thinking, there was something in him that made it hopeless to attempt to disguise him. The more I dressed him and the better I dressed him, the more he looked like the slouching fugitive on the marshes. This effect on my anxious fancy was partly referable, no doubt, to his old face and manner growing more familiar to me; but I believe too that he dragged one of his legs as if there were still a weight of iron on it, and that from head to foot there was Convict in the very grain of the man.\n\nThe influences of his solitary hut-life were upon him besides, and gave him a savage air that no dress could tame; added to these were the influences of his subsequent branded life among men, and, crowning all, his consciousness that he was dodging and hiding now. In all his ways of sitting and standing, and eating and drinking,—of brooding about in a high-shouldered reluctant style,—of taking out his great horn-handled jackknife and wiping it on his legs and cutting his food,—of lifting light glasses and cups to his lips, as if they were clumsy pannikins,—of chopping a wedge off his bread, and soaking up with it the last fragments of gravy round and round his plate, as if to make the most of an allowance, and then drying his finger-ends on it, and then swallowing it,—in these ways and a thousand other small nameless instances arising every minute in the day, there was Prisoner, Felon, Bondsman, plain as plain could be.\n\nIt had been his own idea to wear that touch of powder, and I had conceded the powder after overcoming the shorts. But I can compare the effect of it, when on, to nothing but the probable effect of rouge upon the dead; so awful was the manner in which everything in him that it was most desirable to repress, started through that thin layer of pretence, and seemed to come blazing out at the crown of his head. It was abandoned as soon as tried, and he wore his grizzled hair cut short.\n\nWords cannot tell what a sense I had, at the same time, of the dreadful mystery that he was to me. When he fell asleep of an evening, with his knotted hands clenching the sides of the easy-chair, and his bald head tattooed with deep wrinkles falling forward on his breast, I would sit and look at him, wondering what he had done, and loading him with all the crimes in the Calendar, until the impulse was powerful on me to start up and fly from him. Every hour so increased my abhorrence of him, that I even think I might have yielded to this impulse in the first agonies of being so haunted, notwithstanding all he had done for me and the risk he ran, but for the knowledge that Herbert must soon come back. Once, I actually did start out of bed in the night, and begin to dress myself in my worst clothes, hurriedly intending to leave him there with everything else I possessed, and enlist for India as a private soldier.\n\nI doubt if a ghost could have been more terrible to me, up in those lonely rooms in the long evenings and long nights, with the wind and the rain always rushing by. A ghost could not have been taken and hanged on my account, and the consideration that he could be, and the dread that he would be, were no small addition to my horrors. When he was not asleep, or playing a complicated kind of Patience with a ragged pack of cards of his own,—a game that I never saw before or since, and in which he recorded his winnings by sticking his jackknife into the table,—when he was not engaged in either of these pursuits, he would ask me to read to him,—“Foreign language, dear boy!” While I complied, he, not comprehending a single word, would stand before the fire surveying me with the air of an Exhibitor, and I would see him, between the fingers of the hand with which I shaded my face, appealing in dumb show to the furniture to take notice of my proficiency. The imaginary student pursued by the misshapen creature he had impiously made, was not more wretched than I, pursued by the creature who had made me, and recoiling from him with a stronger repulsion, the more he admired me and the fonder he was of me.\n\nThis is written of, I am sensible, as if it had lasted a year. It lasted about five days. Expecting Herbert all the time, I dared not go out, except when I took Provis for an airing after dark. At length, one evening when dinner was over and I had dropped into a slumber quite worn out,—for my nights had been agitated and my rest broken by fearful dreams,—I was roused by the welcome footstep on the staircase. Provis, who had been asleep too, staggered up at the noise I made, and in an instant I saw his jackknife shining in his hand.\n\n“Quiet! It’s Herbert!” I said; and Herbert came bursting in, with the airy freshness of six hundred miles of France upon him.\n\n“Handel, my dear fellow, how are you, and again how are you, and again how are you? I seem to have been gone a twelvemonth! Why, so I must have been, for you have grown quite thin and pale! Handel, my—Halloa! I beg your pardon.”\n\nHe was stopped in his running on and in his shaking hands with me, by seeing Provis. Provis, regarding him with a fixed attention, was slowly putting up his jackknife, and groping in another pocket for something else.\n\n“Herbert, my dear friend,” said I, shutting the double doors, while Herbert stood staring and wondering, “something very strange has happened. This is—a visitor of mine.”\n\n“It’s all right, dear boy!” said Provis coming forward, with his little clasped black book, and then addressing himself to Herbert. “Take it in your right hand. Lord strike you dead on the spot, if ever you split in any way sumever! Kiss it!”\n\n“Do so, as he wishes it,” I said to Herbert. So, Herbert, looking at me with a friendly uneasiness and amazement, complied, and Provis immediately shaking hands with him, said, “Now you’re on your oath, you know. And never believe me on mine, if Pip shan’t make a gentleman on you!”\n\n# Chapter XLI.\n\nIn vain should I attempt to describe the astonishment and disquiet of Herbert, when he and I and Provis sat down before the fire, and I recounted the whole of the secret. Enough, that I saw my own feelings reflected in Herbert’s face, and not least among them, my repugnance towards the man who had done so much for me.\n\nWhat would alone have set a division between that man and us, if there had been no other dividing circumstance, was his triumph in my story. Saving his troublesome sense of having been “low” on one occasion since his return,—on which point he began to hold forth to Herbert, the moment my revelation was finished,—he had no perception of the possibility of my finding any fault with my good fortune. His boast that he had made me a gentleman, and that he had come to see me support the character on his ample resources, was made for me quite as much as for himself. And that it was a highly agreeable boast to both of us, and that we must both be very proud of it, was a conclusion quite established in his own mind.\n\n“Though, look’ee here, Pip’s comrade,” he said to Herbert, after having discoursed for some time, “I know very well that once since I come back—for half a minute—I’ve been low. I said to Pip, I knowed as I had been low. But don’t you fret yourself on that score. I ain’t made Pip a gentleman, and Pip ain’t a-going to make you a gentleman, not fur me not to know what’s due to ye both. Dear boy, and Pip’s comrade, you two may count upon me always having a genteel muzzle on. Muzzled I have been since that half a minute when I was betrayed into lowness, muzzled I am at the present time, muzzled I ever will be.”\n\nHerbert said, “Certainly,” but looked as if there were no specific consolation in this, and remained perplexed and dismayed. We were anxious for the time when he would go to his lodging and leave us together, but he was evidently jealous of leaving us together, and sat late. It was midnight before I took him round to Essex Street, and saw him safely in at his own dark door. When it closed upon him, I experienced the first moment of relief I had known since the night of his arrival.\n\nNever quite free from an uneasy remembrance of the man on the stairs, I had always looked about me in taking my guest out after dark, and in bringing him back; and I looked about me now. Difficult as it is in a large city to avoid the suspicion of being watched, when the mind is conscious of danger in that regard, I could not persuade myself that any of the people within sight cared about my movements. The few who were passing passed on their several ways, and the street was empty when I turned back into the Temple. Nobody had come out at the gate with us, nobody went in at the gate with me. As I crossed by the fountain, I saw his lighted back windows looking bright and quiet, and, when I stood for a few moments in the doorway of the building where I lived, before going up the stairs, Garden Court was as still and lifeless as the staircase was when I ascended it.\n\nHerbert received me with open arms, and I had never felt before so blessedly what it is to have a friend. When he had spoken some sound words of sympathy and encouragement, we sat down to consider the question, What was to be done?\n\nThe chair that Provis had occupied still remaining where it had stood,—for he had a barrack way with him of hanging about one spot, in one unsettled manner, and going through one round of observances with his pipe and his negro-head and his jackknife and his pack of cards, and what not, as if it were all put down for him on a slate,—I say his chair remaining where it had stood, Herbert unconsciously took it, but next moment started out of it, pushed it away, and took another. He had no occasion to say after that that he had conceived an aversion for my patron, neither had I occasion to confess my own. We interchanged that confidence without shaping a syllable.\n\n“What,” said I to Herbert, when he was safe in another chair,—“what is to be done?”\n\n“My poor dear Handel,” he replied, holding his head, “I am too stunned to think.”\n\n“So was I, Herbert, when the blow first fell. Still, something must be done. He is intent upon various new expenses,—horses, and carriages, and lavish appearances of all kinds. He must be stopped somehow.”\n\n“You mean that you can’t accept—”\n\n“How can I?” I interposed, as Herbert paused. “Think of him! Look at him!”\n\nAn involuntary shudder passed over both of us.\n\n“Yet I am afraid the dreadful truth is, Herbert, that he is attached to me, strongly attached to me. Was there ever such a fate!”\n\n“My poor dear Handel,” Herbert repeated.\n\n“Then,” said I, “after all, stopping short here, never taking another penny from him, think what I owe him already! Then again: I am heavily in debt,—very heavily for me, who have now no expectations,—and I have been bred to no calling, and I am fit for nothing.”\n\n“Well, well, well!” Herbert remonstrated. “Don’t say fit for nothing.”\n\n“What am I fit for? I know only one thing that I am fit for, and that is, to go for a soldier. And I might have gone, my dear Herbert, but for the prospect of taking counsel with your friendship and affection.”\n\nOf course I broke down there: and of course Herbert, beyond seizing a warm grip of my hand, pretended not to know it.\n\n“Anyhow, my dear Handel,” said he presently, “soldiering won’t do. If you were to renounce this patronage and these favours, I suppose you would do so with some faint hope of one day repaying what you have already had. Not very strong, that hope, if you went soldiering! Besides, it’s absurd. You would be infinitely better in Clarriker’s house, small as it is. I am working up towards a partnership, you know.”\n\nPoor fellow! He little suspected with whose money.\n\n“But there is another question,” said Herbert. “This is an ignorant, determined man, who has long had one fixed idea. More than that, he seems to me (I may misjudge him) to be a man of a desperate and fierce character.”\n\n“I know he is,” I returned. “Let me tell you what evidence I have seen of it.” And I told him what I had not mentioned in my narrative, of that encounter with the other convict.\n\n“See, then,” said Herbert; “think of this! He comes here at the peril of his life, for the realisation of his fixed idea. In the moment of realisation, after all his toil and waiting, you cut the ground from under his feet, destroy his idea, and make his gains worthless to him. Do you see nothing that he might do, under the disappointment?”\n\n“I have seen it, Herbert, and dreamed of it, ever since the fatal night of his arrival. Nothing has been in my thoughts so distinctly as his putting himself in the way of being taken.”\n\n“Then you may rely upon it,” said Herbert, “that there would be great danger of his doing it. That is his power over you as long as he remains in England, and that would be his reckless course if you forsook him.”\n\nI was so struck by the horror of this idea, which had weighed upon me from the first, and the working out of which would make me regard myself, in some sort, as his murderer, that I could not rest in my chair, but began pacing to and fro. I said to Herbert, meanwhile, that even if Provis were recognised and taken, in spite of himself, I should be wretched as the cause, however innocently. Yes; even though I was so wretched in having him at large and near me, and even though I would far rather have worked at the forge all the days of my life than I would ever have come to this!\n\nBut there was no staving off the question, What was to be done?\n\n“The first and the main thing to be done,” said Herbert, “is to get him out of England. You will have to go with him, and then he may be induced to go.”\n\n“But get him where I will, could I prevent his coming back?”\n\n“My good Handel, is it not obvious that with Newgate in the next street, there must be far greater hazard in your breaking your mind to him and making him reckless, here, than elsewhere? If a pretext to get him away could be made out of that other convict, or out of anything else in his life, now.”\n\n“There, again!” said I, stopping before Herbert, with my open hands held out, as if they contained the desperation of the case. “I know nothing of his life. It has almost made me mad to sit here of a night and see him before me, so bound up with my fortunes and misfortunes, and yet so unknown to me, except as the miserable wretch who terrified me two days in my childhood!”\n\nHerbert got up, and linked his arm in mine, and we slowly walked to and fro together, studying the carpet.\n\n“Handel,” said Herbert, stopping, “you feel convinced that you can take no further benefits from him; do you?”\n\n“Fully. Surely you would, too, if you were in my place?”\n\n“And you feel convinced that you must break with him?”\n\n“Herbert, can you ask me?”\n\n“And you have, and are bound to have, that tenderness for the life he has risked on your account, that you must save him, if possible, from throwing it away. Then you must get him out of England before you stir a finger to extricate yourself. That done, extricate yourself, in Heaven’s name, and we’ll see it out together, dear old boy.”\n\nIt was a comfort to shake hands upon it, and walk up and down again, with only that done.\n\n“Now, Herbert,” said I, “with reference to gaining some knowledge of his history. There is but one way that I know of. I must ask him point blank.”\n\n“Yes. Ask him,” said Herbert, “when we sit at breakfast in the morning.” For he had said, on taking leave of Herbert, that he would come to breakfast with us.\n\nWith this project formed, we went to bed. I had the wildest dreams concerning him, and woke unrefreshed; I woke, too, to recover the fear which I had lost in the night, of his being found out as a returned transport. Waking, I never lost that fear.\n\nHe came round at the appointed time, took out his jackknife, and sat down to his meal. He was full of plans “for his gentleman’s coming out strong, and like a gentleman,” and urged me to begin speedily upon the pocket-book which he had left in my possession. He considered the chambers and his own lodging as temporary residences, and advised me to look out at once for a “fashionable crib” near Hyde Park, in which he could have “a shake-down.” When he had made an end of his breakfast, and was wiping his knife on his leg, I said to him, without a word of preface,—\n\n“After you were gone last night, I told my friend of the struggle that the soldiers found you engaged in on the marshes, when we came up. You remember?”\n\n“Remember!” said he. “I think so!”\n\n“We want to know something about that man—and about you. It is strange to know no more about either, and particularly you, than I was able to tell last night. Is not this as good a time as another for our knowing more?”\n\n“Well!” he said, after consideration. “You’re on your oath, you know, Pip’s comrade?”\n\n“Assuredly,” replied Herbert.\n\n“As to anything I say, you know,” he insisted. “The oath applies to all.”\n\n“I understand it to do so.”\n\n“And look’ee here! Wotever I done is worked out and paid for,” he insisted again.\n\n“So be it.”\n\nHe took out his black pipe and was going to fill it with negro-head, when, looking at the tangle of tobacco in his hand, he seemed to think it might perplex the thread of his narrative. He put it back again, stuck his pipe in a button-hole of his coat, spread a hand on each knee, and after turning an angry eye on the fire for a few silent moments, looked round at us and said what follows.\n\n# Chapter XLII.\n\n“Dear boy and Pip’s comrade. I am not a-going fur to tell you my life like a song, or a story-book. But to give it you short and handy, I’ll put it at once into a mouthful of English. In jail and out of jail, in jail and out of jail, in jail and out of jail. There, you’ve got it. That’s my life pretty much, down to such times as I got shipped off, arter Pip stood my friend.\n\n“I’ve been done everything to, pretty well—except hanged. I’ve been locked up as much as a silver tea-kittle. I’ve been carted here and carted there, and put out of this town, and put out of that town, and stuck in the stocks, and whipped and worried and drove. I’ve no more notion where I was born than you have—if so much. I first become aware of myself down in Essex, a thieving turnips for my living. Summun had run away from me—a man—a tinker—and he’d took the fire with him, and left me wery cold.\n\n“I know’d my name to be Magwitch, chrisen’d Abel. How did I know it? Much as I know’d the birds’ names in the hedges to be chaffinch, sparrer, thrush. I might have thought it was all lies together, only as the birds’ names come out true, I supposed mine did.\n\n“So fur as I could find, there warn’t a soul that see young Abel Magwitch, with us little on him as in him, but wot caught fright at him, and either drove him off, or took him up. I was took up, took up, took up, to that extent that I reg’larly grow’d up took up.\n\n“This is the way it was, that when I was a ragged little creetur as much to be pitied as ever I see (not that I looked in the glass, for there warn’t many insides of furnished houses known to me), I got the name of being hardened. ‘This is a terrible hardened one,’ they says to prison wisitors, picking out me. ‘May be said to live in jails, this boy.’ Then they looked at me, and I looked at them, and they measured my head, some on ’em,—they had better a measured my stomach,—and others on ’em giv me tracts what I couldn’t read, and made me speeches what I couldn’t understand. They always went on agen me about the Devil. But what the Devil was I to do? I must put something into my stomach, mustn’t I?—Howsomever, I’m a getting low, and I know what’s due. Dear boy and Pip’s comrade, don’t you be afeerd of me being low.\n\n“Tramping, begging, thieving, working sometimes when I could,—though that warn’t as often as you may think, till you put the question whether you would ha’ been over-ready to give me work yourselves,—a bit of a poacher, a bit of a labourer, a bit of a wagoner, a bit of a haymaker, a bit of a hawker, a bit of most things that don’t pay and lead to trouble, I got to be a man. A deserting soldier in a Traveller’s Rest, what lay hid up to the chin under a lot of taturs, learnt me to read; and a travelling Giant what signed his name at a penny a time learnt me to write. I warn’t locked up as often now as formerly, but I wore out my good share of key-metal still.\n\n“At Epsom races, a matter of over twenty years ago, I got acquainted wi’ a man whose skull I’d crack wi’ this poker, like the claw of a lobster, if I’d got it on this hob. His right name was Compeyson; and that’s the man, dear boy, what you see me a pounding in the ditch, according to what you truly told your comrade arter I was gone last night.\n\n“He set up fur a gentleman, this Compeyson, and he’d been to a public boarding-school and had learning. He was a smooth one to talk, and was a dab at the ways of gentlefolks. He was good-looking too. It was the night afore the great race, when I found him on the heath, in a booth that I know’d on. Him and some more was a sitting among the tables when I went in, and the landlord (which had a knowledge of me, and was a sporting one) called him out, and said, ‘I think this is a man that might suit you,’—meaning I was.\n\n“Compeyson, he looks at me very noticing, and I look at him. He has a watch and a chain and a ring and a breast-pin and a handsome suit of clothes.\n\n“‘To judge from appearances, you’re out of luck,’ says Compeyson to me.\n\n“‘Yes, master, and I’ve never been in it much.’ (I had come out of Kingston Jail last on a vagrancy committal. Not but what it might have been for something else; but it warn’t.)\n\n“‘Luck changes,’ says Compeyson; ‘perhaps yours is going to change.’\n\n“I says, ‘I hope it may be so. There’s room.’\n\n“‘What can you do?’ says Compeyson.\n\n“‘Eat and drink,’ I says; ‘if you’ll find the materials.’\n\n“Compeyson laughed, looked at me again very noticing, giv me five shillings, and appointed me for next night. Same place.\n\n“I went to Compeyson next night, same place, and Compeyson took me on to be his man and pardner. And what was Compeyson’s business in which we was to go pardners? Compeyson’s business was the swindling, handwriting forging, stolen bank-note passing, and such-like. All sorts of traps as Compeyson could set with his head, and keep his own legs out of and get the profits from and let another man in for, was Compeyson’s business. He’d no more heart than a iron file, he was as cold as death, and he had the head of the Devil afore mentioned.\n\n“There was another in with Compeyson, as was called Arthur,—not as being so chrisen’d, but as a surname. He was in a Decline, and was a shadow to look at. Him and Compeyson had been in a bad thing with a rich lady some years afore, and they’d made a pot of money by it; but Compeyson betted and gamed, and he’d have run through the king’s taxes. So, Arthur was a dying, and a dying poor and with the horrors on him, and Compeyson’s wife (which Compeyson kicked mostly) was a having pity on him when she could, and Compeyson was a having pity on nothing and nobody.\n\n“I might a took warning by Arthur, but I didn’t; and I won’t pretend I was partick’ler—for where ’ud be the good on it, dear boy and comrade? So I begun wi’ Compeyson, and a poor tool I was in his hands. Arthur lived at the top of Compeyson’s house (over nigh Brentford it was), and Compeyson kept a careful account agen him for board and lodging, in case he should ever get better to work it out. But Arthur soon settled the account. The second or third time as ever I see him, he come a tearing down into Compeyson’s parlour late at night, in only a flannel gown, with his hair all in a sweat, and he says to Compeyson’s wife, ‘Sally, she really is upstairs alonger me, now, and I can’t get rid of her. She’s all in white,’ he says, ‘wi’ white flowers in her hair, and she’s awful mad, and she’s got a shroud hanging over her arm, and she says she’ll put it on me at five in the morning.’\n\n“Says Compeyson: ‘Why, you fool, don’t you know she’s got a living body? And how should she be up there, without coming through the door, or in at the window, and up the stairs?’\n\n“‘I don’t know how she’s there,’ says Arthur, shivering dreadful with the horrors, ‘but she’s standing in the corner at the foot of the bed, awful mad. And over where her heart’s broke—you broke it!—there’s drops of blood.’\n\n“Compeyson spoke hardy, but he was always a coward. ‘Go up alonger this drivelling sick man,’ he says to his wife, ‘and Magwitch, lend her a hand, will you?’ But he never come nigh himself.\n\n“Compeyson’s wife and me took him up to bed agen, and he raved most dreadful. ‘Why look at her!’ he cries out. ‘She’s a shaking the shroud at me! Don’t you see her? Look at her eyes! Ain’t it awful to see her so mad?’ Next he cries, ‘She’ll put it on me, and then I’m done for! Take it away from her, take it away!’ And then he catched hold of us, and kep on a talking to her, and answering of her, till I half believed I see her myself.\n\n“Compeyson’s wife, being used to him, giv him some liquor to get the horrors off, and by and by he quieted. ‘O, she’s gone! Has her keeper been for her?’ he says. ‘Yes,’ says Compeyson’s wife. ‘Did you tell him to lock her and bar her in?’ ‘Yes.’ ‘And to take that ugly thing away from her?’ ‘Yes, yes, all right.’ ‘You’re a good creetur,’ he says, ‘don’t leave me, whatever you do, and thank you!’\n\n“He rested pretty quiet till it might want a few minutes of five, and then he starts up with a scream, and screams out, ‘Here she is! She’s got the shroud again. She’s unfolding it. She’s coming out of the corner. She’s coming to the bed. Hold me, both on you—one of each side—don’t let her touch me with it. Hah! she missed me that time. Don’t let her throw it over my shoulders. Don’t let her lift me up to get it round me. She’s lifting me up. Keep me down!’ Then he lifted himself up hard, and was dead.\n\n“Compeyson took it easy as a good riddance for both sides. Him and me was soon busy, and first he swore me (being ever artful) on my own book,—this here little black book, dear boy, what I swore your comrade on.\n\n“Not to go into the things that Compeyson planned, and I done—which ’ud take a week—I’ll simply say to you, dear boy, and Pip’s comrade, that that man got me into such nets as made me his black slave. I was always in debt to him, always under his thumb, always a working, always a getting into danger. He was younger than me, but he’d got craft, and he’d got learning, and he overmatched me five hundred times told and no mercy. My Missis as I had the hard time wi’—Stop though! I ain’t brought her in—”\n\nHe looked about him in a confused way, as if he had lost his place in the book of his remembrance; and he turned his face to the fire, and spread his hands broader on his knees, and lifted them off and put them on again.\n\n“There ain’t no need to go into it,” he said, looking round once more. “The time wi’ Compeyson was a’most as hard a time as ever I had; that said, all’s said. Did I tell you as I was tried, alone, for misdemeanor, while with Compeyson?”\n\nI answered, No.\n\n“Well!” he said, “I was, and got convicted. As to took up on suspicion, that was twice or three times in the four or five year that it lasted; but evidence was wanting. At last, me and Compeyson was both committed for felony,—on a charge of putting stolen notes in circulation,—and there was other charges behind. Compeyson says to me, ‘Separate defences, no communication,’ and that was all. And I was so miserable poor, that I sold all the clothes I had, except what hung on my back, afore I could get Jaggers.\n\n“When we was put in the dock, I noticed first of all what a gentleman Compeyson looked, wi’ his curly hair and his black clothes and his white pocket-handkercher, and what a common sort of a wretch I looked. When the prosecution opened and the evidence was put short, aforehand, I noticed how heavy it all bore on me, and how light on him. When the evidence was giv in the box, I noticed how it was always me that had come for’ard, and could be swore to, how it was always me that the money had been paid to, how it was always me that had seemed to work the thing and get the profit. But when the defence come on, then I see the plan plainer; for, says the counsellor for Compeyson, ‘My lord and gentlemen, here you has afore you, side by side, two persons as your eyes can separate wide; one, the younger, well brought up, who will be spoke to as such; one, the elder, ill brought up, who will be spoke to as such; one, the younger, seldom if ever seen in these here transactions, and only suspected; t’other, the elder, always seen in ’em and always wi’ his guilt brought home. Can you doubt, if there is but one in it, which is the one, and, if there is two in it, which is much the worst one?’ And such-like. And when it come to character, warn’t it Compeyson as had been to the school, and warn’t it his schoolfellows as was in this position and in that, and warn’t it him as had been know’d by witnesses in such clubs and societies, and nowt to his disadvantage? And warn’t it me as had been tried afore, and as had been know’d up hill and down dale in Bridewells and Lock-Ups! And when it come to speech-making, warn’t it Compeyson as could speak to ’em wi’ his face dropping every now and then into his white pocket-handkercher,—ah! and wi’ verses in his speech, too,—and warn’t it me as could only say, ‘Gentlemen, this man at my side is a most precious rascal’? And when the verdict come, warn’t it Compeyson as was recommended to mercy on account of good character and bad company, and giving up all the information he could agen me, and warn’t it me as got never a word but Guilty? And when I says to Compeyson, ‘Once out of this court, I’ll smash that face of yourn!’ ain’t it Compeyson as prays the Judge to be protected, and gets two turnkeys stood betwixt us? And when we’re sentenced, ain’t it him as gets seven year, and me fourteen, and ain’t it him as the Judge is sorry for, because he might a done so well, and ain’t it me as the Judge perceives to be a old offender of wiolent passion, likely to come to worse?”\n\nHe had worked himself into a state of great excitement, but he checked it, took two or three short breaths, swallowed as often, and stretching out his hand towards me said, in a reassuring manner, “I ain’t a-going to be low, dear boy!”\n\nHe had so heated himself that he took out his handkerchief and wiped his face and head and neck and hands, before he could go on.\n\n“I had said to Compeyson that I’d smash that face of his, and I swore Lord smash mine! to do it. We was in the same prison-ship, but I couldn’t get at him for long, though I tried. At last I come behind him and hit him on the cheek to turn him round and get a smashing one at him, when I was seen and seized. The black-hole of that ship warn’t a strong one, to a judge of black-holes that could swim and dive. I escaped to the shore, and I was a hiding among the graves there, envying them as was in ’em and all over, when I first see my boy!”\n\nHe regarded me with a look of affection that made him almost abhorrent to me again, though I had felt great pity for him.\n\n“By my boy, I was giv to understand as Compeyson was out on them marshes too. Upon my soul, I half believe he escaped in his terror, to get quit of me, not knowing it was me as had got ashore. I hunted him down. I smashed his face. ‘And now,’ says I ‘as the worst thing I can do, caring nothing for myself, I’ll drag you back.’ And I’d have swum off, towing him by the hair, if it had come to that, and I’d a got him aboard without the soldiers.\n\n“Of course he’d much the best of it to the last,—his character was so good. He had escaped when he was made half wild by me and my murderous intentions; and his punishment was light. I was put in irons, brought to trial again, and sent for life. I didn’t stop for life, dear boy and Pip’s comrade, being here.”\n\nHe wiped himself again, as he had done before, and then slowly took his tangle of tobacco from his pocket, and plucked his pipe from his button-hole, and slowly filled it, and began to smoke.\n\n“Is he dead?” I asked, after a silence.\n\n“Is who dead, dear boy?”\n\n“Compeyson.”\n“He hopes I am, if he’s alive, you may be sure,” with a fierce look. “I never heerd no more of him.”\n\nHerbert had been writing with his pencil in the cover of a book. He softly pushed the book over to me, as Provis stood smoking with his eyes on the fire, and I read in it:—\n\n“Young Havisham’s name was Arthur. Compeyson is the man who professed to be Miss Havisham’s lover.”\n\nI shut the book and nodded slightly to Herbert, and put the book by; but we neither of us said anything, and both looked at Provis as he stood smoking by the fire.\n\n# Chapter XLIII.\n\nWhy should I pause to ask how much of my shrinking from Provis might be traced to Estella? Why should I loiter on my road, to compare the state of mind in which I had tried to rid myself of the stain of the prison before meeting her at the coach-office, with the state of mind in which I now reflected on the abyss between Estella in her pride and beauty, and the returned transport whom I harboured? The road would be none the smoother for it, the end would be none the better for it, he would not be helped, nor I extenuated.\n\nA new fear had been engendered in my mind by his narrative; or rather, his narrative had given form and purpose to the fear that was already there. If Compeyson were alive and should discover his return, I could hardly doubt the consequence. That Compeyson stood in mortal fear of him, neither of the two could know much better than I; and that any such man as that man had been described to be would hesitate to release himself for good from a dreaded enemy by the safe means of becoming an informer was scarcely to be imagined.\n\nNever had I breathed, and never would I breathe—or so I resolved—a word of Estella to Provis. But, I said to Herbert that, before I could go abroad, I must see both Estella and Miss Havisham. This was when we were left alone on the night of the day when Provis told us his story. I resolved to go out to Richmond next day, and I went.\n\nOn my presenting myself at Mrs. Brandley’s, Estella’s maid was called to tell that Estella had gone into the country. Where? To Satis House, as usual. Not as usual, I said, for she had never yet gone there without me; when was she coming back? There was an air of reservation in the answer which increased my perplexity, and the answer was, that her maid believed she was only coming back at all for a little while. I could make nothing of this, except that it was meant that I should make nothing of it, and I went home again in complete discomfiture.\n\nAnother night consultation with Herbert after Provis was gone home (I always took him home, and always looked well about me), led us to the conclusion that nothing should be said about going abroad until I came back from Miss Havisham’s. In the mean time, Herbert and I were to consider separately what it would be best to say; whether we should devise any pretence of being afraid that he was under suspicious observation; or whether I, who had never yet been abroad, should propose an expedition. We both knew that I had but to propose anything, and he would consent. We agreed that his remaining many days in his present hazard was not to be thought of.\n\nNext day I had the meanness to feign that I was under a binding promise to go down to Joe; but I was capable of almost any meanness towards Joe or his name. Provis was to be strictly careful while I was gone, and Herbert was to take the charge of him that I had taken. I was to be absent only one night, and, on my return, the gratification of his impatience for my starting as a gentleman on a greater scale was to be begun. It occurred to me then, and as I afterwards found to Herbert also, that he might be best got away across the water, on that pretence,—as, to make purchases, or the like.\n\nHaving thus cleared the way for my expedition to Miss Havisham’s, I set off by the early morning coach before it was yet light, and was out on the open country road when the day came creeping on, halting and whimpering and shivering, and wrapped in patches of cloud and rags of mist, like a beggar. When we drove up to the Blue Boar after a drizzly ride, whom should I see come out under the gateway, toothpick in hand, to look at the coach, but Bentley Drummle!\n\nAs he pretended not to see me, I pretended not to see him. It was a very lame pretence on both sides; the lamer, because we both went into the coffee-room, where he had just finished his breakfast, and where I ordered mine. It was poisonous to me to see him in the town, for I very well knew why he had come there.\n\nPretending to read a smeary newspaper long out of date, which had nothing half so legible in its local news, as the foreign matter of coffee, pickles, fish sauces, gravy, melted butter, and wine with which it was sprinkled all over, as if it had taken the measles in a highly irregular form, I sat at my table while he stood before the fire. By degrees it became an enormous injury to me that he stood before the fire. And I got up, determined to have my share of it. I had to put my hand behind his legs for the poker when I went up to the fireplace to stir the fire, but still pretended not to know him.\n\n“Is this a cut?” said Mr. Drummle.\n\n“Oh!” said I, poker in hand; “it’s you, is it? How do you do? I was wondering who it was, who kept the fire off.”\n\nWith that, I poked tremendously, and having done so, planted myself side by side with Mr. Drummle, my shoulders squared and my back to the fire.\n\n“You have just come down?” said Mr. Drummle, edging me a little away with his shoulder.\n\n“Yes,” said I, edging him a little away with my shoulder.\n\n“Beastly place,” said Drummle. “Your part of the country, I think?”\n\n“Yes,” I assented. “I am told it’s very like your Shropshire.”\n\n“Not in the least like it,” said Drummle.\n\nHere Mr. Drummle looked at his boots and I looked at mine, and then Mr. Drummle looked at my boots, and I looked at his.\n\n“Have you been here long?” I asked, determined not to yield an inch of the fire.\n\n“Long enough to be tired of it,” returned Drummle, pretending to yawn, but equally determined.\n\n“Do you stay here long?”\n\n“Can’t say,” answered Mr. Drummle. “Do you?”\n\n“Can’t say,” said I.\n\nI felt here, through a tingling in my blood, that if Mr. Drummle’s shoulder had claimed another hair’s breadth of room, I should have jerked him into the window; equally, that if my own shoulder had urged a similar claim, Mr. Drummle would have jerked me into the nearest box. He whistled a little. So did I.\n\n“Large tract of marshes about here, I believe?” said Drummle.\n\n“Yes. What of that?” said I.\n\nMr. Drummle looked at me, and then at my boots, and then said, “Oh!” and laughed.\n\n“Are you amused, Mr. Drummle?”\n\n“No,” said he, “not particularly. I am going out for a ride in the saddle. I mean to explore those marshes for amusement. Out-of-the-way villages there, they tell me. Curious little public-houses—and smithies—and that. Waiter!”\n\n“Yes, sir.”\n\n“Is that horse of mine ready?”\n\n“Brought round to the door, sir.”\n\n“I say. Look here, you sir. The lady won’t ride to-day; the weather won’t do.”\n\n“Very good, sir.”\n\n“And I don’t dine, because I’m going to dine at the lady’s.”\n\n“Very good, sir.”\n\nThen, Drummle glanced at me, with an insolent triumph on his great-jowled face that cut me to the heart, dull as he was, and so exasperated me, that I felt inclined to take him in my arms (as the robber in the story-book is said to have taken the old lady) and seat him on the fire.\n\nOne thing was manifest to both of us, and that was, that until relief came, neither of us could relinquish the fire. There we stood, well squared up before it, shoulder to shoulder and foot to foot, with our hands behind us, not budging an inch. The horse was visible outside in the drizzle at the door, my breakfast was put on the table, Drummle’s was cleared away, the waiter invited me to begin, I nodded, we both stood our ground.\n\n“Have you been to the Grove since?” said Drummle.\n\n“No,” said I, “I had quite enough of the Finches the last time I was there.”\n\n“Was that when we had a difference of opinion?”\n\n“Yes,” I replied, very shortly.\n\n“Come, come! They let you off easily enough,” sneered Drummle. “You shouldn’t have lost your temper.”\n\n“Mr. Drummle,” said I, “you are not competent to give advice on that subject. When I lose my temper (not that I admit having done so on that occasion), I don’t throw glasses.”\n\n“I do,” said Drummle.\n\nAfter glancing at him once or twice, in an increased state of smouldering ferocity, I said,—\n\n“Mr. Drummle, I did not seek this conversation, and I don’t think it an agreeable one.”\n\n“I am sure it’s not,” said he, superciliously over his shoulder; “I don’t think anything about it.”\n\n“And therefore,” I went on, “with your leave, I will suggest that we hold no kind of communication in future.”\n\n“Quite my opinion,” said Drummle, “and what I should have suggested myself, or done—more likely—without suggesting. But don’t lose your temper. Haven’t you lost enough without that?”\n\n“What do you mean, sir?”\n\n“Waiter!” said Drummle, by way of answering me.\n\nThe waiter reappeared.\n\n“Look here, you sir. You quite understand that the young lady don’t ride to-day, and that I dine at the young lady’s?”\n\n“Quite so, sir!”\n\nWhen the waiter had felt my fast-cooling teapot with the palm of his hand, and had looked imploringly at me, and had gone out, Drummle, careful not to move the shoulder next me, took a cigar from his pocket and bit the end off, but showed no sign of stirring. Choking and boiling as I was, I felt that we could not go a word further, without introducing Estella’s name, which I could not endure to hear him utter; and therefore I looked stonily at the opposite wall, as if there were no one present, and forced myself to silence. How long we might have remained in this ridiculous position it is impossible to say, but for the incursion of three thriving farmers—laid on by the waiter, I think—who came into the coffee-room unbuttoning their great-coats and rubbing their hands, and before whom, as they charged at the fire, we were obliged to give way.\n\nI saw him through the window, seizing his horse’s mane, and mounting in his blundering brutal manner, and sidling and backing away. I thought he was gone, when he came back, calling for a light for the cigar in his mouth, which he had forgotten. A man in a dust-coloured dress appeared with what was wanted,—I could not have said from where: whether from the inn yard, or the street, or where not,—and as Drummle leaned down from the saddle and lighted his cigar and laughed, with a jerk of his head towards the coffee-room windows, the slouching shoulders and ragged hair of this man whose back was towards me reminded me of Orlick.\n\nToo heavily out of sorts to care much at the time whether it were he or no, or after all to touch the breakfast, I washed the weather and the journey from my face and hands, and went out to the memorable old house that it would have been so much the better for me never to have entered, never to have seen.\n\n# Chapter XLIV.\n\nIn the room where the dressing-table stood, and where the wax-candles burnt on the wall, I found Miss Havisham and Estella; Miss Havisham seated on a settee near the fire, and Estella on a cushion at her feet. Estella was knitting, and Miss Havisham was looking on. They both raised their eyes as I went in, and both saw an alteration in me. I derived that, from the look they interchanged.\n\n“And what wind,” said Miss Havisham, “blows you here, Pip?”\n\nThough she looked steadily at me, I saw that she was rather confused. Estella, pausing a moment in her knitting with her eyes upon me, and then going on, I fancied that I read in the action of her fingers, as plainly as if she had told me in the dumb alphabet, that she perceived I had discovered my real benefactor.\n\n“Miss Havisham,” said I, “I went to Richmond yesterday, to speak to Estella; and finding that some wind had blown her here, I followed.”\n\nMiss Havisham motioning to me for the third or fourth time to sit down, I took the chair by the dressing-table, which I had often seen her occupy. With all that ruin at my feet and about me, it seemed a natural place for me, that day.\n\n“What I had to say to Estella, Miss Havisham, I will say before you, presently—in a few moments. It will not surprise you, it will not displease you. I am as unhappy as you can ever have meant me to be.”\n\nMiss Havisham continued to look steadily at me. I could see in the action of Estella’s fingers as they worked that she attended to what I said; but she did not look up.\n\n“I have found out who my patron is. It is not a fortunate discovery, and is not likely ever to enrich me in reputation, station, fortune, anything. There are reasons why I must say no more of that. It is not my secret, but another’s.”\n\nAs I was silent for a while, looking at Estella and considering how to go on, Miss Havisham repeated, “It is not your secret, but another’s. Well?”\n\n“When you first caused me to be brought here, Miss Havisham, when I belonged to the village over yonder, that I wish I had never left, I suppose I did really come here, as any other chance boy might have come,—as a kind of servant, to gratify a want or a whim, and to be paid for it?”\n\n“Ay, Pip,” replied Miss Havisham, steadily nodding her head; “you did.”\n\n“And that Mr. Jaggers—”\n\n“Mr. Jaggers,” said Miss Havisham, taking me up in a firm tone, “had nothing to do with it, and knew nothing of it. His being my lawyer, and his being the lawyer of your patron is a coincidence. He holds the same relation towards numbers of people, and it might easily arise. Be that as it may, it did arise, and was not brought about by any one.”\n\nAny one might have seen in her haggard face that there was no suppression or evasion so far.\n\n“But when I fell into the mistake I have so long remained in, at least you led me on?” said I.\n\n“Yes,” she returned, again nodding steadily, “I let you go on.”\n“Was that kind?”\n\n“Who am I,” cried Miss Havisham, striking her stick upon the floor and flashing into wrath so suddenly that Estella glanced up at her in surprise,—“who am I, for God’s sake, that I should be kind?”\n\nIt was a weak complaint to have made, and I had not meant to make it. I told her so, as she sat brooding after this outburst.\n\n“Well, well, well!” she said. “What else?”\n\n“I was liberally paid for my old attendance here,” I said, to soothe her, “in being apprenticed, and I have asked these questions only for my own information. What follows has another (and I hope more disinterested) purpose. In humouring my mistake, Miss Havisham, you punished—practised on—perhaps you will supply whatever term expresses your intention, without offence—your self-seeking relations?”\n\n“I did. Why, they would have it so! So would you. What has been my history, that I should be at the pains of entreating either them or you not to have it so! You made your own snares. I never made them.”\n\nWaiting until she was quiet again,—for this, too, flashed out of her in a wild and sudden way,—I went on.\n\n“I have been thrown among one family of your relations, Miss Havisham, and have been constantly among them since I went to London. I know them to have been as honestly under my delusion as I myself. And I should be false and base if I did not tell you, whether it is acceptable to you or no, and whether you are inclined to give credence to it or no, that you deeply wrong both Mr. Matthew Pocket and his son Herbert, if you suppose them to be otherwise than generous, upright, open, and incapable of anything designing or mean.”\n\n“They are your friends,” said Miss Havisham.\n\n“They made themselves my friends,” said I, “when they supposed me to have superseded them; and when Sarah Pocket, Miss Georgiana, and Mistress Camilla were not my friends, I think.”\n\nThis contrasting of them with the rest seemed, I was glad to see, to do them good with her. She looked at me keenly for a little while, and then said quietly,—\n\n“What do you want for them?”\n\n“Only,” said I, “that you would not confound them with the others. They may be of the same blood, but, believe me, they are not of the same nature.”\n\nStill looking at me keenly, Miss Havisham repeated,—\n\n“What do you want for them?”\n\n“I am not so cunning, you see,” I said, in answer, conscious that I reddened a little, “as that I could hide from you, even if I desired, that I do want something. Miss Havisham, if you would spare the money to do my friend Herbert a lasting service in life, but which from the nature of the case must be done without his knowledge, I could show you how.”\n\n“Why must it be done without his knowledge?” she asked, settling her hands upon her stick, that she might regard me the more attentively.\n\n“Because,” said I, “I began the service myself, more than two years ago, without his knowledge, and I don’t want to be betrayed. Why I fail in my ability to finish it, I cannot explain. It is a part of the secret which is another person’s and not mine.”\n\nShe gradually withdrew her eyes from me, and turned them on the fire. After watching it for what appeared in the silence and by the light of the slowly wasting candles to be a long time, she was roused by the collapse of some of the red coals, and looked towards me again—at first, vacantly—then, with a gradually concentrating attention. All this time Estella knitted on. When Miss Havisham had fixed her attention on me, she said, speaking as if there had been no lapse in our dialogue,—\n\n“What else?”\n\n“Estella,” said I, turning to her now, and trying to command my trembling voice, “you know I love you. You know that I have loved you long and dearly.”\n\nShe raised her eyes to my face, on being thus addressed, and her fingers plied their work, and she looked at me with an unmoved countenance. I saw that Miss Havisham glanced from me to her, and from her to me.\n\n“I should have said this sooner, but for my long mistake. It induced me to hope that Miss Havisham meant us for one another. While I thought you could not help yourself, as it were, I refrained from saying it. But I must say it now.”\n\nPreserving her unmoved countenance, and with her fingers still going, Estella shook her head.\n\n“I know,” said I, in answer to that action,—“I know. I have no hope that I shall ever call you mine, Estella. I am ignorant what may become of me very soon, how poor I may be, or where I may go. Still, I love you. I have loved you ever since I first saw you in this house.”\n\nLooking at me perfectly unmoved and with her fingers busy, she shook her head again.\n\n“It would have been cruel in Miss Havisham, horribly cruel, to practise on the susceptibility of a poor boy, and to torture me through all these years with a vain hope and an idle pursuit, if she had reflected on the gravity of what she did. But I think she did not. I think that, in the endurance of her own trial, she forgot mine, Estella.”\n\nI saw Miss Havisham put her hand to her heart and hold it there, as she sat looking by turns at Estella and at me.\n\n“It seems,” said Estella, very calmly, “that there are sentiments, fancies,—I don’t know how to call them,—which I am not able to comprehend. When you say you love me, I know what you mean, as a form of words; but nothing more. You address nothing in my breast, you touch nothing there. I don’t care for what you say at all. I have tried to warn you of this; now, have I not?”\n\nI said in a miserable manner, “Yes.”\n\n“Yes. But you would not be warned, for you thought I did not mean it. Now, did you not think so?”\n\n“I thought and hoped you could not mean it. You, so young, untried, and beautiful, Estella! Surely it is not in Nature.”\n\n“It is in my nature,” she returned. And then she added, with a stress upon the words, “It is in the nature formed within me. I make a great difference between you and all other people when I say so much. I can do no more.”\n\n“Is it not true,” said I, “that Bentley Drummle is in town here, and pursuing you?”\n\n“It is quite true,” she replied, referring to him with the indifference of utter contempt.\n\n“That you encourage him, and ride out with him, and that he dines with you this very day?”\n\nShe seemed a little surprised that I should know it, but again replied, “Quite true.”\n\n“You cannot love him, Estella!”\n\nHer fingers stopped for the first time, as she retorted rather angrily, “What have I told you? Do you still think, in spite of it, that I do not mean what I say?”\n\n“You would never marry him, Estella?”\n\nShe looked towards Miss Havisham, and considered for a moment with her work in her hands. Then she said, “Why not tell you the truth? I am going to be married to him.”\n\nI dropped my face into my hands, but was able to control myself better than I could have expected, considering what agony it gave me to hear her say those words. When I raised my face again, there was such a ghastly look upon Miss Havisham’s, that it impressed me, even in my passionate hurry and grief.\n\n“Estella, dearest Estella, do not let Miss Havisham lead you into this fatal step. Put me aside for ever,—you have done so, I well know,—but bestow yourself on some worthier person than Drummle. Miss Havisham gives you to him, as the greatest slight and injury that could be done to the many far better men who admire you, and to the few who truly love you. Among those few there may be one who loves you even as dearly, though he has not loved you as long, as I. Take him, and I can bear it better, for your sake!”\n\nMy earnestness awoke a wonder in her that seemed as if it would have been touched with compassion, if she could have rendered me at all intelligible to her own mind.\n\n“I am going,” she said again, in a gentler voice, “to be married to him. The preparations for my marriage are making, and I shall be married soon. Why do you injuriously introduce the name of my mother by adoption? It is my own act.”\n\n“Your own act, Estella, to fling yourself away upon a brute?”\n\n“On whom should I fling myself away?” she retorted, with a smile. “Should I fling myself away upon the man who would the soonest feel (if people do feel such things) that I took nothing to him? There! It is done. I shall do well enough, and so will my husband. As to leading me into what you call this fatal step, Miss Havisham would have had me wait, and not marry yet; but I am tired of the life I have led, which has very few charms for me, and I am willing enough to change it. Say no more. We shall never understand each other.”\n\n“Such a mean brute, such a stupid brute!” I urged, in despair.\n\n“Don’t be afraid of my being a blessing to him,” said Estella; “I shall not be that. Come! Here is my hand. Do we part on this, you visionary boy—or man?”\n\n“O Estella!” I answered, as my bitter tears fell fast on her hand, do what I would to restrain them; “even if I remained in England and could hold my head up with the rest, how could I see you Drummle’s wife?”\n\n“Nonsense,” she returned,—“nonsense. This will pass in no time.”\n\n“Never, Estella!”\n\n“You will get me out of your thoughts in a week.”\n\n“Out of my thoughts! You are part of my existence, part of myself. You have been in every line I have ever read since I first came here, the rough common boy whose poor heart you wounded even then. You have been in every prospect I have ever seen since,—on the river, on the sails of the ships, on the marshes, in the clouds, in the light, in the darkness, in the wind, in the woods, in the sea, in the streets. You have been the embodiment of every graceful fancy that my mind has ever become acquainted with. The stones of which the strongest London buildings are made are not more real, or more impossible to be displaced by your hands, than your presence and influence have been to me, there and everywhere, and will be. Estella, to the last hour of my life, you cannot choose but remain part of my character, part of the little good in me, part of the evil. But, in this separation, I associate you only with the good; and I will faithfully hold you to that always, for you must have done me far more good than harm, let me feel now what sharp distress I may. O God bless you, God forgive you!”\n\nIn what ecstasy of unhappiness I got these broken words out of myself, I don’t know. The rhapsody welled up within me, like blood from an inward wound, and gushed out. I held her hand to my lips some lingering moments, and so I left her. But ever afterwards, I remembered,—and soon afterwards with stronger reason,—that while Estella looked at me merely with incredulous wonder, the spectral figure of Miss Havisham, her hand still covering her heart, seemed all resolved into a ghastly stare of pity and remorse.\n\nAll done, all gone! So much was done and gone, that when I went out at the gate, the light of the day seemed of a darker colour than when I went in. For a while, I hid myself among some lanes and by-paths, and then struck off to walk all the way to London. For, I had by that time come to myself so far as to consider that I could not go back to the inn and see Drummle there; that I could not bear to sit upon the coach and be spoken to; that I could do nothing half so good for myself as tire myself out.\n\nIt was past midnight when I crossed London Bridge. Pursuing the narrow intricacies of the streets which at that time tended westward near the Middlesex shore of the river, my readiest access to the Temple was close by the river-side, through Whitefriars. I was not expected till to-morrow; but I had my keys, and, if Herbert were gone to bed, could get to bed myself without disturbing him.\n\nAs it seldom happened that I came in at that Whitefriars gate after the Temple was closed, and as I was very muddy and weary, I did not take it ill that the night-porter examined me with much attention as he held the gate a little way open for me to pass in. To help his memory I mentioned my name.\n\n“I was not quite sure, sir, but I thought so. Here’s a note, sir. The messenger that brought it, said would you be so good as read it by my lantern?”\n\nMuch surprised by the request, I took the note. It was directed to Philip Pip, Esquire, and on the top of the superscription were the words, “PLEASE READ THIS, HERE.” I opened it, the watchman holding up his light, and read inside, in Wemmick’s writing,—\n\n# Chapter XLV.\n\nTurning from the Temple gate as soon as I had read the warning, I made the best of my way to Fleet Street, and there got a late hackney chariot and drove to the Hummums in Covent Garden. In those times a bed was always to be got there at any hour of the night, and the chamberlain, letting me in at his ready wicket, lighted the candle next in order on his shelf, and showed me straight into the bedroom next in order on his list. It was a sort of vault on the ground floor at the back, with a despotic monster of a four-post bedstead in it, straddling over the whole place, putting one of his arbitrary legs into the fireplace and another into the doorway, and squeezing the wretched little washing-stand in quite a Divinely Righteous manner.\n\nAs I had asked for a night-light, the chamberlain had brought me in, before he left me, the good old constitutional rushlight of those virtuous days—an object like the ghost of a walking-cane, which instantly broke its back if it were touched, which nothing could ever be lighted at, and which was placed in solitary confinement at the bottom of a high tin tower, perforated with round holes that made a staringly wide-awake pattern on the walls. When I had got into bed, and lay there footsore, weary, and wretched, I found that I could no more close my own eyes than I could close the eyes of this foolish Argus. And thus, in the gloom and death of the night, we stared at one another.\n\nWhat a doleful night! How anxious, how dismal, how long! There was an inhospitable smell in the room, of cold soot and hot dust; and, as I looked up into the corners of the tester over my head, I thought what a number of blue-bottle flies from the butchers’, and earwigs from the market, and grubs from the country, must be holding on up there, lying by for next summer. This led me to speculate whether any of them ever tumbled down, and then I fancied that I felt light falls on my face,—a disagreeable turn of thought, suggesting other and more objectionable approaches up my back. When I had lain awake a little while, those extraordinary voices with which silence teems began to make themselves audible. The closet whispered, the fireplace sighed, the little washing-stand ticked, and one guitar-string played occasionally in the chest of drawers. At about the same time, the eyes on the wall acquired a new expression, and in every one of those staring rounds I saw written, DON’T GO HOME.\n\nWhatever night-fancies and night-noises crowded on me, they never warded off this DON’T GO HOME. It plaited itself into whatever I thought of, as a bodily pain would have done. Not long before, I had read in the newspapers, how a gentleman unknown had come to the Hummums in the night, and had gone to bed, and had destroyed himself, and had been found in the morning weltering in blood. It came into my head that he must have occupied this very vault of mine, and I got out of bed to assure myself that there were no red marks about; then opened the door to look out into the passages, and cheer myself with the companionship of a distant light, near which I knew the chamberlain to be dozing. But all this time, why I was not to go home, and what had happened at home, and when I should go home, and whether Provis was safe at home, were questions occupying my mind so busily, that one might have supposed there could be no more room in it for any other theme. Even when I thought of Estella, and how we had parted that day forever, and when I recalled all the circumstances of our parting, and all her looks and tones, and the action of her fingers while she knitted,—even then I was pursuing, here and there and everywhere, the caution, Don’t go home. When at last I dozed, in sheer exhaustion of mind and body, it became a vast shadowy verb which I had to conjugate. Imperative mood, present tense: Do not thou go home, let him not go home, let us not go home, do not ye or you go home, let not them go home. Then potentially: I may not and I cannot go home; and I might not, could not, would not, and should not go home; until I felt that I was going distracted, and rolled over on the pillow, and looked at the staring rounds upon the wall again.\n\nI had left directions that I was to be called at seven; for it was plain that I must see Wemmick before seeing any one else, and equally plain that this was a case in which his Walworth sentiments only could be taken. It was a relief to get out of the room where the night had been so miserable, and I needed no second knocking at the door to startle me from my uneasy bed.\n\nThe Castle battlements arose upon my view at eight o’clock. The little servant happening to be entering the fortress with two hot rolls, I passed through the postern and crossed the drawbridge in her company, and so came without announcement into the presence of Wemmick as he was making tea for himself and the Aged. An open door afforded a perspective view of the Aged in bed.\n\n“Halloa, Mr. Pip!” said Wemmick. “You did come home, then?”\n\n“Yes,” I returned; “but I didn’t go home.”\n\n“That’s all right,” said he, rubbing his hands. “I left a note for you at each of the Temple gates, on the chance. Which gate did you come to?”\n\nI told him.\n“I’ll go round to the others in the course of the day and destroy the notes,” said Wemmick; “it’s a good rule never to leave documentary evidence if you can help it, because you don’t know when it may be put in. I’m going to take a liberty with you. Would you mind toasting this sausage for the Aged P.?”\n\nI said I should be delighted to do it.\n\n“Then you can go about your work, Mary Anne,” said Wemmick to the little servant; “which leaves us to ourselves, don’t you see, Mr. Pip?” he added, winking, as she disappeared.\n\nI thanked him for his friendship and caution, and our discourse proceeded in a low tone, while I toasted the Aged’s sausage and he buttered the crumb of the Aged’s roll.\n\n“Now, Mr. Pip, you know,” said Wemmick, “you and I understand one another. We are in our private and personal capacities, and we have been engaged in a confidential transaction before to-day. Official sentiments are one thing. We are extra official.”\n\nI cordially assented. I was so very nervous, that I had already lighted the Aged’s sausage like a torch, and been obliged to blow it out.\n\n“I accidentally heard, yesterday morning,” said Wemmick, “being in a certain place where I once took you,—even between you and me, it’s as well not to mention names when avoidable—”\n\n“Much better not,” said I. “I understand you.”\n\n“I heard there by chance, yesterday morning,” said Wemmick, “that a certain person not altogether of uncolonial pursuits, and not unpossessed of portable property,—I don’t know who it may really be,—we won’t name this person—”\n\n“Not necessary,” said I.\n\n“—Had made some little stir in a certain part of the world where a good many people go, not always in gratification of their own inclinations, and not quite irrespective of the government expense—”\n\nIn watching his face, I made quite a firework of the Aged’s sausage, and greatly discomposed both my own attention and Wemmick’s; for which I apologised.\n\n“—By disappearing from such place, and being no more heard of thereabouts. From which,” said Wemmick, “conjectures had been raised and theories formed. I also heard that you at your chambers in Garden Court, Temple, had been watched, and might be watched again.”\n\n“By whom?” said I.\n\n“I wouldn’t go into that,” said Wemmick, evasively, “it might clash with official responsibilities. I heard it, as I have in my time heard other curious things in the same place. I don’t tell it you on information received. I heard it.”\n\nHe took the toasting-fork and sausage from me as he spoke, and set forth the Aged’s breakfast neatly on a little tray. Previous to placing it before him, he went into the Aged’s room with a clean white cloth, and tied the same under the old gentleman’s chin, and propped him up, and put his nightcap on one side, and gave him quite a rakish air. Then he placed his breakfast before him with great care, and said, “All right, ain’t you, Aged P.?” To which the cheerful Aged replied, “All right, John, my boy, all right!” As there seemed to be a tacit understanding that the Aged was not in a presentable state, and was therefore to be considered invisible, I made a pretence of being in complete ignorance of these proceedings.\n\n“This watching of me at my chambers (which I have once had reason to suspect),” I said to Wemmick when he came back, “is inseparable from the person to whom you have adverted; is it?”\n\nWemmick looked very serious. “I couldn’t undertake to say that, of my own knowledge. I mean, I couldn’t undertake to say it was at first. But it either is, or it will be, or it’s in great danger of being.”\n\nAs I saw that he was restrained by fealty to Little Britain from saying as much as he could, and as I knew with thankfulness to him how far out of his way he went to say what he did, I could not press him. But I told him, after a little meditation over the fire, that I would like to ask him a question, subject to his answering or not answering, as he deemed right, and sure that his course would be right. He paused in his breakfast, and crossing his arms, and pinching his shirt-sleeves (his notion of in-door comfort was to sit without any coat), he nodded to me once, to put my question.\n\n“You have heard of a man of bad character, whose true name is Compeyson?”\n\nHe answered with one other nod.\n\n“Is he living?”\nOne other nod.\n\n“Is he in London?”\n\nHe gave me one other nod, compressed the post-office exceedingly, gave me one last nod, and went on with his breakfast.\n\n“Now,” said Wemmick, “questioning being over,” which he emphasised and repeated for my guidance, “I come to what I did, after hearing what I heard. I went to Garden Court to find you; not finding you, I went to Clarriker’s to find Mr. Herbert.”\n\n“And him you found?” said I, with great anxiety.\n\n“And him I found. Without mentioning any names or going into any details, I gave him to understand that if he was aware of anybody—Tom, Jack, or Richard—being about the chambers, or about the immediate neighbourhood, he had better get Tom, Jack, or Richard out of the way while you were out of the way.”\n\n“He would be greatly puzzled what to do?”\n\n“He was puzzled what to do; not the less, because I gave him my opinion that it was not safe to try to get Tom, Jack, or Richard too far out of the way at present. Mr. Pip, I’ll tell you something. Under existing circumstances, there is no place like a great city when you are once in it. Don’t break cover too soon. Lie close. Wait till things slacken, before you try the open, even for foreign air.”\n\nI thanked him for his valuable advice, and asked him what Herbert had done?\n\n“Mr. Herbert,” said Wemmick, “after being all of a heap for half an hour, struck out a plan. He mentioned to me as a secret, that he is courting a young lady who has, as no doubt you are aware, a bedridden Pa. Which Pa, having been in the Purser line of life, lies a-bed in a bow-window where he can see the ships sail up and down the river. You are acquainted with the young lady, most probably?”\n\n“Not personally,” said I.\n\nThe truth was, that she had objected to me as an expensive companion who did Herbert no good, and that, when Herbert had first proposed to present me to her, she had received the proposal with such very moderate warmth, that Herbert had felt himself obliged to confide the state of the case to me, with a view to the lapse of a little time before I made her acquaintance. When I had begun to advance Herbert’s prospects by stealth, I had been able to bear this with cheerful philosophy: he and his affianced, for their part, had naturally not been very anxious to introduce a third person into their interviews; and thus, although I was assured that I had risen in Clara’s esteem, and although the young lady and I had long regularly interchanged messages and remembrances by Herbert, I had never seen her. However, I did not trouble Wemmick with these particulars.\n\n“The house with the bow-window,” said Wemmick, “being by the river-side, down the Pool there between Limehouse and Greenwich, and being kept, it seems, by a very respectable widow who has a furnished upper floor to let, Mr. Herbert put it to me, what did I think of that as a temporary tenement for Tom, Jack, or Richard? Now, I thought very well of it, for three reasons I’ll give you. That is to say: Firstly. It’s altogether out of all your beats, and is well away from the usual heap of streets great and small. Secondly. Without going near it yourself, you could always hear of the safety of Tom, Jack, or Richard, through Mr. Herbert. Thirdly. After a while and when it might be prudent, if you should want to slip Tom, Jack, or Richard on board a foreign packet-boat, there he is—ready.”\n\nMuch comforted by these considerations, I thanked Wemmick again and again, and begged him to proceed.\n\n“Well, sir! Mr. Herbert threw himself into the business with a will, and by nine o’clock last night he housed Tom, Jack, or Richard,—whichever it may be,—you and I don’t want to know,—quite successfully. At the old lodgings it was understood that he was summoned to Dover, and, in fact, he was taken down the Dover road and cornered out of it. Now, another great advantage of all this is, that it was done without you, and when, if any one was concerning himself about your movements, you must be known to be ever so many miles off and quite otherwise engaged. This diverts suspicion and confuses it; and for the same reason I recommended that, even if you came back last night, you should not go home. It brings in more confusion, and you want confusion.”\n\nWemmick, having finished his breakfast, here looked at his watch, and began to get his coat on.\n\n“And now, Mr. Pip,” said he, with his hands still in the sleeves, “I have probably done the most I can do; but if I can ever do more,—from a Walworth point of view, and in a strictly private and personal capacity,—I shall be glad to do it. Here’s the address. There can be no harm in your going here to-night, and seeing for yourself that all is well with Tom, Jack, or Richard, before you go home,—which is another reason for your not going home last night. But, after you have gone home, don’t go back here. You are very welcome, I am sure, Mr. Pip”; his hands were now out of his sleeves, and I was shaking them; “and let me finally impress one important point upon you.” He laid his hands upon my shoulders, and added in a solemn whisper: “Avail yourself of this evening to lay hold of his portable property. You don’t know what may happen to him. Don’t let anything happen to the portable property.”\n\nQuite despairing of making my mind clear to Wemmick on this point, I forbore to try.\n\n“Time’s up,” said Wemmick, “and I must be off. If you had nothing more pressing to do than to keep here till dark, that’s what I should advise. You look very much worried, and it would do you good to have a perfectly quiet day with the Aged,—he’ll be up presently,—and a little bit of—you remember the pig?”\n\n“Of course,” said I.\n\n“Well; and a little bit of him. That sausage you toasted was his, and he was in all respects a first-rater. Do try him, if it is only for old acquaintance sake. Good-bye, Aged Parent!” in a cheery shout.\n\n“All right, John; all right, my boy!” piped the old man from within.\n\nI soon fell asleep before Wemmick’s fire, and the Aged and I enjoyed one another’s society by falling asleep before it more or less all day. We had loin of pork for dinner, and greens grown on the estate; and I nodded at the Aged with a good intention whenever I failed to do it drowsily. When it was quite dark, I left the Aged preparing the fire for toast; and I inferred from the number of teacups, as well as from his glances at the two little doors in the wall, that Miss Skiffins was expected.\n\n# Chapter XLVI.\n\nEight o’clock had struck before I got into the air, that was scented, not disagreeably, by the chips and shavings of the long-shore boat-builders, and mast, oar, and block makers. All that water-side region of the upper and lower Pool below Bridge was unknown ground to me; and when I struck down by the river, I found that the spot I wanted was not where I had supposed it to be, and was anything but easy to find. It was called Mill Pond Bank, Chinks’s Basin; and I had no other guide to Chinks’s Basin than the Old Green Copper Rope-walk.\n\nIt matters not what stranded ships repairing in dry docks I lost myself among, what old hulls of ships in course of being knocked to pieces, what ooze and slime and other dregs of tide, what yards of ship-builders and ship-breakers, what rusty anchors blindly biting into the ground, though for years off duty, what mountainous country of accumulated casks and timber, how many rope-walks that were not the Old Green Copper. After several times falling short of my destination and as often overshooting it, I came unexpectedly round a corner, upon Mill Pond Bank. It was a fresh kind of place, all circumstances considered, where the wind from the river had room to turn itself round; and there were two or three trees in it, and there was the stump of a ruined windmill, and there was the Old Green Copper Rope-walk,—whose long and narrow vista I could trace in the moonlight, along a series of wooden frames set in the ground, that looked like superannuated haymaking-rakes which had grown old and lost most of their teeth.\n\nSelecting from the few queer houses upon Mill Pond Bank a house with a wooden front and three stories of bow-window (not bay-window, which is another thing), I looked at the plate upon the door, and read there, Mrs. Whimple. That being the name I wanted, I knocked, and an elderly woman of a pleasant and thriving appearance responded. She was immediately deposed, however, by Herbert, who silently led me into the parlour and shut the door. It was an odd sensation to see his very familiar face established quite at home in that very unfamiliar room and region; and I found myself looking at him, much as I looked at the corner-cupboard with the glass and china, the shells upon the chimney-piece, and the coloured engravings on the wall, representing the death of Captain Cook, a ship-launch, and his Majesty King George the Third in a state coachman’s wig, leather-breeches, and top-boots, on the terrace at Windsor.\n\n“All is well, Handel,” said Herbert, “and he is quite satisfied, though eager to see you. My dear girl is with her father; and if you’ll wait till she comes down, I’ll make you known to her, and then we’ll go upstairs. That’s her father.”\n\nI had become aware of an alarming growling overhead, and had probably expressed the fact in my countenance.\n\n“I am afraid he is a sad old rascal,” said Herbert, smiling, “but I have never seen him. Don’t you smell rum? He is always at it.”\n\n“At rum?” said I.\n\n“Yes,” returned Herbert, “and you may suppose how mild it makes his gout. He persists, too, in keeping all the provisions upstairs in his room, and serving them out. He keeps them on shelves over his head, and will weigh them all. His room must be like a chandler’s shop.”\n\nWhile he thus spoke, the growling noise became a prolonged roar, and then died away.\n\n“What else can be the consequence,” said Herbert, in explanation, “if he will cut the cheese? A man with the gout in his right hand—and everywhere else—can’t expect to get through a Double Gloucester without hurting himself.”\n\nHe seemed to have hurt himself very much, for he gave another furious roar.\n\n“To have Provis for an upper lodger is quite a godsend to Mrs. Whimple,” said Herbert, “for of course people in general won’t stand that noise. A curious place, Handel; isn’t it?”\n\nIt was a curious place, indeed; but remarkably well kept and clean.\n\n“Mrs. Whimple,” said Herbert, when I told him so, “is the best of housewives, and I really do not know what my Clara would do without her motherly help. For, Clara has no mother of her own, Handel, and no relation in the world but old Gruffandgrim.”\n\n“Surely that’s not his name, Herbert?”\n\n“No, no,” said Herbert, “that’s my name for him. His name is Mr. Barley. But what a blessing it is for the son of my father and mother to love a girl who has no relations, and who can never bother herself or anybody else about her family!”\n\nHerbert had told me on former occasions, and now reminded me, that he first knew Miss Clara Barley when she was completing her education at an establishment at Hammersmith, and that on her being recalled home to nurse her father, he and she had confided their affection to the motherly Mrs. Whimple, by whom it had been fostered and regulated with equal kindness and discretion, ever since. It was understood that nothing of a tender nature could possibly be confided to old Barley, by reason of his being totally unequal to the consideration of any subject more psychological than Gout, Rum, and Purser’s stores.\n\nAs we were thus conversing in a low tone while Old Barley’s sustained growl vibrated in the beam that crossed the ceiling, the room door opened, and a very pretty, slight, dark-eyed girl of twenty or so came in with a basket in her hand: whom Herbert tenderly relieved of the basket, and presented, blushing, as “Clara.” She really was a most charming girl, and might have passed for a captive fairy, whom that truculent Ogre, Old Barley, had pressed into his service.\n\n“Look here,” said Herbert, showing me the basket, with a compassionate and tender smile, after we had talked a little; “here’s poor Clara’s supper, served out every night. Here’s her allowance of bread, and here’s her slice of cheese, and here’s her rum,—which I drink. This is Mr. Barley’s breakfast for to-morrow, served out to be cooked. Two mutton-chops, three potatoes, some split peas, a little flour, two ounces of butter, a pinch of salt, and all this black pepper. It’s stewed up together, and taken hot, and it’s a nice thing for the gout, I should think!”\n\nThere was something so natural and winning in Clara’s resigned way of looking at these stores in detail, as Herbert pointed them out; and something so confiding, loving, and innocent in her modest manner of yielding herself to Herbert’s embracing arm; and something so gentle in her, so much needing protection on Mill Pond Bank, by Chinks’s Basin, and the Old Green Copper Rope-walk, with Old Barley growling in the beam,—that I would not have undone the engagement between her and Herbert for all the money in the pocket-book I had never opened.\n\nI was looking at her with pleasure and admiration, when suddenly the growl swelled into a roar again, and a frightful bumping noise was heard above, as if a giant with a wooden leg were trying to bore it through the ceiling to come at us. Upon this Clara said to Herbert, “Papa wants me, darling!” and ran away.\n\n“There is an unconscionable old shark for you!” said Herbert. “What do you suppose he wants now, Handel?”\n\n“I don’t know,” said I. “Something to drink?”\n\n“That’s it!” cried Herbert, as if I had made a guess of extraordinary merit. “He keeps his grog ready mixed in a little tub on the table. Wait a moment, and you’ll hear Clara lift him up to take some. There he goes!” Another roar, with a prolonged shake at the end. “Now,” said Herbert, as it was succeeded by silence, “he’s drinking. Now,” said Herbert, as the growl resounded in the beam once more, “he’s down again on his back!”\n\nClara returned soon afterwards, and Herbert accompanied me upstairs to see our charge. As we passed Mr. Barley’s door, he was heard hoarsely muttering within, in a strain that rose and fell like wind, the following Refrain, in which I substitute good wishes for something quite the reverse:—\n\n“Ahoy! Bless your eyes, here’s old Bill Barley. Here’s old Bill Barley, bless your eyes. Here’s old Bill Barley on the flat of his back, by the Lord. Lying on the flat of his back like a drifting old dead flounder, here’s your old Bill Barley, bless your eyes. Ahoy! Bless you.”\n\nIn this strain of consolation, Herbert informed me the invisible Barley would commune with himself by the day and night together; Often, while it was light, having, at the same time, one eye at a telescope which was fitted on his bed for the convenience of sweeping the river.\n\nIn his two cabin rooms at the top of the house, which were fresh and airy, and in which Mr. Barley was less audible than below, I found Provis comfortably settled. He expressed no alarm, and seemed to feel none that was worth mentioning; but it struck me that he was softened,—indefinably, for I could not have said how, and could never afterwards recall how when I tried, but certainly.\n\nThe opportunity that the day’s rest had given me for reflection had resulted in my fully determining to say nothing to him respecting Compeyson. For anything I knew, his animosity towards the man might otherwise lead to his seeking him out and rushing on his own destruction. Therefore, when Herbert and I sat down with him by his fire, I asked him first of all whether he relied on Wemmick’s judgment and sources of information?\n\n“Ay, ay, dear boy!” he answered, with a grave nod, “Jaggers knows.”\n\n“Then, I have talked with Wemmick,” said I, “and have come to tell you what caution he gave me and what advice.”\n\nThis I did accurately, with the reservation just mentioned; and I told him how Wemmick had heard, in Newgate prison (whether from officers or prisoners I could not say), that he was under some suspicion, and that my chambers had been watched; how Wemmick had recommended his keeping close for a time, and my keeping away from him; and what Wemmick had said about getting him abroad. I added, that of course, when the time came, I should go with him, or should follow close upon him, as might be safest in Wemmick’s judgment. What was to follow that I did not touch upon; neither, indeed, was I at all clear or comfortable about it in my own mind, now that I saw him in that softer condition, and in declared peril for my sake. As to altering my way of living by enlarging my expenses, I put it to him whether in our present unsettled and difficult circumstances, it would not be simply ridiculous, if it were no worse?\n\nHe could not deny this, and indeed was very reasonable throughout. His coming back was a venture, he said, and he had always known it to be a venture. He would do nothing to make it a desperate venture, and he had very little fear of his safety with such good help.\n\nHerbert, who had been looking at the fire and pondering, here said that something had come into his thoughts arising out of Wemmick’s suggestion, which it might be worth while to pursue. “We are both good watermen, Handel, and could take him down the river ourselves when the right time comes. No boat would then be hired for the purpose, and no boatmen; that would save at least a chance of suspicion, and any chance is worth saving. Never mind the season; don’t you think it might be a good thing if you began at once to keep a boat at the Temple stairs, and were in the habit of rowing up and down the river? You fall into that habit, and then who notices or minds? Do it twenty or fifty times, and there is nothing special in your doing it the twenty-first or fifty-first.”\n\nI liked this scheme, and Provis was quite elated by it. We agreed that it should be carried into execution, and that Provis should never recognise us if we came below Bridge, and rowed past Mill Pond Bank. But we further agreed that he should pull down the blind in that part of his window which gave upon the east, whenever he saw us and all was right.\n\nOur conference being now ended, and everything arranged, I rose to go; remarking to Herbert that he and I had better not go home together, and that I would take half an hour’s start of him. “I don’t like to leave you here,” I said to Provis, “though I cannot doubt your being safer here than near me. Good-bye!”\n\n“Dear boy,” he answered, clasping my hands, “I don’t know when we may meet again, and I don’t like good-bye. Say good-night!”\n\n“Good-night! Herbert will go regularly between us, and when the time comes you may be certain I shall be ready. Good-night, good-night!”\n\nWe thought it best that he should stay in his own rooms; and we left him on the landing outside his door, holding a light over the stair-rail to light us downstairs. Looking back at him, I thought of the first night of his return, when our positions were reversed, and when I little supposed my heart could ever be as heavy and anxious at parting from him as it was now.\n\nOld Barley was growling and swearing when we repassed his door, with no appearance of having ceased or of meaning to cease. When we got to the foot of the stairs, I asked Herbert whether he had preserved the name of Provis. He replied, certainly not, and that the lodger was Mr. Campbell. He also explained that the utmost known of Mr. Campbell there was, that he (Herbert) had Mr. Campbell consigned to him, and felt a strong personal interest in his being well cared for, and living a secluded life. So, when we went into the parlour where Mrs. Whimple and Clara were seated at work, I said nothing of my own interest in Mr. Campbell, but kept it to myself.\n\nWhen I had taken leave of the pretty, gentle, dark-eyed girl, and of the motherly woman who had not outlived her honest sympathy with a little affair of true love, I felt as if the Old Green Copper Rope-walk had grown quite a different place. Old Barley might be as old as the hills, and might swear like a whole field of troopers, but there were redeeming youth and trust and hope enough in Chinks’s Basin to fill it to overflowing. And then I thought of Estella, and of our parting, and went home very sadly.\n\nAll things were as quiet in the Temple as ever I had seen them. The windows of the rooms on that side, lately occupied by Provis, were dark and still, and there was no lounger in Garden Court. I walked past the fountain twice or thrice before I descended the steps that were between me and my rooms, but I was quite alone. Herbert, coming to my bedside when he came in,—for I went straight to bed, dispirited and fatigued,—made the same report. Opening one of the windows after that, he looked out into the moonlight, and told me that the pavement was as solemnly empty as the pavement of any cathedral at that same hour.\n\nNext day I set myself to get the boat. It was soon done, and the boat was brought round to the Temple stairs, and lay where I could reach her within a minute or two. Then, I began to go out as for training and practice: sometimes alone, sometimes with Herbert. I was often out in cold, rain, and sleet, but nobody took much note of me after I had been out a few times. At first, I kept above Blackfriars Bridge; but as the hours of the tide changed, I took towards London Bridge. It was Old London Bridge in those days, and at certain states of the tide there was a race and fall of water there which gave it a bad reputation. But I knew well enough how to ‘shoot’ the bridge after seeing it done, and so began to row about among the shipping in the Pool, and down to Erith. The first time I passed Mill Pond Bank, Herbert and I were pulling a pair of oars; and, both in going and returning, we saw the blind towards the east come down. Herbert was rarely there less frequently than three times in a week, and he never brought me a single word of intelligence that was at all alarming. Still, I knew that there was cause for alarm, and I could not get rid of the notion of being watched. Once received, it is a haunting idea; how many undesigning persons I suspected of watching me, it would be hard to calculate.\n\nIn short, I was always full of fears for the rash man who was in hiding. Herbert had sometimes said to me that he found it pleasant to stand at one of our windows after dark, when the tide was running down, and to think that it was flowing, with everything it bore, towards Clara. But I thought with dread that it was flowing towards Magwitch, and that any black mark on its surface might be his pursuers, going swiftly, silently, and surely, to take him.\n\n# Chapter XLVII.\n\nSome weeks passed without bringing any change. We waited for Wemmick, and he made no sign. If I had never known him out of Little Britain, and had never enjoyed the privilege of being on a familiar footing at the Castle, I might have doubted him; not so for a moment, knowing him as I did.\n\nMy worldly affairs began to wear a gloomy appearance, and I was pressed for money by more than one creditor. Even I myself began to know the want of money (I mean of ready money in my own pocket), and to relieve it by converting some easily spared articles of jewelery into cash. But I had quite determined that it would be a heartless fraud to take more money from my patron in the existing state of my uncertain thoughts and plans. Therefore, I had sent him the unopened pocket-book by Herbert, to hold in his own keeping, and I felt a kind of satisfaction—whether it was a false kind or a true, I hardly know—in not having profited by his generosity since his revelation of himself.\n\nAs the time wore on, an impression settled heavily upon me that Estella was married. Fearful of having it confirmed, though it was all but a conviction, I avoided the newspapers, and begged Herbert (to whom I had confided the circumstances of our last interview) never to speak of her to me. Why I hoarded up this last wretched little rag of the robe of hope that was rent and given to the winds, how do I know? Why did you who read this, commit that not dissimilar inconsistency of your own last year, last month, last week?\n\nIt was an unhappy life that I lived; and its one dominant anxiety, towering over all its other anxieties, like a high mountain above a range of mountains, never disappeared from my view. Still, no new cause for fear arose. Let me start from my bed as I would, with the terror fresh upon me that he was discovered; let me sit listening, as I would with dread, for Herbert’s returning step at night, lest it should be fleeter than ordinary, and winged with evil news,—for all that, and much more to like purpose, the round of things went on. Condemned to inaction and a state of constant restlessness and suspense, I rowed about in my boat, and waited, waited, waited, as I best could.\n\nThere were states of the tide when, having been down the river, I could not get back through the eddy-chafed arches and starlings of old London Bridge; then, I left my boat at a wharf near the Custom House, to be brought up afterwards to the Temple stairs. I was not averse to doing this, as it served to make me and my boat a commoner incident among the water-side people there. From this slight occasion sprang two meetings that I have now to tell of.\n\nOne afternoon, late in the month of February, I came ashore at the wharf at dusk. I had pulled down as far as Greenwich with the ebb tide, and had turned with the tide. It had been a fine bright day, but had become foggy as the sun dropped, and I had had to feel my way back among the shipping, pretty carefully. Both in going and returning, I had seen the signal in his window, All well.\n\nAs it was a raw evening, and I was cold, I thought I would comfort myself with dinner at once; and as I had hours of dejection and solitude before me if I went home to the Temple, I thought I would afterwards go to the play. The theatre where Mr. Wopsle had achieved his questionable triumph was in that water-side neighbourhood (it is nowhere now), and to that theatre I resolved to go. I was aware that Mr. Wopsle had not succeeded in reviving the Drama, but, on the contrary, had rather partaken of its decline. He had been ominously heard of, through the play-bills, as a faithful Black, in connection with a little girl of noble birth, and a monkey. And Herbert had seen him as a predatory Tartar of comic propensities, with a face like a red brick, and an outrageous hat all over bells.\n\nI dined at what Herbert and I used to call a geographical chop-house, where there were maps of the world in porter-pot rims on every half-yard of the tablecloths, and charts of gravy on every one of the knives,—to this day there is scarcely a single chop-house within the Lord Mayor’s dominions which is not geographical,—and wore out the time in dozing over crumbs, staring at gas, and baking in a hot blast of dinners. By and by, I roused myself, and went to the play.\n\nThere, I found a virtuous boatswain in His Majesty’s service,—a most excellent man, though I could have wished his trousers not quite so tight in some places, and not quite so loose in others,—who knocked all the little men’s hats over their eyes, though he was very generous and brave, and who wouldn’t hear of anybody’s paying taxes, though he was very patriotic. He had a bag of money in his pocket, like a pudding in the cloth, and on that property married a young person in bed-furniture, with great rejoicings; the whole population of Portsmouth (nine in number at the last census) turning out on the beach to rub their own hands and shake everybody else’s, and sing “Fill, fill!” A certain dark-complexioned Swab, however, who wouldn’t fill, or do anything else that was proposed to him, and whose heart was openly stated (by the boatswain) to be as black as his figure-head, proposed to two other Swabs to get all mankind into difficulties; which was so effectually done (the Swab family having considerable political influence) that it took half the evening to set things right, and then it was only brought about through an honest little grocer with a white hat, black gaiters, and red nose, getting into a clock, with a gridiron, and listening, and coming out, and knocking everybody down from behind with the gridiron whom he couldn’t confute with what he had overheard. This led to Mr. Wopsle’s (who had never been heard of before) coming in with a star and garter on, as a plenipotentiary of great power direct from the Admiralty, to say that the Swabs were all to go to prison on the spot, and that he had brought the boatswain down the Union Jack, as a slight acknowledgment of his public services. The boatswain, unmanned for the first time, respectfully dried his eyes on the Jack, and then cheering up, and addressing Mr. Wopsle as Your Honour, solicited permission to take him by the fin. Mr. Wopsle, conceding his fin with a gracious dignity, was immediately shoved into a dusty corner, while everybody danced a hornpipe; and from that corner, surveying the public with a discontented eye, became aware of me.\n\nThe second piece was the last new grand comic Christmas pantomime, in the first scene of which, it pained me to suspect that I detected Mr. Wopsle with red worsted legs under a highly magnified phosphoric countenance and a shock of red curtain-fringe for his hair, engaged in the manufacture of thunderbolts in a mine, and displaying great cowardice when his gigantic master came home (very hoarse) to dinner. But he presently presented himself under worthier circumstances; for, the Genius of Youthful Love being in want of assistance,—on account of the parental brutality of an ignorant farmer who opposed the choice of his daughter’s heart, by purposely falling upon the object, in a flour-sack, out of the first-floor window,—summoned a sententious Enchanter; and he, coming up from the antipodes rather unsteadily, after an apparently violent journey, proved to be Mr. Wopsle in a high-crowned hat, with a necromantic work in one volume under his arm. The business of this enchanter on earth being principally to be talked at, sung at, butted at, danced at, and flashed at with fires of various colours, he had a good deal of time on his hands. And I observed, with great surprise, that he devoted it to staring in my direction as if he were lost in amazement.\n\nThere was something so remarkable in the increasing glare of Mr. Wopsle’s eye, and he seemed to be turning so many things over in his mind and to grow so confused, that I could not make it out. I sat thinking of it long after he had ascended to the clouds in a large watch-case, and still I could not make it out. I was still thinking of it when I came out of the theatre an hour afterwards, and found him waiting for me near the door.\n\n“How do you do?” said I, shaking hands with him as we turned down the street together. “I saw that you saw me.”\n\n“Saw you, Mr. Pip!” he returned. “Yes, of course I saw you. But who else was there?”\n\n“Who else?”\n\n“It is the strangest thing,” said Mr. Wopsle, drifting into his lost look again; “and yet I could swear to him.”\n\nBecoming alarmed, I entreated Mr. Wopsle to explain his meaning.\n\n“Whether I should have noticed him at first but for your being there,” said Mr. Wopsle, going on in the same lost way, “I can’t be positive; yet I think I should.”\n\nInvoluntarily I looked round me, as I was accustomed to look round me when I went home; for these mysterious words gave me a chill.\n\n“Oh! He can’t be in sight,” said Mr. Wopsle. “He went out before I went off. I saw him go.”\n\nHaving the reason that I had for being suspicious, I even suspected this poor actor. I mistrusted a design to entrap me into some admission. Therefore I glanced at him as we walked on together, but said nothing.\n\n“I had a ridiculous fancy that he must be with you, Mr. Pip, till I saw that you were quite unconscious of him, sitting behind you there like a ghost.”\n\nMy former chill crept over me again, but I was resolved not to speak yet, for it was quite consistent with his words that he might be set on to induce me to connect these references with Provis. Of course, I was perfectly sure and safe that Provis had not been there.\n\n“I dare say you wonder at me, Mr. Pip; indeed, I see you do. But it is so very strange! You’ll hardly believe what I am going to tell you. I could hardly believe it myself, if you told me.”\n\n“Indeed?” said I.\n\n“No, indeed. Mr. Pip, you remember in old times a certain Christmas Day, when you were quite a child, and I dined at Gargery’s, and some soldiers came to the door to get a pair of handcuffs mended?”\n\n“I remember it very well.”\n\n“And you remember that there was a chase after two convicts, and that we joined in it, and that Gargery took you on his back, and that I took the lead, and you kept up with me as well as you could?”\n\n“I remember it all very well.” Better than he thought,—except the last clause.\n\n“And you remember that we came up with the two in a ditch, and that there was a scuffle between them, and that one of them had been severely handled and much mauled about the face by the other?”\n\n“I see it all before me.”\n\n“And that the soldiers lighted torches, and put the two in the centre, and that we went on to see the last of them, over the black marshes, with the torchlight shining on their faces,—I am particular about that,—with the torchlight shining on their faces, when there was an outer ring of dark night all about us?”\n\n“Yes,” said I. “I remember all that.”\n\n“Then, Mr. Pip, one of those two prisoners sat behind you tonight. I saw him over your shoulder.”\n\n“Steady!” I thought. I asked him then, “Which of the two do you suppose you saw?”\n\n“The one who had been mauled,” he answered readily, “and I’ll swear I saw him! The more I think of him, the more certain I am of him.”\n\n“This is very curious!” said I, with the best assumption I could put on of its being nothing more to me. “Very curious indeed!”\n\nI cannot exaggerate the enhanced disquiet into which this conversation threw me, or the special and peculiar terror I felt at Compeyson’s having been behind me “like a ghost.” For if he had ever been out of my thoughts for a few moments together since the hiding had begun, it was in those very moments when he was closest to me; and to think that I should be so unconscious and off my guard after all my care was as if I had shut an avenue of a hundred doors to keep him out, and then had found him at my elbow. I could not doubt, either, that he was there, because I was there, and that, however slight an appearance of danger there might be about us, danger was always near and active.\n\nI put such questions to Mr. Wopsle as, When did the man come in? He could not tell me that; he saw me, and over my shoulder he saw the man. It was not until he had seen him for some time that he began to identify him; but he had from the first vaguely associated him with me, and known him as somehow belonging to me in the old village time. How was he dressed? Prosperously, but not noticeably otherwise; he thought, in black. Was his face at all disfigured? No, he believed not. I believed not too, for, although in my brooding state I had taken no especial notice of the people behind me, I thought it likely that a face at all disfigured would have attracted my attention.\n\nWhen Mr. Wopsle had imparted to me all that he could recall or I extract, and when I had treated him to a little appropriate refreshment, after the fatigues of the evening, we parted. It was between twelve and one o’clock when I reached the Temple, and the gates were shut. No one was near me when I went in and went home.\n\nHerbert had come in, and we held a very serious council by the fire. But there was nothing to be done, saving to communicate to Wemmick what I had that night found out, and to remind him that we waited for his hint. As I thought that I might compromise him if I went too often to the Castle, I made this communication by letter. I wrote it before I went to bed, and went out and posted it; and again no one was near me. Herbert and I agreed that we could do nothing else but be very cautious. And we were very cautious indeed,—more cautious than before, if that were possible,—and I for my part never went near Chinks’s Basin, except when I rowed by, and then I only looked at Mill Pond Bank as I looked at anything else.\n\n# Chapter XLVIII.\n\nThe second of the two meetings referred to in the last chapter occurred about a week after the first. I had again left my boat at the wharf below Bridge; the time was an hour earlier in the afternoon; and, undecided where to dine, I had strolled up into Cheapside, and was strolling along it, surely the most unsettled person in all the busy concourse, when a large hand was laid upon my shoulder by some one overtaking me. It was Mr. Jaggers’s hand, and he passed it through my arm.\n\n“As we are going in the same direction, Pip, we may walk together. Where are you bound for?”\n\n“For the Temple, I think,” said I.\n\n“Don’t you know?” said Mr. Jaggers.\n\n“Well,” I returned, glad for once to get the better of him in cross-examination, “I do not know, for I have not made up my mind.”\n\n“You are going to dine?” said Mr. Jaggers. “You don’t mind admitting that, I suppose?”\n\n“No,” I returned, “I don’t mind admitting that.”\n\n“And are not engaged?”\n\n“I don’t mind admitting also that I am not engaged.”\n\n“Then,” said Mr. Jaggers, “come and dine with me.”\n\nI was going to excuse myself, when he added, “Wemmick’s coming.” So I changed my excuse into an acceptance,—the few words I had uttered, serving for the beginning of either,—and we went along Cheapside and slanted off to Little Britain, while the lights were springing up brilliantly in the shop windows, and the street lamp-lighters, scarcely finding ground enough to plant their ladders on in the midst of the afternoon’s bustle, were skipping up and down and running in and out, opening more red eyes in the gathering fog than my rushlight tower at the Hummums had opened white eyes in the ghostly wall.\n\nAt the office in Little Britain there was the usual letter-writing, hand-washing, candle-snuffing, and safe-locking, that closed the business of the day. As I stood idle by Mr. Jaggers’s fire, its rising and falling flame made the two casts on the shelf look as if they were playing a diabolical game at bo-peep with me; while the pair of coarse, fat office candles that dimly lighted Mr. Jaggers as he wrote in a corner were decorated with dirty winding-sheets, as if in remembrance of a host of hanged clients.\n\nWe went to Gerrard Street, all three together, in a hackney-coach: And, as soon as we got there, dinner was served. Although I should not have thought of making, in that place, the most distant reference by so much as a look to Wemmick’s Walworth sentiments, yet I should have had no objection to catching his eye now and then in a friendly way. But it was not to be done. He turned his eyes on Mr. Jaggers whenever he raised them from the table, and was as dry and distant to me as if there were twin Wemmicks, and this was the wrong one.\n\n“Did you send that note of Miss Havisham’s to Mr. Pip, Wemmick?” Mr. Jaggers asked, soon after we began dinner.\n\n“No, sir,” returned Wemmick; “it was going by post, when you brought Mr. Pip into the office. Here it is.” He handed it to his principal instead of to me.\n\n“It’s a note of two lines, Pip,” said Mr. Jaggers, handing it on, “sent up to me by Miss Havisham on account of her not being sure of your address. She tells me that she wants to see you on a little matter of business you mentioned to her. You’ll go down?”\n\n“Yes,” said I, casting my eyes over the note, which was exactly in those terms.\n\n“When do you think of going down?”\n\n“I have an impending engagement,” said I, glancing at Wemmick, who was putting fish into the post-office, “that renders me rather uncertain of my time. At once, I think.”\n\n“If Mr. Pip has the intention of going at once,” said Wemmick to Mr. Jaggers, “he needn’t write an answer, you know.”\n\nReceiving this as an intimation that it was best not to delay, I settled that I would go to-morrow, and said so. Wemmick drank a glass of wine, and looked with a grimly satisfied air at Mr. Jaggers, but not at me.\n\n“So, Pip! Our friend the Spider,” said Mr. Jaggers, “has played his cards. He has won the pool.”\n\nIt was as much as I could do to assent.\n\n“Hah! He is a promising fellow—in his way—but he may not have it all his own way. The stronger will win in the end, but the stronger has to be found out first. If he should turn to, and beat her—”\n\n“Surely,” I interrupted, with a burning face and heart, “you do not seriously think that he is scoundrel enough for that, Mr. Jaggers?”\n\n“I didn’t say so, Pip. I am putting a case. If he should turn to and beat her, he may possibly get the strength on his side; if it should be a question of intellect, he certainly will not. It would be chance work to give an opinion how a fellow of that sort will turn out in such circumstances, because it’s a toss-up between two results.”\n\n“May I ask what they are?”\n\n“A fellow like our friend the Spider,” answered Mr. Jaggers, “either beats or cringes. He may cringe and growl, or cringe and not growl; but he either beats or cringes. Ask Wemmick his opinion.”\n\n“Either beats or cringes,” said Wemmick, not at all addressing himself to me.\n\n“So here’s to Mrs. Bentley Drummle,” said Mr. Jaggers, taking a decanter of choicer wine from his dumb-waiter, and filling for each of us and for himself, “and may the question of supremacy be settled to the lady’s satisfaction! To the satisfaction of the lady and the gentleman, it never will be. Now, Molly, Molly, Molly, Molly, how slow you are to-day!”\n\nShe was at his elbow when he addressed her, putting a dish upon the table. As she withdrew her hands from it, she fell back a step or two, nervously muttering some excuse. And a certain action of her fingers, as she spoke, arrested my attention.\n\n“What’s the matter?” said Mr. Jaggers.\n\n“Nothing. Only the subject we were speaking of,” said I, “was rather painful to me.”\n\nThe action of her fingers was like the action of knitting. She stood looking at her master, not understanding whether she was free to go, or whether he had more to say to her and would call her back if she did go. Her look was very intent. Surely, I had seen exactly such eyes and such hands on a memorable occasion very lately!\n\nHe dismissed her, and she glided out of the room. But she remained before me as plainly as if she were still there. I looked at those hands, I looked at those eyes, I looked at that flowing hair; and I compared them with other hands, other eyes, other hair, that I knew of, and with what those might be after twenty years of a brutal husband and a stormy life. I looked again at those hands and eyes of the housekeeper, and thought of the inexplicable feeling that had come over me when I last walked—not alone—in the ruined garden, and through the deserted brewery. I thought how the same feeling had come back when I saw a face looking at me, and a hand waving to me from a stage-coach window; and how it had come back again and had flashed about me like lightning, when I had passed in a carriage—not alone—through a sudden glare of light in a dark street. I thought how one link of association had helped that identification in the theatre, and how such a link, wanting before, had been riveted for me now, when I had passed by a chance swift from Estella’s name to the fingers with their knitting action, and the attentive eyes. And I felt absolutely certain that this woman was Estella’s mother.\n\nMr. Jaggers had seen me with Estella, and was not likely to have missed the sentiments I had been at no pains to conceal. He nodded when I said the subject was painful to me, clapped me on the back, put round the wine again, and went on with his dinner.\n\nOnly twice more did the housekeeper reappear, and then her stay in the room was very short, and Mr. Jaggers was sharp with her. But her hands were Estella’s hands, and her eyes were Estella’s eyes, and if she had reappeared a hundred times I could have been neither more sure nor less sure that my conviction was the truth.\n\nIt was a dull evening, for Wemmick drew his wine, when it came round, quite as a matter of business,—just as he might have drawn his salary when that came round,—and with his eyes on his chief, sat in a state of perpetual readiness for cross-examination. As to the quantity of wine, his post-office was as indifferent and ready as any other post-office for its quantity of letters. From my point of view, he was the wrong twin all the time, and only externally like the Wemmick of Walworth.\n\nWe took our leave early, and left together. Even when we were groping among Mr. Jaggers’s stock of boots for our hats, I felt that the right twin was on his way back; and we had not gone half a dozen yards down Gerrard Street in the Walworth direction, before I found that I was walking arm in arm with the right twin, and that the wrong twin had evaporated into the evening air.\n\n“Well!” said Wemmick, “that’s over! He’s a wonderful man, without his living likeness; but I feel that I have to screw myself up when I dine with him,—and I dine more comfortably unscrewed.”\n\nI felt that this was a good statement of the case, and told him so.\n\n“Wouldn’t say it to anybody but yourself,” he answered. “I know that what is said between you and me goes no further.”\n\nI asked him if he had ever seen Miss Havisham’s adopted daughter, Mrs. Bentley Drummle. He said no. To avoid being too abrupt, I then spoke of the Aged and of Miss Skiffins. He looked rather sly when I mentioned Miss Skiffins, and stopped in the street to blow his nose, with a roll of the head, and a flourish not quite free from latent boastfulness.\n\n“Wemmick,” said I, “do you remember telling me, before I first went to Mr. Jaggers’s private house, to notice that housekeeper?”\n\n“Did I?” he replied. “Ah, I dare say I did. Deuce take me,” he added, suddenly, “I know I did. I find I am not quite unscrewed yet.”\n\n“A wild beast tamed, you called her.”\n\n“And what do you call her?”\n\n“The same. How did Mr. Jaggers tame her, Wemmick?”\n\n“That’s his secret. She has been with him many a long year.”\n\n“I wish you would tell me her story. I feel a particular interest in being acquainted with it. You know that what is said between you and me goes no further.”\n\n“Well!” Wemmick replied, “I don’t know her story,—that is, I don’t know all of it. But what I do know I’ll tell you. We are in our private and personal capacities, of course.”\n\n“Of course.”\n\n“A score or so of years ago, that woman was tried at the Old Bailey for murder, and was acquitted. She was a very handsome young woman, and I believe had some gypsy blood in her. Anyhow, it was hot enough when it was up, as you may suppose.”\n\n“But she was acquitted.”\n\n“Mr. Jaggers was for her,” pursued Wemmick, with a look full of meaning, “and worked the case in a way quite astonishing. It was a desperate case, and it was comparatively early days with him then, and he worked it to general admiration; in fact, it may almost be said to have made him. He worked it himself at the police-office, day after day for many days, contending against even a committal; and at the trial where he couldn’t work it himself, sat under counsel, and—every one knew—put in all the salt and pepper. The murdered person was a woman,—a woman a good ten years older, very much larger, and very much stronger. It was a case of jealousy. They both led tramping lives, and this woman in Gerrard Street here had been married very young, over the broomstick (as we say), to a tramping man, and was a perfect fury in point of jealousy. The murdered woman,—more a match for the man, certainly, in point of years—was found dead in a barn near Hounslow Heath. There had been a violent struggle, perhaps a fight. She was bruised and scratched and torn, and had been held by the throat, at last, and choked. Now, there was no reasonable evidence to implicate any person but this woman, and on the improbabilities of her having been able to do it Mr. Jaggers principally rested his case. You may be sure,” said Wemmick, touching me on the sleeve, “that he never dwelt upon the strength of her hands then, though he sometimes does now.”\n\nI had told Wemmick of his showing us her wrists, that day of the dinner party.\n\n“Well, sir!” Wemmick went on; “it happened—happened, don’t you see?—that this woman was so very artfully dressed from the time of her apprehension, that she looked much slighter than she really was; in particular, her sleeves are always remembered to have been so skilfully contrived that her arms had quite a delicate look. She had only a bruise or two about her,—nothing for a tramp,—but the backs of her hands were lacerated, and the question was, Was it with finger-nails? Now, Mr. Jaggers showed that she had struggled through a great lot of brambles which were not as high as her face; but which she could not have got through and kept her hands out of; and bits of those brambles were actually found in her skin and put in evidence, as well as the fact that the brambles in question were found on examination to have been broken through, and to have little shreds of her dress and little spots of blood upon them here and there. But the boldest point he made was this: it was attempted to be set up, in proof of her jealousy, that she was under strong suspicion of having, at about the time of the murder, frantically destroyed her child by this man—some three years old—to revenge herself upon him. Mr. Jaggers worked that in this way: “We say these are not marks of finger-nails, but marks of brambles, and we show you the brambles. You say they are marks of finger-nails, and you set up the hypothesis that she destroyed her child. You must accept all consequences of that hypothesis. For anything we know, she may have destroyed her child, and the child in clinging to her may have scratched her hands. What then? You are not trying her for the murder of her child; why don’t you? As to this case, if you will have scratches, we say that, for anything we know, you may have accounted for them, assuming for the sake of argument that you have not invented them?” “To sum up, sir,” said Wemmick, “Mr. Jaggers was altogether too many for the jury, and they gave in.”\n\n“Has she been in his service ever since?”\n\n“Yes; but not only that,” said Wemmick, “she went into his service immediately after her acquittal, tamed as she is now. She has since been taught one thing and another in the way of her duties, but she was tamed from the beginning.”\n\n“Do you remember the sex of the child?”\n\n“Said to have been a girl.”\n\n“You have nothing more to say to me to-night?”\n\n“Nothing. I got your letter and destroyed it. Nothing.”\n\nWe exchanged a cordial good-night, and I went home, with new matter for my thoughts, though with no relief from the old.\n\n# Chapter XLIX.\n\nPutting Miss Havisham’s note in my pocket, that it might serve as my credentials for so soon reappearing at Satis House, in case her waywardness should lead her to express any surprise at seeing me, I went down again by the coach next day. But I alighted at the Halfway House, and breakfasted there, and walked the rest of the distance; for I sought to get into the town quietly by the unfrequented ways, and to leave it in the same manner.\n\nThe best light of the day was gone when I passed along the quiet echoing courts behind the High Street. The nooks of ruin where the old monks had once had their refectories and gardens, and where the strong walls were now pressed into the service of humble sheds and stables, were almost as silent as the old monks in their graves. The cathedral chimes had at once a sadder and a more remote sound to me, as I hurried on avoiding observation, than they had ever had before; so, the swell of the old organ was borne to my ears like funeral music; and the rooks, as they hovered about the grey tower and swung in the bare high trees of the priory garden, seemed to call to me that the place was changed, and that Estella was gone out of it for ever.\n\nAn elderly woman, whom I had seen before as one of the servants who lived in the supplementary house across the back courtyard, opened the gate. The lighted candle stood in the dark passage within, as of old, and I took it up and ascended the staircase alone. Miss Havisham was not in her own room, but was in the larger room across the landing. Looking in at the door, after knocking in vain, I saw her sitting on the hearth in a ragged chair, close before, and lost in the contemplation of, the ashy fire.\n\nDoing as I had often done, I went in, and stood touching the old chimney-piece, where she could see me when she raised her eyes. There was an air of utter loneliness upon her, that would have moved me to pity though she had wilfully done me a deeper injury than I could charge her with. As I stood compassionating her, and thinking how, in the progress of time, I too had come to be a part of the wrecked fortunes of that house, her eyes rested on me. She stared, and said in a low voice, “Is it real?”\n\n“It is I, Pip. Mr. Jaggers gave me your note yesterday, and I have lost no time.”\n\n“Thank you. Thank you.”\n\nAs I brought another of the ragged chairs to the hearth and sat down, I remarked a new expression on her face, as if she were afraid of me.\n\n“I want,” she said, “to pursue that subject you mentioned to me when you were last here, and to show you that I am not all stone. But perhaps you can never believe, now, that there is anything human in my heart?”\n\nWhen I said some reassuring words, she stretched out her tremulous right hand, as though she was going to touch me; but she recalled it again before I understood the action, or knew how to receive it.\n\n“You said, speaking for your friend, that you could tell me how to do something useful and good. Something that you would like done, is it not?”\n\n“Something that I would like done very much.”\n\n“What is it?”\n\nI began explaining to her that secret history of the partnership. I had not got far into it, when I judged from her looks that she was thinking in a discursive way of me, rather than of what I said. It seemed to be so; for, when I stopped speaking, many moments passed before she showed that she was conscious of the fact.\n\n“Do you break off,” she asked then, with her former air of being afraid of me, “because you hate me too much to bear to speak to me?”\n\n“No, no,” I answered, “how can you think so, Miss Havisham! I stopped because I thought you were not following what I said.”\n\n“Perhaps I was not,” she answered, putting a hand to her head. “Begin again, and let me look at something else. Stay! Now tell me.”\n\nShe set her hand upon her stick in the resolute way that sometimes was habitual to her, and looked at the fire with a strong expression of forcing herself to attend. I went on with my explanation, and told her how I had hoped to complete the transaction out of my means, but how in this I was disappointed. That part of the subject (I reminded her) involved matters which could form no part of my explanation, for they were the weighty secrets of another.\n\n“So!” said she, assenting with her head, but not looking at me. “And how much money is wanting to complete the purchase?”\n\nI was rather afraid of stating it, for it sounded a large sum. “Nine hundred pounds.”\n\n“If I give you the money for this purpose, will you keep my secret as you have kept your own?”\n\n“Quite as faithfully.”\n\n“And your mind will be more at rest?”\n\n“Much more at rest.”\n\n“Are you very unhappy now?”\n\nShe asked this question, still without looking at me, but in an unwonted tone of sympathy. I could not reply at the moment, for my voice failed me. She put her left arm across the head of her stick, and softly laid her forehead on it.\n\n“I am far from happy, Miss Havisham; but I have other causes of disquiet than any you know of. They are the secrets I have mentioned.”\n\nAfter a little while, she raised her head, and looked at the fire again.\n\n“It is noble in you to tell me that you have other causes of unhappiness. Is it true?”\n\n“Too true.”\n“Can I only serve you, Pip, by serving your friend? Regarding that as done, is there nothing I can do for you yourself?”\n\n“Nothing. I thank you for the question. I thank you even more for the tone of the question. But there is nothing.”\n\nShe presently rose from her seat, and looked about the blighted room for the means of writing. There were none there, and she took from her pocket a yellow set of ivory tablets, mounted in tarnished gold, and wrote upon them with a pencil in a case of tarnished gold that hung from her neck.\n\n“You are still on friendly terms with Mr. Jaggers?”\n\n“Quite. I dined with him yesterday.”\n\n“This is an authority to him to pay you that money, to lay out at your irresponsible discretion for your friend. I keep no money here; but if you would rather Mr. Jaggers knew nothing of the matter, I will send it to you.”\n\n“Thank you, Miss Havisham; I have not the least objection to receiving it from him.”\n\nShe read me what she had written; and it was direct and clear, and evidently intended to absolve me from any suspicion of profiting by the receipt of the money. I took the tablets from her hand, and it trembled again, and it trembled more as she took off the chain to which the pencil was attached, and put it in mine. All this she did without looking at me.\n\n“My name is on the first leaf. If you can ever write under my name, “I forgive her,” though ever so long after my broken heart is dust pray do it!”\n\n“O Miss Havisham,” said I, “I can do it now. There have been sore mistakes; and my life has been a blind and thankless one; and I want forgiveness and direction far too much, to be bitter with you.”\n\nShe turned her face to me for the first time since she had averted it, and, to my amazement, I may even add to my terror, dropped on her knees at my feet; with her folded hands raised to me in the manner in which, when her poor heart was young and fresh and whole, they must often have been raised to heaven from her mother’s side.\n\nTo see her with her white hair and her worn face kneeling at my feet gave me a shock through all my frame. I entreated her to rise, and got my arms about her to help her up; but she only pressed that hand of mine which was nearest to her grasp, and hung her head over it and wept. I had never seen her shed a tear before, and, in the hope that the relief might do her good, I bent over her without speaking. She was not kneeling now, but was down upon the ground.\n\n“O!” she cried, despairingly. “What have I done! What have I done!”\n\n“If you mean, Miss Havisham, what have you done to injure me, let me answer. Very little. I should have loved her under any circumstances. Is she married?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\nIt was a needless question, for a new desolation in the desolate house had told me so.\n\n“What have I done! What have I done!” She wrung her hands, and crushed her white hair, and returned to this cry over and over again. “What have I done!”\n\nI knew not how to answer, or how to comfort her. That she had done a grievous thing in taking an impressionable child to mould into the form that her wild resentment, spurned affection, and wounded pride found vengeance in, I knew full well. But that, in shutting out the light of day, she had shut out infinitely more; that, in seclusion, she had secluded herself from a thousand natural and healing influences; that, her mind, brooding solitary, had grown diseased, as all minds do and must and will that reverse the appointed order of their Maker, I knew equally well. And could I look upon her without compassion, seeing her punishment in the ruin she was, in her profound unfitness for this earth on which she was placed, in the vanity of sorrow which had become a master mania, like the vanity of penitence, the vanity of remorse, the vanity of unworthiness, and other monstrous vanities that have been curses in this world?\n\n“Until you spoke to her the other day, and until I saw in you a looking-glass that showed me what I once felt myself, I did not know what I had done. What have I done! What have I done!” And so again, twenty, fifty times over, What had she done!\n\n“Miss Havisham,” I said, when her cry had died away, “you may dismiss me from your mind and conscience. But Estella is a different case, and if you can ever undo any scrap of what you have done amiss in keeping a part of her right nature away from her, it will be better to do that than to bemoan the past through a hundred years.”\n\n“Yes, yes, I know it. But, Pip—my dear!” There was an earnest womanly compassion for me in her new affection. “My dear! Believe this: when she first came to me, I meant to save her from misery like my own. At first, I meant no more.”\n“Well, well!” said I. “I hope so.”\n\n“But as she grew, and promised to be very beautiful, I gradually did worse, and with my praises, and with my jewels, and with my teachings, and with this figure of myself always before her, a warning to back and point my lessons, I stole her heart away, and put ice in its place.”\n\n“Better,” I could not help saying, “to have left her a natural heart, even to be bruised or broken.”\n\nWith that, Miss Havisham looked distractedly at me for a while, and then burst out again, What had she done!\n\n“If you knew all my story,” she pleaded, “you would have some compassion for me and a better understanding of me.”\n\n“Miss Havisham,” I answered, as delicately as I could, “I believe I may say that I do know your story, and have known it ever since I first left this neighbourhood. It has inspired me with great commiseration, and I hope I understand it and its influences. Does what has passed between us give me any excuse for asking you a question relative to Estella? Not as she is, but as she was when she first came here?”\n\nShe was seated on the ground, with her arms on the ragged chair, and her head leaning on them. She looked full at me when I said this, and replied, “Go on.”\n\n“Whose child was Estella?”\n\nShe shook her head.\n\n“You don’t know?”\n\nShe shook her head again.\n\n“But Mr. Jaggers brought her here, or sent her here?”\n\n“Brought her here.”\n\n“Will you tell me how that came about?”\n\nShe answered in a low whisper and with caution: “I had been shut up in these rooms a long time (I don’t know how long; you know what time the clocks keep here), when I told him that I wanted a little girl to rear and love, and save from my fate. I had first seen him when I sent for him to lay this place waste for me; having read of him in the newspapers, before I and the world parted. He told me that he would look about him for such an orphan child. One night he brought her here asleep, and I called her Estella.”\n\n“Might I ask her age then?”\n\n“Two or three. She herself knows nothing, but that she was left an orphan and I adopted her.”\n\nSo convinced I was of that woman’s being her mother, that I wanted no evidence to establish the fact in my own mind. But, to any mind, I thought, the connection here was clear and straight.\n\nWhat more could I hope to do by prolonging the interview? I had succeeded on behalf of Herbert, Miss Havisham had told me all she knew of Estella, I had said and done what I could to ease her mind. No matter with what other words we parted; we parted.\n\nTwilight was closing in when I went downstairs into the natural air. I called to the woman who had opened the gate when I entered, that I would not trouble her just yet, but would walk round the place before leaving. For I had a presentiment that I should never be there again, and I felt that the dying light was suited to my last view of it.\n\nBy the wilderness of casks that I had walked on long ago, and on which the rain of years had fallen since, rotting them in many places, and leaving miniature swamps and pools of water upon those that stood on end, I made my way to the ruined garden. I went all round it; round by the corner where Herbert and I had fought our battle; round by the paths where Estella and I had walked. So cold, so lonely, so dreary all!\n\nTaking the brewery on my way back, I raised the rusty latch of a little door at the garden end of it, and walked through. I was going out at the opposite door,—not easy to open now, for the damp wood had started and swelled, and the hinges were yielding, and the threshold was encumbered with a growth of fungus,—when I turned my head to look back. A childish association revived with wonderful force in the moment of the slight action, and I fancied that I saw Miss Havisham hanging to the beam. So strong was the impression, that I stood under the beam shuddering from head to foot before I knew it was a fancy,—though to be sure I was there in an instant.\n\nThe mournfulness of the place and time, and the great terror of this illusion, though it was but momentary, caused me to feel an indescribable awe as I came out between the open wooden gates where I had once wrung my hair after Estella had wrung my heart. Passing on into the front courtyard, I hesitated whether to call the woman to let me out at the locked gate of which she had the key, or first to go upstairs and assure myself that Miss Havisham was as safe and well as I had left her. I took the latter course and went up.\n\nI looked into the room where I had left her, and I saw her seated in the ragged chair upon the hearth close to the fire, with her back towards me. In the moment when I was withdrawing my head to go quietly away, I saw a great flaming light spring up. In the same moment I saw her running at me, shrieking, with a whirl of fire blazing all about her, and soaring at least as many feet above her head as she was high.\n\nI had a double-caped great-coat on, and over my arm another thick coat. That I got them off, closed with her, threw her down, and got them over her; that I dragged the great cloth from the table for the same purpose, and with it dragged down the heap of rottenness in the midst, and all the ugly things that sheltered there; that we were on the ground struggling like desperate enemies, and that the closer I covered her, the more wildly she shrieked and tried to free herself,—that this occurred I knew through the result, but not through anything I felt, or thought, or knew I did. I knew nothing until I knew that we were on the floor by the great table, and that patches of tinder yet alight were floating in the smoky air, which, a moment ago, had been her faded bridal dress.\n\nThen, I looked round and saw the disturbed beetles and spiders running away over the floor, and the servants coming in with breathless cries at the door. I still held her forcibly down with all my strength, like a prisoner who might escape; and I doubt if I even knew who she was, or why we had struggled, or that she had been in flames, or that the flames were out, until I saw the patches of tinder that had been her garments no longer alight but falling in a black shower around us.\n\nShe was insensible, and I was afraid to have her moved, or even touched. Assistance was sent for, and I held her until it came, as if I unreasonably fancied (I think I did) that, if I let her go, the fire would break out again and consume her. When I got up, on the surgeon’s coming to her with other aid, I was astonished to see that both my hands were burnt; for, I had no knowledge of it through the sense of feeling.\n\nOn examination it was pronounced that she had received serious hurts, but that they of themselves were far from hopeless; the danger lay mainly in the nervous shock. By the surgeon’s directions, her bed was carried into that room and laid upon the great table, which happened to be well suited to the dressing of her injuries. When I saw her again, an hour afterwards, she lay, indeed, where I had seen her strike her stick, and had heard her say that she would lie one day.\n\nThough every vestige of her dress was burnt, as they told me, she still had something of her old ghastly bridal appearance; for, they had covered her to the throat with white cotton-wool, and as she lay with a white sheet loosely overlying that, the phantom air of something that had been and was changed was still upon her.\n\nI found, on questioning the servants, that Estella was in Paris, and I got a promise from the surgeon that he would write to her by the next post. Miss Havisham’s family I took upon myself; intending to communicate with Mr. Matthew Pocket only, and leave him to do as he liked about informing the rest. This I did next day, through Herbert, as soon as I returned to town.\n\nThere was a stage, that evening, when she spoke collectedly of what had happened, though with a certain terrible vivacity. Towards midnight she began to wander in her speech; and after that it gradually set in that she said innumerable times in a low solemn voice, “What have I done!” And then, “When she first came, I meant to save her from misery like mine.” And then, “Take the pencil and write under my name, ‘I forgive her!’” She never changed the order of these three sentences, but she sometimes left out a word in one or other of them; never putting in another word, but always leaving a blank and going on to the next word.\n\nAs I could do no service there, and as I had, nearer home, that pressing reason for anxiety and fear which even her wanderings could not drive out of my mind, I decided, in the course of the night that I would return by the early morning coach, walking on a mile or so, and being taken up clear of the town. At about six o’clock of the morning, therefore, I leaned over her and touched her lips with mine, just as they said, not stopping for being touched, “Take the pencil and write under my name, ‘I forgive her.’”\n\n# Chapter L.\n\nMy hands had been dressed twice or thrice in the night, and again in the morning. My left arm was a good deal burned to the elbow, and, less severely, as high as the shoulder; it was very painful, but the flames had set in that direction, and I felt thankful it was no worse. My right hand was not so badly burnt but that I could move the fingers. It was bandaged, of course, but much less inconveniently than my left hand and arm; those I carried in a sling; and I could only wear my coat like a cloak, loose over my shoulders and fastened at the neck. My hair had been caught by the fire, but not my head or face.\n\nWhen Herbert had been down to Hammersmith and seen his father, he came back to me at our chambers, and devoted the day to attending on me. He was the kindest of nurses, and at stated times took off the bandages, and steeped them in the cooling liquid that was kept ready, and put them on again, with a patient tenderness that I was deeply grateful for.\n\nAt first, as I lay quiet on the sofa, I found it painfully difficult, I might say impossible, to get rid of the impression of the glare of the flames, their hurry and noise, and the fierce burning smell. If I dozed for a minute, I was awakened by Miss Havisham’s cries, and by her running at me with all that height of fire above her head. This pain of the mind was much harder to strive against than any bodily pain I suffered; and Herbert, seeing that, did his utmost to hold my attention engaged.\n\nNeither of us spoke of the boat, but we both thought of it. That was made apparent by our avoidance of the subject, and by our agreeing—without agreement—to make my recovery of the use of my hands a question of so many hours, not of so many weeks.\n\nMy first question when I saw Herbert had been of course, whether all was well down the river? As he replied in the affirmative, with perfect confidence and cheerfulness, we did not resume the subject until the day was wearing away. But then, as Herbert changed the bandages, more by the light of the fire than by the outer light, he went back to it spontaneously.\n\n“I sat with Provis last night, Handel, two good hours.”\n\n“Where was Clara?”\n\n“Dear little thing!” said Herbert. “She was up and down with Gruffandgrim all the evening. He was perpetually pegging at the floor the moment she left his sight. I doubt if he can hold out long, though. What with rum and pepper,—and pepper and rum,—I should think his pegging must be nearly over.”\n\n“And then you will be married, Herbert?”\n\n“How can I take care of the dear child otherwise?—Lay your arm out upon the back of the sofa, my dear boy, and I’ll sit down here, and get the bandage off so gradually that you shall not know when it comes. I was speaking of Provis. Do you know, Handel, he improves?”\n\n“I said to you I thought he was softened when I last saw him.”\n\n“So you did. And so he is. He was very communicative last night, and told me more of his life. You remember his breaking off here about some woman that he had had great trouble with.—Did I hurt you?”\n\nI had started, but not under his touch. His words had given me a start.\n\n“I had forgotten that, Herbert, but I remember it now you speak of it.”\n\n“Well! He went into that part of his life, and a dark wild part it is. Shall I tell you? Or would it worry you just now?”\n\n“Tell me by all means. Every word.”\n\nHerbert bent forward to look at me more nearly, as if my reply had been rather more hurried or more eager than he could quite account for. “Your head is cool?” he said, touching it.\n\n“Quite,” said I. “Tell me what Provis said, my dear Herbert.”\n\n“It seems,” said Herbert, “—there’s a bandage off most charmingly, and now comes the cool one,—makes you shrink at first, my poor dear fellow, don’t it? but it will be comfortable presently,—it seems that the woman was a young woman, and a jealous woman, and a revengeful woman; revengeful, Handel, to the last degree.”\n\n“To what last degree?”\n\n“Murder.—Does it strike too cold on that sensitive place?”\n\n“I don’t feel it. How did she murder? Whom did she murder?”\n\n“Why, the deed may not have merited quite so terrible a name,” said Herbert, “but, she was tried for it, and Mr. Jaggers defended her, and the reputation of that defence first made his name known to Provis. It was another and a stronger woman who was the victim, and there had been a struggle—in a barn. Who began it, or how fair it was, or how unfair, may be doubtful; but how it ended is certainly not doubtful, for the victim was found throttled.”\n\n“Was the woman brought in guilty?”\n\n“No; she was acquitted.—My poor Handel, I hurt you!”\n\n“It is impossible to be gentler, Herbert. Yes? What else?”\n\n“This acquitted young woman and Provis had a little child; a little child of whom Provis was exceedingly fond. On the evening of the very night when the object of her jealousy was strangled as I tell you, the young woman presented herself before Provis for one moment, and swore that she would destroy the child (which was in her possession), and he should never see it again; then she vanished.—There’s the worst arm comfortably in the sling once more, and now there remains but the right hand, which is a far easier job. I can do it better by this light than by a stronger, for my hand is steadiest when I don’t see the poor blistered patches too distinctly.—You don’t think your breathing is affected, my dear boy? You seem to breathe quickly.”\n\n“Perhaps I do, Herbert. Did the woman keep her oath?”\n\n“There comes the darkest part of Provis’s life. She did.”\n\n“That is, he says she did.”\n\n“Why, of course, my dear boy,” returned Herbert, in a tone of surprise, and again bending forward to get a nearer look at me. “He says it all. I have no other information.”\n\n“No, to be sure.”\n\n“Now, whether,” pursued Herbert, “he had used the child’s mother ill, or whether he had used the child’s mother well, Provis doesn’t say; but she had shared some four or five years of the wretched life he described to us at this fireside, and he seems to have felt pity for her, and forbearance towards her. Therefore, fearing he should be called upon to depose about this destroyed child, and so be the cause of her death, he hid himself (much as he grieved for the child), kept himself dark, as he says, out of the way and out of the trial, and was only vaguely talked of as a certain man called Abel, out of whom the jealousy arose. After the acquittal she disappeared, and thus he lost the child and the child’s mother.”\n\n“I want to ask—”\n\n“A moment, my dear boy, and I have done. That evil genius, Compeyson, the worst of scoundrels among many scoundrels, knowing of his keeping out of the way at that time and of his reasons for doing so, of course afterwards held the knowledge over his head as a means of keeping him poorer and working him harder. It was clear last night that this barbed the point of Provis’s animosity.”\n\n“I want to know,” said I, “and particularly, Herbert, whether he told you when this happened?”\n\n“Particularly? Let me remember, then, what he said as to that. His expression was, ‘a round score o’ year ago, and a’most directly after I took up wi’ Compeyson.’ How old were you when you came upon him in the little churchyard?”\n\n“I think in my seventh year.”\n\n“Ay. It had happened some three or four years then, he said, and you brought into his mind the little girl so tragically lost, who would have been about your age.”\n\n“Herbert,” said I, after a short silence, in a hurried way, “can you see me best by the light of the window, or the light of the fire?”\n\n“By the firelight,” answered Herbert, coming close again.\n\n“Look at me.”\n\n“I do look at you, my dear boy.”\n\n“Touch me.”\n\n“I do touch you, my dear boy.”\n\n“You are not afraid that I am in any fever, or that my head is much disordered by the accident of last night?”\n\n“N-no, my dear boy,” said Herbert, after taking time to examine me. “You are rather excited, but you are quite yourself.”\n\n“I know I am quite myself. And the man we have in hiding down the river, is Estella’s Father.”\n\n# Chapter LI.\n\nWhat purpose I had in view when I was hot on tracing out and proving Estella’s parentage, I cannot say. It will presently be seen that the question was not before me in a distinct shape until it was put before me by a wiser head than my own.\n\nBut when Herbert and I had held our momentous conversation, I was seized with a feverish conviction that I ought to hunt the matter down,—that I ought not to let it rest, but that I ought to see Mr. Jaggers, and come at the bare truth. I really do not know whether I felt that I did this for Estella’s sake, or whether I was glad to transfer to the man in whose preservation I was so much concerned some rays of the romantic interest that had so long surrounded me. Perhaps the latter possibility may be the nearer to the truth.\n\nAny way, I could scarcely be withheld from going out to Gerrard Street that night. Herbert’s representations that, if I did, I should probably be laid up and stricken useless, when our fugitive’s safety would depend upon me, alone restrained my impatience. On the understanding, again and again reiterated, that, come what would, I was to go to Mr. Jaggers to-morrow, I at length submitted to keep quiet, and to have my hurts looked after, and to stay at home. Early next morning we went out together, and at the corner of Giltspur Street by Smithfield, I left Herbert to go his way into the City, and took my way to Little Britain.\n\nThere were periodical occasions when Mr. Jaggers and Wemmick went over the office accounts, and checked off the vouchers, and put all things straight. On these occasions, Wemmick took his books and papers into Mr. Jaggers’s room, and one of the upstairs clerks came down into the outer office. Finding such clerk on Wemmick’s post that morning, I knew what was going on; but I was not sorry to have Mr. Jaggers and Wemmick together, as Wemmick would then hear for himself that I said nothing to compromise him.\n\nMy appearance, with my arm bandaged and my coat loose over my shoulders, favoured my object. Although I had sent Mr. Jaggers a brief account of the accident as soon as I had arrived in town, yet I had to give him all the details now; and the speciality of the occasion caused our talk to be less dry and hard, and less strictly regulated by the rules of evidence, than it had been before. While I described the disaster, Mr. Jaggers stood, according to his wont, before the fire. Wemmick leaned back in his chair, staring at me, with his hands in the pockets of his trousers, and his pen put horizontally into the post. The two brutal casts, always inseparable in my mind from the official proceedings, seemed to be congestively considering whether they didn’t smell fire at the present moment.\n\nMy narrative finished, and their questions exhausted, I then produced Miss Havisham’s authority to receive the nine hundred pounds for Herbert. Mr. Jaggers’s eyes retired a little deeper into his head when I handed him the tablets, but he presently handed them over to Wemmick, with instructions to draw the check for his signature. While that was in course of being done, I looked on at Wemmick as he wrote, and Mr. Jaggers, poising and swaying himself on his well-polished boots, looked on at me. “I am sorry, Pip,” said he, as I put the check in my pocket, when he had signed it, “that we do nothing for you.”\n\n“Miss Havisham was good enough to ask me,” I returned, “whether she could do nothing for me, and I told her No.”\n\n“Everybody should know his own business,” said Mr. Jaggers. And I saw Wemmick’s lips form the words “portable property.”\n\n“I should not have told her No, if I had been you,” said Mr Jaggers; “but every man ought to know his own business best.”\n\n“Every man’s business,” said Wemmick, rather reproachfully towards me, “is portable property.”\n\nAs I thought the time was now come for pursuing the theme I had at heart, I said, turning on Mr. Jaggers:—\n\n“I did ask something of Miss Havisham, however, sir. I asked her to give me some information relative to her adopted daughter, and she gave me all she possessed.”\n\n“Did she?” said Mr. Jaggers, bending forward to look at his boots and then straightening himself. “Hah! I don’t think I should have done so, if I had been Miss Havisham. But she ought to know her own business best.”\n\n“I know more of the history of Miss Havisham’s adopted child than Miss Havisham herself does, sir. I know her mother.”\n\nMr. Jaggers looked at me inquiringly, and repeated “Mother?”\n\n“I have seen her mother within these three days.”\n\n“Yes?” said Mr. Jaggers.\n\n“And so have you, sir. And you have seen her still more recently.”\n\n“Yes?” said Mr. Jaggers.\n\n“Perhaps I know more of Estella’s history than even you do,” said I. “I know her father too.”\n\nA certain stop that Mr. Jaggers came to in his manner—he was too self-possessed to change his manner, but he could not help its being brought to an indefinably attentive stop—assured me that he did not know who her father was. This I had strongly suspected from Provis’s account (as Herbert had repeated it) of his having kept himself dark; which I pieced on to the fact that he himself was not Mr. Jaggers’s client until some four years later, and when he could have no reason for claiming his identity. But, I could not be sure of this unconsciousness on Mr. Jaggers’s part before, though I was quite sure of it now.\n\n“So! You know the young lady’s father, Pip?” said Mr. Jaggers.\n\n“Yes,” I replied, “and his name is Provis—from New South Wales.”\n\nEven Mr. Jaggers started when I said those words. It was the slightest start that could escape a man, the most carefully repressed and the sooner checked, but he did start, though he made it a part of the action of taking out his pocket-handkerchief. How Wemmick received the announcement I am unable to say; for I was afraid to look at him just then, lest Mr. Jaggers’s sharpness should detect that there had been some communication unknown to him between us.\n\n“And on what evidence, Pip,” asked Mr. Jaggers, very coolly, as he paused with his handkerchief half way to his nose, “does Provis make this claim?”\n\n“He does not make it,” said I, “and has never made it, and has no knowledge or belief that his daughter is in existence.”\n\nFor once, the powerful pocket-handkerchief failed. My reply was so unexpected, that Mr. Jaggers put the handkerchief back into his pocket without completing the usual performance, folded his arms, and looked with stern attention at me, though with an immovable face.\n\nThen I told him all I knew, and how I knew it; with the one reservation that I left him to infer that I knew from Miss Havisham what I in fact knew from Wemmick. I was very careful indeed as to that. Nor did I look towards Wemmick until I had finished all I had to tell, and had been for some time silently meeting Mr. Jaggers’s look. When I did at last turn my eyes in Wemmick’s direction, I found that he had unposted his pen, and was intent upon the table before him.\n\n“Hah!” said Mr. Jaggers at last, as he moved towards the papers on the table. “What item was it you were at, Wemmick, when Mr. Pip came in?”\n\nBut I could not submit to be thrown off in that way, and I made a passionate, almost an indignant appeal, to him to be more frank and manly with me. I reminded him of the false hopes into which I had lapsed, the length of time they had lasted, and the discovery I had made: and I hinted at the danger that weighed upon my spirits. I represented myself as being surely worthy of some little confidence from him, in return for the confidence I had just now imparted. I said that I did not blame him, or suspect him, or mistrust him, but I wanted assurance of the truth from him. And if he asked me why I wanted it, and why I thought I had any right to it, I would tell him, little as he cared for such poor dreams, that I had loved Estella dearly and long, and that although I had lost her, and must live a bereaved life, whatever concerned her was still nearer and dearer to me than anything else in the world. And seeing that Mr. Jaggers stood quite still and silent, and apparently quite obdurate, under this appeal, I turned to Wemmick, and said, “Wemmick, I know you to be a man with a gentle heart. I have seen your pleasant home, and your old father, and all the innocent, cheerful playful ways with which you refresh your business life. And I entreat you to say a word for me to Mr. Jaggers, and to represent to him that, all circumstances considered, he ought to be more open with me!”\n\nI have never seen two men look more oddly at one another than Mr. Jaggers and Wemmick did after this apostrophe. At first, a misgiving crossed me that Wemmick would be instantly dismissed from his employment; but it melted as I saw Mr. Jaggers relax into something like a smile, and Wemmick become bolder.\n\n“What’s all this?” said Mr. Jaggers. “You with an old father, and you with pleasant and playful ways?”\n\n“Well!” returned Wemmick. “If I don’t bring ’em here, what does it matter?”\n\n“Pip,” said Mr. Jaggers, laying his hand upon my arm, and smiling openly, “this man must be the most cunning impostor in all London.”\n\n“Not a bit of it,” returned Wemmick, growing bolder and bolder. “I think you’re another.”\n\nAgain they exchanged their former odd looks, each apparently still distrustful that the other was taking him in.\n\n“You with a pleasant home?” said Mr. Jaggers.\n\n“Since it don’t interfere with business,” returned Wemmick, “let it be so. Now, I look at you, sir, I shouldn’t wonder if you might be planning and contriving to have a pleasant home of your own one of these days, when you’re tired of all this work.”\n\nMr. Jaggers nodded his head retrospectively two or three times, and actually drew a sigh. “Pip,” said he, “we won’t talk about ‘poor dreams;’ you know more about such things than I, having much fresher experience of that kind. But now about this other matter. I’ll put a case to you. Mind! I admit nothing.”\n\nHe waited for me to declare that I quite understood that he expressly said that he admitted nothing.\n\n“Now, Pip,” said Mr. Jaggers, “put this case. Put the case that a woman, under such circumstances as you have mentioned, held her child concealed, and was obliged to communicate the fact to her legal adviser, on his representing to her that he must know, with an eye to the latitude of his defence, how the fact stood about that child. Put the case that, at the same time he held a trust to find a child for an eccentric rich lady to adopt and bring up.”\n\n“I follow you, sir.”\n\n“Put the case that he lived in an atmosphere of evil, and that all he saw of children was their being generated in great numbers for certain destruction. Put the case that he often saw children solemnly tried at a criminal bar, where they were held up to be seen; put the case that he habitually knew of their being imprisoned, whipped, transported, neglected, cast out, qualified in all ways for the hangman, and growing up to be hanged. Put the case that pretty nigh all the children he saw in his daily business life he had reason to look upon as so much spawn, to develop into the fish that were to come to his net,—to be prosecuted, defended, forsworn, made orphans, bedevilled somehow.”\n\n“I follow you, sir.”\n\n“Put the case, Pip, that here was one pretty little child out of the heap who could be saved; whom the father believed dead, and dared make no stir about; as to whom, over the mother, the legal adviser had this power: “I know what you did, and how you did it. You came so and so, you did such and such things to divert suspicion. I have tracked you through it all, and I tell it you all. Part with the child, unless it should be necessary to produce it to clear you, and then it shall be produced. Give the child into my hands, and I will do my best to bring you off. If you are saved, your child is saved too; if you are lost, your child is still saved.” Put the case that this was done, and that the woman was cleared.”\n\n“I understand you perfectly.”\n\n“But that I make no admissions?”\n\n“That you make no admissions.” And Wemmick repeated, “No admissions.”\n\n“Put the case, Pip, that passion and the terror of death had a little shaken the woman’s intellects, and that when she was set at liberty, she was scared out of the ways of the world, and went to him to be sheltered. Put the case that he took her in, and that he kept down the old, wild, violent nature whenever he saw an inkling of its breaking out, by asserting his power over her in the old way. Do you comprehend the imaginary case?”\n\n“Quite.”\n\n“Put the case that the child grew up, and was married for money. That the mother was still living. That the father was still living. That the mother and father, unknown to one another, were dwelling within so many miles, furlongs, yards if you like, of one another. That the secret was still a secret, except that you had got wind of it. Put that last case to yourself very carefully.”\n\n“I do.”\n“I ask Wemmick to put it to himself very carefully.”\n\nAnd Wemmick said, “I do.”\n\n“For whose sake would you reveal the secret? For the father’s? I think he would not be much the better for the mother. For the mother’s? I think if she had done such a deed she would be safer where she was. For the daughter’s? I think it would hardly serve her to establish her parentage for the information of her husband, and to drag her back to disgrace, after an escape of twenty years, pretty secure to last for life. But add the case that you had loved her, Pip, and had made her the subject of those ‘poor dreams’ which have, at one time or another, been in the heads of more men than you think likely, then I tell you that you had better—and would much sooner when you had thought well of it—chop off that bandaged left hand of yours with your bandaged right hand, and then pass the chopper on to Wemmick there, to cut that off too.”\n\nI looked at Wemmick, whose face was very grave. He gravely touched his lips with his forefinger. I did the same. Mr. Jaggers did the same. “Now, Wemmick,” said the latter then, resuming his usual manner, “what item was it you were at when Mr. Pip came in?”\n\nStanding by for a little, while they were at work, I observed that the odd looks they had cast at one another were repeated several times: with this difference now, that each of them seemed suspicious, not to say conscious, of having shown himself in a weak and unprofessional light to the other. For this reason, I suppose, they were now inflexible with one another; Mr. Jaggers being highly dictatorial, and Wemmick obstinately justifying himself whenever there was the smallest point in abeyance for a moment. I had never seen them on such ill terms; for generally they got on very well indeed together.\n\nBut they were both happily relieved by the opportune appearance of Mike, the client with the fur cap and the habit of wiping his nose on his sleeve, whom I had seen on the very first day of my appearance within those walls. This individual, who, either in his own person or in that of some member of his family, seemed to be always in trouble (which in that place meant Newgate), called to announce that his eldest daughter was taken up on suspicion of shoplifting. As he imparted this melancholy circumstance to Wemmick, Mr. Jaggers standing magisterially before the fire and taking no share in the proceedings, Mike’s eye happened to twinkle with a tear.\n\n“What are you about?” demanded Wemmick, with the utmost indignation. “What do you come snivelling here for?”\n\n“I didn’t go to do it, Mr. Wemmick.”\n\n“You did,” said Wemmick. “How dare you? You’re not in a fit state to come here, if you can’t come here without spluttering like a bad pen. What do you mean by it?”\n\n“A man can’t help his feelings, Mr. Wemmick,” pleaded Mike.\n\n“His what?” demanded Wemmick, quite savagely. “Say that again!”\n\n“Now look here my man,” said Mr. Jaggers, advancing a step, and pointing to the door. “Get out of this office. I’ll have no feelings here. Get out.”\n\n“It serves you right,” said Wemmick, “Get out.”\n\nSo, the unfortunate Mike very humbly withdrew, and Mr. Jaggers and Wemmick appeared to have re-established their good understanding, and went to work again with an air of refreshment upon them as if they had just had lunch.\n\n# Chapter LII.\n\nFrom Little Britain I went, with my check in my pocket, to Miss Skiffins’s brother, the accountant; and Miss Skiffins’s brother, the accountant, going straight to Clarriker’s and bringing Clarriker to me, I had the great satisfaction of concluding that arrangement. It was the only good thing I had done, and the only completed thing I had done, since I was first apprised of my great expectations.\n\nClarriker informing me on that occasion that the affairs of the House were steadily progressing, that he would now be able to establish a small branch-house in the East which was much wanted for the extension of the business, and that Herbert in his new partnership capacity would go out and take charge of it, I found that I must have prepared for a separation from my friend, even though my own affairs had been more settled. And now, indeed, I felt as if my last anchor were loosening its hold, and I should soon be driving with the winds and waves.\n\nBut there was recompense in the joy with which Herbert would come home of a night and tell me of these changes, little imagining that he told me no news, and would sketch airy pictures of himself conducting Clara Barley to the land of the Arabian Nights, and of me going out to join them (with a caravan of camels, I believe), and of our all going up the Nile and seeing wonders. Without being sanguine as to my own part in those bright plans, I felt that Herbert’s way was clearing fast, and that old Bill Barley had but to stick to his pepper and rum, and his daughter would soon be happily provided for.\n\nWe had now got into the month of March. My left arm, though it presented no bad symptoms, took, in the natural course, so long to heal that I was still unable to get a coat on. My right arm was tolerably restored; disfigured, but fairly serviceable.\n\nOn a Monday morning, when Herbert and I were at breakfast, I received the following letter from Wemmick by the post.\n\n“Walworth. Burn this as soon as read. Early in the week, or say Wednesday, you might do what you know of, if you felt disposed to try it. Now burn.”\n\nWhen I had shown this to Herbert and had put it in the fire—but not before we had both got it by heart—we considered what to do. For, of course my being disabled could now be no longer kept out of view.\n\n“I have thought it over again and again,” said Herbert, “and I think I know a better course than taking a Thames waterman. Take Startop. A good fellow, a skilled hand, fond of us, and enthusiastic and honourable.”\n\nI had thought of him more than once.\n\n“But how much would you tell him, Herbert?”\n\n“It is necessary to tell him very little. Let him suppose it a mere freak, but a secret one, until the morning comes: then let him know that there is urgent reason for your getting Provis aboard and away. You go with him?”\n\n“No doubt.”\n\n“Where?”\n\nIt had seemed to me, in the many anxious considerations I had given the point, almost indifferent what port we made for,—Hamburg, Rotterdam, Antwerp,—the place signified little, so that he was out of England. Any foreign steamer that fell in our way and would take us up would do. I had always proposed to myself to get him well down the river in the boat; certainly well beyond Gravesend, which was a critical place for search or inquiry if suspicion were afoot. As foreign steamers would leave London at about the time of high-water, our plan would be to get down the river by a previous ebb-tide, and lie by in some quiet spot until we could pull off to one. The time when one would be due where we lay, wherever that might be, could be calculated pretty nearly, if we made inquiries beforehand.\n\nHerbert assented to all this, and we went out immediately after breakfast to pursue our investigations. We found that a steamer for Hamburg was likely to suit our purpose best, and we directed our thoughts chiefly to that vessel. But we noted down what other foreign steamers would leave London with the same tide, and we satisfied ourselves that we knew the build and colour of each. We then separated for a few hours: I, to get at once such passports as were necessary; Herbert, to see Startop at his lodgings. We both did what we had to do without any hindrance, and when we met again at one o’clock reported it done. I, for my part, was prepared with passports; Herbert had seen Startop, and he was more than ready to join.\n\nThose two should pull a pair of oars, we settled, and I would steer; our charge would be sitter, and keep quiet; as speed was not our object, we should make way enough. We arranged that Herbert should not come home to dinner before going to Mill Pond Bank that evening; that he should not go there at all to-morrow evening, Tuesday; that he should prepare Provis to come down to some stairs hard by the house, on Wednesday, when he saw us approach, and not sooner; that all the arrangements with him should be concluded that Monday night; and that he should be communicated with no more in any way, until we took him on board.\n\nThese precautions well understood by both of us, I went home.\n\nOn opening the outer door of our chambers with my key, I found a letter in the box, directed to me; a very dirty letter, though not ill-written. It had been delivered by hand (of course, since I left home), and its contents were these:—\n\n“If you are not afraid to come to the old marshes to-night or to-morrow night at nine, and to come to the little sluice-house by the limekiln, you had better come. If you want information regarding your uncle Provis, you had much better come and tell no one, and lose no time. You must come alone. Bring this with you.”\n\nI had had load enough upon my mind before the receipt of this strange letter. What to do now, I could not tell. And the worst was, that I must decide quickly, or I should miss the afternoon coach, which would take me down in time for to-night. To-morrow night I could not think of going, for it would be too close upon the time of the flight. And again, for anything I knew, the proffered information might have some important bearing on the flight itself.\n\nIf I had had ample time for consideration, I believe I should still have gone. Having hardly any time for consideration,—my watch showing me that the coach started within half an hour,—I resolved to go. I should certainly not have gone, but for the reference to my Uncle Provis. That, coming on Wemmick’s letter and the morning’s busy preparation, turned the scale.\n\nIt is so difficult to become clearly possessed of the contents of almost any letter, in a violent hurry, that I had to read this mysterious epistle again twice, before its injunction to me to be secret got mechanically into my mind. Yielding to it in the same mechanical kind of way, I left a note in pencil for Herbert, telling him that as I should be so soon going away, I knew not for how long, I had decided to hurry down and back, to ascertain for myself how Miss Havisham was faring. I had then barely time to get my great-coat, lock up the chambers, and make for the coach-office by the short by-ways. If I had taken a hackney-chariot and gone by the streets, I should have missed my aim; going as I did, I caught the coach just as it came out of the yard. I was the only inside passenger, jolting away knee-deep in straw, when I came to myself.\n\nFor I really had not been myself since the receipt of the letter; it had so bewildered me, ensuing on the hurry of the morning. The morning hurry and flutter had been great; for, long and anxiously as I had waited for Wemmick, his hint had come like a surprise at last. And now I began to wonder at myself for being in the coach, and to doubt whether I had sufficient reason for being there, and to consider whether I should get out presently and go back, and to argue against ever heeding an anonymous communication, and, in short, to pass through all those phases of contradiction and indecision to which I suppose very few hurried people are strangers. Still, the reference to Provis by name mastered everything. I reasoned as I had reasoned already without knowing it,—if that be reasoning,—in case any harm should befall him through my not going, how could I ever forgive myself!\n\nIt was dark before we got down, and the journey seemed long and dreary to me, who could see little of it inside, and who could not go outside in my disabled state. Avoiding the Blue Boar, I put up at an inn of minor reputation down the town, and ordered some dinner. While it was preparing, I went to Satis House and inquired for Miss Havisham; she was still very ill, though considered something better.\n\nMy inn had once been a part of an ancient ecclesiastical house, and I dined in a little octagonal common-room, like a font. As I was not able to cut my dinner, the old landlord with a shining bald head did it for me. This bringing us into conversation, he was so good as to entertain me with my own story,—of course with the popular feature that Pumblechook was my earliest benefactor and the founder of my fortunes.\n\n“Do you know the young man?” said I.\n\n“Know him!” repeated the landlord. “Ever since he was—no height at all.”\n\n“Does he ever come back to this neighbourhood?”\n\n“Ay, he comes back,” said the landlord, “to his great friends, now and again, and gives the cold shoulder to the man that made him.”\n\n“What man is that?”\n\n“Him that I speak of,” said the landlord. “Mr. Pumblechook.”\n\n“Is he ungrateful to no one else?”\n\n“No doubt he would be, if he could,” returned the landlord, “but he can’t. And why? Because Pumblechook done everything for him.”\n\n“Does Pumblechook say so?”\n\n“Say so!” replied the landlord. “He han’t no call to say so.”\n\n“But does he say so?”\n\n“It would turn a man’s blood to white wine winegar to hear him tell of it, sir,” said the landlord.\n\nI thought, “Yet Joe, dear Joe, you never tell of it. Long-suffering and loving Joe, you never complain. Nor you, sweet-tempered Biddy!”\n\n“Your appetite’s been touched like by your accident,” said the landlord, glancing at the bandaged arm under my coat. “Try a tenderer bit.”\n\n“No, thank you,” I replied, turning from the table to brood over the fire. “I can eat no more. Please take it away.”\n\nI had never been struck at so keenly, for my thanklessness to Joe, as through the brazen impostor Pumblechook. The falser he, the truer Joe; the meaner he, the nobler Joe.\n\nMy heart was deeply and most deservedly humbled as I mused over the fire for an hour or more. The striking of the clock aroused me, but not from my dejection or remorse, and I got up and had my coat fastened round my neck, and went out. I had previously sought in my pockets for the letter, that I might refer to it again; but I could not find it, and was uneasy to think that it must have been dropped in the straw of the coach. I knew very well, however, that the appointed place was the little sluice-house by the limekiln on the marshes, and the hour nine. Towards the marshes I now went straight, having no time to spare.\n\n# Chapter LIII.\n\nIt was a dark night, though the full moon rose as I left the enclosed lands, and passed out upon the marshes. Beyond their dark line there was a ribbon of clear sky, hardly broad enough to hold the red large moon. In a few minutes she had ascended out of that clear field, in among the piled mountains of cloud.\n\nThere was a melancholy wind, and the marshes were very dismal. A stranger would have found them insupportable, and even to me they were so oppressive that I hesitated, half inclined to go back. But I knew them well, and could have found my way on a far darker night, and had no excuse for returning, being there. So, having come there against my inclination, I went on against it.\n\nThe direction that I took was not that in which my old home lay, nor that in which we had pursued the convicts. My back was turned towards the distant Hulks as I walked on, and, though I could see the old lights away on the spits of sand, I saw them over my shoulder. I knew the limekiln as well as I knew the old Battery, but they were miles apart; so that, if a light had been burning at each point that night, there would have been a long strip of the blank horizon between the two bright specks.\n\nAt first, I had to shut some gates after me, and now and then to stand still while the cattle that were lying in the banked-up pathway arose and blundered down among the grass and reeds. But after a little while I seemed to have the whole flats to myself.\n\nIt was another half-hour before I drew near to the kiln. The lime was burning with a sluggish stifling smell, but the fires were made up and left, and no workmen were visible. Hard by was a small stone-quarry. It lay directly in my way, and had been worked that day, as I saw by the tools and barrows that were lying about.\n\nComing up again to the marsh level out of this excavation,—for the rude path lay through it,—I saw a light in the old sluice-house. I quickened my pace, and knocked at the door with my hand. Waiting for some reply, I looked about me, noticing how the sluice was abandoned and broken, and how the house—of wood with a tiled roof—would not be proof against the weather much longer, if it were so even now, and how the mud and ooze were coated with lime, and how the choking vapour of the kiln crept in a ghostly way towards me. Still there was no answer, and I knocked again. No answer still, and I tried the latch.\n\nIt rose under my hand, and the door yielded. Looking in, I saw a lighted candle on a table, a bench, and a mattress on a truckle bedstead. As there was a loft above, I called, “Is there any one here?” but no voice answered. Then I looked at my watch, and, finding that it was past nine, called again, “Is there any one here?” There being still no answer, I went out at the door, irresolute what to do.\n\nIt was beginning to rain fast. Seeing nothing save what I had seen already, I turned back into the house, and stood just within the shelter of the doorway, looking out into the night. While I was considering that some one must have been there lately and must soon be coming back, or the candle would not be burning, it came into my head to look if the wick were long. I turned round to do so, and had taken up the candle in my hand, when it was extinguished by some violent shock; and the next thing I comprehended was, that I had been caught in a strong running noose, thrown over my head from behind.\n\n“Now,” said a suppressed voice with an oath, “I’ve got you!”\n\n“What is this?” I cried, struggling. “Who is it? Help, help, help!”\n\nNot only were my arms pulled close to my sides, but the pressure on my bad arm caused me exquisite pain. Sometimes, a strong man’s hand, sometimes a strong man’s breast, was set against my mouth to deaden my cries, and with a hot breath always close to me, I struggled ineffectually in the dark, while I was fastened tight to the wall. “And now,” said the suppressed voice with another oath, “call out again, and I’ll make short work of you!”\n\nFaint and sick with the pain of my injured arm, bewildered by the surprise, and yet conscious how easily this threat could be put in execution, I desisted, and tried to ease my arm were it ever so little. But, it was bound too tight for that. I felt as if, having been burnt before, it were now being boiled.\n\nThe sudden exclusion of the night, and the substitution of black darkness in its place, warned me that the man had closed a shutter. After groping about for a little, he found the flint and steel he wanted, and began to strike a light. I strained my sight upon the sparks that fell among the tinder, and upon which he breathed and breathed, match in hand, but I could only see his lips, and the blue point of the match; even those but fitfully. The tinder was damp,—no wonder there,—and one after another the sparks died out.\n\nThe man was in no hurry, and struck again with the flint and steel. As the sparks fell thick and bright about him, I could see his hands, and touches of his face, and could make out that he was seated and bending over the table; but nothing more. Presently I saw his blue lips again, breathing on the tinder, and then a flare of light flashed up, and showed me Orlick.\n\nWhom I had looked for, I don’t know. I had not looked for him. Seeing him, I felt that I was in a dangerous strait indeed, and I kept my eyes upon him.\n\nHe lighted the candle from the flaring match with great deliberation, and dropped the match, and trod it out. Then he put the candle away from him on the table, so that he could see me, and sat with his arms folded on the table and looked at me. I made out that I was fastened to a stout perpendicular ladder a few inches from the wall,—a fixture there,—the means of ascent to the loft above.\n\n“Now,” said he, when we had surveyed one another for some time, “I’ve got you.”\n\n“Unbind me. Let me go!”\n\n“Ah!” he returned, “I’ll let you go. I’ll let you go to the moon, I’ll let you go to the stars. All in good time.”\n\n“Why have you lured me here?”\n\n“Don’t you know?” said he, with a deadly look.\n\n“Why have you set upon me in the dark?”\n\n“Because I mean to do it all myself. One keeps a secret better than two. O you enemy, you enemy!”\n\nHis enjoyment of the spectacle I furnished, as he sat with his arms folded on the table, shaking his head at me and hugging himself, had a malignity in it that made me tremble. As I watched him in silence, he put his hand into the corner at his side, and took up a gun with a brass-bound stock.\n\n“Do you know this?” said he, making as if he would take aim at me. “Do you know where you saw it afore? Speak, wolf!”\n\n“Yes,” I answered.\n\n“You cost me that place. You did. Speak!”\n\n“What else could I do?”\n\n“You did that, and that would be enough, without more. How dared you to come betwixt me and a young woman I liked?”\n\n“When did I?”\n“When didn’t you? It was you as always give Old Orlick a bad name to her.”\n\n“You gave it to yourself; you gained it for yourself. I could have done you no harm, if you had done yourself none.”\n\n“You’re a liar. And you’ll take any pains, and spend any money, to drive me out of this country, will you?” said he, repeating my words to Biddy in the last interview I had with her. “Now, I’ll tell you a piece of information. It was never so well worth your while to get me out of this country as it is to-night. Ah! If it was all your money twenty times told, to the last brass farden!” As he shook his heavy hand at me, with his mouth snarling like a tiger’s, I felt that it was true.\n\n“What are you going to do to me?”\n\n“I’m a-going,” said he, bringing his fist down upon the table with a heavy blow, and rising as the blow fell to give it greater force,—“I’m a-going to have your life!”\n\nHe leaned forward staring at me, slowly unclenched his hand and drew it across his mouth as if his mouth watered for me, and sat down again.\n\n“You was always in Old Orlick’s way since ever you was a child. You goes out of his way this present night. He’ll have no more on you. You’re dead.”\n\nI felt that I had come to the brink of my grave. For a moment I looked wildly round my trap for any chance of escape; but there was none.\n\n“More than that,” said he, folding his arms on the table again, “I won’t have a rag of you, I won’t have a bone of you, left on earth. I’ll put your body in the kiln,—I’d carry two such to it, on my shoulders—and, let people suppose what they may of you, they shall never know nothing.”\n\nMy mind, with inconceivable rapidity followed out all the consequences of such a death. Estella’s father would believe I had deserted him, would be taken, would die accusing me; even Herbert would doubt me, when he compared the letter I had left for him with the fact that I had called at Miss Havisham’s gate for only a moment; Joe and Biddy would never know how sorry I had been that night, none would ever know what I had suffered, how true I had meant to be, what an agony I had passed through. The death close before me was terrible, but far more terrible than death was the dread of being misremembered after death. And so quick were my thoughts, that I saw myself despised by unborn generations,—Estella’s children, and their children,—while the wretch’s words were yet on his lips.\n\n“Now, wolf,” said he, “afore I kill you like any other beast,—which is wot I mean to do and wot I have tied you up for,—I’ll have a good look at you and a good goad at you. O you enemy!”\n\nIt had passed through my thoughts to cry out for help again; though few could know better than I, the solitary nature of the spot, and the hopelessness of aid. But as he sat gloating over me, I was supported by a scornful detestation of him that sealed my lips. Above all things, I resolved that I would not entreat him, and that I would die making some last poor resistance to him. Softened as my thoughts of all the rest of men were in that dire extremity; humbly beseeching pardon, as I did, of Heaven; melted at heart, as I was, by the thought that I had taken no farewell, and never now could take farewell of those who were dear to me, or could explain myself to them, or ask for their compassion on my miserable errors,—still, if I could have killed him, even in dying, I would have done it.\n\nHe had been drinking, and his eyes were red and bloodshot. Around his neck was slung a tin bottle, as I had often seen his meat and drink slung about him in other days. He brought the bottle to his lips, and took a fiery drink from it; and I smelt the strong spirits that I saw flash into his face.\n\n“Wolf!” said he, folding his arms again, “Old Orlick’s a-going to tell you somethink. It was you as did for your shrew sister.”\n\nAgain my mind, with its former inconceivable rapidity, had exhausted the whole subject of the attack upon my sister, her illness, and her death, before his slow and hesitating speech had formed these words.\n\n“It was you, villain,” said I.\n\n“I tell you it was your doing,—I tell you it was done through you,” he retorted, catching up the gun, and making a blow with the stock at the vacant air between us. “I come upon her from behind, as I come upon you to-night. I giv’ it her! I left her for dead, and if there had been a limekiln as nigh her as there is now nigh you, she shouldn’t have come to life again. But it warn’t Old Orlick as did it; it was you. You was favoured, and he was bullied and beat. Old Orlick bullied and beat, eh? Now you pays for it. You done it; now you pays for it.”\n\nHe drank again, and became more ferocious. I saw by his tilting of the bottle that there was no great quantity left in it. I distinctly understood that he was working himself up with its contents to make an end of me. I knew that every drop it held was a drop of my life. I knew that when I was changed into a part of the vapour that had crept towards me but a little while before, like my own warning ghost, he would do as he had done in my sister’s case,—make all haste to the town, and be seen slouching about there drinking at the alehouses. My rapid mind pursued him to the town, made a picture of the street with him in it, and contrasted its lights and life with the lonely marsh and the white vapour creeping over it, into which I should have dissolved.\n\nIt was not only that I could have summed up years and years and years while he said a dozen words, but that what he did say presented pictures to me, and not mere words. In the excited and exalted state of my brain, I could not think of a place without seeing it, or of persons without seeing them. It is impossible to overstate the vividness of these images, and yet I was so intent, all the time, upon him himself,—who would not be intent on the tiger crouching to spring!—that I knew of the slightest action of his fingers.\n\nWhen he had drunk this second time, he rose from the bench on which he sat, and pushed the table aside. Then, he took up the candle, and, shading it with his murderous hand so as to throw its light on me, stood before me, looking at me and enjoying the sight.\n\n“Wolf, I’ll tell you something more. It was Old Orlick as you tumbled over on your stairs that night.”\n\nI saw the staircase with its extinguished lamps. I saw the shadows of the heavy stair-rails, thrown by the watchman’s lantern on the wall. I saw the rooms that I was never to see again; here, a door half open; there, a door closed; all the articles of furniture around.\n\n“And why was Old Orlick there? I’ll tell you something more, wolf. You and her have pretty well hunted me out of this country, so far as getting a easy living in it goes, and I’ve took up with new companions, and new masters. Some of ’em writes my letters when I wants ’em wrote,—do you mind?—writes my letters, wolf! They writes fifty hands; they’re not like sneaking you, as writes but one. I’ve had a firm mind and a firm will to have your life, since you was down here at your sister’s burying. I han’t seen a way to get you safe, and I’ve looked arter you to know your ins and outs. For, says Old Orlick to himself, ‘Somehow or another I’ll have him!’ What! When I looks for you, I finds your uncle Provis, eh?”\n\nMill Pond Bank, and Chinks’s Basin, and the Old Green Copper Rope-walk, all so clear and plain! Provis in his rooms, the signal whose use was over, pretty Clara, the good motherly woman, old Bill Barley on his back, all drifting by, as on the swift stream of my life fast running out to sea!\n\n“You with a uncle too! Why, I know’d you at Gargery’s when you was so small a wolf that I could have took your weazen betwixt this finger and thumb and chucked you away dead (as I’d thoughts o’ doing, odd times, when I see you loitering amongst the pollards on a Sunday), and you hadn’t found no uncles then. No, not you! But when Old Orlick come for to hear that your uncle Provis had most like wore the leg-iron wot Old Orlick had picked up, filed asunder, on these meshes ever so many year ago, and wot he kep by him till he dropped your sister with it, like a bullock, as he means to drop you—hey?—when he come for to hear that—hey?”\n\nIn his savage taunting, he flared the candle so close at me that I turned my face aside to save it from the flame.\n\n“Ah!” he cried, laughing, after doing it again, “the burnt child dreads the fire! Old Orlick knowed you was burnt, Old Orlick knowed you was smuggling your uncle Provis away, Old Orlick’s a match for you and know’d you’d come to-night! Now I’ll tell you something more, wolf, and this ends it. There’s them that’s as good a match for your uncle Provis as Old Orlick has been for you. Let him ’ware them, when he’s lost his nevvy! Let him ’ware them, when no man can’t find a rag of his dear relation’s clothes, nor yet a bone of his body. There’s them that can’t and that won’t have Magwitch,—yes, I know the name!—alive in the same land with them, and that’s had such sure information of him when he was alive in another land, as that he couldn’t and shouldn’t leave it unbeknown and put them in danger. P’raps it’s them that writes fifty hands, and that’s not like sneaking you as writes but one. ’Ware Compeyson, Magwitch, and the gallows!”\n\nHe flared the candle at me again, smoking my face and hair, and for an instant blinding me, and turned his powerful back as he replaced the light on the table. I had thought a prayer, and had been with Joe and Biddy and Herbert, before he turned towards me again.\n\nThere was a clear space of a few feet between the table and the opposite wall. Within this space, he now slouched backwards and forwards. His great strength seemed to sit stronger upon him than ever before, as he did this with his hands hanging loose and heavy at his sides, and with his eyes scowling at me. I had no grain of hope left. Wild as my inward hurry was, and wonderful the force of the pictures that rushed by me instead of thoughts, I could yet clearly understand that, unless he had resolved that I was within a few moments of surely perishing out of all human knowledge, he would never have told me what he had told.\n\nOf a sudden, he stopped, took the cork out of his bottle, and tossed it away. Light as it was, I heard it fall like a plummet. He swallowed slowly, tilting up the bottle by little and little, and now he looked at me no more. The last few drops of liquor he poured into the palm of his hand, and licked up. Then, with a sudden hurry of violence and swearing horribly, he threw the bottle from him, and stooped; and I saw in his hand a stone-hammer with a long heavy handle.\n\nThe resolution I had made did not desert me, for, without uttering one vain word of appeal to him, I shouted out with all my might, and struggled with all my might. It was only my head and my legs that I could move, but to that extent I struggled with all the force, until then unknown, that was within me. In the same instant I heard responsive shouts, saw figures and a gleam of light dash in at the door, heard voices and tumult, and saw Orlick emerge from a struggle of men, as if it were tumbling water, clear the table at a leap, and fly out into the night.\n\nAfter a blank, I found that I was lying unbound, on the floor, in the same place, with my head on some one’s knee. My eyes were fixed on the ladder against the wall, when I came to myself,—had opened on it before my mind saw it,—and thus as I recovered consciousness, I knew that I was in the place where I had lost it.\n\nToo indifferent at first, even to look round and ascertain who supported me, I was lying looking at the ladder, when there came between me and it a face. The face of Trabb’s boy!\n\n“I think he’s all right!” said Trabb’s boy, in a sober voice; “but ain’t he just pale though!”\n\nAt these words, the face of him who supported me looked over into mine, and I saw my supporter to be—\n\n“Herbert! Great Heaven!”\n\n“Softly,” said Herbert. “Gently, Handel. Don’t be too eager.”\n\n“And our old comrade, Startop!” I cried, as he too bent over me.\n\n“Remember what he is going to assist us in,” said Herbert, “and be calm.”\n\nThe allusion made me spring up; though I dropped again from the pain in my arm. “The time has not gone by, Herbert, has it? What night is to-night? How long have I been here?” For, I had a strange and strong misgiving that I had been lying there a long time—a day and a night,—two days and nights,—more.\n\n“The time has not gone by. It is still Monday night.”\n\n“Thank God!”\n\n“And you have all to-morrow, Tuesday, to rest in,” said Herbert. “But you can’t help groaning, my dear Handel. What hurt have you got? Can you stand?”\n\n“Yes, yes,” said I, “I can walk. I have no hurt but in this throbbing arm.”\n\nThey laid it bare, and did what they could. It was violently swollen and inflamed, and I could scarcely endure to have it touched. But, they tore up their handkerchiefs to make fresh bandages, and carefully replaced it in the sling, until we could get to the town and obtain some cooling lotion to put upon it. In a little while we had shut the door of the dark and empty sluice-house, and were passing through the quarry on our way back. Trabb’s boy—Trabb’s overgrown young man now—went before us with a lantern, which was the light I had seen come in at the door. But, the moon was a good two hours higher than when I had last seen the sky, and the night, though rainy, was much lighter. The white vapour of the kiln was passing from us as we went by, and as I had thought a prayer before, I thought a thanksgiving now.\n\nEntreating Herbert to tell me how he had come to my rescue,—which at first he had flatly refused to do, but had insisted on my remaining quiet,—I learnt that I had in my hurry dropped the letter, open, in our chambers, where he, coming home to bring with him Startop whom he had met in the street on his way to me, found it, very soon after I was gone. Its tone made him uneasy, and the more so because of the inconsistency between it and the hasty letter I had left for him. His uneasiness increasing instead of subsiding, after a quarter of an hour’s consideration, he set off for the coach-office with Startop, who volunteered his company, to make inquiry when the next coach went down. Finding that the afternoon coach was gone, and finding that his uneasiness grew into positive alarm, as obstacles came in his way, he resolved to follow in a post-chaise. So he and Startop arrived at the Blue Boar, fully expecting there to find me, or tidings of me; but, finding neither, went on to Miss Havisham’s, where they lost me. Hereupon they went back to the hotel (doubtless at about the time when I was hearing the popular local version of my own story) to refresh themselves and to get some one to guide them out upon the marshes. Among the loungers under the Boar’s archway happened to be Trabb’s Boy,—true to his ancient habit of happening to be everywhere where he had no business,—and Trabb’s boy had seen me passing from Miss Havisham’s in the direction of my dining-place. Thus Trabb’s boy became their guide, and with him they went out to the sluice-house, though by the town way to the marshes, which I had avoided. Now, as they went along, Herbert reflected, that I might, after all, have been brought there on some genuine and serviceable errand tending to Provis’s safety, and, bethinking himself that in that case interruption must be mischievous, left his guide and Startop on the edge of the quarry, and went on by himself, and stole round the house two or three times, endeavouring to ascertain whether all was right within. As he could hear nothing but indistinct sounds of one deep rough voice (this was while my mind was so busy), he even at last began to doubt whether I was there, when suddenly I cried out loudly, and he answered the cries, and rushed in, closely followed by the other two.\n\nWhen I told Herbert what had passed within the house, he was for our immediately going before a magistrate in the town, late at night as it was, and getting out a warrant. But, I had already considered that such a course, by detaining us there, or binding us to come back, might be fatal to Provis. There was no gainsaying this difficulty, and we relinquished all thoughts of pursuing Orlick at that time. For the present, under the circumstances, we deemed it prudent to make rather light of the matter to Trabb’s boy; who, I am convinced, would have been much affected by disappointment, if he had known that his intervention saved me from the limekiln. Not that Trabb’s boy was of a malignant nature, but that he had too much spare vivacity, and that it was in his constitution to want variety and excitement at anybody’s expense. When we parted, I presented him with two guineas (which seemed to meet his views), and told him that I was sorry ever to have had an ill opinion of him (which made no impression on him at all).\n\nWednesday being so close upon us, we determined to go back to London that night, three in the post-chaise; the rather, as we should then be clear away before the night’s adventure began to be talked of. Herbert got a large bottle of stuff for my arm; and by dint of having this stuff dropped over it all the night through, I was just able to bear its pain on the journey. It was daylight when we reached the Temple, and I went at once to bed, and lay in bed all day.\n\nMy terror, as I lay there, of falling ill, and being unfitted for to-morrow, was so besetting, that I wonder it did not disable me of itself. It would have done so, pretty surely, in conjunction with the mental wear and tear I had suffered, but for the unnatural strain upon me that to-morrow was. So anxiously looked forward to, charged with such consequences, its results so impenetrably hidden, though so near.\n\nNo precaution could have been more obvious than our refraining from communication with him that day; yet this again increased my restlessness. I started at every footstep and every sound, believing that he was discovered and taken, and this was the messenger to tell me so. I persuaded myself that I knew he was taken; that there was something more upon my mind than a fear or a presentiment; that the fact had occurred, and I had a mysterious knowledge of it. As the days wore on, and no ill news came, as the day closed in and darkness fell, my overshadowing dread of being disabled by illness before to-morrow morning altogether mastered me. My burning arm throbbed, and my burning head throbbed, and I fancied I was beginning to wander. I counted up to high numbers, to make sure of myself, and repeated passages that I knew in prose and verse. It happened sometimes that in the mere escape of a fatigued mind, I dozed for some moments or forgot; then I would say to myself with a start, “Now it has come, and I am turning delirious!”\n\nThey kept me very quiet all day, and kept my arm constantly dressed, and gave me cooling drinks. Whenever I fell asleep, I awoke with the notion I had had in the sluice-house, that a long time had elapsed and the opportunity to save him was gone. About midnight I got out of bed and went to Herbert, with the conviction that I had been asleep for four-and-twenty hours, and that Wednesday was past. It was the last self-exhausting effort of my fretfulness, for after that I slept soundly.\n\nWednesday morning was dawning when I looked out of window. The winking lights upon the bridges were already pale, the coming sun was like a marsh of fire on the horizon. The river, still dark and mysterious, was spanned by bridges that were turning coldly grey, with here and there at top a warm touch from the burning in the sky. As I looked along the clustered roofs, with church-towers and spires shooting into the unusually clear air, the sun rose up, and a veil seemed to be drawn from the river, and millions of sparkles burst out upon its waters. From me too, a veil seemed to be drawn, and I felt strong and well.\n\nHerbert lay asleep in his bed, and our old fellow-student lay asleep on the sofa. I could not dress myself without help; but I made up the fire, which was still burning, and got some coffee ready for them. In good time they too started up strong and well, and we admitted the sharp morning air at the windows, and looked at the tide that was still flowing towards us.\n\n“When it turns at nine o’clock,” said Herbert, cheerfully, “look out for us, and stand ready, you over there at Mill Pond Bank!”\n\n# Chapter LIV.\n\nIt was one of those March days when the sun shines hot and the wind blows cold: when it is summer in the light, and winter in the shade. We had our pea-coats with us, and I took a bag. Of all my worldly possessions I took no more than the few necessaries that filled the bag. Where I might go, what I might do, or when I might return, were questions utterly unknown to me; nor did I vex my mind with them, for it was wholly set on Provis’s safety. I only wondered for the passing moment, as I stopped at the door and looked back, under what altered circumstances I should next see those rooms, if ever.\n\nWe loitered down to the Temple stairs, and stood loitering there, as if we were not quite decided to go upon the water at all. Of course, I had taken care that the boat should be ready and everything in order. After a little show of indecision, which there were none to see but the two or three amphibious creatures belonging to our Temple stairs, we went on board and cast off; Herbert in the bow, I steering. It was then about high-water,—half-past eight.\n\nOur plan was this. The tide, beginning to run down at nine, and being with us until three, we intended still to creep on after it had turned, and row against it until dark. We should then be well in those long reaches below Gravesend, between Kent and Essex, where the river is broad and solitary, where the water-side inhabitants are very few, and where lone public-houses are scattered here and there, of which we could choose one for a resting-place. There, we meant to lie by all night. The steamer for Hamburg and the steamer for Rotterdam would start from London at about nine on Thursday morning. We should know at what time to expect them, according to where we were, and would hail the first; so that, if by any accident we were not taken abroad, we should have another chance. We knew the distinguishing marks of each vessel.\n\nThe relief of being at last engaged in the execution of the purpose was so great to me that I felt it difficult to realise the condition in which I had been a few hours before. The crisp air, the sunlight, the movement on the river, and the moving river itself,—the road that ran with us, seeming to sympathise with us, animate us, and encourage us on,—freshened me with new hope. I felt mortified to be of so little use in the boat; but, there were few better oarsmen than my two friends, and they rowed with a steady stroke that was to last all day.\n\nAt that time, the steam-traffic on the Thames was far below its present extent, and watermen’s boats were far more numerous. Of barges, sailing colliers, and coasting-traders, there were perhaps, as many as now; but of steam-ships, great and small, not a tithe or a twentieth part so many. Early as it was, there were plenty of scullers going here and there that morning, and plenty of barges dropping down with the tide; the navigation of the river between bridges, in an open boat, was a much easier and commoner matter in those days than it is in these; and we went ahead among many skiffs and wherries briskly.\n\nOld London Bridge was soon passed, and old Billingsgate Market with its oyster-boats and Dutchmen, and the White Tower and Traitor’s Gate, and we were in among the tiers of shipping. Here were the Leith, Aberdeen, and Glasgow steamers, loading and unloading goods, and looking immensely high out of the water as we passed alongside; here, were colliers by the score and score, with the coal-whippers plunging off stages on deck, as counterweights to measures of coal swinging up, which were then rattled over the side into barges; here, at her moorings was to-morrow’s steamer for Rotterdam, of which we took good notice; and here to-morrow’s for Hamburg, under whose bowsprit we crossed. And now I, sitting in the stern, could see, with a faster beating heart, Mill Pond Bank and Mill Pond stairs.\n\n“Is he there?” said Herbert.\n“Not yet.”\n\n“Right! He was not to come down till he saw us. Can you see his signal?”\n\n“Not well from here; but I think I see it.—Now I see him! Pull both. Easy, Herbert. Oars!”\n\nWe touched the stairs lightly for a single moment, and he was on board, and we were off again. He had a boat-cloak with him, and a black canvas bag; and he looked as like a river-pilot as my heart could have wished.\n\n“Dear boy!” he said, putting his arm on my shoulder, as he took his seat. “Faithful dear boy, well done. Thankye, thankye!”\n\nAgain among the tiers of shipping, in and out, avoiding rusty chain-cables frayed hempen hawsers and bobbing buoys, sinking for the moment floating broken baskets, scattering floating chips of wood and shaving, cleaving floating scum of coal, in and out, under the figure-head of the John of Sunderland making a speech to the winds (as is done by many Johns), and the Betsy of Yarmouth with a firm formality of bosom and her knobby eyes starting two inches out of her head; in and out, hammers going in ship-builders’ yards, saws going at timber, clashing engines going at things unknown, pumps going in leaky ships, capstans going, ships going out to sea, and unintelligible sea-creatures roaring curses over the bulwarks at respondent lightermen, in and out,—out at last upon the clearer river, where the ships’ boys might take their fenders in, no longer fishing in troubled waters with them over the side, and where the festooned sails might fly out to the wind.\n\nAt the stairs where we had taken him abroad, and ever since, I had looked warily for any token of our being suspected. I had seen none. We certainly had not been, and at that time as certainly we were not either attended or followed by any boat. If we had been waited on by any boat, I should have run in to shore, and have obliged her to go on, or to make her purpose evident. But we held our own without any appearance of molestation.\n\nHe had his boat-cloak on him, and looked, as I have said, a natural part of the scene. It was remarkable (but perhaps the wretched life he had led accounted for it) that he was the least anxious of any of us. He was not indifferent, for he told me that he hoped to live to see his gentleman one of the best of gentlemen in a foreign country; he was not disposed to be passive or resigned, as I understood it; but he had no notion of meeting danger half way. When it came upon him, he confronted it, but it must come before he troubled himself.\n\n“If you knowed, dear boy,” he said to me, “what it is to sit here alonger my dear boy and have my smoke, arter having been day by day betwixt four walls, you’d envy me. But you don’t know what it is.”\n\n“I think I know the delights of freedom,” I answered.\n\n“Ah,” said he, shaking his head gravely. “But you don’t know it equal to me. You must have been under lock and key, dear boy, to know it equal to me,—but I ain’t a-going to be low.”\n\nIt occurred to me as inconsistent, that, for any mastering idea, he should have endangered his freedom, and even his life. But I reflected that perhaps freedom without danger was too much apart from all the habit of his existence to be to him what it would be to another man. I was not far out, since he said, after smoking a little:—\n\n“You see, dear boy, when I was over yonder, t’other side the world, I was always a looking to this side; and it come flat to be there, for all I was a growing rich. Everybody knowed Magwitch, and Magwitch could come, and Magwitch could go, and nobody’s head would be troubled about him. They ain’t so easy concerning me here, dear boy,—wouldn’t be, leastwise, if they knowed where I was.”\n\n“If all goes well,” said I, “you will be perfectly free and safe again within a few hours.”\n\n“Well,” he returned, drawing a long breath, “I hope so.”\n\n“And think so?”\n\nHe dipped his hand in the water over the boat’s gunwale, and said, smiling with that softened air upon him which was not new to me:—\n\n“Ay, I s’pose I think so, dear boy. We’d be puzzled to be more quiet and easy-going than we are at present. But—it’s a flowing so soft and pleasant through the water, p’raps, as makes me think it—I was a thinking through my smoke just then, that we can no more see to the bottom of the next few hours than we can see to the bottom of this river what I catches hold of. Nor yet we can’t no more hold their tide than I can hold this. And it’s run through my fingers and gone, you see!” holding up his dripping hand.\n\n“But for your face I should think you were a little despondent,” said I.\n\n“Not a bit on it, dear boy! It comes of flowing on so quiet, and of that there rippling at the boat’s head making a sort of a Sunday tune. Maybe I’m a growing a trifle old besides.”\n\nHe put his pipe back in his mouth with an undisturbed expression of face, and sat as composed and contented as if we were already out of England. Yet he was as submissive to a word of advice as if he had been in constant terror; for, when we ran ashore to get some bottles of beer into the boat, and he was stepping out, I hinted that I thought he would be safest where he was, and he said. “Do you, dear boy?” and quietly sat down again.\n\nThe air felt cold upon the river, but it was a bright day, and the sunshine was very cheering. The tide ran strong, I took care to lose none of it, and our steady stroke carried us on thoroughly well. By imperceptible degrees, as the tide ran out, we lost more and more of the nearer woods and hills, and dropped lower and lower between the muddy banks, but the tide was yet with us when we were off Gravesend. As our charge was wrapped in his cloak, I purposely passed within a boat or two’s length of the floating Custom House, and so out to catch the stream, alongside of two emigrant ships, and under the bows of a large transport with troops on the forecastle looking down at us. And soon the tide began to slacken, and the craft lying at anchor to swing, and presently they had all swung round, and the ships that were taking advantage of the new tide to get up to the Pool began to crowd upon us in a fleet, and we kept under the shore, as much out of the strength of the tide now as we could, standing carefully off from low shallows and mudbanks.\n\nOur oarsmen were so fresh, by dint of having occasionally let her drive with the tide for a minute or two, that a quarter of an hour’s rest proved full as much as they wanted. We got ashore among some slippery stones while we ate and drank what we had with us, and looked about. It was like my own marsh country, flat and monotonous, and with a dim horizon; while the winding river turned and turned, and the great floating buoys upon it turned and turned, and everything else seemed stranded and still. For now the last of the fleet of ships was round the last low point we had headed; and the last green barge, straw-laden, with a brown sail, had followed; and some ballast-lighters, shaped like a child’s first rude imitation of a boat, lay low in the mud; and a little squat shoal-lighthouse on open piles stood crippled in the mud on stilts and crutches; and slimy stakes stuck out of the mud, and slimy stones stuck out of the mud, and red landmarks and tidemarks stuck out of the mud, and an old landing-stage and an old roofless building slipped into the mud, and all about us was stagnation and mud.\n\nWe pushed off again, and made what way we could. It was much harder work now, but Herbert and Startop persevered, and rowed and rowed and rowed until the sun went down. By that time the river had lifted us a little, so that we could see above the bank. There was the red sun, on the low level of the shore, in a purple haze, fast deepening into black; and there was the solitary flat marsh; and far away there were the rising grounds, between which and us there seemed to be no life, save here and there in the foreground a melancholy gull.\n\nAs the night was fast falling, and as the moon, being past the full, would not rise early, we held a little council; a short one, for clearly our course was to lie by at the first lonely tavern we could find. So, they plied their oars once more, and I looked out for anything like a house. Thus we held on, speaking little, for four or five dull miles. It was very cold, and, a collier coming by us, with her galley-fire smoking and flaring, looked like a comfortable home. The night was as dark by this time as it would be until morning; and what light we had, seemed to come more from the river than the sky, as the oars in their dipping struck at a few reflected stars.\n\nAt this dismal time we were evidently all possessed by the idea that we were followed. As the tide made, it flapped heavily at irregular intervals against the shore; and whenever such a sound came, one or other of us was sure to start, and look in that direction. Here and there, the set of the current had worn down the bank into a little creek, and we were all suspicious of such places, and eyed them nervously. Sometimes, “What was that ripple?” one of us would say in a low voice. Or another, “Is that a boat yonder?” And afterwards we would fall into a dead silence, and I would sit impatiently thinking with what an unusual amount of noise the oars worked in the thowels.\n\nAt length we descried a light and a roof, and presently afterwards ran alongside a little causeway made of stones that had been picked up hard by. Leaving the rest in the boat, I stepped ashore, and found the light to be in a window of a public-house. It was a dirty place enough, and I dare say not unknown to smuggling adventurers; but there was a good fire in the kitchen, and there were eggs and bacon to eat, and various liquors to drink. Also, there were two double-bedded rooms,—“such as they were,” the landlord said. No other company was in the house than the landlord, his wife, and a grizzled male creature, the “Jack” of the little causeway, who was as slimy and smeary as if he had been low-water mark too.\n\nWith this assistant, I went down to the boat again, and we all came ashore, and brought out the oars, and rudder and boat-hook, and all else, and hauled her up for the night. We made a very good meal by the kitchen fire, and then apportioned the bedrooms: Herbert and Startop were to occupy one; I and our charge the other. We found the air as carefully excluded from both, as if air were fatal to life; and there were more dirty clothes and bandboxes under the beds than I should have thought the family possessed. But we considered ourselves well off, notwithstanding, for a more solitary place we could not have found.\n\nWhile we were comforting ourselves by the fire after our meal, the Jack—who was sitting in a corner, and who had a bloated pair of shoes on, which he had exhibited while we were eating our eggs and bacon, as interesting relics that he had taken a few days ago from the feet of a drowned seaman washed ashore—asked me if we had seen a four-oared galley going up with the tide? When I told him No, he said she must have gone down then, and yet she “took up too,” when she left there.\n\n“They must ha’ thought better on’t for some reason or another,” said the Jack, “and gone down.”\n\n“A four-oared galley, did you say?” said I.\n\n“A four,” said the Jack, “and two sitters.”\n\n“Did they come ashore here?”\n\n“They put in with a stone two-gallon jar for some beer. I’d ha’ been glad to pison the beer myself,” said the Jack, “or put some rattling physic in it.”\n\n“Why?”\n“I know why,” said the Jack. He spoke in a slushy voice, as if much mud had washed into his throat.\n\n“He thinks,” said the landlord, a weakly meditative man with a pale eye, who seemed to rely greatly on his Jack,—“he thinks they was, what they wasn’t.”\n\n“I knows what I thinks,” observed the Jack.\n\n“You thinks Custom ’Us, Jack?” said the landlord.\n\n“I do,” said the Jack.\n\n“Then you’re wrong, Jack.”\n\nIn the infinite meaning of his reply and his boundless confidence in his views, the Jack took one of his bloated shoes off, looked into it, knocked a few stones out of it on the kitchen floor, and put it on again. He did this with the air of a Jack who was so right that he could afford to do anything.\n\n“Why, what do you make out that they done with their buttons then, Jack?” asked the landlord, vacillating weakly.\n\n“Done with their buttons?” returned the Jack. “Chucked ’em overboard. Swallered ’em. Sowed ’em, to come up small salad. Done with their buttons!”\n\n“Don’t be cheeky, Jack,” remonstrated the landlord, in a melancholy and pathetic way.\n\n“A Custom ’Us officer knows what to do with his Buttons,” said the Jack, repeating the obnoxious word with the greatest contempt, “when they comes betwixt him and his own light. A four and two sitters don’t go hanging and hovering, up with one tide and down with another, and both with and against another, without there being Custom ’Us at the bottom of it.” Saying which he went out in disdain; and the landlord, having no one to reply upon, found it impracticable to pursue the subject.\n\nThis dialogue made us all uneasy, and me very uneasy. The dismal wind was muttering round the house, the tide was flapping at the shore, and I had a feeling that we were caged and threatened. A four-oared galley hovering about in so unusual a way as to attract this notice was an ugly circumstance that I could not get rid of. When I had induced Provis to go up to bed, I went outside with my two companions (Startop by this time knew the state of the case), and held another council. Whether we should remain at the house until near the steamer’s time, which would be about one in the afternoon, or whether we should put off early in the morning, was the question we discussed. On the whole we deemed it the better course to lie where we were, until within an hour or so of the steamer’s time, and then to get out in her track, and drift easily with the tide. Having settled to do this, we returned into the house and went to bed.\n\nI lay down with the greater part of my clothes on, and slept well for a few hours. When I awoke, the wind had risen, and the sign of the house (the Ship) was creaking and banging about, with noises that startled me. Rising softly, for my charge lay fast asleep, I looked out of the window. It commanded the causeway where we had hauled up our boat, and, as my eyes adapted themselves to the light of the clouded moon, I saw two men looking into her. They passed by under the window, looking at nothing else, and they did not go down to the landing-place which I could discern to be empty, but struck across the marsh in the direction of the Nore.\n\nMy first impulse was to call up Herbert, and show him the two men going away. But reflecting, before I got into his room, which was at the back of the house and adjoined mine, that he and Startop had had a harder day than I, and were fatigued, I forbore. Going back to my window, I could see the two men moving over the marsh. In that light, however, I soon lost them, and, feeling very cold, lay down to think of the matter, and fell asleep again.\n\nWe were up early. As we walked to and fro, all four together, before breakfast, I deemed it right to recount what I had seen. Again our charge was the least anxious of the party. It was very likely that the men belonged to the Custom House, he said quietly, and that they had no thought of us. I tried to persuade myself that it was so,—as, indeed, it might easily be. However, I proposed that he and I should walk away together to a distant point we could see, and that the boat should take us aboard there, or as near there as might prove feasible, at about noon. This being considered a good precaution, soon after breakfast he and I set forth, without saying anything at the tavern.\n\nHe smoked his pipe as we went along, and sometimes stopped to clap me on the shoulder. One would have supposed that it was I who was in danger, not he, and that he was reassuring me. We spoke very little. As we approached the point, I begged him to remain in a sheltered place, while I went on to reconnoitre; for it was towards it that the men had passed in the night. He complied, and I went on alone. There was no boat off the point, nor any boat drawn up anywhere near it, nor were there any signs of the men having embarked there. But, to be sure, the tide was high, and there might have been some footprints under water.\n\nWhen he looked out from his shelter in the distance, and saw that I waved my hat to him to come up, he rejoined me, and there we waited; sometimes lying on the bank, wrapped in our coats, and sometimes moving about to warm ourselves, until we saw our boat coming round. We got aboard easily, and rowed out into the track of the steamer. By that time it wanted but ten minutes of one o’clock, and we began to look out for her smoke.\n\nBut, it was half-past one before we saw her smoke, and soon afterwards we saw behind it the smoke of another steamer. As they were coming on at full speed, we got the two bags ready, and took that opportunity of saying good-bye to Herbert and Startop. We had all shaken hands cordially, and neither Herbert’s eyes nor mine were quite dry, when I saw a four-oared galley shoot out from under the bank but a little way ahead of us, and row out into the same track.\n\nA stretch of shore had been as yet between us and the steamer’s smoke, by reason of the bend and wind of the river; but now she was visible, coming head on. I called to Herbert and Startop to keep before the tide, that she might see us lying by for her, and I adjured Provis to sit quite still, wrapped in his cloak. He answered cheerily, “Trust to me, dear boy,” and sat like a statue. Meantime the galley, which was very skilfully handled, had crossed us, let us come up with her, and fallen alongside. Leaving just room enough for the play of the oars, she kept alongside, drifting when we drifted, and pulling a stroke or two when we pulled. Of the two sitters one held the rudder-lines, and looked at us attentively,—as did all the rowers; the other sitter was wrapped up, much as Provis was, and seemed to shrink, and whisper some instruction to the steerer as he looked at us. Not a word was spoken in either boat.\n\nStartop could make out, after a few minutes, which steamer was first, and gave me the word “Hamburg,” in a low voice, as we sat face to face. She was nearing us very fast, and the beating of her peddles grew louder and louder. I felt as if her shadow were absolutely upon us, when the galley hailed us. I answered.\n\n“You have a returned Transport there,” said the man who held the lines. “That’s the man, wrapped in the cloak. His name is Abel Magwitch, otherwise Provis. I apprehend that man, and call upon him to surrender, and you to assist.”\n\nAt the same moment, without giving any audible direction to his crew, he ran the galley abroad of us. They had pulled one sudden stroke ahead, had got their oars in, had run athwart us, and were holding on to our gunwale, before we knew what they were doing. This caused great confusion on board the steamer, and I heard them calling to us, and heard the order given to stop the paddles, and heard them stop, but felt her driving down upon us irresistibly. In the same moment, I saw the steersman of the galley lay his hand on his prisoner’s shoulder, and saw that both boats were swinging round with the force of the tide, and saw that all hands on board the steamer were running forward quite frantically. Still, in the same moment, I saw the prisoner start up, lean across his captor, and pull the cloak from the neck of the shrinking sitter in the galley. Still in the same moment, I saw that the face disclosed, was the face of the other convict of long ago. Still, in the same moment, I saw the face tilt backward with a white terror on it that I shall never forget, and heard a great cry on board the steamer, and a loud splash in the water, and felt the boat sink from under me.\n\nIt was but for an instant that I seemed to struggle with a thousand mill-weirs and a thousand flashes of light; that instant past, I was taken on board the galley. Herbert was there, and Startop was there; but our boat was gone, and the two convicts were gone.\n\nWhat with the cries aboard the steamer, and the furious blowing off of her steam, and her driving on, and our driving on, I could not at first distinguish sky from water or shore from shore; but the crew of the galley righted her with great speed, and, pulling certain swift strong strokes ahead, lay upon their oars, every man looking silently and eagerly at the water astern. Presently a dark object was seen in it, bearing towards us on the tide. No man spoke, but the steersman held up his hand, and all softly backed water, and kept the boat straight and true before it. As it came nearer, I saw it to be Magwitch, swimming, but not swimming freely. He was taken on board, and instantly manacled at the wrists and ankles.\n\nThe galley was kept steady, and the silent, eager look-out at the water was resumed. But, the Rotterdam steamer now came up, and apparently not understanding what had happened, came on at speed. By the time she had been hailed and stopped, both steamers were drifting away from us, and we were rising and falling in a troubled wake of water. The look-out was kept, long after all was still again and the two steamers were gone; but everybody knew that it was hopeless now.\n\nAt length we gave it up, and pulled under the shore towards the tavern we had lately left, where we were received with no little surprise. Here I was able to get some comforts for Magwitch,—Provis no longer,—who had received some very severe injury in the chest, and a deep cut in the head.\n\nHe told me that he believed himself to have gone under the keel of the steamer, and to have been struck on the head in rising. The injury to his chest (which rendered his breathing extremely painful) he thought he had received against the side of the galley. He added that he did not pretend to say what he might or might not have done to Compeyson, but that, in the moment of his laying his hand on his cloak to identify him, that villain had staggered up and staggered back, and they had both gone overboard together, when the sudden wrenching of him (Magwitch) out of our boat, and the endeavour of his captor to keep him in it, had capsized us. He told me in a whisper that they had gone down fiercely locked in each other’s arms, and that there had been a struggle under water, and that he had disengaged himself, struck out, and swum away.\n\nI never had any reason to doubt the exact truth of what he thus told me. The officer who steered the galley gave the same account of their going overboard.\n\nWhen I asked this officer’s permission to change the prisoner’s wet clothes by purchasing any spare garments I could get at the public-house, he gave it readily: merely observing that he must take charge of everything his prisoner had about him. So the pocket-book which had once been in my hands passed into the officer’s. He further gave me leave to accompany the prisoner to London; but declined to accord that grace to my two friends.\n\nThe Jack at the Ship was instructed where the drowned man had gone down, and undertook to search for the body in the places where it was likeliest to come ashore. His interest in its recovery seemed to me to be much heightened when he heard that it had stockings on. Probably, it took about a dozen drowned men to fit him out completely; and that may have been the reason why the different articles of his dress were in various stages of decay.\n\nWe remained at the public-house until the tide turned, and then Magwitch was carried down to the galley and put on board. Herbert and Startop were to get to London by land, as soon as they could. We had a doleful parting, and when I took my place by Magwitch’s side, I felt that that was my place henceforth while he lived.\n\nFor now, my repugnance to him had all melted away; and in the hunted, wounded, shackled creature who held my hand in his, I only saw a man who had meant to be my benefactor, and who had felt affectionately, gratefully, and generously, towards me with great constancy through a series of years. I only saw in him a much better man than I had been to Joe.\n\nHis breathing became more difficult and painful as the night drew on, and often he could not repress a groan. I tried to rest him on the arm I could use, in any easy position; but it was dreadful to think that I could not be sorry at heart for his being badly hurt, since it was unquestionably best that he should die. That there were, still living, people enough who were able and willing to identify him, I could not doubt. That he would be leniently treated, I could not hope. He who had been presented in the worst light at his trial, who had since broken prison and had been tried again, who had returned from transportation under a life sentence, and who had occasioned the death of the man who was the cause of his arrest.\n\nAs we returned towards the setting sun we had yesterday left behind us, and as the stream of our hopes seemed all running back, I told him how grieved I was to think that he had come home for my sake.\n\n“Dear boy,” he answered, “I’m quite content to take my chance. I’ve seen my boy, and he can be a gentleman without me.”\n\nNo. I had thought about that, while we had been there side by side. No. Apart from any inclinations of my own, I understood Wemmick’s hint now. I foresaw that, being convicted, his possessions would be forfeited to the Crown.\n\n“Lookee here, dear boy,” said he “It’s best as a gentleman should not be knowed to belong to me now. Only come to see me as if you come by chance alonger Wemmick. Sit where I can see you when I am swore to, for the last o’ many times, and I don’t ask no more.”\n\n“I will never stir from your side,” said I, “when I am suffered to be near you. Please God, I will be as true to you as you have been to me!”\n\nI felt his hand tremble as it held mine, and he turned his face away as he lay in the bottom of the boat, and I heard that old sound in his throat,—softened now, like all the rest of him. It was a good thing that he had touched this point, for it put into my mind what I might not otherwise have thought of until too late,—that he need never know how his hopes of enriching me had perished.\n\n# Chapter LV.\n\nHe was taken to the Police Court next day, and would have been immediately committed for trial, but that it was necessary to send down for an old officer of the prison-ship from which he had once escaped, to speak to his identity. Nobody doubted it; but Compeyson, who had meant to depose to it, was tumbling on the tides, dead, and it happened that there was not at that time any prison officer in London who could give the required evidence. I had gone direct to Mr. Jaggers at his private house, on my arrival over night, to retain his assistance, and Mr. Jaggers on the prisoner’s behalf would admit nothing. It was the sole resource; for he told me that the case must be over in five minutes when the witness was there, and that no power on earth could prevent its going against us.\n\nI imparted to Mr. Jaggers my design of keeping him in ignorance of the fate of his wealth. Mr. Jaggers was querulous and angry with me for having “let it slip through my fingers,” and said we must memorialise by and by, and try at all events for some of it. But he did not conceal from me that, although there might be many cases in which the forfeiture would not be exacted, there were no circumstances in this case to make it one of them. I understood that very well. I was not related to the outlaw, or connected with him by any recognisable tie; he had put his hand to no writing or settlement in my favour before his apprehension, and to do so now would be idle. I had no claim, and I finally resolved, and ever afterwards abided by the resolution, that my heart should never be sickened with the hopeless task of attempting to establish one.\n\nThere appeared to be reason for supposing that the drowned informer had hoped for a reward out of this forfeiture, and had obtained some accurate knowledge of Magwitch’s affairs. When his body was found, many miles from the scene of his death, and so horribly disfigured that he was only recognisable by the contents of his pockets, notes were still legible, folded in a case he carried. Among these were the name of a banking-house in New South Wales, where a sum of money was, and the designation of certain lands of considerable value. Both these heads of information were in a list that Magwitch, while in prison, gave to Mr. Jaggers, of the possessions he supposed I should inherit. His ignorance, poor fellow, at last served him; he never mistrusted but that my inheritance was quite safe, with Mr. Jaggers’s aid.\n\nAfter three days’ delay, during which the crown prosecution stood over for the production of the witness from the prison-ship, the witness came, and completed the easy case. He was committed to take his trial at the next Sessions, which would come on in a month.\n\nIt was at this dark time of my life that Herbert returned home one evening, a good deal cast down, and said,—\n\n“My dear Handel, I fear I shall soon have to leave you.”\n\nHis partner having prepared me for that, I was less surprised than he thought.\n\n“We shall lose a fine opportunity if I put off going to Cairo, and I am very much afraid I must go, Handel, when you most need me.”\n\n“Herbert, I shall always need you, because I shall always love you; but my need is no greater now than at another time.”\n\n“You will be so lonely.”\n\n“I have not leisure to think of that,” said I. “You know that I am always with him to the full extent of the time allowed, and that I should be with him all day long, if I could. And when I come away from him, you know that my thoughts are with him.”\n\nThe dreadful condition to which he was brought, was so appalling to both of us, that we could not refer to it in plainer words.\n\n“My dear fellow,” said Herbert, “let the near prospect of our separation—for, it is very near—be my justification for troubling you about yourself. Have you thought of your future?”\n\n“No, for I have been afraid to think of any future.”\n\n“But yours cannot be dismissed; indeed, my dear dear Handel, it must not be dismissed. I wish you would enter on it now, as far as a few friendly words go, with me.”\n\n“I will,” said I.\n\n“In this branch house of ours, Handel, we must have a—”\n\nI saw that his delicacy was avoiding the right word, so I said, “A clerk.”\n\n“A clerk. And I hope it is not at all unlikely that he may expand (as a clerk of your acquaintance has expanded) into a partner. Now, Handel,—in short, my dear boy, will you come to me?”\n\nThere was something charmingly cordial and engaging in the manner in which after saying “Now, Handel,” as if it were the grave beginning of a portentous business exordium, he had suddenly given up that tone, stretched out his honest hand, and spoken like a schoolboy.\n\n“Clara and I have talked about it again and again,” Herbert pursued, “and the dear little thing begged me only this evening, with tears in her eyes, to say to you that, if you will live with us when we come together, she will do her best to make you happy, and to convince her husband’s friend that he is her friend too. We should get on so well, Handel!”\n\nI thanked her heartily, and I thanked him heartily, but said I could not yet make sure of joining him as he so kindly offered. Firstly, my mind was too preoccupied to be able to take in the subject clearly. Secondly,—Yes! Secondly, there was a vague something lingering in my thoughts that will come out very near the end of this slight narrative.\n\n“But if you thought, Herbert, that you could, without doing any injury to your business, leave the question open for a little while—”\n\n“For any while,” cried Herbert. “Six months, a year!”\n\n“Not so long as that,” said I. “Two or three months at most.”\n\nHerbert was highly delighted when we shook hands on this arrangement, and said he could now take courage to tell me that he believed he must go away at the end of the week.\n\n“And Clara?” said I.\n\n“The dear little thing,” returned Herbert, “holds dutifully to her father as long as he lasts; but he won’t last long. Mrs. Whimple confides to me that he is certainly going.”\n\n“Not to say an unfeeling thing,” said I, “he cannot do better than go.”\n\n“I am afraid that must be admitted,” said Herbert; “and then I shall come back for the dear little thing, and the dear little thing and I will walk quietly into the nearest church. Remember! The blessed darling comes of no family, my dear Handel, and never looked into the red book, and hasn’t a notion about her grandpapa. What a fortune for the son of my mother!”\n\nOn the Saturday in that same week, I took my leave of Herbert,—full of bright hope, but sad and sorry to leave me,—as he sat on one of the seaport mail coaches. I went into a coffee-house to write a little note to Clara, telling her he had gone off, sending his love to her over and over again, and then went to my lonely home,—if it deserved the name; for it was now no home to me, and I had no home anywhere.\n\nOn the stairs I encountered Wemmick, who was coming down, after an unsuccessful application of his knuckles to my door. I had not seen him alone since the disastrous issue of the attempted flight; and he had come, in his private and personal capacity, to say a few words of explanation in reference to that failure.\n\n“The late Compeyson,” said Wemmick, “had by little and little got at the bottom of half of the regular business now transacted; and it was from the talk of some of his people in trouble (some of his people being always in trouble) that I heard what I did. I kept my ears open, seeming to have them shut, until I heard that he was absent, and I thought that would be the best time for making the attempt. I can only suppose now, that it was a part of his policy, as a very clever man, habitually to deceive his own instruments. You don’t blame me, I hope, Mr. Pip? I am sure I tried to serve you, with all my heart.”\n\n“I am as sure of that, Wemmick, as you can be, and I thank you most earnestly for all your interest and friendship.”\n\n“Thank you, thank you very much. It’s a bad job,” said Wemmick, scratching his head, “and I assure you I haven’t been so cut up for a long time. What I look at is the sacrifice of so much portable property. Dear me!”\n\n“What I think of, Wemmick, is the poor owner of the property.”\n\n“Yes, to be sure,” said Wemmick. “Of course, there can be no objection to your being sorry for him, and I’d put down a five-pound note myself to get him out of it. But what I look at is this. The late Compeyson having been beforehand with him in intelligence of his return, and being so determined to bring him to book, I do not think he could have been saved. Whereas, the portable property certainly could have been saved. That’s the difference between the property and the owner, don’t you see?”\n\nI invited Wemmick to come upstairs, and refresh himself with a glass of grog before walking to Walworth. He accepted the invitation. While he was drinking his moderate allowance, he said, with nothing to lead up to it, and after having appeared rather fidgety,—\n\n“What do you think of my meaning to take a holiday on Monday, Mr. Pip?”\n\n“Why, I suppose you have not done such a thing these twelve months.”\n\n“These twelve years, more likely,” said Wemmick. “Yes. I’m going to take a holiday. More than that; I’m going to take a walk. More than that; I’m going to ask you to take a walk with me.”\n\nI was about to excuse myself, as being but a bad companion just then, when Wemmick anticipated me.\n\n“I know your engagements,” said he, “and I know you are out of sorts, Mr. Pip. But if you could oblige me, I should take it as a kindness. It ain’t a long walk, and it’s an early one. Say it might occupy you (including breakfast on the walk) from eight to twelve. Couldn’t you stretch a point and manage it?”\n\nHe had done so much for me at various times, that this was very little to do for him. I said I could manage it,—would manage it,—and he was so very much pleased by my acquiescence, that I was pleased too. At his particular request, I appointed to call for him at the Castle at half past eight on Monday morning, and so we parted for the time.\n\nPunctual to my appointment, I rang at the Castle gate on the Monday morning, and was received by Wemmick himself, who struck me as looking tighter than usual, and having a sleeker hat on. Within, there were two glasses of rum and milk prepared, and two biscuits. The Aged must have been stirring with the lark, for, glancing into the perspective of his bedroom, I observed that his bed was empty.\n\nWhen we had fortified ourselves with the rum and milk and biscuits, and were going out for the walk with that training preparation on us, I was considerably surprised to see Wemmick take up a fishing-rod, and put it over his shoulder. “Why, we are not going fishing!” said I. “No,” returned Wemmick, “but I like to walk with one.”\n\nI thought this odd; however, I said nothing, and we set off. We went towards Camberwell Green, and when we were thereabouts, Wemmick said suddenly,—\n\n“Halloa! Here’s a church!”\n\nThere was nothing very surprising in that; but again, I was rather surprised, when he said, as if he were animated by a brilliant idea,—\n\n“Let’s go in!”\n\nWe went in, Wemmick leaving his fishing-rod in the porch, and looked all round. In the mean time, Wemmick was diving into his coat-pockets, and getting something out of paper there.\n\n“Halloa!” said he. “Here’s a couple of pair of gloves! Let’s put ’em on!”\n\nAs the gloves were white kid gloves, and as the post-office was widened to its utmost extent, I now began to have my strong suspicions. They were strengthened into certainty when I beheld the Aged enter at a side door, escorting a lady.\n\n“Halloa!” said Wemmick. “Here’s Miss Skiffins! Let’s have a wedding.”\n\nThat discreet damsel was attired as usual, except that she was now engaged in substituting for her green kid gloves a pair of white. The Aged was likewise occupied in preparing a similar sacrifice for the altar of Hymen. The old gentleman, however, experienced so much difficulty in getting his gloves on, that Wemmick found it necessary to put him with his back against a pillar, and then to get behind the pillar himself and pull away at them, while I for my part held the old gentleman round the waist, that he might present an equal and safe resistance. By dint of this ingenious scheme, his gloves were got on to perfection.\n\nThe clerk and clergyman then appearing, we were ranged in order at those fatal rails. True to his notion of seeming to do it all without preparation, I heard Wemmick say to himself, as he took something out of his waistcoat-pocket before the service began, “Halloa! Here’s a ring!”\n\nI acted in the capacity of backer, or best-man, to the bridegroom; while a little limp pew-opener in a soft bonnet like a baby’s, made a feint of being the bosom friend of Miss Skiffins. The responsibility of giving the lady away devolved upon the Aged, which led to the clergyman’s being unintentionally scandalised, and it happened thus. When he said, “Who giveth this woman to be married to this man?” the old gentleman, not in the least knowing what point of the ceremony we had arrived at, stood most amiably beaming at the ten commandments. Upon which, the clergyman said again, “WHO giveth this woman to be married to this man?” The old gentleman being still in a state of most estimable unconsciousness, the bridegroom cried out in his accustomed voice, “Now Aged P. you know; who giveth?” To which the Aged replied with great briskness, before saying that he gave, “All right, John, all right, my boy!” And the clergyman came to so gloomy a pause upon it, that I had doubts for the moment whether we should get completely married that day.\n\nIt was completely done, however, and when we were going out of church Wemmick took the cover off the font, and put his white gloves in it, and put the cover on again. Mrs. Wemmick, more heedful of the future, put her white gloves in her pocket and assumed her green. “Now, Mr. Pip,” said Wemmick, triumphantly shouldering the fishing-rod as we came out, “let me ask you whether anybody would suppose this to be a wedding-party!”\n\nBreakfast had been ordered at a pleasant little tavern, a mile or so away upon the rising ground beyond the green; and there was a bagatelle board in the room, in case we should desire to unbend our minds after the solemnity. It was pleasant to observe that Mrs. Wemmick no longer unwound Wemmick’s arm when it adapted itself to her figure, but sat in a high-backed chair against the wall, like a violoncello in its case, and submitted to be embraced as that melodious instrument might have done.\n\nWe had an excellent breakfast, and when any one declined anything on table, Wemmick said, “Provided by contract, you know; don’t be afraid of it!” I drank to the new couple, drank to the Aged, drank to the Castle, saluted the bride at parting, and made myself as agreeable as I could.\n\nWemmick came down to the door with me, and I again shook hands with him, and wished him joy.\n\n“Thankee!” said Wemmick, rubbing his hands. “She’s such a manager of fowls, you have no idea. You shall have some eggs, and judge for yourself. I say, Mr. Pip!” calling me back, and speaking low. “This is altogether a Walworth sentiment, please.”\n\n“I understand. Not to be mentioned in Little Britain,” said I.\n\nWemmick nodded. “After what you let out the other day, Mr. Jaggers may as well not know of it. He might think my brain was softening, or something of the kind.”\n\n# Chapter LVI.\n\nHe lay in prison very ill, during the whole interval between his committal for trial and the coming round of the Sessions. He had broken two ribs, they had wounded one of his lungs, and he breathed with great pain and difficulty, which increased daily. It was a consequence of his hurt that he spoke so low as to be scarcely audible; therefore he spoke very little. But he was ever ready to listen to me; and it became the first duty of my life to say to him, and read to him, what I knew he ought to hear.\n\nBeing far too ill to remain in the common prison, he was removed, after the first day or so, into the infirmary. This gave me opportunities of being with him that I could not otherwise have had. And but for his illness he would have been put in irons, for he was regarded as a determined prison-breaker, and I know not what else.\n\nAlthough I saw him every day, it was for only a short time; hence, the regularly recurring spaces of our separation were long enough to record on his face any slight changes that occurred in his physical state. I do not recollect that I once saw any change in it for the better; he wasted, and became slowly weaker and worse, day by day, from the day when the prison door closed upon him.\n\nThe kind of submission or resignation that he showed was that of a man who was tired out. I sometimes derived an impression, from his manner or from a whispered word or two which escaped him, that he pondered over the question whether he might have been a better man under better circumstances. But he never justified himself by a hint tending that way, or tried to bend the past out of its eternal shape.\n\nIt happened on two or three occasions in my presence, that his desperate reputation was alluded to by one or other of the people in attendance on him. A smile crossed his face then, and he turned his eyes on me with a trustful look, as if he were confident that I had seen some small redeeming touch in him, even so long ago as when I was a little child. As to all the rest, he was humble and contrite, and I never knew him complain.\n\nWhen the Sessions came round, Mr. Jaggers caused an application to be made for the postponement of his trial until the following Sessions. It was obviously made with the assurance that he could not live so long, and was refused. The trial came on at once, and, when he was put to the bar, he was seated in a chair. No objection was made to my getting close to the dock, on the outside of it, and holding the hand that he stretched forth to me.\n\nThe trial was very short and very clear. Such things as could be said for him were said,—how he had taken to industrious habits, and had thriven lawfully and reputably. But nothing could unsay the fact that he had returned, and was there in presence of the Judge and Jury. It was impossible to try him for that, and do otherwise than find him guilty.\n\nAt that time, it was the custom (as I learnt from my terrible experience of that Sessions) to devote a concluding day to the passing of Sentences, and to make a finishing effect with the Sentence of Death. But for the indelible picture that my remembrance now holds before me, I could scarcely believe, even as I write these words, that I saw two-and-thirty men and women put before the Judge to receive that sentence together. Foremost among the two-and-thirty was he; seated, that he might get breath enough to keep life in him.\n\nThe whole scene starts out again in the vivid colours of the moment, down to the drops of April rain on the windows of the court, glittering in the rays of April sun. Penned in the dock, as I again stood outside it at the corner with his hand in mine, were the two-and-thirty men and women; some defiant, some stricken with terror, some sobbing and weeping, some covering their faces, some staring gloomily about. There had been shrieks from among the women convicts; but they had been stilled, and a hush had succeeded. The sheriffs with their great chains and nosegays, other civic gewgaws and monsters, criers, ushers, a great gallery full of people,—a large theatrical audience,—looked on, as the two-and-thirty and the Judge were solemnly confronted. Then the Judge addressed them. Among the wretched creatures before him whom he must single out for special address was one who almost from his infancy had been an offender against the laws; who, after repeated imprisonments and punishments, had been at length sentenced to exile for a term of years; and who, under circumstances of great violence and daring, had made his escape and been re-sentenced to exile for life. That miserable man would seem for a time to have become convinced of his errors, when far removed from the scenes of his old offences, and to have lived a peaceable and honest life. But in a fatal moment, yielding to those propensities and passions, the indulgence of which had so long rendered him a scourge to society, he had quitted his haven of rest and repentance, and had come back to the country where he was proscribed. Being here presently denounced, he had for a time succeeded in evading the officers of Justice, but being at length seized while in the act of flight, he had resisted them, and had—he best knew whether by express design, or in the blindness of his hardihood—caused the death of his denouncer, to whom his whole career was known. The appointed punishment for his return to the land that had cast him out, being Death, and his case being this aggravated case, he must prepare himself to Die.\n\nThe sun was striking in at the great windows of the court, through the glittering drops of rain upon the glass, and it made a broad shaft of light between the two-and-thirty and the Judge, linking both together, and perhaps reminding some among the audience how both were passing on, with absolute equality, to the greater Judgment that knoweth all things, and cannot err. Rising for a moment, a distinct speck of face in this way of light, the prisoner said, “My Lord, I have received my sentence of Death from the Almighty, but I bow to yours,” and sat down again. There was some hushing, and the Judge went on with what he had to say to the rest. Then they were all formally doomed, and some of them were supported out, and some of them sauntered out with a haggard look of bravery, and a few nodded to the gallery, and two or three shook hands, and others went out chewing the fragments of herb they had taken from the sweet herbs lying about. He went last of all, because of having to be helped from his chair, and to go very slowly; and he held my hand while all the others were removed, and while the audience got up (putting their dresses right, as they might at church or elsewhere), and pointed down at this criminal or at that, and most of all at him and me.\n\nI earnestly hoped and prayed that he might die before the Recorder’s Report was made; but, in the dread of his lingering on, I began that night to write out a petition to the Home Secretary of State, setting forth my knowledge of him, and how it was that he had come back for my sake. I wrote it as fervently and pathetically as I could; and when I had finished it and sent it in, I wrote out other petitions to such men in authority as I hoped were the most merciful, and drew up one to the Crown itself. For several days and nights after he was sentenced I took no rest except when I fell asleep in my chair, but was wholly absorbed in these appeals. And after I had sent them in, I could not keep away from the places where they were, but felt as if they were more hopeful and less desperate when I was near them. In this unreasonable restlessness and pain of mind I would roam the streets of an evening, wandering by those offices and houses where I had left the petitions. To the present hour, the weary western streets of London on a cold, dusty spring night, with their ranges of stern, shut-up mansions, and their long rows of lamps, are melancholy to me from this association.\n\nThe daily visits I could make him were shortened now, and he was more strictly kept. Seeing, or fancying, that I was suspected of an intention of carrying poison to him, I asked to be searched before I sat down at his bedside, and told the officer who was always there, that I was willing to do anything that would assure him of the singleness of my designs. Nobody was hard with him or with me. There was duty to be done, and it was done, but not harshly. The officer always gave me the assurance that he was worse, and some other sick prisoners in the room, and some other prisoners who attended on them as sick nurses, (malefactors, but not incapable of kindness, God be thanked!) always joined in the same report.\n\nAs the days went on, I noticed more and more that he would lie placidly looking at the white ceiling, with an absence of light in his face until some word of mine brightened it for an instant, and then it would subside again. Sometimes he was almost or quite unable to speak, then he would answer me with slight pressures on my hand, and I grew to understand his meaning very well.\n\nThe number of the days had risen to ten, when I saw a greater change in him than I had seen yet. His eyes were turned towards the door, and lighted up as I entered.\n\n“Dear boy,” he said, as I sat down by his bed: “I thought you was late. But I knowed you couldn’t be that.”\n\n“It is just the time,” said I. “I waited for it at the gate.”\n\n“You always waits at the gate; don’t you, dear boy?”\n\n“Yes. Not to lose a moment of the time.”\n\n“Thank’ee dear boy, thank’ee. God bless you! You’ve never deserted me, dear boy.”\n\nI pressed his hand in silence, for I could not forget that I had once meant to desert him.\n\n“And what’s the best of all,” he said, “you’ve been more comfortable alonger me, since I was under a dark cloud, than when the sun shone. That’s best of all.”\n\nHe lay on his back, breathing with great difficulty. Do what he would, and love me though he did, the light left his face ever and again, and a film came over the placid look at the white ceiling.\n\n“Are you in much pain to-day?”\n\n“I don’t complain of none, dear boy.”\n\n“You never do complain.”\n\nHe had spoken his last words. He smiled, and I understood his touch to mean that he wished to lift my hand, and lay it on his breast. I laid it there, and he smiled again, and put both his hands upon it.\n\nThe allotted time ran out, while we were thus; but, looking round, I found the governor of the prison standing near me, and he whispered, “You needn’t go yet.” I thanked him gratefully, and asked, “Might I speak to him, if he can hear me?”\n\nThe governor stepped aside, and beckoned the officer away. The change, though it was made without noise, drew back the film from the placid look at the white ceiling, and he looked most affectionately at me.\n\n“Dear Magwitch, I must tell you now, at last. You understand what I say?”\n\nA gentle pressure on my hand.\n\n“You had a child once, whom you loved and lost.”\n\nA stronger pressure on my hand.\n\n“She lived, and found powerful friends. She is living now. She is a lady and very beautiful. And I love her!”\n\nWith a last faint effort, which would have been powerless but for my yielding to it and assisting it, he raised my hand to his lips. Then, he gently let it sink upon his breast again, with his own hands lying on it. The placid look at the white ceiling came back, and passed away, and his head dropped quietly on his breast.\n\nMindful, then, of what we had read together, I thought of the two men who went up into the Temple to pray, and I knew there were no better words that I could say beside his bed, than “O Lord, be merciful to him a sinner!”\n\n# Chapter LVII.\n\nNow that I was left wholly to myself, I gave notice of my intention to quit the chambers in the Temple as soon as my tenancy could legally determine, and in the meanwhile to underlet them. At once I put bills up in the windows; for, I was in debt, and had scarcely any money, and began to be seriously alarmed by the state of my affairs. I ought rather to write that I should have been alarmed if I had had energy and concentration enough to help me to the clear perception of any truth beyond the fact that I was falling very ill. The late stress upon me had enabled me to put off illness, but not to put it away; I knew that it was coming on me now, and I knew very little else, and was even careless as to that.\n\nFor a day or two, I lay on the sofa, or on the floor,—anywhere, according as I happened to sink down,—with a heavy head and aching limbs, and no purpose, and no power. Then there came, one night which appeared of great duration, and which teemed with anxiety and horror; and when in the morning I tried to sit up in my bed and think of it, I found I could not do so.\n\nWhether I really had been down in Garden Court in the dead of the night, groping about for the boat that I supposed to be there; whether I had two or three times come to myself on the staircase with great terror, not knowing how I had got out of bed; whether I had found myself lighting the lamp, possessed by the idea that he was coming up the stairs, and that the lights were blown out; whether I had been inexpressibly harassed by the distracted talking, laughing, and groaning of some one, and had half suspected those sounds to be of my own making; whether there had been a closed iron furnace in a dark corner of the room, and a voice had called out, over and over again, that Miss Havisham was consuming within it,—these were things that I tried to settle with myself and get into some order, as I lay that morning on my bed. But the vapour of a limekiln would come between me and them, disordering them all, and it was through the vapour at last that I saw two men looking at me.\n\n“What do you want?” I asked, starting; “I don’t know you.”\n\n“Well, sir,” returned one of them, bending down and touching me on the shoulder, “this is a matter that you’ll soon arrange, I dare say, but you’re arrested.”\n\n“What is the debt?”\n\n“Hundred and twenty-three pound, fifteen, six. Jeweller’s account, I think.”\n\n“What is to be done?”\n\n“You had better come to my house,” said the man. “I keep a very nice house.”\n\nI made some attempt to get up and dress myself. When I next attended to them, they were standing a little off from the bed, looking at me. I still lay there.\n\n“You see my state,” said I. “I would come with you if I could; but indeed I am quite unable. If you take me from here, I think I shall die by the way.”\n\nPerhaps they replied, or argued the point, or tried to encourage me to believe that I was better than I thought. Forasmuch as they hang in my memory by only this one slender thread, I don’t know what they did, except that they forbore to remove me.\n\nThat I had a fever and was avoided, that I suffered greatly, that I often lost my reason, that the time seemed interminable, that I confounded impossible existences with my own identity; that I was a brick in the house-wall, and yet entreating to be released from the giddy place where the builders had set me; that I was a steel beam of a vast engine, clashing and whirling over a gulf, and yet that I implored in my own person to have the engine stopped, and my part in it hammered off; that I passed through these phases of disease, I know of my own remembrance, and did in some sort know at the time. That I sometimes struggled with real people, in the belief that they were murderers, and that I would all at once comprehend that they meant to do me good, and would then sink exhausted in their arms, and suffer them to lay me down, I also knew at the time. But, above all, I knew that there was a constant tendency in all these people,—who, when I was very ill, would present all kinds of extraordinary transformations of the human face, and would be much dilated in size,—above all, I say, I knew that there was an extraordinary tendency in all these people, sooner or later, to settle down into the likeness of Joe.\n\nAfter I had turned the worst point of my illness, I began to notice that while all its other features changed, this one consistent feature did not change. Whoever came about me, still settled down into Joe. I opened my eyes in the night, and I saw, in the great chair at the bedside, Joe. I opened my eyes in the day, and, sitting on the window-seat, smoking his pipe in the shaded open window, still I saw Joe. I asked for cooling drink, and the dear hand that gave it me was Joe’s. I sank back on my pillow after drinking, and the face that looked so hopefully and tenderly upon me was the face of Joe.\n\nAt last, one day, I took courage, and said, “Is it Joe?”\n\nAnd the dear old home-voice answered, “Which it air, old chap.”\n\n“O Joe, you break my heart! Look angry at me, Joe. Strike me, Joe. Tell me of my ingratitude. Don’t be so good to me!”\n\nFor Joe had actually laid his head down on the pillow at my side, and put his arm round my neck, in his joy that I knew him.\n\n“Which dear old Pip, old chap,” said Joe, “you and me was ever friends. And when you’re well enough to go out for a ride—what larks!”\n\nAfter which, Joe withdrew to the window, and stood with his back towards me, wiping his eyes. And as my extreme weakness prevented me from getting up and going to him, I lay there, penitently whispering, “O God bless him! O God bless this gentle Christian man!”\n\nJoe’s eyes were red when I next found him beside me; but I was holding his hand, and we both felt happy.\n\n“How long, dear Joe?”\n\n“Which you meantersay, Pip, how long have your illness lasted, dear old chap?”\n\n“Yes, Joe.”\n\n“It’s the end of May, Pip. To-morrow is the first of June.”\n\n“And have you been here all that time, dear Joe?”\n\n“Pretty nigh, old chap. For, as I says to Biddy when the news of your being ill were brought by letter, which it were brought by the post, and being formerly single he is now married though underpaid for a deal of walking and shoe-leather, but wealth were not a object on his part, and marriage were the great wish of his hart—”\n\n“It is so delightful to hear you, Joe! But I interrupt you in what you said to Biddy.”\n\n“Which it were,” said Joe, “that how you might be amongst strangers, and that how you and me having been ever friends, a wisit at such a moment might not prove unacceptabobble. And Biddy, her word were, ‘Go to him, without loss of time.’ That,” said Joe, summing up with his judicial air, “were the word of Biddy. ‘Go to him,’ Biddy say, ‘without loss of time.’ In short, I shouldn’t greatly deceive you,” Joe added, after a little grave reflection, “if I represented to you that the word of that young woman were, ‘without a minute’s loss of time.’”\n\nThere Joe cut himself short, and informed me that I was to be talked to in great moderation, and that I was to take a little nourishment at stated frequent times, whether I felt inclined for it or not, and that I was to submit myself to all his orders. So I kissed his hand, and lay quiet, while he proceeded to indite a note to Biddy, with my love in it.\n\nEvidently Biddy had taught Joe to write. As I lay in bed looking at him, it made me, in my weak state, cry again with pleasure to see the pride with which he set about his letter. My bedstead, divested of its curtains, had been removed, with me upon it, into the sitting-room, as the airiest and largest, and the carpet had been taken away, and the room kept always fresh and wholesome night and day. At my own writing-table, pushed into a corner and cumbered with little bottles, Joe now sat down to his great work, first choosing a pen from the pen-tray as if it were a chest of large tools, and tucking up his sleeves as if he were going to wield a crow-bar or sledgehammer. It was necessary for Joe to hold on heavily to the table with his left elbow, and to get his right leg well out behind him, before he could begin; and when he did begin he made every downstroke so slowly that it might have been six feet long, while at every upstroke I could hear his pen spluttering extensively. He had a curious idea that the inkstand was on the side of him where it was not, and constantly dipped his pen into space, and seemed quite satisfied with the result. Occasionally, he was tripped up by some orthographical stumbling-block; but on the whole he got on very well indeed; and when he had signed his name, and had removed a finishing blot from the paper to the crown of his head with his two forefingers, he got up and hovered about the table, trying the effect of his performance from various points of view, as it lay there, with unbounded satisfaction.\n\nNot to make Joe uneasy by talking too much, even if I had been able to talk much, I deferred asking him about Miss Havisham until next day. He shook his head when I then asked him if she had recovered.\n\n“Is she dead, Joe?”\n\n“Why you see, old chap,” said Joe, in a tone of remonstrance, and by way of getting at it by degrees, “I wouldn’t go so far as to say that, for that’s a deal to say; but she ain’t—”\n\n“Living, Joe?”\n“That’s nigher where it is,” said Joe; “she ain’t living.”\n\n“Did she linger long, Joe?”\n\n“Arter you was took ill, pretty much about what you might call (if you was put to it) a week,” said Joe; still determined, on my account, to come at everything by degrees.\n\n“Dear Joe, have you heard what becomes of her property?”\n\n“Well, old chap,” said Joe, “it do appear that she had settled the most of it, which I meantersay tied it up, on Miss Estella. But she had wrote out a little coddleshell in her own hand a day or two afore the accident, leaving a cool four thousand to Mr. Matthew Pocket. And why, do you suppose, above all things, Pip, she left that cool four thousand unto him? ‘Because of Pip’s account of him, the said Matthew.’ I am told by Biddy, that air the writing,” said Joe, repeating the legal turn as if it did him infinite good, “‘account of him the said Matthew.’ And a cool four thousand, Pip!”\n\nI never discovered from whom Joe derived the conventional temperature of the four thousand pounds; but it appeared to make the sum of money more to him, and he had a manifest relish in insisting on its being cool.\n\nThis account gave me great joy, as it perfected the only good thing I had done. I asked Joe whether he had heard if any of the other relations had any legacies?\n\n“Miss Sarah,” said Joe, “she have twenty-five pound perannium fur to buy pills, on account of being bilious. Miss Georgiana, she have twenty pound down. Mrs.—what’s the name of them wild beasts with humps, old chap?”\n\n“Camels?” said I, wondering why he could possibly want to know.\n\nJoe nodded. “Mrs. Camels,” by which I presently understood he meant Camilla, “she have five pound fur to buy rushlights to put her in spirits when she wake up in the night.”\n\nThe accuracy of these recitals was sufficiently obvious to me, to give me great confidence in Joe’s information. “And now,” said Joe, “you ain’t that strong yet, old chap, that you can take in more nor one additional shovelful to-day. Old Orlick he’s been a bustin’ open a dwelling-ouse.”\n\n“Whose?” said I.\n\n“Not, I grant you, but what his manners is given to blusterous,” said Joe, apologetically; “still, a Englishman’s ouse is his Castle, and castles must not be busted ’cept when done in war time. And wotsume’er the failings on his part, he were a corn and seedsman in his hart.”\n\n“Is it Pumblechook’s house that has been broken into, then?”\n\n“That’s it, Pip,” said Joe; “and they took his till, and they took his cash-box, and they drinked his wine, and they partook of his wittles, and they slapped his face, and they pulled his nose, and they tied him up to his bedpust, and they giv’ him a dozen, and they stuffed his mouth full of flowering annuals to prewent his crying out. But he knowed Orlick, and Orlick’s in the county jail.”\n\nBy these approaches we arrived at unrestricted conversation. I was slow to gain strength, but I did slowly and surely become less weak, and Joe stayed with me, and I fancied I was little Pip again.\n\nFor the tenderness of Joe was so beautifully proportioned to my need, that I was like a child in his hands. He would sit and talk to me in the old confidence, and with the old simplicity, and in the old unassertive protecting way, so that I would half believe that all my life since the days of the old kitchen was one of the mental troubles of the fever that was gone. He did everything for me except the household work, for which he had engaged a very decent woman, after paying off the laundress on his first arrival. “Which I do assure you, Pip,” he would often say, in explanation of that liberty; “I found her a tapping the spare bed, like a cask of beer, and drawing off the feathers in a bucket, for sale. Which she would have tapped yourn next, and draw’d it off with you a laying on it, and was then a carrying away the coals gradiwally in the soup-tureen and wegetable-dishes, and the wine and spirits in your Wellington boots.”\n\nWe looked forward to the day when I should go out for a ride, as we had once looked forward to the day of my apprenticeship. And when the day came, and an open carriage was got into the Lane, Joe wrapped me up, took me in his arms, carried me down to it, and put me in, as if I were still the small helpless creature to whom he had so abundantly given of the wealth of his great nature.\n\nAnd Joe got in beside me, and we drove away together into the country, where the rich summer growth was already on the trees and on the grass, and sweet summer scents filled all the air. The day happened to be Sunday, and when I looked on the loveliness around me, and thought how it had grown and changed, and how the little wild-flowers had been forming, and the voices of the birds had been strengthening, by day and by night, under the sun and under the stars, while poor I lay burning and tossing on my bed, the mere remembrance of having burned and tossed there came like a check upon my peace. But when I heard the Sunday bells, and looked around a little more upon the outspread beauty, I felt that I was not nearly thankful enough,—that I was too weak yet to be even that,—and I laid my head on Joe’s shoulder, as I had laid it long ago when he had taken me to the Fair or where not, and it was too much for my young senses.\n\nMore composure came to me after a while, and we talked as we used to talk, lying on the grass at the old Battery. There was no change whatever in Joe. Exactly what he had been in my eyes then, he was in my eyes still; just as simply faithful, and as simply right.\n\nWhen we got back again, and he lifted me out, and carried me—so easily!—across the court and up the stairs, I thought of that eventful Christmas Day when he had carried me over the marshes. We had not yet made any allusion to my change of fortune, nor did I know how much of my late history he was acquainted with. I was so doubtful of myself now, and put so much trust in him, that I could not satisfy myself whether I ought to refer to it when he did not.\n\n“Have you heard, Joe,” I asked him that evening, upon further consideration, as he smoked his pipe at the window, “who my patron was?”\n\n“I heerd,” returned Joe, “as it were not Miss Havisham, old chap.”\n\n“Did you hear who it was, Joe?”\n\n“Well! I heerd as it were a person what sent the person what giv’ you the bank-notes at the Jolly Bargemen, Pip.”\n\n“So it was.”\n\n“Astonishing!” said Joe, in the placidest way.\n\n“Did you hear that he was dead, Joe?” I presently asked, with increasing diffidence.\n\n“Which? Him as sent the bank-notes, Pip?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“I think,” said Joe, after meditating a long time, and looking rather evasively at the window-seat, “as I did hear tell that how he were something or another in a general way in that direction.”\n\n“Did you hear anything of his circumstances, Joe?”\n\n“Not partickler, Pip.”\n\n“If you would like to hear, Joe—” I was beginning, when Joe got up and came to my sofa.\n\n“Lookee here, old chap,” said Joe, bending over me. “Ever the best of friends; ain’t us, Pip?”\n\nI was ashamed to answer him.\n\n“Wery good, then,” said Joe, as if I had answered; “that’s all right; that’s agreed upon. Then why go into subjects, old chap, which as betwixt two sech must be for ever onnecessary? There’s subjects enough as betwixt two sech, without onnecessary ones. Lord! To think of your poor sister and her Rampages! And don’t you remember Tickler?”\n\n“I do indeed, Joe.”\n\n“Lookee here, old chap,” said Joe. “I done what I could to keep you and Tickler in sunders, but my power were not always fully equal to my inclinations. For when your poor sister had a mind to drop into you, it were not so much,” said Joe, in his favourite argumentative way, “that she dropped into me too, if I put myself in opposition to her, but that she dropped into you always heavier for it. I noticed that. It ain’t a grab at a man’s whisker, not yet a shake or two of a man (to which your sister was quite welcome), that ’ud put a man off from getting a little child out of punishment. But when that little child is dropped into heavier for that grab of whisker or shaking, then that man naterally up and says to himself, ‘Where is the good as you are a-doing? I grant you I see the ’arm,’ says the man, ‘but I don’t see the good. I call upon you, sir, therefore, to pint out the good.’”\n\n“The man says?” I observed, as Joe waited for me to speak.\n\n“The man says,” Joe assented. “Is he right, that man?”\n\n“Dear Joe, he is always right.”\n\n“Well, old chap,” said Joe, “then abide by your words. If he’s always right (which in general he’s more likely wrong), he’s right when he says this: Supposing ever you kep any little matter to yourself, when you was a little child, you kep it mostly because you know’d as J. Gargery’s power to part you and Tickler in sunders were not fully equal to his inclinations. Theerfore, think no more of it as betwixt two sech, and do not let us pass remarks upon onnecessary subjects. Biddy giv’ herself a deal o’ trouble with me afore I left (for I am almost awful dull), as I should view it in this light, and, viewing it in this light, as I should so put it. Both of which,” said Joe, quite charmed with his logical arrangement, “being done, now this to you a true friend, say. Namely. You mustn’t go a overdoing on it, but you must have your supper and your wine and water, and you must be put betwixt the sheets.”\n\nThe delicacy with which Joe dismissed this theme, and the sweet tact and kindness with which Biddy—who with her woman’s wit had found me out so soon—had prepared him for it, made a deep impression on my mind. But whether Joe knew how poor I was, and how my great expectations had all dissolved, like our own marsh mists before the sun, I could not understand.\n\nAnother thing in Joe that I could not understand when it first began to develop itself, but which I soon arrived at a sorrowful comprehension of, was this: As I became stronger and better, Joe became a little less easy with me. In my weakness and entire dependence on him, the dear fellow had fallen into the old tone, and called me by the old names, the dear “old Pip, old chap,” that now were music in my ears. I too had fallen into the old ways, only happy and thankful that he let me. But, imperceptibly, though I held by them fast, Joe’s hold upon them began to slacken; and whereas I wondered at this, at first, I soon began to understand that the cause of it was in me, and that the fault of it was all mine.\n\nAh! Had I given Joe no reason to doubt my constancy, and to think that in prosperity I should grow cold to him and cast him off? Had I given Joe’s innocent heart no cause to feel instinctively that as I got stronger, his hold upon me would be weaker, and that he had better loosen it in time and let me go, before I plucked myself away?\n\nIt was on the third or fourth occasion of my going out walking in the Temple Gardens leaning on Joe’s arm, that I saw this change in him very plainly. We had been sitting in the bright warm sunlight, looking at the river, and I chanced to say as we got up,—\n\n“See, Joe! I can walk quite strongly. Now, you shall see me walk back by myself.”\n\n“Which do not overdo it, Pip,” said Joe; “but I shall be happy fur to see you able, sir.”\n\nThe last word grated on me; but how could I remonstrate! I walked no further than the gate of the gardens, and then pretended to be weaker than I was, and asked Joe for his arm. Joe gave it me, but was thoughtful.\n\nI, for my part, was thoughtful too; for, how best to check this growing change in Joe was a great perplexity to my remorseful thoughts. That I was ashamed to tell him exactly how I was placed, and what I had come down to, I do not seek to conceal; but I hope my reluctance was not quite an unworthy one. He would want to help me out of his little savings, I knew, and I knew that he ought not to help me, and that I must not suffer him to do it.\n\nIt was a thoughtful evening with both of us. But, before we went to bed, I had resolved that I would wait over to-morrow,—to-morrow being Sunday,—and would begin my new course with the new week. On Monday morning I would speak to Joe about this change, I would lay aside this last vestige of reserve, I would tell him what I had in my thoughts (that Secondly, not yet arrived at), and why I had not decided to go out to Herbert, and then the change would be conquered for ever. As I cleared, Joe cleared, and it seemed as though he had sympathetically arrived at a resolution too.\n\nWe had a quiet day on the Sunday, and we rode out into the country, and then walked in the fields.\n\n“I feel thankful that I have been ill, Joe,” I said.\n\n“Dear old Pip, old chap, you’re a’most come round, sir.”\n\n“It has been a memorable time for me, Joe.”\n\n“Likeways for myself, sir,” Joe returned.\n\n“We have had a time together, Joe, that I can never forget. There were days once, I know, that I did for a while forget; but I never shall forget these.”\n\n“Pip,” said Joe, appearing a little hurried and troubled, “there has been larks. And, dear sir, what have been betwixt us—have been.”\n\nAt night, when I had gone to bed, Joe came into my room, as he had done all through my recovery. He asked me if I felt sure that I was as well as in the morning?\n\n“Yes, dear Joe, quite.”\n\n“And are always a getting stronger, old chap?”\n\n“Yes, dear Joe, steadily.”\n\nJoe patted the coverlet on my shoulder with his great good hand, and said, in what I thought a husky voice, “Good night!”\n\nWhen I got up in the morning, refreshed and stronger yet, I was full of my resolution to tell Joe all, without delay. I would tell him before breakfast. I would dress at once and go to his room and surprise him; for, it was the first day I had been up early. I went to his room, and he was not there. Not only was he not there, but his box was gone.\n\nI hurried then to the breakfast-table, and on it found a letter. These were its brief contents:—\n\n“Not wishful to intrude I have departured fur you are well again dear Pip and will do better without\n\n“P.S. Ever the best of friends.”\n\nEnclosed in the letter was a receipt for the debt and costs on which I had been arrested. Down to that moment, I had vainly supposed that my creditor had withdrawn, or suspended proceedings until I should be quite recovered. I had never dreamed of Joe’s having paid the money; but Joe had paid it, and the receipt was in his name.\n\nWhat remained for me now, but to follow him to the dear old forge, and there to have out my disclosure to him, and my penitent remonstrance with him, and there to relieve my mind and heart of that reserved Secondly, which had begun as a vague something lingering in my thoughts, and had formed into a settled purpose?\n\nThe purpose was, that I would go to Biddy, that I would show her how humbled and repentant I came back, that I would tell her how I had lost all I once hoped for, that I would remind her of our old confidences in my first unhappy time. Then I would say to her, “Biddy, I think you once liked me very well, when my errant heart, even while it strayed away from you, was quieter and better with you than it ever has been since. If you can like me only half as well once more, if you can take me with all my faults and disappointments on my head, if you can receive me like a forgiven child (and indeed I am as sorry, Biddy, and have as much need of a hushing voice and a soothing hand), I hope I am a little worthier of you that I was,—not much, but a little. And, Biddy, it shall rest with you to say whether I shall work at the forge with Joe, or whether I shall try for any different occupation down in this country, or whether we shall go away to a distant place where an opportunity awaits me which I set aside, when it was offered, until I knew your answer. And now, dear Biddy, if you can tell me that you will go through the world with me, you will surely make it a better world for me, and me a better man for it, and I will try hard to make it a better world for you.”\n\nSuch was my purpose. After three days more of recovery, I went down to the old place to put it in execution. And how I sped in it is all I have left to tell.\n\n# Chapter LVIII.\n\nThe tidings of my high fortunes having had a heavy fall had got down to my native place and its neighbourhood before I got there. I found the Blue Boar in possession of the intelligence, and I found that it made a great change in the Boar’s demeanour. Whereas the Boar had cultivated my good opinion with warm assiduity when I was coming into property, the Boar was exceedingly cool on the subject now that I was going out of property.\n\nIt was evening when I arrived, much fatigued by the journey I had so often made so easily. The Boar could not put me into my usual bedroom, which was engaged (probably by some one who had expectations), and could only assign me a very indifferent chamber among the pigeons and post-chaises up the yard. But I had as sound a sleep in that lodging as in the most superior accommodation the Boar could have given me, and the quality of my dreams was about the same as in the best bedroom.\n\nEarly in the morning, while my breakfast was getting ready, I strolled round by Satis House. There were printed bills on the gate and on bits of carpet hanging out of the windows, announcing a sale by auction of the Household Furniture and Effects, next week. The House itself was to be sold as old building materials, and pulled down. LOT 1 was marked in whitewashed knock-knee letters on the brew house; LOT 2 on that part of the main building which had been so long shut up. Other lots were marked off on other parts of the structure, and the ivy had been torn down to make room for the inscriptions, and much of it trailed low in the dust and was withered already. Stepping in for a moment at the open gate, and looking around me with the uncomfortable air of a stranger who had no business there, I saw the auctioneer’s clerk walking on the casks and telling them off for the information of a catalogue-compiler, pen in hand, who made a temporary desk of the wheeled chair I had so often pushed along to the tune of Old Clem.\n\nWhen I got back to my breakfast in the Boar’s coffee-room, I found Mr. Pumblechook conversing with the landlord. Mr. Pumblechook (not improved in appearance by his late nocturnal adventure) was waiting for me, and addressed me in the following terms:—\n\n“Young man, I am sorry to see you brought low. But what else could be expected! what else could be expected!”\n\nAs he extended his hand with a magnificently forgiving air, and as I was broken by illness and unfit to quarrel, I took it.\n\n“William,” said Mr. Pumblechook to the waiter, “put a muffin on table. And has it come to this! Has it come to this!”\n\nI frowningly sat down to my breakfast. Mr. Pumblechook stood over me and poured out my tea—before I could touch the teapot—with the air of a benefactor who was resolved to be true to the last.\n\n“William,” said Mr. Pumblechook, mournfully, “put the salt on. In happier times,” addressing me, “I think you took sugar? And did you take milk? You did. Sugar and milk. William, bring a watercress.”\n\n“Thank you,” said I, shortly, “but I don’t eat watercresses.”\n\n“You don’t eat ’em,” returned Mr. Pumblechook, sighing and nodding his head several times, as if he might have expected that, and as if abstinence from watercresses were consistent with my downfall. “True. The simple fruits of the earth. No. You needn’t bring any, William.”\n\nI went on with my breakfast, and Mr. Pumblechook continued to stand over me, staring fishily and breathing noisily, as he always did.\n\n“Little more than skin and bone!” mused Mr. Pumblechook, aloud. “And yet when he went from here (I may say with my blessing), and I spread afore him my humble store, like the Bee, he was as plump as a Peach!”\n\nThis reminded me of the wonderful difference between the servile manner in which he had offered his hand in my new prosperity, saying, “May I?” and the ostentatious clemency with which he had just now exhibited the same fat five fingers.\n\n“Hah!” he went on, handing me the bread and butter. “And air you a-going to Joseph?”\n\n“In heaven’s name,” said I, firing in spite of myself, “what does it matter to you where I am going? Leave that teapot alone.”\n\nIt was the worst course I could have taken, because it gave Pumblechook the opportunity he wanted.\n\n“Yes, young man,” said he, releasing the handle of the article in question, retiring a step or two from my table, and speaking for the behoof of the landlord and waiter at the door, “I will leave that teapot alone. You are right, young man. For once you are right. I forgit myself when I take such an interest in your breakfast, as to wish your frame, exhausted by the debilitating effects of prodigygality, to be stimilated by the ’olesome nourishment of your forefathers. And yet,” said Pumblechook, turning to the landlord and waiter, and pointing me out at arm’s length, “this is him as I ever sported with in his days of happy infancy! Tell me not it cannot be; I tell you this is him!”\n\nA low murmur from the two replied. The waiter appeared to be particularly affected.\n\n“This is him,” said Pumblechook, “as I have rode in my shay-cart. This is him as I have seen brought up by hand. This is him untoe the sister of which I was uncle by marriage, as her name was Georgiana M’ria from her own mother, let him deny it if he can!”\n\nThe waiter seemed convinced that I could not deny it, and that it gave the case a black look.\n\n“Young man,” said Pumblechook, screwing his head at me in the old fashion, “you air a-going to Joseph. What does it matter to me, you ask me, where you air a-going? I say to you, Sir, you air a-going to Joseph.”\n\nThe waiter coughed, as if he modestly invited me to get over that.\n\n“Now,” said Pumblechook, and all this with a most exasperating air of saying in the cause of virtue what was perfectly convincing and conclusive, “I will tell you what to say to Joseph. Here is Squires of the Boar present, known and respected in this town, and here is William, which his father’s name was Potkins if I do not deceive myself.”\n\n“You do not, sir,” said William.\n\n“In their presence,” pursued Pumblechook, “I will tell you, young man, what to say to Joseph. Says you, “Joseph, I have this day seen my earliest benefactor and the founder of my fortun’s. I will name no names, Joseph, but so they are pleased to call him up town, and I have seen that man.”\n\n“I swear I don’t see him here,” said I.\n\n“Say that likewise,” retorted Pumblechook. “Say you said that, and even Joseph will probably betray surprise.”\n\n“There you quite mistake him,” said I. “I know better.”\n\n“Says you,” Pumblechook went on, “‘Joseph, I have seen that man, and that man bears you no malice and bears me no malice. He knows your character, Joseph, and is well acquainted with your pig-headedness and ignorance; and he knows my character, Joseph, and he knows my want of gratitoode. Yes, Joseph,’ says you,” here Pumblechook shook his head and hand at me, “‘he knows my total deficiency of common human gratitoode. He knows it, Joseph, as none can. You do not know it, Joseph, having no call to know it, but that man do.’”\n\nWindy donkey as he was, it really amazed me that he could have the face to talk thus to mine.\n\n“Says you, ‘Joseph, he gave me a little message, which I will now repeat. It was that, in my being brought low, he saw the finger of Providence. He knowed that finger when he saw Joseph, and he saw it plain. It pinted out this writing, Joseph. Reward of ingratitoode to his earliest benefactor, and founder of fortun’s. But that man said he did not repent of what he had done, Joseph. Not at all. It was right to do it, it was kind to do it, it was benevolent to do it, and he would do it again.’”\n\n“It’s pity,” said I, scornfully, as I finished my interrupted breakfast, “that the man did not say what he had done and would do again.”\n\n“Squires of the Boar!” Pumblechook was now addressing the landlord, “and William! I have no objections to your mentioning, either up town or down town, if such should be your wishes, that it was right to do it, kind to do it, benevolent to do it, and that I would do it again.”\n\nWith those words the Impostor shook them both by the hand, with an air, and left the house; leaving me much more astonished than delighted by the virtues of that same indefinite “it.” I was not long after him in leaving the house too, and when I went down the High Street I saw him holding forth (no doubt to the same effect) at his shop door to a select group, who honoured me with very unfavourable glances as I passed on the opposite side of the way.\n\nBut, it was only the pleasanter to turn to Biddy and to Joe, whose great forbearance shone more brightly than before, if that could be, contrasted with this brazen pretender. I went towards them slowly, for my limbs were weak, but with a sense of increasing relief as I drew nearer to them, and a sense of leaving arrogance and untruthfulness further and further behind.\n\nThe June weather was delicious. The sky was blue, the larks were soaring high over the green corn, I thought all that countryside more beautiful and peaceful by far than I had ever known it to be yet. Many pleasant pictures of the life that I would lead there, and of the change for the better that would come over my character when I had a guiding spirit at my side whose simple faith and clear home wisdom I had proved, beguiled my way. They awakened a tender emotion in me; for my heart was softened by my return, and such a change had come to pass, that I felt like one who was toiling home barefoot from distant travel, and whose wanderings had lasted many years.\n\nThe schoolhouse where Biddy was mistress I had never seen; but, the little roundabout lane by which I entered the village, for quietness’ sake, took me past it. I was disappointed to find that the day was a holiday; no children were there, and Biddy’s house was closed. Some hopeful notion of seeing her, busily engaged in her daily duties, before she saw me, had been in my mind and was defeated.\n\nBut the forge was a very short distance off, and I went towards it under the sweet green limes, listening for the clink of Joe’s hammer. Long after I ought to have heard it, and long after I had fancied I heard it and found it but a fancy, all was still. The limes were there, and the white thorns were there, and the chestnut-trees were there, and their leaves rustled harmoniously when I stopped to listen; but, the clink of Joe’s hammer was not in the midsummer wind.\n\nAlmost fearing, without knowing why, to come in view of the forge, I saw it at last, and saw that it was closed. No gleam of fire, no glittering shower of sparks, no roar of bellows; all shut up, and still.\n\nBut the house was not deserted, and the best parlour seemed to be in use, for there were white curtains fluttering in its window, and the window was open and gay with flowers. I went softly towards it, meaning to peep over the flowers, when Joe and Biddy stood before me, arm in arm.\n\nAt first Biddy gave a cry, as if she thought it was my apparition, but in another moment she was in my embrace. I wept to see her, and she wept to see me; I, because she looked so fresh and pleasant; she, because I looked so worn and white.\n\n“But dear Biddy, how smart you are!”\n\n“Yes, dear Pip.”\n\n“And Joe, how smart you are!”\n\n“Yes, dear old Pip, old chap.”\n\nI looked at both of them, from one to the other, and then—\n\n“It’s my wedding-day!” cried Biddy, in a burst of happiness, “and I am married to Joe!”\n\nThey had taken me into the kitchen, and I had laid my head down on the old deal table. Biddy held one of my hands to her lips, and Joe’s restoring touch was on my shoulder. “Which he warn’t strong enough, my dear, fur to be surprised,” said Joe. And Biddy said, “I ought to have thought of it, dear Joe, but I was too happy.” They were both so overjoyed to see me, so proud to see me, so touched by my coming to them, so delighted that I should have come by accident to make their day complete!\n\nMy first thought was one of great thankfulness that I had never breathed this last baffled hope to Joe. How often, while he was with me in my illness, had it risen to my lips! How irrevocable would have been his knowledge of it, if he had remained with me but another hour!\n\n“Dear Biddy,” said I, “you have the best husband in the whole world, and if you could have seen him by my bed you would have—But no, you couldn’t love him better than you do.”\n\n“No, I couldn’t indeed,” said Biddy.\n\n“And, dear Joe, you have the best wife in the whole world, and she will make you as happy as even you deserve to be, you dear, good, noble Joe!”\n\nJoe looked at me with a quivering lip, and fairly put his sleeve before his eyes.\n\n“And Joe and Biddy both, as you have been to church to-day, and are in charity and love with all mankind, receive my humble thanks for all you have done for me, and all I have so ill repaid! And when I say that I am going away within the hour, for I am soon going abroad, and that I shall never rest until I have worked for the money with which you have kept me out of prison, and have sent it to you, don’t think, dear Joe and Biddy, that if I could repay it a thousand times over, I suppose I could cancel a farthing of the debt I owe you, or that I would do so if I could!”\n\nThey were both melted by these words, and both entreated me to say no more.\n\n“But I must say more. Dear Joe, I hope you will have children to love, and that some little fellow will sit in this chimney-corner of a winter night, who may remind you of another little fellow gone out of it for ever. Don’t tell him, Joe, that I was thankless; don’t tell him, Biddy, that I was ungenerous and unjust; only tell him that I honoured you both, because you were both so good and true, and that, as your child, I said it would be natural to him to grow up a much better man than I did.”\n\n“I ain’t a-going,” said Joe, from behind his sleeve, “to tell him nothink o’ that natur, Pip. Nor Biddy ain’t. Nor yet no one ain’t.”\n\n“And now, though I know you have already done it in your own kind hearts, pray tell me, both, that you forgive me! Pray let me hear you say the words, that I may carry the sound of them away with me, and then I shall be able to believe that you can trust me, and think better of me, in the time to come!”\n\n“O dear old Pip, old chap,” said Joe. “God knows as I forgive you, if I have anythink to forgive!”\n\n“Amen! And God knows I do!” echoed Biddy.\n\n“Now let me go up and look at my old little room, and rest there a few minutes by myself. And then, when I have eaten and drunk with you, go with me as far as the finger-post, dear Joe and Biddy, before we say good-bye!”\n\nI sold all I had, and put aside as much as I could, for a composition with my creditors,—who gave me ample time to pay them in full,—and I went out and joined Herbert. Within a month, I had quitted England, and within two months I was clerk to Clarriker and Co., and within four months I assumed my first undivided responsibility. For the beam across the parlour ceiling at Mill Pond Bank had then ceased to tremble under old Bill Barley’s growls and was at peace, and Herbert had gone away to marry Clara, and I was left in sole charge of the Eastern Branch until he brought her back.\n\nMany a year went round before I was a partner in the House; but I lived happily with Herbert and his wife, and lived frugally, and paid my debts, and maintained a constant correspondence with Biddy and Joe. It was not until I became third in the Firm, that Clarriker betrayed me to Herbert; but he then declared that the secret of Herbert’s partnership had been long enough upon his conscience, and he must tell it. So he told it, and Herbert was as much moved as amazed, and the dear fellow and I were not the worse friends for the long concealment. I must not leave it to be supposed that we were ever a great House, or that we made mints of money. We were not in a grand way of business, but we had a good name, and worked for our profits, and did very well. We owed so much to Herbert’s ever cheerful industry and readiness, that I often wondered how I had conceived that old idea of his inaptitude, until I was one day enlightened by the reflection, that perhaps the inaptitude had never been in him at all, but had been in me.\n\n# Chapter LIX.\n\nFor eleven years, I had not seen Joe nor Biddy with my bodily eyes,—though they had both been often before my fancy in the East,—when, upon an evening in December, an hour or two after dark, I laid my hand softly on the latch of the old kitchen door. I touched it so softly that I was not heard, and looked in unseen. There, smoking his pipe in the old place by the kitchen firelight, as hale and as strong as ever, though a little grey, sat Joe; and there, fenced into the corner with Joe’s leg, and sitting on my own little stool looking at the fire, was—I again!\n\n“We giv’ him the name of Pip for your sake, dear old chap,” said Joe, delighted, when I took another stool by the child’s side (but I did not rumple his hair), “and we hoped he might grow a little bit like you, and we think he do.”\n\nI thought so too, and I took him out for a walk next morning, and we talked immensely, understanding one another to perfection. And I took him down to the churchyard, and set him on a certain tombstone there, and he showed me from that elevation which stone was sacred to the memory of Philip Pirrip, late of this Parish, and Also Georgiana, Wife of the Above.\n\n“Biddy,” said I, when I talked with her after dinner, as her little girl lay sleeping in her lap, “you must give Pip to me one of these days; or lend him, at all events.”\n\n“No, no,” said Biddy, gently. “You must marry.”\n\n“So Herbert and Clara say, but I don’t think I shall, Biddy. I have so settled down in their home, that it’s not at all likely. I am already quite an old bachelor.”\n\nBiddy looked down at her child, and put its little hand to her lips, and then put the good matronly hand with which she had touched it into mine. There was something in the action, and in the light pressure of Biddy’s wedding-ring, that had a very pretty eloquence in it.\n\n“Dear Pip,” said Biddy, “you are sure you don’t fret for her?”\n\n“O no,—I think not, Biddy.”\n\n“Tell me as an old, old friend. Have you quite forgotten her?\n\n“My dear Biddy, I have forgotten nothing in my life that ever had a foremost place there, and little that ever had any place there. But that poor dream, as I once used to call it, has all gone by, Biddy,—all gone by!”\n\nNevertheless, I knew, while I said those words, that I secretly intended to revisit the site of the old house that evening, alone, for her sake. Yes, even so. For Estella’s sake.\n\nI had heard of her as leading a most unhappy life, and as being separated from her husband, who had used her with great cruelty, and who had become quite renowned as a compound of pride, avarice, brutality, and meanness. And I had heard of the death of her husband, from an accident consequent on his ill-treatment of a horse. This release had befallen her some two years before; for anything I knew, she was married again.\n\nThe early dinner hour at Joe’s, left me abundance of time, without hurrying my talk with Biddy, to walk over to the old spot before dark. But, what with loitering on the way to look at old objects and to think of old times, the day had quite declined when I came to the place.\n\nThere was no house now, no brewery, no building whatever left, but the wall of the old garden. The cleared space had been enclosed with a rough fence, and looking over it, I saw that some of the old ivy had struck root anew, and was growing green on low quiet mounds of ruin. A gate in the fence standing ajar, I pushed it open, and went in.\n\nA cold silvery mist had veiled the afternoon, and the moon was not yet up to scatter it. But, the stars were shining beyond the mist, and the moon was coming, and the evening was not dark. I could trace out where every part of the old house had been, and where the brewery had been, and where the gates, and where the casks. I had done so, and was looking along the desolate garden walk, when I beheld a solitary figure in it.\n\nThe figure showed itself aware of me, as I advanced. It had been moving towards me, but it stood still. As I drew nearer, I saw it to be the figure of a woman. As I drew nearer yet, it was about to turn away, when it stopped, and let me come up with it. Then, it faltered, as if much surprised, and uttered my name, and I cried out,—\n\n“Estella!”\n\n“I am greatly changed. I wonder you know me.”\n\nThe freshness of her beauty was indeed gone, but its indescribable majesty and its indescribable charm remained. Those attractions in it, I had seen before; what I had never seen before, was the saddened, softened light of the once proud eyes; what I had never felt before was the friendly touch of the once insensible hand.\n\nWe sat down on a bench that was near, and I said, “After so many years, it is strange that we should thus meet again, Estella, here where our first meeting was! Do you often come back?”\n\n“I have never been here since.”\n“Nor I.”\n\nThe moon began to rise, and I thought of the placid look at the white ceiling, which had passed away. The moon began to rise, and I thought of the pressure on my hand when I had spoken the last words he had heard on earth.\n\nEstella was the next to break the silence that ensued between us.\n\n“I have very often hoped and intended to come back, but have been prevented by many circumstances. Poor, poor old place!”\n\nThe silvery mist was touched with the first rays of the moonlight, and the same rays touched the tears that dropped from her eyes. Not knowing that I saw them, and setting herself to get the better of them, she said quietly,—\n\n“Were you wondering, as you walked along, how it came to be left in this condition?”\n\n“Yes, Estella.”\n\n“The ground belongs to me. It is the only possession I have not relinquished. Everything else has gone from me, little by little, but I have kept this. It was the subject of the only determined resistance I made in all the wretched years.”\n\n“Is it to be built on?”\n\n“At last, it is. I came here to take leave of it before its change. And you,” she said, in a voice of touching interest to a wanderer,—“you live abroad still?”\n\n“Still.”\n“And do well, I am sure?”\n\n“I work pretty hard for a sufficient living, and therefore—yes, I do well.”\n\n“I have often thought of you,” said Estella.\n\n“Have you?”\n\n“Of late, very often. There was a long hard time when I kept far from me the remembrance of what I had thrown away when I was quite ignorant of its worth. But since my duty has not been incompatible with the admission of that remembrance, I have given it a place in my heart.”\n\n“You have always held your place in my heart,” I answered.\n\nAnd we were silent again until she spoke.\n\n“I little thought,” said Estella, “that I should take leave of you in taking leave of this spot. I am very glad to do so.”\n\n“Glad to part again, Estella? To me, parting is a painful thing. To me, the remembrance of our last parting has been ever mournful and painful.”\n\n“But you said to me,” returned Estella, very earnestly, “‘God bless you, God forgive you!’ And if you could say that to me then, you will not hesitate to say that to me now,—now, when suffering has been stronger than all other teaching, and has taught me to understand what your heart used to be. I have been bent and broken, but—I hope—into a better shape. Be as considerate and good to me as you were, and tell me we are friends.”\n\n“We are friends,” said I, rising and bending over her, as she rose from the bench.\n\n“And will continue friends apart,” said Estella.\n\nI took her hand in mine, and we went out of the ruined place; and, as the morning mists had risen long ago when I first left the forge, so the evening mists were rising now, and in all the broad expanse of tranquil light they showed to me, I saw no shadow of another parting from her.";
if(typeof BODIES_TRANS!=="undefined") BODIES_TRANS["gb-1400"]="우리 아버지의 성이 피립이고 내 세례명이 필립이었던 까닭에, 어린 시절 내 혀로는 그 두 이름을 합쳐 핍보다 더 길거나 더 분명하게 발음할 수가 없었다. 그래서 나는 스스로를 핍이라 불렀고, 남들도 나를 핍이라 부르게 되었다.\n\n아버지의 성이 피립이라는 것은 그분의 묘비와 누나, 즉 대장장이와 결혼한 조 가저리 부인의 권위에 따른 것이다. 나는 아버지도 어머니도 본 적이 없고, 두 분의 초상조차 본 적이 없었으므로(사진이 나오기 한참 전의 시절이었으니까), 두 분이 어떤 모습이었을까에 대한 내 첫 상상은 터무니없게도 그 묘비에서 비롯되었다. 아버지 묘비에 새겨진 글자의 모양은, 그분이 네모지고 다부지며 가무잡잡한 데다 검은 고수머리를 한 사람이었으리라는 묘한 생각을 내게 심어 주었다. \"또한 위 사람의 아내 조지애나\"라는 비명의 글씨체와 분위기로부터, 나는 어머니가 주근깨가 있고 병약했으리라는 어린아이다운 결론을 끌어냈다. 부모님의 무덤 옆에 가지런히 한 줄로 늘어선, 길이가 각각 한 자 반쯤 되는 다섯 개의 작은 마름모꼴 돌은 다섯 형제들의 추억에 바쳐진 것이었는데 — 그 형제들은 만인이 겪는 그 싸움 속에서 지극히 일찍이 살기를 포기해 버린 이들이었다 — 그 돌들 덕분에 나는, 형들이 모두 손을 바지 주머니에 찔러 넣은 채 등을 대고 태어났으며 이승에 사는 동안 한 번도 그 손을 빼지 않았으리라는 믿음을 경건하게 품게 되었다.\n\n우리 고장은 강가 저 아래, 강물이 굽이쳐 흐르는 길로 바다에서 이십 마일 안쪽에 있는 습지 지방이었다. 사물의 정체에 대한 내 최초의 가장 또렷하고 폭넓은 인상은, 저녁이 가까워 오던 어느 잊지 못할 음산한 오후에 얻은 것이었던 듯싶다. 바로 그런 때에 나는 비로소 확실히 깨달았다. 쐐기풀이 무성하게 자란 이 황량한 곳이 교회 묘지라는 것을, 그리고 이 교구의 고인이 된 필립 피립과 또한 위 사람의 아내 조지애나가 죽어 묻혀 있다는 것을, 그리고 앞서 말한 두 사람의 어린 자식들인 알렉산더, 바돌로뮤, 에이브러햄, 토비아스, 로저 또한 죽어 묻혀 있다는 것을, 그리고 도랑과 둑과 문으로 가로질러지고 군데군데 흩어진 소 떼가 풀을 뜯고 있는, 묘지 너머의 어둡고 평평한 황야가 습지라는 것을, 그리고 그 너머의 나지막한 납빛 선이 강이라는 것을, 그리고 바람이 휘몰아쳐 나오는 저 멀리 야만스러운 소굴이 바다라는 것을, 그리고 그 모든 것이 무서워지기 시작해 울먹이는 작은 떨림 덩어리가 핍이라는 것을.\n\n\"시끄러워!\" 교회 현관 옆 무덤들 사이에서 한 사내가 벌떡 일어서며 무시무시한 목소리로 외쳤다. \"가만있어, 이 작은 악마야, 안 그러면 목을 따 버리겠다!\"\n\n거친 잿빛 옷을 걸치고, 다리에는 커다란 쇠고랑을 찬 무서운 사내였다. 모자도 없고, 신발은 다 해졌으며, 머리에는 낡은 누더기를 두른 사내였다. 물에 흠뻑 젖고, 진흙을 뒤집어쓰고, 돌에 차여 절뚝거리고, 부싯돌에 베이고, 쐐기풀에 쏘이고, 가시덤불에 찢긴 사내였다. 절뚝거리고, 부들부들 떨고, 노려보고, 으르렁대던 사내였다. 그리고 내 턱을 움켜쥐었을 때 그 입 안에서 이가 딱딱 부딪치던 사내였다.\n\n\"아! 제 목을 따지 마세요, 아저씨.\" 나는 겁에 질려 애원했다. \"제발 그러지 마세요, 아저씨.\"\n\n\"이름을 말해 봐!\" 사내가 말했다. \"어서!\"\n\n\"핍이에요, 아저씨.\"\n\n\"한 번 더.\" 사내가 나를 노려보며 말했다. \"똑똑히 말해!\"\n\n\"핍이요. 핍이에요, 아저씨.\"\n\n\"어디 사는지 보여 봐.\" 사내가 말했다. \"그곳을 가리켜!\"\n\n나는 교회에서 일 마일쯤 떨어진, 오리나무와 가지치기한 나무들 사이 강가 평지에 자리 잡은 우리 마을을 가리켰다.\n\n사내는 잠시 나를 바라보더니, 나를 거꾸로 뒤집어 주머니를 비워 냈다. 그 안에는 빵 한 조각밖에 없었다. 교회가 제자리로 돌아왔을 때 — 사내가 어찌나 갑작스럽고 힘이 셌던지 교회가 내 눈앞에서 곤두박질치고 첨탑이 내 발밑에 보일 정도였으니까 — 그러니까 교회가 제자리로 돌아왔을 때, 나는 어느 높은 묘비 위에 앉아 부들부들 떨고 있었고, 그동안 사내는 그 빵을 게걸스레 먹어 치웠다.\n\n\"이 어린 개자식아.\" 사내가 입맛을 다시며 말했다. \"뺨이 어찌 그리 통통하냐.\"\n\n내 뺨이 통통했던 건 사실이라고 생각한다. 비록 그 시절 나는 나이에 비해 몸집이 작고 튼튼하지도 못했지만.\n\n\"제기랄, 이걸 먹어 치우지 못할 것도 없지.\" 사내가 머리를 위협적으로 흔들며 말했다. \"게다가 그러고 싶은 마음이 절반은 든단 말이야!\"\n\n나는 부디 그러지 말아 달라고 간절히 빌었고, 사내가 나를 앉혀 놓은 묘비를 더욱 단단히 붙들었다. 한편으로는 그 위에서 떨어지지 않으려고, 또 한편으로는 울음을 터뜨리지 않으려고.\n\n\"자 이봐!\" 사내가 말했다. \"네 어미는 어디 있냐?\"\n\n\"저기요, 아저씨!\" 내가 말했다.\n\n사내는 흠칫 놀라더니 잠깐 달려가다가 멈춰 서서 어깨 너머로 돌아보았다.\n\n\"저기요, 아저씨!\" 나는 머뭇거리며 설명했다. \"또한 조지애나. 저게 우리 엄마예요.\"\n\n\"아!\" 사내가 돌아오며 말했다. \"그럼 네 어미 옆에 있는 게 네 아비냐?\"\n\n\"네, 아저씨.\" 내가 말했다. \"그분도요. 이 교구의 고인이세요.\"\n\n\"하!\" 그러더니 사내가 곰곰이 생각하며 중얼거렸다. \"넌 누구랑 사느냐 — 살게 내버려 둔다고 치고 말이야. 아직 마음을 정하진 못했다만.\"\n\n\"누나랑 살아요, 아저씨. 조 가저리 부인이요. 대장장이 조 가저리의 아내예요, 아저씨.\"\n\n\"대장장이라, 응?\" 사내가 말했다. 그러더니 자기 다리를 내려다보았다.\n\n사내는 어둡게 자기 다리와 나를 몇 번이나 번갈아 보더니, 내가 앉은 묘비로 더 가까이 다가와 두 팔을 잡고는 붙들 수 있는 데까지 나를 뒤로 젖혔다. 그래서 그의 두 눈은 더없이 강렬하게 내 눈을 내리쏘았고, 내 두 눈은 더없이 무력하게 그의 눈을 올려다보았다.\n\n\"자 이봐.\" 사내가 말했다. \"문제는 너를 살려 두느냐 마느냐다. 줄칼이 뭔지 알지?\"\n\n\"네, 아저씨.\"\n\n\"그럼 먹을거리가 뭔지도 알지?\"\n\n\"네, 아저씨.\"\n\n질문을 던질 때마다 사내는 나를 조금씩 더 젖혀, 더 큰 무력감과 위기감을 안겨 주었다.\n\n\"너는 나한테 줄칼을 가져온다.\" 그가 나를 다시 젖혔다. \"그리고 먹을거리를 가져온다.\" 그가 다시 젖혔다. \"그 둘을 다 나한테 가져오는 거다.\" 그가 다시 젖혔다. \"안 그러면 네 심장과 간을 도려내겠다.\" 그가 다시 젖혔다.\n\n나는 끔찍이도 겁이 나고 어지러워, 두 손으로 사내에게 매달리며 말했다. \"제발 저를 똑바로 세워 주시면 좋겠어요, 아저씨, 그럼 토하지 않을 것 같고, 더 잘 알아들을 수 있을 것 같아요.\"\n\n사내가 나를 어찌나 엄청나게 푹 떨어뜨렸다가 빙 돌렸던지, 교회가 제 풍향계를 넘어 펄쩍 뛰어오를 정도였다. 그러더니 사내는 내 두 팔을 잡고 묘비 꼭대기에 똑바로 세운 채, 다음과 같은 무서운 말투로 이어 갔다.\n\n\"내일 아침 일찍 그 줄칼과 그 먹을거리를 나한테 가져와라. 그 일습을 죄다, 저기 저 너머 오래된 포대로 가져와. 그것만 해내고, 나 같은 사람을 봤다거나 그 어떤 사람이든 봤다는 말이나 기색을 감히 한마디도 내비치지 않는다면, 너는 살게 될 거다. 만약 실패하거나, 아무리 사소한 거라도 내 말에서 조금이라도 벗어난다면, 네 심장과 간은 도려내져 구워지고 먹힐 거다. 그리고 말이야, 내가 혼자라고 생각하겠지만 그렇지 않아. 나랑 같이 숨어 있는 젊은 놈이 하나 있는데, 그놈에 비하면 나는 천사야. 그 젊은 놈은 내가 하는 말을 다 듣고 있어. 그 젊은 놈은 사내아이한테, 그 심장한테, 그 간한테 다가가는 저만의 비밀스러운 수법을 가지고 있지. 사내아이가 그 젊은 놈한테서 숨으려 해 봤자 헛수고야. 아이가 문을 잠그고, 이불 속에서 따뜻하게 몸을 웅크리고, 옷을 머리까지 끌어 덮고 제 딴엔 편안하고 안전하다 여겨도, 그 젊은 놈은 살그머니 기고 또 기어서 그 아이한테 다가가 배를 찢어 버릴 거다. 나는 지금 이 순간 그 젊은 놈이 너를 해치지 못하게 가까스로 막고 있는 거야. 그 젊은 놈을 네 뱃속에서 떼어 놓기가 여간 힘든 게 아니다. 자, 이제 어떻게 할 거냐?\"\n\n나는 줄칼을 가져다 드리겠다고, 그리고 먹다 남은 음식이든 뭐든 구할 수 있는 만큼 가져다 드리겠다고, 그리고 아침 일찍 포대로 찾아가겠다고 말했다.\n\n\"안 그러면 벼락 맞아 뒈진다고 말해!\" 사내가 말했다.\n\n나는 그렇게 말했고, 사내는 나를 내려 주었다.\n\n\"이제.\" 사내가 말을 이었다. \"네가 약속한 걸 잊지 말고, 그 젊은 놈도 잊지 말고, 어서 집에 가!\"\n\n\"아, 안녕히 계세요, 아저씨.\" 나는 더듬더듬 말했다.\n\n\"안녕은 무슨!\" 사내가 차갑고 축축한 평지를 둘러보며 말했다. \"내가 개구리였으면 좋겠다. 아니면 뱀장어든지!\"\n\n그와 동시에 사내는 부들부들 떠는 제 몸을 두 팔로 끌어안았다 — 몸이 흩어지지 않게 붙들어 두려는 듯 제 몸을 꽉 껴안고는 — 나지막한 교회 담장을 향해 절뚝거리며 갔다. 사내가 쐐기풀 사이로, 그리고 푸른 둑을 동여맨 가시덤불 사이로 길을 헤쳐 가는 모습을 보고 있자니, 어린 내 눈에는 마치 그가 죽은 자들의 손길을 피해 가는 것처럼 보였다. 무덤 속에서 조심스레 뻗어 나와 그의 발목을 비틀어 끌어들이려는 손길을.\n\n나지막한 교회 담장에 이르자 사내는, 다리가 마비되어 뻣뻣해진 사람처럼 그것을 넘어가더니, 나를 찾으려고 몸을 돌렸다. 그가 돌아서는 것을 보자 나는 집 쪽으로 얼굴을 향하고 두 다리를 한껏 놀려 내달렸다. 그러나 이내 어깨 너머로 돌아보니, 사내는 여전히 두 팔로 제 몸을 끌어안고, 비가 많이 오거나 밀물이 들 때 디딤돌로 쓰려고 습지 여기저기에 떨어뜨려 놓은 커다란 돌들 사이로 성한 데 없는 발을 디뎌 가며, 다시금 강 쪽으로 가고 있었다.\n\n내가 멈춰 서서 사내의 뒷모습을 바라볼 때, 습지는 그저 길고 검은 수평선 하나였고, 강은 또 하나의 수평선일 뿐이었으나 그만큼 넓지도 그만큼 검지도 않았으며, 하늘은 그저 길게 성난 붉은 선과 짙은 검은 선이 뒤섞여 늘어선 한 줄이었다. 강가에는 그 모든 풍경 가운데 유일하게 똑바로 서 있는 듯한 두 개의 검은 물체가 어렴풋이 보였다. 그중 하나는 뱃사람들이 방향을 잡는 데 쓰는 표지등이었는데, 장대 위에 얹힌 테 없는 통 같았으니, 가까이서 보면 흉한 물건이었다. 다른 하나는 교수대였는데, 한때 어느 해적을 매달아 두었던 사슬 몇 가닥이 거기 늘어져 있었다. 사내는 마치 그 해적이 되살아나 내려왔다가 다시 제 몸을 매달러 돌아가는 것처럼, 그 교수대 쪽으로 절뚝절뚝 가고 있었다. 그런 생각이 들자 나는 끔찍한 전율에 휩싸였다. 그리고 소 떼가 고개를 들어 사내의 뒷모습을 바라보는 것을 보면서, 저들도 같은 생각을 하는 걸까 싶었다. 나는 그 무서운 젊은 놈이 있는지 사방을 둘러보았으나, 그자의 기척은 어디에도 보이지 않았다. 그러나 이제 다시 겁이 난 나는, 멈추지 않고 집으로 내달렸다.\n\n# Chapter II.\n\n누나, 즉 조 가저리 부인은 나보다 스무 살도 넘게 위였으며, 나를 \"손수\" 길러 냈다는 이유로 자기 자신에게나 이웃들에게나 대단한 명성을 쌓아 두고 있었다. 그 시절 나는 그 표현이 무슨 뜻인지 스스로 알아내야만 했는데, 누나의 손이 단단하고 묵직하며 그 손을 나뿐만 아니라 남편에게도 곧잘 갖다 대는 버릇이 있음을 알고 있었으므로, 나는 조 가저리와 나 둘 다 손수 길러진 것이려니 짐작했다.\n\n누나는 미인이 아니었다. 그래서 나는 누나가 조 가저리로 하여금 자기와 결혼하도록 손수 만든 것이리라는 막연한 인상을 품고 있었다. 조는 살결이 흰 사내로, 매끈한 얼굴 양옆으로 아마빛 곱슬머리가 드리워 있었고, 두 눈은 어찌나 분간하기 어려운 푸른빛이었던지 어쩐지 제 흰자위와 뒤섞여 버린 것처럼 보일 정도였다. 그는 온순하고, 성품이 좋고, 마음씨가 곱고, 무던하고, 어리숙하고, 사랑스러운 사람이었으니 — 힘에 있어서나 또한 약함에 있어서나 일종의 헤라클레스 같은 인물이었다.\n\n검은 머리에 검은 눈을 한 누나, 조 부인은 살결이 어찌나 온통 붉던지, 나는 이따금 저 사람이 비누 대신 육두구 강판으로 몸을 씻는 게 아닐까 하는 생각마저 하곤 했다. 누나는 키가 크고 뼈마디가 굵었으며, 거의 언제나 거친 앞치마를 둘렀는데, 그것은 등 뒤에서 두 개의 고리로 몸에 동여매여 있었고, 앞쪽에는 핀과 바늘이 잔뜩 꽂힌, 네모지고 난공불락의 가슴받이가 달려 있었다. 누나는 이 앞치마를 그토록 자주 두르는 것을 스스로에게는 대단한 미덕으로, 조에게는 강한 책망거리로 삼았다. 하지만 나로서는 누나가 도대체 왜 그 앞치마를 두를 필요가 있었는지, 또 정 두른다 치더라도 어째서 평생 단 하루도 벗어 두지 않았는지 도무지 까닭을 알 수 없다.\n\n조의 대장간은 우리 집과 붙어 있었는데, 우리 집은 목조 가옥이었으니, 그 시절 우리 고장의 살림집이 대개 그러했듯이 — 거의 다 그러했다. 내가 교회 묘지에서 집으로 달려왔을 때 대장간은 닫혀 있었고, 조는 부엌에 홀로 앉아 있었다. 조와 나는 함께 고통받는 동지로서 그런 처지에 어울리는 은밀한 마음을 나누는 사이였으므로, 내가 문빗장을 들어 올리고 맞은편 벽난로 구석에 앉아 있는 그를 들여다본 바로 그 순간, 조는 내게 속내를 털어놓았다.\n\n\"조 부인이 너 찾으러 열두 번이나 나갔다 왔다, 핍. 그리고 지금도 나가 있으니 합쳐서 열세 번째지.\"\n\n\"그래요?\"\n\n\"그래, 핍.\" 조가 말했다. \"게다가 더 나쁜 건, 간질이 막대기까지 들고 갔다는 거야.\"\n\n이 우울한 소식에 나는 조끼에 달린 단 하나뿐인 단추를 빙글빙글 돌리며, 깊은 낙담에 잠겨 불을 바라보았다. 간질이 막대기란 끝에 밀랍을 입힌 회초리로, 간질여지는 내 몸뚱이와 부딪히는 통에 반들반들 닳아 있었다.\n\n\"누나가 앉았다가.\" 조가 말했다. \"다시 일어나서, 간질이 막대기를 움켜쥐더니, 길길이 날뛰며 나갔어. 그렇게 하더라고.\" 조가 부지깽이로 아래쪽 살 사이의 불을 천천히 헤집으며 그것을 바라보고 말했다. \"길길이 날뛰며 나갔단 말이다, 핍.\"\n\n\"나간 지 오래됐어요, 조?\" 나는 늘 그를 더 큰 종류의 아이로, 그러니까 내 동무 정도로 대했다.\n\n\"글쎄.\" 조가 네덜란드제 괘종시계를 흘긋 올려다보며 말했다. \"이번에 길길이 날뛰러 나간 지는 한 오 분쯤 됐다, 핍. 오는구나! 문 뒤로 가, 이 친구야, 그리고 닦기 수건을 사이에 두고 있어라.\"\n\n나는 그 충고를 따랐다. 누나, 조 부인은 문을 활짝 열어젖혔다가 그 뒤에 무언가 걸리는 것이 있음을 깨닫고는 곧장 그 원인을 알아채고, 간질이 막대기를 들이밀어 더 깊이 조사에 착수했다. 그러고는 마무리로 나를 — 나는 종종 부부 싸움의 발사체 노릇을 했다 — 조에게 내던졌는데, 조는 어떤 조건으로든 나를 붙들게 되어 기뻐하며 나를 벽난로 안쪽으로 받아 넘기고는 자기 굵은 다리로 조용히 거기에 나를 가두어 막아 주었다.\n\n\"어디 갔다 왔어, 이 어린 원숭이 녀석아?\" 조 부인이 발을 구르며 말했다. \"당장 말해, 사람을 이렇게 애태우고 겁주고 졸이게 해서 진을 빼 놓으니 네가 무슨 짓을 하고 다녔는지. 안 그러면 네가 핍이 쉰 명이고 저자가 가저리가 오백 명이라 해도 그 구석에서 끄집어내고 말겠다.\"\n\n\"그냥 교회 묘지에 갔다 왔을 뿐이에요.\" 나는 걸상에 앉아 울며 몸을 문지르고서 말했다.\n\n\"교회 묘지라!\" 누나가 되받았다. \"내가 아니었으면 넌 진작에 교회 묘지에 갔다가 거기 눌러앉았을 거다. 누가 너를 손수 키웠어?\"\n\n\"누나가요.\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"그래, 내가 왜 그랬는지, 그것 좀 알고 싶구나!\" 누나가 소리쳤다.\n\n나는 훌쩍이며 말했다. \"모르겠어요.\"\n\n\"나도 모르겠다!\" 누나가 말했다. \"다시는 안 할 거다! 그것만은 분명해. 정말이지 너를 낳은 이후로 이 앞치마를 단 한 번도 벗어 본 적이 없다고 말할 수 있다. 대장장이의 아내라는 것만으로도(게다가 가저리라는 작자의) 고된 노릇인데, 거기다 네 어미 노릇까지 하고 있으니.\"\n\n나는 침울하게 불을 바라보면서 그 물음에서 생각이 빗나가 버렸다. 다리에 쇠고랑을 찬 채 습지에 나가 있는 도망자, 그 수수께끼 같은 젊은 놈, 줄칼, 먹을거리, 그리고 이 나를 거두어 주는 집에서 도둑질을 저지르기로 한 그 무서운 맹세가, 복수의 화염처럼 이글거리는 석탄 속에서 내 앞에 떠올랐던 것이다.\n\n\"하!\" 조 부인이 간질이 막대기를 제자리에 도로 꽂으며 말했다. \"교회 묘지라니, 어디 보자! 너희 둘 다 교회 묘지 운운할 만하지.\" 그런데 그건 그렇고, 우리 둘 중 하나는 그런 말을 입 밖에 낸 적조차 없었다. \"너희 둘이서 작당해 머잖아 나를 교회 묘지로 몰아넣고 말 게다. 오, 나 없으면 너희는 참으로 한심한 한 쌍이 되겠지!\"\n\n누나가 찻그릇을 차리는 일에 매달리는 동안, 조는 제 다리 너머로 나를 슬쩍 내려다보았다. 마치 나와 자기를 머릿속으로 셈해 보면서, 앞서 예고된 그 비참한 상황 속에서 우리가 실제로 어떤 한 쌍이 될지를 가늠해 보는 듯했다. 그러고는 자기 오른쪽 아마빛 곱슬머리와 구레나룻을 만지작거리며, 푸른 눈으로 부엌을 오가는 조 부인을 좇았으니, 이는 풍파가 일 때면 늘 보이는 그의 버릇이었다.\n\n누나에게는 우리에게 줄 버터 바른 빵을 써는 단호한 방식이 있었는데, 그것은 한 번도 달라지는 법이 없었다. 먼저 왼손으로 빵 덩어리를 가슴받이에 대고 세게 꽉 눌렀다 — 그러면 거기 꽂힌 핀이, 때로는 바늘이 빵에 박혀 들었고, 우리는 나중에 그것을 입속에 넣게 되곤 했다. 그런 다음 칼로 버터를 조금(너무 많지 않게) 떠서, 마치 고약을 바르는 듯한 약제사 같은 솜씨로 빵에 펴 발랐는데 — 찰싹찰싹 능란하게 칼의 양면을 다 써 가며, 껍질 가장자리를 빙 둘러 버터를 다듬고 매만졌다. 그러고는 고약 가장자리에 칼을 마지막으로 한 번 야무지게 쓱 닦고, 빵 덩어리에서 아주 두툼한 한 조각을 톱질하듯 잘라 냈다. 그리고 그것을 덩어리에서 완전히 떼어 내기 전에 끝으로 둘로 쪼개어, 그중 하나는 조가, 다른 하나는 내가 받았다.\n\n이번에는 배가 고팠으나 나는 감히 내 몫을 먹을 수가 없었다. 그 무서운 친구와, 그의 동맹자인 한층 더 무서운 젊은 놈을 위해 무언가 비축해 두어야 한다고 느꼈던 것이다. 나는 조 부인의 살림 솜씨가 극도로 빈틈없다는 것을, 그래서 도둑질하듯 뒤져 보아도 찬장에서 건질 만한 것이 아무것도 없으리라는 것을 알고 있었다. 그래서 나는 버터 바른 빵 덩어리를 바지 가랑이 속으로 떨어뜨려 두기로 결심했다.\n\n이 목적을 이루는 데 필요한 결심의 노력이 어찌나 끔찍했던지. 그것은 마치 높은 집 꼭대기에서 뛰어내리거나, 깊은 물속으로 곤두박질치기로 마음먹어야 하는 것과도 같았다. 게다가 아무것도 모르는 조 때문에 그 일은 한층 더 어려워졌다. 앞서 말한 대로 함께 고통받는 동지로서의 우리만의 비밀 결사 속에서, 또 그가 내게 베푸는 다정한 우정 속에서, 우리는 저녁마다 빵 조각을 어떻게 베어 무는지 견주어 보는 버릇이 있었다. 이따금 말없이 서로에게 빵을 들어 보여 감탄을 자아내는 식이었는데 — 그것이 우리를 새로운 분발로 자극하곤 했다. 오늘 밤에도 조는 빠르게 줄어드는 자기 빵 조각을 내보이며, 평소의 다정한 경쟁에 들어서자고 여러 번 나를 부추겼다. 그러나 그는 그때마다, 한쪽 무릎에는 노란 찻잔을, 다른 쪽 무릎에는 손도 대지 않은 버터 바른 빵을 올려놓고 있는 나를 발견할 따름이었다. 마침내 나는, 내가 꾀하던 일을 반드시 해치워야 하며, 형편에 맞게 가장 덜 이상해 보이는 방식으로 해내는 것이 상책이라고 필사적으로 마음을 정했다. 나는 조가 막 나를 쳐다본 순간을 틈타, 버터 바른 빵을 바지 가랑이 속으로 내려보냈다.\n\n조는 내가 입맛을 잃은 것이라 짐작하고는 분명 마음이 불편해진 듯, 자기 빵 조각을 생각에 잠겨 한입 베어 물었으나 도무지 즐기는 기색이 아니었다. 그는 그것을 평소보다 훨씬 오래 입안에서 굴리며 한참 곰곰이 생각하더니, 결국 알약 삼키듯 꿀꺽 넘겼다. 그가 또 한입 베어 물려고 빵을 단단히 물기 좋게 고개를 한쪽으로 막 기울였을 때, 그의 눈이 내게 떨어졌고, 그는 내 버터 바른 빵이 사라진 것을 보았다.\n\n조가 베어 물려던 동작을 멈추고 나를 빤히 쳐다보던 그 경악과 당혹은 너무도 역력하여, 누나의 관찰을 피해 갈 수 없었다.\n\n\"이번엔 또 무슨 일이야?\" 누나가 찻잔을 내려놓으며 매섭게 말했다.\n\n\"이거 봐, 너 말이다!\" 조가 자못 진지하게 나무라듯 내게 고개를 내저으며 중얼거렸다. \"핍, 이 친구야! 너 그러다 큰일 난다. 어딘가에 걸릴 거야. 그걸 다 씹었을 리가 없잖아, 핍.\"\n\n\"이번엔 또 무슨 일이냐니까?\" 누나가 아까보다 더 날카롭게 되물었다.\n\n\"조금이라도 그걸 기침으로 게워 낼 수 있거든, 핍, 그렇게 하라고 권하고 싶다.\" 조가 잔뜩 겁에 질려 말했다. \"예의는 예의지만, 그래도 건강은 건강이니까.\"\n\n이쯤 되자 누나는 완전히 격분하여, 조에게 달려들어 양쪽 구레나룻을 움켜쥐고는 잠시 그의 머리를 뒤쪽 벽에다 쿵쿵 찧어 댔고, 그동안 나는 구석에 앉아 죄책감에 사로잡혀 지켜보았다.\n\n\"자, 이제는 무슨 일인지 말할 테냐.\" 누나가 숨을 헐떡이며 말했다. \"이 멀뚱히 쳐다보기만 하는 큰 멧돼지 같은 작자야.\"\n\n조는 어쩔 줄 모르는 얼굴로 누나를 바라보더니, 어쩔 줄 모르고 한입 베어 물고는, 다시 나를 바라보았다.\n\n\"있잖아, 핍.\" 조가 마지막 한입을 볼에 문 채 엄숙하게, 마치 우리 둘만 단둘이 있는 양 은밀한 목소리로 말했다. \"너랑 나는 언제나 친구고, 나는 무슨 일이 있어도 절대로 너를 일러바칠 사람이 아니야. 하지만 그런 — \" 그는 의자를 옮기며 우리 사이의 바닥을 둘러보더니 다시 나를 바라보았다 — \"그토록 보기 드물게 한꺼번에 꿀꺽 삼키는 짓이라니!\"\n\n\"그 녀석이 음식을 통째로 삼켰단 말이야?\" 누나가 소리쳤다.\n\n\"있잖아, 이 친구야.\" 조가 조 부인이 아니라 나를 바라보며, 여전히 한입을 볼에 문 채 말했다. \"나도 네 나이 때는 통째로 삼키곤 했지 — 자주 그랬어 — 어릴 적에 통째로 삼키는 녀석들 틈에서 자랐다만, 너처럼 삼키는 건 여태 한 번도 못 봤다, 핍. 통째로 삼키다 죽지 않은 게 천만다행이야.\"\n\n누나가 내게 달려들어 머리채를 잡고 나를 낚아 올리고는, \"이리 와서 약 좀 먹어야겠다\"라는 그 무시무시한 말 외에는 아무 말도 하지 않았다.\n\n그 시절 어느 돌팔이 같은 작자가 타르 물을 훌륭한 약이랍시고 다시 유행시켜 놓은 터라, 조 부인은 늘 그것을 찬장에 갖춰 두고 있었으니, 그 약효를 고약함에 걸맞게 굳게 믿고 있었던 것이다. 형편이 가장 좋을 때조차도 이 영약은 좋은 보양제랍시고 내게 어찌나 많이 투여되었던지, 나는 갓 칠한 울타리 같은 냄새를 풍기며 돌아다니고 있다는 것을 스스로 의식할 정도였다. 바로 그날 밤은 내 사정이 위급하다 하여 이 혼합물 한 파인트가 필요했고, 그것은 더 큰 안락을 위해 내 목구멍으로 들이부어졌는데, 그러는 동안 조 부인은 마치 구둣주걱에 장화를 끼우듯 내 머리를 제 겨드랑이에 끼고 있었다. 조는 반 파인트로 모면했으나, 그것마저 삼켜야 했으니(\"그 녀석도 한 차례 발작이 있었으니까\"라는 이유에서였는데, 그는 불 앞에 앉아 천천히 우물거리며 생각에 잠긴 채 무척이나 곤혹스러워했다). 내 경우로 미루어 보건대, 그가 그 전에 발작이 없었다 하더라도 그 후에는 분명 한 차례 있었으리라고 말해 두고 싶다.\n\n양심이란 어른이든 아이든 사람을 책망할 때면 무서운 것이다. 그러나 아이의 경우, 그 은밀한 짐이 바지 가랑이 속에 든 또 하나의 은밀한 짐과 합세할 때면, 그것은 (내가 증언할 수 있거니와) 크나큰 형벌이 된다. 내가 조 부인의 것을 훔치려 한다는 죄스러운 자각이 — 나는 조의 것을 훔친다고는 한 번도 생각지 않았으니, 살림살이 재산 가운데 어느 것도 그의 것이라 여겨 본 적이 없었던 까닭이다 — 앉아 있을 때나 부엌에서 자잘한 심부름으로 이리저리 부려질 때나 늘 한 손은 버터 바른 빵에 얹어 두어야 하는 필요와 한데 어우러져, 나는 거의 미칠 지경이었다. 그러다가 습지의 바람이 불을 이글거리며 타오르게 하면, 나는 다리에 쇠고랑을 차고 내게 비밀을 맹세시킨 그 사내의 목소리가 밖에서 들려오는 것만 같았으니, 내일까지는 굶을 수 없고 굶지도 않겠다, 지금 당장 먹어야겠다고 외치는 소리였다. 또 어떤 때에는 이런 생각이 들었다. 그 젊은 놈이, 가까스로 제 손을 내 몸에 적시지 못하게 막아 두었던 그자가, 타고난 조급함에 못 이기거나 시간을 잘못 알아서, 내일이 아니라 오늘 밤 내 심장과 간에 손을 댈 권한을 얻었다고 제멋대로 여기면 어쩌나! 만일 누군가 공포에 머리카락이 곤두선 적이 있다면, 그때 내 머리카락이 분명 그러했으리라. 하지만 어쩌면, 그 누구도 실제로 그런 적은 없었을까?\n\n크리스마스이브였고, 나는 이튿날 먹을 푸딩을 구리 막대기로 저어야 했으니, 네덜란드제 괘종시계로 일곱 시부터 여덟 시까지였다. 나는 다리에 짐을 얹은 채 그 일을 해 보았는데(그러자 자기 다리에 짐을 얹은 그 사내가 새삼 떠올랐다), 몸을 움직이는 통에 버터 바른 빵이 발목께로 자꾸 비어져 나오려는 성향이 도무지 감당이 안 됨을 알았다. 다행히 나는 슬그머니 빠져나가, 내 양심의 그 한 부분을 다락방 침실에 갖다 두었다.\n\n\"들어 봐요!\" 젓는 일을 마치고, 잠자리로 보내지기 전 벽난로 구석에서 마지막으로 몸을 녹이고 있던 나는 말했다. \"저거 대포 소리였어요, 조?\"\n\n\"아!\" 조가 말했다. \"또 죄수 하나가 달아났구나.\"\n\n\"그게 무슨 뜻이에요, 조?\" 내가 말했다.\n\n언제나 설명하는 일을 자청하던 조 부인이 퉁명스럽게 말했다. \"탈옥했다고. 탈옥.\" 타르 물을 먹이듯 그 정의를 투여하는 식이었다.\n\n조 부인이 바느질감 위로 고개를 숙이고 앉아 있는 동안, 나는 입을 놀려 조에게 \"죄수가 뭐예요?\" 하고 묻는 시늉을 했다. 그러자 조도 입을 놀려 무척이나 정교하게 다듬은 대답을 돌려주는 시늉을 했는데, 나는 거기서 \"핍\"이라는 단 한 단어밖에는 알아들을 수가 없었다.\n\n\"어젯밤에 죄수 하나가 달아났어.\" 조가 소리 내어 말했다. \"일몰 신호포가 울린 다음에 말이야. 그래서 그자를 알리는 경고 포를 쏘았지. 그리고 지금은 또 다른 놈을 알리는 경고 포를 쏘는 모양이다.\"\n\n\"누가 쏘는 거예요?\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"저 녀석 좀 보게.\" 누나가 바느질감 너머로 나를 향해 인상을 쓰며 끼어들었다. \"어찌 저리 캐묻기를 좋아하는지. 아무것도 묻지 않으면, 거짓말 들을 일도 없는 법이야.\"\n\n설령 내가 정말로 묻는다 한들 누나가 거짓말을 들려주겠다는 뜻을 내비치는 것은 누나 자신에게도 그리 점잖은 노릇은 아니라고 나는 생각했다. 하지만 누나는 손님이 있을 때가 아니면 결코 점잖은 법이 없었다.\n\n이 대목에서 조는 한껏 공을 들여 입을 아주 크게 벌려, 내게는 \"토라짐\"처럼 보이는 단어의 모양을 만들어 보임으로써 내 호기심을 잔뜩 부추겼다. 그래서 나는 자연스레 조 부인을 가리키며, 입을 놀려 \"누나요?\" 하고 묻는 시늉을 했다. 그러나 조는 그 말을 도무지 들으려 하지 않고, 다시 입을 아주 크게 벌리더니 그 안에서 더없이 힘주어 어떤 단어의 모양을 흔들어 내보였다. 그러나 나는 그 단어를 도통 알아낼 수가 없었다.\n\n\"조 부인.\" 나는 최후의 수단으로 말했다. \"알고 싶은데요 — 너무 귀찮지 않으시다면 — 그 포 쏘는 소리는 어디서 나는 거예요?\"\n\n\"이런, 저 녀석 좀 보게나!\" 누나가 외쳤는데, 정말로 그런 뜻이라기보다는 오히려 그 반대의 뜻인 듯한 말투였다. \"감옥선에서 나는 거다!\"\n\n\"오오!\" 나는 조를 바라보며 말했다. \"감옥선!\"\n\n조는 나무라듯 헛기침을 했는데, 마치 \"거 봐라, 내가 말했잖니\" 하는 듯했다.\n\n\"그런데요, 감옥선이 뭐예요?\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"이 녀석이 늘 이렇다니까!\" 누나가 바늘과 실로 나를 가리키며, 내게 고개를 내저으며 외쳤다. \"한 가지 물음에 대답해 주면, 곧장 열두 가지를 더 물어 댄다니까. 감옥선이란 죄수를 가두는 배다, 저 습지 바로 건너편에 있는.\" 우리 고장에서는 늘 습지를 그렇게 불렀다.\n\n\"누가 죄수선에 갇히는지, 그리고 왜 거기 갇히는지 궁금하네요.\" 나는 막연히, 그리고 조용한 절망에 잠겨 말했다.\n\n이것은 조 부인에게 너무 지나친 것이어서, 누나는 곧장 자리에서 일어섰다. \"내 말 잘 들어, 이 어린 녀석아.\" 누나가 말했다. \"나는 사람들 들들 볶아 진을 빼라고 너를 손수 키운 게 아니다. 그랬다면 그건 내게 칭찬거리가 아니라 흠이 됐겠지. 사람들이 감옥선에 갇히는 건 살인을 저지르고, 도둑질을 하고, 문서를 위조하고, 온갖 못된 짓을 다 하기 때문이야. 그리고 그자들은 늘 캐묻는 것에서부터 시작하지. 자, 이제 어서 자러 가!\"\n\n나는 잠자리로 가는 길을 밝힐 촛불을 단 한 번도 허락받지 못했으므로, 머리가 얼얼한 채로 — 누나의 마지막 말에 장단을 맞추느라 골무가 탬버린처럼 내 머리를 두들겨 댄 탓이었다 — 어둠 속에서 위층으로 올라가면서, 감옥선이 내게 이토록 가까이 있다는 것이 참으로 편리하다는 사실을 무섭게 절감했다. 나는 분명 그곳으로 가는 길에 들어선 셈이었다. 나는 캐묻는 것에서부터 시작했고, 이제 조 부인의 것을 훔치려 하고 있었으니까.\n\n이제는 까마득히 멀어진 그 시절 이후로, 나는 공포에 사로잡힌 어린아이 속에 얼마나 깊은 비밀이 도사리고 있는지를 아는 사람이 드물다는 생각을 자주 했다. 그 공포가 아무리 터무니없는 것이라 해도, 어쨌든 공포이기만 하면 그렇다. 나는 내 심장과 간을 노리는 그 젊은 놈에게 죽도록 겁을 먹었고, 쇠고랑을 찬 다리로 나와 이야기를 나눈 그자에게 죽도록 겁을 먹었으며, 무서운 약속을 강요당한 나 자신에게마저 죽도록 겁을 먹고 있었다. 무슨 일에든 나를 내치기만 하는 그 막강한 누나를 통해 구원받을 가망은 없었다. 내 공포의 은밀함 속에서, 만일 요구받았더라면 내가 무슨 짓을 저질렀을지는 생각하기조차 두렵다.\n\n그날 밤 잠이 들었다 한들, 그것은 그저 거센 봄철 밀물을 타고 강을 따라 감옥선으로 떠내려가는 내 모습을 그려 보는 것일 뿐이었다. 내가 교수대가 선 곳을 지날 때, 어느 유령 같은 해적이 나팔통을 통해 내게 외쳐 댔으니, 차라리 뭍으로 올라와 지금 당장 거기서 목매달려 죽는 게 낫지 미루지 말라는 것이었다. 잠들고 싶은 마음이 있었다 해도 잠들기가 두려웠으니, 동이 트는 첫 희미한 빛이 비치면 식료품 저장실을 털어야 한다는 것을 알고 있었기 때문이다. 그것은 밤중에는 도무지 할 수 없는 일이었다. 그 시절에는 손쉽게 마찰을 일으켜 불을 켤 길이 없었던 까닭이다. 불을 켜려면 부싯돌과 부시를 쳐서 일으켜야 했을 테고, 그러면 영락없이 그 해적이 제 사슬을 절그럭거리는 것 같은 소리를 냈을 것이다.\n\n내 작은 창문 밖의 그 거대한 검은 비로드 휘장에 잿빛이 어리자마자, 나는 일어나 아래층으로 내려갔다. 가는 길의 널빤지마다, 그리고 널빤지마다의 갈라진 틈마다 내 뒤에서 외쳐 댔다. \"도둑 잡아라!\" 그리고 \"일어나요, 조 부인!\" 하고. 식료품 저장실은 철 덕분에 평소보다 훨씬 풍성하게 채워져 있었는데, 나는 거기 뒷다리가 매달려 걸려 있는 산토끼 한 마리에 무척 놀랐으니, 내가 등을 반쯤 돌렸을 때 그놈이 윙크를 하는 것만 같았던 것이다. 확인해 볼 겨를도, 고를 겨를도, 그 무엇을 할 겨를도 없었으니, 한순간도 허비할 시간이 없었던 까닭이다. 나는 빵을 조금, 치즈 껍질을 조금, 다진 고기 절임 반 단지가량을(이것은 어젯밤의 빵 조각과 함께 손수건에 싸 두었다), 돌병에 든 브랜디를 조금(이것은 내가 위층 방에서 그 취하게 만드는 음료, 즉 스페인 감초 물을 만들 때 몰래 쓰던 유리병에 따라 옮겨 담았고, 돌병은 부엌 찬장의 물주전자에서 물을 부어 묽게 채워 놓았다), 살점이 거의 안 붙은 고기 뼈다귀 하나, 그리고 보기 좋게 동그랗고 단단한 돼지고기 파이 하나를 훔쳤다. 하마터면 파이는 두고 갈 뻔했으나, 구석에 뚜껑 덮인 질그릇 접시 속에 그토록 정성스레 치워 둔 것이 무엇인가 보고 싶은 유혹에 선반 위로 기어올랐다가, 그것이 파이임을 알아냈고, 그것이 당장 쓰려고 둔 것이 아니어서 한동안은 없어진 줄도 모르리라는 바람에 그것을 집어 들었다.\n\n부엌에는 대장간으로 통하는 문이 하나 있었다. 나는 그 문의 자물쇠와 빗장을 풀고, 조의 연장들 사이에서 줄칼을 하나 꺼냈다. 그러고는 잠금장치를 원래 있던 대로 도로 채워 두고, 어젯밤 집으로 달려왔을 때 들어왔던 그 문을 열고, 그것을 닫은 뒤, 안개 자욱한 습지를 향해 달려갔다.\n\n# Chapter III.\n\n서리가 내려앉은 아침이었고, 몹시 축축했다. 잠에서 깨어났을 때 나는 내 작은 창문 바깥에 습기가 어려 있는 것을 보았는데, 마치 어떤 도깨비가 밤새도록 거기서 울며 그 창문을 손수건 삼아 쓰기라도 한 것 같았다. 이제 나는 그 습기가 헐벗은 산울타리와 듬성듬성한 풀 위에, 더 거친 종류의 거미줄처럼 얹혀 있는 것을 보았다. 잔가지에서 잔가지로, 풀잎에서 풀잎으로 매달려 있었다. 모든 난간과 문에는 물기가 끈적하게 들러붙어 있었고, 습지의 안개가 어찌나 짙던지, 길가의 기둥 위에서 사람들에게 우리 마을 쪽을 가리키고 있는 나무 손가락 표지판이 — 사람들이 결코 받아들이지 않는 안내였다, 그곳에 오는 이가 없었으니까 — 그 바로 밑에 바싹 다가설 때까지도 내게는 보이지 않았다. 그러다 물방울을 뚝뚝 떨어뜨리고 있는 그것을 올려다보았을 때, 짓눌린 내 양심에는 그것이 마치 나를 죄수 호송선에 바치려는 유령처럼 보였다.\n\n습지에 들어서니 안개는 더욱 짙어졌고, 그래서 내가 모든 것을 향해 달려가는 것이 아니라 모든 것이 나를 향해 달려드는 것만 같았다. 이것은 죄지은 마음에는 더없이 거북한 일이었다. 문과 도랑과 둑이 안개를 뚫고 내게 와락 달려들었는데, 마치 더없이 분명한 목소리로 \"남의 돼지고기 파이를 든 아이다! 저놈을 잡아라!\" 하고 외치는 듯했다. 소 떼도 그처럼 불쑥 내게 다가와, 두 눈을 부릅뜨고 콧구멍으로 김을 내뿜으며 \"어이, 꼬마 도둑놈!\" 하는 듯했다. 흰 넥타이를 두른 검은 황소 한 마리는 — 깨어난 내 양심에는 어딘가 성직자 같은 분위기마저 풍기는 놈이었는데 — 어찌나 끈질기게 두 눈으로 나를 노려보던지, 그리고 내가 몸을 돌리면 그 뭉툭한 머리를 어찌나 나무라는 듯한 태도로 돌리던지, 나는 그놈을 향해 엉엉 울며 내뱉었다. \"어쩔 수 없었어요! 제가 먹으려고 가져온 게 아니란 말이에요!\" 그러자 황소는 고개를 숙이고 콧구멍으로 연기 한 무더기를 내뿜더니, 뒷다리를 차올리고 꼬리를 한 번 휘저으며 사라져 버렸다.\n\n이러는 내내 나는 강 쪽으로 나아가고 있었다. 그러나 아무리 빨리 가도 발은 따뜻해지지 않았으니, 마치 내가 만나러 달려가는 그 사내의 다리에 쇠고랑이 박혀 있던 것처럼, 그 축축한 한기가 내 발에 박혀 있는 것만 같았다. 나는 포대로 가는 길을 꽤 똑바로 알고 있었다. 어느 일요일에 조와 함께 그곳에 내려가 본 적이 있었기 때문인데, 그때 조는 낡은 대포 위에 걸터앉아, 내가 정식으로 계약을 맺고 그의 도제가 되면 우리가 거기서 얼마나 신나게 놀게 될지 이야기해 주었다. 그러나 안개 속에서 혼란에 빠진 나는 결국 너무 오른쪽으로 치우쳐 있는 것을 알게 되었고, 그래서 강가를 따라, 진흙 위로 놓인 헐거운 돌무더기 둑과 밀물을 막아 세운 말뚝들 위로 되짚어 가야 했다. 한껏 서두르며 이곳을 따라 나아가다가, 포대에 아주 가깝다는 걸 알고 있던 도랑을 막 건너고, 그 도랑 너머의 둔덕을 막 기어 올라갔을 때, 나는 내 앞에 앉아 있는 사내를 보았다. 그는 내게 등을 돌린 채 두 팔을 가슴에 모으고, 잠에 겨워 앞으로 꾸벅꾸벅 졸고 있었다.\n\n나는 이렇게 뜻밖의 방식으로 아침거리를 들고 다가가면 그가 더 기뻐하리라 생각했고, 그래서 살그머니 다가가 그의 어깨를 건드렸다. 그는 즉각 벌떡 일어났는데, 같은 사내가 아니라 다른 사내였다!\n\n그런데도 이 사내 역시 거친 잿빛 옷을 걸치고, 다리에는 커다란 쇠고랑을 차고 있었으며, 절뚝거리고, 목이 쉬었고, 추위에 떨고 있었으며, 그 다른 사내가 지녔던 모든 것을 다 지니고 있었다. 다만 같은 얼굴이 아니었고, 챙이 평평하고 넓으며 꼭대기가 낮은 펠트 모자를 쓰고 있다는 점만 달랐다. 이 모든 것을 나는 한순간에 보았는데, 그것을 볼 시간이 한순간밖에 없었기 때문이다. 사내는 내게 욕설을 퍼붓고 한 대 후려치려 했으나 — 그것은 빗나가 나를 맞히지도 못하고 오히려 그 자신을 거의 쓰러뜨릴 뻔한, 둥글고 힘없는 주먹질이어서, 그를 비틀거리게 만들었다 — 그러고는 안개 속으로 달아났는데, 가는 동안 두 번이나 비틀거렸고, 나는 그를 놓치고 말았다.\n\n\"바로 그 젊은 놈이다!\" 나는 그를 알아보며 가슴이 철렁 내려앉는 것을 느끼며 생각했다. 내 간이 어디 있는지 알았더라면, 분명 간에도 통증을 느꼈으리라.\n\n그 일이 있고 나서 나는 곧 포대에 이르렀고, 거기에는 제대로 된 그 사내가 — 마치 밤새도록 한 번도 제 몸을 끌어안고 절뚝거리기를 멈추지 않은 듯이, 제 몸을 끌어안고 이리저리 절뚝거리며 — 나를 기다리고 있었다. 정말이지 그는 지독하게 추워 보였다. 나는 그가 내 눈앞에서 푹 쓰러져 끔찍한 추위로 죽어 버리는 게 아닐까 반쯤 예상할 정도였다. 그의 두 눈 또한 어찌나 무섭게 굶주려 보이던지, 내가 줄칼을 건네주자 그가 그것을 풀밭에 내려놓는 것을 보며, 만약 내 보따리를 보지 못했더라면 그는 그 줄칼이라도 먹어 치우려 했으리라는 생각이 들었다. 이번에는 그가 내가 가진 것을 빼앗으려고 나를 거꾸로 뒤집지 않고, 내가 보따리를 풀고 주머니를 비우는 동안 나를 바로 세워 두었다.\n\n\"병에 든 게 뭐냐, 얘야?\" 사내가 말했다.\n\n\"브랜디예요.\" 내가 말했다.\n\n그는 이미 다진 고기를 더없이 기묘한 방식으로 목구멍으로 넘기고 있었는데 — 그것을 먹는 사람이라기보다는 무언가를 어디론가 사납게 서둘러 치워 버리는 사람에 더 가까웠다 — 그러나 그 술을 좀 마시려고 잠시 멈추었다. 그러는 내내 어찌나 격하게 떨던지, 병 주둥이를 물어뜯어 버리지 않고 이 사이에 물고 있는 것조차 그로서는 여간 힘든 일이 아니었다.\n\n\"학질에 걸리신 것 같아요.\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"네 말이 옳은 것 같다, 얘야.\" 그가 말했다.\n\n\"이 근방은 그게 심해요.\" 내가 그에게 일러 주었다. \"습지에 누워 계셨잖아요. 거긴 학질이 지독해요. 류머티즘도요.\"\n\n\"그것들 때문에 죽기 전에 이 아침밥부터 먹어 치우겠다.\" 그가 말했다. \"먹고 나서 곧장 저기 저 너머에 있는 교수대에 매달리게 된다 해도, 나는 그렇게 할 거다. 이 떨림쯤은 그때까지 충분히 이겨 낼 거야, 내기를 해도 좋다.\"\n\n그는 다진 고기와 고기 뼈, 빵, 치즈, 돼지고기 파이를 한꺼번에 게걸스레 먹어 치우고 있었다. 그러면서 우리를 둘러싼 안개를 의심스럽게 노려보았고, 이따금 멈추어 — 심지어 턱의 움직임마저 멈추고 — 귀를 기울였다. 실제든 상상이든 어떤 소리, 강 위에서 나는 쨍그랑하는 소리나 습지에서 들리는 짐승의 숨소리가 그를 흠칫 놀라게 했고, 그는 불쑥 말했다.\n\n\"너 속임수 부리는 도깨비 같은 놈은 아니겠지? 누구도 데려오지 않았겠지?\"\n\n\"네, 아저씨! 안 데려왔어요!\"\n\n\"누구한테 너를 따라오라고 일러 두지도 않았고?\"\n\n\"안 했어요!\"\n\"그래.\" 사내가 말했다. \"네 말을 믿으마. 네 나이에, 이 가엾고 비참한 짐승만큼이나 죽음과 두엄 더미 가까이로 쫓기는 그런 짐승을 사냥하는 일을 거든다면, 너야말로 정말 모진 어린 사냥개일 테니까!\"\n\n마치 그의 몸속에 시계 같은 장치가 들어 있어 곧 종을 칠 듯이, 그의 목구멍에서 무언가가 딸깍하는 소리를 냈다. 그러고는 그는 너덜너덜하고 거친 소매로 두 눈을 문질러 닦았다.\n\n그의 처량한 신세가 가여워, 그가 차츰 파이에 자리 잡고 먹어 가는 모습을 지켜보다가, 나는 용기를 내어 말했다. \"맛있게 드시니 다행이에요.\"\n\n\"뭐라고 했냐?\"\n\n\"맛있게 드시니 다행이라고 했어요.\"\n\n\"고맙다, 얘야. 정말 맛있구나.\"\n\n나는 우리 집의 커다란 개가 먹이를 먹는 모습을 종종 지켜보곤 했는데, 이제 그 개의 먹는 방식과 이 사내의 방식 사이에 뚜렷한 닮은 점이 있음을 알아차렸다. 사내는 그 개와 꼭 마찬가지로, 세고 날카롭게 불쑥불쑥 베어 물었다. 그리고 입에 넣은 것을 모조리 너무 빨리, 너무 성급하게 삼켰다 — 아니, 차라리 덥석 낚아챘다고 해야 옳다. 또 먹는 동안 사방으로 곁눈질을 했는데, 마치 누군가 그 파이를 빼앗으러 사방에서 다가올 위험이 있다고 여기는 것 같았다. 내 생각에 그는 그것에 대해 마음이 너무도 불안정한 나머지, 그것을 느긋하게 음미하지도 못했고, 또 누군가와 함께 식사를 한다면 그 손님을 향해 턱을 한 번 덥석 물어 버리지 않고는 못 배길 것 같았다. 이 모든 점에서 그는 그 개와 무척 닮아 있었다.\n\n\"그 사람한테 남겨 주실 게 없을까 봐 걱정돼요.\" 내가 머뭇거리며 말했다. 그 말을 하는 것이 예의에 어긋나지 않을까 망설이며 침묵을 지킨 뒤였다. \"그게 나온 데서는 더 구할 게 없거든요.\" 이 사실이 확실했기에 나는 그 말을 넌지시 꺼낼 마음이 들었던 것이다.\n\n\"그놈한테 남겨 두라고? 그놈이 누구냐?\" 내 친구가 파이 껍질을 와작와작 씹던 것을 멈추며 말했다.\n\n\"그 젊은 사람이요. 아저씨가 말씀하신 사람. 아저씨랑 같이 숨어 있다는 사람이요.\"\n\n\"아, 그래!\" 그가 무뚝뚝한 웃음 비슷한 것을 지으며 대꾸했다. \"그놈? 그래, 그래! 그놈은 먹을 게 필요 없어.\"\n\n\"제가 보기엔 필요한 것 같던데요.\" 내가 말했다.\n\n사내는 먹기를 멈추고, 더없이 날카로운 눈초리와 더없이 큰 놀라움으로 나를 살펴보았다.\n\n\"보였다고? 언제?\"\n\n\"방금요.\"\n\n\"어디서?\"\n\n\"저쪽이요.\" 내가 손가락으로 가리키며 말했다. \"저기 저쪽, 그 사람이 꾸벅꾸벅 졸고 있는 걸 보고 아저씨인 줄 알았던 데서요.\"\n\n그는 내 멱살을 붙잡고 어찌나 나를 노려보던지, 나는 내 목을 따겠다던 그의 처음 생각이 되살아난 게 아닐까 하는 생각이 들기 시작했다.\n\n\"아저씨처럼 옷을 입었는데, 모자만 쓰고 있었어요.\" 나는 떨면서 설명했다. \"그리고 — 그리고\" — 나는 이 말을 조심스럽게 꺼내고 싶은 마음이 간절했다 — \"그리고 — 줄칼을 빌리고 싶어 할 만한, 똑같은 이유를 가지고 있었어요. 어젯밤에 대포 소리 못 들으셨어요?\"\n\n\"그럼 발포가 있었던 거로군!\" 그가 혼잣말로 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"그걸 모르고 계셨다니 이상해요.\" 내가 대꾸했다. \"우리는 집에서도 그 소리를 들었는데, 거기는 더 멀고, 게다가 우리는 문도 다 닫고 있었거든요.\"\n\n\"그게 말이다, 들어 봐라!\" 그가 말했다. \"이런 벌판에 사람이 혼자 있으면서, 머리가 어지럽고 뱃속이 텅 빈 채로, 추위와 굶주림에 죽어 가다 보면, 밤새도록 들리는 거라곤 총 쏘는 소리와 부르는 목소리뿐이야. 들린다고? 보이기까지 하지. 앞세운 횃불에 붉은 군복이 환히 비친 군인들이 자기를 에워싸고 좁혀 들어오는 게 보여. 자기 번호가 불리는 게 들리고, 정지하라는 호령이 들리고, 머스킷 총의 철컥거리는 소리가 들리고, '준비! 겨눠! 똑바로 조준해, 제군들!' 하는 명령이 들리고 — 그러다 손이 자기 몸에 닿고 — 그런데 아무것도 없어! 그게 말이다, 어젯밤에 추격대 하나를 봤다 치면 — 줄을 맞춰 다가오면서, 빌어먹을, 저벅저벅 발소리를 내면서 말이야 — 백 개는 봤다는 거야. 그리고 발포로 말하자면! 그게, 날이 환히 밝은 다음에도 대포 소리에 안개가 흔들리는 게 보였단 말이다. 그런데 이 사내는\" 하고, 그는 마치 내가 거기 있다는 사실을 잊은 듯 나머지 말을 다 한 뒤였다. \"그놈한테서 뭔가 알아챈 게 있냐?\"\n\n\"얼굴에 멍이 심하게 들어 있었어요.\" 나는 내가 안다는 사실조차 거의 몰랐던 것을 떠올리며 말했다.\n\n\"여기 아니냐?\" 사내가 손바닥으로 자기 왼쪽 뺨을 사정없이 후려치며 외쳤다.\n\n\"네, 거기예요!\"\n\n\"그놈 어디 있냐?\" 그는 남은 얼마 안 되는 음식을 잿빛 윗도리 가슴팍에 쑤셔 넣었다. \"그놈이 간 길을 가르쳐 다오. 핏자국을 쫓는 사냥개처럼 끌어내리고 말겠다. 성한 데 없는 이 다리에 채워진 쇠고랑, 빌어먹을! 줄칼 이리 내놔라, 얘야.\"\n\n나는 안개가 그 다른 사내를 가린 방향을 일러 주었고, 그는 한순간 그쪽을 올려다보았다. 그러나 그는 곧 무성하고 축축한 풀밭에 주저앉아, 미친 사람처럼 쇠고랑을 줄로 갈아 댔는데, 나도, 자기 다리도 아랑곳하지 않았다. 그 다리에는 오래된 쓸린 상처가 있어 피가 흐르고 있었지만, 그는 마치 그것이 줄칼만큼이나 감각이 없다는 듯이 거칠게 다루었다. 그가 이렇게 사나운 조급함에 빠져드는 것을 보니, 나는 다시금 그가 몹시 무서워졌고, 마찬가지로 더 이상 집을 비우고 있는 것이 몹시 두려워졌다. 나는 가야겠다고 그에게 말했으나, 그는 들은 척도 하지 않았다. 그래서 내가 할 수 있는 가장 나은 일은 슬그머니 빠져나가는 것이겠다 싶었다. 내가 마지막으로 본 그의 모습은, 무릎 위로 고개를 숙이고 차꼬를 열심히 갈아 대며, 그 차꼬와 자기 다리를 향해 참지 못하고 저주를 중얼거리고 있는 것이었다. 내가 마지막으로 들은 그의 소리는, 안개 속에 멈춰 서서 귀를 기울였을 때, 여전히 줄칼이 갈리고 있는 소리였다.\n\n# Chapter IV.\n\n부엌에 순경이 나를 잡아가려고 기다리고 있을 거라고 나는 단단히 각오하고 있었다. 그러나 거기에는 순경이 없었을 뿐만 아니라, 도둑맞은 일조차 아직 발각되지 않은 상태였다. 조 부인은 그날의 잔치를 위해 집을 단장하느라 엄청나게 바빴고, 조는 부엌 문턱에 내쫓겨 있었는데, 그것은 그를 쓰레받기로부터 떼어 놓기 위함이었다. 누나가 자기 집의 마룻바닥을 기운차게 쓸어 댈 때면, 그의 운명은 늘 머지않아 그 물건 속으로 그를 끌어들이곤 했던 것이다.\n\n\"그래, 도대체 너는 어디 가 있었던 거냐?\" 나와 내 양심이 모습을 드러내자, 조 부인이 건넨 크리스마스 인사가 이러했다.\n\n나는 캐럴을 들으러 내려갔었다고 말했다. \"아! 그래!\" 조 부인이 말했다. \"더 나쁜 짓을 할 수도 있었겠지.\" 그건 의심할 여지가 없다고 나는 생각했다.\n\n\"내가 대장장이 마누라가 아니고, (그게 그거지만) 앞치마를 한시도 벗을 수 없는 노예가 아니었다면, 나도 캐럴을 들으러 갔을지 모르지.\" 조 부인이 말했다. \"나는 캐럴을 꽤나 좋아하는 편이거든. 그러니 도무지 한 번도 못 듣는 게 당연하지.\"\n\n조는 쓰레받기가 우리 앞에서 물러간 덕에 나를 따라 부엌으로 들어와 있었는데, 조 부인이 그에게 눈길을 쏘아 던지자 화해를 청하는 기색으로 손등을 코에 갖다 댔다가, 그녀의 눈이 거두어지자 슬그머니 두 집게손가락을 엇갈려 놓고는 그것을 내게 내보였다. 조 부인이 심사가 뒤틀려 있다는 우리만의 신호였다. 그것은 너무도 그녀의 평상시 상태였으므로, 조와 나는 몇 주일씩 내리, 우리 손가락에 관한 한, 다리를 그렇게 꼬고 있는 십자군 기념 조각상 같은 꼴이 되곤 했다.\n\n우리는 절인 돼지고기 다리에 야채를 곁들인 요리와, 속을 채운 통닭구이 두 마리로 이루어진 훌륭한 만찬을 들기로 되어 있었다. 어제 아침에 보기 좋은 민스파이가 만들어졌고(다진 고기가 없어진 것이 눈에 띄지 않았던 까닭이 여기에 있었다), 푸딩은 이미 끓는 중이었다. 이런 대대적인 준비 탓에 우리는 아침 식사에 관해서는 인정사정없이 끊겨 버렸다. \"암.\" 조 부인이 말했다. \"내 앞에 이렇게 할 일이 산더미인데, 지금 격식 차려서 처먹고 배 두드리고 설거지하고 그럴 마음은 추호도 없다, 그건 내가 장담하지!\"\n\n그리하여 우리는, 집에 있는 어른 하나 아이 하나가 아니라 마치 강행군 중인 이천 명의 군대라도 되는 양, 빵을 한 조각씩 배급받았다. 그리고 우리는 미안해하는 낯빛으로 찬장 위의 주전자에서 따른 우유 탄 물을 꿀꺽꿀꺽 들이켰다. 그러는 사이 조 부인은 깨끗한 흰 커튼을 달고, 넓은 벽난로 위에 헌것을 대신할 새 꽃무늬 주름 장식을 박아 붙였으며, 복도 건너편의 작은 응접실을 덮개에서 벗겨 냈다. 그곳은 다른 때에는 결코 덮개가 벗겨지는 일이 없이, 한 해의 나머지를 은종이의 서늘한 안개 속에서 보내는 곳이었다. 그 은종이는 벽난로 선반 위의 작은 흰 도자기 푸들 네 마리에까지 씌워져 있었는데, 그 푸들들은 저마다 검은 코에 입에는 꽃바구니를 물고 있었으며, 하나가 다른 하나와 똑같이 닮은 꼴이었다. 조 부인은 무척 깨끗한 살림꾼이었지만, 자신의 청결함을 더러움 그 자체보다도 더 불편하고 받아들이기 힘든 것으로 만드는 절묘한 재주가 있었다. 청결은 경건함에 버금가는 것이라 했는데, 어떤 이들은 자기네 신앙을 가지고도 꼭 그렇게 한다.\n\n우리 누나는 할 일이 너무 많아서 대리로 교회에 가기로 했다. 다시 말해, 조와 내가 가는 것이었다. 작업복을 입었을 때 조는 다부지게 균형 잡힌, 대장장이다운 풍모의 사내였다. 그러나 나들이옷을 입으면 그는 무엇보다도 형편이 넉넉한 허수아비에 더 가까웠다. 그때 그가 걸친 것은 무엇 하나 그에게 맞거나 그의 것처럼 보이는 게 없었고, 그때 그가 걸친 것은 무엇 하나 그의 살갗을 쓸어 대지 않는 게 없었다. 이 잔칫날에도 그는, 흥겨운 종소리가 울려 퍼질 때, 일요일용 참회복 한 벌을 빈틈없이 갖춰 입은 비참함 그 자체의 모습으로 제 방에서 나왔다. 나로 말하자면, 우리 누나는 내가 산파 노릇을 하는 순경에게 (내 생일에) 붙들려 그녀에게 넘겨진 어린 범죄자이며, 모욕당한 법의 위엄에 따라 처분되어야 마땅한 존재라는 막연한 관념을 품고 있었던 게 틀림없다고 나는 생각한다. 나는 늘, 이성과 종교와 도덕의 명령을 거스르고, 또 나를 가장 아끼는 친구들의 만류하는 충고를 무릅쓰고 기어이 태어나겠다고 우긴 자인 양 취급받았다. 새 옷을 한 벌 맞추러 데려갈 때조차도, 재단사는 그것을 일종의 소년원복처럼 짓되, 어떤 경우에도 내가 사지를 자유로이 놀리지 못하게 하라는 지시를 받았다.\n\n따라서 조와 내가 교회에 가는 광경은 동정심 많은 이들에게는 가슴 뭉클한 볼거리였음에 틀림없다. 그러나 내가 겉으로 겪은 고통은 속으로 감당한 것에 비하면 아무것도 아니었다. 조 부인이 식료품 저장실 근처로 가거나 방을 나설 때마다 나를 엄습하던 공포는, 오직 내 두 손이 저지른 짓을 곱씹으며 내 마음에 들러붙던 회한에만 견줄 만한 것이었다. 나는 내 사악한 비밀의 무게에 짓눌린 채, 만일 내가 교회라는 기관에 그것을 털어놓는다면 교회가 저 무서운 젊은 놈의 복수로부터 나를 지켜 줄 만큼 충분히 강한 곳일까 하고 곰곰이 생각했다. 나는 혼인 예고가 낭독되고 목사가 \"이제 그것을 밝히시오!\"라고 말하는 그 순간이, 내가 자리에서 일어나 제의실에서 은밀한 면담을 청할 때라는 생각을 떠올렸다. 그날이 크리스마스이지 일요일이 아니었다는 점만 아니었다면, 내가 이 극단적인 수단에 호소하여 우리의 작은 신도들을 깜짝 놀라게 하지 않았으리라고는 결코 장담할 수 없다.\n\n교회 서기인 웝슬 씨가 우리와 함께 식사하기로 되어 있었고, 수레바퀴 만드는 허블 씨와 허블 부인, 그리고 펌블추크 아저씨도 함께였다. 펌블추크 아저씨는 (조의 삼촌이었지만 조 부인이 제 것으로 챙겨 두었다) 가장 가까운 읍내에서 형편이 넉넉한 곡물상을 하며 자기 소유의 이륜마차를 손수 몰았다. 만찬 시각은 한 시 반이었다. 조와 내가 집에 돌아와 보니, 식탁은 차려져 있고, 조 부인은 옷을 차려입었고, 음식은 한창 마무리되는 중이었으며, 손님들이 들어올 수 있도록 현관문은 열려 있었고(다른 때에는 결코 그런 일이 없었다), 모든 것이 더없이 으리으리했다. 그런데도 여전히, 도둑맞은 일에 대해서는 한마디도 없었다.\n\n내 심정에 아무런 위안도 가져다주지 못한 채 시간이 왔고, 손님들이 왔다. 로마식 매부리코에 크고 번들거리는 대머리 이마를 가진 웝슬 씨는, 자신이 유난히 자랑스러워하는 굵은 목소리의 소유자였다. 실제로 그의 지인들 사이에서는, 그에게 마음껏 하게만 내버려 두면 목사를 발작이 나도록 압도하며 성경을 읽어 낼 거라는 평이 있었다. 그 자신도, 교회가 — 경쟁에 — \"개방\"되기만 한다면 거기서 두각을 나타내는 일을 단념하지 않겠노라고 고백했다. 교회가 \"개방\"되지 않았으므로, 앞서 말했듯이 그는 우리 교회의 서기였다. 그러나 그는 아멘을 어마어마하게 강조했고, 시편을 낭송할 때면 — 늘 한 구절을 통째로 읊었는데 — 먼저 신도들을 빙 둘러보았다. 마치 이렇게 말하는 듯이. \"여러분은 방금 머리 위에 있는 내 친구의 낭독을 들으셨소. 그럼 이번엔 이 방식에 대한 여러분의 의견을 들려주시구려!\"\n\n나는 손님들에게 문을 열어 주었는데 — 마치 우리가 그 문을 열어 주는 것이 으레 있는 일인 양 꾸며 가면서 — 먼저 웝슬 씨에게, 다음으로 허블 씨 부부에게, 그리고 마지막으로 펌블추크 아저씨에게 열어 주었다. 덧붙이자면, 나는 더없이 혹독한 벌을 무릅쓰지 않고서는 그를 아저씨라고 부를 수 없었다.\n\n\"조 부인.\" 펌블추크 아저씨가 말했다. 그는 숨소리가 거칠고 동작이 굼뜬 덩치 큰 중년 사내로, 물고기 같은 입에 멍하니 응시하는 흐릿한 눈을 가졌고, 모래빛 머리카락이 머리에 곤두서 있어서 마치 방금 거의 목이 졸렸다가 그 순간 정신이 돌아온 사람처럼 보였다. \"이 계절의 인사로 가져왔습니다 — 부인, 셰리주 한 병을 가져왔습니다 — 그리고 부인, 포트와인 한 병도 가져왔습니다.\"\n\n해마다 크리스마스가 되면 그는, 마치 굉장한 진기함이라도 되는 양, 토씨 하나 틀리지 않은 똑같은 말을 하며, 두 병을 아령처럼 들고 모습을 드러냈다. 해마다 크리스마스가 되면 조 부인은, 지금 대꾸하는 것과 똑같이 대꾸했다. \"오, 펌—블추—크 아—저씨! 정말 친절하시기도 하지!\" 해마다 크리스마스가 되면 그는, 지금 받아치는 것과 똑같이 받아쳤다. \"당신이 받을 만한 것 이상은 아니올시다. 그래, 다들 잘 지내시오? 그리고 반 페니짜리 육 펜스어치는 어떻게 지내나?\" 나를 두고 하는 말이었다.\n\n이런 날이면 우리는 부엌에서 식사를 하고, 견과류와 오렌지와 사과를 들기 위해 응접실로 자리를 옮겼다. 그것은 작업복에서 나들이옷으로 갈아입는 조의 변신과 무척이나 닮은 변화였다. 누나는 이날따라 유난히 활기찼는데, 사실 그녀는 다른 누구와 함께 있을 때보다 허블 부인과 어울릴 때면 대체로 더 상냥했다. 내 기억에 허블 부인은, 하늘색 옷을 입은 자그마하고 곱슬머리에 모가 진 사람으로, 관습적으로 어린 사람 행세를 했는데, 이는 그녀가 — 얼마나 오래전인지는 모르겠지만 — 자기보다 훨씬 나이 많은 허블 씨와 결혼했을 때 한참 어렸던 까닭이었다. 허블 씨는, 내 기억에, 질기고 어깨가 높이 솟아 구부정한 톱밥 냄새가 나는 노인이었는데, 다리를 어찌나 엄청나게 벌리고 있던지, 어린 시절 그를 골목길에서 마주칠 때면 나는 늘 그의 두 다리 사이로 몇 마일이나 되는 탁 트인 들판이 보이는 것 같았다.\n\n이런 좋은 분들 가운데 끼어 있자니, 설령 내가 저장실을 털지 않았더라도 나는 거북한 처지에 놓인 기분이었을 것이다. 그것은 내가 식탁보의 예각진 모서리에 끼인 채, 식탁 모서리가 내 가슴팍을 누르고 펌블추크식 팔꿈치가 내 눈을 찔러서가 아니었고, 내가 말하는 것이 허락되지 않아서도 아니었으며(나는 말하고 싶지도 않았다), 비늘 같은 닭다리 끄트머리와, 돼지가 살아 있을 적에 가장 자랑스러워할 이유가 없었을 그 후미진 돼지고기 부위를 대접받아서도 아니었다. 아니다. 그저 나를 가만히 내버려 두기만 했다면 그런 건 개의치 않았을 것이다. 그러나 그들은 나를 가만히 두지 않았다. 그들은 이따금 화제를 내게로 겨누어 그 끝을 내게 콱 찔러 넣지 않으면, 모처럼의 기회를 놓치는 거라고 여기는 듯했다. 나는 이런 도덕적인 막대기들에 어찌나 따끔따끔 찔려 댔던지, 마치 스페인 투기장에 끌려 나온 가엾은 작은 황소가 된 기분이었다.\n\n그것은 우리가 식탁에 앉는 순간부터 시작되었다. 웝슬 씨가 연극조의 낭독으로 식전 기도를 올렸는데 — 지금 돌이켜 보면, 무슨 「햄릿」에 나오는 유령과 리처드 삼세를 종교적으로 교배해 놓은 것 같았다 — 우리가 진정으로 감사할 줄 알게 해 달라는 더없이 지당한 소망으로 끝맺었다. 그러자 누나는 나를 눈으로 쏘아보며, 낮고 책망하는 목소리로 말했다. \"저 말 들었지? 감사한 줄 알아라.\"\n\n\"특히 말이다.\" 펌블추크 씨가 말했다. \"얘야, 손수 너를 키워 준 분들께 감사할 줄 알아라.\"\n\n허블 부인은 고개를 절레절레 흔들고는, 내가 끝내 변변치 못한 인간이 되리라는 애처로운 예감으로 나를 들여다보며 물었다. \"어째서 젊은것들은 도무지 감사할 줄을 모를까요?\" 이 도덕적 수수께끼는 좌중에게 너무도 벅찬 것이었으나, 마침내 허블 씨가 \"천성이 고약해서지\"라고 간명하게 풀어 주었다. 그러자 모두가 \"옳거니!\" 하고 웅얼거리며, 유난히 불쾌하고도 노골적인 태도로 나를 바라보았다.\n\n손님이 있을 때 조의 지위와 영향력은 (가능한 일이라면) 손님이 없을 때보다 한층 더 미약했다. 그러나 그는 자기 나름의 어떤 방식으로 할 수 있을 때마다 늘 나를 거들고 위로해 주었고, 식사 때면 늘 그렇게 했다. 곧 고기 국물이 있으면 그것을 내게 떠 주는 것이었다. 오늘은 국물이 넉넉했으므로, 이 대목에서 조는 내 접시에 반 파인트쯤 떠 주었다.\n\n만찬이 조금 더 진행되자, 웝슬 씨는 다소 준엄하게 설교를 평하고는 — 교회가 \"개방\"된다는 그 으레 나오는 가정의 경우에 — 자기라면 어떤 설교를 해 주었을지를 넌지시 비쳤다. 그 설교의 요점 몇 가지를 좌중에게 베풀어 준 다음, 그는 오늘 설교의 주제 선정이 잘못되었다고 평했는데, 게다가 \"돌아다니는\" 주제가 그토록 많은 마당에 그것은 더더욱 변명의 여지가 없는 일이라고 덧붙였다.\n\n\"그것도 옳은 말씀이오.\" 펌블추크 아저씨가 말했다. \"정곡을 찌르셨소이다! 꼬리에 소금을 칠 줄 아는 사람에게라면, 돌아다니는 주제야 얼마든지 있지요. 바로 그게 필요한 거지요. 소금통만 준비되어 있다면야, 주제를 찾으러 멀리 갈 것도 없는 법이오.\" 펌블추크 씨는 잠시 생각에 잠겼다가 덧붙였다. \"돼지고기만 봐도 그렇소. 그게 바로 주제 아니겠소! 주제를 원한다면, 돼지고기를 보시구려!\"\n\n\"옳은 말씀입니다. 젊은이를 위한 교훈도 숱하지요.\" 웝슬 씨가 응수했다 — 나는 그가 말을 꺼내기도 전에 또 나를 끌어들이려 한다는 걸 알았다. \"저 본문에서 얼마든지 끌어낼 수 있지요.\"\n\n(\"너는 이걸 잘 들어 둬.\" 누나가 준엄한 곁말로 내게 말했다.)\n\n조가 내게 국물을 좀 더 떠 주었다.\n\n\"돼지란.\" 웝슬 씨가, 마치 내 세례명이라도 부르듯이 내 붉어진 얼굴에 포크를 겨누며, 더없이 굵은 목소리로 말을 이었다. \"돼지란 탕자의 동무였소이다. 돼지의 탐식은 젊은이를 위한 본보기로 우리 앞에 제시되어 있지요.\" (살이 통통하고 즙이 풍부하다고 그 돼지고기를 한껏 추켜세우던 사람으로서는 꽤나 가관이라고 나는 생각했다.) \"돼지에게서 가증스러운 것은 사내아이에게서는 더욱 가증스러운 법이오.\"\n\n\"아니면 계집아이든지요.\" 허블 씨가 거들었다.\n\n\"물론이지요, 아니면 계집아이든지요, 허블 씨.\" 웝슬 씨가 다소 짜증스럽게 동의했다. \"하지만 여기 계집아이는 없잖소.\"\n\n\"게다가 말이다.\" 펌블추크 씨가 홱 나를 돌아보며 말했다. \"네가 무엇에 감사해야 하는지 생각해 봐라. 만일 네가 꽥꽥거리는 새끼 돼지로 태어났더라면 —\"\n\n\"태어났고말고요. 그런 자식이 세상에 있다면 바로 저놈이지요.\" 누나가 더없이 힘주어 말했다.\n\n조가 내게 국물을 좀 더 떠 주었다.\n\n\"아니, 내 말은 네발 달린 새끼 돼지 말이다.\" 펌블추크 씨가 말했다. \"만일 네가 그런 걸로 태어났더라면, 지금 여기 이렇게 있겠느냐? 천만에 —\"\n\n\"저 접시 위의 모습이라면 모를까요.\" 웝슬 씨가 요리 접시 쪽으로 고갯짓하며 말했다.\n\n\"하지만 나는 그런 모습을 말하는 게 아니오, 선생.\" 말이 끊기는 것을 싫어하는 펌블추크 씨가 응수했다. \"내 말은, 어른들과 윗사람들과 어울려 즐기고, 그분들의 대화로 자기 자신을 갈고닦으며, 호사스러운 품 안에서 뒹군다는 거요. 이놈이 그러고 있겠느냐 이 말이오? 아니지, 그러지 못할 거요. 그럼 네 운명이 어찌 되었겠느냐?\" 그가 다시 나를 향해 돌아섰다. \"물건의 시세에 따라 몇 실링에 처분되어 버렸을 테고, 정육점의 던스터블이 짚더미에 누운 네게 다가와서는, 너를 왼팔 밑에 척 끼고, 오른손으로는 조끼 주머니에서 주머니칼을 꺼내려고 작업복 자락을 걷어 올리고는, 네 피를 쏟게 하고 네 목숨을 거두어 갔겠지. 그러면 손수 길러 주는 일 따위는 없었을 거다. 어림도 없지!\"\n\n조가 내게 국물을 좀 더 권했지만, 나는 받기가 두려웠다.\n\n\"이 애가 여간 속을 썩이지 않았겠어요, 부인.\" 허블 부인이 누나를 위로하며 말했다.\n\n\"속을 썩이느냐고요?\" 누나가 되받았다. \"속을 썩여요?\" 그러더니 누나는, 내가 저지른 온갖 병치레와, 내가 범한 온갖 밤잠 설치게 한 짓과, 내가 굴러떨어진 온갖 높은 곳과, 내가 빠져든 온갖 낮은 곳과, 내가 내 몸에 입힌 온갖 상처와, 나를 무덤에 처넣고 싶어 한 온갖 순간들과, 그런데도 내가 고집스럽게 그리로 가기를 마다한 일들의 무시무시한 목록을 늘어놓기 시작했다.\n\n로마인들은 자기네 코로 서로를 무던히도 짜증나게 했을 게 틀림없다고 나는 생각한다. 어쩌면 그 탓에 그들이 그토록 가만있지 못하는 족속이 되었는지도 모른다. 어쨌거나 내 비행이 낭송되는 동안 웝슬 씨의 로마식 매부리코가 어찌나 나를 짜증나게 했던지, 나는 그가 비명을 지를 때까지 그것을 잡아당겨 주고 싶었다. 그러나 이때까지 내가 견뎌 온 모든 것은, 누나의 낭송에 뒤이은 그 정적이 깨졌을 때 나를 사로잡은 끔찍한 심정에 비하면 아무것도 아니었다. 그 정적이 이어지는 동안, 모두가 (나는 괴롭도록 또렷이 느꼈다) 분노와 혐오의 눈길로 나를 바라보았던 것이다.\n\n\"그래도 말이오.\" 펌블추크 씨가, 좌중을 그들이 빗나간 화제로 슬며시 되돌리며 말했다. \"돼지고기는 — 삶은 것으로 치면 — 또 기름지지 않소이까?\"\n\n\"브랜디 좀 드세요, 아저씨.\" 누나가 말했다.\n\n오 하늘이시여, 마침내 올 것이 오고 말았다! 그는 그것이 약하다는 걸 알아챌 테고, 약하다고 말할 테고, 그러면 나는 끝장이었다! 나는 식탁보 밑으로 두 손으로 식탁 다리를 꽉 붙들고 내 운명을 기다렸다.\n\n누나는 돌로 만든 병을 가지러 갔다가, 그 돌 병을 들고 돌아와 그에게 브랜디를 따라 주었다. 다른 누구도 마시지 않았다. 그 가련한 사내는 잔을 만지작거리며 — 들어 올렸다가, 빛에 비춰 보고, 도로 내려놓으면서 — 내 고통을 질질 늘였다. 그러는 동안 내내 조 부인과 조는 파이와 푸딩을 위해 분주히 식탁을 치우고 있었다.\n\n나는 그에게서 눈을 뗄 수가 없었다. 줄곧 손과 발로 식탁 다리를 꽉 붙든 채로, 나는 그 가련한 작자가 장난스럽게 잔을 만지작거리고, 그것을 들어 올리고, 미소 짓고, 고개를 뒤로 젖혀 브랜디를 단숨에 들이켜는 모습을 지켜보았다. 그 직후, 좌중은 이루 말할 수 없는 경악에 휩싸였다. 그가 벌떡 일어서더니, 소름 끼치는 발작적 백일해 춤이라도 추듯 몇 번이나 빙빙 돌다가, 문밖으로 뛰쳐나갔던 것이다. 이윽고 그는 창문 너머로, 격렬하게 몸부림치고 침을 뱉어 대며, 더없이 흉측한 표정을 짓는, 영락없이 실성한 듯한 모습으로 나타났다.\n\n조 부인과 조가 그에게 달려가는 동안에도 나는 꽉 붙들고 있었다. 어떻게 한 일인지는 알 수 없었으나, 내가 어떻게든 그를 죽인 게 틀림없다고 나는 의심치 않았다. 이 끔찍한 처지에서, 그가 다시 안으로 끌려 들어와, 마치 좌중이 자기 비위를 거스르기라도 한 양 사람들을 빙 둘러보고는, 의미심장하게 한 번 헐떡이며 \"타르!\" 하고 의자에 털썩 주저앉았을 때, 그것은 차라리 안도였다.\n\n나는 타르 물 주전자에서 그 병을 채워 두었던 것이다. 나는 그가 머지않아 더 나빠지리라는 걸 알았다. 나는 오늘날의 영매(靈媒)처럼, 보이지 않는 손길로 식탁을 힘껏 거머쥔 채 그것을 움직였다.\n\n\"타르라니!\" 누나가 어리둥절하여 외쳤다. \"아니, 어떻게 타르가 거기에 들어갔담?\"\n\n그러나 그 부엌에서 전능한 존재였던 펌블추크 아저씨는, 그 말을 들으려 하지도, 그 화제를 입에 올리려고도 하지 않고, 위엄 있게 손을 내저어 그 모든 것을 물리치고는, 뜨거운 진에 물을 탄 것을 청했다. 불안하리만치 골똘히 생각에 잠기기 시작했던 누나는, 진과 뜨거운 물과 설탕과 레몬 껍질을 가져다 섞느라 분주히 손을 놀려야 했다. 적어도 당분간은 나는 살아난 것이었다. 나는 여전히 식탁 다리를 붙들고 있었으나, 이제는 그것을 감사의 열정으로 꽉 거머쥐었다.\n\n차츰 나는 손아귀를 풀고 푸딩을 들 만큼 진정되었다. 펌블추크 씨도 푸딩을 들었다. 모두가 푸딩을 들었다. 그 순서가 끝나고, 펌블추크 씨는 진에 물을 탄 것의 흐뭇한 기운에 젖어 환히 빛나기 시작했다. 이제 이날을 무사히 넘기겠구나 싶어질 무렵, 누나가 조에게 말했다. \"깨끗한 접시들을 — 차가운 걸로.\"\n\n나는 즉시 다시 식탁 다리를 거머쥐고, 마치 그것이 내 어린 시절의 동무이자 내 영혼의 벗이라도 되는 양 가슴에 꼭 끌어안았다. 나는 무엇이 닥쳐올지 내다보았고, 이번에야말로 정말로 끝장이라는 것을 느꼈다.\n\n\"여러분, 맛보셔야 해요.\" 누나가 더없이 우아한 태도로 손님들에게 말했다. \"마무리로 꼭 맛보셔야 해요. 펌블추크 아저씨가 주신 이 즐겁고도 맛있는 선물을 말이에요!\"\n\n맛본다고! 그들이 그것을 맛볼 가망일랑 품지도 마시라!\n\n\"실은 말이에요.\" 누나가 일어서며 말했다. \"이건 파이예요. 맛있는 돼지고기 파이랍니다.\"\n\n손님들이 웅얼웅얼 칭찬을 늘어놓았다. 자기가 동료 인간들에게 칭송받을 만한 일을 했음을 의식한 펌블추크 아저씨는, 이 모든 정황을 감안하면 꽤나 활기차게 말했다. \"그럼, 조 부인, 우리 최선을 다해 봅시다. 어디 그 파이를 한번 잘라 보십시다.\"\n\n누나는 그것을 가지러 나갔다. 나는 그녀의 발소리가 저장실 쪽으로 향하는 것을 들었다. 나는 펌블추크 씨가 나이프를 가늠하는 것을 보았다. 나는 웝슬 씨의 로마식 콧구멍에서 식욕이 되살아나는 것을 보았다. 나는 허블 씨가 \"맛있는 돼지고기 파이 한 조각이라면 무엇 위에 얹어도 다 잘 어울리고, 해될 게 없지요\"라고 말하는 것을 들었고, 조가 \"너도 좀 먹어야지, 핍\" 하고 말하는 것을 들었다. 내가 공포에 질려 날카로운 비명을 내질렀던 것이 한낱 마음속에서였는지, 아니면 좌중이 실제로 귀로 들을 만큼이었는지는 나로서도 끝내 확실히 알 수 없다. 나는 더는 견딜 수 없다는 것을, 그리고 달아나야만 한다는 것을 느꼈다. 나는 식탁 다리를 놓고, 죽기 살기로 내달렸다.\n\n그러나 나는 현관문보다 더 멀리는 달아나지 못했다. 거기서 나는 머스킷 총을 든 한 무리의 군인들에게 머리부터 들이받고 말았기 때문이다. 그중 하나가 내게 수갑 한 벌을 내밀며 말했다. \"여기 있구나, 꾸물대지 말고, 어서 가자!\"\n\n# Chapter V.\n\n장전된 머스킷의 개머리판을 우리 집 문간에 쾅쾅 내리찧으며 줄지어 나타난 군인들의 출현은, 식사 모임 사람들을 우왕좌왕 식탁에서 일어서게 만들었고, 빈손으로 부엌에 들어서던 조 부인을 우뚝 멈춰 세워 멍하니 바라보게 만들면서, 의아해하는 그 한탄을 자아냈다. \"어머나 세상에 맙소사, 어떻게 된 거야 — 그 — 파이가!\"\n\n조 부인이 멍하니 서서 바라보고 있을 때 상사와 나는 부엌에 있었는데, 바로 그 위기의 순간에 나는 정신의 기능을 어느 정도 되찾았다. 내게 말을 건넨 사람은 그 상사였고, 그는 이제 오른손에 든 수갑을 사람들 쪽으로 권하듯 내밀고 왼손은 내 어깨에 얹은 채, 좌중을 둘러보고 있었다.\n\n\"실례하겠습니다, 신사 숙녀 여러분.\" 상사가 말했다. \"다만 제가 문간에서 이 영리한 꼬마 녀석에게 이미 말씀드렸다시피,\" (그런 적은 없었다) \"저는 국왕의 이름으로 추적 중이며, 대장장이를 찾고 있습니다.\"\n\n\"그래 그 사람한테 무슨 볼일이 있으시다는 거죠?\" 누나가 받아쳤다. 누나는 남편이 누군가에게 필요한 존재로 거론되는 것 자체에 발끈하는 데 빨랐다.\n\n\"부인.\" 늠름한 상사가 대답했다. \"제 개인적으로 말씀드리자면, 부인같이 훌륭한 분과 알게 된 영광과 기쁨이라고 답하겠고, 국왕을 대신해 말씀드리자면, 자그마한 일거리 하나라고 답하겠습니다.\"\n\n이것이 상사로서는 제법 깔끔한 응답으로 받아들여졌고, 그리하여 펌블추크 씨가 들리도록 소리쳤다. \"또 한 방 멋지군!\"\n\n\"실은 말입니다, 대장장이 양반.\" 이때쯤 눈으로 조를 가려낸 상사가 말했다. \"이것들에 사고가 좀 생겼는데, 그중 하나는 자물쇠가 잘못됐고 연결 고리도 제대로 작동하질 않습니다. 당장 써먹어야 하는 물건들이니, 한번 살펴봐 주시겠소?\"\n\n조는 그것들을 한번 살펴보더니, 그 일을 하려면 화덕 불을 지펴야 하고, 한 시간보다는 두 시간에 가깝게 걸릴 것이라고 단언했다. \"그래요? 그럼 곧장 시작해 주시겠소, 대장장이 양반?\" 격식 없는 상사가 말했다. \"국왕 폐하의 일이니 말이오. 그리고 내 부하들이 어디서든 거들 수 있다면 쓸모 있게 부려 보시구려.\" 그러고서 그는 부하들을 불렀고, 그들은 차례차례 우르르 부엌으로 들어와 무기를 한쪽 구석에 쌓아 두었다. 그러고는 군인들이 으레 그러듯 여기저기 서 있었다. 어떤 이는 두 손을 앞으로 느슨히 맞잡고, 어떤 이는 무릎이나 어깨를 어딘가에 기대고, 어떤 이는 허리띠나 탄낭을 느슨히 풀고, 또 어떤 이는 문을 열고 높이 두른 옷깃 너머로 뻣뻣하게 마당으로 침을 뱉었다.\n\n이 모든 것을 나는 보면서도 그때는 보고 있다는 것을 알지 못했으니, 두려움의 고통 속에 빠져 있었기 때문이다. 그러나 그 수갑이 나를 위한 것이 아니라는 것, 그리고 군인들이 적어도 파이를 뒷전으로 밀어낼 만큼은 그것을 압도해 버렸다는 것을 차츰 알아차리면서, 나는 흩어졌던 정신을 조금 더 추슬렀다.\n\n\"몇 시인지 알려 주시겠소?\" 상사가 펌블추크 씨를 향해 물었는데, 마치 그 사람의 안목이라면 능히 시간을 알 만한 인물이라는 추론이 정당화된다는 투였다.\n\n\"이제 막 두 시 반이 지났소이다.\"\n\n\"그리 나쁘지 않군.\" 상사가 생각에 잠겨 말했다. \"설령 여기서 두 시간 가까이 발이 묶인다 해도 그 정도면 됐어. 여기서 습지까지 얼마나 된다고들 보시오? 일 마일이 채 안 되겠지?\"\n\n\"딱 일 마일이에요.\" 조 부인이 말했다.\n\n\"그 정도면 됐어. 우리는 해 질 녘쯤 그놈들을 좁혀 들어가기 시작하지. 해 지기 조금 전에 한다는 게 내 명령이오. 그 정도면 됐어.\"\n\n\"죄수들입니까, 상사님?\" 우프슬 씨가 당연하다는 투로 물었다.\n\n\"그렇소!\" 상사가 대답했다. \"둘이오. 그놈들이 아직 습지에 나와 있다는 건 제법 알려진 사실이고, 어두워지기 전에는 거기서 벗어나려 들지 않을 거요. 여기 누구든 그런 사냥감을 본 사람이 있소?\"\n\n나를 제외한 모두가 자신 있게 아니라고 말했다. 아무도 나를 떠올리지 못했다.\n\n\"그래!\" 상사가 말했다. \"그놈들은 제 예상보다 빨리 포위망에 갇힌 신세가 될 거요. 자, 대장장이 양반! 당신만 준비됐다면, 국왕 폐하께서는 준비되셨소.\"\n\n조는 외투와 조끼와 넥타이를 벗고 가죽 앞치마를 두른 채 화덕으로 들어갔다. 군인 한 명이 나무 덧창을 열었고, 다른 한 명이 불을 지폈으며, 또 다른 한 명이 풀무로 돌아섰고, 나머지는 이내 활활 타오르는 불길 둘레에 서 있었다. 그러자 조가 망치질을 시작했다, 쾅 챙, 쾅 챙, 그리고 우리는 모두 그 광경을 지켜보았다.\n\n다가올 추격에 대한 관심은 모두의 주의를 사로잡았을 뿐 아니라, 우리 누나마저도 후하게 만들었다. 누나는 군인들을 위해 통에서 맥주 한 주전자를 따랐고, 상사에게는 브랜디 한 잔을 권했다. 그러나 펌블추크 씨가 날카롭게 말했다. \"포도주를 드리시오, 부인. 그건 타르 냄새가 안 날 거라고 내가 보증하리다.\" 그리하여 상사는 그에게 고마워하며, 자기는 타르가 안 든 술을 좋아하니 마찬가지로 괜찮다면 포도주를 마시겠다고 했다. 포도주가 주어지자 그는 국왕 폐하의 만수무강과 계절의 안부를 빌며 단숨에 들이켜고는 입맛을 다셨다.\n\n\"좋은 물건이지요, 상사 양반?\" 펌블추크 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"내 한 가지 말씀드리리다.\" 상사가 대답했다. \"그 물건이 댁에서 마련한 것 아닌가 싶소만.\"\n\n펌블추크 씨가 살집 좋은 웃음을 지으며 말했다. \"허허, 그렇소? 어째서요?\"\n\n\"왜냐하면,\" 상사가 그의 어깨를 탁 치며 대답했다. \"댁은 뭐가 뭔지 아는 사람이니까요.\"\n\n\"그렇게 보시오?\" 펌블추크 씨가 아까와 같은 웃음을 지으며 말했다. \"한 잔 더 드시오!\"\n\n\"댁과 함께라면. 자 건배합시다.\" 상사가 대답했다. \"내 잔 위쪽을 댁 잔 바닥에 — 댁 잔 바닥을 내 잔 위쪽에 — 한 번 울리고, 두 번 울리고 — 유리잔 악기로 켜는 가장 멋진 가락이지! 댁의 건강을 위하여. 천 년을 사시고, 지금 이 순간 좋은 물건을 가려내는 안목보다 결코 못한 안목을 갖는 일이 없으시길!\"\n\n상사는 다시 잔을 비웠고 또 한 잔을 받을 준비가 단단히 되어 보였다. 나는 펌블추크 씨가 손님 대접에 열을 올리느라 자기가 그 포도주를 선물한 사실은 잊어버린 듯, 조 부인에게서 병을 받아 들고는 흥겨움을 흘려 가며 그것을 돌리는 공을 죄다 차지하는 것을 눈여겨보았다. 나에게도 조금 돌아왔다. 그리고 그는 포도주에 어찌나 후했던지, 첫 병이 비자 다른 병까지 가져오라 청하고는 그것도 똑같이 후하게 돌렸다.\n\n그들이 모두 화덕 둘레에 모여 서서 그토록 즐거워하는 것을 지켜보면서, 나는 습지에 있는 내 도망자 친구가 식사를 위한 얼마나 무시무시한 양념인지를 생각했다. 그가 제공한 흥분으로 그 자리가 환해지기 전까지는, 그들이 그 사분의 일만큼도 즐거워하지 않았던 것이다. 그리고 이제, 그들 모두가 \"두 악당\"이 붙잡히기를 들떠 고대하고 있을 때, 그리고 풀무가 그 도망자들을 위해 으르렁대고, 불이 그들을 위해 활활 타오르고, 연기가 그들을 뒤쫓아 황급히 사라지고, 조가 그들을 위해 쾅 챙 망치질을 하고, 벽에 어린 음침한 그림자들이 모두 불길이 솟았다 잦아들고 시뻘건 불티가 떨어졌다 사그라드는 데 따라 위협하듯 그들을 향해 흔들리는 듯 보일 때, 바깥의 창백한 오후는 가엾이 여기는 내 어린 상상 속에서, 그 가련한 작자들 때문에 창백해진 듯이만 보였다.\n\n마침내 조의 일이 끝났고, 망치 소리와 으르렁대는 소리가 멈췄다. 조는 외투를 걸치면서 용기를 내어, 우리 중 몇몇이 군인들과 함께 내려가 사냥이 어찌 되는지 보자고 제안했다. 펌블추크 씨와 허블 씨는 담배와 부인네들과의 자리를 핑계 삼아 사양했으나, 우프슬 씨는 조가 간다면 자기도 가겠다고 했다. 조는 자기는 괜찮으며, 조 부인이 허락한다면 나도 데려가겠다고 했다. 분명 조 부인이 그 일의 자초지종과 결말을 알고 싶어 하는 호기심이 없었더라면, 우리는 결코 허락을 받지 못했을 것이다. 사정이 그러했기에, 누나는 그저 이렇게 단서를 달았다. \"애 머리가 머스킷에 산산조각 나서 데려올 거면, 그걸 다시 붙여 달라고 나한테는 바라지도 말아요.\"\n\n상사는 부인네들에게 정중히 작별을 고하고, 펌블추크 씨와는 전우와 헤어지듯 작별했다. 다만 마른 형편에서도 그 양반의 미덕을, 무언가 촉촉한 것이 오갈 때만큼이나 온전히 알아주었는지는 의심스럽다. 부하들은 다시 머스킷을 들고 대열을 갖췄다. 우프슬 씨와 조와 나는 뒤쪽에 붙어 있고 습지에 다다른 뒤로는 한마디도 하지 말라는 엄한 명령을 받았다. 우리 모두가 차가운 바깥 공기 속으로 나와 우리의 임무를 향해 꾸준히 나아갈 때, 나는 반역하듯 조에게 속삭였다. \"조, 그자들을 못 찾았으면 좋겠어요.\" 그러자 조가 내게 속삭였다. \"그놈들이 줄행랑을 쳤다면 일 실링이라도 내겠다, 핍.\"\n\n마을에서 함께 나선 어슬렁이는 사람은 아무도 없었으니, 날씨는 춥고 험악했으며, 길은 음산하고, 발 딛기는 나빴고, 어둠이 다가오고 있었으며, 사람들은 집 안에 불을 잘 지펴 놓고 그날을 즐기고 있었던 것이다. 몇몇 얼굴이 환히 빛나는 창가로 황급히 다가와 우리 뒷모습을 바라보았으나, 밖으로 나오는 사람은 없었다. 우리는 이정표를 지나 곧장 교회 묘지로 나아갔다. 거기서 상사의 손짓 신호에 따라 우리는 몇 분 멈춰 섰고, 그동안 부하 두세 명이 무덤들 사이로 흩어져 현관도 살펴보았다. 그들은 아무것도 찾지 못한 채 다시 돌아왔고, 그러고 나서 우리는 교회 묘지 옆 문을 지나 탁 트인 습지로 나아갔다. 여기서 동풍을 타고 매서운 진눈깨비가 후두두 우리에게 들이쳤고, 조가 나를 등에 업었다.\n\n이제 우리는 그 황량한 황야로 나와 있었으니, 그들은 내가 여덟아홉 시간 안에 이곳에 와 있었고 두 사내가 숨은 것을 다 보았다는 사실을 까맣게 몰랐다. 나는 처음으로 크나큰 두려움에 잠겨, 만약 우리가 그자들과 마주친다면, 내 그 특별한 죄수는 군인들을 여기로 데려온 것이 바로 나라고 여기지 않을까 하고 생각했다. 그는 내게 속임수를 쓰는 도깨비냐고 물었었고, 만약 내가 자기를 잡으려는 사냥에 끼어든다면 사나운 어린 사냥개가 될 거라고 했었다. 그는 내가 배신의 진심으로 도깨비이자 사냥개가 되어, 자기를 팔아넘겼다고 믿을까?\n\n이제 와서 내게 이런 질문을 던져 봐야 소용없었다. 나는 조의 등에 업혀 있었고, 그 아래에는 조가 있어 사냥꾼처럼 도랑을 향해 돌진하며, 우프슬 씨에게 그 로마식 코를 처박지 말고 우리를 따라오라고 다그치고 있었다. 군인들은 우리 앞쪽에서 사람과 사람 사이에 간격을 두고 제법 넓은 횡렬을 이루며 뻗어 나가고 있었다. 우리는 내가 처음에 들어섰다가 안개 속에서 벗어났던 바로 그 길을 가고 있었다. 안개가 아직 다시 끼지 않았거나, 아니면 바람이 그것을 흩어 버린 모양이었다. 낮게 붉게 타는 노을 아래, 표지등과 교수대와 포대의 둔덕과 강 건너편 기슭이 또렷이 보였으니, 모두가 물기 어린 납빛이긴 했지만.\n\n대장장이의 망치질처럼 쿵쿵 뛰는 심장을 안고 조의 떡 벌어진 어깨에 매달린 채, 나는 죄수들의 기척이 있을까 사방을 두루 살폈다. 아무것도 보이지 않았고, 아무것도 들리지 않았다. 우프슬 씨가 헐떡이며 거칠게 내쉬는 숨소리에 한 번 이상 크게 놀랐으나, 이때쯤에는 그 소리를 알아차려 추격의 대상과 떼어 생각할 수 있었다. 줄칼 가는 소리가 아직 들린다고 여겼을 때 끔찍이 놀랐으나, 그것은 양의 방울 소리일 뿐이었다. 양들은 풀 뜯기를 멈추고 겁먹은 듯 우리를 바라보았고, 소들은 바람과 진눈깨비를 등진 채 마치 그 두 가지 괴로움을 다 우리 탓으로 여기는 양 성난 눈으로 노려보았다. 그러나 이런 것들과, 풀잎 하나하나에 어린 저무는 하루의 떨림을 빼면, 습지의 황량한 정적을 깨뜨리는 것은 아무것도 없었다.\n\n군인들은 옛 포대 방향으로 나아가고 있었고, 우리는 그들 조금 뒤에서 나아가고 있었는데, 그때 느닷없이 우리 모두가 멈춰 섰다. 비바람의 날개를 타고 길게 외치는 소리가 우리에게 닿았기 때문이다. 그 소리가 되풀이되었다. 동쪽으로 멀리 떨어진 곳에서 났으나 길고 컸다. 아니, 소리의 혼란스러움으로 미루어 판단하자면, 두 사람 이상이 함께 내지르는 외침인 듯했다.\n\n조와 내가 다가갔을 때, 상사와 가장 가까이 있는 부하들이 이런 취지로 낮은 목소리로 말하고 있었다. 잠시 더 귀를 기울인 뒤, 조는 (잘 분간하는 사람이었다) 동의했고, 우프슬 씨도 (서툴게 분간하는 사람이었다) 동의했다. 결단력 있는 사람인 상사는, 그 소리에 응답하지 말되 진로를 바꿔 부하들이 그쪽을 향해 \"구보로\" 나아가라고 명령했다. 그리하여 우리는 오른쪽으로 (동쪽이 있는 곳이었다) 비스듬히 꺾어 들었고, 조가 어찌나 놀랍게 내달렸던지 나는 자리를 지키려고 꽉 붙들어야 했다.\n\n이제는 그야말로 달음박질이었으니, 조가 그동안 내내 입에 올린 단 두 마디로 표현하자면 \"쏜살\" 같은 것이었다. 둑을 내려갔다 오르고, 문을 넘고, 도랑에 첨벙 빠지고, 거친 골풀을 헤치고 — 누구도 자기가 어디로 가는지 따위는 개의치 않았다. 외침에 가까워질수록, 그것이 한 사람 이상의 목소리로 이루어진 것임이 점점 더 분명해졌다. 때로는 아예 멈추는 듯했고, 그러면 군인들도 멈췄다. 그것이 다시 터져 나오면, 군인들은 어느 때보다 더 빠르게 그쪽으로 내달렸고, 우리도 그 뒤를 따랐다. 얼마 후 우리는 그 소리에 바싹 따라붙어, 한 목소리가 \"사람 죽인다!\"라고 외치고 다른 목소리가 \"죄수다! 탈주범이다! 경비병! 탈주한 죄수들은 이쪽이다!\"라고 외치는 것을 들을 수 있었다. 그러더니 두 목소리가 다 몸싸움 속에 묻혀 버리는 듯하다가, 다시 터져 나오곤 했다. 그리고 이 지경에 이르자, 군인들은 사슴처럼 내달렸고, 조 역시 그러했다.\n\n우리가 그 소리를 완전히 따라잡았을 때, 상사가 맨 먼저 뛰어들었고 부하 둘이 바싹 뒤따라 뛰어들었다. 우리가 모두 뛰어들었을 때 그들의 총은 공이가 당겨지고 겨눠져 있었다.\n\n\"두 놈 다 여기 있다!\" 상사가 도랑 바닥에서 몸싸움을 벌이며 헐떡였다. \"항복해, 이 둘! 이 두 마리 들짐승 같은 놈들, 빌어먹을! 떨어져!\"\n\n물이 첨벙거리고, 진흙이 튀고, 욕설이 오가고, 주먹질이 벌어지는 가운데, 군인 몇몇이 더 도랑으로 내려가 상사를 거들었고, 내 죄수와 다른 한 명을 따로따로 끌어냈다. 둘 다 피를 흘리며 헐떡이고 저주하며 발버둥 쳤으나, 물론 나는 둘 다 단번에 알아보았다.\n\n\"잘 봐 둬!\" 내 죄수가 너덜너덜한 소맷자락으로 얼굴의 피를 닦고 손가락에 엉킨 뜯긴 머리카락을 털어 내며 말했다. \"내가 저놈을 잡았어! 내가 저놈을 너희한테 넘겨주는 거야! 그걸 잘 봐 둬!\"\n\n\"그리 따져 들 일은 못 되네.\" 상사가 말했다. \"그래 봤자 자네한테 좋을 거 별로 없어, 이 사람아, 자네도 똑같은 처지인데. 거기 수갑 채워!\"\n\n\"좋을 게 있길 바라지도 않아. 지금 좋은 것보다 더 좋아지길 바라지도 않아.\" 내 죄수가 탐욕스러운 웃음을 지으며 말했다. \"내가 저놈을 잡았어. 저놈도 알아. 나한텐 그걸로 충분해.\"\n\n다른 죄수는 보기에 시퍼렇게 질려 있었고, 얼굴 왼쪽의 묵은 멍에 더해 온몸이 멍들고 찢긴 듯했다. 둘 다 따로따로 수갑이 채워질 때까지 그는 말을 할 숨조차 쉬지 못한 채, 쓰러지지 않으려고 군인 하나에게 기대고 있었다.\n\n\"잘 봐 두시오, 경비병 양반 — 이자가 나를 죽이려 했소.\" 그가 처음으로 내뱉은 말이었다.\n\n\"이자를 죽이려 했다고?\" 내 죄수가 경멸하듯 말했다. \"시도만 하고 못 했다? 나는 저놈을 잡아서 넘겨줬어. 그게 내가 한 일이야. 나는 저놈이 습지에서 빠져나가는 걸 막았을 뿐 아니라, 저놈을 여기까지 끌고 왔어 — 돌아가는 길 이만큼이나 끌고 온 거야. 이 악당이, 이래 봬도 신사라 이 말이지. 자, 나 덕분에 감옥선이 제 신사를 다시 갖게 됐어. 저놈을 죽인다고? 더 고약한 짓을 해서 끌고 돌아올 수 있는데, 굳이 죽이느라 수고할 까닭이 있겠나!\"\n\n다른 한 명은 여전히 헐떡이며 말했다. \"이자가 — 이자가 — 나를 — 죽이려 했소. 증인 — 증인이 되어 주시오.\"\n\n\"이봐, 들어 보라고!\" 내 죄수가 상사에게 말했다. \"나는 혼자 힘으로 감옥선에서 빠져나왔어. 단숨에 내달려 해낸 거야. 이 죽도록 차가운 벌판에서도 마찬가지로 빠져나갈 수 있었어 — 내 다리를 봐, 쇠붙이가 별로 없을걸 — 저놈이 여기 있다는 걸 알아채지만 않았어도 말이야. 저놈을 풀어 줘? 내가 알아낸 수단으로 저놈이 덕을 보게 둬? 저놈이 나를 또다시 도구로 부려먹게 둬? 한 번 더? 안 돼, 안 돼, 안 돼. 내가 저기 바닥에서 죽었더라도,\" 그러면서 그는 수갑 찬 두 손을 도랑 쪽으로 힘차게 휘둘렀다. \"이 손아귀로 저놈을 붙들고 늘어졌을 테니, 당신들은 내 손아귀에서 틀림없이 저놈을 찾아냈을 거야.\"\n\n자기 동료를 극도로 무서워하는 게 분명한 다른 도망자가 되풀이했다. \"이자가 나를 죽이려 했소. 당신들이 오지 않았으면 나는 죽은 목숨이었을 거요.\"\n\n\"거짓말이야!\" 내 죄수가 사납게 기운을 뻗치며 말했다. \"저놈은 타고난 거짓말쟁이고, 거짓말쟁이로 죽을 거야. 저놈 얼굴을 봐. 거기 다 쓰여 있지 않나? 저놈더러 그 눈을 나한테 돌려 보라고 해. 못 할걸, 내 장담하지.\"\n\n다른 한 명은 비웃는 웃음을 지으려 애썼으나, 그것이 입가의 신경질적인 떨림을 어떤 정해진 표정으로 그러모으지는 못한 채, 군인들을 바라보고 습지와 하늘을 두루 둘러보았으나, 분명코 말하는 자는 바라보지 않았다.\n\n\"저놈 보이지?\" 내 죄수가 계속했다. \"저놈이 얼마나 악당인지 보이지? 저 비굴하게 굴며 이리저리 헤매는 눈깔이 보이지? 우리가 함께 재판받을 때도 저랬어. 한 번도 나를 쳐다보지 않았지.\"\n\n다른 한 명은 마른 입술을 자꾸자꾸 움직이고 눈을 멀리 가까이 쉴 새 없이 굴리다가, 마침내 잠깐 그 눈을 말하는 자에게 돌리며, \"당신, 봐줄 만한 꼴은 못 되는군.\" 하고 말하고는, 묶인 손을 반쯤 빈정대는 눈길로 흘끔 보았다. 그 순간 내 죄수는 어찌나 미친 듯이 격분했던지, 군인들이 가로막지 않았더라면 그에게 달려들었을 것이다. \"내가 말하지 않았소,\" 그러자 다른 죄수가 말했다. \"할 수만 있다면 이자가 나를 죽일 거라고?\" 그리고 그가 두려움에 떨고 있다는 것, 그리고 그 입술에 묽은 눈송이 같은 기묘한 흰 거품이 일고 있다는 것을 누구든 알아볼 수 있었다.\n\n\"이 입씨름은 이만하면 됐다.\" 상사가 말했다. \"저 횃불에 불을 붙여라.\"\n\n총 대신 바구니를 든 군인 하나가 그것을 열려고 한쪽 무릎을 꿇었을 때, 내 죄수는 처음으로 주위를 둘러보다가 나를 보았다. 우리가 다가왔을 때 나는 도랑 가장자리에서 조의 등에서 내렸고, 그 후로 움직이지 않고 있었다. 그가 나를 바라볼 때 나는 간절히 그를 바라보았고, 손을 살짝 움직이며 고개를 가로저었다. 나는 내 결백을 그에게 알리려고, 그가 나를 봐 주기를 기다리고 있었던 것이다. 그가 내 의도를 이해하기라도 했는지는 전혀 드러나지 않았으니, 그가 내게 보낸 눈길을 나는 알아들을 수 없었고, 그 모든 것이 한순간에 지나가 버렸다. 그러나 설령 그가 한 시간이나 하루를 나를 바라보았더라도, 나는 그 후로 그 얼굴이 그보다 더 골똘했던 것으로 기억할 수는 없었을 것이다.\n\n바구니를 든 군인은 이내 불을 얻어 횃불 서너 개에 불을 붙였고, 자기가 하나를 들고 나머지를 나눠 주었다. 아까도 거의 어두웠으나, 이제는 완전히 어두워 보였고, 곧이어 몹시 어두워졌다. 우리가 그 자리를 떠나기 전에, 군인 넷이 둥글게 서서 허공에 두 번 발포했다. 이윽고 우리 뒤로 얼마쯤 떨어진 곳에서 다른 횃불들이 켜지는 것이, 그리고 강 건너편 기슭 습지에서 또 다른 횃불들이 켜지는 것이 보였다. \"좋아.\" 상사가 말했다. \"행군.\"\n\n얼마 가지 않아 우리 앞쪽에서 대포가 세 발 발사되었는데, 그 소리가 내 귓속의 무언가를 터뜨리는 듯했다. \"배에서 자네를 기다리고 있네.\" 상사가 내 죄수에게 말했다. \"자네가 온다는 걸 알고 있지. 처지지 말게, 이 사람아. 여기 바싹 붙어.\"\n\n두 사람은 떨어뜨려져, 각자 별도의 경비병에게 둘러싸인 채 걸었다. 나는 이제 조의 손을 잡고 있었고, 조는 횃불 하나를 들고 있었다. 우프슬 씨는 돌아가려 했으나 조는 끝까지 지켜보기로 마음먹은 터라, 우리는 일행과 함께 계속 나아갔다. 이제는 제법 괜찮은 길이 나 있었는데, 대개는 강가를 따라 났고, 도랑이 나오는 곳마다 여기저기 갈래가 갈렸으며, 그 도랑에는 자그마한 풍차와 진흙투성이 수문이 달려 있었다. 돌아보니 우리 뒤로 다른 불빛들이 따라 들어오는 것이 보였다. 우리가 든 횃불들은 길 위로 커다란 불 얼룩을 떨어뜨렸고, 그것들이 연기를 내며 타닥타닥 타고 있는 것도 보였다. 그 밖에는 시커먼 어둠뿐 아무것도 보이지 않았다. 우리의 불빛은 송진 같은 불길로 주위 공기를 데웠고, 두 죄수는 머스킷들 한가운데서 절뚝거리며 가면서도 그것을 오히려 좋아하는 듯했다. 그들이 절뚝거리는 탓에 우리는 빨리 갈 수 없었고, 그들이 너무 지친 나머지 두세 번은 그들이 쉬는 동안 멈춰 서야 했다.\n\n이렇게 한 시간쯤 걸은 끝에, 우리는 허름한 나무 오두막과 선착장에 이르렀다. 오두막에는 경비병이 있어 수하를 물었고, 상사가 답했다. 그러고 나서 우리는 오두막 안으로 들어갔는데, 거기에는 담배와 회칠 냄새가 났고, 환한 불과 등잔, 머스킷 걸이, 북 하나, 그리고 기계 장치 없는 거대한 빨래 짜는 롤러 같은 낮은 나무 침상이 있었으니, 한 번에 군인 열두어 명은 너끈히 누일 만했다. 외투를 걸친 채 그 위에 누워 있던 군인 서넛은 우리에게 별 관심이 없었고, 그저 고개를 들어 졸린 눈으로 멍하니 쳐다보고는 다시 누웠다. 상사는 일종의 보고를 하고 장부에 무언가를 적었으며, 그러고 나서 내가 다른 죄수라 부르는 그자가 먼저 배에 오르도록 경비병과 함께 끌려 나갔다.\n\n내 죄수는 그 한 번을 빼고는 결코 나를 보지 않았다. 우리가 오두막 안에 서 있는 동안, 그는 불 앞에 서서 골똘히 그것을 바라보거나, 두 발을 번갈아 화덕 선반에 올려놓고는 마치 최근의 모험을 겪은 그 발들을 가엾이 여기듯 골똘히 내려다보았다. 그러다 갑자기 그가 상사에게 돌아서서 말했다.\n\n\"이번 탈주에 관해 한마디 하고 싶소. 어떤 사람들이 나랑 같이 의심받는 걸 막을 수 있을지도 모르니.\"\n\n\"하고 싶은 말은 해도 좋네.\" 상사가 팔짱을 낀 채 차갑게 그를 바라보며 대답했다. \"하지만 여기서 할 까닭은 없어. 일이 다 끝나기 전에 그것에 관해 말할 기회도, 들을 기회도 자네한테 충분히 있을 테니, 알겠나.\"\n\n\"알겠소, 하지만 이건 또 다른 문제요, 별개의 일이오. 사람이 굶을 수야 없잖소. 적어도 나는 못 굶소. 나는 저기 저 너머 마을에서 — 교회가 거의 습지로 튀어나와 서 있는 그 마을에서 — 먹을 것을 좀 가져왔소.\"\n\n\"훔쳤다는 말이군.\" 상사가 말했다.\n\n\"어디서 가져왔는지도 말해 주리다. 대장장이 집에서요.\"\n\n\"이런!\" 상사가 조를 빤히 바라보며 말했다.\n\n\"이런, 핍!\" 조가 나를 빤히 바라보며 말했다.\n\n\"먹다 남은 음식 부스러기였소 — 그게 다요 — 그리고 술 한 모금하고, 파이 하나요.\"\n\n\"혹시 파이 같은 물건이 없어진 일이 있었소, 대장장이 양반?\" 상사가 은근하게 물었다.\n\n\"내 아내가 그랬소, 바로 당신이 들어오던 그 순간에. 너도 알지, 핍?\"\n\n\"그래서,\" 내 죄수가 침울한 태도로, 나는 조금도 거들떠보지 않은 채 조에게 눈길을 돌리며 말했다. \"그래서 당신이 그 대장장이로군, 그렇소? 그렇다면 안된 말이지만, 내가 당신 파이를 먹어 치웠소.\"\n\n\"맘껏 드셨대도 좋소 — 그게 내 것이었던 한에서는 말이오.\" 조가 대답했는데, 그 속에는 조 부인을 떠올린 조심스러움이 깃들어 있었다. \"당신이 무슨 짓을 했는지야 우리가 모르지만, 그렇다고 당신이 그 때문에 굶어 죽기를 바라지는 않소, 가엾고 비참한 동료 인간 양반. — 안 그러냐, 핍?\"\n\n앞서 내가 알아챘던 그 무언가가 사내의 목구멍에서 다시 딸깍 소리를 냈고, 그는 등을 돌렸다. 보트가 돌아와 있었고 그의 경비병도 준비가 되어 있어, 우리는 거친 말뚝과 돌로 만든 선착장까지 그를 따라가, 그가 보트에 태워지는 것을 지켜보았다. 보트는 그와 같은 죄수들로 이루어진 일행이 노를 젓고 있었다. 그를 보고 놀라거나, 그를 보는 데 흥미를 느끼거나, 그를 보고 반가워하거나 안타까워하거나, 한마디라도 건네는 사람은 아무도 없는 듯했다. 다만 보트 안의 누군가가 개에게 하듯 으르렁댔다. \"노 저어, 이놈들아!\" 그것이 노를 담그라는 신호였다. 횃불 빛으로, 우리는 시커먼 감옥선이 못된 노아의 방주처럼 기슭의 진흙에서 조금 떨어져 떠 있는 것을 보았다. 좁은 우리에 갇히고 빗장이 질리고 육중한 녹슨 쇠사슬에 매여 있는 그 감옥선은, 내 어린 눈에는 마치 죄수들처럼 쇠고랑이 채워진 듯 보였다. 우리는 보트가 그 옆에 가 닿는 것을 보았고, 그가 뱃전 위로 끌어 올려져 사라지는 것을 보았다. 그러고 나서 횃불 끄트머리들이 쉭쉭 소리를 내며 물속으로 내던져져 꺼졌으니, 마치 그에 관한 모든 것이 다 끝나 버린 듯했다.\n\n# Chapter VI.\n\n그토록 뜻밖에 혐의를 벗게 된 그 좀도둑질에 대한 내 마음 상태는, 나로 하여금 솔직히 털어놓도록 떠밀지는 못했다. 그러나 그 밑바닥에는 얼마간 선한 찌꺼기가 남아 있었기를 바란다.\n\n발각될 두려움이 내게서 걷히고 나서, 누나에 대해 양심의 가책 같은 것을 느꼈던 기억은 없다. 그러나 나는 조를 사랑했다. 어쩌면 그 어린 시절에는, 그 다정한 사람이 내가 자기를 사랑하도록 내버려 두었다는 것 말고는 더 나은 이유가 없었는지도 모른다. 그리고 그를 두고 말하자면, 내 속마음은 그리 쉽사리 가라앉지 않았다. 조에게 모든 진실을 털어놓아야 한다는 생각이 줄곧 마음을 짓눌렀다(특히 그가 자기 줄칼을 두리번거리며 찾는 모습을 처음 보았을 때 그러했다). 그런데도 나는 그러지 않았으니, 만일 그렇게 한다면 그가 나를 실제보다 더 나쁜 아이로 여길까 봐 미덥지 못했던 까닭이었다. 조의 신뢰를 잃을지도 모른다는 두려움, 그래서 그날 이후로 밤마다 굴뚝 옆 구석에 앉아 영영 잃어버린 내 동무이자 벗을 음울하게 바라보게 될지도 모른다는 두려움이 내 혀를 묶어 버렸다. 나는 병적으로 이렇게 상상했다. 만일 조가 그 일을 알게 된다면, 그 뒤로는 그가 난롯가에서 자기 금빛 구레나룻을 매만지는 모습을 볼 때마다, 그가 그 일을 곱씹고 있다는 생각을 하지 않고는 못 배기리라고. 만일 조가 그 일을 알게 된다면, 어제 먹던 고기나 푸딩이 오늘 식탁에 다시 올라올 때 그가 아무리 무심코 그것을 흘끗 본다 해도, 그가 내가 식료품 저장실에 들어갔던 건 아닌지 헤아리고 있다는 생각을 하지 않고는 그 모습을 볼 수 없으리라고. 만일 조가 그 일을 알게 되어, 그 뒤 우리 둘의 살림살이 어느 때든 자기 맥주가 김이 빠졌다거나 텁텁하다고 말한다면, 그가 거기 타르가 들었으리라 의심한다는 확신에 내 얼굴로 피가 확 몰려올 것이라고. 한마디로, 나는 옳다고 아는 일을 하기에는 너무 겁이 많았으니, 그릇되다고 아는 일을 피하기에도 그만큼 겁이 많았던 것과 마찬가지였다. 그 무렵 나는 세상과 아무런 교류가 없었고, 이런 식으로 행동하는 세상의 그 숱한 사람들을 흉내 낸 것도 아니었다. 전혀 배운 바 없는 천재처럼, 나는 그 행동 방식을 스스로 발견해 낸 것이었다.\n\n감옥선에서 그리 멀어지기도 전에 나는 졸음이 쏟아졌으므로, 조는 다시 나를 등에 업고 집으로 데려갔다. 그에게는 꽤 고된 여정이었을 것이다. 워프슬 씨가 녹초가 되어 어찌나 심사가 사나웠던지, 교회 문이 활짝 열려 있었더라면 그는 아마 이 원정대 전부를, 조와 나부터 시작해서 죄다 파문해 버렸을 테니까. 평신도의 처지로서도 그는 그토록 미친 듯이 기어이 축축한 땅바닥에 주저앉아 댔으므로, 부엌 불에 말리려고 그의 외투를 벗겼을 때 그의 바지에 남은 정황 증거만으로도, 만일 그것이 사형에 해당하는 죄였다면 그를 교수형에 처하기에 충분했을 것이다.\n\n그 무렵 나는 부엌 바닥에서 작은 술꾼처럼 비틀거리고 있었으니, 갓 다시 두 발로 세워진 데다, 깊이 잠들어 있다가, 열기와 불빛과 떠들썩한 말소리 속에서 깨어났던 까닭이었다. (어깨뼈 사이로 떨어진 묵직한 한 대와, \"야! 세상에 이런 애가 또 있을까!\" 하는 누나의 정신 번쩍 들게 하는 외침의 도움으로) 정신을 차리고 보니, 조는 사람들에게 죄수의 자백 이야기를 들려주고 있었고, 손님들은 저마다 그가 어떻게 식료품 저장실에 들어갔을지 다른 방법들을 내놓고 있었다. 펌블추크 씨는 현장을 꼼꼼히 살펴본 끝에, 그가 먼저 대장간 지붕에 올라갔다가, 다음에 집 지붕으로 올라갔다가, 그런 다음 자기 침구를 가닥가닥 잘라 만든 밧줄을 타고 부엌 굴뚝으로 내려왔다는 결론을 끌어냈다. 그리고 펌블추크 씨는 매우 단정적인 사람인 데다 자기 이륜마차를 모는 사람이었으므로 — 모두를 깔아뭉개며 — 틀림없이 그러했으리라는 데 의견이 모였다. 사실 워프슬 씨는 지친 사람 특유의 힘없는 악의로 \"아니오!\" 하고 거칠게 외치기는 했으나, 그에게는 아무런 이론도 없고 외투도 걸치지 않은 터라, 만장일치로 무시당하고 말았다. 게다가 그가 축축한 기운을 빼내려고 부엌 불에 등을 대고 서서 뒤쪽으로 모락모락 김을 피워 올리고 있었다는 점은 말할 것도 없으니, 그 꼴은 신뢰를 자아낼 만한 것이 못 되었다.\n\n그날 밤 내가 들은 것은 이것이 전부였으니, 그 뒤로는 누나가 좌중의 눈에 거슬리는 졸린 골칫거리라며 나를 움켜쥐고는, 어찌나 억센 손으로 거들어 잠자리로 올려 보냈던지, 마치 내가 장화를 쉰 켤레쯤 신고서 그것을 죄다 계단 모서리마다 부딪치며 대롱대롱 매달려 가는 것만 같았다. 앞서 적은 내 마음 상태는 아침에 자리에서 일어나기도 전에 시작되어, 그 화제가 사그라들고 특별한 경우가 아니고서는 더 이상 입에 오르내리지 않게 된 뒤로도 오래도록 이어졌다.\n\n# Chapter VII.\n\n내가 교회 묘지에 서서 집안의 묘비들을 읽던 무렵, 나는 겨우 그 글자들을 떠듬떠듬 풀어낼 만큼의 학식밖에 없었다. 그 단순한 뜻조차 나는 제대로 새기지 못했으니, \"위 사람의 아내\"라는 구절을 두고 나는 아버지가 더 나은 세상으로 드높여졌음을 기리는 찬사로 읽었던 것이다. 그리고 만약 죽은 친척 가운데 누군가가 \"아래 사람\"이라 일컬어져 있었다면, 나는 분명 그 식구에 대해 더없이 나쁜 생각을 품었으리라. 교리문답이 나를 묶어 둔 신학적 입장에 대한 내 관념도 도무지 정확하지 않았다. 내가 \"평생토록 그 안에서 걷겠나이다\"라고 선언한 것이, 우리 집에서 마을을 지날 때 늘 어느 한 방향으로만 다녀야 하며 수레바퀴 장인의 집 쪽으로 꺾어 내려가거나 방앗간 쪽으로 올라가 그 길을 바꾸어서는 안 된다는 의무를 내게 지웠다고 여겼던 것을, 나는 생생히 기억하고 있으니 말이다.\n\n나이가 차면 나는 조의 도제가 될 예정이었고, 그 지위를 떠맡을 수 있을 때까지는 조 부인이 \"폼피\"라고 부르는, 다시 말해(내 식으로 옮기자면) 응석받이로 자라서는 안 되었다. 그래서 나는 대장간의 잡일꾼 노릇을 했을 뿐 아니라, 이웃 가운데 누군가가 새를 쫓거나 돌을 줍거나 그 비슷한 일을 시킬 아이가 따로 필요하다 싶으면 그 일자리를 내가 받는 영광을 누렸다. 그러나 그로 인해 우리의 우월한 위신이 손상되지 않도록, 부엌 벽난로 선반 위에는 돈통이 놓여 있었고, 내가 번 돈은 모조리 그 안에 들어간다는 사실이 공공연히 알려져 있었다. 그 돈이 결국 국가 부채를 갚는 데 보태질 거라는 인상을 나는 받았으나, 그 보물에 내가 몸소 한몫 끼리라는 희망은 조금도 없었음을 나는 안다.\n\n웝슬 씨의 대고모는 마을에서 야간 학교를 운영했다. 다시 말해, 그분은 재산은 한정되어 있고 노쇠함은 한이 없는 우스꽝스러운 노파였는데, 저녁 여섯 시부터 일곱 시까지 잠이 들곤 했으며, 그 잠드는 광경을 지켜보는 유익한 기회를 위해 일주일에 이 펜스씩 내는 젊은이들이 그 곁을 지켰다. 그분은 작은 오두막을 빌려 살았고, 웝슬 씨는 위층 방을 썼는데, 우리 학생들은 그가 더없이 위엄 있고 무시무시한 어조로 소리 내어 책 읽는 소리를, 그리고 이따금 천장을 쿵쿵 울리는 소리를 엿듣곤 했다. 웝슬 씨가 분기마다 한 번씩 학생들을 \"시험한다\"는 허구가 있었다. 그런 자리에서 그가 실제로 한 일이란 소맷부리를 걷어 올리고, 머리카락을 곧추세우고, 카이사르의 주검 앞에서 마르쿠스 안토니우스가 한 연설을 우리에게 들려주는 것이었다. 이어서 늘 콜린스의 「정념에 부치는 송시」가 따라 나왔는데, 거기서 나는 특히 복수의 신으로 분한 웝슬 씨를, 피로 물든 칼을 천둥 같은 소리로 내던지고 시들게 하는 듯한 눈길로 전쟁을 알리는 나팔을 집어 드는 그 모습을 우러러보았다. 그때의 나는 훗날의 나와는 달랐다. 훗날 나는 정념들의 무리 속에 떨어져 그것들을 콜린스와 웝슬과 견주어 보게 되었고, 그 비교는 두 신사 모두에게 불리하게 작용했다.\n\n웝슬 씨의 대고모는 이 교육 기관을 운영하는 한편, 같은 방에서 작은 잡화점도 했다. 그분은 자기 가게에 어떤 물건이 있는지, 그 값이 얼마인지 전혀 알지 못했다. 그러나 서랍 속에는 기름때 묻은 작은 비망록이 들어 있어 가격표 구실을 했고, 비디는 이 신탁에 따라 가게의 모든 거래를 처리했다. 비디는 웝슬 씨의 대고모의 손녀였다. 솔직히 고백하자면 그가 웝슬 씨와 어떤 관계인가 하는 문제를 푸는 일은 내 능력 밖이었다. 그 애는 나처럼 고아였고, 또한 나처럼 손으로 길러진 아이였다. 내 생각에 그 애의 가장 두드러진 점은 손발의 끄트머리였다. 머리는 늘 빗질이 필요했고, 손은 늘 씻을 필요가 있었으며, 신발은 늘 수선하고 뒤꿈치를 추켜올릴 필요가 있었으니 말이다. 다만 이 설명은 평일에 한정해 받아들여야 한다. 일요일이면 그 애는 한껏 차려입고 교회에 갔다.\n\n남의 도움 없이 나 혼자서, 그리고 웝슬 씨의 대고모보다는 비디의 도움을 더 많이 받아, 나는 알파벳을 마치 가시덤불인 양 헤치고 나아갔다. 글자 하나하나에 어지간히 시달리고 긁히면서. 그러고 나서 나는 아홉 개의 숫자라는 도둑들 사이에 떨어졌는데, 그것들은 저녁마다 변장을 새로 꾸며 알아보지 못하게 하려고 무언가 새로운 짓을 하는 것만 같았다. 그러나 마침내 나는 반쯤 눈먼 채 더듬거리는 식으로나마, 지극히 작은 규모로 읽고 쓰고 셈하기 시작했다.\n\n어느 날 밤 나는 굴뚝 옆 구석에 석판을 들고 앉아, 조에게 보낼 편지 한 통을 만들어 내는 데 엄청난 공을 들이고 있었다. 그것은 우리가 습지에서 사냥을 벌인 지 꼬박 일 년은 지난 뒤의 일이었을 것이다. 그로부터 한참 지난 시기였고, 겨울이었으며, 모진 서리가 내려 있었으니까. 발치의 화롯가에 참고용 알파벳을 펴 놓고, 나는 한두 시간 만에 다음과 같은 서한을 찍어 내고 문질러 가며 완성했다.\n\n\"내사랑하넌조에게 잘디내시기바라요 나는곳 조 당신을 가르칠수 인게되기바라요 그러면 우리는 무척 기쁠꺼에요 그리고내가조당신의도제가되면 얼마나 신날까요 나를미더주세요 친애하넌 핍.\"\n\n조에게 편지로 소식을 전해야 할 절실한 필요는 전혀 없었다. 그가 내 곁에 앉아 있었고 우리 둘뿐이었으니 말이다. 그러나 나는 이 글로 쓴 통신문을(석판째로) 내 손으로 직접 건넸고, 조는 그것을 박학의 기적인 양 받아 들었다.\n\n\"이봐, 핍, 친구!\" 조가 푸른 눈을 휘둥그레 뜨며 외쳤다. \"넌 정말 대단한 학자로구나! 안 그러냐?\"\n\n\"그렇게 되고 싶어요.\" 나는 조가 들고 있는 석판을 흘끗 보며 말했다. 글씨가 영 울퉁불퉁하다는 불안한 마음을 품은 채.\n\n\"이런, 여기 J가 있구나.\" 조가 말했다. \"그리고 무엇과도 견줄 만한 O가 있어! 여기 J가 있고 O가 있다, 핍, 그래서 J-O, 조구나.\"\n\n나는 조가 이 한 음절보다 더 길게 소리 내어 읽는 것을 들어 본 적이 없었고, 지난 일요일 교회에서 내가 우연히 기도서를 거꾸로 들고 있었을 때, 그것이 똑바로 놓여 있을 때나 다름없이 그에게 편해 보인다는 사실을 눈치챘다. 조를 가르칠 때 정말 맨 처음부터 시작해야 하는지를 알아낼 이번 기회를 잡고 싶어, 나는 말했다. \"아! 그럼 나머지도 읽어 보세요, 조.\"\n\n\"나머지라, 응, 핍?\" 조가 느릿느릿 살피는 눈으로 그것을 들여다보며 말했다. \"하나, 둘, 셋. 이런, 여기 J가 셋, O가 셋, 그리고 J-O 조가 셋이나 들어 있구나, 핍!\"\n\n나는 조에게 몸을 기울이고, 집게손가락의 도움을 받아 편지 전체를 그에게 읽어 주었다.\n\n\"놀랍구나!\" 내가 다 읽자 조가 말했다. \"넌 정말 학자야.\"\n\n\"가저리는 어떻게 쓰는 거예요, 조?\" 나는 짐짓 너그러운 척하며 물었다.\n\n\"난 그걸 아예 못 쓴다.\" 조가 말했다.\n\n\"하지만 쓴다고 치면요?\"\n\n\"그건 칠 수가 없는 일이야.\" 조가 말했다. \"하기야 나도 책 읽기를 유별나게 좋아하긴 하지만 말이다.\"\n\n\"그래요, 조?\"\n\n\"유별나게 좋아한다니까. 나한테 좋은 책이나 좋은 신문 한 부를 주고,\" 조가 말했다. \"좋은 불 앞에 앉혀 놓으면, 더 바랄 게 없어. 이런!\" 그가 무릎을 좀 문지르고 나서 말을 이었다. \"정말로 J와 O를 만나서, '자, 드디어 J-O 조가 나왔구나' 하고 말하게 되면, 읽기란 얼마나 흥미로운 일이냐!\"\n\n이로부터 나는 조의 학식이 증기 기관처럼 아직 걸음마 단계에 있다는 것을 알아챘다. 그 주제를 더 파고들어, 나는 물었다.\n\n\"조는 나만큼 어렸을 때 학교에 다닌 적이 한 번도 없어요, 조?\"\n\n\"없다, 핍.\"\n\n\"왜 나만큼 어렸을 때 학교에 한 번도 안 다녔어요, 조?\"\n\n\"그게 말이다, 핍.\" 조가 부지깽이를 집어 들며, 생각에 잠길 때면 으레 하던 버릇대로 아래쪽 살 사이의 불을 천천히 긁어 헤치는 일에 자세를 잡고 말했다. \"이야기해 주마. 우리 아버지 말이다, 핍, 그분은 술에 빠져 사셨는데, 술기운에 휩싸이면 우리 어머니를 사정없이 두들겨 패셨지. 사실 그분이 두들긴 거라곤 그게 거의 다였어, 나만 빼면 말이다. 그리고 나를 두들길 때면 그 기세가 어찌나 사납던지, 모루를 두들기지 않을 때의 그 기세에나 견줄 만했지. 듣고 또 알아듣고 있냐, 핍?\"\n\n\"네, 조.\"\n\n\"그러다 보니 우리 어머니와 나는 아버지한테서 여러 번 도망쳤단다. 그러면 어머니는 일을 나가시면서 이렇게 말씀하셨지. '조,' 하시고는, '얘야, 이제 하느님이 도우시면 너도 학교 교육을 받게 될 거다,' 하시면서 나를 학교에 넣어 주셨어. 그런데 우리 아버지는 마음씨가 어찌나 착하셨던지 우리 없이는 견디지를 못하셨단다. 그래서 엄청난 무리를 끌고 와서는, 우리가 사는 집 문 앞에서 어찌나 소동을 피우던지, 그 집 사람들은 우리와 더는 엮이지 않으려고 우리를 그분한테 넘겨주는 수밖에 없었지. 그러면 그분은 우리를 집으로 데려가 두들겨 팼어. 그게 말이다, 핍,\" 조가 생각에 잠겨 불을 긁던 손을 멈추고 나를 바라보며 말했다. \"내 배움에는 걸림돌이 됐지.\"\n\n\"정말 그렇겠어요, 가엾은 조!\"\n\n\"그래도 말이다, 핍.\" 조가 부지깽이로 위쪽 살을 한두 번 판단하듯 두드리며 말했다. \"모든 사람에게 마땅한 몫을 돌리고, 사람과 사람 사이에 공평한 정의를 지킨다는 점에서 보면, 우리 아버지는 마음씨가 그렇게 착하셨단다, 안 그러냐?\"\n\n나는 그렇게 보이지 않았다. 그러나 그렇게 말하지는 않았다.\n\n\"하지만!\" 조가 말을 이었다. \"누군가는 솥을 계속 끓여야 하는 법이지, 핍, 안 그러면 솥이 끓지를 않거든, 모르겠냐?\"\n\n나는 그건 알겠어서, 그렇다고 말했다.\n\n\"그러다 보니 우리 아버지는 내가 일하러 가는 데는 반대하지 않으셨어. 그래서 나는 지금의 이 일을 하러 나갔지. 아버지가 따랐다면 그분의 일이기도 했을 테고. 그리고 나는 제법 열심히 일했단다, 정말이야, 핍. 시간이 지나 나는 아버지를 부양할 수 있게 됐고, 그분이 시뻘건 얼굴로 발작을 일으켜 돌아가실 때까지 모셨지. 그리고 나는 그분 묘비에 이렇게 새겨 드릴 작정이었어. 그분 쪽의 허물이 무엇이었든, 독자여 기억하라 그분은 마음씨가 그렇게 착하셨음을.\"\n\n조가 이 대구를 어찌나 뚜렷한 자부심과 조심스러운 또렷함으로 읊었던지, 나는 그것을 그가 직접 지었느냐고 물었다.\n\n\"내가 지었지.\" 조가 말했다. \"내 손으로 직접. 한순간에 지어냈어. 마치 단번에 말편자 하나를 완전하게 두들겨 내는 것 같았지. 평생 그렇게 놀란 적이 없었단다. 내 머리를 믿을 수가 없었어. 사실대로 말하면, 그게 정말 내 머리인지조차 거의 믿기지가 않았지. 아까 말했듯이, 핍, 나는 그걸 그분 위에 새겨 드릴 작정이었어. 하지만 시는 돈이 들거든, 크게 새기든 작게 새기든 말이다, 그래서 못 했어. 운구꾼들 비용은 말할 것도 없고, 아낄 수 있는 돈은 죄다 우리 어머니한테 필요했으니까. 어머니는 건강이 좋지 못하셨고, 완전히 쇠약해지셨어. 가엾은 양반, 어머니도 오래 가지 않아 뒤따라 가셨고, 마침내 그분 몫의 평안이 찾아왔단다.\"\n\n조의 푸른 눈이 약간 촉촉해졌다. 그는 부지깽이 끝의 둥근 손잡이로, 한쪽 눈을 먼저, 그러고 나서 다른 쪽 눈을 더없이 어울리지 않고 불편한 모양새로 문질렀다.\n\n\"그러고 나니 그때는 좀 적적했지.\" 조가 말했다. \"여기서 혼자 사는 게 말이다. 그러다 네 누나와 알게 됐단다. 자, 핍,\" 조는 내가 자기 말에 동의하지 않으리라는 걸 안다는 듯 나를 똑바로 바라보았다. \"네 누나는 풍채가 훌륭한 여자야.\"\n\n나는 분명히 의심스러운 표정으로 불을 바라보지 않을 수 없었다.\n\n\"그 일에 대해 집안의 의견이 어떻든, 세상의 의견이 어떻든 간에, 핍, 네 누나는 말이다,\" 조는 다음 한 마디 한 마디마다 부지깽이로 위쪽 살을 두드렸다. \"풍채가-훌륭한-여자야!\"\n\n나는 \"그렇게 생각하신다니 다행이에요, 조\"라는 말보다 더 나은 말을 떠올릴 수가 없었다.\n\n\"나도 그렇다.\" 조가 내 말을 받아 응수했다. \"나도 그렇게 생각해서 다행이야, 핍. 여기저기 좀 불그스레하거나 좀 뼈가 두드러지는 게 있다 한들, 그게 나한테 무슨 대수겠냐?\"\n\n나는 약삭빠르게, 그게 그에게 대수가 아니라면 대체 누구에게 대수겠느냐고 짚어 말했다.\n\n\"그렇고말고!\" 조가 맞장구쳤다. \"바로 그거야. 네 말이 옳다, 친구! 내가 네 누나와 알게 됐을 때, 누나가 너를 손으로 길러 키운다는 게 사람들 입에 오르내리고 있었어. 사람들은 누나가 참 친절하다고 입을 모았고, 나도 사람들과 함께 그렇게 말했지. 너로 말하자면,\" 조가 정말이지 아주 끔찍한 무언가를 보는 듯한 표정으로 말을 이었다. \"네가 얼마나 작고 물렁물렁하고 보잘것없었는지 알았더라면, 이런, 너는 스스로에 대해 더없이 한심한 생각을 품었을 거다!\"\n\n이 말이 썩 달갑지 않아, 나는 말했다. \"저 신경 쓰지 마세요, 조.\"\n\n\"하지만 난 너를 신경 썼단다, 핍.\" 그가 다정하고 꾸밈없이 응수했다. \"내가 네 누나한테 함께 지내자고, 그리고 누나가 대장간으로 올 마음이 생겨 준비되었을 때 교회에서 혼인 예고를 하자고 청하면서, 나는 누나한테 이렇게 말했어. '그 가엾은 어린 녀석도 데려와요. 그 가엾은 어린 녀석한테 하느님의 축복이 있기를,' 나는 네 누나한테 그렇게 말했지. '대장간에는 그 녀석이 들어올 자리가 있어요!'\"\n\n나는 울음을 터뜨리며 용서를 빌었고, 조의 목을 끌어안았다. 그는 나를 끌어안으려고 부지깽이를 떨어뜨리며 말했다. \"우리는 언제까지나 둘도 없는 친구지, 안 그러냐, 핍? 울지 마라, 친구!\"\n\n이 짧은 막간이 끝나자, 조가 말을 이었다.\n\n\"그러니까 말이다, 핍, 이렇게 우리가 함께 있는 거다! 결국 그렇게 된 셈이지. 이렇게 함께 있는 거야! 자, 네가 내 배움을 맡아 가르칠 때 말이다, 핍(미리 말해 두지만 나는 지독하게 둔하단다, 정말이지 끔찍하게 둔해), 조 부인이 우리가 하는 짓을 너무 많이 봐서는 안 돼. 그건, 말하자면, 몰래 해야 하는 거야. 그런데 왜 몰래 하느냐고? 그 까닭을 말해 주마, 핍.\"\n\n그는 다시 부지깽이를 집어 들었다. 그것이 없었다면 그가 자기 설명을 이어 갈 수 있었을지 의심스럽다.\n\n\"네 누나는 통치에 빠져 있단다.\"\n\n\"통치에 빠져 있다고요, 조?\" 나는 흠칫 놀랐다. 조가 해군성이나 재무성의 높은 분들에게 누나를 넘겨 이혼해 버렸다는 어렴풋한 생각을(그리고 유감스럽게도 덧붙이자면, 그런 희망을) 품었던 것이다.\n\n\"통치에 빠져 있어.\" 조가 말했다. \"내 말은 너와 나를 통치하는 일에 빠져 있다는 거다.\"\n\n\"아!\"\n\n\"그리고 누나는 집 안에 학자가 있는 걸 그리 달가워하지 않아.\" 조가 말을 이었다. \"그중에서도 특히 내가 학자가 되는 걸 그리 달가워하지 않을 거야, 내가 들고일어날까 봐 겁이 나서 말이지. 일종의 반역자처럼 말이다, 알겠냐?\"\n\n나는 질문을 던져 되받아치려고 \"왜─\"까지 말을 꺼냈는데, 조가 나를 막았다.\n\n\"잠깐만. 네가 무슨 말을 하려는지 안다, 핍. 잠깐만! 네 누나가 이따금 우리한테 황제 노릇을 한다는 걸 부정하진 않아. 누나가 우리를 메다꽂는다는 것도, 우리한테 호되게 내리덮친다는 것도 부정하지 않는다. 네 누나가 길길이 날뛸 때면 말이다, 핍,\" 조가 목소리를 속삭임으로 낮추며 문 쪽을 흘끗 보았다. \"솔직히 인정하지 않을 수 없는데, 누나는 정말 무서운 사람이야.\"\n\n조는 이 말을 마치 적어도 열두 개의 대문자로 시작하기라도 하는 듯이 발음했다.\n\n\"내가 왜 들고일어나지 않느냐고? 그게 내가 말을 끊었을 때 네가 짚으려던 거지, 핍?\"\n\n\"네, 조.\"\n\n\"그게 말이다,\" 조가 부지깽이를 왼손으로 옮겨 쥐며 말했다. 한쪽 구레나룻을 매만지려는 것이었는데, 그가 그 평온한 일에 손을 댈 때면 나는 그에게서 아무런 희망도 가질 수 없었다. \"네 누나는 비상한 머리를 가졌어. 비상한 머리지.\"\n\n\"그게 뭐예요?\" 나는 그를 막다른 곳으로 몰아넣을까 하는 얼마간의 희망을 품고 물었다. 그러나 조는 내 예상보다 그 정의를 더 빨리 내놓았고, 빙빙 도는 논법으로 나를 완전히 가로막은 채, 한곳을 응시하는 눈빛으로 대답했다. \"네 누나지.\"\n\n\"그리고 나는 비상한 머리가 아니야.\" 응시하던 눈길을 풀고 다시 구레나룻으로 돌아온 조가 말을 이었다. \"그리고 마지막으로 말이다, 핍, 이건 너한테 아주 진지하게 하고 싶은 말인데, 친구야, 나는 우리 가엾은 어머니한테서 너무 많은 걸 보았단다. 한 여자가 죽도록 일하고 종처럼 고생하며 정직한 마음을 짓이기면서도 이승에 사는 동안 한 번도 평안을 누리지 못하는 걸 말이다. 그래서 나는 여자한테 마땅한 도리를 하지 않는 쪽으로 잘못되는 게 죽도록 무섭단다. 둘 중에 하나를 고르라면, 나는 차라리 반대쪽으로 잘못되어 나 자신이 좀 불편을 겪는 쪽이 훨씬 나아. 곤란을 겪는 게 나 하나뿐이었으면 좋겠다, 핍. 너한테 회초리가 없었으면 좋겠어, 친구. 내가 그걸 다 떠맡을 수 있으면 좋겠구나. 하지만 이게 그 일의 위아래와 곧은 사정 전부란다, 핍. 그러니 부족한 점은 네가 너그러이 봐주기를 바란다.\"\n\n어렸지만, 나는 그날 밤부터 조에 대한 새로운 흠모가 시작되었다고 믿는다. 그 뒤로도 우리는 예전처럼 동등한 사이였다. 그러나 그 뒤 조용한 때에 조를 바라보며 그에 대해 생각하노라면, 나는 마음속으로 조를 우러러보고 있음을 의식하는 새로운 감각을 느꼈다.\n\n\"그건 그렇고,\" 조가 불을 더 지피려고 일어서며 말했다. \"여기 네덜란드 시계가 여덟 시를 칠 만큼 힘을 끌어모으고 있는데, 누나는 아직 집에 안 왔구나! 펌블추크 아저씨의 암말이 얼음 조각에 앞발을 디뎠다가 넘어진 게 아니어야 할 텐데.\"\n\n조 부인은 장날이면 이따금 펌블추크 아저씨와 함께 길을 나서, 여자의 안목이 필요한 살림 도구며 물건들을 사는 일을 거들곤 했다. 펌블추크 아저씨는 홀아비였고 자기 집 하녀에게는 그런 일을 도무지 맡기지 않았던 것이다. 이날이 장날이었고, 조 부인은 그런 나들이 가운데 하나를 나가 있었다.\n\n조는 불을 지피고 화롯가를 쓸었고, 그러고 나서 우리는 이륜마차 소리를 들으려고 문가로 갔다. 건조하고 추운 밤이었으며, 바람이 매섭게 불었고, 서리는 희고 단단했다. 오늘 밤 습지에 누워 있다가는 사람이 얼어 죽겠구나, 하고 나는 생각했다. 그러고 나서 별들을 올려다보며, 사람이 얼어 죽어 가면서 그 별들을 향해 얼굴을 쳐들었는데, 그 반짝이는 무리 가운데 어디서도 도움이나 연민을 찾지 못한다면 얼마나 끔찍할까 하고 헤아려 보았다.\n\n\"암말이 오고 있구나.\" 조가 말했다. \"한바탕 종소리처럼 울리면서 말이다!\"\n\n단단한 길 위를 디디는 암말의 쇠편자 소리는, 여느 때보다 훨씬 활기찬 걸음으로 다가오면서 제법 음악적이었다. 우리는 조 부인이 내려설 수 있도록 의자를 하나 내놓고, 그들이 환한 창을 볼 수 있도록 불을 돋우었으며, 무엇 하나 제자리에서 벗어난 것이 없도록 부엌을 마지막으로 한 번 둘러보았다. 이런 채비를 마쳤을 때, 그들이 눈만 빼고 온몸을 칭칭 감싼 채 마차를 몰고 다가왔다. 조 부인이 이내 내려섰고, 펌블추크 아저씨도 이내 내려와 암말에 천을 덮어 주었으며, 우리는 곧 모두 부엌으로 들어섰다. 어찌나 많은 찬 공기를 안고 들어왔던지, 그것이 불의 열기를 죄다 몰아내는 것만 같았다.\n\n\"자,\" 조 부인이 서둘러 흥분한 손길로 몸을 감싼 것을 풀고, 보닛을 어깨 뒤로 젖혀 끈에 매달린 채 늘어뜨리며 말했다. \"이 녀석이 오늘 밤 고마운 줄을 모른다면, 평생 고마운 줄 모르고 살 거예요!\"\n\n나는, 왜 그런 표정을 지어야 하는지 도무지 모르는 처지의 아이가 지을 수 있는 한 더없이 고마워하는 표정을 지었다.\n\n\"바라는 거라곤,\" 누나가 말했다. \"이 녀석이 응석받이가 되지 않는 거예요. 하지만 영 걱정이 돼서요.\"\n\n\"그분은 그럴 분이 아닙니다, 부인.\" 펌블추크 씨가 말했다. \"그분은 그보다는 분별이 있으시지요.\"\n\n그분? 나는 조를 바라보며 입술과 눈썹으로 \"그분?\"이라는 동작을 지어 보였다. 조도 나를 바라보며 자기 입술과 눈썹으로 \"그분?\"이라는 동작을 지어 보였다. 누나가 그 짓을 하던 그를 붙잡자, 그는 그런 자리에서 늘 짓던 화해의 태도로 손등을 코에 슬쩍 문지르고는 누나를 바라보았다.\n\n\"왜요?\" 누나가 톡 쏘는 투로 말했다. \"뭘 그렇게 빤히 봐요? 집에 불이라도 났어요?\"\n\n\"─어떤 분이,\" 조가 정중하게 넌지시 말했다. \"말했잖소─그분이라고.\"\n\n\"그러니까 그분은 여자분이겠지요, 안 그래요?\" 누나가 말했다. \"당신이 해비셤 양을 남자라고 부를 셈이 아니라면 말이에요. 설마 당신이라도 그 정도까지 갈 것 같진 않은데요.\"\n\n\"읍내 위쪽의 해비셤 양 말이오?\" 조가 말했다.\n\n\"읍내 아래쪽에 무슨 해비셤 양이라도 있나요?\" 누나가 되받았다. \"그분이 이 녀석을 거기 가서 놀게 하고 싶어 하세요. 그러니 당연히 이 녀석은 갈 거예요. 그리고 거기서 잘 노는 게 좋을 거다.\" 누나가 더없이 발랄하고 활달하게 굴라는 격려랍시고 나를 향해 고개를 흔들며 말했다. \"안 그러면 호되게 부려 먹을 테니까.\"\n\n나는 읍내 위쪽의 해비셤 양에 대한 이야기를 들은 적이 있었다. 몇 마일 안의 사람이라면 누구나 읍내 위쪽의 해비셤 양에 대해 들어 알고 있었으니까. 그분은 어마어마하게 부유하고 무뚝뚝한 부인으로, 강도를 막으려고 빗장을 단단히 걸어 잠근 크고 음산한 저택에 살며, 세상과 등진 은둔의 삶을 살고 있다고들 했다.\n\n\"이거 참 놀랍군!\" 조가 어안이 벙벙해져 말했다. \"그분이 어떻게 핍을 알게 됐는지 모르겠는걸!\"\n\n\"멍청이 같으니!\" 누나가 소리쳤다. \"누가 그분이 이 녀석을 안다고 했어요?\"\n\n\"─어떤 분이,\" 조가 다시 정중하게 넌지시 말했다. \"이 녀석을 거기 가서 놀게 하고 싶어 한다고 말했잖소.\"\n\n\"그럼 그분이 펌블추크 아저씨한테 거기 가서 놀 만한 아이를 아느냐고 물어볼 수도 있는 거 아니에요? 펌블추크 아저씨가 그분의 세입자일 가능성이, 그래서 이따금─분기마다라거나 반년마다라고는 안 할게요, 그건 당신한테 너무 많은 걸 바라는 거일 테니─하지만 이따금─그 집에 집세를 내러 갈 가능성이 그저 조금이라도 있는 거 아니냐고요? 그렇다면 그분이 펌블추크 아저씨한테 거기 가서 놀 만한 아이를 아느냐고 물어볼 수도 있는 거 아니에요? 그리고 펌블추크 아저씨가, 늘 우리를 헤아리고 마음 써 주시는 분이니─당신은 그렇게 생각 안 할지 몰라도요, 조지프,\" 마치 그가 더없이 무정한 조카라도 되는 양 더없이 깊은 책망의 어조로, \"그러니 펌블추크 아저씨가 여기 우쭐대며 서 있는 이 녀석을 말씀하실 수도 있는 거 아니냐고요\"─나는 결코 그러고 있지 않았음을 엄숙히 단언한다─\"내가 이제껏 기꺼이 종노릇해 온 이 녀석을 말이에요?\"\n\n\"또 한 번 잘 짚으셨군!\" 펌블추크 아저씨가 외쳤다. \"참 적절한 말씀입니다! 어찌나 깔끔하게 짚으시는지! 정말 옳은 말씀이오! 자, 조지프, 이제 자네도 사정을 알겠지.\"\n\n\"아니에요, 조지프.\" 조가 미안한 듯 손등으로 코를 자꾸자꾸 문지르는 동안, 누나가 여전히 책망하는 투로 말했다. \"당신은 아직도─당신은 그렇게 생각 안 할지 몰라도─사정을 모르고 있어요. 안다고 여길지 몰라도, 모른다고요, 조지프. 왜냐하면 당신은 펌블추크 아저씨가, 우리로서는 어찌 될지 알 수 없으나 이 녀석이 해비셤 양 댁에 가는 것으로 이 녀석의 팔자가 펴질지도 모른다는 걸 분별하시고서, 오늘 밤 이 녀석을 자기 이륜마차에 태워 읍내로 데려가, 오늘 밤은 데리고 계시다가, 내일 아침 자기 손으로 직접 해비셤 양 댁에 데려다주겠다고 하신 걸 모르니까요. 아이고, 맙소사!\" 누나가 갑작스러운 절망에 보닛을 홱 벗어 던지며 외쳤다. \"펌블추크 아저씨는 기다리시고, 암말은 문 앞에서 감기에 걸리고, 이 녀석은 머리끝부터 발끝까지 검댕과 흙투성이인데, 나는 여기 서서 한낱 얼간이들하고 입씨름이나 하고 있다니!\"\n\n그 말과 함께 누나는, 어린 양을 덮치는 독수리처럼 나를 와락 낚아챘고, 내 얼굴은 개수대의 나무 대야 속으로 짓눌렸으며, 머리는 빗물통의 물꼭지 밑에 처박혔고, 나는 비누칠을 당하고, 주물러지고, 수건질을 당하고, 두들겨 맞고, 갈퀴질당하고, 줄로 쓸리듯 문질러져, 정말이지 거의 제정신이 아닐 지경이 되었다.(여기서 짚어 두건대, 결혼반지가 인간의 얼굴 위를 인정사정없이 지나갈 때의 그 골진 자국에 대해서는, 내가 살아 있는 어떤 권위자보다도 더 잘 알고 있으리라 생각한다.)\n\n내 목욕재계가 끝나자, 나는 마치 어린 참회자가 거친 베옷을 입듯, 더없이 빳빳한 깨끗한 속옷을 입고서, 가장 꽉 끼고 가장 무시무시한 정장 속에 옭아매어졌다. 그러고 나서 나는 펌블추크 씨에게 넘겨졌는데, 그는 마치 자기가 주 장관이라도 되는 양 나를 격식 갖춰 인계받고는, 내내 못 해서 안달이 났던 게 분명한 그 연설을 내게 쏘아붙였다. \"얘야, 모든 친구에게 영원토록 고마워하되, 특히 너를 손으로 길러 키운 분들께 그리하여라!\"\n\n\"안녕히 계세요, 조!\"\n\n\"하느님의 축복이 있기를, 핍, 친구!\"\n\n나는 그 전까지 한 번도 그와 헤어져 본 적이 없었고, 내 감정 탓도 있고 비눗물 탓도 있어, 처음에는 이륜마차에서 별 하나 보이지 않았다. 그러나 별들은 하나둘 반짝이며 모습을 드러냈으되, 내가 대체 무슨 까닭으로 해비셤 양 댁에 놀러 가는지, 그리고 거기서 대체 무엇을 하고 놀기를 기대받는지 하는 물음에는 아무런 빛도 비춰 주지 않았다.\n\n# Chapter VIII.\n\n시장 거리에 자리한 펌블추크 씨의 가게는 곡물상이며 종묘상의 가게가 으레 그러하듯, 후추 알갱이 같고 곡물 가루 같은 분위기를 풍기는 곳이었다. 가게 안에 그렇게나 작은 서랍을 많이 거느리고 있으니, 그분은 참으로 행복한 사람임에 틀림없으리라는 생각이 들었다. 그리고 나는 아래쪽 칸의 서랍 한두 개를 들여다보고는, 그 안에 끈으로 묶인 갈색 종이 꾸러미들이 들어 있는 것을 보며, 저 꽃씨와 알뿌리들이 날씨 좋은 어느 날엔가 그 감옥을 깨고 나와 꽃피우고 싶어 한 적은 없었을까 궁금해했다.\n\n이런 상상에 잠긴 것은 그곳에 도착한 다음 날 이른 아침이었다. 전날 밤 나는 곧장 지붕이 비스듬히 기운 다락방의 잠자리로 보내졌는데, 침대가 놓인 구석은 천장이 어찌나 낮은지 기왓장이 내 눈썹에서 한 자도 채 안 되는 거리에 있다고 어림되었다. 그 같은 이른 아침에 나는 씨앗과 코르덴 사이에 묘한 친연성이 있음을 발견했다. 펌블추크 씨는 코르덴 옷을 입고 있었고, 그 가게 점원도 마찬가지였다. 그런데 어쩐 일인지 코르덴에는 씨앗을 빼닮은 전반적인 기운과 풍미가 감돌았고, 씨앗에는 코르덴을 빼닮은 전반적인 기운과 풍미가 감돌아서, 나로서는 어느 것이 어느 것인지 분간하기가 어려울 지경이었다. 그 같은 기회에 나는 또 한 가지 사실을 알아차렸으니, 펌블추크 씨는 길 건너 마구상을 바라보는 것으로 제 장사를 꾸려 가는 듯했고, 그 마구상은 마차 제조공을 눈여겨보는 것으로 자기 장사를 처리하는 듯했으며, 그 마차 제조공은 두 손을 주머니에 찔러 넣은 채 제빵사를 응시하는 것으로 살림을 꾸려 가는 듯했고, 그 제빵사는 또 그 나름대로 팔짱을 끼고 식료품상을 노려보았으며, 식료품상은 제 가게 문가에 서서 약제사를 향해 하품을 했다. 한쪽 눈에 확대경을 끼우고 늘 작은 책상에 코를 박고 있는, 그리고 가게 진열창 유리를 통해 그를 들여다보는 작업복 차림의 무리에게 늘 살펴봄을 당하던 시계공만이, 이 시장 거리에서 제 생업에 정신을 쏟고 있는 거의 유일한 사람인 듯싶었다.\n\n펌블추크 씨와 나는 여덟 시에 가게 뒤편 거실에서 아침을 먹었고, 점원은 앞쪽 가게에서 완두콩 자루 위에 앉아 차 한 잔과 버터 바른 빵 한 덩이를 들었다. 나는 펌블추크 씨가 형편없는 말동무라고 여겼다. 내 식사에는 굴욕과 참회의 성격이 깃들어야 한다는 누나의 생각에 사로잡혀 있는 데다 — 버터는 되도록 적게 바르고 빵 속살은 되도록 많이 주며, 내 우유에는 따뜻한 물을 어찌나 많이 타 넣었던지 차라리 우유를 아예 빼는 편이 더 솔직했으리만치 — 그분의 대화란 산수 외에는 아무것도 없었다. 내가 공손히 안녕히 주무셨느냐고 인사하자, 그분은 거드름을 피우며 \"칠 곱하기 구는, 얘야?\" 하고 말했다. 그런 식으로 낯선 곳에서, 빈속에 기습을 당하고서야 내가 어찌 대답을 할 수 있겠는가! 나는 배가 고팠으나, 한 입 삼키기도 전에 그분은 아침 식사 내내 이어지는 연속 계산을 시작했다. \"칠은?\" \"거기에 사는?\" \"거기에 팔은?\" \"거기에 육은?\" \"거기에 이는?\" \"거기에 십은?\" 하는 식이었다. 그리고 숫자 하나가 처리될 때마다, 다음 숫자가 닥치기 전에 한 입 베어 물거나 한 모금 들이켜는 것이 내가 할 수 있는 전부였다. 그동안 그분은 아무것도 셈할 필요 없이 느긋이 앉아, (이런 표현을 써도 된다면) 게걸스레 처넣고 폭식하는 태도로 베이컨과 따끈한 빵을 먹어 댔다.\n\n그런 까닭에 열 시가 되어 우리가 해비셤 양의 집으로 출발했을 때 나는 무척 기뻤다. 비록 그 부인의 지붕 아래에서 내가 어떻게 처신해야 할지에 대해서는 조금도 마음이 편치 않았지만 말이다. 십오 분도 안 되어 우리는 해비셤 양의 집에 이르렀는데, 그 집은 낡은 벽돌로 지어진 음울한 건물로, 쇠창살이 무척 많이 둘러쳐져 있었다. 창문 가운데 일부는 벽돌로 막혀 있었고, 남아 있는 것들 중에서도 아래쪽 창은 모조리 녹슨 창살로 막혀 있었다. 집 앞에는 안뜰이 있었는데, 그곳에도 창살이 둘려 있었다. 그래서 우리는 종을 울린 뒤, 누군가 나와서 문을 열어 줄 때까지 기다려야 했다. 문간에서 기다리는 동안 나는 안을 들여다보았는데(그때에도 펌블추크 씨는 \"거기에 십사는?\" 하고 물었으나 나는 못 들은 척했다), 집 한쪽 곁에 큰 양조장이 있는 것이 보였다. 그 안에서는 술을 빚는 일이 진행되고 있지 않았고, 오래오래 그래 온 듯했다.\n\n창문 하나가 올라가더니, 맑은 목소리가 \"이름은?\" 하고 물었다. 그 물음에 나의 인도자는 \"펌블추크요.\" 하고 답했다. 목소리는 \"맞아요.\" 하고 돌려주었고, 창문은 다시 닫혔으며, 손에 열쇠를 든 한 어린 아가씨가 안뜰을 가로질러 다가왔다.\n\n\"이 애가,\" 펌블추크 씨가 말했다. \"핍입니다.\"\n\n\"이 애가 핍이라고요?\" 어린 아가씨가 되받았다. 그 아이는 무척 예뻤고 무척 도도해 보였다. \"들어와, 핍.\"\n\n펌블추크 씨도 따라 들어오려 했으나, 그녀는 문으로 그를 막아섰다.\n\n\"어머!\" 그녀가 말했다. \"해비셤 양을 뵙고 싶으셨나요?\"\n\n\"해비셤 양께서 저를 보고자 하신다면야.\" 펌블추크 씨가 무안해하며 되받았다.\n\n\"아!\" 소녀가 말했다. \"그렇지만 보시다시피 그분은 그러길 원치 않으세요.\"\n\n그녀가 그 말을 어찌나 단호하게, 그리고 더는 따질 여지 없는 투로 말했던지, 펌블추크 씨는 위엄이 상한 처지였음에도 항의할 수가 없었다. 그러나 그분은 — 마치 내가 그분에게 무슨 짓이라도 한 양! — 나를 매섭게 노려보더니, 책망하듯 이런 말을 남기고 떠나갔다. \"얘야! 여기서 네 행실이, 손수 너를 길러 주신 분들의 명예가 되도록 해라!\" 나는 그분이 다시 돌아와 문 너머로 \"거기에 십육은?\" 하고 물어 올까 봐 걱정을 떨치지 못했다. 그러나 그러지는 않았다.\n\n나의 어린 인도자는 문을 잠갔고, 우리는 안뜰을 가로질러 갔다. 그곳은 포장이 되어 있고 깨끗했으나, 틈마다 풀이 자라 있었다. 양조장 건물들이 그 안뜰과 작은 통로로 이어져 있었는데, 그 통로의 나무 문은 열려 있었고, 그 너머의 양조장 전체가 저 멀리 둘러친 높은 담장까지 활짝 열려 있었으며, 모든 것이 텅 비고 쓰임을 잃은 채였다. 그곳의 찬 바람은 문밖보다도 더 차갑게 부는 듯했고, 양조장의 트인 옆구리로 휘몰아 들고 나며 날카로운 소리를 냈으니, 바다에 떠 있는 배의 삭구 사이로 부는 바람 소리 같았다.\n\n내가 그곳을 바라보는 것을 보고 그녀가 말했다. \"지금 저기서 빚어지는 독한 맥주를 다 마셔도 탈이 안 날걸, 얘.\"\n\n\"그럴 것 같네요, 아가씨.\" 나는 수줍게 말했다.\n\n\"이제는 저기서 맥주를 빚지 않는 게 나을 거야, 안 그러면 시큼해질 테니까, 얘. 그렇게 생각하지 않니?\"\n\n\"그래 보여요, 아가씨.\"\n\n\"누가 빚어 보겠다는 건 아니지만.\" 그녀가 덧붙였다. \"다 끝난 일이고, 이곳은 무너질 때까지 지금처럼 놀려진 채 서 있을 테니까. 독한 맥주라면, 이미 지하 저장고에 이 영주관을 잠기게 할 만큼 충분히 있고.\"\n\n\"이 집 이름이 영주관인가요, 아가씨?\"\n\n\"그 이름 가운데 하나야, 얘.\"\n\n\"그럼 이름이 하나 이상인가요, 아가씨?\"\n\n\"하나 더 있어. 다른 이름은 새티스였어. 그리스어인지, 라틴어인지, 히브리어인지, 아니면 셋 다인지 — 내겐 다 매한가지지만 — 충분하다는 뜻이야.\"\n\n\"충분한 집이라니.\" 내가 말했다. \"묘한 이름이네요, 아가씨.\"\n\n\"그래.\" 그녀가 답했다. \"하지만 말 그대로 이상의 뜻이 있었어. 이 이름이 붙여졌을 때 그것은, 이 집을 가진 사람은 더 바랄 게 없다는 뜻이었지. 그 시절 사람들은 쉽게 만족했던 모양이야. 하지만 꾸물거리지 마, 얘.\"\n\n그녀는 나를 그토록 자주 \"얘\"라고 불렀고, 그것도 칭찬과는 거리가 먼 무심한 투였으나, 나이는 나와 엇비슷했다. 물론 그녀는 나보다 훨씬 나이 들어 보였으니, 여자아이인 데다 아름답고 침착했기 때문이었다. 그리고 그녀는 마치 스물한 살 먹은 여왕이라도 되는 듯 나를 깔보았다.\n\n우리는 옆문으로 집 안에 들어갔다. 으리으리한 정문은 바깥쪽으로 사슬 두 가닥이 가로질러 채워져 있었다. 그리고 내가 맨 먼저 알아챈 것은, 복도가 죄다 어둡고, 그녀가 그곳에 촛불 하나를 켜 둔 채 두었다는 사실이었다. 그녀는 그 촛불을 집어 들었고, 우리는 다시 복도 몇 개를 지나 계단을 올라갔는데, 여전히 사방은 어두웠고 오직 그 촛불만이 우리를 밝혀 주었다.\n\n마침내 우리는 어느 방의 문 앞에 이르렀고, 그녀가 말했다. \"들어가.\"\n\n나는 공손함보다는 수줍음에서 답했다. \"먼저 들어가세요, 아가씨.\"\n\n이에 그녀가 되받았다. \"바보 같은 소리 마, 얘. 난 안 들어가.\" 그러고는 경멸하듯 걸어가 버렸고 — 더 고약하게도 — 촛불을 가지고 가 버렸다.\n\n이것은 무척 불편한 일이었고, 나는 반쯤 겁이 났다. 그러나 할 수 있는 일이라곤 문을 두드리는 것뿐이었으므로, 나는 문을 두드렸고, 안에서 들어오라는 말이 들려왔다. 그래서 나는 들어갔고, 밀랍 양초로 환히 밝혀진 꽤 큰 방 안에 있는 나 자신을 발견했다. 그 안에서는 햇빛이라곤 한 줄기도 보이지 않았다. 가구로 미루어 짐작하건대 그곳은 화장방이었으나, 그 가구의 상당수는 당시 내게는 형태도 쓰임새도 전혀 알 수 없는 것들이었다. 그러나 그 가운데 가장 눈에 띄는 것은 천을 두르고 금테 거울이 달린 탁자였는데, 나는 첫눈에 그것이 어느 귀부인의 화장대임을 알아보았다.\n\n거기에 귀부인이 앉아 있지 않았더라면 내가 이 물건을 그토록 빨리 알아보았을지는 모르겠다. 팔걸이의자에 앉아, 한쪽 팔꿈치를 탁자에 괴고 그 손에 머리를 기댄 채, 내가 지금껏 본 적도 없고 앞으로도 보지 못할 가장 기이한 부인이 앉아 있었다.\n\n그녀는 값진 옷감으로 — 새틴과 레이스와 비단으로 — 차려입고 있었는데, 모두가 흰색이었다. 신발도 희었다. 그리고 머리에는 길고 흰 면사포를 늘어뜨리고 신부의 꽃을 꽂고 있었으나, 머리카락은 하얗게 세어 있었다. 목과 두 손에서는 몇몇 영롱한 보석이 반짝였고, 탁자 위에도 다른 보석들이 반짝이며 놓여 있었다. 그녀가 입은 것보다 덜 화려한 옷가지들과, 절반쯤 꾸려진 트렁크들이 여기저기 흩어져 있었다. 그녀는 채 단장을 끝내지 못한 상태였다. 신발은 한 짝만 신고 있었고 — 나머지 한 짝은 그녀의 손 가까이 탁자 위에 있었으며 — 면사포는 반쯤만 정돈되어 있었고, 시계와 사슬은 차지 않은 채였으며, 가슴에 달 레이스 조각이 그 장신구들과 함께, 그녀의 손수건과 장갑과 꽃 몇 송이와 기도서와 더불어 거울 둘레에 어지러이 쌓여 있었다.\n\n이 모든 것을 본 것은 처음 몇 순간 사이의 일이 아니었지만, 그래도 그 처음 몇 순간 동안 짐작보다 많은 것을 보긴 했다. 그러나 나는 보았다. 흰색이어야 마땅한 내 시야 안의 모든 것이 오래전에 한때 희었으나, 광택을 잃고 빛이 바래어 누렇게 변해 있음을. 나는 보았다. 신부의 드레스 속에 든 신부가 그 드레스처럼, 그리고 그 꽃들처럼 시들어 버려, 움푹 꺼진 두 눈의 광채 말고는 아무런 광채도 남아 있지 않음을. 나는 보았다. 그 드레스가 어느 젊은 여인의 둥그스름한 몸매 위에 입혀졌었으나, 이제 그것이 헐겁게 걸쳐진 그 몸은 가죽과 뼈로 쪼그라들었음을. 한번은 시장에서 나는, 무어라 형언할 수 없는 어떤 있을 법하지 않은 인물이 안치된 모습을 본뜬 끔찍한 밀랍 인형을 보러 끌려간 적이 있었다. 한번은 우리 고장의 오래된 습지 교회 가운데 한 곳으로, 교회 바닥 아래 지하 묘실에서 파낸, 호화로운 옷의 잿더미 속에 든 해골을 보러 끌려간 적이 있었다. 이제 그 밀랍 인형과 해골이 어두운 두 눈을 가지고서, 그 눈을 움직여 나를 바라보는 것만 같았다. 할 수만 있었다면 나는 비명을 질렀으리라.\n\n\"누구냐?\" 탁자에 앉은 부인이 말했다.\n\n\"핍이에요, 마님.\"\n\n\"핍?\"\n\n\"펌블추크 씨네 아이예요, 마님. 놀러 — 왔어요.\"\n\n\"가까이 오너라. 너를 좀 보자. 바싹 다가와.\"\n\n그녀의 눈길을 피하며 그녀 앞에 섰을 때, 나는 비로소 주변의 물건들을 세세히 살피게 되었고, 그녀의 시계가 아홉 시 이십 분 전에 멈춰 있다는 것을, 그리고 방 안의 괘종시계 또한 아홉 시 이십 분 전에 멈춰 있다는 것을 보았다.\n\n\"나를 보아라.\" 해비셤 양이 말했다. \"네가 태어난 뒤로 한 번도 해를 본 적 없는 여자가 무섭지는 않으냐?\"\n\n유감스럽지만 나는 \"아니요\"라는 대답에 담긴 그 엄청난 거짓말을 하는 것이 두렵지 않았다고 적어 둔다.\n\n\"내가 여기 무엇을 짚고 있는지 아느냐?\" 그녀가 두 손을 포개어 자기 왼쪽 가슴에 얹으며 말했다.\n\n\"네, 마님.\" (그 말에 나는 그 젊은 놈이 떠올랐다.)\n\n\"내가 무엇을 짚고 있느냐?\"\n\n\"마님의 심장이요.\"\n\n\"부서진!\"\n\n그녀는 그 말을, 간절한 눈빛으로, 강한 힘을 주어, 그리고 일종의 자랑이 담긴 기괴한 미소를 띠며 내뱉었다. 그러고는 한동안 거기에 두 손을 얹고 있다가, 마치 손이 무겁기라도 한 듯 천천히 거두어 들였다.\n\n\"나는 지쳤다.\" 해비셤 양이 말했다. \"기분 전환이 필요해. 남자도 여자도 이젠 진절머리가 난다. 놀아 보아라.\"\n\n이런 상황에서, 한 가엾은 사내아이에게 그녀가 시킬 수 있었던 것 가운데, 이 넓디넓은 세상에서 이보다 더 하기 어려운 일은 없었으리라는 점은, 가장 트집 잡기 좋아하는 독자라도 인정해 주리라 생각한다.\n\n\"나는 가끔 병적인 공상에 빠지지.\" 그녀가 말을 이었다. \"그리고 지금 나는 누가 노는 모습을 좀 보고 싶다는 병적인 공상에 빠져 있단다. 자, 어서!\" 오른손 손가락을 초조하게 까딱이며 말했다. \"놀아, 놀아, 놀란 말이야!\"\n\n순간, 누나가 나를 두들길 것이라는 두려움이 눈앞에 어른거려, 나는 펌블추크 씨의 이륜마차 흉내라도 내며 방 안을 빙빙 돌까 하는 필사적인 생각을 했다. 그러나 도무지 그 연기를 해낼 자신이 없어 그만두었고, 해비셤 양을 바라보며 서 있었는데, 그녀가 그것을 고집스러운 태도로 받아들인 모양이었다. 우리가 서로를 한참 들여다본 뒤에 그녀가 이렇게 말했으니까.\n\n\"너는 부루퉁하고 고집이 센 게냐?\"\n\n\"아니에요, 마님, 저는 마님이 무척 가엾고, 지금 당장 놀지 못하는 게 무척 죄송해요. 마님이 저를 일러바치시면 저는 누나한테 혼이 날 테니, 할 수 있다면 하겠어요. 하지만 여기는 너무 낯설고, 너무 이상하고, 너무 으리으리하고 — 그리고 서글퍼서 —.\" 나는 너무 많은 말을 할까 봐, 혹은 이미 해 버렸을까 봐 두려워 말을 멈추었고, 우리는 다시 서로를 들여다보았다.\n\n그녀가 다시 입을 열기 전에, 그녀는 나에게서 눈길을 거두어 자기가 입은 드레스를 바라보고, 화장대를 바라보고, 끝내는 거울 속의 자기 자신을 바라보았다.\n\n\"저 애한테는 너무 새롭고,\" 그녀가 중얼거렸다. \"내겐 너무 오래되었지. 저 애한테는 너무 낯설고, 내겐 너무 익숙하지. 우리 둘 다에게는 너무 서글프고! 에스텔라를 불러라.\"\n\n그녀가 여전히 거울에 비친 자기 모습을 바라보고 있었으므로, 나는 그녀가 아직 혼잣말을 하고 있는 줄 알고 잠자코 있었다.\n\n\"에스텔라를 불러라.\" 그녀가 나를 향해 휙 눈길을 쏘며 거듭 말했다. \"그건 할 수 있겠지. 에스텔라를 불러. 문간에서.\"\n\n낯선 집의 어느 으스스한 복도에서 어둠 속에 서서, 보이지도 대답하지도 않는 도도한 어린 아가씨를 향해 에스텔라를 외쳐 부르는 것은, 그리고 그렇게 그녀의 이름을 큰 소리로 외치는 것이 끔찍한 무례처럼 느껴지는 것은, 시키는 대로 노는 일만큼이나 거의 못 할 노릇이었다. 그러나 그녀는 마침내 대답했고, 그녀의 불빛이 어두운 복도를 따라 별처럼 다가왔다.\n\n해비셤 양은 그녀에게 가까이 오라고 손짓하더니, 탁자에서 보석 하나를 집어 들어 그녀의 곱고 앳된 가슴께와 예쁜 갈색 머리에 견주어 그 효과를 가늠해 보았다. \"언젠가는 네 것이 될 거란다, 얘야, 너는 이걸 잘 쓸 게다. 이 애와 카드 놀이 하는 걸 좀 보여 다오.\"\n\n\"이 애랑요? 아니, 이 앤 그냥 천한 막일하는 아이잖아요!\"\n\n나는 해비셤 양이 — 다만 너무 있을 법하지 않은 일이라 그렇게 들렸을 뿐인지도 모르나 — 이렇게 대답하는 것을 엿들었다고 생각했다. \"그래서? 네가 저 애의 마음을 부숴 놓으면 되지.\"\n\n\"무슨 놀이 할 줄 아니, 얘?\" 에스텔라가 더없는 멸시를 담아 나에게 물었다.\n\n\"거지 만들기밖에 못 해요, 아가씨.\"\n\n\"이 애를 거지로 만들어 버려.\" 해비셤 양이 에스텔라에게 말했다. 그래서 우리는 카드 놀이를 하려고 자리에 앉았다.\n\n그제야 나는 방 안의 모든 것이, 그 시계와 괘종시계처럼, 오래전에 멈춰 버렸음을 알아차리기 시작했다. 나는 해비셤 양이 집어 들었던 자리에 그 보석을 정확히 도로 내려놓는 것을 보았다. 에스텔라가 카드를 돌리는 동안, 나는 화장대를 다시 흘끗 보았고, 그 위에 놓인, 한때 희었으나 이제 누레진 신발이 한 번도 신긴 적 없는 것임을 보았다. 나는 그 신발이 없는 발을 내려다보았고, 그 발에 신긴, 한때 희었으나 이제 누레진 비단 양말이 너덜너덜해지도록 밟혀 있음을 보았다. 모든 것이 이렇게 멈추어 있지 않았다면, 이 모든 빛바래고 썩어 가는 물건들이 이렇게 정지해 있지 않았다면, 무너진 몸 위의 시든 신부 드레스조차 그토록 수의(壽衣)처럼 보이지도, 그 긴 면사포가 그토록 시신을 덮는 천처럼 보이지도 않았으리라.\n\n우리가 카드 놀이를 하는 동안 그녀는 그렇게 시체처럼 앉아 있었다. 그녀의 신부 드레스에 달린 주름 장식과 가두리 장식은 흙빛으로 바랜 종이 같았다. 그 무렵 나는, 이따금 발굴되는 옛 시대의 매장된 시신이 또렷이 눈에 들어오는 순간 가루로 무너져 내린다는 사실을 전혀 알지 못했다. 그러나 나는 그 뒤로 자주 생각했다. 그녀는 마치 자연스러운 한낮의 빛이 들어오기만 하면 곧 한 줌 먼지로 부스러져 버릴 것처럼 보였으리라고.\n\n\"이 애는 으뜸패를 잭이라고 부르네요!\" 첫 판이 채 끝나기도 전에 에스텔라가 멸시를 담아 말했다. \"게다가 손은 어쩜 이리 거칠담! 신발은 또 어쩜 이리 투박하고!\"\n\n나는 전에는 한 번도 내 손을 부끄러워해 본 적이 없었다. 그러나 이제 그것이 무척 보잘것없는 한 쌍이라는 생각이 들기 시작했다. 나를 향한 그녀의 멸시가 어찌나 강렬했던지, 그것이 전염되어 나에게도 옮아 버렸다.\n\n그녀가 그 판을 이겼고, 이번엔 내가 카드를 돌렸다. 그녀가 내가 실수하기를 노리며 기다리고 있다는 것을 알고 있었으니, 카드를 잘못 돌린 것은 지극히 당연한 일이었다. 그러자 그녀는 나를 멍청하고 굼뜬 막일하는 아이라고 욕했다.\n\n\"너는 저 애에 대해 아무 말도 하지 않는구나.\" 해비셤 양이 지켜보며 나에게 말했다. \"저 애는 너에 대해 모진 말을 많이 하는데, 너는 저 애에 대해 아무 말도 하지 않아. 저 애를 어떻게 생각하느냐?\"\n\n\"말하고 싶지 않아요.\" 나는 더듬거렸다.\n\n\"내 귀에 대고 말해 보아라.\" 해비셤 양이 몸을 굽히며 말했다.\n\n\"저 누나는 무척 도도한 것 같아요.\" 나는 속삭이며 답했다.\n\n\"그 밖에는?\"\n\n\"무척 예쁜 것 같아요.\"\n\n\"그 밖에는?\"\n\n\"무척 사람을 모욕하는 것 같아요.\" (그때 그녀는 더없는 혐오의 눈길로 나를 바라보고 있었다.)\n\n\"그 밖에는?\"\n\n\"집에 갔으면 좋겠어요.\"\n\n\"저 애가 저토록 예쁜데도, 다시는 안 보고 싶으냐?\"\n\n\"다시 보는 게 싫지는 않을 것 같지만, 지금은 집에 가고 싶어요.\"\n\n\"곧 가게 될 게다.\" 해비셤 양이 소리 내어 말했다. \"판이나 끝내거라.\"\n\n처음에 보았던 그 기괴한 미소 한 번만 아니었다면, 나는 해비셤 양의 얼굴이 도저히 미소 지을 수 없는 것이라고 거의 확신했으리라. 그 얼굴은 — 십중팔구 그녀를 둘러싼 모든 것이 정지해 버렸을 그 무렵에 — 경계하며 곱씹는 듯한 표정으로 굳어 떨어진 채였고, 다시는 무엇으로도 그것을 들어 올릴 수 없을 것처럼 보였다. 그녀의 가슴은 푹 꺼져 그녀는 구부정했고, 목소리도 가라앉아 그녀는 낮게, 죽은 듯한 적막을 두르고 말했다. 한마디로 그녀는 짓이기는 일격의 무게 아래, 몸도 영혼도, 안도 밖도 모조리 가라앉아 버린 모습이었다.\n\n나는 에스텔라와 그 판을 끝까지 했고, 그녀는 나를 거지로 만들어 버렸다. 그녀는 카드를 모두 따자 그것을 탁자 위에 내던졌는데, 마치 나에게서 따낸 것이라 하여 그 카드들을 경멸하기라도 하는 듯했다.\n\n\"언제 다시 너를 오게 할까?\" 해비셤 양이 말했다. \"어디 보자.\"\n\n나는 오늘이 수요일이라는 것을 그녀에게 일러 주려던 참이었는데, 그녀가 아까처럼 오른손 손가락을 초조하게 까딱이며 나를 가로막았다.\n\n\"됐다, 됐어! 나는 한 주의 요일이 어떤지도 모르고, 한 해의 주가 어떤지도 모른다. 엿새 뒤에 다시 오너라. 알았느냐?\"\n\n\"네, 마님.\"\n\n\"에스텔라, 이 애를 데리고 내려가거라. 먹을 것을 좀 주고, 먹는 동안 여기저기 거닐며 둘러보게 해라. 가거라, 핍.\"\n\n나는 촛불을 따라 올라왔던 것처럼, 촛불을 따라 내려갔고, 그녀는 우리가 그것을 찾았던 자리에 그것을 세워 두었다. 그녀가 옆문을 열기 전까지, 나는 별생각 없이 막연히 지금이 밤일 거라고 여기고 있었다. 쏟아져 드는 한낮의 빛이 나를 완전히 어리둥절하게 만들었고, 마치 내가 그 기이한 방의 촛불 속에서 여러 시간을 보낸 것 같은 느낌이 들게 했다.\n\n\"넌 여기서 기다려, 얘.\" 에스텔라가 말했다. 그러고는 사라져 문을 닫아 버렸다.\n\n나는 안뜰에 혼자 있게 된 김에 내 거친 두 손과 투박한 신발을 들여다보았다. 그 부속물들에 대한 내 평가는 호의적이지 못했다. 그것들은 전에는 한 번도 나를 괴롭힌 적이 없었으나, 이제는 천박한 군더더기로서 나를 괴롭혔다. 나는 으뜸패는 본디 으뜸패라 불러야 마땅한 것을 어째서 잭이라고 부르도록 가르쳤느냐고 조에게 물어보리라 마음먹었다. 나는 조가 좀 더 점잖게 자랐더라면 좋았으리라고, 그랬다면 나도 그러했으리라고 바랐다.\n\n그녀가 빵과 고기와 작은 맥주 한 잔을 들고 돌아왔다. 그녀는 그 잔을 안뜰의 돌바닥 위에 내려놓고, 마치 내가 망신을 당한 개라도 되는 양 거만하게, 나를 쳐다보지도 않은 채 빵과 고기를 건넸다. 나는 어찌나 굴욕스럽고, 상처받고, 멸시당하고, 모욕당하고, 화가 나고, 서러웠던지 — 그 쓰라림에 알맞은 이름을 도무지 찾을 수가 없는데 — 그 이름이 무엇이었는지는 신만이 아시리라 — 두 눈에 눈물이 솟구쳤다. 그것이 솟아오른 순간, 그 소녀는 자기가 그 눈물의 원인이 되었다는 데에 재빠른 기쁨을 느끼며 나를 바라보았다. 그것이 나에게 눈물을 참고 그녀를 마주 볼 힘을 주었다. 그러자 그녀는 경멸하듯 고개를 홱 젖혔다 — 그러나 내가 그토록 상처받았다고 너무 확신해 버린 것은 아닐까 하는 기색이 비쳤다고, 나는 생각했다 — 그러고는 나를 두고 가 버렸다.\n\n그러나 그녀가 가 버리자, 나는 얼굴을 가릴 곳을 찾아 둘러보다가, 양조장 통로의 문들 가운데 하나 뒤로 숨어, 거기 벽에 소맷자락을 받치고, 그 위에 이마를 기댄 채 울었다. 울면서 나는 벽을 걷어찼고, 내 머리카락을 사납게 잡아 비틀었다. 그만큼 내 감정은 쓰라렸고, 그 이름 없는 쓰라림은 어떻게든 풀어내야 할 만큼 날카로웠다.\n\n누나의 양육 방식은 나를 예민하게 만들어 놓았다. 누가 길러 주든 간에, 아이들이 그들 나름의 삶을 살아가는 그 작은 세계에서, 불의(不義)만큼 예민하게 감지되고 예민하게 느껴지는 것은 없다. 아이가 겪을 수 있는 불의란 사소한 것일 수도 있다. 그러나 아이는 작고, 그 세계도 작으며, 그 흔들목마는 비례로 따지면 골격이 큰 아일랜드 사냥말만큼이나 키가 크다. 내 안에서, 나는 갓난아기 적부터 불의와 끊임없는 다툼을 견뎌 왔다. 말을 할 수 있게 된 때부터, 나는 누나가 그 변덕스럽고 난폭한 강압으로 나에게 불공평하다는 것을 알고 있었다. 누나가 나를 손수 길렀다 해서, 그것이 나를 거칠게 잡아채며 기를 권리까지 주는 것은 아니라는 깊은 확신을 나는 마음에 품어 왔다. 온갖 벌과 망신과 굶김과 밤샘과 그 밖의 참회의 의식들을 거치는 내내, 나는 이 확신을 키워 왔다. 그리고 외롭고 보호받지 못한 처지에서 그것을 그토록 곱씹어 온 탓에, 내가 도덕적으로 겁이 많고 무척 예민해진 것이라고 나는 대체로 여긴다.\n\n나는 그 상한 감정을 한동안, 양조장 벽을 걷어차 그리로 차 넣고, 머리카락을 비틀어 거기서 짜내는 것으로 떨쳐 냈다. 그런 다음 소맷자락으로 얼굴을 매만지고 문 뒤에서 나왔다. 빵과 고기는 반가웠고, 맥주는 몸을 데우고 짜릿하게 했으니, 나는 곧 기운을 차려 주위를 둘러보게 되었다.\n\n과연 그곳은 버려진 곳이었으니, 양조장 마당의 비둘기집에 이르기까지 그러했다. 그 비둘기집은 무슨 거센 바람에 장대 위에서 비뚜로 떠밀린 채였으니, 흔들어 줄 비둘기가 한 마리라도 거기 있었더라면 그 비둘기들은 자기들이 바다에 떠 있다고 여겼을 법했다. 그러나 비둘기집에는 비둘기가 없었고, 마구간에는 말이 없었고, 돼지우리에는 돼지가 없었고, 곳간에는 엿기름이 없었으며, 가마솥이나 양조통에는 곡물과 맥주의 냄새가 없었다. 양조장의 온갖 쓰임과 향내는 그 마지막 연기와 함께 증발해 버린 모양이었다. 한쪽 곁마당에는 빈 술통들이 황야처럼 늘어서 있었는데, 거기에는 더 나았던 시절에 대한 어떤 시큼한 추억이 어려 있었다. 그러나 그것은 사라진 맥주의 견본으로 받아들이기에는 너무 시큼했다. 이 점에서 나는 그 은둔자 같은 술통들이 여느 은둔자들 대부분과 닮았다고 기억한다.\n\n양조장의 맨 끝 너머에는 낡은 담장을 두른 무성한 정원이 있었다. 그 담은 그리 높지 않아서, 나는 기어올라 한참 매달려 그 너머를 넘겨다볼 수 있었고, 그 무성한 정원이 바로 그 집의 정원이며, 엉클어진 잡초가 우거져 있으나, 푸르고 누런 오솔길 위에는 누군가 가끔 거기를 거니는 듯 발자취가 나 있고, 바로 그때에도 에스텔라가 나에게서 멀어지며 걸어가고 있음을 보았다. 그러나 그녀는 어디에나 있는 듯했다. 내가 술통들이 안기는 유혹에 굴복하여 그 위를 걷기 시작했을 때, 통이 늘어선 마당 저쪽 끝에서 그 위를 걸어가는 그녀를 보았으니까. 그녀는 나에게 등을 돌린 채, 예쁜 갈색 머리를 두 손으로 펼쳐 든 채, 한 번도 돌아보지 않고 곧장 내 시야 밖으로 사라졌다. 양조장 안에서도 그러했다 — 내가 양조장이라 함은, 그들이 맥주를 빚던, 그리고 양조 기구들이 아직 남아 있는, 포장된 높다란 큰 공간을 두고 하는 말이다. 내가 처음 그 안에 들어가, 그 음울함에 다소 짓눌린 채 문가에 서서 둘러보고 있었을 때, 나는 그녀가 꺼진 화덕들 사이를 지나, 가벼운 쇠 계단을 올라, 머리 위 높은 회랑을 통해 밖으로 나가는 것을 보았는데, 마치 하늘 속으로 걸어 나가는 듯했다.\n\n바로 이곳에서, 바로 이 순간에, 내 공상에 기이한 일이 일어났다. 그때에도 나는 그것을 기이한 일로 여겼고, 한참 뒤에는 더욱 기이한 일로 여겼다. 나는 — 서리 같은 빛을 올려다보느라 조금 흐려진 — 두 눈을, 내 오른편 가까이 건물의 낮은 구석에 있는 커다란 나무 들보 쪽으로 돌렸고, 거기에 한 형상이 목이 매달려 있는 것을 보았다. 온통 누르스름한 흰빛에, 발에는 신발이 한 짝뿐인 형상이었다. 그리고 그것은 그렇게 매달려 있어서, 나는 그 드레스의 빛바랜 가두리 장식이 흙빛 종이 같다는 것을, 그리고 그 얼굴이 해비셤 양의 얼굴이며, 마치 나를 부르려 하는 듯 온 얼굴에 어떤 움직임이 스쳐 지나가는 것을 알아볼 수 있었다. 그 형상을 본 공포 속에서, 그리고 조금 전까지만 해도 그것이 거기 없었다고 확신한 공포 속에서, 나는 처음엔 그것에게서 달아났다가, 이내 그것을 향해 달려갔다. 그리고 거기에 아무 형상도 없음을 알았을 때, 나의 공포는 무엇보다도 가장 컸다.\n\n명랑한 하늘의 서리 같은 빛도, 안뜰 문의 창살 너머로 지나가는 사람들의 모습도, 남은 빵과 고기와 맥주가 되살려 주는 기운도, 그 무엇이라도 모자랐다면 나는 제정신을 차리지 못했으리라. 그런 도움이 있었음에도, 에스텔라가 나를 내보내 주려고 열쇠를 들고 다가오는 것을 보지 못했다면, 나는 그토록 빨리 정신을 차리지 못했을지도 모른다. 만약 그녀가 내가 겁먹은 모습을 본다면, 나를 깔볼 그럴듯한 이유가 생길 거라고 나는 생각했다. 그녀에게 그럴듯한 이유를 주지는 않으리라.\n\n그녀는 나를 지나치며 의기양양한 눈길을 던졌는데, 마치 내 손이 그토록 거칠고 내 신발이 그토록 투박한 것을 기뻐하기라도 하는 듯했다. 그러고는 문을 열어 그것을 붙든 채 서 있었다. 내가 그녀를 보지 않고 막 나가려는데, 그녀가 비웃는 듯한 손길로 나를 건드렸다.\n\n\"넌 왜 안 우니?\"\n\n\"울고 싶지 않으니까요.\"\n\n\"울고 싶잖아.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"넌 반쯤 눈이 멀 만큼 울었고, 지금도 또 울 것 같은데 뭐.\"\n\n그녀는 경멸하듯 웃고는 나를 밖으로 떠밀고, 내 뒤로 문을 잠가 버렸다. 나는 곧장 펌블추크 씨네로 갔고, 그분이 집에 없는 것을 알고는 한없이 마음이 놓였다. 그래서 언제 다시 해비셤 양 댁에 와야 하는지를 점원에게 일러두고, 나는 우리 대장간까지 사 마일 길을 걷기 시작했다. 가는 내내 내가 본 모든 것을 곰곰이 되씹으며, 나는 그저 천한 막일하는 아이라는 것을, 내 손은 거칠다는 것을, 내 신발은 투박하다는 것을, 내가 으뜸패를 잭이라 부르는 천박한 버릇에 빠져 있었다는 것을, 어젯밤 스스로 여겼던 것보다 훨씬 더 무지하다는 것을, 그리고 대체로 내가 비천하고 못난 처지에 있다는 것을 깊이 곱씹었다.\n\n# Chapter IX.\n\n집에 돌아오자 누나는 미스 해비셤 댁에 관한 모든 것을 몹시 궁금해하며 수많은 질문을 퍼부었다. 그리고 나는 머지않아 뒤에서 목덜미와 등허리를 호되게 쥐어박히고, 그 질문들에 충분히 길게 대답하지 않았다는 이유로 얼굴이 부엌 벽에 굴욕적으로 짓눌리는 신세가 되었다.\n\n남에게 이해받지 못하리라는 두려움이 다른 어린아이들의 가슴속에도, 내 가슴속에 숨어 있던 만큼이나 깊이 숨겨져 있다면 — 내가 별나게 괴상한 아이였다고 의심할 특별한 이유가 없으니 그럴 법하다고 나는 본다 — 그것은 많은 침묵의 열쇠가 된다. 내가 미스 해비셤 댁을 내 눈으로 본 그대로 묘사한다면 이해받지 못하리라는 확신이 들었다. 그뿐 아니라 미스 해비셤 역시 이해받지 못하리라는 확신이 들었다. 그리고 그분은 나로서는 도무지 알 수 없는 사람이었지만, 그분의 진짜 모습을(미스 에스텔라는 말할 것도 없고) 끌어내어 조 부인의 눈앞에 펼쳐 보이는 데에는 뭔가 천박하고 배신적인 구석이 있으리라는 인상을 품고 있었다. 그래서 나는 되도록 말을 아꼈고, 얼굴이 부엌 벽에 짓눌렸다.\n\n가장 곤란했던 것은, 내가 보고 들은 모든 것을 알아내고 싶다는 게걸스러운 호기심에 사로잡힌 저 윽박지르기 좋아하는 늙은 펌블추크가, 그 내막을 낱낱이 캐내려고 차 마실 시간에 이륜마차를 몰고 입을 떡 벌린 채 건너왔다는 점이었다. 그리고 흐리멍덩한 물고기 같은 눈과 떡 벌린 입, 캐묻듯 곤두선 모래빛 머리칼, 그리고 허풍스러운 셈을 하느라 들썩이는 조끼를 한 그 골칫덩이의 모습만 보아도, 나는 입을 다무는 데 더욱 심술이 났다.\n\n\"자, 얘야.\" 펌블추크 아저씨가 난롯가의 상석에 자리를 잡자마자 입을 열었다. \"읍내에서는 어떻게 지냈느냐?\"\n\n나는 \"그럭저럭이요, 아저씨\"라고 대답했고, 누나는 내게 주먹을 흔들어 보였다.\n\n\"그럭저럭이라고?\" 펌블추크 씨가 되받았다. \"그럭저럭은 대답이 아니다. 그럭저럭이라는 게 무슨 뜻인지 말해 봐라, 얘야.\"\n\n이마에 묻은 회반죽이 뇌를 굳혀 고집스러운 상태로 만드는지도 모른다. 어쨌든 벽에서 묻어난 회반죽을 이마에 묻힌 채, 내 고집은 금강석처럼 단단했다. 나는 한동안 곰곰이 생각하더니, 마치 새로운 발상이라도 발견한 듯 대답했다. \"그럭저럭이라는 뜻이에요.\"\n\n누나는 못 참겠다는 탄성을 지르며 내게 달려들려 했고 — 조는 대장간에서 일하느라 바빴으니, 나로선 방어할 길이 조금도 없었다 — 바로 그때 펌블추크 씨가 끼어들며 말했다. \"안 됩니다! 화내지 마세요. 이 녀석은 제게 맡기시지요, 부인. 이 녀석은 제게 맡기세요.\" 그러더니 펌블추크 씨는 내 머리카락이라도 자를 듯이 나를 자기 쪽으로 돌려세우고는 말했다.\n\n\"먼저(우리 생각을 정리하기 위해서다) 사십삼 펜스는?\"\n\n나는 \"사백 파운드요\"라고 대답할 경우의 결과를 가늠해 보았으나, 그것이 내게 불리하다는 것을 알고는 최대한 정답에 가깝게 — 여덟 펜스쯤 모자란 답을 — 댔다. 그러자 펌블추크 씨는 \"십이 펜스가 일 실링\"부터 \"사십 펜스가 삼 실링 사 펜스\"까지 펜스 구구단을 줄줄이 외게 하더니, 마치 나를 끝장내 버렸다는 듯 의기양양하게 다그쳤다. \"자! 사십삼 펜스는 얼마냐?\" 이에 나는 한참을 생각한 끝에 대답했다. \"모르겠어요.\" 어찌나 약이 올랐던지, 내가 정말 알고 있었는지조차 의심스러울 지경이었다.\n\n펌블추크 씨는 나사를 비틀어 빼내듯 머리를 비틀어 가며 물었다. \"예를 들어, 사십삼 펜스가 칠 실링 육 펜스 삼 파딩이냐?\"\n\n\"네!\" 내가 말했다. 그러자 누나가 당장 내 귀싸대기를 올려붙였지만, 그 대답이 그의 농담을 망쳐 그를 꼼짝 못하게 멈춰 세운 것을 보니 무척 흐뭇했다.\n\n\"얘야! 미스 해비셤은 어떻게 생겼더냐?\" 정신을 차린 펌블추크 씨가 두 팔을 가슴에 단단히 포개고 다시 나사를 죄며 입을 열었다.\n\n\"아주 키가 크고 검은 분이에요.\" 나는 그에게 말했다.\n\n\"그래요, 아저씨?\" 누나가 물었다.\n\n펌블추크 씨는 눈을 찡긋하며 그렇다고 끄덕였다. 그것으로 나는 그가 미스 해비셤을 한 번도 본 적이 없음을 곧장 알아차렸다. 그분은 전혀 그런 모습이 아니었기 때문이다.\n\n\"좋아!\" 펌블추크 씨가 우쭐거리며 말했다. (\"이 녀석은 이렇게 다루는 겁니다! 이제 우리가 우위를 잡아 가는 것 같지요, 부인?\")\n\n\"정말이지, 아저씨.\" 조 부인이 받았다. \"이 애를 늘 맡아 주셨으면 좋겠어요. 다루는 법을 어쩜 그리 잘 아시는지요.\"\n\n\"자, 얘야! 오늘 들어갔을 때 그분은 뭘 하고 계셨느냐?\" 펌블추크 씨가 물었다.\n\n\"앉아 계셨어요.\" 나는 대답했다. \"검은 벨벳 마차 안에요.\"\n\n펌블추크 씨와 조 부인은 — 당연하게도 — 서로를 빤히 쳐다보더니, 둘 다 되뇌었다. \"검은 벨벳 마차 안에?\"\n\n\"네.\" 내가 말했다. \"그리고 미스 에스텔라가 — 그분 조카인 것 같아요 — 마차 창문으로 금쟁반에 담은 케이크와 포도주를 건네 드렸어요. 그리고 우리 모두 금쟁반에 케이크와 포도주를 받아 먹었어요. 저는 마차 뒤에 올라가서 제 몫을 먹었는데, 그분이 그러라고 하셔서요.\"\n\n\"다른 사람은 또 없었느냐?\" 펌블추크 씨가 물었다.\n\n\"개 네 마리요.\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"큰 개냐, 작은 개냐?\"\n\n\"엄청나게 컸어요.\" 내가 말했다. \"그리고 그 개들이 은바구니에서 송아지 고기 커틀릿을 두고 서로 다퉜어요.\"\n\n펌블추크 씨와 조 부인은 다시금 서로를 멍하니 쳐다보았다. 완전히 얼이 빠진 채로. 나는 완전히 제정신이 아니었다 — 고문 아래서 무모하게 굴던 증인처럼 — 무슨 말이든 둘러댔을 것이다.\n\n\"세상에, 그 마차가 대체 어디 있었다는 거냐?\" 누나가 물었다.\n\n\"미스 해비셤의 방에요.\" 그들은 다시 빤히 쳐다보았다. \"그런데 말은 안 매여 있었어요.\" 나는 마구를 채울까 하고 미친 생각을 했던 화려하게 장식한 준마 네 마리를 막판에 물리치며, 이 구원의 단서를 덧붙였다.\n\n\"이게 말이 됩니까, 아저씨?\" 조 부인이 물었다. \"이 애가 대체 무슨 소릴 하는 거지요?\"\n\n\"제가 말씀드리지요, 부인.\" 펌블추크 씨가 말했다. \"제 생각엔 그건 가마입니다. 아시다시피 그분은 변덕스러우니까요 — 아주 변덕스럽지요 — 날을 가마 속에서 보낼 만큼이나 변덕스러운 분이고말고요.\"\n\n\"그분이 그 안에 있는 걸 보신 적 있으세요, 아저씨?\" 조 부인이 물었다.\n\n\"내가 어떻게 보았겠습니까.\" 그가 마지못해 인정하며 대꾸했다. \"평생 그분을 본 적이 없는데 말입니다. 단 한 번도 눈으로 본 적이 없어요!\"\n\n\"어머나, 아저씨! 그런데도 그분과 말씀은 나누셨다고요?\"\n\n\"아니, 모르십니까.\" 펌블추크 씨가 짜증스레 말했다. \"내가 거기 갔을 때, 그분 방문 바깥까지 안내받아 갔고, 문이 빠끔히 열려 있었으며, 그분이 그렇게 문 너머로 내게 말을 건네셨다는 걸 말입니다. 그것까지 모른다고는 하지 마세요, 부인. 어쨌거나, 이 애는 거기 놀러 갔지요. 무슨 놀이를 했느냐, 얘야?\"\n\n\"우리는 깃발 놀이를 했어요.\" 나는 말했다. (이 자리에서 내가 늘어놓은 거짓말들을 떠올리면, 나 자신을 두고 경악하게 된다는 점을 밝혀 두고 싶다.)\n\n\"깃발!\" 누나가 따라 말했다.\n\n\"네.\" 내가 말했다. \"에스텔라는 파란 깃발을 흔들고, 저는 빨간 깃발을 흔들고, 미스 해비셤은 작은 금별이 온통 흩뿌려진 깃발을 마차 창문 밖으로 흔드셨어요. 그러고 나서 우리 모두 칼을 휘두르며 만세를 불렀어요.\"\n\n\"칼이라니!\" 누나가 되뇌었다. \"칼은 또 어디서 났단 말이냐?\"\n\n\"찬장에서요.\" 내가 말했다. \"그리고 그 안에서 권총도 봤어요 — 잼도 — 그리고 알약도요. 그리고 그 방엔 햇빛이 하나도 안 들었는데, 온통 촛불로 환하게 밝혀져 있었어요.\"\n\n\"그건 사실입니다, 부인.\" 펌블추크 씨가 엄숙하게 고개를 끄덕이며 말했다. \"그게 실정이지요. 그 정도는 나도 직접 봤으니까요.\" 그러더니 둘 다 나를 빤히 쳐다보았고, 나는 천연덕스러움을 짐짓 도드라지게 얼굴에 드러낸 채 그들을 빤히 마주 보며, 오른손으로 바지 오른쪽 가랑이를 주름잡아 만지작거렸다.\n\n그들이 내게 질문을 조금만 더 했다면, 나는 틀림없이 스스로 들통이 났을 것이다. 그 순간에도 마당에 기구(氣球)가 있었다는 말까지 꺼내려던 참이었으니까. 다만 내 지어내는 솜씨가 그 진귀한 물건과 양조장에 있던 곰 사이에서 갈라져 있던 덕에 그 말을 무릅쓰지 않았을 뿐이다. 그러나 그들은 내가 이미 그들의 검토거리로 내놓은 그 경이로운 이야기들을 논하느라 너무 몰두해 있던 터라, 나는 무사히 빠져나갔다. 조가 일을 마치고 차 한 잔 마시러 들어왔을 때에도 그 화제는 여전히 두 사람을 붙들고 있었다. 누나는 그의 흥미를 채워 주기보다는 제 마음의 부담을 덜려는 셈으로, 내가 꾸며 낸 경험담을 그에게 들려주었다.\n\n자, 조가 그 푸른 눈을 크게 뜨고 어쩔 줄 모르는 경악에 차서 부엌을 빙 둘러보는 것을 보았을 때, 나는 회한에 사로잡혔다. 그러나 그것은 오직 그에 대해서일 뿐, 다른 두 사람에 대해서는 조금도 그렇지 않았다. 조에 대해서, 오직 조에 대해서만, 나는 스스로를 어린 괴물로 여겼다. 그러는 사이 그들은 미스 해비셤과 가까워지고 그분의 총애를 얻은 일이 내게 어떤 결과를 가져올지를 두고 앉아서 따지고 있었다. 그들은 미스 해비셤이 나를 위해 \"무언가를 해 줄\" 것임을 의심치 않았다. 그들의 의문은 그 무언가가 어떤 형태를 띨 것인가에 관한 것이었다. 누나는 \"재산\"이라는 쪽을 고집했다. 펌블추크 씨는 나를 어느 점잖은 직종 — 이를테면 곡물 종자 장사 같은 직종에 도제로 들여보내는 데 두둑한 수업료를 대 주는 쪽을 지지했다. 조는 어쩌면 송아지 커틀릿을 두고 싸우던 그 개들 가운데 한 마리를 내가 받게 될지도 모른다는 명석한 제안을 내놓았다가, 두 사람 모두에게서 가장 큰 망신을 샀다. \"바보 머리에서 그것보다 나은 의견이 안 나온다면 말이야.\" 누나가 말했다. \"할 일이 있거든, 가서 그 일이나 하는 게 낫겠어.\" 그래서 그는 갔다.\n\n펌블추크 씨가 마차를 몰고 떠난 뒤, 누나가 설거지를 하고 있을 때, 나는 살그머니 대장간으로 조에게 가서, 그가 그날 밤일을 마칠 때까지 곁에 머물렀다. 그러고는 말했다. \"불이 꺼지기 전에요, 조, 아저씨한테 하고 싶은 말이 있어요.\"\n\n\"그래, 핍?\" 조가 편자 박는 걸상을 대장간 가까이 끌어당기며 말했다. \"그럼 말해 봐. 뭔데, 핍?\"\n\n\"조.\" 나는 그의 걷어올린 셔츠 소매를 붙잡고 엄지와 검지 사이로 비비 꼬며 말했다. \"미스 해비셤 댁에 관한 그 얘기들 다 기억하시죠?\"\n\n\"기억하느냐고?\" 조가 말했다. \"암 기억하고말고! 굉장했지!\"\n\n\"그게 끔찍한 일이에요, 조. 사실이 아니에요.\"\n\n\"무슨 소릴 하는 거냐, 핍?\" 조가 더없는 경악에 차서 뒤로 물러서며 외쳤다. \"설마 그게—\"\n\n\"네, 그래요. 거짓말이에요, 조.\"\n\n\"그래도 전부는 아니겠지? 설마, 핍, 검은 벨벳 마—차가 없었다는 건 아니겠지, 응?\" 내가 고개를 가로젓고 서 있었기 때문이다. \"그래도 적어도 개들은 있었지, 핍? 자, 핍.\" 조가 달래듯 말했다. \"송아지 커틀릿은 없었다 쳐도, 적어도 개들은 있었지?\"\n\"없었어요, 조.\"\n\n\"개 한 마리는?\" 조가 말했다. \"강아지 한 마리는? 어때?\"\n\n\"없어요, 조. 그런 건 하나도 없었어요.\"\n\n내가 절망적으로 조에게 눈길을 박자, 조는 당혹스러운 얼굴로 나를 바라보았다. \"핍, 이 친구야! 이러면 못쓴다, 이 사람아! 이거 참! 너 대체 어디로 갈 작정이냐?\"\n\n\"끔찍하지요, 조? 그렇죠?\"\n\n\"끔찍하다고?\" 조가 외쳤다. \"기막힌 노릇이지! 대체 뭐에 씌었던 거냐?\"\n\n\"저도 뭐에 씌었는지 모르겠어요, 조.\" 나는 그의 셔츠 소매를 놓고, 그의 발치 잿더미 위에 주저앉아 고개를 떨군 채 대답했다. \"하지만 아저씨가 카드의 크네이브를 잭이라고 부르는 법을 안 가르쳐 주셨더라면 좋았을걸, 그리고 제 장화가 그렇게 투박하지 않고 제 손이 그렇게 거칠지 않았더라면 좋았을걸 싶어요.\"\n\n그러고 나서 나는 조에게, 내가 몹시 비참한 심정이라고, 그리고 그토록 무례하게 굴던 조 부인과 펌블추크에게 나 자신을 제대로 설명할 수가 없었다고, 그리고 미스 해비셤 댁에 무섭도록 도도한 아름다운 아가씨가 있었다고, 그리고 그 아가씨가 나를 천하다고 했고, 나도 내가 천하다는 걸 알고 있으며, 천하지 않았으면 좋겠다고, 그리고 어찌 된 영문인지는 모르나 그 거짓말들이 거기서 비롯되었다고 말했다.\n\n이것은 형이상학의 문제로, 적어도 나에게 그러했던 만큼이나 조에게도 다루기 어려운 일이었다. 그러나 조는 그 문제를 형이상학의 영역에서 통째로 끄집어냈고, 그렇게 함으로써 그것을 정복했다.\n\n\"한 가지는 너도 확실히 알아 둘 수 있다, 핍.\" 조가 한참 곱씹은 끝에 말했다. \"그러니까, 거짓말은 거짓말이라는 거야. 어떻게 나오든, 나와서는 안 되는 거고, 거짓의 아비한테서 나와서 다시 그쪽으로 돌아가는 법이지. 다시는 그런 걸 늘어놓지 마라, 핍. 그건 천한 신세를 벗어나는 길이 아니야, 이 친구야. 그리고 천하다는 것 말인데, 난 도무지 그게 무슨 소린지 잘 모르겠다. 너는 어떤 면에서는 천하지 않아. 너는 보기 드물게 몸집이 작지. 게다가 너는 보기 드문 학자고.\"\n\n\"아니에요, 저는 무식하고 뒤처졌어요, 조.\"\n\n\"아니, 어젯밤에 네가 쓴 편지를 좀 봐라! 인쇄 글씨로까지 썼잖느냐! 나는 편지를 본 적이 있는데 — 아! 그것도 양반님네한테서 온 편지 말이다! — 그게 인쇄 글씨로 쓰인 게 아니었다고 맹세할 수 있어.\" 조가 말했다.\n\n\"저는 배운 게 거의 없어요, 조. 아저씨가 저를 대단하게 봐 주실 뿐이에요. 그뿐이라고요.\"\n\n\"글쎄, 핍.\" 조가 말했다. \"그렇든 안 그렇든, 보기 드문 학자가 되려면 먼저 평범한 학자부터 되어야 하는 게 아니겠냐, 나는 그렇게 본다! 왕좌에 앉아 머리에 왕관을 쓴 임금님도, 출세 못 한 왕자였던 시절에 알파벳부터 시작하지 않고서는, 가만히 앉아 인쇄 글씨로 의회 법령을 써 내려갈 수 없는 법이야. — 아!\" 조가 의미심장하게 고개를 한 번 가로저으며 덧붙였다. \"그것도 A부터 시작해서, 차근차근 Z까지 나아간 거지. 그게 어떤 일인지 나도 알아, 비록 내가 정확히 그걸 해냈다고는 말 못 하지만 말이다.\"\n\n이 지혜의 한 토막에는 약간의 희망이 깃들어 있었고, 그것은 나를 제법 북돋아 주었다.\n\n\"직업이나 벌이로 따져 천한 사람들이 말이다.\" 조가 사색에 잠겨 말을 이었다. \"천한 사람들끼리 어울려 지내는 편이, 보기 드문 사람들과 놀러 나가는 것보다 나을지도 모른다는 거지 — 그러고 보니 혹시 깃발은 정말 있었으려나 싶기도 한데?\"\n\n\"없었어요, 조.\"\n\n\"(깃발이 없었다니 안됐다, 핍.) 그게 그렇든 안 그렇든, 그건 지금 따져 볼 수 없는 일이야. 네 누나를 불같이 길길이 날뛰게 하지 않고서는 말이지. 그건 일부러 저질러서는 안 될 일이고. 자 봐라, 핍, 참된 친구가 너한테 하는 말을. 이건 참된 친구가 너한테 하는 말이야. 곧게 가서도 보기 드문 사람이 못 된다면, 굽은 길로 가서는 결코 그렇게 되지 못해. 그러니 다시는 그런 걸 늘어놓지 마라, 핍, 그리고 잘 살고 행복하게 죽으렴.\"\n\n\"저한테 화나신 거 아니지요, 조?\"\n\n\"아니다, 이 친구야. 다만 내가 말하려던 그것들이 깜짝 놀랄 만큼 엉뚱한 종류였다는 걸 새겨 둔다면 말이다 — 송아지 커틀릿이니 개싸움이니 하는 데 걸쳐 있던 그것들 말이야 — 진심으로 너를 위하는 사람이라면, 핍, 그것들을 곰곰이 따져 보거라 충고하겠다, 네가 이층으로 잠자리에 올라갈 때 말이야. 그게 다다, 이 친구야, 그리고 다시는 절대 그러지 마라.\"\n\n내 작은 방으로 올라가 기도를 드릴 때, 나는 조의 충고를 잊지 않았다. 그러나 내 어린 마음은 그토록 어지럽고 감사할 줄 모르는 상태였던지라, 자리에 누운 뒤로도 한참을 이런 생각에 잠겼다. 에스텔라가 한낱 대장장이인 조를 얼마나 천하게 여길까, 그의 장화는 얼마나 투박하며 그의 손은 얼마나 거칠까. 나는 생각했다. 조와 누나가 지금 부엌에 앉아 있고, 나는 그 부엌에서 잠자리로 올라왔는데, 미스 해비셤과 에스텔라는 결코 부엌 따위에 앉는 법 없이 그런 천한 일거리들보다 한참 높은 곳에 있구나 하고. 나는 미스 해비셤 댁에 있을 때 \"늘 하곤 했던\" 일들을 떠올리며 잠이 들었다. 마치 몇 시간이 아니라 몇 주, 몇 달을 거기 머물렀던 것처럼. 그리고 마치 그것이 바로 그날에야 비로소 생겨난 일이 아니라, 아주 오래된 추억거리이기라도 한 것처럼.\n\n그날은 내게 잊지 못할 날이었다. 그날이 내 안에 커다란 변화를 일으켰기 때문이다. 그러나 어느 인생이든 마찬가지다. 그 가운데서 어느 하루를 골라 도려냈다고 상상해 보라, 그리고 그 행로가 얼마나 달라졌을지를 생각해 보라. 이 글을 읽는 그대여, 잠시 멈추어, 어느 잊지 못할 하루에 첫 고리 하나가 만들어지지 않았더라면 결코 그대를 옭아매지 못했을, 쇠로 된 혹은 금으로 된, 가시로 된 혹은 꽃으로 된 그 긴 사슬을 한순간 떠올려 보라.\n\n# Chapter X.\n\n하루나 이틀쯤 뒤 어느 날 아침 잠에서 깨었을 때, 나는 행복한 착상 하나를 떠올렸다. 내가 비범해지기 위해 밟을 수 있는 최선의 길은 비디가 아는 것을 모조리 캐내는 것이라는 생각이었다. 이 빛나는 구상을 좇아, 밤에 우프슬 씨의 대고모님 댁에 갔을 때 나는 비디에게, 내가 출세하고 싶은 특별한 이유가 있노라고, 그러니 비디가 자기 학식을 전부 내게 나누어 준다면 더없이 고맙겠노라고 일러두었다. 비디는 더할 나위 없이 친절한 소녀였으므로, 곧장 그러겠노라 대답하고는, 정말로 오 분도 채 지나지 않아 그 약속을 실행하기 시작했다.\n\n우프슬 씨의 대고모님이 세운 교육 제도 혹은 교과 과정은 다음과 같은 개요로 정리할 수 있다. 학생들은 우프슬 씨의 대고모님이 기운을 모아 자작나무 회초리를 들고 마구잡이로 비틀거리며 달려들기 전까지, 사과를 먹고 서로의 등에 지푸라기를 찔러 넣었다. 그 공격을 온갖 비웃음의 기색으로 받아 낸 다음, 학생들은 한 줄로 늘어서서 웅성거리며 너덜너덜한 책 한 권을 손에서 손으로 넘겼다. 그 책에는 알파벳과 약간의 숫자와 표, 그리고 약간의 철자법이 들어 있었다 — 다시 말해, 한때는 들어 있었다. 이 책이 돌기 시작하기가 무섭게 우프슬 씨의 대고모님은 혼수 상태에 빠졌는데, 그것은 잠 때문이거나 류머티즘 발작 때문이었다. 그러면 학생들끼리 부츠를 주제로 한 경쟁 시험에 돌입하여, 누가 누구의 발가락을 가장 세게 밟을 수 있는지를 가려냈다. 이 정신 수양은 비디가 학생들에게 달려들어, 보기 흉하게 일그러진 성경책 세 권(무언가의 토막 난 끝을 서투르게 잘라 낸 듯한 모양이었다)을 나누어 줄 때까지 이어졌다. 그 성경책들은 잘 봐주어도 내가 그 후 마주친 그 어떤 문헌 진품보다도 알아보기 어렵게 인쇄되어 있었고, 온통 쇠녹 얼룩으로 점점이 뒤덮여 있었으며, 책장 사이에는 곤충 세계의 갖가지 표본이 짓눌려 있었다. 이 과정의 단계는 대개 비디와 고분고분하지 않은 학생들 사이의 일대일 격투 몇 차례로 활기를 띠었다. 싸움이 끝나면 비디가 쪽 번호를 알려 주었고, 그러면 우리 모두는 읽을 수 있는 것을 — 혹은 읽을 수 없는 것을 — 무시무시한 합창으로 소리 내어 읽었다. 비디가 높고 날카롭고 단조로운 목소리로 선창했으며, 우리 가운데 누구도 우리가 무엇을 읽고 있는지 조금도 알지 못했고, 그것을 향한 경외심 따위도 전혀 없었다. 이 끔찍한 소음이 일정 시간 이어지면, 그것이 기계적으로 우프슬 씨의 대고모님을 깨웠다. 대고모님은 우연히 가까이 있던 한 소년에게 비틀거리며 달려들어 그 아이의 귀를 잡아당겼다. 이것은 그날 저녁 과정의 종료를 뜻하는 것으로 이해되었고, 우리는 지적 승리의 환호성을 지르며 바깥 공기 속으로 쏟아져 나왔다. 공평하게 짚어 두자면, 어떤 학생이 석판으로, 혹은 잉크가 있을 때는 잉크로 스스로를 즐기는 것을 금하는 규정은 없었다. 다만 겨울철에는 그 학문의 갈래를 추구하기가 쉽지 않았는데, 수업이 열리던 그 자그마한 잡화점이 — 그곳은 동시에 우프슬 씨의 대고모님의 거실이자 침실이기도 했다 — 심지 자르는 가위도 없이 기운 없이 타들어가는 양초 한 자루의 힘을 빌려 어슴푸레하게만 밝혀져 있었기 때문이다.\n\n이런 형편에서는 비범해지는 데 시간이 걸리리라는 생각이 들었다. 그럼에도 나는 한번 해 보기로 결심했고, 바로 그날 저녁 비디는 우리의 특별 협정에 착수하여, 황설탕 항목 아래 자기의 자그마한 물품 시세표에서 얼마간의 정보를 일러 주고, 어느 신문 제호에서 본떠 그린 커다란 고풍의 영어 대문자 D를 집에서 베껴 쓰라며 내게 빌려주었다. 그것이 무엇인지 비디가 일러 주기 전까지 나는 그것을 어느 버클의 도안인 줄로만 알았다.\n\n물론 마을에는 술집이 하나 있었고, 물론 조는 이따금 그곳에서 담배 피우기를 좋아했다. 그날 저녁 나는 학교에서 돌아오는 길에 쾌활한 세 뱃사공 술집에 들러 조를 찾아내, 무슨 일이 있어도 그를 집에 데려오라는 누나의 엄한 명령을 받은 터였다. 그리하여 나는 쾌활한 세 뱃사공 술집으로 발걸음을 옮겼다.\n\n쾌활한 뱃사공 술집에는 바가 하나 있었는데, 문 옆 벽에는 분필로 적힌 외상 장부의 길고 섬뜩한 기록들이 있었고, 그것들은 내 눈에 결코 갚아지는 법이 없는 것처럼 보였다. 그 기록들은 내가 기억하는 한 줄곧 그 자리에 있었고, 나보다도 더 많이 자라 있었다. 그러나 우리 고장에는 분필이 넉넉히 났으니, 어쩌면 사람들이 그것을 써먹을 기회를 하나도 놓치지 않았는지도 모를 일이었다.\n\n토요일 밤이었으므로, 나는 주인이 그 기록들을 다소 음울한 표정으로 바라보고 있는 것을 보았다. 그러나 내 볼일은 그가 아니라 조에게 있었으므로, 나는 그저 안녕하시냐는 인사만 건네고 복도 끝의 공동 방으로 들어갔다. 그곳에는 환하고 큼직한 부엌 난롯불이 타고 있었고, 조가 우프슬 씨와 어느 낯선 사내와 함께 담배를 피우고 있었다. 조는 여느 때처럼 \"여어, 핍, 우리 꼬마 친구!\" 하고 나를 맞이했는데, 그가 그렇게 말하는 순간 낯선 사내가 고개를 돌려 나를 바라보았다.\n\n처음 보는, 어딘가 비밀스러워 보이는 사내였다. 머리는 온통 한쪽으로 기울어져 있었고, 한쪽 눈은 보이지 않는 총으로 무언가를 겨누기라도 하는 양 반쯤 감겨 있었다. 입에는 담뱃대를 물고 있었는데, 그것을 빼더니 천천히 연기를 다 내뿜으며 내내 나를 뚫어지게 쳐다본 끝에 고개를 끄덕였다. 그래서 나도 고개를 끄덕였고, 그러자 그는 다시 한 번 끄덕이고는 내가 거기 앉을 수 있도록 자기 옆자리 긴 의자를 비워 주었다.\n\n그러나 나는 그 휴식처에 들를 때면 늘 조의 옆에 앉는 버릇이 있었으므로 \"아니에요, 고맙습니다, 아저씨\" 하고는, 맞은편 긴 의자에 조가 내게 마련해 준 자리에 끼어 앉았다. 낯선 사내는 조를 흘끗 보고 그의 주의가 다른 데 쏠려 있는 것을 확인하더니, 내가 자리를 잡자 다시 한 번 내게 고개를 끄덕이고는 자기 다리를 문질렀다 — 내가 보기에 무척 묘한 방식이었다.\n\n\"댁이 말씀하시기를,\" 낯선 사내가 조에게로 몸을 돌리며 말했다. \"대장장이라고 하셨지요.\"\n\n\"그렇소. 내가 그렇게 말했지요.\" 조가 말했다.\n\n\"뭘 드시겠소, 미스터…? 그러고 보니 성함을 말씀 안 하셨군요.\"\n\n조가 이제 자기 이름을 댔고, 낯선 사내는 그를 그 이름으로 불렀다. \"뭘 드시겠소, 가저리 씨? 내가 한턱내리다. 마무리로 한잔하시지요?\"\n\n\"글쎄요,\" 조가 말했다. \"솔직히 말하자면, 나는 내 돈 말고 남의 돈으로 술 마시는 버릇이 별로 없소이다.\"\n\n\"버릇이라? 그렇겠지요.\" 낯선 사내가 대꾸했다. \"하지만 어쩌다 한 번쯤은, 게다가 토요일 밤이기도 한데. 자! 뭘 드실지 대 보시오, 가저리 씨.\"\n\n\"뻣뻣한 술벗이 되긴 싫으니,\" 조가 말했다. \"럼주로 하지요.\"\n\n\"럼주라.\" 낯선 사내가 되뇌었다. \"그럼 저 다른 신사분도 한마디 거드시겠소?\"\n\n\"럼주요.\" 우프슬 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"럼주 세 잔!\" 낯선 사내가 주인을 부르며 외쳤다. \"다들 한 잔씩!\"\n\n\"이 다른 신사분으로 말하자면,\" 조가 우프슬 씨를 소개하려는 듯 말했다. \"낭송하시는 걸 들으면 댁이 마음에 들어 하실 분이오. 우리 교회 서기시지요.\"\n\n\"아하!\" 낯선 사내가 재빨리 말하며 내게 눈을 치켜떴다. \"그 외딴 교회, 무덤들에 둘러싸인 채 습지 한복판에 덩그러니 있는 그 교회 말이로군요!\"\n\n\"바로 그렇소.\" 조가 말했다.\n\n낯선 사내는 담뱃대를 물고 만족스러운 듯한 끙 소리를 내며, 혼자 차지한 긴 의자 위로 두 다리를 올려놓았다. 그는 챙이 넓어 펄럭이는 여행자용 모자를 쓰고 있었고, 그 아래에는 손수건을 모자처럼 머리에 둘러 묶고 있어서 머리카락이 전혀 보이지 않았다. 그가 불을 바라볼 때, 나는 그의 얼굴에 교활한 표정이 떠오르고 이어 반쯤 웃는 기색이 스치는 것을 본 듯했다.\n\n\"나는 이 고장을 잘 모르오, 여러분, 하지만 강 쪽으로 갈수록 적막한 고장 같소이다.\"\n\n\"습지란 대개 적막하지요.\" 조가 말했다.\n\n\"그렇겠지요, 그렇겠지요. 그래, 저 바깥에서 집시나 떠돌이, 혹은 그 비슷한 부랑자 같은 자들을 더러 보시오?\"\n\n\"아니오,\" 조가 말했다. \"이따금 탈주한 죄수나 보일 뿐이지요. 게다가 그놈들도 쉽게 찾아내지는 못하오. 그렇지요, 우프슬 씨?\"\n\n우프슬 씨는 옛 낭패의 위엄 어린 기억을 떠올리며 동의했으나, 그리 열의가 담기진 않았다.\n\n\"그런 자들을 잡으러 나가 보신 적이 있는 모양이군요?\" 낯선 사내가 물었다.\n\n\"한 번 있소.\" 조가 대꾸했다. \"잡고 싶어서 그런 건 아니었지요, 아시겠소만. 우리는 구경꾼으로 따라나선 거였소. 나하고, 우프슬 씨하고, 핍이 말이오. 안 그러냐, 핍?\"\n\n\"맞아요, 조.\"\n\n낯선 사내는 다시 나를 바라보았는데 — 마치 보이지 않는 총으로 일부러 나를 겨누기라도 하는 양 여전히 눈을 치켜뜬 채로 — 이렇게 말했다. \"제법 쓸 만한 어린 뼈다귀 보따리로군. 얘를 뭐라고 부르시오?\"\n\n\"핍이오.\" 조가 말했다.\n\n\"세례명이 핍이오?\"\n\n\"아니, 세례명이 핍은 아니오.\"\n\n\"성이 핍이오?\"\n\n\"아니오,\" 조가 말했다. \"그건 얘가 갓난아기였을 때 스스로 지어 붙인 일종의 집안 별칭 같은 거라서, 그렇게 부르는 거요.\"\n\n\"댁 아들이오?\"\n\n\"글쎄요,\" 조가 곰곰이 생각하며 말했는데, 물론 그것을 두고 어떤 식으로든 곰곰이 따져 볼 필요가 있어서가 아니라, 쾌활한 뱃사공 술집에서는 담뱃대를 물고 오가는 모든 화제를 깊이 숙고하는 척하는 것이 관례였기 때문이다. \"글쎄요 — 아니오. 아니, 얘는 아니오.\"\n\n\"조카요?\" 낯선 사내가 말했다.\n\n\"글쎄요,\" 조가 여전히 깊디깊은 사색에 잠긴 듯한 모습으로 말했다. \"얘는 아니오 — 아니, 댁을 속이지 않고 말하자면, 얘는 내 조카가 아니오.\"\n\n\"그럼 도대체 얘는 뭐요?\" 낯선 사내가 물었다. 그것은 내게 쓸데없이 거센 물음으로 들렸다.\n\n거기서 우프슬 씨가 끼어들었다. 그는 남자가 어떤 여자 친척과 혼인할 수 없는지를 직업상 늘 염두에 두어야 했던 까닭에, 친족 관계라면 죄다 꿰뚫고 있는 사람답게, 나와 조 사이의 인연을 풀어 설명했다. 내친김에 우프슬 씨는 「리처드 3세」에서 무시무시하게 으르렁대는 한 대목을 읊으며 마무리 지었고, \"— 시인이 말하듯이 말이오\" 하고 덧붙이고는 그것으로 충분히 설명이 되었다고 여기는 듯했다.\n\n여기서 한마디 짚어 두자면, 우프슬 씨가 나를 언급할 때면 으레 내 머리카락을 헝클어뜨려 두 눈을 찌르는 것을 그런 언급에 빠져선 안 될 절차로 여겼다는 점이다. 우리 집을 찾아오는 그 정도 지위의 사람들이 어째서 비슷한 상황에서 늘 내게 같은 자극적인 처치를 가했는지, 나로서는 도무지 까닭을 헤아릴 수 없다. 그렇지만 더 어렸던 시절, 우리 사교적인 식구 모임에서 내가 화제에 오를 때마다 어김없이 어떤 손 큰 인물이 나를 우대한답시고 그런 안과적인 조처를 취하지 않은 적이 있었던 기억은 떠오르지 않는다.\n\n이 모든 동안, 낯선 사내는 나 말고는 아무도 보지 않았고, 마침내 나를 한 방 쏘아 거꾸러뜨리기로 작정한 듯한 눈빛으로 나를 바라보았다. 그러나 도대체 얘는 뭐냐는 발언을 던진 뒤로는, 럼주와 물이 든 잔들이 올 때까지 아무 말도 하지 않았다. 그러더니 그는 한 방을 쏘았는데, 그것은 더없이 기이한 한 방이었다.\n\n그것은 말로 하는 발언이 아니라 무언극으로 이루어진 동작이었고, 분명하게 나를 겨냥한 것이었다. 그는 나를 똑바로 향한 채 럼주에 물을 탄 술을 저었고, 나를 똑바로 향한 채 그것을 맛보았다. 그가 그것을 젓고 맛볼 때 쓴 것은, 그에게 가져다준 숟가락이 아니라 줄칼이었다.\n\n그는 나 말고는 아무도 그 줄칼을 보지 못하도록 그렇게 했다. 그러고 나서 그 줄칼을 닦아 가슴 안주머니에 넣었다. 그 도구를 본 순간 나는 그것이 조의 줄칼이라는 것을, 그리고 이 사내가 내 죄수를 알고 있다는 것을 알아차렸다. 나는 넋이 나간 채 그를 바라보며 앉아 있었다. 그러나 이제 그는 긴 의자에 비스듬히 기댄 채, 나에게는 거의 눈길도 주지 않고 주로 순무 이야기만 늘어놓았다.\n\n토요일 밤이면 우리 마을에는 말끔히 치우고 나서, 다시 살아가기 시작하기에 앞서 잠시 조용히 한숨 돌리는 흐뭇한 정취가 감돌았는데, 그것이 조를 부추겨 토요일에는 다른 때보다 반 시간쯤 더 밖에 머물 용기를 내게 했다. 그 반 시간과 럼주에 물 탄 술이 함께 바닥나자, 조는 일어나 자리를 뜨며 내 손을 잡았다.\n\n\"잠깐만 더 계시오, 가저리 씨.\" 낯선 사내가 말했다. \"내 주머니 어딘가에 반짝이는 새 실링이 한 닢 있을 성싶은데, 만약 있다면 저 아이에게 줘야겠소.\"\n\n그는 한 줌의 잔돈 가운데서 그것을 골라내어 구겨진 종이에 싼 다음 내게 건넸다. \"네 거다!\" 그가 말했다. \"잘 들어 둬! 네 것이야.\"\n\n나는 예의의 한도를 한참 넘어서까지 그를 빤히 쳐다보며, 조에게 바짝 매달린 채 고맙다고 인사했다. 그는 조에게 잘 가라고 인사하고, 우프슬 씨에게도(우프슬 씨는 우리와 함께 나왔다) 잘 가라고 인사했으나, 내게는 그 겨누는 눈으로 눈길 한 번만을 던졌다 — 아니, 눈길이라 할 수도 없었으니 그는 그 눈을 감아 버렸기 때문이다. 그러나 눈은 그것을 감춤으로써 놀라운 일을 해낼 수 있는 법이다.\n\n집으로 돌아가는 길에, 내가 이야기할 기분이었다 한들 그 이야기는 죄다 내 몫이었을 것이다. 우프슬 씨는 쾌활한 뱃사공 술집 문간에서 우리와 헤어졌고, 조는 집까지 줄곧 입을 떡 벌린 채로 걸으며 가능한 한 많은 공기로 럼주를 씻어 내려 했기 때문이다. 그러나 나는 오래전의 내 잘못과 오래전의 아는 자가 이렇게 불쑥 다시 나타난 일에 어찌 보면 멍해져, 다른 어떤 것도 생각할 수 없었다.\n\n우리가 부엌에 모습을 드러냈을 때 누나는 그리 험악한 기분은 아니었고, 그 보기 드문 상황에 용기를 얻은 조가 누나에게 반짝이는 실링 이야기를 했다. \"분명 위조 돈일 거야,\" 조 부인이 의기양양하게 말했다. \"아니라면 아이한테 줬을 리가 없지! 어디 좀 봅시다.\"\n\n내가 종이에서 그것을 꺼내 보니 멀쩡한 진짜 동전이었다. \"그런데 이건 뭐지?\" 조 부인이 실링을 내던지고 그 종이를 낚아채며 말했다. \"1파운드짜리 지폐가 두 장이라고?\"\n\n다름 아닌, 두툼하게 땀이라도 밴 듯한 1파운드짜리 지폐 두 장이었는데, 그것은 이 주의 온갖 가축 시장과 더없이 따뜻한 친분이라도 나눈 듯한 모양새였다. 조는 다시 모자를 집어 들고 그 지폐들을 주인에게 돌려주려고 쾌활한 뱃사공 술집으로 달려갔다. 그가 간 사이, 나는 늘 앉던 걸상에 앉아 누나를 멍하니 바라보며, 그 사내가 거기 없으리라는 것을 꽤 확신하고 있었다.\n\n이윽고 조가 돌아와, 사내는 떠나고 없었으나 자기, 곧 조가 그 지폐에 관해 쾌활한 세 뱃사공 술집에 말을 남겨 두었노라고 했다. 그러자 누나는 그 지폐들을 종이에 싸서 봉한 다음, 격식 갖춘 응접실 찬장 위에 놓인 장식용 찻주전자 안, 마른 장미 잎 밑에 넣어 두었다. 그 지폐들은 거기 머물며, 숱한 밤낮 동안 내게 악몽이 되었다.\n\n잠자리에 들었을 때 나는 잠을 몹시 설쳤다. 그 낯선 사내가 보이지 않는 총으로 나를 겨누던 일이며, 죄수들과 은밀히 공모하는 사이가 된다는 것이 얼마나 죄스럽고 거칠고 천한 일인가 하는 생각 때문이었는데 — 그것은 내 비루한 행적 중 한 대목으로, 그전에는 잊고 있던 것이었다. 줄칼 역시 나를 따라다니며 괴롭혔다. 가장 방심한 순간에 그 줄칼이 다시 나타나리라는 두려움이 나를 사로잡았다. 나는 다음 수요일 해비셤 양 댁에 갈 일을 떠올리며 스스로를 어르고 달래 잠에 빠져들었으나, 꿈속에서 나는 누가 들고 있는지는 보이지 않은 채 어느 문에서 줄칼이 나를 향해 다가오는 것을 보았고, 비명을 지르다 스스로 잠에서 깨었다.\n\n# Chapter XI.\n\n정해진 시각에 나는 다시 해비셤 양의 집으로 갔고, 문 앞에서 머뭇거리며 종을 울리자 에스텔러가 나왔다. 그녀는 지난번처럼 나를 들인 뒤 문을 잠그고, 다시금 양초가 놓인 어두운 복도로 앞장서 나를 데려갔다. 그녀는 촛불을 손에 들 때까지 나를 거들떠보지도 않다가, 손에 들고 나서야 어깨 너머로 돌아보며 거만하게 말했다. \"오늘은 이쪽으로 와.\" 그러고는 나를 집의 전혀 다른 부분으로 데려갔다.\n\n복도는 길었고, 저택의 네모진 지하층 전체에 두루 뻗어 있는 듯했다. 그러나 우리는 그 네모의 한 면만을 가로질렀고, 그 끝에서 그녀는 걸음을 멈추고 촛불을 내려놓더니 문 하나를 열었다. 여기에서 햇빛이 다시 나타났고, 나는 어느 작은 포석 깔린 안뜰에 들어와 있는 나 자신을 발견했는데, 그 맞은편은 한 채의 별채로 이루어져 있었으니, 그 별채는 한때 폐업한 양조장의 지배인이나 수석 사무원의 거처였던 것처럼 보였다. 이 집의 바깥벽에는 시계가 하나 있었다. 해비셤 양의 방에 있는 시계처럼, 또 해비셤 양의 회중시계처럼, 그것 역시 아홉 시 이십 분 전에 멈춰 있었다.\n\n우리는 열려 있던 문으로 들어가, 뒤편 일층에 자리한 천장이 낮고 음울한 방으로 들어섰다. 방 안에는 손님 몇이 있었고, 에스텔러는 그들 사이로 끼어들며 내게 말했다. \"꼬마야, 너는 가서 저기 서 있다가 부르면 와.\" 그 \"저기\"란 창가였으므로, 나는 창가로 건너가, 더없이 불편한 심정으로 \"저기\" 서서 바깥을 내다보았다.\n\n창문은 바닥까지 열려 있어, 방치된 정원의 더없이 비참한 한구석을, 무성하게 썩어 가는 양배추 줄기들과, 오래전 푸딩처럼 둥그렇게 다듬어졌으나 꼭대기에 모양도 빛깔도 다른 새 가지가 돋아난 회양목 한 그루를 내다보고 있었으니, 마치 그 푸딩의 그 부분이 냄비에 들러붙어 타 버린 것 같았다. 회양목을 바라보며 떠오른 것이 이런 소박한 생각이었다. 간밤에 가벼운 눈이 내렸는데, 내가 아는 한 다른 곳에는 눈이 쌓이지 않았건만, 이 정원 한 귀퉁이의 차가운 그늘에서는 다 녹지 않은 채로 남아 있었고, 바람이 그것을 작은 소용돌이로 휘말아 올려 창문에 흩뿌렸으니, 마치 내가 거기 온 것을 두고 나를 매질하는 듯했다.\n\n내가 온 탓에 방 안의 대화가 멈추었고, 다른 사람들이 나를 바라보고 있다는 것을 나는 짐작했다. 창유리에 비치는 불빛 외에는 방 안의 어떤 것도 볼 수 없었지만, 나는 면밀한 검열을 받고 있다는 의식에 온몸의 관절이 빳빳하게 굳었다.\n\n방에는 부인이 셋, 신사가 하나 있었다. 내가 창가에 선 지 오 분도 채 지나지 않아, 그들은 어떻게든 내게 이런 인상을 전해 왔다. 그들 모두가 아첨꾼이자 사기꾼이라는 것을, 그러나 저마다 다른 이들이 아첨꾼이자 사기꾼임을 모르는 척한다는 것을. 자기가 그것을 안다고 인정하는 순간, 자기 자신이 아첨꾼이자 사기꾼임을 드러내는 셈이 되기 때문이었다.\n\n그들 모두 누군가의 기분을 기다리는 듯한 나른하고 음울한 태도를 띠고 있었고, 부인들 가운데 가장 말이 많은 이는 하품을 억누르느라 매우 딱딱하게 말을 해야 했다. 카밀라라는 이름의 이 부인은 우리 누나를 몹시 떠올리게 했는데, 다만 그녀가 더 나이가 많고, (그녀를 흘끗 보았을 때 알았듯이) 이목구비가 더 무딘 꼴이라는 점이 달랐다. 사실, 그녀를 더 잘 알게 되었을 때, 나는 그녀에게 이목구비라는 게 그나마 있다는 것 자체가 자비라는 생각이 들기 시작했다. 그녀의 얼굴이라는 죽은 벽은 그토록 텅 비고 그토록 높았으니까.\n\n\"가엾은 양반!\" 이 부인이, 영락없이 우리 누나 같은 불쑥거리는 태도로 말했다. \"남이 아니라 제 자신이 원수인 사람이지!\"\n\n\"차라리 남의 원수가 되는 편이 훨씬 칭찬받을 만하지요.\" 신사가 말했다. \"그게 훨씬 자연스러운 일이고요.\"\n\n\"레이먼드 사촌.\" 또 다른 부인이 말했다. \"우리는 이웃을 사랑해야 하는 거예요.\"\n\n\"세라 포켓.\" 레이먼드 사촌이 받았다. \"사람이 자기 자신의 이웃이 아니라면, 누가 이웃이겠소?\"\n\n포켓 양이 웃었고, 카밀라도 웃으며 말했다(하품을 참으면서). \"그런 생각을 다!\" 그러나 내가 보기에는 그들도 그것을 꽤 그럴듯한 생각이라 여기는 듯했다. 아직 말을 하지 않았던 또 다른 부인이 엄숙하고 힘주어 말했다. \"지당하신 말씀!\"\n\n\"가엾은 양반!\" 곧이어 카밀라가 말을 이었다(그러는 동안 그들 모두 나를 보고 있었다는 것을 나는 알았다). \"그이는 어찌나 별난지! 톰의 아내가 죽었을 때, 아이들 상복에 가장 깊은 가두리 장식을 다는 일의 중요성을 그이가 도무지 깨닫지 못했다는 걸 누가 믿겠어요? '맙소사!' 하고 그이가 말하더군요. '카밀라, 가엾게 부모 잃은 어린것들이 검은 옷만 입고 있으면 됐지, 그게 무슨 대수란 말이오?' 영락없는 매슈예요! 그런 생각을 다!\"\n\n\"좋은 점도 있지요, 좋은 점도 있어요.\" 레이먼드 사촌이 말했다. \"그이에게 좋은 점이 있다는 걸 내가 부정할 리가 없지요. 하지만 그이는 예의범절이란 걸 가져 본 적도 없고, 앞으로도 가질 일이 없을 겁니다.\"\n\n\"내가 어쩔 수 없이,\" 카밀라가 말했다. \"내가 어쩔 수 없이 단호해야 했던 거예요. 내가 그랬지요. '그건 안 돼요. 집안의 체면을 봐서라도.' 깊은 가두리 장식이 없으면 집안이 망신을 당한다고 그이에게 말했어요. 그 일로 아침부터 저녁까지 울었답니다. 소화에 탈이 날 지경이었어요. 그러다 끝내 그이가 그 거친 성미로 화를 벌컥 내며, 입에 담지 못할 욕까지 섞어 말하더군요. '그럼 당신 좋을 대로 하시오.' 그래도 내가 곧장 퍼붓는 빗속에 나가서 그것들을 사 왔다는 걸 안다는 게, 두고두고 내게 위안이 되리란 점은 다행한 일이지요.\"\n\n\"그 값은 그이가 치렀잖아요, 안 그래요?\" 에스텔러가 물었다.\n\n\"누가 값을 치렀느냐가 문제가 아니란다, 얘야.\" 카밀라가 받았다. \"내가 그것들을 샀어. 그리고 나는 한밤에 깨어났을 때 그 일을 평온한 마음으로 자주 떠올리게 될 거란다.\"\n\n멀리서 종이 울리는 소리가, 내가 들어온 그 복도를 따라 메아리치는 어떤 외침이나 부름 소리와 어우러져, 대화를 끊어 놓았고, 에스텔러로 하여금 내게 이렇게 말하게 했다. \"자, 꼬마야!\" 내가 돌아서자, 그들 모두 더없는 경멸의 눈으로 나를 바라보았고, 내가 나갈 때 세라 포켓이 \"원, 세상에! 다음엔 또 뭘!\" 하고 말하는 것이 들렸으며, 카밀라가 분개하여 덧붙였다. \"저런 별난 생각은 또 처음일세! 별, 생, 각, 을, 다!\"\n\n촛불을 들고 어두운 복도를 따라 가던 중, 에스텔러는 별안간 걸음을 멈추더니, 홱 돌아서서, 제 얼굴을 내 얼굴에 바싹 들이대고는, 그 비웃는 태도로 말했다.\n\n\"그래서?\"\n\n\"네? 무슨 말이에요, 아가씨?\" 나는 그녀에게 부딪칠 뻔하다가 가까스로 멈춰 서며 대답했다.\n\n그녀는 서서 나를 바라보았고, 나도 물론 서서 그녀를 바라보았다.\n\n\"나 예뻐?\"\n\n\"응, 아주 예쁜 것 같아.\"\n\n\"내가 모욕적이야?\"\n\n\"지난번만큼은 아니야.\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"그렇게 심하진 않다고?\"\n\n\"응.\"\n\n마지막 질문을 던질 때 그녀는 발끈했고, 내가 대답하자 있는 힘껏 내 뺨을 후려쳤다.\n\n\"지금은?\" 그녀가 말했다. \"이 천박한 작은 괴물아, 지금은 내가 어떻게 보여?\"\n\n\"말하지 않을 거야.\"\n\n\"위층에 가서 일러바칠 거니까 그렇지. 그렇지?\"\n\n\"아니야.\" 내가 말했다. \"그게 아니야.\"\n\n\"왜 또 안 우는 거야, 이 가엾은 것아?\"\n\n\"다시는 너 때문에 울지 않을 테니까.\" 내가 말했다. 짐작건대 그것은 일찍이 내뱉어진 그 어떤 선언만큼이나 거짓된 선언이었다. 왜냐하면 나는 그때 속으로 그녀 때문에 울고 있었고, 그 후로 그녀가 내게 안긴 고통이 어떠했는지를 나는 잘 알고 있으니까.\n\n이 일이 있은 뒤 우리는 위층으로 올라갔다. 올라가던 중, 우리는 더듬더듬 길을 찾아 내려오는 한 신사와 마주쳤다.\n\n\"여기 이건 누구냐?\" 신사가 걸음을 멈추고 나를 보며 물었다.\n\n\"남자애예요.\" 에스텔러가 말했다.\n\n그는 안색이 지독히도 가무잡잡한 건장한 사내로, 머리가 유난히 컸고, 거기에 걸맞게 손도 컸다. 그는 그 큰 손으로 내 턱을 잡고 촛불에 비춰 내 얼굴을 들여다보았다. 머리 꼭대기는 일찍부터 벗어졌고, 검고 숱 많은 눈썹은 가라앉을 줄 모르고 빳빳하게 곤두서 있었다. 두 눈은 머릿속 깊이 박혀 있었고, 불쾌하리만치 날카롭고 의심에 차 있었다. 그는 커다란 회중시계 줄을 차고 있었고, 수염을 길렀더라면 턱수염과 구레나룻이 났을 자리에는 거뭇거뭇하고 굵은 점들이 박혀 있었다. 그는 내게 아무것도 아닌 사람이었고, 그때 나는 그가 언젠가 내게 어떤 의미를 지닌 사람이 되리라고는 조금도 내다볼 수 없었지만, 마침 나는 그를 잘 관찰할 이 기회를 얻은 것이었다.\n\n\"이 동네 아이냐? 응?\" 그가 말했다.\n\n\"네, 그렇습니다.\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"넌 어떻게 여기 왔지?\"\n\n\"해비셤 양이 부르셨어요.\" 내가 설명했다.\n\n\"좋아! 행실 바르게 굴어라. 나는 사내아이들이라면 꽤 많이 겪어 봤는데, 너희는 못된 족속이거든. 자, 명심해!\" 그가 나를 향해 인상을 찌푸리며 제 굵은 집게손가락 옆구리를 깨물고 말했다. \"행실을 바르게 해!\"\n\n그 말과 함께 그는 나를 놓아주었는데 — 그의 손에서 향내 나는 비누 냄새가 났으므로 나는 그것이 반가웠다 — 그러고는 제 갈 길로 아래층으로 내려갔다. 나는 그가 의사일까 생각해 보았으나, 아니지, 하고 생각을 고쳤다. 의사일 리가 없어, 의사라면 좀 더 조용하고 좀 더 설득력 있는 태도를 지녔을 테니까. 그 문제를 곰곰 생각할 겨를은 많지 않았으니, 우리는 곧 해비셤 양의 방에 이르렀고, 그곳의 그녀와 다른 모든 것은 내가 떠났을 때 그대로였다. 에스텔러는 나를 문 가까이 세워 두고 떠났고, 나는 해비셤 양이 화장대에서 내게 눈길을 던질 때까지 거기 서 있었다.\n\n\"그래!\" 그녀가 놀라거나 당황하는 기색 없이 말했다. \"날이 다 지나갔구나, 그렇지?\"\n\n\"네, 아주머니. 오늘은—\"\n\n\"됐다, 됐어, 됐어!\" 손가락을 조바심치며 놀리면서 그녀가 말했다. \"알고 싶지 않다. 놀 준비는 됐느냐?\"\n\n나는 다소 당황하여 대답하지 않을 수 없었다. \"그럴 것 같지 않아요, 아주머니.\"\n\n\"또 카드는 안 되겠다는 거냐?\" 그녀가 살피는 눈빛으로 따져 물었다.\n\n\"네, 아주머니. 시키시면 그건 할 수 있어요.\"\n\n\"이 집이 네게 낡고 음울하게 느껴지고,\" 해비셤 양이 조바심치며 말했다. \"네가 놀기를 꺼린다면, 일을 할 마음은 있느냐?\"\n\n이 물음에는 다른 질문에 답할 때 끌어낼 수 있었던 것보다 한결 가벼운 마음으로 답할 수 있었기에, 나는 기꺼이 그러겠다고 말했다.\n\n\"그럼 저 맞은편 방으로 가거라.\" 그녀가 시든 손으로 내 뒤의 문을 가리키며 말했다. \"그리고 내가 갈 때까지 거기서 기다려.\"\n\n나는 계단참을 가로질러, 그녀가 가리킨 방으로 들어갔다. 그 방에서도 햇빛은 완전히 차단되어 있었고, 숨이 막히도록 답답한, 공기가 통하지 않는 냄새가 났다. 눅눅하고 구식인 벽난로 쇠살대에 근래 불이 지펴졌으나, 활활 타오르기보다는 꺼지려는 기색이 더했고, 방 안에 떠도는 마지못한 연기는 더 맑은 공기보다도 더 차가워 보였으니, 마치 우리 고장의 늪 안개 같았다. 높은 벽난로 선반 위에 놓인 겨울처럼 앙상한 촛대 몇 개가 방을 희미하게 밝히고 있었다. 아니, 방의 어둠을 희미하게 어지럽혔다고 하는 편이 더 정확하겠다. 방은 널찍했고, 짐작건대 한때는 보기 좋았을 테지만, 그 안의 분간할 수 있는 모든 것은 먼지와 곰팡이로 뒤덮인 채 조각조각 허물어지고 있었다. 가장 눈에 띄는 것은 식탁보가 펼쳐진 긴 식탁이었으니, 마치 집과 시계가 모두 한꺼번에 멈추었을 때 잔치 준비가 한창이었던 것 같았다. 이 식탁보 한가운데에는 무슨 장식 받침대 같은 것이 놓여 있었는데, 거미줄이 어찌나 두껍게 뒤덮였던지 그 형체를 도무지 분간할 수 없었다. 그리고 내 기억에 그것이 검은 버섯처럼 자라 나온 듯하던 그 누런 평면을 죽 훑어보다가, 나는 점박이 다리에 얼룩진 몸뚱이를 한 거미들이 그것을 향해 집으로 달려가고, 또 그것에서 달려 나오는 것을 보았으니, 마치 거미 사회에 더없이 막중한 공적 사건이 방금 일어나기라도 한 것 같았다.\n\n생쥐들이 벽판 뒤에서 부스럭거리는 소리도 들렸는데, 마치 똑같은 사건이 그들의 이해와도 관련된 것 같았다. 그러나 검은 딱정벌레들은 그 소동에 아랑곳하지 않고, 근시에 귀까지 어두운 데다 서로 사이마저 좋지 않다는 듯, 묵직하고 늙수그레한 걸음걸이로 난로 가를 더듬거리며 돌아다녔다.\n\n이 기어 다니는 것들이 내 주의를 사로잡아, 나는 멀찍이서 그것들을 지켜보고 있었는데, 그때 해비셤 양이 내 어깨에 손을 얹었다. 다른 손에는 손잡이가 목발 머리처럼 생긴 지팡이를 짚고 있었으니, 그녀는 마치 이곳의 마녀처럼 보였다.\n\n\"여기가,\" 그녀가 지팡이로 긴 식탁을 가리키며 말했다. \"내가 죽으면 누일 자리다. 사람들이 여기로 와서 나를 들여다볼 테지.\"\n\n그녀가 당장 그 식탁 위에 올라가 그 자리에서 죽어 버려, 장터의 그 섬뜩한 밀랍 인형이 그대로 실현되는 것은 아닐까 하는 어렴풋한 불안에, 나는 그녀의 손길 아래에서 움츠러들었다.\n\n\"저게 뭐라고 생각하느냐?\" 그녀가 다시 지팡이로 가리키며 물었다. \"저기, 거미줄이 쳐진 저것 말이다.\"\n\n\"저게 뭔지 짐작이 안 돼요, 아주머니.\"\n\n\"커다란 케이크다. 웨딩케이크지. 내 거야!\"\n\n그녀는 번득이는 눈빛으로 방 안을 죽 둘러보더니, 손으로 내 어깨를 움켜쥐어 당기며 내게 기댄 채 말했다. \"자, 자, 자! 나를 걸려라, 나를 걸려!\"\n\n이로써 나는 내가 해야 할 일이 해비셤 양을 부축해 방 안을 빙빙 도는 것임을 알아차렸다. 그리하여 나는 곧장 걷기 시작했고, 그녀는 내 어깨에 기댔으며, 우리는 (그 지붕 아래에서 처음 든 충동에 따라) 펌블추크 씨의 이륜마차를 흉내 냈다 할 만한 속도로 나아갔다.\n\n그녀는 체력이 강하지 못해, 얼마 지나지 않아 말했다. \"더 천천히!\" 그래도 우리는 조바심 어린 들쭉날쭉한 속도로 걸었고, 걸어가는 동안 그녀는 내 어깨에 얹은 손을 씰룩거리고 입을 옴짝거리며, 우리가 빨리 가는 것은 그녀의 생각이 빨리 흐르기 때문이라고 내가 믿게 만들었다. 얼마 후 그녀가 말했다. \"에스텔러를 불러라!\" 그래서 나는 계단참으로 나가, 지난번에 그랬듯이 그 이름을 큰 소리로 외쳤다. 그녀의 불빛이 나타나자 나는 해비셤 양에게 돌아갔고, 우리는 다시 방 안을 빙빙 돌기 시작했다.\n\n에스텔러가 우리가 하는 일을 구경하러 온 것만으로도 나는 충분히 못마땅했을 테지만, 그녀가 아래층에서 보았던 부인 셋과 신사를 데리고 왔으므로, 나는 어찌할 바를 몰랐다. 예의를 차리느라 나는 멈추고 싶었으나, 해비셤 양이 내 어깨를 씰룩 잡아당겨, 우리는 계속 나아갔으니 — 그러는 내내 나는 그들이 이 모든 게 다 내 짓이라 여길 거라는 부끄러운 의식에 시달렸다.\n\n\"해비셤 양.\" 세라 포켓 양이 말했다. \"안색이 참 좋아 보이세요!\"\n\n\"안 좋아.\" 해비셤 양이 받았다. \"나는 누런 살가죽에 뼈뿐이야.\"\n\n포켓 양이 이렇게 면박을 당하자 카밀라의 얼굴이 환해졌고, 그녀는 애처롭게 해비셤 양을 바라보며 중얼거렸다. \"가엾은 양반! 안색이 좋으리라고 기대할 수야 없지요, 가엾기도 하지. 그런 생각을 다!\"\n\n\"그래, 너는 어떠냐?\" 해비셤 양이 카밀라에게 말했다. 그때 우리는 카밀라 가까이에 있었으므로, 나는 당연히 멈추려 했지만, 해비셤 양이 멈추려 들지 않았다. 우리는 휙 지나쳤고, 나는 내가 카밀라에게 몹시 밉살스러운 존재가 되었음을 느꼈다.\n\n\"고마워요, 해비셤 양.\" 그녀가 받았다. \"이만하면 나름대로 잘 지내고 있지요.\"\n\n\"아니, 너한테 무슨 일이라도 있느냐?\" 해비셤 양이 유난히 날카롭게 물었다.\n\n\"입에 올릴 만한 건 아니에요.\" 카밀라가 대답했다. \"내 감정을 드러내고 싶진 않지만, 밤이면 으레 내가 감당할 수 있는 것 이상으로 양을 생각하곤 한답니다.\"\n\n\"그럼 내 생각은 하지 마라.\" 해비셤 양이 쏘아붙였다.\n\n\"말이야 쉽지요!\" 카밀라가, 윗입술이 씰룩거리고 눈물이 넘쳐흐르는 가운데, 흐느낌을 상냥하게 억누르며 말했다. \"밤이면 내가 생강과 각성용 암모니아수를 얼마나 들이켜야 하는지는 레이먼드가 증인이에요. 밤이면 내 다리가 얼마나 신경질적으로 떨리는지도 레이먼드가 증인이고요. 하지만 사랑하는 이들을 근심하며 생각할 때면 숨이 막히고 신경질적으로 떨리는 건 내겐 새삼스러운 일도 아니랍니다. 내가 정이 덜하고 덜 예민했더라면, 소화도 더 잘되고 신경도 무쇠처럼 단단했겠지요. 정말이지 그럴 수 있었으면 싶어요. 하지만 밤에 양 생각을 안 한다는 건—그런 생각을 다!\" 여기서 그녀는 울음을 터뜨렸다.\n\n언급된 그 레이먼드란 그 자리에 있는 신사를 가리키는 것임을, 나는 알아차렸고, 그가 카밀라 씨라는 것도 알아차렸다. 그가 이 대목에서 구원에 나서, 위로하면서도 추켜세우는 목소리로 말했다. \"카밀라, 여보, 당신의 집안에 대한 정 때문에 점점 몸이 축나서, 한쪽 다리가 다른 쪽보다 짧아질 지경이라는 건 다들 아는 사실이오.\"\n\n\"제가 알기로는,\" 내가 딱 한 번 들었던 그 엄숙한 부인이 말했다. \"어떤 사람을 생각한다고 해서 그 사람에게 무슨 큰 권리를 주장하는 건 아니랍니다, 얘야.\"\n\n이제 보니 세라 포켓 양은 자그마하고 메마르고 거무스레한 데다 쪼글쪼글한 노부인으로, 호두 껍데기로 빚은 듯한 작은 얼굴에, 수염 없는 고양이 같은 커다란 입을 지니고 있었는데, 그녀가 이 견해를 거들어 말했다. \"정말 그렇지요, 얘야. 흠!\"\n\n\"생각하는 거야 충분히 쉬운 일이지요.\" 그 엄숙한 부인이 말했다.\n\n\"그보다 쉬운 게 또 어디 있겠어요?\" 세라 포켓 양이 맞장구쳤다.\n\n\"오, 그래요, 그래요!\" 카밀라가 외쳤는데, 부글거리는 그녀의 감정이 다리에서 가슴으로 치솟는 듯했다. \"다 지당한 말씀이에요! 이토록 정이 깊은 게 약점이긴 하지만, 어쩔 수가 없는걸요. 그렇지 않았더라면 내 건강이 훨씬 나았으리란 건 의심할 여지가 없지만, 그래도 할 수만 있다 해도 내 천성을 바꾸진 않겠어요. 그 때문에 숱한 고통을 겪지만, 한밤에 깨어났을 때 내가 그런 천성을 지녔다는 걸 안다는 게 위안이 되거든요.\" 여기서 또 한차례 감정이 북받쳐 올랐다.\n\n해비셤 양과 나는 이 내내 한 번도 멈추지 않고, 방 안을 빙빙 돌고 또 돌았으니, 어느 때는 손님들의 치맛자락을 스치고, 어느 때는 음침한 방의 끝에서 끝까지를 그들에게 내주었다.\n\n\"매슈는 또 어떻고요!\" 카밀라가 말했다. \"천륜의 정이라곤 도무지 섞이려 들지 않고, 해비셤 양이 어떻게 지내는지 보러 여기 한번 오는 법도 없으니! 나는 코르셋 끈을 끊고 소파에 드러누워, 머리를 옆으로 늘어뜨리고, 머리카락을 다 풀어헤친 채, 발은 어디 두었는지도 모르게—\"\n\n(\"당신 머리보다 훨씬 높은 데 있었지, 여보.\" 카밀라 씨가 말했다.)\n\n\"—매슈의 괴상하고 도무지 알 수 없는 행실 때문에 그런 상태로 몇 시간이고 정신을 잃고 있었건만, 아무도 내게 고마워하지 않더군요.\"\n\n\"정말이지, 그러긴 어려웠겠다 싶군요!\" 엄숙한 부인이 끼어들었다.\n\n\"이봐요, 얘야,\" 세라 포켓 양이 (점잖게 표독스러운 인물이었다) 덧붙였다. \"스스로에게 던져야 할 질문은, 도대체 누가 네게 고마워해 주기를 기대했느냐 하는 거란다, 얘야.\"\n\n\"고마움이든 그 비슷한 무엇이든 기대하지 않고도,\" 카밀라가 말을 이었다. \"나는 몇 시간이고 그런 상태로 있었고, 내가 얼마나 숨이 막혔는지, 그리고 생강이 얼마나 아무 소용이 없었는지는 레이먼드가 증인이에요. 길 건너 피아노 조율사 집까지 내 소리가 들려서, 그 가엾은 철없는 아이들은 멀리서 비둘기가 구구대는 소리인 줄로 알았다지 뭐예요—그런데 이제 와서 이런 말을 듣다니—\" 여기서 카밀라는 손을 목으로 가져갔고, 거기서 새로운 화합물을 만들어 내듯 자못 화학적인 양상을 띠기 시작했다.\n\n이 매슈라는 이름이 거론되자, 해비셤 양은 나와 자신을 멈춰 세우고, 서서 말하는 이를 바라보았다. 이 변화는 카밀라의 화학 작용을 별안간 끝장내는 데 큰 영향을 미쳤다.\n\n\"매슈는 마침내 나를 보러 올 게다.\" 해비셤 양이 준엄하게 말했다. \"내가 저 식탁 위에 누이게 될 때 말이야. 저기가 그자의 자리가 될 거다—저기,\" 지팡이로 식탁을 내리치며, \"내 머리맡에! 그리고 네 자리는 저기! 네 남편 자리는 저기! 세라 포켓의 자리는 저기! 조지애나의 자리는 저기! 자, 너희 모두 나를 뜯어 먹으러 올 때 어느 자리에 들어설지 이제 알겠지. 자, 이제들 가거라!\"\n\n이름 하나하나가 불릴 때마다, 그녀는 지팡이로 식탁의 새로운 자리를 내리쳤다. 이제 그녀가 말했다. \"나를 걸려라, 나를 걸려!\" 그리하여 우리는 다시 걷기 시작했다.\n\n\"별수 없겠지요.\" 카밀라가 외쳤다. \"따르고 물러가는 수밖에. 이토록 짧은 동안이나마 사랑과 도리의 대상을 뵈었다는 것만으로도 어디예요. 한밤에 깨어났을 때 우울한 만족감으로 그 일을 떠올리겠지요. 매슈도 그런 위안을 누릴 수 있었으면 싶지만, 그이는 그걸 한사코 마다하니. 내 감정을 드러내지 않으리라 단단히 마음먹고 있지만, 마치 거인이라도 되는 양 친척들을 뜯어 먹으려 든다는 소리를 듣고, 가라는 말을 듣는다는 건 참으로 견디기 힘든 일이에요. 그런 생각을 다!\"\n\n카밀라 부인이 들먹이는 가슴에 손을 얹자, 카밀라 씨가 끼어들었고, 그 부인은 부자연스럽게 꿋꿋한 태도를 지어 보였으니, 내 짐작에 그것은 시야에서 벗어나면 쓰러져 숨이 막혀 보이겠다는 의도를 드러내는 것이었다. 그녀는 해비셤 양에게 손에 입을 맞춰 작별 인사를 보내고는, 부축을 받으며 나갔다. 세라 포켓과 조지애나는 서로 마지막까지 남으려고 다투었으나, 세라는 워낙 약삭빨라서 지지 않았으니, 어찌나 교묘하고 미끈하게 조지애나의 둘레를 종종걸음 치며 돌던지 조지애나가 어쩔 수 없이 먼저 나서야 했다. 그러고 나서 세라 포켓은 따로 효과를 노려, \"복 받으세요, 해비셤 양!\" 하며, 나머지 사람들의 약점을 너그러이 측은히 여기는 미소를 그 호두 껍데기 같은 얼굴에 띤 채 떠났다.\n\n에스텔러가 그들에게 불을 비춰 내려보내러 가 있는 동안, 해비셤 양은 여전히 내 어깨에 손을 얹은 채 걸었으나, 점점 더 느려졌다. 마침내 그녀는 난롯불 앞에 멈춰 서서, 몇 초간 그것을 바라보며 무어라 중얼거리더니 말했다—\n\n\"오늘이 내 생일이란다, 핍.\"\n\n내가 만수무강을 빌어 드리려는데, 그녀가 지팡이를 들어 올렸다.\n\n\"나는 그 말이 입에 오르는 걸 용납하지 않는다. 방금 여기 있던 자들이든 그 누구든, 그 말을 하는 걸 용납하지 않아. 그자들은 이날 여기 오긴 하지만, 감히 그 일을 입에 올리진 못하지.\"\n\n물론 나는 그 일을 입에 올리려는 시도를 더는 하지 않았다.\n\n\"한 해의 이날에, 네가 태어나기 한참 전에, 이 썩은 더미가,\" 그녀가 목발 지팡이로 식탁 위의 거미줄 무더기를 찌르는 시늉을 했으나 닿지는 않았다. \"여기 들여왔단다. 그것과 나는 함께 닳아 왔지. 생쥐들이 그것을 갉아먹었고, 생쥐의 이빨보다 더 날카로운 이빨이 나를 갉아먹었어.\"\n\n그녀는 식탁을 바라보며 서서, 지팡이 머리를 제 가슴에 갖다 댔다. 한때 흰빛이던 그녀의 드레스는 온통 누렇게 시들었고, 한때 흰빛이던 식탁보도 온통 누렇게 시들었으며, 주위의 모든 것은 손만 대면 바스러질 상태에 있었다.\n\n\"이 폐허가 다 완성되었을 때,\" 그녀가 섬뜩한 표정으로 말했다. \"그리고 그들이 죽은 나를, 신부의 드레스를 입은 채 신부의 식탁 위에 누일 때—그건 반드시 이루어질 것이고, 그것이 그자에게 내리는 완성된 저주가 될 거다—그 일이 이날 이루어진다면 더더욱 좋겠지!\"\n\n그녀는 마치 거기 누운 제 모습을 바라보고 서 있기라도 한 듯이 식탁을 바라보며 서 있었다. 나는 가만히 있었다. 에스텔러가 돌아왔고, 그녀 역시 가만히 있었다. 내가 보기에 우리는 오랫동안 그렇게 있었던 듯하다. 방 안의 묵직한 공기 속에서, 그리고 더 멀찍한 구석마다 서려 있는 묵직한 어둠 속에서, 나는 에스텔러와 내가 곧 썩어 가기 시작할지도 모른다는 섬뜩한 공상마저 들었다.\n\n마침내, 그 넋 나간 상태에서 차츰차츰이 아니라 일순간에 빠져나오며, 해비셤 양이 말했다. \"너희 둘이 카드놀이 하는 걸 보자꾸나. 왜 아직 시작하지 않았느냐?\" 그 말과 함께 우리는 그녀의 방으로 돌아가, 전처럼 자리에 앉았다. 나는 전처럼 빈털터리가 되었고, 다시금 전처럼, 해비셤 양은 내내 우리를 지켜보며 에스텔러의 아름다움에 내 주의를 돌렸고, 제 보석들을 에스텔러의 가슴과 머리에 대 보면서 내가 그것을 더욱 눈여겨보게 만들었다.\n\n에스텔러는, 제 쪽으로도 마찬가지로 나를 전처럼 대했는데, 다만 내게 말을 거는 친절을 베풀지 않는다는 점만 달랐다. 우리가 여섯 판쯤 놀고 나자, 내가 다시 올 날이 정해졌고, 나는 안뜰로 끌려 내려가 전과 같이 개에게 주듯 먹을 것을 받았다. 거기서도 나는 다시금 내 멋대로 돌아다니도록 내버려졌다.\n\n지난번에 내가 기어올라 너머를 엿보았던 그 정원 담의 문이, 그 지난번에 열려 있었는지 닫혀 있었는지는 그리 중요한 문제가 아니다. 그때는 문이 보이지 않았고 지금은 하나가 보였다는 것으로 족하다. 그것이 열려 있었고, 또 에스텔러가 손님들을 내보냈다는 것을 — 그녀가 열쇠를 손에 들고 돌아왔으므로 — 나는 알고 있었기에, 나는 정원으로 어슬렁어슬렁 들어가 그 안을 두루 거닐었다. 그곳은 그야말로 황무지였고, 안에는 낡은 멜론 모종틀과 오이 모종틀이 있었는데, 그것들은 쇠락해 가면서 낡은 모자며 장화 조각을 닮은 빈약한 시도들을 제멋대로 피워 낸 듯했고, 이따금 우중충하게 뻗어 나온 곁가지는 찌그러진 냄비를 빼닮은 꼴이었다.\n\n정원과, 쓰러진 포도 덩굴과 병 몇 개 외에는 아무것도 없는 온실을 다 돌아보고 나니, 나는 창문 너머로 내다보았던 그 음침한 구석에 와 있었다. 이제 집이 비었으리라는 것을 추호도 의심하지 않은 채, 나는 다른 창문 안을 들여다보다가, 크게 놀랍게도, 눈꺼풀이 붉고 머리카락이 옅은 빛깔인 어느 창백한 도련님과 멀거니 마주 보고 있는 나 자신을 발견했다.\n\n이 창백한 도련님은 휙 사라지더니, 어느새 내 곁에 다시 나타났다. 내가 그를 멀거니 바라보고 있던 그때 그는 책을 보던 중이었던지, 이제 보니 그는 잉크투성이였다.\n\n\"어이!\" 그가 말했다. \"젊은 친구!\"\n\n어이라는 말은 으레 그 자신으로 되받는 것이 상책이라고 나는 늘 알고 있었으므로, 나도 \"어이!\" 하고 말했다. 젊은 친구라는 말은 정중히 빼놓고서.\n\n\"누가 너를 들여보냈지?\" 그가 말했다.\n\n\"에스텔러 아가씨.\"\n\n\"누가 너더러 어슬렁거려도 좋다고 했고?\"\n\n\"에스텔러 아가씨.\"\n\n\"이리 와서 한판 붙자.\" 창백한 도련님이 말했다.\n\n그를 따라가는 것 말고 내가 무엇을 할 수 있었겠는가? 그 후로 나는 종종 그 물음을 스스로에게 던져 보았지만, 달리 무엇을 할 수 있었겠는가? 그의 태도가 어찌나 단호하고 나는 어찌나 놀랐던지, 나는 무슨 마법에라도 걸린 듯 그가 이끄는 곳으로 따라갔다.\n\n\"잠깐, 그런데,\" 우리가 몇 걸음 가기도 전에 그가 홱 돌아서며 말했다. \"너한테 싸울 명분도 줘야겠지. 자, 받아라!\" 더없이 약을 올리는 태도로 그는 대뜸 두 손을 마주쳤다가, 한쪽 다리를 뒤로 우아하게 차올렸다가, 내 머리카락을 잡아당기고, 다시 두 손을 마주치고, 머리를 숙이더니, 그것으로 내 배를 들이받았다.\n\n방금 말한 황소 같은 짓거리는, 그것이 의심할 여지 없이 무례한 행동으로 여겨질 만한 것이었던 데다, 빵과 고기를 먹은 직후라 유난히 불쾌했다. 그래서 나는 그를 향해 한 대 날렸고 또 한 대를 날리려는데, 그가 \"아하! 그럴 테냐?\" 하고는, 내 좁은 경험으로는 견줄 데 없는 방식으로 앞뒤로 춤추듯 움직이기 시작했다.\n\n\"시합의 규칙대로!\" 그가 말했다. 여기서 그는 왼발에서 오른발로 깡충 뛰었다. \"정식 규칙대로!\" 여기서 그는 오른발에서 왼발로 깡충 뛰었다. \"자리를 잡고, 예비 절차를 밟자고!\" 여기서 그는 앞뒤로 요리조리 피하며 온갖 동작을 다 했는데, 그동안 나는 어쩔 줄 모르고 그를 바라보았다.\n\n그가 그토록 날렵한 것을 보니 속으로 그가 무서워졌으나, 그의 옅은 빛깔 머리통이 내 명치를 들이받을 까닭이 도무지 없었으며, 그것이 그렇게 내 주의에 들이밀어진 이상 나는 그것을 당치 않은 일로 여길 권리가 있다고 나는 도덕적으로나 육체적으로나 확신했다. 그리하여 나는 한마디도 없이 그를 따라, 두 담장이 만나는 자리에 이루어진, 잡동사니로 가려진 정원의 외진 구석으로 갔다. 그가 내게 이 자리가 마음에 드느냐고 묻기에 그렇다고 대답하자, 그는 잠시 자리를 비우겠노라 양해를 구하더니, 물 한 병과 식초에 적신 해면을 들고 금세 돌아왔다. \"둘 다 쓸 수 있어.\" 그가 그것들을 담벼락에 기대 놓으며 말했다. 그러고는 재킷과 조끼뿐 아니라 셔츠까지 벗어 던지기 시작했는데, 그 태도가 명랑하면서도 사무적이고 또한 피에 굶주린 듯했다.\n\n그가 그리 건강해 보이지는 않았음에도 — 얼굴에 여드름이 나 있고, 입가가 헐어 있었으니 — 이 끔찍한 채비는 나를 자못 질리게 했다. 내가 보기에 그는 나와 비슷한 또래였으나, 키는 나보다 훨씬 컸고, 몸을 빙빙 돌리는 품이 자못 그럴싸했다. 그 밖에는, 그는 (싸우려고 옷을 벗지 않았을 때는) 회색 양복을 입은 도련님으로, 팔꿈치며 무릎이며 손목이며 발꿈치가 발육에 있어 몸의 나머지보다 한참 앞서 있었다.\n\n기계처럼 정교한 온갖 시늉으로 내게 자세를 잡고, 마치 어느 뼈를 칠지 꼼꼼히 고르기라도 하듯 내 몸뚱이를 노려보는 그를 보니, 나는 가슴이 철렁했다. 내가 첫 일격을 날렸을 때, 그가 코피를 흘리고 얼굴이 잔뜩 일그러진 채 벌렁 나자빠져 나를 올려다보는 것을 보았을 때만큼, 내 평생 그토록 놀란 적은 없었다.\n\n그러나 그는 곧장 일어서더니, 더없이 날렵한 솜씨를 한껏 과시하며 해면으로 제 몸을 닦고는, 다시 자세를 잡기 시작했다. 그가 다시 시퍼렇게 멍든 눈으로 나를 올려다보며 벌렁 나자빠진 것을 본 것이, 내 평생 두 번째로 가장 크게 놀란 일이었다.\n\n그의 기개는 내게 커다란 존경심을 불러일으켰다. 그는 도무지 힘이라곤 없어 보였고, 단 한 번도 나를 세게 치지 못했으며, 늘 얻어맞고 나가떨어졌다. 하지만 그는 금세 다시 일어나, 더없이 흡족한 표정으로 격식에 맞게 스스로를 챙기며 해면으로 몸을 닦거나 물병의 물을 들이켜고는, 마치 이번에야말로 정말로 나를 끝장낼 것 같은 기세와 시늉으로 내게 덤벼들었다. 그는 심하게 멍이 들었으니, 유감스럽게도 기록하건대 내가 그를 칠수록 더 세게 쳤기 때문이다. 그러나 그는 거듭거듭 또거듭 일어났고, 마침내 뒤통수를 담벼락에 호되게 부딪히며 나가떨어졌다. 우리 사이의 그 위기를 겪고 나서도, 그는 일어나 내가 어디 있는지도 모른 채 어리둥절하게 몇 차례 빙빙 돌더니, 끝내 제 해면을 향해 무릎을 꿇고 그것을 던져 올렸다. 그와 동시에 숨을 헐떡이며 내뱉었다. \"이건 네가 이겼다는 뜻이야.\"\n\n그가 어찌나 용감하고 순진해 보이던지, 내가 이 시합을 제안한 것도 아니건만, 나는 내 승리에 그저 우울한 만족밖에 느끼지 못했다. 실은, 나는 옷을 입으면서 스스로를 일종의 야만스러운 어린 늑대나 다른 들짐승쯤으로 여겼다고까지 말하고 싶다. 어쨌거나 나는 옷을 입고, 이따금 피투성이 얼굴을 침울하게 닦아 가며, \"도와줄까?\" 하고 말했고, 그는 \"아니, 됐어\" 하고 말했으며, 나는 \"그럼 잘 있어\" 하고 말했고, 그는 \"너도 잘 가\" 하고 말했다.\n\n안뜰로 들어서니, 에스텔러가 열쇠를 들고 기다리고 있었다. 그러나 그녀는 내가 어디 있었는지도, 왜 자기를 기다리게 했는지도 묻지 않았다. 그녀의 얼굴에는 무슨 기쁜 일이라도 생긴 양 환한 홍조가 떠올라 있었다. 게다가 그녀는 곧장 문으로 가는 대신, 복도 안쪽으로 물러서더니 내게 손짓을 했다.\n\n\"이리 와! 원한다면 내게 입맞춰도 좋아.\"\n\n나는 그녀가 내 쪽으로 돌린 뺨에 입을 맞추었다. 그녀의 뺨에 입맞추기 위해서라면 나는 어지간한 일이라도 견뎌 냈으리라 생각한다. 그러나 나는 그 입맞춤이 동전 한 닢을 던져 주듯 천하고 평범한 사내아이에게 베풀어진 것임을, 그리고 그것이 아무 값어치 없는 것임을 느꼈다.\n\n생일날 찾아온 손님들이며, 카드놀이며, 그 싸움이며 해서, 내가 머문 시간이 어찌나 길어졌던지, 집 가까이 이르렀을 때는 늪지의 곶 너머 모래톱 위의 등불이 검은 밤하늘을 배경으로 가물거리고 있었고, 조의 화덕은 길 건너로 불의 길을 던지고 있었다.\n\n# Chapter XII.\n\n창백한 얼굴의 도련님 문제로 내 마음은 몹시 불안해졌다. 그 싸움을 생각하면 할수록, 그리고 그 창백한 도련님이 등을 댄 채 여러 단계로 부어오르고 피투성이가 되던 얼굴을 떠올릴수록, 나에게 무슨 일이 일어나리라는 것이 점점 더 확실하게 느껴졌다. 나는 그 창백한 도련님의 피가 내 머리 위에 있고, 법이 그 피의 복수를 하리라는 느낌이 들었다. 내가 저지른 형벌에 대해 뚜렷한 관념은 없었으나, 시골 소년이 들판을 어슬렁거리며 신사 양반들의 집을 약탈하고 영국의 면학하는 청년을 두들겨 패면서 무거운 처벌에 노출되지 않을 수는 없다는 것만은 분명했다. 며칠 동안 나는 집 안에만 틀어박혀 있었고, 심부름을 가기 전에는 주(州) 감옥의 관리들이 나에게 덮쳐들지 않을까 하여 더없이 조심스럽고 떨리는 마음으로 부엌문 밖을 내다보았다. 그 창백한 도련님의 코피가 내 바지에 얼룩을 남긴 탓에, 나는 깊은 밤중에 그 죄의 증거를 씻어 내려 애썼다. 나는 그 창백한 도련님의 이에 부딪쳐 손가락 관절이 까졌는데, 판사들 앞에 끌려갔을 때 그 죄증이 될 만한 정황을 둘러댈 믿기 어려운 방도들을 꾸며 내느라 내 상상력을 천 갈래로 비틀어 댔다.\n\n그 폭력 행위의 현장으로 다시 돌아갈 날이 돌아오자, 나의 공포는 절정에 이르렀다. 런던에서 특별히 파견된 정의의 졸개들이 문 뒤에 매복해 있을지도 모른다는 것, 자기 집에 가해진 능욕에 대해 친히 복수하기를 더 좋아한 해비셤 양이 그 수의(壽衣) 같은 옷을 걸친 채 일어나 권총을 빼 들어 나를 쏘아 죽일지도 모른다는 것, 매수당한 소년들 ─ 수많은 용병 무리 ─ 이 양조장에서 나에게 달려들어 내가 숨이 끊어질 때까지 두들겨 패도록 고용될지도 모른다는 것, 이런 생각들이 떠올랐지만 그 창백한 도련님에게 그 보복의 공모자라는 혐의를 한 번도 씌우지 않았다는 것은 내가 그의 기개를 얼마나 신뢰했는지를 잘 증언해 준다. 그것들은 언제나, 그의 얼굴 꼴에 부추김을 받고 집안 내력의 생김새에 분개한 동정심을 품은, 그의 분별없는 친척들의 소행으로 내 머릿속에 떠올랐다.\n\n그러나 해비셤 양에게 가야만 했고, 또 갔다. 그런데 보라! 지난번 다툼에서는 아무 일도 일어나지 않았다. 그 일은 어떤 식으로도 언급되지 않았고, 그 집 어디에서도 창백한 도련님은 찾을 수 없었다. 나는 똑같은 문이 열려 있는 것을 발견했고, 정원을 살펴보았으며, 심지어 별채의 창문 안까지 들여다보았다. 그러나 안쪽에 닫힌 덧창에 시야가 갑자기 막혔고, 모든 것이 생기 없이 죽어 있었다. 오직 그 격투가 벌어진 구석에서만 그 도련님이 존재했다는 어떤 증거를 찾아낼 수 있었다. 그 자리에는 그의 핏자국이 남아 있었고, 나는 사람들의 눈에 띄지 않도록 그것을 정원의 흙으로 덮어 버렸다.\n\n해비셤 양의 방과, 긴 식탁이 차려진 그 다른 방 사이의 널찍한 층계참에서 나는 정원용 의자를 보았다. 뒤에서 미는, 바퀴 달린 가벼운 의자였다. 그것은 내가 지난번 다녀간 뒤에 거기 놓인 것이었는데, 바로 그날부터 나는 (해비셤 양이 내 어깨에 손을 얹고 걷는 데 지치면) 이 의자에 그녀를 태우고 자기 방을 돌고, 층계참을 가로지르고, 다른 방을 도는 일을 규칙적으로 맡게 되었다. 우리는 이런 여정을 거듭하고 또 거듭했으며, 때로는 한 번에 세 시간이나 이어지기도 했다. 나도 모르게 이 여정을 두루뭉술하게 여러 차례라고 언급하게 되는 것은, 이 일을 위해 격일로 정오마다 다시 오기로 곧바로 정해졌기 때문이고, 또 지금 내가 적어도 여덟 달이나 열 달에 이르는 기간을 한꺼번에 요약하려 하기 때문이다.\n\n서로에게 점점 익숙해지면서 해비셤 양은 나에게 말을 더 많이 걸었고, 무엇을 배웠으며 무엇이 될 작정이냐 같은 질문을 던지곤 했다. 나는 아마 조에게 도제로 들어가게 될 것 같다고 대답했다. 그리고 그녀가 그 바람직한 목적을 향해 무슨 도움을 줄지도 모른다는 희망을 품고서, 내가 아무것도 모르며 모든 것을 알고 싶어 한다는 점을 늘어놓았다. 그러나 그녀는 그러지 않았다. 오히려 내가 무지한 편을 더 좋아하는 듯했다. 또한 그녀는 나에게 돈을 준 적도 없고 ─ 매일의 저녁 식사 말고는 그 무엇도 준 적이 없으며 ─ 내가 봉사한 대가로 보수를 받아야 한다고 명시한 적도 없었다.\n\n에스텔라는 늘 주변에 있었고, 늘 나를 들이고 내보내 주었으나, 다시 입을 맞춰도 좋다는 말은 결코 하지 않았다. 어떤 때는 차갑게 나를 참아 주었고, 어떤 때는 나에게 너그럽게 굴었으며, 어떤 때는 아주 친근하게 대했고, 또 어떤 때는 나를 미워한다고 기운차게 말하기도 했다. 해비셤 양은 자주 속삭이듯, 혹은 우리 둘만 있을 때 나에게 묻곤 했다. \"저 애가 점점 더 예뻐지지 않니, 핍?\" 그리고 내가 그렇다고 대답하면(실제로 그랬으니까), 그것을 탐욕스럽게 음미하는 것 같았다. 또 우리가 카드놀이를 할 때면 해비셤 양은 에스텔라의 기분이 어떻든 그것을 인색하게 즐기며 지켜보았다. 그리고 때로는 에스텔라의 기분이 너무 변덕스럽고 서로 모순되어 내가 무슨 말을 하고 무엇을 해야 할지 어쩔 줄 모르고 있으면, 해비셤 양은 그녀를 아낌없는 애정으로 끌어안고는 그 귀에 무언가를 속삭였는데, 그것은 마치 \"그놈들의 가슴을 찢어 놓아라, 내 자랑이자 희망아, 그놈들의 가슴을 찢고 자비를 베풀지 마라!\" 하는 소리로 들렸다.\n\n조가 대장간에서 토막토막 흥얼거리던 노래가 하나 있었는데, 그 후렴이 올드 클렘이었다. 수호성인에게 경의를 표하기에는 그리 의례적인 방식이 아니었으나, 올드 클렘이 대장장이들에게 그러한 관계에 있었던 모양이다. 그것은 쇠를 두드리는 박자를 흉내 낸 노래로, 올드 클렘이라는 존귀한 이름을 끌어들이기 위한 한낱 서정적인 구실에 지나지 않았다. 이런 식이었다. 자, 둘러서서 두드려라, 얘들아 ─ 올드 클렘! 쾅 소리 한 번 울려라 ─ 올드 클렘! 두들겨라, 두들겨라 ─ 올드 클렘! 튼튼한 놈을 위해 쩌렁 한 번 ─ 올드 클렘! 불을 지펴라, 불을 지펴라 ─ 올드 클렘! 더 메마르게 으르렁대고, 더 높이 치솟아라 ─ 올드 클렘! 그 의자가 나타나고 얼마 안 된 어느 날, 해비셤 양이 손가락을 안달하듯 까딱이며 갑자기 나에게 \"자, 자, 자! 노래 불러라!\" 하고 말했고, 나는 그녀를 마룻바닥 위로 밀고 다니면서 얼떨결에 이 가락을 흥얼거리게 되었다. 그것이 어쩌다 그녀의 마음에 들었던지, 그녀는 마치 잠결에 노래하듯 낮고 골똘한 목소리로 그것을 따라 부르기 시작했다. 그 뒤로 우리는 돌아다니며 그 노래를 부르는 것이 관례처럼 되었고, 에스텔라도 종종 함께 부르곤 했다. 비록 셋이 모였을 때조차 그 가락 전체가 어찌나 나직했던지, 그 음산한 옛 집 안에서 그것은 가장 미약한 바람의 숨결보다도 작은 소리를 냈지만.\n\n이런 환경에서 나는 무엇이 될 수 있었을까? 어떻게 내 성격이 그것들의 영향을 받지 않을 수 있었을까? 안개 낀 누런 방들에서 자연의 빛 속으로 나왔을 때, 내 눈이 그러했듯 내 생각마저 멍해졌다 한들 그것이 이상한 일일까?\n\n만약 내가 앞서 고백한 그 엄청난 거짓말 속으로 미리 빠져들지만 않았더라면, 나는 어쩌면 조에게 그 창백한 도련님 이야기를 했을지도 모른다. 사정이 그러했으니, 조는 그 창백한 도련님을 검은 벨벳 마차에 태우기에 알맞은 또 하나의 승객으로 알아볼 것이 틀림없다는 느낌이 들었다. 그래서 나는 그에 대해 아무 말도 하지 않았다. 게다가 처음부터 나를 사로잡았던, 해비셤 양과 에스텔라가 입에 오르내리는 것에 대한 그 움츠러드는 마음은 시간이 갈수록 훨씬 더 강해졌다. 나는 비디 말고는 그 누구에게도 온전히 마음을 내맡기지 못했다. 그러나 가엾은 비디에게는 모든 것을 털어놓았다. 어째서 그렇게 하는 것이 나에게 자연스러웠는지, 그리고 어째서 비디가 내가 들려주는 모든 일에 깊은 관심을 가졌는지, 그때는 알지 못했으나 지금은 안다고 생각한다.\n\n그러는 동안 집 부엌에서는 평의회가 계속되었는데, 격앙된 내 심정에는 거의 견디기 힘든 부아를 안겨 주는 것이었다. 그 멍청이 펌블추크는 밤이면 자주 건너와 누나와 함께 내 장래를 논하곤 했다. 그리고 나는 정말로 믿는다(지금 이 순간까지도 마땅히 느껴야 할 만큼의 뉘우침은 없이), 만약 이 두 손이 그의 이륜마차에서 굴대 빗장을 빼낼 수 있었다면 정말로 그렇게 했으리라는 것을. 그 가련한 자는 정신머리가 어찌나 옹졸하고 둔했던지, 나를 앞에 세워 놓지 않고서는 ─ 말하자면 나를 가지고 시술이라도 하듯 ─ 내 장래를 논할 수가 없었다. 그래서 그는 한쪽 구석에서 얌전히 있는 나를 걸상에서(대개는 멱살을 잡고) 끌어올려, 마치 나를 요리라도 할 것처럼 난롯불 앞에 세워 놓고는 이렇게 운을 떼곤 했다. \"자, 부인, 여기 이 아이가 있습니다! 손수 길러 내신 바로 이 아이 말입니다. 고개를 들어라, 얘야, 그리고 그렇게 해 주신 분들께 영원히 감사할 줄 알아야지. 자, 부인, 이 아이에 관해서 말하자면!\" 그러고는 내 머리카락을 결과 반대로 헝클어뜨리곤 했는데 ─ 앞서 넌지시 밝혔듯 나는 아주 어린 시절의 기억 이래로, 그 어떤 동족에게도 그럴 권리가 있다는 것을 내 영혼으로 부정해 왔다 ─ 그러고는 소맷자락을 잡고 나를 자기 앞에 붙들어 두었으니, 그 어리석음의 광경에 견줄 만한 것은 오직 그 자신뿐이었다.\n\n그런 다음 그와 누나는 해비셤 양에 관해, 그리고 그녀가 나에게 무엇을 해 줄 것이며 나를 위해 무엇을 해 줄 것인가에 관해, 어찌나 터무니없는 억측을 주거니 받거니 했던지, 나는 ─ 자못 고통스럽게도 ─ 분한 눈물을 왈칵 쏟으며 펌블추크에게 달려들어 그를 온통 두들겨 패고 싶어지곤 했다. 이런 대화 속에서 누나는 나를 언급할 때마다 마치 내 이를 하나씩 모질게 뽑아내기라도 하듯 나에게 말을 건넸고, 한편 스스로 내 후견인을 자처한 펌블추크는, 도무지 수지가 맞지 않는 일을 떠맡았다고 여기는 내 운명의 건축가라도 되는 양, 깔보는 눈으로 나를 감독하며 앉아 있었다.\n\n이런 논의에 조는 끼어들지 않았다. 그러나 그것이 진행되는 동안, 내가 대장간에서 떨어져 나가는 것을 조가 탐탁지 않게 여긴다는 것을 조 부인이 알아챈 탓에, 조는 자주 그 화살의 표적이 되었다. 이제 나는 조에게 도제로 들어가기에 충분한 나이였다. 그리고 조가 부지깽이를 무릎에 얹고 골똘히 아래쪽 살창 사이의 재를 긁어내고 있으면, 누나는 그 천진한 행동을 어찌나 또렷이 조의 반대 표시로 해석했던지, 그에게 와락 달려들어 부지깽이를 손에서 빼앗고 그를 흔들어 댄 뒤 그것을 치워 버리곤 했다. 이런 논쟁은 하나같이 더없이 약을 올리는 식으로 끝이 났다. 어떤 빌미도 없이 별안간, 누나는 하품을 하다 말고 멈추더니, 마치 우연인 듯 나를 발견하고는 나에게 달려들며 이렇게 쏘아붙이곤 했다. \"이리 와! 네 꼴은 이제 됐다! 너는 가서 잠이나 자! 하룻밤에 충분히 말썽을 부렸을 텐데, 그렇겠지!\" 마치 내가 제발 내 인생을 들들 볶아 달라고 그들에게 간청이라도 한 것처럼.\n\n우리는 오랫동안 이런 식으로 지냈고, 앞으로도 오랫동안 이런 식으로 지내게 될 듯싶었다. 그러던 어느 날, 해비셤 양은 그녀가 내 어깨에 기댄 채 나와 함께 걷다가 우뚝 멈춰 서더니, 약간 못마땅한 투로 말했다.\n\n\"너 키가 자라고 있구나, 핍!\"\n\n나는 이것이 내가 어찌할 수 없는 사정에서 비롯된 일인지도 모른다는 뜻을, 생각에 잠긴 듯한 눈빛을 통해 넌지시 비치는 것이 상책이라고 여겼다.\n\n그녀는 그때는 더 말하지 않았다. 그러나 곧 멈춰 서서 다시 나를 바라보았고, 또 곧 다시 그러했으며, 그러고 나서는 얼굴을 찌푸린 채 시무룩한 표정을 지었다. 다음번에 내가 시중을 들러 간 날, 우리의 평소 운동이 끝나고 내가 그녀를 화장대 앞에 데려다 놓자, 그녀는 안달하는 손가락을 움직여 나를 멈춰 세웠다.\n\n\"네 그 대장장이라는 사람 이름을 다시 한 번 말해 보아라.\"\n\n\"조 가저리예요, 부인.\"\n\n\"네가 도제로 들어갈 그 주인 말이지?\"\n\n\"네, 해비셤 양.\"\n\n\"당장 도제로 들어가는 게 좋겠다. 가저리가 너와 함께 여기 와서 네 도제 계약서를 가져올 것 같으냐?\"\n\n나는 그렇게 청을 받는 것을 그가 영광으로 여길 것이 틀림없다는 뜻을 내비쳤다.\n\n\"그럼 그를 오라고 해라.\"\n\n\"특별히 정한 때가 있나요, 해비셤 양?\"\n\n\"이런, 이런! 나는 때 같은 건 모른다. 곧 오라고 해라, 그리고 너와 함께 오게 해라.\"\n\n그날 밤 집에 돌아와 이 전갈을 조에게 전했더니, 누나는 그 어느 때보다도 무섭게 \"길길이 날뛰었다.\" 그녀는 나와 조에게 자기가 우리 발밑에 깔린 현관 깔개쯤 되는 줄 아느냐고, 감히 어떻게 자기를 그렇게 부려 먹을 수 있느냐고, 우리가 황송하게도 자기를 어떤 패거리에나 어울리는 여자로 여기느냐고 따져 물었다. 그런 질문을 폭포처럼 쏟아 내며 기진맥진하고 나자, 그녀는 조에게 촛대를 집어던지고, 큰 소리로 흐느껴 울기 시작하더니, 쓰레받기를 꺼내 들고 ─ 이것은 언제나 아주 불길한 징조였다 ─ 거친 앞치마를 두르고는 끔찍할 만큼 대청소를 시작했다. 마른걸레질로는 성에 차지 않았던지, 그녀는 양동이와 솔까지 동원해 우리를 집에서 깡그리 씻어 내쫓는 바람에, 우리는 뒷마당에 서서 덜덜 떨어야 했다. 밤 열 시가 되어서야 우리는 겨우 다시 안으로 기어들 엄두를 냈는데, 그러자 그녀는 조에게 어째서 진작 흑인 노예 계집이랑 결혼하지 않았느냐고 물었다. 조는 아무 대답도 하지 못하고, 가엾게도 자기 구레나룻을 만지작거리며 풀이 죽은 채 나를 바라보기만 했다. 마치 그 편이 정말로 더 나은 셈속이었을지도 모른다고 생각하는 듯이.\n\n# Chapter XIII.\n\n그다음다음 날, 조가 미스 해비셤 댁에 나를 데려가려고 나들이옷을 차려입는 모습을 지켜보는 일은 내 심정에 적잖은 시련이었다. 하지만 그는 이 자리에 예복이 꼭 필요하다고 여겼으므로, 작업복 차림이 훨씬 나아 보인다고 그에게 말할 수는 없는 노릇이었다. 더구나 나는 그가 오로지 나를 위해 그토록 끔찍하게 불편을 감수하고 있다는 것을, 그리고 나를 위해 셔츠 깃을 뒤로 어찌나 높이 추켜올렸던지 정수리의 머리카락이 깃털 다발처럼 곤두서 있다는 것을 알고 있었기에 더욱 그러했다.\n\n아침 식사 때 누나는 우리와 함께 읍내에 가겠다고, 그래서 펌블추크 아저씨 댁에 남아 있다가 \"우리가 귀부인들과 볼일을 다 본 뒤에\" 데리러 오라고 선언했다. 이런 말투에서 조는 최악을 점치는 듯한 기색이었다. 대장간은 그날 하루 문을 닫았고, 조는 (일을 하지 않는 지극히 드문 경우에 늘 하던 버릇대로) 문에다 분필로 \"외출\"이라는 한마디를 적고, 그 옆에 그가 향한 방향으로 날아간다고 짐작되는 화살 그림을 그려 넣었다.\n\n우리는 걸어서 읍내로 갔는데, 누나는 아주 커다란 비버 가죽 보닛을 쓰고 앞장섰으며, 짚을 엮어 만든 영국 국새만 한 바구니와 나막신 한 켤레, 여분의 숄 한 장, 그리고 우산을 들고 있었다. 날씨가 화창하고 맑았는데도 말이다. 이 물건들을 회개하는 마음으로 들고 다닌 것인지 아니면 과시하려고 들고 다닌 것인지 나로서는 확실치 않으나, 아무래도 그것들을 재산으로서 전시했던 것 같다. 마치 클레오파트라나, 잔뜩 성이 나 길길이 날뛰는 그 어떤 군주 마님이 행렬이나 행진에서 제 부를 과시하듯이.\n\n펌블추크 아저씨 댁에 이르자 누나는 펄쩍 뛰어들어 가더니 우리를 두고 사라졌다. 거의 정오가 되었으므로, 조와 나는 곧장 미스 해비셤 댁으로 향했다. 에스텔라가 여느 때처럼 문을 열어 주었는데, 그녀가 나타나는 순간 조는 모자를 벗어 두 손으로 챙을 잡고 무게를 가늠하듯 들고 섰다. 마치 그 무게를 사분의 일 온스의 절반까지 정확히 따져야 할 절박한 이유라도 있는 사람처럼.\n\n에스텔라는 우리 둘 다 거들떠보지 않은 채, 내가 익히 아는 길로 우리를 안내했다. 나는 그녀의 바로 뒤를 따랐고, 조가 맨 끝에 왔다. 긴 복도에서 조를 돌아보니, 그는 여전히 더없이 조심스럽게 제 모자의 무게를 가늠하면서, 발끝으로 성큼성큼 우리를 뒤따라오고 있었다.\n\n에스텔라가 우리 둘 다 들어가야 한다고 일러 주었으므로, 나는 조의 윗옷 소맷부리를 잡고 그를 미스 해비셤 앞으로 이끌었다. 그녀는 화장대 앞에 앉아 있다가 곧바로 우리 쪽을 돌아보았다.\n\n\"오!\" 그녀가 조에게 말했다. \"당신이 이 아이 누나의 남편이로군요?\"\n\n다정하고 늙은 조가 그토록 제 모습과 동떨어져 보이거나, 그토록 별난 새처럼 보일 수 있으리라고는 나는 도무지 상상하지 못했을 것이다. 그는 깃털 다발을 곤두세운 채, 마치 벌레라도 한 마리 바라는 듯 입을 벌리고, 말 한마디 못 하고 서 있었다.\n\n\"당신이 이 아이 누나의 남편이지요?\" 미스 해비셤이 되풀이했다.\n\n몹시 답답한 노릇이었으나, 면담이 진행되는 내내 조는 미스 해비셤이 아니라 한사코 나에게만 말을 걸었다.\n\n\"그러니까 내 말은 말이지, 핍.\" 조는 이제 강력한 논증과 엄격한 신뢰와 더없는 공손함이 한꺼번에 묻어나는 태도로 입을 열었다. \"내가 벌떡 일어나 네 누나하고 혼인을 했고, 그때 나는 (네가 어떻게든 그리 부르고 싶다면) 말하자면 독신 남자였단 말이지.\"\n\n\"그래요!\" 미스 해비셤이 말했다. \"그래서 이 아이를 도제로 삼을 작정으로 길러 온 거고요. 그렇지요, 가저리 씨?\"\n\n\"있잖아, 핍.\" 조가 대답했다. \"너하고 나는 늘 친구였고, 우리 둘 사이에선 그게 신나는 일로 이어질 거라고 손꼽아 기다려 온 일이었잖아. 그렇다고 해서, 핍, 만에 하나 네가 그 일에 대해 이의를 제기한 적이 있었다면 말이야 — 이를테면 그게 검댕과 그을음 따위에 노출되어 있다든가 하는 그런 이의 말이야 — 그렇다고 해서 그 이의가 받아들여지지 않았으리란 건 아니야, 알겠지?\"\n\n\"이 아이가.\" 미스 해비셤이 말했다. \"이의를 제기한 적이 있나요? 이 일을 좋아하나요?\"\n\n\"그건 네 자신이 익히 잘 알고 있잖아, 핍.\" 조는 앞서의 논증과 신뢰와 공손함의 혼합을 한층 강화하며 대답했다. \"그게 바로 네 마음속 소원이었다는 거 말이야.\" (나는 그가 다음 말을 잇기 전에, 그 묘비명을 이 상황에 맞게 고쳐 쓰겠다는 생각이 문득 그에게 떠오르는 것을 보았다.) \"그리고 네 쪽에선 아무 이의도 없었고, 핍 그건 네 마음속 크나큰 소원이었지!\"\n\n그에게 미스 해비셤에게 말을 해야 한다는 점을 일깨우려는 내 노력은 전혀 헛수고였다. 내가 그렇게 하라고 표정을 짓고 몸짓을 할수록, 그는 더욱더 은밀하고 논증적이며 공손하게 한사코 나에게만 굴었다.\n\n\"그 아이의 도제 계약서를 가져왔나요?\" 미스 해비셤이 물었다.\n\n\"그게 말이지, 핍, 알다시피.\" 조는 그것이 다소 부당한 질문이라는 듯 대답했다. \"네가 내가 그걸 모자에 넣는 걸 직접 봤으니, 그게 여기 있다는 걸 너도 알잖아.\" 그러면서 그는 그것을 꺼내, 미스 해비셤이 아니라 나에게 건넸다. 에스텔라가 미스 해비셤의 의자 뒤에 서서 두 눈으로 짓궂게 웃고 있는 것을 보았을 때, 나는 그 다정하고 착한 친구가 부끄러웠던 것 같다 — 분명 나는 그가 부끄러웠다. 나는 그의 손에서 계약서를 받아 미스 해비셤에게 건넸다.\n\n\"이 아이에게 도제금을 받으리라고는 기대하지 않았겠지요?\" 미스 해비셤이 계약서를 훑어보며 말했다.\n\n\"조!\" 그가 도무지 아무 대답도 하지 않기에 나는 항의했다. \"왜 대답을 안 해요 —\"\n\n\"핍.\" 조는 상처라도 받은 듯 내 말을 가로막으며 대꾸했다. \"그러니까 내 말은, 그건 너하고 나 사이에 대답이 필요한 질문이 아니라는 거고, 그 대답이 단연코 '아니오'라는 걸 너도 익히 알고 있잖아. 너도 그게 '아니오'라는 걸 알고 있는데, 내가 무엇 하러 그 말을 하겠어, 핍?\"\n\n미스 해비셤은, 거기서 그가 어떤 사람인지를 보고서, 그의 됨됨이를 내가 가능하리라 여겼던 것보다 더 잘 헤아린다는 듯 그를 흘끗 쳐다보았다. 그러고는 옆 탁자에서 작은 주머니 하나를 집어 들었다.\n\n\"핍은 여기서 도제금을 벌었어요.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"여기 있어요. 이 주머니에 이십오 기니가 들어 있어요. 네 주인에게 드려라, 핍.\"\n\n마치 그녀의 기이한 행색과 그 기이한 방이 불러일으킨 경이로움에 완전히 넋이 나간 사람처럼, 조는 이 마당에 이르러서도 한사코 나에게만 말을 걸었다.\n\n\"이건 네 쪽에서 참으로 후한 일이야, 핍.\" 조가 말했다. \"그래서 이렇게 받아 들고 고맙게 환영하는 바이지만, 멀리서든 가까이서든 그 어디서도 결코 바란 적이 없는 일이지. 그리고 이제, 친구.\" 조가 말했는데, 그 정겨운 호칭이 마치 미스 해비셤에게 쓰이는 것만 같아 나는 처음엔 화끈거리다가 이내 얼어붙는 느낌이 들었다. \"그리고 이제, 친구, 우리 본분을 다하자꾸나! 너와 내가, 우리 둘 다, 서로에게, 그리고 네 후한 선물이 — 전해 준 — 그 마음의 — 만족을 위해 — 결코 — 한 적이 없는 — 그분들에게 본분을 다하자꾸나.\" 여기서 조는 자신이 무서운 곤경에 빠졌음을 느낀 기색을 내비치다가, 마침내 \"그리고 나로 말하면 어림없는 일이지!\"라는 말로 의기양양하게 스스로를 구해 냈다. 이 말이 그에게는 어찌나 매끄럽고 설득력 있게 들렸던지, 그는 그것을 두 번이나 되뇌었다.\n\n\"잘 가렴, 핍!\" 미스 해비셤이 말했다. \"내보내 드려라, 에스텔라.\"\n\n\"또 와야 하나요, 미스 해비셤?\" 내가 물었다.\n\n\"아니. 이제 가저리가 네 주인이다. 가저리! 한마디만!\"\n\n내가 문을 나서는 참에 그녀가 이렇게 그를 불러 세우더니, 또렷하고 힘준 목소리로 조에게 이렇게 말하는 것이 들렸다. \"이 아이는 여기서 착하게 굴었고, 그것이 그 보답이에요. 물론 정직한 사람으로서, 당신은 그 밖의 다른 것이나 그 이상을 기대하지 않겠지요.\"\n\n조가 어떻게 그 방을 나왔는지 나는 끝내 알아내지 못했으나, 그가 정작 나왔을 때 아래로 내려오는 대신 꾸준히 위층으로 올라가고 있었다는 것, 그리고 내가 그를 쫓아가 붙들기 전까지는 어떤 만류에도 귀를 기울이지 않았다는 것만은 안다. 잠시 후 우리는 문밖으로 나왔고, 문은 잠겼으며, 에스텔라는 가 버린 뒤였다. 다시 단둘이 햇빛 속에 섰을 때, 조는 벽에 등을 기대고 나에게 말했다. \"놀랍구나!\" 그러고는 거기 그렇게 오래도록 머무르며 이따금 어찌나 자주 \"놀랍구나\"를 되뇌던지, 나는 그의 정신이 영영 돌아오지 않는 게 아닐까 생각하기 시작했다. 마침내 그는 그 말을 \"핍, 정말이지 너에게 단언하건대 이건 놀-라-운 일이야!\"로 길게 늘이더니, 그렇게 차츰 말이 통하게 되었고 걸어 나갈 수 있게 되었다.\n\n조의 지력이 방금 겪어 낸 그 만남으로 한층 또렷해졌으며, 펌블추크 아저씨 댁으로 가는 길에 그가 교묘하고 심오한 계략 하나를 꾸며 냈다고 나는 생각할 만한 근거가 있다. 그 근거는 펌블추크 씨의 응접실에서 벌어진 일에서 찾을 수 있는데, 우리가 그곳에 모습을 드러냈을 때 누나는 그 얄미운 종묘상과 마주 앉아 의논을 나누고 있었다.\n\n\"그래?\" 누나가 우리 둘에게 한꺼번에 대고 소리쳤다. \"그래서 너희들한테 무슨 일이 있었던 거야? 이런 보잘것없는 곳까지 황송하게도 돌아와 주다니 신기할 따름이네, 정말이지!\"\n\n\"미스 해비셤께서.\" 조가 마치 기억을 더듬으려 애쓰듯 나를 빤히 바라보며 말했다. \"그분께 우리가 꼭 전해 드려야 한다고 아주 각별히 당부하시기를 — 그게 안부였던가 경의였던가, 핍?\"\n\n\"안부요.\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"그게 바로 내 생각이었어.\" 조가 대답했다. \"J. 가저리 부인께 안부를 —\"\n\n\"그게 나한테 무슨 큰 도움이 되겠어!\" 누나가 말했으나, 그래도 다소 흐뭇해하는 기색이었다.\n\n\"그리고 바라시기를.\" 조가 또 한 번 기억을 더듬으려 애쓰듯 나를 빤히 바라보며 말을 이었다. \"미스 해비셤의 건강 상태가 — 허락할 만한 그런 것이었더라면, 그랬더라면, 핍?\"\n\n\"기쁨을 누리실.\" 내가 덧붙였다.\n\n\"귀부인들과 어울리는 기쁨을요.\" 조가 말했다. 그러고는 긴 숨을 내쉬었다.\n\n\"그래!\" 누나가 펌블추크 씨에게 누그러진 눈길을 던지며 소리쳤다. \"처음부터 그 전갈을 보낼 만한 예의는 갖췄을 법한데, 그래도 늦더라도 안 하는 것보단 낫지. 그래서 여기 이 어린 망나니한테는 뭘 줬다는 거야?\"\n\n\"그 애한테는.\" 조가 말했다. \"아무것도 안 줬어.\"\n\n조 부인이 막 폭발하려는 참에, 조가 말을 이었다.\n\n\"그분이 주신 건.\" 조가 말했다. \"그 애 친지들한테 주신 거지. '그리고 그 애 친지들이라 함은,' 하고 그분이 설명하시기를, '그 애의 누나 J. 가저리 부인의 손에 쥐여 드린다는 뜻입니다.' 그게 바로 그분의 말씀이었어. 'J. 가저리 부인.' 그분은 어쩌면 모르셨을 거야.\" 조가 곰곰이 생각하는 기색으로 덧붙였다. \"그게 조인지 조지인지는 말이야.\"\n\n누나는 펌블추크를 바라보았고, 그는 나무 안락의자의 팔걸이를 쓰다듬으며 마치 미리 다 알고 있었다는 듯 누나에게도, 난롯불에게도 고개를 끄덕여 보였다.\n\n\"그래서 얼마나 받았어?\" 누나가 웃으며 물었다. 분명히 웃으면서!\n\n\"여기 계신 분들은 십 파운드라면 뭐라 하실까?\" 조가 물었다.\n\n\"꽤 괜찮다고 하겠지.\" 누나가 퉁명스레 대꾸했다. \"너무 많은 건 아니지만, 그래도 꽤 괜찮다고.\"\n\n\"그럼 그것보다 많아.\" 조가 말했다.\n\n저 무시무시한 사기꾼 펌블추크가 즉시 고개를 끄덕이며, 의자 팔걸이를 문지르면서 말했다. \"그것보다 많지요, 부인.\"\n\n\"아니, 설마 그 말은 —\" 누나가 입을 떼었다.\n\n\"네, 그렇습니다, 부인.\" 펌블추크가 말했다. \"하지만 잠깐 기다려 보세요. 계속하게, 조지프. 잘하는군! 계속해!\"\n\n\"여기 계신 분들은.\" 조가 말을 이었다. \"이십 파운드라면 뭐라 하실까?\"\n\n\"근사하다는 말이 나오겠지.\" 누나가 대꾸했다.\n\n\"그럼, 그렇다면.\" 조가 말했다. \"이십 파운드보다 많아.\"\n\n저 비열한 위선자 펌블추크가 또 고개를 끄덕이더니, 생색내는 웃음을 띠며 말했다. \"그것보다 많지요, 부인. 또 맞혔습니다! 계속 몰아붙이세요, 조지프!\"\n\n\"그럼 이걸로 끝을 내자면.\" 조가 신이 나서 누나에게 주머니를 건네며 말했다. \"이십오 파운드야.\"\n\n\"이십오 파운드입니다, 부인.\" 저 가장 야비한 사기꾼 펌블추크가 따라 외치며 일어나 누나와 악수를 했다. \"그리고 그건 (제 의견을 물으셨을 때 제가 말씀드렸듯이) 오로지 부인의 공덕에 걸맞은 액수일 뿐이며, 이 돈으로 부디 기쁨을 누리시길 빕니다!\"\n\n그 악당이 여기서 멈추었더라면 그의 행태도 충분히 끔찍한 정도로 그쳤겠지만, 그는 한술 더 떠 나를 떠맡고 나섰으니, 그 후견인 행세는 그가 앞서 저지른 온갖 죄상을 까마득히 능가하는 것이었다.\n\n\"자 보세요, 조지프 그리고 부인.\" 펌블추크가 내 팔의 팔꿈치 위쪽을 붙잡으며 말했다. \"나는 한번 시작한 일은 늘 끝까지 밀고 나가는 사람들 중 하나입니다. 이 아이는 당장 계약을 맺어야 합니다. 그게 내 방식이지요. 당장 계약을 맺어야 한다는 겁니다.\"\n\n\"아무렴요, 펌블추크 아저씨.\" 누나가 (돈을 움켜쥐며) 말했다. \"저희가 아저씨께 크게 신세를 졌네요.\"\n\n\"내 생각은 마세요, 부인.\" 그 악마 같은 곡물상이 대꾸했다. \"기쁨이란 어디서나 다 기쁨이지요. 하지만 이 아이는, 아시다시피 계약을 맺어야 합니다. 솔직히 말씀드리면, 내가 그 일을 처리하겠노라 말씀드린 바 있지요.\"\n\n치안 판사들이 바로 가까운 읍사무소에 출석해 있었으므로, 우리는 곧바로 그리로 건너가 판사 앞에서 나를 조의 도제로 계약시켰다. 우리가 건너갔다고 말은 하지만, 사실 나는 펌블추크에게 떠밀려 건너갔는데, 그 꼴이 마치 내가 방금 소매치기를 했거나 건초더미에 불이라도 지른 사람 같았다. 실제로 법정 안에서는 내가 현행범으로 붙잡혀 왔다는 것이 전반적인 인상이었다. 펌블추크가 군중 사이로 나를 앞세워 밀고 나가는 동안, 어떤 이들은 \"쟤가 뭘 했대?\"라고 말하고, 또 어떤 이들은 \"아직 어린 녀석인데, 인상이 험상궂지 않아?\"라고 말하는 소리가 들렸으니까. 심지어 온화하고 자비로운 인상의 한 사람은, 족쇄를 완벽한 소시지 가게처럼 주렁주렁 매단 흉악한 청년의 목판화로 장식되고 「나의 감방에서 읽을지어다」라는 제목이 붙은 전도지 한 장을 나에게 건네주기까지 했다.\n\n법정은 묘한 곳이라고 나는 생각했다. 교회보다 높은 좌석이 있었고 — 사람들이 그 좌석에 몸을 기댄 채 구경하고 있었으며 — 위풍당당한 판사들(한 사람은 머리에 분을 발랐다)이 팔짱을 끼고 의자에 등을 기댄 채, 코담배를 들이켜거나, 졸거나, 무언가를 쓰거나, 신문을 읽고 있었고 — 벽에는 반들거리는 검은 초상화 몇 점이 걸려 있었는데, 미술에 문외한인 내 눈에는 그것들이 호두엿과 반창고를 뒤섞어 빚은 것처럼 보였다. 여기 한 구석에서 내 도제 계약서가 정식으로 서명되고 입증되었으며, 나는 \"계약에 묶였다.\" 그러는 내내 펌블추크 씨는, 마치 우리가 교수대로 가는 길에 잠깐 들러 이런 자질구레한 절차를 처리하기라도 하는 양 나를 붙들고 있었다.\n\n우리가 다시 밖으로 나와, 내가 공개적으로 고문당하는 광경을 보리라는 기대에 잔뜩 신이 나 있다가 내 친지들이 그저 나를 둘러싸고 격려하는 것뿐임을 알고 크게 실망한 아이들을 떼어 낸 뒤, 우리는 펌블추크 아저씨 댁으로 돌아왔다. 그리고 거기서 누나는 그 이십오 기니에 어찌나 들떴던지, 그 횡재로 블루 보어 여관에서 만찬을 들어야겠다고, 펌블추크가 이륜마차를 몰고 가서 허블 부부와 워프슬 씨를 데려와야겠다고, 그러지 않고서는 도무지 직성이 풀리지 않았다.\n\n그렇게 하기로 의견이 모였고, 나는 더없이 우울한 하루를 보냈다. 왜냐하면 도무지 헤아릴 수 없게도, 좌중 모두의 마음속에서 내가 이 연회의 군더더기라는 것이 너무도 당연한 이치로 여겨지는 듯했기 때문이다. 게다가 설상가상으로, 그들은 이따금 — 요컨대 달리 할 일이 없을 때마다 — 나에게 왜 즐기지 않느냐고 물어 댔다. 그러니 그때 내가 즐기고 있노라고 말하는 것 말고 달리 무엇을 할 수 있었겠는가 — 즐기고 있지도 않은데 말이다!\n\n그러나 그들은 다 큰 어른들이었고 제 마음대로 했으며, 그 자리를 한껏 누렸다. 저 사기꾼 펌블추크는 이 모든 일을 베푼 자비로운 기획자로 떠받들려, 실제로 상석을 차지했다. 그리고 나의 계약 건에 대해 좌중에게 일장 연설을 늘어놓으며, 내가 카드 노름을 하거나, 독한 술을 마시거나, 밤늦게까지 쏘다니거나, 나쁜 친구들과 어울리거나, 그 밖에 내 도제 계약서의 형식이 거의 불가피한 일로 상정하고 있는 듯한 온갖 탈선에 빠질 경우 투옥될 수 있다는 점을 악마처럼 축하한 뒤, 그는 자기 논평을 실연해 보이려고 나를 자기 옆 의자 위에 세워 두었다.\n\n그 성대한 잔치에 관해 내게 남은 다른 기억이라고는 이런 것들뿐이다. 그들은 나를 자게 내버려 두지 않고, 내가 꾸벅꾸벅 졸기 시작하는 것을 볼 때마다 깨워서는 즐기라고 했다는 것. 저녁이 꽤 깊었을 무렵 워프슬 씨가 콜린스의 송시를 읊으며 피로 물든 검을 천둥처럼 내던졌는데, 그 효과가 어찌나 대단했던지 종업원이 들어와 \"아래층 외판원들께서 안부를 전하시며, 여기가 곡예사 술집은 아니라고 하십니다\"라고 말했다는 것. 집으로 돌아가는 길에 그들이 모두 더없이 들뜬 기분으로 「오, 아리따운 아가씨여!」를 불렀는데, 워프슬 씨가 저음부를 맡아, (그 노래를 이끌면서 모든 이의 사사로운 일을 죄다 캐물으려 드는 더없이 무례한 태도의 그 꼬치꼬치 캐묻는 따분한 사내에게 대답하듯) 자신이 바로 그 백발을 흩날리는 사내이며, 전체로 보아 자신이 가장 연약한 순례자라고 엄청나게 우렁찬 목소리로 단언했다는 것.\n\n마지막으로, 내가 기억하기로 내 작은 침실로 들어갔을 때 나는 정말이지 비참한 심정이었고, 결코 조의 일을 좋아하게 되지 못하리라는 강한 확신에 사로잡혀 있었다. 한때는 그 일을 좋아했지만, 그 한때는 이제 더 이상 지금이 아니었다.\n\n# Chapter XIV.\n\n집을 부끄럽게 여긴다는 것은 더할 나위 없이 비참한 일이다. 그 안에는 새까만 배은망덕이 깃들어 있을지도 모르고, 그에 대한 벌이 응보로서 마땅한 것일지도 모른다. 그러나 그것이 비참한 일이라는 것만은 내가 증언할 수 있다.\n\n집은 누나의 성미 탓에 내게 그리 즐거운 곳이었던 적이 없었다. 그러나 조가 그곳을 거룩하게 만들어 주었고, 나는 그것을 믿었다. 나는 안쪽 응접실을 더없이 우아한 객실이라 믿었고, 앞문을 엄숙하게 열릴 때마다 통닭구이의 제물이 따르는, 신비로운 국가 신전의 정문이라 믿었으며, 부엌을 웅장하지는 않아도 정결한 방이라 믿었고, 대장간을 어른이 되어 자립으로 나아가는 이글거리는 길이라 믿었다. 단 한 해 사이에 이 모든 것이 변해 버렸다. 이제 그 모든 것이 거칠고 천하게만 보였고, 나는 무슨 일이 있어도 미스 해비셤과 에스텔라에게 그것을 보이고 싶지 않았다.\n\n이런 볼썽사나운 내 마음 상태가 어느 만큼이 내 탓이었는지, 어느 만큼이 미스 해비셤의 탓이었는지, 어느 만큼이 누나의 탓이었는지는, 이제 내게도 그 누구에게도 중요하지 않다. 변화는 내 안에서 일어났고, 일은 이미 벌어진 것이다. 잘 벌어졌든 잘못 벌어졌든, 용서받을 만하든 용서받을 수 없든, 어쨌든 일은 벌어졌다.\n\n한때 나는, 마침내 셔츠 소매를 걷어붙이고 조의 도제로서 대장간에 들어서게 될 날이면, 내가 떳떳하고 행복하리라 여겼었다. 그러나 막상 그 현실이 손안에 들어오고 보니, 나는 그저 잔석탄의 먼지를 뒤집어쓴 처지라는 것만을 느꼈고, 모루조차 깃털처럼 가벼워 보일 만큼 무거운 짐이 나날의 마음을 짓누르고 있음을 느낄 뿐이었다. 훗날 내 인생에는 (대개의 인생이 그러하리라 생각하지만) 그 모든 흥취와 낭만 위로 두꺼운 장막이 드리워져, 무딘 인내 말고는 그 무엇으로부터도 나를 차단해 버리는 듯한 시기가 한동안 있곤 했다. 그러나 그 장막이, 조의 도제 생활이라는 새로 들어선 길을 통해 내 인생의 행로가 내 앞에 일직선으로 뻗어 있을 때만큼 무겁고 막막하게 드리운 적은 없었다.\n\n기억하건대 도제 \"기간\"의 후반 무렵, 나는 일요일 저녁 밤이 내릴 때면 교회 묘지 언저리에 서서, 내 앞날의 전망을 바람 부는 습지의 풍경과 견주어 보며, 둘 다 얼마나 평평하고 나지막한지, 그리고 둘 다 그 위로 알 수 없는 길과 어두운 안개가 펼쳐졌다가 이윽고 바다가 나타나는지를 헤아려, 그 둘 사이에서 어떤 닮은 점을 찾아내곤 했다. 도제 생활 첫날의 나는 그 훗날만큼이나 의기소침했다. 그러나 도제 계약이 이어지는 동안 내가 조에게 단 한마디 불평도 입 밖에 내지 않았다는 것은 다행으로 안다. 그 일에 관해 내가 스스로에 대해 다행이라 여길 수 있는 거의 유일한 점이다.\n\n이제 내가 덧붙이려는 말이 거기에 포함되긴 하지만, 그렇게 덧붙이려는 일의 공로는 모두 조의 것이었다. 내가 달아나 군인이나 뱃사람이 되어 버리지 않은 것은 내가 충직해서가 아니라 조가 충직했기 때문이었다. 내가 마음에 내키지 않으면서도 그런대로 열성을 내어 일한 것은 내게 근면의 미덕에 대한 강한 의식이 있어서가 아니라 조에게 그런 의식이 강했기 때문이었다. 상냥하고 정직한 마음으로 제 본분을 다하는 사람의 영향이 세상으로 얼마나 멀리 뻗어 나가는지는 알 길이 없다. 그러나 그 영향이 곁을 스쳐 지나며 자기 자신을 어떻게 어루만졌는지는 충분히 알 수 있는 법이니, 내 도제 생활에 섞여 든 좋은 것은 무엇이든 잠시도 가만있지 못하고 위만 쳐다보며 불만에 찬 나에게서가 아니라, 수수하고 만족할 줄 아는 조에게서 나왔음을 나는 똑똑히 알고 있다.\n\n내가 무엇을 원했는지, 그 누가 말할 수 있겠는가? 나 스스로도 결코 알지 못했는데, 내가 어찌 말할 수 있겠는가? 내가 두려워한 것은, 언젠가 어느 불운한 순간에 가장 꾀죄죄하고 가장 천한 꼴을 하고 있던 내가 문득 눈을 들었다가, 에스텔라가 대장간의 나무 창문 하나로 들여다보고 있는 것을 보게 되지나 않을까 하는 것이었다. 나는 그녀가 머지않아, 얼굴과 손이 새까매진 채 일 가운데서도 가장 거친 부분을 하고 있는 나를 발견하고는, 나를 두고 의기양양해하며 멸시하지나 않을까 하는 두려움에 사로잡혀 있었다. 어둠이 내린 뒤 조를 위해 풀무를 당기며 우리가 「올드 클렘」을 부를 때면, 그리고 미스 해비셤의 집에서 그 노래를 부르던 일이 떠오르며 불길 속에 에스텔라의 얼굴이 — 어여쁜 머리칼을 바람에 나부끼고 두 눈으로 나를 비웃는 얼굴이 — 보이는 듯할 때면, 그런 순간이면 나는 그때 나무 창문이던 그 벽의 새까만 밤의 판자들 쪽을 바라보며, 그녀가 막 얼굴을 거두어 가는 것을 본 것만 같은 환상에 빠지고, 끝내 그녀가 찾아온 것이라 믿곤 했다.\n\n그러고 나서 저녁을 먹으러 안으로 들어가면, 그 자리와 그 식사가 어느 때보다 더 초라해 보였고, 나는 내 볼썽사나운 가슴속에서 어느 때보다 더 집을 부끄러워하곤 했다.\n\n# Chapter XV.\n\n내가 워프슬 씨 종조모의 방에 다니기에는 몸이 너무 커진 탓에, 그 터무니없는 노파 밑에서 받던 내 교육은 끝이 났다. 그러나 비디가 자신이 아는 것을 모조리 — 자그마한 물가표에서부터 언젠가 반 페니를 주고 산 우스개 노래에 이르기까지 — 내게 전수해 준 뒤에야 그리 된 일이었다. 그 후자의 문학 작품에서 그나마 앞뒤가 맞는 부분이라고는 첫머리 몇 줄뿐이었지만,\n\n내가 런던 거리에 갔을 때 여러분, 투 룰 루 룰 투 룰 루 룰 나 보기 좋게 골탕먹지 않았던가요 여러분? 투 룰 루 룰 투 룰 루 룰\n\n— 그래도 나는 더 현명해지고 싶다는 일념으로 이 작품을 더없이 진지하게 외워 버렸다. 그리고 그 가치를 의심한 기억도 없다. 다만 (지금도 그렇게 생각하지만) 투 룰의 양이 시의 분량에 비해 다소 지나치다고 여겼을 뿐이다. 지식에 굶주린 나머지 나는 워프슬 씨에게 지적 빵부스러기라도 몇 점 베풀어 달라고 청했고, 그는 친절히 응해 주었다. 그러나 알고 보니 그가 나를 필요로 한 것은 오로지 연극의 모형 인형으로서, 반박당하고 끌어안기고 그 위에서 눈물 흘려지고 윽박질러지고 움켜잡히고 칼에 찔리고 갖가지 방식으로 두들겨 맞기 위함이었으니, 나는 이내 그 가르침의 길을 사양하고 말았다. 다만 워프슬 씨가 자신의 시적 광란 속에서 나를 호되게 두들겨 패고 난 뒤의 일이었지만.\n\n내가 무엇을 익히든, 나는 그것을 조에게 전수하려 했다. 이 말은 듣기에 너무도 그럴듯하므로, 나는 양심상 이것을 설명 없이 넘어갈 수가 없다. 나는 조가 덜 무지하고 덜 천박해져, 내 교제 상대로서 더 가치 있는 사람이 되고 에스텔라의 비난에 덜 노출되기를 바랐던 것이다.\n\n습지에 나가 있는 오래된 포대가 우리의 공부 장소였고, 깨진 석판 하나와 짤막한 석필 한 토막이 우리의 교육 도구였다. 거기에 조는 늘 담배 한 대를 더했다. 나는 조가 한 일요일에서 다음 일요일까지 무언가를 기억해 내거나, 내 가르침 아래 그 어떤 지식이든 한 조각이라도 익히는 것을 본 적이 없다. 그래도 그는 그 포대에서 담배를 피울 때면 다른 어디에서보다 훨씬 더 슬기로운 표정으로 — 심지어 학식 있는 표정으로 — 마치 자신이 엄청나게 발전하고 있다고 여기는 듯했다. 가엾은 친구, 부디 그가 정말 그러했기를 바란다.\n\n거기 바깥은 즐겁고 고요했다. 강 위의 돛들이 토루 너머로 지나가고, 이따금 썰물 때면 그 돛들이 마치 물밑에서 아직도 항해를 계속하는 가라앉은 배들에 달린 것처럼 보이기도 했다. 하얀 돛을 활짝 펼친 채 바다로 나아가는 배들을 바라볼 때마다, 나는 어쩐지 해비셤 양과 에스텔라를 떠올렸다. 그리고 저 멀리 구름이나 돛이나 푸른 언덕 비탈이나 수평선 위로 빛이 비스듬히 내리쬘 때마다, 그것은 꼭 마찬가지였다. 해비셤 양과 에스텔라와 그 기이한 집과 그 기이한 삶은, 그림처럼 아름다운 모든 것과 어떤 연관이 있는 듯싶었다.\n\n어느 일요일, 조가 담배를 무척이나 즐기며 자신이 \"지독히도 둔하다\"는 것에 어찌나 우쭐대던지 내가 그날은 그를 포기해 버렸을 때, 나는 한동안 토루 위에 턱을 손에 괴고 누워, 하늘에서도 물에서도 온 풍경 도처에서 해비셤 양과 에스텔라의 자취를 더듬어 찾다가, 마침내 그들에 관해 줄곧 내 머릿속에 맴돌던 한 가지 생각을 입 밖에 내기로 마음먹었다.\n\n\"조.\" 내가 말했다. \"내가 해비셤 양을 한번 찾아뵙는 게 좋지 않을까요?\"\n\n\"글쎄다, 핍.\" 조가 천천히 곰곰이 생각하며 대답했다. \"무엇 때문에?\"\n\n\"무엇 때문이라뇨, 조? 어떤 방문이든 무엇 때문에 하는 거겠어요?\"\n\n\"방문 중에는 말이다, 핍.\" 조가 말했다. \"영영 따져 봐도 답이 안 나오는 방문도 있는 법이지. 한데 해비셤 양을 찾아뵙는 일로 말하자면, 그분은 네가 무언가를 바란다고 — 자기한테서 무언가를 기대한다고 여기실지도 모른다.\"\n\n\"내가 그렇지 않다고 말하면 되지 않을까요, 조?\"\n\n\"그럴 수도 있겠지, 이 친구야.\" 조가 말했다. \"그러면 그분이 그 말을 믿어 주실 수도 있고. 마찬가지로 안 믿으실 수도 있고.\"\n\n조는 나와 마찬가지로 자기가 거기서 한 가지 핵심을 짚었다고 느꼈고, 그것을 거듭 말해 약화시키지 않으려고 담뱃대를 힘껏 빨아 댔다.\n\n\"있잖아, 핍.\" 그 위기를 넘기자마자 조가 말을 이었다. \"해비셤 양은 너한테 후하게 대해 주셨다. 해비셤 양이 너한테 후하게 대해 주셨을 때, 나를 다시 불러서 그게 전부라고 말씀하셨지.\"\n\n\"네, 조. 저도 들었어요.\"\n\n\"전부라고.\" 조가 무척 힘주어 거듭 말했다.\n\n\"네, 조. 말씀드렸잖아요, 저도 들었다고.\"\n\n\"그러니까 내 말은, 핍, 그분 뜻이 어쩌면 이런 거였는지도 모른다는 거다. 이걸로 끝을 내자! 지금 이대로! 나는 북으로, 너는 남으로! 따로따로 떨어져 지내자!\"\n\n나도 그 생각을 해 본 터였고, 그가 그런 생각을 했다는 것을 알게 되니 도무지 위안이 되지 않았다. 그것이 그 가능성을 더 짙게 만드는 듯했기 때문이다.\n\n\"그런데, 조.\"\n\n\"왜, 이 친구야.\"\n\n\"이렇게 도제 생활 첫해를 보내고 있는데, 계약을 맺은 그날 이후로 저는 해비셤 양께 감사를 드린 적도, 안부를 여쭌 적도, 제가 그분을 기억하고 있다는 표시를 한 적도 없어요.\"\n\n\"그건 맞다, 핍. 그리고 네가 그분께 신발 한 벌을 네 짝 다 만들어 드리지 않는 한 — 한데 내 말은, 발굽이라곤 도무지 없는 분께는 신발 네 짝짜리 한 벌도 선물로 받아들여지지 않을 수도 있다는 거다만—\"\n\n\"그런 식의 기억을 말하는 게 아니에요, 조. 선물 같은 걸 말하는 게 아니라고요.\"\n\n그러나 조는 일단 선물이라는 생각을 머릿속에 담아 두자 그것을 물고 늘어져야만 했다. \"아니면 말이다.\" 그가 말했다. \"네가 거들어서 그분 현관문에 새 사슬을 하나 달아 드린다든지 — 아니면 두루 쓰시라고 상어 머리 나사못 한두 그로스라든지 — 아니면 가벼운 멋들어진 물건, 이를테면 머핀을 드실 때 쓰실 토스트 포크라든지 — 아니면 스프랫이나 그런 걸 드실 때 쓰실 석쇠라든지—\"\n\n\"무슨 선물이든 아예 말하는 게 아니에요, 조.\" 내가 끼어들었다.\n\n\"글쎄.\" 조가, 마치 내가 그것을 특별히 강하게 밀어붙이기라도 한 것처럼 여전히 그 이야기를 물고 늘어지며 말했다. \"내가 너라면, 핍, 나는 안 하겠다. 그래, 절대로 안 해. 그분이 늘 사슬을 걸어 두고 계신데 현관 사슬이 다 무슨 소용이겠냐? 게다가 상어 머리 나사못은 오해를 살 여지가 있어. 그리고 토스트 포크라면 너는 놋쇠 세공에 손을 대게 될 텐데 영 체면이 안 설 거다. 또 아무리 솜씨 좋은 직공이라도 석쇠로는 솜씨 좋은 티를 못 내. 석쇠는 어디까지나 석쇠니까.\" 조가, 마치 나를 어떤 고정된 망상에서 깨우려 애쓰기라도 하듯 그 점을 내게 단단히 새겨 넣으며 말했다. \"네가 무얼 노리든 좋다만, 결국 나오는 건 석쇠뿐이야. 네가 좋든 싫든 석쇠로 나올 테고, 너로서는 어쩔 도리가 없어—\"\n\n\"이봐요, 조.\" 내가 절망에 빠져 그의 웃옷을 붙들며 외쳤다. \"그런 식으로 계속 말씀하지 마세요. 저는 해비셤 양께 무슨 선물을 드릴 생각 같은 건 한 적도 없어요.\"\n\n\"그래, 핍.\" 조가, 마치 줄곧 그 점을 두고 다투기라도 했다는 듯 수긍했다. \"그래서 내가 너한테 하는 말이, 네 말이 옳다는 거다, 핍.\"\n\n\"네, 조. 그런데 제가 하고 싶었던 말은, 요즘 일이 좀 한가하니까 내일 반나절만 쉬게 해 주시면, 읍내에 올라가 에스 — 해비셤 양을 한번 찾아뵐까 한다는 거예요.\"\n\n\"한데 그분 성함은 말이다.\" 조가 진지하게 말했다. \"에스타비셤이 아니다, 핍. 다시 세례를 받으셨다면 또 모를까.\"\n\n\"알아요, 조, 알아요. 제 말실수예요. 어떻게 생각하세요, 조?\"\n\n요컨대, 조는 내가 그것을 좋게 여긴다면 자기도 좋게 여긴다는 생각이었다. 그러나 그는, 만약 내가 따뜻하게 맞아들여지지 않거나, 다른 속셈은 전혀 없이 베풀어 주신 호의에 대한 감사의 표시일 뿐인 방문으로서 다시 찾아오라는 권유를 받지 못한다면, 이 시험적인 나들이에 다음번이란 없어야 한다는 것을 특히 까다롭게 못 박았다. 나는 이 조건들을 지키겠노라 약속했다.\n\n그런데 조는 주급을 주고 직공 하나를 부리고 있었으니, 그 이름은 올릭이었다. 그는 자기 세례명이 돌지라고 우겨 댔는데 — 그건 명백히 있을 수 없는 일이었지만 — 그는 워낙 고집스러운 기질의 사내라서, 나는 그가 이 점에서 어떤 망상에 사로잡혀 있었던 것이 아니라, 마을 사람들의 분별력을 모욕하려고 일부러 그 이름을 마을에 강요했던 것이라고 믿는다. 그는 어깨가 떡 벌어지고 팔다리가 늘어진 가무잡잡한 사내로, 힘이 장사였으며, 결코 서두르는 법이 없고, 늘 구부정하게 어슬렁거렸다. 그는 일하러 올 때조차 일부러 오는 것처럼 보이는 법이 없이, 그저 우연인 듯 어슬렁어슬렁 들어왔다. 그리고 졸리 바지선 주막에 점심을 먹으러 가거나 밤에 돌아갈 때면, 카인이나 떠돌이 유대인처럼, 마치 어디로 가는지도 모르고 다시 돌아올 작정도 없다는 듯 어슬렁어슬렁 나갔다. 그는 습지에 나가 있는 어느 수문지기 집에 묵었는데, 일하는 날이면 두 손을 주머니에 찔러 넣고 점심을 보따리에 헐겁게 싸 목에 둘러 등에 대롱대롱 매단 채, 그 은둔처에서 어슬렁어슬렁 걸어 나오곤 했다. 일요일이면 대개 종일 수문 위에 드러눕거나 낟가리와 곳간에 기대어 서 있었다. 그는 늘 땅바닥에 눈을 박은 채 어슬렁어슬렁 옮겨 다녔다. 그리고 누가 말을 걸거나 달리 눈을 들어야 할 때면, 절반은 분개하고 절반은 어리둥절한 표정으로 올려다보았으니, 마치 그가 품은 유일한 생각이라고는 자기가 결코 아무 생각도 하지 않는다는 것이 다소 묘하고도 부당한 사실이라는 것뿐이라는 듯했다.\n\n이 시무룩한 직공은 나를 좋아하지 않았다. 내가 아주 어리고 겁이 많았을 적에, 그는 대장간의 시커먼 구석에 악마가 살고 있으며 자기는 그 악귀를 아주 잘 안다고 내게 일러 주었다. 또한 칠 년에 한 번씩 살아 있는 소년으로 불을 지펴 줘야 하는 법이며, 나야말로 그 땔감이라 여겨도 된다고 했다. 내가 조의 도제가 되자, 올릭은 어쩌면 내가 자기 자리를 밀어낼 거라는 어떤 의심을 굳혔는지도 모른다. 어쨌든 그는 나를 한층 덜 좋아하게 되었다. 그가 공공연히 적의를 드러내는 무슨 말을 하거나 무슨 짓을 한 것은 아니었다. 다만 나는, 그가 늘 자기 불똥을 내 쪽으로 튀긴다는 것과, 내가 늙은 클렘을 부를 때면 언제나 박자를 틀리게 끼어든다는 것을 알아챘을 뿐이다.\n\n이튿날, 내가 조에게 내 반나절 휴가를 일깨웠을 때, 돌지 올릭이 일을 하며 그 자리에 있었다. 그 순간 그는 아무 말도 하지 않았는데, 그와 조가 마침 시뻘건 쇳덩이를 사이에 두고 있었고 나는 풀무를 잡고 있었기 때문이다. 그러나 이윽고 그가 망치에 몸을 기대며 말했다.\n\n\"이봐요, 주인장! 우리 중 한 사람만 봐주실 건 아니겠지요. 젊은 핍이 반나절 쉰다면, 늙은 올릭한테도 그만큼 해 주쇼.\" 그는 아마 스물다섯 살쯤 되었으리라 짐작되지만, 늘 자신을 노인네인 양 일컬었다.\n\n\"아니, 자네가 반나절 쉬어서 뭘 하려고?\" 조가 말했다.\n\n\"내가 그걸로 뭘 하느냐고요! 저 녀석은 그걸로 뭘 하는데요? 나도 저 녀석만큼은 하겠소.\" 올릭이 말했다.\n\n\"핍으로 말하면, 읍내에 올라간다네.\" 조가 말했다.\n\n\"그럼 늙은 올릭으로 말하면, 그도 읍내에 올라가지요.\" 그 잘난 사내가 받아쳤다. \"둘이서 읍내에 올라갈 수 있잖소. 읍내에 올라갈 수 있는 게 한 사람뿐인 건 아니지요.\"\n\n\"화내지 말게.\" 조가 말했다.\n\n\"내키면 낼 거요.\" 올릭이 으르렁거렸다. \"원, 읍내 나들이 좋아하시네! 자, 주인장! 어서요. 이 가게에서 편애는 안 됩니다. 사내답게 구쇼!\"\n\n직공이 좀 더 기분이 풀릴 때까지 주인이 그 문제를 거론하기를 거부하자, 올릭은 화덕으로 달려들어 시뻘겋게 단 쇠막대를 끄집어내더니, 그것으로 내 몸을 꿰뚫기라도 할 듯 나를 향해 달려들었다가, 내 머리 둘레로 휙휙 휘두르고는, 모루 위에 얹어 두들겨 폈다. 마치 그것이 나이고 불똥은 내가 뿜어내는 피이기라도 한 듯이, 나는 그렇게 느꼈다. 그러고는 마침내, 자기 몸은 뜨겁게 달구고 쇠는 식혀 놓은 다음 다시 망치에 몸을 기대며 말했다.\n\n\"자, 주인장!\"\n\n\"이제 괜찮은가?\" 조가 물었다.\n\n\"아! 난 괜찮소.\" 무뚝뚝한 늙은 올릭이 말했다.\n\n\"그럼, 자네가 대체로 웬만한 사람들 못지않게 일에 충실하니까.\" 조가 말했다. \"모두에게 반나절 휴가로 하지.\"\n\n내 누나가 그동안 마당에 말없이 서서 — 그녀는 더없이 거리낌 없는 염탐꾼이자 엿듣기꾼이었다 — 죄다 듣고 있다가, 즉시 창문 하나로 안을 들여다보았다.\n\n\"꼴좋네, 이 바보 같은 양반!\" 그녀가 조에게 말했다. \"저런 게으르고 덩치만 큰 멍청이한테 휴가를 다 주고. 정말이지 당신은 부자인가 봐, 그런 식으로 품삯을 낭비하다니. 내가 저자의 주인이었으면 좋겠네!\"\n\n\"당신은 배짱만 있으면 누구한테든 주인 노릇을 하려 들겠지.\" 올릭이 험상궂게 씩 웃으며 받아쳤다.\n\n(\"저 사람 좀 내버려 두게.\" 조가 말했다.)\n\n\"나는 온갖 얼간이며 온갖 악당이며 다 상대해 줄 수 있어.\" 누나가 점점 무서운 분노로 자신을 몰아가기 시작하며 대꾸했다. \"그리고 얼간이들을 당해 내려면, 얼간이들의 멍청한 왕초인 당신 주인부터 당해 내야 할 테고. 또 악당들을 당해 내려면, 여기서 프랑스 사이에 가장 시커먼 상판을 한 최악의 악당인 너부터 당해 내야겠지. 어때!\"\n\n\"당신은 더러운 앙칼쟁이요, 가저리 아주머니.\" 직공이 으르렁거렸다. \"그게 악당을 가려내는 안목이 된다면, 당신이야말로 제대로 가려내겠구려.\"\n\n(\"저 사람 좀 내버려 두라니까, 응?\" 조가 말했다.)\n\n\"뭐라고 했어?\" 누나가 비명을 지르기 시작하며 외쳤다. \"뭐라고 했냐고? 저 올릭이란 작자가 나한테 뭐라고 했어, 핍? 내 남편이 옆에 멀쩡히 서 있는데, 나를 뭐라고 불렀어? 오! 오! 오!\" 이 외침들은 하나하나가 다 비명이었다. 그리고 나는 내 누나에 대해, 내가 이제껏 본 모든 사나운 여인들에게 똑같이 해당되는 사실을 한마디 짚어 두어야겠다. 즉 격정은 그녀의 변명이 될 수 없었으니, 그녀가 격정에 빠져드는 것이 아니라 의식적으로 또 일부러 비상한 애를 써서 스스로를 격정 속으로 몰아넣었으며, 차근차근 단계를 밟아 가며 눈먼 격분에 이르렀다는 것은 부정할 수 없는 사실이기 때문이다. \"나를 지켜 주겠다고 맹세한 비열한 사내 앞에서 저자가 나를 뭐라고 불렀냐고? 오! 날 좀 붙들어 줘! 오!\"\n\n\"아-아-아!\" 직공이 이를 갈며 으르렁거렸다. \"당신이 내 마누라였으면 내가 붙들어 주지. 펌프 밑에 처박고 졸라서 토해 내게 해 줄 텐데.\"\n\n(\"내버려 두라고 했지.\" 조가 말했다.)\n\n\"오! 저 말 좀 들어 봐!\" 누나가, 박수를 치는 동시에 비명을 지르며 외쳤다. 그것은 그녀의 다음 단계였다. \"저자가 나한테 붙이는 이름들 좀 들어 보라고! 저 올릭이란 작자가! 내 집에서! 어엿한 유부녀인 나한테! 내 남편이 옆에 멀쩡히 서 있는데! 오! 오!\" 여기서 누나는, 한바탕 박수와 비명을 터뜨린 뒤, 두 손으로 제 가슴과 무릎을 두드리고, 모자를 벗어 던지고, 머리를 풀어 헤쳤다. 그것이 광란에 이르는 그녀의 마지막 단계들이었다. 이쯤 되자 완벽한 복수의 여신이요 완전한 성공작이 된 그녀는, 다행히도 내가 잠가 두었던 문으로 달려들었다.\n\n이제 가엾은 조가 무엇을 할 수 있었겠는가. 무시당한 자신의 삽입구식 만류가 거듭된 끝에, 직공에게 맞서서 어찌하여 자기와 조 부인 사이에 끼어드느냐고 따져 묻고, 더 나아가 그럴 만한 사내라면 어디 한번 덤벼 보라고 하는 것밖에. 늙은 올릭은 이 상황이 덤벼드는 것 외에는 달리 받아들일 여지가 없음을 직감하고 곧장 방어 자세를 취했다. 그리하여 둘은 그을리고 탄 앞치마를 벗을 새도 없이, 두 거인처럼 서로에게 달려들었다. 그러나 그 동네에서 조에게 오래 맞설 수 있는 사내가 있었다면, 나는 그런 사내를 본 적이 없다. 올릭은, 마치 그 창백한 젊은 신사보다 나을 게 없다는 듯, 순식간에 석탄 먼지 속에 나뒹굴었고, 거기서 좀처럼 빠져나오려 들지 않았다. 그러자 조는 문을 열고, 창가에서 정신을 잃고 쓰러진 (그러나 내 생각에 먼저 싸움 구경부터 한) 누나를 들어 올렸다. 누나는 집 안으로 옮겨져 눕혀졌고, 정신을 차리도록 권유받았으나, 발버둥치며 조의 머리카락을 움켜쥐는 것 외에는 아무것도 하려 들지 않았다. 그러고는 모든 소동 뒤에 뒤따르는 그 묘한 정적과 고요가 찾아왔다. 그러고는, 내가 늘 그런 잠잠함과 결부시켜 온 그 막연한 느낌 — 곧 일요일이고 누군가가 죽었다는 느낌 — 과 함께, 나는 옷을 갈아입으러 위층으로 올라갔다.\n\n내가 다시 내려와 보니, 조와 올릭은 마당을 쓸고 있었고, 올릭의 콧구멍 하나에 찢긴 자국이 난 것 말고는 흐트러진 흔적이라곤 없었는데, 그 자국은 인상적이지도 보기 좋지도 않았다. 졸리 바지선 주막에서 맥주 한 단지가 와 있었고, 둘은 그것을 번갈아 가며 평화롭게 나눠 마시고 있었다. 그 잠잠함은 조에게 진정시키고 사색에 잠기게 하는 영향을 끼쳐서, 그는 내가 길로 나설 때까지 따라 나오더니, 내게 도움이 될 만한 작별의 한마디라며 이렇게 말했다. \"한바탕 날뛰다가도, 핍, 그 날뛰는 것이 가라앉기 마련이니, 핍 — 인생이란 그런 거다!\"\n\n내가 어떤 우스꽝스러운 감정으로 (어른에게는 자못 진지한 감정도 소년에게서는 퍽 우스꽝스럽게 여기는 법이니) 다시금 해비셤 양의 집으로 가게 되었는지는, 여기서 별로 중요치 않다. 내가 종을 울릴 결심이 설 때까지 몇 번이고 대문 앞을 오갔는지도, 종을 울리지 않고 그냥 돌아갈까 어떻게 망설였는지도, 만약 내 시간이 온전히 내 것이었다면 틀림없이 다시 오기로 하고 그냥 돌아갔으리라는 것도, 다 마찬가지로 중요치 않다.\n\n세라 포켓 양이 대문으로 나왔다. 에스텔라는 없었다.\n\n\"어쩐 일이지? 또 왔어?\" 포켓 양이 말했다. \"뭘 원하는 거지?\"\n\n내가 그저 해비셤 양이 어떻게 지내시는지 보러 왔을 뿐이라고 말하자, 세라는 나를 돌려보낼지 말지 분명히 망설였다. 그러나 그 책임을 떠안기는 꺼려졌던지, 그녀는 나를 들여보냈고, 이윽고 \"올라오라\"는 무뚝뚝한 전갈을 가져왔다.\n\n모든 것이 그대로였고, 해비셤 양은 혼자였다.\n\n\"그래?\" 그녀가 내게 두 눈을 박으며 말했다. \"아무것도 바라는 게 없기를 바란다. 너는 아무것도 못 얻을 테니.\"\n\n\"정말 아무것도 없어요, 해비셤 양. 그저 제가 도제 생활을 아주 잘해 나가고 있으며, 늘 당신께 깊이 감사드리고 있다는 걸 알려 드리고 싶었을 뿐이에요.\"\n\n\"됐다, 됐어!\" 예의 그 가만있지 못하는 손가락을 놀리며 그녀가 말했다. \"이따금 오너라. 네 생일에 오너라. 아!\" 그녀가 갑자기 외치며 자기 몸과 의자를 내 쪽으로 돌렸다. \"에스텔라를 찾아 두리번거리는 거지? 응?\"\n\n나는 두리번거리고 있었다 — 사실 에스텔라를 찾아서. 그래서 나는 그녀가 잘 지내기를 바란다고 더듬더듬 말했다.\n\n\"외국에 가 있다.\" 해비셤 양이 말했다. \"숙녀가 되도록 교육받고 있지. 손이 닿지 않을 만큼 멀리. 그 어느 때보다 예뻐졌고. 그 애를 보는 이마다 다들 우러러본단다. 그 애를 잃었다는 느낌이 드느냐?\"\n\n그 마지막 말을 입에 올리는 그녀의 어조에는 어찌나 악의에 찬 즐거움이 서려 있었던지, 그리고 어찌나 듣기 싫은 웃음을 터뜨렸던지, 나는 무어라 말해야 할지 갈피를 잡지 못했다. 그녀는 나를 물러가게 함으로써, 내가 고민하는 수고를 덜어 주었다. 호두 껍데기 같은 얼굴을 한 세라가 내 등 뒤로 대문을 닫았을 때, 나는 그 어느 때보다도 내 집과 내 일과 모든 것에 불만을 느꼈다. 그리고 그것이 그 나들이로 내가 얻은 전부였다.\n\n내가 큰길을 따라 어슬렁거리며 가게의 진열창들을 시무룩하게 들여다보면서, 내가 신사라면 무엇을 살까 궁리하고 있을 때, 책방에서 누가 나오나 했더니 다름 아닌 워프슬 씨였다. 워프슬 씨는 손에 그 애절한 비극 「조지 반웰」을 들고 있었는데, 그것은 바로 그 순간 그가 육 펜스를 들여 사들인 것으로, 펌블추크와 함께 차를 마실 참이었던 그가 그 한마디 한마디를 죄다 펌블추크의 머리 위에 쏟아부을 작정이었던 것이다. 그는 나를 보자마자, 마치 특별한 섭리가 자기 앞에 낭독을 들어 줄 도제 하나를 보내 주었다고 여기는 듯했다. 그러더니 나를 붙들고는, 펌블추크의 응접실까지 자기와 동행하라고 우겨 댔다. 집에 가 봤자 비참하리라는 것을 아는 데다, 밤은 어둡고 길은 음산했으며, 길동무라면 그 누구든 없는 것보다는 나았으므로, 나는 별 저항을 하지 않았다. 그리하여 우리는, 거리와 가게들에 막 불이 켜질 무렵 펌블추크의 집으로 들어섰다.\n\n나는 「조지 반웰」의 다른 어떤 공연에도 입회한 적이 없으니, 그것이 보통 얼마나 걸리는지는 모른다. 그러나 그날 밤 그것이 아홉 시 반까지 걸렸다는 것만은 똑똑히 안다. 그리고 워프슬 씨가 뉴게이트 감옥에 이르렀을 때, 나는 그가 도무지 교수대로 가지 않으리라 여겼으니, 그가 그 치욕스러운 행로의 그 어느 시기보다도 훨씬 더 느려졌기 때문이다. 나는 그가 결국 한창때에 꺾여 버린다고 한탄하는 것이 좀 지나치다 싶었다. 마치 자기 행로가 시작된 이래로 잎이 하나둘 떨어지듯 줄곧 시들어 가고 있지 않았다는 듯이 말이다. 그러나 이것은 단지 길이와 지루함의 문제일 뿐이었다. 나를 따끔하게 쑤신 것은, 그 사건 전체가 아무 죄 없는 나 자신과 동일시되었다는 점이었다. 반웰이 그릇된 길로 들어서기 시작했을 때, 단언컨대 나는 정말이지 미안한 심정마저 느꼈으니, 펌블추크의 분개한 응시가 어찌나 그 죄를 내게 추궁해 댔던지. 워프슬 또한 나를 가장 나쁜 모습으로 그려 내려고 애를 썼다. 흉포하면서도 눈물겨운 모습으로, 나는 그 어떤 정상 참작의 여지도 없이 내 숙부를 살해한 자로 둔갑했다. 밀우드는 매번 논쟁에서 나를 꺾어 눌렀다. 내 주인의 딸이 나 같은 것에 손톱만큼이라도 마음을 쓴다는 것은 순전한 편집광적 망상이 되어 버렸다. 그리고 그 운명의 아침에 내가 보인 헐떡이며 우물쭈물하던 행태에 대해 내가 변명할 수 있는 것이라곤, 그것이 내 성품의 전반적 무기력함에 걸맞은 것이었다는 말뿐이다. 내가 무사히 교수형을 당하고 워프슬이 책을 덮은 뒤에도, 펌블추크는 앉아서 나를 빤히 노려보고, 고개를 절레절레 흔들며, \"정신 차려라, 얘야, 정신 차려!\" 하고 말했다. 마치 내가, 누군가를 꾀어 내 은인이 되는 약점을 부리게만 할 수 있다면 가까운 친척을 살해할 작정이라는 것이 익히 알려진 사실이기라도 하다는 듯이.\n\n그 모든 게 끝나고, 내가 워프슬 씨와 함께 집으로 걸어 나섰을 때는 무척 어두운 밤이었다. 읍내를 벗어나자, 우리는 짙은 안개를 만났고, 그것은 축축하고 두텁게 내려앉았다. 통행료 징수소의 등불은 흐릿하게 번져, 보아하니 평소의 자리에서 한참 벗어나 있는 듯했고, 그 빛줄기는 안개 위에서 단단한 물체처럼 보였다. 우리가 그것을 눈여겨보며, 우리 습지의 어느 방향에서 바람이 바뀌면 안개가 피어오른다고 이야기하고 있을 때, 우리는 통행료 징수소 바람막이 쪽에 어슬렁거리며 서 있는 한 사내와 마주쳤다.\n\n\"여어!\" 우리는 걸음을 멈추며 말했다. \"올릭인가?\"\n\n\"아!\" 그가 어슬렁어슬렁 걸어 나오며 대답했다. \"혹시 길동무라도 있을까 해서 잠깐 서 있었지요.\"\n\n\"늦었구먼.\" 내가 한마디 했다.\n\n올릭은 당연하다는 듯 대꾸했다. \"그래? 댁들도 늦었잖소.\"\n\n\"우리는 말이오.\" 방금 끝낸 자기 공연으로 한껏 들떠 있던 워프슬 씨가 말했다. \"우리는 말이오, 올릭 씨, 지적인 저녁 시간을 만끽하고 오는 길이라오.\"\n\n늙은 올릭은 그에 대해 할 말이 없다는 듯 으르렁거렸고, 우리는 다 함께 걸음을 이었다. 나는 이윽고 그에게 반나절 휴가를 읍내에서 이리저리 다니며 보냈느냐고 물었다.\n\n\"그래.\" 그가 말했다. \"내내 그랬지. 댁네 바로 뒤를 따라 들어왔어. 댁네를 보진 못했지만, 꽤 가까이 뒤따라온 게 틀림없어. 그건 그렇고, 또 대포가 울리고 있구먼.\"\n\n\"감옥선에서?\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"그래! 새장에서 새 몇 마리가 날아갔나 봐. 어두워질 무렵부터 대포가 울리고 있어. 이제 곧 한 발 들릴 거야.\"\n\n과연, 우리가 몇 야드 더 채 걷기도 전에, 그 익히 기억하는 굉음이 안개에 먹먹해진 채 우리 쪽으로 다가왔고, 강가의 낮은 땅을 따라 무겁게 굴러갔다. 마치 도망자들을 뒤쫓으며 위협하기라도 하는 듯이.\n\n\"빠져나가기 딱 좋은 밤이지.\" 올릭이 말했다. \"이런 밤엔 날아오른 감옥새를 떨어뜨리기가 영 난감할 거야.\"\n\n그 화제는 내게 여러 생각을 불러일으켰고, 나는 잠자코 그것을 곱씹었다. 워프슬 씨는, 그날 저녁 비극에서 보답받지 못한 숙부 역답게, 캠버웰의 제 정원에서 소리 내어 명상에 잠기기 시작했다. 올릭은 두 손을 주머니에 찔러 넣은 채, 내 옆에서 무겁게 어슬렁거렸다. 무척 어둡고, 무척 축축하고, 무척 질척거려서, 우리는 그렇게 첨벙첨벙 걸어갔다. 이따금 그 신호 대포 소리가 다시 우리에게 들이닥쳤고, 또다시 강줄기를 따라 시무룩하게 굴러갔다. 나는 나 자신과 내 생각에만 잠겨 있었다. 워프슬 씨는 캠버웰에서 온화하게 죽고, 보즈워스 들판에서 더없이 용맹하게 죽고, 글래스턴베리에서 극심한 고통 속에 죽었다. 올릭은 이따금 으르렁거렸다. \"두들겨 펴라, 두들겨 펴라 — 늙은 클렘! 튼튼한 놈한텐 한 잔 쨍 — 늙은 클렘!\" 나는 그가 술을 마신 줄 알았으나, 그는 취한 것은 아니었다.\n\n그렇게 우리는 마을에 이르렀다. 우리가 마을로 다가간 길은 세 졸리 바지선 주막을 지나게 되어 있었는데, 우리는 — 열한 시였는데도 — 그곳이 소동에 휩싸여, 문이 활짝 열려 있고, 황급히 집어 들었다 내려놓은 듯한 평소답지 않은 불빛들이 여기저기 흩어져 있는 것을 보고 놀랐다. 워프슬 씨가 무슨 일인지 물어보려고 (탈옥수가 잡힌 거라고 짐작하며) 들어갔으나, 이내 다급히 뛰쳐나왔다.\n\n\"무슨 일이 났어.\" 그가 멈추지도 않고 말했다. \"자네 집에서, 핍. 다들 뛰어!\"\n\n\"무슨 일인데요?\" 내가 그에게 보조를 맞추며 물었다. 올릭도 내 옆에서 그렇게 했다.\n\n\"나도 잘 모르겠어. 조 가저리가 나가 있는 동안 집에 누가 난폭하게 침입한 모양이야. 탈옥수들 소행으로 짐작돼. 누군가 습격당해서 다쳤다는군.\"\n\n우리는 너무 빨리 달리느라 더 말을 주고받을 틈도 없었고, 우리 집 부엌에 들어설 때까지 한 번도 멈추지 않았다. 부엌은 사람들로 가득했다. 온 마을 사람이 거기에, 혹은 마당에 나와 있었다. 그리고 외과 의사가 있었고, 조가 있었고, 한 무리의 여인들이 있었으니, 모두가 부엌 한가운데 바닥에 모여 있었다. 일 없이 구경하던 사람들이 나를 보고는 물러섰고, 그리하여 나는 내 누나를 알아보게 되었다. 누나는 의식도 미동도 없이 맨바닥에 누워 있었으니, 얼굴을 불 쪽으로 돌리고 있을 때 어떤 알 수 없는 손이 뒤통수에 가한 무시무시한 일격에 쓰러뜨려진 것이었다. 그리고 조의 아내로 있는 한, 누나는 다시는 한바탕 날뛸 운명이 못 되었다.\n\n# Chapter XVI.\n\n조지 반웰* 생각으로 머릿속이 가득 찼던 나는, 처음에는 누나가 당한 그 습격에 나 역시 어떤 식으로든 손을 댔음에 틀림없다고, 적어도 누나의 가까운 친척이자 세상이 다 알다시피 그녀에게 신세를 진 처지인 만큼, 나야말로 다른 누구보다 더 정당한 혐의의 대상이라고 믿고 싶어졌다. 그러나 이튿날 아침 한결 또렷한 빛 속에서 그 일을 다시 곰곰이 따져 보고 사방에서 오가는 논의를 듣기 시작하면서, 나는 좀 더 이치에 닿는 다른 견해를 갖게 되었다.\n* 조지 반웰: 조지 릴로의 희곡 「런던의 상인」 주인공으로, 숙부를 살해하고 처형되는 인물.\n\n조는 여덟 시 십오 분부터 열 시 십오 분 전까지 「세 명랑한 뱃사공」에서 담배를 피우고 있었다. 그가 거기 있는 동안, 누나는 부엌문 앞에 서 있는 것이 목격되었고, 집으로 돌아가던 한 농장 일꾼과 안녕히 가시라는 인사를 주고받았다. 그 사내는 누나를 본 시각에 대해 아홉 시 이전이었음에 틀림없다는 것 이상으로 더 정확히 말하지 못했다(더 정확히 말하려고 애쓰자 그는 도리어 갈피를 못 잡고 헤맸다). 조가 열 시 오 분 전에 집에 돌아왔을 때, 그는 누나가 바닥에 쓰러져 있는 것을 발견하고 곧바로 사람을 불러 도움을 청했다. 그때 불은 유난히 사위어 있지도 않았고, 촛불 심지도 그리 길지 않았다. 다만 촛불은 꺼져 있었다.\n\n집 안 어느 구석에서도 없어진 물건은 없었다. 또한 촛불이 꺼진 것을 — 그것은 문과 누나 사이의 탁자 위에 놓여 있었으니, 그녀가 불을 바라보고 서서 가격당했을 때는 그녀의 등 뒤에 있었던 셈이다 — 제외하면, 부엌 안에 흐트러진 흔적이라곤, 그녀 자신이 쓰러지고 피를 흘리면서 만든 것 외에는 아무것도 없었다. 그러나 그 현장에는 주목할 만한 증거 한 가지가 있었다. 그녀는 무언가 무디고 무거운 것으로 머리와 등을 가격당했던 것이다. 그렇게 매를 맞은 뒤, 그녀가 엎드린 채 쓰러져 있을 때 무언가 무거운 것이 상당한 힘으로 그녀에게 내던져졌다. 그리고 조가 그녀를 일으켜 안았을 때 그녀 옆 바닥에는, 줄칼로 잘려 두 동강 난 죄수의 다리 쇠고랑이 있었다.\n\n그런데 조는 대장장이의 눈으로 그 쇠고랑을 살펴보고는, 그것이 얼마 전에 줄칼로 잘린 것이라고 단언했다. 그 소식을 좇아 사람들이 감옥선으로 달려갔고, 거기서 사람들이 와서 그 쇠고랑을 살펴본 끝에 조의 견해는 확증되었다. 그들은 그것이, 한때 분명히 속해 있었을 그 감옥선을 언제 떠났는지까지는 단정 짓지 못했으나, 그 특정한 족쇄가 어젯밤 탈주한 두 죄수 중 어느 쪽도 차고 있던 것이 아니라는 점만은 확실히 안다고 주장했다. 게다가 그 두 사람 중 하나는 이미 다시 붙잡혔는데, 자기 쇠고랑을 벗어 버리지 못한 상태였다.\n\n내가 아는 바를 알고 있던 나는, 여기서 나 나름의 추론을 세웠다. 나는 그 쇠고랑이 나의 죄수의 쇠고랑이라고 — 습지에서 그가 줄칼로 갈고 있던 것을 내가 보고 또 그 소리를 들었던 바로 그 쇠고랑이라고 — 믿었으나, 내 마음은 그가 그것을 그 마지막 용도로 썼다고 그를 탓하지는 않았다. 왜냐하면 나는 다른 두 사람 가운데 하나가 그것을 손에 넣어 이 잔인한 짓에 썼으리라고 믿었기 때문이다. 올릭이거나, 아니면 내게 줄칼을 보여 주었던 그 낯선 사내거나.\n\n자, 올릭으로 말하자면, 그는 우리가 통행세 징수소에서 그를 만났을 때 우리에게 말한 그대로 읍내에 갔었고, 저녁 내내 읍내 여기저기서 목격되었으며, 여러 선술집에서 갖가지 패거리와 어울렸고, 나와 워플 씨와 함께 돌아왔다. 그에게 불리한 것이라곤 그 말다툼 하나뿐이었는데, 누나는 그뿐 아니라 자기 주변의 모든 이와 수만 번도 더 다툰 사람이었다. 그 낯선 사내로 말하자면, 만약 그가 자기 지폐 두 장을 되찾으러 돌아온 것이라면 그것을 두고 다툴 일은 있을 수 없었다. 누나는 그것을 돌려줄 만반의 채비가 되어 있었으니까. 게다가 언쟁이라곤 없었다. 습격한 자는 너무도 소리 없이 갑작스레 들어와, 그녀가 미처 돌아볼 새도 없이 쓰러뜨려졌던 것이다.\n\n내가, 비록 전혀 의도치 않았다 해도, 그 흉기를 제공한 셈이라고 생각하니 끔찍했으나, 달리 생각하기란 좀처럼 어려웠다. 마침내 내 어린 시절의 그 주술을 풀고 조에게 모든 이야기를 털어놓아야 할 것인가를 거듭 곱씹는 동안, 나는 이루 말할 수 없는 고통에 시달렸다. 그 후로 몇 달 동안, 나는 날마다 그 문제를 끝내 안 된다는 쪽으로 결론지었다가, 이튿날 아침이면 다시 그 문제를 꺼내 새로이 따지곤 했다. 그 다툼은 결국 이러한 데로 귀착되었다. 그 비밀은 이제 너무도 오래된 것이어서, 내 안에 깊이 자라나 나 자신의 일부가 되어 버린 까닭에, 나로서는 그것을 뜯어낼 수가 없었다. 이미 그토록 많은 화를 불러일으킨 마당에, 만약 조가 그 이야기를 믿는다면 이제 그 어느 때보다도 조를 나에게서 멀어지게 할 가능성이 크다는 두려움에 더하여, 나에게는 또 다른 억제하는 두려움이 있었으니, 그가 그것을 믿지 않고 도리어 그 허무맹랑한 개들이며 송아지 고기 토막과 한데 묶어 터무니없는 지어낸 이야기쯤으로 치부해 버리리라는 두려움이었다. 그러나 나는 물론 나 자신과 타협하고 말았는데 — 일이 늘 그렇게 되고 마는 마당에, 나는 옳고 그름 사이에서 흔들리고 있던 게 아니었던가? — 그리하여, 습격한 자를 찾아내는 데 도움이 될 새로운 기회와도 같은 그러한 새로운 계기를 보게 된다면 모든 것을 낱낱이 털어놓으리라 마음먹었다.\n\n그 순경들과 런던에서 온 보 스트리트 경관들은 — 이 일은 빨간 조끼를 입은 경찰이 사라지기 전 시절에 일어난 것이었으니까 — 한두 주일 동안 집 주위를 맴돌며, 다른 그런 사건들에서 이런 부류의 권력자들이 했다고 내가 듣고 읽은 것과 거의 비슷한 일들을 했다. 그들은 누가 봐도 엉뚱한 사람을 여럿 잡아들였고, 엉뚱한 생각에 머리를 호되게 들이받았으며, 정황에서 생각을 끌어내려 하기보다 정황을 그 생각에 끼워 맞추려고 끈질기게 애썼다. 또한 그들은 「명랑한 뱃사공」 문 앞에 서서, 동네 전체를 감탄으로 가득 채울 만큼 무언가 다 안다는 듯하고 점잔 빼는 표정을 지었다. 그리고 술을 마시는 데도 어딘가 알 수 없는 비밀스러운 태도가 있어, 그것은 거의 범인을 잡는 것이나 다름없어 보였다. 다만 완전히 그렇지는 못했으니, 그들은 끝내 범인을 잡지 못했기 때문이다.\n* 보 스트리트 경관: 런던 보 스트리트에 본부를 둔 초기 형사대원.\n\n이 헌법상의 권력자들이 흩어진 뒤로도 한참 동안, 누나는 자리에 누운 채 몹시 앓았다. 시력에 장애가 생겨 사물이 여러 개로 겹쳐 보였고, 실제 물건 대신 허깨비 같은 찻잔이며 포도주잔을 더듬어 잡으려 했다. 청력도 크게 손상되었고, 기억력도 마찬가지였으며, 말도 알아들을 수 없었다. 마침내 부축을 받아 아래층으로 내려올 만큼 회복되었을 때에도, 여전히 내 석판을 늘 그녀 곁에 두어, 말로 가리키지 못하는 것을 글로 가리킬 수 있게 해야 했다. 누나는 (지독히 서투른 글씨체는 차치하고라도) 철자가 형편없는 사람이었고, 조 또한 읽는 데는 영 시원찮은 사람이었던지라, 두 사람 사이에 별난 혼선이 빚어지곤 했고, 나는 늘 그것을 풀라고 불려 나갔다. 약 대신 양고기를 가져다 바치고, 조(Joe) 대신 차(Tea)를, 베이컨(bacon) 대신 빵집 주인(baker)을 갖다 댄 것 따위는 내 실수 가운데서도 가장 가벼운 축에 들었다.\n\n그러나 누나의 성미는 크게 나아졌고, 그녀는 참을성이 있었다. 온몸의 동작이 떨리며 불안정한 상태가 이내 그녀의 평소 모습의 일부가 되었고, 그 후로 두세 달 간격으로 그녀는 곧잘 두 손을 머리에 갖다 대고는, 한 번에 일주일쯤 어떤 음울한 정신착란에 빠져 있곤 했다. 우리는 그녀를 돌볼 마땅한 사람을 찾지 못해 난감해하던 차에, 마침맞게 한 가지 사정이 생겨 우리의 짐을 덜어 주었다. 워플 씨의 종조모가, 빠져 있던 그 끈질긴 살아 있는 버릇을 끝내 이겨 냈고, 비디가 우리 살림의 일원이 된 것이다.\n\n누나가 부엌에 다시 모습을 드러낸 지 한 달쯤 지났을 무렵, 비디는 자기의 온 세간을 죄다 담은 작은 얼룩무늬 상자 하나를 들고 우리에게 와서, 이 집안의 축복이 되었다. 무엇보다도 그녀는 조에게 축복이었다. 그 정다운 늙은이는 망가진 아내의 모습을 끊임없이 바라보느라 마음이 몹시 무너져 있었고, 저녁이면 그녀를 보살피다 말고 이따금 나를 돌아보며, 푸른 두 눈을 적신 채 이렇게 말하곤 했던 것이다. \"한때는 참으로 훤칠한 여인이었는데 말이다, 핍!\" 비디가 마치 갓난아기 적부터 그녀를 익혀 온 사람처럼 당장에 더없이 영리하게 그녀를 떠맡자, 조는 어느 정도 한층 조용해진 자기 삶을 누릴 줄 알게 되었고, 이따금 「명랑한 뱃사공」에 내려가 기분을 푸는 변화도 가질 수 있게 되었다. 경찰들이 너 나 할 것 없이 가엾은 조를 어느 정도 의심했다는 것(정작 그는 결코 알지 못했지만), 그리고 그들이 하나같이 그를 일찍이 마주친 가장 음흉한 인물 가운데 하나로 여기는 데 의견을 같이했다는 것은, 그 경찰들다운 노릇이었다.\n\n비디가 새 소임에서 거둔 첫 개가는, 나를 완전히 두 손 들게 했던 한 가지 난제를 풀어낸 것이었다. 나는 그것을 두고 무던히도 애썼으나 도무지 갈피를 잡지 못했다. 일은 이러했다.\n\n누나는 거듭거듭 석판 위에, 묘하게 생긴 T 자처럼 보이는 글자 하나를 그려 놓고는, 그것이 자기가 특별히 원하는 무엇인 양 더없이 간절하게 우리의 주의를 그리로 끌었다. 나는 타르(tar)에서 토스트(toast), 통(tub)에 이르기까지 T로 시작하는 것이라면 손에 잡히는 대로 다 가져다 대 보았으나 헛수고였다. 마침내 그 표시가 망치처럼 보인다는 데 생각이 미쳐, 내가 그 단어를 누나의 귀에 대고 우렁차게 외치자, 누나는 탁자를 두드리기 시작하면서 어느 정도 동의하는 기색을 보였다. 그래서 나는 우리 집에 있는 망치란 망치는 하나씩 차례로 다 들여왔으나, 소용이 없었다. 그다음에 나는 목발을 떠올렸는데, 모양이 꽤 비슷했던 것이다. 그래서 마을에서 하나를 빌려 와 상당한 자신감을 가지고 누나에게 내보였다. 그러나 그것을 보여 주자 누나가 어찌나 세차게 고개를 흔들던지, 우리는 그 쇠약하고 망가진 상태에서 그녀가 목을 삐지나 않을까 겁이 났다.\n\n누나는 비디가 자기 뜻을 아주 빨리 알아챈다는 것을 알게 되자, 이 알 수 없는 표시를 다시 석판에 그려 놓았다. 비디는 그것을 골똘히 들여다보고, 내 설명을 듣고, 누나를 골똘히 바라보고, 조를(석판 위에서 그는 늘 이름의 첫 글자로 나타내어졌다) 골똘히 바라보더니, 대장간으로 달려갔고 조와 나도 그 뒤를 따랐다.\n\n\"아니, 당연하잖아요!\" 비디가 의기양양한 얼굴로 외쳤다. \"모르시겠어요? 바로 그 사람이라고요!\"\n\n틀림없이 올릭이었다! 누나는 그의 이름을 잊어버려, 그의 망치로밖에 그를 나타낼 수 없었던 것이다. 우리는 그에게 어째서 부엌으로 들어와 주었으면 하는지를 말했고, 그는 천천히 망치를 내려놓고, 팔로 이마를 훔치고, 다시 앞치마로 한 번 더 닦더니, 무릎을 헐겁게 구부정하니 굽힌, 그를 유난히 두드러지게 하던 그 묘한 부랑자 같은 걸음새로 어슬렁어슬렁 나왔다.\n\n고백하건대, 나는 누나가 그를 고발하리라 예상했고, 그와는 다른 결과에 실망했다. 누나는 그와 좋은 사이로 지내고 싶다는 더없는 열망을 드러냈고, 마침내 그가 데려와진 것을 보고 분명 무척 기뻐했으며, 그에게 마실 것을 좀 주었으면 하는 시늉을 했다. 그녀는 그가 자신의 환대를 흔쾌히 받아들이는지 특별히 확인하고 싶어 하는 듯 그의 얼굴빛을 살폈고, 그의 마음을 누그러뜨리려는 온갖 바람을 다 내보였으며, 그 모든 행동에는 비굴하게 비위를 맞추려는 기색이 어려 있었으니, 그것은 내가 모진 주인을 대하는 아이의 태도에서 본 적이 있는 그런 기색이었다. 그날 이후로, 누나가 석판에 망치를 그리지 않는 날이 드물었고, 올릭이 어슬렁어슬렁 들어와, 나와 다름없이 그것을 어찌 받아들여야 할지 모르겠다는 듯, 그녀 앞에 무뚝뚝하게 버티고 서지 않는 날 또한 드물었다.\n\n# Chapter XVII.\n\n이제 나는 도제 생활의 규칙적인 일상에 빠져들었는데, 그 일상이 마을과 습지의 경계를 넘어 달라지는 일이라곤, 내 생일이 돌아오고 내가 미스 해비셤을 다시 한번 찾아가는 것보다 더 특기할 만한 사건은 없었다. 미스 새러 포켓은 여전히 문간에서 임무를 수행하고 있었고, 미스 해비셤은 내가 떠날 때 그대로의 모습이었으며, 똑같은 말은 아닐지라도 똑같은 방식으로 에스텔라에 대해 이야기했다. 면담은 몇 분밖에 이어지지 않았고, 내가 돌아갈 때 그녀는 내게 일 기니를 주며 다음 생일에 또 오라고 일렀다. 곧바로 말해 두자면, 이것은 해마다 거르지 않는 관례가 되었다. 첫 번에는 그 기니 받기를 사양하려 했으나, 더 바라는 게 있느냐고 그녀가 몹시 성을 내며 묻게 만드는 것 외에는 별 소득이 없었다. 그래서 그때도, 그 이후로도, 나는 그것을 받았다.\n\n그 음침한 옛집도, 어두운 방 안의 누런 불빛도, 화장대 거울 옆 의자에 앉은 빛바랜 유령도 어찌나 변함이 없던지, 나는 마치 멈춰 선 시계들이 그 신비로운 곳의 시간을 멈춰 세운 것만 같았고, 나와 그 바깥의 다른 모든 것이 나이 들어 가는 동안에도 그곳만은 가만히 정지해 있는 듯한 느낌을 받았다. 실제 사실에 있어서나, 그 집에 대한 내 생각과 기억에 있어서나, 그 집에는 햇빛이 결코 들지 않았다. 그것이 나를 혼란스럽게 했고, 그 영향 아래 나는 마음속으로 여전히 내 직업을 미워하고 집을 부끄러워했다.\n\n그러나 어느새 나는 비디에게 일어난 변화를 의식하게 되었다. 그녀의 신발은 뒤축이 닳지 않게 잘 갖추어졌고, 머리칼은 윤기 있고 단정해졌으며, 손은 늘 깨끗했다. 그녀는 아름답지 않았다. 평범했고, 에스텔라처럼 될 수는 없었다. 그러나 그녀는 상냥하고 건강하고 마음씨가 고왔다. 그녀가 우리와 함께 지낸 지 일 년이 채 안 되었을 무렵(그 생각이 문득 들었을 때 그녀가 상복을 막 벗은 참이었던 것을 기억한다), 나는 어느 날 저녁 그녀에게 묘하게 사려 깊고 주의 깊은 눈, 무척 예쁘고 무척 선한 눈이 있다는 것을 스스로 깨달았다.\n\n그것은 내가 골몰하던 일에서 — 일종의 책략으로 한꺼번에 두 가지 방향으로 나를 향상시키려고 어느 책의 몇 구절을 베껴 쓰던 일에서 — 고개를 들었다가, 비디가 내 하는 일을 지켜보고 있는 것을 보았기 때문이었다. 나는 펜을 내려놓았고, 비디는 바느질감을 내려놓지는 않은 채 손을 멈추었다.\n\n\"비디,\" 내가 말했다. \"넌 어떻게 그러는 거야? 내가 아주 멍청한 거든지, 아니면 네가 아주 영리한 거든지 둘 중 하나야.\"\n\n\"내가 뭘 어떻게 한다는 거지? 나는 모르겠는걸.\" 비디가 미소 지으며 대꾸했다.\n\n그녀는 우리 집안 살림 전체를 꾸려 나갔고, 그것도 놀라우리만치 잘 해냈다. 그러나 내가 말한 것은 그게 아니었다. 다만 그 살림 솜씨가 내가 정작 말하려던 바를 한층 더 놀랍게 만들어 줄 따름이었다.\n\n\"비디, 넌 어떻게 그래.\" 내가 말했다. \"내가 배우는 걸 다 배우고, 늘 나를 따라잡는 거 말이야.\" 나는 내 지식에 다소 우쭐대기 시작하던 참이었다. 생일에 받은 기니를 거기 쓰고, 용돈의 대부분을 같은 투자에 떼어 두었기 때문이다. 다만 그 보잘것없는 지식이 그 값에 비하면 터무니없이 비싼 것이었음은, 지금에 와서는 의심할 여지가 없다.\n\n\"나야말로 너한테 묻고 싶은걸.\" 비디가 말했다. \"넌 어떻게 그러느냐고.\"\n\n\"아니지. 나는 밤에 대장간에서 들어오면, 누구나 내가 거기 매달리는 걸 볼 수 있으니까. 그런데 너는 도무지 거기 매달리는 법이 없잖아, 비디.\"\n\n\"감기처럼 옮은 모양이지, 뭐.\" 비디가 조용히 말하고는 다시 바느질을 계속했다.\n\n나무 의자에 등을 기대고 비디가 고개를 한쪽으로 갸웃한 채 바느질하는 모습을 바라보며 내 생각을 좇다 보니, 나는 그녀가 어지간히 비범한 처녀라는 생각이 들기 시작했다. 이제야 떠올랐기 때문이다. 그녀가 우리 일의 용어며, 우리가 하는 갖가지 작업의 이름이며, 우리가 쓰는 여러 연장에 대해서도 똑같이 통달해 있다는 것이. 요컨대, 내가 아는 것은 무엇이든 비디도 알았다. 이론상으로는 그녀는 이미 나만큼, 아니 나보다 더 훌륭한 대장장이였다.\n\n\"비디, 넌 그런 부류야.\" 내가 말했다. \"기회란 기회는 다 최대한 살리는 부류 말이야. 넌 여기 오기 전엔 기회라곤 없었는데, 이렇게 발전한 걸 보라고!\"\n\n비디는 잠깐 나를 바라보고는 다시 바느질을 계속했다. \"그래도 네 첫 선생은 나였잖아. 안 그래?\" 그녀가 바느질하며 말했다.\n\n\"비디!\" 나는 놀라서 소리쳤다. \"아니, 너 울고 있잖아!\"\n\n\"아니, 안 울어.\" 비디가 고개를 들고 웃으며 말했다. \"그런 생각은 어디서 났대?\"\n\n그녀의 바느질감 위로 눈물 한 방울이 반짝이며 떨어지는 것 말고, 그런 생각이 어디서 났겠는가? 나는 잠자코 앉아, 우프슬 씨의 왕고모할머니가 그토록 살아 있는 나쁜 버릇을 — 어떤 사람들에게는 떨쳐 버리는 것이 그토록 바람직한 버릇을 — 마침내 이겨 내고 세상을 뜨기 전까지, 비디가 얼마나 고된 종살이를 했던가를 되새겼다. 나는 그 초라한 작은 가게와 그 초라하고 시끄러운 작은 야학에서, 늘 끌고 떠메야 했던 그 초라한 무능의 늙은 짐덩어리와 함께, 그녀를 에워쌌던 그 가망 없는 처지를 떠올렸다. 나는 생각했다. 그토록 불우한 시절에도, 지금 비디 안에서 피어나고 있는 그 무언가가 그녀 안에 잠재해 있었음이 틀림없다고. 왜냐하면 처음 마음이 불안하고 불만스러워졌을 때, 나는 당연한 일처럼 그녀에게 도움을 구하러 갔으니까. 비디는 조용히 앉아 바느질하며 더는 눈물을 흘리지 않았고, 내가 그녀를 바라보며 그 모든 것을 곰곰이 생각하는 동안, 어쩌면 내가 비디에게 충분히 고마워하지 않았던 게 아닐까 하는 생각이 들었다. 나는 어쩌면 너무 데면데면했던 것일지도 모르고, 내 속내를 털어놓아 그녀를 좀 더 보살펴 주었어야 했다(비록 내 사색 속에서 그 정확한 표현을 쓴 것은 아니지만).\n\n\"그래, 비디.\" 그것을 다 곱씹고 나서 내가 말했다. \"넌 내 첫 선생이었어. 그것도 우리가 언젠가 이렇게, 이 부엌에서 함께 있게 되리라곤 거의 생각조차 못 하던 시절에 말이야.\"\n\n\"아, 가엾어라!\" 비디가 대답했다. 그 말을 누나에게로 돌리고 자리에서 일어나 누나를 분주히 보살피며 더 편안하게 해 주는 것이, 꼭 자기를 잊고 사는 그녀다웠다. \"그건 참 가슴 아프게도 사실이야!\"\n\n\"자!\" 내가 말했다. \"우리, 예전에 그랬던 것처럼 좀 더 자주 이야기를 나눠야겠어. 그리고 예전처럼 너한테 좀 더 자주 상의도 해야겠고. 비디, 다음 일요일에 습지로 조용히 산책을 가서, 오래도록 이야기를 나누자.\"\n\n이제 누나는 결코 혼자 남겨지는 법이 없었다. 그러나 그 일요일 오후 조는 기꺼이, 그것도 기꺼운 마음 이상으로 누나를 돌보는 일을 떠맡았고, 비디와 나는 함께 밖으로 나갔다. 여름철이었고, 날씨가 아름다웠다. 마을과 교회와 교회 묘지를 지나 습지로 나서서 배들이 미끄러지듯 지나가는 돛을 보기 시작하자, 나는 늘 하던 식으로 미스 해비셤과 에스텔라를 그 풍경과 한데 엮기 시작했다. 강가에 이르러 둑에 앉으니, 발치에서 물이 찰랑거리며 그 소리가 없을 때보다 모든 것을 한결 고요하게 만들어 주었고, 나는 비디를 내 깊은 속마음 안으로 들이기에 좋은 때와 장소라는 결심을 굳혔다.\n\n\"비디,\" 그녀에게 비밀을 지키겠다는 다짐을 받고서 내가 말했다. \"나는 신사가 되고 싶어.\"\n\n\"어머, 나라면 안 그러겠는데!\" 그녀가 대꾸했다. \"그게 잘될 것 같지 않거든.\"\n\n\"비디,\" 내가 다소 매섭게 말했다. \"나한텐 신사가 되고 싶은 특별한 이유가 있어.\"\n\n\"네가 제일 잘 알겠지, 핍. 하지만 넌 지금 이대로가 더 행복하다고 생각하지 않아?\"\n\n\"비디,\" 나는 안달이 나서 소리쳤다. \"나는 지금 이대로는 조금도 행복하지 않아. 나는 내 일도 내 인생도 진저리가 나. 도제로 묶인 뒤로 단 한 번도 둘 중 어느 쪽에도 정이 든 적이 없다고. 바보 같은 소리 하지 마.\"\n\n\"내가 바보 같았어?\" 비디가 조용히 눈썹을 치켜올리며 말했다. \"그렇다면 미안해. 그럴 생각은 아니었어. 나는 그저 네가 잘되고, 네가 편안하기를 바랄 뿐이야.\"\n\n\"그래, 그렇다면 단번에 알아 둬. 지금 사는 삶과 아주 다른 종류의 삶을 살 수 없는 한, 나는 결코 편안할 수도, 편안해질 수도 없어. 비참한 것 말고는 아무것도 될 수 없다고. 자, 비디!\"\n\n\"그것 참 안됐구나!\" 비디가 슬픈 기색으로 고개를 저으며 말했다.\n\n그런데 나 역시 그것을 너무도 자주 안된 일이라 여겨 온 터라, 늘 마음속에서 벌이던 그 묘한 자기와의 다툼 속에서, 비디가 그녀의 심정이자 곧 나 자신의 심정을 입 밖에 내자, 나는 분하고 괴로운 눈물을 쏟을 뻔했다. 나는 그녀의 말이 옳다고, 그것이 무척이나 유감스러운 일임을 안다고, 그러나 그래도 어쩔 도리가 없다고 그녀에게 말했다.\n\n\"내가 정착할 수만 있었다면.\" 내가 손 닿는 데 있는 짧은 풀을 쥐어뜯으며 비디에게 말했다. 한때 머리카락에서 내 감정을 쥐어뜯어 양조장 담벼락에 차 던지던 그때와 흡사하게. \"내가 정착할 수만 있었다면, 그래서 어릴 적에 그랬던 절반만큼이라도 대장간을 좋아할 수만 있었다면, 그게 나한테 훨씬 나았으리라는 걸 알아. 그랬다면 너와 나와 조는 아쉬울 게 없었을 거고, 내가 도제살이를 마쳤을 때 조와 나는 어쩌면 동업자가 되었을지도 모르고, 나는 어쩌면 자라서 너와 사귀게 되었을지도 모르고, 우리는 어느 화창한 일요일에 바로 이 둑에 앉아 있었을지도 모르지. 지금과는 사뭇 다른 사람들이 되어서 말이야. 그랬다면 나는 너한테 충분히 괜찮은 사람이었겠지. 안 그래, 비디?\"\n\n비디는 미끄러져 가는 배들을 바라보며 한숨을 쉬고는 대답으로 말했다. \"응. 나는 그렇게 까다롭지 않으니까.\" 듣기 좋은 말로는 도무지 들리지 않았으나, 나는 그녀의 뜻이 선한 것임을 알았다.\n\n\"그런데 그러기는커녕.\" 풀을 더 쥐어뜯어 한두 잎 씹으며 내가 말했다. \"내가 어떻게 살고 있나 봐. 불만스럽고, 불편하고 — 그리고 아무도 나한테 그런 말을 하지 않았더라면, 내가 거칠고 천하다 한들 나한테 무슨 대수였겠어!\"\n\n비디가 갑자기 얼굴을 내 쪽으로 돌리고는, 미끄러져 가는 배들을 바라볼 때보다 훨씬 더 주의 깊게 나를 보았다.\n\n\"그건 그리 옳은 말도, 그리 점잖은 말도 아니었어.\" 그녀가 다시 배들로 눈길을 돌리며 말했다. \"누가 그런 말을 했는데?\"\n\n나는 당황했다. 어디로 가는지도 채 알지 못한 채 내달려 버린 셈이었기 때문이다. 그러나 이제 와서 얼버무릴 수는 없었으므로, 나는 대답했다. \"미스 해비셤 댁의 아름다운 아가씨가. 그 애는 여태 누구보다도 아름답고, 나는 그 애를 끔찍이 흠모해. 그래서 그 애 때문에 신사가 되고 싶은 거야.\" 이 미친 고백을 하고서, 나는 쥐어뜯은 풀을 강물에 던지기 시작했다. 마치 나 자신도 그것을 따라갈 마음이라도 있는 것처럼.\n\n\"넌 그 애한테 앙갚음을 하려고 신사가 되고 싶은 거야, 아니면 그 애의 마음을 얻으려고?\" 비디가 잠시 뜸을 들인 뒤 조용히 내게 물었다.\n\n\"모르겠어.\" 나는 시무룩하게 대답했다.\n\n\"왜냐하면, 만약 앙갚음을 하려는 거라면.\" 비디가 말을 이었다. \"내 생각엔 — 하지만 네가 제일 잘 알겠지 — 그 애의 말 따위 조금도 마음에 두지 않는 편이 더 낫고 더 떳떳할 것 같아. 그리고 만약 그 애의 마음을 얻으려는 거라면, 내 생각엔 — 하지만 네가 제일 잘 알겠지 — 그 애는 마음을 얻을 만한 가치가 없어.\"\n\n내가 스스로도 여러 번 생각했던 것과 똑같았다. 그 순간 내게 더없이 분명했던 것과 똑같았다. 그러나 가엾고 얼떨떨한 시골 소년인 내가, 가장 훌륭하고 가장 지혜로운 사람들조차 날마다 빠지는 그 놀라운 모순을 어찌 피할 수 있었으랴?\n\n\"다 옳은 말일지도 몰라.\" 내가 비디에게 말했다. \"하지만 나는 그 애를 끔찍이 흠모하는걸.\"\n\n요컨대, 나는 그 대목에 이르자 엎드려 머리 양쪽의 머리카락을 단단히 움켜쥐고 힘껏 쥐어뜯었다. 내 마음의 광기가 어찌나 그토록 미치고 빗나간 것인지를 그 내내 알고 있었으므로, 내가 머리카락을 잡고 얼굴을 들어 올려, 그런 바보에게 속한 죄로 자갈에 처박는 벌을 준다 해도 마땅하리라는 것을 똑똑히 자각하고 있었다.\n\n비디는 더없이 지혜로운 처녀였으니, 더는 나를 타이르려 하지 않았다. 그녀는 일에 거칠어졌어도 편안한 그 손을 내 두 손 위에 차례로 얹고는, 머리카락에서 부드럽게 떼어 냈다. 그러고는 내 어깨를 살며시 토닥이며 달래 주었고, 그동안 나는 소맷자락에 얼굴을 묻은 채 조금 울었다. 꼭 양조장 마당에서 그랬던 것처럼. 그리고 어렴풋이, 내가 누군가에게, 아니 모두에게 몹시 부당한 대접을 받고 있다는 확신이 들었다. 어느 쪽인지는 말할 수 없지만.\n\n\"한 가지는 기쁘다.\" 비디가 말했다. \"그건 네가 나한테 속마음을 털어놓아도 되겠다고 느꼈다는 거야, 핍. 그리고 또 한 가지가 기쁜데, 그건 물론 네가, 내가 그 비밀을 지키고 늘 그만큼 신뢰받을 만하게 처신하리라는 걸 믿어도 된다는 걸 안다는 거야. 만일 네 첫 선생이(저런! 그토록 변변찮은 선생이었고, 자기야말로 가르침이 그토록 절실히 필요했던 선생이었지!) 지금 이 시점에도 네 선생이었다면, 그 선생은 자기가 어떤 가르침을 내줘야 할지 안다고 생각해. 하지만 그건 배우기 어려운 가르침일 테고, 너는 이미 그 선생을 넘어섰으니, 이제 아무 소용이 없겠지.\" 그러고 나서 비디는 나를 위해 조용히 한숨짓고는 둑에서 일어나, 산뜻하고 명랑하게 바뀐 목소리로 말했다. \"조금 더 걸을까, 아니면 집에 갈까?\"\n\n\"비디,\" 나는 일어서서 그녀의 목에 팔을 두르고 입을 맞추며 소리쳤다. \"나는 너한테는 언제나 뭐든지 다 이야기할게.\"\n\n\"네가 신사가 될 때까지겠지.\" 비디가 말했다.\n\n\"내가 결코 신사가 못 되리란 거 너도 알잖아. 그러니까 언제까지나야. 하기야 너한테 뭘 이야기할 일이랄 것도 없지. 너는 내가 아는 건 전부 다 아니까. 며칠 전 밤에 집에서 너한테 말했듯이 말이야.\"\n\n\"아!\" 비디가 거의 속삭이듯이 말하며, 멀리 배들로 눈길을 돌렸다. 그러더니 아까처럼 명랑하게 바뀐 목소리로 되풀이했다. \"조금 더 걸을까, 아니면 집에 갈까?\"\n\n나는 비디에게 조금 더 걷자고 말했고, 우리는 그렇게 했으며, 여름 오후는 차츰 여름 저녁으로 누그러져 갔고, 무척이나 아름다웠다. 나는 생각하기 시작했다. 멈춰 선 시계들이 있는 그 방에서 촛불 아래 거지 떼어 먹기 노름이나 하고 에스텔라에게 멸시당하는 것보다는, 결국 이런 처지에 있는 편이 나로서는 더 자연스럽고 더 건강한 것이 아닐까 하고. 그 애를, 그 밖의 모든 기억과 공상과 함께 머릿속에서 떨쳐 낼 수만 있다면, 그래서 내가 해야 할 일을 기꺼이 즐기겠다고 마음먹고 일에 임해, 그것을 꾸준히 붙들고, 최선을 다할 수만 있다면, 그게 나한테 무척 좋으리라고 생각했다. 나는 스스로에게 물어보았다. 지금 이 순간 비디 대신 에스텔라가 내 곁에 있다면, 그 애가 나를 비참하게 만들리라는 걸 내가 분명히 알고 있지 않으냐고. 나는 그것을 확실히 안다고 인정하지 않을 수 없었고, 스스로에게 말했다. \"핍, 넌 정말 바보로구나!\"\n\n우리는 걸으면서 꽤 많은 이야기를 나누었고, 비디가 하는 말은 죄다 옳게 들렸다. 비디는 결코 모욕을 주는 법도, 변덕을 부리는 법도, 오늘은 비디였다가 내일은 딴사람이 되는 법도 없었다. 나에게 고통을 준다면 그녀는 괴로움만 얻을 뿐 기쁨이라곤 얻지 못했으리라. 그녀는 내 가슴에 상처를 입히느니 차라리 제 가슴에 상처를 입히는 편을 훨씬 더 바랐으리라. 그렇다면 어찌하여 나는 둘 중에 그녀를 한결 더 좋아하지 못했던 것일까?\n\n\"비디,\" 우리가 집으로 걸어가던 중에 내가 말했다. \"네가 나를 바로잡아 줄 수 있으면 좋겠어.\"\n\n\"나도 그럴 수 있으면 좋겠어!\" 비디가 말했다.\n\n\"내가 너를 사랑하게끔 마음만 먹을 수 있다면 — 이렇게 오랜 친구한테 이토록 터놓고 말하는 거, 너 언짢지 않지?\"\n\n\"어머나, 전혀!\" 비디가 말했다. \"나는 신경 쓰지 마.\"\n\n\"내가 그렇게 마음만 먹을 수 있다면, 그게 나한테는 딱 맞는 일일 텐데.\"\n\n\"하지만 넌 절대 그러지 못할걸, 알잖아.\" 비디가 말했다.\n\n그날 저녁의 나로서는, 그것이 몇 시간 전에 그 이야기를 했더라면 들렸을 만큼 그렇게 가망 없는 일로 여겨지지는 않았다. 그래서 나는 그건 그리 확신할 수 없다고 말했다. 그러나 비디는 자기는 확신한다고, 그것도 단호하게 말했다. 마음속으로는 그녀가 옳다고 믿었으나, 그래도 그녀가 그 점에 대해 그토록 단정적인 것이 나는 자못 못마땅했다.\n\n교회 묘지 가까이 이르렀을 때, 우리는 둑길을 건너고 수문 옆 층계 울타리를 넘어야 했다. 그 문에서인지, 골풀 속에서인지, 진창에서인지(그것은 그자의 그 정체된 습성에 꼭 어울리는 곳이었다), 늙은 올릭이 불쑥 솟아올랐다.\n\n\"어이!\" 그가 으르렁댔다. \"둘이 어디 가는 거냐?\"\n\n\"집 말고 어디로 가겠어요?\"\n\n\"그렇다면,\" 그가 말했다. \"내가 너희 집까지 바래다주지 않으면 내가 무지러진 놈이지!\"\n\n이 무지러진다는 형벌은 그가 즐겨 들먹이는 가상의 사례였다. 내가 아는 한 그는 그 말에 무슨 분명한 뜻을 붙인 것은 아니었으나, 자기가 멋대로 갖다 붙인 세례명처럼, 인류를 모욕하고 무언가 야만스럽게 해를 끼친다는 관념을 전하려고 그 말을 썼다. 어렸을 적에 나는, 만일 그가 나를 몸소 무지른다면 날카롭고 뒤틀린 갈고리로 그러리라는 막연한 믿음을 품고 있었다.\n\n비디는 그가 우리와 동행하는 것을 몹시 꺼려서, 내게 속삭였다. \"오지 못하게 해. 나는 저 사람 싫어.\" 나도 그가 싫었으므로, 나는 외람되지만 고맙기는 하나 바래다줄 필요는 없다고 말했다. 그는 그 소식을 너털웃음 소리로 받아넘기고는 뒤로 처졌으나, 조금 떨어진 거리에서 어슬렁어슬렁 우리를 뒤따라왔다.\n\n비디가, 누나가 도무지 진상을 밝히지 못했던 그 흉악한 습격에 그가 가담했던 것은 아닌가 의심하고 있는지 궁금해서, 나는 왜 그가 싫은지 그녀에게 물었다.\n\n\"아!\" 그녀가 어슬렁어슬렁 뒤따라오는 그를 어깨 너머로 흘끔거리며 대답했다. \"왜냐하면 나는 — 나는 그 사람이 나를 좋아할까 봐 무서워.\"\n\n\"저자가 너한테 좋아한다고 말한 적이 있어?\" 나는 분개해서 물었다.\n\n\"아니.\" 비디가 다시 어깨 너머로 흘끔거리며 말했다. \"그렇게 말한 적은 없어. 하지만 내 눈을 마주칠 수 있을 때마다 나한테 춤을 춰 보인단 말이야.\"\n\n이 애정의 표시가 아무리 색다르고 유별난 것이었어도, 나는 그 해석의 정확성을 의심하지 않았다. 나는 늙은 올릭이 감히 그녀를 흠모한다는 데 정말이지 몹시 화가 났다. 마치 그것이 나 자신에 대한 능욕이기라도 한 듯 분이 치솟았다.\n\n\"하지만 그게 너랑은 아무 상관 없잖아, 알지.\" 비디가 차분히 말했다.\n\n\"그래, 비디, 나랑은 아무 상관 없지. 다만 나는 그게 싫어. 나는 그걸 용납할 수가 없어.\"\n\n\"나도 마찬가지야.\" 비디가 말했다. \"그게 너랑은 아무 상관 없지만 말이야.\"\n\n\"바로 그거지.\" 내가 말했다. \"하지만 말해 두는데, 비디, 네가 동의해서 저자가 너한테 춤을 춰 보이는 거라면, 나는 너를 못 볼 거야.\"\n\n그날 밤 이후로 나는 올릭을 눈여겨보았고, 그가 비디에게 춤을 춰 보이기에 좋은 상황만 되면 언제든 그의 앞을 가로막아 그 짓거리를 가려 버렸다. 그는 누나가 갑작스레 그를 마음에 들어 한 까닭에 조의 작업장에 뿌리를 내린 터였으니, 그렇지만 않았다면 나는 그를 내쫓으려고 애를 썼을 것이다. 그는 내 선한 의도를 똑똑히 알아채고 똑같이 갚아 주었는데, 그 점은 그 후에 알게 되었다.\n\n그리고 이제, 내 마음이 그 전에도 충분히 어지럽지 않았다는 듯, 나는 그 어지러움을 오만 갈래로 더 복잡하게 만들어 놓았다. 어떤 때, 어떤 시절에는, 비디가 에스텔라보다 헤아릴 수 없이 더 낫다는 것, 그리고 내가 태어나면서부터 짊어진 그 소박하고 정직한 노동의 삶에는 부끄러워할 만한 구석이라곤 없으며 오히려 자존감과 행복을 누릴 충분한 방편을 내게 베풀어 준다는 것이 분명하게 보였기 때문이다. 그런 때면 나는 결론을 내리곤 했다. 그리운 조와 대장간에 대한 내 반감은 사라졌고, 나는 조와 동업자가 되고 비디와 사귀는 떳떳한 길로 자라 가고 있다고. 그러던 차에 한순간, 해비셤 댁 시절의 어떤 어지러운 기억이 파괴적인 포탄처럼 나를 덮쳐 내 분별을 다시금 흩어 놓곤 했다. 흩어진 분별은 다시 그러모으는 데 오랜 시간이 걸리는 법이고, 게다가 그것을 채 잘 추스르기도 전에, 어쩌면 결국 내 도제살이가 끝나면 미스 해비셤이 내 출셋길을 열어 줄 작정인지도 모른다는 한 가닥 엉뚱한 생각에 그것이 사방으로 흩어져 버리는 일이 잦았다.\n\n만일 내 도제살이의 기한이 다 찼더라면, 짐작건대 그것은 나를 여전히 그 혼란의 절정에 내버려 둔 채 끝나고 말았으리라. 그러나 그 기한은 결코 다 차지 않았으니, 이제 이야기하려는 대로 때 이른 종말을 맞고 말았다.\n\n# Chapter XVIII.\n\n조에게 도제로 들어간 지 사년째 되던 해, 어느 토요일 밤이었다. 세 명랑한 뱃사공 주막의 난롯가에 한 무리가 둘러앉아, 신문을 소리 내어 읽는 윕슬 씨에게 귀를 기울이고 있었다. 나도 그 무리 가운데 하나였다.\n\n세상의 이목을 크게 끈 살인 사건이 일어났던 터라, 윕슬 씨는 눈썹까지 피로 물들어 있었다. 그는 그 묘사에 나오는 끔찍한 형용사 하나하나를 음미하듯 즐겼고, 검시 심문에 나오는 증인이란 증인에 모두 자신을 일치시켰다. 피해자가 되어서는 \"나는 끝장이다.\"라고 가냘프게 신음했고, 살인자가 되어서는 \"너를 손봐 주겠다.\"라고 야만스럽게 고함쳤다. 의학적 증언을 할 때는 우리 고장 의사를 노골적으로 흉내 냈고, 가격하는 소리를 들었다는 늙은 통행료 징수원이 되어서는 그 증인의 정신적 능력을 의심하게 할 만큼 마비된 모양으로 가늘게 떨며 새된 소리를 냈다. 검시관은 윕슬 씨의 손에서 아테네의 티몬이 되었고, 정리는 코리올라누스가 되었다. 그는 더할 나위 없이 자신을 즐겼고, 우리도 모두 즐거워하며 흐뭇하게 편안했다. 이렇게 아늑한 마음 상태로 우리는 고의적 살인이라는 평결에 이르렀다.\n\n그제야, 그 이전이 아니라 비로소, 나는 내 맞은편 긴 의자 등받이에 몸을 기댄 채 지켜보고 있는 낯선 신사 하나를 알아차렸다. 그의 얼굴에는 경멸의 표정이 어려 있었고, 그는 그 여러 얼굴들의 무리를 살피며 커다란 집게손가락 옆구리를 깨물고 있었다.\n\n\"흠!\" 낭독이 끝나자 그 낯선 사내가 윕슬 씨에게 말했다. \"당신은 모든 걸 당신 마음에 흡족하도록 결판내 버렸겠지요, 의심할 여지가 없이?\"\n\n마치 살인자라도 나타난 듯, 모두가 흠칫 놀라며 고개를 들었다. 그는 모두를 차갑고 빈정대는 눈으로 바라보았다.\n\n\"물론 유죄겠지요?\" 그가 말했다. \"어디 말해 보시오. 자!\"\n\n\"선생.\" 윕슬 씨가 대답했다. \"당신과 안면을 트는 영광은 누리지 못했으나, 나는 유죄라고 말하겠소.\" 이에 우리는 모두 용기를 내어 동의하는 웅성거림으로 한목소리를 냈다.\n\n\"당신이 그러리란 걸 알고 있소.\" 낯선 사내가 말했다. \"그러리라는 걸 알고 있었지. 내가 그렇게 말하지 않았소. 그런데 이제 내가 한 가지 묻겠소. 당신은 영국 법이 모든 사람을 유죄로 입증되기 전까지는, 입증되기 전까지는 말이오, 무죄로 추정한다는 사실을 아시오, 모르시오?\"\n\n\"선생,\" 윕슬 씨가 대답하기 시작했다. \"나도 영국인으로서, 나는—\"\n\n\"자!\" 낯선 사내가 그를 향해 집게손가락을 깨물며 말했다. \"질문을 회피하지 마시오. 당신은 그걸 알거나, 아니면 모르거나, 둘 중 하나요. 어느 쪽이오?\"\n\n그는 고개를 한쪽으로 기울이고 몸도 한쪽으로 비스듬히 한 채, 윽박지르듯 따져 묻는 태도로 서서, 마치 윕슬 씨를 콕 집어내려는 듯 그를 향해 집게손가락을 내던졌다가 다시 그 손가락을 깨물었다.\n\n\"자!\" 그가 말했다. \"당신은 그걸 아시오, 모르시오?\"\n\n\"물론 알고 있소.\" 윕슬 씨가 대답했다.\n\n\"물론 알고 있다. 그렇다면 어째서 처음부터 그렇게 말하지 않았소? 자, 이제 한 가지 더 묻겠소.\" 그는 마치 윕슬 씨에 대한 권리라도 가진 듯 그를 틀어쥐었다. \"당신은 이 증인들 가운데 누구도 아직 반대 신문을 받지 않았다는 사실을 아시오?\"\n\n윕슬 씨가 \"내가 말할 수 있는 건 다만—\" 하고 입을 떼는데, 낯선 사내가 그를 막았다.\n\n\"뭐요? 예, 아니요로 대답하지 않겠다는 거요? 자, 다시 한번 해 봅시다.\" 그가 다시 손가락을 내던졌다. \"잘 들으시오. 당신은 이 증인들 가운데 누구도 아직 반대 신문을 받지 않았다는 사실을 알고 있소, 모르고 있소? 자, 나는 당신에게서 단 한마디만 듣고 싶소. 예, 아니면 아니요?\"\n\n윕슬 씨가 머뭇거리자, 우리는 모두 그에 대해 다소 시원찮은 평가를 품기 시작했다.\n\n\"자!\" 낯선 사내가 말했다. \"내가 도와주리다. 당신은 도움을 받을 자격이 없지만, 그래도 도와주겠소. 당신 손에 든 그 신문을 보시오. 그게 뭐요?\"\n\n\"이게 뭐냐고요?\" 윕슬 씨가 어쩔 줄 몰라 하며 그것을 들여다보고는 되물었다.\n\n\"그게,\" 낯선 사내가 더없이 빈정대고 의심하는 투로 말을 이었다. \"당신이 방금 읽고 있던 그 인쇄된 신문이오?\"\n\n\"틀림없이 그렇소.\"\n\n\"틀림없이 그렇다. 자, 그 신문을 펼쳐서, 피고가 자기 법률 고문이 변론을 전적으로 유보하라고 일러 주었다고 명백히 진술했다는 사실이 거기에 또렷이 적혀 있는지 말해 보시오.\"\n\n\"방금 그걸 읽었소.\" 윕슬 씨가 항변했다.\n\n\"방금 뭘 읽었는지는 상관없소, 선생. 나는 당신이 방금 무엇을 읽었는지 묻는 게 아니오. 원한다면 주기도문을 거꾸로 읽어도 좋소. 어쩌면 오늘 이전에 그래 본 적도 있을 테고. 신문을 펼치시오. 아니, 아니, 아니, 친구, 단 윗부분 말고. 그 정도는 알 만하지 않소. 아래쪽, 아래쪽 말이오.\" (우리는 모두 윕슬 씨가 잔꾀로 가득한 사람이라고 여기기 시작했다.) \"어떻소? 찾았소?\"\n\n\"여기 있소.\" 윕슬 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"자, 그 대목을 눈으로 따라가며, 피고가 자기 법률 고문에게서 변론을 전적으로 유보하라는 지시를 받았다고 명백히 진술했다는 사실이 거기 또렷이 적혀 있는지 말해 보시오. 자! 그렇게 읽히오?\"\n\n윕슬 씨가 대답했다. \"정확히 그 단어들은 아니오.\"\n\n\"정확히 그 단어들은 아니다!\" 그 신사가 쓴웃음을 지으며 되받았다. \"그게 정확한 요지요?\"\n\n\"그렇소.\" 윕슬 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"그렇다.\" 낯선 사내가 증인인 윕슬을 향해 오른손을 뻗은 채 좌중의 나머지 사람들을 둘러보며 되풀이했다. \"자 이제 내가 당신에게 묻겠소. 그 대목을 눈앞에 두고서도, 동료 인간을 그 변론도 들어 보지 않고 유죄로 선고한 뒤 베개에 머리를 누일 수 있는 그런 자의 양심에 대해 당신은 뭐라 말하겠소?\"\n\n우리는 모두 윕슬 씨가 우리가 여겨 온 그런 사람이 아니며, 이제 그 정체가 드러나기 시작하는 게 아닐까 의심하기 시작했다.\n\n\"그리고 잊지 마시오, 바로 그 같은 사람이,\" 신사가 윕슬 씨를 향해 손가락을 묵직하게 내던지며 말을 이었다. \"바로 그 같은 사람이 다름 아닌 이 재판에 배심원으로 소환될 수도 있고, 그렇게 스스로를 깊이 옭아맨 채로도, 우리의 군주이신 국왕 폐하와 피고석의 죄수 사이에 벌어진 쟁점을 충실하고 진실하게 심리하며 증거에 따라 진실한 평결을 내리겠노라고, 하느님께 맹세하노라고 신중히 선서한 뒤에, 제 가족의 품으로 돌아가 베개에 머리를 누일 수도 있다는 말이오!\"\n\n우리는 모두 그 가엾은 윕슬이 너무 멀리 갔으며, 아직 시간이 있을 때 그 무모한 행보를 멈추는 편이 낫겠다고 깊이 확신하게 되었다.\n\n그 낯선 신사는 반박할 수 없는 권위를 풍기는 데다, 우리 한 사람 한 사람에 대해 마음만 먹으면 폭로하여 각자를 영락없이 끝장낼 만한 비밀을 알고 있는 듯한 태도로, 긴 의자 등받이에서 몸을 떼고 두 긴 의자 사이, 난롯불 앞 공간으로 들어와, 왼손은 주머니에 찔러 넣고 오른손 집게손가락은 깨물면서 그대로 서 있었다.\n\n\"내가 입수한 정보에 따르면,\" 우리가 모두 그 앞에서 주눅이 들어 있는 동안 그가 우리를 둘러보며 말했다. \"이 가운데 조지프—혹은 조—가저리라는 이름의 대장장이가 있다고 믿을 만한 근거가 있소. 그게 누구요?\"\n\n\"여기 있소이다.\" 조가 말했다.\n\n낯선 신사가 손짓으로 그를 제자리에서 불러냈고, 조는 나아갔다.\n\n\"당신에게는 도제가 하나 있소.\" 낯선 사내가 말을 이었다. \"흔히 핍이라 불리는 자 말이오. 그가 여기 있소?\"\n\n\"제가 여기 있습니다!\" 내가 외쳤다.\n그 낯선 사내는 나를 알아보지 못했으나, 나는 그가 미스 해비셤을 두 번째로 찾아갔을 때 계단에서 마주쳤던 그 신사임을 알아보았다. 그가 긴 의자 너머로 살피던 순간부터 나는 그를 알아보았고, 이제 그가 내 어깨에 손을 얹은 채 마주 서게 되자, 나는 그의 큼직한 머리, 가무잡잡한 안색, 깊이 들어간 눈, 무성한 검은 눈썹, 큼직한 시곗줄, 진한 검은 점처럼 박힌 턱수염과 구레나룻, 그리고 그 커다란 손에서 풍기는 향내 나는 비누 냄새까지, 다시금 하나하나 꼽아 보았다.\n\n\"나는 두 분과 사적으로 이야기를 나누고 싶소.\" 그가 나를 느긋하게 살펴본 뒤 말했다. \"시간이 조금 걸릴 거요. 아무래도 두 분 댁으로 가는 편이 낫겠소. 여기서 내 전할 말을 미리 꺼내고 싶지는 않으니. 그 일은 나중에 두 분이 친구들에게 원하는 만큼 많이든 적게든 전하면 될 일이오. 그건 나와 상관없는 일이오.\"\n\n의아스러운 침묵 속에서 우리 셋은 명랑한 뱃사공 주막을 걸어 나왔고, 또 의아스러운 침묵 속에서 집까지 걸어갔다. 가는 동안 그 낯선 신사는 이따금 나를 쳐다보았고, 이따금 손가락 옆구리를 깨물었다. 집에 가까워지자, 조는 이 자리를 인상적이고 격식 있는 자리로 어렴풋이 받아들였는지, 앞장서 가서 현관문을 열었다. 우리의 회담은 촛불 하나가 희미하게 밝히는 그 고급 응접실에서 열렸다.\n\n회담은 그 낯선 신사가 탁자에 앉아 촛불을 제 쪽으로 끌어당기고 수첩의 몇몇 적힌 내용을 들여다보는 것으로 시작되었다. 그러더니 그는 수첩을 도로 집어넣고, 촛불 너머로 어둠 속의 조와 나를 들여다보며 누가 누구인지 확인한 다음, 촛불을 한쪽으로 조금 밀어 놓았다.\n\n\"내 이름은,\" 그가 말했다. \"재거스이고, 나는 런던의 변호사요. 제법 이름이 알려져 있소. 나는 당신들과 처리해야 할 예사롭지 않은 일이 있는데, 우선 이것이 내 발의로 비롯된 일이 아니라는 점부터 밝혀 두겠소. 만약 내 의견을 물었더라면, 나는 여기 있지 않았을 거요. 묻지 않았기에, 이렇게 여기 와 있는 거요. 나는 다른 사람의 비밀 대리인으로서 해야 할 일을 할 뿐이오. 덜도 아니고 더도 아니오.\"\n\n앉은 자리에서는 우리가 잘 보이지 않자, 그는 일어서서 한쪽 다리를 의자 등받이 너머로 걸치고 거기에 몸을 기댔다. 그리하여 한 발은 의자 좌석 위에, 한 발은 바닥에 둔 자세가 되었다.\n\n\"자, 조지프 가저리, 나는 당신의 도제인 이 젊은이를 당신 손에서 풀어 주자는 제안을 전하러 온 사람이오. 그의 요청에 따라, 또 그의 이익을 위해 그의 도제 계약을 해지하는 데 반대하지는 않겠지요? 그렇게 하는 대가로 아무것도 바라지 않을 테고?\"\n\n\"핍의 앞길을 막지 않는 대가로 제가 뭘 바라다니, 하느님께서 막아 주실 일이지요.\" 조가 멍하니 바라보며 말했다.\n\n\"하느님께서 막아 주신다는 건 경건한 말이지만, 요점에는 맞지 않소.\" 재거스 씨가 받아쳤다. \"문제는, 당신이 무언가를 바랄 것이냐 하는 거요. 당신은 무언가를 바라오?\"\n\n\"대답은,\" 조가 단호하게 받았다. \"아니오, 요.\"\n\n재거스 씨가 조의 그런 사심 없음을 두고 그를 어리석은 자로 여기는 듯 흘끗 보았다고 나는 생각했다. 그러나 나는 숨 막히는 호기심과 놀라움 사이에서 너무나 얼떨떨해, 그것을 확신할 수는 없었다.\n\n\"아주 좋소.\" 재거스 씨가 말했다. \"당신이 한 그 인정을 기억해 두고, 곧이라도 거기서 물러나려 들지 마시오.\"\n\n\"누가 물러나려 든답디까?\" 조가 쏘아붙였다.\n\n\"누가 그런다는 건 아니오. 당신은 개를 기르오?\"\n\n\"네, 개를 기르지요.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 이걸 명심하시오. 짖어 대는 개도 좋은 개지만, 꽉 무는 개가 더 나은 개라는 걸. 그걸 명심하겠소?\" 재거스 씨는 무언가를 용서해 주기라도 하듯 눈을 감고 조를 향해 고개를 끄덕이며 되풀이했다. \"자, 이제 이 젊은이 이야기로 돌아가겠소. 내가 전해야 할 소식은, 이 사람에게 막대한 유산에 대한 기대가 있다는 거요.\"\n\n조와 나는 숨을 들이켜며 서로를 바라보았다.\n\n\"나는 이 사람에게 다음과 같이 전하라는 지시를 받았소.\" 재거스 씨가 나를 향해 옆으로 손가락을 내던지며 말했다. \"그가 상당한 재산을 물려받게 되리라는 것을. 또한 그 재산의 현재 소유자의 바람은, 그가 지금의 생활 영역과 이곳에서 즉시 떠나, 신사로—한마디로, 막대한 유산에 대한 기대를 지닌 젊은이로 양육되어야 한다는 것이오.\"\n\n내 꿈이 실현된 것이다. 내 터무니없던 공상을 냉철한 현실이 능가해 버렸다. 미스 해비셤이 나의 운명을 거창한 규모로 만들어 주려는 것이었다.\n\n\"자, 핍 군.\" 변호사가 말을 이었다. \"이제 나머지 할 말은 자네에게 하겠네. 우선 자네가 이해해 둘 것은, 내가 지시를 받는 그분의 요청이 자네가 항상 핍이라는 이름을 지녀야 한다는 것이라는 점이네. 그 손쉬운 조건이 자네의 막대한 유산에 얹힌다고 해서 자네가 이의를 제기하진 않으리라 생각하네. 하지만 혹시 이의가 있다면, 지금이 그걸 말할 때일세.\"\n\n내 심장은 어찌나 빨리 뛰던지, 또 귓속에서 어찌나 윙윙거리던지, 나는 가까스로 더듬거리며 이의가 없다고 말할 수 있었다.\n\n\"그렇겠지! 자, 둘째로 이해해 둘 것은, 핍 군, 자네의 그 후한 은인의 이름이, 그분이 직접 밝히기로 택할 때까지는 철저한 비밀로 남는다는 것이네. 나는 그분이 그것을 직접 자네에게 입으로 밝힐 의향이라는 점은 말해도 좋다는 권한을 받았네. 그 의향이 언제 어디서 실행될지는 나도 말할 수 없고, 누구도 말할 수 없네. 몇 해 뒤가 될 수도 있지. 자, 자네는 이 점을 분명히 이해해 두게. 자네는 나와 나눌 어떤 대화에서도, 이 사안에 관해 어떤 질문도 하지 못하도록, 또 그 어떤 인물을 두고서든 그분이라고 암시하거나 언급하는 일을, 아무리 에둘러서라도, 절대로 하지 못하도록 단연코 금지되어 있다는 것을. 자네 가슴속에 어떤 의심이 든다면, 그 의심은 자네 가슴속에 묻어 두게. 이 금지의 이유가 무엇인지는 조금도 중요하지 않네. 그것은 더없이 강력하고 중대한 이유일 수도 있고, 그저 변덕일 수도 있네. 그것은 자네가 캐물을 일이 아닐세. 조건은 정해졌네. 자네가 그것을 받아들이고 구속력 있는 것으로 지키는 것이, 내가 지시를 받고 있고 그 밖의 일로는 내가 책임지지 않는 그분에게서 부여받은, 남은 유일한 조건이네. 그분이 바로 자네의 유산이 비롯되는 분이며, 그 비밀은 오직 그분과 나만이 쥐고 있네. 거듭 말하지만, 이 정도 행운의 도약에 얹힌다고 해서 그리 어려운 조건은 아닐세. 하지만 혹시 이의가 있다면, 지금이 그걸 말할 때일세. 어디 말해 보게.\"\n\n나는 다시 한번 힘겹게 더듬거리며 이의가 없다고 말했다.\n\n\"그렇겠지! 자, 핍 군, 조건에 관한 이야기는 이제 끝났네.\" 그는 나를 핍 군이라 부르며 다소 친근하게 대하기 시작했으나, 여전히 윽박지르듯 의심하는 분위기를 떨쳐 내지는 못했고, 지금도 말하는 동안 이따금 눈을 감고 내게 손가락을 내던졌는데, 그것은 마치 마음만 먹으면 나를 깎아내릴 온갖 일을 들춰낼 수 있다는 것을 드러내는 듯했다. \"다음은 단순한 준비 사항의 세부로 넘어가겠네. 자네가 알아 둘 것은, 내가 '기대'라는 말을 여러 번 썼지만, 자네에게 주어진 것이 기대뿐만은 아니라는 점이네. 자네의 적절한 교육과 생활 유지에 충분하고도 남을 만한 금액이 이미 내 손에 맡겨져 있네. 나를 자네의 후견인으로 여겨 주게. 오!\" 내가 그에게 고마움을 표하려 하자 그가 말했다. \"당장 말해 두는데, 나는 내 일에 대한 보수를 받고 있네. 그렇지 않으면 그 일을 하지 않을 걸세. 자네가 바뀐 처지에 걸맞게 더 나은 교육을 받아야 한다는 것, 또 그 혜택을 즉시 누리기 시작하는 일이 얼마나 중요하고 긴요한지를 자네가 절감하게 되리라는 것이 그분의 뜻일세.\"\n\n나는 늘 그것을 갈망해 왔노라고 말했다.\n\n\"자네가 늘 무엇을 갈망해 왔는지는 상관없네, 핍 군.\" 그가 받아쳤다. \"기록된 것만 따지세. 지금 그것을 갈망한다면, 그걸로 충분하네. 자네가 당장 적절한 가정교사 밑에 들어갈 준비가 되었다는 대답으로 알아들으면 되겠나? 그런 건가?\"\n\n나는 더듬더듬 그렇다고, 그렇다고 답했다.\n\n\"좋네. 자, 자네의 의향도 참작해야겠지. 명심하게, 나는 그게 현명하다고 생각하진 않네만, 그것이 내가 맡은 일일세. 다른 사람보다 더 마음에 드는 가정교사를 누구든 들어 본 적이 있나?\"\n\n나는 비디와 윕슬 씨의 대고모 말고는 어떤 가정교사도 들어 본 적이 없었으므로, 없다고 대답했다.\n\n\"내가 어느 정도 아는 가정교사가 한 사람 있는데, 그가 그 목적에 맞을 것 같네.\" 재거스 씨가 말했다. \"유념하게, 나는 그를 추천하는 건 아닐세. 나는 누구도 추천하는 법이 없으니까. 내가 말하는 그 신사는 매슈 포켓이라는 사람일세.\"\n\n아! 나는 곧바로 그 이름을 붙잡았다. 미스 해비셤의 친척. 커밀라 부부가 말하던 그 매슈. 미스 해비셤이 신부 드레스를 입은 채 신부 식탁 위에 죽어 누우면, 그 머리맡에 자리하기로 되어 있다던 그 매슈.\n\n\"그 이름을 아나?\" 재거스 씨가 나를 예리하게 살피더니, 내 대답을 기다리며 눈을 감았다.\n\n내 대답은, 그 이름을 들어 본 적이 있다는 것이었다.\n\n\"오!\" 그가 말했다. \"그 이름을 들어 봤다. 하지만 문제는, 그것에 대해 자네가 뭐라 말하느냐일세.\"\n\n나는 그가 추천해 준 것에 대해 무척 고맙다고 말했다, 아니, 말하려 했다—\n\n\"아니야, 젊은 친구!\" 그가 큼직한 머리를 아주 천천히 가로저으며 말을 끊었다. \"정신 차리게!\"\n\n정신을 차리지 못한 채, 나는 다시 그가 추천해 준 것에 대해 무척 고맙다고 말하기 시작했다—\n\n\"아니야, 젊은 친구.\" 그가 고개를 가로저으며, 얼굴을 찌푸리는 동시에 미소 지으며 말을 끊었다. \"아니, 아니, 아니. 아주 잘하긴 했네만, 그래서는 안 되네. 자네는 너무 어려서 그런 식으로 나를 옭아맬 수 없어. 추천이라는 말은 맞지 않네, 핍 군. 다른 말을 써 보게.\"\n\n나는 스스로 고쳐, 그가 매슈 포켓 씨를 언급해 준 것에 대해 무척 고맙다고 말했다—\n\n\"그게 더 낫군!\" 재거스 씨가 외쳤다. 그래서 (나는 덧붙여) 기꺼이 그 신사에게 배워 보겠노라고 말했다.\n\n\"좋네. 그의 집에서 직접 배워 보는 편이 낫겠네. 자네를 위해 길을 마련해 두겠네. 자네는 먼저 런던에 있는 그의 아들을 만나 볼 수 있을 걸세. 언제 런던에 오겠나?\"\n\n나는 (꼼짝도 않고 서서 지켜보던 조를 흘끗 보며) 당장이라도 올 수 있을 것 같다고 말했다.\n\n\"우선,\" 재거스 씨가 말했다. \"올 때 입을 새 옷이 좀 있어야겠는데, 그건 작업복이어서는 안 되네. 오늘로부터 일주일 뒤라고 해 두지. 돈도 좀 필요하겠지. 자네에게 이십 기니를 두고 갈까?\"\n\n그는 더없이 침착하게 긴 지갑을 꺼내, 탁자 위에 그것을 세어 놓고는 내 쪽으로 밀어 주었다. 이때가 그가 의자에서 다리를 처음으로 내린 순간이었다. 돈을 밀어 준 뒤 그는 의자에 걸터앉아, 지갑을 흔들거리며 조를 바라보았다.\n\n\"자, 조지프 가저리? 어안이 벙벙한 모양이군?\"\n\n\"그렇소!\" 조가 아주 단호한 태도로 말했다.\n\n\"당신은 자신을 위해서는 아무것도 바라지 않는다고 양해된 걸로 아는데, 기억하시오?\"\n\n\"양해됐었지요.\" 조가 말했다. \"그리고 양해되고 있고요. 그리고 앞으로도 늘 마찬가지로 양해될 거요.\"\n\n\"하지만 만일,\" 재거스 씨가 지갑을 흔들며 말했다. \"내 지시에 당신에게 보상으로 선물을 하라는 내용이 들어 있다면 어떻겠소?\"\n\n\"무엇에 대한 보상으로요?\" 조가 따져 물었다.\n\n\"이 아이의 노동력을 잃는 데 대한 보상으로 말이오.\"\n\n조는 여인의 손길과도 같이 부드럽게 내 어깨에 손을 얹었다. 그 후로 나는 종종 그를, 사람을 으스러뜨릴 수도 있고 달걀 껍질을 토닥일 수도 있는 증기 해머 같다고, 그렇게 힘과 부드러움을 겸비한 사람이라고 생각해 왔다. \"핍이 자기 노동력을 떨치고 나가 명예와 행운을 누리는 거라면, 그야 말로 다 못할 만큼 진심으로 환영하는 일이지요.\" 조가 말했다. \"하지만 당신이 돈이라는 게, 대장간에 찾아와 준 그 어린아이를—그리고 언제까지나 더없이 좋은 친구인 그 아이를 잃는 데 대해 내게 보상이 될 수 있다고 생각한다면—\"\n\n오, 다정하고 선량한 조여, 내가 그토록 선뜻 떠나려 했고 그토록 고마워할 줄 몰랐던 조여, 나는 다시금 당신을 본다. 우락부락한 대장장이의 팔뚝으로 두 눈을 가린 채, 넓은 가슴을 들썩이며, 목소리가 잦아들던 당신을. 오, 다정하고 선량하고 충직하며 자상한 조여, 나는 마치 천사의 날갯짓 소리이기라도 했던 듯, 오늘 이날 엄숙하게도, 내 팔에 얹힌 당신 손의 사랑 어린 떨림을 느낀다.\n\n그러나 그때 나는 조를 다독였다. 나는 다가올 내 행운의 미로 속에서 길을 잃은 채, 우리가 함께 밟아 온 그 샛길들을 되짚어 갈 수가 없었다. 나는 조에게 마음을 가라앉히라고 빌었다. (그의 말마따나) 우리는 언제나 더없이 좋은 친구였고, (내 말마따나) 앞으로도 언제까지나 그러할 테니까. 조는 자기 눈을 후벼 내기라도 하려는 듯, 빈 손목으로 두 눈을 훔쳤으나, 더는 한마디도 하지 않았다.\n\n재거스 씨는, 조에게서는 마을의 바보를, 내게서는 그 보호자를 알아본 사람처럼 이 광경을 지켜보고 있었다. 그것이 끝나자, 그는 흔들기를 멈춘 지갑을 손에 무게를 가늠하듯 들고서 말했다.\n\n\"자, 조지프 가저리, 경고하건대 이것이 당신의 마지막 기회요. 나한테는 어중간한 건 통하지 않소. 내가 당신에게 하라고 맡은 그 선물을 받을 작정이라면, 분명히 말하시오, 그러면 받게 될 거요. 반대로 만약 당신이 말하려는 것이—\" 여기서, 그가 크게 놀란 것은, 조가 갑자기 그를 향해 몸을 휘돌리며 흉맹한 권투 자세를 온몸으로 드러내는 바람에 말이 막혀 버렸기 때문이다.\n\n\"내가 말하려는 건,\" 조가 외쳤다. \"당신이 내 집에 들어와 황소 부리듯 나를 들볶고 못살게 군다면, 나가시오! 내 말하려는 건 즉슨, 당신이 사내라면, 덤벼 보란 말이오! 내가 말하려는 건, 내가 한 말은, 내가 진심으로 하는 말이고, 그 말에 목숨을 걸겠다는 거요!\"\n\n내가 조를 끌어내자, 그는 곧 누그러졌다. 다만 자기 집에서 황소 부리듯 들볶이고 못살게 굴림을 당하지는 않겠노라고, 친절한 투로, 또 그에 해당될 만한 누구에게든 정중히 항의하여 일러두는 식으로, 내게 밝혔을 뿐이다. 조가 그렇게 나서자 재거스 씨는 일어나 문 가까이로 물러나 있었다. 다시 안으로 들어올 기색은 조금도 보이지 않은 채, 그는 거기서 작별의 말을 전했다. 그 말은 이러했다.\n\n\"자, 핍 군, 자네가—신사가 될 몸이니—여기를 빨리 떠날수록 좋을 듯하네. 오늘로부터 일주일 뒤로 해 두지. 그동안 자네에게 내 인쇄된 주소를 보내겠네. 런던의 역마차 사무소에서 삯마차를 잡아타고 곧장 내게 오면 되네. 알아 두게, 나는 내가 떠맡은 이 임무에 대해 이렇다 저렇다 어떤 의견도 표하지 않네. 나는 그 일을 떠맡는 대가로 보수를 받고, 그래서 그 일을 하는 것이네. 자, 그 점을 명심하게. 그것을 명심해!\"\n\n그는 우리 둘을 향해 손가락을 내던지고 있었고, 더 말을 이어 갈 것 같았으나, 조가 위험하다고 여겼는지 그대로 떠나가 버렸다.\n\n문득 머릿속에 떠오른 무언가에 이끌려, 나는 그가 빌린 마차를 세워 둔 명랑한 뱃사공 주막 쪽으로 내려가는 그를 뒤쫓아 달려갔다.\n\n\"실례합니다, 재거스 씨.\"\n\n\"이런!\" 그가 홱 돌아서며 말했다. \"무슨 일인가?\"\n\n\"저는 모든 걸 제대로 하고 싶고, 선생의 지시를 따르고 싶어서요. 그래서 여쭤 보는 게 낫겠다 싶었습니다. 제가 떠나기 전에 이 근방에서 아는 사람에게 작별 인사를 하는 데 무슨 문제가 있을까요?\"\n\n\"없네.\" 그가 내 말을 거의 알아듣지 못하겠다는 표정으로 말했다.\n\n\"이 마을에서만이 아니라, 읍내에서도 말입니다만?\"\n\n\"없네.\" 그가 말했다. \"문제 될 것 없네.\"\n\n나는 그에게 고맙다고 하고 다시 집으로 달려갔는데, 가 보니 조는 벌써 현관문을 잠그고 고급 응접실을 비운 채, 부엌 난롯가에 양손을 무릎에 하나씩 얹고 앉아 타오르는 석탄을 골똘히 바라보고 있었다. 나도 난롯불 앞에 앉아 석탄을 바라보았고, 한참 동안 아무 말도 오가지 않았다.\n\n누나는 한구석에 놓인 쿠션을 댄 의자에 앉아 있었고, 비디는 난롯불 앞에서 바느질을 하며 앉아 있었고, 조는 비디 옆에, 나는 누나 맞은편 구석에서 조 옆에 앉아 있었다. 이글거리는 석탄을 들여다보면 볼수록 나는 조를 바라보기가 더 어려워졌고, 침묵이 길어지면 길어질수록 입을 떼기가 더 힘들어졌다.\n\n마침내 나는 가까스로 입을 뗐다. \"조, 비디한테 말했어요?\"\n\n\"아니, 핍.\" 조가 여전히 난롯불을 바라보며, 마치 무릎이 어딘가로 달아날 작정이라는 은밀한 정보라도 들은 듯 두 무릎을 꽉 붙든 채 대답했다. \"그건 네게 맡겨 뒀단다, 핍.\"\n\n\"조가 말해 주면 좋겠어요.\"\n\n\"그럼 핍이 행운을 거머쥔 신사가 됐다는구나.\" 조가 말했다. \"그 일에 하느님의 축복이 있기를!\"\n\n비디가 바느질거리를 내려놓고 나를 바라보았다. 조는 무릎을 붙든 채 나를 바라보았다. 나는 두 사람을 바라보았다. 잠시 뒤 두 사람은 진심으로 나를 축하해 주었다. 그러나 그 축하 속에는 어딘가 슬픔의 기색이 묻어 있어, 나는 그것이 다소 마음에 거슬렸다.\n\n나는 나서서, 내 행운을 만들어 준 사람에 대해 아무것도 알려 하지 말고 아무 말도 하지 말아야 한다는, 내가 내 벗들이 마땅히 져야 한다고 여긴 그 무거운 의무를 비디에게(그리고 비디를 통해 조에게) 새겨 주려 했다. 때가 되면 다 밝혀질 거라고, 그동안에는 내가 어느 알 수 없는 후원자에게서 막대한 유산에 대한 기대를 갖게 되었다는 것 말고는 아무 말도 해서는 안 된다고 나는 일렀다. 비디는 다시 바느질거리를 집어 들며 난롯불을 향해 생각에 잠긴 듯 고개를 끄덕이고는, 매우 조심하겠노라고 말했다. 그리고 조는, 여전히 무릎을 붙든 채 \"그래, 그래, 나도 똑같이 조심하마, 핍.\" 하고 말했다. 그러더니 두 사람은 다시 나를 축하해 주었고, 내가 신사가 된다는 발상에 어찌나 놀라움을 거듭 드러내던지, 나는 그것이 그다지 달갑지 않았다.\n\n이어 비디는 무슨 일이 일어났는지를 누나에게 어떻게든 이해시키려고 무진 애를 썼다. 내가 믿기로는, 그 노력은 완전히 실패로 돌아갔다. 누나는 웃으며 고개를 여러 번 끄덕였고, 심지어 비디를 따라 \"핍\"이니 \"재산\"이니 하는 말을 되뇌기까지 했다. 그러나 그 말들에 선거 구호 이상의 의미가 담겨 있었을지는 의문이며, 나는 누나의 정신 상태를 이보다 더 어둡게 그려 보일 길이 없다.\n\n겪어 보지 않았다면 나는 결코 믿지 못했을 일이지만, 조와 비디가 다시금 즐거이 마음 편해질수록 나는 점점 우울해졌다. 물론 내 행운에 불만이 있을 리는 없었다. 그러나 나도 모르는 사이에, 어쩌면 나는 나 자신에게 불만을 품고 있었는지도 모른다.\n\n어쨌든 나는 무릎에 팔꿈치를 괴고 손에 얼굴을 받친 채, 그 두 사람이 내가 떠나는 일이며, 내가 없으면 자기들은 어찌해야 하나 하는 따위의 이야기를 나누는 동안 난롯불을 들여다보고 앉아 있었다. 그리고 두 사람 중 누구든 나를 바라보는 것을 알아챌 때마다, 그 눈빛이 아무리 다정하더라도 (그들은 자주 나를 바라보았는데, 특히 비디가 그랬다), 나는 마치 그들이 나를 못 미더워하는 마음을 드러내기라도 하는 양 기분이 상했다. 하늘이 알다시피 그들은 말로든 기색으로든 결코 그런 적이 없었는데도 말이다.\n\n그럴 때면 나는 일어나 문밖을 내다보곤 했다. 우리 부엌문은 바로 바깥의 밤으로 통해 있어, 여름 저녁이면 방을 환기하려고 활짝 열어 두었으니까. 그때 내가 눈을 들어 올려다본 바로 그 별들조차도, 내가 평생을 보내 온 그 촌스러운 것들 위에서 반짝이는 데에나 어울리는, 초라하고 변변찮은 별들이라고 여겼던 것 같아 두렵다.\n\n\"토요일 밤이로구나.\" 우리가 빵과 치즈와 맥주로 저녁을 들 때 내가 말했다. \"닷새만 더 지나면, 그러면 바로 그날의 전날이네! 금세 지나가겠지.\"\n\n\"그래, 핍.\" 조가 말했는데, 그 목소리는 맥주잔 속에서 텅 빈 듯 울렸다. \"금세 지나가겠지.\"\n\n\"금세, 금세 지나가겠죠.\" 비디가 말했다.\n\n\"내가 생각해 봤는데요, 조, 월요일에 읍내에 내려가 새 옷을 주문할 때, 재단사한테 가서 거기서 옷을 입겠다고 하든지, 아니면 펌블추크 씨 댁으로 보내 달라고 할까 봐요. 여기 사람들이 다 빤히 쳐다보면 무척 거북할 테니까요.\"\n\n\"허블 부부도 자네의 새 점잖은 모습을 보고 싶어 할지 모르네, 핍.\" 조가 왼손바닥 위에서 빵에 치즈를 얹어 부지런히 자르며, 마치 우리가 빵 조각 크기를 견주던 시절을 떠올리기라도 하듯 손도 대지 않은 내 저녁을 흘끗 보며 말했다. \"윕슬도 그럴 테고. 그리고 명랑한 뱃사공 주막 사람들도 그걸 영광으로 여길지 모르지.\"\n\n\"바로 그게 제가 싫은 거예요, 조. 그 사람들은 그걸 가지고 어찌나 야단법석을 떨지—어찌나 거칠고 천박한 소동을 벌일지—저는 견딜 수가 없을 거예요.\"\n\n\"아, 그건 정말 그렇지, 핍!\" 조가 말했다. \"네 스스로를 견딜 수 없을 정도라면야—\"\n\n이때 비디가 누나의 접시를 든 채 앉아서 내게 물었다. \"가저리 씨와 누님과 저한테는 언제 모습을 보여 줄지 생각해 봤어요? 우리한테는 모습을 보여 줄 거죠, 그렇죠?\"\n\n\"비디,\" 나는 다소 발끈하여 받아쳤다. \"당신은 어찌나 빠른지 따라가기가 어렵군요.\"\n\n(\"저 애는 늘 빨랐지.\" 조가 한마디 했다.)\n\n\"잠깐만 더 기다렸더라면, 비디, 내가 어느 날 저녁에—아마도 떠나기 전날 저녁에—옷을 보따리에 싸서 이리 가져오겠다고 말하는 걸 들었을 텐데.\"\n\n비디는 더는 말이 없었다. 나는 너그러이 그녀를 용서하고, 곧 그녀와 조에게 다정하게 잘 자라는 인사를 건넨 뒤 잠자리로 올라갔다. 내 작은 방에 들어서자, 나는 앉아서 그 방을, 머지않아 영영 작별하고 그 위로 올라서게 될 누추하고 작은 방을 오래도록 바라보았다. 그 방에는 싱그럽고 어린 추억들 또한 갖추어져 있었으니, 바로 그 순간에도 나는, 대장간과 미스 해비셤의 집 사이에서, 또 비디와 에스텔라 사이에서 그토록 자주 빠지곤 했던 것과 꼭 같은, 그 방과 내가 가게 될 더 나은 방들 사이의 어수선하게 갈라진 마음에 사로잡혔다.\n\n하루 종일 다락방 지붕 위로 해가 밝게 내리쬔 터라 방은 따뜻했다. 창문을 열고 서서 밖을 내다보니, 아래쪽 어두운 문간에서 조가 천천히 나와 바깥바람을 쐬며 한두 바퀴 거니는 것이 보였다. 그러더니 비디가 나와 그에게 파이프를 가져다주고 불을 붙여 주는 것이 보였다. 그는 그렇게 밤늦게 담배를 피우는 법이 없었으니, 그것은 내게 그가 무슨 까닭에선지 위로받고 싶어 한다는 것을 넌지시 알려 주는 듯했다.\n\n이내 그는 바로 내 아래쪽 문간에 서서 파이프를 피웠고, 비디도 거기 서서 그와 조용히 이야기를 나누었는데, 나는 두 사람이 내 이야기를 하고 있음을 알았다. 두 사람 다 한 번 이상 다정한 어조로 내 이름을 입에 올리는 것을 들었기 때문이다. 설령 더 들을 수 있었더라도, 나는 더 엿듣지는 않았을 것이다. 그래서 나는 창가에서 물러나, 침대맡의 하나뿐인 의자에 앉아, 내 빛나는 행운의 이 첫날 밤이 내가 알아 온 그 어느 밤보다 가장 외로운 밤이 되었다는 것이 무척 서글프고 기이하다고 느꼈다.\n\n열린 창문 쪽으로 눈을 돌리니, 조의 파이프에서 피어오른 옅은 연기 고리들이 거기 떠도는 것이 보였고, 나는 그것이 마치 조가 보내는 축복 같다고 여겼다. 내게 강요하거나 내 앞에 과시하는 것이 아니라, 우리가 함께 나누던 그 공기 속에 스며드는 축복 말이다. 나는 등불을 끄고 침대 속으로 기어들었다. 그러나 이제 그것은 불편한 잠자리였고, 나는 다시는 그 침대에서 예전의 그 깊은 잠을 자지 못했다.\n\n# Chapter XIX.\n\n아침이 오자 내 인생 전반에 대한 전망은 사뭇 달라졌고, 어찌나 환해졌던지 도무지 같은 인생으로 보이지 않을 정도였다. 내 마음을 가장 무겁게 짓누른 것은, 떠나는 날까지 엿새가 남았다는 생각이었다. 그동안 런던에 무슨 일이 생겨, 막상 내가 도착했을 때 그곳이 크게 쇠락해 있거나 아예 깨끗이 사라져 버렸을지도 모른다는 불안을 떨쳐 낼 수 없었기 때문이다.\n\n다가오는 우리의 이별을 입에 올렸을 때, 조와 비디는 더없이 다정하고 따뜻하게 굴었으나, 내가 먼저 그 이야기를 꺼낼 때에만 그 일을 언급했다. 아침을 먹은 뒤, 조는 제일 좋은 응접실의 찬장에서 내 도제 계약서를 꺼내 왔고, 우리는 그것을 불 속에 넣었으며, 나는 내가 자유로워졌음을 느꼈다. 해방의 온갖 새로움에 사로잡힌 채, 나는 조와 함께 교회에 갔고, 목사님이 모든 사정을 알았더라면 부자와 천국에 관한 그 구절은 읽지 않았을지도 모른다고 생각했다.\n\n이른 점심을 먹은 뒤, 나는 습지를 단숨에 다 돌아보고 끝장을 내 버리리라 작정하고 홀로 어슬렁어슬렁 나섰다. 교회를 지날 때, 나는 (그날 아침 예배 때 느꼈던 것처럼) 일요일이면 일요일마다, 평생토록 그곳에 가도록 운명 지워진 채 끝내 나지막한 푸른 봉분 사이에 이름 없이 묻힐 가엾은 이들을 향해 숭고한 연민을 느꼈다. 나는 언젠가 그들을 위해 무언가를 해 주리라 스스로 다짐했고, 마을 사람 모두에게 구운 쇠고기와 자두 푸딩 한 끼, 맥주 한 파인트, 그리고 너그러운 은혜 한 갤런을 베푸는 계획의 윤곽을 짜 보았다.\n\n언젠가 저 무덤들 사이를 절뚝거리며 가던 그 탈주자와 한패였다는 사실을, 전에도 나는 부끄러움에 가까운 감정을 품고서 곧잘 떠올리곤 했거니와, 그 장소가 죄수의 쇠고랑과 표지를 단 채 누더기를 걸치고 부들부들 떨던 그 가련한 자를 다시 불러일으킨 이 일요일에 내 심정은 어떠했겠는가! 그나마 위안이 된 것은, 그 일이 오래전에 일어났다는 것, 그자는 틀림없이 멀리멀리 유형을 떠났으리라는 것, 그리고 그자는 나에게는 죽은 사람이나 마찬가지이며, 어쩌면 정말로 죽어 버렸을지도 모른다는 것이었다.\n\n이제 더는 나지막하고 축축한 땅도, 더는 도랑과 수문도, 더는 풀을 뜯는 저 소 떼도 없으리라 — 비록 그 소들은 그 굼뜬 태도로나마 이제는 한결 공손한 기색을 띠고, 그토록 큰 유산을 거머쥔 사람을 되도록 오래 바라보려는 듯 고개를 돌려 마주 보는 듯했지만 — 잘 있거라, 내 어린 시절의 단조로운 벗들이여, 이제부터 나는 런던과 위대함을 향해 가는 몸이지, 대장간 일 따위와 너희를 위한 몸이 아니었다! 나는 의기양양하게 옛 포대로 발걸음을 옮겼고, 거기 드러누워 해비셤 양이 나를 에스텔라의 짝으로 점지한 것인가 하는 문제를 곰곰 따져 보다가 그만 잠이 들고 말았다.\n\n잠에서 깨어났을 때, 조가 파이프를 물고 내 곁에 앉아 있는 것을 보고 나는 적잖이 놀랐다. 내가 눈을 뜨자 그는 유쾌한 미소로 나를 맞이하며 말했다.\n\n\"마지막이다 싶어서, 핍, 따라와 봤다.\"\n\n\"조, 따라와 줘서 정말 기뻐요.\"\n\n\"고맙다, 핍.\"\n\n\"분명히 말해 두지만, 조.\" 우리가 악수를 나눈 뒤 나는 말을 이었다. \"나는 형을 절대 잊지 않을 거예요.\"\n\n\"아냐, 아냐, 핍!\" 조가 편안한 어조로 말했다. \"그건 내가 확신한단다. 그럼, 그렇고말고, 이 사람아! 그저 사람 마음속에 잘 새겨 두기만 하면 확실해지는 거지. 그런데 마음속에 제대로 새기기까지가 좀 시간이 걸렸어, 변화가 어찌나 느닷없이 통째로 닥쳐왔던지 말이다. 안 그러냐?\"\n\n어쩐 일인지, 조가 나를 그토록 굳게 믿어 의심치 않는 것이 나는 그리 달갑지 않았다. 차라리 그가 감정을 드러내거나, \"그건 다 네가 잘난 덕이지, 핍\" 같은 말이라도 해 주었으면 싶었다. 그래서 나는 조의 첫 번째 이야기에는 아무 말도 하지 않았고, 두 번째 이야기에 대해서만, 소식이 정말 갑작스레 오긴 했으나 나는 늘 신사가 되고 싶었으며, 만약 신사가 된다면 무엇을 할까 하고 이따금 곰곰 궁리하곤 했다고 말했다.\n\n\"그랬단 말이냐?\" 조가 말했다. \"놀랍구나!\"\n\n\"이제 와서 안타까운 건요, 조.\" 내가 말했다. \"여기서 우리가 공부할 때 형이 좀 더 진전을 보지 못한 거예요. 안 그래요?\"\n\n\"글쎄, 모르겠다.\" 조가 대답했다. \"내가 워낙 지독히도 둔하잖니. 난 그저 내 일에나 능할 뿐이지. 내가 그토록 지독히 둔한 게 늘 안타깝긴 했지만, 그건 지금이라고 더 안타까울 것도 없어, 일 년 전 오늘이나 매한가지지. 안 그러냐?\"\n\n내가 말하려던 것은, 내가 재산을 물려받아 조를 위해 무언가를 해 줄 수 있게 되었을 때, 그가 신분 상승에 좀 더 어울리는 자질을 갖추었더라면 한결 보기 좋았으리라는 뜻이었다. 그러나 그는 내 속뜻을 너무도 천진하게 알아채지 못했으므로, 나는 차라리 비디에게 그 이야기를 꺼내는 편이 낫겠다고 생각했다.\n\n그리하여 우리가 집까지 걸어와 차를 마신 뒤, 나는 비디를 데리고 오솔길 옆 우리 집 작은 정원으로 나갔고, 그녀의 기운을 북돋아 주려고 두루뭉술하게나마 너를 결코 잊지 않겠다는 말을 던진 다음, 그녀에게 부탁할 일이 하나 있다고 말했다.\n\n\"그건 말이야, 비디.\" 내가 말했다. \"조를 조금이라도 발전시킬 기회가 생기면 하나도 놓치지 말아 달라는 거야.\"\n\n\"어떻게 발전시키라는 거예요?\" 비디가 차분한 눈길로 물었다.\n\n\"그게! 조는 더없이 착하고 좋은 사람이야 — 사실 나는 그렇게 좋은 사람은 세상에 없다고 생각해 — 하지만 어떤 면에서는 좀 뒤처져 있잖아. 이를테면, 비디, 학식이나 예절에서 말이야.\"\n\n말하는 동안 나는 비디를 바라보고 있었고, 또 내 말이 끝나자 그녀가 눈을 휘둥그렇게 떴음에도, 그녀는 나를 보지 않았다.\n\n\"아, 예절요! 그럼 조의 예절로는 안 된다는 건가요?\" 비디가 까치밥나무 잎을 하나 뜯으며 물었다.\n\n\"비디야, 여기서는 그 예절로도 충분히 괜찮지만 —\"\n\n\"아! 여기서는 충분히 괜찮다고요?\" 비디가 손에 든 잎을 가까이 들여다보며 말을 끊었다.\n\n\"끝까지 들어 봐 — 하지만 만약 내가 조를 더 높은 자리로 옮긴다면, 내가 재산을 온전히 물려받았을 때 그렇게 해 줄 작정인데, 그땐 그 예절로는 조가 제대로 대접받기 어려울 거야.\"\n\n\"그걸 조가 모를 것 같아요?\" 비디가 물었다.\n\n그것은 어찌나 약을 올리는 질문이었던지(그런 생각은 단 한 번도, 아주 어렴풋이도 내 머리에 떠오른 적이 없었으니까), 나는 퉁명스럽게 내뱉었다.\n\n\"비디, 그게 무슨 소리야?\"\n\n비디는 두 손바닥 사이에서 그 잎을 비벼 부스러뜨리고는 — 그 후로 까치밥나무 덤불의 냄새는 늘 내게 오솔길 옆 작은 정원에서의 그 저녁을 떠올리게 한다 — 이렇게 말했다. \"조가 자존심이 강한 사람일 수도 있다는 생각은 안 해 보셨어요?\"\n\n\"자존심?\" 나는 경멸하듯 힘주어 되뇌었다.\n\n\"아! 자존심에도 여러 종류가 있잖아요.\" 비디가 나를 똑바로 바라보며 고개를 저었다. \"자존심이라고 다 같은 게 아니에요 —\"\n\n\"그래서? 왜 말을 멈추는 거야?\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"다 같은 게 아니에요.\" 비디가 말을 이었다. \"조는 자기가 능히 채울 수 있고, 잘 채우고 있고, 또 존경받으며 채우고 있는 자리에서 누군가 자기를 끌어내려는 걸 못 견딜 만큼 자존심이 강할 수도 있어요. 사실대로 말하면, 저는 조가 그렇다고 생각해요. 제가 이렇게 말하는 게 당돌하게 들릴지 몰라도요. 도련님이 저보다 조를 훨씬 잘 알 테니까요.\"\n\n\"이봐, 비디.\" 내가 말했다. \"네게서 이런 모습을 보게 되어 정말 유감이야. 네게서 이런 걸 보리라곤 생각지 못했어. 너는 시샘을 하고 있어, 비디, 그리고 못마땅해하고 있어. 너는 내 운수가 트인 것에 불만을 품고서, 그걸 드러내지 않고는 못 배기는 거야.\"\n\n\"정말 그렇게 생각할 마음이 있으시다면,\" 비디가 대답했다. \"그렇게 말씀하세요. 정말 그렇게 생각할 마음이 있으시다면, 몇 번이고 거듭 그렇게 말씀하세요.\"\n\n\"그렇게 굴 마음이 있는 건 너겠지, 비디.\" 나는 점잖고도 우월한 어조로 말했다. \"내게 떠넘기지 마. 이런 모습을 보게 되어 나는 정말 유감이고, 이건 — 이건 인간 본성의 못된 일면이야. 나는 내가 떠난 뒤에 네게 어떤 사소한 기회라도 생기거든 그걸 살려서 착한 조를 좀 더 나아지게 해 달라고 부탁하려고 했어. 하지만 이렇게 된 이상 네게 아무것도 부탁하지 않겠어. 네게서 이런 걸 보게 되어 정말이지 유감이야, 비디.\" 나는 거듭 말했다. \"이건 — 이건 인간 본성의 못된 일면이야.\"\n\n\"저를 꾸짖으시든 칭찬하시든,\" 가엾은 비디가 대답했다. \"제가 여기서, 언제나, 제 힘이 닿는 데까지 다 하려고 애쓰리라는 것만은 똑같이 믿으셔도 돼요. 그리고 도련님이 저에 대해 어떤 생각을 품고 떠나시든, 그것이 제가 도련님을 기억하는 데에는 아무런 차이도 만들지 못할 거예요. 그래도 신사라면 부당하게 굴어서는 안 되는 법이지요.\" 비디는 고개를 돌리며 말했다.\n\n나는 그것이 인간 본성의 못된 일면이라고 다시금 열을 내어 거듭 말했고(그 적용 대상은 차치하더라도, 그 정서 자체에 관해서는 그 후로 내가 옳았다고 여길 만한 이유를 발견했다), 비디에게서 멀어지며 그 작은 오솔길을 걸어 내려갔다. 비디는 집 안으로 들어갔고, 나는 정원 문을 나서 저녁 시간까지 풀 죽은 채 어슬렁어슬렁 거닐었다. 내 빛나는 행운의 둘째 날 밤마저 첫째 날 밤만큼이나 외롭고 허전하다는 사실이 또다시 몹시도 서글프고 기이하게 느껴졌다.\n\n그러나 아침은 다시 한번 내 마음을 환하게 밝혀 주었고, 나는 비디에게 자비를 베풀었으며, 우리는 그 이야기를 더 꺼내지 않았다. 가진 옷 가운데 가장 좋은 것을 차려입고, 나는 가게들이 문을 열었으리라 기대할 만한 이른 시각에 읍내로 나가, 재단사 트랩 씨 앞에 나섰다. 그는 가게 뒤편 거실에서 아침을 먹는 중이었는데, 굳이 나를 맞으러 나올 것도 없다는 듯이 나를 안으로 불러들였다.\n\n\"이런!\" 트랩 씨가 친구라도 만난 양 허물없이 말했다. \"잘 지내나, 무슨 일로 왔지?\"\n\n트랩 씨는 따뜻한 빵을 세 겹 깃털 이불처럼 저며 놓고, 그 이불 사이사이에 버터를 밀어 넣어 덮고 있었다. 그는 형편이 넉넉한 늙은 독신남이었고, 활짝 열린 그의 창은 풍성한 작은 정원과 과수원으로 트여 있었으며, 벽난로 옆 벽에는 든든한 무쇠 금고가 박혀 있었으니, 그 안에 그의 풍요가 자루째 수북이 쟁여져 있으리라는 것을 나는 의심치 않았다.\n\n\"트랩 씨.\" 내가 말했다. \"꺼내기 거북한 이야기예요, 자랑처럼 들릴 테니까요. 하지만 저는 상당한 재산을 물려받게 되었어요.\"\n\n트랩 씨에게 변화가 일었다. 그는 이불 속의 버터를 까맣게 잊은 채 식탁 곁에서 일어나, 식탁보에 손가락을 닦으며 외쳤다. \"맙소사, 이런 경사가!\"\n\n\"저는 런던에 계신 제 후견인에게 올라갈 참인데요.\" 나는 주머니에서 기니 몇 닢을 무심한 듯 꺼내 들여다보며 말했다. \"거기 가서 입을 유행하는 정장 한 벌이 필요해요. 옷값은요\" — 그러지 않으면 그가 옷을 만드는 시늉만 할지도 모른다고 생각해서 한마디 덧붙였다 — \"현금으로 치를게요.\"\n\n\"이봐요, 도련님.\" 트랩 씨가 공손히 허리를 굽히고 두 팔을 벌리며, 외람되게도 내 양쪽 팔꿈치 바깥을 슬쩍 짚고서 말했다. \"그런 말씀으로 절 무안하게 마세요. 감히 축하의 말씀을 올려도 될는지요? 황송하오나 가게 안으로 드시겠습니까?\"\n\n트랩 씨네 점원 아이는 그 일대에서 가장 뻔뻔한 녀석이었다. 내가 들어섰을 때 그는 가게 바닥을 쓸고 있었는데, 나를 쓸어 넘기는 시늉으로 제 노동에 단맛을 더하고 있었다. 내가 트랩 씨와 함께 가게로 나왔을 때에도 녀석은 여전히 바닥을 쓸고 있었으며, 빗자루를 온갖 모서리며 장애물에 부딪쳐 가며, (내가 이해하기로는) 죽었든 살았든 어느 대장장이와도 대등하다는 뜻을 드러내고 있었다.\n\n\"그 소란 그쳐라.\" 트랩 씨가 더없이 매서운 어조로 말했다. \"안 그러면 머리통을 날려 버릴 테다! — 자, 앉으시지요, 도련님. 자, 이것은요.\" 트랩 씨는 옷감 한 필을 끌어내려 계산대 위에 좌르르 펼쳐 놓고는, 그 밑으로 손을 넣어 윤기를 보여 주려고 채비하며 말했다. \"아주 근사한 물건입니다. 도련님의 용도에 추천드릴 만합니다, 정말이지 특상품이니까요. 하지만 다른 것들도 보셔야지요. 4번 가져와, 너!\" (점원 아이에게 한 말이었는데, 그 못된 녀석이 옷감으로 나를 슬쩍 건드리거나 다른 허물없는 짓을 할 위험을 미리 내다보고서, 더없이 무섭게 노려보며 한 말이었다.)\n\n트랩 씨는 그 아이가 4번 옷감을 계산대에 내려놓고 다시 안전한 거리로 물러설 때까지 매서운 눈길을 한순간도 떼지 않았다. 그러고 나서 그는 5번과 8번을 가져오라 명했다. \"여기서 잔재주 부릴 생각일랑 마라.\" 트랩 씨가 말했다. \"안 그랬다간 살아 있는 동안 두고두고 후회하게 될 거다, 이 어린 망나니 같으니.\"\n\n이윽고 트랩 씨는 4번 위로 몸을 굽히고, 일종의 공손한 은밀함을 담아, 그것을 여름용으로 가벼운 물건이며 귀족과 신사 계층에서 크게 유행하는 물건이고, 명망 있는 동향 사람이(나를 동향 사람으로 칠 수 있다면 말이지만) 그것을 입었다고 떠올리는 일이 자기로서는 두고두고 영광이 될 만한 물건이라며 내게 권했다. \"5번과 8번은 가져오는 거냐, 이 부랑아 녀석아.\" 그러고서 트랩 씨가 아이에게 말했다. \"아니면 내가 너를 가게 밖으로 걷어차 내고 직접 가져올까?\"\n\n나는 트랩 씨의 안목에 도움을 받아 정장 한 벌의 옷감을 골랐고, 치수를 재러 다시 거실로 들어갔다. 트랩 씨에게는 이미 내 치수가 있었고, 전에는 그것으로 충분히 만족했으면서도, 그는 미안한 듯 그 치수로는 \"지금 상황에서는 안 된답니다, 도련님 — 도무지 안 돼요\"라고 말했다. 그리하여 트랩 씨는 마치 내가 토지이고 자기는 최고급 측량 기사라도 되는 양 거실에서 내 치수를 재고 계산하며, 어찌나 엄청난 수고를 들이던지, 옷 한 벌 값으로는 도저히 그 노고를 보상할 수 없으리라는 생각이 들 정도였다. 마침내 일을 끝낸 그는 목요일 저녁에 펌블추크 씨 댁으로 물건을 보내기로 정하고, 거실 문고리에 손을 얹은 채 말했다. \"압니다, 도련님, 런던 신사분들께서 으레 시골 솜씨를 봐주시리라 기대할 수야 없겠지요. 하지만 동향 사람 자격으로 이따금 일감을 주신다면, 저로서는 그것을 크나큰 영광으로 여기겠습니다. 안녕히 가십시오, 도련님, 정말 감사합니다. — 문 열어라!\"\n\n마지막 말은 점원 아이를 향해 내던져진 것이었는데, 녀석은 그게 무슨 뜻인지 도통 알지 못했다. 그러나 주인이 두 손으로 나를 떠받들어 배웅하는 동안 녀석이 풀이 죽는 것을 나는 보았으며, 돈의 어마어마한 위력에 대한 내 첫 번째 또렷한 경험은, 그것이 트랩네 점원 아이를 도덕적으로 납작 엎드리게 만들었다는 것이었다.\n\n이 기억할 만한 사건 뒤에, 나는 모자 가게며 구두 가게며 양말 가게에 갔는데, 그 채비에 그토록 많은 직종의 손길이 필요하다니, 나 자신이 마치 머더 허버드의 개라도 된 듯한 기분이 들었다. 나는 또 역마차 사무소에 가서 토요일 아침 일곱 시 자리를 예매했다. 가는 곳마다 내가 상당한 재산을 물려받게 되었노라 설명할 필요까지는 없었으나, 그런 취지의 말을 한마디라도 흘리면, 어김없이 그 일을 맡은 상인은 큰길 쪽 창밖으로 흩어지던 주의를 거두고 온 마음을 내게로 쏟았다. 원하는 것을 죄다 주문하고 나서, 나는 펌블추크 씨 댁으로 발걸음을 옮겼는데, 그 신사의 사업장에 다가가면서 보니 그가 문간에 서 있었다.\n\n그는 몹시 안달이 난 채 나를 기다리고 있었다. 그는 아침 일찍 경마차를 몰고 나가 대장간에 들러 그 소식을 들었던 것이다. 그는 반웰 거실에 나를 위해 가벼운 잔칫상을 차려 놓았고, 내 거룩한 몸이 지나갈 때 그 역시 점원에게 \"통로에서 비켜라\" 하고 명했다.\n\n\"이보게, 내 친애하는 벗.\" 펌블추크 씨는 그와 나, 그리고 잔칫상만 남게 되자 내 두 손을 맞잡으며 말했다. \"자네의 행운을 진심으로 축하하네. 마땅히 누릴 만하지, 마땅히 누릴 만하고말고!\"\n\n이것은 핵심을 찌르는 말이었고, 나는 그가 제법 사리에 맞게 표현했다고 생각했다.\n\n\"생각해 보면 말일세.\" 펌블추크 씨가 한동안 콧김을 내뿜으며 나를 흠모하듯 바라보고 나서 말했다. \"이 일에 이르도록 길을 터 준 변변찮은 도구가 바로 나였다는 것은, 자랑스러운 보답이 아닐 수 없네.\"\n\n나는 펌블추크 씨에게, 그 점에 관해서는 절대로 무슨 말이나 암시를 입에 올려서는 안 된다는 것을 명심해 달라고 청했다.\n\n\"이보게, 내 친애하는 젊은 벗.\" 펌블추크 씨가 말했다. \"내가 자네를 그렇게 불러도 된다면 말일세 —\"\n\n나는 \"물론이지요\" 하고 나직이 중얼거렸고, 펌블추크 씨는 다시 내 두 손을 맞잡고서, 비록 꽤 아래쪽이긴 했으나 감격에 겨운 듯한 몸짓을 제 조끼에 전했다. \"친애하는 젊은 벗, 내가 없는 동안에 조지프의 마음속에 그 사실을 늘 일깨워 줌으로써 나의 작은 정성을 다하리라는 것을 믿어 주게. — 조지프!\" 펌블추크 씨가 가엾어하며 타이르듯 말했다. \"조지프!! 조지프!!!\" 그러고서 그는 고개를 가로젓고 손가락으로 톡톡 두드리며, 조지프의 모자람에 대한 자신의 소견을 드러냈다.\n\n\"하지만 친애하는 젊은 벗.\" 펌블추크 씨가 말했다. \"시장할 테고, 기진맥진할 테지. 앉게나. 여기 보어 여관에서 가져온 닭고기가 있고, 보어에서 가져온 혀고기가 있고, 보어에서 가져온 자질구레한 것이 한두 가지 더 있는데, 부디 업신여기지 말아 주게. 그런데 내가 말일세.\" 펌블추크 씨는 앉자마자 곧바로 다시 일어서며 말했다. \"내 앞에 있는 사람이, 행복한 어린 시절에 내가 함께 어울려 놀던 바로 그 아이가 맞는가? 그러니 한번 — 한번 — 그래도 되겠나?\"\n\n이 \"한번 그래도 되겠나\"는 악수를 해도 되느냐는 뜻이었다. 나는 동의했고, 그는 열렬했으며, 그러고 나서 다시 자리에 앉았다.\n\n\"여기 포도주가 있네.\" 펌블추크 씨가 말했다. \"마시세, 행운의 여신께 감사를. 그리고 그분이 언제까지나 한결같은 안목으로 자신의 총아를 골라내시기를! 그렇지만 나는 도무지.\" 펌블추크 씨가 다시 일어서며 말했다. \"내 앞에 한 사람을 두고 — 또한 그 한 사람을 위해 잔을 들면서 — 다시금 청하지 않고는 견딜 수가 없네. 한번 — 한번 — 그래도 되겠나?\"\n\n나는 그래도 된다고 말했고, 그는 다시 나와 악수한 다음, 잔을 비우고 거꾸로 뒤집었다. 나도 그렇게 했는데, 만일 내가 마시기 전에 나 자신을 거꾸로 뒤집었더라도, 포도주가 이보다 더 곧장 내 머리로 올라갈 수는 없었을 것이다.\n\n펌블추크 씨는 내게 간이 붙은 날개살과 가장 좋은 혀고기 한 점을 권하면서(이제는 그 외진 막다른 골목 같은 돼지고기 따위는 없었다), 자신은 비교적 말하자면 전혀 챙기지 않았다. \"아! 가금아, 가금아! 너는 미처 생각지도 못했겠지.\" 펌블추크 씨가 접시 위의 새에게 말을 건네며 말했다. \"어린 솜털 새끼였을 적에, 네게 어떤 운명이 마련되어 있는지 말이다. 너는 이 누추한 지붕 아래에서, 그러니까 — 약점이라 부르고 싶으면 그러게나.\" 펌블추크 씨가 다시 일어서며 말했다. \"하지만 한번 — 한번 — 그래도 되겠나?\"\n\n그래도 되느냐는 그 절차를 되풀이해 묻는 것조차 불필요해지기 시작했으므로, 그는 곧장 그렇게 했다. 그가 어떻게 그토록 자주, 내 나이프에 다치지도 않고 그 짓을 해냈는지 나는 모르겠다.\n\n\"그리고 자네 누님 말일세.\" 그는 한동안 꾸준히 먹고 나서 말을 이었다. \"손수 자네를 길러 내는 영광을 누리셨던 그분 말이야! 이제는 그분이 그 영광을 온전히 헤아릴 만한 처지가 못 된다는 걸 생각하면, 참으로 가슴 아픈 광경이지. 한번 —\"\n\n나는 그가 또다시 내게 달려들려는 것을 보고 그를 막았다.\n\n\"누님의 건강을 위해 마셔요.\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"아!\" 펌블추크 씨가 의자에 등을 기대며, 흠모에 겨워 완전히 맥이 풀린 채 외쳤다. \"바로 그래서 다들 그분들을 알아본다니까요, 도련님!\" (그 도련님이 누구인지 나는 모르겠으나, 분명 나는 아니었고, 그 자리에 제삼자도 없었다.) \"바로 그래서 다들 고결한 분들을 알아보는 거예요, 도련님! 늘 너그럽고 늘 상냥하시지. 이건.\" 비굴한 펌블추크 씨가 입도 대지 않은 잔을 황급히 내려놓고 다시 일어서며 말했다. \"범상한 사람에게는, 같은 짓을 되풀이하는 것처럼 보일지도 모르겠으나 — 그래도 한번 — 그래도 되겠나?\"\n\n그렇게 하고 나서 그는 자리로 돌아가 내 누나의 건강을 위해 잔을 들었다. \"우리가 결코 눈멀어서는 안 되겠지.\" 펌블추크 씨가 말했다. \"그분의 성마른 결함에는 말일세. 그래도 그분 본심은 좋았으리라 믿어야지.\"\n\n이쯤 되자 나는 그의 얼굴이 점점 벌게지는 것을 알아채기 시작했다. 나로 말하자면, 온몸이 다 얼굴이 된 듯, 포도주에 흠뻑 젖어 화끈거렸다.\n\n나는 펌블추크 씨에게 새 옷을 그의 집으로 보내게 하고 싶다고 말했고, 그는 내가 자신을 그렇게 각별히 대접하자 황홀해했다. 나는 마을에서 남의 눈에 띄는 것을 피하고 싶은 까닭을 이야기했고, 그는 그것을 하늘에 닿도록 칭송했다. 그는 자기 말고는 내 신뢰를 받을 만한 사람이 아무도 없다는 뜻을 넌지시 비치고는 — 요컨대, 한번 그래도 되겠느냐는 것이었다. 그러고 나서 그는 다정스레, 우리가 어린 시절 셈하기 놀이를 하던 것을 기억하느냐고, 또 나를 도제로 들이러 함께 갔던 것을, 그리고 사실상 자기야말로 늘 내가 가장 좋아하던 사람이자 내가 가려 뽑은 벗이 아니었느냐고 물었다. 설령 내가 마신 것보다 열 배나 많은 포도주를 마셨더라도, 나는 그가 결코 나에게 그런 관계에 있은 적이 없음을 알았을 테고, 마음 깊은 곳에서는 그런 생각을 부인했을 것이다. 그럼에도 불구하고, 나는 내가 그를 크게 오해해 왔으며, 그가 분별 있고 실속 있고 마음씨 고운 더없이 훌륭한 사람이라고 확신하게 되었던 것을 기억한다.\n\n그는 차츰차츰 나에게 큰 신뢰를 두기에 이르러, 자기 자신의 일에 관해서까지 내 조언을 구했다. 그는 만약 이 사업장을 넓히기만 한다면, 이 동네에서든 다른 어느 동네에서든 일찍이 없었던, 곡물과 종자 거래의 대대적인 합병과 독점의 기회가 있노라고 말했다. 막대한 재산의 실현에 오로지 모자란 것이 무엇인가 하면, 그는 그것을 더 많은 자본이라 여겼다. 바로 그 두 마디, 더 많은 자본 말이다. 그런데 그가(펌블추크 씨가) 보기에, 만약 그 자본을 익명의 동업자를 통해 이 사업에 끌어들인다면, 도련님, 그 익명의 동업자는 그저 마음 내킬 때마다 몸소든 대리인을 통해서든 걸어 들어와 장부를 검사하고 — 일 년에 두 번 걸어 들어와 오할의 이문을 제 주머니에 챙겨 가기만 하면 되니 — 그가 보기에 이것은 기개와 재산을 겸비한 젊은 신사에게 눈여겨볼 만한 좋은 기회가 될 수 있겠다는 것이었다. 그런데 내 생각은 어떠냐고? 그는 내 의견을 크게 신뢰하니, 내 생각은 어떠냐고? 나는 내 의견을 내놓았다. \"좀 두고 봅시다!\" 이 견해의 광대함과 명료함이 한데 어우러져 어찌나 그를 사로잡았던지, 그는 이제 나와 악수해도 되느냐고 묻지도 않고, 정말로 해야겠다고 말하며 — 그렇게 했다.\n\n우리는 포도주를 다 마셨고, 펌블추크 씨는 조지프가 본분을 지키도록 다잡고(무슨 본분인지 나는 모르겠다), 또 내게 효과적이고 한결같은 봉사를 다하겠노라(무슨 봉사인지 나는 모르겠다) 거듭거듭 맹세했다. 그는 또 내 평생 처음으로, 그리고 분명 제 비밀을 놀랍도록 잘 지켜 오다가, 자기가 늘 나를 두고 \"저 아이는 범상한 아이가 아니다. 내 말을 명심해라, 저 아이의 운수도 범상한 운수가 아닐 게다\" 하고 말해 왔노라 털어놓았다. 그는 눈물 어린 미소를 지으며, 이제 와 생각하니 참으로 묘한 일이라고 말했고, 나도 그렇다고 말했다. 마침내 나는 햇살의 행태에 뭔가 예사롭지 않은 데가 있다는 어렴풋한 느낌과 함께 바깥 공기 속으로 나왔는데, 정신을 차려 보니 길에 대해서는 아무 의식도 없이 어느새 비몽사몽 통행세 징수소까지 와 있었다.\n\n거기서 나는 펌블추크 씨가 부르는 소리에 정신이 들었다. 그는 볕이 잘 드는 거리 저 아래쪽에서, 멈추라며 요란하게 손짓을 하고 있었다. 나는 걸음을 멈추었고, 그가 숨을 헐떡이며 다가왔다.\n\n\"안 되지, 이보게, 내 친애하는 벗.\" 그는 말할 만큼 숨을 돌리고 나서 말했다. \"내가 막을 수 있는 한은 안 되지. 이 자리를 자네의 그 상냥함 없이 그냥 보낼 수야 없잖나. — 한번, 오랜 벗이자 자네를 아끼는 사람으로서, 한번 그래도 되겠나? 그래도 되겠나?\"\n\n우리는 적어도 백 번째로 악수를 나누었고, 그는 더없이 분개한 태도로 어느 젊은 짐마차꾼더러 내 앞에서 비키라고 호령했다. 그러고서 그는 내게 축복을 빌며, 내가 길모퉁이를 돌아 사라질 때까지 손을 흔들며 서 있었다. 그리고 나는 어느 밭으로 접어들어 산울타리 아래에서 한참 낮잠을 잔 다음에야 집으로 가는 길을 마저 갔다.\n\n런던으로 가져갈 짐은 변변찮았으니, 내가 가진 그 얼마 안 되는 것 가운데서도 새로운 신분에 어울리는 것은 거의 없었기 때문이다. 그러나 나는 바로 그날 오후부터 짐을 꾸리기 시작했고, 한순간도 허비할 수 없다는 허구 속에서, 이튿날 아침에야 필요할 줄 뻔히 아는 물건들까지 정신없이 챙겨 넣었다.\n\n그렇게 화요일과 수요일과 목요일이 지나갔고, 금요일 아침에 나는 새 옷을 차려입고 해비셤 양을 방문하러 펌블추크 씨 댁으로 갔다. 옷을 갈아입으라고 펌블추크 씨 자신의 방이 내게 내어졌는데, 그 행사를 위해 일부러 깨끗한 수건들로 꾸며져 있었다. 새 옷은 물론 다소 실망스러웠다. 아마 옷이라는 것이 생겨난 이래로 새로 지어 입은, 잔뜩 고대하던 옷치고 입는 이의 기대에 조금이나마 못 미치지 않은 것은 하나도 없으리라. 그러나 새 정장을 입은 지 반 시간쯤 지나, 다리를 보려는 헛된 시도로 펌블추크 씨의 몹시도 작은 거울 앞에서 끝없이 갖은 자세를 취해 본 뒤에는, 한결 잘 맞는 듯이 보였다. 십 마일쯤 떨어진 이웃 읍내에 장이 서는 아침이라, 펌블추크 씨는 집에 없었다. 나는 그에게 정확히 언제 떠날 작정인지 말하지 않았으니, 출발하기 전에 그와 다시 악수할 일도 없을 듯했다. 이 모든 것이 마땅히 그러해야 할 대로였다. 나는 새 차림으로 길을 나섰는데, 점원 앞을 지나야 한다는 것이 끔찍이도 부끄러웠고, 결국 내가 외양에서 불리한 처지에 있는 게 아닌가, 일요일 정장을 입은 조의 모습과 어딘가 비슷한 게 아닌가 하는 의심마저 들었다.\n\n나는 온갖 뒷길로 빙 둘러 해비셤 양의 집으로 갔고, 장갑의 빳빳하고 긴 손가락 탓에 어색하게 초인종을 울렸다. 세라 포켓이 문으로 나왔는데, 그토록 달라진 나를 보고는 정말이지 휘청 뒤로 물러섰다. 호두 껍데기 같은 그녀의 얼굴빛도 갈색에서 초록과 노랑으로 변했다.\n\n\"너?\" 그녀가 말했다. \"너라고? 어머나 세상에! 무슨 일이냐?\"\n\n\"저는 런던에 갑니다, 포켓 양.\" 내가 말했다. \"그래서 해비셤 양께 작별 인사를 드리고 싶어요.\"\n\n내가 올 줄은 몰랐는지, 그녀는 나를 들여보내도 되는지 물으러 가는 동안 마당에 가둬 둔 채 떠났다. 아주 잠깐 지체한 뒤 그녀는 돌아와 나를 데리고 올라갔는데, 내내 나를 빤히 쳐다보았다.\n\n해비셤 양은 그 길게 차려진 식탁이 있는 방에서, 목발 지팡이에 기댄 채 운동을 하고 있었다. 방은 예전과 다름없이 불이 밝혀져 있었고, 우리가 들어서는 소리에 그녀는 걸음을 멈추고 돌아섰다. 그때 그녀는 마침 그 썩은 웨딩 케이크와 나란한 위치에 있었다.\n\n\"가지 마라, 세라.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"그래, 핍?\"\n\n\"저는 내일 런던으로 떠납니다, 해비셤 양.\" 나는 무슨 말을 하든 몹시 조심했다. \"그래서 작별 인사를 드려도 언짢아하지 않으시리라 생각했어요.\"\n\n\"화사한 차림새로구나, 핍.\" 그녀가 마치 나를 변신시킨 요정 대모가 마지막 선물을 내리기라도 하듯, 목발 지팡이를 내 둘레로 빙빙 놀리며 말했다.\n\n\"지난번에 뵌 뒤로 저는 이렇게 큰 행운을 얻었어요, 해비셤 양.\" 나는 나직이 말했다. \"그리고 정말 감사드려요, 해비셤 양!\"\n\n\"그래, 그래!\" 그녀가 무안해하며 시샘하는 세라를 더없이 흐뭇하게 바라보며 말했다. \"재거스 씨를 만났단다. 나도 그 이야기를 들었지, 핍. 그래, 내일 떠난다고?\"\n\n\"네, 해비셤 양.\"\n\n\"그리고 어느 부유한 분에게 입양된 거고?\"\n\n\"네, 해비셤 양.\"\n\n\"이름은 밝혀지지 않았고?\"\n\n\"네, 해비셤 양.\"\n\n\"그리고 재거스 씨가 네 후견인이 되었고?\"\n\n\"네, 해비셤 양.\"\n\n세라 포켓의 질투에 찬 낭패감을 즐기는 그녀의 흥취가 어찌나 컸던지, 그녀는 이 질문과 대답을 그야말로 흡족하게 음미했다. \"좋아!\" 그녀가 말을 이었다. \"네 앞에는 전도유망한 길이 펼쳐져 있구나. 착하게 굴고 — 그럴 자격을 갖추고 — 재거스 씨의 지시를 따르거라.\" 그녀는 나를 보고, 또 세라를 보았으며, 세라의 얼굴에서는 경계심에 찬 표정으로부터 잔인한 미소가 비어져 나왔다. \"잘 가거라, 핍! — 너는 언제까지나 핍이라는 이름을 지녀야 한단다, 알겠지.\"\n\n\"네, 해비셤 양.\"\n\n\"잘 가거라, 핍!\"\n\n그녀가 손을 내밀었고, 나는 한쪽 무릎을 꿇고 그 손을 입술에 가져다 댔다. 그녀에게 어떻게 작별 인사를 해야 할지 미리 생각해 둔 것은 아니었으나, 그 순간 자연스레 이렇게 하게 되었다. 그녀는 그 기이한 눈에 승리감을 담아 세라 포켓을 바라보았고, 그렇게 나는 나의 요정 대모를 두고 떠났다. 두 손을 다 목발 지팡이에 얹은 채, 거미줄에 가려진 썩은 웨딩 케이크 곁에서, 어슴푸레 불 밝혀진 방 한가운데 서 있는 그녀를.\n\n세라 포켓은 마치 내가 반드시 배웅해 내보내야 할 유령이라도 되는 양 나를 아래층으로 인도했다. 그녀는 내 달라진 모습을 도무지 받아들이지 못했고, 극도로 어리둥절해했다. 나는 \"안녕히 계세요, 포켓 양\" 하고 말했으나, 그녀는 그저 멍하니 쳐다볼 뿐, 내가 말을 했다는 것조차 알아챌 만큼 정신이 멀쩡해 보이지 않았다. 집을 벗어나자, 나는 부지런히 펌블추크 씨 댁으로 돌아가 새 옷을 벗고 보따리로 꾸린 다음, 헌 옷차림으로 그것을 들고 집으로 향했는데 — 사실대로 말하면 — 보따리를 들었으면서도 한결 마음이 편안했다.\n\n그리하여 그토록 더디게 흘러가리라던 엿새가 어느새 빠르게 흘러 다 지나갔고, 내일이라는 날이 내가 그것을 마주 볼 수 있는 것보다 더 의연하게 나를 마주 보았다. 엿새의 저녁이 닷새로, 나흘로, 사흘로, 이틀로 차츰 줄어드는 동안, 나는 조와 비디와 함께하는 시간을 점점 더 소중히 여기게 되었다. 이 마지막 저녁에 나는 두 사람을 기쁘게 해 주려고 새 옷을 차려입고, 잠자리에 들 때까지 내 화려함 속에 앉아 있었다. 그 기념으로 우리는 따뜻한 저녁을 들었는데, 으레 빠지지 않는 통구이 새가 상을 빛냈고, 마무리로 플립을 좀 마셨다. 우리는 모두 몹시 가라앉아 있었고, 기운찬 척해 본들 조금도 나아지지 않았다.\n\n나는 작은 손가방을 들고 아침 다섯 시에 우리 마을을 떠나기로 되어 있었고, 조에게는 나 혼자 걸어가고 싶다고 말해 둔 터였다. 두렵건대 — 가슴 아프게도 두렵건대 — 이 작정은 우리가 함께 역마차에 가면 나와 조 사이에 드러날 대조에 대한 내 의식에서 비롯된 것이었다. 나는 그 결정에 그런 더러운 구석이 전혀 없는 척 스스로를 속여 왔으나, 이 마지막 밤에 내 작은 방으로 올라가면서, 어쩌면 그럴지도 모른다는 것을 인정하지 않을 수 없었고, 다시 내려가 조에게 아침에 나와 함께 걸어가 달라고 간청하고픈 충동에 사로잡혔다. 그러나 나는 그러지 않았다.\n\n밤새도록 내 어수선한 잠 속에는 역마차들이 나타나, 런던이 아니라 엉뚱한 곳으로 가고, 그 멍에에는 말은 한 마리도 없이 어떤 때는 개가, 어떤 때는 고양이가, 어떤 때는 돼지가, 어떤 때는 사람이 매여 있었다. 기괴하게 어그러진 여정들이 날이 밝아 새들이 지저귈 때까지 나를 사로잡았다. 그러고 나서 나는 일어나 옷을 대충 걸치고, 마지막으로 한 번 바깥을 내다보려고 창가에 앉았는데, 그러다가 그만 잠이 들고 말았다.\n\n비디는 어찌나 일찍 일어나 내 아침을 차렸던지, 내가 창가에서 한 시간도 채 자지 못했음에도, 화들짝 깨어나며 분명 오후 늦은 시각일 거라는 끔찍한 생각에 사로잡혔을 때 부엌 불의 연기 냄새가 났다. 그러나 그 한참 뒤에도, 또 찻잔 부딪치는 소리를 듣고 채비가 다 끝난 한참 뒤에도, 나는 아래층으로 내려갈 결심이 서지 않았다. 결국 나는 위층에 머문 채, 비디가 늦었다고 나를 부를 때까지, 내 작은 손가방을 거듭 열어 끈을 풀었다가 다시 잠그고 끈을 동여매기를 되풀이했다.\n\n아무 맛도 없는 황급한 아침 식사였다. 나는 식사 자리에서 일어나며, 이제야 막 떠올랐다는 듯이 일종의 활기를 띠고 말했다. \"자! 이제 그만 가 봐야겠어요!\" 그러고는 늘 앉던 의자에서 웃고 고개를 끄덕이며 몸을 떨고 있는 누나에게 입을 맞추고, 비디에게 입을 맞추고, 조의 목을 두 팔로 끌어안았다. 그런 다음 작은 손가방을 들고 밖으로 걸어 나왔다. 내가 마지막으로 그들을 본 것은, 곧이어 등 뒤에서 부산한 기척이 들려 돌아보았을 때였는데, 조가 내 뒤로 헌 신발 한 짝을 던지고 비디가 또 다른 헌 신발 한 짝을 던지는 것이 보였다. 그래서 나는 걸음을 멈추고 모자를 흔들었고, 정다운 조는 억센 오른팔을 머리 위로 흔들며 목쉰 소리로 \"만세!\" 하고 외쳤으며, 비디는 앞치마를 얼굴에 갖다 댔다.\n\n나는 떠나는 것이 생각했던 것보다 수월하다고 여기며 빠른 걸음으로 걸어 나갔고, 큰길 모두가 지켜보는 앞에서 역마차 뒤로 헌 신발을 던지는 일이 있어서는 결코 안 되었으리라 생각했다. 나는 휘파람을 불며 떠나는 것을 대수롭잖게 여겼다. 그러나 마을은 더없이 평화롭고 고요했고, 옅은 안개가 마치 내게 세상을 보여 주려는 듯 엄숙히 피어오르고 있었으며, 나는 그곳에서 그토록 순진하고 보잘것없는 존재였던 데다, 그 너머의 모든 것은 그토록 미지의 거대한 것이었으니, 한순간 나는 거센 흐느낌과 함께 울음을 터뜨리고 말았다. 그것은 마을 끝 이정표 옆에서였는데, 나는 그 위에 손을 얹고 말했다. \"잘 있어, 오 내 정다운, 정다운 벗이여!\"\n\n우리의 눈물을 결코 부끄러워할 필요가 없음을 하늘은 안다. 눈물이란 우리의 굳은 마음을 뒤덮은 채 눈을 멀게 하는 이 땅의 먼지 위에 내리는 비이기 때문이다. 나는 울고 난 뒤가 울기 전보다 나았다 — 더 애틋하고, 내 배은망덕을 더 깊이 깨닫고, 더 부드러워졌다. 만약 진작 울었더라면, 그때 조가 내 곁에 있었을 텐데.\n\n그 눈물에, 그리고 그 고요한 길을 걷는 동안 다시금 터져 나온 눈물에 어찌나 마음이 가라앉았던지, 역마차에 올라타 읍내를 벗어났을 때, 나는 아픈 마음으로, 말을 바꾸는 곳에서 내려 걸어 돌아가 집에서 하룻저녁을 더 보내고 더 나은 작별을 할까 곰곰 망설였다. 말을 바꾸었는데도 나는 마음을 정하지 못한 채, 다음번에 말을 바꿀 때 내려 걸어 돌아가는 것도 얼마든지 가능하리라며 스스로 위안 삼아 거듭 생각했다. 그리고 이런 망설임에 잠겨 있는 동안, 길을 따라 우리 쪽으로 다가오는 어떤 사내에게서 조와 똑 닮은 모습을 떠올리며, 내 가슴은 두근거리곤 했다. — 마치 그가 거기 있을 수 있기라도 한 것처럼!\n\n우리는 말을 바꾸고, 또다시 바꾸었으며, 이제는 돌아가기에 너무 늦고 너무 멀어졌기에, 나는 계속 나아갔다. 그리고 안개는 이제 모두 엄숙히 피어올라, 세상이 내 앞에 펼쳐져 있었다.\n이것으로 핍의 유산 그 첫 번째 단계가 끝난다.\n\n# Chapter XX.\n\n우리 고장에서 대도시까지의 여정은 다섯 시간쯤 걸리는 길이었다. 내가 승객으로 탄 사두마차가 런던 칩사이드의 우드 스트리트, 크로스 키스 부근에서 풀린 실밥처럼 흐트러진 차량들의 혼잡 속으로 들어섰을 때는 정오를 조금 지난 무렵이었다.\n\n당시 우리 영국인들은, 우리가 무엇이든 최고의 것을 소유하고 또 우리 자신이 최고라는 사실을 의심하는 것은 반역이라고 특히 단단히 못 박아 두고 있었다. 그렇지 않았더라면, 런던의 그 어마어마한 규모에 겁을 먹는 한편으로 나는, 이곳이 오히려 추하고, 구불구불하고, 비좁고, 더러운 곳은 아닌지 어렴풋한 의심을 품었을지도 모른다.\n\n재거스 씨는 자신의 주소를 빠짐없이 적어 보내 주었다. 그것은 리틀 브리튼이었는데, 그는 자기 명함에다 그 뒤에 \"스미스필드 바로 옆, 그리고 마차 사무소와 가까운 곳\"이라고 써 두었다. 그럼에도 한 삯마차 마부는 — 기름때 절은 그 외투에 달린 망토 자락이 제 나이만큼이나 많은 듯 보였다 — 나를 자기 마차에 꾸려 넣고는, 접었다 펴는 짤랑거리는 발판의 장벽으로 나를 가두었다. 마치 나를 오십 마일이나 데려가기라도 할 것처럼. 그가 마부석에 올라타는 것은 — 그 자리는 비바람에 얼룩지고 좀이 슬어 누더기가 된 낡은 완두콩빛 마부석 덮개로 장식되어 있었던 것으로 기억한다 — 그야말로 시간이 걸리는 작업이었다. 그것은 놀라운 마차였으니, 바깥쪽에는 커다란 백작 문장이 여섯 개나 달려 있고, 뒤쪽에는 몇 명인지도 모를 하인들이 붙잡고 매달리라고 누더기 같은 것들이 늘어져 있었으며, 그 아래에는 어설픈 풋내기 하인들이 매달릴 유혹에 넘어가지 못하도록 써레가 달려 있었다.\n\n마차를 즐기며 이것이 어쩌면 그리도 짚더미 마당 같으면서 또한 어쩌면 그리도 넝마전 같은지 생각하고, 말의 여물자루는 왜 안쪽에 매달아 두는지 의아해 할 겨를도 거의 없었는데, 어느새 마부가 곧 멈출 양 마부석에서 내려오기 시작하는 것이 눈에 들어왔다. 그리고 우리는 정말로 곧 멈추었다. 어느 음산한 거리에서, 열린 문 위에 \"재거스 씨\"라고 칠해진 어떤 사무소 앞에서.\n\n\"얼마요?\" 나는 마부에게 물었다.\n\n마부가 대답했다. \"한 실링이오 — 손님이 더 주고 싶으시다면 모를까.\"\n\n나는 당연히 더 줄 마음은 없다고 말했다.\n\n\"그럼 한 실링이겠구먼요.\" 마부가 말했다. \"괜히 골치 아픈 일에 휘말리고 싶진 않으니까. 나는 그 양반을 안단 말이오!\" 그는 재거스 씨의 이름에 음험하게 한쪽 눈을 감고는 고개를 절레절레 흔들었다.\n\n마부가 한 실링을 받고, 이윽고 마부석으로 오르는 일을 마무리하고, 그리고 (마음이 놓이는 듯이 보이게) 떠나가 버렸을 때, 나는 작은 여행 가방을 손에 들고 앞쪽 사무소로 들어가 재거스 씨가 계시느냐고 물었다.\n\n\"안 계십니다.\" 서기가 대답했다. \"지금 법정에 가 계십니다. 제가 핍 씨를 뵙고 있는 건가요?\"\n\n나는 그가 핍 씨를 뵙고 있는 것이 맞다고 알렸다.\n\n\"재거스 씨께서, 방에서 기다려 달라는 말씀을 남기셨습니다. 사건을 맡고 계시니 얼마나 걸릴지는 말씀드릴 수 없다고요. 하지만 그분 시간이 귀중한 분이니, 어쩔 수 없는 것 이상으로 오래 끄시지는 않을 게 당연하지요.\"\n\n그 말과 함께 서기는 문을 열고 나를 뒤편 안쪽 방으로 안내했다. 거기서 우리는, 벨벳 양복에 무릎까지 오는 반바지를 입은 외눈박이 신사 한 명을 발견했는데, 그는 신문을 정독하다가 방해를 받자 소매로 코를 훔쳤다.\n\n\"나가서 밖에서 기다려, 마이크.\" 서기가 말했다.\n\n내가 행여 방해가 되지 않기를 바란다고 말하려 하는데, 서기는 그 신사를 내가 본 적 없는 무뚝뚝한 손짓으로 떠밀어 내보내고는, 그의 모피 모자까지 뒤따라 던져 준 다음, 나를 혼자 남겨 두었다.\n\n재거스 씨의 방은 천창으로만 빛이 들어왔는데, 더없이 음산한 곳이었다. 천창은 깨진 머리통처럼 괴상하게 기울어져 있었고, 잇닿은 일그러진 집들은 마치 그 창을 통해 나를 내려다보려고 제 몸을 비틀기라도 한 것처럼 보였다. 내가 으레 보리라 예상했던 만큼 서류가 많지는 않았다. 그리고 내가 보리라고 예상하지 못했던 기이한 물건들이 몇 가지 놓여 있었으니 — 이를테면 녹슨 낡은 권총 하나, 칼집에 든 검 한 자루, 야릇하게 생긴 상자와 꾸러미 몇 개, 그리고 선반 위에 놓인 끔찍한 석고 마스크 두 개였는데, 그 얼굴들은 유난히 부어 있고 코 언저리가 씰룩거리는 듯했다. 재거스 씨 자신의 등받이 높은 의자는 죽음처럼 새카만 말총으로 되어 있고, 둘레에 놋쇠 못이 줄지어 박혀 있어 관(棺)을 연상시켰다. 그리고 나는 그가 그 의자에 등을 기댄 채 의뢰인들을 향해 검지를 깨무는 모습을 눈앞에 그려 볼 수 있을 것 같았다. 방은 작은 편이었고, 의뢰인들은 벽에 등을 기대고 물러서는 버릇이 있었던 모양이었다. 벽은, 특히 재거스 씨의 의자 맞은편 벽은, 어깨가 닿아 번질번질했다. 또한 나는, 내가 죄 없이 그 외눈박이 신사를 쫓겨나게 만든 원인이 되었을 때, 그가 벽에 붙어 발을 끌며 나가던 것을 떠올렸다.\n\n나는 재거스 씨의 의자와 마주 보게 놓인 의뢰인용 의자에 앉았고, 그곳의 음산한 분위기에 사로잡혔다. 나는 그 서기 역시 제 주인과 똑같이, 다른 사람 누구에게나 불리한 무언가를 알고 있다는 투의 분위기를 풍긴다는 사실을 떠올렸다. 나는 위층에 서기가 또 몇 명이나 있을지, 그리고 그들 모두가 제 동료 인간들을 그렇게 해로운 방식으로 손아귀에 쥐고 있다고 자처하는지 궁금했다. 나는 방 안에 어질러진 그 모든 기이한 잡동사니의 내력은 무엇이며, 어쩌다 그것들이 여기에 오게 되었는지 궁금했다. 나는 그 부어오른 두 얼굴이 재거스 씨의 가족인지, 그리고 만약 그가 그처럼 흉하게 생긴 친척을 한 쌍이나 두는 불운을 겪었다면, 어째서 그들을 집안에 자리 잡아 주는 대신 검댕과 파리가 내려앉도록 저 먼지투성이 횃대 위에 붙박아 두었는지 궁금했다. 물론 나는 런던의 여름날을 겪어 본 적이 없었으니, 후덥지근하고 진이 빠진 공기와, 모든 것 위에 두껍게 쌓인 먼지와 모래티 때문에 기분이 짓눌렸는지도 모른다. 그러나 나는 재거스 씨의 답답한 방에서 의아해하고 기다리며 앉아 있다가, 마침내 재거스 씨의 의자 위쪽 선반에 놓인 그 두 개의 석고상을 정말이지 견딜 수 없게 되어, 자리에서 일어나 밖으로 나갔다.\n\n내가 서기에게 기다리는 동안 바람이나 좀 쐬고 오겠다고 했더니, 그는 모퉁이를 돌면 스미스필드가 나올 거라고 일러 주었다. 그래서 나는 스미스필드로 들어섰다. 그 수치스러운 곳은 온통 오물과 기름과 피와 거품으로 범벅이 되어, 마치 내 몸에 들러붙는 것만 같았다. 그래서 나는 한시바삐 그것을 떨쳐 내려고 어느 거리로 접어들었는데, 거기서 한 행인이 뉴게이트 감옥이라고 일러 준 음산한 석조 건물 뒤편으로 세인트 폴 대성당의 거대한 검은 돔이 내게로 불룩 솟아오른 것이 보였다. 감옥 담장을 따라가다 보니, 지나가는 차량들의 소음을 죽이려고 도로에 짚을 깔아 놓은 것이 눈에 띄었다. 이것으로 미루어, 또 독한 술과 맥주 냄새를 풀풀 풍기며 주변에 모여 선 사람들이 많은 것으로 미루어, 나는 재판이 진행 중이라고 추측했다.\n\n내가 여기서 이리저리 둘러보고 있는데, 몹시 더럽고 얼근히 취한 사법(司法)의 종복 하나가 나더러 안에 들어가서 재판이나 몇 건 구경하지 않겠느냐고 물었다. 반(半) 크라운만 내면 앞자리를 잡아 줄 테니, 거기서 가발에 법복을 걸친 대법원장의 모습을 한눈에 볼 수 있을 거라고 알려 주면서 — 그는 그 어마어마한 인물을 마치 밀랍 인형이라도 되는 양 들먹이더니, 곧 일 실링 육 펜스라는 깎은 값에 그를 내놓았다. 내가 약속이 있다는 핑계로 그 제안을 거절하자, 그는 친절하게도 나를 어느 마당으로 데려가 교수대가 보관된 곳을 보여 주고, 또 사람들이 공개적으로 매질당하는 곳도 보여 주더니, 이어서 채무자의 문을 보여 주었다. 죄인들이 교수형을 당하러 나오는 문이었다. 그는 그 무시무시한 문의 흥미를 한층 돋우려고, \"그놈들 중 넷\"이 모레 아침 여덟 시에 그 문으로 나와 한 줄로 죽임을 당할 거라고 일러 주었다. 이것은 끔찍한 일이었고, 런던에 대해 메스꺼운 인상을 내게 안겨 주었다. 더욱이 그 대법원장의 주인장이 (모자에서부터 장화에 이르기까지, 그리고 다시 거슬러 올라가 손수건까지 포함해서) 곰팡이 슨 옷을 걸치고 있었는데, 그 옷은 분명 원래 그의 것이 아니었고, 나는 그가 사형 집행인에게서 헐값에 사들였으리라는 생각이 머릿속에 박혀 버렸기에 더욱 그러했다. 이런 형편이고 보니, 나는 일 실링으로 그를 떨쳐 낸 것이 잘된 일이라고 생각했다.\n\n나는 재거스 씨가 들어오셨는지 물으려고 사무소에 잠깐 들렀으나, 아직 오지 않았다는 것을 알고 다시 어슬렁어슬렁 걸어 나왔다. 이번에는 리틀 브리튼을 한 바퀴 돌고 바살러뮤 클로스로 접어들었는데, 이제 나는 나 말고도 다른 사람들이 재거스 씨를 기다리며 서성이고 있다는 것을 알아차렸다. 바살러뮤 클로스에는 은밀한 인상을 풍기는 두 사내가 빈둥거리며, 이야기를 나누면서 생각에 잠긴 듯 발을 보도의 틈새에 맞춰 끼우고 있었는데, 그중 하나가 나를 처음 지나칠 때 다른 하나에게 \"이 일이 될 수 있는 일이라면 재거스가 해낼 거야\"라고 말했다. 어느 모퉁이에는 세 남자와 두 여자가 한 무리를 이루고 서 있었는데, 여자 중 하나는 더러운 숄에 얼굴을 묻고 울고 있었고, 다른 하나는 제 숄을 어깨에 끌어 두르며 \"재거스가 그 사람 편이잖아, 어밀리아, 그보다 더 뭘 바라겠어?\" 하고 위로하고 있었다. 내가 그곳에서 어정거리는 동안, 두 번째 작은 유대인을 데리고 클로스로 들어온, 눈이 충혈된 작은 유대인이 하나 있었는데, 그는 그 두 번째 유대인을 심부름 보냈다. 그리고 그 심부름꾼이 가 있는 동안, 나는 이 유대인을 눈여겨보았다. 매우 흥분하기 쉬운 기질의 그 사내는 가로등 기둥 아래에서 불안의 지그를 추며, 일종의 광란에 빠진 채 이렇게 제 가락을 붙이고 있었다. \"오 재거쓰, 재거쓰, 재거쓰! 다른 놈들은 죄다 캐그매그쓰지, 나에게 재거쓰를 다오!\" 내 후견인의 인기를 입증하는 이런 증언들은 내게 깊은 인상을 남겼고, 나는 그 어느 때보다 더욱 감탄하며 의아해했다.\n\n마침내 내가 바살러뮤 클로스의 철문 밖으로 리틀 브리튼 쪽을 내다보고 있을 때, 재거스 씨가 길을 가로질러 내게로 다가오는 것이 보였다. 기다리던 다른 모든 이들도 그를 동시에 알아보았고, 그를 향해 우르르 몰려들었다. 재거스 씨는 한 손을 내 어깨에 얹고 나에게는 아무 말 없이 나를 곁에 끼고 걸으면서, 자기 추종자들을 향해 말을 건넸다.\n\n먼저 그는 그 은밀한 두 사내를 상대했다.\n\n\"자, 나는 너희에게 할 말이 없다.\" 재거스 씨가 그들을 향해 손가락을 내던지며 말했다. \"내가 아는 것 이상은 알고 싶지 않다. 결과로 말하자면, 그건 동전 던지기다. 처음부터 동전 던지기라고 말해 두었지. 웨믹에게 돈은 지불했나?\"\n\n\"오늘 아침에 돈을 마련했습니다, 선생님.\" 한 사내가 순순히 말했고, 다른 하나는 재거스 씨의 얼굴을 찬찬히 살폈다.\n\n\"언제 마련했는지, 어디서 마련했는지, 아니 마련하기는 했는지, 그런 건 묻지 않는다. 웨믹이 그걸 받았나?\"\n\n\"네, 선생님.\" 두 사내가 한목소리로 말했다.\n\n\"좋아, 그럼 가도 된다. 자, 더는 안 된다!\" 재거스 씨가 그들을 뒤로 물리려고 손을 휘저으며 말했다. \"나에게 한마디라도 더 하면, 이 사건을 내던져 버리겠다.\"\n\n\"저희는 생각하기를, 재거스 씨—\" 한 사내가 모자를 벗으며 입을 떼었다.\n\n\"그게 바로 하지 말라고 한 짓이다.\" 재거스 씨가 말했다. \"너희가 생각해! 너희를 위해서는 내가 생각한다. 그걸로 족하다. 너희가 필요하면 내가 어디서 찾을지 안다. 너희가 나를 찾아오는 건 바라지 않아. 자, 더는 안 돼. 한마디도 듣지 않겠다.\"\n\n재거스 씨가 다시 그들을 뒤로 물리는 손짓을 하자 두 사내는 서로를 마주 보더니, 굽실거리며 물러나 더는 아무 소리도 들리지 않았다.\n\n\"그리고 이제 당신들 차례군!\" 재거스 씨가 갑자기 걸음을 멈추고, 세 남자가 얌전히 떨어져 나간 숄 두른 두 여자에게로 돌아서며 말했다. \"오! 어밀리아인가?\"\n\n\"네, 재거스 씨.\"\n\n\"그래, 기억하는가.\" 재거스 씨가 쏘아붙였다. \"내가 아니었으면 당신은 여기 있지도 못했고, 여기 있을 수도 없었다는 것을?\"\n\n\"오, 네, 선생님!\" 두 여자가 한목소리로 외쳤다. \"선생님께 하느님의 축복이 있기를요, 그야 저희도 잘 알지요!\"\n\n\"그럼 어째서,\" 재거스 씨가 말했다. \"당신들은 여기를 찾아오나?\"\n\n\"제 빌 때문이에요, 선생님!\" 울던 여자가 호소했다.\n\n\"자, 내가 말해 두지!\" 재거스 씨가 말했다. \"이번 한 번으로 끝이다. 당신 빌이 좋은 손에 맡겨져 있다는 걸 당신이 모른다면, 나는 안다. 그리고 당신이 빌 때문에 여기 와서 귀찮게 군다면, 나는 빌과 당신 둘 다를 본보기로 삼고, 그자를 내 손가락 사이로 빠져나가게 두겠다. 웨믹에게 돈은 지불했나?\"\n\n\"오, 네, 선생님! 한 푼도 빠짐없이요.\"\n\n\"좋아. 그럼 당신이 할 일은 다 한 거다. 한마디만 더 — 단 한마디만 더 하면 — 웨믹이 당신 돈을 도로 돌려줄 거야.\"\n\n이 무시무시한 위협에 두 여자는 즉시 물러났다. 이제 남은 것은 흥분 잘하는 그 유대인뿐이었는데, 그는 이미 여러 차례 재거스 씨의 외투 자락을 입술에 갖다 댄 터였다.\n\n\"나는 이 사람을 모른다!\" 재거스 씨가 똑같이 사람을 짓뭉개는 어조로 말했다. \"이 친구가 원하는 게 뭔가?\"\n\n\"나의 친애하는 재거쓰 씨. 에이브러햄 래써루쓰의 친형제 일로요?\"\n\n\"그게 누군가?\" 재거스 씨가 말했다. \"내 외투 놓게.\"\n\n청원자는 외투를 놓아주기 전에 다시 한 번 그 자락에 입 맞추고 대답했다. \"에이브러햄 래써루쓰요, 은식기 절도 혐의로요.\"\n\n\"자네는 너무 늦었네.\" 재거스 씨가 말했다. \"나는 반대편일세.\"\n\n\"오 하느님 아버지, 재거쓰 씨!\" 흥분 잘하는 나의 새 지인이 새파랗게 질려서 외쳤다. \"에이브러햄 래써루쓰에게 맞서신다는 말씀은 마세요!\"\n\n\"맞서네.\" 재거스 씨가 말했다. \"그리고 그걸로 끝이야. 비키게.\"\n\n\"재거쓰 씨! 잠깐만요! 제 사촌이 바로 지금 이 순간 웨믹 씨께 가서, 어떤 조건이든 제시하고 있단 말입니다. 재거쓰 씨! 아주 잠깐만요! 만약 황송하게도 저쪽 편에서 매수당해 주실 수만 있다면 — 아무리 더 높은 값을 치르더라도요! — 돈은 문제가 아닙니다! — 재거쓰 씨 — 재거쓰—!\"\n\n내 후견인은 더없이 무심하게 그 탄원자를 떨쳐 내고는, 그를 마치 보도가 시뻘겋게 달아오르기라도 한 듯 그 위에서 발을 동동 구르도록 내버려 두었다. 더는 방해받지 않고 우리는 앞쪽 사무소에 이르렀고, 거기서 서기와, 모피 모자를 쓴 벨벳 옷의 사내를 발견했다.\n\n\"마이크가 와 있습니다.\" 서기가 걸상에서 내려와 재거스 씨에게 은밀하게 다가오며 말했다.\n\n\"오!\" 재거스 씨가 그 사내에게로 돌아서며 말했는데, 사내는 마치 「수탉 로빈」의 황소가 종 줄을 당기듯, 이마 한가운데의 머리털 한 가닥을 잡아당기고 있었다. \"자네 사람이 오늘 오후에 출두한다고. 그래?\"\n\n\"네, 재거스 나리.\" 마이크가 만성 감기 환자 같은 목소리로 대답했다. \"한참을 애먹은 끝에, 쓸 만한 자를 하나 찾았습니다요, 나리.\"\n\n\"그자가 무엇을 맹세할 준비가 되어 있나?\"\n\n\"네, 재거스 나리.\" 마이크가 이번에는 모피 모자에 코를 훔치며 말했다. \"대체로 말해서, 뭐든지요.\"\n\n재거스 씨는 갑자기 더없이 격노했다. \"내가 전에 경고했지.\" 그가 겁에 질린 의뢰인을 향해 검지를 내던지며 말했다. \"여기서 또 한 번 감히 그따위로 지껄였다간, 너를 본보기로 삼겠다고 말이야. 이 천하의 악당아, 어떻게 감히 나에게 그런 소리를 하느냔 말이다?\"\n\n의뢰인은 겁먹은 표정이었으나, 동시에 어리둥절한 기색이기도 했으니, 마치 자기가 무슨 짓을 했는지조차 모르는 사람 같았다.\n\n\"이 멍청아!\" 서기가 낮은 목소리로 그를 팔꿈치로 쿡 찌르며 말했다. \"이 맹추야! 그걸 꼭 면전에서 말해야겠어?\"\n\n\"자, 내가 묻겠다, 이 어리석은 얼간이야.\" 내 후견인이 매우 엄하게 말했다. \"다시 한 번, 그리고 이것이 마지막이다. 네가 여기 데려온 사람이 무엇을 맹세할 준비가 되어 있느냐?\"\n\n마이크는 마치 그의 얼굴에서 무슨 교훈이라도 배우려는 듯 내 후견인을 뚫어지게 바라보더니, 천천히 대답했다. \"인품에 관해서든지, 아니면 문제의 그날 밤 내내 그자와 함께 있었고 한 번도 그자를 떠나지 않았다는 거든지요.\"\n\n\"자, 조심해라. 이 사람은 어떤 신분이냐?\"\n\n마이크는 자기 모자를 보고, 바닥을 보고, 천장을 보고, 서기를 보고, 심지어 나까지 본 다음, 초조한 태도로 대답하기 시작했다. \"저희가 그자를 이렇게 차려입혔습니다요—\" 그러자 내 후견인이 버럭 호통을 쳤다.\n\n\"뭐라고? 기어이 그러겠다는 거냐?\"\n\n(\"이 멍청아!\" 서기가 또 한 번 쿡 찌르며 덧붙였다.)\n\n어찌할 바를 모르고 한참을 더듬거리던 마이크는 표정이 환해지더니 다시 입을 열었다.\n\n\"점잖은 파이 장수처럼 차려입었습니다요. 일종의 제과사처럼요.\"\n\n\"그자가 여기 있나?\" 내 후견인이 물었다.\n\n\"그자를 두고 왔습니다요.\" 마이크가 말했다. \"모퉁이를 돈 어느 집 현관 계단에 앉혀 두었습니다요.\"\n\n\"저 창문 앞으로 지나가게 해서, 내가 보게 하라.\"\n\n가리킨 창문은 사무소 창문이었다. 우리 셋은 모두 철망 블라인드 뒤편으로 가서 그쪽으로 다가갔는데, 이윽고 그 의뢰인이 우연인 척 지나가는 것이 보였다. 살기등등하게 생긴 키 큰 사내와 함께였는데, 그자는 짧은 흰 리넨 옷에 종이 모자를 쓰고 있었다. 이 순진해 빠진 제과사는 결코 맨정신이 아니었고, 회복 중에 녹색으로 변한 멍든 눈을 하나 달고 있었는데, 그 위에 화장을 발라 가려 놓았다.\n\n\"저자더러 당장 자기 증인을 데리고 가라고 일러.\" 내 후견인이 극도의 혐오감을 드러내며 서기에게 말했다. \"그리고 저런 작자를 데려오다니 도대체 무슨 작정이냐고 물어봐.\"\n\n그러고 나서 내 후견인은 나를 자기 방으로 데려갔고, 샌드위치 상자와 셰리주가 든 휴대용 술병에서 선 채로 점심을 들면서 (그는 샌드위치를 먹으면서도 그 샌드위치마저 윽박지르는 듯했다), 나를 위해 어떤 준비를 해 두었는지 알려 주었다. 나는 \"바너드 인\"의 젊은 포켓 씨의 거처로 가게 되어 있었는데, 거기에는 나를 위한 침대가 들여져 있었다. 나는 월요일까지 젊은 포켓 씨와 함께 지내고, 월요일에는 그와 함께 그의 아버지 댁을 방문하여, 그곳이 마음에 드는지 시험 삼아 지내 보기로 되어 있었다. 또한 나는 내 용돈이 얼마인지도 들었는데 — 매우 후한 액수였다 — 후견인의 서랍들 중 하나에서, 온갖 종류의 옷가지며 그 밖에 내가 사리에 맞게 필요로 할 만한 물건들을 거래하기로 되어 있는 어떤 상인들의 명함을 건네받았다. \"자네 신용은 좋을 걸세, 핍 군.\" 내 후견인이 말했는데, 그가 황급히 원기를 돋우는 동안 그 셰리주 병은 통째로 한 통은 되는 듯한 냄새를 풍겼다. \"하지만 나는 이렇게 함으로써 자네 청구서를 점검할 수 있고, 만약 자네가 분에 넘치게 쓰는 걸 발견하면 자네를 단속할 수 있을 걸세. 물론 자네는 어떤 식으로든 빗나가겠지만, 그건 내 잘못이 아닐세.\"\n\n이 고무적인 소회를 잠시 곱씹은 다음, 나는 재거스 씨에게 마차를 부를 수 있겠느냐고 물었다. 그는 그럴 것까지 없다고, 목적지가 워낙 가까우니, 원한다면 웨믹이 함께 걸어가 줄 거라고 했다.\n\n그때 나는 웨믹이 바로 옆방의 그 서기라는 것을 알게 되었다. 그가 자리를 비운 동안 그 자리를 대신하라고 위층에서 다른 서기 하나가 종소리에 불려 내려왔고, 나는 후견인과 악수를 나눈 뒤 웨믹과 함께 거리로 나섰다. 우리는 밖에서 서성이는 새로운 무리의 사람들을 발견했지만, 웨믹은 차분하면서도 단호하게 \"내 말하지만 소용없소. 그분은 당신들 중 누구에게도 할 말이 없으실 거요\" 하고 말하여 그들 사이로 길을 텄다. 우리는 곧 그들에게서 벗어나, 나란히 걸어갔다.\n\n# Chapter XXI.\n\n웨믹 씨가 어떤 사람인지 대낮의 빛 속에서 살펴보려고 함께 걸어가며 그에게 눈길을 던졌더니, 그는 메마른 사람으로, 키가 다소 작고, 네모진 나무 같은 얼굴을 하고 있었는데, 그 표정은 무딘 끌로 어설프게 깎아 낸 것처럼 보였다. 그 얼굴에는 재료가 좀 더 부드럽고 연장이 좀 더 정교했더라면 보조개가 되었을 법한 자국들이 몇 개 있었으나, 실제로는 그저 움푹 팬 자국에 불과했다. 끌은 그의 코 위에다 그런 장식의 시도를 서너 번 했으나, 그것을 매끄럽게 다듬으려는 노력은 전혀 하지 않은 채 내버려 둔 셈이었다. 나는 그의 닳아 해진 셔츠 상태로 미루어 그가 독신이리라 판단했고, 또한 그는 적잖은 사별을 겪은 듯했다. 적어도 네 개의 상중(喪中) 반지를 끼고 있었던 데다, 유골 단지가 얹힌 무덤 옆에 한 부인과 수양버들이 그려진 브로치까지 달고 있었으니까. 나는 또한 그의 회중시계 줄에 여러 개의 반지와 인장이 매달려 있어, 마치 그가 세상을 떠난 친구들의 추억을 잔뜩 짊어지고 있는 것 같다는 것도 눈치챘다. 그는 반짝이는 눈을 — 작고, 날카롭고, 검은 눈을 — 가졌으며, 입술은 얇고 넓고 얼룩덜룩했다. 내가 믿는 바로는, 그는 그 눈과 입술을 마흔 살에서 쉰 살에 이르도록 지녀 온 듯했다.\n\n\"그래, 런던에는 처음 와 보는 거요?\" 웨믹 씨가 내게 말했다.\n\n\"네.\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"나도 한때 여기가 낯설었지요.\" 웨믹 씨가 말했다. \"지금 생각하면 우습소만!\"\n\n\"이제는 이곳을 잘 아시겠군요?\"\n\n\"뭐, 그렇소.\" 웨믹 씨가 말했다. \"이곳이 어떻게 돌아가는지는 압니다.\"\n\n\"몹시 사악한 곳인가요?\" 나는 무얼 알고자 함보다 그저 무슨 말이든 하려고 물었다.\n\n\"런던에선 속고, 털리고, 살해당할 수 있소. 하지만 그런 짓을 당신한테 해 줄 사람이야 어디에든 잔뜩 있지요.\"\n\n\"당신과 그들 사이에 나쁜 감정이 있다면 말이지요.\" 나는 그 말을 조금 누그러뜨려 보려고 말했다.\n\n\"오! 나쁜 감정 같은 건 모르겠소.\" 웨믹 씨가 대꾸했다. \"나쁜 감정이랄 게 그리 많지도 않고. 거기서 얻을 게 뭐라도 있으면 그자들은 해치우는 거요.\"\n\n\"그게 더 고약하군요.\"\n\n\"그렇게 생각하시오?\" 웨믹 씨가 대꾸했다. \"거기서 거기라고 봅니다만.\"\n\n그는 모자를 뒤통수에 얹은 채 똑바로 앞만 바라보았다. 거리에 자기 주의를 끌 만한 것이라곤 아무것도 없다는 듯, 자족적인 태도로 걸어갔다. 그의 입은 그야말로 우체통 같은 입이어서, 기계적으로 미소 짓는 듯한 인상을 풍겼다. 그것이 그저 기계적인 인상일 뿐이며 그가 전혀 미소 짓고 있는 게 아니라는 걸 내가 깨달은 것은, 우리가 홀번 언덕 꼭대기에 다다른 뒤였다.\n\n\"매슈 포켓 씨가 어디 사시는지 아세요?\" 나는 웨믹 씨에게 물었다.\n\n\"알지요.\" 그가 그 방향으로 고개를 끄덕이며 말했다. \"런던 서쪽, 해머스미스에 살고 있소.\"\n\n\"먼가요?\"\n\n\"글쎄! 오 마일쯤 된다고 합시다.\"\n\n\"그분을 아세요?\"\n\n\"이런, 당신은 영락없는 반대 심문가로구려!\" 웨믹 씨가 흡족하다는 듯한 표정으로 나를 바라보며 말했다. \"그렇소, 그를 알지요. 내가 그를 안단 말이오!\"\n\n이 말을 내뱉는 그의 어조에는 너그러운 묵인 같기도 하고 깎아내림 같기도 한 기색이 깃들어 있어, 나는 오히려 풀이 죽었다. 그래서 나는 그 본문에 붙은 격려의 주석이라도 찾으려는 듯 여전히 그의 토막 같은 얼굴을 곁눈질하고 있었는데, 그때 그가 여기가 바너드 여관이라고 말했다. 그 통고에도 내 우울함은 가시지 않았다. 나는 그것이 바너드 씨가 운영하는 호텔이며, 우리 고장의 블루 보어 따위는 거기에 비하면 한낱 선술집에 불과하리라고 짐작했던 것이다. 그런데 이제 와 보니 바너드란 형체 없는 영혼이거나 허구의 인물이었고, 그 여관이란 도둑고양이들의 사교 클럽으로나 쓰일 법하게 음습한 구석에 다닥다닥 비좁게 쑤셔 넣은, 이제껏 본 중 가장 칙칙한 누추한 건물들의 집합이었다.\n\n우리는 쪽문을 지나 이 안식처로 들어섰고, 입구의 통로를 거쳐 평평한 묘지처럼 보이는 침울한 작은 광장으로 토해졌다. 거기에는 내가 이제껏 본 가운데 가장 음울한 나무들과, 가장 음울한 참새들과, 가장 음울한 고양이들과, 가장 음울한 집들이(그 수는 여섯 채 남짓이었다) 있는 것 같았다. 그 집들이 나뉘어 이룬 셋방들의 창문은 하나같이, 다 망가진 블라인드와 커튼, 다리 부러진 화분, 금 간 유리, 먼지 앉은 쇠락, 비참한 임시변통의 온갖 단계를 보여 주는 듯했다. 한편 텅 빈 방들에서는 셋방 있음, 셋방 있음, 셋방 있음이라는 글자가 나를 향해 번득였는데, 마치 새로운 가련한 자들이 결코 다시는 그곳에 들지 않을 것이며, 바너드의 영혼이 품은 복수심이 지금 세든 자들의 점진적인 자살과 자갈 밑에 묻히는 불경한 매장으로 서서히 달래지고 있기라도 한 듯했다. 그을음과 매연으로 이루어진 꾀죄죄한 상복이 이 처량한 바너드의 피조물을 휘감고 있었고, 그것은 제 머리에 재를 뿌린 채, 한낱 쓰레기 구덩이로서 속죄와 굴욕을 치르고 있는 셈이었다. 여기까지가 내 시각에 호소해 온 것이었고, 한편으로는 마른 부패와 젖은 부패, 그리고 방치된 지붕과 지하실에서 소리 없이 썩어 가는 온갖 부패 — 게다가 쥐와 생쥐와 벌레의 부패, 그리고 가까이 있는 역마차 마구간의 부패까지 — 가 내 후각에 어렴풋이 말을 걸어 오며 신음했다. \"바너드표 혼합 향수를 써 보시지.\"\n\n내 위대한 유산의 첫 실현이 이토록 형편없었던 까닭에, 나는 낭패스러운 표정으로 웨믹 씨를 바라보았다. \"아!\" 그가 나를 오해하며 말했다. \"이 한적함이 시골을 떠올리게 하나 보군. 나도 그렇소.\"\n\n그는 나를 한쪽 구석으로 이끌어 한 층계 위로 안내했는데 — 그 층계는 내게 천천히 톱밥으로 무너져 내리는 것처럼 보여, 머지않은 어느 날 위층 세입자들이 제 방문 밖을 내다보면 내려올 방도가 사라져 있으리라 싶었다 — 꼭대기 층의 한 셋방으로 데려갔다. 문에는 소(小)포켓 씨라고 칠해져 있었고, 우편함에는 \"곧 돌아옴\"이라는 쪽지가 붙어 있었다.\n\n\"당신이 이렇게 일찍 오리라곤 미처 생각 못 했나 보오.\" 웨믹 씨가 설명했다. \"나한테 더는 볼일이 없겠지요?\"\n\n\"네, 고맙습니다.\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"돈은 내가 관리하니까,\" 웨믹 씨가 말했다. \"우린 아마 꽤 자주 만나게 될 거요. 그럼 안녕히.\"\n\n\"안녕히 가세요.\"\n\n내가 손을 내밀자, 웨믹 씨는 처음엔 내가 무얼 바라기라도 하는 줄 아는 듯 그 손을 바라보았다. 그러더니 나를 보고는, 스스로 고쳐 말했다.\n\n\"아, 그렇지! 그래요. 당신은 악수하는 습관이 있구려?\"\n\n나는 그것이 런던의 풍습에 어긋나는 게 아닐까 싶어 다소 당황했으나, 그렇다고 대답했다.\n\n\"나는 그 습관에서 어찌나 멀어졌던지!\" 웨믹 씨가 말했다. \"마지막 순간이 아니고서는 말이오. 알게 되어 정말 반갑소이다. 그럼 안녕히!\"\n\n우리가 악수를 나누고 그가 떠난 뒤, 나는 층계 창문을 열었다가 하마터면 내 목이 잘릴 뻔했다. 창틀 줄이 다 삭아 버려, 창문이 단두대처럼 떨어져 내렸던 것이다. 다행히도 워낙 순식간이라 미처 머리를 내밀지 않은 참이었다. 이 위기를 모면한 뒤, 나는 창문에 더께처럼 낀 때 너머로 여관을 안개 낀 듯 흐릿하게 바라보는 것으로 만족하며, 런던은 정녕 과대평가된 곳이라고 혼잣말을 하며 시무룩하게 서서 밖을 내다보았다.\n\n소포켓 씨가 생각하는 \"곧\"은 내가 생각하는 \"곧\"이 아니었다. 나는 반 시간이나 밖을 내다보며 거의 미칠 지경이 되었고, 창문의 모든 유리에 낀 때 위에 손가락으로 내 이름을 몇 번이나 써 본 뒤에야 비로소 층계에서 발소리를 들었으니까. 차츰 내 앞에 모습을 드러낸 것은, 나와 엇비슷한 처지인 한 사교계 일원의 모자와 머리, 목도리, 조끼, 바지, 장화였다. 그는 양쪽 겨드랑이에 종이봉투를 하나씩 끼고 한 손에는 딸기 한 바구니를 든 채 숨을 헐떡이고 있었다.\n\n\"핍 씨인가요?\" 그가 말했다.\n\n\"포켓 씨인가요?\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"이런!\" 그가 소리쳤다. \"정말 죄송합니다. 다만 당신 고향 쪽에서 정오에 떠나는 마차가 있다는 걸 알고 있어서, 그걸 타고 오시리라 생각했지 뭡니까. 실은 당신을 위해 나가 있었던 겁니다 — 그렇다고 변명이 되는 건 아니지만 — 시골에서 오셨으니 저녁 식사 후에 과일을 좀 드시고 싶을지도 모른다는 생각이 들어, 좋은 걸로 구하려고 코번트 가든 시장에 다녀온 거지요.\"\n\n나로서는 그럴 만한 까닭이 있어, 마치 두 눈이 머리에서 튀어나올 것만 같았다. 나는 두서없이 그의 배려에 감사를 표하면서, 이게 꿈이 아닌가 생각하기 시작했다.\n\n\"이런!\" 소포켓 씨가 말했다. \"이 문이 어찌나 뻑뻑한지!\"\n\n그가 양 겨드랑이에 종이봉투를 낀 채 문과 씨름하느라 제 과일을 빠르게 잼으로 만들고 있는 통에, 나는 그 봉투들을 내가 들어 주게 해 달라고 청했다. 그는 상냥한 미소를 지으며 봉투를 넘겨주고는, 문을 마치 야수라도 되는 양 상대로 싸웠다. 마침내 문이 어찌나 갑작스레 열렸던지, 그는 내 쪽으로 비틀거리며 넘어졌고, 나는 또 맞은편 문 쪽으로 비틀거리며 넘어졌으며, 우리 둘 다 웃음을 터뜨렸다. 그러나 나는 여전히 두 눈이 머리에서 튀어나올 것만 같았고, 이게 꿈만 같았다.\n\n\"자, 들어오십시오.\" 소포켓 씨가 말했다. \"제가 앞장서지요. 이곳이 좀 휑하긴 합니다만, 월요일까지는 그럭저럭 견디실 수 있으리라 봅니다. 제 아버지께서는 당신이 내일 하루는 아버지보다 저와 함께 보내는 편이 더 즐거울 거라고, 또 런던 구경을 다니고 싶어 할지도 모른다고 생각하셨거든요. 저야 당신께 런던을 보여 드릴 수 있다면 더없이 기쁘지요. 우리 식탁으로 말하자면, 그리 나쁘진 않으리라 봅니다. 여기 우리 커피점에서 들여올 테니까요. 그리고 (이건 덧붙여 말해 두는 게 옳겠습니다만) 비용은 당신 부담이지요. 재거스 씨의 지시가 그러하니 말입니다. 우리 숙소로 말하자면 결코 으리으리하다고는 할 수 없습니다. 저는 제 빵벌이를 제 손으로 해야 하고, 아버지께선 제게 주실 게 아무것도 없으며, 설령 있다 한들 저는 받고 싶지도 않으니까요. 여기가 우리 거실입니다 — 보시다시피 의자며 탁자며 양탄자 따위가, 집에서 떼어 줄 수 있을 만한 것들로 갖춰져 있지요. 식탁보와 숟가락과 양념병만큼은 제 공으로 치지 마십시오. 그것들은 당신을 위해 커피점에서 오는 것이니까요. 여기가 제 작은 침실입니다. 좀 퀴퀴하지요, 하지만 바너드 여관이라는 데가 본디 퀴퀴하니까요. 여기가 당신 침실입니다. 가구는 이번 일을 위해 빌린 것이지만, 쓰임새에는 맞으리라 믿습니다. 혹 필요한 게 있으면 제가 가서 가져오지요. 이 셋방은 한적해서 우리 둘만 함께 지내게 될 텐데, 다투지는 않겠지요, 아마. 아, 이런, 실례했습니다. 당신이 여태 과일을 들고 계셨군요. 부디 그 봉투들을 제가 받게 해 주십시오. 정말 부끄럽습니다.\"\n\n내가 소포켓 씨와 마주 서서 그에게 봉투를 하나, 둘 건네는 동안, 나는 내 눈에 떠올라 있는 줄로 알고 있던 그 튀어나올 듯한 기색이 그의 눈에도 떠오르는 것을 보았고, 그는 뒤로 물러서며 말했다.\n\n\"맙소사, 당신은 그때 그 어슬렁거리던 녀석이로군요!\"\n\n\"그리고 당신은,\" 내가 말했다. \"그 창백한 젊은 신사고요!\"\n\n# Chapter XXII.\n\n창백한 얼굴의 젊은 신사와 나는 바너드 여관에서 서로를 마주 바라보며 서 있다가, 마침내 둘 다 웃음을 터뜨리고 말았다. \"그게 너였다니!\" 그가 말했다. \"그게 바로 너였다니!\" 내가 말했다. 그러고는 우리는 다시 서로를 바라보았고, 또 한 번 웃었다. \"자!\" 창백한 얼굴의 젊은 신사가 사람 좋게 손을 내밀며 말했다. \"이제 다 끝난 일이겠지, 그러길 바라네. 내가 자네를 그렇게 마구 두들겨 팼던 걸 용서해 준다면 그건 자네가 아주 너그러운 사람이라는 증거일 거야.\"\n\n이 말로 미루어 나는 허버트 포켓 씨가(허버트란 창백한 얼굴의 젊은 신사 이름이었다) 여전히 자기 의도와 실행을 다소 혼동하고 있음을 알아차렸다. 그러나 나는 겸손하게 대답했고, 우리는 따뜻하게 악수를 나누었다.\n\n\"그때는 자네 행운이 아직 굴러들어오지 않았을 때였지?\" 허버트 포켓이 말했다.\n\n\"그래.\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"맞아.\" 그가 수긍했다. \"아주 최근에 일어난 일이라고 들었네. 나야말로 그 무렵 행운을 좀 노리고 있던 참이었지.\"\n\n\"그래?\"\n\n\"그래. 해비셤 양이 나를 마음에 들어 할 수 있을지 보겠다고 나를 부른 거였어. 하지만 그분은 그러질 못했지 — 적어도, 그러지 않았어.\"\n\n나는 그 말을 들으니 놀랍다고 한마디 하는 게 예의겠다 싶었다.\n\n\"안목이 형편없는 거지.\" 허버트가 웃으며 말했다. \"하지만 사실이 그래. 그래, 그분은 나를 시험 삼아 한번 방문하라고 불렀던 거고, 만약 내가 그걸 무사히 통과했더라면 아마 살길이 마련됐을 거야. 어쩌면 에스텔라하고 그 뭐시기가 됐을지도 모르지.\"\n\n\"그게 뭔데?\" 나는 갑자기 진지해져서 물었다.\n\n그는 우리가 이야기하는 동안 과일을 접시에 담고 있었는데, 그 일에 정신이 팔린 탓에 그렇게 말을 더듬은 것이었다. \"약혼한 사이 말이야.\" 그는 여전히 과일을 매만지며 설명했다. \"정혼한 사이. 혼약을 맺은 사이. 그 뭐시기. 뭐 그런 종류의 단어 말이야.\"\n\n\"그 실망을 어떻게 견뎠나?\" 내가 물었다.\n\n\"흥!\" 그가 말했다. \"별로 신경 쓰지 않았어. 그 여자는 사나운 말괄량이거든.\"\n\n\"해비셤 양이?\"\n\n\"그것도 아니라곤 안 하겠네만, 내가 말한 건 에스텔라야. 그 아이는 더할 나위 없이 냉정하고 오만하고 변덕스러운데, 해비셤 양이 모든 남성에게 복수를 퍼붓도록 키워 놓은 거지.\"\n\n\"그 아이가 해비셤 양하고는 어떤 관계인데?\"\n\n\"아무 관계도 아니야.\" 그가 말했다. \"그저 양녀일 뿐이지.\"\n\n\"왜 모든 남성에게 복수를 퍼부어야 하는데? 무슨 복수?\"\n\n\"아니, 핍 씨!\" 그가 말했다. \"자네 모르나?\"\n\n\"몰라.\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"이런! 그건 제법 긴 이야기라, 저녁 식사 때까지 아껴 두기로 하지. 그건 그렇고, 이제 내가 외람되게 한 가지 물어봐도 되겠나. 그날 자네는 어쩌다 거기 오게 됐나?\"\n\n나는 그에게 사정을 이야기했고, 그는 내가 다 마칠 때까지 귀 기울여 듣더니, 다시 웃음을 터뜨리며 그 후로 아프지 않았느냐고 물었다. 나는 그에게 너야말로 아프지 않았느냐고 묻지 않았는데, 그 점에 관해서라면 내 확신이 더없이 확고하게 서 있었기 때문이다.\n\n\"재거스 씨가 자네 후견인이라고 알고 있네만?\" 그가 말을 이었다.\n\n\"그래.\"\n\n\"그분이 해비셤 양의 사무 대리인이자 변호사이고, 그 누구도 받지 못하는 그분의 신임을 받고 있다는 건 알지?\"\n\n이건 나를(나는 그렇게 느꼈다) 위험한 영역으로 끌어가는 것이었다. 나는 굳이 감추려 들지 않은 어색함을 드러내며 대답하기를, 우리가 한판 붙던 바로 그날 해비셤 양의 집에서 재거스 씨를 본 적은 있으나 그 밖의 어떤 때에도 본 적이 없으며, 그분은 거기서 나를 봤다는 사실을 전혀 기억하지 못하리라고 믿는다고 했다.\n\n\"그분이 친절을 베풀어 우리 아버지를 자네 가정교사로 추천했고, 그걸 제안하려고 우리 아버지를 찾아오신 거야. 물론 그분은 해비셤 양과의 인연을 통해 우리 아버지에 대해 알고 있었지. 우리 아버지는 해비셤 양의 사촌이야. 그렇다고 두 분 사이에 허물없는 왕래가 있다는 건 아니야. 아버지는 비위를 맞추는 데 서투른 분이라 그분의 환심을 사려 들지 않거든.\"\n\n허버트 포켓에게는 솔직하고 편안한 태도가 있어, 그것이 무척 호감을 자아냈다. 그 무렵에도, 또 그 후로도, 나는 그처럼 모든 눈빛과 말투에서 무언가 은밀하고 비열한 짓이라곤 천성적으로 할 줄 모른다는 것을 내게 그토록 강하게 드러내 보인 사람을 본 적이 없다. 그의 전반적인 분위기에는 놀랍도록 희망에 찬 무언가가 있었으나, 동시에 그가 결코 크게 성공하거나 부유해지지는 못하리라고 내게 속삭이는 무언가도 있었다. 어찌하여 그러했는지는 모르겠다. 나는 그 첫 만남에서, 저녁 식사 자리에 앉기도 전에 그런 생각에 젖어들었으나, 무엇 때문에 그랬는지는 딱히 짚어 말할 수가 없다.\n\n그는 여전히 창백한 얼굴의 젊은 신사였고, 활기와 민첩함 한가운데서도 어딘가 패배에 길든 듯한 나른함을 지니고 있었는데, 그것이 타고난 강건함을 드러내 보이는 것 같지는 않았다. 잘생긴 얼굴은 아니었으나, 잘생긴 것보다 나았다. 더없이 상냥하고 쾌활한 얼굴이었으니까. 그의 몸매는, 내 주먹이 그토록 함부로 굴었던 그 시절과 마찬가지로 조금 볼품없었으나, 언제까지나 가뿐하고 젊어 보일 것만 같았다. 트래브 씨의 시골 솜씨로 지은 옷이 나보다 그에게 더 우아하게 어울렸을지는 의문이지만, 그가 다소 낡은 옷을 입고도 내가 새 양복을 입은 것보다 훨씬 더 멋스럽게 소화해 냈다는 것만은 내가 인정하는 바이다.\n\n그가 그토록 거리낌 없이 털어놓는 터라, 나는 내 쪽에서 입을 닫고 있는 것이 우리 나이에 어울리지 않는 못된 보답이 되겠다 싶었다. 그래서 나는 그에게 내 변변찮은 사연을 들려주었고, 내 은인이 누구인지 캐묻는 것이 금지되어 있다는 점을 강조했다. 나아가 나는 시골에서 대장장이로 자랐기에 예의범절에 대해 아는 것이 거의 없으니, 내가 어쩔 줄 모르거나 잘못하는 것을 볼 때마다 그가 귀띔을 해 준다면 그것을 큰 친절로 받아들이겠다고 덧붙였다.\n\n\"기꺼이 그러지.\" 그가 말했다. \"하지만 자네에게 귀띔이 필요할 일은 거의 없을 거라고 감히 예언하네. 우리는 앞으로 자주 함께 지내게 될 테고, 나는 우리 사이에 쓸데없는 거북함은 다 없애 버리고 싶네. 부디 지금 당장 나를 세례명인 허버트로 불러 주는 호의를 베풀어 주겠나?\"\n\n나는 그에게 고맙다고 하며 그러겠다고 했다. 그 답례로 나는 내 세례명이 필립이라고 알려 주었다.\n\n\"필립은 마음에 안 드는데.\" 그가 미소 지으며 말했다. \"그건 철자 교본에 나오는 무슨 교훈조의 사내아이 이름 같단 말이야. 너무 게을러서 연못에 빠졌다든가, 너무 뚱뚱해서 제 눈으로 앞을 못 봤다든가, 너무 욕심이 많아서 케이크를 쥐가 다 갉아먹을 때까지 잠가 두었다든가, 새 둥지를 털겠다고 어찌나 작정을 했던지 마침 근처에 살던 곰들한테 잡아먹혔다든가 하는 그런 아이 말이지. 내가 어떻게 부르고 싶은지 말해 줄게. 우리는 이렇게 잘 어울리고, 자네는 대장장이였잖아 — 괜찮겠나?\"\n\n\"자네가 제안하는 거라면 뭐든 상관없네.\" 내가 대답했다. \"하지만 무슨 말인지 모르겠는데.\"\n\n\"헨델이라고 친근하게 부르면 싫겠나? 헨델이 작곡한 멋진 곡 중에 「쾌활한 대장장이」라는 게 있거든.\"\n\n\"아주 좋네.\"\n\n\"그럼, 친애하는 헨델,\" 그가 문이 열리자 몸을 돌리며 말했다. \"여기 저녁 식사가 왔네. 그리고 자네에게 상석에 앉아 주기를 청해야겠어. 이 식사는 자네가 마련한 것이니까.\"\n\n이건 내가 도무지 들어줄 수 없는 일이었으므로, 그가 상석에 앉고 내가 그 맞은편에 앉았다. 그것은 자그마한 멋진 식사였는데 — 그때 내게는 마치 런던 시장의 연회처럼 여겨졌다 — 곁에 노인네 하나 없고 사방이 온통 런던인 그런 자유로운 상황에서 먹는다는 사실 덕분에 한층 더 맛깔스럽게 느껴졌다. 게다가 그 잔치를 돋보이게 하는 어딘가 집시 같은 정취가 그 맛을 더욱 북돋아 주었다. 식탁은, 펌블추크 씨라면 호사의 극치라고 했을 법하게 — 온통 커피점에서 가져온 것들로 차려졌으나 — 그 둘레의 거실 영역은 비교적 황량하고 안정감 없는 분위기였던 까닭에, 시중드는 사람에게 이리저리 떠도는 버릇을 강요했으니, 그는 접시 덮개를 바닥에 놓고(그러다 거기에 걸려 넘어지고), 녹인 버터를 안락의자에 놓고, 빵을 책장에 놓고, 치즈를 석탄통에 놓고, 삶은 닭고기를 옆방 내 침대에 놓아두었던 것이다 — 밤에 잠자리에 들 때 보니 거기 곁들여 둔 파슬리와 버터가 상당히 굳어 있는 것을 발견했다. 이 모든 것이 그 잔치를 즐겁게 만들었고, 시중꾼이 나를 지켜보러 곁에 있지 않을 때면 내 즐거움은 한 점 흠도 없이 온전했다.\n\n저녁 식사가 어지간히 진행되었을 무렵, 나는 허버트에게 해비셤 양에 관해 이야기해 주기로 한 약속을 일깨워 주었다.\n\n\"맞아.\" 그가 대꾸했다. \"당장 그 약속을 지키지. 헨델, 이 화제를 꺼내기에 앞서 한마디 하자면, 런던에서는 칼을 입에 넣지 않는 게 관습이야 — 사고가 날까 봐 그러는 거지 — 그리고 그런 용도로는 포크가 쓰이지만, 그것도 필요 이상으로 깊이 넣지는 않아. 입에 담을 가치도 없는 얘기지만, 그래도 남들 하는 대로 하는 게 좋으니까. 또, 숟가락은 보통 손등을 위로 해서 쓰지 않고 아래로 해서 써. 거기엔 두 가지 이점이 있지. 입에 더 잘 가져갈 수 있고(어쨌든 그게 목적이니까), 게다가 오른쪽 팔꿈치가 마치 굴 껍데기를 까는 듯한 자세를 취하는 일을 상당히 덜 수 있거든.\"\n\n그가 이런 다정한 충고를 어찌나 활기차게 건넸던지, 우리는 둘 다 웃었고 나는 거의 얼굴을 붉히지도 않았다.\n\n\"자 그럼,\" 그가 말을 이었다. \"해비셤 양에 관한 얘긴데. 알아 둬야 할 건, 해비셤 양이 응석받이로 자란 아이였다는 거야. 그분 어머니는 그분이 갓난아기일 때 돌아가셨고, 아버지는 그분에게 무엇 하나 거절하는 법이 없었지. 그분 아버지는 자네 고향 쪽의 시골 신사였는데, 양조업자였어. 어째서 양조업자가 대단한 직업인지는 모르겠네만, 빵을 구우면서 도저히 점잖은 신사일 수 없는 반면, 술을 빚으면서는 더없이 점잖은 신사일 수 있다는 건 반박할 수 없는 사실이지. 그런 건 날마다 보는 일이잖나.\"\n\n\"하지만 신사가 선술집을 운영해서는 안 되겠지, 안 그런가?\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"무슨 일이 있어도 안 되지.\" 허버트가 대꾸했다. \"하지만 선술집이 신사를 먹여 살릴 수는 있어. 자! 해비셤 씨는 무척 부유했고 무척 거만했지. 그 딸도 마찬가지였고.\"\n\n\"해비셤 양은 외동딸이었나?\" 내가 조심스레 물어보았다.\n\n\"잠깐만, 이제 그 얘기로 가는 참이야. 아니, 외동딸은 아니었어. 그분에겐 배다른 오라비가 있었지. 아버지가 몰래 재혼을 했거든 — 자기 집 요리사하고, 아마 그랬던 것 같아.\"\n\n\"거만한 사람이었다며.\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"이보게 헨델, 그렇긴 했지. 거만했기 때문에 두 번째 아내를 몰래 맞아들인 거고, 세월이 흘러 그 여자도 죽었어. 그 여자가 죽고 나서야, 내 짐작에 그분은 비로소 딸에게 자기가 한 일을 털어놓았고, 그러고서 그 아들이 가족의 일원이 되어, 자네도 잘 아는 그 집에 살게 된 거지. 그 아들은 자라서 청년이 되자, 난폭하고 방탕하고 불효막심한 — 한마디로 못된 인간이 되어 버렸어. 결국 아버지는 그를 상속에서 폐했지. 하지만 죽을 때가 되니 마음이 누그러져서, 해비셤 양만큼은 도저히 못 되어도 그런대로 넉넉한 살림은 물려주었어 — 포도주 한 잔 더 들게나. 그리고 한마디 덧붙이는 걸 양해해 주게. 사교계라는 집단은, 잔을 비우는 데 있어 잔 테두리를 콧등에 댄 채 바닥이 위로 가도록 거꾸로 뒤집을 만큼 그렇게까지 철저히 양심적이기를 바라지는 않는다네.\"\n\n나는 그의 이야기에 지나치게 정신이 팔린 나머지 바로 그 짓을 하고 있던 참이었다. 나는 그에게 고맙다고 하며 사과했다. 그는 \"천만에\"라고 하고는 이야기를 다시 이어 갔다.\n\n\"이제 해비셤 양은 상속녀가 되었고, 자네도 짐작하다시피 대단한 혼처감으로 눈독을 받게 됐지. 그분의 배다른 오라비는 이제 다시 넉넉한 재산을 손에 넣었으나, 빚이며 새로운 광기며 하는 통에 그것을 또다시 무섭도록 탕진해 버렸어. 그와 그분 사이에는, 예전에 그와 아버지 사이에 있던 것보다 더 심한 불화가 있었고, 아버지의 노여움을 부추긴 것이 그분이었다고 여겨 그가 그분에게 깊고 치명적인 원한을 품었으리라고 짐작되고 있지. 자, 이제 이야기의 잔혹한 대목으로 들어가네 — 다만, 친애하는 헨델, 잠깐 멈추고 한마디 하자면, 식탁용 냅킨은 텀블러 잔 속에 들어가지 않는다는 거야.\"\n\n내가 어째서 내 냅킨을 텀블러 잔 속에 쑤셔 넣으려 하고 있었는지는 나로서도 도무지 설명할 수가 없다. 다만 내가, 훨씬 더 가치 있는 일에나 어울릴 끈기로, 그것을 그 좁은 한계 안에 욱여넣으려고 더없이 안간힘을 쓰고 있는 나 자신을 발견했다는 것만은 안다. 나는 다시 그에게 고맙다고 하며 사과했고, 그는 다시금 더없이 쾌활한 태도로 \"천만에, 정말이야!\"라고 하고는 이야기를 이어 갔다.\n\n\"무대 위에 한 남자가 나타났어 — 경마장이라 해도 좋고, 공개 무도회라 해도 좋고, 아무 데나 자네 좋을 대로 생각하게 — 해비셤 양에게 구애한 남자였지. 나는 그자를 본 적이 없지만(이건 스물다섯 해 전, 자네도 나도 태어나기 전에 일어난 일이니까, 헨델), 우리 아버지 말씀으로는 그가 겉만 번지르르한 남자였고, 그런 일에 딱 어울리는 부류의 사내였다더군. 하지만 무지나 편견에 빠지지 않고서야 그를 신사로 잘못 알아볼 수는 없었으리라고, 아버지는 아주 단호하게 단언하셔. 마음속으로 진정한 신사가 아닌 자가, 천지가 개벽한 이래 태도에서 진정한 신사였던 적은 한 번도 없다는 게 아버지의 신조거든. 아버지 말씀이, 그 어떤 칠도 나뭇결을 감출 수는 없고, 칠을 덧바르면 덧바를수록 나뭇결은 더욱더 제 모습을 드러낸다는 거야. 자! 이 남자는 해비셤 양을 바짝 따라다니며 그분에게 헌신하는 척했지. 내가 알기로 그분은 그때까지 별다른 감수성을 내보인 적이 없었으나, 그분이 지닌 감수성이란 감수성은 분명 그때 죄다 쏟아져 나왔고, 그분은 그를 열렬히 사랑했어. 그분이 그를 완전히 우상처럼 떠받들었다는 건 의심할 여지가 없지. 그자는 그분의 애정을 그토록 계획적으로 이용해 먹어서, 그분에게서 거액의 돈을 뜯어냈고, 그분이 양조장에서 오라비의 지분을(아버지가 어리석게도 그 오라비에게 남겨 준 것이었지) 엄청난 값을 치르고 사들이도록 부추겼는데, 자기가 그분의 남편이 되면 그 모든 걸 자기가 쥐고 관리해야 한다는 구실을 댔던 거야. 그 무렵 자네 후견인은 해비셤 양의 자문을 맡고 있지 않았고, 그분은 너무 도도하고 너무 사랑에 빠진 나머지 그 누구의 조언도 들으려 하지 않았어. 그분의 친척들은 우리 아버지만 빼고는 죄다 가난하고 잇속만 챙기는 자들이었지. 아버지도 가난하기는 매한가지였으나, 권세에 빌붙거나 시샘하는 분은 아니었어. 그들 가운데 유일하게 줏대 있는 분이었던 아버지는, 그분이 이 남자를 위해 너무 많은 걸 하고 있고, 너무 거리낌 없이 제 몸을 그자의 손아귀에 내맡기고 있다고 경고했지. 그러자 그분은 첫 기회를 잡자마자, 그 정혼자가 보는 앞에서, 노발대발하며 우리 아버지를 집에서 내쫓아 버렸고, 그 후로 아버지는 그분을 다시는 뵌 적이 없어.\"\n\n나는 그분이 \"내가 저 식탁 위에 시신으로 누워 있을 때에야 매슈가 마침내 나를 보러 올 거야\"라고 했던 말을 떠올리고는, 허버트에게 그의 아버지가 그분에게 그토록 깊은 앙심을 품고 있는 거냐고 물었다.\n\n\"그건 아니야.\" 그가 말했다. \"다만 그분이, 그 정혼자가 보는 앞에서, 아버지가 제 출세를 위해 자기에게 아첨할 속셈이었다가 그게 어긋나니 실망한 거라고 몰아세운 거지. 그러니 만약 이제 와서 아버지가 그분을 찾아간다면, 그게 사실인 것처럼 보일 거야 — 아버지 자신에게도, 또 그분에게도 말이지. 자, 다시 그 남자 얘기로 돌아가서 그자의 결말을 짓자고. 결혼식 날이 정해졌고, 웨딩드레스도 사 두었고, 신혼여행 계획도 다 짜 두었고, 하객들도 초대했어. 그날이 왔지만, 신랑은 오지 않았어. 그자는 그분에게 편지 한 통을 써 보냈는데 —\"\n\n\"그분이 그 편지를 받은 게,\" 내가 끼어들었다. \"결혼식 차림을 하고 있을 때였지? 아홉 시 이십 분 전에?\"\n\n\"바로 그 시각, 그 분에.\" 허버트가 고개를 끄덕이며 말했다. \"그분이 나중에 모든 시계를 멈춰 세운 그 시각 말이지. 그 편지에 그 결혼을 더없이 매정하게 깨뜨려 버린 것 말고 또 무슨 말이 적혀 있었는지는, 나도 모르니까 자네에게 말해 줄 수가 없네. 그분은 그때 앓은 큰 병에서 회복되자, 자네가 본 그대로 그 집을 온통 폐허로 만들어 버렸고, 그 후로는 한 번도 햇빛을 본 적이 없어.\"\n\n\"그게 이야기의 전부인가?\" 곰곰이 생각해 본 뒤 내가 물었다.\n\n\"내가 아는 건 그게 전부야. 사실 이만큼이라도 아는 것도, 내가 혼자 이리저리 짜 맞춰 본 덕분이지. 아버지는 늘 그 얘기를 피하시고, 해비셤 양이 나를 거기 가도록 초대했을 때조차, 내가 꼭 알아 둬야 할 만큼만 일러 주셨을 뿐 그 이상은 말씀하지 않으셨거든. 그런데 한 가지 잊은 게 있군. 그분이 잘못 믿어 버린 그 남자가 처음부터 끝까지 그분의 배다른 오라비와 짜고 행동했다고 — 그러니까 둘 사이의 음모였고, 그 이득을 나눠 가졌다고 추정되고 있어.\"\n\n\"그자가 그분과 결혼해서 재산을 몽땅 차지하지 않은 게 이상한데.\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"그자는 이미 결혼한 몸이었을지도 모르고, 그분이 당한 그 잔인한 굴욕이 배다른 오라비 계략의 일부였을 수도 있어.\" 허버트가 말했다. \"명심하게! 나도 그건 모르는 일이야.\"\n\n\"그 두 남자는 어떻게 됐나?\" 다시금 그 문제를 곱씹어 본 뒤 내가 물었다.\n\n\"더 깊은 수치와 타락에 — 더 깊은 게 있을 수 있다면 말이지 — 그리고 파멸에 빠졌지.\"\n\n\"지금도 살아 있나?\"\n\n\"모르겠어.\"\n\n\"자네 방금 에스텔라가 해비셤 양과 혈연관계가 아니라 양녀라고 했지. 언제 입양됐나?\"\n\n허버트가 어깨를 으쓱했다. \"내가 해비셤 양이라는 분에 대해 들어 온 이래로, 에스텔라는 늘 있었어. 그 이상은 나도 몰라. 자, 그럼 헨델,\" 그가 이쯤에서 그 이야기를 마치 훌훌 털어 버리듯 말했다. \"이제 우리 사이엔 완전히 터놓은 양해가 선 셈이야. 내가 해비셤 양에 대해 아는 건 다 자네도 알고 있는 거지.\"\n\n\"그리고 내가 아는 것도 모두,\" 내가 응수했다. \"자네가 알고 있는 거지.\"\n\n\"전적으로 그렇게 믿네. 그러니 자네와 나 사이엔 경쟁이나 곤혹스러움이 있을 수 없어. 그리고 자네가 인생에서 출세를 거머쥐게 된 그 조건, 즉 자네가 누구 덕분에 그렇게 됐는지 캐묻거나 입에 올려서는 안 된다는 그 조건에 관해서라면, 나나 내게 속한 그 누구도 결코 그 영역을 침범하거나 심지어 가까이 다가가는 일조차 없으리라고 자네는 아주 확신해도 좋네.\"\n\n사실 그가 어찌나 섬세하게 이 말을 했던지, 비록 내가 앞으로 여러 해 동안 그의 아버지 지붕 아래 머물게 된다 해도 이 화제만큼은 이미 끝나 버린 것이라는 느낌이 들었다. 그러면서도 그는 또한 어찌나 의미심장하게 그 말을 했던지, 그가, 내가 그 사실을 알고 있는 것과 똑같이, 해비셤 양이 내 은인이라는 것을 완벽하게 알고 있다는 느낌이 들었다.\n\n그가 이 화제를 우리 사이에서 깨끗이 치워 버리려는 목적으로 거기까지 끌고 왔다는 생각은 전에는 미처 들지 않았는데, 그것을 터놓고 나니 우리는 한결 가뿐하고 편안해진 터라, 나는 이제 그게 사실임을 알아차렸다. 우리는 무척 유쾌하고 정답게 어울렸고, 나는 이야기를 나누던 중에 그에게 무슨 일을 하느냐고 물었다. 그는 \"자본가야 — 선박 보험업자지\"라고 대답했다. 내가 선박이나 자본의 흔적이라도 찾으려고 방 안을 두리번거리는 것을 봤던지, 그는 \"시티에서 말이야\"라고 덧붙였다.\n* 시티: 런던의 금융 중심가.\n\n나는 시티의 선박 보험업자들이 누리는 부와 위세에 대해 거창한 생각을 품고 있던 터라, 내가 어린 보험업자 하나를 벌렁 나자빠뜨리고, 진취적인 그의 눈을 멍들게 하고, 막중한 책임을 진 그의 머리통을 째 놓았다는 사실을 떠올리고는 외경심마저 일기 시작했다. 그러나 다시금, 다행스럽게도, 허버트 포켓이 결코 크게 성공하거나 부유해지지는 못하리라는 그 묘한 인상이 내게 찾아들었다.\n\n\"나는 그저 내 자본을 선박 보험에 쓰는 것만으로 만족하고 주저앉진 않을 거야. 괜찮은 생명 보험 주식을 좀 사들여서 이사진에 끼어들 거지. 광산 쪽에서도 조금 손을 댈 거고. 이런 일들 가운데 어느 것도 내가 직접 몇천 톤짜리 배를 빌리는 데 방해가 되진 않을 거야. 내 생각엔,\" 그가 의자에 등을 기대며 말했다. \"동인도와 교역을 할 것 같아. 비단, 숄, 향신료, 염료, 약재, 그리고 진귀한 목재를 들여오는 거지. 흥미로운 무역이거든.\"\n\n\"그래서 이윤은 큰가?\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"엄청나지!\" 그가 말했다.\n\n나는 다시 흔들렸고, 여기에 내 것보다 더 큰 유산에의 기대가 있는 게 아닌가 하는 생각이 들기 시작했다.\n\n\"그리고 또 내 생각엔,\" 그가 조끼 주머니에 양 엄지손가락을 찔러 넣으며 말했다. \"서인도와도 교역할 것 같아. 설탕, 담배, 럼주를 들여오는 거지. 실론하고도, 특히 코끼리 상아를 들여오려고 말이야.\"\n\n\"배가 꽤 많이 필요하겠는데.\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"완전한 선단(船團)이 필요하지.\" 그가 말했다.\n\n이런 거래들의 웅장함에 완전히 압도되어, 나는 그에게 그가 보험을 든 배들이 지금은 주로 어디와 교역을 하느냐고 물었다.\n\n\"아직 보험 일을 시작하진 않았어.\" 그가 대답했다. \"나는 주위를 살피고 있는 중이야.\"\n\n어쩐지 그 일이야말로 바너드 여관에 더 어울리는 것 같았다. 나는 (납득했다는 투로) \"아하!\" 하고 말했다.\n\n\"그래. 나는 회계 사무소에 들어가 있고, 주위를 살피고 있는 중이지.\"\n\n\"회계 사무소는 돈벌이가 되나?\" 내가 물었다.\n\n\"누구한테 — 그러니까 거기 들어가 있는 젊은 친구한테 말인가?\" 그가 되물었다.\n\n\"그래, 자네한테 말이야.\"\n\n\"글쎄, 아—니, 나한테는 안 돼.\" 그는 마치 신중하게 셈을 따져 수지를 맞춰 보는 사람 같은 태도로 이렇게 말했다. \"직접적으로 돈벌이가 되진 않아. 그러니까, 거기서 내게 한 푼도 주는 게 없고, 나는 — 내 앞가림을 직접 해야 하지.\"\n\n이건 분명 돈벌이가 되는 모양새는 아니었으므로, 나는 그런 수입원에서 쌓아 둘 만한 자본을 많이 모으기란 어려운 일이겠다는 뜻을 내비치기라도 하듯 고개를 가로저었다.\n\n\"하지만 중요한 건 말이야,\" 허버트 포켓이 말했다. \"자네가 주위를 살핀다는 거지. 바로 그게 대단한 일이거든. 알다시피 자네는 회계 사무소에 들어가 있고, 그러면서 주위를 살피는 거야.\"\n\n회계 사무소 밖에 나와 있으면 주위를 살필 수 없다는 게 묘한 함의로 다가왔지만, 나는 잠자코 그의 경험에 따르기로 했다.\n\n\"그러다가 때가 오는 거지,\" 허버트가 말했다. \"자네에게 활로가 보이는 그때가 말이야. 그러면 자네는 뛰어들어서, 그걸 덮쳐 거머쥐고, 자본을 만드는 거고, 그러면 자네는 떡하니 자리 잡는 거지! 일단 자본을 한번 만들고 나면, 그걸 굴리기만 하면 되는 거야.\"\n\n이건 그가 정원에서 그 한판 대결을 치르던 방식과 매우 흡사했다. 아주 흡사했다. 그가 제 가난을 견뎌 내는 태도 또한, 그가 그 패배를 견뎌 내던 태도와 정확히 일치했다. 내가 보기에 그는 지금 온갖 매질과 타격을, 그때 내 주먹질을 받아들이던 것과 꼭 같은 태도로 받아들이고 있었다. 그의 둘레엔 더없이 단출한 필수품 외엔 아무것도 없는 것이 분명했으니, 내가 무언가를 두고 한마디 할 때마다 그것은 죄다 나를 위해 커피점이나 다른 어디에선가 들여온 것으로 드러났던 것이다.\n\n그러면서도 그는 이미 제 머릿속으로는 한밑천 잡아 놓고서도 그 일에 어찌나 잘난 척이 없던지, 나는 그가 우쭐대지 않는 것이 도리어 고마울 지경이었다. 그것은 그의 천성적으로 유쾌한 태도에 보태진 흐뭇한 덤이었고, 우리는 더없이 잘 어울렸다. 저녁에 우리는 거리로 산책을 나갔고, 반값을 내고 극장에 들어갔다. 그리고 이튿날 우리는 웨스트민스터 사원에 예배를 보러 갔고, 오후에는 공원들을 거닐었다. 나는 거기 있는 그 많은 말들의 편자를 누가 다 박아 줄까 궁금했고, 그게 조였으면 싶었다.\n\n어림잡아 헤아려 보아도, 그 일요일은 내가 조와 비디를 떠나온 지 여러 달이나 지난 듯했다. 나와 그들 사이에 놓인 거리도 그렇게 부풀어 올라, 우리 고향의 습지는 아득히 멀게만 느껴졌다. 바로 직전의 일요일, 그러니까 여태껏 있었던 일요일 중 가장 최근의 그 일요일에 내가 옛 교회 나들이옷을 입고 우리 옛 교회에 있었다는 사실은, 지리적으로나 사회적으로나, 또 해와 달로 따져 봐도, 도무지 있을 수 없는 일들이 한데 뒤엉킨 것처럼만 여겨졌다. 그런데도, 사람들로 그토록 북적이고 저녁 어스름 속에 그토록 휘황하게 불 밝힌 런던 거리에는, 내가 고향의 그 가엾고 정든 부엌을 그토록 멀리 밀어내 버린 데 대한 우울한 질책의 기색이 어려 있었다. 그리고 깊은 밤이면, 바너드 여관을 지킨답시고 그 언저리를 빈둥빈둥 거니는 어느 무능한 가짜 수위의 발소리가 내 가슴에 공허하게 울려 퍼졌다.\n\n월요일 아침 아홉 시 십오 분 전에, 허버트는 출근을 알리려고 — 내 짐작엔 주위를 살피러 가는 것이기도 하겠지만 — 회계 사무소로 갔고, 나는 그를 따라나섰다. 그는 한두 시간 뒤에 일을 마치고 나와 나를 해머스미스로 데려다주기로 했고, 나는 그동안 근처에서 기다리기로 했다. 갓 부화한 어린 보험업자들이 알에서 깨어나는 그 알은, 마치 타조 알처럼 먼지와 열기 속에서 품어지는 모양이라는 생각이 들었으니, 그 풋내기 거인들이 월요일 아침에 찾아드는 장소들로 미루어 보건대 그러했다. 허버트가 일을 거드는 그 회계 사무소도 내 눈에는 도무지 썩 좋은 관측소로는 보이지 않았으니, 어느 안마당으로 들어가 이 층 뒤편에 자리 잡고, 모든 면에서 꾀죄죄한 행색인 데다, 밖을 내다보는 전망이라기보다는 또 다른 이 층 뒤편을 들여다보는 꼴이었기 때문이다.\n\n나는 정오가 될 때까지 근처에서 기다리다가, 증권 거래소에 들어가 보았는데, 거기서 선박에 관한 게시문들 아래 솜털처럼 부스스한 사람들이 앉아 있는 것을 보았다. 나는 그들을 대단한 상인들이려니 짐작했으나, 어째서 그들이 죄다 풀이 죽어 있는지는 이해할 수가 없었다. 허버트가 오자, 우리는 어느 이름난 가게에 가서 점심을 먹었는데, 그때 나는 그곳을 자못 떠받들었으나 지금 와 생각하면 유럽에서 가장 한심한 미신이 아니었나 싶다. 그리고 그곳에서 나는, 그때조차도, 고기 스테이크보다 식탁보며 나이프며 종업원들 옷에 묻은 육즙이 훨씬 더 많다는 것을 알아차리지 않을 수 없었다. 이 간단한 식사를 적당한 값에(공짜로 따라온 그 기름값은 안 친 셈이니) 해치우고서, 우리는 바너드 여관으로 돌아가 내 자그마한 여행 가방을 챙긴 다음, 마차를 타고 해머스미스로 향했다. 우리는 오후 두세 시쯤 거기 도착했고, 포켓 씨의 집까지는 걸어갈 길이 얼마 되지 않았다. 대문 빗장을 들어 올리고, 우리는 곧장 강이 내려다보이는 자그마한 정원으로 들어섰는데, 거기서는 포켓 씨의 아이들이 뛰놀고 있었다. 그리고 내 이해관계나 선입견이 결코 얽혀 있지 않은 이 한 가지 점에서 내가 스스로를 속이는 게 아니라면, 나는 포켓 씨 내외의 아이들이 자라나거나 길러지고 있는 게 아니라, 곤두박질치며 크고 있다는 것을 보았다.\n\n포켓 부인은 나무 아래 정원 의자에 앉아, 두 다리를 또 다른 정원 의자에 얹은 채 책을 읽고 있었고, 포켓 부인의 두 유모는 아이들이 노는 동안 이리저리 둘러보고 있었다. \"엄마,\" 허버트가 말했다. \"이쪽은 젊은 핍 씨예요.\" 그러자 포켓 부인은 상냥한 위엄을 갖춘 모습으로 나를 맞이했다.\n\n\"앨릭 도련님하고 제인 아가씨,\" 유모 하나가 아이들 중 둘에게 소리쳤다. \"그렇게 저 덤불에 막 부딪쳐 가며 뛰다간 강물에 곤두박질쳐서 빠져 죽을 거예요. 그러면 아버님이 뭐라고 하시겠어요?\"\n\n이렇게 말하면서 그 유모는 포켓 부인의 손수건을 주워 들고 말했다. \"이걸 떨어뜨리신 게 이번이 여섯 번째 아니에요, 마님!\" 그러자 포켓 부인은 웃으며 \"고마워요, 플롭슨\"이라고 하고는, 의자 하나에만 자세를 고쳐 앉아 다시 책을 읽기 시작했다. 그녀의 얼굴은 마치 일주일 내내 책을 읽어 온 사람처럼 곧장 미간을 찌푸린 골똘한 표정을 지었으나, 채 여섯 줄도 읽기 전에 그녀는 내게 눈길을 고정하고 말했다. \"댁의 어머님은 안녕히 잘 계시지요?\" 이 뜻밖의 질문에 나는 어찌나 난처해졌던지, 만약 그런 분이 계시기라도 했다면 분명 안녕히 잘 계셨을 거고 무척 고마워하셨을 거고 안부를 전하셨을 거라는 둥 더없이 터무니없는 식으로 주절거리기 시작했는데, 그때 그 유모가 나를 구해 주었다.\n\n\"어머나!\" 그녀가 손수건을 주워 들며 소리쳤다. \"이번이 일곱 번째잖아요! 도대체 오늘 오후엔 뭘 하고 계신 거예요, 마님!\" 포켓 부인은 자기 물건을 받아 들고는, 처음엔 마치 난생처음 보는 물건이기라도 한 듯 이루 말할 수 없이 놀란 표정을 짓더니, 이윽고 알아보았다는 듯 웃음을 터뜨리며 \"고마워요, 플롭슨\"이라고 하고는, 나를 잊어버린 채 다시 책을 읽어 나갔다.\n\n이제 한가로이 헤아려 볼 짬이 나서 세어 보니, 그 자리엔 어린 포켓네 아이들이 자그마치 여섯이나 있었는데, 저마다 곤두박질치며 크는 갖가지 단계에 있었다. 내가 미처 그 총수를 헤아리기도 전에, 일곱 번째 아이가 마치 공중 어디쯤에선가 구슬프게 울어 대는 소리로 들려왔다.\n\n\"아니, 아기잖아!\" 플롭슨이 그게 무척 놀라운 일이라도 되는 양 말했다. \"어서 올라가 봐요, 밀러스.\"\n\n다른 유모였던 밀러스가 집 안으로 들어갔고, 아이의 울음소리는 마치 입에 뭔가 문 어린 복화술사라도 되는 듯 차츰차츰 잦아들다가 그쳤다. 포켓 부인은 그동안 내내 책을 읽고 있었고, 나는 그 책이 도대체 무슨 책일까 궁금했다.\n\n내 짐작에 우리는 포켓 씨가 우리에게 나와 주기를 기다리고 있었던 모양이다. 아무튼 우리는 거기서 기다리고 있었고, 그래서 나는 그 집안의 한 가지 주목할 만한 현상을 관찰할 기회를 얻었으니, 그건 바로 아이들 중 누구든 놀다가 포켓 부인 곁으로 다가오기만 하면 늘 제풀에 발이 걸려 그녀 위로 고꾸라진다는 것이었다 — 그럴 때마다 그녀는 그 순간만큼은 몹시 놀라고, 아이들은 그보다 더 오래 가는 울음을 터뜨렸다. 나는 이 놀라운 정황을 어떻게 설명해야 할지 갈피를 잡지 못한 채, 그것에 대해 이런저런 추측에 골몰하지 않을 수 없었는데, 이윽고 밀러스가 아기를 안고 내려왔고, 그 아기는 플롭슨에게 건네졌으며, 플롭슨이 그것을 포켓 부인에게 건네려던 참에, 그녀마저 아기고 뭐고 할 것 없이 포켓 부인 위로 영락없이 머리부터 고꾸라졌고, 허버트와 내가 그녀를 붙잡았다.\n\n\"어머나, 플롭슨!\" 포켓 부인이 잠깐 책에서 눈을 떼며 말했다. \"다들 고꾸라지는구려!\"\n\n\"어머나라뇨, 마님!\" 플롭슨이 얼굴이 시뻘게져서 대꾸했다. \"거기 깔고 계신 게 뭐예요?\"\n\n\"내가 깔고 있는 거라니, 플롭슨?\" 포켓 부인이 물었다.\n\n\"아니, 마님 발받침대잖아요!\" 플롭슨이 외쳤다. \"그걸 그렇게 치마 밑에 깔고 계시니, 안 고꾸라질 사람이 어디 있겠어요? 자! 아기를 받으세요, 마님. 그리고 그 책은 제게 주시고요.\"\n\n포켓 부인은 그 충고에 따라, 다른 아이들이 그 둘레에서 노는 동안, 서툰 솜씨로 무릎 위의 아기를 가볍게 어르며 흔들었다. 그러나 그것도 아주 잠깐이었을 뿐, 포켓 부인은 아이들을 죄다 집 안으로 데려가 낮잠을 재우라는 즉결 명령을 내렸다. 그리하여 나는 그 첫날, 어린 포켓네 아이들을 기르는 일이란 곤두박질치는 것과 드러눕는 것이 번갈아 이어지는 것임을 두 번째로 깨달았다.\n\n이런 상황에서, 플롭슨과 밀러스가 아이들을 마치 한 무리의 양 떼처럼 집 안으로 몰아넣고, 포켓 씨가 나와 인사를 나누려고 그 집에서 나왔을 때, 나는 포켓 씨가 어딘가 어리둥절한 표정을 짓고, 새하얗게 센 머리가 머리 위에서 헝클어진 채인, 마치 무엇 하나 바로잡을 길을 도무지 찾지 못하는 듯한 신사라는 것을 알고도 그다지 놀라지 않았다.\n\n# Chapter XXIII.\n\n포켓 씨는 나를 만나서 반갑다고 했고, 자기를 만난 것이 내게 언짢은 일이 아니기를 바란다고 했다. \"사실 나는,\" 그는 아들과 똑같은 미소를 띠며 덧붙였다. \"그리 무서운 사람이 아니라네.\" 그는 온갖 곤혹스러운 일들과 잔뜩 센 머리에도 불구하고 젊어 보이는 사람이었고, 태도는 무척 자연스러워 보였다. 내가 자연스럽다는 말을 쓰는 것은 꾸밈이 없다는 뜻에서다. 그의 산만한 태도에는 어딘가 우스꽝스러운 데가 있었는데, 마치 그 자신이 그것이 우스꽝스럽기 직전이라는 것을 알아차리지 못했더라면 그야말로 가관이었을 법한 그런 산만함이었다. 그는 나와 잠시 이야기를 나누더니, 검고 잘생긴 눈썹을 다소 근심스럽게 찌푸리며 포켓 부인에게 말했다. \"벨린다, 핍 군을 잘 맞아 주었기를 바라오.\" 그러자 부인은 읽던 책에서 눈을 들어 \"네\"라고 말했다. 그러고는 마음이 딴 데 가 있는 채로 내게 미소를 짓더니, 오렌지꽃 물의 맛을 좋아하느냐고 물었다. 그 질문은 앞서 있었던 일이나 뒤에 이어질 일과 가깝게도 멀게도 아무런 관련이 없었으므로, 나는 그것이 앞서의 응대들과 마찬가지로 대화에서 으레 베푸는 일반적인 겸양의 표시로 던져진 말이라 여겼다.\n\n나는 몇 시간 안에 알게 되었고, 여기서 곧바로 밝혀 두는 편이 좋겠는데, 포켓 부인은 어쩌다 작위를 받은 어느 작고한 기사의 외동딸이었다. 그 기사는, 작고한 자기 아버지가 순전히 사사로운 동기에서 비롯된 누군가의 완강한 반대만 없었더라면 준남작이 되었을 것이라는 확신을 스스로 지어냈는데 — 내가 들은 적이 있었다 한들 그게 누구였는지는 잊어버렸다 — 국왕이든, 총리든, 대법관이든, 캔터베리 대주교든, 그 누구든 간에 — 그리고 그 지극히 가공된 사실을 근거로 자신을 이 세상의 귀족 반열에 끼워 넣었던 것이다. 짐작건대 그가 기사 작위를 받은 것은, 어느 건물인가의 주춧돌을 놓는 자리에서 송아지 가죽 위에 정성껏 베껴 쓴 절박한 축사로 영문법을 펜 끝으로 들이쳤기 때문이며, 또한 어느 왕족에게 흙손이든 회반죽이든을 건네 드렸기 때문이었다. 어찌 됐든 간에, 그는 포켓 부인을 요람에 있을 때부터 사물의 이치상 반드시 작위 있는 사람과 결혼해야 할 여자로, 그리고 천박한 살림 지식의 습득으로부터 보호받아야 할 여자로 길러지도록 지시해 두었다.\n\n이 분별 있는 부친에 의해 그 아가씨에 대한 감시와 보호가 어찌나 성공적으로 이루어졌던지, 그녀는 더없이 장식적이면서도 완벽하게 무력하고 쓸모없는 사람으로 자라났다. 이렇듯 다행스럽게 성품이 형성된 그녀는, 청춘이 한창 꽃피던 시절에 포켓 씨를 만났다. 포켓 씨 역시 청춘이 한창 꽃피던 무렵이었고, 대법관 자리에 오를지 아니면 주교관을 쓰고 지붕 삼아 살아갈지 아직 결정을 내리지 못한 상태였다. 그가 이쪽이 되든 저쪽이 되든 그것은 그저 시간 문제일 뿐이었으므로, 그와 포켓 부인은 시간이라는 놈의 앞머리채를 움켜잡았고(그 길이로 미루어 보건대 머리를 깎을 때가 된 듯했으니), 분별 있는 부친 모르게 결혼해 버렸다. 분별 있는 부친은 축복 말고는 줄 것도 거둘 것도 없었던지라, 잠시 옥신각신한 끝에 그 지참금을 두 사람에게 후하게 내려 주었고, 포켓 씨에게 그의 아내가 \"왕자에게 어울릴 보물\"이라고 일러 주었다. 그 후로 포켓 씨는 그 왕자의 보물을 세상살이에 투자해 왔는데, 그것이 그에게 가져다준 이자는 시원찮았다는 게 중론이었다. 그래도 포켓 부인은 대체로 묘하게 정중한 동정의 대상이 되었으니, 작위 있는 사람과 결혼하지 못했기 때문이었다. 한편 포켓 씨는 묘하게 너그러운 책망의 대상이 되었으니, 작위를 끝내 얻지 못했기 때문이었다.\n\n포켓 씨는 나를 집 안으로 데리고 들어가 내 방을 보여 주었다. 쾌적한 방이었고, 나만의 개인 거실로도 편안히 쓸 수 있을 만큼 가구가 갖춰져 있었다. 그러고는 비슷한 다른 방 두 개의 문을 두드려, 그 방에 묵는 이들을 내게 소개해 주었는데, 이름이 각각 드러믈과 스타톱이었다. 드러믈은 묵직한 양식으로 지어진 듯, 늙어 보이는 젊은이였는데, 휘파람을 불고 있었다. 스타톱은 나이도 외모도 더 젊었는데, 머리를 감싸 쥔 채 책을 읽고 있었으니, 마치 지식을 너무 강하게 장전한 나머지 제 머리가 폭발할 위험에 처했다고 여기는 것 같았다.\n\n포켓 씨도 포켓 부인도 어딘가 남의 손에 맡겨져 있는 듯한 기색이 어찌나 두드러졌던지, 나는 대체 누가 이 집을 차지하고서 저들을 여기 살게 해 주는 것일까 궁금했는데, 마침내 그 미지의 권력이 하인들임을 알아냈다. 그것은 어쩌면 수고를 더는 면에서는 매끄러운 살림 방식이었으나, 돈이 많이 드는 방식으로 보였다. 하인들은 잘 먹고 잘 마시는 것을, 그리고 아래층에서 손님들을 많이 치르는 것을 스스로에게 진 의무로 여겼기 때문이다. 그들은 포켓 씨 부부에게 매우 후한 식탁을 허락해 주었으나, 내게는 이 집에서 묵기에 단연 가장 좋은 곳은 부엌이었으리라는 생각이 늘 들었다. 물론 묵는 사람이 제 몸을 지킬 능력이 있다는 전제하에서였는데, 내가 그곳에 머문 지 일주일도 채 안 되어, 이 집안과 개인적으로 알지도 못하는 이웃 부인이, 밀러스가 아기를 찰싹 때리는 것을 보았노라고 편지를 보내왔기 때문이다. 이 일로 포켓 부인은 몹시 상심하여, 그 쪽지를 받고는 울음을 터뜨리며, 이웃들이 제 일에나 신경 쓰지 못하는 것은 참으로 별난 일이라고 말했다.\n\n나는 차츰, 주로 허버트에게서 알게 되었는데, 포켓 씨는 해로와 케임브리지에서 교육을 받았고 거기서 두각을 나타냈으나, 인생의 아주 이른 시기에 포켓 부인과 결혼하는 행복을 누리는 바람에 자기 앞길을 망치고 가정교사 노릇에 나섰다고 했다. 머리 둔한 칼날들을 여럿 갈아 준 끝에 — 그 칼날들로 말하자면, 그 아버지들이 영향력 있는 자리에 있을 때에는 언제나 그에게 좋은 자리를 마련해 주겠다 하면서도, 막상 칼날들이 숫돌을 떠나고 나면 언제나 그러기를 잊어버린다는 점이 특이했다 — 그는 그 변변찮은 일에 진력이 나서 런던으로 왔다. 여기서 그는 더 높은 희망들이 차츰 무너진 끝에, 기회가 없었거나 기회를 흘려보낸 갖가지 사람들과 함께 \"공부\"를 했고, 특별한 행사를 앞둔 여러 사람들을 손질해 주었으며, 자기가 쌓은 학식을 문헌 편찬과 교정에 활용했다. 그렇게 번 돈에다 지극히 변변찮은 사적 재산을 보태어, 그는 내가 본 그 집을 여전히 꾸려 가고 있었다.\n\n포켓 씨 부부에게는 알랑거리는 이웃이 하나 있었다. 매우 공감 잘하는 천성을 지닌 어느 미망인으로, 모든 사람의 말에 동의하고, 모든 사람을 축복하며, 상황에 따라 모든 사람에게 미소도 눈물도 뿌리는 부인이었다. 이 부인의 이름은 코일러 부인이었는데, 내가 이 집에 자리 잡은 날 그녀를 식당으로 모시고 내려가는 영광을 누렸다. 그녀는 계단에서 내게, 친애하는 포켓 씨가 자기 밑에서 공부할 신사들을 받아들이지 않으면 안 되는 처지라는 것이 친애하는 포켓 부인에게는 하나의 타격이라고 넌지시 일러 주었다. 다만 그것이 내게는 해당되지 않는다고, 그녀는 사랑과 신뢰가 넘쳐흐르는 어조로 말했다(그때 나는 그녀를 안 지 채 오 분도 되지 않았다). 만약 다들 나 같은 사람이라면 사정이 사뭇 다를 것이라고.\n\n\"하지만 친애하는 포켓 부인은,\" 코일러 부인이 말했다. \"젊은 날의 그 실망 뒤에(친애하는 포켓 씨에게 그 일의 책임이 있다는 건 아니지만), 무척이나 많은 호사와 우아함을 필요로 하시는 분이라—\"\n\n\"네, 부인.\" 나는 그녀가 울음을 터뜨릴까 봐 두려워 말을 끊으려고 그렇게 말했다.\n\n\"게다가 그토록 귀족적인 기질을 지니신 분이라—\"\n\n\"네, 부인.\" 나는 앞서와 같은 의도로 다시 말했다.\n\n\"—그러니 친애하는 포켓 씨의 시간과 관심이 친애하는 포켓 부인에게서 딴 데로 돌려진다는 것은 정말이지 힘든 일이지요.\" 코일러 부인이 말했다.\n\n나는 차라리 푸줏간 주인의 시간과 관심이 친애하는 포켓 부인에게서 딴 데로 돌려지는 편이 더 힘든 일이 아닐까 하는 생각을 떨칠 수 없었다. 그러나 나는 아무 말도 하지 않았고, 사실 사교상의 예법을 수줍게나마 놓치지 않으려 신경 쓰느라 정신이 없었다.\n\n내가 나이프와 포크, 숟가락, 잔, 그 밖의 자멸 도구들에 골몰해 있는 동안 포켓 부인과 드러믈 사이에 오간 이야기를 통해, 나는 드러믈이 — 그의 세례명은 벤틀리였다 — 실은 어느 준남작 작위의 차차기 상속자라는 사실을 알게 되었다. 또한 내가 정원에서 포켓 부인이 읽는 것을 보았던 그 책이 온통 작위에 관한 것이며, 그녀가 자기 할아버지가 그 책에 오를 수 있었던 — 정말로 오를 수만 있었더라면 — 정확한 날짜까지 알고 있다는 것도 드러났다. 드러믈은 말수가 적었으나, 그 한정된 방식으로(그는 내게 뚱한 부류의 사내로 보였다) 선택받은 자의 한 사람처럼 말했고, 포켓 부인을 한 여인이자 한 자매로 인정했다. 그들 자신과 알랑거리는 이웃 코일러 부인 말고는 아무도 대화의 이 대목에 관심을 보이지 않았고, 내가 보기에 그것은 허버트에게 괴로운 일인 듯했다. 그런데 그 이야기가 오래도록 이어질 기세이던 차에, 시동이 들어와 집안의 변고를 알렸다. 요컨대 요리사가 쇠고기를 어디다 두었는지 잃어버렸다는 것이었다. 내가 말로 다 할 수 없을 만큼 놀랐던 것은, 그때 처음으로 포켓 씨가 마음의 짐을 더는 모습을 본 일이었는데, 그것은 내게 무척이나 기이하게 비쳤으나 다른 누구에게도 아무런 인상을 주지 않았고, 머지않아 나도 남들처럼 거기에 익숙해졌다. 그는 — 마침 그 순간 고기를 썰고 있었는지라 — 고기 써는 나이프와 포크를 내려놓더니, 두 손을 헝클어진 머리에 찔러 넣고, 그 머리채를 잡고 제 몸을 들어 올리려는 듯 엄청난 노력을 기울이는 것이었다. 그렇게 하고도 몸이 조금도 들리지 않자, 그는 조용히 하던 일을 다시 이어 갔다.\n\n그러자 코일러 부인은 화제를 바꾸어 내게 아첨하기 시작했다. 처음 얼마 동안은 그것이 기분 좋았으나, 그녀가 어찌나 노골적으로 아첨을 해 대던지 그 즐거움도 이내 사라졌다. 그녀는 내가 떠나온 친구들과 고장에 대해 사뭇 깊은 관심이 있는 척하면서 내게 바싹 다가오는 뱀 같은 버릇이 있었는데, 그 모양새가 영락없이 뱀처럼 갈라진 혀를 날름거리는 듯했다. 그러다 그녀가 이따금 스타톱에게(그는 그녀에게 거의 말을 하지 않았다), 혹은 드러믈에게(그는 더더욱 말을 하지 않았다) 불쑥 달려들 때면, 나는 차라리 식탁 맞은편에 앉은 그들이 부럽기까지 했다.\n\n저녁 식사 후에 아이들이 소개되었고, 코일러 부인은 아이들의 눈과 코와 다리에 대해 찬탄의 말을 늘어놓았으니 — 아이들의 지성을 키우는 데에는 참으로 현명한 방법이었다. 어느 쪽인지 분간이 안 가는 아기와, 아직 어느 쪽도 아닌 그 아기 다음 차례의 아이를 빼고도, 어린 여자아이가 넷, 어린 남자아이가 둘 있었다. 아이들은 플롭슨과 밀러스가 데리고 들어왔는데, 마치 그 두 하사관이 어디선가 아이들을 모집하러 다니다 이 아이들을 입대시키기라도 한 것 같았다. 한편 포켓 부인은 마땅히 귀족이 되었어야 할 그 어린것들을 바라보면서, 전에 이들을 살펴보는 기쁨을 누린 적이 있는 듯도 하나 도무지 이들을 어찌 보아야 할지 모르겠다는 표정이었다.\n\n\"자! 부인, 포크를 제게 주시고 아기를 받으세요.\" 플롭슨이 말했다. \"그렇게 안으시면 안 돼요, 안 그러면 아기 머리가 식탁 밑으로 들어가요.\"\n\n이렇게 일러 주는 대로 포켓 부인은 아기를 반대로 안았고, 그 바람에 아기 머리가 식탁 위에 부딪쳤다. 엄청난 충돌음이 그 자리에 있던 모두에게 그 사실을 알렸다.\n\n\"어머, 어머! 부인, 도로 제게 주세요.\" 플롭슨이 말했다. \"그리고 제인 아가씨, 이리 와서 아기한테 춤춰 줘요, 어서요!\"\n\n어린 여자아이들 가운데 하나가 — 조그마한 꼬마였는데, 어쩐지 제 또래보다 일찍이 다른 아이들을 돌보는 책임 같은 것을 떠맡은 듯한 아이였다 — 내 옆자리에서 일어나, 아기가 울음을 그치고 웃을 때까지 아기를 향해 오락가락하며 춤을 추었다. 그러자 아이들이 모두 웃었고, 포켓 씨도(그사이 두 번이나 제 머리채를 잡고 몸을 들어 올리려 애썼던 그가) 웃었으며, 우리 모두 웃으며 즐거워했다.\n\n플롭슨은 네덜란드 인형처럼 관절마다 아기를 접어 가며 무사히 포켓 부인의 무릎 위에 앉히고는, 가지고 놀라며 호두까기를 쥐여 주었다. 동시에 포켓 부인에게 그 도구의 손잡이가 아기 눈과 맞지 않을 듯하니 유념하시라고 일렀고, 제인 아가씨에게는 그 점을 잘 살피라고 야무지게 당부했다. 그러고 나서 두 보모는 방을 나갔는데, 계단에서 저녁 시중을 들었던 어느 방탕한 시동과 한바탕 활기찬 몸싸움을 벌였다. 그 시동은 노름판에서 단추를 절반은 잃어버린 게 분명했다.\n\n나는 포켓 부인이, 설탕과 포도주에 절인 오렌지 조각을 먹으면서 드러믈과 두 준남작 작위에 관한 토론에 빠져드는 바람에 무척 마음이 불편해졌다. 그녀는 제 무릎 위의 아기를 까맣게 잊고 있었는데, 그 아기는 호두까기로 더없이 끔찍한 짓들을 저지르고 있었다. 마침내 꼬마 제인이 그 어린 머리통이 위태롭다는 것을 알아채고, 살그머니 제자리를 떠나 갖은 잔꾀를 부려 그 위험한 흉기를 살살 빼앗았다. 포켓 부인은 거의 같은 무렵에 오렌지를 다 먹었는데, 이 일을 못마땅해하며 제인에게 말했다—\n\n\"이 못된 것, 어디 감히? 당장 가서 앉지 못해!\"\n\n\"엄마,\" 어린 여자아이가 혀짤배기소리로 말했다. \"아기가 눈을 찌를 뻔했어요.\"\n\n\"네가 어디 감히 그런 말을 하느냐?\" 포켓 부인이 쏘아붙였다. \"당장 가서 네 의자에 앉아!\"\n\n포켓 부인의 위엄이 어찌나 위압적이던지, 나는 마치 내가 그 위엄을 불러일으킬 무슨 짓이라도 한 양 몹시 부끄러워졌다.\n\n\"벨린다,\" 식탁 저쪽 끝에서 포켓 씨가 항의했다. \"어찌 그리 이치에 닿지 않게 구는 거요? 제인은 그저 아기를 지키려고 끼어든 것뿐인데.\"\n\n\"나는 아무도 끼어드는 걸 용납하지 않겠어요.\" 포켓 부인이 말했다. \"매슈, 당신이 나를 이렇게 끼어듦의 모욕에 노출시키다니 놀랍군요.\"\n\n\"맙소사!\" 포켓 씨가 황량한 절망에 사로잡혀 외쳤다. \"아기들을 호두까기로 무덤 속까지 밀어 넣어도, 아무도 그 아이들을 구해서는 안 된단 말이오?\"\n\n\"나는 제인에게 간섭당하지 않겠어요.\" 포켓 부인은 그 죄 없는 어린 죄인에게 위엄 어린 시선을 던지며 말했다. \"내 가엾은 할아버지의 신분이 어떤 것인지 나는 잘 알고 있다고 생각해요. 제인 따위가!\"\n\n포켓 씨는 다시 머리채에 두 손을 가져갔고, 이번에는 정말로 의자에서 몇 인치쯤 제 몸을 들어 올렸다. \"이 말 좀 들어 보시오!\" 그는 천지신명을 향해 속절없이 외쳤다. \"아기들을 호두까기로 죽여도 되는 거랍니다. 사람들의 가엾은 할아버지의 신분을 위해서 말이오!\" 그러고는 다시 몸을 내려놓고 입을 다물었다.\n\n이런 일이 벌어지는 동안 우리는 모두 어색하게 식탁보만 바라보았다. 침묵이 뒤따랐고, 그동안 정직하고 억누를 길 없는 아기는 꼬마 제인을 향해 연거푸 깡충깡충 뛰며 까르르거렸으니, 내가 보기에 제인은 (하인들을 제외하고) 그 아기가 확실히 낯을 익힌 유일한 식구인 듯했다.\n\n\"드러믈 씨,\" 포켓 부인이 말했다. \"플롭슨을 부르게 종을 울려 주시겠어요? 제인, 이 본분도 모르는 것, 가서 누워 있어. 자, 아가야, 엄마한테 오렴!\"\n\n아기는 명예의 화신이라, 있는 힘을 다해 항의했다. 아기는 포켓 부인의 팔 위로 거꾸로 몸을 접더니, 그 보드라운 얼굴 대신 뜨개질한 신발과 보조개진 발목을 좌중에 내보이며, 더없이 반항이 극에 달한 상태로 실려 나갔다. 그리고 결국 제 뜻을 이루었으니, 몇 분 뒤 나는 창문을 통해 꼬마 제인이 그 아기를 어르고 있는 것을 보았다.\n\n마침 플롭슨에게 무슨 사사로운 볼일이 있었던 데다, 다른 다섯 아이들은 그 누구의 소관도 아니었던 탓에, 그 아이들은 식탁에 그대로 남겨졌다. 그리하여 나는 그 아이들과 포켓 씨 사이의 상호 관계를 알게 되었는데, 그것은 다음과 같은 식으로 드러났다. 포켓 씨는 평소의 곤혹스러운 얼굴빛이 한층 짙어지고 머리가 헝클어진 채로, 몇 분 동안 아이들을 바라보았으니, 마치 이 아이들이 어쩌다 이 집에서 먹고 자게 되었는지, 그리고 어째서 자연이 이 아이들을 다른 누군가에게 떠맡기지 않았는지 도무지 알 수 없다는 표정이었다. 그러고는 멀찍이 떨어진 선교사 같은 투로 아이들에게 이런저런 질문을 던졌으니 — 이를테면 어린 조의 옷주름에 왜 저런 구멍이 났느냐고 묻자, 조는 \"아빠, 플롭슨이 시간 나면 기워 준대요\" 하고 대답했고 — 또 어린 패니의 손가락이 어쩌다 저렇게 곪았느냐고 묻자, 패니는 \"아빠, 밀러스가 안 잊어버리면 찜질해 준대요\" 하고 대답하는 식이었다. 그러더니 그는 어버이다운 다정함에 마음이 녹아, 아이들에게 일 실링씩 나눠 주며 가서 놀라고 했다. 그런 다음 아이들이 나가자, 제 머리채를 잡고 몸을 들어 올리려 한 차례 무척이나 힘껏 애쓰면서, 그 가망 없는 문제를 떨쳐 버렸다.\n\n저녁에는 강에서 뱃놀이가 있었다. 드러믈과 스타톱이 저마다 배를 한 척씩 가지고 있었으므로, 나는 내 배를 마련하여 그 둘을 앞지르기로 마음먹었다. 나는 시골 아이들이 능숙한 운동이라면 대개 제법 잘하는 편이었으나, 템스강에서 — 다른 강은 말할 것도 없고 — 노 젓는 자세의 품격이 부족하다는 것을 자각하고 있었으므로, 곧장 우리 선착장에서 일하던, 상을 탄 적이 있는 노잡이의 지도를 받기로 했다. 그는 내 새 친구들이 내게 소개해 준 사람이었다. 이 실전의 권위자는 내게 대장장이의 팔을 지녔다고 말함으로써 나를 무척 당혹스럽게 했다. 만약 그가 그 칭찬으로 자기 제자를 잃을 뻔했다는 것을 알았더라면, 과연 그런 말을 했을지 의문이다.\n\n그날 밤 집에 돌아온 뒤에는 야식 쟁반이 차려졌고, 다소 불쾌한 집안일만 없었더라면 우리 모두 즐거운 시간을 보냈으리라 생각한다. 포켓 씨가 기분이 좋아 보이던 차에, 하녀 하나가 들어와 말했다. \"죄송하지만 나리, 나리께 드릴 말씀이 있습니다.\"\n\n\"네 주인에게 말을 하겠다고?\" 다시금 위엄이 발동한 포켓 부인이 말했다. \"어떻게 그런 생각을 다 하느냐? 가서 플롭슨에게 말해. 아니면 나한테 말하든지 — 다른 때에.\"\n\n\"송구합니다만, 부인,\" 하녀가 대꾸했다. \"지금 당장, 그리고 나리께 말씀드리고 싶습니다.\"\n\n이에 포켓 씨가 방을 나갔고, 우리는 그가 돌아올 때까지 저마다 어색함을 견뎠다.\n\n\"이거 참 가관이구려, 벨린다!\" 슬픔과 절망이 어린 얼굴로 돌아오며 포켓 씨가 말했다. \"글쎄 요리사가 부엌 바닥에 인사불성으로 취해 뻗어 있고, 찬장에는 기름으로 팔아넘기려고 싱싱한 버터를 한 다발 큼지막하게 꾸려 놓았지 뭐요!\"\n\n포켓 부인은 즉시 자못 상냥한 감정을 드러내며 말했다. \"이건 그 밉살스러운 소피아의 짓이에요!\"\n\n\"무슨 소리요, 벨린다?\" 포켓 씨가 물었다.\n\n\"소피아가 당신에게 일러바친 거잖아요.\" 포켓 부인이 말했다. \"방금 그 애가 방으로 들어와 당신께 말씀드리겠다고 청하는 것을 내가 두 눈으로 똑똑히 보고 두 귀로 똑똑히 듣지 않았느냐고요?\"\n\n\"하지만 벨린다, 그 애가 나를 아래층으로 데려가서,\" 포켓 씨가 대꾸했다. \"그 여자도, 그 버터 다발도 보여 주지 않았소?\"\n\n\"그래서 당신은, 매슈,\" 포켓 부인이 말했다. \"그 애가 분란을 일으킨 걸 두둔하는 거예요?\"\n\n포켓 씨는 침울한 신음을 내뱉었다.\n\n\"이 할아버지의 손녀인 내가, 이 집에서 아무것도 아니란 말이에요?\" 포켓 부인이 말했다. \"게다가 그 요리사는 늘 아주 착하고 공손한 여자였어요. 이 일자리를 보러 왔을 때도 더없이 자연스러운 태도로, 내가 공작 부인이 되려고 태어난 사람이라는 게 느껴진다고 했단 말이에요.\"\n\n포켓 씨가 서 있던 자리에는 소파가 하나 있었는데, 그는 「죽어 가는 검투사」 같은 자세로 그 위에 풀썩 쓰러졌다. 여전히 그 자세로 그는 공허한 목소리로 \"안녕히 주무시게, 핍 군\" 하고 말했고, 나는 그를 두고 잠자리에 드는 편이 좋겠다고 판단했다.\n\n# Chapter XXIV.\n\n이삼일 뒤, 내 방에 자리를 잡고, 런던을 여러 차례 오가고, 상인들에게 필요한 물건을 모두 주문하고 나자, 포켓 씨와 나는 오랜 이야기를 나누었다. 그는 내가 앞으로 걸어갈 길에 대해 나 자신보다 더 잘 알고 있었는데, 재거스 씨로부터 내가 어떤 전문 직업을 위해 길러지는 것이 아니며, 만약 내가 유복한 환경에 놓인 평범한 젊은이들 사이에서 \"제 몫을 해낼\" 수 있을 정도라면 내 운명에 걸맞게 충분히 교육받은 셈이 된다는 말을 들었다고 했다. 나는 물론 그 말에 동의했으니, 달리 알고 있는 바가 없었기 때문이다.\n\n그는 내가 원하는 그 정도의 기초 지식을 얻기 위해 런던의 몇몇 곳에 다닐 것을 권했고, 내 모든 학업의 해설자이자 지도자라는 직분을 자신에게 맡기라고 했다. 그는 지각 있는 도움을 받으면 내가 낙담할 일은 거의 없을 것이며, 머지않아 그의 도움 외에는 어떤 조력도 필요 없게 되리라 기대했다. 이렇게 말하는 방식과, 그 비슷한 취지의 여러 말을 통해 그는 감탄스러울 만큼 자연스럽게 나와 흉허물 없는 사이가 되었다. 그리고 한마디로 말하건대, 그는 나와의 약속을 이행하는 데 늘 열성적이고 정직했으므로, 나 역시 그와의 약속을 이행하는 데 열성적이고 정직해질 수밖에 없었다. 만약 그가 스승으로서 무관심한 태도를 보였다면, 나도 제자로서 그에 걸맞게 응했으리라는 데에는 의심의 여지가 없다. 그러나 그는 내게 그런 구실을 주지 않았고, 우리는 서로에게 정당히 대했다. 또한 나는 그가 나를 가르치며 보이는 태도에서 우스꽝스러운 구석을 — 진지하고 정직하며 선량한 것 외의 무엇을 — 단 한 번도 느낀 적이 없었다.\n\n이런 점들이 정리되고, 내가 본격적으로 공부를 시작할 만큼 실행에 옮겨지자, 만약 바너드 여관의 내 침실을 그대로 유지할 수 있다면 내 생활이 한결 다채로워질 것이며, 게다가 허버트와 어울려 지내는 것이 내 몸가짐에 해가 될 리 없으리라는 생각이 떠올랐다. 포켓 씨는 이 계획에 반대하지 않았으나, 그 일에 어떤 조처를 취하기에 앞서 반드시 내 후견인에게 의논해야 한다고 강조했다. 나는 이 신중함이 그 계획이 허버트의 비용을 얼마간 덜어 주리라는 고려에서 비롯된 것임을 느꼈으므로, 리틀 브리튼으로 가서 재거스 씨에게 내 바람을 전했다.\n\n\"지금 제게 빌려 준 가구를 사들일 수 있다면,\" 내가 말했다. \"그리고 한두 가지 자질구레한 물건도 더해서요, 거기서 아주 편안히 지낼 수 있을 것 같습니다.\"\n\n\"해 보게!\" 재거스 씨가 짤막하게 웃으며 말했다. \"자네가 잘 풀려 나갈 거라고 했잖나. 그래! 얼마나 필요한가?\"\n\n나는 얼마나 필요한지 모르겠다고 했다.\n\n\"이봐!\" 재거스 씨가 받아쳤다. \"얼마나? 오십 파운드?\"\n\n\"아, 그렇게까지는 안 됩니다.\"\n\n\"오 파운드?\" 재거스 씨가 말했다.\n\n이것은 너무나 큰 추락이었으므로, 나는 당혹스러워하며 말했다. \"아, 그보다는 더 듭니다.\"\n\n\"그보다 더 든다, 응!\" 재거스 씨가 두 손을 주머니에 넣고, 고개를 한쪽으로 기울이고, 내 뒤편의 벽에 눈길을 둔 채, 나를 옭아매려는 듯 받아쳤다. \"얼마나 더?\"\n\n\"액수를 정하기가 참 어렵네요.\" 나는 머뭇거리며 말했다.\n\n\"이봐!\" 재거스 씨가 말했다. \"이렇게 따져 보세. 오의 두 배면 되겠나? 오의 세 배면 되겠나? 오의 네 배면 되겠나?\"\n\n나는 그 정도면 넉넉하겠다고 했다.\n\n\"오의 네 배면 넉넉하겠다, 그렇단 말이지?\" 재거스 씨가 눈썹을 찌푸리며 말했다. \"자, 오의 네 배면 자네는 얼마라고 보는가?\"\n\n\"제가 얼마라고 보느냐고요?\"\n\n\"그래!\" 재거스 씨가 말했다. \"얼마지?\"\n\n\"이십 파운드로 치시는 거겠지요.\" 내가 미소 지으며 말했다.\n\n\"내가 얼마로 치든 그건 신경 쓰지 말게, 친구.\" 재거스 씨가 짐짓 알겠다는 듯, 그러면서도 반박하듯 고개를 까딱하며 말했다. \"나는 자네가 얼마로 보는지를 알고 싶은 거야.\"\n\n\"물론 이십 파운드지요.\"\n\n\"웨믹!\" 재거스 씨가 자기 사무실 문을 열며 말했다. \"핍 씨의 서면 지시를 받아서 이십 파운드를 지급하게.\"\n\n이렇게 유난히 도드라진 일 처리 방식은 내게 유난히 도드라진 인상을 남겼는데, 그것은 결코 유쾌한 종류의 인상이 아니었다. 재거스 씨는 결코 웃지 않았다. 그러나 그는 크고 번쩍거리며 삐걱대는 장화를 신고 다녔는데, 그 장화 위에서 몸의 균형을 잡고, 커다란 머리를 숙이고, 두 눈썹을 맞붙인 채 대답을 기다리노라면, 이따금 그 장화가 삐걱거리게 만들어, 마치 그 장화가 메마르고 미심쩍은 투로 웃는 것처럼 보였다. 마침 그가 지금 밖으로 나갔고, 웨믹은 활달하고 말이 많은 편이었으므로, 나는 웨믹에게 재거스 씨의 태도를 어떻게 받아들여야 할지 도무지 모르겠다고 말했다.\n\n\"그 말을 그분께 하면, 그분은 칭찬으로 받아들일 겁니다.\" 웨믹이 대답했다. \"그분은 당신이 그걸 어떻게 받아들일지 알기를 바라지도 않으니까요. 아!\" 내가 놀란 표정을 짓자 그가 덧붙였다. \"그건 개인적인 게 아니라 직업적인 거예요. 순전히 직업적인 거죠.\"\n\n웨믹은 자기 책상에 앉아 마른 딱딱한 비스킷으로 점심을 먹고 있었다. 정확히는 우두둑 씹고 있었다. 그는 그 조각들을 이따금 자기 입의 틈새로 던져 넣었는데, 마치 그것들을 우체통에 부치는 것 같았다.\n\n\"제가 보기에 그분은 늘,\" 웨믹이 말했다. \"사람 잡는 덫을 놓고 그것을 지켜보고 있는 것 같단 말입니다. 그러다 별안간 — 철컥 — 잡히는 거죠!\"\n\n사람 잡는 덫이 인생의 즐거움 축에는 들지 못한다는 말은 굳이 하지 않은 채, 나는 그가 무척 솜씨가 좋은가 보다고 했다.\n\n\"깊지요,\" 웨믹이 말했다. \"오스트레일리아만큼이나요.\" 그는 펜으로 사무실 바닥을 가리켰는데, 이 비유의 취지상 오스트레일리아가 지구 정반대편의 대칭점에 있다고 보면 된다는 뜻이었다. \"그보다 더 깊은 게 있다면,\" 웨믹이 펜을 종이에 갖다 대며 덧붙였다. \"그분이 바로 그것일 겁니다.\"\n\n이어서 나는 그가 좋은 사업을 꾸리고 있나 보다고 했고, 웨믹은 \"굉-장-하-죠!\" 하고 말했다. 그다음 나는 서기가 많으냐고 물었고, 그가 이렇게 대답했다.\n\n\"우리는 서기를 그리 많이 두지 않습니다. 재거스라는 사람은 오직 한 분뿐이고, 사람들은 그분을 거쳐 온 손에서 받으려 하질 않으니까요. 우리는 모두 넷뿐입니다. 그들을 보시겠어요? 당신도 어떤 의미에선 우리 식구나 다름없으니까요.\"\n\n나는 그 제안을 받아들였다. 웨믹 씨가 비스킷을 죄다 우체통에 부치고, 금고 안의 현금 상자에서 내 돈을 지급한 다음 — 그 금고 열쇠는 등 뒤 어딘가에 간직해 두었다가 무쇠 변발이라도 되는 양 외투 깃에서 끄집어냈다 — 우리는 위층으로 올라갔다. 그 집은 어둡고 초라했으며, 재거스 씨의 방에 자국을 남긴 그 기름진 어깨들이 수년에 걸쳐 그 계단을 오르내리며 비벼 댄 듯했다. 정면 이 층에서는, 술집 주인과 쥐잡이꾼의 중간쯤 되어 보이는 서기 한 명이 — 덩치가 크고 핼쑥하며 부석부석 부어오른 사내였다 — 행색이 초라한 서너 사람을 정성껏 상대하고 있었는데, 그는 재거스 씨의 금고에 보탬이 되는 이들이라면 누구나 받는 듯한 무례한 대접 그대로 그들을 대했다. \"증거를 모으는 중이지요,\" 우리가 나오면서 웨믹 씨가 말했다. \"올드 베일리에 낼 것을요.\" 그 위층 방에서는, 늘어진 털을 가진 작고 물렁한 테리어 같은 서기가 — 강아지였을 적에 털 깎아 주기를 잊은 듯했다 — 마찬가지로 눈이 침침한 사내를 상대하고 있었는데, 웨믹 씨는 그자가 늘 도가니를 끓여 두고 내가 원하는 것은 무엇이든 녹여 줄 수 있는 제련공이라고 소개했다. 그 사내는 마치 제 솜씨를 제 몸에 시험해 본 것처럼 온통 허옇게 땀을 흘리고 있었다. 뒤편 방에서는, 어깨가 솟고 얼굴이 아파 더러운 플란넬로 싸맨 사내가 — 밀랍을 먹인 듯한 낡고 검은 옷을 입고 있었다 — 몸을 구부려, 앞서의 두 신사가 적은 기록을 재거스 씨가 직접 쓰도록 정서하는 일에 매달려 있었다.\n\n이것이 사무소의 전부였다. 우리가 다시 아래층으로 내려오자, 웨믹은 나를 후견인의 방으로 데리고 들어가 말했다. \"여기는 이미 보셨지요.\"\n\n\"실례지만,\" 경련하듯 곁눈질하는 그 두 흉측한 흉상이 다시 내 눈에 들어왔으므로 내가 말했다. \"저것들은 누구의 얼굴을 본뜬 겁니까?\"\n\n\"이것들요?\" 웨믹이 의자 위에 올라서서, 그 끔찍한 머리들을 내리기에 앞서 먼지를 후 불며 말했다. \"이건 유명한 두 사람입니다. 우리에게 엄청난 명성을 안겨 준 이름난 고객들이죠. 이 친구로 말하자면 (이런, 자네는 밤에 몰래 내려와 잉크병 속을 들여다본 모양이군, 눈썹에 이런 얼룩이 묻은 걸 보니, 이 늙은 악당아!) 제 주인을 살해했는데, 증인으로 자라난 처지가 아니었던 걸 감안하면 꽤나 영리하게 일을 꾸민 셈입니다.\"\n\n\"그자를 닮았나요?\" 웨믹이 그 흉상의 눈썹에 침을 뱉고 소매로 문질러 닦는 동안, 나는 그 짐승 같은 얼굴에서 움찔 물러서며 물었다.\n\n\"닮았느냐고요? 이건 바로 그자 자신인걸요. 이 본은 그자를 끌어내린 직후에 뉴게이트에서 떴습니다. 자네는 나를 유달리 좋아했지, 안 그런가, 이 늙은 능구렁이?\" 웨믹이 말했다. 그러고는 항아리가 얹힌 무덤 곁에서 버드나무를 두고 우는 여인을 새긴 자기 브로치를 만지며 이 다정한 호칭의 연유를 설명했다. \"나를 위해 일부러 맞춰 준 거랍니다!\"\n\n\"그 여인은 누구입니까?\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"아닙니다,\" 웨믹이 대답했다. \"그저 이 친구가 꾸며 낸 거예요. (자네는 그런 잔재미를 좋아했지, 안 그래?) 아니요, 이 사건에 여인이라곤 눈곱만치도 없습니다, 핍 씨. 단 한 사람만 빼고요. 그런데 그 여자는 이렇게 가냘프고 숙녀 같은 부류가 아니었고, 항아리 안에 마실 거라도 들어 있지 않은 다음에야 그 여자가 이 항아리를 들여다보고 있는 꼴은 결코 보지 못했을 겁니다.\" 웨믹의 주의가 이렇게 자기 브로치로 쏠리자, 그는 흉상을 내려놓고 손수건으로 브로치를 닦았다.\n\n\"저 다른 자도 같은 최후를 맞았나요?\" 내가 물었다. \"같은 표정을 짓고 있네요.\"\n\n\"맞습니다,\" 웨믹이 말했다. \"그게 진짜 그 표정이죠. 마치 한쪽 콧구멍을 말총과 작은 낚싯바늘로 위로 끌어 올린 것 같지요. 그래요, 이자도 같은 최후를 맞았습니다. 여기선 지극히 자연스러운 최후이지요, 장담합니다. 이 친구는 유언장을 위조했어요, 추정 유언자들을 잠재워 버리지까지는 않았다 쳐도 말입니다. 그래도 자네는 신사다운 작자였지,\" (웨믹이 다시 그 흉상을 향해 말을 건넸다.) \"그리고 그리스어를 쓸 줄 안다고 했지. 야, 이 허풍쟁이야! 자네 같은 거짓말쟁이는 없었어! 자네 같은 거짓말쟁이는 평생 만나 본 적이 없다고!\" 이 고인이 된 친구를 다시 선반에 올려놓기에 앞서, 웨믹은 자기 추모용 반지 중에서 가장 큰 것을 만지며 말했다. \"바로 그 전날 나를 위해 사다 달라고 사람을 보냈었지요.\"\n\n그가 다른 흉상을 마저 올려놓고 의자에서 내려오는 동안, 그의 몸에 걸린 장신구가 모두 비슷한 출처에서 비롯되었으리라는 생각이 내 머리를 스쳤다. 그가 이 화제에 조금도 거리낌을 보이지 않았으므로, 그가 손을 털며 내 앞에 섰을 때, 나는 외람되이 그 질문을 던져 보았다.\n\n\"아, 그렇습니다,\" 그가 대답했다. \"이건 다 그런 식의 선물이죠. 하나가 또 하나를 불러오게 마련입니다, 아시겠지요. 그게 이치예요. 저는 그것들을 늘 받아 둡니다. 진귀한 물건이니까요. 게다가 재산이기도 하고요. 값나가는 건 아닐지 몰라도, 어쨌든 재산이고 게다가 들고 다닐 수 있는 거니까요. 당신처럼 앞날이 찬란한 분께야 대수롭지 않은 일이겠지만, 저로 말하자면, 제 길잡이별은 언제나 '들고 다닐 수 있는 재산을 손에 넣어라'랍니다.\"\n\n내가 이 신념에 경의를 표하자, 그는 다정한 태도로 말을 이었다.\n\n\"혹 언젠가 달리 할 일이 없을 적에, 월워스로 저를 보러 와 주신다면, 잠자리도 마련해 드릴 수 있고, 저로서는 영광으로 여기겠습니다. 보여 드릴 만한 게 그리 많지는 않습니다만, 제가 가진 두어 가지 진기한 물건쯤은 둘러보실 만할 거고요. 게다가 저는 자그마한 정원과 정자를 무척 아낀답니다.\"\n\n나는 기꺼이 그 환대를 받겠노라고 했다.\n\n\"고맙습니다,\" 그가 말했다. \"그럼 형편이 닿는 대로 그렇게 하기로 하지요. 재거스 씨와 식사는 아직 안 해 보셨지요?\"\n\n\"아직요.\"\n\n\"그렇군요,\" 웨믹이 말했다. \"그분은 당신에게 포도주를 내실 겁니다, 그것도 좋은 포도주를요. 저는 펀치를 대접하지요, 그것도 그리 나쁘지 않은 펀치를요. 그리고 이제 한 가지 일러 드리겠습니다. 재거스 씨와 식사하러 가시거든, 그분의 가정부를 잘 보십시오.\"\n\n\"무척 보기 드문 것을 보게 됩니까?\"\n\n\"글쎄요,\" 웨믹이 말했다. \"길들여진 야수를 보시게 될 겁니다. 그리 보기 드문 건 아니라고 하시겠지요. 그건 그 짐승이 본디 얼마나 사나웠는지, 또 얼마나 길들였는지에 달렸다고 답하겠습니다. 그걸 보시더라도 재거스 씨의 역량에 대한 당신의 평가가 깎이지는 않을 겁니다. 눈여겨보세요.\"\n\n나는 그의 그런 사전 언질이 불러일으킨 온갖 흥미와 호기심을 안고서 그렇게 하겠다고 했다. 내가 막 떠나려 할 때, 그는 오 분쯤 짬을 내어 재거스 씨가 \"일하는\" 모습을 보겠느냐고 물었다.\n\n몇 가지 이유에서, 그중에서도 재거스 씨가 대체 무슨 \"일\"을 하는 모습으로 발견될지 분명히 알지 못했다는 이유가 적지 않아, 나는 그러겠다고 대답했다. 우리는 시내 한복판으로 파고들었다가, 사람들로 붐비는 경찰 법정으로 나왔는데, 그곳에서는 브로치에 별난 취향을 가졌던 그 고인의 (살인이라는 의미에서의) 혈육 한 사람이 피고석에 서서 거북하게 무언가를 우물거리고 있었다. 한편 내 후견인은 한 여자를 신문, 혹은 반대 신문 — 어느 쪽인지는 모르겠다 — 하고 있었는데, 그 여자와 판사석과 그 자리에 있는 모든 이에게 경외감을 안기고 있었다. 누구든, 어떤 신분의 사람이든, 그가 못마땅해하는 말을 한마디라도 하면, 그는 즉각 그것을 \"기록해 둘\" 것을 요구했다. 누구든 자백하려 들지 않으면, 그는 \"내가 자네한테서 기어이 끄집어내고 말겠어!\" 하고 말했고, 누구든 자백을 하면, \"이제 자네를 잡았군!\" 하고 말했다. 치안 판사들은 그가 손가락을 한 번 깨무는 것만으로도 부르르 떨었다. 도둑들과 도둑 잡는 자들이 두려운 황홀경에 빠져 그의 말에 매달렸고, 그의 눈썹 한 올이 자기네 쪽으로 돌아오기만 해도 움츠러들었다. 그가 어느 편인지 나로서는 도무지 알 수 없었으니, 내게는 그가 그곳 전체를 방앗간에 넣고 갈아 대고 있는 것처럼 보였기 때문이다. 다만 내가 발끝으로 살그머니 빠져나올 무렵 그가 판사석 편은 아니었다는 것만은 분명히 안다. 그도 그럴 것이, 그는 그날 그 자리에서 영국의 법과 정의를 대표하는 자로서 늙은 재판장의 처신을 규탄함으로써, 탁자 아래 그 노신사의 두 다리를 완전히 경련으로 떨게 만들고 있었으니까.\n\n# Chapter XXV.\n\n벤틀리 드러믈은 어찌나 뚱한 위인이었던지, 책 한 권을 집어 들 때조차 마치 그 책을 쓴 사람이 자신에게 해코지라도 한 것처럼 굴었으니, 사람을 사귈 때라고 해서 더 상냥한 마음가짐으로 임할 리 없었다. 몸집도 동작도 이해력도 굼떴고 — 얼굴빛은 칙칙하니 활기가 없었으며, 크고 어설픈 혀는 그가 방 안을 어슬렁거리듯 입 안에서 늘어져 어슬렁대는 듯했으니 — 그는 게으르고, 거만하고, 인색하고, 속을 드러내지 않으며, 의심이 많은 사람이었다. 그는 서머싯셔 시골의 부유한 집안 출신이었는데, 그 집안은 이런 자질의 조합을 고이 길러 오다가 마침내 그것이 다 자라 성년에 이르도록 멍청이임을 발견하기에 이르렀다. 그리하여 벤틀리 드러믈이 포켓 선생에게 왔을 때, 그는 그 신사보다 머리 하나만큼 키가 컸고, 여느 신사들보다 머리 여섯 개만큼 둔했다.\n\n스타톱은 마음 약한 어머니 탓에 응석받이로 자라, 마땅히 학교에 가 있어야 할 나이에 집에 머물렀으나, 어머니를 극진히 따랐고 한없이 흠모했다. 그는 여자처럼 섬세한 이목구비를 지녔는데 — \"자네는 그 부인을 한 번도 본 적이 없겠지만, 보면 알 거야.\" 하고 허버트가 내게 말했다 — \"그분과 영락없이 똑같다네.\" 내가 드러믈보다 그에게 훨씬 더 살갑게 끌린 것은 지극히 자연스러운 일이었고, 우리가 막 뱃놀이를 시작하던 초저녁부터 그와 내가 나란히 배를 저어 집으로 돌아오며 이 배에서 저 배로 이야기를 주고받는 동안, 벤틀리 드러믈이 우리 뒤꽁무니에서 홀로 늘어진 강둑 아래와 골풀 사이를 헤치며 올라오던 것 또한 자연스러운 일이었다. 그는 밀물이 그를 길로 빨리 떠밀어 줄 때조차도, 어딘가 불편한 양서류 같은 짐승처럼 늘 물가로 기어들곤 했다. 그래서 나는 그를 떠올릴 때면, 우리 두 배가 강 한복판에서 노을이나 달빛을 가르고 나아갈 때 그가 어둠 속이나 후미진 물길로 우리 뒤를 따라오는 모습으로 늘 기억하게 된다.\n\n허버트는 나의 절친한 동무이자 벗이었다. 나는 그에게 내 배의 절반을 나누어 주었는데, 그 덕분에 그는 자주 해머스미스로 내려오게 되었다. 그리고 내가 그의 거처에 절반의 지분을 가진 덕에 나는 자주 런던으로 올라가게 되었다. 우리는 시간을 가리지 않고 그 두 곳 사이를 걸어다니곤 했다. 나는 지금도 그 길에 애정을 품고 있는데(지금은 그때만큼 즐거운 길은 못 되지만), 그것은 아직 세상에 부딪혀 보지 않은 젊음과 희망의 감수성 속에서 빚어진 애정이다.\n\n내가 포켓 선생 댁에 머문 지 한두 달쯤 되었을 때, 커밀러 부부가 나타났다. 커밀러는 포켓 선생의 누이였다. 같은 자리에서 미스 해비셤의 집에서 보았던 조지애나도 나타났다. 그녀는 사촌으로 — 소화 불량을 앓는 독신녀였는데, 자신의 완고함을 신앙심이라 일컫고 자신의 짜증을 사랑이라 일컫는 사람이었다. 이 사람들은 탐욕과 실망에서 비롯된 증오심으로 나를 미워했다. 당연한 일이지만, 그들은 내가 형편이 펴자 더없이 비열하게 알랑거렸다. 자기 잇속이라곤 도무지 모르는 다 큰 어린애로 여기던 포켓 선생에게는, 내가 일찍이 그들 입에서 들었던 그 잘난 척하는 너그러움을 베풀었다. 포켓 부인은 멸시했으나, 그 가엾은 사람이 인생에서 크게 실망을 겪었다는 점만은 인정해 주었으니, 그것이 그들 자신에게 희미하게나마 반사된 빛을 비추어 주었기 때문이었다.\n\n내가 자리를 잡고 학업에 정진하게 된 것은 바로 이런 환경 속에서였다. 나는 곧 돈이 많이 드는 습관에 물들었고, 몇 달 전만 해도 거의 터무니없다고 여겼을 만한 액수의 돈을 쓰기 시작했다. 그러나 좋든 나쁘든 나는 책에서 손을 떼지 않았다. 여기에 무슨 갸륵한 데가 있었던 것은 아니고, 다만 내가 부족함을 느낄 만한 분별은 갖추고 있었던 것뿐이다. 포켓 선생과 허버트 덕분에 나는 빠르게 나아갔다. 그리고 둘 중 하나가 늘 내 곁에서 필요한 출발점을 잡아 주고 내 앞길의 장애물을 치워 주었으니, 만약 내가 그만큼도 못 해냈다면 나는 드러믈만큼이나 큰 얼간이였을 것이다.\n\n웨믹 씨를 보지 못한 지 몇 주가 되었을 무렵, 나는 그에게 쪽지를 써서 어느 날 저녁 함께 그의 집으로 가자고 청해 보면 어떨까 생각했다. 그는 그렇게 해 주면 무척 기쁘겠다고, 여섯 시에 사무소에서 나를 기다리겠다고 답했다. 나는 그리로 갔고, 거기서 시계가 종을 칠 때 금고 열쇠를 등 뒤로 늘어뜨려 넣고 있는 그를 만났다.\n\n\"월워스까지 걸어 내려갈 생각이오?\" 그가 말했다.\n\n\"물론입니다,\" 내가 말했다. \"괜찮으시다면요.\"\n\n\"아주 좋소,\" 웨믹의 대답이었다. \"하루 종일 다리를 책상 밑에 처박고 있었더니, 좀 펴 보고 싶던 참이오. 자, 저녁으로 뭘 마련해 두었는지 말해 드리지요, 핍 씨. 스튜 스테이크가 있는데 — 이건 집에서 만든 것이고 — 차가운 통구이 닭이 있는데 — 이건 음식점에서 산 것이오. 아마 연할 거요, 그 가게 주인이 며칠 전 우리 사건 몇 건에서 배심원을 맡았는데, 우리가 그를 봐줬거든. 닭을 사면서 그 일을 일깨워 주고는 이렇게 말했지요. '좋은 놈으로 하나 골라 주시오, 영감, 안 그랬으면 며칠 더 배심원석에 잡아 둘 수도 있었으니까.' 그랬더니 그가 말합디다. '가게에서 제일 좋은 닭을 선물로 드리지요.' 물론 나야 그러라고 했소. 어쨌든 그게 어디요, 재산이고 들고 다닐 수 있는 것이니. 노부모를 모시는 건 괜찮으시겠지요?\"\n\n나는 그가 여전히 닭 이야기를 하는 줄로만 알았는데, 그가 이렇게 덧붙였다. \"내 집에 늙으신 아버님이 계시거든요.\" 그제야 나는 예의상 마땅한 말을 했다.\n\n\"그래, 아직 재거스 씨와 식사를 함께 한 적은 없지요?\" 그가 걸어가며 말을 이었다.\n\n\"아직 없습니다.\"\n\n\"오후에 당신이 온다는 말을 듣고 그가 내게 그렇게 말합디다. 내일 초대를 받게 될 거요. 당신 동무들도 부르려 한답니다. 셋이지요, 아마?\"\n\n나는 드러믈을 내 가까운 동무 가운데 하나로 꼽는 버릇은 없었으나, \"네.\" 하고 대답했다.\n\n\"음, 그 패거리를 통째로 부른다는데\" — 그 말이 그리 칭찬으로 들리지는 않았다 — \"뭘 대접하든 좋은 걸로 줄 거요. 종류가 다양하리라고 기대하진 마시오, 하지만 최상품을 맛보게 될 테니. 그리고 그 집에는 또 한 가지 묘한 게 있소,\" 하고 웨믹은 잠시 말을 멈춘 뒤, 마치 앞서 말한 가정부 이야기에 이어지는 말이라는 듯 계속했다. \"그는 밤에 문이든 창이든 결코 잠그게 하지 않는다오.\"\n\n\"도둑을 맞은 적이 없습니까?\"\n\n\"바로 그거요!\" 웨믹이 대꾸했다. \"그가 늘 하는 말이, 또 공공연히 떠벌리는 말이, '나를 털 놈이 있으면 한번 봤으면 좋겠군.'이오. 맙소사, 나는 그가 우리 사무소 앞방에서 상습 강도들한테 이렇게 말하는 걸 한두 번도 아니고 백 번은 들었소. '너희들 내가 어디 사는지 알지. 거긴 빗장 한 번 거는 법이 없어. 어디 한번 나랑 거래 좀 해 보지그래? 자, 내가 솔깃하게 만들어 줄까?' 하지만 그자들 가운데 어느 누구도, 돈을 준대도 사랑을 준대도, 감히 그걸 시도해 볼 만큼 배짱 있는 놈은 없었소.\"\n\n\"그를 그토록 무서워합니까?\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"무서워하느냐고요.\" 웨믹이 말했다. \"정말이지 무서워하고말고요. 하지만 그가 그자들을 깔보는 데도 다 잔꾀가 숨어 있소. 은붙이라곤 없거든요. 숟가락 하나하나가 죄다 브리타니아 합금이오.\"\n\n\"그러니까 그자들이 손에 넣을 것도 별로 없겠군요,\" 하고 내가 말했다. \"설령 그자들이——\"\n\n\"아! 하지만 그자는 손에 넣을 게 많지.\" 웨믹이 내 말을 자르며 말했다. \"그자들도 그걸 알고 있소. 그는 그자들의 목숨을 차지할 거요, 수십 명의 목숨을 말이오. 손에 넣을 수 있는 건 죄다 차지하겠지. 마음만 먹으면 손에 넣지 못할 게 뭐가 있을지, 도무지 가늠할 수가 없는 사람이오.\"\n\n내가 후견인의 위대함을 두고 골똘히 생각에 잠기려는데, 웨믹이 말했다.\n\n\"은붙이가 없는 건 말이오, 그저 그 사람 천성의 깊이일 뿐이오. 강은 강 나름의 깊이가 있고, 그도 그 나름의 깊이가 있는 거지요. 그의 시곗줄을 보시오. 그건 진짜배기요.\"\n\n\"꽤 묵직하더군요.\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"묵직하다?\" 웨믹이 되뇌었다. \"그렇소이다. 게다가 그 시계는 금딱지 리피터인데, 한 푼어치라도 값이 나간다면 백 파운드는 족히 나갈 물건이오. 핍 씨, 이 도시에는 그 시계에 대해 죄다 알고 있는 도둑이 칠백 명쯤 되는데, 그 가운데 남자건 여자건 아이건, 그 줄의 가장 작은 고리 하나라도 알아보지 못할 자가 없고, 또 어쩌다 그걸 만지게 꾐을 당한다면 마치 시뻘겋게 달군 쇠라도 되는 양 떨어뜨려 버리지 않을 자가 없을 거요.\"\n\n처음에는 그런 이야기로, 그다음에는 좀 더 일반적인 화제의 대화로, 웨믹 씨와 나는 시간과 길을 달래며 갔는데, 마침내 그가 우리가 월워스 지역에 다다랐음을 일러 주었다.\n\n그곳은 뒷골목과 도랑과 자그마한 텃밭들이 모여 있는 듯한 동네로, 다소 쓸쓸한 은거지 같은 모습을 띠고 있었다. 웨믹의 집은 텃밭들 한가운데 자리 잡은 작은 목조 오두막이었는데, 꼭대기는 마치 대포를 얹은 포대처럼 잘라 내고 칠을 해 두었다.\n\n\"내가 직접 한 거요.\" 웨믹이 말했다. \"보기 좋지요, 안 그렇소?\"\n\n나는 그것을 크게 칭찬했다. 내가 본 집 가운데 가장 작은 집이었지 싶다. 가장 기묘한 고딕식 창문이 달려 있었고(그 대부분은 가짜였지만), 들어가기에도 너무 작다 싶은 고딕식 문이 달려 있었다.\n\n\"저게 진짜 깃대요, 보이지요.\" 웨믹이 말했다. \"일요일이면 진짜 깃발을 올린다오. 그리고 여길 보시오. 내가 이 다리를 건너고 나면, 이렇게—이걸 끌어올려서—연락을 끊어 버리지요.\"\n\n그 다리란 널빤지 하나로, 너비 4피트에 깊이 2피트쯤 되는 도랑 위에 걸쳐져 있었다. 그러나 그가 그것을 끌어올려 단단히 고정시키는 그 자랑스러운 모습을 보는 것은 무척 흐뭇한 일이었다. 그는 그렇게 하면서, 단지 기계적으로가 아니라 진심으로 음미하는 듯 미소를 지었다.\n\n\"매일 밤 아홉 시, 그리니치 시간으로 말이오,\" 웨믹이 말했다. \"대포가 발사되오. 저기 있지요, 보이지요! 그놈이 발사되는 소리를 들으면, 당신도 저놈이 한 방 매운 놈이라고 할 거요.\"\n\n이른바 그 대포는 격자 세공으로 지은 따로 떨어진 요새 안에 얹혀 있었다. 그것은 우산처럼 생긴 기발하고 작은 방수포 장치로 비바람을 막아 두고 있었다.\n\n\"그리고 뒤편에는 말이오,\" 웨믹이 말했다. \"요새라는 발상에 방해가 되지 않도록 눈에 띄지 않는 곳에다—이건 내 신조인데, 어떤 발상이 있으면 그걸 실행에 옮기고 또 유지해야 하는 법이오—그게 당신 생각과 같은지는 모르겠소만——\"\n\n나는 단호히 같다고 말했다.\n\n\"—뒤편에는 돼지 한 마리가 있고, 닭과 토끼도 있소. 그리고 보다시피 직접 작은 틀을 짜 맞춰서 오이를 기르는데, 저녁때 내가 어떤 샐러드를 길러 내는지 당신이 평가해 보게 될 거요. 그러니 말이오,\" 하고 웨믹은 다시 미소를 지으면서도 진지하게 고개를 가로저으며 말했다. \"이 작은 곳이 포위당했다고 가정해 본다면, 식량 면에서 어지간히 오래 버텨 낼 수 있을 거요.\"\n\n그러고서 그는 열두 야드쯤 떨어진 정자로 나를 데려갔는데, 그 길이 어찌나 기발하게 구불구불 꺾여 있던지 거기에 이르는 데 꽤 오랜 시간이 걸렸다. 그리고 이 은신처에는 우리 술잔이 이미 차려져 있었다. 우리가 마실 펀치는 장식용 호수에 담겨 식고 있었는데, 정자는 바로 그 호수 가에 세워져 있었다. 이 호수는(한가운데에 섬이 하나 있었는데, 저녁 식사에 쓸 샐러드밭이었지 싶다) 원형이었고, 그는 그 안에 분수를 만들어 두었다. 작은 물레방아를 돌리고 관에서 마개를 뽑으면, 그 분수는 어찌나 세차게 솟구치는지 손등이 흠뻑 젖을 정도였다.\n\n\"나는 내 집 기사이자, 목수이자, 배관공이자, 정원사이자, 만물박이라오.\" 웨믹이 내 칭찬에 답하며 말했다. \"뭐, 이게 좋은 일이지요, 아시다시피. 뉴게이트의 거미줄을 털어 내고, 노친네를 즐겁게 해 드리니까. 곧장 노친네를 소개받는 건 꺼리지 않으시겠지요? 거북하지 않으시겠소?\"\n\n나는 기꺼이 그러겠노라는 뜻을 비쳤고, 우리는 성으로 들어갔다. 거기서 우리는 플란넬 외투를 입고 난롯가에 앉은 한 노인을 만났다. 깨끗하고, 쾌활하고, 편안하고, 보살핌을 잘 받고 있었으나, 귀가 심하게 먹은 노인이었다.\n\n\"안녕하시오, 늙으신 아버님,\" 하고 웨믹은 다정하면서도 익살스러운 투로 그와 악수하며 말했다. \"기분이 어떠시우?\"\n\n\"좋다, 존, 좋아!\" 노인이 대답했다.\n\n\"핍 씨가 오셨소, 아버님.\" 웨믹이 말했다. \"이분 성함을 들으실 수 있으면 좋으련만. 이분께 고개를 끄덕여 보이시오, 핍 씨. 그걸 좋아하시거든요. 부디 그분께 고개를 끄덕여 주시오, 눈 깜빡이듯 자주 말이오!\"\n\n\"이건 우리 아들이 마련한 훌륭한 곳이라오, 선생.\" 내가 있는 힘껏 고개를 끄덕이는 동안 노인이 큰 소리로 말했다. \"참 아름다운 유원지지요, 선생. 이 자리하며 그 위에 지어진 이 멋진 시설들은, 우리 아들이 세상을 뜬 뒤에도 백성들이 즐길 수 있도록 나라가 한데 보존해야 마땅하오.\"\n\n\"펀치 인형만큼이나 자랑스러워하시지요, 안 그러우, 아버님?\" 하고 웨믹은 노인을 바라보며 말했는데, 그 딱딱한 얼굴이 정말로 부드러워져 있었다. \"자, 끄덕여 드리리다,\" 하면서 어마어마하게 한 번 끄덕여 보이고, \"자, 한 번 더 끄덕여 드리지요,\" 하면서 한층 더 어마어마하게 끄덕여 보였다. \"이게 좋으시지요? 핍 씨, 만일 지치지 않았다면—낯선 사람에게는 지치는 일인 줄 알지만—한 번만 더 끄덕여 드리겠소? 이게 얼마나 그분을 기쁘게 하는지 당신은 짐작도 못 할 거요.\"\n\n나는 그에게 몇 번 더 끄덕여 보였고, 그러자 노인은 무척 기분이 좋아졌다. 우리는 그가 부지런히 일어나 닭에게 모이를 주러 가도록 내버려 두고, 정자에 자리를 잡고 앉아 펀치를 들었다. 거기서 웨믹은 파이프 담배를 피우며, 이 집터를 지금의 완성된 경지로 끌어올리기까지 여러 해가 걸렸노라고 내게 말해 주었다.\n\n\"이게 당신 소유의 것입니까, 웨믹 씨?\"\n\n\"오, 그렇소,\" 웨믹이 말했다. \"한 조각씩 한 조각씩 손에 넣었지요. 맙소사, 자유 보유지라오!\"\n\n\"정말입니까? 재거스 씨께서도 이걸 보면 감탄하시겠군요?\"\n\n\"본 적이 없소.\" 웨믹이 말했다. \"들은 적도 없고. 노친네를 본 적도 없고, 그분 얘기를 들은 적도 없지요. 천만에. 사무소는 사무소고, 사생활은 사생활이오. 사무소에 들어갈 때면 나는 성을 뒤에 두고 가고, 성에 들어올 때면 사무소를 뒤에 두고 오지요. 당신에게 조금이라도 거북한 일이 아니라면, 당신도 그렇게 해 주면 고맙겠소. 사무소에서 이 일이 입에 오르내리는 걸 원치 않거든요.\"\n\n물론 나는 그의 부탁을 지키는 일에 내 신의가 걸려 있다고 느꼈다. 펀치가 무척 맛이 좋아, 우리는 거기 앉아 그것을 마시며 이야기를 나누었고, 그러는 사이 거의 아홉 시가 다 되었다. \"대포 쏠 시간이 가까워지는군.\" 그때 웨믹이 파이프를 내려놓으며 말했다. \"노친네가 베푸는 선물이지요.\"\n\n다시 성 안으로 들어가 보니, 노친네가 이 거창한 밤의 의식을 거행하기에 앞서, 기대에 찬 눈빛으로 부지깽이를 달구고 있었다. 웨믹은 손에 시계를 든 채 서 있다가, 노친네에게서 시뻘겋게 단 부지깽이를 건네받아 포대로 갈 순간이 오기를 기다렸다. 그는 부지깽이를 받아 들고 밖으로 나갔고, 이윽고 그 매운 놈이 쾅 하고 터졌는데, 그 소리에 다 쓰러져 가는 상자 같은 작은 오두막이 산산조각이라도 날 듯 흔들렸고, 안에 있던 유리잔과 찻잔이 죄다 쟁그랑거렸다. 그러자 노친네는—팔걸이를 붙들고 있지 않았더라면 안락의자에서 날아가 버렸을 것이라고 나는 믿거니와—신이 나서 외쳤다. \"쏘았구나! 내가 들었어!\" 그리고 나는 그 노신사를 향해 어찌나 고개를 끄덕여 댔던지, 마침내는 그가 보이지조차 않게 되었다고 말한대도 결코 과장된 표현이 아닐 지경이었다.\n\n그때부터 저녁 식사까지의 짬을 웨믹은 자신의 진귀한 수집품을 내게 보여 주는 데 바쳤다. 그것들은 대개 흉악한 성질의 물건들로, 어느 유명한 위조 사건에 쓰인 펜이며, 이름난 면도칼 한두 개며, 머리카락 몇 타래며, 사형 선고를 받고 쓴 자필 고백서 몇 장이 들어 있었는데—이 고백서들을 웨믹 씨는 특히 소중히 여겼으니, 그의 표현을 그대로 빌리자면 \"하나같이 다 거짓말이거든요, 선생.\" 이런 까닭에서였다. 이것들 사이에는 작은 도자기와 유리 표본들이며, 이 박물관 주인이 손수 만든 갖가지 깔끔한 자질구레한 물건들이며, 노친네가 깎아 만든 담뱃대 다지개 몇 개가 보기 좋게 흩어져 있었다. 그것들은 죄다, 내가 처음 안내받아 들어갔던 그 성의 방 안에 진열되어 있었는데, 그 방은 단지 거실 노릇만 하는 게 아니라 부엌 노릇도 했으니, 화덕 선반 위의 냄비며, 고기 굽는 회전 꼬챙이를 걸어 두려고 벽난로 위에 마련해 둔 놋쇠 장식물로 미루어 그렇게 판단할 수 있었다.\n\n낮 동안 노친네를 돌보는 단정한 소녀가 하나 시중을 들고 있었다. 그 아이가 저녁 식탁보를 깔고 나자, 그 아이가 나갈 수 있도록 다리를 내려 주었고, 아이는 밤을 보내러 물러갔다. 저녁 식사는 훌륭했다. 그리고 성이 워낙 마른 썩음병에 시달리던 터라 상한 호두 같은 맛이 났고, 또 돼지가 좀 더 멀리 떨어져 있었더라면 좋았겠다 싶었음에도, 나는 그 모든 대접에 진심으로 흡족했다. 내 작은 망루 침실에도 흠잡을 데라곤 없었으니, 다만 나와 깃대 사이에 천장이 어찌나 얇았던지, 침대에 반듯이 누우면 밤새도록 그 깃대를 이마 위에 얹어 균형을 잡고 있어야 할 것만 같았던 것뿐이었다.\n\n웨믹은 아침 일찍 일어났는데, 안타깝게도 그가 내 구두를 닦는 소리가 들리는 듯했다. 그러고 나서 그는 정원 일에 들어갔고, 나는 내 고딕식 창문으로 그가 노친네에게 일을 시키는 척하면서 더없이 정성스러운 태도로 고개를 끄덕여 보이는 모습을 지켜보았다. 우리 아침 식사는 저녁 식사 못지않게 훌륭했고, 정확히 여덟 시 반에 우리는 리틀브리튼을 향해 출발했다. 길을 가는 동안 웨믹은 점점 메마르고 딱딱해졌으며, 그의 입은 다시 우편함 모양으로 굳게 다물어졌다. 마침내 그의 일터에 다다라 그가 외투 깃에서 열쇠를 꺼냈을 때, 그는 마치 성이며 도개교며 정자며 호수며 분수며 노친네가 모두 그 매운 놈의 마지막 발사와 함께 허공으로 날아가 버리기라도 한 듯, 자신의 월워스 소유지 따위는 까맣게 잊은 표정이었다.\n\n# Chapter XXVI.\n\n웨믹이 말한 그대로, 나는 일찍이 내 후견인의 살림살이를 그의 출납계 겸 서기의 살림살이와 비교해 볼 기회를 얻게 되었다. 내가 월워스에서 사무소로 들어섰을 때, 후견인은 자기 방에서 향내 나는 비누로 손을 씻고 있었다. 그는 나를 불러, 웨믹이 미리 받게 되리라고 일러 주었던 그 초대를 나와 내 친구들 앞으로 건넸다. \"격식은 차리지 말고,\" 그가 조건을 달았다. \"정장도 입지 말고, 내일이라고 전해라.\" 나는 어디로 가야 하느냐고 물었다(나는 그가 어디 사는지 전혀 몰랐으니까). 그러자 그는, 무언가를 인정하는 듯한 말은 일절 하지 않으려는 그 특유의 거부감 탓이었다고 나는 믿는데, 이렇게 대답했다. \"여기로 와라, 그러면 내가 너를 집으로 데려가마.\" 나는 이 기회를 빌려, 그가 마치 외과 의사나 치과 의사라도 되는 양 의뢰인들을 씻어 내곤 했다는 점을 적어 두고자 한다. 그의 방에는 그 목적에 맞게 꾸며진 벽장이 하나 있었는데, 향수 가게처럼 그 향내 나는 비누 냄새가 풍겼다. 문 안쪽에는 굴림대에 걸린 유난히 큰 수건이 하나 있었고, 그는 경찰 법정에서 돌아오거나 의뢰인을 방에서 내보낼 때마다 손을 씻고 그 수건에 두루 닦아 말리곤 했다. 이튿날 여섯 시에 나와 친구들이 그를 찾아갔을 때, 그는 평소보다 한층 음침한 빛깔의 사건을 맡고 있었던 모양이었다. 그가 그 벽장에 머리를 처박고 손을 씻을 뿐만 아니라 얼굴까지 씻고 목구멍까지 헹구고 있는 것을 보았으니 말이다. 그 모든 일을 마치고 그 수건에 두루 닦은 다음에도, 그는 주머니칼을 꺼내 외투를 걸치기 전에 손톱 밑에서 그 사건을 긁어냈다.\n\n우리가 거리로 나섰을 때, 여느 때처럼 몇몇 사람들이 슬그머니 어슬렁대고 있었는데, 그들은 분명 그와 말을 나누고 싶어 안달이 난 듯했다. 그러나 그의 존재를 둘러싼 향내 나는 비누의 후광에는 무언가 단호한 데가 있어서, 그들은 그날만은 단념하고 말았다. 우리가 서쪽으로 걸어가는 동안, 그는 거리의 군중 속 누군가의 얼굴에 이따금씩 알아보였고, 그럴 때마다 그는 내게 더 큰 목소리로 말을 건넸다. 그러나 그밖에는 누구도 알은체하지 않았고, 누가 자기를 알아보았다는 것조차 내색하지 않았다.\n\n그는 우리를 소호의 제라드 거리로, 그 거리 남쪽에 있는 어느 집으로 데려갔다. 그런 부류로서는 제법 위풍 있는 집이었으나, 페인트칠이 절실히 필요할 만큼 한심한 꼴이었고, 창문은 더러웠다. 그는 열쇠를 꺼내 문을 열었고, 우리 모두는 헐벗고 음울하며 거의 쓰지 않는 듯한 돌바닥 현관으로 들어섰다. 그러고는 거무튀튀한 갈색 계단을 올라, 이 층에 늘어선 거무튀튀한 갈색 방 셋으로 들어갔다. 판자를 댄 벽에는 새겨진 꽃 줄 장식이 있었는데, 그가 그 사이에 서서 우리를 맞이할 때, 나는 그것들이 내 눈에 어떤 종류의 올가미처럼 보였는지 잘 안다.\n\n저녁 식사는 그 방들 가운데 가장 좋은 방에 차려져 있었다. 둘째 방은 그의 옷 갈아입는 방이었고, 셋째 방은 침실이었다. 그는 우리에게 집 전체를 쓰고 있으나 우리가 본 것 이상은 좀처럼 쓰지 않는다고 말했다. 식탁은 안락하게 차려져 있었는데 — 물론 식기에 은제품은 없었다 — 그의 의자 옆에는 널찍한 보조 탁자가 있어, 그 위에는 갖가지 병과 디캔터가, 그리고 후식용 과일이 담긴 접시 네 개가 놓여 있었다. 나는 그가 시종일관 모든 것을 자기 손안에 두고 몸소 모든 것을 나눠 준다는 것을 알아챘다.\n\n방에는 책장이 하나 있었다. 나는 책등을 보고, 그것들이 증거, 형법, 범죄자 전기, 재판, 의회 법령 따위에 관한 것임을 알았다. 가구는 모두 그의 시곗줄처럼 아주 튼튼하고 좋았다. 그러나 어딘지 사무적인 분위기가 있었고, 단순히 장식만을 위한 것은 하나도 보이지 않았다. 한쪽 구석에는 갓을 씌운 등이 놓인 서류용 작은 탁자가 있었다. 그래서 그는 그 점에서도 사무소를 집으로 가져와, 저녁이면 그것을 굴려 내어 일에 매달리는 것 같았다.\n\n그는 지금껏 내 세 동행을 거의 본 적이 없었으므로 — 그와 나는 함께 걸어왔으니까 — 종을 울린 뒤 벽난로 앞 깔개에 선 채 그들을 샅샅이 뜯어보았다. 놀랍게도 그는 대번에, 오로지라고는 못해도 주로 드러믈에게 관심을 두는 듯했다.\n\n\"핍,\" 그가 커다란 손을 내 어깨에 얹고 나를 창가로 데려가며 말했다. \"누가 누군지 모르겠구나. 저 거미는 누구냐?\"\n\n\"거미요?\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"얼룩덜룩하고, 늘어져 있고, 뚱한 녀석 말이다.\"\n\n\"저 사람은 벤틀리 드러믈입니다.\" 내가 대답했다. \"섬세한 얼굴을 한 쪽이 스타톱이고요.\"\n\n\"섬세한 얼굴을 한 쪽\"에는 조금도 관심을 두지 않은 채, 그가 대꾸했다. \"벤틀리 드러믈이 저 녀석 이름이라, 그래? 저 녀석 생김새가 마음에 드는구나.\"\n\n그는 곧장 드러믈에게 말을 걸기 시작했다. 드러믈이 그 둔하고 과묵한 투로 대답하는 것에도 전혀 굴하지 않았고, 오히려 그 덕에 그에게서 말을 짜내는 데로 이끌려 가는 듯했다. 내가 그 둘을 바라보고 있을 때, 가정부가 식탁에 올릴 첫 접시를 들고 나와 그들 사이를 가로질렀다.\n\n내 짐작에 그녀는 마흔쯤 된 여자였다 — 하지만 실제보다 젊게 보았는지도 모른다. 키가 제법 크고, 몸매가 나긋나긋하고 날렵했으며, 몹시 창백하고, 빛바랜 큰 눈에, 머리카락이 흘러내릴 듯 풍성했다. 어떤 심장병이 있어서 그녀의 입술이 헐떡이는 듯 벌어져 있고 얼굴에 갑작스럽고 떨리는 듯한 묘한 표정이 어려 있었던 것인지는 알 수 없다. 그러나 나는 그 하루나 이틀 전 극장에서 「맥베스」를 보았는데, 그녀의 얼굴이 내게는 마치 마녀들의 솥에서 떠오르던 얼굴들처럼, 온통 불타는 공기에 휘저어진 것처럼 보였다는 것만큼은 안다.\n\n그녀는 접시를 내려놓고, 손가락으로 내 후견인의 팔을 조용히 건드려 식사가 준비되었음을 알리고는 사라졌다. 우리는 둥근 식탁에 자리를 잡았고, 후견인은 드러믈을 한쪽에, 스타톱을 다른 쪽에 두었다. 가정부가 식탁에 올린 것은 훌륭한 생선 요리였고, 뒤이어 똑같이 고른 양고기 한 덩이가, 그다음에는 똑같이 고른 새 요리가 나왔다. 소스, 포도주, 우리가 원하는 온갖 곁들이 음식이, 그것도 모두 최상품으로, 주인의 보조 탁자에서 내어졌다. 그것들이 식탁을 한 바퀴 돌고 나면 그는 언제나 다시 제자리에 돌려놓았다. 마찬가지로 그는 우리에게 각 코스마다 깨끗한 접시와 나이프와 포크를 나눠 주고, 방금 쓴 것들은 자기 의자 옆 바닥에 놓인 두 개의 바구니에 떨어뜨렸다. 가정부 외에는 시중드는 사람이 아무도 나타나지 않았다. 그녀는 접시를 일일이 차려 냈고, 나는 늘 그녀의 얼굴에서 그 솥에서 떠오르던 얼굴을 보았다. 여러 해가 지난 뒤, 나는 흘러내리는 머리카락 말고는 그 여자와 본디 닮은 데가 전혀 없는 어떤 얼굴을, 어두운 방에서 활활 타오르는 술 한 그릇 뒤로 지나가게 함으로써, 그 여자의 무서운 초상을 만들어 냈다.\n\n그녀 자신의 인상적인 외모와 웨믹의 사전 언질 양쪽에서 그 가정부를 각별히 눈여겨보게 된 나는, 그녀가 방 안에 있을 때면 언제나 후견인에게서 눈을 떼지 않는다는 것을, 그리고 그 앞에 내려놓은 어떤 접시에서든 손을 머뭇거리며 거두어들인다는 것을 알아챘다. 마치 그가 자기를 다시 부를까 두려워하고, 그가 무슨 할 말이 있다면 자기가 가까이 있을 때 말해 주기를 바라는 것처럼 말이다. 나는 그의 태도에서 이를 의식하고 있다는 것과, 그녀를 늘 마음 졸이게 두려는 의도를 간파할 수 있을 것만 같았다.\n\n저녁 식사는 흥겹게 진행되었고, 후견인은 화제를 꺼내기보다는 따라가는 듯 보였으나, 나는 그가 우리 성향에서 가장 약한 부분을 비틀어 끄집어내고 있음을 알았다. 나 자신으로 말하자면, 나는 입을 열었다는 것을 채 깨닫기도 전에 헤픈 씀씀이에 대한 내 성향과, 허버트를 후원하는 태도와, 내 대단한 장래에 대한 자랑을 늘어놓고 있었다. 우리 모두가 그랬지만, 드러믈만큼 심한 자는 없었다. 인색하고 의심 많은 투로 나머지 사람들을 헐뜯으려는 그의 성벽이 비틀려 나온 것은 생선 요리가 치워지기도 전이었다.\n\n화제가 우리의 조정 솜씨로 돌아간 것은 그때가 아니라 우리가 치즈에 이르렀을 때였는데, 그제야 드러믈은 밤에 그 느릿한 양서류 같은 방식으로 뒤쫓아 온다고 놀림을 받았다. 이에 드러믈은 주인에게, 자기는 우리와 어울리느니 우리 방을 훨씬 좋아한다고, 그리고 솜씨로 따지자면 자기는 우리보다 한 수 위라고, 또 힘으로 말하자면 우리를 검불처럼 흩어 버릴 수 있다고 알렸다. 어떤 보이지 않는 작용으로, 후견인은 이 사소한 일을 두고 그를 거의 사나움에 가까운 지경까지 부추겨 올렸다. 그러자 그는 자기 팔이 얼마나 근육질인지 보이려고 팔을 드러내고 한 뼘 한 뼘 재기 시작했고, 우리도 모두 우스꽝스러운 꼴로 팔을 드러내고 재기 시작했다.\n\n그때 가정부는 식탁을 치우고 있었다. 후견인은 그녀에게 아랑곳하지 않고, 얼굴을 그녀에게서 돌린 채, 의자에 기대앉아 집게손가락 옆을 깨물며, 내게는 도무지 이해할 수 없는 관심을 드러믈에게 보이고 있었다. 그러다가 갑자기, 그녀가 식탁 너머로 손을 뻗었을 때, 그는 커다란 손으로 덫처럼 그녀의 손을 덮쳤다. 어찌나 갑작스럽고 날쌔게 그리했던지, 우리 모두는 그 어리석은 다툼을 멈추고 말았다.\n\n\"힘 이야기를 하는 거라면,\" 재거스 씨가 말했다. \"내가 손목을 하나 보여 주지. 몰리, 이 사람들한테 네 손목을 보여 줘라.\"\n\n덫에 걸린 그녀의 손은 식탁 위에 있었으나, 그녀는 이미 다른 손을 허리 뒤로 돌려 두고 있었다. \"주인님,\" 그녀가 낮은 목소리로, 간절하게 그에게 시선을 고정한 채 말했다. \"그러지 마세요.\"\n\n\"내가 손목을 하나 보여 주겠다.\" 재거스 씨가 기어이 보여 주겠다는 흔들림 없는 결심으로 되풀이했다. \"몰리, 이 사람들한테 네 손목을 보여 줘.\"\n\n\"주인님,\" 그녀가 다시 중얼거렸다. \"제발요!\"\n\n\"몰리,\" 재거스 씨가 그녀를 보지 않고 방 맞은편을 고집스레 바라보며 말했다. \"이 사람들한테 양쪽 손목을 다 보여 줘. 보여 주라니까. 어서!\"\n\n그는 그녀의 손에서 자기 손을 떼고, 그 손목을 식탁 위에 뒤집어 보였다. 그녀는 뒤로 돌렸던 다른 손을 가져와, 두 손을 나란히 내밀었다. 나중에 내민 손목은 몹시 흉하게 일그러져 있었다 — 깊은 흉터가 가로세로로 어지러이 나 있었다. 두 손을 내밀면서 그녀는 재거스 씨에게서 눈을 떼어, 나머지 우리 한 사람 한 사람에게 차례로 경계하는 눈길을 돌렸다.\n\n\"여기에 힘이 있지.\" 재거스 씨가 집게손가락으로 힘줄을 더듬어 짚으며 냉정하게 말했다. \"이 여자만한 손목 힘을 가진 남자는 거의 없어. 이 손에 깃든 그저 움켜쥐는 힘만 해도 놀라울 정도지. 나는 숱한 손을 눈여겨볼 기회가 있었지만, 그 점에서 이보다 더 센 손은 남자든 여자든 본 적이 없다.\"\n\n그가 느긋하고 비평하는 듯한 투로 이런 말을 하는 동안, 그녀는 앉아 있는 우리 한 사람 한 사람을 차례차례 계속 바라보았다. 그가 말을 멈추는 순간, 그녀는 다시 그를 바라보았다. \"됐다, 몰리.\" 재거스 씨가 그녀에게 가볍게 고갯짓하며 말했다. \"감탄을 받았으니, 이제 가도 좋다.\" 그녀가 손을 거두고 방을 나가자, 재거스 씨는 보조 탁자에서 디캔터를 꺼내 놓고, 자기 잔을 채운 다음 포도주를 돌렸다.\n\n\"여러분,\" 그가 말했다. \"아홉 시 반에는 우리가 자리를 파해야 합니다. 부디 시간을 알차게 쓰시오. 모두 만나서 반갑소. 드러믈 군, 자네를 위해 한잔하지.\"\n\n드러믈을 골라낸 그의 목적이 그를 한층 더 끌어내려는 것이었다면, 그것은 완벽하게 성공한 셈이었다. 뚱한 승리감에 젖은 드러믈은, 나머지 우리에 대한 침울한 멸시를 점점 더 고약한 정도로 드러내더니, 마침내 도저히 견딜 수 없는 지경에 이르렀다. 그 모든 단계 내내, 재거스 씨는 똑같은 기이한 관심으로 그를 좇았다. 그는 정말로 재거스 씨의 포도주에 풍미를 더해 주는 양념 노릇을 하는 듯했다.\n\n소년다운 분별없음에서 우리는 술을 너무 많이 마셨던 것 같고, 너무 많이 떠들어 댔던 것은 분명하다. 우리가 돈을 너무 헤프게 쓴다는 드러믈의 어떤 상스러운 빈정거림을 두고 우리는 유난히 발끈했다. 그 때문에 나는, 분별보다 열의가 앞서서, 그런 말은 그에게 어울리지 않게 못난 소리라고, 바로 한 주쯤 전에 내가 보는 앞에서 스타톱이 그에게 돈을 빌려주지 않았느냐고 쏘아붙였다.\n\n\"흥,\" 드러믈이 받아쳤다. \"갚을 거다.\"\n\n\"안 갚을 거라는 말이 아니야.\" 내가 말했다. \"다만 그러면 자네가 우리와 우리 돈 이야기에 입을 다물게 될 만도 하다는 거지, 내 생각엔.\"\n\n\"네 생각엔!\" 드러믈이 받아쳤다. \"원, 맙소사!\"\n\n\"내 짐작엔 말이야,\" 나는 아주 매섭게 굴 작정으로 말을 이었다. \"우리 중 누가 돈이 필요해도 자네는 빌려주지 않겠지.\"\n\n\"맞아.\" 드러믈이 말했다. \"나는 너희 중 누구한테도 육 펜스 한 푼 빌려주지 않을 거다. 나는 누구한테도 육 펜스 한 푼 빌려주지 않아.\"\n\n\"그런 처지라면 빌리는 건 좀 치사한 짓이라고 해야겠는데, 내 생각엔.\"\n\n\"네 생각엔.\" 드러믈이 되풀이했다. \"원, 맙소사!\"\n\n이건 어찌나 부아가 치미는 일이었던지 — 그의 뚱한 둔감함에 내가 도무지 당해 내지 못하는 것을 깨닫자 더더욱 그러했는데 — 나는 나를 말리려는 허버트의 애씀을 무시하고 말했다.\n\n\"이봐, 드러믈, 이왕 이 이야기가 나왔으니, 자네가 그 돈을 빌릴 때 여기 허버트와 나 사이에 오간 말을 들려주지.\"\n\n\"나는 거기 허버트와 너 사이에 무슨 말이 오갔는지 알고 싶지 않아.\" 드러믈이 으르렁댔다. 그리고 우리 둘 다 악마에게나 가서 몸이나 떨라는 말을 더 낮게 으르렁대며 덧붙인 것 같았다.\n\n\"하지만 들려주지.\" 내가 말했다. \"자네가 알고 싶든 말든. 우리는 이렇게 말했어. 자네가 그 돈을 받게 되어 무척 기뻐하며 주머니에 넣을 때, 그렇게 멍청하게 돈을 빌려주는 그가 자네한테는 더없이 우습게 보이는 모양이라고 말이야.\"\n\n드러믈은 대놓고 웃음을 터뜨리며, 두 손을 주머니에 찌르고 둥근 어깨를 추켜올린 채, 우리 면전에서 웃어 댔다. 그것이 전적으로 사실이며, 자기는 우리를 모두 멍청이로 여겨 경멸한다는 뜻을 분명히 내비치면서.\n\n이때 스타톱이, 내가 보인 것보다는 훨씬 점잖은 태도로, 그를 다루어 좀 더 상냥하게 굴라고 타일렀다. 스타톱은 활기차고 명랑한 청년이었고 드러믈은 정반대였던 터라, 후자는 늘 전자를 마치 직접적인 인신공격이라도 되는 양 여겨 발끈하는 기질이 있었다. 그는 이제 거칠고 둔중한 투로 받아쳤고, 스타톱은 우리 모두를 웃게 만든 어떤 자그마한 농담으로 그 논쟁을 슬쩍 비껴 보려 했다. 이 소소한 성공에 무엇보다 발끈한 드러믈은, 아무런 위협도 경고도 없이 주머니에서 두 손을 빼고, 둥근 어깨를 내리고, 욕설을 내뱉고, 커다란 잔을 집어 들어 상대의 머리를 향해 내던지려 했다. 그러나 바로 그 목적으로 그것을 치켜든 순간, 우리 주최자가 날쌔게 그것을 낚아챘다.\n\n\"여러분,\" 재거스 씨가 잔을 천천히 내려놓고 묵직한 줄에 매단 금시계를 꺼내며 말했다. \"심히 유감스럽지만 아홉 시 반이 되었음을 알리는 바입니다.\"\n\n이 암시에 우리는 모두 일어나 떠날 채비를 했다. 우리가 길로 난 현관문에 이르기도 전에, 스타톱은 아무 일도 없었다는 듯 명랑하게 드러믈을 \"이 친구야\" 하고 불렀다. 그러나 그 친구는 응하기는커녕, 같은 길가 쪽으로 해머스미스까지 함께 걸으려 하지도 않았다. 그래서 시내에 남은 허버트와 나는 그 둘이 거리 양편으로 갈라져 내려가는 것을 보았다. 스타톱이 앞장서고, 드러믈은 집들의 그림자 속에서 뒤처져 갔는데, 그것은 그가 평소 배에서 따라오던 모습과 흡사했다.\n\n문이 아직 닫히지 않았기에, 나는 잠시 허버트를 거기 남겨 두고 다시 위층으로 뛰어올라가 후견인에게 한마디 하고 와야겠다고 생각했다. 나는 그가 옷 갈아입는 방에서 쌓아 둔 장화들에 둘러싸인 채, 이미 한창 우리에게서 손을 씻고 있는 것을 보았다.\n\n나는 다시 올라온 것은 무언가 불쾌한 일이 벌어진 것이 정말 죄송해서이며, 부디 나를 너무 탓하지 않으셨으면 한다고 말했다.\n\n\"흥!\" 그가 얼굴에 물을 끼얹고 물방울 사이로 말했다. \"별일 아니다, 핍. 그래도 나는 그 거미가 마음에 들어.\"\n\n그는 이제 내 쪽으로 돌아서서 고개를 흔들며, 푸푸 소리를 내고, 수건으로 몸을 닦고 있었다.\n\n\"마음에 드신다니 다행이군요, 선생님.\" 내가 말했다. \"하지만 저는 아닙니다.\"\n\n\"그래, 그래.\" 후견인이 맞장구쳤다. \"그 녀석과 너무 어울리지는 마라. 되도록 멀리 떨어져 지내. 하지만 나는 그 녀석이 마음에 들어, 핍. 그는 진짜배기 부류야. 거참, 내가 점쟁이라면—\"\n\n수건 사이로 내다보다가, 그는 내 눈과 마주쳤다.\n\n\"하지만 나는 점쟁이가 아니지.\" 그가 고개를 수건 자락 속으로 떨구고 두 귀를 닦아 대며 말했다. \"내가 뭐 하는 사람인지는 알지, 안 그러냐? 잘 가라, 핍.\"\n\n\"안녕히 계세요, 선생님.\"\n\n그로부터 한 달쯤 뒤, 그 거미가 포켓 씨 밑에서 보내던 시간이 영영 끝났고, 포켓 부인을 뺀 온 집안의 큰 안도 속에, 그는 자기 집안의 소굴로 돌아갔다.\n\n이 글은 가저리 씨의 부탁으로 대신 쓰는 것으로, 그분이 워프슬 씨와 함께 런던에 가시게 되었고, 괜찮으시다면 도련님을 뵙고 싶어 하신다는 것을 알려 드리려는 것입니다. 그분은 화요일 아침 아홉 시에 바너드 호텔로 찾아가실 예정이오니, 만약 곤란하시면 부디 그곳에 말을 남겨 주십시오. 가엾은 누님은 도련님이 떠나실 때와 별반 다름없으십니다. 우리는 매일 밤 부엌에서 도련님 이야기를 나누며, 도련님이 무슨 말을 하시고 무엇을 하고 계실까 궁금해합니다. 혹시 이것이 주제넘은 일로 여겨지신다면, 정다웠던 옛 시절을 생각하시어 너그러이 용서해 주십시오. 더 드릴 말씀 없이, 친애하는 핍 도련님,\n\n언제나 은혜를 입은, 도련님을 아끼는 하인,\n비디 올림.\n\n추신. 그분은 부디 무슨 신난다는 말을 꼭 적어 달라고 신신당부하셨습니다. 도련님이라면 아실 거라고 하십니다. 비록 신사가 되셨다 해도 도련님은 늘 마음씨가 고우셨으니, 그분을 뵙는 일이 도련님께 즐거우시리라 저는 바라고 또 의심치 않습니다. 그분은 참으로, 참으로 훌륭한 분이십니다. 저는 마지막 짧은 한 문장만 빼고 이 편지를 그분께 전부 읽어 드렸는데, 그분은 부디 무슨 신난다는 말을 다시 한번 꼭 적어 달라고 신신당부하셨습니다.」\n\n나는 이 편지를 월요일 아침 우편으로 받았으니, 그 약속은 바로 다음 날로 잡힌 셈이었다. 내가 조의 방문을 어떤 심정으로 기다렸는지 정확히 고백해 두기로 하자.\n\n기쁜 마음은 아니었다, 비록 그토록 많은 인연으로 그와 맺어져 있었음에도. 그렇다, 적잖은 동요와 약간의 굴욕감, 그리고 어울리지 않는다는 날카로운 느낌을 안고서였다. 돈을 치러 그가 오지 않게 할 수만 있었다면, 나는 분명히 돈을 치렀을 것이다. 가장 큰 위안이라면, 그가 해머스미스가 아니라 바너드 인으로 온다는 것, 따라서 벤틀리 드러믈과 마주칠 일이 없으리라는 점이었다. 그가 허버트나 그의 아버지의 눈에 띄는 것에는 별 거리낌이 없었으니, 두 사람 모두 내가 존경하는 이들이었기 때문이다. 그러나 내가 경멸하던 드러믈의 눈에 그가 띄는 것에는 더없이 예민하게 반응했다. 이렇듯 우리는 평생토록, 가장 멸시하는 사람들을 위하여 가장 추악한 약점과 비열함을 저지르는 법이다.\n\n나는 어느샌가 늘 그 방을 무어라 말할 수 없이 불필요하고 어울리지 않는 방식으로 꾸미기 시작했는데, 바너드를 상대로 한 그 씨름은 결국 무척이나 비싼 것으로 드러났다. 이 무렵에 이르러 그 방들은 내가 처음 보았던 모습과는 판이하게 달라져 있었고, 나는 이웃 가구상의 장부에 몇 페이지나 떡하니 이름을 올리는 영예를 누리고 있었다. 근래에 어찌나 빨리 일을 벌였던지, 나는 심지어 부츠를 신은 소년 하나를 — 그것도 목이 긴 부츠를 — 부리기 시작했으니, 그 아이의 속박과 노예 노릇에 매여 나날을 보냈다고 해도 좋을 정도였다. 왜냐하면 나는 그 괴물을 (세탁부 식구 가운데 남아도는 아이로) 만들어 내고, 파란 윗도리와 카나리아빛 조끼, 흰 넥타이, 크림빛 반바지, 그리고 앞서 말한 부츠로 차려 입힌 뒤에는, 아이에게 줄 일거리를 조금, 그리고 먹일 음식을 잔뜩 마련해 주어야 했기 때문이다. 그 끔찍한 두 가지 요건으로 그 아이는 내 삶을 따라다니며 괴롭혔다.\n\n이 복수의 망령은 화요일 아침 여덟 시에 현관에서 (그 현관은 가로세로 두 자 넓이로, 바닥 깔개 값으로 청구된 만큼이었다) 대기하라는 명을 받았고, 허버트는 조가 좋아하리라 여겨지는 몇 가지 음식을 아침 식사로 제안했다. 그가 그토록 관심을 가지고 사려 깊게 굴어 주는 것에 진심으로 고마움을 느끼면서도, 한편으로는 묘하게 반쯤 부아가 치미는 의심이 들었으니, 만약 조가 그를 만나러 오는 길이었다면 그가 이토록 활기차게 굴지는 않았으리라는 것이었다.\n\n어쨌든 나는 조를 맞을 채비를 하려고 월요일 밤 시내로 들어왔고, 아침 일찍 일어나 거실과 아침 식탁을 더없이 화려한 모습으로 꾸며 놓았다. 공교롭게도 아침에는 이슬비가 내렸으니, 바너드가 창밖에서 마치 굴뚝 청소부 같은 어느 허약한 거인처럼 검댕의 눈물을 흘리고 있다는 사실은 천사라 해도 감출 수 없었을 것이다.\n\n시간이 다가오자 나는 달아나고 싶었으나, 명령에 따라 복수자는 현관에 버티고 있었고, 이윽고 나는 계단에서 조의 기척을 들었다. 그가 조라는 것을 나는 알았으니, 그가 계단을 올라오는 그 어설픈 몸짓으로 — 그의 나들이 부츠는 늘 그에게 너무 컸으니까 — 그리고 그가 올라오는 동안 다른 층의 문패에 적힌 이름들을 읽느라 들이는 시간으로 알 수 있었다. 마침내 그가 우리 집 문밖에서 멈춰 섰을 때, 나는 그의 손가락이 페인트로 그려진 내 이름의 글자들을 더듬어 가는 소리를 들을 수 있었고, 그러고 나서 그가 열쇠 구멍에 대고 숨을 들이쉬는 소리를 또렷이 들었다. 마침내 그가 희미하게 한 번 문을 두드렸고, 페퍼가 — 그것이 그 복수의 소년을 두고 타협 끝에 붙인 이름이었다 — \"가저리 씨!\"라고 알렸다. 나는 그가 발을 닦는 일을 영영 끝내지 못할 것만 같아, 내가 나가서 그를 매트에서 들어 올려야 하나 싶었으나, 그는 마침내 들어왔다.\n\n\"조, 잘 지냈어, 조?\"\n\n\"핍, 자아네 잘 지냈는가, 핍?\"\n\n그 선량하고 정직한 얼굴이 온통 붉게 빛나고 환히 반짝이는 가운데, 모자를 우리 사이 바닥에 내려놓은 채, 그는 내 두 손을 붙들고 위아래로 곧장 펌프질하듯 흔들어 댔으니, 마치 내가 막 특허를 받은 최신식 펌프라도 되는 양했다.\n\n\"만나서 반가워, 조. 모자 이리 줘.\"\n\n그러나 조는 그것을, 알이 든 새 둥지라도 되는 양 두 손으로 조심스레 집어 들면서, 그 소중한 물건을 내놓는다는 말은 들으려고도 하지 않았고, 한사코 그것을 끌어안은 채 서서 더없이 거북한 모양으로 이야기를 이어 갔다.\n\n\"이렇게나 자라고,\" 조가 말했다. \"이렇게나 늠름해지고, 이렇게나 신사티가 나고,\" 조는 이 말을 찾아내기까지 잠시 궁리했다. \"그러니 자네야말로 임금님과 나라의 자랑거리가 분명하다네.\"\n\n\"형도, 조, 아주 좋아 보여.\"\n\n\"고맙게도,\" 조가 말했다. \"나야 남들만큼은 거뜬하지. 그리고 자네 누님은, 자네가 떠날 때보다 나빠지진 않았어. 그리고 비디는, 늘 한결같이 미덥고 빈틈없고. 그리고 친구들은 죄다, 나아진 건 없어도 못해지진 않았지. 워프슬만 빼고. 그 사람은 한바탕 떨어져 나갔거든.\"\n\n이러는 내내 (여전히 두 손으로 새 둥지를 끔찍이도 소중히 받쳐 든 채) 조는 두 눈을 굴려 방 안을 빙빙 둘러보고, 또 내 실내복의 꽃무늬를 빙빙 둘러보고 있었다.\n\n\"떨어져 나갔다고, 조?\"\n\n\"아 그게 말이지,\" 조가 목소리를 낮추며 말했다. \"그 사람이 교회를 떠나서 연극에 발을 들였어. 그 연극이 또 마찬가지로 그 사람을 나하고 같이 런던까지 데려온 거고. 그리고 그 사람의 바람은 말이야,\" 조는 잠시 새 둥지를 왼팔 밑으로 옮겨 끼고 오른손으로 그 안에서 알을 더듬어 찾으며 말했다. \"괜찮다면, 이걸 자네한테 건네주라는 거였어.\"\n\n나는 조가 건넨 것을 받아 들었는데, 그것은 어느 작은 시내 극장의 구겨진 연극 전단으로, 바로 그 주에 \"로스키우스의 명성에 빛나는 저명한 지방 아마추어 배우\"가 처음으로 무대에 오른다고 알리는 것이었으니, \"우리 민족 시인의 가장 드높은 비극 영역에서 펼치는 그 독보적 연기가 근래 이 지방 연극계에 그토록 큰 반향을 불러일으킨\" 인물이라고 했다.\n\n\"형도 그 사람 공연을 봤어, 조?\" 내가 물었다.\n\n\"보았지.\" 조가 힘주어, 그리고 자못 엄숙하게 말했다.\n\n\"큰 반향이 일었어?\"\n\n\"아 그게 말이지,\" 조가 말했다. \"그래, 분명히 오렌지 껍질이 한 무더기 날아들기는 했어. 특히 그 사람이 유령을 봤을 때 말이야. 하지만 자네한테 한번 물어보겠네만, 도련님, 한 사람이 좋은 기운으로 제 일에 몰두하려는데, 그 사람과 유령 사이에 끊임없이 '아멘!' 하고 끼어드는 게 과연 그 사람한테 도움이 될 만한 짓이었겠나? 사람이 불운을 겪고 교회에 몸담은 적이 있다고 해서,\" 조가 목소리를 따지듯, 그러면서도 사뭇 감정에 젖은 어조로 낮추며 말했다. \"그렇다고 해서 그런 때에 그 사람을 야유해 쫓아낼 이유는 못 되는 거 아닌가. 그러니까 내 말은, 제 친아버지의 유령조차 그 사람의 주목을 끌어선 안 된다면, 대체 뭐가 끌 수 있다는 건가, 도련님? 더구나 그 사람 상복 모자가 공교롭게도 어찌나 작게 만들어졌던지, 검은 깃털 무게에 자꾸 벗겨져서, 아무리 붙들어 두려 애써도 소용이 없는 마당에 말이야.\"\n\n조의 얼굴에 마치 유령이라도 본 듯한 빛이 떠오르는 것을 보고, 나는 허버트가 방에 들어왔음을 알았다. 그래서 나는 조를 허버트에게 소개했고, 허버트는 손을 내밀었으나, 조는 그 손을 피해 뒷걸음질 치며 새 둥지를 꼭 붙들었다.\n\n\"안녕하십니까, 선생님,\" 조가 말했다. \"부디 선생님과 핍이\" — 여기서 그의 눈길이, 토스트를 식탁에 올려놓고 있던 복수자에게 떨어졌는데, 그 어린 신사를 한 식구로 끼워 줄 작정임을 어찌나 빤히 드러냈던지, 나는 인상을 찌푸려 그를 단념시켰고 그 바람에 그를 더욱 당황하게 만들었다 — \"그러니까 내 말은, 두 분 신사께서, 이 비좁은 데서 건강은 잘 챙기고 계신지요? 지금 이곳이 런던 사람들 생각으로야 아주 훌륭한 여관일는지 모르고,\" 조가 은밀하게 말했다. \"그 평판이 그만하다는 것도 믿습니다만, 저 같으면 여기서 돼지 한 마리도 안 키울 겁니다요. 그놈을 건강하게 살찌워 부드러운 풍미가 돌게 잡아먹고 싶은 경우라면 더더욱 말입니다요.\"\n\n우리 거처의 장점에 대해 이토록 우쭐한 증언을 늘어놓고, 또 그 와중에 나를 \"도련님\"이라 부르려는 이 버릇을 내비친 끝에, 조는 식탁에 앉으라는 권유를 받고서, 모자를 내려놓을 만한 적당한 자리를 찾아 방 안을 두루 둘러보았으니 — 마치 자연계의 지극히 희귀한 몇몇 물질 위에서만 그것이 안식처를 찾을 수 있기라도 한 듯했다 — 결국에는 벽난로 선반 맨 끝 모서리에 그것을 세워 놓았고, 그 뒤로 그것은 시도 때도 없이 거기서 굴러떨어졌다.\n\n\"차를 드시겠습니까, 아니면 커피를 드시겠습니까, 가저리 씨?\" 늘 아침 식탁을 주관하던 허버트가 물었다.\n\n\"고맙습니다, 선생님,\" 조가 머리끝부터 발끝까지 뻣뻣하게 굳은 채 말했다. \"선생님께 가장 편하신 쪽으로 아무거나 들겠습니다요.\"\n\n\"커피는 어떠십니까?\"\n\n\"고맙습니다, 선생님,\" 조가, 그 제안에 분명히 풀이 죽은 기색으로 대답했다. \"선생님께서 그렇게 친절히 커피를 골라 주시니, 저야 선생님 뜻을 거스를 생각이 없습니다요. 한데 커피는 좀 속이 타는 것 같지 않습니까요?\"\n\n\"그럼 차로 하시지요.\" 허버트가 차를 따르며 말했다.\n\n이때 조의 모자가 벽난로 선반에서 굴러떨어졌고, 그는 의자에서 벌떡 일어나 그것을 주워 다시 똑같은 그 자리에 정확히 맞춰 놓았다. 마치 그것이 곧 다시 굴러떨어지는 것이 절대적인 예법의 요체이기라도 한 양했다.\n\n\"언제 시내에 오셨습니까, 가저리 씨?\"\n\n\"그게 어제 오후였던가?\" 조가 손으로 입을 가리고 기침을 하고서 말했는데, 마치 그가 온 뒤로 백일해라도 옮을 만한 시간이 흐른 것 같았다. \"아니, 아니었지. 맞아, 그랬어. 맞아. 어제 오후였어.\" (지혜로움과 안도감, 그리고 엄정한 공정함이 한데 뒤섞인 듯한 표정을 지으며.)\n\n\"런던 구경은 좀 하셨습니까?\"\n\n\"아 그게, 그럼요, 선생님,\" 조가 말했다. \"나하고 워프슬이 곧장 구두약 창고를 보러 갔지요. 한데 그게 가게 문마다 붙은 빨간 광고지에 그려진 것만 못하더란 말입니다요. 그러니까 내 말은,\" 조가 설명조로 덧붙였다. \"거기엔 너무 건추웅저억으로 그려 놨거든요.\"\n\n* 구두약 창고: 디킨스 자신이 소년 시절 일했던 워런 구두약 공장을 떠올리게 하는 대목이다.\n나는 조가 정말로 이 말을 (내가 아는 어떤 건축 양식을 두고 내 머릿속에 자못 강렬한 인상을 주는 말이었다) 늘여 빼서 온전한 합창으로까지 만들었을 것이라 정말로 믿는데, 다행히도 그의 주의가 위태롭게 흔들리던 모자에 끌린 덕분에 그러지 못했다. 사실 그 모자는 그에게 끊임없는 주의와, 위켓키퍼에게 요구되는 것과 흡사한 눈과 손의 민첩함을 요구했다. 그는 그 모자를 가지고 비상한 묘기를 부렸고 더없는 솜씨를 선보였으니, 어느 때는 그것이 떨어질 때 달려들어 깔끔히 낚아챘고, 어느 때는 떨어지는 중간에 그저 멈춰 세웠다가 다시 쳐올려, 방 안 여기저기서, 또 벽지 무늬의 상당 부분을 상대로 그것을 어르고 달랜 끝에야 안전하게 거머쥘 만하다고 여겼으며, 끝내는 그것을 개숫물 대접 속에 첨벙 빠뜨렸으니, 그제야 나는 외람되이 손을 뻗어 그것을 집어 들었다.\n\n그의 셔츠 깃과 윗도리 깃으로 말하자면, 곰곰이 생각해 볼수록 사람을 어리둥절하게 만드는 것이었다 — 둘 다 풀 길 없는 수수께끼였다. 어찌하여 사람이 자신을 완전히 차려입었다고 여기기까지 그토록 제 몸을 박박 긁어야 한단 말인가? 어찌하여 그는 나들이옷을 위해 고통을 겪어 가며 정화되어야 한다고 여겼던 것일까? 그러더니 그는, 포크를 접시와 입 중간쯤에 든 채로 영문 모를 명상에 빠져들곤 했고, 두 눈은 야릇한 방향으로 끌려가곤 했으며, 놀랄 만한 기침에 시달렸고, 식탁에서 한참이나 떨어져 앉아, 먹는 것보다 흘리는 것이 훨씬 많았으면서도 그것을 흘리지 않은 척했으니, 허버트가 우리를 두고 시티로 떠났을 때 나는 진심으로 마음이 놓였다.\n\n이 모든 것이 다 내 잘못이라는 것을, 그리고 내가 조를 더 편하게 대했더라면 조도 나를 더 편하게 대했으리라는 것을 알 만한 분별도, 그만한 선량한 마음씨도 나는 갖추지 못했다. 나는 그가 답답하게 느껴졌고 그에게 짜증이 났으니, 그러는 와중에 그는 내 머리 위에 숯불을 쌓아 올린 셈이었다.\n* 머리 위에 숯불을 쌓다: 성경 잠언의 구절로, 악을 선으로 갚아 상대를 부끄럽게 한다는 뜻이다.\n\n\"이제 우리 둘만 남았으니, 도련님\" — 조가 입을 열었다.\n\n\"조,\" 나는 짜증스레 말을 가로막았다. \"어떻게 나를 도련님이라 부를 수 있어?\"\n\n조는 잠깐 동안 무언가 어렴풋이 책망하는 듯한 빛으로 나를 바라보았다. 그의 넥타이가, 또 그의 깃이 아무리 터무니없었다 해도, 나는 그 눈빛에서 일종의 위엄을 느꼈다.\n\n\"이제 우리 둘만 남았으니,\" 조가 다시 입을 열었다. \"그리고 나로서는 몇 분 더 머물 생각도 그럴 처지도 못 되니, 이제 마무리 짓겠네 — 아니, 시작하겠네 — 무엇이 나로 하여금 오늘 이 영광을 누리게 했는지를 말이야. 왜냐하면,\" 조가 늘 그러던 또렷한 설명조로 말했다. \"자네에게 도움이 되고 싶다는 것이 내 유일한 바람이 아니었더라면, 나는 신사들과 한자리에서, 신사들의 거처에서 음식을 나누는 영광 따위 누리지 않았을 테니까.\"\n\n나는 그 눈빛을 다시 보기가 너무도 싫었던 터라, 이 호칭에 대해 한마디도 따지지 않았다.\n\n\"그래서 말이지, 도련님,\" 조가 말을 이었다. \"사정은 이러했어. 며칠 전 밤에 나는 뱃사람 주막에 있었거든, 핍.\" — 그가 다정한 마음으로 가라앉을 때면 그는 나를 핍이라 불렀고, 다시 예의로 되돌아갈 때면 도련님이라 불렀다 — \"그때 펌블축이 제 마차를 몰고 나타난 거야. 바로 그 작자가 말이야,\" 조가 새로운 갈래로 빠지며 말했다. \"이따금 끔찍하게 내 비위를 거꾸로 긁어 놓는단 말이지. 온 동네방네 떠들고 다니길, 자네 어린 시절 동무가 자기였다는 둥, 자네가 자기를 놀이 친구로 여겼다는 둥 하면서 말이야.\"\n\n\"말도 안 돼. 그건 형이었잖아, 조.\"\n\n\"나도 진작에 그렇게 굳게 믿었지, 핍,\" 조가 고개를 슬쩍 젖히며 말했다. \"이제 와선 별 의미 없는 일이다만, 도련님. 어쨌든, 핍. 바로 그 작자가, 본디 으스대는 버릇이 있는 그 작자가, 뱃사람 주막에 있는 나한테 와서는 (담배 한 대와 맥주 한 잔이 일하는 사람한테 기운을 돋워 주거든, 도련님, 과하게 자극하지도 않고 말이야), 이러는 거야, '조지프, 해비셤 아씨께서 자네하고 얘기를 나누고 싶어 하시네.'\"\n\n\"해비셤 아씨가, 조?\"\n\n\"'얘기를 나누고 싶어 하시네,' 그게 펌블축의 말이었어, '자네하고.'\" 조는 앉은 채로 두 눈을 굴려 천장을 올려다보았다.\n\n\"그래서, 조? 어서 계속해 봐.\"\n\n\"이튿날, 도련님,\" 조가, 마치 내가 저 멀리 떨어져 있기라도 한 듯 나를 바라보며 말했다. \"몸을 씻고 나서 나는 가서 A 아씨를 뵈었지.\"\n\n\"A 아씨라니, 조? 해비셤 아씨 말이야?\"\n\n\"내 말이 그 말이라네, 도련님,\" 조가, 마치 유언장이라도 작성하는 양 법률적 격식을 갖춘 태도로 대답했다. \"A 아씨, 다른 말로 해비셤 아씨 말이야. 그분 말씀이 그러니까 이러했어. '가저리 씨. 핍 씨와 서신을 주고받고 계신가요?' 자네한테서 편지를 받았던 터라, 나는 '그렇습니다'라고 말할 수 있었지. (자네 누님과 결혼할 때, 도련님, 나는 '그러겠습니다'라고 했고, 자네 친구한테 대답할 때, 핍, 나는 '그렇습니다'라고 했어.) '그럼,' 그분이 말씀하시길, '에스텔라가 집에 돌아왔고 그를 만나면 기뻐할 거라고 전해 주시겠어요?'\"\n\n조를 바라보는 동안 나는 얼굴이 확 달아오르는 것을 느꼈다. 그 한 가지 먼 원인이, 만약 그의 용건을 미리 알았더라면 그를 더 격려해 주었을 텐데 하는 자각이었기를 나는 바란다.\n\n\"비디는,\" 조가 말을 이었다. \"내가 집에 돌아와 자네한테 그 전갈을 적어 달라고 부탁했더니, 좀 머뭇거리더군. 비디가 그러길, '직접 입으로 들으면 그분이 무척 기뻐하실 거예요. 마침 휴가철이고, 당신도 그분을 보고 싶어 하시잖아요. 가세요!' 이제 다 마쳤네, 도련님,\" 조가 의자에서 일어서며 말했다. \"그리고, 핍, 나는 자네가 언제까지나 잘 지내고, 언제까지나 점점 더 높은 곳으로 번창해 가기를 비네.\"\n\n\"하지만 지금 가는 건 아니지, 조?\"\n\n\"가야지.\" 조가 말했다.\n\n\"하지만 저녁 식사 때 다시 올 거지, 조?\"\n\n\"아니, 안 와.\" 조가 말했다.\n\n우리의 눈이 마주쳤고, 그가 내게 손을 내밀 때 그 사내다운 마음에서 \"도련님\"이라는 말은 죄다 녹아내렸다.\n\n\"핍, 정다운 친구, 인생이란, 말하자면, 헤아릴 수 없이 많은 이별이 한데 용접되어 이루어진 것이라네. 그리고 어떤 사람은 대장장이고, 어떤 사람은 양철장이고, 어떤 사람은 금세공장이고, 어떤 사람은 구리세공장이지. 그런 사람들 사이에 갈라짐이란 으레 오게 마련이고, 오는 대로 맞이해야 하는 거야. 오늘 무슨 잘못이 조금이라도 있었다면, 그건 내 탓이야. 자네와 나는 런던에서 함께 어울릴 두 사람이 못 돼. 친구들 사이에서 은밀하고, 잘 알려지고, 이해되는 자리가 아닌 다른 어디에서도 마찬가지고. 내가 자존심을 부리는 게 아니라, 그저 바르게 처신하고 싶은 거야. 그러니 이런 옷차림을 한 나는 두 번 다시 못 볼 걸세. 나는 이런 옷차림이 어울리지 않아. 대장간이나 부엌이나 습지를 벗어나면 나는 어울리지 않는 사람이야. 자네가 나를 떠올릴 때, 대장간 작업복 차림으로, 손에 망치를 들고, 아니 담뱃대라도 문 모습으로 떠올린다면, 나한테서 절반만큼도 흠을 찾지 못할 걸세. 행여 언젠가 자네가 나를 보고 싶어진다면, 대장간 창문으로 머리를 들이밀고, 거기 낡은 모루 앞에서, 낡고 그을린 앞치마를 두른 채, 옛 일에 매달려 있는 대장장이 조를 본다고 치면, 자네는 나한테서 절반만큼도 흠을 찾지 못할 걸세. 나는 지독히도 둔한 사람이지만, 그래도 마침내 이 일의 옳은 이치에 얼추 가까운 무언가를 두들겨 내긴 한 것 같아 좋네. 그러니 신의 가호가 있기를, 정다운 핍, 정다운 친구, 신의 가호가 있기를!\"\n\n그에게 소박한 위엄이 깃들어 있다고 여긴 내 상상은 틀린 것이 아니었다. 그가 이 말을 하는 동안, 그의 옷차림의 모양새는, 천국에서 그러할 수 없는 만큼이나 조금도 그 위엄을 가로막지 못했다. 그는 내 이마를 부드럽게 어루만지고는 밖으로 나갔다. 나는 가까스로 정신을 추스르자마자 황급히 그를 뒤따라 나가 이웃 거리들에서 그를 찾았으나, 그는 이미 가 버리고 없었다.\n\n# Chapter XXVIII.\n\n이튿날 우리 고장으로 가야 한다는 것은 분명했고, 처음 솟구쳐 오른 뉘우침의 물결 속에서, 조의 집에 묵어야 한다는 것 또한 마찬가지로 분명했다. 그러나 내일 떠나는 마차의 좌석을 예약하고, 포켓 씨 댁에 다녀온 뒤에는, 마지막 그 점에 대해 결코 확신이 서지 않았고, 블루 보어 여관에 묵을 핑계를 꾸며 대고 이런저런 변명을 지어내기 시작했다. 조의 집에 가면 폐만 끼칠 것이다. 내가 갈 줄 모르고 있을 테니 침상도 준비되어 있지 않을 것이다. 미스 해비셤의 집과 너무 멀 텐데, 그분은 까다로운 데다 그걸 못마땅해할지도 모른다. 이 세상의 그 어떤 사기꾼도 자기 자신을 속이는 사기꾼에 비하면 아무것도 아니며, 나는 그런 핑계들로 나 자신을 속였다. 참으로 묘한 일이다. 내가 남이 만든 가짜 반 크라운 동전을 영문도 모르고 받아 드는 것이야 충분히 그럴 법한 일이지만, 내 손으로 만든 가짜 동전을 알면서도 진짜 돈으로 쳐 주다니! 어느 친절한 낯선 사내가 안전을 위해 내 지폐를 차곡차곡 접어 준다는 핑계로 그 지폐를 빼돌리고 내게 호두 껍데기를 쥐여 주는 것이라면, 그자의 손재간 따위가 어찌 내 것에 비하랴. 나는 내 손으로 호두 껍데기를 접어 그것을 지폐랍시고 나 자신에게 슬쩍 떠넘기고 있으니 말이다.\n\n블루 보어에 묵어야 한다고 마음을 정하고 나니, 어벤저를 데려가야 할지 말지 결정을 내리지 못해 마음이 몹시 어지러웠다. 그 값비싼 고용병이 블루 보어 마구간 마당의 아치문 아래에서 자기 장화를 사람들 앞에 떡하니 드러내는 모습을 떠올리는 것은 솔깃한 일이었다. 그를 양복점에 무심한 듯 데려가, 무례한 트래브의 점원 녀석의 분별을 어리둥절하게 만드는 장면을 상상하니 거의 엄숙하기까지 했다. 그러나 다른 한편으로, 트래브의 점원 녀석이 그와 친한 사이로 슬그머니 파고들어 이런저런 이야기를 털어놓게 만들 수도 있고, 혹은 내가 익히 아는 대로 그자가 막무가내에 자포자기한 인간인지라 큰길에서 그를 향해 야유를 퍼부을 수도 있었다. 게다가 내 후원자께서도 그에 대한 소문을 들으면 못마땅해하실지 몰랐다. 결국 나는 어벤저를 두고 가기로 마음먹었다.\n\n내가 좌석을 예약한 것은 오후 마차였고, 이제 겨울이 돌아왔으니 목적지에 닿는 것은 해가 진 뒤 두세 시간은 지나서일 터였다. 크로스 키스에서 출발하는 시각은 두 시였다. 나는 십오 분쯤 여유를 두고 그곳에 도착했는데, 어벤저를 거느린 채였다 — 어떻게든 피할 수만 있다면 결코 나를 거든 적이 없는 자에게 그런 표현을 갖다 붙여도 된다면 말이다.\n\n그 시절에는 죄수들을 역마차로 조선소까지 호송하는 것이 관례였다. 나는 죄수들이 마차 바깥쪽 승객으로 실려 가는 이야기를 자주 들었고, 큰길에서 쇠고랑을 찬 다리를 마차 지붕 너머로 늘어뜨리고 있는 죄수들을 한두 번 본 적도 있었던지라, 마당에서 나를 만난 허버트가 다가와 나와 함께 내려갈 죄수가 둘 있다고 말했을 때 놀랄 까닭이 없었다. 그러나 나는 \"죄수\"라는 말을 들을 때마다 체질적으로 움찔하게 만드는, 이제는 오래된 까닭 하나를 품고 있었다.\n\n\"저자들이 신경 쓰이는 건 아니지, 핸들?\" 허버트가 말했다.\n\n\"아, 아니!\"\n\n\"자네가 저자들을 별로 좋아하지 않는 것처럼 보여서 말이야.\"\n\n\"좋아하는 척은 못 하겠고, 자네도 딱히 좋아하진 않겠지. 하지만 신경 쓰이진 않아.\"\n\n\"저것 봐! 저기 있군.\" 허버트가 말했다. \"술집에서 나오고 있어. 얼마나 추하고 천한 광경인지!\"\n\n아마 자기네 호송인에게 한턱을 낸 모양이었다. 간수가 하나 딸려 있었고, 셋 모두 손등으로 입을 닦으며 나왔으니 말이다. 두 죄수는 함께 수갑이 채워져 있었고, 다리에는 쇠고랑이 채워져 있었다 — 내가 잘 아는 모양의 쇠고랑이었다. 그들은 내가 마찬가지로 잘 아는 옷차림을 하고 있었다. 그들의 감시인은 권총 두 자루를 지니고 옆구리에 끝이 두툼한 곤봉을 끼고 있었으나, 죄수들과는 서로 좋게 통하는 사이였고, 그들을 곁에 세워 둔 채 말을 마차에 매는 모습을 지켜보고 있었는데, 그 태도가 마치 죄수들이 당장은 정식으로 공개되지 않은 흥미로운 전시품이고 자신은 그 관리인이라도 되는 듯했다. 한 사람은 다른 사람보다 키도 크고 몸집도 좋았는데, 죄수든 자유인이든 가리지 않는 이 세상의 알 수 없는 방식에 따라 당연한 일이라는 듯, 더 작은 옷이 그에게 배정되어 있었다. 그의 팔다리는 그런 모양으로 만든 커다란 바늘꽂이 같았고, 그 옷차림이 그를 우스꽝스럽게 가려 놓았으나, 나는 한눈에 그 반쯤 감긴 눈을 알아보았다. 어느 토요일 밤 세 명랑한 뱃사공 술집의 긴 의자에 앉아 있던, 그리고 보이지 않는 총으로 나를 쓰러뜨렸던 그 사내가 거기 서 있었던 것이다!\n\n아직까지는 그가, 평생 나를 본 적이 없는 것과 다름없이 나를 알아보지 못한다는 것을 확인하기란 쉬웠다. 그는 나를 건너다보았고, 그의 눈이 내 시곗줄을 가늠하더니, 이윽고 아무렇지 않게 침을 뱉고는 다른 죄수에게 무어라 말했다. 그러자 둘은 웃으며 그들을 잇는 수갑의 쩔그렁 소리와 함께 몸을 휙 돌려 다른 무언가를 바라보았다. 등에 박힌 커다란 번호는 마치 그들이 거리의 출입문이라도 되는 듯했고, 거칠고 누추하고 볼품없는 겉모습은 마치 그들이 하등 동물이라도 되는 듯했으며, 쇠고랑 찬 다리는 면구스럽다는 듯 손수건으로 화환처럼 둘러져 있었고, 그 자리에 있는 모든 이가 그들을 바라보면서도 그들에게서 멀찍이 떨어져 있는 모양새는 — (허버트가 말한 대로) 그들을 더없이 불쾌하고 천한 구경거리로 만들었다.\n\n그러나 이것이 최악은 아니었다. 알고 보니 마차 뒤편 좌석은 런던에서 이사 가는 한 가족이 통째로 차지하고 있었고, 두 죄수가 앉을 자리라고는 마부 뒤편 앞쪽 좌석밖에 없었다. 그러자 그 좌석의 네 번째 자리를 잡고 있던 성마른 신사가 더없이 격렬한 분노에 휩싸여, 자기를 그런 흉악한 무리와 한데 섞는 것은 계약 위반이며, 독스럽고, 해롭고, 파렴치하고, 수치스럽고, 그 밖에 또 무어라 무어라 떠들어 댔다. 이때 마차는 채비를 마쳤고 마부는 안달이 나 있었으며, 우리 모두 올라타려던 참이었고, 죄수들은 그들의 감시인과 함께 다가왔다 — 죄수의 존재에 따라붙는 그 묘한 빵 찜질약과 무명천과 노끈 부스러기와 화덕돌의 냄새를 풍기면서 말이다.\n\n\"그렇게 언짢게 여기지 마십시오, 손님.\" 감시인이 성난 승객에게 사정했다. \"제가 손님 옆에 앉겠습니다. 저자들은 줄 바깥쪽에 앉히지요. 손님을 거치적거리게 하지 않을 겁니다. 저자들이 거기 있다는 것조차 모르셔도 됩니다.\"\n\n\"그리고 나를 탓하진 마쇼.\" 내가 알아본 죄수가 으르렁거렸다. \"나는 가고 싶지도 않소. 나야 뒤에 남는 것도 얼마든지 좋고. 나로 말하자면, 누구든 내 자리에 앉으라고 하시오.\"\n\n\"내 자리도 마찬가지요.\" 다른 죄수가 퉁명스럽게 말했다. \"내 뜻대로 됐다면 댁들 누구한테도 폐 끼치지 않았을 거요.\" 그러더니 둘은 함께 웃으며 호두를 깨물어 까서는 껍데기를 여기저기 뱉어 댔다. — 내가 그들의 처지가 되어 그렇게 멸시를 받았더라면 나 역시 정말이지 그렇게 하고 싶었으리라는 생각이 든다.\n\n마침내 성난 신사에게는 달리 도리가 없으니, 우연히 함께하게 된 일행과 같이 가든지 아니면 뒤에 남든지 해야 한다는 쪽으로 의견이 모였다. 그리하여 그는 여전히 불평을 늘어놓으며 제자리에 올라탔고, 감시인은 그 옆자리에 올라탔으며, 죄수들은 할 수 있는 대로 끙끙대며 기어올랐고, 내가 알아본 그 죄수는 내 머리카락에 숨결이 닿을 만큼 바로 내 뒤에 앉았다.\n\n\"잘 가게, 핸들!\" 마차가 출발할 때 허버트가 소리쳐 불렀다. 그가 핍이 아닌 다른 이름을 내게 붙여 준 것이 얼마나 다행한 행운인가 싶었다.\n\n그 죄수의 숨결이 내 뒤통수뿐 아니라 척추를 따라 죽 느껴지던 그 예민함은 도무지 말로 표현할 수 없을 정도였다. 그 감각은 마치 어떤 매캐하고 파고드는 산이 골수에 닿은 것 같았고, 그야말로 이가 시릴 지경이었다. 그는 다른 사람보다 호흡할 일이 더 많은 듯했고, 호흡하면서 더 많은 소리를 내는 듯했다. 나는 그를 막아 내려고 한쪽으로 움츠리느라 어깨가 한쪽만 솟아오르고 있음을 의식했다.\n\n날씨는 비참할 만큼 으슬으슬했고, 두 사람은 추위를 저주했다. 얼마 가지 않아 우리 모두 나른해졌고, 중간 역참을 지나친 뒤에는 으레 졸다가 떨다가 말이 없어지곤 했다. 나 또한 이 인간이 시야에서 사라지기 전에 2파운드를 돌려주어야 할지, 또 그것을 어떻게 하는 것이 가장 좋을지 하는 문제를 곰곰이 생각하다가 깜빡 잠이 들었다. 마치 말들 사이에서 멱이라도 감으려는 듯 앞으로 고꾸라지는 순간, 나는 화들짝 놀라 깨어나 그 문제를 다시 붙들었다.\n\n그러나 생각보다 더 오래 잠들었던 모양이다. 어둠 속에서, 그리고 우리 마차 등불의 가물거리는 빛과 그림자 속에서 아무것도 알아볼 수 없었음에도, 우리에게 불어오는 차고 축축한 바람 속에서 습지대임을 감지했으니 말이다. 온기를 찾아, 또 바람을 막는 가림막으로 나를 삼으려 앞으로 잔뜩 웅크린 죄수들은 전보다 더 내게 바싹 붙어 있었다. 의식이 돌아오면서 그들이 주고받는 첫 말로 내가 들은 것은, 바로 내 생각 속의 그 말이었다. \"1파운드짜리 지폐 두 장.\"\n\n\"그자가 그걸 어떻게 구했답니까?\" 내가 한 번도 본 적 없는 죄수가 말했다.\n\n\"낸들 어찌 알겠나?\" 다른 죄수가 대꾸했다. \"어떻게 해서든 챙겨 뒀던 게지. 친구들이 줬겠지, 아마.\"\n\n\"내가 말이지,\" 다른 죄수가 추위에 대고 쓰디쓴 욕설을 퍼부으며 말했다. \"그걸 여기 가지고 있다면 좋겠소.\"\n\n\"1파운드짜리 지폐 두 장이오, 아니면 친구들이오?\"\n\n\"1파운드짜리 지폐 두 장 말이오. 나라면 평생 사귄 친구를 죄다 팔아서라도 한 장 마련하고, 그걸 더없이 잘한 흥정이라 여기겠소. 그래서? 그자가 뭐라고 했다고?\"\n\n\"그래서 그자 말이,\" 내가 알아본 죄수가 다시 입을 열었다. \"조선소의 목재 더미 뒤에서 채 반 분도 안 되는 사이에 다 말하고 끝낸 일인데 — '이제 풀려나는 거요?' 그래, 그랬지. 그자한테 먹을 걸 가져다주고 비밀을 지켜 준 그 아이를 찾아내서, 그 1파운드짜리 두 장을 전해 줄 텐가? 그래, 그러겠다고 했지. 그래서 그렇게 했고.\"\n\n\"멍청하기 짝이 없군.\" 다른 죄수가 으르렁거렸다. \"나 같으면 그걸 어른한테, 먹을거리랑 마실거리에 썼을 거요. 그 아이는 풋내기였던 게 분명해. 그러니까 댁에 대해선 아무것도 몰랐단 말이지?\"\n\n\"털끝만큼도 몰랐어. 다른 무리, 다른 배였으니까. 그자는 탈옥죄로 다시 재판을 받아 종신형이 됐지.\"\n\n\"그래서 그게 — 솔직히 말해서! — 이 지방에서 자네가 형기를 산 유일한 때였나?\"\n\n\"유일한 때였지.\"\n\n\"그곳에 대한 자네 생각은 어땠겠나?\"\n\n\"더없이 끔찍한 곳이지. 진흙 둑, 안개, 늪, 그리고 노역. 노역, 늪, 안개, 그리고 진흙 둑.\"\n\n둘은 아주 거센 말로 그곳을 저주하더니, 차츰 으르렁대다 잦아들어, 더는 할 말이 없었다.\n\n이 대화를 엿들은 뒤, 그 사내가 내 정체를 조금도 의심하지 않는다고 확신하지 못했더라면 나는 분명 마차에서 내려 큰길의 고독과 어둠 속에 남겨졌을 것이다. 사실 나는 세월의 흐름에 따라 변했을 뿐만 아니라, 옷차림도 처지도 너무나 달라져 있어, 우연한 도움 없이는 그가 나를 알아볼 가능성이 거의 없었다. 그럼에도 우리가 같은 마차에 함께 타게 된 우연은 충분히 기이한 것이어서, 어느 순간 또 다른 우연이 그가 듣는 데서 나를 내 이름과 연결지을지도 모른다는 두려움이 나를 가득 채웠다. 그런 까닭에 나는 마차가 고장에 닿는 즉시 내려서, 그가 들을 수 없는 곳으로 몸을 빼기로 마음먹었다. 이 책략을 나는 성공적으로 실행에 옮겼다. 내 작은 여행 가방은 발밑 짐칸에 있었으니, 경첩 하나만 돌리면 꺼낼 수 있었다. 나는 그것을 앞으로 던져 놓고 뒤따라 내려서서, 고장 포도의 첫 돌바닥 위, 첫 가로등 곁에 남겨졌다. 죄수들로 말하자면, 그들은 마차와 함께 제 갈 길을 갔고, 나는 어느 지점에서 그들이 강으로 빼돌려질지 알고 있었다. 내 상상 속에서, 나는 점액으로 미끈거리는 계단가에서 죄수 선원들을 태운 채 그들을 기다리는 배를 보았고 — 마치 개에게 던지는 명령 같은 그 퉁명스러운 \"저어라, 이놈들!\" 하는 소리를 다시 들었으며 — 검은 물 위에 떠 있는 그 사악한 노아의 방주를 다시 보았다.\n\n내가 무엇을 두려워하는지는 말할 수 없었으니, 내 두려움은 전적으로 막연하고 어렴풋했으나, 큰 두려움이 나를 짓누르고 있었다. 호텔을 향해 걸어가면서, 단지 괴롭거나 불쾌한 신원 확인에 대한 우려를 훨씬 넘어서는 어떤 공포가 나를 떨게 한다는 것을 느꼈다. 나는 확신하건대, 그것은 어떤 또렷한 형체를 띠지 않았으며, 어린 시절의 그 공포가 몇 분간 되살아난 것이었다.\n\n블루 보어의 커피 룸은 비어 있었고, 나는 거기서 저녁을 주문했을 뿐 아니라 자리에 앉아 식사를 하고 있었는데, 그때까지도 종업원은 나를 알아보지 못했다. 그는 자기 기억이 무딘 것을 사과하자마자, 펌블추크 씨에게 사람을 보낼까요, 하고 내게 물었다.\n\n\"아니오.\" 내가 말했다. \"절대 그러지 마시오.\"\n\n그 종업원은(내가 도제로 묶이던 날 외판원들의 그 거창한 항의를 위층으로 올려 보냈던 바로 그자였다) 놀란 듯하더니, 더러운 지역 신문 한 부를 내가 보지 않을 수 없도록 곧장 내 앞에 들이미는 기회를 냉큼 잡았고, 그래서 나는 그것을 집어 들고 이런 구절을 읽었다.\n\n본지 독자들께서는, 이 고장의 한 젊은 철공이 최근에 낭만적으로 운수가 트인 일과 관련하여(말이 나온 김에, 아직 널리 인정받지는 못했으나 본지 지면의 시인인 우리 고장 사람 투비의 마법 같은 붓에 얼마나 어울리는 주제인가!) 적잖은 흥미를 가지고 알게 되실 것이다. 그 젊은이의 가장 이른 후원자요, 동료요, 벗이었던 분은 곡물 및 종자 거래와 전혀 무관하지 않은 매우 존경받는 인사였으며, 그의 더없이 편리하고 널찍한 영업장은 큰길에서 백 마일 이내에 자리하고 있다는 것을. 우리가 이분을 우리 젊은 텔레마코스의 멘토로 기록하는 것은 우리의 사사로운 감정과 전혀 무관하지 않으니, 후자의 부의 기틀을 닦은 이를 우리 고장이 배출했다는 사실을 아는 것은 좋은 일인 까닭이다. 이 고장 현인의 사색으로 찌푸려진 이마나 이 고장 미인의 빛나는 눈은 누구의 부를 말하느냐고 물을 것인가? 우리가 알기로 퀜틴 마시스는 안트베르펜의 대장장이였다. 현명한 자에게는 한마디면 족하다.\n* 텔레마코스: 그리스 신화에서 오디세우스의 아들로, 그의 스승이자 인도자가 멘토였다. * 퀜틴 마시스: 본래 대장장이였다가 화가가 되었다고 전해지는 16세기 플랑드르의 화가.\n\n나는 풍부한 경험에 근거하여, 만약 내가 영화를 누리던 시절에 북극에 갔더라면, 거기서도 누군가를, 떠도는 에스키모든 문명인이든 누군가를 만났을 것이고, 그자가 내게 펌블추크가 나의 가장 이른 후원자이며 내 부의 기틀을 닦은 이라고 일러 주었으리라는 확신을 품고 있다.\n\n# Chapter XXIX.\n\n아침 일찍 나는 일어나 밖으로 나섰다. 미스 해비셤을 찾아가기에는 아직 너무 일렀으므로, 나는 마을에서 미스 해비셤 쪽에 해당하는 — 조가 사는 쪽은 아니었으니, 그쪽은 내일 가면 될 일이었다 — 시골로 어슬렁어슬렁 걸어 나가, 내 후원자에 대해 생각하며 그분이 나를 위해 세운 계획의 찬란한 그림들을 그려 보았다.\n\n그분은 에스텔라를 양녀로 들였고, 나 역시 양자나 다름없이 거두어 주었으니, 우리 둘을 맺어 주려는 것이 그분의 의도임은 의심할 여지가 없었다. 그분은 이 황폐한 집을 되살리는 일, 어두운 방마다 햇살을 들이는 일, 멈춘 시계들을 다시 돌게 하고 차가운 화로마다 불길을 일으키는 일, 거미줄을 걷어 내고 해충을 박멸하는 일 — 요컨대 로맨스 속 젊은 기사가 행하는 온갖 빛나는 위업을 다 이루고 마침내 공주와 결혼하는 일을 나를 위해 남겨 둔 것이었다. 나는 지나는 길에 걸음을 멈추고 그 집을 바라보았다. 검게 그을린 붉은 벽돌담, 막혀 버린 창문들, 그리고 잔가지와 덩굴손으로 굴뚝 더미마저 마치 억센 노인의 팔처럼 휘감고 있는 무성한 푸른 담쟁이가 어우러져, 내가 주인공인 풍요롭고 매혹적인 신비를 빚어내고 있었다. 물론 에스텔라가 그 신비의 영감이요, 그 심장이었다. 그러나 그녀가 나를 그토록 강하게 사로잡고 있었음에도, 내 공상과 희망이 온통 그녀에게 쏠려 있었음에도, 그녀가 내 소년 시절의 삶과 성격에 미친 영향이 절대적이었음에도, 나는 그 낭만적인 아침에조차 그녀가 실제로 지닌 것 외의 어떤 자질도 그녀에게 부여하지 않았다. 내가 이 자리에서 굳이 이 점을 언급하는 것은, 이것이 나라는 사람을 따라 나의 가련한 미궁 속으로 들어가게 해 주는 실마리이기 때문이다. 내 경험에 비추어 보건대, 연인에 대한 통념이 늘 참일 수는 없다. 가식 없는 진실은, 내가 한 남자의 사랑으로 에스텔라를 사랑했을 때, 그저 그녀가 거역할 수 없는 존재였기에 사랑했다는 것이다. 단번에 말해 두자. 나는 슬프게도 자주, 늘은 아닐지언정 거듭거듭 알고 있었다. 내가 이성을 거슬러, 약속을 거슬러, 평온을 거슬러, 희망을 거슬러, 행복을 거슬러, 있을 수 있는 온갖 단념의 이유를 거슬러 그녀를 사랑한다는 것을. 단번에 말해 두자. 그것을 알면서도 나는 조금도 덜 사랑하지 않았으니, 그 앎은 나를 억누르는 데에 아무런 힘도 발휘하지 못했고, 그것은 내가 그녀를 인간적 완벽 그 자체라고 경건히 믿었던 경우와 다를 바 없었다.\n\n나는 옛 시각에 맞추어 대문에 닿도록 걸음을 조절했다. 떨리는 손으로 종을 울린 뒤, 나는 대문에 등을 돌린 채 숨을 고르고 두근거리는 가슴을 어지간히 가라앉히려 애썼다. 옆문이 열리고 안마당을 가로질러 다가오는 발소리가 들렸으나, 녹슨 경첩에서 대문이 삐걱이며 열렸을 때조차 나는 못 들은 척했다.\n\n마침내 어깨에 손길이 닿자, 나는 흠칫 놀라 돌아섰다. 그러고는 한결 더 자연스럽게 흠칫했으니, 칙칙한 잿빛 옷을 입은 한 사내와 마주하게 된 까닭이었다. 미스 해비셤의 문 앞에서 문지기 노릇을 하고 있으리라고는 가장 상상하기 어려운 사내였다.\n\n\"올릭!\"\n\n\"아, 도련님, 변한 게 도련님만은 아니지요. 어서 들어오세요, 어서. 대문을 열어 둔 채로 두는 건 제 규칙에 어긋나니까요.\"\n\n내가 들어서자 그는 대문을 닫고 잠근 뒤 열쇠를 빼냈다. \"그래요!\" 그는 집 쪽으로 몇 걸음 무뚝뚝하게 앞장서다가 홱 돌아서며 말했다. \"내가 여기 있는 거요!\"\n\n\"어쩌다 여기 오게 됐죠?\"\n\n\"여기 온 거요.\" 그가 쏘아붙였다. \"내 발로 왔지. 짐 상자는 손수레에 실어 옆에 끼고 왔고.\"\n\n\"아주 눌러앉을 작정인가요?\"\n\n\"해를 끼치려고 온 건 아닐 텐데요, 도련님, 안 그렇소?\"\n\n그것만은 확신할 수 없었다. 그가 보도에서부터 내 다리와 팔을 거쳐 얼굴에 이르기까지 그 무거운 시선을 천천히 들어 올리는 동안, 나는 그 응수를 마음속으로 곱씹어 볼 여유가 있었다.\n\n\"그럼 대장간을 그만뒀군요?\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"이게 대장간으로 보이쇼?\" 올릭이 모욕이라도 당한 듯한 기색으로 시선을 사방으로 휘둘러 보내며 대꾸했다. \"자, 이게 대장간 같소?\"\n\n나는 가저리네 대장간을 떠난 지 얼마나 됐느냐고 물었다.\n\n\"여기선 하루하루가 어찌나 똑같은지.\" 그가 대답했다. \"헤아려 보지 않고선 모르겠소. 어쨌든 도련님이 떠난 뒤로 얼마쯤 지나서 왔지요.\"\n\n\"그쯤은 내가 알려 줄 수도 있었어요, 올릭.\"\n\n\"아!\" 그가 무뚝뚝하게 말했다. \"하긴 도련님은 이제 학식 있는 분이 되셨으니.\"\n\n이쯤에서 우리는 집에 이르렀는데, 그의 방은 옆문 바로 안쪽에 있는, 안마당이 내다보이는 작은 창이 달린 방이었다. 그 비좁은 규모는 파리에서 문지기에게 으레 내주는 그런 공간과 다를 바 없었다. 벽에는 열쇠 몇 개가 걸려 있었고, 그는 이제 거기에 대문 열쇠를 보탰다. 헝겊을 이어 붙인 이불이 덮인 침대는 안쪽으로 칸막이가 진 작은 우묵한 자리에 놓여 있었다. 그 전체가 너저분하고 답답하고 졸음에 겨운 모양새여서, 마치 사람 겨울잠쥐를 가두는 우리 같았다. 한편 창가 모퉁이의 그늘 속에 검고 육중하게 어른거리는 그는, 그 우리에 꼭 맞게 마련된 사람 겨울잠쥐처럼 보였으니 — 사실 그가 바로 그러한 존재였다.\n\n\"이 방은 전에 본 적이 없네요.\" 내가 말했다. \"하지만 여긴 문지기란 게 없었잖아요.\"\n\n\"그랬지요.\" 그가 말했다. \"이 집에 아무 보호 장치가 없다는 소문이 돌고, 죄수에다 온갖 부랑배 잡것들이 들락날락하니 위험하다고들 여기게 되기 전까지는요. 그래서 상대가 주는 만큼 그대로 갚아 줄 수 있는 사람으로 이 자리에 추천을 받았고, 나는 그걸 맡았소. 풀무질하고 망치질하는 것보단 수월하니까. — 저건 장전돼 있는 거요, 저건.\"\n\n내 눈은 벽난로 위에 놓인, 개머리판을 놋쇠로 두른 총에 가 닿아 있었고, 그의 눈도 내 시선을 따라온 것이었다.\n\n\"그럼.\" 더는 대화를 나누고 싶지 않아 내가 말했다. \"미스 해비셤께 올라가 볼까요?\"\n\n\"내가 그걸 알면 벼락을 맞지!\" 그가 먼저 기지개를 켜고 나서 몸을 한 번 부르르 흔들며 응수했다. \"내 임무는 여기까지요, 도련님. 내가 이 망치로 이 종을 한 번 두드리면, 도련님은 누구든 만날 때까지 복도를 따라가면 되는 거지.\"\n\n\"내가 올 줄 알고 계실 텐데요?\"\n\n\"내가 그걸 말할 수 있으면 벼락을 두 번 맞지!\" 그가 말했다.\n\n그러자 나는, 옛날 두툼한 부츠를 신고 처음 밟았던 그 긴 복도로 접어들었고, 그는 종을 울렸다. 종소리가 아직 울려 퍼지는 동안 복도 끝에서 나는 세라 포켓을 만났는데, 그녀는 이제 나로 인해 체질적으로 푸르뎅뎅하고 누리끼리해진 듯 보였다.\n\n\"오!\" 그녀가 말했다. \"당신이로군요, 핍 도련님?\"\n\n\"그렇습니다, 미스 포켓. 포켓 씨와 가족분들 모두 안녕히 지내신다는 소식을 전하게 되어 기쁩니다.\"\n\n\"좀 더 철이 들었나요?\" 세라가 침울하게 고개를 가로저으며 말했다. \"안녕한 것보다는 철이 드는 편이 나을 텐데. 아, 매슈, 매슈! 길은 아시지요, 도련님?\"\n\n웬만큼은 알았다. 어둠 속에서 그 계단을 오른 적이 한두 번이 아니었으니까. 나는 이제 옛날보다 가벼운 부츠를 신고 그 계단을 올라, 미스 해비셤의 방문을 예전 방식대로 두드렸다. \"핍이 두드리는 소리로구나.\" 그녀가 곧바로 말하는 것이 들렸다. \"들어오너라, 핍.\"\n\n그녀는 옛 탁자 곁의 의자에, 옛 드레스를 입고 앉아 있었으니, 두 손을 지팡이 위에 포개고 그 위에 턱을 괴고서 두 눈은 불을 응시하고 있었다. 그녀 곁에는, 한 번도 신은 적 없는 그 흰 구두를 손에 들고 고개를 숙여 그것을 들여다보고 있는, 내가 한 번도 본 적 없는 우아한 여인이 앉아 있었다.\n\n\"들어오너라, 핍.\" 미스 해비셤은 돌아보거나 고개를 들지도 않은 채 계속 중얼거렸다. \"들어오너라, 핍, 잘 지냈느냐, 핍? 내가 여왕이라도 되는 듯 내 손에 입을 맞추는구나, 응? — 어떠냐?\"\n\n그녀가 두 눈만 움직여 불쑥 나를 올려다보더니, 음산하면서도 장난기 어린 투로 되풀이했다.\n\n\"어떠냐?\"\n\n\"제가 듣기로는, 미스 해비셤,\" 나는 다소 당황하여 말했다. \"찾아와 뵙기를 친절히도 바라신다기에, 곧장 달려왔습니다.\"\n\n\"어떠냐?\"\n\n내가 전에 본 적 없는 그 여인이 두 눈을 들어 짓궂게 나를 바라보았고, 그제야 나는 그 두 눈이 에스텔라의 눈이라는 것을 알았다. 그러나 그녀는 어찌나 변했는지, 어찌나 더 아름답고, 어찌나 더 여인다워졌으며, 모든 면에서 감탄을 자아내고, 어찌나 놀랍게 성장했는지, 나는 도무지 한 발짝도 나아가지 못한 것만 같았다. 그녀를 바라보고 있자니, 나는 다시 거칠고 천한 소년으로 가망 없이 미끄러져 돌아가는 듯했다. 아, 내게 밀려온 그 거리감과 격차의 느낌, 그리고 그녀를 에워싼 그 범접할 수 없음이라니!\n\n그녀가 내게 손을 내밀었다. 나는 다시 그녀를 보게 되어 기쁘다는 것, 오래오래 이날을 고대해 왔다는 것에 대해 무언가를 더듬더듬 말했다.\n\n\"많이 변한 것 같으냐, 핍?\" 미스 해비셤이 탐욕스러운 눈빛으로 물으며, 나더러 거기 앉으라는 신호로 두 사람 사이에 놓인 의자를 지팡이로 톡톡 쳤다.\n\n\"들어왔을 때는, 미스 해비셤, 얼굴이며 자태에서 에스텔라의 모습이라곤 전혀 찾을 수 없다고 생각했습니다. 하지만 지금은 모든 것이 묘하게도 옛—\"\n\n\"뭐? 설마 옛 에스텔라로 자리 잡는다고 말하려는 건 아니겠지?\" 미스 해비셤이 말을 가로챘다. \"그 아이는 도도하고 모욕적이었고, 너는 그 아이에게서 달아나고 싶어 했지. 기억나지 않느냐?\"\n\n나는 당황하여, 그건 오래전 일이고, 그때는 내가 철이 없어서 그랬다는 둥의 말을 했다. 에스텔라는 더없이 침착하게 미소 지으며, 내가 전적으로 옳았다는 것과 자기가 매우 못되게 굴었다는 것을 조금도 의심치 않는다고 말했다.\n\n\"이 애는 변했느냐?\" 미스 해비셤이 그녀에게 물었다.\n\n\"아주 많이요.\" 에스텔라가 나를 바라보며 말했다.\n\n\"덜 거칠고 덜 천해졌느냐?\" 미스 해비셤이 에스텔라의 머리카락을 만지작거리며 말했다.\n\n에스텔라는 웃었고, 손에 든 구두를 들여다보고는 다시 웃더니, 나를 바라보고 구두를 내려놓았다. 그녀는 여전히 나를 소년 다루듯 했으나, 그러면서도 나를 끌어당겼다.\n\n우리는 나를 그토록 사로잡았던 그 옛 기이한 기운들에 둘러싸인 채 꿈결 같은 방에 앉아 있었고, 나는 그녀가 프랑스에서 막 돌아왔으며 이제 런던으로 갈 참이라는 것을 알게 되었다. 옛날처럼 도도하고 제멋대로였으나, 그녀는 그러한 성품마저 자신의 아름다움에 고스란히 복속시켜 놓았던 터라, 그 성품을 그녀의 아름다움에서 떼어 내기란 불가능하고 자연의 이치에 어긋나는 일로 — 적어도 내겐 그렇게 — 여겨졌다. 참으로, 그녀의 존재를, 내 소년 시절을 어지럽혔던 그 돈과 신사다움을 향한 비참한 갈망들로부터 — 처음으로 내게 집과 조를 부끄럽게 여기게 만든, 그 다스려지지 않은 온갖 열망들로부터 — 활활 타오르는 불길 속에 그녀의 얼굴을 떠올리게 하고, 모루 위 쇳덩이에서 그것을 두드려 내게 하고, 밤의 어둠 속에서 그것을 끄집어내어 대장간의 나무 창으로 들여다보다 휙 사라지게 하던 그 온갖 환영들로부터 떼어 놓기란 불가능했다. 한마디로, 과거에서든 현재에서든, 그녀를 내 생명의 가장 깊은 삶으로부터 떼어 놓는 것은 내게 불가능한 일이었다.\n\n그날 남은 시간을 모두 거기서 보내고, 밤에는 호텔로, 이튿날에는 런던으로 돌아가기로 정해졌다. 우리가 한동안 이야기를 나눈 뒤, 미스 해비셤은 우리 둘을 내보내어 방치된 정원을 거닐게 했다. 이따가 들어오면, 그녀가 말하기를, 옛날처럼 내가 자기 의자를 조금 밀어 데리고 다녀 달라고 했다.\n\n그리하여 에스텔라와 나는, 지난날 내가 길을 잘못 들어 그 창백한 젊은 신사 — 지금의 허버트 — 와 맞붙게 되었던 그 대문을 지나 정원으로 나갔다. 나는 마음으로 떨며 그녀 드레스의 옷자락마저 숭배하고 있었고, 그녀는 더없이 침착하여 내 옷자락 따위는 단연코 숭배하지 않았다. 그 격투의 장소에 가까워지자 그녀가 걸음을 멈추고 말했다.\n\n\"그날 숨어서 그 격투를 지켜보다니, 나도 참 유별난 어린것이었어요. 하지만 정말 그랬고, 무척이나 즐거웠지요.\"\n\n\"당신은 제게 큰 상을 주었지요.\"\n\n\"내가요?\" 그녀가 무심하고 까맣게 잊은 듯한 투로 대꾸했다. \"당신 상대 아이한테 내가 몹시 거부감을 품었던 건 기억나요. 그 애가 여기 끌려와서 함께 있겠다며 나를 성가시게 구는 게 못마땅했거든요.\"\n\n\"그 친구와 저는 이제 둘도 없는 사이랍니다.\"\n\n\"그래요? 그래도 당신이 그 애 아버지에게서 공부를 했던 건 기억나는 것 같은데요?\"\n\n\"네.\"\n\n나는 마지못해 그 사실을 인정했으니, 그것이 어쩐지 소년 티가 나 보였고, 그녀는 이미 차고 넘치도록 나를 소년 대하듯 하고 있었기 때문이다.\n\n\"운수와 앞날이 바뀌고 나서, 당신은 어울리는 사람들도 바꾸었군요.\" 에스텔라가 말했다.\n\n\"당연하지요.\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"그리고 그래야만 하고요.\" 그녀가 도도한 어조로 덧붙였다. \"한때 당신에게 어울리던 사람들이, 이제는 영 어울리지 않을 테니까요.\"\n\n양심에 비추어 말하건대, 그때 내게 조를 보러 가려는 미련이 조금이라도 남아 있었는지는 무척 의심스럽다. 하지만 설령 남아 있었다 한들, 이 말 한마디가 그것을 깨끗이 날려 버렸을 것이다.\n\n\"그 시절엔, 당신에게 닥쳐올 행운에 대해 전혀 짐작도 못 했지요?\" 에스텔라가, 그 격투하던 시절을 뜻하는 양 손을 가볍게 흔들며 말했다.\n\n\"전혀요.\"\n\n내 곁을 걷는 그녀의 그 완숙하고 우월한 분위기와, 그녀 곁을 걷는 나의 그 풋풋하고 순종적인 분위기가 빚어내는 대조를 나는 절절히 느꼈다. 만일 내가, 그녀를 위해 따로 마련되고 그녀에게 짝지어진 처지인 까닭에 내가 그 대조를 자아내는 것이라 여기지 않았더라면, 그것은 실제 이상으로 나를 쓰라리게 했을 것이다.\n\n정원은 너무 무성하고 거칠게 우거져 편히 거닐 수 없었으므로, 두세 바퀴를 돈 뒤 우리는 다시 양조장 마당으로 나왔다. 나는 그 첫 옛날에 그녀가 술통들 위를 걷던 모습을 본 자리를 정확히 짚어 보였고, 그녀는 그쪽을 차갑고 무심한 눈길로 바라보며 \"내가요?\" 하고 말했다. 나는 그녀가 집에서 나와 내게 먹을 것과 마실 것을 내주던 자리를 일깨워 주었으나, 그녀는 \"기억나지 않아요.\" 하고 말했다. \"나를 울린 일도 기억나지 않나요?\" 내가 말했다. \"네.\" 그녀가 말하며 고개를 가로젓고 주위를 둘러보았다. 진정 나는 믿는다. 그녀가 기억하지 못하고 조금도 개의치 않는다는 사실이, 내 안에서 다시금 나를 울게 만들었다는 것을 — 그리고 그것이야말로 가장 쓰라린 울음이라는 것을.\n\n\"알아 둬야 할 게 있어요.\" 에스텔라가, 눈부시고 아름다운 여인이 으레 그러듯 나를 굽어보며 말했다. \"나는 심장이 없어요 — 그게 내 기억력과 무슨 상관이 있다면 말이지요.\"\n\n나는, 외람되나 그 말을 의심한다는 뜻의 횡설수설을 어찌어찌 늘어놓았다. 나는 그렇지 않다는 걸 더 잘 안다고. 그것 없이는 이런 아름다움이 있을 수 없다고.\n\n\"오! 찔리거나 총 맞을 심장이야 분명 있지요.\" 에스텔라가 말했다. \"그게 멈추면 나도 멈출 테고요. 하지만 내 말뜻은 알 거예요. 나는 거기에 부드러움이라곤 없어요. 동정도 — 감상도 — 그 따위 부질없는 것도 없지요.\"\n\n그녀가 가만히 멈추어 서서 나를 골똘히 바라보았을 때, 내 마음에 사무쳐 든 그것은 대체 무엇이었던가? 내가 미스 해비셤에게서 본 그 무엇이었던가? 아니다. 그녀의 어떤 표정과 몸짓에는, 함께 오래 지내며 세상과 격리되어 어른에게서 아이가 물려받은 것을 흔히 볼 수 있는 그런, 미스 해비셤을 어렴풋이 닮은 기색이 깃들어 있었으니, 그것은 어린 시절이 지난 뒤에는 본디 전혀 다른 두 얼굴 사이에서 이따금 놀라운 표정의 유사함을 빚어내곤 하는 그런 것이었다. 그런데도 나는 그것을 미스 해비셤에게로 더듬어 올라갈 수 없었다. 다시 바라보았으나, 그녀가 여전히 나를 바라보고 있는데도 그 암시는 사라지고 없었다.\n그것은 대체 무엇이었던가?\n\n\"나는 진지해요.\" 에스텔라가, 찌푸린 표정이라기보다는(그녀의 이마는 매끈했으니까) 얼굴이 어두워지는 빛으로 말했다. \"우리가 앞으로 자주 한자리에 있게 될 거라면, 차라리 지금 당장 그걸 믿어 두는 편이 나아요. 아니!\" 내가 입을 떼려 하자 그녀가 위엄 있게 나를 가로막았다. \"나는 어디에도 다정함을 베풀어 본 적이 없어요. 애초에 그런 걸 가져 본 적이 없으니까요.\"\n\n다음 순간 우리는, 그토록 오래 쓰이지 않은 그 양조장 안에 들어와 있었고, 그녀는 바로 그 첫날 그녀가 나가는 것을 내가 보았던 그 높은 회랑을 가리키며, 자기가 거기 올라갔던 것과 아래에서 겁에 질려 서 있던 나를 본 것을 기억한다고 말했다. 내 눈이 그녀의 흰 손을 좇는 사이, 도무지 붙잡을 수 없는 그 똑같은 희미한 암시가 다시 한번 나를 스쳐 갔다. 내가 저도 모르게 흠칫하자 그녀는 내 팔에 손을 얹었다. 순간 그 유령은 또 한 번 지나가 버리고 없었다.\n\n그것은 대체 무엇이었던가?\n\"왜 그래요?\" 에스텔라가 물었다. \"또 겁이 났나요?\"\n\n\"방금 당신이 한 말을 믿었다면 그랬겠지요.\" 나는 얼버무리려고 대답했다.\n\n\"그럼 안 믿는군요? 좋아요. 어쨌든 말은 해 두었으니까요. 미스 해비셤이 곧 당신을 옛 자리에서 기다리실 거예요. 하긴 이제는 그것도 다른 옛 물건들과 함께 치워 버려도 될 것 같지만요. 정원을 한 바퀴만 더 돌고 들어가요. 자! 오늘은 내 잔인함 때문에 눈물 흘리지 말아요. 당신은 내 시동이 되어, 어깨를 빌려주세요.\"\n\n그녀의 고운 드레스가 땅바닥에 끌리고 있었다. 그녀는 이제 한 손으로 그것을 잡고, 다른 한 손으로는 걸으며 내 어깨를 가볍게 짚었다. 우리는 그 폐허가 된 정원을 두세 바퀴 더 돌았고, 그것은 내게 온통 꽃이 만발한 곳이었다. 낡은 담장 틈새에 푸르고 누렇게 돋아난 잡초가 일찍이 피어난 적 없는 가장 귀한 꽃들이었다 한들, 내 기억 속에서 그보다 더 애틋할 수는 없었을 것이다.\n\n우리 사이에는 그녀를 내게서 멀리 떼어 놓을 만한 나이 차이가 없었으니, 우리는 거의 동갑이었다 — 물론 그녀에게는 나이가 나보다 더 큰 의미를 지녔지만. 그러나 그녀의 아름다움과 몸가짐이 그녀에게 부여한 그 범접할 수 없는 분위기는, 내 환희의 한복판에서 나를 괴롭혔으니, 우리의 후원자가 우리를 서로의 짝으로 점지했다는 확신이 절정에 달한 그 순간에도 그러했다. 가련한 소년 같으니!\n\n마침내 우리는 집으로 돌아갔고, 거기서 나는 놀랍게도, 내 후견인이 볼일이 있어 미스 해비셤을 만나러 내려와 있으며 만찬에 다시 오리라는 소식을 들었다. 곰팡이 슨 그 탁자가 차려진 방의, 겨울 가지처럼 앙상한 옛 샹들리에들이 우리가 나가 있는 동안 불이 밝혀져 있었고, 미스 해비셤은 자기 의자에 앉아 나를 기다리고 있었다.\n\n신부의 잔칫상 잿더미를 빙 도는 그 옛 느릿한 순행을 시작하자니, 마치 의자 그 자체를 과거 속으로 밀어 넣는 것만 같았다. 그러나 그 장례식 같은 방, 무덤 같은 형상이 의자에 등을 기댄 채 그녀에게 두 눈을 못 박고 있는 그 방에서, 에스텔라는 전보다 한결 더 환하고 아름다워 보였고, 나는 더욱 강한 마법에 사로잡혔다.\n\n시간이 어찌나 녹아내리듯 흘러갔던지, 이른 만찬 시각이 코앞에 닥쳐, 에스텔라는 채비를 하러 우리 곁을 떠났다. 우리는 그 긴 탁자의 중앙께에서 걸음을 멈춘 참이었고, 미스 해비셤은 시든 한쪽 팔을 의자 밖으로 뻗어, 그 움켜쥔 손을 누런 식탁보 위에 얹고 있었다. 에스텔라가 문으로 나가기 전 어깨 너머로 돌아보자, 미스 해비셤은 그 손으로 그녀에게 입맞춤을 날려 보냈으니, 그 게걸스러운 격렬함은 그 나름으로 자못 섬뜩한 것이었다.\n\n그러고 나서, 에스텔라가 떠나 우리 둘만 남게 되자, 그녀가 내게로 돌아서서 속삭이듯 말했다.\n\n\"그 아이가 아름답지, 우아하고, 잘 자랐지? 너도 그 아이에게 반했느냐?\"\n\n\"그 아이를 보는 사람이라면 누구나 그럴 수밖에 없겠지요, 미스 해비셤.\"\n\n그녀는 한쪽 팔로 내 목을 감아, 의자에 앉은 채 내 머리를 자기 머리 가까이로 끌어내렸다. \"그 아이를 사랑해라, 사랑해라, 사랑해라! 그 아이가 너를 어떻게 대하느냐?\"\n\n내가 미처 대답하기도 전에(그토록 어려운 물음에 내가 대답할 수 있었다 한들), 그녀가 되풀이했다. \"그 아이를 사랑해라, 사랑해라, 사랑해라! 그 아이가 너를 어여삐 여기거든, 사랑해라. 그 아이가 너를 상처 입히거든, 사랑해라. 그 아이가 네 심장을 갈가리 찢어 놓거든 — 그리고 그것이 더 늙고 더 단단해질수록 더 깊이 찢어 놓을 테지만 — 사랑해라, 사랑해라, 사랑해라!\"\n\n그 말들을 토해 낼 때 그녀가 보인 것만큼 격정적인 열망을 나는 일찍이 본 적이 없었다. 내 목을 감은 그 가느다란 팔의 근육이, 그녀를 사로잡은 격렬함으로 부풀어 오르는 것을 나는 느낄 수 있었다.\n\n\"내 말 들어라, 핍! 나는 사랑받게 하려고 그 아이를 양녀로 들였다. 사랑받게 하려고 그 아이를 기르고 가르쳤다. 사랑받게 하려고 그 아이를 지금의 모습으로 키워 냈다. 그 아이를 사랑해라!\"\n\n그녀는 그 말을 충분히 여러 번 했고, 그녀가 그 말을 하려 했다는 데에는 의심의 여지가 없었다. 그러나 그 거듭 되풀이된 말이 사랑이 아니라 증오였다 한들 — 절망이든 — 복수든 — 끔찍한 죽음이든 — 그녀의 입술에서 그보다 더 저주처럼 울려 나올 수는 없었을 것이다.\n\n\"내가 말해 주마.\" 그녀가 여전히 다급하고 격정적인 속삭임으로 말했다. \"진정한 사랑이 무엇인지를. 그건 눈먼 헌신이고, 따져 묻지 않는 자기 비하이며, 철저한 복종이고, 너 자신을 거스르고 온 세상을 거슬러서 바치는 신뢰와 믿음이며, 너를 내리치는 자에게 네 온 마음과 영혼을 송두리째 내주는 것이다 — 내가 그랬듯이!\"\n\n그녀가 그 대목에, 그리고 뒤이은 광란의 비명에 이르렀을 때, 나는 그녀의 허리를 와락 끌어안았다. 그녀가 수의 같은 드레스를 걸친 채 의자에서 벌떡 일어나, 마치 차라리 벽에 제 몸을 부딪쳐 죽기라도 하려는 듯 허공을 후려쳤기 때문이다.\n\n이 모든 것이 몇 초 사이에 벌어졌다. 그녀를 의자로 끌어 앉히는 사이, 나는 익히 아는 어떤 향내를 느꼈고, 돌아보니 내 후견인이 방 안에 있었다.\n\n그는 늘(아직 언급하지 않은 것 같은데) 값진 비단으로 된, 위풍당당한 크기의 손수건을 지니고 다녔으니, 그것은 그의 직업에서 그에게 큰 쓸모가 있는 물건이었다. 나는 그가 의뢰인이나 증인을, 이 손수건을 마치 당장 코라도 풀려는 듯 점잔 빼며 펼쳐 보이고는, 그 의뢰인이나 증인이 제 발 저려 말실수를 저지르기 전에 코 풀 겨를이 없으리라는 걸 안다는 양 동작을 멈추는 것만으로 어찌나 겁에 질리게 했던지, 그러면 그 자백이 으레 당연한 수순처럼 곧장 뒤따라 나오는 것을 본 적이 있다. 내가 방 안에서 그를 보았을 때, 그는 그 의미심장한 손수건을 두 손에 든 채 우리를 바라보고 있었다. 내 눈과 마주치자, 그는 그 자세 그대로 잠시 말없이 멈춤으로써 분명하게 말했다. \"그래? 별일이군!\" 그러고는 놀라운 효과를 발휘하며 그 손수건을 제 본래 용도에 갖다 썼다.\n\n미스 해비셤도 나만큼이나 일찌감치 그를 알아보았고, (다른 모든 이들처럼) 그를 두려워했다. 그녀는 마음을 가다듬으려 애써 노력하며, 그가 변함없이 시간을 잘 지킨다고 더듬더듬 말했다.\n\n\"변함없이 시간을 잘 지키지요.\" 그가 우리 쪽으로 다가오며 되풀이했다. \"(잘 지냈느냐, 핍? 한 바퀴 밀어 드릴까요, 미스 해비셤? 한 바퀴만?) 그래, 너도 여기 와 있었구나, 핍?\"\n\n나는 그에게 내가 언제 도착했는지, 그리고 미스 해비셤이 내가 와서 에스텔라를 보기를 바라셨다는 것을 말했다. 그러자 그가 대꾸했다. \"아! 참으로 아리따운 아가씨지!\" 그러고는 큼직한 한 손으로 미스 해비셤의 의자를 앞으로 밀고, 다른 한 손은 마치 그 주머니가 비밀로 가득 차기라도 한 듯 바지 주머니에 찔러 넣었다.\n\n\"그래, 핍! 전에 미스 에스텔라를 몇 번이나 봤느냐?\" 그가 걸음을 멈추며 말했다.\n\n\"몇 번이냐고요?\"\n\n\"그래! 몇 번이나? 만 번?\"\n\n\"오! 그렇게 많지는 않지요.\"\n\n\"두 번?\"\n\n\"재거스,\" 미스 해비셤이 끼어들었으니, 내겐 무척이나 다행스러운 일이었다. \"내 핍은 그만 내버려 두고, 데리고 가서 식사나 하세요.\"\n\n그가 따랐고, 우리는 함께 어두운 계단을 더듬더듬 내려갔다. 뒤편 포석 깔린 마당 너머의 그 별채로 가는 길에서, 그는 내게 미스 해비셤이 먹고 마시는 것을 몇 번이나 보았느냐고 물으며, 여느 때처럼 백 번에서 한 번 사이에서 폭넓게 고를 여지를 주었다.\n\n나는 곰곰이 생각해 보고 말했다. \"한 번도요.\"\n\n\"앞으로도 못 볼 거다, 핍.\" 그가 찌푸린 미소를 지으며 대꾸했다. \"그분은 지금의 이 삶을 살게 된 뒤로, 그 어느 쪽이든 남에게 보이는 걸 결코 허락한 적이 없지. 밤이면 이리저리 돌아다니다가, 그러다 손에 잡히는 음식을 집어 드는 거야.\"\n\n\"여쭙건대, 선생님,\" 내가 말했다. \"한 가지 여쭤봐도 될까요?\"\n\n\"물어도 좋다.\" 그가 말했다. \"그리고 나는 대답을 거절할 수도 있지. 질문해 보아라.\"\n\n\"에스텔라의 성 말입니다. 해비셤인가요, 아니면—?\" 나는 더 덧붙일 말이 없었다.\n\n\"아니면 뭐냐?\" 그가 말했다.\n\n\"해비셤인가요?\"\n\n\"해비셤이다.\"\n\n이렇게 해서 우리는 만찬 식탁에 이르렀고, 거기서는 그녀와 세라 포켓이 우리를 기다리고 있었다. 재거스 씨가 좌장 노릇을 했고, 에스텔라는 그의 맞은편에, 나는 그 푸르뎅뎅하고 누리끼리한 벗을 마주 보고 앉았다. 우리는 아주 잘 먹었고, 내가 그 집을 드나든 그 숱한 동안 한 번도 본 적 없는 하녀의 시중을 받았는데, 어쩌면 그 여자는 내가 알지 못할 뿐 그 신비로운 집에 줄곧 있었는지도 모를 일이었다. 만찬이 끝나자 내 후견인 앞에는 진귀한 묵은 포트와인 한 병이 놓였고(그는 분명 그 빈티지를 잘 아는 듯했다), 두 숙녀는 우리 곁을 떠났다.\n\n그 지붕 아래에서 보인 재거스 씨의 그 작심한 듯한 과묵함에 견줄 만한 것을, 나는 다른 어디서도, 심지어 그 자신에게서조차 본 적이 없다. 그는 자기 표정마저 속으로 감추었고, 만찬 내내 에스텔라의 얼굴로 눈길을 한 번도 제대로 보내지 않았다. 그녀가 그에게 말을 걸면 그는 귀 기울여 듣고 때맞춰 대답했으나, 내가 보기에 그녀를 결코 바라보지는 않았다. 반면에 그녀는, 불신까지는 아니더라도 흥미와 호기심을 담아 자주 그를 바라보았으나, 그의 얼굴은 조금도 그것을 의식하는 빛을 내비치지 않았다. 만찬 내내 그는 나와의 대화에서 내 유산에 대해 자주 들먹임으로써 세라 포켓을 더 푸르뎅뎅하고 더 누리끼리하게 만드는 데에서 무미건조한 즐거움을 맛보았다. 그러나 여기서도 그는 의식하는 기색이라곤 없었고, 심지어 그 언급들을 순진한 내 입에서 억지로 끄집어내는 것처럼 — 실제로 끄집어내기도 했으니, 어떻게 그랬는지는 모르겠지만 — 보이게 만들었다.\n\n그리고 그와 나 둘만 남게 되자, 그는 자기가 쥐고 있는 정보 덕분에 전반적으로 느긋이 웅크리고 있는 듯한 분위기를 풍기며 앉아 있었으니, 그것은 정말이지 내겐 감당하기 벅찬 것이었다. 그는 달리 손댈 것이 없을 때면 자기 포도주마저 추궁하듯 따져 댔다. 그것을 자기와 촛불 사이에 들어 비추어 보고, 포트와인을 맛보고, 입 안에서 굴리고, 삼키고, 다시 잔을 들여다보고, 포트와인의 향을 맡고, 음미하고, 마시고, 다시 채우고, 또다시 잔을 추궁했으니, 나는 마치 그 포도주가 그에게 나한테 불리한 무언가를 일러바치고 있다는 걸 알기라도 한 듯 안절부절못했다. 서너 번쯤 나는 맥없이 대화를 시작해 볼까 생각했으나, 내가 무언가를 물으려는 낌새를 보일 때마다, 그는 잔을 손에 든 채, 입 안에서 포도주를 굴리면서, 마치 소용없는 짓이니 잘 새겨 두라는 듯, 자기는 대답할 수 없다는 듯 나를 바라보았다.\n\n미스 포켓은, 나를 보는 것이 미칠 지경으로 들볶이는 위험을, 그리하여 어쩌면 모슬린 자루 같은 — 지극히 흉측한 — 자기 모자를 잡아 뜯고, 분명 제 머리에서 자란 것일 리 없는 그 머리카락을 바닥에 흩뿌릴 위험을 무릅쓰는 처지임을 스스로 의식하고 있었던 것 같다. 나중에 우리가 미스 해비셤의 방으로 올라가 넷이서 휘스트를 칠 때, 그녀는 모습을 보이지 않았다. 그 사이에 미스 해비셤은, 기상천외한 방식으로, 자기 화장대의 가장 아름다운 보석 몇 점을 에스텔라의 머리와 가슴과 팔에 둘러 주었다. 그리고 나는, 그렇게 보석의 광채와 빛깔이 흐드러진 그녀의 사랑스러움이 눈앞에 놓이자, 내 후견인마저 짙은 눈썹 아래로 그녀를 바라보며 눈썹을 살짝 치켜올리는 것을 보았다.\n\n그가 어떤 식으로, 또 어느 정도로 우리의 으뜸패를 거두어 가두어 버리고는, 우리 킹과 퀸의 영광이 송두리째 짓밟히도록 판이 끝날 때마다 보잘것없는 시시한 패를 내놓았는지에 대해서는, 나는 아무 말도 하지 않겠다. 또한 그가 우리를 한 사람 한 사람, 자기가 오래전에 이미 풀어 버린 너무도 빤하고 시시한 세 개의 수수께끼쯤으로 여기고 있는 듯하다는 데서 내가 느낀 감정에 대해서도 말하지 않겠다. 내가 괴로웠던 것은, 그의 차가운 존재와 에스텔라를 향한 내 감정 사이의 그 양립 불가능함이었다. 내가 결코 그에게 그녀에 대해 말하는 것을 견딜 수 없으리라는 것을 안다거나, 그가 그녀에게 부츠 소리를 삐걱이는 것을 결코 들을 수 없으리라는 것을 안다거나, 그가 그녀에게서 손을 떼어 버리는 것을 결코 볼 수 없으리라는 것을 안다거나 하는 것이 아니었다. 내가 괴로웠던 것은, 내 흠모가 그에게서 한두 자 거리 안에 있다는 것 — 내 감정이 그와 같은 자리에 놓여 있다는 것 — 바로 그것이 그 견디기 힘든 사정이었다.\n\n우리는 아홉 시까지 카드놀이를 했고, 그런 다음 에스텔라가 런던에 올 때면 그녀가 온다는 것을 내게 미리 알려 주어 내가 역마차로 그녀를 마중 나가기로 정해졌다. 그러고 나서 나는 그녀에게 작별을 고하고, 그녀의 손에 닿았다가 그녀를 떠났다.\n\n내 후견인은 보어 여관, 내 방 옆방에 묵었다. 밤이 깊도록, 미스 해비셤의 그 말, \"그 아이를 사랑해라, 사랑해라, 사랑해라!\"가 내 귓전에 울렸다. 나는 그 말을 내 식으로 고쳐 되뇌며, 베개를 향해 \"나는 그녀를 사랑해, 사랑해, 사랑해!\" 하고 수백 번을 말했다. 그러자 한때 대장장이의 소년이었던 내게 그녀가 점지되어 있다는 사실에 감격이 북받쳐 올랐다. 그러다 나는, 그녀가 내 두려움대로 아직은 그 운명에 조금도 황홀히 감사하지 않는다면, 그녀가 대체 언제부터 나에게 관심을 갖기 시작할까 하고 생각했다. 지금은 말없이 잠들어 있는 그녀 안의 심장을, 나는 언제쯤 깨울 수 있을까?\n\n아, 이런! 나는 그것들이 고결하고 위대한 감정이라고 여겼다. 그러나 내가 조에게서 멀어진 데에 — 그녀가 그를 경멸하리라는 것을 안다는 이유로 그리한 데에 — 비천하고 옹졸한 구석이 있다고는 결코 생각지 못했다. 겨우 하루 전만 해도, 조는 내 눈에 눈물을 고이게 했었다. 그 눈물은 이내 말라 버렸으니, 하느님, 저를 용서하소서! 이내 말라 버렸다.\n\n# 제28장\n\n아침에 블루 보어에서 옷을 갈아입으며 그 문제를 충분히 곰곰이 따져 본 끝에, 나는 후견인에게 올릭이 미스 해비셤 댁의 신임받는 자리를 맡기에 적합한 부류의 사람인지 의심스럽다고 말하기로 결심했다.\n\"물론 그자는 적합한 부류의 사람이 아니지, 핍.\" 후견인은 그 일반적인 명제에 대해 미리 흡족하게 만족한 듯 말했다. \"신임받는 자리를 맡는 사람이라는 게 애당초 적합한 부류의 사람이었던 적이 없으니까.\" 이 특정한 자리 역시 예외 없이 적합한 부류의 사람이 차지하고 있지 않다는 사실을 알게 되자 그는 자못 신이 난 듯했고, 내가 올릭에 대해 알고 있는 바를 말하는 동안 만족스러운 태도로 귀를 기울였다. \"아주 좋아, 핍.\" 내가 이야기를 마치자 그가 말했다. \"내가 곧 들러서 우리 친구를 해고하지.\" 이런 즉결 처분에 다소 놀란 나는 잠시 미루는 편이 어떻겠느냐고 했고, 심지어 그 친구 본인을 다루기가 까다로울지도 모른다는 말까지 넌지시 비쳤다. \"오, 아니, 그렇지 않을 거야.\" 후견인은 더없는 자신감으로 손수건을 펼치는 시늉을 하며 말했다. \"그자가 나하고 그 문제를 가지고 따져 보겠다면 어디 한번 보고 싶군.\"\n\n우리는 정오 마차로 함께 런던으로 돌아갈 참이었고, 나는 펌블추크에 대한 두려움 때문에 찻잔조차 제대로 들지 못할 지경으로 아침 식사를 했으므로, 이를 기회 삼아 산책을 좀 하고 싶다고 말했다. 재거스 씨가 일을 보는 동안 나는 런던으로 향하는 길을 따라 먼저 가 있겠으며, 마차가 나를 따라잡으면 자리에 오르겠으니 마부에게 그리 일러 달라고 했다. 그리하여 나는 아침 식사를 마치자마자 곧장 블루 보어에서 달아날 수 있었다. 그런 다음 펌블추크의 가게 뒤편으로 펼쳐진 들판으로 이 마일쯤 빙 돌아 나가, 그 함정을 조금 지난 지점에서 다시 큰길로 접어들었고, 그제야 비교적 안전하다는 느낌이 들었다.\n\n조용한 옛 고장에 다시 와 있는 것은 흥미로운 일이었고, 여기저기서 불쑥 사람들에게 알아보아지고 뒤에서 눈길을 받는 것도 그리 싫지는 않았다. 상인 한둘은 가게에서 뛰쳐나와 내 앞에서 거리를 조금 내려가더니, 마치 무언가를 깜빡 잊은 양 몸을 돌려 나와 정면으로 마주치곤 했는데, 그럴 때면 그들이 모르는 척하는 연기와 내가 못 본 척하는 연기 가운데 어느 쪽이 더 서툴렀는지 나로서는 알 수 없었다. 그래도 내 처지는 남다른 것이었고, 나는 그것에 조금도 불만이 없었다. 운명이 그 한량없는 악당, 트래브네 점원 녀석을 내 앞길에 던져 놓기 전까지는 말이다.\n\n길을 가다 어느 지점에서 거리를 따라 시선을 던지니, 트래브네 점원 녀석이 빈 파란 가방으로 제 몸을 후려치며 다가오는 것이 보였다. 평온하고 무심한 태도로 그를 바라보는 것이 내게 가장 어울리며, 또한 그자의 못된 심사를 잠재우는 데 가장 효과적이리라 판단한 나는, 그런 표정을 지은 채 앞으로 나아갔고, 내 성공을 자축할 만하다고 여기던 참이었다. 그런데 별안간 트래브네 점원 녀석의 두 무릎이 맞부딪치고, 머리카락이 곤두서고, 모자가 벗겨지고, 사지가 격렬하게 떨리더니, 비틀거리며 길 한복판으로 나가 군중을 향해 \"날 좀 잡아 줘요! 너무 무서워요!\" 하고 외치며, 내 위풍당당한 행색에 압도되어 공포와 회한의 발작을 일으킨 척하는 것이었다. 내가 그 곁을 지나가자 녀석은 입속에서 이를 요란하게 딱딱 부딪치며, 극도의 굴종을 드러내는 온갖 몸짓과 함께 흙먼지 속에 납작 엎드렸다.\n\n이것만 해도 견디기 힘든 일이었지만, 이건 아무것도 아니었다. 채 이백 야드도 더 가지 못했을 때, 이루 말할 수 없는 공포와 경악과 분노 속에서, 나는 또다시 트래브네 점원 녀석이 다가오는 것을 보았다. 녀석은 좁은 모퉁이를 돌아 나오고 있었다. 파란 가방을 어깨에 둘러메고, 두 눈에는 정직한 근면이 빛났으며, 발걸음에는 트래브네 가게로 기운차고 활기차게 가겠다는 결의가 어려 있었다. 녀석은 화들짝 놀라며 나를 알아보고는 앞서와 똑같이 호되게 발작을 일으켰는데, 이번에는 그 동작이 회전식이어서, 더욱 가련하게 휘청대는 무릎으로 내 둘레를 빙글빙글 돌며, 두 손을 치켜들고 마치 자비를 비는 듯한 시늉을 했다. 녀석의 고통은 한 무리의 구경꾼들에게 더없는 환호로 맞아들여졌고, 나는 완전히 어쩔 줄을 몰랐다.\n\n거리를 따라 우체국까지도 채 못 갔을 때, 나는 또다시 트래브네 점원 녀석이 뒷길로 쏜살같이 돌아 나오는 것을 보았다. 이번에 녀석은 완전히 딴판으로 변해 있었다. 파란 가방을 내 외투인 양 걸치고서, 길 건너편 인도를 따라 내 쪽으로 거들먹거리며 걸어왔는데, 신이 난 어린 친구들 한 무리를 거느리고 이따금 그들을 향해 손을 휘저으며 \"난 저 사람 몰라요!\" 하고 외쳤다. 트래브네 점원 녀석이 내 옆을 나란히 지나가며 셔츠 깃을 세우고, 옆머리를 배배 꼬고, 한 팔을 허리에 척 짚고, 팔꿈치와 몸뚱이를 흐느적흐느적 흔들며 요란하게 능글맞은 웃음을 흘리고는, 거느린 무리에게 \"난 저 사람 몰라, 난 저 사람 몰라, 맹세코 난 저 사람 몰라!\" 하고 느릿느릿 내뱉었을 때 내가 받은 모욕과 상처의 크기란, 말로는 도저히 다 표현할 수 없다. 그러고 나서 녀석이 곧장 닭 우는 소리를 내기 시작하더니, 내가 대장장이였던 시절을 알던 어느 몹시 풀 죽은 닭이라도 되는 양 닭 울음으로 다리 건너까지 나를 쫓아오던 그 망신스러움은, 내가 그 고장을 떠나며 당한 망신의 절정이었고, 말하자면 나는 그렇게 그 고장에서 내쫓겨 들판으로 밀려난 셈이었다.\n\n그러나 그때 내가 트래브네 점원 녀석의 목숨을 끊어 놓기라도 했다면 모를까, 참고 견디는 것 외에 달리 무엇을 할 수 있었을지 지금도 나로서는 정말 알 수가 없다. 길거리에서 녀석과 몸싸움을 벌이거나, 녀석의 심장에서 가장 좋은 피보다 못한 어떤 보상이라도 받아 내려 했다면, 그것은 헛되고 품위를 떨어뜨리는 일이었으리라. 게다가 녀석은 누구도 해칠 수 없는 아이였다. 무적의, 요리조리 잘도 빠져나가는 뱀 같은 놈이어서, 구석으로 몰리면 잡으려는 자의 두 다리 사이로 다시 빠져나가며 비웃듯 캥캥거리는 것이었다. 그렇지만 나는 이튿날 우편으로 트래브 씨에게 편지를 보내, 사회의 최선의 이익에 대해 자신이 짊어진 책무를 그토록 망각한 나머지, 점잖은 사람이라면 누구나 혐오감을 느낄 만한 점원을 부리는 자와는 핍 씨가 더 이상 거래를 하지 않겠노라고 전했다.\n\n재거스 씨를 태운 마차가 때맞춰 당도했고, 나는 다시 마부석 자리에 올라 무사히 런던에 도착했다. 그러나 온전한 상태는 아니었다. 내 마음이 떠나 버렸으니까. 도착하자마자 나는 참회의 뜻으로 대구 한 마리와 굴 한 통을 조에게 보낸 뒤(직접 찾아가지 못한 데 대한 보상이었다), 바너드 여관으로 향했다.\n\n가 보니 허버트는 냉육으로 저녁 식사를 하고 있다가, 돌아온 나를 반갑게 맞아 주었다. 어벤저를 커피 하우스로 보내 저녁 식사에 음식을 좀 더 보태도록 시켜 놓은 뒤, 나는 바로 그날 저녁 내 벗이자 친구에게 가슴속을 털어놓아야겠다는 마음이 들었다. 그런데 어벤저가 현관에 있어서는 속내를 터놓을 수가 없었으니, 그 현관이란 열쇠 구멍으로 이어지는 대기실쯤으로 여길 만한 곳이었기 때문이다. 그래서 나는 녀석을 연극 구경이나 가라고 내보냈다. 그 부려 먹는 상전에게 내가 얼마나 심하게 매여 있었는지를, 녀석에게 일거리를 마련해 주려고 끊임없이 짜내야 했던 그 한심한 궁리들보다 더 잘 보여 주는 증거는 없으리라. 막다른 처지란 어찌나 옹색한지, 나는 때때로 녀석을 하이드 파크 모퉁이까지 보내 지금 몇 시인지 보고 오라고 시키기까지 했다.\n\n저녁 식사가 끝나고 우리가 난로 받침대에 발을 올린 채 앉아 있을 때, 나는 허버트에게 말했다. \"이보게, 허버트, 자네한테 아주 특별히 할 이야기가 있네.\"\n\n\"이보게, 헨델.\" 그가 대답했다. \"자네가 털어놓는 비밀을 소중히 여기고 존중하겠네.\"\n\n\"내 자신에 관한 일이야, 허버트.\" 내가 말했다. \"그리고 또 한 사람에 관한 일이지.\"\n\n허버트는 발을 꼬고, 고개를 한쪽으로 기울인 채 불을 바라보았으나, 한동안 헛되이 불만 들여다보다가, 내가 말을 잇지 않자 나를 쳐다보았다.\n\n\"허버트.\" 나는 그의 무릎에 손을 얹으며 말했다. \"나는 에스텔러를 사랑해. 흠모하고 있어.\"\n\n허버트는 깜짝 놀라 굳어지기는커녕, 아주 태연하고 당연하다는 듯이 대답했다. \"그렇군. 그래서?\"\n\n\"그래서라니, 허버트? 자네가 할 말이 그게 다인가? 그래서라니?\"\n\n\"그러니까 그다음은 뭐냐는 말이야.\" 허버트가 말했다. \"물론 나도 그건 알고 있으니까.\"\n\n\"그걸 어떻게 알았나?\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"그걸 어떻게 알았느냐고, 헨델? 아니, 자네한테서 알았지.\"\n\n\"난 자네한테 말한 적이 없는데.\"\n\"말이라니! 자네는 머리를 언제 잘랐는지도 내게 말한 적이 없지만, 나는 그걸 알아챌 만한 감각은 있다네. 자네는 내가 알게 된 이래로 줄곧 그 여자를 흠모해 왔어. 자네는 흠모하는 마음과 여행 가방을 함께 이리로 가져왔던 거야. 말이라니! 아니, 자네는 하루 종일 줄곧 내게 말하고 있었네. 자네가 자기 살아온 이야기를 들려줄 때, 아주 어릴 적 그 여자를 처음 본 순간부터 흠모하기 시작했다고 분명히 말했잖나.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 좋아.\" 이것이 내게는 새롭고도 싫지 않은 깨달음이었던 까닭에 나는 말했다. \"나는 한 번도 그녀를 흠모하기를 멈춘 적이 없어. 그리고 그녀가 더없이 아름답고 더없이 우아한 여인이 되어 돌아왔지. 나는 어제 그녀를 보았어. 전에도 그녀를 흠모했다면, 이제는 곱절로 흠모하고 있네.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 자네한테는 행운이로군, 헨델.\" 허버트가 말했다. \"자네가 그녀를 위해 점지되고 그녀에게 배정된 셈이니 말이야. 금지된 영역을 침범하지 않더라도, 우리끼리라면 그 사실에 의심의 여지가 없다고 감히 말해도 좋겠지. 그런데 자네는 그 흠모 문제에 대한 에스텔러의 생각이 어떤지 아직 짐작이라도 하고 있나?\"\n\n나는 침울하게 고개를 저었다. \"아! 그녀는 나한테서 수천 마일은 떨어져 있어.\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"참게나, 이보게 헨델. 시간은 충분해, 시간은 충분하다고. 그런데 자네, 더 할 말이 있는 게로군?\"\n\n\"말하기가 부끄럽네.\" 내가 대답했다. \"하지만 그걸 말하는 게 마음에 품는 것보다 더 나쁠 것도 없겠지. 자네는 나를 운 좋은 친구라고 했지. 물론 나는 그래. 어제까지만 해도 나는 대장장이의 조수 아이였는데, 오늘은 — 오늘의 나는 뭐라고 해야 할까?\"\n\n\"굳이 한마디로 표현하고 싶다면, 좋은 친구라고 해 두게.\" 허버트가 미소를 지으며 내 손등을 제 손으로 토닥이며 대답했다. \"좋은 친구, 그 안에 격정과 망설임, 대담함과 소심함, 행동과 몽상이 묘하게 뒤섞여 있는 친구 말이야.\"\n\n나는 잠시 멈추어 정말로 내 성격에 그런 혼합이 들어 있는지 곰곰이 생각해 보았다. 대체로 나는 그 분석을 결코 인정할 수 없었으나, 굳이 따져 볼 가치는 없다고 여겼다.\n\n\"오늘의 나를 뭐라 불러야 하느냐고 물은 건, 허버트.\" 나는 말을 이었다. \"내 생각 속에 있는 바를 넌지시 비치려는 거였어. 자네는 내가 운이 좋다고 하지. 나도 알아, 내가 스스로 입신하기 위해 한 일이라곤 아무것도 없고, 오로지 운명만이 나를 끌어올렸다는 걸. 그게 바로 대단히 운이 좋다는 거지. 그런데도 에스텔러를 생각하면—\"\n\n(\"그런데 자네가 그녀를 생각하지 않을 때가 언제 있나?\" 허버트가 불에 시선을 둔 채 한마디 끼워 넣었는데, 나는 그것이 그의 다정하고 공감 어린 마음이라고 생각했다.)\n\n\"—그러면, 이보게 허버트, 내가 얼마나 의존적이고 불안정하게 느끼는지, 얼마나 수백 가지 우연에 내맡겨져 있다고 느끼는지 자네한테 말로는 다 할 수가 없네. 방금 자네가 그랬듯 금지된 영역을 피해서 말하더라도, 내 모든 기대가 어느 한 사람의 변함없는 마음에(누구라고 이름은 대지 않겠네) 달려 있다고는 말할 수 있겠지. 그런데 잘되어 봤자, 그 기대가 무엇인지 이토록 어렴풋이밖에 모른다는 게 얼마나 막연하고 불만스러운 일인지!\" 이렇게 말하면서 나는, 어제 이후로 가장 심했던 것이 분명하긴 해도 늘 어느 정도는 거기 자리 잡고 있던 그 마음의 짐을 덜어 냈다.\n\n\"이보게, 헨델.\" 허버트가 그 특유의 명랑하고 희망찬 어조로 대답했다. \"내가 보기엔 우리가 이 애틋한 연정에 풀이 죽은 나머지, 거저 받은 말의 입속을 돋보기로 들여다보고 있는 격이야. 게다가 내가 보기엔, 그렇게 그 검사에만 정신을 쏟느라, 우리가 그 말이 지닌 가장 좋은 점 하나를 통째로 놓치고 있는 것 같단 말이지. 자네 후견인 재거스 씨가 애당초 자네한테, 자네가 기대만을 물려받은 게 아니라고 말했다지 않았나? 설령 그가 그런 말을 하지 않았다 하더라도—물론 그게 아주 큰 가정이라는 건 인정하네만—런던의 모든 사람들 가운데 하필 재거스 씨가, 자기 발판이 확실하지도 않은데 자네에게 지금과 같은 관계를 유지할 그런 사람이라고 자네는 믿을 수 있겠나?\"\n\n나는 그것이 강력한 논점임을 부인할 수 없다고 말했다. 나는 그 말을(사람들은 이런 경우 흔히 그러는 법인데), 진실과 정의에 마지못해 양보하기라도 하듯 내뱉었다. 마치 그것을 부인하고 싶기라도 한 것처럼!\n\n\"내 생각에도 그건 강력한 논점이야.\" 허버트가 말했다. \"그리고 자네가 이보다 더 강력한 논점을 떠올리려야 떠올릴 수 없으리라 생각하네. 그 나머지는, 자네가 후견인의 때를 기다려야 하고, 그 후견인은 또 자기 의뢰인의 때를 기다려야 하는 거지. 자네는 어느새 스물한 살이 될 테고, 그때쯤이면 아마 무언가 더 환히 알게 될 걸세. 어쨌든 거기에 더 가까이 다가가게 될 거야. 결국엔 반드시 밝혀질 테니까.\"\n\n\"자네는 어쩌면 그리도 희망에 찬 기질을 지녔나!\" 나는 그의 쾌활한 태도에 고마워하며 감탄하여 말했다.\n\n\"그래야지.\" 허버트가 말했다. \"달리 가진 게 별로 없으니까. 그런데 말이 나온 김에 인정하자면, 방금 내가 한 말의 분별 있는 부분은 내 것이 아니라 우리 아버지의 것이야. 자네 사연에 대해 아버지가 하시는 말씀을 내가 들은 건 딱 한 번, 마지막에 하신 이 말뿐이었네. '그 일은 이미 정해지고 매듭지어진 거다. 안 그랬다면 재거스 씨가 거기 끼어 있을 리 없으니까.' 그런데 이제 우리 아버지나 그 아버지의 아들에 대해 더 이야기하고, 비밀에 비밀로 보답하기에 앞서, 잠시 자네한테 진지하게 못마땅한 사람이 되어 보려 하네. 아주 정나미가 떨어지는 사람으로 말이지.\"\n\n\"성공하지 못할걸.\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"아니, 성공할 거야!\" 그가 말했다. \"하나, 둘, 셋, 자 이제 시작이네. 헨델, 이 사람아.\" 비록 그가 이렇게 가벼운 어조로 말하긴 했어도, 그는 무척 진지했다. \"우리가 이 난로 받침대에 발을 올리고 이야기를 나누는 동안 줄곧 생각해 봤는데, 자네 후견인이 에스텔러를 한 번도 언급하지 않았다면, 그녀가 자네 상속의 조건일 리는 결코 없을 것 같단 말이지. 그러니까 자네가 한 말을, 그가 그녀를 직접적으로든 간접적으로든 그 어떤 식으로도 결코 언급하지 않았다는 뜻으로 내가 이해하는 게 옳은가? 이를테면 자네의 후원자가 궁극적으로 자네의 혼사에 대해 어떤 뜻을 품고 있을지도 모른다는 것조차, 단 한 번도 내비치지 않았다는 거지?\"\n\n\"한 번도.\"\n\n\"이보게, 헨델, 내 영혼과 명예를 걸고 맹세하지만, 나는 신 포도 같은 심사에서는 완전히 자유롭다네! 자네가 그녀에게 매여 있는 것도 아닌데, 그녀에게서 마음을 떼어 낼 수는 없겠나?—내가 못마땅한 사람이 되겠다고 했잖나.\"\n\n나는 고개를 돌렸다. 바다에서 밀려 올라오던 그 옛 습지의 바람처럼 한 줄기 감정이 거세게 휘몰아쳐, 안개가 엄숙하게 피어오르던 아침, 내가 대장간을 떠나 마을 이정표에 손을 얹었을 때 나를 무너뜨렸던 바로 그것과 똑같은 감정이, 다시금 내 가슴을 후려쳤기 때문이다. 잠시 우리 사이에 침묵이 흘렀다.\n\n\"그래, 하지만 이보게 헨델.\" 허버트는 우리가 침묵하고 있었던 게 아니라 계속 이야기를 나누고 있었던 것처럼 말을 이었다. \"그게 천성과 환경 탓에 그토록 낭만적인 기질을 지니게 된 한 소년의 가슴속에 그토록 깊이 뿌리내렸다는 사실이, 이 일을 아주 심각하게 만드는 거야. 그녀가 어떻게 자라났는지 생각해 보게, 그리고 미스 해비셤을 생각해 보게. 그녀 자신이 어떤 사람인지 생각해 보란 말이야(이제 나는 정나미 떨어지는 사람이 되었고 자네는 나를 혐오하겠지). 이건 비참한 결말로 이어질 수도 있어.\"\n\n\"나도 알아, 허버트.\" 나는 여전히 고개를 돌린 채 말했다. \"하지만 어쩔 수가 없어.\"\n\n\"마음을 떼어 낼 수가 없나?\"\n\n\"없어. 불가능해!\"\n\n\"애써 볼 수도 없겠나, 헨델?\"\n\n\"없어. 불가능해!\"\n\n\"좋아!\" 허버트는 마치 잠에서 깬 사람처럼 활기차게 몸을 한 번 흔들며 일어나 불을 쑤석이며 말했다. \"이제 다시 유쾌한 사람이 되어 보도록 애써 봐야겠군!\"\n\n그러고서 그는 방 안을 돌아다니며 커튼을 털고, 의자를 제자리에 놓고, 여기저기 흩어져 있던 책 따위를 정돈하고, 현관을 들여다보고, 우편함을 살짝 엿보고, 문을 닫고서, 난롯가의 자기 의자로 돌아왔다. 그러고는 자리에 앉아 두 팔로 자신의 왼쪽 다리를 끌어안았다.\n\n\"우리 아버지와 그 아버지의 아들에 관해 두어 마디 하려던 참이었지, 헨델. 우리 아버지의 살림이 그 가정 운영에 있어 그다지 훌륭하지는 못하다는 것쯤은, 그 아버지의 아들이 굳이 말할 필요도 없을 것 같네만.\"\n\n\"늘 풍족하던걸, 허버트.\" 나는 무언가 격려가 될 말을 하려고 말했다.\n\n\"오, 그렇지! 내 생각에 쓰레기 치우는 사람도 더없이 흡족해하며 그렇게 말할 테고, 뒷골목의 잡화 가게도 그럴 거야. 진지하게 말해서, 헨델, 이건 제법 진지한 문제이니 하는 말인데, 자네도 나만큼이나 사정을 잘 알지 않나. 한때는 우리 아버지가 살림을 내팽개치지 않으셨던 시절이 있었던 것 같아. 하지만 설령 그런 시절이 있었다 한들, 그 시절은 이미 지나갔어. 자네한테 한 가지 물어봐도 될까. 자네 고향에서, 그다지 어울리지 않는 혼인으로 태어난 자식들이 하나같이 유난히 결혼을 하고 싶어 안달하더라는 걸, 혹시 눈여겨본 적이 있나?\"\n\n이것이 어찌나 묘한 질문이던지, 나는 도리어 그에게 되물었다. \"그런가?\"\n\n\"나도 모르겠네.\" 허버트가 말했다. \"그게 바로 내가 알고 싶은 거야. 왜냐하면 우리 집은 확실히 그러하거든. 가엾은 내 누이 샬럿은, 내 바로 아래였는데 열네 살도 되기 전에 죽었지만, 그야말로 두드러진 예였지. 어린 제인도 마찬가지야. 혼인으로 자리 잡고 싶어 하는 그 열망을 보면, 그 짧은 생애를 줄곧 가정의 행복을 마음에 그리며 보냈다고 여겨질 정도라니까. 아직 치마저고리를 입고 다니는 어린 알릭조차도 벌써 큐에 사는 어울리는 처녀와 짝지을 채비를 다 해 놓았다네. 사실 막내 갓난아기만 빼면 우리는 다들 약혼한 셈인 것 같아.\"\n\n\"그럼 자네도 약혼했단 말인가?\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"그렇다네.\" 허버트가 말했다. \"하지만 비밀이야.\"\n나는 그 비밀을 지키겠노라고 그를 안심시키고, 좀 더 자세한 사정을 들려주기를 청했다. 그가 내 약점에 대해 그토록 분별 있고 다정하게 말해 주었기에, 나는 그의 강점에 대해서도 무언가 알고 싶었다.\n\n\"이름을 물어봐도 될까?\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"이름은 클래라라네.\" 허버트가 말했다.\n\n\"런던에 사나?\"\n\n\"그래, 어쩌면 이 말은 해 둬야겠군.\" 흥미로운 화제로 접어든 뒤로 묘하게 풀이 죽고 고분고분해진 허버트가 말했다. \"그녀는 우리 어머니의 그 터무니없는 가문 관념에 비추면 다소 격이 낮은 편이야. 그녀 아버지는 여객선의 식량 조달과 관련된 일을 했지. 일종의 사무장 같은 사람이었던 것 같아.\"\n\n\"지금은 무슨 일을 하시나?\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"지금은 병자라네.\" 허버트가 대답했다.\n\n\"무엇으로 살아가시는데?\"\n\n\"이 층에서 살고 계시지.\" 허버트가 말했다. 내가 의도한 바는 전혀 그게 아니었으니, 내 질문은 그의 생계 수단을 두고 한 것이었기 때문이다. \"나는 그분을 한 번도 본 적이 없네. 내가 클래라를 알게 된 이래로 그분은 줄곧 위층 자기 방에만 틀어박혀 계셨으니까. 하지만 그분 소리는 끊임없이 들었지. 무시무시한 소란을 피우신다네. 고래고래 소리를 지르고, 뭔가 끔찍한 도구로 바닥을 쾅쾅 두드리지.\" 나를 바라보다가 호탕하게 웃으면서, 허버트는 잠시나마 평소의 명랑한 태도를 되찾았다.\n\n\"그분을 만나게 될 거라고 기대하지는 않나?\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"오, 그럼, 나는 끊임없이 그분을 만나게 되리라 기대하지.\" 허버트가 대답했다. \"그분 소리를 들을 때마다 천장을 뚫고 와르르 떨어져 내리시지 않을까 하는 기대를 안 한 적이 없으니까. 다만 그 서까래가 얼마나 더 버텨 줄지는 모르겠네만.\"\n\n또 한 번 호탕하게 웃고 나서 그는 다시 고분고분해지더니, 자본을 좀 모으기 시작하는 순간 이 아가씨와 결혼할 작정이라고 내게 말했다. 그러고는 풀 죽게 만드는 자명한 명제라도 되는 양 이렇게 덧붙였다. \"하지만 알다시피, 이리저리 기회를 엿보고 있는 동안에는 결혼이란 걸 할 수가 없는 법이지.\"\n\n우리가 불을 바라보고 있는 동안, 그리고 바로 그 자본이라는 것이 때로는 얼마나 실현하기 어려운 환상인가를 내가 생각하고 있는 동안, 나는 주머니에 손을 찔러 넣었다. 그중 한쪽 주머니에 든 접힌 종잇조각이 눈길을 끌기에, 나는 그것을 펴 보았고, 그것이 조에게서 받은 연극 광고지, 그러니까 그 명성 자자한 로스키우스풍의 지방 아마추어 배우에 관한 광고지임을 알았다. \"아니, 이런 세상에.\" 나는 나도 모르게 소리 내어 덧붙였다. \"그게 바로 오늘 밤이잖아!\"\n\n이 일로 화제가 단숨에 바뀌었고, 우리는 부랴부랴 연극을 보러 가기로 마음먹었다. 그리하여 내가 허버트의 연애 문제에 있어 가능한 수단이든 불가능한 수단이든 가리지 않고 그를 위로하고 돕겠노라 다짐하고, 허버트가 자기 약혼녀는 이미 소문으로 나를 알고 있으며 내가 그녀에게 소개될 거라고 말하고, 우리가 서로의 신뢰를 두고 뜨겁게 악수를 나누고 나서, 우리는 촛불을 끄고, 난롯불을 돋우고, 문을 잠근 뒤, 워프슬 씨와 덴마크를 찾아 길을 나섰다.\n* 로스키우스: 고대 로마의 이름난 희극 배우. 명배우를 가리키는 대명사로 쓰인다.\n\n# Chapter XXXI.\n\n덴마크에 도착해 보니, 그 나라의 왕과 왕비가 부엌 식탁 위에 놓인 두 개의 안락의자에 높이 올라앉아 어전 회의를 열고 있었다. 덴마크 귀족 일동이 시중을 들고 있었는데, 그 면면은 이러했다. 거인 같은 조상에게서 물려받은 무두질 가죽 장화를 신은 고귀한 소년 하나, 인생 후반에야 평민에서 출세한 듯 보이는 더러운 얼굴의 위엄 있는 귀족 하나, 그리고 머리에 빗을 꽂고 흰 비단 다리를 한 채 전체적으로 여성스러운 분위기를 풍기는 덴마크 기사도였다. 재능을 타고난 나의 고향 사람은 팔짱을 끼고 침울하게 한쪽에 떨어져 서 있었는데, 나는 그의 곱슬머리와 이마가 좀 더 그럴듯해 보였으면 하고 바랐다.\n\n극이 진행되는 동안 몇 가지 신기하고 자잘한 일들이 벌어졌다. 이 나라의 선왕은 서거할 무렵 기침에 시달리고 있었던 듯할 뿐 아니라, 그 기침을 무덤까지 가지고 갔다가 다시 가지고 돌아온 듯했다. 또 그 왕의 망령은 지휘봉 둘레에 유령의 원고를 지니고 다녔는데, 이따금 그것을 들여다보는 모양새였고, 그것도 불안한 기색으로 어디를 보고 있었는지 곧잘 잊어버리는 품이, 살아 있는 인간의 상태를 떠오르게 했다. 내 짐작으로는 바로 이 때문에 망령은 위층 관람석으로부터 \"넘겨!\" 하는 충고를 들었을 텐데 — 그 망령은 이 권고를 몹시 언짢게 받아들였다. 또 이 장엄한 혼령에 대해 짚어 둘 점은, 언제나 오랫동안 바깥에 나와 있다가 엄청난 거리를 걸어온 듯한 분위기로 등장하면서도, 정작 바로 옆에 붙은 벽에서 나오는 것이 빤히 보였다는 사실이다. 이 탓에 그의 공포는 비웃음으로 받아들여졌다. 덴마크 왕비는 매우 풍만한 부인이었는데, 역사적으로야 의심할 바 없이 뻔뻔한 여자였겠으나, 관객들은 그녀가 몸에 놋쇠를 너무 많이 두르고 있다고 여겼다. 그녀의 턱은 (마치 화려한 치통이라도 앓는 듯이) 넓은 놋쇠 띠로 왕관에 붙들어 매여 있었고, 허리도 또 다른 놋쇠 띠로 둘려 있었으며, 두 팔도 각각 또 다른 띠로 감겨 있었으니, 사람들은 대놓고 그녀를 \"큰북\"이라고 불렀다. 조상의 장화를 신은 고귀한 소년은 일관성이 없었으니, 말하자면 한숨에 자기 자신을 유능한 뱃사람이자, 떠돌이 배우이자, 무덤 파는 인부이자, 성직자이자, 궁정 검술 시합에서 더없이 중요한 인물 — 그 노련한 눈과 정확한 판별력의 권위로 가장 멋진 칼놀림을 가려내는 인물 — 로 내세웠다. 이 때문에 차츰 그에 대한 관용이 사라졌고, 급기야 — 그가 성직에 몸담은 것이 들통나고도 장례식 집전을 거부하자 — 군중의 분노가 호두알 세례의 형태로 터져 나오기에 이르렀다. 끝으로, 오필리아는 어찌나 더디게 음악적인 광기에 사로잡혀 있던지, 시간이 한참 흘러 그녀가 흰 모슬린 스카프를 벗어 개켜서 묻고 있을 때, 위층 관람석 맨 앞줄에서 오래도록 안달 난 코를 철창에 갖다 대며 식히고 있던 한 뚱한 사내가 으르렁대듯 말했다. \"이제 아기도 재웠으니 저녁이나 먹읍시다!\" 아무리 좋게 말한대도 그것은 분위기에 어울리지 않는 소리였다.\n\n이 모든 사건은 나의 가엾은 고향 사람 위에 장난스러운 효과를 더하며 차곡차곡 쌓여 갔다. 그 우유부단한 왕자가 무언가를 묻거나 의문을 토로해야 할 때마다, 관객은 그를 거들어 주었다. 예컨대, 마음속으로 견디는 것이 더 고귀한가 어떤가 하는 물음이 나오자, 누구는 그렇다고 고함치고 누구는 아니라고 고함쳤으며, 양쪽 의견에 다 솔깃해진 누구는 \"동전 던져서 정해!\" 하고 외쳤으니, 흡사 토론회 하나가 생겨난 셈이었다. 그가 자기 같은 작자들이 하늘과 땅 사이를 기어 다니며 무엇을 해야 하느냐고 물었을 때, 그는 \"옳소, 옳소!\" 하는 우렁찬 외침으로 격려를 받았다. 그가 양말을 흐트러뜨린 채 등장했을 때(관례에 따라 그 흐트러짐은 양말 윗부분의 아주 깔끔한 주름 하나로 표현되었는데, 내 짐작으로는 늘 다리미로 잡은 것이리라), 위층 관람석에서는 그의 다리가 창백한 것을 두고, 그것이 유령이 그에게 안겨 준 충격 탓인지 아닌지에 관해 대화가 오갔다. 그가 리코더를 집어 들었을 때 — 그것은 방금 오케스트라석에서 연주하다가 문간으로 건네받은 작고 검은 플루트와 매우 흡사했는데 — 관객들은 한목소리로 그에게 \"지배하라 브리타니아\"를 청했다. 그가 배우에게 그런 식으로 허공을 톱질하듯 휘젓지 말라고 충고하자, 그 뚱한 사내가 말했다. \"당신도 그러지 마쇼, 당신이 저 사람보다 훨씬 더 심하니까!\" 그리고 안타깝게도 덧붙여 말하건대, 이런 일이 있을 때마다 웝슬 씨에게는 와르르 터지는 폭소가 쏟아졌다.\n\n그러나 그가 가장 혹독한 시련을 겪은 곳은 교회 묘지였으니, 그곳은 한쪽에 작은 교회 부속 세탁장 같은 것이, 다른 쪽에는 통행료 징수 문이 있어, 흡사 태곳적 원시림 같은 모양새였다. 통이 넓은 검은 망토를 두른 웝슬 씨가 통행료 징수 문으로 들어서는 것이 눈에 띄자, 무덤 파는 인부에게 친절한 충고가 날아들었다. \"조심해! 저기 장의사가 자네 일이 어떻게 돼 가나 보러 오는구먼!\" 헌정 국가에서라면 웝슬 씨가 해골을 두고 일장 훈계를 늘어놓은 뒤, 품에서 꺼낸 흰 냅킨에 손가락의 먼지를 털지 않고서는 도저히 그것을 돌려놓을 수 없었으리라는 것이 익히 알려진 사실이라고 나는 믿는다. 그러나 그 순결하고도 없어서는 안 될 동작조차 \"웨이터!\" 하는 외침 없이는 지나가지 못했다. 매장할 시신이 (뚜껑이 덜렁덜렁 열린 빈 검은 상자에 담겨) 도착한 것은 온통 환호를 자아내는 신호였는데, 그 환호는 관을 멘 사람들 가운데서 신원을 들켜 미움을 살 만한 인물 하나가 발견되면서 한층 드높아졌다. 그 환호는 오케스트라석과 무덤의 가장자리에서 웝슬 씨가 레어티즈와 격투를 벌이는 내내 따라다녔고, 그가 부엌 식탁에서 왕을 떨어뜨리고 발목부터 위로 차츰차츰 죽어 갈 때까지 조금도 누그러지지 않았다.\n\n처음에 우리는 웝슬 씨에게 박수를 보내려고 맥없이 몇 번 애를 써 보았으나, 그것은 너무도 가망 없는 일이라 계속할 수가 없었다. 그래서 우리는 그를 절절히 가엾게 여기면서도, 그럼에도 입이 귀에 걸리도록 웃으며 앉아 있었다. 나는 내내 나 자신도 어쩌지 못한 채 웃었다. 그 모든 것이 어찌나 우스꽝스러웠던지. 그러면서도 나는 웝슬 씨의 낭독에 어딘가 단연 훌륭한 데가 있다는 막연한 인상을 품고 있었다 — 옛정 때문은 아닐까 싶어 두렵지만, 사실은 그것이 몹시 느리고, 몹시 음울하고, 몹시 오르락내리락했으며, 삶이나 죽음의 그 어떤 자연스러운 상황에서도 사람이 무언가에 대해 자신을 표현하는 그 어떤 방식과도 몹시 달랐기 때문이었다. 비극이 끝나고, 그가 호명을 받고 야유를 받았을 때, 나는 허버트에게 말했다. \"당장 갑시다, 안 그러면 그와 마주칠지도 모르니까요.\"\n\n우리는 있는 힘껏 서둘러 아래층으로 내려갔으나, 그것마저도 충분히 빠르지 못했다. 문간에 부자연스럽게 짙은 눈썹 칠을 한 유대인 사내가 서 있었는데, 우리가 다가가자 그 눈이 내 시선을 붙잡았고, 우리가 가까이 이르자 이렇게 말했다.\n\n\"핍 씨와 친구분이신가요?\"\n\n핍 씨와 친구라는 신원을 우리는 시인했다.\n\n\"월든가버 씨께서,\" 사내가 말했다. \"두 분을 모시는 영광을 누리고 싶어 하십니다.\"\n\n\"월든가버요?\" 내가 되물었다 — 그때 허버트가 내 귀에 속삭였다. \"아마 웝슬일 겁니다.\"\n\n\"아!\" 내가 말했다. \"네. 따라가면 됩니까?\"\n\n\"몇 걸음만요.\" 우리가 옆 골목에 들어서자 사내는 돌아서서 물었다. \"그분이 어때 보이던가요? — 제가 의상을 입혀 드렸지요.\"\n\n나는 그가 어때 보였는지 알 수 없었다, 장례식 같다는 것 말고는. 거기에 파란 리본으로 목에 매단 커다란 덴마크 태양인지 별인지가 더해져, 흡사 무슨 별난 화재 보험에라도 든 사람 같은 꼴이었다. 그러나 나는 그가 아주 근사해 보였다고 말했다.\n\n\"그분이 무덤가에 이르렀을 때,\" 우리를 안내하던 사내가 말했다. \"망토를 아주 멋들어지게 보여 주셨지요. 하지만 무대 옆에서 보자니, 왕비의 침실에서 유령을 봤을 때는 양말을 좀 더 잘 살릴 수도 있지 않았나 싶더군요.\"\n\n나는 겸손하게 동의했고, 우리 모두는 작고 더러운 회전문을 지나 그 바로 뒤에 있는 무슨 뜨거운 포장 상자 같은 곳으로 우르르 떨어지듯 들어섰다. 여기서 웝슬 씨는 덴마크 의상을 벗고 있었고, 우리는 그 포장 상자의 문, 즉 뚜껑을 활짝 열어 둔 채 서로의 어깨너머로 그를 들여다볼 만한 자리밖에 없었다.\n\n\"여러분,\" 웝슬 씨가 말했다. \"두 분을 뵙게 되어 자랑스럽습니다. 핍 씨, 사람을 보내 청한 것을 부디 용서해 주시기 바랍니다. 저는 지난날 당신을 알게 된 행복을 누렸고, 연극이란 늘 고귀한 분들과 부유한 분들에게 권리를 주장해 왔으며, 그 권리는 늘 인정받아 왔지요.\"\n\n그러는 동안 월든가버 씨는 무시무시하게 땀을 뻘뻘 흘리며 왕자의 검은 의상에서 빠져나오려 애쓰고 있었다.\n\n\"월든가버 씨한테서 양말 가죽 벗기듯 살살 벗겨 내,\" 그 의상의 주인이 말했다. \"안 그러면 터뜨려 먹어. 터뜨렸다 하면 삼십오 실링을 터뜨리는 거다. 셰익스피어도 이보다 더 좋은 양말을 받쳐 입은 적이 없어. 자, 의자에 가만히 앉아 계시고, 나한테 맡기쇼.\"\n\n그 말과 함께 사내는 무릎을 꿇고 자기 희생자의 가죽을 벗기기 시작했는데, 첫 번째 양말이 벗겨질 때 그 사람은 의자째 뒤로 나자빠질 것이 분명했으나, 도무지 어디로든 넘어질 자리가 없는 덕분에 그러지 않았다.\n\n그때까지 나는 연극에 관해 한마디 꺼내기가 두려웠다. 그러나 그때 월든가버 씨가 흡족한 얼굴로 우리를 올려다보며 말했다.\n\n\"여러분, 앞에서 보시기에 공연이 어떻게 진행되는 것 같던가요?\"\n\n허버트가 뒤에서 (동시에 나를 쿡 찌르며) 말했다. \"훌륭했지요.\" 그래서 나도 \"훌륭했습니다\" 하고 말했다.\n\n\"제가 그 배역을 해석한 방식이 마음에 드셨습니까, 여러분?\" 월든가버 씨가 거의, 완전히는 아니더라도, 거의 생색을 내는 투로 말했다.\n\n허버트가 뒤에서 (다시 나를 쿡 찌르며) 말했다. \"묵직하고 견고했지요.\" 그래서 나는 마치 그 말을 내가 처음 생각해 내기라도 한 듯, 그리고 그 점을 꼭 강조해야겠다는 듯 대담하게 말했다. \"묵직하고 견고했습니다.\"\n\n\"여러분의 찬사를 받으니 기쁩니다.\" 월든가버 씨가 위엄 있는 태도로 말했는데, 그러는 동안에도 그는 벽에 밀쳐져 의자 바닥을 붙들고 있었다.\n\n\"하지만 한 가지 말씀드리지요, 월든가버 씨,\" 무릎을 꿇고 있던 사내가 말했다. \"당신 해석에서 빗나간 데가 있어요. 자, 잘 들으쇼! 누가 반대하든 난 상관 안 합니다. 당신한테 그렇게 말하는 거요. 당신은 다리를 옆모습으로 둘 때 햄릿 해석이 빗나가요. 내가 마지막으로 의상을 입혀 준 햄릿도 연습 때 같은 실수를 했었지요, 그래서 내가 그 사람 정강이마다 큼직한 빨간 봉인지를 붙이게 했는데, 그러고 나서 그 연습 때(그게 마지막 연습이었어요) 내가 객석 앞쪽, 일층 뒤편까지 나가서, 그 사람 해석이 옆모습으로 갈 때마다 '봉인지가 안 보이는데!' 하고 외쳐 댔지요. 그랬더니 밤 공연 때는 그 사람 해석이 아주 근사하더군요.\"\n\n월든가버 씨는 나를 향해 미소 지었는데, 흡사 \"충직한 아랫것이지요 — 저는 저자의 어리석음을 너그러이 봐줍니다\" 하고 말하는 듯했다. 그러더니 소리 내어 말했다. \"제 견해는 여기 사람들이 보기엔 좀 고전적이고 사색적이지요. 하지만 저들도 나아질 겁니다, 나아질 거예요.\"\n\n허버트와 나는 한목소리로 말했다. 아, 그들도 틀림없이 나아질 거라고.\n\n\"여러분, 보셨습니까,\" 월든가버 씨가 말했다. \"위층 관람석에 그 예배를 — 그러니까, 그 공연을 비웃으려 든 자가 하나 있던 것을?\"\n\n우리는 비굴하게도, 아무래도 그런 자를 본 것 같다고 대답했다. 나는 덧붙였다. \"틀림없이 술에 취했을 겁니다.\"\n\n\"아, 천만에요, 선생,\" 웝슬 씨가 말했다. \"취한 게 아닙니다. 그자의 고용주가 그렇게 두지 않을 테니까요, 선생. 그자의 고용주가 그를 취하도록 내버려 두지 않을 겁니다.\"\n\n\"그자의 고용주를 아십니까?\" 내가 말했다.\n\n웝슬 씨는 눈을 감았다가 다시 떴는데, 그 두 동작을 모두 매우 천천히 해냈다. \"여러분도 보셨을 겁니다,\" 그가 말했다. \"긁어 대는 듯한 목청과 저열한 악의가 드러나는 표정을 한, 무지하고 떠들썩한 멍청이가 — 감히 소화해 냈다고는 말하지 않겠습니다만 — 덴마크 왕 클로디어스의 배역(프랑스 말을 빌려 써도 된다면)을 그럭저럭 해 나가는 것을요. 그자가 바로 그 고용주입니다, 여러분. 이게 바로 이 직업이라는 것이지요!\"\n\n웝슬 씨가 절망에 빠져 있었더라면 그가 더 가여웠을지 어땠을지 분명히 알 수는 없었으나, 나는 지금 이대로의 그가 어찌나 가엾던지, 그가 멜빵을 채우려고 몸을 돌린 틈 — 그 바람에 우리는 문간으로 밀려났는데 — 을 타서, 허버트에게 그를 집으로 데려가 저녁을 대접하면 어떻겠느냐고 물었다. 허버트는 그것이 친절한 일일 것 같다고 했다. 그래서 나는 그를 초대했고, 그는 눈까지 푹 싸맨 채 우리와 함께 바너드 여관으로 갔으며, 우리는 그를 위해 최선을 다했다. 그는 새벽 두 시까지 앉아 자신의 성공을 돌이켜 보고 앞으로의 계획을 펼쳐 놓았다. 그 계획이 자세히 어떤 것이었는지는 잊어버렸으나, 그가 연극을 부흥시키는 일로 시작해 연극을 짓밟는 일로 끝맺으려 했다는 것만큼은 어렴풋이 기억난다. 그도 그럴 것이, 그가 죽고 나면 연극은 완전히 버려져 일말의 기회도 희망도 없어질 터였으니까.\n\n결국 나는 비참한 심정으로 잠자리에 들었고, 비참하게 에스텔러를 생각했으며, 비참하게도 내 유산이 죄다 취소되어, 허버트의 클래라와 결혼하여 손을 내주거나, 아니면 단 스무 마디도 외우지 못한 채 이만 명의 관객 앞에서 해비셤 양의 유령을 상대로 햄릿을 연기해야 하는 꿈을 꾸었다.\n\n# Chapter XXXII.\n\n어느 날, 내가 책과 포켓 씨를 상대로 분주히 공부하고 있을 때, 나는 우편으로 한 통의 쪽지를 받았다. 그 겉봉만 보고도 나는 큰 동요에 휩싸였다. 비록 그 주소를 쓴 필체를 한 번도 본 적은 없었으나, 나는 그것이 누구의 손에서 나온 것인지 직감했던 것이다. 친애하는 핍 씨, 혹은 친애하는 핍, 혹은 친애하는 선생님, 혹은 친애하는 그 무엇이라는 식의 정해진 서두도 없이, 그 글은 이렇게 이어졌다.\n\n\"모레 정오 마차로 런던에 가게 되었어요. 당신이 저를 마중하기로 정해진 것으로 알고 있는데요? 어쨌든 해비셤 양께서 그렇게 알고 계셔서, 저는 그 뜻에 따라 이 글을 씁니다. 그분이 안부를 전하라 하시네요.\n\n당신의 에스텔러로부터.\"\n\n시간이 있었더라면 나는 아마 이번 기회를 위해 옷을 여러 벌 맞추었을 것이다. 그러나 시간이 없었으므로, 나는 가진 옷으로 만족하는 수밖에 없었다. 입맛은 순식간에 사라졌고, 그날이 오기까지 나는 평온도 안식도 알지 못했다. 그렇다고 그날이 온다고 해서 평온이나 안식이 찾아온 것도 아니었다. 그날이 되자 나는 어느 때보다 더 안절부절못했고, 우리 마을의 블루 보어에서 마차가 아직 떠나지도 않았을 무렵부터 이미 칩사이드의 우드 가에 있는 마차 사무소 주변을 서성이기 시작했으니까. 이 사실을 더없이 잘 알고 있었음에도, 나는 여전히 마차 사무소를 한 번에 오 분 이상 시야에서 놓치는 것은 안전하지 않다고 느꼈다. 이런 비이성적인 상태로 나는 네댓 시간에 걸친 감시의 첫 삼십 분을 보내고 있었는데, 그때 웨믹이 나와 마주쳐 부딪쳤다.\n\n\"이런, 핍 씨.\" 그가 말했다. \"안녕하십니까? 여기가 당신이 다니실 구역이라고는 도무지 생각지 못했는데요.\"\n\n나는 마차로 올라오는 누군가를 마중하려고 기다리는 중이라고 설명한 뒤, 캐슬과 노인장의 안부를 물었다.\n\n\"덕분에 둘 다 잘 지냅니다.\" 웨믹이 말했다. \"특히 노인장께서요. 기력이 아주 좋으시지요. 다음 생신이면 여든둘이 되십니다. 이웃들이 불평만 하지 않고, 제 그 대포가 그 압력을 견뎌 준다면, 여든두 발을 쏠까 하는 생각이 있습니다. 하지만 이건 런던에서 할 이야기는 아니지요. 제가 지금 어디로 가는 길인지 아시겠습니까?\"\n\n\"사무소로요?\" 그가 그쪽 방향으로 향하고 있었기에 내가 말했다.\n\n\"거기랑 가까운 데지요.\" 웨믹이 대답했다. \"뉴게이트로 가는 길입니다. 지금 은행 소포 사건을 하나 맡고 있는데, 제가 그 길을 따라 내려가 사건 현장을 한번 살펴보고 왔고, 그러고 나서 우리 의뢰인과 한두 마디 나눠야 하거든요.\"\n\n\"당신 의뢰인이 그 강도질을 저질렀습니까?\" 내가 물었다.\n\n\"천만의 말씀, 아닙니다.\" 웨믹이 아주 무미건조하게 대답했다. \"하지만 그 혐의를 받고 있지요. 당신이나 나도 그럴 수 있습니다. 우리 둘 중 누구라도 그런 혐의를 받을 수 있는 거예요, 아시다시피.\"\n\n\"다만 우리 둘 다 그렇지 않을 뿐이죠.\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"하!\" 웨믹이 집게손가락으로 내 가슴을 툭 건드리며 말했다. \"당신은 보통이 아니시군요, 핍 씨! 뉴게이트를 한번 둘러보시겠습니까? 시간 여유가 있으신지요?\"\n\n내게는 시간 여유가 어찌나 많았던지, 그 제안은 마차 사무소에서 눈을 떼지 않으려는 내 은밀한 욕구와는 도무지 양립할 수 없었음에도, 오히려 안도감으로 다가왔다. 나는 그와 함께 걸어갈 시간이 있는지 알아보겠다고 중얼거리며 사무소로 들어가, 마차가 도착할 수 있는 가장 이른 시각을 직원에게서 더없이 세밀한 정확성으로, 또한 그의 인내심을 적잖이 시험해 가며 확인했다. 그 시각은 그 직원만큼이나 나도 미리 잘 알고 있던 것이었다. 그러고 나서 나는 웨믹 씨에게로 돌아가, 회중시계를 들여다보는 척하고 방금 들은 정보에 놀란 척하면서, 그의 제안을 받아들였다.\n\n우리는 몇 분 만에 뉴게이트에 이르렀고, 헐벗은 벽에 감옥 규칙들 사이로 족쇄 몇 개가 걸려 있는 수위실을 지나 감옥 안쪽으로 들어갔다. 그 시절에는 감옥이 몹시 등한시되었고, 모든 공적 비행에 뒤따르는 과장된 반작용의 시기 — 그것이야말로 언제나 가장 무겁고 가장 긴 형벌인데 — 는 아직 한참 멀었다. 그래서 중죄인들이 병사들보다 (빈민들은 말할 것도 없고) 더 나은 숙식을 제공받는 일도 없었고, 수프 맛을 개선한다는 그럴듯한 명목으로 제 감옥에 불을 지르는 일도 좀처럼 없었다. 웨믹이 나를 데리고 들어간 때는 면회 시간이었고, 맥줏집 사환 하나가 맥주를 들고 돌아다니고 있었다. 그리고 마당의 창살 뒤편에서 죄수들은 맥주를 사거나 친구들과 이야기를 나누고 있었다. 추레하고 흉하고 무질서하며 사람의 기를 꺾는 광경이었다.\n\n웨믹이 죄수들 사이를 걸어 다니는 모습은, 마치 정원사가 제 화초들 사이를 거니는 것과 흡사하다는 생각이 내게 들었다. 이런 생각이 처음 떠오른 것은, 그가 밤새 돋아난 새싹 하나를 보고는 \"아니, 톰 선장 아니신가? 자네가 거기 있었어? 아, 그렇구먼!\" 하고 말하는 것을 보았을 때였다. 또한 \"물통 뒤에 있는 게 블랙 빌인가? 이런, 자네를 두 달 동안이나 못 봤군그래. 어떻게 지내나?\" 하고 말하는 것을 들었을 때이기도 했다. 창살 앞에 멈춰 서서 — 언제나 한 사람씩 — 근심에 차 속삭이는 자들에게 귀를 기울일 때도 마찬가지로, 웨믹은 그 우체통 같은 입을 꿈쩍도 않는 상태로 둔 채 그들과 의논하며, 마치 지난번에 보았을 때 이후로 그들이 재판에서 활짝 꽃피어 나오기까지 얼마나 진척되었는지를 각별히 주목하는 듯한 눈길로 그들을 바라보았다.\n\n그는 대단히 인기가 있었고, 나는 그가 재거스 씨 업무 중에서 친근하게 사람을 대하는 부분을 맡고 있음을 알게 되었다. 다만 그에게도 재거스 씨 특유의 위엄 같은 것이 얼마간 감돌아, 일정한 선을 넘어 다가오는 것을 허락하지 않았다. 잇따라 찾아오는 의뢰인 하나하나를 그가 알아보았다는 표시는, 고갯짓 한 번과, 두 손으로 모자를 머리 위에 좀 더 편하게 고쳐 쓰고는, 이어 우체통 같은 입을 꽉 다물고 두 손을 주머니에 찔러 넣는 것으로 끝났다. 한두 번은 수임료를 마련하는 문제로 곤란한 일이 있었는데, 그럴 때면 웨믹 씨는 내밀어진 부족한 돈에서 가능한 한 멀찍이 물러서며 이렇게 말했다. \"소용없네, 이 사람아. 나는 한낱 부하일 뿐이야. 그건 받을 수 없어. 부하한테 그런 식으로 굴지 말게. 자네가 정해진 액수를 채울 수 없다면, 이 사람아, 차라리 주인 되는 분께 직접 말씀드리는 게 나아. 이 바닥에 주인 되는 사람은 얼마든지 있고, 한 사람에게는 수고할 가치가 없는 일이 다른 사람에게는 수고할 가치가 있을 수도 있는 법이지. 그게 부하 된 입장에서 자네에게 드리는 권고일세. 쓸데없는 수단은 부리려 들지 말게. 그래 봤자 뭘 하겠나? 자, 다음은 누구지?\"\n\n이리하여 우리는 웨믹의 온실을 두루 거닐었고, 마침내 그가 내게 돌아서서 말했다. \"내가 악수를 나눌 사람을 눈여겨보십시오.\" 그가 일러 주지 않았더라도 나는 그렇게 했을 것이다. 그때까지 그는 아무와도 악수를 나누지 않았으니까.\n\n그가 말을 마치기가 무섭게, 풍채 좋고 꼿꼿한 한 사내가 (지금 이 글을 쓰는 순간에도 눈에 선하다) 닳고 닳은 올리브색 프록코트 차림으로, 불그레한 안색 위로 묘한 창백함이 번지고, 눈을 한곳에 고정하려 해도 자꾸만 이리저리 헤매는 눈을 한 채, 창살 한 귀퉁이로 다가와 모자에 손을 가져갔다. 차게 식은 묽은 수프처럼 번들거리고 기름진 표면을 한 그 모자에. 그러고는 반쯤은 진지하고 반쯤은 익살스러운 군대식 거수경례를 했다.\n\n\"대령께 인사 올립니다!\" 웨믹이 말했다. \"어떻게 지내십니까, 대령?\"\n\n\"좋습니다, 웨믹 씨.\"\n\n\"할 수 있는 일은 다 했습니다만, 증거가 우리로서는 너무 강력했지요, 대령.\"\n\n\"그래요, 너무 강력했지요, 선생. 하지만 나는 개의치 않습니다.\"\n\n\"네, 네.\" 웨믹이 차분하게 말했다. \"당신은 개의치 않으시지요.\" 그러고는 내게로 돌아서며 말했다. \"이 사람은 폐하를 위해 복무했습니다. 정규 보병으로 군 복무를 하다가 돈을 주고 제대했지요.\"\n\n내가 \"그렇습니까?\" 하고 말하자, 사내의 두 눈이 나를 보았다가, 다시 내 머리 위를 보았다가, 다시 내 주위를 빙 둘러보았고, 그러고 나서 그는 손으로 제 입술을 쓱 훔치며 웃었다.\n\n\"저는 월요일이면 여기서 나갈 것 같습니다, 선생.\" 그가 웨믹에게 말했다.\n\n\"아마도요.\" 내 친구가 대답했다. \"하지만 알 수 없는 일이지요.\"\n\n\"이렇게 작별 인사를 드릴 기회가 있어 기쁩니다, 웨믹 씨.\" 사내가 창살 두 개 사이로 손을 내밀며 말했다.\n\n\"고맙습니다.\" 웨믹이 그와 악수하며 말했다. \"당신도 잘 지내시길, 대령.\"\n\n\"제가 붙잡혔을 때 몸에 지니고 있던 것이 진짜였더라면, 웨믹 씨.\" 사내가 그의 손을 좀처럼 놓으려 하지 않으며 말했다. \"당신이 베풀어 주신 배려에 대한 감사의 표시로, 반지를 하나 더 끼워 주십사 청을 드렸을 텐데요.\"\n\n\"그 뜻만으로도 한 것이나 다름없이 받겠습니다.\" 웨믹이 말했다. \"그건 그렇고, 당신은 비둘기 애호가셨다지요.\" 사내가 하늘을 올려다보았다. \"공중제비 비둘기로 아주 보기 드문 종을 기르셨다고 들었습니다. 더 이상 쓸데가 없으시다면, 친구분께 부탁해 제게 한 쌍을 가져다주게 하실 수 있겠습니까?\"\n\n\"그렇게 하지요, 선생.\"\n\n\"좋습니다.\" 웨믹이 말했다. \"잘 보살펴 주겠습니다. 안녕히 계십시오, 대령. 그럼 또!\" 두 사람은 다시 악수했고, 우리가 걸어 나오는 동안 웨믹이 내게 말했다. \"위조 주화범인데, 솜씨가 아주 좋은 자입니다. 오늘 재판 기록관의 보고가 올라가니, 월요일이면 틀림없이 처형될 겁니다. 하지만 보시다시피, 어쨌든 그것대로, 비둘기 한 쌍은 그래도 운반할 수 있는 재산이지요.\" 그 말과 함께 그는 뒤를 돌아보고 이 죽은 화초를 향해 고갯짓을 하더니, 마당을 빠져나오면서, 마치 그 자리에 어떤 화분이 가장 잘 어울릴까를 궁리하는 사람처럼 주위로 눈길을 둘렀다.\n\n우리가 수위실을 거쳐 감옥에서 나올 때, 나는 내 후견인의 그 대단한 위세가 그들이 맡아 가두고 있는 자들 못지않게 간수들에게도 인정받고 있음을 알게 되었다. \"그런데 말입니다, 웨믹 씨.\" 대못 박힌 두 짝의 수위실 문 사이에 우리를 세워 두고, 한쪽을 잠근 뒤에야 다른 쪽을 여는 간수가 말했다. \"그 강가 살인 사건은 재거스 씨가 어떻게 처리하실 작정입니까? 과실치사로 만드실 건가요, 아니면 어떻게 만드실 작정이래요?\"\n\n\"왜 그분께 직접 여쭤보지 않으시오?\" 웨믹이 대꾸했다.\n\n\"오, 그래요, 여쭤보고말고요!\" 간수가 말했다.\n\n\"바로 저런 식이라니까요, 핍 씨.\" 웨믹이 그 우체통 같은 입을 길쭉하게 늘이며 내게로 돌아서서 말했다. \"부하인 나한테는 뭐든 거리낌 없이 물어 대지만, 내 주인 되는 분께 무얼 묻는 모습은 결코 보지 못하실 겁니다.\"\n\n\"이 젊은 신사분은 댁의 사무소에 다니는 견습생이나 수습 서기 가운데 한 사람입니까?\" 간수가 웨믹의 농담에 씩 웃으며 물었다.\n\n\"또 저런다니까요, 보십시오!\" 웨믹이 외쳤다. \"제가 말씀드렸지요! 첫 번째 질문에 답할 잉크도 마르기 전에 부하한테 또 질문을 던진다니까요! 자, 핍 씨가 그중 한 사람이라고 칩시다. 그러면요?\"\n\n\"그렇다면야.\" 간수가 다시 씩 웃으며 말했다. \"재거스 씨가 어떤 분인지 알 거 아니오.\"\n\n\"하!\" 웨믹이 익살스럽게 별안간 간수를 향해 주먹질하는 시늉을 하며 외쳤다. \"내 주인 되는 분을 상대할 때면 당신도 당신 열쇠 중 하나만큼이나 입을 꾹 다물면서, 잘도 그러시는군. 어서 내보내 주시오, 이 늙은 여우 양반, 안 그러면 내가 그분께 부탁해서 당신을 불법 감금죄로 고소하게 만들 테니.\"\n\n간수는 웃으며 우리에게 잘 가라고 인사했고, 우리가 계단을 내려가 거리로 들어설 때 쪽문의 대못 너머로 우리를 바라보며 서서 웃고 있었다.\n\n\"잘 새겨 두십시오, 핍 씨.\" 웨믹이 좀 더 은밀히 이야기하려 내 팔을 잡으며 진지하게 내 귀에 대고 말했다. \"내 보기엔 재거스 씨가 그토록 높은 자리를 지켜 내는 그 방식보다 더 잘하는 일은 없는 것 같습니다. 그분은 늘 그렇게 높이 계시지요. 그 한결같은 높이가 그분의 엄청난 역량과 한 짝을 이룹니다. 저 대령이 그분께 작별 인사를 감히 건넬 수 없는 것은, 저 간수가 어느 사건을 두고 그분의 의중을 감히 묻지 못하는 것과 마찬가지지요. 그러고서 그분은 당신의 그 높이와 그들 사이에 부하인 나를 슬쩍 끼워 넣는 겁니다. 아시겠습니까? 그렇게 해서 그들을 송두리째, 몸과 마음을 다 손아귀에 쥐는 거지요.\"\n\n나는 내 후견인의 그 교묘함에 깊은 감명을 받았는데, 이번이 처음은 아니었다. 솔직히 고백하자면, 나는 차라리 역량이 그보다 못한 다른 후견인을 두었더라면 좋았을 것이라고 진심으로 바랐는데, 이 또한 처음 있는 일이 아니었다.\n\n웨믹 씨와 나는 리틀 브리튼의 사무소에서 헤어졌는데, 그곳에는 재거스 씨의 눈에 들기를 바라는 청원자들이 여느 때처럼 서성이고 있었다. 그리고 나는 세 시간쯤을 남겨 둔 채, 마차 사무소가 있는 거리에서 다시 감시 자세로 돌아갔다. 나는 그 시간 내내, 내가 감옥과 범죄의 이 온갖 오욕에 둘러싸여 있다는 것이 얼마나 기이한 일인가를 생각하며 보냈다. 어린 시절 어느 겨울 저녁 우리 고장의 외딴 습지에서 내가 그것과 처음 마주쳤다는 것, 그것이 바랬으되 사라지지는 않은 얼룩처럼 두 차례나 다시 불쑥 나타났다는 것, 그것이 이제 이런 새로운 방식으로 내 재산과 출세에 두루 스며들고 있다는 것을 생각하면서. 이렇게 마음이 어지러운 와중에, 나는 도도하고 기품 있는 아름다운 젊은 에스텔러가 내게로 다가오는 모습을 떠올렸고, 감옥과 그녀 사이의 그 대비를 절대적인 혐오감으로 떠올렸다. 차라리 웨믹이 나와 마주치지 않았더라면, 아니면 내가 그의 말에 넘어가 그를 따라가지 않았더라면 좋았을 것을, 그리하여 일 년 중 하필 이날만은 내 숨결과 옷에 뉴게이트를 묻혀 두지 않았더라면 좋았을 것을 하고 바랐다. 나는 이리저리 거니는 동안 발에서 감옥의 먼지를 털어 냈고, 옷에서도 그것을 떨어냈으며, 내 폐에서도 그 공기를 내뿜었다. 누가 오는지를 떠올리니 어찌나 오염된 기분이었던지, 마차는 그래도 빨리 도착해 버렸고, 마차 창가에 어른거리는 그녀의 얼굴과 내게 흔드는 그녀의 손을 보았을 때 나는 아직 웨믹 씨의 그 온실에 더럽혀진 의식에서 벗어나지도 못한 상태였다.\n\n그 한순간에 또다시 스쳐 지나간 그 이름 모를 그림자는 대체 무엇이었을까?\n\n# Chapter XXXIII.\n\n털 장식이 달린 여행복을 입은 에스텔라는, 내 눈에조차도, 지금껏 보아 온 그 어느 때보다 한층 섬세하게 아름다워 보였다. 그녀의 태도는 전에 내게 보여 주려 하던 것보다 더 사람을 끄는 데가 있었고, 나는 그 변화에서 해비셤 양의 영향을 본 듯한 생각이 들었다.\n\n우리는 여관 마당에 서 있었고, 그녀는 내게 자기 짐을 가리켜 주었다. 짐이 모두 모이자, 그동안 그녀 말고는 모든 것을 잊고 있었던 나는, 그녀가 어디로 가는지조차 아무것도 모른다는 사실을 떠올렸다.\n\n\"리치먼드로 가요.\" 그녀가 내게 말했다. \"우리가 배운 바로는 리치먼드가 둘인데, 하나는 서리에 있고 하나는 요크셔에 있다지요. 내가 가는 곳은 서리의 리치먼드예요. 거리는 십 마일이고요. 나는 마차를 타게 되어 있고, 당신은 나를 데려다주게 되어 있어요. 이건 내 지갑이고, 당신은 이 안에서 내 비용을 치러야 해요. 아, 지갑을 받아야 해요! 당신과 나는, 지시를 따르는 것 말고는 달리 선택의 여지가 없어요. 당신과 나는 우리 뜻대로 할 자유가 없는 거예요.\"\n\n지갑을 건네며 그녀가 나를 바라보았을 때, 나는 그 말에 어떤 숨은 뜻이 있기를 바랐다. 그녀는 무심하게 그 말을 했지만, 못마땅한 기색은 아니었다.\n\n\"마차를 불러야겠군요, 에스텔라. 여기서 잠깐 쉬겠어요?\"\n\n\"네, 여기서 잠깐 쉬고, 차를 좀 마시기로 되어 있어요. 그동안 당신이 나를 돌봐 주기로 되어 있고요.\"\n\n그녀는 마땅히 그래야 한다는 듯 내 팔에 자기 팔을 끼웠고, 나는 평생 그런 물건은 본 적도 없는 사람처럼 마차를 빤히 쳐다보고 있던 웨이터에게 별실 하나를 안내해 달라고 청했다. 그러자 그는, 그것이 없으면 이층으로 가는 길도 못 찾는 마법의 실마리라도 되는 양 냅킨을 꺼내 들고는, 우리를 그 가게의 검은 굴 속으로 이끌었다. 그곳에는 점점 작아 보이게 하는 거울 하나(굴의 크기를 생각하면 아주 쓸데없는 물건이었다)와 멸치 소스 병 하나, 그리고 누군가의 나막신이 놓여 있었다. 내가 이 은신처에 이의를 제기하자, 그는 우리를 서른 명이 앉을 만찬 탁자가 놓인 다른 방으로 데려갔는데, 벽난로 쇠살대 안에는 한 말은 됨직한 석탄 가루 밑에 그을린 습자책 한 장이 들어 있었다. 그는 이 꺼져 버린 화재를 들여다보고는 고개를 절레절레 흔들더니 내 주문을 받았다. 그런데 그 주문이라는 것이 고작 \"이 숙녀분께 차 좀 주시오\"였던 탓에, 그는 몹시 기가 죽은 채로 방을 나갔다.\n\n이 방의 공기는, 마구간 냄새와 수프 국물 냄새가 강하게 뒤섞인 탓에, 마차 영업 부문이 신통치 못하고 진취적인 주인이 음식 부문을 위해 말을 고아 내고 있다는 추측을 불러일으킬 만했다. 나는 그때도 그것을 알았고 지금도 안다. 그러나 에스텔라가 그 안에 있었으므로, 그 방은 내게 전부였다. 나는 그녀와 함께라면 그곳에서 평생 행복할 수 있으리라 생각했다. (잘 봐 두시라, 나는 그때 그곳에서 조금도 행복하지 않았고, 그것을 잘 알고 있었다.)\n\n\"리치먼드에서는 어디로 가나요?\" 나는 에스텔라에게 물었다.\n\n\"가서 살게 돼요.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"큰돈을 들여, 그곳의 한 부인과 함께요. 그 부인은 나를 데리고 다니며 소개해 주고, 사람들을 내게 보여 주고 나를 사람들에게 보여 줄 힘을 가졌다나요. 아니, 가졌다고 하더군요.\"\n\n\"변화도 있고 사람들의 찬사도 받게 되니 기쁘겠군요?\"\n\n\"네, 그럴 것 같네요.\"\n\n그녀가 어찌나 무심하게 대답하던지, 나는 이렇게 말했다. \"당신은 자기 얘기를 마치 다른 사람 얘기하듯 하는군요.\"\n\n\"내가 남들 얘기를 어떻게 하는지는 또 어디서 배웠대요? 이봐요, 이봐요.\" 에스텔라가 매혹적으로 미소 지으며 말했다. \"내가 당신한테 배우러 다닐 거라곤 기대하지 말아요. 나는 내 방식대로 말할 거예요. 포켓 선생님 댁에서는 잘 지내요?\"\n\n\"거기서 아주 즐겁게 지내요. 적어도ㅡ\" 나는 기회를 놓치고 있는 듯한 느낌이 들었다.\n\n\"적어도?\" 에스텔라가 따라 말했다.\n\n\"당신을 떠나 어디서든 지낼 수 있는 만큼은 즐겁게요.\"\n\n\"바보 같은 사람.\" 에스텔라가 아주 침착하게 말했다. \"어떻게 그런 허튼소리를 할 수 있죠? 당신 친구 매슈 선생님은, 내가 듣기로 그 집안의 다른 사람들보다 훨씬 나으신 분이라던데요?\"\n\n\"정말 훨씬 나으신 분이죠. 그분은 누구의 적도 아니ㅡ\"\n\n\"자기 자신 말고는, 이라고 덧붙이진 말아요.\" 에스텔라가 끼어들었다. \"나는 그런 부류의 사람을 싫어하니까요. 하지만 그분은 정말로 사심이 없고, 자잘한 시기나 악의 따위를 초월한 분이라던데, 맞나요?\"\n\n\"그렇게 말할 만한 충분한 이유가 내겐 있다고 확신해요.\"\n\n\"그 집안의 다른 사람들에 대해서는 그렇게 말할 이유가 도무지 없을걸요.\" 에스텔라가, 진지하면서도 놀리는 듯한 표정으로 내게 고개를 끄덕이며 말했다. \"그 사람들은 당신한테 불리한 소문과 빈정거림으로 해비셤 양을 들들 볶고 있으니까요. 그들은 당신을 감시하고, 당신을 모함하고, 당신에 관한 편지를 쓰고(때로는 익명으로요), 당신은 그들 삶의 고민거리이자 일거리예요. 그 사람들이 당신한테 품고 있는 증오가 어느 정도인지는 당신 스스로도 거의 실감하지 못할 거예요.\"\n\n\"그들이 내게 해를 끼치진 못하겠죠?\"\n\n대답하는 대신 에스텔라는 웃음을 터뜨렸다. 이것은 내게 무척 묘한 일이어서, 나는 적잖이 어리둥절해하며 그녀를 바라보았다. 그녀가 웃음을 그쳤을 때ㅡ그녀는 나른하게가 아니라 진심으로 즐거워하며 웃었던 것인데ㅡ나는 그녀를 대할 때면 늘 그러하듯 머뭇거리며 말했다.\n\n\"그들이 내게 무슨 해라도 끼친다면 당신이 즐거워하지는 않으리라고 생각해도 되겠지요.\"\n\n\"그럼요, 그럼요, 그건 믿어도 돼요.\" 에스텔라가 말했다. \"내가 웃는 건 그들이 실패하기 때문이라는 걸 확실히 믿어도 좋아요. 아, 해비셤 양 곁에 붙어 있는 그 사람들이라니, 또 그들이 겪는 고통이라니!\" 그녀가 다시 웃었고, 이제 이유를 말해 준 뒤에도 그 웃음은 내게 무척 묘하게 느껴졌다. 진심이 아닐 리는 없었지만, 그렇다고 하기엔 이 상황에 비해 너무 지나친 웃음 같았기 때문이다. 나는 여기에 내가 아는 것보다 무언가 더 있는 게 분명하다고 생각했다. 그녀는 내 마음속의 그 생각을 알아채고는 거기에 답했다.\n\n\"그 사람들이 좌절하는 꼴을 보는 것이 내게 얼마나 큰 만족을 주는지, 그들이 우스운 꼴이 될 때 내가 얼마나 그 우스꽝스러움을 즐기는지는, 당신조차도 알기 쉽지 않을 거예요.\" 에스텔라가 말했다. \"당신은 갓난아기 때부터 그 이상한 집에서 자라지 않았으니까요. 나는 그랬어요. 당신은 동정이며 연민이며 그 밖에 부드럽고 다독여 주는 척하는 온갖 가면 아래에서, 억눌리고 무방비인 채, 자기를 노리는 음모들 때문에 어린 분별력을 날카롭게 벼리지 않았으니까요. 나는 그랬어요. 당신은 그 사기꾼 같은 여자를 발견하면서 동그란 어린아이 눈을 점점 더 크게 뜨지는 않았으니까요. 그 여자는 밤중에 깨어났을 때를 대비해 자기 마음의 평안을 비축해 두며 계산하는 사람이지요. 나는 그랬어요.\"\n\n이제 에스텔라에게 그것은 웃을 일이 아니었고, 그녀가 이런 기억들을 불러내는 곳도 어떤 얕은 데가 아니었다. 내 기대의 전부를 한데 쌓아 준다 해도, 나는 그녀의 그런 표정을 불러일으킨 장본인이 되고 싶지는 않았다.\n\n\"두 가지를 말해 줄 수 있어요.\" 에스텔라가 말했다. \"첫째, 낙숫물이 댓돌을 뚫는다는 속담이 있긴 하지만, 그 사람들이 해비셤 양에 대한 당신의 입지를 크든 작든 어떤 점에서도 결코 손상시키지 못하리라는 것ㅡ백 년이 가도 못 하리라는 것은 마음 놓아도 돼요. 둘째, 그들이 그토록 분주하고 그토록 비열하게 굴어도 헛수고가 되게 만든 원인이 당신이니, 나는 당신에게 빚을 진 셈이에요. 자, 여기 그 증표로 내 손을 내밀게요.\"\n\n그녀가 장난스럽게 손을 내밀었기에ㅡ그녀의 어두운 기분은 잠깐뿐이었으니까ㅡ나는 그 손을 잡아 입술에 갖다 댔다. \"우스운 사람.\" 에스텔라가 말했다. \"당신은 끝내 경고를 새겨듣지 않을 건가요? 아니면 예전에 내가 당신더러 내 뺨에 입 맞추게 했을 때와 똑같은 심정으로 내 손에 입 맞추는 건가요?\"\n\n\"그게 무슨 심정이었는데요?\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"잠깐 생각해 봐야겠네요. 아첨꾼들과 모사꾼들을 향한 경멸의 심정이었죠.\"\n\n\"그렇다고 대답하면, 다시 뺨에 입 맞춰도 될까요?\"\n\n\"손을 건드리기 전에 물었어야죠. 하지만, 좋아요, 그러고 싶다면요.\"\n\n나는 몸을 숙였고, 그녀의 차분한 얼굴은 조각상의 그것 같았다. \"자.\" 내가 뺨에 입을 대는 순간 미끄러지듯 물러나며 에스텔라가 말했다. \"당신은 내가 차를 마실 수 있게 신경 써 주고, 나를 리치먼드까지 데려다주기로 되어 있어요.\"\n\n마치 우리의 동행이 우리에게 강요된 것이고 우리는 한낱 꼭두각시에 불과하다는 듯, 그녀가 다시 그런 말투로 돌아간 것이 내게는 괴로웠다. 그러나 우리가 주고받는 모든 것이 내게는 괴로움이었다. 그녀가 내게 어떤 말투를 쓰든, 나는 거기에 아무 신뢰도 둘 수 없었고 어떤 희망도 쌓아 올릴 수 없었다. 그러면서도 나는 신뢰를 거슬러, 희망을 거슬러 계속 나아갔다. 이것을 천 번이고 되풀이해 말한들 무엇하랴? 늘 그런 식이었다.\n\n나는 종을 울려 차를 청했고, 웨이터는 마법의 실마리를 들고 다시 나타나더니, 그 다과에 딸린 부속품 쉰 가지쯤을 차례차례 들여왔으되, 정작 차는 그림자도 비치지 않았다. 찻쟁반, 찻잔과 받침, 접시들, 나이프와 포크(고기 써는 큰 것까지 포함해서), 숟가락(여러 가지), 소금 그릇들, 튼튼한 쇠 뚜껑 아래 더없이 조심스레 갇힌 얌전한 작은 머핀 하나, 잔뜩 쌓인 파슬리 속에 놓인 부드러운 버터 한 조각으로 형상화된 갈대밭의 모세, 머리에 가루를 뿌린 빛바랜 빵 한 덩이, 세모난 빵 조각 위에 부엌 벽난로 쇠살대의 모양이 고스란히 찍힌 자국 두 점, 그리고 마침내 뚱뚱한 가족용 주전자 하나ㅡ웨이터는 얼굴에 무게와 고통을 드러내며 그것을 비틀비틀 들고 들어왔다. 이 대접의 단계에 이르러 한참을 자리를 비웠다가, 그는 마침내 잔가지가 담긴 값나가 보이는 함 하나를 들고 돌아왔다. 나는 그 잔가지를 뜨거운 물에 우려, 결국 이 온갖 기구들로부터 에스텔라를 위해 무엇인지 모를 차 한 잔을 우려냈다.\n\n계산을 치르고, 웨이터에게 사례하고, 마부도 잊지 않고, 객실 하녀까지 고려하고ㅡ한마디로, 온 여관을 경멸과 적의의 상태에 빠지도록 매수하고, 에스텔라의 지갑은 한결 가벼워진 다음ㅡ우리는 역마차에 올라 떠났다. 칩사이드로 접어들어 뉴게이트 거리를 덜컹거리며 올라가자, 우리는 이내 내가 그토록 부끄러워하던 그 담벼락 아래에 이르렀다.\n\n\"저긴 무슨 곳이에요?\" 에스텔라가 내게 물었다.\n\n나는 처음에는 알아보지 못하는 척 어리석은 시늉을 하다가, 이윽고 그녀에게 일러 주었다. 그녀가 그곳을 바라보다가 다시 고개를 들이밀며 \"가엾은 것들!\" 하고 중얼거리는 동안, 나는 무슨 일이 있어도 내가 거기 다녀왔다는 사실은 털어놓지 않았으리라.\n\n\"재거스 씨는요.\" 나는 그것을 누군가 다른 사람에게 깔끔하게 떠넘기는 셈치고 말했다. \"런던의 그 누구보다도 저 음산한 곳의 비밀에 정통하다는 평판이 있죠.\"\n\n\"내 생각엔, 그분은 어디든 모든 곳의 비밀에 더 정통한 것 같아요.\" 에스텔라가 낮은 목소리로 말했다.\n\n\"당신은 그분을 자주 보는 데 익숙하겠지요?\"\n\n\"기억할 수 있는 때부터 줄곧, 정해지지 않은 간격을 두고 그분을 봐 오는 데 익숙해요. 하지만 지금도 그분을 또렷이 말도 못 하던 시절보다 더 잘 알지는 못해요. 당신은 그분에 대해 어떤 경험을 했나요? 그분과 사이가 진전되나요?\"\n\n\"일단 그분의 의심 많은 태도에 익숙해지고 나서는.\" 내가 말했다. \"아주 잘 지내 왔어요.\"\n\n\"친밀한가요?\"\n\n\"그분 자택에서 함께 식사한 적이 있어요.\"\n\n\"내 생각엔.\" 에스텔라가 움츠러들며 말했다. \"거긴 분명 묘한 곳일 거예요.\"\n\n\"묘한 곳이지요.\"\n\n나는 그녀에게라도 내 후견인 이야기를 너무 함부로 하는 것은 삼갔어야 했을 것이다. 그러나 그때 우리가 별안간 가스등의 환한 불빛 속으로 들어서지만 않았더라면, 나는 그 화제를 이어 가 제라드 거리의 만찬 이야기까지 묘사했을 것이다. 그 불빛은 그것이 지속되는 동안, 전에 내가 느꼈던 그 설명할 수 없는 감정으로 온통 환하게 살아 있는 듯했다. 그리고 우리가 그 불빛을 벗어났을 때, 나는 마치 번개를 맞기라도 한 것처럼 잠깐 동안 멍해 있었다.\n\n그래서 우리는 다른 이야기로 빠져들었는데, 주로 우리가 가고 있는 길에 관한 것, 그리고 런던의 어느 지역이 이쪽에 있고 어느 지역이 저쪽에 있는지에 관한 것이었다. 그녀가 내게 말하기를, 이 거대한 도시는 자기에게 거의 낯선 곳이라고 했다. 프랑스로 떠나기 전까지는 해비셤 양의 동네를 한 번도 벗어난 적이 없었고, 그때도 오가는 길에 그저 런던을 지나쳤을 뿐이라는 것이었다. 나는 그녀가 이곳에 머무는 동안 내 후견인이 그녀를 맡고 있는지 물었다. 그러자 그녀는 단호하게 \"당치도 않아요!\"라고만 말하고 더는 말하지 않았다.\n\n그녀가 나를 끌어당기려 마음 쓰고 있다는 것, 스스로를 매력적으로 만들고 있으며 설령 그 일에 애를 써야 했더라도 나를 사로잡았으리라는 것을, 나는 도저히 못 본 척할 수 없었다. 그러나 이것이 나를 조금도 더 행복하게 만들지는 못했다. 설령 그녀가 우리가 남들에 의해 처분되는 처지라는 그런 말투를 쓰지 않았더라도, 나는 그녀가 내 마음을 손아귀에 쥐고 있는 까닭이, 그 마음을 짓이겨 내버리는 일이 그녀에게 어떤 애틋함이라도 자아내기 때문이 아니라, 단지 그녀가 일부러 그러기로 택했기 때문임을 느꼈을 테니까.\n\n해머스미스를 지날 때 나는 그녀에게 매슈 포켓 선생이 사는 곳을 가리켜 보이며, 리치먼드에서 그리 멀지 않으니 이따금 당신을 볼 수 있기를 바란다고 말했다.\n\n\"아 그럼요, 나를 보게 될 거예요. 적당하다 싶을 때 오면 돼요. 당신 이야기는 그 댁 식구들에게 일러두기로 되어 있어요. 실은 이미 일러두었고요.\"\n\n나는 그녀가 식구가 될 그 집이 큰 집안이냐고 물었다.\n\n\"아니요, 둘뿐이에요. 어머니와 딸이지요. 그 어머니는 신분이 좀 있는 부인인데, 그렇다고 수입을 늘리는 일을 마다하지는 않아요.\"\n\n\"해비셤 양이 그렇게 빨리 당신을 또 떠나보낼 수 있다니 의외군요.\"\n\n\"그게 나에 대한 해비셤 양의 계획의 일부예요, 핍.\" 에스텔라가 지친 듯 한숨을 쉬며 말했다. \"나는 그분께 끊임없이 편지를 쓰고 정기적으로 찾아뵙고 어떻게 지내는지 보고해야 해요. 나도, 그리고 보석들도요. 이제 그 보석들은 거의 다 내 것이거든요.\"\n\n그녀가 내 이름을 부른 것은 그때가 처음이었다. 물론 그녀는 일부러 그렇게 한 것이었고, 내가 그것을 소중히 간직하리라는 것을 알고 있었다.\n\n우리는 너무도 빨리 리치먼드에 이르렀고, 그곳 우리의 목적지는 잔디밭 곁에 있는 한 집이었다. 차분하고 오래된 집으로, 둥근 테 버팀살과 머리분과 애교점, 수놓은 외투, 둘둘 말아 올린 양말, 주름 장식과 검 따위가 여러 번 그 화려한 사교의 나날을 누렸던 곳이었다. 집 앞의 오래된 나무 몇 그루는 여전히, 그 둥근 테나 가발이나 빳빳한 치마만큼이나 격식 차린 부자연스러운 모양으로 다듬어져 있었다. 그러나 죽은 것들의 그 거대한 행렬에서 그들 자신에게 정해진 자리도 멀지 않았으니, 그들도 곧 거기에 떨어져 나머지 모든 것이 간 그 고요한 길을 따라가게 될 터였다.\n\n낡은 목소리를 지닌 종 하나가ㅡ아마도 그 시절에는 이 집을 향해, 초록색 버팀치마가 왔습니다, 다이아몬드 자루 검이 왔습니다, 빨간 굽 구두와 푸른 외알 보석 장식이 왔습니다, 하고 곧잘 알렸을 그 종이ㅡ달빛 속에서 장중하게 울렸고, 버찌 빛 옷을 입은 하녀 둘이 팔랑팔랑 나와 에스텔라를 맞았다. 현관은 이내 그녀의 짐 상자들을 빨아들였고, 그녀는 내게 손과 미소를 건네며 잘 자라는 인사를 하고는, 그녀 또한 그렇게 빨려 들어갔다. 그래도 나는 여전히 그 집을 바라보며 서서, 그녀와 함께 저곳에서 산다면 얼마나 행복할까 생각하면서도, 내가 그녀와 함께 있어 결코 행복했던 적이 없으며 늘 비참했을 뿐임을 알고 있었다.\n\n나는 해머스미스로 돌아가기 위해 마차에 올랐고, 가슴앓이를 안고 올랐다가, 더 심한 가슴앓이를 안고 내렸다. 우리 집 문 앞에서 나는, 어린 제인 포켓이 어린 연인의 호위를 받으며 어느 조촐한 모임에서 집으로 돌아오는 것을 보았다. 그리고 나는 그 어린 연인이 플롭슨에게 휘둘리는 처지인데도 불구하고, 그가 부러웠다.\n\n포켓 선생은 강연을 하러 나가고 없었다. 그는 가정 경제에 관한 더없이 유쾌한 강연자였으며, 아이들과 하인들을 다루는 법에 관한 그의 논저들은 그 주제에 관한 한 가장 훌륭한 교본으로 여겨졌으니 말이다. 그러나 포켓 부인은 집에 있었고, 작은 곤경에 빠져 있었다. 밀러스가 (근위 보병대에 있는 친척에게 가느라) 영문 모를 자리를 비운 사이, 아기를 조용히 시키느라 바늘쌈지를 쥐여 주었던 탓이었다. 그런데 사라진 바늘의 수가, 그토록 어린 환자가 겉에 바르든 강장제 삼아 먹든 도무지 몸에 이롭다고 볼 수 없을 만큼이나 많았다.\n\n포켓 선생은 더없이 훌륭한 실질적 조언을 해 주는 것으로, 또 사물에 대한 분명하고 건전한 통찰과 매우 사려 깊은 정신을 지닌 것으로 마땅히 이름이 높았던 터라, 나는 가슴앓이 속에서 그에게 내 비밀을 털어놓아 달라 청해 볼까 하는 생각이 어렴풋이 들었다. 그러나 아기에게 잠자리를 만병통치약으로 처방해 준 뒤 작위(爵位) 명부를 읽고 앉아 있는 포켓 부인을 마침 올려다보다가, 나는 생각했다ㅡ글쎄ㅡ아니, 그만두자고.\n\n# Chapter XXXIV.\n\n내가 내 유산에 익숙해지면서, 나는 어느새 그것이 나 자신과 주위 사람들에게 끼치는 영향을 눈여겨보기 시작했다. 그것이 내 성격에 미친 영향을 나는 가능한 한 스스로 인정하지 않으려 애썼으나, 그것이 결코 다 좋은 것만은 아니라는 사실은 너무도 잘 알고 있었다. 나는 조에게 대하는 내 태도에 관해 만성적인 불안 상태에 빠져 살았다. 비디에 대한 내 양심도 결코 편안하지 않았다. 한밤중에 깨어날 때면 — 카밀라처럼 — 나는 마음에 무거운 권태를 느끼며 이런 생각을 하곤 했다. 미스 해비셤의 얼굴을 한 번도 보지 않았더라면, 그리고 정직한 옛 대장간에서 조와 동업자가 되는 것에 만족하며 어른이 되었더라면, 나는 더 행복하고 더 나은 사람이 되었으리라고. 저녁이면 여러 번, 혼자 앉아 불을 바라보면서, 나는 따지고 보면 집 대장간의 불과 부엌의 불만 한 불은 어디에도 없다고 생각했다.\n\n그러나 에스텔라는 내 모든 안절부절못함과 마음의 불안과 너무도 떼어 놓을 수 없었으므로, 나는 그 불안을 만들어 내는 데 내 몫이 어디까지인지 그 한계를 두고 정말로 혼란에 빠지고 말았다. 다시 말해, 만약 내게 유산에 대한 기대가 전혀 없었다 해도 여전히 에스텔라를 생각해야 했다면, 내 처지가 지금보다 훨씬 나았으리라고 만족스럽게 결론 내릴 수가 없었던 것이다. 그런데 내 처지가 다른 사람들에게 미치는 영향에 관해서는 그런 어려움이 없었으니, 그래서 나는 — 아마 어렴풋이나마 — 그것이 누구에게도 이롭지 않다는 것을, 무엇보다도 허버트에게 이롭지 않다는 것을 알아차렸다. 내 헤픈 습관은 그의 무던한 천성을 그가 감당할 수 없는 지출로 이끌었고, 그의 소박한 삶을 타락시켰으며, 그의 평온을 근심과 후회로 어지럽혔다. 나는 포켓 집안의 다른 곁가지들을 무심코 그들이 부리던 그 옹졸한 술수로 몰아넣은 것에 대해서는 조금도 자책하지 않았다. 그러한 자질구레함은 그들의 타고난 기질이었으므로, 내가 그것을 잠들게 내버려 두었더라도 다른 누군가에 의해 불려 나왔을 것이기 때문이다. 그러나 허버트의 경우는 사정이 아주 달랐으니, 검소하게 꾸며진 그의 방을 어울리지 않는 가구 장식으로 가득 채우고 카나리아 가슴빛 복수자를 그가 부리도록 안겨 줌으로써 그에게 못쓸 짓을 했다는 생각이 자주 나를 찔러 댔다.\n\n그래서 이제, 작은 안락을 큰 안락으로 만드는 틀림없는 방법으로, 나는 빚을 잔뜩 끌어들이기 시작했다. 내가 시작하기가 무섭게 허버트도 따라 시작할 수밖에 없었으니, 그도 곧 뒤를 따랐다. 스타톱의 제안으로 우리는 '숲속의 핀치들'이라는 클럽의 회원으로 선출되도록 이름을 올렸다. 그 단체의 목적이 회원들이 보름에 한 번씩 비싼 식사를 하고, 식후에는 가능한 한 자기들끼리 싸우고, 여섯 명의 종업원을 계단에서 취하게 만드는 것이 아니라면, 나는 그 목적을 끝내 짐작조차 할 수 없었다. 이 흐뭇한 사교적 목표들이 어찌나 한결같이 달성되었던지, 허버트와 나는 이 단체의 첫 번째 정례 건배사가 다른 무엇을 가리키는 것이라고는 도무지 이해할 수 없었다. 그 건배사는 이러했다. \"신사 여러분, 부디 지금 이 화기애애한 분위기가 숲속의 핀치들 사이에 길이 으뜸으로 군림하기를.\"\n\n핀치들은 돈을 어리석게 써 댔으며(우리가 식사한 호텔은 코번트 가든에 있었다), 내가 영광스럽게도 숲에 가입했을 때 처음 본 핀치는 벤틀리 드러믈이었다. 그는 그 무렵 자기 마차를 몰고 시내를 비틀비틀 헤매고 다니면서 길모퉁이 기둥들에 막대한 손해를 입히고 있었다. 이따금 그는 자기 마차의 가림막 너머로 머리부터 곤두박질쳐 떨어지곤 했다. 한번은 그가 이런 의도치 않은 방식으로 — 마치 석탄처럼 — 숲의 문 앞에 자기 몸을 부려 놓는 것을 본 적도 있었다. 그러나 여기서 나는 조금 앞서 가고 있는 셈이니, 나는 핀치가 아니었고, 또 이 단체의 신성한 규약에 따르면 성년이 되기 전까지는 핀치가 될 수도 없었기 때문이다.\n\n내 재력에 대한 자신감에서, 나는 기꺼이 허버트의 비용을 내가 떠맡고 싶었으나, 허버트는 자존심이 강했으므로 그에게 그런 제안을 할 수가 없었다. 그래서 그는 사방에서 곤경에 빠진 채, 계속해서 두리번거리며 기회를 살폈다. 우리가 차츰 늦은 시간과 늦은 교제에 빠져들면서, 나는 그가 아침 식사 때면 낙담한 눈으로 주위를 둘러보고, 한낮쯤이면 좀 더 희망에 찬 눈으로 둘러보기 시작하며, 저녁 식사 자리에 들어올 때면 풀이 죽고, 식후에는 멀리 자본이 꽤 또렷이 보이는 듯하고, 자정 무렵에는 그 자본을 거의 손에 넣은 것 같다가, 새벽 두 시쯤이면 다시금 너무도 깊이 낙담해서, 소총을 한 자루 사서 미국으로 건너가, 버펄로 떼를 닦달해 한밑천 잡겠다는 막연한 계획을 이야기하는 것을 눈여겨보았다.\n\n나는 보통 일주일의 절반쯤은 해머스미스에 가 있었고, 해머스미스에 있을 때면 리치먼드를 들락거렸는데, 그 일은 따로 차차 이야기하겠다. 내가 그곳에 있을 때면 허버트도 자주 해머스미스로 오곤 했는데, 그럴 때면 그의 아버지가 이따금 그가 찾고 있는 기회가 아직 나타나지 않았다는 것을 스쳐 가듯 깨닫기도 했으리라 생각한다. 그러나 온 식구가 한데 뒤엉켜 굴러가는 와중에, 그가 인생의 어딘가로 굴러 나가는 일은 어떻게든 알아서 처리되어야 할 일이었다. 그러는 동안 포켓 씨는 머리가 더 희끗희끗해졌고, 자신의 곤혹스러움에서 벗어나려 제 머리카락을 잡아당겨 자신을 들어 올리려는 시도를 더 자주 했다. 한편 포켓 부인은 발받침으로 식구들을 걸려 넘어지게 하고, 작위에 관한 책을 읽고, 손수건을 잃어버리고, 우리에게 자기 할아버지 이야기를 늘어놓고, 어린 자식이 눈에 띌 때마다 침대 속으로 던져 넣음으로써 그 어린 새싹이 자라나는 법을 가르쳤다.\n\n이제 나는 앞길을 정리하기 위한 목적에서 내 인생의 한 시기를 통틀어 이야기하고 있으므로, 바너즈 인에서의 우리의 일상적인 생활 방식과 습관에 대한 묘사를 곧장 마무리 짓는 것보다 더 나은 방법은 없을 듯싶다.\n\n우리는 쓸 수 있는 만큼 돈을 썼고, 사람들이 마음먹고 내줄 수 있는 만큼 적은 대가만을 그 돈으로 얻었다. 우리는 늘 다소간 비참했고, 우리가 아는 사람들 대부분도 같은 처지였다. 우리 사이에는 우리가 끊임없이 즐기며 지낸다는 유쾌한 허구와, 결코 그러지 못한다는 앙상한 진실이 함께 있었다. 내가 믿는 바로는, 우리의 경우는 그 마지막 측면에서 꽤나 흔한 사례였다.\n\n매일 아침 허버트는 늘 새로운 기색으로, 기회를 살피러 시티로 나갔다. 나는 그가 잉크 항아리 하나, 모자걸이 하나, 석탄통 하나, 노끈통 하나, 달력 하나, 책상과 걸상 하나, 그리고 자 하나와 더불어 지내는 그 어두컴컴한 뒷방으로 자주 그를 찾아갔다. 그러나 그가 두리번거리며 기회를 살피는 것 말고 다른 무언가를 하는 모습은 본 기억이 없다. 만약 우리 모두가 하기로 한 일을 허버트만큼 충실히 해낸다면, 우리는 미덕의 공화국에서 살 수 있을 것이다. 가엾은 친구에게는, 매일 오후 일정한 시간에 \"로이즈에 가는\" 것 말고는 — 내 생각엔 자기 윗사람을 알현하는 의식을 치르려고 — 달리 할 일이 없었다. 내가 알아낼 수 있는 한, 그가 로이즈와 관련해 한 일이라고는 다시 돌아오는 것 말고는 아무것도 없었다. 자기 사정이 유난히 심각하다고, 그래서 무슨 일이 있어도 기회를 찾아내야 한다고 느낄 때면, 그는 거래소가 붐비는 시간에 그곳에 가서, 모여 있는 거물들 사이를 일종의 음울한 시골 춤사위처럼 이리저리 드나들곤 했다. \"이봐,\" 그런 특별한 경우의 어느 날 저녁 식사를 하러 집에 돌아오며 허버트가 내게 말했다. \"내가 깨달은 진실은 말이야, 핸들, 기회란 사람한테 찾아오는 게 아니라 사람이 그리로 찾아가야 한다는 거야 — 그래서 다녀왔지.\"\n\n만약 우리가 서로에게 덜 정들어 있었다면, 우리는 아마 매일 아침 어김없이 서로를 미워했을 것이다. 그 참회의 시기에 나는 그 방을 이루 말할 수 없이 혐오했고, 복수자의 제복을 보는 것도 견딜 수 없었다. 그 제복은 하루 스물네 시간 중 다른 어느 때보다 그 시각에 더 값비싸고도 덜 본전 뽑는 모양새를 띠었다. 우리가 점점 더 빚더미에 빠져들면서, 아침 식사는 점점 더 공허한 형식으로 변해 갔고, 한번은 아침 식사 시간에 (편지로) 법적 절차를 밟겠다는 위협을 받았는데 — 우리 동네 신문이라면 \"보석류와 전혀 무관하지 않은\"이라고 표현했을 — 나는 우리가 빵 한 조각을 원한다고 감히 넘겨짚었다는 이유로, 복수자의 푸른 옷깃을 움켜쥐고 발이 땅에서 떨어지도록 흔들어 대기까지 했다 — 그래서 그는 마치 장화 신은 큐피드처럼 정말로 공중에 떠 있었다.\n\n어떤 때면 — 정해지지 않은 때를 뜻하는데, 그것은 우리 기분에 달려 있었으니까 — 나는 마치 대단한 발견이라도 한 듯 허버트에게 이렇게 말하곤 했다.\n\n\"이보게 허버트, 우리 형편이 영 안 좋아지고 있군.\"\n\n\"이보게 핸들,\" 허버트는 더없이 진지하게 내게 대꾸하곤 했다. \"내 말을 믿어 준다면, 묘한 우연이지만 바로 그 말이 내 입술에 막 맴돌고 있었다네.\"\n\n\"그러면 허버트,\" 나는 응수하곤 했다. \"우리 형편을 한번 따져 보세.\"\n\n우리는 늘 이 목적을 위한 약속을 정하는 데서 깊은 만족을 얻었다. 나는 언제나 이것이야말로 일이라고, 이것이 문제를 정면으로 마주하는 방식이라고, 이것이 적의 멱살을 잡는 방식이라고 생각했다. 그리고 허버트도 그렇게 생각했다는 것을 나는 안다.\n\n우리는 이 행사를 위해 마음의 무장을 단단히 하고 제대로 임할 수 있도록, 저녁 식사로 다소 특별한 무언가를 시켰고, 그에 못지않게 평범하지 않은 무언가를 한 병 곁들였다. 식사가 끝나면, 우리는 펜 한 다발과 넉넉한 양의 잉크, 그리고 그득한 필기지와 압지를 꺼내 놓았다. 문방구가 잔뜩 있다는 데에는 무척 마음 든든한 구석이 있었기 때문이다.\n\n그런 다음 나는 종이 한 장을 집어 들고, 깔끔한 글씨로 맨 위에 가로질러 \"핍의 빚 명세서\"라는 제목을 적고, 바너즈 인과 날짜를 아주 정성스레 덧붙였다. 허버트도 종이 한 장을 집어 들어, 비슷한 격식을 갖추어 가로질러 \"허버트의 빚 명세서\"라고 적었다.\n\n그러고 나서 우리는 각자 곁에 놓인 어수선한 종이 더미를 들춰 보았는데, 그것들은 서랍 속에 처박혀 있거나, 주머니 속에서 닳아 구멍이 나거나, 촛불을 켜다가 반쯤 타거나, 몇 주씩 거울 틈에 끼워져 있거나, 그 밖의 방식으로 망가진 것들이었다. 우리의 펜이 사각거리는 소리는 우리를 어찌나 새롭게 북돋아 주던지, 나는 이따금 이 교훈적인 사무 처리와 실제로 돈을 갚는 것을 구별하기가 어렵다고 느낄 정도였다. 갸륵한 성격으로 따지자면, 그 두 가지는 거의 막상막하로 보였다.\n\n한참 동안 적고 나면, 나는 허버트에게 일이 잘 되어 가느냐고 묻곤 했다. 허버트는 아마 자기 숫자들이 불어나는 것을 보고는 더없이 침통한 태도로 머리를 긁적이고 있었을 것이다.\n\n\"점점 불어나는군, 핸들,\" 허버트는 말하곤 했다. \"정말이지, 점점 불어나고 있어.\"\n\n\"굳세게 마음먹게, 허버트,\" 나는 내 펜을 무척 부지런히 놀리며 받아치곤 했다. \"문제를 정면으로 바라보게. 자네 형편을 들여다보게. 노려봐서 기를 죽여 버리게.\"\n\n\"나도 그러고 싶네만, 핸들, 외려 그것들이 나를 노려봐서 기를 죽이고 있으니 탈일세.\"\n\n그러나 나의 단호한 태도는 효과를 발휘했고, 허버트는 다시 일에 매달리곤 했다. 얼마 후 그는 코브스의 청구서를, 아니면 로브스의, 또는 노브스의 청구서를, 경우에 따라 아직 못 받았다는 핑계로 다시 한 번 손을 놓곤 했다.\n\n\"그러면 허버트, 어림잡게. 어림수로 헤아려서 적어 두게.\"\n\n\"자네는 어찌 그리 수완이 좋은가!\" 내 친구는 감탄하며 대꾸하곤 했다. \"정말이지 자네의 사무 능력은 무척 놀랍다네.\"\n\n나도 그렇게 생각했다. 나는 이런 경우에 스스로에게 일류 사업가라는 평판을 — 신속하고, 결단력 있고, 정력적이고, 명료하며, 냉철한 사람이라는 평판을 — 세워 두었다. 내 모든 부채를 내 명세서에 적어 내려간 뒤, 나는 그 하나하나를 청구서와 견주어 보고 표시를 해 나갔다. 한 항목에 표시를 할 때마다 느끼는 자기만족은 그야말로 사치스러운 감각이었다. 더 이상 표시할 것이 없으면, 나는 청구서들을 모두 가지런히 접어 뒷면에 각각 표제를 달고, 전부를 좌우 균형 잡힌 다발로 묶었다. 그런 다음 나는 허버트의 것도 똑같이 해 주었고(그는 겸손하게 자기에겐 나 같은 관리의 재능이 없다고 말했다), 내가 그의 형편을 그를 위해 한데 초점에 모아 주었다고 느꼈다.\n\n내 사무 습관에는 또 하나의 빛나는 특징이 있었으니, 나는 그것을 \"여유분 남기기\"라고 불렀다. 예를 들어, 허버트의 빚이 백육십사 파운드 사 실링 이 펜스라고 치면, 나는 이렇게 말하곤 했다. \"여유를 남겨서, 이백 파운드로 적어 두게.\" 또는 내 빚이 그 네 배라고 치면, 나는 여유를 남겨 칠백 파운드로 적어 두었다. 나는 이 여유분이라는 것의 지혜를 더없이 높이 평가했으나, 돌이켜 보건대 그것이 값비싼 술책이었음을 인정하지 않을 수 없다. 우리는 늘 그 여유분의 한도까지 곧장 새 빚을 끌어들였고, 때로는 그것이 안겨 주는 자유와 지급 능력의 느낌에 취해, 또 다른 여유분 속으로 꽤 멀리 들어가기도 했기 때문이다.\n\n그러나 우리의 형편을 이렇게 점검하고 나면 뒤따르는 어떤 평온함, 어떤 안식, 어떤 미덕에 찬 고요함이 있어, 그것이 한동안 나로 하여금 나 자신에 대해 더없이 흐뭇한 견해를 품게 했다. 나의 노력과, 나의 방식과, 허버트의 칭찬에 마음이 누그러진 채, 나는 내 앞 탁자 위의 문방구들 사이에 그의 균형 잡힌 다발과 내 다발을 놓아두고 앉아, 한 개인이라기보다는 일종의 은행이라도 된 듯한 기분을 느끼곤 했다.\n\n우리는 이런 엄숙한 경우에는 방해받지 않도록 바깥문을 닫아걸었다. 어느 날 저녁 내가 그 고요한 상태에 빠져들었을 때, 바로 그 문의 틈으로 편지 한 통이 떨어져 바닥에 닿는 소리가 들렸다. \"자네한테 온 거야, 핸들,\" 허버트가 나가서 그것을 가지고 돌아오며 말했다. \"별일 아니어야 할 텐데.\" 이는 그 편지의 묵직한 검은 봉인과 검은 테두리를 두고 한 말이었다.\n\n그 편지에는 트래브 상회의 서명이 있었고, 그 내용인즉 간단히, 내가 존경하는 귀하시라는 것과, 조 가저리 부인이 지난 월요일 저녁 여섯 시 이십 분에 이 세상을 떠나셨음을 삼가 알려 드린다는 것과, 다음 월요일 오후 세 시의 장례식에 참석해 주시기를 바란다는 것이었다.\n\n# Chapter XXXV.\n\n내 인생의 길 위에 무덤이 입을 벌린 것은 그때가 처음이었고, 그것이 평탄하던 땅에 남긴 틈은 놀라운 것이었다. 부엌 난롯가 의자에 앉아 있던 누나의 모습이 밤낮으로 나를 따라다녔다. 그곳이 누나 없이도 존재할 수 있다는 사실은 내 정신으로는 도무지 헤아릴 수 없는 무엇이었다. 그리고 요즈음 누나가 내 생각 속에 떠오른 적이 거의, 아니 전혀 없었음에도, 이제는 누나가 거리에서 나를 향해 걸어오고 있다거나 곧 문을 두드릴 것 같다는 더없이 기이한 상상이 들었다. 누나와는 조금도 연관된 적 없는 내 방에서조차, 한순간 죽음의 공허함이 깃들면서 동시에 누나의 목소리가, 그 얼굴이나 몸의 움직임이 끊임없이 떠오르는 것이었다. 마치 누나가 아직 살아 있어 그곳에 자주 와 있었던 것처럼.\n\n내 처지가 어떠했든 간에, 나는 누나를 그리 다정한 마음으로 떠올리기는 어려웠을 것이다. 그러나 다정함은 별로 없으면서도 존재할 수 있는 회한의 충격이라는 것이 있는 모양이다. 그 영향 아래(어쩌면 그 부드러운 감정의 결핍을 메우기 위해), 나는 누나에게 그토록 큰 고통을 안긴 그 가해자를 향한 격렬한 분노에 사로잡혔다. 그리고 충분한 증거만 있다면 올릭이든 그 누구든 끝까지 복수의 일념으로 쫓을 수 있으리라고 느꼈다.\n\n조에게 편지를 써서 위로의 말을 전하고 장례식에 가겠다고 약속한 뒤, 나는 앞서 잠깐 언급한 그 묘한 심정으로 그사이의 며칠을 보냈다. 나는 아침 일찍 내려가, 대장간까지 걸어갈 수 있도록 넉넉한 시각에 블루 보어 여관에 닿았다.\n\n다시금 화창한 여름 날씨였고, 길을 따라 걷노라니 내가 가냘프고 무력한 어린것이었고 누나가 나를 가차 없이 대하던 시절이 생생하게 되살아났다. 그러나 그 시절은 부드러운 색조를 띠고 돌아와, 간질이 막대기의 날카로움마저 누그러뜨렸다. 이제 콩과 토끼풀의 바로 그 숨결이, 언젠가 햇살 속을 걷는 다른 이들이 나를 떠올리며 마음을 누그러뜨려 준다면 내 추억에 좋은 일이 될 그런 날이 반드시 오리라고 내 가슴에 속삭였기 때문이다.\n* 간질이 막대기: 누나가 핍을 매질하던 회초리에 붙인 별명.\n\n마침내 나는 집이 보이는 곳에 이르렀고, 트랩 상회가 장례 집행에 들어가 그 집을 점령했음을 알았다. 우스꽝스러울 만큼 침울한 두 사람이, 검은 붕대로 감싼 지팡이를 저마다 과시하듯 내보이며—마치 그 도구가 누군가에게 위안을 전해 줄 수 있기라도 한 양—현관 앞에 배치되어 있었는데, 그중 한 사람은 술에 취해 말 목을 두 팔로 끌어안고 타야 할 지경이 되는 바람에 어느 신혼부부를 결혼식 날 아침 톱질 구덩이에 처박은 죄로 보어 여관에서 쫓겨난 마부였음을 나는 알아보았다. 마을의 아이들 전부와 여자들 대다수가 이 검은 옷의 문지기들과 굳게 닫힌 집과 대장간의 창문들을 감탄하며 바라보고 있었다. 내가 다가가자 두 문지기 중 하나가(그 마부가) 문을 두드렸는데—그것은 내가 슬픔에 너무도 지친 나머지 스스로 두드릴 힘조차 남아 있지 않으리라는 뜻이었다.\n\n또 다른 검은 옷의 문지기가(한때 내기로 거위 두 마리를 먹어 치운 적이 있는 목수였다) 문을 열고 나를 가장 좋은 응접실로 안내했다. 그곳에서 트랩 씨는 가장 좋은 탁자를 차지하고 받침판을 죄다 펼쳐 올려 놓은 채, 검은 핀을 잔뜩 동원해 일종의 검은 시장판을 벌이고 있었다. 내가 도착한 순간, 그는 막 누군가의 모자를 아프리카 아기처럼 검은 긴 포대기로 감싸는 일을 끝낸 참이었기에, 내 모자를 받으려고 손을 내밀었다. 그러나 나는 그 동작에 잘못 이끌리고 또 상황에 당황한 나머지, 더없이 따뜻한 정을 다해 그와 악수를 나누고 말았다.\n\n가엾고 정다운 조는, 턱밑에 커다란 매듭으로 묶은 작은 검은 망토에 휘감긴 채 방의 윗자리에 따로 앉아 있었는데, 상주로서 트랩이 분명 그를 그 자리에 배치해 둔 모양이었다. 내가 몸을 굽혀 \"조 아저씨, 좀 어떠세요?\" 하고 묻자, 그는 \"핍, 이 친구야, 자네는 누나가 한창 좋은 모습이었을 적 그—\" 하고 말하더니, 내 손을 꼭 쥐고는 더는 말을 잇지 못했다.\n\n비디는 검은 옷차림으로 무척 단정하고 얌전해 보였는데, 조용히 이리저리 오가며 큰 도움을 주었다. 비디에게 말을 건넨 뒤, 나는 지금이 이야기를 나눌 때가 아니라고 여겨 조 곁으로 가 앉았고, 그러고는 그것이—그분이—내 누나가 이 집의 어디쯤 있을까 하고 생각하기 시작했다. 응접실의 공기에 단 과자 냄새가 희미하게 배어 있어, 나는 다과상을 찾아 둘러보았다. 그것은 어둠에 눈이 익기 전에는 거의 보이지 않았으나, 그 위에는 자른 자두 케이크가 있었고, 자른 오렌지와 샌드위치와 비스킷이, 그리고 평생 장식품으로만 잘 알고 있었을 뿐 한 번도 쓰이는 것을 본 적 없는 유리병 두 개가 놓여 있었다. 하나에는 포트와인이, 다른 하나에는 셰리주가 가득 차 있었다. 이 탁자 앞에 서 있노라니, 나는 검은 망토를 두르고 몇 야드나 되는 모자 띠를 늘어뜨린 비굴한 펌블추크가 번갈아 가며 음식을 입에 욱여넣다가 내 주의를 끌려고 굽실거리는 동작을 하고 있음을 알아챘다. 마침내 성공하자 그는 (셰리주와 빵 부스러기 냄새를 풍기며) 내게 다가와 나직한 목소리로 \"허락해 주시겠습니까, 도련님?\" 하고 말하더니 그렇게 했다. 그러고 나서 나는 허블 씨 부부를 발견했는데, 부인은 한쪽 구석에서 점잖게 말없이 발작하듯 흐느끼고 있었다. 우리 모두는 \"뒤따를\" 참이었고, 모두가 (트랩의 손에) 우스꽝스러운 꾸러미로 하나씩 따로따로 묶이고 있는 중이었다.\n\n\"그러니까 제 말은 말입니다, 핍,\" 응접실에서 트랩 씨가 \"정렬\"이라고 부른 대로 우리가 둘씩 둘씩 짝지어지던 중에—그것은 무슨 음산한 춤을 위한 준비와 끔찍이도 닮아 있었다—조가 내게 속삭였다. \"그러니까 제 말은 말입니다, 도련님, 저로서야 차라리 흔쾌한 마음과 팔로 와 줄 서너 명의 다정한 친구들과 함께 제가 직접 누나를 교회로 모시고 싶었습니다만, 이웃들이 그런 걸 깔보면서 예의에 어긋난다고 여길 거라는 의견들이 있었던 거지요.\"\n\n\"손수건을 꺼내십시오, 모두!\" 이때 트랩 씨가 침울하고 사무적인 목소리로 외쳤다. \"손수건을 꺼내십시오! 준비됐습니다!\"\n\n그리하여 우리는 모두 코피라도 나는 양 손수건을 얼굴에 갖다 대고 둘씩 둘씩 줄지어 나갔다. 조와 나, 비디와 펌블추크, 허블 씨 부부 순이었다. 가엾은 누나의 유해는 부엌문으로 돌아 운구되어 나왔는데, 흰 가장자리를 두른 무시무시한 검은 벨벳 천 아래 여섯 명의 운구꾼이 숨이 막히고 앞이 가려진 채 가야 하는 것이 장의 의식의 한 관례였던 까닭에, 그 전체 모습은 마치 열두 개의 사람 다리가 달린 눈먼 괴물이 두 명의 관리인—마부와 그 동료—의 인도 아래 발을 질질 끌며 비척비척 나아가는 듯이 보였다.\n\n그러나 이웃들은 이러한 배치를 크게 흡족해했고, 우리가 마을을 지나는 동안 우리는 무척 감탄의 대상이 되었다. 마을 사람들 가운데 젊고 혈기 왕성한 축들은 이따금 앞질러 우리 길을 가로막으려 내달았고, 좋은 길목에 매복해 있다가 우리를 맞으려 했다. 그럴 때면 그중 더 들뜬 자들은, 우리가 예상되는 모퉁이를 돌아 나타나기라도 하면 흥분한 투로 \"온다, 온다!\" \"여기 왔다!\" 하고 외쳐 댔고, 우리는 환호를 받기 직전이었다. 이렇게 나아가는 동안 나는 그 비굴한 펌블추크 때문에 무척 짜증이 났는데, 그는 내 뒤에 서서 가는 내내 세심한 배려라며 흩날리는 내 모자 띠를 매만지고 내 망토를 쓸어 주기를 고집했다. 게다가 내 생각은 허블 씨 부부의 지나친 자긍심 때문에 더욱 산만해졌으니, 그들은 이토록 격조 있는 행렬의 일원이 된 것에 대해 더할 나위 없이 우쭐하고 으스대고 있었다.\n\n이제 늪지대가 우리 앞에 훤히 펼쳐졌고, 강 위 배들의 돛이 그 위로 솟아오르고 있었다. 우리는 교회 묘지로 들어가, 내가 알지 못하는 부모님—이 교구의 고인이 된 필립 피립과 또한 위 사람의 아내 조지애나—의 무덤 가까이로 갔다. 그리고 그곳에서, 종다리들이 그 위 높은 곳에서 노래하고 가벼운 바람이 구름과 나무의 아름다운 그림자를 흩뿌리는 가운데, 누나는 조용히 땅에 묻혔다.\n\n이 일이 진행되는 동안 그 속물적인 펌블추크의 행동에 관해서는, 그것이 죄다 나를 향한 것이었다는 점, 그리고 인간이 이 세상에 아무것도 가지고 오지 않았으니 아무것도 가지고 갈 수 없으며 그림자처럼 사라져 한곳에 오래 머무르지 못한다는 것을 일깨우는 그 고귀한 구절들이 낭독될 때조차, 그가 뜻밖에 큰 재산을 물려받은 한 젊은 신사의 경우는 예외라는 단서를 기침으로 덧붙이는 소리를 내가 들었다는 점 외에는 더 말하고 싶지 않다. 우리가 돌아왔을 때, 그는 뻔뻔하게도 누나가 내가 자신에게 이토록 큰 영광을 안겨 주었음을 알았더라면 좋았을 것이라고 내게 말했고, 누나가 자기 죽음의 대가로 그 영광을 치렀더라도 그것을 합당한 거래로 여겼으리라는 뜻까지 넌지시 내비쳤다. 그런 다음 그는 남은 셰리주를 모조리 마셨고, 허블 씨는 포트와인을 마셨으며, 두 사람은 (이후 나는 그런 경우에 으레 그러함을 관찰했다) 마치 자기들이 고인과는 전혀 다른 종족이며 누구나 다 아는 불사신이기라도 한 양 떠들어 댔다. 마침내 그는 허블 씨 부부와 함께 떠나갔는데—저녁 시간을 그렇게 즐기려는 것임을, 그리고 졸리 바지선꾼 술집에 가서 자신이 내 행운의 창시자이자 가장 이른 은인이라고 떠벌리려는 것임을 나는 확신했다.\n\n그들이 모두 떠나고, 트랩과 그 일꾼들이—그러나 그 점원 녀석은 아니었으니, 나는 그를 찾아보았다—제 장식 도구들을 자루에 쑤셔 넣고 그들마저 떠나가자, 집 안은 한결 정갈한 느낌이 들었다. 곧이어 비디와 조와 나는 함께 차가운 만찬을 들었다. 그러나 우리는 옛 부엌이 아니라 가장 좋은 응접실에서 식사를 했고, 조는 나이프와 포크와 소금 그릇이며 이런저런 것을 다루는 데 어찌나 지나치게 신경을 쓰던지, 우리 사이에는 큰 어색함이 감돌았다. 그러나 식사가 끝난 뒤, 내가 그에게 파이프를 물게 하고, 그와 함께 대장간 주위를 어슬렁거리고, 그 바깥의 커다란 돌덩이 위에 함께 앉고 나니, 우리는 한결 나아졌다. 나는 조가 장례식이 끝난 뒤 옷을 갈아입어, 일요일 나들이옷과 작업복 사이의 절충을 이루어 놓은 것을 알아챘는데, 그 차림새 속에서 정다운 친구는 자연스러워 보였고, 본래 그가 어떤 사람인지 그대로 드러나 보였다.\n\n내가 내 작은 방에서 자도 되겠느냐고 묻자 그는 무척 기뻐했고, 나 또한 기뻤다. 그런 청을 한 것이 다소 대단한 일을 한 셈이라고 느꼈기 때문이다. 저녁 그림자가 짙게 드리워질 무렵, 나는 잠깐 이야기를 나누려고 비디와 함께 정원으로 나갈 기회를 잡았다.\n\n\"비디,\" 내가 말했다. \"이 슬픈 일들에 대해 자네가 내게 편지를 써 줄 수도 있었을 텐데.\"\n\n\"그렇게 생각하세요, 핍 도련님?\" 비디가 말했다. \"그렇게 여겼더라면 편지를 썼을 거예요.\"\n\n\"내가 자네에게 야박하게 굴려는 거라고는 여기지 말게, 비디. 나는 자네가 그렇게 여겼어야 했다고 생각한다는 말일세.\"\n\n\"그렇게 생각하세요, 핍 도련님?\"\n\n비디는 어찌나 차분하고, 또 어찌나 단정하고 선량하고 어여쁜 태도를 지녔던지, 나는 그녀를 다시 울리고 싶지 않았다. 내 곁에서 걷는 동안 내리뜬 그녀의 눈을 잠시 바라본 뒤, 나는 그 문제를 접었다.\n\n\"이제 자네가 이곳에 남아 있기는 어렵겠지, 비디?\"\n\n\"아! 그럴 수는 없어요, 핍 도련님,\" 비디가 아쉬워하면서도 여전히 조용한 확신이 담긴 어조로 말했다. \"허블 부인과 이야기를 나누었는데, 내일 그분에게로 갈 거예요. 가저리 씨가 자리를 잡으실 때까지 우리가 함께 그분을 좀 보살펴 드릴 수 있으면 좋겠어요.\"\n\n\"자네는 어떻게 살아갈 작정인가, 비디? 만일 돈이 좀 더 필요하다—\"\n\n\"제가 어떻게 살아갈 작정이냐고요?\" 비디가 얼굴에 잠깐 홍조를 띠며 끼어들어 되풀이했다. \"말씀드릴게요, 핍 도련님. 저는 여기 거의 다 지어진 새 학교의 여교사 자리를 얻어 보려고 해요. 이웃들 모두에게서 좋은 추천을 받을 수 있고, 부지런하고 참을성 있게, 남을 가르치면서 저 자신도 가르칠 수 있기를 바라고 있어요. 아시잖아요, 핍 도련님,\" 비디가 내 얼굴로 눈을 들며 미소를 띠고 말을 이었다. \"새 학교는 옛날 학교와는 다르지만, 저는 그때 이후로 도련님께 많은 걸 배웠고, 그 뒤로 실력을 갈고닦을 시간도 있었으니까요.\"\n\n\"비디, 자네는 어떤 상황에서든 늘 실력을 갈고닦을 사람이라고 생각하네.\"\n\n\"아! 제 인간 본성의 못된 쪽만 빼고요,\" 비디가 중얼거렸다.\n\n그것은 책망이라기보다는 억누를 수 없이 새어 나온 혼잣말에 가까웠다. 그래, 나는 그 문제 또한 접는 게 좋겠다고 생각했다. 그래서 나는 비디와 조금 더 걸으며, 내리뜬 그녀의 눈을 말없이 바라보았다.\n\n\"비디, 나는 누나가 어떻게 죽었는지 자세한 이야기를 듣지 못했네.\"\n\n\"별것 없어요, 가엾은 분. 나흘 동안 발작 상태에 빠져 계셨는데—요즘 들어선 그게 더 나빠지기보다는 오히려 좀 나아지긴 했지만요—저녁에, 마침 차 마실 시간에 그 상태에서 깨어나시더니 또렷하게 '조' 하고 말씀하셨어요. 오랫동안 한마디도 안 하셨던 터라, 저는 달려가서 대장간에 계신 가저리 씨를 모셔 왔지요. 그분은 가저리 씨가 자기 곁에 가까이 앉기를 바라고, 제가 자기 두 팔을 그분 목에 둘러 주기를 바란다는 몸짓을 하셨어요. 그래서 제가 그 팔을 그분 목에 둘러 드렸더니, 그분은 아주 흡족하고 만족스러운 듯 머리를 그분 어깨에 기대셨어요. 그러고는 곧 다시 '조' 하고 말씀하셨고, 한 번은 '용서를', 또 한 번은 '핍' 하고 말씀하셨어요. 그러고는 다시는 고개를 들지 않으셨고, 한 시간쯤 지나서 우리는 그분이 가셨음을 알았기에 그분의 머리를 그분 침대에 누여 드렸어요.\"\n\n비디는 울었다. 어두워지는 정원과 좁은 길, 돋아나기 시작한 별들이 내 눈에도 흐릿하게 번졌다.\n\n\"끝내 아무것도 밝혀지지 않았나, 비디?\"\n\n\"아무것도요.\"\n\n\"올릭이 어떻게 됐는지 아는가?\"\n\n\"옷 색깔로 보아 채석장에서 일하고 있는 것 같아요.\"\n\n\"그럼 물론 그를 본 적이 있다는 거로군?—왜 저 길의 어두운 나무를 보고 있나?\"\n\n\"누나가 돌아가시던 날 밤에 거기서 그를 봤어요.\"\n\n\"그게 마지막도 아니었던 거지, 비디?\"\n\n\"네. 우리가 여기서 걷는 동안에도 거기서 그를 봤어요.—소용없어요,\" 내가 막 달려 나가려 하자 비디가 내 팔에 손을 얹으며 말했다. \"제가 도련님을 속이지 않을 거라는 건 아시잖아요. 그는 거기 채 일 분도 없었고, 이미 가 버렸어요.\"\n\n그녀가 여전히 이 사내에게 쫓기고 있음을 알자 내 분노는 극에 달했고, 나는 그에게 사무치는 적의를 느꼈다. 나는 그녀에게 그렇게 말했고, 그를 이 고장에서 쫓아내기 위해서라면 얼마든 돈을 쓰고 어떤 수고든 아끼지 않겠다고 말했다. 그녀는 차츰 나를 좀 더 차분한 이야기로 이끌었고, 조가 나를 얼마나 사랑하는지, 그리고 조가 어떤 일에 대해서도—나에 대해서라고는 말하지 않았으나, 그럴 필요도 없었으니, 나는 그녀의 뜻을 알았다—결코 불평하지 않고, 굳센 손과 조용한 혀와 다정한 마음으로 자기 삶의 방식 안에서 늘 제 본분을 다해 왔는지를 들려주었다.\n\n\"정말이지, 그분에 대해서는 아무리 좋게 말해도 지나치지 않을 거야,\" 내가 말했다. \"그리고 비디, 우리는 이런 이야기들을 자주 나눠야 하네. 물론 나는 이제 이곳에 자주 내려올 테니까. 가엾은 조 아저씨를 외롭게 내버려 두지 않을 거야.\"\n\n비디는 단 한마디도 하지 않았다.\n\n\"비디, 내 말 안 들리나?\"\n\n\"네, 핍 도련님.\"\n\n\"나를 핍 도련님이라고 부르는 건 차치하고라도—그것도 내겐 영 못마땅한 취향으로 보입니다만, 비디—자네 무슨 뜻으로 그러는 건가?\"\n\n\"무슨 뜻이냐고요?\" 비디가 머뭇거리며 물었다.\n\n\"비디,\" 내가 점잖게 제 옳음을 내세우는 투로 말했다. \"자네가 이러는 게 무슨 뜻인지 좀 알아야겠네.\"\n\n\"이러는 거요?\" 비디가 말했다.\n\n\"이봐, 메아리처럼 따라 하지 말게,\" 내가 쏘아붙였다. \"예전엔 메아리처럼 따라 하지 않았잖나, 비디.\"\n\n\"하지 않았다고요!\" 비디가 말했다. \"아, 핍 도련님! 예전엔이라니요!\"\n그래! 나는 그 문제 역시 접는 게 좋겠다고 생각했다. 정원을 또 한 바퀴 말없이 돈 뒤, 나는 본래의 입장으로 돌아갔다.\n\n\"비디,\" 내가 말했다. \"내가 조 아저씨를 뵈러 이곳에 자주 내려올 거라는 말을 했는데, 자네는 그 말을 뚜렷한 침묵으로 받아들였네. 비디, 부디 그 까닭을 말해 주게.\"\n\n\"그럼 도련님은 정말로 그분을 자주 뵈러 오시리라고 확신하세요?\" 비디가 좁은 정원 길에서 걸음을 멈추고, 별빛 아래 맑고 정직한 눈으로 나를 바라보며 물었다.\n\n\"아, 이런!\" 내가, 절망 속에서 비디를 단념할 수밖에 없다는 듯이 말했다. \"이거야말로 인간 본성의 참으로 못된 쪽이로군! 제발, 비디, 더는 아무 말도 하지 말게. 이건 나를 몹시 충격에 빠뜨리네.\"\n\n이 그럴듯한 이유로 나는 저녁 식사 내내 비디를 멀찍이 두었고, 내 옛 작은 방으로 올라갈 때에는 교회 묘지와 그날의 일에 비추어 내 웅얼거리는 영혼이 어울린다고 여길 만한 한도 내에서 더없이 위엄 있게 그녀에게 작별을 고했다. 밤중에 잠 못 이룰 때마다, 그것도 십오 분마다였는데, 나는 비디가 내게 얼마나 야박하게, 얼마나 큰 해를, 얼마나 큰 부당함을 안겼는지 곱씹었다.\n\n나는 아침 일찍 떠나기로 되어 있었다. 아침 일찍 나는 밖으로 나가, 들키지 않은 채 대장간의 나무 창 하나로 안을 들여다보았다. 거기서 나는 몇 분 동안 서서 조를 바라보았다. 그는 벌써 일을 하고 있었는데, 그의 얼굴에는 건강과 힘의 광채가 어려, 마치 그를 위해 마련된 인생의 밝은 태양이 그 얼굴을 비추고 있는 듯이 보였다.\n\n\"안녕히 계세요, 정다운 조 아저씨!—아니, 닦지 마세요—제발, 그 검게 그을린 손을 제게 내밀어 주세요!—곧, 또 자주 내려올게요.\"\n\n\"아무리 일러도 좋고,\" 조가 말했다. \"아무리 자주여도 좋네, 핍!\"\n\n비디는 새로 짠 우유 한 잔과 빵 한 조각을 들고 부엌문에서 나를 기다리고 있었다. \"비디,\" 작별하며 그녀에게 손을 내밀 때 내가 말했다. \"나는 화가 난 게 아니라, 마음이 상한 거라네.\"\n\n\"아니에요, 마음 상해하지 마세요,\" 그녀가 자못 애처롭게 호소했다. \"제가 너그럽지 못했던 거라면, 마음 상해야 할 사람은 저 하나뿐이게 해 주세요.\"\n\n다시 한번, 내가 걸어 나오는 동안 안개가 피어오르고 있었다. 만일 그 안개가, 내 짐작대로, 내가 돌아오지 않으리라는 것을, 그리고 비디가 전적으로 옳았다는 것을 내게 드러내 보인 것이라면, 내가 할 수 있는 말은 이것뿐이다—그 안개 또한 전적으로 옳았다고.\n\n# Chapter XXXVI.\n\n허버트와 나는 빚을 늘리고, 형편을 들여다보고, 여백을 남겨 두는 따위의 모범적인 거래를 거듭하며 점점 더 나빠져 갔다. 그리고 세월은, 늘 그러하듯, 좋든 싫든 흘러갔다. 그리하여 나는 성년이 되었다. 어느새 정신을 차리기도 전에 성년이 될 거라던 허버트의 예언이 그대로 이루어진 셈이었다.\n\n허버트 자신은 나보다 여덟 달 앞서 성년이 되었다. 하지만 그에게는 성년이 된다는 것 말고 달리 물려받을 것이 없었으므로, 그 사건은 바너드 여관에 깊은 파문을 일으키지 못했다. 그러나 우리는 내 스물한 번째 생일을 온갖 추측과 기대를 안고 손꼽아 기다렸다. 그날만은 후견인이 무언가 분명한 말을 하지 않을 수 없으리라고 우리 둘 다 여겼기 때문이다.\n\n나는 내 생일이 언제인지 리틀브리튼에 잘 알려지도록 미리 손을 써 두었다. 생일 전날, 나는 웨믹에게서 공식적인 쪽지를 받았는데, 그 길한 날 오후 다섯 시에 재거스 씨를 방문해 준다면 그분이 기뻐하시리라는 내용이었다. 이로써 우리는 무언가 대단한 일이 일어나리라 확신했고, 시간을 칼같이 지켜 후견인의 사무실로 향할 때 나는 평소와 달리 가슴이 두근거렸다.\n\n바깥 사무실에서 웨믹은 내게 축하 인사를 건넸고, 그러는 김에 보기 좋게 접은 박엽지로 코 옆을 문질렀는데, 나는 그 모양새가 마음에 들었다. 그러나 그는 그것에 대해 아무 말도 하지 않고, 고갯짓으로 나를 후견인의 방으로 들여보냈다. 십일월이었고, 후견인은 두 손을 코트 자락 밑에 넣은 채 등을 벽난로 선반에 기대고 불 앞에 서 있었다.\n\n\"이런, 핍.\" 그가 말했다. \"오늘은 자네를 핍 씨라고 불러야겠군. 축하하네, 핍 씨.\"\n\n우리는 악수를 나누었고 — 그는 언제나 유난히 짧게 손을 잡는 사람이었다 — 나는 그에게 고맙다고 했다.\n\n\"의자에 앉게, 핍 씨.\" 후견인이 말했다.\n\n내가 앉는 동안에도 그는 그 자세를 그대로 유지한 채 눈썹을 찌푸리며 자기 장화를 내려다보았는데, 나는 불리한 처지에 놓인 듯한 기분이 들었고, 그것은 묘비 위에 올려졌던 그 옛 시절을 떠올리게 했다. 선반 위의 그 무시무시한 석고 두상 두 개가 그에게서 멀지 않은 곳에 있었는데, 그 표정은 마치 대화에 귀를 기울이려고 멍청하게 뇌졸중이라도 일으킬 듯 애쓰는 것 같았다.\n\n\"자, 젊은 친구.\" 후견인은 마치 내가 증인석에 선 증인이라도 되는 양 입을 열었다. \"자네와 한두 마디 나눠 볼 참이네.\"\n\n\"그러시지요, 선생님.\"\n\n\"자네는 짐작하나.\" 재거스 씨가 몸을 앞으로 숙여 바닥을 보더니, 이내 고개를 뒤로 젖혀 천장을 쳐다보며 말했다. \"자네가 어느 정도 규모로 살고 있다고 짐작하나?\"\n\n\"규모라니요, 선생님?\"\n\n\"어느,\" 재거스 씨가 여전히 천장을 보며 되풀이했다. \"정도—규모—로 말이야?\" 그러고는 방 안을 빙 둘러보더니, 손수건을 든 손을 코까지 절반쯤 가져가다 멈추었다.\n\n나는 내 형편을 하도 자주 들여다본 나머지, 그 형편의 내막에 대해 어렴풋이나마 가졌을 법한 관념마저 깡그리 무너뜨려 버린 터였다. 나는 마지못해 그 질문에 도저히 답할 수 없노라고 실토했다. 이 대답이 재거스 씨에게는 흡족했던 모양인지, 그는 \"그럴 줄 알았네!\" 하고는 만족스러운 기색으로 코를 풀었다.\n\n\"자, 내가 자네한테 질문을 했네, 친구.\" 재거스 씨가 말했다. \"자네는 나한테 물어볼 게 있나?\"\n\n\"물론 여러 가지를 여쭤볼 수 있다면 제게는 큰 위안이 되겠지만, 선생님, 저는 선생님의 금지령을 기억하고 있습니다.\"\n\n\"하나만 물어보게.\" 재거스 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"제 은인이 오늘 제게 밝혀지는 겁니까?\"\n\n\"아니. 다른 걸 물어보게.\"\n\n\"그 비밀이 곧 제게 전해지는 겁니까?\"\n\n\"그건 잠깐 접어 두게.\" 재거스 씨가 말했다. \"그리고 다른 걸 물어보게.\"\n\n나는 주위를 둘러보았지만, 이제 이 물음을 피할 길은 도저히 없을 듯했다. \"제가—받을 것이 있습니까, 선생님?\" 그러자 재거스 씨는 의기양양하게 말했다. \"그리로 올 줄 알았지!\" 그러고는 웨믹을 불러 그 종이를 달라고 했다. 웨믹이 나타나 그것을 건네고는 사라졌다.\n\n\"자, 핍 씨.\" 재거스 씨가 말했다. \"잘 들어 두게. 자네는 이곳에서 꽤 마음껏 돈을 인출해 왔지. 웨믹의 출납부에 자네 이름이 꽤 자주 등장하네. 한데 자네는 물론 빚을 지고 있겠지?\"\n\n\"그렇다고 말씀드려야 할 듯합니다, 선생님.\"\n\n\"그렇다고 말할 수밖에 없다는 걸 알지, 안 그런가?\" 재거스 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"네, 선생님.\"\n\n\"자네가 얼마를 빚졌는지는 묻지 않겠네, 자네도 모를 테니까. 설령 안다 해도 내게 말하지 않을 테고, 실제보다 적게 말하겠지. 그래, 그래, 친구.\" 내가 항의하는 시늉을 하자 재거스 씨는 집게손가락을 흔들어 나를 막으며 외쳤다. \"자네는 그러지 않으리라 생각하겠지만, 자네는 그럴 걸세. 미안하지만, 내가 자네보다 더 잘 알아. 자, 이 종이를 손에 쥐어 보게. 쥐었나? 좋아. 이제 펴서 그게 뭔지 내게 말해 보게.\"\n\n\"이건 은행권입니다.\" 내가 말했다. \"오백 파운드짜리요.\"\n\n\"그건 오백 파운드짜리 은행권일세.\" 재거스 씨가 되받았다. \"그리고 내 생각엔 꽤 두둑한 금액이기도 하지. 자네도 그렇게 여기나?\"\n\n\"어찌 달리 생각하겠습니까!\"\n\n\"아! 하지만 질문에 답하게.\" 재거스 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"의심할 바 없이 그렇습니다.\"\n\"자네는 의심할 바 없이 그것을 두둑한 금액으로 여긴다는 거지. 자, 그 두둑한 금액은, 핍, 자네 것일세. 자네의 유산을 보증하는 뜻으로, 오늘 자네에게 주는 선물이지. 그리고 자네는 그 두둑한 금액을 연간 한도로 삼아, 그 이상은 넘지 않는 선에서, 그 모든 것을 베푸는 분이 나타날 때까지 살아가야 하네. 다시 말해, 이제 자네는 자네의 금전 문제를 전적으로 자네 손에 맡게 되는 것이고, 자네가 근원지와 직접 연락이 닿을 때까지는, 그러니까 한낱 대리인이 아닌 그분과 닿을 때까지는, 웨믹에게서 분기마다 백이십오 파운드를 인출하게 되네. 전에도 말했듯, 나는 한낱 대리인일 뿐이야. 나는 내 지시를 이행하고, 그 대가로 보수를 받지. 나는 그 지시가 분별없다고 여기지만, 그 옳고 그름에 대해 의견을 내는 일로 보수를 받는 건 아니라네.\"\n\n나는 내가 받은 그 큰 관대함에 대해 은인에게 감사의 뜻을 표하려던 참이었는데, 재거스 씨가 나를 막았다. \"나는 자네 말을 누군가에게 전하라고 보수를 받는 게 아닐세, 핍.\" 그가 차갑게 말했다. 그러고는, 화제를 거두어들이듯 코트 자락을 그러모으고 서서, 마치 자기 장화가 자기에게 무슨 음모라도 꾸미는 게 아닌가 의심하듯 눈썹을 찌푸린 채 그것을 노려보았다.\n\n잠시 뜸을 들인 뒤, 나는 넌지시 말을 꺼냈다.\n\n\"방금 전에, 재거스 씨, 잠깐 접어 두라고 하신 질문이 하나 있었지요. 그걸 다시 여쭤도 제가 잘못하는 게 아니길 바랍니다만?\"\n\n\"그게 뭔가?\" 그가 말했다.\n\n그가 결코 나를 거들어 주지 않으리라는 것쯤은 미리 알았어야 했다. 하지만 그 질문을 마치 전혀 새것인 양 다시 빚어내야 한다는 사실에 나는 당황했다. \"혹시,\" 나는 머뭇거리다 말했다. \"제 후원자, 그러니까 선생님께서 말씀하신 그 근원지가, 재거스 씨, 곧—\" 거기서 나는 조심스레 말을 멈추었다.\n\n\"곧 뭐가 어떻다는 건가?\" 재거스 씨가 물었다. \"그대로는 질문이 안 되네, 알겠나.\"\n\n\"곧 런던에 오실는지요.\" 나는 정확한 표현을 골똘히 더듬다가 말했다. \"아니면 저를 어딘가 다른 곳으로 부르실는지요?\"\n\n\"자, 여기서,\" 재거스 씨가 그 어둡고 깊숙이 박힌 눈으로 처음으로 나를 똑바로 응시하며 대답했다. \"우리는 자네 마을에서 처음 서로 마주쳤던 그 저녁으로 되돌아가야 하네. 그때 내가 자네한테 뭐라고 했지, 핍?\"\n\n\"그 사람이 나타나는 건 지금부터 몇 년 뒤가 될 수도 있다고 하셨습니다, 재거스 씨.\"\n\n\"바로 그걸세.\" 재거스 씨가 말했다. \"그게 내 대답이야.\"\n\n우리가 서로를 정면으로 바라볼 때, 그에게서 무언가를 끌어내려는 강렬한 열망에 내 숨이 가빠지는 것을 느꼈다. 그리고 내 숨이 가빠지는 것을 느끼고, 또 그가 내 숨이 가빠지는 것을 알아챘음을 느끼면서, 나는 그에게서 무언가를 끌어낼 가망이 그 어느 때보다도 적다는 것을 느꼈다.\n\n\"그게 여전히 몇 년 뒤일 거라고 보십니까, 재거스 씨?\"\n\n재거스 씨는 고개를 가로저었다. 그 질문을 부정하는 것이 아니라, 어떤 식으로든 자기가 그 질문에 답하도록 끌려갈 수 있다는 생각 자체를 통째로 부정하는 몸짓이었다. 그리고 내 눈길이 위로 흘러갔을 때, 그 일그러진 얼굴의 무시무시한 두상 두 개는, 마치 멈춰 있던 주의력이 어떤 절정에 다다라 막 재채기라도 하려는 것처럼 보였다.\n\n\"이보게!\" 재거스 씨가 따뜻하게 데운 손등으로 자기 종아리 뒤를 덥히며 말했다. \"내 솔직하게 말함세, 친구 핍. 그건 내게 물어서는 안 되는 질문이야. 그게 나를 곤란하게 만들 수 있는 질문이라고 말하면, 자네도 더 잘 이해하겠지. 이보게! 자네한테 한 걸음 더 나아가 줌세. 무언가 좀 더 말해 주지.\"\n\n그는 자기 장화를 향해 어찌나 낮게 몸을 숙여 눈썹을 찌푸렸던지, 그렇게 멈춰 있는 동안 자기 종아리를 문지를 수 있을 정도였다.\n\n\"그 사람이 정체를 드러내면,\" 재거스 씨가 몸을 똑바로 펴며 말했다. \"자네와 그 사람이 직접 자네들의 일을 처리하게 될 걸세. 그 사람이 정체를 드러내면, 이 일에서 내가 맡은 몫은 끝나고 마무리되네. 그 사람이 정체를 드러내면, 내가 그것에 대해 무언가를 알 필요는 없어지지. 그게 내가 할 말의 전부일세.\"\n\n우리는 서로를 바라보다가, 내가 눈길을 거두어 생각에 잠긴 채 바닥을 내려다보았다. 이 마지막 말에서 나는 이런 생각을 끌어냈다. 즉, 해비셤 양이, 무슨 까닭에서든 혹은 아무 까닭 없이든, 나를 에스텔라의 짝으로 점찍은 자신의 계획에 대해서는 그를 속내까지 끌어들이지 않았다는 것, 그래서 그가 이를 못마땅해하며 일종의 질투를 느끼고 있거나, 아니면 그가 정말로 그 계획에 반대하여 거기에 일절 관여하지 않으려 한다는 것이었다. 내가 다시 눈을 들었을 때, 나는 그가 그동안 줄곧 나를 빈틈없이 살펴보고 있었으며, 지금도 그러고 있다는 것을 알아차렸다.\n\n\"그게 하실 말씀의 전부라면, 선생님,\" 내가 말했다. \"저로서도 더 할 말이 남아 있을 리 없겠지요.\"\n\n그는 그렇다는 듯 고개를 끄덕이고는, 도둑들이 두려워하는 그 시계를 꺼내더니, 어디서 저녁을 먹을 거냐고 물었다. 나는 내 거처에서 허버트와 함께 먹을 거라고 답했다. 자연히 뒤따르는 순서로, 나는 그에게 함께해 주시겠느냐고 청했고, 그는 그 초대를 선뜻 받아들였다. 다만 그는 나더러 자기 때문에 따로 준비를 하지 못하도록 나와 함께 걸어가겠다고 고집했고, 우선은 편지를 한두 통 써야 하며, (당연하게도) 손을 씻어야 한다고 했다. 그래서 나는 바깥 사무실로 가서 웨믹과 이야기를 나누겠다고 했다.\n\n사실인즉, 그 오백 파운드가 내 주머니에 들어왔을 때, 전부터 종종 떠올랐던 한 가지 생각이 머릿속에 다시 떠올랐던 것이다. 그리고 그런 생각에 관해서라면 웨믹이야말로 의논하기에 좋은 사람으로 보였다.\n\n그는 이미 금고를 잠그고 집에 갈 채비를 마친 뒤였다. 그는 책상을 떠나, 기름때 묻은 사무실 촛대 두 개를 꺼내어 곧 끌 수 있도록 문 가까운 석판 위에 심지가위와 나란히 세워 두었고, 불을 낮게 긁어내려 두었으며, 모자와 외투를 준비해 두고는, 업무를 마친 뒤의 운동이라도 하듯 금고 열쇠로 제 가슴팍 여기저기를 두드리고 있었다.\n\n\"웨믹 씨.\" 내가 말했다. \"당신 의견을 좀 여쭙고 싶습니다. 저는 친구 하나를 몹시 돕고 싶습니다.\"\n\n웨믹은 자기 우체통을 단단히 다물고 고개를 가로저었는데, 마치 그런 종류의 치명적인 약점이라면 무엇이든 자기 의견이 단호히 반대한다는 듯한 모습이었다.\n\n\"이 친구는,\" 나는 말을 이었다. \"상업계에서 자리를 잡으려 하는데, 돈이 없어서, 시작을 떼기가 어렵고 또 의욕마저 꺾이는 처지입니다. 그래서 저는 어떻게든 그가 첫발을 떼도록 돕고 싶습니다.\"\n\n\"현금을 대서 말이오?\" 웨믹이 톱밥보다도 더 메마른 어조로 말했다.\n\n\"얼마간의 현금을 대서요.\" 나는 대답했다. 집에 있는 그 가지런히 묶인 종이 다발이 불안하게 머릿속을 스쳐 지나갔기 때문이다. \"얼마간의 현금을 대고, 어쩌면 제 유산 일부를 미리 끌어다가요.\"\n\n\"핍 씨.\" 웨믹이 말했다. \"괜찮으시다면 첼시 리치까지 거슬러 올라가며 여러 다리의 이름을 손가락으로 함께 꼽아 보고 싶군요. 어디 봅시다. 런던교가 하나, 서더크교가 둘, 블랙프라이어스교가 셋, 워털루교가 넷, 웨스트민스터교가 다섯, 복스홀교가 여섯이오.\" 그는 금고 열쇠 손잡이로 손바닥을 짚어 가며 다리를 차례차례 헤아렸다. \"보다시피 골라잡을 다리가 여섯이나 되지요.\"\n\n\"무슨 말씀이신지 모르겠습니다.\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"다리를 하나 고르시오, 핍 씨.\" 웨믹이 되받았다. \"그러고는 그 다리 위로 산책을 나가서, 그 다리 한가운데 아치 너머로 돈을 템스강에 던져 버리시오. 그 끝이 어찌 될지는 당신도 알 거요. 그 돈으로 친구를 돕더라도, 그 끝이 어찌 될지는 마찬가지로 알 수 있을 테지요. 다만 그쪽이 덜 즐겁고 덜 이로운 끝일 뿐이오.\"\n\n이 말을 하고 나서 그가 입을 어찌나 크게 벌렸던지, 그 안에 신문이라도 한 장 부쳐 넣을 수 있을 것 같았다.\n\n\"이거 참 맥 빠지는 말씀이군요.\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"그러라고 한 말이오.\" 웨믹이 말했다.\n\n\"그렇다면 당신 의견으로는,\" 나는 다소 분개하며 물었다. \"사람이 결코—\"\n\n\"—친구한테 운반 가능한 재산을 투자해서는 안 된다는 거냐고요?\" 웨믹이 말했다. \"당연히 안 되지요. 그 친구를 떼어 내고 싶은 게 아니라면 말이오. 그렇다면 이제 그 친구를 떼어 내는 데 운반 가능한 재산이 얼마쯤 들 만한 가치가 있느냐 하는 문제가 되지요.\"\n\n\"그러면 그게,\" 내가 말했다. \"당신의 신중한 의견입니까, 웨믹 씨?\"\n\n\"그게,\" 그가 되받았다. \"이 사무실에서의 제 신중한 의견이오.\"\n\n\"아!\" 나는 여기서 그에게 빠져나갈 틈이 보이는 듯하여 다그쳐 물었다. \"하지만 그게 월워스에서도 당신 의견일까요?\"\n\n\"핍 씨.\" 그가 진지하게 대답했다. \"월워스는 한 곳이고, 이 사무실은 또 다른 곳이오. 마치 노친께서 한 사람이고, 재거스 씨가 또 다른 사람이듯이 말이오. 그 둘을 한데 뒤섞어서는 안 되지요. 제 월워스의 정서는 월워스에서 받아들여야 하고, 이 사무실에서는 제 공적인 정서밖에 받아들일 수 없소.\"\n\n\"아주 좋습니다.\" 나는 크게 안도하며 말했다. \"그러면 월워스로 당신을 찾아가겠습니다, 틀림없이요.\"\n\n\"핍 씨.\" 그가 되받았다. \"사적이고 개인적인 자격이라면, 그곳에서 당신을 환영하겠소.\"\n\n우리는 이 대화를 낮은 목소리로 나누었는데, 후견인의 귀가 둘도 없이 날카롭다는 것을 잘 알고 있었기 때문이다. 이제 그가 손을 닦으며 문간에 나타나자, 웨믹은 외투를 걸치고 촛불을 끄려고 곁에 섰다. 우리 셋은 함께 거리로 나섰고, 문간 계단에서 웨믹은 자기 길로, 재거스 씨와 나는 우리 길로 갈라졌다.\n\n그날 저녁 나는 재거스 씨에게도 제라드 가에 노친 같은 이가, 혹은 스팅어든, 혹은 어떤 무엇이든, 혹은 어떤 누구든, 그의 찌푸린 눈썹을 조금이나마 펴 줄 존재가 있었으면 하는 바람을 한 번 이상 품지 않을 수 없었다. 스물한 번째 생일에, 그가 빚어 놓은 것 같은 그토록 경계심 많고 의심 많은 세상에서는 성년이 된다는 것 자체가 도무지 그럴 만한 가치가 없어 보인다는 것은, 마음을 불편하게 하는 생각이었다. 그는 웨믹보다 천 배는 더 박식하고 영리했지만, 그럼에도 나는 천 배는 더 웨믹을 저녁 식사에 모시고 싶었다. 그리고 재거스 씨가 우울하게 만든 사람은 나 하나만이 아니었으니, 그가 떠난 뒤 허버트는 불을 응시한 채 자기 자신에 대해 이렇게 말했다. 자기가 무슨 중죄라도 저지르고는 그 자세한 내막을 잊어버린 게 틀림없는 것 같다고, 그만큼 풀이 죽고 죄스러운 기분이 든다고.\n\n# Chapter XXXVII.\n\n일요일이 웨믹 씨의 월워스식 정서를 살피기에 가장 좋은 날이라 여긴 나는, 다음에 돌아온 일요일 오후를 성으로의 순례에 바쳤다. 성벽 앞에 다다라 보니 유니언 잭이 펄럭이고 도개교가 올려져 있었다. 그러나 이런 항거와 저항의 과시에 굴하지 않고 나는 문에서 종을 울렸고, 노인장의 더없이 평화로운 응대를 받으며 안으로 들어섰다.\n\n\"제 아들이 말입니다.\" 노인이 도개교를 단단히 고정한 뒤 말했다. \"혹시 손님께서 들르실지도 모른다고 어느 정도 생각하고 있었지요. 오후 산책에서 곧 돌아오겠노라 일러두었습니다. 제 아들은 산책이 아주 규칙적이거든요. 매사에 아주 규칙적이지요, 제 아들은.\"\n\n나는 웨믹 본인이 끄덕였을 법한 모양새로 노신사에게 고개를 끄덕였고, 우리는 안으로 들어가 난롯가에 앉았다.\n\n\"제 아들과 알게 되신 게 말이지요, 손님.\" 노인이 불길에 두 손을 쬐며 그 지저귀는 듯한 말투로 말했다. \"그 애 사무소에서였겠지요?\" 나는 끄덕였다. \"허! 제 아들이 제 일에 솜씨가 대단하다고 들었는데요, 손님?\" 나는 힘차게 끄덕였다. \"예, 다들 그렇게들 말합디다. 그 애 일이 법률 쪽이지요?\" 나는 더 힘차게 끄덕였다. \"그러니 제 아들로선 더 놀라운 일이지요.\" 노인이 말했다. \"그 애는 법률을 배운 게 아니라 포도주 통 만드는 일을 배웠으니까요.\"\n\n노신사가 재거스 씨의 평판에 관해 어느 정도 알고 있는지 궁금해진 나는, 그 이름을 그에게 큰 소리로 외쳤다. 그러자 노인은 진심으로 웃으며 아주 쾌활한 태도로, \"아니지요, 그렇고말고요. 손님 말씀이 옳습니다.\"라고 대답하여 나를 더없는 혼란에 빠뜨렸다. 그리고 나는 지금 이 순간까지도 그가 무슨 뜻으로 한 말인지, 또 내가 무슨 농담을 했다고 그가 여긴 것인지 전혀 짐작조차 못 하고 있다.\n\n그렇다고 그에게 흥미를 끌 다른 시도도 없이 마냥 거기 앉아 끄덕이고만 있을 수는 없었으므로, 나는 그분 자신의 평생 직업이 \"포도주 통 만드는 일\"이었는지를 큰 소리로 물었다. 그 말을 몇 번이나 애써 짜내고, 그것을 그분과 연결 짓고자 노신사의 가슴을 톡톡 두드린 끝에, 나는 마침내 내 뜻을 알아듣게 하는 데 성공했다.\n\n\"아니지요.\" 노신사가 말했다. \"창고업, 창고업이었습니다. 처음엔 저 너머에서\"—그는 굴뚝 위를 가리키는 듯했으나, 내 짐작에는 리버풀을 두고 한 말인 듯했다—\"그러고는 여기 런던 시내에서요. 한데, 몸에 탈이 나서 말이지요—제가 귀가 어둡거든요, 손님—\"\n\n나는 더없는 경악을 몸짓으로 나타냈다.\n\n\"—예, 귀가 어둡지요. 그런 탈이 제게 닥쳐오니, 제 아들이 법률 쪽으로 들어가 저를 떠맡았고, 조금씩 조금씩 이 우아하고 아름다운 재산을 일구어 냈답니다. 한데 손님께서 하신 말씀으로 돌아가자면 말이지요.\" 노인이 다시 진심으로 웃으며 말을 이었다. \"제가 드리는 말씀은, 아니지요, 그렇고말고요, 손님 말씀이 옳다는 겁니다.\"\n\n나는 내 온갖 재간을 다 짜낸들 이 가상의 농담의 절반만큼이라도 그를 즐겁게 할 만한 말을 할 수 있었을까 겸손히 자문하던 차에, 굴뚝 한쪽 벽에서 느닷없이 딸깍 소리가 나고 \"존\"이라고 쓰인 작은 나무 덮개가 유령처럼 펄렁 열리는 바람에 흠칫 놀랐다. 노인은 내 눈길을 좇더니 의기양양하게 외쳤다. \"제 아들이 돌아왔어요!\" 그러고는 우리 둘 다 도개교로 나갔다.\n\n손쉽게 해자 너머로 악수를 나눌 만한 거리인데도, 웨믹이 저편에서 내게 인사를 보내며 손을 흔드는 광경은 그 어떤 돈을 주고도 볼 만한 것이었다. 노인장이 도개교를 작동시키는 일을 어찌나 즐거워하던지, 나는 거들겠다고 나서지 않고 가만히 서 있었고, 마침내 웨믹이 건너와 자신과 동행한 부인인 스키핀스 양에게 나를 소개했다.\n\n스키핀스 양은 목석 같은 외양이었고, 그녀의 동반자와 마찬가지로 우편 업무 분야에 종사하고 있었다. 웨믹보다 두세 살쯤 어려 보였고, 동산을 좀 소유하고 있는 듯싶었다. 앞뒤로 허리 위쪽 옷의 재단 모양 때문에 그녀의 몸매는 영락없이 사내아이가 날리는 연 같았다. 그리고 나는 그녀의 드레스가 좀 지나치게 단호히 주황색이고, 장갑이 좀 지나치게 짙은 초록색이라고 단언했을 법하다. 그래도 그녀는 사람 됨됨이가 좋은 축인 듯했고, 노인장을 매우 존중했다. 그녀가 성을 자주 찾는 손님이라는 것을 알아채는 데는 오래 걸리지 않았다. 우리가 안으로 들어가서, 노인장에게 자신의 귀가를 알리는 그 기발한 장치를 내가 웨믹에게 칭찬하자, 그는 잠시 굴뚝 반대편에 주목해 달라고 청하고는 사라졌기 때문이다. 곧 또 한 번 딸깍 소리가 나며, \"스키핀스 양\"이라고 쓰인 또 다른 작은 문이 펄렁 열렸다. 이어 스키핀스 양이 닫히고 존이 펄렁 열렸으며, 그러고는 스키핀스 양과 존이 함께 펄렁 열렸다가 마침내 함께 닫혔다. 이 기계 장치들을 작동시키고 돌아온 웨믹에게 나는 그것들에 대해 품은 크나큰 감탄을 표했고, 그는 이렇게 말했다. \"음, 아시다시피 이건 노인장께 즐겁기도 하고 쓸모도 있는 것들이지요. 그리고 정말이지, 손님, 이건 말할 만한 일인데, 이 문으로 찾아오는 모든 사람들 중에 그 줄들의 비밀을 아는 건 노인장과 스키핀스 양과 저뿐이랍니다!\"\n\n\"그리고 웨믹 씨가 만드셨어요.\" 스키핀스 양이 덧붙였다. \"당신 머리에서 나온 생각으로, 당신 손수요.\"\n\n스키핀스 양이 보닛을 벗는 동안(그녀는 손님이 와 있다는 외적이고 가시적인 표시로 저녁 내내 초록 장갑은 끼고 있었다), 웨믹은 함께 재산을 한 바퀴 둘러보며 겨울철에 이 섬이 어떤 모습인지 보자고 나를 청했다. 그가 내게 월워스식 정서를 살필 기회를 주려고 그러는 것이라 여긴 나는, 성을 벗어나자마자 그 기회를 붙잡았다.\n\n이 문제를 신중히 생각해 둔 터라, 나는 전에 한 번도 그것을 비친 적이 없는 듯이 본론에 다가갔다. 나는 웨믹에게 허버트 포켓을 위해 마음이 쓰인다고 알리고, 우리가 처음 어떻게 만났는지, 또 어떻게 싸웠는지를 이야기했다. 나는 허버트의 가정과 그의 인품, 그리고 그가 아버지에게 의존하는 것 말고는 다른 수단이 없다는 것—그나마도 불확실하고 들쭉날쭉한 것임을—을 짚었다. 나는 미숙하고 무지하던 시절 그와의 교제에서 얻은 이로움을 언급했고, 그것을 제대로 갚지 못한 것이 두렵다는 것, 그리고 그가 나와 내 유산이 없었더라면 더 잘 지냈을지도 모른다는 것을 고백했다. 해비셤 양은 멀찍이 뒤편에 두면서도, 나는 내가 그의 앞날에서 그와 경쟁했을 가능성과, 그가 너그러운 영혼을 지녀 그 어떤 치사한 불신이나 보복이나 술책 따위와는 거리가 먼 사람임이 분명하다는 것을 넌지시 비쳤다. 이 모든 이유들 때문에(나는 웨믹에게 말했다), 그리고 그가 나의 젊은 동무이자 친구이며 내가 그에게 큰 애정을 품고 있기에, 나는 나 자신의 행운이 그에게도 몇 줄기 빛을 비춰 주기를 바란다고, 그래서 내 재력으로 어떻게 하면 가장 잘 허버트를 도와 당장의 얼마간의 수입을—이를테면 그가 좋은 희망과 기운을 유지할 수 있도록 일 년에 백 파운드쯤을—마련해 주고, 차츰 작은 동업의 몫을 사 줄 수 있을지, 사람과 세상사에 관한 웨믹의 경험과 식견에서 조언을 구한다고 말했다. 끝으로 나는 웨믹에게, 내 도움은 언제나 허버트가 알거나 눈치채지 못하게 베풀어져야 한다는 것, 그리고 이 세상에 의논할 사람이 그 말고는 아무도 없다는 것을 헤아려 달라고 청했다. 나는 그의 어깨에 손을 얹으며 이렇게 매듭지었다. \"당신에게 이게 성가신 일임을 알면서도 당신을 믿고 털어놓지 않을 수 없군요. 하지만 그건 애초에 나를 이곳으로 데려온 당신 탓입니다.\"\n\n웨믹은 잠시 말이 없더니, 흠칫하는 듯한 태도로 입을 열었다. \"음, 그러니까 핍 씨, 한 가지는 말씀드려야겠군요. 이건 지독히도 훌륭한 일입니다.\"\n\n\"그럼 내가 훌륭한 사람이 되도록 도와주겠다고 말해 줘요.\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"이런.\" 웨믹이 고개를 저으며 대답했다. \"그건 제 장사가 아니지요.\"\n\n\"여기도 당신의 장사터가 아니잖아요.\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"맞습니다.\" 그가 응수했다. \"정곡을 찌르셨군요. 핍 씨, 제가 곰곰이 궁리하는 모자를 한번 써 보겠습니다. 손님께서 하시려는 일은 차츰차츰 다 이루어질 수 있을 거라 봅니다. 스키핀스라는 사람이(그게 그녀의 오라비입니다) 회계사이자 대리인이지요. 그를 찾아가 손님을 위해 일을 추진해 보겠습니다.\"\n\n\"수만 번 감사드립니다.\"\n\n\"천만에요.\" 그가 말했다. \"오히려 제가 손님께 감사드립니다. 우리가 엄밀히 사적이고 개인적인 처지로 하는 일이긴 하나, 그래도 말씀드릴 만한 건, 주위에 뉴게이트의 거미줄이 끼어 있는데, 이런 일이 그것을 쓸어 없애 준다는 거지요.\"\n\n같은 취지로 조금 더 이야기를 나눈 뒤, 우리는 성으로 돌아왔고, 거기서 스키핀스 양이 차를 준비하는 것을 보았다. 토스트를 굽는 막중한 임무는 노인장에게 맡겨졌는데, 그 훌륭한 노신사가 어찌나 거기에 골몰하던지 내 눈에는 그가 눈알을 녹여 버릴 위험에 처한 것처럼 보였다. 우리가 하려는 것은 이름뿐인 식사가 아니라 기운찬 실체였다. 노인장은 버터 바른 토스트를 어찌나 건초 더미처럼 쌓아 올렸던지, 그것이 맨 위 가로대에 걸린 무쇠 받침대 위에서 지글거리는 동안 나는 그 너머로 그의 모습을 보기조차 어려웠다. 한편 스키핀스 양은 차를 어찌나 큰 동이로 끓였던지, 뒤편 마당에 있던 돼지가 몹시 흥분하여 이 잔치에 끼고 싶은 욕구를 거듭 드러냈다.\n\n깃발은 내려졌고 대포는 알맞은 시각에 발사되었으니, 나는 해자가 너비 삼십 피트에 깊이도 그만큼 되기라도 한 듯 월워스의 나머지 세상으로부터 아늑하게 차단된 기분이 들었다. 성의 평온을 어지럽히는 것은, 이따금 펄렁 열리는 존과 스키핀스 양뿐이었다. 그 작은 문들은 어떤 경련성 질환에 시달리는 듯하여, 익숙해지기 전까지는 나도 덩달아 마음이 편치 않았다. 스키핀스 양의 준비가 짜임새 있는 것으로 미루어, 나는 그녀가 일요일 밤마다 거기서 차를 끓이는 것이라 짐작했다. 그리고 그녀가 단 고전풍의 브로치는—매우 곧은 코와 아주 가느다란 초승달을 가진, 그다지 탐탁잖은 여인의 옆얼굴을 새긴 것이었는데—웨믹이 그녀에게 준 동산의 일부가 아닐까 하는 의심이 들었다.\n\n우리는 토스트를 죄다 먹어 치우고 그에 걸맞게 차를 마셨는데, 그러고 나서 우리 모두가 어찌나 따뜻해지고 기름기가 도는지 보는 것이 즐거웠다. 특히 노인장은 막 기름칠을 한, 어느 깔끔한 야만족 늙은 추장으로 통할 만했다. 잠시 쉬고 난 뒤, 일요일 오후면 가족의 품으로 돌아가는 모양인 어린 하녀가 없는 터라, 스키핀스 양이 우리 가운데 누구의 체면도 손상하지 않는 사소하고 숙녀다운 아마추어 솜씨로 찻그릇을 설거지했다. 그러고 나서 그녀는 다시 장갑을 끼었고, 우리는 난롯가에 둘러앉았으며, 웨믹이 말했다. \"자, 노친네, 신문 좀 펼쳐 주시지요.\"\n\n노인장이 안경을 꺼내는 동안 웨믹이 내게 설명하기를, 이것이 관례에 따른 것이며 노신사는 뉴스를 소리 내어 읽는 일에서 한없는 만족을 얻는다고 했다. \"양해를 구하지는 않겠습니다.\" 웨믹이 말했다. \"이분은 누리실 수 있는 즐거움이 많지 않으시거든요—그렇지요, 노친네?\"\n\n\"좋다, 존, 좋아.\" 자기에게 하는 말임을 알아챈 노인이 대답했다.\n\n\"그저 이따금 이분이 신문에서 눈을 떼실 때 끄덕여만 주시면.\" 웨믹이 말했다. \"임금님처럼 행복해하실 겁니다. 자, 다들 경청하고 있습니다, 노친네.\"\n\n\"좋다, 존, 좋아!\" 명랑한 노인이 어찌나 분주하고 어찌나 흐뭇해하던지, 정말이지 더없이 사랑스러웠다.\n\n노인장의 낭독은 워플 씨 종조할머니네 교실을 떠올리게 했는데, 다만 그것이 열쇠 구멍을 통해 새어 나오는 듯하다는 더 유쾌한 특색이 있었다. 그는 촛불을 바싹 가까이 두고 싶어 했고, 늘 제 머리든 신문이든 그 속에 처박을 지경이었으므로, 화약 공장만큼이나 지켜봐야 했다. 그러나 웨믹은 그 경계에 있어 한결같이 지칠 줄 모르고 부드러웠으며, 노인장은 자신이 여러 번 구출된 것을 전혀 알아채지 못한 채 계속 읽어 나갔다. 그가 우리를 쳐다볼 때마다 우리는 모두 더없는 흥미와 경탄을 표하며, 그가 다시 읽기 시작할 때까지 고개를 끄덕였다.\n\n웨믹과 스키핀스 양이 나란히 앉아 있고 나는 어둑한 구석에 앉아 있었던 까닭에, 나는 웨믹 씨의 입이 더디고 차차 길어지는 것을 관찰했는데, 그것은 그가 천천히 또 차츰 스키핀스 양의 허리로 팔을 둘러 가고 있음을 강력히 시사했다. 이윽고 나는 그의 손이 스키핀스 양의 반대편에 나타나는 것을 보았다. 그러나 바로 그 순간 스키핀스 양은 초록 장갑으로 그를 깔끔하게 저지하고는, 그 팔을 무슨 옷가지라도 되는 양 다시 풀어내어, 더없이 신중하게 자기 앞 탁자 위에 올려놓았다. 이 일을 하는 동안 스키핀스 양의 침착함은 내가 여태껏 본 가장 놀라운 광경 가운데 하나였으며, 만일 그 행동이 정신이 딴 데 팔린 상태와 양립할 수 있다고 여길 수 있었다면, 나는 스키핀스 양이 그것을 기계적으로 해낸 것이라 판단했을 것이다.\n\n얼마 지나지 않아, 나는 웨믹의 팔이 다시 사라지기 시작해 차츰 시야에서 흐릿해져 가는 것을 알아챘다. 곧이어 그의 입이 다시 벌어지기 시작했다. 나로서는 자못 사로잡히고 거의 고통스럽기까지 한 한동안의 긴장 끝에, 나는 그의 손이 스키핀스 양의 반대편에 나타나는 것을 보았다. 즉시 스키핀스 양은 차분한 권투 선수의 솜씨로 그것을 저지하고, 앞서처럼 그 허리띠 혹은 케스투스를 벗겨 내어 탁자 위에 올려놓았다. 탁자를 미덕의 길에 비긴다면, 노인장이 낭독하던 내내 웨믹의 팔이 미덕의 길에서 벗어나 헤매다가 스키핀스 양에 의해 그리로 다시 불려 돌아왔다고 진술해도 무방하리라.\n\n* 케스투스: 고대 그리스·로마에서 권투 선수가 손에 감던 가죽끈.\n마침내 노인장은 신문을 읽다가 가벼운 선잠에 빠져들었다. 이제 웨믹이 작은 주전자 하나와 유리잔 쟁반 하나, 그리고 불그레하고 사교적인 인상의 어느 성직 고관을 본뜬, 도자기 마개를 씌운 검은 병 하나를 꺼낼 때였다. 이 도구들의 도움으로 우리는 모두 따뜻한 마실 것을 한잔씩 들었는데, 어느새 다시 잠을 깬 노인장도 함께였다. 스키핀스 양이 술을 탔고, 나는 그녀와 웨믹이 한 잔으로 나누어 마시는 것을 관찰했다. 물론 나는 스키핀스 양을 집까지 바래다주겠다고 나설 만큼 눈치 없지는 않았고, 사정이 그러하니 내가 먼저 자리를 뜨는 것이 상책이라 생각했다. 그래서 나는 노인장에게 정답게 작별을 고하고, 즐거운 저녁을 보낸 뒤 먼저 떠났다.\n\n일주일이 채 지나기 전에, 나는 웨믹에게서 월워스라는 발신지가 적힌 쪽지 한 통을 받았다. 우리의 사적이고 개인적인 처지에 속하는 그 일에 어느 정도 진전을 보았기를 바라며, 그 일로 내가 다시 한번 찾아와 줄 수 있다면 기쁘겠다는 내용이었다. 그리하여 나는 다시 월워스로 나갔고, 또 한 번, 또 한 번 나갔으며, 약속을 잡아 시내에서도 그를 여러 번 만났으나, 리틀 브리튼 안에서나 그 근처에서는 그 일에 관해 그와 아무런 교신도 하지 않았다. 그 결과, 우리는 사업을 시작한 지 오래되지 않아 영민한 조력과 자본을 필요로 하고, 때가 무르익으면 동업자도 원하게 될 어느 훌륭한 젊은 상인 겸 선박 중개인을 찾아냈다. 그와 나 사이에 허버트를 대상으로 한 비밀 계약서가 서명되었고, 나는 내 오백 파운드 중 절반을 즉석에서 치렀으며, 그 밖의 여러 지급도 약정했다. 그중 일부는 내 수입에서 정해진 날짜에 치러질 것이었고, 일부는 내가 유산을 물려받게 되는 데 달린 것이었다. 스키핀스 양의 오라비가 그 협상을 이끌었다. 웨믹은 그 일 전반에 두루 스며 있었으나, 결코 표면에 나서지 않았다.\n\n이 모든 일이 어찌나 교묘하게 처리되었던지, 허버트는 거기에 내 손길이 닿아 있으리라고는 조금도 의심하지 못했다. 나는 어느 날 오후 그가 환히 빛나는 얼굴로 집에 돌아와, 대단한 소식이랍시고, 클래리커(그 젊은 상인의 이름이다)라는 사람과 우연히 만난 일이며, 클래리커가 그에게 유별난 호의를 보인 일이며, 드디어 기회가 찾아왔다는 자기 믿음을 내게 들려주던 그 모습을 결코 잊지 못할 것이다. 날이 갈수록 그의 희망이 더 굳어지고 그의 얼굴이 더 밝아짐에 따라, 그는 나를 점점 더 다정한 친구라 여겼음이 분명한데, 그가 그토록 행복해하는 것을 보면서 나는 승리의 눈물을 억누르기가 여간 힘들지 않았기 때문이다. 마침내 일이 다 이루어지고, 그날 그가 클래리커 상회에 들어가게 되어, 기쁨과 성공에 들떠 저녁 내내 내게 이야기를 늘어놓은 뒤, 나는 잠자리에 들면서, 내 유산이 누군가에게 얼마간의 보탬이 되었다는 생각에 정말이지 진심으로 울었다.\n\n내 인생의 크나큰 사건, 내 인생의 전환점이 이제 내 눈앞에 펼쳐진다. 그러나 그것을 이야기하기에 앞서, 또 그것이 수반한 온갖 변화로 넘어가기에 앞서, 나는 한 장을 에스텔라에게 바쳐야겠다. 그토록 오래 내 마음을 채웠던 그 주제에 바치기에 한 장은 결코 많은 것이 아니다.\n\n# Chapter XXXVIII.\n\n리치먼드의 그린 광장 가까이에 있는 그 차분한 옛집이 내가 죽은 뒤 언젠가 유령이 나오는 집이 된다면, 그 집에 출몰하는 것은 분명 내 유령일 것이다. 아, 에스텔라가 그곳에 살던 시절, 내 안의 안식 없는 영혼이 그 집을 떠돌던 수많고도 수많은 밤과 낮이여! 내 몸이 어디에 있든, 내 영혼은 언제나 그 집 언저리를 헤매고 또 헤매고 또 헤맸다.\n\n에스텔라가 맡겨진 집의 부인은 브랜들리 부인이라는 이름의 과부였는데, 에스텔라보다 몇 살 위인 딸 하나를 두고 있었다. 어머니는 젊어 보였고 딸은 늙어 보였으며, 어머니의 안색은 발그레했고 딸의 안색은 누르께했으며, 어머니는 경박함을 자처했고 딸은 신학을 자처했다. 그들은 이른바 좋은 위치에 있었고, 많은 사람들을 방문했으며 많은 사람들의 방문을 받았다. 그들과 에스텔라 사이에는 감정의 공감이라 할 만한 것이 거의, 아니 전혀 없었으나, 그들이 에스텔라에게 필요하고 에스텔라가 그들에게 필요하다는 양해는 성립되어 있었다. 브랜들리 부인은 미스 해비셤이 칩거에 들기 전부터 그분의 친구였다.\n\n브랜들리 부인의 집 안에서나 밖에서나, 나는 에스텔라가 내게 안길 수 있는 온갖 종류와 정도의 고문을 겪었다. 그녀와 나의 관계는 친밀한 처지에 나를 두면서도 총애받는 처지에 두지는 않는 성질의 것이었으니, 그것이 나를 더없이 산란하게 만들었다. 그녀는 나를 이용해 다른 숭배자들을 약 올렸고, 그녀와 나 사이의 그 친밀함 자체를 이용해, 그녀를 향한 내 헌신을 끊임없이 멸시하는 수단으로 삼았다. 내가 그녀의 비서나 집사나 이복 오라비나 가난한 친척이었더라도 — 그녀가 정혼한 남편의 동생이었더라도 — 그녀에게 가장 가까이 있을 때조차 나는 내 희망에서 그보다 더 멀어진 듯 느껴질 수는 없었을 것이다. 그녀를 이름으로 부르고 그녀가 나를 이름으로 부르는 것을 듣는 그 특권은, 이런 사정 아래서는 내 시련을 가중시키는 것이 되었다. 그리고 그것이 그녀의 다른 연인들을 거의 미치게 했으리라는 짐작이 들지만, 그것이 나를 거의 미치게 했다는 것만큼은 너무도 확실히 안다.\n\n그녀에게는 숭배자가 끝도 없이 많았다. 내 질투가 그녀에게 다가가는 모든 이를 숭배자로 만들어 버렸음은 의심할 바 없으나, 그런 것을 빼고도 그들은 차고 넘칠 만큼 많았다.\n\n나는 리치먼드에서 그녀를 자주 보았고, 시내에서 그녀에 관한 소문을 자주 들었으며, 그녀와 브랜들리 모녀를 데리고 자주 뱃놀이를 나가곤 했다. 소풍이며 축제일이며 연극이며 오페라며 음악회며 파티며, 온갖 종류의 즐거운 자리가 있었고, 그 자리들을 통해 나는 그녀를 좇았다 — 그러나 그 모든 것이 내게는 괴로움이었다. 나는 그녀와 함께한 자리에서 단 한 시간의 행복도 누려 본 적이 없었으되, 내 마음은 하루 스물네 시간 내내 죽을 때까지 그녀를 곁에 둔다는 행복을 되뇌고 있었다.\n\n우리가 교제하던 이 시기 내내 — 곧 보게 되겠지만 그것은 그 당시 내가 길다고 여긴 만큼 이어졌다 — 그녀는 우리의 교제가 우리에게 강요된 것임을 드러내는 그 말투로 으레 되돌아가곤 했다. 또 어떤 때는 이 말투와 그녀의 그 숱한 말투들에서 별안간 멈추어 서서, 나를 가엾이 여기는 듯 보이기도 했다.\n\n\"핍, 핍.\" 어느 날 저녁, 리치먼드 집의 어둑해 가는 창가에 우리가 떨어져 앉아 있을 때, 그녀가 그렇게 멈추어 서며 말했다. \"당신은 끝내 경고를 새기지 않을 건가요?\"\n\"무엇에 대한 경고요?\"\n\n\"나에 대한 경고요.\"\n\n\"당신에게 끌리지 말라는 경고를 말하는 건가요, 에스텔라?\"\n\n\"말하는 거냐고요! 내 말뜻을 모른다면, 당신은 눈먼 사람이에요.\"\n\n사랑은 흔히 눈멀었다고들 하지 않느냐고 받아쳤어야 마땅했으나, 나는 늘 한 가지 감정에 매여 있었으니 — 이것이 내 괴로움 가운데 결코 작은 것이 아니었다 — 그것은, 그녀가 미스 해비셤에게 복종하는 수밖에 달리 선택할 길이 없음을 스스로 아는 마당에 그녀에게 나를 들이대는 것은 점잖지 못한 일이라는 느낌이었다. 그녀의 이 자각이, 그녀의 자존심 앞에서 나를 심히 불리한 처지에 놓이게 하고, 그녀의 가슴속에서 일어나는 반항적 갈등의 대상으로 나를 만들지나 않을까 — 그것이 늘 내 두려움이었다.\n\n\"어쨌거나,\" 내가 말했다. \"지금 막은 내게 아무 경고도 주어지지 않았어요. 이번에는 당신이 내게 와 달라고 편지를 썼으니까요.\"\n\n\"그건 사실이에요.\" 에스텔라가 늘 나를 오싹하게 하던 그 차갑고 무심한 미소를 띠며 말했다.\n\n잠시 바깥의 황혼을 바라보더니, 그녀는 말을 이었다.\n\n\"미스 해비셤이 나를 하루 새티스로 데려가고 싶어 하시는 때가 다시 돌아왔어요. 당신이 괜찮다면, 나를 그곳에 데려갔다가 다시 데려와 주세요. 그분은 내가 혼자 여행하는 걸 원치 않으시고, 내 하녀를 들이는 것도 마다하세요. 그런 부류의 사람들 입에 오르내리는 데 예민하리만치 진저리를 치시니까요. 나를 데려가 줄 수 있나요?\"\n\n\"내가 당신을 데려갈 수 있느냐고요, 에스텔라!\"\n\n\"그럼 데려가 줄 수 있는 거죠? 괜찮다면 모레요. 비용은 모두 내 지갑에서 치르도록 하세요. 당신이 동행하는 조건을 들었지요?\"\n\n\"따를 수밖에요.\" 내가 말했다.\n\n그것이 그 방문에 대해, 혹은 그와 비슷한 다른 방문들에 대해 내가 받은 준비의 전부였다. 미스 해비셤은 내게 한 번도 편지를 쓴 적이 없었고, 나는 그분의 필체조차 본 적이 없었다. 우리는 그다음다음 날 내려갔고, 내가 그분을 처음 보았던 바로 그 방에서 그분을 만났는데, 새티스 하우스에 아무런 변화도 없었다는 것은 굳이 덧붙일 필요가 없으리라.\n\n그분은 내가 두 사람을 함께 본 마지막 때보다도 한층 더 무섭도록 에스텔라를 애지중지했다. 나는 일부러 그 말을 다시 쓴다. 그분의 눈길과 포옹의 열기에는 실로 무서운 무언가가 깃들어 있었으니까. 그분은 에스텔라의 아름다움에 매달리고, 그녀의 말에 매달리고, 그녀의 몸짓에 매달렸으며, 그녀를 바라보면서 자신의 떨리는 손가락을 우물우물 씹고 앉아 있었는데, 마치 자신이 길러 낸 그 아름다운 피조물을 집어삼키기라도 하려는 듯했다.\n\n에스텔라에게서 눈을 돌려 그분은 나를 바라보았는데, 내 가슴속을 캐고 들어 그 상처를 헤집는 듯한 살피는 눈길이었다. \"그 애가 너를 어떻게 대하더냐, 핍, 어떻게 대하더냐?\" 에스텔라가 듣고 있는 자리에서까지 그분은 마녀 같은 열의로 내게 거듭 물었다. 그러나 밤에 우리가 그분의 가물거리는 난롯가에 앉아 있을 때, 그분은 더없이 기괴했다. 그때 그분은 에스텔라의 손을 자신의 팔에 꿰어 자기 손으로 움켜쥔 채, 에스텔라가 정기적으로 보낸 편지에 적어 보낸 내용을 거듭 들먹여 가며, 그녀가 호려 놓은 사내들의 이름과 처지를 그녀에게서 끌어냈다. 그리고 미스 해비셤이 치명적으로 상하고 병든 마음의 강렬함으로 그 명단을 곱씹는 동안, 그분은 다른 손을 목발 지팡이에 얹고 그 위에 턱을 괸 채, 핼쑥하게 번득이는 눈으로 나를 노려보고 앉아 있었으니, 영락없는 망령이었다.\n\n나는 이 광경에서 — 그것이 나를 비참하게 만들고, 그것이 일깨운 의존의 감각, 나아가 굴욕의 감각이 쓰라렸음에도 — 나는 이 광경에서, 에스텔라가 사내들에게 미스 해비셤의 복수를 행하도록 정해져 있다는 것을, 그리고 그녀가 한동안 그 복수를 채워 줄 때까지는 내게 주어지지 않으리라는 것을 보았다. 나는 이 광경에서, 그녀가 미리부터 내게 정해져 있던 이유를 보았다. 그녀를 내보내어 사내들을 끌고 괴롭히고 해코지하게 하면서, 미스 해비셤은 그녀가 어떤 숭배자도 손 닿지 못할 곳에 있다는, 그리고 거기에 운을 거는 자는 모조리 잃게끔 정해져 있다는 악의에 찬 확신을 함께 들려 보냈던 것이다. 나는 이 광경에서, 상이 나를 위해 따로 마련되어 있는 동안조차 나 또한 비뚤어진 술책에 의해 괴로움을 당하고 있다는 것을 보았다. 나는 이 광경에서, 내가 그토록 오래 따돌림당해 온 이유를, 그리고 나의 지난날 후견인이 그런 음모를 공식적으로 안다고 자처하기를 마다한 이유를 보았다. 한마디로, 나는 이 광경에서, 그때 그 자리에서 내 눈앞에 있던 그대로의, 그리고 늘 내 눈앞에 있어 온 그대로의 미스 해비셤을 보았다. 그리고 나는 이 광경에서, 그분의 삶이 햇빛으로부터 숨겨져 있던 그 어둡고 병든 집의 또렷한 그림자를 보았다.\n\n그분의 그 방을 밝히던 촛불들은 벽에 붙은 촛대에 꽂혀 있었다. 그것들은 바닥에서 높이 떨어져 있었고, 좀처럼 새 공기가 들지 않는 대기 속에서 인공조명 특유의 한결같은 둔탁함으로 타고 있었다. 그 촛불들을, 그리고 그것들이 자아내는 희뿌연 어스름을, 그리고 멈춰 선 시계를, 그리고 탁자와 바닥 위에 놓인 시들어 버린 신부 예복 따위를, 그리고 난롯불이 천장과 벽에 던져 크게 비춘 망령 같은 그림자와 더불어 그분 자신의 끔찍한 형체를 둘러보면서, 나는 모든 것 속에서 내 마음이 다다른 그 결론이 거듭 비치고 내게 되던져지는 것을 보았다. 내 생각은 층계참 건너편의, 식탁이 차려져 있는 그 큰 방으로 옮겨 갔고, 거기서 나는 그 결론이 적혀 있는 것을 보았으니 — 가운데 장식에서 늘어진 거미줄에, 식탁보 위를 기어 다니는 거미들에, 작고 다급해진 심장을 안고 벽판 뒤로 내빼는 생쥐들의 발자취에, 그리고 마룻바닥 위에서 더듬거리며 멈칫대는 딱정벌레들에, 그것은 적혀 있었다.\n\n이 방문 때에 마침 에스텔라와 미스 해비셤 사이에 날 선 말이 오가는 일이 있었다. 두 사람이 맞서는 것을 내가 본 것은 그때가 처음이었다.\n\n방금 묘사한 대로 우리는 난롯가에 앉아 있었고, 미스 해비셤은 여전히 에스텔라의 팔을 자신의 팔에 꿰고 에스텔라의 손을 자기 손으로 움켜쥐고 있었는데, 그때 에스텔라가 차츰 몸을 빼기 시작했다. 그녀는 그 전에도 한두 번 거만한 짜증을 내비친 적이 있었고, 그 격렬한 애정을 받아들이거나 되돌려 주기보다는 차라리 견뎌 내고 있던 터였다.\n\n\"뭐라고!\" 미스 해비셤이 그녀에게 두 눈을 번득이며 말했다. \"내가 지겨워졌느냐?\"\n\n\"그저 제 자신이 조금 지겨워졌을 뿐이에요.\" 에스텔라가 팔을 빼며 대답하고는, 커다란 벽난로 선반 쪽으로 옮겨 가, 그 앞에 서서 불을 내려다보았다.\n\n\"사실대로 말해, 이 배은망덕한 것아!\" 미스 해비셤이 격정에 차서 지팡이로 바닥을 내리치며 외쳤다. \"내가 지겨워진 게지.\"\n\n에스텔라는 더없이 침착하게 그분을 바라보다가, 다시 불을 내려다보았다. 그녀의 우아한 자태와 아름다운 얼굴은 상대의 그 광포한 열기에 대한, 거의 잔인하다 할 만큼 차분한 무관심을 드러내고 있었다.\n\n\"이 목석 같은 것!\" 미스 해비셤이 부르짖었다. \"이 차디찬, 차디찬 마음을 가진 것!\"\n\n\"뭐라고요?\" 에스텔라가 커다란 벽난로 선반에 기댄 채 무관심한 태도를 그대로 지키며, 눈동자만 움직여 말했다. \"제가 차갑다고 나무라시는 건가요? 당신이요?\"\n\n\"네가 차갑지 않다는 게냐?\" 사나운 반박이 돌아왔다.\n\n\"그건 당신이 아셔야지요.\" 에스텔라가 말했다. \"저는 당신이 만든 그대로의 사람이에요. 칭찬도 다 가지시고, 비난도 다 가지세요. 성공도 다 가지시고, 실패도 다 가지세요. 한마디로, 저를 다 가지세요.\"\n\n\"오, 저것 좀 봐, 저것 좀 봐!\" 미스 해비셤이 쓰라리게 외쳤다. \"저렇게 모질고 은혜를 모르는 꼴을, 저 애가 길러진 바로 이 화롯가에서! 처음으로 찔린 상처에서 피를 흘리던 이 비참한 가슴에 내가 저 애를 받아들였던 곳, 그리고 내가 여러 해의 다정함을 저 애에게 아낌없이 쏟아부었던 이곳에서!\"\n\n\"적어도 저는 그 약조에 끼어든 적이 없어요.\" 에스텔라가 말했다. \"그 약조가 맺어졌을 때 제가 걷고 말할 수 있었다 한들, 그게 제가 할 수 있는 전부였으니까요. 하지만 무엇을 원하세요? 당신은 제게 무척 잘해 주셨고, 저는 모든 것을 당신께 빚지고 있어요. 무엇을 원하세요?\"\n\n\"사랑이지.\" 상대가 대답했다.\n\n\"그건 가지고 계세요.\"\n\n\"못 가졌다.\" 미스 해비셤이 말했다.\n\n\"양어머니,\" 에스텔라가, 그 편안하고 우아한 태도에서 조금도 벗어나지 않은 채, 상대처럼 목소리를 높이는 일도 없이, 노여움에도 다정함에도 굴하는 일 없이 받아쳤다. \"양어머니, 저는 모든 것을 당신께 빚지고 있다고 말했어요. 제가 가진 모든 것은 거리낌 없이 당신 것이에요. 당신이 제게 주신 모든 것은, 도로 거두어 가시겠다면 당신 뜻대로예요. 그 너머로는 제게 아무것도 없어요. 그런데 당신이 한 번도 제게 주지 않은 것을 당신께 드리라 하신다면, 제 감사와 도리로도 불가능한 일은 할 수 없는 거예요.\"\n\n\"내가 저 애에게 사랑을 준 적이 없다고!\" 미스 해비셤이 격하게 내게로 돌아서며 외쳤다. \"저 애가 내게 저렇게 말하는 마당에도, 내가 저 애에게 늘 질투와 떼려야 뗄 수 없고 쓰라린 아픔과도 떼려야 뗄 수 없는 불타는 사랑을 준 적이 없다고! 나더러 미쳤다고 하라지, 미쳤다고 하라지!\"\n\n\"제가 왜 당신을 미쳤다고 하겠어요.\" 에스텔라가 응수했다. \"하필 제가요? 당신이 어떤 작정을 품으셨는지, 저만큼 잘 아는 사람이 세상에 살아 있나요? 당신의 기억이 얼마나 한결같은지, 저만큼 잘 아는 사람이 세상에 살아 있나요? 지금도 당신 곁에 놓여 있는 저 작은 걸상에 앉아 바로 이 화롯가에서, 당신의 가르침을 익히며 당신 얼굴을 올려다보던 저인데요. 그 얼굴이 낯설어 저를 겁먹게 하던 때에 말이에요!\"\n\n\"금세 잊었구나!\" 미스 해비셤이 신음했다. \"그 시절을 금세 잊었어!\"\n\n\"아니에요, 잊지 않았어요.\" 에스텔라가 받아쳤다. \"잊지 않고, 제 기억 속에 소중히 간직해 두었어요. 제가 당신의 가르침을 저버린 적이 언제 있었나요? 제가 당신의 교훈을 소홀히 한 적이 언제 있었나요? 제가 여기에,\" 그녀가 손으로 자기 가슴을 짚었다. \"당신이 막아 두신 어떤 것이든 들여놓은 적이 언제 있었나요? 제게 공정하게 대해 주세요.\"\n\n\"저토록 거만하구나, 저토록 거만해!\" 미스 해비셤이 두 손으로 자신의 잿빛 머리카락을 밀어 헤치며 신음했다.\n\n\"누가 저를 거만하게끔 가르쳤나요?\" 에스텔라가 응수했다. \"제가 그 가르침을 익혔을 때 누가 저를 칭찬했나요?\"\n\n\"저토록 모질구나, 저토록 모질어!\" 미스 해비셤이 앞서와 같은 동작을 하며 신음했다.\n\n\"누가 저를 모질게끔 가르쳤나요?\" 에스텔라가 응수했다. \"제가 그 가르침을 익혔을 때 누가 저를 칭찬했나요?\"\n\n\"하지만 나에게 거만하고 모질다니!\" 미스 해비셤이 두 팔을 뻗으며 거의 비명을 질렀다. \"에스텔라, 에스텔라, 에스텔라, 나에게 거만하고 모질다니!\"\n\n에스텔라는 일종의 차분한 의아함을 담아 잠시 그분을 바라보았으나, 그 밖에 달리 흔들리지는 않았다. 그 순간이 지나자, 그녀는 다시 불을 내려다보았다.\n\n\"이별 끝에 제가 당신을 뵈러 왔는데,\" 잠시 침묵하던 에스텔라가 눈을 들며 말했다. \"당신이 어찌하여 그토록 사리에 맞지 않게 구시는지 저는 모르겠어요. 저는 당신이 입은 부당한 일과 그 까닭을 한 번도 잊은 적이 없어요. 저는 당신께도, 당신의 가르침에도 한 번도 거스른 적이 없어요. 저는 스스로 탓할 만한 어떤 나약함도 보인 적이 없어요.\"\n\n\"내 사랑에 보답하는 것이 나약함이라는 게냐?\" 미스 해비셤이 외쳤다. \"그래, 그래, 저 애는 그걸 나약함이라 부르겠지!\"\n\n\"이제 어렴풋이 알 것 같아요.\" 또 한순간 차분한 의아함에 잠겼던 에스텔라가, 곰곰이 생각하는 투로 말했다. \"어쩌다 이리되었는지 거의 알 것 같아요. 만약 당신이 양딸을 이 방들의 어두운 감금 속에서만 길러 내고, 그 애가 당신 얼굴을 한 번도 본 적 없게 한 그 햇빛이라는 것이 세상에 있다는 걸 끝내 알지 못하게 하셨다면 — 그렇게 해 놓고는, 어떤 목적을 위해 그 애가 햇빛을 이해하고 그것에 관해 다 알기를 바라셨다면, 당신은 실망하고 노여워하셨겠지요?\"\n\n미스 해비셤은 두 손에 머리를 묻은 채 나직이 신음하며 의자에 앉아 몸을 앞뒤로 흔들고 있었으나, 아무 대답도 하지 않았다.\n\n\"아니면,\" 에스텔라가 말했다. \"이쪽이 더 가까운 경우인데요 — 만약 당신이, 그 애의 지각이 트일 때부터 온 힘과 기력을 다해, 햇빛이라는 것이 세상에 있기는 하나 그것은 그 애의 적이요 파괴자가 되게끔 만들어진 것이니 늘 그것에 등을 돌려야 한다고, 그것이 당신을 망쳐 놓았고 그러지 않으면 그 애마저 망쳐 놓으리라고 가르치셨다면 — 그렇게 해 놓고는, 어떤 목적을 위해 그 애가 햇빛에 자연스레 다가가기를 바라셨는데 그 애가 그러지 못한다면, 당신은 실망하고 노여워하셨겠지요?\"\n\n미스 해비셤은 귀 기울여 듣고 앉아 있었으나 (그렇게 보였다, 그분의 얼굴은 볼 수 없었으니까) 여전히 아무 대답도 하지 않았다.\n\n\"그러니,\" 에스텔라가 말했다. \"저는 만들어진 그대로 받아들여져야 해요. 성공도 제 것이 아니고, 실패도 제 것이 아니지만, 그 둘이 합쳐져 저를 이루는 거예요.\"\n\n미스 해비셤은, 어찌 된 영문인지 나도 잘 모르는 사이에, 바닥에 흩어진 빛바랜 신부 예복의 잔해들 가운데로 주저앉아 있었다. 나는 그 순간을 틈타 — 처음부터 나는 그런 순간을 노리고 있었다 — 손짓으로 에스텔라에게 그분을 보살펴 달라고 청한 뒤, 방을 나왔다. 내가 나올 때, 에스텔라는 줄곧 서 있던 그대로, 여전히 커다란 벽난로 선반 곁에 서 있었다. 미스 해비셤의 잿빛 머리카락은 다른 신부 예복의 잔해들 사이로 온통 흐트러진 채 바닥에 널려 있었으니, 보기에 처량한 광경이었다.\n\n나는 침울한 마음으로 한 시간 남짓, 별빛 아래 안뜰을, 양조장을, 그리고 폐허가 된 정원을 거닐었다. 마침내 용기를 내어 방으로 돌아가 보니, 에스텔라가 미스 해비셤의 무릎 곁에 앉아, 너덜너덜 헤어져 가던 그 낡은 예복 가운데 하나에 몇 땀 바느질을 하고 있었다. 그 예복은, 그 뒤로 내가 대성당에 걸려 있는 것을 본, 빛바랜 누더기 옛 깃발들을 통해 종종 떠올리게 된 바로 그런 물건이었다. 그 후 에스텔라와 나는 옛날처럼 카드놀이를 했는데 — 다만 이제 우리는 솜씨가 늘어, 프랑스식 놀이를 했다 — 그렇게 저녁이 저물어 갔고, 나는 잠자리에 들었다.\n\n나는 안뜰 건너편의 그 별채에서 잤다. 새티스 하우스에서 내가 쉬려고 누워 본 것은 그때가 처음이었는데, 잠은 좀처럼 내게 다가오려 하지 않았다. 천 명의 미스 해비셤이 나를 따라다녔다. 그분은 내 베개 이쪽에, 저쪽에, 침대 머리맡에, 발치에, 반쯤 열린 화장실 문 뒤에, 화장실 안에, 위층 방에, 아래층 방에 — 어디에나 있었다. 마침내 밤이 느릿느릿 두 시를 향해 기어가던 무렵, 나는 더 이상은 이곳을 누워 쉴 곳으로 도저히 견딜 수 없으며, 일어나야만 한다는 느낌에 사로잡혔다. 그래서 나는 일어나 옷을 입고, 마음을 달래려고 바깥뜰로 나가 그곳을 거닐 작정으로, 뜰을 가로질러 긴 돌 복도로 나섰다. 그러나 복도에 들어서기가 무섭게 나는 촛불을 꺼 버렸다. 미스 해비셤이 망령처럼 그 복도를 따라 걸어가며 나직이 흐느끼는 소리를 내고 있는 것을 보았기 때문이다. 나는 멀찍이서 그분을 뒤따랐고, 그분이 층계를 올라가는 것을 보았다. 그분은 손에 촛대도 없는 맨 양초 하나를 들고 있었는데, 아마도 자기 방의 촛대 가운데 하나에서 빼 든 것이었을 테고, 그 불빛 속의 그분은 더없이 섬뜩한 형상이었다. 층계 밑에 서서, 나는 그분이 문을 여는 것을 보지는 못한 채 그 잔치방의 곰팡내 나는 공기를 느꼈고, 그분이 그 안을 거니는 소리를, 그러고는 자기 방으로 건너갔다가 다시 그 방으로 건너오는 소리를 들었으니, 나직한 흐느낌은 그칠 줄을 몰랐다. 한참 뒤, 나는 어둠 속에서 밖으로 나가 보려고도, 도로 돌아가 보려고도 했으나, 한 줄기 새벽빛이 새어 들어 손 짚을 곳을 일러 줄 때까지는 어느 쪽도 할 수 없었다. 그동안 내내, 내가 층계 밑으로 갈 때마다, 나는 그분의 발소리를 듣고, 그분의 불빛이 위로 지나가는 것을 보고, 그분의 그칠 줄 모르는 나직한 흐느낌을 들었다.\n\n이튿날 우리가 떠나기 전, 그분과 에스텔라 사이의 그 불화가 되살아나는 일은 없었고, 그와 비슷한 어떤 자리에서도 그것이 되살아난 적은 없었다. 그리고 내 기억이 옳다면, 그와 비슷한 자리는 네 번 있었다. 또한 미스 해비셤이 에스텔라를 대하는 태도도 조금도 변하지 않았으니, 다만 그 예전의 특징들 가운데 두려움 비슷한 무언가가 스며든 것 같다고 나는 믿었다.\n\n벤틀리 드러믈의 이름을 거기 적어 넣지 않고서는 내 인생의 이 책장을 넘길 수가 없다. 그러지 않을 수만 있다면 나는 기꺼이 그러고 싶지만.\n\n언젠가 핀치 회원들이 한껏 모여, 아무도 다른 누구의 말에 동의하지 않는 평소의 방식으로 좋은 분위기가 무르익어 가던 자리에서, 사회를 맡은 핀치가 그로브의 정숙을 청했다. 드러믈 씨가 아직 어느 숙녀를 위해 건배를 올리지 않았기 때문이었는데, 이 모임의 엄숙한 규약에 따르자면 그날 그 짐승이 그 일을 할 차례였던 것이다. 술병이 돌아가는 동안 그자가 나를 향해 흉하게 곁눈질하며 히죽거리는 것을 본 듯했으나, 우리 사이에 정이라곤 없었으니 그건 능히 그럴 법한 일이었다. 그자가 좌중에게 \"에스텔라!\"를 위해 함께 건배하자고 청했을 때, 내가 느낀 분개에 찬 놀라움이란!\n\n\"어디의 에스텔라요?\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"당신이 알 바 아니오.\" 드러믈이 받아쳤다.\n\n\"어디 사는 에스텔라요?\" 내가 말했다. \"어디 사는지를 말해야 하오.\" 핀치 회원으로서 그는 마땅히 그래야 했다.\n\n\"리치먼드의 에스텔라요, 여러분.\" 드러믈이 나를 무시한 채 말했다. \"비할 데 없는 미인이지요.\"\n\n비할 데 없는 미인에 대해 그자가 무얼 안다고, 야비하고 한심한 멍청이 같으니! 나는 허버트에게 속삭였다.\n\n\"나는 그 숙녀를 안다네.\" 건배가 끝난 뒤 허버트가 식탁 너머로 말했다.\n\n\"그래?\" 드러믈이 말했다.\n\n\"나도 알지.\" 내가 새빨개진 얼굴로 덧붙였다.\n\n\"그래?\" 드러믈이 말했다. \"허, 이런!\"\n\n이 무거운 짐승이 할 수 있는 응수란 — 유리잔이나 사기그릇을 던지는 것 말고는 — 그것뿐이었다. 그러나 나는 그것이 마치 위트로 날을 세운 것이기라도 한 양 잔뜩 분개해, 그 자리에서 즉시 일어나, 자기가 전혀 알지도 못하는 숙녀를 추천하려고 이 그로브로 내려오다니 — 우리는 늘 그 그로브로 \"내려온다\"고 말했으니, 의회식의 말끔한 표현법을 본뜬 것이었다 — 그 그로브로 내려오다니, 명예로운 핀치 회원의 뻔뻔함이라고밖에 볼 수 없다고 말했다. 이에 드러믈 씨가 벌떡 일어나, 그게 무슨 뜻이냐고 따져 물었다. 그러자 나는 그에게, 나를 어디서 찾을 수 있는지는 그가 알 거라고 믿는다는 극단적인 대꾸를 했다.\n\n이 일이 있은 뒤, 기독교 국가에서 피를 보지 않고 일을 마무리할 수 있느냐 하는 것이 핀치 회원들 사이에서 의견이 갈리는 문제였다. 그것을 둘러싼 논쟁이 어찌나 활기를 띠었던지, 논의가 진행되는 동안 적어도 여섯 명의 명예로운 회원이 또 다른 여섯 명에게, 자기들을 어디서 찾을 수 있는지는 자기들도 알 거라고 믿는다고 말했을 정도였다. 그러나 마침내 (그로브는 명예 법정이었으므로) 다음과 같이 결정되었다. 만약 드러믈 씨가 그 숙녀와 알고 지내는 영광을 누렸음을 알리는, 아무리 사소한 것이라도 그 숙녀의 증명서를 가져온다면, 핍 씨는 신사로서 또한 핀치 회원으로서, \"어떠한 흥분에 빠졌던\" 것을 유감으로 표해야 한다는 것이었다. 증명서 제출은 (지체하다 우리의 명예가 식어 버릴까 하여) 이튿날로 정해졌고, 이튿날 드러믈은, 자기가 그와 여러 차례 춤추는 영광을 누렸다는, 에스텔라의 필체로 된 정중하고 자그마한 인정의 글을 들고 나타났다. 이로써 나는 \"어떠한 흥분에 빠졌던\" 것을 유감으로 표하고, 또 전체적으로는 나를 어디서든 찾을 수 있으리라는 생각을 근거 없는 것으로 철회하는 수밖에 다른 길이 없었다. 그리하여 드러믈과 나는 한 시간 동안 서로를 향해 콧방귀를 뀌며 앉아 있었고, 그동안 그로브는 가리지 않는 반박에 몰두했으며, 마침내 좋은 분위기의 증진이 놀라운 속도로 진척되었다고 선언되었다.\n\n나는 이 일을 가볍게 이야기하지만, 내게는 결코 가벼운 일이 아니었다. 에스텔라가, 평균에도 한참 못 미치는, 그토록 경멸스럽고 굼뜨고 뚱한 멍청이에게 조금이라도 호의를 보였으리라는 생각이 내게 안긴 고통이 어떤 것이었는지, 나는 도저히 제대로 표현할 수가 없다. 지금 이 순간까지도 나는, 그녀가 그 개 같은 자에게 몸을 굽히는 것을 차마 견딜 수 없었던 까닭이, 그녀를 향한 내 사랑 속의 어떤 순수한 관대함과 사심 없음의 불꽃에서 비롯된 것이었다고 믿는다. 그녀가 누구에게 호의를 보였든 나는 비참했으리라는 데 의심의 여지가 없으나, 그보다 더 합당한 상대였다면 내게 다른 종류와 정도의 괴로움을 안겼을 것이다.\n\n드러믈이 그녀를 바짝 따라다니기 시작했다는 것, 그리고 그녀가 그러도록 내버려 두고 있다는 것을 알아내기란 내게 쉬운 일이었고, 나는 곧 그것을 알아냈다. 얼마 지나지 않아 그는 늘 그녀를 좇고 있었고, 그와 나는 날마다 마주쳤다. 그는 둔하고 끈질긴 태도로 매달렸고, 에스텔라는 그를 붙들어 두었다. 어떤 때는 부추기고, 어떤 때는 낙담시키고, 어떤 때는 거의 그를 추켜세우고, 어떤 때는 대놓고 그를 멸시하고, 어떤 때는 그를 아주 잘 아는 듯하다가, 어떤 때는 그가 누구였는지 거의 기억도 못 하는 듯이.\n\n그러나 재거스 씨가 거미라 불렀던 그자는 매복에 익숙했고, 제 족속다운 인내심을 지니고 있었다. 거기에 더해, 그는 제 돈과 제 가문의 위세에 대한 멍청한 자신감을 지니고 있었는데, 그것이 이따금 그에게 큰 도움이 되었으니 — 집중력과 굳은 결의를 거의 대신할 정도였다. 그리하여 거미는, 끈덕지게 에스텔라를 지켜보면서, 저보다 훨씬 영민한 숱한 벌레들보다 더 오래 지켜보았고, 종종 적시에 몸을 풀어 뚝 떨어지곤 했다.\n\n리치먼드의 어느 무도회에서 (그 시절엔 거의 모든 곳에 무도회가 있곤 했다), 에스텔라가 다른 모든 미인들을 압도하던 그 자리에서, 이 얼뜬 드러믈이 그녀 곁을 어찌나 떠나지 못하던지, 게다가 그녀 쪽에서도 어찌나 너그러이 봐주던지, 나는 그에 관해 그녀에게 말해야겠다고 결심했다. 나는 다음 기회를 잡았다. 그녀가 브랜들리 부인이 자기를 집에 데려가 주기를 기다리며, 떠날 채비를 한 채 꽃들 사이에 떨어져 앉아 있을 때였다. 나는 그녀와 함께 있었으니, 그런 자리를 오갈 때 나는 거의 언제나 그들을 동행했던 것이다.\n\n\"피곤한가요, 에스텔라?\"\n\n\"좀 그래요, 핍.\"\n\n\"그럴 만도 하지요.\"\n\n\"차라리 피곤하지 말아야 한다고 하는 편이 낫겠어요. 잠들기 전에 새티스 하우스로 보낼 편지를 써야 하니까요.\"\n\n\"오늘 밤의 개가를 늘어놓는 건가요?\" 내가 말했다. \"참으로 보잘것없는 개가지요, 에스텔라.\"\n\n\"무슨 말이에요? 무슨 개가가 있었다는 건지 난 몰랐는데요.\"\n\n\"에스텔라,\" 내가 말했다. \"저기 저 구석에서 이쪽의 우리를 건너다보고 있는 저 친구 좀 봐요.\"\n\n\"내가 왜 그를 봐야 하죠?\" 에스텔라가 그 대신 나를 바라보며 응수했다. \"저기 저 구석의 저 친구에게 — 당신 말을 빌리자면 — 내가 봐야 할 무엇이 있다는 거죠?\"\n\n\"실은 그게 바로 내가 당신에게 묻고 싶은 거예요.\" 내가 말했다. \"그가 밤새도록 당신 주위를 맴돌고 있었으니까요.\"\n\n\"나방이며 온갖 흉한 것들은,\" 에스텔라가 그를 향해 흘긋 눈길을 던지며 대꾸했다. \"불 켜진 촛불 주위를 맴도는 법이지요. 그게 촛불 탓이겠어요?\"\n\n\"아니지요.\" 내가 받았다. \"하지만 그 에스텔라는 어쩔 수 없는 걸까요?\"\n\n\"글쎄요!\" 그녀가 잠시 뒤 웃으며 말했다. \"어쩌면요. 그래요. 당신 좋을 대로요.\"\n\n\"하지만, 에스텔라, 부디 내 말 좀 들어 봐요. 드러믈처럼 두루 멸시받는 사내를 당신이 부추긴다는 게 나를 비참하게 만들어요. 그가 멸시받는 자라는 걸 당신도 알잖아요.\"\n\n\"그래서요?\" 그녀가 말했다.\n\n\"그가 겉모습만큼이나 속도 볼품없다는 걸 당신도 알잖아요. 모자라고, 성마르고, 음침하고, 멍청한 친구라는 걸요.\"\n\n\"그래서요?\" 그녀가 말했다.\n\n\"그를 추천할 거라곤 돈과, 머리가 텅 빈 조상들의 우스꽝스러운 명부밖에 없다는 걸 당신도 알잖아요. 자, 그렇지 않나요?\"\n\n\"그래서요?\" 그녀가 다시 말했다. 그리고 그 말을 할 때마다, 그녀는 그 사랑스러운 두 눈을 점점 더 크게 떴다.\n\n그 외마디를 넘어서기가 어려워, 나는 그 말을 그녀에게서 가져다, 힘주어 되풀이하며 말했다. \"그래서라니요! 그러니까, 그래서 나를 비참하게 만든다는 거예요.\"\n\n만약 그녀가 나를 — 바로 나를 — 비참하게 만들려는 어떤 생각으로 드러믈에게 호의를 보이는 것이라고 믿을 수 있었다면, 나는 그에 관해 한결 마음이 나았을 것이다. 그러나 그녀는 그 늘 하던 식으로, 나를 너무도 철저히 안중에 두지 않았으니, 나는 그런 것이라곤 도무지 믿을 수 없었다.\n\n\"핍,\" 에스텔라가 방 안을 휘 둘러보며 말했다. \"그것이 당신에게 미치는 영향에 대해 어리석게 굴지 말아요. 그것이 다른 이들에게는 영향을 미칠지도 모르고, 또 그러도록 의도된 것인지도 모르지요. 따져 볼 가치도 없는 일이에요.\"\n\n\"가치가 있어요.\" 내가 말했다. \"사람들이 '저 여자는 자신의 우아함과 매력을, 무리 가운데 가장 천한 한낱 촌뜨기에게 내던지고 있군' 하고 말하는 걸 나는 견딜 수가 없으니까요.\"\n\n\"나는 견딜 수 있어요.\" 에스텔라가 말했다.\n\n\"오! 그렇게 거만하게, 그렇게 완고하게 굴지 말아요, 에스텔라.\"\n\n\"같은 입으로 나더러 거만하고 완고하다고 하다니요!\" 에스텔라가 두 손을 펼치며 말했다. \"그리고 바로 앞서는, 내가 촌뜨기에게 몸을 굽힌다고 나무라고서는!\"\n\n\"당신이 그러는 건 의심할 여지가 없어요.\" 나는 다소 다급하게 말했다. \"바로 오늘 밤에도, 당신이 그에게, 한 번도 나에게는 주지 않은 그런 눈길과 미소를 주는 걸 내가 보았으니까요.\"\n\n\"그러면 당신은,\" 에스텔라가 노여운 것까지는 아니더라도 고정되고 진지한 표정으로 별안간 돌아서며 말했다. \"내가 당신을 속이고 옭아매기를 바라는 건가요?\"\n\n\"당신은 그를 속이고 옭아매나요, 에스텔라?\"\n\n\"그래요, 그리고 다른 많은 이들도요 — 당신만 빼고 모두를요. 저기 브랜들리 부인이 오시네요. 더는 말하지 않겠어요.\"\n\n그리하여 내 마음을 그토록 가득 채웠고, 그토록 자주 내 가슴을 아프게 하고 또 아프게 했던 그 주제에 한 장을 바쳤으니, 나는 거침없이 나아가, 그보다도 더 오래 내 위에 드리워져 있던 그 사건으로 옮겨 간다. 내가 세상에 에스텔라라는 존재가 있다는 것을 알기도 전에, 그리고 그녀의 어린 지각이 미스 해비셤의 메말라 가는 손길에서 그 첫 왜곡을 받고 있던 그 시절에 이미 준비되기 시작했던 그 사건으로.\n\n동방의 이야기 속에서, 정복의 환희에 들뜬 옥좌의 침상 위로 떨어지도록 마련된 그 무거운 석판은 채석장에서 더디게 깎여 나왔고, 그것을 제자리에 붙들어 둘 밧줄을 꿸 굴은 몇 리에 걸친 바위를 뚫고 더디게 파였으며, 석판은 더디게 들어 올려져 천장에 끼워졌고, 밧줄이 거기 꿰여 몇 마일에 이르는 빈 통로를 지나 그 커다란 쇠고리까지 더디게 이끌렸다. 숱한 수고로 모든 것이 채비를 갖추고 그 시각이 다다르자, 술탄은 깊은 밤중에 깨워져, 그 커다란 쇠고리에서 밧줄을 끊어 낼 잘 벼린 도끼가 그의 손에 쥐여졌고, 그가 그것으로 내리치자 밧줄이 끊어져 휙 달아나 버렸으며, 천장이 무너져 내렸다. 그러니, 내 경우도 그러했다. 가깝고도 먼 곳에서, 그 종말로 향해 가던 모든 작업이 다 이루어져 있었던 것이다. 그리하여 한순간에 일격이 가해졌고, 내 보루의 지붕이 내 위로 떨어져 내렸다.\n\n# Chapter XXXIX.\n\n나는 스물세 살이었다. 내 유산에 관한 문제를 밝혀 줄 한마디도 더 듣지 못한 채, 내 스물세 번째 생일이 지나간 지 일주일이 되었다. 우리는 바너드 여관을 떠난 지 일 년이 넘었고, 이제 템플에서 살고 있었다. 우리 거처는 강가 아래쪽 가든코트에 있었다.\n\n포켓 군과 나는 우리의 원래 관계에 관한 한 얼마간 손을 떼었지만, 더없이 좋은 사이는 그대로 이어 가고 있었다. 무엇 하나에도 진득하게 정착하지 못하는 내 무능함에도 불구하고 — 그것은 불안정하고 미완인 조건 위에 내 재력을 쥐고 있던 데서 비롯된 것이기를 바라거니와 — 나는 독서에 취미가 있어, 하루에 정해진 시간만큼 꾸준히 책을 읽었다. 허버트의 그 일도 여전히 진척되어 가고 있었고, 나에 관한 모든 사정은 바로 앞 장의 끝에서 내가 적어 내려온 그대로였다.\n\n볼일이 있어 허버트는 마르세유로 여행을 떠난 참이었다. 나는 혼자였고, 혼자라는 것에 둔한 느낌을 받고 있었다. 의기소침하고 불안해진 채, 내일이나 다음 주면 내 앞길이 열리리라 오래 기대했다가 오래 실망해 온 터라, 나는 친구의 쾌활한 얼굴과 시원시원한 응답이 사무치게 그리웠다.\n\n날씨는 고약했다. 비바람이 몰아치고 또 몰아쳤다. 그리고 진흙, 진흙, 진흙이 거리란 거리마다 깊이 깔려 있었다. 날이면 날마다 거대하고 묵직한 장막이 동쪽에서부터 런던 위로 휘몰아쳐 왔고, 마치 동쪽에 구름과 바람의 영원이 자리하고 있기라도 한 듯 여전히 휘몰아쳤다. 돌풍이 어찌나 사나웠던지, 시내의 높은 건물들은 지붕의 납 덮개가 벗겨져 나갔다. 시골에서는 나무가 뿌리째 뽑히고 풍차의 날개가 날아갔으며, 해안에서는 난파와 죽음에 관한 음울한 소식이 들려왔다. 거센 빗줄기가 이 바람의 광란을 동반했고, 내가 책을 읽으려 앉았을 때 막 저물어 가던 그날은 그 모든 날 가운데 가장 험악한 날이었다.\n\n그 이후로 템플의 그 일대는 손질이 가해져, 이제는 그때만큼 외진 분위기도 아니고, 강에 그토록 노출되어 있지도 않다. 우리는 맨 끝 집 꼭대기에 살고 있었는데, 그날 밤 강을 거슬러 치달아 오는 바람은 대포의 발사나 바다의 부서짐처럼 집을 뒤흔들었다. 거기에 비까지 실려 와 창문을 후려치자, 나는 흔들리는 창들로 눈을 들어 올리며, 폭풍에 두들겨 맞는 등대 안에 있는 듯한 착각마저 들 법하다고 생각했다. 이따금 연기는 그런 밤 속으로 빠져나가기가 견디기 힘들다는 듯이 굴뚝을 타고 도로 굴러 내려왔다. 문을 열어 계단을 내려다보니, 계단의 등불은 바람에 꺼져 있었다. 두 손으로 얼굴을 가리고 검은 창들을 통해 내다보니(그런 비바람의 기세 앞에서 창문을 손톱만큼이라도 여는 일은 어림도 없었다), 안마당의 등불도 꺼져 있었고, 다리와 강변의 등불은 부들부들 떨고 있었으며, 강 위 거룻배에 피운 석탄불은 빗속에서 벌겋게 단 물보라처럼 바람에 실려 가고 있는 것이 보였다.\n\n나는 탁자 위에 시계를 올려놓고, 열한 시에 책을 덮으리라 작정하며 읽었다. 책을 덮는 순간, 세인트폴 대성당을 비롯해 시내의 그 많은 교회 시계들이 — 어떤 것은 앞장서고, 어떤 것은 같이 울리고, 어떤 것은 뒤따르며 — 그 시각을 알렸다. 그 소리는 바람에 묘하게 일그러져 있었다. 나는 그 소리에 귀를 기울이며, 바람이 어떻게 그것을 공격하고 갈가리 찢어 놓는가를 생각하고 있던 차에, 계단에서 발소리를 들었다.\n\n어떤 신경질적인 어리석음이 나를 흠칫 놀라게 했고, 그 소리를 죽은 누나의 발소리와 끔찍하게도 연결 짓게 했는지는 중요하지 않다. 그것은 한순간에 지나갔고, 나는 다시 귀를 기울였으며, 발소리가 올라오다 비틀거리는 것을 들었다. 그제야 계단 등불이 꺼졌다는 사실이 떠올라, 나는 독서용 등불을 집어 들고 계단참으로 나갔다. 아래에 있던 사람이 누구든 내 등불을 보고 멈춰 선 모양이었으니, 온통 조용했기 때문이다.\n\n\"거기 아래 누가 있군요, 그렇지요?\" 나는 아래를 내려다보며 소리쳤다.\n\n\"그렇소.\" 아래 어둠 속에서 한 목소리가 대답했다.\n\n\"몇 층을 찾으십니까?\"\n\n\"꼭대기요. 핍 씨.\"\n\n\"그게 제 이름입니다만. 무슨 일이라도 있는 겁니까?\"\n\n\"무슨 일은 없소.\" 그 목소리가 대답했다. 그러고는 사내가 올라왔다.\n\n나는 등불을 계단 난간 너머로 내밀고 서 있었고, 사내는 천천히 그 불빛 안으로 들어왔다. 그것은 책을 비추도록 갓을 씌운 등불이라 불빛의 둘레가 몹시 좁았으므로, 사내는 그저 한순간 그 안에 들어왔다가 곧 벗어났다. 그 한순간에 나는 내게 낯선 얼굴 하나를 보았는데, 나를 보는 것만으로 마음이 움직여 흡족해진다는 듯한, 도무지 영문 모를 기색으로 나를 올려다보고 있었다.\n\n사내가 움직이는 대로 등불을 옮겨 가며, 나는 그가 옷차림은 그럴듯하나 거칠어서 바다를 떠도는 여행자 같다는 것을 알아보았다. 길고 희끗한 잿빛 머리칼을 가진 사내였다. 나이는 예순쯤 되어 보였다. 근육질에 다리가 튼튼했고, 비바람에 노출되어 살갗이 거뭇하게 그을고 단단해진 사내였다. 그가 마지막 한두 계단을 올라와 내 등불 빛이 우리 둘을 함께 감쌌을 때, 나는 멍청한 종류의 경악과 함께, 그가 두 손을 내게로 내밀고 있는 것을 보았다.\n\n\"무슨 볼일이신지요?\" 나는 그에게 물었다.\n\n\"내 볼일?\" 그가 잠시 멈추며 되뇌었다. \"아! 그렇지. 괜찮으시다면 내 볼일을 설명하리다.\"\n\n\"들어오시겠습니까?\"\n\n\"그렇소.\" 그가 대답했다. \"들어가고 싶소이다, 나리.\"\n\n나는 그 질문을 꽤나 박정하게 던졌으니, 그의 얼굴에 여전히 빛나고 있던, 환하고 만족스러워하는 그 알아봄의 기색이 나는 거슬렸기 때문이다. 그것이 거슬린 것은, 내가 거기에 화답하기를 그가 기대하고 있음을 암시하는 듯했기 때문이다. 그래도 나는 그를 방금 내가 떠나온 방으로 들였고, 등불을 탁자 위에 올려놓은 뒤, 되도록 정중하게 그에게 사정을 설명해 달라고 청했다.\n\n사내는 더없이 묘한 기색으로 — 마치 자기가 감탄하는 그 물건들에 자기 몫이 얼마간 있기라도 하다는 듯, 의아해하면서도 흐뭇해하는 기색으로 — 주위를 둘러보더니, 거친 겉옷과 모자를 벗었다. 그제야 나는 그의 머리가 주름지고 벗어졌으며, 길고 희끗한 잿빛 머리칼은 양옆에만 나 있다는 것을 알아보았다. 그러나 그를 조금이라도 설명해 줄 만한 것은 아무것도 보이지 않았다. 오히려 다음 순간 나는, 그가 또다시 두 손을 내게로 내미는 것을 보았다.\n\n\"무슨 뜻입니까?\" 나는 그가 미친 게 아닌가 반쯤 의심하며 말했다.\n\n사내는 나를 바라보던 것을 멈추고, 오른손으로 천천히 자기 머리를 문질렀다. \"사람한테 실망스러운 일이지.\" 그가 거칠고 갈라진 목소리로 말했다. \"그렇게 멀리서부터 고대해 오고, 그렇게 먼 길을 왔는데 말이야. 하지만 그건 자네 탓이 아냐. 우리 둘 다 탓할 일이 아니지. 반 분만 있으면 말하리다. 부디 반 분만 주시오.\"\n\n사내는 난롯불 앞에 놓인 의자에 앉아, 굵고 거뭇하고 핏줄이 불거진 두 손으로 이마를 가렸다. 그때 나는 그를 유심히 바라보다가 그에게서 조금 움찔 물러났다. 그러나 나는 그가 누군지 알지 못했다.\n\n\"근처에 아무도 없겠지.\" 그가 어깨 너머를 돌아보며 말했다. \"없지?\"\n\n\"낯선 사람이 이런 밤중에 내 방에 들어와서, 어째서 그런 걸 묻는 겁니까?\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"자넨 배짱이 있는 친구야.\" 그가 신중한 애정을 담아 내게 고개를 절레절레 흔들며 대꾸했는데, 그것은 더없이 알아들을 수 없으면서도 더없이 부아를 돋우는 짓이었다. \"자네가 배짱 있는 친구로 자라나서 기쁘구먼! 하지만 날 붙잡지는 말게. 나중에 그랬던 걸 후회하게 될 테니.\"\n\n나는 그가 알아챈 그 마음을 거두었다. 나는 그를 알아본 것이다! 아직도 이목구비 하나조차 떠올릴 수 없었으나, 나는 그를 알았다! 만일 비바람이 그 사이의 세월을 몰아내고, 그 사이의 모든 것들을 흩뜨려 버리고, 우리가 그토록 다른 처지에서 처음 얼굴을 맞대고 섰던 그 교회 묘지로 우리를 쓸어다 놓았다 한들, 나는 지금 난롯불 앞 의자에 앉아 있는 그를 알아보는 것보다 더 또렷이 내 죄수를 알아보지는 못했을 것이다. 주머니에서 줄칼을 꺼내 내게 보일 필요도 없었다. 목에서 손수건을 풀어 머리에 둘러맬 필요도 없었다. 두 팔로 제 몸을 끌어안고, 알아봐 달라는 듯 나를 돌아보며 부들부들 떨면서 방을 가로질러 한 바퀴 돌 필요도 없었다. 한순간 전만 해도 그의 정체를 어렴풋이나마 의심하고 있다는 의식조차 없었건만, 나는 그가 그런 도움 중 하나라도 베풀기 전에 이미 그를 알아보았던 것이다.\n\n사내는 내가 서 있는 데로 돌아와, 또다시 두 손을 내밀었다. 어찌할 바를 몰라 — 경악한 나머지 평정심을 잃어버린 터라 — 나는 마지못해 그에게 두 손을 내주었다. 그는 내 손을 진심으로 움켜쥐고, 입술로 들어 올려 입을 맞춘 뒤, 여전히 놓지 않았다.\n\n\"자넨 고귀하게 행동했어, 얘야.\" 그가 말했다. \"고귀했지, 핍! 난 그걸 한 번도 잊은 적이 없어!\"\n\n마치 나를 끌어안기라도 할 것처럼 그의 태도가 바뀌는 것을 보고, 나는 한 손을 그의 가슴에 대고 그를 밀쳐 냈다.\n\n\"잠깐만요!\" 내가 말했다. \"물러서요! 내가 어린아이였을 때 한 일에 고마워한다면, 그 고마움은 당신이 삶의 방식을 고쳐 살아온 것으로 보여 줬으면 합니다. 내게 고맙다는 말을 하러 여기 온 거라면, 그럴 필요는 없었어요. 그래도, 당신이 어떻게 나를 찾아냈든, 당신을 여기까지 이끈 그 마음에는 무언가 선한 것이 있을 테니, 나는 당신을 매정하게 내치지는 않겠습니다. 하지만 분명히 알아 두셔야 할 것이 — 나는 —\"\n\n나를 향한 그의 그 고정된 시선이 어찌나 기묘했던지, 거기에 마음을 빼앗겨 말이 혀끝에서 사그라들고 말았다.\n\n\"자네가 방금 한 말은,\" 우리가 말없이 서로를 마주 보고 있던 끝에 그가 입을 열었다. \"내가 분명히 알아 둬야 할 게 있다는 거였지. 대체 뭘 분명히 알아 둬야 한다는 건가?\"\n\n\"이렇게 처지가 달라진 마당에, 오래전 우연히 맺은 당신과의 그 인연을 다시 잇고 싶을 수는 없다는 겁니다. 당신이 뉘우치고 자신을 추슬렀다고 믿게 되어 기쁩니다. 그렇게 말씀드릴 수 있어 기쁩니다. 내가 고마워할 만한 사람이라 여기고 고마움을 전하러 와 주셔서 기쁩니다. 하지만 그래도 우리가 가는 길은 서로 다른 길입니다. 당신은 젖었고, 지쳐 보이는군요. 가시기 전에 뭐라도 좀 드시겠습니까?\"\n\n사내는 목수건을 다시 느슨하게 둘러맨 채, 그 기다란 끝자락을 깨물면서, 예리하게 나를 살피며 서 있었다. \"내 생각에는,\" 그가 여전히 그 끝자락을 입에 문 채, 여전히 나를 살피며 대답했다. \"가기 전에 한잔 마시기는, 마시겠소이다(고맙소).\"\n\n곁탁자 위에 쟁반이 마련되어 있었다. 나는 그것을 난롯가 탁자로 가져와, 그에게 무엇을 들겠느냐고 물었다. 그는 쳐다보지도 말하지도 않은 채 병 하나를 손으로 짚었고, 나는 그에게 뜨거운 럼주에 물을 탄 것을 만들어 주었다. 그러는 동안 손을 떨지 않으려 애썼지만, 의자에 기대앉아 목수건의 길게 늘어진 끝자락을 — 그새 잊어버린 것이 분명한 그것을 — 이 사이에 물고 나를 바라보는 그 시선 탓에 손을 다스리기가 여간 어려운 게 아니었다. 마침내 잔을 그에게 내밀었을 때, 나는 그의 두 눈에 눈물이 가득 고여 있는 것을 보고 놀랐다.\n\n이때까지 나는 그가 가 주기를 바란다는 것을 굳이 감추지 않으려 선 채로 있었다. 그러나 사내의 누그러진 모습에 나도 마음이 누그러졌고, 한 가닥 자책감을 느꼈다. \"바라건대,\" 나는 서둘러 내 잔에도 뭔가를 따르고, 의자를 탁자 쪽으로 끌어당기며 말했다. \"방금 내가 당신에게 모질게 말했다고는 생각하지 말아 주세요. 그럴 뜻은 없었고, 만일 그랬다면 미안합니다. 당신이 잘되고 행복하기를 빕니다!\"\n\n내가 잔을 입술에 가져가자, 사내는 입을 열 때 입에서 떨어지는 목수건 끝자락을 놀란 듯 흘끔 보더니, 손을 내밀었다. 나는 그에게 손을 내주었고, 그러자 그는 마신 뒤 소매로 눈과 이마를 훔쳤다.\n\n\"어떻게 지내십니까?\" 나는 그에게 물었다.\n\n\"양 치는 농장도 하고, 가축도 길러 봤고, 그 밖에 다른 장사도 했지. 저 멀리 신세계에서 말이야.\" 그가 말했다. \"여기서부터 사나운 바닷물로 수천 마일 떨어진 데서.\"\n\n\"잘 풀리셨기를 바랍니다.\"\n\n\"놀랄 만큼 잘 풀렸지. 나하고 같이 나간 다른 자들도 잘된 이가 있긴 하지만, 나만큼 잘된 사람은 아무도 없어. 그걸로 내가 유명하다네.\"\n\n\"그 말씀을 들으니 기쁩니다.\"\n\n\"자네 입에서 그 말을 듣고 싶었네, 얘야.\"\n\n그 말이나 그 말을 하는 어조를 굳이 멈춰 서서 헤아려 보지 않고, 나는 막 머릿속에 떠오른 한 가지로 화제를 돌렸다.\n\n\"전에 당신이 내게 보냈던 심부름꾼을,\" 나는 물었다. \"그가 그 일을 맡은 뒤로 만난 적이 있습니까?\"\n\n\"그자를 다시는 본 적 없네. 그럴 일도 없었고.\"\n\n\"그는 충실하게 와서, 1파운드짜리 지폐 두 장을 내게 전해 주었습니다. 당신도 알다시피 그때 나는 가난한 아이였고, 가난한 아이에게 그건 작은 횡재였지요. 하지만 당신처럼 나도 그 뒤로 형편이 폈으니, 그 돈은 돌려드리게 해 주세요. 다른 어느 가난한 아이를 위해 쓰시면 될 겁니다.\" 나는 지갑을 꺼냈다.\n\n사내는 내가 지갑을 탁자에 올려놓고 여는 것을 지켜보았고, 내가 그 속에서 1파운드짜리 지폐 두 장을 골라내는 것도 지켜보았다. 깨끗한 새 지폐였고, 나는 그것을 펴서 그에게 건넸다. 여전히 나를 지켜보던 그는, 그것을 한 장 위에 다른 한 장을 포개어 길게 접고는, 한 번 비틀어, 등불에 불을 붙여, 재를 쟁반 위에 떨어뜨렸다.\n\n\"한 가지 외람되게 물어봐도 되겠나,\" 그러더니 그는, 찡그림 같은 미소와 미소 같은 찡그림을 띤 채 말했다. \"자네와 내가 그 외롭고 쓸쓸한 습지에 나가 있던 이후로, 자네가 대체 어떻게 형편이 폈는지 말일세.\"\n\"어떻게라니요?\"\n\n\"어떻게라니요?\" \"아!\"\n\n사내는 잔을 비우고 일어나, 거뭇하고 묵직한 손을 벽난로 선반에 얹은 채 난롯가에 섰다. 한쪽 발을 말리고 데우려고 쇠살대에 올려놓자, 젖은 장화에서 김이 나기 시작했다. 그러나 그는 장화도 난롯불도 보지 않고, 줄곧 나를 바라보았다. 내가 떨기 시작한 것은 바로 그제야였다.\n\n입술이 벌어져 소리 없는 몇 마디 말을 빚어냈을 때, 나는 (또렷하게는 못 했지만) 억지로 그에게 말했다. 내가 어떤 재산을 물려받을 사람으로 정해졌다고.\n\n\"하찮은 미물이 어떤 재산인지 물어봐도 되나?\" 그가 말했다.\n\n나는 더듬거렸다. \"모릅니다.\"\n\n\"하찮은 미물이 누구 재산인지 물어봐도 되나?\" 그가 말했다.\n\n나는 다시 더듬거렸다. \"모릅니다.\"\n\n\"내가 한번 알아맞혀 볼까,\" 죄수가 말했다. \"자네가 성년이 된 뒤 받은 수입을 말이야! 자, 우선 첫 숫자라고 치고. 5인가?\"\n\n심장이 마구 어지러이 뛰는 묵직한 망치처럼 두근거리는 가운데, 나는 의자에서 일어나, 등받이에 한 손을 얹은 채, 그를 멍하니 바라보며 섰다.\n\n\"후견인에 관해서 말인데,\" 그가 말을 이었다. \"자네가 미성년이던 동안엔 후견인 같은 게, 뭐 그런 누가 있었을 게 분명해. 변호사일지도 모르지. 자, 그 변호사 이름 첫 글자라고 치고. 그게 혹시 J인가?\"\n\n내 처지의 진실 전부가 번개처럼 내게 들이쳤다. 그리고 그 모든 종류의 실망, 위험, 치욕, 결과들이 어찌나 떼 지어 밀려들었던지, 나는 그 무게에 짓눌려 숨 한 번 쉴 때마다 안간힘을 써야 했다.\n\n\"이렇게 쳐 보세,\" 그가 다시 말을 이었다. \"이름이 J로 시작하고, 어쩌면 재거스일지도 모를, 그 변호사를 부리는 고용주가 말이야 — 그자가 바다를 건너 포츠머스로 와서, 거기 상륙해서, 자네한테로 오고 싶어 했다고 쳐 보세. '당신이 어떻게 나를 찾아냈든' 하고 자네가 방금 말했지. 그런데 말이야! 내가 어떻게 자네를 찾아냈을까? 글쎄, 나는 포츠머스에서 런던에 있는 어떤 사람한테 편지를 써서 자네 주소를 자세히 알아냈다네. 그 사람 이름이 뭐냐고? 글쎄, 웨믹이지.\"\n\n목숨을 구하기 위해서라 해도 나는 한마디도 할 수 없었을 것이다. 나는 한 손을 의자 등받이에, 다른 한 손을 숨이 막히는 듯한 가슴에 얹고 서 있었다. 그렇게 서서, 의자를 와락 붙들 때까지 그를 멍하니 바라보았고, 그러자 방이 출렁이며 빙빙 돌기 시작했다. 사내는 나를 붙잡아 소파로 끌어다, 쿠션에 기대 앉히고는, 내 앞에 한쪽 무릎을 꿇고서, 이제 똑똑히 기억나는, 그래서 내가 몸서리치게 되는 그 얼굴을 내 얼굴 아주 가까이 들이댔다.\n\n\"그래, 핍, 얘야, 내가 자넬 신사로 만들었어! 그걸 해낸 게 바로 날세! 그때 나는 맹세했지, 내가 1기니를 벌어들이는 것만큼이나 틀림없이, 그 1기니는 자네한테 가게 하겠다고. 그 뒤에도 맹세했네, 내가 투기를 해서 부자가 되는 것만큼이나 틀림없이, 자네도 부자가 되게 하겠다고. 나는 거칠게 살았어, 자네가 편하게 살게 하려고. 나는 고되게 일했지, 자네가 일 따위 안 해도 되게 하려고. 그게 무슨 대수겠나, 얘야? 내가 이걸 자네한테 빚진 마음을 느끼게 하려고 말하는 줄 아나? 천만에. 내가 이걸 말하는 건, 자네가 목숨을 부지시켜 준 그 쫓기던 똥개가, 신사 하나 만들 수 있을 만큼 고개를 높이 쳐들었다는 걸 알라는 거야 — 그리고, 핍, 그 신사가 바로 자넬세!\"\n\n내가 그 사내에게 품은 혐오, 그에게 느낀 두려움, 그에게서 움찔 물러나게 하던 거부감은, 설령 그가 어떤 무시무시한 짐승이었다 한들 그보다 더하지는 못했을 것이다.\n\n\"이걸 보게, 핍. 나는 자네의 두 번째 아비일세. 자네는 내 아들이야 — 그 어떤 아들보다도 내게 더한 아들이지. 나는 돈을 모아 뒀어, 오직 자네가 쓰라고. 외딴 오두막에서 품팔이 양치기 노릇을 할 때, 양들 얼굴 말고는 사람 얼굴이라곤 보질 못해서 남자고 여자고 얼굴이 어떻게 생겼는지 반쯤 잊어버릴 지경이었을 때도, 나는 자네 얼굴을 봤네. 그 오두막에서 점심이나 저녁을 먹다가 나는 몇 번이고 칼을 떨어뜨리며 말했지. '여기 또 그 애가 있구나, 내가 먹고 마시는 걸 지켜보면서!' 안개 낀 그 습지에서 자넬 보던 것만큼이나 또렷하게, 나는 거기서 자네를 몇 번이고 봤네. '하느님, 차라리 날 쳐 죽이시오!' 하고 매번 나는 말했어 — 그리고 그 말을 탁 트인 하늘 아래서 하려고 바깥바람 속으로 나가곤 했지 — '하지만 내가 자유와 돈을 얻게 되면, 저 애를 신사로 만들어 주고 말겠소!' 그리고 나는 그걸 해냈어. 자, 자네 좀 보게, 얘야! 영주에게나 어울릴 자네의 이 거처를 좀 보게! 영주라고? 아! 자넨 영주들과 돈을 걸고 겨뤄서 이길 수도 있을 게야!\"\n\n자기 열기와 승리감에 휩싸인 데다, 내가 거의 기절할 뻔했다는 것을 안 까닭에, 그는 이 모든 말을 받아들이는 내 태도에 대해서는 토를 달지 않았다. 그것이 내게는 한 톨의 위안이었다.\n\n\"이걸 보게!\" 그가 말을 이으며, 내 주머니에서 시계를 꺼내고, 내 손가락의 반지를 자기 쪽으로 돌려 보았는데, 그동안 나는 마치 그가 뱀이기라도 한 듯 그의 손길에서 움찔 물러났다. \"금붙이에다 일품이로구먼. 이게 바로 신사의 것이렷다! 둘레를 온통 루비로 박은 다이아몬드라. 이게 바로 신사의 것이렷다! 자네 속옷을 보게. 곱고 아름답지! 자네 옷을 보게. 이보다 더 좋은 건 구할 수도 없을 게야! 그리고 자네 책들도,\" 그가 방 안을 빙 둘러보며 말했다. \"선반에 수백 권씩 쌓여 올라가는구먼! 그리고 자넨 그걸 읽지, 안 그런가? 내가 들어왔을 때 자네가 그걸 읽고 있던 걸 봤네. 하, 하, 하! 자네가 그걸 내게 읽어 주게, 얘야! 그리고 그게 내가 알지 못하는 외국 말로 쓰인 거라 해도, 나는 알아듣는 것만큼이나 자랑스러울 게야.\"\n\n또다시 그는 내 두 손을 잡아 입술에 갖다 댔고, 그동안 내 핏줄 속 피는 싸늘하게 식어 갔다.\n\n\"말하려고 애쓰지 말게, 핍.\" 그는 다시 소매로 눈과 이마를 훔치고 나서, 내가 똑똑히 기억하는 그 딸깍하는 소리가 목구멍에서 울리는 가운데 말했다 — 그가 그토록 진심이라는 점이 내게는 그를 한층 더 끔찍하게 만들었다. \"가만히 있는 것보다 더 나은 건 없어, 얘야. 자네는 나처럼 이걸 천천히 고대해 온 게 아니지. 나처럼 이걸 마음의 준비를 해 둔 게 아니야. 하지만 그게 나일지도 모른다고는 한 번도 생각 못 했나?\"\n\n\"아, 아니요, 아니요, 아니요.\" 나는 대답했다. \"단 한 번도, 단 한 번도요!\"\n\n\"음, 보다시피 그게 나였어, 나 혼자서 한 일이지. 나 자신하고 재거스 씨 말고는 거기 낀 사람이 아무도 없었네.\"\n\n\"다른 사람은 아무도 없었습니까?\" 내가 물었다.\n\n\"없네.\" 그가 놀란 눈빛으로 말했다. \"달리 누가 또 있겠나? 그런데, 얘야, 자네 참 잘생기게 자랐구먼! 어디 빛나는 두 눈이 있겠지 — 응? 어디 자네가 떠올리기를 사랑하는, 그런 빛나는 두 눈이 있지 않나?\"\n\n오, 에스텔라, 에스텔라!\n\n\"그 두 눈도 자네 것이 될 걸세, 얘야, 돈으로 살 수만 있다면 말이야. 자네같이 그렇게 잘 차려입은 신사가 제 솜씨로 그 눈을 손에 넣지 못하리라는 게 아니라, 그래도 돈이 자넬 뒷받침해 줄 거란 거지! 내가 자네한테 들려주던 얘기를 마저 끝내게 해 주게, 얘야. 그 오두막과 그 품팔이살이에서, 나는 주인이 — 그 사람도 죽었는데, 나하고 똑같은 처지였지 — 남겨 준 돈을 받았고, 자유의 몸이 되어 내 사업을 시작했네. 내가 손댄 모든 일을, 단 하나하나, 다 자네를 위해서 했어. '하느님, 이 일에 천벌을 내리시오.' 하고 나는 내가 손대는 게 뭐든 간에 그렇게 말했지, '이게 저 애를 위한 게 아니라면!' 모든 게 놀랄 만큼 잘 풀렸네. 방금 자네한테 알려 줬다시피, 그걸로 내가 유명하지. 내가 처음 몇 해 동안 자네를 위해 — 죄다 자네를 위해 — 재거스 씨한테 부쳐 보낸 게 바로 그 물려받은 돈과 그 벌어들인 이문이었어, 그가 내 편지에 따라 자네를 처음 찾아갔을 때 말일세.\"\n\n오, 그가 차라리 오지 않았더라면! 그가 나를 그 대장간에 — 결코 만족스럽지는 않았으나, 그래도 비교하자면 행복했던 그곳에 — 그냥 내버려 두었더라면!\n\n\"그러고 나서, 얘야, 내겐 보답이었네, 이걸 보게, 내가 신사 하나를 만들어 내고 있다는 걸 남몰래 안다는 것이 말이야. 내가 길을 걸을 때 그 식민지 사람들의 명마들이 먼지를 일으켜 나한테 끼얹어도, 내가 뭐라고 했겠나? 나는 속으로 말했지. '나는 자네들이 앞으로 될 그 어떤 신사보다도 더 나은 신사를 만들고 있어!' 그자들 중 하나가 다른 자한테 '저 사람은 몇 해 전엔 죄수였고, 운은 좀 텄어도 지금도 무식한 막돼먹은 놈이야.' 하고 말할 때, 내가 뭐라고 했겠나? 나는 속으로 말했어. '내가 신사가 아니라 한들, 또 배움이 없다 한들, 나는 그런 걸 가진 사람의 주인일세. 자네들은 죄다 가축이며 땅을 가졌지. 그중에 누가 런던에서 길러 낸 신사를 가졌나?' 이렇게 나는 나 자신을 버티게 했네. 그리고 이렇게 나는 마음속에 굳게 다잡았지, 언젠가 반드시 와서 내 아이를 보고, 그 아이가 사는 그곳에서 나를 그 아이에게 알리겠다고 말이야.\"\n\n그가 내 어깨에 손을 얹었다. 나는 내가 알지 못하는 어떤 사정으로든 그 손이 피로 물들어 있을지도 모른다는 생각에 몸서리쳤다.\n\n\"내가 그 고장을 떠나기란 쉽지 않았네, 핍, 그렇다고 안전한 일도 아니었지. 하지만 나는 그 결심을 고수했고, 그게 어려우면 어려울수록 더 단단히 붙들었어. 나는 작정했고, 마음을 굳게 정했으니까. 마침내 나는 해냈네. 얘야, 내가 해냈어!\"\n\n나는 생각을 추스르려 애썼지만, 멍해 있었다. 처음부터 줄곧, 나는 그보다는 비바람에 더 귀를 기울이고 있는 것만 같았다. 지금조차도, 그 목소리들은 요란하고 그의 목소리는 잠잠했건만, 나는 그의 목소리를 그 목소리들에서 떼어 낼 수가 없었다.\n\n\"날 어디 재워 줄 텐가?\" 이윽고 그가 물었다. \"어디든 날 재워 줘야지, 얘야.\"\n\n\"주무시게요?\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"그래. 길게 푹 자야지.\" 그가 대답했다. \"여러 달, 여러 달을 바다에서 시달리고 바닷물에 씻겼으니 말일세.\"\n\n\"제 친구이자 동거인은,\" 나는 소파에서 일어나며 말했다. \"지금 자리에 없습니다. 그 사람 방을 쓰시면 됩니다.\"\n\n\"그 사람, 내일은 안 돌아오겠지?\"\n\n\"네.\" 나는 안간힘을 다했음에도, 거의 기계적으로 대답했다. \"내일은요.\"\n\n\"왜냐하면, 이걸 보게, 얘야,\" 그가 목소리를 낮추고, 긴 손가락을 인상적으로 내 가슴에 갖다 대며 말했다. \"조심이 필요하거든.\"\n\n\"무슨 뜻이야? 조심하라고?\"\n\n\"맙소사, 죽음이잖아!\" \"무엇이 죽음이라는 겁니까?\"\n\n\"나는 종신형으로 보내졌어. 돌아오는 건 곧 죽음이지. 요 몇 해 사이에 돌아오는 일이 너무 많았던 통에, 만약 잡히면 나는 영락없이 교수형이야.\"\n\n이것 말고는 더 필요한 게 없었다. 그 가엾은 사내는, 여러 해 동안 가엾은 나에게 황금과 은의 사슬을 짊어지운 끝에, 목숨을 걸고 내게로 왔고, 나는 그 목숨을 내 손안에 쥐고 있었던 것이다! 만일 내가 그를 혐오하는 대신 사랑했더라면, 만일 더없이 강한 거부감으로 그에게서 움찔 물러나는 대신 더없이 강한 흠모와 애정으로 그에게 끌렸더라면, 사정이 이보다 더 나빠질 수는 없었을 것이다. 오히려 그편이 나았을 테니, 그렇다면 그를 지켜 내는 일이 자연스럽고도 다정하게 내 마음에 호소했을 것이기 때문이다.\n\n내가 가장 먼저 마음 쓴 일은, 밖에서 불빛이 새어 나가지 않도록 덧문을 닫는 것이었고, 그다음으로는 문들을 닫아 단단히 잠그는 것이었다. 그러는 동안 그는 탁자 앞에 서서 럼주를 마시고 비스킷을 먹고 있었다. 그가 그러고 있는 모습을 보자, 나는 습지에서 끼니를 먹던 내 죄수를 다시 보는 듯했다. 그가 이내 몸을 숙여 제 다리에 채운 쇠고랑을 줄칼로 갈기 시작하지나 않을까 싶을 지경이었다.\n\n내가 허버트의 방으로 들어가, 그 방과 계단 사이에 우리가 이야기를 나눈 방을 거치는 것 말고는 다른 통로를 죄다 막아 버린 뒤, 나는 그에게 잠자리에 들겠느냐고 물었다. 그는 그러겠다고 했지만, 아침에 입을 내 \"신사용 속옷\"을 좀 달라고 청했다. 나는 그것을 꺼내 그가 쓰도록 챙겨 두었고, 그가 잘 자라는 인사를 하려고 또다시 내 두 손을 맞잡았을 때, 내 핏줄 속 피는 다시금 싸늘하게 식었다.\n\n나는 어떻게 그렇게 했는지도 모르는 채 그에게서 빠져나와, 우리가 함께 있던 방의 난롯불을 돋우고는, 잠자리에 들기가 두려워 그 곁에 앉았다. 한 시간 남짓 동안, 나는 너무 얼이 빠져 아무 생각도 할 수 없었다. 그리고 비로소 생각을 하기 시작하고 나서야, 내가 얼마나 파선했는지를, 내가 타고 항해하던 배가 얼마나 산산조각 났는지를 온전히 알게 되었다.\n\n나를 향한 해비셤 양의 의도는 죄다 한낱 꿈에 지나지 않았고, 에스텔라는 나를 위해 점지된 사람이 아니었으며, 나는 그저 하나의 편의로서 새티스 하우스에서 시달림을 당했던 것이다. 탐욕스러운 친척들을 찌르는 가시로서, 그리고 달리 연습할 상대가 없을 때 그 기계 같은 심장을 상대로 연습할 본보기로서 말이다. 그것이 내가 맨 먼저 느낀 쓰라림이었다. 그러나 그 모든 것 중에서도 가장 날카롭고 깊은 고통은, 내가 알지 못하는 죄를 저질렀고 내가 앉아 생각에 잠겨 있는 이 방에서 끌려 나가 올드베일리 문간에서 교수형을 당할지도 모를 그 죄수를 위하여, 내가 조를 저버렸다는 것이었다.\n\n지금은 그 무엇을 고려한다 해도 조에게로 돌아가지 않을 것이며, 비디에게로도 돌아가지 않을 것이었다. 짐작건대 그것은 단지, 그들에게 내가 취한 그 보잘것없는 처신에 대한 자각이 그 어떤 고려보다도 컸기 때문이리라. 그들의 꾸밈없음과 한결같음에서 내가 얻었을 위안을, 세상의 그 어떤 지혜로도 내게 줄 수는 없었을 것이다. 그러나 나는 내가 저지른 일을 결코, 결코 돌이킬 수 없었다.\n\n바람이 미쳐 날뛰고 비가 쏟아질 때마다, 나는 추격자들의 소리를 들었다. 두 번이나, 나는 바깥문을 두드리고 속닥거리는 소리가 있었다고 맹세할 수도 있었다. 이런 두려움에 사로잡힌 채, 나는 이 사내가 다가오고 있다는 신비한 경고를 받았었다고 상상하거나 떠올리기 시작했다. 지난 몇 주 동안 거리에서 그와 닮았다 싶은 얼굴들을 스쳐 지나왔다고. 그리고 그가 바다를 건너오며 점점 가까워짐에 따라, 그 닮은 얼굴들이 점점 더 많아졌다고. 그의 사악한 영혼이 어떻게든 이 전령들을 내 영혼에게로 보냈고, 이제 이 폭풍우 치는 밤에 그가 그 약속대로 나와 함께 있게 되었다고 말이다.\n\n이런 상념들과 함께 떼 지어 밀려든 것은, 내가 어린아이의 눈으로 그를 무섭도록 난폭한 사내로 보았다는 상념이었다. 그 다른 죄수가 그가 자기를 죽이려 했다고 거듭 말하는 것을 들었다는 것, 그리고 그가 도랑에 처박혀 야수처럼 물어뜯고 싸우는 것을 보았다는 것이었다. 그런 기억들로부터 나는, 이 거칠고 외로운 밤이 깊었을 때 그와 함께 여기 갇혀 있는 것이 안전하지 않을지도 모른다는 어렴풋한 공포를 난롯불 빛 속으로 끄집어냈다. 이 공포는 점점 부풀어 방 안을 가득 채웠고, 마침내 나는 촛불을 들고 들어가 내 끔찍한 짐을 들여다보지 않을 수 없게 되었다.\n\n그는 손수건을 머리에 둘둘 둘렀고, 잠든 그의 얼굴은 굳어 있으며 험상궂게 찌푸려져 있었다. 그러나 그는 잠들어 있었고, 베개 위에 권총을 한 자루 올려놓고 있긴 했어도, 조용히 잠들어 있었다. 그것을 확인한 나는, 그의 방문 열쇠를 가만히 바깥쪽으로 옮겨, 그를 가둔 채 잠그고 나서야 다시 난롯가에 앉았다. 차츰 나는 의자에서 미끄러져 내려와 바닥에 누웠다. 잠을 자면서도 내 비참함에 대한 자각을 놓아 버리지 못한 채 깨어났을 때, 동쪽 교회들의 시계는 다섯 시를 치고 있었고, 촛불은 다 타 버렸으며, 난롯불은 꺼졌고, 비바람은 짙은 검은 어둠을 한층 더 깊게 했다.\n이로써 핍의 유산 이야기 제2부가 끝난다.\n\n# Chapter XL.\n\n무서운 방문객의 안전을(내가 할 수 있는 한) 확실히 지키기 위해 미리 손을 써 두어야 했던 것은, 내게는 다행스러운 일이었다. 잠에서 깨었을 때 그 생각이 나를 짓눌렀으므로, 다른 온갖 생각들은 어수선한 무리를 이룬 채 멀찍이 물러나 있었기 때문이다.\n\n그를 거처에 숨겨 둔다는 것이 불가능하다는 사실은 자명했다. 그건 될 일이 아니었고, 그렇게 하려 시도하다가는 필연코 의심을 사게 마련이었다. 사실 지금 내게는 어벤저라는 하인이 없었지만, 나는 걸핏하면 흥분하는 늙은 여자에게 시중을 받고 있었고, 그 여자는 자기 조카라고 부르는 살아 움직이는 넝마 자루 같은 여자의 도움을 받았다. 그들에게 방 하나를 비밀로 한다는 것은 곧 호기심과 과장을 불러들이는 짓이었다. 두 사람 다 눈이 침침했는데, 나는 오래전부터 그것이 노상 열쇠 구멍을 들여다보는 버릇 탓이라고 여겨 왔다. 또 그들은 필요 없을 때면 어김없이 곁에 있었으니, 사실 그것이야말로 도둑질을 빼면 그들에게서 믿을 수 있는 유일한 자질이었다. 이런 사람들과 수수께끼를 꾸미는 일을 만들지 않으려고, 나는 아침에 시골에서 삼촌이 뜻밖에 찾아왔노라고 알리기로 마음먹었다.\n\n이 방침을 정한 것은, 어둠 속에서 불을 밝힐 방도를 더듬어 찾고 있을 때였다. 끝내 그 방도를 찾아내지 못한 나는, 부득이 가까운 수위실로 나가 그곳 야경꾼에게 등불을 들고 와 달라고 청할 수밖에 없었다. 그런데 캄캄한 계단을 더듬어 내려가다가 나는 무언가에 걸려 넘어졌고, 그 무언가는 한쪽 구석에 웅크리고 있던 사내였다.\n\n그곳에서 뭘 하느냐고 물었으나 사내는 아무 대답도 하지 않고 말없이 내 손길을 피하기에, 나는 수위실로 달려가 야경꾼에게 어서 와 달라고 재촉했다. 돌아오는 길에 나는 그에게 그 일을 이야기해 주었다. 바람이 여전히 사납게 불었으므로, 우리는 계단에서 꺼진 등불을 다시 켜다가 등불의 불을 위태롭게 할 마음은 들지 않았고, 그저 계단을 아래에서 위까지 살펴보았으나 거기에는 아무도 없었다. 그때 문득 그 사내가 내 방으로 몰래 숨어들었을지도 모른다는 생각이 들었다. 그래서 나는 야경꾼의 등불로 내 초에 불을 붙이고, 그를 문간에 세워 둔 채, 내 무서운 손님이 잠들어 있는 방까지 포함하여 방들을 꼼꼼히 살폈다. 모든 것이 조용했고, 그 거처 안에 다른 사람은 분명 아무도 없었다.\n\n한 해 중에서도 하필이면 그 밤에 계단에 숨어 있던 자가 있었다는 사실이 나를 불안하게 했다. 그래서 나는 문간에서 야경꾼에게 술 한 잔을 건네면서, 혹시 무언가 마음을 놓이게 할 만한 설명을 끌어낼 수 있을까 싶어, 밖에서 만찬을 들고 온 티가 뚜렷이 나는 신사를 그가 문으로 들여보낸 적이 있느냐고 물었다. 그렇다고, 그가 말했다. 밤중에 여러 차례, 세 사람이 있었다고 했다. 한 사람은 파운틴 코트에 살았고, 다른 두 사람은 레인에 살았으며, 그는 그들이 모두 집으로 들어가는 것을 보았다고 했다. 또한 내 거처가 속한 그 집에 사는 다른 단 한 사람은 몇 주째 시골에 가 있었고, 그가 밤중에 돌아오지 않은 것은 분명했으니, 우리가 계단을 올라오면서 그의 봉인이 찍힌 문을 보았기 때문이다.\n\n\"밤이 워낙 사나워서요, 나리.\" 야경꾼이 내 잔을 돌려주며 말했다. \"유난히 적은 사람만 제 문으로 들어왔습니다. 제가 말씀드린 그 세 신사 말고는, 열한 시쯤 어떤 낯선 사람이 나리를 찾았던 것 말고는 달리 떠오르는 이가 없습니다.\"\n\"내 삼촌이오.\" 나는 중얼거렸다. \"그렇소.\"\n\n\"그분을 보셨습니까, 나리?\"\n\n\"보았소. 아, 그럼.\"\n\n\"같이 온 사람도요?\"\n\n\"같이 온 사람이라니!\" 나는 되뇌었다.\n\n\"제가 보기엔 그 사람이 함께 온 것 같았습니다.\" 야경꾼이 대답했다. \"그 사람은 그분이 멈춰 서서 제게 묻는 동안 함께 멈춰 섰고, 그분이 이쪽 길로 가니 그 사람도 이쪽 길로 갔거든요.\"\n\n\"어떤 사람이던가?\"\n\n야경꾼은 딱히 눈여겨보지는 않았다고 했다. 일하는 사람 같았다고나 할까, 그가 보기로는 짙은 외투 안에 먼지 빛깔의 옷 같은 것을 걸치고 있었다고 했다. 야경꾼은 나보다 이 일을 훨씬 가볍게 여겼는데, 그도 그럴 것이 거기에 무게를 둘 까닭이 내게는 있었으나 그에게는 없었으니, 당연한 일이었다.\n\n설명을 길게 끌지 않고 그를 떼어 보내는 편이 좋겠다 싶어 그렇게 한 뒤, 이 두 가지 정황을 한데 묶어 생각하노라니 내 마음은 몹시 어지러웠다. 따로 떼어 놓고 보면 죄 없는 해명이 쉽사리 가능한 것이었으니 — 이를테면, 야경꾼의 문 근처에는 얼씬도 하지 않은 채 밖에서든 집에서든 만찬을 든 누군가가 내 계단으로 잘못 들어와 거기서 잠들었을 수도 있고, 이름 모를 내 방문객이 길을 안내하라고 누군가를 데려왔을 수도 있었다 — 그래도 그 둘을 한데 합쳐 놓으면, 불과 몇 시간 사이의 변화로 의심과 두려움에 빠지기 쉬워진 나 같은 자에게는 흉한 낌새로 보였다.\n\n나는 불을 지폈는데, 그 아침 시각에 불은 설익은 듯 희끄무레한 불꽃을 일으키며 타올랐고, 나는 그 앞에서 꾸벅꾸벅 졸기 시작했다. 시계가 여섯 시를 칠 때, 나는 마치 밤새도록 졸았던 것 같았다. 날이 밝으려면 아직 한 시간 반은 족히 남아 있었으므로, 나는 다시 졸았다. 어느 때는 아무것도 아닌 일에 관한 장황한 대화가 귓가에 맴돌며 불안하게 잠을 깼고, 어느 때는 굴뚝을 지나는 바람 소리를 천둥소리로 만들어 냈으며, 마침내 깊은 잠에 빠져들었다가 날이 밝아 오는 빛에 화들짝 놀라 깨어났다.\n\n이 모든 시간 동안 나는 내 처지를 도무지 헤아릴 수 없었고, 지금도 여전히 그러지 못했다. 거기에 마음을 쏟을 기력이 내게는 없었다. 나는 몹시 풀이 죽고 괴로웠으나, 그것은 갈피를 잡을 수 없이 막연하고 두루뭉술한 괴로움이었다. 앞날에 대한 계획을 세운다는 것은, 코끼리 한 마리를 만들어 내는 일이나 다름없었다. 덧문을 열고 온통 납빛으로 물든 축축하고 거친 아침을 내다볼 때, 이 방 저 방을 거닐 때, 빨래하는 여자가 나타나기를 기다리며 불 앞에서 다시 부들부들 떨며 앉아 있을 때, 나는 내가 얼마나 비참한지를 생각했으나, 왜 그러한지, 언제부터 그러했는지, 무슨 요일에 그런 생각을 하고 있는지, 심지어 그런 생각을 하는 내가 누구인지조차 거의 알지 못했다.\n\n마침내 늙은 여자와 그 조카가 들어왔는데 — 조카라는 여자는 머리가 자기 먼지투성이 빗자루와 좀처럼 구별되지 않았다 — 두 사람은 나와 불을 보고는 놀라는 기색을 보였다. 나는 그들에게 삼촌이 밤중에 와서 지금 잠들어 있으며, 그에 맞춰 아침 식사 준비를 달리해야 한다고 일러 주었다. 그러고 나서 그들이 가구를 이리저리 두드리며 먼지를 일으키는 동안, 나는 씻고 옷을 입었다. 그리하여 일종의 꿈속에서, 혹은 잠과 깨어남 사이에서, 나는 다시 불 앞에 앉아 그가, 그분이, 아침을 들러 나오기를 기다리는 나 자신을 발견했다.\n\n이윽고 그의 방문이 열리며 그가 나왔다. 나는 도저히 그를 바로 볼 수가 없었고, 그가 환한 대낮에는 한층 더 험상궂게 보인다고 생각했다.\n\n\"나는 당신을 무슨 이름으로 불러야 할지조차 모르겠습니다.\" 나는 그가 식탁에 자리를 잡을 때 나직이 말했다. \"나는 당신이 내 삼촌이라고 둘러대 두었습니다.\"\n\n\"바로 그거다, 얘야! 나를 삼촌이라고 불러라.\"\n\n\"배에서는 무슨 이름을 쓰셨겠지요?\"\n\n\"그래, 얘야. 나는 프로비스라는 이름을 썼다.\"\n\n\"그 이름을 계속 쓰실 작정인가요?\"\n\n\"왜, 그래, 얘야, 다른 이름이나 매한가지니까 — 네가 다른 이름을 바라지 않는다면 말이다.\"\n\n\"진짜 이름은 무엇입니까?\" 나는 속삭이듯 그에게 물었다.\n\n\"매그위치다.\" 그가 같은 어조로 대답했다. \"세례명은 에이벌이고.\"\n\n\"무슨 일을 하도록 길러지셨나요?\"\n\n\"해충이지, 얘야.\"\n\n그는 자못 진지하게 대답했고, 마치 그것이 무슨 직업의 이름이기라도 한 듯 그 말을 썼다.\n\n\"어젯밤 당신이 템플에 들어왔을 때 — \" 나는 그것이 정말로 어젯밤 일이었던가 의아해하며 말을 멈추었으니, 그것이 까마득히 오래전 일처럼 여겨졌던 것이다.\n\n\"그래, 얘야?\"\n\n\"당신이 문으로 들어와 야경꾼에게 여기 오는 길을 물었을 때, 누군가 같이 있었습니까?\"\n\n\"나랑? 아니다, 얘야.\"\n\n\"하지만 거기 누군가 있었습니다.\"\n\n\"딱히 눈여겨보지는 않았다만.\" 그가 미심쩍은 듯 말했다. \"여기 사정을 몰라서 말이다. 하지만 나하고 같이 들어온 사람이 하나 있긴 했던 것 같다.\"\n\n\"런던에 당신을 아는 사람이 있습니까?\"\n\n\"없기를 바라야지!\" 그가 집게손가락으로 제 목을 홱 그어 보이며 말했는데, 그 바람에 나는 얼굴이 화끈 달아오르며 속이 메스꺼워졌다.\n\n\"예전에 런던에 당신을 아는 사람이 있었나요?\"\n\n\"그리 많지는 않았다, 얘야. 나는 주로 지방에 있었으니까.\"\n\n\"런던에서 — 재판을 받으셨나요?\"\n\n\"어느 때 말이냐?\" 그가 날카로운 눈초리로 물었다.\n\n\"마지막 때요.\"\n\n그가 고개를 끄덕였다. \"그렇게 해서 처음 재거스 씨를 알게 됐다. 재거스가 내 변호를 맡았지.\"\n\n그가 무슨 죄로 재판을 받았느냐고 물으려는 말이 입가에 맴돌았으나, 그는 칼을 집어 들고 한 번 휘두르더니, \"그리고 내가 저지른 짓은 다 치르고 갚았다!\"라는 말과 함께 아침 식사에 달려들었다.\n\n그는 몹시 게걸스럽게 먹었는데 그 꼴이 여간 거북한 게 아니었고, 그의 모든 동작은 거칠고 시끄럽고 탐욕스러웠다. 내가 습지에서 그가 먹는 것을 본 이후로 이가 몇 개 빠진 듯, 음식을 입안에서 굴리며 가장 튼튼한 어금니로 씹으려고 고개를 옆으로 돌리는 모습은 흡사 굶주린 늙은 개와도 같아 끔찍하기 짝이 없었다. 내게 처음에 어떤 식욕이 있었더라도 그가 그것을 가셔 놓았을 것이고, 나는 지금처럼 — 도저히 떨칠 수 없는 혐오에 그에게서 밀려난 채 침울하게 식탁보만 바라보며 — 앉아 있었을 것이다.\n\n\"나는 대식가다, 얘야.\" 그가 식사를 마치면서 일종의 정중한 사과처럼 말했다. \"하지만 늘 그랬다. 좀 적게 먹는 체질로 타고났더라면, 어쩌면 좀 가벼운 곤경에 빠졌을지도 모르지. 마찬가지로, 나는 담배를 피워야 한다. 처음 세상 저편에서 양치기로 고용되었을 때, 담배가 없었더라면 나 자신이 우울증에 미친 양으로 변해 버렸을 거라고 나는 믿는다.\"\n\n그렇게 말하면서 그는 식탁에서 일어나, 입고 있던 두꺼운 모직 외투의 가슴께에 손을 넣어 짤막한 검은 파이프 하나와, 흑인 머리라 불리는 종류의 부스러기 담배 한 줌을 꺼냈다. 파이프를 채우고 나서 그는 남은 담배를 다시 도로 집어넣었는데, 마치 자기 주머니가 서랍이라도 되는 양했다. 그러고는 부젓가락으로 불에서 이글거리는 숯 한 덩이를 집어 파이프에 불을 붙이고, 벽난로 앞 깔개 위에서 불을 등진 채 몸을 돌려, 두 손을 내밀어 내 손을 잡으려는 그 좋아하는 동작을 되풀이했다.\n\n\"그리고 이게,\" 그가 파이프를 뻐끔거리며 제 두 손으로 내 손을 위아래로 어르듯 흔들면서 말했다. \"그리고 이게 내가 만든 신사다! 진짜배기 신사 말이다! 너를 보고 있으면 마음이 흐뭇해진다, 핍. 내가 바라는 건 오직, 곁에 서서 너를 바라보는 것뿐이야, 얘야!\"\n\n나는 되도록 빨리 손을 빼냈고, 내가 천천히 내 처지를 곰곰이 따져 보는 마음 상태로 가라앉기 시작했음을 깨달았다. 내가 무엇에, 그리고 얼마나 무겁게 매여 있는지가, 그의 쉰 목소리를 듣고 깊은 주름이 새겨진 그의 대머리와 양옆의 무쇠 같은 잿빛 머리칼을 올려다보며 앉아 있노라니, 차츰 또렷하게 다가왔다.\n\n\"내 신사가 거리의 진창을 밟고 다니는 꼴을 봐선 안 되지. 그의 구두에 흙 한 점 묻어선 안 돼. 내 신사는 말을 가져야 한다, 핍! 타고 다닐 말, 몰고 다닐 말, 그리고 그의 하인이 타고 몰 말도 말이다. 식민지 사람들이 자기네 말을(그것도 순종 말을, 천만의 말씀, 맙소사!) 갖는데, 내 런던 신사가 못 가질 게 무어냐? 아니, 안 될 말이지. 우리는 그자들한테 그것보다 한 수 위의 솜씨를 보여 줄 거다, 핍, 안 그러냐?\"\n\n그는 주머니에서 서류로 터질 듯한 두툼한 지갑 하나를 꺼내 식탁 위에 휙 던졌다.\n\n\"그 책 속에 쓸 만한 게 들어 있다, 얘야. 그건 네 거다. 내가 가진 건 전부 내 것이 아니라 네 것이야. 그걸 두려워 말아라. 그게 나온 데서 더 많이 나올 테니까. 나는 내 신사가 신사답게 돈을 쓰는 꼴을 보려고 옛 고향으로 돌아온 거다. 그게 내 기쁨이 될 거다. 그가 그렇게 하는 걸 보는 게 내 기쁨이란 말이다. 그리고 너희 모두 망해 버려라!\" 그는 방 안을 둘러보며 큰 소리로 한 번 손가락을 딱 튀기고는 이렇게 끝맺었다. \"가발 쓴 판사부터 먼지를 일으키는 식민지 놈에 이르기까지, 너희 한 놈도 빠짐없이 다 망해 버려라. 나는 너희를 죄다 합쳐 놓은 것보다 더 나은 신사를 보여 줄 테니까!\"\n\n\"그만!\" 나는 거의 두려움과 혐오에 미칠 지경이 되어 말했다. \"당신과 얘기를 좀 해야겠습니다. 나는 어떻게 해야 할지 알고 싶습니다. 어떻게 하면 당신을 위험에서 지킬 수 있는지, 얼마나 오래 머무를 작정인지, 무슨 계획을 가지고 있는지 알고 싶습니다.\"\n\n\"이것 봐라, 핍.\" 그가 별안간 달라진 가라앉은 태도로 내 팔에 손을 얹으며 말했다. \"무엇보다 먼저, 이것 봐라. 내가 조금 전에 분수를 잊었다. 내가 한 말은 천박했어. 바로 그거다, 천박했지. 이것 봐라, 핍. 눈감아 다오. 나는 천박하게 굴지 않을 거다.\"\n\n\"먼저,\" 나는 반쯤 신음하듯 말을 이었다. \"당신이 발각되어 붙잡히지 않으려면 어떤 대책을 세울 수 있겠습니까?\"\n\n\"아니다, 얘야.\" 그가 아까와 같은 어조로 말했다. \"그게 먼저가 아니다. 천박함이 먼저야. 내가 신사 하나 만드는 데 그 숱한 세월을 보낸 게, 그에게 마땅히 갖춰야 할 도리를 모르고서 한 일이 아니란 말이다. 이것 봐라, 핍. 나는 천박했다. 바로 그거였어, 천박했지. 눈감아 다오, 얘야.\"\n\n어딘가 음산하면서도 우스꽝스러운 느낌에 나는 짜증 섞인 웃음이 나와, 이렇게 대꾸했다. \"이미 눈감았습니다. 제발 부탁이니, 그 얘긴 그만 되풀이하세요!\"\n\n\"그래, 하지만 이것 봐라.\" 그가 고집스레 말했다. \"얘야, 내가 이 먼 길을 온 게 천박하게 굴려고 온 게 아니다. 자, 계속해라, 얘야. 네가 말하던 게 — \"\n\n\"당신이 무릅쓴 위험으로부터 어떻게 당신을 지켜야 할까요?\"\n\n\"그게 말이다, 얘야, 위험이 그리 크진 않다. 다시 누가 밀고만 안 한다면, 위험이랄 것도 그다지 대단치 않아. 재거스가 있고, 웨믹이 있고, 네가 있지. 그 밖에 밀고할 자가 누가 있겠느냐?\"\n\n\"거리에서 우연히 당신을 알아볼 사람은 없을까요?\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"글쎄,\" 그가 대꾸했다. \"그런 사람은 많지 않다. 게다가 나는 보터니 만에서 돌아온 A.M.이라는 이름으로 신문에 내 광고를 낼 생각도 없고, 세월도 많이 흘렀는데, 누가 그걸로 득을 보겠느냐? 그래도 이것 봐라, 핍. 설령 위험이 쉰 배는 더 컸더라도, 나는 너를 보러 왔을 거다. 명심해라, 똑같이 말이야.\"\n\n\"그럼 얼마나 머무르실 작정인가요?\"\n\n\"얼마나라고?\" 그가 입에서 검은 파이프를 떼고, 나를 빤히 바라보며 턱을 떨어뜨린 채 말했다. \"나는 돌아가지 않는다. 아주 돌아온 거다.\"\n\n\"어디서 사실 겁니까?\" 내가 말했다. \"당신을 어찌해야 합니까? 어디라야 당신이 안전하겠어요?\"\n\n\"얘야,\" 그가 대답했다. \"돈만 있으면 변장용 가발도 살 수 있고, 머릿가루도, 안경도, 검은 옷도 — 반바지며 뭐며 다 있다. 전에도 다른 자들이 무사히 해냈으니, 전에 남들이 해낸 일이라면 또 남들도 다시 해낼 수 있는 법이지. 어디서 어떻게 사느냐 하는 건, 얘야, 네 의견을 좀 들려다오.\"\n\n\"지금은 태평하게 말씀하시지만,\" 내가 말했다. \"어젯밤엔 그게 곧 죽음이라고 맹세하며 자못 심각하셨잖습니까.\"\n\n\"그래, 나는 그게 죽음이라고 맹세한다.\" 그가 파이프를 도로 입에 물며 말했다. \"그것도 교수형으로, 여기서 멀지 않은 한길 한복판에서 당하는 죽음이지. 네가 그것을 그렇게 똑똑히 이해하는 게 중요한 일이고말고. 그렇다면 어쩌겠느냐, 일단 그렇게 된 마당에? 나는 여기 있다. 이제 와서 돌아가는 건 버티고 있는 것만큼이나 나쁜 일이야 — 아니, 더 나쁘지. 게다가, 핍, 내가 여기 온 건 여러 해 동안, 오랜 세월 너를 마음에 두고 있었기 때문이다. 내가 무엇을 무릅쓰느냐 하면, 나는 이제 늙은 새다. 깃털이 처음 돋은 이래로 온갖 덫을 다 무릅써 온 늙은 새 말이다. 그러니 허수아비 위에 내려앉는 것쯤 두렵지 않아. 그 안에 죽음이 숨어 있다면, 그렇다면 그렇겠지. 어디 나오라지, 그럼 내가 맞서 줄 테니. 그때 가서야 나는 그것을 믿을 거다, 그 전엔 안 믿어. 자, 이제 내 신사를 다시 한 번 보게 해 다오.\"\n\n다시 한 번 그는 내 두 손을 잡고, 흐뭇해하는 소유주의 태도로 나를 찬찬히 뜯어보았다. 그러는 내내 그는 더없이 만족스럽게 담배를 피워 댔다.\n\n내가 할 수 있는 최선은, 그에게 바로 가까운 곳에 조용한 거처를 마련해 주는 것이리라 여겨졌다. 이삼 일 안에 돌아오리라 예상되던 허버트가 오면 그가 그곳을 차지하면 될 터였다. 이 비밀을 허버트와 나눔으로써 내가 얻게 될 엄청난 안도감을 차치하더라도, 피할 수 없는 필연으로 그 비밀을 그에게 털어놓아야 한다는 것은 내게 분명한 일이었다. 그러나 프로비스 씨에게는(나는 그를 그 이름으로 부르기로 마음먹었다) 그것이 결코 그리 분명한 일이 아니었으니, 그는 허버트를 직접 보고 그 관상에 호의적인 판단을 내릴 때까지는 그를 끼워 주는 데 동의하기를 유보했다. \"그리고 그때 가서도, 얘야,\" 그는 주머니에서 기름때 묻은 작고 검은, 걸쇠 달린 신약 성서를 꺼내며 말했다. \"그에게 맹세를 시켜야 한다.\"\n\n나의 그 무서운 후원자가 이 작은 검은 책을 세상천지에 들고 다닌 것이 오로지 긴급한 경우에 사람들에게 맹세를 시키기 위해서였다고 단언한다면, 내가 끝내 확실히 밝혀내지 못한 사실을 단언하는 셈이 되리라. 그러나 이것만은 말할 수 있다. 나는 그가 그 책을 다른 어떤 용도로 쓰는 것을 한 번도 본 적이 없다. 그 책 자체는 어느 법정에서 훔쳐 온 듯한 모양새였고, 어쩌면 그 책의 내력에 대한 그의 지식이, 그 방면에서 쌓은 그 자신의 경험과 어우러져, 일종의 법적 주술이나 부적 같은 그 책의 힘에 그가 의지를 두게 했는지도 몰랐다. 그가 그 책을 꺼내 보인 이 첫 번째 순간에, 나는 오래전 그가 교회 묘지에서 내게 충성을 맹세시켰던 일과, 어젯밤 그가 고독 속에서 늘 자신의 결심을 두고 맹세하곤 했노라고 스스로를 설명하던 일을 떠올렸다.\n\n그가 지금은 마치 앵무새나 시가를 팔러 다니는 사람처럼 보이는 뱃사람용 헐렁한 작업복 차림이었으므로, 나는 다음으로 그에게 어떤 옷을 입혀야 할지 의논했다. 그는 변장 수단으로서 반바지의 효능에 대해 유별난 믿음을 품고 있었고, 제 머릿속으로는 자기 옷차림을 그려 두었는데, 그대로라면 그는 성당 참사회장과 치과 의사 사이쯤 되는 무엇이 되었을 것이다. 형편 넉넉한 농부 비슷한 옷차림을 받아들이도록 그를 설득하는 데는 적잖은 애를 먹었다. 그리고 우리는 그가 머리를 짧게 깎고 머릿가루를 약간 바르기로 정했다. 끝으로, 빨래하는 여자나 그 조카가 아직 그를 보지 못했으므로, 옷을 갈아입을 때까지는 그들 눈에 띄지 않도록 몸을 숨기고 있기로 했다.\n\n이런 대비책을 정하는 일이야 간단해 보일 테지만, 멍하다 못해 정신이 흩어진 내 상태로는 어찌나 오래 걸렸던지, 나는 그것들을 실행에 옮기러 나가지도 못한 채 오후 두세 시가 되어서야 겨우 밖으로 나섰다. 내가 나가 있는 동안 그는 거처에 틀어박혀 있기로 했고, 어떤 일이 있어도 문을 열어선 안 되었다.\n\n내가 알기로 에식스 가에 점잖은 하숙집이 하나 있었는데, 그 뒤편은 템플 쪽을 향해 있어 거의 내 창문에서 부르면 들릴 만한 거리였다. 나는 무엇보다 먼저 그 집으로 가서, 다행히도 삼촌인 프로비스 씨를 위해 삼 층을 구할 수 있었다. 그러고는 이 가게 저 가게를 다니며 그의 외양을 바꾸는 데 필요한 물건들을 사들였다. 이 일을 마치고 나서, 나는 내 일을 보러 리틀 브리튼으로 발길을 돌렸다. 재거스 씨는 책상 앞에 있었으나, 내가 들어서는 것을 보자 곧바로 일어나 벽난로 앞에 섰다.\n\n\"자, 핍,\" 그가 말했다. \"조심하게.\"\n\n\"그러겠습니다, 선생님.\" 나는 대답했다. 오는 길에 무슨 말을 할지 곰곰이 생각해 두었던 것이다.\n\n\"자네 자신을 옭아매지 말게.\" 재거스 씨가 말했다. \"그리고 그 누구도 옭아매지 말게. 알겠나 — 그 누구도. 내게 아무것도 말하지 말게. 나는 아무것도 알고 싶지 않으니까. 나는 궁금하지 않네.\"\n\n물론 나는 그가 그 사람이 왔다는 사실을 알고 있음을 알아챘다.\n\n\"제가 바라는 건 다만, 재거스 씨,\" 내가 말했다. \"제가 들은 바가 사실인지 확인하려는 것뿐입니다. 그게 거짓이리라는 기대는 없습니다만, 적어도 그것만은 확인해 둘 수 있겠지요.\"\n\n재거스 씨가 고개를 끄덕였다. \"그런데 자네 '들었다'고 했나, '통지받았다'고 했나?\" 그가 고개를 한쪽으로 기울인 채, 나를 보지 않고, 귀를 기울이는 듯한 태도로 바닥을 내려다보며 물었다. \"들었다고 하면 구두로 전달받은 것을 뜻하는 듯한데. 자네도 알다시피 뉴사우스웨일스에 있는 사람과는 구두로 의사를 주고받을 수 없네.\"\n\n\"통지받았다고 하겠습니다, 재거스 씨.\"\n\n\"좋아.\"\n\n\"에이벌 매그위치라는 사람에게서, 그가 오래도록 제게 알려지지 않았던 그 후원자라는 통지를 받았습니다.\"\n\n\"그게 바로 그 사람이지.\" 재거스 씨가 말했다. \"뉴사우스웨일스에 있는.\"\n\n\"그리고 오직 그 사람뿐인가요?\" 내가 말했다. \"그리고 오직 그 사람뿐이오.\" 재거스 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"제가 제 잘못과 그릇된 결론을 두고 선생님께 책임이 있다고 여길 만큼 분별없는 사람은 아닙니다만, 저는 줄곧 그것이 미스 해비셤이라고 짐작했습니다.\"\n\n\"자네 말마따나, 핍,\" 재거스 씨가 차갑게 내게 눈길을 돌리고 집게손가락을 한 번 깨물며 대꾸했다. \"그 일에 나는 조금도 책임이 없네.\"\n\n\"하지만 그것이 어찌나 그래 보였는지요, 선생님.\" 나는 풀이 죽은 가슴으로 하소연했다.\n\n\"증거라곤 티끌만큼도 없었네, 핍.\" 재거스 씨가 고개를 저으며 옷자락을 추슬렀다. \"겉모습으로는 아무것도 받아들이지 말게. 모든 것을 증거로 받아들이게. 그보다 더 나은 법칙은 없네.\"\n\n\"더는 드릴 말씀이 없습니다.\" 잠시 말없이 서 있다가 나는 한숨을 쉬며 말했다. \"제가 통지받은 바를 확인했으니, 그것으로 끝입니다.\"\n\n\"그리고 매그위치가 — 뉴사우스웨일스에 있는 — 마침내 제 정체를 드러냈으니,\" 재거스 씨가 말했다. \"핍, 자네는 내가 자네와 주고받은 모든 일에서 얼마나 엄정하게 늘 사실의 엄격한 선만을 고수해 왔는지 이해하겠지. 사실의 엄격한 선에서 단 한 번도 벗어난 적이 없네. 자네도 그건 잘 알고 있겠지?\"\n\"잘 알고 있습니다, 선생님.\"\n\n\"나는 매그위치에게 — 뉴사우스웨일스에 있는 — 그가 처음 내게 편지를 보냈을 때 — 뉴사우스웨일스에서 — 내가 사실의 엄격한 선에서 결코 벗어나리라 기대해선 안 된다는 주의를 주었네. 나는 또 그에게 다른 주의도 하나 주었지. 내가 보기에 그는 편지에서, 여기 영국에서 자네를 만나겠다는 어떤 막연한 생각을 은연중에 내비친 듯했네. 나는 그 일에 관해서는 더 듣고 싶지 않다고, 그가 사면을 받을 가능성은 전혀 없다고, 그는 평생을 기한으로 국외로 추방된 몸이라고, 그러니 그가 이 나라에 모습을 나타내는 것은 곧 중죄 행위로서 법의 극형에 처해질 수 있는 일이라고 주의를 주었네. 나는 매그위치에게 그렇게 주의를 주었지.\" 재거스 씨가 나를 빤히 바라보며 말했다. \"그것을 뉴사우스웨일스로 써 보냈네. 그는 틀림없이 그에 따라 처신했을 걸세.\"\n\n\"틀림없이 그랬겠지요.\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"나는 웨믹에게서 통지받았네.\" 재거스 씨가 여전히 나를 빤히 바라보며 말을 이었다. \"그가 퍼비스인지 뭔지 하는 이름의 식민지 사람에게서 포츠머스 소인이 찍힌 편지 한 통을 받았다고 말일세 — \"\n\n\"프로비스 말씀이시겠죠.\" 내가 일러 주었다.\n\n\"아니면 프로비스 — 고맙네, 핍. 어쩌면 프로비스인가? 어쩌면 자네는 그게 프로비스라는 걸 알고 있나?\"\n\n\"네.\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"자네는 그게 프로비스라는 걸 알고 있군. 프로비스라는 이름의 식민지 사람에게서, 포츠머스 소인이 찍혀 온 편지 한 통. 매그위치를 대신하여 자네 주소의 상세한 내용을 묻는 편지였지. 내가 알기로 웨믹이 그에게 회신으로 그 상세한 내용을 보냈네. 아마도 자네가 매그위치에 관한 — 뉴사우스웨일스에 있는 — 해명을 전해 받은 것도 프로비스를 통해서겠지?\"\n\n\"프로비스를 통해 전해 받았습니다.\" 나는 대답했다.\n\n\"그럼 잘 가게, 핍.\" 재거스 씨가 손을 내밀며 말했다. \"만나서 반가웠네. 우편으로 매그위치에게 — 뉴사우스웨일스에 있는 — 편지를 쓰거나, 프로비스를 통해 그와 연락할 때, 우리의 오랜 거래 내역의 상세한 명세와 증빙 서류를 잔액과 함께 자네에게 보내겠다고 부디 전해 주게. 아직 남아 있는 잔액이 있으니 말일세. 잘 가게, 핍!\"\n\n우리는 악수를 나누었고, 그는 나를 볼 수 있는 한 끝까지 빤히 바라보았다. 내가 문간에서 돌아보니, 그는 여전히 나를 빤히 바라보고 있었고, 그동안 선반 위의 그 흉측한 두 석고상은 부어오른 눈꺼풀을 애써 떠 보려 하고, 부풀어 오른 목구멍에서 \"오, 정말 대단한 사람이로군!\" 하는 소리를 가까스로 짜내려 애쓰는 것만 같았다.\n\n웨믹은 자리에 없었고, 설령 그가 책상 앞에 있었다 한들 나를 위해 해 줄 수 있는 일이라곤 없었을 것이다. 나는 곧장 템플로 돌아왔고, 거기서 그 무서운 프로비스가 럼주에 물을 타 마시며 흑인 머리 담배를 피우면서 무사히 있는 것을 발견했다.\n\n이튿날 내가 주문한 옷이 모두 집으로 배달되어 왔고, 그는 그것들을 입었다. 그가 무엇을 걸치든, (내게는 침울하게도) 전에 입고 있던 것보다 덜 어울리는 것만 같았다. 내 생각에는, 그를 변장시키려는 시도를 가망 없게 만드는 무언가가 그에게 있었다. 그에게 옷을 입히면 입힐수록, 더 좋은 옷을 입히면 입힐수록, 그는 점점 더 습지에서 어슬렁대던 그 도망자처럼 보였다. 내 불안한 상상에 미친 이런 영향은, 두말할 것 없이 그의 옛 얼굴과 거동이 내게 점점 더 낯익어졌기 때문이기도 했을 것이다. 그러나 나는 또한 그가 마치 아직도 쇠고랑의 무게가 매달려 있는 듯 한쪽 다리를 질질 끌고 다니며, 머리끝부터 발끝까지 그 사람의 됨됨이 결마다 죄수가 배어 있다고 믿었다.\n\n게다가 그에게는 외로운 오두막 생활의 영향이 배어 있어, 어떤 옷으로도 길들일 수 없는 야성적인 분위기를 풍겼다. 거기에 더해, 그 뒤로 사람들 틈에서 낙인찍힌 채 살아온 삶의 영향이 있었고, 그 모든 것의 정점에는 자신이 지금 몸을 피하고 숨어 다니고 있다는 의식이 있었다. 앉고 서는 모든 몸짓에서, 먹고 마시는 모든 몸짓에서 — 어깨를 잔뜩 추켜올린 채 내키지 않는 듯한 태도로 곰곰 생각에 잠기는 모습에서, 뿔 손잡이가 달린 큼직한 잭나이프를 꺼내 다리에 쓱쓱 닦고는 음식을 써는 모습에서, 마치 투박한 양철 잔이라도 되는 양 얇은 유리잔과 찻잔을 입가로 들어 올리는 모습에서, 빵에서 한 조각을 베어 내어 그것으로 접시 둘레에 남은 그레이비의 마지막 부스러기까지 정해진 양을 한 톨이라도 더 알뜰히 쓰려는 듯 빙빙 돌려 가며 닦아 내고는, 그 빵으로 손가락 끝까지 훔친 다음 삼켜 버리는 모습에서 — 이런 몸짓들과, 하루 중에도 매 순간 일어나는 이름 없는 천 가지 다른 사소한 일들에서, 죄수가, 중죄인이, 도형수가 더없이 또렷하게 드러났다.\n\n머릿가루를 약간 바르자는 것은 그 자신의 생각이었고, 나는 반바지 문제를 잠재운 뒤에 그 머릿가루는 양보해 준 터였다. 그러나 그것을 바른 뒤의 효과는, 죽은 사람에게 연지를 발랐을 때 났음 직한 효과 말고는 무엇에도 견줄 수가 없었다. 그토록 끔찍하게도, 그에게서 가장 억눌러야 마땅한 모든 것이 그 얄팍한 위장의 막을 뚫고 솟아올라, 마치 그의 정수리에서 활활 타오르며 터져 나오는 것만 같았다. 그것은 시도하자마자 곧 포기되었고, 그는 희끗희끗한 머리를 짧게 깎은 채로 지냈다.\n\n그와 동시에 그가 내게 얼마나 무시무시한 수수께끼였던지, 그 느낌은 말로 다 할 수가 없다. 저녁이면 그가 옹이 진 두 손으로 안락의자의 팔걸이를 움켜쥐고, 깊은 주름이 문신처럼 새겨진 대머리를 앞으로 떨어뜨려 가슴에 묻은 채 잠들 때면, 나는 앉아서 그를 바라보며, 그가 무슨 짓을 저질렀을까 헤아려 보고, 그에게 죄목록에 오른 온갖 죄를 죄다 뒤집어씌우다가, 마침내 벌떡 일어나 그에게서 달아나고픈 충동에 휩싸이곤 했다. 시시각각 그에 대한 내 혐오가 어찌나 커져 갔던지, 그가 나를 위해 해 준 그 모든 일과 그가 무릅쓰고 있는 위험에도 불구하고, 그런 시달림에 처음 휩싸여 괴로워하던 무렵에 나는, 허버트가 곧 돌아오리라는 사실을 알지 못했더라면 정말 이 충동에 굴복해 버렸을지도 모른다는 생각마저 든다. 한번은 실제로 한밤중에 침대에서 벌떡 일어나, 그를 내가 가진 다른 모든 것과 함께 거기 남겨 둔 채 떠나, 사병으로 인도로 떠날 작정으로 황급히 가장 허름한 옷을 주워 입기 시작한 적도 있었다.\n\n길고 긴 저녁과 길고 긴 밤마다, 바람과 비가 끊임없이 휘몰아쳐 지나가는 그 외딴 방에서, 유령이라 한들 내게 그보다 더 무서웠을까 싶다. 유령은 나 때문에 붙잡혀 교수형을 당할 리 없었으나, 그는 그럴 수도 있다는 생각과, 그가 그렇게 되리라는 두려움은 내 공포를 적잖이 키워 놓았다. 그가 잠들어 있지도, 자기만의 너덜너덜한 카드 한 벌로 — 나는 그 전에도 그 후에도 본 적 없는 놀이로, 그는 잭나이프를 식탁에 푹 꽂아 가며 자기가 딴 점수를 기록했다 — 복잡한 패 맞추기 놀이를 하고 있지도 않을 때면, 그는 내게 책을 읽어 달라고 청하곤 했다. \"외국어로, 얘야!\" 내가 그 청을 들어주는 동안, 그는 단 한마디도 알아듣지 못하면서, 불 앞에 서서 마치 전시 흥행사 같은 태도로 나를 바라보았고, 나는 손으로 얼굴을 가린 그 손가락 사이로, 그가 내 능숙함을 좀 알아 달라고 가구를 향해 말없는 몸짓으로 호소하는 모습을 보곤 했다. 자신이 불경하게 만들어 낸 흉측한 괴물에게 쫓기는 그 가상의 학생도, 나보다 더 비참하지는 않았으리라. 나를 만들어 낸 그 괴물에게 쫓기면서, 그가 나를 우러러보면 볼수록, 나를 더 애지중지하면 할수록, 더 강한 거부감으로 그에게서 움츠러드는 나보다는.\n\n이 일을 마치 한 해 동안 계속된 것처럼 적고 있다는 것을, 나도 잘 안다. 그것은 닷새쯤 이어졌을 뿐이다. 그동안 줄곧 허버트를 기다리느라, 나는 날이 저문 뒤 프로비스를 바람 쐬러 데리고 나갈 때 말고는 감히 밖에 나가지 못했다. 마침내 어느 저녁, 저녁 식사가 끝나고 내가 완전히 지쳐 선잠에 빠져들었을 때 — 밤이면 마음이 들뜨고 무서운 꿈에 잠을 설쳐 왔던 까닭에 — 나는 계단을 올라오는 반가운 발소리에 잠이 깨었다. 마찬가지로 잠들어 있던 프로비스는 내가 내는 소리에 비틀거리며 일어났고, 다음 순간 나는 그의 손에서 잭나이프가 번뜩이는 것을 보았다.\n\n\"조용히! 허버트입니다!\" 나는 말했다. 그러자 허버트가, 프랑스 육백 마일의 상쾌한 생기를 온몸에 두른 채 와락 뛰어들었다.\n\n\"핸들, 이 친구야, 잘 있었나, 또 잘 있었나, 또 잘 있었나? 마치 일 년은 떠나 있었던 것 같군! 아니, 정말 그랬던 게 틀림없어. 자네가 아주 야위고 핼쑥해졌으니 말이야! 핸들, 내 — 아니! 실례했네.\"\n\n그는 프로비스를 보고는, 신나게 떠들며 내 손을 흔들어 대던 것을 멈추었다. 프로비스는 그를 뚫어지게 주시하며, 천천히 잭나이프를 도로 집어넣고, 다른 주머니에서 무언가를 더듬어 찾고 있었다.\n\n\"허버트, 내 소중한 친구,\" 나는 이중문을 닫으며 말했고, 그동안 허버트는 빤히 바라보며 어리둥절해 서 있었다. \"아주 이상한 일이 일어났다네. 이분은 — 내 손님일세.\"\n\n\"괜찮네, 얘야!\" 프로비스가 걸쇠 달린 작고 검은 책을 들고 앞으로 나오며 말하더니, 이내 허버트를 향해 말했다. \"그걸 오른손에 쥐어라. 어떤 식으로든 털어놓는 날에는 하느님이 그 자리에서 자네를 쳐 죽이시리라! 거기 입을 맞춰라!\"\n\n\"그분이 원하시는 대로 하게.\" 나는 허버트에게 말했다. 그러자 허버트는 다정하면서도 불안하고 놀란 눈빛으로 나를 바라보며 시키는 대로 했고, 프로비스는 곧바로 그와 악수하며 말했다. \"이제 자네는 맹세한 거다, 알겠나. 핍이 자네를 신사로 만들어 주지 못한다면, 내 맹세도 두 번 다시 믿지 마라!\"\n\n# Chapter XLI.\n\n허버트와 나, 그리고 프로비스가 난롯가에 둘러앉고 내가 그 비밀을 죄다 털어놓았을 때 허버트가 보인 경악과 동요를 묘사하려 한들 부질없는 노릇일 것이다. 다만 나는 내 자신의 감정이 허버트의 얼굴에 고스란히 비치는 것을 보았고, 그중에서도 나를 위해 그토록 많은 일을 해 준 그 사내에 대한 혐오감이 적지 않게 드러나 있었다는 것만으로 충분하리라.\n\n다른 가르는 사정이 전혀 없었다 하더라도 그 사내와 우리 사이에 홀로 분단을 그어 놓았을 것은, 내 이야기에 대한 그의 의기양양함이었다. 돌아온 뒤로 한 번 자신이 \"천박했다\"는, 그를 괴롭히는 자각만 빼면 — 내 폭로가 끝나기가 무섭게 그는 그 점에 관해 허버트에게 일장 연설을 늘어놓기 시작했다 — 그에게는 내가 내 행운에서 그 어떤 흠을 잡을 수도 있으리라는 생각이 전혀 없었다. 자신이 나를 신사로 만들었으며, 자신의 넉넉한 재력으로 내가 그 신분을 떠받치는 것을 보러 왔다는 그의 자랑은 자기 자신을 위한 것인 만큼이나 나를 위한 것이기도 했다. 그리고 그것이 우리 둘 모두에게 더없이 유쾌한 자랑이며, 우리 둘 다 그것을 몹시 자랑스러워해야 마땅하다는 것이 그의 마음속에서는 확고하게 굳어진 결론이었다.\n\n\"하지만 이봐, 핍의 동무.\" 한동안 떠들어 댄 뒤에 그가 허버트에게 말했다. \"내가 돌아온 뒤로 한 번 — 반 분 동안 — 천박했다는 건 나도 아주 잘 알아. 핍한테 말했지, 내가 천박했었다는 걸 알고 있다고. 하지만 그 일로 마음 쓰지 마. 내가 핍을 신사로 만들었고, 핍이 자네를 신사로 만들 텐데, 내가 자네들 둘에게 마땅히 해야 할 도리를 모를 리가 있나. 얘야, 그리고 핍의 동무, 자네 둘은 내가 언제나 점잖게 입에 재갈을 물고 있으리라 믿어도 좋아. 천박함에 넘어갔던 그 반 분 이후로 줄곧 재갈을 물어 왔고, 지금 이 순간에도 재갈을 물고 있고, 앞으로도 영원히 재갈을 물고 있을 테니까.\"\n\n허버트는 \"그럼요.\" 하고 대꾸했지만, 그 말에 별다른 위안이 없다는 듯한 표정이었고, 여전히 당혹스럽고 낙담한 채였다. 우리는 그가 자기 숙소로 가서 우리 둘만 남겨 둘 시간을 애타게 기다렸으나, 그는 우리 둘만 남겨 두는 것을 분명 시샘하는지 늦게까지 앉아 있었다. 내가 그를 에식스 가까지 데려다주고 그 어두운 자기 집 문 안으로 무사히 들어가는 것을 본 것은 자정이 넘어서였다. 문이 그의 등 뒤로 닫혔을 때, 나는 그가 도착하던 밤 이후로 처음으로 안도의 순간을 맛보았다.\n\n계단에 있던 그 사내에 대한 불안한 기억에서 완전히 자유로운 적이 없었던 터라, 나는 날이 저문 뒤 손님을 데리고 나가거나 다시 데려올 때면 늘 주위를 살폈고, 이번에도 주위를 둘러보았다. 그런 점에서 위험을 의식하고 있을 때 큰 도시에서 감시당하고 있다는 의심을 떨치기란 어려운 일이지만, 그럼에도 시야에 들어오는 사람들 중 누가 내 행동에 관심을 둔다고는 도무지 믿어지지 않았다. 지나가던 몇 안 되는 이들은 저마다의 길로 지나쳐 갔고, 내가 템플로 돌아섰을 때 거리는 텅 비어 있었다. 아무도 우리와 함께 문으로 나오지 않았고, 아무도 나와 함께 문으로 들어가지 않았다. 분수대 옆을 지날 때 그의 집 불 켜진 뒤쪽 창문들이 환하고 고요하게 보였고, 계단을 오르기 전 내가 사는 건물 입구에 잠시 서 있을 때, 가든 코트는 내가 올라간 그 계단만큼이나 적막하고 생기가 없었다.\n\n허버트는 두 팔을 벌려 나를 맞아 주었고, 나는 친구가 있다는 것이 어떤 것인지 그토록 복되게 느껴 본 적이 일찍이 없었다. 그가 진심 어린 동정과 격려의 말을 몇 마디 건넨 뒤, 우리는 자리에 앉아 문제를 곰곰이 따져 보기 시작했다. 어떻게 할 것인가, 하는 문제를.\n\n프로비스가 앉았던 의자는 여전히 놓여 있던 자리에 그대로 있었는데 — 그에게는 한 자리를 떠나지 못하고 어슬렁대는, 한결같이 안정되지 못한 태도로, 파이프와 흑인 머리 담배와 잭나이프와 카드 한 벌이며 그밖에 이것저것을 가지고 마치 석판에 정해 적혀 있기라도 한 듯 똑같은 일과를 되풀이하는, 병영 같은 버릇이 있었으므로 — 다시 말해 그의 의자가 놓여 있던 자리에 그대로 있었으므로, 허버트는 무심코 거기에 앉았다가 다음 순간 벌떡 일어나 그것을 밀어내고 다른 의자에 앉았다. 그 뒤로 그가 내 후원자에게 혐오감을 품게 되었노라고 말할 필요도 없었거니와, 나 또한 내 혐오감을 고백할 필요가 없었다. 우리는 한마디 말로 빚어내지 않고도 그 속내를 주고받았다.\n\n\"어떻게.\" 허버트가 다른 의자에 안전하게 앉았을 때 내가 그에게 말했다. \"어떻게 해야 할까?\"\n\n\"가엾은 친구 핸들.\" 그가 머리를 감싸 쥐며 대답했다. \"나는 너무 얼떨떨해서 생각이 안 돼.\"\n\n\"나도 그랬어, 허버트. 처음 그 일격을 맞았을 때는. 그래도 뭔가는 해야 해. 그는 갖가지 새로운 지출에 골몰해 있어 — 말이니, 마차니, 온갖 종류의 호사스러운 겉치레니. 어떻게든 그를 말려야 해.\"\n\n\"네 말은, 네가 받아들일 수 없다는—\"\n\n\"내가 어떻게 받겠어?\" 허버트가 말을 멈추자 내가 끼어들었다. \"그를 생각해 봐! 그를 봐!\"\n\n우리 둘 모두에게 저도 모르게 몸서리가 스쳐 지나갔다.\n\n\"하지만 두려운 건, 허버트, 그 끔찍한 진실은 그가 나를 따른다는 거야, 그것도 단단히 따른다는 거지. 이런 운명이 또 어디 있겠어!\"\n\n\"가엾은 친구 핸들.\" 허버트가 거듭 말했다.\n\n\"그렇다고.\" 내가 말했다. \"결국 여기서 딱 멈추고, 그에게서 더는 한 푼도 받지 않는다 쳐도, 내가 이미 그에게 진 빚을 생각해 봐! 게다가 또 이렇잖아. 나는 빚이 잔뜩 있어 — 이제 아무 유산도 바랄 게 없는 나로서는 아주 무거운 빚이지 — 그리고 나는 아무런 직업도 익히지 못했고, 아무짝에도 쓸모가 없어.\"\n\n\"이런, 이런, 이런!\" 허버트가 나무라듯 말했다. \"아무짝에도 쓸모없다는 말은 하지 마.\"\n\n\"내가 뭐에 쓸모가 있겠어? 내가 쓸모 있을 만한 일은 딱 하나밖에 모르겠어. 그건 군인이 되는 거지. 그리고 자네의 우정과 애정에 의논을 구할 가망만 아니었다면, 사랑하는 허버트, 나는 그렇게 갔을지도 몰라.\"\n\n물론 나는 거기서 무너지고 말았다. 그리고 물론 허버트는 내 손을 따뜻하게 꽉 쥐는 것 말고는 그것을 모르는 척했다.\n\n\"어쨌든, 사랑하는 핸들.\" 그가 이윽고 말했다. \"군인 노릇은 안 될 일이야. 만약 자네가 이 후원과 이 호의를 마다한다면, 짐작건대 자네는 이미 받은 것을 언젠가 갚으리라는 희미한 희망이라도 품고 그렇게 하겠지. 자네가 군인이 되어 버린다면 그 희망이 그리 든든할 리가 없잖아! 게다가 그건 터무니없는 짓이야. 자네는 클래리커 상회가 작긴 해도 거기 있는 편이 한없이 나을 거야. 알다시피 나는 동업자가 되려고 노력하는 중이고 말이야.\"\n\n가엾은 친구! 그는 그게 누구의 돈인지 거의 짐작도 못하고 있었다.\n\n\"하지만 또 다른 문제가 있어.\" 허버트가 말했다. \"이 사람은 무지하고 고집 센 사람이고, 오랫동안 단 하나의 고정관념을 품어 온 사람이야. 그뿐만 아니라, 내가 보기엔 (내가 잘못 판단하는 걸지도 모르지만) 그는 필사적이고 사나운 성격의 사람인 것 같아.\"\n\n\"그래, 맞아.\" 내가 대답했다. \"내가 그걸 어떤 증거로 봤는지 말해 줄게.\" 그리고 나는 내 이야기에서 언급하지 않았던 것, 그 다른 죄수와 맞붙었던 일을 그에게 들려주었다.\n\n\"그렇다면 봐.\" 허버트가 말했다. \"이걸 생각해 봐! 그는 자신의 고정관념을 실현하기 위해 목숨을 걸고 여기 온 거야. 그 모든 고생과 기다림 끝에, 막상 실현되는 순간에 자네가 그의 발밑 땅을 무너뜨리고, 그의 관념을 부수고, 그가 얻은 것을 그에게 무가치하게 만들어 버린다면. 그 실망 속에서 그가 무슨 짓을 저지를지 짐작되는 게 없어?\"\n\n\"짐작하고 있어, 허버트, 게다가 그게 도착한 그 불길한 밤 이후로 줄곧 그 꿈까지 꾸고 있어. 그가 스스로 잡힐 길로 뛰어드는 것만큼 내 머릿속에 또렷이 박혀 있는 건 없었어.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 믿어도 좋아.\" 허버트가 말했다. \"그가 그런 짓을 저지를 위험이 크다는 걸. 그게 그가 잉글랜드에 머무는 한 자네를 옭아매는 힘이고, 자네가 그를 저버린다면 그가 택할 무모한 행보일 거야.\"\n\n처음부터 나를 짓눌러 온 이 생각의 끔찍함에, 그리고 그것이 현실로 이루어진다면 내가 어떤 의미에서는 나 자신을 그의 살인자로 여기게 되리라는 데에 너무도 충격을 받은 나머지, 나는 의자에 가만히 앉아 있을 수가 없어 이리저리 서성이기 시작했다. 그러는 동안 나는 허버트에게, 설령 프로비스가 본인의 뜻과 상관없이 신원이 드러나 잡힌다 해도, 내가 그 원인이라는 점에서, 아무리 죄가 없다 해도 나는 비참할 것이라고 말했다. 그렇다. 그를 풀어 둔 채 가까이 두는 것이 그토록 비참하고, 또 이 지경에 이르느니 차라리 평생을 그 대장간에서 일하는 편이 훨씬 나았으리라 여기는데도 말이다!\n\n그러나 어떻게 할 것인가, 하는 문제를 미뤄 둘 길은 없었다.\n\n\"제일 먼저, 그리고 가장 중요하게 해야 할 일은.\" 허버트가 말했다. \"그를 잉글랜드 밖으로 빼내는 거야. 자네가 그와 함께 가야 할 거고, 그래야 그도 떠나도록 설득될 테니까.\"\n\n\"하지만 그를 어디로 데려가든, 그가 돌아오는 걸 내가 막을 수 있을까?\"\n\n\"착한 핸들, 바로 옆 거리에 뉴게이트 감옥이 있는데, 자네 속내를 그에게 털어놓아 그를 무모하게 만드는 것이 여기서가 다른 어디서보다 훨씬 더 큰 위험을 안긴다는 게 뻔하지 않나? 그 다른 죄수 일이든, 아니면 그의 인생에 있었던 그 무엇이든을 구실 삼아 그를 떠나보낼 핑계를 만들어 낼 수 있다면 말이야, 지금.\"\n\n\"그것도 또 그래!\" 내가 두 손을 펼쳐 마치 그 안에 이 사태의 절망이 담겨 있기라도 한 듯 허버트 앞에 멈춰 서서 말했다. \"나는 그의 인생에 대해 아무것도 몰라. 밤마다 여기 앉아, 내 행운과 불운에 그토록 얽혀 있으면서도, 어린 시절 이틀 동안 나를 공포에 떨게 한 가엾은 비참한 인간이라는 것 말고는 그토록 알려진 게 없는 그를 눈앞에 두고 보고 있노라면, 거의 미칠 지경이었어!\"\n\n허버트는 일어나 내 팔에 자기 팔을 끼웠고, 우리는 카펫을 들여다보며 천천히 함께 이리저리 거닐었다.\n\n\"핸들.\" 허버트가 멈춰 서서 말했다. \"자네는 그에게서 더 이상 아무 도움도 받을 수 없다고 확신하지, 그렇지?\"\n\n\"그래, 전적으로. 자네가 내 처지라도 분명 그러지 않겠어?\"\n\n\"그리고 자네는 그와 인연을 끊어야 한다고 확신하지?\"\n\n\"허버트, 그걸 물어볼 게 있어?\"\n\n\"그리고 자네는 그가 자네를 위해 걸었던 목숨에 대해, 그 다정함을 품고 있고, 또 품을 수밖에 없으니, 가능하다면 그가 그 목숨을 내던지지 않게 구해야 해. 그렇다면 자네 자신을 빼내려고 손가락 하나 까딱하기 전에, 먼저 그를 잉글랜드 밖으로 빼내야 해. 그 일이 끝나거든, 부디 자네 자신을 빼내게. 그러면 우리 함께 끝까지 헤쳐 나가는 거야, 사랑하는 친구.\"\n\n그 일에 대해 악수를 나누고, 그것만이라도 정해 두고 다시 이리저리 거닐 수 있다는 것은 위안이 되었다.\n\n\"자, 허버트.\" 내가 말했다. \"그의 내력을 좀 알아내는 일 말인데. 내가 아는 길은 단 하나뿐이야. 그에게 단도직입적으로 물어보는 수밖에 없어.\"\n\n\"그래. 물어봐.\" 허버트가 말했다. \"내일 아침 식사 자리에 앉았을 때.\" 그가 허버트에게 작별을 고하면서 우리와 함께 아침을 먹으러 오겠다고 했던 것이다.\n\n이 계획을 세우고 우리는 잠자리에 들었다. 나는 그에 관한 더없이 어수선한 꿈을 꾸었고, 개운치 않은 채로 잠에서 깼다. 또한 잠에서 깨면서, 밤사이 잊고 있었던 두려움, 그가 돌아온 유형수임이 발각될지 모른다는 두려움이 되살아났다. 깨어 있는 동안 나는 그 두려움을 결코 떨치지 못했다.\n\n그는 약속한 시각에 와서 잭나이프를 꺼내 들고 식사 앞에 앉았다. 그는 \"자기 신사가 보란 듯이, 신사답게 두각을 나타낼\" 갖가지 계획으로 가득 차 있었고, 자기가 내게 맡겨 둔 그 수첩을 어서 쓰기 시작하라고 재촉했다. 그는 이 거처와 자기 숙소를 임시 거처로 여겼으며, 하이드 파크 근처에 자기가 \"하룻밤 묵을 자리\"를 둘 수 있는 \"근사한 처소\"를 당장 알아보라고 권했다. 그가 아침 식사를 끝내고 칼을 자기 다리에 닦고 있을 때, 나는 아무런 서두도 없이 그에게 말을 꺼냈다.\n\n\"어젯밤 아저씨가 가신 뒤에, 우리가 다가갔을 때 군인들이 습지에서 아저씨가 맞붙어 있는 걸 발견했던 그 몸싸움 이야기를 친구한테 했어요. 기억하시죠?\"\n\n\"기억하느냐고!\" 그가 말했다. \"당연하지!\"\n\n\"우리는 그 사내에 대해 — 그리고 아저씨에 대해 좀 알고 싶어요. 어느 쪽에 대해서든, 특히 아저씨에 대해 어젯밤 내가 말할 수 있었던 것보다 더 아는 게 없다는 게 이상해요. 우리가 더 아는 데 지금만 한 때가 또 있을까요?\"\n\n\"그래!\" 그가 한참 생각한 끝에 말했다. \"자네는 맹세를 한 몸이지, 그렇지, 핍의 동무?\"\n\n\"틀림없습니다.\" 허버트가 대답했다.\n\n\"내가 무슨 말을 하든 말이야, 알겠지.\" 그가 다짐을 두었다. \"그 맹세는 전부에 적용돼.\"\n\n\"그렇게 알아듣고 있습니다.\"\n\n\"그리고 이봐! 내가 무슨 짓을 했든 그건 다 형을 치르고 값을 치른 거야.\" 그가 다시 다짐을 두었다.\n\n\"그렇게 하지요.\"\n\n그는 검은 파이프를 꺼내 흑인 머리 담배를 채워 넣으려다가, 손안에 엉킨 담배를 들여다보더니 그것이 자기 이야기의 가닥을 헝클어뜨릴지도 모른다고 여기는 듯했다. 그는 그것을 도로 집어넣고, 파이프를 외투 단춧구멍에 꽂은 다음, 양 무릎에 한 손씩 펼쳐 얹고는, 잠시 말없이 성난 눈길을 난롯불에 던지고 나서, 우리를 둘러보며 다음과 같이 말했다.\n\n# Chapter XLII.\n\n「사랑하는 도련님, 그리고 핍의 친구분. 나는 노래나 이야기책처럼 내 인생을 늘어놓을 생각은 없소. 다만 짤막하고 손쉽게 일러 드리려고, 단숨에 영어 한 입거리로 줄여 보겠소. 감옥에 들어갔다 나오고, 들어갔다 나오고, 또 들어갔다 나오고. 자, 다 들으셨소. 그게 대체로 내 인생이오, 핍이 내 편이 되어 주고 나서 배에 실려 보내질 때까지 말이오.\n\n「나는 거의 별의별 일을 다 당했소. 목 매달리는 것만 빼고. 은 찻주전자처럼 자주 갇혀 있었지. 이리 끌려가고 저리 끌려가고, 이 마을에서 쫓겨나고 저 마을에서 쫓겨나고, 차꼬에 채워지고, 매질당하고, 시달리고, 내몰렸소. 내가 어디서 태어났는지는 당신들이 아는 만큼도 모르오 — 그만큼도 못 되지. 나는 에식스 지방에서, 먹고살려고 순무를 훔치다가 처음으로 나라는 존재를 깨달았소. 누군가가 내게서 달아났더군 — 어떤 남자 — 땜장이였는데 — 그자가 불씨를 가지고 가 버려서, 나를 몹시 춥게 내버려 두었지.\n\n「내 이름이 매그위치고, 에이블이라고 세례받았다는 건 알았소. 그걸 어떻게 알았느냐고? 산울타리의 새 이름이 되새, 참새, 개똥지빠귀라는 걸 아는 정도로 알았던 거요. 그게 다 거짓말일 수도 있다고 생각할 뻔했지만, 새 이름들이 다 참인 걸 보니 내 이름도 참이려니 했소.\n\n「내가 알아낼 수 있는 한, 몸뚱이에 걸친 것이라곤 그 속에 든 것만큼이나 변변찮은 이 어린 에이블 매그위치를 본 사람치고, 그 아이를 보고 겁먹지 않은 영혼은 하나도 없었소. 그래서 그들은 나를 쫓아 버리거나 잡아들였지. 나는 잡혀가고, 잡혀가고, 또 잡혀가서, 그야말로 잡혀가며 자란 셈이오.\n\n「사정은 이러했소. 내가 여태 본 그 누구 못지않게 가엾은, 누더기를 걸친 어린것이었을 적에(거울을 들여다본 건 아니오. 가구가 갖춰진 집 안을 내가 본 적이 별로 없었으니까), 나는 천성이 모진 놈이라는 평판을 얻었소. ‘이놈은 지독하게 모진 놈입니다.’ 그들은 감옥 방문객들에게 나를 콕 집어 그리 말했지. ‘이 아이는 감옥에서 산다고 해도 될 지경입니다.’ 그러면 그들은 나를 보고, 나는 그들을 보고, 그중 몇몇은 내 머리 크기를 재고 — 차라리 내 뱃속이나 잴 것이지 — 또 몇몇은 내가 읽지도 못하는 전도책자를 주고, 알아듣지도 못하는 연설을 늘어놓았소. 그들은 늘 악마 이야기로 나를 몰아붙였지. 한데 대체 이 악마 같은 처지에 내가 뭘 어쩌란 말이오? 뱃속에 뭐라도 채워 넣어야 하지 않겠소? — 그건 그렇고, 내 말이 점점 처량해지는구려, 도리는 안다오. 사랑하는 도련님, 그리고 핍의 친구분, 내가 처량해질까 봐 두려워 마시오.\n\n「떠돌고, 구걸하고, 훔치고, 할 수 있을 땐 이따금 일도 하면서 — 하긴 당신들이 생각하는 것만큼 자주는 아니었지. 당신들 스스로가 나 같은 놈한테 일거리를 선뜻 내줄 마음이 있었겠는지 한번 따져 보면 알 거요 — 약간은 밀렵꾼, 약간은 막일꾼, 약간은 짐마차꾼, 약간은 건초꾼, 약간은 행상꾼, 돈 안 되고 말썽만 부르는 거의 모든 일을 조금씩 하면서, 나는 어른이 되었소. ‘나그네의 쉼터’라는 데서, 감자 무더기 밑에 턱까지 숨어 있던 탈영병 하나가 내게 글 읽는 법을 가르쳐 주었고, 한 번에 한 푼씩 받고 제 이름을 써 주던 떠돌이 거인 하나가 글 쓰는 법을 가르쳐 주었지. 이제 예전만큼 자주 갇히지는 않았지만, 그래도 자물쇠 쇠붙이는 여전히 내 몫만큼 닳게 했소.\n\n「이십 년도 더 전에 엡섬 경마장에서, 나는 한 사내와 알게 되었소. 만약 그자의 머리통이 지금 이 화로 받침대 위에 있다면, 나는 이 부지깽이로 바닷가재 집게발 부수듯 박살 내고 말 거요. 그자의 본명은 콤피슨이었소. 그리고 그자가 바로, 사랑하는 도련님, 어젯밤 내가 떠난 뒤에 당신이 친구분께 사실대로 말한 대로, 내가 도랑에서 두들겨 패던 그 사내요.\n\n「이 콤피슨이라는 자는 신사 행세를 했소. 사립 기숙학교에 다녔고 배운 것도 있었지. 말솜씨가 매끄러웠고, 상류층의 몸가짐에도 능했소. 인물도 잘났고. 큰 경마가 열리기 전날 밤, 나는 황야에서, 내가 잘 아는 어느 천막 술집에서 그자를 만났소. 내가 들어갔을 때 그자와 몇몇이 탁자 사이에 앉아 있었는데, 주인이(그자는 나를 알았고, 노름을 즐기는 위인이었지) 그자를 불러내며 말했소. ‘이 사람이 당신한테 쓸모가 있을 것 같소만.’ — 나를 두고 한 말이었지.\n\n「콤피슨, 그자는 나를 아주 유심히 뜯어보고, 나도 그자를 봤소. 그자는 시계와 시곗줄과 반지와 가슴 핀에다, 말쑥한 옷 한 벌을 갖추고 있었소.\n\n「‘겉모습으로 보아하니, 당신은 운이 없는 사람이로군.’ 콤피슨이 내게 말했소.\n\n「‘그렇소, 나리, 운이라곤 별로 누려 본 적이 없지요.’(나는 마지막으로 부랑죄 송치로 킹스턴 감옥에서 막 나온 참이었소. 다른 죄목일 수도 있었겠지만, 그건 아니었지.)\n\n「‘운은 바뀌는 법이지.’ 콤피슨이 말했소. ‘어쩌면 당신 운도 바뀌려나 보군.’\n\n「나는 말했소. ‘그랬으면 좋겠소. 바뀔 여지는 충분하니까.’\n\n「‘당신, 뭘 할 줄 아시오?’ 콤피슨이 물었소.\n\n「‘먹고 마시는 거요.’ 내가 말했소. ‘당신이 그 재료를 대 준다면 말이오.’\n\n「콤피슨은 웃더니, 다시 나를 아주 유심히 뜯어보고는, 내게 오 실링을 주고 다음 날 밤에 만나자고 약속했소. 같은 장소에서.\n\n「나는 다음 날 밤 같은 장소로 콤피슨을 찾아갔고, 콤피슨은 나를 제 부하이자 동업자로 거두었소. 그래, 우리가 동업하기로 한 콤피슨의 사업이란 게 뭐였느냐고? 콤피슨의 사업은 사기, 필적 위조, 훔친 지폐 유통, 그런 따위였소. 콤피슨이 제 머리로 꾸밀 수 있는 온갖 덫, 제 다리는 거기서 빼고, 이익은 챙기고, 다른 놈은 걸려들게 하는 그런 덫이 바로 콤피슨의 사업이었지. 그자는 쇠줄칼만큼도 인정이 없었고, 죽음처럼 차가웠으며, 앞서 말한 악마의 머리를 가진 자였소.\n\n「콤피슨과 한패인 자가 또 하나 있었는데, 아서라고 불렀소 — 세례명이 아니라 성이었지. 그자는 폐병을 앓고 있어서, 보기에 그림자나 다름없었소. 그자와 콤피슨은 몇 해 전에 어느 부유한 부인을 상대로 못된 짓을 벌여, 그걸로 큰돈을 한밑천 잡았더랬소. 한데 콤피슨이 노름과 도박에 빠져, 임금의 세금까지도 탕진할 위인이었지. 그래서 아서는 죽어 가고 있었소. 가난뱅이로, 헛것에 시달리며 죽어 가고 있었지. 콤피슨의 아내는(콤피슨이 걸핏하면 발길질하던 여자였소) 할 수 있을 때면 그자를 가엾이 여겼지만, 콤피슨은 그 무엇도, 그 누구도 가엾이 여기는 법이 없었소.\n\n「나는 아서를 보고 경계할 수도 있었으련만, 그러지 않았소. 그렇다고 내가 까다롭게 굴었던 척하진 않겠소 — 그래 봤자 무슨 소용이겠소, 사랑하는 도련님, 그리고 친구분? 그렇게 나는 콤피슨과 손을 잡았고, 그자 손아귀에서 한낱 변변찮은 도구 노릇을 했지. 아서는 콤피슨네 집 꼭대기 층에 살았는데(브렌트퍼드 근처였소), 콤피슨은 그자가 혹 병이 나아 일해서 갚을 때를 대비해, 숙식비를 빈틈없이 셈해 두었소. 한데 아서는 곧 그 셈을 다 치러 버렸지. 내가 그자를 처음 본 지 두세 번째쯤 되던 때였는데, 그자가 밤늦게 플란넬 잠옷 하나만 걸친 채, 머리는 온통 땀에 젖어, 콤피슨네 거실로 허둥지둥 뛰어 내려와 콤피슨의 아내에게 말했소. ‘샐리, 그 여자가 지금 위층에서 정말로 나랑 같이 있는데, 도무지 떼어 낼 수가 없어. 온통 하얀 옷을 입고,’ 그자가 말하기를, ‘머리엔 흰 꽃을 꽂고, 무섭도록 미쳐서, 팔에 수의를 걸치고는, 새벽 다섯 시에 그걸 내게 입히겠다는 거야.’\n\n「콤피슨이 말했소. ‘이런 멍청한 놈, 그 여자가 멀쩡히 살아 있는 몸이란 걸 모르나? 게다가 문으로 들어오지도, 창문으로 들어오지도, 계단을 올라오지도 않고서 어떻게 거기 위에 있단 말이야?’\n\n「‘어떻게 거기 있는지는 나도 몰라.’ 아서가 헛것에 무섭게 떨면서 말했소. ‘하지만 그 여자가 침대 발치 구석에 서 있다고, 무섭도록 미친 채로. 그리고 그 여자 심장이 부서진 데 — 당신이 부쉈잖아! — 거기서 핏방울이 떨어진단 말이야.’\n\n「콤피슨은 짐짓 거칠게 말했지만, 본래 늘 겁쟁이였소. ‘이 헛소리하는 병자랑 같이 위로 올라가 봐.’ 그자는 제 아내에게 말했소. ‘그리고 매그위치, 자네도 좀 거들어 주겠나?’ 한데 그자 자신은 결코 가까이 오지 않았지.\n\n「콤피슨의 아내와 나는 그자를 다시 침대로 데려갔는데, 그자는 무섭도록 헛소리를 해 댔소. ‘아, 저것 좀 봐!’ 그자가 외쳤소. ‘저 여자가 나한테 수의를 흔들고 있어! 안 보여? 저 눈 좀 봐! 저렇게 미친 꼴을 보는 게 끔찍하지 않아?’ 그러더니 외치기를, ‘저 여자가 그걸 내게 입힐 거야, 그럼 난 끝장이야! 저 여자한테서 그걸 치워, 치워 버려!’ 그러고는 우리를 붙들고, 그 여자한테 계속 말을 걸며 대꾸까지 해 대니, 나도 그 여자가 정말 보이는 것만 같았소.\n\n「콤피슨의 아내는 그자에게 이골이 난지라, 헛것을 떨쳐 내라고 술을 좀 먹였더니, 그자는 차츰 잠잠해졌소. ‘오, 그 여자가 갔어! 그 여자 간수가 데리러 왔던가?’ 그자가 물었소. ‘그래요.’ 콤피슨의 아내가 말했지. ‘그 여자를 가두고 빗장 채우라고 일렀어?’ ‘그럼요.’ ‘그리고 그 흉측한 물건도 치우라고?’ ‘그럼요, 그럼, 다 됐어요.’ ‘당신은 참 착한 사람이야.’ 그자가 말했소. ‘무슨 일이 있어도 날 떠나지 마, 그리고 고마워!’\n\n「그자는 다섯 시가 몇 분쯤 못 미쳤을 무렵까지 꽤 잠잠히 쉬고 있다가, 갑자기 비명을 지르며 벌떡 일어나 외쳤소. ‘여기 왔어! 또 수의를 들었어. 그걸 펼치고 있어. 구석에서 나오고 있어. 침대로 다가와. 날 붙들어, 둘이서 — 양쪽에서 한 명씩 — 그걸로 날 건드리지 못하게 해. 하! 이번엔 빗나갔어. 그걸 내 어깨에 못 걸치게 해. 그걸 둘러씌우려고 날 일으키지 못하게 해. 저 여자가 날 일으키고 있어. 날 눌러 둬!’ 그러더니 그자는 힘껏 제 몸을 일으키고는, 죽어 버렸소.\n\n「콤피슨은 그걸 양쪽에 다 잘된 일이라며 대수롭잖게 받아들였소. 그자와 나는 곧 일에 바빠졌는데, 그자는 먼저(어디까지나 교활한 위인이라) 내게 직접 책에 대고 맹세를 시켰소 — 바로 이 작고 검은 책에 대고 말이오, 사랑하는 도련님, 당신 친구분께 맹세시킨 그 책 말이오.\n\n「콤피슨이 꾸미고 내가 해치운 일들을 일일이 늘어놓지는 않겠소 — 그러자면 일주일은 걸릴 테니 — 다만 이것만 말해 두겠소, 사랑하는 도련님, 그리고 핍의 친구분. 그자는 나를 그물에 옭아매어, 제 검은 노예로 부려 먹었소. 나는 늘 그자에게 빚을 졌고, 늘 그자 손아귀에 잡혀 있었고, 늘 일에 매여 있었고, 늘 위험에 빠져들었지. 그자는 나보다 어렸지만, 약삭빨랐고, 배운 것도 있어서, 나보다 오백 배는 더 나았고 인정사정이라곤 없었소. 내가 그토록 모진 세월을 함께 보낸 마누라는 — 한데 잠깐! 나는 아직 그 여자 얘기는 꺼내지도 않았구려 — 」\n\n그는 마치 제 기억의 책에서 읽던 자리를 잃어버리기라도 한 듯, 어리둥절한 모양으로 주위를 둘러보았다. 그러더니 불 쪽으로 얼굴을 돌리고, 무릎 위에 두 손을 더 넓게 펴 놓았다가, 떼었다가, 다시 얹었다.\n\n「그 얘기까지 들어갈 건 없소.」 그는 다시 한번 둘러보며 말했다. 「콤피슨과 함께한 세월은, 내가 여태 겪은 가장 모진 세월에 거의 맞먹었소. 그 말로 다 한 셈이지. 내가 콤피슨과 함께 있을 적에, 혼자서 경범죄로 재판받았던 일을 도련님께 말했던가요?」\n\n나는 아니라고 대답했다.\n\n「그래!」 그가 말했다. 「내가 그랬소. 유죄 판결을 받았지. 혐의를 받고 잡혀간 거로 치자면, 그게 사오 년 동안 두세 번은 됐소. 한데 증거가 없었지. 마침내 나와 콤피슨은 둘 다 중죄로 송치되었소 — 훔친 지폐를 유통한 혐의로 — 그리고 그 뒤에 다른 혐의들도 줄줄이 있었지. 콤피슨이 내게 말합디다. ‘변론은 따로따로, 서로 연락하지 말 것.’ 그게 다였소. 그리고 나는 어찌나 비참하게 가난했던지, 등에 걸친 것만 빼고 가진 옷을 죄다 팔고 나서야 재거스를 살 수 있었소.\n\n「우리가 피고석에 세워졌을 때, 나는 무엇보다도 먼저, 콤피슨이 곱슬머리에 검은 옷에 흰 손수건을 갖춰, 얼마나 신사처럼 보이는지, 그리고 내가 얼마나 천한 비렁뱅이 꼴로 보이는지 알아챘소. 검사 측이 사건을 펼치며 증거를 미리 짤막하게 늘어놓았을 때, 나는 그 모든 게 내게는 얼마나 무겁게, 그자에게는 얼마나 가볍게 걸리는지 알아챘지. 증언대에서 증거가 제시될 때, 나는 늘 앞에 나서서 지목당할 수 있었던 게 나였고, 돈이 늘 건네진 게 나였고, 늘 그 일을 꾸미고 이익을 챙긴 것처럼 보인 게 나였다는 걸 알아챘소. 한데 변론에 들어가니, 그제야 그 수작이 더 또렷이 보입디다. 콤피슨의 변호사가 말하기를, ‘재판장님과 배심원 여러분, 여기 여러분 앞에 나란히 서 있는 두 사람이 있는데, 여러분의 눈에도 그 차이가 훤히 갈릴 것입니다. 한 사람은 더 젊고, 잘 자랐으니, 그에 걸맞게 다뤄질 것이요. 한 사람은 더 늙고, 못되게 자랐으니, 그에 걸맞게 다뤄질 것입니다. 한 사람, 더 젊은 쪽은 이런 거래에 거의 모습을 드러낸 적이 없고, 그저 의심만 받았을 뿐이며, 다른 한 사람, 더 늙은 쪽은 늘 거기 모습을 드러냈고 늘 그 죄가 들통났습니다. 이 일에 한 사람만 끼어 있다면 그게 누구인지, 또 두 사람이 끼어 있다면 어느 쪽이 훨씬 더 악질인지, 의심할 여지가 있겠습니까?’ 뭐 그런 따위였소. 그리고 인품 얘기에 이르러서는, 학교에 다닌 게 콤피슨 아니던가? 이런 지위 저런 지위에 오른 옛 동창들이 있는 게 그자 아니던가? 이런저런 클럽이며 단체에서 증인들에게 알려져 있고, 흠잡힐 데라곤 없던 게 그자 아니던가? 그리고 전에도 재판받은 적이 있고, 감화원이며 유치장에서 안 알려진 데가 없던 게 나 아니던가! 그리고 변론하는 데 이르러서는, 흰 손수건에 이따금 얼굴을 파묻어 가며 — 아! 게다가 연설 중에 시구절까지 섞어 가며 — 그자들에게 말할 수 있었던 게 콤피슨 아니던가? 그리고 ‘여러분, 제 옆에 있는 이 사람은 더없이 고약한 악당이올시다’라는 말밖에 못 한 게 나 아니던가? 그리고 평결이 내려졌을 때, 좋은 인품과 나쁜 친구를 잘못 만난 탓이라는 정상참작에다, 나에 대해 알 수 있는 정보는 죄다 넘긴 공로로 선처가 권고된 게 콤피슨 아니던가? 그리고 ‘유죄’라는 한마디밖에 못 들은 게 나 아니던가? 그리고 내가 콤피슨에게 ‘이 법정을 나가기만 해 봐라, 그 낯짝을 박살 내 주마!’라고 했을 때, 판사에게 보호를 청해 우리 사이에 간수 둘을 세우게 한 게 콤피슨 아니던가? 그리고 우리가 선고를 받을 때, 칠 년을 받은 게 그자고, 십사 년을 받은 게 나 아니던가? 그자는 잘될 수도 있었던 자라며 판사가 안타까워한 게 그자고, 더 험한 꼴을 보기 십상인, 난폭한 성정의 상습범이라고 판사가 본 게 나 아니던가?」\n\n그는 격한 흥분 상태로 스스로를 몰아넣었으나, 이내 그것을 억누르고, 두세 번 짧게 숨을 들이쉬고, 그만큼 자주 침을 삼키더니, 내게 손을 뻗으며 안심시키려는 투로 말했다. 「나는 처량해지지 않을 거요, 사랑하는 도련님!」\n\n그는 어찌나 몸이 달아올랐던지, 말을 잇기 전에 손수건을 꺼내 얼굴과 머리와 목과 손을 닦아야 했다.\n\n「나는 콤피슨에게 그 낯짝을 박살 내 주겠다고 말했고, 내 낯짝을 망할 신이 박살 내도 좋으니 기어이 해내겠다고 맹세했소. 우리는 같은 죄수선에 실렸지만, 나는 한동안 그자에게 다가가지 못했소. 무던히도 애를 썼는데 말이오. 마침내 나는 그자 뒤로 다가가 뺨을 쳐서, 그자를 돌려세워 제대로 한 방 박살 내려 했지. 그 순간 들켜서 붙잡히고 말았소. 헤엄치고 자맥질할 줄 아는, 영창에 도가 튼 자가 보기에 그 배의 영창은 그리 튼튼한 게 아니었소. 나는 뭍으로 달아나, 그 무덤들 사이에 숨어서, 그 안에 누워 모든 게 끝나 버린 자들을 부러워하고 있었지, 바로 그때 내 아들을 처음 봤던 거요!」\n\n그는 다정한 눈길로 나를 바라보았는데, 나는 그를 몹시 가엾이 여겨 왔건만도, 그 눈길에 다시금 그가 거의 혐오스럽게 느껴졌다.\n\n「내 아들 덕분에, 콤피슨도 그 습지에 나와 있다는 걸 알게 됐소. 내 영혼을 걸고 말하건대, 그자는 겁에 질린 나머지, 뭍에 오른 게 나라는 걸 모른 채, 나한테서 벗어나려고 달아난 게 아닌가 싶소. 나는 그자를 쫓아가 사냥했소. 그 낯짝을 박살 냈지. ‘그리고 이제,’ 내가 말했소, ‘내 한 몸 따위 아랑곳 않고, 내가 할 수 있는 가장 모진 짓으로, 너를 끌고 돌아가 주마.’ 정 그래야 했다면, 나는 그자 머리채를 끌고 헤엄쳐 가기라도 했을 거고, 군인들 없이도 그자를 배에 실어 보냈을 거요.\n\n「물론 그자는 끝까지 훨씬 유리했지 — 인품이 워낙 좋았으니까. 그자는 나와 내 살의에 시달려 반쯤 미쳐 버린 상태에서 달아난 거였고, 그래서 그자가 받은 벌은 가벼웠소. 나는 쇠고랑이 채워져 다시 재판에 부쳐졌고, 종신형을 선고받았소. 한데 종신토록 머물러 있진 않았지, 사랑하는 도련님, 그리고 핍의 친구분, 이렇게 여기 와 있으니 말이오.」\n\n그는 아까처럼 다시 제 몸을 닦더니, 호주머니에서 엉킨 담배 뭉치를 천천히 꺼내고, 단춧구멍에서 파이프를 뽑아, 천천히 채워 넣고는, 담배를 피우기 시작했다.\n「그자는 죽었습니까?」 잠시 침묵이 흐른 뒤 내가 물었다.\n\n「그가 죽었나요?」 나는 잠시 침묵한 뒤에 물었다.\n\n「누가 죽었다는 거요, 도련님?」\n\n「그자가 살아 있다면, 차라리 내가 죽었기를 바라고 있을 거요, 그건 틀림없소.」 사나운 눈빛으로 그가 말했다. 「그자 소식은 그 뒤로 한 번도 들은 적이 없소.」\n\n허버트는 연필로 책 표지에 무언가를 적고 있었다. 프로비스가 두 눈을 불에 둔 채 담배를 피우며 서 있는 동안, 허버트는 그 책을 슬며시 내 쪽으로 밀었고, 나는 거기 적힌 글을 읽었다.\n\n「젊은 해비셤의 이름이 아서였어. 콤피슨은 해비셤 양의 연인 행세를 하던 바로 그 사내야.」\n\n나는 책을 덮고 허버트에게 가볍게 고개를 끄덕인 뒤 책을 옆으로 치워 두었다. 그러나 우리 둘 다 아무 말도 하지 않은 채, 불가에 서서 담배를 피우는 프로비스를 바라보기만 했다.\n\n# Chapter XLIII.\n\n프로비스에게서 내가 본능적으로 움츠러드는 그 감정이 어느 만큼이나 에스텔라에게서 비롯된 것인지, 무엇하러 발걸음을 멈추고 따져 보겠는가? 마차 사무소에서 그녀를 만나기 전에 감옥의 얼룩을 씻어 내려 애쓰던 그때의 심경과, 자긍심과 아름다움을 갖춘 에스텔라와 내가 거두어 들인 돌아온 유형수 사이에 가로놓인 심연을 지금 곱씹는 심경을 비교하려고, 무엇하러 길 위에서 어정거리겠는가? 그런다고 가는 길이 더 평탄해질 것도 아니요, 끝이 더 나아질 것도 아니며, 그가 도움을 받을 것도, 내 처지가 덜어질 것도 아니다.\n\n그의 이야기가 내 마음속에 새로운 두려움을 낳았다. 아니, 정확히 말하자면 그의 이야기는 이미 거기 있던 두려움에 형체와 목적을 부여한 셈이었다. 만약 컴페이슨이 살아 있어 그가 돌아온 사실을 알아챈다면, 그 결과가 어떠하리라는 것은 내게 거의 의심의 여지가 없었다. 컴페이슨이 그를 죽도록 두려워한다는 사실은 두 사람 가운데 누구도 나만큼 잘 알지는 못했을 것이며, 그자에 대해 묘사된 바와 같은 인간이라면 밀고자가 되는 안전한 수단으로 두려운 원수에게서 영영 벗어나기를 망설일 리는 좀처럼 상상하기 어려웠다.\n\n에스텔라에 관해서는 단 한마디도 프로비스에게 입에 올린 적이 없었고, 앞으로도 결코 올리지 않으리라 — 나는 그렇게 결심했다. 그러나 나는 외국으로 떠나기 전에 에스텔라와 해비셤 양을 둘 다 만나야겠다고 허버트에게 말했다. 프로비스가 우리에게 자기 이야기를 들려준 그날 밤, 둘만 남게 되었을 때였다. 나는 이튿날 리치먼드로 나가 보기로 마음먹었고, 실제로 갔다.\n\n브랜들리 부인 댁에 찾아가 내가 왔음을 알리자, 에스텔라가 시골로 떠났다는 말을 전하려고 그녀의 하녀가 불려 나왔다. 어디로 갔느냐? 늘 그렇듯 새티스 하우스로 갔다. 늘 그렇지는 않다고 나는 말했다. 그녀가 나 없이 그곳에 간 적은 여태 한 번도 없었으니까. 언제 돌아오느냐? 그 대답에는 어딘가 말을 아끼는 기색이 감돌아 내 당혹감을 더욱 키웠는데, 하녀의 대답인즉 자기가 알기로는 그녀가 잠깐 동안만 돌아올 뿐이라는 것이었다. 나는 이 말에서 아무런 영문도 알아낼 수 없었으니, 다만 내가 아무 영문도 알아내지 못하게 하려는 의도였다는 것 외에는 알 길이 없었고, 나는 완전히 낙담한 채 다시 집으로 돌아왔다.\n\n프로비스가 집으로 돌아간 뒤(나는 늘 그를 데려다주었고, 늘 주위를 잘 살폈다) 허버트와 다시 밤에 의논한 끝에, 내가 해비셤 양에게 다녀올 때까지는 외국으로 떠나는 일에 관해 아무 말도 하지 않기로 결론을 내렸다. 그동안 허버트와 나는 따로따로 어떻게 말을 꺼내는 것이 가장 좋을지 궁리하기로 했다. 그가 의심스러운 감시를 받고 있는 듯해 두렵다는 핑계를 꾸며 댈지, 아니면 여태 외국에 나가 본 적이 없는 내가 여행을 가자고 제안할지 말이다. 우리는 둘 다 내가 무엇을 제안하든 그가 동의하리라는 것을 알고 있었다. 우리는 그가 지금의 위태로운 처지에서 여러 날 더 머무는 일은 생각조차 할 수 없다는 데 의견이 일치했다.\n\n이튿날 나는 조에게 다녀오기로 한 굳은 약속이 있는 척하는 비열한 짓을 저질렀다. 그러나 나는 조나 그의 이름에 대해서라면 거의 어떤 비열한 짓이든 저지를 수 있는 인간이었다. 내가 떠나 있는 동안 프로비스는 각별히 조심하기로 했고, 내가 맡았던 그를 돌보는 일은 허버트가 맡기로 했다. 나는 하룻밤만 자리를 비울 작정이었고, 돌아오는 대로 내가 더 큰 규모의 신사로 출발하기를 바라는 그의 조바심을 채워 주는 일을 시작하기로 했다. 그때 나는, 또 나중에 알고 보니 허버트 역시, 그 핑계를 빌려 — 이를테면 물건을 사들이러 간다거나 하는 식으로 — 그를 바다 건너로 데려가는 것이 상책이지 않을까 하는 생각이 들었다.\n\n이렇게 해비셤 양에게 다녀올 길을 마련해 놓고, 나는 아직 동이 트기도 전인 이른 아침 마차로 길을 나섰는데, 날이 밝아 올 무렵엔 어느덧 탁 트인 시골길 위에 나와 있었다. 그날은 거지처럼 절뚝거리고 흐느끼며 부들부들 떨면서, 군데군데 구름 조각과 안개 누더기를 두른 채 살금살금 기어오고 있었다. 보슬비 속을 달려 블루 보어 여관에 마차가 다다랐을 때, 이쑤시개를 손에 들고 마차를 구경하러 정문 아래로 나오는 사람이 누구였는가 하면, 바로 벤틀리 드러믈이었다!\n\n그가 나를 못 본 척했으므로, 나도 그를 못 본 척했다. 양쪽 다 어설프기 짝이 없는 시늉이었다. 더욱 어설펐던 것은, 우리 둘 다 그 커피실로 들어갔기 때문인데, 그는 거기서 막 아침 식사를 마친 참이었고 나는 거기서 내 아침을 주문했다. 그를 그 마을에서 보는 일은 내게 더없이 괴로웠으니, 그가 무엇 때문에 거기 왔는지 나는 너무도 잘 알고 있었기 때문이다.\n\n나는 시기가 한참 지난 얼룩투성이 신문을 읽는 척하며 내 식탁에 앉아 있었고, 그동안 그는 난롯불 앞에 서 있었다. 그 신문에 실린 지역 소식이라고 해 봐야, 마치 몹시 변칙적인 형태의 홍역에라도 걸린 듯 온통 뿌려져 얼룩진 커피, 피클, 생선 소스, 그레이비, 녹인 버터, 포도주 같은 외래의 물질만큼도 알아볼 수 없는 것이었다. 차츰 그가 난롯불 앞에 서 있다는 사실이 내게 엄청난 해코지처럼 느껴졌다. 그래서 나는 내 몫의 불을 차지하기로 마음먹고 일어섰다. 불을 쑤시려고 난로 쪽으로 다가갔을 때 나는 부지깽이를 집으려고 그의 두 다리 뒤로 손을 뻗어야 했지만, 그래도 여전히 그를 모르는 척했다.\n\n\"이거 일부러 모른 체하는 건가?\" 드러믈 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"아!\" 나는 부지깽이를 손에 든 채 말했다. \"당신이었군. 안녕하시오? 누가 불을 가리고 있나 궁금하던 참이었는데.\"\n\n그러고는 나는 사정없이 불을 쑤셔 댔고, 그러고 나서 어깨를 떡 벌리고 불에 등을 댄 채 드러믈 씨와 나란히 자리를 잡았다.\n\n\"방금 내려온 거요?\" 드러믈 씨가 어깨로 나를 슬쩍 밀어내며 말했다.\n\n\"그렇소.\" 나는 내 어깨로 그를 슬쩍 밀어내며 말했다.\n\n\"형편없는 곳이군.\" 드러믈이 말했다. \"당신 고향이라지, 아마?\"\n\n\"그렇소.\" 나는 수긍했다. \"당신네 슈롭셔하고 아주 비슷하다던데.\"\n\n\"전혀 안 비슷한데.\" 드러믈이 말했다.\n\n이때 드러믈 씨는 자기 장화를 내려다보았고 나는 내 장화를 내려다보았으며, 그런 다음 드러믈 씨는 내 장화를 보았고 나는 그의 장화를 보았다.\n\n\"여기 온 지 오래됐소?\" 나는 불자리를 한 치도 양보하지 않기로 작정하고 물었다.\n\n\"지겨워질 만큼은 됐소.\" 드러믈이 하품하는 척하면서도 똑같이 작정한 태도로 대꾸했다.\n\n\"오래 머물 거요?\"\n\n\"모르겠소.\" 드러믈 씨가 답했다. \"당신은?\"\n\n\"모르겠소.\" 내가 말했다.\n\n이때 나는 피가 짜릿하게 솟구치는 것을 느끼며, 만약 드러믈 씨의 어깨가 머리카락 한 올만큼이라도 더 자리를 요구했다면 내가 그를 창문 쪽으로 홱 밀쳐 버렸으리라는 것을, 또한 마찬가지로 내 어깨가 비슷한 요구를 했다면 드러믈 씨가 나를 가장 가까운 칸막이 자리로 홱 밀쳐 버렸으리라는 것을 알았다. 그가 휘파람을 조금 불었다. 나도 그렇게 했다.\n\n\"이 부근에 넓은 습지가 펼쳐져 있다지, 아마?\" 드러믈이 말했다.\n\n\"그렇소. 그게 어쨌다는 거요?\" 내가 말했다.\n\n드러믈 씨는 나를 보았다가 내 장화를 보더니, \"아!\" 하고는 웃었다.\n\n\"드러믈 씨, 뭐가 그리 우습소?\"\n\n\"아니.\" 그가 말했다. \"특별히 우스운 건 아니오. 나는 말을 타고 나가 보려는 참이오. 재미 삼아 그 습지를 탐험할 작정이거든. 그 안에 외진 마을들이 있다더군. 별난 작은 선술집들, 대장간들, 뭐 그런 것들 말이오. 웨이터!\"\n\n\"네, 손님.\"\n\n\"내 말 준비됐나?\"\n\n\"문 앞에 끌고 와 두었습니다, 손님.\"\n\n\"이봐. 잘 들어, 자네. 그 부인은 오늘 말을 타지 않으실 거야. 날씨가 안 좋으니까.\"\n\n\"알겠습니다, 손님.\"\n\n\"그리고 나는 식사하지 않겠네. 부인 댁에서 저녁을 들 테니까.\"\n\n\"알겠습니다, 손님.\"\n\n그러더니 드러믈은, 그 둔한 위인답지 않게, 살이 두툼한 얼굴에 오만한 승리감을 띠고 나를 흘끔 보았는데, 그것이 내 가슴을 후벼 파고 나를 어찌나 격분시켰던지, 나는 (이야기책 속의 도둑이 노부인을 안아 들었다는 식으로) 그를 두 팔로 안아다가 난롯불 위에 앉혀 버리고 싶은 충동이 일었다.\n\n우리 둘에게 분명한 사실이 하나 있었으니, 그것은 구원이 올 때까지 어느 쪽도 불자리를 내놓을 수 없다는 것이었다. 거기서 우리는 불 앞에 어깨를 떡 벌리고 어깨를 맞대고 발을 맞댄 채, 두 손을 등 뒤로 한 채 한 치도 물러서지 않고 버티고 서 있었다. 밖에서는 보슬비 속에 문간에 매인 말이 보였고, 내 아침 식사는 식탁 위에 차려졌으며, 드러믈의 식탁은 치워졌고, 웨이터가 내게 들라고 권했고, 나는 고개를 끄덕였으며, 우리 둘은 자리를 지키고 서 있었다.\n\n\"그 뒤로 그로브에 가 봤소?\" 드러믈이 말했다.\n\n\"아니.\" 내가 말했다. \"지난번에 갔을 때 핀치 회원들이라면 진절머리가 나도록 겪었으니까.\"\n\n\"그게 우리가 의견이 엇갈렸던 그때였나?\"\n\n\"그렇소.\" 나는 아주 짧게 대답했다.\n\n\"이런, 이런! 그 사람들이 당신을 어지간히 봐줬지.\" 드러믈이 빈정거렸다. \"당신이 성을 내지 말았어야 했어.\"\n\n\"드러믈 씨.\" 내가 말했다. \"당신은 그 문제에 관해 충고할 자격이 없소. 나는 성이 나도(그때 그랬다고 인정하는 건 아니지만) 술잔을 던지진 않거든.\"\n\n\"나는 던지지.\" 드러믈이 말했다.\n\n부글부글 끓어오르는 사나운 기운이 점점 짙어진 채로 그를 한두 번 흘끔 보고 나서, 나는 말했다.\n\n\"드러믈 씨, 나는 이 대화를 청한 적이 없고, 이게 유쾌한 대화라고도 생각지 않소.\"\n\n\"유쾌하지 않은 건 확실하지.\" 그가 어깨 너머로 거만하게 말했다. \"나는 이 대화에 대해 아무 생각도 안 하니까.\"\n\n\"그러니까.\" 나는 말을 이었다. \"당신만 괜찮다면, 앞으로 우리 어떤 식의 교류도 하지 말자고 제안하고 싶소.\"\n\n\"전적으로 내 생각도 그렇소.\" 드러믈이 말했다. \"내가 먼저 제안했을 법한, 아니, 더 그럴듯하게는 제안도 없이 그냥 해 버렸을 일이지. 하지만 성은 내지 마시오. 그것 말고도 이미 충분히 잃은 게 아니오?\"\n\n\"무슨 뜻이오?\"\n\n\"웨이터!\" 드러믈이 내게 대답하는 대신 그렇게 외쳤다.\n\n웨이터가 다시 나타났다.\n\n\"이봐, 자네. 그 젊은 아가씨가 오늘 말을 타지 않으신다는 것, 그리고 내가 그 젊은 아가씨 댁에서 저녁을 든다는 것, 잘 알아들었지?\"\n\n\"잘 알겠습니다, 손님!\"\n\n웨이터가 빠르게 식어 가는 내 찻주전자를 손바닥으로 만져 보고는 애원하듯 나를 쳐다본 뒤 나가 버리자, 드러믈은 내 쪽 어깨를 움직이지 않으려 조심하면서 주머니에서 시가를 꺼내 끝을 물어뜯었지만, 자리를 뜰 기색은 전혀 보이지 않았다. 숨이 막히도록 끓어오르던 나는, 한마디만 더 나아가면 에스텔라의 이름이 나올 수밖에 없다는 것을 느꼈는데, 그의 입에서 그 이름이 흘러나오는 것을 도저히 견딜 수 없었으므로, 마치 그 자리에 아무도 없는 듯 맞은편 벽을 돌처럼 굳은 눈으로 바라보며 억지로 침묵을 지켰다. 우리가 이 우스꽝스러운 자세로 얼마나 오래 머물렀을지는 알 수 없으나, 잘나가는 농부 셋이 — 내 생각엔 웨이터가 데려온 듯한데 — 외투 단추를 풀고 두 손을 비비며 커피실로 들어오는 통에, 그들이 불을 향해 돌진해 오자 우리는 어쩔 수 없이 자리를 비켜 줘야 했다.\n\n나는 그가 창문 너머로, 말갈기를 움켜쥐고 그 서투르고 거친 솜씨로 올라타서는, 옆걸음질로 뒷걸음질 치며 멀어져 가는 모습을 보았다. 그가 떠났구나 싶었는데, 그는 입에 문 시가에 불을 붙일 것을 깜빡 잊었다며 불을 가져오라고 외치며 되돌아왔다. 먼지 빛깔 옷을 입은 한 사내가 그가 원하는 것을 들고 나타났는데 — 그가 여관 마당에서 나왔는지, 길에서 나왔는지, 그 밖에 어디서 나왔는지 나로서는 알 수가 없었다 — 드러믈이 안장에서 몸을 굽혀 시가에 불을 붙이고는 커피실 창문 쪽으로 고갯짓을 하며 웃을 때, 내게 등을 돌린 그 사내의 구부정한 어깨와 텁수룩한 머리는 올릭을 떠올리게 했다.\n\n그자가 올릭이든 아니든 당장은 별로 신경 쓸 마음도 없을 만큼, 또 결국 아침 식사에 손댈 마음도 없을 만큼 몸도 마음도 잔뜩 상해 있던 나는, 얼굴과 두 손에서 날씨와 여행의 흔적을 씻어 내고는, 차라리 한 번도 발을 들이지 않았더라면, 한 번도 보지 않았더라면 내게 그토록 더 나았을 그 잊지 못할 낡은 집을 향해 길을 나섰다.\n\n# Chapter XLIV.\n\n화장대가 놓여 있고 벽의 양초들이 타오르던 그 방에서, 나는 해비셤 양과 에스텔러를 발견했다. 해비셤 양은 난롯가 가까운 긴 의자에 앉아 있었고, 에스텔러는 그녀의 발치 방석 위에 앉아 있었다. 에스텔러는 뜨개질을 하고 있었고, 해비셤 양은 그것을 바라보고 있었다. 내가 들어서자 두 사람 모두 눈을 들었고, 두 사람 모두 내게 일어난 변화를 알아차렸다. 나는 그것을 두 사람이 주고받은 눈빛에서 짐작했다.\n\n\"무슨 바람이,\" 해비셤 양이 말했다. \"너를 여기로 불어 보냈느냐, 핍?\"\n\n그녀가 나를 똑바로 바라보고는 있었지만, 나는 그녀가 다소 당황해 있음을 알아챘다. 에스텔러가 나를 바라보며 뜨개질을 잠깐 멈췄다가 다시 이어 가는 모습을 보면서, 나는 그녀의 손가락 움직임에서 마치 그녀가 수화 알파벳으로 내게 일러 주기라도 한 듯 또렷이 읽어 냈다. 그녀가 내가 진짜 후원자를 알아냈음을 감지했다는 것을.\n\n\"해비셤 양,\" 내가 말했다. \"저는 어제 에스텔러와 이야기하려고 리치먼드에 갔습니다. 그런데 무슨 바람이 그녀를 이리로 불어 보냈다는 걸 알고, 뒤따라왔습니다.\"\n\n해비셤 양이 세 번째인지 네 번째인지 내게 앉으라고 손짓했으므로, 나는 화장대 옆 의자에 앉았다. 그녀가 거기 앉아 있는 모습을 나는 자주 보아 왔다. 그 모든 폐허를 발치에, 그리고 주위에 둔 채, 그날 그곳은 내게 자연스러운 자리처럼 느껴졌다.\n\n\"에스텔러에게 할 말을, 해비셤 양, 곧 당신 앞에서 하겠습니다 — 잠시 후에요. 놀라실 것도 없고, 언짢으실 것도 없을 겁니다. 저는 당신이 의도하셨을 만큼이나 불행합니다.\"\n\n해비셤 양은 계속해서 나를 똑바로 바라보았다. 나는 뜨개질을 하는 에스텔러의 손가락 움직임에서 그녀가 내 말에 귀를 기울이고 있음을 알 수 있었다. 그러나 그녀는 눈을 들지 않았다.\n\n\"저는 제 후원자가 누구인지 알아냈습니다. 그것은 행운의 발견이 아니며, 명성으로든 지위로든 재산으로든 그 무엇으로든 결코 저를 부유하게 해 줄 것 같지도 않습니다. 그에 관해서는 더 말씀드릴 수 없는 이유들이 있습니다. 그것은 제 비밀이 아니라 다른 사람의 비밀이니까요.\"\n\n내가 에스텔러를 바라보며 어떻게 말을 이어 갈지 궁리하느라 한동안 잠자코 있자, 해비셤 양이 되풀이해 말했다. \"네 비밀이 아니라 다른 사람의 비밀이라. 그래서?\"\n\n\"처음에 저를 이곳으로 데려오게 하셨을 때, 해비셤 양, 그러니까 제가 저 너머 마을에 속해 있던 — 차라리 떠나지 말았어야 했다고 생각하는 그 마을에 — 그때 저는 정말로, 다른 어떤 운수 사나운 아이가 왔을 법한 모양으로 이곳에 온 거라고 생각합니다. 일종의 하인으로서, 어떤 욕구나 변덕을 채워 주고 그 대가를 받기 위해서요. 그렇지요?\"\n\n\"그래, 핍.\" 해비셤 양이 꾸준히 고개를 끄덕이며 대답했다. \"그랬지.\"\n\n\"그리고 재거스 씨가 —\"\n\n\"재거스 씨는,\" 해비셤 양이 단호한 어조로 내 말을 가로채며 말했다. \"그 일과는 아무 관계가 없고, 그 일에 대해 아무것도 몰랐다. 그가 내 변호사이고 또 네 후원자의 변호사이기도 한 것은 우연이야. 그는 수많은 사람들과 똑같은 관계를 맺고 있으니, 그런 일은 얼마든지 생길 수 있지. 어찌 됐든 간에, 그렇게 된 거고, 누가 일부러 꾸민 것이 아니다.\"\n\n거기까지는 그녀의 초췌한 얼굴에서 어떤 은폐도 회피도 없음을, 누구라도 알아볼 수 있었을 것이다.\n\n\"하지만 제가 그토록 오래 빠져 있던 그 오해에 빠졌을 때, 적어도 당신은 저를 그쪽으로 이끄셨지요?\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"그래.\" 그녀가 다시 꾸준히 고개를 끄덕이며 대답했다. \"내가 네가 그러도록 내버려 두었지.\"\n\n\"그게 친절한 일이었습니까?\"\n\"내가 누구냐,\" 해비셤 양이 지팡이로 바닥을 내리치며, 너무도 갑작스럽게 분노로 타올라 에스텔러가 놀라 그녀를 올려다볼 정도로 외쳤다. \"내가 도대체 누구이기에, 친절해야 한단 말이냐?\"\n\n그것은 해 봐야 부질없는 항변이었고, 나도 그런 말을 할 작정은 아니었다. 그녀가 이 격발 뒤에 골똘히 생각에 잠겨 앉아 있을 때, 나는 그렇다고 그녀에게 말했다.\n\n\"그래, 그래, 그래!\" 그녀가 말했다. \"또 뭐냐?\"\n\n\"이곳에서 옛날에 시중든 대가는 후하게 받았습니다.\" 나는 그녀를 달래려고 말했다. \"도제 수업을 받게 되었으니까요. 그리고 제가 이런 질문들을 드린 것은 오로지 저 자신이 알고 싶어서였습니다. 다음에 드릴 말씀은 다른 (그리고 좀 더 사심 없는 것이기를 바라는) 목적이 있습니다. 제 오해를 받아 주시면서, 해비셤 양, 당신은 — 벌하셨던 거지요. 시험하신 — 어쩌면 당신은 당신의 의도를 표현할 말이라면 무엇이든, 노여워 마시고 채워 주실 테지요 — 사욕을 채우려는 친척들을요?\"\n\n\"그랬지. 그야, 그들이 그렇게 되기를 자초했으니까! 너도 마찬가지였고. 내 사연이 어떠했기에, 그들에게든 너에게든 그러지 말아 달라고 애써 사정해야 한단 말이냐! 너는 네 덫을 스스로 놓은 거야. 나는 결코 그것을 놓지 않았다.\"\n\n그녀가 다시 잠잠해질 때까지 기다렸다가 — 이 역시 격렬하고 갑작스럽게 그녀에게서 터져 나온 것이었으므로 — 나는 말을 이었다.\n\n\"저는 당신의 친척 한 가족 사이로 던져졌고, 해비셤 양, 런던에 간 이후로 줄곧 그들과 함께 지냈습니다. 저는 그들이 저 자신만큼이나 솔직하게 그 망상에 빠져 있었음을 알고 있습니다. 그리고 이 말이 당신께 받아들여지든 아니든, 당신이 그것을 믿어 줄 마음이 있든 없든, 제가 이 말씀을 드리지 않는다면 저는 거짓되고 비열한 자가 될 겁니다. 만약 당신이 매슈 포켓 씨와 그의 아들 허버트를 너그럽고 올곧고 솔직하며, 음흉하거나 비열한 짓이라곤 할 줄 모르는 사람이 아니라고 여기신다면, 당신은 그 두 사람에게 깊이 부당한 일을 하시는 겁니다.\"\n\n\"그들은 네 친구들이지.\" 해비셤 양이 말했다.\n\n\"그들은 스스로 제 친구가 되었습니다.\" 내가 말했다. \"제가 자기들의 자리를 빼앗았다고 여겼을 때 말입니다. 그리고 새러 포켓과 조지애나 양과 커밀라 부인은 제 친구가 아니었지요.\"\n\n그들을 나머지 친척들과 대비시킨 것이, 기쁘게도, 그녀에게 그들의 인상을 좋게 만든 듯했다. 그녀는 잠시 나를 날카롭게 바라보더니 조용히 말했다.\n\n\"그들을 위해 무엇을 바라느냐?\"\n\n\"단지,\" 내가 말했다. \"당신이 그들을 나머지와 혼동하지 않으시기를 바랄 뿐입니다. 그들은 같은 핏줄일지 몰라도, 믿어 주십시오, 같은 본성을 지니지는 않았습니다.\"\n\n여전히 나를 날카롭게 바라보면서, 해비셤 양이 되풀이했다.\n\n\"그들을 위해 무엇을 바라느냐?\"\n\n\"보시다시피 저는 그리 교활하지가 못합니다.\" 나는 얼굴이 조금 붉어지는 것을 의식하며 대답했다. \"그래서 설령 감추고 싶어도, 제가 무언가를 바라고 있다는 것을 당신께 감출 수가 없군요. 해비셤 양, 만약 당신이 제 친구 허버트에게 평생토록 도움이 될 일을 해 줄 돈을 내어 주신다면 — 다만 그 일의 성격상 그가 모르게 이루어져야만 하는데 — 그 방법을 보여 드릴 수 있습니다.\"\n\n\"왜 그 사람이 모르게 해야 하느냐?\" 그녀가 나를 더 주의 깊게 살피려는 듯 두 손을 지팡이 위에 모으며 물었다.\n\n\"왜냐하면,\" 내가 말했다. \"제가 그 일을 직접 시작했기 때문입니다. 이 년도 더 전에, 그가 모르게요. 그리고 저는 들통나고 싶지 않습니다. 어째서 그 일을 끝낼 능력이 모자라게 되었는지는 설명드릴 수 없습니다. 그것은 제 것이 아니라 다른 사람의 것인 그 비밀의 일부니까요.\"\n\n그녀는 차츰 나에게서 눈을 거두어 난롯불로 돌렸다. 그 침묵 속에서, 그리고 천천히 사위어 가는 촛불에 비추어 보아 긴 시간처럼 여겨지는 동안 불을 지켜보던 그녀는, 붉은 석탄 몇 덩이가 무너지는 소리에 정신이 들어 다시 나를 바라보았다 — 처음에는 멍하니, 그러다가 차츰 주의를 모으면서. 그동안 내내 에스텔러는 뜨개질을 계속했다. 해비셤 양은 나에게 주의를 고정시킨 다음, 우리 대화에 아무런 끊김도 없었던 듯이 말했다.\n\n\"또 뭐냐?\"\n\n\"에스텔러.\" 나는 이제 그녀에게로 몸을 돌리며, 떨리는 목소리를 다잡으려 애쓰면서 말했다. \"당신은 내가 당신을 사랑한다는 걸 알지요. 내가 오래도록, 그리고 애틋하게 당신을 사랑해 왔다는 걸 알 거요.\"\n\n이렇게 말을 건네받자 그녀는 내 얼굴로 눈을 들었고, 손가락은 일감을 놀렸으며, 무표정한 얼굴로 나를 바라보았다. 나는 해비셤 양이 나에게서 그녀에게로, 그녀에게서 나에게로 눈길을 옮기는 것을 보았다.\n\n\"내 오랜 오해만 아니었다면 이 말을 더 일찍 했어야 했소. 그 오해가, 해비셤 양이 우리 둘을 짝지을 작정이라는 희망을 내게 품게 했지요. 당신이 말하자면 어쩔 도리가 없다고 생각하는 동안에는, 나는 이 말을 삼갔소. 하지만 이제는 해야겠소.\"\n\n무표정한 얼굴을 그대로 유지한 채, 손가락은 여전히 놀리면서, 에스텔러는 고개를 저었다.\n\n\"알아요.\" 나는 그 몸짓에 답하여 말했다. \"알아요. 당신을 내 것이라 부를 수 있으리란 희망은 내게 없소, 에스텔러. 나는 머지않아 내가 어찌 될지, 내가 얼마나 가난해질지, 어디로 가게 될지 알지 못하오. 그래도, 나는 당신을 사랑하오. 이 집에서 당신을 처음 본 그 순간부터 줄곧 당신을 사랑해 왔소.\"\n\n조금도 동요 없이 손가락을 분주히 놀리며 나를 바라보던 그녀는, 다시 고개를 저었다.\n\n\"해비셤 양이 자신이 한 일의 무게를 헤아렸더라면, 가엾은 한 소년의 감수성을 시험거리로 삼고, 이 모든 세월 동안 부질없는 희망과 헛된 추구로 나를 괴롭힌 것은 잔인한 일이었을 거요. 끔찍이도 잔인한 일이었지요. 하지만 그분은 그러지 않으셨다고 생각하오. 자신의 시련을 견뎌 내는 가운데, 그분은 내 시련을 잊으셨던 거라고 나는 생각하오, 에스텔러.\"\n\n나는 해비셤 양이 손을 가슴에 갖다 대고 거기 짚은 채, 에스텔러와 나를 번갈아 바라보며 앉아 있는 것을 보았다.\n\n\"아무래도,\" 에스텔러가 아주 차분하게 말했다. \"내가 이해할 수 없는 어떤 감정, 어떤 공상 — 뭐라고 불러야 할지 모르겠지만 — 그런 것들이 있나 봐요. 당신이 나를 사랑한다고 말할 때, 나는 그것이 하나의 말 형식으로서 무슨 뜻인지는 알아요. 하지만 그 이상은 모르겠어요. 당신은 내 가슴속 그 무엇에도 가닿지 못하고, 그 안의 그 무엇도 건드리지 못해요. 나는 당신이 하는 말에 도무지 관심이 없어요. 나는 이것을 당신에게 경고하려고 애써 왔지요. 자, 그러지 않았나요?\"\n\n나는 비참한 태도로 말했다. \"그랬소.\"\n\n\"그래요. 하지만 당신은 경고를 받아들이려 하지 않았어요. 내가 진심으로 하는 말이 아니라고 생각했으니까요. 자, 그렇게 생각하지 않았나요?\"\n\n\"당신이 진심일 리 없다고 생각했고, 또 그러기를 바랐소. 당신은, 그토록 젊고, 세상을 모르고, 아름다운데, 에스텔러! 그게 자연의 이치일 리 없잖소.\"\n\n\"그게 내 본성이에요.\" 그녀가 대답했다. 그러고는 그 말에 힘을 주어 덧붙였다. \"그게 내 안에서 만들어진 본성이지요. 내가 이만큼이나 말하는 건, 당신을 다른 모든 사람들과 크게 구별해서예요. 나는 그 이상은 할 수 없어요.\"\n\n\"벤틀리 드러믈이 여기 시내에 와 있고, 당신을 쫓아다닌다는 게 사실 아니오?\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"전적으로 사실이에요.\" 그녀가 더할 나위 없는 경멸이 담긴 무심함으로 그를 언급하며 대답했다.\n\n\"당신이 그를 부추기고, 그와 함께 말을 타고 나가며, 바로 오늘 그가 당신과 식사를 한다는 것도요?\"\n\n내가 그것을 안다는 데 그녀는 조금 놀란 듯했으나, 다시 대답했다. \"전적으로 사실이에요.\"\n\n\"당신이 그를 사랑할 리 없소, 에스텔러!\"\n\n처음으로 그녀의 손가락이 멈췄고, 그녀는 다소 성난 어조로 받아쳤다. \"내가 당신에게 뭐라고 했지요? 그런데도 당신은 여전히, 내가 하는 말이 진심이 아니라고 생각하나요?\"\n\n\"당신이 그와 결혼할 리 없잖소, 에스텔러?\"\n\n그녀는 해비셤 양 쪽을 바라보았고, 손에 일감을 든 채 잠시 생각에 잠겼다. 그러더니 말했다. \"사실대로 말하지 않을 까닭이 없겠지요? 나는 그와 결혼할 거예요.\"\n\n나는 얼굴을 두 손에 묻었으나, 그 말을 듣는 것이 내게 얼마나 큰 고통을 주었는지를 생각하면, 예상보다는 훨씬 잘 나 자신을 다스릴 수 있었다. 다시 얼굴을 들었을 때, 해비셤 양의 얼굴에는 너무도 섬뜩한 표정이 어려 있어서, 격정에 사로잡힌 황망함과 비탄 속에서도 그것이 내게 강하게 와닿았다.\n\n\"에스텔러, 사랑하는 에스텔러, 해비셤 양이 당신을 이 치명적인 걸음으로 이끌게 두지 마시오. 나를 영영 제쳐 두시오 — 당신이 이미 그렇게 했다는 걸 나는 잘 알지만 — 그래도 드러믈보다 더 가치 있는 사람에게 당신 자신을 주시오. 해비셤 양은 당신을 그에게 줌으로써, 당신을 흠모하는 훨씬 더 훌륭한 수많은 남자들에게, 그리고 진정으로 당신을 사랑하는 몇 안 되는 이들에게, 가할 수 있는 가장 큰 모욕과 상처를 가하는 거요. 그 몇 안 되는 이들 가운데에는, 비록 나만큼 오래 사랑하지는 못했어도 나만큼이나 애틋하게 당신을 사랑하는 사람이 하나 있을지도 모르오. 그 사람을 택하시오, 그러면 나는 당신을 위해 이 일을 더 잘 견딜 수 있을 거요!\"\n\n나의 간절함은 그녀 안에 어떤 경이를 불러일으켰는데, 만약 그녀가 자기 마음으로 나를 조금이라도 이해할 수 있었더라면 그것은 연민으로 물들었을 법한 경이였다.\n\n\"나는 그와 결혼할 거예요.\" 그녀가 다시, 한결 부드러운 목소리로 말했다. \"내 결혼 준비가 진행되고 있고, 나는 곧 결혼할 거예요. 어째서 당신은 내 양어머니의 이름을 끌어들여 모욕을 주려 하지요? 이건 내 자신의 행동이에요.\"\n\n\"당신 자신의 행동이라니, 에스텔러, 한낱 짐승 같은 자에게 당신 자신을 내던지는 게?\"\n\n\"그럼 내가 누구에게 나 자신을 내던져야 하지요?\" 그녀가 미소를 띠며 받아쳤다. \"내가 그에게 아무것도 주지 않는다는 걸 (사람이 그런 걸 느낀다면) 가장 빨리 느낄 그런 남자에게 나 자신을 내던져야 하나요? 자! 이미 끝난 일이에요. 나는 그럭저럭 잘 지낼 거고, 내 남편도 그럴 거예요. 당신이 말하는 이 치명적인 걸음으로 나를 이끌었다는 것에 관해 말하자면, 해비셤 양은 오히려 내가 기다리며 아직 결혼하지 말기를 바라셨어요. 하지만 나는 내가 살아온 삶에 지쳤어요. 그 삶에는 내게 매력이라곤 거의 없으니, 그것을 바꾸는 데 나는 기꺼이 응할 마음이에요. 더는 말하지 말아요. 우리는 결코 서로를 이해하지 못할 거예요.\"\n\n\"그런 비열한 짐승, 그런 멍청한 짐승을!\" 나는 절망에 빠져 다그쳤다.\n\n\"내가 그에게 축복이 될까 봐 걱정하진 말아요.\" 에스텔러가 말했다. \"나는 그런 게 되지 않을 테니까요. 자! 여기 내 손이 있어요. 우리는 이렇게 헤어지는 건가요, 당신, 몽상에 빠진 소년 — 아니 어른?\"\n\n\"오, 에스텔러!\" 나는 아무리 참으려 해도 그녀의 손 위로 쓰디쓴 눈물이 빠르게 떨어지는 가운데 대답했다. \"설령 내가 영국에 남아 다른 사람들과 어깨를 나란히 하고 고개를 들 수 있다 한들, 당신이 드러믈의 아내가 된 모습을 내가 어찌 볼 수 있겠소?\"\n\n\"부질없는 소리예요.\" 그녀가 대꾸했다. \"부질없는 소리. 이런 건 금세 지나갈 거예요.\"\n\n\"결코 그렇지 않소, 에스텔러!\"\n\n\"당신은 일주일이면 나를 생각에서 지워 버릴 거예요.\"\n\n\"생각에서 지운다니! 당신은 내 존재의 일부, 나 자신의 일부요. 이곳에 처음 온 이후로 — 그토록 당신이 그 가엾은 마음에 상처를 입혔던 그 거칠고 천한 소년이 온 이후로 — 내가 읽은 모든 글줄마다 당신이 있었소. 그 이후로 내가 본 모든 풍경마다 당신이 있었소 — 강 위에, 배의 돛 위에, 습지에, 구름 속에, 빛 속에, 어둠 속에, 바람 속에, 숲속에, 바다에, 거리에. 당신은 내 마음이 알게 된 모든 우아한 공상의 화신이었소. 런던에서 가장 튼튼한 건물들을 이루는 돌덩이들도, 당신의 존재와 영향이 그곳에서나 어디에서나 내게 그러했고 또 앞으로 그러할 만큼 더 실재하지도, 당신의 손으로도 더 옮길 수 없을 만큼 단단하지도 않소. 에스텔러, 내 생의 마지막 순간까지, 당신은 내 인격의 일부, 내 안의 작은 선의 일부, 악의 일부로 남을 수밖에 없소. 그러나 이 이별 속에서, 나는 당신을 오직 그 선과 결부시키겠소. 그리고 나는 언제나 당신을 충실히 그렇게 간직하겠소. 지금 내가 아무리 모진 고통을 느낀다 한들, 당신은 분명 내게 해악보다 훨씬 더 큰 선을 베풀어 주었을 테니까. 오, 신의 가호가 당신에게 있기를, 신이 당신을 용서하시기를!\"\n\n이 토막 난 말들을 어떤 불행의 황홀경 속에서 나 자신에게서 끄집어냈는지, 나는 모른다. 그 격정의 토로는 마치 속으로 난 상처에서 흘러나오는 피처럼 내 안에서 솟구쳐 올라 쏟아져 나왔다. 나는 그녀의 손을 입술에 대고 여운 어린 몇 순간을 머물렀고, 그렇게 그녀를 떠났다. 그러나 그 후로 줄곧 — 그리고 머지않아 더 강한 까닭으로 — 나는 기억했다. 에스텔러가 그저 믿기지 않는다는 듯한 경이로 나를 바라보는 동안, 손을 여전히 가슴에 얹은 채 유령 같던 해비셤 양의 모습이, 온통 섬뜩한 연민과 회한의 응시로 화한 듯 보였던 것을.\n\n다 끝나고, 다 가 버렸다! 너무나 많은 것이 끝나고 가 버려서, 내가 정문을 나설 때 낮의 빛은 들어갈 때보다 더 어두운 빛깔로 보였다. 한동안 나는 좁은 길과 샛길 사이로 몸을 숨겼고, 그러다가 런던까지 내내 걸어서 가기로 방향을 잡았다. 그 무렵 나는 어느 정도 정신을 차려, 여관으로 돌아가 그곳의 드러믈을 볼 수는 없다는 것, 마차에 앉아 누군가 말을 거는 것을 견딜 수 없다는 것, 나 자신을 위해 할 수 있는 일 가운데 기진맥진하도록 몸을 지치게 하는 것의 절반만큼이라도 좋은 일은 없다는 것을 헤아릴 수 있게 되었다.\n\n내가 런던교를 건넌 것은 자정이 지나서였다. 그 무렵 강의 미들섹스 쪽 기슭 가까이서 서쪽으로 뻗어 있던 거리들의 좁고 뒤얽힌 골목들을 따라가다 보니, 템플로 가는 가장 손쉬운 길은 강가 바로 옆, 화이트프라이어스를 통과하는 것이었다. 나는 내일이나 되어야 올 사람으로 예정되어 있었지만, 열쇠를 가지고 있었으므로, 허버트가 잠자리에 들었다면 그를 깨우지 않고 나도 잠자리에 들 수 있을 터였다.\n\n템플이 닫힌 뒤 그 화이트프라이어스 문으로 내가 들어오는 일은 좀처럼 없었던 데다, 내가 진흙투성이에 몹시 지쳐 있었으므로, 야간 수위가 내가 지나가도록 문을 조금 열어 둔 채 나를 매우 주의 깊게 살피는 것을 나는 언짢게 여기지 않았다. 그의 기억을 돕기 위해 나는 내 이름을 댔다.\n\n\"확실치는 않았습니다만, 그러시리라 생각했지요. 여기 쪽지가 있습니다, 손님. 이걸 가져온 심부름꾼이 말하길, 제 등불로 이걸 읽어 주시면 고맙겠다더군요.\"\n\n그 부탁에 몹시 놀라며, 나는 쪽지를 받아 들었다. 그것은 필립 핍 귀하 앞으로 되어 있었고, 수신인 표기 맨 위에는 \"이것을, 여기서, 읽으십시오\"라는 말이 적혀 있었다. 수위가 등불을 들어 비추는 가운데 나는 그것을 펼쳤고, 안에서 웨믹의 필체로 적힌 글을 읽었다.\n\"집에 가지 마시오.\"\n\n# Chapter XLV.\n\n경고문을 읽자마자 나는 템플 정문에서 발길을 돌려 한껏 서둘러 플리트 스트리트로 갔고, 거기서 늦은 시각의 삯마차를 잡아타고 코번트 가든의 허맘스로 향했다. 그 시절에는 한밤중 어느 시각에라도 그곳에서 잠자리를 구할 수 있었으니, 객실 담당자는 늘 열려 있는 쪽문으로 나를 들여보내고, 선반 위에 차례로 놓인 양초 중 다음 것에 불을 붙여, 명부에 적힌 차례대로 다음 침실로 곧장 나를 안내했다. 그곳은 건물 뒤편 일층에 자리한 일종의 지하실 같은 방이었는데, 그 안에는 사주식 침대라는 폭군 같은 괴물이 방 전체를 가로질러 떡 버티고 서서, 제멋대로 뻗은 다리 하나를 벽난로 속에 들이밀고 또 하나를 출입구에 들이민 채, 가엾고 자그마한 세면대를 자못 거룩하고 정의로운 태도로 짓누르고 있었다.\n\n내가 야간등을 부탁했던 터라, 객실 담당자는 나를 두고 나가기 전에 그 미덕 넘치던 시절의 유서 깊고 정통적인 골풀 양초를 가져다주었다. 그것은 마치 지팡이의 유령 같은 물건이어서, 건드리기만 하면 곧장 허리가 부러져 버렸고, 어떤 것에도 결코 불을 붙일 수 없었으며, 둥근 구멍이 숭숭 뚫린 높다란 양철 탑 밑바닥에 독방에 갇히듯 놓여 있었으니, 그 구멍들이 벽에 빤히 뜬 눈처럼 잠 못 드는 무늬를 드리웠다. 침대에 들어가 발이 부르트고 지치고 비참한 몸으로 누워 있노라니, 나는 내 두 눈을 감을 수 없는 것이, 이 어리석은 아르고스의 눈을 감길 수 없는 것과 다를 바 없음을 깨달았다. 그리하여 밤의 음울함과 죽음 같은 정적 속에서, 우리는 서로를 빤히 마주 보았다.\n\n얼마나 서글픈 밤이었던가! 얼마나 불안하고, 얼마나 음산하며, 얼마나 길었던가! 방 안에는 차가운 검댕과 뜨거운 먼지가 뒤섞인, 손님을 반기지 않는 냄새가 감돌았다. 그리고 머리맡 침대 덮개의 구석구석을 올려다보면서, 나는 푸줏간에서 날아온 쇠파리며, 시장에서 기어든 집게벌레며, 시골에서 들어온 굼벵이가 얼마나 많이 저 위에 매달려 다음 여름을 기다리며 숨죽이고 있을까 생각했다. 그러다 보니 그것들 중 어느 하나라도 굴러떨어지지나 않을까 하는 생각에 이르렀고, 이내 내 얼굴 위로 무언가 가볍게 떨어지는 듯한 느낌이 들었다. 등을 타고 기어오르는 다른, 더욱 거북한 것들의 접근을 떠올리게 하는, 불쾌하기 짝이 없는 상념의 전환이었다. 한동안 잠 못 이루고 누워 있자니, 정적이 잔뜩 품고 있는 그 기묘한 목소리들이 차츰 들려오기 시작했다. 벽장은 속삭이고, 벽난로는 한숨짓고, 자그마한 세면대는 똑딱거렸으며, 서랍장 속에서는 이따금 기타 줄 하나가 울렸다. 거의 같은 무렵, 벽 위의 그 눈들은 새로운 표정을 띠었고, 빤히 뜬 둥근 눈마다 집에 가지 마라라고 쓰인 것이 보였다.\n\n밤의 온갖 망상과 밤의 온갖 소음이 내게 몰려들어도, 그것들은 결코 이 집에 가지 마라를 물리치지 못했다. 그것은 마치 육체의 고통이 그러하듯, 내가 무엇을 생각하든 그 속에 얽혀 들었다. 얼마 전 나는 신문에서, 어느 알려지지 않은 신사가 밤중에 허맘스에 와서 잠자리에 들었다가 스스로 목숨을 끊고는 이튿날 아침 피투성이로 뒹군 채 발견되었다는 기사를 읽은 적이 있었다. 그자가 바로 이 나의 지하실 같은 방을 썼던 게 틀림없다는 생각이 머리에 떠올랐고, 나는 침대에서 일어나 핏자국 같은 것이 없는지 직접 확인했다. 그러고는 문을 열고 복도를 내다보며, 객실 담당자가 졸고 있으리라 짐작되는 저 멀리 등불의 동행으로 마음을 달랬다. 그러나 이 모든 와중에도, 어째서 내가 집에 가서는 안 되는지, 집에서 무슨 일이 일어났는지, 언제 집에 가야 하는지, 프로비스는 집에서 무사한지 하는 의문들이 어찌나 분주히 내 머릿속을 차지하고 있던지, 다른 어떤 주제가 들어설 자리가 더는 없으리라 여겨질 정도였다. 에스텔라를 떠올리며, 그날 우리가 영영 작별했음을, 그 작별의 온갖 정황과 그녀의 갖가지 표정과 말투, 뜨개질하던 손가락의 움직임을 되새길 때조차도, 나는 여기서고 저기서고 어디서고 그 경고를, 집에 가지 마라를 좇고 있었다. 마침내 심신이 완전히 소진되어 깜박 졸음에 빠져들자, 그것은 내가 활용해야 할 거대하고 어렴풋한 동사가 되었다. 명령법 현재 시제로, 너는 집에 가지 마라, 그로 하여금 집에 가지 못하게 하라, 우리 집에 가지 말자, 너희는 집에 가지 마라, 그들로 하여금 집에 가지 못하게 하라. 그다음에는 가능법으로, 나는 집에 가서는 안 되고 갈 수도 없다, 나는 집에 가지 않을지도, 갈 수 없을지도, 가려 하지 않을지도, 가서는 안 될지도 모른다. 그러다 결국 정신이 산란해지는 것을 느끼고는, 베개 위로 몸을 뒤척이며 벽에 빤히 뜬 둥근 눈들을 다시 바라보았다.\n\n나는 일곱 시에 깨워 달라는 당부를 남겨 두었다. 다른 누구를 만나기에 앞서 웨믹부터 만나야 함이 분명했고, 또한 이번 일이 오직 그의 월워스식 감정만을 취할 수 있는 경우라는 것 역시 분명했기 때문이다. 그토록 비참한 밤을 보낸 방에서 나오게 된 것은 다행한 일이었고, 불편한 잠자리에서 화들짝 깨어나는 데 문을 두 번 두드릴 필요도 없었다.\n\n여덟 시에 그 성의 흉벽이 내 눈앞에 솟아올랐다. 마침 어린 하녀가 따끈한 빵 두 개를 들고 그 요새로 들어가던 참이라, 나는 그녀와 동행하여 뒷문을 지나 도개교를 건넜고, 그리하여 별다른 기별 없이, 자신과 노친을 위해 차를 끓이고 있던 웨믹 앞에 이르렀다. 열린 문 너머로 침대에 누운 노친의 모습이 멀찍이 보였다.\n\n\"이런, 핍 씨!\" 웨믹이 말했다. \"그래도 시내로 돌아오셨군요?\"\n\n\"네.\" 나는 대답했다. \"하지만 집에는 가지 않았습니다.\"\n\n\"잘하셨습니다.\" 그가 두 손을 비비며 말했다. \"혹시나 해서 템플 정문마다 쪽지를 하나씩 남겨 두었지요. 어느 문으로 오셨습니까?\"\n나는 어느 문이었는지 말해 주었다.\n\n\"오늘 중에 나머지 문들을 돌아 그 쪽지들을 없애야겠군요.\" 웨믹이 말했다. \"되도록이면 문서로 된 증거는 결코 남기지 않는 게 좋은 원칙입니다. 그게 언제 들이밀어질지 모르니까요. 한 가지 실례를 무릅쓰겠습니다. 노친께 드릴 이 소시지를 좀 구워 주시겠습니까?\"\n\n나는 기꺼이 그러겠다고 말했다.\n\n\"그럼 너는 네 일을 보러 가도 되겠다, 메리 앤.\" 웨믹이 어린 하녀에게 말했다. \"그러면 우리끼리만 남게 되는 셈이지요, 안 그렇습니까, 핍 씨?\" 하녀가 사라지자 그는 눈을 찡긋하며 덧붙였다.\n\n나는 그의 우정과 신중함에 고마움을 표했고, 내가 노친의 소시지를 굽고 그가 노친의 빵 속살에 버터를 바르는 동안, 우리의 대화는 낮은 목소리로 이어졌다.\n\n\"자, 핍 씨, 아시다시피,\" 웨믹이 말했다. \"당신과 나는 서로를 이해하고 있지요. 우리는 지금 사사로운 개인 자격으로 있는 것이고, 또 우리는 오늘 이전에도 은밀한 거래를 함께한 적이 있습니다. 공적인 감정은 별개의 것이지요. 우리는 공무 바깥에 있는 겁니다.\"\n\n나는 진심으로 동의했다. 어찌나 긴장했던지, 나는 이미 노친의 소시지를 횃불처럼 활활 태워 버려 입으로 불어 꺼야 했다.\n\n\"어제 아침에 우연히 들었습니다.\" 웨믹이 말했다. \"언젠가 당신을 데려간 적이 있는 어떤 장소에 있다가 말이지요. 당신과 나 사이라 해도, 피할 수 있다면 이름은 거론하지 않는 편이 좋겠습니다만.\"\n\n\"거론하지 않는 편이 훨씬 낫지요.\" 내가 말했다. \"무슨 말씀인지 알겠습니다.\"\n\n\"어제 아침에 우연히 거기서 들었는데,\" 웨믹이 말했다. \"전적으로 식민지와 무관한 일에 종사한다고는 할 수 없고, 또 운반 가능한 재산을 지니지 않았다고도 할 수 없는 어떤 인물이 말입니다. 그게 정말 누구인지는 나도 모릅니다만, 이 인물의 이름은 거론하지 않기로 하지요.\"\n\n\"그럴 필요 없습니다.\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"꽤 많은 사람들이 가는, 세상 어느 한 지역에서 약간의 소동을 일으켰다는 겁니다. 그곳에 가는 자들이 늘 제 좋아서 가는 것도 아니고, 또 정부의 비용과 전혀 무관하지도 않은 곳이지요.\"\n\n그의 얼굴을 살피느라, 나는 노친의 소시지를 그야말로 불꽃놀이로 만들어 버려서 나 자신의 주의력은 물론 웨믹의 주의력까지 크게 흩뜨려 놓았으니, 그 점에 대해 사과했다.\n\n\"바로 그곳에서 사라져, 그 일대에서 더는 소식이 들리지 않게 됨으로써 말이지요. 그로 인해,\" 웨믹이 말했다. \"추측이 일고 갖가지 억측이 세워졌다는 겁니다. 또 들으니, 템플 가든 코트에 있는 당신의 거처가 감시당해 왔으며, 앞으로도 다시 감시당할 수 있다더군요.\"\n\n\"누구한테요?\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"그건 파고들지 않는 게 좋겠습니다.\" 웨믹이 둘러대며 말했다. \"공적인 책무와 충돌할 수도 있으니까요. 나는 그 말을, 같은 장소에서 그동안 다른 기이한 일들을 들어 온 것처럼 들었을 뿐입니다. 입수한 정보를 근거로 당신에게 말하는 게 아니라, 그저 들은 겁니다.\"\n\n그는 그렇게 말하면서 내게서 굽는 포크와 소시지를 받아 들고는, 작은 쟁반 위에 노친의 아침 식사를 정갈하게 차렸다. 그것을 노친 앞에 놓기에 앞서, 그는 깨끗한 흰 천을 들고 노친의 방으로 들어가 그 노신사의 턱 밑에 그것을 매어 주고, 몸을 받쳐 일으켜 세우고, 나이트캡을 한쪽으로 비스듬히 씌워 자못 멋들어진 분위기를 만들어 주었다. 그러고는 더없이 정성스레 아침 식사를 그 앞에 차려 놓고 말했다. \"이만하면 됐지요, 노친?\" 그러자 명랑한 노친이 대답했다. \"됐다, 존, 얘야, 됐어!\" 노친이 남 앞에 내놓을 만한 차림새가 아니어서 보이지 않는 존재로 여겨야 한다는 무언의 양해가 있는 듯했으므로, 나는 이 모든 일을 전혀 모르는 척 꾸몄다.\n\n\"내 거처에서 나를 감시한다는 이 일은(나도 한번 의심할 만한 까닭이 있었습니다만),\" 웨믹이 돌아왔을 때 내가 그에게 말했다. \"방금 당신이 언급한 그 인물과 떼어 놓을 수 없는 일이지요, 그렇습니까?\"\n\n웨믹은 자못 심각한 표정을 지었다. \"그것까지는 내 직접 아는 바로 단언할 수 없습니다. 그러니까, 처음부터 그랬다고는 단언할 수 없다는 말이지요. 하지만 그것은 그렇거나, 그렇게 될 것이거나, 그렇게 될 위험이 다분합니다.\"\n\n그가 리틀 브리튼에 대한 충심에 매여 자기가 할 수 있는 만큼 다 말하지는 못하고 있음을 알았고, 또 그가 이만큼 말해 주려고 얼마나 자기 본분에서 벗어나 애쓰고 있는지 고맙게 헤아렸던 터라, 나는 그를 다그칠 수 없었다. 다만 나는 난롯불 앞에서 잠시 곰곰이 생각한 뒤, 그에게 한 가지 질문을 하고 싶다고 말했다. 답하든 답하지 않든 옳다고 여기는 대로 하시라고, 그리고 그가 택하는 길이 옳으리라 믿는다고 덧붙였다. 그는 아침 식사를 멈추고, 두 팔을 포갠 채 셔츠 소매를 집어 올리며(실내에서 편안하게 지내는 그의 방식이란 외투 없이 앉아 있는 것이었다), 내게 한 번 고개를 끄덕여 질문을 해 보라는 뜻을 내비쳤다.\n\n\"본명이 콤피슨인 평판 나쁜 사내에 대해 들어 보셨지요?\"\n그는 또 한 번 고개를 끄덕여 답했다.\n\n\"그자가 살아 있습니까?\"\n\n또 한 번 고개를 끄덕였다.\n\n\"런던에 있습니까?\"\n\n그는 또 한 번 고개를 끄덕이고는, 우체통 같은 입을 한껏 오므렸다가, 마지막으로 한 번 더 고개를 끄덕이고 다시 아침 식사를 이어 갔다.\n\n\"자,\" 웨믹이 말했다. \"질문은 끝났으니\" 하고, 그는 내가 새겨듣도록 이 말을 힘주어 되풀이했다. \"내가 들은 바를 듣고 나서 한 일에 대해 말하지요. 나는 당신을 찾으러 가든 코트에 갔습니다. 당신이 없기에, 허버트 씨를 찾으러 클래리커 상회로 갔지요.\"\n\n\"그래서 그를 만나셨습니까?\" 나는 몹시 불안해하며 말했다.\n\n\"만났지요. 이름은 일절 거론하지 않고 세세한 내막도 들추지 않은 채, 그에게 이렇게 일러두었습니다. 만약 누군가가, 톰이든 잭이든 리처드든, 그 거처 근처나 바로 이웃에 어슬렁거리는 걸 그가 알고 있다면, 당신이 자리를 비운 동안 그 톰이든 잭이든 리처드든 어디 멀찍이 치워 두는 게 좋겠다고 말입니다.\"\n\n\"그가 어떻게 해야 할지 몹시 난감해했겠군요?\"\n\n\"난감해하긴 했지요. 게다가 지금 당장 톰이든 잭이든 리처드든 너무 멀리 치워 두려 드는 건 안전하지 않다고 내가 의견을 밝혔으니 더욱 그러했습니다. 핍 씨, 한 가지 말씀드리지요. 지금 같은 상황에서는, 일단 들어와 있기만 하다면 대도시만 한 곳이 없습니다. 너무 일찍 몸을 드러내지 마십시오. 바짝 엎드려 계세요. 사정이 누그러질 때까지 기다렸다가, 바깥으로 나서십시오. 외국 공기를 쐬러 가는 것이라 해도 말이지요.\"\n\n나는 그 값진 충고에 고마움을 표하고, 허버트가 어떻게 했는지 물었다.\n\n\"허버트 씨는,\" 웨믹이 말했다. \"반 시간쯤 넋이 나가 있다가 한 가지 방책을 떠올렸습니다. 그가 비밀이라며 내게 일러 주기를, 자기가 어느 젊은 아가씨와 사귀고 있는데, 당신도 분명 알고 계시겠지만, 그 아가씨에게는 자리보전한 아버지가 계시다는 겁니다. 그 아버지는 한때 사무장 일을 하던 분이라, 강을 오르내리는 배들을 내다볼 수 있는 활처럼 굽은 내닫이창 곁 침대에 누워 지낸다더군요. 당신도 그 젊은 아가씨와 아마 아는 사이겠지요?\"\n\n\"직접 아는 사이는 아닙니다.\" 내가 말했다.\n\n사실인즉, 그녀는 나를 두고 허버트에게 아무 보탬도 안 되는 돈만 많이 드는 친구라며 못마땅해했고, 허버트가 처음 나를 그녀에게 소개하겠다고 했을 때 그 제안을 어찌나 미적지근하게 받아들였던지, 허버트는 내가 그녀와 인사를 나누기까지 얼마간 시간을 두는 게 좋겠다는 뜻으로 그 사정을 내게 털어놓을 수밖에 없었던 것이다. 내가 몰래 허버트의 앞날을 돕기 시작한 뒤로, 나는 이 일을 명랑한 달관으로 견딜 수 있었다. 그들 두 사람, 그와 그의 약혼녀로서도 당연히 둘만의 만남에 제삼자를 끼워 넣고 싶어 하지 않았을 터였다. 그리하여 비록 내가 클라라의 마음속에서 평판이 높아졌다는 말을 들었고, 또 그 젊은 아가씨와 내가 오래도록 허버트를 통해 꼬박꼬박 안부와 인사를 주고받기는 했어도, 나는 그녀를 한 번도 본 적이 없었다. 어쨌거나 나는 이런 세세한 사정으로 웨믹을 번거롭게 하지는 않았다.\n\n\"그 내닫이창이 달린 집이,\" 웨믹이 말했다. \"라임하우스와 그리니치 사이의 그 풀 강가에 있는데, 듣자 하니 가구가 갖춰진 위층을 세놓고 있는 아주 점잖은 과부가 꾸려 가는 집이랍니다. 그래서 허버트 씨가 내게 묻기를, 그곳을 톰이든 잭이든 리처드든의 임시 거처로 삼는 게 어떻겠느냐는 것이었지요. 그런데 나는 그곳을 아주 마음에 들어 했습니다. 세 가지 이유에서였는데, 그것을 말씀드리지요. 즉, 첫째. 그곳은 당신이 다니는 길과는 전혀 동떨어진 곳이고, 크고 작은 거리들이 흔히 몰린 데서도 멀찍이 떨어져 있습니다. 둘째. 당신이 직접 그 근처에 가지 않고도, 허버트 씨를 통해 톰이든 잭이든 리처드든의 안위를 늘 전해 들을 수 있지요. 셋째. 얼마 뒤 신중을 기하는 게 마땅한 때가 와서, 만약 당신이 톰이든 잭이든 리처드든을 외국으로 가는 우편선에 슬쩍 태우고자 한다면, 거기 바로 — 채비가 되어 있는 셈이지요.\"\n\n이런 고려들에 적잖이 마음이 놓인 나는, 웨믹에게 거듭거듭 고마움을 표하고 말을 이어 달라 청했다.\n\n\"자, 그래서 말입니다! 허버트 씨는 그 일에 흔쾌히 몸을 던졌고, 어젯밤 아홉 시 무렵에는 톰이든 잭이든 리처드든을 — 그게 누구든 간에, 당신이나 나나 알 필요는 없습니다만 — 아주 성공적으로 거처에 들여놓았습니다. 예전 하숙에서는 그가 도버로 불려 갔다고 알려졌고, 실제로 그를 도버 가도로 데리고 내려갔다가 거기서 빠져나오게 한 거지요. 그런데 이 모든 일의 또 다른 큰 이점은, 당신 없이 해치워졌다는 데 있습니다. 그래서 만약 누군가가 당신의 동태에 신경을 쓰고 있었다면, 당신은 몇십 마일이나 떨어진 데서 아주 딴 일에 매여 있는 걸로 알려져 있을 게 틀림없거든요. 이것이 의심을 딴 데로 돌리고 헷갈리게 만들지요. 같은 이유로 나는, 설령 당신이 어젯밤 돌아온다 하더라도 집에는 가지 말라고 권했던 겁니다. 그래야 혼란이 더 보태지고, 당신에게는 그 혼란이 필요하니까요.\"\n\n아침 식사를 마친 웨믹은 여기서 시계를 들여다보더니, 외투를 걸치기 시작했다.\n\n\"자 이제, 핍 씨,\" 그가 두 손을 아직 소매에 꿴 채 말했다. \"나는 아마 내가 할 수 있는 일은 거의 다 한 것 같습니다. 하지만 앞으로 더 할 수 있는 일이 있다면 — 월워스의 관점에서, 또 철저히 사사로운 개인 자격으로 — 기꺼이 하겠습니다. 여기 주소가 있습니다. 집에 가기 전에 오늘 밤 이리로 가서, 톰이든 잭이든 리처드든이 무탈한지 직접 확인하셔도 해로울 건 없겠지요. 그러는 게 어젯밤 집에 가지 말아야 했던 또 하나의 이유이기도 합니다. 다만, 집에 다녀온 뒤에는 이리로 다시 오지 마십시오. 정말이지 언제든 환영입니다, 핍 씨.\" 이제 그의 두 손이 소매에서 나와 있었고, 나는 그 손을 맞잡고 흔들고 있었다. \"그리고 마지막으로 한 가지 중요한 점을 새겨 두시도록 하겠습니다.\" 그는 두 손을 내 어깨에 얹고는, 자못 엄숙한 속삭임으로 덧붙였다. \"오늘 저녁을 이용해 그의 운반 가능한 재산을 손에 넣으십시오. 그에게 무슨 일이 닥칠지 모릅니다. 그 운반 가능한 재산에는 아무 일도 일어나지 않게 하십시오.\"\n\n이 점에 관해서는 웨믹에게 내 뜻을 분명히 이해시키기를 도무지 단념한 터라, 나는 굳이 시도하지 않았다.\n\n\"시간이 다 됐군요.\" 웨믹이 말했다. \"나는 가 봐야겠습니다. 만약 어두워질 때까지 여기 머무는 것보다 더 급한 일이 없다면, 나로서는 그러시기를 권하겠습니다. 몹시 근심에 차 보이는군요. 노친과 함께 더없이 조용한 하루를 보내시면 좋을 겁니다 — 그분도 곧 일어나실 거고요 — 그리고 한 점쯤 — 그 돼지 기억하시지요?\"\n\n\"물론이지요.\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"네. 그놈을 한 점 드시는 것 말입니다. 당신이 구운 그 소시지가 그놈이었는데, 그놈은 어느 모로 보나 일등급이었지요. 그저 옛정을 봐서라도 한번 맛보십시오. 안녕히, 노친 어른!\" 그가 명랑하게 외쳤다.\n\n\"됐다, 존, 됐어, 얘야!\" 안에서 노인이 가느다란 목소리로 화답했다.\n\n나는 이내 웨믹의 난롯불 앞에서 잠이 들었고, 노친과 나는 하루 종일 그 앞에서 이래저래 잠에 빠져들며 서로의 벗이 되어 주는 즐거움을 누렸다. 점심으로는 돼지 허릿살에 그 영지에서 기른 푸성귀를 곁들여 먹었다. 그리고 졸음에 겨워 끄덕이지 못할 때면, 나는 좋은 뜻으로 노친에게 일부러 고개를 끄덕여 보였다. 날이 완전히 어두워지자, 나는 토스트를 구우려 불을 지피는 노친을 남겨 두고 자리를 떴다. 찻잔의 수효로 보나, 벽에 달린 그 자그마한 두 문을 향한 그의 눈길로 보나, 나는 스키핀스 양이 올 예정임을 짐작할 수 있었다.\n\n# Chapter XLVI.\n\n본문에 들어섰을 때는 이미 여덟 시를 알리는 종이 친 뒤였는데, 강가의 보트 건조공들과 돛대, 노, 도르래 만드는 이들이 남긴 나뭇조각과 대팻밥 냄새가 코를 찔렀으나 불쾌하지는 않았다. 다리 아래쪽, 풀 상류와 하류에 걸친 그 강변 일대는 내게 낯선 땅이었다. 그래서 강을 따라 내려가다 보니, 내가 찾던 곳이 짐작했던 자리에 있지 않았고, 도무지 찾기가 쉽지 않았다. 그곳은 칭크스 만의 밀폰드 둑이라 불렸는데, 칭크스 만을 찾는 데 내가 의지할 길잡이라고는 오래된 푸른 구리 밧줄 제조장밖에 없었다.\n\n내가 마른 선거에서 수리 중인 좌초선들 사이에서 얼마나 길을 잃었는지, 산산조각으로 부서지고 있던 낡은 배의 선체들이 얼마나 많았는지, 진창과 점액과 조수가 남긴 온갖 찌꺼기가 어떠했는지, 조선공들과 해체공들의 작업장이 어떠했는지, 몇 해 동안 쓰이지 않았으면서도 맹목적으로 땅을 물어뜯고 있던 녹슨 닻들이 어떠했는지, 쌓아 올린 통과 목재가 산악 지대처럼 이루어진 광경이 어떠했는지, 오래된 푸른 구리 제조장이 아닌 밧줄 제조장이 또 얼마나 많았는지는 따질 필요가 없겠다. 목적지에 몇 번이나 못 미쳤다가 또 그만큼 지나치기를 거듭한 끝에, 나는 뜻밖에도 어느 모퉁이를 돌아서서 밀폰드 둑에 이르렀다. 모든 사정을 헤아려 볼 때 그곳은 산뜻한 편의 장소였으니, 강에서 불어오는 바람이 몸을 돌릴 여유가 있는 곳이었다. 그 안에는 나무가 두세 그루 있었고, 허물어진 풍차의 그루터기가 있었으며, 오래된 푸른 구리 밧줄 제조장이 있었다. 그 길고 좁은 풍경은 달빛 속에서 땅에 박힌 일련의 나무 틀을 따라 더듬어 볼 수 있었는데, 그 틀들은 마치 나이가 들어 이가 거의 다 빠진 낡은 건초 갈퀴처럼 보였다.\n\n밀폰드 둑에 늘어선 몇 채의 기묘한 집들 가운데서, 정면이 나무로 되어 있고 삼층에 활꼴 창문(베이 창과는 다른, 별개의 것이다)이 달린 집을 골라, 나는 문에 붙은 명패를 보고 거기서 윔플 부인이라는 이름을 읽었다. 그것이 내가 찾던 이름이었으므로, 나는 문을 두드렸고, 유쾌하고 풍채 좋은 노부인이 나왔다. 그러나 부인은 곧 허버트에게 자리를 내주었으니, 허버트는 말없이 나를 응접실로 이끌어 들이고 문을 닫았다. 그토록 익숙한 그의 얼굴이 그토록 낯선 방과 동네에 아주 편안하게 자리 잡고 있는 것을 보는 것은 묘한 느낌이었다. 그리고 나는 어느새 그를 바라보고 있었는데, 그것은 마치 유리와 도자기가 든 모퉁이 찬장이며, 벽난로 선반 위의 조개껍데기며, 벽에 걸린 채색 판화들을 바라보는 것과 흡사했다. 그 판화들은 쿡 선장의 죽음과, 진수식 광경과, 윈저궁 테라스에서 궁정 마부의 가발과 가죽 반바지와 장화 차림을 한 국왕 조지 삼세 폐하를 그린 것이었다.\n\n\"다 잘되었네, 핸들.\" 허버트가 말했다. \"그분은 무척 만족해하시는데, 자네를 몹시 보고 싶어 하시지. 내 사랑스러운 아가씨는 지금 아버지 곁에 있어. 그녀가 내려올 때까지 기다려 주면 자네에게 소개해 주고, 그러고 나서 위층으로 올라가세. 저게 바로 그녀의 아버지일세.\"\n\n머리 위에서 들려오는 으르렁대는 소리에 나는 섬뜩해졌고, 아마 그 사실이 내 얼굴에 드러났던 모양이다.\n\n\"안됐지만 저분은 딱한 늙은 망나니라네.\" 허버트가 미소 지으며 말했다. \"그래도 나는 한 번도 그분을 본 적이 없어. 럼주 냄새가 나지 않나? 늘 그걸 들이켜고 계시거든.\"\n\n\"럼주를요?\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"그래.\" 허버트가 대답했다. \"그러니 그분의 통풍이 얼마나 가벼워지겠나 짐작이 가겠지. 게다가 그분은 모든 식료품을 위층 자기 방에 두고 손수 나누어 주겠다고 고집을 부리신다네. 머리 위 선반에 그것들을 얹어 두고는 일일이 무게를 달아야만 직성이 풀리시지. 그분 방은 잡화상 가게 같을 게 분명해.\"\n\n그가 이렇게 말하는 동안, 으르렁대던 소리가 길게 끄는 포효로 변했다가, 이윽고 잦아들었다.\n\n\"달리 무슨 결과가 나오겠나.\" 허버트가 설명조로 말했다. \"그분이 굳이 치즈를 손수 자르겠다고 하시니 말이야. 오른손에—아니, 온몸에—통풍을 앓는 사람이 더블 글로스터 치즈 한 덩이를 자기 몸을 다치지 않고 다 잘라 낼 수 있다고 기대할 수야 없지.\"\n\n그는 몹시 다친 모양이었으니, 또 한 번 사납게 포효했기 때문이다.\n\n\"프로비스 같은 위층 하숙인을 두게 된 건 윔플 부인에게는 정말 횡재일세.\" 허버트가 말했다. \"왜냐하면 보통 사람들은 저런 소음을 참아 내지 못할 테니까. 묘한 곳이지, 핸들? 그렇지 않나?\"\n\n과연 묘한 곳이었다. 하지만 놀랄 만큼 잘 정돈되어 있고 깨끗했다.\n\n\"윔플 부인은.\" 내가 그렇게 말하자 허버트가 말했다. \"더없이 훌륭한 주부라네. 그녀의 어머니 같은 보살핌이 없었더라면 내 클라라가 어떻게 지냈을지 나는 정말 모르겠어. 클라라에게는 친어머니가 없거든, 핸들. 그리고 이 세상에 늙은 그러프앤드그림 말고는 아무 친척도 없다네.\"\n\n\"설마 그게 그분 이름은 아니겠지, 허버트?\"\n\n\"아니, 아니야.\" 허버트가 말했다. \"그건 내가 그분을 부르는 별명이야. 본명은 발리 씨라네. 하지만 우리 부모님의 아들로서, 친척이 하나도 없고, 자기 집안 문제로 자기 자신이나 남을 결코 성가시게 할 일이 없는 아가씨를 사랑하게 되었으니 이 얼마나 복된 일인가!\"\n\n허버트는 전에도 내게 들려준 적이 있고 지금 다시 일깨워 주었는데, 그가 처음 클라라 발리 양을 알게 된 것은 그녀가 해머스미스의 한 학원에서 학업을 마무리하던 무렵이었고, 그녀가 아버지를 간병하러 집으로 불려 돌아간 뒤로 그와 그녀는 어머니 같은 윔플 부인에게 그들의 애정을 털어놓았으며, 그 후로 줄곧 부인이 그 애정을 한결같은 친절과 사려로 길러 주고 다스려 주었다는 것이다. 통풍과 럼주와 선상 물품 관리 외에 그보다 심리적인 어떤 주제도 헤아릴 능력이 도무지 없는 사람인지라, 늙은 발리에게는 그 어떤 애틋한 이야기도 결코 털어놓을 수 없으리라는 것이 자명한 일이었다.\n\n천장을 가로지르는 들보 속에서 늙은 발리의 끊임없는 으르렁거림이 울리는 가운데 우리가 이렇게 낮은 목소리로 이야기를 나누고 있을 때, 방문이 열리더니 스무 살쯤 된, 무척 예쁘고 가냘프며 검은 눈동자를 한 아가씨가 바구니를 손에 들고 들어왔다. 허버트는 다정하게 그녀에게서 바구니를 받아 주고는, 얼굴을 붉히며 \"클라라\"라고 소개했다. 그녀는 정말이지 더없이 매력적인 아가씨였으니, 저 사나운 식인귀 늙은 발리가 강제로 부려 먹는 사로잡힌 요정으로 보아도 좋을 정도였다.\n\n\"여기 보게.\" 우리가 잠시 이야기를 나눈 뒤, 허버트가 측은하고 다정한 미소를 지으며 내게 바구니를 보여 주었다. \"이게 가엾은 클라라의 저녁 식사인데, 매일 밤 이렇게 배급되는 거라네. 이게 그녀 몫의 빵이고, 이게 그녀의 치즈 한 조각이고, 이게 그녀의 럼주인데—이건 내가 마셔 버리지. 이건 발리 씨의 내일 아침 식사로, 조리하라고 내준 거야. 양고기 갈빗살 두 점, 감자 세 알, 쪼갠 완두콩 약간, 밀가루 조금, 버터 두 온스, 소금 한 자밤, 그리고 이 검은 후추 전부. 이걸 함께 끓여서 뜨겁게 들이시는데, 통풍에는 아주 좋은 것이렷다, 내 생각엔 말이야!\"\n\n허버트가 하나하나 가리키는 대로, 그 식료품들을 자세히 들여다보는 클라라의 체념 어린 모습에는 무언가 그토록 천연덕스럽고 마음을 끄는 데가 있었고, 허버트의 끌어안는 팔에 다소곳이 몸을 내맡기는 그 겸손한 태도에는 무언가 그토록 미덥고 사랑스럽고 순수한 데가 있었으며, 늙은 발리가 들보 속에서 으르렁거리는 가운데 칭크스 만 곁 오래된 푸른 구리 밧줄 제조장 옆 밀폰드 둑에서 그토록 보호가 필요한, 그녀 안의 무언가 그토록 온화한 데가 있었다. 그리하여 나는 내가 한 번도 열어 보지 않은 그 지갑 속 돈을 전부 준다 해도 그녀와 허버트 사이의 약혼을 깨뜨리고 싶지 않았다.\n\n내가 기쁨과 감탄에 차서 그녀를 바라보고 있을 때, 별안간 으르렁거림이 다시 포효로 부풀어 오르더니, 위에서 무시무시하게 쿵쿵대는 소리가 들려왔다. 마치 나무다리를 한 거인이 우리에게 다다르려고 천장을 뚫고 내려오려는 것 같았다. 이에 클라라가 허버트에게 \"아빠가 나를 부르세요, 여보!\" 하고는 달려가 버렸다.\n\n\"저런 양심도 없는 늙은 상어가 어디 또 있겠나!\" 허버트가 말했다. \"이번엔 또 뭘 원하시는 것 같나, 핸들?\"\n\n\"모르겠는데.\" 내가 말했다. \"뭐 마실 거라도?\"\n\n\"바로 그거야!\" 허버트가 마치 내가 대단히 훌륭한 추측이라도 해낸 것처럼 외쳤다. \"그분은 탁자 위 작은 통에 그로그주를 미리 타서 늘 준비해 두신다네. 잠깐만 기다려 보게, 클라라가 그분을 일으켜 한 모금 들이게 하는 소리가 들릴 테니. 자, 시작하시는군!\" 또 한 번의 포효가, 끝에 가서 길게 떨리며 들려왔다. \"자.\" 그것에 뒤이어 침묵이 찾아오자 허버트가 말했다. \"이제 마시고 계셔. 자.\" 들보 속에서 으르렁거림이 다시금 울려 퍼지자 허버트가 말했다. \"이제 다시 등을 대고 누우셨군!\"\n\n클라라는 곧 돌아왔고, 허버트는 우리가 맡은 이를 보러 나를 위층으로 데려갔다. 우리가 발리 씨의 방문 앞을 지날 때, 그가 안에서 바람처럼 높아졌다 낮아졌다 하는 곡조로 다음과 같은 후렴을 쉰 목소리로 중얼거리는 소리가 들렸는데, 나는 여기서 정반대의 뜻을 가진 말들을 좋은 축원의 말로 바꾸어 옮기겠다.\n\n\"어이! 자네에게 축복을, 여기 늙은 빌 발리가 있도다. 여기 늙은 빌 발리가 있도다, 자네에게 축복을. 여기 늙은 빌 발리가 등을 대고 누워 있도다, 맹세코. 떠다니는 늙은 죽은 가자미처럼 등을 대고 누워서, 여기 자네의 늙은 빌 발리가 있도다, 자네에게 축복을. 어이! 축복을.\"\n\n이런 위안의 곡조로, 보이지 않는 발리는 밤낮을 가리지 않고 혼잣말로 자기 자신과 교감하는 것이라고 허버트가 일러 주었다. 흔히 날이 밝을 때는 강을 훑어보기 편하도록 침대에 장치해 둔 망원경에 한쪽 눈을 댄 채로 그러기도 한다는 것이었다.\n\n집 꼭대기에 있는, 산뜻하고 통풍이 잘되며 발리 씨의 소리가 아래층보다 덜 들리는 그의 두 선실 같은 방에서, 나는 프로비스가 편안하게 자리 잡고 있는 것을 보았다. 그는 아무런 불안도 내비치지 않았고, 입에 담을 만한 불안을 느끼는 것 같지도 않았다. 그러나 내게는 그가 부드러워진 듯 여겨졌으니—어떻게 그러한지는 말할 수 없을 만큼 막연했고, 훗날 떠올리려 애써도 도무지 어떻게였는지 되살릴 수 없었지만, 그래도 분명히 그러했다.\n\n그날의 휴식이 내게 안겨 준 사색의 기회는, 컴페이슨에 관해서는 그에게 한마디도 하지 않겠다는 결심을 굳히는 것으로 귀결되었다. 내가 아는 한, 그러지 않았다가는 그 사내에 대한 그의 적개심이 그를 부추겨 그자를 찾아 나서게 하고 제 손으로 파멸로 뛰어들게 만들지도 몰랐다. 그래서 허버트와 내가 그의 난롯가에 그와 함께 앉았을 때, 나는 무엇보다 먼저 그가 웨믹의 판단과 정보의 출처를 믿느냐고 물었다.\n\n\"암, 암, 얘야!\" 그가 진지하게 고개를 끄덕이며 대답했다. \"재거스라면 알지.\"\n\n\"그래서 제가 웨믹과 이야기를 나누었어요.\" 내가 말했다. \"그가 제게 어떤 주의를 주었고 어떤 충고를 했는지 말씀드리려고 왔습니다.\"\n\n나는 방금 말한 단서만 빼고는 이것을 정확히 전했다. 그리고 그에게, 웨믹이 뉴게이트 감옥에서(간수에게서인지 죄수에게서인지는 나도 알 수 없었지만) 그가 어느 정도 의심받고 있다는 것과 내 거처가 감시당하고 있다는 것을 들었다는 사실, 그리고 웨믹이 그에게 한동안 몸을 숨기고 있으라고, 또 내게는 그를 멀리하라고 권했다는 사실, 그리고 그를 국외로 빼돌리는 일에 관해 웨믹이 무슨 말을 했는지를 일러 주었다. 나는 덧붙이기를, 물론 때가 오면 웨믹의 판단에 따라 가장 안전한 쪽으로, 내가 그와 함께 가거나 그의 뒤를 바짝 따라가겠다고 했다. 그다음에 무엇이 이어질지에 대해서는 건드리지 않았다. 사실 나 또한 그 점에 대해서는 내 마음속에서도 전혀 분명하지도 편안하지도 않았으니, 이제 그가 그토록 부드러워진 상태로, 게다가 나를 위해 명백히 위험에 처해 있는 모습을 본 마당이었다. 씀씀이를 늘려 내 생활 방식을 바꾸는 일에 관해서는, 지금처럼 불안정하고 어려운 우리 처지에서 그것이 더 나쁜 일까지는 아니더라도 그저 우스꽝스러운 짓이 되지 않겠느냐고 그에게 물었다.\n\n그는 이를 부인할 수 없었고, 사실 처음부터 끝까지 매우 사리에 밝았다. 자기가 돌아온 것은 하나의 모험이며, 자기는 그것이 모험임을 늘 알고 있었다고 그는 말했다. 자기는 그것을 무모한 모험으로 만들 어떤 짓도 하지 않을 것이며, 이토록 든든한 도움을 받고 있으니 제 안전에 대해서는 거의 두려움이 없다고 했다.\n\n줄곧 난롯불을 바라보며 골똘히 생각하던 허버트가 여기서 입을 열어, 웨믹의 제안에서 떠오른 생각이 하나 있는데 그것을 추진해 볼 만하겠다고 말했다. \"우리 둘 다 노 솜씨가 좋으니, 핸들, 때가 무르익으면 우리 손으로 그분을 강 아래로 모셔 갈 수 있어. 그러면 그 일을 위해 배를 빌릴 필요도, 뱃사공을 쓸 필요도 없게 되지. 그것만으로도 적어도 의심받을 여지 하나는 덜게 될 텐데, 여지는 어떤 것이든 더는 게 좋아. 철은 신경 쓰지 말게. 자네가 지금 당장 템플 선착장에 배 한 척을 갖다 두고, 강을 오르내리며 노 젓는 일을 습관처럼 해 두면 좋지 않겠나? 자네가 그런 습관에 빠져들면, 그다음엔 누가 눈여겨보거나 마음에 두겠나? 스무 번이고 쉰 번이고 그렇게 하면, 스물한 번째나 쉰한 번째에 자네가 그러는 것도 별스러울 게 없어지지.\"\n\n나는 이 계획이 마음에 들었고, 프로비스는 그것에 무척 들떴다. 우리는 이를 실행에 옮기기로, 그리고 우리가 다리 아래로 내려가 밀폰드 둑을 지나 노를 저어 갈 때 프로비스는 결코 우리를 아는 체하지 않기로 합의했다. 그러나 우리는 한 걸음 더 나아가, 그가 우리를 보고 모든 것이 무사할 때면 언제든 동쪽으로 난 창문 부분의 블라인드를 내리기로 합의했다.\n\n우리의 협의가 이제 끝나고 모든 것이 정해졌으므로, 나는 일어나 떠나려 했다. 그러면서 허버트에게, 그와 내가 함께 집으로 가지 않는 편이 낫겠다고, 내가 그보다 삼십 분 먼저 출발하겠다고 일렀다. \"여기 당신을 두고 가는 게 마음에 걸립니다.\" 나는 프로비스에게 말했다. \"당신이 제 곁에 있는 것보다 여기 있는 게 더 안전하다는 건 의심할 여지가 없지만요. 안녕히 계세요!\"\n\n\"얘야.\" 그가 내 두 손을 맞잡으며 대답했다. \"우리가 언제 다시 만날 수 있을지 모르겠구나. 나는 작별 인사가 싫다. 잘 자라고만 해 다오!\"\n\n\"안녕히 주무세요! 허버트가 우리 사이를 꼬박꼬박 오갈 거예요. 그리고 때가 오면 제가 채비를 갖추고 있으리라는 걸 믿으셔도 됩니다. 안녕히 주무세요, 안녕히 주무세요!\"\n\n그가 자기 방에 머무는 편이 가장 좋겠다고 우리는 생각했고, 그래서 그를 방문 밖 층계참에 남겨 둔 채 떠났다. 그는 우리가 아래층으로 내려가는 길을 밝혀 주려고 계단 난간 너머로 등불을 들고 있었다. 그를 돌아보며, 나는 그가 돌아온 첫날 밤을 떠올렸다. 그때는 우리의 처지가 지금과 뒤바뀌어 있었고, 그와 헤어지는 마당에 내 마음이 지금처럼 이토록 무겁고 불안해지리라고는 그때 나는 거의 짐작조차 하지 못했다.\n\n우리가 다시 그의 방문 앞을 지날 때, 늙은 발리는 으르렁대며 욕을 퍼붓고 있었는데, 그칠 기색도, 그칠 마음도 전혀 없어 보였다. 계단 아래에 다다랐을 때, 나는 허버트에게 프로비스라는 이름을 그대로 두었느냐고 물었다. 그는 절대 그러지 않았으며, 하숙인은 캠벨 씨로 되어 있다고 대답했다. 또한 여기서 캠벨 씨에 관해 알려진 것이라고는 기껏해야, 그(허버트)가 캠벨 씨를 위탁받았으며 그가 잘 보살핌을 받고 은둔 생활을 하는 데 깊은 개인적 관심을 두고 있다는 정도가 전부라고 설명했다. 그래서 우리가 윔플 부인과 클라라가 앉아 바느질을 하고 있는 응접실로 들어갔을 때, 나는 캠벨 씨에 대한 나 자신의 관심에 대해서는 한마디도 하지 않고 마음속에 묻어 두었다.\n\n예쁘고 온화하며 검은 눈동자를 한 그 아가씨에게, 그리고 진실한 사랑의 자그마한 사연에 대한 진솔한 동정심을 여태 잃지 않은 그 어머니 같은 부인에게 작별을 고하고 나니, 오래된 푸른 구리 밧줄 제조장이 사뭇 다른 곳이 된 듯한 느낌이 들었다. 늙은 발리는 산만큼이나 늙었을지 모르고, 한 부대의 기병들처럼 욕을 퍼부을지 모르지만, 칭크스 만에는 그곳을 차고 넘치도록 채울 만한, 그 모든 것을 만회하는 젊음과 믿음과 희망이 있었다. 그러고 나서 나는 에스텔라를, 그리고 우리의 이별을 떠올리며, 무척 서글픈 심정으로 집으로 돌아갔다.\n\n템플은 내가 여태껏 본 것 못지않게 모든 것이 고요했다. 얼마 전까지 프로비스가 쓰던 그쪽 방들의 창문은 어둡고 조용했으며, 가든 코트에는 어슬렁거리는 사람 하나 없었다. 나는 나와 내 방 사이에 놓인 계단을 내려가기에 앞서 분수 곁을 두세 번 지나쳐 걸었으나, 정말로 나 혼자뿐이었다. 허버트는 안으로 들어와—나는 풀이 죽고 지쳐 곧장 잠자리에 들었으므로—내 침대 곁으로 와서는 똑같은 보고를 했다. 그러고 나서 창문 하나를 열고 달빛 속을 내다보더니, 그 시각 어느 대성당의 포도(鋪道) 못지않게 길바닥이 엄숙하리만치 텅 비어 있다고 내게 일러 주었다.\n\n이튿날 나는 배를 마련하는 일에 착수했다. 그것은 곧 처리되었고, 배는 템플 선착장으로 옮겨져, 내가 일이 분이면 손이 닿을 수 있는 곳에 매여 있었다. 그러고 나서 나는 훈련과 연습을 한다는 명목으로 나가기 시작했다. 어떤 때는 혼자서, 어떤 때는 허버트와 함께. 추위와 비와 진눈깨비 속에 나가는 일이 잦았으나, 몇 번 나가고 나니 아무도 나를 그다지 눈여겨보지 않았다. 처음에는 블랙프라이어스 다리 위쪽에만 머물렀으나, 조수의 시각이 바뀌자 런던 다리 쪽으로 향했다. 그 시절에는 옛 런던 다리였는데, 조수가 어떤 상태일 때면 거기 거센 물살과 낙차가 있어 평판이 나빴다. 그러나 나는 남이 하는 것을 보고 나서 다리를 '쏘듯 통과하는' 법을 충분히 익혔으므로, 풀의 선박들 사이를 누비며, 그리고 에리스까지 내려가며 노를 젓기 시작했다. 내가 처음으로 밀폰드 둑을 지날 때 허버트와 나는 노 한 쌍을 저어 가고 있었는데, 갈 때나 돌아올 때나 우리는 동쪽을 향한 블라인드가 내려오는 것을 보았다. 허버트는 일주일에 세 번보다 드물게 그곳에 가는 일이 거의 없었고, 조금이라도 섬뜩한 소식을 단 한마디도 내게 전해 온 적이 없었다. 그래도 나는 섬뜩해할 까닭이 있음을 알고 있었고, 감시당하고 있다는 생각을 떨쳐 낼 수가 없었다. 한번 자리 잡고 나면 그것은 사람을 들볶는 생각이니, 내가 얼마나 많은 무고한 사람들을 나를 감시하는 자로 의심했는지는 헤아리기 어려울 정도였다.\n\n요컨대, 나는 숨어 지내는 그 무모한 사내에 대한 두려움으로 늘 가득 차 있었다. 허버트는 가끔 내게, 날이 저문 뒤 조수가 빠져나갈 때 우리 창문 하나에 서서, 그 물결이 거기 실린 온갖 것과 더불어 클라라를 향해 흘러간다고 생각하면 흐뭇하다고 말하곤 했다. 그러나 나는 두려움에 차서, 그 물결이 매그위치를 향해 흘러간다고, 그리고 그 수면 위의 어떤 검은 자국이든 그를 붙잡으러 빠르고 소리 없이 그리고 어김없이 가는 그의 추격자들일지 모른다고 생각했다.\n\n# Chapter XLVII.\n\n몇 주가 지나갔으나 아무런 변화도 찾아오지 않았다. 우리는 웨믹을 기다렸지만, 그는 아무런 기척도 보이지 않았다. 만약 내가 그를 리틀브리튼 바깥에서 한 번도 본 적이 없고, 그 성에서 허물없이 지내는 특권을 누려 본 적이 없었더라면, 나는 그를 의심했을지도 모른다. 그러나 내가 아는 그의 사람됨으로 보아, 단 한순간도 그러지 않았다.\n\n내 세속적인 형편이 점차 음울한 빛을 띠기 시작했고, 나는 한둘이 아닌 채권자들로부터 돈 독촉에 시달렸다. 나 자신조차 돈이 궁한 처지(내 호주머니 속 현금이 궁하다는 말이다)를 알게 되었고, 그것을 덜어 보려고 어렵잖게 처분할 만한 보석 몇 점을 현금으로 바꾸기에 이르렀다. 그러나 나는, 내 생각과 계획이 이토록 불확실한 형편에서 후원자에게서 돈을 더 받는 것은 인정머리 없는 사기 행위라고 단단히 마음을 굳혔다. 그래서 나는 뜯지 않은 그 지갑을 허버트 편에 보내 그가 직접 간직하도록 했고, 그가 자기 정체를 밝힌 이후로 그의 후의를 누리지 않았다는 데서 일종의 만족감을 느꼈다. 그것이 거짓된 만족인지 참된 만족인지는 나도 잘 모르겠다.\n\n시간이 흐를수록, 에스텔라가 결혼했다는 인상이 내 마음을 무겁게 짓눌렀다. 이미 거의 확신에 가까웠음에도, 나는 그것이 확증되는 것이 두려워 신문을 멀리했고, 허버트에게(나는 그에게 우리의 마지막 대면이 어떠했는지 털어놓았다) 그녀에 관한 말은 절대 내게 꺼내지 말아 달라고 청했다. 이미 갈가리 찢겨 바람결에 흩어져 버린 희망의 옷자락, 그 마지막 가련한 자투리를 내가 왜 그토록 움켜쥐고 있었는지, 내가 어찌 알겠는가? 이 글을 읽는 당신은 또 어찌하여 작년에, 지난달에, 지난주에, 이와 그리 다르지 않은 모순을 스스로 저질렀는가?\n\n내가 살던 삶은 불행한 삶이었다. 그리고 그 모든 다른 근심들 위로 우뚝 솟은, 산맥 위로 솟구친 높은 산과도 같은, 한 가지 지배적인 근심이 결코 내 시야에서 사라지지 않았다. 그래도 새로운 두려움의 빌미는 생겨나지 않았다. 그가 발각되었다는 갓 솟아난 공포에 사로잡혀 잠자리에서 벌떡 일어나든, 밤마다 허버트의 돌아오는 발소리가 여느 때보다 빠르고 불길한 소식의 날개를 단 것은 아닐까 두려워하며 귀를 곤두세우고 앉아 있든 — 그 모든 것에도 불구하고, 또 그와 비슷한 수많은 일들에도 불구하고, 세상사의 쳇바퀴는 그저 굴러갔다. 아무것도 할 수 없는 처지에, 끝없는 불안과 초조 속에 갇힌 채, 나는 내 보트를 저어 다니며 할 수 있는 한 최선을 다해 기다리고, 또 기다리고, 또 기다렸다.\n\n강 아래쪽으로 내려갔다가, 물살이 소용돌이치며 부딪치는 옛 런던교의 아치며 교각 보호용 말뚝 사이로 되돌아올 수 없는 밀물 때도 있었다. 그럴 때면 나는 세관 근처 부두에 보트를 매어 두었다가, 나중에 템플 선착장까지 끌어 올리도록 했다. 나는 이 일을 마다하지 않았는데, 그렇게 함으로써 나와 내 보트가 그곳 강가 사람들 사이에서 흔히 보는 광경이 되었기 때문이다. 이 사소한 계기에서 이제 내가 이야기하려는 두 차례의 만남이 비롯되었다.\n\n이월 하순 어느 오후, 나는 해 질 무렵 그 부두에 닻을 내렸다. 썰물을 타고 그리니치까지 내려갔다가, 물때가 바뀌자 방향을 돌려 돌아온 것이었다. 맑고 화창한 날이었으나, 해가 지면서 안개가 끼기 시작해, 나는 정박해 있는 배들 사이로 꽤 조심스레 길을 더듬어 돌아와야 했다. 갈 때나 돌아올 때나, 나는 그의 창문에 걸린 신호, 이상 없음을 보았다.\n\n쌀쌀한 저녁이었고 몸도 추웠던 터라, 나는 우선 저녁이라도 들며 몸을 녹이는 게 좋겠다고 생각했다. 그리고 템플의 집으로 돌아가 봤자 우울과 고독의 시간만이 기다리고 있을 터이니, 식사 뒤에는 연극이나 보러 갈까 싶었다. 워플 씨가 그 미심쩍은 승리를 거둔 적이 있는 극장이 바로 그 강가 동네에 있었으므로(지금은 어디에도 남아 있지 않다), 나는 그 극장으로 가기로 마음먹었다. 워플 씨가 연극을 부흥시키는 데 성공하기는커녕, 오히려 그 쇠락에 한몫 거들었다는 것은 나도 알고 있었다. 그가 고귀한 가문의 어린 소녀와 원숭이 한 마리와 더불어, 충직한 흑인 노릇을 맡았다는 불길한 소식이 연극 전단을 통해 들려왔다. 또 허버트는 그가 벽돌처럼 붉은 얼굴에 온통 방울이 달린 터무니없는 모자를 쓰고, 익살스러운 성정의 약탈자 타타르인 역을 하는 것을 본 적이 있었다.\n\n나는 허버트와 내가 이른바 지리학적 음식점이라 부르곤 하던 곳에서 저녁을 들었다. 그곳은 식탁보 반 야드마다 흑맥주 잔 자국으로 세계 지도가 그려져 있고, 나이프마다 육즙으로 해도가 그려져 있는 집이었다 — 오늘날까지도 런던 시장의 관할 구역 안에 지리학적이지 않은 음식점은 거의 한 군데도 없다 — 나는 빵 부스러기 위로 꾸벅꾸벅 졸고, 가스등을 멍하니 바라보고, 음식들이 뿜어내는 뜨거운 열기에 몸을 구우며 시간을 죽였다. 이윽고 나는 정신을 차리고 연극을 보러 갔다.\n\n거기서 나는 영국 해군에 복무하는 덕망 높은 갑판장 한 사람을 보았는데 — 더없이 훌륭한 사내였으나, 다만 그의 바지가 어떤 데서는 그토록 꽉 끼지 않고 어떤 데서는 그토록 헐렁하지 않았더라면 싶었다 — 그는 더없이 너그럽고 용감했음에도 작은 사내들의 모자를 죄다 눈까지 푹 눌러 내려 버렸고, 더없이 애국적이었음에도 누구든 세금을 내겠다는 소리는 들으려 하지도 않았다. 그는 호주머니에 보자기에 싼 푸딩 같은 돈주머니를 지니고 있었고, 그 재산을 밑천 삼아 침대보 차림의 한 젊은 여인과 성대한 잔치를 벌이며 결혼했다. 포츠머스의 온 주민(마지막 인구 조사에서 아홉 명이었다)이 해변으로 몰려나와 제 손을 비비고 남들의 손을 잡아 흔들며 \"채워라, 채워라!\" 하고 노래를 불렀다. 그러나 살갗이 거무튀튀한 어떤 갑판 잡것 하나는, 잔을 채우려고도, 자기에게 권하는 다른 무엇도 하려 들지 않았으며, 그 마음이 자기 선수상만큼이나 시커멓다고(갑판장의 입을 통해) 공공연히 밝혀진 자였는데, 다른 두 갑판 잡것에게 온 인류를 곤경에 빠뜨리자고 부추겼다. 그 일이 어찌나 감쪽같이 이루어졌던지(이 갑판 잡것 일가가 상당한 정치적 영향력을 지닌 까닭이었다), 사태를 바로잡는 데 저녁 시간의 절반이 걸렸고, 그나마도 흰 모자에 검은 각반을 차고 빨간 코를 한 정직한 작은 식료품 장수 하나가 석쇠를 들고 괘종시계 안으로 들어가 엿듣다가 나와서는, 엿들은 말로 논박할 수 없는 자는 누구든 뒤에서 그 석쇠로 때려눕힌 덕분에야 겨우 해결되었다. 이 일로 워플 씨가(그때까지 한 번도 언급된 적이 없던 인물이었다) 가슴에 별 훈장을 달고 가터 훈장을 두른 채, 해군성에서 곧장 파견된 막강한 권한의 전권 대사로 등장하여, 갑판 잡것들은 모두 그 자리에서 감옥으로 끌려가야 한다고, 그리고 갑판장의 공로에 대한 작은 사례로 유니언잭을 가져왔다고 선언했다. 갑판장은 처음으로 마음이 약해져 그 깃발에 공손히 눈물을 닦더니, 이내 기운을 차리고 워플 씨를 각하라 부르며 그의 지느러미를 잡게 해 달라고 청했다. 워플 씨가 우아하고 위엄 있게 자기 지느러미를 내어 주자, 그는 곧장 먼지투성이 구석으로 떠밀려 났고, 그동안 모두가 호른파이프 춤을 추었다. 그리고 그 구석에서 못마땅한 눈초리로 관객들을 둘러보던 그가, 나를 알아보았다.\n\n두 번째 작품은 새로 올린 성대한 희극풍 크리스마스 무언극으로, 그 첫 장면에서 나는 워플 씨를 알아본 듯하여 마음이 아팠다. 그는 빨간 털실로 짠 다리를 하고, 잔뜩 부풀린 인광 빛 얼굴에, 빨간 커튼 술 같은 더부룩한 머리칼을 늘어뜨린 채, 광산 안에서 벼락을 만들어 내는 일에 종사하다가, 그의 거대한 주인이 (몹시 쉰 목소리로) 저녁을 먹으러 집에 돌아오자 엄청난 겁쟁이 짓을 부렸다. 그러나 그는 이내 좀 더 점잖은 사정 아래 모습을 드러냈다. 젊은 사랑의 정령이 도움을 필요로 했기 때문이다 — 무지한 농부 하나가 제 딸의 마음이 택한 상대를 반대하여, 일부러 밀가루 자루를 뒤집어쓴 채 이 층 창문에서 그 상대 위로 떨어지는 식의 부모다운 횡포를 부린 탓이었다 — 그리하여 정령은 격언을 늘어놓는 어느 마법사를 불러냈고, 보아하니 험난한 여정 끝에 지구 반대편에서 비틀비틀 올라온 그 마법사는, 챙 높은 모자를 쓰고 강령술에 관한 한 권짜리 책을 옆구리에 낀 워플 씨로 밝혀졌다. 이 마법사가 지상에서 하는 일이란 주로 사람들이 그에게 말을 퍼붓고, 노래를 퍼붓고, 머리로 들이받고, 춤을 춰 대고, 갖가지 빛깔의 불꽃을 터뜨려 대는 것을 받아 내는 데 있었던 까닭에, 그에게는 빈 시간이 꽤 많았다. 그리고 나는 크게 놀라며, 그가 그 시간을 마치 경이에 넋을 잃은 사람처럼 내 쪽을 빤히 바라보는 데 바치고 있음을 알아챘다.\n\n워플 씨의 눈빛이 점점 더 형형해지는 데는 무언가 심상치 않은 구석이 있었고, 그가 마음속으로 온갖 생각을 굴리며 어찌나 어리둥절해하는 듯 보였던지, 나는 도무지 영문을 알 수 없었다. 그가 커다란 회중시계 갑 속에 담겨 구름 위로 올라간 뒤에도 나는 한참을 그 일을 생각하며 앉아 있었으나, 여전히 영문을 알 수 없었다. 한 시간 뒤 극장을 나서며 문 가까이에서 나를 기다리는 그를 발견했을 때에도, 나는 여전히 그 일을 생각하고 있었다.\n\n\"안녕하셨습니까?\" 함께 거리를 걸어 내려가며 나는 그와 악수하며 말했다. \"선생께서 저를 보신 걸 저도 알았습니다.\"\n\n\"보았지요, 핍 씨!\" 그가 대답했다. \"그럼요, 당연히 보았고말고요. 한데 거기 또 누가 있었지요?\"\n\n\"또 누구라니요?\"\n\n\"참으로 이상한 일이오.\" 다시 넋이 나간 표정으로 빠져들며 워플 씨가 말했다. \"그렇지만 나는 그자라고 맹세할 수 있소.\"\n\n나는 불안해져, 무슨 뜻인지 설명해 달라고 워플 씨에게 간청했다.\n\n\"당신이 거기 없었더라면 내가 애초에 그자를 알아챘을지는,\" 여전히 같은 넋 나간 투로 워플 씨가 말을 이었다. \"확언할 수 없소. 그래도 알아챘을 거라 생각하오.\"\n\n나는 나도 모르게 주위를 둘러보았다. 집으로 돌아갈 때면 으레 그러던 버릇대로. 이 알 수 없는 말이 내게 오싹한 한기를 안겨 주었던 것이다.\n\n\"오! 그자가 보일 리는 없소.\" 워플 씨가 말했다. \"그자는 내가 무대를 떠나기 전에 나갔으니까. 나가는 걸 내가 보았소.\"\n\n의심을 품을 만한 까닭을 지니고 있던 나는, 이 가련한 배우조차도 의심했다. 나에게서 무언가 시인을 끌어내려는 계략이 아닌가 미덥지 않았던 것이다. 그래서 나는 함께 걸어가면서 그를 흘끔거렸을 뿐, 아무 말도 하지 않았다.\n\n\"나는 그자가 틀림없이 당신과 함께 온 사람이려니 하는 어처구니없는 생각을 했소, 핍 씨, 당신이 그자를 전혀 알아채지 못하고 있다는 걸 알기 전까지는 말이오. 그자는 유령처럼 거기 당신 뒤에 앉아 있었거든.\"\n\n아까의 한기가 다시 나를 스멀스멀 덮쳐 왔으나, 나는 아직은 입을 열지 않기로 마음먹었다. 그가 이 언급들을 프로비스와 결부시키도록 나를 꾀어내라고 누군가에게 사주받았을 수도 있다는 것이, 그의 말과 충분히 맞아떨어졌기 때문이다. 물론 나는 프로비스가 거기 있지 않았다는 것을 완벽히 확신하며 마음이 놓였다.\n\n\"내가 이상해 보이는가 보오, 핍 씨. 정말이지, 그렇게 여기는 게 보이는구려. 하지만 너무나도 이상한 일이라서 말이오! 내가 지금 하려는 말을 당신은 좀처럼 믿지 못할 거요. 당신이 내게 그런 말을 한다면 나 자신도 좀처럼 믿지 못할 테니까.\"\n\n\"그렇습니까?\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"그렇소, 정말이오. 핍 씨, 당신이 아주 어린아이였던 옛 시절, 어느 크리스마스 날을 기억하오? 내가 가저리네 집에서 저녁을 들고, 군인 몇이 수갑 한 짝을 고치러 문간에 찾아왔던 그날 말이오.\"\n\n\"아주 잘 기억합니다.\"\n\n\"그리고 죄수 둘을 뒤쫓는 추격이 벌어졌고, 우리도 거기 끼어들었고, 가저리가 당신을 등에 업었고, 내가 앞장섰고, 당신이 힘닿는 데까지 나를 따라붙었던 걸 기억하오?\"\n\n\"그 일을 모두 아주 잘 기억합니다.\" 그가 생각하는 것보다 더 잘 — 다만 마지막 대목만은 빼고.\n\n\"그리고 우리가 도랑 속에서 그 둘을 따라잡았던 걸, 그 둘 사이에 격투가 벌어졌던 걸, 그중 하나가 다른 하나에게 호되게 당하고 얼굴이 마구 찢긴 걸 기억하오?\"\n\n\"눈앞에 그대로 떠오릅니다.\"\n\n\"그리고 군인들이 횃불을 밝혀 그 둘을 가운데 세웠던 걸, 우리가 그들의 마지막 모습을 보려고 검은 습지를 가로질러 따라갔던 걸, 그들의 얼굴 위로 횃불 빛이 비치던 걸 — 나는 이 대목을 분명히 해 두고 싶소 — 우리를 사방으로 에워싼 바깥 둘레는 캄캄한 밤이었는데, 그들의 얼굴 위로 횃불 빛이 비치던 걸 기억하오?\"\n\n\"네.\" 내가 말했다. \"그 일을 모두 기억합니다.\"\n\n\"그렇다면, 핍 씨, 그 두 죄수 가운데 하나가 오늘 밤 당신 뒤에 앉아 있었소. 나는 당신 어깨 너머로 그자를 보았소.\"\n\n'침착하자!' 나는 마음속으로 생각했다. 그러고는 그에게 물었다. \"그 둘 중 어느 쪽을 보았다고 생각하십니까?\"\n\n\"호되게 당했던 쪽이오.\" 그가 거침없이 대답했다. \"맹세코 내가 그자를 보았소! 그자를 생각하면 할수록, 그자가 틀림없다는 확신이 더 굳어지오.\"\n\n\"참으로 묘한 일이군요!\" 나는 그 일이 내게는 그 이상 아무것도 아니라는 시늉을 할 수 있는 최선을 다해 말했다. \"정말이지 참으로 묘한 일입니다!\"\n\n이 대화가 나를 얼마나 더한 불안 속으로 몰아넣었는지, 또 콤페이슨이 \"유령처럼\" 내 등 뒤에 있었다는 데서 내가 느낀 그 각별하고도 기묘한 공포가 어떠했는지는, 아무리 부풀려 말해도 지나치지 않을 것이다. 은신이 시작된 이래로 그가 단 몇 분이라도 한꺼번에 내 머릿속을 떠나 있었던 적이 있다면, 그것은 바로 그가 내게 가장 가까이 다가와 있던 그 순간들이었으니까. 그리고 그 온갖 조심에도 불구하고 내가 그토록 무방비로 넋을 놓고 있었다고 생각하니, 마치 그를 막으려고 백 개의 문이 달린 길목을 닫아걸고서는, 그러고 나서 바로 내 팔꿈치 곁에 그가 있는 것을 발견한 격이었다. 또한 그가 거기 있었던 것은 내가 거기 있었기 때문이며, 우리를 둘러싼 위험이 아무리 사소해 보일지라도 위험은 늘 가까이서 살아 움직이고 있다는 것을, 나는 의심할 수 없었다.\n\n나는 워플 씨에게 여러 가지를 물었다. 이를테면, 그 사내가 언제 들어왔느냐고. 그는 그것을 내게 말해 주지 못했다. 그는 나를 보았고, 내 어깨 너머로 그 사내를 보았던 것이다. 한동안 그를 보고 나서야 비로소 그자가 누구인지 알아보기 시작했으나, 처음부터 그는 어렴풋이 그 사내를 나와 결부시켰고, 옛 마을 시절에 어떻게든 나와 관계있는 자로 알아보았다. 그 사내는 어떤 차림이었는가? 형편이 넉넉해 보였으나, 그 밖에 눈에 띌 만한 데는 없었다. 그가 보기에는 검은 옷차림인 듯했다. 얼굴에 무슨 흉터라도 있었는가? 아니, 없었던 것 같다고 했다. 나 역시 없었으리라 여겼다. 골똘히 생각에 잠겨 있던 터라 내 뒤의 사람들을 특별히 눈여겨보지는 않았으나, 조금이라도 흉이 있는 얼굴이었다면 내 주의를 끌었을 법하다고 생각했기 때문이다.\n\n워플 씨가 기억해 낼 수 있는 것이든 내가 캐낼 수 있는 것이든 죄다 내게 들려준 뒤, 저녁의 노고를 위로하고자 그에게 알맞은 다과를 조금 대접하고 나서, 우리는 헤어졌다. 내가 템플에 이르렀을 때는 열두 시와 한 시 사이였고, 대문은 닫혀 있었다. 내가 안으로 들어가 집으로 향할 때 내 가까이에는 아무도 없었다.\n\n허버트가 돌아와 있어서, 우리는 난롯가에서 매우 진지하게 의논했다. 그러나 그날 밤 내가 알아낸 것을 웨믹에게 알리고, 우리가 그의 언질을 기다리고 있음을 그에게 상기시키는 것 외에는, 달리 할 수 있는 일이 없었다. 내가 너무 자주 그 성에 드나들면 그를 곤란하게 만들 수도 있겠다 싶어, 나는 이 전갈을 편지로 보냈다. 잠자리에 들기 전에 편지를 써서 나가 부쳤는데, 이번에도 내 가까이에는 아무도 없었다. 허버트와 나는 그저 몹시 조심하는 것밖에는 달리 도리가 없다는 데 뜻을 모았다. 그리고 우리는 정말이지 몹시 조심했다 — 가능한 일이라면, 전보다 더 조심했다 — 그리고 나로 말하자면, 보트를 저어 지나갈 때를 빼고는 결코 칭크스 만 근처에 가지 않았으며, 그럴 때에도 다른 무엇을 바라보듯 그저 밀폰드 둑을 바라볼 뿐이었다.\n\n# Chapter XLVIII.\n\n앞 장에서 언급한 두 차례의 만남 가운데 두 번째 것은, 첫 번째 만남이 있고 나서 일주일쯤 지나 일어났다. 나는 다시금 다리 아래 부두에 내 배를 매어 두었는데, 시간은 오후로도 한 시간쯤 이른 때였다. 어디서 저녁을 먹을지 정하지 못한 채 나는 칩사이드 쪽으로 어슬렁어슬렁 올라갔고, 그 분주한 인파 가운데서도 분명 가장 갈피를 못 잡는 사람의 모습으로 그 거리를 따라 거닐고 있었다. 그때 누군가 나를 앞질러 오며 커다란 손을 내 어깨에 얹었다. 그것은 재거스 씨의 손이었고, 그는 그 손을 내 팔에 끼워 넣었다.\n\n\"우리가 같은 방향으로 가니, 핍, 함께 걸어도 되겠군. 어디로 가는 길인가?\"\n\n\"템플로 갈까 합니다.\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"모르겠다는 건가?\" 재거스 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"글쎄요.\" 나는 한 번쯤은 그를 반대 신문에서 이겨 보았다는 것이 기뻐서 대꾸했다. \"저는 정말 모릅니다. 아직 마음을 정하지 못했으니까요.\"\n\n\"저녁을 먹으러 가는 길이지?\" 재거스 씨가 말했다. \"그건 인정해도 상관없을 테지?\"\n\n\"네.\" 내가 대꾸했다. \"그건 인정해도 상관없습니다.\"\n\n\"그리고 선약은 없고?\"\n\n\"선약이 없다는 것도 인정해도 상관없습니다.\"\n\n\"그렇다면,\" 재거스 씨가 말했다. \"나와 함께 저녁을 들지.\"\n\n내가 막 사양하려는데, 그가 덧붙였다. \"웨믹도 올 거야.\" 그래서 나는 사양을 받아들임으로 바꾸었다 — 내가 내뱉은 몇 마디 말은 둘 중 어느 쪽의 시작으로도 쓸 수 있었으니까 — 그리고 우리는 칩사이드를 따라 걷다가 비스듬히 꺾어 리틀브리튼 쪽으로 들어섰다. 그동안 상점 진열창마다 불빛이 화려하게 피어올랐고, 거리의 가로등지기들은 오후의 북새통 한가운데서 사다리를 세울 땅조차 좀체 찾지 못한 채 위아래로 폴짝거리고 이리저리 뛰어다니며, 후멈스 여관에서 내 골풀 양초 탑이 그 으스스한 벽에 흰 눈을 떴던 것보다 더 많은 붉은 눈을, 모여드는 안개 속에 떠 올리고 있었다.\n\n리틀브리튼의 사무소에서는 하루의 업무를 마무리하는 늘 그대로의 편지 쓰기, 손 씻기, 촛불 끄기, 금고 잠그기가 이루어지고 있었다. 내가 재거스 씨의 난롯가에 우두커니 서 있자니, 오르락내리락하는 불꽃 때문에 선반 위의 두 석고상은 마치 나를 상대로 까꿍 놀이 같은 흉악한 장난을 치고 있는 것처럼 보였다. 한편 한쪽 구석에서 글을 쓰고 있는 재거스 씨를 침침하게 비추는 거칠고 굵은 사무용 양초 한 쌍은, 마치 교수형당한 의뢰인들의 무리를 추억하기라도 하듯 더러운 수의 같은 촛농을 두르고 있었다.\n\n우리는 셋이 다 함께 삯마차를 타고 제라드 거리로 갔다. 그리고 그곳에 닿자마자 저녁상이 차려졌다. 비록 그곳에서라면 나는 눈짓 하나로라도 웨믹의 월워스식 정서에 대해 아무리 먼 언급조차 떠올리지 않았을 테지만, 그래도 이따금 다정하게 그의 눈길과 마주치는 것쯤은 마다하지 않았을 것이다. 하지만 그것은 가능한 일이 아니었다. 그는 식탁에서 눈을 들 때마다 그 눈길을 재거스 씨에게로 돌렸고, 마치 웨믹이 쌍둥이라도 되어 이쪽은 엉뚱한 쪽이기라도 한 듯, 나에게는 메마르고 서먹하기만 했다.\n\n\"미스 해비셤의 그 쪽지를 핍 군에게 보냈나, 웨믹?\" 우리가 저녁을 들기 시작한 지 얼마 되지 않아 재거스 씨가 물었다.\n\n\"아닙니다, 선생님.\" 웨믹이 대답했다. \"막 우편으로 부치려던 참에 선생님께서 핍 군을 사무소로 데리고 오셨지요. 여기 있습니다.\" 그는 그것을 나에게가 아니라 자기 상관에게 건넸다.\n\n\"두 줄짜리 쪽지일세, 핍.\" 재거스 씨가 그것을 다시 넘겨주며 말했다. \"미스 해비셤이 자네 주소를 확실히 알지 못해 나한테 보내온 거야. 자네가 그분에게 말했던 어떤 사소한 용무 때문에 자네를 보고 싶다는군. 내려가 볼 텐가?\"\n\n\"네.\" 내가 그 쪽지에 눈을 던지며 말했는데, 내용은 정확히 그런 말투였다.\n\n\"언제 내려갈 생각인가?\"\n\n\"임박한 약속이 하나 있어서요.\" 나는 생선을 우편함에 집어넣고 있는 웨믹을 흘긋 보며 말했다. \"시간이 좀 불확실합니다만. 곧장 가야겠다고 생각합니다.\"\n\n\"핍 군이 곧장 갈 작정이라면,\" 웨믹이 재거스 씨에게 말했다. \"답장은 쓸 필요가 없겠군요, 아시겠지만.\"\n\n이 말을 지체하지 않는 편이 낫다는 암시로 받아들인 나는, 내일 가기로 마음을 정하고 그렇게 말했다. 웨믹은 포도주를 한 잔 마시고는 음울하게 만족스러운 기색으로 재거스 씨를 바라보았으나, 나는 보지 않았다.\n\n\"그래서 말이지, 핍! 우리 친구 거미가,\" 재거스 씨가 말했다. \"패를 다 펼쳤어. 판돈을 따 갔지.\"\n\n나로서는 동의하는 것만으로도 벅찼다.\n\n\"하! 그자는 장래성 있는 친구야 — 제 나름으로는 말이야 — 하지만 모든 일이 제 뜻대로 되지만은 않을 거야. 결국엔 더 센 쪽이 이기겠지만, 누가 더 센지는 우선 가려져야 하지. 만약 그자가 마음을 돌려, 그녀를 두들겨 패기라도 한다면 —\"\n\n\"설마,\" 내가 얼굴과 가슴이 달아오른 채 말을 가로막았다. \"그자가 그럴 만큼 비열한 놈이라고 진지하게 생각하시는 건 아니겠지요, 재거스 씨?\"\n\n\"나는 그렇다고 말하지 않았네, 핍. 하나의 경우를 가정하는 거야. 만약 그자가 마음을 돌려 그녀를 두들겨 팬다면, 어쩌면 힘에서는 자기 쪽이 우위를 점할지도 모르지. 하지만 그것이 지력의 문제라면, 그자는 분명 이기지 못할 거야. 그런 종류의 친구가 그런 상황에서 어떻게 될지에 대해 의견을 내놓는 건 요행에 맡기는 일이야. 두 가지 결과 사이의 동전 던지기니까.\"\n\n\"그 두 가지가 무엇인지 여쭤봐도 될까요?\"\n\n\"우리 친구 거미 같은 친구는,\" 재거스 씨가 대답했다. \"두들겨 패거나 아니면 굽실거리거나, 둘 중 하나야. 굽실거리며 으르렁댈 수도 있고, 굽실거리되 으르렁대지 않을 수도 있지. 하지만 그자는 두들겨 패거나 아니면 굽실거려. 웨믹에게 그의 의견을 물어보게.\"\n\n\"두들겨 패거나 아니면 굽실거리거나죠.\" 웨믹은 조금도 나를 향해 말하지 않은 채 말했다.\n\n\"그러니, 벤틀리 드러믈 부인을 위하여.\" 재거스 씨가 운반대에서 더 고급한 포도주가 든 디캔터를 집어 우리 각자와 자신의 잔에 따르며 말했다. \"우열의 문제가 그 부인이 만족할 만하게 결판나기를! 부인과 신사 양쪽이 다 만족할 만하게야 결코 결판나지 않을 테지. 자, 몰리, 몰리, 몰리, 몰리, 오늘은 어찌 그리 굼뜬가!\"\n\n그가 그녀에게 말을 건넸을 때 그녀는 식탁 위에 접시를 놓으며 그의 팔꿈치 곁에 있었다. 그녀는 접시에서 손을 떼며 한두 걸음 물러섰고, 무언가 변명을 신경질적으로 중얼거렸다. 그리고 그녀가 말할 때 그 손가락의 어떤 움직임이 내 주의를 사로잡았다.\n\n\"무슨 일인가?\" 재거스 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"아무것도 아닙니다. 다만 우리가 이야기하던 그 화제가,\" 내가 말했다. \"저에게는 좀 괴로운 것이라서요.\"\n\n그녀의 손가락 움직임은 뜨개질하는 동작 같았다. 그녀는 자기 주인을 바라보며 서 있었는데, 자기가 가도 되는 것인지, 아니면 그가 더 할 말이 있어 자기가 가면 다시 불러 세울 것인지를 알지 못하는 듯했다. 그녀의 눈길은 무척 골똘했다. 분명 나는 바로 이런 눈과 이런 손을, 아주 최근의 어느 잊지 못할 자리에서 본 적이 있었다!\n\n그가 그녀를 물러가게 하자, 그녀는 미끄러지듯 방을 나갔다. 그러나 그녀는 여전히 거기 있는 것처럼 또렷이 내 앞에 남아 있었다. 나는 그 손을 보았고, 그 눈을 보았고, 그 흘러내리는 머리카락을 보았다. 그리고 그것들을, 내가 알고 있는 다른 손, 다른 눈, 다른 머리카락과 견주어 보았고, 그것들이 잔인한 남편과 험난한 삶을 이십 년 겪고 나면 어떻게 될지와도 견주어 보았다. 나는 그 가정부의 손과 눈을 다시 바라보며, 내가 지난번에 — 혼자가 아니라 — 그 폐허가 된 정원을 거닐고 버려진 양조장을 지날 때 나를 덮쳤던 그 설명할 수 없는 느낌을 떠올렸다. 나는 그 똑같은 느낌이, 어느 역마차 창문에서 나를 바라보던 얼굴과 나를 향해 흔들던 손을 보았을 때 다시 돌아왔던 일을 떠올렸다. 그리고 그것이, 내가 마차를 타고 — 혼자가 아니라 — 어두운 거리에서 별안간 쏟아진 불빛 사이를 지날 때, 또다시 돌아와 번개처럼 내 주위에서 번쩍였던 일을 떠올렸다. 나는 어떻게 연상의 한 고리가 그 극장에서의 식별을 도왔는지를, 그리고 전에는 빠져 있던 그런 고리가, 내가 우연히 에스텔러라는 이름에서 그 뜨개질하는 동작의 손가락과 그 골똘한 눈으로 잽싸게 옮겨 갔을 때 어떻게 내게 단단히 박혀 버렸는지를 떠올렸다. 그리고 나는 이 여자가 에스텔러의 어머니라는 것을 절대적으로 확신했다.\n\n재거스 씨는 내가 에스텔러와 함께 있는 것을 본 적이 있었으니, 내가 굳이 감추려 애쓰지 않았던 그 감정을 놓쳤을 리 없었다. 그는 내가 그 화제가 괴롭다고 말하자 고개를 끄덕이고는, 내 등을 툭 치고, 다시 포도주를 돌리고, 저녁 식사를 계속했다.\n\n가정부가 다시 나타난 것은 단 두 번뿐이었고, 그나마 방에 머무는 시간은 무척 짧았으며, 재거스 씨는 그녀에게 퉁명스러웠다. 그러나 그녀의 손은 에스텔러의 손이었고, 그녀의 눈은 에스텔러의 눈이었으니, 설령 그녀가 백 번을 다시 나타났다 해도 나는 내 확신이 진실이라는 것에 대해 더 확신하지도 덜 확신하지도 못했을 것이다.\n\n그날 저녁은 따분했다. 웨믹은 포도주가 돌아오면 그것을 순전히 업무처럼 따라 마셨으니 — 마치 봉급날이 돌아오면 봉급을 타듯 — 그러면서 줄곧 상관에게 눈을 둔 채, 반대 신문에 끊임없이 대비하고 있는 상태로 앉아 있었다. 포도주의 양으로 말하자면, 그의 우편함은 다른 어떤 우편함이 편지의 양에 대해 그러하듯 무심하고 또 채비되어 있었다. 내 시각에서 보자면, 그는 내내 엉뚱한 쪽 쌍둥이였고, 겉모습만 월워스의 웨믹을 닮았을 뿐이었다.\n\n우리는 일찍 작별을 고하고 함께 자리를 떴다. 재거스 씨의 장화 무더기 속을 더듬어 우리 모자를 찾고 있을 때조차, 나는 진짜 쪽 쌍둥이가 돌아오고 있음을 느꼈다. 그리고 우리가 제라드 거리를 월워스 방향으로 여섯 걸음도 채 가기 전에, 나는 내가 진짜 쪽 쌍둥이와 팔짱을 끼고 걷고 있으며, 엉뚱한 쪽 쌍둥이는 저녁 공기 속으로 증발해 버렸음을 알았다.\n\n\"자!\" 웨믹이 말했다. \"그게 끝났군요! 그분은 산 채로 짝을 찾을 수 없는 놀라운 사람이지요. 하지만 저는 그분과 저녁을 들 때면 제 나사를 단단히 죄어야 한다는 느낌이 들어요 — 그리고 나사를 풀고 나면 더 편하게 저녁을 들지요.\"\n\n나는 이것이 사정을 잘 짚은 말이라고 느꼈고, 그에게 그렇게 말했다.\n\n\"당신 말고 다른 사람한테는 안 할 말이지요.\" 그가 대답했다. \"당신과 나 사이에 오간 말은 더 멀리 가지 않는다는 걸 아니까요.\"\n\n나는 그에게 미스 해비셤의 양녀인 벤틀리 드러믈 부인을 본 적이 있느냐고 물었다. 그는 없다고 했다. 너무 갑작스럽지 않으려고, 나는 이어서 노친네와 미스 스키핀스 이야기를 꺼냈다. 미스 스키핀스를 언급하자 그는 사뭇 음흉한 표정을 짓더니, 거리에 멈춰 서서 고개를 한 번 휘젓고는, 은근한 자랑기가 아주 없지는 않은 손짓으로 코를 풀었다.\n\n\"웨믹,\" 내가 말했다. \"내가 처음 재거스 씨의 사택에 가기 전에, 당신이 나더러 저 가정부를 눈여겨보라고 했던 걸 기억합니까?\"\n\n\"제가요?\" 그가 대답했다. \"아, 그랬을 법하군요. 제기랄,\" 그가 불쑥 덧붙였다. \"그랬지요, 기억나요. 보아하니 제 나사가 아직 다 풀리지 않았나 봅니다.\"\n\n\"길들여진 야수라고, 당신이 그녀를 그렇게 불렀지요.\"\n\n\"그럼 당신은 그녀를 뭐라고 부릅니까?\"\n\n\"같은 말로요. 재거스 씨가 그녀를 어떻게 길들였습니까, 웨믹?\"\n\n\"그건 그분의 비밀이지요. 그녀는 그분과 함께한 지 오랜 세월이 되었습니다.\"\n\n\"그녀의 사연을 좀 들려주면 좋겠습니다. 나는 그것을 알게 되는 데 특별한 흥미를 느끼거든요. 당신과 나 사이에 오간 말은 더 멀리 가지 않는다는 걸 알지 않습니까.\"\n\n\"글쎄요!\" 웨믹이 대답했다. \"저도 그녀의 사연을 다 알지는 못합니다 — 그러니까 전부는 모른다는 거지요. 하지만 제가 아는 만큼은 말씀드리지요. 물론 우리는 지금 사적이고 개인적인 자격으로 있는 거고요.\"\n\n\"물론이지요.\"\n\n\"한 이십 년쯤 전에, 그 여자는 살인죄로 올드베일리에서 재판을 받았고, 무죄로 풀려났습니다. 그녀는 무척 잘생긴 젊은 여자였고, 제 생각에 집시 피가 좀 섞여 있었던 것 같아요. 어쨌든, 짐작하시겠지만, 한번 끓어오르면 어지간히 뜨거운 여자였지요.\"\n\n\"하지만 무죄로 풀려났다고요.\"\n\n\"재거스 씨가 그녀의 변호를 맡았는데,\" 웨믹이 의미심장한 표정을 가득 담아 말을 이었다. \"정말 놀라운 방식으로 그 사건을 다루었지요. 절망적인 사건이었고, 그분으로서는 비교적 초창기였는데도, 그분은 모두의 감탄을 받을 만큼 그 사건을 요리했어요. 사실, 그 사건이 그분을 만들어 주었다고 해도 거의 과언이 아닙니다. 그분은 경찰서에서 여러 날 날마다 손수 매달려 기소조차 못 하게 맞섰고, 정작 자기가 직접 변론할 수 없는 재판에서는 변호인 밑에 앉아서 — 다들 알고 있었지요 — 모든 소금과 후추를 다 쳐 넣었지요. 살해된 사람은 여자였습니다 — 나이가 족히 열 살은 더 많고, 훨씬 덩치가 크고, 훨씬 힘이 센 여자였어요. 질투로 인한 사건이었습니다. 둘 다 떠돌이 생활을 했는데, 여기 제라드 거리의 이 여자는 아주 젊은 나이에, 우리 식으로 말하면 빗자루를 뛰어넘는 식으로 어느 떠돌이 사내와 혼인했고, 질투심으로 말하자면 완벽한 분노의 화신이었지요. 살해된 여자는 — 나이로 보면 그 사내에게 분명 더 어울리는 짝이었는데 — 하운슬로 히스 근처의 어느 헛간에서 죽은 채 발견되었습니다. 격렬한 몸싸움이, 어쩌면 격투가 있었던 모양이에요. 그녀는 멍이 들고 긁히고 찢긴 데다, 끝내는 목을 졸려 질식당했습니다. 그런데, 이 여자 말고 다른 어떤 사람을 연루시킬 만한 합당한 증거가 없었고, 재거스 씨는 주로 그녀가 그 일을 해낼 수 있었을 리 없다는 그 개연성 없음에 사건의 근거를 두었지요. 분명히 말씀드리지만,\" 웨믹이 내 소맷자락을 건드리며 말했다. \"그분은 그때만큼은 그녀의 손 힘에 대해 결코 늘어놓지 않았답니다. 요즘은 가끔 그러기는 합니다만.\"\n\n나는 그 만찬 날에 그분이 우리에게 그녀의 손목을 내보였던 일을 웨믹에게 이야기한 바 있었다.\n\n\"그런데 말입니다, 선생!\" 웨믹이 말을 이었다. \"마침 그렇게 됐어요 — 그렇게 됐다는 거, 아시겠어요? — 이 여자가 체포된 순간부터 어찌나 교묘하게 옷을 차려입었던지, 실제보다 훨씬 가냘파 보였단 말이지요. 특히, 그녀의 소매는 어찌나 솜씨 있게 만들어졌던지 팔이 아주 가녀린 모양새로 보였다고 다들 기억하고 있습니다. 그녀 몸에는 멍이 한두 군데 있을 뿐이었어요 — 떠돌이에게는 아무것도 아닌 정도였지요 — 그런데 손등은 찢겨 있었고, 그래서 문제는 이것이었습니다, 그게 손톱으로 그런 것이냐? 그런데 재거스 씨는, 그녀가 제 얼굴 높이만큼 높지는 않으나 손을 빼낸 채로는 도저히 빠져나갈 수 없는 무성한 가시덤불 사이를 헤치고 나왔다는 것을 보여 주었지요. 그리고 그 가시덤불 조각이 실제로 그녀의 살갗에서 발견되어 증거로 제출되었고, 게다가 문제의 그 가시덤불을 조사해 보니 누군가 뚫고 지나간 흔적이 있고, 여기저기 그녀의 옷자락 조각과 작은 핏방울이 묻어 있더라는 사실도 함께 제출되었습니다. 하지만 그분이 펼친 가장 대담한 수는 이것이었어요. 그녀의 질투심을 입증하려고, 그녀가 살인이 벌어진 무렵에 그 사내에게 복수하려고 그 사내와의 사이에서 난 자기 아이를 — 세 살쯤 된 — 미친 듯이 죽였다는 강한 혐의를 받고 있다는 점을 세우려는 시도가 있었거든요. 재거스 씨는 그것을 이런 식으로 받아쳤지요. '우리는 이것이 손톱 자국이 아니라 가시덤불 자국이라고 주장하며, 여러분께 그 가시덤불을 보여 드립니다. 여러분은 그것이 손톱 자국이라고 주장하며, 그녀가 자기 아이를 죽였다는 가설을 세웁니다. 그렇다면 여러분은 그 가설의 모든 귀결을 받아들여야 합니다. 우리가 알 수 없는 일이나, 그녀가 제 아이를 죽였을 수도 있고, 그 아이가 그녀에게 매달리다가 그 손을 할퀴었을 수도 있습니다. 그렇다면 어떻습니까? 여러분은 그녀를 제 아이를 죽인 죄로 재판하고 있지 않습니다. 어째서 그러지 않습니까? 이 사건으로 말하자면, 여러분이 기어이 할퀸 자국을 들이대겠다면, 우리는 이렇게 말합니다, 여러분이 그것을 지어낸 것이 아니라고 논의를 위해 가정하더라도, 우리가 알 수 없는 일이나, 여러분 스스로가 이미 그것을 설명해 버린 것일 수도 있다고 말이지요.' 요컨대 말입니다, 선생,\" 웨믹이 말했다. \"재거스 씨는 배심원단으로서는 도저히 당해 낼 수 없는 상대였고, 그들은 손을 들고 말았지요.\"\n\n\"그 뒤로 그녀는 줄곧 그분 밑에서 일해 온 겁니까?\"\n\n\"네. 하지만 그뿐이 아닙니다.\" 웨믹이 말했다. \"그녀는 무죄로 풀려난 직후 곧장 그분 밑으로 들어갔어요, 지금처럼 길들여진 채로 말이지요. 그 이후로 직무에 관련된 이런저런 일을 배우기는 했지만, 그녀는 처음부터 길들여져 있었습니다.\"\n\n\"그 아이의 성별을 기억합니까?\"\n\n\"여자아이였다고 합니다.\"\n\n\"오늘 밤 나에게 더 할 말은 없습니까?\"\n\n\"없습니다. 당신 편지는 받아서 없애 버렸어요. 없습니다.\"\n\n우리는 다정하게 작별 인사를 나누었고, 나는 새로운 생각거리를 안은 채 집으로 돌아갔다. 다만 묵은 생각으로부터는 아무런 위안도 얻지 못한 채로.\n\n# Chapter XLIX.\n\n미스 해비셤의 쪽지를 호주머니에 넣어 두었다. 혹시 그녀가 변덕을 부려 내가 이렇게 빨리 다시 새티스 하우스에 나타난 것을 보고 놀라움을 표할 경우, 그것이 내 신분 증명이 되어 줄 것이었기 때문이다. 이튿날 나는 다시 역마차를 타고 내려갔다. 그러나 중간 지점의 여관에서 내려 그곳에서 아침을 먹고, 남은 거리는 걸어서 갔다. 사람들 눈에 띄지 않는 한적한 길로 조용히 읍내에 들어가고, 또 같은 방식으로 떠나려 했기 때문이다.\n\n번화가 뒤편의 메아리 울리는 고요한 안뜰들을 지날 무렵, 하루 중 가장 좋은 햇빛은 이미 사라지고 없었다. 옛날 수도사들이 한때 식당과 정원을 두었던, 그리고 이제는 그 튼튼한 벽들이 초라한 헛간과 마구간 노릇에 떠밀려 쓰이고 있는 폐허의 구석들은, 무덤 속의 그 옛 수도사들만큼이나 적막했다. 남의 눈을 피해 서둘러 걸어가는 내 귀에, 대성당의 종소리는 전에 없이 한결 더 슬프고 한결 더 아득하게 들려왔다. 그리고 오래된 오르간의 울림은 마치 장송곡처럼 내 귀에 실려 왔다. 또한 까마귀들은 잿빛 탑 주위를 맴돌고 수도원 정원의 헐벗은 높은 나무들 위에서 흔들거리며, 이곳이 변해 버렸노라고, 에스텔라는 영영 이곳을 떠나 버렸노라고 내게 외치는 것만 같았다.\n\n전에 본 적이 있는 어느 나이 든 여자가 문을 열어 주었다. 뒤뜰 건너편의 별채에 사는 하인들 가운데 하나였다. 불을 켠 양초가 옛날처럼 어두운 복도 안에 놓여 있었고, 나는 그것을 집어 들고 혼자서 계단을 올라갔다. 미스 해비셤은 자기 방에 있지 않고, 층계참 건너편의 더 큰 방에 있었다. 문 앞에서 헛되이 두드린 끝에 안을 들여다보니, 그녀는 다 해진 의자에 앉아 잿빛으로 사그라진 난롯불에 바싹 다가앉아, 그 불을 응시하는 데 넋을 잃고 있었다.\n\n전에 곧잘 그러했듯이, 나는 안으로 들어가, 그녀가 눈을 들면 나를 볼 수 있는 곳에, 낡은 벽난로 선반에 손을 댄 채 섰다. 그녀에게는 더없는 외로움의 기운이 감돌고 있었다. 설령 그녀가 내가 그녀 탓으로 돌릴 수 있는 것보다 더 깊은 상처를 고의로 내게 입혔다 한들, 나는 연민에 사로잡혔을 것이다. 내가 그녀를 가엾이 여기며 서서, 세월이 흐르는 동안 나 또한 그 집의 몰락한 운명의 한 부분이 되어 버렸구나 하고 생각하고 있을 때, 그녀의 눈이 내게 머물렀다. 그녀는 빤히 바라보더니 낮은 목소리로 말했다. \"이게 진짜인가?\"\n\n\"저예요, 핍. 어제 재거스 씨가 부인의 쪽지를 전해 주셨고, 저는 한시도 지체하지 않았습니다.\"\n\n\"고맙네. 고마워.\"\n\n내가 다 해진 의자를 또 하나 난롯가로 가져와 앉을 때, 나는 그녀의 얼굴에 떠오른 새로운 표정을 알아차렸다. 마치 나를 두려워하는 듯한 표정이었다.\n\n\"내가 하고 싶은 건,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"자네가 지난번에 여기 왔을 때 내게 꺼냈던 그 이야기를 매듭짓는 일일세. 그리고 내가 온통 돌덩이만은 아니라는 걸 자네에게 보여 주고 싶네. 하지만 어쩌면 자네는, 이제 내 마음속에 인간적인 무언가가 있다는 걸 도무지 믿지 못할지도 모르겠군?\"\n\n내가 안심시키는 몇 마디를 건네자, 그녀는 마치 나를 만지려는 듯 떨리는 오른손을 뻗었다. 그러나 내가 그 동작의 의미를 이해하기도 전에, 또 어떻게 받아들여야 할지 알기도 전에, 그녀는 그 손을 도로 거두어들였다.\n\n\"자네는 자네 친구를 대신해 말하면서, 유익하고 좋은 일을 어떻게 하면 되는지 내게 일러 줄 수 있다고 했었지. 자네가 이루어졌으면 하는 어떤 일이라고, 그렇지 않았나?\"\n\n\"제가 무척이나 이루어졌으면 하는 일이지요.\"\n\n\"그게 뭔가?\"\n\n나는 그녀에게 그 동업 관계의 비밀스러운 내막을 설명하기 시작했다. 그러나 그리 멀리 나아가지 못해, 나는 그녀의 표정으로 미루어 그녀가 내 말의 내용보다는 나에 대해 두서없이 생각하고 있다고 판단했다. 정말로 그런 것 같았다. 내가 말을 멈추었을 때, 그녀가 그 사실을 의식한다는 기색을 보이기까지 한참이 지났으니까.\n\n\"자네가 말을 멈춘 건,\" 그녀가 그때, 전처럼 나를 두려워하는 기색으로 물었다. \"내가 너무도 미워서, 나한테 말을 거는 걸 견딜 수 없어서인가?\"\n\n\"아닙니다, 아니에요.\" 나는 대답했다. \"어떻게 그런 생각을 하실 수 있어요, 미스 해비셤! 제가 멈춘 건, 부인이 제 말을 따라오지 못하신다고 생각해서였습니다.\"\n\n\"아마 그랬는지도 모르지.\" 그녀가 한 손을 머리에 짚으며 대답했다. \"다시 시작해 주게. 나는 다른 무언가를 보고 있을 테니. 잠깐! 자, 이제 말해 보게.\"\n\n그녀는 가끔 그녀의 버릇이 되어 있던 그 단호한 태도로 한 손을 지팡이에 얹고, 억지로 정신을 집중하려는 강한 표정을 지으며 불을 바라보았다. 나는 설명을 이어 가, 내 돈으로 그 거래를 완결 짓고 싶었으나 이 점에서 좌절을 겪었다고 그녀에게 말했다. 그 이야기의 그 부분은(나는 그녀에게 일러 두었다) 내 설명의 일부가 될 수 없는 사정들과 얽혀 있는데, 그것이 다른 사람의 묵직한 비밀이기 때문이라고 했다.\n\n\"그래서!\" 그녀가 고개를 끄덕여 수긍하면서도 나를 보지는 않은 채 말했다. \"그래, 그 매입을 완결 짓는 데 돈이 얼마나 모자라는가?\"\n\n나는 그것을 말하기가 적이 두려웠다. 큰 액수로 들렸기 때문이다. \"구백 파운드입니다.\"\n\n\"내가 이 일에 쓸 돈을 자네에게 준다면, 자네는 자네 자신의 비밀을 지켜 온 만큼 내 비밀도 지켜 주겠나?\"\n\n\"꼭 그만큼 충실히 지키겠습니다.\"\n\n\"그러면 자네 마음이 더 편안해지겠나?\"\n\n\"훨씬 더 편안해지겠지요.\"\n\n\"자네는 지금 몹시 불행한가?\"\n\n그녀는 여전히 나를 보지 않은 채, 그러나 평소답지 않은 동정 어린 어조로 이 질문을 던졌다. 나는 그 순간 대답할 수가 없었다. 목소리가 나오지 않았기 때문이다. 그녀는 왼팔을 지팡이 머리 위에 가로 얹고, 그 위에 가만히 이마를 댔다.\n\n\"행복과는 거리가 멉니다, 미스 해비셤. 하지만 제게는 부인이 아시는 그 어떤 것과도 다른 불안의 원인들이 있습니다. 그것이 제가 말씀드렸던 그 비밀들입니다.\"\n\n잠시 후, 그녀는 고개를 들어 다시 불을 바라보았다.\n\n\"자네에게 다른 불행의 원인들이 있다고 내게 말해 주다니, 고결하군. 그게 정말인가?\"\n\"너무도 정말입니다.\"\n\n\"핍, 내가 자네를 위해 해 줄 수 있는 건 자네 친구를 돕는 일뿐인가? 그 일이 이루어진 것으로 친다면, 자네 자신을 위해 내가 해 줄 수 있는 일은 아무것도 없는가?\"\n\n\"없습니다. 그렇게 물어봐 주셔서 감사합니다. 그렇게 물어봐 주시는 그 어조에는 더욱 감사하고요. 하지만 아무것도 없습니다.\"\n\n그녀는 이내 자리에서 일어나, 황폐해진 방 안에서 글씨 쓸 도구를 찾아 둘러보았다. 그곳에는 아무것도 없었으므로, 그녀는 호주머니에서 빛바랜 금테를 두른 누런 상아 수첩을 꺼내, 목에 걸린 빛바랜 금 케이스에 든 연필로 거기에 글을 썼다.\n\n\"자네는 아직도 재거스 씨와 사이좋게 지내고 있나?\"\n\n\"그럼요. 어제 그분과 저녁을 함께 했습니다.\"\n\n\"이건 그가 자네에게 그 돈을 지급하라는 위임장일세. 자네 친구를 위해 자네가 책임 없이 마음대로 쓰라고 말이야. 나는 이곳에 돈을 두지 않네. 하지만 자네가 차라리 재거스 씨가 이 일을 전혀 모르길 바란다면, 내가 자네에게 돈을 보내 주겠네.\"\n\n\"감사합니다, 미스 해비셤. 그분에게서 받는 것에 저는 조금도 거리낌이 없습니다.\"\n\n그녀는 자기가 쓴 것을 내게 읽어 주었다. 그것은 직설적이고 명료했으며, 분명 내가 그 돈을 받음으로써 사사로운 이익을 챙긴다는 의심에서 나를 벗어나게 하려는 의도였다. 나는 그녀의 손에서 수첩을 건네받았는데, 그 손은 또다시 떨렸고, 연필이 매달린 사슬을 풀어 내 손에 쥐여 줄 때는 더욱 심하게 떨렸다. 이 모든 일을 그녀는 나를 보지 않은 채 했다.\n\n\"첫 장에 내 이름이 적혀 있네. 혹시 자네가 언젠가 내 이름 아래에 '나는 그녀를 용서한다'라고 써 줄 수 있다면, 설령 내 부서진 심장이 한 줌 먼지가 되고도 한참 뒤일지라도, 부디 그렇게 해 주게!\"\n\n\"오, 미스 해비셤.\" 내가 말했다. \"지금 당장이라도 그렇게 할 수 있습니다. 쓰라린 잘못들이 있었지요. 제 삶은 눈먼, 감사할 줄 모르는 삶이었습니다. 그리고 저는 용서와 인도가 너무도 절실히 필요한 처지라, 부인을 원망하고 있을 수가 없습니다.\"\n\n그녀는 얼굴을 돌린 이래 처음으로 내게로 얼굴을 향하더니, 놀랍게도, 아니 두렵게도, 내 발치에 무릎을 꿇었다. 두 손을 모아 내게로 들어 올렸는데, 그것은 그녀의 가엾은 심장이 어리고 싱싱하고 온전했던 시절, 어머니 곁에서 하늘을 향해 자주 들어 올렸을 그 모습 그대로였다.\n\n백발에 초췌한 얼굴을 한 그녀가 내 발치에 무릎 꿇은 것을 보자 온몸에 전율이 일었다. 나는 일어나시라고 간청하며 그녀를 일으켜 세우려 두 팔로 감쌌으나, 그녀는 자기 손에 가장 가까이 닿는 내 손을 꼭 쥐고는, 그 위에 고개를 떨군 채 흐느낄 뿐이었다. 나는 그녀가 눈물을 흘리는 것을 그때까지 한 번도 본 적이 없었고, 그렇게 감정을 쏟아 내는 것이 그녀에게 도움이 될지도 모른다는 바람에서, 아무 말 없이 그녀 위로 몸을 굽혔다. 그녀는 이제 무릎을 꿇은 게 아니라, 바닥에 주저앉아 있었다.\n\n\"오!\" 그녀가 절망스럽게 부르짖었다. \"내가 무슨 짓을 한 거지! 내가 무슨 짓을 한 거지!\"\n\n\"미스 해비셤, 부인께서 저를 해친 게 무엇이냐는 뜻이라면, 제가 대답해 드리지요. 아주 사소합니다. 어떤 상황에서든 저는 그녀를 사랑했을 테니까요. 그녀가 결혼했습니까?\"\n\n\"그래.\"\n\n그것은 굳이 물을 필요도 없는 질문이었다. 황폐한 집 안에 감도는 또 하나의 새로운 황량함이 이미 내게 그렇게 일러 주었으니까.\n\n\"내가 무슨 짓을 한 거지! 내가 무슨 짓을 한 거지!\" 그녀는 두 손을 비틀고 백발을 움켜쥐며, 이 부르짖음으로 거듭거듭 돌아왔다. \"내가 무슨 짓을 한 거지!\"\n\n나는 어떻게 대답해야 할지, 어떻게 그녀를 위로해야 할지 알 수 없었다. 감수성이 예민한 아이를 데려다, 그녀의 광적인 원한과 짓밟힌 애정과 상처 입은 자존심이 복수의 길을 찾아낸 그 틀에 맞춰 빚어낸 것이 통탄할 짓이라는 것을 나는 너무도 잘 알았다. 그러나 그녀가 햇빛을 차단함으로써 그보다 한없이 더 많은 것을 차단해 버렸다는 것을, 칩거함으로써 천 가지 자연스럽고 치유하는 영향들로부터 스스로를 가두어 버렸다는 것을, 외로이 골똘히 생각에 잠긴 그녀의 마음이 병들어 버렸다는 것을 — 자신을 지으신 분이 정해 놓은 질서를 거스르는 모든 마음이 그러하듯, 그리고 그래야만 하고 또 그러기 마련이듯 — 나는 똑같이 잘 알았다. 그러니 내가 어찌 연민 없이 그녀를 바라볼 수 있었겠는가. 그녀가 처한 폐허 속에서, 자신이 놓인 이 땅에 그토록 깊이 어울리지 않는 모습 속에서, 그 형벌을 보면서 말이다. 참회의 헛됨, 회한의 헛됨, 무가치함의 헛됨, 그리고 이 세상의 저주가 되어 온 그 밖의 끔찍한 헛됨들처럼, 하나의 광적인 집념이 되어 버린 슬픔의 헛됨 속에서 말이다.\n\n\"자네가 며칠 전 그녀에게 말을 거는 걸 보기 전까지, 그리고 한때 내가 직접 느꼈던 것을 비춰 보여 주는 거울을 자네에게서 보기 전까지, 나는 내가 무슨 짓을 했는지 알지 못했네. 내가 무슨 짓을 한 거지! 내가 무슨 짓을 한 거지!\" 그렇게 그녀는 또다시, 스무 번이고 쉰 번이고, 자신이 무슨 짓을 했느냐고 되뇌었다!\n\n\"미스 해비셤.\" 그녀의 부르짖음이 잦아들었을 때, 내가 말했다. \"부인의 마음과 양심에서 저는 떨쳐 버리셔도 좋습니다. 하지만 에스텔라는 다른 경우예요. 만약 부인이 그녀의 올바른 본성의 일부를 그녀에게서 떼어 놓은, 그 그릇되이 저지른 일을 단 한 조각이라도 되돌릴 수 있다면, 그렇게 하는 것이 백 년 동안 과거를 한탄하는 것보다 나을 겁니다.\"\n\n\"그래, 그래, 나도 안다네. 하지만, 핍 — 이보게!\" 그녀의 새로운 애정 속에는 나를 향한 진심 어린, 여성다운 연민이 깃들어 있었다. \"이보게! 이것만은 믿어 주게. 그 애가 처음 내게 왔을 때, 나는 그 애를 나 같은 비참함에서 구해 주려 했네. 처음에는 그 이상은 바라지 않았어.\"\n\n\"그러셨겠지요, 그러셨겠지요!\" 내가 말했다. \"그러셨기를 바랍니다.\"\n\"하지만 그 애가 자라면서, 무척 아름다워질 조짐을 보이자, 나는 점점 더 못된 짓을 했네. 칭찬으로, 보석으로, 가르침으로, 그리고 내 교훈을 뒷받침하고 일깨워 주는 경고로서 늘 그 애 앞에 놓여 있던 이 내 꼬락서니로, 나는 그 애의 마음을 훔쳐 내고 그 자리에 얼음을 박아 넣었어.\"\n\n\"차라리,\" 나는 말하지 않을 수 없었다. \"멍들거나 부서질지언정, 그녀에게 자연스러운 마음을 그대로 남겨 두는 편이 나았을 겁니다.\"\n\n그 말에 미스 해비셤은 한동안 넋이 나간 듯 나를 바라보더니, 다시 터뜨렸다. 자신이 무슨 짓을 했느냐고!\n\n\"내 이야기를 전부 안다면,\" 그녀가 호소했다. \"자네는 나를 어느 정도 가엾이 여기고, 나를 좀 더 잘 이해하게 될 걸세.\"\n\n\"미스 해비셤.\" 나는 할 수 있는 한 조심스럽게 대답했다. \"저는 부인의 이야기를 정말로 알고 있다고 말씀드릴 수 있을 것 같습니다. 그리고 제가 이 동네를 처음 떠난 이래로 줄곧 알고 있었습니다. 그 이야기는 제게 커다란 연민을 불러일으켰고, 저는 그 이야기와 그것이 미친 영향들을 이해한다고 생각합니다. 우리 사이에 오간 이야기가, 에스텔라에 관해 제가 부인께 한 가지 여쭐 만한 구실이 될까요? 지금의 그녀가 아니라, 그녀가 처음 이곳에 왔을 때의 그녀에 관해서요?\"\n\n그녀는 다 해진 의자에 두 팔을 얹고, 그 위에 머리를 기댄 채 바닥에 앉아 있었다. 내가 이렇게 말하자 그녀는 나를 정면으로 바라보며 대답했다. \"계속하게.\"\n\n\"에스텔라는 누구의 아이였습니까?\"\n\n그녀는 고개를 가로저었다.\n\n\"모르세요?\"\n\n그녀는 다시 고개를 가로저었다.\n\n\"하지만 재거스 씨가 그녀를 이곳으로 데려왔거나, 이곳으로 보냈겠지요?\"\n\n\"데려왔지.\"\n\n\"어떻게 그렇게 되었는지 말씀해 주시겠습니까?\"\n\n그녀는 낮은 속삭임으로, 조심스럽게 대답했다. \"나는 오랫동안 이 방들에 갇혀 지내고 있었네(얼마나 오래였는지는 모르겠어. 여기 시계들이 어떤 시간을 가리키는지는 자네도 알 테니). 그때 나는 그에게, 기르고 사랑하고 내 운명에서 구해 줄 어린 여자아이를 하나 갖고 싶다고 말했지. 그를 처음 본 건, 나를 위해 이곳을 폐허로 만들어 달라고 그를 불렀을 때였네. 나와 세상이 갈라서기 전에, 신문에서 그에 관해 읽었던 거지. 그는 그런 고아 아이를 한번 찾아보겠노라고 했어. 어느 날 밤 그가 잠든 그 애를 이곳으로 데려왔고, 나는 그 애를 에스텔라라고 불렀네.\"\n\n\"그때 그녀가 몇 살이었는지 여쭤도 될까요?\"\n\n\"두세 살쯤. 그 애 자신은 아무것도 몰라. 자기가 고아로 남겨졌고 내가 거두었다는 것밖에는.\"\n\n나는 그 여자가 그녀의 어머니라는 것을 너무도 확신한 나머지, 내 마음속에서 그 사실을 입증할 어떤 증거도 필요하지 않았다. 그러나 누구의 마음에라도, 이 둘의 연관은 분명하고 곧바른 것이라고 나는 생각했다.\n\n대화를 더 길게 끈다고 한들, 내가 더 무엇을 이루기를 바랄 수 있었겠는가? 나는 허버트를 위한 일에 성공했고, 미스 해비셤은 에스텔라에 관해 자기가 아는 것을 모두 내게 말해 주었으며, 나는 그녀의 마음을 달래 주기 위해 할 수 있는 말과 행동을 다 했다. 우리가 또 어떤 말로 헤어졌는지는 중요하지 않다. 우리는 헤어졌다.\n\n내가 아래층으로 내려가 바깥 공기 속으로 나왔을 때는 땅거미가 내리고 있었다. 나는 들어올 때 문을 열어 주었던 여자에게, 아직은 수고를 끼치지 않겠지만 떠나기 전에 이곳을 한 바퀴 둘러보겠노라고 일러 두었다. 다시는 이곳에 오지 못하리라는 예감이 들었고, 사그라드는 빛이 이곳을 마지막으로 바라보기에 어울린다고 느꼈기 때문이다.\n\n오래전에 내가 그 위를 걸었던, 그리고 그 이후로 여러 해 동안 빗물이 떨어져 군데군데 썩어 들고, 세워져 있는 통 위에는 작은 늪과 물웅덩이를 남겨 놓은, 그 어수선하게 널린 술통들 곁을 지나, 나는 폐허가 된 정원으로 걸음을 옮겼다. 나는 정원을 빙 둘러보았다. 허버트와 내가 한판 싸움을 벌였던 그 구석을 돌아, 에스텔라와 내가 함께 거닐던 그 오솔길들을 돌아서. 모든 것이 어찌나 차갑고, 어찌나 외롭고, 어찌나 음산하던지!\n\n돌아오는 길에 양조장을 거치며, 나는 그 정원 쪽 끝에 난 작은 문의 녹슨 빗장을 들어 올리고 안으로 걸어 들어갔다. 반대편 문으로 나가려던 참이었는데 — 이제는 열기가 쉽지 않았다. 축축한 나무가 뒤틀려 부풀고, 경첩이 헐거워지고, 문지방에는 곰팡이가 무성하게 자라 가로막고 있었으니까 — 그때 나는 뒤를 돌아보려고 고개를 돌렸다. 그 사소한 동작의 순간에 어린 시절의 한 기억이 놀라운 힘으로 되살아났고, 나는 미스 해비셤이 들보에 목매달려 있는 것을 본 것만 같았다. 그 인상이 어찌나 강렬했던지, 나는 그것이 환상임을 깨닫기도 전에 들보 아래 서서 머리끝부터 발끝까지 부들부들 떨었다 — 물론 곧바로 정신을 차리기는 했지만.\n\n그곳과 그 시각의 애처로움, 그리고 비록 한순간이었으나 이 환영이 안겨 준 크나큰 공포로 인해, 나는 한때 에스텔라가 내 마음을 쥐어짰던 그 자리에서 내 머리카락을 쥐어뜯었던, 그 활짝 열린 나무문 사이로 나오면서 형언할 수 없는 두려움을 느꼈다. 앞뜰로 걸어 들어가면서, 나는 망설였다. 그 여자가 열쇠를 가지고 있는 잠긴 대문에서 나를 내보내 달라고 그녀를 부를지, 아니면 먼저 위층으로 올라가 미스 해비셤이 내가 두고 온 그대로 안전하고 무사한지 확인할지. 나는 후자를 택해 위층으로 올라갔다.\n\n나는 그녀를 두고 왔던 방 안을 들여다보았고, 그녀가 난롯불에 바싹 다가앉아 다 해진 의자에 등을 내게로 향한 채 앉아 있는 것을 보았다. 조용히 물러가려고 고개를 빼던 그 순간, 나는 커다란 불길이 확 치솟는 것을 보았다. 같은 순간, 나는 그녀가 비명을 지르며 내게로 달려오는 것을 보았다. 온몸에 휘몰아치는 불길을 두른 채, 적어도 자기 키만큼이나 높이 머리 위로 불꽃을 솟구치며.\n\n나는 두 겹 망토가 달린 두꺼운 외투를 입고 있었고, 팔에는 또 다른 두툼한 코트를 걸치고 있었다. 내가 그것들을 벗어, 그녀에게 달려들어, 그녀를 쓰러뜨리고, 그것들로 그녀를 뒤덮은 것. 같은 목적으로 식탁에서 그 커다란 천을 끌어내렸고, 그러면서 그 한가운데 쌓여 있던 썩은 더미와 그곳에 깃들어 있던 그 모든 흉측한 것들까지 함께 끌어 내린 것. 우리가 바닥에서 필사적인 원수처럼 몸싸움을 벌였고, 내가 그녀를 바싹 뒤덮을수록 그녀가 더욱 미친 듯이 비명을 지르며 벗어나려 한 것 — 이런 일들이 벌어졌다는 것을 나는 그 결과를 통해 알았지, 내가 느끼거나 생각하거나 내가 무엇을 했는지 안 것을 통해서가 아니었다. 우리가 그 커다란 식탁 옆 마룻바닥에 있다는 것을, 그리고 한순간 전만 해도 그녀의 빛바랜 신부 드레스였던 것이 아직 타고 있는 불씨 조각들이 되어 연기 자욱한 공기 속을 떠다닌다는 것을 알기 전까지, 나는 아무것도 알지 못했다.\n\n그러고 나서, 나는 둘러보았고, 놀란 딱정벌레들과 거미들이 마룻바닥 위로 달아나는 것을, 그리고 하인들이 숨 가쁜 비명을 지르며 문으로 들어오는 것을 보았다. 나는 여전히, 달아날지도 모르는 죄수를 붙들 듯, 온 힘을 다해 그녀를 억지로 짓누르고 있었다. 그리고 나는 그녀가 누구인지, 우리가 왜 몸싸움을 벌였는지, 그녀가 불길에 휩싸였었는지, 또 그 불길이 꺼졌는지조차 알았을까 싶다. 그녀의 옷이었던 그 불씨 조각들이 더는 타지 않고 우리 주위로 검은 소나기처럼 떨어지는 것을 볼 때까지는 말이다.\n\n그녀는 의식이 없었고, 나는 그녀를 옮기거나, 심지어 건드리기조차 두려웠다. 도움을 청해 사람을 보냈고, 나는 도움이 올 때까지 그녀를 붙들고 있었다. 마치, 만약 그녀를 놓아 버리면 불이 다시 일어나 그녀를 삼켜 버리기라도 할 것처럼 터무니없이 상상했던 것처럼(실제로 그랬던 것 같다). 외과 의사가 다른 구조 인력과 함께 그녀에게 다가왔을 때 내가 일어서고 보니, 놀랍게도 내 두 손이 모두 화상을 입어 있었다. 촉각으로는 그것을 전혀 알아차리지 못했던 것이다.\n\n진찰 결과, 그녀가 심각한 부상을 입었으나, 그 부상 자체만으로는 결코 가망 없는 것이 아니라는 진단이 내려졌다. 위험은 주로 신경성 충격에 있었다. 외과 의사의 지시에 따라, 그녀의 침대를 그 방으로 옮겨 와 커다란 식탁 위에 얹었는데, 마침 그 식탁이 그녀의 상처를 처치하기에 안성맞춤이었다. 한 시간 후 그녀를 다시 보았을 때, 그녀는 정말로, 일찍이 내가 그녀가 지팡이로 내려치는 것을 보았던 그 자리에, 언젠가 자기가 누워 있게 될 거라고 말하는 것을 들었던 바로 그 자리에 누워 있었다.\n\n사람들 말로는 그녀의 옷은 흔적도 없이 다 타 버렸다는데도, 그녀에게는 여전히 그 옛날의 섬뜩한 신부 같은 모습이 어느 정도 남아 있었다. 사람들이 그녀를 목까지 흰 솜으로 덮어 놓았고, 그 위에 흰 시트를 헐겁게 덮어씌운 채 누워 있었으므로, 한때 존재했다가 변해 버린 어떤 것의 유령 같은 기운이 여전히 그녀에게 감돌고 있었던 것이다.\n\n하인들에게 물어, 나는 에스텔라가 파리에 있다는 것을 알아냈고, 외과 의사로부터 다음 편 우편으로 그녀에게 편지를 써 보내겠다는 약속을 받아 냈다. 미스 해비셤의 가족 문제는 내가 떠맡았다. 매슈 포켓 씨에게만 연락하고, 나머지 사람들에게 알리는 일은 그가 알아서 하도록 맡겨 둘 작정이었다. 이 일을 나는 이튿날, 읍내로 돌아오자마자 허버트를 통해 처리했다.\n\n그날 저녁, 그녀가 일어난 일에 대해 차분하게, 그러나 어딘가 무시무시한 생기를 띠고 이야기하던 한때가 있었다. 자정 무렵부터 그녀는 헛소리를 하기 시작했다. 그 후로는 차츰, 낮고 엄숙한 목소리로 \"내가 무슨 짓을 한 거지!\" 하고 수없이 되뇌는 것이 굳어졌다. 그러고는 \"그 애가 처음 왔을 때, 나는 그 애를 나 같은 비참함에서 구해 주려 했어.\" 그러고는 \"연필을 들고 내 이름 아래에 써 주게, '나는 그녀를 용서한다!'라고.\" 그녀는 이 세 문장의 순서를 결코 바꾸지 않았으나, 가끔 그중 하나에서 한 단어를 빠뜨리곤 했다. 다른 단어를 끼워 넣는 일은 결코 없이, 늘 빈자리를 남겨 둔 채 다음 단어로 넘어갔다.\n\n그곳에서 내가 해 줄 수 있는 일이 없었고, 또 집과 더 가까운 곳에, 그녀의 헛소리조차 내 마음에서 몰아낼 수 없었던 그 절박한 불안과 두려움의 까닭이 있었으므로, 나는 그날 밤 사이에 마음을 정했다. 이른 아침 역마차로 돌아가되, 일 마일쯤 걸어 나가 읍내를 벗어난 곳에서 마차를 타기로. 그리하여 아침 여섯 시쯤, 나는 그녀 위로 몸을 굽혀 내 입술을 그녀의 입술에 댔다. 마침 그녀가, 입맞춤을 받는 일 따위로 멈추지 않은 채, \"연필을 들고 내 이름 아래에 써 주게, '나는 그녀를 용서한다'라고.\" 하고 말하던 바로 그때였다.\n\n# Chapter L.\n\n간밤에 두세 번, 그리고 아침에 다시 한 번 내 손은 새로 처치를 받았다. 왼팔은 팔꿈치까지 심하게 데었고, 어깨께까지는 그보다 덜한 화상을 입었다. 몹시 아팠으나 불길이 그쪽으로 번졌으니, 나는 이만하기를 다행으로 여겼다. 오른손은 손가락을 움직일 수 있을 만큼 덜 데었다. 물론 붕대를 감았으나, 왼손과 왼팔에 비하면 훨씬 덜 불편했다. 그 둘은 삼각건에 매달아 두었다. 그리고 나는 외투를 망토처럼, 어깨에 헐겁게 걸치고 목께에서 여미는 식으로밖에 걸칠 수가 없었다. 불에 머리카락이 그슬리긴 했으나, 머리나 얼굴은 무사했다.\n\n허버트는 해머스미스에 내려가 자기 아버지를 뵙고 온 뒤, 우리 하숙으로 돌아와 그날 하루를 나를 돌보는 데 바쳤다. 그는 더없이 다정한 간병인이었다. 정해진 시간마다 붕대를 풀어, 미리 준비해 둔 차가운 약물에 적셨다가 다시 감아 주었는데, 그 참을성 있는 부드러움에 나는 깊이 감사했다.\n\n처음에 소파에 가만히 누워 있을 때, 나는 불길의 번쩍임과 그 황급함과 소음, 그리고 사납게 타오르던 냄새의 인상을 떨쳐 내기가 고통스러우리만치 어려웠다. 아니 불가능했다고 해도 좋을 것이다. 잠깐이라도 졸면, 미스 해비셤의 비명에, 그리고 머리 위로 그 높다란 불기둥을 인 채 내게로 달려드는 그녀의 모습에 잠을 깼다. 이 마음의 고통은 내가 겪은 그 어떤 육체의 고통보다 맞서 싸우기가 훨씬 힘들었다. 그것을 알아챈 허버트는 내 주의를 딴 데로 붙들어 두려고 온갖 애를 썼다.\n\n우리 둘 다 그 배 이야기는 입에 올리지 않았으나, 둘 다 그것을 생각하고 있었다. 그 사실은 우리가 그 화제를 피한다는 점에서, 그리고 내 손이 다시 쓸 수 있게 되는 것을 몇 주가 아니라 몇 시간의 문제로 삼자고 — 무언의 — 합의를 보았다는 점에서 분명히 드러났다.\n\n허버트를 보았을 때 내가 던진 첫 질문은 물론 강 아래쪽이 다 무사한가 하는 것이었다. 그가 더없는 확신과 명랑함으로 그렇다고 답했으므로, 우리는 하루가 저물어 갈 때까지 그 화제를 다시 꺼내지 않았다. 그러나 그때, 바깥 빛보다는 난롯불 빛에 기대어 붕대를 갈던 허버트가 스스로 그 이야기로 돌아갔다.\n\n\"어젯밤에 프로비스랑 같이 앉아 있었어, 핸들, 꼬박 두 시간을 말이야.\"\n\n\"클라라는 어디 있었고?\"\n\n\"가엾고 사랑스러운 사람!\" 허버트가 말했다. \"저녁 내내 그루팬드그림 영감 곁을 오르락내리락했지. 그 양반은 클라라가 눈앞에서 사라지기만 하면 줄곧 바닥을 쿵쿵 두드려 댔어. 하지만 영감이 오래 버틸 것 같진 않아. 럼주에 후추를 — 후추에 럼주를 곁들여 대니, 그 쿵쿵거림도 머지않아 끝날 것 같단 말이지.\"\n\n\"그럼 그때 자네는 결혼하게 되는 건가, 허버트?\"\n\n\"안 그러고서야 그 사랑스러운 사람을 어떻게 보살피겠나? — 팔을 소파 등받이에 얹어 봐, 친구. 나는 여기 앉아서, 언제 떨어지는지도 모를 만큼 천천히 붕대를 풀어 줄 테니. 프로비스 얘기를 하던 참이지. 그런데 말이야, 핸들, 그 사람이 점점 나아지고 있어.\"\n\n\"지난번에 마지막으로 봤을 때 그분이 한결 부드러워졌더라고 자네한테 말했잖나.\"\n\n\"그랬지. 정말 그래. 어젯밤엔 무척 말이 많아져서, 자기 살아온 이야기를 더 들려주더군. 전에 여기서 어떤 여자 얘기를 하다가, 그 여자 때문에 엄청 고생했다면서 말을 끊었던 거 기억나? — 아팠어?\"\n\n나는 움찔했으나, 그의 손길 때문은 아니었다. 그의 말이 나를 흠칫하게 했던 것이다.\n\n\"그건 잊고 있었는데, 자네가 말하니까 이제 기억이 나는군, 허버트.\"\n\n\"그래! 그 사람이 자기 인생의 그 부분으로 들어갔는데, 어둡고 험한 대목이야. 들려줄까? 아니면 지금은 자네한테 부담이 될까?\"\n\n\"꼭 들려주게. 한마디도 빼지 말고.\"\n\n허버트는 내 대답이 그가 헤아릴 수 있는 것보다 다소 다급하거나 간절했다는 듯, 몸을 앞으로 숙여 나를 더 가까이 들여다보았다. \"머리는 시원해?\" 그가 내 머리를 만지며 말했다.\n\n\"아주 시원해.\" 내가 말했다. \"프로비스가 뭐라고 했는지 말해 주게, 허버트.\"\n\n\"그게 말이야,\" 허버트가 말했다. \"— 붕대 하나가 아주 멋지게 풀렸군, 이제 차가운 걸 댈 거야 — 처음엔 움츠러들게 되지, 가엾은 친구, 안 그래? 하지만 곧 편안해질 거야 — 그게 말이지, 그 여자는 젊은 여자였고, 질투심이 강한 여자였고, 복수심에 불타는 여자였어. 복수심이라면, 핸들, 더할 나위 없는 정도였지.\"\n\n\"더할 나위 없다니, 어느 정도였는데?\"\n\n\"살인까지. — 그 예민한 데가 너무 차갑게 와닿진 않아?\"\n\n\"느껴지지 않아. 어떻게 죽였대? 누구를 죽였대?\"\n\n\"뭐, 그 짓이 그렇게 끔찍한 이름값까진 못 했을지도 몰라.\" 허버트가 말했다. \"하지만 그 일로 재판을 받았고, 재거스 씨가 그 여자를 변호했지. 그 변호로 얻은 명성 덕분에 프로비스가 처음 그 양반 이름을 알게 된 거야. 희생자는 그 여자보다 더 힘센 다른 여자였는데, 둘 사이에 — 헛간에서 — 몸싸움이 있었어. 누가 먼저 시작했는지, 얼마나 정당했는지 부당했는지는 알 수 없을지 몰라도, 어떻게 끝났는지는 분명히 의심할 여지가 없어. 희생자가 목이 졸려 죽은 채로 발견됐으니까.\"\n\n\"그 여자는 유죄 판결을 받았고?\"\n\n\"아니, 무죄로 풀려났어. — 가엾은 핸들, 아프게 했군!\"\n\n\"이보다 더 부드러울 순 없네, 허버트. 그래서? 또 뭐가 있나?\"\n\n\"무죄로 풀려난 이 젊은 여자와 프로비스 사이엔 어린아이가 하나 있었어. 프로비스가 무척이나 아끼던 어린아이였지. 내가 말한 대로 그 질투의 대상이 목 졸려 죽은 바로 그날 밤, 그 젊은 여자가 잠깐 프로비스 앞에 나타나서는, 그 아이를(아이는 자기가 데리고 있었어) 없애 버리겠다고, 다시는 못 보게 하겠다고 맹세하고는 사라져 버렸어. — 자, 제일 안 좋은 팔이 다시 삼각건에 편안히 들어갔고, 이제 오른손만 남았는데, 이건 훨씬 쉬운 일이지. 이 빛으로 하는 게 더 밝은 빛으로 하는 것보다 나아. 가엾게 물집 잡힌 자리가 너무 또렷이 보이지 않을 때 손이 제일 안 떨리거든. — 숨쉬기가 불편한 건 아니지, 친구? 호흡이 가빠 보이는데.\"\n\n\"아마 그런가 봐, 허버트. 그 여자는 맹세를 지켰어?\"\n\n\"여기서부터가 프로비스 인생의 가장 어두운 대목이야. 지켰어.\"\n\n\"그러니까, 그 사람이 지켰다고 말하는 거겠지.\"\n\n\"아니, 당연하지, 친구.\" 허버트가 놀란 어조로 대답하며, 다시 몸을 앞으로 숙여 나를 가까이 들여다보았다. \"그 사람이 전부 말한 거야. 내겐 다른 정보가 없어.\"\n\n\"그렇지, 그야 당연하지.\"\n\n\"자, 그가 그 아이 어미를 나쁘게 대했는지, 아니면 잘 대했는지는,\" 허버트가 말을 이었다. \"프로비스도 말하지 않아. 하지만 그 여자는 그가 이 난롯가에서 우리한테 들려준 그 비참한 삶을 사오 년쯤 함께했고, 그는 그 여자한테 연민을 느꼈고, 너그럽게 굴었던 모양이야. 그래서 이 죽임당한 아이에 대해 증언하라는 요구를 받게 될까 봐, 그리하여 그 여자의 죽음에 빌미를 줄까 봐, 그는 (아이를 그토록 애통해하면서도) 몸을 숨기고, 그의 표현대로 어둠 속에 자취를 감추어, 사건에서도 재판에서도 비켜나 있었어. 그저 질투가 비롯된 발단이 된 에이블이라는 어떤 사내로 막연히 입에 오를 뿐이었지. 무죄 판결 뒤에 그 여자는 사라졌고, 그렇게 그는 아이도 아이 어미도 잃어버렸어.\"\n\n\"하나 묻고 싶은데 —\"\n\n\"잠깐만, 친구, 거의 다 됐어. 그 악령 같은 콤피슨, 숱한 악당들 가운데서도 가장 못된 그 악당이, 그 시절 프로비스가 자취를 감추고 있었다는 것과 그렇게 한 까닭을 알고서, 물론 나중에 그 약점을 빌미 삼아 그를 더 가난하게 옭아매고 더 혹독하게 부려 먹었던 거야. 어젯밤에도 분명히 드러나더군. 바로 이 점이 프로비스의 적개심에 가시 돋친 날을 세워 주었다는 게.\"\n\n\"내가 알고 싶은 건,\" 내가 말했다. \"특히, 허버트, 그 사람이 이 일이 언제 일어났는지 말하던가?\"\n\n\"특히 그거 말이지? 그럼 그 점에 대해 그가 뭐라 했는지 기억을 더듬어 보지. 그 사람 표현이, '한 이십 년쯤 전에, 콤피슨이란 놈하고 어울리기 시작한 거의 바로 그 직후에' 그랬어. 그 작은 교회 묘지에서 그 사람을 마주쳤을 때 자네 몇 살이었지?\"\n\n\"일곱 살 무렵이었던 것 같아.\"\n\n\"그래. 그러면 그땐 그 일이 일어난 지 삼사 년쯤 됐다고 그러더군. 그리고 자네를 보니, 그렇게 비극적으로 잃어버린 어린 딸이 떠올랐다는 거야. 그 아이가 살아 있었다면 자네 또래쯤 됐을 거라면서.\"\n\n\"허버트,\" 잠시 침묵한 뒤 나는 다급한 투로 말했다. \"내가 창문 빛으로 더 잘 보여, 아니면 난롯불 빛으로 더 잘 보여?\"\n\n\"난롯불 빛으로 잘 보여.\" 허버트가 다시 가까이 다가오며 대답했다.\n\n\"나를 봐.\"\n\n\"보고 있어, 친구.\"\n\n\"나를 만져 봐.\"\n\n\"만지고 있어, 친구.\"\n\n\"내가 무슨 열에 들떠 있다거나, 어젯밤 사고로 머리가 많이 어지러워졌다고 걱정하는 건 아니지?\"\n\n\"아-아니, 친구.\" 허버트가 나를 한참 살펴본 뒤에 말했다. \"좀 흥분하긴 했지만, 자네는 멀쩡해.\"\n\n\"내가 멀쩡하다는 건 나도 알아. 그리고 우리가 강 아래쪽에 숨겨 둔 그 사람이, 에스텔라의 아버지야.\"\n\n# Chapter LI.\n\n에스텔라의 혈통을 캐내고 입증하는 일에 열을 올렸을 때 내가 어떤 목적을 마음에 품고 있었는지, 나는 말할 수 없다. 그 문제가 나보다 더 현명한 사람의 입을 통해 내 앞에 던져지기 전까지는, 내 머릿속에서 또렷한 형태를 갖추지 못했음이 곧 드러날 것이다.\n\n그러나 허버트와 그 중대한 대화를 나누고 나자, 나는 이 일을 끝까지 추적해야 한다는, 그대로 내버려 두어서는 안 되며 재거스 씨를 만나 적나라한 진실에 다다라야 한다는 열병 같은 확신에 사로잡혔다. 내가 정말로 이것을 에스텔라를 위해 한다고 느꼈는지, 아니면 그토록 오래 나를 둘러싸고 있던 낭만적 흥미의 몇 가닥 빛줄기를, 그 안위를 내가 그토록 걱정하던 사내에게로 옮겨 놓는 것이 기뻤던 것인지, 나는 정말 알 수가 없다. 어쩌면 후자의 가능성이 진실에 더 가까울지도 모른다.\n\n어쨌든 그날 밤 나는 제라드 가로 달려 나가는 것을 좀처럼 자제할 수가 없었다. 만약 그렇게 한다면 십중팔구 몸져누워 아무짝에도 쓸모없게 될 텐데, 그렇게 되면 우리 도망자의 안위가 나에게 달려 있는 마당에 큰일이라는 허버트의 설득만이 내 조바심을 붙들어 매 주었다. 무슨 일이 있어도 내일은 재거스 씨에게 가겠다는 것을 거듭거듭 다짐한 끝에, 나는 마침내 가만히 있기로, 상처를 돌보게 하기로, 집에 머물기로 굴복했다. 이튿날 아침 일찍 우리는 함께 나섰고, 스미스필드 옆 길트스퍼 가 모퉁이에서 나는 허버트를 시티 쪽으로 가는 그의 길로 보내고, 나는 리틀브리튼으로 향했다.\n\n재거스 씨와 웨믹이 사무소 장부를 검토하고, 영수증을 대조하고, 모든 것을 정리하는 정기적인 때가 있었다. 그런 때면 웨믹은 자기 장부와 서류를 들고 재거스 씨의 방으로 들어갔고, 위층 사무원 가운데 하나가 바깥 사무실로 내려왔다. 그날 아침 웨믹의 자리에 그런 사무원이 있는 것을 보고 나는 무슨 일이 벌어지고 있는지 알았다. 그러나 재거스 씨와 웨믹이 함께 있는 것이 나로서는 싫지 않았다. 그러면 웨믹이 내가 그를 곤란하게 할 말을 한마디도 하지 않는다는 것을 직접 들을 수 있을 테니까.\n\n팔에 붕대를 감고 외투를 어깨에 헐겁게 걸친 내 모습은 내 목적에 도움이 되었다. 도시에 도착하자마자 재거스 씨에게 사고에 관한 간략한 보고를 보내 두긴 했으나, 이제는 그에게 모든 세부 사항을 들려주어야 했다. 그리고 이 상황의 특수성 덕분에 우리의 대화는 전보다 덜 메마르고 덜 딱딱했으며, 증거의 규칙에 덜 엄격하게 매여 있었다. 내가 그 재난을 묘사하는 동안 재거스 씨는 평소 버릇대로 난로 앞에 서 있었다. 웨믹은 의자에 등을 기댄 채, 두 손을 바지 주머니에 넣고, 펜을 우편함 꽂이에 가로질러 꽂아 둔 채 나를 응시했다. 내 마음속에서 그 공무적 절차와 떼려야 뗄 수 없는 그 야만스러운 두 개의 석고상은, 지금 이 순간 불 냄새가 나는 것은 아닌지 충혈된 얼굴로 곰곰이 따져 보는 듯했다.\n\n내 이야기가 끝나고 그들의 질문도 동나자, 나는 허버트를 위해 구백 파운드를 수령할 수 있다는 미스 해비셤의 위임장을 내놓았다. 내가 그 수첩을 건네자 재거스 씨의 두 눈은 머릿속으로 조금 더 깊이 물러났으나, 그는 이내 그것을 웨믹에게 넘기며 자기 서명을 받아 수표를 끊으라고 지시했다. 그 일이 진행되는 동안 나는 글씨를 쓰는 웨믹을 지켜보았고, 재거스 씨는 잘 닦은 장화 위에서 몸을 가누며 좌우로 흔들면서 나를 지켜보았다. \"유감이군, 핍.\" 그가 서명한 수표를 내가 주머니에 넣자 그가 말했다. \"우리가 자네를 위해서는 아무것도 해 주는 게 없으니 말이야.\"\n\n\"미스 해비셤께서 친절하게도,\" 내가 대답했다, \"저를 위해 뭔가 해 줄 일이 없겠느냐고 물으셨는데, 저는 없다고 말씀드렸습니다.\"\n\n\"누구나 제 일은 제가 알아야 하는 법이지.\" 재거스 씨가 말했다. 그리고 나는 웨믹의 입술이 \"동산\"이라는 말을 빚어내는 것을 보았다.\n\n\"내가 자네였다면 없다고는 말하지 않았을 거야.\" 재거스 씨가 말했다. \"하지만 사람은 누구나 제 일을 제일 잘 아는 법이니.\"\n\n\"사람의 일이란,\" 웨믹이 나를 향해 다소 나무라는 투로 말했다, \"곧 동산이지요.\"\n\n이제야말로 내 마음에 품고 있던 주제를 좇을 때가 왔다고 여긴 나는, 재거스 씨를 향해 돌아서며 말했다.\n\n\"그렇지만 저는 미스 해비셤께 무언가를 청하긴 했습니다, 선생님. 그분의 양녀에 관해 어떤 정보를 좀 알려 달라고 청했고, 그분은 자신이 가진 것을 모두 제게 주셨습니다.\"\n\n\"그랬나?\" 재거스 씨가 몸을 앞으로 굽혀 자기 장화를 들여다보더니 다시 몸을 폈다. \"허! 내가 미스 해비셤이었다면 그렇게는 하지 않았을 것 같군. 하지만 그분이야 제 일을 제일 잘 아시겠지.\"\n\n\"저는 미스 해비셤의 양녀의 내력을 미스 해비셤 자신보다 더 많이 알고 있습니다, 선생님. 저는 그 애의 어머니를 압니다.\"\n\n재거스 씨가 캐묻듯 나를 바라보며 \"어머니?\" 하고 되뇌었다.\n\n\"저는 사흘 안에 그 애의 어머니를 본 적이 있습니다.\"\n\n\"그래?\" 재거스 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"그리고 선생님께서도 보셨지요. 게다가 선생님께서는 그보다 훨씬 더 최근에 보셨고요.\"\n\n\"그래?\" 재거스 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"어쩌면 저는 에스텔라의 내력을 선생님보다도 더 많이 알고 있는지도 모릅니다.\" 내가 말했다. \"저는 그 애의 아버지도 압니다.\"\n\n재거스 씨의 태도에 어떤 멈춤이 일었는데 — 그는 너무도 침착해 태도를 바꾸지는 않았으나, 그것이 형언할 수 없을 만큼 주의 깊은 멈춤에 이르는 것까지는 어쩌지 못했다 — 그 멈춤은 그가 그 애의 아버지가 누구인지 모른다는 것을 내게 확신시켜 주었다. 이는 프로비스가 자신을 어둠 속에 숨겨 왔다는 그의 이야기(허버트가 되풀이해 들려준 대로)에서 내가 강하게 의심하던 바였다. 나는 그것을, 프로비스 자신이 사 년쯤 뒤에야, 더는 자기 신원을 주장할 이유가 없어졌을 때에야 재거스 씨의 의뢰인이 되었다는 사실에 짜 맞추었다. 그러나 전에는 재거스 씨 쪽의 이런 무지를 확신할 수 없었지만, 지금은 제법 확신할 수 있었다.\n\n\"그래! 자네가 그 아가씨의 아버지를 안다고, 핍?\" 재거스 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"네.\" 내가 대답했다. \"그리고 그의 이름은 프로비스입니다. 뉴사우스웨일스에서 온.\"\n\n내가 그 말을 하자 재거스 씨조차 흠칫했다. 그것은 사람이 보일 수 있는 가장 미미한 흠칫함이었고, 가장 조심스럽게 억눌리고 가장 빨리 제압된 것이었으나, 그래도 그는 흠칫했다. 비록 그것을 손수건을 꺼내는 동작의 일부로 만들어 버리긴 했지만. 웨믹이 그 발표를 어떻게 받아들였는지 나는 말할 수 없다. 그때는 그를 차마 쳐다볼 수 없었으니까. 재거스 씨의 예리함이, 그와 나 사이에 그가 모르는 어떤 교감이 있었음을 알아챌까 두려웠기 때문이다.\n\n\"그래, 핍, 무슨 증거로,\" 손수건을 코까지 절반쯤 가져가다 멈춘 채 재거스 씨가 아주 냉정하게 물었다, \"프로비스가 이런 주장을 하는 건가?\"\n\n\"그는 그런 주장을 하지 않습니다.\" 내가 말했다. \"한 번도 한 적이 없고, 자기 딸이 살아 있다는 것을 알지도 믿지도 못하고 있습니다.\"\n\n이번만큼은 그 강력한 손수건도 제구실을 못했다. 내 대답이 너무도 뜻밖이어서, 재거스 씨는 늘 하던 동작을 마무리하지 못한 채 손수건을 도로 주머니에 넣고, 팔짱을 끼고는, 미동도 없는 얼굴로, 그러나 준엄하게 주의를 기울여 나를 바라보았다.\n\n그리하여 나는 그에게 내가 아는 모든 것을, 그리고 그것을 어떻게 알게 되었는지를 들려주었다. 다만 한 가지만은 유보하여, 실은 웨믹에게서 알게 된 것을 미스 해비셤에게서 알게 된 것으로 그가 추측하도록 내버려 두었다. 나는 정말이지 그 점에 대해서는 무척 조심했다. 또한 내가 들려줄 이야기를 모두 마치고, 한동안 말없이 재거스 씨의 시선을 마주하고 난 뒤에야 비로소 나는 웨믹 쪽으로 시선을 돌렸다. 마침내 웨믹 쪽으로 눈을 돌렸을 때, 나는 그가 펜을 꽂이에서 빼 들고 자기 앞의 탁자에 몰두해 있는 것을 보았다.\n\n\"허!\" 재거스 씨가 마침내 탁자 위의 서류 쪽으로 움직이며 말했다. \"웨믹, 핍 군이 들어왔을 때 자네가 어느 항목을 보고 있었지?\"\n\n그러나 나는 그런 식으로 떨쳐지는 것을 받아들일 수 없어, 그에게 나를 좀 더 솔직하고 사내답게 대해 달라고 격정적인, 거의 분개에 가까운 호소를 했다. 나는 그에게 내가 빠져들었던 헛된 희망과, 그것이 지속된 시간의 길이와, 내가 해낸 발견을 일깨워 주었다. 그리고 내 마음을 짓누르는 위험을 넌지시 비쳤다. 나는 방금 내가 전한 신뢰의 보답으로, 그에게서 약간의 신뢰를 받을 만한 자격이 분명히 있다고 내 처지를 표명했다. 나는 그를 탓하지도, 의심하지도, 불신하지도 않으나, 다만 그에게서 진실에 대한 확언을 듣고 싶을 뿐이라고 말했다. 그리고 만약 그가 어째서 그것을 원하는지, 어째서 내게 그럴 권리가 있다고 여기는지 묻는다면, 그가 그런 가련한 꿈 따위에 아무리 관심이 없다 해도, 나는 에스텔라를 오래도록 진심으로 사랑해 왔으며, 비록 그 애를 잃고 빼앗긴 삶을 살아가야 할지언정, 그 애와 관련된 것은 무엇이든 이 세상 그 무엇보다 여전히 더 가깝고 소중하다고 그에게 말하겠노라고 했다. 그리고 이 호소에도 재거스 씨가 미동도 없이 침묵한 채, 보아하니 도무지 완강하게 서 있는 것을 보고, 나는 웨믹을 향해 돌아서서 말했다. \"웨믹, 저는 당신이 다정한 마음을 가진 사람이라는 걸 압니다. 저는 당신의 즐거운 집과, 당신의 노부친과, 당신이 자기 직업 생활을 새롭게 해 주는 그 모든 순박하고 명랑하고 장난기 어린 방식들을 보았습니다. 그러니 부디 저를 위해 재거스 씨에게 한마디 해 주시고, 모든 정황을 고려할 때 그가 제게 좀 더 터놓고 대해야 한다고 그분께 말씀해 주시기를 간청합니다!\"\n\n이 외침이 있은 뒤 재거스 씨와 웨믹이 서로를 바라본 것보다 더 기묘하게 서로를 바라보는 두 사람을 나는 본 적이 없다. 처음에는 웨믹이 당장 일자리에서 해고되는 것은 아닐까 하는 불안이 스쳤으나, 재거스 씨가 미소 비슷한 것으로 누그러지고 웨믹이 더 대담해지는 것을 보자 그 불안은 녹아 사라졌다.\n\n\"이게 다 무슨 소린가?\" 재거스 씨가 말했다. \"자네한테 노부친이 있다니, 게다가 자네한테 즐겁고 장난기 어린 방식이라니?\"\n\n\"뭐 어떻습니까!\" 웨믹이 응수했다. \"그것들을 여기로 데려오지만 않으면, 무슨 상관입니까?\"\n\n\"핍.\" 재거스 씨가 내 팔에 손을 얹고 드러내 놓고 미소 지으며 말했다. \"이자는 분명 온 런던에서 가장 교활한 사기꾼일 걸세.\"\n\n\"천만의 말씀입니다.\" 웨믹이 점점 더 대담해지며 응수했다. \"제가 보기엔 선생님이야말로 또 한 사람이시지요.\"\n\n다시 두 사람은 앞서의 그 기묘한 눈길을 주고받았는데, 저마다 여전히 상대가 자신을 속이고 있는 것은 아닌지 미심쩍어하는 기색이었다.\n\n\"자네한테 즐거운 집이 있다고?\" 재거스 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"업무에 지장만 안 준다면야,\" 웨믹이 응수했다, \"그렇다고 해 두지요. 자, 이렇게 선생님을 보고 있자니, 선생님께서도 이 모든 일에 싫증이 날 즈음이면 언젠가 선생님만의 즐거운 집을 가지려고 계획하고 궁리하고 계신다 해도 저는 놀라지 않겠습니다.\"\n\n재거스 씨는 지난날을 돌이키듯 두세 번 고개를 끄덕이더니, 실제로 한숨까지 내쉬었다. \"핍.\" 그가 말했다. \"'가련한 꿈' 따위는 이야기하지 마세나. 그런 일이라면 자네가 나보다 더 잘 알 테니까. 그런 종류의 경험이 자네 쪽이 훨씬 더 생생하지 않은가. 하지만 이제 이 다른 문제로 가 보세. 자네에게 한 가지 경우를 들어 보겠네. 명심하게! 나는 아무것도 인정하지 않네.\"\n\n그는 자신이 아무것도 인정하지 않는다고 명시적으로 말했음을 내가 충분히 이해했다고 선언하기를 기다렸다.\n\n\"자, 핍.\" 재거스 씨가 말했다. \"이런 경우를 가정해 보세. 어떤 여자가 자네가 말한 것과 같은 상황에서 제 아이를 숨겨 두었는데, 그녀의 법률 고문이, 자기 변호의 폭을 고려할 때 그 아이에 관한 사정이 어떤지 반드시 알아야겠다고 그녀에게 밝히는 바람에, 그 사실을 그에게 알려야만 했다고 가정해 보세. 그리고 같은 시기에 그가, 어느 별난 부유한 부인이 입양해서 길러 줄 아이를 하나 찾아 주는 임무를 맡고 있었다고 가정해 보세.\"\n\n\"따라가고 있습니다, 선생님.\"\n\n\"그가 악의 분위기 속에서 살았고, 그가 아이들에 대해 본 것이라곤 아이들이 정해진 파멸을 위해 엄청난 수로 태어나는 것뿐이었다고 가정해 보세. 그가 아이들이 형사 법정에서 엄숙하게 재판받으며 사람들 앞에 내세워지는 것을 자주 보았다고 가정해 보세. 그가 아이들이 투옥되고, 매질당하고, 유형 보내지고, 방치되고, 내쫓기고, 온갖 식으로 교수형 집행인에게 어울리는 자격을 갖추고, 자라나서 교수형에 처해지는 것을 늘 알고 지냈다고 가정해 보세. 그가 매일의 업무 생활에서 본 거의 모든 아이를, 그의 그물로 들어올 물고기로 자라날 알 무더기로 — 기소되고, 변호받고, 위증당하고, 고아가 되고, 어떤 식으로든 괴롭힘당할 알 무더기로 — 바라볼 만한 이유가 있었다고 가정해 보세.\"\n\n\"따라가고 있습니다, 선생님.\"\n\n\"핍, 이렇게 가정해 보세. 그 무더기 가운데 구해 낼 수 있는 예쁘고 어린 아이가 하나 있었는데, 아버지는 그 애가 죽었다고 믿어 감히 아무런 소동도 일으키지 못했고, 그 어머니에 관해서는 법률 고문이 이런 힘을 쥐고 있었다고 말이야. '나는 당신이 무슨 짓을 했고 어떻게 했는지 알고 있소. 당신은 이러저러하게 왔고, 혐의를 돌리려고 이러저러한 짓을 했소. 나는 그 전부를 통해 당신을 추적해 왔고, 그것을 당신에게 모두 말해 주는 거요. 당신의 혐의를 벗기기 위해 그 아이를 내세워야 할 필요가 생기지 않는 한 그 아이와 헤어지시오. 그리고 그럴 필요가 생기면 그 아이는 내세워질 거요. 그 아이를 내 손에 맡기시오, 그러면 나는 당신을 빼내기 위해 최선을 다하겠소. 당신이 구원받으면 당신 아이도 구원받는 것이고, 당신이 파멸한다 해도 당신 아이는 여전히 구원받는 거요.' 이 일이 행해졌고, 그 여자는 혐의를 벗었다고 가정해 보세.\"\n\n\"완벽하게 이해했습니다.\"\n\n\"하지만 나는 아무것도 인정하지 않는 거지?\"\n\n\"선생님께서는 아무것도 인정하지 않으시는 거지요.\" 그러자 웨믹이 되풀이했다. \"인정 없음.\"\n\n\"핍, 이렇게 가정해 보세. 격정과 죽음의 공포가 그 여자의 지성을 조금 흔들어 놓아, 그녀가 풀려났을 때 세상의 방식에서 겁을 집어먹고, 보호를 구하러 그에게 갔다고 말이야. 그가 그녀를 거두어 주었고, 그 옛날의 거칠고 사나운 본성이 터져 나오려는 낌새를 볼 때마다 옛날 방식대로 그녀에 대한 자신의 힘을 행사하여 그것을 억눌렀다고 가정해 보세. 이 가상의 경우를 이해하겠나?\"\n\n\"충분히요.\"\n\n\"그 아이가 자라나서 돈을 보고 시집갔다고 가정해 보세. 어머니가 아직 살아 있다고. 아버지가 아직 살아 있다고. 어머니와 아버지가 서로를 모른 채, 서로에게서 몇 마일, 몇 펄롱, 굳이 말하자면 몇 야드 안쪽에 살고 있다고. 그리고 그 비밀이, 자네가 그 낌새를 챈 것을 빼면, 여전히 비밀이라고 가정해 보세. 그 마지막 경우를 자네 자신에게 아주 신중하게 적용해 보게.\"\n\"그러겠습니다.\"\n\n\"웨믹에게도 그것을 자기 자신에게 아주 신중하게 적용해 보라고 청하겠네.\"\n\n그러자 웨믹이 말했다. \"그러겠습니다.\"\n\n\"누구를 위해 자네는 그 비밀을 밝히겠나? 아버지를 위해? 어머니를 찾는다고 해서 그가 별로 나아질 것 같지는 않은데. 어머니를 위해? 그녀가 그런 짓을 저질렀다면, 지금 있는 곳에 있는 편이 더 안전할 것 같은데. 딸을 위해? 남편에게 알려 주려고 그녀의 혈통을 밝혀내고, 이십 년이나 도망쳐 나와 거의 평생 안전하게 지속될 그 도피를 깨뜨려 그녀를 다시 치욕 속으로 끌고 들어가는 일이, 그녀에게 도움이 될 것 같지는 않은데. 하지만 핍, 자네가 그녀를 사랑했고, 자네가 생각하는 것보다 더 많은 사내들의 머릿속에 이런저런 때에 들어앉았던 그 '가련한 꿈'의 대상으로 그녀를 삼았다는 경우를 보태 보세. 그렇다면 자네에게 말하겠는데, 자네는 — 그리고 곰곰이 생각해 본다면 차라리 그편을 훨씬 빨리 택할 텐데 — 그 붕대 감은 왼손을 그 붕대 감은 오른손으로 잘라 내고, 그러고 나서 그 도끼를 저기 저 웨믹에게 넘겨 그것마저 잘라 내게 하는 편이 나을 걸세.\"\n\n나는 웨믹을 바라보았는데, 그의 얼굴은 무척 엄숙했다. 그는 엄숙하게 집게손가락으로 자기 입술을 건드렸다. 나도 똑같이 했다. 재거스 씨도 똑같이 했다. \"자, 웨믹.\" 그러더니 재거스 씨가 평소의 태도로 돌아가며 말했다. \"핍 군이 들어왔을 때 자네가 어느 항목을 보고 있었지?\"\n\n그들이 일하는 동안 잠시 곁에 서 있으면서, 나는 그들이 서로에게 던졌던 그 기묘한 눈길이 여러 번 되풀이되는 것을 보았다. 다만 이번에는 다른 점이 있었으니, 저마다 자신이 상대에게 약하고 직업적이지 못한 모습을 드러냈다는 사실을 의식까지는 아니더라도 미심쩍어하는 듯했다. 짐작건대 그런 이유로, 그들은 이제 서로에게 완강했다. 재거스 씨는 몹시 위압적이었고, 웨믹은 잠시라도 미결로 남은 사소한 점이 하나라도 있으면 고집스럽게 자기를 정당화했다. 나는 그들이 그토록 사이가 나쁜 것을 본 적이 없었다. 보통 때 그들은 정말이지 무척 잘 지냈으니까.\n\n그러나 다행히도 두 사람은 마이크가 때맞춰 나타나 준 덕에 한시름 놓았다. 마이크는 모피 모자를 쓰고 소매로 코를 닦는 버릇이 있는 그 의뢰인으로, 내가 그 벽 안에 처음 발을 들인 바로 그날 본 적이 있는 자였다. 자신이든 가족 중 누군가든 늘 곤경에 처해 있는 듯한(그곳에서 그것은 곧 뉴게이트를 뜻했다) 이 사내는, 자기 맏딸이 가게 좀도둑질 혐의로 붙잡혔다는 것을 알리러 들렀다. 재거스 씨가 난로 앞에 위엄 있게 선 채 그 일에 끼어들지 않는 가운데, 마이크가 이 우울한 사정을 웨믹에게 전하다가, 그의 눈에 그만 눈물이 한 방울 반짝였다.\n\n\"자네 지금 뭐 하는 건가?\" 웨믹이 더없이 분개하여 따져 물었다. \"여기 와서 뭣 하러 훌쩍거리는 거야?\"\n\n\"일부러 그런 게 아닙니다, 웨믹 씨.\"\n\n\"일부러 그런 거잖아.\" 웨믹이 말했다. \"어떻게 감히? 망가진 펜처럼 그렇게 부적부적 흘리지 않고는 여기 올 수 없다면, 자네는 여기 올 만한 상태가 아니야. 대체 무슨 셈으로 그러는 건가?\"\n\n\"사람이 제 감정을 어쩌겠습니까, 웨믹 씨.\" 마이크가 간청했다.\n\n\"제 뭐라고?\" 웨믹이 아주 사납게 따져 물었다. \"다시 말해 봐!\"\n\n\"이보게, 자네.\" 재거스 씨가 한 걸음 다가서며 문을 가리키고 말했다. \"이 사무실에서 나가게. 여기서 감정 따위는 용납하지 않아. 나가.\"\n\n\"꼴좋군.\" 웨믹이 말했다. \"나가.\"\n\n그리하여 가엾은 마이크는 아주 공손하게 물러났고, 재거스 씨와 웨믹은 좋은 관계를 다시 회복한 듯, 마치 방금 점심이라도 먹고 온 것처럼 산뜻한 기색으로 다시 일에 착수했다.\n\n# Chapter LII.\n\n리틀 브리튼을 나선 나는, 수표를 주머니에 넣고서 스키핀스 양의 오라버니인 회계사를 찾아갔다. 그리고 스키핀스 양의 오라버니인 회계사가 곧장 클래리커에게 가서 그를 내게 데려왔으므로, 나는 그 일을 마무리 짓는 크나큰 만족을 누렸다. 그것은 내가 막대한 유산에 관한 소식을 처음 들은 이래, 내가 한 유일하게 좋은 일이자 유일하게 끝까지 해낸 일이었다.\n\n그때 클래리커가 내게 알려 주기를, 회사의 사정이 꾸준히 나아가고 있어, 이제 사업 확장을 위해 절실히 필요했던 작은 동방 지점을 세울 수 있게 되었고, 새로이 동업자가 된 허버트가 그곳으로 나가 그 일을 맡으리라는 것이었다. 나는 설령 내 처지가 더 안정되어 있었다 하더라도, 친구와의 이별을 미리 각오해 두어야 했으리라는 것을 깨달았다. 그리고 지금이야말로 정말이지, 나의 마지막 닻이 그 잡은 자리를 놓아 가는 듯했고, 머지않아 나는 바람과 파도에 떠밀려 가게 될 것만 같았다.\n\n그러나 그에 대한 보상이라면, 허버트가 밤이면 집에 돌아와 이런 변화들을 내게 이야기해 줄 때의 그 기쁨이었다. 그는 자기가 내게 들려주는 것이 전혀 새로운 소식이 아니라는 것은 조금도 짐작하지 못한 채, 자신이 클래라 발리를 데리고 『아라비안 나이트』의 나라로 가는 그림을, 그리고 내가 (낙타 대상隊商을 거느리고서, 라고 했던 것 같다) 그들과 합류하러 떠나는 그림을, 그리고 우리 모두가 나일강을 거슬러 올라가며 온갖 경이를 구경하는 그림을 허공에 즐겨 그려 보이곤 했다. 그 빛나는 계획들 속의 내 몫에 대해서는 그리 낙관하지 않았지만, 나는 허버트의 앞길이 빠르게 트이고 있다는 것, 그리고 늙은 빌 발리가 후추와 럼주만 붙잡고 있어 주면 그의 딸은 머지않아 행복하게 자리를 잡으리라는 것을 느꼈다.\n\n이제 우리는 삼월에 접어들어 있었다. 내 왼팔은 나쁜 징후를 보이지는 않았으나, 자연스러운 경과에 따라 아무는 데 워낙 오래 걸려서 나는 아직도 윗도리를 걸칠 수가 없었다. 오른팔은 그런대로 회복되어, 보기 흉하긴 해도 제법 쓸 만했다.\n\n어느 월요일 아침, 허버트와 내가 식사를 하고 있을 때, 나는 우편으로 웨믹에게서 다음과 같은 편지를 받았다.\n\n\"월워스에서. 읽는 즉시 태워 버릴 것. 이번 주 초, 이를테면 수요일쯤에, 마음이 내키거든 자네가 아는 그 일을 해도 좋을 것이네. 자, 태우게.\"\n\n내가 이것을 허버트에게 보여 주고 불 속에 넣은 뒤 — 그러나 우리 둘 다 그것을 외워 두기 전에는 아니었다 — 우리는 어찌할지를 궁리했다. 왜냐하면 물론, 이제 내가 몸을 쓰지 못한다는 사실을 더는 못 본 체할 수가 없었기 때문이다.\n\n\"몇 번이고 거듭 생각해 봤는데,\" 허버트가 말했다. \"템스강 뱃사공을 쓰는 것보다 나은 방도를 알 것 같아. 스타톱을 쓰자고. 좋은 친구에다, 노 솜씨도 능숙하고, 우리를 아끼는 데다, 열의도 있고 의리도 있는 사람이야.\"\n\n나도 그를 한 번 이상 떠올린 적이 있었다.\n\n\"하지만 그에게 어디까지 말할 셈인가, 허버트?\"\n\n\"아주 조금만 말하면 돼. 아침이 올 때까지는 그저 한낱 변덕이려니, 다만 비밀스러운 변덕이려니 여기게 해 두는 거지. 그러다 막상 그날 아침이 되면, 프로비스를 배에 태워 떠나보내야 할 절박한 사정이 있다는 걸 알려 주는 거야. 자네도 그와 함께 가는 거지?\"\n\n\"물론이지.\"\n\n\"어디로?\"\n\n이 문제를 두고 여러 차례 노심초사하며 궁리하는 동안, 우리가 어느 항구를 향하든 거의 상관없는 일처럼 여겨졌다. 함부르크든, 로테르담이든, 안트베르펜이든 — 그가 영국 밖으로만 나가면 그곳이 어디인지는 별로 중요치 않았다. 우리 앞길에 나타나 우리를 태워 줄 외국 기선이라면 어느 것이든 좋았다. 나는 늘 그를 배에 태워 강 하류로 멀찍이 데려가리라 마음먹고 있었다. 적어도 그레이브젠드를 훌쩍 지나서까지는. 혹시라도 의심이 일고 있다면 그곳이야말로 수색이나 심문이 벌어지기 쉬운 위험한 길목이었으니까. 외국 기선들은 만조 무렵에 런던을 떠날 터이므로, 우리의 계획은 그에 앞선 썰물을 타고 강 하류로 내려가, 한 척에 다가갈 수 있을 때까지 어느 한적한 곳에 숨어 기다리는 것이었다. 우리가 숨어 있는 곳이 어디가 되었든, 그곳에 배가 당도할 시각은 미리 알아보아 두면 제법 정확히 가늠할 수 있었다.\n\n허버트는 이 모든 것에 동의했고, 우리는 아침 식사를 마치자마자 곧장 밖으로 나가 조사에 착수했다. 우리는 함부르크행 기선이 우리 목적에 가장 잘 맞을 듯하다는 것을 알아내고, 주로 그 배에 생각을 모았다. 그러나 같은 물때에 런던을 떠날 다른 외국 기선들도 적어 두었고, 그 배들 각각의 선체 모양과 빛깔을 익혀 두었음을 스스로 확인했다. 그런 다음 우리는 몇 시간 동안 헤어졌다. 나는 당장 필요한 여권들을 마련하러, 허버트는 스타톱을 그의 하숙으로 찾아가 만나러. 우리 둘 다 별다른 방해 없이 해야 할 일을 해냈고, 한 시에 다시 만났을 때 일이 끝났음을 서로 알렸다. 내 쪽에서는 여권이 준비되어 있었고, 허버트는 스타톱을 만났는데, 그는 기꺼이 합류하겠다고 했다.\n\n우리는 그 두 사람이 한 쌍의 노를 젓고, 내가 키를 잡기로 정했다. 우리가 데려갈 사람은 앉은 자리에 가만히 있기로 했다. 속도가 우리의 목적은 아니었으니, 그 정도로도 충분히 나아갈 수 있을 터였다. 우리는 이렇게 짜 두었다. 허버트가 그날 저녁 밀 폰드 뱅크로 가기 전에 집에 와서 저녁을 먹지 않을 것, 내일 저녁인 화요일에는 그곳에 아예 가지 않을 것, 수요일에 우리가 다가오는 것을 보거든, 그러나 그 전에는 아니되, 프로비스가 집 가까이에 있는 어느 층계로 내려오도록 그를 채비시켜 둘 것, 그와의 모든 약속은 그 월요일 밤에 마무리 지을 것, 그리고 우리가 그를 배에 태울 때까지는 더 이상 어떤 식으로도 그에게 연락하지 않을 것.\n\n이 모든 조심스러운 절차를 우리 둘 다 잘 새겨 둔 뒤, 나는 집으로 돌아갔다.\n\n내 열쇠로 우리 거처의 바깥문을 여는데, 함 속에 내게 온 편지 한 통이 들어 있었다. 몹시 더러운 편지였으나, 글씨가 서투르지는 않았다. 그것은 (물론 내가 집을 비운 사이에) 인편으로 전해진 것이었고, 그 내용은 이러했다.\n\n\"오늘 밤이나 내일 밤 아홉 시에 옛 습지로, 석회 굽는 가마 곁의 작은 수문 막사로 오기가 두렵지 않거든, 오는 편이 좋을 것이다. 네 외숙 프로비스에 관한 정보를 원한다면, 와서 아무에게도 말하지 말고, 한시도 지체하지 않는 편이 훨씬 좋을 것이다. 반드시 혼자 와야 한다. 이것을 가지고 와라.\"\n\n이 기이한 편지를 받기 전부터도 내 마음에는 짐이 충분히 얹혀 있었다. 이제 어찌해야 할지 알 수가 없었다. 그리고 가장 곤란한 것은, 빨리 결정을 내리지 않으면 오늘 밤에 맞춰 나를 그곳으로 데려다줄 오후 마차를 놓치게 된다는 점이었다. 내일 밤에 가는 것은 생각조차 할 수 없었으니, 도주 시각과 너무 가까웠기 때문이다. 게다가 내가 아는 한, 그 제안된 정보가 도주 그 자체와 어떤 중요한 관련이 있을지도 모를 일이었다.\n\n만약 내게 곰곰이 생각할 넉넉한 시간이 있었더라도, 나는 그래도 갔으리라고 믿는다. 생각할 겨를이 거의 없는 터에 — 시계를 보니 마차가 삼십 분 안에 떠나게 되어 있었으므로 — 나는 가기로 마음을 정했다. 프로비스 외숙에 대한 언급만 아니었다면 나는 분명 가지 않았을 것이다. 그 한마디가, 웨믹의 편지와 아침나절의 분주한 채비에 더해져, 저울추를 기울게 했다.\n\n극심한 황망함 속에서는 거의 어떤 편지든 그 내용을 또렷이 머릿속에 담기가 워낙 어려운지라, 나는 이 수상쩍은 서한을 다시 두 번이나 읽고 나서야 비밀을 지키라는 그 당부가 기계적으로 머리에 들어왔다. 똑같이 기계적인 식으로 그에 따르며, 나는 허버트에게 연필로 쪽지 한 장을 남겼다. 머지않아, 그것도 얼마나 오래일지 모를 채로 떠나게 될 터이니, 미스 해비셤이 어떻게 지내는지 내 눈으로 확인하러 서둘러 내려갔다 오기로 했다는 내용이었다. 그러고 나니 외투를 챙기고, 거처를 잠그고, 지름길로 마차 사무소를 향해 갈 시간이 가까스로 남았을 뿐이었다. 만약 내가 삯마차를 잡아 큰길로 갔더라면 시간을 맞추지 못했을 것이다. 그러나 그렇게 간 덕분에, 나는 마차가 막 마당을 빠져나오는 순간에 그것을 붙잡았다. 정신을 차리고 보니, 나는 무릎까지 차오른 짚더미 속에서 덜컹거리며 가는 유일한 객실 안 승객이었다.\n\n사실 나는 그 편지를 받은 뒤로 줄곧 제정신이 아니었다. 아침의 그 황망함에 이어진 터라, 그것이 나를 어찌나 어지럽혀 놓았던지. 아침의 그 분주함과 안절부절못함은 대단한 것이었다. 내가 웨믹을 그토록 오래, 그토록 애타게 기다려 왔으면서도, 막상 그의 기별은 결국 뜻밖의 일처럼 닥쳐왔던 것이다. 그리고 이제 나는 내가 마차 안에 있다는 것이 새삼 의아해지기 시작했고, 내게 거기 있을 만한 충분한 이유가 있는지 의심스러워졌고, 이내 내려서 되돌아가야 하지 않을까 따져 보게 되었고, 익명의 편지 따위에 귀를 기울이는 짓에 대해 스스로 반박하게 되었다. 한마디로, 급히 서두르는 사람치고 모르는 이가 거의 없으리라 싶은 그 온갖 모순과 망설임의 단계를 두루 거쳤다. 그래도 프로비스라는 이름의 언급이 모든 것을 압도했다. 나는, 그것이 추론이라 할 수 있다면, 이미 나도 모르게 했던 그 추론을 다시 되풀이했다. 만약 내가 가지 않은 탓에 그에게 어떤 해라도 닥친다면, 내가 어찌 나 자신을 용서할 수 있겠는가!\n\n우리가 도착하기 전에 날이 저물었고, 객실 안에 있어 바깥을 거의 볼 수 없었던 데다 몸을 쓰지 못하는 처지라 바깥자리에 나가 앉을 수도 없었던 나에게 그 여정은 길고도 음울하게만 느껴졌다. 나는 블루 보어를 피해, 읍내 아래쪽의 그리 이름나지 않은 어느 여관에 들어 저녁을 시켰다. 음식이 준비되는 동안 나는 새티스 하우스로 가서 미스 해비셤의 안부를 물었다. 그녀는 여전히 몹시 앓고 있었으나, 다소 차도가 있다고 여겨지고 있었다.\n\n내가 든 여관은 한때 어느 오래된 교회 부속 건물의 일부였던 곳으로, 나는 세례반처럼 생긴 작은 팔각형 공동 식당에서 식사를 했다. 내가 음식을 썰 수 없었으므로, 번들번들 빛나는 대머리의 늙은 주인이 나를 대신해 썰어 주었다. 이 일로 우리가 이야기를 나누게 되자, 그는 친절하게도 내 이야기를 들려주며 나를 즐겁게 해 주었다 — 물론 펌블추크가 나의 최초의 은인이요 내 재산의 토대를 닦은 사람이라는, 세간에 널리 퍼진 대목을 곁들여서.\n\n\"그 젊은이를 아십니까?\" 내가 물었다.\n\n\"알다마다요!\" 주인이 되받았다. \"그 사람이 — 키도 채 자라기 전부터 알았지요.\"\n\n\"그 사람이 이 동네로 돌아오는 일이 있습니까?\"\n\n\"예, 돌아오지요,\" 주인이 말했다. \"이따금씩 자기 잘난 친구들한테로 돌아와서는, 자기를 사람 만들어 준 이한테는 쌀쌀맞게 등을 돌리지요.\"\n\n\"그게 누구 말씀입니까?\"\n\n\"제가 말씀드린 그 사람이지요,\" 주인이 말했다. \"펌블추크 씨 말입니다.\"\n\n\"다른 누구에게도 배은망덕하게 굽니까?\"\n\n\"할 수만 있다면야 그러고도 남겠지요,\" 주인이 대답했다. \"하지만 그러질 못해요. 왜냐? 펌블추크가 그 사람을 위해 모든 걸 다 해 주었으니까요.\"\n\n\"펌블추크가 그렇게 말합니까?\"\n\n\"말이라뇨!\" 주인이 대꾸했다. \"굳이 입에 올릴 것도 없지요.\"\n\n\"하지만 그렇게 말하긴 합니까?\"\n\n\"그 사람이 그 얘기를 늘어놓는 걸 듣고 있노라면, 멀쩡한 사람 피가 백포도주 식초로 변할 지경입니다, 손님.\" 주인이 말했다.\n\n나는 생각했다. \"그런데도 조, 사랑하는 조, 당신은 결코 그런 얘기를 입에 올리지 않지. 묵묵히 견디며 사랑이 넘치는 조, 당신은 결코 불평하는 법이 없어. 너도 그렇지, 마음씨 고운 비디!\"\n\n\"그 사고 때문에 입맛이 좀 상하셨나 봅니다.\" 주인이 내 윗도리 밑으로 붕대를 감은 팔을 흘끔 보며 말했다. \"좀 더 연한 부위로 들어 보시지요.\"\n\n\"아니요, 괜찮습니다.\" 나는 식탁에서 몸을 돌려 난롯불을 바라보며 골똘히 잠긴 채 대꾸했다. \"더는 못 먹겠어요. 좀 치워 주세요.\"\n\n조에 대한 나의 배은망덕함이, 저 뻔뻔스러운 사기꾼 펌블추크를 통해서만큼 그토록 날카롭게 가슴을 후벼 든 적은 없었다. 그가 거짓될수록 조는 더 참되었고, 그가 비열할수록 조는 더 고결했다.\n\n한 시간 남짓 난롯불을 바라보며 깊은 생각에 잠겨 있는 동안, 내 마음은 깊이, 그리고 마땅한 만큼 겸허해졌다. 시계 치는 소리에 정신이 들었으나, 내 의기소침이나 회한에서 깨어난 것은 아니었다. 나는 일어나 외투를 목에 둘러 여미고 밖으로 나섰다. 그에 앞서 나는 그 편지를 다시 들여다보려고 주머니를 뒤졌으나 찾을 수가 없었다. 마차의 짚더미 속에 떨어뜨린 게 틀림없으리라는 생각에 마음이 편치 않았다. 그러나 약속된 장소가 습지의 석회 가마 곁 작은 수문 막사이며, 시각은 아홉 시라는 것은 잘 알고 있었다. 한시도 지체할 틈이 없었으므로, 나는 이제 곧장 습지를 향해 나아갔다.\n\n# Chapter LIII.\n\n어두운 밤이었다. 내가 울타리 쳐진 농지를 벗어나 습지로 나설 무렵 보름달이 떠오르기는 했지만 말이다. 습지의 검은 윤곽선 너머로는 맑은 하늘이 한 줄기 띠처럼 펼쳐져 있었으나, 크고 붉은 달을 담기에는 너무 좁았다. 몇 분도 지나지 않아 달은 그 맑은 들판에서 솟아올라, 겹겹이 쌓인 구름의 산봉우리들 사이로 사라졌다.\n\n구슬픈 바람이 불었고, 습지는 몹시 음산했다. 낯선 사람이라면 견디기 힘들었을 것이며, 나에게조차 그 풍경은 어찌나 짓누르는 듯했던지, 절반쯤은 돌아갈 마음이 들어 망설였다. 그러나 나는 그곳을 잘 알았고, 이보다 훨씬 더 어두운 밤이라도 길을 찾을 수 있었으며, 이왕 거기까지 온 마당에 돌아갈 핑계가 없었다. 그래서 마음에 거슬리면서도 그곳에 왔으니, 마음에 거슬리면서도 계속 나아갔다.\n\n내가 잡은 방향은 옛집이 있는 쪽도, 우리가 죄수들을 쫓던 쪽도 아니었다. 걸어가는 동안 멀리 떨어진 감옥선들은 내 등 뒤에 두었고, 모래톱 위로 멀찍이 떠 있는 옛 불빛들이 보이기는 했으나 어깨 너머로 보았을 뿐이다. 나는 옛 포대를 알듯이 석회 가마를 잘 알았으나, 둘은 몇 마일이나 떨어져 있었다. 그러니 그날 밤 두 지점에 각각 불이 켜져 있었다 한들, 두 개의 밝은 점 사이에는 텅 빈 지평선이 길게 한 줄 가로놓여 있었을 것이다.\n\n처음에는 지나면서 몇 개의 문을 닫아야 했고, 이따금 둑길에 누워 있던 소들이 일어나 풀과 갈대 사이로 비틀거리며 내려갈 때까지 가만히 서 있어야 했다. 그러나 얼마 지나지 않아 그 너른 평지가 온통 나 혼자만의 것이 된 듯싶었다.\n\n가마에 다가가기까지는 다시 반 시간이 걸렸다. 석회는 굼뜨고 숨 막히는 냄새를 풍기며 타고 있었으나, 불은 지펴 놓은 채 버려져 있었고 일꾼은 하나도 보이지 않았다. 바로 곁에는 작은 채석장이 있었다. 그것은 내 길목에 곧장 놓여 있었으며, 여기저기 흩어진 연장과 손수레로 보아 그날 작업이 있었던 것 같았다.\n\n이 굴토장에서 다시 습지 높이로 올라오면서 — 거친 길이 그곳을 가로질러 나 있었으므로 — 나는 낡은 수문집에서 새어 나오는 불빛을 보았다. 나는 걸음을 재촉해 손으로 문을 두드렸다. 무슨 대답을 기다리며 나는 주위를 둘러보았다. 수문이 얼마나 버려지고 부서져 있는지, 그리고 기와지붕을 인 그 나무집이 — 지금도 이미 그러하다면 — 머지않아 비바람을 막아 내지 못하리라는 것을, 그리고 진흙과 진창이 석회로 덮여 있는 것을, 그리고 가마의 숨 막히는 증기가 유령처럼 내 쪽으로 기어오는 것을 눈여겨보았다. 여전히 대답이 없어 나는 다시 두드렸다. 그래도 대답이 없자, 나는 빗장을 밀어 보았다.\n\n빗장은 내 손에 들려 올라갔고, 문이 열렸다. 안을 들여다보니 탁자 위에 불 켜진 양초 하나와 긴 의자 하나, 그리고 바퀴 달린 나직한 침대 위에 매트리스 하나가 보였다. 위에 다락이 있기에 나는 \"거기 누구 있습니까?\" 하고 불렀으나, 대답하는 목소리는 없었다. 이어 나는 시계를 보았고, 아홉 시가 지났음을 확인하고는 다시 \"거기 누구 있습니까?\" 하고 불렀다. 그래도 대답이 없자, 나는 어찌해야 할지 갈피를 잡지 못한 채 문밖으로 나갔다.\n\n비가 거세게 내리기 시작했다. 이미 본 것 말고는 아무것도 보이지 않아, 나는 다시 집 안으로 돌아와 문간의 그늘진 자리에 막 들어선 채 서서 밤을 내다보았다. 누군가 최근에 여기 있었던 게 분명하고 곧 돌아올 게 틀림없다고, 그렇지 않다면 촛불이 타고 있을 리 없다고 생각하던 차에, 심지가 긴지 살펴봐야겠다는 생각이 머리에 떠올랐다. 그러려고 몸을 돌려 손에 촛불을 막 집어 든 순간, 촛불이 어떤 거센 충격에 꺼져 버렸다. 그리고 다음으로 내가 깨달은 것은, 뒤에서 내 머리 위로 던져진 단단한 올가미에 내가 옭아매였다는 사실이었다.\n\n\"자.\" 욕설과 함께 억눌린 목소리가 말했다. \"이제 너를 잡았다!\"\n\n\"이게 무슨 짓이야?\" 나는 발버둥치며 외쳤다. \"누구냐? 사람 살려, 살려, 살려!\"\n\n내 두 팔이 옆구리에 바싹 죄어졌을 뿐 아니라, 다친 팔에 가해진 압박이 견딜 수 없는 고통을 안겼다. 어떤 때는 억센 사내의 손이, 어떤 때는 억센 사내의 가슴이 내 입을 틀어막아 비명을 잠재웠고, 뜨거운 숨결이 늘 가까이 닿는 가운데, 나는 벽에 단단히 붙들린 채 어둠 속에서 헛되이 발버둥쳤다. \"자, 이제.\" 억눌린 목소리가 또 한 번 욕설과 함께 말했다. \"다시 한번 소리 질러 봐, 그럼 단번에 끝장내 주마!\"\n\n다친 팔의 고통으로 정신이 아득하고 속이 메스꺼웠으며, 갑작스러운 일에 어리둥절했으나, 그러면서도 이 협박이 얼마나 쉽게 실행될 수 있는지 의식하고 있던 나는 몸부림을 멈추고, 단 조금이라도 팔을 편하게 해 보려 했다. 그러나 팔은 그러기에는 너무 단단히 묶여 있었다. 전에 데었던 그 팔이 이제는 삶기고 있는 것만 같았다.\n\n밤이 별안간 차단되고 그 자리를 새카만 어둠이 대신한 것으로 보아, 사내가 덧문을 닫았음을 나는 알아차렸다. 잠시 더듬거린 끝에 사내는 원하던 부싯돌과 쇠를 찾아내, 불을 켜기 시작했다. 나는 부싯깃 사이로 떨어지는 불꽃에 시선을 모았다. 사내는 성냥을 손에 든 채 그 위로 거듭거듭 입김을 불었으나, 나는 그의 입술과 성냥의 푸른 끝밖에 볼 수 없었으며, 그것조차 띄엄띄엄 보였을 뿐이다. 부싯깃은 축축했으니 — 그곳에서야 당연한 일이었다 — 불꽃은 하나둘 잇따라 사그라들었다.\n\n사내는 서두르지 않고 다시 부싯돌과 쇠를 쳤다. 불꽃이 그의 주위로 빽빽하고 밝게 떨어지자, 나는 그의 두 손과 얼굴의 한두 군데를 볼 수 있었으며, 그가 앉아서 탁자 위로 몸을 숙이고 있다는 것을 알아볼 수 있었다. 그러나 그 이상은 보이지 않았다. 이윽고 부싯깃에 입김을 부는 그의 푸른 입술이 다시 보이는가 싶더니, 불꽃이 확 타올라 내게 올릭의 모습을 비추어 주었다.\n\n내가 누구를 예상했는지는 모르겠다. 그를 예상한 것은 아니었다. 그를 보는 순간 나는 내가 정말로 위험한 궁지에 처했음을 느꼈고, 그에게서 눈을 떼지 않았다.\n\n그는 활활 타는 성냥불로 아주 신중하게 양초에 불을 붙였고, 성냥을 떨어뜨려 발로 비벼 껐다. 그런 다음 나를 볼 수 있도록 양초를 탁자 위 자기에게서 떨어진 자리에 놓고는, 탁자 위에 팔짱을 낀 채 앉아 나를 바라보았다. 나는 내가 벽에서 몇 인치 떨어진 곳에 세워진 튼튼한 수직 사다리에 — 그곳에 붙박이로 고정된 것이었으며, 위쪽 다락으로 오르는 수단이었다 — 묶여 있음을 알아차렸다.\n\n\"자.\" 우리가 한동안 서로를 살핀 뒤에 그가 말했다. \"이제 너를 잡았다.\"\n\n\"풀어 줘. 나를 보내 줘!\"\n\n\"하!\" 그가 대꾸했다. \"내가 너를 보내 주지. 달나라로 보내 주고, 별나라로 보내 주마. 다 때가 되면 말이야.\"\n\n\"왜 나를 여기로 꾀어냈지?\"\n\n\"모르겠냐?\" 그가 살기 어린 눈빛으로 말했다.\n\n\"왜 어둠 속에서 나를 덮친 거지?\"\n\n\"이 일을 죄다 나 혼자 해치울 작정이니까. 비밀은 둘보다 하나가 더 잘 지켜지는 법이지. 오, 이 원수 놈, 이 원수 놈아!\"\n\n탁자 위에 팔짱을 낀 채 앉아, 내게 고개를 절레절레 흔들며 제 몸을 끌어안고서 내가 보여 주는 광경을 즐기는 그의 모습에는, 나를 떨게 만드는 악의가 서려 있었다. 내가 말없이 그를 지켜보는 동안, 그는 옆쪽 구석에 손을 넣어 놋쇠로 개머리판을 두른 총 한 자루를 집어 들었다.\n\n\"이걸 알아보겠냐?\" 그가 마치 나를 겨누려는 듯한 시늉을 하며 말했다. \"이걸 전에 어디서 봤는지 알겠냐? 말해 봐, 이 이리 새끼야!\"\n\n\"그래.\" 나는 대답했다.\n\n\"네놈이 그 일자리를 잃게 만들었지. 네가 그랬어. 말해 봐!\"\n\n\"그럼 내가 달리 뭘 할 수 있었겠어?\"\n\n\"네가 그랬어, 그것만으로도 충분하지, 더 따질 것도 없이. 어디 감히 나와, 내가 좋아하던 처녀 사이에 끼어든단 말이냐?\"\n\"내가 언제 그랬다는 거지?\"\n\n\"안 그런 적이 언제 있었냐? 그 처녀한테 늙은 올릭의 험담을 늘어놓은 게 늘 너였잖아.\"\n\n\"그 험담은 네가 스스로 자초한 거야. 네가 스스로 벌어들인 거지. 네가 너 자신에게 해를 끼치지 않았다면, 나도 너한테 아무런 해를 끼치지 않았을 거다.\"\n\n\"거짓말쟁이 같으니. 그래서 무슨 수고를 들여서라도, 돈을 얼마든지 써서라도 나를 이 고장에서 쫓아내겠다, 그거였냐?\" 그가 비디와 마지막으로 만났을 때 내가 했던 말을 그대로 되뇌며 말했다. \"자, 이제 한 가지 알려 주마. 나를 이 고장에서 쫓아내는 게 오늘 밤만큼 너한테 보람 있는 적은 없었을 거다. 하! 그게 네 돈을 스무 배로 불려서 마지막 한 푼까지 다 들이부어도 그렇단 말이야!\" 그가 호랑이처럼 으르렁대는 입으로 묵직한 손을 내게 흔들어 보였을 때, 나는 그 말이 사실임을 느꼈다.\n\n\"나한테 무슨 짓을 하려는 거지?\"\n\n\"내가 하려는 건.\" 그가 주먹을 묵직하게 탁자에 내리치며, 그 일격에 더 큰 힘을 싣고자 내리치는 동시에 몸을 일으키며 말했다. \"네 목숨을 가지려는 거다!\"\n\n그는 몸을 앞으로 기울여 나를 노려보더니, 마치 내 생각에 입에 침이 고이기라도 한 듯 천천히 주먹을 펴서 입가를 훔치고는, 다시 자리에 앉았다.\n\n\"넌 어릴 적부터 늘 늙은 올릭의 앞길을 가로막았지. 오늘 밤 이걸로 그 앞길에서 비켜나게 되는 거야. 그가 더는 너 때문에 골치 썩을 일이 없게 말이지. 넌 이제 죽은 목숨이다.\"\n\n나는 내가 무덤 가장자리에 이르렀음을 느꼈다. 한순간 나는 갇힌 그 자리를 둘러보며 빠져나갈 기회를 미친 듯이 찾았으나, 그런 것은 없었다.\n\n\"게다가 말이야.\" 그가 다시 탁자 위에 팔짱을 끼며 말했다. \"네 살점 한 조각, 뼈 한 토막도 이 땅에 남겨 두지 않을 거다. 네 시체를 가마에 처넣을 거야 — 너 같은 놈 둘쯤은 어깨에 메고 가지 — 그러면 사람들이 너에 대해 무슨 짐작을 하든, 끝내 아무것도 알아내지 못할 거다.\"\n\n내 마음은 헤아릴 수 없을 만큼 빠르게 그런 죽음의 온갖 결과를 좇았다. 에스텔라의 아버지는 내가 자기를 저버렸다고 믿을 것이며, 붙잡혀, 나를 원망하며 죽어 갈 것이다. 허버트조차 내가 그에게 남긴 편지를, 내가 미스 해비셤의 대문에 잠깐만 들렀다는 사실과 견주어 보고는 나를 의심할 것이다. 조와 비디는 그날 밤 내가 얼마나 미안해했는지 결코 알지 못할 것이며, 내가 무엇을 견뎌 냈는지, 얼마나 진실하고자 했는지, 어떤 고뇌를 거쳤는지 아무도 알지 못할 것이다. 코앞에 닥친 죽음은 끔찍했으나, 죽음보다 훨씬 더 끔찍한 것은 죽은 뒤에 잘못 기억되리라는 두려움이었다. 그리고 내 생각이 어찌나 빨랐던지, 그 비열한 자의 말이 아직 입가에 남아 있는 동안, 나는 아직 태어나지도 않은 세대들에게 — 에스텔라의 자식들과, 그 자식들의 자식들에게 — 멸시당하는 내 모습을 보았다.\n\n\"자, 이리 새끼야.\" 그가 말했다. \"여느 짐승처럼 너를 죽이기 전에 — 그게 내가 하려는 일이고, 그러려고 너를 묶어 놓은 거니까 — 너를 실컷 들여다보고, 실컷 찔러 대 주마. 오, 이 원수 놈!\"\n\n다시 한번 소리쳐 도움을 청할까 하는 생각이 머릿속을 스쳤다. 그 장소가 얼마나 외진 곳인지, 도움을 바랄 수 없다는 것이 얼마나 가망 없는 일인지를 나만큼 잘 아는 사람은 거의 없었지만 말이다. 그러나 그가 앉아 나를 흡족하게 바라보는 동안, 나는 그에 대한 경멸과 혐오에 떠받쳐 입을 다물었다. 무엇보다도 나는 그에게 애원하지 않으리라, 그리고 그에게 보잘것없으나마 마지막 저항이라도 하면서 죽으리라 결심했다. 그 끔찍한 절체절명의 순간, 다른 모든 사람을 향한 내 마음이 한껏 누그러졌고, 하늘을 향해 겸허히 용서를 빌었으며, 내가 사랑하는 이들에게 작별 인사 한마디 건네지 못했고 이제 영영 작별을 고할 수도, 그들에게 나 자신을 해명할 수도, 내 비참한 과오에 대한 그들의 동정을 구할 수도 없으리라는 생각에 가슴이 미어졌으나 — 그래도 만약 그를 죽일 수만 있다면, 비록 죽어 가면서라도 나는 그를 죽였을 것이다.\n\n그는 술을 마신 터라 두 눈이 벌겋게 충혈되어 있었다. 목에는 양철 병이 매달려 있었는데, 지난 시절 그가 먹을 것과 마실 것을 몸에 매달고 다니던 모습을 나는 자주 보았었다. 그는 병을 입가로 가져가 독한 술을 벌컥 들이켰다. 그리고 나는 그의 얼굴로 확 끼쳐 오르는, 내 눈에도 보이던 독주의 냄새를 맡았다.\n\n\"이리 새끼야!\" 그가 다시 팔짱을 끼며 말했다. \"늙은 올릭이 너한테 한 가지 일러 줄 게 있다. 네 그 표독스러운 누나를 결딴낸 게 바로 너였어.\"\n\n다시금 내 마음은 그 예전의 헤아릴 수 없는 빠르기로, 그의 느리고 머뭇거리는 말이 이런 문장을 이루기도 전에, 누나에 대한 습격과 그녀의 병환과 죽음에 관한 모든 주제를 남김없이 다 훑어 냈다.\n\n\"그건 너였어, 이 악당아.\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"네가 한 짓이라니까 — 너 때문에 벌어진 일이라니까.\" 그가 총을 집어 들어, 우리 사이의 빈 허공에 개머리판을 한 차례 휘두르며 받아쳤다. \"오늘 밤 너한테 다가가듯, 난 그 여자한테 뒤에서 다가갔지. 내가 후려쳤어! 죽은 줄 알고 내버려 뒀지. 그리고 지금 너 곁에 석회 가마가 있는 것처럼 그때 그 여자 곁에 가마가 있었다면, 그 여자는 다시 살아나지 못했을 거다. 하지만 그 짓을 한 건 늙은 올릭이 아니었어. 너였지. 넌 귀염을 받았고, 그는 괄시받고 두들겨 맞았어. 늙은 올릭이 괄시받고 두들겨 맞았다, 그거지? 이제 네가 그 값을 치르는 거야. 네가 저지른 일이니, 이제 네가 값을 치러.\"\n\n그가 다시 술을 마시고는 더욱 사나워졌다. 병을 기울이는 모양으로 보아 안에 술이 많이 남지 않은 것을 알 수 있었다. 그가 그 안의 술기운으로 자신을 부추겨 나를 끝장내려 한다는 것을 나는 분명히 알아챘다. 그 병에 든 한 방울 한 방울이 곧 내 목숨의 한 방울임을 나는 알았다. 내가, 조금 전 마치 나 자신을 경고하는 유령처럼 내 쪽으로 기어오던 그 증기의 일부로 변하고 나면, 그는 누나의 경우에 했던 그대로 — 부랴부랴 읍내로 가서 술집들을 어슬렁거리며 술을 마시는 모습을 사람들 눈에 보일 — 짓을 하리라는 것을 나는 알았다. 빠르게 돌아가는 내 마음은 그를 좇아 읍내까지 가서, 그가 있는 거리의 풍경을 그려 냈고, 그 불빛과 활기를, 외로운 습지와 그 위로 기어오는 흰 증기 — 내가 녹아들고 말 그 증기 — 와 대비시켰다.\n\n그가 열두 마디를 말하는 동안 내가 몇 년이고 몇 년이고 또 몇 년을 다 헤아릴 수 있었을 뿐 아니라, 그가 실제로 한 말이 내게 한낱 말이 아니라 그림으로 떠올랐다. 흥분하고 고조된 내 머릿속에서는, 어떤 장소를 떠올리면 곧 그곳이 보였고, 어떤 사람을 떠올리면 곧 그 사람이 보였다. 이 영상들의 생생함은 아무리 과장해도 지나치지 않으련만, 그러면서도 나는 줄곧 그 사람 자신에게 — 막 덮치려 웅크린 호랑이에게 누군들 정신을 쏟지 않으랴! — 어찌나 골똘했던지, 그의 손가락이 미세하게 움직이는 것까지 다 알아챌 수 있었다.\n\n이 두 번째 술을 들이켜고 나서, 그는 앉아 있던 긴 의자에서 일어나 탁자를 옆으로 밀쳤다. 그런 다음 양초를 집어 들어, 그 불빛이 내게 비치도록 살기 어린 손으로 가린 채 내 앞에 서서, 나를 바라보며 그 광경을 즐겼다.\n\n\"이리 새끼야, 한 가지 더 일러 주마. 그날 밤 네가 계단에서 걸려 넘어진 게 바로 늙은 올릭이었어.\"\n\n나는 불이 꺼진 등이 달린 그 계단을 보았다. 야경꾼의 등불이 벽에 드리운 묵직한 계단 난간의 그림자들을 보았다. 다시는 보지 못할 방들을 보았다. 이쪽에는 반쯤 열린 문 하나, 저쪽에는 닫힌 문 하나, 그리고 주위의 온갖 가구들을.\n\n\"그런데 늙은 올릭이 거기 왜 있었느냐? 한 가지 더 일러 주마, 이리 새끼야. 너랑 그 여자가 이 고장에서, 적어도 편히 먹고사는 문제에 관한 한 나를 거의 다 몰아냈지. 그래서 나는 새 동료들, 새 주인들과 어울리게 됐어. 그중 몇은 내가 편지를 쓰고 싶을 때 대신 써 준다 — 알겠냐? — 내 편지를 써 준단 말이야, 이리 새끼야! 그자들은 쉰 가지 필체로 쓰지. 한 가지 필체밖에 못 쓰는 너 같은 비열한 놈과는 다르단 말이야. 네가 여기 네 누나 장례에 내려왔을 때부터, 나는 네 목숨을 가지겠다는 굳은 마음과 굳은 의지를 품어 왔다. 너를 감쪽같이 처리할 방도가 보이지 않아서, 네 사정을 속속들이 알아내려고 너를 줄곧 지켜봤지. 늙은 올릭은 속으로 이렇게 말했거든. '어떻게든 저놈을 손에 넣고 말겠다!' 그런데 이게 웬일이냐! 너를 노리다 보니, 네 삼촌 프로비스를 찾아낸 게 아니냐, 응?\"\n\n밀폰드 뱅크와 칭크스 만, 그리고 옛 그린 코퍼 밧줄 공장이, 모두 어쩌면 그리도 또렷하고 선명하게 떠올랐던지! 자기 방에 있는 프로비스, 이제 쓸모를 다한 신호, 어여쁜 클라라, 그 인자한 어머니 같은 여인, 등을 대고 누운 늙은 빌 발리, 그 모든 것이, 마치 바다로 빠르게 빠져나가는 내 생명의 급류 위를 흘러가듯 스쳐 지나갔다!\n\n\"너한테 삼촌도 다 있다고! 흥, 난 네가 가저리네에 있던 시절부터 너를 알았어. 그때 넌 어찌나 조그만 이리 새끼였던지, 이 손가락하고 엄지 사이에 네 모가지를 끼워서 죽은 채로 내던질 수도 있었지(일요일에 네가 가지치기한 나무들 사이를 어슬렁대는 걸 볼 때면, 가끔 그러고 싶은 마음이 들곤 했거든). 그때 넌 무슨 삼촌 따위도 못 찾아냈잖아. 아무렴, 네까짓 게! 그런데 말이야, 늙은 올릭이, 네 삼촌 프로비스가 차고 있던 그 다리 쇠고랑이 — 늙은 올릭이 여러 해 전에 이 습지에서 주워다가 줄로 끊어 놓은 그 쇠고랑, 그리고 곁에 간직해 두었다가 황소 잡듯 네 누나를 그걸로 후려쳐 쓰러뜨린, 그래서 너도 그렇게 쓰러뜨리려는 — 바로 그 쇠고랑이 거의 틀림없다는 소식을 듣게 됐을 때 — 응? — 그 소식을 듣게 됐을 때 말이야 — 응?\"\n\n그는 사납게 빈정대면서 양초를 내게 어찌나 바싹 들이댔던지, 나는 불꽃을 피하려고 얼굴을 옆으로 돌렸다.\n\n\"하!\" 그가 한 번 더 그러고는 웃으며 외쳤다. \"불에 덴 아이가 불을 무서워하는 법이지! 늙은 올릭은 네가 데었다는 걸 알았어. 늙은 올릭은 네가 네 삼촌 프로비스를 몰래 빼돌리고 있다는 걸 알았지. 늙은 올릭은 네 적수가 되고도 남아서, 네가 오늘 밤 올 줄 알았던 거야! 자, 한 가지 더 일러 주마, 이리 새끼야. 이걸로 끝이다. 늙은 올릭이 너한테 그랬던 것만큼이나 네 삼촌 프로비스한테 만만찮은 적수가 되는 자들이 있어. 조카를 잃거든 그자들을 조심하라고 해라! 그 소중한 친척의 옷 한 조각도, 몸의 뼈 한 토막도 아무도 못 찾게 되거든 그자들을 조심하라고 해! 매그위치를 — 그래, 나는 그 이름을 안다! — 같은 땅에서 살아 숨 쉬게 둘 수 없고 둘 마음도 없는 자들이 있어. 그자가 다른 땅에서 살아 있을 때에도, 그자가 자기들 모르게 그 땅을 떠나 자기들을 위험에 빠뜨리는 일이 없도록, 그자에 관해 어찌나 확실한 정보를 쥐고 있던지 말이야. 어쩌면 그게 바로 쉰 가지 필체로 쓰는 자들, 한 가지밖에 못 쓰는 너 같은 비열한 놈과는 다른 자들이겠지. 콤피슨과, 매그위치와, 교수대를 조심하라고 해!\"\n\n그가 다시 양초를 내게 들이대 내 얼굴과 머리카락을 그을리고는, 한순간 내 눈을 멀게 하더니, 양초를 탁자에 도로 놓으며 그 우람한 등을 돌렸다. 그가 다시 내 쪽으로 돌아서기 전에, 나는 기도를 한 자락 떠올렸고, 조와 비디와 허버트와 함께 있었다.\n\n탁자와 맞은편 벽 사이에는 몇 피트의 빈 공간이 있었다. 이제 그는 그 공간 안에서 어슬렁어슬렁 앞뒤로 오갔다. 두 손을 옆구리에 늘어뜨려 묵직하게 떨군 채, 나를 사납게 노려보며 그렇게 거니는 그의 어마어마한 힘은, 전에 없이 더 단단하게 그의 몸에 자리 잡은 듯했다. 내게는 한 톨의 희망도 남아 있지 않았다. 내 속의 다급함이 아무리 미쳐 날뛰고, 생각 대신 스쳐 가는 영상들의 힘이 아무리 놀라웠다 한들, 나는 그래도 또렷이 이해할 수 있었다. 즉, 그가 내가 몇 순간 안에 모든 인간의 기억에서 영영 사라지리라 단정하지 않았다면, 그는 결코 자기가 들려준 그 모든 것을 내게 들려주지 않았으리라는 사실을.\n\n별안간 그가 멈춰 서더니, 병의 마개를 뽑아 내던졌다. 그토록 가벼운 것인데도, 나는 그것이 추처럼 떨어지는 소리를 들었다. 그는 병을 조금씩 조금씩 기울여 가며 천천히 삼켰고, 이제 더는 나를 보지 않았다. 마지막 몇 방울의 술을 그는 손바닥에 부어 핥아 먹었다. 그런 다음, 갑작스레 폭력적으로 서두르며 끔찍하게 욕설을 퍼붓더니, 병을 내던지고 몸을 굽혔다. 그리고 그의 손에는 길고 묵직한 자루가 달린 돌 망치가 들려 있었다.\n\n내가 세운 결심은 나를 저버리지 않았으니, 나는 그에게 애원하는 헛된 말 한마디 내뱉지 않고, 있는 힘껏 소리를 질러 대고 있는 힘껏 몸부림쳤다. 움직일 수 있는 것은 머리와 다리뿐이었으나, 그 정도로나마 나는 그때까지 미처 몰랐던 내 안의 온 힘을 다해 버둥거렸다. 바로 그 순간 나는 화답하는 외침 소리를 들었고, 사람 형체들과 한 줄기 불빛이 문으로 뛰어드는 것을 보았으며, 목소리들과 소란을 듣고는, 올릭이 사람들의 격투 속에서 — 마치 그것이 솟구치는 물결이라도 되는 듯 — 빠져나와, 단숨에 탁자를 뛰어넘어 밤 속으로 달아나는 것을 보았다.\n\n한바탕 정신을 잃은 뒤, 나는 같은 자리 바닥에 묶이지 않은 채 누워 있고, 머리는 누군가의 무릎에 얹혀 있음을 알게 되었다. 정신이 들었을 때 — 내 마음이 그것을 알아보기도 전에 그쪽으로 눈이 떠져 있었으니 — 내 두 눈은 벽에 세워진 그 사다리에 고정되어 있었다. 그리하여 의식을 되찾으면서, 나는 내가 의식을 잃었던 바로 그 자리에 있음을 알았다.\n\n처음에는 나를 떠받치고 있는 이가 누군지 둘러보아 확인할 마음조차 없을 만큼 무기력했던 나는, 사다리를 바라보며 누워 있었는데, 그때 나와 사다리 사이로 얼굴 하나가 들어왔다. 트래브 점원 아이의 얼굴이었다!\n\n\"이만하면 괜찮은 것 같아요!\" 트래브 점원 아이가 차분한 목소리로 말했다. \"근데 어찌 이리 핼쑥하담!\"\n\n이 말에, 나를 떠받치고 있던 이의 얼굴이 내 얼굴을 들여다보았고, 나는 나를 떠받친 사람이 누구인지 알아보았다 —\n\n\"허버트! 맙소사!\"\n\n\"가만히.\" 허버트가 말했다. \"찬찬히, 핸들. 너무 서두르지 마.\"\n\n\"그리고 우리 옛 동지, 스타톱!\" 그도 내 위로 몸을 숙이자 나는 외쳤다.\n\n\"그가 우리를 도와 할 일이 무엇인지 기억하고.\" 허버트가 말했다. \"마음을 가라앉혀.\"\n\n그 말에 나는 벌떡 몸을 일으켰으나, 팔의 통증으로 다시 주저앉고 말았다. \"아직 시간이 다 가지 않았지, 허버트, 그렇지? 오늘이 무슨 요일 밤이야? 내가 여기 얼마나 있었던 거지?\" 내가 거기 오랫동안 — 하루 낮 하루 밤이고, 이틀 낮 이틀 밤이고, 그보다 더 오래 — 누워 있었던 게 아닐까 하는 이상하고도 강한 불안이 들었기 때문이다.\n\n\"시간은 다 가지 않았어. 아직 월요일 밤이야.\"\n\n\"고마우신 하느님!\"\n\n\"그리고 너한테는 내일 화요일 하루가 통째로 남아 있으니, 그때 쉬면 돼.\" 허버트가 말했다. \"하지만 신음 소리가 절로 나오는구나, 핸들. 어디를 다친 거야? 일어설 수 있겠어?\"\n\n\"응, 응.\" 내가 말했다. \"걸을 수 있어. 이 욱신거리는 팔 말고는 다친 데가 없어.\"\n\n그들은 팔을 드러내 놓고 할 수 있는 처치를 했다. 팔은 심하게 부어올라 벌겋게 달아 있었고, 나는 손이 닿는 것조차 거의 견디지 못했다. 그러나 그들은 손수건을 찢어 새 붕대를 만들었고, 우리가 읍내에 이르러 바를 만한 시원한 물약을 구할 때까지, 조심스레 팔을 다시 삼각건에 걸어 주었다. 이윽고 우리는 어둡고 텅 빈 수문집의 문을 닫고, 돌아가는 길에 채석장을 가로질러 가고 있었다. 트래브 점원 아이가 — 이제는 트래브의 덩치 큰 젊은이가 — 등불을 들고 우리 앞에 섰는데, 그것이 바로 아까 문으로 들어오는 것을 내가 보았던 그 불빛이었다. 그러나 달은 내가 마지막으로 하늘을 보았을 때보다 족히 두 시간은 더 높이 떠 있었고, 밤은 비가 오는데도 한결 밝았다. 우리가 지나는 동안 가마의 흰 증기는 우리에게서 멀어져 갔고, 앞서 기도를 떠올렸던 나는 이제 감사 기도를 떠올렸다.\n\n내가 어떻게 나를 구하러 오게 되었는지 들려 달라고 허버트에게 간청하니 — 처음에 그는 딱 잘라 거절하며 내가 가만히 있어야 한다고 우겼으나 — 나는 다음과 같은 사정을 알게 되었다. 다급한 나머지 나는 우리 숙소에 그 편지를 펼친 채로 떨어뜨려 두었던 것이다. 허버트는 내게 오는 길에 거리에서 스타톱을 만나 그를 데리고 집으로 돌아왔다가, 내가 떠난 지 얼마 되지 않아 그 편지를 발견했다. 편지의 어조가 그를 불안하게 했고, 내가 그에게 남긴 황급한 편지와 그 내용이 어긋나는 탓에 더더욱 그러했다. 그의 불안은 가라앉기는커녕 점점 커졌고, 십오 분쯤 곰곰이 생각한 끝에, 동행을 자청한 스타톱과 함께 역마차 사무소로 가서 다음 마차가 언제 내려가는지 알아보았다. 오후 마차는 이미 떠난 것을 알게 되었고, 또 일이 막힐 때마다 그의 불안이 분명한 경악으로 자라나자, 그는 역마차를 빌려 뒤따르기로 했다. 그리하여 그와 스타톱은 블루 보어에 도착했는데, 거기서 나를, 혹은 내 소식을 찾으리라 잔뜩 기대했으나, 둘 다 찾지 못하자 미스 해비셤의 집으로 갔고, 거기서 내 행적을 놓쳐 버렸다. 그래서 그들은 (필시 내가 내 이야기에 관한 그 고장의 떠도는 소문을 듣고 있던 무렵에) 요기도 하고 습지로 안내해 줄 사람도 구하러 호텔로 돌아왔다. 보어 여관 아치문 아래 빈둥거리던 자들 가운데 마침 트래브 점원 아이가 — 제 볼일도 없는 데마다 으레 나타나곤 하는 그 오랜 버릇에 충실하게 — 끼어 있었고, 트래브 점원 아이는 내가 미스 해비셤의 집에서 식사하기로 한 곳 쪽으로 지나가는 것을 보았던 터였다. 그리하여 트래브 점원 아이가 그들의 길잡이가 되었고, 그들은 그를 데리고 수문집으로 나섰다 — 다만 내가 피했던, 읍내를 거쳐 습지로 가는 길로 갔다. 그런데 길을 가는 동안, 허버트는 어쩌면 내가 결국 프로비스의 안전과 관련된 어떤 진짜 긴요한 용무로 거기 불려 왔을지도 모른다는 데 생각이 미쳤고, 만약 그렇다면 끼어드는 것이 해가 되리라 헤아려, 길잡이와 스타톱을 채석장 가장자리에 남겨 두고 혼자 나아가, 안에서 모든 일이 무탈한지 알아보고자 두세 차례 집 둘레를 살그머니 돌았다. 그가 들을 수 있는 것이라곤 거칠고 굵은 목소리 하나의 어렴풋한 소리뿐이었기에(이것은 내 마음이 그토록 분주히 돌아가던 동안의 일이었다), 그는 마침내 내가 거기 있기는 한 건지 의심하기 시작하던 차에, 별안간 내가 큰 소리로 외쳤고, 그가 그 외침에 화답하며 안으로 뛰어들었으며, 나머지 두 사람이 그 뒤를 바짝 따랐던 것이다.\n\n내가 집 안에서 벌어진 일을 허버트에게 들려주자, 그는 밤이 늦었음에도 당장 읍내의 치안판사에게 가서 영장을 발부받자고 했다. 그러나 나는 이미, 그렇게 하면 우리가 거기 발이 묶이거나 다시 돌아와야 할 처지가 되어, 프로비스에게 치명적일 수 있다는 점을 헤아려 둔 터였다. 이 난점은 부인할 도리가 없었고, 그래서 우리는 그때로서는 올릭을 추적할 생각을 모두 단념했다. 당장은 사정이 사정인 만큼, 우리는 그 일을 트래브 점원 아이에게 가벼운 일인 양 둘러대는 편이 현명하다고 여겼다. 만약 자기가 끼어든 덕에 내가 석회 가마에서 구해졌다는 것을 그 아이가 알았더라면, 분명 크게 실망해 어지간히 마음을 다쳤을 것이다. 트래브 점원 아이가 악의 있는 성품이어서가 아니라, 남아도는 활기가 너무 많아서, 누구를 희생시켜서라도 변화와 자극을 갈구하는 것이 그 천성이었기 때문이다. 헤어질 때 나는 그에게 2기니를 건넸고(그것은 그의 바람에 들어맞는 듯했다), 한때 너를 나쁘게 여겼던 것이 미안하다고 말했다(이 말은 그에게 아무런 감흥도 주지 못했다).\n\n수요일이 이토록 코앞에 닥쳤으므로, 우리는 셋이 역마차를 타고 그날 밤 런던으로 돌아가기로 마음먹었다. 그래야 그날 밤의 소동이 입에 오르내리기 전에 빠져나갈 수 있으리라는 이유도 있었다. 허버트는 내 팔에 바를 큰 약병 하나를 구해 왔고, 밤새도록 그 약을 팔에 떨어뜨려 준 덕에, 나는 여정 동안 그 고통을 가까스로 견딜 수 있었다. 우리가 템플에 다다랐을 때는 날이 밝아 있었고, 나는 곧장 잠자리에 들어 하루 종일 누워 있었다.\n\n거기 누워 있는 동안, 병이 나서 내일 일을 감당하지 못하게 되리라는 두려움이 어찌나 끈질기게 나를 따라다녔던지, 그것이 그 자체만으로도 나를 쓰러뜨리지 않은 게 신기할 정도다. 내가 겪은 정신적 소모와 맞물렸다면, 그 두려움은 거의 틀림없이 나를 쓰러뜨렸을 것이다. 그러나 내일이라는 것이 내게 가한 그 비상한 긴장이 그것을 막아 주었다. 그토록 애타게 기다려 온 내일, 그토록 중대한 결과를 품은 내일, 그토록 가까이 다가왔으면서도 그 결말은 그토록 헤아릴 수 없게 감추어진 내일이.\n\n그날 그와 연락하기를 삼가는 것보다 더 당연한 조처는 없었다. 그러나 이것이 또 내 불안을 더했다. 나는 발소리 하나, 소리 하나에도 깜짝깜짝 놀라며, 그가 발각되어 잡혔고 이것이 내게 그 소식을 전하러 온 전령이라고 믿었다. 나는 그가 잡혔음을 내가 안다고, 두려움이나 예감 이상의 무언가가 내 마음을 짓누르고 있다고, 그 일이 실제로 일어났고 내게 그것에 대한 알 수 없는 앎이 있다고 스스로를 다그쳐 믿었다. 날이 저물어 가도 흉한 소식이 오지 않고, 하루해가 기울어 어둠이 내리자, 내일 아침이 오기 전에 병으로 쓰러지고 말리라는 그 무겁게 짓누르는 두려움이 나를 완전히 사로잡았다. 화끈거리는 팔이 욱신거리고, 화끈거리는 머리가 욱신거렸으며, 나는 내가 헛소리를 하기 시작하는 것이 아닌가 싶었다. 나는 정신을 다잡으려고 큰 수까지 헤아렸고, 산문이며 운문이며 외고 있던 구절들을 되뇌었다. 더러는 지친 마음이 잠시 빠져나갈 틈을 타, 몇 순간 깜빡 졸거나 깜빡 잊기도 했다. 그러면 나는 흠칫 놀라며 스스로에게 말하곤 했다. \"이제 그게 닥쳐온 거야, 나는 헛소리에 빠져들고 있어!\"\n\n그들은 온종일 나를 아주 조용히 있게 했고, 끊임없이 내 팔에 새 처치를 해 주었으며, 시원한 마실 것을 주었다. 잠이 들 때마다 나는 수문집에서 가졌던 그 생각, 즉 오랜 시간이 흘러 그를 구할 기회가 사라져 버렸다는 생각과 함께 깨어났다. 자정 무렵 나는 침대에서 나와, 내가 스물네 시간이나 잠들어 있었고 수요일이 지나가 버렸다고 굳게 믿은 채 허버트에게로 갔다. 그것이 내 안달이 스스로를 소진시킨 마지막 발버둥이었으니, 그 뒤로 나는 깊이 잠들었다.\n\n창밖을 내다보았을 때 수요일 아침이 밝아 오고 있었다. 다리 위에서 깜박이던 불빛들은 이미 희미해졌고, 떠오르는 해는 지평선 위의 불타는 습지 같았다. 강은 여전히 어둡고 신비로웠으며, 차갑게 잿빛으로 물들어 가는 다리들이 강을 가로질렀는데, 그 꼭대기에는 여기저기 하늘에서 타오르는 불의 따스한 빛이 한 점씩 닿아 있었다. 유난히 맑은 대기 속으로 교회 탑들과 첨탑들이 솟아오른, 다닥다닥 모인 지붕들을 따라 내가 바라보고 있노라니, 해가 떠올랐고, 강에서 베일이 한 겹 벗겨지는 듯하더니 수백만 개의 반짝임이 그 물 위로 한꺼번에 터져 나왔다. 나에게서도 베일이 한 겹 벗겨지는 듯했고, 나는 몸이 튼튼하고 거뜬해진 것을 느꼈다.\n\n허버트는 침대에서 잠들어 있었고, 우리의 옛 동창은 소파에서 잠들어 있었다. 나는 누구의 도움 없이는 옷을 입을 수 없었으나, 아직 타고 있던 불을 지피고 그들을 위해 커피를 좀 끓여 두었다. 때맞춰 그들도 튼튼하고 거뜬한 몸으로 벌떡 일어났고, 우리는 창문으로 상쾌한 아침 공기를 들이고는, 여전히 우리 쪽으로 밀려오고 있는 밀물을 바라보았다.\n\n\"아홉 시에 물이 바뀌거든.\" 허버트가 쾌활하게 말했다. \"우리를 잘 살펴보고, 채비를 갖추고 있게나, 저기 밀폰드 뱅크에 있는 자네 말일세!\"\n\n# Chapter LIV.\n\n삼월의 어느 날이었다. 햇볕은 뜨겁게 내리쬐는데 바람은 차갑게 불어, 빛 속은 여름이요 그늘 속은 겨울인 그런 날 가운데 하나였다. 우리는 두꺼운 모직 외투를 챙겼고, 나는 가방 하나를 들었다. 내가 가진 세간 가운데 그 가방을 채울 몇 가지 필수품 외에는 아무것도 가져가지 않았다. 내가 어디로 갈지, 무엇을 할지, 또 언제 돌아올지는 내게 전혀 알 수 없는 물음이었다. 그러나 나는 그런 물음으로 마음을 어지럽히지 않았으니, 내 마음은 온전히 프로비스의 안전에만 쏠려 있었던 것이다. 다만 나는 문 앞에 멈춰 서서 뒤를 돌아보며, 다음번에 — 만약 다시 보게 된다면 — 어떤 달라진 처지에서 이 방들을 보게 될까 하고 스쳐 가는 순간 동안만 궁금해했을 뿐이다.\n\n우리는 어슬렁어슬렁 템플 선착장으로 내려가, 아예 물 위로 나설 결심이 서지 않은 사람들처럼 그곳에서 한참을 서성였다. 물론 나는 배가 준비되어 있고 모든 것이 제자리에 갖추어져 있도록 미리 손을 써 두었다. 우리 템플 선착장에 딸린 두세 명의 물뭍 양서류 같은 사내들 말고는 보는 이도 없는데, 잠시 결심이 서지 않는 척 시늉을 한 뒤, 우리는 배에 올라 줄을 풀었다. 허버트가 뱃머리에, 내가 키를 잡았다. 그때가 만조 무렵, 여덟 시 반쯤이었다.\n\n우리 계획은 이러했다. 아홉 시에 썰물이 시작되어 세 시까지 우리 쪽으로 흘러가니, 물때가 바뀐 뒤에도 계속 슬금슬금 나아가다가 어두워질 때까지 물살을 거슬러 노를 저을 작정이었다. 그렇게 하면 그레이브젠드 아래쪽, 켄트와 에식스 사이로 길게 뻗은 그 물줄기에 이를 터였다. 그곳은 강이 넓고 한적하며, 강가에 사는 이도 매우 드물고, 외딴 선술집들이 여기저기 흩어져 있어 그중 하나를 골라 쉴 곳으로 삼을 수 있었다. 거기서 우리는 밤새 머물 작정이었다. 함부르크행 증기선과 로테르담행 증기선은 목요일 아침 아홉 시쯤 런던에서 출발할 예정이었다. 우리가 어디쯤 있는가에 따라 그 배들을 몇 시쯤 만나리라는 것을 알 수 있을 터였고, 먼저 오는 배에 손짓해 부를 작정이었다. 그래서 혹여 무슨 일이 생겨 먼저 배에 오르지 못하더라도 또 한 번의 기회가 남는 셈이었다. 우리는 두 배를 구별하는 표지를 알고 있었다.\n\n마침내 그 목적을 실행에 옮기는 일에 착수했다는 안도감이 어찌나 컸던지, 나는 불과 몇 시간 전에 내가 어떤 상태에 있었는지조차 실감하기 어려웠다. 상쾌한 공기, 햇빛, 강 위의 움직임, 그리고 흐르는 강물 그 자체 — 우리와 나란히 달리며 우리에게 공감하고, 우리를 북돋우고, 앞으로 나아가라 격려해 주는 듯한 그 길 — 그것들이 나를 새로운 희망으로 새롭게 했다. 배 안에서 내가 거의 쓸모가 없다는 사실에 부끄러움을 느꼈지만, 내 두 친구보다 나은 노잡이는 드물었고, 그들은 하루 종일 이어 갈 만한 한결같은 노질로 배를 저었다.\n\n그 시절 템스강의 증기선 통행량은 지금에 비해 훨씬 적었고, 뱃사공의 거룻배는 훨씬 많았다. 바지선이며 석탄 운반 범선이며 연안 무역선은 아마 지금만큼은 있었을 것이다. 그러나 크고 작은 증기선은 지금의 십분의 일도, 이십분의 일도 되지 않았다. 이른 시각이었음에도 그날 아침 여기저기 노 젓는 작은 배들이 잔뜩 오갔고, 물때를 따라 내려가는 바지선도 수두룩했다. 다리와 다리 사이를 트인 배로 오가는 일은 요즘보다 그 시절에 훨씬 수월하고 흔한 일이었으니, 우리는 수많은 작은 배와 나룻배 사이를 활기차게 헤치고 나아갔다.\n\n이내 옛 런던 다리를 지났고, 굴 까는 배들과 네덜란드인들로 북적이는 옛 빌링스게이트 시장과 화이트 타워와 반역자의 문을 지나, 우리는 줄지어 늘어선 배들 사이로 들어섰다. 거기에는 리스, 애버딘, 글래스고로 가는 증기선들이 화물을 싣고 부리고 있었는데, 우리가 옆을 지날 때 보니 물 위로 어마어마하게 높이 솟아 있었다. 거기에는 석탄선이 수십 척, 또 수십 척이나 있었고, 갑판의 발판에서 석탄 운반꾼들이 뛰어내리며 위로 끌어올려지는 석탄 무게의 평형추 노릇을 했는데, 그 석탄은 곧 뱃전 너머 바지선으로 덜커덕덜커덕 쏟아졌다. 거기, 정박줄에 매여 있는 것이 내일 로테르담으로 떠날 증기선이라, 우리는 그것을 눈여겨보았다. 그리고 거기, 우리가 그 제1사장(斜檣) 아래로 지나간 것이 내일 함부르크로 떠날 배였다. 이제 고물에 앉은 나는, 한결 빨라진 가슴의 고동과 함께 밀폰드 둑과 밀폰드 선착장을 볼 수 있었다.\n\n\"그분이 거기 계셔?\" 허버트가 말했다.\n\n\"아직 안 보여.\"\n\"좋아! 우리를 보기 전에는 내려오지 않기로 했으니까. 신호가 보여?\"\n\n\"여기서는 잘 안 보이는데, 보이는 것 같기도 해. — 이제 보인다! 자, 둘 다 저어. 천천히, 허버트. 노 멈춰!\"\n\n우리는 한순간 선착장에 가볍게 닿았고, 그분이 배에 올랐으며, 우리는 다시 출발했다. 그분은 뱃사람용 망토와 검은 캔버스 가방을 지니고 있었고, 내 마음이 바랄 수 있는 만큼 영락없는 강물 도선사처럼 보였다.\n\n\"얘야!\" 그분이 자리에 앉으며 내 어깨에 팔을 올리고 말했다. \"충실한 내 아들아, 잘했다. 고맙다, 고마워!\"\n\n다시 줄지어 늘어선 배들 사이로, 들어갔다 나오며, 녹슨 사슬 닻줄과 닳아 해진 삼밧줄과 까딱거리는 부표를 피하고, 떠다니는 부서진 바구니를 잠깐씩 가라앉히고, 떠다니는 나뭇조각과 대팻밥을 흩뜨리고, 떠다니는 석탄 찌끼를 가르며, 들어갔다 나오며, 바람을 향해 일장 연설을 하는 (수많은 존이 그러하듯) 선더랜드의 존호의 선수상 아래로, 그리고 가슴팍을 의젓하고 위엄 있게 내밀고 두 눈이 머리에서 두 치나 튀어나온 듯 불거진 야머스의 벳시호 아래로, 들어갔다 나오며, 조선소마다 망치 소리가 울리고, 톱이 목재를 켜고, 알 수 없는 무언가를 두드리는 기계들이 쟁그랑거리고, 물 새는 배에서 펌프가 돌아가고, 캡스턴이 돌아가고, 배들이 바다로 나아가고, 알아들을 수 없는 바다 짐승 같은 사내들이 뱃전 너머로 거룻배꾼들에게 저주를 퍼붓고 거룻배꾼들이 맞받아치는 가운데, 들어갔다 나오며, — 마침내 더 맑은 강 위로 나왔다. 거기서는 갑판 소년들이 뱃전 너머에 늘어뜨려 흙탕물 속에서 고기를 낚는 짓을 그만두고 방현재를 거두어들일 수 있었고, 꽃줄처럼 늘어진 돛들이 바람을 받아 활짝 펼쳐질 수 있었다.\n\n우리가 그분을 태운 선착장에서부터, 그리고 그 후로 줄곧, 나는 우리가 의심받고 있다는 어떤 낌새라도 있는지 조심스레 살폈다. 아무것도 보이지 않았다. 우리는 분명 미행당하지 않았으며, 그때 역시 분명 어떤 배에 따라붙임을 당하거나 미행당하지 않았다. 만약 어떤 배가 우리를 노리고 있었다면, 나는 배를 기슭에 댄 뒤 그 배가 먼저 지나가게 하거나 그 의도를 분명히 드러내도록 했을 것이다. 그러나 우리는 어떤 방해의 기색도 없이 우리 길을 지켰다.\n\n그분은 뱃사람용 망토를 두르고 있어, 앞서 말했듯 풍경 속에 자연스레 녹아드는 한 부분처럼 보였다. (어쩌면 그분이 살아온 비참한 삶이 그 까닭일지도 모르나) 그분이 우리 가운데 가장 덜 불안해한다는 것은 주목할 만한 일이었다. 그분이 무심한 것은 아니었으니, 자기 신사가 머나먼 나라에서 가장 훌륭한 신사 가운데 하나가 되는 것을 살아서 보고 싶다고 내게 말했기 때문이다. 또 내가 보기에 그분은 수동적이거나 체념하려는 사람도 아니었다. 다만 위험을 미리 마중하러 나갈 생각이 없을 뿐이었다. 위험이 닥쳐오면 그분은 그것에 맞섰으나, 위험은 그분이 스스로를 괴롭히기에 앞서 먼저 와야만 하는 것이었다.\n\n\"얘야, 네가 안다면 말이다.\" 그분이 내게 말했다. \"날이면 날마다 사면이 벽으로 둘러싸인 곳에 갇혀 있다가, 이렇게 내 사랑하는 아들 곁에 앉아 담배를 피운다는 게 어떤 건지 안다면, 너는 나를 부러워할 거다. 하지만 너는 그게 어떤 건지 모른다.\"\n\n\"저도 자유의 기쁨은 좀 안다고 생각해요.\" 내가 대답했다.\n\n\"아.\" 그분이 진지하게 고개를 저으며 말했다. \"하지만 너는 나만큼은 모른다. 나만큼 알려면, 얘야, 자물쇠와 열쇠에 갇혀 봤어야 한다. — 하지만 처량한 소리는 하지 않으마.\"\n\n어떤 사로잡힌 생각 하나 때문에 그분이 제 자유와, 심지어 제 목숨까지 위태롭게 했다는 것은 앞뒤가 맞지 않는 일처럼 느껴졌다. 그러나 어쩌면 위험 없는 자유라는 것은, 그분이 살아온 모든 습성과 너무나 동떨어진 것이어서, 다른 사람에게라면 의미를 지닐 그것이 그분에게는 그렇지 않았으리라고 나는 곰곰이 생각했다. 내 짐작은 크게 빗나가지 않았으니, 그분이 잠시 담배를 피운 뒤 이렇게 말했기 때문이다. —\n\n\"있잖니, 얘야, 내가 저 너머, 세상 반대편에 있을 때, 나는 늘 이쪽을 바라보고 있었다. 그리고 부자가 되어 가는데도 거기 있는 게 영 시들했지. 누구나 매그위치를 알았고, 매그위치는 오든 가든, 누구도 그자에 대해 머리를 썩이지 않았다. 그런데 여기서는 나를 그렇게 마음 편히 내버려 두지 않아, 얘야. — 적어도 내가 어디 있는지 알면 그렇지 않을 거란 말이다.\"\n\n\"모든 일이 잘 풀리면,\" 내가 말했다. \"몇 시간 안에 다시 완전히 자유롭고 안전해지실 거예요.\"\n\n\"그래.\" 그분이 긴 숨을 내쉬며 대꾸했다. \"그러길 바란다.\"\n\n\"그렇게 생각도 하시고요?\"\n\n그분이 뱃전 너머 물에 손을 담그더니, 내게 낯설지 않은 그 부드러워진 기색을 띠고 미소 지으며 말했다. —\n\n\"그래, 그렇게 생각하는가 보다, 얘야. 지금 우리보다 더 조용하고 느긋하기도 어렵겠지. 하지만 — 어쩌면 물 위로 이리 부드럽고 기분 좋게 흘러가는 탓에 그런 생각이 드는지도 모르겠다만 — 방금 담배를 피우면서 이런 생각을 했다. 우리가 앞으로 닥칠 몇 시간의 바닥을 들여다볼 수 없는 게, 내가 손으로 움켜쥐는 이 강물의 바닥을 들여다볼 수 없는 거나 매한가지라고 말이다. 게다가 그 시간의 물때를 붙잡아 둘 수 없는 게, 내가 이 물을 붙잡아 둘 수 없는 거나 똑같지. 봐라, 손가락 사이로 다 흘러가 버렸잖니!\" 그분이 물방울 듣는 손을 들어 보이며 말했다.\n\n\"그 얼굴만 아니라면 저는 아저씨가 좀 풀이 죽으셨나 생각했을 거예요.\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"천만에, 얘야! 이렇게 조용히 흘러가는 데다, 저 뱃머리에서 찰랑거리는 소리가 무슨 주일 찬송가 가락 같아서 그런 거다. 어쩌면 내가 조금 늙어 가는 탓도 있겠지.\"\n\n그분은 흐트러짐 없는 표정으로 다시 파이프를 입에 물고, 우리가 이미 영국을 벗어나기라도 한 양 침착하고 흡족하게 앉아 있었다. 그러면서도 그분은 끊임없이 두려움에 떨고 있는 사람처럼 한마디 충고에도 고분고분했다. 우리가 맥주 몇 병을 배에 실으려고 기슭에 댔을 때, 그분이 내리려 하기에 나는 그분이 있던 자리에 그대로 있는 편이 가장 안전할 것 같다고 넌지시 말했고, 그러자 그분은 \"그러냐, 얘야?\" 하더니 조용히 다시 앉았다.\n\n강 위의 공기는 차갑게 느껴졌지만 화창한 날이었고, 햇볕은 무척 기운을 북돋아 주었다. 물때가 거세게 흘러, 나는 그 한 자락도 놓치지 않도록 주의했고, 우리의 한결같은 노질은 우리를 더없이 잘 실어 날랐다. 썰물이 빠져나가면서, 알아챌 수 없을 만큼 조금씩, 우리는 가까운 숲과 언덕을 점점 더 잃어 가며 진흙 둑 사이로 점점 더 낮게 내려갔지만, 그레이브젠드를 벗어날 때까지도 물때는 아직 우리 편이었다. 우리의 화물 같은 그분이 망토에 싸여 있었으므로, 나는 일부러 떠 있는 세관 배에서 한두 척 길이 안쪽으로 지나, 두 척의 이민선 옆을 따라 본류로 빠져나갔는데, 앞갑판에 병사들을 태우고 우리를 내려다보는 큰 수송선의 뱃머리 밑을 지났다. 이윽고 물때가 느슨해지기 시작했고, 닻을 내리고 있던 배들이 빙 돌기 시작했으며, 곧 그것들이 모두 한 방향으로 돌아서더니, 새 물때를 타고 풀로 거슬러 오르려는 배들이 한 무리를 이루어 우리에게 밀려들기 시작했다. 우리는 이제 물살의 거센 곳을 되도록 피해 기슭에 바싹 붙어, 얕은 여울과 진흙 둑을 조심스레 비켜 가며 나아갔다.\n\n우리 노잡이들은 이따금 일이 분씩 배를 물때에 맡겨 흘러가게 한 덕에 워낙 기운이 팔팔해서, 십오 분만 쉬어도 충분하고도 남았다. 우리는 미끄러운 돌들 사이로 기슭에 올라 가져온 것을 먹고 마시며 주위를 둘러보았다. 그곳은 평평하고 단조로우며 지평선이 흐릿한 것이, 꼭 내 고향 습지 같았다. 구불구불한 강은 굽이굽이 돌고, 그 위에 떠 있는 커다란 부표들도 빙글빙글 돌았지만, 그 밖의 모든 것은 좌초된 듯 멈춰 있는 것 같았다. 이제 그 배의 무리 가운데 마지막 배가 우리가 돌아 나온 마지막 낮은 곶을 돌아갔고, 갈색 돛을 단 채 짚을 가득 실은 마지막 초록 바지선이 그 뒤를 따랐다. 어린아이가 처음으로 서툴게 흉내 낸 배 모양 같은 바닥짐 거룻배 몇 척이 진흙 속에 나지막이 누워 있었고, 트인 말뚝 위에 얹힌 작고 땅딸막한 여울 등대 하나가 진흙 속에서 죽마와 목발에 의지한 듯 절뚝거리며 서 있었다. 진흙에서는 미끈미끈한 말뚝이 삐죽 솟아 있고, 미끈미끈한 돌이 삐죽 솟아 있고, 붉은 표지와 조수 표지가 삐죽 솟아 있었으며, 낡은 선착장과 지붕 없는 낡은 건물이 진흙 속으로 미끄러져 들어가고 있었으니, 우리 주위는 온통 정체와 진흙뿐이었다.\n\n우리는 다시 배를 밀어내고 갈 수 있는 만큼 나아갔다. 이제는 훨씬 힘든 일이었지만, 허버트와 스타톱은 끈질기게 버티며 해가 질 때까지 젓고 또 젓고 또 저었다. 그 무렵 강물이 우리를 조금 들어 올려, 우리는 둑 위로 시야를 둘 수 있었다. 거기, 기슭의 나지막한 수평선 위로 붉은 해가, 보랏빛 안개 속에 빠르게 검은빛으로 짙어 가며 걸려 있었다. 그리고 거기 외로운 평평한 습지가 있었고, 저 멀리 솟아오른 땅들이 있었는데, 그 사이와 우리 사이에는 앞쪽 군데군데 처량한 갈매기 한 마리 말고는 아무 생명도 없는 것 같았다.\n\n밤이 빠르게 내리고, 달은 만월을 지난지라 일찍 뜨지 않을 터였으므로, 우리는 잠깐 의논을 했다. 짧은 의논이었으니, 우리가 갈 길은 분명 찾을 수 있는 첫 외딴 선술집에서 밤을 보내는 것이었기 때문이다. 그래서 그들은 다시 한 번 노를 저었고, 나는 집처럼 보이는 무언가가 없는지 살폈다. 그렇게 우리는 거의 말을 않고 따분한 사오 마일을 나아갔다. 무척 추웠고, 조리용 화덕에서 연기와 불길을 내며 석탄선 한 척이 우리 곁을 지나가는데, 그것이 마치 아늑한 보금자리처럼 보였다. 이때쯤 밤은 아침까지 그러할 만큼 캄캄했고, 우리가 가진 빛이라곤 하늘보다 강에서 더 많이 비쳐 나오는 듯했으니, 물에 잠겼다 나오는 노가 몇 개의 반사된 별들을 두드렸기 때문이다.\n\n이 음산한 시각, 우리는 모두가 누군가에게 미행당하고 있다는 생각에 사로잡혀 있는 것이 분명했다. 밀물이 들면서, 불규칙한 간격으로 기슭을 무겁게 철썩철썩 때렸다. 그런 소리가 날 때마다 우리 가운데 누군가는 어김없이 흠칫 놀라 그쪽을 바라보았다. 군데군데 물살이 둑을 깎아 작은 후미를 이루어 놓았는데, 우리는 그런 곳을 모두 의심하며 신경질적으로 살폈다. 이따금 \"저 잔물결은 뭐지?\" 하고 누군가 낮은 목소리로 말했다. 또 다른 누군가는 \"저기 저게 배 아냐?\" 했다. 그러고 나면 우리는 죽은 듯한 침묵에 빠졌고, 나는 노가 노받이에서 어찌 그리 유난스레 큰 소리를 내며 움직이나 하고 안달하며 앉아 있었다.\n\n마침내 우리는 불빛 하나와 지붕 하나를 알아보았고, 곧이어 가까이서 주워 모은 돌로 쌓은 작은 둑길에 배를 댔다. 나머지를 배에 남겨 둔 채 나는 기슭에 올랐고, 그 불빛이 어느 선술집 창문에서 나오는 것임을 알았다. 꽤나 지저분한 곳으로, 밀수꾼들에게도 낯설지 않을 성싶었다. 그러나 부엌에는 불이 잘 피워져 있었고, 먹을 달걀과 베이컨이 있었으며, 마실 술도 여러 가지 있었다. 또 침대가 둘씩 든 방이 두 개 있었는데 — \"그래 봤자 그 모양이지만요\" 하고 주인이 말했다. 집 안에 있는 사람이라곤 주인과 그 아내, 그리고 그 작은 둑길의 \"잭\"이라는, 마치 자기도 간조선이기라도 한 양 미끈거리고 꾀죄죄한 희끗희끗한 사내 하나뿐이었다.\n\n이 일꾼의 도움을 받아 나는 다시 배로 내려갔고, 우리 모두 기슭에 올라 노와 키와 보트 갈고리며 그 밖의 모든 것을 꺼내고, 배를 끌어 올려 밤을 대비했다. 우리는 부엌 불가에서 아주 잘 먹은 뒤 침실을 나누었다. 허버트와 스타톱이 한 방을, 나와 우리의 화물 같은 그분이 다른 방을 쓰기로 했다. 두 방 모두 마치 공기가 생명에 치명적이기라도 한 양 공기가 어찌나 정성껏 차단되어 있던지, 침대 밑에는 그 집이 가졌으리라 짐작되는 것보다 더 많은 더러운 옷가지와 모자 상자가 들어 있었다. 그래도 우리는 형편이 좋은 편이라 여겼으니, 이보다 더 외진 곳은 찾을 수 없었을 것이기 때문이다.\n\n식사 후 불가에서 몸을 녹이고 있는데, 잭이 — 그는 한구석에 앉아 있었고, 우리가 달걀과 베이컨을 먹는 동안 며칠 전 기슭으로 떠밀려 온 익사한 뱃사람의 발에서 벗겨 낸 흥미로운 유물이라며 퉁퉁 부어오른 신발 한 켤레를 신어 보였었다 — 나에게 네 사람이 노 젓는 갤리선 한 척이 물때를 따라 거슬러 올라가는 것을 보았느냐고 물었다. 못 봤다고 했더니, 그렇다면 그 배는 도로 내려갔을 거라며, 그래도 거기를 떠날 때는 분명 \"거슬러도 올라갔는데\" 하는 것이었다.\n\n\"무슨 까닭에선지 마음을 고쳐먹고,\" 잭이 말했다. \"다시 내려간 게 틀림없어요.\"\n\n\"네 사람이 노 젓는 갤리선이라고 했소?\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"네 명이,\" 잭이 말했다. \"거기다 앉은 사람이 둘.\"\n\n\"여기 기슭에 댔소?\"\n\n\"2갤런짜리 돌 단지를 가지고 맥주를 좀 받으러 들어왔지요. 나 같으면 그 맥주에 독이라도 타고 싶었어요.\" 잭이 말했다. \"아니면 무슨 지독한 설사약이라도 넣든지.\"\n\n\"왜?\" \"내가 왜 그러는지는 나도 알지요.\" 잭이 말했다. 그는 목구멍에 진흙이 잔뜩 씻겨 들어가기라도 한 양 질척한 목소리로 말했다.\n\n\"저놈은 생각하지요.\" 자기 잭에게 크게 의지하는 듯한, 핏기 없는 눈을 한 병약하고 골똘한 사내인 주인이 말했다. \"저들이 아닌 것을 두고 그것이라고 생각하는 거예요.\"\n\n\"내가 뭘 생각하는지는 내가 알아요.\" 잭이 한마디 했다.\n\n\"세관이라고 생각하나, 잭?\" 주인이 말했다.\n\n\"그렇소.\" 잭이 말했다.\n\n\"그럼 자네가 틀렸어, 잭.\"\n\n\"내가 틀렸다고요!\"\n그 대답에 담긴 무한한 의미와 제 견해에 대한 끝없는 자신감을 보이며, 잭은 퉁퉁 부은 신발 한 짝을 벗어 안을 들여다보더니, 부엌 바닥에 돌 몇 개를 털어 내고 다시 신었다. 그는 자기가 너무나 옳아서 무슨 짓이든 해도 괜찮은 잭이라는 듯한 태도로 그리했다.\n\n\"그럼 잭, 자네는 저들이 단추를 어떻게 했다고 보는 거야?\" 주인이 미적지근하게 흔들리며 물었다.\n\n\"단추를 어떻게 했느냐고요?\" 잭이 되받았다. \"뱃전 너머로 던져 버렸겠지요. 삼켜 버렸든지. 새싹 샐러드처럼 돋아나라고 심었든지. 단추를 어떻게 했느냐니!\"\n\n\"건방 떨지 말게, 잭.\" 주인이 우울하고 가련한 투로 나무랐다.\n\n\"세관 관리는 제 단추를 어떻게 해야 할지 다 알지요.\" 잭이 그 역겨운 낱말을 더없는 경멸을 담아 되풀이하며 말했다. \"그게 자기와 제 정체 사이에 끼어들 때 말이오. 네 사람에 앉은 사람 둘짜리 배가, 한 물때엔 올라갔다 다른 물때엔 내려갔다, 또 어떤 물때엔 거슬렀다 따라갔다 하면서 어슬렁어슬렁 맴도는 데에는, 그 밑바닥에 세관이 깔려 있지 않고서야 그럴 리가 없는 법이오.\" 그러고는 경멸하듯 밖으로 나가 버렸다. 받아 줄 사람이 없어진 주인은 그 이야기를 더 끌고 갈 수가 없게 되었다.\n\n이 대화는 우리 모두를 불안하게 했고, 특히 나를 몹시 불안하게 했다. 음산한 바람이 집 둘레에서 웅얼거렸고, 밀물은 기슭을 철썩였으며, 나는 우리가 갇힌 채 위협받고 있는 듯한 느낌이 들었다. 이토록 눈에 띌 만큼 유별나게 맴돌아 사람들의 주목을 끄는 네 명짜리 갤리선이라니, 그것은 내가 떨쳐 버릴 수 없는 흉한 정황이었다. 프로비스를 설득해 잠자리에 들게 한 뒤, 나는 두 친구와 함께 (이때쯤 스타톱도 사정을 알고 있었다) 밖으로 나가 다시 한 번 의논을 했다. 오후 한 시쯤일 증기선이 올 시각이 가까워질 때까지 이 집에 머물 것인가, 아니면 아침 일찍 떠날 것인가가 우리가 논의한 문제였다. 결국 우리는, 증기선이 올 시각의 한 시간쯤 전까지 있던 자리에 머물러 있다가 그 항로로 나가 물때를 타고 느긋하게 흘러가는 편이 더 나은 방책이라 여겼다. 그렇게 하기로 정한 뒤, 우리는 집 안으로 돌아가 잠자리에 들었다.\n\n나는 옷을 거의 다 입은 채 누워, 몇 시간 동안 잘 잤다. 깨어 보니 바람이 거세져, 집의 간판이 (그 배 모양 간판이) 삐걱거리고 쾅쾅거리며 나를 놀라게 하는 소리를 내고 있었다. 내 화물 같은 그분이 깊이 잠들어 있었으므로, 나는 가만히 일어나 창밖을 내다보았다. 창은 우리가 배를 끌어 올려 둔 둑길을 향해 나 있었는데, 눈이 흐린 달빛에 익숙해지자, 나는 두 사내가 그 배를 들여다보고 있는 것을 보았다. 그들은 다른 것은 아무것도 보지 않은 채 창 밑을 지나갔고, 내가 보기에 텅 비어 있는 선착장으로는 내려가지 않고, 노어 방향으로 습지를 가로질러 갔다.\n\n내 첫 충동은 허버트를 깨워 그 두 사내가 가는 것을 보여 주는 것이었다. 그러나 집 뒤편에 있어 내 방과 붙은 그의 방으로 들어가기 전에, 그와 스타톱이 나보다 더 고된 하루를 보내 지쳐 있다는 데 생각이 미쳐, 나는 참았다. 다시 창가로 돌아가 보니, 두 사내가 습지를 가로질러 움직이는 것이 보였다. 그러나 그 빛 속에서 나는 곧 그들을 놓쳤고, 몹시 추워져, 그 일을 곰곰이 생각하려고 누웠다가 다시 잠이 들었다.\n\n우리는 일찍 일어났다. 아침 식사 전에 넷이 함께 이리저리 거닐며, 나는 내가 본 것을 이야기하는 것이 옳다고 여겼다. 또다시 우리의 화물 같은 그분이 일행 가운데 가장 덜 불안해했다. 그 사내들은 십중팔구 세관 사람들일 테고, 우리 따위는 안중에도 없을 거라고 그분이 차분히 말했다. 나도 그러려니 — 사실 충분히 그럴 수도 있는 일이었다 — 스스로를 설득해 보려 했다. 그렇지만 나는, 우리 눈에 보이는 먼 곳까지 그분과 내가 함께 걸어가고, 정오쯤 배가 거기서, 혹은 그 근처 형편 닿는 데서 우리를 태우자고 제안했다. 이것이 좋은 예방책이라 여겨져, 아침 식사 후 얼마 지나지 않아 그분과 나는 선술집에는 아무 말 없이 길을 나섰다.\n\n그분은 걸어가며 파이프를 피웠고, 이따금 멈춰 서서 내 어깨를 토닥였다. 누가 봐도 위험에 처한 것은 그분이 아니라 나인 듯, 또 그분이 나를 안심시키는 듯이 보였을 것이다. 우리는 거의 말을 하지 않았다. 그 곶에 가까워지자, 나는 그분에게 가려진 곳에 머물러 달라고 청한 뒤 나 혼자 정찰을 나갔다. 간밤에 사내들이 지나간 것이 바로 그쪽 방향이었기 때문이다. 그분이 따랐고, 나는 혼자 나아갔다. 곶 앞에는 배가 한 척도 없었고, 그 근처 어디에도 끌어 올려 둔 배가 없었으며, 사내들이 거기서 배에 올랐다는 어떤 흔적도 없었다. 하지만 물론, 물때가 높아 물밑에 발자국이 남아 있었을지도 모를 일이었다.\n\n그분이 멀리 자기가 숨은 곳에서 내다보다가 내가 모자를 흔들어 올라오라는 신호를 보내는 것을 보고는 내게 다시 합류했고, 우리는 거기서 기다렸다. 때로는 외투를 두르고 둑에 누웠다가, 때로는 몸을 녹이려 이리저리 움직이며, 우리 배가 돌아 나오는 것을 볼 때까지. 우리는 수월하게 배에 올라, 증기선의 항로로 노 저어 나갔다. 그 무렵엔 한 시까지 십 분밖에 남지 않았고, 우리는 그 배의 연기를 살피기 시작했다.\n\n그러나 우리가 그 연기를 본 것은 한 시 반이 되어서였고, 곧이어 그 뒤로 또 다른 증기선의 연기가 보였다. 두 배가 전속력으로 다가오고 있었으므로, 우리는 가방 두 개를 챙기고, 그 틈을 타 허버트와 스타톱에게 작별을 고했다. 우리는 모두 다정하게 악수를 나누었고, 허버트의 눈도 내 눈도 채 마르지 않았을 때, 나는 우리 바로 앞쪽으로 네 명짜리 갤리선 한 척이 둑 밑에서 쏜살같이 튀어나와 같은 항로로 노 저어 나오는 것을 보았다.\n\n강의 굽이와 휘어짐 탓에 우리와 증기선의 연기 사이에는 아직 한 자락 기슭이 가로놓여 있었지만, 이제 그 배가 정면으로 다가오는 것이 보였다. 나는 허버트와 스타톱에게 물때 앞쪽을 지키라고, 그래야 우리가 그 배를 기다리고 있다는 것을 알아볼 수 있다고 외쳤고, 프로비스에게는 망토에 싸인 채 꼼짝 말고 앉아 있으라고 간곡히 일렀다. 그분은 \"나만 믿어라, 얘야\" 하고 명랑하게 대답하더니, 조각상처럼 앉아 있었다. 그러는 사이, 매우 능숙하게 다루어진 그 갤리선이 우리를 가로질러, 우리가 따라잡게 한 뒤 옆에 바싹 붙었다. 노가 움직일 만큼의 틈만 남긴 채, 그 배는 우리 곁에 붙어, 우리가 흘러가면 함께 흘러가고, 우리가 노를 저으면 한두 번 노를 당겼다. 앉아 있는 두 사람 가운데 하나는 키 줄을 잡고 우리를 주의 깊게 바라보았으니 — 노잡이들 모두가 그러했다 — 다른 한 사람은 프로비스와 꼭 마찬가지로 망토에 싸여, 몸을 움츠린 채 우리를 바라보며 키잡이에게 무어라 지시를 속삭이는 듯했다. 어느 배에서도 한마디 말이 없었다.\n\n스타톱이 몇 분 뒤 어느 증기선이 먼저인지 알아보고는, 우리가 마주 앉아 있던 터라 낮은 목소리로 \"함부르크\" 하고 일러 주었다. 그 배는 우리를 향해 무서운 속도로 다가왔고, 외륜이 물을 때리는 소리가 점점 더 커졌다. 그 그림자가 영락없이 우리 위에 드리워진 것처럼 느껴졌을 때, 갤리선에서 우리를 불렀다. 내가 대답했다.\n\n\"당신네 배에 귀환한 유형수가 타고 있소.\" 키 줄을 잡은 사내가 말했다. \"저 사람이오, 망토에 싸인 자. 이름은 에이블 매그위치, 다른 이름으로는 프로비스. 나는 저 사람을 체포하니, 저자에게는 투항을, 당신들에게는 협조를 요구하오.\"\n\n바로 그 순간, 자기 선원들에게 들리는 어떤 지시도 내리지 않은 채, 그는 갤리선을 우리에게 바짝 들이댔다. 우리가 그들이 무엇을 하는지 알아차리기도 전에, 그들은 갑자기 노를 한 번 힘껏 저어 앞으로 나아가더니, 노를 거두고, 우리를 가로질러 들이박고는, 우리 뱃전을 붙들었다. 이 때문에 증기선에서는 큰 혼란이 일어났고, 나는 그들이 우리에게 외치는 소리와, 외륜을 멈추라는 명령이 내려지는 소리, 그리고 외륜이 멈추는 소리를 들었으나, 그 배가 거역할 수 없는 기세로 우리를 향해 내리밀려 오는 것을 느꼈다. 같은 순간, 나는 갤리선의 키잡이가 제 죄수의 어깨에 손을 얹는 것을 보았고, 두 배가 물때의 힘에 떠밀려 빙 돌아가는 것을 보았으며, 증기선 위의 선원들이 모두 미친 듯이 앞으로 달려 나오는 것을 보았다. 여전히 같은 순간, 나는 죄수가 벌떡 일어나, 자기를 붙잡은 자를 가로질러 몸을 기울여, 갤리선 안에서 움츠리고 있던 사람의 목에서 망토를 잡아채는 것을 보았다. 여전히 같은 순간, 나는 그렇게 드러난 얼굴이 오래전 그 또 다른 죄수의 얼굴임을 보았다. 여전히 같은 순간, 나는 그 얼굴이 내가 결코 잊지 못할 새하얀 공포를 띤 채 뒤로 젖혀지는 것을 보았고, 증기선에서 터져 나오는 큰 비명과 물속의 요란한 첨벙 소리를 들었으며, 발밑에서 배가 가라앉는 것을 느꼈다.\n\n내가 천 개의 물방아 둑과 천 개의 번뜩이는 빛과 씨름하는 듯했던 것은 한순간뿐이었다. 그 한순간이 지나자, 나는 갤리선에 끌어 올려져 있었다. 허버트도 거기 있었고, 스타톱도 거기 있었다. 그러나 우리 배는 사라졌고, 두 죄수도 사라졌다.\n\n증기선에서 터지는 비명 소리에, 사납게 뿜어내는 증기 소리에, 그 배가 밀려오고 우리가 떠밀려 가는 통에, 나는 처음에는 하늘과 물을, 이 기슭과 저 기슭을 분간할 수 없었다. 그러나 갤리선의 선원들이 재빠른 솜씨로 배를 바로 세웠고, 빠르고 힘찬 노질로 앞으로 몇 번 나아간 뒤 노를 멈추고, 저마다 말없이 간절하게 고물 쪽 물을 바라보았다. 이윽고 물속에 검은 물체 하나가 보였는데, 물때를 타고 우리 쪽으로 떠내려오고 있었다. 아무도 말하지 않았으나, 키잡이가 손을 들자 모두가 가만히 노를 거꾸로 저어, 그것을 향해 배를 똑바로 곧게 두었다. 가까이 다가오자, 나는 그것이 헤엄치고 있는 매그위치임을 알아보았다. 그러나 자유로이 헤엄치는 것은 아니었다. 그분은 배에 끌어 올려졌고, 곧바로 양 손목과 발목에 쇠고랑이 채워졌다.\n\n갤리선은 흔들림 없이 유지되었고, 말없이 간절한 물의 감시가 다시 시작되었다. 그러나 이제 로테르담행 증기선이 다가왔는데, 무슨 일이 벌어졌는지 모르는 듯 빠른 속도로 다가왔다. 그 배를 불러 세웠을 때쯤에는 두 증기선이 모두 우리에게서 멀어져 가고 있었고, 우리는 어지러이 일렁이는 물결의 항적 속에서 오르내리고 있었다. 모든 것이 다시 잠잠해지고 두 증기선이 사라진 뒤로도 한참 동안 감시는 계속되었다. 그러나 이제는 가망이 없다는 것을 모두가 알고 있었다.\n\n마침내 우리는 그것을 단념하고, 조금 전 떠나온 선술집을 향해 기슭 가까이로 노 저어 갔다. 거기서 우리는 적잖은 놀라움 속에 맞아들여졌다. 여기서 나는 매그위치를 — 이제 더는 프로비스가 아니었다 — 위해 몇 가지 편의를 마련할 수 있었다. 그분은 가슴에 매우 심한 부상을 입었고, 머리에는 깊은 상처가 나 있었다.\n\n그분은 자기가 증기선의 용골 밑으로 들어갔다가 떠오르면서 머리를 부딪친 것 같다고 내게 말했다. 가슴의 부상은 (그 때문에 숨쉬기가 몹시 고통스러웠다) 갤리선 뱃전에 부딪혀 입은 것 같다고 했다. 그분은 자기가 콤피슨에게 무엇을 했는지 안 했는지 굳이 말하지는 않겠다고 덧붙였지만, 그자를 알아보려고 망토에 손을 댄 순간, 그 악당이 비틀거리며 일어나 비틀거리며 뒤로 물러났고, 둘이 함께 뱃전 너머로 떨어졌다고 했다. 바로 그때 그분(매그위치)이 우리 배에서 갑작스레 잡아채여 나가고, 그분을 붙잡은 자가 그분을 배 안에 붙들어 두려 한 것이 우리 배를 뒤집어 버린 것이었다. 그분은 둘이 서로의 팔에 사납게 얽혀 가라앉았고, 물속에서 몸싸움이 있었으며, 자기가 몸을 떼어 내 헤엄쳐 빠져나왔노라고 내게 속삭였다.\n\n나는 그분이 이렇게 들려준 이야기가 한 치도 틀림없는 사실임을 의심할 까닭이 전혀 없었다. 갤리선의 키를 잡았던 관리도 그들이 뱃전 너머로 떨어진 일에 대해 똑같이 진술했다.\n\n내가 이 관리에게, 선술집에서 구할 수 있는 여분의 옷을 사서 죄수의 젖은 옷을 갈아입히도록 허락해 달라고 청하자, 그는 흔쾌히 허락했다. 다만 죄수가 몸에 지닌 것은 전부 자기가 맡아야 한다고만 일러 두었다. 그리하여 한때 내 손에 있던 수첩이 그 관리의 손으로 넘어갔다. 그는 또 죄수를 따라 런던까지 동행하도록 허락해 주었으나, 내 두 친구에게는 그 호의를 베풀기를 거절했다.\n\n배 간판을 단 선술집의 잭은 익사한 사내가 가라앉은 곳을 전해 듣고, 시신이 기슭으로 떠밀려 올라올 가능성이 가장 높은 곳들에서 그것을 찾아보기로 했다. 그것을 거두는 일에 대한 그의 관심은, 그 시신이 양말을 신고 있다는 말을 듣자 내가 보기에 한층 높아진 듯했다. 아마 그를 완전히 차려입히는 데에는 익사한 사내 열두 명쯤이 들었을 터이고, 그 때문에 그의 옷가지가 저마다 다른 정도로 썩어 있었던 것인지도 모른다.\n\n우리는 물때가 바뀔 때까지 선술집에 머물렀고, 그런 뒤 매그위치를 갤리선으로 옮겨 태웠다. 허버트와 스타톱은 되도록 빨리 육로로 런던에 가기로 했다. 우리는 서글픈 작별을 나누었고, 내가 매그위치 곁에 자리를 잡았을 때, 나는 그것이 그분이 살아 있는 한 앞으로 내 자리이리라는 것을 느꼈다.\n\n이제 그분에 대한 내 혐오감은 모두 녹아 사라졌으니, 내 손을 제 손에 쥔 그 쫓기고 다치고 사슬에 묶인 사람에게서, 나는 그저 나의 은인이 되고자 했던 한 사내, 그리고 여러 해에 걸쳐 한결같이 나를 향해 애정 어리고 고마워하며 너그러운 마음을 품어 온 한 사내를 볼 뿐이었다. 나는 그분에게서, 내가 조에게 그래 왔던 것보다 훨씬 나은 사람을 볼 뿐이었다.\n\n밤이 깊어 갈수록 그분의 숨은 더욱 힘들고 고통스러워졌으며, 종종 신음을 억누르지 못했다. 나는 쓸 수 있는 한쪽 팔로 그분을 편한 자세로 받쳐 주려 애썼다. 그러나 그분이 죽는 것이 의심할 여지 없이 가장 나은 일인 까닭에, 그분이 심하게 다친 것을 마음속으로 슬퍼할 수 없다는 것을 생각하니 끔찍한 노릇이었다. 그분의 신원을 확인할 능력과 의향을 가진 사람들이 아직 충분히 살아 있다는 것을, 나는 의심할 수 없었다. 그분이 관대한 처분을 받으리라고는, 나는 바랄 수 없었다. 재판에서 가장 나쁜 모습으로 비추어졌고, 그 후 탈옥하여 다시 재판을 받았으며, 종신형을 받은 유형지에서 돌아왔고, 자기를 체포케 한 원인인 사내의 죽음을 초래한 그분이었으니.\n\n우리가 어제 뒤로하고 떠났던 그 지는 해를 향해 돌아가는 동안, 그리고 우리 희망의 물줄기가 모두 거꾸로 흘러가는 듯한 동안, 나는 그분에게 나를 위해 고국으로 돌아오셨다는 생각이 얼마나 가슴 아픈지 모르겠다고 말했다.\n\n\"얘야,\" 그분이 대답했다. \"나는 내 운수를 받아들이는 게 아주 만족스럽다. 내 아들을 봤으니, 그 애는 나 없이도 신사가 될 수 있어.\"\n\n아니었다. 나는 우리가 거기 나란히 앉아 있는 동안 그 일을 생각해 보았다. 아니었다. 내 자신의 어떤 마음과는 별개로, 나는 이제 웨믹의 암시를 이해했다. 그분이 유죄 판결을 받으면, 그분의 재산은 국왕에게 몰수되리라는 것을 나는 내다보았다.\n\n\"잘 들어라, 얘야.\" 그분이 말했다. \"이제 어떤 신사가 나한테 딸린 사람으로 알려지지 않는 게 상책이다. 그저 웨믹과 함께 우연히 들른 것처럼 나를 보러 와 주려무나. 내가 여러 번 가운데 마지막으로 선서를 할 때, 너를 볼 수 있는 자리에 앉아 다오. 그 이상은 바라지 않으마.\"\n\n\"제가 곁에 있도록 허락받는 한,\" 내가 말했다. \"저는 결코 아저씨 곁을 떠나지 않을 거예요. 부디 하느님의 뜻이라면, 아저씨가 제게 그러셨던 만큼 저도 아저씨께 진실하겠어요!\"\n\n내 손을 쥔 그분의 손이 떨리는 것이 느껴졌고, 그분은 배 바닥에 누운 채 얼굴을 돌렸으며, 나는 그분의 목구멍에서 그 옛 소리가 — 이제는 그분의 다른 모든 것과 마찬가지로 부드러워진 그 소리가 — 나는 것을 들었다. 그분이 이 점을 꺼낸 것은 다행한 일이었다. 그 덕분에, 그러지 않았더라면 너무 늦을 때까지 내가 미처 생각지 못했을 것 하나가 내 마음에 떠올랐기 때문이다. 그것은, 나를 부유하게 해 주려던 그분의 희망이 어떻게 스러졌는지를 그분이 결코 알 필요가 없다는 것이었다.\n\n# Chapter LV.\n\n그는 이튿날 즉결 재판소로 끌려갔고, 곧바로 정식 재판에 회부될 뻔했다. 다만 그가 한때 탈출했던 죄수 호송선의 늙은 간수를 불러 내려와 그의 신원을 증언하게 해야 했기 때문에, 일은 미뤄졌다. 신원을 의심하는 이는 아무도 없었다. 그러나 그것을 증언하려 했던 컴피슨은 죽어서 조수에 떠밀려 나뒹굴고 있었고, 마침 그때 런던에는 필요한 증언을 해 줄 만한 교도관이 한 사람도 없었다. 나는 간밤에 도착하자마자 곧장 재거스 씨의 자택으로 찾아가 그의 도움을 청해 두었으나, 재거스 씨는 죄수를 위해서는 아무것도 인정하려 들지 않았다. 그것이 유일한 방책이었다. 그는 증인이 나타나면 재판은 오 분 안에 끝나 버릴 것이며, 이 세상 그 어떤 힘으로도 우리에게 불리하게 흘러가는 것을 막을 수 없으리라고 내게 말했기 때문이다.\n\n나는 그가 자기 재산의 운명에 대해 모르도록 해 두려는 내 계획을 재거스 씨에게 전했다. 재거스 씨는 내가 \"그것을 손가락 사이로 흘려보냈다\"며 투덜대고 화를 냈다. 그러면서 머지않아 진정서를 올려 어떻게든 그 일부만이라도 건져 보아야 한다고 말했다. 그러나 재산 몰수가 강행되지 않는 경우도 많기는 하나, 이 사건에는 그런 예외에 해당될 만한 사정이 전혀 없다는 사실도 그는 내게 숨기지 않았다. 나는 그것을 아주 잘 이해하고 있었다. 나는 그 무법자와 혈연도 아니었고, 인정받을 만한 어떤 유대로 그와 얽혀 있지도 않았다. 그는 체포되기 전에 나를 위한 어떤 증서나 양도 문서에도 손을 대 두지 않았으며, 이제 와서 그렇게 하는 것도 부질없는 일이었다. 내게는 아무런 권리도 없었으니, 나는 마침내 결심했고, 그 후로도 줄곧 그 결심을 지켰다. 권리를 세워 보려는 가망 없는 일로 내 마음을 병들게 하지는 않으리라고.\n\n익사한 밀고자가 이 몰수에서 보상을 바랐으며, 매그위치의 재산 사정에 대해 어느 정도 정확한 정보를 얻어 두었으리라고 추측할 만한 근거가 있는 듯했다. 그의 시신이 죽은 자리에서 수 마일이나 떨어진 곳에서 발견되었을 때, 그리고 어찌나 끔찍하게 훼손되었던지 주머니에 든 물건으로만 신원을 알아볼 수 있을 정도였을 때, 그가 지니고 다니던 지갑 속에 접혀 있던 쪽지들은 여전히 읽을 수 있었다. 그 가운데에는 어느 정도의 돈이 예치된 뉴사우스웨일스의 한 은행 이름과, 상당한 가치가 있는 어떤 토지의 지명이 적혀 있었다. 이 두 항목의 정보는 매그위치가 옥중에서 재거스 씨에게 건넨, 내가 상속받으리라 여긴 재산의 목록에 들어 있던 것이었다. 그 가엾은 사람은 결국 자신의 무지 덕을 본 셈이었다. 그는 재거스 씨의 도움이 있으면 내 상속이 완전히 안전하리라는 것을 단 한 번도 의심하지 않았다.\n\n호송선에서 증인을 불러오느라 검찰 측 기소가 보류된 채 사흘이 지연된 뒤, 마침내 증인이 도착해 그 손쉬운 사건을 매듭지었다. 그는 다음 분기 형사 회기에서 재판을 받도록 회부되었고, 그 회기는 한 달 뒤에 열릴 예정이었다.\n\n내 인생의 그 어두운 시기에, 어느 날 저녁 허버트가 잔뜩 풀이 죽은 채 집으로 돌아와 말했다.\n\n\"이보게 헨델, 아무래도 머지않아 자네 곁을 떠나야 할 것 같네.\"\n\n그의 동업자가 이미 그 일에 대해 내게 언질을 주었던 터라, 나는 그가 생각한 것만큼 놀라지 않았다.\n\n\"카이로 가는 일을 미루면 좋은 기회를 놓치게 될 걸세. 그런데 자네가 나를 가장 필요로 하는 이때에, 아무래도 떠나야만 할 것 같아서 정말 두렵네, 헨델.\"\n\n\"허버트, 나는 자네를 언제까지나 필요로 할 걸세. 자네를 언제까지나 사랑할 테니까. 하지만 지금 내가 자네를 필요로 하는 정도가 다른 때보다 더한 것은 아니라네.\"\n\n\"자네가 너무 외로울 텐데.\"\n\n\"그런 걸 생각할 겨를이 없네.\" 내가 말했다. \"자네도 알다시피 나는 허락된 시간을 한껏 다 채워 늘 그분 곁에 있고, 할 수만 있다면 온종일 그분 곁에 있을 걸세. 그리고 그분에게서 떠나와도, 자네도 알다시피 내 생각은 그분과 함께 있다네.\"\n\n그가 처하게 된 그 끔찍한 처지는 우리 둘 모두에게 너무도 참담한 것이어서, 우리는 그것을 더 노골적인 말로 입에 올릴 수 없었다.\n\n\"이보게 친구.\" 허버트가 말했다. \"우리가 헤어질 날이 가까웠으니 — 정말로 무척 가까웠으니 — 그것을 핑계 삼아 자네 자신에 관해 자네를 좀 성가시게 해도 되겠지. 자네의 앞날을 생각해 본 적 있나?\"\n\n\"아니, 어떤 앞날이든 생각하기가 두려웠으니까.\"\n\n\"하지만 자네의 앞날은 그냥 묻어 둘 수 없네. 정말이지, 이보게 헨델, 묻어 두어서는 안 되네. 지금 나와 함께 몇 마디 다정한 말을 나누는 정도로라도, 그 문제에 들어서 보았으면 하네.\"\n\n\"그러지.\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"우리 그 지점에는 말일세, 헨델, 한 사람의 — 가 필요하다네.\"\n\n나는 그의 조심스러움이 정확한 단어를 피하고 있음을 알아채고서 말했다. \"사무원 말이지.\"\n\n\"사무원이지. 그리고 그가 (자네가 아는 어느 사무원이 그러했듯이) 동업자로 성장할 가능성이 결코 없지 않으리라 생각하네. 그러니 헨델 — 한마디로, 이보게, 자네 나에게로 오지 않겠나?\"\n\n\"그러니 헨델\"이라고, 마치 대단한 사업 연설의 엄숙한 서두라도 되는 양 말해 놓고는, 별안간 그 어조를 거두고 정직한 손을 내밀며 학생처럼 말하는 그의 태도에는, 매혹적일 만큼 진심 어리고 사람을 끄는 무언가가 있었다.\n\n\"클래라와 나는 그 얘기를 몇 번이고 거듭 나누었네.\" 허버트가 말을 이었다. \"그리고 그 사랑스러운 사람이 바로 오늘 저녁에도 눈물을 글썽이며 자네에게 이렇게 전해 달라고 내게 간청했네. 우리가 함께 살게 될 때 자네도 우리와 같이 산다면, 자네를 행복하게 해 주려고, 그리고 남편의 친구가 자기 친구이기도 하다는 것을 자기 자신에게 납득시키려고 최선을 다하겠다고 말일세. 우리 정말 잘 지낼 수 있을 걸세, 헨델!\"\n\n나는 그녀에게 진심으로 고마워했고, 그에게도 진심으로 고마워했다. 그러나 그가 그토록 친절히 제안해 주었음에도 아직은 그와 합류하리라 확언할 수 없다고 말했다. 첫째로, 내 마음이 너무 사로잡혀 있어 그 문제를 또렷이 받아들일 수가 없었다. 둘째로 — 그렇다! 둘째로, 이 짤막한 이야기의 끝자락에 가서야 드러날, 어떤 막연한 무언가가 내 생각 속에 어른거리고 있었다.\n\n\"하지만 허버트, 자네 사업에 아무 해를 끼치지 않으면서 이 문제를 잠시 동안 미결로 남겨 둘 수 있겠다고 여겨진다면 —\"\n\n\"얼마든지 그래도 되네.\" 허버트가 외쳤다. \"여섯 달이든, 일 년이든!\"\n\n\"그렇게까지 길게는 아닐세.\" 내가 말했다. \"기껏해야 두세 달이지.\"\n\n이 합의에 우리가 악수를 나누자 허버트는 몹시 기뻐하며, 이제 용기를 내어 말할 수 있겠다면서 아무래도 이번 주말에는 떠나야 할 것 같다고 했다.\n\n\"그럼 클래라는?\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"그 사랑스러운 사람은.\" 허버트가 대답했다. \"아버지가 살아 계시는 한 도리를 다해 곁을 지킬 거라네. 하지만 그분도 오래 못 버티실 걸세. 윔플 부인이 내게 귀띔하기를, 분명 가실 날이 멀지 않았다더군.\"\n\n\"매정한 말이 되겠네만.\" 내가 말했다. \"그분으로서는 가시는 것보다 나은 일이 없겠지.\"\n\n\"안타깝지만 그건 인정해야겠지.\" 허버트가 말했다. \"그러면 나는 그 사랑스러운 사람을 데리러 돌아올 걸세. 그리고 그 사랑스러운 사람과 나는 가장 가까운 교회로 조용히 걸어 들어가는 거지. 잊지 말게! 그 복받은 사람은 이렇다 할 가문 출신도 아니고, 이보게 헨델, 귀족 명부 따위 들여다본 적도 없으며, 자기 할아버지가 누구인지도 전혀 모른다네. 우리 어머니 아들에게 이 얼마나 큰 행운인가!\"\n* 귀족 명부: 영국 귀족·상류층의 가계를 기록한 명부.\n\n바로 그 주 토요일에 나는 허버트와 작별했다. 항구로 가는 우편 마차 좌석 하나에 앉은 그는 밝은 희망에 부풀어 있으면서도, 나를 두고 떠나는 것을 슬퍼하고 안타까워했다. 나는 어느 커피점에 들어가 클래라에게 짤막한 편지를 썼다. 그가 떠났다는 것과, 그녀에게 거듭거듭 사랑을 전한다는 말을 적었다. 그러고 나서 나는 외로운 내 집으로 — 그것을 집이라 부를 자격이 있다면 — 돌아갔다. 이제 그곳은 내게 더 이상 집이 아니었고, 나는 어디에도 집이라 할 곳이 없었으니까.\n\n계단에서 나는 웨믹과 마주쳤다. 그는 내 문을 두드려 보았으나 헛수고로 끝난 뒤 내려오는 길이었다. 탈주 시도가 처참하게 끝난 이래로 그를 단둘이 만난 것은 처음이었다. 그는 사사로운 개인 자격으로, 그 실패에 관해 몇 마디 해명을 하러 온 것이었다.\n\n\"고인이 된 컴피슨은 말입니다.\" 웨믹이 말했다. \"지금 처리되는 정규 업무의 절반쯤은 조금씩조금씩 그 속내를 캐내 두었더군요. 그리고 곤경에 빠진 그자의 패거리 몇몇이 (그자의 패거리 중에는 늘 곤경에 빠진 자가 있게 마련이지요) 떠드는 얘기를 통해 제가 그 정보를 들은 겁니다. 저는 귀를 닫은 척하면서 줄곧 귀를 열어 두었지요. 그러다 그자가 자리를 비웠다는 말을 듣고는, 그때가 결행하기에 가장 좋은 때라고 생각했던 겁니다. 이제 와 짐작건대, 매우 영리한 자였던 그자가 제 수족까지 늘 속여 두는 것이 그자의 술책의 일부였던 모양입니다. 저를 탓하지는 않으시겠지요, 핍 씨? 저는 진심을 다해 당신을 도우려 애썼노라 분명히 말씀드릴 수 있습니다.\"\n\n\"그 점은 웨믹 당신만큼이나 저도 확신합니다. 그리고 당신의 온갖 관심과 우정에 더없이 진심으로 감사드립니다.\"\n\n\"고맙습니다, 정말 고맙습니다. 안된 일이지요.\" 웨믹이 머리를 긁적이며 말했다. \"정말이지 이토록 속이 상해 본 게 오랜만입니다. 제가 안타까워하는 건 그 많은 동산이 날아가 버렸다는 겁니다. 저런, 저런!\"\n\n\"제가 마음 쓰는 건 말입니다, 웨믹, 그 재산의 가엾은 주인이지요.\"\n\n\"네, 그야 물론이지요.\" 웨믹이 말했다. \"당연히 당신이 그를 가엾게 여기는 데야 이의가 있을 수 없지요. 그를 거기서 빼내 줄 수만 있다면 저라도 5파운드짜리 지폐 한 장쯤은 내놓겠습니다. 하지만 제가 보는 건 이렇습니다. 고인이 된 컴피슨이 그의 귀환을 미리 알아채고 있었고, 또 그토록 단단히 그를 잡아넣겠다고 작정한 마당이었으니, 저는 그가 구제될 수 없었으리라고 봅니다. 반면에 동산은 분명히 구해 낼 수 있었지요. 그게 바로 재산과 주인의 차이입니다, 아시겠지요?\"\n\n나는 웨믹에게 위층으로 올라와 월워스로 걸어가기 전에 그로그 한 잔으로 몸을 풀라고 청했다. 그는 그 청을 받아들였다. 그가 적당한 양을 마시는 동안, 그는 다소 안절부절못하는 기색을 보이더니, 별다른 운도 떼지 않고 불쑥 말했다.\n\n\"월요일에 제가 휴가를 낼까 하는데, 핍 씨, 어떻게 생각하십니까?\"\n\n\"글쎄요, 지난 열두 달 동안 그런 일을 해 본 적이 없으시잖습니까.\"\n\n\"열두 해라 하는 게 맞겠지요.\" 웨믹이 말했다. \"네. 저는 휴가를 낼 작정입니다. 그뿐만이 아니라, 산책을 나갈 작정이지요. 게다가, 당신께 함께 산책 나가시자고 청할 작정입니다.\"\n\n나는 지금으로서는 영 변변찮은 길동무일 뿐이라며 사양하려던 참이었으나, 웨믹이 먼저 내 말을 막았다.\n\n\"당신의 사정은 압니다.\" 그가 말했다. \"그리고 당신이 마음이 편치 않으시다는 것도 알지요, 핍 씨. 하지만 만일 저를 위해 시간을 내 주실 수 있다면, 그것을 호의로 여기겠습니다. 긴 산책도 아니고, 이른 산책이지요. 이를테면 (도중에 아침 식사를 하는 것까지 쳐서) 여덟 시부터 열두 시까지면 될 겁니다. 한 번만 무리해서 시간을 내 주실 순 없겠습니까?\"\n\n그는 여러 차례 나를 위해 너무도 많은 일을 해 주었던 터라, 이쯤은 그를 위해 해 줄 일도 못 되었다. 나는 그럴 수 있다고, 그렇게 하겠다고 말했고, 그는 내 승낙에 어찌나 기뻐하던지 나도 덩달아 기뻤다. 그의 각별한 부탁에 따라, 나는 월요일 아침 여덟 시 반에 캐슬로 그를 데리러 가기로 약속했고, 그렇게 우리는 일단 헤어졌다.\n\n월요일 아침, 나는 약속 시간에 어김없이 캐슬 문 앞에서 종을 울렸고, 웨믹이 몸소 나를 맞았다. 그는 평소보다 한결 말쑥해 보였고, 더 윤기 나는 모자를 쓰고 있었다. 안에는 럼주를 탄 우유 두 잔과 비스킷 두 개가 준비되어 있었다. 노인장은 종달새와 함께 일어난 모양이었으니, 그의 침실 안쪽을 흘끗 들여다보니 침대가 비어 있는 것이 눈에 들어왔다.\n\n럼주를 탄 우유와 비스킷으로 든든히 배를 채우고, 그렇게 채비를 갖춘 채 산책을 나서려는데, 웨믹이 낚싯대를 집어 어깨에 둘러메는 것을 보고 나는 적잖이 놀랐다. \"아니, 우리 낚시하러 가는 게 아니잖습니까!\" 내가 말했다. \"아니지요.\" 웨믹이 대답했다. \"하지만 저는 낚싯대를 들고 걷는 걸 좋아합니다.\"\n\n나는 이것이 이상하다고 여겼으나, 아무 말도 하지 않고 우리는 길을 나섰다. 우리는 캠버웰 그린 쪽으로 갔고, 그 근방에 이르자 웨믹이 불쑥 말했다.\n\n\"어이쿠! 여기 교회가 있네요!\"\n\n거기에 별로 놀랄 만한 것은 없었다. 그러나 그가 마치 기막힌 생각이 떠올라 들뜬 사람처럼 이렇게 말했을 때, 나는 또 한 번 다소 놀랐다.\n\n\"들어가 봅시다!\"\n\n우리는 안으로 들어갔다. 웨믹은 낚싯대를 현관에 세워 두고는, 사방을 두루 둘러보았다. 그러는 사이 웨믹은 자기 외투 주머니를 뒤지더니, 거기서 종이에 싼 무언가를 꺼냈다.\n\n\"어이쿠!\" 그가 말했다. \"여기 장갑이 두 켤레 있네요! 끼워 봅시다!\"\n\n장갑이 흰 새끼 염소 가죽 장갑이었고, 게다가 그의 우체통 같은 입이 한껏 옆으로 벌어졌으므로, 나는 이제 강한 의심이 들기 시작했다. 그리고 노인장이 한 부인을 모시고 옆문으로 들어서는 것을 보자, 그 의심은 확신으로 굳어졌다.\n\n\"어이쿠!\" 웨믹이 말했다. \"스키핀스 양이 오셨네요! 결혼식이나 올립시다.\"\n\n그 신중한 처녀는 평소처럼 차려입고 있었는데, 다만 이제 자기 초록색 새끼 염소 가죽 장갑을 흰 것으로 바꿔 끼는 일에 골몰해 있다는 점만은 달랐다. 노인장도 마찬가지로, 히멘의 제단에 바칠 비슷한 제물을 준비하느라 여념이 없었다. 그러나 노신사는 장갑을 끼는 데 어찌나 애를 먹던지, 웨믹은 부득이 그를 기둥에 등을 대고 세운 다음, 자신은 기둥 뒤로 돌아가 장갑을 잡아당겨야 했고, 나는 나대로 노신사가 한결같이 안정된 저항을 보일 수 있도록 그의 허리를 끌어안고 있어야 했다. 이 기발한 방책 덕분에, 그의 장갑은 더할 나위 없이 완벽하게 끼워졌다.\n* 히멘: 그리스 신화의 결혼의 신.\n\n이윽고 서기와 목사가 나타나자, 우리는 그 운명의 난간 앞에 차례로 늘어섰다. 무엇이든 미리 준비하지 않고 그 자리에서 해치우는 것처럼 보이려는 그의 지론에 충실하게도, 예식이 시작되기 전 그가 조끼 주머니에서 무언가를 꺼내며 혼잣말로 이렇게 중얼거리는 것이 들렸다. \"어이쿠! 여기 반지가 있네!\"\n\n나는 신랑의 후원자, 즉 들러리 역할을 맡았다. 한편 갓난아기 모자 같은 부드러운 보닛을 쓴, 자그마하고 맥없어 보이는 좌석 안내인 여자가 스키핀스 양의 절친한 벗인 척하는 시늉을 했다. 신부를 인도하는 책임은 노인장에게 돌아갔는데, 이 때문에 목사가 본의 아니게 망신을 당하는 일이 벌어졌다. 사연인즉 이러했다. 목사가 \"누가 이 여자를 이 남자에게 시집보내려 데려왔습니까?\" 하고 물었을 때, 노신사는 예식이 어느 대목에 이르렀는지 전혀 모른 채, 더없이 흐뭇한 얼굴로 십계명 판을 향해 환히 미소 지으며 서 있었다. 이에 목사는 다시 \"누가 이 여자를 이 남자에게 시집보내려 데려왔습니까?\" 하고 물었다. 노신사는 여전히 더없이 갸륵한 무심 상태에 빠져 있었으므로, 신랑이 평소의 그 목소리로 외쳤다. \"자 노인장 피 씨, 아시잖아요. 누가 데려왔냐고요?\" 이에 노인장은 자기가 데려왔노라 말하기에 앞서 아주 활기차게 대답했다. \"좋아, 존, 잘했다, 얘야!\" 그러자 목사가 그 말에 어찌나 침울하게 말문이 막혀 멈추던지, 나는 순간 우리가 그날 결혼식을 온전히 마칠 수 있을까 하는 의구심마저 들었다.\n\n그러나 결혼식은 온전히 끝났고, 우리가 교회를 나설 때 웨믹은 세례반 뚜껑을 열어 그 안에 자기 흰 장갑을 넣고는 다시 뚜껑을 덮었다. 앞날을 더 헤아릴 줄 아는 웨믹 부인은 자기 흰 장갑을 주머니에 넣고 초록색 장갑을 끼었다. \"자, 핍 씨.\" 우리가 밖으로 나올 때 웨믹이 낚싯대를 의기양양하게 어깨에 둘러메며 말했다. \"누가 이걸 보고 결혼식 일행이라 짐작이나 하겠는지 한번 여쭤봅시다!\"\n\n아침 식사는 그린 너머 언덕 위로 일 마일쯤 떨어진, 아담하고 정겨운 선술집에 주문해 두었다. 그리고 그 방에는, 우리가 예식의 엄숙함을 떨치고 마음을 풀고 싶어질 경우를 대비해 바가텔 판이 놓여 있었다. 웨믹 부인이 웨믹의 팔이 자기 몸매에 맞춰 둘려도 더 이상 그것을 풀어내지 않고, 등받이가 높은 의자에 마치 케이스에 담긴 첼로처럼 벽에 기대 앉아, 그 가락 고운 악기가 그러하듯 얌전히 안기는 모습을 지켜보는 것은 흐뭇한 일이었다.\n\n우리는 더할 나위 없이 훌륭한 아침을 먹었고, 누군가가 식탁 위의 무언가를 사양할 때면 웨믹이 말했다. \"정해진 값에 다 받아 둔 거니까, 사양 말고 드세요!\" 나는 새 부부를 위해 잔을 들었고, 노인장을 위해 잔을 들었으며, 캐슬을 위해 잔을 들었고, 헤어질 때 신부에게 인사를 건넸으며, 할 수 있는 한 상냥하게 처신했다.\n\n웨믹이 나를 배웅하러 문간까지 내려왔고, 나는 다시 그와 악수하며 그의 행복을 빌었다.\n\n\"고맙습니다!\" 웨믹이 두 손을 비비며 말했다. \"그 사람이 닭을 어찌나 잘 치는지 당신은 모르실 겁니다. 달걀을 좀 보내 드릴 테니, 직접 판단해 보시지요. 그런데 핍 씨!\" 그가 나를 다시 불러 세우더니 목소리를 낮추어 말했다. \"이건 어디까지나 월워스의 정서이니, 부디 그리 알아주십시오.\"\n\n\"알겠습니다. 리틀 브리튼에서는 입에 올리지 말라는 말씀이지요.\" 내가 말했다.\n\n웨믹이 고개를 끄덕였다. \"요전 날 당신이 무심코 흘리신 일도 있으니, 재거스 씨는 이 일을 모르는 편이 낫겠습니다. 그분은 제 머리가 물러진다거나, 뭐 그런 식으로 여기실지도 모르니까요.\"\n\n# Chapter LVI.\n\n그는 재판에 회부된 뒤 개정기가 돌아올 때까지의 그 기간 내내 감옥에 누워 몹시 앓았다. 그는 갈비뼈가 두 대 부러졌고, 한쪽 폐가 다쳤으며, 큰 고통과 곤란 속에서 숨을 쉬었는데, 그 고통은 날로 더해 갔다. 부상의 여파로 그는 목소리가 어찌나 낮은지 거의 알아들을 수 없을 지경이었다. 그래서 그는 말을 아주 적게 했다. 그러나 그는 내 말을 들으려는 마음만큼은 언제나 준비되어 있었다. 그가 마땅히 들어야 한다고 내가 여기는 것을 그에게 말해 주고 읽어 주는 일이, 내 삶의 첫째 의무가 되었다.\n\n그는 일반 감방에 머무르기엔 너무도 위중했으므로, 하루 이틀이 지난 뒤 의무실로 옮겨졌다. 덕분에 나는 달리는 결코 가질 수 없었을, 그와 함께할 기회를 얻었다. 그리고 그의 병이 아니었다면 그는 쇠고랑이 채워졌을 것이다. 그는 작정하고 탈옥을 시도한 자로, 그 밖에 무엇으로 또 여겨졌는지 나로서는 알 수 없지만, 어쨌든 그렇게 간주되었기 때문이다.\n\n나는 그를 날마다 보았으나, 그저 짧은 시간 동안이었다. 그러므로 규칙적으로 되풀이되는 우리의 헤어짐의 간격은, 그의 신체 상태에 일어난 사소한 변화라도 그의 얼굴에 새겨 보여 줄 만큼 충분히 길었다. 나는 그 얼굴에서 단 한 번이라도 나은 쪽으로의 변화를 본 기억이 없다. 그는 감방 문이 그를 가둔 그날부터 날이 갈수록 야위어 갔고, 서서히 더 약해지고 더 나빠져 갔다.\n\n그가 보인 그런 종류의 순종 혹은 체념은, 기진맥진한 사람의 그것이었다. 나는 이따금 그의 태도에서, 또는 그의 입에서 새어 나온 한두 마디 속삭임에서, 그가 더 나은 환경에서라면 더 나은 사람이 되었을지를 곰곰이 헤아리고 있다는 인상을 받았다. 그러나 그는 그런 방향의 암시로 결코 자신을 변호하지 않았고, 영원히 굳어 버린 과거를 그 형태에서 비틀어 내려고도 하지 않았다.\n\n내가 있는 자리에서 두세 번, 그를 돌보던 사람들 가운데 누군가가 그의 무시무시한 평판을 넌지시 입에 올린 일이 있었다. 그러면 그의 얼굴에 미소가 스쳤고, 그는 마치 내가 그에게서 작으나마 그를 구원해 줄 어떤 면모를 보았다고, 그것도 내가 어린아이였던 그 까마득한 시절에 이미 보았다고 믿어 의심치 않는 듯, 신뢰에 찬 눈빛으로 나에게 눈을 돌리는 것이었다. 그 밖의 모든 것에 대해 그는 겸허하고 회개하는 마음이었고, 나는 그가 불평하는 것을 한 번도 본 적이 없다.\n\n개정기가 돌아오자, 재거스 씨는 그의 재판을 다음 개정기까지 연기해 달라는 신청을 제출하게 했다. 그것은 분명 그가 그렇게 오래 살지 못하리라는 확신에서 낸 신청이었으나, 기각되었다. 재판은 즉시 시작되었고, 그가 피고석에 세워졌을 때, 그는 의자에 앉혀졌다. 내가 피고석 바깥쪽으로 바싹 다가가, 그가 나에게 내미는 손을 잡는 일에는 아무런 이의도 제기되지 않았다.\n\n재판은 아주 짧고 아주 명료했다. 그를 위해 할 수 있는 말은 다 나왔다. 그가 어떻게 근면한 습관을 들였고, 합법적이고 떳떳하게 번창했는지에 대해서. 그러나 그가 돌아왔다는 사실, 그리고 지금 판사와 배심원 앞에 와 있다는 사실은 그 무엇으로도 없던 일로 만들 수 없었다. 그 일로 그를 재판하면서 유죄가 아닌 다른 결론을 내리는 것은 불가능했다.\n\n당시에는(나는 그 개정기의 끔찍한 경험을 통해 알게 되었거니와) 마지막 하루를 선고에 바치고, 사형 선고로써 절정의 효과를 내는 것이 관례였다. 내 기억이 지금 내 앞에 들이미는 이 지워지지 않는 그림이 아니라면, 나는 이 글을 쓰는 지금조차도, 서른두 명의 남녀가 판사 앞에 세워져 그 선고를 함께 받는 것을 내가 보았다는 사실을 도무지 믿기 어려울 것이다. 그 서른두 명 가운데 맨 앞에 그가 있었다. 목숨을 부지할 만큼의 숨을 들이쉬기 위해 그는 앉아 있었다.\n\n그 장면 전체가 다시금 그 순간의 생생한 색채로 떠오른다. 법정 창문에 맺힌 사월의 빗방울이 사월의 햇살을 받아 반짝이던 것까지도. 내가 다시 피고석 모퉁이 바깥에 서서 그의 손을 내 손에 쥐고 있는 동안, 그 우리 안에 갇힌 것은 서른두 명의 남녀였다. 더러는 반항적이었고, 더러는 공포에 사로잡혀 있었으며, 더러는 흐느끼며 울고 있었고, 더러는 얼굴을 가리고 있었으며, 더러는 침울하게 사방을 노려보고 있었다. 여죄수들 사이에서 비명이 터져 나왔으나, 이내 가라앉았고, 정적이 뒤따랐다. 거창한 사슬과 꽃다발을 두른 보안관들, 그 밖의 갖가지 시정의 장식물과 괴물 같은 물건들, 정리들, 안내인들, 사람으로 가득 찬 거대한 방청석 — 연극을 보러 온 듯한 수많은 관중 — 이 모두가, 서른두 명과 판사가 엄숙하게 마주 서는 것을 지켜보고 있었다. 이윽고 판사가 그들에게 말을 건넸다. 그가 특별히 따로 골라 말을 건네야 할, 그의 앞에 선 비참한 자들 가운데 하나는, 거의 갓난아기 시절부터 법을 어겨 온 자였다. 거듭된 투옥과 형벌 끝에 마침내 몇 년간의 유형을 선고받았고, 엄청난 폭력과 대담함을 동원하여 탈출했다가 다시 종신 유형을 선고받은 자였다. 그 가련한 사내는, 옛 죄를 저지른 무대에서 멀리 떨어져 있는 동안에는 한때 자신의 과오를 깨달은 듯, 평화롭고 정직한 삶을 산 듯도 보였다. 그러나 어느 운명적인 순간, 그토록 오랫동안 그를 사회의 재앙으로 만들어 온 그 성향과 정념에 굴복하여, 그는 안식과 회개의 안식처를 떠나, 자신에게 추방령이 내려진 나라로 되돌아왔다. 이곳에서 이내 고발당한 그는, 한동안 사법 당국의 추적을 따돌리는 데 성공했으나, 마침내 도주하던 중에 붙잡혔을 때 그들에게 저항하였고, 그리고 — 명백한 의도에서였는지, 아니면 무모함의 맹목 속에서였는지는 그 자신이 가장 잘 알겠지만 — 자신의 모든 행적을 알고 있던 고발자를 죽음에 이르게 하였다. 자신을 내쫓은 땅으로 되돌아온 데 대해 정해진 형벌이 사형이며, 그의 경우가 이처럼 가중된 사례이므로, 그는 죽을 각오를 해야 한다는 것이었다.\n\n햇빛이 유리에 맺힌 반짝이는 빗방울을 뚫고 법정의 커다란 창문으로 비쳐 들었고, 그것은 서른두 명과 판사 사이에 한 줄기 넓은 빛의 기둥을 만들어 양쪽을 하나로 잇고 있었다. 어쩌면 그것은 방청객 가운데 몇몇에게, 양쪽 모두가 절대적인 평등 속에서, 모든 것을 알며 결코 그르치지 않는 더 큰 심판을 향해 나아가고 있음을 일깨워 주었는지도 모른다. 이 빛줄기 속에서 또렷한 얼굴 한 점으로, 잠시 몸을 일으키며, 그 죄수는 이렇게 말했다. \"재판장님, 저는 전능하신 분으로부터 사형 선고를 받았습니다만, 재판장님의 선고에도 머리 숙입니다.\" 그러고는 다시 앉았다. 잠시 조용히 하라는 소리가 일었고, 판사는 나머지 사람들에게 할 말을 이어 갔다. 그리하여 그들은 모두 정식으로 운명이 결정되었고, 더러는 부축을 받아 끌려 나갔으며, 더러는 초췌하면서도 용감한 척하는 얼굴로 어슬렁어슬렁 걸어 나갔고, 몇몇은 방청석을 향해 고개를 끄덕였으며, 두세 명은 악수를 나누었고, 또 더러는 여기저기 놓인 향초 더미에서 집어 든 풀잎 조각을 씹으며 나갔다. 그는 맨 마지막에 나갔는데, 의자에서 부축을 받아야 했고, 또 아주 천천히 걸어야 했기 때문이다. 다른 사람들이 모두 끌려 나가는 동안, 그리고 방청객들이 (교회에서나 다른 데서나 그러듯 옷매무새를 바로잡으며) 일어나, 이 죄인 저 죄인을 손가락질하고, 무엇보다도 그와 나를 손가락질하는 동안에도, 그는 내 손을 잡고 있었다.\n\n나는 그가 형 집행 권고 보고서가 작성되기 전에 죽기를 간절히 바라고 기도했다. 그러나 그가 질질 끌며 살아남을까 두려워, 나는 그날 밤 내무 장관에게 보낼 청원서를 쓰기 시작했다. 그에 대해 내가 아는 바와, 그가 나를 위해 어떻게 돌아오게 되었는지를 적었다. 나는 할 수 있는 한 열렬하고 애절하게 그것을 썼다. 그리고 다 써서 제출한 뒤에는, 가장 자비로우리라고 내가 기대하는 권력자들에게 보낼 또 다른 청원서들을 썼고, 왕실 자체에 올릴 청원서까지 한 통 작성했다. 그가 선고를 받은 뒤로 며칠 밤낮 동안 나는 의자에서 잠들 때를 빼고는 쉬지 않았으며, 오로지 이 호소에만 온전히 몰두했다. 그리고 그것들을 제출한 뒤에는, 그것들이 놓여 있는 장소에서 도무지 멀리 떨어져 있을 수가 없었고, 내가 그 가까이에 있을 때면 그것들이 더 희망차고 덜 절망적으로 느껴지는 듯했다. 이 까닭 모를 안절부절못함과 마음의 고통 속에서, 나는 저녁이면 거리를 헤매며, 청원서를 두고 온 그 관청과 저택들 곁을 배회하곤 했다. 지금 이 순간까지도, 춥고 먼지 자욱한 봄밤의 런던 서쪽의 그 황량한 거리들은, 줄지어 늘어선 근엄하게 닫힌 저택들과 길게 이어진 가로등의 행렬과 더불어, 이 기억의 연상으로 인해 내게 우수에 찬 곳으로 남아 있다.\n\n내가 그를 찾아갈 수 있는 매일의 면회는 이제 더 짧아졌고, 그에 대한 감시는 더욱 엄해졌다. 내가 그에게 독약을 가져다주려는 의도를 의심받고 있다는 것을 알아차렸거나, 혹은 그렇게 짐작한 나는, 그의 침상 곁에 앉기 전에 몸수색을 해 달라고 청했고, 늘 그곳에 있던 관리에게, 내 의도가 오직 하나뿐임을 그가 확신할 수 있도록 무엇이든 기꺼이 하겠다고 말했다. 그에게도 나에게도 모질게 구는 사람은 없었다. 해야 할 의무가 있었고, 그것은 수행되었으나, 가혹하지는 않았다. 관리는 늘 그가 더 나빠졌다고 일러 주었고, 그 방에 있던 다른 병든 죄수들과, 그들을 병자 간병인으로서 돌보던 다른 죄수들(죄인들이긴 했으나, 친절을 베풀 줄 모르는 자들은 아니었으니, 하느님께 감사할 일이다!)도 늘 같은 보고에 입을 모았다.\n\n날이 갈수록 나는, 그가 흰 천장을 평온하게 바라보며 누워 있는 것을 점점 더 자주 보게 되었는데, 그의 얼굴에는 빛이 없다가, 내 어떤 말이 한순간 그것을 환하게 밝히면, 그 빛은 이내 다시 잦아들곤 했다. 때로 그는 거의, 혹은 전혀 말을 할 수 없었고, 그러면 내 손을 가볍게 눌러 대답했는데, 나는 그의 뜻을 아주 잘 헤아리게 되었다.\n\n날수가 열흘에 이르렀을 때, 나는 지금까지 본 것보다 더 큰 변화를 그에게서 보았다. 그의 두 눈은 문 쪽을 향해 있었고, 내가 들어서자 환해졌다.\n\n\"얘야,\" 내가 그의 침상 곁에 앉자 그가 말했다. \"네가 늦은 줄 알았다. 하지만 네가 그럴 리 없다는 것도 알았지.\"\n\n\"꼭 시간에 맞춰 왔어요.\" 내가 말했다. \"문 앞에서 기다리고 있었거든요.\"\n\n\"넌 늘 문 앞에서 기다리는구나. 그렇지, 얘야?\"\n\n\"네. 한순간도 놓치지 않으려고요.\"\n\n\"고맙다, 얘야, 고마워. 하느님의 축복이 있기를! 넌 한 번도 나를 저버리지 않았어, 얘야.\"\n\n나는 말없이 그의 손을 꼭 쥐었다. 한때 내가 그를 저버리려 했던 일을 잊을 수가 없었기 때문이다.\n\n\"그리고 무엇보다 좋은 건 말이다,\" 그가 말했다. \"내가 어두운 먹구름 아래 놓인 뒤로, 너는 햇빛이 비치던 때보다 나와 함께 있는 걸 더 편하게 여겼다는 거야. 그게 무엇보다 좋은 일이지.\"\n\n그는 등을 대고 누운 채 몹시 힘겹게 숨을 쉬고 있었다. 무슨 짓을 하든, 또 나를 그토록 사랑하든, 그의 얼굴에서는 이따금 빛이 사라졌고, 흰 천장을 향한 그 평온한 눈빛 위로 막이 끼었다.\n\n\"오늘은 많이 아프세요?\"\n\n\"아무것도 불평할 게 없다, 얘야.\"\n\n\"한 번도 불평하시는 법이 없네요.\"\n\n그것이 그가 말한 마지막 말이었다. 그는 미소를 지었고, 나는 그가 내 손을 들어 올려 자기 가슴에 얹어 주기를 바란다는 뜻으로 그의 손길을 알아들었다. 나는 손을 거기에 얹었고, 그는 다시 미소를 지으며 두 손을 그 위에 포갰다.\n\n우리가 그렇게 있는 동안 허락된 면회 시간이 다 되었다. 그러나 둘러보니, 감옥의 소장이 내 곁에 서 있었고, 그가 속삭였다. \"아직 가지 않아도 됩니다.\" 나는 고마운 마음으로 그에게 감사를 표하고는 물었다. \"그가 제 말을 들을 수 있다면, 그에게 말을 해도 되겠습니까?\"\n\n소장은 옆으로 비켜서며 관리에게 물러가라고 손짓했다. 그 변화는 소리 없이 이루어졌으나, 흰 천장을 향한 그 평온한 눈빛에서 막을 걷어 냈고, 그는 더없이 다정하게 나를 바라보았다.\n\n\"매그위치 아저씨, 이제 마침내 말씀드려야겠어요. 제 말을 알아들으시겠어요?\"\n\n내 손을 가볍게 누르는 손길.\n\n\"아저씨에겐 한때 사랑했다가 잃은 아이가 있었지요.\"\n\n내 손을 더 힘주어 누르는 손길.\n\n\"그 아이는 살아 있었고, 힘 있는 후견인들을 만났어요. 지금도 살아 있고요. 그 아이는 숙녀가 되었고, 무척 아름다워요. 그리고 저는 그녀를 사랑해요!\"\n\n마지막 남은 희미한 힘으로 — 내가 그것에 응해 거들지 않았다면 아무 소용없었을 그 힘으로 — 그는 내 손을 자기 입술로 들어 올렸다. 그러고는 자기 두 손을 그 위에 얹은 채, 다시 부드럽게 그것을 자기 가슴 위에 내려놓았다. 흰 천장을 향한 그 평온한 눈빛이 되돌아왔다가, 사라져 갔고, 그의 머리가 조용히 가슴 위로 떨어졌다.\n\n그때, 우리가 함께 읽었던 것을 떠올리며, 나는 기도하러 성전에 올라갔던 두 사람을 생각했다. 그리고 나는 그의 침상 곁에서 할 수 있는 말 가운데, \"오 주여, 죄인인 그에게 자비를 베푸소서!\"보다 더 나은 말은 없음을 알았다.\n\n# Chapter LVII.\n\n이제 완전히 홀로 남겨진 나는, 법적으로 임차 기간이 끝나는 대로 템플의 거처를 비우겠다는 뜻을 알리고, 그동안에는 그곳을 다시 세놓기로 했다. 곧바로 창문에 임대 광고지를 내붙였다. 빚이 있었고 가진 돈이 거의 없어서, 내 살림 형편에 심각한 두려움을 느끼기 시작했기 때문이다. 차라리 이렇게 적어야 옳겠다. 내가 몹시 앓아 가고 있다는 사실 너머로 그 어떤 진실이든 또렷이 헤아릴 만한 기력과 집중력이 내게 있었더라면 두려움을 느꼈을 거라고. 근래의 긴장은 내가 병을 미뤄 두게는 해 주었으나, 떨쳐 내게 해 주지는 못했다. 나는 이제 그 병이 내게 닥쳐오고 있다는 것을 알았고, 그 외에는 거의 아무것도 알지 못했으며, 심지어 그것조차도 개의치 않았다.\n\n하루 이틀 동안, 나는 소파 위에, 또는 바닥에 — 그러니까 마침 쓰러진 데가 어디든 그곳에 — 무거운 머리와 쑤시는 사지를 늘어뜨린 채, 아무런 목적도 없고 아무런 힘도 없이 누워 있었다. 그러던 어느 밤, 한없이 길게 느껴지고 불안과 공포로 가득한 밤이 찾아왔다. 그리고 아침이 되어 침대에서 몸을 일으켜 그 일을 곱씹어 보려 했을 때, 나는 그럴 수 없음을 깨달았다.\n\n내가 정말로 한밤중에 가든 코트로 내려가, 거기 있으리라고 여긴 보트를 더듬어 찾았던 것인지, 내가 어떻게 침대에서 빠져나왔는지도 모른 채 두세 번이나 큰 공포에 휩싸여 계단에서 제정신을 차렸던 것인지, 그가 계단을 올라오고 있으며 등불이 다 꺼졌다는 생각에 사로잡혀 등불을 켜고 있는 나 자신을 발견했던 것인지, 누군가가 정신없이 떠들고 웃고 신음하는 소리에 형언할 수 없을 만큼 시달렸으며 그 소리가 실은 내가 내는 소리가 아닐까 반쯤 의심했던 것인지, 방 어두운 구석에 닫힌 쇠 화로가 하나 있어 어떤 목소리가 미스 해비셤이 그 안에서 타고 있다고 거듭거듭 외쳐 댔던 것인지 — 이런 것들이, 그 아침 침대에 누운 채 내가 스스로 정리하여 어떤 질서를 잡아 보려 애쓰던 일들이었다. 그러나 석회 가마의 수증기가 나와 그것들 사이로 끼어들어 모조리 흐트러뜨리곤 했고, 마침내 그 수증기 너머로 나는 두 사내가 나를 바라보고 있는 것을 보았다.\n\n\"무슨 일이오?\" 나는 흠칫 놀라 물었다. \"당신들을 모르는데.\"\n\n\"저, 선생님.\" 그중 하나가 몸을 굽혀 내 어깨를 짚으며 대답했다. \"이건 곧 정리하실 수 있는 일일 겁니다, 아마도. 하지만 선생님은 체포되셨습니다.\"\n\n\"빚이 얼마요?\"\n\n\"백이십삼 파운드 십오 실링 육 펜스입니다. 제 생각엔 보석상 외상값인 것 같군요.\"\n\n\"어떻게 해야 하오?\"\n\n\"제 집으로 가시는 게 좋겠습니다.\" 사내가 말했다. \"아주 괜찮은 집을 운영하고 있지요.\"\n\n나는 일어나 옷을 입어 보려고 얼마간 애썼다. 그러나 다음번에 그들에게 주의를 돌렸을 때, 그들은 침대에서 조금 떨어져 서서 나를 바라보고 있었다. 나는 여전히 그곳에 누워 있었다.\n\n\"내 상태를 보시오.\" 내가 말했다. \"갈 수 있다면 당신들과 가겠소만, 정말로 도저히 갈 수가 없소. 여기서 나를 데려간다면, 나는 가는 길에 죽고 말 거요.\"\n\n아마도 그들은 대답을 했거나, 그 점을 두고 따졌거나, 내가 생각하는 것보다 상태가 낫다고 믿도록 나를 격려하려 했을 것이다. 내 기억 속에 오직 이 한 가닥 가느다란 실로만 매달려 있는 까닭에, 그들이 나를 옮기기를 보류했다는 것 말고는 무엇을 했는지 나는 알지 못한다.\n\n내가 열병에 걸려 사람들이 나를 피했다는 것, 내가 몹시 고통받았다는 것, 내가 자주 이성을 잃었다는 것, 시간이 끝없이 느껴졌다는 것, 내가 있을 수 없는 존재들과 내 정체성을 뒤섞었다는 것, 내가 집의 벽에 박힌 벽돌이 되어 건축공들이 나를 박아 놓은 그 아찔한 자리에서 빼내 달라고 애원했다는 것, 내가 거대한 기관의 강철 들보가 되어 심연 위에서 부딪치며 빙빙 돌면서도 내 자신의 몸으로 그 기관을 멈추고 거기 박힌 내 부분을 두드려 떼어 달라고 간청했다는 것, 내가 병의 이런 국면들을 거쳤다는 것을, 나는 내 기억으로 알고 있으며, 당시에도 어느 정도는 알고 있었다. 내가 때때로 진짜 사람들을 살인자라 믿고서 그들과 몸싸움을 벌이다가, 별안간 그들이 내게 좋은 일을 하려 한다는 것을 깨닫고는 지쳐 그들의 품에 쓰러져 그들이 나를 눕히도록 내맡겼다는 것 또한 당시에도 알고 있었다. 그러나 무엇보다도 나는, 이 모든 사람들에게 — 내가 몹시 앓을 때면 온갖 기이한 모습으로 인간의 얼굴을 변형시키고 몸집이 한껏 부풀어 오르던 이 사람들에게 — 무엇보다도, 다시 말하건대 이 모든 사람들에게, 머잖아 조의 모습으로 가라앉아 정착하려는 기이한 경향이 있다는 것을 알고 있었다.\n\n병의 고비를 넘긴 뒤, 나는 병의 다른 온갖 양상은 변하면서도 이 한 가지 일관된 양상만은 변하지 않는다는 것을 알아채기 시작했다. 누가 내 곁에 오든 여전히 조의 모습으로 가라앉아 정착했다. 밤에 눈을 뜨면, 침대 곁 큰 안락의자에 앉은 조가 보였다. 낮에 눈을 뜨면, 차양을 드리운 열린 창가 자리에 앉아 파이프를 피우는 조가 여전히 보였다. 시원한 마실 것을 청하면, 그것을 건네주는 그 정다운 손이 조의 손이었다. 마시고 나서 다시 베개에 몸을 누이면, 그토록 희망에 차고 다정하게 나를 굽어보는 얼굴이 조의 얼굴이었다.\n\n마침내 어느 날, 나는 용기를 내어 말했다. \"이게 정말 조예요?\"\n\n그러자 그 정다운 옛 고향의 목소리가 대답했다. \"그래 나다, 우리 친구야.\"\n\n\"아, 조, 가슴이 미어져요! 나한테 화를 내요, 조. 나를 때려요, 조. 내 배은망덕을 꾸짖어요. 나한테 그렇게 잘해 주지 말아요!\"\n\n내가 자기를 알아본 기쁨에, 조는 정말로 내 곁 베개에 머리를 누이고 한 팔로 내 목을 감싸 안았던 것이다.\n\n\"우리 정다운 핍, 친구야.\" 조가 말했다. \"너랑 나랑은 언제나 친구였잖니. 그리고 네가 마차 타고 바깥바람 쐴 만큼 나으면 — 얼마나 신날까!\"\n\n그러고 나서 조는 창가로 물러나, 내게 등을 돌린 채 서서 눈을 닦았다. 그리고 극심한 쇠약 탓에 나는 일어나 그에게 다가갈 수가 없었으므로, 그저 거기 누워 참회하듯 속삭였다. \"오, 신이여 그를 축복하소서! 오, 신이여 이 온화한 기독교인을 축복하소서!\"\n\n다음번에 내 곁에서 조를 보았을 때 그의 눈은 붉어져 있었다. 그러나 나는 그의 손을 잡고 있었고, 우리 둘 다 행복했다.\n\n\"얼마나 됐어요, 조?\"\n\n\"네 말은, 핍, 네 병이 얼마나 오래갔느냐는 거지, 우리 친구야?\"\n\n\"네, 조.\"\n\n\"오월 말이다, 핍. 내일이 유월 초하루지.\"\n\n\"그럼 그 내내 여기 있었던 거예요, 조?\"\n\n\"거의 그랬지, 친구야. 네가 아프다는 소식이 편지로 전해졌을 때, 그러니까 우편으로 왔을 때 비디한테 내가 말했지, 그 사람이 전엔 홀몸이었는데 이젠 결혼을 했지만 많이 걷고 구두 가죽 닳리는 거에 비하면 박봉이긴 해도, 재산은 그 사람 쪽에서 따질 게 못 됐고, 결혼이야말로 그 사람 가슴속의 큰 소원이었으니—\"\n\n\"당신 말을 듣는 게 너무 즐거워요, 조! 그런데 비디한테 무슨 말을 했는지 도중에 끊었네요.\"\n\n\"그러니까 말이지.\" 조가 말했다. \"네가 낯선 사람들 틈에 있을지도 모르겠고, 너랑 나랑은 언제나 친구였으니, 이런 때에 한 번 들여다보는 게 영 달갑잖은 일은 아닐 거라고 했지. 그랬더니 비디 말이, '한시도 지체 말고 그에게 가세요.' 그게 말이다.\" 조가 판관 같은 태도로 매듭지으며 말했다. \"비디의 말이었어. '그에게 가세요.' 비디가 그러더라, '한시도 지체 말고.' 한마디로.\" 조가 잠시 진지하게 곱씹은 뒤 덧붙였다. \"그 젊은 여자의 말이 '일 분도 지체 말고'였다고 네게 전한다 해도, 나는 너를 크게 속이는 게 아닐 거다.\"\n\n거기서 조는 말을 뚝 끊고, 내게 아주 적당히만 말을 시켜야 한다고, 그리고 내가 내키든 내키지 않든 정해진 시간마다 자주 영양분을 조금씩 섭취해야 한다고, 그리고 내가 그의 모든 지시에 따라야 한다고 일러 주었다. 그래서 나는 그의 손에 입을 맞추고 조용히 누워 있었고, 그동안 그는 내 사랑을 담아 비디에게 보내는 짧은 편지를 쓰기 시작했다.\n\n비디가 조에게 글쓰기를 가르친 게 분명했다. 침대에 누워 그를 바라보고 있자니, 그가 편지에 착수하는 모습에 깃든 그 자부심을 보고서 쇠약해진 나는 기쁨에 또다시 눈물이 났다. 커튼을 떼어 낸 내 침대는, 나를 그 위에 누인 채로, 가장 바람이 잘 통하고 가장 넓은 거실로 옮겨져 있었으며, 양탄자는 걷어 냈고, 방은 밤낮으로 늘 신선하고 청결하게 유지되었다. 한쪽 구석으로 밀어 두어 작은 약병들이 어수선하게 놓인 내 책상에, 조는 이제 그 위대한 작업을 위해 앉았다. 먼저 펜꽂이에서 펜을 하나 고르는데 마치 커다란 연장이 든 상자에서 고르는 듯했고, 마치 쇠지레나 큰 망치를 휘두를 것처럼 소매를 걷어붙였다. 조는 시작하기에 앞서 왼쪽 팔꿈치로 책상을 묵직하게 짚고 오른쪽 다리를 뒤로 한껏 빼야만 했다. 그리고 막상 시작하자, 내려긋는 획마다 어찌나 천천히 그었던지 그 길이가 육 피트는 됨 직했고, 올려긋는 획마다 그의 펜이 사방으로 잉크를 튀기며 긁어 대는 소리가 들렸다. 그는 잉크병이 실제 놓인 쪽이 아닌 반대쪽에 있다는 묘한 생각을 품고서, 끊임없이 펜을 허공에 적셔 댔고, 그 결과에 자못 만족한 듯 보였다. 이따금 어떤 철자상의 걸림돌에 발이 걸리기도 했으나, 전체적으로는 정말이지 아주 잘해 나갔다. 그리고 자기 이름을 서명하고, 마무리로 종이에 묻은 얼룩 한 점을 두 검지로 떠서 자기 정수리에 옮겨 붙인 뒤, 그는 일어나 책상 주위를 맴돌며, 거기 놓인 자기 작품의 효과를 이런저런 각도에서 한없는 만족감으로 가늠해 보았다.\n\n말을 너무 많이 해서 조를 불편하게 하지 않으려고 — 설령 내가 많이 말할 수 있었다 하더라도 — 나는 미스 해비셤에 대해 묻는 일을 다음 날로 미뤘다. 그래서 그녀가 회복되었는지 물었을 때, 조는 고개를 가로저었다.\n\n\"돌아가셨어요, 조?\"\n\n\"저, 그게 말이다, 친구야.\" 조가 만류하는 듯한 어조로, 차근차근 본론에 다가갈 양으로 말했다. \"거기까지 말하진 않으련다, 그건 너무 큰 말이니까. 하지만 그분은—\"\n\"살아 계시지 않다는 거죠, 조?\"\n\n\"그게 더 가까운 말이지.\" 조가 말했다. \"그분은 살아 계시지 않아.\"\n\n\"오래 버티셨어요, 조?\"\n\n\"네가 앓아눕고 나서, 굳이 따지자면 한 일주일쯤이라 할 만했지.\" 조가 말했다. 여전히 나를 위해 모든 것에 차근차근 다가가기로 작정한 듯했다.\n\n\"정다운 조, 그분 재산이 어떻게 됐는지 들었어요?\"\n\n\"저, 친구야.\" 조가 말했다. \"그분이 그 대부분을 미스 에스텔라 앞으로 정리해 두신 것 같더라, 그러니까 묶어 두셨다는 말이지. 그런데 사고 나기 하루 이틀 전에 직접 손으로 작은 추가 유언장을 써 두셔서, 매슈 포켓 씨한테 사천 파운드를 거뜬히 남기셨다는구나. 그런데 핍, 무엇보다도 그분이 왜 그 거뜬한 사천 파운드를 그에게 남기셨는지 짐작이 가니? '핍이 그에 대해, 그러니까 그 매슈에 대해 한 이야기 덕분에.' 비디한테 들었는데, 그게 그 글귀였다더라.\" 조가 그 법률적 어투를 한없이 흐뭇해하며 되풀이했다. \"'그에 대해, 그러니까 그 매슈에 대한 이야기.' 거기다 거뜬한 사천 파운드라니, 핍!\"\n\n조가 그 사천 파운드에 으레 붙이던 '거뜬한'이라는 표현을 누구에게서 따왔는지 나는 끝내 알아내지 못했다. 그러나 그것이 그에게는 그 금액을 더욱 대단하게 만들어 주는 듯했고, 그는 그것이 거뜬하다고 우기는 데서 역력한 즐거움을 느꼈다.\n\n이 이야기는 내게 큰 기쁨을 주었으니, 내가 한 유일한 선한 일을 완성해 준 셈이었기 때문이다. 나는 조에게 다른 친척들 가운데 유산을 받은 이가 있는지 들었느냐고 물었다.\n\n\"미스 새러 말이다.\" 조가 말했다. \"담즙 과다라서 알약 사 먹으라고 매년 이십오 파운드를 받는다는구나. 미스 조지애나는 이십 파운드를 일시불로 받고. 그리고 어, 혹 달린 그 야수들 이름이 뭐였더라, 친구야?\"\n\n\"낙타요?\" 나는 그가 그걸 왜 알고 싶어 하는지 의아해하며 말했다.\n\n조는 고개를 끄덕였다. \"낙타 부인 말이다.\" 잠시 후 나는 그가 커밀라를 두고 하는 말임을 알아들었다. \"그분은 밤중에 잠 깰 때 기운 차리게 골풀 양초 사 두라고 오 파운드를 받는다는구나.\"\n\n이 이야기들의 정확성은 내게 충분히 명백했으므로, 조의 정보에 큰 신뢰가 갔다. \"그리고 이제.\" 조가 말했다. \"넌 아직 그렇게 튼튼하진 않아서, 오늘 한 삽 이상 더 떠넣을 형편은 못 된다, 친구야. 늙은 올릭이 어느 집을 부수고 들어갔단다.\"\n\n\"누구 집을요?\" 내가 물었다.\n\n\"하기야 그자 행실이 떠들썩하게 굴기 일쑤인 건 인정하다만.\" 조가 변명하듯 말했다. \"그래도 영국인의 집은 그 사람의 성채이고, 성채는 전시가 아니면 부숴서는 안 되는 법이지. 그리고 그 사람 쪽의 결점이 무엇이든 간에, 그는 가슴속으로는 곡물상이자 종묘상이었어.\"\n\n\"그럼 펌블추크의 집이 털린 거예요?\"\n\n\"바로 그거다, 핍.\" 조가 말했다. \"놈들이 그의 돈궤를 가져갔고, 금고를 가져갔고, 그의 포도주를 마셨고, 그의 음식을 나눠 먹었고, 그의 뺨을 갈겼고, 그의 코를 잡아당겼고, 그를 침대 기둥에 묶었고, 매를 열두 대 안겼고, 소리치지 못하게 그의 입에 일년생 꽃을 잔뜩 쑤셔 넣었지. 하지만 그가 올릭을 알아봤고, 올릭은 지금 군 감옥에 있다.\"\n\n이런 식으로 다가가다 보니 우리는 거리낌 없는 대화에 이르렀다. 나는 더디게 기력을 회복했으나, 더디지만 분명하게 점점 덜 쇠약해졌고, 조는 내 곁에 머물렀으며, 나는 내가 다시 어린 핍이 된 듯한 기분이 들었다.\n\n조의 다정함이 내 필요에 어찌나 아름답게 들어맞았던지, 나는 그의 손안에서 어린아이 같았다. 그는 앉아서 옛 그대로의 격의 없음으로, 옛 그대로의 소박함으로, 옛 그대로의 나서지 않으면서 감싸 주는 태도로 내게 말을 건넸고, 그래서 나는 옛 부엌 시절 이후의 내 모든 삶이 이제는 지나가 버린 그 열병이 빚어낸 정신적 착란의 하나였던 게 아닐까 반쯤 믿을 지경이었다. 그는 집안일만 빼고 나를 위해 모든 것을 해 주었는데, 그 집안일을 위해 그는 처음 도착했을 때 세탁부를 내보낸 뒤 아주 점잖은 여자를 한 사람 고용해 두었다. \"정말이지 단언하건대, 핍.\" 그는 그 멋대로 한 일을 해명하느라 곧잘 이렇게 말하곤 했다. \"그 여편네가 손님용 침대를 맥주통처럼 콕콕 찔러서, 깃털을 양동이에 뽑아내 팔아먹고 있더라니까. 다음번엔 네가 쓰는 침대도 찔러서, 네가 그 위에 누워 있는 채로 뽑아낼 판이었고, 그러고는 석탄을 수프 그릇이랑 야채 접시에 담아 조금씩 빼돌리고, 포도주랑 독한 술은 네 웰링턴 부츠에 담아 날라 가고 있었지.\"\n\n우리는 내가 마차를 타고 바깥바람을 쐬러 나갈 날을, 한때 내 도제 입문 날을 손꼽아 기다렸던 것처럼 손꼽아 기다렸다. 그리고 그날이 와서 골목에 포장 없는 마차가 대기되자, 조는 나를 둘둘 싸매고 두 팔로 안아 그것까지 데려가 태웠다. 마치 내가 여전히, 그가 자기의 위대한 천성의 풍요로움을 그토록 아낌없이 나누어 주었던 그 작고 무력한 존재인 것처럼.\n\n그리고 조는 내 곁에 올라탔고, 우리는 함께 시골로 마차를 몰아 나갔다. 그곳에서는 풍성한 여름의 생장이 이미 나무와 풀에 깃들어 있었고, 달콤한 여름 향내가 온 대기를 가득 채웠다. 마침 그날은 일요일이었는데, 내 주위의 사랑스러움을 바라보며, 그것이 어떻게 자라고 변해 왔는지를, 그리고 가엾은 내가 침대 위에서 불덩이가 되어 뒤척이는 동안에도 작은 들꽃들이 어떻게 피어났고 새들의 노랫소리가 어떻게 낮이고 밤이고, 태양 아래서 또 별빛 아래서 어떻게 우렁차졌는지를 생각하니, 거기서 불덩이가 되어 뒤척였다는 기억 그 자체가 내 평온함에 제동을 거는 듯이 다가왔다. 그러나 일요일의 종소리를 듣고, 펼쳐진 아름다움을 좀 더 둘러보았을 때, 나는 내가 충분히 감사하기에는 한참 모자란다는 것을 — 아직 그러기에도 너무 쇠약하다는 것을 — 느꼈고, 오래전에 조가 나를 장터며 이런저런 곳에 데려갔을 때 그랬듯이 그의 어깨에 머리를 기댔는데, 그것은 어린 내 감각에 벅찬 일이었다.\n\n얼마 후 나는 좀 더 평정을 되찾았고, 우리는 옛 포대의 풀밭에 누워 그러곤 했듯이 이야기를 나누었다. 조에게는 아무런 변화도 없었다. 그때 내 눈에 비쳤던 모습 그대로, 그는 여전히 내 눈에 비쳤다. 그저 한결같이 충실했고, 그저 한결같이 옳았다.\n\n다시 돌아와 그가 나를 안아 내려, 어찌나 수월하게! 안마당을 가로질러 계단을 올라 데려갈 때, 나는 그가 나를 안고 습지를 가로질러 데려갔던 그 잊지 못할 크리스마스 날을 떠올렸다. 우리는 내 운명의 변화에 대해서는 아직 아무 언급도 한 적이 없었고, 그가 내 근래의 내력을 얼마나 알고 있는지도 나는 알지 못했다. 나는 이제 나 자신에 대해 너무도 미덥지 못했고 그를 너무도 신뢰했던 까닭에, 그가 그것을 들먹이지 않는데도 내가 거기 대해 말을 꺼내야 옳은지 어떤지 스스로 판단을 내릴 수가 없었다.\n\n\"들었어요, 조.\" 그날 저녁, 그가 창가에서 파이프를 피울 때, 나는 곰곰이 생각한 끝에 그에게 물었다. \"내 후원자가 누구였는지?\"\n\n\"내가 듣기론.\" 조가 대답했다. \"미스 해비셤은 아니었다더라, 친구야.\"\n\n\"누구였는지 들었어요, 조?\"\n\n\"글쎄! 졸리 바지맨에서 너한테 그 지폐를 건넨 사람을 보낸 그 사람이었다고 듣긴 했다, 핍.\"\n\n\"그래요, 그랬죠.\"\n\n\"놀라운 일이지!\" 조가 더없이 차분하게 말했다.\n\n\"그 사람이 죽었다는 것도 들었어요, 조?\" 나는 이내 점점 더 머뭇거리며 물었다.\n\n\"누구? 그 지폐를 보낸 사람 말이냐, 핍?\"\n\n\"네.\"\n\n\"내 생각엔 말이다.\" 조가 한참 곰곰이 생각하고 나서, 창가 자리를 다소 피하는 듯한 눈길로 보며 말했다. \"그가 그쪽 방면에서 대충 무슨 일인가에 관련돼 있다는 얘긴 듣긴 들은 것 같다.\"\n\n\"그의 처지에 대해 뭔가 들은 게 있어요, 조?\"\n\n\"특별한 건 없다, 핍.\"\n\n\"혹시 듣고 싶으면, 조—\" 내가 말을 꺼내려는데, 조가 일어나 내 소파로 다가왔다.\n\n\"이봐라, 친구야.\" 조가 내 위로 몸을 굽히며 말했다. \"우린 늘 둘도 없는 친구잖니, 안 그러냐, 핍?\"\n\n나는 부끄러워 그에게 대답할 수가 없었다.\n\n\"그럼 됐다.\" 마치 내가 대답이라도 한 것처럼 조가 말했다. \"그럼 괜찮아. 그렇게 합의된 거다. 그럼 우리 같은 두 사람 사이에 영영 불필요할 수밖에 없는 그런 주제는 왜 파고들겠니? 우리 같은 두 사람 사이엔 불필요한 것들 말고도 이야깃거리가 충분하잖아. 원, 세상에! 네 가엾은 누나랑 그 누나의 발작을 생각해 봐라! 그리고 티클러 기억 안 나니?\"\n\n\"정말 기억나요, 조.\"\n\n\"이봐라, 친구야.\" 조가 말했다. \"나는 너랑 티클러를 떼어 놓으려고 할 수 있는 건 다 했다만, 내 힘이 늘 내 마음만큼 충분치는 못했지. 네 가엾은 누나가 너를 한바탕 두들겨 패려고 마음먹으면 말이다.\" 조가 즐겨 쓰는 따지는 투로 말했다. \"내가 누나한테 맞서면 누나가 나까지 두들겨 팼다는 게 문제가 아니라, 그 때문에 너를 늘 더 호되게 두들겨 팼다는 게 문제였지. 나는 그걸 알아챘다. 사내 수염을 한번 잡아채거나, 사내를 한두 번 흔들어 댄다고 해서 (네 누나야 얼마든지 그래도 좋았다만) 어린아이를 매에서 구해 내려는 마음이 꺾이는 건 아니야. 하지만 그 수염 잡아채기나 흔들어 대기 때문에 그 어린아이가 더 호되게 두들겨 맞는다면, 그러면 그 사내는 자연히 떨치고 일어나 스스로에게 말하지, '네가 행하는 선이 어디 있느냐? 해악은 내 보고 있다만,' 그 사내가 말하길, '선은 안 보인단 말이다. 그러니 선생, 그 선이 어디 있는지 짚어 보시오.'\"\n\n\"그 사내가 그렇게 말한다고요?\" 조가 내 말을 기다리기에 내가 끼어들었다.\n\n\"그 사내가 그렇게 말하지.\" 조가 수긍했다. \"그 사내 말이 옳으냐?\"\n\n\"정다운 조, 그 사람은 늘 옳지요.\"\n\n\"그럼 말이다, 친구야.\" 조가 말했다. \"네가 한 말을 지켜야지. 그가 늘 옳다면 (하기야 대체로는 틀릴 가능성이 더 많지만), 이 말을 할 때도 그가 옳은 거다. 네가 어린아이였을 때 혹 어떤 사소한 일을 혼자만 간직했다면, 너는 주로 J. 가저리가 너랑 티클러를 떼어 놓을 힘이 그 마음만큼 충분치 못하다는 걸 알았기 때문에 그랬던 거다. 그러니 우리 같은 두 사람 사이의 일로 더는 그 일을 생각지 말고, 불필요한 주제에 대해선 우리 왈가왈부하지 말자꾸나. 내가 떠나기 전에 비디가 나 때문에 어지간히 애를 먹었지 (내가 워낙 끔찍하게 둔하니까), 내가 그걸 이런 식으로 봐야 한다고, 그리고 이런 식으로 보고서 이렇게 말해야 한다고 말이다. 그 둘 다.\" 조가 자기의 논리적 정리에 자못 흐뭇해하며 말했다. \"다 마쳤으니, 이제 진정한 친구로서 네게 이 말을 하마. 그러니까. 너는 무리해선 안 되고, 저녁을 먹고 포도주에 물 탄 걸 마시고, 이불 사이에 눕혀져야 한다.\"\n\n조가 이 주제를 거두어들이는 그 섬세함, 그리고 — 여자 특유의 기지로 나를 그토록 일찌감치 꿰뚫어 본 — 비디가 그를 그 일에 미리 대비시킨 그 다정한 재치와 친절함은, 내 마음에 깊은 인상을 남겼다. 그러나 조가 내가 얼마나 가난해졌는지, 그리고 내 위대한 유산이 — 태양 앞의 우리 습지 안개처럼 — 어떻게 죄다 녹아 사라졌는지를 알고 있는지 어떤지는, 나로서는 헤아릴 수 없었다.\n\n조에게서 처음 드러나기 시작했을 때는 이해할 수 없었으나 이내 서글픈 깨달음에 이르게 된 또 한 가지는 이것이었다. 내가 점점 튼튼해지고 나아지면서, 조가 나를 대하는 것이 조금씩 덜 편안해졌다는 것. 내가 쇠약하여 그에게 전적으로 의지하던 동안, 그 정다운 사람은 옛 어조로 돌아가, 옛 이름으로, 그러니까 이제는 내 귀에 음악이던 그 정다운 \"정다운 핍, 친구야\"라고 나를 불렀다. 나 역시 옛 방식으로 돌아가, 그가 그렇게 하도록 내버려 두는 것만으로 그저 행복하고 감사했다. 그러나 내가 그것을 단단히 붙들고 있는데도, 알아차릴 수 없을 만큼 슬그머니 조가 그것을 붙드는 힘이 느슨해지기 시작했다. 그리고 처음엔 이를 의아해했으나, 나는 이내 그 원인이 내게 있다는 것을, 그리고 그 잘못이 온전히 내 것이라는 것을 깨닫기 시작했다.\n\n아! 내가 조에게, 내 한결같음을 의심할 빌미를, 그리고 형편이 피면 내가 그에게 냉랭해져 그를 내쳐 버리리라 여길 빌미를 주었던 것은 아닐까? 내가 조의 순진한 마음에, 내가 튼튼해질수록 나에 대한 그의 손길이 약해지리라는 것을, 그래서 내가 스스로 몸을 빼내기 전에 제때에 그 손길을 늦추어 나를 놓아 보내는 편이 낫겠다는 것을, 본능적으로 느낄 까닭을 주었던 것은 아닐까?\n\n조의 팔에 기대어 템플 정원으로 산책을 나간 세 번째인가 네 번째 날에, 나는 그의 이 변화를 아주 또렷이 보았다. 우리는 밝고 따뜻한 햇살 속에 앉아 강을 바라보고 있었는데, 일어서며 나는 마침 이렇게 말했다.\n\n\"보세요, 조! 나 아주 튼튼하게 걸을 수 있어요. 자, 내가 혼자서 걸어 돌아가는 걸 보여 드릴게요.\"\n\n\"무리하진 말아라, 핍.\" 조가 말했다. \"하지만 선생님이 그럴 수 있게 된 걸 보니 기쁩니다.\"\n\n마지막 그 호칭이 내 마음을 거슬렀다. 그러나 어찌 따지겠는가! 나는 정원 문 앞까지밖에 걷지 못했고, 그러고는 실제보다 더 쇠약한 척하며 조에게 팔을 청했다. 조는 팔을 내주었으나, 생각에 잠겨 있었다.\n\n나 또한 나대로 생각에 잠겨 있었다. 조에게서 점점 자라나는 이 변화를 어떻게 하면 가장 잘 막을까 하는 것이, 회한에 찬 내 생각에 큰 골칫거리였기 때문이다. 내가 정확히 어떤 처지에 놓였는지, 그리고 내가 어디까지 영락했는지를 그에게 말하기가 부끄러웠다는 것을, 나는 감추려 하지 않는다. 그러나 내 망설임이 그리 비루하기만 한 것은 아니었기를 바란다. 그가 자기의 변변찮은 저축에서 나를 도우려 들 것임을 나는 알았고, 또 그가 나를 도와서는 안 된다는 것을, 그리고 내가 그렇게 하도록 그를 내버려 두어서는 안 된다는 것을 알고 있었다.\n\n우리 둘 다에게 생각이 많은 저녁이었다. 그러나 잠자리에 들기 전, 나는 내일까지 기다리기로 — 내일이 일요일이었으므로 — 그리고 새로운 한 주와 더불어 나의 새로운 행로를 시작하기로 결심했다. 월요일 아침에 나는 조에게 이 변화에 대해 말하리라, 이 마지막 남은 거리낌의 자취를 떨쳐 버리리라, 내가 마음에 품은 것을 (아직 다다르지 못한 그 '둘째'를) 그에게 말하리라, 그리고 어째서 허버트에게로 가기로 결정하지 못했는지를 말하리라, 그러면 이 변화는 영영 정복되리라. 내 마음이 개자, 조의 마음도 갰고, 마치 그도 공감하여 어떤 결심에 다다른 듯이 보였다.\n\n우리는 일요일을 조용히 보냈고, 마차를 타고 시골로 나가, 그러고는 들판을 거닐었다.\n\n\"내가 앓았던 게 감사하게 느껴져요, 조.\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"정다운 핍, 친구야, 너 이제 거의 다 나았구나.\"\n\n\"내겐 잊지 못할 시간이었어요, 조.\"\n\n\"저도 마찬가지지요, 선생님.\" 조가 대답했다.\n\n\"우린 함께 보낸 시간이 있었지요, 조, 내가 결코 잊을 수 없는 시간이요. 한때, 한동안 잊어버렸던 날들이 있었다는 걸 나도 알아요. 하지만 이 시간만은 결코 잊지 않을 거예요.\"\n\n\"핍.\" 조가 다소 황급하고 곤혹스러워하는 듯이 말했다. \"신나는 일들이 있었지. 그리고, 친애하는 선생님, 우리 사이에 있었던 일은 — 그저 있었던 일이고요.\"\n\n밤이 되어 내가 잠자리에 들자, 조는 내 회복 기간 내내 그래 왔듯이 내 방으로 들어왔다. 그는 내가 아침과 다름없이 잘 지내고 있다고 확신하느냐고 물었다.\n\n\"네, 정다운 조, 아주요.\"\n\n\"그리고 늘 점점 튼튼해지고 있고, 친구야?\"\n\n\"네, 정다운 조, 꾸준히요.\"\n\n조는 그 크고 선량한 손으로 내 어깨께 이불을 토닥이고는, 내가 듣기에 잠긴 듯한 목소리로 말했다. \"잘 자라!\"\n\n아침에 일어났을 때, 한결 상쾌하고 더욱 튼튼해진 나는, 지체 없이 조에게 모든 것을 말하리라는 결심으로 가득 차 있었다. 아침 식사 전에 그에게 말하리라. 당장 옷을 입고 그의 방으로 가서 그를 놀래 주리라. 일찍 일어난 것은 그날이 처음이었으니까. 나는 그의 방으로 갔으나, 그는 거기 없었다. 그가 없을 뿐만 아니라, 그의 짐 상자도 사라지고 없었다.\n\n나는 서둘러 아침 식탁으로 갔고, 그 위에서 편지 한 통을 발견했다. 그 짤막한 내용은 이러했다.\n\n\"폐를 끼치고 싶지 않아 나는 떠난다 정다운 핍 네가 다시 건강해졌으니 내가 없는 편이 더 나을 게다\n조.\n\n추신. 우린 늘 둘도 없는 친구다.\"\n\n편지 안에는 내가 체포되었던 그 빚과 비용에 대한 영수증이 동봉되어 있었다. 그 순간까지도 나는, 내 채권자가 절차를 철회했거나, 내가 완전히 회복될 때까지 절차를 미뤄 둔 것이리라 부질없이 짐작하고 있었다. 조가 그 돈을 갚았으리라고는 꿈에도 생각지 못했다. 그러나 조가 그것을 갚았고, 영수증은 그의 이름으로 되어 있었다.\n\n이제 내게 남은 일이라곤, 그를 따라 정다운 옛 대장간으로 내려가, 거기서 그에게 내 고백을 털어놓고, 참회에 찬 항변을 그에게 하고, 그리고 처음엔 내 생각 속에 어렴풋이 머물던 무언가로 시작했다가 굳은 결심으로 자리 잡은 그 미뤄 둔 '둘째'로부터 내 마음과 가슴을 풀어 주는 것 말고 무엇이 있겠는가?\n\n그 결심이란, 내가 비디에게로 가서, 내가 얼마나 겸허해지고 뉘우치며 돌아왔는지를 그녀에게 보여 주고, 내가 한때 바라던 모든 것을 어떻게 잃었는지를 그녀에게 말하고, 내가 처음 불행했던 시절 우리가 나누었던 옛 흉금을 그녀에게 일깨우겠다는 것이었다. 그러고 나서 나는 그녀에게 말하리라. \"비디, 내 마음이 한때 당신을 무척 좋아했던 것 같아요. 내 방황하는 마음이 당신에게서 멀리 헤매던 그때조차도, 그 후 어느 때보다 당신 곁에서 더 잔잔하고 더 선했으니까요. 만약 당신이 단 절반만큼이라도 다시 한번 나를 좋아해 줄 수 있다면, 만약 내 머리 위에 짊어진 온갖 결함과 좌절과 더불어 나를 받아들여 줄 수 있다면, 만약 용서받은 아이처럼 나를 맞아 줄 수 있다면 (정말이지 나는 그만큼 미안하고, 비디, 달래 주는 목소리와 어루만지는 손길이 그만큼 절실해요), 나는 내가 예전의 나보다 당신에게 조금은 더 어울리는 사람이기를 바라요. 많이는 아니어도, 조금은요. 그리고, 비디, 내가 조와 함께 대장간에서 일할지, 아니면 이 시골에서 다른 일자리를 찾아볼지, 아니면 우리가 멀리 어딘가로 떠날지는 당신이 정하기에 달려 있어요. 그 먼 곳에는, 제안받았을 때 내가 당신의 대답을 알게 될 때까지 미뤄 둔 어떤 기회가 나를 기다리고 있거든요. 그러니 이제, 정다운 비디, 만약 당신이 나와 함께 이 세상을 헤쳐 가겠다고 말해 준다면, 당신은 분명 내게 이 세상을 더 나은 곳으로 만들어 줄 것이고, 나를 그로 인해 더 나은 사람으로 만들어 줄 것이며, 나 또한 당신을 위해 이 세상을 더 나은 곳으로 만들고자 힘껏 애쓰겠어요.\"\n\n이것이 나의 결심이었다. 사흘을 더 회복한 뒤, 나는 그것을 실행에 옮기러 옛 고장으로 내려갔다. 그리고 내가 그 일에서 어떻게 되었는지가, 내게 남은 이야기의 전부다.\n\n# Chapter LVIII.\n\n내 드높은 재산이 무겁게 추락했다는 소식은 내가 도착하기도 전에 이미 고향과 그 인근까지 전해져 있었다. 블루 보어 여관도 그 정보를 알고 있었고, 그것이 보어의 태도에 큰 변화를 가져왔음을 나는 알아차렸다. 내가 재산을 손에 넣어 가던 무렵 보어는 따뜻한 열성으로 내 호감을 사려 애썼으나, 이제 내가 재산을 잃어 가는 처지가 되자 보어는 그 화제에 대해 몹시 냉랭했다.\n\n내가 도착한 것은 저녁이었고, 그토록 자주 그토록 수월하게 다니던 그 여정에 나는 몹시 지쳐 있었다. 보어는 내가 늘 쓰던 침실을 내주지 못했는데, 그 방은 (아마도 무슨 유산을 기대하는 누군가에게) 이미 예약되어 있었기에, 안뜰 위쪽 비둘기들과 역마차들 사이의 지극히 변변찮은 방 하나밖에 내게 배정해 주지 못했다. 그러나 나는 그 숙소에서, 보어가 내줄 수 있었을 가장 고급스러운 방에서만큼이나 깊은 잠을 잤고, 내 꿈의 질도 가장 좋은 침실에서와 별반 다르지 않았다.\n\n이른 아침, 아침 식사가 준비되는 동안 나는 새티스 하우스 쪽으로 어슬렁거리며 돌아보았다. 대문에도, 창밖으로 늘어뜨린 카펫 조각에도 인쇄된 벽보가 붙어 있었는데, 다음 주에 가재도구와 동산을 경매로 매각한다는 공고였다. 저택 자체도 낡은 건축 자재로 팔려 헐릴 예정이었다. 양조장 건물에는 \"제1호\"라는 글씨가 휘청거리는 안짱다리 같은 흰 칠로 새겨져 있었고, 그토록 오래 닫혀 있던 본관의 한 부분에는 \"제2호\"가 적혀 있었다. 구조물의 다른 부분들에도 다른 번호가 매겨져 있었고, 그 글씨를 적을 자리를 마련하느라 담쟁이덩굴은 뜯겨 나가, 그 상당 부분이 흙먼지 속에 낮게 늘어진 채 벌써 시들어 있었다. 열린 대문 안으로 잠시 발을 들여, 거기 볼일이 없는 낯선 자의 거북한 태도로 사방을 둘러보다가, 나는 경매인의 서기가 술통 위를 걸어 다니며 목록을 작성하는 자에게 그것들을 일러 주는 모습을 보았는데, 그 목록 작성자는 펜을 손에 쥔 채, 내가 그토록 자주 \"올드 클렘\" 가락에 맞추어 밀고 다니던 그 바퀴 달린 의자를 임시 책상 삼아 쓰고 있었다.\n\n보어의 커피실로 돌아와 아침 식사 앞에 앉으니, 펌블추크 씨가 주인과 이야기를 나누고 있는 것이 보였다. 펌블추크 씨는 (지난번 야밤의 모험으로 외모가 나아진 바는 없었다) 나를 기다리고 있었고, 다음과 같은 말로 내게 말을 걸었다.\n\n\"젊은이, 자네가 영락한 꼴을 보게 되어 유감일세. 하지만 달리 무엇을 기대할 수 있었겠나! 달리 무엇을 기대할 수 있었겠어!\"\n\n그가 더없이 관대하게 용서하는 태도로 손을 내미는데, 나는 병으로 쇠약해져 다툴 형편이 못 되었으므로, 그 손을 잡았다.\n\n\"윌리엄,\" 펌블추크 씨가 웨이터에게 말했다. \"탁자에 머핀을 올리게나. 일이 이 지경에 이르다니! 일이 이 지경에 이르다니!\"\n\n나는 얼굴을 찌푸린 채 아침 식사 앞에 앉았다. 펌블추크 씨는 내 위로 우뚝 서서, 내가 찻주전자에 손을 대기도 전에, 끝까지 진실하기로 작정한 은인의 태도로 내 차를 따라 주었다.\n\n\"윌리엄,\" 펌블추크 씨가 구슬프게 말했다. \"소금을 놓아 주게. 더 행복하던 시절에는,\" 그가 내게 말을 돌렸다. \"설탕을 넣으셨던 것 같은데? 그리고 우유도 넣으셨지요? 그러셨고말고. 설탕과 우유라. 윌리엄, 물냉이를 좀 가져오게.\"\n\n\"고맙습니다만,\" 나는 짧게 말했다. \"저는 물냉이는 먹지 않습니다.\"\n\n\"안 드신다고요,\" 펌블추크 씨가 마치 그럴 줄 알았다는 듯, 그리고 물냉이를 멀리하는 것이 내 몰락과 부합한다는 듯, 한숨을 내쉬며 여러 번 고개를 끄덕이고는 대꾸했다. \"옳거니. 땅이 내어 주는 소박한 결실인데. 그렇지요. 가져올 것 없네, 윌리엄.\"\n\n나는 아침 식사를 계속했고, 펌블추크 씨는 늘 그러하듯 물고기 같은 눈으로 빤히 쳐다보고 시끄럽게 숨을 몰아쉬며 내 위에 우뚝 서 있었다.\n\n\"가죽과 뼈밖에 안 남았구먼!\" 펌블추크 씨가 소리 내어 곰곰이 읊조렸다. \"그런데도 이 사람이 여기를 떠날 적에는 (감히 내 축복을 받으며 떠났다고 말할 수 있겠지), 그리고 내가 꿀벌처럼 변변찮은 내 곳간을 그 앞에 펼쳐 놓았을 적에는, 복숭아처럼 통통했단 말이야!\"\n\n이 말은, 내가 새로이 번창하던 무렵에 그가 \"그래도 되겠습니까?\" 하며 비굴하게 손을 내밀던 태도와, 방금 그 똑같은 살진 다섯 손가락을 과시하듯 관대하게 내밀던 태도 사이의 놀라운 차이를 내게 일깨워 주었다.\n\n\"하!\" 그가 내게 버터 바른 빵을 건네며 말을 이었다. \"그래, 조지프한테 가는 길인가?\"\n\n\"맙소사,\" 나는 나도 모르게 발끈하여 말했다. \"제가 어디를 가든 그게 당신과 무슨 상관입니까? 그 찻주전자 좀 가만 놔두세요.\"\n\n그것은 내가 취할 수 있는 최악의 처사였으니, 펌블추크에게 그가 바라던 기회를 주고 말았기 때문이다.\n\n\"그렇지, 젊은이,\" 그가 문제의 그 물건 손잡이를 놓고, 내 탁자에서 한두 걸음 물러서며, 문간의 주인과 웨이터가 들으라는 듯 말했다. \"그 찻주전자, 가만 놔두지. 자네 말이 옳네, 젊은이. 모처럼 자네 말이 옳아. 방종이 가져온 쇠약의 폐해로 기진맥진한 자네 몸뚱이가 조상 대대로 내려온 건강한 자양분으로 기운을 차리기를 바라는 마음에서, 자네 아침 식사에 이토록 관심을 쏟다가 그만 내가 분수를 잊었네그려. 하지만 말이야,\" 펌블추크가 주인과 웨이터 쪽으로 돌아서며, 팔을 쭉 뻗어 나를 가리켜 보였다. \"이 사람이 바로, 행복한 어린 시절에 내가 늘 데리고 놀아 주던 그 아이라네! 그럴 리 없다고는 말하지 말게. 내가 말하지 않나, 이 사람이 바로 그 아이라고!\"\n\n두 사람에게서 나직한 웅성거림이 답으로 돌아왔다. 웨이터는 특히 감동한 듯이 보였다.\n\n\"이 사람이 바로,\" 펌블추크가 말했다. \"내가 내 이륜마차에 태우고 다니던 그 아이라네. 이 사람이 바로 손수 길러지는 것을 내가 지켜본 그 아이야. 이 사람이 바로, 친정어머니에게서 물려받은 이름이 조지애나 마리아였던, 내가 혼인으로 외삼촌이 된 그 누나의 동생이라네. 아니라고 부인할 수 있거든 어디 해 보라지!\"\n\n웨이터는 내가 그것을 부인할 수 없으리라는 것을, 그리고 그것이 이 사건을 음험하게 보이게 만든다는 것을 확신하는 듯했다.\n\n\"젊은이,\" 펌블추크가 예전 그 버릇대로 내 쪽으로 고개를 비틀며 말했다. \"자네는 조지프한테 가는 길이지. 자네가 어디를 가든 그게 나와 무슨 상관이냐고, 자네는 내게 묻지. 내 자네에게 말하지, 이보게, 자네는 조지프한테 가는 길이야.\"\n\n웨이터는 마치 그쯤은 넘어가 달라고 점잖게 청하기라도 하듯 기침을 했다.\n\n\"자,\" 펌블추크가 말했는데, 이 모든 것을 더없이 약을 올리는 태도로, 더할 나위 없이 설득력 있고 결정적인 말을 미덕을 위하여 베푸는 듯이 늘어놓았다. \"조지프한테 무어라 말해야 할지 일러 주지. 여기 이 고장에서 알려지고 존경받는 보어의 주인장 스콰이어스 씨가 계시고, 또 여기 윌리엄이 있는데, 내가 잘못 안 게 아니라면 그 아버지 이름이 포트킨스였지.\"\n\n\"맞습니다, 어르신,\" 윌리엄이 말했다.\n\n\"이분들 앞에서,\" 펌블추크가 말을 이었다. \"내 자네에게, 젊은이, 조지프한테 무어라 말해야 할지 일러 주겠네. 자네는 이렇게 말하는 거야. '조지프, 나는 오늘 내 가장 이른 은인이자 내 재산의 창시자를 만났소. 이름은 대지 않겠소, 조지프, 하지만 시내에서들 그렇게 기꺼이 부르고 있고, 나는 그분을 만났단 말이오.'\"\n\n\"맹세컨대 저는 여기서 그런 분을 못 보겠는데요,\" 나는 말했다.\n\n\"그것도 같이 말하게,\" 펌블추크가 받아쳤다. \"자네가 그렇게 말했다고 전하게. 그러면 조지프조차도 아마 놀라움을 드러낼 걸세.\"\n\n\"그 점에서는 당신이 그 사람을 완전히 잘못 봤습니다,\" 나는 말했다. \"제가 더 잘 압니다.\"\n\n\"자네는 이렇게 말하는 거야,\" 펌블추크가 말을 이었다. \"'조지프, 나는 그분을 만났는데, 그분은 당신에게도 악의가 없고 내게도 악의가 없소. 그분은 당신의 성품을 아오, 조지프, 그리고 당신의 옹고집과 무지를 잘 알고 있소. 그리고 그분은 내 성품도 아오, 조지프, 또 내가 감사할 줄 모른다는 것도 아오. 그렇소, 조지프,' 자네는 이렇게 말하는 거야,\" 여기서 펌블추크는 내게 고개와 손을 흔들어 보였다. \"'그분은 내게 사람으로서 마땅히 갖춰야 할 보통의 감사하는 마음이 완전히 결여되어 있다는 것을 아오. 그분은 그것을 아오, 조지프, 누구도 알 수 없을 만큼 아오. 당신은 그것을 모르오, 조지프, 알 까닭이 없으니까, 하지만 그분은 안단 말이오.'\"\n\n허풍선이 멍청이 주제에, 감히 그런 말을 내 면전에 늘어놓을 낯짝이 있다는 것이 나는 정말이지 어처구니없었다.\n\n\"자네는 이렇게 말하는 거야. '조지프, 그분이 내게 짤막한 전갈을 주었는데, 그것을 지금 그대로 옮기겠소. 그것은, 내가 영락한 데서 그분은 섭리의 손가락을 보았다는 것이오. 그분은 조지프를 보았을 때 그 손가락을 알아보았고, 그것을 똑똑히 보았소. 그것은 이런 글귀를 가리키고 있었소, 조지프. 자신의 가장 이른 은인이자 재산의 창시자에 대한 배은망덕의 대가. 하지만 그분은 자신이 한 일을 후회하지 않는다고 말했소, 조지프. 조금도. 그렇게 한 것은 옳은 일이었고, 친절한 일이었고, 자비로운 일이었으며, 다시라도 그렇게 하겠노라고 했소.'\"\n\n\"딱하기도 하지,\" 나는 중단되었던 아침 식사를 마치며 비웃듯이 말했다. \"그분이 대체 무슨 일을 했고 다시라도 무슨 일을 하겠다는 건지는 말하지 않았다니 말입니다.\"\n\n\"보어의 스콰이어스 씨!\" 펌블추크는 이제 주인에게 말을 걸고 있었다. \"그리고 윌리엄! 자네들이 원한다면, 시내에서든 변두리에서든, 그렇게 한 것이 옳은 일이었고, 친절한 일이었고, 자비로운 일이었으며, 내가 다시라도 그렇게 하겠노라고 했다는 걸 언급해도 나는 이의가 없네.\"\n\n그 말과 함께 그 사기꾼은 거드름을 피우며 두 사람과 악수를 나눈 뒤 여관을 떠났는데, 나는 그 막연한 \"그 일\"이라는 것의 미덕에 기뻐했다기보다는 훨씬 더 어리둥절해질 뿐이었다. 나도 그의 뒤를 이어 곧 여관을 나섰고, 큰길을 따라 내려가다 보니 그가 자기 가게 문 앞에서 한 무리의 선택된 사람들을 향해 (분명 같은 취지로) 일장 연설을 늘어놓고 있는 것이 보였는데, 내가 길 반대편으로 지나가자 그들은 내게 자못 곱지 않은 눈길을 보내는 영광을 베풀어 주었다.\n\n그러나 이 뻔뻔한 사기꾼과 대비될 때, 그 큰 아량이 (그럴 수 있다면) 전보다 한층 더 밝게 빛나는 비디와 조에게로 돌아서니 그저 더없이 기쁠 뿐이었다. 다리가 약했던 까닭에 나는 천천히 그들에게로 다가갔으나, 가까이 갈수록 점점 커지는 안도감과, 오만과 거짓을 점점 더 멀리 뒤로하고 떠나오는 듯한 느낌을 안고 걸었다.\n\n6월의 날씨는 더없이 상쾌했다. 하늘은 푸르렀고, 종달새들은 푸른 보리밭 위 높이 날아오르고 있었으니, 나는 그 온 시골 풍경이 여태껏 알아 온 그 어느 때보다도 한결 더 아름답고 평화롭다고 생각했다. 내가 그곳에서 누리게 될 삶의 즐거운 그림들이, 그리고 그 소박한 믿음과 맑은 가정적 지혜를 내가 몸소 겪어 알게 된 길잡이가 곁에 있을 때 내 성품에 찾아올 더 나은 방향으로의 변화가, 가는 길을 즐겁게 해 주었다. 그것들은 내 안에 다정한 감정을 불러일으켰으니, 돌아왔다는 것만으로도 내 마음은 누그러졌고, 또한 이런 변화가 이미 일어나 있었던지라, 나는 마치 먼 여행에서 맨발로 고생스레 집으로 돌아오는, 그 방랑이 여러 해나 이어진 사람처럼 느껴졌다.\n\n비디가 선생으로 있는 학교는 한 번도 본 적이 없었다. 그러나 조용히 가려고 마을로 들어선 그 작고 구불구불한 샛길이 나를 그 앞으로 지나가게 했다. 그날이 휴일이라는 것을 알고 나는 실망했다. 아이들도 없었고, 비디의 집도 닫혀 있었다. 비디가 나를 보기 전에 그녀가 날마다의 일에 바쁘게 매달려 있는 모습을 보았으면 하는 어떤 기대 어린 마음이 내 안에 있었으나, 그것은 무산되고 말았다.\n\n그러나 대장간은 아주 가까운 거리에 있었으므로, 나는 향긋한 푸른 라임나무 아래로 조의 망치 소리가 들리려나 귀를 기울이며 그쪽으로 걸어갔다. 진작 그 소리를 들었어야 할 때가 한참 지나도록, 그리고 그 소리를 들은 듯싶었으나 그저 착각이었음을 알게 된 때가 한참 지나도록, 사방은 고요하기만 했다. 라임나무도 거기 있었고, 흰 가시나무도 거기 있었고, 밤나무도 거기 있어, 내가 걸음을 멈추고 귀 기울일 때 그 잎들이 어우러져 사각거렸으나, 조의 망치 소리는 한여름의 바람 속에 없었다.\n\n까닭도 모른 채 대장간이 시야에 들어오는 것이 거의 두려워질 무렵, 나는 마침내 그것을 보았고, 그것이 닫혀 있음을 보았다. 불빛 한 점, 반짝이며 쏟아지는 불똥 한 줄기, 풀무의 울부짖음 하나 없이, 죄다 닫힌 채 고요했다.\n\n그러나 집은 버려진 것이 아니었고, 제일 좋은 응접실은 쓰이고 있는 듯했으니, 그 창문에는 흰 커튼이 나부끼고 있었고, 창문은 열려 꽃으로 화사했기 때문이다. 나는 꽃 너머로 안을 들여다볼 작정으로 살그머니 그쪽으로 다가갔는데, 그때 조와 비디가 팔짱을 낀 채 내 앞에 나타났다.\n\n처음에 비디는 내 유령이라도 본 듯 비명을 질렀으나, 다음 순간 그녀는 내 품에 안겨 있었다. 나는 그녀를 보고 울었고, 그녀도 나를 보고 울었다. 나는 그녀가 그토록 싱그럽고 정겨워 보였기 때문이었고, 그녀는 내가 그토록 수척하고 핼쑥해 보였기 때문이었다.\n\n\"하지만 비디, 이렇게 말끔하게 차려입었구나!\"\n\n\"응, 핍.\"\n\n\"그리고 조, 형도 이렇게 말끔하게 차려입었네요!\"\n\n\"그래, 그래 우리 핍, 친구.\"\n\n나는 두 사람을 번갈아 바라보다가, 그러고 나서—\n\n\"오늘이 내 결혼식 날이야!\" 비디가 행복에 겨워 터뜨리듯 외쳤다. \"나, 조랑 결혼했어!\"\n\n그들은 나를 부엌으로 데려갔고, 나는 낡은 전나무 탁자에 머리를 묻었다. 비디는 내 한 손을 잡아 제 입술에 갖다 댔고, 조의 위로해 주는 손길이 내 어깨에 놓여 있었다. \"이 애가 놀라기에는 충분히 튼튼하지가 못했지 뭐야, 여보,\" 조가 말했다. 그러자 비디가 말했다. \"내가 미처 그 생각을 했어야 했는데, 여보, 너무 행복해서 그만.\" 두 사람은 나를 보고 너무도 기뻐했고, 나를 보고 너무도 자랑스러워했고, 내가 그들을 찾아온 것에 너무도 감동했고, 내가 우연히 찾아와 그들의 날을 온전하게 채워 준 것을 너무도 흐뭇해했다!\n\n내 첫 생각은, 마지막으로 좌절된 이 희망을 조에게 결코 입 밖에 내지 않았다는 데 대한 크나큰 감사였다. 그가 내 병상을 지키는 동안 그 말이 얼마나 자주 내 입술까지 치밀어 올랐던가! 만일 그가 단 한 시간만 더 내 곁에 머물렀더라면, 그가 그것을 알게 되는 일이 얼마나 돌이킬 수 없는 것이 되었겠는가!\n\n\"비디,\" 나는 말했다. \"너는 온 세상에서 가장 좋은 남편을 얻은 거야. 그가 내 침상 곁에서 어떻게 했는지 네가 보았더라면 너는—아니, 그래도 지금보다 더 그를 사랑할 수는 없겠지.\"\n\n\"응, 정말 더는 못 할 거야,\" 비디가 말했다.\n\n\"그리고 조, 형은 온 세상에서 가장 좋은 아내를 얻은 거예요. 비디는 형이 누릴 자격이 있는 만큼 형을 행복하게 해 줄 거예요. 친절하고, 선량하고, 고귀한 우리 조!\"\n\n조는 입술을 떨며 나를 바라보다가, 급기야 소매로 눈을 가렸다.\n\n\"그리고 조도 비디도, 두 사람 오늘 교회에 다녀와 온 인류를 향한 자비와 사랑에 차 있으니, 두 사람이 내게 베풀어 준 모든 것에 대해, 그리고 내가 그토록 형편없이 갚은 그 모든 것에 대해, 내 변변찮은 감사를 받아 줘요! 그리고 내가 한 시간 안에 떠난다고, 머지않아 외국으로 나갈 거라고, 그리고 두 사람이 나를 감옥에서 벗어나게 해 준 그 돈을 벌어 갚아 두 사람에게 보낼 때까지 결코 마음 편히 쉬지 않을 거라고 말한다 해서, 부디 생각하지는 말아 줘요, 조, 비디, 내가 그 돈을 천 배로 갚는다 한들 두 사람에게 진 빚을 단 한 푼이라도 청산할 수 있으리라고, 혹은 그럴 수 있다 한들 내가 그렇게 청산해 버리려 한다고는!\"\n\n두 사람은 이 말에 마음이 녹아내려, 둘 다 내게 더는 말하지 말라고 간청했다.\n\n\"하지만 더 말해야겠어요. 조, 형이 사랑할 자식들을 두기를 바라요. 그리고 어느 겨울밤 이 굴뚝 모퉁이에 한 어린 녀석이 앉아, 영영 그곳을 떠나 버린 또 다른 어린 녀석을 형에게 떠올리게 해 주기를 바라요. 그 아이에게 말하지 말아요, 조, 내가 은혜를 모르는 놈이었다고는. 그 아이에게 말하지 말아줘, 비디, 내가 야박하고 부당한 놈이었다고는. 다만 그 아이에게, 내가 두 사람을 다 존경했다고만 말해 줘요, 두 사람이 다 그토록 선량하고 진실했기 때문에. 그리고 두 사람의 자식이니, 나보다 훨씬 더 나은 사람으로 자라나는 것이 그 아이에게는 당연한 일이리라고 내가 말했다고만.\"\n\n\"난 말하지 않을 거다,\" 조가 소매 뒤에서 말했다. \"그 아이한테 그런 종류의 말은 아무것도 안 할 거야, 핍. 비디도 안 할 거고. 그 누구도 안 할 거다.\"\n\n\"그리고 이제, 두 사람의 그 너그러운 마음속으로는 이미 그렇게 해 주었다는 걸 알지만, 부디 둘 다 내게 말해 줘요, 나를 용서한다고! 부디 그 말을 들려줘요, 그 소리를 내가 지니고 떠날 수 있도록. 그러면 나는 앞으로 두 사람이 나를 믿어 주고, 나를 더 좋게 여겨 주리라고 믿을 수 있을 거예요!\"\n\n\"오, 우리 핍, 친구,\" 조가 말했다. \"내가 용서할 게 뭐라도 있다면, 하느님은 아실 거다, 내가 너를 용서한다는 걸!\"\n\n\"아멘! 그리고 하느님은 아셔, 나도 그렇다는 걸!\" 비디가 그 말을 따라 했다.\n\n\"이제 위로 올라가 옛날 내 작은 방을 좀 보고, 거기서 혼자 몇 분만 쉬게 해 줘요. 그러고 나서 두 사람과 함께 먹고 마신 뒤에, 작별 인사를 하기 전에 이정표가 있는 데까지만 나와 함께 가 줘요, 조, 비디!\"\n\n나는 가진 것을 모두 팔고, 채권자들과의 화해를 위해 — 그들은 내게 전액을 갚도록 넉넉한 시간을 주었다 — 가능한 한 많은 돈을 따로 떼어 두고서, 길을 나서 허버트와 합류했다. 한 달 안에 나는 영국을 떠났고, 두 달 안에 클라리커 상회의 서기가 되었으며, 넉 달 안에 처음으로 온전히 내 몫의 책임을 맡게 되었다. 그 무렵에는 밀 폰드 뱅크의 응접실 천장을 가로지른 들보가 늙은 빌 발리의 으르렁거림 아래 떨기를 그치고 평온에 들었고, 허버트는 클라라와 결혼하러 떠났으며, 나는 그가 그녀를 데리고 돌아올 때까지 동부 지점을 혼자서 도맡게 되었다.\n\n내가 그 상회의 동업자가 되기까지는 여러 해가 흘렀다. 그러나 나는 허버트와 그의 아내와 더불어 행복하게 살았고, 검소하게 살았고, 빚을 갚았으며, 비디와 조와 끊임없이 서신을 주고받았다. 내가 상회의 세 번째 인물이 되고 나서야 비로소 클라리커가 허버트에게 내 비밀을 털어놓았는데, 그때 그는 허버트의 동업 자격에 얽힌 그 비밀이 오랫동안 자기 양심을 짓눌러 왔으니 이제는 말하지 않을 수 없다고 했다. 그래서 그는 그것을 털어놓았고, 허버트는 놀란 만큼이나 깊이 감동했으며, 그 다정한 친구와 나는 오랜 비밀로 인해 사이가 나빠지기는커녕 조금도 변함이 없었다. 우리가 대단한 상회였다거나, 우리가 어마어마한 돈을 벌었다고 짐작하게 두어서는 안 되겠다. 우리는 대규모로 사업을 하지는 않았으나, 평판이 좋았고, 이익을 위해 부지런히 일했으며, 아주 잘해 나갔다. 우리는 허버트의 한결같이 쾌활한 근면과 민첩함에 많은 것을 빚지고 있었으니, 나는 어쩌다 그가 무능하다는 그 옛 생각을 품게 되었던가 자주 의아해하다가, 어느 날 문득 깨달음을 얻었다. 어쩌면 그 무능함은 애당초 그에게 있었던 것이 아니라, 내게 있었던 것인지도 모른다는 깨달음을.\n\n# Chapter LIX.\n\n십일 년 동안 나는 조도 비디도 이 두 눈으로 보지 못했다. 비록 동방에 있는 동안 두 사람의 모습이 종종 내 머릿속에 떠오르긴 했지만 말이다. 그러던 어느 십이월 저녁, 날이 저물고 한두 시간이 지났을 무렵, 나는 그 낡은 부엌문 빗장 위에 살그머니 손을 얹었다. 어찌나 살며시 닿았던지 아무도 내 기척을 듣지 못했고, 나는 들키지 않은 채 안을 들여다보았다. 거기, 부엌 난롯불 가의 그 옛 자리에서 여전히 정정하고 건장한 모습으로 — 머리가 약간 희끗해지긴 했어도 — 파이프 담배를 피우며 앉아 있는 이는 조였다. 그리고 거기, 조의 다리에 둘러싸여 구석에 갇히듯이, 내 작은 의자에 앉아 불을 바라보고 있는 이는 — 또 한 명의 나였다!\n\n\"우리는 이 녀석한테 자네를 생각해서 핍이라는 이름을 붙여 줬다네, 정다운 친구.\" 내가 아이 곁에 또 다른 의자를 가져다 앉았을 때(그러나 나는 아이의 머리를 헝클어뜨리지는 않았다) 조가 기뻐하며 말했다. \"그리고 우린 이 녀석이 자네를 조금이라도 닮아 가길 바랐는데, 우리가 보기엔 정말 그런 것 같아.\"\n\n나도 그렇게 생각했고, 이튿날 아침 아이를 데리고 산책을 나갔다. 우리는 엄청나게 많은 이야기를 나누었고, 서로를 더없이 완벽하게 이해했다. 나는 아이를 교회 묘지로 데려가, 거기 어느 묘비 위에 앉혀 주었다. 아이는 그 높다란 자리에서 어느 돌이 이 교구의 고인이 된 필립 피립과, 또한 위 사람의 아내 조지애나를 기리는 것인지 내게 가리켜 보였다.\n\n\"비디.\" 저녁 식사 후, 비디의 어린 딸아이가 그녀의 무릎에서 잠들어 있을 때 나는 그녀와 이야기를 나누며 말했다. \"언젠가 핍을 나한테 줘야 해. 아니, 적어도 빌려주기라도 해야 한다고.\"\n\n\"안 돼요, 안 돼요.\" 비디가 부드럽게 말했다. \"당신은 결혼을 하셔야지요.\"\n\n\"허버트와 클라라도 그렇게 말하지만, 난 그러지 않을 것 같아, 비디. 난 그 사람들 집에 아주 자리를 잡고 정착해서, 그럴 가능성은 전혀 없어. 난 이미 어엿한 노총각이라고.\"\n\n비디는 제 아이를 내려다보며 그 작은 손을 입술에 가져다 댔고, 그러고는 그 아이를 어루만지던 후덕한 손을 내 손 안에 쥐여 주었다. 그 동작에는, 그리고 비디의 결혼반지가 살짝 눌러 오는 감촉에는, 무언가 더없이 아름다운 웅변이 담겨 있었다.\n\n\"가엾은 핍.\" 비디가 말했다. \"당신, 그 사람 때문에 마음 아파하지 않는 거 정말 맞아요?\"\n\n\"오, 아니야 — 그렇지 않은 것 같아, 비디.\"\n\n\"아주아주 오래된 벗으로서 말해 줘요. 그 사람을 완전히 잊었어요?\"\n\n\"비디, 내 평생 한때 가장 앞자리를 차지했던 것이라면 그 무엇도 잊은 적이 없고, 어디든 한자리를 차지했던 것조차 거의 잊지 않았어. 하지만 내가 한때 즐겨 부르던 그 가엾은 꿈은, 모두 지나가 버렸어, 비디 — 다 지나가 버렸다고!\"\n\n그렇지만 그 말을 하면서도 나는 알고 있었다. 그날 저녁 그 옛집 자리를 혼자서, 그녀를 생각하며 다시 찾아가 보려고 남몰래 마음먹고 있었다는 것을. 그렇다, 정말로 그랬다. 에스텔라를 위해서였다.\n\n나는 그녀가 더없이 불행한 삶을 살고 있다는 소식을, 그리고 자기를 몹시 잔인하게 다루던 남편과 헤어졌다는 소식을 들은 바 있었다. 그 남편이라는 자는 오만과 탐욕과 야만과 비열함을 한데 뭉쳐 놓은 인간으로 제법 악명을 떨치게 되었다. 그리고 나는 그 남편이 말을 학대하다가 일어난 사고로 죽었다는 소식도 들었다. 그녀가 이렇게 풀려난 것은 이 년쯤 전의 일이었으니, 내가 아는 바로는 그녀가 다시 결혼했을지도 모를 일이었다.\n\n조의 집에서는 저녁 식사 시간이 일렀던 덕분에, 나는 비디와의 이야기를 서두르지 않고도 어두워지기 전에 그 옛 자리로 걸어갈 시간이 넉넉했다. 그러나 가는 길에 낡은 것들을 바라보고 옛 시절을 떠올리느라 어슬렁거린 탓에, 그곳에 이르렀을 때는 날이 이미 저물어 있었다.\n\n이제 집도, 양조장도, 그 어떤 건물도 남아 있지 않았고, 오직 옛 정원의 담장만이 남아 있었다. 말끔히 치워진 공터는 거친 울타리로 둘러쳐져 있었는데, 그 너머를 들여다보니 옛 담쟁이덩굴 일부가 다시 뿌리를 내려, 폐허의 나지막하고 고요한 흙더미 위에서 푸르게 자라고 있었다. 울타리의 문이 빠끔히 열려 있어, 나는 그것을 밀어 열고 안으로 들어갔다.\n\n차갑고 은빛 어린 안개가 오후를 베일처럼 덮고 있었으나, 그것을 흩어 줄 달은 아직 떠오르지 않았다. 그러나 안개 너머로 별들이 빛나고 있었고, 달이 떠오르고 있었으며, 저녁은 어둡지 않았다. 나는 옛집의 어느 부분이 어디에 있었는지, 양조장이 어디에 있었는지, 대문이 어디였는지, 술통이 어디에 있었는지 하나하나 더듬어 짚어 갈 수 있었다. 그렇게 다 짚어 보고 나서 황량한 정원 산책로를 따라 시선을 옮기고 있을 때, 나는 거기 외로이 서 있는 한 사람의 모습을 보았다.\n\n내가 다가가자 그 모습은 나를 알아챈 기색을 보였다. 그것은 나를 향해 다가오고 있었으나, 이내 멈춰 섰다. 더 가까이 다가가 보니 그것은 한 여인의 모습이었다. 더욱 가까이 다가가자 그 여인은 막 돌아서려다가 멈춰 서서, 내가 다가오도록 그대로 두었다. 그러더니 몹시 놀란 듯 머뭇거리며 내 이름을 불렀고, 나는 외쳤다 —\n\n\"에스텔라!\"\n\n\"저는 많이 변했어요. 당신이 저를 알아보다니 놀랍군요.\"\n\n그녀의 아름다움이 지녔던 싱그러움은 정말로 사라지고 없었으나, 그 형언할 수 없는 위엄과 형언할 수 없는 매력만은 그대로 남아 있었다. 그 안에 깃든 그러한 매혹은 나도 전에 본 적이 있었다. 그러나 내가 전에 한 번도 본 적 없는 것은, 한때 그토록 오만하던 두 눈에 어린 슬프고 부드러운 빛이었다. 그리고 내가 전에 한 번도 느껴 본 적 없는 것은, 한때 그토록 무감하던 손길의 다정한 감촉이었다.\n\n우리는 가까이 있는 벤치에 앉았고, 나는 말했다. \"이렇게 오랜 세월이 흐른 뒤에, 우리가 처음 만났던 바로 이곳에서 이렇게 다시 만나다니 이상한 일이군요, 에스텔라! 자주 이곳에 돌아오나요?\"\n\n\"그 후로 한 번도 여기 온 적이 없어요.\"\n\n\"나도 그래요.\"\n달이 떠오르기 시작했고, 나는 사라져 버린, 흰 천장을 향하던 그 평온한 눈길을 떠올렸다. 달이 떠오르기 시작했고, 나는 그가 이 세상에서 들은 마지막 말을 내가 건넸을 때 내 손을 눌러 오던 그 감촉을 떠올렸다.\n\n우리 사이에 뒤이은 침묵을 먼저 깨뜨린 것은 에스텔라였다.\n\n\"저는 아주 여러 번 이곳에 돌아오기를 바라고 또 그러려고 마음먹었지만, 여러 사정에 가로막혔어요. 가엾고 가엾은 옛 자리!\"\n\n은빛 안개가 달빛의 첫 줄기에 어렴풋이 물들었고, 그 같은 빛줄기가 그녀의 두 눈에서 떨어지는 눈물을 어루만졌다. 내가 그것을 보았다는 사실을 모른 채, 마음을 다잡아 눈물을 추스르려 애쓰면서 그녀는 조용히 말했다 —\n\n\"걸어오면서, 이곳이 어쩌다 이런 모습으로 버려지게 되었을까 궁금해하셨나요?\"\n\n\"그래요, 에스텔라.\"\n\n\"이 땅은 제 것이에요. 제가 포기하지 않은 유일한 재산이지요. 다른 모든 것은 조금씩 제게서 떠나갔지만, 저는 이것만은 지켰어요. 그 비참한 세월 내내 제가 단호하게 저항했던 단 하나의 대상이 바로 이것이었어요.\"\n\n\"여기에 무언가를 지을 건가요?\"\n\n\"마침내, 그렇게 됐어요. 그것이 바뀌기 전에 작별을 고하러 여기 온 거예요. 그런데 당신은.\" 그녀가 한 방랑자에게 건네는 듯한, 가슴에 와닿는 관심 어린 목소리로 말했다. \"아직도 외국에 사세요?\"\n\"여전히 그래요.\"\n\n\"그리고 잘 지내시겠지요, 분명히?\"\n\n\"충분히 먹고살 만큼은 꽤 열심히 일하니까, 그래서 — 네, 잘 지내요.\"\n\n\"저는 당신을 자주 생각했어요.\" 에스텔라가 말했다.\n\n\"그랬나요?\"\n\n\"요즘 들어, 아주 자주요. 그 가치를 전혀 모른 채 제가 내던져 버린 것에 대한 기억을, 오랫동안 모질게 제게서 멀리 떼어 놓았던 시절이 있었어요. 하지만 그 기억을 받아들이는 것이 제 본분과 어긋나지 않게 된 뒤로는, 저는 그것에 제 마음속 한자리를 내주었어요.\"\n\n\"당신은 언제나 내 마음속 한자리를 차지하고 있었어요.\" 나는 대답했다.\n\n그리고 우리는 그녀가 다시 입을 열 때까지 또다시 침묵했다.\n\n\"이 자리에 작별을 고하면서,\" 에스텔라가 말했다. \"당신에게도 작별을 고하게 되리라고는 거의 생각지 못했어요. 그렇게 되어 정말 기뻐요.\"\n\n\"다시 헤어지게 되어 기쁘다는 건가요, 에스텔라? 내게 이별은 고통스러운 일이에요. 내게는 우리의 지난 이별에 대한 기억이 늘 슬프고 고통스러운 것이었어요.\"\n\n\"하지만 당신은 제게 말했잖아요.\" 에스텔라가 아주 진지하게 대답했다. \"'신의 가호가 있기를, 신께서 당신을 용서하시기를!' 하고요. 그때 제게 그렇게 말할 수 있었다면, 지금도 주저 없이 제게 그렇게 말해 주시겠지요 — 지금, 고통이 그 어떤 가르침보다도 더 강력했고, 그 고통이 당신의 마음이 어떠했는지를 제게 깨닫게 해 준 지금에 와서는요. 저는 구부러지고 부서졌어요, 하지만 — 부디 바라건대 — 더 나은 모습으로 그리되었기를요. 예전처럼 제게 사려 깊고 다정하게 대해 주세요. 그리고 우리가 친구라고 말해 주세요.\"\n\n\"우리는 친구예요.\" 그녀가 벤치에서 일어서자 나도 일어나 그녀에게로 몸을 굽히며 말했다.\n\n\"그리고 떨어져 있어도 계속 친구일 거예요.\" 에스텔라가 말했다.\n\n나는 그녀의 손을 내 손에 잡았고, 우리는 그 폐허가 된 곳을 빠져나왔다. 그리고 내가 처음 대장간을 떠날 때 아침 안개가 이미 오래전에 걷혀 올랐던 것처럼, 이제 저녁 안개가 피어오르고 있었으며, 그 안개가 내게 보여 주는 드넓게 펼쳐진 고요한 빛 속에서, 나는 그녀와 또다시 헤어지게 되리라는 그 어떤 그림자도 보지 못했다.";
if(typeof BODIES_SENT!=="undefined") BODIES_SENT["gb-1400"]=[[["My father’s family name being Pirrip, and my Christian name Philip, my infant tongue could make of both names nothing longer or more explicit than Pip.", "우리 아버지의 성이 피립이고 내 세례명이 필립이었던 까닭에, 어린 시절 내 혀로는 그 두 이름을 합쳐 핍보다 더 길거나 더 분명하게 발음할 수가 없었다."], ["So, I called myself Pip, and came to be called Pip.", "그래서 나는 스스로를 핍이라 불렀고, 남들도 나를 핍이라 부르게 되었다."]], [["I give Pirrip as my father’s family name, on the authority of his tombstone and my sister,—Mrs. Joe Gargery, who married the blacksmith.", "아버지의 성이 피립이라는 것은 그분의 묘비와 누나, 즉 대장장이와 결혼한 조 가저리 부인의 권위에 따른 것이다."], ["As I never saw my father or my mother, and never saw any likeness of either of them (for their days were long before the days of photographs), my first fancies regarding what they were like were unreasonably derived from their tombstones.", "나는 아버지도 어머니도 본 적이 없고, 두 분의 초상조차 본 적이 없었으므로(사진이 나오기 한참 전의 시절이었으니까), 두 분이 어떤 모습이었을까에 대한 내 첫 상상은 터무니없게도 그 묘비에서 비롯되었다."], ["The shape of the letters on my father’s, gave me an odd idea that he was a square, stout, dark man, with curly black hair.", "아버지 묘비에 새겨진 글자의 모양은, 그분이 네모지고 다부지며 가무잡잡한 데다 검은 고수머리를 한 사람이었으리라는 묘한 생각을 내게 심어 주었다."], ["From the character and turn of the inscription, “Also Georgiana Wife of the Above,” I drew a childish conclusion that my mother was freckled and sickly.", "\"또한 위 사람의 아내 조지애나\"라는 비명의 글씨체와 분위기로부터, 나는 어머니가 주근깨가 있고 병약했으리라는 어린아이다운 결론을 끌어냈다."], ["To five little stone lozenges, each about a foot and a half long, which were arranged in a neat row beside their grave, and were sacred to the memory of five little brothers of mine,—who gave up trying to get a living, exceedingly early in that universal struggle,—I am indebted for a belief I religiously entertained that they had all been born on their backs with their hands in their trousers-pockets, and had never taken them out in this state of existence.", "부모님의 무덤 옆에 가지런히 한 줄로 늘어선, 길이가 각각 한 자 반쯤 되는 다섯 개의 작은 마름모꼴 돌은 다섯 형제들의 추억에 바쳐진 것이었는데 — 그 형제들은 만인이 겪는 그 싸움 속에서 지극히 일찍이 살기를 포기해 버린 이들이었다 — 그 돌들 덕분에 나는, 형들이 모두 손을 바지 주머니에 찔러 넣은 채 등을 대고 태어났으며 이승에 사는 동안 한 번도 그 손을 빼지 않았으리라는 믿음을 경건하게 품게 되었다."]], [["Ours was the marsh country, down by the river, within, as the river wound, twenty miles of the sea.", "우리 고장은 강가 저 아래, 강물이 굽이쳐 흐르는 길로 바다에서 이십 마일 안쪽에 있는 습지 지방이었다."], ["My first most vivid and broad impression of the identity of things seems to me to have been gained on a memorable raw afternoon towards evening.", "사물의 정체에 대한 내 최초의 가장 또렷하고 폭넓은 인상은, 저녁이 가까워 오던 어느 잊지 못할 음산한 오후에 얻은 것이었던 듯싶다."], ["At such a time I found out for certain that this bleak place overgrown with nettles was the churchyard; and that Philip Pirrip, late of this parish, and also Georgiana wife of the above, were dead and buried; and that Alexander, Bartholomew, Abraham, Tobias, and Roger, infant children of the aforesaid, were also dead and buried; and that the dark flat wilderness beyond the churchyard, intersected with dikes and mounds and gates, with scattered cattle feeding on it, was the marshes; and that the low leaden line beyond was the river; and that the distant savage lair from which the wind was rushing was the sea; and that the small bundle of shivers growing afraid of it all and beginning to cry, was Pip.", "바로 그런 때에 나는 비로소 확실히 깨달았다. 쐐기풀이 무성하게 자란 이 황량한 곳이 교회 묘지라는 것을, 그리고 이 교구의 고인이 된 필립 피립과 또한 위 사람의 아내 조지애나가 죽어 묻혀 있다는 것을, 그리고 앞서 말한 두 사람의 어린 자식들인 알렉산더, 바돌로뮤, 에이브러햄, 토비아스, 로저 또한 죽어 묻혀 있다는 것을, 그리고 도랑과 둑과 문으로 가로질러지고 군데군데 흩어진 소 떼가 풀을 뜯고 있는, 묘지 너머의 어둡고 평평한 황야가 습지라는 것을, 그리고 그 너머의 나지막한 납빛 선이 강이라는 것을, 그리고 바람이 휘몰아쳐 나오는 저 멀리 야만스러운 소굴이 바다라는 것을, 그리고 그 모든 것이 무서워지기 시작해 울먹이는 작은 떨림 덩어리가 핍이라는 것을."]], [["“Hold your noise!”", "\"시끄러워!\""], ["cried a terrible voice, as a man started up from among the graves at the side of the church porch.", "교회 현관 옆 무덤들 사이에서 한 사내가 벌떡 일어서며 무시무시한 목소리로 외쳤다."], ["“Keep still, you little devil, or I’ll cut your throat!”", "\"가만있어, 이 작은 악마야, 안 그러면 목을 따 버리겠다!\""]], [["A fearful man, all in coarse grey, with a great iron on his leg.", "거친 잿빛 옷을 걸치고, 다리에는 커다란 쇠고랑을 찬 무서운 사내였다."], ["A man with no hat, and with broken shoes, and with an old rag tied round his head.", "모자도 없고, 신발은 다 해졌으며, 머리에는 낡은 누더기를 두른 사내였다."], ["A man who had been soaked in water, and smothered in mud, and lamed by stones, and cut by flints, and stung by nettles, and torn by briars; who limped, and shivered, and glared, and growled; and whose teeth chattered in his head as he seized me by the chin.", "물에 흠뻑 젖고, 진흙을 뒤집어쓰고, 돌에 차여 절뚝거리고, 부싯돌에 베이고, 쐐기풀에 쏘이고, 가시덤불에 찢긴 사내였다. 절뚝거리고, 부들부들 떨고, 노려보고, 으르렁대던 사내였다. 그리고 내 턱을 움켜쥐었을 때 그 입 안에서 이가 딱딱 부딪치던 사내였다."]], [["“Oh!", "\"아!"], ["Don’t cut my throat, sir,”", "제 목을 따지 마세요, 아저씨.\""], ["I pleaded in terror.", "나는 겁에 질려 애원했다."], ["“Pray don’t do it, sir.”", "\"제발 그러지 마세요, 아저씨.\""]], [["“Tell us your name!”", "\"이름을 말해 봐!\""], ["said the man. “Quick!”", "사내가 말했다. \"어서!\""]], [["“Pip, sir.”", "\"핍이에요, 아저씨.\""]], [["“Once more,”", "\"한 번 더.\""], ["said the man, staring at me.", "사내가 나를 노려보며 말했다."], ["“Give it mouth!”", "\"똑똑히 말해!\""]], [["“Pip.", "\"핍이요."], ["Pip, sir.”", "핍이에요, 아저씨.\""]], [["“Show us where you live,”", "\"어디 사는지 보여 봐.\""], ["said the man.", "사내가 말했다."], ["“Pint out the place!”", "\"그곳을 가리켜!\""]], [["I pointed to where our village lay, on the flat in-shore among the alder-trees and pollards, a mile or more from the church.", "나는 교회에서 일 마일쯤 떨어진, 오리나무와 가지치기한 나무들 사이 강가 평지에 자리 잡은 우리 마을을 가리켰다."]], [["The man, after looking at me for a moment, turned me upside down, and emptied my pockets.", "사내는 잠시 나를 바라보더니, 나를 거꾸로 뒤집어 주머니를 비워 냈다."], ["There was nothing in them but a piece of bread.", "그 안에는 빵 한 조각밖에 없었다."], ["When the church came to itself,—for he was so sudden and strong that he made it go head over heels before me, and I saw the steeple under my feet,—when the church came to itself, I say, I was seated on a high tombstone, trembling while he ate the bread ravenously.", "교회가 제자리로 돌아왔을 때 — 사내가 어찌나 갑작스럽고 힘이 셌던지 교회가 내 눈앞에서 곤두박질치고 첨탑이 내 발밑에 보일 정도였으니까 — 그러니까 교회가 제자리로 돌아왔을 때, 나는 어느 높은 묘비 위에 앉아 부들부들 떨고 있었고, 그동안 사내는 그 빵을 게걸스레 먹어 치웠다."]], [["“You young dog,”", "\"이 어린 개자식아.\""], ["said the man, licking his lips, “what fat cheeks you ha’ got.”", "사내가 입맛을 다시며 말했다. \"뺨이 어찌 그리 통통하냐.\""]], [["I believe they were fat, though I was at that time undersized for my years, and not strong.", "내 뺨이 통통했던 건 사실이라고 생각한다. 비록 그 시절 나는 나이에 비해 몸집이 작고 튼튼하지도 못했지만."]], [["“Darn me if I couldn’t eat ’em,”", "\"제기랄, 이걸 먹어 치우지 못할 것도 없지.\""], ["said the man, with a threatening shake of his head, “and if I han’t half a mind to’t!”", "사내가 머리를 위협적으로 흔들며 말했다. \"게다가 그러고 싶은 마음이 절반은 든단 말이야!\""]], [["I earnestly expressed my hope that he wouldn’t, and held tighter to the tombstone on which he had put me; partly, to keep myself upon it; partly, to keep myself from crying.", "나는 부디 그러지 말아 달라고 간절히 빌었고, 사내가 나를 앉혀 놓은 묘비를 더욱 단단히 붙들었다. 한편으로는 그 위에서 떨어지지 않으려고, 또 한편으로는 울음을 터뜨리지 않으려고."]], [["“Now lookee here!”", "\"자 이봐!\""], ["said the man.", "사내가 말했다."], ["“Where’s your mother?”", "\"네 어미는 어디 있냐?\""]], [["“There, sir!”", "\"저기요, 아저씨!\""], ["said I.", "내가 말했다."]], [["He started, made a short run, and stopped and looked over his shoulder.", "사내는 흠칫 놀라더니 잠깐 달려가다가 멈춰 서서 어깨 너머로 돌아보았다."]], [["“There, sir!”", "\"저기요, 아저씨!\""], ["I timidly explained.", "나는 머뭇거리며 설명했다."], ["“Also Georgiana.", "\"또한 조지애나."], ["That’s my mother.”", "저게 우리 엄마예요.\""]], [["“Oh!”", "\"아!\""], ["said he, coming back.", "사내가 돌아오며 말했다."], ["“And is that your father alonger your mother?”", "\"그럼 네 어미 옆에 있는 게 네 아비냐?\""]], [["“Yes, sir,”", "\"네, 아저씨.\""], ["said I; “him too; late of this parish.”", "내가 말했다. \"그분도요. 이 교구의 고인이세요.\""]], [["“Ha!”", "\"하!\""], ["he muttered then, considering.", "그러더니 사내가 곰곰이 생각하며 중얼거렸다."], ["“Who d’ye live with,—supposin’ you’re kindly let to live, which I han’t made up my mind about?”", "\"넌 누구랑 사느냐 — 살게 내버려 둔다고 치고 말이야. 아직 마음을 정하진 못했다만.\""]], [["“My sister, sir,—Mrs.", "\"누나랑 살아요, 아저씨."], ["Joe Gargery,—wife of Joe Gargery, the blacksmith, sir.”", "조 가저리 부인이요. 대장장이 조 가저리의 아내예요, 아저씨.\""]], [["“Blacksmith, eh?” said he.", "\"대장장이라, 응?\" 사내가 말했다."], ["And looked down at his leg.", "그러더니 자기 다리를 내려다보았다."]], [["After darkly looking at his leg and me several times, he came closer to my tombstone, took me by both arms, and tilted me back as far as he could hold me; so that his eyes looked most powerfully down into mine, and mine looked most helplessly up into his.", "사내는 어둡게 자기 다리와 나를 몇 번이나 번갈아 보더니, 내가 앉은 묘비로 더 가까이 다가와 두 팔을 잡고는 붙들 수 있는 데까지 나를 뒤로 젖혔다. 그래서 그의 두 눈은 더없이 강렬하게 내 눈을 내리쏘았고, 내 두 눈은 더없이 무력하게 그의 눈을 올려다보았다."]], [["“Now lookee here,”", "\"자 이봐.\""], ["he said, “the question being whether you’re to be let to live.", "사내가 말했다. \"문제는 너를 살려 두느냐 마느냐다."], ["You know what a file is?”", "줄칼이 뭔지 알지?\""]], [["“Yes, sir.”", "\"네, 아저씨.\""]], [["“And you know what wittles is?”", "\"그럼 먹을거리가 뭔지도 알지?\""]], [["“Yes, sir.”", "\"네, 아저씨.\""]], [["After each question he tilted me over a little more, so as to give me a greater sense of helplessness and danger.", "질문을 던질 때마다 사내는 나를 조금씩 더 젖혀, 더 큰 무력감과 위기감을 안겨 주었다."]], [["“You get me a file.”", "\"너는 나한테 줄칼을 가져온다.\""], ["He tilted me again.", "그가 나를 다시 젖혔다."], ["“And you get me wittles.”", "\"그리고 먹을거리를 가져온다.\""], ["He tilted me again.", "그가 다시 젖혔다."], ["“You bring ’em both to me.”", "\"그 둘을 다 나한테 가져오는 거다.\""], ["He tilted me again.", "그가 다시 젖혔다."], ["“Or I’ll have your heart and liver out.”", "\"안 그러면 네 심장과 간을 도려내겠다.\""], ["He tilted me again.", "그가 다시 젖혔다."]], [["I was dreadfully frightened, and so giddy that I clung to him with both hands, and said, “If you would kindly please to let me keep upright, sir, perhaps I shouldn’t be sick, and perhaps I could attend more.”", "나는 끔찍이도 겁이 나고 어지러워, 두 손으로 사내에게 매달리며 말했다. \"제발 저를 똑바로 세워 주시면 좋겠어요, 아저씨, 그럼 토하지 않을 것 같고, 더 잘 알아들을 수 있을 것 같아요.\""]], [["He gave me a most tremendous dip and roll, so that the church jumped over its own weathercock.", "사내가 나를 어찌나 엄청나게 푹 떨어뜨렸다가 빙 돌렸던지, 교회가 제 풍향계를 넘어 펄쩍 뛰어오를 정도였다."], ["Then, he held me by the arms, in an upright position on the top of the stone, and went on in these fearful terms:—", "그러더니 사내는 내 두 팔을 잡고 묘비 꼭대기에 똑바로 세운 채, 다음과 같은 무서운 말투로 이어 갔다."]], [["“You bring me, to-morrow morning early, that file and them wittles.", "\"내일 아침 일찍 그 줄칼과 그 먹을거리를 나한테 가져와라."], ["You bring the lot to me, at that old Battery over yonder.", "그 일습을 죄다, 저기 저 너머 오래된 포대로 가져와."], ["You do it, and you never dare to say a word or dare to make a sign concerning your having seen such a person as me, or any person sumever, and you shall be let to live.", "그것만 해내고, 나 같은 사람을 봤다거나 그 어떤 사람이든 봤다는 말이나 기색을 감히 한마디도 내비치지 않는다면, 너는 살게 될 거다."], ["You fail, or you go from my words in any partickler, no matter how small it is, and your heart and your liver shall be tore out, roasted, and ate. Now, I ain’t alone, as you may think I am.", "만약 실패하거나, 아무리 사소한 거라도 내 말에서 조금이라도 벗어난다면, 네 심장과 간은 도려내져 구워지고 먹힐 거다. 그리고 말이야, 내가 혼자라고 생각하겠지만 그렇지 않아."], ["There’s a young man hid with me, in comparison with which young man I am a Angel.", "나랑 같이 숨어 있는 젊은 놈이 하나 있는데, 그놈에 비하면 나는 천사야."], ["That young man hears the words I speak.", "그 젊은 놈은 내가 하는 말을 다 듣고 있어."], ["That young man has a secret way pecooliar to himself, of getting at a boy, and at his heart, and at his liver.", "그 젊은 놈은 사내아이한테, 그 심장한테, 그 간한테 다가가는 저만의 비밀스러운 수법을 가지고 있지."], ["It is in wain for a boy to attempt to hide himself from that young man.", "사내아이가 그 젊은 놈한테서 숨으려 해 봤자 헛수고야."], ["A boy may lock his door, may be warm in bed, may tuck himself up, may draw the clothes over his head, may think himself comfortable and safe, but that young man will softly creep and creep his way to him and tear him open.", "아이가 문을 잠그고, 이불 속에서 따뜻하게 몸을 웅크리고, 옷을 머리까지 끌어 덮고 제 딴엔 편안하고 안전하다 여겨도, 그 젊은 놈은 살그머니 기고 또 기어서 그 아이한테 다가가 배를 찢어 버릴 거다."], ["I am a keeping that young man from harming of you at the present moment, with great difficulty.", "나는 지금 이 순간 그 젊은 놈이 너를 해치지 못하게 가까스로 막고 있는 거야."], ["I find it wery hard to hold that young man off of your inside.", "그 젊은 놈을 네 뱃속에서 떼어 놓기가 여간 힘든 게 아니다."], ["Now, what do you say?”", "자, 이제 어떻게 할 거냐?\""]], [["I said that I would get him the file, and I would get him what broken bits of food I could, and I would come to him at the Battery, early in the morning.", "나는 줄칼을 가져다 드리겠다고, 그리고 먹다 남은 음식이든 뭐든 구할 수 있는 만큼 가져다 드리겠다고, 그리고 아침 일찍 포대로 찾아가겠다고 말했다."]], [["“Say Lord strike you dead if you don’t!”", "\"안 그러면 벼락 맞아 뒈진다고 말해!\""], ["said the man.", "사내가 말했다."]], [["I said so, and he took me down.", "나는 그렇게 말했고, 사내는 나를 내려 주었다."]], [["“Now,”", "\"이제.\""], ["he pursued, “you remember what you’ve undertook, and you remember that young man, and you get home!”", "사내가 말을 이었다. \"네가 약속한 걸 잊지 말고, 그 젊은 놈도 잊지 말고, 어서 집에 가!\""]], [["“Goo-good night, sir,”", "\"아, 안녕히 계세요, 아저씨.\""], ["I faltered.", "나는 더듬더듬 말했다."]], [["“Much of that!”", "\"안녕은 무슨!\""], ["said he, glancing about him over the cold wet flat.", "사내가 차갑고 축축한 평지를 둘러보며 말했다."], ["“I wish I was a frog. Or a eel!”", "\"내가 개구리였으면 좋겠다. 아니면 뱀장어든지!\""]], [["At the same time, he hugged his shuddering body in both his arms,—clasping himself, as if to hold himself together,—and limped towards the low church wall.", "그와 동시에 사내는 부들부들 떠는 제 몸을 두 팔로 끌어안았다 — 몸이 흩어지지 않게 붙들어 두려는 듯 제 몸을 꽉 껴안고는 — 나지막한 교회 담장을 향해 절뚝거리며 갔다."], ["As I saw him go, picking his way among the nettles, and among the brambles that bound the green mounds, he looked in my young eyes as if he were eluding the hands of the dead people, stretching up cautiously out of their graves, to get a twist upon his ankle and pull him in.", "사내가 쐐기풀 사이로, 그리고 푸른 둑을 동여맨 가시덤불 사이로 길을 헤쳐 가는 모습을 보고 있자니, 어린 내 눈에는 마치 그가 죽은 자들의 손길을 피해 가는 것처럼 보였다. 무덤 속에서 조심스레 뻗어 나와 그의 발목을 비틀어 끌어들이려는 손길을."]], [["When he came to the low church wall, he got over it, like a man whose legs were numbed and stiff, and then turned round to look for me.", "나지막한 교회 담장에 이르자 사내는, 다리가 마비되어 뻣뻣해진 사람처럼 그것을 넘어가더니, 나를 찾으려고 몸을 돌렸다."], ["When I saw him turning, I set my face towards home, and made the best use of my legs.", "그가 돌아서는 것을 보자 나는 집 쪽으로 얼굴을 향하고 두 다리를 한껏 놀려 내달렸다."], ["But presently I looked over my shoulder, and saw him going on again towards the river, still hugging himself in both arms, and picking his way with his sore feet among the great stones dropped into the marshes here and there, for stepping-places when the rains were heavy or the tide was in.", "그러나 이내 어깨 너머로 돌아보니, 사내는 여전히 두 팔로 제 몸을 끌어안고, 비가 많이 오거나 밀물이 들 때 디딤돌로 쓰려고 습지 여기저기에 떨어뜨려 놓은 커다란 돌들 사이로 성한 데 없는 발을 디뎌 가며, 다시금 강 쪽으로 가고 있었다."]], [["The marshes were just a long black horizontal line then, as I stopped to look after him; and the river was just another horizontal line, not nearly so broad nor yet so black; and the sky was just a row of long angry red lines and dense black lines intermixed.", "내가 멈춰 서서 사내의 뒷모습을 바라볼 때, 습지는 그저 길고 검은 수평선 하나였고, 강은 또 하나의 수평선일 뿐이었으나 그만큼 넓지도 그만큼 검지도 않았으며, 하늘은 그저 길게 성난 붉은 선과 짙은 검은 선이 뒤섞여 늘어선 한 줄이었다."], ["On the edge of the river I could faintly make out the only two black things in all the prospect that seemed to be standing upright; one of these was the beacon by which the sailors steered,—like an unhooped cask upon a pole,—an ugly thing when you were near it; the other, a gibbet, with some chains hanging to it which had once held a pirate.", "강가에는 그 모든 풍경 가운데 유일하게 똑바로 서 있는 듯한 두 개의 검은 물체가 어렴풋이 보였다. 그중 하나는 뱃사람들이 방향을 잡는 데 쓰는 표지등이었는데, 장대 위에 얹힌 테 없는 통 같았으니, 가까이서 보면 흉한 물건이었다. 다른 하나는 교수대였는데, 한때 어느 해적을 매달아 두었던 사슬 몇 가닥이 거기 늘어져 있었다."], ["The man was limping on towards this latter, as if he were the pirate come to life, and come down, and going back to hook himself up again.", "사내는 마치 그 해적이 되살아나 내려왔다가 다시 제 몸을 매달러 돌아가는 것처럼, 그 교수대 쪽으로 절뚝절뚝 가고 있었다."], ["It gave me a terrible turn when I thought so; and as I saw the cattle lifting their heads to gaze after him, I wondered whether they thought so too.", "그런 생각이 들자 나는 끔찍한 전율에 휩싸였다. 그리고 소 떼가 고개를 들어 사내의 뒷모습을 바라보는 것을 보면서, 저들도 같은 생각을 하는 걸까 싶었다."], ["I looked all round for the horrible young man, and could see no signs of him.", "나는 그 무서운 젊은 놈이 있는지 사방을 둘러보았으나, 그자의 기척은 어디에도 보이지 않았다."], ["But now I was frightened again, and ran home without stopping.", "그러나 이제 다시 겁이 난 나는, 멈추지 않고 집으로 내달렸다."]], [["# Chapter II.", "# Chapter II."]], [["My sister, Mrs. Joe Gargery, was more than twenty years older than I, and had established a great reputation with herself and the neighbours because she had brought me up “by hand.”", "누나, 즉 조 가저리 부인은 나보다 스무 살도 넘게 위였으며, 나를 \"손수\" 길러 냈다는 이유로 자기 자신에게나 이웃들에게나 대단한 명성을 쌓아 두고 있었다."], ["Having at that time to find out for myself what the expression meant, and knowing her to have a hard and heavy hand, and to be much in the habit of laying it upon her husband as well as upon me, I supposed that Joe Gargery and I were both brought up by hand.", "그 시절 나는 그 표현이 무슨 뜻인지 스스로 알아내야만 했는데, 누나의 손이 단단하고 묵직하며 그 손을 나뿐만 아니라 남편에게도 곧잘 갖다 대는 버릇이 있음을 알고 있었으므로, 나는 조 가저리와 나 둘 다 손수 길러진 것이려니 짐작했다."]], [["She was not a good-looking woman, my sister; and I had a general impression that she must have made Joe Gargery marry her by hand.", "누나는 미인이 아니었다. 그래서 나는 누나가 조 가저리로 하여금 자기와 결혼하도록 손수 만든 것이리라는 막연한 인상을 품고 있었다."], ["Joe was a fair man, with curls of flaxen hair on each side of his smooth face, and with eyes of such a very undecided blue that they seemed to have somehow got mixed with their own whites.", "조는 살결이 흰 사내로, 매끈한 얼굴 양옆으로 아마빛 곱슬머리가 드리워 있었고, 두 눈은 어찌나 분간하기 어려운 푸른빛이었던지 어쩐지 제 흰자위와 뒤섞여 버린 것처럼 보일 정도였다."], ["He was a mild, good-natured, sweet-tempered, easy-going, foolish, dear fellow,—a sort of Hercules in strength, and also in weakness.", "그는 온순하고, 성품이 좋고, 마음씨가 곱고, 무던하고, 어리숙하고, 사랑스러운 사람이었으니 — 힘에 있어서나 또한 약함에 있어서나 일종의 헤라클레스 같은 인물이었다."]], [["My sister, Mrs. Joe, with black hair and eyes, had such a prevailing redness of skin that I sometimes used to wonder whether it was possible she washed herself with a nutmeg-grater instead of soap.", "검은 머리에 검은 눈을 한 누나, 조 부인은 살결이 어찌나 온통 붉던지, 나는 이따금 저 사람이 비누 대신 육두구 강판으로 몸을 씻는 게 아닐까 하는 생각마저 하곤 했다."], ["She was tall and bony, and almost always wore a coarse apron, fastened over her figure behind with two loops, and having a square impregnable bib in front, that was stuck full of pins and needles.", "누나는 키가 크고 뼈마디가 굵었으며, 거의 언제나 거친 앞치마를 둘렀는데, 그것은 등 뒤에서 두 개의 고리로 몸에 동여매여 있었고, 앞쪽에는 핀과 바늘이 잔뜩 꽂힌, 네모지고 난공불락의 가슴받이가 달려 있었다."], ["She made it a powerful merit in herself, and a strong reproach against Joe, that she wore this apron so much.", "누나는 이 앞치마를 그토록 자주 두르는 것을 스스로에게는 대단한 미덕으로, 조에게는 강한 책망거리로 삼았다."], ["Though I really see no reason why she should have worn it at all; or why, if she did wear it at all, she should not have taken it off, every day of her life.", "하지만 나로서는 누나가 도대체 왜 그 앞치마를 두를 필요가 있었는지, 또 정 두른다 치더라도 어째서 평생 단 하루도 벗어 두지 않았는지 도무지 까닭을 알 수 없다."]], [["Joe’s forge adjoined our house, which was a wooden house, as many of the dwellings in our country were,—most of them, at that time.", "조의 대장간은 우리 집과 붙어 있었는데, 우리 집은 목조 가옥이었으니, 그 시절 우리 고장의 살림집이 대개 그러했듯이 — 거의 다 그러했다."], ["When I ran home from the churchyard, the forge was shut up, and Joe was sitting alone in the kitchen.", "내가 교회 묘지에서 집으로 달려왔을 때 대장간은 닫혀 있었고, 조는 부엌에 홀로 앉아 있었다."], ["Joe and I being fellow-sufferers, and having confidences as such, Joe imparted a confidence to me, the moment I raised the latch of the door and peeped in at him opposite to it, sitting in the chimney corner.", "조와 나는 함께 고통받는 동지로서 그런 처지에 어울리는 은밀한 마음을 나누는 사이였으므로, 내가 문빗장을 들어 올리고 맞은편 벽난로 구석에 앉아 있는 그를 들여다본 바로 그 순간, 조는 내게 속내를 털어놓았다."]], [["“Mrs. Joe has been out a dozen times, looking for you, Pip.", "\"조 부인이 너 찾으러 열두 번이나 나갔다 왔다, 핍."], ["And she’s out now, making it a baker’s dozen.”", "그리고 지금도 나가 있으니 합쳐서 열세 번째지.\""]], [["“Is she?”", "\"그래요?\""]], [["“Yes, Pip,”", "\"그래, 핍.\""], ["said Joe; “and what’s worse, she’s got Tickler with her.”", "조가 말했다. \"게다가 더 나쁜 건, 간질이 막대기까지 들고 갔다는 거야.\""]], [["At this dismal intelligence, I twisted the only button on my waistcoat round and round, and looked in great depression at the fire.", "이 우울한 소식에 나는 조끼에 달린 단 하나뿐인 단추를 빙글빙글 돌리며, 깊은 낙담에 잠겨 불을 바라보았다."], ["Tickler was a wax-ended piece of cane, worn smooth by collision with my tickled frame.", "간질이 막대기란 끝에 밀랍을 입힌 회초리로, 간질여지는 내 몸뚱이와 부딪히는 통에 반들반들 닳아 있었다."]], [["“She sot down,”", "\"누나가 앉았다가.\""], ["said Joe, “and she got up, and she made a grab at Tickler, and she Ram-paged out.", "조가 말했다. \"다시 일어나서, 간질이 막대기를 움켜쥐더니, 길길이 날뛰며 나갔어."], ["That’s what she did,”", "그렇게 하더라고.\""], ["said Joe, slowly clearing the fire between the lower bars with the poker, and looking at it; “she Ram-paged out, Pip.”", "조가 부지깽이로 아래쪽 살 사이의 불을 천천히 헤집으며 그것을 바라보고 말했다. \"길길이 날뛰며 나갔단 말이다, 핍.\""]], [["“Has she been gone long, Joe?”", "\"나간 지 오래됐어요, 조?\""], ["I always treated him as a larger species of child, and as no more than my equal.", "나는 늘 그를 더 큰 종류의 아이로, 그러니까 내 동무 정도로 대했다."]], [["“Well,”", "\"글쎄.\""], ["said Joe, glancing up at the Dutch clock, “she’s been on the Ram-page, this last spell, about five minutes, Pip. She’s a-coming!", "조가 네덜란드제 괘종시계를 흘긋 올려다보며 말했다. \"이번에 길길이 날뛰러 나간 지는 한 오 분쯤 됐다, 핍. 오는구나!"], ["Get behind the door, old chap, and have the jack-towel betwixt you.”", "문 뒤로 가, 이 친구야, 그리고 닦기 수건을 사이에 두고 있어라.\""]], [["I took the advice.", "나는 그 충고를 따랐다."], ["My sister, Mrs. Joe, throwing the door wide open, and finding an obstruction behind it, immediately divined the cause, and applied Tickler to its further investigation.", "누나, 조 부인은 문을 활짝 열어젖혔다가 그 뒤에 무언가 걸리는 것이 있음을 깨닫고는 곧장 그 원인을 알아채고, 간질이 막대기를 들이밀어 더 깊이 조사에 착수했다."], ["She concluded by throwing me—I often served as a connubial missile—at Joe, who, glad to get hold of me on any terms, passed me on into the chimney and quietly fenced me up there with his great leg.", "그러고는 마무리로 나를 — 나는 종종 부부 싸움의 발사체 노릇을 했다 — 조에게 내던졌는데, 조는 어떤 조건으로든 나를 붙들게 되어 기뻐하며 나를 벽난로 안쪽으로 받아 넘기고는 자기 굵은 다리로 조용히 거기에 나를 가두어 막아 주었다."]], [["“Where have you been, you young monkey?”", "\"어디 갔다 왔어, 이 어린 원숭이 녀석아?\""], ["said Mrs. Joe, stamping her foot.", "조 부인이 발을 구르며 말했다."], ["“Tell me directly what you’ve been doing to wear me away with fret and fright and worrit, or I’d have you out of that corner if you was fifty Pips, and he was five hundred Gargerys.”", "\"당장 말해, 사람을 이렇게 애태우고 겁주고 졸이게 해서 진을 빼 놓으니 네가 무슨 짓을 하고 다녔는지. 안 그러면 네가 핍이 쉰 명이고 저자가 가저리가 오백 명이라 해도 그 구석에서 끄집어내고 말겠다.\""]], [["“I have only been to the churchyard,”", "\"그냥 교회 묘지에 갔다 왔을 뿐이에요.\""], ["said I, from my stool, crying and rubbing myself.", "나는 걸상에 앉아 울며 몸을 문지르고서 말했다."]], [["“Churchyard!”", "\"교회 묘지라!\""], ["repeated my sister.", "누나가 되받았다."], ["“If it warn’t for me you’d have been to the churchyard long ago, and stayed there.", "\"내가 아니었으면 넌 진작에 교회 묘지에 갔다가 거기 눌러앉았을 거다."], ["Who brought you up by hand?”", "누가 너를 손수 키웠어?\""]], [["“You did,”", "\"누나가요.\""], ["said I.", "내가 말했다."]], [["“And why did I do it, I should like to know?”", "\"그래, 내가 왜 그랬는지, 그것 좀 알고 싶구나!\""], ["exclaimed my sister.", "누나가 소리쳤다."]], [["I whimpered, “I don’t know.”", "나는 훌쩍이며 말했다. \"모르겠어요.\""]], [["“I don’t!” said my sister.", "\"나도 모르겠다!\" 누나가 말했다."], ["“I’d never do it again!", "\"다시는 안 할 거다!"], ["I know that.", "그것만은 분명해."], ["I may truly say I’ve never had this apron of mine off since born you were.", "정말이지 너를 낳은 이후로 이 앞치마를 단 한 번도 벗어 본 적이 없다고 말할 수 있다."], ["It’s bad enough to be a blacksmith’s wife (and him a Gargery) without being your mother.”", "대장장이의 아내라는 것만으로도(게다가 가저리라는 작자의) 고된 노릇인데, 거기다 네 어미 노릇까지 하고 있으니.\""]], [["My thoughts strayed from that question as I looked disconsolately at the fire.", "나는 침울하게 불을 바라보면서 그 물음에서 생각이 빗나가 버렸다."], ["For the fugitive out on the marshes with the ironed leg, the mysterious young man, the file, the food, and the dreadful pledge I was under to commit a larceny on those sheltering premises, rose before me in the avenging coals.", "다리에 쇠고랑을 찬 채 습지에 나가 있는 도망자, 그 수수께끼 같은 젊은 놈, 줄칼, 먹을거리, 그리고 이 나를 거두어 주는 집에서 도둑질을 저지르기로 한 그 무서운 맹세가, 복수의 화염처럼 이글거리는 석탄 속에서 내 앞에 떠올랐던 것이다."]], [["“Hah!”", "\"하!\""], ["said Mrs. Joe, restoring Tickler to his station.", "조 부인이 간질이 막대기를 제자리에 도로 꽂으며 말했다."], ["“Churchyard, indeed!", "\"교회 묘지라니, 어디 보자!"], ["You may well say churchyard, you two.” One of us, by the by, had not said it at all.", "너희 둘 다 교회 묘지 운운할 만하지.\" 그런데 그건 그렇고, 우리 둘 중 하나는 그런 말을 입 밖에 낸 적조차 없었다."], ["“You’ll drive me to the churchyard betwixt you, one of these days, and O, a pr-r-recious pair you’d be without me!”", "\"너희 둘이서 작당해 머잖아 나를 교회 묘지로 몰아넣고 말 게다. 오, 나 없으면 너희는 참으로 한심한 한 쌍이 되겠지!\""]], [["As she applied herself to set the tea-things, Joe peeped down at me over his leg, as if he were mentally casting me and himself up, and calculating what kind of pair we practically should make, under the grievous circumstances foreshadowed.", "누나가 찻그릇을 차리는 일에 매달리는 동안, 조는 제 다리 너머로 나를 슬쩍 내려다보았다. 마치 나와 자기를 머릿속으로 셈해 보면서, 앞서 예고된 그 비참한 상황 속에서 우리가 실제로 어떤 한 쌍이 될지를 가늠해 보는 듯했다."], ["After that, he sat feeling his right-side flaxen curls and whisker, and following Mrs. Joe about with his blue eyes, as his manner always was at squally times.", "그러고는 자기 오른쪽 아마빛 곱슬머리와 구레나룻을 만지작거리며, 푸른 눈으로 부엌을 오가는 조 부인을 좇았으니, 이는 풍파가 일 때면 늘 보이는 그의 버릇이었다."]], [["My sister had a trenchant way of cutting our bread and butter for us, that never varied.", "누나에게는 우리에게 줄 버터 바른 빵을 써는 단호한 방식이 있었는데, 그것은 한 번도 달라지는 법이 없었다."], ["First, with her left hand she jammed the loaf hard and fast against her bib,—where it sometimes got a pin into it, and sometimes a needle, which we afterwards got into our mouths.", "먼저 왼손으로 빵 덩어리를 가슴받이에 대고 세게 꽉 눌렀다 — 그러면 거기 꽂힌 핀이, 때로는 바늘이 빵에 박혀 들었고, 우리는 나중에 그것을 입속에 넣게 되곤 했다."], ["Then she took some butter (not too much) on a knife and spread it on the loaf, in an apothecary kind of way, as if she were making a plaster,—using both sides of the knife with a slapping dexterity, and trimming and moulding the butter off round the crust.", "그런 다음 칼로 버터를 조금(너무 많지 않게) 떠서, 마치 고약을 바르는 듯한 약제사 같은 솜씨로 빵에 펴 발랐는데 — 찰싹찰싹 능란하게 칼의 양면을 다 써 가며, 껍질 가장자리를 빙 둘러 버터를 다듬고 매만졌다."], ["Then, she gave the knife a final smart wipe on the edge of the plaster, and then sawed a very thick round off the loaf: which she finally, before separating from the loaf, hewed into two halves, of which Joe got one, and I the other.", "그러고는 고약 가장자리에 칼을 마지막으로 한 번 야무지게 쓱 닦고, 빵 덩어리에서 아주 두툼한 한 조각을 톱질하듯 잘라 냈다. 그리고 그것을 덩어리에서 완전히 떼어 내기 전에 끝으로 둘로 쪼개어, 그중 하나는 조가, 다른 하나는 내가 받았다."]], [["On the present occasion, though I was hungry, I dared not eat my slice.", "이번에는 배가 고팠으나 나는 감히 내 몫을 먹을 수가 없었다."], ["I felt that I must have something in reserve for my dreadful acquaintance, and his ally the still more dreadful young man.", "그 무서운 친구와, 그의 동맹자인 한층 더 무서운 젊은 놈을 위해 무언가 비축해 두어야 한다고 느꼈던 것이다."], ["I knew Mrs. Joe’s housekeeping to be of the strictest kind, and that my larcenous researches might find nothing available in the safe.", "나는 조 부인의 살림 솜씨가 극도로 빈틈없다는 것을, 그래서 도둑질하듯 뒤져 보아도 찬장에서 건질 만한 것이 아무것도 없으리라는 것을 알고 있었다."], ["Therefore I resolved to put my hunk of bread and butter down the leg of my trousers.", "그래서 나는 버터 바른 빵 덩어리를 바지 가랑이 속으로 떨어뜨려 두기로 결심했다."]], [["The effort of resolution necessary to the achievement of this purpose I found to be quite awful.", "이 목적을 이루는 데 필요한 결심의 노력이 어찌나 끔찍했던지."], ["It was as if I had to make up my mind to leap from the top of a high house, or plunge into a great depth of water.", "그것은 마치 높은 집 꼭대기에서 뛰어내리거나, 깊은 물속으로 곤두박질치기로 마음먹어야 하는 것과도 같았다."], ["And it was made the more difficult by the unconscious Joe.", "게다가 아무것도 모르는 조 때문에 그 일은 한층 더 어려워졌다."], ["In our already-mentioned freemasonry as fellow-sufferers, and in his good-natured companionship with me, it was our evening habit to compare the way we bit through our slices, by silently holding them up to each other’s admiration now and then,—which stimulated us to new exertions.", "앞서 말한 대로 함께 고통받는 동지로서의 우리만의 비밀 결사 속에서, 또 그가 내게 베푸는 다정한 우정 속에서, 우리는 저녁마다 빵 조각을 어떻게 베어 무는지 견주어 보는 버릇이 있었다. 이따금 말없이 서로에게 빵을 들어 보여 감탄을 자아내는 식이었는데 — 그것이 우리를 새로운 분발로 자극하곤 했다."], ["To-night, Joe several times invited me, by the display of his fast diminishing slice, to enter upon our usual friendly competition; but he found me, each time, with my yellow mug of tea on one knee, and my untouched bread and butter on the other.", "오늘 밤에도 조는 빠르게 줄어드는 자기 빵 조각을 내보이며, 평소의 다정한 경쟁에 들어서자고 여러 번 나를 부추겼다. 그러나 그는 그때마다, 한쪽 무릎에는 노란 찻잔을, 다른 쪽 무릎에는 손도 대지 않은 버터 바른 빵을 올려놓고 있는 나를 발견할 따름이었다."], ["At last, I desperately considered that the thing I contemplated must be done, and that it had best be done in the least improbable manner consistent with the circumstances.", "마침내 나는, 내가 꾀하던 일을 반드시 해치워야 하며, 형편에 맞게 가장 덜 이상해 보이는 방식으로 해내는 것이 상책이라고 필사적으로 마음을 정했다."], ["I took advantage of a moment when Joe had just looked at me, and got my bread and butter down my leg.", "나는 조가 막 나를 쳐다본 순간을 틈타, 버터 바른 빵을 바지 가랑이 속으로 내려보냈다."]], [["Joe was evidently made uncomfortable by what he supposed to be my loss of appetite, and took a thoughtful bite out of his slice, which he didn’t seem to enjoy.", "조는 내가 입맛을 잃은 것이라 짐작하고는 분명 마음이 불편해진 듯, 자기 빵 조각을 생각에 잠겨 한입 베어 물었으나 도무지 즐기는 기색이 아니었다."], ["He turned it about in his mouth much longer than usual, pondering over it a good deal, and after all gulped it down like a pill.", "그는 그것을 평소보다 훨씬 오래 입안에서 굴리며 한참 곰곰이 생각하더니, 결국 알약 삼키듯 꿀꺽 넘겼다."], ["He was about to take another bite, and had just got his head on one side for a good purchase on it, when his eye fell on me, and he saw that my bread and butter was gone.", "그가 또 한입 베어 물려고 빵을 단단히 물기 좋게 고개를 한쪽으로 막 기울였을 때, 그의 눈이 내게 떨어졌고, 그는 내 버터 바른 빵이 사라진 것을 보았다."]], [["The wonder and consternation with which Joe stopped on the threshold of his bite and stared at me, were too evident to escape my sister’s observation.", "조가 베어 물려던 동작을 멈추고 나를 빤히 쳐다보던 그 경악과 당혹은 너무도 역력하여, 누나의 관찰을 피해 갈 수 없었다."]], [["“What’s the matter now?”", "\"이번엔 또 무슨 일이야?\""], ["said she, smartly, as she put down her cup.", "누나가 찻잔을 내려놓으며 매섭게 말했다."]], [["“I say, you know!” muttered Joe, shaking his head at me in very serious remonstrance.", "\"이거 봐, 너 말이다!\" 조가 자못 진지하게 나무라듯 내게 고개를 내저으며 중얼거렸다."], ["“Pip, old chap!", "\"핍, 이 친구야!"], ["You’ll do yourself a mischief.", "너 그러다 큰일 난다."], ["It’ll stick somewhere.", "어딘가에 걸릴 거야."], ["You can’t have chawed it, Pip.”", "그걸 다 씹었을 리가 없잖아, 핍.\""]], [["“What’s the matter now?” repeated my sister, more sharply than before.", "\"이번엔 또 무슨 일이냐니까?\" 누나가 아까보다 더 날카롭게 되물었다."]], [["“If you can cough any trifle on it up, Pip, I’d recommend you to do it,”", "\"조금이라도 그걸 기침으로 게워 낼 수 있거든, 핍, 그렇게 하라고 권하고 싶다.\""], ["said Joe, all aghast. “Manners is manners, but still your elth’s your elth.”", "조가 잔뜩 겁에 질려 말했다. \"예의는 예의지만, 그래도 건강은 건강이니까.\""]], [["By this time, my sister was quite desperate, so she pounced on Joe, and, taking him by the two whiskers, knocked his head for a little while against the wall behind him, while I sat in the corner, looking guiltily on.", "이쯤 되자 누나는 완전히 격분하여, 조에게 달려들어 양쪽 구레나룻을 움켜쥐고는 잠시 그의 머리를 뒤쪽 벽에다 쿵쿵 찧어 댔고, 그동안 나는 구석에 앉아 죄책감에 사로잡혀 지켜보았다."]], [["“Now, perhaps you’ll mention what’s the matter,”", "\"자, 이제는 무슨 일인지 말할 테냐.\""], ["said my sister, out of breath, “you staring great stuck pig.”", "누나가 숨을 헐떡이며 말했다. \"이 멀뚱히 쳐다보기만 하는 큰 멧돼지 같은 작자야.\""]], [["Joe looked at her in a helpless way, then took a helpless bite, and looked at me again.", "조는 어쩔 줄 모르는 얼굴로 누나를 바라보더니, 어쩔 줄 모르고 한입 베어 물고는, 다시 나를 바라보았다."]], [["“You know, Pip,”", "\"있잖아, 핍.\""], ["said Joe, solemnly, with his last bite in his cheek, and speaking in a confidential voice, as if we two were quite alone, “you and me is always friends, and I’d be the last to tell upon you, any time.", "조가 마지막 한입을 볼에 문 채 엄숙하게, 마치 우리 둘만 단둘이 있는 양 은밀한 목소리로 말했다. \"너랑 나는 언제나 친구고, 나는 무슨 일이 있어도 절대로 너를 일러바칠 사람이 아니야. 하지만 그런 — \""], ["But such a—” he moved his chair and looked about the floor between us, and then again at me—“such a most oncommon Bolt as that!”", "그는 의자를 옮기며 우리 사이의 바닥을 둘러보더니 다시 나를 바라보았다 — \"그토록 보기 드물게 한꺼번에 꿀꺽 삼키는 짓이라니!\""]], [["“Been bolting his food, has he?”", "\"그 녀석이 음식을 통째로 삼켰단 말이야?\""], ["cried my sister.", "누나가 소리쳤다."]], [["“You know, old chap,”", "\"있잖아, 이 친구야.\""], ["said Joe, looking at me, and not at Mrs.", "조가 조 부인이 아니라 나를 바라보며, 여전히 한입을 볼에 문 채 말했다."], ["Joe, with his bite still in his cheek, “I Bolted, myself, when I was your age—frequent—and as a boy I’ve been among a many Bolters; but I never see your Bolting equal yet, Pip, and it’s a mercy you ain’t Bolted dead.”", "\"나도 네 나이 때는 통째로 삼키곤 했지 — 자주 그랬어 — 어릴 적에 통째로 삼키는 녀석들 틈에서 자랐다만, 너처럼 삼키는 건 여태 한 번도 못 봤다, 핍. 통째로 삼키다 죽지 않은 게 천만다행이야.\""]], [["My sister made a dive at me, and fished me up by the hair, saying nothing more than the awful words, “You come along and be dosed.”", "누나가 내게 달려들어 머리채를 잡고 나를 낚아 올리고는, \"이리 와서 약 좀 먹어야겠다\"라는 그 무시무시한 말 외에는 아무 말도 하지 않았다."]], [["Some medical beast had revived Tar-water in those days as a fine medicine, and Mrs. Joe always kept a supply of it in the cupboard; having a belief in its virtues correspondent to its nastiness.", "그 시절 어느 돌팔이 같은 작자가 타르 물을 훌륭한 약이랍시고 다시 유행시켜 놓은 터라, 조 부인은 늘 그것을 찬장에 갖춰 두고 있었으니, 그 약효를 고약함에 걸맞게 굳게 믿고 있었던 것이다."], ["At the best of times, so much of this elixir was administered to me as a choice restorative, that I was conscious of going about, smelling like a new fence.", "형편이 가장 좋을 때조차도 이 영약은 좋은 보양제랍시고 내게 어찌나 많이 투여되었던지, 나는 갓 칠한 울타리 같은 냄새를 풍기며 돌아다니고 있다는 것을 스스로 의식할 정도였다."], ["On this particular evening the urgency of my case demanded a pint of this mixture, which was poured down my throat, for my greater comfort, while Mrs. Joe held my head under her arm, as a boot would be held in a bootjack.", "바로 그날 밤은 내 사정이 위급하다 하여 이 혼합물 한 파인트가 필요했고, 그것은 더 큰 안락을 위해 내 목구멍으로 들이부어졌는데, 그러는 동안 조 부인은 마치 구둣주걱에 장화를 끼우듯 내 머리를 제 겨드랑이에 끼고 있었다."], ["Joe got off with half a pint; but was made to swallow that (much to his disturbance, as he sat slowly munching and meditating before the fire), “because he had had a turn.”", "조는 반 파인트로 모면했으나, 그것마저 삼켜야 했으니(\"그 녀석도 한 차례 발작이 있었으니까\"라는 이유에서였는데, 그는 불 앞에 앉아 천천히 우물거리며 생각에 잠긴 채 무척이나 곤혹스러워했다)."], ["Judging from myself, I should say he certainly had a turn afterwards, if he had had none before.", "내 경우로 미루어 보건대, 그가 그 전에 발작이 없었다 하더라도 그 후에는 분명 한 차례 있었으리라고 말해 두고 싶다."]], [["Conscience is a dreadful thing when it accuses man or boy; but when, in the case of a boy, that secret burden co-operates with another secret burden down the leg of his trousers, it is (as I can testify) a great punishment.", "양심이란 어른이든 아이든 사람을 책망할 때면 무서운 것이다. 그러나 아이의 경우, 그 은밀한 짐이 바지 가랑이 속에 든 또 하나의 은밀한 짐과 합세할 때면, 그것은 (내가 증언할 수 있거니와) 크나큰 형벌이 된다."], ["The guilty knowledge that I was going to rob Mrs. Joe—I never thought I was going to rob Joe, for I never thought of any of the housekeeping property as his—united to the necessity of always keeping one hand on my bread and butter as I sat, or when I was ordered about the kitchen on any small errand, almost drove me out of my mind.", "내가 조 부인의 것을 훔치려 한다는 죄스러운 자각이 — 나는 조의 것을 훔친다고는 한 번도 생각지 않았으니, 살림살이 재산 가운데 어느 것도 그의 것이라 여겨 본 적이 없었던 까닭이다 — 앉아 있을 때나 부엌에서 자잘한 심부름으로 이리저리 부려질 때나 늘 한 손은 버터 바른 빵에 얹어 두어야 하는 필요와 한데 어우러져, 나는 거의 미칠 지경이었다."], ["Then, as the marsh winds made the fire glow and flare, I thought I heard the voice outside, of the man with the iron on his leg who had sworn me to secrecy, declaring that he couldn’t and wouldn’t starve until to-morrow, but must be fed now.", "그러다가 습지의 바람이 불을 이글거리며 타오르게 하면, 나는 다리에 쇠고랑을 차고 내게 비밀을 맹세시킨 그 사내의 목소리가 밖에서 들려오는 것만 같았으니, 내일까지는 굶을 수 없고 굶지도 않겠다, 지금 당장 먹어야겠다고 외치는 소리였다."], ["At other times, I thought, What if the young man who was with so much difficulty restrained from imbruing his hands in me should yield to a constitutional impatience, or should mistake the time, and should think himself accredited to my heart and liver to-night, instead of to-morrow!", "또 어떤 때에는 이런 생각이 들었다. 그 젊은 놈이, 가까스로 제 손을 내 몸에 적시지 못하게 막아 두었던 그자가, 타고난 조급함에 못 이기거나 시간을 잘못 알아서, 내일이 아니라 오늘 밤 내 심장과 간에 손을 댈 권한을 얻었다고 제멋대로 여기면 어쩌나!"], ["If ever anybody’s hair stood on end with terror, mine must have done so then. But, perhaps, nobody’s ever did?", "만일 누군가 공포에 머리카락이 곤두선 적이 있다면, 그때 내 머리카락이 분명 그러했으리라. 하지만 어쩌면, 그 누구도 실제로 그런 적은 없었을까?"]], [["It was Christmas Eve, and I had to stir the pudding for next day, with a copper-stick, from seven to eight by the Dutch clock.", "크리스마스이브였고, 나는 이튿날 먹을 푸딩을 구리 막대기로 저어야 했으니, 네덜란드제 괘종시계로 일곱 시부터 여덟 시까지였다."], ["I tried it with the load upon my leg (and that made me think afresh of the man with the load on his leg), and found the tendency of exercise to bring the bread and butter out at my ankle, quite unmanageable.", "나는 다리에 짐을 얹은 채 그 일을 해 보았는데(그러자 자기 다리에 짐을 얹은 그 사내가 새삼 떠올랐다), 몸을 움직이는 통에 버터 바른 빵이 발목께로 자꾸 비어져 나오려는 성향이 도무지 감당이 안 됨을 알았다."], ["Happily I slipped away, and deposited that part of my conscience in my garret bedroom.", "다행히 나는 슬그머니 빠져나가, 내 양심의 그 한 부분을 다락방 침실에 갖다 두었다."]], [["“Hark!”", "\"들어 봐요!\""], ["said I, when I had done my stirring, and was taking a final warm in the chimney corner before being sent up to bed; “was that great guns, Joe?”", "젓는 일을 마치고, 잠자리로 보내지기 전 벽난로 구석에서 마지막으로 몸을 녹이고 있던 나는 말했다. \"저거 대포 소리였어요, 조?\""]], [["“Ah!”", "\"아!\""], ["said Joe.", "조가 말했다."], ["“There’s another conwict off.”", "\"또 죄수 하나가 달아났구나.\""]], [["“What does that mean, Joe?” said I.", "\"그게 무슨 뜻이에요, 조?\" 내가 말했다."]], [["Mrs. Joe, who always took explanations upon herself, said, snappishly, “Escaped.", "언제나 설명하는 일을 자청하던 조 부인이 퉁명스럽게 말했다. \"탈옥했다고. 탈옥.\""], ["Escaped.” Administering the definition like Tar-water.", "타르 물을 먹이듯 그 정의를 투여하는 식이었다."]], [["While Mrs. Joe sat with her head bending over her needlework, I put my mouth into the forms of saying to Joe, “What’s a convict?”", "조 부인이 바느질감 위로 고개를 숙이고 앉아 있는 동안, 나는 입을 놀려 조에게 \"죄수가 뭐예요?\" 하고 묻는 시늉을 했다."], ["Joe put his mouth into the forms of returning such a highly elaborate answer, that I could make out nothing of it but the single word “Pip.”", "그러자 조도 입을 놀려 무척이나 정교하게 다듬은 대답을 돌려주는 시늉을 했는데, 나는 거기서 \"핍\"이라는 단 한 단어밖에는 알아들을 수가 없었다."]], [["“There was a conwict off last night,”", "\"어젯밤에 죄수 하나가 달아났어.\" 조가 소리 내어 말했다."], ["said Joe, aloud, “after sunset-gun.", "\"일몰 신호포가 울린 다음에 말이야."], ["And they fired warning of him.", "그래서 그자를 알리는 경고 포를 쏘았지."], ["And now it appears they’re firing warning of another.”", "그리고 지금은 또 다른 놈을 알리는 경고 포를 쏘는 모양이다.\""]], [["“Who’s firing?”", "\"누가 쏘는 거예요?\""], ["said I.", "내가 말했다."]], [["“Drat that boy,”", "\"저 녀석 좀 보게.\""], ["interposed my sister, frowning at me over her work, “what a questioner he is.", "누나가 바느질감 너머로 나를 향해 인상을 쓰며 끼어들었다. \"어찌 저리 캐묻기를 좋아하는지."], ["Ask no questions, and you’ll be told no lies.”", "아무것도 묻지 않으면, 거짓말 들을 일도 없는 법이야.\""]], [["It was not very polite to herself, I thought, to imply that I should be told lies by her even if I did ask questions.", "설령 내가 정말로 묻는다 한들 누나가 거짓말을 들려주겠다는 뜻을 내비치는 것은 누나 자신에게도 그리 점잖은 노릇은 아니라고 나는 생각했다."], ["But she never was polite unless there was company.", "하지만 누나는 손님이 있을 때가 아니면 결코 점잖은 법이 없었다."]], [["At this point Joe greatly augmented my curiosity by taking the utmost pains to open his mouth very wide, and to put it into the form of a word that looked to me like “sulks.”", "이 대목에서 조는 한껏 공을 들여 입을 아주 크게 벌려, 내게는 \"토라짐\"처럼 보이는 단어의 모양을 만들어 보임으로써 내 호기심을 잔뜩 부추겼다."], ["Therefore, I naturally pointed to Mrs. Joe, and put my mouth into the form of saying, “her?”", "그래서 나는 자연스레 조 부인을 가리키며, 입을 놀려 \"누나요?\" 하고 묻는 시늉을 했다."], ["But Joe wouldn’t hear of that, at all, and again opened his mouth very wide, and shook the form of a most emphatic word out of it.", "그러나 조는 그 말을 도무지 들으려 하지 않고, 다시 입을 아주 크게 벌리더니 그 안에서 더없이 힘주어 어떤 단어의 모양을 흔들어 내보였다."], ["But I could make nothing of the word.", "그러나 나는 그 단어를 도통 알아낼 수가 없었다."]], [["“Mrs. Joe,”", "\"조 부인.\""], ["said I, as a last resort, “I should like to know—if you wouldn’t much mind—where the firing comes from?”", "나는 최후의 수단으로 말했다. \"알고 싶은데요 — 너무 귀찮지 않으시다면 — 그 포 쏘는 소리는 어디서 나는 거예요?\""]], [["“Lord bless the boy!”", "\"이런, 저 녀석 좀 보게나!\""], ["exclaimed my sister, as if she didn’t quite mean that but rather the contrary.", "누나가 외쳤는데, 정말로 그런 뜻이라기보다는 오히려 그 반대의 뜻인 듯한 말투였다."], ["“From the Hulks!”", "\"감옥선에서 나는 거다!\""]], [["“Oh-h!”", "\"오오!\""], ["said I, looking at Joe.", "나는 조를 바라보며 말했다."], ["“Hulks!”", "\"감옥선!\""]], [["Joe gave a reproachful cough, as much as to say, “Well, I told you so.”", "조는 나무라듯 헛기침을 했는데, 마치 \"거 봐라, 내가 말했잖니\" 하는 듯했다."]], [["“And please, what’s Hulks?” said I.", "\"그런데요, 감옥선이 뭐예요?\" 내가 말했다."]], [["“That’s the way with this boy!”", "\"이 녀석이 늘 이렇다니까!\""], ["exclaimed my sister, pointing me out with her needle and thread, and shaking her head at me.", "누나가 바늘과 실로 나를 가리키며, 내게 고개를 내저으며 외쳤다."], ["“Answer him one question, and he’ll ask you a dozen directly.", "\"한 가지 물음에 대답해 주면, 곧장 열두 가지를 더 물어 댄다니까."], ["Hulks are prison-ships, right ’cross th’ meshes.” We always used that name for marshes, in our country.", "감옥선이란 죄수를 가두는 배다, 저 습지 바로 건너편에 있는.\" 우리 고장에서는 늘 습지를 그렇게 불렀다."]], [["“I wonder who’s put into prison-ships, and why they’re put there?”", "\"누가 죄수선에 갇히는지, 그리고 왜 거기 갇히는지 궁금하네요.\""], ["said I, in a general way, and with quiet desperation.", "나는 막연히, 그리고 조용한 절망에 잠겨 말했다."]], [["It was too much for Mrs. Joe, who immediately rose.", "이것은 조 부인에게 너무 지나친 것이어서, 누나는 곧장 자리에서 일어섰다."], ["“I tell you what, young fellow,”", "\"내 말 잘 들어, 이 어린 녀석아.\""], ["said she, “I didn’t bring you up by hand to badger people’s lives out.", "누나가 말했다. \"나는 사람들 들들 볶아 진을 빼라고 너를 손수 키운 게 아니다."], ["It would be blame to me and not praise, if I had.", "그랬다면 그건 내게 칭찬거리가 아니라 흠이 됐겠지."], ["People are put in the Hulks because they murder, and because they rob, and forge, and do all sorts of bad; and they always begin by asking questions.", "사람들이 감옥선에 갇히는 건 살인을 저지르고, 도둑질을 하고, 문서를 위조하고, 온갖 못된 짓을 다 하기 때문이야. 그리고 그자들은 늘 캐묻는 것에서부터 시작하지."], ["Now, you get along to bed!”", "자, 이제 어서 자러 가!\""]], [["I was never allowed a candle to light me to bed, and, as I went upstairs in the dark, with my head tingling,—from Mrs. Joe’s thimble having played the tambourine upon it, to accompany her last words,—I felt fearfully sensible of the great convenience that the hulks were handy for me.", "나는 잠자리로 가는 길을 밝힐 촛불을 단 한 번도 허락받지 못했으므로, 머리가 얼얼한 채로 — 누나의 마지막 말에 장단을 맞추느라 골무가 탬버린처럼 내 머리를 두들겨 댄 탓이었다 — 어둠 속에서 위층으로 올라가면서, 감옥선이 내게 이토록 가까이 있다는 것이 참으로 편리하다는 사실을 무섭게 절감했다."], ["I was clearly on my way there.", "나는 분명 그곳으로 가는 길에 들어선 셈이었다."], ["I had begun by asking questions, and I was going to rob Mrs. Joe.", "나는 캐묻는 것에서부터 시작했고, 이제 조 부인의 것을 훔치려 하고 있었으니까."]], [["Since that time, which is far enough away now, I have often thought that few people know what secrecy there is in the young under terror.", "이제는 까마득히 멀어진 그 시절 이후로, 나는 공포에 사로잡힌 어린아이 속에 얼마나 깊은 비밀이 도사리고 있는지를 아는 사람이 드물다는 생각을 자주 했다."], ["No matter how unreasonable the terror, so that it be terror.", "그 공포가 아무리 터무니없는 것이라 해도, 어쨌든 공포이기만 하면 그렇다."], ["I was in mortal terror of the young man who wanted my heart and liver; I was in mortal terror of my interlocutor with the iron leg; I was in mortal terror of myself, from whom an awful promise had been extracted; I had no hope of deliverance through my all-powerful sister, who repulsed me at every turn; I am afraid to think of what I might have done on requirement, in the secrecy of my terror.", "나는 내 심장과 간을 노리는 그 젊은 놈에게 죽도록 겁을 먹었고, 쇠고랑을 찬 다리로 나와 이야기를 나눈 그자에게 죽도록 겁을 먹었으며, 무서운 약속을 강요당한 나 자신에게마저 죽도록 겁을 먹고 있었다. 무슨 일에든 나를 내치기만 하는 그 막강한 누나를 통해 구원받을 가망은 없었다. 내 공포의 은밀함 속에서, 만일 요구받았더라면 내가 무슨 짓을 저질렀을지는 생각하기조차 두렵다."]], [["If I slept at all that night, it was only to imagine myself drifting down the river on a strong spring-tide, to the Hulks; a ghostly pirate calling out to me through a speaking-trumpet, as I passed the gibbet-station, that I had better come ashore and be hanged there at once, and not put it off.", "그날 밤 잠이 들었다 한들, 그것은 그저 거센 봄철 밀물을 타고 강을 따라 감옥선으로 떠내려가는 내 모습을 그려 보는 것일 뿐이었다. 내가 교수대가 선 곳을 지날 때, 어느 유령 같은 해적이 나팔통을 통해 내게 외쳐 댔으니, 차라리 뭍으로 올라와 지금 당장 거기서 목매달려 죽는 게 낫지 미루지 말라는 것이었다."], ["I was afraid to sleep, even if I had been inclined, for I knew that at the first faint dawn of morning I must rob the pantry.", "잠들고 싶은 마음이 있었다 해도 잠들기가 두려웠으니, 동이 트는 첫 희미한 빛이 비치면 식료품 저장실을 털어야 한다는 것을 알고 있었기 때문이다."], ["There was no doing it in the night, for there was no getting a light by easy friction then; to have got one I must have struck it out of flint and steel, and have made a noise like the very pirate himself rattling his chains.", "그것은 밤중에는 도무지 할 수 없는 일이었다. 그 시절에는 손쉽게 마찰을 일으켜 불을 켤 길이 없었던 까닭이다. 불을 켜려면 부싯돌과 부시를 쳐서 일으켜야 했을 테고, 그러면 영락없이 그 해적이 제 사슬을 절그럭거리는 것 같은 소리를 냈을 것이다."]], [["As soon as the great black velvet pall outside my little window was shot with grey, I got up and went downstairs; every board upon the way, and every crack in every board calling after me, “Stop thief!”", "내 작은 창문 밖의 그 거대한 검은 비로드 휘장에 잿빛이 어리자마자, 나는 일어나 아래층으로 내려갔다. 가는 길의 널빤지마다, 그리고 널빤지마다의 갈라진 틈마다 내 뒤에서 외쳐 댔다. \"도둑 잡아라!\""], ["and “Get up, Mrs. Joe!” In the pantry, which was far more abundantly supplied than usual, owing to the season, I was very much alarmed by a hare hanging up by the heels, whom I rather thought I caught, when my back was half turned, winking.", "그리고 \"일어나요, 조 부인!\" 하고. 식료품 저장실은 철 덕분에 평소보다 훨씬 풍성하게 채워져 있었는데, 나는 거기 뒷다리가 매달려 걸려 있는 산토끼 한 마리에 무척 놀랐으니, 내가 등을 반쯤 돌렸을 때 그놈이 윙크를 하는 것만 같았던 것이다."], ["I had no time for verification, no time for selection, no time for anything, for I had no time to spare.", "확인해 볼 겨를도, 고를 겨를도, 그 무엇을 할 겨를도 없었으니, 한순간도 허비할 시간이 없었던 까닭이다."], ["I stole some bread, some rind of cheese, about half a jar of mincemeat (which I tied up in my pocket-handkerchief with my last night’s slice), some brandy from a stone bottle (which I decanted into a glass bottle I had secretly used for making that intoxicating fluid, Spanish-liquorice-water, up in my room: diluting the stone bottle from a jug in the kitchen cupboard), a meat bone with very little on it, and a beautiful round compact pork pie.", "나는 빵을 조금, 치즈 껍질을 조금, 다진 고기 절임 반 단지가량을(이것은 어젯밤의 빵 조각과 함께 손수건에 싸 두었다), 돌병에 든 브랜디를 조금(이것은 내가 위층 방에서 그 취하게 만드는 음료, 즉 스페인 감초 물을 만들 때 몰래 쓰던 유리병에 따라 옮겨 담았고, 돌병은 부엌 찬장의 물주전자에서 물을 부어 묽게 채워 놓았다), 살점이 거의 안 붙은 고기 뼈다귀 하나, 그리고 보기 좋게 동그랗고 단단한 돼지고기 파이 하나를 훔쳤다."], ["I was nearly going away without the pie, but I was tempted to mount upon a shelf, to look what it was that was put away so carefully in a covered earthenware dish in a corner, and I found it was the pie, and I took it in the hope that it was not intended for early use, and would not be missed for some time.", "하마터면 파이는 두고 갈 뻔했으나, 구석에 뚜껑 덮인 질그릇 접시 속에 그토록 정성스레 치워 둔 것이 무엇인가 보고 싶은 유혹에 선반 위로 기어올랐다가, 그것이 파이임을 알아냈고, 그것이 당장 쓰려고 둔 것이 아니어서 한동안은 없어진 줄도 모르리라는 바람에 그것을 집어 들었다."]], [["There was a door in the kitchen, communicating with the forge; I unlocked and unbolted that door, and got a file from among Joe’s tools.", "부엌에는 대장간으로 통하는 문이 하나 있었다. 나는 그 문의 자물쇠와 빗장을 풀고, 조의 연장들 사이에서 줄칼을 하나 꺼냈다."], ["Then I put the fastenings as I had found them, opened the door at which I had entered when I ran home last night, shut it, and ran for the misty marshes.", "그러고는 잠금장치를 원래 있던 대로 도로 채워 두고, 어젯밤 집으로 달려왔을 때 들어왔던 그 문을 열고, 그것을 닫은 뒤, 안개 자욱한 습지를 향해 달려갔다."]], [["# Chapter III.", "# Chapter III."]], [["It was a rimy morning, and very damp.", "서리가 내려앉은 아침이었고, 몹시 축축했다."], ["I had seen the damp lying on the outside of my little window, as if some goblin had been crying there all night, and using the window for a pocket-handkerchief.", "잠에서 깨어났을 때 나는 내 작은 창문 바깥에 습기가 어려 있는 것을 보았는데, 마치 어떤 도깨비가 밤새도록 거기서 울며 그 창문을 손수건 삼아 쓰기라도 한 것 같았다."], ["Now, I saw the damp lying on the bare hedges and spare grass, like a coarser sort of spiders’ webs; hanging itself from twig to twig and blade to blade.", "이제 나는 그 습기가 헐벗은 산울타리와 듬성듬성한 풀 위에, 더 거친 종류의 거미줄처럼 얹혀 있는 것을 보았다. 잔가지에서 잔가지로, 풀잎에서 풀잎으로 매달려 있었다."], ["On every rail and gate, wet lay clammy, and the marsh mist was so thick, that the wooden finger on the post directing people to our village—a direction which they never accepted, for they never came there—was invisible to me until I was quite close under it.", "모든 난간과 문에는 물기가 끈적하게 들러붙어 있었고, 습지의 안개가 어찌나 짙던지, 길가의 기둥 위에서 사람들에게 우리 마을 쪽을 가리키고 있는 나무 손가락 표지판이 — 사람들이 결코 받아들이지 않는 안내였다, 그곳에 오는 이가 없었으니까 — 그 바로 밑에 바싹 다가설 때까지도 내게는 보이지 않았다."], ["Then, as I looked up at it, while it dripped, it seemed to my oppressed conscience like a phantom devoting me to the Hulks.", "그러다 물방울을 뚝뚝 떨어뜨리고 있는 그것을 올려다보았을 때, 짓눌린 내 양심에는 그것이 마치 나를 죄수 호송선에 바치려는 유령처럼 보였다."]], [["The mist was heavier yet when I got out upon the marshes, so that instead of my running at everything, everything seemed to run at me.", "습지에 들어서니 안개는 더욱 짙어졌고, 그래서 내가 모든 것을 향해 달려가는 것이 아니라 모든 것이 나를 향해 달려드는 것만 같았다."], ["This was very disagreeable to a guilty mind.", "이것은 죄지은 마음에는 더없이 거북한 일이었다."], ["The gates and dikes and banks came bursting at me through the mist, as if they cried as plainly as could be, “A boy with somebody else’s pork pie! Stop him!”", "문과 도랑과 둑이 안개를 뚫고 내게 와락 달려들었는데, 마치 더없이 분명한 목소리로 \"남의 돼지고기 파이를 든 아이다! 저놈을 잡아라!\""], ["The cattle came upon me with like suddenness, staring out of their eyes, and steaming out of their nostrils, “Halloa, young thief!”", "하고 외치는 듯했다. 소 떼도 그처럼 불쑥 내게 다가와, 두 눈을 부릅뜨고 콧구멍으로 김을 내뿜으며 \"어이, 꼬마 도둑놈!\" 하는 듯했다."], ["One black ox, with a white cravat on,—who even had to my awakened conscience something of a clerical air,—fixed me so obstinately with his eyes, and moved his blunt head round in such an accusatory manner as I moved round, that I blubbered out to him, “I couldn’t help it, sir!", "흰 넥타이를 두른 검은 황소 한 마리는 — 깨어난 내 양심에는 어딘가 성직자 같은 분위기마저 풍기는 놈이었는데 — 어찌나 끈질기게 두 눈으로 나를 노려보던지, 그리고 내가 몸을 돌리면 그 뭉툭한 머리를 어찌나 나무라는 듯한 태도로 돌리던지, 나는 그놈을 향해 엉엉 울며 내뱉었다. \"어쩔 수 없었어요!"], ["It wasn’t for myself I took it!” Upon which he put down his head, blew a cloud of smoke out of his nose, and vanished with a kick-up of his hind-legs and a flourish of his tail.", "제가 먹으려고 가져온 게 아니란 말이에요!\" 그러자 황소는 고개를 숙이고 콧구멍으로 연기 한 무더기를 내뿜더니, 뒷다리를 차올리고 꼬리를 한 번 휘저으며 사라져 버렸다."]], [["All this time, I was getting on towards the river; but however fast I went, I couldn’t warm my feet, to which the damp cold seemed riveted, as the iron was riveted to the leg of the man I was running to meet.", "이러는 내내 나는 강 쪽으로 나아가고 있었다. 그러나 아무리 빨리 가도 발은 따뜻해지지 않았으니, 마치 내가 만나러 달려가는 그 사내의 다리에 쇠고랑이 박혀 있던 것처럼, 그 축축한 한기가 내 발에 박혀 있는 것만 같았다."], ["I knew my way to the Battery, pretty straight, for I had been down there on a Sunday with Joe, and Joe, sitting on an old gun, had told me that when I was ’prentice to him, regularly bound, we would have such Larks there!", "나는 포대로 가는 길을 꽤 똑바로 알고 있었다. 어느 일요일에 조와 함께 그곳에 내려가 본 적이 있었기 때문인데, 그때 조는 낡은 대포 위에 걸터앉아, 내가 정식으로 계약을 맺고 그의 도제가 되면 우리가 거기서 얼마나 신나게 놀게 될지 이야기해 주었다."], ["However, in the confusion of the mist, I found myself at last too far to the right, and consequently had to try back along the river-side, on the bank of loose stones above the mud and the stakes that staked the tide out.", "그러나 안개 속에서 혼란에 빠진 나는 결국 너무 오른쪽으로 치우쳐 있는 것을 알게 되었고, 그래서 강가를 따라, 진흙 위로 놓인 헐거운 돌무더기 둑과 밀물을 막아 세운 말뚝들 위로 되짚어 가야 했다."], ["Making my way along here with all despatch, I had just crossed a ditch which I knew to be very near the Battery, and had just scrambled up the mound beyond the ditch, when I saw the man sitting before me.", "한껏 서두르며 이곳을 따라 나아가다가, 포대에 아주 가깝다는 걸 알고 있던 도랑을 막 건너고, 그 도랑 너머의 둔덕을 막 기어 올라갔을 때, 나는 내 앞에 앉아 있는 사내를 보았다."], ["His back was towards me, and he had his arms folded, and was nodding forward, heavy with sleep.", "그는 내게 등을 돌린 채 두 팔을 가슴에 모으고, 잠에 겨워 앞으로 꾸벅꾸벅 졸고 있었다."]], [["I thought he would be more glad if I came upon him with his breakfast, in that unexpected manner, so I went forward softly and touched him on the shoulder.", "나는 이렇게 뜻밖의 방식으로 아침거리를 들고 다가가면 그가 더 기뻐하리라 생각했고, 그래서 살그머니 다가가 그의 어깨를 건드렸다."], ["He instantly jumped up, and it was not the same man, but another man!", "그는 즉각 벌떡 일어났는데, 같은 사내가 아니라 다른 사내였다!"]], [["And yet this man was dressed in coarse grey, too, and had a great iron on his leg, and was lame, and hoarse, and cold, and was everything that the other man was; except that he had not the same face, and had a flat broad-brimmed low-crowned felt hat on.", "그런데도 이 사내 역시 거친 잿빛 옷을 걸치고, 다리에는 커다란 쇠고랑을 차고 있었으며, 절뚝거리고, 목이 쉬었고, 추위에 떨고 있었으며, 그 다른 사내가 지녔던 모든 것을 다 지니고 있었다. 다만 같은 얼굴이 아니었고, 챙이 평평하고 넓으며 꼭대기가 낮은 펠트 모자를 쓰고 있다는 점만 달랐다."], ["All this I saw in a moment, for I had only a moment to see it in: he swore an oath at me, made a hit at me,—it was a round weak blow that missed me and almost knocked himself down, for it made him stumble,—and then he ran into the mist, stumbling twice as he went, and I lost him.", "이 모든 것을 나는 한순간에 보았는데, 그것을 볼 시간이 한순간밖에 없었기 때문이다. 사내는 내게 욕설을 퍼붓고 한 대 후려치려 했으나 — 그것은 빗나가 나를 맞히지도 못하고 오히려 그 자신을 거의 쓰러뜨릴 뻔한, 둥글고 힘없는 주먹질이어서, 그를 비틀거리게 만들었다 — 그러고는 안개 속으로 달아났는데, 가는 동안 두 번이나 비틀거렸고, 나는 그를 놓치고 말았다."]], [["“It’s the young man!”", "\"바로 그 젊은 놈이다!\""], ["I thought, feeling my heart shoot as I identified him.", "나는 그를 알아보며 가슴이 철렁 내려앉는 것을 느끼며 생각했다."], ["I dare say I should have felt a pain in my liver, too, if I had known where it was.", "내 간이 어디 있는지 알았더라면, 분명 간에도 통증을 느꼈으리라."]], [["I was soon at the Battery after that, and there was the right man,—hugging himself and limping to and fro, as if he had never all night left off hugging and limping,—waiting for me.", "그 일이 있고 나서 나는 곧 포대에 이르렀고, 거기에는 제대로 된 그 사내가 — 마치 밤새도록 한 번도 제 몸을 끌어안고 절뚝거리기를 멈추지 않은 듯이, 제 몸을 끌어안고 이리저리 절뚝거리며 — 나를 기다리고 있었다."], ["He was awfully cold, to be sure.", "정말이지 그는 지독하게 추워 보였다."], ["I half expected to see him drop down before my face and die of deadly cold.", "나는 그가 내 눈앞에서 푹 쓰러져 끔찍한 추위로 죽어 버리는 게 아닐까 반쯤 예상할 정도였다."], ["His eyes looked so awfully hungry too, that when I handed him the file and he laid it down on the grass, it occurred to me he would have tried to eat it, if he had not seen my bundle.", "그의 두 눈 또한 어찌나 무섭게 굶주려 보이던지, 내가 줄칼을 건네주자 그가 그것을 풀밭에 내려놓는 것을 보며, 만약 내 보따리를 보지 못했더라면 그는 그 줄칼이라도 먹어 치우려 했으리라는 생각이 들었다."], ["He did not turn me upside down this time to get at what I had, but left me right side upwards while I opened the bundle and emptied my pockets.", "이번에는 그가 내가 가진 것을 빼앗으려고 나를 거꾸로 뒤집지 않고, 내가 보따리를 풀고 주머니를 비우는 동안 나를 바로 세워 두었다."]], [["“What’s in the bottle, boy?” said he.", "\"병에 든 게 뭐냐, 얘야?\" 사내가 말했다."]], [["“Brandy,”", "\"브랜디예요.\""], ["said I.", "내가 말했다."]], [["He was already handing mincemeat down his throat in the most curious manner,—more like a man who was putting it away somewhere in a violent hurry, than a man who was eating it,—but he left off to take some of the liquor.", "그는 이미 다진 고기를 더없이 기묘한 방식으로 목구멍으로 넘기고 있었는데 — 그것을 먹는 사람이라기보다는 무언가를 어디론가 사납게 서둘러 치워 버리는 사람에 더 가까웠다 — 그러나 그 술을 좀 마시려고 잠시 멈추었다."], ["He shivered all the while so violently, that it was quite as much as he could do to keep the neck of the bottle between his teeth, without biting it off.", "그러는 내내 어찌나 격하게 떨던지, 병 주둥이를 물어뜯어 버리지 않고 이 사이에 물고 있는 것조차 그로서는 여간 힘든 일이 아니었다."]], [["“I think you have got the ague,” said I.", "\"학질에 걸리신 것 같아요.\" 내가 말했다."]], [["“I’m much of your opinion, boy,” said he.", "\"네 말이 옳은 것 같다, 얘야.\" 그가 말했다."]], [["“It’s bad about here,” I told him.", "\"이 근방은 그게 심해요.\""], ["“You’ve been lying out on the meshes, and they’re dreadful aguish.", "내가 그에게 일러 주었다. \"습지에 누워 계셨잖아요. 거긴 학질이 지독해요."], ["Rheumatic too.”", "류머티즘도요.\""]], [["“I’ll eat my breakfast afore they’re the death of me,” said he.", "\"그것들 때문에 죽기 전에 이 아침밥부터 먹어 치우겠다.\" 그가 말했다."], ["“I’d do that, if I was going to be strung up to that there gallows as there is over there, directly afterwards. I’ll beat the shivers so far, I’ll bet you.”", "\"먹고 나서 곧장 저기 저 너머에 있는 교수대에 매달리게 된다 해도, 나는 그렇게 할 거다. 이 떨림쯤은 그때까지 충분히 이겨 낼 거야, 내기를 해도 좋다.\""]], [["He was gobbling mincemeat, meatbone, bread, cheese, and pork pie, all at once: staring distrustfully while he did so at the mist all round us, and often stopping—even stopping his jaws—to listen.", "그는 다진 고기와 고기 뼈, 빵, 치즈, 돼지고기 파이를 한꺼번에 게걸스레 먹어 치우고 있었다. 그러면서 우리를 둘러싼 안개를 의심스럽게 노려보았고, 이따금 멈추어 — 심지어 턱의 움직임마저 멈추고 — 귀를 기울였다."], ["Some real or fancied sound, some clink upon the river or breathing of beast upon the marsh, now gave him a start, and he said, suddenly,—", "실제든 상상이든 어떤 소리, 강 위에서 나는 쨍그랑하는 소리나 습지에서 들리는 짐승의 숨소리가 그를 흠칫 놀라게 했고, 그는 불쑥 말했다."]], [["“You’re not a deceiving imp? You brought no one with you?”", "\"너 속임수 부리는 도깨비 같은 놈은 아니겠지? 누구도 데려오지 않았겠지?\""]], [["“No, sir! No!”", "\"네, 아저씨! 안 데려왔어요!\""]], [["“Nor giv’ no one the office to follow you?” “No!”", "\"누구한테 너를 따라오라고 일러 두지도 않았고?\""]], [["“Well,” said he, “I believe you.", "\"안 했어요!\" \"그래.\" 사내가 말했다."], ["You’d be but a fierce young hound indeed, if at your time of life you could help to hunt a wretched warmint hunted as near death and dunghill as this poor wretched warmint is!”", "\"네 말을 믿으마. 네 나이에, 이 가엾고 비참한 짐승만큼이나 죽음과 두엄 더미 가까이로 쫓기는 그런 짐승을 사냥하는 일을 거든다면, 너야말로 정말 모진 어린 사냥개일 테니까!\""]], [["Something clicked in his throat as if he had works in him like a clock, and was going to strike.", "마치 그의 몸속에 시계 같은 장치가 들어 있어 곧 종을 칠 듯이, 그의 목구멍에서 무언가가 딸깍하는 소리를 냈다."], ["And he smeared his ragged rough sleeve over his eyes.", "그러고는 그는 너덜너덜하고 거친 소매로 두 눈을 문질러 닦았다."]], [["Pitying his desolation, and watching him as he gradually settled down upon the pie, I made bold to say, “I am glad you enjoy it.”", "그의 처량한 신세가 가여워, 그가 차츰 파이에 자리 잡고 먹어 가는 모습을 지켜보다가, 나는 용기를 내어 말했다. \"맛있게 드시니 다행이에요.\""]], [["“Did you speak?”", "\"뭐라고 했냐?\""]], [["“I said I was glad you enjoyed it.”", "\"맛있게 드시니 다행이라고 했어요.\""]], [["“Thankee, my boy. I do.”", "\"고맙다, 얘야. 정말 맛있구나.\""]], [["I had often watched a large dog of ours eating his food; and I now noticed a decided similarity between the dog’s way of eating, and the man’s.", "나는 우리 집의 커다란 개가 먹이를 먹는 모습을 종종 지켜보곤 했는데, 이제 그 개의 먹는 방식과 이 사내의 방식 사이에 뚜렷한 닮은 점이 있음을 알아차렸다."], ["The man took strong sharp sudden bites, just like the dog.", "사내는 그 개와 꼭 마찬가지로, 세고 날카롭게 불쑥불쑥 베어 물었다."], ["He swallowed, or rather snapped up, every mouthful, too soon and too fast; and he looked sideways here and there while he ate, as if he thought there was danger in every direction of somebody’s coming to take the pie away.", "그리고 입에 넣은 것을 모조리 너무 빨리, 너무 성급하게 삼켰다 — 아니, 차라리 덥석 낚아챘다고 해야 옳다. 또 먹는 동안 사방으로 곁눈질을 했는데, 마치 누군가 그 파이를 빼앗으러 사방에서 다가올 위험이 있다고 여기는 것 같았다."], ["He was altogether too unsettled in his mind over it, to appreciate it comfortably I thought, or to have anybody to dine with him, without making a chop with his jaws at the visitor.", "내 생각에 그는 그것에 대해 마음이 너무도 불안정한 나머지, 그것을 느긋하게 음미하지도 못했고, 또 누군가와 함께 식사를 한다면 그 손님을 향해 턱을 한 번 덥석 물어 버리지 않고는 못 배길 것 같았다."], ["In all of which particulars he was very like the dog.", "이 모든 점에서 그는 그 개와 무척 닮아 있었다."]], [["“I am afraid you won’t leave any of it for him,”", "\"그 사람한테 남겨 주실 게 없을까 봐 걱정돼요.\""], ["said I, timidly; after a silence during which I had hesitated as to the politeness of making the remark.", "내가 머뭇거리며 말했다. 그 말을 하는 것이 예의에 어긋나지 않을까 망설이며 침묵을 지킨 뒤였다."], ["“There’s no more to be got where that came from.”", "\"그게 나온 데서는 더 구할 게 없거든요.\""], ["It was the certainty of this fact that impelled me to offer the hint.", "이 사실이 확실했기에 나는 그 말을 넌지시 꺼낼 마음이 들었던 것이다."]], [["“Leave any for him?", "\"그놈한테 남겨 두라고?"], ["Who’s him?”", "그놈이 누구냐?\""], ["said my friend, stopping in his crunching of pie-crust.", "내 친구가 파이 껍질을 와작와작 씹던 것을 멈추며 말했다."]], [["“The young man.", "\"그 젊은 사람이요."], ["That you spoke of.", "아저씨가 말씀하신 사람."], ["That was hid with you.”", "아저씨랑 같이 숨어 있다는 사람이요.\""]], [["“Oh ah!” he returned, with something like a gruff laugh. “Him?", "\"아, 그래!\" 그가 무뚝뚝한 웃음 비슷한 것을 지으며 대꾸했다. \"그놈?"], ["Yes, yes!", "그래, 그래!"], ["He don’t want no wittles.”", "그놈은 먹을 게 필요 없어.\""]], [["“I thought he looked as if he did,” said I.", "\"제가 보기엔 필요한 것 같던데요.\" 내가 말했다."]], [["The man stopped eating, and regarded me with the keenest scrutiny and the greatest surprise.", "사내는 먹기를 멈추고, 더없이 날카로운 눈초리와 더없이 큰 놀라움으로 나를 살펴보았다."]], [["“Looked? When?”", "\"보였다고? 언제?\""]], [["“Just now.”", "\"방금요.\""]], [["“Where?”", "\"어디서?\""]], [["“Yonder,”", "\"저쪽이요.\""], ["said I, pointing; “over there, where I found him nodding asleep, and thought it was you.”", "내가 손가락으로 가리키며 말했다. \"저기 저쪽, 그 사람이 꾸벅꾸벅 졸고 있는 걸 보고 아저씨인 줄 알았던 데서요.\""]], [["He held me by the collar and stared at me so, that I began to think his first idea about cutting my throat had revived.", "그는 내 멱살을 붙잡고 어찌나 나를 노려보던지, 나는 내 목을 따겠다던 그의 처음 생각이 되살아난 게 아닐까 하는 생각이 들기 시작했다."]], [["“Dressed like you, you know, only with a hat,”", "\"아저씨처럼 옷을 입었는데, 모자만 쓰고 있었어요.\""], ["I explained, trembling; “and—and”—I was very anxious to put this delicately—“and with—the same reason for wanting to borrow a file.", "나는 떨면서 설명했다. \"그리고 — 그리고\" — 나는 이 말을 조심스럽게 꺼내고 싶은 마음이 간절했다 — \"그리고 — 줄칼을 빌리고 싶어 할 만한, 똑같은 이유를 가지고 있었어요."], ["Didn’t you hear the cannon last night?”", "어젯밤에 대포 소리 못 들으셨어요?\""]], [["“Then there was firing!”", "\"그럼 발포가 있었던 거로군!\""], ["he said to himself.", "그가 혼잣말로 중얼거렸다."]], [["“I wonder you shouldn’t have been sure of that,”", "\"그걸 모르고 계셨다니 이상해요.\" 내가 대꾸했다."], ["I returned, “for we heard it up at home, and that’s farther away, and we were shut in besides.”", "\"우리는 집에서도 그 소리를 들었는데, 거기는 더 멀고, 게다가 우리는 문도 다 닫고 있었거든요.\""]], [["“Why, see now!” said he.", "\"그게 말이다, 들어 봐라!\""], ["“When a man’s alone on these flats, with a light head and a light stomach, perishing of cold and want, he hears nothin’ all night, but guns firing, and voices calling. Hears?", "그가 말했다. \"이런 벌판에 사람이 혼자 있으면서, 머리가 어지럽고 뱃속이 텅 빈 채로, 추위와 굶주림에 죽어 가다 보면, 밤새도록 들리는 거라곤 총 쏘는 소리와 부르는 목소리뿐이야. 들린다고?"], ["He sees the soldiers, with their red coats lighted up by the torches carried afore, closing in round him.", "보이기까지 하지. 앞세운 횃불에 붉은 군복이 환히 비친 군인들이 자기를 에워싸고 좁혀 들어오는 게 보여."], ["Hears his number called, hears himself challenged, hears the rattle of the muskets, hears the orders ‘Make ready! Present!", "자기 번호가 불리는 게 들리고, 정지하라는 호령이 들리고, 머스킷 총의 철컥거리는 소리가 들리고, '준비! 겨눠! 똑바로 조준해, 제군들!'"], ["Cover him steady, men!’ and is laid hands on—and there’s nothin’!", "하는 명령이 들리고 — 그러다 손이 자기 몸에 닿고 — 그런데 아무것도 없어!"], ["Why, if I see one pursuing party last night—coming up in order, Damn ’em, with their tramp, tramp—I see a hundred.", "그게 말이다, 어젯밤에 추격대 하나를 봤다 치면 — 줄을 맞춰 다가오면서, 빌어먹을, 저벅저벅 발소리를 내면서 말이야 — 백 개는 봤다는 거야. 그리고 발포로 말하자면! 그게, 날이 환히 밝은 다음에도 대포 소리에 안개가 흔들리는 게 보였단 말이다."], ["And as to firing!", "그런데 이 사내는\""], ["Why, I see the mist shake with the cannon, arter it was broad day,—But this man”; he had said all the rest, as if he had forgotten my being there; “did you notice anything in him?”", "하고, 그는 마치 내가 거기 있다는 사실을 잊은 듯 나머지 말을 다 한 뒤였다. \"그놈한테서 뭔가 알아챈 게 있냐?\""]], [["“He had a badly bruised face,”", "\"얼굴에 멍이 심하게 들어 있었어요.\""], ["said I, recalling what I hardly knew I knew.", "나는 내가 안다는 사실조차 거의 몰랐던 것을 떠올리며 말했다."]], [["“Not here?” exclaimed the man, striking his left cheek mercilessly, with the flat of his hand.", "\"여기 아니냐?\" 사내가 손바닥으로 자기 왼쪽 뺨을 사정없이 후려치며 외쳤다."]], [["“Yes, there!”", "\"네, 거기예요!\""]], [["“Where is he?”", "\"그놈 어디 있냐?\""], ["He crammed what little food was left, into the breast of his grey jacket.", "그는 남은 얼마 안 되는 음식을 잿빛 윗도리 가슴팍에 쑤셔 넣었다."], ["“Show me the way he went.", "\"그놈이 간 길을 가르쳐 다오."], ["I’ll pull him down, like a bloodhound.", "핏자국을 쫓는 사냥개처럼 끌어내리고 말겠다."], ["Curse this iron on my sore leg! Give us hold of the file, boy.”", "성한 데 없는 이 다리에 채워진 쇠고랑, 빌어먹을! 줄칼 이리 내놔라, 얘야.\""]], [["I indicated in what direction the mist had shrouded the other man, and he looked up at it for an instant.", "나는 안개가 그 다른 사내를 가린 방향을 일러 주었고, 그는 한순간 그쪽을 올려다보았다."], ["But he was down on the rank wet grass, filing at his iron like a madman, and not minding me or minding his own leg, which had an old chafe upon it and was bloody, but which he handled as roughly as if it had no more feeling in it than the file.", "그러나 그는 곧 무성하고 축축한 풀밭에 주저앉아, 미친 사람처럼 쇠고랑을 줄로 갈아 댔는데, 나도, 자기 다리도 아랑곳하지 않았다. 그 다리에는 오래된 쓸린 상처가 있어 피가 흐르고 있었지만, 그는 마치 그것이 줄칼만큼이나 감각이 없다는 듯이 거칠게 다루었다."], ["I was very much afraid of him again, now that he had worked himself into this fierce hurry, and I was likewise very much afraid of keeping away from home any longer.", "그가 이렇게 사나운 조급함에 빠져드는 것을 보니, 나는 다시금 그가 몹시 무서워졌고, 마찬가지로 더 이상 집을 비우고 있는 것이 몹시 두려워졌다. 나는 가야겠다고 그에게 말했으나, 그는 들은 척도 하지 않았다."], ["I told him I must go, but he took no notice, so I thought the best thing I could do was to slip off.", "그래서 내가 할 수 있는 가장 나은 일은 슬그머니 빠져나가는 것이겠다 싶었다."], ["The last I saw of him, his head was bent over his knee and he was working hard at his fetter, muttering impatient imprecations at it and at his leg.", "내가 마지막으로 본 그의 모습은, 무릎 위로 고개를 숙이고 차꼬를 열심히 갈아 대며, 그 차꼬와 자기 다리를 향해 참지 못하고 저주를 중얼거리고 있는 것이었다."], ["The last I heard of him, I stopped in the mist to listen, and the file was still going.", "내가 마지막으로 들은 그의 소리는, 안개 속에 멈춰 서서 귀를 기울였을 때, 여전히 줄칼이 갈리고 있는 소리였다."]], [["# Chapter IV.", "# Chapter IV."]], [["I fully expected to find a Constable in the kitchen, waiting to take me up.", "부엌에 순경이 나를 잡아가려고 기다리고 있을 거라고 나는 단단히 각오하고 있었다."], ["But not only was there no Constable there, but no discovery had yet been made of the robbery. Mrs.", "그러나 거기에는 순경이 없었을 뿐만 아니라, 도둑맞은 일조차 아직 발각되지 않은 상태였다."], ["Joe was prodigiously busy in getting the house ready for the festivities of the day, and Joe had been put upon the kitchen doorstep to keep him out of the dust-pan,—an article into which his destiny always led him, sooner or later, when my sister was vigorously reaping the floors of her establishment.", "조 부인은 그날의 잔치를 위해 집을 단장하느라 엄청나게 바빴고, 조는 부엌 문턱에 내쫓겨 있었는데, 그것은 그를 쓰레받기로부터 떼어 놓기 위함이었다. 누나가 자기 집의 마룻바닥을 기운차게 쓸어 댈 때면, 그의 운명은 늘 머지않아 그 물건 속으로 그를 끌어들이곤 했던 것이다."]], [["“And where the deuce ha’ you been?” was Mrs.", "\"그래, 도대체 너는 어디 가 있었던 거냐?\""], ["Joe’s Christmas salutation, when I and my conscience showed ourselves.", "나와 내 양심이 모습을 드러내자, 조 부인이 건넨 크리스마스 인사가 이러했다."]], [["I said I had been down to hear the Carols. “Ah! well!”", "나는 캐럴을 들으러 내려갔었다고 말했다. \"아! 그래!\""], ["observed Mrs. Joe.", "조 부인이 말했다."], ["“You might ha’ done worse.”", "\"더 나쁜 짓을 할 수도 있었겠지.\""], ["Not a doubt of that I thought.", "그건 의심할 여지가 없다고 나는 생각했다."]], [["“Perhaps if I warn’t a blacksmith’s wife, and (what’s the same thing) a slave with her apron never off, I should have been to hear the Carols,”", "\"내가 대장장이 마누라가 아니고, (그게 그거지만) 앞치마를 한시도 벗을 수 없는 노예가 아니었다면, 나도 캐럴을 들으러 갔을지 모르지.\""], ["said Mrs. Joe.", "조 부인이 말했다."], ["“I’m rather partial to Carols, myself, and that’s the best of reasons for my never hearing any.”", "\"나는 캐럴을 꽤나 좋아하는 편이거든. 그러니 도무지 한 번도 못 듣는 게 당연하지.\""]], [["Joe, who had ventured into the kitchen after me as the dustpan had retired before us, drew the back of his hand across his nose with a conciliatory air, when Mrs. Joe darted a look at him, and, when her eyes were withdrawn, secretly crossed his two forefingers, and exhibited them to me, as our token that Mrs.", "조는 쓰레받기가 우리 앞에서 물러간 덕에 나를 따라 부엌으로 들어와 있었는데, 조 부인이 그에게 눈길을 쏘아 던지자 화해를 청하는 기색으로 손등을 코에 갖다 댔다가, 그녀의 눈이 거두어지자 슬그머니 두 집게손가락을 엇갈려 놓고는 그것을 내게 내보였다. 조 부인이 심사가 뒤틀려 있다는 우리만의 신호였다."], ["Joe was in a cross temper. This was so much her normal state, that Joe and I would often, for weeks together, be, as to our fingers, like monumental Crusaders as to their legs.", "그것은 너무도 그녀의 평상시 상태였으므로, 조와 나는 몇 주일씩 내리, 우리 손가락에 관한 한, 다리를 그렇게 꼬고 있는 십자군 기념 조각상 같은 꼴이 되곤 했다."]], [["We were to have a superb dinner, consisting of a leg of pickled pork and greens, and a pair of roast stuffed fowls.", "우리는 절인 돼지고기 다리에 야채를 곁들인 요리와, 속을 채운 통닭구이 두 마리로 이루어진 훌륭한 만찬을 들기로 되어 있었다."], ["A handsome mince-pie had been made yesterday morning (which accounted for the mincemeat not being missed), and the pudding was already on the boil.", "어제 아침에 보기 좋은 민스파이가 만들어졌고(다진 고기가 없어진 것이 눈에 띄지 않았던 까닭이 여기에 있었다), 푸딩은 이미 끓는 중이었다."], ["These extensive arrangements occasioned us to be cut off unceremoniously in respect of breakfast; “for I ain’t,” said Mrs.", "이런 대대적인 준비 탓에 우리는 아침 식사에 관해서는 인정사정없이 끊겨 버렸다. \"암.\" 조 부인이 말했다."], ["Joe,—“I ain’t a-going to have no formal cramming and busting and washing up now, with what I’ve got before me, I promise you!”", "\"내 앞에 이렇게 할 일이 산더미인데, 지금 격식 차려서 처먹고 배 두드리고 설거지하고 그럴 마음은 추호도 없다, 그건 내가 장담하지!\""]], [["So, we had our slices served out, as if we were two thousand troops on a forced march instead of a man and boy at home; and we took gulps of milk and water, with apologetic countenances, from a jug on the dresser. In the meantime, Mrs.", "그리하여 우리는, 집에 있는 어른 하나 아이 하나가 아니라 마치 강행군 중인 이천 명의 군대라도 되는 양, 빵을 한 조각씩 배급받았다. 그리고 우리는 미안해하는 낯빛으로 찬장 위의 주전자에서 따른 우유 탄 물을 꿀꺽꿀꺽 들이켰다."], ["Joe put clean white curtains up, and tacked a new flowered flounce across the wide chimney to replace the old one, and uncovered the little state parlour across the passage, which was never uncovered at any other time, but passed the rest of the year in a cool haze of silver paper, which even extended to the four little white crockery poodles on the mantel-shelf, each with a black nose and a basket of flowers in his mouth, and each the counterpart of the other. Mrs.", "그러는 사이 조 부인은 깨끗한 흰 커튼을 달고, 넓은 벽난로 위에 헌것을 대신할 새 꽃무늬 주름 장식을 박아 붙였으며, 복도 건너편의 작은 응접실을 덮개에서 벗겨 냈다. 그곳은 다른 때에는 결코 덮개가 벗겨지는 일이 없이, 한 해의 나머지를 은종이의 서늘한 안개 속에서 보내는 곳이었다. 그 은종이는 벽난로 선반 위의 작은 흰 도자기 푸들 네 마리에까지 씌워져 있었는데, 그 푸들들은 저마다 검은 코에 입에는 꽃바구니를 물고 있었으며, 하나가 다른 하나와 똑같이 닮은 꼴이었다."], ["Joe was a very clean housekeeper, but had an exquisite art of making her cleanliness more uncomfortable and unacceptable than dirt itself.", "조 부인은 무척 깨끗한 살림꾼이었지만, 자신의 청결함을 더러움 그 자체보다도 더 불편하고 받아들이기 힘든 것으로 만드는 절묘한 재주가 있었다."], ["Cleanliness is next to Godliness, and some people do the same by their religion.", "청결은 경건함에 버금가는 것이라 했는데, 어떤 이들은 자기네 신앙을 가지고도 꼭 그렇게 한다."]], [["My sister, having so much to do, was going to church vicariously, that is to say, Joe and I were going.", "우리 누나는 할 일이 너무 많아서 대리로 교회에 가기로 했다. 다시 말해, 조와 내가 가는 것이었다."], ["In his working-clothes, Joe was a well-knit characteristic-looking blacksmith; in his holiday clothes, he was more like a scarecrow in good circumstances, than anything else.", "작업복을 입었을 때 조는 다부지게 균형 잡힌, 대장장이다운 풍모의 사내였다. 그러나 나들이옷을 입으면 그는 무엇보다도 형편이 넉넉한 허수아비에 더 가까웠다."], ["Nothing that he wore then fitted him or seemed to belong to him; and everything that he wore then grazed him.", "그때 그가 걸친 것은 무엇 하나 그에게 맞거나 그의 것처럼 보이는 게 없었고, 그때 그가 걸친 것은 무엇 하나 그의 살갗을 쓸어 대지 않는 게 없었다."], ["On the present festive occasion he emerged from his room, when the blithe bells were going, the picture of misery, in a full suit of Sunday penitentials.", "이 잔칫날에도 그는, 흥겨운 종소리가 울려 퍼질 때, 일요일용 참회복 한 벌을 빈틈없이 갖춰 입은 비참함 그 자체의 모습으로 제 방에서 나왔다."], ["As to me, I think my sister must have had some general idea that I was a young offender whom an Accoucheur Policeman had taken up (on my birthday) and delivered over to her, to be dealt with according to the outraged majesty of the law.", "나로 말하자면, 우리 누나는 내가 산파 노릇을 하는 순경에게 (내 생일에) 붙들려 그녀에게 넘겨진 어린 범죄자이며, 모욕당한 법의 위엄에 따라 처분되어야 마땅한 존재라는 막연한 관념을 품고 있었던 게 틀림없다고 나는 생각한다."], ["I was always treated as if I had insisted on being born in opposition to the dictates of reason, religion, and morality, and against the dissuading arguments of my best friends.", "나는 늘, 이성과 종교와 도덕의 명령을 거스르고, 또 나를 가장 아끼는 친구들의 만류하는 충고를 무릅쓰고 기어이 태어나겠다고 우긴 자인 양 취급받았다."], ["Even when I was taken to have a new suit of clothes, the tailor had orders to make them like a kind of Reformatory, and on no account to let me have the free use of my limbs.", "새 옷을 한 벌 맞추러 데려갈 때조차도, 재단사는 그것을 일종의 소년원복처럼 짓되, 어떤 경우에도 내가 사지를 자유로이 놀리지 못하게 하라는 지시를 받았다."]], [["Joe and I going to church, therefore, must have been a moving spectacle for compassionate minds.", "따라서 조와 내가 교회에 가는 광경은 동정심 많은 이들에게는 가슴 뭉클한 볼거리였음에 틀림없다."], ["Yet, what I suffered outside was nothing to what I underwent within.", "그러나 내가 겉으로 겪은 고통은 속으로 감당한 것에 비하면 아무것도 아니었다."], ["The terrors that had assailed me whenever Mrs. Joe had gone near the pantry, or out of the room, were only to be equalled by the remorse with which my mind dwelt on what my hands had done.", "조 부인이 식료품 저장실 근처로 가거나 방을 나설 때마다 나를 엄습하던 공포는, 오직 내 두 손이 저지른 짓을 곱씹으며 내 마음에 들러붙던 회한에만 견줄 만한 것이었다."], ["Under the weight of my wicked secret, I pondered whether the Church would be powerful enough to shield me from the vengeance of the terrible young man, if I divulged to that establishment.", "나는 내 사악한 비밀의 무게에 짓눌린 채, 만일 내가 교회라는 기관에 그것을 털어놓는다면 교회가 저 무서운 젊은 놈의 복수로부터 나를 지켜 줄 만큼 충분히 강한 곳일까 하고 곰곰이 생각했다."], ["I conceived the idea that the time when the banns were read and when the clergyman said, “Ye are now to declare it!” would be the time for me to rise and propose a private conference in the vestry.", "나는 혼인 예고가 낭독되고 목사가 \"이제 그것을 밝히시오!\"라고 말하는 그 순간이, 내가 자리에서 일어나 제의실에서 은밀한 면담을 청할 때라는 생각을 떠올렸다."], ["I am far from being sure that I might not have astonished our small congregation by resorting to this extreme measure, but for its being Christmas Day and no Sunday.", "그날이 크리스마스이지 일요일이 아니었다는 점만 아니었다면, 내가 이 극단적인 수단에 호소하여 우리의 작은 신도들을 깜짝 놀라게 하지 않았으리라고는 결코 장담할 수 없다."]], [["Mr. Wopsle, the clerk at church, was to dine with us; and Mr. Hubble the wheelwright and Mrs.", "교회 서기인 웝슬 씨가 우리와 함께 식사하기로 되어 있었고, 수레바퀴 만드는 허블 씨와 허블 부인, 그리고 펌블추크 아저씨도 함께였다."], ["Hubble; and Uncle Pumblechook (Joe’s uncle, but Mrs. Joe appropriated him), who was a well-to-do cornchandler in the nearest town, and drove his own chaise-cart.", "펌블추크 아저씨는 (조의 삼촌이었지만 조 부인이 제 것으로 챙겨 두었다) 가장 가까운 읍내에서 형편이 넉넉한 곡물상을 하며 자기 소유의 이륜마차를 손수 몰았다."], ["The dinner hour was half-past one.", "만찬 시각은 한 시 반이었다."], ["When Joe and I got home, we found the table laid, and Mrs. Joe dressed, and the dinner dressing, and the front door unlocked (it never was at any other time) for the company to enter by, and everything most splendid.", "조와 내가 집에 돌아와 보니, 식탁은 차려져 있고, 조 부인은 옷을 차려입었고, 음식은 한창 마무리되는 중이었으며, 손님들이 들어올 수 있도록 현관문은 열려 있었고(다른 때에는 결코 그런 일이 없었다), 모든 것이 더없이 으리으리했다."], ["And still, not a word of the robbery.", "그런데도 여전히, 도둑맞은 일에 대해서는 한마디도 없었다."]], [["The time came, without bringing with it any relief to my feelings, and the company came. Mr.", "내 심정에 아무런 위안도 가져다주지 못한 채 시간이 왔고, 손님들이 왔다."], ["Wopsle, united to a Roman nose and a large shining bald forehead, had a deep voice which he was uncommonly proud of; indeed it was understood among his acquaintance that if you could only give him his head, he would read the clergyman into fits; he himself confessed that if the Church was “thrown open,”", "로마식 매부리코에 크고 번들거리는 대머리 이마를 가진 웝슬 씨는, 자신이 유난히 자랑스러워하는 굵은 목소리의 소유자였다. 실제로 그의 지인들 사이에서는, 그에게 마음껏 하게만 내버려 두면 목사를 발작이 나도록 압도하며 성경을 읽어 낼 거라는 평이 있었다. 그 자신도, 교회가 — 경쟁에 — \"개방\"되기만 한다면 거기서 두각을 나타내는 일을 단념하지 않겠노라고 고백했다."], ["meaning to competition, he would not despair of making his mark in it.", "교회가 \"개방\"되지 않았으므로, 앞서 말했듯이 그는 우리 교회의 서기였다."], ["The Church not being “thrown open,” he was, as I have said, our clerk.", "그러나 그는 아멘을 어마어마하게 강조했고, 시편을 낭송할 때면 — 늘 한 구절을 통째로 읊었는데 — 먼저 신도들을 빙 둘러보았다."], ["But he punished the Amens tremendously; and when he gave out the psalm,—always giving the whole verse,—he looked all round the congregation first, as much as to say, “You have heard my friend overhead; oblige me with your opinion of this style!”", "마치 이렇게 말하는 듯이. \"여러분은 방금 머리 위에 있는 내 친구의 낭독을 들으셨소. 그럼 이번엔 이 방식에 대한 여러분의 의견을 들려주시구려!\""]], [["I opened the door to the company,—making believe that it was a habit of ours to open that door,—and I opened it first to Mr. Wopsle, next to Mr. and Mrs.", "나는 손님들에게 문을 열어 주었는데 — 마치 우리가 그 문을 열어 주는 것이 으레 있는 일인 양 꾸며 가면서 — 먼저 웝슬 씨에게, 다음으로 허블 씨 부부에게, 그리고 마지막으로 펌블추크 아저씨에게 열어 주었다."], ["Hubble, and last of all to Uncle Pumblechook. N.B. I was not allowed to call him uncle, under the severest penalties.", "덧붙이자면, 나는 더없이 혹독한 벌을 무릅쓰지 않고서는 그를 아저씨라고 부를 수 없었다."]], [["“Mrs. Joe,”", "\"조 부인.\""], ["said Uncle Pumblechook, a large hard-breathing middle-aged slow man, with a mouth like a fish, dull staring eyes, and sandy hair standing upright on his head, so that he looked as if he had just been all but choked, and had that moment come to, “I have brought you as the compliments of the season—I have brought you, Mum, a bottle of sherry wine—and I have brought you, Mum, a bottle of port wine.”", "펌블추크 아저씨가 말했다. 그는 숨소리가 거칠고 동작이 굼뜬 덩치 큰 중년 사내로, 물고기 같은 입에 멍하니 응시하는 흐릿한 눈을 가졌고, 모래빛 머리카락이 머리에 곤두서 있어서 마치 방금 거의 목이 졸렸다가 그 순간 정신이 돌아온 사람처럼 보였다. \"이 계절의 인사로 가져왔습니다 — 부인, 셰리주 한 병을 가져왔습니다 — 그리고 부인, 포트와인 한 병도 가져왔습니다.\""]], [["Every Christmas Day he presented himself, as a profound novelty, with exactly the same words, and carrying the two bottles like dumb-bells.", "해마다 크리스마스가 되면 그는, 마치 굉장한 진기함이라도 되는 양, 토씨 하나 틀리지 않은 똑같은 말을 하며, 두 병을 아령처럼 들고 모습을 드러냈다. 해마다 크리스마스가 되면 조 부인은, 지금 대꾸하는 것과 똑같이 대꾸했다."], ["Every Christmas Day, Mrs.", "\"오, 펌—블추—크 아—저씨!"], ["Joe replied, as she now replied, “O, Un—cle Pum-ble—chook! This is kind!”", "정말 친절하시기도 하지!\" 해마다 크리스마스가 되면 그는, 지금 받아치는 것과 똑같이 받아쳤다."], ["Every Christmas Day, he retorted, as he now retorted, “It’s no more than your merits.", "\"당신이 받을 만한 것 이상은 아니올시다. 그래, 다들 잘 지내시오?"], ["And now are you all bobbish, and how’s Sixpennorth of halfpence?” meaning me.", "그리고 반 페니짜리 육 펜스어치는 어떻게 지내나?\" 나를 두고 하는 말이었다."]], [["We dined on these occasions in the kitchen, and adjourned, for the nuts and oranges and apples to the parlour; which was a change very like Joe’s change from his working-clothes to his Sunday dress.", "이런 날이면 우리는 부엌에서 식사를 하고, 견과류와 오렌지와 사과를 들기 위해 응접실로 자리를 옮겼다. 그것은 작업복에서 나들이옷으로 갈아입는 조의 변신과 무척이나 닮은 변화였다."], ["My sister was uncommonly lively on the present occasion, and indeed was generally more gracious in the society of Mrs. Hubble than in other company.", "누나는 이날따라 유난히 활기찼는데, 사실 그녀는 다른 누구와 함께 있을 때보다 허블 부인과 어울릴 때면 대체로 더 상냥했다."], ["I remember Mrs. Hubble as a little curly sharp-edged person in sky-blue, who held a conventionally juvenile position, because she had married Mr. Hubble,—I don’t know at what remote period,—when she was much younger than he.", "내 기억에 허블 부인은, 하늘색 옷을 입은 자그마하고 곱슬머리에 모가 진 사람으로, 관습적으로 어린 사람 행세를 했는데, 이는 그녀가 — 얼마나 오래전인지는 모르겠지만 — 자기보다 훨씬 나이 많은 허블 씨와 결혼했을 때 한참 어렸던 까닭이었다."], ["I remember Mr Hubble as a tough, high-shouldered, stooping old man, of a sawdusty fragrance, with his legs extraordinarily wide apart: so that in my short days I always saw some miles of open country between them when I met him coming up the lane.", "허블 씨는, 내 기억에, 질기고 어깨가 높이 솟아 구부정한 톱밥 냄새가 나는 노인이었는데, 다리를 어찌나 엄청나게 벌리고 있던지, 어린 시절 그를 골목길에서 마주칠 때면 나는 늘 그의 두 다리 사이로 몇 마일이나 되는 탁 트인 들판이 보이는 것 같았다."]], [["Among this good company I should have felt myself, even if I hadn’t robbed the pantry, in a false position.", "이런 좋은 분들 가운데 끼어 있자니, 설령 내가 저장실을 털지 않았더라도 나는 거북한 처지에 놓인 기분이었을 것이다."], ["Not because I was squeezed in at an acute angle of the tablecloth, with the table in my chest, and the Pumblechookian elbow in my eye, nor because I was not allowed to speak (I didn’t want to speak), nor because I was regaled with the scaly tips of the drumsticks of the fowls, and with those obscure corners of pork of which the pig, when living, had had the least reason to be vain.", "그것은 내가 식탁보의 예각진 모서리에 끼인 채, 식탁 모서리가 내 가슴팍을 누르고 펌블추크식 팔꿈치가 내 눈을 찔러서가 아니었고, 내가 말하는 것이 허락되지 않아서도 아니었으며(나는 말하고 싶지도 않았다), 비늘 같은 닭다리 끄트머리와, 돼지가 살아 있을 적에 가장 자랑스러워할 이유가 없었을 그 후미진 돼지고기 부위를 대접받아서도 아니었다. 아니다."], ["No; I should not have minded that, if they would only have left me alone.", "그저 나를 가만히 내버려 두기만 했다면 그런 건 개의치 않았을 것이다."], ["But they wouldn’t leave me alone.", "그러나 그들은 나를 가만히 두지 않았다."], ["They seemed to think the opportunity lost, if they failed to point the conversation at me, every now and then, and stick the point into me.", "그들은 이따금 화제를 내게로 겨누어 그 끝을 내게 콱 찔러 넣지 않으면, 모처럼의 기회를 놓치는 거라고 여기는 듯했다."], ["I might have been an unfortunate little bull in a Spanish arena, I got so smartingly touched up by these moral goads.", "나는 이런 도덕적인 막대기들에 어찌나 따끔따끔 찔려 댔던지, 마치 스페인 투기장에 끌려 나온 가엾은 작은 황소가 된 기분이었다."]], [["It began the moment we sat down to dinner. Mr.", "그것은 우리가 식탁에 앉는 순간부터 시작되었다."], ["Wopsle said grace with theatrical declamation,—as it now appears to me, something like a religious cross of the Ghost in Hamlet with Richard the Third,—and ended with the very proper aspiration that we might be truly grateful.", "웝슬 씨가 연극조의 낭독으로 식전 기도를 올렸는데 — 지금 돌이켜 보면, 무슨 「햄릿」에 나오는 유령과 리처드 삼세를 종교적으로 교배해 놓은 것 같았다 — 우리가 진정으로 감사할 줄 알게 해 달라는 더없이 지당한 소망으로 끝맺었다."], ["Upon which my sister fixed me with her eye, and said, in a low reproachful voice, “Do you hear that?", "그러자 누나는 나를 눈으로 쏘아보며, 낮고 책망하는 목소리로 말했다. \"저 말 들었지?"], ["Be grateful.”", "감사한 줄 알아라.\""]], [["“Especially,”", "\"특히 말이다.\""], ["said Mr. Pumblechook, “be grateful, boy, to them which brought you up by hand.”", "펌블추크 씨가 말했다. \"얘야, 손수 너를 키워 준 분들께 감사할 줄 알아라.\""]], [["Mrs. Hubble shook her head, and contemplating me with a mournful presentiment that I should come to no good, asked, “Why is it that the young are never grateful?”", "허블 부인은 고개를 절레절레 흔들고는, 내가 끝내 변변치 못한 인간이 되리라는 애처로운 예감으로 나를 들여다보며 물었다. \"어째서 젊은것들은 도무지 감사할 줄을 모를까요?\""], ["This moral mystery seemed too much for the company until Mr. Hubble tersely solved it by saying, “Naterally wicious.”", "이 도덕적 수수께끼는 좌중에게 너무도 벅찬 것이었으나, 마침내 허블 씨가 \"천성이 고약해서지\"라고 간명하게 풀어 주었다."], ["Everybody then murmured “True!”", "그러자 모두가 \"옳거니!\""], ["and looked at me in a particularly unpleasant and personal manner.", "하고 웅얼거리며, 유난히 불쾌하고도 노골적인 태도로 나를 바라보았다."]], [["Joe’s station and influence were something feebler (if possible) when there was company than when there was none.", "손님이 있을 때 조의 지위와 영향력은 (가능한 일이라면) 손님이 없을 때보다 한층 더 미약했다."], ["But he always aided and comforted me when he could, in some way of his own, and he always did so at dinner-time by giving me gravy, if there were any.", "그러나 그는 자기 나름의 어떤 방식으로 할 수 있을 때마다 늘 나를 거들고 위로해 주었고, 식사 때면 늘 그렇게 했다. 곧 고기 국물이 있으면 그것을 내게 떠 주는 것이었다."], ["There being plenty of gravy to-day, Joe spooned into my plate, at this point, about half a pint.", "오늘은 국물이 넉넉했으므로, 이 대목에서 조는 내 접시에 반 파인트쯤 떠 주었다."]], [["A little later on in the dinner, Mr. Wopsle reviewed the sermon with some severity, and intimated—in the usual hypothetical case of the Church being “thrown open”—what kind of sermon he would have given them.", "만찬이 조금 더 진행되자, 웝슬 씨는 다소 준엄하게 설교를 평하고는 — 교회가 \"개방\"된다는 그 으레 나오는 가정의 경우에 — 자기라면 어떤 설교를 해 주었을지를 넌지시 비쳤다."], ["After favouring them with some heads of that discourse, he remarked that he considered the subject of the day’s homily, ill chosen; which was the less excusable, he added, when there were so many subjects “going about.”", "그 설교의 요점 몇 가지를 좌중에게 베풀어 준 다음, 그는 오늘 설교의 주제 선정이 잘못되었다고 평했는데, 게다가 \"돌아다니는\" 주제가 그토록 많은 마당에 그것은 더더욱 변명의 여지가 없는 일이라고 덧붙였다."]], [["“True again,” said Uncle Pumblechook.", "\"그것도 옳은 말씀이오.\" 펌블추크 아저씨가 말했다."], ["“You’ve hit it, sir!", "\"정곡을 찌르셨소이다!"], ["Plenty of subjects going about, for them that know how to put salt upon their tails.", "꼬리에 소금을 칠 줄 아는 사람에게라면, 돌아다니는 주제야 얼마든지 있지요."], ["That’s what’s wanted.", "바로 그게 필요한 거지요."], ["A man needn’t go far to find a subject, if he’s ready with his salt-box.” Mr.", "소금통만 준비되어 있다면야, 주제를 찾으러 멀리 갈 것도 없는 법이오.\""], ["Pumblechook added, after a short interval of reflection, “Look at Pork alone.", "펌블추크 씨는 잠시 생각에 잠겼다가 덧붙였다. \"돼지고기만 봐도 그렇소."], ["There’s a subject!", "그게 바로 주제 아니겠소!"], ["If you want a subject, look at Pork!”", "주제를 원한다면, 돼지고기를 보시구려!\""]], [["“True, sir.", "\"옳은 말씀입니다."], ["Many a moral for the young,”", "젊은이를 위한 교훈도 숱하지요.\""], ["returned Mr. Wopsle,—and I knew he was going to lug me in, before he said it; “might be deduced from that text.”", "웝슬 씨가 응수했다 — 나는 그가 말을 꺼내기도 전에 또 나를 끌어들이려 한다는 걸 알았다. \"저 본문에서 얼마든지 끌어낼 수 있지요.\""]], [["(“You listen to this,” said my sister to me, in a severe parenthesis.)", "(\"너는 이걸 잘 들어 둬.\" 누나가 준엄한 곁말로 내게 말했다.)"]], [["Joe gave me some more gravy.", "조가 내게 국물을 좀 더 떠 주었다."]], [["“Swine,”", "\"돼지란.\""], ["pursued Mr. Wopsle, in his deepest voice, and pointing his fork at my blushes, as if he were mentioning my Christian name,—“swine were the companions of the prodigal.", "웝슬 씨가, 마치 내 세례명이라도 부르듯이 내 붉어진 얼굴에 포크를 겨누며, 더없이 굵은 목소리로 말을 이었다. \"돼지란 탕자의 동무였소이다."], ["The gluttony of Swine is put before us, as an example to the young.”", "돼지의 탐식은 젊은이를 위한 본보기로 우리 앞에 제시되어 있지요.\""], ["(I thought this pretty well in him who had been praising up the pork for being so plump and juicy.) “What is detestable in a pig is more detestable in a boy.”", "(살이 통통하고 즙이 풍부하다고 그 돼지고기를 한껏 추켜세우던 사람으로서는 꽤나 가관이라고 나는 생각했다.) \"돼지에게서 가증스러운 것은 사내아이에게서는 더욱 가증스러운 법이오.\""]], [["“Or girl,”", "\"아니면 계집아이든지요.\""], ["suggested Mr. Hubble.", "허블 씨가 거들었다."]], [["“Of course, or girl, Mr. Hubble,” assented Mr.", "\"물론이지요, 아니면 계집아이든지요, 허블 씨.\""], ["Wopsle, rather irritably, “but there is no girl present.”", "웝슬 씨가 다소 짜증스럽게 동의했다. \"하지만 여기 계집아이는 없잖소.\""]], [["“Besides,” said Mr.", "\"게다가 말이다.\""], ["Pumblechook, turning sharp on me, “think what you’ve got to be grateful for.", "펌블추크 씨가 홱 나를 돌아보며 말했다. \"네가 무엇에 감사해야 하는지 생각해 봐라."], ["If you’d been born a Squeaker—”", "만일 네가 꽥꽥거리는 새끼 돼지로 태어났더라면 —\""]], [["“He was, if ever a child was,”", "\"태어났고말고요. 그런 자식이 세상에 있다면 바로 저놈이지요.\""], ["said my sister, most emphatically.", "누나가 더없이 힘주어 말했다."]], [["Joe gave me some more gravy.", "조가 내게 국물을 좀 더 떠 주었다."]], [["“Well, but I mean a four-footed Squeaker,”", "\"아니, 내 말은 네발 달린 새끼 돼지 말이다.\""], ["said Mr. Pumblechook.", "펌블추크 씨가 말했다."], ["“If you had been born such, would you have been here now?", "\"만일 네가 그런 걸로 태어났더라면, 지금 여기 이렇게 있겠느냐?"], ["Not you—”", "천만에 —\""]], [["“Unless in that form,” said Mr.", "\"저 접시 위의 모습이라면 모를까요.\""], ["Wopsle, nodding towards the dish.", "웝슬 씨가 요리 접시 쪽으로 고갯짓하며 말했다."]], [["“But I don’t mean in that form, sir,” returned Mr.", "\"하지만 나는 그런 모습을 말하는 게 아니오, 선생.\""], ["Pumblechook, who had an objection to being interrupted; “I mean, enjoying himself with his elders and betters, and improving himself with their conversation, and rolling in the lap of luxury.", "말이 끊기는 것을 싫어하는 펌블추크 씨가 응수했다. \"내 말은, 어른들과 윗사람들과 어울려 즐기고, 그분들의 대화로 자기 자신을 갈고닦으며, 호사스러운 품 안에서 뒹군다는 거요."], ["Would he have been doing that? No, he wouldn’t.", "이놈이 그러고 있겠느냐 이 말이오? 아니지, 그러지 못할 거요."], ["And what would have been your destination?” turning on me again.", "그럼 네 운명이 어찌 되었겠느냐?\" 그가 다시 나를 향해 돌아섰다."], ["“You would have been disposed of for so many shillings according to the market price of the article, and Dunstable the butcher would have come up to you as you lay in your straw, and he would have whipped you under his left arm, and with his right he would have tucked up his frock to get a penknife from out of his waistcoat-pocket, and he would have shed your blood and had your life. No bringing up by hand then.", "\"물건의 시세에 따라 몇 실링에 처분되어 버렸을 테고, 정육점의 던스터블이 짚더미에 누운 네게 다가와서는, 너를 왼팔 밑에 척 끼고, 오른손으로는 조끼 주머니에서 주머니칼을 꺼내려고 작업복 자락을 걷어 올리고는, 네 피를 쏟게 하고 네 목숨을 거두어 갔겠지. 그러면 손수 길러 주는 일 따위는 없었을 거다."], ["Not a bit of it!”", "어림도 없지!\""]], [["Joe offered me more gravy, which I was afraid to take.", "조가 내게 국물을 좀 더 권했지만, 나는 받기가 두려웠다."]], [["“He was a world of trouble to you, ma’am,” said Mrs.", "\"이 애가 여간 속을 썩이지 않았겠어요, 부인.\""], ["Hubble, commiserating my sister.", "허블 부인이 누나를 위로하며 말했다."]], [["“Trouble?” echoed my sister; “trouble?”", "\"속을 썩이느냐고요?\" 누나가 되받았다."], ["and then entered on a fearful catalogue of all the illnesses I had been guilty of, and all the acts of sleeplessness I had committed, and all the high places I had tumbled from, and all the low places I had tumbled into, and all the injuries I had done myself, and all the times she had wished me in my grave, and I had contumaciously refused to go there.", "\"속을 썩여요?\" 그러더니 누나는, 내가 저지른 온갖 병치레와, 내가 범한 온갖 밤잠 설치게 한 짓과, 내가 굴러떨어진 온갖 높은 곳과, 내가 빠져든 온갖 낮은 곳과, 내가 내 몸에 입힌 온갖 상처와, 나를 무덤에 처넣고 싶어 한 온갖 순간들과, 그런데도 내가 고집스럽게 그리로 가기를 마다한 일들의 무시무시한 목록을 늘어놓기 시작했다."]], [["I think the Romans must have aggravated one another very much, with their noses.", "로마인들은 자기네 코로 서로를 무던히도 짜증나게 했을 게 틀림없다고 나는 생각한다."], ["Perhaps, they became the restless people they were, in consequence.", "어쩌면 그 탓에 그들이 그토록 가만있지 못하는 족속이 되었는지도 모른다."], ["Anyhow, Mr. Wopsle’s Roman nose so aggravated me, during the recital of my misdemeanours, that I should have liked to pull it until he howled.", "어쨌거나 내 비행이 낭송되는 동안 웝슬 씨의 로마식 매부리코가 어찌나 나를 짜증나게 했던지, 나는 그가 비명을 지를 때까지 그것을 잡아당겨 주고 싶었다."], ["But, all I had endured up to this time was nothing in comparison with the awful feelings that took possession of me when the pause was broken which ensued upon my sister’s recital, and in which pause everybody had looked at me (as I felt painfully conscious) with indignation and abhorrence.", "그러나 이때까지 내가 견뎌 온 모든 것은, 누나의 낭송에 뒤이은 그 정적이 깨졌을 때 나를 사로잡은 끔찍한 심정에 비하면 아무것도 아니었다. 그 정적이 이어지는 동안, 모두가 (나는 괴롭도록 또렷이 느꼈다) 분노와 혐오의 눈길로 나를 바라보았던 것이다."]], [["“Yet,” said Mr.", "\"그래도 말이오.\""], ["Pumblechook, leading the company gently back to the theme from which they had strayed, “Pork—regarded as biled—is rich, too; ain’t it?”", "펌블추크 씨가, 좌중을 그들이 빗나간 화제로 슬며시 되돌리며 말했다. \"돼지고기는 — 삶은 것으로 치면 — 또 기름지지 않소이까?\""]], [["“Have a little brandy, uncle,”", "\"브랜디 좀 드세요, 아저씨.\""], ["said my sister.", "누나가 말했다."]], [["O Heavens, it had come at last!", "오 하늘이시여, 마침내 올 것이 오고 말았다!"], ["He would find it was weak, he would say it was weak, and I was lost!", "그는 그것이 약하다는 걸 알아챌 테고, 약하다고 말할 테고, 그러면 나는 끝장이었다!"], ["I held tight to the leg of the table under the cloth, with both hands, and awaited my fate.", "나는 식탁보 밑으로 두 손으로 식탁 다리를 꽉 붙들고 내 운명을 기다렸다."]], [["My sister went for the stone bottle, came back with the stone bottle, and poured his brandy out: no one else taking any.", "누나는 돌로 만든 병을 가지러 갔다가, 그 돌 병을 들고 돌아와 그에게 브랜디를 따라 주었다. 다른 누구도 마시지 않았다."], ["The wretched man trifled with his glass,—took it up, looked at it through the light, put it down,—prolonged my misery.", "그 가련한 사내는 잔을 만지작거리며 — 들어 올렸다가, 빛에 비춰 보고, 도로 내려놓으면서 — 내 고통을 질질 늘였다."], ["All this time Mrs. Joe and Joe were briskly clearing the table for the pie and pudding.", "그러는 동안 내내 조 부인과 조는 파이와 푸딩을 위해 분주히 식탁을 치우고 있었다."]], [["I couldn’t keep my eyes off him.", "나는 그에게서 눈을 뗄 수가 없었다."], ["Always holding tight by the leg of the table with my hands and feet, I saw the miserable creature finger his glass playfully, take it up, smile, throw his head back, and drink the brandy off.", "줄곧 손과 발로 식탁 다리를 꽉 붙든 채로, 나는 그 가련한 작자가 장난스럽게 잔을 만지작거리고, 그것을 들어 올리고, 미소 짓고, 고개를 뒤로 젖혀 브랜디를 단숨에 들이켜는 모습을 지켜보았다."], ["Instantly afterwards, the company were seized with unspeakable consternation, owing to his springing to his feet, turning round several times in an appalling spasmodic whooping-cough dance, and rushing out at the door; he then became visible through the window, violently plunging and expectorating, making the most hideous faces, and apparently out of his mind.", "그 직후, 좌중은 이루 말할 수 없는 경악에 휩싸였다. 그가 벌떡 일어서더니, 소름 끼치는 발작적 백일해 춤이라도 추듯 몇 번이나 빙빙 돌다가, 문밖으로 뛰쳐나갔던 것이다. 이윽고 그는 창문 너머로, 격렬하게 몸부림치고 침을 뱉어 대며, 더없이 흉측한 표정을 짓는, 영락없이 실성한 듯한 모습으로 나타났다."]], [["I held on tight, while Mrs. Joe and Joe ran to him.", "조 부인과 조가 그에게 달려가는 동안에도 나는 꽉 붙들고 있었다."], ["I didn’t know how I had done it, but I had no doubt I had murdered him somehow.", "어떻게 한 일인지는 알 수 없었으나, 내가 어떻게든 그를 죽인 게 틀림없다고 나는 의심치 않았다."], ["In my dreadful situation, it was a relief when he was brought back, and surveying the company all round as if they had disagreed with him, sank down into his chair with the one significant gasp, “Tar!”", "이 끔찍한 처지에서, 그가 다시 안으로 끌려 들어와, 마치 좌중이 자기 비위를 거스르기라도 한 양 사람들을 빙 둘러보고는, 의미심장하게 한 번 헐떡이며 \"타르!\" 하고 의자에 털썩 주저앉았을 때, 그것은 차라리 안도였다."]], [["I had filled up the bottle from the tar-water jug.", "나는 타르 물 주전자에서 그 병을 채워 두었던 것이다."], ["I knew he would be worse by and by.", "나는 그가 머지않아 더 나빠지리라는 걸 알았다."], ["I moved the table, like a Medium of the present day, by the vigor of my unseen hold upon it.", "나는 오늘날의 영매(靈媒)처럼, 보이지 않는 손길로 식탁을 힘껏 거머쥔 채 그것을 움직였다."]], [["“Tar!” cried my sister, in amazement.", "\"타르라니!\" 누나가 어리둥절하여 외쳤다."], ["“Why, how ever could Tar come there?”", "\"아니, 어떻게 타르가 거기에 들어갔담?\""]], [["But, Uncle Pumblechook, who was omnipotent in that kitchen, wouldn’t hear the word, wouldn’t hear of the subject, imperiously waved it all away with his hand, and asked for hot gin and water.", "그러나 그 부엌에서 전능한 존재였던 펌블추크 아저씨는, 그 말을 들으려 하지도, 그 화제를 입에 올리려고도 하지 않고, 위엄 있게 손을 내저어 그 모든 것을 물리치고는, 뜨거운 진에 물을 탄 것을 청했다."], ["My sister, who had begun to be alarmingly meditative, had to employ herself actively in getting the gin, the hot water, the sugar, and the lemon-peel, and mixing them.", "불안하리만치 골똘히 생각에 잠기기 시작했던 누나는, 진과 뜨거운 물과 설탕과 레몬 껍질을 가져다 섞느라 분주히 손을 놀려야 했다."], ["For the time being at least, I was saved.", "적어도 당분간은 나는 살아난 것이었다."], ["I still held on to the leg of the table, but clutched it now with the fervor of gratitude.", "나는 여전히 식탁 다리를 붙들고 있었으나, 이제는 그것을 감사의 열정으로 꽉 거머쥐었다."]], [["By degrees, I became calm enough to release my grasp and partake of pudding. Mr.", "차츰 나는 손아귀를 풀고 푸딩을 들 만큼 진정되었다."], ["Pumblechook partook of pudding.", "펌블추크 씨도 푸딩을 들었다."], ["All partook of pudding.", "모두가 푸딩을 들었다."], ["The course terminated, and Mr. Pumblechook had begun to beam under the genial influence of gin and water.", "그 순서가 끝나고, 펌블추크 씨는 진에 물을 탄 것의 흐뭇한 기운에 젖어 환히 빛나기 시작했다."], ["I began to think I should get over the day, when my sister said to Joe, “Clean plates,—cold.”", "이제 이날을 무사히 넘기겠구나 싶어질 무렵, 누나가 조에게 말했다. \"깨끗한 접시들을 — 차가운 걸로.\""]], [["I clutched the leg of the table again immediately, and pressed it to my bosom as if it had been the companion of my youth and friend of my soul.", "나는 즉시 다시 식탁 다리를 거머쥐고, 마치 그것이 내 어린 시절의 동무이자 내 영혼의 벗이라도 되는 양 가슴에 꼭 끌어안았다."], ["I foresaw what was coming, and I felt that this time I really was gone.", "나는 무엇이 닥쳐올지 내다보았고, 이번에야말로 정말로 끝장이라는 것을 느꼈다."]], [["“You must taste,”", "\"여러분, 맛보셔야 해요.\""], ["said my sister, addressing the guests with her best grace—“you must taste, to finish with, such a delightful and delicious present of Uncle Pumblechook’s!”", "누나가 더없이 우아한 태도로 손님들에게 말했다. \"마무리로 꼭 맛보셔야 해요. 펌블추크 아저씨가 주신 이 즐겁고도 맛있는 선물을 말이에요!\""]], [["Must they! Let them not hope to taste it!", "맛본다고! 그들이 그것을 맛볼 가망일랑 품지도 마시라!"]], [["“You must know,”", "\"실은 말이에요.\""], ["said my sister, rising, “it’s a pie; a savory pork pie.”", "누나가 일어서며 말했다. \"이건 파이예요. 맛있는 돼지고기 파이랍니다.\""]], [["The company murmured their compliments.", "손님들이 웅얼웅얼 칭찬을 늘어놓았다."], ["Uncle Pumblechook, sensible of having deserved well of his fellow-creatures, said,—quite vivaciously, all things considered,—“Well, Mrs.", "자기가 동료 인간들에게 칭송받을 만한 일을 했음을 의식한 펌블추크 아저씨는, 이 모든 정황을 감안하면 꽤나 활기차게 말했다."], ["Joe, we’ll do our best endeavours; let us have a cut at this same pie.”", "\"그럼, 조 부인, 우리 최선을 다해 봅시다. 어디 그 파이를 한번 잘라 보십시다.\""]], [["My sister went out to get it.", "누나는 그것을 가지러 나갔다."], ["I heard her steps proceed to the pantry.", "나는 그녀의 발소리가 저장실 쪽으로 향하는 것을 들었다."], ["I saw Mr. Pumblechook balance his knife.", "나는 펌블추크 씨가 나이프를 가늠하는 것을 보았다."], ["I saw reawakening appetite in the Roman nostrils of Mr. Wopsle. I heard Mr.", "나는 웝슬 씨의 로마식 콧구멍에서 식욕이 되살아나는 것을 보았다."], ["Hubble remark that “a bit of savory pork pie would lay atop of anything you could mention, and do no harm,” and I heard Joe say, “You shall have some, Pip.”", "나는 허블 씨가 \"맛있는 돼지고기 파이 한 조각이라면 무엇 위에 얹어도 다 잘 어울리고, 해될 게 없지요\"라고 말하는 것을 들었고, 조가 \"너도 좀 먹어야지, 핍\" 하고 말하는 것을 들었다."], ["I have never been absolutely certain whether I uttered a shrill yell of terror, merely in spirit, or in the bodily hearing of the company.", "내가 공포에 질려 날카로운 비명을 내질렀던 것이 한낱 마음속에서였는지, 아니면 좌중이 실제로 귀로 들을 만큼이었는지는 나로서도 끝내 확실히 알 수 없다."], ["I felt that I could bear no more, and that I must run away.", "나는 더는 견딜 수 없다는 것을, 그리고 달아나야만 한다는 것을 느꼈다."], ["I released the leg of the table, and ran for my life.", "나는 식탁 다리를 놓고, 죽기 살기로 내달렸다."]], [["But I ran no farther than the house door, for there I ran head-foremost into a party of soldiers with their muskets, one of whom held out a pair of handcuffs to me, saying, “Here you are, look sharp, come on!”", "그러나 나는 현관문보다 더 멀리는 달아나지 못했다. 거기서 나는 머스킷 총을 든 한 무리의 군인들에게 머리부터 들이받고 말았기 때문이다. 그중 하나가 내게 수갑 한 벌을 내밀며 말했다. \"여기 있구나, 꾸물대지 말고, 어서 가자!\""]], [["# Chapter V.", "# Chapter V."]], [["The apparition of a file of soldiers ringing down the but-ends of their loaded muskets on our door-step, caused the dinner-party to rise from table in confusion, and caused Mrs. Joe re-entering the kitchen empty-handed, to stop short and stare, in her wondering lament of “Gracious goodness gracious me, what’s gone—with the—pie!”", "장전된 머스킷의 개머리판을 우리 집 문간에 쾅쾅 내리찧으며 줄지어 나타난 군인들의 출현은, 식사 모임 사람들을 우왕좌왕 식탁에서 일어서게 만들었고, 빈손으로 부엌에 들어서던 조 부인을 우뚝 멈춰 세워 멍하니 바라보게 만들면서, 의아해하는 그 한탄을 자아냈다. \"어머나 세상에 맙소사, 어떻게 된 거야 — 그 — 파이가!\""]], [["The sergeant and I were in the kitchen when Mrs. Joe stood staring; at which crisis I partially recovered the use of my senses.", "조 부인이 멍하니 서서 바라보고 있을 때 상사와 나는 부엌에 있었는데, 바로 그 위기의 순간에 나는 정신의 기능을 어느 정도 되찾았다."], ["It was the sergeant who had spoken to me, and he was now looking round at the company, with his handcuffs invitingly extended towards them in his right hand, and his left on my shoulder.", "내게 말을 건넨 사람은 그 상사였고, 그는 이제 오른손에 든 수갑을 사람들 쪽으로 권하듯 내밀고 왼손은 내 어깨에 얹은 채, 좌중을 둘러보고 있었다."]], [["“Excuse me, ladies and gentleman,”", "\"실례하겠습니다, 신사 숙녀 여러분.\""], ["said the sergeant, “but as I have mentioned at the door to this smart young shaver,”", "상사가 말했다. \"다만 제가 문간에서 이 영리한 꼬마 녀석에게 이미 말씀드렸다시피,\""], ["(which he hadn’t), “I am on a chase in the name of the king, and I want the blacksmith.”", "(그런 적은 없었다) \"저는 국왕의 이름으로 추적 중이며, 대장장이를 찾고 있습니다.\""]], [["“And pray what might you want with him?”", "\"그래 그 사람한테 무슨 볼일이 있으시다는 거죠?\""], ["retorted my sister, quick to resent his being wanted at all.", "누나가 받아쳤다. 누나는 남편이 누군가에게 필요한 존재로 거론되는 것 자체에 발끈하는 데 빨랐다."]], [["“Missis,”", "\"부인.\""], ["returned the gallant sergeant, “speaking for myself, I should reply, the honour and pleasure of his fine wife’s acquaintance; speaking for the king, I answer, a little job done.”", "늠름한 상사가 대답했다. \"제 개인적으로 말씀드리자면, 부인같이 훌륭한 분과 알게 된 영광과 기쁨이라고 답하겠고, 국왕을 대신해 말씀드리자면, 자그마한 일거리 하나라고 답하겠습니다.\""]], [["This was received as rather neat in the sergeant; insomuch that Mr. Pumblechook cried audibly, “Good again!”", "이것이 상사로서는 제법 깔끔한 응답으로 받아들여졌고, 그리하여 펌블추크 씨가 들리도록 소리쳤다. \"또 한 방 멋지군!\""]], [["“You see, blacksmith,”", "\"실은 말입니다, 대장장이 양반.\""], ["said the sergeant, who had by this time picked out Joe with his eye, “we have had an accident with these, and I find the lock of one of ’em goes wrong, and the coupling don’t act pretty.", "이때쯤 눈으로 조를 가려낸 상사가 말했다. \"이것들에 사고가 좀 생겼는데, 그중 하나는 자물쇠가 잘못됐고 연결 고리도 제대로 작동하질 않습니다."], ["As they are wanted for immediate service, will you throw your eye over them?”", "당장 써먹어야 하는 물건들이니, 한번 살펴봐 주시겠소?\""]], [["Joe threw his eye over them, and pronounced that the job would necessitate the lighting of his forge fire, and would take nearer two hours than one. “Will it?", "조는 그것들을 한번 살펴보더니, 그 일을 하려면 화덕 불을 지펴야 하고, 한 시간보다는 두 시간에 가깝게 걸릴 것이라고 단언했다. \"그래요?"], ["Then will you set about it at once, blacksmith?”", "그럼 곧장 시작해 주시겠소, 대장장이 양반?\""], ["said the off-hand sergeant, “as it’s on his Majesty’s service.", "격식 없는 상사가 말했다. \"국왕 폐하의 일이니 말이오."], ["And if my men can bear a hand anywhere, they’ll make themselves useful.”", "그리고 내 부하들이 어디서든 거들 수 있다면 쓸모 있게 부려 보시구려.\""], ["With that, he called to his men, who came trooping into the kitchen one after another, and piled their arms in a corner.", "그러고서 그는 부하들을 불렀고, 그들은 차례차례 우르르 부엌으로 들어와 무기를 한쪽 구석에 쌓아 두었다."], ["And then they stood about, as soldiers do; now, with their hands loosely clasped before them; now, resting a knee or a shoulder; now, easing a belt or a pouch; now, opening the door to spit stiffly over their high stocks, out into the yard.", "그러고는 군인들이 으레 그러듯 여기저기 서 있었다. 어떤 이는 두 손을 앞으로 느슨히 맞잡고, 어떤 이는 무릎이나 어깨를 어딘가에 기대고, 어떤 이는 허리띠나 탄낭을 느슨히 풀고, 또 어떤 이는 문을 열고 높이 두른 옷깃 너머로 뻣뻣하게 마당으로 침을 뱉었다."]], [["All these things I saw without then knowing that I saw them, for I was in an agony of apprehension.", "이 모든 것을 나는 보면서도 그때는 보고 있다는 것을 알지 못했으니, 두려움의 고통 속에 빠져 있었기 때문이다."], ["But beginning to perceive that the handcuffs were not for me, and that the military had so far got the better of the pie as to put it in the background, I collected a little more of my scattered wits.", "그러나 그 수갑이 나를 위한 것이 아니라는 것, 그리고 군인들이 적어도 파이를 뒷전으로 밀어낼 만큼은 그것을 압도해 버렸다는 것을 차츰 알아차리면서, 나는 흩어졌던 정신을 조금 더 추슬렀다."]], [["“Would you give me the time?”", "\"몇 시인지 알려 주시겠소?\""], ["said the sergeant, addressing himself to Mr. Pumblechook, as to a man whose appreciative powers justified the inference that he was equal to the time.", "상사가 펌블추크 씨를 향해 물었는데, 마치 그 사람의 안목이라면 능히 시간을 알 만한 인물이라는 추론이 정당화된다는 투였다."]], [["“It’s just gone half past two.”", "\"이제 막 두 시 반이 지났소이다.\""]], [["“That’s not so bad,”", "\"그리 나쁘지 않군.\""], ["said the sergeant, reflecting; “even if I was forced to halt here nigh two hours, that’ll do.", "상사가 생각에 잠겨 말했다. \"설령 여기서 두 시간 가까이 발이 묶인다 해도 그 정도면 됐어."], ["How far might you call yourselves from the marshes, hereabouts?", "여기서 습지까지 얼마나 된다고들 보시오?"], ["Not above a mile, I reckon?”", "일 마일이 채 안 되겠지?\""]], [["“Just a mile,”", "\"딱 일 마일이에요.\""], ["said Mrs. Joe.", "조 부인이 말했다."]], [["“That’ll do.", "\"그 정도면 됐어."], ["We begin to close in upon ’em about dusk.", "우리는 해 질 녘쯤 그놈들을 좁혀 들어가기 시작하지."], ["A little before dusk, my orders are.", "해 지기 조금 전에 한다는 게 내 명령이오."], ["That’ll do.”", "그 정도면 됐어.\""]], [["“Convicts, sergeant?” asked Mr.", "\"죄수들입니까, 상사님?\""], ["Wopsle, in a matter-of-course way.", "우프슬 씨가 당연하다는 투로 물었다."]], [["“Ay!” returned the sergeant, “two.", "\"그렇소!\" 상사가 대답했다. \"둘이오."], ["They’re pretty well known to be out on the marshes still, and they won’t try to get clear of ’em before dusk.", "그놈들이 아직 습지에 나와 있다는 건 제법 알려진 사실이고, 어두워지기 전에는 거기서 벗어나려 들지 않을 거요."], ["Anybody here seen anything of any such game?”", "여기 누구든 그런 사냥감을 본 사람이 있소?\""]], [["Everybody, myself excepted, said no, with confidence. Nobody thought of me.", "나를 제외한 모두가 자신 있게 아니라고 말했다. 아무도 나를 떠올리지 못했다."]], [["“Well!”", "\"그래!\""], ["said the sergeant, “they’ll find themselves trapped in a circle, I expect, sooner than they count on.", "상사가 말했다. \"그놈들은 제 예상보다 빨리 포위망에 갇힌 신세가 될 거요. 자, 대장장이 양반!"], ["Now, blacksmith! If you’re ready, his Majesty the King is.”", "당신만 준비됐다면, 국왕 폐하께서는 준비되셨소.\""]], [["Joe had got his coat and waistcoat and cravat off, and his leather apron on, and passed into the forge.", "조는 외투와 조끼와 넥타이를 벗고 가죽 앞치마를 두른 채 화덕으로 들어갔다."], ["One of the soldiers opened its wooden windows, another lighted the fire, another turned to at the bellows, the rest stood round the blaze, which was soon roaring.", "군인 한 명이 나무 덧창을 열었고, 다른 한 명이 불을 지폈으며, 또 다른 한 명이 풀무로 돌아섰고, 나머지는 이내 활활 타오르는 불길 둘레에 서 있었다."], ["Then Joe began to hammer and clink, hammer and clink, and we all looked on.", "그러자 조가 망치질을 시작했다, 쾅 챙, 쾅 챙, 그리고 우리는 모두 그 광경을 지켜보았다."]], [["The interest of the impending pursuit not only absorbed the general attention, but even made my sister liberal.", "다가올 추격에 대한 관심은 모두의 주의를 사로잡았을 뿐 아니라, 우리 누나마저도 후하게 만들었다."], ["She drew a pitcher of beer from the cask for the soldiers, and invited the sergeant to take a glass of brandy. But Mr.", "누나는 군인들을 위해 통에서 맥주 한 주전자를 따랐고, 상사에게는 브랜디 한 잔을 권했다. 그러나 펌블추크 씨가 날카롭게 말했다."], ["Pumblechook said, sharply, “Give him wine, Mum. I’ll engage there’s no tar in that:”", "\"포도주를 드리시오, 부인. 그건 타르 냄새가 안 날 거라고 내가 보증하리다.\""], ["so, the sergeant thanked him and said that as he preferred his drink without tar, he would take wine, if it was equally convenient.", "그리하여 상사는 그에게 고마워하며, 자기는 타르가 안 든 술을 좋아하니 마찬가지로 괜찮다면 포도주를 마시겠다고 했다."], ["When it was given him, he drank his Majesty’s health and compliments of the season, and took it all at a mouthful and smacked his lips.", "포도주가 주어지자 그는 국왕 폐하의 만수무강과 계절의 안부를 빌며 단숨에 들이켜고는 입맛을 다셨다."]], [["“Good stuff, eh, sergeant?”", "\"좋은 물건이지요, 상사 양반?\""], ["said Mr. Pumblechook.", "펌블추크 씨가 말했다."]], [["“I’ll tell you something,”", "\"내 한 가지 말씀드리리다.\""], ["returned the sergeant; “I suspect that stuff’s of your providing.”", "상사가 대답했다. \"그 물건이 댁에서 마련한 것 아닌가 싶소만.\""]], [["Mr.", "펌블추크 씨가 살집 좋은 웃음을 지으며 말했다."], ["Pumblechook, with a fat sort of laugh, said, “Ay, ay? Why?”", "\"허허, 그렇소? 어째서요?\""]], [["“Because,”", "\"왜냐하면,\""], ["returned the sergeant, clapping him on the shoulder, “you’re a man that knows what’s what.”", "상사가 그의 어깨를 탁 치며 대답했다. \"댁은 뭐가 뭔지 아는 사람이니까요.\""]], [["“D’ye think so?”", "\"그렇게 보시오?\""], ["said Mr. Pumblechook, with his former laugh.", "펌블추크 씨가 아까와 같은 웃음을 지으며 말했다."], ["“Have another glass!”", "\"한 잔 더 드시오!\""]], [["“With you. Hob and nob,”", "\"댁과 함께라면."], ["returned the sergeant.", "자 건배합시다.\""], ["“The top of mine to the foot of yours,—the foot of yours to the top of mine,—Ring once, ring twice,—the best tune on the Musical Glasses! Your health.", "상사가 대답했다. \"내 잔 위쪽을 댁 잔 바닥에 — 댁 잔 바닥을 내 잔 위쪽에 — 한 번 울리고, 두 번 울리고 — 유리잔 악기로 켜는 가장 멋진 가락이지!"], ["May you live a thousand years, and never be a worse judge of the right sort than you are at the present moment of your life!”", "댁의 건강을 위하여. 천 년을 사시고, 지금 이 순간 좋은 물건을 가려내는 안목보다 결코 못한 안목을 갖는 일이 없으시길!\""]], [["The sergeant tossed off his glass again and seemed quite ready for another glass.", "상사는 다시 잔을 비웠고 또 한 잔을 받을 준비가 단단히 되어 보였다."], ["I noticed that Mr. Pumblechook in his hospitality appeared to forget that he had made a present of the wine, but took the bottle from Mrs. Joe and had all the credit of handing it about in a gush of joviality.", "나는 펌블추크 씨가 손님 대접에 열을 올리느라 자기가 그 포도주를 선물한 사실은 잊어버린 듯, 조 부인에게서 병을 받아 들고는 흥겨움을 흘려 가며 그것을 돌리는 공을 죄다 차지하는 것을 눈여겨보았다."], ["Even I got some. And he was so very free of the wine that he even called for the other bottle, and handed that about with the same liberality, when the first was gone.", "나에게도 조금 돌아왔다. 그리고 그는 포도주에 어찌나 후했던지, 첫 병이 비자 다른 병까지 가져오라 청하고는 그것도 똑같이 후하게 돌렸다."]], [["As I watched them while they all stood clustering about the forge, enjoying themselves so much, I thought what terrible good sauce for a dinner my fugitive friend on the marshes was.", "그들이 모두 화덕 둘레에 모여 서서 그토록 즐거워하는 것을 지켜보면서, 나는 습지에 있는 내 도망자 친구가 식사를 위한 얼마나 무시무시한 양념인지를 생각했다."], ["They had not enjoyed themselves a quarter so much, before the entertainment was brightened with the excitement he furnished.", "그가 제공한 흥분으로 그 자리가 환해지기 전까지는, 그들이 그 사분의 일만큼도 즐거워하지 않았던 것이다."], ["And now, when they were all in lively anticipation of “the two villains” being taken, and when the bellows seemed to roar for the fugitives, the fire to flare for them, the smoke to hurry away in pursuit of them, Joe to hammer and clink for them, and all the murky shadows on the wall to shake at them in menace as the blaze rose and sank, and the red-hot sparks dropped and died, the pale afternoon outside almost seemed in my pitying young fancy to have turned pale on their account, poor wretches.", "그리고 이제, 그들 모두가 \"두 악당\"이 붙잡히기를 들떠 고대하고 있을 때, 그리고 풀무가 그 도망자들을 위해 으르렁대고, 불이 그들을 위해 활활 타오르고, 연기가 그들을 뒤쫓아 황급히 사라지고, 조가 그들을 위해 쾅 챙 망치질을 하고, 벽에 어린 음침한 그림자들이 모두 불길이 솟았다 잦아들고 시뻘건 불티가 떨어졌다 사그라드는 데 따라 위협하듯 그들을 향해 흔들리는 듯 보일 때, 바깥의 창백한 오후는 가엾이 여기는 내 어린 상상 속에서, 그 가련한 작자들 때문에 창백해진 듯이만 보였다."]], [["At last, Joe’s job was done, and the ringing and roaring stopped.", "마침내 조의 일이 끝났고, 망치 소리와 으르렁대는 소리가 멈췄다."], ["As Joe got on his coat, he mustered courage to propose that some of us should go down with the soldiers and see what came of the hunt. Mr.", "조는 외투를 걸치면서 용기를 내어, 우리 중 몇몇이 군인들과 함께 내려가 사냥이 어찌 되는지 보자고 제안했다."], ["Pumblechook and Mr. Hubble declined, on the plea of a pipe and ladies’ society; but Mr. Wopsle said he would go, if Joe would.", "펌블추크 씨와 허블 씨는 담배와 부인네들과의 자리를 핑계 삼아 사양했으나, 우프슬 씨는 조가 간다면 자기도 가겠다고 했다."], ["Joe said he was agreeable, and would take me, if Mrs. Joe approved.", "조는 자기는 괜찮으며, 조 부인이 허락한다면 나도 데려가겠다고 했다."], ["We never should have got leave to go, I am sure, but for Mrs. Joe’s curiosity to know all about it and how it ended.", "분명 조 부인이 그 일의 자초지종과 결말을 알고 싶어 하는 호기심이 없었더라면, 우리는 결코 허락을 받지 못했을 것이다."], ["As it was, she merely stipulated, “If you bring the boy back with his head blown to bits by a musket, don’t look to me to put it together again.”", "사정이 그러했기에, 누나는 그저 이렇게 단서를 달았다. \"애 머리가 머스킷에 산산조각 나서 데려올 거면, 그걸 다시 붙여 달라고 나한테는 바라지도 말아요.\""]], [["The sergeant took a polite leave of the ladies, and parted from Mr. Pumblechook as from a comrade; though I doubt if he were quite as fully sensible of that gentleman’s merits under arid conditions, as when something moist was going.", "상사는 부인네들에게 정중히 작별을 고하고, 펌블추크 씨와는 전우와 헤어지듯 작별했다. 다만 마른 형편에서도 그 양반의 미덕을, 무언가 촉촉한 것이 오갈 때만큼이나 온전히 알아주었는지는 의심스럽다."], ["His men resumed their muskets and fell in. Mr.", "부하들은 다시 머스킷을 들고 대열을 갖췄다."], ["Wopsle, Joe, and I, received strict charge to keep in the rear, and to speak no word after we reached the marshes.", "우프슬 씨와 조와 나는 뒤쪽에 붙어 있고 습지에 다다른 뒤로는 한마디도 하지 말라는 엄한 명령을 받았다."], ["When we were all out in the raw air and were steadily moving towards our business, I treasonably whispered to Joe, “I hope, Joe, we shan’t find them.”", "우리 모두가 차가운 바깥 공기 속으로 나와 우리의 임무를 향해 꾸준히 나아갈 때, 나는 반역하듯 조에게 속삭였다. \"조, 그자들을 못 찾았으면 좋겠어요.\""], ["and Joe whispered to me, “I’d give a shilling if they had cut and run, Pip.”", "그러자 조가 내게 속삭였다. \"그놈들이 줄행랑을 쳤다면 일 실링이라도 내겠다, 핍.\""]], [["We were joined by no stragglers from the village, for the weather was cold and threatening, the way dreary, the footing bad, darkness coming on, and the people had good fires in-doors and were keeping the day.", "마을에서 함께 나선 어슬렁이는 사람은 아무도 없었으니, 날씨는 춥고 험악했으며, 길은 음산하고, 발 딛기는 나빴고, 어둠이 다가오고 있었으며, 사람들은 집 안에 불을 잘 지펴 놓고 그날을 즐기고 있었던 것이다."], ["A few faces hurried to glowing windows and looked after us, but none came out. We passed the finger-post, and held straight on to the churchyard.", "몇몇 얼굴이 환히 빛나는 창가로 황급히 다가와 우리 뒷모습을 바라보았으나, 밖으로 나오는 사람은 없었다. 우리는 이정표를 지나 곧장 교회 묘지로 나아갔다."], ["There we were stopped a few minutes by a signal from the sergeant’s hand, while two or three of his men dispersed themselves among the graves, and also examined the porch.", "거기서 상사의 손짓 신호에 따라 우리는 몇 분 멈춰 섰고, 그동안 부하 두세 명이 무덤들 사이로 흩어져 현관도 살펴보았다."], ["They came in again without finding anything, and then we struck out on the open marshes, through the gate at the side of the churchyard.", "그들은 아무것도 찾지 못한 채 다시 돌아왔고, 그러고 나서 우리는 교회 묘지 옆 문을 지나 탁 트인 습지로 나아갔다."], ["A bitter sleet came rattling against us here on the east wind, and Joe took me on his back.", "여기서 동풍을 타고 매서운 진눈깨비가 후두두 우리에게 들이쳤고, 조가 나를 등에 업었다."]], [["Now that we were out upon the dismal wilderness where they little thought I had been within eight or nine hours and had seen both men hiding, I considered for the first time, with great dread, if we should come upon them, would my particular convict suppose that it was I who had brought the soldiers there?", "이제 우리는 그 황량한 황야로 나와 있었으니, 그들은 내가 여덟아홉 시간 안에 이곳에 와 있었고 두 사내가 숨은 것을 다 보았다는 사실을 까맣게 몰랐다. 나는 처음으로 크나큰 두려움에 잠겨, 만약 우리가 그자들과 마주친다면, 내 그 특별한 죄수는 군인들을 여기로 데려온 것이 바로 나라고 여기지 않을까 하고 생각했다."], ["He had asked me if I was a deceiving imp, and he had said I should be a fierce young hound if I joined the hunt against him.", "그는 내게 속임수를 쓰는 도깨비냐고 물었었고, 만약 내가 자기를 잡으려는 사냥에 끼어든다면 사나운 어린 사냥개가 될 거라고 했었다."], ["Would he believe that I was both imp and hound in treacherous earnest, and had betrayed him?", "그는 내가 배신의 진심으로 도깨비이자 사냥개가 되어, 자기를 팔아넘겼다고 믿을까?"]], [["It was of no use asking myself this question now.", "이제 와서 내게 이런 질문을 던져 봐야 소용없었다."], ["There I was, on Joe’s back, and there was Joe beneath me, charging at the ditches like a hunter, and stimulating Mr. Wopsle not to tumble on his Roman nose, and to keep up with us.", "나는 조의 등에 업혀 있었고, 그 아래에는 조가 있어 사냥꾼처럼 도랑을 향해 돌진하며, 우프슬 씨에게 그 로마식 코를 처박지 말고 우리를 따라오라고 다그치고 있었다."], ["The soldiers were in front of us, extending into a pretty wide line with an interval between man and man.", "군인들은 우리 앞쪽에서 사람과 사람 사이에 간격을 두고 제법 넓은 횡렬을 이루며 뻗어 나가고 있었다."], ["We were taking the course I had begun with, and from which I had diverged in the mist.", "우리는 내가 처음에 들어섰다가 안개 속에서 벗어났던 바로 그 길을 가고 있었다."], ["Either the mist was not out again yet, or the wind had dispelled it.", "안개가 아직 다시 끼지 않았거나, 아니면 바람이 그것을 흩어 버린 모양이었다."], ["Under the low red glare of sunset, the beacon, and the gibbet, and the mound of the Battery, and the opposite shore of the river, were plain, though all of a watery lead colour.", "낮게 붉게 타는 노을 아래, 표지등과 교수대와 포대의 둔덕과 강 건너편 기슭이 또렷이 보였으니, 모두가 물기 어린 납빛이긴 했지만."]], [["With my heart thumping like a blacksmith at Joe’s broad shoulder, I looked all about for any sign of the convicts.", "대장장이의 망치질처럼 쿵쿵 뛰는 심장을 안고 조의 떡 벌어진 어깨에 매달린 채, 나는 죄수들의 기척이 있을까 사방을 두루 살폈다."], ["I could see none, I could hear none. Mr.", "아무것도 보이지 않았고, 아무것도 들리지 않았다."], ["Wopsle had greatly alarmed me more than once, by his blowing and hard breathing; but I knew the sounds by this time, and could dissociate them from the object of pursuit.", "우프슬 씨가 헐떡이며 거칠게 내쉬는 숨소리에 한 번 이상 크게 놀랐으나, 이때쯤에는 그 소리를 알아차려 추격의 대상과 떼어 생각할 수 있었다."], ["I got a dreadful start, when I thought I heard the file still going; but it was only a sheep-bell.", "줄칼 가는 소리가 아직 들린다고 여겼을 때 끔찍이 놀랐으나, 그것은 양의 방울 소리일 뿐이었다."], ["The sheep stopped in their eating and looked timidly at us; and the cattle, their heads turned from the wind and sleet, stared angrily as if they held us responsible for both annoyances; but, except these things, and the shudder of the dying day in every blade of grass, there was no break in the bleak stillness of the marshes.", "양들은 풀 뜯기를 멈추고 겁먹은 듯 우리를 바라보았고, 소들은 바람과 진눈깨비를 등진 채 마치 그 두 가지 괴로움을 다 우리 탓으로 여기는 양 성난 눈으로 노려보았다. 그러나 이런 것들과, 풀잎 하나하나에 어린 저무는 하루의 떨림을 빼면, 습지의 황량한 정적을 깨뜨리는 것은 아무것도 없었다."]], [["The soldiers were moving on in the direction of the old Battery, and we were moving on a little way behind them, when, all of a sudden, we all stopped.", "군인들은 옛 포대 방향으로 나아가고 있었고, 우리는 그들 조금 뒤에서 나아가고 있었는데, 그때 느닷없이 우리 모두가 멈춰 섰다."], ["For there had reached us on the wings of the wind and rain, a long shout.", "비바람의 날개를 타고 길게 외치는 소리가 우리에게 닿았기 때문이다."], ["It was repeated. It was at a distance towards the east, but it was long and loud.", "그 소리가 되풀이되었다. 동쪽으로 멀리 떨어진 곳에서 났으나 길고 컸다."], ["Nay, there seemed to be two or more shouts raised together,—if one might judge from a confusion in the sound.", "아니, 소리의 혼란스러움으로 미루어 판단하자면, 두 사람 이상이 함께 내지르는 외침인 듯했다."]], [["To this effect the sergeant and the nearest men were speaking under their breath, when Joe and I came up.", "조와 내가 다가갔을 때, 상사와 가장 가까이 있는 부하들이 이런 취지로 낮은 목소리로 말하고 있었다."], ["After another moment’s listening, Joe (who was a good judge) agreed, and Mr. Wopsle (who was a bad judge) agreed.", "잠시 더 귀를 기울인 뒤, 조는 (잘 분간하는 사람이었다) 동의했고, 우프슬 씨도 (서툴게 분간하는 사람이었다) 동의했다."], ["The sergeant, a decisive man, ordered that the sound should not be answered, but that the course should be changed, and that his men should make towards it “at the double.”", "결단력 있는 사람인 상사는, 그 소리에 응답하지 말되 진로를 바꿔 부하들이 그쪽을 향해 \"구보로\" 나아가라고 명령했다."], ["So we slanted to the right (where the East was), and Joe pounded away so wonderfully, that I had to hold on tight to keep my seat.", "그리하여 우리는 오른쪽으로 (동쪽이 있는 곳이었다) 비스듬히 꺾어 들었고, 조가 어찌나 놀랍게 내달렸던지 나는 자리를 지키려고 꽉 붙들어야 했다."]], [["It was a run indeed now, and what Joe called, in the only two words he spoke all the time, “a Winder.”", "이제는 그야말로 달음박질이었으니, 조가 그동안 내내 입에 올린 단 두 마디로 표현하자면 \"쏜살\" 같은 것이었다."], ["Down banks and up banks, and over gates, and splashing into dikes, and breaking among coarse rushes: no man cared where he went.", "둑을 내려갔다 오르고, 문을 넘고, 도랑에 첨벙 빠지고, 거친 골풀을 헤치고 — 누구도 자기가 어디로 가는지 따위는 개의치 않았다."], ["As we came nearer to the shouting, it became more and more apparent that it was made by more than one voice.", "외침에 가까워질수록, 그것이 한 사람 이상의 목소리로 이루어진 것임이 점점 더 분명해졌다."], ["Sometimes, it seemed to stop altogether, and then the soldiers stopped.", "때로는 아예 멈추는 듯했고, 그러면 군인들도 멈췄다."], ["When it broke out again, the soldiers made for it at a greater rate than ever, and we after them.", "그것이 다시 터져 나오면, 군인들은 어느 때보다 더 빠르게 그쪽으로 내달렸고, 우리도 그 뒤를 따랐다."], ["After a while, we had so run it down, that we could hear one voice calling “Murder!” and another voice, “Convicts!", "얼마 후 우리는 그 소리에 바싹 따라붙어, 한 목소리가 \"사람 죽인다!\"라고 외치고 다른 목소리가 \"죄수다! 탈주범이다! 경비병!"], ["Runaways! Guard! This way for the runaway convicts!”", "탈주한 죄수들은 이쪽이다!\"라고 외치는 것을 들을 수 있었다."], ["Then both voices would seem to be stifled in a struggle, and then would break out again.", "그러더니 두 목소리가 다 몸싸움 속에 묻혀 버리는 듯하다가, 다시 터져 나오곤 했다."], ["And when it had come to this, the soldiers ran like deer, and Joe too.", "그리고 이 지경에 이르자, 군인들은 사슴처럼 내달렸고, 조 역시 그러했다."]], [["The sergeant ran in first, when we had run the noise quite down, and two of his men ran in close upon him.", "우리가 그 소리를 완전히 따라잡았을 때, 상사가 맨 먼저 뛰어들었고 부하 둘이 바싹 뒤따라 뛰어들었다."], ["Their pieces were cocked and levelled when we all ran in.", "우리가 모두 뛰어들었을 때 그들의 총은 공이가 당겨지고 겨눠져 있었다."]], [["“Here are both men!” panted the sergeant, struggling at the bottom of a ditch.", "\"두 놈 다 여기 있다!\" 상사가 도랑 바닥에서 몸싸움을 벌이며 헐떡였다."], ["“Surrender, you two!", "\"항복해, 이 둘!"], ["and confound you for two wild beasts! Come asunder!”", "이 두 마리 들짐승 같은 놈들, 빌어먹을! 떨어져!\""]], [["Water was splashing, and mud was flying, and oaths were being sworn, and blows were being struck, when some more men went down into the ditch to help the sergeant, and dragged out, separately, my convict and the other one.", "물이 첨벙거리고, 진흙이 튀고, 욕설이 오가고, 주먹질이 벌어지는 가운데, 군인 몇몇이 더 도랑으로 내려가 상사를 거들었고, 내 죄수와 다른 한 명을 따로따로 끌어냈다."], ["Both were bleeding and panting and execrating and struggling; but of course I knew them both directly.", "둘 다 피를 흘리며 헐떡이고 저주하며 발버둥 쳤으나, 물론 나는 둘 다 단번에 알아보았다."]], [["“Mind!”", "\"잘 봐 둬!\""], ["said my convict, wiping blood from his face with his ragged sleeves, and shaking torn hair from his fingers: “I took him!", "내 죄수가 너덜너덜한 소맷자락으로 얼굴의 피를 닦고 손가락에 엉킨 뜯긴 머리카락을 털어 내며 말했다. \"내가 저놈을 잡았어!"], ["I give him up to you!", "내가 저놈을 너희한테 넘겨주는 거야!"], ["Mind that!”", "그걸 잘 봐 둬!\""]], [["“It’s not much to be particular about,”", "\"그리 따져 들 일은 못 되네.\""], ["said the sergeant; “it’ll do you small good, my man, being in the same plight yourself.", "상사가 말했다. \"그래 봤자 자네한테 좋을 거 별로 없어, 이 사람아, 자네도 똑같은 처지인데."], ["Handcuffs there!”", "거기 수갑 채워!\""]], [["“I don’t expect it to do me any good.", "\"좋을 게 있길 바라지도 않아."], ["I don’t want it to do me more good than it does now,”", "지금 좋은 것보다 더 좋아지길 바라지도 않아.\""], ["said my convict, with a greedy laugh. “I took him.", "내 죄수가 탐욕스러운 웃음을 지으며 말했다. \"내가 저놈을 잡았어."], ["He knows it.", "저놈도 알아."], ["That’s enough for me.”", "나한텐 그걸로 충분해.\""]], [["The other convict was livid to look at, and, in addition to the old bruised left side of his face, seemed to be bruised and torn all over.", "다른 죄수는 보기에 시퍼렇게 질려 있었고, 얼굴 왼쪽의 묵은 멍에 더해 온몸이 멍들고 찢긴 듯했다."], ["He could not so much as get his breath to speak, until they were both separately handcuffed, but leaned upon a soldier to keep himself from falling.", "둘 다 따로따로 수갑이 채워질 때까지 그는 말을 할 숨조차 쉬지 못한 채, 쓰러지지 않으려고 군인 하나에게 기대고 있었다."]], [["“Take notice, guard,—he tried to murder me,”", "\"잘 봐 두시오, 경비병 양반 — 이자가 나를 죽이려 했소.\""], ["were his first words.", "그가 처음으로 내뱉은 말이었다."]], [["“Tried to murder him?”", "\"이자를 죽이려 했다고?\""], ["said my convict, disdainfully.", "내 죄수가 경멸하듯 말했다."], ["“Try, and not do it?", "\"시도만 하고 못 했다?"], ["I took him, and giv’ him up; that’s what I done.", "나는 저놈을 잡아서 넘겨줬어. 그게 내가 한 일이야."], ["I not only prevented him getting off the marshes, but I dragged him here,—dragged him this far on his way back.", "나는 저놈이 습지에서 빠져나가는 걸 막았을 뿐 아니라, 저놈을 여기까지 끌고 왔어 — 돌아가는 길 이만큼이나 끌고 온 거야."], ["He’s a gentleman, if you please, this villain.", "이 악당이, 이래 봬도 신사라 이 말이지."], ["Now, the Hulks has got its gentleman again, through me. Murder him?", "자, 나 덕분에 감옥선이 제 신사를 다시 갖게 됐어. 저놈을 죽인다고?"], ["Worth my while, too, to murder him, when I could do worse and drag him back!”", "더 고약한 짓을 해서 끌고 돌아올 수 있는데, 굳이 죽이느라 수고할 까닭이 있겠나!\""]], [["The other one still gasped, “He tried—he tried-to—murder me.", "다른 한 명은 여전히 헐떡이며 말했다. \"이자가 — 이자가 — 나를 — 죽이려 했소."], ["Bear—bear witness.”", "증인 — 증인이 되어 주시오.\""]], [["“Lookee here!” said my convict to the sergeant.", "\"이봐, 들어 보라고!\" 내 죄수가 상사에게 말했다."], ["“Single-handed I got clear of the prison-ship; I made a dash and I done it.", "\"나는 혼자 힘으로 감옥선에서 빠져나왔어. 단숨에 내달려 해낸 거야."], ["I could ha’ got clear of these death-cold flats likewise—look at my leg: you won’t find much iron on it—if I hadn’t made the discovery that he was here.", "이 죽도록 차가운 벌판에서도 마찬가지로 빠져나갈 수 있었어 — 내 다리를 봐, 쇠붙이가 별로 없을걸 — 저놈이 여기 있다는 걸 알아채지만 않았어도 말이야."], ["Let him go free?", "저놈을 풀어 줘?"], ["Let him profit by the means as I found out?", "내가 알아낸 수단으로 저놈이 덕을 보게 둬?"], ["Let him make a tool of me afresh and again?", "저놈이 나를 또다시 도구로 부려먹게 둬?"], ["Once more?", "한 번 더?"], ["No, no, no. If I had died at the bottom there,”", "안 돼, 안 돼, 안 돼. 내가 저기 바닥에서 죽었더라도,\""], ["and he made an emphatic swing at the ditch with his manacled hands, “I’d have held to him with that grip, that you should have been safe to find him in my hold.”", "그러면서 그는 수갑 찬 두 손을 도랑 쪽으로 힘차게 휘둘렀다. \"이 손아귀로 저놈을 붙들고 늘어졌을 테니, 당신들은 내 손아귀에서 틀림없이 저놈을 찾아냈을 거야.\""]], [["The other fugitive, who was evidently in extreme horror of his companion, repeated, “He tried to murder me.", "자기 동료를 극도로 무서워하는 게 분명한 다른 도망자가 되풀이했다. \"이자가 나를 죽이려 했소."], ["I should have been a dead man if you had not come up.”", "당신들이 오지 않았으면 나는 죽은 목숨이었을 거요.\""]], [["“He lies!”", "\"거짓말이야!\""], ["said my convict, with fierce energy.", "내 죄수가 사납게 기운을 뻗치며 말했다."], ["“He’s a liar born, and he’ll die a liar.", "\"저놈은 타고난 거짓말쟁이고, 거짓말쟁이로 죽을 거야."], ["Look at his face; ain’t it written there?", "저놈 얼굴을 봐. 거기 다 쓰여 있지 않나?"], ["Let him turn those eyes of his on me.", "저놈더러 그 눈을 나한테 돌려 보라고 해."], ["I defy him to do it.”", "못 할걸, 내 장담하지.\""]], [["The other, with an effort at a scornful smile, which could not, however, collect the nervous working of his mouth into any set expression, looked at the soldiers, and looked about at the marshes and at the sky, but certainly did not look at the speaker.", "다른 한 명은 비웃는 웃음을 지으려 애썼으나, 그것이 입가의 신경질적인 떨림을 어떤 정해진 표정으로 그러모으지는 못한 채, 군인들을 바라보고 습지와 하늘을 두루 둘러보았으나, 분명코 말하는 자는 바라보지 않았다."]], [["“Do you see him?”", "\"저놈 보이지?\""], ["pursued my convict.", "내 죄수가 계속했다."], ["“Do you see what a villain he is?", "\"저놈이 얼마나 악당인지 보이지?"], ["Do you see those grovelling and wandering eyes?", "저 비굴하게 굴며 이리저리 헤매는 눈깔이 보이지?"], ["That’s how he looked when we were tried together. He never looked at me.”", "우리가 함께 재판받을 때도 저랬어. 한 번도 나를 쳐다보지 않았지.\""]], [["The other, always working and working his dry lips and turning his eyes restlessly about him far and near, did at last turn them for a moment on the speaker, with the words, “You are not much to look at,”", "다른 한 명은 마른 입술을 자꾸자꾸 움직이고 눈을 멀리 가까이 쉴 새 없이 굴리다가, 마침내 잠깐 그 눈을 말하는 자에게 돌리며, \"당신, 봐줄 만한 꼴은 못 되는군.\""], ["and with a half-taunting glance at the bound hands.", "하고 말하고는, 묶인 손을 반쯤 빈정대는 눈길로 흘끔 보았다."], ["At that point, my convict became so frantically exasperated, that he would have rushed upon him but for the interposition of the soldiers.", "그 순간 내 죄수는 어찌나 미친 듯이 격분했던지, 군인들이 가로막지 않았더라면 그에게 달려들었을 것이다. \"내가 말하지 않았소,\""], ["“Didn’t I tell you,” said the other convict then, “that he would murder me, if he could?”", "그러자 다른 죄수가 말했다. \"할 수만 있다면 이자가 나를 죽일 거라고?\""], ["And any one could see that he shook with fear, and that there broke out upon his lips curious white flakes, like thin snow.", "그리고 그가 두려움에 떨고 있다는 것, 그리고 그 입술에 묽은 눈송이 같은 기묘한 흰 거품이 일고 있다는 것을 누구든 알아볼 수 있었다."]], [["“Enough of this parley,”", "\"이 입씨름은 이만하면 됐다.\""], ["said the sergeant.", "상사가 말했다."], ["“Light those torches.”", "\"저 횃불에 불을 붙여라.\""]], [["As one of the soldiers, who carried a basket in lieu of a gun, went down on his knee to open it, my convict looked round him for the first time, and saw me.", "총 대신 바구니를 든 군인 하나가 그것을 열려고 한쪽 무릎을 꿇었을 때, 내 죄수는 처음으로 주위를 둘러보다가 나를 보았다."], ["I had alighted from Joe’s back on the brink of the ditch when we came up, and had not moved since.", "우리가 다가왔을 때 나는 도랑 가장자리에서 조의 등에서 내렸고, 그 후로 움직이지 않고 있었다."], ["I looked at him eagerly when he looked at me, and slightly moved my hands and shook my head.", "그가 나를 바라볼 때 나는 간절히 그를 바라보았고, 손을 살짝 움직이며 고개를 가로저었다."], ["I had been waiting for him to see me that I might try to assure him of my innocence.", "나는 내 결백을 그에게 알리려고, 그가 나를 봐 주기를 기다리고 있었던 것이다."], ["It was not at all expressed to me that he even comprehended my intention, for he gave me a look that I did not understand, and it all passed in a moment.", "그가 내 의도를 이해하기라도 했는지는 전혀 드러나지 않았으니, 그가 내게 보낸 눈길을 나는 알아들을 수 없었고, 그 모든 것이 한순간에 지나가 버렸다."], ["But if he had looked at me for an hour or for a day, I could not have remembered his face ever afterwards, as having been more attentive.", "그러나 설령 그가 한 시간이나 하루를 나를 바라보았더라도, 나는 그 후로 그 얼굴이 그보다 더 골똘했던 것으로 기억할 수는 없었을 것이다."]], [["The soldier with the basket soon got a light, and lighted three or four torches, and took one himself and distributed the others.", "바구니를 든 군인은 이내 불을 얻어 횃불 서너 개에 불을 붙였고, 자기가 하나를 들고 나머지를 나눠 주었다."], ["It had been almost dark before, but now it seemed quite dark, and soon afterwards very dark.", "아까도 거의 어두웠으나, 이제는 완전히 어두워 보였고, 곧이어 몹시 어두워졌다."], ["Before we departed from that spot, four soldiers standing in a ring, fired twice into the air.", "우리가 그 자리를 떠나기 전에, 군인 넷이 둥글게 서서 허공에 두 번 발포했다."], ["Presently we saw other torches kindled at some distance behind us, and others on the marshes on the opposite bank of the river. “All right,”", "이윽고 우리 뒤로 얼마쯤 떨어진 곳에서 다른 횃불들이 켜지는 것이, 그리고 강 건너편 기슭 습지에서 또 다른 횃불들이 켜지는 것이 보였다. \"좋아.\""], ["said the sergeant. “March.”", "상사가 말했다. \"행군.\""]], [["We had not gone far when three cannon were fired ahead of us with a sound that seemed to burst something inside my ear.", "얼마 가지 않아 우리 앞쪽에서 대포가 세 발 발사되었는데, 그 소리가 내 귓속의 무언가를 터뜨리는 듯했다."], ["“You are expected on board,”", "\"배에서 자네를 기다리고 있네.\""], ["said the sergeant to my convict; “they know you are coming.", "상사가 내 죄수에게 말했다. \"자네가 온다는 걸 알고 있지."], ["Don’t straggle, my man.", "처지지 말게, 이 사람아."], ["Close up here.”", "여기 바싹 붙어.\""]], [["The two were kept apart, and each walked surrounded by a separate guard.", "두 사람은 떨어뜨려져, 각자 별도의 경비병에게 둘러싸인 채 걸었다."], ["I had hold of Joe’s hand now, and Joe carried one of the torches. Mr.", "나는 이제 조의 손을 잡고 있었고, 조는 횃불 하나를 들고 있었다."], ["Wopsle had been for going back, but Joe was resolved to see it out, so we went on with the party.", "우프슬 씨는 돌아가려 했으나 조는 끝까지 지켜보기로 마음먹은 터라, 우리는 일행과 함께 계속 나아갔다."], ["There was a reasonably good path now, mostly on the edge of the river, with a divergence here and there where a dike came, with a miniature windmill on it and a muddy sluice-gate.", "이제는 제법 괜찮은 길이 나 있었는데, 대개는 강가를 따라 났고, 도랑이 나오는 곳마다 여기저기 갈래가 갈렸으며, 그 도랑에는 자그마한 풍차와 진흙투성이 수문이 달려 있었다."], ["When I looked round, I could see the other lights coming in after us.", "돌아보니 우리 뒤로 다른 불빛들이 따라 들어오는 것이 보였다."], ["The torches we carried dropped great blotches of fire upon the track, and I could see those, too, lying smoking and flaring.", "우리가 든 횃불들은 길 위로 커다란 불 얼룩을 떨어뜨렸고, 그것들이 연기를 내며 타닥타닥 타고 있는 것도 보였다."], ["I could see nothing else but black darkness.", "그 밖에는 시커먼 어둠뿐 아무것도 보이지 않았다."], ["Our lights warmed the air about us with their pitchy blaze, and the two prisoners seemed rather to like that, as they limped along in the midst of the muskets.", "우리의 불빛은 송진 같은 불길로 주위 공기를 데웠고, 두 죄수는 머스킷들 한가운데서 절뚝거리며 가면서도 그것을 오히려 좋아하는 듯했다."], ["We could not go fast, because of their lameness; and they were so spent, that two or three times we had to halt while they rested.", "그들이 절뚝거리는 탓에 우리는 빨리 갈 수 없었고, 그들이 너무 지친 나머지 두세 번은 그들이 쉬는 동안 멈춰 서야 했다."]], [["After an hour or so of this travelling, we came to a rough wooden hut and a landing-place.", "이렇게 한 시간쯤 걸은 끝에, 우리는 허름한 나무 오두막과 선착장에 이르렀다."], ["There was a guard in the hut, and they challenged, and the sergeant answered.", "오두막에는 경비병이 있어 수하를 물었고, 상사가 답했다."], ["Then, we went into the hut, where there was a smell of tobacco and whitewash, and a bright fire, and a lamp, and a stand of muskets, and a drum, and a low wooden bedstead, like an overgrown mangle without the machinery, capable of holding about a dozen soldiers all at once.", "그러고 나서 우리는 오두막 안으로 들어갔는데, 거기에는 담배와 회칠 냄새가 났고, 환한 불과 등잔, 머스킷 걸이, 북 하나, 그리고 기계 장치 없는 거대한 빨래 짜는 롤러 같은 낮은 나무 침상이 있었으니, 한 번에 군인 열두어 명은 너끈히 누일 만했다."], ["Three or four soldiers who lay upon it in their great-coats were not much interested in us, but just lifted their heads and took a sleepy stare, and then lay down again.", "외투를 걸친 채 그 위에 누워 있던 군인 서넛은 우리에게 별 관심이 없었고, 그저 고개를 들어 졸린 눈으로 멍하니 쳐다보고는 다시 누웠다."], ["The sergeant made some kind of report, and some entry in a book, and then the convict whom I call the other convict was drafted off with his guard, to go on board first.", "상사는 일종의 보고를 하고 장부에 무언가를 적었으며, 그러고 나서 내가 다른 죄수라 부르는 그자가 먼저 배에 오르도록 경비병과 함께 끌려 나갔다."]], [["My convict never looked at me, except that once.", "내 죄수는 그 한 번을 빼고는 결코 나를 보지 않았다."], ["While we stood in the hut, he stood before the fire looking thoughtfully at it, or putting up his feet by turns upon the hob, and looking thoughtfully at them as if he pitied them for their recent adventures.", "우리가 오두막 안에 서 있는 동안, 그는 불 앞에 서서 골똘히 그것을 바라보거나, 두 발을 번갈아 화덕 선반에 올려놓고는 마치 최근의 모험을 겪은 그 발들을 가엾이 여기듯 골똘히 내려다보았다."], ["Suddenly, he turned to the sergeant, and remarked,—", "그러다 갑자기 그가 상사에게 돌아서서 말했다."]], [["“I wish to say something respecting this escape.", "\"이번 탈주에 관해 한마디 하고 싶소."], ["It may prevent some persons laying under suspicion alonger me.”", "어떤 사람들이 나랑 같이 의심받는 걸 막을 수 있을지도 모르니.\""]], [["“You can say what you like,”", "\"하고 싶은 말은 해도 좋네.\""], ["returned the sergeant, standing coolly looking at him with his arms folded, “but you have no call to say it here.", "상사가 팔짱을 낀 채 차갑게 그를 바라보며 대답했다. \"하지만 여기서 할 까닭은 없어."], ["You’ll have opportunity enough to say about it, and hear about it, before it’s done with, you know.”", "일이 다 끝나기 전에 그것에 관해 말할 기회도, 들을 기회도 자네한테 충분히 있을 테니, 알겠나.\""]], [["“I know, but this is another pint, a separate matter.", "\"알겠소, 하지만 이건 또 다른 문제요, 별개의 일이오."], ["A man can’t starve; at least I can’t.", "사람이 굶을 수야 없잖소. 적어도 나는 못 굶소."], ["I took some wittles, up at the willage over yonder,—where the church stands a’most out on the marshes.”", "나는 저기 저 너머 마을에서 — 교회가 거의 습지로 튀어나와 서 있는 그 마을에서 — 먹을 것을 좀 가져왔소.\""]], [["“You mean stole,”", "\"훔쳤다는 말이군.\""], ["said the sergeant.", "상사가 말했다."]], [["“And I’ll tell you where from.", "\"어디서 가져왔는지도 말해 주리다."], ["From the blacksmith’s.”", "대장장이 집에서요.\""]], [["“Halloa!”", "\"이런!\""], ["said the sergeant, staring at Joe.", "상사가 조를 빤히 바라보며 말했다."]], [["“Halloa, Pip!”", "\"이런, 핍!\""], ["said Joe, staring at me.", "조가 나를 빤히 바라보며 말했다."]], [["“It was some broken wittles—that’s what it was—and a dram of liquor, and a pie.”", "\"먹다 남은 음식 부스러기였소 — 그게 다요 — 그리고 술 한 모금하고, 파이 하나요.\""]], [["“Have you happened to miss such an article as a pie, blacksmith?”", "\"혹시 파이 같은 물건이 없어진 일이 있었소, 대장장이 양반?\""], ["asked the sergeant, confidentially.", "상사가 은근하게 물었다."]], [["“My wife did, at the very moment when you came in.", "\"내 아내가 그랬소, 바로 당신이 들어오던 그 순간에."], ["Don’t you know, Pip?”", "너도 알지, 핍?\""]], [["“So,”", "\"그래서,\""], ["said my convict, turning his eyes on Joe in a moody manner, and without the least glance at me,—“so you’re the blacksmith, are you?", "내 죄수가 침울한 태도로, 나는 조금도 거들떠보지 않은 채 조에게 눈길을 돌리며 말했다. \"그래서 당신이 그 대장장이로군, 그렇소?"], ["Than I’m sorry to say, I’ve eat your pie.”", "그렇다면 안된 말이지만, 내가 당신 파이를 먹어 치웠소.\""]], [["“God knows you’re welcome to it,—so far as it was ever mine,”", "\"맘껏 드셨대도 좋소 — 그게 내 것이었던 한에서는 말이오.\""], ["returned Joe, with a saving remembrance of Mrs. Joe.", "조가 대답했는데, 그 속에는 조 부인을 떠올린 조심스러움이 깃들어 있었다."], ["“We don’t know what you have done, but we wouldn’t have you starved to death for it, poor miserable fellow-creatur.—Would us, Pip?”", "\"당신이 무슨 짓을 했는지야 우리가 모르지만, 그렇다고 당신이 그 때문에 굶어 죽기를 바라지는 않소, 가엾고 비참한 동료 인간 양반. — 안 그러냐, 핍?\""]], [["The something that I had noticed before, clicked in the man’s throat again, and he turned his back.", "앞서 내가 알아챘던 그 무언가가 사내의 목구멍에서 다시 딸깍 소리를 냈고, 그는 등을 돌렸다."], ["The boat had returned, and his guard were ready, so we followed him to the landing-place made of rough stakes and stones, and saw him put into the boat, which was rowed by a crew of convicts like himself.", "보트가 돌아와 있었고 그의 경비병도 준비가 되어 있어, 우리는 거친 말뚝과 돌로 만든 선착장까지 그를 따라가, 그가 보트에 태워지는 것을 지켜보았다. 보트는 그와 같은 죄수들로 이루어진 일행이 노를 젓고 있었다."], ["No one seemed surprised to see him, or interested in seeing him, or glad to see him, or sorry to see him, or spoke a word, except that somebody in the boat growled as if to dogs, “Give way, you!”", "그를 보고 놀라거나, 그를 보는 데 흥미를 느끼거나, 그를 보고 반가워하거나 안타까워하거나, 한마디라도 건네는 사람은 아무도 없는 듯했다. 다만 보트 안의 누군가가 개에게 하듯 으르렁댔다."], ["which was the signal for the dip of the oars.", "\"노 저어, 이놈들아!\" 그것이 노를 담그라는 신호였다."], ["By the light of the torches, we saw the black Hulk lying out a little way from the mud of the shore, like a wicked Noah’s ark.", "횃불 빛으로, 우리는 시커먼 감옥선이 못된 노아의 방주처럼 기슭의 진흙에서 조금 떨어져 떠 있는 것을 보았다."], ["Cribbed and barred and moored by massive rusty chains, the prison-ship seemed in my young eyes to be ironed like the prisoners.", "좁은 우리에 갇히고 빗장이 질리고 육중한 녹슨 쇠사슬에 매여 있는 그 감옥선은, 내 어린 눈에는 마치 죄수들처럼 쇠고랑이 채워진 듯 보였다."], ["We saw the boat go alongside, and we saw him taken up the side and disappear.", "우리는 보트가 그 옆에 가 닿는 것을 보았고, 그가 뱃전 위로 끌어 올려져 사라지는 것을 보았다."], ["Then, the ends of the torches were flung hissing into the water, and went out, as if it were all over with him.", "그러고 나서 횃불 끄트머리들이 쉭쉭 소리를 내며 물속으로 내던져져 꺼졌으니, 마치 그에 관한 모든 것이 다 끝나 버린 듯했다."]], [["# Chapter VI.", "# Chapter VI."]], [["My state of mind regarding the pilfering from which I had been so unexpectedly exonerated did not impel me to frank disclosure; but I hope it had some dregs of good at the bottom of it.", "그토록 뜻밖에 혐의를 벗게 된 그 좀도둑질에 대한 내 마음 상태는, 나로 하여금 솔직히 털어놓도록 떠밀지는 못했다. 그러나 그 밑바닥에는 얼마간 선한 찌꺼기가 남아 있었기를 바란다."]], [["I do not recall that I felt any tenderness of conscience in reference to Mrs. Joe, when the fear of being found out was lifted off me.", "발각될 두려움이 내게서 걷히고 나서, 누나에 대해 양심의 가책 같은 것을 느꼈던 기억은 없다. 그러나 나는 조를 사랑했다."], ["But I loved Joe,—perhaps for no better reason in those early days than because the dear fellow let me love him,—and, as to him, my inner self was not so easily composed.", "어쩌면 그 어린 시절에는, 그 다정한 사람이 내가 자기를 사랑하도록 내버려 두었다는 것 말고는 더 나은 이유가 없었는지도 모른다. 그리고 그를 두고 말하자면, 내 속마음은 그리 쉽사리 가라앉지 않았다."], ["It was much upon my mind (particularly when I first saw him looking about for his file) that I ought to tell Joe the whole truth.", "조에게 모든 진실을 털어놓아야 한다는 생각이 줄곧 마음을 짓눌렀다(특히 그가 자기 줄칼을 두리번거리며 찾는 모습을 처음 보았을 때 그러했다)."], ["Yet I did not, and for the reason that I mistrusted that if I did, he would think me worse than I was.", "그런데도 나는 그러지 않았으니, 만일 그렇게 한다면 그가 나를 실제보다 더 나쁜 아이로 여길까 봐 미덥지 못했던 까닭이었다."], ["The fear of losing Joe’s confidence, and of thenceforth sitting in the chimney corner at night staring drearily at my forever lost companion and friend, tied up my tongue.", "조의 신뢰를 잃을지도 모른다는 두려움, 그래서 그날 이후로 밤마다 굴뚝 옆 구석에 앉아 영영 잃어버린 내 동무이자 벗을 음울하게 바라보게 될지도 모른다는 두려움이 내 혀를 묶어 버렸다."], ["I morbidly represented to myself that if Joe knew it, I never afterwards could see him at the fireside feeling his fair whisker, without thinking that he was meditating on it.", "나는 병적으로 이렇게 상상했다. 만일 조가 그 일을 알게 된다면, 그 뒤로는 그가 난롯가에서 자기 금빛 구레나룻을 매만지는 모습을 볼 때마다, 그가 그 일을 곱씹고 있다는 생각을 하지 않고는 못 배기리라고."], ["That, if Joe knew it, I never afterwards could see him glance, however casually, at yesterday’s meat or pudding when it came on to-day’s table, without thinking that he was debating whether I had been in the pantry.", "만일 조가 그 일을 알게 된다면, 어제 먹던 고기나 푸딩이 오늘 식탁에 다시 올라올 때 그가 아무리 무심코 그것을 흘끗 본다 해도, 그가 내가 식료품 저장실에 들어갔던 건 아닌지 헤아리고 있다는 생각을 하지 않고는 그 모습을 볼 수 없으리라고."], ["That, if Joe knew it, and at any subsequent period of our joint domestic life remarked that his beer was flat or thick, the conviction that he suspected tar in it, would bring a rush of blood to my face.", "만일 조가 그 일을 알게 되어, 그 뒤 우리 둘의 살림살이 어느 때든 자기 맥주가 김이 빠졌다거나 텁텁하다고 말한다면, 그가 거기 타르가 들었으리라 의심한다는 확신에 내 얼굴로 피가 확 몰려올 것이라고."], ["In a word, I was too cowardly to do what I knew to be right, as I had been too cowardly to avoid doing what I knew to be wrong.", "한마디로, 나는 옳다고 아는 일을 하기에는 너무 겁이 많았으니, 그릇되다고 아는 일을 피하기에도 그만큼 겁이 많았던 것과 마찬가지였다."], ["I had had no intercourse with the world at that time, and I imitated none of its many inhabitants who act in this manner.", "그 무렵 나는 세상과 아무런 교류가 없었고, 이런 식으로 행동하는 세상의 그 숱한 사람들을 흉내 낸 것도 아니었다."], ["Quite an untaught genius, I made the discovery of the line of action for myself.", "전혀 배운 바 없는 천재처럼, 나는 그 행동 방식을 스스로 발견해 낸 것이었다."]], [["As I was sleepy before we were far away from the prison-ship, Joe took me on his back again and carried me home.", "감옥선에서 그리 멀어지기도 전에 나는 졸음이 쏟아졌으므로, 조는 다시 나를 등에 업고 집으로 데려갔다."], ["He must have had a tiresome journey of it, for Mr.", "그에게는 꽤 고된 여정이었을 것이다."], ["Wopsle, being knocked up, was in such a very bad temper that if the Church had been thrown open, he would probably have excommunicated the whole expedition, beginning with Joe and myself.", "워프슬 씨가 녹초가 되어 어찌나 심사가 사나웠던지, 교회 문이 활짝 열려 있었더라면 그는 아마 이 원정대 전부를, 조와 나부터 시작해서 죄다 파문해 버렸을 테니까."], ["In his lay capacity, he persisted in sitting down in the damp to such an insane extent, that when his coat was taken off to be dried at the kitchen fire, the circumstantial evidence on his trousers would have hanged him, if it had been a capital offence.", "평신도의 처지로서도 그는 그토록 미친 듯이 기어이 축축한 땅바닥에 주저앉아 댔으므로, 부엌 불에 말리려고 그의 외투를 벗겼을 때 그의 바지에 남은 정황 증거만으로도, 만일 그것이 사형에 해당하는 죄였다면 그를 교수형에 처하기에 충분했을 것이다."]], [["By that time, I was staggering on the kitchen floor like a little drunkard, through having been newly set upon my feet, and through having been fast asleep, and through waking in the heat and lights and noise of tongues. As I came to myself (with the aid of a heavy thump between the shoulders, and the restorative exclamation “Yah!", "그 무렵 나는 부엌 바닥에서 작은 술꾼처럼 비틀거리고 있었으니, 갓 다시 두 발로 세워진 데다, 깊이 잠들어 있다가, 열기와 불빛과 떠들썩한 말소리 속에서 깨어났던 까닭이었다. (어깨뼈 사이로 떨어진 묵직한 한 대와, \"야!"], ["Was there ever such a boy as this!”", "세상에 이런 애가 또 있을까!\""], ["from my sister,) I found Joe telling them about the convict’s confession, and all the visitors suggesting different ways by which he had got into the pantry. Mr. Pumblechook made out, after carefully surveying the premises, that he had first got upon the roof of the forge, and had then got upon the roof of the house, and had then let himself down the kitchen chimney by a rope made of his bedding cut into strips; and as Mr.", "하는 누나의 정신 번쩍 들게 하는 외침의 도움으로) 정신을 차리고 보니, 조는 사람들에게 죄수의 자백 이야기를 들려주고 있었고, 손님들은 저마다 그가 어떻게 식료품 저장실에 들어갔을지 다른 방법들을 내놓고 있었다. 펌블추크 씨는 현장을 꼼꼼히 살펴본 끝에, 그가 먼저 대장간 지붕에 올라갔다가, 다음에 집 지붕으로 올라갔다가, 그런 다음 자기 침구를 가닥가닥 잘라 만든 밧줄을 타고 부엌 굴뚝으로 내려왔다는 결론을 끌어냈다."], ["Pumblechook was very positive and drove his own chaise-cart—over everybody—it was agreed that it must be so. Mr. Wopsle, indeed, wildly cried out, “No!”", "그리고 펌블추크 씨는 매우 단정적인 사람인 데다 자기 이륜마차를 모는 사람이었으므로 — 모두를 깔아뭉개며 — 틀림없이 그러했으리라는 데 의견이 모였다. 사실 워프슬 씨는 지친 사람 특유의 힘없는 악의로 \"아니오!\""], ["with the feeble malice of a tired man; but, as he had no theory, and no coat on, he was unanimously set at naught,—not to mention his smoking hard behind, as he stood with his back to the kitchen fire to draw the damp out: which was not calculated to inspire confidence.", "하고 거칠게 외치기는 했으나, 그에게는 아무런 이론도 없고 외투도 걸치지 않은 터라, 만장일치로 무시당하고 말았다. 게다가 그가 축축한 기운을 빼내려고 부엌 불에 등을 대고 서서 뒤쪽으로 모락모락 김을 피워 올리고 있었다는 점은 말할 것도 없으니, 그 꼴은 신뢰를 자아낼 만한 것이 못 되었다."]], [["This was all I heard that night before my sister clutched me, as a slumberous offence to the company’s eyesight, and assisted me up to bed with such a strong hand that I seemed to have fifty boots on, and to be dangling them all against the edges of the stairs.", "그날 밤 내가 들은 것은 이것이 전부였으니, 그 뒤로는 누나가 좌중의 눈에 거슬리는 졸린 골칫거리라며 나를 움켜쥐고는, 어찌나 억센 손으로 거들어 잠자리로 올려 보냈던지, 마치 내가 장화를 쉰 켤레쯤 신고서 그것을 죄다 계단 모서리마다 부딪치며 대롱대롱 매달려 가는 것만 같았다."], ["My state of mind, as I have described it, began before I was up in the morning, and lasted long after the subject had died out, and had ceased to be mentioned saving on exceptional occasions.", "앞서 적은 내 마음 상태는 아침에 자리에서 일어나기도 전에 시작되어, 그 화제가 사그라들고 특별한 경우가 아니고서는 더 이상 입에 오르내리지 않게 된 뒤로도 오래도록 이어졌다."]], [["# Chapter VII.", "# Chapter VII."]], [["At the time when I stood in the churchyard reading the family tombstones, I had just enough learning to be able to spell them out.", "내가 교회 묘지에 서서 집안의 묘비들을 읽던 무렵, 나는 겨우 그 글자들을 떠듬떠듬 풀어낼 만큼의 학식밖에 없었다."], ["My construction even of their simple meaning was not very correct, for I read “wife of the Above” as a complimentary reference to my father’s exaltation to a better world; and if any one of my deceased relations had been referred to as “Below,”", "그 단순한 뜻조차 나는 제대로 새기지 못했으니, \"위 사람의 아내\"라는 구절을 두고 나는 아버지가 더 나은 세상으로 드높여졌음을 기리는 찬사로 읽었던 것이다. 그리고 만약 죽은 친척 가운데 누군가가 \"아래 사람\"이라 일컬어져 있었다면, 나는 분명 그 식구에 대해 더없이 나쁜 생각을 품었으리라."], ["I have no doubt I should have formed the worst opinions of that member of the family.", "교리문답이 나를 묶어 둔 신학적 입장에 대한 내 관념도 도무지 정확하지 않았다."], ["Neither were my notions of the theological positions to which my Catechism bound me, at all accurate; for, I have a lively remembrance that I supposed my declaration that I was to “walk in the same all the days of my life,” laid me under an obligation always to go through the village from our house in one particular direction, and never to vary it by turning down by the wheelwright’s or up by the mill.", "내가 \"평생토록 그 안에서 걷겠나이다\"라고 선언한 것이, 우리 집에서 마을을 지날 때 늘 어느 한 방향으로만 다녀야 하며 수레바퀴 장인의 집 쪽으로 꺾어 내려가거나 방앗간 쪽으로 올라가 그 길을 바꾸어서는 안 된다는 의무를 내게 지웠다고 여겼던 것을, 나는 생생히 기억하고 있으니 말이다."]], [["When I was old enough, I was to be apprenticed to Joe, and until I could assume that dignity I was not to be what Mrs. Joe called “Pompeyed,” or (as I render it) pampered.", "나이가 차면 나는 조의 도제가 될 예정이었고, 그 지위를 떠맡을 수 있을 때까지는 조 부인이 \"폼피\"라고 부르는, 다시 말해(내 식으로 옮기자면) 응석받이로 자라서는 안 되었다."], ["Therefore, I was not only odd-boy about the forge, but if any neighbour happened to want an extra boy to frighten birds, or pick up stones, or do any such job, I was favoured with the employment.", "그래서 나는 대장간의 잡일꾼 노릇을 했을 뿐 아니라, 이웃 가운데 누군가가 새를 쫓거나 돌을 줍거나 그 비슷한 일을 시킬 아이가 따로 필요하다 싶으면 그 일자리를 내가 받는 영광을 누렸다."], ["In order, however, that our superior position might not be compromised thereby, a money-box was kept on the kitchen mantel-shelf, into which it was publicly made known that all my earnings were dropped.", "그러나 그로 인해 우리의 우월한 위신이 손상되지 않도록, 부엌 벽난로 선반 위에는 돈통이 놓여 있었고, 내가 번 돈은 모조리 그 안에 들어간다는 사실이 공공연히 알려져 있었다."], ["I have an impression that they were to be contributed eventually towards the liquidation of the National Debt, but I know I had no hope of any personal participation in the treasure.", "그 돈이 결국 국가 부채를 갚는 데 보태질 거라는 인상을 나는 받았으나, 그 보물에 내가 몸소 한몫 끼리라는 희망은 조금도 없었음을 나는 안다."]], [["Mr. Wopsle’s great-aunt kept an evening school in the village; that is to say, she was a ridiculous old woman of limited means and unlimited infirmity, who used to go to sleep from six to seven every evening, in the society of youth who paid two pence per week each, for the improving opportunity of seeing her do it.", "웝슬 씨의 대고모는 마을에서 야간 학교를 운영했다. 다시 말해, 그분은 재산은 한정되어 있고 노쇠함은 한이 없는 우스꽝스러운 노파였는데, 저녁 여섯 시부터 일곱 시까지 잠이 들곤 했으며, 그 잠드는 광경을 지켜보는 유익한 기회를 위해 일주일에 이 펜스씩 내는 젊은이들이 그 곁을 지켰다."], ["She rented a small cottage, and Mr. Wopsle had the room upstairs, where we students used to overhear him reading aloud in a most dignified and terrific manner, and occasionally bumping on the ceiling.", "그분은 작은 오두막을 빌려 살았고, 웝슬 씨는 위층 방을 썼는데, 우리 학생들은 그가 더없이 위엄 있고 무시무시한 어조로 소리 내어 책 읽는 소리를, 그리고 이따금 천장을 쿵쿵 울리는 소리를 엿듣곤 했다."], ["There was a fiction that Mr. Wopsle “examined” the scholars once a quarter.", "웝슬 씨가 분기마다 한 번씩 학생들을 \"시험한다\"는 허구가 있었다."], ["What he did on those occasions was to turn up his cuffs, stick up his hair, and give us Mark Antony’s oration over the body of Caesar.", "그런 자리에서 그가 실제로 한 일이란 소맷부리를 걷어 올리고, 머리카락을 곧추세우고, 카이사르의 주검 앞에서 마르쿠스 안토니우스가 한 연설을 우리에게 들려주는 것이었다."], ["This was always followed by Collins’s Ode on the Passions, wherein I particularly venerated Mr. Wopsle as Revenge throwing his blood-stained sword in thunder down, and taking the War-denouncing trumpet with a withering look.", "이어서 늘 콜린스의 「정념에 부치는 송시」가 따라 나왔는데, 거기서 나는 특히 복수의 신으로 분한 웝슬 씨를, 피로 물든 칼을 천둥 같은 소리로 내던지고 시들게 하는 듯한 눈길로 전쟁을 알리는 나팔을 집어 드는 그 모습을 우러러보았다."], ["It was not with me then, as it was in later life, when I fell into the society of the Passions, and compared them with Collins and Wopsle, rather to the disadvantage of both gentlemen.", "그때의 나는 훗날의 나와는 달랐다. 훗날 나는 정념들의 무리 속에 떨어져 그것들을 콜린스와 웝슬과 견주어 보게 되었고, 그 비교는 두 신사 모두에게 불리하게 작용했다."]], [["Mr. Wopsle’s great-aunt, besides keeping this Educational Institution, kept in the same room—a little general shop.", "웝슬 씨의 대고모는 이 교육 기관을 운영하는 한편, 같은 방에서 작은 잡화점도 했다."], ["She had no idea what stock she had, or what the price of anything in it was; but there was a little greasy memorandum-book kept in a drawer, which served as a Catalogue of Prices, and by this oracle Biddy arranged all the shop transactions. Biddy was Mr.", "그분은 자기 가게에 어떤 물건이 있는지, 그 값이 얼마인지 전혀 알지 못했다. 그러나 서랍 속에는 기름때 묻은 작은 비망록이 들어 있어 가격표 구실을 했고, 비디는 이 신탁에 따라 가게의 모든 거래를 처리했다."], ["Wopsle’s great-aunt’s granddaughter; I confess myself quite unequal to the working out of the problem, what relation she was to Mr. Wopsle.", "비디는 웝슬 씨의 대고모의 손녀였다. 솔직히 고백하자면 그가 웝슬 씨와 어떤 관계인가 하는 문제를 푸는 일은 내 능력 밖이었다."], ["She was an orphan like myself; like me, too, had been brought up by hand.", "그 애는 나처럼 고아였고, 또한 나처럼 손으로 길러진 아이였다."], ["She was most noticeable, I thought, in respect of her extremities; for, her hair always wanted brushing, her hands always wanted washing, and her shoes always wanted mending and pulling up at heel.", "내 생각에 그 애의 가장 두드러진 점은 손발의 끄트머리였다. 머리는 늘 빗질이 필요했고, 손은 늘 씻을 필요가 있었으며, 신발은 늘 수선하고 뒤꿈치를 추켜올릴 필요가 있었으니 말이다."], ["This description must be received with a week-day limitation.", "다만 이 설명은 평일에 한정해 받아들여야 한다."], ["On Sundays, she went to church elaborated.", "일요일이면 그 애는 한껏 차려입고 교회에 갔다."]], [["Much of my unassisted self, and more by the help of Biddy than of Mr. Wopsle’s great-aunt, I struggled through the alphabet as if it had been a bramble-bush; getting considerably worried and scratched by every letter.", "남의 도움 없이 나 혼자서, 그리고 웝슬 씨의 대고모보다는 비디의 도움을 더 많이 받아, 나는 알파벳을 마치 가시덤불인 양 헤치고 나아갔다. 글자 하나하나에 어지간히 시달리고 긁히면서."], ["After that I fell among those thieves, the nine figures, who seemed every evening to do something new to disguise themselves and baffle recognition.", "그러고 나서 나는 아홉 개의 숫자라는 도둑들 사이에 떨어졌는데, 그것들은 저녁마다 변장을 새로 꾸며 알아보지 못하게 하려고 무언가 새로운 짓을 하는 것만 같았다."], ["But, at last I began, in a purblind groping way, to read, write, and cipher, on the very smallest scale.", "그러나 마침내 나는 반쯤 눈먼 채 더듬거리는 식으로나마, 지극히 작은 규모로 읽고 쓰고 셈하기 시작했다."]], [["One night I was sitting in the chimney corner with my slate, expending great efforts on the production of a letter to Joe.", "어느 날 밤 나는 굴뚝 옆 구석에 석판을 들고 앉아, 조에게 보낼 편지 한 통을 만들어 내는 데 엄청난 공을 들이고 있었다."], ["I think it must have been a full year after our hunt upon the marshes, for it was a long time after, and it was winter and a hard frost.", "그것은 우리가 습지에서 사냥을 벌인 지 꼬박 일 년은 지난 뒤의 일이었을 것이다. 그로부터 한참 지난 시기였고, 겨울이었으며, 모진 서리가 내려 있었으니까."], ["With an alphabet on the hearth at my feet for reference, I contrived in an hour or two to print and smear this epistle:—", "발치의 화롯가에 참고용 알파벳을 펴 놓고, 나는 한두 시간 만에 다음과 같은 서한을 찍어 내고 문질러 가며 완성했다."]], [["“MI DEER JO i OPE U R KRWITE WELL i OPE i SHAL SON B HABELL 4 2 TEEDGE U JO AN THEN WE SHORL B SO GLODD AN WEN i M PRENGTD 2 U JO WOT LARX AN BLEVE ME INF XN PIP.”", "\"내사랑하넌조에게 잘디내시기바라요 나는곳 조 당신을 가르칠수 인게되기바라요 그러면 우리는 무척 기쁠꺼에요 그리고내가조당신의도제가되면 얼마나 신날까요 나를미더주세요 친애하넌 핍.\""]], [["There was no indispensable necessity for my communicating with Joe by letter, inasmuch as he sat beside me and we were alone.", "조에게 편지로 소식을 전해야 할 절실한 필요는 전혀 없었다. 그가 내 곁에 앉아 있었고 우리 둘뿐이었으니 말이다."], ["But I delivered this written communication (slate and all) with my own hand, and Joe received it as a miracle of erudition.", "그러나 나는 이 글로 쓴 통신문을(석판째로) 내 손으로 직접 건넸고, 조는 그것을 박학의 기적인 양 받아 들었다."]], [["“I say, Pip, old chap!”", "\"이봐, 핍, 친구!\""], ["cried Joe, opening his blue eyes wide, “what a scholar you are!", "조가 푸른 눈을 휘둥그레 뜨며 외쳤다. \"넌 정말 대단한 학자로구나!"], ["An’t you?”", "안 그러냐?\""]], [["“I should like to be,”", "\"그렇게 되고 싶어요.\""], ["said I, glancing at the slate as he held it; with a misgiving that the writing was rather hilly.", "나는 조가 들고 있는 석판을 흘끗 보며 말했다. 글씨가 영 울퉁불퉁하다는 불안한 마음을 품은 채."]], [["“Why, here’s a J,”", "\"이런, 여기 J가 있구나.\""], ["said Joe, “and a O equal to anythink!", "조가 말했다. \"그리고 무엇과도 견줄 만한 O가 있어!"], ["Here’s a J and a O, Pip, and a J-O, Joe.”", "여기 J가 있고 O가 있다, 핍, 그래서 J-O, 조구나.\""]], [["I had never heard Joe read aloud to any greater extent than this monosyllable, and I had observed at church last Sunday, when I accidentally held our Prayer-Book upside down, that it seemed to suit his convenience quite as well as if it had been all right.", "나는 조가 이 한 음절보다 더 길게 소리 내어 읽는 것을 들어 본 적이 없었고, 지난 일요일 교회에서 내가 우연히 기도서를 거꾸로 들고 있었을 때, 그것이 똑바로 놓여 있을 때나 다름없이 그에게 편해 보인다는 사실을 눈치챘다."], ["Wishing to embrace the present occasion of finding out whether in teaching Joe, I should have to begin quite at the beginning, I said, “Ah! But read the rest, Jo.”", "조를 가르칠 때 정말 맨 처음부터 시작해야 하는지를 알아낼 이번 기회를 잡고 싶어, 나는 말했다. \"아! 그럼 나머지도 읽어 보세요, 조.\""]], [["“The rest, eh, Pip?”", "\"나머지라, 응, 핍?\""], ["said Joe, looking at it with a slow, searching eye, “One, two, three.", "조가 느릿느릿 살피는 눈으로 그것을 들여다보며 말했다. \"하나, 둘, 셋."], ["Why, here’s three Js, and three Os, and three J-O, Joes in it, Pip!”", "이런, 여기 J가 셋, O가 셋, 그리고 J-O 조가 셋이나 들어 있구나, 핍!\""]], [["I leaned over Joe, and, with the aid of my forefinger read him the whole letter.", "나는 조에게 몸을 기울이고, 집게손가락의 도움을 받아 편지 전체를 그에게 읽어 주었다."]], [["“Astonishing!”", "\"놀랍구나!\""], ["said Joe, when I had finished.", "내가 다 읽자 조가 말했다."], ["“You ARE a scholar.”", "\"넌 정말 학자야.\""]], [["“How do you spell Gargery, Joe?”", "\"가저리는 어떻게 쓰는 거예요, 조?\""], ["I asked him, with a modest patronage.", "나는 짐짓 너그러운 척하며 물었다."]], [["“I don’t spell it at all,”", "\"난 그걸 아예 못 쓴다.\""], ["said Joe.", "조가 말했다."]], [["“But supposing you did?”", "\"하지만 쓴다고 치면요?\""]], [["“It can’t be supposed,”", "\"그건 칠 수가 없는 일이야.\""], ["said Joe.", "조가 말했다."], ["“Tho’ I’m uncommon fond of reading, too.”", "\"하기야 나도 책 읽기를 유별나게 좋아하긴 하지만 말이다.\""]], [["“Are you, Joe?”", "\"그래요, 조?\""]], [["“On-common. Give me,”", "\"유별나게 좋아한다니까. 나한테 좋은 책이나 좋은 신문 한 부를 주고,\""], ["said Joe, “a good book, or a good newspaper, and sit me down afore a good fire, and I ask no better.", "조가 말했다. \"좋은 불 앞에 앉혀 놓으면, 더 바랄 게 없어. 이런!\" 그가 무릎을 좀 문지르고 나서 말을 이었다."], ["Lord!” he continued, after rubbing his knees a little, “when you do come to a J and a O, and says you, ‘Here, at last, is a J-O, Joe,’ how interesting reading is!”", "\"정말로 J와 O를 만나서, '자, 드디어 J-O 조가 나왔구나' 하고 말하게 되면, 읽기란 얼마나 흥미로운 일이냐!\""]], [["I derived from this, that Joe’s education, like Steam, was yet in its infancy.", "이로부터 나는 조의 학식이 증기 기관처럼 아직 걸음마 단계에 있다는 것을 알아챘다."], ["Pursuing the subject, I inquired,—", "그 주제를 더 파고들어, 나는 물었다."]], [["“Didn’t you ever go to school, Joe, when you were as little as me?”", "\"조는 나만큼 어렸을 때 학교에 다닌 적이 한 번도 없어요, 조?\""]], [["“No, Pip.”", "\"없다, 핍.\""]], [["“Why didn’t you ever go to school, Joe, when you were as little as me?”", "\"왜 나만큼 어렸을 때 학교에 한 번도 안 다녔어요, 조?\""]], [["“Well, Pip,” said Joe, taking up the poker, and settling himself to his usual occupation when he was thoughtful, of slowly raking the fire between the lower bars; “I’ll tell you.", "\"그게 말이다, 핍.\" 조가 부지깽이를 집어 들며, 생각에 잠길 때면 으레 하던 버릇대로 아래쪽 살 사이의 불을 천천히 긁어 헤치는 일에 자세를 잡고 말했다."], ["My father, Pip, he were given to drink, and when he were overtook with drink, he hammered away at my mother, most onmerciful.", "\"이야기해 주마. 우리 아버지 말이다, 핍, 그분은 술에 빠져 사셨는데, 술기운에 휩싸이면 우리 어머니를 사정없이 두들겨 패셨지."], ["It were a’most the only hammering he did, indeed, ’xcepting at myself.", "사실 그분이 두들긴 거라곤 그게 거의 다였어, 나만 빼면 말이다."], ["And he hammered at me with a wigor only to be equalled by the wigor with which he didn’t hammer at his anwil.—You’re a listening and understanding, Pip?”", "그리고 나를 두들길 때면 그 기세가 어찌나 사납던지, 모루를 두들기지 않을 때의 그 기세에나 견줄 만했지. 듣고 또 알아듣고 있냐, 핍?\""]], [["“Yes, Joe.”", "\"네, 조.\""]], [["“Consequence, my mother and me we ran away from my father several times; and then my mother she’d go out to work, and she’d say, “Joe,”", "\"그러다 보니 우리 어머니와 나는 아버지한테서 여러 번 도망쳤단다. 그러면 어머니는 일을 나가시면서 이렇게 말씀하셨지. '조,'"], ["she’d say, “now, please God, you shall have some schooling, child,”", "하시고는, '얘야, 이제 하느님이 도우시면 너도 학교 교육을 받게 될 거다,'"], ["and she’d put me to school.", "하시면서 나를 학교에 넣어 주셨어."], ["But my father were that good in his hart that he couldn’t abear to be without us.", "그런데 우리 아버지는 마음씨가 어찌나 착하셨던지 우리 없이는 견디지를 못하셨단다."], ["So, he’d come with a most tremenjous crowd and make such a row at the doors of the houses where we was, that they used to be obligated to have no more to do with us and to give us up to him.", "그래서 엄청난 무리를 끌고 와서는, 우리가 사는 집 문 앞에서 어찌나 소동을 피우던지, 그 집 사람들은 우리와 더는 엮이지 않으려고 우리를 그분한테 넘겨주는 수밖에 없었지."], ["And then he took us home and hammered us.", "그러면 그분은 우리를 집으로 데려가 두들겨 팼어."], ["Which, you see, Pip,”", "그게 말이다, 핍,\""], ["said Joe, pausing in his meditative raking of the fire, and looking at me, “were a drawback on my learning.”", "조가 생각에 잠겨 불을 긁던 손을 멈추고 나를 바라보며 말했다. \"내 배움에는 걸림돌이 됐지.\""]], [["“Certainly, poor Joe!”", "\"정말 그렇겠어요, 가엾은 조!\""]], [["“Though mind you, Pip,”", "\"그래도 말이다, 핍.\""], ["said Joe, with a judicial touch or two of the poker on the top bar, “rendering unto all their doo, and maintaining equal justice betwixt man and man, my father were that good in his hart, don’t you see?”", "조가 부지깽이로 위쪽 살을 한두 번 판단하듯 두드리며 말했다. \"모든 사람에게 마땅한 몫을 돌리고, 사람과 사람 사이에 공평한 정의를 지킨다는 점에서 보면, 우리 아버지는 마음씨가 그렇게 착하셨단다, 안 그러냐?\""]], [["I didn’t see; but I didn’t say so.", "나는 그렇게 보이지 않았다. 그러나 그렇게 말하지는 않았다."]], [["“Well!”", "\"하지만!\""], ["Joe pursued, “somebody must keep the pot a-biling, Pip, or the pot won’t bile, don’t you know?”", "조가 말을 이었다. \"누군가는 솥을 계속 끓여야 하는 법이지, 핍, 안 그러면 솥이 끓지를 않거든, 모르겠냐?\""]], [["I saw that, and said so.", "나는 그건 알겠어서, 그렇다고 말했다."]], [["“Consequence, my father didn’t make objections to my going to work; so I went to work at my present calling, which were his too, if he would have followed it, and I worked tolerable hard, I assure you, Pip.", "\"그러다 보니 우리 아버지는 내가 일하러 가는 데는 반대하지 않으셨어. 그래서 나는 지금의 이 일을 하러 나갔지."], ["In time I were able to keep him, and I kep him till he went off in a purple leptic fit.", "아버지가 따랐다면 그분의 일이기도 했을 테고. 그리고 나는 제법 열심히 일했단다, 정말이야, 핍."], ["And it were my intentions to have had put upon his tombstone that, Whatsume’er the failings on his part, Remember reader he were that good in his heart.”", "시간이 지나 나는 아버지를 부양할 수 있게 됐고, 그분이 시뻘건 얼굴로 발작을 일으켜 돌아가실 때까지 모셨지. 그리고 나는 그분 묘비에 이렇게 새겨 드릴 작정이었어. 그분 쪽의 허물이 무엇이었든, 독자여 기억하라 그분은 마음씨가 그렇게 착하셨음을.\""]], [["Joe recited this couplet with such manifest pride and careful perspicuity, that I asked him if he had made it himself.", "조가 이 대구를 어찌나 뚜렷한 자부심과 조심스러운 또렷함으로 읊었던지, 나는 그것을 그가 직접 지었느냐고 물었다."]], [["“I made it,”", "\"내가 지었지.\""], ["said Joe, “my own self.", "조가 말했다. \"내 손으로 직접."], ["I made it in a moment.", "한순간에 지어냈어."], ["It was like striking out a horseshoe complete, in a single blow.", "마치 단번에 말편자 하나를 완전하게 두들겨 내는 것 같았지."], ["I never was so much surprised in all my life,—couldn’t credit my own ed,—to tell you the truth, hardly believed it were my own ed.", "평생 그렇게 놀란 적이 없었단다. 내 머리를 믿을 수가 없었어. 사실대로 말하면, 그게 정말 내 머리인지조차 거의 믿기지가 않았지."], ["As I was saying, Pip, it were my intentions to have had it cut over him; but poetry costs money, cut it how you will, small or large, and it were not done.", "아까 말했듯이, 핍, 나는 그걸 그분 위에 새겨 드릴 작정이었어. 하지만 시는 돈이 들거든, 크게 새기든 작게 새기든 말이다, 그래서 못 했어."], ["Not to mention bearers, all the money that could be spared were wanted for my mother.", "운구꾼들 비용은 말할 것도 없고, 아낄 수 있는 돈은 죄다 우리 어머니한테 필요했으니까."], ["She were in poor elth, and quite broke.", "어머니는 건강이 좋지 못하셨고, 완전히 쇠약해지셨어."], ["She weren’t long of following, poor soul, and her share of peace come round at last.”", "가엾은 양반, 어머니도 오래 가지 않아 뒤따라 가셨고, 마침내 그분 몫의 평안이 찾아왔단다.\""]], [["Joe’s blue eyes turned a little watery; he rubbed first one of them, and then the other, in a most uncongenial and uncomfortable manner, with the round knob on the top of the poker.", "조의 푸른 눈이 약간 촉촉해졌다. 그는 부지깽이 끝의 둥근 손잡이로, 한쪽 눈을 먼저, 그러고 나서 다른 쪽 눈을 더없이 어울리지 않고 불편한 모양새로 문질렀다."]], [["“It were but lonesome then,”", "\"그러고 나니 그때는 좀 적적했지.\""], ["said Joe, “living here alone, and I got acquainted with your sister.", "조가 말했다. \"여기서 혼자 사는 게 말이다. 그러다 네 누나와 알게 됐단다."], ["Now, Pip,”—Joe looked firmly at me as if he knew I was not going to agree with him;—“your sister is a fine figure of a woman.”", "자, 핍,\" 조는 내가 자기 말에 동의하지 않으리라는 걸 안다는 듯 나를 똑바로 바라보았다. \"네 누나는 풍채가 훌륭한 여자야.\""]], [["I could not help looking at the fire, in an obvious state of doubt.", "나는 분명히 의심스러운 표정으로 불을 바라보지 않을 수 없었다."]], [["“Whatever family opinions, or whatever the world’s opinions, on that subject may be, Pip, your sister is,”", "\"그 일에 대해 집안의 의견이 어떻든, 세상의 의견이 어떻든 간에, 핍, 네 누나는 말이다,\""], ["Joe tapped the top bar with the poker after every word following, “a-fine-figure—of—a—woman!”", "조는 다음 한 마디 한 마디마다 부지깽이로 위쪽 살을 두드렸다. \"풍채가-훌륭한-여자야!\""]], [["I could think of nothing better to say than “I am glad you think so, Joe.”", "나는 \"그렇게 생각하신다니 다행이에요, 조\"라는 말보다 더 나은 말을 떠올릴 수가 없었다."]], [["“So am I,” returned Joe, catching me up.", "\"나도 그렇다.\" 조가 내 말을 받아 응수했다."], ["“I am glad I think so, Pip.", "\"나도 그렇게 생각해서 다행이야, 핍."], ["A little redness or a little matter of Bone, here or there, what does it signify to Me?”", "여기저기 좀 불그스레하거나 좀 뼈가 두드러지는 게 있다 한들, 그게 나한테 무슨 대수겠냐?\""]], [["I sagaciously observed, if it didn’t signify to him, to whom did it signify?", "나는 약삭빠르게, 그게 그에게 대수가 아니라면 대체 누구에게 대수겠느냐고 짚어 말했다."]], [["“Certainly!”", "\"그렇고말고!\""], ["assented Joe.", "조가 맞장구쳤다."], ["“That’s it.", "\"바로 그거야."], ["You’re right, old chap!", "네 말이 옳다, 친구!"], ["When I got acquainted with your sister, it were the talk how she was bringing you up by hand.", "내가 네 누나와 알게 됐을 때, 누나가 너를 손으로 길러 키운다는 게 사람들 입에 오르내리고 있었어."], ["Very kind of her too, all the folks said, and I said, along with all the folks. As to you,”", "사람들은 누나가 참 친절하다고 입을 모았고, 나도 사람들과 함께 그렇게 말했지. 너로 말하자면,\""], ["Joe pursued with a countenance expressive of seeing something very nasty indeed, “if you could have been aware how small and flabby and mean you was, dear me, you’d have formed the most contemptible opinion of yourself!”", "조가 정말이지 아주 끔찍한 무언가를 보는 듯한 표정으로 말을 이었다. \"네가 얼마나 작고 물렁물렁하고 보잘것없었는지 알았더라면, 이런, 너는 스스로에 대해 더없이 한심한 생각을 품었을 거다!\""]], [["Not exactly relishing this, I said, “Never mind me, Joe.”", "이 말이 썩 달갑지 않아, 나는 말했다. \"저 신경 쓰지 마세요, 조.\""]], [["“But I did mind you, Pip,” he returned with tender simplicity.", "\"하지만 난 너를 신경 썼단다, 핍.\" 그가 다정하고 꾸밈없이 응수했다."], ["“When I offered to your sister to keep company, and to be asked in church at such times as she was willing and ready to come to the forge, I said to her, ‘And bring the poor little child.", "\"내가 네 누나한테 함께 지내자고, 그리고 누나가 대장간으로 올 마음이 생겨 준비되었을 때 교회에서 혼인 예고를 하자고 청하면서, 나는 누나한테 이렇게 말했어. '그 가엾은 어린 녀석도 데려와요."], ["God bless the poor little child,’ I said to your sister, ‘there’s room for him at the forge!’”", "그 가엾은 어린 녀석한테 하느님의 축복이 있기를,' 나는 네 누나한테 그렇게 말했지. '대장간에는 그 녀석이 들어올 자리가 있어요!'\""]], [["I broke out crying and begging pardon, and hugged Joe round the neck: who dropped the poker to hug me, and to say, “Ever the best of friends; an’t us, Pip?", "나는 울음을 터뜨리며 용서를 빌었고, 조의 목을 끌어안았다. 그는 나를 끌어안으려고 부지깽이를 떨어뜨리며 말했다. \"우리는 언제까지나 둘도 없는 친구지, 안 그러냐, 핍?"], ["Don’t cry, old chap!”", "울지 마라, 친구!\""]], [["When this little interruption was over, Joe resumed:—", "이 짧은 막간이 끝나자, 조가 말을 이었다."]], [["“Well, you see, Pip, and here we are!", "\"그러니까 말이다, 핍, 이렇게 우리가 함께 있는 거다!"], ["That’s about where it lights; here we are!", "결국 그렇게 된 셈이지. 이렇게 함께 있는 거야!"], ["Now, when you take me in hand in my learning, Pip (and I tell you beforehand I am awful dull, most awful dull), Mrs. Joe mustn’t see too much of what we’re up to.", "자, 네가 내 배움을 맡아 가르칠 때 말이다, 핍(미리 말해 두지만 나는 지독하게 둔하단다, 정말이지 끔찍하게 둔해), 조 부인이 우리가 하는 짓을 너무 많이 봐서는 안 돼."], ["It must be done, as I may say, on the sly.", "그건, 말하자면, 몰래 해야 하는 거야."], ["And why on the sly?", "그런데 왜 몰래 하느냐고?"], ["I’ll tell you why, Pip.”", "그 까닭을 말해 주마, 핍.\""]], [["He had taken up the poker again; without which, I doubt if he could have proceeded in his demonstration.", "그는 다시 부지깽이를 집어 들었다. 그것이 없었다면 그가 자기 설명을 이어 갈 수 있었을지 의심스럽다."]], [["“Your sister is given to government.”", "\"네 누나는 통치에 빠져 있단다.\""]], [["“Given to government, Joe?”", "\"통치에 빠져 있다고요, 조?\""], ["I was startled, for I had some shadowy idea (and I am afraid I must add, hope) that Joe had divorced her in a favour of the Lords of the Admiralty, or Treasury.", "나는 흠칫 놀랐다. 조가 해군성이나 재무성의 높은 분들에게 누나를 넘겨 이혼해 버렸다는 어렴풋한 생각을(그리고 유감스럽게도 덧붙이자면, 그런 희망을) 품었던 것이다."]], [["“Given to government,”", "\"통치에 빠져 있어.\""], ["said Joe.", "조가 말했다."], ["“Which I meantersay the government of you and myself.”", "\"내 말은 너와 나를 통치하는 일에 빠져 있다는 거다.\""]], [["“Oh!”", "\"아!\""]], [["“And she an’t over partial to having scholars on the premises,”", "\"그리고 누나는 집 안에 학자가 있는 걸 그리 달가워하지 않아.\""], ["Joe continued, “and in partickler would not be over partial to my being a scholar, for fear as I might rise.", "조가 말을 이었다. \"그중에서도 특히 내가 학자가 되는 걸 그리 달가워하지 않을 거야, 내가 들고일어날까 봐 겁이 나서 말이지."], ["Like a sort of rebel, don’t you see?”", "일종의 반역자처럼 말이다, 알겠냐?\""]], [["I was going to retort with an inquiry, and had got as far as “Why—” when Joe stopped me.", "나는 질문을 던져 되받아치려고 \"왜─\"까지 말을 꺼냈는데, 조가 나를 막았다."]], [["“Stay a bit.", "\"잠깐만."], ["I know what you’re a-going to say, Pip; stay a bit!", "네가 무슨 말을 하려는지 안다, 핍. 잠깐만!"], ["I don’t deny that your sister comes the Mo-gul over us, now and again.", "네 누나가 이따금 우리한테 황제 노릇을 한다는 걸 부정하진 않아."], ["I don’t deny that she do throw us back-falls, and that she do drop down upon us heavy.", "누나가 우리를 메다꽂는다는 것도, 우리한테 호되게 내리덮친다는 것도 부정하지 않는다."], ["At such times as when your sister is on the Ram-page, Pip,”", "네 누나가 길길이 날뛸 때면 말이다, 핍,\""], ["Joe sank his voice to a whisper and glanced at the door, “candour compels fur to admit that she is a Buster.”", "조가 목소리를 속삭임으로 낮추며 문 쪽을 흘끗 보았다. \"솔직히 인정하지 않을 수 없는데, 누나는 정말 무서운 사람이야.\""]], [["Joe pronounced this word, as if it began with at least twelve capital Bs.", "조는 이 말을 마치 적어도 열두 개의 대문자로 시작하기라도 하는 듯이 발음했다."]], [["“Why don’t I rise? That were your observation when I broke it off, Pip?”", "\"내가 왜 들고일어나지 않느냐고? 그게 내가 말을 끊었을 때 네가 짚으려던 거지, 핍?\""]], [["“Yes, Joe.”", "\"네, 조.\""]], [["“Well,”", "\"그게 말이다,\""], ["said Joe, passing the poker into his left hand, that he might feel his whisker; and I had no hope of him whenever he took to that placid occupation; “your sister’s a master-mind.", "조가 부지깽이를 왼손으로 옮겨 쥐며 말했다. 한쪽 구레나룻을 매만지려는 것이었는데, 그가 그 평온한 일에 손을 댈 때면 나는 그에게서 아무런 희망도 가질 수 없었다. \"네 누나는 비상한 머리를 가졌어."], ["A master-mind.”", "비상한 머리지.\""]], [["“What’s that?”", "\"그게 뭐예요?\""], ["I asked, in some hope of bringing him to a stand.", "나는 그를 막다른 곳으로 몰아넣을까 하는 얼마간의 희망을 품고 물었다."], ["But Joe was readier with his definition than I had expected, and completely stopped me by arguing circularly, and answering with a fixed look, “Her.”", "그러나 조는 내 예상보다 그 정의를 더 빨리 내놓았고, 빙빙 도는 논법으로 나를 완전히 가로막은 채, 한곳을 응시하는 눈빛으로 대답했다. \"네 누나지.\""]], [["“And I ain’t a master-mind,”", "\"그리고 나는 비상한 머리가 아니야.\" 응시하던 눈길을 풀고 다시 구레나룻으로 돌아온 조가 말을 이었다."], ["Joe resumed, when he had unfixed his look, and got back to his whisker.", "\"그리고 마지막으로 말이다, 핍, 이건 너한테 아주 진지하게 하고 싶은 말인데, 친구야, 나는 우리 가엾은 어머니한테서 너무 많은 걸 보았단다. 한 여자가 죽도록 일하고 종처럼 고생하며 정직한 마음을 짓이기면서도 이승에 사는 동안 한 번도 평안을 누리지 못하는 걸 말이다. 그래서 나는 여자한테 마땅한 도리를 하지 않는 쪽으로 잘못되는 게 죽도록 무섭단다."], ["“And last of all, Pip,—and this I want to say very serious to you, old chap,—I see so much in my poor mother, of a woman drudging and slaving and breaking her honest hart and never getting no peace in her mortal days, that I’m dead afeerd of going wrong in the way of not doing what’s right by a woman, and I’d fur rather of the two go wrong the t’other way, and be a little ill-conwenienced myself.", "둘 중에 하나를 고르라면, 나는 차라리 반대쪽으로 잘못되어 나 자신이 좀 불편을 겪는 쪽이 훨씬 나아. 곤란을 겪는 게 나 하나뿐이었으면 좋겠다, 핍. 너한테 회초리가 없었으면 좋겠어, 친구."], ["I wish it was only me that got put out, Pip; I wish there warn’t no Tickler for you, old chap; I wish I could take it all on myself; but this is the up-and-down-and-straight on it, Pip, and I hope you’ll overlook shortcomings.”", "내가 그걸 다 떠맡을 수 있으면 좋겠구나. 하지만 이게 그 일의 위아래와 곧은 사정 전부란다, 핍. 그러니 부족한 점은 네가 너그러이 봐주기를 바란다.\""]], [["Young as I was, I believe that I dated a new admiration of Joe from that night.", "어렸지만, 나는 그날 밤부터 조에 대한 새로운 흠모가 시작되었다고 믿는다."], ["We were equals afterwards, as we had been before; but, afterwards at quiet times when I sat looking at Joe and thinking about him, I had a new sensation of feeling conscious that I was looking up to Joe in my heart.", "그 뒤로도 우리는 예전처럼 동등한 사이였다. 그러나 그 뒤 조용한 때에 조를 바라보며 그에 대해 생각하노라면, 나는 마음속으로 조를 우러러보고 있음을 의식하는 새로운 감각을 느꼈다."]], [["“However,”", "\"그건 그렇고,\""], ["said Joe, rising to replenish the fire; “here’s the Dutch-clock a-working himself up to being equal to strike Eight of ’em, and she’s not come home yet!", "조가 불을 더 지피려고 일어서며 말했다. \"여기 네덜란드 시계가 여덟 시를 칠 만큼 힘을 끌어모으고 있는데, 누나는 아직 집에 안 왔구나!"], ["I hope Uncle Pumblechook’s mare mayn’t have set a forefoot on a piece o’ ice, and gone down.”", "펌블추크 아저씨의 암말이 얼음 조각에 앞발을 디뎠다가 넘어진 게 아니어야 할 텐데.\""]], [["Mrs. Joe made occasional trips with Uncle Pumblechook on market-days, to assist him in buying such household stuffs and goods as required a woman’s judgment; Uncle Pumblechook being a bachelor and reposing no confidences in his domestic servant.", "조 부인은 장날이면 이따금 펌블추크 아저씨와 함께 길을 나서, 여자의 안목이 필요한 살림 도구며 물건들을 사는 일을 거들곤 했다. 펌블추크 아저씨는 홀아비였고 자기 집 하녀에게는 그런 일을 도무지 맡기지 않았던 것이다."], ["This was market-day, and Mrs. Joe was out on one of these expeditions.", "이날이 장날이었고, 조 부인은 그런 나들이 가운데 하나를 나가 있었다."]], [["Joe made the fire and swept the hearth, and then we went to the door to listen for the chaise-cart.", "조는 불을 지피고 화롯가를 쓸었고, 그러고 나서 우리는 이륜마차 소리를 들으려고 문가로 갔다."], ["It was a dry cold night, and the wind blew keenly, and the frost was white and hard.", "건조하고 추운 밤이었으며, 바람이 매섭게 불었고, 서리는 희고 단단했다."], ["A man would die to-night of lying out on the marshes, I thought.", "오늘 밤 습지에 누워 있다가는 사람이 얼어 죽겠구나, 하고 나는 생각했다."], ["And then I looked at the stars, and considered how awful it would be for a man to turn his face up to them as he froze to death, and see no help or pity in all the glittering multitude.", "그러고 나서 별들을 올려다보며, 사람이 얼어 죽어 가면서 그 별들을 향해 얼굴을 쳐들었는데, 그 반짝이는 무리 가운데 어디서도 도움이나 연민을 찾지 못한다면 얼마나 끔찍할까 하고 헤아려 보았다."]], [["“Here comes the mare,”", "\"암말이 오고 있구나.\""], ["said Joe, “ringing like a peal of bells!”", "조가 말했다. \"한바탕 종소리처럼 울리면서 말이다!\""]], [["The sound of her iron shoes upon the hard road was quite musical, as she came along at a much brisker trot than usual.", "단단한 길 위를 디디는 암말의 쇠편자 소리는, 여느 때보다 훨씬 활기찬 걸음으로 다가오면서 제법 음악적이었다."], ["We got a chair out, ready for Mrs. Joe’s alighting, and stirred up the fire that they might see a bright window, and took a final survey of the kitchen that nothing might be out of its place.", "우리는 조 부인이 내려설 수 있도록 의자를 하나 내놓고, 그들이 환한 창을 볼 수 있도록 불을 돋우었으며, 무엇 하나 제자리에서 벗어난 것이 없도록 부엌을 마지막으로 한 번 둘러보았다."], ["When we had completed these preparations, they drove up, wrapped to the eyes. Mrs.", "이런 채비를 마쳤을 때, 그들이 눈만 빼고 온몸을 칭칭 감싼 채 마차를 몰고 다가왔다."], ["Joe was soon landed, and Uncle Pumblechook was soon down too, covering the mare with a cloth, and we were soon all in the kitchen, carrying so much cold air in with us that it seemed to drive all the heat out of the fire.", "조 부인이 이내 내려섰고, 펌블추크 아저씨도 이내 내려와 암말에 천을 덮어 주었으며, 우리는 곧 모두 부엌으로 들어섰다. 어찌나 많은 찬 공기를 안고 들어왔던지, 그것이 불의 열기를 죄다 몰아내는 것만 같았다."]], [["“Now,”", "\"자,\""], ["said Mrs. Joe, unwrapping herself with haste and excitement, and throwing her bonnet back on her shoulders where it hung by the strings, “if this boy ain’t grateful this night, he never will be!”", "조 부인이 서둘러 흥분한 손길로 몸을 감싼 것을 풀고, 보닛을 어깨 뒤로 젖혀 끈에 매달린 채 늘어뜨리며 말했다. \"이 녀석이 오늘 밤 고마운 줄을 모른다면, 평생 고마운 줄 모르고 살 거예요!\""]], [["I looked as grateful as any boy possibly could, who was wholly uninformed why he ought to assume that expression.", "나는, 왜 그런 표정을 지어야 하는지 도무지 모르는 처지의 아이가 지을 수 있는 한 더없이 고마워하는 표정을 지었다."]], [["“It’s only to be hoped,”", "\"바라는 거라곤,\" 누나가 말했다."], ["said my sister, “that he won’t be Pompeyed.", "\"이 녀석이 응석받이가 되지 않는 거예요."], ["But I have my fears.”", "하지만 영 걱정이 돼서요.\""]], [["“She ain’t in that line, Mum,” said Mr.", "\"그분은 그럴 분이 아닙니다, 부인.\" 펌블추크 씨가 말했다."], ["Pumblechook. “She knows better.”", "\"그분은 그보다는 분별이 있으시지요.\""]], [["She?", "그분?"], ["I looked at Joe, making the motion with my lips and eyebrows, “She?”", "나는 조를 바라보며 입술과 눈썹으로 \"그분?\"이라는 동작을 지어 보였다."], ["Joe looked at me, making the motion with his lips and eyebrows, “She?”", "조도 나를 바라보며 자기 입술과 눈썹으로 \"그분?\"이라는 동작을 지어 보였다."], ["My sister catching him in the act, he drew the back of his hand across his nose with his usual conciliatory air on such occasions, and looked at her.", "누나가 그 짓을 하던 그를 붙잡자, 그는 그런 자리에서 늘 짓던 화해의 태도로 손등을 코에 슬쩍 문지르고는 누나를 바라보았다."]], [["“Well?” said my sister, in her snappish way.", "\"왜요?\" 누나가 톡 쏘는 투로 말했다."], ["“What are you staring at?", "\"뭘 그렇게 빤히 봐요?"], ["Is the house afire?”", "집에 불이라도 났어요?\""]], [["“—Which some individual,”", "\"─어떤 분이,\""], ["Joe politely hinted, “mentioned—she.”", "조가 정중하게 넌지시 말했다. \"말했잖소─그분이라고.\""]], [["“And she is a she, I suppose?”", "\"그러니까 그분은 여자분이겠지요, 안 그래요?\""], ["said my sister. “Unless you call Miss Havisham a he.", "누나가 말했다. \"당신이 해비셤 양을 남자라고 부를 셈이 아니라면 말이에요."], ["And I doubt if even you’ll go so far as that.”", "설마 당신이라도 그 정도까지 갈 것 같진 않은데요.\""]], [["“Miss Havisham, up town?”", "\"읍내 위쪽의 해비셤 양 말이오?\""], ["said Joe.", "조가 말했다."]], [["“Is there any Miss Havisham down town?”", "\"읍내 아래쪽에 무슨 해비셤 양이라도 있나요?\""], ["returned my sister.", "누나가 되받았다."], ["“She wants this boy to go and play there.", "\"그분이 이 녀석을 거기 가서 놀게 하고 싶어 하세요."], ["And of course he’s going.", "그러니 당연히 이 녀석은 갈 거예요."], ["And he had better play there,”", "그리고 거기서 잘 노는 게 좋을 거다.\""], ["said my sister, shaking her head at me as an encouragement to be extremely light and sportive, “or I’ll work him.”", "누나가 더없이 발랄하고 활달하게 굴라는 격려랍시고 나를 향해 고개를 흔들며 말했다. \"안 그러면 호되게 부려 먹을 테니까.\""]], [["I had heard of Miss Havisham up town,—everybody for miles round had heard of Miss Havisham up town,—as an immensely rich and grim lady who lived in a large and dismal house barricaded against robbers, and who led a life of seclusion.", "나는 읍내 위쪽의 해비셤 양에 대한 이야기를 들은 적이 있었다. 몇 마일 안의 사람이라면 누구나 읍내 위쪽의 해비셤 양에 대해 들어 알고 있었으니까. 그분은 어마어마하게 부유하고 무뚝뚝한 부인으로, 강도를 막으려고 빗장을 단단히 걸어 잠근 크고 음산한 저택에 살며, 세상과 등진 은둔의 삶을 살고 있다고들 했다."]], [["“Well to be sure!”", "\"이거 참 놀랍군!\""], ["said Joe, astounded.", "조가 어안이 벙벙해져 말했다."], ["“I wonder how she come to know Pip!”", "\"그분이 어떻게 핍을 알게 됐는지 모르겠는걸!\""]], [["“Noodle!” cried my sister.", "\"멍청이 같으니!\" 누나가 소리쳤다."], ["“Who said she knew him?”", "\"누가 그분이 이 녀석을 안다고 했어요?\""]], [["“—Which some individual,”", "\"─어떤 분이,\""], ["Joe again politely hinted, “mentioned that she wanted him to go and play there.”", "조가 다시 정중하게 넌지시 말했다. \"이 녀석을 거기 가서 놀게 하고 싶어 한다고 말했잖소.\""]], [["“And couldn’t she ask Uncle Pumblechook if he knew of a boy to go and play there?", "\"그럼 그분이 펌블추크 아저씨한테 거기 가서 놀 만한 아이를 아느냐고 물어볼 수도 있는 거 아니에요?"], ["Isn’t it just barely possible that Uncle Pumblechook may be a tenant of hers, and that he may sometimes—we won’t say quarterly or half-yearly, for that would be requiring too much of you—but sometimes—go there to pay his rent?", "펌블추크 아저씨가 그분의 세입자일 가능성이, 그래서 이따금─분기마다라거나 반년마다라고는 안 할게요, 그건 당신한테 너무 많은 걸 바라는 거일 테니─하지만 이따금─그 집에 집세를 내러 갈 가능성이 그저 조금이라도 있는 거 아니냐고요?"], ["And couldn’t she then ask Uncle Pumblechook if he knew of a boy to go and play there?", "그렇다면 그분이 펌블추크 아저씨한테 거기 가서 놀 만한 아이를 아느냐고 물어볼 수도 있는 거 아니에요?"], ["And couldn’t Uncle Pumblechook, being always considerate and thoughtful for us—though you may not think it, Joseph,”", "그리고 펌블추크 아저씨가, 늘 우리를 헤아리고 마음 써 주시는 분이니─당신은 그렇게 생각 안 할지 몰라도요, 조지프,\""], ["in a tone of the deepest reproach, as if he were the most callous of nephews, “then mention this boy, standing Prancing here”—which I solemnly declare I was not doing—“that I have for ever been a willing slave to?”", "마치 그가 더없이 무정한 조카라도 되는 양 더없이 깊은 책망의 어조로, \"그러니 펌블추크 아저씨가 여기 우쭐대며 서 있는 이 녀석을 말씀하실 수도 있는 거 아니냐고요\"─나는 결코 그러고 있지 않았음을 엄숙히 단언한다─\"내가 이제껏 기꺼이 종노릇해 온 이 녀석을 말이에요?\""]], [["“Good again!”", "\"또 한 번 잘 짚으셨군!\""], ["cried Uncle Pumblechook. “Well put!", "펌블추크 아저씨가 외쳤다. \"참 적절한 말씀입니다!"], ["Prettily pointed!", "어찌나 깔끔하게 짚으시는지!"], ["Good indeed!", "정말 옳은 말씀이오!"], ["Now Joseph, you know the case.”", "자, 조지프, 이제 자네도 사정을 알겠지.\""]], [["“No, Joseph,”", "\"아니에요, 조지프.\""], ["said my sister, still in a reproachful manner, while Joe apologetically drew the back of his hand across and across his nose, “you do not yet—though you may not think it—know the case.", "조가 미안한 듯 손등으로 코를 자꾸자꾸 문지르는 동안, 누나가 여전히 책망하는 투로 말했다. \"당신은 아직도─당신은 그렇게 생각 안 할지 몰라도─사정을 모르고 있어요."], ["You may consider that you do, but you do not, Joseph.", "안다고 여길지 몰라도, 모른다고요, 조지프."], ["For you do not know that Uncle Pumblechook, being sensible that for anything we can tell, this boy’s fortune may be made by his going to Miss Havisham’s, has offered to take him into town to-night in his own chaise-cart, and to keep him to-night, and to take him with his own hands to Miss Havisham’s to-morrow morning.", "왜냐하면 당신은 펌블추크 아저씨가, 우리로서는 어찌 될지 알 수 없으나 이 녀석이 해비셤 양 댁에 가는 것으로 이 녀석의 팔자가 펴질지도 모른다는 걸 분별하시고서, 오늘 밤 이 녀석을 자기 이륜마차에 태워 읍내로 데려가, 오늘 밤은 데리고 계시다가, 내일 아침 자기 손으로 직접 해비셤 양 댁에 데려다주겠다고 하신 걸 모르니까요."], ["And Lor-a-mussy me!”", "아이고, 맙소사!\""], ["cried my sister, casting off her bonnet in sudden desperation, “here I stand talking to mere Mooncalfs, with Uncle Pumblechook waiting, and the mare catching cold at the door, and the boy grimed with crock and dirt from the hair of his head to the sole of his foot!”", "누나가 갑작스러운 절망에 보닛을 홱 벗어 던지며 외쳤다. \"펌블추크 아저씨는 기다리시고, 암말은 문 앞에서 감기에 걸리고, 이 녀석은 머리끝부터 발끝까지 검댕과 흙투성이인데, 나는 여기 서서 한낱 얼간이들하고 입씨름이나 하고 있다니!\""]], [["With that, she pounced upon me, like an eagle on a lamb, and my face was squeezed into wooden bowls in sinks, and my head was put under taps of water-butts, and I was soaped, and kneaded, and towelled, and thumped, and harrowed, and rasped, until I really was quite beside myself. (I may here remark that I suppose myself to be better acquainted than any living authority, with the ridgy effect of a wedding-ring, passing unsympathetically over the human countenance.)", "그 말과 함께 누나는, 어린 양을 덮치는 독수리처럼 나를 와락 낚아챘고, 내 얼굴은 개수대의 나무 대야 속으로 짓눌렸으며, 머리는 빗물통의 물꼭지 밑에 처박혔고, 나는 비누칠을 당하고, 주물러지고, 수건질을 당하고, 두들겨 맞고, 갈퀴질당하고, 줄로 쓸리듯 문질러져, 정말이지 거의 제정신이 아닐 지경이 되었다.(여기서 짚어 두건대, 결혼반지가 인간의 얼굴 위를 인정사정없이 지나갈 때의 그 골진 자국에 대해서는, 내가 살아 있는 어떤 권위자보다도 더 잘 알고 있으리라 생각한다.)"]], [["When my ablutions were completed, I was put into clean linen of the stiffest character, like a young penitent into sackcloth, and was trussed up in my tightest and fearfullest suit.", "내 목욕재계가 끝나자, 나는 마치 어린 참회자가 거친 베옷을 입듯, 더없이 빳빳한 깨끗한 속옷을 입고서, 가장 꽉 끼고 가장 무시무시한 정장 속에 옭아매어졌다."], ["I was then delivered over to Mr. Pumblechook, who formally received me as if he were the Sheriff, and who let off upon me the speech that I knew he had been dying to make all along: “Boy, be forever grateful to all friends, but especially unto them which brought you up by hand!”", "그러고 나서 나는 펌블추크 씨에게 넘겨졌는데, 그는 마치 자기가 주 장관이라도 되는 양 나를 격식 갖춰 인계받고는, 내내 못 해서 안달이 났던 게 분명한 그 연설을 내게 쏘아붙였다. \"얘야, 모든 친구에게 영원토록 고마워하되, 특히 너를 손으로 길러 키운 분들께 그리하여라!\""]], [["“Good-bye, Joe!”", "\"안녕히 계세요, 조!\""]], [["“God bless you, Pip, old chap!”", "\"하느님의 축복이 있기를, 핍, 친구!\""]], [["I had never parted from him before, and what with my feelings and what with soapsuds, I could at first see no stars from the chaise-cart.", "나는 그 전까지 한 번도 그와 헤어져 본 적이 없었고, 내 감정 탓도 있고 비눗물 탓도 있어, 처음에는 이륜마차에서 별 하나 보이지 않았다."], ["But they twinkled out one by one, without throwing any light on the questions why on earth I was going to play at Miss Havisham’s, and what on earth I was expected to play at.", "그러나 별들은 하나둘 반짝이며 모습을 드러냈으되, 내가 대체 무슨 까닭으로 해비셤 양 댁에 놀러 가는지, 그리고 거기서 대체 무엇을 하고 놀기를 기대받는지 하는 물음에는 아무런 빛도 비춰 주지 않았다."]], [["# Chapter VIII.", "# Chapter VIII."]], [["Mr. Pumblechook’s premises in the High Street of the market town, were of a peppercorny and farinaceous character, as the premises of a cornchandler and seedsman should be.", "시장 거리에 자리한 펌블추크 씨의 가게는 곡물상이며 종묘상의 가게가 으레 그러하듯, 후추 알갱이 같고 곡물 가루 같은 분위기를 풍기는 곳이었다."], ["It appeared to me that he must be a very happy man indeed, to have so many little drawers in his shop; and I wondered when I peeped into one or two on the lower tiers, and saw the tied-up brown paper packets inside, whether the flower-seeds and bulbs ever wanted of a fine day to break out of those jails, and bloom.", "가게 안에 그렇게나 작은 서랍을 많이 거느리고 있으니, 그분은 참으로 행복한 사람임에 틀림없으리라는 생각이 들었다. 그리고 나는 아래쪽 칸의 서랍 한두 개를 들여다보고는, 그 안에 끈으로 묶인 갈색 종이 꾸러미들이 들어 있는 것을 보며, 저 꽃씨와 알뿌리들이 날씨 좋은 어느 날엔가 그 감옥을 깨고 나와 꽃피우고 싶어 한 적은 없었을까 궁금해했다."]], [["It was in the early morning after my arrival that I entertained this speculation.", "이런 상상에 잠긴 것은 그곳에 도착한 다음 날 이른 아침이었다."], ["On the previous night, I had been sent straight to bed in an attic with a sloping roof, which was so low in the corner where the bedstead was, that I calculated the tiles as being within a foot of my eyebrows.", "전날 밤 나는 곧장 지붕이 비스듬히 기운 다락방의 잠자리로 보내졌는데, 침대가 놓인 구석은 천장이 어찌나 낮은지 기왓장이 내 눈썹에서 한 자도 채 안 되는 거리에 있다고 어림되었다."], ["In the same early morning, I discovered a singular affinity between seeds and corduroys. Mr.", "그 같은 이른 아침에 나는 씨앗과 코르덴 사이에 묘한 친연성이 있음을 발견했다."], ["Pumblechook wore corduroys, and so did his shopman; and somehow, there was a general air and flavour about the corduroys, so much in the nature of seeds, and a general air and flavour about the seeds, so much in the nature of corduroys, that I hardly knew which was which.", "펌블추크 씨는 코르덴 옷을 입고 있었고, 그 가게 점원도 마찬가지였다. 그런데 어쩐 일인지 코르덴에는 씨앗을 빼닮은 전반적인 기운과 풍미가 감돌았고, 씨앗에는 코르덴을 빼닮은 전반적인 기운과 풍미가 감돌아서, 나로서는 어느 것이 어느 것인지 분간하기가 어려울 지경이었다."], ["The same opportunity served me for noticing that Mr. Pumblechook appeared to conduct his business by looking across the street at the saddler, who appeared to transact his business by keeping his eye on the coachmaker, who appeared to get on in life by putting his hands in his pockets and contemplating the baker, who in his turn folded his arms and stared at the grocer, who stood at his door and yawned at the chemist.", "그 같은 기회에 나는 또 한 가지 사실을 알아차렸으니, 펌블추크 씨는 길 건너 마구상을 바라보는 것으로 제 장사를 꾸려 가는 듯했고, 그 마구상은 마차 제조공을 눈여겨보는 것으로 자기 장사를 처리하는 듯했으며, 그 마차 제조공은 두 손을 주머니에 찔러 넣은 채 제빵사를 응시하는 것으로 살림을 꾸려 가는 듯했고, 그 제빵사는 또 그 나름대로 팔짱을 끼고 식료품상을 노려보았으며, 식료품상은 제 가게 문가에 서서 약제사를 향해 하품을 했다."], ["The watchmaker, always poring over a little desk with a magnifying-glass at his eye, and always inspected by a group of smock-frocks poring over him through the glass of his shop-window, seemed to be about the only person in the High Street whose trade engaged his attention.", "한쪽 눈에 확대경을 끼우고 늘 작은 책상에 코를 박고 있는, 그리고 가게 진열창 유리를 통해 그를 들여다보는 작업복 차림의 무리에게 늘 살펴봄을 당하던 시계공만이, 이 시장 거리에서 제 생업에 정신을 쏟고 있는 거의 유일한 사람인 듯싶었다."]], [["Mr. Pumblechook and I breakfasted at eight o’clock in the parlour behind the shop, while the shopman took his mug of tea and hunch of bread and butter on a sack of peas in the front premises.", "펌블추크 씨와 나는 여덟 시에 가게 뒤편 거실에서 아침을 먹었고, 점원은 앞쪽 가게에서 완두콩 자루 위에 앉아 차 한 잔과 버터 바른 빵 한 덩이를 들었다."], ["I considered Mr. Pumblechook wretched company.", "나는 펌블추크 씨가 형편없는 말동무라고 여겼다."], ["Besides being possessed by my sister’s idea that a mortifying and penitential character ought to be imparted to my diet,—besides giving me as much crumb as possible in combination with as little butter, and putting such a quantity of warm water into my milk that it would have been more candid to have left the milk out altogether,—his conversation consisted of nothing but arithmetic.", "내 식사에는 굴욕과 참회의 성격이 깃들어야 한다는 누나의 생각에 사로잡혀 있는 데다 — 버터는 되도록 적게 바르고 빵 속살은 되도록 많이 주며, 내 우유에는 따뜻한 물을 어찌나 많이 타 넣었던지 차라리 우유를 아예 빼는 편이 더 솔직했으리만치 — 그분의 대화란 산수 외에는 아무것도 없었다."], ["On my politely bidding him Good-morning, he said, pompously, “Seven times nine, boy?”", "내가 공손히 안녕히 주무셨느냐고 인사하자, 그분은 거드름을 피우며 \"칠 곱하기 구는, 얘야?\""], ["And how should I be able to answer, dodged in that way, in a strange place, on an empty stomach!", "하고 말했다. 그런 식으로 낯선 곳에서, 빈속에 기습을 당하고서야 내가 어찌 대답을 할 수 있겠는가!"], ["I was hungry, but before I had swallowed a morsel, he began a running sum that lasted all through the breakfast.", "나는 배가 고팠으나, 한 입 삼키기도 전에 그분은 아침 식사 내내 이어지는 연속 계산을 시작했다. \"칠은?\" \"거기에 사는?\""], ["“Seven?” “And four?”", "\"거기에 팔은?\""], ["“And eight?”", "\"거기에 육은?\""], ["“And six?” “And two?”", "\"거기에 이는?\""], ["“And ten?” And so on.", "\"거기에 십은?\""], ["And after each figure was disposed of, it was as much as I could do to get a bite or a sup, before the next came; while he sat at his ease guessing nothing, and eating bacon and hot roll, in (if I may be allowed the expression) a gorging and gormandizing manner.", "하는 식이었다. 그리고 숫자 하나가 처리될 때마다, 다음 숫자가 닥치기 전에 한 입 베어 물거나 한 모금 들이켜는 것이 내가 할 수 있는 전부였다. 그동안 그분은 아무것도 셈할 필요 없이 느긋이 앉아, (이런 표현을 써도 된다면) 게걸스레 처넣고 폭식하는 태도로 베이컨과 따끈한 빵을 먹어 댔다."]], [["For such reasons, I was very glad when ten o’clock came and we started for Miss Havisham’s; though I was not at all at my ease regarding the manner in which I should acquit myself under that lady’s roof.", "그런 까닭에 열 시가 되어 우리가 해비셤 양의 집으로 출발했을 때 나는 무척 기뻤다. 비록 그 부인의 지붕 아래에서 내가 어떻게 처신해야 할지에 대해서는 조금도 마음이 편치 않았지만 말이다."], ["Within a quarter of an hour we came to Miss Havisham’s house, which was of old brick, and dismal, and had a great many iron bars to it.", "십오 분도 안 되어 우리는 해비셤 양의 집에 이르렀는데, 그 집은 낡은 벽돌로 지어진 음울한 건물로, 쇠창살이 무척 많이 둘러쳐져 있었다."], ["Some of the windows had been walled up; of those that remained, all the lower were rustily barred.", "창문 가운데 일부는 벽돌로 막혀 있었고, 남아 있는 것들 중에서도 아래쪽 창은 모조리 녹슨 창살로 막혀 있었다."], ["There was a courtyard in front, and that was barred; so we had to wait, after ringing the bell, until some one should come to open it.", "집 앞에는 안뜰이 있었는데, 그곳에도 창살이 둘려 있었다. 그래서 우리는 종을 울린 뒤, 누군가 나와서 문을 열어 줄 때까지 기다려야 했다."], ["While we waited at the gate, I peeped in (even then Mr. Pumblechook said, “And fourteen?”", "문간에서 기다리는 동안 나는 안을 들여다보았는데(그때에도 펌블추크 씨는 \"거기에 십사는?\""], ["but I pretended not to hear him), and saw that at the side of the house there was a large brewery.", "하고 물었으나 나는 못 들은 척했다), 집 한쪽 곁에 큰 양조장이 있는 것이 보였다."], ["No brewing was going on in it, and none seemed to have gone on for a long long time.", "그 안에서는 술을 빚는 일이 진행되고 있지 않았고, 오래오래 그래 온 듯했다."]], [["A window was raised, and a clear voice demanded “What name?”", "창문 하나가 올라가더니, 맑은 목소리가 \"이름은?\" 하고 물었다."], ["To which my conductor replied, “Pumblechook.”", "그 물음에 나의 인도자는 \"펌블추크요.\""], ["The voice returned, “Quite right,”", "하고 답했다. 목소리는 \"맞아요.\""], ["and the window was shut again, and a young lady came across the court-yard, with keys in her hand.", "하고 돌려주었고, 창문은 다시 닫혔으며, 손에 열쇠를 든 한 어린 아가씨가 안뜰을 가로질러 다가왔다."]], [["“This,”", "\"이 애가,\""], ["said Mr. Pumblechook, “is Pip.”", "펌블추크 씨가 말했다. \"핍입니다.\""]], [["“This is Pip, is it?”", "\"이 애가 핍이라고요?\""], ["returned the young lady, who was very pretty and seemed very proud; “come in, Pip.”", "어린 아가씨가 되받았다. 그 아이는 무척 예뻤고 무척 도도해 보였다. \"들어와, 핍.\""]], [["Mr. Pumblechook was coming in also, when she stopped him with the gate.", "펌블추크 씨도 따라 들어오려 했으나, 그녀는 문으로 그를 막아섰다."]], [["“Oh!”", "\"어머!\""], ["she said. “Did you wish to see Miss Havisham?”", "그녀가 말했다. \"해비셤 양을 뵙고 싶으셨나요?\""]], [["“If Miss Havisham wished to see me,”", "\"해비셤 양께서 저를 보고자 하신다면야.\""], ["returned Mr. Pumblechook, discomfited.", "펌블추크 씨가 무안해하며 되받았다."]], [["“Ah!”", "\"아!\""], ["said the girl; “but you see she don’t.”", "소녀가 말했다. \"그렇지만 보시다시피 그분은 그러길 원치 않으세요.\""]], [["She said it so finally, and in such an undiscussible way, that Mr. Pumblechook, though in a condition of ruffled dignity, could not protest.", "그녀가 그 말을 어찌나 단호하게, 그리고 더는 따질 여지 없는 투로 말했던지, 펌블추크 씨는 위엄이 상한 처지였음에도 항의할 수가 없었다."], ["But he eyed me severely,—as if I had done anything to him!—and departed with the words reproachfully delivered: “Boy!", "그러나 그분은 — 마치 내가 그분에게 무슨 짓이라도 한 양! — 나를 매섭게 노려보더니, 책망하듯 이런 말을 남기고 떠나갔다. \"얘야!"], ["Let your behaviour here be a credit unto them which brought you up by hand!”", "여기서 네 행실이, 손수 너를 길러 주신 분들의 명예가 되도록 해라!\""], ["I was not free from apprehension that he would come back to propound through the gate, “And sixteen?”", "나는 그분이 다시 돌아와 문 너머로 \"거기에 십육은?\" 하고 물어 올까 봐 걱정을 떨치지 못했다."], ["But he didn’t.", "그러나 그러지는 않았다."]], [["My young conductress locked the gate, and we went across the courtyard.", "나의 어린 인도자는 문을 잠갔고, 우리는 안뜰을 가로질러 갔다."], ["It was paved and clean, but grass was growing in every crevice.", "그곳은 포장이 되어 있고 깨끗했으나, 틈마다 풀이 자라 있었다."], ["The brewery buildings had a little lane of communication with it, and the wooden gates of that lane stood open, and all the brewery beyond stood open, away to the high enclosing wall; and all was empty and disused.", "양조장 건물들이 그 안뜰과 작은 통로로 이어져 있었는데, 그 통로의 나무 문은 열려 있었고, 그 너머의 양조장 전체가 저 멀리 둘러친 높은 담장까지 활짝 열려 있었으며, 모든 것이 텅 비고 쓰임을 잃은 채였다."], ["The cold wind seemed to blow colder there than outside the gate; and it made a shrill noise in howling in and out at the open sides of the brewery, like the noise of wind in the rigging of a ship at sea.", "그곳의 찬 바람은 문밖보다도 더 차갑게 부는 듯했고, 양조장의 트인 옆구리로 휘몰아 들고 나며 날카로운 소리를 냈으니, 바다에 떠 있는 배의 삭구 사이로 부는 바람 소리 같았다."]], [["She saw me looking at it, and she said, “You could drink without hurt all the strong beer that’s brewed there now, boy.”", "내가 그곳을 바라보는 것을 보고 그녀가 말했다. \"지금 저기서 빚어지는 독한 맥주를 다 마셔도 탈이 안 날걸, 얘.\""]], [["“I should think I could, miss,”", "\"그럴 것 같네요, 아가씨.\""], ["said I, in a shy way.", "나는 수줍게 말했다."]], [["“Better not try to brew beer there now, or it would turn out sour, boy; don’t you think so?”", "\"이제는 저기서 맥주를 빚지 않는 게 나을 거야, 안 그러면 시큼해질 테니까, 얘. 그렇게 생각하지 않니?\""]], [["“It looks like it, miss.”", "\"그래 보여요, 아가씨.\""]], [["“Not that anybody means to try,”", "\"누가 빚어 보겠다는 건 아니지만.\""], ["she added, “for that’s all done with, and the place will stand as idle as it is till it falls.", "그녀가 덧붙였다. \"다 끝난 일이고, 이곳은 무너질 때까지 지금처럼 놀려진 채 서 있을 테니까."], ["As to strong beer, there’s enough of it in the cellars already, to drown the Manor House.”", "독한 맥주라면, 이미 지하 저장고에 이 영주관을 잠기게 할 만큼 충분히 있고.\""]], [["“Is that the name of this house, miss?”", "\"이 집 이름이 영주관인가요, 아가씨?\""]], [["“One of its names, boy.”", "\"그 이름 가운데 하나야, 얘.\""]], [["“It has more than one, then, miss?”", "\"그럼 이름이 하나 이상인가요, 아가씨?\""]], [["“One more.", "\"하나 더 있어."], ["Its other name was Satis; which is Greek, or Latin, or Hebrew, or all three—or all one to me—for enough.”", "다른 이름은 새티스였어. 그리스어인지, 라틴어인지, 히브리어인지, 아니면 셋 다인지 — 내겐 다 매한가지지만 — 충분하다는 뜻이야.\""]], [["“Enough House,”", "\"충분한 집이라니.\""], ["said I; “that’s a curious name, miss.”", "내가 말했다. \"묘한 이름이네요, 아가씨.\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"그래.\""], ["she replied; “but it meant more than it said.", "그녀가 답했다. \"하지만 말 그대로 이상의 뜻이 있었어."], ["It meant, when it was given, that whoever had this house could want nothing else.", "이 이름이 붙여졌을 때 그것은, 이 집을 가진 사람은 더 바랄 게 없다는 뜻이었지."], ["They must have been easily satisfied in those days, I should think. But don’t loiter, boy.”", "그 시절 사람들은 쉽게 만족했던 모양이야. 하지만 꾸물거리지 마, 얘.\""]], [["Though she called me “boy” so often, and with a carelessness that was far from complimentary, she was of about my own age.", "그녀는 나를 그토록 자주 \"얘\"라고 불렀고, 그것도 칭찬과는 거리가 먼 무심한 투였으나, 나이는 나와 엇비슷했다."], ["She seemed much older than I, of course, being a girl, and beautiful and self-possessed; and she was as scornful of me as if she had been one-and-twenty, and a queen.", "물론 그녀는 나보다 훨씬 나이 들어 보였으니, 여자아이인 데다 아름답고 침착했기 때문이었다. 그리고 그녀는 마치 스물한 살 먹은 여왕이라도 되는 듯 나를 깔보았다."]], [["We went into the house by a side door, the great front entrance had two chains across it outside,—and the first thing I noticed was, that the passages were all dark, and that she had left a candle burning there.", "우리는 옆문으로 집 안에 들어갔다. 으리으리한 정문은 바깥쪽으로 사슬 두 가닥이 가로질러 채워져 있었다. 그리고 내가 맨 먼저 알아챈 것은, 복도가 죄다 어둡고, 그녀가 그곳에 촛불 하나를 켜 둔 채 두었다는 사실이었다."], ["She took it up, and we went through more passages and up a staircase, and still it was all dark, and only the candle lighted us.", "그녀는 그 촛불을 집어 들었고, 우리는 다시 복도 몇 개를 지나 계단을 올라갔는데, 여전히 사방은 어두웠고 오직 그 촛불만이 우리를 밝혀 주었다."]], [["At last we came to the door of a room, and she said, “Go in.”", "마침내 우리는 어느 방의 문 앞에 이르렀고, 그녀가 말했다. \"들어가.\""]], [["I answered, more in shyness than politeness, “After you, miss.”", "나는 공손함보다는 수줍음에서 답했다. \"먼저 들어가세요, 아가씨.\""]], [["To this she returned: “Don’t be ridiculous, boy; I am not going in.”", "이에 그녀가 되받았다. \"바보 같은 소리 마, 얘. 난 안 들어가.\""], ["And scornfully walked away, and—what was worse—took the candle with her.", "그러고는 경멸하듯 걸어가 버렸고 — 더 고약하게도 — 촛불을 가지고 가 버렸다."]], [["This was very uncomfortable, and I was half afraid.", "이것은 무척 불편한 일이었고, 나는 반쯤 겁이 났다."], ["However, the only thing to be done being to knock at the door, I knocked, and was told from within to enter.", "그러나 할 수 있는 일이라곤 문을 두드리는 것뿐이었으므로, 나는 문을 두드렸고, 안에서 들어오라는 말이 들려왔다."], ["I entered, therefore, and found myself in a pretty large room, well lighted with wax candles.", "그래서 나는 들어갔고, 밀랍 양초로 환히 밝혀진 꽤 큰 방 안에 있는 나 자신을 발견했다."], ["No glimpse of daylight was to be seen in it.", "그 안에서는 햇빛이라곤 한 줄기도 보이지 않았다."], ["It was a dressing-room, as I supposed from the furniture, though much of it was of forms and uses then quite unknown to me.", "가구로 미루어 짐작하건대 그곳은 화장방이었으나, 그 가구의 상당수는 당시 내게는 형태도 쓰임새도 전혀 알 수 없는 것들이었다."], ["But prominent in it was a draped table with a gilded looking-glass, and that I made out at first sight to be a fine lady’s dressing-table.", "그러나 그 가운데 가장 눈에 띄는 것은 천을 두르고 금테 거울이 달린 탁자였는데, 나는 첫눈에 그것이 어느 귀부인의 화장대임을 알아보았다."]], [["Whether I should have made out this object so soon if there had been no fine lady sitting at it, I cannot say.", "거기에 귀부인이 앉아 있지 않았더라면 내가 이 물건을 그토록 빨리 알아보았을지는 모르겠다."], ["In an arm-chair, with an elbow resting on the table and her head leaning on that hand, sat the strangest lady I have ever seen, or shall ever see.", "팔걸이의자에 앉아, 한쪽 팔꿈치를 탁자에 괴고 그 손에 머리를 기댄 채, 내가 지금껏 본 적도 없고 앞으로도 보지 못할 가장 기이한 부인이 앉아 있었다."]], [["She was dressed in rich materials,—satins, and lace, and silks,—all of white.", "그녀는 값진 옷감으로 — 새틴과 레이스와 비단으로 — 차려입고 있었는데, 모두가 흰색이었다."], ["Her shoes were white.", "신발도 희었다."], ["And she had a long white veil dependent from her hair, and she had bridal flowers in her hair, but her hair was white.", "그리고 머리에는 길고 흰 면사포를 늘어뜨리고 신부의 꽃을 꽂고 있었으나, 머리카락은 하얗게 세어 있었다."], ["Some bright jewels sparkled on her neck and on her hands, and some other jewels lay sparkling on the table.", "목과 두 손에서는 몇몇 영롱한 보석이 반짝였고, 탁자 위에도 다른 보석들이 반짝이며 놓여 있었다."], ["Dresses, less splendid than the dress she wore, and half-packed trunks, were scattered about.", "그녀가 입은 것보다 덜 화려한 옷가지들과, 절반쯤 꾸려진 트렁크들이 여기저기 흩어져 있었다."], ["She had not quite finished dressing, for she had but one shoe on,—the other was on the table near her hand,—her veil was but half arranged, her watch and chain were not put on, and some lace for her bosom lay with those trinkets, and with her handkerchief, and gloves, and some flowers, and a Prayer-Book all confusedly heaped about the looking-glass.", "그녀는 채 단장을 끝내지 못한 상태였다. 신발은 한 짝만 신고 있었고 — 나머지 한 짝은 그녀의 손 가까이 탁자 위에 있었으며 — 면사포는 반쯤만 정돈되어 있었고, 시계와 사슬은 차지 않은 채였으며, 가슴에 달 레이스 조각이 그 장신구들과 함께, 그녀의 손수건과 장갑과 꽃 몇 송이와 기도서와 더불어 거울 둘레에 어지러이 쌓여 있었다."]], [["It was not in the first few moments that I saw all these things, though I saw more of them in the first moments than might be supposed.", "이 모든 것을 본 것은 처음 몇 순간 사이의 일이 아니었지만, 그래도 그 처음 몇 순간 동안 짐작보다 많은 것을 보긴 했다."], ["But I saw that everything within my view which ought to be white, had been white long ago, and had lost its lustre and was faded and yellow.", "그러나 나는 보았다. 흰색이어야 마땅한 내 시야 안의 모든 것이 오래전에 한때 희었으나, 광택을 잃고 빛이 바래어 누렇게 변해 있음을."], ["I saw that the bride within the bridal dress had withered like the dress, and like the flowers, and had no brightness left but the brightness of her sunken eyes.", "나는 보았다. 신부의 드레스 속에 든 신부가 그 드레스처럼, 그리고 그 꽃들처럼 시들어 버려, 움푹 꺼진 두 눈의 광채 말고는 아무런 광채도 남아 있지 않음을."], ["I saw that the dress had been put upon the rounded figure of a young woman, and that the figure upon which it now hung loose had shrunk to skin and bone.", "나는 보았다. 그 드레스가 어느 젊은 여인의 둥그스름한 몸매 위에 입혀졌었으나, 이제 그것이 헐겁게 걸쳐진 그 몸은 가죽과 뼈로 쪼그라들었음을."], ["Once, I had been taken to see some ghastly waxwork at the Fair, representing I know not what impossible personage lying in state.", "한번은 시장에서 나는, 무어라 형언할 수 없는 어떤 있을 법하지 않은 인물이 안치된 모습을 본뜬 끔찍한 밀랍 인형을 보러 끌려간 적이 있었다."], ["Once, I had been taken to one of our old marsh churches to see a skeleton in the ashes of a rich dress that had been dug out of a vault under the church pavement.", "한번은 우리 고장의 오래된 습지 교회 가운데 한 곳으로, 교회 바닥 아래 지하 묘실에서 파낸, 호화로운 옷의 잿더미 속에 든 해골을 보러 끌려간 적이 있었다."], ["Now, waxwork and skeleton seemed to have dark eyes that moved and looked at me.", "이제 그 밀랍 인형과 해골이 어두운 두 눈을 가지고서, 그 눈을 움직여 나를 바라보는 것만 같았다."], ["I should have cried out, if I could.", "할 수만 있었다면 나는 비명을 질렀으리라."]], [["“Who is it?”", "\"누구냐?\""], ["said the lady at the table.", "탁자에 앉은 부인이 말했다."]], [["“Pip, ma’am.”", "\"핍이에요, 마님.\""]], [["“Pip?”", "\"핍?\""]], [["“Mr. Pumblechook’s boy, ma’am.", "\"펌블추크 씨네 아이예요, 마님."], ["Come—to play.”", "놀러 — 왔어요.\""]], [["“Come nearer; let me look at you.", "\"가까이 오너라. 너를 좀 보자."], ["Come close.”", "바싹 다가와.\""]], [["It was when I stood before her, avoiding her eyes, that I took note of the surrounding objects in detail, and saw that her watch had stopped at twenty minutes to nine, and that a clock in the room had stopped at twenty minutes to nine.", "그녀의 눈길을 피하며 그녀 앞에 섰을 때, 나는 비로소 주변의 물건들을 세세히 살피게 되었고, 그녀의 시계가 아홉 시 이십 분 전에 멈춰 있다는 것을, 그리고 방 안의 괘종시계 또한 아홉 시 이십 분 전에 멈춰 있다는 것을 보았다."]], [["“Look at me,”", "\"나를 보아라.\""], ["said Miss Havisham.", "해비셤 양이 말했다."], ["“You are not afraid of a woman who has never seen the sun since you were born?”", "\"네가 태어난 뒤로 한 번도 해를 본 적 없는 여자가 무섭지는 않으냐?\""]], [["I regret to state that I was not afraid of telling the enormous lie comprehended in the answer “No.”", "유감스럽지만 나는 \"아니요\"라는 대답에 담긴 그 엄청난 거짓말을 하는 것이 두렵지 않았다고 적어 둔다."]], [["“Do you know what I touch here?” she said, laying her hands, one upon the other, on her left side.", "\"내가 여기 무엇을 짚고 있는지 아느냐?\" 그녀가 두 손을 포개어 자기 왼쪽 가슴에 얹으며 말했다."]], [["“Yes, ma’am.”", "\"네, 마님.\""], ["(It made me think of the young man.)", "(그 말에 나는 그 젊은 놈이 떠올랐다.)"]], [["“What do I touch?”", "\"내가 무엇을 짚고 있느냐?\""]], [["“Your heart.”", "\"마님의 심장이요.\""]], [["“Broken!”", "\"부서진!\""]], [["She uttered the word with an eager look, and with strong emphasis, and with a weird smile that had a kind of boast in it.", "그녀는 그 말을, 간절한 눈빛으로, 강한 힘을 주어, 그리고 일종의 자랑이 담긴 기괴한 미소를 띠며 내뱉었다."], ["Afterwards she kept her hands there for a little while, and slowly took them away as if they were heavy.", "그러고는 한동안 거기에 두 손을 얹고 있다가, 마치 손이 무겁기라도 한 듯 천천히 거두어 들였다."]], [["“I am tired,”", "\"나는 지쳤다.\""], ["said Miss Havisham.", "해비셤 양이 말했다."], ["“I want diversion, and I have done with men and women.", "\"기분 전환이 필요해. 남자도 여자도 이젠 진절머리가 난다."], ["Play.”", "놀아 보아라.\""]], [["I think it will be conceded by my most disputatious reader, that she could hardly have directed an unfortunate boy to do anything in the wide world more difficult to be done under the circumstances.", "이런 상황에서, 한 가엾은 사내아이에게 그녀가 시킬 수 있었던 것 가운데, 이 넓디넓은 세상에서 이보다 더 하기 어려운 일은 없었으리라는 점은, 가장 트집 잡기 좋아하는 독자라도 인정해 주리라 생각한다."]], [["“I sometimes have sick fancies,”", "\"나는 가끔 병적인 공상에 빠지지.\""], ["she went on, “and I have a sick fancy that I want to see some play. There, there!”", "그녀가 말을 이었다. \"그리고 지금 나는 누가 노는 모습을 좀 보고 싶다는 병적인 공상에 빠져 있단다."], ["with an impatient movement of the fingers of her right hand; “play, play, play!”", "자, 어서!\" 오른손 손가락을 초조하게 까딱이며 말했다. \"놀아, 놀아, 놀란 말이야!\""]], [["For a moment, with the fear of my sister’s working me before my eyes, I had a desperate idea of starting round the room in the assumed character of Mr.", "순간, 누나가 나를 두들길 것이라는 두려움이 눈앞에 어른거려, 나는 펌블추크 씨의 이륜마차 흉내라도 내며 방 안을 빙빙 돌까 하는 필사적인 생각을 했다."], ["Pumblechook’s chaise-cart. But I felt myself so unequal to the performance that I gave it up, and stood looking at Miss Havisham in what I suppose she took for a dogged manner, inasmuch as she said, when we had taken a good look at each other,—", "그러나 도무지 그 연기를 해낼 자신이 없어 그만두었고, 해비셤 양을 바라보며 서 있었는데, 그녀가 그것을 고집스러운 태도로 받아들인 모양이었다. 우리가 서로를 한참 들여다본 뒤에 그녀가 이렇게 말했으니까."]], [["“Are you sullen and obstinate?”", "\"너는 부루퉁하고 고집이 센 게냐?\""]], [["“No, ma’am, I am very sorry for you, and very sorry I can’t play just now.", "\"아니에요, 마님, 저는 마님이 무척 가엾고, 지금 당장 놀지 못하는 게 무척 죄송해요."], ["If you complain of me I shall get into trouble with my sister, so I would do it if I could; but it’s so new here, and so strange, and so fine,—and melancholy—.”", "마님이 저를 일러바치시면 저는 누나한테 혼이 날 테니, 할 수 있다면 하겠어요. 하지만 여기는 너무 낯설고, 너무 이상하고, 너무 으리으리하고 — 그리고 서글퍼서 —.\""], ["I stopped, fearing I might say too much, or had already said it, and we took another look at each other.", "나는 너무 많은 말을 할까 봐, 혹은 이미 해 버렸을까 봐 두려워 말을 멈추었고, 우리는 다시 서로를 들여다보았다."]], [["Before she spoke again, she turned her eyes from me, and looked at the dress she wore, and at the dressing-table, and finally at herself in the looking-glass.", "그녀가 다시 입을 열기 전에, 그녀는 나에게서 눈길을 거두어 자기가 입은 드레스를 바라보고, 화장대를 바라보고, 끝내는 거울 속의 자기 자신을 바라보았다."]], [["“So new to him,”", "\"저 애한테는 너무 새롭고,\" 그녀가 중얼거렸다."], ["she muttered, “so old to me; so strange to him, so familiar to me; so melancholy to both of us!", "\"내겐 너무 오래되었지. 저 애한테는 너무 낯설고, 내겐 너무 익숙하지. 우리 둘 다에게는 너무 서글프고!"], ["Call Estella.”", "에스텔라를 불러라.\""]], [["As she was still looking at the reflection of herself, I thought she was still talking to herself, and kept quiet.", "그녀가 여전히 거울에 비친 자기 모습을 바라보고 있었으므로, 나는 그녀가 아직 혼잣말을 하고 있는 줄 알고 잠자코 있었다."]], [["“Call Estella,”", "\"에스텔라를 불러라.\""], ["she repeated, flashing a look at me.", "그녀가 나를 향해 휙 눈길을 쏘며 거듭 말했다."], ["“You can do that.", "\"그건 할 수 있겠지."], ["Call Estella.", "에스텔라를 불러."], ["At the door.”", "문간에서.\""]], [["To stand in the dark in a mysterious passage of an unknown house, bawling Estella to a scornful young lady neither visible nor responsive, and feeling it a dreadful liberty so to roar out her name, was almost as bad as playing to order.", "낯선 집의 어느 으스스한 복도에서 어둠 속에 서서, 보이지도 대답하지도 않는 도도한 어린 아가씨를 향해 에스텔라를 외쳐 부르는 것은, 그리고 그렇게 그녀의 이름을 큰 소리로 외치는 것이 끔찍한 무례처럼 느껴지는 것은, 시키는 대로 노는 일만큼이나 거의 못 할 노릇이었다."], ["But she answered at last, and her light came along the dark passage like a star.", "그러나 그녀는 마침내 대답했고, 그녀의 불빛이 어두운 복도를 따라 별처럼 다가왔다."]], [["Miss Havisham beckoned her to come close, and took up a jewel from the table, and tried its effect upon her fair young bosom and against her pretty brown hair.", "해비셤 양은 그녀에게 가까이 오라고 손짓하더니, 탁자에서 보석 하나를 집어 들어 그녀의 곱고 앳된 가슴께와 예쁜 갈색 머리에 견주어 그 효과를 가늠해 보았다."], ["“Your own, one day, my dear, and you will use it well.", "\"언젠가는 네 것이 될 거란다, 얘야, 너는 이걸 잘 쓸 게다."], ["Let me see you play cards with this boy.”", "이 애와 카드 놀이 하는 걸 좀 보여 다오.\""]], [["“With this boy? Why, he is a common labouring-boy!”", "\"이 애랑요? 아니, 이 앤 그냥 천한 막일하는 아이잖아요!\""]], [["I thought I overheard Miss Havisham answer,—only it seemed so unlikely,—“Well?", "나는 해비셤 양이 — 다만 너무 있을 법하지 않은 일이라 그렇게 들렸을 뿐인지도 모르나 — 이렇게 대답하는 것을 엿들었다고 생각했다. \"그래서?"], ["You can break his heart.”", "네가 저 애의 마음을 부숴 놓으면 되지.\""]], [["“What do you play, boy?” asked Estella of myself, with the greatest disdain.", "\"무슨 놀이 할 줄 아니, 얘?\" 에스텔라가 더없는 멸시를 담아 나에게 물었다."]], [["“Nothing but beggar my neighbour, miss.”", "\"거지 만들기밖에 못 해요, 아가씨.\""]], [["“Beggar him,” said Miss Havisham to Estella.", "\"이 애를 거지로 만들어 버려.\" 해비셤 양이 에스텔라에게 말했다."], ["So we sat down to cards.", "그래서 우리는 카드 놀이를 하려고 자리에 앉았다."]], [["It was then I began to understand that everything in the room had stopped, like the watch and the clock, a long time ago.", "그제야 나는 방 안의 모든 것이, 그 시계와 괘종시계처럼, 오래전에 멈춰 버렸음을 알아차리기 시작했다."], ["I noticed that Miss Havisham put down the jewel exactly on the spot from which she had taken it up.", "나는 해비셤 양이 집어 들었던 자리에 그 보석을 정확히 도로 내려놓는 것을 보았다."], ["As Estella dealt the cards, I glanced at the dressing-table again, and saw that the shoe upon it, once white, now yellow, had never been worn.", "에스텔라가 카드를 돌리는 동안, 나는 화장대를 다시 흘끗 보았고, 그 위에 놓인, 한때 희었으나 이제 누레진 신발이 한 번도 신긴 적 없는 것임을 보았다."], ["I glanced down at the foot from which the shoe was absent, and saw that the silk stocking on it, once white, now yellow, had been trodden ragged.", "나는 그 신발이 없는 발을 내려다보았고, 그 발에 신긴, 한때 희었으나 이제 누레진 비단 양말이 너덜너덜해지도록 밟혀 있음을 보았다."], ["Without this arrest of everything, this standing still of all the pale decayed objects, not even the withered bridal dress on the collapsed form could have looked so like grave-clothes, or the long veil so like a shroud.", "모든 것이 이렇게 멈추어 있지 않았다면, 이 모든 빛바래고 썩어 가는 물건들이 이렇게 정지해 있지 않았다면, 무너진 몸 위의 시든 신부 드레스조차 그토록 수의(壽衣)처럼 보이지도, 그 긴 면사포가 그토록 시신을 덮는 천처럼 보이지도 않았으리라."]], [["So she sat, corpse-like, as we played at cards; the frillings and trimmings on her bridal dress, looking like earthy paper.", "우리가 카드 놀이를 하는 동안 그녀는 그렇게 시체처럼 앉아 있었다. 그녀의 신부 드레스에 달린 주름 장식과 가두리 장식은 흙빛으로 바랜 종이 같았다."], ["I knew nothing then of the discoveries that are occasionally made of bodies buried in ancient times, which fall to powder in the moment of being distinctly seen; but, I have often thought since, that she must have looked as if the admission of the natural light of day would have struck her to dust.", "그 무렵 나는, 이따금 발굴되는 옛 시대의 매장된 시신이 또렷이 눈에 들어오는 순간 가루로 무너져 내린다는 사실을 전혀 알지 못했다. 그러나 나는 그 뒤로 자주 생각했다. 그녀는 마치 자연스러운 한낮의 빛이 들어오기만 하면 곧 한 줌 먼지로 부스러져 버릴 것처럼 보였으리라고."]], [["“He calls the knaves Jacks, this boy!”", "\"이 애는 으뜸패를 잭이라고 부르네요!\""], ["said Estella with disdain, before our first game was out.", "첫 판이 채 끝나기도 전에 에스텔라가 멸시를 담아 말했다."], ["“And what coarse hands he has!", "\"게다가 손은 어쩜 이리 거칠담!"], ["And what thick boots!”", "신발은 또 어쩜 이리 투박하고!\""]], [["I had never thought of being ashamed of my hands before; but I began to consider them a very indifferent pair.", "나는 전에는 한 번도 내 손을 부끄러워해 본 적이 없었다. 그러나 이제 그것이 무척 보잘것없는 한 쌍이라는 생각이 들기 시작했다."], ["Her contempt for me was so strong, that it became infectious, and I caught it.", "나를 향한 그녀의 멸시가 어찌나 강렬했던지, 그것이 전염되어 나에게도 옮아 버렸다."]], [["She won the game, and I dealt.", "그녀가 그 판을 이겼고, 이번엔 내가 카드를 돌렸다."], ["I misdealt, as was only natural, when I knew she was lying in wait for me to do wrong; and she denounced me for a stupid, clumsy labouring-boy.", "그녀가 내가 실수하기를 노리며 기다리고 있다는 것을 알고 있었으니, 카드를 잘못 돌린 것은 지극히 당연한 일이었다. 그러자 그녀는 나를 멍청하고 굼뜬 막일하는 아이라고 욕했다."]], [["“You say nothing of her,” remarked Miss Havisham to me, as she looked on.", "\"너는 저 애에 대해 아무 말도 하지 않는구나.\" 해비셤 양이 지켜보며 나에게 말했다."], ["“She says many hard things of you, but you say nothing of her.", "\"저 애는 너에 대해 모진 말을 많이 하는데, 너는 저 애에 대해 아무 말도 하지 않아."], ["What do you think of her?”", "저 애를 어떻게 생각하느냐?\""]], [["“I don’t like to say,”", "\"말하고 싶지 않아요.\""], ["I stammered.", "나는 더듬거렸다."]], [["“Tell me in my ear,” said Miss Havisham, bending down.", "\"내 귀에 대고 말해 보아라.\" 해비셤 양이 몸을 굽히며 말했다."]], [["“I think she is very proud,”", "\"저 누나는 무척 도도한 것 같아요.\""], ["I replied, in a whisper.", "나는 속삭이며 답했다."]], [["“Anything else?”", "\"그 밖에는?\""]], [["“I think she is very pretty.”", "\"무척 예쁜 것 같아요.\""]], [["“Anything else?”", "\"그 밖에는?\""]], [["“I think she is very insulting.”", "\"무척 사람을 모욕하는 것 같아요.\""], ["(She was looking at me then with a look of supreme aversion.)", "(그때 그녀는 더없는 혐오의 눈길로 나를 바라보고 있었다.)"]], [["“Anything else?”", "\"그 밖에는?\""]], [["“I think I should like to go home.”", "\"집에 갔으면 좋겠어요.\""]], [["“And never see her again, though she is so pretty?”", "\"저 애가 저토록 예쁜데도, 다시는 안 보고 싶으냐?\""]], [["“I am not sure that I shouldn’t like to see her again, but I should like to go home now.”", "\"다시 보는 게 싫지는 않을 것 같지만, 지금은 집에 가고 싶어요.\""]], [["“You shall go soon,”", "\"곧 가게 될 게다.\""], ["said Miss Havisham, aloud.", "해비셤 양이 소리 내어 말했다."], ["“Play the game out.”", "\"판이나 끝내거라.\""]], [["Saving for the one weird smile at first, I should have felt almost sure that Miss Havisham’s face could not smile.", "처음에 보았던 그 기괴한 미소 한 번만 아니었다면, 나는 해비셤 양의 얼굴이 도저히 미소 지을 수 없는 것이라고 거의 확신했으리라."], ["It had dropped into a watchful and brooding expression,—most likely when all the things about her had become transfixed,—and it looked as if nothing could ever lift it up again.", "그 얼굴은 — 십중팔구 그녀를 둘러싼 모든 것이 정지해 버렸을 그 무렵에 — 경계하며 곱씹는 듯한 표정으로 굳어 떨어진 채였고, 다시는 무엇으로도 그것을 들어 올릴 수 없을 것처럼 보였다."], ["Her chest had dropped, so that she stooped; and her voice had dropped, so that she spoke low, and with a dead lull upon her; altogether, she had the appearance of having dropped body and soul, within and without, under the weight of a crushing blow.", "그녀의 가슴은 푹 꺼져 그녀는 구부정했고, 목소리도 가라앉아 그녀는 낮게, 죽은 듯한 적막을 두르고 말했다. 한마디로 그녀는 짓이기는 일격의 무게 아래, 몸도 영혼도, 안도 밖도 모조리 가라앉아 버린 모습이었다."]], [["I played the game to an end with Estella, and she beggared me.", "나는 에스텔라와 그 판을 끝까지 했고, 그녀는 나를 거지로 만들어 버렸다."], ["She threw the cards down on the table when she had won them all, as if she despised them for having been won of me.", "그녀는 카드를 모두 따자 그것을 탁자 위에 내던졌는데, 마치 나에게서 따낸 것이라 하여 그 카드들을 경멸하기라도 하는 듯했다."]], [["“When shall I have you here again?”", "\"언제 다시 너를 오게 할까?\""], ["said Miss Havisham.", "해비셤 양이 말했다."], ["“Let me think.”", "\"어디 보자.\""]], [["I was beginning to remind her that to-day was Wednesday, when she checked me with her former impatient movement of the fingers of her right hand.", "나는 오늘이 수요일이라는 것을 그녀에게 일러 주려던 참이었는데, 그녀가 아까처럼 오른손 손가락을 초조하게 까딱이며 나를 가로막았다."]], [["“There, there!", "\"됐다, 됐어!"], ["I know nothing of days of the week; I know nothing of weeks of the year.", "나는 한 주의 요일이 어떤지도 모르고, 한 해의 주가 어떤지도 모른다."], ["Come again after six days.", "엿새 뒤에 다시 오너라."], ["You hear?”", "알았느냐?\""]], [["“Yes, ma’am.”", "\"네, 마님.\""]], [["“Estella, take him down. Let him have something to eat, and let him roam and look about him while he eats.", "\"에스텔라, 이 애를 데리고 내려가거라. 먹을 것을 좀 주고, 먹는 동안 여기저기 거닐며 둘러보게 해라."], ["Go, Pip.”", "가거라, 핍.\""]], [["I followed the candle down, as I had followed the candle up, and she stood it in the place where we had found it.", "나는 촛불을 따라 올라왔던 것처럼, 촛불을 따라 내려갔고, 그녀는 우리가 그것을 찾았던 자리에 그것을 세워 두었다."], ["Until she opened the side entrance, I had fancied, without thinking about it, that it must necessarily be night-time.", "그녀가 옆문을 열기 전까지, 나는 별생각 없이 막연히 지금이 밤일 거라고 여기고 있었다."], ["The rush of the daylight quite confounded me, and made me feel as if I had been in the candlelight of the strange room many hours.", "쏟아져 드는 한낮의 빛이 나를 완전히 어리둥절하게 만들었고, 마치 내가 그 기이한 방의 촛불 속에서 여러 시간을 보낸 것 같은 느낌이 들게 했다."]], [["“You are to wait here, you boy,”", "\"넌 여기서 기다려, 얘.\""], ["said Estella; and disappeared and closed the door.", "에스텔라가 말했다. 그러고는 사라져 문을 닫아 버렸다."]], [["I took the opportunity of being alone in the courtyard to look at my coarse hands and my common boots.", "나는 안뜰에 혼자 있게 된 김에 내 거친 두 손과 투박한 신발을 들여다보았다."], ["My opinion of those accessories was not favourable.", "그 부속물들에 대한 내 평가는 호의적이지 못했다."], ["They had never troubled me before, but they troubled me now, as vulgar appendages.", "그것들은 전에는 한 번도 나를 괴롭힌 적이 없었으나, 이제는 천박한 군더더기로서 나를 괴롭혔다."], ["I determined to ask Joe why he had ever taught me to call those picture-cards Jacks, which ought to be called knaves.", "나는 으뜸패는 본디 으뜸패라 불러야 마땅한 것을 어째서 잭이라고 부르도록 가르쳤느냐고 조에게 물어보리라 마음먹었다."], ["I wished Joe had been rather more genteelly brought up, and then I should have been so too.", "나는 조가 좀 더 점잖게 자랐더라면 좋았으리라고, 그랬다면 나도 그러했으리라고 바랐다."]], [["She came back, with some bread and meat and a little mug of beer.", "그녀가 빵과 고기와 작은 맥주 한 잔을 들고 돌아왔다."], ["She put the mug down on the stones of the yard, and gave me the bread and meat without looking at me, as insolently as if I were a dog in disgrace.", "그녀는 그 잔을 안뜰의 돌바닥 위에 내려놓고, 마치 내가 망신을 당한 개라도 되는 양 거만하게, 나를 쳐다보지도 않은 채 빵과 고기를 건넸다."], ["I was so humiliated, hurt, spurned, offended, angry, sorry,—I cannot hit upon the right name for the smart—God knows what its name was,—that tears started to my eyes.", "나는 어찌나 굴욕스럽고, 상처받고, 멸시당하고, 모욕당하고, 화가 나고, 서러웠던지 — 그 쓰라림에 알맞은 이름을 도무지 찾을 수가 없는데 — 그 이름이 무엇이었는지는 신만이 아시리라 — 두 눈에 눈물이 솟구쳤다."], ["The moment they sprang there, the girl looked at me with a quick delight in having been the cause of them.", "그것이 솟아오른 순간, 그 소녀는 자기가 그 눈물의 원인이 되었다는 데에 재빠른 기쁨을 느끼며 나를 바라보았다."], ["This gave me power to keep them back and to look at her: so, she gave a contemptuous toss—but with a sense, I thought, of having made too sure that I was so wounded—and left me.", "그것이 나에게 눈물을 참고 그녀를 마주 볼 힘을 주었다. 그러자 그녀는 경멸하듯 고개를 홱 젖혔다 — 그러나 내가 그토록 상처받았다고 너무 확신해 버린 것은 아닐까 하는 기색이 비쳤다고, 나는 생각했다 — 그러고는 나를 두고 가 버렸다."]], [["But when she was gone, I looked about me for a place to hide my face in, and got behind one of the gates in the brewery-lane, and leaned my sleeve against the wall there, and leaned my forehead on it and cried.", "그러나 그녀가 가 버리자, 나는 얼굴을 가릴 곳을 찾아 둘러보다가, 양조장 통로의 문들 가운데 하나 뒤로 숨어, 거기 벽에 소맷자락을 받치고, 그 위에 이마를 기댄 채 울었다."], ["As I cried, I kicked the wall, and took a hard twist at my hair; so bitter were my feelings, and so sharp was the smart without a name, that needed counteraction.", "울면서 나는 벽을 걷어찼고, 내 머리카락을 사납게 잡아 비틀었다. 그만큼 내 감정은 쓰라렸고, 그 이름 없는 쓰라림은 어떻게든 풀어내야 할 만큼 날카로웠다."]], [["My sister’s bringing up had made me sensitive.", "누나의 양육 방식은 나를 예민하게 만들어 놓았다."], ["In the little world in which children have their existence whosoever brings them up, there is nothing so finely perceived and so finely felt as injustice.", "누가 길러 주든 간에, 아이들이 그들 나름의 삶을 살아가는 그 작은 세계에서, 불의(不義)만큼 예민하게 감지되고 예민하게 느껴지는 것은 없다."], ["It may be only small injustice that the child can be exposed to; but the child is small, and its world is small, and its rocking-horse stands as many hands high, according to scale, as a big-boned Irish hunter.", "아이가 겪을 수 있는 불의란 사소한 것일 수도 있다. 그러나 아이는 작고, 그 세계도 작으며, 그 흔들목마는 비례로 따지면 골격이 큰 아일랜드 사냥말만큼이나 키가 크다."], ["Within myself, I had sustained, from my babyhood, a perpetual conflict with injustice.", "내 안에서, 나는 갓난아기 적부터 불의와 끊임없는 다툼을 견뎌 왔다."], ["I had known, from the time when I could speak, that my sister, in her capricious and violent coercion, was unjust to me.", "말을 할 수 있게 된 때부터, 나는 누나가 그 변덕스럽고 난폭한 강압으로 나에게 불공평하다는 것을 알고 있었다."], ["I had cherished a profound conviction that her bringing me up by hand gave her no right to bring me up by jerks.", "누나가 나를 손수 길렀다 해서, 그것이 나를 거칠게 잡아채며 기를 권리까지 주는 것은 아니라는 깊은 확신을 나는 마음에 품어 왔다."], ["Through all my punishments, disgraces, fasts, and vigils, and other penitential performances, I had nursed this assurance; and to my communing so much with it, in a solitary and unprotected way, I in great part refer the fact that I was morally timid and very sensitive.", "온갖 벌과 망신과 굶김과 밤샘과 그 밖의 참회의 의식들을 거치는 내내, 나는 이 확신을 키워 왔다. 그리고 외롭고 보호받지 못한 처지에서 그것을 그토록 곱씹어 온 탓에, 내가 도덕적으로 겁이 많고 무척 예민해진 것이라고 나는 대체로 여긴다."]], [["I got rid of my injured feelings for the time by kicking them into the brewery wall, and twisting them out of my hair, and then I smoothed my face with my sleeve, and came from behind the gate.", "나는 그 상한 감정을 한동안, 양조장 벽을 걷어차 그리로 차 넣고, 머리카락을 비틀어 거기서 짜내는 것으로 떨쳐 냈다. 그런 다음 소맷자락으로 얼굴을 매만지고 문 뒤에서 나왔다."], ["The bread and meat were acceptable, and the beer was warming and tingling, and I was soon in spirits to look about me.", "빵과 고기는 반가웠고, 맥주는 몸을 데우고 짜릿하게 했으니, 나는 곧 기운을 차려 주위를 둘러보게 되었다."]], [["To be sure, it was a deserted place, down to the pigeon-house in the brewery-yard, which had been blown crooked on its pole by some high wind, and would have made the pigeons think themselves at sea, if there had been any pigeons there to be rocked by it.", "과연 그곳은 버려진 곳이었으니, 양조장 마당의 비둘기집에 이르기까지 그러했다. 그 비둘기집은 무슨 거센 바람에 장대 위에서 비뚜로 떠밀린 채였으니, 흔들어 줄 비둘기가 한 마리라도 거기 있었더라면 그 비둘기들은 자기들이 바다에 떠 있다고 여겼을 법했다."], ["But there were no pigeons in the dove-cot, no horses in the stable, no pigs in the sty, no malt in the storehouse, no smells of grains and beer in the copper or the vat.", "그러나 비둘기집에는 비둘기가 없었고, 마구간에는 말이 없었고, 돼지우리에는 돼지가 없었고, 곳간에는 엿기름이 없었으며, 가마솥이나 양조통에는 곡물과 맥주의 냄새가 없었다."], ["All the uses and scents of the brewery might have evaporated with its last reek of smoke.", "양조장의 온갖 쓰임과 향내는 그 마지막 연기와 함께 증발해 버린 모양이었다."], ["In a by-yard, there was a wilderness of empty casks, which had a certain sour remembrance of better days lingering about them; but it was too sour to be accepted as a sample of the beer that was gone,—and in this respect I remember those recluses as being like most others.", "한쪽 곁마당에는 빈 술통들이 황야처럼 늘어서 있었는데, 거기에는 더 나았던 시절에 대한 어떤 시큼한 추억이 어려 있었다. 그러나 그것은 사라진 맥주의 견본으로 받아들이기에는 너무 시큼했다. 이 점에서 나는 그 은둔자 같은 술통들이 여느 은둔자들 대부분과 닮았다고 기억한다."]], [["Behind the furthest end of the brewery, was a rank garden with an old wall; not so high but that I could struggle up and hold on long enough to look over it, and see that the rank garden was the garden of the house, and that it was overgrown with tangled weeds, but that there was a track upon the green and yellow paths, as if some one sometimes walked there, and that Estella was walking away from me even then.", "양조장의 맨 끝 너머에는 낡은 담장을 두른 무성한 정원이 있었다. 그 담은 그리 높지 않아서, 나는 기어올라 한참 매달려 그 너머를 넘겨다볼 수 있었고, 그 무성한 정원이 바로 그 집의 정원이며, 엉클어진 잡초가 우거져 있으나, 푸르고 누런 오솔길 위에는 누군가 가끔 거기를 거니는 듯 발자취가 나 있고, 바로 그때에도 에스텔라가 나에게서 멀어지며 걸어가고 있음을 보았다."], ["But she seemed to be everywhere.", "그러나 그녀는 어디에나 있는 듯했다."], ["For when I yielded to the temptation presented by the casks, and began to walk on them, I saw her walking on them at the end of the yard of casks.", "내가 술통들이 안기는 유혹에 굴복하여 그 위를 걷기 시작했을 때, 통이 늘어선 마당 저쪽 끝에서 그 위를 걸어가는 그녀를 보았으니까."], ["She had her back towards me, and held her pretty brown hair spread out in her two hands, and never looked round, and passed out of my view directly.", "그녀는 나에게 등을 돌린 채, 예쁜 갈색 머리를 두 손으로 펼쳐 든 채, 한 번도 돌아보지 않고 곧장 내 시야 밖으로 사라졌다."], ["So, in the brewery itself,—by which I mean the large paved lofty place in which they used to make the beer, and where the brewing utensils still were.", "양조장 안에서도 그러했다 — 내가 양조장이라 함은, 그들이 맥주를 빚던, 그리고 양조 기구들이 아직 남아 있는, 포장된 높다란 큰 공간을 두고 하는 말이다."], ["When I first went into it, and, rather oppressed by its gloom, stood near the door looking about me, I saw her pass among the extinguished fires, and ascend some light iron stairs, and go out by a gallery high overhead, as if she were going out into the sky.", "내가 처음 그 안에 들어가, 그 음울함에 다소 짓눌린 채 문가에 서서 둘러보고 있었을 때, 나는 그녀가 꺼진 화덕들 사이를 지나, 가벼운 쇠 계단을 올라, 머리 위 높은 회랑을 통해 밖으로 나가는 것을 보았는데, 마치 하늘 속으로 걸어 나가는 듯했다."]], [["It was in this place, and at this moment, that a strange thing happened to my fancy.", "바로 이곳에서, 바로 이 순간에, 내 공상에 기이한 일이 일어났다."], ["I thought it a strange thing then, and I thought it a stranger thing long afterwards.", "그때에도 나는 그것을 기이한 일로 여겼고, 한참 뒤에는 더욱 기이한 일로 여겼다."], ["I turned my eyes—a little dimmed by looking up at the frosty light—towards a great wooden beam in a low nook of the building near me on my right hand, and I saw a figure hanging there by the neck.", "나는 — 서리 같은 빛을 올려다보느라 조금 흐려진 — 두 눈을, 내 오른편 가까이 건물의 낮은 구석에 있는 커다란 나무 들보 쪽으로 돌렸고, 거기에 한 형상이 목이 매달려 있는 것을 보았다."], ["A figure all in yellow white, with but one shoe to the feet; and it hung so, that I could see that the faded trimmings of the dress were like earthy paper, and that the face was Miss Havisham’s, with a movement going over the whole countenance as if she were trying to call to me.", "온통 누르스름한 흰빛에, 발에는 신발이 한 짝뿐인 형상이었다. 그리고 그것은 그렇게 매달려 있어서, 나는 그 드레스의 빛바랜 가두리 장식이 흙빛 종이 같다는 것을, 그리고 그 얼굴이 해비셤 양의 얼굴이며, 마치 나를 부르려 하는 듯 온 얼굴에 어떤 움직임이 스쳐 지나가는 것을 알아볼 수 있었다."], ["In the terror of seeing the figure, and in the terror of being certain that it had not been there a moment before, I at first ran from it, and then ran towards it.", "그 형상을 본 공포 속에서, 그리고 조금 전까지만 해도 그것이 거기 없었다고 확신한 공포 속에서, 나는 처음엔 그것에게서 달아났다가, 이내 그것을 향해 달려갔다."], ["And my terror was greatest of all when I found no figure there.", "그리고 거기에 아무 형상도 없음을 알았을 때, 나의 공포는 무엇보다도 가장 컸다."]], [["Nothing less than the frosty light of the cheerful sky, the sight of people passing beyond the bars of the court-yard gate, and the reviving influence of the rest of the bread and meat and beer, would have brought me round.", "명랑한 하늘의 서리 같은 빛도, 안뜰 문의 창살 너머로 지나가는 사람들의 모습도, 남은 빵과 고기와 맥주가 되살려 주는 기운도, 그 무엇이라도 모자랐다면 나는 제정신을 차리지 못했으리라."], ["Even with those aids, I might not have come to myself as soon as I did, but that I saw Estella approaching with the keys, to let me out.", "그런 도움이 있었음에도, 에스텔라가 나를 내보내 주려고 열쇠를 들고 다가오는 것을 보지 못했다면, 나는 그토록 빨리 정신을 차리지 못했을지도 모른다."], ["She would have some fair reason for looking down upon me, I thought, if she saw me frightened; and she would have no fair reason.", "만약 그녀가 내가 겁먹은 모습을 본다면, 나를 깔볼 그럴듯한 이유가 생길 거라고 나는 생각했다. 그녀에게 그럴듯한 이유를 주지는 않으리라."]], [["She gave me a triumphant glance in passing me, as if she rejoiced that my hands were so coarse and my boots were so thick, and she opened the gate, and stood holding it.", "그녀는 나를 지나치며 의기양양한 눈길을 던졌는데, 마치 내 손이 그토록 거칠고 내 신발이 그토록 투박한 것을 기뻐하기라도 하는 듯했다. 그러고는 문을 열어 그것을 붙든 채 서 있었다."], ["I was passing out without looking at her, when she touched me with a taunting hand.", "내가 그녀를 보지 않고 막 나가려는데, 그녀가 비웃는 듯한 손길로 나를 건드렸다."]], [["“Why don’t you cry?”", "\"넌 왜 안 우니?\""]], [["“Because I don’t want to.”", "\"울고 싶지 않으니까요.\""]], [["“You do,” said she.", "\"울고 싶잖아.\""], ["“You have been crying till you are half blind, and you are near crying again now.”", "그녀가 말했다. \"넌 반쯤 눈이 멀 만큼 울었고, 지금도 또 울 것 같은데 뭐.\""]], [["She laughed contemptuously, pushed me out, and locked the gate upon me.", "그녀는 경멸하듯 웃고는 나를 밖으로 떠밀고, 내 뒤로 문을 잠가 버렸다."], ["I went straight to Mr. Pumblechook’s, and was immensely relieved to find him not at home.", "나는 곧장 펌블추크 씨네로 갔고, 그분이 집에 없는 것을 알고는 한없이 마음이 놓였다."], ["So, leaving word with the shopman on what day I was wanted at Miss Havisham’s again, I set off on the four-mile walk to our forge; pondering, as I went along, on all I had seen, and deeply revolving that I was a common labouring-boy; that my hands were coarse; that my boots were thick; that I had fallen into a despicable habit of calling knaves Jacks; that I was much more ignorant than I had considered myself last night, and generally that I was in a low-lived bad way.", "그래서 언제 다시 해비셤 양 댁에 와야 하는지를 점원에게 일러두고, 나는 우리 대장간까지 사 마일 길을 걷기 시작했다. 가는 내내 내가 본 모든 것을 곰곰이 되씹으며, 나는 그저 천한 막일하는 아이라는 것을, 내 손은 거칠다는 것을, 내 신발은 투박하다는 것을, 내가 으뜸패를 잭이라 부르는 천박한 버릇에 빠져 있었다는 것을, 어젯밤 스스로 여겼던 것보다 훨씬 더 무지하다는 것을, 그리고 대체로 내가 비천하고 못난 처지에 있다는 것을 깊이 곱씹었다."]], [["# Chapter IX.", "# Chapter IX."]], [["When I reached home, my sister was very curious to know all about Miss Havisham’s, and asked a number of questions.", "집에 돌아오자 누나는 미스 해비셤 댁에 관한 모든 것을 몹시 궁금해하며 수많은 질문을 퍼부었다."], ["And I soon found myself getting heavily bumped from behind in the nape of the neck and the small of the back, and having my face ignominiously shoved against the kitchen wall, because I did not answer those questions at sufficient length.", "그리고 나는 머지않아 뒤에서 목덜미와 등허리를 호되게 쥐어박히고, 그 질문들에 충분히 길게 대답하지 않았다는 이유로 얼굴이 부엌 벽에 굴욕적으로 짓눌리는 신세가 되었다."]], [["If a dread of not being understood be hidden in the breasts of other young people to anything like the extent to which it used to be hidden in mine,—which I consider probable, as I have no particular reason to suspect myself of having been a monstrosity,—it is the key to many reservations.", "남에게 이해받지 못하리라는 두려움이 다른 어린아이들의 가슴속에도, 내 가슴속에 숨어 있던 만큼이나 깊이 숨겨져 있다면 — 내가 별나게 괴상한 아이였다고 의심할 특별한 이유가 없으니 그럴 법하다고 나는 본다 — 그것은 많은 침묵의 열쇠가 된다."], ["I felt convinced that if I described Miss Havisham’s as my eyes had seen it, I should not be understood.", "내가 미스 해비셤 댁을 내 눈으로 본 그대로 묘사한다면 이해받지 못하리라는 확신이 들었다."], ["Not only that, but I felt convinced that Miss Havisham too would not be understood; and although she was perfectly incomprehensible to me, I entertained an impression that there would be something coarse and treacherous in my dragging her as she really was (to say nothing of Miss Estella) before the contemplation of Mrs. Joe.", "그뿐 아니라 미스 해비셤 역시 이해받지 못하리라는 확신이 들었다. 그리고 그분은 나로서는 도무지 알 수 없는 사람이었지만, 그분의 진짜 모습을(미스 에스텔라는 말할 것도 없고) 끌어내어 조 부인의 눈앞에 펼쳐 보이는 데에는 뭔가 천박하고 배신적인 구석이 있으리라는 인상을 품고 있었다."], ["Consequently, I said as little as I could, and had my face shoved against the kitchen wall.", "그래서 나는 되도록 말을 아꼈고, 얼굴이 부엌 벽에 짓눌렸다."]], [["The worst of it was that that bullying old Pumblechook, preyed upon by a devouring curiosity to be informed of all I had seen and heard, came gaping over in his chaise-cart at tea-time, to have the details divulged to him.", "가장 곤란했던 것은, 내가 보고 들은 모든 것을 알아내고 싶다는 게걸스러운 호기심에 사로잡힌 저 윽박지르기 좋아하는 늙은 펌블추크가, 그 내막을 낱낱이 캐내려고 차 마실 시간에 이륜마차를 몰고 입을 떡 벌린 채 건너왔다는 점이었다."], ["And the mere sight of the torment, with his fishy eyes and mouth open, his sandy hair inquisitively on end, and his waistcoat heaving with windy arithmetic, made me vicious in my reticence.", "그리고 흐리멍덩한 물고기 같은 눈과 떡 벌린 입, 캐묻듯 곤두선 모래빛 머리칼, 그리고 허풍스러운 셈을 하느라 들썩이는 조끼를 한 그 골칫덩이의 모습만 보아도, 나는 입을 다무는 데 더욱 심술이 났다."]], [["“Well, boy,” Uncle Pumblechook began, as soon as he was seated in the chair of honour by the fire.", "\"자, 얘야.\" 펌블추크 아저씨가 난롯가의 상석에 자리를 잡자마자 입을 열었다."], ["“How did you get on up town?”", "\"읍내에서는 어떻게 지냈느냐?\""]], [["I answered, “Pretty well, sir,” and my sister shook her fist at me.", "나는 \"그럭저럭이요, 아저씨\"라고 대답했고, 누나는 내게 주먹을 흔들어 보였다."]], [["“Pretty well?” Mr.", "\"그럭저럭이라고?\""], ["Pumblechook repeated.", "펌블추크 씨가 되받았다."], ["“Pretty well is no answer.", "\"그럭저럭은 대답이 아니다."], ["Tell us what you mean by pretty well, boy?”", "그럭저럭이라는 게 무슨 뜻인지 말해 봐라, 얘야.\""]], [["Whitewash on the forehead hardens the brain into a state of obstinacy perhaps.", "이마에 묻은 회반죽이 뇌를 굳혀 고집스러운 상태로 만드는지도 모른다."], ["Anyhow, with whitewash from the wall on my forehead, my obstinacy was adamantine.", "어쨌든 벽에서 묻어난 회반죽을 이마에 묻힌 채, 내 고집은 금강석처럼 단단했다."], ["I reflected for some time, and then answered as if I had discovered a new idea, “I mean pretty well.”", "나는 한동안 곰곰이 생각하더니, 마치 새로운 발상이라도 발견한 듯 대답했다. \"그럭저럭이라는 뜻이에요.\""]], [["My sister with an exclamation of impatience was going to fly at me,—I had no shadow of defence, for Joe was busy in the forge,—when Mr. Pumblechook interposed with “No!", "누나는 못 참겠다는 탄성을 지르며 내게 달려들려 했고 — 조는 대장간에서 일하느라 바빴으니, 나로선 방어할 길이 조금도 없었다 — 바로 그때 펌블추크 씨가 끼어들며 말했다."], ["Don’t lose your temper.", "\"안 됩니다! 화내지 마세요."], ["Leave this lad to me, ma’am; leave this lad to me.” Mr.", "이 녀석은 제게 맡기시지요, 부인. 이 녀석은 제게 맡기세요.\""], ["Pumblechook then turned me towards him, as if he were going to cut my hair, and said,—", "그러더니 펌블추크 씨는 내 머리카락이라도 자를 듯이 나를 자기 쪽으로 돌려세우고는 말했다."]], [["“First (to get our thoughts in order): Forty-three pence?”", "\"먼저(우리 생각을 정리하기 위해서다) 사십삼 펜스는?\""]], [["I calculated the consequences of replying “Four Hundred Pound,” and finding them against me, went as near the answer as I could—which was somewhere about eightpence off. Mr.", "나는 \"사백 파운드요\"라고 대답할 경우의 결과를 가늠해 보았으나, 그것이 내게 불리하다는 것을 알고는 최대한 정답에 가깝게 — 여덟 펜스쯤 모자란 답을 — 댔다."], ["Pumblechook then put me through my pence-table from “twelve pence make one shilling,” up to “forty pence make three and fourpence,” and then triumphantly demanded, as if he had done for me, “Now!", "그러자 펌블추크 씨는 \"십이 펜스가 일 실링\"부터 \"사십 펜스가 삼 실링 사 펜스\"까지 펜스 구구단을 줄줄이 외게 하더니, 마치 나를 끝장내 버렸다는 듯 의기양양하게 다그쳤다. \"자!"], ["How much is forty-three pence?”", "사십삼 펜스는 얼마냐?\""], ["To which I replied, after a long interval of reflection, “I don’t know.”", "이에 나는 한참을 생각한 끝에 대답했다. \"모르겠어요.\""], ["And I was so aggravated that I almost doubt if I did know.", "어찌나 약이 올랐던지, 내가 정말 알고 있었는지조차 의심스러울 지경이었다."]], [["Mr. Pumblechook worked his head like a screw to screw it out of me, and said, “Is forty-three pence seven and sixpence three fardens, for instance?”", "펌블추크 씨는 나사를 비틀어 빼내듯 머리를 비틀어 가며 물었다. \"예를 들어, 사십삼 펜스가 칠 실링 육 펜스 삼 파딩이냐?\""]], [["“Yes!”", "\"네!\""], ["said I. And although my sister instantly boxed my ears, it was highly gratifying to me to see that the answer spoilt his joke, and brought him to a dead stop.", "내가 말했다. 그러자 누나가 당장 내 귀싸대기를 올려붙였지만, 그 대답이 그의 농담을 망쳐 그를 꼼짝 못하게 멈춰 세운 것을 보니 무척 흐뭇했다."]], [["“Boy! What like is Miss Havisham?” Mr.", "\"얘야! 미스 해비셤은 어떻게 생겼더냐?\""], ["Pumblechook began again when he had recovered; folding his arms tight on his chest and applying the screw.", "정신을 차린 펌블추크 씨가 두 팔을 가슴에 단단히 포개고 다시 나사를 죄며 입을 열었다."]], [["“Very tall and dark,”", "\"아주 키가 크고 검은 분이에요.\""], ["I told him.", "나는 그에게 말했다."]], [["“Is she, uncle?”", "\"그래요, 아저씨?\""], ["asked my sister.", "누나가 물었다."]], [["Mr.", "펌블추크 씨는 눈을 찡긋하며 그렇다고 끄덕였다."], ["Pumblechook winked assent; from which I at once inferred that he had never seen Miss Havisham, for she was nothing of the kind.", "그것으로 나는 그가 미스 해비셤을 한 번도 본 적이 없음을 곧장 알아차렸다. 그분은 전혀 그런 모습이 아니었기 때문이다."]], [["“Good!”", "\"좋아!\""], ["said Mr. Pumblechook conceitedly.", "펌블추크 씨가 우쭐거리며 말했다."], ["(“This is the way to have him!", "(\"이 녀석은 이렇게 다루는 겁니다!"], ["We are beginning to hold our own, I think, Mum?”)", "이제 우리가 우위를 잡아 가는 것 같지요, 부인?\")"]], [["“I am sure, uncle,”", "\"정말이지, 아저씨.\""], ["returned Mrs.", "조 부인이 받았다."], ["Joe, “I wish you had him always; you know so well how to deal with him.”", "\"이 애를 늘 맡아 주셨으면 좋겠어요. 다루는 법을 어쩜 그리 잘 아시는지요.\""]], [["“Now, boy!", "\"자, 얘야!"], ["What was she a-doing of, when you went in today?”", "오늘 들어갔을 때 그분은 뭘 하고 계셨느냐?\""], ["asked Mr. Pumblechook.", "펌블추크 씨가 물었다."]], [["“She was sitting,”", "\"앉아 계셨어요.\""], ["I answered, “in a black velvet coach.”", "나는 대답했다. \"검은 벨벳 마차 안에요.\""]], [["Mr. Pumblechook and Mrs.", "펌블추크 씨와 조 부인은 — 당연하게도 — 서로를 빤히 쳐다보더니, 둘 다 되뇌었다."], ["Joe stared at one another—as they well might—and both repeated, “In a black velvet coach?”", "\"검은 벨벳 마차 안에?\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"네.\""], ["said I. “And Miss Estella—that’s her niece, I think—handed her in cake and wine at the coach-window, on a gold plate.", "내가 말했다. \"그리고 미스 에스텔라가 — 그분 조카인 것 같아요 — 마차 창문으로 금쟁반에 담은 케이크와 포도주를 건네 드렸어요."], ["And we all had cake and wine on gold plates.", "그리고 우리 모두 금쟁반에 케이크와 포도주를 받아 먹었어요."], ["And I got up behind the coach to eat mine, because she told me to.”", "저는 마차 뒤에 올라가서 제 몫을 먹었는데, 그분이 그러라고 하셔서요.\""]], [["“Was anybody else there?”", "\"다른 사람은 또 없었느냐?\""], ["asked Mr. Pumblechook.", "펌블추크 씨가 물었다."]], [["“Four dogs,”", "\"개 네 마리요.\""], ["said I.", "내가 말했다."]], [["“Large or small?”", "\"큰 개냐, 작은 개냐?\""]], [["“Immense,”", "\"엄청나게 컸어요.\""], ["said I.", "내가 말했다."], ["“And they fought for veal-cutlets out of a silver basket.”", "\"그리고 그 개들이 은바구니에서 송아지 고기 커틀릿을 두고 서로 다퉜어요.\""]], [["Mr. Pumblechook and Mrs. Joe stared at one another again, in utter amazement.", "펌블추크 씨와 조 부인은 다시금 서로를 멍하니 쳐다보았다."], ["I was perfectly frantic,—a reckless witness under the torture,—and would have told them anything.", "완전히 얼이 빠진 채로. 나는 완전히 제정신이 아니었다 — 고문 아래서 무모하게 굴던 증인처럼 — 무슨 말이든 둘러댔을 것이다."]], [["“Where was this coach, in the name of gracious?”", "\"세상에, 그 마차가 대체 어디 있었다는 거냐?\""], ["asked my sister.", "누나가 물었다."]], [["“In Miss Havisham’s room.”", "\"미스 해비셤의 방에요.\""], ["They stared again. “But there weren’t any horses to it.”", "그들은 다시 빤히 쳐다보았다. \"그런데 말은 안 매여 있었어요.\""], ["I added this saving clause, in the moment of rejecting four richly caparisoned coursers which I had had wild thoughts of harnessing.", "나는 마구를 채울까 하고 미친 생각을 했던 화려하게 장식한 준마 네 마리를 막판에 물리치며, 이 구원의 단서를 덧붙였다."]], [["“Can this be possible, uncle?”", "\"이게 말이 됩니까, 아저씨?\""], ["asked Mrs. Joe.", "조 부인이 물었다."], ["“What can the boy mean?”", "\"이 애가 대체 무슨 소릴 하는 거지요?\""]], [["“I’ll tell you, Mum,”", "\"제가 말씀드리지요, 부인.\""], ["said Mr. Pumblechook.", "펌블추크 씨가 말했다."], ["“My opinion is, it’s a sedan-chair.", "\"제 생각엔 그건 가마입니다."], ["She’s flighty, you know,—very flighty,—quite flighty enough to pass her days in a sedan-chair.”", "아시다시피 그분은 변덕스러우니까요 — 아주 변덕스럽지요 — 날을 가마 속에서 보낼 만큼이나 변덕스러운 분이고말고요.\""]], [["“Did you ever see her in it, uncle?”", "\"그분이 그 안에 있는 걸 보신 적 있으세요, 아저씨?\""], ["asked Mrs. Joe.", "조 부인이 물었다."]], [["“How could I,”", "\"내가 어떻게 보았겠습니까.\""], ["he returned, forced to the admission, “when I never see her in my life?", "그가 마지못해 인정하며 대꾸했다. \"평생 그분을 본 적이 없는데 말입니다."], ["Never clapped eyes upon her!”", "단 한 번도 눈으로 본 적이 없어요!\""]], [["“Goodness, uncle!", "\"어머나, 아저씨!"], ["And yet you have spoken to her?”", "그런데도 그분과 말씀은 나누셨다고요?\""]], [["“Why, don’t you know,”", "\"아니, 모르십니까.\""], ["said Mr. Pumblechook, testily, “that when I have been there, I have been took up to the outside of her door, and the door has stood ajar, and she has spoke to me that way.", "펌블추크 씨가 짜증스레 말했다. \"내가 거기 갔을 때, 그분 방문 바깥까지 안내받아 갔고, 문이 빠끔히 열려 있었으며, 그분이 그렇게 문 너머로 내게 말을 건네셨다는 걸 말입니다."], ["Don’t say you don’t know that, Mum.", "그것까지 모른다고는 하지 마세요, 부인."], ["Howsever, the boy went there to play.", "어쨌거나, 이 애는 거기 놀러 갔지요."], ["What did you play at, boy?”", "무슨 놀이를 했느냐, 얘야?\""]], [["“We played with flags,”", "\"우리는 깃발 놀이를 했어요.\""], ["I said. (I beg to observe that I think of myself with amazement, when I recall the lies I told on this occasion.)", "나는 말했다. (이 자리에서 내가 늘어놓은 거짓말들을 떠올리면, 나 자신을 두고 경악하게 된다는 점을 밝혀 두고 싶다.)"]], [["“Flags!”", "\"깃발!\""], ["echoed my sister.", "누나가 따라 말했다."]], [["“Yes,”", "\"네.\""], ["said I. “Estella waved a blue flag, and I waved a red one, and Miss Havisham waved one sprinkled all over with little gold stars, out at the coach-window.", "내가 말했다. \"에스텔라는 파란 깃발을 흔들고, 저는 빨간 깃발을 흔들고, 미스 해비셤은 작은 금별이 온통 흩뿌려진 깃발을 마차 창문 밖으로 흔드셨어요."], ["And then we all waved our swords and hurrahed.”", "그러고 나서 우리 모두 칼을 휘두르며 만세를 불렀어요.\""]], [["“Swords!” repeated my sister.", "\"칼이라니!\" 누나가 되뇌었다."], ["“Where did you get swords from?”", "\"칼은 또 어디서 났단 말이냐?\""]], [["“Out of a cupboard,” said I.", "\"찬장에서요.\" 내가 말했다."], ["“And I saw pistols in it,—and jam,—and pills.", "\"그리고 그 안에서 권총도 봤어요 — 잼도 — 그리고 알약도요."], ["And there was no daylight in the room, but it was all lighted up with candles.”", "그리고 그 방엔 햇빛이 하나도 안 들었는데, 온통 촛불로 환하게 밝혀져 있었어요.\""]], [["“That’s true, Mum,” said Mr.", "\"그건 사실입니다, 부인.\""], ["Pumblechook, with a grave nod.", "펌블추크 씨가 엄숙하게 고개를 끄덕이며 말했다."], ["“That’s the state of the case, for that much I’ve seen myself.”", "\"그게 실정이지요. 그 정도는 나도 직접 봤으니까요.\""], ["And then they both stared at me, and I, with an obtrusive show of artlessness on my countenance, stared at them, and plaited the right leg of my trousers with my right hand.", "그러더니 둘 다 나를 빤히 쳐다보았고, 나는 천연덕스러움을 짐짓 도드라지게 얼굴에 드러낸 채 그들을 빤히 마주 보며, 오른손으로 바지 오른쪽 가랑이를 주름잡아 만지작거렸다."]], [["If they had asked me any more questions, I should undoubtedly have betrayed myself, for I was even then on the point of mentioning that there was a balloon in the yard, and should have hazarded the statement but for my invention being divided between that phenomenon and a bear in the brewery.", "그들이 내게 질문을 조금만 더 했다면, 나는 틀림없이 스스로 들통이 났을 것이다. 그 순간에도 마당에 기구(氣球)가 있었다는 말까지 꺼내려던 참이었으니까. 다만 내 지어내는 솜씨가 그 진귀한 물건과 양조장에 있던 곰 사이에서 갈라져 있던 덕에 그 말을 무릅쓰지 않았을 뿐이다."], ["They were so much occupied, however, in discussing the marvels I had already presented for their consideration, that I escaped.", "그러나 그들은 내가 이미 그들의 검토거리로 내놓은 그 경이로운 이야기들을 논하느라 너무 몰두해 있던 터라, 나는 무사히 빠져나갔다."], ["The subject still held them when Joe came in from his work to have a cup of tea.", "조가 일을 마치고 차 한 잔 마시러 들어왔을 때에도 그 화제는 여전히 두 사람을 붙들고 있었다."], ["To whom my sister, more for the relief of her own mind than for the gratification of his, related my pretended experiences.", "누나는 그의 흥미를 채워 주기보다는 제 마음의 부담을 덜려는 셈으로, 내가 꾸며 낸 경험담을 그에게 들려주었다."]], [["Now, when I saw Joe open his blue eyes and roll them all round the kitchen in helpless amazement, I was overtaken by penitence; but only as regarded him,—not in the least as regarded the other two.", "자, 조가 그 푸른 눈을 크게 뜨고 어쩔 줄 모르는 경악에 차서 부엌을 빙 둘러보는 것을 보았을 때, 나는 회한에 사로잡혔다. 그러나 그것은 오직 그에 대해서일 뿐, 다른 두 사람에 대해서는 조금도 그렇지 않았다."], ["Towards Joe, and Joe only, I considered myself a young monster, while they sat debating what results would come to me from Miss Havisham’s acquaintance and favour.", "조에 대해서, 오직 조에 대해서만, 나는 스스로를 어린 괴물로 여겼다. 그러는 사이 그들은 미스 해비셤과 가까워지고 그분의 총애를 얻은 일이 내게 어떤 결과를 가져올지를 두고 앉아서 따지고 있었다."], ["They had no doubt that Miss Havisham would “do something”", "그들은 미스 해비셤이 나를 위해 \"무언가를 해 줄\" 것임을 의심치 않았다."], ["for me; their doubts related to the form that something would take.", "그들의 의문은 그 무언가가 어떤 형태를 띨 것인가에 관한 것이었다."], ["My sister stood out for “property.” Mr.", "누나는 \"재산\"이라는 쪽을 고집했다."], ["Pumblechook was in favour of a handsome premium for binding me apprentice to some genteel trade,—say, the corn and seed trade, for instance.", "펌블추크 씨는 나를 어느 점잖은 직종 — 이를테면 곡물 종자 장사 같은 직종에 도제로 들여보내는 데 두둑한 수업료를 대 주는 쪽을 지지했다."], ["Joe fell into the deepest disgrace with both, for offering the bright suggestion that I might only be presented with one of the dogs who had fought for the veal-cutlets.", "조는 어쩌면 송아지 커틀릿을 두고 싸우던 그 개들 가운데 한 마리를 내가 받게 될지도 모른다는 명석한 제안을 내놓았다가, 두 사람 모두에게서 가장 큰 망신을 샀다."], ["“If a fool’s head can’t express better opinions than that,”", "\"바보 머리에서 그것보다 나은 의견이 안 나온다면 말이야.\""], ["said my sister, “and you have got any work to do, you had better go and do it.”", "누나가 말했다. \"할 일이 있거든, 가서 그 일이나 하는 게 낫겠어.\""], ["So he went.", "그래서 그는 갔다."]], [["After Mr. Pumblechook had driven off, and when my sister was washing up, I stole into the forge to Joe, and remained by him until he had done for the night.", "펌블추크 씨가 마차를 몰고 떠난 뒤, 누나가 설거지를 하고 있을 때, 나는 살그머니 대장간으로 조에게 가서, 그가 그날 밤일을 마칠 때까지 곁에 머물렀다."], ["Then I said, “Before the fire goes out, Joe, I should like to tell you something.”", "그러고는 말했다. \"불이 꺼지기 전에요, 조, 아저씨한테 하고 싶은 말이 있어요.\""]], [["“Should you, Pip?”", "\"그래, 핍?\""], ["said Joe, drawing his shoeing-stool near the forge.", "조가 편자 박는 걸상을 대장간 가까이 끌어당기며 말했다."], ["“Then tell us.", "\"그럼 말해 봐."], ["What is it, Pip?”", "뭔데, 핍?\""]], [["“Joe,”", "\"조.\""], ["said I, taking hold of his rolled-up shirt sleeve, and twisting it between my finger and thumb, “you remember all that about Miss Havisham’s?”", "나는 그의 걷어올린 셔츠 소매를 붙잡고 엄지와 검지 사이로 비비 꼬며 말했다. \"미스 해비셤 댁에 관한 그 얘기들 다 기억하시죠?\""]], [["“Remember?”", "\"기억하느냐고?\""], ["said Joe.", "조가 말했다."], ["“I believe you!", "\"암 기억하고말고!"], ["Wonderful!”", "굉장했지!\""]], [["“It’s a terrible thing, Joe; it ain’t true.”", "\"그게 끔찍한 일이에요, 조. 사실이 아니에요.\""]], [["“What are you telling of, Pip?”", "\"무슨 소릴 하는 거냐, 핍?\""], ["cried Joe, falling back in the greatest amazement. “You don’t mean to say it’s—”", "조가 더없는 경악에 차서 뒤로 물러서며 외쳤다. \"설마 그게—\""]], [["“Yes I do; it’s lies, Joe.”", "\"네, 그래요. 거짓말이에요, 조.\""]], [["“But not all of it?", "\"그래도 전부는 아니겠지?"], ["Why sure you don’t mean to say, Pip, that there was no black welwet co—eh?” For, I stood shaking my head.", "설마, 핍, 검은 벨벳 마—차가 없었다는 건 아니겠지, 응?\" 내가 고개를 가로젓고 서 있었기 때문이다."], ["“But at least there was dogs, Pip?", "\"그래도 적어도 개들은 있었지, 핍?"], ["Come, Pip,”", "자, 핍.\""], ["said Joe, persuasively, “if there warn’t no weal-cutlets, at least there was dogs?”", "조가 달래듯 말했다. \"송아지 커틀릿은 없었다 쳐도, 적어도 개들은 있었지?\" \"없었어요, 조.\""]], [["“No, Joe.”", "\"개 한 마리는?\""], ["“A dog?”", "조가 말했다."], ["said Joe. “A puppy? Come?”", "\"강아지 한 마리는? 어때?\""]], [["“No, Joe, there was nothing at all of the kind.”", "\"없어요, 조. 그런 건 하나도 없었어요.\""]], [["As I fixed my eyes hopelessly on Joe, Joe contemplated me in dismay.", "내가 절망적으로 조에게 눈길을 박자, 조는 당혹스러운 얼굴로 나를 바라보았다."], ["“Pip, old chap!", "\"핍, 이 친구야!"], ["This won’t do, old fellow! I say!", "이러면 못쓴다, 이 사람아! 이거 참!"], ["Where do you expect to go to?”", "너 대체 어디로 갈 작정이냐?\""]], [["“It’s terrible, Joe; ain’t it?”", "\"끔찍하지요, 조? 그렇죠?\""]], [["“Terrible?”", "\"끔찍하다고?\""], ["cried Joe.", "조가 외쳤다."], ["“Awful! What possessed you?”", "\"기막힌 노릇이지! 대체 뭐에 씌었던 거냐?\""]], [["“I don’t know what possessed me, Joe,”", "\"저도 뭐에 씌었는지 모르겠어요, 조.\""], ["I replied, letting his shirt sleeve go, and sitting down in the ashes at his feet, hanging my head; “but I wish you hadn’t taught me to call Knaves at cards Jacks; and I wish my boots weren’t so thick nor my hands so coarse.”", "나는 그의 셔츠 소매를 놓고, 그의 발치 잿더미 위에 주저앉아 고개를 떨군 채 대답했다. \"하지만 아저씨가 카드의 크네이브를 잭이라고 부르는 법을 안 가르쳐 주셨더라면 좋았을걸, 그리고 제 장화가 그렇게 투박하지 않고 제 손이 그렇게 거칠지 않았더라면 좋았을걸 싶어요.\""]], [["And then I told Joe that I felt very miserable, and that I hadn’t been able to explain myself to Mrs. Joe and Pumblechook, who were so rude to me, and that there had been a beautiful young lady at Miss Havisham’s who was dreadfully proud, and that she had said I was common, and that I knew I was common, and that I wished I was not common, and that the lies had come of it somehow, though I didn’t know how.", "그러고 나서 나는 조에게, 내가 몹시 비참한 심정이라고, 그리고 그토록 무례하게 굴던 조 부인과 펌블추크에게 나 자신을 제대로 설명할 수가 없었다고, 그리고 미스 해비셤 댁에 무섭도록 도도한 아름다운 아가씨가 있었다고, 그리고 그 아가씨가 나를 천하다고 했고, 나도 내가 천하다는 걸 알고 있으며, 천하지 않았으면 좋겠다고, 그리고 어찌 된 영문인지는 모르나 그 거짓말들이 거기서 비롯되었다고 말했다."]], [["This was a case of metaphysics, at least as difficult for Joe to deal with as for me.", "이것은 형이상학의 문제로, 적어도 나에게 그러했던 만큼이나 조에게도 다루기 어려운 일이었다."], ["But Joe took the case altogether out of the region of metaphysics, and by that means vanquished it.", "그러나 조는 그 문제를 형이상학의 영역에서 통째로 끄집어냈고, 그렇게 함으로써 그것을 정복했다."]], [["“There’s one thing you may be sure of, Pip,”", "\"한 가지는 너도 확실히 알아 둘 수 있다, 핍.\""], ["said Joe, after some rumination, “namely, that lies is lies.", "조가 한참 곱씹은 끝에 말했다. \"그러니까, 거짓말은 거짓말이라는 거야."], ["Howsever they come, they didn’t ought to come, and they come from the father of lies, and work round to the same.", "어떻게 나오든, 나와서는 안 되는 거고, 거짓의 아비한테서 나와서 다시 그쪽으로 돌아가는 법이지."], ["Don’t you tell no more of ’em, Pip.", "다시는 그런 걸 늘어놓지 마라, 핍."], ["That ain’t the way to get out of being common, old chap.", "그건 천한 신세를 벗어나는 길이 아니야, 이 친구야."], ["And as to being common, I don’t make it out at all clear.", "그리고 천하다는 것 말인데, 난 도무지 그게 무슨 소린지 잘 모르겠다."], ["You are oncommon in some things.", "너는 어떤 면에서는 천하지 않아."], ["You’re oncommon small. Likewise you’re a oncommon scholar.”", "너는 보기 드물게 몸집이 작지. 게다가 너는 보기 드문 학자고.\""]], [["“No, I am ignorant and backward, Joe.”", "\"아니에요, 저는 무식하고 뒤처졌어요, 조.\""]], [["“Why, see what a letter you wrote last night!", "\"아니, 어젯밤에 네가 쓴 편지를 좀 봐라!"], ["Wrote in print even!", "인쇄 글씨로까지 썼잖느냐!"], ["I’ve seen letters—Ah!", "나는 편지를 본 적이 있는데 — 아!"], ["and from gentlefolks!—that I’ll swear weren’t wrote in print,”", "그것도 양반님네한테서 온 편지 말이다! — 그게 인쇄 글씨로 쓰인 게 아니었다고 맹세할 수 있어.\""], ["said Joe.", "조가 말했다."]], [["“I have learnt next to nothing, Joe. You think much of me.", "\"저는 배운 게 거의 없어요, 조. 아저씨가 저를 대단하게 봐 주실 뿐이에요."], ["It’s only that.”", "그뿐이라고요.\""]], [["“Well, Pip,”", "\"글쎄, 핍.\""], ["said Joe, “be it so or be it son’t, you must be a common scholar afore you can be a oncommon one, I should hope!", "조가 말했다. \"그렇든 안 그렇든, 보기 드문 학자가 되려면 먼저 평범한 학자부터 되어야 하는 게 아니겠냐, 나는 그렇게 본다!"], ["The king upon his throne, with his crown upon his ed, can’t sit and write his acts of Parliament in print, without having begun, when he were a unpromoted Prince, with the alphabet.—Ah!”", "왕좌에 앉아 머리에 왕관을 쓴 임금님도, 출세 못 한 왕자였던 시절에 알파벳부터 시작하지 않고서는, 가만히 앉아 인쇄 글씨로 의회 법령을 써 내려갈 수 없는 법이야. — 아!\""], ["added Joe, with a shake of the head that was full of meaning, “and begun at A too, and worked his way to Z.", "조가 의미심장하게 고개를 한 번 가로저으며 덧붙였다. \"그것도 A부터 시작해서, 차근차근 Z까지 나아간 거지."], ["And I know what that is to do, though I can’t say I’ve exactly done it.”", "그게 어떤 일인지 나도 알아, 비록 내가 정확히 그걸 해냈다고는 말 못 하지만 말이다.\""]], [["There was some hope in this piece of wisdom, and it rather encouraged me.", "이 지혜의 한 토막에는 약간의 희망이 깃들어 있었고, 그것은 나를 제법 북돋아 주었다."]], [["“Whether common ones as to callings and earnings,”", "\"직업이나 벌이로 따져 천한 사람들이 말이다.\""], ["pursued Joe, reflectively, “mightn’t be the better of continuing for to keep company with common ones, instead of going out to play with oncommon ones,—which reminds me to hope that there were a flag, perhaps?”", "조가 사색에 잠겨 말을 이었다. \"천한 사람들끼리 어울려 지내는 편이, 보기 드문 사람들과 놀러 나가는 것보다 나을지도 모른다는 거지 — 그러고 보니 혹시 깃발은 정말 있었으려나 싶기도 한데?\""]], [["“No, Joe.”", "\"없었어요, 조.\""]], [["“(I’m sorry there weren’t a flag, Pip). Whether that might be or mightn’t be, is a thing as can’t be looked into now, without putting your sister on the Rampage; and that’s a thing not to be thought of as being done intentional.", "\"(깃발이 없었다니 안됐다, 핍.) 그게 그렇든 안 그렇든, 그건 지금 따져 볼 수 없는 일이야. 네 누나를 불같이 길길이 날뛰게 하지 않고서는 말이지."], ["Lookee here, Pip, at what is said to you by a true friend. Which this to you the true friend say.", "그건 일부러 저질러서는 안 될 일이고. 자 봐라, 핍, 참된 친구가 너한테 하는 말을."], ["If you can’t get to be oncommon through going straight, you’ll never get to do it through going crooked.", "이건 참된 친구가 너한테 하는 말이야. 곧게 가서도 보기 드문 사람이 못 된다면, 굽은 길로 가서는 결코 그렇게 되지 못해."], ["So don’t tell no more on ’em, Pip, and live well and die happy.”", "그러니 다시는 그런 걸 늘어놓지 마라, 핍, 그리고 잘 살고 행복하게 죽으렴.\""]], [["“You are not angry with me, Joe?”", "\"저한테 화나신 거 아니지요, 조?\""]], [["“No, old chap. But bearing in mind that them were which I meantersay of a stunning and outdacious sort,—alluding to them which bordered on weal-cutlets and dog-fighting,—a sincere well-wisher would adwise, Pip, their being dropped into your meditations, when you go upstairs to bed.", "\"아니다, 이 친구야. 다만 내가 말하려던 그것들이 깜짝 놀랄 만큼 엉뚱한 종류였다는 걸 새겨 둔다면 말이다 — 송아지 커틀릿이니 개싸움이니 하는 데 걸쳐 있던 그것들 말이야 — 진심으로 너를 위하는 사람이라면, 핍, 그것들을 곰곰이 따져 보거라 충고하겠다, 네가 이층으로 잠자리에 올라갈 때 말이야."], ["That’s all, old chap, and don’t never do it no more.”", "그게 다다, 이 친구야, 그리고 다시는 절대 그러지 마라.\""]], [["When I got up to my little room and said my prayers, I did not forget Joe’s recommendation, and yet my young mind was in that disturbed and unthankful state, that I thought long after I laid me down, how common Estella would consider Joe, a mere blacksmith; how thick his boots, and how coarse his hands.", "내 작은 방으로 올라가 기도를 드릴 때, 나는 조의 충고를 잊지 않았다. 그러나 내 어린 마음은 그토록 어지럽고 감사할 줄 모르는 상태였던지라, 자리에 누운 뒤로도 한참을 이런 생각에 잠겼다. 에스텔라가 한낱 대장장이인 조를 얼마나 천하게 여길까, 그의 장화는 얼마나 투박하며 그의 손은 얼마나 거칠까."], ["I thought how Joe and my sister were then sitting in the kitchen, and how I had come up to bed from the kitchen, and how Miss Havisham and Estella never sat in a kitchen, but were far above the level of such common doings.", "나는 생각했다. 조와 누나가 지금 부엌에 앉아 있고, 나는 그 부엌에서 잠자리로 올라왔는데, 미스 해비셤과 에스텔라는 결코 부엌 따위에 앉는 법 없이 그런 천한 일거리들보다 한참 높은 곳에 있구나 하고."], ["I fell asleep recalling what I “used to do”", "나는 미스 해비셤 댁에 있을 때 \"늘 하곤 했던\""], ["when I was at Miss Havisham’s; as though I had been there weeks or months, instead of hours; and as though it were quite an old subject of remembrance, instead of one that had arisen only that day.", "일들을 떠올리며 잠이 들었다. 마치 몇 시간이 아니라 몇 주, 몇 달을 거기 머물렀던 것처럼. 그리고 마치 그것이 바로 그날에야 비로소 생겨난 일이 아니라, 아주 오래된 추억거리이기라도 한 것처럼."]], [["That was a memorable day to me, for it made great changes in me.", "그날은 내게 잊지 못할 날이었다. 그날이 내 안에 커다란 변화를 일으켰기 때문이다."], ["But it is the same with any life.", "그러나 어느 인생이든 마찬가지다."], ["Imagine one selected day struck out of it, and think how different its course would have been.", "그 가운데서 어느 하루를 골라 도려냈다고 상상해 보라, 그리고 그 행로가 얼마나 달라졌을지를 생각해 보라."], ["Pause you who read this, and think for a moment of the long chain of iron or gold, of thorns or flowers, that would never have bound you, but for the formation of the first link on one memorable day.", "이 글을 읽는 그대여, 잠시 멈추어, 어느 잊지 못할 하루에 첫 고리 하나가 만들어지지 않았더라면 결코 그대를 옭아매지 못했을, 쇠로 된 혹은 금으로 된, 가시로 된 혹은 꽃으로 된 그 긴 사슬을 한순간 떠올려 보라."]], [["# Chapter X.", "# Chapter X."]], [["The felicitous idea occurred to me a morning or two later when I woke, that the best step I could take towards making myself uncommon was to get out of Biddy everything she knew. In pursuance of this luminous conception I mentioned to Biddy when I went to Mr.", "하루나 이틀쯤 뒤 어느 날 아침 잠에서 깨었을 때, 나는 행복한 착상 하나를 떠올렸다. 내가 비범해지기 위해 밟을 수 있는 최선의 길은 비디가 아는 것을 모조리 캐내는 것이라는 생각이었다."], ["Wopsle’s great-aunt’s at night, that I had a particular reason for wishing to get on in life, and that I should feel very much obliged to her if she would impart all her learning to me.", "이 빛나는 구상을 좇아, 밤에 우프슬 씨의 대고모님 댁에 갔을 때 나는 비디에게, 내가 출세하고 싶은 특별한 이유가 있노라고, 그러니 비디가 자기 학식을 전부 내게 나누어 준다면 더없이 고맙겠노라고 일러두었다."], ["Biddy, who was the most obliging of girls, immediately said she would, and indeed began to carry out her promise within five minutes.", "비디는 더할 나위 없이 친절한 소녀였으므로, 곧장 그러겠노라 대답하고는, 정말로 오 분도 채 지나지 않아 그 약속을 실행하기 시작했다."]], [["The Educational scheme or Course established by Mr. Wopsle’s great-aunt may be resolved into the following synopsis.", "우프슬 씨의 대고모님이 세운 교육 제도 혹은 교과 과정은 다음과 같은 개요로 정리할 수 있다."], ["The pupils ate apples and put straws down one another’s backs, until Mr. Wopsle’s great-aunt collected her energies, and made an indiscriminate totter at them with a birch-rod.", "학생들은 우프슬 씨의 대고모님이 기운을 모아 자작나무 회초리를 들고 마구잡이로 비틀거리며 달려들기 전까지, 사과를 먹고 서로의 등에 지푸라기를 찔러 넣었다."], ["After receiving the charge with every mark of derision, the pupils formed in line and buzzingly passed a ragged book from hand to hand.", "그 공격을 온갖 비웃음의 기색으로 받아 낸 다음, 학생들은 한 줄로 늘어서서 웅성거리며 너덜너덜한 책 한 권을 손에서 손으로 넘겼다."], ["The book had an alphabet in it, some figures and tables, and a little spelling,—that is to say, it had had once.", "그 책에는 알파벳과 약간의 숫자와 표, 그리고 약간의 철자법이 들어 있었다 — 다시 말해, 한때는 들어 있었다."], ["As soon as this volume began to circulate, Mr. Wopsle’s great-aunt fell into a state of coma, arising either from sleep or a rheumatic paroxysm.", "이 책이 돌기 시작하기가 무섭게 우프슬 씨의 대고모님은 혼수 상태에 빠졌는데, 그것은 잠 때문이거나 류머티즘 발작 때문이었다."], ["The pupils then entered among themselves upon a competitive examination on the subject of Boots, with the view of ascertaining who could tread the hardest upon whose toes.", "그러면 학생들끼리 부츠를 주제로 한 경쟁 시험에 돌입하여, 누가 누구의 발가락을 가장 세게 밟을 수 있는지를 가려냈다."], ["This mental exercise lasted until Biddy made a rush at them and distributed three defaced Bibles (shaped as if they had been unskilfully cut off the chump end of something), more illegibly printed at the best than any curiosities of literature I have since met with, speckled all over with ironmould, and having various specimens of the insect world smashed between their leaves.", "이 정신 수양은 비디가 학생들에게 달려들어, 보기 흉하게 일그러진 성경책 세 권(무언가의 토막 난 끝을 서투르게 잘라 낸 듯한 모양이었다)을 나누어 줄 때까지 이어졌다. 그 성경책들은 잘 봐주어도 내가 그 후 마주친 그 어떤 문헌 진품보다도 알아보기 어렵게 인쇄되어 있었고, 온통 쇠녹 얼룩으로 점점이 뒤덮여 있었으며, 책장 사이에는 곤충 세계의 갖가지 표본이 짓눌려 있었다."], ["This part of the Course was usually lightened by several single combats between Biddy and refractory students.", "이 과정의 단계는 대개 비디와 고분고분하지 않은 학생들 사이의 일대일 격투 몇 차례로 활기를 띠었다."], ["When the fights were over, Biddy gave out the number of a page, and then we all read aloud what we could,—or what we couldn’t—in a frightful chorus; Biddy leading with a high, shrill, monotonous voice, and none of us having the least notion of, or reverence for, what we were reading about.", "싸움이 끝나면 비디가 쪽 번호를 알려 주었고, 그러면 우리 모두는 읽을 수 있는 것을 — 혹은 읽을 수 없는 것을 — 무시무시한 합창으로 소리 내어 읽었다. 비디가 높고 날카롭고 단조로운 목소리로 선창했으며, 우리 가운데 누구도 우리가 무엇을 읽고 있는지 조금도 알지 못했고, 그것을 향한 경외심 따위도 전혀 없었다."], ["When this horrible din had lasted a certain time, it mechanically awoke Mr.", "이 끔찍한 소음이 일정 시간 이어지면, 그것이 기계적으로 우프슬 씨의 대고모님을 깨웠다."], ["Wopsle’s great-aunt, who staggered at a boy fortuitously, and pulled his ears.", "대고모님은 우연히 가까이 있던 한 소년에게 비틀거리며 달려들어 그 아이의 귀를 잡아당겼다."], ["This was understood to terminate the Course for the evening, and we emerged into the air with shrieks of intellectual victory.", "이것은 그날 저녁 과정의 종료를 뜻하는 것으로 이해되었고, 우리는 지적 승리의 환호성을 지르며 바깥 공기 속으로 쏟아져 나왔다."], ["It is fair to remark that there was no prohibition against any pupil’s entertaining himself with a slate or even with the ink (when there was any), but that it was not easy to pursue that branch of study in the winter season, on account of the little general shop in which the classes were holden—and which was also Mr. Wopsle’s great-aunt’s sitting-room and bedchamber—being but faintly illuminated through the agency of one low-spirited dip-candle and no snuffers.", "공평하게 짚어 두자면, 어떤 학생이 석판으로, 혹은 잉크가 있을 때는 잉크로 스스로를 즐기는 것을 금하는 규정은 없었다. 다만 겨울철에는 그 학문의 갈래를 추구하기가 쉽지 않았는데, 수업이 열리던 그 자그마한 잡화점이 — 그곳은 동시에 우프슬 씨의 대고모님의 거실이자 침실이기도 했다 — 심지 자르는 가위도 없이 기운 없이 타들어가는 양초 한 자루의 힘을 빌려 어슴푸레하게만 밝혀져 있었기 때문이다."]], [["It appeared to me that it would take time to become uncommon, under these circumstances: nevertheless, I resolved to try it, and that very evening Biddy entered on our special agreement, by imparting some information from her little catalogue of Prices, under the head of moist sugar, and lending me, to copy at home, a large old English D which she had imitated from the heading of some newspaper, and which I supposed, until she told me what it was, to be a design for a buckle.", "이런 형편에서는 비범해지는 데 시간이 걸리리라는 생각이 들었다. 그럼에도 나는 한번 해 보기로 결심했고, 바로 그날 저녁 비디는 우리의 특별 협정에 착수하여, 황설탕 항목 아래 자기의 자그마한 물품 시세표에서 얼마간의 정보를 일러 주고, 어느 신문 제호에서 본떠 그린 커다란 고풍의 영어 대문자 D를 집에서 베껴 쓰라며 내게 빌려주었다. 그것이 무엇인지 비디가 일러 주기 전까지 나는 그것을 어느 버클의 도안인 줄로만 알았다."]], [["Of course there was a public-house in the village, and of course Joe liked sometimes to smoke his pipe there.", "물론 마을에는 술집이 하나 있었고, 물론 조는 이따금 그곳에서 담배 피우기를 좋아했다."], ["I had received strict orders from my sister to call for him at the Three Jolly Bargemen, that evening, on my way from school, and bring him home at my peril.", "그날 저녁 나는 학교에서 돌아오는 길에 쾌활한 세 뱃사공 술집에 들러 조를 찾아내, 무슨 일이 있어도 그를 집에 데려오라는 누나의 엄한 명령을 받은 터였다."], ["To the Three Jolly Bargemen, therefore, I directed my steps.", "그리하여 나는 쾌활한 세 뱃사공 술집으로 발걸음을 옮겼다."]], [["There was a bar at the Jolly Bargemen, with some alarmingly long chalk scores in it on the wall at the side of the door, which seemed to me to be never paid off.", "쾌활한 뱃사공 술집에는 바가 하나 있었는데, 문 옆 벽에는 분필로 적힌 외상 장부의 길고 섬뜩한 기록들이 있었고, 그것들은 내 눈에 결코 갚아지는 법이 없는 것처럼 보였다."], ["They had been there ever since I could remember, and had grown more than I had.", "그 기록들은 내가 기억하는 한 줄곧 그 자리에 있었고, 나보다도 더 많이 자라 있었다."], ["But there was a quantity of chalk about our country, and perhaps the people neglected no opportunity of turning it to account.", "그러나 우리 고장에는 분필이 넉넉히 났으니, 어쩌면 사람들이 그것을 써먹을 기회를 하나도 놓치지 않았는지도 모를 일이었다."]], [["It being Saturday night, I found the landlord looking rather grimly at these records; but as my business was with Joe and not with him, I merely wished him good evening, and passed into the common room at the end of the passage, where there was a bright large kitchen fire, and where Joe was smoking his pipe in company with Mr.", "토요일 밤이었으므로, 나는 주인이 그 기록들을 다소 음울한 표정으로 바라보고 있는 것을 보았다. 그러나 내 볼일은 그가 아니라 조에게 있었으므로, 나는 그저 안녕하시냐는 인사만 건네고 복도 끝의 공동 방으로 들어갔다. 그곳에는 환하고 큼직한 부엌 난롯불이 타고 있었고, 조가 우프슬 씨와 어느 낯선 사내와 함께 담배를 피우고 있었다."], ["Wopsle and a stranger. Joe greeted me as usual with “Halloa, Pip, old chap!”", "조는 여느 때처럼 \"여어, 핍, 우리 꼬마 친구!\""], ["and the moment he said that, the stranger turned his head and looked at me.", "하고 나를 맞이했는데, 그가 그렇게 말하는 순간 낯선 사내가 고개를 돌려 나를 바라보았다."]], [["He was a secret-looking man whom I had never seen before.", "처음 보는, 어딘가 비밀스러워 보이는 사내였다."], ["His head was all on one side, and one of his eyes was half shut up, as if he were taking aim at something with an invisible gun.", "머리는 온통 한쪽으로 기울어져 있었고, 한쪽 눈은 보이지 않는 총으로 무언가를 겨누기라도 하는 양 반쯤 감겨 있었다."], ["He had a pipe in his mouth, and he took it out, and, after slowly blowing all his smoke away and looking hard at me all the time, nodded.", "입에는 담뱃대를 물고 있었는데, 그것을 빼더니 천천히 연기를 다 내뿜으며 내내 나를 뚫어지게 쳐다본 끝에 고개를 끄덕였다."], ["So, I nodded, and then he nodded again, and made room on the settle beside him that I might sit down there.", "그래서 나도 고개를 끄덕였고, 그러자 그는 다시 한 번 끄덕이고는 내가 거기 앉을 수 있도록 자기 옆자리 긴 의자를 비워 주었다."]], [["But as I was used to sit beside Joe whenever I entered that place of resort, I said “No, thank you, sir,”", "그러나 나는 그 휴식처에 들를 때면 늘 조의 옆에 앉는 버릇이 있었으므로 \"아니에요, 고맙습니다, 아저씨\""], ["and fell into the space Joe made for me on the opposite settle.", "하고는, 맞은편 긴 의자에 조가 내게 마련해 준 자리에 끼어 앉았다."], ["The strange man, after glancing at Joe, and seeing that his attention was otherwise engaged, nodded to me again when I had taken my seat, and then rubbed his leg—in a very odd way, as it struck me.", "낯선 사내는 조를 흘끗 보고 그의 주의가 다른 데 쏠려 있는 것을 확인하더니, 내가 자리를 잡자 다시 한 번 내게 고개를 끄덕이고는 자기 다리를 문질렀다 — 내가 보기에 무척 묘한 방식이었다."]], [["“You was saying,”", "\"댁이 말씀하시기를,\""], ["said the strange man, turning to Joe, “that you was a blacksmith.”", "낯선 사내가 조에게로 몸을 돌리며 말했다. \"대장장이라고 하셨지요.\""]], [["“Yes. I said it, you know,”", "\"그렇소. 내가 그렇게 말했지요.\""], ["said Joe.", "조가 말했다."]], [["“What’ll you drink, Mr.—?", "\"뭘 드시겠소, 미스터…?"], ["You didn’t mention your name, by the bye.”", "그러고 보니 성함을 말씀 안 하셨군요.\""]], [["Joe mentioned it now, and the strange man called him by it.", "조가 이제 자기 이름을 댔고, 낯선 사내는 그를 그 이름으로 불렀다."], ["“What’ll you drink, Mr. Gargery?", "\"뭘 드시겠소, 가저리 씨?"], ["At my expense?", "내가 한턱내리다."], ["To top up with?”", "마무리로 한잔하시지요?\""]], [["“Well,”", "\"글쎄요,\""], ["said Joe, “to tell you the truth, I ain’t much in the habit of drinking at anybody’s expense but my own.”", "조가 말했다. \"솔직히 말하자면, 나는 내 돈 말고 남의 돈으로 술 마시는 버릇이 별로 없소이다.\""]], [["“Habit? No,”", "\"버릇이라?"], ["returned the stranger, “but once and away, and on a Saturday night too. Come!", "그렇겠지요.\" 낯선 사내가 대꾸했다. \"하지만 어쩌다 한 번쯤은, 게다가 토요일 밤이기도 한데. 자!"], ["Put a name to it, Mr. Gargery.”", "뭘 드실지 대 보시오, 가저리 씨.\""]], [["“I wouldn’t wish to be stiff company,”", "\"뻣뻣한 술벗이 되긴 싫으니,\" 조가 말했다."], ["said Joe. “Rum.”", "\"럼주로 하지요.\""]], [["“Rum,” repeated the stranger.", "\"럼주라.\" 낯선 사내가 되뇌었다."], ["“And will the other gentleman originate a sentiment.”", "\"그럼 저 다른 신사분도 한마디 거드시겠소?\""]], [["“Rum,”", "\"럼주요.\""], ["said Mr. Wopsle.", "우프슬 씨가 말했다."]], [["“Three Rums!” cried the stranger, calling to the landlord.", "\"럼주 세 잔!\" 낯선 사내가 주인을 부르며 외쳤다."], ["“Glasses round!”", "\"다들 한 잔씩!\""]], [["“This other gentleman,”", "\"이 다른 신사분으로 말하자면,\""], ["observed Joe, by way of introducing Mr.", "조가 우프슬 씨를 소개하려는 듯 말했다."], ["Wopsle, “is a gentleman that you would like to hear give it out.", "\"낭송하시는 걸 들으면 댁이 마음에 들어 하실 분이오."], ["Our clerk at church.”", "우리 교회 서기시지요.\""]], [["“Aha!”", "\"아하!\""], ["said the stranger, quickly, and cocking his eye at me.", "낯선 사내가 재빨리 말하며 내게 눈을 치켜떴다."], ["“The lonely church, right out on the marshes, with graves round it!”", "\"그 외딴 교회, 무덤들에 둘러싸인 채 습지 한복판에 덩그러니 있는 그 교회 말이로군요!\""]], [["“That’s it,”", "\"바로 그렇소.\""], ["said Joe.", "조가 말했다."]], [["The stranger, with a comfortable kind of grunt over his pipe, put his legs up on the settle that he had to himself.", "낯선 사내는 담뱃대를 물고 만족스러운 듯한 끙 소리를 내며, 혼자 차지한 긴 의자 위로 두 다리를 올려놓았다."], ["He wore a flapping broad-brimmed traveller’s hat, and under it a handkerchief tied over his head in the manner of a cap: so that he showed no hair.", "그는 챙이 넓어 펄럭이는 여행자용 모자를 쓰고 있었고, 그 아래에는 손수건을 모자처럼 머리에 둘러 묶고 있어서 머리카락이 전혀 보이지 않았다."], ["As he looked at the fire, I thought I saw a cunning expression, followed by a half-laugh, come into his face.", "그가 불을 바라볼 때, 나는 그의 얼굴에 교활한 표정이 떠오르고 이어 반쯤 웃는 기색이 스치는 것을 본 듯했다."]], [["“I am not acquainted with this country, gentlemen, but it seems a solitary country towards the river.”", "\"나는 이 고장을 잘 모르오, 여러분, 하지만 강 쪽으로 갈수록 적막한 고장 같소이다.\""]], [["“Most marshes is solitary,”", "\"습지란 대개 적막하지요.\""], ["said Joe.", "조가 말했다."]], [["“No doubt, no doubt.", "\"그렇겠지요, 그렇겠지요."], ["Do you find any gypsies, now, or tramps, or vagrants of any sort, out there?”", "그래, 저 바깥에서 집시나 떠돌이, 혹은 그 비슷한 부랑자 같은 자들을 더러 보시오?\""]], [["“No,”", "\"아니오,\""], ["said Joe; “none but a runaway convict now and then.", "조가 말했다. \"이따금 탈주한 죄수나 보일 뿐이지요."], ["And we don’t find them, easy.", "게다가 그놈들도 쉽게 찾아내지는 못하오."], ["Eh, Mr. Wopsle?”", "그렇지요, 우프슬 씨?\""]], [["Mr. Wopsle, with a majestic remembrance of old discomfiture, assented; but not warmly.", "우프슬 씨는 옛 낭패의 위엄 어린 기억을 떠올리며 동의했으나, 그리 열의가 담기진 않았다."]], [["“Seems you have been out after such?”", "\"그런 자들을 잡으러 나가 보신 적이 있는 모양이군요?\""], ["asked the stranger.", "낯선 사내가 물었다."]], [["“Once,” returned Joe.", "\"한 번 있소.\" 조가 대꾸했다."], ["“Not that we wanted to take them, you understand; we went out as lookers on; me, and Mr.", "\"잡고 싶어서 그런 건 아니었지요, 아시겠소만. 우리는 구경꾼으로 따라나선 거였소."], ["Wopsle, and Pip. Didn’t us, Pip?”", "나하고, 우프슬 씨하고, 핍이 말이오. 안 그러냐, 핍?\""]], [["“Yes, Joe.”", "\"맞아요, 조.\""]], [["The stranger looked at me again,—still cocking his eye, as if he were expressly taking aim at me with his invisible gun,—and said, “He’s a likely young parcel of bones that.", "낯선 사내는 다시 나를 바라보았는데 — 마치 보이지 않는 총으로 일부러 나를 겨누기라도 하는 양 여전히 눈을 치켜뜬 채로 — 이렇게 말했다. \"제법 쓸 만한 어린 뼈다귀 보따리로군."], ["What is it you call him?”", "얘를 뭐라고 부르시오?\""]], [["“Pip,”", "\"핍이오.\""], ["said Joe.", "조가 말했다."]], [["“Christened Pip?”", "\"세례명이 핍이오?\""]], [["“No, not christened Pip.”", "\"아니, 세례명이 핍은 아니오.\""]], [["“Surname Pip?”", "\"성이 핍이오?\""]], [["“No,”", "\"아니오,\""], ["said Joe, “it’s a kind of family name what he gave himself when a infant, and is called by.”", "조가 말했다. \"그건 얘가 갓난아기였을 때 스스로 지어 붙인 일종의 집안 별칭 같은 거라서, 그렇게 부르는 거요.\""]], [["“Son of yours?”", "\"댁 아들이오?\""]], [["“Well,”", "\"글쎄요,\""], ["said Joe, meditatively, not, of course, that it could be in anywise necessary to consider about it, but because it was the way at the Jolly Bargemen to seem to consider deeply about everything that was discussed over pipes,—“well—no.", "조가 곰곰이 생각하며 말했는데, 물론 그것을 두고 어떤 식으로든 곰곰이 따져 볼 필요가 있어서가 아니라, 쾌활한 뱃사공 술집에서는 담뱃대를 물고 오가는 모든 화제를 깊이 숙고하는 척하는 것이 관례였기 때문이다. \"글쎄요 — 아니오."], ["No, he ain’t.”", "아니, 얘는 아니오.\""]], [["“Nevvy?”", "\"조카요?\""], ["said the strange man.", "낯선 사내가 말했다."]], [["“Well,”", "\"글쎄요,\""], ["said Joe, with the same appearance of profound cogitation, “he is not—no, not to deceive you, he is not—my nevvy.”", "조가 여전히 깊디깊은 사색에 잠긴 듯한 모습으로 말했다. \"얘는 아니오 — 아니, 댁을 속이지 않고 말하자면, 얘는 내 조카가 아니오.\""]], [["“What the Blue Blazes is he?”", "\"그럼 도대체 얘는 뭐요?\""], ["asked the stranger.", "낯선 사내가 물었다."], ["Which appeared to me to be an inquiry of unnecessary strength.", "그것은 내게 쓸데없이 거센 물음으로 들렸다."]], [["Mr. Wopsle struck in upon that; as one who knew all about relationships, having professional occasion to bear in mind what female relations a man might not marry; and expounded the ties between me and Joe.", "거기서 우프슬 씨가 끼어들었다. 그는 남자가 어떤 여자 친척과 혼인할 수 없는지를 직업상 늘 염두에 두어야 했던 까닭에, 친족 관계라면 죄다 꿰뚫고 있는 사람답게, 나와 조 사이의 인연을 풀어 설명했다."], ["Having his hand in, Mr. Wopsle finished off with a most terrifically snarling passage from Richard the Third, and seemed to think he had done quite enough to account for it when he added, “—as the poet says.”", "내친김에 우프슬 씨는 「리처드 3세」에서 무시무시하게 으르렁대는 한 대목을 읊으며 마무리 지었고, \"— 시인이 말하듯이 말이오\" 하고 덧붙이고는 그것으로 충분히 설명이 되었다고 여기는 듯했다."]], [["And here I may remark that when Mr. Wopsle referred to me, he considered it a necessary part of such reference to rumple my hair and poke it into my eyes.", "여기서 한마디 짚어 두자면, 우프슬 씨가 나를 언급할 때면 으레 내 머리카락을 헝클어뜨려 두 눈을 찌르는 것을 그런 언급에 빠져선 안 될 절차로 여겼다는 점이다."], ["I cannot conceive why everybody of his standing who visited at our house should always have put me through the same inflammatory process under similar circumstances.", "우리 집을 찾아오는 그 정도 지위의 사람들이 어째서 비슷한 상황에서 늘 내게 같은 자극적인 처치를 가했는지, 나로서는 도무지 까닭을 헤아릴 수 없다."], ["Yet I do not call to mind that I was ever in my earlier youth the subject of remark in our social family circle, but some large-handed person took some such ophthalmic steps to patronise me.", "그렇지만 더 어렸던 시절, 우리 사교적인 식구 모임에서 내가 화제에 오를 때마다 어김없이 어떤 손 큰 인물이 나를 우대한답시고 그런 안과적인 조처를 취하지 않은 적이 있었던 기억은 떠오르지 않는다."]], [["All this while, the strange man looked at nobody but me, and looked at me as if he were determined to have a shot at me at last, and bring me down.", "이 모든 동안, 낯선 사내는 나 말고는 아무도 보지 않았고, 마침내 나를 한 방 쏘아 거꾸러뜨리기로 작정한 듯한 눈빛으로 나를 바라보았다."], ["But he said nothing after offering his Blue Blazes observation, until the glasses of rum and water were brought; and then he made his shot, and a most extraordinary shot it was.", "그러나 도대체 얘는 뭐냐는 발언을 던진 뒤로는, 럼주와 물이 든 잔들이 올 때까지 아무 말도 하지 않았다. 그러더니 그는 한 방을 쏘았는데, 그것은 더없이 기이한 한 방이었다."]], [["It was not a verbal remark, but a proceeding in dumb-show, and was pointedly addressed to me.", "그것은 말로 하는 발언이 아니라 무언극으로 이루어진 동작이었고, 분명하게 나를 겨냥한 것이었다."], ["He stirred his rum and water pointedly at me, and he tasted his rum and water pointedly at me.", "그는 나를 똑바로 향한 채 럼주에 물을 탄 술을 저었고, 나를 똑바로 향한 채 그것을 맛보았다."], ["And he stirred it and he tasted it; not with a spoon that was brought to him, but with a file.", "그가 그것을 젓고 맛볼 때 쓴 것은, 그에게 가져다준 숟가락이 아니라 줄칼이었다."]], [["He did this so that nobody but I saw the file; and when he had done it he wiped the file and put it in a breast-pocket.", "그는 나 말고는 아무도 그 줄칼을 보지 못하도록 그렇게 했다. 그러고 나서 그 줄칼을 닦아 가슴 안주머니에 넣었다."], ["I knew it to be Joe’s file, and I knew that he knew my convict, the moment I saw the instrument.", "그 도구를 본 순간 나는 그것이 조의 줄칼이라는 것을, 그리고 이 사내가 내 죄수를 알고 있다는 것을 알아차렸다."], ["I sat gazing at him, spell-bound.", "나는 넋이 나간 채 그를 바라보며 앉아 있었다."], ["But he now reclined on his settle, taking very little notice of me, and talking principally about turnips.", "그러나 이제 그는 긴 의자에 비스듬히 기댄 채, 나에게는 거의 눈길도 주지 않고 주로 순무 이야기만 늘어놓았다."]], [["There was a delicious sense of cleaning-up and making a quiet pause before going on in life afresh, in our village on Saturday nights, which stimulated Joe to dare to stay out half an hour longer on Saturdays than at other times.", "토요일 밤이면 우리 마을에는 말끔히 치우고 나서, 다시 살아가기 시작하기에 앞서 잠시 조용히 한숨 돌리는 흐뭇한 정취가 감돌았는데, 그것이 조를 부추겨 토요일에는 다른 때보다 반 시간쯤 더 밖에 머물 용기를 내게 했다."], ["The half-hour and the rum and water running out together, Joe got up to go, and took me by the hand.", "그 반 시간과 럼주에 물 탄 술이 함께 바닥나자, 조는 일어나 자리를 뜨며 내 손을 잡았다."]], [["“Stop half a moment, Mr. Gargery,”", "\"잠깐만 더 계시오, 가저리 씨.\""], ["said the strange man.", "낯선 사내가 말했다."], ["“I think I’ve got a bright new shilling somewhere in my pocket, and if I have, the boy shall have it.”", "\"내 주머니 어딘가에 반짝이는 새 실링이 한 닢 있을 성싶은데, 만약 있다면 저 아이에게 줘야겠소.\""]], [["He looked it out from a handful of small change, folded it in some crumpled paper, and gave it to me.", "그는 한 줌의 잔돈 가운데서 그것을 골라내어 구겨진 종이에 싼 다음 내게 건넸다. \"네 거다!\""], ["“Yours!”", "그가 말했다."], ["said he. “Mind!", "\"잘 들어 둬!"], ["Your own.”", "네 것이야.\""]], [["I thanked him, staring at him far beyond the bounds of good manners, and holding tight to Joe.", "나는 예의의 한도를 한참 넘어서까지 그를 빤히 쳐다보며, 조에게 바짝 매달린 채 고맙다고 인사했다."], ["He gave Joe good-night, and he gave Mr. Wopsle good-night (who went out with us), and he gave me only a look with his aiming eye,—no, not a look, for he shut it up, but wonders may be done with an eye by hiding it.", "그는 조에게 잘 가라고 인사하고, 우프슬 씨에게도(우프슬 씨는 우리와 함께 나왔다) 잘 가라고 인사했으나, 내게는 그 겨누는 눈으로 눈길 한 번만을 던졌다 — 아니, 눈길이라 할 수도 없었으니 그는 그 눈을 감아 버렸기 때문이다. 그러나 눈은 그것을 감춤으로써 놀라운 일을 해낼 수 있는 법이다."]], [["On the way home, if I had been in a humour for talking, the talk must have been all on my side, for Mr.", "집으로 돌아가는 길에, 내가 이야기할 기분이었다 한들 그 이야기는 죄다 내 몫이었을 것이다."], ["Wopsle parted from us at the door of the Jolly Bargemen, and Joe went all the way home with his mouth wide open, to rinse the rum out with as much air as possible.", "우프슬 씨는 쾌활한 뱃사공 술집 문간에서 우리와 헤어졌고, 조는 집까지 줄곧 입을 떡 벌린 채로 걸으며 가능한 한 많은 공기로 럼주를 씻어 내려 했기 때문이다."], ["But I was in a manner stupefied by this turning up of my old misdeed and old acquaintance, and could think of nothing else.", "그러나 나는 오래전의 내 잘못과 오래전의 아는 자가 이렇게 불쑥 다시 나타난 일에 어찌 보면 멍해져, 다른 어떤 것도 생각할 수 없었다."]], [["My sister was not in a very bad temper when we presented ourselves in the kitchen, and Joe was encouraged by that unusual circumstance to tell her about the bright shilling.", "우리가 부엌에 모습을 드러냈을 때 누나는 그리 험악한 기분은 아니었고, 그 보기 드문 상황에 용기를 얻은 조가 누나에게 반짝이는 실링 이야기를 했다."], ["“A bad un, I’ll be bound,”", "\"분명 위조 돈일 거야,\""], ["said Mrs. Joe triumphantly, “or he wouldn’t have given it to the boy!", "조 부인이 의기양양하게 말했다. \"아니라면 아이한테 줬을 리가 없지!"], ["Let’s look at it.”", "어디 좀 봅시다.\""]], [["I took it out of the paper, and it proved to be a good one.", "내가 종이에서 그것을 꺼내 보니 멀쩡한 진짜 동전이었다."], ["“But what’s this?” said Mrs.", "\"그런데 이건 뭐지?\""], ["Joe, throwing down the shilling and catching up the paper. “Two One-Pound notes?”", "조 부인이 실링을 내던지고 그 종이를 낚아채며 말했다. \"1파운드짜리 지폐가 두 장이라고?\""]], [["Nothing less than two fat sweltering one-pound notes that seemed to have been on terms of the warmest intimacy with all the cattle-markets in the county.", "다름 아닌, 두툼하게 땀이라도 밴 듯한 1파운드짜리 지폐 두 장이었는데, 그것은 이 주의 온갖 가축 시장과 더없이 따뜻한 친분이라도 나눈 듯한 모양새였다."], ["Joe caught up his hat again, and ran with them to the Jolly Bargemen to restore them to their owner.", "조는 다시 모자를 집어 들고 그 지폐들을 주인에게 돌려주려고 쾌활한 뱃사공 술집으로 달려갔다."], ["While he was gone, I sat down on my usual stool and looked vacantly at my sister, feeling pretty sure that the man would not be there.", "그가 간 사이, 나는 늘 앉던 걸상에 앉아 누나를 멍하니 바라보며, 그 사내가 거기 없으리라는 것을 꽤 확신하고 있었다."]], [["Presently, Joe came back, saying that the man was gone, but that he, Joe, had left word at the Three Jolly Bargemen concerning the notes.", "이윽고 조가 돌아와, 사내는 떠나고 없었으나 자기, 곧 조가 그 지폐에 관해 쾌활한 세 뱃사공 술집에 말을 남겨 두었노라고 했다."], ["Then my sister sealed them up in a piece of paper, and put them under some dried rose-leaves in an ornamental teapot on the top of a press in the state parlour.", "그러자 누나는 그 지폐들을 종이에 싸서 봉한 다음, 격식 갖춘 응접실 찬장 위에 놓인 장식용 찻주전자 안, 마른 장미 잎 밑에 넣어 두었다."], ["There they remained, a nightmare to me, many and many a night and day.", "그 지폐들은 거기 머물며, 숱한 밤낮 동안 내게 악몽이 되었다."]], [["I had sadly broken sleep when I got to bed, through thinking of the strange man taking aim at me with his invisible gun, and of the guiltily coarse and common thing it was, to be on secret terms of conspiracy with convicts,—a feature in my low career that I had previously forgotten.", "잠자리에 들었을 때 나는 잠을 몹시 설쳤다. 그 낯선 사내가 보이지 않는 총으로 나를 겨누던 일이며, 죄수들과 은밀히 공모하는 사이가 된다는 것이 얼마나 죄스럽고 거칠고 천한 일인가 하는 생각 때문이었는데 — 그것은 내 비루한 행적 중 한 대목으로, 그전에는 잊고 있던 것이었다."], ["I was haunted by the file too.", "줄칼 역시 나를 따라다니며 괴롭혔다."], ["A dread possessed me that when I least expected it, the file would reappear.", "가장 방심한 순간에 그 줄칼이 다시 나타나리라는 두려움이 나를 사로잡았다."], ["I coaxed myself to sleep by thinking of Miss Havisham’s, next Wednesday; and in my sleep I saw the file coming at me out of a door, without seeing who held it, and I screamed myself awake.", "나는 다음 수요일 해비셤 양 댁에 갈 일을 떠올리며 스스로를 어르고 달래 잠에 빠져들었으나, 꿈속에서 나는 누가 들고 있는지는 보이지 않은 채 어느 문에서 줄칼이 나를 향해 다가오는 것을 보았고, 비명을 지르다 스스로 잠에서 깨었다."]], [["# Chapter XI.", "# Chapter XI."]], [["At the appointed time I returned to Miss Havisham’s, and my hesitating ring at the gate brought out Estella.", "정해진 시각에 나는 다시 해비셤 양의 집으로 갔고, 문 앞에서 머뭇거리며 종을 울리자 에스텔러가 나왔다."], ["She locked it after admitting me, as she had done before, and again preceded me into the dark passage where her candle stood.", "그녀는 지난번처럼 나를 들인 뒤 문을 잠그고, 다시금 양초가 놓인 어두운 복도로 앞장서 나를 데려갔다."], ["She took no notice of me until she had the candle in her hand, when she looked over her shoulder, superciliously saying, “You are to come this way to-day,”", "그녀는 촛불을 손에 들 때까지 나를 거들떠보지도 않다가, 손에 들고 나서야 어깨 너머로 돌아보며 거만하게 말했다. \"오늘은 이쪽으로 와.\""], ["and took me to quite another part of the house.", "그러고는 나를 집의 전혀 다른 부분으로 데려갔다."]], [["The passage was a long one, and seemed to pervade the whole square basement of the Manor House.", "복도는 길었고, 저택의 네모진 지하층 전체에 두루 뻗어 있는 듯했다."], ["We traversed but one side of the square, however, and at the end of it she stopped, and put her candle down and opened a door.", "그러나 우리는 그 네모의 한 면만을 가로질렀고, 그 끝에서 그녀는 걸음을 멈추고 촛불을 내려놓더니 문 하나를 열었다."], ["Here, the daylight reappeared, and I found myself in a small paved courtyard, the opposite side of which was formed by a detached dwelling-house, that looked as if it had once belonged to the manager or head clerk of the extinct brewery.", "여기에서 햇빛이 다시 나타났고, 나는 어느 작은 포석 깔린 안뜰에 들어와 있는 나 자신을 발견했는데, 그 맞은편은 한 채의 별채로 이루어져 있었으니, 그 별채는 한때 폐업한 양조장의 지배인이나 수석 사무원의 거처였던 것처럼 보였다."], ["There was a clock in the outer wall of this house.", "이 집의 바깥벽에는 시계가 하나 있었다."], ["Like the clock in Miss Havisham’s room, and like Miss Havisham’s watch, it had stopped at twenty minutes to nine.", "해비셤 양의 방에 있는 시계처럼, 또 해비셤 양의 회중시계처럼, 그것 역시 아홉 시 이십 분 전에 멈춰 있었다."]], [["We went in at the door, which stood open, and into a gloomy room with a low ceiling, on the ground-floor at the back.", "우리는 열려 있던 문으로 들어가, 뒤편 일층에 자리한 천장이 낮고 음울한 방으로 들어섰다."], ["There was some company in the room, and Estella said to me as she joined it, “You are to go and stand there boy, till you are wanted.”", "방 안에는 손님 몇이 있었고, 에스텔러는 그들 사이로 끼어들며 내게 말했다. \"꼬마야, 너는 가서 저기 서 있다가 부르면 와.\""], ["“There”, being the window, I crossed to it, and stood “there,” in a very uncomfortable state of mind, looking out.", "그 \"저기\"란 창가였으므로, 나는 창가로 건너가, 더없이 불편한 심정으로 \"저기\" 서서 바깥을 내다보았다."]], [["It opened to the ground, and looked into a most miserable corner of the neglected garden, upon a rank ruin of cabbage-stalks, and one box-tree that had been clipped round long ago, like a pudding, and had a new growth at the top of it, out of shape and of a different colour, as if that part of the pudding had stuck to the saucepan and got burnt.", "창문은 바닥까지 열려 있어, 방치된 정원의 더없이 비참한 한구석을, 무성하게 썩어 가는 양배추 줄기들과, 오래전 푸딩처럼 둥그렇게 다듬어졌으나 꼭대기에 모양도 빛깔도 다른 새 가지가 돋아난 회양목 한 그루를 내다보고 있었으니, 마치 그 푸딩의 그 부분이 냄비에 들러붙어 타 버린 것 같았다."], ["This was my homely thought, as I contemplated the box-tree.", "회양목을 바라보며 떠오른 것이 이런 소박한 생각이었다."], ["There had been some light snow, overnight, and it lay nowhere else to my knowledge; but, it had not quite melted from the cold shadow of this bit of garden, and the wind caught it up in little eddies and threw it at the window, as if it pelted me for coming there.", "간밤에 가벼운 눈이 내렸는데, 내가 아는 한 다른 곳에는 눈이 쌓이지 않았건만, 이 정원 한 귀퉁이의 차가운 그늘에서는 다 녹지 않은 채로 남아 있었고, 바람이 그것을 작은 소용돌이로 휘말아 올려 창문에 흩뿌렸으니, 마치 내가 거기 온 것을 두고 나를 매질하는 듯했다."]], [["I divined that my coming had stopped conversation in the room, and that its other occupants were looking at me.", "내가 온 탓에 방 안의 대화가 멈추었고, 다른 사람들이 나를 바라보고 있다는 것을 나는 짐작했다."], ["I could see nothing of the room except the shining of the fire in the window-glass, but I stiffened in all my joints with the consciousness that I was under close inspection.", "창유리에 비치는 불빛 외에는 방 안의 어떤 것도 볼 수 없었지만, 나는 면밀한 검열을 받고 있다는 의식에 온몸의 관절이 빳빳하게 굳었다."]], [["There were three ladies in the room and one gentleman.", "방에는 부인이 셋, 신사가 하나 있었다."], ["Before I had been standing at the window five minutes, they somehow conveyed to me that they were all toadies and humbugs, but that each of them pretended not to know that the others were toadies and humbugs: because the admission that he or she did know it, would have made him or her out to be a toady and humbug.", "내가 창가에 선 지 오 분도 채 지나지 않아, 그들은 어떻게든 내게 이런 인상을 전해 왔다. 그들 모두가 아첨꾼이자 사기꾼이라는 것을, 그러나 저마다 다른 이들이 아첨꾼이자 사기꾼임을 모르는 척한다는 것을. 자기가 그것을 안다고 인정하는 순간, 자기 자신이 아첨꾼이자 사기꾼임을 드러내는 셈이 되기 때문이었다."]], [["They all had a listless and dreary air of waiting somebody’s pleasure, and the most talkative of the ladies had to speak quite rigidly to repress a yawn.", "그들 모두 누군가의 기분을 기다리는 듯한 나른하고 음울한 태도를 띠고 있었고, 부인들 가운데 가장 말이 많은 이는 하품을 억누르느라 매우 딱딱하게 말을 해야 했다."], ["This lady, whose name was Camilla, very much reminded me of my sister, with the difference that she was older, and (as I found when I caught sight of her) of a blunter cast of features.", "카밀라라는 이름의 이 부인은 우리 누나를 몹시 떠올리게 했는데, 다만 그녀가 더 나이가 많고, (그녀를 흘끗 보았을 때 알았듯이) 이목구비가 더 무딘 꼴이라는 점이 달랐다."], ["Indeed, when I knew her better I began to think it was a Mercy she had any features at all, so very blank and high was the dead wall of her face.", "사실, 그녀를 더 잘 알게 되었을 때, 나는 그녀에게 이목구비라는 게 그나마 있다는 것 자체가 자비라는 생각이 들기 시작했다. 그녀의 얼굴이라는 죽은 벽은 그토록 텅 비고 그토록 높았으니까."]], [["“Poor dear soul!”", "\"가엾은 양반!\""], ["said this lady, with an abruptness of manner quite my sister’s. “Nobody’s enemy but his own!”", "이 부인이, 영락없이 우리 누나 같은 불쑥거리는 태도로 말했다. \"남이 아니라 제 자신이 원수인 사람이지!\""]], [["“It would be much more commendable to be somebody else’s enemy,”", "\"차라리 남의 원수가 되는 편이 훨씬 칭찬받을 만하지요.\" 신사가 말했다."], ["said the gentleman; “far more natural.”", "\"그게 훨씬 자연스러운 일이고요.\""]], [["“Cousin Raymond,”", "\"레이먼드 사촌.\""], ["observed another lady, “we are to love our neighbour.”", "또 다른 부인이 말했다. \"우리는 이웃을 사랑해야 하는 거예요.\""]], [["“Sarah Pocket,”", "\"세라 포켓.\""], ["returned Cousin Raymond, “if a man is not his own neighbour, who is?”", "레이먼드 사촌이 받았다. \"사람이 자기 자신의 이웃이 아니라면, 누가 이웃이겠소?\""]], [["Miss Pocket laughed, and Camilla laughed and said (checking a yawn), “The idea!”", "포켓 양이 웃었고, 카밀라도 웃으며 말했다(하품을 참으면서). \"그런 생각을 다!\""], ["But I thought they seemed to think it rather a good idea too.", "그러나 내가 보기에는 그들도 그것을 꽤 그럴듯한 생각이라 여기는 듯했다."], ["The other lady, who had not spoken yet, said gravely and emphatically, “Very true!”", "아직 말을 하지 않았던 또 다른 부인이 엄숙하고 힘주어 말했다. \"지당하신 말씀!\""]], [["“Poor soul!”", "\"가엾은 양반!\""], ["Camilla presently went on (I knew they had all been looking at me in the mean time), “he is so very strange!", "곧이어 카밀라가 말을 이었다(그러는 동안 그들 모두 나를 보고 있었다는 것을 나는 알았다). \"그이는 어찌나 별난지!"], ["Would anyone believe that when Tom’s wife died, he actually could not be induced to see the importance of the children’s having the deepest of trimmings to their mourning?", "톰의 아내가 죽었을 때, 아이들 상복에 가장 깊은 가두리 장식을 다는 일의 중요성을 그이가 도무지 깨닫지 못했다는 걸 누가 믿겠어요? '맙소사!' 하고 그이가 말하더군요."], ["‘Good Lord!’ says he, ‘Camilla, what can it signify so long as the poor bereaved little things are in black?’ So like Matthew!", "'카밀라, 가엾게 부모 잃은 어린것들이 검은 옷만 입고 있으면 됐지, 그게 무슨 대수란 말이오?' 영락없는 매슈예요!"], ["The idea!”", "그런 생각을 다!\""]], [["“Good points in him, good points in him,”", "\"좋은 점도 있지요, 좋은 점도 있어요.\""], ["said Cousin Raymond; “Heaven forbid I should deny good points in him; but he never had, and he never will have, any sense of the proprieties.”", "레이먼드 사촌이 말했다. \"그이에게 좋은 점이 있다는 걸 내가 부정할 리가 없지요. 하지만 그이는 예의범절이란 걸 가져 본 적도 없고, 앞으로도 가질 일이 없을 겁니다.\""]], [["“You know I was obliged,”", "\"내가 어쩔 수 없이,\" 카밀라가 말했다."], ["said Camilla,—“I was obliged to be firm.", "\"내가 어쩔 수 없이 단호해야 했던 거예요. 내가 그랬지요."], ["I said, ‘It WILL NOT DO, for the credit of the family.’ I told him that, without deep trimmings, the family was disgraced.", "'그건 안 돼요. 집안의 체면을 봐서라도.' 깊은 가두리 장식이 없으면 집안이 망신을 당한다고 그이에게 말했어요."], ["I cried about it from breakfast till dinner.", "그 일로 아침부터 저녁까지 울었답니다."], ["I injured my digestion.", "소화에 탈이 날 지경이었어요."], ["And at last he flung out in his violent way, and said, with a D, ‘Then do as you like.’ Thank Goodness it will always be a consolation to me to know that I instantly went out in a pouring rain and bought the things.”", "그러다 끝내 그이가 그 거친 성미로 화를 벌컥 내며, 입에 담지 못할 욕까지 섞어 말하더군요. '그럼 당신 좋을 대로 하시오.' 그래도 내가 곧장 퍼붓는 빗속에 나가서 그것들을 사 왔다는 걸 안다는 게, 두고두고 내게 위안이 되리란 점은 다행한 일이지요.\""]], [["“He paid for them, did he not?”", "\"그 값은 그이가 치렀잖아요, 안 그래요?\""], ["asked Estella.", "에스텔러가 물었다."]], [["“It’s not the question, my dear child, who paid for them,”", "\"누가 값을 치렀느냐가 문제가 아니란다, 얘야.\""], ["returned Camilla.", "카밀라가 받았다."], ["“I bought them.", "\"내가 그것들을 샀어."], ["And I shall often think of that with peace, when I wake up in the night.”", "그리고 나는 한밤에 깨어났을 때 그 일을 평온한 마음으로 자주 떠올리게 될 거란다.\""]], [["The ringing of a distant bell, combined with the echoing of some cry or call along the passage by which I had come, interrupted the conversation and caused Estella to say to me, “Now, boy!”", "멀리서 종이 울리는 소리가, 내가 들어온 그 복도를 따라 메아리치는 어떤 외침이나 부름 소리와 어우러져, 대화를 끊어 놓았고, 에스텔러로 하여금 내게 이렇게 말하게 했다. \"자, 꼬마야!\""], ["On my turning round, they all looked at me with the utmost contempt, and, as I went out, I heard Sarah Pocket say, “Well I am sure!", "내가 돌아서자, 그들 모두 더없는 경멸의 눈으로 나를 바라보았고, 내가 나갈 때 세라 포켓이 \"원, 세상에! 다음엔 또 뭘!\""], ["What next!” and Camilla add, with indignation, “Was there ever such a fancy!", "하고 말하는 것이 들렸으며, 카밀라가 분개하여 덧붙였다. \"저런 별난 생각은 또 처음일세!"], ["The i-de-a!”", "별, 생, 각, 을, 다!\""]], [["As we were going with our candle along the dark passage, Estella stopped all of a sudden, and, facing round, said in her taunting manner, with her face quite close to mine,—", "촛불을 들고 어두운 복도를 따라 가던 중, 에스텔러는 별안간 걸음을 멈추더니, 홱 돌아서서, 제 얼굴을 내 얼굴에 바싹 들이대고는, 그 비웃는 태도로 말했다."]], [["“Well?”", "\"그래서?\""]], [["“Well, miss?”", "\"네?"], ["I answered, almost falling over her and checking myself.", "무슨 말이에요, 아가씨?\" 나는 그녀에게 부딪칠 뻔하다가 가까스로 멈춰 서며 대답했다."]], [["She stood looking at me, and, of course, I stood looking at her.", "그녀는 서서 나를 바라보았고, 나도 물론 서서 그녀를 바라보았다."]], [["“Am I pretty?”", "\"나 예뻐?\""]], [["“Yes; I think you are very pretty.”", "\"응, 아주 예쁜 것 같아.\""]], [["“Am I insulting?”", "\"내가 모욕적이야?\""]], [["“Not so much so as you were last time,” said I.", "\"지난번만큼은 아니야.\" 내가 말했다."]], [["“Not so much so?”", "\"그렇게 심하진 않다고?\""]], [["“No.”", "\"응.\""]], [["She fired when she asked the last question, and she slapped my face with such force as she had, when I answered it.", "마지막 질문을 던질 때 그녀는 발끈했고, 내가 대답하자 있는 힘껏 내 뺨을 후려쳤다."]], [["“Now?” said she.", "\"지금은?\""], ["“You little coarse monster, what do you think of me now?”", "그녀가 말했다. \"이 천박한 작은 괴물아, 지금은 내가 어떻게 보여?\""]], [["“I shall not tell you.”", "\"말하지 않을 거야.\""]], [["“Because you are going to tell upstairs. Is that it?”", "\"위층에 가서 일러바칠 거니까 그렇지. 그렇지?\""]], [["“No,”", "\"아니야.\""], ["said I, “that’s not it.”", "내가 말했다. \"그게 아니야.\""]], [["“Why don’t you cry again, you little wretch?”", "\"왜 또 안 우는 거야, 이 가엾은 것아?\""]], [["“Because I’ll never cry for you again,” said I.", "\"다시는 너 때문에 울지 않을 테니까.\" 내가 말했다."], ["Which was, I suppose, as false a declaration as ever was made; for I was inwardly crying for her then, and I know what I know of the pain she cost me afterwards.", "짐작건대 그것은 일찍이 내뱉어진 그 어떤 선언만큼이나 거짓된 선언이었다. 왜냐하면 나는 그때 속으로 그녀 때문에 울고 있었고, 그 후로 그녀가 내게 안긴 고통이 어떠했는지를 나는 잘 알고 있으니까."]], [["We went on our way upstairs after this episode; and, as we were going up, we met a gentleman groping his way down.", "이 일이 있은 뒤 우리는 위층으로 올라갔다. 올라가던 중, 우리는 더듬더듬 길을 찾아 내려오는 한 신사와 마주쳤다."]], [["“Whom have we here?”", "\"여기 이건 누구냐?\""], ["asked the gentleman, stopping and looking at me.", "신사가 걸음을 멈추고 나를 보며 물었다."]], [["“A boy,”", "\"남자애예요.\""], ["said Estella.", "에스텔러가 말했다."]], [["He was a burly man of an exceedingly dark complexion, with an exceedingly large head, and a corresponding large hand.", "그는 안색이 지독히도 가무잡잡한 건장한 사내로, 머리가 유난히 컸고, 거기에 걸맞게 손도 컸다."], ["He took my chin in his large hand and turned up my face to have a look at me by the light of the candle.", "그는 그 큰 손으로 내 턱을 잡고 촛불에 비춰 내 얼굴을 들여다보았다."], ["He was prematurely bald on the top of his head, and had bushy black eyebrows that wouldn’t lie down but stood up bristling.", "머리 꼭대기는 일찍부터 벗어졌고, 검고 숱 많은 눈썹은 가라앉을 줄 모르고 빳빳하게 곤두서 있었다."], ["His eyes were set very deep in his head, and were disagreeably sharp and suspicious.", "두 눈은 머릿속 깊이 박혀 있었고, 불쾌하리만치 날카롭고 의심에 차 있었다."], ["He had a large watch-chain, and strong black dots where his beard and whiskers would have been if he had let them.", "그는 커다란 회중시계 줄을 차고 있었고, 수염을 길렀더라면 턱수염과 구레나룻이 났을 자리에는 거뭇거뭇하고 굵은 점들이 박혀 있었다."], ["He was nothing to me, and I could have had no foresight then, that he ever would be anything to me, but it happened that I had this opportunity of observing him well.", "그는 내게 아무것도 아닌 사람이었고, 그때 나는 그가 언젠가 내게 어떤 의미를 지닌 사람이 되리라고는 조금도 내다볼 수 없었지만, 마침 나는 그를 잘 관찰할 이 기회를 얻은 것이었다."]], [["“Boy of the neighbourhood? Hey?” said he.", "\"이 동네 아이냐? 응?\" 그가 말했다."]], [["“Yes, sir,”", "\"네, 그렇습니다.\""], ["said I.", "내가 말했다."]], [["“How do you come here?”", "\"넌 어떻게 여기 왔지?\""]], [["“Miss Havisham sent for me, sir,” I explained.", "\"해비셤 양이 부르셨어요.\" 내가 설명했다."]], [["“Well!", "\"좋아!"], ["Behave yourself.", "행실 바르게 굴어라."], ["I have a pretty large experience of boys, and you’re a bad set of fellows. Now mind!”", "나는 사내아이들이라면 꽤 많이 겪어 봤는데, 너희는 못된 족속이거든. 자, 명심해!\""], ["said he, biting the side of his great forefinger as he frowned at me, “you behave yourself!”", "그가 나를 향해 인상을 찌푸리며 제 굵은 집게손가락 옆구리를 깨물고 말했다. \"행실을 바르게 해!\""]], [["With those words, he released me—which I was glad of, for his hand smelt of scented soap—and went his way downstairs.", "그 말과 함께 그는 나를 놓아주었는데 — 그의 손에서 향내 나는 비누 냄새가 났으므로 나는 그것이 반가웠다 — 그러고는 제 갈 길로 아래층으로 내려갔다."], ["I wondered whether he could be a doctor; but no, I thought; he couldn’t be a doctor, or he would have a quieter and more persuasive manner.", "나는 그가 의사일까 생각해 보았으나, 아니지, 하고 생각을 고쳤다. 의사일 리가 없어, 의사라면 좀 더 조용하고 좀 더 설득력 있는 태도를 지녔을 테니까."], ["There was not much time to consider the subject, for we were soon in Miss Havisham’s room, where she and everything else were just as I had left them.", "그 문제를 곰곰 생각할 겨를은 많지 않았으니, 우리는 곧 해비셤 양의 방에 이르렀고, 그곳의 그녀와 다른 모든 것은 내가 떠났을 때 그대로였다."], ["Estella left me standing near the door, and I stood there until Miss Havisham cast her eyes upon me from the dressing-table.", "에스텔러는 나를 문 가까이 세워 두고 떠났고, 나는 해비셤 양이 화장대에서 내게 눈길을 던질 때까지 거기 서 있었다."]], [["“So!”", "\"그래!\""], ["she said, without being startled or surprised: “the days have worn away, have they?”", "그녀가 놀라거나 당황하는 기색 없이 말했다. \"날이 다 지나갔구나, 그렇지?\""]], [["“Yes, ma’am. To-day is—”", "\"네, 아주머니. 오늘은—\""]], [["“There, there, there!”", "\"됐다, 됐어, 됐어!\""], ["with the impatient movement of her fingers.", "손가락을 조바심치며 놀리면서 그녀가 말했다."], ["“I don’t want to know.", "\"알고 싶지 않다."], ["Are you ready to play?”", "놀 준비는 됐느냐?\""]], [["I was obliged to answer in some confusion, “I don’t think I am, ma’am.”", "나는 다소 당황하여 대답하지 않을 수 없었다. \"그럴 것 같지 않아요, 아주머니.\""]], [["“Not at cards again?” she demanded, with a searching look.", "\"또 카드는 안 되겠다는 거냐?\" 그녀가 살피는 눈빛으로 따져 물었다."]], [["“Yes, ma’am; I could do that, if I was wanted.”", "\"네, 아주머니. 시키시면 그건 할 수 있어요.\""]], [["“Since this house strikes you old and grave, boy,”", "\"이 집이 네게 낡고 음울하게 느껴지고,\""], ["said Miss Havisham, impatiently, “and you are unwilling to play, are you willing to work?”", "해비셤 양이 조바심치며 말했다. \"네가 놀기를 꺼린다면, 일을 할 마음은 있느냐?\""]], [["I could answer this inquiry with a better heart than I had been able to find for the other question, and I said I was quite willing.", "이 물음에는 다른 질문에 답할 때 끌어낼 수 있었던 것보다 한결 가벼운 마음으로 답할 수 있었기에, 나는 기꺼이 그러겠다고 말했다."]], [["“Then go into that opposite room,”", "\"그럼 저 맞은편 방으로 가거라.\""], ["said she, pointing at the door behind me with her withered hand, “and wait there till I come.”", "그녀가 시든 손으로 내 뒤의 문을 가리키며 말했다. \"그리고 내가 갈 때까지 거기서 기다려.\""]], [["I crossed the staircase landing, and entered the room she indicated.", "나는 계단참을 가로질러, 그녀가 가리킨 방으로 들어갔다."], ["From that room, too, the daylight was completely excluded, and it had an airless smell that was oppressive.", "그 방에서도 햇빛은 완전히 차단되어 있었고, 숨이 막히도록 답답한, 공기가 통하지 않는 냄새가 났다."], ["A fire had been lately kindled in the damp old-fashioned grate, and it was more disposed to go out than to burn up, and the reluctant smoke which hung in the room seemed colder than the clearer air,—like our own marsh mist.", "눅눅하고 구식인 벽난로 쇠살대에 근래 불이 지펴졌으나, 활활 타오르기보다는 꺼지려는 기색이 더했고, 방 안에 떠도는 마지못한 연기는 더 맑은 공기보다도 더 차가워 보였으니, 마치 우리 고장의 늪 안개 같았다."], ["Certain wintry branches of candles on the high chimney-piece faintly lighted the chamber; or it would be more expressive to say, faintly troubled its darkness.", "높은 벽난로 선반 위에 놓인 겨울처럼 앙상한 촛대 몇 개가 방을 희미하게 밝히고 있었다. 아니, 방의 어둠을 희미하게 어지럽혔다고 하는 편이 더 정확하겠다."], ["It was spacious, and I dare say had once been handsome, but every discernible thing in it was covered with dust and mould, and dropping to pieces.", "방은 널찍했고, 짐작건대 한때는 보기 좋았을 테지만, 그 안의 분간할 수 있는 모든 것은 먼지와 곰팡이로 뒤덮인 채 조각조각 허물어지고 있었다."], ["The most prominent object was a long table with a tablecloth spread on it, as if a feast had been in preparation when the house and the clocks all stopped together.", "가장 눈에 띄는 것은 식탁보가 펼쳐진 긴 식탁이었으니, 마치 집과 시계가 모두 한꺼번에 멈추었을 때 잔치 준비가 한창이었던 것 같았다."], ["An epergne or centre-piece of some kind was in the middle of this cloth; it was so heavily overhung with cobwebs that its form was quite undistinguishable; and, as I looked along the yellow expanse out of which I remember its seeming to grow, like a black fungus, I saw speckle-legged spiders with blotchy bodies running home to it, and running out from it, as if some circumstances of the greatest public importance had just transpired in the spider community.", "이 식탁보 한가운데에는 무슨 장식 받침대 같은 것이 놓여 있었는데, 거미줄이 어찌나 두껍게 뒤덮였던지 그 형체를 도무지 분간할 수 없었다. 그리고 내 기억에 그것이 검은 버섯처럼 자라 나온 듯하던 그 누런 평면을 죽 훑어보다가, 나는 점박이 다리에 얼룩진 몸뚱이를 한 거미들이 그것을 향해 집으로 달려가고, 또 그것에서 달려 나오는 것을 보았으니, 마치 거미 사회에 더없이 막중한 공적 사건이 방금 일어나기라도 한 것 같았다."]], [["I heard the mice too, rattling behind the panels, as if the same occurrence were important to their interests.", "생쥐들이 벽판 뒤에서 부스럭거리는 소리도 들렸는데, 마치 똑같은 사건이 그들의 이해와도 관련된 것 같았다."], ["But the black beetles took no notice of the agitation, and groped about the hearth in a ponderous elderly way, as if they were short-sighted and hard of hearing, and not on terms with one another.", "그러나 검은 딱정벌레들은 그 소동에 아랑곳하지 않고, 근시에 귀까지 어두운 데다 서로 사이마저 좋지 않다는 듯, 묵직하고 늙수그레한 걸음걸이로 난로 가를 더듬거리며 돌아다녔다."]], [["These crawling things had fascinated my attention, and I was watching them from a distance, when Miss Havisham laid a hand upon my shoulder.", "이 기어 다니는 것들이 내 주의를 사로잡아, 나는 멀찍이서 그것들을 지켜보고 있었는데, 그때 해비셤 양이 내 어깨에 손을 얹었다."], ["In her other hand she had a crutch-headed stick on which she leaned, and she looked like the Witch of the place.", "다른 손에는 손잡이가 목발 머리처럼 생긴 지팡이를 짚고 있었으니, 그녀는 마치 이곳의 마녀처럼 보였다."]], [["“This,”", "\"여기가,\""], ["said she, pointing to the long table with her stick, “is where I will be laid when I am dead.", "그녀가 지팡이로 긴 식탁을 가리키며 말했다. \"내가 죽으면 누일 자리다."], ["They shall come and look at me here.”", "사람들이 여기로 와서 나를 들여다볼 테지.\""]], [["With some vague misgiving that she might get upon the table then and there and die at once, the complete realisation of the ghastly waxwork at the Fair, I shrank under her touch.", "그녀가 당장 그 식탁 위에 올라가 그 자리에서 죽어 버려, 장터의 그 섬뜩한 밀랍 인형이 그대로 실현되는 것은 아닐까 하는 어렴풋한 불안에, 나는 그녀의 손길 아래에서 움츠러들었다."]], [["“What do you think that is?”", "\"저게 뭐라고 생각하느냐?\""], ["she asked me, again pointing with her stick; “that, where those cobwebs are?”", "그녀가 다시 지팡이로 가리키며 물었다. \"저기, 거미줄이 쳐진 저것 말이다.\""]], [["“I can’t guess what it is, ma’am.”", "\"저게 뭔지 짐작이 안 돼요, 아주머니.\""]], [["“It’s a great cake.", "\"커다란 케이크다."], ["A bride-cake. Mine!”", "웨딩케이크지. 내 거야!\""]], [["She looked all round the room in a glaring manner, and then said, leaning on me while her hand twitched my shoulder, “Come, come, come!", "그녀는 번득이는 눈빛으로 방 안을 죽 둘러보더니, 손으로 내 어깨를 움켜쥐어 당기며 내게 기댄 채 말했다. \"자, 자, 자!"], ["Walk me, walk me!”", "나를 걸려라, 나를 걸려!\""]], [["I made out from this, that the work I had to do, was to walk Miss Havisham round and round the room.", "이로써 나는 내가 해야 할 일이 해비셤 양을 부축해 방 안을 빙빙 도는 것임을 알아차렸다."], ["Accordingly, I started at once, and she leaned upon my shoulder, and we went away at a pace that might have been an imitation (founded on my first impulse under that roof) of Mr. Pumblechook’s chaise-cart.", "그리하여 나는 곧장 걷기 시작했고, 그녀는 내 어깨에 기댔으며, 우리는 (그 지붕 아래에서 처음 든 충동에 따라) 펌블추크 씨의 이륜마차를 흉내 냈다 할 만한 속도로 나아갔다."]], [["She was not physically strong, and after a little time said, “Slower!”", "그녀는 체력이 강하지 못해, 얼마 지나지 않아 말했다. \"더 천천히!\""], ["Still, we went at an impatient fitful speed, and as we went, she twitched the hand upon my shoulder, and worked her mouth, and led me to believe that we were going fast because her thoughts went fast.", "그래도 우리는 조바심 어린 들쭉날쭉한 속도로 걸었고, 걸어가는 동안 그녀는 내 어깨에 얹은 손을 씰룩거리고 입을 옴짝거리며, 우리가 빨리 가는 것은 그녀의 생각이 빨리 흐르기 때문이라고 내가 믿게 만들었다."], ["After a while she said, “Call Estella!”", "얼마 후 그녀가 말했다. \"에스텔러를 불러라!\""], ["so I went out on the landing and roared that name as I had done on the previous occasion.", "그래서 나는 계단참으로 나가, 지난번에 그랬듯이 그 이름을 큰 소리로 외쳤다."], ["When her light appeared, I returned to Miss Havisham, and we started away again round and round the room.", "그녀의 불빛이 나타나자 나는 해비셤 양에게 돌아갔고, 우리는 다시 방 안을 빙빙 돌기 시작했다."]], [["If only Estella had come to be a spectator of our proceedings, I should have felt sufficiently discontented; but as she brought with her the three ladies and the gentleman whom I had seen below, I didn’t know what to do.", "에스텔러가 우리가 하는 일을 구경하러 온 것만으로도 나는 충분히 못마땅했을 테지만, 그녀가 아래층에서 보았던 부인 셋과 신사를 데리고 왔으므로, 나는 어찌할 바를 몰랐다."], ["In my politeness, I would have stopped; but Miss Havisham twitched my shoulder, and we posted on,—with a shame-faced consciousness on my part that they would think it was all my doing.", "예의를 차리느라 나는 멈추고 싶었으나, 해비셤 양이 내 어깨를 씰룩 잡아당겨, 우리는 계속 나아갔으니 — 그러는 내내 나는 그들이 이 모든 게 다 내 짓이라 여길 거라는 부끄러운 의식에 시달렸다."]], [["“Dear Miss Havisham,”", "\"해비셤 양.\""], ["said Miss Sarah Pocket.", "세라 포켓 양이 말했다."], ["“How well you look!”", "\"안색이 참 좋아 보이세요!\""]], [["“I do not,”", "\"안 좋아.\""], ["returned Miss Havisham.", "해비셤 양이 받았다."], ["“I am yellow skin and bone.”", "\"나는 누런 살가죽에 뼈뿐이야.\""]], [["Camilla brightened when Miss Pocket met with this rebuff; and she murmured, as she plaintively contemplated Miss Havisham, “Poor dear soul!", "포켓 양이 이렇게 면박을 당하자 카밀라의 얼굴이 환해졌고, 그녀는 애처롭게 해비셤 양을 바라보며 중얼거렸다. \"가엾은 양반!"], ["Certainly not to be expected to look well, poor thing. The idea!”", "안색이 좋으리라고 기대할 수야 없지요, 가엾기도 하지. 그런 생각을 다!\""]], [["“And how are you?”", "\"그래, 너는 어떠냐?\""], ["said Miss Havisham to Camilla.", "해비셤 양이 카밀라에게 말했다."], ["As we were close to Camilla then, I would have stopped as a matter of course, only Miss Havisham wouldn’t stop.", "그때 우리는 카밀라 가까이에 있었으므로, 나는 당연히 멈추려 했지만, 해비셤 양이 멈추려 들지 않았다."], ["We swept on, and I felt that I was highly obnoxious to Camilla.", "우리는 휙 지나쳤고, 나는 내가 카밀라에게 몹시 밉살스러운 존재가 되었음을 느꼈다."]], [["“Thank you, Miss Havisham,”", "\"고마워요, 해비셤 양.\""], ["she returned, “I am as well as can be expected.”", "그녀가 받았다. \"이만하면 나름대로 잘 지내고 있지요.\""]], [["“Why, what’s the matter with you?” asked Miss Havisham, with exceeding sharpness.", "\"아니, 너한테 무슨 일이라도 있느냐?\" 해비셤 양이 유난히 날카롭게 물었다."]], [["“Nothing worth mentioning,”", "\"입에 올릴 만한 건 아니에요.\""], ["replied Camilla.", "카밀라가 대답했다."], ["“I don’t wish to make a display of my feelings, but I have habitually thought of you more in the night than I am quite equal to.”", "\"내 감정을 드러내고 싶진 않지만, 밤이면 으레 내가 감당할 수 있는 것 이상으로 양을 생각하곤 한답니다.\""]], [["“Then don’t think of me,”", "\"그럼 내 생각은 하지 마라.\""], ["retorted Miss Havisham.", "해비셤 양이 쏘아붙였다."]], [["“Very easily said!”", "\"말이야 쉽지요!\""], ["remarked Camilla, amiably repressing a sob, while a hitch came into her upper lip, and her tears overflowed.", "카밀라가, 윗입술이 씰룩거리고 눈물이 넘쳐흐르는 가운데, 흐느낌을 상냥하게 억누르며 말했다."], ["“Raymond is a witness what ginger and sal volatile I am obliged to take in the night.", "\"밤이면 내가 생강과 각성용 암모니아수를 얼마나 들이켜야 하는지는 레이먼드가 증인이에요."], ["Raymond is a witness what nervous jerkings I have in my legs.", "밤이면 내 다리가 얼마나 신경질적으로 떨리는지도 레이먼드가 증인이고요."], ["Chokings and nervous jerkings, however, are nothing new to me when I think with anxiety of those I love.", "하지만 사랑하는 이들을 근심하며 생각할 때면 숨이 막히고 신경질적으로 떨리는 건 내겐 새삼스러운 일도 아니랍니다."], ["If I could be less affectionate and sensitive, I should have a better digestion and an iron set of nerves.", "내가 정이 덜하고 덜 예민했더라면, 소화도 더 잘되고 신경도 무쇠처럼 단단했겠지요."], ["I am sure I wish it could be so.", "정말이지 그럴 수 있었으면 싶어요."], ["But as to not thinking of you in the night—The idea!”", "하지만 밤에 양 생각을 안 한다는 건—그런 생각을 다!\""], ["Here, a burst of tears.", "여기서 그녀는 울음을 터뜨렸다."]], [["The Raymond referred to, I understood to be the gentleman present, and him I understood to be Mr.", "언급된 그 레이먼드란 그 자리에 있는 신사를 가리키는 것임을, 나는 알아차렸고, 그가 카밀라 씨라는 것도 알아차렸다."], ["Camilla. He came to the rescue at this point, and said in a consolatory and complimentary voice, “Camilla, my dear, it is well known that your family feelings are gradually undermining you to the extent of making one of your legs shorter than the other.”", "그가 이 대목에서 구원에 나서, 위로하면서도 추켜세우는 목소리로 말했다. \"카밀라, 여보, 당신의 집안에 대한 정 때문에 점점 몸이 축나서, 한쪽 다리가 다른 쪽보다 짧아질 지경이라는 건 다들 아는 사실이오.\""]], [["“I am not aware,”", "\"제가 알기로는,\""], ["observed the grave lady whose voice I had heard but once, “that to think of any person is to make a great claim upon that person, my dear.”", "내가 딱 한 번 들었던 그 엄숙한 부인이 말했다. \"어떤 사람을 생각한다고 해서 그 사람에게 무슨 큰 권리를 주장하는 건 아니랍니다, 얘야.\""]], [["Miss Sarah Pocket, whom I now saw to be a little dry, brown, corrugated old woman, with a small face that might have been made of walnut-shells, and a large mouth like a cat’s without the whiskers, supported this position by saying, “No, indeed, my dear. Hem!”", "이제 보니 세라 포켓 양은 자그마하고 메마르고 거무스레한 데다 쪼글쪼글한 노부인으로, 호두 껍데기로 빚은 듯한 작은 얼굴에, 수염 없는 고양이 같은 커다란 입을 지니고 있었는데, 그녀가 이 견해를 거들어 말했다. \"정말 그렇지요, 얘야. 흠!\""]], [["“Thinking is easy enough,”", "\"생각하는 거야 충분히 쉬운 일이지요.\""], ["said the grave lady.", "그 엄숙한 부인이 말했다."]], [["“What is easier, you know?”", "\"그보다 쉬운 게 또 어디 있겠어요?\""], ["assented Miss Sarah Pocket.", "세라 포켓 양이 맞장구쳤다."]], [["“Oh, yes, yes!” cried Camilla, whose fermenting feelings appeared to rise from her legs to her bosom.", "\"오, 그래요, 그래요!\" 카밀라가 외쳤는데, 부글거리는 그녀의 감정이 다리에서 가슴으로 치솟는 듯했다."], ["“It’s all very true!", "\"다 지당한 말씀이에요!"], ["It’s a weakness to be so affectionate, but I can’t help it.", "이토록 정이 깊은 게 약점이긴 하지만, 어쩔 수가 없는걸요."], ["No doubt my health would be much better if it was otherwise, still I wouldn’t change my disposition if I could.", "그렇지 않았더라면 내 건강이 훨씬 나았으리란 건 의심할 여지가 없지만, 그래도 할 수만 있다 해도 내 천성을 바꾸진 않겠어요."], ["It’s the cause of much suffering, but it’s a consolation to know I possess it, when I wake up in the night.”", "그 때문에 숱한 고통을 겪지만, 한밤에 깨어났을 때 내가 그런 천성을 지녔다는 걸 안다는 게 위안이 되거든요.\""], ["Here another burst of feeling.", "여기서 또 한차례 감정이 북받쳐 올랐다."]], [["Miss Havisham and I had never stopped all this time, but kept going round and round the room; now brushing against the skirts of the visitors, now giving them the whole length of the dismal chamber.", "해비셤 양과 나는 이 내내 한 번도 멈추지 않고, 방 안을 빙빙 돌고 또 돌았으니, 어느 때는 손님들의 치맛자락을 스치고, 어느 때는 음침한 방의 끝에서 끝까지를 그들에게 내주었다."]], [["“There’s Matthew!”", "\"매슈는 또 어떻고요!\""], ["said Camilla.", "카밀라가 말했다."], ["“Never mixing with any natural ties, never coming here to see how Miss Havisham is! I have taken to the sofa with my staylace cut, and have lain there hours insensible, with my head over the side, and my hair all down, and my feet I don’t know where—”", "\"천륜의 정이라곤 도무지 섞이려 들지 않고, 해비셤 양이 어떻게 지내는지 보러 여기 한번 오는 법도 없으니! 나는 코르셋 끈을 끊고 소파에 드러누워, 머리를 옆으로 늘어뜨리고, 머리카락을 다 풀어헤친 채, 발은 어디 두었는지도 모르게—\""]], [["(“Much higher than your head, my love,”", "(\"당신 머리보다 훨씬 높은 데 있었지, 여보.\""], ["said Mr. Camilla.)", "카밀라 씨가 말했다.)"]], [["“I have gone off into that state, hours and hours, on account of Matthew’s strange and inexplicable conduct, and nobody has thanked me.”", "\"—매슈의 괴상하고 도무지 알 수 없는 행실 때문에 그런 상태로 몇 시간이고 정신을 잃고 있었건만, 아무도 내게 고마워하지 않더군요.\""]], [["“Really I must say I should think not!”", "\"정말이지, 그러긴 어려웠겠다 싶군요!\""], ["interposed the grave lady.", "엄숙한 부인이 끼어들었다."]], [["“You see, my dear,”", "\"이봐요, 얘야,\""], ["added Miss Sarah Pocket (a blandly vicious personage), “the question to put to yourself is, who did you expect to thank you, my love?”", "세라 포켓 양이 (점잖게 표독스러운 인물이었다) 덧붙였다. \"스스로에게 던져야 할 질문은, 도대체 누가 네게 고마워해 주기를 기대했느냐 하는 거란다, 얘야.\""]], [["“Without expecting any thanks, or anything of the sort,”", "\"고마움이든 그 비슷한 무엇이든 기대하지 않고도,\""], ["resumed Camilla, “I have remained in that state, hours and hours, and Raymond is a witness of the extent to which I have choked, and what the total inefficacy of ginger has been, and I have been heard at the piano-forte tuner’s across the street, where the poor mistaken children have even supposed it to be pigeons cooing at a distance,—and now to be told—”", "카밀라가 말을 이었다. \"나는 몇 시간이고 그런 상태로 있었고, 내가 얼마나 숨이 막혔는지, 그리고 생강이 얼마나 아무 소용이 없었는지는 레이먼드가 증인이에요. 길 건너 피아노 조율사 집까지 내 소리가 들려서, 그 가엾은 철없는 아이들은 멀리서 비둘기가 구구대는 소리인 줄로 알았다지 뭐예요—그런데 이제 와서 이런 말을 듣다니—\""], ["Here Camilla put her hand to her throat, and began to be quite chemical as to the formation of new combinations there.", "여기서 카밀라는 손을 목으로 가져갔고, 거기서 새로운 화합물을 만들어 내듯 자못 화학적인 양상을 띠기 시작했다."]], [["When this same Matthew was mentioned, Miss Havisham stopped me and herself, and stood looking at the speaker.", "이 매슈라는 이름이 거론되자, 해비셤 양은 나와 자신을 멈춰 세우고, 서서 말하는 이를 바라보았다."], ["This change had a great influence in bringing Camilla’s chemistry to a sudden end.", "이 변화는 카밀라의 화학 작용을 별안간 끝장내는 데 큰 영향을 미쳤다."]], [["“Matthew will come and see me at last,”", "\"매슈는 마침내 나를 보러 올 게다.\""], ["said Miss Havisham, sternly, “when I am laid on that table.", "해비셤 양이 준엄하게 말했다. \"내가 저 식탁 위에 누이게 될 때 말이야."], ["That will be his place,—there,”", "저기가 그자의 자리가 될 거다—저기,\""], ["striking the table with her stick, “at my head!", "지팡이로 식탁을 내리치며, \"내 머리맡에!"], ["And yours will be there!", "그리고 네 자리는 저기!"], ["And your husband’s there!", "네 남편 자리는 저기!"], ["And Sarah Pocket’s there!", "세라 포켓의 자리는 저기!"], ["And Georgiana’s there!", "조지애나의 자리는 저기!"], ["Now you all know where to take your stations when you come to feast upon me. And now go!”", "자, 너희 모두 나를 뜯어 먹으러 올 때 어느 자리에 들어설지 이제 알겠지. 자, 이제들 가거라!\""]], [["At the mention of each name, she had struck the table with her stick in a new place.", "이름 하나하나가 불릴 때마다, 그녀는 지팡이로 식탁의 새로운 자리를 내리쳤다. 이제 그녀가 말했다."], ["She now said, “Walk me, walk me!” and we went on again.", "\"나를 걸려라, 나를 걸려!\" 그리하여 우리는 다시 걷기 시작했다."]], [["“I suppose there’s nothing to be done,”", "\"별수 없겠지요.\" 카밀라가 외쳤다."], ["exclaimed Camilla, “but comply and depart.", "\"따르고 물러가는 수밖에."], ["It’s something to have seen the object of one’s love and duty for even so short a time.", "이토록 짧은 동안이나마 사랑과 도리의 대상을 뵈었다는 것만으로도 어디예요."], ["I shall think of it with a melancholy satisfaction when I wake up in the night.", "한밤에 깨어났을 때 우울한 만족감으로 그 일을 떠올리겠지요."], ["I wish Matthew could have that comfort, but he sets it at defiance.", "매슈도 그런 위안을 누릴 수 있었으면 싶지만, 그이는 그걸 한사코 마다하니."], ["I am determined not to make a display of my feelings, but it’s very hard to be told one wants to feast on one’s relations,—as if one was a Giant,—and to be told to go.", "내 감정을 드러내지 않으리라 단단히 마음먹고 있지만, 마치 거인이라도 되는 양 친척들을 뜯어 먹으려 든다는 소리를 듣고, 가라는 말을 듣는다는 건 참으로 견디기 힘든 일이에요."], ["The bare idea!”", "그런 생각을 다!\""]], [["Mr. Camilla interposing, as Mrs. Camilla laid her hand upon her heaving bosom, that lady assumed an unnatural fortitude of manner which I supposed to be expressive of an intention to drop and choke when out of view, and kissing her hand to Miss Havisham, was escorted forth.", "카밀라 부인이 들먹이는 가슴에 손을 얹자, 카밀라 씨가 끼어들었고, 그 부인은 부자연스럽게 꿋꿋한 태도를 지어 보였으니, 내 짐작에 그것은 시야에서 벗어나면 쓰러져 숨이 막혀 보이겠다는 의도를 드러내는 것이었다."], ["Sarah Pocket and Georgiana contended who should remain last; but Sarah was too knowing to be outdone, and ambled round Georgiana with that artful slipperiness that the latter was obliged to take precedence.", "그녀는 해비셤 양에게 손에 입을 맞춰 작별 인사를 보내고는, 부축을 받으며 나갔다. 세라 포켓과 조지애나는 서로 마지막까지 남으려고 다투었으나, 세라는 워낙 약삭빨라서 지지 않았으니, 어찌나 교묘하고 미끈하게 조지애나의 둘레를 종종걸음 치며 돌던지 조지애나가 어쩔 수 없이 먼저 나서야 했다."], ["Sarah Pocket then made her separate effect of departing with, “Bless you, Miss Havisham dear!”", "그러고 나서 세라 포켓은 따로 효과를 노려, \"복 받으세요, 해비셤 양!\""], ["and with a smile of forgiving pity on her walnut-shell countenance for the weaknesses of the rest.", "하며, 나머지 사람들의 약점을 너그러이 측은히 여기는 미소를 그 호두 껍데기 같은 얼굴에 띤 채 떠났다."]], [["While Estella was away lighting them down, Miss Havisham still walked with her hand on my shoulder, but more and more slowly.", "에스텔러가 그들에게 불을 비춰 내려보내러 가 있는 동안, 해비셤 양은 여전히 내 어깨에 손을 얹은 채 걸었으나, 점점 더 느려졌다."], ["At last she stopped before the fire, and said, after muttering and looking at it some seconds,—", "마침내 그녀는 난롯불 앞에 멈춰 서서, 몇 초간 그것을 바라보며 무어라 중얼거리더니 말했다—"]], [["“This is my birthday, Pip.”", "\"오늘이 내 생일이란다, 핍.\""]], [["I was going to wish her many happy returns, when she lifted her stick.", "내가 만수무강을 빌어 드리려는데, 그녀가 지팡이를 들어 올렸다."]], [["“I don’t suffer it to be spoken of.", "\"나는 그 말이 입에 오르는 걸 용납하지 않는다."], ["I don’t suffer those who were here just now, or any one to speak of it.", "방금 여기 있던 자들이든 그 누구든, 그 말을 하는 걸 용납하지 않아."], ["They come here on the day, but they dare not refer to it.”", "그자들은 이날 여기 오긴 하지만, 감히 그 일을 입에 올리진 못하지.\""]], [["Of course I made no further effort to refer to it.", "물론 나는 그 일을 입에 올리려는 시도를 더는 하지 않았다."]], [["“On this day of the year, long before you were born, this heap of decay,”", "\"한 해의 이날에, 네가 태어나기 한참 전에, 이 썩은 더미가,\""], ["stabbing with her crutched stick at the pile of cobwebs on the table, but not touching it, “was brought here.", "그녀가 목발 지팡이로 식탁 위의 거미줄 무더기를 찌르는 시늉을 했으나 닿지는 않았다. \"여기 들여왔단다."], ["It and I have worn away together.", "그것과 나는 함께 닳아 왔지."], ["The mice have gnawed at it, and sharper teeth than teeth of mice have gnawed at me.”", "생쥐들이 그것을 갉아먹었고, 생쥐의 이빨보다 더 날카로운 이빨이 나를 갉아먹었어.\""]], [["She held the head of her stick against her heart as she stood looking at the table; she in her once white dress, all yellow and withered; the once white cloth all yellow and withered; everything around in a state to crumble under a touch.", "그녀는 식탁을 바라보며 서서, 지팡이 머리를 제 가슴에 갖다 댔다. 한때 흰빛이던 그녀의 드레스는 온통 누렇게 시들었고, 한때 흰빛이던 식탁보도 온통 누렇게 시들었으며, 주위의 모든 것은 손만 대면 바스러질 상태에 있었다."]], [["“When the ruin is complete,”", "\"이 폐허가 다 완성되었을 때,\""], ["said she, with a ghastly look, “and when they lay me dead, in my bride’s dress on the bride’s table,—which shall be done, and which will be the finished curse upon him,—so much the better if it is done on this day!”", "그녀가 섬뜩한 표정으로 말했다. \"그리고 그들이 죽은 나를, 신부의 드레스를 입은 채 신부의 식탁 위에 누일 때—그건 반드시 이루어질 것이고, 그것이 그자에게 내리는 완성된 저주가 될 거다—그 일이 이날 이루어진다면 더더욱 좋겠지!\""]], [["She stood looking at the table as if she stood looking at her own figure lying there.", "그녀는 마치 거기 누운 제 모습을 바라보고 서 있기라도 한 듯이 식탁을 바라보며 서 있었다."], ["I remained quiet.", "나는 가만히 있었다."], ["Estella returned, and she too remained quiet.", "에스텔러가 돌아왔고, 그녀 역시 가만히 있었다."], ["It seemed to me that we continued thus for a long time.", "내가 보기에 우리는 오랫동안 그렇게 있었던 듯하다."], ["In the heavy air of the room, and the heavy darkness that brooded in its remoter corners, I even had an alarming fancy that Estella and I might presently begin to decay.", "방 안의 묵직한 공기 속에서, 그리고 더 멀찍한 구석마다 서려 있는 묵직한 어둠 속에서, 나는 에스텔러와 내가 곧 썩어 가기 시작할지도 모른다는 섬뜩한 공상마저 들었다."]], [["At length, not coming out of her distraught state by degrees, but in an instant, Miss Havisham said, “Let me see you two play cards; why have you not begun?”", "마침내, 그 넋 나간 상태에서 차츰차츰이 아니라 일순간에 빠져나오며, 해비셤 양이 말했다. \"너희 둘이 카드놀이 하는 걸 보자꾸나. 왜 아직 시작하지 않았느냐?\""], ["With that, we returned to her room, and sat down as before; I was beggared, as before; and again, as before, Miss Havisham watched us all the time, directed my attention to Estella’s beauty, and made me notice it the more by trying her jewels on Estella’s breast and hair.", "그 말과 함께 우리는 그녀의 방으로 돌아가, 전처럼 자리에 앉았다. 나는 전처럼 빈털터리가 되었고, 다시금 전처럼, 해비셤 양은 내내 우리를 지켜보며 에스텔러의 아름다움에 내 주의를 돌렸고, 제 보석들을 에스텔러의 가슴과 머리에 대 보면서 내가 그것을 더욱 눈여겨보게 만들었다."]], [["Estella, for her part, likewise treated me as before, except that she did not condescend to speak.", "에스텔러는, 제 쪽으로도 마찬가지로 나를 전처럼 대했는데, 다만 내게 말을 거는 친절을 베풀지 않는다는 점만 달랐다."], ["When we had played some half-dozen games, a day was appointed for my return, and I was taken down into the yard to be fed in the former dog-like manner.", "우리가 여섯 판쯤 놀고 나자, 내가 다시 올 날이 정해졌고, 나는 안뜰로 끌려 내려가 전과 같이 개에게 주듯 먹을 것을 받았다."], ["There, too, I was again left to wander about as I liked.", "거기서도 나는 다시금 내 멋대로 돌아다니도록 내버려졌다."]], [["It is not much to the purpose whether a gate in that garden wall which I had scrambled up to peep over on the last occasion was, on that last occasion, open or shut.", "지난번에 내가 기어올라 너머를 엿보았던 그 정원 담의 문이, 그 지난번에 열려 있었는지 닫혀 있었는지는 그리 중요한 문제가 아니다."], ["Enough that I saw no gate then, and that I saw one now.", "그때는 문이 보이지 않았고 지금은 하나가 보였다는 것으로 족하다."], ["As it stood open, and as I knew that Estella had let the visitors out,—for she had returned with the keys in her hand,—I strolled into the garden, and strolled all over it.", "그것이 열려 있었고, 또 에스텔러가 손님들을 내보냈다는 것을 — 그녀가 열쇠를 손에 들고 돌아왔으므로 — 나는 알고 있었기에, 나는 정원으로 어슬렁어슬렁 들어가 그 안을 두루 거닐었다."], ["It was quite a wilderness, and there were old melon-frames and cucumber-frames in it, which seemed in their decline to have produced a spontaneous growth of weak attempts at pieces of old hats and boots, with now and then a weedy offshoot into the likeness of a battered saucepan.", "그곳은 그야말로 황무지였고, 안에는 낡은 멜론 모종틀과 오이 모종틀이 있었는데, 그것들은 쇠락해 가면서 낡은 모자며 장화 조각을 닮은 빈약한 시도들을 제멋대로 피워 낸 듯했고, 이따금 우중충하게 뻗어 나온 곁가지는 찌그러진 냄비를 빼닮은 꼴이었다."]], [["When I had exhausted the garden and a greenhouse with nothing in it but a fallen-down grape-vine and some bottles, I found myself in the dismal corner upon which I had looked out of the window.", "정원과, 쓰러진 포도 덩굴과 병 몇 개 외에는 아무것도 없는 온실을 다 돌아보고 나니, 나는 창문 너머로 내다보았던 그 음침한 구석에 와 있었다."], ["Never questioning for a moment that the house was now empty, I looked in at another window, and found myself, to my great surprise, exchanging a broad stare with a pale young gentleman with red eyelids and light hair.", "이제 집이 비었으리라는 것을 추호도 의심하지 않은 채, 나는 다른 창문 안을 들여다보다가, 크게 놀랍게도, 눈꺼풀이 붉고 머리카락이 옅은 빛깔인 어느 창백한 도련님과 멀거니 마주 보고 있는 나 자신을 발견했다."]], [["This pale young gentleman quickly disappeared, and reappeared beside me.", "이 창백한 도련님은 휙 사라지더니, 어느새 내 곁에 다시 나타났다."], ["He had been at his books when I had found myself staring at him, and I now saw that he was inky.", "내가 그를 멀거니 바라보고 있던 그때 그는 책을 보던 중이었던지, 이제 보니 그는 잉크투성이였다."]], [["“Halloa!”", "\"어이!\""], ["said he, “young fellow!”", "그가 말했다. \"젊은 친구!\""]], [["Halloa being a general observation which I had usually observed to be best answered by itself, I said, “Halloa!”", "어이라는 말은 으레 그 자신으로 되받는 것이 상책이라고 나는 늘 알고 있었으므로, 나도 \"어이!\" 하고 말했다."], ["politely omitting young fellow.", "젊은 친구라는 말은 정중히 빼놓고서."]], [["“Who let you in?”", "\"누가 너를 들여보냈지?\""], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."]], [["“Miss Estella.”", "\"에스텔러 아가씨.\""]], [["“Who gave you leave to prowl about?”", "\"누가 너더러 어슬렁거려도 좋다고 했고?\""]], [["“Miss Estella.”", "\"에스텔러 아가씨.\""]], [["“Come and fight,” said the pale young gentleman.", "\"이리 와서 한판 붙자.\" 창백한 도련님이 말했다."]], [["What could I do but follow him?", "그를 따라가는 것 말고 내가 무엇을 할 수 있었겠는가?"], ["I have often asked myself the question since; but what else could I do?", "그 후로 나는 종종 그 물음을 스스로에게 던져 보았지만, 달리 무엇을 할 수 있었겠는가?"], ["His manner was so final, and I was so astonished, that I followed where he led, as if I had been under a spell.", "그의 태도가 어찌나 단호하고 나는 어찌나 놀랐던지, 나는 무슨 마법에라도 걸린 듯 그가 이끄는 곳으로 따라갔다."]], [["“Stop a minute, though,”", "\"잠깐, 그런데,\""], ["he said, wheeling round before we had gone many paces.", "우리가 몇 걸음 가기도 전에 그가 홱 돌아서며 말했다."], ["“I ought to give you a reason for fighting, too.", "\"너한테 싸울 명분도 줘야겠지."], ["There it is!”", "자, 받아라!\""], ["In a most irritating manner he instantly slapped his hands against one another, daintily flung one of his legs up behind him, pulled my hair, slapped his hands again, dipped his head, and butted it into my stomach.", "더없이 약을 올리는 태도로 그는 대뜸 두 손을 마주쳤다가, 한쪽 다리를 뒤로 우아하게 차올렸다가, 내 머리카락을 잡아당기고, 다시 두 손을 마주치고, 머리를 숙이더니, 그것으로 내 배를 들이받았다."]], [["The bull-like proceeding last mentioned, besides that it was unquestionably to be regarded in the light of a liberty, was particularly disagreeable just after bread and meat.", "방금 말한 황소 같은 짓거리는, 그것이 의심할 여지 없이 무례한 행동으로 여겨질 만한 것이었던 데다, 빵과 고기를 먹은 직후라 유난히 불쾌했다."], ["I therefore hit out at him and was going to hit out again, when he said, “Aha!", "그래서 나는 그를 향해 한 대 날렸고 또 한 대를 날리려는데, 그가 \"아하!"], ["Would you?”", "그럴 테냐?\""], ["and began dancing backwards and forwards in a manner quite unparalleled within my limited experience.", "하고는, 내 좁은 경험으로는 견줄 데 없는 방식으로 앞뒤로 춤추듯 움직이기 시작했다."]], [["“Laws of the game!”", "\"시합의 규칙대로!\""], ["said he. Here, he skipped from his left leg on to his right.", "그가 말했다. 여기서 그는 왼발에서 오른발로 깡충 뛰었다."], ["“Regular rules!”", "\"정식 규칙대로!\""], ["Here, he skipped from his right leg on to his left.", "여기서 그는 오른발에서 왼발로 깡충 뛰었다."], ["“Come to the ground, and go through the preliminaries!”", "\"자리를 잡고, 예비 절차를 밟자고!\""], ["Here, he dodged backwards and forwards, and did all sorts of things while I looked helplessly at him.", "여기서 그는 앞뒤로 요리조리 피하며 온갖 동작을 다 했는데, 그동안 나는 어쩔 줄 모르고 그를 바라보았다."]], [["I was secretly afraid of him when I saw him so dexterous; but I felt morally and physically convinced that his light head of hair could have had no business in the pit of my stomach, and that I had a right to consider it irrelevant when so obtruded on my attention.", "그가 그토록 날렵한 것을 보니 속으로 그가 무서워졌으나, 그의 옅은 빛깔 머리통이 내 명치를 들이받을 까닭이 도무지 없었으며, 그것이 그렇게 내 주의에 들이밀어진 이상 나는 그것을 당치 않은 일로 여길 권리가 있다고 나는 도덕적으로나 육체적으로나 확신했다."], ["Therefore, I followed him without a word, to a retired nook of the garden, formed by the junction of two walls and screened by some rubbish.", "그리하여 나는 한마디도 없이 그를 따라, 두 담장이 만나는 자리에 이루어진, 잡동사니로 가려진 정원의 외진 구석으로 갔다."], ["On his asking me if I was satisfied with the ground, and on my replying Yes, he begged my leave to absent himself for a moment, and quickly returned with a bottle of water and a sponge dipped in vinegar.", "그가 내게 이 자리가 마음에 드느냐고 묻기에 그렇다고 대답하자, 그는 잠시 자리를 비우겠노라 양해를 구하더니, 물 한 병과 식초에 적신 해면을 들고 금세 돌아왔다."], ["“Available for both,”", "\"둘 다 쓸 수 있어.\""], ["he said, placing these against the wall.", "그가 그것들을 담벼락에 기대 놓으며 말했다."], ["And then fell to pulling off, not only his jacket and waistcoat, but his shirt too, in a manner at once light-hearted, business-like, and bloodthirsty.", "그러고는 재킷과 조끼뿐 아니라 셔츠까지 벗어 던지기 시작했는데, 그 태도가 명랑하면서도 사무적이고 또한 피에 굶주린 듯했다."]], [["Although he did not look very healthy,—having pimples on his face, and a breaking out at his mouth,—these dreadful preparations quite appalled me.", "그가 그리 건강해 보이지는 않았음에도 — 얼굴에 여드름이 나 있고, 입가가 헐어 있었으니 — 이 끔찍한 채비는 나를 자못 질리게 했다."], ["I judged him to be about my own age, but he was much taller, and he had a way of spinning himself about that was full of appearance.", "내가 보기에 그는 나와 비슷한 또래였으나, 키는 나보다 훨씬 컸고, 몸을 빙빙 돌리는 품이 자못 그럴싸했다."], ["For the rest, he was a young gentleman in a grey suit (when not denuded for battle), with his elbows, knees, wrists, and heels considerably in advance of the rest of him as to development.", "그 밖에는, 그는 (싸우려고 옷을 벗지 않았을 때는) 회색 양복을 입은 도련님으로, 팔꿈치며 무릎이며 손목이며 발꿈치가 발육에 있어 몸의 나머지보다 한참 앞서 있었다."]], [["My heart failed me when I saw him squaring at me with every demonstration of mechanical nicety, and eyeing my anatomy as if he were minutely choosing his bone.", "기계처럼 정교한 온갖 시늉으로 내게 자세를 잡고, 마치 어느 뼈를 칠지 꼼꼼히 고르기라도 하듯 내 몸뚱이를 노려보는 그를 보니, 나는 가슴이 철렁했다."], ["I never have been so surprised in my life, as I was when I let out the first blow, and saw him lying on his back, looking up at me with a bloody nose and his face exceedingly fore-shortened.", "내가 첫 일격을 날렸을 때, 그가 코피를 흘리고 얼굴이 잔뜩 일그러진 채 벌렁 나자빠져 나를 올려다보는 것을 보았을 때만큼, 내 평생 그토록 놀란 적은 없었다."]], [["But, he was on his feet directly, and after sponging himself with a great show of dexterity began squaring again.", "그러나 그는 곧장 일어서더니, 더없이 날렵한 솜씨를 한껏 과시하며 해면으로 제 몸을 닦고는, 다시 자세를 잡기 시작했다."], ["The second greatest surprise I have ever had in my life was seeing him on his back again, looking up at me out of a black eye.", "그가 다시 시퍼렇게 멍든 눈으로 나를 올려다보며 벌렁 나자빠진 것을 본 것이, 내 평생 두 번째로 가장 크게 놀란 일이었다."]], [["His spirit inspired me with great respect.", "그의 기개는 내게 커다란 존경심을 불러일으켰다."], ["He seemed to have no strength, and he never once hit me hard, and he was always knocked down; but he would be up again in a moment, sponging himself or drinking out of the water-bottle, with the greatest satisfaction in seconding himself according to form, and then came at me with an air and a show that made me believe he really was going to do for me at last.", "그는 도무지 힘이라곤 없어 보였고, 단 한 번도 나를 세게 치지 못했으며, 늘 얻어맞고 나가떨어졌다. 하지만 그는 금세 다시 일어나, 더없이 흡족한 표정으로 격식에 맞게 스스로를 챙기며 해면으로 몸을 닦거나 물병의 물을 들이켜고는, 마치 이번에야말로 정말로 나를 끝장낼 것 같은 기세와 시늉으로 내게 덤벼들었다."], ["He got heavily bruised, for I am sorry to record that the more I hit him, the harder I hit him; but he came up again and again and again, until at last he got a bad fall with the back of his head against the wall.", "그는 심하게 멍이 들었으니, 유감스럽게도 기록하건대 내가 그를 칠수록 더 세게 쳤기 때문이다. 그러나 그는 거듭거듭 또거듭 일어났고, 마침내 뒤통수를 담벼락에 호되게 부딪히며 나가떨어졌다."], ["Even after that crisis in our affairs, he got up and turned round and round confusedly a few times, not knowing where I was; but finally went on his knees to his sponge and threw it up: at the same time panting out, “That means you have won.”", "우리 사이의 그 위기를 겪고 나서도, 그는 일어나 내가 어디 있는지도 모른 채 어리둥절하게 몇 차례 빙빙 돌더니, 끝내 제 해면을 향해 무릎을 꿇고 그것을 던져 올렸다. 그와 동시에 숨을 헐떡이며 내뱉었다. \"이건 네가 이겼다는 뜻이야.\""]], [["He seemed so brave and innocent, that although I had not proposed the contest, I felt but a gloomy satisfaction in my victory.", "그가 어찌나 용감하고 순진해 보이던지, 내가 이 시합을 제안한 것도 아니건만, 나는 내 승리에 그저 우울한 만족밖에 느끼지 못했다."], ["Indeed, I go so far as to hope that I regarded myself while dressing as a species of savage young wolf or other wild beast.", "실은, 나는 옷을 입으면서 스스로를 일종의 야만스러운 어린 늑대나 다른 들짐승쯤으로 여겼다고까지 말하고 싶다."], ["However, I got dressed, darkly wiping my sanguinary face at intervals, and I said, “Can I help you?”", "어쨌거나 나는 옷을 입고, 이따금 피투성이 얼굴을 침울하게 닦아 가며, \"도와줄까?\" 하고 말했고, 그는 \"아니, 됐어\""], ["and he said “No thankee,”", "하고 말했으며, 나는 \"그럼 잘 있어\""], ["and I said “Good afternoon,” and he said “Same to you.”", "하고 말했고, 그는 \"너도 잘 가\" 하고 말했다."]], [["When I got into the courtyard, I found Estella waiting with the keys.", "안뜰로 들어서니, 에스텔러가 열쇠를 들고 기다리고 있었다."], ["But she neither asked me where I had been, nor why I had kept her waiting; and there was a bright flush upon her face, as though something had happened to delight her.", "그러나 그녀는 내가 어디 있었는지도, 왜 자기를 기다리게 했는지도 묻지 않았다. 그녀의 얼굴에는 무슨 기쁜 일이라도 생긴 양 환한 홍조가 떠올라 있었다."], ["Instead of going straight to the gate, too, she stepped back into the passage, and beckoned me.", "게다가 그녀는 곧장 문으로 가는 대신, 복도 안쪽으로 물러서더니 내게 손짓을 했다."]], [["“Come here!", "\"이리 와!"], ["You may kiss me, if you like.”", "원한다면 내게 입맞춰도 좋아.\""]], [["I kissed her cheek as she turned it to me.", "나는 그녀가 내 쪽으로 돌린 뺨에 입을 맞추었다."], ["I think I would have gone through a great deal to kiss her cheek.", "그녀의 뺨에 입맞추기 위해서라면 나는 어지간한 일이라도 견뎌 냈으리라 생각한다."], ["But I felt that the kiss was given to the coarse common boy as a piece of money might have been, and that it was worth nothing.", "그러나 나는 그 입맞춤이 동전 한 닢을 던져 주듯 천하고 평범한 사내아이에게 베풀어진 것임을, 그리고 그것이 아무 값어치 없는 것임을 느꼈다."]], [["What with the birthday visitors, and what with the cards, and what with the fight, my stay had lasted so long, that when I neared home the light on the spit of sand off the point on the marshes was gleaming against a black night-sky, and Joe’s furnace was flinging a path of fire across the road.", "생일날 찾아온 손님들이며, 카드놀이며, 그 싸움이며 해서, 내가 머문 시간이 어찌나 길어졌던지, 집 가까이 이르렀을 때는 늪지의 곶 너머 모래톱 위의 등불이 검은 밤하늘을 배경으로 가물거리고 있었고, 조의 화덕은 길 건너로 불의 길을 던지고 있었다."]], [["# Chapter XII.", "# Chapter XII."]], [["My mind grew very uneasy on the subject of the pale young gentleman.", "창백한 얼굴의 도련님 문제로 내 마음은 몹시 불안해졌다."], ["The more I thought of the fight, and recalled the pale young gentleman on his back in various stages of puffy and incrimsoned countenance, the more certain it appeared that something would be done to me.", "그 싸움을 생각하면 할수록, 그리고 그 창백한 도련님이 등을 댄 채 여러 단계로 부어오르고 피투성이가 되던 얼굴을 떠올릴수록, 나에게 무슨 일이 일어나리라는 것이 점점 더 확실하게 느껴졌다."], ["I felt that the pale young gentleman’s blood was on my head, and that the Law would avenge it.", "나는 그 창백한 도련님의 피가 내 머리 위에 있고, 법이 그 피의 복수를 하리라는 느낌이 들었다."], ["Without having any definite idea of the penalties I had incurred, it was clear to me that village boys could not go stalking about the country, ravaging the houses of gentlefolks and pitching into the studious youth of England, without laying themselves open to severe punishment.", "내가 저지른 형벌에 대해 뚜렷한 관념은 없었으나, 시골 소년이 들판을 어슬렁거리며 신사 양반들의 집을 약탈하고 영국의 면학하는 청년을 두들겨 패면서 무거운 처벌에 노출되지 않을 수는 없다는 것만은 분명했다."], ["For some days, I even kept close at home, and looked out at the kitchen door with the greatest caution and trepidation before going on an errand, lest the officers of the County Jail should pounce upon me.", "며칠 동안 나는 집 안에만 틀어박혀 있었고, 심부름을 가기 전에는 주(州) 감옥의 관리들이 나에게 덮쳐들지 않을까 하여 더없이 조심스럽고 떨리는 마음으로 부엌문 밖을 내다보았다."], ["The pale young gentleman’s nose had stained my trousers, and I tried to wash out that evidence of my guilt in the dead of night.", "그 창백한 도련님의 코피가 내 바지에 얼룩을 남긴 탓에, 나는 깊은 밤중에 그 죄의 증거를 씻어 내려 애썼다."], ["I had cut my knuckles against the pale young gentleman’s teeth, and I twisted my imagination into a thousand tangles, as I devised incredible ways of accounting for that damnatory circumstance when I should be haled before the Judges.", "나는 그 창백한 도련님의 이에 부딪쳐 손가락 관절이 까졌는데, 판사들 앞에 끌려갔을 때 그 죄증이 될 만한 정황을 둘러댈 믿기 어려운 방도들을 꾸며 내느라 내 상상력을 천 갈래로 비틀어 댔다."]], [["When the day came round for my return to the scene of the deed of violence, my terrors reached their height.", "그 폭력 행위의 현장으로 다시 돌아갈 날이 돌아오자, 나의 공포는 절정에 이르렀다."], ["Whether myrmidons of Justice, especially sent down from London, would be lying in ambush behind the gate;—whether Miss Havisham, preferring to take personal vengeance for an outrage done to her house, might rise in those grave-clothes of hers, draw a pistol, and shoot me dead:—whether suborned boys—a numerous band of mercenaries—might be engaged to fall upon me in the brewery, and cuff me until I was no more;—it was high testimony to my confidence in the spirit of the pale young gentleman, that I never imagined him accessory to these retaliations; they always came into my mind as the acts of injudicious relatives of his, goaded on by the state of his visage and an indignant sympathy with the family features.", "런던에서 특별히 파견된 정의의 졸개들이 문 뒤에 매복해 있을지도 모른다는 것, 자기 집에 가해진 능욕에 대해 친히 복수하기를 더 좋아한 해비셤 양이 그 수의(壽衣) 같은 옷을 걸친 채 일어나 권총을 빼 들어 나를 쏘아 죽일지도 모른다는 것, 매수당한 소년들 ─ 수많은 용병 무리 ─ 이 양조장에서 나에게 달려들어 내가 숨이 끊어질 때까지 두들겨 패도록 고용될지도 모른다는 것, 이런 생각들이 떠올랐지만 그 창백한 도련님에게 그 보복의 공모자라는 혐의를 한 번도 씌우지 않았다는 것은 내가 그의 기개를 얼마나 신뢰했는지를 잘 증언해 준다. 그것들은 언제나, 그의 얼굴 꼴에 부추김을 받고 집안 내력의 생김새에 분개한 동정심을 품은, 그의 분별없는 친척들의 소행으로 내 머릿속에 떠올랐다."]], [["However, go to Miss Havisham’s I must, and go I did.", "그러나 해비셤 양에게 가야만 했고, 또 갔다."], ["And behold!", "그런데 보라!"], ["nothing came of the late struggle.", "지난번 다툼에서는 아무 일도 일어나지 않았다."], ["It was not alluded to in any way, and no pale young gentleman was to be discovered on the premises.", "그 일은 어떤 식으로도 언급되지 않았고, 그 집 어디에서도 창백한 도련님은 찾을 수 없었다."], ["I found the same gate open, and I explored the garden, and even looked in at the windows of the detached house; but my view was suddenly stopped by the closed shutters within, and all was lifeless.", "나는 똑같은 문이 열려 있는 것을 발견했고, 정원을 살펴보았으며, 심지어 별채의 창문 안까지 들여다보았다. 그러나 안쪽에 닫힌 덧창에 시야가 갑자기 막혔고, 모든 것이 생기 없이 죽어 있었다."], ["Only in the corner where the combat had taken place could I detect any evidence of the young gentleman’s existence.", "오직 그 격투가 벌어진 구석에서만 그 도련님이 존재했다는 어떤 증거를 찾아낼 수 있었다."], ["There were traces of his gore in that spot, and I covered them with garden-mould from the eye of man.", "그 자리에는 그의 핏자국이 남아 있었고, 나는 사람들의 눈에 띄지 않도록 그것을 정원의 흙으로 덮어 버렸다."]], [["On the broad landing between Miss Havisham’s own room and that other room in which the long table was laid out, I saw a garden-chair,—a light chair on wheels, that you pushed from behind.", "해비셤 양의 방과, 긴 식탁이 차려진 그 다른 방 사이의 널찍한 층계참에서 나는 정원용 의자를 보았다. 뒤에서 미는, 바퀴 달린 가벼운 의자였다."], ["It had been placed there since my last visit, and I entered, that same day, on a regular occupation of pushing Miss Havisham in this chair (when she was tired of walking with her hand upon my shoulder) round her own room, and across the landing, and round the other room.", "그것은 내가 지난번 다녀간 뒤에 거기 놓인 것이었는데, 바로 그날부터 나는 (해비셤 양이 내 어깨에 손을 얹고 걷는 데 지치면) 이 의자에 그녀를 태우고 자기 방을 돌고, 층계참을 가로지르고, 다른 방을 도는 일을 규칙적으로 맡게 되었다."], ["Over and over and over again, we would make these journeys, and sometimes they would last as long as three hours at a stretch.", "우리는 이런 여정을 거듭하고 또 거듭했으며, 때로는 한 번에 세 시간이나 이어지기도 했다."], ["I insensibly fall into a general mention of these journeys as numerous, because it was at once settled that I should return every alternate day at noon for these purposes, and because I am now going to sum up a period of at least eight or ten months.", "나도 모르게 이 여정을 두루뭉술하게 여러 차례라고 언급하게 되는 것은, 이 일을 위해 격일로 정오마다 다시 오기로 곧바로 정해졌기 때문이고, 또 지금 내가 적어도 여덟 달이나 열 달에 이르는 기간을 한꺼번에 요약하려 하기 때문이다."]], [["As we began to be more used to one another, Miss Havisham talked more to me, and asked me such questions as what had I learnt and what was I going to be?", "서로에게 점점 익숙해지면서 해비셤 양은 나에게 말을 더 많이 걸었고, 무엇을 배웠으며 무엇이 될 작정이냐 같은 질문을 던지곤 했다."], ["I told her I was going to be apprenticed to Joe, I believed; and I enlarged upon my knowing nothing and wanting to know everything, in the hope that she might offer some help towards that desirable end.", "나는 아마 조에게 도제로 들어가게 될 것 같다고 대답했다. 그리고 그녀가 그 바람직한 목적을 향해 무슨 도움을 줄지도 모른다는 희망을 품고서, 내가 아무것도 모르며 모든 것을 알고 싶어 한다는 점을 늘어놓았다."], ["But she did not; on the contrary, she seemed to prefer my being ignorant.", "그러나 그녀는 그러지 않았다. 오히려 내가 무지한 편을 더 좋아하는 듯했다."], ["Neither did she ever give me any money,—or anything but my daily dinner,—nor ever stipulate that I should be paid for my services.", "또한 그녀는 나에게 돈을 준 적도 없고 ─ 매일의 저녁 식사 말고는 그 무엇도 준 적이 없으며 ─ 내가 봉사한 대가로 보수를 받아야 한다고 명시한 적도 없었다."]], [["Estella was always about, and always let me in and out, but never told me I might kiss her again.", "에스텔라는 늘 주변에 있었고, 늘 나를 들이고 내보내 주었으나, 다시 입을 맞춰도 좋다는 말은 결코 하지 않았다."], ["Sometimes, she would coldly tolerate me; sometimes, she would condescend to me; sometimes, she would be quite familiar with me; sometimes, she would tell me energetically that she hated me.", "어떤 때는 차갑게 나를 참아 주었고, 어떤 때는 나에게 너그럽게 굴었으며, 어떤 때는 아주 친근하게 대했고, 또 어떤 때는 나를 미워한다고 기운차게 말하기도 했다."], ["Miss Havisham would often ask me in a whisper, or when we were alone, “Does she grow prettier and prettier, Pip?”", "해비셤 양은 자주 속삭이듯, 혹은 우리 둘만 있을 때 나에게 묻곤 했다. \"저 애가 점점 더 예뻐지지 않니, 핍?\""], ["And when I said yes (for indeed she did), would seem to enjoy it greedily.", "그리고 내가 그렇다고 대답하면(실제로 그랬으니까), 그것을 탐욕스럽게 음미하는 것 같았다."], ["Also, when we played at cards Miss Havisham would look on, with a miserly relish of Estella’s moods, whatever they were.", "또 우리가 카드놀이를 할 때면 해비셤 양은 에스텔라의 기분이 어떻든 그것을 인색하게 즐기며 지켜보았다."], ["And sometimes, when her moods were so many and so contradictory of one another that I was puzzled what to say or do, Miss Havisham would embrace her with lavish fondness, murmuring something in her ear that sounded like “Break their hearts my pride and hope, break their hearts and have no mercy!”", "그리고 때로는 에스텔라의 기분이 너무 변덕스럽고 서로 모순되어 내가 무슨 말을 하고 무엇을 해야 할지 어쩔 줄 모르고 있으면, 해비셤 양은 그녀를 아낌없는 애정으로 끌어안고는 그 귀에 무언가를 속삭였는데, 그것은 마치 \"그놈들의 가슴을 찢어 놓아라, 내 자랑이자 희망아, 그놈들의 가슴을 찢고 자비를 베풀지 마라!\" 하는 소리로 들렸다."]], [["There was a song Joe used to hum fragments of at the forge, of which the burden was Old Clem.", "조가 대장간에서 토막토막 흥얼거리던 노래가 하나 있었는데, 그 후렴이 올드 클렘이었다."], ["This was not a very ceremonious way of rendering homage to a patron saint, but I believe Old Clem stood in that relation towards smiths.", "수호성인에게 경의를 표하기에는 그리 의례적인 방식이 아니었으나, 올드 클렘이 대장장이들에게 그러한 관계에 있었던 모양이다."], ["It was a song that imitated the measure of beating upon iron, and was a mere lyrical excuse for the introduction of Old Clem’s respected name.", "그것은 쇠를 두드리는 박자를 흉내 낸 노래로, 올드 클렘이라는 존귀한 이름을 끌어들이기 위한 한낱 서정적인 구실에 지나지 않았다. 이런 식이었다."], ["Thus, you were to hammer boys round—Old Clem!", "자, 둘러서서 두드려라, 얘들아 ─ 올드 클렘!"], ["With a thump and a sound—Old Clem!", "쾅 소리 한 번 울려라 ─ 올드 클렘!"], ["Beat it out, beat it out—Old Clem!", "두들겨라, 두들겨라 ─ 올드 클렘!"], ["With a clink for the stout—Old Clem!", "튼튼한 놈을 위해 쩌렁 한 번 ─ 올드 클렘!"], ["Blow the fire, blow the fire—Old Clem!", "불을 지펴라, 불을 지펴라 ─ 올드 클렘!"], ["Roaring dryer, soaring higher—Old Clem! One day soon after the appearance of the chair, Miss Havisham suddenly saying to me, with the impatient movement of her fingers, “There, there, there!", "더 메마르게 으르렁대고, 더 높이 치솟아라 ─ 올드 클렘! 그 의자가 나타나고 얼마 안 된 어느 날, 해비셤 양이 손가락을 안달하듯 까딱이며 갑자기 나에게 \"자, 자, 자!"], ["Sing!” I was surprised into crooning this ditty as I pushed her over the floor.", "노래 불러라!\" 하고 말했고, 나는 그녀를 마룻바닥 위로 밀고 다니면서 얼떨결에 이 가락을 흥얼거리게 되었다."], ["It happened so to catch her fancy that she took it up in a low brooding voice as if she were singing in her sleep.", "그것이 어쩌다 그녀의 마음에 들었던지, 그녀는 마치 잠결에 노래하듯 낮고 골똘한 목소리로 그것을 따라 부르기 시작했다."], ["After that, it became customary with us to have it as we moved about, and Estella would often join in; though the whole strain was so subdued, even when there were three of us, that it made less noise in the grim old house than the lightest breath of wind.", "그 뒤로 우리는 돌아다니며 그 노래를 부르는 것이 관례처럼 되었고, 에스텔라도 종종 함께 부르곤 했다. 비록 셋이 모였을 때조차 그 가락 전체가 어찌나 나직했던지, 그 음산한 옛 집 안에서 그것은 가장 미약한 바람의 숨결보다도 작은 소리를 냈지만."]], [["What could I become with these surroundings?", "이런 환경에서 나는 무엇이 될 수 있었을까?"], ["How could my character fail to be influenced by them?", "어떻게 내 성격이 그것들의 영향을 받지 않을 수 있었을까?"], ["Is it to be wondered at if my thoughts were dazed, as my eyes were, when I came out into the natural light from the misty yellow rooms?", "안개 낀 누런 방들에서 자연의 빛 속으로 나왔을 때, 내 눈이 그러했듯 내 생각마저 멍해졌다 한들 그것이 이상한 일일까?"]], [["Perhaps I might have told Joe about the pale young gentleman, if I had not previously been betrayed into those enormous inventions to which I had confessed.", "만약 내가 앞서 고백한 그 엄청난 거짓말 속으로 미리 빠져들지만 않았더라면, 나는 어쩌면 조에게 그 창백한 도련님 이야기를 했을지도 모른다."], ["Under the circumstances, I felt that Joe could hardly fail to discern in the pale young gentleman, an appropriate passenger to be put into the black velvet coach; therefore, I said nothing of him.", "사정이 그러했으니, 조는 그 창백한 도련님을 검은 벨벳 마차에 태우기에 알맞은 또 하나의 승객으로 알아볼 것이 틀림없다는 느낌이 들었다. 그래서 나는 그에 대해 아무 말도 하지 않았다."], ["Besides, that shrinking from having Miss Havisham and Estella discussed, which had come upon me in the beginning, grew much more potent as time went on.", "게다가 처음부터 나를 사로잡았던, 해비셤 양과 에스텔라가 입에 오르내리는 것에 대한 그 움츠러드는 마음은 시간이 갈수록 훨씬 더 강해졌다."], ["I reposed complete confidence in no one but Biddy; but I told poor Biddy everything.", "나는 비디 말고는 그 누구에게도 온전히 마음을 내맡기지 못했다. 그러나 가엾은 비디에게는 모든 것을 털어놓았다."], ["Why it came natural to me to do so, and why Biddy had a deep concern in everything I told her, I did not know then, though I think I know now.", "어째서 그렇게 하는 것이 나에게 자연스러웠는지, 그리고 어째서 비디가 내가 들려주는 모든 일에 깊은 관심을 가졌는지, 그때는 알지 못했으나 지금은 안다고 생각한다."]], [["Meanwhile, councils went on in the kitchen at home, fraught with almost insupportable aggravation to my exasperated spirit.", "그러는 동안 집 부엌에서는 평의회가 계속되었는데, 격앙된 내 심정에는 거의 견디기 힘든 부아를 안겨 주는 것이었다."], ["That ass, Pumblechook, used often to come over of a night for the purpose of discussing my prospects with my sister; and I really do believe (to this hour with less penitence than I ought to feel), that if these hands could have taken a linchpin out of his chaise-cart, they would have done it.", "그 멍청이 펌블추크는 밤이면 자주 건너와 누나와 함께 내 장래를 논하곤 했다. 그리고 나는 정말로 믿는다(지금 이 순간까지도 마땅히 느껴야 할 만큼의 뉘우침은 없이), 만약 이 두 손이 그의 이륜마차에서 굴대 빗장을 빼낼 수 있었다면 정말로 그렇게 했으리라는 것을."], ["The miserable man was a man of that confined stolidity of mind, that he could not discuss my prospects without having me before him,—as it were, to operate upon,—and he would drag me up from my stool (usually by the collar) where I was quiet in a corner, and, putting me before the fire as if I were going to be cooked, would begin by saying, “Now, Mum, here is this boy!", "그 가련한 자는 정신머리가 어찌나 옹졸하고 둔했던지, 나를 앞에 세워 놓지 않고서는 ─ 말하자면 나를 가지고 시술이라도 하듯 ─ 내 장래를 논할 수가 없었다. 그래서 그는 한쪽 구석에서 얌전히 있는 나를 걸상에서(대개는 멱살을 잡고) 끌어올려, 마치 나를 요리라도 할 것처럼 난롯불 앞에 세워 놓고는 이렇게 운을 떼곤 했다. \"자, 부인, 여기 이 아이가 있습니다!"], ["Here is this boy which you brought up by hand.", "손수 길러 내신 바로 이 아이 말입니다."], ["Hold up your head, boy, and be forever grateful unto them which so did do.", "고개를 들어라, 얘야, 그리고 그렇게 해 주신 분들께 영원히 감사할 줄 알아야지."], ["Now, Mum, with respections to this boy!”", "자, 부인, 이 아이에 관해서 말하자면!\""], ["And then he would rumple my hair the wrong way,—which from my earliest remembrance, as already hinted, I have in my soul denied the right of any fellow-creature to do,—and would hold me before him by the sleeve,—a spectacle of imbecility only to be equalled by himself.", "그러고는 내 머리카락을 결과 반대로 헝클어뜨리곤 했는데 ─ 앞서 넌지시 밝혔듯 나는 아주 어린 시절의 기억 이래로, 그 어떤 동족에게도 그럴 권리가 있다는 것을 내 영혼으로 부정해 왔다 ─ 그러고는 소맷자락을 잡고 나를 자기 앞에 붙들어 두었으니, 그 어리석음의 광경에 견줄 만한 것은 오직 그 자신뿐이었다."]], [["Then, he and my sister would pair off in such nonsensical speculations about Miss Havisham, and about what she would do with me and for me, that I used to want—quite painfully—to burst into spiteful tears, fly at Pumblechook, and pummel him all over.", "그런 다음 그와 누나는 해비셤 양에 관해, 그리고 그녀가 나에게 무엇을 해 줄 것이며 나를 위해 무엇을 해 줄 것인가에 관해, 어찌나 터무니없는 억측을 주거니 받거니 했던지, 나는 ─ 자못 고통스럽게도 ─ 분한 눈물을 왈칵 쏟으며 펌블추크에게 달려들어 그를 온통 두들겨 패고 싶어지곤 했다."], ["In these dialogues, my sister spoke to me as if she were morally wrenching one of my teeth out at every reference; while Pumblechook himself, self-constituted my patron, would sit supervising me with a depreciatory eye, like the architect of my fortunes who thought himself engaged on a very unremunerative job.", "이런 대화 속에서 누나는 나를 언급할 때마다 마치 내 이를 하나씩 모질게 뽑아내기라도 하듯 나에게 말을 건넸고, 한편 스스로 내 후견인을 자처한 펌블추크는, 도무지 수지가 맞지 않는 일을 떠맡았다고 여기는 내 운명의 건축가라도 되는 양, 깔보는 눈으로 나를 감독하며 앉아 있었다."]], [["In these discussions, Joe bore no part.", "이런 논의에 조는 끼어들지 않았다."], ["But he was often talked at, while they were in progress, by reason of Mrs. Joe’s perceiving that he was not favourable to my being taken from the forge.", "그러나 그것이 진행되는 동안, 내가 대장간에서 떨어져 나가는 것을 조가 탐탁지 않게 여긴다는 것을 조 부인이 알아챈 탓에, 조는 자주 그 화살의 표적이 되었다."], ["I was fully old enough now to be apprenticed to Joe; and when Joe sat with the poker on his knees thoughtfully raking out the ashes between the lower bars, my sister would so distinctly construe that innocent action into opposition on his part, that she would dive at him, take the poker out of his hands, shake him, and put it away.", "이제 나는 조에게 도제로 들어가기에 충분한 나이였다. 그리고 조가 부지깽이를 무릎에 얹고 골똘히 아래쪽 살창 사이의 재를 긁어내고 있으면, 누나는 그 천진한 행동을 어찌나 또렷이 조의 반대 표시로 해석했던지, 그에게 와락 달려들어 부지깽이를 손에서 빼앗고 그를 흔들어 댄 뒤 그것을 치워 버리곤 했다."], ["There was a most irritating end to every one of these debates.", "이런 논쟁은 하나같이 더없이 약을 올리는 식으로 끝이 났다."], ["All in a moment, with nothing to lead up to it, my sister would stop herself in a yawn, and catching sight of me as it were incidentally, would swoop upon me with, “Come!", "어떤 빌미도 없이 별안간, 누나는 하품을 하다 말고 멈추더니, 마치 우연인 듯 나를 발견하고는 나에게 달려들며 이렇게 쏘아붙이곤 했다. \"이리 와! 네 꼴은 이제 됐다!"], ["there’s enough of you!", "너는 가서 잠이나 자!"], ["You get along to bed; you’ve given trouble enough for one night, I hope!” As if I had besought them as a favour to bother my life out.", "하룻밤에 충분히 말썽을 부렸을 텐데, 그렇겠지!\" 마치 내가 제발 내 인생을 들들 볶아 달라고 그들에게 간청이라도 한 것처럼."]], [["We went on in this way for a long time, and it seemed likely that we should continue to go on in this way for a long time, when one day Miss Havisham stopped short as she and I were walking, she leaning on my shoulder; and said with some displeasure,—", "우리는 오랫동안 이런 식으로 지냈고, 앞으로도 오랫동안 이런 식으로 지내게 될 듯싶었다. 그러던 어느 날, 해비셤 양은 그녀가 내 어깨에 기댄 채 나와 함께 걷다가 우뚝 멈춰 서더니, 약간 못마땅한 투로 말했다."]], [["“You are growing tall, Pip!”", "\"너 키가 자라고 있구나, 핍!\""]], [["I thought it best to hint, through the medium of a meditative look, that this might be occasioned by circumstances over which I had no control.", "나는 이것이 내가 어찌할 수 없는 사정에서 비롯된 일인지도 모른다는 뜻을, 생각에 잠긴 듯한 눈빛을 통해 넌지시 비치는 것이 상책이라고 여겼다."]], [["She said no more at the time; but she presently stopped and looked at me again; and presently again; and after that, looked frowning and moody.", "그녀는 그때는 더 말하지 않았다. 그러나 곧 멈춰 서서 다시 나를 바라보았고, 또 곧 다시 그러했으며, 그러고 나서는 얼굴을 찌푸린 채 시무룩한 표정을 지었다."], ["On the next day of my attendance, when our usual exercise was over, and I had landed her at her dressing-table, she stayed me with a movement of her impatient fingers:—", "다음번에 내가 시중을 들러 간 날, 우리의 평소 운동이 끝나고 내가 그녀를 화장대 앞에 데려다 놓자, 그녀는 안달하는 손가락을 움직여 나를 멈춰 세웠다."]], [["“Tell me the name again of that blacksmith of yours.”", "\"네 그 대장장이라는 사람 이름을 다시 한 번 말해 보아라.\""]], [["“Joe Gargery, ma’am.”", "\"조 가저리예요, 부인.\""]], [["“Meaning the master you were to be apprenticed to?”", "\"네가 도제로 들어갈 그 주인 말이지?\""]], [["“Yes, Miss Havisham.”", "\"네, 해비셤 양.\""]], [["“You had better be apprenticed at once.", "\"당장 도제로 들어가는 게 좋겠다."], ["Would Gargery come here with you, and bring your indentures, do you think?”", "가저리가 너와 함께 여기 와서 네 도제 계약서를 가져올 것 같으냐?\""]], [["I signified that I had no doubt he would take it as an honour to be asked.", "나는 그렇게 청을 받는 것을 그가 영광으로 여길 것이 틀림없다는 뜻을 내비쳤다."]], [["“Then let him come.”", "\"그럼 그를 오라고 해라.\""]], [["“At any particular time, Miss Havisham?”", "\"특별히 정한 때가 있나요, 해비셤 양?\""]], [["“There, there!", "\"이런, 이런!"], ["I know nothing about times.", "나는 때 같은 건 모른다."], ["Let him come soon, and come along with you.”", "곧 오라고 해라, 그리고 너와 함께 오게 해라.\""]], [["When I got home at night, and delivered this message for Joe, my sister “went on the Rampage,”", "그날 밤 집에 돌아와 이 전갈을 조에게 전했더니, 누나는 그 어느 때보다도 무섭게 \"길길이 날뛰었다.\""], ["in a more alarming degree than at any previous period. She asked me and Joe whether we supposed she was door-mats under our feet, and how we dared to use her so, and what company we graciously thought she was fit for?", "그녀는 나와 조에게 자기가 우리 발밑에 깔린 현관 깔개쯤 되는 줄 아느냐고, 감히 어떻게 자기를 그렇게 부려 먹을 수 있느냐고, 우리가 황송하게도 자기를 어떤 패거리에나 어울리는 여자로 여기느냐고 따져 물었다."], ["When she had exhausted a torrent of such inquiries, she threw a candlestick at Joe, burst into a loud sobbing, got out the dustpan,—which was always a very bad sign,—put on her coarse apron, and began cleaning up to a terrible extent.", "그런 질문을 폭포처럼 쏟아 내며 기진맥진하고 나자, 그녀는 조에게 촛대를 집어던지고, 큰 소리로 흐느껴 울기 시작하더니, 쓰레받기를 꺼내 들고 ─ 이것은 언제나 아주 불길한 징조였다 ─ 거친 앞치마를 두르고는 끔찍할 만큼 대청소를 시작했다."], ["Not satisfied with a dry cleaning, she took to a pail and scrubbing-brush, and cleaned us out of house and home, so that we stood shivering in the back-yard.", "마른걸레질로는 성에 차지 않았던지, 그녀는 양동이와 솔까지 동원해 우리를 집에서 깡그리 씻어 내쫓는 바람에, 우리는 뒷마당에 서서 덜덜 떨어야 했다."], ["It was ten o’clock at night before we ventured to creep in again, and then she asked Joe why he hadn’t married a Negress Slave at once?", "밤 열 시가 되어서야 우리는 겨우 다시 안으로 기어들 엄두를 냈는데, 그러자 그녀는 조에게 어째서 진작 흑인 노예 계집이랑 결혼하지 않았느냐고 물었다."], ["Joe offered no answer, poor fellow, but stood feeling his whisker and looking dejectedly at me, as if he thought it really might have been a better speculation.", "조는 아무 대답도 하지 못하고, 가엾게도 자기 구레나룻을 만지작거리며 풀이 죽은 채 나를 바라보기만 했다. 마치 그 편이 정말로 더 나은 셈속이었을지도 모른다고 생각하는 듯이."]], [["# Chapter XIII.", "# Chapter XIII."]], [["It was a trial to my feelings, on the next day but one, to see Joe arraying himself in his Sunday clothes to accompany me to Miss Havisham’s.", "그다음다음 날, 조가 미스 해비셤 댁에 나를 데려가려고 나들이옷을 차려입는 모습을 지켜보는 일은 내 심정에 적잖은 시련이었다."], ["However, as he thought his court-suit necessary to the occasion, it was not for me to tell him that he looked far better in his working-dress; the rather, because I knew he made himself so dreadfully uncomfortable, entirely on my account, and that it was for me he pulled up his shirt-collar so very high behind, that it made the hair on the crown of his head stand up like a tuft of feathers.", "하지만 그는 이 자리에 예복이 꼭 필요하다고 여겼으므로, 작업복 차림이 훨씬 나아 보인다고 그에게 말할 수는 없는 노릇이었다. 더구나 나는 그가 오로지 나를 위해 그토록 끔찍하게 불편을 감수하고 있다는 것을, 그리고 나를 위해 셔츠 깃을 뒤로 어찌나 높이 추켜올렸던지 정수리의 머리카락이 깃털 다발처럼 곤두서 있다는 것을 알고 있었기에 더욱 그러했다."]], [["At breakfast-time my sister declared her intention of going to town with us, and being left at Uncle Pumblechook’s and called for “when we had done with our fine ladies”—a way of putting the case, from which Joe appeared inclined to augur the worst.", "아침 식사 때 누나는 우리와 함께 읍내에 가겠다고, 그래서 펌블추크 아저씨 댁에 남아 있다가 \"우리가 귀부인들과 볼일을 다 본 뒤에\" 데리러 오라고 선언했다. 이런 말투에서 조는 최악을 점치는 듯한 기색이었다."], ["The forge was shut up for the day, and Joe inscribed in chalk upon the door (as it was his custom to do on the very rare occasions when he was not at work) the monosyllable HOUT, accompanied by a sketch of an arrow supposed to be flying in the direction he had taken.", "대장간은 그날 하루 문을 닫았고, 조는 (일을 하지 않는 지극히 드문 경우에 늘 하던 버릇대로) 문에다 분필로 \"외출\"이라는 한마디를 적고, 그 옆에 그가 향한 방향으로 날아간다고 짐작되는 화살 그림을 그려 넣었다."]], [["We walked to town, my sister leading the way in a very large beaver bonnet, and carrying a basket like the Great Seal of England in plaited Straw, a pair of pattens, a spare shawl, and an umbrella, though it was a fine bright day.", "우리는 걸어서 읍내로 갔는데, 누나는 아주 커다란 비버 가죽 보닛을 쓰고 앞장섰으며, 짚을 엮어 만든 영국 국새만 한 바구니와 나막신 한 켤레, 여분의 숄 한 장, 그리고 우산을 들고 있었다. 날씨가 화창하고 맑았는데도 말이다."], ["I am not quite clear whether these articles were carried penitentially or ostentatiously; but I rather think they were displayed as articles of property,—much as Cleopatra or any other sovereign lady on the Rampage might exhibit her wealth in a pageant or procession.", "이 물건들을 회개하는 마음으로 들고 다닌 것인지 아니면 과시하려고 들고 다닌 것인지 나로서는 확실치 않으나, 아무래도 그것들을 재산으로서 전시했던 것 같다. 마치 클레오파트라나, 잔뜩 성이 나 길길이 날뛰는 그 어떤 군주 마님이 행렬이나 행진에서 제 부를 과시하듯이."]], [["When we came to Pumblechook’s, my sister bounced in and left us.", "펌블추크 아저씨 댁에 이르자 누나는 펄쩍 뛰어들어 가더니 우리를 두고 사라졌다."], ["As it was almost noon, Joe and I held straight on to Miss Havisham’s house.", "거의 정오가 되었으므로, 조와 나는 곧장 미스 해비셤 댁으로 향했다."], ["Estella opened the gate as usual, and, the moment she appeared, Joe took his hat off and stood weighing it by the brim in both his hands; as if he had some urgent reason in his mind for being particular to half a quarter of an ounce.", "에스텔라가 여느 때처럼 문을 열어 주었는데, 그녀가 나타나는 순간 조는 모자를 벗어 두 손으로 챙을 잡고 무게를 가늠하듯 들고 섰다. 마치 그 무게를 사분의 일 온스의 절반까지 정확히 따져야 할 절박한 이유라도 있는 사람처럼."]], [["Estella took no notice of either of us, but led us the way that I knew so well.", "에스텔라는 우리 둘 다 거들떠보지 않은 채, 내가 익히 아는 길로 우리를 안내했다."], ["I followed next to her, and Joe came last. When I looked back at Joe in the long passage, he was still weighing his hat with the greatest care, and was coming after us in long strides on the tips of his toes.", "나는 그녀의 바로 뒤를 따랐고, 조가 맨 끝에 왔다. 긴 복도에서 조를 돌아보니, 그는 여전히 더없이 조심스럽게 제 모자의 무게를 가늠하면서, 발끝으로 성큼성큼 우리를 뒤따라오고 있었다."]], [["Estella told me we were both to go in, so I took Joe by the coat-cuff and conducted him into Miss Havisham’s presence.", "에스텔라가 우리 둘 다 들어가야 한다고 일러 주었으므로, 나는 조의 윗옷 소맷부리를 잡고 그를 미스 해비셤 앞으로 이끌었다."], ["She was seated at her dressing-table, and looked round at us immediately.", "그녀는 화장대 앞에 앉아 있다가 곧바로 우리 쪽을 돌아보았다."]], [["“Oh!”", "\"오!\""], ["said she to Joe. “You are the husband of the sister of this boy?”", "그녀가 조에게 말했다. \"당신이 이 아이 누나의 남편이로군요?\""]], [["I could hardly have imagined dear old Joe looking so unlike himself or so like some extraordinary bird; standing as he did speechless, with his tuft of feathers ruffled, and his mouth open as if he wanted a worm.", "다정하고 늙은 조가 그토록 제 모습과 동떨어져 보이거나, 그토록 별난 새처럼 보일 수 있으리라고는 나는 도무지 상상하지 못했을 것이다. 그는 깃털 다발을 곤두세운 채, 마치 벌레라도 한 마리 바라는 듯 입을 벌리고, 말 한마디 못 하고 서 있었다."]], [["“You are the husband,” repeated Miss Havisham, “of the sister of this boy?”", "\"당신이 이 아이 누나의 남편이지요?\" 미스 해비셤이 되풀이했다."]], [["It was very aggravating; but, throughout the interview, Joe persisted in addressing Me instead of Miss Havisham.", "몹시 답답한 노릇이었으나, 면담이 진행되는 내내 조는 미스 해비셤이 아니라 한사코 나에게만 말을 걸었다."]], [["“Which I meantersay, Pip,”", "\"그러니까 내 말은 말이지, 핍.\""], ["Joe now observed in a manner that was at once expressive of forcible argumentation, strict confidence, and great politeness, “as I hup and married your sister, and I were at the time what you might call (if you was anyways inclined) a single man.”", "조는 이제 강력한 논증과 엄격한 신뢰와 더없는 공손함이 한꺼번에 묻어나는 태도로 입을 열었다. \"내가 벌떡 일어나 네 누나하고 혼인을 했고, 그때 나는 (네가 어떻게든 그리 부르고 싶다면) 말하자면 독신 남자였단 말이지.\""]], [["“Well!” said Miss Havisham.", "\"그래요!\" 미스 해비셤이 말했다."], ["“And you have reared the boy, with the intention of taking him for your apprentice; is that so, Mr. Gargery?”", "\"그래서 이 아이를 도제로 삼을 작정으로 길러 온 거고요. 그렇지요, 가저리 씨?\""]], [["“You know, Pip,”", "\"있잖아, 핍.\""], ["replied Joe, “as you and me were ever friends, and it were looked for’ard to betwixt us, as being calc’lated to lead to larks.", "조가 대답했다. \"너하고 나는 늘 친구였고, 우리 둘 사이에선 그게 신나는 일로 이어질 거라고 손꼽아 기다려 온 일이었잖아."], ["Not but what, Pip, if you had ever made objections to the business,—such as its being open to black and sut, or such-like,—not but what they would have been attended to, don’t you see?”", "그렇다고 해서, 핍, 만에 하나 네가 그 일에 대해 이의를 제기한 적이 있었다면 말이야 — 이를테면 그게 검댕과 그을음 따위에 노출되어 있다든가 하는 그런 이의 말이야 — 그렇다고 해서 그 이의가 받아들여지지 않았으리란 건 아니야, 알겠지?\""]], [["“Has the boy,”", "\"이 아이가.\""], ["said Miss Havisham, “ever made any objection?", "미스 해비셤이 말했다. \"이의를 제기한 적이 있나요?"], ["Does he like the trade?”", "이 일을 좋아하나요?\""]], [["“Which it is well beknown to yourself, Pip,”", "\"그건 네 자신이 익히 잘 알고 있잖아, 핍.\""], ["returned Joe, strengthening his former mixture of argumentation, confidence, and politeness, “that it were the wish of your own hart.”", "조는 앞서의 논증과 신뢰와 공손함의 혼합을 한층 강화하며 대답했다. \"그게 바로 네 마음속 소원이었다는 거 말이야.\""], ["(I saw the idea suddenly break upon him that he would adapt his epitaph to the occasion, before he went on to say) “And there weren’t no objection on your part, and Pip it were the great wish of your hart!”", "(나는 그가 다음 말을 잇기 전에, 그 묘비명을 이 상황에 맞게 고쳐 쓰겠다는 생각이 문득 그에게 떠오르는 것을 보았다.) \"그리고 네 쪽에선 아무 이의도 없었고, 핍 그건 네 마음속 크나큰 소원이었지!\""]], [["It was quite in vain for me to endeavour to make him sensible that he ought to speak to Miss Havisham.", "그에게 미스 해비셤에게 말을 해야 한다는 점을 일깨우려는 내 노력은 전혀 헛수고였다."], ["The more I made faces and gestures to him to do it, the more confidential, argumentative, and polite, he persisted in being to Me.", "내가 그렇게 하라고 표정을 짓고 몸짓을 할수록, 그는 더욱더 은밀하고 논증적이며 공손하게 한사코 나에게만 굴었다."]], [["“Have you brought his indentures with you?”", "\"그 아이의 도제 계약서를 가져왔나요?\""], ["asked Miss Havisham.", "미스 해비셤이 물었다."]], [["“Well, Pip, you know,”", "\"그게 말이지, 핍, 알다시피.\""], ["replied Joe, as if that were a little unreasonable, “you yourself see me put ’em in my ’at, and therefore you know as they are here.”", "조는 그것이 다소 부당한 질문이라는 듯 대답했다. \"네가 내가 그걸 모자에 넣는 걸 직접 봤으니, 그게 여기 있다는 걸 너도 알잖아.\""], ["With which he took them out, and gave them, not to Miss Havisham, but to me.", "그러면서 그는 그것을 꺼내, 미스 해비셤이 아니라 나에게 건넸다."], ["I am afraid I was ashamed of the dear good fellow,—I know I was ashamed of him,—when I saw that Estella stood at the back of Miss Havisham’s chair, and that her eyes laughed mischievously.", "에스텔라가 미스 해비셤의 의자 뒤에 서서 두 눈으로 짓궂게 웃고 있는 것을 보았을 때, 나는 그 다정하고 착한 친구가 부끄러웠던 것 같다 — 분명 나는 그가 부끄러웠다."], ["I took the indentures out of his hand and gave them to Miss Havisham.", "나는 그의 손에서 계약서를 받아 미스 해비셤에게 건넸다."]], [["“You expected,” said Miss Havisham, as she looked them over, “no premium with the boy?”", "\"이 아이에게 도제금을 받으리라고는 기대하지 않았겠지요?\" 미스 해비셤이 계약서를 훑어보며 말했다."]], [["“Joe!”", "\"조!\""], ["I remonstrated, for he made no reply at all.", "그가 도무지 아무 대답도 하지 않기에 나는 항의했다."], ["“Why don’t you answer—”", "\"왜 대답을 안 해요 —\""]], [["“Pip,”", "\"핍.\""], ["returned Joe, cutting me short as if he were hurt, “which I meantersay that were not a question requiring a answer betwixt yourself and me, and which you know the answer to be full well No.", "조는 상처라도 받은 듯 내 말을 가로막으며 대꾸했다. \"그러니까 내 말은, 그건 너하고 나 사이에 대답이 필요한 질문이 아니라는 거고, 그 대답이 단연코 '아니오'라는 걸 너도 익히 알고 있잖아."], ["You know it to be No, Pip, and wherefore should I say it?”", "너도 그게 '아니오'라는 걸 알고 있는데, 내가 무엇 하러 그 말을 하겠어, 핍?\""]], [["Miss Havisham glanced at him as if she understood what he really was better than I had thought possible, seeing what he was there; and took up a little bag from the table beside her.", "미스 해비셤은, 거기서 그가 어떤 사람인지를 보고서, 그의 됨됨이를 내가 가능하리라 여겼던 것보다 더 잘 헤아린다는 듯 그를 흘끗 쳐다보았다. 그러고는 옆 탁자에서 작은 주머니 하나를 집어 들었다."]], [["“Pip has earned a premium here,”", "\"핍은 여기서 도제금을 벌었어요.\""], ["she said, “and here it is.", "그녀가 말했다. \"여기 있어요."], ["There are five-and-twenty guineas in this bag.", "이 주머니에 이십오 기니가 들어 있어요."], ["Give it to your master, Pip.”", "네 주인에게 드려라, 핍.\""]], [["As if he were absolutely out of his mind with the wonder awakened in him by her strange figure and the strange room, Joe, even at this pass, persisted in addressing me.", "마치 그녀의 기이한 행색과 그 기이한 방이 불러일으킨 경이로움에 완전히 넋이 나간 사람처럼, 조는 이 마당에 이르러서도 한사코 나에게만 말을 걸었다."]], [["“This is wery liberal on your part, Pip,”", "\"이건 네 쪽에서 참으로 후한 일이야, 핍.\""], ["said Joe, “and it is as such received and grateful welcome, though never looked for, far nor near, nor nowheres.", "조가 말했다. \"그래서 이렇게 받아 들고 고맙게 환영하는 바이지만, 멀리서든 가까이서든 그 어디서도 결코 바란 적이 없는 일이지."], ["And now, old chap,”", "그리고 이제, 친구.\""], ["said Joe, conveying to me a sensation, first of burning and then of freezing, for I felt as if that familiar expression were applied to Miss Havisham,—“and now, old chap, may we do our duty!", "조가 말했는데, 그 정겨운 호칭이 마치 미스 해비셤에게 쓰이는 것만 같아 나는 처음엔 화끈거리다가 이내 얼어붙는 느낌이 들었다. \"그리고 이제, 친구, 우리 본분을 다하자꾸나!"], ["May you and me do our duty, both on us, by one and another, and by them which your liberal present—have-conweyed—to be—for the satisfaction of mind-of—them as never—”", "너와 내가, 우리 둘 다, 서로에게, 그리고 네 후한 선물이 — 전해 준 — 그 마음의 — 만족을 위해 — 결코 — 한 적이 없는 — 그분들에게 본분을 다하자꾸나.\""], ["here Joe showed that he felt he had fallen into frightful difficulties, until he triumphantly rescued himself with the words, “and from myself far be it!”", "여기서 조는 자신이 무서운 곤경에 빠졌음을 느낀 기색을 내비치다가, 마침내 \"그리고 나로 말하면 어림없는 일이지!\"라는 말로 의기양양하게 스스로를 구해 냈다."], ["These words had such a round and convincing sound for him that he said them twice.", "이 말이 그에게는 어찌나 매끄럽고 설득력 있게 들렸던지, 그는 그것을 두 번이나 되뇌었다."]], [["“Good-bye, Pip!”", "\"잘 가렴, 핍!\""], ["said Miss Havisham.", "미스 해비셤이 말했다."], ["“Let them out, Estella.”", "\"내보내 드려라, 에스텔라.\""]], [["“Am I to come again, Miss Havisham?” I asked.", "\"또 와야 하나요, 미스 해비셤?\" 내가 물었다."]], [["“No.", "\"아니."], ["Gargery is your master now. Gargery!", "이제 가저리가 네 주인이다. 가저리!"], ["One word!”", "한마디만!\""]], [["Thus calling him back as I went out of the door, I heard her say to Joe in a distinct emphatic voice, “The boy has been a good boy here, and that is his reward.", "내가 문을 나서는 참에 그녀가 이렇게 그를 불러 세우더니, 또렷하고 힘준 목소리로 조에게 이렇게 말하는 것이 들렸다. \"이 아이는 여기서 착하게 굴었고, 그것이 그 보답이에요."], ["Of course, as an honest man, you will expect no other and no more.”", "물론 정직한 사람으로서, 당신은 그 밖의 다른 것이나 그 이상을 기대하지 않겠지요.\""]], [["How Joe got out of the room, I have never been able to determine; but I know that when he did get out he was steadily proceeding upstairs instead of coming down, and was deaf to all remonstrances until I went after him and laid hold of him.", "조가 어떻게 그 방을 나왔는지 나는 끝내 알아내지 못했으나, 그가 정작 나왔을 때 아래로 내려오는 대신 꾸준히 위층으로 올라가고 있었다는 것, 그리고 내가 그를 쫓아가 붙들기 전까지는 어떤 만류에도 귀를 기울이지 않았다는 것만은 안다."], ["In another minute we were outside the gate, and it was locked, and Estella was gone.", "잠시 후 우리는 문밖으로 나왔고, 문은 잠겼으며, 에스텔라는 가 버린 뒤였다."], ["When we stood in the daylight alone again, Joe backed up against a wall, and said to me, “Astonishing!”", "다시 단둘이 햇빛 속에 섰을 때, 조는 벽에 등을 기대고 나에게 말했다. \"놀랍구나!\""], ["And there he remained so long saying, “Astonishing” at intervals, so often, that I began to think his senses were never coming back.", "그러고는 거기 그렇게 오래도록 머무르며 이따금 어찌나 자주 \"놀랍구나\"를 되뇌던지, 나는 그의 정신이 영영 돌아오지 않는 게 아닐까 생각하기 시작했다."], ["At length he prolonged his remark into “Pip, I do assure you this is as-TON-ishing!” and so, by degrees, became conversational and able to walk away.", "마침내 그는 그 말을 \"핍, 정말이지 너에게 단언하건대 이건 놀-라-운 일이야!\"로 길게 늘이더니, 그렇게 차츰 말이 통하게 되었고 걸어 나갈 수 있게 되었다."]], [["I have reason to think that Joe’s intellects were brightened by the encounter they had passed through, and that on our way to Pumblechook’s he invented a subtle and deep design.", "조의 지력이 방금 겪어 낸 그 만남으로 한층 또렷해졌으며, 펌블추크 아저씨 댁으로 가는 길에 그가 교묘하고 심오한 계략 하나를 꾸며 냈다고 나는 생각할 만한 근거가 있다."], ["My reason is to be found in what took place in Mr. Pumblechook’s parlour: where, on our presenting ourselves, my sister sat in conference with that detested seedsman.", "그 근거는 펌블추크 씨의 응접실에서 벌어진 일에서 찾을 수 있는데, 우리가 그곳에 모습을 드러냈을 때 누나는 그 얄미운 종묘상과 마주 앉아 의논을 나누고 있었다."]], [["“Well?”", "\"그래?\""], ["cried my sister, addressing us both at once.", "누나가 우리 둘에게 한꺼번에 대고 소리쳤다."], ["“And what’s happened to you? I wonder you condescend to come back to such poor society as this, I am sure I do!”", "\"그래서 너희들한테 무슨 일이 있었던 거야? 이런 보잘것없는 곳까지 황송하게도 돌아와 주다니 신기할 따름이네, 정말이지!\""]], [["“Miss Havisham,”", "\"미스 해비셤께서.\""], ["said Joe, with a fixed look at me, like an effort of remembrance, “made it wery partick’ler that we should give her—were it compliments or respects, Pip?”", "조가 마치 기억을 더듬으려 애쓰듯 나를 빤히 바라보며 말했다. \"그분께 우리가 꼭 전해 드려야 한다고 아주 각별히 당부하시기를 — 그게 안부였던가 경의였던가, 핍?\""]], [["“Compliments,” I said.", "\"안부요.\" 내가 말했다."]], [["“Which that were my own belief,”", "\"그게 바로 내 생각이었어.\""], ["answered Joe; “her compliments to Mrs. J. Gargery—”", "조가 대답했다. \"J. 가저리 부인께 안부를 —\""]], [["“Much good they’ll do me!”", "\"그게 나한테 무슨 큰 도움이 되겠어!\""], ["observed my sister; but rather gratified too.", "누나가 말했으나, 그래도 다소 흐뭇해하는 기색이었다."]], [["“And wishing,”", "\"그리고 바라시기를.\""], ["pursued Joe, with another fixed look at me, like another effort of remembrance, “that the state of Miss Havisham’s elth were sitch as would have—allowed, were it, Pip?”", "조가 또 한 번 기억을 더듬으려 애쓰듯 나를 빤히 바라보며 말을 이었다. \"미스 해비셤의 건강 상태가 — 허락할 만한 그런 것이었더라면, 그랬더라면, 핍?\""]], [["“Of her having the pleasure,” I added.", "\"기쁨을 누리실.\" 내가 덧붙였다."]], [["“Of ladies’ company,”", "\"귀부인들과 어울리는 기쁨을요.\""], ["said Joe.", "조가 말했다."], ["And drew a long breath.", "그러고는 긴 숨을 내쉬었다."]], [["“Well!”", "\"그래!\""], ["cried my sister, with a mollified glance at Mr. Pumblechook.", "누나가 펌블추크 씨에게 누그러진 눈길을 던지며 소리쳤다."], ["“She might have had the politeness to send that message at first, but it’s better late than never.", "\"처음부터 그 전갈을 보낼 만한 예의는 갖췄을 법한데, 그래도 늦더라도 안 하는 것보단 낫지."], ["And what did she give young Rantipole here?”", "그래서 여기 이 어린 망나니한테는 뭘 줬다는 거야?\""]], [["“She giv’ him,”", "\"그 애한테는.\""], ["said Joe, “nothing.”", "조가 말했다. \"아무것도 안 줬어.\""]], [["Mrs. Joe was going to break out, but Joe went on.", "조 부인이 막 폭발하려는 참에, 조가 말을 이었다."]], [["“What she giv’,”", "\"그분이 주신 건.\""], ["said Joe, “she giv’ to his friends.", "조가 말했다. \"그 애 친지들한테 주신 거지."], ["‘And by his friends,’ were her explanation, ‘I mean into the hands of his sister Mrs. J.", "'그리고 그 애 친지들이라 함은,' 하고 그분이 설명하시기를, '그 애의 누나 J."], ["Gargery.’ Them were her words; ‘Mrs. J.", "가저리 부인의 손에 쥐여 드린다는 뜻입니다.'"], ["Gargery.’ She mayn’t have know’d,”", "그게 바로 그분의 말씀이었어. 'J. 가저리 부인.'"], ["added Joe, with an appearance of reflection, “whether it were Joe, or Jorge.”", "그분은 어쩌면 모르셨을 거야.\" 조가 곰곰이 생각하는 기색으로 덧붙였다. \"그게 조인지 조지인지는 말이야.\""]], [["My sister looked at Pumblechook: who smoothed the elbows of his wooden arm-chair, and nodded at her and at the fire, as if he had known all about it beforehand.", "누나는 펌블추크를 바라보았고, 그는 나무 안락의자의 팔걸이를 쓰다듬으며 마치 미리 다 알고 있었다는 듯 누나에게도, 난롯불에게도 고개를 끄덕여 보였다."]], [["“And how much have you got?”", "\"그래서 얼마나 받았어?\""], ["asked my sister, laughing.", "누나가 웃으며 물었다."], ["Positively laughing!", "분명히 웃으면서!"]], [["“What would present company say to ten pound?”", "\"여기 계신 분들은 십 파운드라면 뭐라 하실까?\""], ["demanded Joe.", "조가 물었다."]], [["“They’d say,” returned my sister, curtly, “pretty well.", "\"꽤 괜찮다고 하겠지.\" 누나가 퉁명스레 대꾸했다."], ["Not too much, but pretty well.”", "\"너무 많은 건 아니지만, 그래도 꽤 괜찮다고.\""]], [["“It’s more than that, then,” said Joe.", "\"그럼 그것보다 많아.\" 조가 말했다."]], [["That fearful Impostor, Pumblechook, immediately nodded, and said, as he rubbed the arms of his chair, “It’s more than that, Mum.”", "저 무시무시한 사기꾼 펌블추크가 즉시 고개를 끄덕이며, 의자 팔걸이를 문지르면서 말했다. \"그것보다 많지요, 부인.\""]], [["“Why, you don’t mean to say—”", "\"아니, 설마 그 말은 —\""], ["began my sister.", "누나가 입을 떼었다."]], [["“Yes I do, Mum,”", "\"네, 그렇습니다, 부인.\""], ["said Pumblechook; “but wait a bit.", "펌블추크가 말했다. \"하지만 잠깐 기다려 보세요."], ["Go on, Joseph. Good in you! Go on!”", "계속하게, 조지프. 잘하는군! 계속해!\""]], [["“What would present company say,”", "\"여기 계신 분들은.\" 조가 말을 이었다."], ["proceeded Joe, “to twenty pound?”", "\"이십 파운드라면 뭐라 하실까?\""]], [["“Handsome would be the word,”", "\"근사하다는 말이 나오겠지.\""], ["returned my sister.", "누나가 대꾸했다."]], [["“Well, then,”", "\"그럼, 그렇다면.\""], ["said Joe, “It’s more than twenty pound.”", "조가 말했다. \"이십 파운드보다 많아.\""]], [["That abject hypocrite, Pumblechook, nodded again, and said, with a patronizing laugh, “It’s more than that, Mum.", "저 비열한 위선자 펌블추크가 또 고개를 끄덕이더니, 생색내는 웃음을 띠며 말했다. \"그것보다 많지요, 부인."], ["Good again!", "또 맞혔습니다!"], ["Follow her up, Joseph!”", "계속 몰아붙이세요, 조지프!\""]], [["“Then to make an end of it,”", "\"그럼 이걸로 끝을 내자면.\""], ["said Joe, delightedly handing the bag to my sister; “it’s five-and-twenty pound.”", "조가 신이 나서 누나에게 주머니를 건네며 말했다. \"이십오 파운드야.\""]], [["“It’s five-and-twenty pound, Mum,”", "\"이십오 파운드입니다, 부인.\""], ["echoed that basest of swindlers, Pumblechook, rising to shake hands with her; “and it’s no more than your merits (as I said when my opinion was asked), and I wish you joy of the money!”", "저 가장 야비한 사기꾼 펌블추크가 따라 외치며 일어나 누나와 악수를 했다. \"그리고 그건 (제 의견을 물으셨을 때 제가 말씀드렸듯이) 오로지 부인의 공덕에 걸맞은 액수일 뿐이며, 이 돈으로 부디 기쁨을 누리시길 빕니다!\""]], [["If the villain had stopped here, his case would have been sufficiently awful, but he blackened his guilt by proceeding to take me into custody, with a right of patronage that left all his former criminality far behind.", "그 악당이 여기서 멈추었더라면 그의 행태도 충분히 끔찍한 정도로 그쳤겠지만, 그는 한술 더 떠 나를 떠맡고 나섰으니, 그 후견인 행세는 그가 앞서 저지른 온갖 죄상을 까마득히 능가하는 것이었다."]], [["“Now you see, Joseph and wife,”", "\"자 보세요, 조지프 그리고 부인.\""], ["said Pumblechook, as he took me by the arm above the elbow, “I am one of them that always go right through with what they’ve begun.", "펌블추크가 내 팔의 팔꿈치 위쪽을 붙잡으며 말했다. \"나는 한번 시작한 일은 늘 끝까지 밀고 나가는 사람들 중 하나입니다."], ["This boy must be bound, out of hand.", "이 아이는 당장 계약을 맺어야 합니다."], ["That’s my way. Bound out of hand.”", "그게 내 방식이지요. 당장 계약을 맺어야 한다는 겁니다.\""]], [["“Goodness knows, Uncle Pumblechook,”", "\"아무렴요, 펌블추크 아저씨.\""], ["said my sister (grasping the money), “we’re deeply beholden to you.”", "누나가 (돈을 움켜쥐며) 말했다. \"저희가 아저씨께 크게 신세를 졌네요.\""]], [["“Never mind me, Mum,”", "\"내 생각은 마세요, 부인.\""], ["returned that diabolical cornchandler.", "그 악마 같은 곡물상이 대꾸했다."], ["“A pleasure’s a pleasure all the world over.", "\"기쁨이란 어디서나 다 기쁨이지요."], ["But this boy, you know; we must have him bound.", "하지만 이 아이는, 아시다시피 계약을 맺어야 합니다."], ["I said I’d see to it—to tell you the truth.”", "솔직히 말씀드리면, 내가 그 일을 처리하겠노라 말씀드린 바 있지요.\""]], [["The Justices were sitting in the Town Hall near at hand, and we at once went over to have me bound apprentice to Joe in the Magisterial presence.", "치안 판사들이 바로 가까운 읍사무소에 출석해 있었으므로, 우리는 곧바로 그리로 건너가 판사 앞에서 나를 조의 도제로 계약시켰다."], ["I say we went over, but I was pushed over by Pumblechook, exactly as if I had that moment picked a pocket or fired a rick; indeed, it was the general impression in Court that I had been taken red-handed; for, as Pumblechook shoved me before him through the crowd, I heard some people say, “What’s he done?”", "우리가 건너갔다고 말은 하지만, 사실 나는 펌블추크에게 떠밀려 건너갔는데, 그 꼴이 마치 내가 방금 소매치기를 했거나 건초더미에 불이라도 지른 사람 같았다. 실제로 법정 안에서는 내가 현행범으로 붙잡혀 왔다는 것이 전반적인 인상이었다."], ["and others, “He’s a young ’un, too, but looks bad, don’t he?” One person of mild and benevolent aspect even gave me a tract ornamented with a woodcut of a malevolent young man fitted up with a perfect sausage-shop of fetters, and entitled TO BE READ IN MY CELL.", "펌블추크가 군중 사이로 나를 앞세워 밀고 나가는 동안, 어떤 이들은 \"쟤가 뭘 했대?\"라고 말하고, 또 어떤 이들은 \"아직 어린 녀석인데, 인상이 험상궂지 않아?\"라고 말하는 소리가 들렸으니까. 심지어 온화하고 자비로운 인상의 한 사람은, 족쇄를 완벽한 소시지 가게처럼 주렁주렁 매단 흉악한 청년의 목판화로 장식되고 「나의 감방에서 읽을지어다」라는 제목이 붙은 전도지 한 장을 나에게 건네주기까지 했다."]], [["The Hall was a queer place, I thought, with higher pews in it than a church,—and with people hanging over the pews looking on,—and with mighty Justices (one with a powdered head) leaning back in chairs, with folded arms, or taking snuff, or going to sleep, or writing, or reading the newspapers,—and with some shining black portraits on the walls, which my unartistic eye regarded as a composition of hardbake and sticking-plaster.", "법정은 묘한 곳이라고 나는 생각했다. 교회보다 높은 좌석이 있었고 — 사람들이 그 좌석에 몸을 기댄 채 구경하고 있었으며 — 위풍당당한 판사들(한 사람은 머리에 분을 발랐다)이 팔짱을 끼고 의자에 등을 기댄 채, 코담배를 들이켜거나, 졸거나, 무언가를 쓰거나, 신문을 읽고 있었고 — 벽에는 반들거리는 검은 초상화 몇 점이 걸려 있었는데, 미술에 문외한인 내 눈에는 그것들이 호두엿과 반창고를 뒤섞어 빚은 것처럼 보였다."], ["Here, in a corner my indentures were duly signed and attested, and I was “bound”; Mr.", "여기 한 구석에서 내 도제 계약서가 정식으로 서명되고 입증되었으며, 나는 \"계약에 묶였다.\""], ["Pumblechook holding me all the while as if we had looked in on our way to the scaffold, to have those little preliminaries disposed of.", "그러는 내내 펌블추크 씨는, 마치 우리가 교수대로 가는 길에 잠깐 들러 이런 자질구레한 절차를 처리하기라도 하는 양 나를 붙들고 있었다."]], [["When we had come out again, and had got rid of the boys who had been put into great spirits by the expectation of seeing me publicly tortured, and who were much disappointed to find that my friends were merely rallying round me, we went back to Pumblechook’s.", "우리가 다시 밖으로 나와, 내가 공개적으로 고문당하는 광경을 보리라는 기대에 잔뜩 신이 나 있다가 내 친지들이 그저 나를 둘러싸고 격려하는 것뿐임을 알고 크게 실망한 아이들을 떼어 낸 뒤, 우리는 펌블추크 아저씨 댁으로 돌아왔다."], ["And there my sister became so excited by the twenty-five guineas, that nothing would serve her but we must have a dinner out of that windfall at the Blue Boar, and that Pumblechook must go over in his chaise-cart, and bring the Hubbles and Mr. Wopsle.", "그리고 거기서 누나는 그 이십오 기니에 어찌나 들떴던지, 그 횡재로 블루 보어 여관에서 만찬을 들어야겠다고, 펌블추크가 이륜마차를 몰고 가서 허블 부부와 워프슬 씨를 데려와야겠다고, 그러지 않고서는 도무지 직성이 풀리지 않았다."]], [["It was agreed to be done; and a most melancholy day I passed.", "그렇게 하기로 의견이 모였고, 나는 더없이 우울한 하루를 보냈다."], ["For, it inscrutably appeared to stand to reason, in the minds of the whole company, that I was an excrescence on the entertainment.", "왜냐하면 도무지 헤아릴 수 없게도, 좌중 모두의 마음속에서 내가 이 연회의 군더더기라는 것이 너무도 당연한 이치로 여겨지는 듯했기 때문이다."], ["And to make it worse, they all asked me from time to time,—in short, whenever they had nothing else to do,—why I didn’t enjoy myself? And what could I possibly do then, but say I was enjoying myself,—when I wasn’t!", "게다가 설상가상으로, 그들은 이따금 — 요컨대 달리 할 일이 없을 때마다 — 나에게 왜 즐기지 않느냐고 물어 댔다. 그러니 그때 내가 즐기고 있노라고 말하는 것 말고 달리 무엇을 할 수 있었겠는가 — 즐기고 있지도 않은데 말이다!"]], [["However, they were grown up and had their own way, and they made the most of it.", "그러나 그들은 다 큰 어른들이었고 제 마음대로 했으며, 그 자리를 한껏 누렸다."], ["That swindling Pumblechook, exalted into the beneficent contriver of the whole occasion, actually took the top of the table; and, when he addressed them on the subject of my being bound, and had fiendishly congratulated them on my being liable to imprisonment if I played at cards, drank strong liquors, kept late hours or bad company, or indulged in other vagaries which the form of my indentures appeared to contemplate as next to inevitable, he placed me standing on a chair beside him to illustrate his remarks.", "저 사기꾼 펌블추크는 이 모든 일을 베푼 자비로운 기획자로 떠받들려, 실제로 상석을 차지했다. 그리고 나의 계약 건에 대해 좌중에게 일장 연설을 늘어놓으며, 내가 카드 노름을 하거나, 독한 술을 마시거나, 밤늦게까지 쏘다니거나, 나쁜 친구들과 어울리거나, 그 밖에 내 도제 계약서의 형식이 거의 불가피한 일로 상정하고 있는 듯한 온갖 탈선에 빠질 경우 투옥될 수 있다는 점을 악마처럼 축하한 뒤, 그는 자기 논평을 실연해 보이려고 나를 자기 옆 의자 위에 세워 두었다."]], [["My only other remembrances of the great festival are, That they wouldn’t let me go to sleep, but whenever they saw me dropping off, woke me up and told me to enjoy myself. That, rather late in the evening Mr.", "그 성대한 잔치에 관해 내게 남은 다른 기억이라고는 이런 것들뿐이다. 그들은 나를 자게 내버려 두지 않고, 내가 꾸벅꾸벅 졸기 시작하는 것을 볼 때마다 깨워서는 즐기라고 했다는 것."], ["Wopsle gave us Collins’s ode, and threw his bloodstained sword in thunder down, with such effect, that a waiter came in and said, “The Commercials underneath sent up their compliments, and it wasn’t the Tumblers’ Arms.”", "저녁이 꽤 깊었을 무렵 워프슬 씨가 콜린스의 송시를 읊으며 피로 물든 검을 천둥처럼 내던졌는데, 그 효과가 어찌나 대단했던지 종업원이 들어와 \"아래층 외판원들께서 안부를 전하시며, 여기가 곡예사 술집은 아니라고 하십니다\"라고 말했다는 것."], ["That, they were all in excellent spirits on the road home, and sang, O Lady Fair! Mr. Wopsle taking the bass, and asserting with a tremendously strong voice (in reply to the inquisitive bore who leads that piece of music in a most impertinent manner, by wanting to know all about everybody’s private affairs) that he was the man with his white locks flowing, and that he was upon the whole the weakest pilgrim going.", "집으로 돌아가는 길에 그들이 모두 더없이 들뜬 기분으로 「오, 아리따운 아가씨여!」를 불렀는데, 워프슬 씨가 저음부를 맡아, (그 노래를 이끌면서 모든 이의 사사로운 일을 죄다 캐물으려 드는 더없이 무례한 태도의 그 꼬치꼬치 캐묻는 따분한 사내에게 대답하듯) 자신이 바로 그 백발을 흩날리는 사내이며, 전체로 보아 자신이 가장 연약한 순례자라고 엄청나게 우렁찬 목소리로 단언했다는 것."]], [["Finally, I remember that when I got into my little bedroom, I was truly wretched, and had a strong conviction on me that I should never like Joe’s trade. I had liked it once, but once was not now.", "마지막으로, 내가 기억하기로 내 작은 침실로 들어갔을 때 나는 정말이지 비참한 심정이었고, 결코 조의 일을 좋아하게 되지 못하리라는 강한 확신에 사로잡혀 있었다. 한때는 그 일을 좋아했지만, 그 한때는 이제 더 이상 지금이 아니었다."]], [["# Chapter XIV.", "# Chapter XIV."]], [["It is a most miserable thing to feel ashamed of home.", "집을 부끄럽게 여긴다는 것은 더할 나위 없이 비참한 일이다."], ["There may be black ingratitude in the thing, and the punishment may be retributive and well deserved; but that it is a miserable thing, I can testify.", "그 안에는 새까만 배은망덕이 깃들어 있을지도 모르고, 그에 대한 벌이 응보로서 마땅한 것일지도 모른다. 그러나 그것이 비참한 일이라는 것만은 내가 증언할 수 있다."]], [["Home had never been a very pleasant place to me, because of my sister’s temper.", "집은 누나의 성미 탓에 내게 그리 즐거운 곳이었던 적이 없었다."], ["But, Joe had sanctified it, and I had believed in it. I had believed in the best parlour as a most elegant saloon; I had believed in the front door, as a mysterious portal of the Temple of State whose solemn opening was attended with a sacrifice of roast fowls; I had believed in the kitchen as a chaste though not magnificent apartment; I had believed in the forge as the glowing road to manhood and independence.", "그러나 조가 그곳을 거룩하게 만들어 주었고, 나는 그것을 믿었다. 나는 안쪽 응접실을 더없이 우아한 객실이라 믿었고, 앞문을 엄숙하게 열릴 때마다 통닭구이의 제물이 따르는, 신비로운 국가 신전의 정문이라 믿었으며, 부엌을 웅장하지는 않아도 정결한 방이라 믿었고, 대장간을 어른이 되어 자립으로 나아가는 이글거리는 길이라 믿었다."], ["Within a single year all this was changed.", "단 한 해 사이에 이 모든 것이 변해 버렸다."], ["Now it was all coarse and common, and I would not have had Miss Havisham and Estella see it on any account.", "이제 그 모든 것이 거칠고 천하게만 보였고, 나는 무슨 일이 있어도 미스 해비셤과 에스텔라에게 그것을 보이고 싶지 않았다."]], [["How much of my ungracious condition of mind may have been my own fault, how much Miss Havisham’s, how much my sister’s, is now of no moment to me or to any one.", "이런 볼썽사나운 내 마음 상태가 어느 만큼이 내 탓이었는지, 어느 만큼이 미스 해비셤의 탓이었는지, 어느 만큼이 누나의 탓이었는지는, 이제 내게도 그 누구에게도 중요하지 않다."], ["The change was made in me; the thing was done.", "변화는 내 안에서 일어났고, 일은 이미 벌어진 것이다."], ["Well or ill done, excusably or inexcusably, it was done.", "잘 벌어졌든 잘못 벌어졌든, 용서받을 만하든 용서받을 수 없든, 어쨌든 일은 벌어졌다."]], [["Once, it had seemed to me that when I should at last roll up my shirt-sleeves and go into the forge, Joe’s ’prentice, I should be distinguished and happy.", "한때 나는, 마침내 셔츠 소매를 걷어붙이고 조의 도제로서 대장간에 들어서게 될 날이면, 내가 떳떳하고 행복하리라 여겼었다."], ["Now the reality was in my hold, I only felt that I was dusty with the dust of small-coal, and that I had a weight upon my daily remembrance to which the anvil was a feather.", "그러나 막상 그 현실이 손안에 들어오고 보니, 나는 그저 잔석탄의 먼지를 뒤집어쓴 처지라는 것만을 느꼈고, 모루조차 깃털처럼 가벼워 보일 만큼 무거운 짐이 나날의 마음을 짓누르고 있음을 느낄 뿐이었다."], ["There have been occasions in my later life (I suppose as in most lives) when I have felt for a time as if a thick curtain had fallen on all its interest and romance, to shut me out from anything save dull endurance any more.", "훗날 내 인생에는 (대개의 인생이 그러하리라 생각하지만) 그 모든 흥취와 낭만 위로 두꺼운 장막이 드리워져, 무딘 인내 말고는 그 무엇으로부터도 나를 차단해 버리는 듯한 시기가 한동안 있곤 했다."], ["Never has that curtain dropped so heavy and blank, as when my way in life lay stretched out straight before me through the newly entered road of apprenticeship to Joe.", "그러나 그 장막이, 조의 도제 생활이라는 새로 들어선 길을 통해 내 인생의 행로가 내 앞에 일직선으로 뻗어 있을 때만큼 무겁고 막막하게 드리운 적은 없었다."]], [["I remember that at a later period of my “time,” I used to stand about the churchyard on Sunday evenings when night was falling, comparing my own perspective with the windy marsh view, and making out some likeness between them by thinking how flat and low both were, and how on both there came an unknown way and a dark mist and then the sea.", "기억하건대 도제 \"기간\"의 후반 무렵, 나는 일요일 저녁 밤이 내릴 때면 교회 묘지 언저리에 서서, 내 앞날의 전망을 바람 부는 습지의 풍경과 견주어 보며, 둘 다 얼마나 평평하고 나지막한지, 그리고 둘 다 그 위로 알 수 없는 길과 어두운 안개가 펼쳐졌다가 이윽고 바다가 나타나는지를 헤아려, 그 둘 사이에서 어떤 닮은 점을 찾아내곤 했다."], ["I was quite as dejected on the first working-day of my apprenticeship as in that after-time; but I am glad to know that I never breathed a murmur to Joe while my indentures lasted.", "도제 생활 첫날의 나는 그 훗날만큼이나 의기소침했다. 그러나 도제 계약이 이어지는 동안 내가 조에게 단 한마디 불평도 입 밖에 내지 않았다는 것은 다행으로 안다."], ["It is about the only thing I am glad to know of myself in that connection.", "그 일에 관해 내가 스스로에 대해 다행이라 여길 수 있는 거의 유일한 점이다."]], [["For, though it includes what I proceed to add, all the merit of what I proceed to add was Joe’s.", "이제 내가 덧붙이려는 말이 거기에 포함되긴 하지만, 그렇게 덧붙이려는 일의 공로는 모두 조의 것이었다."], ["It was not because I was faithful, but because Joe was faithful, that I never ran away and went for a soldier or a sailor.", "내가 달아나 군인이나 뱃사람이 되어 버리지 않은 것은 내가 충직해서가 아니라 조가 충직했기 때문이었다."], ["It was not because I had a strong sense of the virtue of industry, but because Joe had a strong sense of the virtue of industry, that I worked with tolerable zeal against the grain.", "내가 마음에 내키지 않으면서도 그런대로 열성을 내어 일한 것은 내게 근면의 미덕에 대한 강한 의식이 있어서가 아니라 조에게 그런 의식이 강했기 때문이었다."], ["It is not possible to know how far the influence of any amiable honest-hearted duty-doing man flies out into the world; but it is very possible to know how it has touched one’s self in going by, and I know right well that any good that intermixed itself with my apprenticeship came of plain contented Joe, and not of restlessly aspiring discontented me.", "상냥하고 정직한 마음으로 제 본분을 다하는 사람의 영향이 세상으로 얼마나 멀리 뻗어 나가는지는 알 길이 없다. 그러나 그 영향이 곁을 스쳐 지나며 자기 자신을 어떻게 어루만졌는지는 충분히 알 수 있는 법이니, 내 도제 생활에 섞여 든 좋은 것은 무엇이든 잠시도 가만있지 못하고 위만 쳐다보며 불만에 찬 나에게서가 아니라, 수수하고 만족할 줄 아는 조에게서 나왔음을 나는 똑똑히 알고 있다."]], [["What I wanted, who can say? How can I say, when I never knew?", "내가 무엇을 원했는지, 그 누가 말할 수 있겠는가?"], ["What I dreaded was, that in some unlucky hour I, being at my grimiest and commonest, should lift up my eyes and see Estella looking in at one of the wooden windows of the forge.", "나 스스로도 결코 알지 못했는데, 내가 어찌 말할 수 있겠는가? 내가 두려워한 것은, 언젠가 어느 불운한 순간에 가장 꾀죄죄하고 가장 천한 꼴을 하고 있던 내가 문득 눈을 들었다가, 에스텔라가 대장간의 나무 창문 하나로 들여다보고 있는 것을 보게 되지나 않을까 하는 것이었다."], ["I was haunted by the fear that she would, sooner or later, find me out, with a black face and hands, doing the coarsest part of my work, and would exult over me and despise me.", "나는 그녀가 머지않아, 얼굴과 손이 새까매진 채 일 가운데서도 가장 거친 부분을 하고 있는 나를 발견하고는, 나를 두고 의기양양해하며 멸시하지나 않을까 하는 두려움에 사로잡혀 있었다."], ["Often after dark, when I was pulling the bellows for Joe, and we were singing Old Clem, and when the thought how we used to sing it at Miss Havisham’s would seem to show me Estella’s face in the fire, with her pretty hair fluttering in the wind and her eyes scorning me,—often at such a time I would look towards those panels of black night in the wall which the wooden windows then were, and would fancy that I saw her just drawing her face away, and would believe that she had come at last.", "어둠이 내린 뒤 조를 위해 풀무를 당기며 우리가 「올드 클렘」을 부를 때면, 그리고 미스 해비셤의 집에서 그 노래를 부르던 일이 떠오르며 불길 속에 에스텔라의 얼굴이 — 어여쁜 머리칼을 바람에 나부끼고 두 눈으로 나를 비웃는 얼굴이 — 보이는 듯할 때면, 그런 순간이면 나는 그때 나무 창문이던 그 벽의 새까만 밤의 판자들 쪽을 바라보며, 그녀가 막 얼굴을 거두어 가는 것을 본 것만 같은 환상에 빠지고, 끝내 그녀가 찾아온 것이라 믿곤 했다."]], [["After that, when we went in to supper, the place and the meal would have a more homely look than ever, and I would feel more ashamed of home than ever, in my own ungracious breast.", "그러고 나서 저녁을 먹으러 안으로 들어가면, 그 자리와 그 식사가 어느 때보다 더 초라해 보였고, 나는 내 볼썽사나운 가슴속에서 어느 때보다 더 집을 부끄러워하곤 했다."]], [["# Chapter XV.", "# Chapter XV."]], [["As I was getting too big for Mr. Wopsle’s great-aunt’s room, my education under that preposterous female terminated.", "내가 워프슬 씨 종조모의 방에 다니기에는 몸이 너무 커진 탓에, 그 터무니없는 노파 밑에서 받던 내 교육은 끝이 났다."], ["Not, however, until Biddy had imparted to me everything she knew, from the little catalogue of prices, to a comic song she had once bought for a half-penny.", "그러나 비디가 자신이 아는 것을 모조리 — 자그마한 물가표에서부터 언젠가 반 페니를 주고 산 우스개 노래에 이르기까지 — 내게 전수해 준 뒤에야 그리 된 일이었다."], ["Although the only coherent part of the latter piece of literature were the opening lines,", "그 후자의 문학 작품에서 그나마 앞뒤가 맞는 부분이라고는 첫머리 몇 줄뿐이었지만,"]], [["When I went to Lunnon town sirs, Too rul loo rul Too rul loo rul Wasn’t I done very brown sirs?", "내가 런던 거리에 갔을 때 여러분, 투 룰 루 룰 투 룰 루 룰 나 보기 좋게 골탕먹지 않았던가요 여러분?"], ["Too rul loo rul Too rul loo rul", "투 룰 루 룰 투 룰 루 룰"]], [["—still, in my desire to be wiser, I got this composition by heart with the utmost gravity; nor do I recollect that I questioned its merit, except that I thought (as I still do) the amount of Too rul somewhat in excess of the poetry.", "— 그래도 나는 더 현명해지고 싶다는 일념으로 이 작품을 더없이 진지하게 외워 버렸다. 그리고 그 가치를 의심한 기억도 없다. 다만 (지금도 그렇게 생각하지만) 투 룰의 양이 시의 분량에 비해 다소 지나치다고 여겼을 뿐이다."], ["In my hunger for information, I made proposals to Mr. Wopsle to bestow some intellectual crumbs upon me, with which he kindly complied.", "지식에 굶주린 나머지 나는 워프슬 씨에게 지적 빵부스러기라도 몇 점 베풀어 달라고 청했고, 그는 친절히 응해 주었다."], ["As it turned out, however, that he only wanted me for a dramatic lay-figure, to be contradicted and embraced and wept over and bullied and clutched and stabbed and knocked about in a variety of ways, I soon declined that course of instruction; though not until Mr. Wopsle in his poetic fury had severely mauled me.", "그러나 알고 보니 그가 나를 필요로 한 것은 오로지 연극의 모형 인형으로서, 반박당하고 끌어안기고 그 위에서 눈물 흘려지고 윽박질러지고 움켜잡히고 칼에 찔리고 갖가지 방식으로 두들겨 맞기 위함이었으니, 나는 이내 그 가르침의 길을 사양하고 말았다. 다만 워프슬 씨가 자신의 시적 광란 속에서 나를 호되게 두들겨 패고 난 뒤의 일이었지만."]], [["Whatever I acquired, I tried to impart to Joe.", "내가 무엇을 익히든, 나는 그것을 조에게 전수하려 했다."], ["This statement sounds so well, that I cannot in my conscience let it pass unexplained.", "이 말은 듣기에 너무도 그럴듯하므로, 나는 양심상 이것을 설명 없이 넘어갈 수가 없다."], ["I wanted to make Joe less ignorant and common, that he might be worthier of my society and less open to Estella’s reproach.", "나는 조가 덜 무지하고 덜 천박해져, 내 교제 상대로서 더 가치 있는 사람이 되고 에스텔라의 비난에 덜 노출되기를 바랐던 것이다."]], [["The old Battery out on the marshes was our place of study, and a broken slate and a short piece of slate-pencil were our educational implements: to which Joe always added a pipe of tobacco.", "습지에 나가 있는 오래된 포대가 우리의 공부 장소였고, 깨진 석판 하나와 짤막한 석필 한 토막이 우리의 교육 도구였다. 거기에 조는 늘 담배 한 대를 더했다."], ["I never knew Joe to remember anything from one Sunday to another, or to acquire, under my tuition, any piece of information whatever.", "나는 조가 한 일요일에서 다음 일요일까지 무언가를 기억해 내거나, 내 가르침 아래 그 어떤 지식이든 한 조각이라도 익히는 것을 본 적이 없다."], ["Yet he would smoke his pipe at the Battery with a far more sagacious air than anywhere else,—even with a learned air,—as if he considered himself to be advancing immensely. Dear fellow, I hope he did.", "그래도 그는 그 포대에서 담배를 피울 때면 다른 어디에서보다 훨씬 더 슬기로운 표정으로 — 심지어 학식 있는 표정으로 — 마치 자신이 엄청나게 발전하고 있다고 여기는 듯했다. 가엾은 친구, 부디 그가 정말 그러했기를 바란다."]], [["It was pleasant and quiet, out there with the sails on the river passing beyond the earthwork, and sometimes, when the tide was low, looking as if they belonged to sunken ships that were still sailing on at the bottom of the water.", "거기 바깥은 즐겁고 고요했다. 강 위의 돛들이 토루 너머로 지나가고, 이따금 썰물 때면 그 돛들이 마치 물밑에서 아직도 항해를 계속하는 가라앉은 배들에 달린 것처럼 보이기도 했다."], ["Whenever I watched the vessels standing out to sea with their white sails spread, I somehow thought of Miss Havisham and Estella; and whenever the light struck aslant, afar off, upon a cloud or sail or green hillside or water-line, it was just the same.—Miss Havisham and Estella and the strange house and the strange life appeared to have something to do with everything that was picturesque.", "하얀 돛을 활짝 펼친 채 바다로 나아가는 배들을 바라볼 때마다, 나는 어쩐지 해비셤 양과 에스텔라를 떠올렸다. 그리고 저 멀리 구름이나 돛이나 푸른 언덕 비탈이나 수평선 위로 빛이 비스듬히 내리쬘 때마다, 그것은 꼭 마찬가지였다. 해비셤 양과 에스텔라와 그 기이한 집과 그 기이한 삶은, 그림처럼 아름다운 모든 것과 어떤 연관이 있는 듯싶었다."]], [["One Sunday when Joe, greatly enjoying his pipe, had so plumed himself on being “most awful dull,” that I had given him up for the day, I lay on the earthwork for some time with my chin on my hand, descrying traces of Miss Havisham and Estella all over the prospect, in the sky and in the water, until at last I resolved to mention a thought concerning them that had been much in my head.", "어느 일요일, 조가 담배를 무척이나 즐기며 자신이 \"지독히도 둔하다\"는 것에 어찌나 우쭐대던지 내가 그날은 그를 포기해 버렸을 때, 나는 한동안 토루 위에 턱을 손에 괴고 누워, 하늘에서도 물에서도 온 풍경 도처에서 해비셤 양과 에스텔라의 자취를 더듬어 찾다가, 마침내 그들에 관해 줄곧 내 머릿속에 맴돌던 한 가지 생각을 입 밖에 내기로 마음먹었다."]], [["“Joe,”", "\"조.\""], ["said I; “don’t you think I ought to make Miss Havisham a visit?”", "내가 말했다. \"내가 해비셤 양을 한번 찾아뵙는 게 좋지 않을까요?\""]], [["“Well, Pip,”", "\"글쎄다, 핍.\""], ["returned Joe, slowly considering.", "조가 천천히 곰곰이 생각하며 대답했다."], ["“What for?”", "\"무엇 때문에?\""]], [["“What for, Joe?", "\"무엇 때문이라뇨, 조?"], ["What is any visit made for?”", "어떤 방문이든 무엇 때문에 하는 거겠어요?\""]], [["“There is some wisits p’r’aps,”", "\"방문 중에는 말이다, 핍.\""], ["said Joe, “as for ever remains open to the question, Pip.", "조가 말했다. \"영영 따져 봐도 답이 안 나오는 방문도 있는 법이지."], ["But in regard to wisiting Miss Havisham. She might think you wanted something,—expected something of her.”", "한데 해비셤 양을 찾아뵙는 일로 말하자면, 그분은 네가 무언가를 바란다고 — 자기한테서 무언가를 기대한다고 여기실지도 모른다.\""]], [["“Don’t you think I might say that I did not, Joe?”", "\"내가 그렇지 않다고 말하면 되지 않을까요, 조?\""]], [["“You might, old chap,”", "\"그럴 수도 있겠지, 이 친구야.\""], ["said Joe.", "조가 말했다."], ["“And she might credit it.", "\"그러면 그분이 그 말을 믿어 주실 수도 있고."], ["Similarly she mightn’t.”", "마찬가지로 안 믿으실 수도 있고.\""]], [["Joe felt, as I did, that he had made a point there, and he pulled hard at his pipe to keep himself from weakening it by repetition.", "조는 나와 마찬가지로 자기가 거기서 한 가지 핵심을 짚었다고 느꼈고, 그것을 거듭 말해 약화시키지 않으려고 담뱃대를 힘껏 빨아 댔다."]], [["“You see, Pip,”", "\"있잖아, 핍.\""], ["Joe pursued, as soon as he was past that danger, “Miss Havisham done the handsome thing by you.", "그 위기를 넘기자마자 조가 말을 이었다. \"해비셤 양은 너한테 후하게 대해 주셨다."], ["When Miss Havisham done the handsome thing by you, she called me back to say to me as that were all.”", "해비셤 양이 너한테 후하게 대해 주셨을 때, 나를 다시 불러서 그게 전부라고 말씀하셨지.\""]], [["“Yes, Joe.", "\"네, 조."], ["I heard her.”", "저도 들었어요.\""]], [["“ALL,” Joe repeated, very emphatically.", "\"전부라고.\" 조가 무척 힘주어 거듭 말했다."]], [["“Yes, Joe.", "\"네, 조."], ["I tell you, I heard her.”", "말씀드렸잖아요, 저도 들었다고.\""]], [["“Which I meantersay, Pip, it might be that her meaning were,—Make a end on it!—As you was!—Me to the North, and you to the South!—Keep in sunders!”", "\"그러니까 내 말은, 핍, 그분 뜻이 어쩌면 이런 거였는지도 모른다는 거다. 이걸로 끝을 내자! 지금 이대로! 나는 북으로, 너는 남으로! 따로따로 떨어져 지내자!\""]], [["I had thought of that too, and it was very far from comforting to me to find that he had thought of it; for it seemed to render it more probable.", "나도 그 생각을 해 본 터였고, 그가 그런 생각을 했다는 것을 알게 되니 도무지 위안이 되지 않았다. 그것이 그 가능성을 더 짙게 만드는 듯했기 때문이다."]], [["“But, Joe.”", "\"그런데, 조.\""]], [["“Yes, old chap.”", "\"왜, 이 친구야.\""]], [["“Here am I, getting on in the first year of my time, and, since the day of my being bound, I have never thanked Miss Havisham, or asked after her, or shown that I remember her.”", "\"이렇게 도제 생활 첫해를 보내고 있는데, 계약을 맺은 그날 이후로 저는 해비셤 양께 감사를 드린 적도, 안부를 여쭌 적도, 제가 그분을 기억하고 있다는 표시를 한 적도 없어요.\""]], [["“That’s true, Pip; and unless you was to turn her out a set of shoes all four round,—and which I meantersay as even a set of shoes all four round might not be acceptable as a present, in a total wacancy of hoofs—”", "\"그건 맞다, 핍. 그리고 네가 그분께 신발 한 벌을 네 짝 다 만들어 드리지 않는 한 — 한데 내 말은, 발굽이라곤 도무지 없는 분께는 신발 네 짝짜리 한 벌도 선물로 받아들여지지 않을 수도 있다는 거다만—\""]], [["“I don’t mean that sort of remembrance, Joe; I don’t mean a present.”", "\"그런 식의 기억을 말하는 게 아니에요, 조. 선물 같은 걸 말하는 게 아니라고요.\""]], [["But Joe had got the idea of a present in his head and must harp upon it.", "그러나 조는 일단 선물이라는 생각을 머릿속에 담아 두자 그것을 물고 늘어져야만 했다. \"아니면 말이다.\""], ["“Or even,”", "그가 말했다."], ["said he, “if you was helped to knocking her up a new chain for the front door,—or say a gross or two of shark-headed screws for general use,—or some light fancy article, such as a toasting-fork when she took her muffins,—or a gridiron when she took a sprat or such like—”", "\"네가 거들어서 그분 현관문에 새 사슬을 하나 달아 드린다든지 — 아니면 두루 쓰시라고 상어 머리 나사못 한두 그로스라든지 — 아니면 가벼운 멋들어진 물건, 이를테면 머핀을 드실 때 쓰실 토스트 포크라든지 — 아니면 스프랫이나 그런 걸 드실 때 쓰실 석쇠라든지—\""]], [["“I don’t mean any present at all, Joe,”", "\"무슨 선물이든 아예 말하는 게 아니에요, 조.\""], ["I interposed.", "내가 끼어들었다."]], [["“Well,”", "\"글쎄.\""], ["said Joe, still harping on it as though I had particularly pressed it, “if I was yourself, Pip, I wouldn’t.", "조가, 마치 내가 그것을 특별히 강하게 밀어붙이기라도 한 것처럼 여전히 그 이야기를 물고 늘어지며 말했다. \"내가 너라면, 핍, 나는 안 하겠다."], ["No, I would not.", "그래, 절대로 안 해."], ["For what’s a door-chain when she’s got one always up?", "그분이 늘 사슬을 걸어 두고 계신데 현관 사슬이 다 무슨 소용이겠냐?"], ["And shark-headers is open to misrepresentations.", "게다가 상어 머리 나사못은 오해를 살 여지가 있어."], ["And if it was a toasting-fork, you’d go into brass and do yourself no credit.", "그리고 토스트 포크라면 너는 놋쇠 세공에 손을 대게 될 텐데 영 체면이 안 설 거다."], ["And the oncommonest workman can’t show himself oncommon in a gridiron,—for a gridiron IS a gridiron,”", "또 아무리 솜씨 좋은 직공이라도 석쇠로는 솜씨 좋은 티를 못 내. 석쇠는 어디까지나 석쇠니까.\""], ["said Joe, steadfastly impressing it upon me, as if he were endeavouring to rouse me from a fixed delusion, “and you may haim at what you like, but a gridiron it will come out, either by your leave or again your leave, and you can’t help yourself—”", "조가, 마치 나를 어떤 고정된 망상에서 깨우려 애쓰기라도 하듯 그 점을 내게 단단히 새겨 넣으며 말했다. \"네가 무얼 노리든 좋다만, 결국 나오는 건 석쇠뿐이야. 네가 좋든 싫든 석쇠로 나올 테고, 너로서는 어쩔 도리가 없어—\""]], [["“My dear Joe,”", "\"이봐요, 조.\""], ["I cried, in desperation, taking hold of his coat, “don’t go on in that way.", "내가 절망에 빠져 그의 웃옷을 붙들며 외쳤다. \"그런 식으로 계속 말씀하지 마세요."], ["I never thought of making Miss Havisham any present.”", "저는 해비셤 양께 무슨 선물을 드릴 생각 같은 건 한 적도 없어요.\""]], [["“No, Pip,”", "\"그래, 핍.\""], ["Joe assented, as if he had been contending for that, all along; “and what I say to you is, you are right, Pip.”", "조가, 마치 줄곧 그 점을 두고 다투기라도 했다는 듯 수긍했다. \"그래서 내가 너한테 하는 말이, 네 말이 옳다는 거다, 핍.\""]], [["“Yes, Joe; but what I wanted to say, was, that as we are rather slack just now, if you would give me a half-holiday to-morrow, I think I would go uptown and make a call on Miss Est—Havisham.”", "\"네, 조. 그런데 제가 하고 싶었던 말은, 요즘 일이 좀 한가하니까 내일 반나절만 쉬게 해 주시면, 읍내에 올라가 에스 — 해비셤 양을 한번 찾아뵐까 한다는 거예요.\""]], [["“Which her name,”", "\"한데 그분 성함은 말이다.\""], ["said Joe, gravely, “ain’t Estavisham, Pip, unless she have been rechris’ened.”", "조가 진지하게 말했다. \"에스타비셤이 아니다, 핍. 다시 세례를 받으셨다면 또 모를까.\""]], [["“I know, Joe, I know. It was a slip of mine.", "\"알아요, 조, 알아요. 제 말실수예요."], ["What do you think of it, Joe?”", "어떻게 생각하세요, 조?\""]], [["In brief, Joe thought that if I thought well of it, he thought well of it.", "요컨대, 조는 내가 그것을 좋게 여긴다면 자기도 좋게 여긴다는 생각이었다."], ["But, he was particular in stipulating that if I were not received with cordiality, or if I were not encouraged to repeat my visit as a visit which had no ulterior object but was simply one of gratitude for a favour received, then this experimental trip should have no successor.", "그러나 그는, 만약 내가 따뜻하게 맞아들여지지 않거나, 다른 속셈은 전혀 없이 베풀어 주신 호의에 대한 감사의 표시일 뿐인 방문으로서 다시 찾아오라는 권유를 받지 못한다면, 이 시험적인 나들이에 다음번이란 없어야 한다는 것을 특히 까다롭게 못 박았다."], ["By these conditions I promised to abide.", "나는 이 조건들을 지키겠노라 약속했다."]], [["Now, Joe kept a journeyman at weekly wages whose name was Orlick.", "그런데 조는 주급을 주고 직공 하나를 부리고 있었으니, 그 이름은 올릭이었다."], ["He pretended that his Christian name was Dolge,—a clear Impossibility,—but he was a fellow of that obstinate disposition that I believe him to have been the prey of no delusion in this particular, but wilfully to have imposed that name upon the village as an affront to its understanding.", "그는 자기 세례명이 돌지라고 우겨 댔는데 — 그건 명백히 있을 수 없는 일이었지만 — 그는 워낙 고집스러운 기질의 사내라서, 나는 그가 이 점에서 어떤 망상에 사로잡혀 있었던 것이 아니라, 마을 사람들의 분별력을 모욕하려고 일부러 그 이름을 마을에 강요했던 것이라고 믿는다."], ["He was a broadshouldered loose-limbed swarthy fellow of great strength, never in a hurry, and always slouching.", "그는 어깨가 떡 벌어지고 팔다리가 늘어진 가무잡잡한 사내로, 힘이 장사였으며, 결코 서두르는 법이 없고, 늘 구부정하게 어슬렁거렸다."], ["He never even seemed to come to his work on purpose, but would slouch in as if by mere accident; and when he went to the Jolly Bargemen to eat his dinner, or went away at night, he would slouch out, like Cain or the Wandering Jew, as if he had no idea where he was going and no intention of ever coming back.", "그는 일하러 올 때조차 일부러 오는 것처럼 보이는 법이 없이, 그저 우연인 듯 어슬렁어슬렁 들어왔다. 그리고 졸리 바지선 주막에 점심을 먹으러 가거나 밤에 돌아갈 때면, 카인이나 떠돌이 유대인처럼, 마치 어디로 가는지도 모르고 다시 돌아올 작정도 없다는 듯 어슬렁어슬렁 나갔다."], ["He lodged at a sluice-keeper’s out on the marshes, and on working-days would come slouching from his hermitage, with his hands in his pockets and his dinner loosely tied in a bundle round his neck and dangling on his back.", "그는 습지에 나가 있는 어느 수문지기 집에 묵었는데, 일하는 날이면 두 손을 주머니에 찔러 넣고 점심을 보따리에 헐겁게 싸 목에 둘러 등에 대롱대롱 매단 채, 그 은둔처에서 어슬렁어슬렁 걸어 나오곤 했다."], ["On Sundays he mostly lay all day on the sluice-gates, or stood against ricks and barns.", "일요일이면 대개 종일 수문 위에 드러눕거나 낟가리와 곳간에 기대어 서 있었다."], ["He always slouched, locomotively, with his eyes on the ground; and, when accosted or otherwise required to raise them, he looked up in a half-resentful, half-puzzled way, as though the only thought he ever had was, that it was rather an odd and injurious fact that he should never be thinking.", "그는 늘 땅바닥에 눈을 박은 채 어슬렁어슬렁 옮겨 다녔다. 그리고 누가 말을 걸거나 달리 눈을 들어야 할 때면, 절반은 분개하고 절반은 어리둥절한 표정으로 올려다보았으니, 마치 그가 품은 유일한 생각이라고는 자기가 결코 아무 생각도 하지 않는다는 것이 다소 묘하고도 부당한 사실이라는 것뿐이라는 듯했다."]], [["This morose journeyman had no liking for me.", "이 시무룩한 직공은 나를 좋아하지 않았다."], ["When I was very small and timid, he gave me to understand that the Devil lived in a black corner of the forge, and that he knew the fiend very well: also that it was necessary to make up the fire, once in seven years, with a live boy, and that I might consider myself fuel.", "내가 아주 어리고 겁이 많았을 적에, 그는 대장간의 시커먼 구석에 악마가 살고 있으며 자기는 그 악귀를 아주 잘 안다고 내게 일러 주었다. 또한 칠 년에 한 번씩 살아 있는 소년으로 불을 지펴 줘야 하는 법이며, 나야말로 그 땔감이라 여겨도 된다고 했다."], ["When I became Joe’s ’prentice, Orlick was perhaps confirmed in some suspicion that I should displace him; howbeit, he liked me still less.", "내가 조의 도제가 되자, 올릭은 어쩌면 내가 자기 자리를 밀어낼 거라는 어떤 의심을 굳혔는지도 모른다. 어쨌든 그는 나를 한층 덜 좋아하게 되었다."], ["Not that he ever said anything, or did anything, openly importing hostility; I only noticed that he always beat his sparks in my direction, and that whenever I sang Old Clem, he came in out of time.", "그가 공공연히 적의를 드러내는 무슨 말을 하거나 무슨 짓을 한 것은 아니었다. 다만 나는, 그가 늘 자기 불똥을 내 쪽으로 튀긴다는 것과, 내가 늙은 클렘을 부를 때면 언제나 박자를 틀리게 끼어든다는 것을 알아챘을 뿐이다."]], [["Dolge Orlick was at work and present, next day, when I reminded Joe of my half-holiday.", "이튿날, 내가 조에게 내 반나절 휴가를 일깨웠을 때, 돌지 올릭이 일을 하며 그 자리에 있었다."], ["He said nothing at the moment, for he and Joe had just got a piece of hot iron between them, and I was at the bellows; but by and by he said, leaning on his hammer,—", "그 순간 그는 아무 말도 하지 않았는데, 그와 조가 마침 시뻘건 쇳덩이를 사이에 두고 있었고 나는 풀무를 잡고 있었기 때문이다. 그러나 이윽고 그가 망치에 몸을 기대며 말했다."]], [["“Now, master! Sure you’re not a-going to favour only one of us.", "\"이봐요, 주인장! 우리 중 한 사람만 봐주실 건 아니겠지요."], ["If Young Pip has a half-holiday, do as much for Old Orlick.”", "젊은 핍이 반나절 쉰다면, 늙은 올릭한테도 그만큼 해 주쇼.\""], ["I suppose he was about five-and-twenty, but he usually spoke of himself as an ancient person.", "그는 아마 스물다섯 살쯤 되었으리라 짐작되지만, 늘 자신을 노인네인 양 일컬었다."]], [["“Why, what’ll you do with a half-holiday, if you get it?” said Joe.", "\"아니, 자네가 반나절 쉬어서 뭘 하려고?\" 조가 말했다."]], [["“What’ll I do with it!", "\"내가 그걸로 뭘 하느냐고요!"], ["What’ll he do with it?", "저 녀석은 그걸로 뭘 하는데요?"], ["I’ll do as much with it as him,”", "나도 저 녀석만큼은 하겠소.\""], ["said Orlick.", "올릭이 말했다."]], [["“As to Pip, he’s going up town,”", "\"핍으로 말하면, 읍내에 올라간다네.\""], ["said Joe.", "조가 말했다."]], [["“Well then, as to Old Orlick, he’s a-going up town,”", "\"그럼 늙은 올릭으로 말하면, 그도 읍내에 올라가지요.\""], ["retorted that worthy.", "그 잘난 사내가 받아쳤다."], ["“Two can go up town.", "\"둘이서 읍내에 올라갈 수 있잖소."], ["Tain’t only one wot can go up town.", "읍내에 올라갈 수 있는 게 한 사람뿐인 건 아니지요.\""]], [["“Don’t lose your temper,” said Joe.", "\"화내지 말게.\" 조가 말했다."]], [["“Shall if I like,”", "\"내키면 낼 거요.\""], ["growled Orlick.", "올릭이 으르렁거렸다."], ["“Some and their uptowning!", "\"원, 읍내 나들이 좋아하시네!"], ["Now, master! Come.", "자, 주인장! 어서요."], ["No favouring in this shop.", "이 가게에서 편애는 안 됩니다."], ["Be a man!”", "사내답게 구쇼!\""]], [["The master refusing to entertain the subject until the journeyman was in a better temper, Orlick plunged at the furnace, drew out a red-hot bar, made at me with it as if he were going to run it through my body, whisked it round my head, laid it on the anvil, hammered it out,—as if it were I, I thought, and the sparks were my spirting blood,—and finally said, when he had hammered himself hot and the iron cold, and he again leaned on his hammer,—", "직공이 좀 더 기분이 풀릴 때까지 주인이 그 문제를 거론하기를 거부하자, 올릭은 화덕으로 달려들어 시뻘겋게 단 쇠막대를 끄집어내더니, 그것으로 내 몸을 꿰뚫기라도 할 듯 나를 향해 달려들었다가, 내 머리 둘레로 휙휙 휘두르고는, 모루 위에 얹어 두들겨 폈다. 마치 그것이 나이고 불똥은 내가 뿜어내는 피이기라도 한 듯이, 나는 그렇게 느꼈다. 그러고는 마침내, 자기 몸은 뜨겁게 달구고 쇠는 식혀 놓은 다음 다시 망치에 몸을 기대며 말했다."]], [["“Now, master!”", "\"자, 주인장!\""]], [["“Are you all right now?”", "\"이제 괜찮은가?\""], ["demanded Joe.", "조가 물었다."]], [["“Ah!", "\"아!"], ["I am all right,” said gruff Old Orlick.", "난 괜찮소.\" 무뚝뚝한 늙은 올릭이 말했다."]], [["“Then, as in general you stick to your work as well as most men,”", "\"그럼, 자네가 대체로 웬만한 사람들 못지않게 일에 충실하니까.\""], ["said Joe, “let it be a half-holiday for all.”", "조가 말했다. \"모두에게 반나절 휴가로 하지.\""]], [["My sister had been standing silent in the yard, within hearing,—she was a most unscrupulous spy and listener,—and she instantly looked in at one of the windows.", "내 누나가 그동안 마당에 말없이 서서 — 그녀는 더없이 거리낌 없는 염탐꾼이자 엿듣기꾼이었다 — 죄다 듣고 있다가, 즉시 창문 하나로 안을 들여다보았다."]], [["“Like you, you fool!”", "\"꼴좋네, 이 바보 같은 양반!\""], ["said she to Joe, “giving holidays to great idle hulkers like that.", "그녀가 조에게 말했다. \"저런 게으르고 덩치만 큰 멍청이한테 휴가를 다 주고."], ["You are a rich man, upon my life, to waste wages in that way.", "정말이지 당신은 부자인가 봐, 그런 식으로 품삯을 낭비하다니."], ["I wish I was his master!”", "내가 저자의 주인이었으면 좋겠네!\""]], [["“You’d be everybody’s master, if you durst,” retorted Orlick, with an ill-favoured grin.", "\"당신은 배짱만 있으면 누구한테든 주인 노릇을 하려 들겠지.\" 올릭이 험상궂게 씩 웃으며 받아쳤다."]], [["(“Let her alone,”", "(\"저 사람 좀 내버려 두게.\""], ["said Joe.)", "조가 말했다.)"]], [["“I’d be a match for all noodles and all rogues,”", "\"나는 온갖 얼간이며 온갖 악당이며 다 상대해 줄 수 있어.\""], ["returned my sister, beginning to work herself into a mighty rage.", "누나가 점점 무서운 분노로 자신을 몰아가기 시작하며 대꾸했다."], ["“And I couldn’t be a match for the noodles, without being a match for your master, who’s the dunder-headed king of the noodles.", "\"그리고 얼간이들을 당해 내려면, 얼간이들의 멍청한 왕초인 당신 주인부터 당해 내야 할 테고."], ["And I couldn’t be a match for the rogues, without being a match for you, who are the blackest-looking and the worst rogue between this and France. Now!”", "또 악당들을 당해 내려면, 여기서 프랑스 사이에 가장 시커먼 상판을 한 최악의 악당인 너부터 당해 내야겠지. 어때!\""]], [["“You’re a foul shrew, Mother Gargery,”", "\"당신은 더러운 앙칼쟁이요, 가저리 아주머니.\""], ["growled the journeyman.", "직공이 으르렁거렸다."], ["“If that makes a judge of rogues, you ought to be a good’un.”", "\"그게 악당을 가려내는 안목이 된다면, 당신이야말로 제대로 가려내겠구려.\""]], [["(“Let her alone, will you?”", "(\"저 사람 좀 내버려 두라니까, 응?\""], ["said Joe.)", "조가 말했다.)"]], [["“What did you say?”", "\"뭐라고 했어?\""], ["cried my sister, beginning to scream.", "누나가 비명을 지르기 시작하며 외쳤다."], ["“What did you say?", "\"뭐라고 했냐고?"], ["What did that fellow Orlick say to me, Pip?", "저 올릭이란 작자가 나한테 뭐라고 했어, 핍?"], ["What did he call me, with my husband standing by? Oh! oh! oh!”", "내 남편이 옆에 멀쩡히 서 있는데, 나를 뭐라고 불렀어? 오! 오! 오!\" 이 외침들은 하나하나가 다 비명이었다."], ["Each of these exclamations was a shriek; and I must remark of my sister, what is equally true of all the violent women I have ever seen, that passion was no excuse for her, because it is undeniable that instead of lapsing into passion, she consciously and deliberately took extraordinary pains to force herself into it, and became blindly furious by regular stages; “what was the name he gave me before the base man who swore to defend me? Oh!", "그리고 나는 내 누나에 대해, 내가 이제껏 본 모든 사나운 여인들에게 똑같이 해당되는 사실을 한마디 짚어 두어야겠다. 즉 격정은 그녀의 변명이 될 수 없었으니, 그녀가 격정에 빠져드는 것이 아니라 의식적으로 또 일부러 비상한 애를 써서 스스로를 격정 속으로 몰아넣었으며, 차근차근 단계를 밟아 가며 눈먼 격분에 이르렀다는 것은 부정할 수 없는 사실이기 때문이다. \"나를 지켜 주겠다고 맹세한 비열한 사내 앞에서 저자가 나를 뭐라고 불렀냐고? 오!"], ["Hold me! Oh!”", "날 좀 붙들어 줘! 오!\""]], [["“Ah-h-h!”", "\"아-아-아!\""], ["growled the journeyman, between his teeth, “I’d hold you, if you was my wife.", "직공이 이를 갈며 으르렁거렸다. \"당신이 내 마누라였으면 내가 붙들어 주지."], ["I’d hold you under the pump, and choke it out of you.”", "펌프 밑에 처박고 졸라서 토해 내게 해 줄 텐데.\""]], [["(“I tell you, let her alone,” said Joe.)", "(\"내버려 두라고 했지.\" 조가 말했다.)"]], [["“Oh! To hear him!”", "\"오! 저 말 좀 들어 봐!\""], ["cried my sister, with a clap of her hands and a scream together,—which was her next stage.", "누나가, 박수를 치는 동시에 비명을 지르며 외쳤다. 그것은 그녀의 다음 단계였다."], ["“To hear the names he’s giving me!", "\"저자가 나한테 붙이는 이름들 좀 들어 보라고!"], ["That Orlick! In my own house!", "저 올릭이란 작자가! 내 집에서!"], ["Me, a married woman!", "어엿한 유부녀인 나한테!"], ["With my husband standing by! Oh! Oh!”", "내 남편이 옆에 멀쩡히 서 있는데! 오! 오!\""], ["Here my sister, after a fit of clappings and screamings, beat her hands upon her bosom and upon her knees, and threw her cap off, and pulled her hair down,—which were the last stages on her road to frenzy.", "여기서 누나는, 한바탕 박수와 비명을 터뜨린 뒤, 두 손으로 제 가슴과 무릎을 두드리고, 모자를 벗어 던지고, 머리를 풀어 헤쳤다. 그것이 광란에 이르는 그녀의 마지막 단계들이었다."], ["Being by this time a perfect Fury and a complete success, she made a dash at the door which I had fortunately locked.", "이쯤 되자 완벽한 복수의 여신이요 완전한 성공작이 된 그녀는, 다행히도 내가 잠가 두었던 문으로 달려들었다."]], [["What could the wretched Joe do now, after his disregarded parenthetical interruptions, but stand up to his journeyman, and ask him what he meant by interfering betwixt himself and Mrs. Joe; and further whether he was man enough to come on?", "이제 가엾은 조가 무엇을 할 수 있었겠는가. 무시당한 자신의 삽입구식 만류가 거듭된 끝에, 직공에게 맞서서 어찌하여 자기와 조 부인 사이에 끼어드느냐고 따져 묻고, 더 나아가 그럴 만한 사내라면 어디 한번 덤벼 보라고 하는 것밖에."], ["Old Orlick felt that the situation admitted of nothing less than coming on, and was on his defence straightway; so, without so much as pulling off their singed and burnt aprons, they went at one another, like two giants.", "늙은 올릭은 이 상황이 덤벼드는 것 외에는 달리 받아들일 여지가 없음을 직감하고 곧장 방어 자세를 취했다. 그리하여 둘은 그을리고 탄 앞치마를 벗을 새도 없이, 두 거인처럼 서로에게 달려들었다."], ["But, if any man in that neighbourhood could stand uplong against Joe, I never saw the man.", "그러나 그 동네에서 조에게 오래 맞설 수 있는 사내가 있었다면, 나는 그런 사내를 본 적이 없다."], ["Orlick, as if he had been of no more account than the pale young gentleman, was very soon among the coal-dust, and in no hurry to come out of it.", "올릭은, 마치 그 창백한 젊은 신사보다 나을 게 없다는 듯, 순식간에 석탄 먼지 속에 나뒹굴었고, 거기서 좀처럼 빠져나오려 들지 않았다."], ["Then Joe unlocked the door and picked up my sister, who had dropped insensible at the window (but who had seen the fight first, I think), and who was carried into the house and laid down, and who was recommended to revive, and would do nothing but struggle and clench her hands in Joe’s hair.", "그러자 조는 문을 열고, 창가에서 정신을 잃고 쓰러진 (그러나 내 생각에 먼저 싸움 구경부터 한) 누나를 들어 올렸다. 누나는 집 안으로 옮겨져 눕혀졌고, 정신을 차리도록 권유받았으나, 발버둥치며 조의 머리카락을 움켜쥐는 것 외에는 아무것도 하려 들지 않았다."], ["Then came that singular calm and silence which succeed all uproars; and then, with the vague sensation which I have always connected with such a lull,—namely, that it was Sunday, and somebody was dead,—I went upstairs to dress myself.", "그러고는 모든 소동 뒤에 뒤따르는 그 묘한 정적과 고요가 찾아왔다. 그러고는, 내가 늘 그런 잠잠함과 결부시켜 온 그 막연한 느낌 — 곧 일요일이고 누군가가 죽었다는 느낌 — 과 함께, 나는 옷을 갈아입으러 위층으로 올라갔다."]], [["When I came down again, I found Joe and Orlick sweeping up, without any other traces of discomposure than a slit in one of Orlick’s nostrils, which was neither expressive nor ornamental.", "내가 다시 내려와 보니, 조와 올릭은 마당을 쓸고 있었고, 올릭의 콧구멍 하나에 찢긴 자국이 난 것 말고는 흐트러진 흔적이라곤 없었는데, 그 자국은 인상적이지도 보기 좋지도 않았다."], ["A pot of beer had appeared from the Jolly Bargemen, and they were sharing it by turns in a peaceable manner.", "졸리 바지선 주막에서 맥주 한 단지가 와 있었고, 둘은 그것을 번갈아 가며 평화롭게 나눠 마시고 있었다."], ["The lull had a sedative and philosophical influence on Joe, who followed me out into the road to say, as a parting observation that might do me good, “On the Rampage, Pip, and off the Rampage, Pip:—such is Life!”", "그 잠잠함은 조에게 진정시키고 사색에 잠기게 하는 영향을 끼쳐서, 그는 내가 길로 나설 때까지 따라 나오더니, 내게 도움이 될 만한 작별의 한마디라며 이렇게 말했다. \"한바탕 날뛰다가도, 핍, 그 날뛰는 것이 가라앉기 마련이니, 핍 — 인생이란 그런 거다!\""]], [["With what absurd emotions (for we think the feelings that are very serious in a man quite comical in a boy) I found myself again going to Miss Havisham’s, matters little here.", "내가 어떤 우스꽝스러운 감정으로 (어른에게는 자못 진지한 감정도 소년에게서는 퍽 우스꽝스럽게 여기는 법이니) 다시금 해비셤 양의 집으로 가게 되었는지는, 여기서 별로 중요치 않다."], ["Nor, how I passed and repassed the gate many times before I could make up my mind to ring. Nor, how I debated whether I should go away without ringing; nor, how I should undoubtedly have gone, if my time had been my own, to come back.", "내가 종을 울릴 결심이 설 때까지 몇 번이고 대문 앞을 오갔는지도, 종을 울리지 않고 그냥 돌아갈까 어떻게 망설였는지도, 만약 내 시간이 온전히 내 것이었다면 틀림없이 다시 오기로 하고 그냥 돌아갔으리라는 것도, 다 마찬가지로 중요치 않다."]], [["Miss Sarah Pocket came to the gate. No Estella.", "세라 포켓 양이 대문으로 나왔다. 에스텔라는 없었다."]], [["“How, then? You here again?”", "\"어쩐 일이지? 또 왔어?\""], ["said Miss Pocket.", "포켓 양이 말했다."], ["“What do you want?”", "\"뭘 원하는 거지?\""]], [["When I said that I only came to see how Miss Havisham was, Sarah evidently deliberated whether or no she should send me about my business.", "내가 그저 해비셤 양이 어떻게 지내시는지 보러 왔을 뿐이라고 말하자, 세라는 나를 돌려보낼지 말지 분명히 망설였다."], ["But unwilling to hazard the responsibility, she let me in, and presently brought the sharp message that I was to “come up.”", "그러나 그 책임을 떠안기는 꺼려졌던지, 그녀는 나를 들여보냈고, 이윽고 \"올라오라\"는 무뚝뚝한 전갈을 가져왔다."]], [["Everything was unchanged, and Miss Havisham was alone.", "모든 것이 그대로였고, 해비셤 양은 혼자였다."]], [["“Well?”", "\"그래?\""], ["said she, fixing her eyes upon me.", "그녀가 내게 두 눈을 박으며 말했다."], ["“I hope you want nothing?", "\"아무것도 바라는 게 없기를 바란다."], ["You’ll get nothing.”", "너는 아무것도 못 얻을 테니.\""]], [["“No indeed, Miss Havisham.", "\"정말 아무것도 없어요, 해비셤 양."], ["I only wanted you to know that I am doing very well in my apprenticeship, and am always much obliged to you.”", "그저 제가 도제 생활을 아주 잘해 나가고 있으며, 늘 당신께 깊이 감사드리고 있다는 걸 알려 드리고 싶었을 뿐이에요.\""]], [["“There, there!”", "\"됐다, 됐어!\""], ["with the old restless fingers. “Come now and then; come on your birthday.—Ay!”", "예의 그 가만있지 못하는 손가락을 놀리며 그녀가 말했다. \"이따금 오너라."], ["she cried suddenly, turning herself and her chair towards me, “You are looking round for Estella? Hey?”", "네 생일에 오너라. 아!\" 그녀가 갑자기 외치며 자기 몸과 의자를 내 쪽으로 돌렸다. \"에스텔라를 찾아 두리번거리는 거지? 응?\""]], [["I had been looking round,—in fact, for Estella,—and I stammered that I hoped she was well.", "나는 두리번거리고 있었다 — 사실 에스텔라를 찾아서. 그래서 나는 그녀가 잘 지내기를 바란다고 더듬더듬 말했다."]], [["“Abroad,”", "\"외국에 가 있다.\""], ["said Miss Havisham; “educating for a lady; far out of reach; prettier than ever; admired by all who see her.", "해비셤 양이 말했다. \"숙녀가 되도록 교육받고 있지. 손이 닿지 않을 만큼 멀리."], ["Do you feel that you have lost her?”", "그 어느 때보다 예뻐졌고. 그 애를 보는 이마다 다들 우러러본단다. 그 애를 잃었다는 느낌이 드느냐?\""]], [["There was such a malignant enjoyment in her utterance of the last words, and she broke into such a disagreeable laugh, that I was at a loss what to say.", "그 마지막 말을 입에 올리는 그녀의 어조에는 어찌나 악의에 찬 즐거움이 서려 있었던지, 그리고 어찌나 듣기 싫은 웃음을 터뜨렸던지, 나는 무어라 말해야 할지 갈피를 잡지 못했다."], ["She spared me the trouble of considering, by dismissing me.", "그녀는 나를 물러가게 함으로써, 내가 고민하는 수고를 덜어 주었다."], ["When the gate was closed upon me by Sarah of the walnut-shell countenance, I felt more than ever dissatisfied with my home and with my trade and with everything; and that was all I took by that motion.", "호두 껍데기 같은 얼굴을 한 세라가 내 등 뒤로 대문을 닫았을 때, 나는 그 어느 때보다도 내 집과 내 일과 모든 것에 불만을 느꼈다. 그리고 그것이 그 나들이로 내가 얻은 전부였다."]], [["As I was loitering along the High Street, looking in disconsolately at the shop windows, and thinking what I would buy if I were a gentleman, who should come out of the bookshop but Mr.", "내가 큰길을 따라 어슬렁거리며 가게의 진열창들을 시무룩하게 들여다보면서, 내가 신사라면 무엇을 살까 궁리하고 있을 때, 책방에서 누가 나오나 했더니 다름 아닌 워프슬 씨였다."], ["Wopsle. Mr. Wopsle had in his hand the affecting tragedy of George Barnwell, in which he had that moment invested sixpence, with the view of heaping every word of it on the head of Pumblechook, with whom he was going to drink tea.", "워프슬 씨는 손에 그 애절한 비극 「조지 반웰」을 들고 있었는데, 그것은 바로 그 순간 그가 육 펜스를 들여 사들인 것으로, 펌블추크와 함께 차를 마실 참이었던 그가 그 한마디 한마디를 죄다 펌블추크의 머리 위에 쏟아부을 작정이었던 것이다."], ["No sooner did he see me, than he appeared to consider that a special Providence had put a ’prentice in his way to be read at; and he laid hold of me, and insisted on my accompanying him to the Pumblechookian parlour.", "그는 나를 보자마자, 마치 특별한 섭리가 자기 앞에 낭독을 들어 줄 도제 하나를 보내 주었다고 여기는 듯했다. 그러더니 나를 붙들고는, 펌블추크의 응접실까지 자기와 동행하라고 우겨 댔다."], ["As I knew it would be miserable at home, and as the nights were dark and the way was dreary, and almost any companionship on the road was better than none, I made no great resistance; consequently, we turned into Pumblechook’s just as the street and the shops were lighting up.", "집에 가 봤자 비참하리라는 것을 아는 데다, 밤은 어둡고 길은 음산했으며, 길동무라면 그 누구든 없는 것보다는 나았으므로, 나는 별 저항을 하지 않았다. 그리하여 우리는, 거리와 가게들에 막 불이 켜질 무렵 펌블추크의 집으로 들어섰다."]], [["As I never assisted at any other representation of George Barnwell, I don’t know how long it may usually take; but I know very well that it took until half-past nine o’ clock that night, and that when Mr.", "나는 「조지 반웰」의 다른 어떤 공연에도 입회한 적이 없으니, 그것이 보통 얼마나 걸리는지는 모른다. 그러나 그날 밤 그것이 아홉 시 반까지 걸렸다는 것만은 똑똑히 안다."], ["Wopsle got into Newgate, I thought he never would go to the scaffold, he became so much slower than at any former period of his disgraceful career.", "그리고 워프슬 씨가 뉴게이트 감옥에 이르렀을 때, 나는 그가 도무지 교수대로 가지 않으리라 여겼으니, 그가 그 치욕스러운 행로의 그 어느 시기보다도 훨씬 더 느려졌기 때문이다."], ["I thought it a little too much that he should complain of being cut short in his flower after all, as if he had not been running to seed, leaf after leaf, ever since his course began.", "나는 그가 결국 한창때에 꺾여 버린다고 한탄하는 것이 좀 지나치다 싶었다. 마치 자기 행로가 시작된 이래로 잎이 하나둘 떨어지듯 줄곧 시들어 가고 있지 않았다는 듯이 말이다."], ["This, however, was a mere question of length and wearisomeness.", "그러나 이것은 단지 길이와 지루함의 문제일 뿐이었다."], ["What stung me, was the identification of the whole affair with my unoffending self.", "나를 따끔하게 쑤신 것은, 그 사건 전체가 아무 죄 없는 나 자신과 동일시되었다는 점이었다."], ["When Barnwell began to go wrong, I declare that I felt positively apologetic, Pumblechook’s indignant stare so taxed me with it.", "반웰이 그릇된 길로 들어서기 시작했을 때, 단언컨대 나는 정말이지 미안한 심정마저 느꼈으니, 펌블추크의 분개한 응시가 어찌나 그 죄를 내게 추궁해 댔던지."], ["Wopsle, too, took pains to present me in the worst light.", "워프슬 또한 나를 가장 나쁜 모습으로 그려 내려고 애를 썼다."], ["At once ferocious and maudlin, I was made to murder my uncle with no extenuating circumstances whatever; Millwood put me down in argument, on every occasion; it became sheer monomania in my master’s daughter to care a button for me; and all I can say for my gasping and procrastinating conduct on the fatal morning, is, that it was worthy of the general feebleness of my character.", "흉포하면서도 눈물겨운 모습으로, 나는 그 어떤 정상 참작의 여지도 없이 내 숙부를 살해한 자로 둔갑했다. 밀우드는 매번 논쟁에서 나를 꺾어 눌렀다. 내 주인의 딸이 나 같은 것에 손톱만큼이라도 마음을 쓴다는 것은 순전한 편집광적 망상이 되어 버렸다."], ["Even after I was happily hanged and Wopsle had closed the book, Pumblechook sat staring at me, and shaking his head, and saying, “Take warning, boy, take warning!”", "그리고 그 운명의 아침에 내가 보인 헐떡이며 우물쭈물하던 행태에 대해 내가 변명할 수 있는 것이라곤, 그것이 내 성품의 전반적 무기력함에 걸맞은 것이었다는 말뿐이다. 내가 무사히 교수형을 당하고 워프슬이 책을 덮은 뒤에도, 펌블추크는 앉아서 나를 빤히 노려보고, 고개를 절레절레 흔들며, \"정신 차려라, 얘야, 정신 차려!\""], ["as if it were a well-known fact that I contemplated murdering a near relation, provided I could only induce one to have the weakness to become my benefactor.", "하고 말했다. 마치 내가, 누군가를 꾀어 내 은인이 되는 약점을 부리게만 할 수 있다면 가까운 친척을 살해할 작정이라는 것이 익히 알려진 사실이기라도 하다는 듯이."]], [["It was a very dark night when it was all over, and when I set out with Mr. Wopsle on the walk home.", "그 모든 게 끝나고, 내가 워프슬 씨와 함께 집으로 걸어 나섰을 때는 무척 어두운 밤이었다."], ["Beyond town, we found a heavy mist out, and it fell wet and thick.", "읍내를 벗어나자, 우리는 짙은 안개를 만났고, 그것은 축축하고 두텁게 내려앉았다."], ["The turnpike lamp was a blur, quite out of the lamp’s usual place apparently, and its rays looked solid substance on the fog.", "통행료 징수소의 등불은 흐릿하게 번져, 보아하니 평소의 자리에서 한참 벗어나 있는 듯했고, 그 빛줄기는 안개 위에서 단단한 물체처럼 보였다."], ["We were noticing this, and saying how that the mist rose with a change of wind from a certain quarter of our marshes, when we came upon a man, slouching under the lee of the turnpike house.", "우리가 그것을 눈여겨보며, 우리 습지의 어느 방향에서 바람이 바뀌면 안개가 피어오른다고 이야기하고 있을 때, 우리는 통행료 징수소 바람막이 쪽에 어슬렁거리며 서 있는 한 사내와 마주쳤다."]], [["“Halloa!”", "\"여어!\""], ["we said, stopping. “Orlick there?”", "우리는 걸음을 멈추며 말했다. \"올릭인가?\""]], [["“Ah!”", "\"아!\""], ["he answered, slouching out. “I was standing by a minute, on the chance of company.”", "그가 어슬렁어슬렁 걸어 나오며 대답했다. \"혹시 길동무라도 있을까 해서 잠깐 서 있었지요.\""]], [["“You are late,” I remarked.", "\"늦었구먼.\" 내가 한마디 했다."]], [["Orlick not unnaturally answered, “Well?", "올릭은 당연하다는 듯 대꾸했다. \"그래?"], ["And you’re late.”", "댁들도 늦었잖소.\""]], [["“We have been,” said Mr.", "\"우리는 말이오.\""], ["Wopsle, exalted with his late performance,—“we have been indulging, Mr. Orlick, in an intellectual evening.”", "방금 끝낸 자기 공연으로 한껏 들떠 있던 워프슬 씨가 말했다. \"우리는 말이오, 올릭 씨, 지적인 저녁 시간을 만끽하고 오는 길이라오.\""]], [["Old Orlick growled, as if he had nothing to say about that, and we all went on together.", "늙은 올릭은 그에 대해 할 말이 없다는 듯 으르렁거렸고, 우리는 다 함께 걸음을 이었다."], ["I asked him presently whether he had been spending his half-holiday up and down town?", "나는 이윽고 그에게 반나절 휴가를 읍내에서 이리저리 다니며 보냈느냐고 물었다."]], [["“Yes,”", "\"그래.\""], ["said he, “all of it.", "그가 말했다. \"내내 그랬지."], ["I come in behind yourself.", "댁네 바로 뒤를 따라 들어왔어."], ["I didn’t see you, but I must have been pretty close behind you.", "댁네를 보진 못했지만, 꽤 가까이 뒤따라온 게 틀림없어."], ["By the by, the guns is going again.”", "그건 그렇고, 또 대포가 울리고 있구먼.\""]], [["“At the Hulks?” said I.", "\"감옥선에서?\" 내가 말했다."]], [["“Ay! There’s some of the birds flown from the cages.", "\"그래! 새장에서 새 몇 마리가 날아갔나 봐."], ["The guns have been going since dark, about.", "어두워질 무렵부터 대포가 울리고 있어."], ["You’ll hear one presently.”", "이제 곧 한 발 들릴 거야.\""]], [["In effect, we had not walked many yards further, when the well-remembered boom came towards us, deadened by the mist, and heavily rolled away along the low grounds by the river, as if it were pursuing and threatening the fugitives.", "과연, 우리가 몇 야드 더 채 걷기도 전에, 그 익히 기억하는 굉음이 안개에 먹먹해진 채 우리 쪽으로 다가왔고, 강가의 낮은 땅을 따라 무겁게 굴러갔다. 마치 도망자들을 뒤쫓으며 위협하기라도 하는 듯이."]], [["“A good night for cutting off in,”", "\"빠져나가기 딱 좋은 밤이지.\""], ["said Orlick.", "올릭이 말했다."], ["“We’d be puzzled how to bring down a jail-bird on the wing, to-night.”", "\"이런 밤엔 날아오른 감옥새를 떨어뜨리기가 영 난감할 거야.\""]], [["The subject was a suggestive one to me, and I thought about it in silence. Mr.", "그 화제는 내게 여러 생각을 불러일으켰고, 나는 잠자코 그것을 곱씹었다."], ["Wopsle, as the ill-requited uncle of the evening’s tragedy, fell to meditating aloud in his garden at Camberwell.", "워프슬 씨는, 그날 저녁 비극에서 보답받지 못한 숙부 역답게, 캠버웰의 제 정원에서 소리 내어 명상에 잠기기 시작했다."], ["Orlick, with his hands in his pockets, slouched heavily at my side.", "올릭은 두 손을 주머니에 찔러 넣은 채, 내 옆에서 무겁게 어슬렁거렸다."], ["It was very dark, very wet, very muddy, and so we splashed along.", "무척 어둡고, 무척 축축하고, 무척 질척거려서, 우리는 그렇게 첨벙첨벙 걸어갔다."], ["Now and then, the sound of the signal cannon broke upon us again, and again rolled sulkily along the course of the river.", "이따금 그 신호 대포 소리가 다시 우리에게 들이닥쳤고, 또다시 강줄기를 따라 시무룩하게 굴러갔다."], ["I kept myself to myself and my thoughts. Mr.", "나는 나 자신과 내 생각에만 잠겨 있었다."], ["Wopsle died amiably at Camberwell, and exceedingly game on Bosworth Field, and in the greatest agonies at Glastonbury.", "워프슬 씨는 캠버웰에서 온화하게 죽고, 보즈워스 들판에서 더없이 용맹하게 죽고, 글래스턴베리에서 극심한 고통 속에 죽었다."], ["Orlick sometimes growled, “Beat it out, beat it out,—Old Clem!", "올릭은 이따금 으르렁거렸다. \"두들겨 펴라, 두들겨 펴라 — 늙은 클렘!"], ["With a clink for the stout,—Old Clem!”", "튼튼한 놈한텐 한 잔 쨍 — 늙은 클렘!\""], ["I thought he had been drinking, but he was not drunk.", "나는 그가 술을 마신 줄 알았으나, 그는 취한 것은 아니었다."]], [["Thus, we came to the village.", "그렇게 우리는 마을에 이르렀다."], ["The way by which we approached it took us past the Three Jolly Bargemen, which we were surprised to find—it being eleven o’clock—in a state of commotion, with the door wide open, and unwonted lights that had been hastily caught up and put down scattered about. Mr.", "우리가 마을로 다가간 길은 세 졸리 바지선 주막을 지나게 되어 있었는데, 우리는 — 열한 시였는데도 — 그곳이 소동에 휩싸여, 문이 활짝 열려 있고, 황급히 집어 들었다 내려놓은 듯한 평소답지 않은 불빛들이 여기저기 흩어져 있는 것을 보고 놀랐다."], ["Wopsle dropped in to ask what was the matter (surmising that a convict had been taken), but came running out in a great hurry.", "워프슬 씨가 무슨 일인지 물어보려고 (탈옥수가 잡힌 거라고 짐작하며) 들어갔으나, 이내 다급히 뛰쳐나왔다."]], [["“There’s something wrong,”", "\"무슨 일이 났어.\""], ["said he, without stopping, “up at your place, Pip.", "그가 멈추지도 않고 말했다. \"자네 집에서, 핍."], ["Run all!”", "다들 뛰어!\""]], [["“What is it?”", "\"무슨 일인데요?\""], ["I asked, keeping up with him.", "내가 그에게 보조를 맞추며 물었다."], ["So did Orlick, at my side.", "올릭도 내 옆에서 그렇게 했다."]], [["“I can’t quite understand.", "\"나도 잘 모르겠어."], ["The house seems to have been violently entered when Joe Gargery was out.", "조 가저리가 나가 있는 동안 집에 누가 난폭하게 침입한 모양이야."], ["Supposed by convicts.", "탈옥수들 소행으로 짐작돼."], ["Somebody has been attacked and hurt.”", "누군가 습격당해서 다쳤다는군.\""]], [["We were running too fast to admit of more being said, and we made no stop until we got into our kitchen.", "우리는 너무 빨리 달리느라 더 말을 주고받을 틈도 없었고, 우리 집 부엌에 들어설 때까지 한 번도 멈추지 않았다."], ["It was full of people; the whole village was there, or in the yard; and there was a surgeon, and there was Joe, and there were a group of women, all on the floor in the midst of the kitchen.", "부엌은 사람들로 가득했다. 온 마을 사람이 거기에, 혹은 마당에 나와 있었다. 그리고 외과 의사가 있었고, 조가 있었고, 한 무리의 여인들이 있었으니, 모두가 부엌 한가운데 바닥에 모여 있었다."], ["The unemployed bystanders drew back when they saw me, and so I became aware of my sister,—lying without sense or movement on the bare boards where she had been knocked down by a tremendous blow on the back of the head, dealt by some unknown hand when her face was turned towards the fire,—destined never to be on the Rampage again, while she was the wife of Joe.", "일 없이 구경하던 사람들이 나를 보고는 물러섰고, 그리하여 나는 내 누나를 알아보게 되었다. 누나는 의식도 미동도 없이 맨바닥에 누워 있었으니, 얼굴을 불 쪽으로 돌리고 있을 때 어떤 알 수 없는 손이 뒤통수에 가한 무시무시한 일격에 쓰러뜨려진 것이었다. 그리고 조의 아내로 있는 한, 누나는 다시는 한바탕 날뛸 운명이 못 되었다."]], [["# Chapter XVI.", "# Chapter XVI."]], [["With my head full of George Barnwell, I was at first disposed to believe that I must have had some hand in the attack upon my sister, or at all events that as her near relation, popularly known to be under obligations to her, I was a more legitimate object of suspicion than any one else.", "조지 반웰* 생각으로 머릿속이 가득 찼던 나는, 처음에는 누나가 당한 그 습격에 나 역시 어떤 식으로든 손을 댔음에 틀림없다고, 적어도 누나의 가까운 친척이자 세상이 다 알다시피 그녀에게 신세를 진 처지인 만큼, 나야말로 다른 누구보다 더 정당한 혐의의 대상이라고 믿고 싶어졌다."], ["But when, in the clearer light of next morning, I began to reconsider the matter and to hear it discussed around me on all sides, I took another view of the case, which was more reasonable.", "그러나 이튿날 아침 한결 또렷한 빛 속에서 그 일을 다시 곰곰이 따져 보고 사방에서 오가는 논의를 듣기 시작하면서, 나는 좀 더 이치에 닿는 다른 견해를 갖게 되었다. * 조지 반웰: 조지 릴로의 희곡 「런던의 상인」 주인공으로, 숙부를 살해하고 처형되는 인물."]], [["Joe had been at the Three Jolly Bargemen, smoking his pipe, from a quarter after eight o’clock to a quarter before ten.", "조는 여덟 시 십오 분부터 열 시 십오 분 전까지 「세 명랑한 뱃사공」에서 담배를 피우고 있었다."], ["While he was there, my sister had been seen standing at the kitchen door, and had exchanged Good Night with a farm-labourer going home.", "그가 거기 있는 동안, 누나는 부엌문 앞에 서 있는 것이 목격되었고, 집으로 돌아가던 한 농장 일꾼과 안녕히 가시라는 인사를 주고받았다."], ["The man could not be more particular as to the time at which he saw her (he got into dense confusion when he tried to be), than that it must have been before nine.", "그 사내는 누나를 본 시각에 대해 아홉 시 이전이었음에 틀림없다는 것 이상으로 더 정확히 말하지 못했다(더 정확히 말하려고 애쓰자 그는 도리어 갈피를 못 잡고 헤맸다)."], ["When Joe went home at five minutes before ten, he found her struck down on the floor, and promptly called in assistance.", "조가 열 시 오 분 전에 집에 돌아왔을 때, 그는 누나가 바닥에 쓰러져 있는 것을 발견하고 곧바로 사람을 불러 도움을 청했다."], ["The fire had not then burnt unusually low, nor was the snuff of the candle very long; the candle, however, had been blown out.", "그때 불은 유난히 사위어 있지도 않았고, 촛불 심지도 그리 길지 않았다. 다만 촛불은 꺼져 있었다."]], [["Nothing had been taken away from any part of the house.", "집 안 어느 구석에서도 없어진 물건은 없었다."], ["Neither, beyond the blowing out of the candle,—which stood on a table between the door and my sister, and was behind her when she stood facing the fire and was struck,—was there any disarrangement of the kitchen, excepting such as she herself had made, in falling and bleeding.", "또한 촛불이 꺼진 것을 — 그것은 문과 누나 사이의 탁자 위에 놓여 있었으니, 그녀가 불을 바라보고 서서 가격당했을 때는 그녀의 등 뒤에 있었던 셈이다 — 제외하면, 부엌 안에 흐트러진 흔적이라곤, 그녀 자신이 쓰러지고 피를 흘리면서 만든 것 외에는 아무것도 없었다."], ["But, there was one remarkable piece of evidence on the spot.", "그러나 그 현장에는 주목할 만한 증거 한 가지가 있었다."], ["She had been struck with something blunt and heavy, on the head and spine; after the blows were dealt, something heavy had been thrown down at her with considerable violence, as she lay on her face.", "그녀는 무언가 무디고 무거운 것으로 머리와 등을 가격당했던 것이다. 그렇게 매를 맞은 뒤, 그녀가 엎드린 채 쓰러져 있을 때 무언가 무거운 것이 상당한 힘으로 그녀에게 내던져졌다."], ["And on the ground beside her, when Joe picked her up, was a convict’s leg-iron which had been filed asunder.", "그리고 조가 그녀를 일으켜 안았을 때 그녀 옆 바닥에는, 줄칼로 잘려 두 동강 난 죄수의 다리 쇠고랑이 있었다."]], [["Now, Joe, examining this iron with a smith’s eye, declared it to have been filed asunder some time ago.", "그런데 조는 대장장이의 눈으로 그 쇠고랑을 살펴보고는, 그것이 얼마 전에 줄칼로 잘린 것이라고 단언했다."], ["The hue and cry going off to the Hulks, and people coming thence to examine the iron, Joe’s opinion was corroborated.", "그 소식을 좇아 사람들이 감옥선으로 달려갔고, 거기서 사람들이 와서 그 쇠고랑을 살펴본 끝에 조의 견해는 확증되었다."], ["They did not undertake to say when it had left the prison-ships to which it undoubtedly had once belonged; but they claimed to know for certain that that particular manacle had not been worn by either of the two convicts who had escaped last night.", "그들은 그것이, 한때 분명히 속해 있었을 그 감옥선을 언제 떠났는지까지는 단정 짓지 못했으나, 그 특정한 족쇄가 어젯밤 탈주한 두 죄수 중 어느 쪽도 차고 있던 것이 아니라는 점만은 확실히 안다고 주장했다."], ["Further, one of those two was already retaken, and had not freed himself of his iron.", "게다가 그 두 사람 중 하나는 이미 다시 붙잡혔는데, 자기 쇠고랑을 벗어 버리지 못한 상태였다."]], [["Knowing what I knew, I set up an inference of my own here.", "내가 아는 바를 알고 있던 나는, 여기서 나 나름의 추론을 세웠다."], ["I believed the iron to be my convict’s iron,—the iron I had seen and heard him filing at, on the marshes,—but my mind did not accuse him of having put it to its latest use.", "나는 그 쇠고랑이 나의 죄수의 쇠고랑이라고 — 습지에서 그가 줄칼로 갈고 있던 것을 내가 보고 또 그 소리를 들었던 바로 그 쇠고랑이라고 — 믿었으나, 내 마음은 그가 그것을 그 마지막 용도로 썼다고 그를 탓하지는 않았다."], ["For I believed one of two other persons to have become possessed of it, and to have turned it to this cruel account.", "왜냐하면 나는 다른 두 사람 가운데 하나가 그것을 손에 넣어 이 잔인한 짓에 썼으리라고 믿었기 때문이다."], ["Either Orlick, or the strange man who had shown me the file.", "올릭이거나, 아니면 내게 줄칼을 보여 주었던 그 낯선 사내거나."]], [["Now, as to Orlick; he had gone to town exactly as he told us when we picked him up at the turnpike, he had been seen about town all the evening, he had been in divers companies in several public-houses, and he had come back with myself and Mr. Wopsle.", "자, 올릭으로 말하자면, 그는 우리가 통행세 징수소에서 그를 만났을 때 우리에게 말한 그대로 읍내에 갔었고, 저녁 내내 읍내 여기저기서 목격되었으며, 여러 선술집에서 갖가지 패거리와 어울렸고, 나와 워플 씨와 함께 돌아왔다."], ["There was nothing against him, save the quarrel; and my sister had quarrelled with him, and with everybody else about her, ten thousand times.", "그에게 불리한 것이라곤 그 말다툼 하나뿐이었는데, 누나는 그뿐 아니라 자기 주변의 모든 이와 수만 번도 더 다툰 사람이었다."], ["As to the strange man; if he had come back for his two bank-notes there could have been no dispute about them, because my sister was fully prepared to restore them.", "그 낯선 사내로 말하자면, 만약 그가 자기 지폐 두 장을 되찾으러 돌아온 것이라면 그것을 두고 다툴 일은 있을 수 없었다. 누나는 그것을 돌려줄 만반의 채비가 되어 있었으니까."], ["Besides, there had been no altercation; the assailant had come in so silently and suddenly, that she had been felled before she could look round.", "게다가 언쟁이라곤 없었다. 습격한 자는 너무도 소리 없이 갑작스레 들어와, 그녀가 미처 돌아볼 새도 없이 쓰러뜨려졌던 것이다."]], [["It was horrible to think that I had provided the weapon, however undesignedly, but I could hardly think otherwise.", "내가, 비록 전혀 의도치 않았다 해도, 그 흉기를 제공한 셈이라고 생각하니 끔찍했으나, 달리 생각하기란 좀처럼 어려웠다."], ["I suffered unspeakable trouble while I considered and reconsidered whether I should at last dissolve that spell of my childhood and tell Joe all the story.", "마침내 내 어린 시절의 그 주술을 풀고 조에게 모든 이야기를 털어놓아야 할 것인가를 거듭 곱씹는 동안, 나는 이루 말할 수 없는 고통에 시달렸다."], ["For months afterwards, I every day settled the question finally in the negative, and reopened and reargued it next morning.", "그 후로 몇 달 동안, 나는 날마다 그 문제를 끝내 안 된다는 쪽으로 결론지었다가, 이튿날 아침이면 다시 그 문제를 꺼내 새로이 따지곤 했다."], ["The contention came, after all, to this;—the secret was such an old one now, had so grown into me and become a part of myself, that I could not tear it away.", "그 다툼은 결국 이러한 데로 귀착되었다. 그 비밀은 이제 너무도 오래된 것이어서, 내 안에 깊이 자라나 나 자신의 일부가 되어 버린 까닭에, 나로서는 그것을 뜯어낼 수가 없었다."], ["In addition to the dread that, having led up to so much mischief, it would be now more likely than ever to alienate Joe from me if he believed it, I had a further restraining dread that he would not believe it, but would assort it with the fabulous dogs and veal-cutlets as a monstrous invention.", "이미 그토록 많은 화를 불러일으킨 마당에, 만약 조가 그 이야기를 믿는다면 이제 그 어느 때보다도 조를 나에게서 멀어지게 할 가능성이 크다는 두려움에 더하여, 나에게는 또 다른 억제하는 두려움이 있었으니, 그가 그것을 믿지 않고 도리어 그 허무맹랑한 개들이며 송아지 고기 토막과 한데 묶어 터무니없는 지어낸 이야기쯤으로 치부해 버리리라는 두려움이었다."], ["However, I temporized with myself, of course—for, was I not wavering between right and wrong, when the thing is always done?—and resolved to make a full disclosure if I should see any such new occasion as a new chance of helping in the discovery of the assailant.", "그러나 나는 물론 나 자신과 타협하고 말았는데 — 일이 늘 그렇게 되고 마는 마당에, 나는 옳고 그름 사이에서 흔들리고 있던 게 아니었던가? — 그리하여, 습격한 자를 찾아내는 데 도움이 될 새로운 기회와도 같은 그러한 새로운 계기를 보게 된다면 모든 것을 낱낱이 털어놓으리라 마음먹었다."]], [["The Constables and the Bow Street men from London—for, this happened in the days of the extinct red-waistcoated police—were about the house for a week or two, and did pretty much what I have heard and read of like authorities doing in other such cases.", "그 순경들과 런던에서 온 보 스트리트 경관들은 — 이 일은 빨간 조끼를 입은 경찰이 사라지기 전 시절에 일어난 것이었으니까 — 한두 주일 동안 집 주위를 맴돌며, 다른 그런 사건들에서 이런 부류의 권력자들이 했다고 내가 듣고 읽은 것과 거의 비슷한 일들을 했다."], ["They took up several obviously wrong people, and they ran their heads very hard against wrong ideas, and persisted in trying to fit the circumstances to the ideas, instead of trying to extract ideas from the circumstances.", "그들은 누가 봐도 엉뚱한 사람을 여럿 잡아들였고, 엉뚱한 생각에 머리를 호되게 들이받았으며, 정황에서 생각을 끌어내려 하기보다 정황을 그 생각에 끼워 맞추려고 끈질기게 애썼다. 또한 그들은 「명랑한 뱃사공」"], ["Also, they stood about the door of the Jolly Bargemen, with knowing and reserved looks that filled the whole neighbourhood with admiration; and they had a mysterious manner of taking their drink, that was almost as good as taking the culprit.", "문 앞에 서서, 동네 전체를 감탄으로 가득 채울 만큼 무언가 다 안다는 듯하고 점잔 빼는 표정을 지었다. 그리고 술을 마시는 데도 어딘가 알 수 없는 비밀스러운 태도가 있어, 그것은 거의 범인을 잡는 것이나 다름없어 보였다. 다만 완전히 그렇지는 못했으니, 그들은 끝내 범인을 잡지 못했기 때문이다."], ["But not quite, for they never did it.", "* 보 스트리트 경관: 런던 보 스트리트에 본부를 둔 초기 형사대원."]], [["Long after these constitutional powers had dispersed, my sister lay very ill in bed.", "이 헌법상의 권력자들이 흩어진 뒤로도 한참 동안, 누나는 자리에 누운 채 몹시 앓았다."], ["Her sight was disturbed, so that she saw objects multiplied, and grasped at visionary teacups and wineglasses instead of the realities; her hearing was greatly impaired; her memory also; and her speech was unintelligible.", "시력에 장애가 생겨 사물이 여러 개로 겹쳐 보였고, 실제 물건 대신 허깨비 같은 찻잔이며 포도주잔을 더듬어 잡으려 했다. 청력도 크게 손상되었고, 기억력도 마찬가지였으며, 말도 알아들을 수 없었다."], ["When, at last, she came round so far as to be helped downstairs, it was still necessary to keep my slate always by her, that she might indicate in writing what she could not indicate in speech.", "마침내 부축을 받아 아래층으로 내려올 만큼 회복되었을 때에도, 여전히 내 석판을 늘 그녀 곁에 두어, 말로 가리키지 못하는 것을 글로 가리킬 수 있게 해야 했다."], ["As she was (very bad handwriting apart) a more than indifferent speller, and as Joe was a more than indifferent reader, extraordinary complications arose between them which I was always called in to solve.", "누나는 (지독히 서투른 글씨체는 차치하고라도) 철자가 형편없는 사람이었고, 조 또한 읽는 데는 영 시원찮은 사람이었던지라, 두 사람 사이에 별난 혼선이 빚어지곤 했고, 나는 늘 그것을 풀라고 불려 나갔다."], ["The administration of mutton instead of medicine, the substitution of Tea for Joe, and the baker for bacon, were among the mildest of my own mistakes.", "약 대신 양고기를 가져다 바치고, 조(Joe) 대신 차(Tea)를, 베이컨(bacon) 대신 빵집 주인(baker)을 갖다 댄 것 따위는 내 실수 가운데서도 가장 가벼운 축에 들었다."]], [["However, her temper was greatly improved, and she was patient.", "그러나 누나의 성미는 크게 나아졌고, 그녀는 참을성이 있었다."], ["A tremulous uncertainty of the action of all her limbs soon became a part of her regular state, and afterwards, at intervals of two or three months, she would often put her hands to her head, and would then remain for about a week at a time in some gloomy aberration of mind.", "온몸의 동작이 떨리며 불안정한 상태가 이내 그녀의 평소 모습의 일부가 되었고, 그 후로 두세 달 간격으로 그녀는 곧잘 두 손을 머리에 갖다 대고는, 한 번에 일주일쯤 어떤 음울한 정신착란에 빠져 있곤 했다."], ["We were at a loss to find a suitable attendant for her, until a circumstance happened conveniently to relieve us. Mr.", "우리는 그녀를 돌볼 마땅한 사람을 찾지 못해 난감해하던 차에, 마침맞게 한 가지 사정이 생겨 우리의 짐을 덜어 주었다."], ["Wopsle’s great-aunt conquered a confirmed habit of living into which she had fallen, and Biddy became a part of our establishment.", "워플 씨의 종조모가, 빠져 있던 그 끈질긴 살아 있는 버릇을 끝내 이겨 냈고, 비디가 우리 살림의 일원이 된 것이다."]], [["It may have been about a month after my sister’s reappearance in the kitchen, when Biddy came to us with a small speckled box containing the whole of her worldly effects, and became a blessing to the household.", "누나가 부엌에 다시 모습을 드러낸 지 한 달쯤 지났을 무렵, 비디는 자기의 온 세간을 죄다 담은 작은 얼룩무늬 상자 하나를 들고 우리에게 와서, 이 집안의 축복이 되었다."], ["Above all, she was a blessing to Joe, for the dear old fellow was sadly cut up by the constant contemplation of the wreck of his wife, and had been accustomed, while attending on her of an evening, to turn to me every now and then and say, with his blue eyes moistened, “Such a fine figure of a woman as she once were, Pip!”", "무엇보다도 그녀는 조에게 축복이었다. 그 정다운 늙은이는 망가진 아내의 모습을 끊임없이 바라보느라 마음이 몹시 무너져 있었고, 저녁이면 그녀를 보살피다 말고 이따금 나를 돌아보며, 푸른 두 눈을 적신 채 이렇게 말하곤 했던 것이다. \"한때는 참으로 훤칠한 여인이었는데 말이다, 핍!\""], ["Biddy instantly taking the cleverest charge of her as though she had studied her from infancy; Joe became able in some sort to appreciate the greater quiet of his life, and to get down to the Jolly Bargemen now and then for a change that did him good.", "비디가 마치 갓난아기 적부터 그녀를 익혀 온 사람처럼 당장에 더없이 영리하게 그녀를 떠맡자, 조는 어느 정도 한층 조용해진 자기 삶을 누릴 줄 알게 되었고, 이따금 「명랑한 뱃사공」에 내려가 기분을 푸는 변화도 가질 수 있게 되었다."], ["It was characteristic of the police people that they had all more or less suspected poor Joe (though he never knew it), and that they had to a man concurred in regarding him as one of the deepest spirits they had ever encountered.", "경찰들이 너 나 할 것 없이 가엾은 조를 어느 정도 의심했다는 것(정작 그는 결코 알지 못했지만), 그리고 그들이 하나같이 그를 일찍이 마주친 가장 음흉한 인물 가운데 하나로 여기는 데 의견을 같이했다는 것은, 그 경찰들다운 노릇이었다."]], [["Biddy’s first triumph in her new office, was to solve a difficulty that had completely vanquished me.", "비디가 새 소임에서 거둔 첫 개가는, 나를 완전히 두 손 들게 했던 한 가지 난제를 풀어낸 것이었다."], ["I had tried hard at it, but had made nothing of it.", "나는 그것을 두고 무던히도 애썼으나 도무지 갈피를 잡지 못했다."], ["Thus it was:—", "일은 이러했다."]], [["Again and again and again, my sister had traced upon the slate, a character that looked like a curious T, and then with the utmost eagerness had called our attention to it as something she particularly wanted. I had in vain tried everything producible that began with a T, from tar to toast and tub.", "누나는 거듭거듭 석판 위에, 묘하게 생긴 T 자처럼 보이는 글자 하나를 그려 놓고는, 그것이 자기가 특별히 원하는 무엇인 양 더없이 간절하게 우리의 주의를 그리로 끌었다. 나는 타르(tar)에서 토스트(toast), 통(tub)에 이르기까지 T로 시작하는 것이라면 손에 잡히는 대로 다 가져다 대 보았으나 헛수고였다."], ["At length it had come into my head that the sign looked like a hammer, and on my lustily calling that word in my sister’s ear, she had begun to hammer on the table and had expressed a qualified assent.", "마침내 그 표시가 망치처럼 보인다는 데 생각이 미쳐, 내가 그 단어를 누나의 귀에 대고 우렁차게 외치자, 누나는 탁자를 두드리기 시작하면서 어느 정도 동의하는 기색을 보였다."], ["Thereupon, I had brought in all our hammers, one after another, but without avail.", "그래서 나는 우리 집에 있는 망치란 망치는 하나씩 차례로 다 들여왔으나, 소용이 없었다."], ["Then I bethought me of a crutch, the shape being much the same, and I borrowed one in the village, and displayed it to my sister with considerable confidence.", "그다음에 나는 목발을 떠올렸는데, 모양이 꽤 비슷했던 것이다. 그래서 마을에서 하나를 빌려 와 상당한 자신감을 가지고 누나에게 내보였다."], ["But she shook her head to that extent when she was shown it, that we were terrified lest in her weak and shattered state she should dislocate her neck.", "그러나 그것을 보여 주자 누나가 어찌나 세차게 고개를 흔들던지, 우리는 그 쇠약하고 망가진 상태에서 그녀가 목을 삐지나 않을까 겁이 났다."]], [["When my sister found that Biddy was very quick to understand her, this mysterious sign reappeared on the slate.", "누나는 비디가 자기 뜻을 아주 빨리 알아챈다는 것을 알게 되자, 이 알 수 없는 표시를 다시 석판에 그려 놓았다."], ["Biddy looked thoughtfully at it, heard my explanation, looked thoughtfully at my sister, looked thoughtfully at Joe (who was always represented on the slate by his initial letter), and ran into the forge, followed by Joe and me.", "비디는 그것을 골똘히 들여다보고, 내 설명을 듣고, 누나를 골똘히 바라보고, 조를(석판 위에서 그는 늘 이름의 첫 글자로 나타내어졌다) 골똘히 바라보더니, 대장간으로 달려갔고 조와 나도 그 뒤를 따랐다."]], [["“Why, of course!”", "\"아니, 당연하잖아요!\""], ["cried Biddy, with an exultant face.", "비디가 의기양양한 얼굴로 외쳤다."], ["“Don’t you see? It’s him!”", "\"모르시겠어요? 바로 그 사람이라고요!\""]], [["Orlick, without a doubt!", "틀림없이 올릭이었다!"], ["She had lost his name, and could only signify him by his hammer.", "누나는 그의 이름을 잊어버려, 그의 망치로밖에 그를 나타낼 수 없었던 것이다."], ["We told him why we wanted him to come into the kitchen, and he slowly laid down his hammer, wiped his brow with his arm, took another wipe at it with his apron, and came slouching out, with a curious loose vagabond bend in the knees that strongly distinguished him.", "우리는 그에게 어째서 부엌으로 들어와 주었으면 하는지를 말했고, 그는 천천히 망치를 내려놓고, 팔로 이마를 훔치고, 다시 앞치마로 한 번 더 닦더니, 무릎을 헐겁게 구부정하니 굽힌, 그를 유난히 두드러지게 하던 그 묘한 부랑자 같은 걸음새로 어슬렁어슬렁 나왔다."]], [["I confess that I expected to see my sister denounce him, and that I was disappointed by the different result.", "고백하건대, 나는 누나가 그를 고발하리라 예상했고, 그와는 다른 결과에 실망했다."], ["She manifested the greatest anxiety to be on good terms with him, was evidently much pleased by his being at length produced, and motioned that she would have him given something to drink.", "누나는 그와 좋은 사이로 지내고 싶다는 더없는 열망을 드러냈고, 마침내 그가 데려와진 것을 보고 분명 무척 기뻐했으며, 그에게 마실 것을 좀 주었으면 하는 시늉을 했다."], ["She watched his countenance as if she were particularly wishful to be assured that he took kindly to his reception, she showed every possible desire to conciliate him, and there was an air of humble propitiation in all she did, such as I have seen pervade the bearing of a child towards a hard master.", "그녀는 그가 자신의 환대를 흔쾌히 받아들이는지 특별히 확인하고 싶어 하는 듯 그의 얼굴빛을 살폈고, 그의 마음을 누그러뜨리려는 온갖 바람을 다 내보였으며, 그 모든 행동에는 비굴하게 비위를 맞추려는 기색이 어려 있었으니, 그것은 내가 모진 주인을 대하는 아이의 태도에서 본 적이 있는 그런 기색이었다."], ["After that day, a day rarely passed without her drawing the hammer on her slate, and without Orlick’s slouching in and standing doggedly before her, as if he knew no more than I did what to make of it.", "그날 이후로, 누나가 석판에 망치를 그리지 않는 날이 드물었고, 올릭이 어슬렁어슬렁 들어와, 나와 다름없이 그것을 어찌 받아들여야 할지 모르겠다는 듯, 그녀 앞에 무뚝뚝하게 버티고 서지 않는 날 또한 드물었다."]], [["# Chapter XVII.", "# Chapter XVII."]], [["I now fell into a regular routine of apprenticeship life, which was varied beyond the limits of the village and the marshes, by no more remarkable circumstance than the arrival of my birthday and my paying another visit to Miss Havisham.", "이제 나는 도제 생활의 규칙적인 일상에 빠져들었는데, 그 일상이 마을과 습지의 경계를 넘어 달라지는 일이라곤, 내 생일이 돌아오고 내가 미스 해비셤을 다시 한번 찾아가는 것보다 더 특기할 만한 사건은 없었다."], ["I found Miss Sarah Pocket still on duty at the gate; I found Miss Havisham just as I had left her, and she spoke of Estella in the very same way, if not in the very same words.", "미스 새러 포켓은 여전히 문간에서 임무를 수행하고 있었고, 미스 해비셤은 내가 떠날 때 그대로의 모습이었으며, 똑같은 말은 아닐지라도 똑같은 방식으로 에스텔라에 대해 이야기했다."], ["The interview lasted but a few minutes, and she gave me a guinea when I was going, and told me to come again on my next birthday.", "면담은 몇 분밖에 이어지지 않았고, 내가 돌아갈 때 그녀는 내게 일 기니를 주며 다음 생일에 또 오라고 일렀다."], ["I may mention at once that this became an annual custom.", "곧바로 말해 두자면, 이것은 해마다 거르지 않는 관례가 되었다."], ["I tried to decline taking the guinea on the first occasion, but with no better effect than causing her to ask me very angrily, if I expected more? Then, and after that, I took it.", "첫 번에는 그 기니 받기를 사양하려 했으나, 더 바라는 게 있느냐고 그녀가 몹시 성을 내며 묻게 만드는 것 외에는 별 소득이 없었다. 그래서 그때도, 그 이후로도, 나는 그것을 받았다."]], [["So unchanging was the dull old house, the yellow light in the darkened room, the faded spectre in the chair by the dressing-table glass, that I felt as if the stopping of the clocks had stopped Time in that mysterious place, and, while I and everything else outside it grew older, it stood still.", "그 음침한 옛집도, 어두운 방 안의 누런 불빛도, 화장대 거울 옆 의자에 앉은 빛바랜 유령도 어찌나 변함이 없던지, 나는 마치 멈춰 선 시계들이 그 신비로운 곳의 시간을 멈춰 세운 것만 같았고, 나와 그 바깥의 다른 모든 것이 나이 들어 가는 동안에도 그곳만은 가만히 정지해 있는 듯한 느낌을 받았다."], ["Daylight never entered the house as to my thoughts and remembrances of it, any more than as to the actual fact.", "실제 사실에 있어서나, 그 집에 대한 내 생각과 기억에 있어서나, 그 집에는 햇빛이 결코 들지 않았다."], ["It bewildered me, and under its influence I continued at heart to hate my trade and to be ashamed of home.", "그것이 나를 혼란스럽게 했고, 그 영향 아래 나는 마음속으로 여전히 내 직업을 미워하고 집을 부끄러워했다."]], [["Imperceptibly I became conscious of a change in Biddy, however.", "그러나 어느새 나는 비디에게 일어난 변화를 의식하게 되었다."], ["Her shoes came up at the heel, her hair grew bright and neat, her hands were always clean.", "그녀의 신발은 뒤축이 닳지 않게 잘 갖추어졌고, 머리칼은 윤기 있고 단정해졌으며, 손은 늘 깨끗했다."], ["She was not beautiful,—she was common, and could not be like Estella,—but she was pleasant and wholesome and sweet-tempered.", "그녀는 아름답지 않았다. 평범했고, 에스텔라처럼 될 수는 없었다. 그러나 그녀는 상냥하고 건강하고 마음씨가 고왔다."], ["She had not been with us more than a year (I remember her being newly out of mourning at the time it struck me), when I observed to myself one evening that she had curiously thoughtful and attentive eyes; eyes that were very pretty and very good.", "그녀가 우리와 함께 지낸 지 일 년이 채 안 되었을 무렵(그 생각이 문득 들었을 때 그녀가 상복을 막 벗은 참이었던 것을 기억한다), 나는 어느 날 저녁 그녀에게 묘하게 사려 깊고 주의 깊은 눈, 무척 예쁘고 무척 선한 눈이 있다는 것을 스스로 깨달았다."]], [["It came of my lifting up my own eyes from a task I was poring at—writing some passages from a book, to improve myself in two ways at once by a sort of stratagem—and seeing Biddy observant of what I was about.", "그것은 내가 골몰하던 일에서 — 일종의 책략으로 한꺼번에 두 가지 방향으로 나를 향상시키려고 어느 책의 몇 구절을 베껴 쓰던 일에서 — 고개를 들었다가, 비디가 내 하는 일을 지켜보고 있는 것을 보았기 때문이었다."], ["I laid down my pen, and Biddy stopped in her needlework without laying it down.", "나는 펜을 내려놓았고, 비디는 바느질감을 내려놓지는 않은 채 손을 멈추었다."]], [["“Biddy,”", "\"비디,\""], ["said I, “how do you manage it?", "내가 말했다. \"넌 어떻게 그러는 거야?"], ["Either I am very stupid, or you are very clever.”", "내가 아주 멍청한 거든지, 아니면 네가 아주 영리한 거든지 둘 중 하나야.\""]], [["“What is it that I manage?", "\"내가 뭘 어떻게 한다는 거지?"], ["I don’t know,”", "나는 모르겠는걸.\""], ["returned Biddy, smiling.", "비디가 미소 지으며 대꾸했다."]], [["She managed our whole domestic life, and wonderfully too; but I did not mean that, though that made what I did mean more surprising.", "그녀는 우리 집안 살림 전체를 꾸려 나갔고, 그것도 놀라우리만치 잘 해냈다. 그러나 내가 말한 것은 그게 아니었다. 다만 그 살림 솜씨가 내가 정작 말하려던 바를 한층 더 놀랍게 만들어 줄 따름이었다."]], [["“How do you manage, Biddy,”", "\"비디, 넌 어떻게 그래.\""], ["said I, “to learn everything that I learn, and always to keep up with me?”", "내가 말했다. \"내가 배우는 걸 다 배우고, 늘 나를 따라잡는 거 말이야.\""], ["I was beginning to be rather vain of my knowledge, for I spent my birthday guineas on it, and set aside the greater part of my pocket-money for similar investment; though I have no doubt, now, that the little I knew was extremely dear at the price.", "나는 내 지식에 다소 우쭐대기 시작하던 참이었다. 생일에 받은 기니를 거기 쓰고, 용돈의 대부분을 같은 투자에 떼어 두었기 때문이다. 다만 그 보잘것없는 지식이 그 값에 비하면 터무니없이 비싼 것이었음은, 지금에 와서는 의심할 여지가 없다."]], [["“I might as well ask you,”", "\"나야말로 너한테 묻고 싶은걸.\""], ["said Biddy, “how you manage?”", "비디가 말했다. \"넌 어떻게 그러느냐고.\""]], [["“No; because when I come in from the forge of a night, any one can see me turning to at it.", "\"아니지. 나는 밤에 대장간에서 들어오면, 누구나 내가 거기 매달리는 걸 볼 수 있으니까."], ["But you never turn to at it, Biddy.”", "그런데 너는 도무지 거기 매달리는 법이 없잖아, 비디.\""]], [["“I suppose I must catch it like a cough,”", "\"감기처럼 옮은 모양이지, 뭐.\""], ["said Biddy, quietly; and went on with her sewing.", "비디가 조용히 말하고는 다시 바느질을 계속했다."]], [["Pursuing my idea as I leaned back in my wooden chair, and looked at Biddy sewing away with her head on one side, I began to think her rather an extraordinary girl.", "나무 의자에 등을 기대고 비디가 고개를 한쪽으로 갸웃한 채 바느질하는 모습을 바라보며 내 생각을 좇다 보니, 나는 그녀가 어지간히 비범한 처녀라는 생각이 들기 시작했다."], ["For I called to mind now, that she was equally accomplished in the terms of our trade, and the names of our different sorts of work, and our various tools.", "이제야 떠올랐기 때문이다. 그녀가 우리 일의 용어며, 우리가 하는 갖가지 작업의 이름이며, 우리가 쓰는 여러 연장에 대해서도 똑같이 통달해 있다는 것이."], ["In short, whatever I knew, Biddy knew.", "요컨대, 내가 아는 것은 무엇이든 비디도 알았다."], ["Theoretically, she was already as good a blacksmith as I, or better.", "이론상으로는 그녀는 이미 나만큼, 아니 나보다 더 훌륭한 대장장이였다."]], [["“You are one of those, Biddy,”", "\"비디, 넌 그런 부류야.\" 내가 말했다."], ["said I, “who make the most of every chance.", "\"기회란 기회는 다 최대한 살리는 부류 말이야."], ["You never had a chance before you came here, and see how improved you are!”", "넌 여기 오기 전엔 기회라곤 없었는데, 이렇게 발전한 걸 보라고!\""]], [["Biddy looked at me for an instant, and went on with her sewing.", "비디는 잠깐 나를 바라보고는 다시 바느질을 계속했다."], ["“I was your first teacher though; wasn’t I?”", "\"그래도 네 첫 선생은 나였잖아. 안 그래?\""], ["said she, as she sewed.", "그녀가 바느질하며 말했다."]], [["“Biddy!”", "\"비디!\""], ["I exclaimed, in amazement.", "나는 놀라서 소리쳤다."], ["“Why, you are crying!”", "\"아니, 너 울고 있잖아!\""]], [["“No I am not,” said Biddy, looking up and laughing.", "\"아니, 안 울어.\" 비디가 고개를 들고 웃으며 말했다."], ["“What put that in your head?”", "\"그런 생각은 어디서 났대?\""]], [["What could have put it in my head but the glistening of a tear as it dropped on her work?", "그녀의 바느질감 위로 눈물 한 방울이 반짝이며 떨어지는 것 말고, 그런 생각이 어디서 났겠는가?"], ["I sat silent, recalling what a drudge she had been until Mr. Wopsle’s great-aunt successfully overcame that bad habit of living, so highly desirable to be got rid of by some people.", "나는 잠자코 앉아, 우프슬 씨의 왕고모할머니가 그토록 살아 있는 나쁜 버릇을 — 어떤 사람들에게는 떨쳐 버리는 것이 그토록 바람직한 버릇을 — 마침내 이겨 내고 세상을 뜨기 전까지, 비디가 얼마나 고된 종살이를 했던가를 되새겼다."], ["I recalled the hopeless circumstances by which she had been surrounded in the miserable little shop and the miserable little noisy evening school, with that miserable old bundle of incompetence always to be dragged and shouldered.", "나는 그 초라한 작은 가게와 그 초라하고 시끄러운 작은 야학에서, 늘 끌고 떠메야 했던 그 초라한 무능의 늙은 짐덩어리와 함께, 그녀를 에워쌌던 그 가망 없는 처지를 떠올렸다. 나는 생각했다."], ["I reflected that even in those untoward times there must have been latent in Biddy what was now developing, for, in my first uneasiness and discontent I had turned to her for help, as a matter of course.", "그토록 불우한 시절에도, 지금 비디 안에서 피어나고 있는 그 무언가가 그녀 안에 잠재해 있었음이 틀림없다고. 왜냐하면 처음 마음이 불안하고 불만스러워졌을 때, 나는 당연한 일처럼 그녀에게 도움을 구하러 갔으니까."], ["Biddy sat quietly sewing, shedding no more tears, and while I looked at her and thought about it all, it occurred to me that perhaps I had not been sufficiently grateful to Biddy.", "비디는 조용히 앉아 바느질하며 더는 눈물을 흘리지 않았고, 내가 그녀를 바라보며 그 모든 것을 곰곰이 생각하는 동안, 어쩌면 내가 비디에게 충분히 고마워하지 않았던 게 아닐까 하는 생각이 들었다."], ["I might have been too reserved, and should have patronised her more (though I did not use that precise word in my meditations) with my confidence.", "나는 어쩌면 너무 데면데면했던 것일지도 모르고, 내 속내를 털어놓아 그녀를 좀 더 보살펴 주었어야 했다(비록 내 사색 속에서 그 정확한 표현을 쓴 것은 아니지만)."]], [["“Yes, Biddy,”", "\"그래, 비디.\""], ["I observed, when I had done turning it over, “you were my first teacher, and that at a time when we little thought of ever being together like this, in this kitchen.”", "그것을 다 곱씹고 나서 내가 말했다. \"넌 내 첫 선생이었어. 그것도 우리가 언젠가 이렇게, 이 부엌에서 함께 있게 되리라곤 거의 생각조차 못 하던 시절에 말이야.\""]], [["“Ah, poor thing!”", "\"아, 가엾어라!\""], ["replied Biddy.", "비디가 대답했다."], ["It was like her self-forgetfulness to transfer the remark to my sister, and to get up and be busy about her, making her more comfortable; “that’s sadly true!”", "그 말을 누나에게로 돌리고 자리에서 일어나 누나를 분주히 보살피며 더 편안하게 해 주는 것이, 꼭 자기를 잊고 사는 그녀다웠다. \"그건 참 가슴 아프게도 사실이야!\""]], [["“Well!”", "\"자!\""], ["said I, “we must talk together a little more, as we used to do.", "내가 말했다. \"우리, 예전에 그랬던 것처럼 좀 더 자주 이야기를 나눠야겠어."], ["And I must consult you a little more, as I used to do.", "그리고 예전처럼 너한테 좀 더 자주 상의도 해야겠고."], ["Let us have a quiet walk on the marshes next Sunday, Biddy, and a long chat.”", "비디, 다음 일요일에 습지로 조용히 산책을 가서, 오래도록 이야기를 나누자.\""]], [["My sister was never left alone now; but Joe more than readily undertook the care of her on that Sunday afternoon, and Biddy and I went out together. It was summer-time, and lovely weather.", "이제 누나는 결코 혼자 남겨지는 법이 없었다. 그러나 그 일요일 오후 조는 기꺼이, 그것도 기꺼운 마음 이상으로 누나를 돌보는 일을 떠맡았고, 비디와 나는 함께 밖으로 나갔다."], ["When we had passed the village and the church and the churchyard, and were out on the marshes and began to see the sails of the ships as they sailed on, I began to combine Miss Havisham and Estella with the prospect, in my usual way.", "여름철이었고, 날씨가 아름다웠다. 마을과 교회와 교회 묘지를 지나 습지로 나서서 배들이 미끄러지듯 지나가는 돛을 보기 시작하자, 나는 늘 하던 식으로 미스 해비셤과 에스텔라를 그 풍경과 한데 엮기 시작했다."], ["When we came to the river-side and sat down on the bank, with the water rippling at our feet, making it all more quiet than it would have been without that sound, I resolved that it was a good time and place for the admission of Biddy into my inner confidence.", "강가에 이르러 둑에 앉으니, 발치에서 물이 찰랑거리며 그 소리가 없을 때보다 모든 것을 한결 고요하게 만들어 주었고, 나는 비디를 내 깊은 속마음 안으로 들이기에 좋은 때와 장소라는 결심을 굳혔다."]], [["“Biddy,”", "\"비디,\""], ["said I, after binding her to secrecy, “I want to be a gentleman.”", "그녀에게 비밀을 지키겠다는 다짐을 받고서 내가 말했다. \"나는 신사가 되고 싶어.\""]], [["“O, I wouldn’t, if I was you!”", "\"어머, 나라면 안 그러겠는데!\""], ["she returned.", "그녀가 대꾸했다."], ["“I don’t think it would answer.”", "\"그게 잘될 것 같지 않거든.\""]], [["“Biddy,”", "\"비디,\""], ["said I, with some severity, “I have particular reasons for wanting to be a gentleman.”", "내가 다소 매섭게 말했다. \"나한텐 신사가 되고 싶은 특별한 이유가 있어.\""]], [["“You know best, Pip; but don’t you think you are happier as you are?”", "\"네가 제일 잘 알겠지, 핍. 하지만 넌 지금 이대로가 더 행복하다고 생각하지 않아?\""]], [["“Biddy,”", "\"비디,\""], ["I exclaimed, impatiently, “I am not at all happy as I am.", "나는 안달이 나서 소리쳤다. \"나는 지금 이대로는 조금도 행복하지 않아."], ["I am disgusted with my calling and with my life. I have never taken to either, since I was bound.", "나는 내 일도 내 인생도 진저리가 나. 도제로 묶인 뒤로 단 한 번도 둘 중 어느 쪽에도 정이 든 적이 없다고."], ["Don’t be absurd.”", "바보 같은 소리 하지 마.\""]], [["“Was I absurd?”", "\"내가 바보 같았어?\""], ["said Biddy, quietly raising her eyebrows; “I am sorry for that; I didn’t mean to be.", "비디가 조용히 눈썹을 치켜올리며 말했다. \"그렇다면 미안해. 그럴 생각은 아니었어."], ["I only want you to do well, and to be comfortable.”", "나는 그저 네가 잘되고, 네가 편안하기를 바랄 뿐이야.\""]], [["“Well, then, understand once for all that I never shall or can be comfortable—or anything but miserable—there, Biddy!—unless I can lead a very different sort of life from the life I lead now.”", "\"그래, 그렇다면 단번에 알아 둬. 지금 사는 삶과 아주 다른 종류의 삶을 살 수 없는 한, 나는 결코 편안할 수도, 편안해질 수도 없어. 비참한 것 말고는 아무것도 될 수 없다고. 자, 비디!\""]], [["“That’s a pity!” said Biddy, shaking her head with a sorrowful air.", "\"그것 참 안됐구나!\" 비디가 슬픈 기색으로 고개를 저으며 말했다."]], [["Now, I too had so often thought it a pity, that, in the singular kind of quarrel with myself which I was always carrying on, I was half inclined to shed tears of vexation and distress when Biddy gave utterance to her sentiment and my own.", "그런데 나 역시 그것을 너무도 자주 안된 일이라 여겨 온 터라, 늘 마음속에서 벌이던 그 묘한 자기와의 다툼 속에서, 비디가 그녀의 심정이자 곧 나 자신의 심정을 입 밖에 내자, 나는 분하고 괴로운 눈물을 쏟을 뻔했다."], ["I told her she was right, and I knew it was much to be regretted, but still it was not to be helped.", "나는 그녀의 말이 옳다고, 그것이 무척이나 유감스러운 일임을 안다고, 그러나 그래도 어쩔 도리가 없다고 그녀에게 말했다."]], [["“If I could have settled down,”", "\"내가 정착할 수만 있었다면.\""], ["I said to Biddy, plucking up the short grass within reach, much as I had once upon a time pulled my feelings out of my hair and kicked them into the brewery wall,—“if I could have settled down and been but half as fond of the forge as I was when I was little, I know it would have been much better for me.", "내가 손 닿는 데 있는 짧은 풀을 쥐어뜯으며 비디에게 말했다. 한때 머리카락에서 내 감정을 쥐어뜯어 양조장 담벼락에 차 던지던 그때와 흡사하게. \"내가 정착할 수만 있었다면, 그래서 어릴 적에 그랬던 절반만큼이라도 대장간을 좋아할 수만 있었다면, 그게 나한테 훨씬 나았으리라는 걸 알아."], ["You and I and Joe would have wanted nothing then, and Joe and I would perhaps have gone partners when I was out of my time, and I might even have grown up to keep company with you, and we might have sat on this very bank on a fine Sunday, quite different people.", "그랬다면 너와 나와 조는 아쉬울 게 없었을 거고, 내가 도제살이를 마쳤을 때 조와 나는 어쩌면 동업자가 되었을지도 모르고, 나는 어쩌면 자라서 너와 사귀게 되었을지도 모르고, 우리는 어느 화창한 일요일에 바로 이 둑에 앉아 있었을지도 모르지. 지금과는 사뭇 다른 사람들이 되어서 말이야."], ["I should have been good enough for you; shouldn’t I, Biddy?”", "그랬다면 나는 너한테 충분히 괜찮은 사람이었겠지. 안 그래, 비디?\""]], [["Biddy sighed as she looked at the ships sailing on, and returned for answer, “Yes; I am not over-particular.”", "비디는 미끄러져 가는 배들을 바라보며 한숨을 쉬고는 대답으로 말했다. \"응. 나는 그렇게 까다롭지 않으니까.\""], ["It scarcely sounded flattering, but I knew she meant well.", "듣기 좋은 말로는 도무지 들리지 않았으나, 나는 그녀의 뜻이 선한 것임을 알았다."]], [["“Instead of that,”", "\"그런데 그러기는커녕.\""], ["said I, plucking up more grass and chewing a blade or two, “see how I am going on.", "풀을 더 쥐어뜯어 한두 잎 씹으며 내가 말했다. \"내가 어떻게 살고 있나 봐."], ["Dissatisfied, and uncomfortable, and—what would it signify to me, being coarse and common, if nobody had told me so!”", "불만스럽고, 불편하고 — 그리고 아무도 나한테 그런 말을 하지 않았더라면, 내가 거칠고 천하다 한들 나한테 무슨 대수였겠어!\""]], [["Biddy turned her face suddenly towards mine, and looked far more attentively at me than she had looked at the sailing ships.", "비디가 갑자기 얼굴을 내 쪽으로 돌리고는, 미끄러져 가는 배들을 바라볼 때보다 훨씬 더 주의 깊게 나를 보았다."]], [["“It was neither a very true nor a very polite thing to say,”", "\"그건 그리 옳은 말도, 그리 점잖은 말도 아니었어.\""], ["she remarked, directing her eyes to the ships again. “Who said it?”", "그녀가 다시 배들로 눈길을 돌리며 말했다. \"누가 그런 말을 했는데?\""]], [["I was disconcerted, for I had broken away without quite seeing where I was going to.", "나는 당황했다. 어디로 가는지도 채 알지 못한 채 내달려 버린 셈이었기 때문이다."], ["It was not to be shuffled off now, however, and I answered, “The beautiful young lady at Miss Havisham’s, and she’s more beautiful than anybody ever was, and I admire her dreadfully, and I want to be a gentleman on her account.”", "그러나 이제 와서 얼버무릴 수는 없었으므로, 나는 대답했다. \"미스 해비셤 댁의 아름다운 아가씨가. 그 애는 여태 누구보다도 아름답고, 나는 그 애를 끔찍이 흠모해."], ["Having made this lunatic confession, I began to throw my torn-up grass into the river, as if I had some thoughts of following it.", "그래서 그 애 때문에 신사가 되고 싶은 거야.\" 이 미친 고백을 하고서, 나는 쥐어뜯은 풀을 강물에 던지기 시작했다. 마치 나 자신도 그것을 따라갈 마음이라도 있는 것처럼."]], [["“Do you want to be a gentleman, to spite her or to gain her over?”", "\"넌 그 애한테 앙갚음을 하려고 신사가 되고 싶은 거야, 아니면 그 애의 마음을 얻으려고?\""], ["Biddy quietly asked me, after a pause.", "비디가 잠시 뜸을 들인 뒤 조용히 내게 물었다."]], [["“I don’t know,” I moodily answered.", "\"모르겠어.\" 나는 시무룩하게 대답했다."]], [["“Because, if it is to spite her,”", "\"왜냐하면, 만약 앙갚음을 하려는 거라면.\""], ["Biddy pursued, “I should think—but you know best—that might be better and more independently done by caring nothing for her words.", "비디가 말을 이었다. \"내 생각엔 — 하지만 네가 제일 잘 알겠지 — 그 애의 말 따위 조금도 마음에 두지 않는 편이 더 낫고 더 떳떳할 것 같아."], ["And if it is to gain her over, I should think—but you know best—she was not worth gaining over.”", "그리고 만약 그 애의 마음을 얻으려는 거라면, 내 생각엔 — 하지만 네가 제일 잘 알겠지 — 그 애는 마음을 얻을 만한 가치가 없어.\""]], [["Exactly what I myself had thought, many times.", "내가 스스로도 여러 번 생각했던 것과 똑같았다."], ["Exactly what was perfectly manifest to me at the moment.", "그 순간 내게 더없이 분명했던 것과 똑같았다."], ["But how could I, a poor dazed village lad, avoid that wonderful inconsistency into which the best and wisest of men fall every day?", "그러나 가엾고 얼떨떨한 시골 소년인 내가, 가장 훌륭하고 가장 지혜로운 사람들조차 날마다 빠지는 그 놀라운 모순을 어찌 피할 수 있었으랴?"]], [["“It may be all quite true,”", "\"다 옳은 말일지도 몰라.\""], ["said I to Biddy, “but I admire her dreadfully.”", "내가 비디에게 말했다. \"하지만 나는 그 애를 끔찍이 흠모하는걸.\""]], [["In short, I turned over on my face when I came to that, and got a good grasp on the hair on each side of my head, and wrenched it well.", "요컨대, 나는 그 대목에 이르자 엎드려 머리 양쪽의 머리카락을 단단히 움켜쥐고 힘껏 쥐어뜯었다."], ["All the while knowing the madness of my heart to be so very mad and misplaced, that I was quite conscious it would have served my face right, if I had lifted it up by my hair, and knocked it against the pebbles as a punishment for belonging to such an idiot.", "내 마음의 광기가 어찌나 그토록 미치고 빗나간 것인지를 그 내내 알고 있었으므로, 내가 머리카락을 잡고 얼굴을 들어 올려, 그런 바보에게 속한 죄로 자갈에 처박는 벌을 준다 해도 마땅하리라는 것을 똑똑히 자각하고 있었다."]], [["Biddy was the wisest of girls, and she tried to reason no more with me.", "비디는 더없이 지혜로운 처녀였으니, 더는 나를 타이르려 하지 않았다."], ["She put her hand, which was a comfortable hand though roughened by work, upon my hands, one after another, and gently took them out of my hair.", "그녀는 일에 거칠어졌어도 편안한 그 손을 내 두 손 위에 차례로 얹고는, 머리카락에서 부드럽게 떼어 냈다. 그러고는 내 어깨를 살며시 토닥이며 달래 주었고, 그동안 나는 소맷자락에 얼굴을 묻은 채 조금 울었다."], ["Then she softly patted my shoulder in a soothing way, while with my face upon my sleeve I cried a little,—exactly as I had done in the brewery yard,—and felt vaguely convinced that I was very much ill-used by somebody, or by everybody; I can’t say which.", "꼭 양조장 마당에서 그랬던 것처럼. 그리고 어렴풋이, 내가 누군가에게, 아니 모두에게 몹시 부당한 대접을 받고 있다는 확신이 들었다. 어느 쪽인지는 말할 수 없지만."]], [["“I am glad of one thing,”", "\"한 가지는 기쁘다.\""], ["said Biddy, “and that is, that you have felt you could give me your confidence, Pip.", "비디가 말했다. \"그건 네가 나한테 속마음을 털어놓아도 되겠다고 느꼈다는 거야, 핍."], ["And I am glad of another thing, and that is, that of course you know you may depend upon my keeping it and always so far deserving it.", "그리고 또 한 가지가 기쁜데, 그건 물론 네가, 내가 그 비밀을 지키고 늘 그만큼 신뢰받을 만하게 처신하리라는 걸 믿어도 된다는 걸 안다는 거야."], ["If your first teacher (dear!", "만일 네 첫 선생이(저런!"], ["such a poor one, and so much in need of being taught herself!) had been your teacher at the present time, she thinks she knows what lesson she would set.", "그토록 변변찮은 선생이었고, 자기야말로 가르침이 그토록 절실히 필요했던 선생이었지!) 지금 이 시점에도 네 선생이었다면, 그 선생은 자기가 어떤 가르침을 내줘야 할지 안다고 생각해."], ["But it would be a hard one to learn, and you have got beyond her, and it’s of no use now.”", "하지만 그건 배우기 어려운 가르침일 테고, 너는 이미 그 선생을 넘어섰으니, 이제 아무 소용이 없겠지.\""], ["So, with a quiet sigh for me, Biddy rose from the bank, and said, with a fresh and pleasant change of voice, “Shall we walk a little farther, or go home?”", "그러고 나서 비디는 나를 위해 조용히 한숨짓고는 둑에서 일어나, 산뜻하고 명랑하게 바뀐 목소리로 말했다. \"조금 더 걸을까, 아니면 집에 갈까?\""]], [["“Biddy,”", "\"비디,\""], ["I cried, getting up, putting my arm round her neck, and giving her a kiss, “I shall always tell you everything.”", "나는 일어서서 그녀의 목에 팔을 두르고 입을 맞추며 소리쳤다. \"나는 너한테는 언제나 뭐든지 다 이야기할게.\""]], [["“Till you’re a gentleman,”", "\"네가 신사가 될 때까지겠지.\""], ["said Biddy.", "비디가 말했다."]], [["“You know I never shall be, so that’s always.", "\"내가 결코 신사가 못 되리란 거 너도 알잖아. 그러니까 언제까지나야."], ["Not that I have any occasion to tell you anything, for you know everything I know,—as I told you at home the other night.”", "하기야 너한테 뭘 이야기할 일이랄 것도 없지. 너는 내가 아는 건 전부 다 아니까. 며칠 전 밤에 집에서 너한테 말했듯이 말이야.\""]], [["“Ah!” said Biddy, quite in a whisper, as she looked away at the ships.", "\"아!\" 비디가 거의 속삭이듯이 말하며, 멀리 배들로 눈길을 돌렸다."], ["And then repeated, with her former pleasant change, “shall we walk a little farther, or go home?”", "그러더니 아까처럼 명랑하게 바뀐 목소리로 되풀이했다. \"조금 더 걸을까, 아니면 집에 갈까?\""]], [["I said to Biddy we would walk a little farther, and we did so, and the summer afternoon toned down into the summer evening, and it was very beautiful.", "나는 비디에게 조금 더 걷자고 말했고, 우리는 그렇게 했으며, 여름 오후는 차츰 여름 저녁으로 누그러져 갔고, 무척이나 아름다웠다."], ["I began to consider whether I was not more naturally and wholesomely situated, after all, in these circumstances, than playing beggar my neighbour by candle-light in the room with the stopped clocks, and being despised by Estella.", "나는 생각하기 시작했다. 멈춰 선 시계들이 있는 그 방에서 촛불 아래 거지 떼어 먹기 노름이나 하고 에스텔라에게 멸시당하는 것보다는, 결국 이런 처지에 있는 편이 나로서는 더 자연스럽고 더 건강한 것이 아닐까 하고."], ["I thought it would be very good for me if I could get her out of my head, with all the rest of those remembrances and fancies, and could go to work determined to relish what I had to do, and stick to it, and make the best of it.", "그 애를, 그 밖의 모든 기억과 공상과 함께 머릿속에서 떨쳐 낼 수만 있다면, 그래서 내가 해야 할 일을 기꺼이 즐기겠다고 마음먹고 일에 임해, 그것을 꾸준히 붙들고, 최선을 다할 수만 있다면, 그게 나한테 무척 좋으리라고 생각했다."], ["I asked myself the question whether I did not surely know that if Estella were beside me at that moment instead of Biddy, she would make me miserable?", "나는 스스로에게 물어보았다. 지금 이 순간 비디 대신 에스텔라가 내 곁에 있다면, 그 애가 나를 비참하게 만들리라는 걸 내가 분명히 알고 있지 않으냐고."], ["I was obliged to admit that I did know it for a certainty, and I said to myself, “Pip, what a fool you are!”", "나는 그것을 확실히 안다고 인정하지 않을 수 없었고, 스스로에게 말했다. \"핍, 넌 정말 바보로구나!\""]], [["We talked a good deal as we walked, and all that Biddy said seemed right.", "우리는 걸으면서 꽤 많은 이야기를 나누었고, 비디가 하는 말은 죄다 옳게 들렸다."], ["Biddy was never insulting, or capricious, or Biddy to-day and somebody else to-morrow; she would have derived only pain, and no pleasure, from giving me pain; she would far rather have wounded her own breast than mine.", "비디는 결코 모욕을 주는 법도, 변덕을 부리는 법도, 오늘은 비디였다가 내일은 딴사람이 되는 법도 없었다. 나에게 고통을 준다면 그녀는 괴로움만 얻을 뿐 기쁨이라곤 얻지 못했으리라. 그녀는 내 가슴에 상처를 입히느니 차라리 제 가슴에 상처를 입히는 편을 훨씬 더 바랐으리라."], ["How could it be, then, that I did not like her much the better of the two?", "그렇다면 어찌하여 나는 둘 중에 그녀를 한결 더 좋아하지 못했던 것일까?"]], [["“Biddy,”", "\"비디,\""], ["said I, when we were walking homeward, “I wish you could put me right.”", "우리가 집으로 걸어가던 중에 내가 말했다. \"네가 나를 바로잡아 줄 수 있으면 좋겠어.\""]], [["“I wish I could!”", "\"나도 그럴 수 있으면 좋겠어!\""], ["said Biddy.", "비디가 말했다."]], [["“If I could only get myself to fall in love with you,—you don’t mind my speaking so openly to such an old acquaintance?”", "\"내가 너를 사랑하게끔 마음만 먹을 수 있다면 — 이렇게 오랜 친구한테 이토록 터놓고 말하는 거, 너 언짢지 않지?\""]], [["“Oh dear, not at all!”", "\"어머나, 전혀!\""], ["said Biddy.", "비디가 말했다."], ["“Don’t mind me.”", "\"나는 신경 쓰지 마.\""]], [["“If I could only get myself to do it, that would be the thing for me.”", "\"내가 그렇게 마음만 먹을 수 있다면, 그게 나한테는 딱 맞는 일일 텐데.\""]], [["“But you never will, you see,”", "\"하지만 넌 절대 그러지 못할걸, 알잖아.\""], ["said Biddy.", "비디가 말했다."]], [["It did not appear quite so unlikely to me that evening, as it would have done if we had discussed it a few hours before.", "그날 저녁의 나로서는, 그것이 몇 시간 전에 그 이야기를 했더라면 들렸을 만큼 그렇게 가망 없는 일로 여겨지지는 않았다."], ["I therefore observed I was not quite sure of that.", "그래서 나는 그건 그리 확신할 수 없다고 말했다."], ["But Biddy said she was, and she said it decisively.", "그러나 비디는 자기는 확신한다고, 그것도 단호하게 말했다."], ["In my heart I believed her to be right; and yet I took it rather ill, too, that she should be so positive on the point.", "마음속으로는 그녀가 옳다고 믿었으나, 그래도 그녀가 그 점에 대해 그토록 단정적인 것이 나는 자못 못마땅했다."]], [["When we came near the churchyard, we had to cross an embankment, and get over a stile near a sluice-gate.", "교회 묘지 가까이 이르렀을 때, 우리는 둑길을 건너고 수문 옆 층계 울타리를 넘어야 했다."], ["There started up, from the gate, or from the rushes, or from the ooze (which was quite in his stagnant way), Old Orlick.", "그 문에서인지, 골풀 속에서인지, 진창에서인지(그것은 그자의 그 정체된 습성에 꼭 어울리는 곳이었다), 늙은 올릭이 불쑥 솟아올랐다."]], [["“Halloa!”", "\"어이!\""], ["he growled, “where are you two going?”", "그가 으르렁댔다. \"둘이 어디 가는 거냐?\""]], [["“Where should we be going, but home?”", "\"집 말고 어디로 가겠어요?\""]], [["“Well, then,”", "\"그렇다면,\""], ["said he, “I’m jiggered if I don’t see you home!”", "그가 말했다. \"내가 너희 집까지 바래다주지 않으면 내가 무지러진 놈이지!\""]], [["This penalty of being jiggered was a favourite supposititious case of his.", "이 무지러진다는 형벌은 그가 즐겨 들먹이는 가상의 사례였다."], ["He attached no definite meaning to the word that I am aware of, but used it, like his own pretended Christian name, to affront mankind, and convey an idea of something savagely damaging.", "내가 아는 한 그는 그 말에 무슨 분명한 뜻을 붙인 것은 아니었으나, 자기가 멋대로 갖다 붙인 세례명처럼, 인류를 모욕하고 무언가 야만스럽게 해를 끼친다는 관념을 전하려고 그 말을 썼다."], ["When I was younger, I had had a general belief that if he had jiggered me personally, he would have done it with a sharp and twisted hook.", "어렸을 적에 나는, 만일 그가 나를 몸소 무지른다면 날카롭고 뒤틀린 갈고리로 그러리라는 막연한 믿음을 품고 있었다."]], [["Biddy was much against his going with us, and said to me in a whisper, “Don’t let him come; I don’t like him.”", "비디는 그가 우리와 동행하는 것을 몹시 꺼려서, 내게 속삭였다. \"오지 못하게 해. 나는 저 사람 싫어.\""], ["As I did not like him either, I took the liberty of saying that we thanked him, but we didn’t want seeing home.", "나도 그가 싫었으므로, 나는 외람되지만 고맙기는 하나 바래다줄 필요는 없다고 말했다."], ["He received that piece of information with a yell of laughter, and dropped back, but came slouching after us at a little distance.", "그는 그 소식을 너털웃음 소리로 받아넘기고는 뒤로 처졌으나, 조금 떨어진 거리에서 어슬렁어슬렁 우리를 뒤따라왔다."]], [["Curious to know whether Biddy suspected him of having had a hand in that murderous attack of which my sister had never been able to give any account, I asked her why she did not like him.", "비디가, 누나가 도무지 진상을 밝히지 못했던 그 흉악한 습격에 그가 가담했던 것은 아닌가 의심하고 있는지 궁금해서, 나는 왜 그가 싫은지 그녀에게 물었다."]], [["“Oh!”", "\"아!\""], ["she replied, glancing over her shoulder as he slouched after us, “because I—I am afraid he likes me.”", "그녀가 어슬렁어슬렁 뒤따라오는 그를 어깨 너머로 흘끔거리며 대답했다. \"왜냐하면 나는 — 나는 그 사람이 나를 좋아할까 봐 무서워.\""]], [["“Did he ever tell you he liked you?”", "\"저자가 너한테 좋아한다고 말한 적이 있어?\""], ["I asked indignantly.", "나는 분개해서 물었다."]], [["“No,”", "\"아니.\""], ["said Biddy, glancing over her shoulder again, “he never told me so; but he dances at me, whenever he can catch my eye.”", "비디가 다시 어깨 너머로 흘끔거리며 말했다. \"그렇게 말한 적은 없어. 하지만 내 눈을 마주칠 수 있을 때마다 나한테 춤을 춰 보인단 말이야.\""]], [["However novel and peculiar this testimony of attachment, I did not doubt the accuracy of the interpretation.", "이 애정의 표시가 아무리 색다르고 유별난 것이었어도, 나는 그 해석의 정확성을 의심하지 않았다."], ["I was very hot indeed upon Old Orlick’s daring to admire her; as hot as if it were an outrage on myself.", "나는 늙은 올릭이 감히 그녀를 흠모한다는 데 정말이지 몹시 화가 났다. 마치 그것이 나 자신에 대한 능욕이기라도 한 듯 분이 치솟았다."]], [["“But it makes no difference to you, you know,”", "\"하지만 그게 너랑은 아무 상관 없잖아, 알지.\""], ["said Biddy, calmly.", "비디가 차분히 말했다."]], [["“No, Biddy, it makes no difference to me; only I don’t like it; I don’t approve of it.”", "\"그래, 비디, 나랑은 아무 상관 없지. 다만 나는 그게 싫어. 나는 그걸 용납할 수가 없어.\""]], [["“Nor I neither,”", "\"나도 마찬가지야.\""], ["said Biddy.", "비디가 말했다."], ["“Though that makes no difference to you.”", "\"그게 너랑은 아무 상관 없지만 말이야.\""]], [["“Exactly,”", "\"바로 그거지.\""], ["said I; “but I must tell you I should have no opinion of you, Biddy, if he danced at you with your own consent.”", "내가 말했다. \"하지만 말해 두는데, 비디, 네가 동의해서 저자가 너한테 춤을 춰 보이는 거라면, 나는 너를 못 볼 거야.\""]], [["I kept an eye on Orlick after that night, and, whenever circumstances were favourable to his dancing at Biddy, got before him to obscure that demonstration.", "그날 밤 이후로 나는 올릭을 눈여겨보았고, 그가 비디에게 춤을 춰 보이기에 좋은 상황만 되면 언제든 그의 앞을 가로막아 그 짓거리를 가려 버렸다."], ["He had struck root in Joe’s establishment, by reason of my sister’s sudden fancy for him, or I should have tried to get him dismissed.", "그는 누나가 갑작스레 그를 마음에 들어 한 까닭에 조의 작업장에 뿌리를 내린 터였으니, 그렇지만 않았다면 나는 그를 내쫓으려고 애를 썼을 것이다."], ["He quite understood and reciprocated my good intentions, as I had reason to know thereafter.", "그는 내 선한 의도를 똑똑히 알아채고 똑같이 갚아 주었는데, 그 점은 그 후에 알게 되었다."]], [["And now, because my mind was not confused enough before, I complicated its confusion fifty thousand-fold, by having states and seasons when I was clear that Biddy was immeasurably better than Estella, and that the plain honest working life to which I was born had nothing in it to be ashamed of, but offered me sufficient means of self-respect and happiness.", "그리고 이제, 내 마음이 그 전에도 충분히 어지럽지 않았다는 듯, 나는 그 어지러움을 오만 갈래로 더 복잡하게 만들어 놓았다. 어떤 때, 어떤 시절에는, 비디가 에스텔라보다 헤아릴 수 없이 더 낫다는 것, 그리고 내가 태어나면서부터 짊어진 그 소박하고 정직한 노동의 삶에는 부끄러워할 만한 구석이라곤 없으며 오히려 자존감과 행복을 누릴 충분한 방편을 내게 베풀어 준다는 것이 분명하게 보였기 때문이다."], ["At those times, I would decide conclusively that my disaffection to dear old Joe and the forge was gone, and that I was growing up in a fair way to be partners with Joe and to keep company with Biddy,—when all in a moment some confounding remembrance of the Havisham days would fall upon me like a destructive missile, and scatter my wits again.", "그런 때면 나는 결론을 내리곤 했다. 그리운 조와 대장간에 대한 내 반감은 사라졌고, 나는 조와 동업자가 되고 비디와 사귀는 떳떳한 길로 자라 가고 있다고. 그러던 차에 한순간, 해비셤 댁 시절의 어떤 어지러운 기억이 파괴적인 포탄처럼 나를 덮쳐 내 분별을 다시금 흩어 놓곤 했다."], ["Scattered wits take a long time picking up; and often before I had got them well together, they would be dispersed in all directions by one stray thought, that perhaps after all Miss Havisham was going to make my fortune when my time was out.", "흩어진 분별은 다시 그러모으는 데 오랜 시간이 걸리는 법이고, 게다가 그것을 채 잘 추스르기도 전에, 어쩌면 결국 내 도제살이가 끝나면 미스 해비셤이 내 출셋길을 열어 줄 작정인지도 모른다는 한 가닥 엉뚱한 생각에 그것이 사방으로 흩어져 버리는 일이 잦았다."]], [["If my time had run out, it would have left me still at the height of my perplexities, I dare say.", "만일 내 도제살이의 기한이 다 찼더라면, 짐작건대 그것은 나를 여전히 그 혼란의 절정에 내버려 둔 채 끝나고 말았으리라."], ["It never did run out, however, but was brought to a premature end, as I proceed to relate.", "그러나 그 기한은 결코 다 차지 않았으니, 이제 이야기하려는 대로 때 이른 종말을 맞고 말았다."]], [["# Chapter XVIII.", "# Chapter XVIII."]], [["It was in the fourth year of my apprenticeship to Joe, and it was a Saturday night.", "조에게 도제로 들어간 지 사년째 되던 해, 어느 토요일 밤이었다."], ["There was a group assembled round the fire at the Three Jolly Bargemen, attentive to Mr. Wopsle as he read the newspaper aloud.", "세 명랑한 뱃사공 주막의 난롯가에 한 무리가 둘러앉아, 신문을 소리 내어 읽는 윕슬 씨에게 귀를 기울이고 있었다."], ["Of that group I was one.", "나도 그 무리 가운데 하나였다."]], [["A highly popular murder had been committed, and Mr. Wopsle was imbrued in blood to the eyebrows.", "세상의 이목을 크게 끈 살인 사건이 일어났던 터라, 윕슬 씨는 눈썹까지 피로 물들어 있었다."], ["He gloated over every abhorrent adjective in the description, and identified himself with every witness at the Inquest.", "그는 그 묘사에 나오는 끔찍한 형용사 하나하나를 음미하듯 즐겼고, 검시 심문에 나오는 증인이란 증인에 모두 자신을 일치시켰다."], ["He faintly moaned, “I am done for,” as the victim, and he barbarously bellowed, “I’ll serve you out,” as the murderer.", "피해자가 되어서는 \"나는 끝장이다.\"라고 가냘프게 신음했고, 살인자가 되어서는 \"너를 손봐 주겠다.\"라고 야만스럽게 고함쳤다."], ["He gave the medical testimony, in pointed imitation of our local practitioner; and he piped and shook, as the aged turnpike-keeper who had heard blows, to an extent so very paralytic as to suggest a doubt regarding the mental competency of that witness.", "의학적 증언을 할 때는 우리 고장 의사를 노골적으로 흉내 냈고, 가격하는 소리를 들었다는 늙은 통행료 징수원이 되어서는 그 증인의 정신적 능력을 의심하게 할 만큼 마비된 모양으로 가늘게 떨며 새된 소리를 냈다."], ["The coroner, in Mr. Wopsle’s hands, became Timon of Athens; the beadle, Coriolanus.", "검시관은 윕슬 씨의 손에서 아테네의 티몬이 되었고, 정리는 코리올라누스가 되었다."], ["He enjoyed himself thoroughly, and we all enjoyed ourselves, and were delightfully comfortable.", "그는 더할 나위 없이 자신을 즐겼고, 우리도 모두 즐거워하며 흐뭇하게 편안했다."], ["In this cosey state of mind we came to the verdict Wilful Murder.", "이렇게 아늑한 마음 상태로 우리는 고의적 살인이라는 평결에 이르렀다."]], [["Then, and not sooner, I became aware of a strange gentleman leaning over the back of the settle opposite me, looking on.", "그제야, 그 이전이 아니라 비로소, 나는 내 맞은편 긴 의자 등받이에 몸을 기댄 채 지켜보고 있는 낯선 신사 하나를 알아차렸다."], ["There was an expression of contempt on his face, and he bit the side of a great forefinger as he watched the group of faces.", "그의 얼굴에는 경멸의 표정이 어려 있었고, 그는 그 여러 얼굴들의 무리를 살피며 커다란 집게손가락 옆구리를 깨물고 있었다."]], [["“Well!”", "\"흠!\""], ["said the stranger to Mr. Wopsle, when the reading was done, “you have settled it all to your own satisfaction, I have no doubt?”", "낭독이 끝나자 그 낯선 사내가 윕슬 씨에게 말했다. \"당신은 모든 걸 당신 마음에 흡족하도록 결판내 버렸겠지요, 의심할 여지가 없이?\""]], [["Everybody started and looked up, as if it were the murderer.", "마치 살인자라도 나타난 듯, 모두가 흠칫 놀라며 고개를 들었다."], ["He looked at everybody coldly and sarcastically.", "그는 모두를 차갑고 빈정대는 눈으로 바라보았다."]], [["“Guilty, of course?”", "\"물론 유죄겠지요?\""], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."], ["“Out with it. Come!”", "\"어디 말해 보시오. 자!\""]], [["“Sir,”", "\"선생.\""], ["returned Mr. Wopsle, “without having the honour of your acquaintance, I do say Guilty.”", "윕슬 씨가 대답했다. \"당신과 안면을 트는 영광은 누리지 못했으나, 나는 유죄라고 말하겠소.\""], ["Upon this we all took courage to unite in a confirmatory murmur.", "이에 우리는 모두 용기를 내어 동의하는 웅성거림으로 한목소리를 냈다."]], [["“I know you do,” said the stranger; “I knew you would.", "\"당신이 그러리란 걸 알고 있소.\" 낯선 사내가 말했다."], ["I told you so. But now I’ll ask you a question.", "\"그러리라는 걸 알고 있었지. 내가 그렇게 말하지 않았소."], ["Do you know, or do you not know, that the law of England supposes every man to be innocent, until he is proved—proved—to be guilty?”", "그런데 이제 내가 한 가지 묻겠소. 당신은 영국 법이 모든 사람을 유죄로 입증되기 전까지는, 입증되기 전까지는 말이오, 무죄로 추정한다는 사실을 아시오, 모르시오?\""]], [["“Sir,” Mr.", "\"선생,\""], ["Wopsle began to reply, “as an Englishman myself, I—”", "윕슬 씨가 대답하기 시작했다. \"나도 영국인으로서, 나는—\""]], [["“Come!”", "\"자!\""], ["said the stranger, biting his forefinger at him.", "낯선 사내가 그를 향해 집게손가락을 깨물며 말했다."], ["“Don’t evade the question.", "\"질문을 회피하지 마시오."], ["Either you know it, or you don’t know it. Which is it to be?”", "당신은 그걸 알거나, 아니면 모르거나, 둘 중 하나요. 어느 쪽이오?\""]], [["He stood with his head on one side and himself on one side, in a bullying, interrogative manner, and he threw his forefinger at Mr. Wopsle,—as it were to mark him out—before biting it again.", "그는 고개를 한쪽으로 기울이고 몸도 한쪽으로 비스듬히 한 채, 윽박지르듯 따져 묻는 태도로 서서, 마치 윕슬 씨를 콕 집어내려는 듯 그를 향해 집게손가락을 내던졌다가 다시 그 손가락을 깨물었다."]], [["“Now!”", "\"자!\""], ["said he. “Do you know it, or don’t you know it?”", "그가 말했다. \"당신은 그걸 아시오, 모르시오?\""]], [["“Certainly I know it,”", "\"물론 알고 있소.\""], ["replied Mr. Wopsle.", "윕슬 씨가 대답했다."]], [["“Certainly you know it.", "\"물론 알고 있다."], ["Then why didn’t you say so at first? Now, I’ll ask you another question,”—taking possession of Mr.", "그렇다면 어째서 처음부터 그렇게 말하지 않았소? 자, 이제 한 가지 더 묻겠소.\""], ["Wopsle, as if he had a right to him,—“do you know that none of these witnesses have yet been cross-examined?”", "그는 마치 윕슬 씨에 대한 권리라도 가진 듯 그를 틀어쥐었다. \"당신은 이 증인들 가운데 누구도 아직 반대 신문을 받지 않았다는 사실을 아시오?\""]], [["Mr. Wopsle was beginning, “I can only say—”", "윕슬 씨가 \"내가 말할 수 있는 건 다만—\""], ["when the stranger stopped him.", "하고 입을 떼는데, 낯선 사내가 그를 막았다."]], [["“What?", "\"뭐요?"], ["You won’t answer the question, yes or no?", "예, 아니요로 대답하지 않겠다는 거요?"], ["Now, I’ll try you again.”", "자, 다시 한번 해 봅시다.\""], ["Throwing his finger at him again.", "그가 다시 손가락을 내던졌다."], ["“Attend to me.", "\"잘 들으시오."], ["Are you aware, or are you not aware, that none of these witnesses have yet been cross-examined?", "당신은 이 증인들 가운데 누구도 아직 반대 신문을 받지 않았다는 사실을 알고 있소, 모르고 있소?"], ["Come, I only want one word from you. Yes, or no?”", "자, 나는 당신에게서 단 한마디만 듣고 싶소. 예, 아니면 아니요?\""]], [["Mr. Wopsle hesitated, and we all began to conceive rather a poor opinion of him.", "윕슬 씨가 머뭇거리자, 우리는 모두 그에 대해 다소 시원찮은 평가를 품기 시작했다."]], [["“Come!”", "\"자!\""], ["said the stranger, “I’ll help you.", "낯선 사내가 말했다. \"내가 도와주리다."], ["You don’t deserve help, but I’ll help you. Look at that paper you hold in your hand.", "당신은 도움을 받을 자격이 없지만, 그래도 도와주겠소. 당신 손에 든 그 신문을 보시오."], ["What is it?”", "그게 뭐요?\""]], [["“What is it?”", "\"이게 뭐냐고요?\""], ["repeated Mr. Wopsle, eyeing it, much at a loss.", "윕슬 씨가 어쩔 줄 몰라 하며 그것을 들여다보고는 되물었다."]], [["“Is it,”", "\"그게,\""], ["pursued the stranger in his most sarcastic and suspicious manner, “the printed paper you have just been reading from?”", "낯선 사내가 더없이 빈정대고 의심하는 투로 말을 이었다. \"당신이 방금 읽고 있던 그 인쇄된 신문이오?\""]], [["“Undoubtedly.”", "\"틀림없이 그렇소.\""]], [["“Undoubtedly. Now, turn to that paper, and tell me whether it distinctly states that the prisoner expressly said that his legal advisers instructed him altogether to reserve his defence?”", "\"틀림없이 그렇다. 자, 그 신문을 펼쳐서, 피고가 자기 법률 고문이 변론을 전적으로 유보하라고 일러 주었다고 명백히 진술했다는 사실이 거기에 또렷이 적혀 있는지 말해 보시오.\""]], [["“I read that just now,” Mr. Wopsle pleaded.", "\"방금 그걸 읽었소.\" 윕슬 씨가 항변했다."]], [["“Never mind what you read just now, sir; I don’t ask you what you read just now.", "\"방금 뭘 읽었는지는 상관없소, 선생. 나는 당신이 방금 무엇을 읽었는지 묻는 게 아니오."], ["You may read the Lord’s Prayer backwards, if you like,—and, perhaps, have done it before to-day.", "원한다면 주기도문을 거꾸로 읽어도 좋소. 어쩌면 오늘 이전에 그래 본 적도 있을 테고."], ["Turn to the paper.", "신문을 펼치시오."], ["No, no, no my friend; not to the top of the column; you know better than that; to the bottom, to the bottom.”", "아니, 아니, 아니, 친구, 단 윗부분 말고. 그 정도는 알 만하지 않소. 아래쪽, 아래쪽 말이오.\""], ["(We all began to think Mr. Wopsle full of subterfuge.) “Well? Have you found it?”", "(우리는 모두 윕슬 씨가 잔꾀로 가득한 사람이라고 여기기 시작했다.) \"어떻소? 찾았소?\""]], [["“Here it is,”", "\"여기 있소.\""], ["said Mr. Wopsle.", "윕슬 씨가 말했다."]], [["“Now, follow that passage with your eye, and tell me whether it distinctly states that the prisoner expressly said that he was instructed by his legal advisers wholly to reserve his defence? Come!", "\"자, 그 대목을 눈으로 따라가며, 피고가 자기 법률 고문에게서 변론을 전적으로 유보하라는 지시를 받았다고 명백히 진술했다는 사실이 거기 또렷이 적혀 있는지 말해 보시오. 자!"], ["Do you make that of it?”", "그렇게 읽히오?\""]], [["Mr. Wopsle answered, “Those are not the exact words.”", "윕슬 씨가 대답했다. \"정확히 그 단어들은 아니오.\""]], [["“Not the exact words!”", "\"정확히 그 단어들은 아니다!\""], ["repeated the gentleman bitterly.", "그 신사가 쓴웃음을 지으며 되받았다."], ["“Is that the exact substance?”", "\"그게 정확한 요지요?\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"그렇소.\""], ["said Mr. Wopsle.", "윕슬 씨가 말했다."]], [["“Yes,” repeated the stranger, looking round at the rest of the company with his right hand extended towards the witness, Wopsle.", "\"그렇다.\" 낯선 사내가 증인인 윕슬을 향해 오른손을 뻗은 채 좌중의 나머지 사람들을 둘러보며 되풀이했다."], ["“And now I ask you what you say to the conscience of that man who, with that passage before his eyes, can lay his head upon his pillow after having pronounced a fellow-creature guilty, unheard?”", "\"자 이제 내가 당신에게 묻겠소. 그 대목을 눈앞에 두고서도, 동료 인간을 그 변론도 들어 보지 않고 유죄로 선고한 뒤 베개에 머리를 누일 수 있는 그런 자의 양심에 대해 당신은 뭐라 말하겠소?\""]], [["We all began to suspect that Mr. Wopsle was not the man we had thought him, and that he was beginning to be found out.", "우리는 모두 윕슬 씨가 우리가 여겨 온 그런 사람이 아니며, 이제 그 정체가 드러나기 시작하는 게 아닐까 의심하기 시작했다."]], [["“And that same man, remember,” pursued the gentleman, throwing his finger at Mr.", "\"그리고 잊지 마시오, 바로 그 같은 사람이,\" 신사가 윕슬 씨를 향해 손가락을 묵직하게 내던지며 말을 이었다."], ["Wopsle heavily,—“that same man might be summoned as a juryman upon this very trial, and, having thus deeply committed himself, might return to the bosom of his family and lay his head upon his pillow, after deliberately swearing that he would well and truly try the issue joined between Our Sovereign Lord the King and the prisoner at the bar, and would a true verdict give according to the evidence, so help him God!”", "\"바로 그 같은 사람이 다름 아닌 이 재판에 배심원으로 소환될 수도 있고, 그렇게 스스로를 깊이 옭아맨 채로도, 우리의 군주이신 국왕 폐하와 피고석의 죄수 사이에 벌어진 쟁점을 충실하고 진실하게 심리하며 증거에 따라 진실한 평결을 내리겠노라고, 하느님께 맹세하노라고 신중히 선서한 뒤에, 제 가족의 품으로 돌아가 베개에 머리를 누일 수도 있다는 말이오!\""]], [["We were all deeply persuaded that the unfortunate Wopsle had gone too far, and had better stop in his reckless career while there was yet time.", "우리는 모두 그 가엾은 윕슬이 너무 멀리 갔으며, 아직 시간이 있을 때 그 무모한 행보를 멈추는 편이 낫겠다고 깊이 확신하게 되었다."]], [["The strange gentleman, with an air of authority not to be disputed, and with a manner expressive of knowing something secret about every one of us that would effectually do for each individual if he chose to disclose it, left the back of the settle, and came into the space between the two settles, in front of the fire, where he remained standing, his left hand in his pocket, and he biting the forefinger of his right.", "그 낯선 신사는 반박할 수 없는 권위를 풍기는 데다, 우리 한 사람 한 사람에 대해 마음만 먹으면 폭로하여 각자를 영락없이 끝장낼 만한 비밀을 알고 있는 듯한 태도로, 긴 의자 등받이에서 몸을 떼고 두 긴 의자 사이, 난롯불 앞 공간으로 들어와, 왼손은 주머니에 찔러 넣고 오른손 집게손가락은 깨물면서 그대로 서 있었다."]], [["“From information I have received,”", "\"내가 입수한 정보에 따르면,\""], ["said he, looking round at us as we all quailed before him, “I have reason to believe there is a blacksmith among you, by name Joseph—or Joe—Gargery.", "우리가 모두 그 앞에서 주눅이 들어 있는 동안 그가 우리를 둘러보며 말했다. \"이 가운데 조지프—혹은 조—가저리라는 이름의 대장장이가 있다고 믿을 만한 근거가 있소."], ["Which is the man?”", "그게 누구요?\""]], [["“Here is the man,”", "\"여기 있소이다.\""], ["said Joe.", "조가 말했다."]], [["The strange gentleman beckoned him out of his place, and Joe went.", "낯선 신사가 손짓으로 그를 제자리에서 불러냈고, 조는 나아갔다."]], [["“You have an apprentice,”", "\"당신에게는 도제가 하나 있소.\""], ["pursued the stranger, “commonly known as Pip? Is he here?”", "낯선 사내가 말을 이었다. \"흔히 핍이라 불리는 자 말이오."], ["“I am here!” I cried.", "그가 여기 있소?\""]], [["The stranger did not recognise me, but I recognised him as the gentleman I had met on the stairs, on the occasion of my second visit to Miss Havisham.", "\"제가 여기 있습니다!\" 내가 외쳤다. 그 낯선 사내는 나를 알아보지 못했으나, 나는 그가 미스 해비셤을 두 번째로 찾아갔을 때 계단에서 마주쳤던 그 신사임을 알아보았다."], ["I had known him the moment I saw him looking over the settle, and now that I stood confronting him with his hand upon my shoulder, I checked off again in detail his large head, his dark complexion, his deep-set eyes, his bushy black eyebrows, his large watch-chain, his strong black dots of beard and whisker, and even the smell of scented soap on his great hand.", "그가 긴 의자 너머로 살피던 순간부터 나는 그를 알아보았고, 이제 그가 내 어깨에 손을 얹은 채 마주 서게 되자, 나는 그의 큼직한 머리, 가무잡잡한 안색, 깊이 들어간 눈, 무성한 검은 눈썹, 큼직한 시곗줄, 진한 검은 점처럼 박힌 턱수염과 구레나룻, 그리고 그 커다란 손에서 풍기는 향내 나는 비누 냄새까지, 다시금 하나하나 꼽아 보았다."]], [["“I wish to have a private conference with you two,”", "\"나는 두 분과 사적으로 이야기를 나누고 싶소.\""], ["said he, when he had surveyed me at his leisure.", "그가 나를 느긋하게 살펴본 뒤 말했다."], ["“It will take a little time.", "\"시간이 조금 걸릴 거요."], ["Perhaps we had better go to your place of residence.", "아무래도 두 분 댁으로 가는 편이 낫겠소."], ["I prefer not to anticipate my communication here; you will impart as much or as little of it as you please to your friends afterwards; I have nothing to do with that.”", "여기서 내 전할 말을 미리 꺼내고 싶지는 않으니. 그 일은 나중에 두 분이 친구들에게 원하는 만큼 많이든 적게든 전하면 될 일이오. 그건 나와 상관없는 일이오.\""]], [["Amidst a wondering silence, we three walked out of the Jolly Bargemen, and in a wondering silence walked home.", "의아스러운 침묵 속에서 우리 셋은 명랑한 뱃사공 주막을 걸어 나왔고, 또 의아스러운 침묵 속에서 집까지 걸어갔다."], ["While going along, the strange gentleman occasionally looked at me, and occasionally bit the side of his finger.", "가는 동안 그 낯선 신사는 이따금 나를 쳐다보았고, 이따금 손가락 옆구리를 깨물었다."], ["As we neared home, Joe vaguely acknowledging the occasion as an impressive and ceremonious one, went on ahead to open the front door.", "집에 가까워지자, 조는 이 자리를 인상적이고 격식 있는 자리로 어렴풋이 받아들였는지, 앞장서 가서 현관문을 열었다."], ["Our conference was held in the state parlour, which was feebly lighted by one candle.", "우리의 회담은 촛불 하나가 희미하게 밝히는 그 고급 응접실에서 열렸다."]], [["It began with the strange gentleman’s sitting down at the table, drawing the candle to him, and looking over some entries in his pocket-book.", "회담은 그 낯선 신사가 탁자에 앉아 촛불을 제 쪽으로 끌어당기고 수첩의 몇몇 적힌 내용을 들여다보는 것으로 시작되었다."], ["He then put up the pocket-book and set the candle a little aside, after peering round it into the darkness at Joe and me, to ascertain which was which.", "그러더니 그는 수첩을 도로 집어넣고, 촛불 너머로 어둠 속의 조와 나를 들여다보며 누가 누구인지 확인한 다음, 촛불을 한쪽으로 조금 밀어 놓았다."]], [["“My name,”", "\"내 이름은,\""], ["he said, “is Jaggers, and I am a lawyer in London.", "그가 말했다. \"재거스이고, 나는 런던의 변호사요."], ["I am pretty well known.", "제법 이름이 알려져 있소."], ["I have unusual business to transact with you, and I commence by explaining that it is not of my originating.", "나는 당신들과 처리해야 할 예사롭지 않은 일이 있는데, 우선 이것이 내 발의로 비롯된 일이 아니라는 점부터 밝혀 두겠소."], ["If my advice had been asked, I should not have been here.", "만약 내 의견을 물었더라면, 나는 여기 있지 않았을 거요."], ["It was not asked, and you see me here.", "묻지 않았기에, 이렇게 여기 와 있는 거요."], ["What I have to do as the confidential agent of another, I do. No less, no more.”", "나는 다른 사람의 비밀 대리인으로서 해야 할 일을 할 뿐이오. 덜도 아니고 더도 아니오.\""]], [["Finding that he could not see us very well from where he sat, he got up, and threw one leg over the back of a chair and leaned upon it; thus having one foot on the seat of the chair, and one foot on the ground.", "앉은 자리에서는 우리가 잘 보이지 않자, 그는 일어서서 한쪽 다리를 의자 등받이 너머로 걸치고 거기에 몸을 기댔다. 그리하여 한 발은 의자 좌석 위에, 한 발은 바닥에 둔 자세가 되었다."]], [["“Now, Joseph Gargery, I am the bearer of an offer to relieve you of this young fellow your apprentice.", "\"자, 조지프 가저리, 나는 당신의 도제인 이 젊은이를 당신 손에서 풀어 주자는 제안을 전하러 온 사람이오."], ["You would not object to cancel his indentures at his request and for his good?", "그의 요청에 따라, 또 그의 이익을 위해 그의 도제 계약을 해지하는 데 반대하지는 않겠지요?"], ["You would want nothing for so doing?”", "그렇게 하는 대가로 아무것도 바라지 않을 테고?\""]], [["“Lord forbid that I should want anything for not standing in Pip’s way,”", "\"핍의 앞길을 막지 않는 대가로 제가 뭘 바라다니, 하느님께서 막아 주실 일이지요.\""], ["said Joe, staring.", "조가 멍하니 바라보며 말했다."]], [["“Lord forbidding is pious, but not to the purpose,”", "\"하느님께서 막아 주신다는 건 경건한 말이지만, 요점에는 맞지 않소.\""], ["returned Mr. Jaggers.", "재거스 씨가 받아쳤다."], ["“The question is, Would you want anything?", "\"문제는, 당신이 무언가를 바랄 것이냐 하는 거요."], ["Do you want anything?”", "당신은 무언가를 바라오?\""]], [["“The answer is,”", "\"대답은,\""], ["returned Joe, sternly, “No.”", "조가 단호하게 받았다. \"아니오, 요.\""]], [["I thought Mr. Jaggers glanced at Joe, as if he considered him a fool for his disinterestedness.", "재거스 씨가 조의 그런 사심 없음을 두고 그를 어리석은 자로 여기는 듯 흘끗 보았다고 나는 생각했다."], ["But I was too much bewildered between breathless curiosity and surprise, to be sure of it.", "그러나 나는 숨 막히는 호기심과 놀라움 사이에서 너무나 얼떨떨해, 그것을 확신할 수는 없었다."]], [["“Very well,”", "\"아주 좋소.\""], ["said Mr. Jaggers.", "재거스 씨가 말했다."], ["“Recollect the admission you have made, and don’t try to go from it presently.”", "\"당신이 한 그 인정을 기억해 두고, 곧이라도 거기서 물러나려 들지 마시오.\""]], [["“Who’s a-going to try?”", "\"누가 물러나려 든답디까?\""], ["retorted Joe.", "조가 쏘아붙였다."]], [["“I don’t say anybody is.", "\"누가 그런다는 건 아니오."], ["Do you keep a dog?”", "당신은 개를 기르오?\""]], [["“Yes, I do keep a dog.”", "\"네, 개를 기르지요.\""]], [["“Bear in mind then, that Brag is a good dog, but Holdfast is a better. Bear that in mind, will you?”", "\"그렇다면 이걸 명심하시오. 짖어 대는 개도 좋은 개지만, 꽉 무는 개가 더 나은 개라는 걸."], ["repeated Mr. Jaggers, shutting his eyes and nodding his head at Joe, as if he were forgiving him something.", "그걸 명심하겠소?\" 재거스 씨는 무언가를 용서해 주기라도 하듯 눈을 감고 조를 향해 고개를 끄덕이며 되풀이했다."], ["“Now, I return to this young fellow.", "\"자, 이제 이 젊은이 이야기로 돌아가겠소."], ["And the communication I have got to make is, that he has great expectations.”", "내가 전해야 할 소식은, 이 사람에게 막대한 유산에 대한 기대가 있다는 거요.\""]], [["Joe and I gasped, and looked at one another.", "조와 나는 숨을 들이켜며 서로를 바라보았다."]], [["“I am instructed to communicate to him,” said Mr.", "\"나는 이 사람에게 다음과 같이 전하라는 지시를 받았소.\""], ["Jaggers, throwing his finger at me sideways, “that he will come into a handsome property.", "재거스 씨가 나를 향해 옆으로 손가락을 내던지며 말했다."], ["Further, that it is the desire of the present possessor of that property, that he be immediately removed from his present sphere of life and from this place, and be brought up as a gentleman,—in a word, as a young fellow of great expectations.”", "\"그가 상당한 재산을 물려받게 되리라는 것을. 또한 그 재산의 현재 소유자의 바람은, 그가 지금의 생활 영역과 이곳에서 즉시 떠나, 신사로—한마디로, 막대한 유산에 대한 기대를 지닌 젊은이로 양육되어야 한다는 것이오.\""]], [["My dream was out; my wild fancy was surpassed by sober reality; Miss Havisham was going to make my fortune on a grand scale.", "내 꿈이 실현된 것이다. 내 터무니없던 공상을 냉철한 현실이 능가해 버렸다. 미스 해비셤이 나의 운명을 거창한 규모로 만들어 주려는 것이었다."]], [["“Now, Mr. Pip,”", "\"자, 핍 군.\""], ["pursued the lawyer, “I address the rest of what I have to say, to you.", "변호사가 말을 이었다. \"이제 나머지 할 말은 자네에게 하겠네."], ["You are to understand, first, that it is the request of the person from whom I take my instructions that you always bear the name of Pip.", "우선 자네가 이해해 둘 것은, 내가 지시를 받는 그분의 요청이 자네가 항상 핍이라는 이름을 지녀야 한다는 것이라는 점이네."], ["You will have no objection, I dare say, to your great expectations being encumbered with that easy condition.", "그 손쉬운 조건이 자네의 막대한 유산에 얹힌다고 해서 자네가 이의를 제기하진 않으리라 생각하네."], ["But if you have any objection, this is the time to mention it.”", "하지만 혹시 이의가 있다면, 지금이 그걸 말할 때일세.\""]], [["My heart was beating so fast, and there was such a singing in my ears, that I could scarcely stammer I had no objection.", "내 심장은 어찌나 빨리 뛰던지, 또 귓속에서 어찌나 윙윙거리던지, 나는 가까스로 더듬거리며 이의가 없다고 말할 수 있었다."]], [["“I should think not!", "\"그렇겠지!"], ["Now you are to understand, secondly, Mr. Pip, that the name of the person who is your liberal benefactor remains a profound secret, until the person chooses to reveal it.", "자, 둘째로 이해해 둘 것은, 핍 군, 자네의 그 후한 은인의 이름이, 그분이 직접 밝히기로 택할 때까지는 철저한 비밀로 남는다는 것이네."], ["I am empowered to mention that it is the intention of the person to reveal it at first hand by word of mouth to yourself.", "나는 그분이 그것을 직접 자네에게 입으로 밝힐 의향이라는 점은 말해도 좋다는 권한을 받았네."], ["When or where that intention may be carried out, I cannot say; no one can say.", "그 의향이 언제 어디서 실행될지는 나도 말할 수 없고, 누구도 말할 수 없네."], ["It may be years hence.", "몇 해 뒤가 될 수도 있지."], ["Now, you are distinctly to understand that you are most positively prohibited from making any inquiry on this head, or any allusion or reference, however distant, to any individual whomsoever as the individual, in all the communications you may have with me.", "자, 자네는 이 점을 분명히 이해해 두게. 자네는 나와 나눌 어떤 대화에서도, 이 사안에 관해 어떤 질문도 하지 못하도록, 또 그 어떤 인물을 두고서든 그분이라고 암시하거나 언급하는 일을, 아무리 에둘러서라도, 절대로 하지 못하도록 단연코 금지되어 있다는 것을."], ["If you have a suspicion in your own breast, keep that suspicion in your own breast.", "자네 가슴속에 어떤 의심이 든다면, 그 의심은 자네 가슴속에 묻어 두게."], ["It is not the least to the purpose what the reasons of this prohibition are; they may be the strongest and gravest reasons, or they may be mere whim.", "이 금지의 이유가 무엇인지는 조금도 중요하지 않네. 그것은 더없이 강력하고 중대한 이유일 수도 있고, 그저 변덕일 수도 있네."], ["This is not for you to inquire into.", "그것은 자네가 캐물을 일이 아닐세."], ["The condition is laid down.", "조건은 정해졌네."], ["Your acceptance of it, and your observance of it as binding, is the only remaining condition that I am charged with, by the person from whom I take my instructions, and for whom I am not otherwise responsible.", "자네가 그것을 받아들이고 구속력 있는 것으로 지키는 것이, 내가 지시를 받고 있고 그 밖의 일로는 내가 책임지지 않는 그분에게서 부여받은, 남은 유일한 조건이네."], ["That person is the person from whom you derive your expectations, and the secret is solely held by that person and by me.", "그분이 바로 자네의 유산이 비롯되는 분이며, 그 비밀은 오직 그분과 나만이 쥐고 있네."], ["Again, not a very difficult condition with which to encumber such a rise in fortune; but if you have any objection to it, this is the time to mention it.", "거듭 말하지만, 이 정도 행운의 도약에 얹힌다고 해서 그리 어려운 조건은 아닐세. 하지만 혹시 이의가 있다면, 지금이 그걸 말할 때일세."], ["Speak out.”", "어디 말해 보게.\""]], [["Once more, I stammered with difficulty that I had no objection.", "나는 다시 한번 힘겹게 더듬거리며 이의가 없다고 말했다."]], [["“I should think not!", "\"그렇겠지!"], ["Now, Mr. Pip, I have done with stipulations.” Though he called me Mr.", "자, 핍 군, 조건에 관한 이야기는 이제 끝났네.\""], ["Pip, and began rather to make up to me, he still could not get rid of a certain air of bullying suspicion; and even now he occasionally shut his eyes and threw his finger at me while he spoke, as much as to express that he knew all kinds of things to my disparagement, if he only chose to mention them.", "그는 나를 핍 군이라 부르며 다소 친근하게 대하기 시작했으나, 여전히 윽박지르듯 의심하는 분위기를 떨쳐 내지는 못했고, 지금도 말하는 동안 이따금 눈을 감고 내게 손가락을 내던졌는데, 그것은 마치 마음만 먹으면 나를 깎아내릴 온갖 일을 들춰낼 수 있다는 것을 드러내는 듯했다."], ["“We come next, to mere details of arrangement.", "\"다음은 단순한 준비 사항의 세부로 넘어가겠네."], ["You must know that, although I have used the term ‘expectations’ more than once, you are not endowed with expectations only.", "자네가 알아 둘 것은, 내가 '기대'라는 말을 여러 번 썼지만, 자네에게 주어진 것이 기대뿐만은 아니라는 점이네."], ["There is already lodged in my hands a sum of money amply sufficient for your suitable education and maintenance.", "자네의 적절한 교육과 생활 유지에 충분하고도 남을 만한 금액이 이미 내 손에 맡겨져 있네. 나를 자네의 후견인으로 여겨 주게. 오!\""], ["You will please consider me your guardian. Oh!”", "내가 그에게 고마움을 표하려 하자 그가 말했다."], ["for I was going to thank him, “I tell you at once, I am paid for my services, or I shouldn’t render them.", "\"당장 말해 두는데, 나는 내 일에 대한 보수를 받고 있네. 그렇지 않으면 그 일을 하지 않을 걸세."], ["It is considered that you must be better educated, in accordance with your altered position, and that you will be alive to the importance and necessity of at once entering on that advantage.”", "자네가 바뀐 처지에 걸맞게 더 나은 교육을 받아야 한다는 것, 또 그 혜택을 즉시 누리기 시작하는 일이 얼마나 중요하고 긴요한지를 자네가 절감하게 되리라는 것이 그분의 뜻일세.\""]], [["I said I had always longed for it.", "나는 늘 그것을 갈망해 왔노라고 말했다."]], [["“Never mind what you have always longed for, Mr. Pip,”", "\"자네가 늘 무엇을 갈망해 왔는지는 상관없네, 핍 군.\""], ["he retorted; “keep to the record.", "그가 받아쳤다. \"기록된 것만 따지세."], ["If you long for it now, that’s enough.", "지금 그것을 갈망한다면, 그걸로 충분하네."], ["Am I answered that you are ready to be placed at once under some proper tutor?", "자네가 당장 적절한 가정교사 밑에 들어갈 준비가 되었다는 대답으로 알아들으면 되겠나?"], ["Is that it?”", "그런 건가?\""]], [["I stammered yes, that was it.", "나는 더듬더듬 그렇다고, 그렇다고 답했다."]], [["“Good.", "\"좋네."], ["Now, your inclinations are to be consulted. I don’t think that wise, mind, but it’s my trust.", "자, 자네의 의향도 참작해야겠지. 명심하게, 나는 그게 현명하다고 생각하진 않네만, 그것이 내가 맡은 일일세."], ["Have you ever heard of any tutor whom you would prefer to another?”", "다른 사람보다 더 마음에 드는 가정교사를 누구든 들어 본 적이 있나?\""]], [["I had never heard of any tutor but Biddy and Mr. Wopsle’s great-aunt; so, I replied in the negative.", "나는 비디와 윕슬 씨의 대고모 말고는 어떤 가정교사도 들어 본 적이 없었으므로, 없다고 대답했다."]], [["“There is a certain tutor, of whom I have some knowledge, who I think might suit the purpose,” said Mr.", "\"내가 어느 정도 아는 가정교사가 한 사람 있는데, 그가 그 목적에 맞을 것 같네.\" 재거스 씨가 말했다."], ["Jaggers. “I don’t recommend him, observe; because I never recommend anybody.", "\"유념하게, 나는 그를 추천하는 건 아닐세. 나는 누구도 추천하는 법이 없으니까."], ["The gentleman I speak of is one Mr. Matthew Pocket.”", "내가 말하는 그 신사는 매슈 포켓이라는 사람일세.\""]], [["Ah!", "아!"], ["I caught at the name directly.", "나는 곧바로 그 이름을 붙잡았다."], ["Miss Havisham’s relation.", "미스 해비셤의 친척."], ["The Matthew whom Mr. and Mrs.", "커밀라 부부가 말하던 그 매슈."], ["Camilla had spoken of. The Matthew whose place was to be at Miss Havisham’s head, when she lay dead, in her bride’s dress on the bride’s table.", "미스 해비셤이 신부 드레스를 입은 채 신부 식탁 위에 죽어 누우면, 그 머리맡에 자리하기로 되어 있다던 그 매슈."]], [["“You know the name?” said Mr.", "\"그 이름을 아나?\""], ["Jaggers, looking shrewdly at me, and then shutting up his eyes while he waited for my answer.", "재거스 씨가 나를 예리하게 살피더니, 내 대답을 기다리며 눈을 감았다."]], [["My answer was, that I had heard of the name.", "내 대답은, 그 이름을 들어 본 적이 있다는 것이었다."]], [["“Oh!”", "\"오!\""], ["said he. “You have heard of the name.", "그가 말했다. \"그 이름을 들어 봤다."], ["But the question is, what do you say of it?”", "하지만 문제는, 그것에 대해 자네가 뭐라 말하느냐일세.\""]], [["I said, or tried to say, that I was much obliged to him for his recommendation—", "나는 그가 추천해 준 것에 대해 무척 고맙다고 말했다, 아니, 말하려 했다—"]], [["“No, my young friend!”", "\"아니야, 젊은 친구!\""], ["he interrupted, shaking his great head very slowly.", "그가 큼직한 머리를 아주 천천히 가로저으며 말을 끊었다."], ["“Recollect yourself!”", "\"정신 차리게!\""]], [["Not recollecting myself, I began again that I was much obliged to him for his recommendation—", "정신을 차리지 못한 채, 나는 다시 그가 추천해 준 것에 대해 무척 고맙다고 말하기 시작했다—"]], [["“No, my young friend,”", "\"아니야, 젊은 친구.\""], ["he interrupted, shaking his head and frowning and smiling both at once,—“no, no, no; it’s very well done, but it won’t do; you are too young to fix me with it.", "그가 고개를 가로저으며, 얼굴을 찌푸리는 동시에 미소 지으며 말을 끊었다. \"아니, 아니, 아니. 아주 잘하긴 했네만, 그래서는 안 되네."], ["Recommendation is not the word, Mr. Pip.", "자네는 너무 어려서 그런 식으로 나를 옭아맬 수 없어. 추천이라는 말은 맞지 않네, 핍 군."], ["Try another.”", "다른 말을 써 보게.\""]], [["Correcting myself, I said that I was much obliged to him for his mention of Mr. Matthew Pocket—", "나는 스스로 고쳐, 그가 매슈 포켓 씨를 언급해 준 것에 대해 무척 고맙다고 말했다—"]], [["“That’s more like it!” cried Mr.", "\"그게 더 낫군!\" 재거스 씨가 외쳤다."], ["Jaggers.—And (I added), I would gladly try that gentleman.", "그래서 (나는 덧붙여) 기꺼이 그 신사에게 배워 보겠노라고 말했다."]], [["“Good.", "\"좋네."], ["You had better try him in his own house.", "그의 집에서 직접 배워 보는 편이 낫겠네."], ["The way shall be prepared for you, and you can see his son first, who is in London.", "자네를 위해 길을 마련해 두겠네. 자네는 먼저 런던에 있는 그의 아들을 만나 볼 수 있을 걸세."], ["When will you come to London?”", "언제 런던에 오겠나?\""]], [["I said (glancing at Joe, who stood looking on, motionless), that I supposed I could come directly.", "나는 (꼼짝도 않고 서서 지켜보던 조를 흘끗 보며) 당장이라도 올 수 있을 것 같다고 말했다."]], [["“First,”", "\"우선,\""], ["said Mr. Jaggers, “you should have some new clothes to come in, and they should not be working-clothes.", "재거스 씨가 말했다. \"올 때 입을 새 옷이 좀 있어야겠는데, 그건 작업복이어서는 안 되네."], ["Say this day week. You’ll want some money.", "오늘로부터 일주일 뒤라고 해 두지. 돈도 좀 필요하겠지."], ["Shall I leave you twenty guineas?”", "자네에게 이십 기니를 두고 갈까?\""]], [["He produced a long purse, with the greatest coolness, and counted them out on the table and pushed them over to me.", "그는 더없이 침착하게 긴 지갑을 꺼내, 탁자 위에 그것을 세어 놓고는 내 쪽으로 밀어 주었다."], ["This was the first time he had taken his leg from the chair.", "이때가 그가 의자에서 다리를 처음으로 내린 순간이었다."], ["He sat astride of the chair when he had pushed the money over, and sat swinging his purse and eyeing Joe.", "돈을 밀어 준 뒤 그는 의자에 걸터앉아, 지갑을 흔들거리며 조를 바라보았다."]], [["“Well, Joseph Gargery?", "\"자, 조지프 가저리?"], ["You look dumbfoundered?”", "어안이 벙벙한 모양이군?\""]], [["“I am!” said Joe, in a very decided manner.", "\"그렇소!\" 조가 아주 단호한 태도로 말했다."]], [["“It was understood that you wanted nothing for yourself, remember?”", "\"당신은 자신을 위해서는 아무것도 바라지 않는다고 양해된 걸로 아는데, 기억하시오?\""]], [["“It were understood,” said Joe.", "\"양해됐었지요.\" 조가 말했다."], ["“And it are understood.", "\"그리고 양해되고 있고요."], ["And it ever will be similar according.”", "그리고 앞으로도 늘 마찬가지로 양해될 거요.\""]], [["“But what,” said Mr.", "\"하지만 만일,\""], ["Jaggers, swinging his purse,—“what if it was in my instructions to make you a present, as compensation?”", "재거스 씨가 지갑을 흔들며 말했다. \"내 지시에 당신에게 보상으로 선물을 하라는 내용이 들어 있다면 어떻겠소?\""]], [["“As compensation what for?”", "\"무엇에 대한 보상으로요?\""], ["Joe demanded.", "조가 따져 물었다."]], [["“For the loss of his services.”", "\"이 아이의 노동력을 잃는 데 대한 보상으로 말이오.\""]], [["Joe laid his hand upon my shoulder with the touch of a woman.", "조는 여인의 손길과도 같이 부드럽게 내 어깨에 손을 얹었다."], ["I have often thought him since, like the steam-hammer that can crush a man or pat an egg-shell, in his combination of strength with gentleness.", "그 후로 나는 종종 그를, 사람을 으스러뜨릴 수도 있고 달걀 껍질을 토닥일 수도 있는 증기 해머 같다고, 그렇게 힘과 부드러움을 겸비한 사람이라고 생각해 왔다."], ["“Pip is that hearty welcome,” said Joe, “to go free with his services, to honour and fortun’, as no words can tell him.", "\"핍이 자기 노동력을 떨치고 나가 명예와 행운을 누리는 거라면, 그야 말로 다 못할 만큼 진심으로 환영하는 일이지요.\" 조가 말했다."], ["But if you think as Money can make compensation to me for the loss of the little child—what come to the forge—and ever the best of friends!—”", "\"하지만 당신이 돈이라는 게, 대장간에 찾아와 준 그 어린아이를—그리고 언제까지나 더없이 좋은 친구인 그 아이를 잃는 데 대해 내게 보상이 될 수 있다고 생각한다면—\""]], [["O dear good Joe, whom I was so ready to leave and so unthankful to, I see you again, with your muscular blacksmith’s arm before your eyes, and your broad chest heaving, and your voice dying away.", "오, 다정하고 선량한 조여, 내가 그토록 선뜻 떠나려 했고 그토록 고마워할 줄 몰랐던 조여, 나는 다시금 당신을 본다. 우락부락한 대장장이의 팔뚝으로 두 눈을 가린 채, 넓은 가슴을 들썩이며, 목소리가 잦아들던 당신을."], ["O dear good faithful tender Joe, I feel the loving tremble of your hand upon my arm, as solemnly this day as if it had been the rustle of an angel’s wing!", "오, 다정하고 선량하고 충직하며 자상한 조여, 나는 마치 천사의 날갯짓 소리이기라도 했던 듯, 오늘 이날 엄숙하게도, 내 팔에 얹힌 당신 손의 사랑 어린 떨림을 느낀다."]], [["But I encouraged Joe at the time.", "그러나 그때 나는 조를 다독였다."], ["I was lost in the mazes of my future fortunes, and could not retrace the by-paths we had trodden together.", "나는 다가올 내 행운의 미로 속에서 길을 잃은 채, 우리가 함께 밟아 온 그 샛길들을 되짚어 갈 수가 없었다."], ["I begged Joe to be comforted, for (as he said) we had ever been the best of friends, and (as I said) we ever would be so.", "나는 조에게 마음을 가라앉히라고 빌었다. (그의 말마따나) 우리는 언제나 더없이 좋은 친구였고, (내 말마따나) 앞으로도 언제까지나 그러할 테니까."], ["Joe scooped his eyes with his disengaged wrist, as if he were bent on gouging himself, but said not another word.", "조는 자기 눈을 후벼 내기라도 하려는 듯, 빈 손목으로 두 눈을 훔쳤으나, 더는 한마디도 하지 않았다."]], [["Mr. Jaggers had looked on at this, as one who recognised in Joe the village idiot, and in me his keeper.", "재거스 씨는, 조에게서는 마을의 바보를, 내게서는 그 보호자를 알아본 사람처럼 이 광경을 지켜보고 있었다."], ["When it was over, he said, weighing in his hand the purse he had ceased to swing:—", "그것이 끝나자, 그는 흔들기를 멈춘 지갑을 손에 무게를 가늠하듯 들고서 말했다."]], [["“Now, Joseph Gargery, I warn you this is your last chance.", "\"자, 조지프 가저리, 경고하건대 이것이 당신의 마지막 기회요."], ["No half measures with me.", "나한테는 어중간한 건 통하지 않소."], ["If you mean to take a present that I have it in charge to make you, speak out, and you shall have it.", "내가 당신에게 하라고 맡은 그 선물을 받을 작정이라면, 분명히 말하시오, 그러면 받게 될 거요."], ["If on the contrary you mean to say—”", "반대로 만약 당신이 말하려는 것이—\""], ["Here, to his great amazement, he was stopped by Joe’s suddenly working round him with every demonstration of a fell pugilistic purpose.", "여기서, 그가 크게 놀란 것은, 조가 갑자기 그를 향해 몸을 휘돌리며 흉맹한 권투 자세를 온몸으로 드러내는 바람에 말이 막혀 버렸기 때문이다."]], [["“Which I meantersay,”", "\"내가 말하려는 건,\""], ["cried Joe, “that if you come into my place bull-baiting and badgering me, come out!", "조가 외쳤다. \"당신이 내 집에 들어와 황소 부리듯 나를 들볶고 못살게 군다면, 나가시오!"], ["Which I meantersay as sech if you’re a man, come on!", "내 말하려는 건 즉슨, 당신이 사내라면, 덤벼 보란 말이오!"], ["Which I meantersay that what I say, I meantersay and stand or fall by!”", "내가 말하려는 건, 내가 한 말은, 내가 진심으로 하는 말이고, 그 말에 목숨을 걸겠다는 거요!\""]], [["I drew Joe away, and he immediately became placable; merely stating to me, in an obliging manner and as a polite expostulatory notice to any one whom it might happen to concern, that he were not a-going to be bull-baited and badgered in his own place. Mr.", "내가 조를 끌어내자, 그는 곧 누그러졌다. 다만 자기 집에서 황소 부리듯 들볶이고 못살게 굴림을 당하지는 않겠노라고, 친절한 투로, 또 그에 해당될 만한 누구에게든 정중히 항의하여 일러두는 식으로, 내게 밝혔을 뿐이다."], ["Jaggers had risen when Joe demonstrated, and had backed near the door.", "조가 그렇게 나서자 재거스 씨는 일어나 문 가까이로 물러나 있었다."], ["Without evincing any inclination to come in again, he there delivered his valedictory remarks.", "다시 안으로 들어올 기색은 조금도 보이지 않은 채, 그는 거기서 작별의 말을 전했다."], ["They were these.", "그 말은 이러했다."]], [["“Well, Mr. Pip, I think the sooner you leave here—as you are to be a gentleman—the better.", "\"자, 핍 군, 자네가—신사가 될 몸이니—여기를 빨리 떠날수록 좋을 듯하네."], ["Let it stand for this day week, and you shall receive my printed address in the meantime.", "오늘로부터 일주일 뒤로 해 두지. 그동안 자네에게 내 인쇄된 주소를 보내겠네."], ["You can take a hackney-coach at the stage-coach office in London, and come straight to me.", "런던의 역마차 사무소에서 삯마차를 잡아타고 곧장 내게 오면 되네."], ["Understand, that I express no opinion, one way or other, on the trust I undertake.", "알아 두게, 나는 내가 떠맡은 이 임무에 대해 이렇다 저렇다 어떤 의견도 표하지 않네."], ["I am paid for undertaking it, and I do so. Now, understand that, finally.", "나는 그 일을 떠맡는 대가로 보수를 받고, 그래서 그 일을 하는 것이네. 자, 그 점을 명심하게."], ["Understand that!”", "그것을 명심해!\""]], [["He was throwing his finger at both of us, and I think would have gone on, but for his seeming to think Joe dangerous, and going off.", "그는 우리 둘을 향해 손가락을 내던지고 있었고, 더 말을 이어 갈 것 같았으나, 조가 위험하다고 여겼는지 그대로 떠나가 버렸다."]], [["Something came into my head which induced me to run after him, as he was going down to the Jolly Bargemen, where he had left a hired carriage.", "문득 머릿속에 떠오른 무언가에 이끌려, 나는 그가 빌린 마차를 세워 둔 명랑한 뱃사공 주막 쪽으로 내려가는 그를 뒤쫓아 달려갔다."]], [["“I beg your pardon, Mr. Jaggers.”", "\"실례합니다, 재거스 씨.\""]], [["“Halloa!”", "\"이런!\""], ["said he, facing round, “what’s the matter?”", "그가 홱 돌아서며 말했다. \"무슨 일인가?\""]], [["“I wish to be quite right, Mr. Jaggers, and to keep to your directions; so I thought I had better ask.", "\"저는 모든 걸 제대로 하고 싶고, 선생의 지시를 따르고 싶어서요. 그래서 여쭤 보는 게 낫겠다 싶었습니다."], ["Would there be any objection to my taking leave of any one I know, about here, before I go away?”", "제가 떠나기 전에 이 근방에서 아는 사람에게 작별 인사를 하는 데 무슨 문제가 있을까요?\""]], [["“No,” said he, looking as if he hardly understood me.", "\"없네.\" 그가 내 말을 거의 알아듣지 못하겠다는 표정으로 말했다."]], [["“I don’t mean in the village only, but up town?”", "\"이 마을에서만이 아니라, 읍내에서도 말입니다만?\""]], [["“No,”", "\"없네.\""], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."], ["“No objection.”", "\"문제 될 것 없네.\""]], [["I thanked him and ran home again, and there I found that Joe had already locked the front door and vacated the state parlour, and was seated by the kitchen fire with a hand on each knee, gazing intently at the burning coals.", "나는 그에게 고맙다고 하고 다시 집으로 달려갔는데, 가 보니 조는 벌써 현관문을 잠그고 고급 응접실을 비운 채, 부엌 난롯가에 양손을 무릎에 하나씩 얹고 앉아 타오르는 석탄을 골똘히 바라보고 있었다."], ["I too sat down before the fire and gazed at the coals, and nothing was said for a long time.", "나도 난롯불 앞에 앉아 석탄을 바라보았고, 한참 동안 아무 말도 오가지 않았다."]], [["My sister was in her cushioned chair in her corner, and Biddy sat at her needle-work before the fire, and Joe sat next Biddy, and I sat next Joe in the corner opposite my sister.", "누나는 한구석에 놓인 쿠션을 댄 의자에 앉아 있었고, 비디는 난롯불 앞에서 바느질을 하며 앉아 있었고, 조는 비디 옆에, 나는 누나 맞은편 구석에서 조 옆에 앉아 있었다."], ["The more I looked into the glowing coals, the more incapable I became of looking at Joe; the longer the silence lasted, the more unable I felt to speak.", "이글거리는 석탄을 들여다보면 볼수록 나는 조를 바라보기가 더 어려워졌고, 침묵이 길어지면 길어질수록 입을 떼기가 더 힘들어졌다."]], [["At length I got out, “Joe, have you told Biddy?”", "마침내 나는 가까스로 입을 뗐다. \"조, 비디한테 말했어요?\""]], [["“No, Pip,”", "\"아니, 핍.\""], ["returned Joe, still looking at the fire, and holding his knees tight, as if he had private information that they intended to make off somewhere, “which I left it to yourself, Pip.”", "조가 여전히 난롯불을 바라보며, 마치 무릎이 어딘가로 달아날 작정이라는 은밀한 정보라도 들은 듯 두 무릎을 꽉 붙든 채 대답했다. \"그건 네게 맡겨 뒀단다, 핍.\""]], [["“I would rather you told, Joe.”", "\"조가 말해 주면 좋겠어요.\""]], [["“Pip’s a gentleman of fortun’ then,”", "\"그럼 핍이 행운을 거머쥔 신사가 됐다는구나.\""], ["said Joe, “and God bless him in it!”", "조가 말했다. \"그 일에 하느님의 축복이 있기를!\""]], [["Biddy dropped her work, and looked at me.", "비디가 바느질거리를 내려놓고 나를 바라보았다."], ["Joe held his knees and looked at me.", "조는 무릎을 붙든 채 나를 바라보았다."], ["I looked at both of them.", "나는 두 사람을 바라보았다."], ["After a pause, they both heartily congratulated me; but there was a certain touch of sadness in their congratulations that I rather resented.", "잠시 뒤 두 사람은 진심으로 나를 축하해 주었다. 그러나 그 축하 속에는 어딘가 슬픔의 기색이 묻어 있어, 나는 그것이 다소 마음에 거슬렸다."]], [["I took it upon myself to impress Biddy (and through Biddy, Joe) with the grave obligation I considered my friends under, to know nothing and say nothing about the maker of my fortune.", "나는 나서서, 내 행운을 만들어 준 사람에 대해 아무것도 알려 하지 말고 아무 말도 하지 말아야 한다는, 내가 내 벗들이 마땅히 져야 한다고 여긴 그 무거운 의무를 비디에게(그리고 비디를 통해 조에게) 새겨 주려 했다."], ["It would all come out in good time, I observed, and in the meanwhile nothing was to be said, save that I had come into great expectations from a mysterious patron.", "때가 되면 다 밝혀질 거라고, 그동안에는 내가 어느 알 수 없는 후원자에게서 막대한 유산에 대한 기대를 갖게 되었다는 것 말고는 아무 말도 해서는 안 된다고 나는 일렀다."], ["Biddy nodded her head thoughtfully at the fire as she took up her work again, and said she would be very particular; and Joe, still detaining his knees, said, “Ay, ay, I’ll be ekervally partickler, Pip;”", "비디는 다시 바느질거리를 집어 들며 난롯불을 향해 생각에 잠긴 듯 고개를 끄덕이고는, 매우 조심하겠노라고 말했다. 그리고 조는, 여전히 무릎을 붙든 채 \"그래, 그래, 나도 똑같이 조심하마, 핍.\" 하고 말했다."], ["and then they congratulated me again, and went on to express so much wonder at the notion of my being a gentleman that I didn’t half like it.", "그러더니 두 사람은 다시 나를 축하해 주었고, 내가 신사가 된다는 발상에 어찌나 놀라움을 거듭 드러내던지, 나는 그것이 그다지 달갑지 않았다."]], [["Infinite pains were then taken by Biddy to convey to my sister some idea of what had happened.", "이어 비디는 무슨 일이 일어났는지를 누나에게 어떻게든 이해시키려고 무진 애를 썼다."], ["To the best of my belief, those efforts entirely failed.", "내가 믿기로는, 그 노력은 완전히 실패로 돌아갔다."], ["She laughed and nodded her head a great many times, and even repeated after Biddy, the words “Pip” and “Property.”", "누나는 웃으며 고개를 여러 번 끄덕였고, 심지어 비디를 따라 \"핍\"이니 \"재산\"이니 하는 말을 되뇌기까지 했다."], ["But I doubt if they had more meaning in them than an election cry, and I cannot suggest a darker picture of her state of mind.", "그러나 그 말들에 선거 구호 이상의 의미가 담겨 있었을지는 의문이며, 나는 누나의 정신 상태를 이보다 더 어둡게 그려 보일 길이 없다."]], [["I never could have believed it without experience, but as Joe and Biddy became more at their cheerful ease again, I became quite gloomy.", "겪어 보지 않았다면 나는 결코 믿지 못했을 일이지만, 조와 비디가 다시금 즐거이 마음 편해질수록 나는 점점 우울해졌다."], ["Dissatisfied with my fortune, of course I could not be; but it is possible that I may have been, without quite knowing it, dissatisfied with myself.", "물론 내 행운에 불만이 있을 리는 없었다. 그러나 나도 모르는 사이에, 어쩌면 나는 나 자신에게 불만을 품고 있었는지도 모른다."]], [["Anyhow, I sat with my elbow on my knee and my face upon my hand, looking into the fire, as those two talked about my going away, and about what they should do without me, and all that.", "어쨌든 나는 무릎에 팔꿈치를 괴고 손에 얼굴을 받친 채, 그 두 사람이 내가 떠나는 일이며, 내가 없으면 자기들은 어찌해야 하나 하는 따위의 이야기를 나누는 동안 난롯불을 들여다보고 앉아 있었다."], ["And whenever I caught one of them looking at me, though never so pleasantly (and they often looked at me,—particularly Biddy), I felt offended: as if they were expressing some mistrust of me.", "그리고 두 사람 중 누구든 나를 바라보는 것을 알아챌 때마다, 그 눈빛이 아무리 다정하더라도 (그들은 자주 나를 바라보았는데, 특히 비디가 그랬다), 나는 마치 그들이 나를 못 미더워하는 마음을 드러내기라도 하는 양 기분이 상했다."], ["Though Heaven knows they never did by word or sign.", "하늘이 알다시피 그들은 말로든 기색으로든 결코 그런 적이 없었는데도 말이다."]], [["At those times I would get up and look out at the door; for our kitchen door opened at once upon the night, and stood open on summer evenings to air the room.", "그럴 때면 나는 일어나 문밖을 내다보곤 했다. 우리 부엌문은 바로 바깥의 밤으로 통해 있어, 여름 저녁이면 방을 환기하려고 활짝 열어 두었으니까."], ["The very stars to which I then raised my eyes, I am afraid I took to be but poor and humble stars for glittering on the rustic objects among which I had passed my life.", "그때 내가 눈을 들어 올려다본 바로 그 별들조차도, 내가 평생을 보내 온 그 촌스러운 것들 위에서 반짝이는 데에나 어울리는, 초라하고 변변찮은 별들이라고 여겼던 것 같아 두렵다."]], [["“Saturday night,”", "\"토요일 밤이로구나.\""], ["said I, when we sat at our supper of bread and cheese and beer.", "우리가 빵과 치즈와 맥주로 저녁을 들 때 내가 말했다."], ["“Five more days, and then the day before the day!", "\"닷새만 더 지나면, 그러면 바로 그날의 전날이네!"], ["They’ll soon go.”", "금세 지나가겠지.\""]], [["“Yes, Pip,”", "\"그래, 핍.\""], ["observed Joe, whose voice sounded hollow in his beer-mug.", "조가 말했는데, 그 목소리는 맥주잔 속에서 텅 빈 듯 울렸다."], ["“They’ll soon go.”", "\"금세 지나가겠지.\""]], [["“Soon, soon go,”", "\"금세, 금세 지나가겠죠.\""], ["said Biddy.", "비디가 말했다."]], [["“I have been thinking, Joe, that when I go down town on Monday, and order my new clothes, I shall tell the tailor that I’ll come and put them on there, or that I’ll have them sent to Mr. Pumblechook’s.", "\"내가 생각해 봤는데요, 조, 월요일에 읍내에 내려가 새 옷을 주문할 때, 재단사한테 가서 거기서 옷을 입겠다고 하든지, 아니면 펌블추크 씨 댁으로 보내 달라고 할까 봐요."], ["It would be very disagreeable to be stared at by all the people here.”", "여기 사람들이 다 빤히 쳐다보면 무척 거북할 테니까요.\""]], [["“Mr. and Mrs. Hubble might like to see you in your new gen-teel figure too, Pip,”", "\"허블 부부도 자네의 새 점잖은 모습을 보고 싶어 할지 모르네, 핍.\""], ["said Joe, industriously cutting his bread, with his cheese on it, in the palm of his left hand, and glancing at my untasted supper as if he thought of the time when we used to compare slices.", "조가 왼손바닥 위에서 빵에 치즈를 얹어 부지런히 자르며, 마치 우리가 빵 조각 크기를 견주던 시절을 떠올리기라도 하듯 손도 대지 않은 내 저녁을 흘끗 보며 말했다. \"윕슬도 그럴 테고."], ["“So might Wopsle. And the Jolly Bargemen might take it as a compliment.”", "그리고 명랑한 뱃사공 주막 사람들도 그걸 영광으로 여길지 모르지.\""]], [["“That’s just what I don’t want, Joe.", "\"바로 그게 제가 싫은 거예요, 조."], ["They would make such a business of it,—such a coarse and common business,—that I couldn’t bear myself.”", "그 사람들은 그걸 가지고 어찌나 야단법석을 떨지—어찌나 거칠고 천박한 소동을 벌일지—저는 견딜 수가 없을 거예요.\""]], [["“Ah, that indeed, Pip!”", "\"아, 그건 정말 그렇지, 핍!\""], ["said Joe.", "조가 말했다."], ["“If you couldn’t abear yourself—”", "\"네 스스로를 견딜 수 없을 정도라면야—\""]], [["Biddy asked me here, as she sat holding my sister’s plate, “Have you thought about when you’ll show yourself to Mr. Gargery, and your sister and me?", "이때 비디가 누나의 접시를 든 채 앉아서 내게 물었다. \"가저리 씨와 누님과 저한테는 언제 모습을 보여 줄지 생각해 봤어요?"], ["You will show yourself to us; won’t you?”", "우리한테는 모습을 보여 줄 거죠, 그렇죠?\""]], [["“Biddy,”", "\"비디,\""], ["I returned with some resentment, “you are so exceedingly quick that it’s difficult to keep up with you.”", "나는 다소 발끈하여 받아쳤다. \"당신은 어찌나 빠른지 따라가기가 어렵군요.\""]], [["(“She always were quick,”", "(\"저 애는 늘 빨랐지.\""], ["observed Joe.)", "조가 한마디 했다.)"]], [["“If you had waited another moment, Biddy, you would have heard me say that I shall bring my clothes here in a bundle one evening,—most likely on the evening before I go away.”", "\"잠깐만 더 기다렸더라면, 비디, 내가 어느 날 저녁에—아마도 떠나기 전날 저녁에—옷을 보따리에 싸서 이리 가져오겠다고 말하는 걸 들었을 텐데.\""]], [["Biddy said no more.", "비디는 더는 말이 없었다."], ["Handsomely forgiving her, I soon exchanged an affectionate good night with her and Joe, and went up to bed.", "나는 너그러이 그녀를 용서하고, 곧 그녀와 조에게 다정하게 잘 자라는 인사를 건넨 뒤 잠자리로 올라갔다."], ["When I got into my little room, I sat down and took a long look at it, as a mean little room that I should soon be parted from and raised above, for ever.", "내 작은 방에 들어서자, 나는 앉아서 그 방을, 머지않아 영영 작별하고 그 위로 올라서게 될 누추하고 작은 방을 오래도록 바라보았다."], ["It was furnished with fresh young remembrances too, and even at the same moment I fell into much the same confused division of mind between it and the better rooms to which I was going, as I had been in so often between the forge and Miss Havisham’s, and Biddy and Estella.", "그 방에는 싱그럽고 어린 추억들 또한 갖추어져 있었으니, 바로 그 순간에도 나는, 대장간과 미스 해비셤의 집 사이에서, 또 비디와 에스텔라 사이에서 그토록 자주 빠지곤 했던 것과 꼭 같은, 그 방과 내가 가게 될 더 나은 방들 사이의 어수선하게 갈라진 마음에 사로잡혔다."]], [["The sun had been shining brightly all day on the roof of my attic, and the room was warm.", "하루 종일 다락방 지붕 위로 해가 밝게 내리쬔 터라 방은 따뜻했다."], ["As I put the window open and stood looking out, I saw Joe come slowly forth at the dark door, below, and take a turn or two in the air; and then I saw Biddy come, and bring him a pipe and light it for him.", "창문을 열고 서서 밖을 내다보니, 아래쪽 어두운 문간에서 조가 천천히 나와 바깥바람을 쐬며 한두 바퀴 거니는 것이 보였다. 그러더니 비디가 나와 그에게 파이프를 가져다주고 불을 붙여 주는 것이 보였다."], ["He never smoked so late, and it seemed to hint to me that he wanted comforting, for some reason or other.", "그는 그렇게 밤늦게 담배를 피우는 법이 없었으니, 그것은 내게 그가 무슨 까닭에선지 위로받고 싶어 한다는 것을 넌지시 알려 주는 듯했다."]], [["He presently stood at the door immediately beneath me, smoking his pipe, and Biddy stood there too, quietly talking to him, and I knew that they talked of me, for I heard my name mentioned in an endearing tone by both of them more than once.", "이내 그는 바로 내 아래쪽 문간에 서서 파이프를 피웠고, 비디도 거기 서서 그와 조용히 이야기를 나누었는데, 나는 두 사람이 내 이야기를 하고 있음을 알았다. 두 사람 다 한 번 이상 다정한 어조로 내 이름을 입에 올리는 것을 들었기 때문이다."], ["I would not have listened for more, if I could have heard more; so I drew away from the window, and sat down in my one chair by the bedside, feeling it very sorrowful and strange that this first night of my bright fortunes should be the loneliest I had ever known.", "설령 더 들을 수 있었더라도, 나는 더 엿듣지는 않았을 것이다. 그래서 나는 창가에서 물러나, 침대맡의 하나뿐인 의자에 앉아, 내 빛나는 행운의 이 첫날 밤이 내가 알아 온 그 어느 밤보다 가장 외로운 밤이 되었다는 것이 무척 서글프고 기이하다고 느꼈다."]], [["Looking towards the open window, I saw light wreaths from Joe’s pipe floating there, and I fancied it was like a blessing from Joe,—not obtruded on me or paraded before me, but pervading the air we shared together.", "열린 창문 쪽으로 눈을 돌리니, 조의 파이프에서 피어오른 옅은 연기 고리들이 거기 떠도는 것이 보였고, 나는 그것이 마치 조가 보내는 축복 같다고 여겼다. 내게 강요하거나 내 앞에 과시하는 것이 아니라, 우리가 함께 나누던 그 공기 속에 스며드는 축복 말이다."], ["I put my light out, and crept into bed; and it was an uneasy bed now, and I never slept the old sound sleep in it any more.", "나는 등불을 끄고 침대 속으로 기어들었다. 그러나 이제 그것은 불편한 잠자리였고, 나는 다시는 그 침대에서 예전의 그 깊은 잠을 자지 못했다."]], [["# Chapter XIX.", "# Chapter XIX."]], [["Morning made a considerable difference in my general prospect of Life, and brightened it so much that it scarcely seemed the same.", "아침이 오자 내 인생 전반에 대한 전망은 사뭇 달라졌고, 어찌나 환해졌던지 도무지 같은 인생으로 보이지 않을 정도였다."], ["What lay heaviest on my mind was, the consideration that six days intervened between me and the day of departure; for I could not divest myself of a misgiving that something might happen to London in the meanwhile, and that, when I got there, it would be either greatly deteriorated or clean gone.", "내 마음을 가장 무겁게 짓누른 것은, 떠나는 날까지 엿새가 남았다는 생각이었다. 그동안 런던에 무슨 일이 생겨, 막상 내가 도착했을 때 그곳이 크게 쇠락해 있거나 아예 깨끗이 사라져 버렸을지도 모른다는 불안을 떨쳐 낼 수 없었기 때문이다."]], [["Joe and Biddy were very sympathetic and pleasant when I spoke of our approaching separation; but they only referred to it when I did.", "다가오는 우리의 이별을 입에 올렸을 때, 조와 비디는 더없이 다정하고 따뜻하게 굴었으나, 내가 먼저 그 이야기를 꺼낼 때에만 그 일을 언급했다."], ["After breakfast, Joe brought out my indentures from the press in the best parlour, and we put them in the fire, and I felt that I was free.", "아침을 먹은 뒤, 조는 제일 좋은 응접실의 찬장에서 내 도제 계약서를 꺼내 왔고, 우리는 그것을 불 속에 넣었으며, 나는 내가 자유로워졌음을 느꼈다."], ["With all the novelty of my emancipation on me, I went to church with Joe, and thought perhaps the clergyman wouldn’t have read that about the rich man and the kingdom of Heaven, if he had known all.", "해방의 온갖 새로움에 사로잡힌 채, 나는 조와 함께 교회에 갔고, 목사님이 모든 사정을 알았더라면 부자와 천국에 관한 그 구절은 읽지 않았을지도 모른다고 생각했다."]], [["After our early dinner, I strolled out alone, purposing to finish off the marshes at once, and get them done with.", "이른 점심을 먹은 뒤, 나는 습지를 단숨에 다 돌아보고 끝장을 내 버리리라 작정하고 홀로 어슬렁어슬렁 나섰다."], ["As I passed the church, I felt (as I had felt during service in the morning) a sublime compassion for the poor creatures who were destined to go there, Sunday after Sunday, all their lives through, and to lie obscurely at last among the low green mounds.", "교회를 지날 때, 나는 (그날 아침 예배 때 느꼈던 것처럼) 일요일이면 일요일마다, 평생토록 그곳에 가도록 운명 지워진 채 끝내 나지막한 푸른 봉분 사이에 이름 없이 묻힐 가엾은 이들을 향해 숭고한 연민을 느꼈다."], ["I promised myself that I would do something for them one of these days, and formed a plan in outline for bestowing a dinner of roast-beef and plum-pudding, a pint of ale, and a gallon of condescension, upon everybody in the village.", "나는 언젠가 그들을 위해 무언가를 해 주리라 스스로 다짐했고, 마을 사람 모두에게 구운 쇠고기와 자두 푸딩 한 끼, 맥주 한 파인트, 그리고 너그러운 은혜 한 갤런을 베푸는 계획의 윤곽을 짜 보았다."]], [["If I had often thought before, with something allied to shame, of my companionship with the fugitive whom I had once seen limping among those graves, what were my thoughts on this Sunday, when the place recalled the wretch, ragged and shivering, with his felon iron and badge!", "언젠가 저 무덤들 사이를 절뚝거리며 가던 그 탈주자와 한패였다는 사실을, 전에도 나는 부끄러움에 가까운 감정을 품고서 곧잘 떠올리곤 했거니와, 그 장소가 죄수의 쇠고랑과 표지를 단 채 누더기를 걸치고 부들부들 떨던 그 가련한 자를 다시 불러일으킨 이 일요일에 내 심정은 어떠했겠는가!"], ["My comfort was, that it happened a long time ago, and that he had doubtless been transported a long way off, and that he was dead to me, and might be veritably dead into the bargain.", "그나마 위안이 된 것은, 그 일이 오래전에 일어났다는 것, 그자는 틀림없이 멀리멀리 유형을 떠났으리라는 것, 그리고 그자는 나에게는 죽은 사람이나 마찬가지이며, 어쩌면 정말로 죽어 버렸을지도 모른다는 것이었다."]], [["No more low, wet grounds, no more dikes and sluices, no more of these grazing cattle,—though they seemed, in their dull manner, to wear a more respectful air now, and to face round, in order that they might stare as long as possible at the possessor of such great expectations,—farewell, monotonous acquaintances of my childhood, henceforth I was for London and greatness; not for smith’s work in general, and for you!", "이제 더는 나지막하고 축축한 땅도, 더는 도랑과 수문도, 더는 풀을 뜯는 저 소 떼도 없으리라 — 비록 그 소들은 그 굼뜬 태도로나마 이제는 한결 공손한 기색을 띠고, 그토록 큰 유산을 거머쥔 사람을 되도록 오래 바라보려는 듯 고개를 돌려 마주 보는 듯했지만 — 잘 있거라, 내 어린 시절의 단조로운 벗들이여, 이제부터 나는 런던과 위대함을 향해 가는 몸이지, 대장간 일 따위와 너희를 위한 몸이 아니었다!"], ["I made my exultant way to the old Battery, and, lying down there to consider the question whether Miss Havisham intended me for Estella, fell asleep.", "나는 의기양양하게 옛 포대로 발걸음을 옮겼고, 거기 드러누워 해비셤 양이 나를 에스텔라의 짝으로 점지한 것인가 하는 문제를 곰곰 따져 보다가 그만 잠이 들고 말았다."]], [["When I awoke, I was much surprised to find Joe sitting beside me, smoking his pipe.", "잠에서 깨어났을 때, 조가 파이프를 물고 내 곁에 앉아 있는 것을 보고 나는 적잖이 놀랐다."], ["He greeted me with a cheerful smile on my opening my eyes, and said,—", "내가 눈을 뜨자 그는 유쾌한 미소로 나를 맞이하며 말했다."]], [["“As being the last time, Pip, I thought I’d foller.”", "\"마지막이다 싶어서, 핍, 따라와 봤다.\""]], [["“And Joe, I am very glad you did so.”", "\"조, 따라와 줘서 정말 기뻐요.\""]], [["“Thankee, Pip.”", "\"고맙다, 핍.\""]], [["“You may be sure, dear Joe,”", "\"분명히 말해 두지만, 조.\""], ["I went on, after we had shaken hands, “that I shall never forget you.”", "우리가 악수를 나눈 뒤 나는 말을 이었다. \"나는 형을 절대 잊지 않을 거예요.\""]], [["“No, no, Pip!” said Joe, in a comfortable tone, “I’m sure of that.", "\"아냐, 아냐, 핍!\" 조가 편안한 어조로 말했다."], ["Ay, ay, old chap!", "\"그건 내가 확신한단다."], ["Bless you, it were only necessary to get it well round in a man’s mind, to be certain on it.", "그럼, 그렇고말고, 이 사람아! 그저 사람 마음속에 잘 새겨 두기만 하면 확실해지는 거지."], ["But it took a bit of time to get it well round, the change come so oncommon plump; didn’t it?”", "그런데 마음속에 제대로 새기기까지가 좀 시간이 걸렸어, 변화가 어찌나 느닷없이 통째로 닥쳐왔던지 말이다. 안 그러냐?\""]], [["Somehow, I was not best pleased with Joe’s being so mightily secure of me.", "어쩐 일인지, 조가 나를 그토록 굳게 믿어 의심치 않는 것이 나는 그리 달갑지 않았다."], ["I should have liked him to have betrayed emotion, or to have said, “It does you credit, Pip,” or something of that sort.", "차라리 그가 감정을 드러내거나, \"그건 다 네가 잘난 덕이지, 핍\" 같은 말이라도 해 주었으면 싶었다."], ["Therefore, I made no remark on Joe’s first head; merely saying as to his second, that the tidings had indeed come suddenly, but that I had always wanted to be a gentleman, and had often and often speculated on what I would do, if I were one.", "그래서 나는 조의 첫 번째 이야기에는 아무 말도 하지 않았고, 두 번째 이야기에 대해서만, 소식이 정말 갑작스레 오긴 했으나 나는 늘 신사가 되고 싶었으며, 만약 신사가 된다면 무엇을 할까 하고 이따금 곰곰 궁리하곤 했다고 말했다."]], [["“Have you though?”", "\"그랬단 말이냐?\""], ["said Joe. “Astonishing!”", "조가 말했다. \"놀랍구나!\""]], [["“It’s a pity now, Joe,”", "\"이제 와서 안타까운 건요, 조.\""], ["said I, “that you did not get on a little more, when we had our lessons here; isn’t it?”", "내가 말했다. \"여기서 우리가 공부할 때 형이 좀 더 진전을 보지 못한 거예요. 안 그래요?\""]], [["“Well, I don’t know,”", "\"글쎄, 모르겠다.\""], ["returned Joe.", "조가 대답했다."], ["“I’m so awful dull. I’m only master of my own trade.", "\"내가 워낙 지독히도 둔하잖니. 난 그저 내 일에나 능할 뿐이지."], ["It were always a pity as I was so awful dull; but it’s no more of a pity now, than it was—this day twelvemonth—don’t you see?”", "내가 그토록 지독히 둔한 게 늘 안타깝긴 했지만, 그건 지금이라고 더 안타까울 것도 없어, 일 년 전 오늘이나 매한가지지. 안 그러냐?\""]], [["What I had meant was, that when I came into my property and was able to do something for Joe, it would have been much more agreeable if he had been better qualified for a rise in station.", "내가 말하려던 것은, 내가 재산을 물려받아 조를 위해 무언가를 해 줄 수 있게 되었을 때, 그가 신분 상승에 좀 더 어울리는 자질을 갖추었더라면 한결 보기 좋았으리라는 뜻이었다."], ["He was so perfectly innocent of my meaning, however, that I thought I would mention it to Biddy in preference.", "그러나 그는 내 속뜻을 너무도 천진하게 알아채지 못했으므로, 나는 차라리 비디에게 그 이야기를 꺼내는 편이 낫겠다고 생각했다."]], [["So, when we had walked home and had had tea, I took Biddy into our little garden by the side of the lane, and, after throwing out in a general way for the elevation of her spirits, that I should never forget her, said I had a favour to ask of her.", "그리하여 우리가 집까지 걸어와 차를 마신 뒤, 나는 비디를 데리고 오솔길 옆 우리 집 작은 정원으로 나갔고, 그녀의 기운을 북돋아 주려고 두루뭉술하게나마 너를 결코 잊지 않겠다는 말을 던진 다음, 그녀에게 부탁할 일이 하나 있다고 말했다."]], [["“And it is, Biddy,”", "\"그건 말이야, 비디.\""], ["said I, “that you will not omit any opportunity of helping Joe on, a little.”", "내가 말했다. \"조를 조금이라도 발전시킬 기회가 생기면 하나도 놓치지 말아 달라는 거야.\""]], [["“How helping him on?” asked Biddy, with a steady sort of glance.", "\"어떻게 발전시키라는 거예요?\" 비디가 차분한 눈길로 물었다."]], [["“Well!", "\"그게!"], ["Joe is a dear good fellow,—in fact, I think he is the dearest fellow that ever lived,—but he is rather backward in some things.", "조는 더없이 착하고 좋은 사람이야 — 사실 나는 그렇게 좋은 사람은 세상에 없다고 생각해 — 하지만 어떤 면에서는 좀 뒤처져 있잖아."], ["For instance, Biddy, in his learning and his manners.”", "이를테면, 비디, 학식이나 예절에서 말이야.\""]], [["Although I was looking at Biddy as I spoke, and although she opened her eyes very wide when I had spoken, she did not look at me.", "말하는 동안 나는 비디를 바라보고 있었고, 또 내 말이 끝나자 그녀가 눈을 휘둥그렇게 떴음에도, 그녀는 나를 보지 않았다."]], [["“O, his manners!", "\"아, 예절요!"], ["won’t his manners do then?”", "그럼 조의 예절로는 안 된다는 건가요?\""], ["asked Biddy, plucking a black-currant leaf.", "비디가 까치밥나무 잎을 하나 뜯으며 물었다."]], [["“My dear Biddy, they do very well here—”", "\"비디야, 여기서는 그 예절로도 충분히 괜찮지만 —\""]], [["“O! they do very well here?”", "\"아! 여기서는 충분히 괜찮다고요?\""], ["interrupted Biddy, looking closely at the leaf in her hand.", "비디가 손에 든 잎을 가까이 들여다보며 말을 끊었다."]], [["“Hear me out,—but if I were to remove Joe into a higher sphere, as I shall hope to remove him when I fully come into my property, they would hardly do him justice.”", "\"끝까지 들어 봐 — 하지만 만약 내가 조를 더 높은 자리로 옮긴다면, 내가 재산을 온전히 물려받았을 때 그렇게 해 줄 작정인데, 그땐 그 예절로는 조가 제대로 대접받기 어려울 거야.\""]], [["“And don’t you think he knows that?”", "\"그걸 조가 모를 것 같아요?\""], ["asked Biddy.", "비디가 물었다."]], [["It was such a very provoking question (for it had never in the most distant manner occurred to me), that I said, snappishly,—", "그것은 어찌나 약을 올리는 질문이었던지(그런 생각은 단 한 번도, 아주 어렴풋이도 내 머리에 떠오른 적이 없었으니까), 나는 퉁명스럽게 내뱉었다."]], [["“Biddy, what do you mean?”", "\"비디, 그게 무슨 소리야?\""]], [["Biddy, having rubbed the leaf to pieces between her hands,—and the smell of a black-currant bush has ever since recalled to me that evening in the little garden by the side of the lane,—said, “Have you never considered that he may be proud?”", "비디는 두 손바닥 사이에서 그 잎을 비벼 부스러뜨리고는 — 그 후로 까치밥나무 덤불의 냄새는 늘 내게 오솔길 옆 작은 정원에서의 그 저녁을 떠올리게 한다 — 이렇게 말했다. \"조가 자존심이 강한 사람일 수도 있다는 생각은 안 해 보셨어요?\""]], [["“Proud?” I repeated, with disdainful emphasis.", "\"자존심?\" 나는 경멸하듯 힘주어 되뇌었다."]], [["“O! there are many kinds of pride,”", "\"아! 자존심에도 여러 종류가 있잖아요.\""], ["said Biddy, looking full at me and shaking her head; “pride is not all of one kind—”", "비디가 나를 똑바로 바라보며 고개를 저었다. \"자존심이라고 다 같은 게 아니에요 —\""]], [["“Well?", "\"그래서?"], ["What are you stopping for?” said I.", "왜 말을 멈추는 거야?\" 내가 말했다."]], [["“Not all of one kind,”", "\"다 같은 게 아니에요.\""], ["resumed Biddy.", "비디가 말을 이었다."], ["“He may be too proud to let any one take him out of a place that he is competent to fill, and fills well and with respect.", "\"조는 자기가 능히 채울 수 있고, 잘 채우고 있고, 또 존경받으며 채우고 있는 자리에서 누군가 자기를 끌어내려는 걸 못 견딜 만큼 자존심이 강할 수도 있어요."], ["To tell you the truth, I think he is; though it sounds bold in me to say so, for you must know him far better than I do.”", "사실대로 말하면, 저는 조가 그렇다고 생각해요. 제가 이렇게 말하는 게 당돌하게 들릴지 몰라도요. 도련님이 저보다 조를 훨씬 잘 알 테니까요.\""]], [["“Now, Biddy,”", "\"이봐, 비디.\""], ["said I, “I am very sorry to see this in you.", "내가 말했다. \"네게서 이런 모습을 보게 되어 정말 유감이야."], ["I did not expect to see this in you.", "네게서 이런 걸 보리라곤 생각지 못했어."], ["You are envious, Biddy, and grudging. You are dissatisfied on account of my rise in fortune, and you can’t help showing it.”", "너는 시샘을 하고 있어, 비디, 그리고 못마땅해하고 있어. 너는 내 운수가 트인 것에 불만을 품고서, 그걸 드러내지 않고는 못 배기는 거야.\""]], [["“If you have the heart to think so,”", "\"정말 그렇게 생각할 마음이 있으시다면,\""], ["returned Biddy, “say so.", "비디가 대답했다. \"그렇게 말씀하세요."], ["Say so over and over again, if you have the heart to think so.”", "정말 그렇게 생각할 마음이 있으시다면, 몇 번이고 거듭 그렇게 말씀하세요.\""]], [["“If you have the heart to be so, you mean, Biddy,”", "\"그렇게 굴 마음이 있는 건 너겠지, 비디.\""], ["said I, in a virtuous and superior tone; “don’t put it off upon me.", "나는 점잖고도 우월한 어조로 말했다. \"내게 떠넘기지 마."], ["I am very sorry to see it, and it’s a—it’s a bad side of human nature.", "이런 모습을 보게 되어 나는 정말 유감이고, 이건 — 이건 인간 본성의 못된 일면이야."], ["I did intend to ask you to use any little opportunities you might have after I was gone, of improving dear Joe.", "나는 내가 떠난 뒤에 네게 어떤 사소한 기회라도 생기거든 그걸 살려서 착한 조를 좀 더 나아지게 해 달라고 부탁하려고 했어."], ["But after this I ask you nothing.", "하지만 이렇게 된 이상 네게 아무것도 부탁하지 않겠어."], ["I am extremely sorry to see this in you, Biddy,”", "네게서 이런 걸 보게 되어 정말이지 유감이야, 비디.\""], ["I repeated.", "나는 거듭 말했다."], ["“It’s a—it’s a bad side of human nature.”", "\"이건 — 이건 인간 본성의 못된 일면이야.\""]], [["“Whether you scold me or approve of me,”", "\"저를 꾸짖으시든 칭찬하시든,\""], ["returned poor Biddy, “you may equally depend upon my trying to do all that lies in my power, here, at all times.", "가엾은 비디가 대답했다. \"제가 여기서, 언제나, 제 힘이 닿는 데까지 다 하려고 애쓰리라는 것만은 똑같이 믿으셔도 돼요."], ["And whatever opinion you take away of me, shall make no difference in my remembrance of you.", "그리고 도련님이 저에 대해 어떤 생각을 품고 떠나시든, 그것이 제가 도련님을 기억하는 데에는 아무런 차이도 만들지 못할 거예요."], ["Yet a gentleman should not be unjust neither,”", "그래도 신사라면 부당하게 굴어서는 안 되는 법이지요.\""], ["said Biddy, turning away her head.", "비디는 고개를 돌리며 말했다."]], [["I again warmly repeated that it was a bad side of human nature (in which sentiment, waiving its application, I have since seen reason to think I was right), and I walked down the little path away from Biddy, and Biddy went into the house, and I went out at the garden gate and took a dejected stroll until supper-time; again feeling it very sorrowful and strange that this, the second night of my bright fortunes, should be as lonely and unsatisfactory as the first.", "나는 그것이 인간 본성의 못된 일면이라고 다시금 열을 내어 거듭 말했고(그 적용 대상은 차치하더라도, 그 정서 자체에 관해서는 그 후로 내가 옳았다고 여길 만한 이유를 발견했다), 비디에게서 멀어지며 그 작은 오솔길을 걸어 내려갔다. 비디는 집 안으로 들어갔고, 나는 정원 문을 나서 저녁 시간까지 풀 죽은 채 어슬렁어슬렁 거닐었다. 내 빛나는 행운의 둘째 날 밤마저 첫째 날 밤만큼이나 외롭고 허전하다는 사실이 또다시 몹시도 서글프고 기이하게 느껴졌다."]], [["But, morning once more brightened my view, and I extended my clemency to Biddy, and we dropped the subject.", "그러나 아침은 다시 한번 내 마음을 환하게 밝혀 주었고, 나는 비디에게 자비를 베풀었으며, 우리는 그 이야기를 더 꺼내지 않았다."], ["Putting on the best clothes I had, I went into town as early as I could hope to find the shops open, and presented myself before Mr.", "가진 옷 가운데 가장 좋은 것을 차려입고, 나는 가게들이 문을 열었으리라 기대할 만한 이른 시각에 읍내로 나가, 재단사 트랩 씨 앞에 나섰다."], ["Trabb, the tailor, who was having his breakfast in the parlour behind his shop, and who did not think it worth his while to come out to me, but called me in to him.", "그는 가게 뒤편 거실에서 아침을 먹는 중이었는데, 굳이 나를 맞으러 나올 것도 없다는 듯이 나를 안으로 불러들였다."]], [["“Well!” said Mr.", "\"이런!\""], ["Trabb, in a hail-fellow-well-met kind of way.", "트랩 씨가 친구라도 만난 양 허물없이 말했다."], ["“How are you, and what can I do for you?”", "\"잘 지내나, 무슨 일로 왔지?\""]], [["Mr. Trabb had sliced his hot roll into three feather-beds, and was slipping butter in between the blankets, and covering it up.", "트랩 씨는 따뜻한 빵을 세 겹 깃털 이불처럼 저며 놓고, 그 이불 사이사이에 버터를 밀어 넣어 덮고 있었다."], ["He was a prosperous old bachelor, and his open window looked into a prosperous little garden and orchard, and there was a prosperous iron safe let into the wall at the side of his fireplace, and I did not doubt that heaps of his prosperity were put away in it in bags.", "그는 형편이 넉넉한 늙은 독신남이었고, 활짝 열린 그의 창은 풍성한 작은 정원과 과수원으로 트여 있었으며, 벽난로 옆 벽에는 든든한 무쇠 금고가 박혀 있었으니, 그 안에 그의 풍요가 자루째 수북이 쟁여져 있으리라는 것을 나는 의심치 않았다."]], [["“Mr. Trabb,”", "\"트랩 씨.\""], ["said I, “it’s an unpleasant thing to have to mention, because it looks like boasting; but I have come into a handsome property.”", "내가 말했다. \"꺼내기 거북한 이야기예요, 자랑처럼 들릴 테니까요. 하지만 저는 상당한 재산을 물려받게 되었어요.\""]], [["A change passed over Mr. Trabb.", "트랩 씨에게 변화가 일었다."], ["He forgot the butter in bed, got up from the bedside, and wiped his fingers on the tablecloth, exclaiming, “Lord bless my soul!”", "그는 이불 속의 버터를 까맣게 잊은 채 식탁 곁에서 일어나, 식탁보에 손가락을 닦으며 외쳤다. \"맙소사, 이런 경사가!\""]], [["“I am going up to my guardian in London,”", "\"저는 런던에 계신 제 후견인에게 올라갈 참인데요.\""], ["said I, casually drawing some guineas out of my pocket and looking at them; “and I want a fashionable suit of clothes to go in.", "나는 주머니에서 기니 몇 닢을 무심한 듯 꺼내 들여다보며 말했다. \"거기 가서 입을 유행하는 정장 한 벌이 필요해요. 옷값은요\""], ["I wish to pay for them,” I added—otherwise I thought he might only pretend to make them, “with ready money.”", "— 그러지 않으면 그가 옷을 만드는 시늉만 할지도 모른다고 생각해서 한마디 덧붙였다 — \"현금으로 치를게요.\""]], [["“My dear sir,” said Mr.", "\"이봐요, 도련님.\""], ["Trabb, as he respectfully bent his body, opened his arms, and took the liberty of touching me on the outside of each elbow, “don’t hurt me by mentioning that.", "트랩 씨가 공손히 허리를 굽히고 두 팔을 벌리며, 외람되게도 내 양쪽 팔꿈치 바깥을 슬쩍 짚고서 말했다. \"그런 말씀으로 절 무안하게 마세요."], ["May I venture to congratulate you?", "감히 축하의 말씀을 올려도 될는지요?"], ["Would you do me the favour of stepping into the shop?”", "황송하오나 가게 안으로 드시겠습니까?\""]], [["Mr. Trabb’s boy was the most audacious boy in all that country-side.", "트랩 씨네 점원 아이는 그 일대에서 가장 뻔뻔한 녀석이었다."], ["When I had entered he was sweeping the shop, and he had sweetened his labours by sweeping over me.", "내가 들어섰을 때 그는 가게 바닥을 쓸고 있었는데, 나를 쓸어 넘기는 시늉으로 제 노동에 단맛을 더하고 있었다."], ["He was still sweeping when I came out into the shop with Mr. Trabb, and he knocked the broom against all possible corners and obstacles, to express (as I understood it) equality with any blacksmith, alive or dead.", "내가 트랩 씨와 함께 가게로 나왔을 때에도 녀석은 여전히 바닥을 쓸고 있었으며, 빗자루를 온갖 모서리며 장애물에 부딪쳐 가며, (내가 이해하기로는) 죽었든 살았든 어느 대장장이와도 대등하다는 뜻을 드러내고 있었다."]], [["“Hold that noise,” said Mr.", "\"그 소란 그쳐라.\""], ["Trabb, with the greatest sternness, “or I’ll knock your head off!—Do me the favour to be seated, sir.", "트랩 씨가 더없이 매서운 어조로 말했다. \"안 그러면 머리통을 날려 버릴 테다! — 자, 앉으시지요, 도련님."], ["Now, this,” said Mr.", "자, 이것은요.\""], ["Trabb, taking down a roll of cloth, and tiding it out in a flowing manner over the counter, preparatory to getting his hand under it to show the gloss, “is a very sweet article.", "트랩 씨는 옷감 한 필을 끌어내려 계산대 위에 좌르르 펼쳐 놓고는, 그 밑으로 손을 넣어 윤기를 보여 주려고 채비하며 말했다. \"아주 근사한 물건입니다."], ["I can recommend it for your purpose, sir, because it really is extra super.", "도련님의 용도에 추천드릴 만합니다, 정말이지 특상품이니까요."], ["But you shall see some others.", "하지만 다른 것들도 보셔야지요."], ["Give me Number Four, you!”", "4번 가져와, 너!\""], ["(To the boy, and with a dreadfully severe stare; foreseeing the danger of that miscreant’s brushing me with it, or making some other sign of familiarity.)", "(점원 아이에게 한 말이었는데, 그 못된 녀석이 옷감으로 나를 슬쩍 건드리거나 다른 허물없는 짓을 할 위험을 미리 내다보고서, 더없이 무섭게 노려보며 한 말이었다.)"]], [["Mr. Trabb never removed his stern eye from the boy until he had deposited number four on the counter and was at a safe distance again.", "트랩 씨는 그 아이가 4번 옷감을 계산대에 내려놓고 다시 안전한 거리로 물러설 때까지 매서운 눈길을 한순간도 떼지 않았다."], ["Then he commanded him to bring number five, and number eight.", "그러고 나서 그는 5번과 8번을 가져오라 명했다."], ["“And let me have none of your tricks here,” said Mr.", "\"여기서 잔재주 부릴 생각일랑 마라.\" 트랩 씨가 말했다."], ["Trabb, “or you shall repent it, you young scoundrel, the longest day you have to live.”", "\"안 그랬다간 살아 있는 동안 두고두고 후회하게 될 거다, 이 어린 망나니 같으니.\""]], [["Mr. Trabb then bent over number four, and in a sort of deferential confidence recommended it to me as a light article for summer wear, an article much in vogue among the nobility and gentry, an article that it would ever be an honour to him to reflect upon a distinguished fellow-townsman’s (if he might claim me for a fellow-townsman) having worn.", "이윽고 트랩 씨는 4번 위로 몸을 굽히고, 일종의 공손한 은밀함을 담아, 그것을 여름용으로 가벼운 물건이며 귀족과 신사 계층에서 크게 유행하는 물건이고, 명망 있는 동향 사람이(나를 동향 사람으로 칠 수 있다면 말이지만) 그것을 입었다고 떠올리는 일이 자기로서는 두고두고 영광이 될 만한 물건이라며 내게 권했다."], ["“Are you bringing numbers five and eight, you vagabond,”", "\"5번과 8번은 가져오는 거냐, 이 부랑아 녀석아.\""], ["said Mr. Trabb to the boy after that, “or shall I kick you out of the shop and bring them myself?”", "그러고서 트랩 씨가 아이에게 말했다. \"아니면 내가 너를 가게 밖으로 걷어차 내고 직접 가져올까?\""]], [["I selected the materials for a suit, with the assistance of Mr. Trabb’s judgment, and re-entered the parlour to be measured.", "나는 트랩 씨의 안목에 도움을 받아 정장 한 벌의 옷감을 골랐고, 치수를 재러 다시 거실로 들어갔다."], ["For although Mr. Trabb had my measure already, and had previously been quite contented with it, he said apologetically that it “wouldn’t do under existing circumstances, sir,—wouldn’t do at all.” So, Mr.", "트랩 씨에게는 이미 내 치수가 있었고, 전에는 그것으로 충분히 만족했으면서도, 그는 미안한 듯 그 치수로는 \"지금 상황에서는 안 된답니다, 도련님 — 도무지 안 돼요\"라고 말했다."], ["Trabb measured and calculated me in the parlour, as if I were an estate and he the finest species of surveyor, and gave himself such a world of trouble that I felt that no suit of clothes could possibly remunerate him for his pains. When he had at last done and had appointed to send the articles to Mr.", "그리하여 트랩 씨는 마치 내가 토지이고 자기는 최고급 측량 기사라도 되는 양 거실에서 내 치수를 재고 계산하며, 어찌나 엄청난 수고를 들이던지, 옷 한 벌 값으로는 도저히 그 노고를 보상할 수 없으리라는 생각이 들 정도였다. 마침내 일을 끝낸 그는 목요일 저녁에 펌블추크 씨 댁으로 물건을 보내기로 정하고, 거실 문고리에 손을 얹은 채 말했다."], ["Pumblechook’s on the Thursday evening, he said, with his hand upon the parlour lock, “I know, sir, that London gentlemen cannot be expected to patronise local work, as a rule; but if you would give me a turn now and then in the quality of a townsman, I should greatly esteem it.", "\"압니다, 도련님, 런던 신사분들께서 으레 시골 솜씨를 봐주시리라 기대할 수야 없겠지요. 하지만 동향 사람 자격으로 이따금 일감을 주신다면, 저로서는 그것을 크나큰 영광으로 여기겠습니다. 안녕히 가십시오, 도련님, 정말 감사합니다."], ["Good-morning, sir, much obliged.—Door!”", "— 문 열어라!\""]], [["The last word was flung at the boy, who had not the least notion what it meant.", "마지막 말은 점원 아이를 향해 내던져진 것이었는데, 녀석은 그게 무슨 뜻인지 도통 알지 못했다."], ["But I saw him collapse as his master rubbed me out with his hands, and my first decided experience of the stupendous power of money was, that it had morally laid upon his back Trabb’s boy.", "그러나 주인이 두 손으로 나를 떠받들어 배웅하는 동안 녀석이 풀이 죽는 것을 나는 보았으며, 돈의 어마어마한 위력에 대한 내 첫 번째 또렷한 경험은, 그것이 트랩네 점원 아이를 도덕적으로 납작 엎드리게 만들었다는 것이었다."]], [["After this memorable event, I went to the hatter’s, and the bootmaker’s, and the hosier’s, and felt rather like Mother Hubbard’s dog whose outfit required the services of so many trades.", "이 기억할 만한 사건 뒤에, 나는 모자 가게며 구두 가게며 양말 가게에 갔는데, 그 채비에 그토록 많은 직종의 손길이 필요하다니, 나 자신이 마치 머더 허버드의 개라도 된 듯한 기분이 들었다."], ["I also went to the coach-office and took my place for seven o’clock on Saturday morning.", "나는 또 역마차 사무소에 가서 토요일 아침 일곱 시 자리를 예매했다."], ["It was not necessary to explain everywhere that I had come into a handsome property; but whenever I said anything to that effect, it followed that the officiating tradesman ceased to have his attention diverted through the window by the High Street, and concentrated his mind upon me.", "가는 곳마다 내가 상당한 재산을 물려받게 되었노라 설명할 필요까지는 없었으나, 그런 취지의 말을 한마디라도 흘리면, 어김없이 그 일을 맡은 상인은 큰길 쪽 창밖으로 흩어지던 주의를 거두고 온 마음을 내게로 쏟았다."], ["When I had ordered everything I wanted, I directed my steps towards Pumblechook’s, and, as I approached that gentleman’s place of business, I saw him standing at his door.", "원하는 것을 죄다 주문하고 나서, 나는 펌블추크 씨 댁으로 발걸음을 옮겼는데, 그 신사의 사업장에 다가가면서 보니 그가 문간에 서 있었다."]], [["He was waiting for me with great impatience.", "그는 몹시 안달이 난 채 나를 기다리고 있었다."], ["He had been out early with the chaise-cart, and had called at the forge and heard the news.", "그는 아침 일찍 경마차를 몰고 나가 대장간에 들러 그 소식을 들었던 것이다."], ["He had prepared a collation for me in the Barnwell parlour, and he too ordered his shopman to “come out of the gangway” as my sacred person passed.", "그는 반웰 거실에 나를 위해 가벼운 잔칫상을 차려 놓았고, 내 거룩한 몸이 지나갈 때 그 역시 점원에게 \"통로에서 비켜라\" 하고 명했다."]], [["“My dear friend,” said Mr.", "\"이보게, 내 친애하는 벗.\""], ["Pumblechook, taking me by both hands, when he and I and the collation were alone, “I give you joy of your good fortune.", "펌블추크 씨는 그와 나, 그리고 잔칫상만 남게 되자 내 두 손을 맞잡으며 말했다. \"자네의 행운을 진심으로 축하하네."], ["Well deserved, well deserved!”", "마땅히 누릴 만하지, 마땅히 누릴 만하고말고!\""]], [["This was coming to the point, and I thought it a sensible way of expressing himself.", "이것은 핵심을 찌르는 말이었고, 나는 그가 제법 사리에 맞게 표현했다고 생각했다."]], [["“To think,” said Mr.", "\"생각해 보면 말일세.\""], ["Pumblechook, after snorting admiration at me for some moments, “that I should have been the humble instrument of leading up to this, is a proud reward.”", "펌블추크 씨가 한동안 콧김을 내뿜으며 나를 흠모하듯 바라보고 나서 말했다. \"이 일에 이르도록 길을 터 준 변변찮은 도구가 바로 나였다는 것은, 자랑스러운 보답이 아닐 수 없네.\""]], [["I begged Mr. Pumblechook to remember that nothing was to be ever said or hinted, on that point.", "나는 펌블추크 씨에게, 그 점에 관해서는 절대로 무슨 말이나 암시를 입에 올려서는 안 된다는 것을 명심해 달라고 청했다."]], [["“My dear young friend,”", "\"이보게, 내 친애하는 젊은 벗.\""], ["said Mr. Pumblechook; “if you will allow me to call you so—”", "펌블추크 씨가 말했다. \"내가 자네를 그렇게 불러도 된다면 말일세 —\""]], [["I murmured “Certainly,”", "나는 \"물론이지요\""], ["and Mr. Pumblechook took me by both hands again, and communicated a movement to his waistcoat, which had an emotional appearance, though it was rather low down, “My dear young friend, rely upon my doing my little all in your absence, by keeping the fact before the mind of Joseph.—Joseph!”", "하고 나직이 중얼거렸고, 펌블추크 씨는 다시 내 두 손을 맞잡고서, 비록 꽤 아래쪽이긴 했으나 감격에 겨운 듯한 몸짓을 제 조끼에 전했다. \"친애하는 젊은 벗, 내가 없는 동안에 조지프의 마음속에 그 사실을 늘 일깨워 줌으로써 나의 작은 정성을 다하리라는 것을 믿어 주게."], ["said Mr. Pumblechook, in the way of a compassionate adjuration.", "— 조지프!\" 펌블추크 씨가 가엾어하며 타이르듯 말했다."], ["“Joseph!!", "\"조지프!!"], ["Joseph!!!”", "조지프!!!\""], ["Thereupon he shook his head and tapped it, expressing his sense of deficiency in Joseph.", "그러고서 그는 고개를 가로젓고 손가락으로 톡톡 두드리며, 조지프의 모자람에 대한 자신의 소견을 드러냈다."]], [["“But my dear young friend,”", "\"하지만 친애하는 젊은 벗.\""], ["said Mr. Pumblechook, “you must be hungry, you must be exhausted.", "펌블추크 씨가 말했다. \"시장할 테고, 기진맥진할 테지. 앉게나."], ["Be seated. Here is a chicken had round from the Boar, here is a tongue had round from the Boar, here’s one or two little things had round from the Boar, that I hope you may not despise.", "여기 보어 여관에서 가져온 닭고기가 있고, 보어에서 가져온 혀고기가 있고, 보어에서 가져온 자질구레한 것이 한두 가지 더 있는데, 부디 업신여기지 말아 주게."], ["But do I,” said Mr.", "그런데 내가 말일세.\""], ["Pumblechook, getting up again the moment after he had sat down, “see afore me, him as I ever sported with in his times of happy infancy?", "펌블추크 씨는 앉자마자 곧바로 다시 일어서며 말했다. \"내 앞에 있는 사람이, 행복한 어린 시절에 내가 함께 어울려 놀던 바로 그 아이가 맞는가?"], ["And may I—may I—?”", "그러니 한번 — 한번 — 그래도 되겠나?\""]], [["This May I, meant might he shake hands?", "이 \"한번 그래도 되겠나\"는 악수를 해도 되느냐는 뜻이었다."], ["I consented, and he was fervent, and then sat down again.", "나는 동의했고, 그는 열렬했으며, 그러고 나서 다시 자리에 앉았다."]], [["“Here is wine,”", "\"여기 포도주가 있네.\""], ["said Mr. Pumblechook.", "펌블추크 씨가 말했다."], ["“Let us drink, Thanks to Fortune, and may she ever pick out her favourites with equal judgment!", "\"마시세, 행운의 여신께 감사를. 그리고 그분이 언제까지나 한결같은 안목으로 자신의 총아를 골라내시기를! 그렇지만 나는 도무지.\""], ["And yet I cannot,” said Mr.", "펌블추크 씨가 다시 일어서며 말했다."], ["Pumblechook, getting up again, “see afore me One—and likewise drink to One—without again expressing—May I—may I—?”", "\"내 앞에 한 사람을 두고 — 또한 그 한 사람을 위해 잔을 들면서 — 다시금 청하지 않고는 견딜 수가 없네. 한번 — 한번 — 그래도 되겠나?\""]], [["I said he might, and he shook hands with me again, and emptied his glass and turned it upside down.", "나는 그래도 된다고 말했고, 그는 다시 나와 악수한 다음, 잔을 비우고 거꾸로 뒤집었다."], ["I did the same; and if I had turned myself upside down before drinking, the wine could not have gone more direct to my head.", "나도 그렇게 했는데, 만일 내가 마시기 전에 나 자신을 거꾸로 뒤집었더라도, 포도주가 이보다 더 곧장 내 머리로 올라갈 수는 없었을 것이다."]], [["Mr. Pumblechook helped me to the liver wing, and to the best slice of tongue (none of those out-of-the-way No Thoroughfares of Pork now), and took, comparatively speaking, no care of himself at all. “Ah!", "펌블추크 씨는 내게 간이 붙은 날개살과 가장 좋은 혀고기 한 점을 권하면서(이제는 그 외진 막다른 골목 같은 돼지고기 따위는 없었다), 자신은 비교적 말하자면 전혀 챙기지 않았다. \"아!"], ["poultry, poultry!", "가금아, 가금아!"], ["You little thought,” said Mr.", "너는 미처 생각지도 못했겠지.\""], ["Pumblechook, apostrophising the fowl in the dish, “when you was a young fledgling, what was in store for you.", "펌블추크 씨가 접시 위의 새에게 말을 건네며 말했다. \"어린 솜털 새끼였을 적에, 네게 어떤 운명이 마련되어 있는지 말이다."], ["You little thought you was to be refreshment beneath this humble roof for one as—Call it a weakness, if you will,” said Mr.", "너는 이 누추한 지붕 아래에서, 그러니까 — 약점이라 부르고 싶으면 그러게나.\" 펌블추크 씨가 다시 일어서며 말했다."], ["Pumblechook, getting up again, “but may I? may I—?”", "\"하지만 한번 — 한번 — 그래도 되겠나?\""]], [["It began to be unnecessary to repeat the form of saying he might, so he did it at once.", "그래도 되느냐는 그 절차를 되풀이해 묻는 것조차 불필요해지기 시작했으므로, 그는 곧장 그렇게 했다."], ["How he ever did it so often without wounding himself with my knife, I don’t know.", "그가 어떻게 그토록 자주, 내 나이프에 다치지도 않고 그 짓을 해냈는지 나는 모르겠다."]], [["“And your sister,”", "\"그리고 자네 누님 말일세.\""], ["he resumed, after a little steady eating, “which had the honour of bringing you up by hand!", "그는 한동안 꾸준히 먹고 나서 말을 이었다. \"손수 자네를 길러 내는 영광을 누리셨던 그분 말이야!"], ["It’s a sad picter, to reflect that she’s no longer equal to fully understanding the honour. May—”", "이제는 그분이 그 영광을 온전히 헤아릴 만한 처지가 못 된다는 걸 생각하면, 참으로 가슴 아픈 광경이지. 한번 —\""]], [["I saw he was about to come at me again, and I stopped him.", "나는 그가 또다시 내게 달려들려는 것을 보고 그를 막았다."]], [["“We’ll drink her health,” said I.", "\"누님의 건강을 위해 마셔요.\" 내가 말했다."]], [["“Ah!”", "\"아!\""], ["cried Mr. Pumblechook, leaning back in his chair, quite flaccid with admiration, “that’s the way you know ’em, sir!”", "펌블추크 씨가 의자에 등을 기대며, 흠모에 겨워 완전히 맥이 풀린 채 외쳤다. \"바로 그래서 다들 그분들을 알아본다니까요, 도련님!\""], ["(I don’t know who Sir was, but he certainly was not I, and there was no third person present); “that’s the way you know the noble-minded, sir!", "(그 도련님이 누구인지 나는 모르겠으나, 분명 나는 아니었고, 그 자리에 제삼자도 없었다.) \"바로 그래서 다들 고결한 분들을 알아보는 거예요, 도련님!"], ["Ever forgiving and ever affable.", "늘 너그럽고 늘 상냥하시지. 이건.\""], ["It might,” said the servile Pumblechook, putting down his untasted glass in a hurry and getting up again, “to a common person, have the appearance of repeating—but may I—?”", "비굴한 펌블추크 씨가 입도 대지 않은 잔을 황급히 내려놓고 다시 일어서며 말했다. \"범상한 사람에게는, 같은 짓을 되풀이하는 것처럼 보일지도 모르겠으나 — 그래도 한번 — 그래도 되겠나?\""]], [["When he had done it, he resumed his seat and drank to my sister.", "그렇게 하고 나서 그는 자리로 돌아가 내 누나의 건강을 위해 잔을 들었다."], ["“Let us never be blind,” said Mr.", "\"우리가 결코 눈멀어서는 안 되겠지.\""], ["Pumblechook, “to her faults of temper, but it is to be hoped she meant well.”", "펌블추크 씨가 말했다. \"그분의 성마른 결함에는 말일세. 그래도 그분 본심은 좋았으리라 믿어야지.\""]], [["At about this time, I began to observe that he was getting flushed in the face; as to myself, I felt all face, steeped in wine and smarting.", "이쯤 되자 나는 그의 얼굴이 점점 벌게지는 것을 알아채기 시작했다. 나로 말하자면, 온몸이 다 얼굴이 된 듯, 포도주에 흠뻑 젖어 화끈거렸다."]], [["I mentioned to Mr. Pumblechook that I wished to have my new clothes sent to his house, and he was ecstatic on my so distinguishing him.", "나는 펌블추크 씨에게 새 옷을 그의 집으로 보내게 하고 싶다고 말했고, 그는 내가 자신을 그렇게 각별히 대접하자 황홀해했다."], ["I mentioned my reason for desiring to avoid observation in the village, and he lauded it to the skies.", "나는 마을에서 남의 눈에 띄는 것을 피하고 싶은 까닭을 이야기했고, 그는 그것을 하늘에 닿도록 칭송했다."], ["There was nobody but himself, he intimated, worthy of my confidence, and—in short, might he?", "그는 자기 말고는 내 신뢰를 받을 만한 사람이 아무도 없다는 뜻을 넌지시 비치고는 — 요컨대, 한번 그래도 되겠느냐는 것이었다."], ["Then he asked me tenderly if I remembered our boyish games at sums, and how we had gone together to have me bound apprentice, and, in effect, how he had ever been my favourite fancy and my chosen friend?", "그러고 나서 그는 다정스레, 우리가 어린 시절 셈하기 놀이를 하던 것을 기억하느냐고, 또 나를 도제로 들이러 함께 갔던 것을, 그리고 사실상 자기야말로 늘 내가 가장 좋아하던 사람이자 내가 가려 뽑은 벗이 아니었느냐고 물었다."], ["If I had taken ten times as many glasses of wine as I had, I should have known that he never had stood in that relation towards me, and should in my heart of hearts have repudiated the idea.", "설령 내가 마신 것보다 열 배나 많은 포도주를 마셨더라도, 나는 그가 결코 나에게 그런 관계에 있은 적이 없음을 알았을 테고, 마음 깊은 곳에서는 그런 생각을 부인했을 것이다."], ["Yet for all that, I remember feeling convinced that I had been much mistaken in him, and that he was a sensible, practical, good-hearted prime fellow.", "그럼에도 불구하고, 나는 내가 그를 크게 오해해 왔으며, 그가 분별 있고 실속 있고 마음씨 고운 더없이 훌륭한 사람이라고 확신하게 되었던 것을 기억한다."]], [["By degrees he fell to reposing such great confidence in me, as to ask my advice in reference to his own affairs.", "그는 차츰차츰 나에게 큰 신뢰를 두기에 이르러, 자기 자신의 일에 관해서까지 내 조언을 구했다."], ["He mentioned that there was an opportunity for a great amalgamation and monopoly of the corn and seed trade on those premises, if enlarged, such as had never occurred before in that or any other neighbourhood.", "그는 만약 이 사업장을 넓히기만 한다면, 이 동네에서든 다른 어느 동네에서든 일찍이 없었던, 곡물과 종자 거래의 대대적인 합병과 독점의 기회가 있노라고 말했다."], ["What alone was wanting to the realisation of a vast fortune, he considered to be More Capital.", "막대한 재산의 실현에 오로지 모자란 것이 무엇인가 하면, 그는 그것을 더 많은 자본이라 여겼다."], ["Those were the two little words, more capital.", "바로 그 두 마디, 더 많은 자본 말이다."], ["Now it appeared to him (Pumblechook) that if that capital were got into the business, through a sleeping partner, sir,—which sleeping partner would have nothing to do but walk in, by self or deputy, whenever he pleased, and examine the books,—and walk in twice a year and take his profits away in his pocket, to the tune of fifty per cent,—it appeared to him that that might be an opening for a young gentleman of spirit combined with property, which would be worthy of his attention.", "그런데 그가(펌블추크 씨가) 보기에, 만약 그 자본을 익명의 동업자를 통해 이 사업에 끌어들인다면, 도련님, 그 익명의 동업자는 그저 마음 내킬 때마다 몸소든 대리인을 통해서든 걸어 들어와 장부를 검사하고 — 일 년에 두 번 걸어 들어와 오할의 이문을 제 주머니에 챙겨 가기만 하면 되니 — 그가 보기에 이것은 기개와 재산을 겸비한 젊은 신사에게 눈여겨볼 만한 좋은 기회가 될 수 있겠다는 것이었다. 그런데 내 생각은 어떠냐고?"], ["But what did I think? He had great confidence in my opinion, and what did I think?", "그는 내 의견을 크게 신뢰하니, 내 생각은 어떠냐고? 나는 내 의견을 내놓았다."], ["I gave it as my opinion.", "\"좀 두고 봅시다!\""], ["“Wait a bit!” The united vastness and distinctness of this view so struck him, that he no longer asked if he might shake hands with me, but said he really must,—and did.", "이 견해의 광대함과 명료함이 한데 어우러져 어찌나 그를 사로잡았던지, 그는 이제 나와 악수해도 되느냐고 묻지도 않고, 정말로 해야겠다고 말하며 — 그렇게 했다."]], [["We drank all the wine, and Mr. Pumblechook pledged himself over and over again to keep Joseph up to the mark (I don’t know what mark), and to render me efficient and constant service (I don’t know what service).", "우리는 포도주를 다 마셨고, 펌블추크 씨는 조지프가 본분을 지키도록 다잡고(무슨 본분인지 나는 모르겠다), 또 내게 효과적이고 한결같은 봉사를 다하겠노라(무슨 봉사인지 나는 모르겠다) 거듭거듭 맹세했다."], ["He also made known to me for the first time in my life, and certainly after having kept his secret wonderfully well, that he had always said of me, “That boy is no common boy, and mark me, his fortun’ will be no common fortun’.”", "그는 또 내 평생 처음으로, 그리고 분명 제 비밀을 놀랍도록 잘 지켜 오다가, 자기가 늘 나를 두고 \"저 아이는 범상한 아이가 아니다. 내 말을 명심해라, 저 아이의 운수도 범상한 운수가 아닐 게다\" 하고 말해 왔노라 털어놓았다."], ["He said with a tearful smile that it was a singular thing to think of now, and I said so too.", "그는 눈물 어린 미소를 지으며, 이제 와 생각하니 참으로 묘한 일이라고 말했고, 나도 그렇다고 말했다."], ["Finally, I went out into the air, with a dim perception that there was something unwonted in the conduct of the sunshine, and found that I had slumberously got to the turnpike without having taken any account of the road.", "마침내 나는 햇살의 행태에 뭔가 예사롭지 않은 데가 있다는 어렴풋한 느낌과 함께 바깥 공기 속으로 나왔는데, 정신을 차려 보니 길에 대해서는 아무 의식도 없이 어느새 비몽사몽 통행세 징수소까지 와 있었다."]], [["There, I was roused by Mr. Pumblechook’s hailing me.", "거기서 나는 펌블추크 씨가 부르는 소리에 정신이 들었다."], ["He was a long way down the sunny street, and was making expressive gestures for me to stop. I stopped, and he came up breathless.", "그는 볕이 잘 드는 거리 저 아래쪽에서, 멈추라며 요란하게 손짓을 하고 있었다. 나는 걸음을 멈추었고, 그가 숨을 헐떡이며 다가왔다."]], [["“No, my dear friend,” said he, when he had recovered wind for speech.", "\"안 되지, 이보게, 내 친애하는 벗.\" 그는 말할 만큼 숨을 돌리고 나서 말했다."], ["“Not if I can help it.", "\"내가 막을 수 있는 한은 안 되지."], ["This occasion shall not entirely pass without that affability on your part.—May I, as an old friend and well-wisher?", "이 자리를 자네의 그 상냥함 없이 그냥 보낼 수야 없잖나. — 한번, 오랜 벗이자 자네를 아끼는 사람으로서, 한번 그래도 되겠나?"], ["May I?”", "그래도 되겠나?\""]], [["We shook hands for the hundredth time at least, and he ordered a young carter out of my way with the greatest indignation.", "우리는 적어도 백 번째로 악수를 나누었고, 그는 더없이 분개한 태도로 어느 젊은 짐마차꾼더러 내 앞에서 비키라고 호령했다."], ["Then, he blessed me and stood waving his hand to me until I had passed the crook in the road; and then I turned into a field and had a long nap under a hedge before I pursued my way home.", "그러고서 그는 내게 축복을 빌며, 내가 길모퉁이를 돌아 사라질 때까지 손을 흔들며 서 있었다. 그리고 나는 어느 밭으로 접어들어 산울타리 아래에서 한참 낮잠을 잔 다음에야 집으로 가는 길을 마저 갔다."]], [["I had scant luggage to take with me to London, for little of the little I possessed was adapted to my new station.", "런던으로 가져갈 짐은 변변찮았으니, 내가 가진 그 얼마 안 되는 것 가운데서도 새로운 신분에 어울리는 것은 거의 없었기 때문이다."], ["But I began packing that same afternoon, and wildly packed up things that I knew I should want next morning, in a fiction that there was not a moment to be lost.", "그러나 나는 바로 그날 오후부터 짐을 꾸리기 시작했고, 한순간도 허비할 수 없다는 허구 속에서, 이튿날 아침에야 필요할 줄 뻔히 아는 물건들까지 정신없이 챙겨 넣었다."]], [["So, Tuesday, Wednesday, and Thursday, passed; and on Friday morning I went to Mr. Pumblechook’s, to put on my new clothes and pay my visit to Miss Havisham. Mr.", "그렇게 화요일과 수요일과 목요일이 지나갔고, 금요일 아침에 나는 새 옷을 차려입고 해비셤 양을 방문하러 펌블추크 씨 댁으로 갔다."], ["Pumblechook’s own room was given up to me to dress in, and was decorated with clean towels expressly for the event.", "옷을 갈아입으라고 펌블추크 씨 자신의 방이 내게 내어졌는데, 그 행사를 위해 일부러 깨끗한 수건들로 꾸며져 있었다."], ["My clothes were rather a disappointment, of course. Probably every new and eagerly expected garment ever put on since clothes came in, fell a trifle short of the wearer’s expectation.", "새 옷은 물론 다소 실망스러웠다. 아마 옷이라는 것이 생겨난 이래로 새로 지어 입은, 잔뜩 고대하던 옷치고 입는 이의 기대에 조금이나마 못 미치지 않은 것은 하나도 없으리라."], ["But after I had had my new suit on some half an hour, and had gone through an immensity of posturing with Mr. Pumblechook’s very limited dressing-glass, in the futile endeavour to see my legs, it seemed to fit me better.", "그러나 새 정장을 입은 지 반 시간쯤 지나, 다리를 보려는 헛된 시도로 펌블추크 씨의 몹시도 작은 거울 앞에서 끝없이 갖은 자세를 취해 본 뒤에는, 한결 잘 맞는 듯이 보였다."], ["It being market morning at a neighbouring town some ten miles off, Mr.", "십 마일쯤 떨어진 이웃 읍내에 장이 서는 아침이라, 펌블추크 씨는 집에 없었다."], ["Pumblechook was not at home. I had not told him exactly when I meant to leave, and was not likely to shake hands with him again before departing.", "나는 그에게 정확히 언제 떠날 작정인지 말하지 않았으니, 출발하기 전에 그와 다시 악수할 일도 없을 듯했다. 이 모든 것이 마땅히 그러해야 할 대로였다."], ["This was all as it should be, and I went out in my new array, fearfully ashamed of having to pass the shopman, and suspicious after all that I was at a personal disadvantage, something like Joe’s in his Sunday suit.", "나는 새 차림으로 길을 나섰는데, 점원 앞을 지나야 한다는 것이 끔찍이도 부끄러웠고, 결국 내가 외양에서 불리한 처지에 있는 게 아닌가, 일요일 정장을 입은 조의 모습과 어딘가 비슷한 게 아닌가 하는 의심마저 들었다."]], [["I went circuitously to Miss Havisham’s by all the back ways, and rang at the bell constrainedly, on account of the stiff long fingers of my gloves.", "나는 온갖 뒷길로 빙 둘러 해비셤 양의 집으로 갔고, 장갑의 빳빳하고 긴 손가락 탓에 어색하게 초인종을 울렸다."], ["Sarah Pocket came to the gate, and positively reeled back when she saw me so changed; her walnut-shell countenance likewise turned from brown to green and yellow.", "세라 포켓이 문으로 나왔는데, 그토록 달라진 나를 보고는 정말이지 휘청 뒤로 물러섰다. 호두 껍데기 같은 그녀의 얼굴빛도 갈색에서 초록과 노랑으로 변했다."]], [["“You?”", "\"너?\""], ["said she. “You?", "그녀가 말했다. \"너라고?"], ["Good gracious!", "어머나 세상에!"], ["What do you want?”", "무슨 일이냐?\""]], [["“I am going to London, Miss Pocket,”", "\"저는 런던에 갑니다, 포켓 양.\""], ["said I, “and want to say good-bye to Miss Havisham.”", "내가 말했다. \"그래서 해비셤 양께 작별 인사를 드리고 싶어요.\""]], [["I was not expected, for she left me locked in the yard, while she went to ask if I were to be admitted.", "내가 올 줄은 몰랐는지, 그녀는 나를 들여보내도 되는지 물으러 가는 동안 마당에 가둬 둔 채 떠났다."], ["After a very short delay, she returned and took me up, staring at me all the way.", "아주 잠깐 지체한 뒤 그녀는 돌아와 나를 데리고 올라갔는데, 내내 나를 빤히 쳐다보았다."]], [["Miss Havisham was taking exercise in the room with the long spread table, leaning on her crutch stick.", "해비셤 양은 그 길게 차려진 식탁이 있는 방에서, 목발 지팡이에 기댄 채 운동을 하고 있었다."], ["The room was lighted as of yore, and at the sound of our entrance, she stopped and turned.", "방은 예전과 다름없이 불이 밝혀져 있었고, 우리가 들어서는 소리에 그녀는 걸음을 멈추고 돌아섰다."], ["She was then just abreast of the rotted bride-cake.", "그때 그녀는 마침 그 썩은 웨딩 케이크와 나란한 위치에 있었다."]], [["“Don’t go, Sarah,”", "\"가지 마라, 세라.\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["“Well, Pip?”", "\"그래, 핍?\""]], [["“I start for London, Miss Havisham, to-morrow,”", "\"저는 내일 런던으로 떠납니다, 해비셤 양.\""], ["I was exceedingly careful what I said, “and I thought you would kindly not mind my taking leave of you.”", "나는 무슨 말을 하든 몹시 조심했다. \"그래서 작별 인사를 드려도 언짢아하지 않으시리라 생각했어요.\""]], [["“This is a gay figure, Pip,”", "\"화사한 차림새로구나, 핍.\""], ["said she, making her crutch stick play round me, as if she, the fairy godmother who had changed me, were bestowing the finishing gift.", "그녀가 마치 나를 변신시킨 요정 대모가 마지막 선물을 내리기라도 하듯, 목발 지팡이를 내 둘레로 빙빙 놀리며 말했다."]], [["“I have come into such good fortune since I saw you last, Miss Havisham,”", "\"지난번에 뵌 뒤로 저는 이렇게 큰 행운을 얻었어요, 해비셤 양.\""], ["I murmured. “And I am so grateful for it, Miss Havisham!”", "나는 나직이 말했다. \"그리고 정말 감사드려요, 해비셤 양!\""]], [["“Ay, ay!” said she, looking at the discomfited and envious Sarah, with delight.", "\"그래, 그래!\" 그녀가 무안해하며 시샘하는 세라를 더없이 흐뭇하게 바라보며 말했다."], ["“I have seen Mr. Jaggers.", "\"재거스 씨를 만났단다."], ["I have heard about it, Pip.", "나도 그 이야기를 들었지, 핍."], ["So you go to-morrow?”", "그래, 내일 떠난다고?\""]], [["“Yes, Miss Havisham.”", "\"네, 해비셤 양.\""]], [["“And you are adopted by a rich person?”", "\"그리고 어느 부유한 분에게 입양된 거고?\""]], [["“Yes, Miss Havisham.”", "\"네, 해비셤 양.\""]], [["“Not named?”", "\"이름은 밝혀지지 않았고?\""]], [["“No, Miss Havisham.”", "\"네, 해비셤 양.\""]], [["“And Mr. Jaggers is made your guardian?”", "\"그리고 재거스 씨가 네 후견인이 되었고?\""]], [["“Yes, Miss Havisham.”", "\"네, 해비셤 양.\""]], [["She quite gloated on these questions and answers, so keen was her enjoyment of Sarah Pocket’s jealous dismay.", "세라 포켓의 질투에 찬 낭패감을 즐기는 그녀의 흥취가 어찌나 컸던지, 그녀는 이 질문과 대답을 그야말로 흡족하게 음미했다. \"좋아!\""], ["“Well!” she went on; “you have a promising career before you.", "그녀가 말을 이었다. \"네 앞에는 전도유망한 길이 펼쳐져 있구나."], ["Be good—deserve it—and abide by Mr. Jaggers’s instructions.”", "착하게 굴고 — 그럴 자격을 갖추고 — 재거스 씨의 지시를 따르거라.\""], ["She looked at me, and looked at Sarah, and Sarah’s countenance wrung out of her watchful face a cruel smile.", "그녀는 나를 보고, 또 세라를 보았으며, 세라의 얼굴에서는 경계심에 찬 표정으로부터 잔인한 미소가 비어져 나왔다."], ["“Good-bye, Pip!—you will always keep the name of Pip, you know.”", "\"잘 가거라, 핍! — 너는 언제까지나 핍이라는 이름을 지녀야 한단다, 알겠지.\""]], [["“Yes, Miss Havisham.”", "\"네, 해비셤 양.\""]], [["“Good-bye, Pip!”", "\"잘 가거라, 핍!\""]], [["She stretched out her hand, and I went down on my knee and put it to my lips.", "그녀가 손을 내밀었고, 나는 한쪽 무릎을 꿇고 그 손을 입술에 가져다 댔다."], ["I had not considered how I should take leave of her; it came naturally to me at the moment to do this.", "그녀에게 어떻게 작별 인사를 해야 할지 미리 생각해 둔 것은 아니었으나, 그 순간 자연스레 이렇게 하게 되었다."], ["She looked at Sarah Pocket with triumph in her weird eyes, and so I left my fairy godmother, with both her hands on her crutch stick, standing in the midst of the dimly lighted room beside the rotten bride-cake that was hidden in cobwebs.", "그녀는 그 기이한 눈에 승리감을 담아 세라 포켓을 바라보았고, 그렇게 나는 나의 요정 대모를 두고 떠났다. 두 손을 다 목발 지팡이에 얹은 채, 거미줄에 가려진 썩은 웨딩 케이크 곁에서, 어슴푸레 불 밝혀진 방 한가운데 서 있는 그녀를."]], [["Sarah Pocket conducted me down, as if I were a ghost who must be seen out.", "세라 포켓은 마치 내가 반드시 배웅해 내보내야 할 유령이라도 되는 양 나를 아래층으로 인도했다."], ["She could not get over my appearance, and was in the last degree confounded.", "그녀는 내 달라진 모습을 도무지 받아들이지 못했고, 극도로 어리둥절해했다."], ["I said “Good-bye, Miss Pocket;”", "나는 \"안녕히 계세요, 포켓 양\""], ["but she merely stared, and did not seem collected enough to know that I had spoken. Clear of the house, I made the best of my way back to Pumblechook’s, took off my new clothes, made them into a bundle, and went back home in my older dress, carrying it—to speak the truth—much more at my ease too, though I had the bundle to carry.", "하고 말했으나, 그녀는 그저 멍하니 쳐다볼 뿐, 내가 말을 했다는 것조차 알아챌 만큼 정신이 멀쩡해 보이지 않았다. 집을 벗어나자, 나는 부지런히 펌블추크 씨 댁으로 돌아가 새 옷을 벗고 보따리로 꾸린 다음, 헌 옷차림으로 그것을 들고 집으로 향했는데 — 사실대로 말하면 — 보따리를 들었으면서도 한결 마음이 편안했다."]], [["And now, those six days which were to have run out so slowly, had run out fast and were gone, and to-morrow looked me in the face more steadily than I could look at it.", "그리하여 그토록 더디게 흘러가리라던 엿새가 어느새 빠르게 흘러 다 지나갔고, 내일이라는 날이 내가 그것을 마주 볼 수 있는 것보다 더 의연하게 나를 마주 보았다."], ["As the six evenings had dwindled away, to five, to four, to three, to two, I had become more and more appreciative of the society of Joe and Biddy.", "엿새의 저녁이 닷새로, 나흘로, 사흘로, 이틀로 차츰 줄어드는 동안, 나는 조와 비디와 함께하는 시간을 점점 더 소중히 여기게 되었다."], ["On this last evening, I dressed myself out in my new clothes for their delight, and sat in my splendour until bedtime.", "이 마지막 저녁에 나는 두 사람을 기쁘게 해 주려고 새 옷을 차려입고, 잠자리에 들 때까지 내 화려함 속에 앉아 있었다."], ["We had a hot supper on the occasion, graced by the inevitable roast fowl, and we had some flip to finish with.", "그 기념으로 우리는 따뜻한 저녁을 들었는데, 으레 빠지지 않는 통구이 새가 상을 빛냈고, 마무리로 플립을 좀 마셨다."], ["We were all very low, and none the higher for pretending to be in spirits.", "우리는 모두 몹시 가라앉아 있었고, 기운찬 척해 본들 조금도 나아지지 않았다."]], [["I was to leave our village at five in the morning, carrying my little hand-portmanteau, and I had told Joe that I wished to walk away all alone.", "나는 작은 손가방을 들고 아침 다섯 시에 우리 마을을 떠나기로 되어 있었고, 조에게는 나 혼자 걸어가고 싶다고 말해 둔 터였다."], ["I am afraid—sore afraid—that this purpose originated in my sense of the contrast there would be between me and Joe, if we went to the coach together.", "두렵건대 — 가슴 아프게도 두렵건대 — 이 작정은 우리가 함께 역마차에 가면 나와 조 사이에 드러날 대조에 대한 내 의식에서 비롯된 것이었다."], ["I had pretended with myself that there was nothing of this taint in the arrangement; but when I went up to my little room on this last night, I felt compelled to admit that it might be so, and had an impulse upon me to go down again and entreat Joe to walk with me in the morning. I did not.", "나는 그 결정에 그런 더러운 구석이 전혀 없는 척 스스로를 속여 왔으나, 이 마지막 밤에 내 작은 방으로 올라가면서, 어쩌면 그럴지도 모른다는 것을 인정하지 않을 수 없었고, 다시 내려가 조에게 아침에 나와 함께 걸어가 달라고 간청하고픈 충동에 사로잡혔다. 그러나 나는 그러지 않았다."]], [["All night there were coaches in my broken sleep, going to wrong places instead of to London, and having in the traces, now dogs, now cats, now pigs, now men,—never horses.", "밤새도록 내 어수선한 잠 속에는 역마차들이 나타나, 런던이 아니라 엉뚱한 곳으로 가고, 그 멍에에는 말은 한 마리도 없이 어떤 때는 개가, 어떤 때는 고양이가, 어떤 때는 돼지가, 어떤 때는 사람이 매여 있었다."], ["Fantastic failures of journeys occupied me until the day dawned and the birds were singing.", "기괴하게 어그러진 여정들이 날이 밝아 새들이 지저귈 때까지 나를 사로잡았다."], ["Then, I got up and partly dressed, and sat at the window to take a last look out, and in taking it fell asleep.", "그러고 나서 나는 일어나 옷을 대충 걸치고, 마지막으로 한 번 바깥을 내다보려고 창가에 앉았는데, 그러다가 그만 잠이 들고 말았다."]], [["Biddy was astir so early to get my breakfast, that, although I did not sleep at the window an hour, I smelt the smoke of the kitchen fire when I started up with a terrible idea that it must be late in the afternoon.", "비디는 어찌나 일찍 일어나 내 아침을 차렸던지, 내가 창가에서 한 시간도 채 자지 못했음에도, 화들짝 깨어나며 분명 오후 늦은 시각일 거라는 끔찍한 생각에 사로잡혔을 때 부엌 불의 연기 냄새가 났다."], ["But long after that, and long after I had heard the clinking of the teacups and was quite ready, I wanted the resolution to go downstairs.", "그러나 그 한참 뒤에도, 또 찻잔 부딪치는 소리를 듣고 채비가 다 끝난 한참 뒤에도, 나는 아래층으로 내려갈 결심이 서지 않았다."], ["After all, I remained up there, repeatedly unlocking and unstrapping my small portmanteau and locking and strapping it up again, until Biddy called to me that I was late.", "결국 나는 위층에 머문 채, 비디가 늦었다고 나를 부를 때까지, 내 작은 손가방을 거듭 열어 끈을 풀었다가 다시 잠그고 끈을 동여매기를 되풀이했다."]], [["It was a hurried breakfast with no taste in it.", "아무 맛도 없는 황급한 아침 식사였다."], ["I got up from the meal, saying with a sort of briskness, as if it had only just occurred to me, “Well!", "나는 식사 자리에서 일어나며, 이제야 막 떠올랐다는 듯이 일종의 활기를 띠고 말했다. \"자!"], ["I suppose I must be off!”", "이제 그만 가 봐야겠어요!\""], ["and then I kissed my sister who was laughing and nodding and shaking in her usual chair, and kissed Biddy, and threw my arms around Joe’s neck.", "그러고는 늘 앉던 의자에서 웃고 고개를 끄덕이며 몸을 떨고 있는 누나에게 입을 맞추고, 비디에게 입을 맞추고, 조의 목을 두 팔로 끌어안았다."], ["Then I took up my little portmanteau and walked out.", "그런 다음 작은 손가방을 들고 밖으로 걸어 나왔다."], ["The last I saw of them was, when I presently heard a scuffle behind me, and looking back, saw Joe throwing an old shoe after me and Biddy throwing another old shoe.", "내가 마지막으로 그들을 본 것은, 곧이어 등 뒤에서 부산한 기척이 들려 돌아보았을 때였는데, 조가 내 뒤로 헌 신발 한 짝을 던지고 비디가 또 다른 헌 신발 한 짝을 던지는 것이 보였다."], ["I stopped then, to wave my hat, and dear old Joe waved his strong right arm above his head, crying huskily “Hooroar!”", "그래서 나는 걸음을 멈추고 모자를 흔들었고, 정다운 조는 억센 오른팔을 머리 위로 흔들며 목쉰 소리로 \"만세!\""], ["and Biddy put her apron to her face.", "하고 외쳤으며, 비디는 앞치마를 얼굴에 갖다 댔다."]], [["I walked away at a good pace, thinking it was easier to go than I had supposed it would be, and reflecting that it would never have done to have had an old shoe thrown after the coach, in sight of all the High Street. I whistled and made nothing of going.", "나는 떠나는 것이 생각했던 것보다 수월하다고 여기며 빠른 걸음으로 걸어 나갔고, 큰길 모두가 지켜보는 앞에서 역마차 뒤로 헌 신발을 던지는 일이 있어서는 결코 안 되었으리라 생각했다. 나는 휘파람을 불며 떠나는 것을 대수롭잖게 여겼다."], ["But the village was very peaceful and quiet, and the light mists were solemnly rising, as if to show me the world, and I had been so innocent and little there, and all beyond was so unknown and great, that in a moment with a strong heave and sob I broke into tears.", "그러나 마을은 더없이 평화롭고 고요했고, 옅은 안개가 마치 내게 세상을 보여 주려는 듯 엄숙히 피어오르고 있었으며, 나는 그곳에서 그토록 순진하고 보잘것없는 존재였던 데다, 그 너머의 모든 것은 그토록 미지의 거대한 것이었으니, 한순간 나는 거센 흐느낌과 함께 울음을 터뜨리고 말았다."], ["It was by the finger-post at the end of the village, and I laid my hand upon it, and said, “Good-bye, O my dear, dear friend!”", "그것은 마을 끝 이정표 옆에서였는데, 나는 그 위에 손을 얹고 말했다. \"잘 있어, 오 내 정다운, 정다운 벗이여!\""]], [["Heaven knows we need never be ashamed of our tears, for they are rain upon the blinding dust of earth, overlying our hard hearts.", "우리의 눈물을 결코 부끄러워할 필요가 없음을 하늘은 안다. 눈물이란 우리의 굳은 마음을 뒤덮은 채 눈을 멀게 하는 이 땅의 먼지 위에 내리는 비이기 때문이다."], ["I was better after I had cried than before,—more sorry, more aware of my own ingratitude, more gentle.", "나는 울고 난 뒤가 울기 전보다 나았다 — 더 애틋하고, 내 배은망덕을 더 깊이 깨닫고, 더 부드러워졌다."], ["If I had cried before, I should have had Joe with me then.", "만약 진작 울었더라면, 그때 조가 내 곁에 있었을 텐데."]], [["So subdued I was by those tears, and by their breaking out again in the course of the quiet walk, that when I was on the coach, and it was clear of the town, I deliberated with an aching heart whether I would not get down when we changed horses and walk back, and have another evening at home, and a better parting.", "그 눈물에, 그리고 그 고요한 길을 걷는 동안 다시금 터져 나온 눈물에 어찌나 마음이 가라앉았던지, 역마차에 올라타 읍내를 벗어났을 때, 나는 아픈 마음으로, 말을 바꾸는 곳에서 내려 걸어 돌아가 집에서 하룻저녁을 더 보내고 더 나은 작별을 할까 곰곰 망설였다."], ["We changed, and I had not made up my mind, and still reflected for my comfort that it would be quite practicable to get down and walk back, when we changed again.", "말을 바꾸었는데도 나는 마음을 정하지 못한 채, 다음번에 말을 바꿀 때 내려 걸어 돌아가는 것도 얼마든지 가능하리라며 스스로 위안 삼아 거듭 생각했다."], ["And while I was occupied with these deliberations, I would fancy an exact resemblance to Joe in some man coming along the road towards us, and my heart would beat high.—As if he could possibly be there!", "그리고 이런 망설임에 잠겨 있는 동안, 길을 따라 우리 쪽으로 다가오는 어떤 사내에게서 조와 똑 닮은 모습을 떠올리며, 내 가슴은 두근거리곤 했다. — 마치 그가 거기 있을 수 있기라도 한 것처럼!"]], [["We changed again, and yet again, and it was now too late and too far to go back, and I went on.", "우리는 말을 바꾸고, 또다시 바꾸었으며, 이제는 돌아가기에 너무 늦고 너무 멀어졌기에, 나는 계속 나아갔다."], ["And the mists had all solemnly risen now, and the world lay spread before me.", "그리고 안개는 이제 모두 엄숙히 피어올라, 세상이 내 앞에 펼쳐져 있었다. 이것으로 핍의 유산 그 첫 번째 단계가 끝난다."]], [["# Chapter XX.", "# Chapter XX."]], [["The journey from our town to the metropolis was a journey of about five hours.", "우리 고장에서 대도시까지의 여정은 다섯 시간쯤 걸리는 길이었다."], ["It was a little past midday when the four-horse stage-coach by which I was a passenger, got into the ravel of traffic frayed out about the Cross Keys, Wood Street, Cheapside, London.", "내가 승객으로 탄 사두마차가 런던 칩사이드의 우드 스트리트, 크로스 키스 부근에서 풀린 실밥처럼 흐트러진 차량들의 혼잡 속으로 들어섰을 때는 정오를 조금 지난 무렵이었다."]], [["We Britons had at that time particularly settled that it was treasonable to doubt our having and our being the best of everything: otherwise, while I was scared by the immensity of London, I think I might have had some faint doubts whether it was not rather ugly, crooked, narrow, and dirty.", "당시 우리 영국인들은, 우리가 무엇이든 최고의 것을 소유하고 또 우리 자신이 최고라는 사실을 의심하는 것은 반역이라고 특히 단단히 못 박아 두고 있었다. 그렇지 않았더라면, 런던의 그 어마어마한 규모에 겁을 먹는 한편으로 나는, 이곳이 오히려 추하고, 구불구불하고, 비좁고, 더러운 곳은 아닌지 어렴풋한 의심을 품었을지도 모른다."]], [["Mr. Jaggers had duly sent me his address; it was, Little Britain, and he had written after it on his card, “just out of Smithfield, and close by the coach-office.”", "재거스 씨는 자신의 주소를 빠짐없이 적어 보내 주었다. 그것은 리틀 브리튼이었는데, 그는 자기 명함에다 그 뒤에 \"스미스필드 바로 옆, 그리고 마차 사무소와 가까운 곳\"이라고 써 두었다."], ["Nevertheless, a hackney-coachman, who seemed to have as many capes to his greasy great-coat as he was years old, packed me up in his coach and hemmed me in with a folding and jingling barrier of steps, as if he were going to take me fifty miles.", "그럼에도 한 삯마차 마부는 — 기름때 절은 그 외투에 달린 망토 자락이 제 나이만큼이나 많은 듯 보였다 — 나를 자기 마차에 꾸려 넣고는, 접었다 펴는 짤랑거리는 발판의 장벽으로 나를 가두었다. 마치 나를 오십 마일이나 데려가기라도 할 것처럼."], ["His getting on his box, which I remember to have been decorated with an old weather-stained pea-green hammercloth moth-eaten into rags, was quite a work of time.", "그가 마부석에 올라타는 것은 — 그 자리는 비바람에 얼룩지고 좀이 슬어 누더기가 된 낡은 완두콩빛 마부석 덮개로 장식되어 있었던 것으로 기억한다 — 그야말로 시간이 걸리는 작업이었다."], ["It was a wonderful equipage, with six great coronets outside, and ragged things behind for I don’t know how many footmen to hold on by, and a harrow below them, to prevent amateur footmen from yielding to the temptation.", "그것은 놀라운 마차였으니, 바깥쪽에는 커다란 백작 문장이 여섯 개나 달려 있고, 뒤쪽에는 몇 명인지도 모를 하인들이 붙잡고 매달리라고 누더기 같은 것들이 늘어져 있었으며, 그 아래에는 어설픈 풋내기 하인들이 매달릴 유혹에 넘어가지 못하도록 써레가 달려 있었다."]], [["I had scarcely had time to enjoy the coach and to think how like a straw-yard it was, and yet how like a rag-shop, and to wonder why the horses’ nose-bags were kept inside, when I observed the coachman beginning to get down, as if we were going to stop presently.", "마차를 즐기며 이것이 어쩌면 그리도 짚더미 마당 같으면서 또한 어쩌면 그리도 넝마전 같은지 생각하고, 말의 여물자루는 왜 안쪽에 매달아 두는지 의아해 할 겨를도 거의 없었는데, 어느새 마부가 곧 멈출 양 마부석에서 내려오기 시작하는 것이 눈에 들어왔다."], ["And stop we presently did, in a gloomy street, at certain offices with an open door, whereon was painted MR. JAGGERS.", "그리고 우리는 정말로 곧 멈추었다. 어느 음산한 거리에서, 열린 문 위에 \"재거스 씨\"라고 칠해진 어떤 사무소 앞에서."]], [["“How much?”", "\"얼마요?\""], ["I asked the coachman.", "나는 마부에게 물었다."]], [["The coachman answered, “A shilling—unless you wish to make it more.”", "마부가 대답했다. \"한 실링이오 — 손님이 더 주고 싶으시다면 모를까.\""]], [["I naturally said I had no wish to make it more.", "나는 당연히 더 줄 마음은 없다고 말했다."]], [["“Then it must be a shilling,”", "\"그럼 한 실링이겠구먼요.\" 마부가 말했다."], ["observed the coachman. “I don’t want to get into trouble.", "\"괜히 골치 아픈 일에 휘말리고 싶진 않으니까. 나는 그 양반을 안단 말이오!\""], ["I know him!” He darkly closed an eye at Mr. Jaggers’s name, and shook his head.", "그는 재거스 씨의 이름에 음험하게 한쪽 눈을 감고는 고개를 절레절레 흔들었다."]], [["When he had got his shilling, and had in course of time completed the ascent to his box, and had got away (which appeared to relieve his mind), I went into the front office with my little portmanteau in my hand and asked, Was Mr. Jaggers at home?", "마부가 한 실링을 받고, 이윽고 마부석으로 오르는 일을 마무리하고, 그리고 (마음이 놓이는 듯이 보이게) 떠나가 버렸을 때, 나는 작은 여행 가방을 손에 들고 앞쪽 사무소로 들어가 재거스 씨가 계시느냐고 물었다."]], [["“He is not,”", "\"안 계십니다.\""], ["returned the clerk.", "서기가 대답했다."], ["“He is in Court at present.", "\"지금 법정에 가 계십니다."], ["Am I addressing Mr. Pip?”", "제가 핍 씨를 뵙고 있는 건가요?\""]], [["I signified that he was addressing Mr. Pip.", "나는 그가 핍 씨를 뵙고 있는 것이 맞다고 알렸다."]], [["“Mr. Jaggers left word, would you wait in his room.", "\"재거스 씨께서, 방에서 기다려 달라는 말씀을 남기셨습니다."], ["He couldn’t say how long he might be, having a case on.", "사건을 맡고 계시니 얼마나 걸릴지는 말씀드릴 수 없다고요."], ["But it stands to reason, his time being valuable, that he won’t be longer than he can help.”", "하지만 그분 시간이 귀중한 분이니, 어쩔 수 없는 것 이상으로 오래 끄시지는 않을 게 당연하지요.\""]], [["With those words, the clerk opened a door, and ushered me into an inner chamber at the back.", "그 말과 함께 서기는 문을 열고 나를 뒤편 안쪽 방으로 안내했다."], ["Here, we found a gentleman with one eye, in a velveteen suit and knee-breeches, who wiped his nose with his sleeve on being interrupted in the perusal of the newspaper.", "거기서 우리는, 벨벳 양복에 무릎까지 오는 반바지를 입은 외눈박이 신사 한 명을 발견했는데, 그는 신문을 정독하다가 방해를 받자 소매로 코를 훔쳤다."]], [["“Go and wait outside, Mike,”", "\"나가서 밖에서 기다려, 마이크.\""], ["said the clerk.", "서기가 말했다."]], [["I began to say that I hoped I was not interrupting, when the clerk shoved this gentleman out with as little ceremony as I ever saw used, and tossing his fur cap out after him, left me alone.", "내가 행여 방해가 되지 않기를 바란다고 말하려 하는데, 서기는 그 신사를 내가 본 적 없는 무뚝뚝한 손짓으로 떠밀어 내보내고는, 그의 모피 모자까지 뒤따라 던져 준 다음, 나를 혼자 남겨 두었다."]], [["Mr. Jaggers’s room was lighted by a skylight only, and was a most dismal place; the skylight, eccentrically pitched like a broken head, and the distorted adjoining houses looking as if they had twisted themselves to peep down at me through it.", "재거스 씨의 방은 천창으로만 빛이 들어왔는데, 더없이 음산한 곳이었다. 천창은 깨진 머리통처럼 괴상하게 기울어져 있었고, 잇닿은 일그러진 집들은 마치 그 창을 통해 나를 내려다보려고 제 몸을 비틀기라도 한 것처럼 보였다."], ["There were not so many papers about, as I should have expected to see; and there were some odd objects about, that I should not have expected to see,—such as an old rusty pistol, a sword in a scabbard, several strange-looking boxes and packages, and two dreadful casts on a shelf, of faces peculiarly swollen, and twitchy about the nose. Mr.", "내가 으레 보리라 예상했던 만큼 서류가 많지는 않았다. 그리고 내가 보리라고 예상하지 못했던 기이한 물건들이 몇 가지 놓여 있었으니 — 이를테면 녹슨 낡은 권총 하나, 칼집에 든 검 한 자루, 야릇하게 생긴 상자와 꾸러미 몇 개, 그리고 선반 위에 놓인 끔찍한 석고 마스크 두 개였는데, 그 얼굴들은 유난히 부어 있고 코 언저리가 씰룩거리는 듯했다."], ["Jaggers’s own high-backed chair was of deadly black horsehair, with rows of brass nails round it, like a coffin; and I fancied I could see how he leaned back in it, and bit his forefinger at the clients.", "재거스 씨 자신의 등받이 높은 의자는 죽음처럼 새카만 말총으로 되어 있고, 둘레에 놋쇠 못이 줄지어 박혀 있어 관(棺)을 연상시켰다. 그리고 나는 그가 그 의자에 등을 기댄 채 의뢰인들을 향해 검지를 깨무는 모습을 눈앞에 그려 볼 수 있을 것 같았다."], ["The room was but small, and the clients seemed to have had a habit of backing up against the wall; the wall, especially opposite to Mr. Jaggers’s chair, being greasy with shoulders.", "방은 작은 편이었고, 의뢰인들은 벽에 등을 기대고 물러서는 버릇이 있었던 모양이었다. 벽은, 특히 재거스 씨의 의자 맞은편 벽은, 어깨가 닿아 번질번질했다."], ["I recalled, too, that the one-eyed gentleman had shuffled forth against the wall when I was the innocent cause of his being turned out.", "또한 나는, 내가 죄 없이 그 외눈박이 신사를 쫓겨나게 만든 원인이 되었을 때, 그가 벽에 붙어 발을 끌며 나가던 것을 떠올렸다."]], [["I sat down in the cliental chair placed over against Mr. Jaggers’s chair, and became fascinated by the dismal atmosphere of the place.", "나는 재거스 씨의 의자와 마주 보게 놓인 의뢰인용 의자에 앉았고, 그곳의 음산한 분위기에 사로잡혔다."], ["I called to mind that the clerk had the same air of knowing something to everybody else’s disadvantage, as his master had.", "나는 그 서기 역시 제 주인과 똑같이, 다른 사람 누구에게나 불리한 무언가를 알고 있다는 투의 분위기를 풍긴다는 사실을 떠올렸다."], ["I wondered how many other clerks there were upstairs, and whether they all claimed to have the same detrimental mastery of their fellow-creatures.", "나는 위층에 서기가 또 몇 명이나 있을지, 그리고 그들 모두가 제 동료 인간들을 그렇게 해로운 방식으로 손아귀에 쥐고 있다고 자처하는지 궁금했다."], ["I wondered what was the history of all the odd litter about the room, and how it came there.", "나는 방 안에 어질러진 그 모든 기이한 잡동사니의 내력은 무엇이며, 어쩌다 그것들이 여기에 오게 되었는지 궁금했다."], ["I wondered whether the two swollen faces were of Mr. Jaggers’s family, and, if he were so unfortunate as to have had a pair of such ill-looking relations, why he stuck them on that dusty perch for the blacks and flies to settle on, instead of giving them a place at home.", "나는 그 부어오른 두 얼굴이 재거스 씨의 가족인지, 그리고 만약 그가 그처럼 흉하게 생긴 친척을 한 쌍이나 두는 불운을 겪었다면, 어째서 그들을 집안에 자리 잡아 주는 대신 검댕과 파리가 내려앉도록 저 먼지투성이 횃대 위에 붙박아 두었는지 궁금했다."], ["Of course I had no experience of a London summer day, and my spirits may have been oppressed by the hot exhausted air, and by the dust and grit that lay thick on everything.", "물론 나는 런던의 여름날을 겪어 본 적이 없었으니, 후덥지근하고 진이 빠진 공기와, 모든 것 위에 두껍게 쌓인 먼지와 모래티 때문에 기분이 짓눌렸는지도 모른다."], ["But I sat wondering and waiting in Mr. Jaggers’s close room, until I really could not bear the two casts on the shelf above Mr. Jaggers’s chair, and got up and went out.", "그러나 나는 재거스 씨의 답답한 방에서 의아해하고 기다리며 앉아 있다가, 마침내 재거스 씨의 의자 위쪽 선반에 놓인 그 두 개의 석고상을 정말이지 견딜 수 없게 되어, 자리에서 일어나 밖으로 나갔다."]], [["When I told the clerk that I would take a turn in the air while I waited, he advised me to go round the corner and I should come into Smithfield.", "내가 서기에게 기다리는 동안 바람이나 좀 쐬고 오겠다고 했더니, 그는 모퉁이를 돌면 스미스필드가 나올 거라고 일러 주었다."], ["So I came into Smithfield; and the shameful place, being all asmear with filth and fat and blood and foam, seemed to stick to me.", "그래서 나는 스미스필드로 들어섰다. 그 수치스러운 곳은 온통 오물과 기름과 피와 거품으로 범벅이 되어, 마치 내 몸에 들러붙는 것만 같았다."], ["So, I rubbed it off with all possible speed by turning into a street where I saw the great black dome of Saint Paul’s bulging at me from behind a grim stone building which a bystander said was Newgate Prison.", "그래서 나는 한시바삐 그것을 떨쳐 내려고 어느 거리로 접어들었는데, 거기서 한 행인이 뉴게이트 감옥이라고 일러 준 음산한 석조 건물 뒤편으로 세인트 폴 대성당의 거대한 검은 돔이 내게로 불룩 솟아오른 것이 보였다."], ["Following the wall of the jail, I found the roadway covered with straw to deaden the noise of passing vehicles; and from this, and from the quantity of people standing about smelling strongly of spirits and beer, I inferred that the trials were on.", "감옥 담장을 따라가다 보니, 지나가는 차량들의 소음을 죽이려고 도로에 짚을 깔아 놓은 것이 눈에 띄었다. 이것으로 미루어, 또 독한 술과 맥주 냄새를 풀풀 풍기며 주변에 모여 선 사람들이 많은 것으로 미루어, 나는 재판이 진행 중이라고 추측했다."]], [["While I looked about me here, an exceedingly dirty and partially drunk minister of justice asked me if I would like to step in and hear a trial or so: informing me that he could give me a front place for half a crown, whence I should command a full view of the Lord Chief Justice in his wig and robes,—mentioning that awful personage like waxwork, and presently offering him at the reduced price of eighteen-pence.", "내가 여기서 이리저리 둘러보고 있는데, 몹시 더럽고 얼근히 취한 사법(司法)의 종복 하나가 나더러 안에 들어가서 재판이나 몇 건 구경하지 않겠느냐고 물었다. 반(半) 크라운만 내면 앞자리를 잡아 줄 테니, 거기서 가발에 법복을 걸친 대법원장의 모습을 한눈에 볼 수 있을 거라고 알려 주면서 — 그는 그 어마어마한 인물을 마치 밀랍 인형이라도 되는 양 들먹이더니, 곧 일 실링 육 펜스라는 깎은 값에 그를 내놓았다."], ["As I declined the proposal on the plea of an appointment, he was so good as to take me into a yard and show me where the gallows was kept, and also where people were publicly whipped, and then he showed me the Debtors’ Door, out of which culprits came to be hanged; heightening the interest of that dreadful portal by giving me to understand that “four on ’em”", "내가 약속이 있다는 핑계로 그 제안을 거절하자, 그는 친절하게도 나를 어느 마당으로 데려가 교수대가 보관된 곳을 보여 주고, 또 사람들이 공개적으로 매질당하는 곳도 보여 주더니, 이어서 채무자의 문을 보여 주었다. 죄인들이 교수형을 당하러 나오는 문이었다. 그는 그 무시무시한 문의 흥미를 한층 돋우려고, \"그놈들 중 넷\"이 모레 아침 여덟 시에 그 문으로 나와 한 줄로 죽임을 당할 거라고 일러 주었다."], ["would come out at that door the day after to-morrow at eight in the morning, to be killed in a row.", "이것은 끔찍한 일이었고, 런던에 대해 메스꺼운 인상을 내게 안겨 주었다."], ["This was horrible, and gave me a sickening idea of London; the more so as the Lord Chief Justice’s proprietor wore (from his hat down to his boots and up again to his pocket-handkerchief inclusive) mildewed clothes which had evidently not belonged to him originally, and which I took it into my head he had bought cheap of the executioner.", "더욱이 그 대법원장의 주인장이 (모자에서부터 장화에 이르기까지, 그리고 다시 거슬러 올라가 손수건까지 포함해서) 곰팡이 슨 옷을 걸치고 있었는데, 그 옷은 분명 원래 그의 것이 아니었고, 나는 그가 사형 집행인에게서 헐값에 사들였으리라는 생각이 머릿속에 박혀 버렸기에 더욱 그러했다."], ["Under these circumstances I thought myself well rid of him for a shilling.", "이런 형편이고 보니, 나는 일 실링으로 그를 떨쳐 낸 것이 잘된 일이라고 생각했다."]], [["I dropped into the office to ask if Mr. Jaggers had come in yet, and I found he had not, and I strolled out again.", "나는 재거스 씨가 들어오셨는지 물으려고 사무소에 잠깐 들렀으나, 아직 오지 않았다는 것을 알고 다시 어슬렁어슬렁 걸어 나왔다."], ["This time, I made the tour of Little Britain, and turned into Bartholomew Close; and now I became aware that other people were waiting about for Mr. Jaggers, as well as I.", "이번에는 리틀 브리튼을 한 바퀴 돌고 바살러뮤 클로스로 접어들었는데, 이제 나는 나 말고도 다른 사람들이 재거스 씨를 기다리며 서성이고 있다는 것을 알아차렸다."], ["There were two men of secret appearance lounging in Bartholomew Close, and thoughtfully fitting their feet into the cracks of the pavement as they talked together, one of whom said to the other when they first passed me, that “Jaggers would do it if it was to be done.”", "바살러뮤 클로스에는 은밀한 인상을 풍기는 두 사내가 빈둥거리며, 이야기를 나누면서 생각에 잠긴 듯 발을 보도의 틈새에 맞춰 끼우고 있었는데, 그중 하나가 나를 처음 지나칠 때 다른 하나에게 \"이 일이 될 수 있는 일이라면 재거스가 해낼 거야\"라고 말했다."], ["There was a knot of three men and two women standing at a corner, and one of the women was crying on her dirty shawl, and the other comforted her by saying, as she pulled her own shawl over her shoulders, “Jaggers is for him, ’Melia, and what more could you have?”", "어느 모퉁이에는 세 남자와 두 여자가 한 무리를 이루고 서 있었는데, 여자 중 하나는 더러운 숄에 얼굴을 묻고 울고 있었고, 다른 하나는 제 숄을 어깨에 끌어 두르며 \"재거스가 그 사람 편이잖아, 어밀리아, 그보다 더 뭘 바라겠어?\" 하고 위로하고 있었다."], ["There was a red-eyed little Jew who came into the Close while I was loitering there, in company with a second little Jew whom he sent upon an errand; and while the messenger was gone, I remarked this Jew, who was of a highly excitable temperament, performing a jig of anxiety under a lamp-post and accompanying himself, in a kind of frenzy, with the words, “O Jaggerth, Jaggerth, Jaggerth!", "내가 그곳에서 어정거리는 동안, 두 번째 작은 유대인을 데리고 클로스로 들어온, 눈이 충혈된 작은 유대인이 하나 있었는데, 그는 그 두 번째 유대인을 심부름 보냈다. 그리고 그 심부름꾼이 가 있는 동안, 나는 이 유대인을 눈여겨보았다. 매우 흥분하기 쉬운 기질의 그 사내는 가로등 기둥 아래에서 불안의 지그를 추며, 일종의 광란에 빠진 채 이렇게 제 가락을 붙이고 있었다."], ["all otherth ith Cag-Maggerth, give me Jaggerth!”", "\"오 재거쓰, 재거쓰, 재거쓰!"], ["These testimonies to the popularity of my guardian made a deep impression on me, and I admired and wondered more than ever.", "다른 놈들은 죄다 캐그매그쓰지, 나에게 재거쓰를 다오!\" 내 후견인의 인기를 입증하는 이런 증언들은 내게 깊은 인상을 남겼고, 나는 그 어느 때보다 더욱 감탄하며 의아해했다."]], [["At length, as I was looking out at the iron gate of Bartholomew Close into Little Britain, I saw Mr. Jaggers coming across the road towards me.", "마침내 내가 바살러뮤 클로스의 철문 밖으로 리틀 브리튼 쪽을 내다보고 있을 때, 재거스 씨가 길을 가로질러 내게로 다가오는 것이 보였다."], ["All the others who were waiting saw him at the same time, and there was quite a rush at him. Mr.", "기다리던 다른 모든 이들도 그를 동시에 알아보았고, 그를 향해 우르르 몰려들었다."], ["Jaggers, putting a hand on my shoulder and walking me on at his side without saying anything to me, addressed himself to his followers.", "재거스 씨는 한 손을 내 어깨에 얹고 나에게는 아무 말 없이 나를 곁에 끼고 걸으면서, 자기 추종자들을 향해 말을 건넸다."]], [["First, he took the two secret men.", "먼저 그는 그 은밀한 두 사내를 상대했다."]], [["“Now, I have nothing to say to you,”", "\"자, 나는 너희에게 할 말이 없다.\""], ["said Mr. Jaggers, throwing his finger at them.", "재거스 씨가 그들을 향해 손가락을 내던지며 말했다."], ["“I want to know no more than I know.", "\"내가 아는 것 이상은 알고 싶지 않다."], ["As to the result, it’s a toss-up.", "결과로 말하자면, 그건 동전 던지기다."], ["I told you from the first it was a toss-up.", "처음부터 동전 던지기라고 말해 두었지."], ["Have you paid Wemmick?”", "웨믹에게 돈은 지불했나?\""]], [["“We made the money up this morning, sir,”", "\"오늘 아침에 돈을 마련했습니다, 선생님.\""], ["said one of the men, submissively, while the other perused Mr. Jaggers’s face.", "한 사내가 순순히 말했고, 다른 하나는 재거스 씨의 얼굴을 찬찬히 살폈다."]], [["“I don’t ask you when you made it up, or where, or whether you made it up at all.", "\"언제 마련했는지, 어디서 마련했는지, 아니 마련하기는 했는지, 그런 건 묻지 않는다."], ["Has Wemmick got it?”", "웨믹이 그걸 받았나?\""]], [["“Yes, sir,”", "\"네, 선생님.\""], ["said both the men together.", "두 사내가 한목소리로 말했다."]], [["“Very well; then you may go.", "\"좋아, 그럼 가도 된다."], ["Now, I won’t have it!”", "자, 더는 안 된다!\""], ["said Mr Jaggers, waving his hand at them to put them behind him.", "재거스 씨가 그들을 뒤로 물리려고 손을 휘저으며 말했다."], ["“If you say a word to me, I’ll throw up the case.”", "\"나에게 한마디라도 더 하면, 이 사건을 내던져 버리겠다.\""]], [["“We thought, Mr. Jaggers—”", "\"저희는 생각하기를, 재거스 씨—\""], ["one of the men began, pulling off his hat.", "한 사내가 모자를 벗으며 입을 떼었다."]], [["“That’s what I told you not to do,”", "\"그게 바로 하지 말라고 한 짓이다.\""], ["said Mr. Jaggers.", "재거스 씨가 말했다."], ["“You thought!", "\"너희가 생각해!"], ["I think for you; that’s enough for you.", "너희를 위해서는 내가 생각한다. 그걸로 족하다."], ["If I want you, I know where to find you; I don’t want you to find me.", "너희가 필요하면 내가 어디서 찾을지 안다. 너희가 나를 찾아오는 건 바라지 않아."], ["Now I won’t have it.", "자, 더는 안 돼."], ["I won’t hear a word.”", "한마디도 듣지 않겠다.\""]], [["The two men looked at one another as Mr. Jaggers waved them behind again, and humbly fell back and were heard no more.", "재거스 씨가 다시 그들을 뒤로 물리는 손짓을 하자 두 사내는 서로를 마주 보더니, 굽실거리며 물러나 더는 아무 소리도 들리지 않았다."]], [["“And now you!” said Mr.", "\"그리고 이제 당신들 차례군!\""], ["Jaggers, suddenly stopping, and turning on the two women with the shawls, from whom the three men had meekly separated,—“Oh!", "재거스 씨가 갑자기 걸음을 멈추고, 세 남자가 얌전히 떨어져 나간 숄 두른 두 여자에게로 돌아서며 말했다. \"오!"], ["Amelia, is it?”", "어밀리아인가?\""]], [["“Yes, Mr. Jaggers.”", "\"네, 재거스 씨.\""]], [["“And do you remember,” retorted Mr.", "\"그래, 기억하는가.\" 재거스 씨가 쏘아붙였다."], ["Jaggers, “that but for me you wouldn’t be here and couldn’t be here?”", "\"내가 아니었으면 당신은 여기 있지도 못했고, 여기 있을 수도 없었다는 것을?\""]], [["“O yes, sir!” exclaimed both women together.", "\"오, 네, 선생님!\" 두 여자가 한목소리로 외쳤다."], ["“Lord bless you, sir, well we knows that!”", "\"선생님께 하느님의 축복이 있기를요, 그야 저희도 잘 알지요!\""]], [["“Then why,”", "\"그럼 어째서,\""], ["said Mr. Jaggers, “do you come here?”", "재거스 씨가 말했다. \"당신들은 여기를 찾아오나?\""]], [["“My Bill, sir!” the crying woman pleaded.", "\"제 빌 때문이에요, 선생님!\" 울던 여자가 호소했다."]], [["“Now, I tell you what!”", "\"자, 내가 말해 두지!\""], ["said Mr. Jaggers.", "재거스 씨가 말했다."], ["“Once for all. If you don’t know that your Bill’s in good hands, I know it.", "\"이번 한 번으로 끝이다. 당신 빌이 좋은 손에 맡겨져 있다는 걸 당신이 모른다면, 나는 안다."], ["And if you come here bothering about your Bill, I’ll make an example of both your Bill and you, and let him slip through my fingers.", "그리고 당신이 빌 때문에 여기 와서 귀찮게 군다면, 나는 빌과 당신 둘 다를 본보기로 삼고, 그자를 내 손가락 사이로 빠져나가게 두겠다."], ["Have you paid Wemmick?”", "웨믹에게 돈은 지불했나?\""]], [["“O yes, sir!", "\"오, 네, 선생님!"], ["Every farden.”", "한 푼도 빠짐없이요.\""]], [["“Very well.", "\"좋아."], ["Then you have done all you have got to do.", "그럼 당신이 할 일은 다 한 거다."], ["Say another word—one single word—and Wemmick shall give you your money back.”", "한마디만 더 — 단 한마디만 더 하면 — 웨믹이 당신 돈을 도로 돌려줄 거야.\""]], [["This terrible threat caused the two women to fall off immediately.", "이 무시무시한 위협에 두 여자는 즉시 물러났다."], ["No one remained now but the excitable Jew, who had already raised the skirts of Mr. Jaggers’s coat to his lips several times.", "이제 남은 것은 흥분 잘하는 그 유대인뿐이었는데, 그는 이미 여러 차례 재거스 씨의 외투 자락을 입술에 갖다 댄 터였다."]], [["“I don’t know this man!”", "\"나는 이 사람을 모른다!\""], ["said Mr. Jaggers, in the same devastating strain: “What does this fellow want?”", "재거스 씨가 똑같이 사람을 짓뭉개는 어조로 말했다. \"이 친구가 원하는 게 뭔가?\""]], [["“Ma thear Mithter Jaggerth.", "\"나의 친애하는 재거쓰 씨."], ["Hown brother to Habraham Latharuth?”", "에이브러햄 래써루쓰의 친형제 일로요?\""]], [["“Who’s he?”", "\"그게 누군가?\""], ["said Mr. Jaggers.", "재거스 씨가 말했다."], ["“Let go of my coat.”", "\"내 외투 놓게.\""]], [["The suitor, kissing the hem of the garment again before relinquishing it, replied, “Habraham Latharuth, on thuthpithion of plate.”", "청원자는 외투를 놓아주기 전에 다시 한 번 그 자락에 입 맞추고 대답했다. \"에이브러햄 래써루쓰요, 은식기 절도 혐의로요.\""]], [["“You’re too late,”", "\"자네는 너무 늦었네.\""], ["said Mr. Jaggers.", "재거스 씨가 말했다."], ["“I am over the way.”", "\"나는 반대편일세.\""]], [["“Holy father, Mithter Jaggerth!”", "\"오 하느님 아버지, 재거쓰 씨!\""], ["cried my excitable acquaintance, turning white, “don’t thay you’re again Habraham Latharuth!”", "흥분 잘하는 나의 새 지인이 새파랗게 질려서 외쳤다. \"에이브러햄 래써루쓰에게 맞서신다는 말씀은 마세요!\""]], [["“I am,” said Mr.", "\"맞서네.\""], ["Jaggers, “and there’s an end of it. Get out of the way.”", "재거스 씨가 말했다. \"그리고 그걸로 끝이야. 비키게.\""]], [["“Mithter Jaggerth! Half a moment!", "\"재거쓰 씨! 잠깐만요!"], ["My hown cuthen’th gone to Mithter Wemmick at thith prethent minute, to hoffer him hany termth.", "제 사촌이 바로 지금 이 순간 웨믹 씨께 가서, 어떤 조건이든 제시하고 있단 말입니다."], ["Mithter Jaggerth!", "재거쓰 씨!"], ["Half a quarter of a moment!", "아주 잠깐만요!"], ["If you’d have the condethenthun to be bought off from the t’other thide—at hany thuperior prithe!—money no object!—Mithter Jaggerth—Mithter—!”", "만약 황송하게도 저쪽 편에서 매수당해 주실 수만 있다면 — 아무리 더 높은 값을 치르더라도요! — 돈은 문제가 아닙니다! — 재거쓰 씨 — 재거쓰—!\""]], [["My guardian threw his supplicant off with supreme indifference, and left him dancing on the pavement as if it were red hot.", "내 후견인은 더없이 무심하게 그 탄원자를 떨쳐 내고는, 그를 마치 보도가 시뻘겋게 달아오르기라도 한 듯 그 위에서 발을 동동 구르도록 내버려 두었다."], ["Without further interruption, we reached the front office, where we found the clerk and the man in velveteen with the fur cap.", "더는 방해받지 않고 우리는 앞쪽 사무소에 이르렀고, 거기서 서기와, 모피 모자를 쓴 벨벳 옷의 사내를 발견했다."]], [["“Here’s Mike,”", "\"마이크가 와 있습니다.\""], ["said the clerk, getting down from his stool, and approaching Mr. Jaggers confidentially.", "서기가 걸상에서 내려와 재거스 씨에게 은밀하게 다가오며 말했다."]], [["“Oh!” said Mr.", "\"오!\""], ["Jaggers, turning to the man, who was pulling a lock of hair in the middle of his forehead, like the Bull in Cock Robin pulling at the bell-rope; “your man comes on this afternoon. Well?”", "재거스 씨가 그 사내에게로 돌아서며 말했는데, 사내는 마치 「수탉 로빈」의 황소가 종 줄을 당기듯, 이마 한가운데의 머리털 한 가닥을 잡아당기고 있었다. \"자네 사람이 오늘 오후에 출두한다고. 그래?\""]], [["“Well, Mas’r Jaggers,”", "\"네, 재거스 나리.\""], ["returned Mike, in the voice of a sufferer from a constitutional cold; “arter a deal o’ trouble, I’ve found one, sir, as might do.”", "마이크가 만성 감기 환자 같은 목소리로 대답했다. \"한참을 애먹은 끝에, 쓸 만한 자를 하나 찾았습니다요, 나리.\""]], [["“What is he prepared to swear?”", "\"그자가 무엇을 맹세할 준비가 되어 있나?\""]], [["“Well, Mas’r Jaggers,”", "\"네, 재거스 나리.\""], ["said Mike, wiping his nose on his fur cap this time; “in a general way, anythink.”", "마이크가 이번에는 모피 모자에 코를 훔치며 말했다. \"대체로 말해서, 뭐든지요.\""]], [["Mr. Jaggers suddenly became most irate.", "재거스 씨는 갑자기 더없이 격노했다."], ["“Now, I warned you before,”", "\"내가 전에 경고했지.\""], ["said he, throwing his forefinger at the terrified client, “that if you ever presumed to talk in that way here, I’d make an example of you.", "그가 겁에 질린 의뢰인을 향해 검지를 내던지며 말했다. \"여기서 또 한 번 감히 그따위로 지껄였다간, 너를 본보기로 삼겠다고 말이야."], ["You infernal scoundrel, how dare you tell ME that?”", "이 천하의 악당아, 어떻게 감히 나에게 그런 소리를 하느냔 말이다?\""]], [["The client looked scared, but bewildered too, as if he were unconscious what he had done.", "의뢰인은 겁먹은 표정이었으나, 동시에 어리둥절한 기색이기도 했으니, 마치 자기가 무슨 짓을 했는지조차 모르는 사람 같았다."]], [["“Spooney!” said the clerk, in a low voice, giving him a stir with his elbow.", "\"이 멍청아!\" 서기가 낮은 목소리로 그를 팔꿈치로 쿡 찌르며 말했다."], ["“Soft Head!", "\"이 맹추야!"], ["Need you say it face to face?”", "그걸 꼭 면전에서 말해야겠어?\""]], [["“Now, I ask you, you blundering booby,”", "\"자, 내가 묻겠다, 이 어리석은 얼간이야.\""], ["said my guardian, very sternly, “once more and for the last time, what the man you have brought here is prepared to swear?”", "내 후견인이 매우 엄하게 말했다. \"다시 한 번, 그리고 이것이 마지막이다. 네가 여기 데려온 사람이 무엇을 맹세할 준비가 되어 있느냐?\""]], [["Mike looked hard at my guardian, as if he were trying to learn a lesson from his face, and slowly replied, “Ayther to character, or to having been in his company and never left him all the night in question.”", "마이크는 마치 그의 얼굴에서 무슨 교훈이라도 배우려는 듯 내 후견인을 뚫어지게 바라보더니, 천천히 대답했다. \"인품에 관해서든지, 아니면 문제의 그날 밤 내내 그자와 함께 있었고 한 번도 그자를 떠나지 않았다는 거든지요.\""]], [["“Now, be careful.", "\"자, 조심해라."], ["In what station of life is this man?”", "이 사람은 어떤 신분이냐?\""]], [["Mike looked at his cap, and looked at the floor, and looked at the ceiling, and looked at the clerk, and even looked at me, before beginning to reply in a nervous manner, “We’ve dressed him up like—”", "마이크는 자기 모자를 보고, 바닥을 보고, 천장을 보고, 서기를 보고, 심지어 나까지 본 다음, 초조한 태도로 대답하기 시작했다. \"저희가 그자를 이렇게 차려입혔습니다요—\""], ["when my guardian blustered out,—", "그러자 내 후견인이 버럭 호통을 쳤다."]], [["“What?", "\"뭐라고?"], ["You WILL, will you?”", "기어이 그러겠다는 거냐?\""]], [["(“Spooney!” added the clerk again, with another stir.)", "(\"이 멍청아!\" 서기가 또 한 번 쿡 찌르며 덧붙였다.)"]], [["After some helpless casting about, Mike brightened and began again:—", "어찌할 바를 모르고 한참을 더듬거리던 마이크는 표정이 환해지더니 다시 입을 열었다."]], [["“He is dressed like a ’spectable pieman.", "\"점잖은 파이 장수처럼 차려입었습니다요."], ["A sort of a pastry-cook.”", "일종의 제과사처럼요.\""]], [["“Is he here?” asked my guardian.", "\"그자가 여기 있나?\" 내 후견인이 물었다."]], [["“I left him,”", "\"그자를 두고 왔습니다요.\""], ["said Mike, “a setting on some doorsteps round the corner.”", "마이크가 말했다. \"모퉁이를 돈 어느 집 현관 계단에 앉혀 두었습니다요.\""]], [["“Take him past that window, and let me see him.”", "\"저 창문 앞으로 지나가게 해서, 내가 보게 하라.\""]], [["The window indicated was the office window.", "가리킨 창문은 사무소 창문이었다."], ["We all three went to it, behind the wire blind, and presently saw the client go by in an accidental manner, with a murderous-looking tall individual, in a short suit of white linen and a paper cap.", "우리 셋은 모두 철망 블라인드 뒤편으로 가서 그쪽으로 다가갔는데, 이윽고 그 의뢰인이 우연인 척 지나가는 것이 보였다. 살기등등하게 생긴 키 큰 사내와 함께였는데, 그자는 짧은 흰 리넨 옷에 종이 모자를 쓰고 있었다."], ["This guileless confectioner was not by any means sober, and had a black eye in the green stage of recovery, which was painted over.", "이 순진해 빠진 제과사는 결코 맨정신이 아니었고, 회복 중에 녹색으로 변한 멍든 눈을 하나 달고 있었는데, 그 위에 화장을 발라 가려 놓았다."]], [["“Tell him to take his witness away directly,”", "\"저자더러 당장 자기 증인을 데리고 가라고 일러.\""], ["said my guardian to the clerk, in extreme disgust, “and ask him what he means by bringing such a fellow as that.”", "내 후견인이 극도의 혐오감을 드러내며 서기에게 말했다. \"그리고 저런 작자를 데려오다니 도대체 무슨 작정이냐고 물어봐.\""]], [["My guardian then took me into his own room, and while he lunched, standing, from a sandwich-box and a pocket-flask of sherry (he seemed to bully his very sandwich as he ate it), informed me what arrangements he had made for me. I was to go to “Barnard’s Inn,”", "그러고 나서 내 후견인은 나를 자기 방으로 데려갔고, 샌드위치 상자와 셰리주가 든 휴대용 술병에서 선 채로 점심을 들면서 (그는 샌드위치를 먹으면서도 그 샌드위치마저 윽박지르는 듯했다), 나를 위해 어떤 준비를 해 두었는지 알려 주었다."], ["to young Mr. Pocket’s rooms, where a bed had been sent in for my accommodation; I was to remain with young Mr.", "나는 \"바너드 인\"의 젊은 포켓 씨의 거처로 가게 되어 있었는데, 거기에는 나를 위한 침대가 들여져 있었다."], ["Pocket until Monday; on Monday I was to go with him to his father’s house on a visit, that I might try how I liked it. Also, I was told what my allowance was to be,—it was a very liberal one,—and had handed to me from one of my guardian’s drawers, the cards of certain tradesmen with whom I was to deal for all kinds of clothes, and such other things as I could in reason want.", "나는 월요일까지 젊은 포켓 씨와 함께 지내고, 월요일에는 그와 함께 그의 아버지 댁을 방문하여, 그곳이 마음에 드는지 시험 삼아 지내 보기로 되어 있었다. 또한 나는 내 용돈이 얼마인지도 들었는데 — 매우 후한 액수였다 — 후견인의 서랍들 중 하나에서, 온갖 종류의 옷가지며 그 밖에 내가 사리에 맞게 필요로 할 만한 물건들을 거래하기로 되어 있는 어떤 상인들의 명함을 건네받았다."], ["“You will find your credit good, Mr. Pip,”", "\"자네 신용은 좋을 걸세, 핍 군.\""], ["said my guardian, whose flask of sherry smelt like a whole caskful, as he hastily refreshed himself, “but I shall by this means be able to check your bills, and to pull you up if I find you outrunning the constable.", "내 후견인이 말했는데, 그가 황급히 원기를 돋우는 동안 그 셰리주 병은 통째로 한 통은 되는 듯한 냄새를 풍겼다. \"하지만 나는 이렇게 함으로써 자네 청구서를 점검할 수 있고, 만약 자네가 분에 넘치게 쓰는 걸 발견하면 자네를 단속할 수 있을 걸세."], ["Of course you’ll go wrong somehow, but that’s no fault of mine.”", "물론 자네는 어떤 식으로든 빗나가겠지만, 그건 내 잘못이 아닐세.\""]], [["After I had pondered a little over this encouraging sentiment, I asked Mr. Jaggers if I could send for a coach?", "이 고무적인 소회를 잠시 곱씹은 다음, 나는 재거스 씨에게 마차를 부를 수 있겠느냐고 물었다."], ["He said it was not worth while, I was so near my destination; Wemmick should walk round with me, if I pleased.", "그는 그럴 것까지 없다고, 목적지가 워낙 가까우니, 원한다면 웨믹이 함께 걸어가 줄 거라고 했다."]], [["I then found that Wemmick was the clerk in the next room.", "그때 나는 웨믹이 바로 옆방의 그 서기라는 것을 알게 되었다."], ["Another clerk was rung down from upstairs to take his place while he was out, and I accompanied him into the street, after shaking hands with my guardian.", "그가 자리를 비운 동안 그 자리를 대신하라고 위층에서 다른 서기 하나가 종소리에 불려 내려왔고, 나는 후견인과 악수를 나눈 뒤 웨믹과 함께 거리로 나섰다."], ["We found a new set of people lingering outside, but Wemmick made a way among them by saying coolly yet decisively, “I tell you it’s no use; he won’t have a word to say to one of you;”", "우리는 밖에서 서성이는 새로운 무리의 사람들을 발견했지만, 웨믹은 차분하면서도 단호하게 \"내 말하지만 소용없소. 그분은 당신들 중 누구에게도 할 말이 없으실 거요\" 하고 말하여 그들 사이로 길을 텄다."], ["and we soon got clear of them, and went on side by side.", "우리는 곧 그들에게서 벗어나, 나란히 걸어갔다."]], [["# Chapter XXI.", "# Chapter XXI."]], [["Casting my eyes on Mr. Wemmick as we went along, to see what he was like in the light of day, I found him to be a dry man, rather short in stature, with a square wooden face, whose expression seemed to have been imperfectly chipped out with a dull-edged chisel.", "웨믹 씨가 어떤 사람인지 대낮의 빛 속에서 살펴보려고 함께 걸어가며 그에게 눈길을 던졌더니, 그는 메마른 사람으로, 키가 다소 작고, 네모진 나무 같은 얼굴을 하고 있었는데, 그 표정은 무딘 끌로 어설프게 깎아 낸 것처럼 보였다."], ["There were some marks in it that might have been dimples, if the material had been softer and the instrument finer, but which, as it was, were only dints.", "그 얼굴에는 재료가 좀 더 부드럽고 연장이 좀 더 정교했더라면 보조개가 되었을 법한 자국들이 몇 개 있었으나, 실제로는 그저 움푹 팬 자국에 불과했다."], ["The chisel had made three or four of these attempts at embellishment over his nose, but had given them up without an effort to smooth them off.", "끌은 그의 코 위에다 그런 장식의 시도를 서너 번 했으나, 그것을 매끄럽게 다듬으려는 노력은 전혀 하지 않은 채 내버려 둔 셈이었다."], ["I judged him to be a bachelor from the frayed condition of his linen, and he appeared to have sustained a good many bereavements; for he wore at least four mourning rings, besides a brooch representing a lady and a weeping willow at a tomb with an urn on it.", "나는 그의 닳아 해진 셔츠 상태로 미루어 그가 독신이리라 판단했고, 또한 그는 적잖은 사별을 겪은 듯했다. 적어도 네 개의 상중(喪中) 반지를 끼고 있었던 데다, 유골 단지가 얹힌 무덤 옆에 한 부인과 수양버들이 그려진 브로치까지 달고 있었으니까."], ["I noticed, too, that several rings and seals hung at his watch-chain, as if he were quite laden with remembrances of departed friends.", "나는 또한 그의 회중시계 줄에 여러 개의 반지와 인장이 매달려 있어, 마치 그가 세상을 떠난 친구들의 추억을 잔뜩 짊어지고 있는 것 같다는 것도 눈치챘다."], ["He had glittering eyes,—small, keen, and black,—and thin wide mottled lips.", "그는 반짝이는 눈을 — 작고, 날카롭고, 검은 눈을 — 가졌으며, 입술은 얇고 넓고 얼룩덜룩했다."], ["He had had them, to the best of my belief, from forty to fifty years.", "내가 믿는 바로는, 그는 그 눈과 입술을 마흔 살에서 쉰 살에 이르도록 지녀 온 듯했다."]], [["“So you were never in London before?”", "\"그래, 런던에는 처음 와 보는 거요?\""], ["said Mr. Wemmick to me.", "웨믹 씨가 내게 말했다."]], [["“No,”", "\"네.\""], ["said I.", "내가 말했다."]], [["“I was new here once,”", "\"나도 한때 여기가 낯설었지요.\""], ["said Mr. Wemmick.", "웨믹 씨가 말했다."], ["“Rum to think of now!”", "\"지금 생각하면 우습소만!\""]], [["“You are well acquainted with it now?”", "\"이제는 이곳을 잘 아시겠군요?\""]], [["“Why, yes,”", "\"뭐, 그렇소.\""], ["said Mr. Wemmick.", "웨믹 씨가 말했다."], ["“I know the moves of it.”", "\"이곳이 어떻게 돌아가는지는 압니다.\""]], [["“Is it a very wicked place?”", "\"몹시 사악한 곳인가요?\""], ["I asked, more for the sake of saying something than for information.", "나는 무얼 알고자 함보다 그저 무슨 말이든 하려고 물었다."]], [["“You may get cheated, robbed, and murdered in London.", "\"런던에선 속고, 털리고, 살해당할 수 있소."], ["But there are plenty of people anywhere, who’ll do that for you.”", "하지만 그런 짓을 당신한테 해 줄 사람이야 어디에든 잔뜩 있지요.\""]], [["“If there is bad blood between you and them,”", "\"당신과 그들 사이에 나쁜 감정이 있다면 말이지요.\""], ["said I, to soften it off a little.", "나는 그 말을 조금 누그러뜨려 보려고 말했다."]], [["“O! I don’t know about bad blood,”", "\"오! 나쁜 감정 같은 건 모르겠소.\""], ["returned Mr. Wemmick; “there’s not much bad blood about.", "웨믹 씨가 대꾸했다. \"나쁜 감정이랄 게 그리 많지도 않고."], ["They’ll do it, if there’s anything to be got by it.”", "거기서 얻을 게 뭐라도 있으면 그자들은 해치우는 거요.\""]], [["“That makes it worse.”", "\"그게 더 고약하군요.\""]], [["“You think so?”", "\"그렇게 생각하시오?\""], ["returned Mr. Wemmick.", "웨믹 씨가 대꾸했다."], ["“Much about the same, I should say.”", "\"거기서 거기라고 봅니다만.\""]], [["He wore his hat on the back of his head, and looked straight before him: walking in a self-contained way as if there were nothing in the streets to claim his attention.", "그는 모자를 뒤통수에 얹은 채 똑바로 앞만 바라보았다. 거리에 자기 주의를 끌 만한 것이라곤 아무것도 없다는 듯, 자족적인 태도로 걸어갔다."], ["His mouth was such a post-office of a mouth that he had a mechanical appearance of smiling.", "그의 입은 그야말로 우체통 같은 입이어서, 기계적으로 미소 짓는 듯한 인상을 풍겼다."], ["We had got to the top of Holborn Hill before I knew that it was merely a mechanical appearance, and that he was not smiling at all.", "그것이 그저 기계적인 인상일 뿐이며 그가 전혀 미소 짓고 있는 게 아니라는 걸 내가 깨달은 것은, 우리가 홀번 언덕 꼭대기에 다다른 뒤였다."]], [["“Do you know where Mr. Matthew Pocket lives?”", "\"매슈 포켓 씨가 어디 사시는지 아세요?\""], ["I asked Mr. Wemmick.", "나는 웨믹 씨에게 물었다."]], [["“Yes,” said he, nodding in the direction.", "\"알지요.\" 그가 그 방향으로 고개를 끄덕이며 말했다."], ["“At Hammersmith, west of London.”", "\"런던 서쪽, 해머스미스에 살고 있소.\""]], [["“Is that far?”", "\"먼가요?\""]], [["“Well!", "\"글쎄!"], ["Say five miles.”", "오 마일쯤 된다고 합시다.\""]], [["“Do you know him?”", "\"그분을 아세요?\""]], [["“Why, you’re a regular cross-examiner!”", "\"이런, 당신은 영락없는 반대 심문가로구려!\""], ["said Mr. Wemmick, looking at me with an approving air.", "웨믹 씨가 흡족하다는 듯한 표정으로 나를 바라보며 말했다."], ["“Yes, I know him. I know him!”", "\"그렇소, 그를 알지요. 내가 그를 안단 말이오!\""]], [["There was an air of toleration or depreciation about his utterance of these words that rather depressed me; and I was still looking sideways at his block of a face in search of any encouraging note to the text, when he said here we were at Barnard’s Inn.", "이 말을 내뱉는 그의 어조에는 너그러운 묵인 같기도 하고 깎아내림 같기도 한 기색이 깃들어 있어, 나는 오히려 풀이 죽었다. 그래서 나는 그 본문에 붙은 격려의 주석이라도 찾으려는 듯 여전히 그의 토막 같은 얼굴을 곁눈질하고 있었는데, 그때 그가 여기가 바너드 여관이라고 말했다."], ["My depression was not alleviated by the announcement, for, I had supposed that establishment to be an hotel kept by Mr. Barnard, to which the Blue Boar in our town was a mere public-house.", "그 통고에도 내 우울함은 가시지 않았다. 나는 그것이 바너드 씨가 운영하는 호텔이며, 우리 고장의 블루 보어 따위는 거기에 비하면 한낱 선술집에 불과하리라고 짐작했던 것이다."], ["Whereas I now found Barnard to be a disembodied spirit, or a fiction, and his inn the dingiest collection of shabby buildings ever squeezed together in a rank corner as a club for Tom-cats.", "그런데 이제 와 보니 바너드란 형체 없는 영혼이거나 허구의 인물이었고, 그 여관이란 도둑고양이들의 사교 클럽으로나 쓰일 법하게 음습한 구석에 다닥다닥 비좁게 쑤셔 넣은, 이제껏 본 중 가장 칙칙한 누추한 건물들의 집합이었다."]], [["We entered this haven through a wicket-gate, and were disgorged by an introductory passage into a melancholy little square that looked to me like a flat burying-ground.", "우리는 쪽문을 지나 이 안식처로 들어섰고, 입구의 통로를 거쳐 평평한 묘지처럼 보이는 침울한 작은 광장으로 토해졌다."], ["I thought it had the most dismal trees in it, and the most dismal sparrows, and the most dismal cats, and the most dismal houses (in number half a dozen or so), that I had ever seen.", "거기에는 내가 이제껏 본 가운데 가장 음울한 나무들과, 가장 음울한 참새들과, 가장 음울한 고양이들과, 가장 음울한 집들이(그 수는 여섯 채 남짓이었다) 있는 것 같았다."], ["I thought the windows of the sets of chambers into which those houses were divided were in every stage of dilapidated blind and curtain, crippled flower-pot, cracked glass, dusty decay, and miserable makeshift; while To Let, To Let, To Let, glared at me from empty rooms, as if no new wretches ever came there, and the vengeance of the soul of Barnard were being slowly appeased by the gradual suicide of the present occupants and their unholy interment under the gravel.", "그 집들이 나뉘어 이룬 셋방들의 창문은 하나같이, 다 망가진 블라인드와 커튼, 다리 부러진 화분, 금 간 유리, 먼지 앉은 쇠락, 비참한 임시변통의 온갖 단계를 보여 주는 듯했다. 한편 텅 빈 방들에서는 셋방 있음, 셋방 있음, 셋방 있음이라는 글자가 나를 향해 번득였는데, 마치 새로운 가련한 자들이 결코 다시는 그곳에 들지 않을 것이며, 바너드의 영혼이 품은 복수심이 지금 세든 자들의 점진적인 자살과 자갈 밑에 묻히는 불경한 매장으로 서서히 달래지고 있기라도 한 듯했다."], ["A frowzy mourning of soot and smoke attired this forlorn creation of Barnard, and it had strewn ashes on its head, and was undergoing penance and humiliation as a mere dust-hole.", "그을음과 매연으로 이루어진 꾀죄죄한 상복이 이 처량한 바너드의 피조물을 휘감고 있었고, 그것은 제 머리에 재를 뿌린 채, 한낱 쓰레기 구덩이로서 속죄와 굴욕을 치르고 있는 셈이었다."], ["Thus far my sense of sight; while dry rot and wet rot and all the silent rots that rot in neglected roof and cellar,—rot of rat and mouse and bug and coaching-stables near at hand besides—addressed themselves faintly to my sense of smell, and moaned, “Try Barnard’s Mixture.”", "여기까지가 내 시각에 호소해 온 것이었고, 한편으로는 마른 부패와 젖은 부패, 그리고 방치된 지붕과 지하실에서 소리 없이 썩어 가는 온갖 부패 — 게다가 쥐와 생쥐와 벌레의 부패, 그리고 가까이 있는 역마차 마구간의 부패까지 — 가 내 후각에 어렴풋이 말을 걸어 오며 신음했다. \"바너드표 혼합 향수를 써 보시지.\""]], [["So imperfect was this realisation of the first of my great expectations, that I looked in dismay at Mr.", "내 위대한 유산의 첫 실현이 이토록 형편없었던 까닭에, 나는 낭패스러운 표정으로 웨믹 씨를 바라보았다. \"아!\""], ["Wemmick. “Ah!” said he, mistaking me; “the retirement reminds you of the country.", "그가 나를 오해하며 말했다. \"이 한적함이 시골을 떠올리게 하나 보군."], ["So it does me.”", "나도 그렇소.\""]], [["He led me into a corner and conducted me up a flight of stairs,—which appeared to me to be slowly collapsing into sawdust, so that one of those days the upper lodgers would look out at their doors and find themselves without the means of coming down,—to a set of chambers on the top floor. MR.", "그는 나를 한쪽 구석으로 이끌어 한 층계 위로 안내했는데 — 그 층계는 내게 천천히 톱밥으로 무너져 내리는 것처럼 보여, 머지않은 어느 날 위층 세입자들이 제 방문 밖을 내다보면 내려올 방도가 사라져 있으리라 싶었다 — 꼭대기 층의 한 셋방으로 데려갔다."], ["POCKET, JUN., was painted on the door, and there was a label on the letter-box, “Return shortly.”", "문에는 소(小)포켓 씨라고 칠해져 있었고, 우편함에는 \"곧 돌아옴\"이라는 쪽지가 붙어 있었다."]], [["“He hardly thought you’d come so soon,” Mr.", "\"당신이 이렇게 일찍 오리라곤 미처 생각 못 했나 보오.\""], ["Wemmick explained.", "웨믹 씨가 설명했다."], ["“You don’t want me any more?”", "\"나한테 더는 볼일이 없겠지요?\""]], [["“No, thank you,”", "\"네, 고맙습니다.\""], ["said I.", "내가 말했다."]], [["“As I keep the cash,” Mr.", "\"돈은 내가 관리하니까,\""], ["Wemmick observed, “we shall most likely meet pretty often.", "웨믹 씨가 말했다. \"우린 아마 꽤 자주 만나게 될 거요."], ["Good day.”", "그럼 안녕히.\""]], [["“Good day.”", "\"안녕히 가세요.\""]], [["I put out my hand, and Mr. Wemmick at first looked at it as if he thought I wanted something.", "내가 손을 내밀자, 웨믹 씨는 처음엔 내가 무얼 바라기라도 하는 줄 아는 듯 그 손을 바라보았다."], ["Then he looked at me, and said, correcting himself,—", "그러더니 나를 보고는, 스스로 고쳐 말했다."]], [["“To be sure! Yes. You’re in the habit of shaking hands?”", "\"아, 그렇지! 그래요. 당신은 악수하는 습관이 있구려?\""]], [["I was rather confused, thinking it must be out of the London fashion, but said yes.", "나는 그것이 런던의 풍습에 어긋나는 게 아닐까 싶어 다소 당황했으나, 그렇다고 대답했다."]], [["“I have got so out of it!” said Mr.", "\"나는 그 습관에서 어찌나 멀어졌던지!\""], ["Wemmick,—“except at last.", "웨믹 씨가 말했다."], ["Very glad, I’m sure, to make your acquaintance.", "\"마지막 순간이 아니고서는 말이오. 알게 되어 정말 반갑소이다."], ["Good day!”", "그럼 안녕히!\""]], [["When we had shaken hands and he was gone, I opened the staircase window and had nearly beheaded myself, for, the lines had rotted away, and it came down like the guillotine.", "우리가 악수를 나누고 그가 떠난 뒤, 나는 층계 창문을 열었다가 하마터면 내 목이 잘릴 뻔했다. 창틀 줄이 다 삭아 버려, 창문이 단두대처럼 떨어져 내렸던 것이다."], ["Happily it was so quick that I had not put my head out.", "다행히도 워낙 순식간이라 미처 머리를 내밀지 않은 참이었다."], ["After this escape, I was content to take a foggy view of the Inn through the window’s encrusting dirt, and to stand dolefully looking out, saying to myself that London was decidedly overrated.", "이 위기를 모면한 뒤, 나는 창문에 더께처럼 낀 때 너머로 여관을 안개 낀 듯 흐릿하게 바라보는 것으로 만족하며, 런던은 정녕 과대평가된 곳이라고 혼잣말을 하며 시무룩하게 서서 밖을 내다보았다."]], [["Mr. Pocket, Junior’s, idea of Shortly was not mine, for I had nearly maddened myself with looking out for half an hour, and had written my name with my finger several times in the dirt of every pane in the window, before I heard footsteps on the stairs.", "소포켓 씨가 생각하는 \"곧\"은 내가 생각하는 \"곧\"이 아니었다. 나는 반 시간이나 밖을 내다보며 거의 미칠 지경이 되었고, 창문의 모든 유리에 낀 때 위에 손가락으로 내 이름을 몇 번이나 써 본 뒤에야 비로소 층계에서 발소리를 들었으니까."], ["Gradually there arose before me the hat, head, neckcloth, waistcoat, trousers, boots, of a member of society of about my own standing.", "차츰 내 앞에 모습을 드러낸 것은, 나와 엇비슷한 처지인 한 사교계 일원의 모자와 머리, 목도리, 조끼, 바지, 장화였다."], ["He had a paper-bag under each arm and a pottle of strawberries in one hand, and was out of breath.", "그는 양쪽 겨드랑이에 종이봉투를 하나씩 끼고 한 손에는 딸기 한 바구니를 든 채 숨을 헐떡이고 있었다."]], [["“Mr. Pip?”", "\"핍 씨인가요?\""], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."]], [["“Mr. Pocket?”", "\"포켓 씨인가요?\""], ["said I.", "내가 말했다."]], [["“Dear me!”", "\"이런!\""], ["he exclaimed.", "그가 소리쳤다."], ["“I am extremely sorry; but I knew there was a coach from your part of the country at midday, and I thought you would come by that one.", "\"정말 죄송합니다. 다만 당신 고향 쪽에서 정오에 떠나는 마차가 있다는 걸 알고 있어서, 그걸 타고 오시리라 생각했지 뭡니까."], ["The fact is, I have been out on your account,—not that that is any excuse,—for I thought, coming from the country, you might like a little fruit after dinner, and I went to Covent Garden Market to get it good.”", "실은 당신을 위해 나가 있었던 겁니다 — 그렇다고 변명이 되는 건 아니지만 — 시골에서 오셨으니 저녁 식사 후에 과일을 좀 드시고 싶을지도 모른다는 생각이 들어, 좋은 걸로 구하려고 코번트 가든 시장에 다녀온 거지요.\""]], [["For a reason that I had, I felt as if my eyes would start out of my head.", "나로서는 그럴 만한 까닭이 있어, 마치 두 눈이 머리에서 튀어나올 것만 같았다."], ["I acknowledged his attention incoherently, and began to think this was a dream.", "나는 두서없이 그의 배려에 감사를 표하면서, 이게 꿈이 아닌가 생각하기 시작했다."]], [["“Dear me!”", "\"이런!\""], ["said Mr. Pocket, Junior.", "소포켓 씨가 말했다."], ["“This door sticks so!”", "\"이 문이 어찌나 뻑뻑한지!\""]], [["As he was fast making jam of his fruit by wrestling with the door while the paper-bags were under his arms, I begged him to allow me to hold them.", "그가 양 겨드랑이에 종이봉투를 낀 채 문과 씨름하느라 제 과일을 빠르게 잼으로 만들고 있는 통에, 나는 그 봉투들을 내가 들어 주게 해 달라고 청했다."], ["He relinquished them with an agreeable smile, and combated with the door as if it were a wild beast.", "그는 상냥한 미소를 지으며 봉투를 넘겨주고는, 문을 마치 야수라도 되는 양 상대로 싸웠다."], ["It yielded so suddenly at last, that he staggered back upon me, and I staggered back upon the opposite door, and we both laughed.", "마침내 문이 어찌나 갑작스레 열렸던지, 그는 내 쪽으로 비틀거리며 넘어졌고, 나는 또 맞은편 문 쪽으로 비틀거리며 넘어졌으며, 우리 둘 다 웃음을 터뜨렸다."], ["But still I felt as if my eyes must start out of my head, and as if this must be a dream.", "그러나 나는 여전히 두 눈이 머리에서 튀어나올 것만 같았고, 이게 꿈만 같았다."]], [["“Pray come in,” said Mr.", "\"자, 들어오십시오.\""], ["Pocket, Junior. “Allow me to lead the way.", "소포켓 씨가 말했다. \"제가 앞장서지요."], ["I am rather bare here, but I hope you’ll be able to make out tolerably well till Monday.", "이곳이 좀 휑하긴 합니다만, 월요일까지는 그럭저럭 견디실 수 있으리라 봅니다."], ["My father thought you would get on more agreeably through to-morrow with me than with him, and might like to take a walk about London.", "제 아버지께서는 당신이 내일 하루는 아버지보다 저와 함께 보내는 편이 더 즐거울 거라고, 또 런던 구경을 다니고 싶어 할지도 모른다고 생각하셨거든요."], ["I am sure I shall be very happy to show London to you.", "저야 당신께 런던을 보여 드릴 수 있다면 더없이 기쁘지요."], ["As to our table, you won’t find that bad, I hope, for it will be supplied from our coffee-house here, and (it is only right I should add) at your expense, such being Mr.", "우리 식탁으로 말하자면, 그리 나쁘진 않으리라 봅니다. 여기 우리 커피점에서 들여올 테니까요. 그리고 (이건 덧붙여 말해 두는 게 옳겠습니다만) 비용은 당신 부담이지요."], ["Jaggers’s directions.", "재거스 씨의 지시가 그러하니 말입니다."], ["As to our lodging, it’s not by any means splendid, because I have my own bread to earn, and my father hasn’t anything to give me, and I shouldn’t be willing to take it, if he had.", "우리 숙소로 말하자면 결코 으리으리하다고는 할 수 없습니다. 저는 제 빵벌이를 제 손으로 해야 하고, 아버지께선 제게 주실 게 아무것도 없으며, 설령 있다 한들 저는 받고 싶지도 않으니까요."], ["This is our sitting-room,—just such chairs and tables and carpet and so forth, you see, as they could spare from home.", "여기가 우리 거실입니다 — 보시다시피 의자며 탁자며 양탄자 따위가, 집에서 떼어 줄 수 있을 만한 것들로 갖춰져 있지요."], ["You mustn’t give me credit for the tablecloth and spoons and castors, because they come for you from the coffee-house.", "식탁보와 숟가락과 양념병만큼은 제 공으로 치지 마십시오. 그것들은 당신을 위해 커피점에서 오는 것이니까요. 여기가 제 작은 침실입니다."], ["This is my little bedroom; rather musty, but Barnard’s is musty.", "좀 퀴퀴하지요, 하지만 바너드 여관이라는 데가 본디 퀴퀴하니까요."], ["This is your bedroom; the furniture’s hired for the occasion, but I trust it will answer the purpose; if you should want anything, I’ll go and fetch it.", "여기가 당신 침실입니다. 가구는 이번 일을 위해 빌린 것이지만, 쓰임새에는 맞으리라 믿습니다. 혹 필요한 게 있으면 제가 가서 가져오지요."], ["The chambers are retired, and we shall be alone together, but we shan’t fight, I dare say.", "이 셋방은 한적해서 우리 둘만 함께 지내게 될 텐데, 다투지는 않겠지요, 아마."], ["But dear me, I beg your pardon, you’re holding the fruit all this time.", "아, 이런, 실례했습니다. 당신이 여태 과일을 들고 계셨군요."], ["Pray let me take these bags from you.", "부디 그 봉투들을 제가 받게 해 주십시오."], ["I am quite ashamed.”", "정말 부끄럽습니다.\""]], [["As I stood opposite to Mr. Pocket, Junior, delivering him the bags, One, Two, I saw the starting appearance come into his own eyes that I knew to be in mine, and he said, falling back,—", "내가 소포켓 씨와 마주 서서 그에게 봉투를 하나, 둘 건네는 동안, 나는 내 눈에 떠올라 있는 줄로 알고 있던 그 튀어나올 듯한 기색이 그의 눈에도 떠오르는 것을 보았고, 그는 뒤로 물러서며 말했다."]], [["“Lord bless me, you’re the prowling boy!”", "\"맙소사, 당신은 그때 그 어슬렁거리던 녀석이로군요!\""]], [["“And you,”", "\"그리고 당신은,\""], ["said I, “are the pale young gentleman!”", "내가 말했다. \"그 창백한 젊은 신사고요!\""]], [["# Chapter XXII.", "# Chapter XXII."]], [["The pale young gentleman and I stood contemplating one another in Barnard’s Inn, until we both burst out laughing.", "창백한 얼굴의 젊은 신사와 나는 바너드 여관에서 서로를 마주 바라보며 서 있다가, 마침내 둘 다 웃음을 터뜨리고 말았다."], ["“The idea of its being you!” said he.", "\"그게 너였다니!\" 그가 말했다."], ["“The idea of its being you!” said I.", "\"그게 바로 너였다니!\" 내가 말했다."], ["And then we contemplated one another afresh, and laughed again. “Well!”", "그러고는 우리는 다시 서로를 바라보았고, 또 한 번 웃었다. \"자!\""], ["said the pale young gentleman, reaching out his hand good-humouredly, “it’s all over now, I hope, and it will be magnanimous in you if you’ll forgive me for having knocked you about so.”", "창백한 얼굴의 젊은 신사가 사람 좋게 손을 내밀며 말했다. \"이제 다 끝난 일이겠지, 그러길 바라네. 내가 자네를 그렇게 마구 두들겨 팼던 걸 용서해 준다면 그건 자네가 아주 너그러운 사람이라는 증거일 거야.\""]], [["I derived from this speech that Mr. Herbert Pocket (for Herbert was the pale young gentleman’s name) still rather confounded his intention with his execution.", "이 말로 미루어 나는 허버트 포켓 씨가(허버트란 창백한 얼굴의 젊은 신사 이름이었다) 여전히 자기 의도와 실행을 다소 혼동하고 있음을 알아차렸다."], ["But I made a modest reply, and we shook hands warmly.", "그러나 나는 겸손하게 대답했고, 우리는 따뜻하게 악수를 나누었다."]], [["“You hadn’t come into your good fortune at that time?”", "\"그때는 자네 행운이 아직 굴러들어오지 않았을 때였지?\""], ["said Herbert Pocket.", "허버트 포켓이 말했다."]], [["“No,”", "\"그래.\""], ["said I.", "내가 말했다."]], [["“No,”", "\"맞아.\""], ["he acquiesced: “I heard it had happened very lately.", "그가 수긍했다. \"아주 최근에 일어난 일이라고 들었네."], ["I was rather on the lookout for good fortune then.”", "나야말로 그 무렵 행운을 좀 노리고 있던 참이었지.\""]], [["“Indeed?”", "\"그래?\""]], [["“Yes.", "\"그래."], ["Miss Havisham had sent for me, to see if she could take a fancy to me.", "해비셤 양이 나를 마음에 들어 할 수 있을지 보겠다고 나를 부른 거였어."], ["But she couldn’t,—at all events, she didn’t.”", "하지만 그분은 그러질 못했지 — 적어도, 그러지 않았어.\""]], [["I thought it polite to remark that I was surprised to hear that.", "나는 그 말을 들으니 놀랍다고 한마디 하는 게 예의겠다 싶었다."]], [["“Bad taste,” said Herbert, laughing, “but a fact.", "\"안목이 형편없는 거지.\" 허버트가 웃으며 말했다."], ["Yes, she had sent for me on a trial visit, and if I had come out of it successfully, I suppose I should have been provided for; perhaps I should have been what-you-may-called it to Estella.”", "\"하지만 사실이 그래. 그래, 그분은 나를 시험 삼아 한번 방문하라고 불렀던 거고, 만약 내가 그걸 무사히 통과했더라면 아마 살길이 마련됐을 거야. 어쩌면 에스텔라하고 그 뭐시기가 됐을지도 모르지.\""]], [["“What’s that?”", "\"그게 뭔데?\""], ["I asked, with sudden gravity.", "나는 갑자기 진지해져서 물었다."]], [["He was arranging his fruit in plates while we talked, which divided his attention, and was the cause of his having made this lapse of a word.", "그는 우리가 이야기하는 동안 과일을 접시에 담고 있었는데, 그 일에 정신이 팔린 탓에 그렇게 말을 더듬은 것이었다. \"약혼한 사이 말이야.\""], ["“Affianced,” he explained, still busy with the fruit.", "그는 여전히 과일을 매만지며 설명했다. \"정혼한 사이."], ["“Betrothed. Engaged.", "혼약을 맺은 사이."], ["What’s-his-named. Any word of that sort.”", "그 뭐시기. 뭐 그런 종류의 단어 말이야.\""]], [["“How did you bear your disappointment?” I asked.", "\"그 실망을 어떻게 견뎠나?\" 내가 물었다."]], [["“Pooh!”", "\"흥!\""], ["said he, “I didn’t care much for it.", "그가 말했다. \"별로 신경 쓰지 않았어."], ["She’s a Tartar.”", "그 여자는 사나운 말괄량이거든.\""]], [["“Miss Havisham?”", "\"해비셤 양이?\""]], [["“I don’t say no to that, but I meant Estella. That girl’s hard and haughty and capricious to the last degree, and has been brought up by Miss Havisham to wreak revenge on all the male sex.”", "\"그것도 아니라곤 안 하겠네만, 내가 말한 건 에스텔라야. 그 아이는 더할 나위 없이 냉정하고 오만하고 변덕스러운데, 해비셤 양이 모든 남성에게 복수를 퍼붓도록 키워 놓은 거지.\""]], [["“What relation is she to Miss Havisham?”", "\"그 아이가 해비셤 양하고는 어떤 관계인데?\""]], [["“None,” said he.", "\"아무 관계도 아니야.\" 그가 말했다."], ["“Only adopted.”", "\"그저 양녀일 뿐이지.\""]], [["“Why should she wreak revenge on all the male sex?", "\"왜 모든 남성에게 복수를 퍼부어야 하는데?"], ["What revenge?”", "무슨 복수?\""]], [["“Lord, Mr. Pip!”", "\"아니, 핍 씨!\""], ["said he. “Don’t you know?”", "그가 말했다. \"자네 모르나?\""]], [["“No,”", "\"몰라.\""], ["said I.", "내가 말했다."]], [["“Dear me!", "\"이런!"], ["It’s quite a story, and shall be saved till dinner-time.", "그건 제법 긴 이야기라, 저녁 식사 때까지 아껴 두기로 하지."], ["And now let me take the liberty of asking you a question.", "그건 그렇고, 이제 내가 외람되게 한 가지 물어봐도 되겠나."], ["How did you come there, that day?”", "그날 자네는 어쩌다 거기 오게 됐나?\""]], [["I told him, and he was attentive until I had finished, and then burst out laughing again, and asked me if I was sore afterwards?", "나는 그에게 사정을 이야기했고, 그는 내가 다 마칠 때까지 귀 기울여 듣더니, 다시 웃음을 터뜨리며 그 후로 아프지 않았느냐고 물었다."], ["I didn’t ask him if he was, for my conviction on that point was perfectly established.", "나는 그에게 너야말로 아프지 않았느냐고 묻지 않았는데, 그 점에 관해서라면 내 확신이 더없이 확고하게 서 있었기 때문이다."]], [["“Mr. Jaggers is your guardian, I understand?”", "\"재거스 씨가 자네 후견인이라고 알고 있네만?\""], ["he went on.", "그가 말을 이었다."]], [["“Yes.”", "\"그래.\""]], [["“You know he is Miss Havisham’s man of business and solicitor, and has her confidence when nobody else has?”", "\"그분이 해비셤 양의 사무 대리인이자 변호사이고, 그 누구도 받지 못하는 그분의 신임을 받고 있다는 건 알지?\""]], [["This was bringing me (I felt) towards dangerous ground.", "이건 나를(나는 그렇게 느꼈다) 위험한 영역으로 끌어가는 것이었다."], ["I answered with a constraint I made no attempt to disguise, that I had seen Mr. Jaggers in Miss Havisham’s house on the very day of our combat, but never at any other time, and that I believed he had no recollection of having ever seen me there.", "나는 굳이 감추려 들지 않은 어색함을 드러내며 대답하기를, 우리가 한판 붙던 바로 그날 해비셤 양의 집에서 재거스 씨를 본 적은 있으나 그 밖의 어떤 때에도 본 적이 없으며, 그분은 거기서 나를 봤다는 사실을 전혀 기억하지 못하리라고 믿는다고 했다."]], [["“He was so obliging as to suggest my father for your tutor, and he called on my father to propose it.", "\"그분이 친절을 베풀어 우리 아버지를 자네 가정교사로 추천했고, 그걸 제안하려고 우리 아버지를 찾아오신 거야."], ["Of course he knew about my father from his connection with Miss Havisham.", "물론 그분은 해비셤 양과의 인연을 통해 우리 아버지에 대해 알고 있었지."], ["My father is Miss Havisham’s cousin; not that that implies familiar intercourse between them, for he is a bad courtier and will not propitiate her.”", "우리 아버지는 해비셤 양의 사촌이야. 그렇다고 두 분 사이에 허물없는 왕래가 있다는 건 아니야. 아버지는 비위를 맞추는 데 서투른 분이라 그분의 환심을 사려 들지 않거든.\""]], [["Herbert Pocket had a frank and easy way with him that was very taking.", "허버트 포켓에게는 솔직하고 편안한 태도가 있어, 그것이 무척 호감을 자아냈다."], ["I had never seen any one then, and I have never seen any one since, who more strongly expressed to me, in every look and tone, a natural incapacity to do anything secret and mean.", "그 무렵에도, 또 그 후로도, 나는 그처럼 모든 눈빛과 말투에서 무언가 은밀하고 비열한 짓이라곤 천성적으로 할 줄 모른다는 것을 내게 그토록 강하게 드러내 보인 사람을 본 적이 없다."], ["There was something wonderfully hopeful about his general air, and something that at the same time whispered to me he would never be very successful or rich.", "그의 전반적인 분위기에는 놀랍도록 희망에 찬 무언가가 있었으나, 동시에 그가 결코 크게 성공하거나 부유해지지는 못하리라고 내게 속삭이는 무언가도 있었다."], ["I don’t know how this was.", "어찌하여 그러했는지는 모르겠다."], ["I became imbued with the notion on that first occasion before we sat down to dinner, but I cannot define by what means.", "나는 그 첫 만남에서, 저녁 식사 자리에 앉기도 전에 그런 생각에 젖어들었으나, 무엇 때문에 그랬는지는 딱히 짚어 말할 수가 없다."]], [["He was still a pale young gentleman, and had a certain conquered languor about him in the midst of his spirits and briskness, that did not seem indicative of natural strength.", "그는 여전히 창백한 얼굴의 젊은 신사였고, 활기와 민첩함 한가운데서도 어딘가 패배에 길든 듯한 나른함을 지니고 있었는데, 그것이 타고난 강건함을 드러내 보이는 것 같지는 않았다."], ["He had not a handsome face, but it was better than handsome: being extremely amiable and cheerful.", "잘생긴 얼굴은 아니었으나, 잘생긴 것보다 나았다. 더없이 상냥하고 쾌활한 얼굴이었으니까."], ["His figure was a little ungainly, as in the days when my knuckles had taken such liberties with it, but it looked as if it would always be light and young.", "그의 몸매는, 내 주먹이 그토록 함부로 굴었던 그 시절과 마찬가지로 조금 볼품없었으나, 언제까지나 가뿐하고 젊어 보일 것만 같았다."], ["Whether Mr. Trabb’s local work would have sat more gracefully on him than on me, may be a question; but I am conscious that he carried off his rather old clothes much better than I carried off my new suit.", "트래브 씨의 시골 솜씨로 지은 옷이 나보다 그에게 더 우아하게 어울렸을지는 의문이지만, 그가 다소 낡은 옷을 입고도 내가 새 양복을 입은 것보다 훨씬 더 멋스럽게 소화해 냈다는 것만은 내가 인정하는 바이다."]], [["As he was so communicative, I felt that reserve on my part would be a bad return unsuited to our years.", "그가 그토록 거리낌 없이 털어놓는 터라, 나는 내 쪽에서 입을 닫고 있는 것이 우리 나이에 어울리지 않는 못된 보답이 되겠다 싶었다."], ["I therefore told him my small story, and laid stress on my being forbidden to inquire who my benefactor was.", "그래서 나는 그에게 내 변변찮은 사연을 들려주었고, 내 은인이 누구인지 캐묻는 것이 금지되어 있다는 점을 강조했다."], ["I further mentioned that as I had been brought up a blacksmith in a country place, and knew very little of the ways of politeness, I would take it as a great kindness in him if he would give me a hint whenever he saw me at a loss or going wrong.", "나아가 나는 시골에서 대장장이로 자랐기에 예의범절에 대해 아는 것이 거의 없으니, 내가 어쩔 줄 모르거나 잘못하는 것을 볼 때마다 그가 귀띔을 해 준다면 그것을 큰 친절로 받아들이겠다고 덧붙였다."]], [["“With pleasure,”", "\"기꺼이 그러지.\""], ["said he, “though I venture to prophesy that you’ll want very few hints.", "그가 말했다. \"하지만 자네에게 귀띔이 필요할 일은 거의 없을 거라고 감히 예언하네."], ["I dare say we shall be often together, and I should like to banish any needless restraint between us.", "우리는 앞으로 자주 함께 지내게 될 테고, 나는 우리 사이에 쓸데없는 거북함은 다 없애 버리고 싶네."], ["Will you do me the favour to begin at once to call me by my Christian name, Herbert?”", "부디 지금 당장 나를 세례명인 허버트로 불러 주는 호의를 베풀어 주겠나?\""]], [["I thanked him and said I would. I informed him in exchange that my Christian name was Philip.", "나는 그에게 고맙다고 하며 그러겠다고 했다. 그 답례로 나는 내 세례명이 필립이라고 알려 주었다."]], [["“I don’t take to Philip,”", "\"필립은 마음에 안 드는데.\""], ["said he, smiling, “for it sounds like a moral boy out of the spelling-book, who was so lazy that he fell into a pond, or so fat that he couldn’t see out of his eyes, or so avaricious that he locked up his cake till the mice ate it, or so determined to go a bird’s-nesting that he got himself eaten by bears who lived handy in the neighbourhood.", "그가 미소 지으며 말했다. \"그건 철자 교본에 나오는 무슨 교훈조의 사내아이 이름 같단 말이야. 너무 게을러서 연못에 빠졌다든가, 너무 뚱뚱해서 제 눈으로 앞을 못 봤다든가, 너무 욕심이 많아서 케이크를 쥐가 다 갉아먹을 때까지 잠가 두었다든가, 새 둥지를 털겠다고 어찌나 작정을 했던지 마침 근처에 살던 곰들한테 잡아먹혔다든가 하는 그런 아이 말이지."], ["I tell you what I should like.", "내가 어떻게 부르고 싶은지 말해 줄게."], ["We are so harmonious, and you have been a blacksmith,—would you mind it?”", "우리는 이렇게 잘 어울리고, 자네는 대장장이였잖아 — 괜찮겠나?\""]], [["“I shouldn’t mind anything that you propose,”", "\"자네가 제안하는 거라면 뭐든 상관없네.\""], ["I answered, “but I don’t understand you.”", "내가 대답했다. \"하지만 무슨 말인지 모르겠는데.\""]], [["“Would you mind Handel for a familiar name?", "\"헨델이라고 친근하게 부르면 싫겠나?"], ["There’s a charming piece of music by Handel, called the Harmonious Blacksmith.”", "헨델이 작곡한 멋진 곡 중에 「쾌활한 대장장이」라는 게 있거든.\""]], [["“I should like it very much.”", "\"아주 좋네.\""]], [["“Then, my dear Handel,”", "\"그럼, 친애하는 헨델,\" 그가 문이 열리자 몸을 돌리며 말했다."], ["said he, turning round as the door opened, “here is the dinner, and I must beg of you to take the top of the table, because the dinner is of your providing.”", "\"여기 저녁 식사가 왔네. 그리고 자네에게 상석에 앉아 주기를 청해야겠어. 이 식사는 자네가 마련한 것이니까.\""]], [["This I would not hear of, so he took the top, and I faced him. It was a nice little dinner,—seemed to me then a very Lord Mayor’s Feast,—and it acquired additional relish from being eaten under those independent circumstances, with no old people by, and with London all around us.", "이건 내가 도무지 들어줄 수 없는 일이었으므로, 그가 상석에 앉고 내가 그 맞은편에 앉았다. 그것은 자그마한 멋진 식사였는데 — 그때 내게는 마치 런던 시장의 연회처럼 여겨졌다 — 곁에 노인네 하나 없고 사방이 온통 런던인 그런 자유로운 상황에서 먹는다는 사실 덕분에 한층 더 맛깔스럽게 느껴졌다."], ["This again was heightened by a certain gypsy character that set the banquet off; for while the table was, as Mr.", "게다가 그 잔치를 돋보이게 하는 어딘가 집시 같은 정취가 그 맛을 더욱 북돋아 주었다."], ["Pumblechook might have said, the lap of luxury,—being entirely furnished forth from the coffee-house,—the circumjacent region of sitting-room was of a comparatively pastureless and shifty character; imposing on the waiter the wandering habits of putting the covers on the floor (where he fell over them), the melted butter in the arm-chair, the bread on the bookshelves, the cheese in the coal-scuttle, and the boiled fowl into my bed in the next room,—where I found much of its parsley and butter in a state of congelation when I retired for the night.", "식탁은, 펌블추크 씨라면 호사의 극치라고 했을 법하게 — 온통 커피점에서 가져온 것들로 차려졌으나 — 그 둘레의 거실 영역은 비교적 황량하고 안정감 없는 분위기였던 까닭에, 시중드는 사람에게 이리저리 떠도는 버릇을 강요했으니, 그는 접시 덮개를 바닥에 놓고(그러다 거기에 걸려 넘어지고), 녹인 버터를 안락의자에 놓고, 빵을 책장에 놓고, 치즈를 석탄통에 놓고, 삶은 닭고기를 옆방 내 침대에 놓아두었던 것이다 — 밤에 잠자리에 들 때 보니 거기 곁들여 둔 파슬리와 버터가 상당히 굳어 있는 것을 발견했다."], ["All this made the feast delightful, and when the waiter was not there to watch me, my pleasure was without alloy.", "이 모든 것이 그 잔치를 즐겁게 만들었고, 시중꾼이 나를 지켜보러 곁에 있지 않을 때면 내 즐거움은 한 점 흠도 없이 온전했다."]], [["We had made some progress in the dinner, when I reminded Herbert of his promise to tell me about Miss Havisham.", "저녁 식사가 어지간히 진행되었을 무렵, 나는 허버트에게 해비셤 양에 관해 이야기해 주기로 한 약속을 일깨워 주었다."]], [["“True,”", "\"맞아.\""], ["he replied.", "그가 대꾸했다."], ["“I’ll redeem it at once.", "\"당장 그 약속을 지키지."], ["Let me introduce the topic, Handel, by mentioning that in London it is not the custom to put the knife in the mouth,—for fear of accidents,—and that while the fork is reserved for that use, it is not put further in than necessary.", "헨델, 이 화제를 꺼내기에 앞서 한마디 하자면, 런던에서는 칼을 입에 넣지 않는 게 관습이야 — 사고가 날까 봐 그러는 거지 — 그리고 그런 용도로는 포크가 쓰이지만, 그것도 필요 이상으로 깊이 넣지는 않아."], ["It is scarcely worth mentioning, only it’s as well to do as other people do.", "입에 담을 가치도 없는 얘기지만, 그래도 남들 하는 대로 하는 게 좋으니까."], ["Also, the spoon is not generally used over-hand, but under.", "또, 숟가락은 보통 손등을 위로 해서 쓰지 않고 아래로 해서 써."], ["This has two advantages.", "거기엔 두 가지 이점이 있지."], ["You get at your mouth better (which after all is the object), and you save a good deal of the attitude of opening oysters, on the part of the right elbow.”", "입에 더 잘 가져갈 수 있고(어쨌든 그게 목적이니까), 게다가 오른쪽 팔꿈치가 마치 굴 껍데기를 까는 듯한 자세를 취하는 일을 상당히 덜 수 있거든.\""]], [["He offered these friendly suggestions in such a lively way, that we both laughed and I scarcely blushed.", "그가 이런 다정한 충고를 어찌나 활기차게 건넸던지, 우리는 둘 다 웃었고 나는 거의 얼굴을 붉히지도 않았다."]], [["“Now,” he pursued, “concerning Miss Havisham.", "\"자 그럼,\" 그가 말을 이었다."], ["Miss Havisham, you must know, was a spoilt child.", "\"해비셤 양에 관한 얘긴데."], ["Her mother died when she was a baby, and her father denied her nothing.", "알아 둬야 할 건, 해비셤 양이 응석받이로 자란 아이였다는 거야."], ["Her father was a country gentleman down in your part of the world, and was a brewer.", "그분 어머니는 그분이 갓난아기일 때 돌아가셨고, 아버지는 그분에게 무엇 하나 거절하는 법이 없었지."], ["I don’t know why it should be a crack thing to be a brewer; but it is indisputable that while you cannot possibly be genteel and bake, you may be as genteel as never was and brew.", "그분 아버지는 자네 고향 쪽의 시골 신사였는데, 양조업자였어. 어째서 양조업자가 대단한 직업인지는 모르겠네만, 빵을 구우면서 도저히 점잖은 신사일 수 없는 반면, 술을 빚으면서는 더없이 점잖은 신사일 수 있다는 건 반박할 수 없는 사실이지."], ["You see it every day.”", "그런 건 날마다 보는 일이잖나.\""]], [["“Yet a gentleman may not keep a public-house; may he?” said I.", "\"하지만 신사가 선술집을 운영해서는 안 되겠지, 안 그런가?\" 내가 말했다."]], [["“Not on any account,”", "\"무슨 일이 있어도 안 되지.\""], ["returned Herbert; “but a public-house may keep a gentleman. Well! Mr.", "허버트가 대꾸했다. \"하지만 선술집이 신사를 먹여 살릴 수는 있어. 자!"], ["Havisham was very rich and very proud.", "해비셤 씨는 무척 부유했고 무척 거만했지."], ["So was his daughter.”", "그 딸도 마찬가지였고.\""]], [["“Miss Havisham was an only child?” I hazarded.", "\"해비셤 양은 외동딸이었나?\" 내가 조심스레 물어보았다."]], [["“Stop a moment, I am coming to that.", "\"잠깐만, 이제 그 얘기로 가는 참이야."], ["No, she was not an only child; she had a half-brother.", "아니, 외동딸은 아니었어. 그분에겐 배다른 오라비가 있었지."], ["Her father privately married again—his cook, I rather think.”", "아버지가 몰래 재혼을 했거든 — 자기 집 요리사하고, 아마 그랬던 것 같아.\""]], [["“I thought he was proud,” said I.", "\"거만한 사람이었다며.\" 내가 말했다."]], [["“My good Handel, so he was.", "\"이보게 헨델, 그렇긴 했지."], ["He married his second wife privately, because he was proud, and in course of time she died.", "거만했기 때문에 두 번째 아내를 몰래 맞아들인 거고, 세월이 흘러 그 여자도 죽었어."], ["When she was dead, I apprehend he first told his daughter what he had done, and then the son became a part of the family, residing in the house you are acquainted with.", "그 여자가 죽고 나서야, 내 짐작에 그분은 비로소 딸에게 자기가 한 일을 털어놓았고, 그러고서 그 아들이 가족의 일원이 되어, 자네도 잘 아는 그 집에 살게 된 거지."], ["As the son grew a young man, he turned out riotous, extravagant, undutiful,—altogether bad.", "그 아들은 자라서 청년이 되자, 난폭하고 방탕하고 불효막심한 — 한마디로 못된 인간이 되어 버렸어. 결국 아버지는 그를 상속에서 폐했지."], ["At last his father disinherited him; but he softened when he was dying, and left him well off, though not nearly so well off as Miss Havisham.—Take another glass of wine, and excuse my mentioning that society as a body does not expect one to be so strictly conscientious in emptying one’s glass, as to turn it bottom upwards with the rim on one’s nose.”", "하지만 죽을 때가 되니 마음이 누그러져서, 해비셤 양만큼은 도저히 못 되어도 그런대로 넉넉한 살림은 물려주었어 — 포도주 한 잔 더 들게나. 그리고 한마디 덧붙이는 걸 양해해 주게. 사교계라는 집단은, 잔을 비우는 데 있어 잔 테두리를 콧등에 댄 채 바닥이 위로 가도록 거꾸로 뒤집을 만큼 그렇게까지 철저히 양심적이기를 바라지는 않는다네.\""]], [["I had been doing this, in an excess of attention to his recital.", "나는 그의 이야기에 지나치게 정신이 팔린 나머지 바로 그 짓을 하고 있던 참이었다."], ["I thanked him, and apologised.", "나는 그에게 고맙다고 하며 사과했다."], ["He said, “Not at all,” and resumed.", "그는 \"천만에\"라고 하고는 이야기를 다시 이어 갔다."]], [["“Miss Havisham was now an heiress, and you may suppose was looked after as a great match.", "\"이제 해비셤 양은 상속녀가 되었고, 자네도 짐작하다시피 대단한 혼처감으로 눈독을 받게 됐지."], ["Her half-brother had now ample means again, but what with debts and what with new madness wasted them most fearfully again.", "그분의 배다른 오라비는 이제 다시 넉넉한 재산을 손에 넣었으나, 빚이며 새로운 광기며 하는 통에 그것을 또다시 무섭도록 탕진해 버렸어."], ["There were stronger differences between him and her than there had been between him and his father, and it is suspected that he cherished a deep and mortal grudge against her as having influenced the father’s anger.", "그와 그분 사이에는, 예전에 그와 아버지 사이에 있던 것보다 더 심한 불화가 있었고, 아버지의 노여움을 부추긴 것이 그분이었다고 여겨 그가 그분에게 깊고 치명적인 원한을 품었으리라고 짐작되고 있지."], ["Now, I come to the cruel part of the story,—merely breaking off, my dear Handel, to remark that a dinner-napkin will not go into a tumbler.”", "자, 이제 이야기의 잔혹한 대목으로 들어가네 — 다만, 친애하는 헨델, 잠깐 멈추고 한마디 하자면, 식탁용 냅킨은 텀블러 잔 속에 들어가지 않는다는 거야.\""]], [["Why I was trying to pack mine into my tumbler, I am wholly unable to say.", "내가 어째서 내 냅킨을 텀블러 잔 속에 쑤셔 넣으려 하고 있었는지는 나로서도 도무지 설명할 수가 없다."], ["I only know that I found myself, with a perseverance worthy of a much better cause, making the most strenuous exertions to compress it within those limits.", "다만 내가, 훨씬 더 가치 있는 일에나 어울릴 끈기로, 그것을 그 좁은 한계 안에 욱여넣으려고 더없이 안간힘을 쓰고 있는 나 자신을 발견했다는 것만은 안다."], ["Again I thanked him and apologised, and again he said in the cheerfullest manner, “Not at all, I am sure!” and resumed.", "나는 다시 그에게 고맙다고 하며 사과했고, 그는 다시금 더없이 쾌활한 태도로 \"천만에, 정말이야!\"라고 하고는 이야기를 이어 갔다."]], [["“There appeared upon the scene—say at the races, or the public balls, or anywhere else you like—a certain man, who made love to Miss Havisham.", "\"무대 위에 한 남자가 나타났어 — 경마장이라 해도 좋고, 공개 무도회라 해도 좋고, 아무 데나 자네 좋을 대로 생각하게 — 해비셤 양에게 구애한 남자였지."], ["I never saw him (for this happened five-and-twenty years ago, before you and I were, Handel), but I have heard my father mention that he was a showy man, and the kind of man for the purpose.", "나는 그자를 본 적이 없지만(이건 스물다섯 해 전, 자네도 나도 태어나기 전에 일어난 일이니까, 헨델), 우리 아버지 말씀으로는 그가 겉만 번지르르한 남자였고, 그런 일에 딱 어울리는 부류의 사내였다더군."], ["But that he was not to be, without ignorance or prejudice, mistaken for a gentleman, my father most strongly asseverates; because it is a principle of his that no man who was not a true gentleman at heart ever was, since the world began, a true gentleman in manner.", "하지만 무지나 편견에 빠지지 않고서야 그를 신사로 잘못 알아볼 수는 없었으리라고, 아버지는 아주 단호하게 단언하셔. 마음속으로 진정한 신사가 아닌 자가, 천지가 개벽한 이래 태도에서 진정한 신사였던 적은 한 번도 없다는 게 아버지의 신조거든."], ["He says, no varnish can hide the grain of the wood; and that the more varnish you put on, the more the grain will express itself. Well!", "아버지 말씀이, 그 어떤 칠도 나뭇결을 감출 수는 없고, 칠을 덧바르면 덧바를수록 나뭇결은 더욱더 제 모습을 드러낸다는 거야. 자!"], ["This man pursued Miss Havisham closely, and professed to be devoted to her.", "이 남자는 해비셤 양을 바짝 따라다니며 그분에게 헌신하는 척했지."], ["I believe she had not shown much susceptibility up to that time; but all the susceptibility she possessed certainly came out then, and she passionately loved him.", "내가 알기로 그분은 그때까지 별다른 감수성을 내보인 적이 없었으나, 그분이 지닌 감수성이란 감수성은 분명 그때 죄다 쏟아져 나왔고, 그분은 그를 열렬히 사랑했어."], ["There is no doubt that she perfectly idolized him.", "그분이 그를 완전히 우상처럼 떠받들었다는 건 의심할 여지가 없지."], ["He practised on her affection in that systematic way, that he got great sums of money from her, and he induced her to buy her brother out of a share in the brewery (which had been weakly left him by his father) at an immense price, on the plea that when he was her husband he must hold and manage it all.", "그자는 그분의 애정을 그토록 계획적으로 이용해 먹어서, 그분에게서 거액의 돈을 뜯어냈고, 그분이 양조장에서 오라비의 지분을(아버지가 어리석게도 그 오라비에게 남겨 준 것이었지) 엄청난 값을 치르고 사들이도록 부추겼는데, 자기가 그분의 남편이 되면 그 모든 걸 자기가 쥐고 관리해야 한다는 구실을 댔던 거야."], ["Your guardian was not at that time in Miss Havisham’s counsels, and she was too haughty and too much in love to be advised by any one.", "그 무렵 자네 후견인은 해비셤 양의 자문을 맡고 있지 않았고, 그분은 너무 도도하고 너무 사랑에 빠진 나머지 그 누구의 조언도 들으려 하지 않았어."], ["Her relations were poor and scheming, with the exception of my father; he was poor enough, but not time-serving or jealous.", "그분의 친척들은 우리 아버지만 빼고는 죄다 가난하고 잇속만 챙기는 자들이었지. 아버지도 가난하기는 매한가지였으나, 권세에 빌붙거나 시샘하는 분은 아니었어."], ["The only independent one among them, he warned her that she was doing too much for this man, and was placing herself too unreservedly in his power.", "그들 가운데 유일하게 줏대 있는 분이었던 아버지는, 그분이 이 남자를 위해 너무 많은 걸 하고 있고, 너무 거리낌 없이 제 몸을 그자의 손아귀에 내맡기고 있다고 경고했지."], ["She took the first opportunity of angrily ordering my father out of the house, in his presence, and my father has never seen her since.”", "그러자 그분은 첫 기회를 잡자마자, 그 정혼자가 보는 앞에서, 노발대발하며 우리 아버지를 집에서 내쫓아 버렸고, 그 후로 아버지는 그분을 다시는 뵌 적이 없어.\""]], [["I thought of her having said, “Matthew will come and see me at last when I am laid dead upon that table;” and I asked Herbert whether his father was so inveterate against her?", "나는 그분이 \"내가 저 식탁 위에 시신으로 누워 있을 때에야 매슈가 마침내 나를 보러 올 거야\"라고 했던 말을 떠올리고는, 허버트에게 그의 아버지가 그분에게 그토록 깊은 앙심을 품고 있는 거냐고 물었다."]], [["“It’s not that,”", "\"그건 아니야.\""], ["said he, “but she charged him, in the presence of her intended husband, with being disappointed in the hope of fawning upon her for his own advancement, and, if he were to go to her now, it would look true—even to him—and even to her.", "그가 말했다. \"다만 그분이, 그 정혼자가 보는 앞에서, 아버지가 제 출세를 위해 자기에게 아첨할 속셈이었다가 그게 어긋나니 실망한 거라고 몰아세운 거지. 그러니 만약 이제 와서 아버지가 그분을 찾아간다면, 그게 사실인 것처럼 보일 거야 — 아버지 자신에게도, 또 그분에게도 말이지."], ["To return to the man and make an end of him.", "자, 다시 그 남자 얘기로 돌아가서 그자의 결말을 짓자고."], ["The marriage day was fixed, the wedding dresses were bought, the wedding tour was planned out, the wedding guests were invited.", "결혼식 날이 정해졌고, 웨딩드레스도 사 두었고, 신혼여행 계획도 다 짜 두었고, 하객들도 초대했어."], ["The day came, but not the bridegroom. He wrote her a letter—”", "그날이 왔지만, 신랑은 오지 않았어. 그자는 그분에게 편지 한 통을 써 보냈는데 —\""]], [["“Which she received,”", "\"그분이 그 편지를 받은 게,\""], ["I struck in, “when she was dressing for her marriage?", "내가 끼어들었다. \"결혼식 차림을 하고 있을 때였지?"], ["At twenty minutes to nine?”", "아홉 시 이십 분 전에?\""]], [["“At the hour and minute,”", "\"바로 그 시각, 그 분에.\""], ["said Herbert, nodding, “at which she afterwards stopped all the clocks.", "허버트가 고개를 끄덕이며 말했다. \"그분이 나중에 모든 시계를 멈춰 세운 그 시각 말이지."], ["What was in it, further than that it most heartlessly broke the marriage off, I can’t tell you, because I don’t know.", "그 편지에 그 결혼을 더없이 매정하게 깨뜨려 버린 것 말고 또 무슨 말이 적혀 있었는지는, 나도 모르니까 자네에게 말해 줄 수가 없네."], ["When she recovered from a bad illness that she had, she laid the whole place waste, as you have seen it, and she has never since looked upon the light of day.”", "그분은 그때 앓은 큰 병에서 회복되자, 자네가 본 그대로 그 집을 온통 폐허로 만들어 버렸고, 그 후로는 한 번도 햇빛을 본 적이 없어.\""]], [["“Is that all the story?”", "\"그게 이야기의 전부인가?\""], ["I asked, after considering it.", "곰곰이 생각해 본 뒤 내가 물었다."]], [["“All I know of it; and indeed I only know so much, through piecing it out for myself; for my father always avoids it, and, even when Miss Havisham invited me to go there, told me no more of it than it was absolutely requisite I should understand.", "\"내가 아는 건 그게 전부야. 사실 이만큼이라도 아는 것도, 내가 혼자 이리저리 짜 맞춰 본 덕분이지. 아버지는 늘 그 얘기를 피하시고, 해비셤 양이 나를 거기 가도록 초대했을 때조차, 내가 꼭 알아 둬야 할 만큼만 일러 주셨을 뿐 그 이상은 말씀하지 않으셨거든."], ["But I have forgotten one thing.", "그런데 한 가지 잊은 게 있군."], ["It has been supposed that the man to whom she gave her misplaced confidence acted throughout in concert with her half-brother; that it was a conspiracy between them; and that they shared the profits.”", "그분이 잘못 믿어 버린 그 남자가 처음부터 끝까지 그분의 배다른 오라비와 짜고 행동했다고 — 그러니까 둘 사이의 음모였고, 그 이득을 나눠 가졌다고 추정되고 있어.\""]], [["“I wonder he didn’t marry her and get all the property,” said I.", "\"그자가 그분과 결혼해서 재산을 몽땅 차지하지 않은 게 이상한데.\" 내가 말했다."]], [["“He may have been married already, and her cruel mortification may have been a part of her half-brother’s scheme,”", "\"그자는 이미 결혼한 몸이었을지도 모르고, 그분이 당한 그 잔인한 굴욕이 배다른 오라비 계략의 일부였을 수도 있어.\""], ["said Herbert.", "허버트가 말했다."], ["“Mind! I don’t know that.”", "\"명심하게! 나도 그건 모르는 일이야.\""]], [["“What became of the two men?”", "\"그 두 남자는 어떻게 됐나?\""], ["I asked, after again considering the subject.", "다시금 그 문제를 곱씹어 본 뒤 내가 물었다."]], [["“They fell into deeper shame and degradation—if there can be deeper—and ruin.”", "\"더 깊은 수치와 타락에 — 더 깊은 게 있을 수 있다면 말이지 — 그리고 파멸에 빠졌지.\""]], [["“Are they alive now?”", "\"지금도 살아 있나?\""]], [["“I don’t know.”", "\"모르겠어.\""]], [["“You said just now that Estella was not related to Miss Havisham, but adopted.", "\"자네 방금 에스텔라가 해비셤 양과 혈연관계가 아니라 양녀라고 했지."], ["When adopted?”", "언제 입양됐나?\""]], [["Herbert shrugged his shoulders.", "허버트가 어깨를 으쓱했다."], ["“There has always been an Estella, since I have heard of a Miss Havisham.", "\"내가 해비셤 양이라는 분에 대해 들어 온 이래로, 에스텔라는 늘 있었어."], ["I know no more.", "그 이상은 나도 몰라."], ["And now, Handel,”", "자, 그럼 헨델,\""], ["said he, finally throwing off the story as it were, “there is a perfectly open understanding between us.", "그가 이쯤에서 그 이야기를 마치 훌훌 털어 버리듯 말했다. \"이제 우리 사이엔 완전히 터놓은 양해가 선 셈이야."], ["All that I know about Miss Havisham, you know.”", "내가 해비셤 양에 대해 아는 건 다 자네도 알고 있는 거지.\""]], [["“And all that I know,”", "\"그리고 내가 아는 것도 모두,\""], ["I retorted, “you know.”", "내가 응수했다. \"자네가 알고 있는 거지.\""]], [["“I fully believe it.", "\"전적으로 그렇게 믿네."], ["So there can be no competition or perplexity between you and me.", "그러니 자네와 나 사이엔 경쟁이나 곤혹스러움이 있을 수 없어."], ["And as to the condition on which you hold your advancement in life,—namely, that you are not to inquire or discuss to whom you owe it,—you may be very sure that it will never be encroached upon, or even approached, by me, or by any one belonging to me.”", "그리고 자네가 인생에서 출세를 거머쥐게 된 그 조건, 즉 자네가 누구 덕분에 그렇게 됐는지 캐묻거나 입에 올려서는 안 된다는 그 조건에 관해서라면, 나나 내게 속한 그 누구도 결코 그 영역을 침범하거나 심지어 가까이 다가가는 일조차 없으리라고 자네는 아주 확신해도 좋네.\""]], [["In truth, he said this with so much delicacy, that I felt the subject done with, even though I should be under his father’s roof for years and years to come.", "사실 그가 어찌나 섬세하게 이 말을 했던지, 비록 내가 앞으로 여러 해 동안 그의 아버지 지붕 아래 머물게 된다 해도 이 화제만큼은 이미 끝나 버린 것이라는 느낌이 들었다."], ["Yet he said it with so much meaning, too, that I felt he as perfectly understood Miss Havisham to be my benefactress, as I understood the fact myself.", "그러면서도 그는 또한 어찌나 의미심장하게 그 말을 했던지, 그가, 내가 그 사실을 알고 있는 것과 똑같이, 해비셤 양이 내 은인이라는 것을 완벽하게 알고 있다는 느낌이 들었다."]], [["It had not occurred to me before, that he had led up to the theme for the purpose of clearing it out of our way; but we were so much the lighter and easier for having broached it, that I now perceived this to be the case.", "그가 이 화제를 우리 사이에서 깨끗이 치워 버리려는 목적으로 거기까지 끌고 왔다는 생각은 전에는 미처 들지 않았는데, 그것을 터놓고 나니 우리는 한결 가뿐하고 편안해진 터라, 나는 이제 그게 사실임을 알아차렸다."], ["We were very gay and sociable, and I asked him, in the course of conversation, what he was?", "우리는 무척 유쾌하고 정답게 어울렸고, 나는 이야기를 나누던 중에 그에게 무슨 일을 하느냐고 물었다."], ["He replied, “A capitalist,—an Insurer of Ships.”", "그는 \"자본가야 — 선박 보험업자지\"라고 대답했다."], ["I suppose he saw me glancing about the room in search of some tokens of Shipping, or capital, for he added, “In the City.”", "내가 선박이나 자본의 흔적이라도 찾으려고 방 안을 두리번거리는 것을 봤던지, 그는 \"시티에서 말이야\"라고 덧붙였다. * 시티: 런던의 금융 중심가."]], [["I had grand ideas of the wealth and importance of Insurers of Ships in the City, and I began to think with awe of having laid a young Insurer on his back, blackened his enterprising eye, and cut his responsible head open.", "나는 시티의 선박 보험업자들이 누리는 부와 위세에 대해 거창한 생각을 품고 있던 터라, 내가 어린 보험업자 하나를 벌렁 나자빠뜨리고, 진취적인 그의 눈을 멍들게 하고, 막중한 책임을 진 그의 머리통을 째 놓았다는 사실을 떠올리고는 외경심마저 일기 시작했다."], ["But again there came upon me, for my relief, that odd impression that Herbert Pocket would never be very successful or rich.", "그러나 다시금, 다행스럽게도, 허버트 포켓이 결코 크게 성공하거나 부유해지지는 못하리라는 그 묘한 인상이 내게 찾아들었다."]], [["“I shall not rest satisfied with merely employing my capital in insuring ships.", "\"나는 그저 내 자본을 선박 보험에 쓰는 것만으로 만족하고 주저앉진 않을 거야."], ["I shall buy up some good Life Assurance shares, and cut into the Direction.", "괜찮은 생명 보험 주식을 좀 사들여서 이사진에 끼어들 거지."], ["I shall also do a little in the mining way.", "광산 쪽에서도 조금 손을 댈 거고."], ["None of these things will interfere with my chartering a few thousand tons on my own account. I think I shall trade,”", "이런 일들 가운데 어느 것도 내가 직접 몇천 톤짜리 배를 빌리는 데 방해가 되진 않을 거야. 내 생각엔,\""], ["said he, leaning back in his chair, “to the East Indies, for silks, shawls, spices, dyes, drugs, and precious woods.", "그가 의자에 등을 기대며 말했다. \"동인도와 교역을 할 것 같아. 비단, 숄, 향신료, 염료, 약재, 그리고 진귀한 목재를 들여오는 거지."], ["It’s an interesting trade.”", "흥미로운 무역이거든.\""]], [["“And the profits are large?” said I.", "\"그래서 이윤은 큰가?\" 내가 말했다."]], [["“Tremendous!” said he.", "\"엄청나지!\" 그가 말했다."]], [["I wavered again, and began to think here were greater expectations than my own.", "나는 다시 흔들렸고, 여기에 내 것보다 더 큰 유산에의 기대가 있는 게 아닌가 하는 생각이 들기 시작했다."]], [["“I think I shall trade, also,”", "\"그리고 또 내 생각엔,\""], ["said he, putting his thumbs in his waist-coat pockets, “to the West Indies, for sugar, tobacco, and rum.", "그가 조끼 주머니에 양 엄지손가락을 찔러 넣으며 말했다. \"서인도와도 교역할 것 같아. 설탕, 담배, 럼주를 들여오는 거지."], ["Also to Ceylon, especially for elephants’ tusks.”", "실론하고도, 특히 코끼리 상아를 들여오려고 말이야.\""]], [["“You will want a good many ships,” said I.", "\"배가 꽤 많이 필요하겠는데.\" 내가 말했다."]], [["“A perfect fleet,”", "\"완전한 선단(船團)이 필요하지.\""], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."]], [["Quite overpowered by the magnificence of these transactions, I asked him where the ships he insured mostly traded to at present?", "이런 거래들의 웅장함에 완전히 압도되어, 나는 그에게 그가 보험을 든 배들이 지금은 주로 어디와 교역을 하느냐고 물었다."]], [["“I haven’t begun insuring yet,”", "\"아직 보험 일을 시작하진 않았어.\""], ["he replied.", "그가 대답했다."], ["“I am looking about me.”", "\"나는 주위를 살피고 있는 중이야.\""]], [["Somehow, that pursuit seemed more in keeping with Barnard’s Inn.", "어쩐지 그 일이야말로 바너드 여관에 더 어울리는 것 같았다."], ["I said (in a tone of conviction), “Ah-h!”", "나는 (납득했다는 투로) \"아하!\" 하고 말했다."]], [["“Yes.", "\"그래."], ["I am in a counting-house, and looking about me.”", "나는 회계 사무소에 들어가 있고, 주위를 살피고 있는 중이지.\""]], [["“Is a counting-house profitable?” I asked.", "\"회계 사무소는 돈벌이가 되나?\" 내가 물었다."]], [["“To—do you mean to the young fellow who’s in it?” he asked, in reply.", "\"누구한테 — 그러니까 거기 들어가 있는 젊은 친구한테 말인가?\" 그가 되물었다."]], [["“Yes; to you.”", "\"그래, 자네한테 말이야.\""]], [["“Why, n-no; not to me.”", "\"글쎄, 아—니, 나한테는 안 돼.\""], ["He said this with the air of one carefully reckoning up and striking a balance.", "그는 마치 신중하게 셈을 따져 수지를 맞춰 보는 사람 같은 태도로 이렇게 말했다."], ["“Not directly profitable.", "\"직접적으로 돈벌이가 되진 않아."], ["That is, it doesn’t pay me anything, and I have to—keep myself.”", "그러니까, 거기서 내게 한 푼도 주는 게 없고, 나는 — 내 앞가림을 직접 해야 하지.\""]], [["This certainly had not a profitable appearance, and I shook my head as if I would imply that it would be difficult to lay by much accumulative capital from such a source of income.", "이건 분명 돈벌이가 되는 모양새는 아니었으므로, 나는 그런 수입원에서 쌓아 둘 만한 자본을 많이 모으기란 어려운 일이겠다는 뜻을 내비치기라도 하듯 고개를 가로저었다."]], [["“But the thing is,”", "\"하지만 중요한 건 말이야,\""], ["said Herbert Pocket, “that you look about you.", "허버트 포켓이 말했다. \"자네가 주위를 살핀다는 거지."], ["That’s the grand thing.", "바로 그게 대단한 일이거든."], ["You are in a counting-house, you know, and you look about you.”", "알다시피 자네는 회계 사무소에 들어가 있고, 그러면서 주위를 살피는 거야.\""]], [["It struck me as a singular implication that you couldn’t be out of a counting-house, you know, and look about you; but I silently deferred to his experience.", "회계 사무소 밖에 나와 있으면 주위를 살필 수 없다는 게 묘한 함의로 다가왔지만, 나는 잠자코 그의 경험에 따르기로 했다."]], [["“Then the time comes,”", "\"그러다가 때가 오는 거지,\""], ["said Herbert, “when you see your opening.", "허버트가 말했다. \"자네에게 활로가 보이는 그때가 말이야."], ["And you go in, and you swoop upon it and you make your capital, and then there you are!", "그러면 자네는 뛰어들어서, 그걸 덮쳐 거머쥐고, 자본을 만드는 거고, 그러면 자네는 떡하니 자리 잡는 거지!"], ["When you have once made your capital, you have nothing to do but employ it.”", "일단 자본을 한번 만들고 나면, 그걸 굴리기만 하면 되는 거야.\""]], [["This was very like his way of conducting that encounter in the garden; very like.", "이건 그가 정원에서 그 한판 대결을 치르던 방식과 매우 흡사했다. 아주 흡사했다."], ["His manner of bearing his poverty, too, exactly corresponded to his manner of bearing that defeat.", "그가 제 가난을 견뎌 내는 태도 또한, 그가 그 패배를 견뎌 내던 태도와 정확히 일치했다."], ["It seemed to me that he took all blows and buffets now with just the same air as he had taken mine then.", "내가 보기에 그는 지금 온갖 매질과 타격을, 그때 내 주먹질을 받아들이던 것과 꼭 같은 태도로 받아들이고 있었다."], ["It was evident that he had nothing around him but the simplest necessaries, for everything that I remarked upon turned out to have been sent in on my account from the coffee-house or somewhere else.", "그의 둘레엔 더없이 단출한 필수품 외엔 아무것도 없는 것이 분명했으니, 내가 무언가를 두고 한마디 할 때마다 그것은 죄다 나를 위해 커피점이나 다른 어디에선가 들여온 것으로 드러났던 것이다."]], [["Yet, having already made his fortune in his own mind, he was so unassuming with it that I felt quite grateful to him for not being puffed up.", "그러면서도 그는 이미 제 머릿속으로는 한밑천 잡아 놓고서도 그 일에 어찌나 잘난 척이 없던지, 나는 그가 우쭐대지 않는 것이 도리어 고마울 지경이었다."], ["It was a pleasant addition to his naturally pleasant ways, and we got on famously.", "그것은 그의 천성적으로 유쾌한 태도에 보태진 흐뭇한 덤이었고, 우리는 더없이 잘 어울렸다."], ["In the evening we went out for a walk in the streets, and went half-price to the Theatre; and next day we went to church at Westminster Abbey, and in the afternoon we walked in the Parks; and I wondered who shod all the horses there, and wished Joe did.", "저녁에 우리는 거리로 산책을 나갔고, 반값을 내고 극장에 들어갔다. 그리고 이튿날 우리는 웨스트민스터 사원에 예배를 보러 갔고, 오후에는 공원들을 거닐었다. 나는 거기 있는 그 많은 말들의 편자를 누가 다 박아 줄까 궁금했고, 그게 조였으면 싶었다."]], [["On a moderate computation, it was many months, that Sunday, since I had left Joe and Biddy.", "어림잡아 헤아려 보아도, 그 일요일은 내가 조와 비디를 떠나온 지 여러 달이나 지난 듯했다."], ["The space interposed between myself and them partook of that expansion, and our marshes were any distance off.", "나와 그들 사이에 놓인 거리도 그렇게 부풀어 올라, 우리 고향의 습지는 아득히 멀게만 느껴졌다."], ["That I could have been at our old church in my old church-going clothes, on the very last Sunday that ever was, seemed a combination of impossibilities, geographical and social, solar and lunar.", "바로 직전의 일요일, 그러니까 여태껏 있었던 일요일 중 가장 최근의 그 일요일에 내가 옛 교회 나들이옷을 입고 우리 옛 교회에 있었다는 사실은, 지리적으로나 사회적으로나, 또 해와 달로 따져 봐도, 도무지 있을 수 없는 일들이 한데 뒤엉킨 것처럼만 여겨졌다."], ["Yet in the London streets so crowded with people and so brilliantly lighted in the dusk of evening, there were depressing hints of reproaches for that I had put the poor old kitchen at home so far away; and in the dead of night, the footsteps of some incapable impostor of a porter mooning about Barnard’s Inn, under pretence of watching it, fell hollow on my heart.", "그런데도, 사람들로 그토록 북적이고 저녁 어스름 속에 그토록 휘황하게 불 밝힌 런던 거리에는, 내가 고향의 그 가엾고 정든 부엌을 그토록 멀리 밀어내 버린 데 대한 우울한 질책의 기색이 어려 있었다. 그리고 깊은 밤이면, 바너드 여관을 지킨답시고 그 언저리를 빈둥빈둥 거니는 어느 무능한 가짜 수위의 발소리가 내 가슴에 공허하게 울려 퍼졌다."]], [["On the Monday morning at a quarter before nine, Herbert went to the counting-house to report himself,—to look about him, too, I suppose,—and I bore him company.", "월요일 아침 아홉 시 십오 분 전에, 허버트는 출근을 알리려고 — 내 짐작엔 주위를 살피러 가는 것이기도 하겠지만 — 회계 사무소로 갔고, 나는 그를 따라나섰다."], ["He was to come away in an hour or two to attend me to Hammersmith, and I was to wait about for him.", "그는 한두 시간 뒤에 일을 마치고 나와 나를 해머스미스로 데려다주기로 했고, 나는 그동안 근처에서 기다리기로 했다."], ["It appeared to me that the eggs from which young Insurers were hatched were incubated in dust and heat, like the eggs of ostriches, judging from the places to which those incipient giants repaired on a Monday morning.", "갓 부화한 어린 보험업자들이 알에서 깨어나는 그 알은, 마치 타조 알처럼 먼지와 열기 속에서 품어지는 모양이라는 생각이 들었으니, 그 풋내기 거인들이 월요일 아침에 찾아드는 장소들로 미루어 보건대 그러했다."], ["Nor did the counting-house where Herbert assisted, show in my eyes as at all a good Observatory; being a back second floor up a yard, of a grimy presence in all particulars, and with a look into another back second floor, rather than a look out.", "허버트가 일을 거드는 그 회계 사무소도 내 눈에는 도무지 썩 좋은 관측소로는 보이지 않았으니, 어느 안마당으로 들어가 이 층 뒤편에 자리 잡고, 모든 면에서 꾀죄죄한 행색인 데다, 밖을 내다보는 전망이라기보다는 또 다른 이 층 뒤편을 들여다보는 꼴이었기 때문이다."]], [["I waited about until it was noon, and I went upon ’Change, and I saw fluey men sitting there under the bills about shipping, whom I took to be great merchants, though I couldn’t understand why they should all be out of spirits.", "나는 정오가 될 때까지 근처에서 기다리다가, 증권 거래소에 들어가 보았는데, 거기서 선박에 관한 게시문들 아래 솜털처럼 부스스한 사람들이 앉아 있는 것을 보았다. 나는 그들을 대단한 상인들이려니 짐작했으나, 어째서 그들이 죄다 풀이 죽어 있는지는 이해할 수가 없었다."], ["When Herbert came, we went and had lunch at a celebrated house which I then quite venerated, but now believe to have been the most abject superstition in Europe, and where I could not help noticing, even then, that there was much more gravy on the tablecloths and knives and waiters’ clothes, than in the steaks.", "허버트가 오자, 우리는 어느 이름난 가게에 가서 점심을 먹었는데, 그때 나는 그곳을 자못 떠받들었으나 지금 와 생각하면 유럽에서 가장 한심한 미신이 아니었나 싶다. 그리고 그곳에서 나는, 그때조차도, 고기 스테이크보다 식탁보며 나이프며 종업원들 옷에 묻은 육즙이 훨씬 더 많다는 것을 알아차리지 않을 수 없었다."], ["This collation disposed of at a moderate price (considering the grease, which was not charged for), we went back to Barnard’s Inn and got my little portmanteau, and then took coach for Hammersmith.", "이 간단한 식사를 적당한 값에(공짜로 따라온 그 기름값은 안 친 셈이니) 해치우고서, 우리는 바너드 여관으로 돌아가 내 자그마한 여행 가방을 챙긴 다음, 마차를 타고 해머스미스로 향했다."], ["We arrived there at two or three o’clock in the afternoon, and had very little way to walk to Mr.", "우리는 오후 두세 시쯤 거기 도착했고, 포켓 씨의 집까지는 걸어갈 길이 얼마 되지 않았다."], ["Pocket’s house. Lifting the latch of a gate, we passed direct into a little garden overlooking the river, where Mr. Pocket’s children were playing about.", "대문 빗장을 들어 올리고, 우리는 곧장 강이 내려다보이는 자그마한 정원으로 들어섰는데, 거기서는 포켓 씨의 아이들이 뛰놀고 있었다."], ["And unless I deceive myself on a point where my interests or prepossessions are certainly not concerned, I saw that Mr. and Mrs. Pocket’s children were not growing up or being brought up, but were tumbling up.", "그리고 내 이해관계나 선입견이 결코 얽혀 있지 않은 이 한 가지 점에서 내가 스스로를 속이는 게 아니라면, 나는 포켓 씨 내외의 아이들이 자라나거나 길러지고 있는 게 아니라, 곤두박질치며 크고 있다는 것을 보았다."]], [["Mrs. Pocket was sitting on a garden chair under a tree, reading, with her legs upon another garden chair; and Mrs. Pocket’s two nurse-maids were looking about them while the children played.", "포켓 부인은 나무 아래 정원 의자에 앉아, 두 다리를 또 다른 정원 의자에 얹은 채 책을 읽고 있었고, 포켓 부인의 두 유모는 아이들이 노는 동안 이리저리 둘러보고 있었다. \"엄마,\""], ["“Mamma,” said Herbert, “this is young Mr. Pip.”", "허버트가 말했다. \"이쪽은 젊은 핍 씨예요.\""], ["Upon which Mrs. Pocket received me with an appearance of amiable dignity.", "그러자 포켓 부인은 상냥한 위엄을 갖춘 모습으로 나를 맞이했다."]], [["“Master Alick and Miss Jane,”", "\"앨릭 도련님하고 제인 아가씨,\""], ["cried one of the nurses to two of the children, “if you go a bouncing up against them bushes you’ll fall over into the river and be drownded, and what’ll your pa say then?”", "유모 하나가 아이들 중 둘에게 소리쳤다. \"그렇게 저 덤불에 막 부딪쳐 가며 뛰다간 강물에 곤두박질쳐서 빠져 죽을 거예요. 그러면 아버님이 뭐라고 하시겠어요?\""]], [["At the same time this nurse picked up Mrs. Pocket’s handkerchief, and said, “If that don’t make six times you’ve dropped it, Mum!”", "이렇게 말하면서 그 유모는 포켓 부인의 손수건을 주워 들고 말했다. \"이걸 떨어뜨리신 게 이번이 여섯 번째 아니에요, 마님!\""], ["Upon which Mrs. Pocket laughed and said, “Thank you, Flopson,” and settling herself in one chair only, resumed her book.", "그러자 포켓 부인은 웃으며 \"고마워요, 플롭슨\"이라고 하고는, 의자 하나에만 자세를 고쳐 앉아 다시 책을 읽기 시작했다."], ["Her countenance immediately assumed a knitted and intent expression as if she had been reading for a week, but before she could have read half a dozen lines, she fixed her eyes upon me, and said, “I hope your mamma is quite well?”", "그녀의 얼굴은 마치 일주일 내내 책을 읽어 온 사람처럼 곧장 미간을 찌푸린 골똘한 표정을 지었으나, 채 여섯 줄도 읽기 전에 그녀는 내게 눈길을 고정하고 말했다. \"댁의 어머님은 안녕히 잘 계시지요?\""], ["This unexpected inquiry put me into such a difficulty that I began saying in the absurdest way that if there had been any such person I had no doubt she would have been quite well and would have been very much obliged and would have sent her compliments, when the nurse came to my rescue.", "이 뜻밖의 질문에 나는 어찌나 난처해졌던지, 만약 그런 분이 계시기라도 했다면 분명 안녕히 잘 계셨을 거고 무척 고마워하셨을 거고 안부를 전하셨을 거라는 둥 더없이 터무니없는 식으로 주절거리기 시작했는데, 그때 그 유모가 나를 구해 주었다."]], [["“Well!”", "\"어머나!\""], ["she cried, picking up the pocket-handkerchief, “if that don’t make seven times!", "그녀가 손수건을 주워 들며 소리쳤다. \"이번이 일곱 번째잖아요!"], ["What ARE you a-doing of this afternoon, Mum!” Mrs.", "도대체 오늘 오후엔 뭘 하고 계신 거예요, 마님!\""], ["Pocket received her property, at first with a look of unutterable surprise as if she had never seen it before, and then with a laugh of recognition, and said, “Thank you, Flopson,” and forgot me, and went on reading.", "포켓 부인은 자기 물건을 받아 들고는, 처음엔 마치 난생처음 보는 물건이기라도 한 듯 이루 말할 수 없이 놀란 표정을 짓더니, 이윽고 알아보았다는 듯 웃음을 터뜨리며 \"고마워요, 플롭슨\"이라고 하고는, 나를 잊어버린 채 다시 책을 읽어 나갔다."]], [["I found, now I had leisure to count them, that there were no fewer than six little Pockets present, in various stages of tumbling up.", "이제 한가로이 헤아려 볼 짬이 나서 세어 보니, 그 자리엔 어린 포켓네 아이들이 자그마치 여섯이나 있었는데, 저마다 곤두박질치며 크는 갖가지 단계에 있었다."], ["I had scarcely arrived at the total when a seventh was heard, as in the region of air, wailing dolefully.", "내가 미처 그 총수를 헤아리기도 전에, 일곱 번째 아이가 마치 공중 어디쯤에선가 구슬프게 울어 대는 소리로 들려왔다."]], [["“If there ain’t Baby!”", "\"아니, 아기잖아!\""], ["said Flopson, appearing to think it most surprising.", "플롭슨이 그게 무척 놀라운 일이라도 되는 양 말했다."], ["“Make haste up, Millers.”", "\"어서 올라가 봐요, 밀러스.\""]], [["Millers, who was the other nurse, retired into the house, and by degrees the child’s wailing was hushed and stopped, as if it were a young ventriloquist with something in its mouth. Mrs. Pocket read all the time, and I was curious to know what the book could be.", "다른 유모였던 밀러스가 집 안으로 들어갔고, 아이의 울음소리는 마치 입에 뭔가 문 어린 복화술사라도 되는 듯 차츰차츰 잦아들다가 그쳤다. 포켓 부인은 그동안 내내 책을 읽고 있었고, 나는 그 책이 도대체 무슨 책일까 궁금했다."]], [["We were waiting, I supposed, for Mr.", "내 짐작에 우리는 포켓 씨가 우리에게 나와 주기를 기다리고 있었던 모양이다."], ["Pocket to come out to us; at any rate we waited there, and so I had an opportunity of observing the remarkable family phenomenon that whenever any of the children strayed near Mrs. Pocket in their play, they always tripped themselves up and tumbled over her,—always very much to her momentary astonishment, and their own more enduring lamentation.", "아무튼 우리는 거기서 기다리고 있었고, 그래서 나는 그 집안의 한 가지 주목할 만한 현상을 관찰할 기회를 얻었으니, 그건 바로 아이들 중 누구든 놀다가 포켓 부인 곁으로 다가오기만 하면 늘 제풀에 발이 걸려 그녀 위로 고꾸라진다는 것이었다 — 그럴 때마다 그녀는 그 순간만큼은 몹시 놀라고, 아이들은 그보다 더 오래 가는 울음을 터뜨렸다."], ["I was at a loss to account for this surprising circumstance, and could not help giving my mind to speculations about it, until by and by Millers came down with the baby, which baby was handed to Flopson, which Flopson was handing it to Mrs. Pocket, when she too went fairly head foremost over Mrs. Pocket, baby and all, and was caught by Herbert and myself.", "나는 이 놀라운 정황을 어떻게 설명해야 할지 갈피를 잡지 못한 채, 그것에 대해 이런저런 추측에 골몰하지 않을 수 없었는데, 이윽고 밀러스가 아기를 안고 내려왔고, 그 아기는 플롭슨에게 건네졌으며, 플롭슨이 그것을 포켓 부인에게 건네려던 참에, 그녀마저 아기고 뭐고 할 것 없이 포켓 부인 위로 영락없이 머리부터 고꾸라졌고, 허버트와 내가 그녀를 붙잡았다."]], [["“Gracious me, Flopson!”", "\"어머나, 플롭슨!\""], ["said Mrs. Pocket, looking off her book for a moment, “everybody’s tumbling!”", "포켓 부인이 잠깐 책에서 눈을 떼며 말했다. \"다들 고꾸라지는구려!\""]], [["“Gracious you, indeed, Mum!”", "\"어머나라뇨, 마님!\""], ["returned Flopson, very red in the face; “what have you got there?”", "플롭슨이 얼굴이 시뻘게져서 대꾸했다. \"거기 깔고 계신 게 뭐예요?\""]], [["“I got here, Flopson?”", "\"내가 깔고 있는 거라니, 플롭슨?\""], ["asked Mrs. Pocket.", "포켓 부인이 물었다."]], [["“Why, if it ain’t your footstool!”", "\"아니, 마님 발받침대잖아요!\""], ["cried Flopson.", "플롭슨이 외쳤다."], ["“And if you keep it under your skirts like that, who’s to help tumbling? Here!", "\"그걸 그렇게 치마 밑에 깔고 계시니, 안 고꾸라질 사람이 어디 있겠어요? 자!"], ["Take the baby, Mum, and give me your book.”", "아기를 받으세요, 마님. 그리고 그 책은 제게 주시고요.\""]], [["Mrs. Pocket acted on the advice, and inexpertly danced the infant a little in her lap, while the other children played about it.", "포켓 부인은 그 충고에 따라, 다른 아이들이 그 둘레에서 노는 동안, 서툰 솜씨로 무릎 위의 아기를 가볍게 어르며 흔들었다."], ["This had lasted but a very short time, when Mrs. Pocket issued summary orders that they were all to be taken into the house for a nap.", "그러나 그것도 아주 잠깐이었을 뿐, 포켓 부인은 아이들을 죄다 집 안으로 데려가 낮잠을 재우라는 즉결 명령을 내렸다."], ["Thus I made the second discovery on that first occasion, that the nurture of the little Pockets consisted of alternately tumbling up and lying down.", "그리하여 나는 그 첫날, 어린 포켓네 아이들을 기르는 일이란 곤두박질치는 것과 드러눕는 것이 번갈아 이어지는 것임을 두 번째로 깨달았다."]], [["Under these circumstances, when Flopson and Millers had got the children into the house, like a little flock of sheep, and Mr. Pocket came out of it to make my acquaintance, I was not much surprised to find that Mr. Pocket was a gentleman with a rather perplexed expression of face, and with his very grey hair disordered on his head, as if he didn’t quite see his way to putting anything straight.", "이런 상황에서, 플롭슨과 밀러스가 아이들을 마치 한 무리의 양 떼처럼 집 안으로 몰아넣고, 포켓 씨가 나와 인사를 나누려고 그 집에서 나왔을 때, 나는 포켓 씨가 어딘가 어리둥절한 표정을 짓고, 새하얗게 센 머리가 머리 위에서 헝클어진 채인, 마치 무엇 하나 바로잡을 길을 도무지 찾지 못하는 듯한 신사라는 것을 알고도 그다지 놀라지 않았다."]], [["# Chapter XXIII.", "# Chapter XXIII."]], [["Mr. Pocket said he was glad to see me, and he hoped I was not sorry to see him.", "포켓 씨는 나를 만나서 반갑다고 했고, 자기를 만난 것이 내게 언짢은 일이 아니기를 바란다고 했다. \"사실 나는,\""], ["“For, I really am not,” he added, with his son’s smile, “an alarming personage.”", "그는 아들과 똑같은 미소를 띠며 덧붙였다. \"그리 무서운 사람이 아니라네.\""], ["He was a young-looking man, in spite of his perplexities and his very grey hair, and his manner seemed quite natural.", "그는 온갖 곤혹스러운 일들과 잔뜩 센 머리에도 불구하고 젊어 보이는 사람이었고, 태도는 무척 자연스러워 보였다."], ["I use the word natural, in the sense of its being unaffected; there was something comic in his distraught way, as though it would have been downright ludicrous but for his own perception that it was very near being so. When he had talked with me a little, he said to Mrs.", "내가 자연스럽다는 말을 쓰는 것은 꾸밈이 없다는 뜻에서다. 그의 산만한 태도에는 어딘가 우스꽝스러운 데가 있었는데, 마치 그 자신이 그것이 우스꽝스럽기 직전이라는 것을 알아차리지 못했더라면 그야말로 가관이었을 법한 그런 산만함이었다."], ["Pocket, with a rather anxious contraction of his eyebrows, which were black and handsome, “Belinda, I hope you have welcomed Mr. Pip?”", "그는 나와 잠시 이야기를 나누더니, 검고 잘생긴 눈썹을 다소 근심스럽게 찌푸리며 포켓 부인에게 말했다. \"벨린다, 핍 군을 잘 맞아 주었기를 바라오.\""], ["And she looked up from her book, and said, “Yes.”", "그러자 부인은 읽던 책에서 눈을 들어 \"네\"라고 말했다."], ["She then smiled upon me in an absent state of mind, and asked me if I liked the taste of orange-flower water?", "그러고는 마음이 딴 데 가 있는 채로 내게 미소를 짓더니, 오렌지꽃 물의 맛을 좋아하느냐고 물었다."], ["As the question had no bearing, near or remote, on any foregone or subsequent transaction, I consider it to have been thrown out, like her previous approaches, in general conversational condescension.", "그 질문은 앞서 있었던 일이나 뒤에 이어질 일과 가깝게도 멀게도 아무런 관련이 없었으므로, 나는 그것이 앞서의 응대들과 마찬가지로 대화에서 으레 베푸는 일반적인 겸양의 표시로 던져진 말이라 여겼다."]], [["I found out within a few hours, and may mention at once, that Mrs. Pocket was the only daughter of a certain quite accidental deceased Knight, who had invented for himself a conviction that his deceased father would have been made a Baronet but for somebody’s determined opposition arising out of entirely personal motives,—I forget whose, if I ever knew,—the Sovereign’s, the Prime Minister’s, the Lord Chancellor’s, the Archbishop of Canterbury’s, anybody’s,—and had tacked himself on to the nobles of the earth in right of this quite supposititious fact.", "나는 몇 시간 안에 알게 되었고, 여기서 곧바로 밝혀 두는 편이 좋겠는데, 포켓 부인은 어쩌다 작위를 받은 어느 작고한 기사의 외동딸이었다. 그 기사는, 작고한 자기 아버지가 순전히 사사로운 동기에서 비롯된 누군가의 완강한 반대만 없었더라면 준남작이 되었을 것이라는 확신을 스스로 지어냈는데 — 내가 들은 적이 있었다 한들 그게 누구였는지는 잊어버렸다 — 국왕이든, 총리든, 대법관이든, 캔터베리 대주교든, 그 누구든 간에 — 그리고 그 지극히 가공된 사실을 근거로 자신을 이 세상의 귀족 반열에 끼워 넣었던 것이다."], ["I believe he had been knighted himself for storming the English grammar at the point of the pen, in a desperate address engrossed on vellum, on the occasion of the laying of the first stone of some building or other, and for handing some Royal Personage either the trowel or the mortar.", "짐작건대 그가 기사 작위를 받은 것은, 어느 건물인가의 주춧돌을 놓는 자리에서 송아지 가죽 위에 정성껏 베껴 쓴 절박한 축사로 영문법을 펜 끝으로 들이쳤기 때문이며, 또한 어느 왕족에게 흙손이든 회반죽이든을 건네 드렸기 때문이었다."], ["Be that as it may, he had directed Mrs. Pocket to be brought up from her cradle as one who in the nature of things must marry a title, and who was to be guarded from the acquisition of plebeian domestic knowledge.", "어찌 됐든 간에, 그는 포켓 부인을 요람에 있을 때부터 사물의 이치상 반드시 작위 있는 사람과 결혼해야 할 여자로, 그리고 천박한 살림 지식의 습득으로부터 보호받아야 할 여자로 길러지도록 지시해 두었다."]], [["So successful a watch and ward had been established over the young lady by this judicious parent, that she had grown up highly ornamental, but perfectly helpless and useless.", "이 분별 있는 부친에 의해 그 아가씨에 대한 감시와 보호가 어찌나 성공적으로 이루어졌던지, 그녀는 더없이 장식적이면서도 완벽하게 무력하고 쓸모없는 사람으로 자라났다."], ["With her character thus happily formed, in the first bloom of her youth she had encountered Mr.", "이렇듯 다행스럽게 성품이 형성된 그녀는, 청춘이 한창 꽃피던 시절에 포켓 씨를 만났다."], ["Pocket: who was also in the first bloom of youth, and not quite decided whether to mount to the Woolsack, or to roof himself in with a mitre.", "포켓 씨 역시 청춘이 한창 꽃피던 무렵이었고, 대법관 자리에 오를지 아니면 주교관을 쓰고 지붕 삼아 살아갈지 아직 결정을 내리지 못한 상태였다."], ["As his doing the one or the other was a mere question of time, he and Mrs. Pocket had taken Time by the forelock (when, to judge from its length, it would seem to have wanted cutting), and had married without the knowledge of the judicious parent.", "그가 이쪽이 되든 저쪽이 되든 그것은 그저 시간 문제일 뿐이었으므로, 그와 포켓 부인은 시간이라는 놈의 앞머리채를 움켜잡았고(그 길이로 미루어 보건대 머리를 깎을 때가 된 듯했으니), 분별 있는 부친 모르게 결혼해 버렸다."], ["The judicious parent, having nothing to bestow or withhold but his blessing, had handsomely settled that dower upon them after a short struggle, and had informed Mr. Pocket that his wife was “a treasure for a Prince.” Mr.", "분별 있는 부친은 축복 말고는 줄 것도 거둘 것도 없었던지라, 잠시 옥신각신한 끝에 그 지참금을 두 사람에게 후하게 내려 주었고, 포켓 씨에게 그의 아내가 \"왕자에게 어울릴 보물\"이라고 일러 주었다."], ["Pocket had invested the Prince’s treasure in the ways of the world ever since, and it was supposed to have brought him in but indifferent interest.", "그 후로 포켓 씨는 그 왕자의 보물을 세상살이에 투자해 왔는데, 그것이 그에게 가져다준 이자는 시원찮았다는 게 중론이었다."], ["Still, Mrs. Pocket was in general the object of a queer sort of respectful pity, because she had not married a title; while Mr.", "그래도 포켓 부인은 대체로 묘하게 정중한 동정의 대상이 되었으니, 작위 있는 사람과 결혼하지 못했기 때문이었다."], ["Pocket was the object of a queer sort of forgiving reproach, because he had never got one.", "한편 포켓 씨는 묘하게 너그러운 책망의 대상이 되었으니, 작위를 끝내 얻지 못했기 때문이었다."]], [["Mr. Pocket took me into the house and showed me my room: which was a pleasant one, and so furnished as that I could use it with comfort for my own private sitting-room.", "포켓 씨는 나를 집 안으로 데리고 들어가 내 방을 보여 주었다. 쾌적한 방이었고, 나만의 개인 거실로도 편안히 쓸 수 있을 만큼 가구가 갖춰져 있었다."], ["He then knocked at the doors of two other similar rooms, and introduced me to their occupants, by name Drummle and Startop.", "그러고는 비슷한 다른 방 두 개의 문을 두드려, 그 방에 묵는 이들을 내게 소개해 주었는데, 이름이 각각 드러믈과 스타톱이었다."], ["Drummle, an old-looking young man of a heavy order of architecture, was whistling.", "드러믈은 묵직한 양식으로 지어진 듯, 늙어 보이는 젊은이였는데, 휘파람을 불고 있었다."], ["Startop, younger in years and appearance, was reading and holding his head, as if he thought himself in danger of exploding it with too strong a charge of knowledge.", "스타톱은 나이도 외모도 더 젊었는데, 머리를 감싸 쥔 채 책을 읽고 있었으니, 마치 지식을 너무 강하게 장전한 나머지 제 머리가 폭발할 위험에 처했다고 여기는 것 같았다."]], [["Both Mr. and Mrs. Pocket had such a noticeable air of being in somebody else’s hands, that I wondered who really was in possession of the house and let them live there, until I found this unknown power to be the servants.", "포켓 씨도 포켓 부인도 어딘가 남의 손에 맡겨져 있는 듯한 기색이 어찌나 두드러졌던지, 나는 대체 누가 이 집을 차지하고서 저들을 여기 살게 해 주는 것일까 궁금했는데, 마침내 그 미지의 권력이 하인들임을 알아냈다."], ["It was a smooth way of going on, perhaps, in respect of saving trouble; but it had the appearance of being expensive, for the servants felt it a duty they owed to themselves to be nice in their eating and drinking, and to keep a deal of company downstairs. They allowed a very liberal table to Mr.", "그것은 어쩌면 수고를 더는 면에서는 매끄러운 살림 방식이었으나, 돈이 많이 드는 방식으로 보였다. 하인들은 잘 먹고 잘 마시는 것을, 그리고 아래층에서 손님들을 많이 치르는 것을 스스로에게 진 의무로 여겼기 때문이다."], ["and Mrs. Pocket, yet it always appeared to me that by far the best part of the house to have boarded in would have been the kitchen,—always supposing the boarder capable of self-defence, for, before I had been there a week, a neighbouring lady with whom the family were personally unacquainted, wrote in to say that she had seen Millers slapping the baby.", "그들은 포켓 씨 부부에게 매우 후한 식탁을 허락해 주었으나, 내게는 이 집에서 묵기에 단연 가장 좋은 곳은 부엌이었으리라는 생각이 늘 들었다. 물론 묵는 사람이 제 몸을 지킬 능력이 있다는 전제하에서였는데, 내가 그곳에 머문 지 일주일도 채 안 되어, 이 집안과 개인적으로 알지도 못하는 이웃 부인이, 밀러스가 아기를 찰싹 때리는 것을 보았노라고 편지를 보내왔기 때문이다."], ["This greatly distressed Mrs. Pocket, who burst into tears on receiving the note, and said that it was an extraordinary thing that the neighbours couldn’t mind their own business.", "이 일로 포켓 부인은 몹시 상심하여, 그 쪽지를 받고는 울음을 터뜨리며, 이웃들이 제 일에나 신경 쓰지 못하는 것은 참으로 별난 일이라고 말했다."]], [["By degrees I learnt, and chiefly from Herbert, that Mr. Pocket had been educated at Harrow and at Cambridge, where he had distinguished himself; but that when he had had the happiness of marrying Mrs. Pocket very early in life, he had impaired his prospects and taken up the calling of a Grinder.", "나는 차츰, 주로 허버트에게서 알게 되었는데, 포켓 씨는 해로와 케임브리지에서 교육을 받았고 거기서 두각을 나타냈으나, 인생의 아주 이른 시기에 포켓 부인과 결혼하는 행복을 누리는 바람에 자기 앞길을 망치고 가정교사 노릇에 나섰다고 했다."], ["After grinding a number of dull blades,—of whom it was remarkable that their fathers, when influential, were always going to help him to preferment, but always forgot to do it when the blades had left the Grindstone,—he had wearied of that poor work and had come to London.", "머리 둔한 칼날들을 여럿 갈아 준 끝에 — 그 칼날들로 말하자면, 그 아버지들이 영향력 있는 자리에 있을 때에는 언제나 그에게 좋은 자리를 마련해 주겠다 하면서도, 막상 칼날들이 숫돌을 떠나고 나면 언제나 그러기를 잊어버린다는 점이 특이했다 — 그는 그 변변찮은 일에 진력이 나서 런던으로 왔다."], ["Here, after gradually failing in loftier hopes, he had “read” with divers who had lacked opportunities or neglected them, and had refurbished divers others for special occasions, and had turned his acquirements to the account of literary compilation and correction, and on such means, added to some very moderate private resources, still maintained the house I saw.", "여기서 그는 더 높은 희망들이 차츰 무너진 끝에, 기회가 없었거나 기회를 흘려보낸 갖가지 사람들과 함께 \"공부\"를 했고, 특별한 행사를 앞둔 여러 사람들을 손질해 주었으며, 자기가 쌓은 학식을 문헌 편찬과 교정에 활용했다. 그렇게 번 돈에다 지극히 변변찮은 사적 재산을 보태어, 그는 내가 본 그 집을 여전히 꾸려 가고 있었다."]], [["Mr. and Mrs. Pocket had a toady neighbour; a widow lady of that highly sympathetic nature that she agreed with everybody, blessed everybody, and shed smiles and tears on everybody, according to circumstances.", "포켓 씨 부부에게는 알랑거리는 이웃이 하나 있었다."], ["This lady’s name was Mrs. Coiler, and I had the honour of taking her down to dinner on the day of my installation.", "매우 공감 잘하는 천성을 지닌 어느 미망인으로, 모든 사람의 말에 동의하고, 모든 사람을 축복하며, 상황에 따라 모든 사람에게 미소도 눈물도 뿌리는 부인이었다."], ["She gave me to understand on the stairs, that it was a blow to dear Mrs.", "이 부인의 이름은 코일러 부인이었는데, 내가 이 집에 자리 잡은 날 그녀를 식당으로 모시고 내려가는 영광을 누렸다."], ["Pocket that dear Mr. Pocket should be under the necessity of receiving gentlemen to read with him.", "그녀는 계단에서 내게, 친애하는 포켓 씨가 자기 밑에서 공부할 신사들을 받아들이지 않으면 안 되는 처지라는 것이 친애하는 포켓 부인에게는 하나의 타격이라고 넌지시 일러 주었다."], ["That did not extend to me, she told me in a gush of love and confidence (at that time, I had known her something less than five minutes); if they were all like Me, it would be quite another thing.", "다만 그것이 내게는 해당되지 않는다고, 그녀는 사랑과 신뢰가 넘쳐흐르는 어조로 말했다(그때 나는 그녀를 안 지 채 오 분도 되지 않았다). 만약 다들 나 같은 사람이라면 사정이 사뭇 다를 것이라고."]], [["“But dear Mrs. Pocket,” said Mrs.", "\"하지만 친애하는 포켓 부인은,\""], ["Coiler, “after her early disappointment (not that dear Mr. Pocket was to blame in that), requires so much luxury and elegance—”", "코일러 부인이 말했다. \"젊은 날의 그 실망 뒤에(친애하는 포켓 씨에게 그 일의 책임이 있다는 건 아니지만), 무척이나 많은 호사와 우아함을 필요로 하시는 분이라—\""]], [["“Yes, ma’am,”", "\"네, 부인.\""], ["I said, to stop her, for I was afraid she was going to cry.", "나는 그녀가 울음을 터뜨릴까 봐 두려워 말을 끊으려고 그렇게 말했다."]], [["“And she is of so aristocratic a disposition—”", "\"게다가 그토록 귀족적인 기질을 지니신 분이라—\""]], [["“Yes, ma’am,”", "\"네, 부인.\""], ["I said again, with the same object as before.", "나는 앞서와 같은 의도로 다시 말했다."]], [["“—That it is hard,” said Mrs. Coiler, “to have dear Mr.", "\"—그러니 친애하는 포켓 씨의 시간과 관심이 친애하는 포켓 부인에게서 딴 데로 돌려진다는 것은 정말이지 힘든 일이지요.\""], ["Pocket’s time and attention diverted from dear Mrs. Pocket.”", "코일러 부인이 말했다."]], [["I could not help thinking that it might be harder if the butcher’s time and attention were diverted from dear Mrs.", "나는 차라리 푸줏간 주인의 시간과 관심이 친애하는 포켓 부인에게서 딴 데로 돌려지는 편이 더 힘든 일이 아닐까 하는 생각을 떨칠 수 없었다."], ["Pocket; but I said nothing, and indeed had enough to do in keeping a bashful watch upon my company manners.", "그러나 나는 아무 말도 하지 않았고, 사실 사교상의 예법을 수줍게나마 놓치지 않으려 신경 쓰느라 정신이 없었다."]], [["It came to my knowledge, through what passed between Mrs. Pocket and Drummle while I was attentive to my knife and fork, spoon, glasses, and other instruments of self-destruction, that Drummle, whose Christian name was Bentley, was actually the next heir but one to a baronetcy.", "내가 나이프와 포크, 숟가락, 잔, 그 밖의 자멸 도구들에 골몰해 있는 동안 포켓 부인과 드러믈 사이에 오간 이야기를 통해, 나는 드러믈이 — 그의 세례명은 벤틀리였다 — 실은 어느 준남작 작위의 차차기 상속자라는 사실을 알게 되었다."], ["It further appeared that the book I had seen Mrs. Pocket reading in the garden was all about titles, and that she knew the exact date at which her grandpapa would have come into the book, if he ever had come at all.", "또한 내가 정원에서 포켓 부인이 읽는 것을 보았던 그 책이 온통 작위에 관한 것이며, 그녀가 자기 할아버지가 그 책에 오를 수 있었던 — 정말로 오를 수만 있었더라면 — 정확한 날짜까지 알고 있다는 것도 드러났다."], ["Drummle didn’t say much, but in his limited way (he struck me as a sulky kind of fellow) he spoke as one of the elect, and recognised Mrs. Pocket as a woman and a sister.", "드러믈은 말수가 적었으나, 그 한정된 방식으로(그는 내게 뚱한 부류의 사내로 보였다) 선택받은 자의 한 사람처럼 말했고, 포켓 부인을 한 여인이자 한 자매로 인정했다."], ["No one but themselves and Mrs. Coiler the toady neighbour showed any interest in this part of the conversation, and it appeared to me that it was painful to Herbert; but it promised to last a long time, when the page came in with the announcement of a domestic affliction.", "그들 자신과 알랑거리는 이웃 코일러 부인 말고는 아무도 대화의 이 대목에 관심을 보이지 않았고, 내가 보기에 그것은 허버트에게 괴로운 일인 듯했다. 그런데 그 이야기가 오래도록 이어질 기세이던 차에, 시동이 들어와 집안의 변고를 알렸다."], ["It was, in effect, that the cook had mislaid the beef.", "요컨대 요리사가 쇠고기를 어디다 두었는지 잃어버렸다는 것이었다."], ["To my unutterable amazement, I now, for the first time, saw Mr. Pocket relieve his mind by going through a performance that struck me as very extraordinary, but which made no impression on anybody else, and with which I soon became as familiar as the rest.", "내가 말로 다 할 수 없을 만큼 놀랐던 것은, 그때 처음으로 포켓 씨가 마음의 짐을 더는 모습을 본 일이었는데, 그것은 내게 무척이나 기이하게 비쳤으나 다른 누구에게도 아무런 인상을 주지 않았고, 머지않아 나도 남들처럼 거기에 익숙해졌다."], ["He laid down the carving-knife and fork,—being engaged in carving, at the moment,—put his two hands into his disturbed hair, and appeared to make an extraordinary effort to lift himself up by it.", "그는 — 마침 그 순간 고기를 썰고 있었는지라 — 고기 써는 나이프와 포크를 내려놓더니, 두 손을 헝클어진 머리에 찔러 넣고, 그 머리채를 잡고 제 몸을 들어 올리려는 듯 엄청난 노력을 기울이는 것이었다."], ["When he had done this, and had not lifted himself up at all, he quietly went on with what he was about.", "그렇게 하고도 몸이 조금도 들리지 않자, 그는 조용히 하던 일을 다시 이어 갔다."]], [["Mrs. Coiler then changed the subject and began to flatter me.", "그러자 코일러 부인은 화제를 바꾸어 내게 아첨하기 시작했다."], ["I liked it for a few moments, but she flattered me so very grossly that the pleasure was soon over.", "처음 얼마 동안은 그것이 기분 좋았으나, 그녀가 어찌나 노골적으로 아첨을 해 대던지 그 즐거움도 이내 사라졌다."], ["She had a serpentine way of coming close at me when she pretended to be vitally interested in the friends and localities I had left, which was altogether snaky and fork-tongued; and when she made an occasional bounce upon Startop (who said very little to her), or upon Drummle (who said less), I rather envied them for being on the opposite side of the table.", "그녀는 내가 떠나온 친구들과 고장에 대해 사뭇 깊은 관심이 있는 척하면서 내게 바싹 다가오는 뱀 같은 버릇이 있었는데, 그 모양새가 영락없이 뱀처럼 갈라진 혀를 날름거리는 듯했다. 그러다 그녀가 이따금 스타톱에게(그는 그녀에게 거의 말을 하지 않았다), 혹은 드러믈에게(그는 더더욱 말을 하지 않았다) 불쑥 달려들 때면, 나는 차라리 식탁 맞은편에 앉은 그들이 부럽기까지 했다."]], [["After dinner the children were introduced, and Mrs. Coiler made admiring comments on their eyes, noses, and legs,—a sagacious way of improving their minds.", "저녁 식사 후에 아이들이 소개되었고, 코일러 부인은 아이들의 눈과 코와 다리에 대해 찬탄의 말을 늘어놓았으니 — 아이들의 지성을 키우는 데에는 참으로 현명한 방법이었다."], ["There were four little girls, and two little boys, besides the baby who might have been either, and the baby’s next successor who was as yet neither.", "어느 쪽인지 분간이 안 가는 아기와, 아직 어느 쪽도 아닌 그 아기 다음 차례의 아이를 빼고도, 어린 여자아이가 넷, 어린 남자아이가 둘 있었다."], ["They were brought in by Flopson and Millers, much as though those two non-commissioned officers had been recruiting somewhere for children and had enlisted these, while Mrs.", "아이들은 플롭슨과 밀러스가 데리고 들어왔는데, 마치 그 두 하사관이 어디선가 아이들을 모집하러 다니다 이 아이들을 입대시키기라도 한 것 같았다."], ["Pocket looked at the young Nobles that ought to have been as if she rather thought she had had the pleasure of inspecting them before, but didn’t quite know what to make of them.", "한편 포켓 부인은 마땅히 귀족이 되었어야 할 그 어린것들을 바라보면서, 전에 이들을 살펴보는 기쁨을 누린 적이 있는 듯도 하나 도무지 이들을 어찌 보아야 할지 모르겠다는 표정이었다."]], [["“Here!", "\"자!"], ["Give me your fork, Mum, and take the baby,”", "부인, 포크를 제게 주시고 아기를 받으세요.\""], ["said Flopson.", "플롭슨이 말했다."], ["“Don’t take it that way, or you’ll get its head under the table.”", "\"그렇게 안으시면 안 돼요, 안 그러면 아기 머리가 식탁 밑으로 들어가요.\""]], [["Thus advised, Mrs. Pocket took it the other way, and got its head upon the table; which was announced to all present by a prodigious concussion.", "이렇게 일러 주는 대로 포켓 부인은 아기를 반대로 안았고, 그 바람에 아기 머리가 식탁 위에 부딪쳤다. 엄청난 충돌음이 그 자리에 있던 모두에게 그 사실을 알렸다."]], [["“Dear, dear!", "\"어머, 어머!"], ["Give it me back, Mum,”", "부인, 도로 제게 주세요.\""], ["said Flopson; “and Miss Jane, come and dance to baby, do!”", "플롭슨이 말했다. \"그리고 제인 아가씨, 이리 와서 아기한테 춤춰 줘요, 어서요!\""]], [["One of the little girls, a mere mite who seemed to have prematurely taken upon herself some charge of the others, stepped out of her place by me, and danced to and from the baby until it left off crying, and laughed.", "어린 여자아이들 가운데 하나가 — 조그마한 꼬마였는데, 어쩐지 제 또래보다 일찍이 다른 아이들을 돌보는 책임 같은 것을 떠맡은 듯한 아이였다 — 내 옆자리에서 일어나, 아기가 울음을 그치고 웃을 때까지 아기를 향해 오락가락하며 춤을 추었다."], ["Then, all the children laughed, and Mr. Pocket (who in the meantime had twice endeavoured to lift himself up by the hair) laughed, and we all laughed and were glad.", "그러자 아이들이 모두 웃었고, 포켓 씨도(그사이 두 번이나 제 머리채를 잡고 몸을 들어 올리려 애썼던 그가) 웃었으며, 우리 모두 웃으며 즐거워했다."]], [["Flopson, by dint of doubling the baby at the joints like a Dutch doll, then got it safely into Mrs. Pocket’s lap, and gave it the nut-crackers to play with; at the same time recommending Mrs.", "플롭슨은 네덜란드 인형처럼 관절마다 아기를 접어 가며 무사히 포켓 부인의 무릎 위에 앉히고는, 가지고 놀라며 호두까기를 쥐여 주었다."], ["Pocket to take notice that the handles of that instrument were not likely to agree with its eyes, and sharply charging Miss Jane to look after the same.", "동시에 포켓 부인에게 그 도구의 손잡이가 아기 눈과 맞지 않을 듯하니 유념하시라고 일렀고, 제인 아가씨에게는 그 점을 잘 살피라고 야무지게 당부했다."], ["Then, the two nurses left the room, and had a lively scuffle on the staircase with a dissipated page who had waited at dinner, and who had clearly lost half his buttons at the gaming-table.", "그러고 나서 두 보모는 방을 나갔는데, 계단에서 저녁 시중을 들었던 어느 방탕한 시동과 한바탕 활기찬 몸싸움을 벌였다. 그 시동은 노름판에서 단추를 절반은 잃어버린 게 분명했다."]], [["I was made very uneasy in my mind by Mrs. Pocket’s falling into a discussion with Drummle respecting two baronetcies, while she ate a sliced orange steeped in sugar and wine, and, forgetting all about the baby on her lap, who did most appalling things with the nut-crackers.", "나는 포켓 부인이, 설탕과 포도주에 절인 오렌지 조각을 먹으면서 드러믈과 두 준남작 작위에 관한 토론에 빠져드는 바람에 무척 마음이 불편해졌다. 그녀는 제 무릎 위의 아기를 까맣게 잊고 있었는데, 그 아기는 호두까기로 더없이 끔찍한 짓들을 저지르고 있었다."], ["At length little Jane, perceiving its young brains to be imperilled, softly left her place, and with many small artifices coaxed the dangerous weapon away. Mrs.", "마침내 꼬마 제인이 그 어린 머리통이 위태롭다는 것을 알아채고, 살그머니 제자리를 떠나 갖은 잔꾀를 부려 그 위험한 흉기를 살살 빼앗았다."], ["Pocket finishing her orange at about the same time, and not approving of this, said to Jane,—", "포켓 부인은 거의 같은 무렵에 오렌지를 다 먹었는데, 이 일을 못마땅해하며 제인에게 말했다—"]], [["“You naughty child, how dare you?", "\"이 못된 것, 어디 감히?"], ["Go and sit down this instant!”", "당장 가서 앉지 못해!\""]], [["“Mamma dear,”", "\"엄마,\""], ["lisped the little girl, “baby ood have put hith eyeth out.”", "어린 여자아이가 혀짤배기소리로 말했다. \"아기가 눈을 찌를 뻔했어요.\""]], [["“How dare you tell me so?” retorted Mrs.", "\"네가 어디 감히 그런 말을 하느냐?\""], ["Pocket. “Go and sit down in your chair this moment!”", "포켓 부인이 쏘아붙였다. \"당장 가서 네 의자에 앉아!\""]], [["Mrs. Pocket’s dignity was so crushing, that I felt quite abashed, as if I myself had done something to rouse it.", "포켓 부인의 위엄이 어찌나 위압적이던지, 나는 마치 내가 그 위엄을 불러일으킬 무슨 짓이라도 한 양 몹시 부끄러워졌다."]], [["“Belinda,”", "\"벨린다,\""], ["remonstrated Mr. Pocket, from the other end of the table, “how can you be so unreasonable?", "식탁 저쪽 끝에서 포켓 씨가 항의했다. \"어찌 그리 이치에 닿지 않게 구는 거요?"], ["Jane only interfered for the protection of baby.”", "제인은 그저 아기를 지키려고 끼어든 것뿐인데.\""]], [["“I will not allow anybody to interfere,” said Mrs.", "\"나는 아무도 끼어드는 걸 용납하지 않겠어요.\""], ["Pocket. “I am surprised, Matthew, that you should expose me to the affront of interference.”", "포켓 부인이 말했다. \"매슈, 당신이 나를 이렇게 끼어듦의 모욕에 노출시키다니 놀랍군요.\""]], [["“Good God!”", "\"맙소사!\""], ["cried Mr. Pocket, in an outbreak of desolate desperation.", "포켓 씨가 황량한 절망에 사로잡혀 외쳤다."], ["“Are infants to be nut-crackered into their tombs, and is nobody to save them?”", "\"아기들을 호두까기로 무덤 속까지 밀어 넣어도, 아무도 그 아이들을 구해서는 안 된단 말이오?\""]], [["“I will not be interfered with by Jane,”", "\"나는 제인에게 간섭당하지 않겠어요.\""], ["said Mrs. Pocket, with a majestic glance at that innocent little offender.", "포켓 부인은 그 죄 없는 어린 죄인에게 위엄 어린 시선을 던지며 말했다."], ["“I hope I know my poor grandpapa’s position.", "\"내 가엾은 할아버지의 신분이 어떤 것인지 나는 잘 알고 있다고 생각해요."], ["Jane, indeed!”", "제인 따위가!\""]], [["Mr. Pocket got his hands in his hair again, and this time really did lift himself some inches out of his chair.", "포켓 씨는 다시 머리채에 두 손을 가져갔고, 이번에는 정말로 의자에서 몇 인치쯤 제 몸을 들어 올렸다."], ["“Hear this!” he helplessly exclaimed to the elements.", "\"이 말 좀 들어 보시오!\" 그는 천지신명을 향해 속절없이 외쳤다."], ["“Babies are to be nut-crackered dead, for people’s poor grandpapa’s positions!”", "\"아기들을 호두까기로 죽여도 되는 거랍니다. 사람들의 가엾은 할아버지의 신분을 위해서 말이오!\""], ["Then he let himself down again, and became silent.", "그러고는 다시 몸을 내려놓고 입을 다물었다."]], [["We all looked awkwardly at the tablecloth while this was going on.", "이런 일이 벌어지는 동안 우리는 모두 어색하게 식탁보만 바라보았다."], ["A pause succeeded, during which the honest and irrepressible baby made a series of leaps and crows at little Jane, who appeared to me to be the only member of the family (irrespective of servants) with whom it had any decided acquaintance.", "침묵이 뒤따랐고, 그동안 정직하고 억누를 길 없는 아기는 꼬마 제인을 향해 연거푸 깡충깡충 뛰며 까르르거렸으니, 내가 보기에 제인은 (하인들을 제외하고) 그 아기가 확실히 낯을 익힌 유일한 식구인 듯했다."]], [["“Mr. Drummle,”", "\"드러믈 씨,\""], ["said Mrs. Pocket, “will you ring for Flopson?", "포켓 부인이 말했다. \"플롭슨을 부르게 종을 울려 주시겠어요?"], ["Jane, you undutiful little thing, go and lie down.", "제인, 이 본분도 모르는 것, 가서 누워 있어."], ["Now, baby darling, come with ma!”", "자, 아가야, 엄마한테 오렴!\""]], [["The baby was the soul of honour, and protested with all its might.", "아기는 명예의 화신이라, 있는 힘을 다해 항의했다."], ["It doubled itself up the wrong way over Mrs. Pocket’s arm, exhibited a pair of knitted shoes and dimpled ankles to the company in lieu of its soft face, and was carried out in the highest state of mutiny.", "아기는 포켓 부인의 팔 위로 거꾸로 몸을 접더니, 그 보드라운 얼굴 대신 뜨개질한 신발과 보조개진 발목을 좌중에 내보이며, 더없이 반항이 극에 달한 상태로 실려 나갔다."], ["And it gained its point after all, for I saw it through the window within a few minutes, being nursed by little Jane.", "그리고 결국 제 뜻을 이루었으니, 몇 분 뒤 나는 창문을 통해 꼬마 제인이 그 아기를 어르고 있는 것을 보았다."]], [["It happened that the other five children were left behind at the dinner-table, through Flopson’s having some private engagement, and their not being anybody else’s business.", "마침 플롭슨에게 무슨 사사로운 볼일이 있었던 데다, 다른 다섯 아이들은 그 누구의 소관도 아니었던 탓에, 그 아이들은 식탁에 그대로 남겨졌다."], ["I thus became aware of the mutual relations between them and Mr. Pocket, which were exemplified in the following manner. Mr.", "그리하여 나는 그 아이들과 포켓 씨 사이의 상호 관계를 알게 되었는데, 그것은 다음과 같은 식으로 드러났다."], ["Pocket, with the normal perplexity of his face heightened and his hair rumpled, looked at them for some minutes, as if he couldn’t make out how they came to be boarding and lodging in that establishment, and why they hadn’t been billeted by Nature on somebody else.", "포켓 씨는 평소의 곤혹스러운 얼굴빛이 한층 짙어지고 머리가 헝클어진 채로, 몇 분 동안 아이들을 바라보았으니, 마치 이 아이들이 어쩌다 이 집에서 먹고 자게 되었는지, 그리고 어째서 자연이 이 아이들을 다른 누군가에게 떠맡기지 않았는지 도무지 알 수 없다는 표정이었다."], ["Then, in a distant Missionary way he asked them certain questions,—as why little Joe had that hole in his frill, who said, Pa, Flopson was going to mend it when she had time,—and how little Fanny came by that whitlow, who said, Pa, Millers was going to poultice it when she didn’t forget.", "그러고는 멀찍이 떨어진 선교사 같은 투로 아이들에게 이런저런 질문을 던졌으니 — 이를테면 어린 조의 옷주름에 왜 저런 구멍이 났느냐고 묻자, 조는 \"아빠, 플롭슨이 시간 나면 기워 준대요\" 하고 대답했고 — 또 어린 패니의 손가락이 어쩌다 저렇게 곪았느냐고 묻자, 패니는 \"아빠, 밀러스가 안 잊어버리면 찜질해 준대요\" 하고 대답하는 식이었다."], ["Then, he melted into parental tenderness, and gave them a shilling apiece and told them to go and play; and then as they went out, with one very strong effort to lift himself up by the hair he dismissed the hopeless subject.", "그러더니 그는 어버이다운 다정함에 마음이 녹아, 아이들에게 일 실링씩 나눠 주며 가서 놀라고 했다. 그런 다음 아이들이 나가자, 제 머리채를 잡고 몸을 들어 올리려 한 차례 무척이나 힘껏 애쓰면서, 그 가망 없는 문제를 떨쳐 버렸다."]], [["In the evening there was rowing on the river. As Drummle and Startop had each a boat, I resolved to set up mine, and to cut them both out.", "저녁에는 강에서 뱃놀이가 있었다. 드러믈과 스타톱이 저마다 배를 한 척씩 가지고 있었으므로, 나는 내 배를 마련하여 그 둘을 앞지르기로 마음먹었다."], ["I was pretty good at most exercises in which country boys are adepts, but as I was conscious of wanting elegance of style for the Thames,—not to say for other waters,—I at once engaged to place myself under the tuition of the winner of a prize-wherry who plied at our stairs, and to whom I was introduced by my new allies.", "나는 시골 아이들이 능숙한 운동이라면 대개 제법 잘하는 편이었으나, 템스강에서 — 다른 강은 말할 것도 없고 — 노 젓는 자세의 품격이 부족하다는 것을 자각하고 있었으므로, 곧장 우리 선착장에서 일하던, 상을 탄 적이 있는 노잡이의 지도를 받기로 했다. 그는 내 새 친구들이 내게 소개해 준 사람이었다."], ["This practical authority confused me very much by saying I had the arm of a blacksmith.", "이 실전의 권위자는 내게 대장장이의 팔을 지녔다고 말함으로써 나를 무척 당혹스럽게 했다."], ["If he could have known how nearly the compliment lost him his pupil, I doubt if he would have paid it.", "만약 그가 그 칭찬으로 자기 제자를 잃을 뻔했다는 것을 알았더라면, 과연 그런 말을 했을지 의문이다."]], [["There was a supper-tray after we got home at night, and I think we should all have enjoyed ourselves, but for a rather disagreeable domestic occurrence. Mr.", "그날 밤 집에 돌아온 뒤에는 야식 쟁반이 차려졌고, 다소 불쾌한 집안일만 없었더라면 우리 모두 즐거운 시간을 보냈으리라 생각한다."], ["Pocket was in good spirits, when a housemaid came in, and said, “If you please, sir, I should wish to speak to you.”", "포켓 씨가 기분이 좋아 보이던 차에, 하녀 하나가 들어와 말했다. \"죄송하지만 나리, 나리께 드릴 말씀이 있습니다.\""]], [["“Speak to your master?” said Mrs.", "\"네 주인에게 말을 하겠다고?\""], ["Pocket, whose dignity was roused again.", "다시금 위엄이 발동한 포켓 부인이 말했다."], ["“How can you think of such a thing?", "\"어떻게 그런 생각을 다 하느냐?"], ["Go and speak to Flopson.", "가서 플롭슨에게 말해."], ["Or speak to me—at some other time.”", "아니면 나한테 말하든지 — 다른 때에.\""]], [["“Begging your pardon, ma’am,”", "\"송구합니다만, 부인,\""], ["returned the housemaid, “I should wish to speak at once, and to speak to master.”", "하녀가 대꾸했다. \"지금 당장, 그리고 나리께 말씀드리고 싶습니다.\""]], [["Hereupon, Mr. Pocket went out of the room, and we made the best of ourselves until he came back.", "이에 포켓 씨가 방을 나갔고, 우리는 그가 돌아올 때까지 저마다 어색함을 견뎠다."]], [["“This is a pretty thing, Belinda!” said Mr.", "\"이거 참 가관이구려, 벨린다!\""], ["Pocket, returning with a countenance expressive of grief and despair.", "슬픔과 절망이 어린 얼굴로 돌아오며 포켓 씨가 말했다."], ["“Here’s the cook lying insensibly drunk on the kitchen floor, with a large bundle of fresh butter made up in the cupboard ready to sell for grease!”", "\"글쎄 요리사가 부엌 바닥에 인사불성으로 취해 뻗어 있고, 찬장에는 기름으로 팔아넘기려고 싱싱한 버터를 한 다발 큼지막하게 꾸려 놓았지 뭐요!\""]], [["Mrs. Pocket instantly showed much amiable emotion, and said, “This is that odious Sophia’s doing!”", "포켓 부인은 즉시 자못 상냥한 감정을 드러내며 말했다. \"이건 그 밉살스러운 소피아의 짓이에요!\""]], [["“What do you mean, Belinda?”", "\"무슨 소리요, 벨린다?\""], ["demanded Mr. Pocket.", "포켓 씨가 물었다."]], [["“Sophia has told you,” said Mrs.", "\"소피아가 당신에게 일러바친 거잖아요.\""], ["Pocket. “Did I not see her with my own eyes and hear her with my own ears, come into the room just now and ask to speak to you?”", "포켓 부인이 말했다. \"방금 그 애가 방으로 들어와 당신께 말씀드리겠다고 청하는 것을 내가 두 눈으로 똑똑히 보고 두 귀로 똑똑히 듣지 않았느냐고요?\""]], [["“But has she not taken me downstairs, Belinda,”", "\"하지만 벨린다, 그 애가 나를 아래층으로 데려가서,\""], ["returned Mr. Pocket, “and shown me the woman, and the bundle too?”", "포켓 씨가 대꾸했다. \"그 여자도, 그 버터 다발도 보여 주지 않았소?\""]], [["“And do you defend her, Matthew,”", "\"그래서 당신은, 매슈,\" 포켓 부인이 말했다."], ["said Mrs. Pocket, “for making mischief?”", "\"그 애가 분란을 일으킨 걸 두둔하는 거예요?\""]], [["Mr. Pocket uttered a dismal groan.", "포켓 씨는 침울한 신음을 내뱉었다."]], [["“Am I, grandpapa’s granddaughter, to be nothing in the house?”", "\"이 할아버지의 손녀인 내가, 이 집에서 아무것도 아니란 말이에요?\""], ["said Mrs. Pocket.", "포켓 부인이 말했다."], ["“Besides, the cook has always been a very nice respectful woman, and said in the most natural manner when she came to look after the situation, that she felt I was born to be a Duchess.”", "\"게다가 그 요리사는 늘 아주 착하고 공손한 여자였어요. 이 일자리를 보러 왔을 때도 더없이 자연스러운 태도로, 내가 공작 부인이 되려고 태어난 사람이라는 게 느껴진다고 했단 말이에요.\""]], [["There was a sofa where Mr. Pocket stood, and he dropped upon it in the attitude of the Dying Gladiator.", "포켓 씨가 서 있던 자리에는 소파가 하나 있었는데, 그는 「죽어 가는 검투사」 같은 자세로 그 위에 풀썩 쓰러졌다."], ["Still in that attitude he said, with a hollow voice, “Good night, Mr. Pip,”", "여전히 그 자세로 그는 공허한 목소리로 \"안녕히 주무시게, 핍 군\""], ["when I deemed it advisable to go to bed and leave him.", "하고 말했고, 나는 그를 두고 잠자리에 드는 편이 좋겠다고 판단했다."]], [["# Chapter XXIV.", "# Chapter XXIV."]], [["After two or three days, when I had established myself in my room and had gone backwards and forwards to London several times, and had ordered all I wanted of my tradesmen, Mr.", "이삼일 뒤, 내 방에 자리를 잡고, 런던을 여러 차례 오가고, 상인들에게 필요한 물건을 모두 주문하고 나자, 포켓 씨와 나는 오랜 이야기를 나누었다."], ["Pocket and I had a long talk together. He knew more of my intended career than I knew myself, for he referred to his having been told by Mr. Jaggers that I was not designed for any profession, and that I should be well enough educated for my destiny if I could “hold my own”", "그는 내가 앞으로 걸어갈 길에 대해 나 자신보다 더 잘 알고 있었는데, 재거스 씨로부터 내가 어떤 전문 직업을 위해 길러지는 것이 아니며, 만약 내가 유복한 환경에 놓인 평범한 젊은이들 사이에서 \"제 몫을 해낼\""], ["with the average of young men in prosperous circumstances. I acquiesced, of course, knowing nothing to the contrary.", "수 있을 정도라면 내 운명에 걸맞게 충분히 교육받은 셈이 된다는 말을 들었다고 했다. 나는 물론 그 말에 동의했으니, 달리 알고 있는 바가 없었기 때문이다."]], [["He advised my attending certain places in London, for the acquisition of such mere rudiments as I wanted, and my investing him with the functions of explainer and director of all my studies.", "그는 내가 원하는 그 정도의 기초 지식을 얻기 위해 런던의 몇몇 곳에 다닐 것을 권했고, 내 모든 학업의 해설자이자 지도자라는 직분을 자신에게 맡기라고 했다."], ["He hoped that with intelligent assistance I should meet with little to discourage me, and should soon be able to dispense with any aid but his.", "그는 지각 있는 도움을 받으면 내가 낙담할 일은 거의 없을 것이며, 머지않아 그의 도움 외에는 어떤 조력도 필요 없게 되리라 기대했다."], ["Through his way of saying this, and much more to similar purpose, he placed himself on confidential terms with me in an admirable manner; and I may state at once that he was always so zealous and honourable in fulfilling his compact with me, that he made me zealous and honourable in fulfilling mine with him.", "이렇게 말하는 방식과, 그 비슷한 취지의 여러 말을 통해 그는 감탄스러울 만큼 자연스럽게 나와 흉허물 없는 사이가 되었다. 그리고 한마디로 말하건대, 그는 나와의 약속을 이행하는 데 늘 열성적이고 정직했으므로, 나 역시 그와의 약속을 이행하는 데 열성적이고 정직해질 수밖에 없었다."], ["If he had shown indifference as a master, I have no doubt I should have returned the compliment as a pupil; he gave me no such excuse, and each of us did the other justice.", "만약 그가 스승으로서 무관심한 태도를 보였다면, 나도 제자로서 그에 걸맞게 응했으리라는 데에는 의심의 여지가 없다. 그러나 그는 내게 그런 구실을 주지 않았고, 우리는 서로에게 정당히 대했다."], ["Nor did I ever regard him as having anything ludicrous about him—or anything but what was serious, honest, and good—in his tutor communication with me.", "또한 나는 그가 나를 가르치며 보이는 태도에서 우스꽝스러운 구석을 — 진지하고 정직하며 선량한 것 외의 무엇을 — 단 한 번도 느낀 적이 없었다."]], [["When these points were settled, and so far carried out as that I had begun to work in earnest, it occurred to me that if I could retain my bedroom in Barnard’s Inn, my life would be agreeably varied, while my manners would be none the worse for Herbert’s society. Mr.", "이런 점들이 정리되고, 내가 본격적으로 공부를 시작할 만큼 실행에 옮겨지자, 만약 바너드 여관의 내 침실을 그대로 유지할 수 있다면 내 생활이 한결 다채로워질 것이며, 게다가 허버트와 어울려 지내는 것이 내 몸가짐에 해가 될 리 없으리라는 생각이 떠올랐다."], ["Pocket did not object to this arrangement, but urged that before any step could possibly be taken in it, it must be submitted to my guardian.", "포켓 씨는 이 계획에 반대하지 않았으나, 그 일에 어떤 조처를 취하기에 앞서 반드시 내 후견인에게 의논해야 한다고 강조했다."], ["I felt that this delicacy arose out of the consideration that the plan would save Herbert some expense, so I went off to Little Britain and imparted my wish to Mr. Jaggers.", "나는 이 신중함이 그 계획이 허버트의 비용을 얼마간 덜어 주리라는 고려에서 비롯된 것임을 느꼈으므로, 리틀 브리튼으로 가서 재거스 씨에게 내 바람을 전했다."]], [["“If I could buy the furniture now hired for me,”", "\"지금 제게 빌려 준 가구를 사들일 수 있다면,\" 내가 말했다."], ["said I, “and one or two other little things, I should be quite at home there.”", "\"그리고 한두 가지 자질구레한 물건도 더해서요, 거기서 아주 편안히 지낼 수 있을 것 같습니다.\""]], [["“Go it!” said Mr.", "\"해 보게!\""], ["Jaggers, with a short laugh.", "재거스 씨가 짤막하게 웃으며 말했다."], ["“I told you you’d get on. Well! How much do you want?”", "\"자네가 잘 풀려 나갈 거라고 했잖나. 그래! 얼마나 필요한가?\""]], [["I said I didn’t know how much.", "나는 얼마나 필요한지 모르겠다고 했다."]], [["“Come!”", "\"이봐!\""], ["retorted Mr. Jaggers. “How much?", "재거스 씨가 받아쳤다. \"얼마나?"], ["Fifty pounds?”", "오십 파운드?\""]], [["“O, not nearly so much.”", "\"아, 그렇게까지는 안 됩니다.\""]], [["“Five pounds?”", "\"오 파운드?\""], ["said Mr. Jaggers.", "재거스 씨가 말했다."]], [["This was such a great fall, that I said in discomfiture, “O, more than that.”", "이것은 너무나 큰 추락이었으므로, 나는 당혹스러워하며 말했다. \"아, 그보다는 더 듭니다.\""]], [["“More than that, eh!”", "\"그보다 더 든다, 응!\""], ["retorted Mr. Jaggers, lying in wait for me, with his hands in his pockets, his head on one side, and his eyes on the wall behind me; “how much more?”", "재거스 씨가 두 손을 주머니에 넣고, 고개를 한쪽으로 기울이고, 내 뒤편의 벽에 눈길을 둔 채, 나를 옭아매려는 듯 받아쳤다. \"얼마나 더?\""]], [["“It is so difficult to fix a sum,”", "\"액수를 정하기가 참 어렵네요.\""], ["said I, hesitating.", "나는 머뭇거리며 말했다."]], [["“Come!”", "\"이봐!\""], ["said Mr. Jaggers.", "재거스 씨가 말했다."], ["“Let’s get at it.", "\"이렇게 따져 보세."], ["Twice five; will that do?", "오의 두 배면 되겠나?"], ["Three times five; will that do?", "오의 세 배면 되겠나?"], ["Four times five; will that do?”", "오의 네 배면 되겠나?\""]], [["I said I thought that would do handsomely.", "나는 그 정도면 넉넉하겠다고 했다."]], [["“Four times five will do handsomely, will it?”", "\"오의 네 배면 넉넉하겠다, 그렇단 말이지?\""], ["said Mr. Jaggers, knitting his brows.", "재거스 씨가 눈썹을 찌푸리며 말했다."], ["“Now, what do you make of four times five?”", "\"자, 오의 네 배면 자네는 얼마라고 보는가?\""]], [["“What do I make of it?”", "\"제가 얼마라고 보느냐고요?\""]], [["“Ah!”", "\"그래!\""], ["said Mr. Jaggers; “how much?”", "재거스 씨가 말했다. \"얼마지?\""]], [["“I suppose you make it twenty pounds,” said I, smiling.", "\"이십 파운드로 치시는 거겠지요.\" 내가 미소 지으며 말했다."]], [["“Never mind what I make it, my friend,”", "\"내가 얼마로 치든 그건 신경 쓰지 말게, 친구.\""], ["observed Mr. Jaggers, with a knowing and contradictory toss of his head.", "재거스 씨가 짐짓 알겠다는 듯, 그러면서도 반박하듯 고개를 까딱하며 말했다."], ["“I want to know what you make it.”", "\"나는 자네가 얼마로 보는지를 알고 싶은 거야.\""]], [["“Twenty pounds, of course.”", "\"물론 이십 파운드지요.\""]], [["“Wemmick!”", "\"웨믹!\""], ["said Mr. Jaggers, opening his office door.", "재거스 씨가 자기 사무실 문을 열며 말했다."], ["“Take Mr. Pip’s written order, and pay him twenty pounds.”", "\"핍 씨의 서면 지시를 받아서 이십 파운드를 지급하게.\""]], [["This strongly marked way of doing business made a strongly marked impression on me, and that not of an agreeable kind. Mr.", "이렇게 유난히 도드라진 일 처리 방식은 내게 유난히 도드라진 인상을 남겼는데, 그것은 결코 유쾌한 종류의 인상이 아니었다."], ["Jaggers never laughed; but he wore great bright creaking boots, and, in poising himself on these boots, with his large head bent down and his eyebrows joined together, awaiting an answer, he sometimes caused the boots to creak, as if they laughed in a dry and suspicious way.", "재거스 씨는 결코 웃지 않았다. 그러나 그는 크고 번쩍거리며 삐걱대는 장화를 신고 다녔는데, 그 장화 위에서 몸의 균형을 잡고, 커다란 머리를 숙이고, 두 눈썹을 맞붙인 채 대답을 기다리노라면, 이따금 그 장화가 삐걱거리게 만들어, 마치 그 장화가 메마르고 미심쩍은 투로 웃는 것처럼 보였다."], ["As he happened to go out now, and as Wemmick was brisk and talkative, I said to Wemmick that I hardly knew what to make of Mr. Jaggers’s manner.", "마침 그가 지금 밖으로 나갔고, 웨믹은 활달하고 말이 많은 편이었으므로, 나는 웨믹에게 재거스 씨의 태도를 어떻게 받아들여야 할지 도무지 모르겠다고 말했다."]], [["“Tell him that, and he’ll take it as a compliment,”", "\"그 말을 그분께 하면, 그분은 칭찬으로 받아들일 겁니다.\""], ["answered Wemmick; “he don’t mean that you should know what to make of it.—Oh!”", "웨믹이 대답했다. \"그분은 당신이 그걸 어떻게 받아들일지 알기를 바라지도 않으니까요. 아!\""], ["for I looked surprised, “it’s not personal; it’s professional: only professional.”", "내가 놀란 표정을 짓자 그가 덧붙였다. \"그건 개인적인 게 아니라 직업적인 거예요. 순전히 직업적인 거죠.\""]], [["Wemmick was at his desk, lunching—and crunching—on a dry hard biscuit; pieces of which he threw from time to time into his slit of a mouth, as if he were posting them.", "웨믹은 자기 책상에 앉아 마른 딱딱한 비스킷으로 점심을 먹고 있었다. 정확히는 우두둑 씹고 있었다. 그는 그 조각들을 이따금 자기 입의 틈새로 던져 넣었는데, 마치 그것들을 우체통에 부치는 것 같았다."]], [["“Always seems to me,”", "\"제가 보기에 그분은 늘,\""], ["said Wemmick, “as if he had set a man-trap and was watching it.", "웨믹이 말했다. \"사람 잡는 덫을 놓고 그것을 지켜보고 있는 것 같단 말입니다."], ["Suddenly—click—you’re caught!”", "그러다 별안간 — 철컥 — 잡히는 거죠!\""]], [["Without remarking that man-traps were not among the amenities of life, I said I supposed he was very skilful?", "사람 잡는 덫이 인생의 즐거움 축에는 들지 못한다는 말은 굳이 하지 않은 채, 나는 그가 무척 솜씨가 좋은가 보다고 했다."]], [["“Deep,” said Wemmick, “as Australia.”", "\"깊지요,\" 웨믹이 말했다."], ["Pointing with his pen at the office floor, to express that Australia was understood, for the purposes of the figure, to be symmetrically on the opposite spot of the globe.", "\"오스트레일리아만큼이나요.\" 그는 펜으로 사무실 바닥을 가리켰는데, 이 비유의 취지상 오스트레일리아가 지구 정반대편의 대칭점에 있다고 보면 된다는 뜻이었다."], ["“If there was anything deeper,”", "\"그보다 더 깊은 게 있다면,\""], ["added Wemmick, bringing his pen to paper, “he’d be it.”", "웨믹이 펜을 종이에 갖다 대며 덧붙였다. \"그분이 바로 그것일 겁니다.\""]], [["Then, I said I supposed he had a fine business, and Wemmick said, “Ca-pi-tal!”", "이어서 나는 그가 좋은 사업을 꾸리고 있나 보다고 했고, 웨믹은 \"굉-장-하-죠!\""], ["Then I asked if there were many clerks? to which he replied,—", "하고 말했다. 그다음 나는 서기가 많으냐고 물었고, 그가 이렇게 대답했다."]], [["“We don’t run much into clerks, because there’s only one Jaggers, and people won’t have him at second hand.", "\"우리는 서기를 그리 많이 두지 않습니다. 재거스라는 사람은 오직 한 분뿐이고, 사람들은 그분을 거쳐 온 손에서 받으려 하질 않으니까요."], ["There are only four of us.", "우리는 모두 넷뿐입니다."], ["Would you like to see ’em? You are one of us, as I may say.”", "그들을 보시겠어요? 당신도 어떤 의미에선 우리 식구나 다름없으니까요.\""]], [["I accepted the offer. When Mr.", "나는 그 제안을 받아들였다."], ["Wemmick had put all the biscuit into the post, and had paid me my money from a cash-box in a safe, the key of which safe he kept somewhere down his back and produced from his coat-collar like an iron-pigtail, we went upstairs.", "웨믹 씨가 비스킷을 죄다 우체통에 부치고, 금고 안의 현금 상자에서 내 돈을 지급한 다음 — 그 금고 열쇠는 등 뒤 어딘가에 간직해 두었다가 무쇠 변발이라도 되는 양 외투 깃에서 끄집어냈다 — 우리는 위층으로 올라갔다."], ["The house was dark and shabby, and the greasy shoulders that had left their mark in Mr. Jaggers’s room seemed to have been shuffling up and down the staircase for years.", "그 집은 어둡고 초라했으며, 재거스 씨의 방에 자국을 남긴 그 기름진 어깨들이 수년에 걸쳐 그 계단을 오르내리며 비벼 댄 듯했다."], ["In the front first floor, a clerk who looked something between a publican and a rat-catcher—a large pale, puffed, swollen man—was attentively engaged with three or four people of shabby appearance, whom he treated as unceremoniously as everybody seemed to be treated who contributed to Mr. Jaggers’s coffers.", "정면 이 층에서는, 술집 주인과 쥐잡이꾼의 중간쯤 되어 보이는 서기 한 명이 — 덩치가 크고 핼쑥하며 부석부석 부어오른 사내였다 — 행색이 초라한 서너 사람을 정성껏 상대하고 있었는데, 그는 재거스 씨의 금고에 보탬이 되는 이들이라면 누구나 받는 듯한 무례한 대접 그대로 그들을 대했다. \"증거를 모으는 중이지요,\""], ["“Getting evidence together,” said Mr.", "우리가 나오면서 웨믹 씨가 말했다."], ["Wemmick, as we came out, “for the Bailey.”", "\"올드 베일리에 낼 것을요.\""], ["In the room over that, a little flabby terrier of a clerk with dangling hair (his cropping seemed to have been forgotten when he was a puppy) was similarly engaged with a man with weak eyes, whom Mr. Wemmick presented to me as a smelter who kept his pot always boiling, and who would melt me anything I pleased,—and who was in an excessive white-perspiration, as if he had been trying his art on himself.", "그 위층 방에서는, 늘어진 털을 가진 작고 물렁한 테리어 같은 서기가 — 강아지였을 적에 털 깎아 주기를 잊은 듯했다 — 마찬가지로 눈이 침침한 사내를 상대하고 있었는데, 웨믹 씨는 그자가 늘 도가니를 끓여 두고 내가 원하는 것은 무엇이든 녹여 줄 수 있는 제련공이라고 소개했다. 그 사내는 마치 제 솜씨를 제 몸에 시험해 본 것처럼 온통 허옇게 땀을 흘리고 있었다."], ["In a back room, a high-shouldered man with a face-ache tied up in dirty flannel, who was dressed in old black clothes that bore the appearance of having been waxed, was stooping over his work of making fair copies of the notes of the other two gentlemen, for Mr. Jaggers’s own use.", "뒤편 방에서는, 어깨가 솟고 얼굴이 아파 더러운 플란넬로 싸맨 사내가 — 밀랍을 먹인 듯한 낡고 검은 옷을 입고 있었다 — 몸을 구부려, 앞서의 두 신사가 적은 기록을 재거스 씨가 직접 쓰도록 정서하는 일에 매달려 있었다."]], [["This was all the establishment.", "이것이 사무소의 전부였다."], ["When we went downstairs again, Wemmick led me into my guardian’s room, and said, “This you’ve seen already.”", "우리가 다시 아래층으로 내려오자, 웨믹은 나를 후견인의 방으로 데리고 들어가 말했다. \"여기는 이미 보셨지요.\""]], [["“Pray,”", "\"실례지만,\""], ["said I, as the two odious casts with the twitchy leer upon them caught my sight again, “whose likenesses are those?”", "경련하듯 곁눈질하는 그 두 흉측한 흉상이 다시 내 눈에 들어왔으므로 내가 말했다. \"저것들은 누구의 얼굴을 본뜬 겁니까?\""]], [["“These?” said Wemmick, getting upon a chair, and blowing the dust off the horrible heads before bringing them down.", "\"이것들요?\" 웨믹이 의자 위에 올라서서, 그 끔찍한 머리들을 내리기에 앞서 먼지를 후 불며 말했다."], ["“These are two celebrated ones.", "\"이건 유명한 두 사람입니다."], ["Famous clients of ours that got us a world of credit.", "우리에게 엄청난 명성을 안겨 준 이름난 고객들이죠."], ["This chap (why you must have come down in the night and been peeping into the inkstand, to get this blot upon your eyebrow, you old rascal!) murdered his master, and, considering that he wasn’t brought up to evidence, didn’t plan it badly.”", "이 친구로 말하자면 (이런, 자네는 밤에 몰래 내려와 잉크병 속을 들여다본 모양이군, 눈썹에 이런 얼룩이 묻은 걸 보니, 이 늙은 악당아!) 제 주인을 살해했는데, 증인으로 자라난 처지가 아니었던 걸 감안하면 꽤나 영리하게 일을 꾸민 셈입니다.\""]], [["“Is it like him?”", "\"그자를 닮았나요?\""], ["I asked, recoiling from the brute, as Wemmick spat upon his eyebrow and gave it a rub with his sleeve.", "웨믹이 그 흉상의 눈썹에 침을 뱉고 소매로 문질러 닦는 동안, 나는 그 짐승 같은 얼굴에서 움찔 물러서며 물었다."]], [["“Like him? It’s himself, you know.", "\"닮았느냐고요? 이건 바로 그자 자신인걸요."], ["The cast was made in Newgate, directly after he was taken down.", "이 본은 그자를 끌어내린 직후에 뉴게이트에서 떴습니다."], ["You had a particular fancy for me, hadn’t you, Old Artful?”", "자네는 나를 유달리 좋아했지, 안 그런가, 이 늙은 능구렁이?\""], ["said Wemmick.", "웨믹이 말했다."], ["He then explained this affectionate apostrophe, by touching his brooch representing the lady and the weeping willow at the tomb with the urn upon it, and saying, “Had it made for me, express!”", "그러고는 항아리가 얹힌 무덤 곁에서 버드나무를 두고 우는 여인을 새긴 자기 브로치를 만지며 이 다정한 호칭의 연유를 설명했다. \"나를 위해 일부러 맞춰 준 거랍니다!\""]], [["“Is the lady anybody?” said I.", "\"그 여인은 누구입니까?\" 내가 말했다."]], [["“No,” returned Wemmick.", "\"아닙니다,\" 웨믹이 대답했다."], ["“Only his game. (You liked your bit of game, didn’t you?) No; deuce a bit of a lady in the case, Mr.", "\"그저 이 친구가 꾸며 낸 거예요. (자네는 그런 잔재미를 좋아했지, 안 그래?) 아니요, 이 사건에 여인이라곤 눈곱만치도 없습니다, 핍 씨."], ["Pip, except one,—and she wasn’t of this slender lady-like sort, and you wouldn’t have caught her looking after this urn, unless there was something to drink in it.”", "단 한 사람만 빼고요. 그런데 그 여자는 이렇게 가냘프고 숙녀 같은 부류가 아니었고, 항아리 안에 마실 거라도 들어 있지 않은 다음에야 그 여자가 이 항아리를 들여다보고 있는 꼴은 결코 보지 못했을 겁니다.\""], ["Wemmick’s attention being thus directed to his brooch, he put down the cast, and polished the brooch with his pocket-handkerchief.", "웨믹의 주의가 이렇게 자기 브로치로 쏠리자, 그는 흉상을 내려놓고 손수건으로 브로치를 닦았다."]], [["“Did that other creature come to the same end?” I asked.", "\"저 다른 자도 같은 최후를 맞았나요?\" 내가 물었다."], ["“He has the same look.”", "\"같은 표정을 짓고 있네요.\""]], [["“You’re right,”", "\"맞습니다,\""], ["said Wemmick; “it’s the genuine look.", "웨믹이 말했다. \"그게 진짜 그 표정이죠."], ["Much as if one nostril was caught up with a horse-hair and a little fish-hook.", "마치 한쪽 콧구멍을 말총과 작은 낚싯바늘로 위로 끌어 올린 것 같지요."], ["Yes, he came to the same end; quite the natural end here, I assure you.", "그래요, 이자도 같은 최후를 맞았습니다. 여기선 지극히 자연스러운 최후이지요, 장담합니다."], ["He forged wills, this blade did, if he didn’t also put the supposed testators to sleep too.", "이 친구는 유언장을 위조했어요, 추정 유언자들을 잠재워 버리지까지는 않았다 쳐도 말입니다."], ["You were a gentlemanly Cove, though” (Mr.", "그래도 자네는 신사다운 작자였지,\""], ["Wemmick was again apostrophising), “and you said you could write Greek.", "(웨믹이 다시 그 흉상을 향해 말을 건넸다.) \"그리고 그리스어를 쓸 줄 안다고 했지."], ["Yah, Bounceable!", "야, 이 허풍쟁이야!"], ["What a liar you were!", "자네 같은 거짓말쟁이는 없었어!"], ["I never met such a liar as you!”", "자네 같은 거짓말쟁이는 평생 만나 본 적이 없다고!\""], ["Before putting his late friend on his shelf again, Wemmick touched the largest of his mourning rings and said, “Sent out to buy it for me, only the day before.”", "이 고인이 된 친구를 다시 선반에 올려놓기에 앞서, 웨믹은 자기 추모용 반지 중에서 가장 큰 것을 만지며 말했다. \"바로 그 전날 나를 위해 사다 달라고 사람을 보냈었지요.\""]], [["While he was putting up the other cast and coming down from the chair, the thought crossed my mind that all his personal jewelry was derived from like sources.", "그가 다른 흉상을 마저 올려놓고 의자에서 내려오는 동안, 그의 몸에 걸린 장신구가 모두 비슷한 출처에서 비롯되었으리라는 생각이 내 머리를 스쳤다."], ["As he had shown no diffidence on the subject, I ventured on the liberty of asking him the question, when he stood before me, dusting his hands.", "그가 이 화제에 조금도 거리낌을 보이지 않았으므로, 그가 손을 털며 내 앞에 섰을 때, 나는 외람되이 그 질문을 던져 보았다."]], [["“O yes,”", "\"아, 그렇습니다,\""], ["he returned, “these are all gifts of that kind.", "그가 대답했다. \"이건 다 그런 식의 선물이죠."], ["One brings another, you see; that’s the way of it.", "하나가 또 하나를 불러오게 마련입니다, 아시겠지요. 그게 이치예요."], ["I always take ’em. They’re curiosities.", "저는 그것들을 늘 받아 둡니다. 진귀한 물건이니까요."], ["And they’re property.", "게다가 재산이기도 하고요."], ["They may not be worth much, but, after all, they’re property and portable.", "값나가는 건 아닐지 몰라도, 어쨌든 재산이고 게다가 들고 다닐 수 있는 거니까요."], ["It don’t signify to you with your brilliant lookout, but as to myself, my guiding-star always is, ‘Get hold of portable property’.”", "당신처럼 앞날이 찬란한 분께야 대수롭지 않은 일이겠지만, 저로 말하자면, 제 길잡이별은 언제나 '들고 다닐 수 있는 재산을 손에 넣어라'랍니다.\""]], [["When I had rendered homage to this light, he went on to say, in a friendly manner:—", "내가 이 신념에 경의를 표하자, 그는 다정한 태도로 말을 이었다."]], [["“If at any odd time when you have nothing better to do, you wouldn’t mind coming over to see me at Walworth, I could offer you a bed, and I should consider it an honour.", "\"혹 언젠가 달리 할 일이 없을 적에, 월워스로 저를 보러 와 주신다면, 잠자리도 마련해 드릴 수 있고, 저로서는 영광으로 여기겠습니다."], ["I have not much to show you; but such two or three curiosities as I have got you might like to look over; and I am fond of a bit of garden and a summer-house.”", "보여 드릴 만한 게 그리 많지는 않습니다만, 제가 가진 두어 가지 진기한 물건쯤은 둘러보실 만할 거고요. 게다가 저는 자그마한 정원과 정자를 무척 아낀답니다.\""]], [["I said I should be delighted to accept his hospitality.", "나는 기꺼이 그 환대를 받겠노라고 했다."]], [["“Thankee,”", "\"고맙습니다,\""], ["said he; “then we’ll consider that it’s to come off, when convenient to you.", "그가 말했다. \"그럼 형편이 닿는 대로 그렇게 하기로 하지요."], ["Have you dined with Mr. Jaggers yet?”", "재거스 씨와 식사는 아직 안 해 보셨지요?\""]], [["“Not yet.”", "\"아직요.\""]], [["“Well,”", "\"그렇군요,\""], ["said Wemmick, “he’ll give you wine, and good wine.", "웨믹이 말했다. \"그분은 당신에게 포도주를 내실 겁니다, 그것도 좋은 포도주를요."], ["I’ll give you punch, and not bad punch.", "저는 펀치를 대접하지요, 그것도 그리 나쁘지 않은 펀치를요."], ["And now I’ll tell you something.", "그리고 이제 한 가지 일러 드리겠습니다."], ["When you go to dine with Mr. Jaggers, look at his housekeeper.”", "재거스 씨와 식사하러 가시거든, 그분의 가정부를 잘 보십시오.\""]], [["“Shall I see something very uncommon?”", "\"무척 보기 드문 것을 보게 됩니까?\""]], [["“Well,”", "\"글쎄요,\""], ["said Wemmick, “you’ll see a wild beast tamed.", "웨믹이 말했다. \"길들여진 야수를 보시게 될 겁니다."], ["Not so very uncommon, you’ll tell me.", "그리 보기 드문 건 아니라고 하시겠지요."], ["I reply, that depends on the original wildness of the beast, and the amount of taming.", "그건 그 짐승이 본디 얼마나 사나웠는지, 또 얼마나 길들였는지에 달렸다고 답하겠습니다."], ["It won’t lower your opinion of Mr. Jaggers’s powers.", "그걸 보시더라도 재거스 씨의 역량에 대한 당신의 평가가 깎이지는 않을 겁니다."], ["Keep your eye on it.”", "눈여겨보세요.\""]], [["I told him I would do so, with all the interest and curiosity that his preparation awakened.", "나는 그의 그런 사전 언질이 불러일으킨 온갖 흥미와 호기심을 안고서 그렇게 하겠다고 했다."], ["As I was taking my departure, he asked me if I would like to devote five minutes to seeing Mr. Jaggers “at it?”", "내가 막 떠나려 할 때, 그는 오 분쯤 짬을 내어 재거스 씨가 \"일하는\" 모습을 보겠느냐고 물었다."]], [["For several reasons, and not least because I didn’t clearly know what Mr. Jaggers would be found to be “at,” I replied in the affirmative.", "몇 가지 이유에서, 그중에서도 재거스 씨가 대체 무슨 \"일\"을 하는 모습으로 발견될지 분명히 알지 못했다는 이유가 적지 않아, 나는 그러겠다고 대답했다."], ["We dived into the City, and came up in a crowded police-court, where a blood-relation (in the murderous sense) of the deceased, with the fanciful taste in brooches, was standing at the bar, uncomfortably chewing something; while my guardian had a woman under examination or cross-examination,—I don’t know which,—and was striking her, and the bench, and everybody present, with awe.", "우리는 시내 한복판으로 파고들었다가, 사람들로 붐비는 경찰 법정으로 나왔는데, 그곳에서는 브로치에 별난 취향을 가졌던 그 고인의 (살인이라는 의미에서의) 혈육 한 사람이 피고석에 서서 거북하게 무언가를 우물거리고 있었다. 한편 내 후견인은 한 여자를 신문, 혹은 반대 신문 — 어느 쪽인지는 모르겠다 — 하고 있었는데, 그 여자와 판사석과 그 자리에 있는 모든 이에게 경외감을 안기고 있었다."], ["If anybody, of whatsoever degree, said a word that he didn’t approve of, he instantly required to have it “taken down.”", "누구든, 어떤 신분의 사람이든, 그가 못마땅해하는 말을 한마디라도 하면, 그는 즉각 그것을 \"기록해 둘\" 것을 요구했다."], ["If anybody wouldn’t make an admission, he said, “I’ll have it out of you!”", "누구든 자백하려 들지 않으면, 그는 \"내가 자네한테서 기어이 끄집어내고 말겠어!\""], ["and if anybody made an admission, he said, “Now I have got you!”", "하고 말했고, 누구든 자백을 하면, \"이제 자네를 잡았군!\" 하고 말했다."], ["The magistrates shivered under a single bite of his finger.", "치안 판사들은 그가 손가락을 한 번 깨무는 것만으로도 부르르 떨었다."], ["Thieves and thief-takers hung in dread rapture on his words, and shrank when a hair of his eyebrows turned in their direction.", "도둑들과 도둑 잡는 자들이 두려운 황홀경에 빠져 그의 말에 매달렸고, 그의 눈썹 한 올이 자기네 쪽으로 돌아오기만 해도 움츠러들었다."], ["Which side he was on I couldn’t make out, for he seemed to me to be grinding the whole place in a mill; I only know that when I stole out on tiptoe, he was not on the side of the bench; for, he was making the legs of the old gentleman who presided, quite convulsive under the table, by his denunciations of his conduct as the representative of British law and justice in that chair that day.", "그가 어느 편인지 나로서는 도무지 알 수 없었으니, 내게는 그가 그곳 전체를 방앗간에 넣고 갈아 대고 있는 것처럼 보였기 때문이다. 다만 내가 발끝으로 살그머니 빠져나올 무렵 그가 판사석 편은 아니었다는 것만은 분명히 안다. 그도 그럴 것이, 그는 그날 그 자리에서 영국의 법과 정의를 대표하는 자로서 늙은 재판장의 처신을 규탄함으로써, 탁자 아래 그 노신사의 두 다리를 완전히 경련으로 떨게 만들고 있었으니까."]], [["# Chapter XXV.", "# Chapter XXV."]], [["Bentley Drummle, who was so sulky a fellow that he even took up a book as if its writer had done him an injury, did not take up an acquaintance in a more agreeable spirit.", "벤틀리 드러믈은 어찌나 뚱한 위인이었던지, 책 한 권을 집어 들 때조차 마치 그 책을 쓴 사람이 자신에게 해코지라도 한 것처럼 굴었으니, 사람을 사귈 때라고 해서 더 상냥한 마음가짐으로 임할 리 없었다."], ["Heavy in figure, movement, and comprehension,—in the sluggish complexion of his face, and in the large, awkward tongue that seemed to loll about in his mouth as he himself lolled about in a room,—he was idle, proud, niggardly, reserved, and suspicious.", "몸집도 동작도 이해력도 굼떴고 — 얼굴빛은 칙칙하니 활기가 없었으며, 크고 어설픈 혀는 그가 방 안을 어슬렁거리듯 입 안에서 늘어져 어슬렁대는 듯했으니 — 그는 게으르고, 거만하고, 인색하고, 속을 드러내지 않으며, 의심이 많은 사람이었다."], ["He came of rich people down in Somersetshire, who had nursed this combination of qualities until they made the discovery that it was just of age and a blockhead.", "그는 서머싯셔 시골의 부유한 집안 출신이었는데, 그 집안은 이런 자질의 조합을 고이 길러 오다가 마침내 그것이 다 자라 성년에 이르도록 멍청이임을 발견하기에 이르렀다."], ["Thus, Bentley Drummle had come to Mr. Pocket when he was a head taller than that gentleman, and half a dozen heads thicker than most gentlemen.", "그리하여 벤틀리 드러믈이 포켓 선생에게 왔을 때, 그는 그 신사보다 머리 하나만큼 키가 컸고, 여느 신사들보다 머리 여섯 개만큼 둔했다."]], [["Startop had been spoilt by a weak mother and kept at home when he ought to have been at school, but he was devotedly attached to her, and admired her beyond measure.", "스타톱은 마음 약한 어머니 탓에 응석받이로 자라, 마땅히 학교에 가 있어야 할 나이에 집에 머물렀으나, 어머니를 극진히 따랐고 한없이 흠모했다."], ["He had a woman’s delicacy of feature, and was—“as you may see, though you never saw her,”", "그는 여자처럼 섬세한 이목구비를 지녔는데 — \"자네는 그 부인을 한 번도 본 적이 없겠지만, 보면 알 거야.\""], ["said Herbert to me—“exactly like his mother.”", "하고 허버트가 내게 말했다 — \"그분과 영락없이 똑같다네.\""], ["It was but natural that I should take to him much more kindly than to Drummle, and that, even in the earliest evenings of our boating, he and I should pull homeward abreast of one another, conversing from boat to boat, while Bentley Drummle came up in our wake alone, under the overhanging banks and among the rushes.", "내가 드러믈보다 그에게 훨씬 더 살갑게 끌린 것은 지극히 자연스러운 일이었고, 우리가 막 뱃놀이를 시작하던 초저녁부터 그와 내가 나란히 배를 저어 집으로 돌아오며 이 배에서 저 배로 이야기를 주고받는 동안, 벤틀리 드러믈이 우리 뒤꽁무니에서 홀로 늘어진 강둑 아래와 골풀 사이를 헤치며 올라오던 것 또한 자연스러운 일이었다."], ["He would always creep in-shore like some uncomfortable amphibious creature, even when the tide would have sent him fast upon his way; and I always think of him as coming after us in the dark or by the back-water, when our own two boats were breaking the sunset or the moonlight in mid-stream.", "그는 밀물이 그를 길로 빨리 떠밀어 줄 때조차도, 어딘가 불편한 양서류 같은 짐승처럼 늘 물가로 기어들곤 했다. 그래서 나는 그를 떠올릴 때면, 우리 두 배가 강 한복판에서 노을이나 달빛을 가르고 나아갈 때 그가 어둠 속이나 후미진 물길로 우리 뒤를 따라오는 모습으로 늘 기억하게 된다."]], [["Herbert was my intimate companion and friend.", "허버트는 나의 절친한 동무이자 벗이었다."], ["I presented him with a half-share in my boat, which was the occasion of his often coming down to Hammersmith; and my possession of a half-share in his chambers often took me up to London.", "나는 그에게 내 배의 절반을 나누어 주었는데, 그 덕분에 그는 자주 해머스미스로 내려오게 되었다. 그리고 내가 그의 거처에 절반의 지분을 가진 덕에 나는 자주 런던으로 올라가게 되었다."], ["We used to walk between the two places at all hours.", "우리는 시간을 가리지 않고 그 두 곳 사이를 걸어다니곤 했다."], ["I have an affection for the road yet (though it is not so pleasant a road as it was then), formed in the impressibility of untried youth and hope.", "나는 지금도 그 길에 애정을 품고 있는데(지금은 그때만큼 즐거운 길은 못 되지만), 그것은 아직 세상에 부딪혀 보지 않은 젊음과 희망의 감수성 속에서 빚어진 애정이다."]], [["When I had been in Mr. Pocket’s family a month or two, Mr. and Mrs.", "내가 포켓 선생 댁에 머문 지 한두 달쯤 되었을 때, 커밀러 부부가 나타났다."], ["Camilla turned up. Camilla was Mr.", "커밀러는 포켓 선생의 누이였다."], ["Pocket’s sister. Georgiana, whom I had seen at Miss Havisham’s on the same occasion, also turned up.", "같은 자리에서 미스 해비셤의 집에서 보았던 조지애나도 나타났다."], ["She was a cousin,—an indigestive single woman, who called her rigidity religion, and her liver love.", "그녀는 사촌으로 — 소화 불량을 앓는 독신녀였는데, 자신의 완고함을 신앙심이라 일컫고 자신의 짜증을 사랑이라 일컫는 사람이었다."], ["These people hated me with the hatred of cupidity and disappointment.", "이 사람들은 탐욕과 실망에서 비롯된 증오심으로 나를 미워했다."], ["As a matter of course, they fawned upon me in my prosperity with the basest meanness.", "당연한 일이지만, 그들은 내가 형편이 펴자 더없이 비열하게 알랑거렸다."], ["Towards Mr. Pocket, as a grown-up infant with no notion of his own interests, they showed the complacent forbearance I had heard them express. Mrs.", "자기 잇속이라곤 도무지 모르는 다 큰 어린애로 여기던 포켓 선생에게는, 내가 일찍이 그들 입에서 들었던 그 잘난 척하는 너그러움을 베풀었다."], ["Pocket they held in contempt; but they allowed the poor soul to have been heavily disappointed in life, because that shed a feeble reflected light upon themselves.", "포켓 부인은 멸시했으나, 그 가엾은 사람이 인생에서 크게 실망을 겪었다는 점만은 인정해 주었으니, 그것이 그들 자신에게 희미하게나마 반사된 빛을 비추어 주었기 때문이었다."]], [["These were the surroundings among which I settled down, and applied myself to my education.", "내가 자리를 잡고 학업에 정진하게 된 것은 바로 이런 환경 속에서였다."], ["I soon contracted expensive habits, and began to spend an amount of money that within a few short months I should have thought almost fabulous; but through good and evil I stuck to my books.", "나는 곧 돈이 많이 드는 습관에 물들었고, 몇 달 전만 해도 거의 터무니없다고 여겼을 만한 액수의 돈을 쓰기 시작했다. 그러나 좋든 나쁘든 나는 책에서 손을 떼지 않았다."], ["There was no other merit in this, than my having sense enough to feel my deficiencies.", "여기에 무슨 갸륵한 데가 있었던 것은 아니고, 다만 내가 부족함을 느낄 만한 분별은 갖추고 있었던 것뿐이다."], ["Between Mr. Pocket and Herbert I got on fast; and, with one or the other always at my elbow to give me the start I wanted, and clear obstructions out of my road, I must have been as great a dolt as Drummle if I had done less.", "포켓 선생과 허버트 덕분에 나는 빠르게 나아갔다. 그리고 둘 중 하나가 늘 내 곁에서 필요한 출발점을 잡아 주고 내 앞길의 장애물을 치워 주었으니, 만약 내가 그만큼도 못 해냈다면 나는 드러믈만큼이나 큰 얼간이였을 것이다."]], [["I had not seen Mr. Wemmick for some weeks, when I thought I would write him a note and propose to go home with him on a certain evening.", "웨믹 씨를 보지 못한 지 몇 주가 되었을 무렵, 나는 그에게 쪽지를 써서 어느 날 저녁 함께 그의 집으로 가자고 청해 보면 어떨까 생각했다."], ["He replied that it would give him much pleasure, and that he would expect me at the office at six o’clock.", "그는 그렇게 해 주면 무척 기쁘겠다고, 여섯 시에 사무소에서 나를 기다리겠다고 답했다."], ["Thither I went, and there I found him, putting the key of his safe down his back as the clock struck.", "나는 그리로 갔고, 거기서 시계가 종을 칠 때 금고 열쇠를 등 뒤로 늘어뜨려 넣고 있는 그를 만났다."]], [["“Did you think of walking down to Walworth?” said he.", "\"월워스까지 걸어 내려갈 생각이오?\" 그가 말했다."]], [["“Certainly,”", "\"물론입니다,\""], ["said I, “if you approve.”", "내가 말했다. \"괜찮으시다면요.\""]], [["“Very much,”", "\"아주 좋소,\""], ["was Wemmick’s reply, “for I have had my legs under the desk all day, and shall be glad to stretch them.", "웨믹의 대답이었다. \"하루 종일 다리를 책상 밑에 처박고 있었더니, 좀 펴 보고 싶던 참이오."], ["Now, I’ll tell you what I have got for supper, Mr. Pip.", "자, 저녁으로 뭘 마련해 두었는지 말해 드리지요, 핍 씨."], ["I have got a stewed steak,—which is of home preparation,—and a cold roast fowl,—which is from the cook’s-shop.", "스튜 스테이크가 있는데 — 이건 집에서 만든 것이고 — 차가운 통구이 닭이 있는데 — 이건 음식점에서 산 것이오."], ["I think it’s tender, because the master of the shop was a Juryman in some cases of ours the other day, and we let him down easy.", "아마 연할 거요, 그 가게 주인이 며칠 전 우리 사건 몇 건에서 배심원을 맡았는데, 우리가 그를 봐줬거든."], ["I reminded him of it when I bought the fowl, and I said, “Pick us out a good one, old Briton, because if we had chosen to keep you in the box another day or two, we could easily have done it.”", "닭을 사면서 그 일을 일깨워 주고는 이렇게 말했지요. '좋은 놈으로 하나 골라 주시오, 영감, 안 그랬으면 며칠 더 배심원석에 잡아 둘 수도 있었으니까.' 그랬더니 그가 말합디다."], ["He said to that, “Let me make you a present of the best fowl in the shop.”", "'가게에서 제일 좋은 닭을 선물로 드리지요.' 물론 나야 그러라고 했소."], ["I let him, of course. As far as it goes, it’s property and portable.", "어쨌든 그게 어디요, 재산이고 들고 다닐 수 있는 것이니."], ["You don’t object to an aged parent, I hope?”", "노부모를 모시는 건 괜찮으시겠지요?\""]], [["I really thought he was still speaking of the fowl, until he added, “Because I have got an aged parent at my place.”", "나는 그가 여전히 닭 이야기를 하는 줄로만 알았는데, 그가 이렇게 덧붙였다. \"내 집에 늙으신 아버님이 계시거든요.\""], ["I then said what politeness required.", "그제야 나는 예의상 마땅한 말을 했다."]], [["“So, you haven’t dined with Mr. Jaggers yet?”", "\"그래, 아직 재거스 씨와 식사를 함께 한 적은 없지요?\""], ["he pursued, as we walked along.", "그가 걸어가며 말을 이었다."]], [["“Not yet.”", "\"아직 없습니다.\""]], [["“He told me so this afternoon when he heard you were coming. I expect you’ll have an invitation to-morrow.", "\"오후에 당신이 온다는 말을 듣고 그가 내게 그렇게 말합디다. 내일 초대를 받게 될 거요."], ["He’s going to ask your pals, too.", "당신 동무들도 부르려 한답니다."], ["Three of ’em; ain’t there?”", "셋이지요, 아마?\""]], [["Although I was not in the habit of counting Drummle as one of my intimate associates, I answered, “Yes.”", "나는 드러믈을 내 가까운 동무 가운데 하나로 꼽는 버릇은 없었으나, \"네.\" 하고 대답했다."]], [["“Well, he’s going to ask the whole gang,”—I hardly felt complimented by the word,—“and whatever he gives you, he’ll give you good.", "\"음, 그 패거리를 통째로 부른다는데\" — 그 말이 그리 칭찬으로 들리지는 않았다 — \"뭘 대접하든 좋은 걸로 줄 거요."], ["Don’t look forward to variety, but you’ll have excellence.", "종류가 다양하리라고 기대하진 마시오, 하지만 최상품을 맛보게 될 테니."], ["And there’s another rum thing in his house,”", "그리고 그 집에는 또 한 가지 묘한 게 있소,\""], ["proceeded Wemmick, after a moment’s pause, as if the remark followed on the housekeeper understood; “he never lets a door or window be fastened at night.”", "하고 웨믹은 잠시 말을 멈춘 뒤, 마치 앞서 말한 가정부 이야기에 이어지는 말이라는 듯 계속했다. \"그는 밤에 문이든 창이든 결코 잠그게 하지 않는다오.\""]], [["“Is he never robbed?”", "\"도둑을 맞은 적이 없습니까?\""]], [["“That’s it!”", "\"바로 그거요!\""], ["returned Wemmick.", "웨믹이 대꾸했다."], ["“He says, and gives it out publicly, “I want to see the man who’ll rob me.”", "\"그가 늘 하는 말이, 또 공공연히 떠벌리는 말이, '나를 털 놈이 있으면 한번 봤으면 좋겠군.'이오."], ["Lord bless you, I have heard him, a hundred times, if I have heard him once, say to regular cracksmen in our front office, “You know where I live; now, no bolt is ever drawn there; why don’t you do a stroke of business with me?", "맙소사, 나는 그가 우리 사무소 앞방에서 상습 강도들한테 이렇게 말하는 걸 한두 번도 아니고 백 번은 들었소. '너희들 내가 어디 사는지 알지. 거긴 빗장 한 번 거는 법이 없어."], ["Come; can’t I tempt you?”", "어디 한번 나랑 거래 좀 해 보지그래?"], ["Not a man of them, sir, would be bold enough to try it on, for love or money.”", "자, 내가 솔깃하게 만들어 줄까?' 하지만 그자들 가운데 어느 누구도, 돈을 준대도 사랑을 준대도, 감히 그걸 시도해 볼 만큼 배짱 있는 놈은 없었소.\""]], [["“They dread him so much?” said I.", "\"그를 그토록 무서워합니까?\" 내가 말했다."]], [["“Dread him,”", "\"무서워하느냐고요.\""], ["said Wemmick.", "웨믹이 말했다."], ["“I believe you they dread him.", "\"정말이지 무서워하고말고요."], ["Not but what he’s artful, even in his defiance of them.", "하지만 그가 그자들을 깔보는 데도 다 잔꾀가 숨어 있소."], ["No silver, sir.", "은붙이라곤 없거든요."], ["Britannia metal, every spoon.”", "숟가락 하나하나가 죄다 브리타니아 합금이오.\""]], [["“So they wouldn’t have much,”", "\"그러니까 그자들이 손에 넣을 것도 별로 없겠군요,\""], ["I observed, “even if they—”", "하고 내가 말했다. \"설령 그자들이——\""]], [["“Ah!", "\"아!"], ["But he would have much,”", "하지만 그자는 손에 넣을 게 많지.\""], ["said Wemmick, cutting me short, “and they know it.", "웨믹이 내 말을 자르며 말했다. \"그자들도 그걸 알고 있소."], ["He’d have their lives, and the lives of scores of ’em.", "그는 그자들의 목숨을 차지할 거요, 수십 명의 목숨을 말이오."], ["He’d have all he could get.", "손에 넣을 수 있는 건 죄다 차지하겠지."], ["And it’s impossible to say what he couldn’t get, if he gave his mind to it.”", "마음만 먹으면 손에 넣지 못할 게 뭐가 있을지, 도무지 가늠할 수가 없는 사람이오.\""]], [["I was falling into meditation on my guardian’s greatness, when Wemmick remarked:—", "내가 후견인의 위대함을 두고 골똘히 생각에 잠기려는데, 웨믹이 말했다."]], [["“As to the absence of plate, that’s only his natural depth, you know.", "\"은붙이가 없는 건 말이오, 그저 그 사람 천성의 깊이일 뿐이오."], ["A river’s its natural depth, and he’s his natural depth.", "강은 강 나름의 깊이가 있고, 그도 그 나름의 깊이가 있는 거지요."], ["Look at his watch-chain.", "그의 시곗줄을 보시오."], ["That’s real enough.”", "그건 진짜배기요.\""]], [["“It’s very massive,” said I.", "\"꽤 묵직하더군요.\" 내가 말했다."]], [["“Massive?”", "\"묵직하다?\""], ["repeated Wemmick.", "웨믹이 되뇌었다."], ["“I think so.", "\"그렇소이다."], ["And his watch is a gold repeater, and worth a hundred pound if it’s worth a penny. Mr.", "게다가 그 시계는 금딱지 리피터인데, 한 푼어치라도 값이 나간다면 백 파운드는 족히 나갈 물건이오."], ["Pip, there are about seven hundred thieves in this town who know all about that watch; there’s not a man, a woman, or a child, among them, who wouldn’t identify the smallest link in that chain, and drop it as if it was red hot, if inveigled into touching it.”", "핍 씨, 이 도시에는 그 시계에 대해 죄다 알고 있는 도둑이 칠백 명쯤 되는데, 그 가운데 남자건 여자건 아이건, 그 줄의 가장 작은 고리 하나라도 알아보지 못할 자가 없고, 또 어쩌다 그걸 만지게 꾐을 당한다면 마치 시뻘겋게 달군 쇠라도 되는 양 떨어뜨려 버리지 않을 자가 없을 거요.\""]], [["At first with such discourse, and afterwards with conversation of a more general nature, did Mr. Wemmick and I beguile the time and the road, until he gave me to understand that we had arrived in the district of Walworth.", "처음에는 그런 이야기로, 그다음에는 좀 더 일반적인 화제의 대화로, 웨믹 씨와 나는 시간과 길을 달래며 갔는데, 마침내 그가 우리가 월워스 지역에 다다랐음을 일러 주었다."]], [["It appeared to be a collection of back lanes, ditches, and little gardens, and to present the aspect of a rather dull retirement.", "그곳은 뒷골목과 도랑과 자그마한 텃밭들이 모여 있는 듯한 동네로, 다소 쓸쓸한 은거지 같은 모습을 띠고 있었다."], ["Wemmick’s house was a little wooden cottage in the midst of plots of garden, and the top of it was cut out and painted like a battery mounted with guns.", "웨믹의 집은 텃밭들 한가운데 자리 잡은 작은 목조 오두막이었는데, 꼭대기는 마치 대포를 얹은 포대처럼 잘라 내고 칠을 해 두었다."]], [["“My own doing,”", "\"내가 직접 한 거요.\""], ["said Wemmick.", "웨믹이 말했다."], ["“Looks pretty; don’t it?”", "\"보기 좋지요, 안 그렇소?\""]], [["I highly commended it, I think it was the smallest house I ever saw; with the queerest gothic windows (by far the greater part of them sham), and a gothic door almost too small to get in at.", "나는 그것을 크게 칭찬했다. 내가 본 집 가운데 가장 작은 집이었지 싶다. 가장 기묘한 고딕식 창문이 달려 있었고(그 대부분은 가짜였지만), 들어가기에도 너무 작다 싶은 고딕식 문이 달려 있었다."]], [["“That’s a real flagstaff, you see,”", "\"저게 진짜 깃대요, 보이지요.\""], ["said Wemmick, “and on Sundays I run up a real flag.", "웨믹이 말했다. \"일요일이면 진짜 깃발을 올린다오."], ["Then look here.", "그리고 여길 보시오."], ["After I have crossed this bridge, I hoist it up—so—and cut off the communication.”", "내가 이 다리를 건너고 나면, 이렇게—이걸 끌어올려서—연락을 끊어 버리지요.\""]], [["The bridge was a plank, and it crossed a chasm about four feet wide and two deep.", "그 다리란 널빤지 하나로, 너비 4피트에 깊이 2피트쯤 되는 도랑 위에 걸쳐져 있었다."], ["But it was very pleasant to see the pride with which he hoisted it up and made it fast; smiling as he did so, with a relish and not merely mechanically.", "그러나 그가 그것을 끌어올려 단단히 고정시키는 그 자랑스러운 모습을 보는 것은 무척 흐뭇한 일이었다. 그는 그렇게 하면서, 단지 기계적으로가 아니라 진심으로 음미하는 듯 미소를 지었다."]], [["“At nine o’clock every night, Greenwich time,”", "\"매일 밤 아홉 시, 그리니치 시간으로 말이오,\""], ["said Wemmick, “the gun fires.", "웨믹이 말했다. \"대포가 발사되오."], ["There he is, you see!", "저기 있지요, 보이지요!"], ["And when you hear him go, I think you’ll say he’s a Stinger.”", "그놈이 발사되는 소리를 들으면, 당신도 저놈이 한 방 매운 놈이라고 할 거요.\""]], [["The piece of ordnance referred to, was mounted in a separate fortress, constructed of lattice-work.", "이른바 그 대포는 격자 세공으로 지은 따로 떨어진 요새 안에 얹혀 있었다."], ["It was protected from the weather by an ingenious little tarpaulin contrivance in the nature of an umbrella.", "그것은 우산처럼 생긴 기발하고 작은 방수포 장치로 비바람을 막아 두고 있었다."]], [["“Then, at the back,”", "\"그리고 뒤편에는 말이오,\""], ["said Wemmick, “out of sight, so as not to impede the idea of fortifications,—for it’s a principle with me, if you have an idea, carry it out and keep it up,—I don’t know whether that’s your opinion—”", "웨믹이 말했다. \"요새라는 발상에 방해가 되지 않도록 눈에 띄지 않는 곳에다—이건 내 신조인데, 어떤 발상이 있으면 그걸 실행에 옮기고 또 유지해야 하는 법이오—그게 당신 생각과 같은지는 모르겠소만——\""]], [["I said, decidedly.", "나는 단호히 같다고 말했다."]], [["“—At the back, there’s a pig, and there are fowls and rabbits; then, I knock together my own little frame, you see, and grow cucumbers; and you’ll judge at supper what sort of a salad I can raise.", "\"—뒤편에는 돼지 한 마리가 있고, 닭과 토끼도 있소. 그리고 보다시피 직접 작은 틀을 짜 맞춰서 오이를 기르는데, 저녁때 내가 어떤 샐러드를 길러 내는지 당신이 평가해 보게 될 거요. 그러니 말이오,\""], ["So, sir,” said Wemmick, smiling again, but seriously too, as he shook his head, “if you can suppose the little place besieged, it would hold out a devil of a time in point of provisions.”", "하고 웨믹은 다시 미소를 지으면서도 진지하게 고개를 가로저으며 말했다. \"이 작은 곳이 포위당했다고 가정해 본다면, 식량 면에서 어지간히 오래 버텨 낼 수 있을 거요.\""]], [["Then, he conducted me to a bower about a dozen yards off, but which was approached by such ingenious twists of path that it took quite a long time to get at; and in this retreat our glasses were already set forth.", "그러고서 그는 열두 야드쯤 떨어진 정자로 나를 데려갔는데, 그 길이 어찌나 기발하게 구불구불 꺾여 있던지 거기에 이르는 데 꽤 오랜 시간이 걸렸다. 그리고 이 은신처에는 우리 술잔이 이미 차려져 있었다."], ["Our punch was cooling in an ornamental lake, on whose margin the bower was raised.", "우리가 마실 펀치는 장식용 호수에 담겨 식고 있었는데, 정자는 바로 그 호수 가에 세워져 있었다."], ["This piece of water (with an island in the middle which might have been the salad for supper) was of a circular form, and he had constructed a fountain in it, which, when you set a little mill going and took a cork out of a pipe, played to that powerful extent that it made the back of your hand quite wet.", "이 호수는(한가운데에 섬이 하나 있었는데, 저녁 식사에 쓸 샐러드밭이었지 싶다) 원형이었고, 그는 그 안에 분수를 만들어 두었다. 작은 물레방아를 돌리고 관에서 마개를 뽑으면, 그 분수는 어찌나 세차게 솟구치는지 손등이 흠뻑 젖을 정도였다."]], [["“I am my own engineer, and my own carpenter, and my own plumber, and my own gardener, and my own Jack of all Trades,”", "\"나는 내 집 기사이자, 목수이자, 배관공이자, 정원사이자, 만물박이라오.\" 웨믹이 내 칭찬에 답하며 말했다."], ["said Wemmick, in acknowledging my compliments.", "\"뭐, 이게 좋은 일이지요, 아시다시피."], ["“Well; it’s a good thing, you know. It brushes the Newgate cobwebs away, and pleases the Aged.", "뉴게이트의 거미줄을 털어 내고, 노친네를 즐겁게 해 드리니까."], ["You wouldn’t mind being at once introduced to the Aged, would you?", "곧장 노친네를 소개받는 건 꺼리지 않으시겠지요?"], ["It wouldn’t put you out?”", "거북하지 않으시겠소?\""]], [["I expressed the readiness I felt, and we went into the castle.", "나는 기꺼이 그러겠노라는 뜻을 비쳤고, 우리는 성으로 들어갔다."], ["There we found, sitting by a fire, a very old man in a flannel coat: clean, cheerful, comfortable, and well cared for, but intensely deaf.", "거기서 우리는 플란넬 외투를 입고 난롯가에 앉은 한 노인을 만났다. 깨끗하고, 쾌활하고, 편안하고, 보살핌을 잘 받고 있었으나, 귀가 심하게 먹은 노인이었다."]], [["“Well aged parent,”", "\"안녕하시오, 늙으신 아버님,\""], ["said Wemmick, shaking hands with him in a cordial and jocose way, “how am you?”", "하고 웨믹은 다정하면서도 익살스러운 투로 그와 악수하며 말했다. \"기분이 어떠시우?\""]], [["“All right, John; all right!”", "\"좋다, 존, 좋아!\""], ["replied the old man.", "노인이 대답했다."]], [["“Here’s Mr. Pip, aged parent,”", "\"핍 씨가 오셨소, 아버님.\""], ["said Wemmick, “and I wish you could hear his name.", "웨믹이 말했다. \"이분 성함을 들으실 수 있으면 좋으련만."], ["Nod away at him, Mr. Pip; that’s what he likes.", "이분께 고개를 끄덕여 보이시오, 핍 씨. 그걸 좋아하시거든요."], ["Nod away at him, if you please, like winking!”", "부디 그분께 고개를 끄덕여 주시오, 눈 깜빡이듯 자주 말이오!\""]], [["“This is a fine place of my son’s, sir,”", "\"이건 우리 아들이 마련한 훌륭한 곳이라오, 선생.\""], ["cried the old man, while I nodded as hard as I possibly could.", "내가 있는 힘껏 고개를 끄덕이는 동안 노인이 큰 소리로 말했다."], ["“This is a pretty pleasure-ground, sir.", "\"참 아름다운 유원지지요, 선생."], ["This spot and these beautiful works upon it ought to be kept together by the Nation, after my son’s time, for the people’s enjoyment.”", "이 자리하며 그 위에 지어진 이 멋진 시설들은, 우리 아들이 세상을 뜬 뒤에도 백성들이 즐길 수 있도록 나라가 한데 보존해야 마땅하오.\""]], [["“You’re as proud of it as Punch; ain’t you, Aged?”", "\"펀치 인형만큼이나 자랑스러워하시지요, 안 그러우, 아버님?\""], ["said Wemmick, contemplating the old man, with his hard face really softened; “there’s a nod for you;”", "하고 웨믹은 노인을 바라보며 말했는데, 그 딱딱한 얼굴이 정말로 부드러워져 있었다. \"자, 끄덕여 드리리다,\""], ["giving him a tremendous one; “there’s another for you;” giving him a still more tremendous one; “you like that, don’t you?", "하면서 어마어마하게 한 번 끄덕여 보이고, \"자, 한 번 더 끄덕여 드리지요,\" 하면서 한층 더 어마어마하게 끄덕여 보였다."], ["If you’re not tired, Mr. Pip—though I know it’s tiring to strangers—will you tip him one more?", "\"이게 좋으시지요? 핍 씨, 만일 지치지 않았다면—낯선 사람에게는 지치는 일인 줄 알지만—한 번만 더 끄덕여 드리겠소?"], ["You can’t think how it pleases him.”", "이게 얼마나 그분을 기쁘게 하는지 당신은 짐작도 못 할 거요.\""]], [["I tipped him several more, and he was in great spirits.", "나는 그에게 몇 번 더 끄덕여 보였고, 그러자 노인은 무척 기분이 좋아졌다."], ["We left him bestirring himself to feed the fowls, and we sat down to our punch in the arbour; where Wemmick told me, as he smoked a pipe, that it had taken him a good many years to bring the property up to its present pitch of perfection.", "우리는 그가 부지런히 일어나 닭에게 모이를 주러 가도록 내버려 두고, 정자에 자리를 잡고 앉아 펀치를 들었다. 거기서 웨믹은 파이프 담배를 피우며, 이 집터를 지금의 완성된 경지로 끌어올리기까지 여러 해가 걸렸노라고 내게 말해 주었다."]], [["“Is it your own, Mr. Wemmick?”", "\"이게 당신 소유의 것입니까, 웨믹 씨?\""]], [["“O yes,”", "\"오, 그렇소,\""], ["said Wemmick, “I have got hold of it, a bit at a time.", "웨믹이 말했다. \"한 조각씩 한 조각씩 손에 넣었지요."], ["It’s a freehold, by George!”", "맙소사, 자유 보유지라오!\""]], [["“Is it indeed?", "\"정말입니까?"], ["I hope Mr. Jaggers admires it?”", "재거스 씨께서도 이걸 보면 감탄하시겠군요?\""]], [["“Never seen it,”", "\"본 적이 없소.\""], ["said Wemmick.", "웨믹이 말했다."], ["“Never heard of it.", "\"들은 적도 없고."], ["Never seen the Aged. Never heard of him. No; the office is one thing, and private life is another.", "노친네를 본 적도 없고, 그분 얘기를 들은 적도 없지요. 천만에."], ["When I go into the office, I leave the Castle behind me, and when I come into the Castle, I leave the office behind me.", "사무소는 사무소고, 사생활은 사생활이오. 사무소에 들어갈 때면 나는 성을 뒤에 두고 가고, 성에 들어올 때면 사무소를 뒤에 두고 오지요."], ["If it’s not in any way disagreeable to you, you’ll oblige me by doing the same.", "당신에게 조금이라도 거북한 일이 아니라면, 당신도 그렇게 해 주면 고맙겠소."], ["I don’t wish it professionally spoken about.”", "사무소에서 이 일이 입에 오르내리는 걸 원치 않거든요.\""]], [["Of course I felt my good faith involved in the observance of his request.", "물론 나는 그의 부탁을 지키는 일에 내 신의가 걸려 있다고 느꼈다."], ["The punch being very nice, we sat there drinking it and talking, until it was almost nine o’clock.", "펀치가 무척 맛이 좋아, 우리는 거기 앉아 그것을 마시며 이야기를 나누었고, 그러는 사이 거의 아홉 시가 다 되었다."], ["“Getting near gun-fire,”", "\"대포 쏠 시간이 가까워지는군.\""], ["said Wemmick then, as he laid down his pipe; “it’s the Aged’s treat.”", "그때 웨믹이 파이프를 내려놓으며 말했다. \"노친네가 베푸는 선물이지요.\""]], [["Proceeding into the Castle again, we found the Aged heating the poker, with expectant eyes, as a preliminary to the performance of this great nightly ceremony.", "다시 성 안으로 들어가 보니, 노친네가 이 거창한 밤의 의식을 거행하기에 앞서, 기대에 찬 눈빛으로 부지깽이를 달구고 있었다."], ["Wemmick stood with his watch in his hand until the moment was come for him to take the red-hot poker from the Aged, and repair to the battery.", "웨믹은 손에 시계를 든 채 서 있다가, 노친네에게서 시뻘겋게 단 부지깽이를 건네받아 포대로 갈 순간이 오기를 기다렸다."], ["He took it, and went out, and presently the Stinger went off with a Bang that shook the crazy little box of a cottage as if it must fall to pieces, and made every glass and teacup in it ring.", "그는 부지깽이를 받아 들고 밖으로 나갔고, 이윽고 그 매운 놈이 쾅 하고 터졌는데, 그 소리에 다 쓰러져 가는 상자 같은 작은 오두막이 산산조각이라도 날 듯 흔들렸고, 안에 있던 유리잔과 찻잔이 죄다 쟁그랑거렸다."], ["Upon this, the Aged—who I believe would have been blown out of his arm-chair but for holding on by the elbows—cried out exultingly, “He’s fired!", "그러자 노친네는—팔걸이를 붙들고 있지 않았더라면 안락의자에서 날아가 버렸을 것이라고 나는 믿거니와—신이 나서 외쳤다. \"쏘았구나!"], ["I heerd him!”", "내가 들었어!\""], ["and I nodded at the old gentleman until it is no figure of speech to declare that I absolutely could not see him.", "그리고 나는 그 노신사를 향해 어찌나 고개를 끄덕여 댔던지, 마침내는 그가 보이지조차 않게 되었다고 말한대도 결코 과장된 표현이 아닐 지경이었다."]], [["The interval between that time and supper Wemmick devoted to showing me his collection of curiosities.", "그때부터 저녁 식사까지의 짬을 웨믹은 자신의 진귀한 수집품을 내게 보여 주는 데 바쳤다."], ["They were mostly of a felonious character; comprising the pen with which a celebrated forgery had been committed, a distinguished razor or two, some locks of hair, and several manuscript confessions written under condemnation,—upon which Mr. Wemmick set particular value as being, to use his own words, “every one of ’em Lies, sir.”", "그것들은 대개 흉악한 성질의 물건들로, 어느 유명한 위조 사건에 쓰인 펜이며, 이름난 면도칼 한두 개며, 머리카락 몇 타래며, 사형 선고를 받고 쓴 자필 고백서 몇 장이 들어 있었는데—이 고백서들을 웨믹 씨는 특히 소중히 여겼으니, 그의 표현을 그대로 빌리자면 \"하나같이 다 거짓말이거든요, 선생.\" 이런 까닭에서였다."], ["These were agreeably dispersed among small specimens of china and glass, various neat trifles made by the proprietor of the museum, and some tobacco-stoppers carved by the Aged.", "이것들 사이에는 작은 도자기와 유리 표본들이며, 이 박물관 주인이 손수 만든 갖가지 깔끔한 자질구레한 물건들이며, 노친네가 깎아 만든 담뱃대 다지개 몇 개가 보기 좋게 흩어져 있었다."], ["They were all displayed in that chamber of the Castle into which I had been first inducted, and which served, not only as the general sitting-room but as the kitchen too, if I might judge from a saucepan on the hob, and a brazen bijou over the fireplace designed for the suspension of a roasting-jack.", "그것들은 죄다, 내가 처음 안내받아 들어갔던 그 성의 방 안에 진열되어 있었는데, 그 방은 단지 거실 노릇만 하는 게 아니라 부엌 노릇도 했으니, 화덕 선반 위의 냄비며, 고기 굽는 회전 꼬챙이를 걸어 두려고 벽난로 위에 마련해 둔 놋쇠 장식물로 미루어 그렇게 판단할 수 있었다."]], [["There was a neat little girl in attendance, who looked after the Aged in the day.", "낮 동안 노친네를 돌보는 단정한 소녀가 하나 시중을 들고 있었다."], ["When she had laid the supper-cloth, the bridge was lowered to give her means of egress, and she withdrew for the night.", "그 아이가 저녁 식탁보를 깔고 나자, 그 아이가 나갈 수 있도록 다리를 내려 주었고, 아이는 밤을 보내러 물러갔다."], ["The supper was excellent; and though the Castle was rather subject to dry-rot insomuch that it tasted like a bad nut, and though the pig might have been farther off, I was heartily pleased with my whole entertainment.", "저녁 식사는 훌륭했다. 그리고 성이 워낙 마른 썩음병에 시달리던 터라 상한 호두 같은 맛이 났고, 또 돼지가 좀 더 멀리 떨어져 있었더라면 좋았겠다 싶었음에도, 나는 그 모든 대접에 진심으로 흡족했다."], ["Nor was there any drawback on my little turret bedroom, beyond there being such a very thin ceiling between me and the flagstaff, that when I lay down on my back in bed, it seemed as if I had to balance that pole on my forehead all night.", "내 작은 망루 침실에도 흠잡을 데라곤 없었으니, 다만 나와 깃대 사이에 천장이 어찌나 얇았던지, 침대에 반듯이 누우면 밤새도록 그 깃대를 이마 위에 얹어 균형을 잡고 있어야 할 것만 같았던 것뿐이었다."]], [["Wemmick was up early in the morning, and I am afraid I heard him cleaning my boots.", "웨믹은 아침 일찍 일어났는데, 안타깝게도 그가 내 구두를 닦는 소리가 들리는 듯했다."], ["After that, he fell to gardening, and I saw him from my gothic window pretending to employ the Aged, and nodding at him in a most devoted manner.", "그러고 나서 그는 정원 일에 들어갔고, 나는 내 고딕식 창문으로 그가 노친네에게 일을 시키는 척하면서 더없이 정성스러운 태도로 고개를 끄덕여 보이는 모습을 지켜보았다."], ["Our breakfast was as good as the supper, and at half-past eight precisely we started for Little Britain.", "우리 아침 식사는 저녁 식사 못지않게 훌륭했고, 정확히 여덟 시 반에 우리는 리틀브리튼을 향해 출발했다."], ["By degrees, Wemmick got dryer and harder as we went along, and his mouth tightened into a post-office again.", "길을 가는 동안 웨믹은 점점 메마르고 딱딱해졌으며, 그의 입은 다시 우편함 모양으로 굳게 다물어졌다."], ["At last, when we got to his place of business and he pulled out his key from his coat-collar, he looked as unconscious of his Walworth property as if the Castle and the drawbridge and the arbour and the lake and the fountain and the Aged, had all been blown into space together by the last discharge of the Stinger.", "마침내 그의 일터에 다다라 그가 외투 깃에서 열쇠를 꺼냈을 때, 그는 마치 성이며 도개교며 정자며 호수며 분수며 노친네가 모두 그 매운 놈의 마지막 발사와 함께 허공으로 날아가 버리기라도 한 듯, 자신의 월워스 소유지 따위는 까맣게 잊은 표정이었다."]], [["# Chapter XXVI.", "# Chapter XXVI."]], [["It fell out as Wemmick had told me it would, that I had an early opportunity of comparing my guardian’s establishment with that of his cashier and clerk.", "웨믹이 말한 그대로, 나는 일찍이 내 후견인의 살림살이를 그의 출납계 겸 서기의 살림살이와 비교해 볼 기회를 얻게 되었다."], ["My guardian was in his room, washing his hands with his scented soap, when I went into the office from Walworth; and he called me to him, and gave me the invitation for myself and friends which Wemmick had prepared me to receive.", "내가 월워스에서 사무소로 들어섰을 때, 후견인은 자기 방에서 향내 나는 비누로 손을 씻고 있었다. 그는 나를 불러, 웨믹이 미리 받게 되리라고 일러 주었던 그 초대를 나와 내 친구들 앞으로 건넸다. \"격식은 차리지 말고,\""], ["“No ceremony,” he stipulated, “and no dinner dress, and say to-morrow.”", "그가 조건을 달았다. \"정장도 입지 말고, 내일이라고 전해라.\""], ["I asked him where we should come to (for I had no idea where he lived), and I believe it was in his general objection to make anything like an admission, that he replied, “Come here, and I’ll take you home with me.”", "나는 어디로 가야 하느냐고 물었다(나는 그가 어디 사는지 전혀 몰랐으니까). 그러자 그는, 무언가를 인정하는 듯한 말은 일절 하지 않으려는 그 특유의 거부감 탓이었다고 나는 믿는데, 이렇게 대답했다. \"여기로 와라, 그러면 내가 너를 집으로 데려가마.\""], ["I embrace this opportunity of remarking that he washed his clients off, as if he were a surgeon or a dentist.", "나는 이 기회를 빌려, 그가 마치 외과 의사나 치과 의사라도 되는 양 의뢰인들을 씻어 내곤 했다는 점을 적어 두고자 한다."], ["He had a closet in his room, fitted up for the purpose, which smelt of the scented soap like a perfumer’s shop.", "그의 방에는 그 목적에 맞게 꾸며진 벽장이 하나 있었는데, 향수 가게처럼 그 향내 나는 비누 냄새가 풍겼다."], ["It had an unusually large jack-towel on a roller inside the door, and he would wash his hands, and wipe them and dry them all over this towel, whenever he came in from a police court or dismissed a client from his room.", "문 안쪽에는 굴림대에 걸린 유난히 큰 수건이 하나 있었고, 그는 경찰 법정에서 돌아오거나 의뢰인을 방에서 내보낼 때마다 손을 씻고 그 수건에 두루 닦아 말리곤 했다."], ["When I and my friends repaired to him at six o’clock next day, he seemed to have been engaged on a case of a darker complexion than usual, for we found him with his head butted into this closet, not only washing his hands, but laving his face and gargling his throat.", "이튿날 여섯 시에 나와 친구들이 그를 찾아갔을 때, 그는 평소보다 한층 음침한 빛깔의 사건을 맡고 있었던 모양이었다. 그가 그 벽장에 머리를 처박고 손을 씻을 뿐만 아니라 얼굴까지 씻고 목구멍까지 헹구고 있는 것을 보았으니 말이다."], ["And even when he had done all that, and had gone all round the jack-towel, he took out his penknife and scraped the case out of his nails before he put his coat on.", "그 모든 일을 마치고 그 수건에 두루 닦은 다음에도, 그는 주머니칼을 꺼내 외투를 걸치기 전에 손톱 밑에서 그 사건을 긁어냈다."]], [["There were some people slinking about as usual when we passed out into the street, who were evidently anxious to speak with him; but there was something so conclusive in the halo of scented soap which encircled his presence, that they gave it up for that day.", "우리가 거리로 나섰을 때, 여느 때처럼 몇몇 사람들이 슬그머니 어슬렁대고 있었는데, 그들은 분명 그와 말을 나누고 싶어 안달이 난 듯했다. 그러나 그의 존재를 둘러싼 향내 나는 비누의 후광에는 무언가 단호한 데가 있어서, 그들은 그날만은 단념하고 말았다."], ["As we walked along westward, he was recognised ever and again by some face in the crowd of the streets, and whenever that happened he talked louder to me; but he never otherwise recognised anybody, or took notice that anybody recognised him.", "우리가 서쪽으로 걸어가는 동안, 그는 거리의 군중 속 누군가의 얼굴에 이따금씩 알아보였고, 그럴 때마다 그는 내게 더 큰 목소리로 말을 건넸다. 그러나 그밖에는 누구도 알은체하지 않았고, 누가 자기를 알아보았다는 것조차 내색하지 않았다."]], [["He conducted us to Gerrard Street, Soho, to a house on the south side of that street.", "그는 우리를 소호의 제라드 거리로, 그 거리 남쪽에 있는 어느 집으로 데려갔다."], ["Rather a stately house of its kind, but dolefully in want of painting, and with dirty windows.", "그런 부류로서는 제법 위풍 있는 집이었으나, 페인트칠이 절실히 필요할 만큼 한심한 꼴이었고, 창문은 더러웠다."], ["He took out his key and opened the door, and we all went into a stone hall, bare, gloomy, and little used.", "그는 열쇠를 꺼내 문을 열었고, 우리 모두는 헐벗고 음울하며 거의 쓰지 않는 듯한 돌바닥 현관으로 들어섰다."], ["So, up a dark brown staircase into a series of three dark brown rooms on the first floor.", "그러고는 거무튀튀한 갈색 계단을 올라, 이 층에 늘어선 거무튀튀한 갈색 방 셋으로 들어갔다."], ["There were carved garlands on the panelled walls, and as he stood among them giving us welcome, I know what kind of loops I thought they looked like.", "판자를 댄 벽에는 새겨진 꽃 줄 장식이 있었는데, 그가 그 사이에 서서 우리를 맞이할 때, 나는 그것들이 내 눈에 어떤 종류의 올가미처럼 보였는지 잘 안다."]], [["Dinner was laid in the best of these rooms; the second was his dressing-room; the third, his bedroom.", "저녁 식사는 그 방들 가운데 가장 좋은 방에 차려져 있었다. 둘째 방은 그의 옷 갈아입는 방이었고, 셋째 방은 침실이었다."], ["He told us that he held the whole house, but rarely used more of it than we saw.", "그는 우리에게 집 전체를 쓰고 있으나 우리가 본 것 이상은 좀처럼 쓰지 않는다고 말했다."], ["The table was comfortably laid—no silver in the service, of course—and at the side of his chair was a capacious dumb-waiter, with a variety of bottles and decanters on it, and four dishes of fruit for dessert.", "식탁은 안락하게 차려져 있었는데 — 물론 식기에 은제품은 없었다 — 그의 의자 옆에는 널찍한 보조 탁자가 있어, 그 위에는 갖가지 병과 디캔터가, 그리고 후식용 과일이 담긴 접시 네 개가 놓여 있었다."], ["I noticed throughout, that he kept everything under his own hand, and distributed everything himself.", "나는 그가 시종일관 모든 것을 자기 손안에 두고 몸소 모든 것을 나눠 준다는 것을 알아챘다."]], [["There was a bookcase in the room; I saw from the backs of the books, that they were about evidence, criminal law, criminal biography, trials, acts of Parliament, and such things.", "방에는 책장이 하나 있었다. 나는 책등을 보고, 그것들이 증거, 형법, 범죄자 전기, 재판, 의회 법령 따위에 관한 것임을 알았다."], ["The furniture was all very solid and good, like his watch-chain.", "가구는 모두 그의 시곗줄처럼 아주 튼튼하고 좋았다."], ["It had an official look, however, and there was nothing merely ornamental to be seen.", "그러나 어딘지 사무적인 분위기가 있었고, 단순히 장식만을 위한 것은 하나도 보이지 않았다."], ["In a corner was a little table of papers with a shaded lamp: so that he seemed to bring the office home with him in that respect too, and to wheel it out of an evening and fall to work.", "한쪽 구석에는 갓을 씌운 등이 놓인 서류용 작은 탁자가 있었다. 그래서 그는 그 점에서도 사무소를 집으로 가져와, 저녁이면 그것을 굴려 내어 일에 매달리는 것 같았다."]], [["As he had scarcely seen my three companions until now,—for he and I had walked together,—he stood on the hearth-rug, after ringing the bell, and took a searching look at them.", "그는 지금껏 내 세 동행을 거의 본 적이 없었으므로 — 그와 나는 함께 걸어왔으니까 — 종을 울린 뒤 벽난로 앞 깔개에 선 채 그들을 샅샅이 뜯어보았다."], ["To my surprise, he seemed at once to be principally if not solely interested in Drummle.", "놀랍게도 그는 대번에, 오로지라고는 못해도 주로 드러믈에게 관심을 두는 듯했다."]], [["“Pip,”", "\"핍,\""], ["said he, putting his large hand on my shoulder and moving me to the window, “I don’t know one from the other.", "그가 커다란 손을 내 어깨에 얹고 나를 창가로 데려가며 말했다. \"누가 누군지 모르겠구나."], ["Who’s the Spider?”", "저 거미는 누구냐?\""]], [["“The spider?” said I.", "\"거미요?\" 내가 말했다."]], [["“The blotchy, sprawly, sulky fellow.”", "\"얼룩덜룩하고, 늘어져 있고, 뚱한 녀석 말이다.\""]], [["“That’s Bentley Drummle,”", "\"저 사람은 벤틀리 드러믈입니다.\""], ["I replied; “the one with the delicate face is Startop.”", "내가 대답했다. \"섬세한 얼굴을 한 쪽이 스타톱이고요.\""]], [["Not making the least account of “the one with the delicate face,”", "\"섬세한 얼굴을 한 쪽\"에는 조금도 관심을 두지 않은 채, 그가 대꾸했다."], ["he returned, “Bentley Drummle is his name, is it?", "\"벤틀리 드러믈이 저 녀석 이름이라, 그래?"], ["I like the look of that fellow.”", "저 녀석 생김새가 마음에 드는구나.\""]], [["He immediately began to talk to Drummle: not at all deterred by his replying in his heavy reticent way, but apparently led on by it to screw discourse out of him.", "그는 곧장 드러믈에게 말을 걸기 시작했다. 드러믈이 그 둔하고 과묵한 투로 대답하는 것에도 전혀 굴하지 않았고, 오히려 그 덕에 그에게서 말을 짜내는 데로 이끌려 가는 듯했다."], ["I was looking at the two, when there came between me and them the housekeeper, with the first dish for the table.", "내가 그 둘을 바라보고 있을 때, 가정부가 식탁에 올릴 첫 접시를 들고 나와 그들 사이를 가로질렀다."]], [["She was a woman of about forty, I supposed,—but I may have thought her younger than she was.", "내 짐작에 그녀는 마흔쯤 된 여자였다 — 하지만 실제보다 젊게 보았는지도 모른다."], ["Rather tall, of a lithe nimble figure, extremely pale, with large faded eyes, and a quantity of streaming hair.", "키가 제법 크고, 몸매가 나긋나긋하고 날렵했으며, 몹시 창백하고, 빛바랜 큰 눈에, 머리카락이 흘러내릴 듯 풍성했다."], ["I cannot say whether any diseased affection of the heart caused her lips to be parted as if she were panting, and her face to bear a curious expression of suddenness and flutter; but I know that I had been to see Macbeth at the theatre, a night or two before, and that her face looked to me as if it were all disturbed by fiery air, like the faces I had seen rise out of the Witches’ caldron.", "어떤 심장병이 있어서 그녀의 입술이 헐떡이는 듯 벌어져 있고 얼굴에 갑작스럽고 떨리는 듯한 묘한 표정이 어려 있었던 것인지는 알 수 없다. 그러나 나는 그 하루나 이틀 전 극장에서 「맥베스」를 보았는데, 그녀의 얼굴이 내게는 마치 마녀들의 솥에서 떠오르던 얼굴들처럼, 온통 불타는 공기에 휘저어진 것처럼 보였다는 것만큼은 안다."]], [["She set the dish on, touched my guardian quietly on the arm with a finger to notify that dinner was ready, and vanished.", "그녀는 접시를 내려놓고, 손가락으로 내 후견인의 팔을 조용히 건드려 식사가 준비되었음을 알리고는 사라졌다."], ["We took our seats at the round table, and my guardian kept Drummle on one side of him, while Startop sat on the other.", "우리는 둥근 식탁에 자리를 잡았고, 후견인은 드러믈을 한쪽에, 스타톱을 다른 쪽에 두었다."], ["It was a noble dish of fish that the housekeeper had put on table, and we had a joint of equally choice mutton afterwards, and then an equally choice bird.", "가정부가 식탁에 올린 것은 훌륭한 생선 요리였고, 뒤이어 똑같이 고른 양고기 한 덩이가, 그다음에는 똑같이 고른 새 요리가 나왔다."], ["Sauces, wines, all the accessories we wanted, and all of the best, were given out by our host from his dumb-waiter; and when they had made the circuit of the table, he always put them back again.", "소스, 포도주, 우리가 원하는 온갖 곁들이 음식이, 그것도 모두 최상품으로, 주인의 보조 탁자에서 내어졌다. 그것들이 식탁을 한 바퀴 돌고 나면 그는 언제나 다시 제자리에 돌려놓았다."], ["Similarly, he dealt us clean plates and knives and forks, for each course, and dropped those just disused into two baskets on the ground by his chair.", "마찬가지로 그는 우리에게 각 코스마다 깨끗한 접시와 나이프와 포크를 나눠 주고, 방금 쓴 것들은 자기 의자 옆 바닥에 놓인 두 개의 바구니에 떨어뜨렸다."], ["No other attendant than the housekeeper appeared.", "가정부 외에는 시중드는 사람이 아무도 나타나지 않았다."], ["She set on every dish; and I always saw in her face, a face rising out of the caldron.", "그녀는 접시를 일일이 차려 냈고, 나는 늘 그녀의 얼굴에서 그 솥에서 떠오르던 얼굴을 보았다."], ["Years afterwards, I made a dreadful likeness of that woman, by causing a face that had no other natural resemblance to it than it derived from flowing hair to pass behind a bowl of flaming spirits in a dark room.", "여러 해가 지난 뒤, 나는 흘러내리는 머리카락 말고는 그 여자와 본디 닮은 데가 전혀 없는 어떤 얼굴을, 어두운 방에서 활활 타오르는 술 한 그릇 뒤로 지나가게 함으로써, 그 여자의 무서운 초상을 만들어 냈다."]], [["Induced to take particular notice of the housekeeper, both by her own striking appearance and by Wemmick’s preparation, I observed that whenever she was in the room she kept her eyes attentively on my guardian, and that she would remove her hands from any dish she put before him, hesitatingly, as if she dreaded his calling her back, and wanted him to speak when she was nigh, if he had anything to say.", "그녀 자신의 인상적인 외모와 웨믹의 사전 언질 양쪽에서 그 가정부를 각별히 눈여겨보게 된 나는, 그녀가 방 안에 있을 때면 언제나 후견인에게서 눈을 떼지 않는다는 것을, 그리고 그 앞에 내려놓은 어떤 접시에서든 손을 머뭇거리며 거두어들인다는 것을 알아챘다. 마치 그가 자기를 다시 부를까 두려워하고, 그가 무슨 할 말이 있다면 자기가 가까이 있을 때 말해 주기를 바라는 것처럼 말이다."], ["I fancied that I could detect in his manner a consciousness of this, and a purpose of always holding her in suspense.", "나는 그의 태도에서 이를 의식하고 있다는 것과, 그녀를 늘 마음 졸이게 두려는 의도를 간파할 수 있을 것만 같았다."]], [["Dinner went off gayly, and although my guardian seemed to follow rather than originate subjects, I knew that he wrenched the weakest part of our dispositions out of us.", "저녁 식사는 흥겹게 진행되었고, 후견인은 화제를 꺼내기보다는 따라가는 듯 보였으나, 나는 그가 우리 성향에서 가장 약한 부분을 비틀어 끄집어내고 있음을 알았다."], ["For myself, I found that I was expressing my tendency to lavish expenditure, and to patronise Herbert, and to boast of my great prospects, before I quite knew that I had opened my lips.", "나 자신으로 말하자면, 나는 입을 열었다는 것을 채 깨닫기도 전에 헤픈 씀씀이에 대한 내 성향과, 허버트를 후원하는 태도와, 내 대단한 장래에 대한 자랑을 늘어놓고 있었다."], ["It was so with all of us, but with no one more than Drummle: the development of whose inclination to gird in a grudging and suspicious way at the rest, was screwed out of him before the fish was taken off.", "우리 모두가 그랬지만, 드러믈만큼 심한 자는 없었다. 인색하고 의심 많은 투로 나머지 사람들을 헐뜯으려는 그의 성벽이 비틀려 나온 것은 생선 요리가 치워지기도 전이었다."]], [["It was not then, but when we had got to the cheese, that our conversation turned upon our rowing feats, and that Drummle was rallied for coming up behind of a night in that slow amphibious way of his.", "화제가 우리의 조정 솜씨로 돌아간 것은 그때가 아니라 우리가 치즈에 이르렀을 때였는데, 그제야 드러믈은 밤에 그 느릿한 양서류 같은 방식으로 뒤쫓아 온다고 놀림을 받았다."], ["Drummle upon this, informed our host that he much preferred our room to our company, and that as to skill he was more than our master, and that as to strength he could scatter us like chaff.", "이에 드러믈은 주인에게, 자기는 우리와 어울리느니 우리 방을 훨씬 좋아한다고, 그리고 솜씨로 따지자면 자기는 우리보다 한 수 위라고, 또 힘으로 말하자면 우리를 검불처럼 흩어 버릴 수 있다고 알렸다."], ["By some invisible agency, my guardian wound him up to a pitch little short of ferocity about this trifle; and he fell to baring and spanning his arm to show how muscular it was, and we all fell to baring and spanning our arms in a ridiculous manner.", "어떤 보이지 않는 작용으로, 후견인은 이 사소한 일을 두고 그를 거의 사나움에 가까운 지경까지 부추겨 올렸다. 그러자 그는 자기 팔이 얼마나 근육질인지 보이려고 팔을 드러내고 한 뼘 한 뼘 재기 시작했고, 우리도 모두 우스꽝스러운 꼴로 팔을 드러내고 재기 시작했다."]], [["Now the housekeeper was at that time clearing the table; my guardian, taking no heed of her, but with the side of his face turned from her, was leaning back in his chair biting the side of his forefinger and showing an interest in Drummle, that, to me, was quite inexplicable.", "그때 가정부는 식탁을 치우고 있었다. 후견인은 그녀에게 아랑곳하지 않고, 얼굴을 그녀에게서 돌린 채, 의자에 기대앉아 집게손가락 옆을 깨물며, 내게는 도무지 이해할 수 없는 관심을 드러믈에게 보이고 있었다."], ["Suddenly, he clapped his large hand on the housekeeper’s, like a trap, as she stretched it across the table.", "그러다가 갑자기, 그녀가 식탁 너머로 손을 뻗었을 때, 그는 커다란 손으로 덫처럼 그녀의 손을 덮쳤다."], ["So suddenly and smartly did he do this, that we all stopped in our foolish contention.", "어찌나 갑작스럽고 날쌔게 그리했던지, 우리 모두는 그 어리석은 다툼을 멈추고 말았다."]], [["“If you talk of strength,”", "\"힘 이야기를 하는 거라면,\""], ["said Mr. Jaggers, “I’ll show you a wrist.", "재거스 씨가 말했다. \"내가 손목을 하나 보여 주지."], ["Molly, let them see your wrist.”", "몰리, 이 사람들한테 네 손목을 보여 줘라.\""]], [["Her entrapped hand was on the table, but she had already put her other hand behind her waist.", "덫에 걸린 그녀의 손은 식탁 위에 있었으나, 그녀는 이미 다른 손을 허리 뒤로 돌려 두고 있었다."], ["“Master,”", "\"주인님,\""], ["she said, in a low voice, with her eyes attentively and entreatingly fixed upon him. “Don’t.”", "그녀가 낮은 목소리로, 간절하게 그에게 시선을 고정한 채 말했다. \"그러지 마세요.\""]], [["“I’ll show you a wrist,” repeated Mr.", "\"내가 손목을 하나 보여 주겠다.\""], ["Jaggers, with an immovable determination to show it.", "재거스 씨가 기어이 보여 주겠다는 흔들림 없는 결심으로 되풀이했다."], ["“Molly, let them see your wrist.”", "\"몰리, 이 사람들한테 네 손목을 보여 줘.\""]], [["“Master,”", "\"주인님,\""], ["she again murmured.", "그녀가 다시 중얼거렸다."], ["“Please!”", "\"제발요!\""]], [["“Molly,”", "\"몰리,\""], ["said Mr. Jaggers, not looking at her, but obstinately looking at the opposite side of the room, “let them see both your wrists.", "재거스 씨가 그녀를 보지 않고 방 맞은편을 고집스레 바라보며 말했다. \"이 사람들한테 양쪽 손목을 다 보여 줘."], ["Show them. Come!”", "보여 주라니까. 어서!\""]], [["He took his hand from hers, and turned that wrist up on the table.", "그는 그녀의 손에서 자기 손을 떼고, 그 손목을 식탁 위에 뒤집어 보였다."], ["She brought her other hand from behind her, and held the two out side by side.", "그녀는 뒤로 돌렸던 다른 손을 가져와, 두 손을 나란히 내밀었다."], ["The last wrist was much disfigured,—deeply scarred and scarred across and across.", "나중에 내민 손목은 몹시 흉하게 일그러져 있었다 — 깊은 흉터가 가로세로로 어지러이 나 있었다."], ["When she held her hands out she took her eyes from Mr. Jaggers, and turned them watchfully on every one of the rest of us in succession.", "두 손을 내밀면서 그녀는 재거스 씨에게서 눈을 떼어, 나머지 우리 한 사람 한 사람에게 차례로 경계하는 눈길을 돌렸다."]], [["“There’s power here,”", "\"여기에 힘이 있지.\""], ["said Mr. Jaggers, coolly tracing out the sinews with his forefinger.", "재거스 씨가 집게손가락으로 힘줄을 더듬어 짚으며 냉정하게 말했다."], ["“Very few men have the power of wrist that this woman has.", "\"이 여자만한 손목 힘을 가진 남자는 거의 없어."], ["It’s remarkable what mere force of grip there is in these hands.", "이 손에 깃든 그저 움켜쥐는 힘만 해도 놀라울 정도지."], ["I have had occasion to notice many hands; but I never saw stronger in that respect, man’s or woman’s, than these.”", "나는 숱한 손을 눈여겨볼 기회가 있었지만, 그 점에서 이보다 더 센 손은 남자든 여자든 본 적이 없다.\""]], [["While he said these words in a leisurely, critical style, she continued to look at every one of us in regular succession as we sat.", "그가 느긋하고 비평하는 듯한 투로 이런 말을 하는 동안, 그녀는 앉아 있는 우리 한 사람 한 사람을 차례차례 계속 바라보았다."], ["The moment he ceased, she looked at him again.", "그가 말을 멈추는 순간, 그녀는 다시 그를 바라보았다."], ["“That’ll do, Molly,”", "\"됐다, 몰리.\""], ["said Mr. Jaggers, giving her a slight nod; “you have been admired, and can go.”", "재거스 씨가 그녀에게 가볍게 고갯짓하며 말했다. \"감탄을 받았으니, 이제 가도 좋다.\""], ["She withdrew her hands and went out of the room, and Mr. Jaggers, putting the decanters on from his dumb-waiter, filled his glass and passed round the wine.", "그녀가 손을 거두고 방을 나가자, 재거스 씨는 보조 탁자에서 디캔터를 꺼내 놓고, 자기 잔을 채운 다음 포도주를 돌렸다."]], [["“At half-past nine, gentlemen,”", "\"여러분,\" 그가 말했다."], ["said he, “we must break up. Pray make the best use of your time.", "\"아홉 시 반에는 우리가 자리를 파해야 합니다. 부디 시간을 알차게 쓰시오."], ["I am glad to see you all. Mr. Drummle, I drink to you.”", "모두 만나서 반갑소. 드러믈 군, 자네를 위해 한잔하지.\""]], [["If his object in singling out Drummle were to bring him out still more, it perfectly succeeded.", "드러믈을 골라낸 그의 목적이 그를 한층 더 끌어내려는 것이었다면, 그것은 완벽하게 성공한 셈이었다."], ["In a sulky triumph, Drummle showed his morose depreciation of the rest of us, in a more and more offensive degree, until he became downright intolerable.", "뚱한 승리감에 젖은 드러믈은, 나머지 우리에 대한 침울한 멸시를 점점 더 고약한 정도로 드러내더니, 마침내 도저히 견딜 수 없는 지경에 이르렀다."], ["Through all his stages, Mr. Jaggers followed him with the same strange interest.", "그 모든 단계 내내, 재거스 씨는 똑같은 기이한 관심으로 그를 좇았다."], ["He actually seemed to serve as a zest to Mr. Jaggers’s wine.", "그는 정말로 재거스 씨의 포도주에 풍미를 더해 주는 양념 노릇을 하는 듯했다."]], [["In our boyish want of discretion I dare say we took too much to drink, and I know we talked too much.", "소년다운 분별없음에서 우리는 술을 너무 많이 마셨던 것 같고, 너무 많이 떠들어 댔던 것은 분명하다."], ["We became particularly hot upon some boorish sneer of Drummle’s, to the effect that we were too free with our money.", "우리가 돈을 너무 헤프게 쓴다는 드러믈의 어떤 상스러운 빈정거림을 두고 우리는 유난히 발끈했다."], ["It led to my remarking, with more zeal than discretion, that it came with a bad grace from him, to whom Startop had lent money in my presence but a week or so before.", "그 때문에 나는, 분별보다 열의가 앞서서, 그런 말은 그에게 어울리지 않게 못난 소리라고, 바로 한 주쯤 전에 내가 보는 앞에서 스타톱이 그에게 돈을 빌려주지 않았느냐고 쏘아붙였다."]], [["“Well,”", "\"흥,\""], ["retorted Drummle; “he’ll be paid.”", "드러믈이 받아쳤다. \"갚을 거다.\""]], [["“I don’t mean to imply that he won’t,”", "\"안 갚을 거라는 말이 아니야.\" 내가 말했다."], ["said I, “but it might make you hold your tongue about us and our money, I should think.”", "\"다만 그러면 자네가 우리와 우리 돈 이야기에 입을 다물게 될 만도 하다는 거지, 내 생각엔.\""]], [["“You should think!”", "\"네 생각엔!\""], ["retorted Drummle.", "드러믈이 받아쳤다."], ["“Oh Lord!”", "\"원, 맙소사!\""]], [["“I dare say,”", "\"내 짐작엔 말이야,\""], ["I went on, meaning to be very severe, “that you wouldn’t lend money to any of us if we wanted it.”", "나는 아주 매섭게 굴 작정으로 말을 이었다. \"우리 중 누가 돈이 필요해도 자네는 빌려주지 않겠지.\""]], [["“You are right,”", "\"맞아.\""], ["said Drummle.", "드러믈이 말했다."], ["“I wouldn’t lend one of you a sixpence.", "\"나는 너희 중 누구한테도 육 펜스 한 푼 빌려주지 않을 거다."], ["I wouldn’t lend anybody a sixpence.”", "나는 누구한테도 육 펜스 한 푼 빌려주지 않아.\""]], [["“Rather mean to borrow under those circumstances, I should say.”", "\"그런 처지라면 빌리는 건 좀 치사한 짓이라고 해야겠는데, 내 생각엔.\""]], [["“You should say,”", "\"네 생각엔.\""], ["repeated Drummle.", "드러믈이 되풀이했다."], ["“Oh Lord!”", "\"원, 맙소사!\""]], [["This was so very aggravating—the more especially as I found myself making no way against his surly obtuseness—that I said, disregarding Herbert’s efforts to check me,—", "이건 어찌나 부아가 치미는 일이었던지 — 그의 뚱한 둔감함에 내가 도무지 당해 내지 못하는 것을 깨닫자 더더욱 그러했는데 — 나는 나를 말리려는 허버트의 애씀을 무시하고 말했다."]], [["“Come, Mr. Drummle, since we are on the subject, I’ll tell you what passed between Herbert here and me, when you borrowed that money.”", "\"이봐, 드러믈, 이왕 이 이야기가 나왔으니, 자네가 그 돈을 빌릴 때 여기 허버트와 나 사이에 오간 말을 들려주지.\""]], [["“I don’t want to know what passed between Herbert there and you,”", "\"나는 거기 허버트와 너 사이에 무슨 말이 오갔는지 알고 싶지 않아.\""], ["growled Drummle.", "드러믈이 으르렁댔다."], ["And I think he added in a lower growl, that we might both go to the devil and shake ourselves.", "그리고 우리 둘 다 악마에게나 가서 몸이나 떨라는 말을 더 낮게 으르렁대며 덧붙인 것 같았다."]], [["“I’ll tell you, however,”", "\"하지만 들려주지.\" 내가 말했다."], ["said I, “whether you want to know or not.", "\"자네가 알고 싶든 말든. 우리는 이렇게 말했어."], ["We said that as you put it in your pocket very glad to get it, you seemed to be immensely amused at his being so weak as to lend it.”", "자네가 그 돈을 받게 되어 무척 기뻐하며 주머니에 넣을 때, 그렇게 멍청하게 돈을 빌려주는 그가 자네한테는 더없이 우습게 보이는 모양이라고 말이야.\""]], [["Drummle laughed outright, and sat laughing in our faces, with his hands in his pockets and his round shoulders raised; plainly signifying that it was quite true, and that he despised us as asses all.", "드러믈은 대놓고 웃음을 터뜨리며, 두 손을 주머니에 찌르고 둥근 어깨를 추켜올린 채, 우리 면전에서 웃어 댔다. 그것이 전적으로 사실이며, 자기는 우리를 모두 멍청이로 여겨 경멸한다는 뜻을 분명히 내비치면서."]], [["Hereupon Startop took him in hand, though with a much better grace than I had shown, and exhorted him to be a little more agreeable.", "이때 스타톱이, 내가 보인 것보다는 훨씬 점잖은 태도로, 그를 다루어 좀 더 상냥하게 굴라고 타일렀다."], ["Startop, being a lively, bright young fellow, and Drummle being the exact opposite, the latter was always disposed to resent him as a direct personal affront.", "스타톱은 활기차고 명랑한 청년이었고 드러믈은 정반대였던 터라, 후자는 늘 전자를 마치 직접적인 인신공격이라도 되는 양 여겨 발끈하는 기질이 있었다."], ["He now retorted in a coarse, lumpish way, and Startop tried to turn the discussion aside with some small pleasantry that made us all laugh.", "그는 이제 거칠고 둔중한 투로 받아쳤고, 스타톱은 우리 모두를 웃게 만든 어떤 자그마한 농담으로 그 논쟁을 슬쩍 비껴 보려 했다."], ["Resenting this little success more than anything, Drummle, without any threat or warning, pulled his hands out of his pockets, dropped his round shoulders, swore, took up a large glass, and would have flung it at his adversary’s head, but for our entertainer’s dexterously seizing it at the instant when it was raised for that purpose.", "이 소소한 성공에 무엇보다 발끈한 드러믈은, 아무런 위협도 경고도 없이 주머니에서 두 손을 빼고, 둥근 어깨를 내리고, 욕설을 내뱉고, 커다란 잔을 집어 들어 상대의 머리를 향해 내던지려 했다. 그러나 바로 그 목적으로 그것을 치켜든 순간, 우리 주최자가 날쌔게 그것을 낚아챘다."]], [["“Gentlemen,”", "\"여러분,\""], ["said Mr. Jaggers, deliberately putting down the glass, and hauling out his gold repeater by its massive chain, “I am exceedingly sorry to announce that it’s half past nine.”", "재거스 씨가 잔을 천천히 내려놓고 묵직한 줄에 매단 금시계를 꺼내며 말했다. \"심히 유감스럽지만 아홉 시 반이 되었음을 알리는 바입니다.\""]], [["On this hint we all rose to depart.", "이 암시에 우리는 모두 일어나 떠날 채비를 했다."], ["Before we got to the street door, Startop was cheerily calling Drummle “old boy,” as if nothing had happened.", "우리가 길로 난 현관문에 이르기도 전에, 스타톱은 아무 일도 없었다는 듯 명랑하게 드러믈을 \"이 친구야\" 하고 불렀다."], ["But the old boy was so far from responding, that he would not even walk to Hammersmith on the same side of the way; so Herbert and I, who remained in town, saw them going down the street on opposite sides; Startop leading, and Drummle lagging behind in the shadow of the houses, much as he was wont to follow in his boat.", "그러나 그 친구는 응하기는커녕, 같은 길가 쪽으로 해머스미스까지 함께 걸으려 하지도 않았다. 그래서 시내에 남은 허버트와 나는 그 둘이 거리 양편으로 갈라져 내려가는 것을 보았다. 스타톱이 앞장서고, 드러믈은 집들의 그림자 속에서 뒤처져 갔는데, 그것은 그가 평소 배에서 따라오던 모습과 흡사했다."]], [["As the door was not yet shut, I thought I would leave Herbert there for a moment, and run upstairs again to say a word to my guardian.", "문이 아직 닫히지 않았기에, 나는 잠시 허버트를 거기 남겨 두고 다시 위층으로 뛰어올라가 후견인에게 한마디 하고 와야겠다고 생각했다."], ["I found him in his dressing-room surrounded by his stock of boots, already hard at it, washing his hands of us.", "나는 그가 옷 갈아입는 방에서 쌓아 둔 장화들에 둘러싸인 채, 이미 한창 우리에게서 손을 씻고 있는 것을 보았다."]], [["I told him I had come up again to say how sorry I was that anything disagreeable should have occurred, and that I hoped he would not blame me much.", "나는 다시 올라온 것은 무언가 불쾌한 일이 벌어진 것이 정말 죄송해서이며, 부디 나를 너무 탓하지 않으셨으면 한다고 말했다."]], [["“Pooh!”", "\"흥!\""], ["said he, sluicing his face, and speaking through the water-drops; “it’s nothing, Pip.", "그가 얼굴에 물을 끼얹고 물방울 사이로 말했다. \"별일 아니다, 핍."], ["I like that Spider though.”", "그래도 나는 그 거미가 마음에 들어.\""]], [["He had turned towards me now, and was shaking his head, and blowing, and towelling himself.", "그는 이제 내 쪽으로 돌아서서 고개를 흔들며, 푸푸 소리를 내고, 수건으로 몸을 닦고 있었다."]], [["“I am glad you like him, sir,”", "\"마음에 드신다니 다행이군요, 선생님.\""], ["said I—“but I don’t.”", "내가 말했다. \"하지만 저는 아닙니다.\""]], [["“No, no,”", "\"그래, 그래.\""], ["my guardian assented; “don’t have too much to do with him.", "후견인이 맞장구쳤다. \"그 녀석과 너무 어울리지는 마라."], ["Keep as clear of him as you can.", "되도록 멀리 떨어져 지내."], ["But I like the fellow, Pip; he is one of the true sort.", "하지만 나는 그 녀석이 마음에 들어, 핍. 그는 진짜배기 부류야."], ["Why, if I was a fortune-teller—”", "거참, 내가 점쟁이라면—\""]], [["Looking out of the towel, he caught my eye.", "수건 사이로 내다보다가, 그는 내 눈과 마주쳤다."]], [["“But I am not a fortune-teller,”", "\"하지만 나는 점쟁이가 아니지.\""], ["he said, letting his head drop into a festoon of towel, and towelling away at his two ears. “You know what I am, don’t you?", "그가 고개를 수건 자락 속으로 떨구고 두 귀를 닦아 대며 말했다. \"내가 뭐 하는 사람인지는 알지, 안 그러냐?"], ["Good night, Pip.”", "잘 가라, 핍.\""]], [["“Good night, sir.”", "\"안녕히 계세요, 선생님.\""]], [["In about a month after that, the Spider’s time with Mr. Pocket was up for good, and, to the great relief of all the house but Mrs. Pocket, he went home to the family hole.", "그로부터 한 달쯤 뒤, 그 거미가 포켓 씨 밑에서 보내던 시간이 영영 끝났고, 포켓 부인을 뺀 온 집안의 큰 안도 속에, 그는 자기 집안의 소굴로 돌아갔다."]], [["“I write this by request of Mr. Gargery, for to let you know that he is going to London in company with Mr. Wopsle and would be glad if agreeable to be allowed to see you.", "이 글은 가저리 씨의 부탁으로 대신 쓰는 것으로, 그분이 워프슬 씨와 함께 런던에 가시게 되었고, 괜찮으시다면 도련님을 뵙고 싶어 하신다는 것을 알려 드리려는 것입니다."], ["He would call at Barnard’s Hotel Tuesday morning at nine o’clock, when if not agreeable please leave word.", "그분은 화요일 아침 아홉 시에 바너드 호텔로 찾아가실 예정이오니, 만약 곤란하시면 부디 그곳에 말을 남겨 주십시오."], ["Your poor sister is much the same as when you left.", "가엾은 누님은 도련님이 떠나실 때와 별반 다름없으십니다."], ["We talk of you in the kitchen every night, and wonder what you are saying and doing.", "우리는 매일 밤 부엌에서 도련님 이야기를 나누며, 도련님이 무슨 말을 하시고 무엇을 하고 계실까 궁금해합니다."], ["If now considered in the light of a liberty, excuse it for the love of poor old days.", "혹시 이것이 주제넘은 일로 여겨지신다면, 정다웠던 옛 시절을 생각하시어 너그러이 용서해 주십시오."], ["No more, dear Mr. Pip, from", "더 드릴 말씀 없이, 친애하는 핍 도련님,"]], [["“Your ever obliged, and affectionate servant, “BIDDY.”", "언제나 은혜를 입은, 도련님을 아끼는 하인,\n비디 올림."]], [["“P.S.", "추신."], ["He wishes me most particular to write what larks.", "그분은 부디 무슨 신난다는 말을 꼭 적어 달라고 신신당부하셨습니다."], ["He says you will understand.", "도련님이라면 아실 거라고 하십니다."], ["I hope and do not doubt it will be agreeable to see him, even though a gentleman, for you had ever a good heart, and he is a worthy, worthy man.", "비록 신사가 되셨다 해도 도련님은 늘 마음씨가 고우셨으니, 그분을 뵙는 일이 도련님께 즐거우시리라 저는 바라고 또 의심치 않습니다. 그분은 참으로, 참으로 훌륭한 분이십니다."], ["I have read him all, excepting only the last little sentence, and he wishes me most particular to write again what larks.”", "저는 마지막 짧은 한 문장만 빼고 이 편지를 그분께 전부 읽어 드렸는데, 그분은 부디 무슨 신난다는 말을 다시 한번 꼭 적어 달라고 신신당부하셨습니다.」"]], [["I received this letter by the post on Monday morning, and therefore its appointment was for next day.", "나는 이 편지를 월요일 아침 우편으로 받았으니, 그 약속은 바로 다음 날로 잡힌 셈이었다."], ["Let me confess exactly with what feelings I looked forward to Joe’s coming.", "내가 조의 방문을 어떤 심정으로 기다렸는지 정확히 고백해 두기로 하자."]], [["Not with pleasure, though I was bound to him by so many ties; no; with considerable disturbance, some mortification, and a keen sense of incongruity.", "기쁜 마음은 아니었다, 비록 그토록 많은 인연으로 그와 맺어져 있었음에도. 그렇다, 적잖은 동요와 약간의 굴욕감, 그리고 어울리지 않는다는 날카로운 느낌을 안고서였다."], ["If I could have kept him away by paying money, I certainly would have paid money.", "돈을 치러 그가 오지 않게 할 수만 있었다면, 나는 분명히 돈을 치렀을 것이다."], ["My greatest reassurance was that he was coming to Barnard’s Inn, not to Hammersmith, and consequently would not fall in Bentley Drummle’s way.", "가장 큰 위안이라면, 그가 해머스미스가 아니라 바너드 인으로 온다는 것, 따라서 벤틀리 드러믈과 마주칠 일이 없으리라는 점이었다."], ["I had little objection to his being seen by Herbert or his father, for both of whom I had a respect; but I had the sharpest sensitiveness as to his being seen by Drummle, whom I held in contempt.", "그가 허버트나 그의 아버지의 눈에 띄는 것에는 별 거리낌이 없었으니, 두 사람 모두 내가 존경하는 이들이었기 때문이다. 그러나 내가 경멸하던 드러믈의 눈에 그가 띄는 것에는 더없이 예민하게 반응했다."], ["So, throughout life, our worst weaknesses and meannesses are usually committed for the sake of the people whom we most despise.", "이렇듯 우리는 평생토록, 가장 멸시하는 사람들을 위하여 가장 추악한 약점과 비열함을 저지르는 법이다."]], [["I had begun to be always decorating the chambers in some quite unnecessary and inappropriate way or other, and very expensive those wrestles with Barnard proved to be.", "나는 어느샌가 늘 그 방을 무어라 말할 수 없이 불필요하고 어울리지 않는 방식으로 꾸미기 시작했는데, 바너드를 상대로 한 그 씨름은 결국 무척이나 비싼 것으로 드러났다."], ["By this time, the rooms were vastly different from what I had found them, and I enjoyed the honour of occupying a few prominent pages in the books of a neighbouring upholsterer.", "이 무렵에 이르러 그 방들은 내가 처음 보았던 모습과는 판이하게 달라져 있었고, 나는 이웃 가구상의 장부에 몇 페이지나 떡하니 이름을 올리는 영예를 누리고 있었다."], ["I had got on so fast of late, that I had even started a boy in boots,—top boots,—in bondage and slavery to whom I might have been said to pass my days.", "근래에 어찌나 빨리 일을 벌였던지, 나는 심지어 부츠를 신은 소년 하나를 — 그것도 목이 긴 부츠를 — 부리기 시작했으니, 그 아이의 속박과 노예 노릇에 매여 나날을 보냈다고 해도 좋을 정도였다."], ["For, after I had made the monster (out of the refuse of my washerwoman’s family), and had clothed him with a blue coat, canary waistcoat, white cravat, creamy breeches, and the boots already mentioned, I had to find him a little to do and a great deal to eat; and with both of those horrible requirements he haunted my existence.", "왜냐하면 나는 그 괴물을 (세탁부 식구 가운데 남아도는 아이로) 만들어 내고, 파란 윗도리와 카나리아빛 조끼, 흰 넥타이, 크림빛 반바지, 그리고 앞서 말한 부츠로 차려 입힌 뒤에는, 아이에게 줄 일거리를 조금, 그리고 먹일 음식을 잔뜩 마련해 주어야 했기 때문이다. 그 끔찍한 두 가지 요건으로 그 아이는 내 삶을 따라다니며 괴롭혔다."]], [["This avenging phantom was ordered to be on duty at eight on Tuesday morning in the hall, (it was two feet square, as charged for floorcloth,) and Herbert suggested certain things for breakfast that he thought Joe would like.", "이 복수의 망령은 화요일 아침 여덟 시에 현관에서 (그 현관은 가로세로 두 자 넓이로, 바닥 깔개 값으로 청구된 만큼이었다) 대기하라는 명을 받았고, 허버트는 조가 좋아하리라 여겨지는 몇 가지 음식을 아침 식사로 제안했다."], ["While I felt sincerely obliged to him for being so interested and considerate, I had an odd half-provoked sense of suspicion upon me, that if Joe had been coming to see him, he wouldn’t have been quite so brisk about it.", "그가 그토록 관심을 가지고 사려 깊게 굴어 주는 것에 진심으로 고마움을 느끼면서도, 한편으로는 묘하게 반쯤 부아가 치미는 의심이 들었으니, 만약 조가 그를 만나러 오는 길이었다면 그가 이토록 활기차게 굴지는 않았으리라는 것이었다."]], [["However, I came into town on the Monday night to be ready for Joe, and I got up early in the morning, and caused the sitting-room and breakfast-table to assume their most splendid appearance.", "어쨌든 나는 조를 맞을 채비를 하려고 월요일 밤 시내로 들어왔고, 아침 일찍 일어나 거실과 아침 식탁을 더없이 화려한 모습으로 꾸며 놓았다."], ["Unfortunately the morning was drizzly, and an angel could not have concealed the fact that Barnard was shedding sooty tears outside the window, like some weak giant of a Sweep.", "공교롭게도 아침에는 이슬비가 내렸으니, 바너드가 창밖에서 마치 굴뚝 청소부 같은 어느 허약한 거인처럼 검댕의 눈물을 흘리고 있다는 사실은 천사라 해도 감출 수 없었을 것이다."]], [["As the time approached I should have liked to run away, but the Avenger pursuant to orders was in the hall, and presently I heard Joe on the staircase.", "시간이 다가오자 나는 달아나고 싶었으나, 명령에 따라 복수자는 현관에 버티고 있었고, 이윽고 나는 계단에서 조의 기척을 들었다."], ["I knew it was Joe, by his clumsy manner of coming upstairs,—his state boots being always too big for him,—and by the time it took him to read the names on the other floors in the course of his ascent.", "그가 조라는 것을 나는 알았으니, 그가 계단을 올라오는 그 어설픈 몸짓으로 — 그의 나들이 부츠는 늘 그에게 너무 컸으니까 — 그리고 그가 올라오는 동안 다른 층의 문패에 적힌 이름들을 읽느라 들이는 시간으로 알 수 있었다."], ["When at last he stopped outside our door, I could hear his finger tracing over the painted letters of my name, and I afterwards distinctly heard him breathing in at the keyhole.", "마침내 그가 우리 집 문밖에서 멈춰 섰을 때, 나는 그의 손가락이 페인트로 그려진 내 이름의 글자들을 더듬어 가는 소리를 들을 수 있었고, 그러고 나서 그가 열쇠 구멍에 대고 숨을 들이쉬는 소리를 또렷이 들었다."], ["Finally he gave a faint single rap, and Pepper—such was the compromising name of the avenging boy—announced “Mr. Gargery!”", "마침내 그가 희미하게 한 번 문을 두드렸고, 페퍼가 — 그것이 그 복수의 소년을 두고 타협 끝에 붙인 이름이었다 — \"가저리 씨!\"라고 알렸다."], ["I thought he never would have done wiping his feet, and that I must have gone out to lift him off the mat, but at last he came in.", "나는 그가 발을 닦는 일을 영영 끝내지 못할 것만 같아, 내가 나가서 그를 매트에서 들어 올려야 하나 싶었으나, 그는 마침내 들어왔다."]], [["“Joe, how are you, Joe?”", "\"조, 잘 지냈어, 조?\""]], [["“Pip, how AIR you, Pip?”", "\"핍, 자아네 잘 지냈는가, 핍?\""]], [["With his good honest face all glowing and shining, and his hat put down on the floor between us, he caught both my hands and worked them straight up and down, as if I had been the last-patented Pump.", "그 선량하고 정직한 얼굴이 온통 붉게 빛나고 환히 반짝이는 가운데, 모자를 우리 사이 바닥에 내려놓은 채, 그는 내 두 손을 붙들고 위아래로 곧장 펌프질하듯 흔들어 댔으니, 마치 내가 막 특허를 받은 최신식 펌프라도 되는 양했다."]], [["“I am glad to see you, Joe.", "\"만나서 반가워, 조."], ["Give me your hat.”", "모자 이리 줘.\""]], [["But Joe, taking it up carefully with both hands, like a bird’s-nest with eggs in it, wouldn’t hear of parting with that piece of property, and persisted in standing talking over it in a most uncomfortable way.", "그러나 조는 그것을, 알이 든 새 둥지라도 되는 양 두 손으로 조심스레 집어 들면서, 그 소중한 물건을 내놓는다는 말은 들으려고도 하지 않았고, 한사코 그것을 끌어안은 채 서서 더없이 거북한 모양으로 이야기를 이어 갔다."]], [["“Which you have that growed,”", "\"이렇게나 자라고,\" 조가 말했다."], ["said Joe, “and that swelled, and that gentle-folked;”", "\"이렇게나 늠름해지고, 이렇게나 신사티가 나고,\""], ["Joe considered a little before he discovered this word; “as to be sure you are a honour to your king and country.”", "조는 이 말을 찾아내기까지 잠시 궁리했다. \"그러니 자네야말로 임금님과 나라의 자랑거리가 분명하다네.\""]], [["“And you, Joe, look wonderfully well.”", "\"형도, 조, 아주 좋아 보여.\""]], [["“Thank God,”", "\"고맙게도,\""], ["said Joe, “I’m ekerval to most.", "조가 말했다. \"나야 남들만큼은 거뜬하지."], ["And your sister, she’s no worse than she were.", "그리고 자네 누님은, 자네가 떠날 때보다 나빠지진 않았어."], ["And Biddy, she’s ever right and ready.", "그리고 비디는, 늘 한결같이 미덥고 빈틈없고."], ["And all friends is no backerder, if not no forarder.", "그리고 친구들은 죄다, 나아진 건 없어도 못해지진 않았지. 워프슬만 빼고."], ["’Ceptin Wopsle; he’s had a drop.”", "그 사람은 한바탕 떨어져 나갔거든.\""]], [["All this time (still with both hands taking great care of the bird’s-nest), Joe was rolling his eyes round and round the room, and round and round the flowered pattern of my dressing-gown.", "이러는 내내 (여전히 두 손으로 새 둥지를 끔찍이도 소중히 받쳐 든 채) 조는 두 눈을 굴려 방 안을 빙빙 둘러보고, 또 내 실내복의 꽃무늬를 빙빙 둘러보고 있었다."]], [["“Had a drop, Joe?”", "\"떨어져 나갔다고, 조?\""]], [["“Why yes,” said Joe, lowering his voice, “he’s left the Church and went into the playacting.", "\"아 그게 말이지,\" 조가 목소리를 낮추며 말했다."], ["Which the playacting have likeways brought him to London along with me. And his wish were,”", "\"그 사람이 교회를 떠나서 연극에 발을 들였어. 그 연극이 또 마찬가지로 그 사람을 나하고 같이 런던까지 데려온 거고."], ["said Joe, getting the bird’s-nest under his left arm for the moment, and groping in it for an egg with his right; “if no offence, as I would ’and you that.”", "그리고 그 사람의 바람은 말이야,\" 조는 잠시 새 둥지를 왼팔 밑으로 옮겨 끼고 오른손으로 그 안에서 알을 더듬어 찾으며 말했다. \"괜찮다면, 이걸 자네한테 건네주라는 거였어.\""]], [["I took what Joe gave me, and found it to be the crumpled play-bill of a small metropolitan theatre, announcing the first appearance, in that very week, of “the celebrated Provincial Amateur of Roscian renown, whose unique performance in the highest tragic walk of our National Bard has lately occasioned so great a sensation in local dramatic circles.”", "나는 조가 건넨 것을 받아 들었는데, 그것은 어느 작은 시내 극장의 구겨진 연극 전단으로, 바로 그 주에 \"로스키우스의 명성에 빛나는 저명한 지방 아마추어 배우\"가 처음으로 무대에 오른다고 알리는 것이었으니, \"우리 민족 시인의 가장 드높은 비극 영역에서 펼치는 그 독보적 연기가 근래 이 지방 연극계에 그토록 큰 반향을 불러일으킨\" 인물이라고 했다."]], [["“Were you at his performance, Joe?”", "\"형도 그 사람 공연을 봤어, 조?\""], ["I inquired.", "내가 물었다."]], [["“I were,”", "\"보았지.\""], ["said Joe, with emphasis and solemnity.", "조가 힘주어, 그리고 자못 엄숙하게 말했다."]], [["“Was there a great sensation?”", "\"큰 반향이 일었어?\""]], [["“Why,” said Joe, “yes, there certainly were a peck of orange-peel.", "\"아 그게 말이지,\" 조가 말했다."], ["Partickler when he see the ghost.", "\"그래, 분명히 오렌지 껍질이 한 무더기 날아들기는 했어."], ["Though I put it to yourself, sir, whether it were calc’lated to keep a man up to his work with a good hart, to be continiwally cutting in betwixt him and the Ghost with “Amen!”", "특히 그 사람이 유령을 봤을 때 말이야. 하지만 자네한테 한번 물어보겠네만, 도련님, 한 사람이 좋은 기운으로 제 일에 몰두하려는데, 그 사람과 유령 사이에 끊임없이 '아멘!' 하고 끼어드는 게 과연 그 사람한테 도움이 될 만한 짓이었겠나?"], ["A man may have had a misfortun’ and been in the Church,”", "사람이 불운을 겪고 교회에 몸담은 적이 있다고 해서,\""], ["said Joe, lowering his voice to an argumentative and feeling tone, “but that is no reason why you should put him out at such a time.", "조가 목소리를 따지듯, 그러면서도 사뭇 감정에 젖은 어조로 낮추며 말했다. \"그렇다고 해서 그런 때에 그 사람을 야유해 쫓아낼 이유는 못 되는 거 아닌가."], ["Which I meantersay, if the ghost of a man’s own father cannot be allowed to claim his attention, what can, Sir?", "그러니까 내 말은, 제 친아버지의 유령조차 그 사람의 주목을 끌어선 안 된다면, 대체 뭐가 끌 수 있다는 건가, 도련님?"], ["Still more, when his mourning ’at is unfortunately made so small as that the weight of the black feathers brings it off, try to keep it on how you may.”", "더구나 그 사람 상복 모자가 공교롭게도 어찌나 작게 만들어졌던지, 검은 깃털 무게에 자꾸 벗겨져서, 아무리 붙들어 두려 애써도 소용이 없는 마당에 말이야.\""]], [["A ghost-seeing effect in Joe’s own countenance informed me that Herbert had entered the room.", "조의 얼굴에 마치 유령이라도 본 듯한 빛이 떠오르는 것을 보고, 나는 허버트가 방에 들어왔음을 알았다."], ["So, I presented Joe to Herbert, who held out his hand; but Joe backed from it, and held on by the bird’s-nest.", "그래서 나는 조를 허버트에게 소개했고, 허버트는 손을 내밀었으나, 조는 그 손을 피해 뒷걸음질 치며 새 둥지를 꼭 붙들었다."]], [["“Your servant, Sir,”", "\"안녕하십니까, 선생님,\""], ["said Joe, “which I hope as you and Pip”—here his eye fell on the Avenger, who was putting some toast on table, and so plainly denoted an intention to make that young gentleman one of the family, that I frowned it down and confused him more—“I meantersay, you two gentlemen,—which I hope as you get your elths in this close spot?", "조가 말했다. \"부디 선생님과 핍이\" — 여기서 그의 눈길이, 토스트를 식탁에 올려놓고 있던 복수자에게 떨어졌는데, 그 어린 신사를 한 식구로 끼워 줄 작정임을 어찌나 빤히 드러냈던지, 나는 인상을 찌푸려 그를 단념시켰고 그 바람에 그를 더욱 당황하게 만들었다 — \"그러니까 내 말은, 두 분 신사께서, 이 비좁은 데서 건강은 잘 챙기고 계신지요?"], ["For the present may be a werry good inn, according to London opinions,”", "지금 이곳이 런던 사람들 생각으로야 아주 훌륭한 여관일는지 모르고,\""], ["said Joe, confidentially, “and I believe its character do stand it; but I wouldn’t keep a pig in it myself,—not in the case that I wished him to fatten wholesome and to eat with a meller flavour on him.”", "조가 은밀하게 말했다. \"그 평판이 그만하다는 것도 믿습니다만, 저 같으면 여기서 돼지 한 마리도 안 키울 겁니다요. 그놈을 건강하게 살찌워 부드러운 풍미가 돌게 잡아먹고 싶은 경우라면 더더욱 말입니다요.\""]], [["Having borne this flattering testimony to the merits of our dwelling-place, and having incidentally shown this tendency to call me “sir,” Joe, being invited to sit down to table, looked all round the room for a suitable spot on which to deposit his hat,—as if it were only on some very few rare substances in nature that it could find a resting place,—and ultimately stood it on an extreme corner of the chimney-piece, from which it ever afterwards fell off at intervals.", "우리 거처의 장점에 대해 이토록 우쭐한 증언을 늘어놓고, 또 그 와중에 나를 \"도련님\"이라 부르려는 이 버릇을 내비친 끝에, 조는 식탁에 앉으라는 권유를 받고서, 모자를 내려놓을 만한 적당한 자리를 찾아 방 안을 두루 둘러보았으니 — 마치 자연계의 지극히 희귀한 몇몇 물질 위에서만 그것이 안식처를 찾을 수 있기라도 한 듯했다 — 결국에는 벽난로 선반 맨 끝 모서리에 그것을 세워 놓았고, 그 뒤로 그것은 시도 때도 없이 거기서 굴러떨어졌다."]], [["“Do you take tea, or coffee, Mr. Gargery?”", "\"차를 드시겠습니까, 아니면 커피를 드시겠습니까, 가저리 씨?\""], ["asked Herbert, who always presided of a morning.", "늘 아침 식탁을 주관하던 허버트가 물었다."]], [["“Thankee, Sir,”", "\"고맙습니다, 선생님,\""], ["said Joe, stiff from head to foot, “I’ll take whichever is most agreeable to yourself.”", "조가 머리끝부터 발끝까지 뻣뻣하게 굳은 채 말했다. \"선생님께 가장 편하신 쪽으로 아무거나 들겠습니다요.\""]], [["“What do you say to coffee?”", "\"커피는 어떠십니까?\""]], [["“Thankee, Sir,”", "\"고맙습니다, 선생님,\""], ["returned Joe, evidently dispirited by the proposal, “since you are so kind as make chice of coffee, I will not run contrairy to your own opinions.", "조가, 그 제안에 분명히 풀이 죽은 기색으로 대답했다. \"선생님께서 그렇게 친절히 커피를 골라 주시니, 저야 선생님 뜻을 거스를 생각이 없습니다요."], ["But don’t you never find it a little ’eating?”", "한데 커피는 좀 속이 타는 것 같지 않습니까요?\""]], [["“Say tea then,” said Herbert, pouring it out.", "\"그럼 차로 하시지요.\" 허버트가 차를 따르며 말했다."]], [["Here Joe’s hat tumbled off the mantel-piece, and he started out of his chair and picked it up, and fitted it to the same exact spot.", "이때 조의 모자가 벽난로 선반에서 굴러떨어졌고, 그는 의자에서 벌떡 일어나 그것을 주워 다시 똑같은 그 자리에 정확히 맞춰 놓았다."], ["As if it were an absolute point of good breeding that it should tumble off again soon.", "마치 그것이 곧 다시 굴러떨어지는 것이 절대적인 예법의 요체이기라도 한 양했다."]], [["“When did you come to town, Mr. Gargery?”", "\"언제 시내에 오셨습니까, 가저리 씨?\""]], [["“Were it yesterday afternoon?”", "\"그게 어제 오후였던가?\""], ["said Joe, after coughing behind his hand, as if he had had time to catch the whooping-cough since he came.", "조가 손으로 입을 가리고 기침을 하고서 말했는데, 마치 그가 온 뒤로 백일해라도 옮을 만한 시간이 흐른 것 같았다."], ["“No it were not.", "\"아니, 아니었지."], ["Yes it were. Yes. It were yesterday afternoon”", "맞아, 그랬어. 맞아. 어제 오후였어.\""], ["(with an appearance of mingled wisdom, relief, and strict impartiality).", "(지혜로움과 안도감, 그리고 엄정한 공정함이 한데 뒤섞인 듯한 표정을 지으며.)"]], [["“Have you seen anything of London yet?”", "\"런던 구경은 좀 하셨습니까?\""]], [["“Why, yes, Sir,”", "\"아 그게, 그럼요, 선생님,\""], ["said Joe, “me and Wopsle went off straight to look at the Blacking Ware’us.", "조가 말했다. \"나하고 워프슬이 곧장 구두약 창고를 보러 갔지요."], ["But we didn’t find that it come up to its likeness in the red bills at the shop doors; which I meantersay,”", "한데 그게 가게 문마다 붙은 빨간 광고지에 그려진 것만 못하더란 말입니다요. 그러니까 내 말은,\""], ["added Joe, in an explanatory manner, “as it is there drawd too architectooralooral.”", "조가 설명조로 덧붙였다. \"거기엔 너무 건추웅저억으로 그려 놨거든요.\""]], [["I really believe Joe would have prolonged this word (mightily expressive to my mind of some architecture that I know) into a perfect Chorus, but for his attention being providentially attracted by his hat, which was toppling. Indeed, it demanded from him a constant attention, and a quickness of eye and hand, very like that exacted by wicket-keeping.", "* 구두약 창고: 디킨스 자신이 소년 시절 일했던 워런 구두약 공장을 떠올리게 하는 대목이다. 나는 조가 정말로 이 말을 (내가 아는 어떤 건축 양식을 두고 내 머릿속에 자못 강렬한 인상을 주는 말이었다) 늘여 빼서 온전한 합창으로까지 만들었을 것이라 정말로 믿는데, 다행히도 그의 주의가 위태롭게 흔들리던 모자에 끌린 덕분에 그러지 못했다."], ["He made extraordinary play with it, and showed the greatest skill; now, rushing at it and catching it neatly as it dropped; now, merely stopping it midway, beating it up, and humouring it in various parts of the room and against a good deal of the pattern of the paper on the wall, before he felt it safe to close with it; finally splashing it into the slop-basin, where I took the liberty of laying hands upon it.", "사실 그 모자는 그에게 끊임없는 주의와, 위켓키퍼에게 요구되는 것과 흡사한 눈과 손의 민첩함을 요구했다. 그는 그 모자를 가지고 비상한 묘기를 부렸고 더없는 솜씨를 선보였으니, 어느 때는 그것이 떨어질 때 달려들어 깔끔히 낚아챘고, 어느 때는 떨어지는 중간에 그저 멈춰 세웠다가 다시 쳐올려, 방 안 여기저기서, 또 벽지 무늬의 상당 부분을 상대로 그것을 어르고 달랜 끝에야 안전하게 거머쥘 만하다고 여겼으며, 끝내는 그것을 개숫물 대접 속에 첨벙 빠뜨렸으니, 그제야 나는 외람되이 손을 뻗어 그것을 집어 들었다."]], [["As to his shirt-collar, and his coat-collar, they were perplexing to reflect upon,—insoluble mysteries both.", "그의 셔츠 깃과 윗도리 깃으로 말하자면, 곰곰이 생각해 볼수록 사람을 어리둥절하게 만드는 것이었다 — 둘 다 풀 길 없는 수수께끼였다."], ["Why should a man scrape himself to that extent, before he could consider himself full dressed?", "어찌하여 사람이 자신을 완전히 차려입었다고 여기기까지 그토록 제 몸을 박박 긁어야 한단 말인가?"], ["Why should he suppose it necessary to be purified by suffering for his holiday clothes?", "어찌하여 그는 나들이옷을 위해 고통을 겪어 가며 정화되어야 한다고 여겼던 것일까?"], ["Then he fell into such unaccountable fits of meditation, with his fork midway between his plate and his mouth; had his eyes attracted in such strange directions; was afflicted with such remarkable coughs; sat so far from the table, and dropped so much more than he ate, and pretended that he hadn’t dropped it; that I was heartily glad when Herbert left us for the City.", "그러더니 그는, 포크를 접시와 입 중간쯤에 든 채로 영문 모를 명상에 빠져들곤 했고, 두 눈은 야릇한 방향으로 끌려가곤 했으며, 놀랄 만한 기침에 시달렸고, 식탁에서 한참이나 떨어져 앉아, 먹는 것보다 흘리는 것이 훨씬 많았으면서도 그것을 흘리지 않은 척했으니, 허버트가 우리를 두고 시티로 떠났을 때 나는 진심으로 마음이 놓였다."]], [["I had neither the good sense nor the good feeling to know that this was all my fault, and that if I had been easier with Joe, Joe would have been easier with me.", "이 모든 것이 다 내 잘못이라는 것을, 그리고 내가 조를 더 편하게 대했더라면 조도 나를 더 편하게 대했으리라는 것을 알 만한 분별도, 그만한 선량한 마음씨도 나는 갖추지 못했다."], ["I felt impatient of him and out of temper with him; in which condition he heaped coals of fire on my head.", "나는 그가 답답하게 느껴졌고 그에게 짜증이 났으니, 그러는 와중에 그는 내 머리 위에 숯불을 쌓아 올린 셈이었다. * 머리 위에 숯불을 쌓다: 성경 잠언의 구절로, 악을 선으로 갚아 상대를 부끄럽게 한다는 뜻이다."]], [["“Us two being now alone, sir,”—began Joe.", "\"이제 우리 둘만 남았으니, 도련님\" — 조가 입을 열었다."]], [["“Joe,”", "\"조,\""], ["I interrupted, pettishly, “how can you call me, sir?”", "나는 짜증스레 말을 가로막았다. \"어떻게 나를 도련님이라 부를 수 있어?\""]], [["Joe looked at me for a single instant with something faintly like reproach.", "조는 잠깐 동안 무언가 어렴풋이 책망하는 듯한 빛으로 나를 바라보았다."], ["Utterly preposterous as his cravat was, and as his collars were, I was conscious of a sort of dignity in the look.", "그의 넥타이가, 또 그의 깃이 아무리 터무니없었다 해도, 나는 그 눈빛에서 일종의 위엄을 느꼈다."]], [["“Us two being now alone,”", "\"이제 우리 둘만 남았으니,\""], ["resumed Joe, “and me having the intentions and abilities to stay not many minutes more, I will now conclude—leastways begin—to mention what have led to my having had the present honour.", "조가 다시 입을 열었다. \"그리고 나로서는 몇 분 더 머물 생각도 그럴 처지도 못 되니, 이제 마무리 짓겠네 — 아니, 시작하겠네 — 무엇이 나로 하여금 오늘 이 영광을 누리게 했는지를 말이야."], ["For was it not,”", "왜냐하면,\""], ["said Joe, with his old air of lucid exposition, “that my only wish were to be useful to you, I should not have had the honour of breaking wittles in the company and abode of gentlemen.”", "조가 늘 그러던 또렷한 설명조로 말했다. \"자네에게 도움이 되고 싶다는 것이 내 유일한 바람이 아니었더라면, 나는 신사들과 한자리에서, 신사들의 거처에서 음식을 나누는 영광 따위 누리지 않았을 테니까.\""]], [["I was so unwilling to see the look again, that I made no remonstrance against this tone.", "나는 그 눈빛을 다시 보기가 너무도 싫었던 터라, 이 호칭에 대해 한마디도 따지지 않았다."]], [["“Well, sir,” pursued Joe, “this is how it were.", "\"그래서 말이지, 도련님,\" 조가 말을 이었다."], ["I were at the Bargemen t’other night, Pip;”—whenever he subsided into affection, he called me Pip, and whenever he relapsed into politeness he called me sir; “when there come up in his shay-cart, Pumblechook.", "\"사정은 이러했어. 며칠 전 밤에 나는 뱃사람 주막에 있었거든, 핍.\" — 그가 다정한 마음으로 가라앉을 때면 그는 나를 핍이라 불렀고, 다시 예의로 되돌아갈 때면 도련님이라 불렀다 — \"그때 펌블축이 제 마차를 몰고 나타난 거야."], ["Which that same identical,”", "바로 그 작자가 말이야,\""], ["said Joe, going down a new track, “do comb my ’air the wrong way sometimes, awful, by giving out up and down town as it were him which ever had your infant companionation and were looked upon as a playfellow by yourself.”", "조가 새로운 갈래로 빠지며 말했다. \"이따금 끔찍하게 내 비위를 거꾸로 긁어 놓는단 말이지. 온 동네방네 떠들고 다니길, 자네 어린 시절 동무가 자기였다는 둥, 자네가 자기를 놀이 친구로 여겼다는 둥 하면서 말이야.\""]], [["“Nonsense.", "\"말도 안 돼."], ["It was you, Joe.”", "그건 형이었잖아, 조.\""]], [["“Which I fully believed it were, Pip,”", "\"나도 진작에 그렇게 굳게 믿었지, 핍,\""], ["said Joe, slightly tossing his head, “though it signify little now, sir.", "조가 고개를 슬쩍 젖히며 말했다. \"이제 와선 별 의미 없는 일이다만, 도련님."], ["Well, Pip; this same identical, which his manners is given to blusterous, come to me at the Bargemen (wot a pipe and a pint of beer do give refreshment to the workingman, sir, and do not over stimilate), and his word were, ‘Joseph, Miss Havisham she wish to speak to you.’”", "어쨌든, 핍. 바로 그 작자가, 본디 으스대는 버릇이 있는 그 작자가, 뱃사람 주막에 있는 나한테 와서는 (담배 한 대와 맥주 한 잔이 일하는 사람한테 기운을 돋워 주거든, 도련님, 과하게 자극하지도 않고 말이야), 이러는 거야, '조지프, 해비셤 아씨께서 자네하고 얘기를 나누고 싶어 하시네.'\""]], [["“Miss Havisham, Joe?”", "\"해비셤 아씨가, 조?\""]], [["“‘She wish,’ were Pumblechook’s word, ‘to speak to you.’”", "\"'얘기를 나누고 싶어 하시네,' 그게 펌블축의 말이었어, '자네하고.'\""], ["Joe sat and rolled his eyes at the ceiling.", "조는 앉은 채로 두 눈을 굴려 천장을 올려다보았다."]], [["“Yes, Joe?", "\"그래서, 조?"], ["Go on, please.”", "어서 계속해 봐.\""]], [["“Next day, sir,”", "\"이튿날, 도련님,\""], ["said Joe, looking at me as if I were a long way off, “having cleaned myself, I go and I see Miss A.”", "조가, 마치 내가 저 멀리 떨어져 있기라도 한 듯 나를 바라보며 말했다. \"몸을 씻고 나서 나는 가서 A 아씨를 뵈었지.\""]], [["“Miss A., Joe?", "\"A 아씨라니, 조?"], ["Miss Havisham?”", "해비셤 아씨 말이야?\""]], [["“Which I say, sir,”", "\"내 말이 그 말이라네, 도련님,\""], ["replied Joe, with an air of legal formality, as if he were making his will, “Miss A., or otherways Havisham.", "조가, 마치 유언장이라도 작성하는 양 법률적 격식을 갖춘 태도로 대답했다. \"A 아씨, 다른 말로 해비셤 아씨 말이야."], ["Her expression air then as follering: ‘Mr. Gargery.", "그분 말씀이 그러니까 이러했어. '가저리 씨."], ["You air in correspondence with Mr.", "핍 씨와 서신을 주고받고 계신가요?'"], ["Pip?’ Having had a letter from you, I were able to say ‘I am.’ (When I married your sister, sir, I said ‘I will;’ and when I answered your friend, Pip, I said ‘I am.’) ‘Would you tell him, then,’ said she, ‘that which Estella has come home and would be glad to see him.’”", "자네한테서 편지를 받았던 터라, 나는 '그렇습니다'라고 말할 수 있었지. (자네 누님과 결혼할 때, 도련님, 나는 '그러겠습니다'라고 했고, 자네 친구한테 대답할 때, 핍, 나는 '그렇습니다'라고 했어.) '그럼,' 그분이 말씀하시길, '에스텔라가 집에 돌아왔고 그를 만나면 기뻐할 거라고 전해 주시겠어요?'\""]], [["I felt my face fire up as I looked at Joe. I hope one remote cause of its firing may have been my consciousness that if I had known his errand, I should have given him more encouragement.", "조를 바라보는 동안 나는 얼굴이 확 달아오르는 것을 느꼈다. 그 한 가지 먼 원인이, 만약 그의 용건을 미리 알았더라면 그를 더 격려해 주었을 텐데 하는 자각이었기를 나는 바란다."]], [["“Biddy,”", "\"비디는,\""], ["pursued Joe, “when I got home and asked her fur to write the message to you, a little hung back.", "조가 말을 이었다. \"내가 집에 돌아와 자네한테 그 전갈을 적어 달라고 부탁했더니, 좀 머뭇거리더군."], ["Biddy says, ‘I know he will be very glad to have it by word of mouth, it is holiday time, you want to see him, go!’ I have now concluded, sir,”", "비디가 그러길, '직접 입으로 들으면 그분이 무척 기뻐하실 거예요. 마침 휴가철이고, 당신도 그분을 보고 싶어 하시잖아요. 가세요!' 이제 다 마쳤네, 도련님,\""], ["said Joe, rising from his chair, “and, Pip, I wish you ever well and ever prospering to a greater and a greater height.”", "조가 의자에서 일어서며 말했다. \"그리고, 핍, 나는 자네가 언제까지나 잘 지내고, 언제까지나 점점 더 높은 곳으로 번창해 가기를 비네.\""]], [["“But you are not going now, Joe?”", "\"하지만 지금 가는 건 아니지, 조?\""]], [["“Yes I am,”", "\"가야지.\""], ["said Joe.", "조가 말했다."]], [["“But you are coming back to dinner, Joe?”", "\"하지만 저녁 식사 때 다시 올 거지, 조?\""]], [["“No I am not,”", "\"아니, 안 와.\""], ["said Joe.", "조가 말했다."]], [["Our eyes met, and all the “Sir” melted out of that manly heart as he gave me his hand.", "우리의 눈이 마주쳤고, 그가 내게 손을 내밀 때 그 사내다운 마음에서 \"도련님\"이라는 말은 죄다 녹아내렸다."]], [["“Pip, dear old chap, life is made of ever so many partings welded together, as I may say, and one man’s a blacksmith, and one’s a whitesmith, and one’s a goldsmith, and one’s a coppersmith.", "\"핍, 정다운 친구, 인생이란, 말하자면, 헤아릴 수 없이 많은 이별이 한데 용접되어 이루어진 것이라네. 그리고 어떤 사람은 대장장이고, 어떤 사람은 양철장이고, 어떤 사람은 금세공장이고, 어떤 사람은 구리세공장이지."], ["Diwisions among such must come, and must be met as they come.", "그런 사람들 사이에 갈라짐이란 으레 오게 마련이고, 오는 대로 맞이해야 하는 거야."], ["If there’s been any fault at all to-day, it’s mine.", "오늘 무슨 잘못이 조금이라도 있었다면, 그건 내 탓이야."], ["You and me is not two figures to be together in London; nor yet anywheres else but what is private, and beknown, and understood among friends.", "자네와 나는 런던에서 함께 어울릴 두 사람이 못 돼. 친구들 사이에서 은밀하고, 잘 알려지고, 이해되는 자리가 아닌 다른 어디에서도 마찬가지고."], ["It ain’t that I am proud, but that I want to be right, as you shall never see me no more in these clothes.", "내가 자존심을 부리는 게 아니라, 그저 바르게 처신하고 싶은 거야. 그러니 이런 옷차림을 한 나는 두 번 다시 못 볼 걸세."], ["I’m wrong in these clothes.", "나는 이런 옷차림이 어울리지 않아."], ["I’m wrong out of the forge, the kitchen, or off th’ meshes.", "대장간이나 부엌이나 습지를 벗어나면 나는 어울리지 않는 사람이야."], ["You won’t find half so much fault in me if you think of me in my forge dress, with my hammer in my hand, or even my pipe.", "자네가 나를 떠올릴 때, 대장간 작업복 차림으로, 손에 망치를 들고, 아니 담뱃대라도 문 모습으로 떠올린다면, 나한테서 절반만큼도 흠을 찾지 못할 걸세."], ["You won’t find half so much fault in me if, supposing as you should ever wish to see me, you come and put your head in at the forge window and see Joe the blacksmith, there, at the old anvil, in the old burnt apron, sticking to the old work.", "행여 언젠가 자네가 나를 보고 싶어진다면, 대장간 창문으로 머리를 들이밀고, 거기 낡은 모루 앞에서, 낡고 그을린 앞치마를 두른 채, 옛 일에 매달려 있는 대장장이 조를 본다고 치면, 자네는 나한테서 절반만큼도 흠을 찾지 못할 걸세."], ["I’m awful dull, but I hope I’ve beat out something nigh the rights of this at last.", "나는 지독히도 둔한 사람이지만, 그래도 마침내 이 일의 옳은 이치에 얼추 가까운 무언가를 두들겨 내긴 한 것 같아 좋네."], ["And so GOD bless you, dear old Pip, old chap, GOD bless you!”", "그러니 신의 가호가 있기를, 정다운 핍, 정다운 친구, 신의 가호가 있기를!\""]], [["I had not been mistaken in my fancy that there was a simple dignity in him.", "그에게 소박한 위엄이 깃들어 있다고 여긴 내 상상은 틀린 것이 아니었다."], ["The fashion of his dress could no more come in its way when he spoke these words than it could come in its way in Heaven.", "그가 이 말을 하는 동안, 그의 옷차림의 모양새는, 천국에서 그러할 수 없는 만큼이나 조금도 그 위엄을 가로막지 못했다."], ["He touched me gently on the forehead, and went out.", "그는 내 이마를 부드럽게 어루만지고는 밖으로 나갔다."], ["As soon as I could recover myself sufficiently, I hurried out after him and looked for him in the neighbouring streets; but he was gone.", "나는 가까스로 정신을 추스르자마자 황급히 그를 뒤따라 나가 이웃 거리들에서 그를 찾았으나, 그는 이미 가 버리고 없었다."]], [["# Chapter XXVIII.", "# Chapter XXVIII."]], [["It was clear that I must repair to our town next day, and in the first flow of my repentance, it was equally clear that I must stay at Joe’s.", "이튿날 우리 고장으로 가야 한다는 것은 분명했고, 처음 솟구쳐 오른 뉘우침의 물결 속에서, 조의 집에 묵어야 한다는 것 또한 마찬가지로 분명했다."], ["But, when I had secured my box-place by to-morrow’s coach, and had been down to Mr. Pocket’s and back, I was not by any means convinced on the last point, and began to invent reasons and make excuses for putting up at the Blue Boar.", "그러나 내일 떠나는 마차의 좌석을 예약하고, 포켓 씨 댁에 다녀온 뒤에는, 마지막 그 점에 대해 결코 확신이 서지 않았고, 블루 보어 여관에 묵을 핑계를 꾸며 대고 이런저런 변명을 지어내기 시작했다. 조의 집에 가면 폐만 끼칠 것이다."], ["I should be an inconvenience at Joe’s; I was not expected, and my bed would not be ready; I should be too far from Miss Havisham’s, and she was exacting and mightn’t like it.", "내가 갈 줄 모르고 있을 테니 침상도 준비되어 있지 않을 것이다. 미스 해비셤의 집과 너무 멀 텐데, 그분은 까다로운 데다 그걸 못마땅해할지도 모른다."], ["All other swindlers upon earth are nothing to the self-swindlers, and with such pretences did I cheat myself.", "이 세상의 그 어떤 사기꾼도 자기 자신을 속이는 사기꾼에 비하면 아무것도 아니며, 나는 그런 핑계들로 나 자신을 속였다."], ["Surely a curious thing.", "참으로 묘한 일이다."], ["That I should innocently take a bad half-crown of somebody else’s manufacture is reasonable enough; but that I should knowingly reckon the spurious coin of my own make as good money!", "내가 남이 만든 가짜 반 크라운 동전을 영문도 모르고 받아 드는 것이야 충분히 그럴 법한 일이지만, 내 손으로 만든 가짜 동전을 알면서도 진짜 돈으로 쳐 주다니!"], ["An obliging stranger, under pretence of compactly folding up my bank-notes for security’s sake, abstracts the notes and gives me nutshells; but what is his sleight of hand to mine, when I fold up my own nutshells and pass them on myself as notes!", "어느 친절한 낯선 사내가 안전을 위해 내 지폐를 차곡차곡 접어 준다는 핑계로 그 지폐를 빼돌리고 내게 호두 껍데기를 쥐여 주는 것이라면, 그자의 손재간 따위가 어찌 내 것에 비하랴. 나는 내 손으로 호두 껍데기를 접어 그것을 지폐랍시고 나 자신에게 슬쩍 떠넘기고 있으니 말이다."]], [["Having settled that I must go to the Blue Boar, my mind was much disturbed by indecision whether or not to take the Avenger.", "블루 보어에 묵어야 한다고 마음을 정하고 나니, 어벤저를 데려가야 할지 말지 결정을 내리지 못해 마음이 몹시 어지러웠다."], ["It was tempting to think of that expensive Mercenary publicly airing his boots in the archway of the Blue Boar’s posting-yard; it was almost solemn to imagine him casually produced in the tailor’s shop, and confounding the disrespectful senses of Trabb’s boy.", "그 값비싼 고용병이 블루 보어 마구간 마당의 아치문 아래에서 자기 장화를 사람들 앞에 떡하니 드러내는 모습을 떠올리는 것은 솔깃한 일이었다. 그를 양복점에 무심한 듯 데려가, 무례한 트래브의 점원 녀석의 분별을 어리둥절하게 만드는 장면을 상상하니 거의 엄숙하기까지 했다."], ["On the other hand, Trabb’s boy might worm himself into his intimacy and tell him things; or, reckless and desperate wretch as I knew he could be, might hoot him in the High Street.", "그러나 다른 한편으로, 트래브의 점원 녀석이 그와 친한 사이로 슬그머니 파고들어 이런저런 이야기를 털어놓게 만들 수도 있고, 혹은 내가 익히 아는 대로 그자가 막무가내에 자포자기한 인간인지라 큰길에서 그를 향해 야유를 퍼부을 수도 있었다."], ["My patroness, too, might hear of him, and not approve.", "게다가 내 후원자께서도 그에 대한 소문을 들으면 못마땅해하실지 몰랐다."], ["On the whole, I resolved to leave the Avenger behind.", "결국 나는 어벤저를 두고 가기로 마음먹었다."]], [["It was the afternoon coach by which I had taken my place, and, as winter had now come round, I should not arrive at my destination until two or three hours after dark.", "내가 좌석을 예약한 것은 오후 마차였고, 이제 겨울이 돌아왔으니 목적지에 닿는 것은 해가 진 뒤 두세 시간은 지나서일 터였다."], ["Our time of starting from the Cross Keys was two o’clock.", "크로스 키스에서 출발하는 시각은 두 시였다."], ["I arrived on the ground with a quarter of an hour to spare, attended by the Avenger,—if I may connect that expression with one who never attended on me if he could possibly help it.", "나는 십오 분쯤 여유를 두고 그곳에 도착했는데, 어벤저를 거느린 채였다 — 어떻게든 피할 수만 있다면 결코 나를 거든 적이 없는 자에게 그런 표현을 갖다 붙여도 된다면 말이다."]], [["At that time it was customary to carry Convicts down to the dock-yards by stage-coach.", "그 시절에는 죄수들을 역마차로 조선소까지 호송하는 것이 관례였다."], ["As I had often heard of them in the capacity of outside passengers, and had more than once seen them on the high road dangling their ironed legs over the coach roof, I had no cause to be surprised when Herbert, meeting me in the yard, came up and told me there were two convicts going down with me.", "나는 죄수들이 마차 바깥쪽 승객으로 실려 가는 이야기를 자주 들었고, 큰길에서 쇠고랑을 찬 다리를 마차 지붕 너머로 늘어뜨리고 있는 죄수들을 한두 번 본 적도 있었던지라, 마당에서 나를 만난 허버트가 다가와 나와 함께 내려갈 죄수가 둘 있다고 말했을 때 놀랄 까닭이 없었다."], ["But I had a reason that was an old reason now for constitutionally faltering whenever I heard the word “convict.”", "그러나 나는 \"죄수\"라는 말을 들을 때마다 체질적으로 움찔하게 만드는, 이제는 오래된 까닭 하나를 품고 있었다."]], [["“You don’t mind them, Handel?”", "\"저자들이 신경 쓰이는 건 아니지, 핸들?\""], ["said Herbert.", "허버트가 말했다."]], [["“O no!”", "\"아, 아니!\""]], [["“I thought you seemed as if you didn’t like them?”", "\"자네가 저자들을 별로 좋아하지 않는 것처럼 보여서 말이야.\""]], [["“I can’t pretend that I do like them, and I suppose you don’t particularly. But I don’t mind them.”", "\"좋아하는 척은 못 하겠고, 자네도 딱히 좋아하진 않겠지. 하지만 신경 쓰이진 않아.\""]], [["“See! There they are,”", "\"저것 봐! 저기 있군.\""], ["said Herbert, “coming out of the Tap.", "허버트가 말했다. \"술집에서 나오고 있어."], ["What a degraded and vile sight it is!”", "얼마나 추하고 천한 광경인지!\""]], [["They had been treating their guard, I suppose, for they had a gaoler with them, and all three came out wiping their mouths on their hands.", "아마 자기네 호송인에게 한턱을 낸 모양이었다. 간수가 하나 딸려 있었고, 셋 모두 손등으로 입을 닦으며 나왔으니 말이다."], ["The two convicts were handcuffed together, and had irons on their legs,—irons of a pattern that I knew well.", "두 죄수는 함께 수갑이 채워져 있었고, 다리에는 쇠고랑이 채워져 있었다 — 내가 잘 아는 모양의 쇠고랑이었다."], ["They wore the dress that I likewise knew well.", "그들은 내가 마찬가지로 잘 아는 옷차림을 하고 있었다."], ["Their keeper had a brace of pistols, and carried a thick-knobbed bludgeon under his arm; but he was on terms of good understanding with them, and stood with them beside him, looking on at the putting-to of the horses, rather with an air as if the convicts were an interesting Exhibition not formally open at the moment, and he the Curator.", "그들의 감시인은 권총 두 자루를 지니고 옆구리에 끝이 두툼한 곤봉을 끼고 있었으나, 죄수들과는 서로 좋게 통하는 사이였고, 그들을 곁에 세워 둔 채 말을 마차에 매는 모습을 지켜보고 있었는데, 그 태도가 마치 죄수들이 당장은 정식으로 공개되지 않은 흥미로운 전시품이고 자신은 그 관리인이라도 되는 듯했다."], ["One was a taller and stouter man than the other, and appeared as a matter of course, according to the mysterious ways of the world, both convict and free, to have had allotted to him the smaller suit of clothes.", "한 사람은 다른 사람보다 키도 크고 몸집도 좋았는데, 죄수든 자유인이든 가리지 않는 이 세상의 알 수 없는 방식에 따라 당연한 일이라는 듯, 더 작은 옷이 그에게 배정되어 있었다."], ["His arms and legs were like great pincushions of those shapes, and his attire disguised him absurdly; but I knew his half-closed eye at one glance.", "그의 팔다리는 그런 모양으로 만든 커다란 바늘꽂이 같았고, 그 옷차림이 그를 우스꽝스럽게 가려 놓았으나, 나는 한눈에 그 반쯤 감긴 눈을 알아보았다."], ["There stood the man whom I had seen on the settle at the Three Jolly Bargemen on a Saturday night, and who had brought me down with his invisible gun!", "어느 토요일 밤 세 명랑한 뱃사공 술집의 긴 의자에 앉아 있던, 그리고 보이지 않는 총으로 나를 쓰러뜨렸던 그 사내가 거기 서 있었던 것이다!"]], [["It was easy to make sure that as yet he knew me no more than if he had never seen me in his life.", "아직까지는 그가, 평생 나를 본 적이 없는 것과 다름없이 나를 알아보지 못한다는 것을 확인하기란 쉬웠다."], ["He looked across at me, and his eye appraised my watch-chain, and then he incidentally spat and said something to the other convict, and they laughed and slued themselves round with a clink of their coupling manacle, and looked at something else.", "그는 나를 건너다보았고, 그의 눈이 내 시곗줄을 가늠하더니, 이윽고 아무렇지 않게 침을 뱉고는 다른 죄수에게 무어라 말했다. 그러자 둘은 웃으며 그들을 잇는 수갑의 쩔그렁 소리와 함께 몸을 휙 돌려 다른 무언가를 바라보았다."], ["The great numbers on their backs, as if they were street doors; their coarse mangy ungainly outer surface, as if they were lower animals; their ironed legs, apologetically garlanded with pocket-handkerchiefs; and the way in which all present looked at them and kept from them; made them (as Herbert had said) a most disagreeable and degraded spectacle.", "등에 박힌 커다란 번호는 마치 그들이 거리의 출입문이라도 되는 듯했고, 거칠고 누추하고 볼품없는 겉모습은 마치 그들이 하등 동물이라도 되는 듯했으며, 쇠고랑 찬 다리는 면구스럽다는 듯 손수건으로 화환처럼 둘러져 있었고, 그 자리에 있는 모든 이가 그들을 바라보면서도 그들에게서 멀찍이 떨어져 있는 모양새는 — (허버트가 말한 대로) 그들을 더없이 불쾌하고 천한 구경거리로 만들었다."]], [["But this was not the worst of it.", "그러나 이것이 최악은 아니었다."], ["It came out that the whole of the back of the coach had been taken by a family removing from London, and that there were no places for the two prisoners but on the seat in front behind the coachman.", "알고 보니 마차 뒤편 좌석은 런던에서 이사 가는 한 가족이 통째로 차지하고 있었고, 두 죄수가 앉을 자리라고는 마부 뒤편 앞쪽 좌석밖에 없었다."], ["Hereupon, a choleric gentleman, who had taken the fourth place on that seat, flew into a most violent passion, and said that it was a breach of contract to mix him up with such villainous company, and that it was poisonous, and pernicious, and infamous, and shameful, and I don’t know what else.", "그러자 그 좌석의 네 번째 자리를 잡고 있던 성마른 신사가 더없이 격렬한 분노에 휩싸여, 자기를 그런 흉악한 무리와 한데 섞는 것은 계약 위반이며, 독스럽고, 해롭고, 파렴치하고, 수치스럽고, 그 밖에 또 무어라 무어라 떠들어 댔다."], ["At this time the coach was ready and the coachman impatient, and we were all preparing to get up, and the prisoners had come over with their keeper,—bringing with them that curious flavour of bread-poultice, baize, rope-yarn, and hearthstone, which attends the convict presence.", "이때 마차는 채비를 마쳤고 마부는 안달이 나 있었으며, 우리 모두 올라타려던 참이었고, 죄수들은 그들의 감시인과 함께 다가왔다 — 죄수의 존재에 따라붙는 그 묘한 빵 찜질약과 무명천과 노끈 부스러기와 화덕돌의 냄새를 풍기면서 말이다."]], [["“Don’t take it so much amiss, sir,”", "\"그렇게 언짢게 여기지 마십시오, 손님.\""], ["pleaded the keeper to the angry passenger; “I’ll sit next you myself.", "감시인이 성난 승객에게 사정했다. \"제가 손님 옆에 앉겠습니다."], ["I’ll put ’em on the outside of the row.", "저자들은 줄 바깥쪽에 앉히지요."], ["They won’t interfere with you, sir.", "손님을 거치적거리게 하지 않을 겁니다."], ["You needn’t know they’re there.”", "저자들이 거기 있다는 것조차 모르셔도 됩니다.\""]], [["“And don’t blame me,” growled the convict I had recognised.", "\"그리고 나를 탓하진 마쇼.\" 내가 알아본 죄수가 으르렁거렸다."], ["“I don’t want to go.", "\"나는 가고 싶지도 않소."], ["I am quite ready to stay behind.", "나야 뒤에 남는 것도 얼마든지 좋고."], ["As fur as I am concerned any one’s welcome to my place.”", "나로 말하자면, 누구든 내 자리에 앉으라고 하시오.\""]], [["“Or mine,” said the other, gruffly.", "\"내 자리도 마찬가지요.\" 다른 죄수가 퉁명스럽게 말했다."], ["“I wouldn’t have incommoded none of you, if I’d had my way.”", "\"내 뜻대로 됐다면 댁들 누구한테도 폐 끼치지 않았을 거요.\""], ["Then they both laughed, and began cracking nuts, and spitting the shells about.—As I really think I should have liked to do myself, if I had been in their place and so despised.", "그러더니 둘은 함께 웃으며 호두를 깨물어 까서는 껍데기를 여기저기 뱉어 댔다. — 내가 그들의 처지가 되어 그렇게 멸시를 받았더라면 나 역시 정말이지 그렇게 하고 싶었으리라는 생각이 든다."]], [["At length, it was voted that there was no help for the angry gentleman, and that he must either go in his chance company or remain behind.", "마침내 성난 신사에게는 달리 도리가 없으니, 우연히 함께하게 된 일행과 같이 가든지 아니면 뒤에 남든지 해야 한다는 쪽으로 의견이 모였다."], ["So he got into his place, still making complaints, and the keeper got into the place next him, and the convicts hauled themselves up as well as they could, and the convict I had recognised sat behind me with his breath on the hair of my head.", "그리하여 그는 여전히 불평을 늘어놓으며 제자리에 올라탔고, 감시인은 그 옆자리에 올라탔으며, 죄수들은 할 수 있는 대로 끙끙대며 기어올랐고, 내가 알아본 그 죄수는 내 머리카락에 숨결이 닿을 만큼 바로 내 뒤에 앉았다."]], [["“Good-bye, Handel!”", "\"잘 가게, 핸들!\""], ["Herbert called out as we started.", "마차가 출발할 때 허버트가 소리쳐 불렀다."], ["I thought what a blessed fortune it was, that he had found another name for me than Pip.", "그가 핍이 아닌 다른 이름을 내게 붙여 준 것이 얼마나 다행한 행운인가 싶었다."]], [["It is impossible to express with what acuteness I felt the convict’s breathing, not only on the back of my head, but all along my spine.", "그 죄수의 숨결이 내 뒤통수뿐 아니라 척추를 따라 죽 느껴지던 그 예민함은 도무지 말로 표현할 수 없을 정도였다."], ["The sensation was like being touched in the marrow with some pungent and searching acid, it set my very teeth on edge.", "그 감각은 마치 어떤 매캐하고 파고드는 산이 골수에 닿은 것 같았고, 그야말로 이가 시릴 지경이었다."], ["He seemed to have more breathing business to do than another man, and to make more noise in doing it; and I was conscious of growing high-shouldered on one side, in my shrinking endeavours to fend him off.", "그는 다른 사람보다 호흡할 일이 더 많은 듯했고, 호흡하면서 더 많은 소리를 내는 듯했다. 나는 그를 막아 내려고 한쪽으로 움츠리느라 어깨가 한쪽만 솟아오르고 있음을 의식했다."]], [["The weather was miserably raw, and the two cursed the cold.", "날씨는 비참할 만큼 으슬으슬했고, 두 사람은 추위를 저주했다."], ["It made us all lethargic before we had gone far, and when we had left the Half-way House behind, we habitually dozed and shivered and were silent.", "얼마 가지 않아 우리 모두 나른해졌고, 중간 역참을 지나친 뒤에는 으레 졸다가 떨다가 말이 없어지곤 했다."], ["I dozed off, myself, in considering the question whether I ought to restore a couple of pounds sterling to this creature before losing sight of him, and how it could best be done.", "나 또한 이 인간이 시야에서 사라지기 전에 2파운드를 돌려주어야 할지, 또 그것을 어떻게 하는 것이 가장 좋을지 하는 문제를 곰곰이 생각하다가 깜빡 잠이 들었다."], ["In the act of dipping forward as if I were going to bathe among the horses, I woke in a fright and took the question up again.", "마치 말들 사이에서 멱이라도 감으려는 듯 앞으로 고꾸라지는 순간, 나는 화들짝 놀라 깨어나 그 문제를 다시 붙들었다."]], [["But I must have lost it longer than I had thought, since, although I could recognise nothing in the darkness and the fitful lights and shadows of our lamps, I traced marsh country in the cold damp wind that blew at us.", "그러나 생각보다 더 오래 잠들었던 모양이다. 어둠 속에서, 그리고 우리 마차 등불의 가물거리는 빛과 그림자 속에서 아무것도 알아볼 수 없었음에도, 우리에게 불어오는 차고 축축한 바람 속에서 습지대임을 감지했으니 말이다."], ["Cowering forward for warmth and to make me a screen against the wind, the convicts were closer to me than before.", "온기를 찾아, 또 바람을 막는 가림막으로 나를 삼으려 앞으로 잔뜩 웅크린 죄수들은 전보다 더 내게 바싹 붙어 있었다."], ["The very first words I heard them interchange as I became conscious, were the words of my own thought, “Two One Pound notes.”", "의식이 돌아오면서 그들이 주고받는 첫 말로 내가 들은 것은, 바로 내 생각 속의 그 말이었다. \"1파운드짜리 지폐 두 장.\""]], [["“How did he get ’em?”", "\"그자가 그걸 어떻게 구했답니까?\""], ["said the convict I had never seen.", "내가 한 번도 본 적 없는 죄수가 말했다."]], [["“How should I know?”", "\"낸들 어찌 알겠나?\""], ["returned the other.", "다른 죄수가 대꾸했다."], ["“He had ’em stowed away somehows.", "\"어떻게 해서든 챙겨 뒀던 게지."], ["Giv him by friends, I expect.”", "친구들이 줬겠지, 아마.\""]], [["“I wish,”", "\"내가 말이지,\""], ["said the other, with a bitter curse upon the cold, “that I had ’em here.”", "다른 죄수가 추위에 대고 쓰디쓴 욕설을 퍼부으며 말했다. \"그걸 여기 가지고 있다면 좋겠소.\""]], [["“Two one pound notes, or friends?”", "\"1파운드짜리 지폐 두 장이오, 아니면 친구들이오?\""]], [["“Two one pound notes.", "\"1파운드짜리 지폐 두 장 말이오."], ["I’d sell all the friends I ever had for one, and think it a blessed good bargain. Well? So he says—?”", "나라면 평생 사귄 친구를 죄다 팔아서라도 한 장 마련하고, 그걸 더없이 잘한 흥정이라 여기겠소. 그래서? 그자가 뭐라고 했다고?\""]], [["“So he says,”", "\"그래서 그자 말이,\""], ["resumed the convict I had recognised,—“it was all said and done in half a minute, behind a pile of timber in the Dock-yard,—‘You’re a-going to be discharged?’ Yes, I was.", "내가 알아본 죄수가 다시 입을 열었다. \"조선소의 목재 더미 뒤에서 채 반 분도 안 되는 사이에 다 말하고 끝낸 일인데 — '이제 풀려나는 거요?' 그래, 그랬지."], ["Would I find out that boy that had fed him and kep his secret, and give him them two one pound notes?", "그자한테 먹을 걸 가져다주고 비밀을 지켜 준 그 아이를 찾아내서, 그 1파운드짜리 두 장을 전해 줄 텐가? 그래, 그러겠다고 했지."], ["Yes, I would. And I did.”", "그래서 그렇게 했고.\""]], [["“More fool you,”", "\"멍청하기 짝이 없군.\""], ["growled the other.", "다른 죄수가 으르렁거렸다."], ["“I’d have spent ’em on a Man, in wittles and drink.", "\"나 같으면 그걸 어른한테, 먹을거리랑 마실거리에 썼을 거요."], ["He must have been a green one.", "그 아이는 풋내기였던 게 분명해."], ["Mean to say he knowed nothing of you?”", "그러니까 댁에 대해선 아무것도 몰랐단 말이지?\""]], [["“Not a ha’porth. Different gangs and different ships.", "\"털끝만큼도 몰랐어. 다른 무리, 다른 배였으니까."], ["He was tried again for prison breaking, and got made a Lifer.”", "그자는 탈옥죄로 다시 재판을 받아 종신형이 됐지.\""]], [["“And was that—Honour!—the only time you worked out, in this part of the country?”", "\"그래서 그게 — 솔직히 말해서! — 이 지방에서 자네가 형기를 산 유일한 때였나?\""]], [["“The only time.”", "\"유일한 때였지.\""]], [["“What might have been your opinion of the place?”", "\"그곳에 대한 자네 생각은 어땠겠나?\""]], [["“A most beastly place.", "\"더없이 끔찍한 곳이지."], ["Mudbank, mist, swamp, and work; work, swamp, mist, and mudbank.”", "진흙 둑, 안개, 늪, 그리고 노역. 노역, 늪, 안개, 그리고 진흙 둑.\""]], [["They both execrated the place in very strong language, and gradually growled themselves out, and had nothing left to say.", "둘은 아주 거센 말로 그곳을 저주하더니, 차츰 으르렁대다 잦아들어, 더는 할 말이 없었다."]], [["After overhearing this dialogue, I should assuredly have got down and been left in the solitude and darkness of the highway, but for feeling certain that the man had no suspicion of my identity.", "이 대화를 엿들은 뒤, 그 사내가 내 정체를 조금도 의심하지 않는다고 확신하지 못했더라면 나는 분명 마차에서 내려 큰길의 고독과 어둠 속에 남겨졌을 것이다."], ["Indeed, I was not only so changed in the course of nature, but so differently dressed and so differently circumstanced, that it was not at all likely he could have known me without accidental help.", "사실 나는 세월의 흐름에 따라 변했을 뿐만 아니라, 옷차림도 처지도 너무나 달라져 있어, 우연한 도움 없이는 그가 나를 알아볼 가능성이 거의 없었다."], ["Still, the coincidence of our being together on the coach, was sufficiently strange to fill me with a dread that some other coincidence might at any moment connect me, in his hearing, with my name.", "그럼에도 우리가 같은 마차에 함께 타게 된 우연은 충분히 기이한 것이어서, 어느 순간 또 다른 우연이 그가 듣는 데서 나를 내 이름과 연결지을지도 모른다는 두려움이 나를 가득 채웠다."], ["For this reason, I resolved to alight as soon as we touched the town, and put myself out of his hearing.", "그런 까닭에 나는 마차가 고장에 닿는 즉시 내려서, 그가 들을 수 없는 곳으로 몸을 빼기로 마음먹었다."], ["This device I executed successfully.", "이 책략을 나는 성공적으로 실행에 옮겼다."], ["My little portmanteau was in the boot under my feet; I had but to turn a hinge to get it out; I threw it down before me, got down after it, and was left at the first lamp on the first stones of the town pavement.", "내 작은 여행 가방은 발밑 짐칸에 있었으니, 경첩 하나만 돌리면 꺼낼 수 있었다. 나는 그것을 앞으로 던져 놓고 뒤따라 내려서서, 고장 포도의 첫 돌바닥 위, 첫 가로등 곁에 남겨졌다."], ["As to the convicts, they went their way with the coach, and I knew at what point they would be spirited off to the river.", "죄수들로 말하자면, 그들은 마차와 함께 제 갈 길을 갔고, 나는 어느 지점에서 그들이 강으로 빼돌려질지 알고 있었다."], ["In my fancy, I saw the boat with its convict crew waiting for them at the slime-washed stairs,—again heard the gruff “Give way, you!”", "내 상상 속에서, 나는 점액으로 미끈거리는 계단가에서 죄수 선원들을 태운 채 그들을 기다리는 배를 보았고 — 마치 개에게 던지는 명령 같은 그 퉁명스러운 \"저어라, 이놈들!\""], ["like an order to dogs,—again saw the wicked Noah’s Ark lying out on the black water.", "하는 소리를 다시 들었으며 — 검은 물 위에 떠 있는 그 사악한 노아의 방주를 다시 보았다."]], [["I could not have said what I was afraid of, for my fear was altogether undefined and vague, but there was great fear upon me.", "내가 무엇을 두려워하는지는 말할 수 없었으니, 내 두려움은 전적으로 막연하고 어렴풋했으나, 큰 두려움이 나를 짓누르고 있었다."], ["As I walked on to the hotel, I felt that a dread, much exceeding the mere apprehension of a painful or disagreeable recognition, made me tremble.", "호텔을 향해 걸어가면서, 단지 괴롭거나 불쾌한 신원 확인에 대한 우려를 훨씬 넘어서는 어떤 공포가 나를 떨게 한다는 것을 느꼈다."], ["I am confident that it took no distinctness of shape, and that it was the revival for a few minutes of the terror of childhood.", "나는 확신하건대, 그것은 어떤 또렷한 형체를 띠지 않았으며, 어린 시절의 그 공포가 몇 분간 되살아난 것이었다."]], [["The coffee-room at the Blue Boar was empty, and I had not only ordered my dinner there, but had sat down to it, before the waiter knew me.", "블루 보어의 커피 룸은 비어 있었고, 나는 거기서 저녁을 주문했을 뿐 아니라 자리에 앉아 식사를 하고 있었는데, 그때까지도 종업원은 나를 알아보지 못했다."], ["As soon as he had apologised for the remissness of his memory, he asked me if he should send Boots for Mr. Pumblechook?", "그는 자기 기억이 무딘 것을 사과하자마자, 펌블추크 씨에게 사람을 보낼까요, 하고 내게 물었다."]], [["“No,”", "\"아니오.\""], ["said I, “certainly not.”", "내가 말했다. \"절대 그러지 마시오.\""]], [["The waiter (it was he who had brought up the Great Remonstrance from the Commercials, on the day when I was bound) appeared surprised, and took the earliest opportunity of putting a dirty old copy of a local newspaper so directly in my way, that I took it up and read this paragraph:—", "그 종업원은(내가 도제로 묶이던 날 외판원들의 그 거창한 항의를 위층으로 올려 보냈던 바로 그자였다) 놀란 듯하더니, 더러운 지역 신문 한 부를 내가 보지 않을 수 없도록 곧장 내 앞에 들이미는 기회를 냉큼 잡았고, 그래서 나는 그것을 집어 들고 이런 구절을 읽었다."]], [["Our readers will learn, not altogether without interest, in reference to the recent romantic rise in fortune of a young artificer in iron of this neighbourhood (what a theme, by the way, for the magic pen of our as yet not universally acknowledged townsman TOOBY, the poet of our columns!) that the youth’s earliest patron, companion, and friend, was a highly respected individual not entirely unconnected with the corn and seed trade, and whose eminently convenient and commodious business premises are situate within a hundred miles of the High Street.", "본지 독자들께서는, 이 고장의 한 젊은 철공이 최근에 낭만적으로 운수가 트인 일과 관련하여(말이 나온 김에, 아직 널리 인정받지는 못했으나 본지 지면의 시인인 우리 고장 사람 투비의 마법 같은 붓에 얼마나 어울리는 주제인가!) 적잖은 흥미를 가지고 알게 되실 것이다. 그 젊은이의 가장 이른 후원자요, 동료요, 벗이었던 분은 곡물 및 종자 거래와 전혀 무관하지 않은 매우 존경받는 인사였으며, 그의 더없이 편리하고 널찍한 영업장은 큰길에서 백 마일 이내에 자리하고 있다는 것을. 우리가 이분을 우리 젊은 텔레마코스의 멘토로 기록하는 것은 우리의 사사로운 감정과 전혀 무관하지 않으니, 후자의 부의 기틀을 닦은 이를 우리 고장이 배출했다는 사실을 아는 것은 좋은 일인 까닭이다."], ["It is not wholly irrespective of our personal feelings that we record HIM as the Mentor of our young Telemachus, for it is good to know that our town produced the founder of the latter’s fortunes.", "이 고장 현인의 사색으로 찌푸려진 이마나 이 고장 미인의 빛나는 눈은 누구의 부를 말하느냐고 물을 것인가? 우리가 알기로 퀜틴 마시스는 안트베르펜의 대장장이였다."], ["Does the thought-contracted brow of the local Sage or the lustrous eye of local Beauty inquire whose fortunes?", "현명한 자에게는 한마디면 족하다. * 텔레마코스: 그리스 신화에서 오디세우스의 아들로, 그의 스승이자 인도자가 멘토였다."], ["We believe that Quintin Matsys was the BLACKSMITH of Antwerp. VERB. SAP.", "* 퀜틴 마시스: 본래 대장장이였다가 화가가 되었다고 전해지는 16세기 플랑드르의 화가."]], [["I entertain a conviction, based upon large experience, that if in the days of my prosperity I had gone to the North Pole, I should have met somebody there, wandering Esquimaux or civilized man, who would have told me that Pumblechook was my earliest patron and the founder of my fortunes.", "나는 풍부한 경험에 근거하여, 만약 내가 영화를 누리던 시절에 북극에 갔더라면, 거기서도 누군가를, 떠도는 에스키모든 문명인이든 누군가를 만났을 것이고, 그자가 내게 펌블추크가 나의 가장 이른 후원자이며 내 부의 기틀을 닦은 이라고 일러 주었으리라는 확신을 품고 있다."]], [["# Chapter XXIX.", "# Chapter XXIX."]], [["Betimes in the morning I was up and out.", "아침 일찍 나는 일어나 밖으로 나섰다."], ["It was too early yet to go to Miss Havisham’s, so I loitered into the country on Miss Havisham’s side of town,—which was not Joe’s side; I could go there to-morrow,—thinking about my patroness, and painting brilliant pictures of her plans for me.", "미스 해비셤을 찾아가기에는 아직 너무 일렀으므로, 나는 마을에서 미스 해비셤 쪽에 해당하는 — 조가 사는 쪽은 아니었으니, 그쪽은 내일 가면 될 일이었다 — 시골로 어슬렁어슬렁 걸어 나가, 내 후원자에 대해 생각하며 그분이 나를 위해 세운 계획의 찬란한 그림들을 그려 보았다."]], [["She had adopted Estella, she had as good as adopted me, and it could not fail to be her intention to bring us together.", "그분은 에스텔라를 양녀로 들였고, 나 역시 양자나 다름없이 거두어 주었으니, 우리 둘을 맺어 주려는 것이 그분의 의도임은 의심할 여지가 없었다."], ["She reserved it for me to restore the desolate house, admit the sunshine into the dark rooms, set the clocks a-going and the cold hearths a-blazing, tear down the cobwebs, destroy the vermin,—in short, do all the shining deeds of the young Knight of romance, and marry the Princess.", "그분은 이 황폐한 집을 되살리는 일, 어두운 방마다 햇살을 들이는 일, 멈춘 시계들을 다시 돌게 하고 차가운 화로마다 불길을 일으키는 일, 거미줄을 걷어 내고 해충을 박멸하는 일 — 요컨대 로맨스 속 젊은 기사가 행하는 온갖 빛나는 위업을 다 이루고 마침내 공주와 결혼하는 일을 나를 위해 남겨 둔 것이었다."], ["I had stopped to look at the house as I passed; and its seared red brick walls, blocked windows, and strong green ivy clasping even the stacks of chimneys with its twigs and tendons, as if with sinewy old arms, had made up a rich attractive mystery, of which I was the hero.", "나는 지나는 길에 걸음을 멈추고 그 집을 바라보았다. 검게 그을린 붉은 벽돌담, 막혀 버린 창문들, 그리고 잔가지와 덩굴손으로 굴뚝 더미마저 마치 억센 노인의 팔처럼 휘감고 있는 무성한 푸른 담쟁이가 어우러져, 내가 주인공인 풍요롭고 매혹적인 신비를 빚어내고 있었다."], ["Estella was the inspiration of it, and the heart of it, of course.", "물론 에스텔라가 그 신비의 영감이요, 그 심장이었다."], ["But, though she had taken such strong possession of me, though my fancy and my hope were so set upon her, though her influence on my boyish life and character had been all-powerful, I did not, even that romantic morning, invest her with any attributes save those she possessed.", "그러나 그녀가 나를 그토록 강하게 사로잡고 있었음에도, 내 공상과 희망이 온통 그녀에게 쏠려 있었음에도, 그녀가 내 소년 시절의 삶과 성격에 미친 영향이 절대적이었음에도, 나는 그 낭만적인 아침에조차 그녀가 실제로 지닌 것 외의 어떤 자질도 그녀에게 부여하지 않았다."], ["I mention this in this place, of a fixed purpose, because it is the clue by which I am to be followed into my poor labyrinth.", "내가 이 자리에서 굳이 이 점을 언급하는 것은, 이것이 나라는 사람을 따라 나의 가련한 미궁 속으로 들어가게 해 주는 실마리이기 때문이다."], ["According to my experience, the conventional notion of a lover cannot be always true.", "내 경험에 비추어 보건대, 연인에 대한 통념이 늘 참일 수는 없다."], ["The unqualified truth is, that when I loved Estella with the love of a man, I loved her simply because I found her irresistible.", "가식 없는 진실은, 내가 한 남자의 사랑으로 에스텔라를 사랑했을 때, 그저 그녀가 거역할 수 없는 존재였기에 사랑했다는 것이다. 단번에 말해 두자."], ["Once for all; I knew to my sorrow, often and often, if not always, that I loved her against reason, against promise, against peace, against hope, against happiness, against all discouragement that could be.", "나는 슬프게도 자주, 늘은 아닐지언정 거듭거듭 알고 있었다. 내가 이성을 거슬러, 약속을 거슬러, 평온을 거슬러, 희망을 거슬러, 행복을 거슬러, 있을 수 있는 온갖 단념의 이유를 거슬러 그녀를 사랑한다는 것을. 단번에 말해 두자."], ["Once for all; I loved her none the less because I knew it, and it had no more influence in restraining me than if I had devoutly believed her to be human perfection.", "그것을 알면서도 나는 조금도 덜 사랑하지 않았으니, 그 앎은 나를 억누르는 데에 아무런 힘도 발휘하지 못했고, 그것은 내가 그녀를 인간적 완벽 그 자체라고 경건히 믿었던 경우와 다를 바 없었다."]], [["I so shaped out my walk as to arrive at the gate at my old time.", "나는 옛 시각에 맞추어 대문에 닿도록 걸음을 조절했다."], ["When I had rung at the bell with an unsteady hand, I turned my back upon the gate, while I tried to get my breath and keep the beating of my heart moderately quiet.", "떨리는 손으로 종을 울린 뒤, 나는 대문에 등을 돌린 채 숨을 고르고 두근거리는 가슴을 어지간히 가라앉히려 애썼다."], ["I heard the side-door open, and steps come across the courtyard; but I pretended not to hear, even when the gate swung on its rusty hinges.", "옆문이 열리고 안마당을 가로질러 다가오는 발소리가 들렸으나, 녹슨 경첩에서 대문이 삐걱이며 열렸을 때조차 나는 못 들은 척했다."]], [["Being at last touched on the shoulder, I started and turned.", "마침내 어깨에 손길이 닿자, 나는 흠칫 놀라 돌아섰다."], ["I started much more naturally then, to find myself confronted by a man in a sober grey dress.", "그러고는 한결 더 자연스럽게 흠칫했으니, 칙칙한 잿빛 옷을 입은 한 사내와 마주하게 된 까닭이었다."], ["The last man I should have expected to see in that place of porter at Miss Havisham’s door.", "미스 해비셤의 문 앞에서 문지기 노릇을 하고 있으리라고는 가장 상상하기 어려운 사내였다."]], [["“Orlick!”", "\"올릭!\""]], [["“Ah, young master, there’s more changes than yours.", "\"아, 도련님, 변한 게 도련님만은 아니지요."], ["But come in, come in.", "어서 들어오세요, 어서."], ["It’s opposed to my orders to hold the gate open.”", "대문을 열어 둔 채로 두는 건 제 규칙에 어긋나니까요.\""]], [["I entered and he swung it, and locked it, and took the key out. “Yes!”", "내가 들어서자 그는 대문을 닫고 잠근 뒤 열쇠를 빼냈다. \"그래요!\""], ["said he, facing round, after doggedly preceding me a few steps towards the house. “Here I am!”", "그는 집 쪽으로 몇 걸음 무뚝뚝하게 앞장서다가 홱 돌아서며 말했다. \"내가 여기 있는 거요!\""]], [["“How did you come here?”", "\"어쩌다 여기 오게 됐죠?\""]], [["“I come here,”", "\"여기 온 거요.\""], ["he retorted, “on my legs.", "그가 쏘아붙였다. \"내 발로 왔지."], ["I had my box brought alongside me in a barrow.”", "짐 상자는 손수레에 실어 옆에 끼고 왔고.\""]], [["“Are you here for good?”", "\"아주 눌러앉을 작정인가요?\""]], [["“I ain’t here for harm, young master, I suppose?”", "\"해를 끼치려고 온 건 아닐 텐데요, 도련님, 안 그렇소?\""]], [["I was not so sure of that.", "그것만은 확신할 수 없었다."], ["I had leisure to entertain the retort in my mind, while he slowly lifted his heavy glance from the pavement, up my legs and arms, to my face.", "그가 보도에서부터 내 다리와 팔을 거쳐 얼굴에 이르기까지 그 무거운 시선을 천천히 들어 올리는 동안, 나는 그 응수를 마음속으로 곱씹어 볼 여유가 있었다."]], [["“Then you have left the forge?” I said.", "\"그럼 대장간을 그만뒀군요?\" 내가 말했다."]], [["“Do this look like a forge?”", "\"이게 대장간으로 보이쇼?\""], ["replied Orlick, sending his glance all round him with an air of injury.", "올릭이 모욕이라도 당한 듯한 기색으로 시선을 사방으로 휘둘러 보내며 대꾸했다."], ["“Now, do it look like it?”", "\"자, 이게 대장간 같소?\""]], [["I asked him how long he had left Gargery’s forge?", "나는 가저리네 대장간을 떠난 지 얼마나 됐느냐고 물었다."]], [["“One day is so like another here,”", "\"여기선 하루하루가 어찌나 똑같은지.\""], ["he replied, “that I don’t know without casting it up.", "그가 대답했다. \"헤아려 보지 않고선 모르겠소."], ["However, I come here some time since you left.”", "어쨌든 도련님이 떠난 뒤로 얼마쯤 지나서 왔지요.\""]], [["“I could have told you that, Orlick.”", "\"그쯤은 내가 알려 줄 수도 있었어요, 올릭.\""]], [["“Ah!”", "\"아!\""], ["said he, dryly.", "그가 무뚝뚝하게 말했다."], ["“But then you’ve got to be a scholar.”", "\"하긴 도련님은 이제 학식 있는 분이 되셨으니.\""]], [["By this time we had come to the house, where I found his room to be one just within the side-door, with a little window in it looking on the courtyard.", "이쯤에서 우리는 집에 이르렀는데, 그의 방은 옆문 바로 안쪽에 있는, 안마당이 내다보이는 작은 창이 달린 방이었다."], ["In its small proportions, it was not unlike the kind of place usually assigned to a gate-porter in Paris.", "그 비좁은 규모는 파리에서 문지기에게 으레 내주는 그런 공간과 다를 바 없었다."], ["Certain keys were hanging on the wall, to which he now added the gate key; and his patchwork-covered bed was in a little inner division or recess.", "벽에는 열쇠 몇 개가 걸려 있었고, 그는 이제 거기에 대문 열쇠를 보탰다. 헝겊을 이어 붙인 이불이 덮인 침대는 안쪽으로 칸막이가 진 작은 우묵한 자리에 놓여 있었다."], ["The whole had a slovenly, confined, and sleepy look, like a cage for a human dormouse; while he, looming dark and heavy in the shadow of a corner by the window, looked like the human dormouse for whom it was fitted up,—as indeed he was.", "그 전체가 너저분하고 답답하고 졸음에 겨운 모양새여서, 마치 사람 겨울잠쥐를 가두는 우리 같았다. 한편 창가 모퉁이의 그늘 속에 검고 육중하게 어른거리는 그는, 그 우리에 꼭 맞게 마련된 사람 겨울잠쥐처럼 보였으니 — 사실 그가 바로 그러한 존재였다."]], [["“I never saw this room before,”", "\"이 방은 전에 본 적이 없네요.\""], ["I remarked; “but there used to be no Porter here.”", "내가 말했다. \"하지만 여긴 문지기란 게 없었잖아요.\""]], [["“No,”", "\"그랬지요.\""], ["said he; “not till it got about that there was no protection on the premises, and it come to be considered dangerous, with convicts and Tag and Rag and Bobtail going up and down.", "그가 말했다. \"이 집에 아무 보호 장치가 없다는 소문이 돌고, 죄수에다 온갖 부랑배 잡것들이 들락날락하니 위험하다고들 여기게 되기 전까지는요."], ["And then I was recommended to the place as a man who could give another man as good as he brought, and I took it.", "그래서 상대가 주는 만큼 그대로 갚아 줄 수 있는 사람으로 이 자리에 추천을 받았고, 나는 그걸 맡았소."], ["It’s easier than bellowsing and hammering.—That’s loaded, that is.”", "풀무질하고 망치질하는 것보단 수월하니까. — 저건 장전돼 있는 거요, 저건.\""]], [["My eye had been caught by a gun with a brass-bound stock over the chimney-piece, and his eye had followed mine.", "내 눈은 벽난로 위에 놓인, 개머리판을 놋쇠로 두른 총에 가 닿아 있었고, 그의 눈도 내 시선을 따라온 것이었다."]], [["“Well,”", "\"그럼.\""], ["said I, not desirous of more conversation, “shall I go up to Miss Havisham?”", "더는 대화를 나누고 싶지 않아 내가 말했다. \"미스 해비셤께 올라가 볼까요?\""]], [["“Burn me, if I know!”", "\"내가 그걸 알면 벼락을 맞지!\""], ["he retorted, first stretching himself and then shaking himself; “my orders ends here, young master.", "그가 먼저 기지개를 켜고 나서 몸을 한 번 부르르 흔들며 응수했다. \"내 임무는 여기까지요, 도련님."], ["I give this here bell a rap with this here hammer, and you go on along the passage till you meet somebody.”", "내가 이 망치로 이 종을 한 번 두드리면, 도련님은 누구든 만날 때까지 복도를 따라가면 되는 거지.\""]], [["“I am expected, I believe?”", "\"내가 올 줄 알고 계실 텐데요?\""]], [["“Burn me twice over, if I can say!”", "\"내가 그걸 말할 수 있으면 벼락을 두 번 맞지!\""], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."]], [["Upon that, I turned down the long passage which I had first trodden in my thick boots, and he made his bell sound.", "그러자 나는, 옛날 두툼한 부츠를 신고 처음 밟았던 그 긴 복도로 접어들었고, 그는 종을 울렸다."], ["At the end of the passage, while the bell was still reverberating, I found Sarah Pocket, who appeared to have now become constitutionally green and yellow by reason of me.", "종소리가 아직 울려 퍼지는 동안 복도 끝에서 나는 세라 포켓을 만났는데, 그녀는 이제 나로 인해 체질적으로 푸르뎅뎅하고 누리끼리해진 듯 보였다."]], [["“Oh!”", "\"오!\""], ["said she.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["“You, is it, Mr. Pip?”", "\"당신이로군요, 핍 도련님?\""]], [["“It is, Miss Pocket.", "\"그렇습니다, 미스 포켓."], ["I am glad to tell you that Mr. Pocket and family are all well.”", "포켓 씨와 가족분들 모두 안녕히 지내신다는 소식을 전하게 되어 기쁩니다.\""]], [["“Are they any wiser?”", "\"좀 더 철이 들었나요?\""], ["said Sarah, with a dismal shake of the head; “they had better be wiser, than well.", "세라가 침울하게 고개를 가로저으며 말했다. \"안녕한 것보다는 철이 드는 편이 나을 텐데."], ["Ah, Matthew, Matthew!", "아, 매슈, 매슈!"], ["You know your way, sir?”", "길은 아시지요, 도련님?\""]], [["Tolerably, for I had gone up the staircase in the dark, many a time.", "웬만큼은 알았다. 어둠 속에서 그 계단을 오른 적이 한두 번이 아니었으니까."], ["I ascended it now, in lighter boots than of yore, and tapped in my old way at the door of Miss Havisham’s room. “Pip’s rap,”", "나는 이제 옛날보다 가벼운 부츠를 신고 그 계단을 올라, 미스 해비셤의 방문을 예전 방식대로 두드렸다. \"핍이 두드리는 소리로구나.\""], ["I heard her say, immediately; “come in, Pip.”", "그녀가 곧바로 말하는 것이 들렸다. \"들어오너라, 핍.\""]], [["She was in her chair near the old table, in the old dress, with her two hands crossed on her stick, her chin resting on them, and her eyes on the fire.", "그녀는 옛 탁자 곁의 의자에, 옛 드레스를 입고 앉아 있었으니, 두 손을 지팡이 위에 포개고 그 위에 턱을 괴고서 두 눈은 불을 응시하고 있었다."], ["Sitting near her, with the white shoe, that had never been worn, in her hand, and her head bent as she looked at it, was an elegant lady whom I had never seen.", "그녀 곁에는, 한 번도 신은 적 없는 그 흰 구두를 손에 들고 고개를 숙여 그것을 들여다보고 있는, 내가 한 번도 본 적 없는 우아한 여인이 앉아 있었다."]], [["“Come in, Pip,”", "\"들어오너라, 핍.\""], ["Miss Havisham continued to mutter, without looking round or up; “come in, Pip, how do you do, Pip?", "미스 해비셤은 돌아보거나 고개를 들지도 않은 채 계속 중얼거렸다. \"들어오너라, 핍, 잘 지냈느냐, 핍?"], ["so you kiss my hand as if I were a queen, eh?—Well?”", "내가 여왕이라도 되는 듯 내 손에 입을 맞추는구나, 응? — 어떠냐?\""]], [["She looked up at me suddenly, only moving her eyes, and repeated in a grimly playful manner,—", "그녀가 두 눈만 움직여 불쑥 나를 올려다보더니, 음산하면서도 장난기 어린 투로 되풀이했다."]], [["“Well?”", "\"어떠냐?\""]], [["“I heard, Miss Havisham,”", "\"제가 듣기로는, 미스 해비셤,\""], ["said I, rather at a loss, “that you were so kind as to wish me to come and see you, and I came directly.”", "나는 다소 당황하여 말했다. \"찾아와 뵙기를 친절히도 바라신다기에, 곧장 달려왔습니다.\""]], [["“Well?”", "\"어떠냐?\""]], [["The lady whom I had never seen before, lifted up her eyes and looked archly at me, and then I saw that the eyes were Estella’s eyes.", "내가 전에 본 적 없는 그 여인이 두 눈을 들어 짓궂게 나를 바라보았고, 그제야 나는 그 두 눈이 에스텔라의 눈이라는 것을 알았다."], ["But she was so much changed, was so much more beautiful, so much more womanly, in all things winning admiration, had made such wonderful advance, that I seemed to have made none.", "그러나 그녀는 어찌나 변했는지, 어찌나 더 아름답고, 어찌나 더 여인다워졌으며, 모든 면에서 감탄을 자아내고, 어찌나 놀랍게 성장했는지, 나는 도무지 한 발짝도 나아가지 못한 것만 같았다."], ["I fancied, as I looked at her, that I slipped hopelessly back into the coarse and common boy again.", "그녀를 바라보고 있자니, 나는 다시 거칠고 천한 소년으로 가망 없이 미끄러져 돌아가는 듯했다."], ["O the sense of distance and disparity that came upon me, and the inaccessibility that came about her!", "아, 내게 밀려온 그 거리감과 격차의 느낌, 그리고 그녀를 에워싼 그 범접할 수 없음이라니!"]], [["She gave me her hand. I stammered something about the pleasure I felt in seeing her again, and about my having looked forward to it, for a long, long time.", "그녀가 내게 손을 내밀었다. 나는 다시 그녀를 보게 되어 기쁘다는 것, 오래오래 이날을 고대해 왔다는 것에 대해 무언가를 더듬더듬 말했다."]], [["“Do you find her much changed, Pip?”", "\"많이 변한 것 같으냐, 핍?\""], ["asked Miss Havisham, with her greedy look, and striking her stick upon a chair that stood between them, as a sign to me to sit down there.", "미스 해비셤이 탐욕스러운 눈빛으로 물으며, 나더러 거기 앉으라는 신호로 두 사람 사이에 놓인 의자를 지팡이로 톡톡 쳤다."]], [["“When I came in, Miss Havisham, I thought there was nothing of Estella in the face or figure; but now it all settles down so curiously into the old—”", "\"들어왔을 때는, 미스 해비셤, 얼굴이며 자태에서 에스텔라의 모습이라곤 전혀 찾을 수 없다고 생각했습니다. 하지만 지금은 모든 것이 묘하게도 옛—\""]], [["“What?", "\"뭐?"], ["You are not going to say into the old Estella?”", "설마 옛 에스텔라로 자리 잡는다고 말하려는 건 아니겠지?\""], ["Miss Havisham interrupted.", "미스 해비셤이 말을 가로챘다."], ["“She was proud and insulting, and you wanted to go away from her.", "\"그 아이는 도도하고 모욕적이었고, 너는 그 아이에게서 달아나고 싶어 했지."], ["Don’t you remember?”", "기억나지 않느냐?\""]], [["I said confusedly that that was long ago, and that I knew no better then, and the like.", "나는 당황하여, 그건 오래전 일이고, 그때는 내가 철이 없어서 그랬다는 둥의 말을 했다."], ["Estella smiled with perfect composure, and said she had no doubt of my having been quite right, and of her having been very disagreeable.", "에스텔라는 더없이 침착하게 미소 지으며, 내가 전적으로 옳았다는 것과 자기가 매우 못되게 굴었다는 것을 조금도 의심치 않는다고 말했다."]], [["“Is he changed?”", "\"이 애는 변했느냐?\""], ["Miss Havisham asked her.", "미스 해비셤이 그녀에게 물었다."]], [["“Very much,”", "\"아주 많이요.\""], ["said Estella, looking at me.", "에스텔라가 나를 바라보며 말했다."]], [["“Less coarse and common?”", "\"덜 거칠고 덜 천해졌느냐?\""], ["said Miss Havisham, playing with Estella’s hair.", "미스 해비셤이 에스텔라의 머리카락을 만지작거리며 말했다."]], [["Estella laughed, and looked at the shoe in her hand, and laughed again, and looked at me, and put the shoe down. She treated me as a boy still, but she lured me on.", "에스텔라는 웃었고, 손에 든 구두를 들여다보고는 다시 웃더니, 나를 바라보고 구두를 내려놓았다. 그녀는 여전히 나를 소년 다루듯 했으나, 그러면서도 나를 끌어당겼다."]], [["We sat in the dreamy room among the old strange influences which had so wrought upon me, and I learnt that she had but just come home from France, and that she was going to London.", "우리는 나를 그토록 사로잡았던 그 옛 기이한 기운들에 둘러싸인 채 꿈결 같은 방에 앉아 있었고, 나는 그녀가 프랑스에서 막 돌아왔으며 이제 런던으로 갈 참이라는 것을 알게 되었다."], ["Proud and wilful as of old, she had brought those qualities into such subjection to her beauty that it was impossible and out of nature—or I thought so—to separate them from her beauty.", "옛날처럼 도도하고 제멋대로였으나, 그녀는 그러한 성품마저 자신의 아름다움에 고스란히 복속시켜 놓았던 터라, 그 성품을 그녀의 아름다움에서 떼어 내기란 불가능하고 자연의 이치에 어긋나는 일로 — 적어도 내겐 그렇게 — 여겨졌다."], ["Truly it was impossible to dissociate her presence from all those wretched hankerings after money and gentility that had disturbed my boyhood,—from all those ill-regulated aspirations that had first made me ashamed of home and Joe,—from all those visions that had raised her face in the glowing fire, struck it out of the iron on the anvil, extracted it from the darkness of night to look in at the wooden window of the forge, and flit away.", "참으로, 그녀의 존재를, 내 소년 시절을 어지럽혔던 그 돈과 신사다움을 향한 비참한 갈망들로부터 — 처음으로 내게 집과 조를 부끄럽게 여기게 만든, 그 다스려지지 않은 온갖 열망들로부터 — 활활 타오르는 불길 속에 그녀의 얼굴을 떠올리게 하고, 모루 위 쇳덩이에서 그것을 두드려 내게 하고, 밤의 어둠 속에서 그것을 끄집어내어 대장간의 나무 창으로 들여다보다 휙 사라지게 하던 그 온갖 환영들로부터 떼어 놓기란 불가능했다."], ["In a word, it was impossible for me to separate her, in the past or in the present, from the innermost life of my life.", "한마디로, 과거에서든 현재에서든, 그녀를 내 생명의 가장 깊은 삶으로부터 떼어 놓는 것은 내게 불가능한 일이었다."]], [["It was settled that I should stay there all the rest of the day, and return to the hotel at night, and to London to-morrow.", "그날 남은 시간을 모두 거기서 보내고, 밤에는 호텔로, 이튿날에는 런던으로 돌아가기로 정해졌다."], ["When we had conversed for a while, Miss Havisham sent us two out to walk in the neglected garden: on our coming in by and by, she said, I should wheel her about a little, as in times of yore.", "우리가 한동안 이야기를 나눈 뒤, 미스 해비셤은 우리 둘을 내보내어 방치된 정원을 거닐게 했다. 이따가 들어오면, 그녀가 말하기를, 옛날처럼 내가 자기 의자를 조금 밀어 데리고 다녀 달라고 했다."]], [["So, Estella and I went out into the garden by the gate through which I had strayed to my encounter with the pale young gentleman, now Herbert; I, trembling in spirit and worshipping the very hem of her dress; she, quite composed and most decidedly not worshipping the hem of mine.", "그리하여 에스텔라와 나는, 지난날 내가 길을 잘못 들어 그 창백한 젊은 신사 — 지금의 허버트 — 와 맞붙게 되었던 그 대문을 지나 정원으로 나갔다. 나는 마음으로 떨며 그녀 드레스의 옷자락마저 숭배하고 있었고, 그녀는 더없이 침착하여 내 옷자락 따위는 단연코 숭배하지 않았다."], ["As we drew near to the place of encounter, she stopped and said,—", "그 격투의 장소에 가까워지자 그녀가 걸음을 멈추고 말했다."]], [["“I must have been a singular little creature to hide and see that fight that day; but I did, and I enjoyed it very much.”", "\"그날 숨어서 그 격투를 지켜보다니, 나도 참 유별난 어린것이었어요. 하지만 정말 그랬고, 무척이나 즐거웠지요.\""]], [["“You rewarded me very much.”", "\"당신은 제게 큰 상을 주었지요.\""]], [["“Did I?”", "\"내가요?\""], ["she replied, in an incidental and forgetful way.", "그녀가 무심하고 까맣게 잊은 듯한 투로 대꾸했다."], ["“I remember I entertained a great objection to your adversary, because I took it ill that he should be brought here to pester me with his company.”", "\"당신 상대 아이한테 내가 몹시 거부감을 품었던 건 기억나요. 그 애가 여기 끌려와서 함께 있겠다며 나를 성가시게 구는 게 못마땅했거든요.\""]], [["“He and I are great friends now.”", "\"그 친구와 저는 이제 둘도 없는 사이랍니다.\""]], [["“Are you?", "\"그래요?"], ["I think I recollect though, that you read with his father?”", "그래도 당신이 그 애 아버지에게서 공부를 했던 건 기억나는 것 같은데요?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"네.\""]], [["I made the admission with reluctance, for it seemed to have a boyish look, and she already treated me more than enough like a boy.", "나는 마지못해 그 사실을 인정했으니, 그것이 어쩐지 소년 티가 나 보였고, 그녀는 이미 차고 넘치도록 나를 소년 대하듯 하고 있었기 때문이다."]], [["“Since your change of fortune and prospects, you have changed your companions,” said Estella.", "\"운수와 앞날이 바뀌고 나서, 당신은 어울리는 사람들도 바꾸었군요.\" 에스텔라가 말했다."]], [["“Naturally,” said I.", "\"당연하지요.\" 내가 말했다."]], [["“And necessarily,”", "\"그리고 그래야만 하고요.\""], ["she added, in a haughty tone; “what was fit company for you once, would be quite unfit company for you now.”", "그녀가 도도한 어조로 덧붙였다. \"한때 당신에게 어울리던 사람들이, 이제는 영 어울리지 않을 테니까요.\""]], [["In my conscience, I doubt very much whether I had any lingering intention left of going to see Joe; but if I had, this observation put it to flight.", "양심에 비추어 말하건대, 그때 내게 조를 보러 가려는 미련이 조금이라도 남아 있었는지는 무척 의심스럽다. 하지만 설령 남아 있었다 한들, 이 말 한마디가 그것을 깨끗이 날려 버렸을 것이다."]], [["“You had no idea of your impending good fortune, in those times?”", "\"그 시절엔, 당신에게 닥쳐올 행운에 대해 전혀 짐작도 못 했지요?\""], ["said Estella, with a slight wave of her hand, signifying in the fighting times.", "에스텔라가, 그 격투하던 시절을 뜻하는 양 손을 가볍게 흔들며 말했다."]], [["“Not the least.”", "\"전혀요.\""]], [["The air of completeness and superiority with which she walked at my side, and the air of youthfulness and submission with which I walked at hers, made a contrast that I strongly felt. It would have rankled in me more than it did, if I had not regarded myself as eliciting it by being so set apart for her and assigned to her.", "내 곁을 걷는 그녀의 그 완숙하고 우월한 분위기와, 그녀 곁을 걷는 나의 그 풋풋하고 순종적인 분위기가 빚어내는 대조를 나는 절절히 느꼈다. 만일 내가, 그녀를 위해 따로 마련되고 그녀에게 짝지어진 처지인 까닭에 내가 그 대조를 자아내는 것이라 여기지 않았더라면, 그것은 실제 이상으로 나를 쓰라리게 했을 것이다."]], [["The garden was too overgrown and rank for walking in with ease, and after we had made the round of it twice or thrice, we came out again into the brewery yard.", "정원은 너무 무성하고 거칠게 우거져 편히 거닐 수 없었으므로, 두세 바퀴를 돈 뒤 우리는 다시 양조장 마당으로 나왔다."], ["I showed her to a nicety where I had seen her walking on the casks, that first old day, and she said, with a cold and careless look in that direction, “Did I?”", "나는 그 첫 옛날에 그녀가 술통들 위를 걷던 모습을 본 자리를 정확히 짚어 보였고, 그녀는 그쪽을 차갑고 무심한 눈길로 바라보며 \"내가요?\" 하고 말했다."], ["I reminded her where she had come out of the house and given me my meat and drink, and she said, “I don’t remember.”", "나는 그녀가 집에서 나와 내게 먹을 것과 마실 것을 내주던 자리를 일깨워 주었으나, 그녀는 \"기억나지 않아요.\" 하고 말했다."], ["“Not remember that you made me cry?”", "\"나를 울린 일도 기억나지 않나요?\""], ["said I. “No,” said she, and shook her head and looked about her.", "내가 말했다. \"네.\" 그녀가 말하며 고개를 가로젓고 주위를 둘러보았다."], ["I verily believe that her not remembering and not minding in the least, made me cry again, inwardly,—and that is the sharpest crying of all.", "진정 나는 믿는다. 그녀가 기억하지 못하고 조금도 개의치 않는다는 사실이, 내 안에서 다시금 나를 울게 만들었다는 것을 — 그리고 그것이야말로 가장 쓰라린 울음이라는 것을."]], [["“You must know,”", "\"알아 둬야 할 게 있어요.\""], ["said Estella, condescending to me as a brilliant and beautiful woman might, “that I have no heart,—if that has anything to do with my memory.”", "에스텔라가, 눈부시고 아름다운 여인이 으레 그러듯 나를 굽어보며 말했다. \"나는 심장이 없어요 — 그게 내 기억력과 무슨 상관이 있다면 말이지요.\""]], [["I got through some jargon to the effect that I took the liberty of doubting that. That I knew better.", "나는, 외람되나 그 말을 의심한다는 뜻의 횡설수설을 어찌어찌 늘어놓았다. 나는 그렇지 않다는 걸 더 잘 안다고."], ["That there could be no such beauty without it.", "그것 없이는 이런 아름다움이 있을 수 없다고."]], [["“Oh!", "\"오!"], ["I have a heart to be stabbed in or shot in, I have no doubt,”", "찔리거나 총 맞을 심장이야 분명 있지요.\" 에스텔라가 말했다."], ["said Estella, “and of course if it ceased to beat I should cease to be.", "\"그게 멈추면 나도 멈출 테고요. 하지만 내 말뜻은 알 거예요."], ["But you know what I mean.", "나는 거기에 부드러움이라곤 없어요."], ["I have no softness there, no—sympathy—sentiment—nonsense.”", "동정도 — 감상도 — 그 따위 부질없는 것도 없지요.\""]], [["What was it that was borne in upon my mind when she stood still and looked attentively at me?", "그녀가 가만히 멈추어 서서 나를 골똘히 바라보았을 때, 내 마음에 사무쳐 든 그것은 대체 무엇이었던가?"], ["Anything that I had seen in Miss Havisham? No.", "내가 미스 해비셤에게서 본 그 무엇이었던가? 아니다."], ["In some of her looks and gestures there was that tinge of resemblance to Miss Havisham which may often be noticed to have been acquired by children, from grown person with whom they have been much associated and secluded, and which, when childhood is passed, will produce a remarkable occasional likeness of expression between faces that are otherwise quite different.", "그녀의 어떤 표정과 몸짓에는, 함께 오래 지내며 세상과 격리되어 어른에게서 아이가 물려받은 것을 흔히 볼 수 있는 그런, 미스 해비셤을 어렴풋이 닮은 기색이 깃들어 있었으니, 그것은 어린 시절이 지난 뒤에는 본디 전혀 다른 두 얼굴 사이에서 이따금 놀라운 표정의 유사함을 빚어내곤 하는 그런 것이었다. 그런데도 나는 그것을 미스 해비셤에게로 더듬어 올라갈 수 없었다."], ["And yet I could not trace this to Miss Havisham. I looked again, and though she was still looking at me, the suggestion was gone.", "다시 바라보았으나, 그녀가 여전히 나를 바라보고 있는데도 그 암시는 사라지고 없었다. 그것은 대체 무엇이었던가?"]], [["What was it?", "\"나는 진지해요.\""], ["“I am serious,” said Estella, not so much with a frown (for her brow was smooth) as with a darkening of her face; “if we are to be thrown much together, you had better believe it at once. No!”", "에스텔라가, 찌푸린 표정이라기보다는(그녀의 이마는 매끈했으니까) 얼굴이 어두워지는 빛으로 말했다. \"우리가 앞으로 자주 한자리에 있게 될 거라면, 차라리 지금 당장 그걸 믿어 두는 편이 나아요. 아니!\""], ["imperiously stopping me as I opened my lips.", "내가 입을 떼려 하자 그녀가 위엄 있게 나를 가로막았다."], ["“I have not bestowed my tenderness anywhere.", "\"나는 어디에도 다정함을 베풀어 본 적이 없어요."], ["I have never had any such thing.”", "애초에 그런 걸 가져 본 적이 없으니까요.\""]], [["In another moment we were in the brewery, so long disused, and she pointed to the high gallery where I had seen her going out on that same first day, and told me she remembered to have been up there, and to have seen me standing scared below.", "다음 순간 우리는, 그토록 오래 쓰이지 않은 그 양조장 안에 들어와 있었고, 그녀는 바로 그 첫날 그녀가 나가는 것을 내가 보았던 그 높은 회랑을 가리키며, 자기가 거기 올라갔던 것과 아래에서 겁에 질려 서 있던 나를 본 것을 기억한다고 말했다."], ["As my eyes followed her white hand, again the same dim suggestion that I could not possibly grasp crossed me.", "내 눈이 그녀의 흰 손을 좇는 사이, 도무지 붙잡을 수 없는 그 똑같은 희미한 암시가 다시 한번 나를 스쳐 갔다."], ["My involuntary start occasioned her to lay her hand upon my arm.", "내가 저도 모르게 흠칫하자 그녀는 내 팔에 손을 얹었다."], ["Instantly the ghost passed once more and was gone. What was it?", "순간 그 유령은 또 한 번 지나가 버리고 없었다."]], [["“What is the matter?”", "그것은 대체 무엇이었던가?"], ["asked Estella.", "\"왜 그래요?\""], ["“Are you scared again?”", "에스텔라가 물었다. \"또 겁이 났나요?\""]], [["“I should be, if I believed what you said just now,”", "\"방금 당신이 한 말을 믿었다면 그랬겠지요.\""], ["I replied, to turn it off.", "나는 얼버무리려고 대답했다."]], [["“Then you don’t? Very well.", "\"그럼 안 믿는군요? 좋아요."], ["It is said, at any rate.", "어쨌든 말은 해 두었으니까요."], ["Miss Havisham will soon be expecting you at your old post, though I think that might be laid aside now, with other old belongings.", "미스 해비셤이 곧 당신을 옛 자리에서 기다리실 거예요. 하긴 이제는 그것도 다른 옛 물건들과 함께 치워 버려도 될 것 같지만요."], ["Let us make one more round of the garden, and then go in. Come!", "정원을 한 바퀴만 더 돌고 들어가요. 자!"], ["You shall not shed tears for my cruelty to-day; you shall be my Page, and give me your shoulder.”", "오늘은 내 잔인함 때문에 눈물 흘리지 말아요. 당신은 내 시동이 되어, 어깨를 빌려주세요.\""]], [["Her handsome dress had trailed upon the ground.", "그녀의 고운 드레스가 땅바닥에 끌리고 있었다."], ["She held it in one hand now, and with the other lightly touched my shoulder as we walked.", "그녀는 이제 한 손으로 그것을 잡고, 다른 한 손으로는 걸으며 내 어깨를 가볍게 짚었다."], ["We walked round the ruined garden twice or thrice more, and it was all in bloom for me.", "우리는 그 폐허가 된 정원을 두세 바퀴 더 돌았고, 그것은 내게 온통 꽃이 만발한 곳이었다."], ["If the green and yellow growth of weed in the chinks of the old wall had been the most precious flowers that ever blew, it could not have been more cherished in my remembrance.", "낡은 담장 틈새에 푸르고 누렇게 돋아난 잡초가 일찍이 피어난 적 없는 가장 귀한 꽃들이었다 한들, 내 기억 속에서 그보다 더 애틋할 수는 없었을 것이다."]], [["There was no discrepancy of years between us to remove her far from me; we were of nearly the same age, though of course the age told for more in her case than in mine; but the air of inaccessibility which her beauty and her manner gave her, tormented me in the midst of my delight, and at the height of the assurance I felt that our patroness had chosen us for one another.", "우리 사이에는 그녀를 내게서 멀리 떼어 놓을 만한 나이 차이가 없었으니, 우리는 거의 동갑이었다 — 물론 그녀에게는 나이가 나보다 더 큰 의미를 지녔지만. 그러나 그녀의 아름다움과 몸가짐이 그녀에게 부여한 그 범접할 수 없는 분위기는, 내 환희의 한복판에서 나를 괴롭혔으니, 우리의 후원자가 우리를 서로의 짝으로 점지했다는 확신이 절정에 달한 그 순간에도 그러했다."], ["Wretched boy!", "가련한 소년 같으니!"]], [["At last we went back into the house, and there I heard, with surprise, that my guardian had come down to see Miss Havisham on business, and would come back to dinner.", "마침내 우리는 집으로 돌아갔고, 거기서 나는 놀랍게도, 내 후견인이 볼일이 있어 미스 해비셤을 만나러 내려와 있으며 만찬에 다시 오리라는 소식을 들었다."], ["The old wintry branches of chandeliers in the room where the mouldering table was spread had been lighted while we were out, and Miss Havisham was in her chair and waiting for me.", "곰팡이 슨 그 탁자가 차려진 방의, 겨울 가지처럼 앙상한 옛 샹들리에들이 우리가 나가 있는 동안 불이 밝혀져 있었고, 미스 해비셤은 자기 의자에 앉아 나를 기다리고 있었다."]], [["It was like pushing the chair itself back into the past, when we began the old slow circuit round about the ashes of the bridal feast.", "신부의 잔칫상 잿더미를 빙 도는 그 옛 느릿한 순행을 시작하자니, 마치 의자 그 자체를 과거 속으로 밀어 넣는 것만 같았다."], ["But, in the funereal room, with that figure of the grave fallen back in the chair fixing its eyes upon her, Estella looked more bright and beautiful than before, and I was under stronger enchantment.", "그러나 그 장례식 같은 방, 무덤 같은 형상이 의자에 등을 기댄 채 그녀에게 두 눈을 못 박고 있는 그 방에서, 에스텔라는 전보다 한결 더 환하고 아름다워 보였고, 나는 더욱 강한 마법에 사로잡혔다."]], [["The time so melted away, that our early dinner-hour drew close at hand, and Estella left us to prepare herself.", "시간이 어찌나 녹아내리듯 흘러갔던지, 이른 만찬 시각이 코앞에 닥쳐, 에스텔라는 채비를 하러 우리 곁을 떠났다."], ["We had stopped near the centre of the long table, and Miss Havisham, with one of her withered arms stretched out of the chair, rested that clenched hand upon the yellow cloth.", "우리는 그 긴 탁자의 중앙께에서 걸음을 멈춘 참이었고, 미스 해비셤은 시든 한쪽 팔을 의자 밖으로 뻗어, 그 움켜쥔 손을 누런 식탁보 위에 얹고 있었다."], ["As Estella looked back over her shoulder before going out at the door, Miss Havisham kissed that hand to her, with a ravenous intensity that was of its kind quite dreadful.", "에스텔라가 문으로 나가기 전 어깨 너머로 돌아보자, 미스 해비셤은 그 손으로 그녀에게 입맞춤을 날려 보냈으니, 그 게걸스러운 격렬함은 그 나름으로 자못 섬뜩한 것이었다."]], [["Then, Estella being gone and we two left alone, she turned to me, and said in a whisper,—", "그러고 나서, 에스텔라가 떠나 우리 둘만 남게 되자, 그녀가 내게로 돌아서서 속삭이듯 말했다."]], [["“Is she beautiful, graceful, well-grown?", "\"그 아이가 아름답지, 우아하고, 잘 자랐지?"], ["Do you admire her?”", "너도 그 아이에게 반했느냐?\""]], [["“Everybody must who sees her, Miss Havisham.”", "\"그 아이를 보는 사람이라면 누구나 그럴 수밖에 없겠지요, 미스 해비셤.\""]], [["She drew an arm round my neck, and drew my head close down to hers as she sat in the chair.", "그녀는 한쪽 팔로 내 목을 감아, 의자에 앉은 채 내 머리를 자기 머리 가까이로 끌어내렸다."], ["“Love her, love her, love her!", "\"그 아이를 사랑해라, 사랑해라, 사랑해라!"], ["How does she use you?”", "그 아이가 너를 어떻게 대하느냐?\""]], [["Before I could answer (if I could have answered so difficult a question at all) she repeated, “Love her, love her, love her!", "내가 미처 대답하기도 전에(그토록 어려운 물음에 내가 대답할 수 있었다 한들), 그녀가 되풀이했다. \"그 아이를 사랑해라, 사랑해라, 사랑해라!"], ["If she favours you, love her.", "그 아이가 너를 어여삐 여기거든, 사랑해라."], ["If she wounds you, love her.", "그 아이가 너를 상처 입히거든, 사랑해라."], ["If she tears your heart to pieces,—and as it gets older and stronger it will tear deeper,—love her, love her, love her!”", "그 아이가 네 심장을 갈가리 찢어 놓거든 — 그리고 그것이 더 늙고 더 단단해질수록 더 깊이 찢어 놓을 테지만 — 사랑해라, 사랑해라, 사랑해라!\""]], [["Never had I seen such passionate eagerness as was joined to her utterance of these words.", "그 말들을 토해 낼 때 그녀가 보인 것만큼 격정적인 열망을 나는 일찍이 본 적이 없었다."], ["I could feel the muscles of the thin arm round my neck swell with the vehemence that possessed her.", "내 목을 감은 그 가느다란 팔의 근육이, 그녀를 사로잡은 격렬함으로 부풀어 오르는 것을 나는 느낄 수 있었다."]], [["“Hear me, Pip!", "\"내 말 들어라, 핍!"], ["I adopted her, to be loved. I bred her and educated her, to be loved.", "나는 사랑받게 하려고 그 아이를 양녀로 들였다. 사랑받게 하려고 그 아이를 기르고 가르쳤다."], ["I developed her into what she is, that she might be loved. Love her!”", "사랑받게 하려고 그 아이를 지금의 모습으로 키워 냈다. 그 아이를 사랑해라!\""]], [["She said the word often enough, and there could be no doubt that she meant to say it; but if the often repeated word had been hate instead of love—despair—revenge—dire death—it could not have sounded from her lips more like a curse.", "그녀는 그 말을 충분히 여러 번 했고, 그녀가 그 말을 하려 했다는 데에는 의심의 여지가 없었다. 그러나 그 거듭 되풀이된 말이 사랑이 아니라 증오였다 한들 — 절망이든 — 복수든 — 끔찍한 죽음이든 — 그녀의 입술에서 그보다 더 저주처럼 울려 나올 수는 없었을 것이다."]], [["“I’ll tell you,”", "\"내가 말해 주마.\""], ["said she, in the same hurried passionate whisper, “what real love is.", "그녀가 여전히 다급하고 격정적인 속삭임으로 말했다. \"진정한 사랑이 무엇인지를."], ["It is blind devotion, unquestioning self-humiliation, utter submission, trust and belief against yourself and against the whole world, giving up your whole heart and soul to the smiter—as I did!”", "그건 눈먼 헌신이고, 따져 묻지 않는 자기 비하이며, 철저한 복종이고, 너 자신을 거스르고 온 세상을 거슬러서 바치는 신뢰와 믿음이며, 너를 내리치는 자에게 네 온 마음과 영혼을 송두리째 내주는 것이다 — 내가 그랬듯이!\""]], [["When she came to that, and to a wild cry that followed that, I caught her round the waist.", "그녀가 그 대목에, 그리고 뒤이은 광란의 비명에 이르렀을 때, 나는 그녀의 허리를 와락 끌어안았다."], ["For she rose up in the chair, in her shroud of a dress, and struck at the air as if she would as soon have struck herself against the wall and fallen dead.", "그녀가 수의 같은 드레스를 걸친 채 의자에서 벌떡 일어나, 마치 차라리 벽에 제 몸을 부딪쳐 죽기라도 하려는 듯 허공을 후려쳤기 때문이다."]], [["All this passed in a few seconds.", "이 모든 것이 몇 초 사이에 벌어졌다."], ["As I drew her down into her chair, I was conscious of a scent that I knew, and turning, saw my guardian in the room.", "그녀를 의자로 끌어 앉히는 사이, 나는 익히 아는 어떤 향내를 느꼈고, 돌아보니 내 후견인이 방 안에 있었다."]], [["He always carried (I have not yet mentioned it, I think) a pocket-handkerchief of rich silk and of imposing proportions, which was of great value to him in his profession.", "그는 늘(아직 언급하지 않은 것 같은데) 값진 비단으로 된, 위풍당당한 크기의 손수건을 지니고 다녔으니, 그것은 그의 직업에서 그에게 큰 쓸모가 있는 물건이었다."], ["I have seen him so terrify a client or a witness by ceremoniously unfolding this pocket-handkerchief as if he were immediately going to blow his nose, and then pausing, as if he knew he should not have time to do it before such client or witness committed himself, that the self-committal has followed directly, quite as a matter of course.", "나는 그가 의뢰인이나 증인을, 이 손수건을 마치 당장 코라도 풀려는 듯 점잔 빼며 펼쳐 보이고는, 그 의뢰인이나 증인이 제 발 저려 말실수를 저지르기 전에 코 풀 겨를이 없으리라는 걸 안다는 양 동작을 멈추는 것만으로 어찌나 겁에 질리게 했던지, 그러면 그 자백이 으레 당연한 수순처럼 곧장 뒤따라 나오는 것을 본 적이 있다."], ["When I saw him in the room he had this expressive pocket-handkerchief in both hands, and was looking at us.", "내가 방 안에서 그를 보았을 때, 그는 그 의미심장한 손수건을 두 손에 든 채 우리를 바라보고 있었다."], ["On meeting my eye, he said plainly, by a momentary and silent pause in that attitude, “Indeed?", "내 눈과 마주치자, 그는 그 자세 그대로 잠시 말없이 멈춤으로써 분명하게 말했다. \"그래?"], ["Singular!”", "별일이군!\""], ["and then put the handkerchief to its right use with wonderful effect.", "그러고는 놀라운 효과를 발휘하며 그 손수건을 제 본래 용도에 갖다 썼다."]], [["Miss Havisham had seen him as soon as I, and was (like everybody else) afraid of him.", "미스 해비셤도 나만큼이나 일찌감치 그를 알아보았고, (다른 모든 이들처럼) 그를 두려워했다."], ["She made a strong attempt to compose herself, and stammered that he was as punctual as ever.", "그녀는 마음을 가다듬으려 애써 노력하며, 그가 변함없이 시간을 잘 지킨다고 더듬더듬 말했다."]], [["“As punctual as ever,”", "\"변함없이 시간을 잘 지키지요.\""], ["he repeated, coming up to us.", "그가 우리 쪽으로 다가오며 되풀이했다."], ["“(How do you do, Pip?", "\"(잘 지냈느냐, 핍?"], ["Shall I give you a ride, Miss Havisham?", "한 바퀴 밀어 드릴까요, 미스 해비셤?"], ["Once round?) And so you are here, Pip?”", "한 바퀴만?) 그래, 너도 여기 와 있었구나, 핍?\""]], [["I told him when I had arrived, and how Miss Havisham had wished me to come and see Estella.", "나는 그에게 내가 언제 도착했는지, 그리고 미스 해비셤이 내가 와서 에스텔라를 보기를 바라셨다는 것을 말했다."], ["To which he replied, “Ah!", "그러자 그가 대꾸했다. \"아!"], ["Very fine young lady!”", "참으로 아리따운 아가씨지!\""], ["Then he pushed Miss Havisham in her chair before him, with one of his large hands, and put the other in his trousers-pocket as if the pocket were full of secrets.", "그러고는 큼직한 한 손으로 미스 해비셤의 의자를 앞으로 밀고, 다른 한 손은 마치 그 주머니가 비밀로 가득 차기라도 한 듯 바지 주머니에 찔러 넣었다."]], [["“Well, Pip!", "\"그래, 핍!"], ["How often have you seen Miss Estella before?”", "전에 미스 에스텔라를 몇 번이나 봤느냐?\""], ["said he, when he came to a stop.", "그가 걸음을 멈추며 말했다."]], [["“How often?”", "\"몇 번이냐고요?\""]], [["“Ah! How many times?", "\"그래!"], ["Ten thousand times?”", "몇 번이나? 만 번?\""]], [["“Oh!", "\"오!"], ["Certainly not so many.”", "그렇게 많지는 않지요.\""]], [["“Twice?”", "\"두 번?\""]], [["“Jaggers,”", "\"재거스,\""], ["interposed Miss Havisham, much to my relief, “leave my Pip alone, and go with him to your dinner.”", "미스 해비셤이 끼어들었으니, 내겐 무척이나 다행스러운 일이었다. \"내 핍은 그만 내버려 두고, 데리고 가서 식사나 하세요.\""]], [["He complied, and we groped our way down the dark stairs together.", "그가 따랐고, 우리는 함께 어두운 계단을 더듬더듬 내려갔다."], ["While we were still on our way to those detached apartments across the paved yard at the back, he asked me how often I had seen Miss Havisham eat and drink; offering me a breadth of choice, as usual, between a hundred times and once.", "뒤편 포석 깔린 마당 너머의 그 별채로 가는 길에서, 그는 내게 미스 해비셤이 먹고 마시는 것을 몇 번이나 보았느냐고 물으며, 여느 때처럼 백 번에서 한 번 사이에서 폭넓게 고를 여지를 주었다."]], [["I considered, and said, “Never.”", "나는 곰곰이 생각해 보고 말했다. \"한 번도요.\""]], [["“And never will, Pip,”", "\"앞으로도 못 볼 거다, 핍.\""], ["he retorted, with a frowning smile.", "그가 찌푸린 미소를 지으며 대꾸했다."], ["“She has never allowed herself to be seen doing either, since she lived this present life of hers.", "\"그분은 지금의 이 삶을 살게 된 뒤로, 그 어느 쪽이든 남에게 보이는 걸 결코 허락한 적이 없지."], ["She wanders about in the night, and then lays hands on such food as she takes.”", "밤이면 이리저리 돌아다니다가, 그러다 손에 잡히는 음식을 집어 드는 거야.\""]], [["“Pray, sir,”", "\"여쭙건대, 선생님,\""], ["said I, “may I ask you a question?”", "내가 말했다. \"한 가지 여쭤봐도 될까요?\""]], [["“You may,”", "\"물어도 좋다.\""], ["said he, “and I may decline to answer it.", "그가 말했다. \"그리고 나는 대답을 거절할 수도 있지."], ["Put your question.”", "질문해 보아라.\""]], [["“Estella’s name.", "\"에스텔라의 성 말입니다."], ["Is it Havisham or—?”", "해비셤인가요, 아니면—?\""], ["I had nothing to add.", "나는 더 덧붙일 말이 없었다."]], [["“Or what?”", "\"아니면 뭐냐?\""], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."]], [["“Is it Havisham?”", "\"해비셤인가요?\""]], [["“It is Havisham.”", "\"해비셤이다.\""]], [["This brought us to the dinner-table, where she and Sarah Pocket awaited us. Mr.", "이렇게 해서 우리는 만찬 식탁에 이르렀고, 거기서는 그녀와 세라 포켓이 우리를 기다리고 있었다."], ["Jaggers presided, Estella sat opposite to him, I faced my green and yellow friend.", "재거스 씨가 좌장 노릇을 했고, 에스텔라는 그의 맞은편에, 나는 그 푸르뎅뎅하고 누리끼리한 벗을 마주 보고 앉았다."], ["We dined very well, and were waited on by a maid-servant whom I had never seen in all my comings and goings, but who, for anything I know, had been in that mysterious house the whole time.", "우리는 아주 잘 먹었고, 내가 그 집을 드나든 그 숱한 동안 한 번도 본 적 없는 하녀의 시중을 받았는데, 어쩌면 그 여자는 내가 알지 못할 뿐 그 신비로운 집에 줄곧 있었는지도 모를 일이었다."], ["After dinner a bottle of choice old port was placed before my guardian (he was evidently well acquainted with the vintage), and the two ladies left us.", "만찬이 끝나자 내 후견인 앞에는 진귀한 묵은 포트와인 한 병이 놓였고(그는 분명 그 빈티지를 잘 아는 듯했다), 두 숙녀는 우리 곁을 떠났다."]], [["Anything to equal the determined reticence of Mr. Jaggers under that roof I never saw elsewhere, even in him.", "그 지붕 아래에서 보인 재거스 씨의 그 작심한 듯한 과묵함에 견줄 만한 것을, 나는 다른 어디서도, 심지어 그 자신에게서조차 본 적이 없다."], ["He kept his very looks to himself, and scarcely directed his eyes to Estella’s face once during dinner.", "그는 자기 표정마저 속으로 감추었고, 만찬 내내 에스텔라의 얼굴로 눈길을 한 번도 제대로 보내지 않았다."], ["When she spoke to him, he listened, and in due course answered, but never looked at her, that I could see.", "그녀가 그에게 말을 걸면 그는 귀 기울여 듣고 때맞춰 대답했으나, 내가 보기에 그녀를 결코 바라보지는 않았다."], ["On the other hand, she often looked at him, with interest and curiosity, if not distrust, but his face never showed the least consciousness.", "반면에 그녀는, 불신까지는 아니더라도 흥미와 호기심을 담아 자주 그를 바라보았으나, 그의 얼굴은 조금도 그것을 의식하는 빛을 내비치지 않았다."], ["Throughout dinner he took a dry delight in making Sarah Pocket greener and yellower, by often referring in conversation with me to my expectations; but here, again, he showed no consciousness, and even made it appear that he extorted—and even did extort, though I don’t know how—those references out of my innocent self.", "만찬 내내 그는 나와의 대화에서 내 유산에 대해 자주 들먹임으로써 세라 포켓을 더 푸르뎅뎅하고 더 누리끼리하게 만드는 데에서 무미건조한 즐거움을 맛보았다. 그러나 여기서도 그는 의식하는 기색이라곤 없었고, 심지어 그 언급들을 순진한 내 입에서 억지로 끄집어내는 것처럼 — 실제로 끄집어내기도 했으니, 어떻게 그랬는지는 모르겠지만 — 보이게 만들었다."]], [["And when he and I were left alone together, he sat with an air upon him of general lying by in consequence of information he possessed, that really was too much for me.", "그리고 그와 나 둘만 남게 되자, 그는 자기가 쥐고 있는 정보 덕분에 전반적으로 느긋이 웅크리고 있는 듯한 분위기를 풍기며 앉아 있었으니, 그것은 정말이지 내겐 감당하기 벅찬 것이었다."], ["He cross-examined his very wine when he had nothing else in hand.", "그는 달리 손댈 것이 없을 때면 자기 포도주마저 추궁하듯 따져 댔다."], ["He held it between himself and the candle, tasted the port, rolled it in his mouth, swallowed it, looked at his glass again, smelt the port, tried it, drank it, filled again, and cross-examined the glass again, until I was as nervous as if I had known the wine to be telling him something to my disadvantage.", "그것을 자기와 촛불 사이에 들어 비추어 보고, 포트와인을 맛보고, 입 안에서 굴리고, 삼키고, 다시 잔을 들여다보고, 포트와인의 향을 맡고, 음미하고, 마시고, 다시 채우고, 또다시 잔을 추궁했으니, 나는 마치 그 포도주가 그에게 나한테 불리한 무언가를 일러바치고 있다는 걸 알기라도 한 듯 안절부절못했다."], ["Three or four times I feebly thought I would start conversation; but whenever he saw me going to ask him anything, he looked at me with his glass in his hand, and rolling his wine about in his mouth, as if requesting me to take notice that it was of no use, for he couldn’t answer.", "서너 번쯤 나는 맥없이 대화를 시작해 볼까 생각했으나, 내가 무언가를 물으려는 낌새를 보일 때마다, 그는 잔을 손에 든 채, 입 안에서 포도주를 굴리면서, 마치 소용없는 짓이니 잘 새겨 두라는 듯, 자기는 대답할 수 없다는 듯 나를 바라보았다."]], [["I think Miss Pocket was conscious that the sight of me involved her in the danger of being goaded to madness, and perhaps tearing off her cap,—which was a very hideous one, in the nature of a muslin mop,—and strewing the ground with her hair,—which assuredly had never grown on her head.", "미스 포켓은, 나를 보는 것이 미칠 지경으로 들볶이는 위험을, 그리하여 어쩌면 모슬린 자루 같은 — 지극히 흉측한 — 자기 모자를 잡아 뜯고, 분명 제 머리에서 자란 것일 리 없는 그 머리카락을 바닥에 흩뿌릴 위험을 무릅쓰는 처지임을 스스로 의식하고 있었던 것 같다."], ["She did not appear when we afterwards went up to Miss Havisham’s room, and we four played at whist.", "나중에 우리가 미스 해비셤의 방으로 올라가 넷이서 휘스트를 칠 때, 그녀는 모습을 보이지 않았다."], ["In the interval, Miss Havisham, in a fantastic way, had put some of the most beautiful jewels from her dressing-table into Estella’s hair, and about her bosom and arms; and I saw even my guardian look at her from under his thick eyebrows, and raise them a little, when her loveliness was before him, with those rich flushes of glitter and colour in it.", "그 사이에 미스 해비셤은, 기상천외한 방식으로, 자기 화장대의 가장 아름다운 보석 몇 점을 에스텔라의 머리와 가슴과 팔에 둘러 주었다. 그리고 나는, 그렇게 보석의 광채와 빛깔이 흐드러진 그녀의 사랑스러움이 눈앞에 놓이자, 내 후견인마저 짙은 눈썹 아래로 그녀를 바라보며 눈썹을 살짝 치켜올리는 것을 보았다."]], [["Of the manner and extent to which he took our trumps into custody, and came out with mean little cards at the ends of hands, before which the glory of our Kings and Queens was utterly abased, I say nothing; nor, of the feeling that I had, respecting his looking upon us personally in the light of three very obvious and poor riddles that he had found out long ago.", "그가 어떤 식으로, 또 어느 정도로 우리의 으뜸패를 거두어 가두어 버리고는, 우리 킹과 퀸의 영광이 송두리째 짓밟히도록 판이 끝날 때마다 보잘것없는 시시한 패를 내놓았는지에 대해서는, 나는 아무 말도 하지 않겠다. 또한 그가 우리를 한 사람 한 사람, 자기가 오래전에 이미 풀어 버린 너무도 빤하고 시시한 세 개의 수수께끼쯤으로 여기고 있는 듯하다는 데서 내가 느낀 감정에 대해서도 말하지 않겠다."], ["What I suffered from, was the incompatibility between his cold presence and my feelings towards Estella.", "내가 괴로웠던 것은, 그의 차가운 존재와 에스텔라를 향한 내 감정 사이의 그 양립 불가능함이었다."], ["It was not that I knew I could never bear to speak to him about her, that I knew I could never bear to hear him creak his boots at her, that I knew I could never bear to see him wash his hands of her; it was, that my admiration should be within a foot or two of him,—it was, that my feelings should be in the same place with him,—that, was the agonizing circumstance.", "내가 결코 그에게 그녀에 대해 말하는 것을 견딜 수 없으리라는 것을 안다거나, 그가 그녀에게 부츠 소리를 삐걱이는 것을 결코 들을 수 없으리라는 것을 안다거나, 그가 그녀에게서 손을 떼어 버리는 것을 결코 볼 수 없으리라는 것을 안다거나 하는 것이 아니었다. 내가 괴로웠던 것은, 내 흠모가 그에게서 한두 자 거리 안에 있다는 것 — 내 감정이 그와 같은 자리에 놓여 있다는 것 — 바로 그것이 그 견디기 힘든 사정이었다."]], [["We played until nine o’clock, and then it was arranged that when Estella came to London I should be forewarned of her coming and should meet her at the coach; and then I took leave of her, and touched her and left her.", "우리는 아홉 시까지 카드놀이를 했고, 그런 다음 에스텔라가 런던에 올 때면 그녀가 온다는 것을 내게 미리 알려 주어 내가 역마차로 그녀를 마중 나가기로 정해졌다. 그러고 나서 나는 그녀에게 작별을 고하고, 그녀의 손에 닿았다가 그녀를 떠났다."]], [["My guardian lay at the Boar in the next room to mine.", "내 후견인은 보어 여관, 내 방 옆방에 묵었다."], ["Far into the night, Miss Havisham’s words, “Love her, love her, love her!” sounded in my ears.", "밤이 깊도록, 미스 해비셤의 그 말, \"그 아이를 사랑해라, 사랑해라, 사랑해라!\"가 내 귓전에 울렸다."], ["I adapted them for my own repetition, and said to my pillow, “I love her, I love her, I love her!”", "나는 그 말을 내 식으로 고쳐 되뇌며, 베개를 향해 \"나는 그녀를 사랑해, 사랑해, 사랑해!\""], ["hundreds of times.", "하고 수백 번을 말했다."], ["Then, a burst of gratitude came upon me, that she should be destined for me, once the blacksmith’s boy.", "그러자 한때 대장장이의 소년이었던 내게 그녀가 점지되어 있다는 사실에 감격이 북받쳐 올랐다."], ["Then I thought if she were, as I feared, by no means rapturously grateful for that destiny yet, when would she begin to be interested in me?", "그러다 나는, 그녀가 내 두려움대로 아직은 그 운명에 조금도 황홀히 감사하지 않는다면, 그녀가 대체 언제부터 나에게 관심을 갖기 시작할까 하고 생각했다."], ["When should I awaken the heart within her that was mute and sleeping now?", "지금은 말없이 잠들어 있는 그녀 안의 심장을, 나는 언제쯤 깨울 수 있을까?"]], [["Ah me! I thought those were high and great emotions.", "아, 이런! 나는 그것들이 고결하고 위대한 감정이라고 여겼다."], ["But I never thought there was anything low and small in my keeping away from Joe, because I knew she would be contemptuous of him.", "그러나 내가 조에게서 멀어진 데에 — 그녀가 그를 경멸하리라는 것을 안다는 이유로 그리한 데에 — 비천하고 옹졸한 구석이 있다고는 결코 생각지 못했다."], ["It was but a day gone, and Joe had brought the tears into my eyes; they had soon dried, God forgive me!", "겨우 하루 전만 해도, 조는 내 눈에 눈물을 고이게 했었다. 그 눈물은 이내 말라 버렸으니, 하느님, 저를 용서하소서!"], ["soon dried.", "이내 말라 버렸다."]], [["# Chapter XXX.", "# 제28장"]], [["After well considering the matter while I was dressing at the Blue Boar in the morning, I resolved to tell my guardian that I doubted Orlick’s being the right sort of man to fill a post of trust at Miss Havisham’s.", "아침에 블루 보어에서 옷을 갈아입으며 그 문제를 충분히 곰곰이 따져 본 끝에, 나는 후견인에게 올릭이 미스 해비셤 댁의 신임받는 자리를 맡기에 적합한 부류의 사람인지 의심스럽다고 말하기로 결심했다."], ["“Why of course he is not the right sort of man, Pip,”", "\"물론 그자는 적합한 부류의 사람이 아니지, 핍.\""], ["said my guardian, comfortably satisfied beforehand on the general head, “because the man who fills the post of trust never is the right sort of man.”", "후견인은 그 일반적인 명제에 대해 미리 흡족하게 만족한 듯 말했다. \"신임받는 자리를 맡는 사람이라는 게 애당초 적합한 부류의 사람이었던 적이 없으니까.\""], ["It seemed quite to put him into spirits to find that this particular post was not exceptionally held by the right sort of man, and he listened in a satisfied manner while I told him what knowledge I had of Orlick.", "이 특정한 자리 역시 예외 없이 적합한 부류의 사람이 차지하고 있지 않다는 사실을 알게 되자 그는 자못 신이 난 듯했고, 내가 올릭에 대해 알고 있는 바를 말하는 동안 만족스러운 태도로 귀를 기울였다."], ["“Very good, Pip,”", "\"아주 좋아, 핍.\""], ["he observed, when I had concluded, “I’ll go round presently, and pay our friend off.”", "내가 이야기를 마치자 그가 말했다. \"내가 곧 들러서 우리 친구를 해고하지.\""], ["Rather alarmed by this summary action, I was for a little delay, and even hinted that our friend himself might be difficult to deal with. “Oh no he won’t,”", "이런 즉결 처분에 다소 놀란 나는 잠시 미루는 편이 어떻겠느냐고 했고, 심지어 그 친구 본인을 다루기가 까다로울지도 모른다는 말까지 넌지시 비쳤다. \"오, 아니, 그렇지 않을 거야.\""], ["said my guardian, making his pocket-handkerchief-point, with perfect confidence; “I should like to see him argue the question with me.”", "후견인은 더없는 자신감으로 손수건을 펼치는 시늉을 하며 말했다. \"그자가 나하고 그 문제를 가지고 따져 보겠다면 어디 한번 보고 싶군.\""]], [["As we were going back together to London by the midday coach, and as I breakfasted under such terrors of Pumblechook that I could scarcely hold my cup, this gave me an opportunity of saying that I wanted a walk, and that I would go on along the London road while Mr. Jaggers was occupied, if he would let the coachman know that I would get into my place when overtaken.", "우리는 정오 마차로 함께 런던으로 돌아갈 참이었고, 나는 펌블추크에 대한 두려움 때문에 찻잔조차 제대로 들지 못할 지경으로 아침 식사를 했으므로, 이를 기회 삼아 산책을 좀 하고 싶다고 말했다. 재거스 씨가 일을 보는 동안 나는 런던으로 향하는 길을 따라 먼저 가 있겠으며, 마차가 나를 따라잡으면 자리에 오르겠으니 마부에게 그리 일러 달라고 했다."], ["I was thus enabled to fly from the Blue Boar immediately after breakfast.", "그리하여 나는 아침 식사를 마치자마자 곧장 블루 보어에서 달아날 수 있었다."], ["By then making a loop of about a couple of miles into the open country at the back of Pumblechook’s premises, I got round into the High Street again, a little beyond that pitfall, and felt myself in comparative security.", "그런 다음 펌블추크의 가게 뒤편으로 펼쳐진 들판으로 이 마일쯤 빙 돌아 나가, 그 함정을 조금 지난 지점에서 다시 큰길로 접어들었고, 그제야 비교적 안전하다는 느낌이 들었다."]], [["It was interesting to be in the quiet old town once more, and it was not disagreeable to be here and there suddenly recognised and stared after.", "조용한 옛 고장에 다시 와 있는 것은 흥미로운 일이었고, 여기저기서 불쑥 사람들에게 알아보아지고 뒤에서 눈길을 받는 것도 그리 싫지는 않았다."], ["One or two of the tradespeople even darted out of their shops and went a little way down the street before me, that they might turn, as if they had forgotten something, and pass me face to face,—on which occasions I don’t know whether they or I made the worse pretence; they of not doing it, or I of not seeing it.", "상인 한둘은 가게에서 뛰쳐나와 내 앞에서 거리를 조금 내려가더니, 마치 무언가를 깜빡 잊은 양 몸을 돌려 나와 정면으로 마주치곤 했는데, 그럴 때면 그들이 모르는 척하는 연기와 내가 못 본 척하는 연기 가운데 어느 쪽이 더 서툴렀는지 나로서는 알 수 없었다."], ["Still my position was a distinguished one, and I was not at all dissatisfied with it, until Fate threw me in the way of that unlimited miscreant, Trabb’s boy.", "그래도 내 처지는 남다른 것이었고, 나는 그것에 조금도 불만이 없었다. 운명이 그 한량없는 악당, 트래브네 점원 녀석을 내 앞길에 던져 놓기 전까지는 말이다."]], [["Casting my eyes along the street at a certain point of my progress, I beheld Trabb’s boy approaching, lashing himself with an empty blue bag.", "길을 가다 어느 지점에서 거리를 따라 시선을 던지니, 트래브네 점원 녀석이 빈 파란 가방으로 제 몸을 후려치며 다가오는 것이 보였다."], ["Deeming that a serene and unconscious contemplation of him would best beseem me, and would be most likely to quell his evil mind, I advanced with that expression of countenance, and was rather congratulating myself on my success, when suddenly the knees of Trabb’s boy smote together, his hair uprose, his cap fell off, he trembled violently in every limb, staggered out into the road, and crying to the populace, “Hold me!", "평온하고 무심한 태도로 그를 바라보는 것이 내게 가장 어울리며, 또한 그자의 못된 심사를 잠재우는 데 가장 효과적이리라 판단한 나는, 그런 표정을 지은 채 앞으로 나아갔고, 내 성공을 자축할 만하다고 여기던 참이었다. 그런데 별안간 트래브네 점원 녀석의 두 무릎이 맞부딪치고, 머리카락이 곤두서고, 모자가 벗겨지고, 사지가 격렬하게 떨리더니, 비틀거리며 길 한복판으로 나가 군중을 향해 \"날 좀 잡아 줘요!"], ["I’m so frightened!”", "너무 무서워요!\""], ["feigned to be in a paroxysm of terror and contrition, occasioned by the dignity of my appearance.", "하고 외치며, 내 위풍당당한 행색에 압도되어 공포와 회한의 발작을 일으킨 척하는 것이었다."], ["As I passed him, his teeth loudly chattered in his head, and with every mark of extreme humiliation, he prostrated himself in the dust.", "내가 그 곁을 지나가자 녀석은 입속에서 이를 요란하게 딱딱 부딪치며, 극도의 굴종을 드러내는 온갖 몸짓과 함께 흙먼지 속에 납작 엎드렸다."]], [["This was a hard thing to bear, but this was nothing.", "이것만 해도 견디기 힘든 일이었지만, 이건 아무것도 아니었다."], ["I had not advanced another two hundred yards when, to my inexpressible terror, amazement, and indignation, I again beheld Trabb’s boy approaching.", "채 이백 야드도 더 가지 못했을 때, 이루 말할 수 없는 공포와 경악과 분노 속에서, 나는 또다시 트래브네 점원 녀석이 다가오는 것을 보았다."], ["He was coming round a narrow corner.", "녀석은 좁은 모퉁이를 돌아 나오고 있었다."], ["His blue bag was slung over his shoulder, honest industry beamed in his eyes, a determination to proceed to Trabb’s with cheerful briskness was indicated in his gait.", "파란 가방을 어깨에 둘러메고, 두 눈에는 정직한 근면이 빛났으며, 발걸음에는 트래브네 가게로 기운차고 활기차게 가겠다는 결의가 어려 있었다."], ["With a shock he became aware of me, and was severely visited as before; but this time his motion was rotatory, and he staggered round and round me with knees more afflicted, and with uplifted hands as if beseeching for mercy.", "녀석은 화들짝 놀라며 나를 알아보고는 앞서와 똑같이 호되게 발작을 일으켰는데, 이번에는 그 동작이 회전식이어서, 더욱 가련하게 휘청대는 무릎으로 내 둘레를 빙글빙글 돌며, 두 손을 치켜들고 마치 자비를 비는 듯한 시늉을 했다."], ["His sufferings were hailed with the greatest joy by a knot of spectators, and I felt utterly confounded.", "녀석의 고통은 한 무리의 구경꾼들에게 더없는 환호로 맞아들여졌고, 나는 완전히 어쩔 줄을 몰랐다."]], [["I had not got as much further down the street as the post-office, when I again beheld Trabb’s boy shooting round by a back way.", "거리를 따라 우체국까지도 채 못 갔을 때, 나는 또다시 트래브네 점원 녀석이 뒷길로 쏜살같이 돌아 나오는 것을 보았다."], ["This time, he was entirely changed.", "이번에 녀석은 완전히 딴판으로 변해 있었다."], ["He wore the blue bag in the manner of my great-coat, and was strutting along the pavement towards me on the opposite side of the street, attended by a company of delighted young friends to whom he from time to time exclaimed, with a wave of his hand, “Don’t know yah!”", "파란 가방을 내 외투인 양 걸치고서, 길 건너편 인도를 따라 내 쪽으로 거들먹거리며 걸어왔는데, 신이 난 어린 친구들 한 무리를 거느리고 이따금 그들을 향해 손을 휘저으며 \"난 저 사람 몰라요!\" 하고 외쳤다."], ["Words cannot state the amount of aggravation and injury wreaked upon me by Trabb’s boy, when passing abreast of me, he pulled up his shirt-collar, twined his side-hair, stuck an arm akimbo, and smirked extravagantly by, wriggling his elbows and body, and drawling to his attendants, “Don’t know yah, don’t know yah, ’pon my soul don’t know yah!”", "트래브네 점원 녀석이 내 옆을 나란히 지나가며 셔츠 깃을 세우고, 옆머리를 배배 꼬고, 한 팔을 허리에 척 짚고, 팔꿈치와 몸뚱이를 흐느적흐느적 흔들며 요란하게 능글맞은 웃음을 흘리고는, 거느린 무리에게 \"난 저 사람 몰라, 난 저 사람 몰라, 맹세코 난 저 사람 몰라!\" 하고 느릿느릿 내뱉었을 때 내가 받은 모욕과 상처의 크기란, 말로는 도저히 다 표현할 수 없다."], ["The disgrace attendant on his immediately afterwards taking to crowing and pursuing me across the bridge with crows, as from an exceedingly dejected fowl who had known me when I was a blacksmith, culminated the disgrace with which I left the town, and was, so to speak, ejected by it into the open country.", "그러고 나서 녀석이 곧장 닭 우는 소리를 내기 시작하더니, 내가 대장장이였던 시절을 알던 어느 몹시 풀 죽은 닭이라도 되는 양 닭 울음으로 다리 건너까지 나를 쫓아오던 그 망신스러움은, 내가 그 고장을 떠나며 당한 망신의 절정이었고, 말하자면 나는 그렇게 그 고장에서 내쫓겨 들판으로 밀려난 셈이었다."]], [["But unless I had taken the life of Trabb’s boy on that occasion, I really do not even now see what I could have done save endure.", "그러나 그때 내가 트래브네 점원 녀석의 목숨을 끊어 놓기라도 했다면 모를까, 참고 견디는 것 외에 달리 무엇을 할 수 있었을지 지금도 나로서는 정말 알 수가 없다."], ["To have struggled with him in the street, or to have exacted any lower recompense from him than his heart’s best blood, would have been futile and degrading.", "길거리에서 녀석과 몸싸움을 벌이거나, 녀석의 심장에서 가장 좋은 피보다 못한 어떤 보상이라도 받아 내려 했다면, 그것은 헛되고 품위를 떨어뜨리는 일이었으리라."], ["Moreover, he was a boy whom no man could hurt; an invulnerable and dodging serpent who, when chased into a corner, flew out again between his captor’s legs, scornfully yelping. I wrote, however, to Mr.", "게다가 녀석은 누구도 해칠 수 없는 아이였다. 무적의, 요리조리 잘도 빠져나가는 뱀 같은 놈이어서, 구석으로 몰리면 잡으려는 자의 두 다리 사이로 다시 빠져나가며 비웃듯 캥캥거리는 것이었다."], ["Trabb by next day’s post, to say that Mr. Pip must decline to deal further with one who could so far forget what he owed to the best interests of society, as to employ a boy who excited Loathing in every respectable mind.", "그렇지만 나는 이튿날 우편으로 트래브 씨에게 편지를 보내, 사회의 최선의 이익에 대해 자신이 짊어진 책무를 그토록 망각한 나머지, 점잖은 사람이라면 누구나 혐오감을 느낄 만한 점원을 부리는 자와는 핍 씨가 더 이상 거래를 하지 않겠노라고 전했다."]], [["The coach, with Mr. Jaggers inside, came up in due time, and I took my box-seat again, and arrived in London safe,—but not sound, for my heart was gone.", "재거스 씨를 태운 마차가 때맞춰 당도했고, 나는 다시 마부석 자리에 올라 무사히 런던에 도착했다. 그러나 온전한 상태는 아니었다."], ["As soon as I arrived, I sent a penitential codfish and barrel of oysters to Joe (as reparation for not having gone myself), and then went on to Barnard’s Inn.", "내 마음이 떠나 버렸으니까. 도착하자마자 나는 참회의 뜻으로 대구 한 마리와 굴 한 통을 조에게 보낸 뒤(직접 찾아가지 못한 데 대한 보상이었다), 바너드 여관으로 향했다."]], [["I found Herbert dining on cold meat, and delighted to welcome me back.", "가 보니 허버트는 냉육으로 저녁 식사를 하고 있다가, 돌아온 나를 반갑게 맞아 주었다."], ["Having despatched The Avenger to the coffee-house for an addition to the dinner, I felt that I must open my breast that very evening to my friend and chum.", "어벤저를 커피 하우스로 보내 저녁 식사에 음식을 좀 더 보태도록 시켜 놓은 뒤, 나는 바로 그날 저녁 내 벗이자 친구에게 가슴속을 털어놓아야겠다는 마음이 들었다."], ["As confidence was out of the question with The Avenger in the hall, which could merely be regarded in the light of an antechamber to the keyhole, I sent him to the Play.", "그런데 어벤저가 현관에 있어서는 속내를 터놓을 수가 없었으니, 그 현관이란 열쇠 구멍으로 이어지는 대기실쯤으로 여길 만한 곳이었기 때문이다. 그래서 나는 녀석을 연극 구경이나 가라고 내보냈다."], ["A better proof of the severity of my bondage to that taskmaster could scarcely be afforded, than the degrading shifts to which I was constantly driven to find him employment.", "그 부려 먹는 상전에게 내가 얼마나 심하게 매여 있었는지를, 녀석에게 일거리를 마련해 주려고 끊임없이 짜내야 했던 그 한심한 궁리들보다 더 잘 보여 주는 증거는 없으리라."], ["So mean is extremity, that I sometimes sent him to Hyde Park corner to see what o’clock it was.", "막다른 처지란 어찌나 옹색한지, 나는 때때로 녀석을 하이드 파크 모퉁이까지 보내 지금 몇 시인지 보고 오라고 시키기까지 했다."]], [["Dinner done and we sitting with our feet upon the fender, I said to Herbert, “My dear Herbert, I have something very particular to tell you.”", "저녁 식사가 끝나고 우리가 난로 받침대에 발을 올린 채 앉아 있을 때, 나는 허버트에게 말했다. \"이보게, 허버트, 자네한테 아주 특별히 할 이야기가 있네.\""]], [["“My dear Handel,”", "\"이보게, 헨델.\""], ["he returned, “I shall esteem and respect your confidence.”", "그가 대답했다. \"자네가 털어놓는 비밀을 소중히 여기고 존중하겠네.\""]], [["“It concerns myself, Herbert,”", "\"내 자신에 관한 일이야, 허버트.\" 내가 말했다."], ["said I, “and one other person.”", "\"그리고 또 한 사람에 관한 일이지.\""]], [["Herbert crossed his feet, looked at the fire with his head on one side, and having looked at it in vain for some time, looked at me because I didn’t go on.", "허버트는 발을 꼬고, 고개를 한쪽으로 기울인 채 불을 바라보았으나, 한동안 헛되이 불만 들여다보다가, 내가 말을 잇지 않자 나를 쳐다보았다."]], [["“Herbert,”", "\"허버트.\""], ["said I, laying my hand upon his knee, “I love—I adore—Estella.”", "나는 그의 무릎에 손을 얹으며 말했다. \"나는 에스텔러를 사랑해. 흠모하고 있어.\""]], [["Instead of being transfixed, Herbert replied in an easy matter-of-course way, “Exactly. Well?”", "허버트는 깜짝 놀라 굳어지기는커녕, 아주 태연하고 당연하다는 듯이 대답했다. \"그렇군. 그래서?\""]], [["“Well, Herbert?", "\"그래서라니, 허버트?"], ["Is that all you say?", "자네가 할 말이 그게 다인가?"], ["Well?”", "그래서라니?\""]], [["“What next, I mean?”", "\"그러니까 그다음은 뭐냐는 말이야.\""], ["said Herbert.", "허버트가 말했다."], ["“Of course I know that.”", "\"물론 나도 그건 알고 있으니까.\""]], [["“How do you know it?” said I.", "\"그걸 어떻게 알았나?\" 내가 말했다."]], [["“How do I know it, Handel?", "\"그걸 어떻게 알았느냐고, 헨델?"], ["Why, from you.” “I never told you.”", "아니, 자네한테서 알았지.\""]], [["“Told me! You have never told me when you have got your hair cut, but I have had senses to perceive it.", "\"난 자네한테 말한 적이 없는데.\" \"말이라니!"], ["You have always adored her, ever since I have known you. You brought your adoration and your portmanteau here together.", "자네는 머리를 언제 잘랐는지도 내게 말한 적이 없지만, 나는 그걸 알아챌 만한 감각은 있다네. 자네는 내가 알게 된 이래로 줄곧 그 여자를 흠모해 왔어."], ["Told me! Why, you have always told me all day long.", "자네는 흠모하는 마음과 여행 가방을 함께 이리로 가져왔던 거야. 말이라니!"], ["When you told me your own story, you told me plainly that you began adoring her the first time you saw her, when you were very young indeed.”", "아니, 자네는 하루 종일 줄곧 내게 말하고 있었네. 자네가 자기 살아온 이야기를 들려줄 때, 아주 어릴 적 그 여자를 처음 본 순간부터 흠모하기 시작했다고 분명히 말했잖나.\""]], [["“Very well, then,”", "\"그렇다면 좋아.\""], ["said I, to whom this was a new and not unwelcome light, “I have never left off adoring her.", "이것이 내게는 새롭고도 싫지 않은 깨달음이었던 까닭에 나는 말했다. \"나는 한 번도 그녀를 흠모하기를 멈춘 적이 없어."], ["And she has come back, a most beautiful and most elegant creature.", "그리고 그녀가 더없이 아름답고 더없이 우아한 여인이 되어 돌아왔지."], ["And I saw her yesterday.", "나는 어제 그녀를 보았어."], ["And if I adored her before, I now doubly adore her.”", "전에도 그녀를 흠모했다면, 이제는 곱절로 흠모하고 있네.\""]], [["“Lucky for you then, Handel,”", "\"그렇다면 자네한테는 행운이로군, 헨델.\""], ["said Herbert, “that you are picked out for her and allotted to her.", "허버트가 말했다. \"자네가 그녀를 위해 점지되고 그녀에게 배정된 셈이니 말이야."], ["Without encroaching on forbidden ground, we may venture to say that there can be no doubt between ourselves of that fact.", "금지된 영역을 침범하지 않더라도, 우리끼리라면 그 사실에 의심의 여지가 없다고 감히 말해도 좋겠지."], ["Have you any idea yet, of Estella’s views on the adoration question?”", "그런데 자네는 그 흠모 문제에 대한 에스텔러의 생각이 어떤지 아직 짐작이라도 하고 있나?\""]], [["I shook my head gloomily. “Oh!", "나는 침울하게 고개를 저었다. \"아!"], ["She is thousands of miles away, from me,” said I.", "그녀는 나한테서 수천 마일은 떨어져 있어.\" 내가 말했다."]], [["“Patience, my dear Handel: time enough, time enough.", "\"참게나, 이보게 헨델. 시간은 충분해, 시간은 충분하다고."], ["But you have something more to say?”", "그런데 자네, 더 할 말이 있는 게로군?\""]], [["“I am ashamed to say it,”", "\"말하기가 부끄럽네.\" 내가 대답했다."], ["I returned, “and yet it’s no worse to say it than to think it.", "\"하지만 그걸 말하는 게 마음에 품는 것보다 더 나쁠 것도 없겠지."], ["You call me a lucky fellow.", "자네는 나를 운 좋은 친구라고 했지."], ["Of course, I am.", "물론 나는 그래."], ["I was a blacksmith’s boy but yesterday; I am—what shall I say I am—to-day?”", "어제까지만 해도 나는 대장장이의 조수 아이였는데, 오늘은 — 오늘의 나는 뭐라고 해야 할까?\""]], [["“Say a good fellow, if you want a phrase,”", "\"굳이 한마디로 표현하고 싶다면, 좋은 친구라고 해 두게.\""], ["returned Herbert, smiling, and clapping his hand on the back of mine—“a good fellow, with impetuosity and hesitation, boldness and diffidence, action and dreaming, curiously mixed in him.”", "허버트가 미소를 지으며 내 손등을 제 손으로 토닥이며 대답했다. \"좋은 친구, 그 안에 격정과 망설임, 대담함과 소심함, 행동과 몽상이 묘하게 뒤섞여 있는 친구 말이야.\""]], [["I stopped for a moment to consider whether there really was this mixture in my character.", "나는 잠시 멈추어 정말로 내 성격에 그런 혼합이 들어 있는지 곰곰이 생각해 보았다."], ["On the whole, I by no means recognised the analysis, but thought it not worth disputing.", "대체로 나는 그 분석을 결코 인정할 수 없었으나, 굳이 따져 볼 가치는 없다고 여겼다."]], [["“When I ask what I am to call myself to-day, Herbert,”", "\"오늘의 나를 뭐라 불러야 하느냐고 물은 건, 허버트.\" 나는 말을 이었다."], ["I went on, “I suggest what I have in my thoughts. You say I am lucky.", "\"내 생각 속에 있는 바를 넌지시 비치려는 거였어. 자네는 내가 운이 좋다고 하지."], ["I know I have done nothing to raise myself in life, and that Fortune alone has raised me; that is being very lucky.", "나도 알아, 내가 스스로 입신하기 위해 한 일이라곤 아무것도 없고, 오로지 운명만이 나를 끌어올렸다는 걸. 그게 바로 대단히 운이 좋다는 거지."], ["And yet, when I think of Estella—”", "그런데도 에스텔러를 생각하면—\""]], [["(“And when don’t you, you know?” Herbert threw in, with his eyes on the fire; which I thought kind and sympathetic of him.)", "(\"그런데 자네가 그녀를 생각하지 않을 때가 언제 있나?\" 허버트가 불에 시선을 둔 채 한마디 끼워 넣었는데, 나는 그것이 그의 다정하고 공감 어린 마음이라고 생각했다.)"]], [["“—Then, my dear Herbert, I cannot tell you how dependent and uncertain I feel, and how exposed to hundreds of chances.", "\"—그러면, 이보게 허버트, 내가 얼마나 의존적이고 불안정하게 느끼는지, 얼마나 수백 가지 우연에 내맡겨져 있다고 느끼는지 자네한테 말로는 다 할 수가 없네."], ["Avoiding forbidden ground, as you did just now, I may still say that on the constancy of one person (naming no person) all my expectations depend.", "방금 자네가 그랬듯 금지된 영역을 피해서 말하더라도, 내 모든 기대가 어느 한 사람의 변함없는 마음에(누구라고 이름은 대지 않겠네) 달려 있다고는 말할 수 있겠지."], ["And at the best, how indefinite and unsatisfactory, only to know so vaguely what they are!”", "그런데 잘되어 봤자, 그 기대가 무엇인지 이토록 어렴풋이밖에 모른다는 게 얼마나 막연하고 불만스러운 일인지!\""], ["In saying this, I relieved my mind of what had always been there, more or less, though no doubt most since yesterday.", "이렇게 말하면서 나는, 어제 이후로 가장 심했던 것이 분명하긴 해도 늘 어느 정도는 거기 자리 잡고 있던 그 마음의 짐을 덜어 냈다."]], [["“Now, Handel,”", "\"이보게, 헨델.\""], ["Herbert replied, in his gay, hopeful way, “it seems to me that in the despondency of the tender passion, we are looking into our gift-horse’s mouth with a magnifying-glass.", "허버트가 그 특유의 명랑하고 희망찬 어조로 대답했다. \"내가 보기엔 우리가 이 애틋한 연정에 풀이 죽은 나머지, 거저 받은 말의 입속을 돋보기로 들여다보고 있는 격이야."], ["Likewise, it seems to me that, concentrating our attention on the examination, we altogether overlook one of the best points of the animal.", "게다가 내가 보기엔, 그렇게 그 검사에만 정신을 쏟느라, 우리가 그 말이 지닌 가장 좋은 점 하나를 통째로 놓치고 있는 것 같단 말이지."], ["Didn’t you tell me that your guardian, Mr. Jaggers, told you in the beginning, that you were not endowed with expectations only?", "자네 후견인 재거스 씨가 애당초 자네한테, 자네가 기대만을 물려받은 게 아니라고 말했다지 않았나?"], ["And even if he had not told you so,—though that is a very large If, I grant,—could you believe that of all men in London, Mr. Jaggers is the man to hold his present relations towards you unless he were sure of his ground?”", "설령 그가 그런 말을 하지 않았다 하더라도—물론 그게 아주 큰 가정이라는 건 인정하네만—런던의 모든 사람들 가운데 하필 재거스 씨가, 자기 발판이 확실하지도 않은데 자네에게 지금과 같은 관계를 유지할 그런 사람이라고 자네는 믿을 수 있겠나?\""]], [["I said I could not deny that this was a strong point.", "나는 그것이 강력한 논점임을 부인할 수 없다고 말했다."], ["I said it (people often do so, in such cases) like a rather reluctant concession to truth and justice;—as if I wanted to deny it!", "나는 그 말을(사람들은 이런 경우 흔히 그러는 법인데), 진실과 정의에 마지못해 양보하기라도 하듯 내뱉었다. 마치 그것을 부인하고 싶기라도 한 것처럼!"]], [["“I should think it was a strong point,”", "\"내 생각에도 그건 강력한 논점이야.\""], ["said Herbert, “and I should think you would be puzzled to imagine a stronger; as to the rest, you must bide your guardian’s time, and he must bide his client’s time.", "허버트가 말했다. \"그리고 자네가 이보다 더 강력한 논점을 떠올리려야 떠올릴 수 없으리라 생각하네. 그 나머지는, 자네가 후견인의 때를 기다려야 하고, 그 후견인은 또 자기 의뢰인의 때를 기다려야 하는 거지."], ["You’ll be one-and-twenty before you know where you are, and then perhaps you’ll get some further enlightenment.", "자네는 어느새 스물한 살이 될 테고, 그때쯤이면 아마 무언가 더 환히 알게 될 걸세."], ["At all events, you’ll be nearer getting it, for it must come at last.”", "어쨌든 거기에 더 가까이 다가가게 될 거야. 결국엔 반드시 밝혀질 테니까.\""]], [["“What a hopeful disposition you have!”", "\"자네는 어쩌면 그리도 희망에 찬 기질을 지녔나!\""], ["said I, gratefully admiring his cheery ways.", "나는 그의 쾌활한 태도에 고마워하며 감탄하여 말했다."]], [["“I ought to have,”", "\"그래야지.\""], ["said Herbert, “for I have not much else.", "허버트가 말했다. \"달리 가진 게 별로 없으니까."], ["I must acknowledge, by the by, that the good sense of what I have just said is not my own, but my father’s.", "그런데 말이 나온 김에 인정하자면, 방금 내가 한 말의 분별 있는 부분은 내 것이 아니라 우리 아버지의 것이야."], ["The only remark I ever heard him make on your story, was the final one, “The thing is settled and done, or Mr. Jaggers would not be in it.”", "자네 사연에 대해 아버지가 하시는 말씀을 내가 들은 건 딱 한 번, 마지막에 하신 이 말뿐이었네. '그 일은 이미 정해지고 매듭지어진 거다."], ["And now before I say anything more about my father, or my father’s son, and repay confidence with confidence, I want to make myself seriously disagreeable to you for a moment,—positively repulsive.”", "안 그랬다면 재거스 씨가 거기 끼어 있을 리 없으니까.' 그런데 이제 우리 아버지나 그 아버지의 아들에 대해 더 이야기하고, 비밀에 비밀로 보답하기에 앞서, 잠시 자네한테 진지하게 못마땅한 사람이 되어 보려 하네. 아주 정나미가 떨어지는 사람으로 말이지.\""]], [["“You won’t succeed,” said I.", "\"성공하지 못할걸.\" 내가 말했다."]], [["“O yes I shall!”", "\"아니, 성공할 거야!\""], ["said he. “One, two, three, and now I am in for it.", "그가 말했다. \"하나, 둘, 셋, 자 이제 시작이네."], ["Handel, my good fellow;”—though he spoke in this light tone, he was very much in earnest,—“I have been thinking since we have been talking with our feet on this fender, that Estella surely cannot be a condition of your inheritance, if she was never referred to by your guardian.", "헨델, 이 사람아.\" 비록 그가 이렇게 가벼운 어조로 말하긴 했어도, 그는 무척 진지했다. \"우리가 이 난로 받침대에 발을 올리고 이야기를 나누는 동안 줄곧 생각해 봤는데, 자네 후견인이 에스텔러를 한 번도 언급하지 않았다면, 그녀가 자네 상속의 조건일 리는 결코 없을 것 같단 말이지."], ["Am I right in so understanding what you have told me, as that he never referred to her, directly or indirectly, in any way?", "그러니까 자네가 한 말을, 그가 그녀를 직접적으로든 간접적으로든 그 어떤 식으로도 결코 언급하지 않았다는 뜻으로 내가 이해하는 게 옳은가?"], ["Never even hinted, for instance, that your patron might have views as to your marriage ultimately?”", "이를테면 자네의 후원자가 궁극적으로 자네의 혼사에 대해 어떤 뜻을 품고 있을지도 모른다는 것조차, 단 한 번도 내비치지 않았다는 거지?\""]], [["“Never.”", "\"한 번도.\""]], [["“Now, Handel, I am quite free from the flavour of sour grapes, upon my soul and honour!", "\"이보게, 헨델, 내 영혼과 명예를 걸고 맹세하지만, 나는 신 포도 같은 심사에서는 완전히 자유롭다네!"], ["Not being bound to her, can you not detach yourself from her?—I told you I should be disagreeable.”", "자네가 그녀에게 매여 있는 것도 아닌데, 그녀에게서 마음을 떼어 낼 수는 없겠나?—내가 못마땅한 사람이 되겠다고 했잖나.\""]], [["I turned my head aside, for, with a rush and a sweep, like the old marsh winds coming up from the sea, a feeling like that which had subdued me on the morning when I left the forge, when the mists were solemnly rising, and when I laid my hand upon the village finger-post, smote upon my heart again.", "나는 고개를 돌렸다. 바다에서 밀려 올라오던 그 옛 습지의 바람처럼 한 줄기 감정이 거세게 휘몰아쳐, 안개가 엄숙하게 피어오르던 아침, 내가 대장간을 떠나 마을 이정표에 손을 얹었을 때 나를 무너뜨렸던 바로 그것과 똑같은 감정이, 다시금 내 가슴을 후려쳤기 때문이다."], ["There was silence between us for a little while.", "잠시 우리 사이에 침묵이 흘렀다."]], [["“Yes; but my dear Handel,”", "\"그래, 하지만 이보게 헨델.\""], ["Herbert went on, as if we had been talking, instead of silent, “its having been so strongly rooted in the breast of a boy whom nature and circumstances made so romantic, renders it very serious.", "허버트는 우리가 침묵하고 있었던 게 아니라 계속 이야기를 나누고 있었던 것처럼 말을 이었다. \"그게 천성과 환경 탓에 그토록 낭만적인 기질을 지니게 된 한 소년의 가슴속에 그토록 깊이 뿌리내렸다는 사실이, 이 일을 아주 심각하게 만드는 거야."], ["Think of her bringing-up, and think of Miss Havisham.", "그녀가 어떻게 자라났는지 생각해 보게, 그리고 미스 해비셤을 생각해 보게."], ["Think of what she is herself (now I am repulsive and you abominate me).", "그녀 자신이 어떤 사람인지 생각해 보란 말이야(이제 나는 정나미 떨어지는 사람이 되었고 자네는 나를 혐오하겠지)."], ["This may lead to miserable things.”", "이건 비참한 결말로 이어질 수도 있어.\""]], [["“I know it, Herbert,”", "\"나도 알아, 허버트.\""], ["said I, with my head still turned away, “but I can’t help it.”", "나는 여전히 고개를 돌린 채 말했다. \"하지만 어쩔 수가 없어.\""]], [["“You can’t detach yourself?”", "\"마음을 떼어 낼 수가 없나?\""]], [["“No. Impossible!”", "\"없어. 불가능해!\""]], [["“You can’t try, Handel?”", "\"애써 볼 수도 없겠나, 헨델?\""]], [["“No. Impossible!”", "\"없어. 불가능해!\""]], [["“Well!”", "\"좋아!\""], ["said Herbert, getting up with a lively shake as if he had been asleep, and stirring the fire, “now I’ll endeavour to make myself agreeable again!”", "허버트는 마치 잠에서 깬 사람처럼 활기차게 몸을 한 번 흔들며 일어나 불을 쑤석이며 말했다. \"이제 다시 유쾌한 사람이 되어 보도록 애써 봐야겠군!\""]], [["So he went round the room and shook the curtains out, put the chairs in their places, tidied the books and so forth that were lying about, looked into the hall, peeped into the letter-box, shut the door, and came back to his chair by the fire: where he sat down, nursing his left leg in both arms.", "그러고서 그는 방 안을 돌아다니며 커튼을 털고, 의자를 제자리에 놓고, 여기저기 흩어져 있던 책 따위를 정돈하고, 현관을 들여다보고, 우편함을 살짝 엿보고, 문을 닫고서, 난롯가의 자기 의자로 돌아왔다. 그러고는 자리에 앉아 두 팔로 자신의 왼쪽 다리를 끌어안았다."]], [["“I was going to say a word or two, Handel, concerning my father and my father’s son.", "\"우리 아버지와 그 아버지의 아들에 관해 두어 마디 하려던 참이었지, 헨델."], ["I am afraid it is scarcely necessary for my father’s son to remark that my father’s establishment is not particularly brilliant in its housekeeping.”", "우리 아버지의 살림이 그 가정 운영에 있어 그다지 훌륭하지는 못하다는 것쯤은, 그 아버지의 아들이 굳이 말할 필요도 없을 것 같네만.\""]], [["“There is always plenty, Herbert,”", "\"늘 풍족하던걸, 허버트.\""], ["said I, to say something encouraging.", "나는 무언가 격려가 될 말을 하려고 말했다."]], [["“O yes! and so the dustman says, I believe, with the strongest approval, and so does the marine-store shop in the back street.", "\"오, 그렇지! 내 생각에 쓰레기 치우는 사람도 더없이 흡족해하며 그렇게 말할 테고, 뒷골목의 잡화 가게도 그럴 거야."], ["Gravely, Handel, for the subject is grave enough, you know how it is as well as I do.", "진지하게 말해서, 헨델, 이건 제법 진지한 문제이니 하는 말인데, 자네도 나만큼이나 사정을 잘 알지 않나."], ["I suppose there was a time once when my father had not given matters up; but if ever there was, the time is gone.", "한때는 우리 아버지가 살림을 내팽개치지 않으셨던 시절이 있었던 것 같아. 하지만 설령 그런 시절이 있었다 한들, 그 시절은 이미 지나갔어."], ["May I ask you if you have ever had an opportunity of remarking, down in your part of the country, that the children of not exactly suitable marriages are always most particularly anxious to be married?”", "자네한테 한 가지 물어봐도 될까. 자네 고향에서, 그다지 어울리지 않는 혼인으로 태어난 자식들이 하나같이 유난히 결혼을 하고 싶어 안달하더라는 걸, 혹시 눈여겨본 적이 있나?\""]], [["This was such a singular question, that I asked him in return, “Is it so?”", "이것이 어찌나 묘한 질문이던지, 나는 도리어 그에게 되물었다. \"그런가?\""]], [["“I don’t know,”", "\"나도 모르겠네.\""], ["said Herbert, “that’s what I want to know.", "허버트가 말했다. \"그게 바로 내가 알고 싶은 거야."], ["Because it is decidedly the case with us.", "왜냐하면 우리 집은 확실히 그러하거든."], ["My poor sister Charlotte, who was next me and died before she was fourteen, was a striking example.", "가엾은 내 누이 샬럿은, 내 바로 아래였는데 열네 살도 되기 전에 죽었지만, 그야말로 두드러진 예였지."], ["Little Jane is the same.", "어린 제인도 마찬가지야."], ["In her desire to be matrimonially established, you might suppose her to have passed her short existence in the perpetual contemplation of domestic bliss.", "혼인으로 자리 잡고 싶어 하는 그 열망을 보면, 그 짧은 생애를 줄곧 가정의 행복을 마음에 그리며 보냈다고 여겨질 정도라니까."], ["Little Alick in a frock has already made arrangements for his union with a suitable young person at Kew.", "아직 치마저고리를 입고 다니는 어린 알릭조차도 벌써 큐에 사는 어울리는 처녀와 짝지을 채비를 다 해 놓았다네."], ["And indeed, I think we are all engaged, except the baby.” “Then you are?” said I.", "사실 막내 갓난아기만 빼면 우리는 다들 약혼한 셈인 것 같아.\""]], [["“I am,” said Herbert; “but it’s a secret.”", "\"그럼 자네도 약혼했단 말인가?\" 내가 말했다."]], [["I assured him of my keeping the secret, and begged to be favoured with further particulars.", "\"그렇다네.\" 허버트가 말했다. \"하지만 비밀이야.\""], ["He had spoken so sensibly and feelingly of my weakness that I wanted to know something about his strength.", "나는 그 비밀을 지키겠노라고 그를 안심시키고, 좀 더 자세한 사정을 들려주기를 청했다. 그가 내 약점에 대해 그토록 분별 있고 다정하게 말해 주었기에, 나는 그의 강점에 대해서도 무언가 알고 싶었다."]], [["“May I ask the name?” I said.", "\"이름을 물어봐도 될까?\" 내가 말했다."]], [["“Name of Clara,”", "\"이름은 클래라라네.\""], ["said Herbert.", "허버트가 말했다."]], [["“Live in London?”", "\"런던에 사나?\""]], [["“Yes, perhaps I ought to mention,”", "\"그래, 어쩌면 이 말은 해 둬야겠군.\""], ["said Herbert, who had become curiously crestfallen and meek, since we entered on the interesting theme, “that she is rather below my mother’s nonsensical family notions.", "흥미로운 화제로 접어든 뒤로 묘하게 풀이 죽고 고분고분해진 허버트가 말했다. \"그녀는 우리 어머니의 그 터무니없는 가문 관념에 비추면 다소 격이 낮은 편이야."], ["Her father had to do with the victualling of passenger-ships.", "그녀 아버지는 여객선의 식량 조달과 관련된 일을 했지."], ["I think he was a species of purser.”", "일종의 사무장 같은 사람이었던 것 같아.\""]], [["“What is he now?”", "\"지금은 무슨 일을 하시나?\""], ["said I.", "내가 말했다."]], [["“He’s an invalid now,”", "\"지금은 병자라네.\""], ["replied Herbert.", "허버트가 대답했다."]], [["“Living on—?”", "\"무엇으로 살아가시는데?\""]], [["“On the first floor,”", "\"이 층에서 살고 계시지.\""], ["said Herbert.", "허버트가 말했다."], ["Which was not at all what I meant, for I had intended my question to apply to his means.", "내가 의도한 바는 전혀 그게 아니었으니, 내 질문은 그의 생계 수단을 두고 한 것이었기 때문이다."], ["“I have never seen him, for he has always kept his room overhead, since I have known Clara.", "\"나는 그분을 한 번도 본 적이 없네. 내가 클래라를 알게 된 이래로 그분은 줄곧 위층 자기 방에만 틀어박혀 계셨으니까."], ["But I have heard him constantly.", "하지만 그분 소리는 끊임없이 들었지."], ["He makes tremendous rows,—roars, and pegs at the floor with some frightful instrument.”", "무시무시한 소란을 피우신다네. 고래고래 소리를 지르고, 뭔가 끔찍한 도구로 바닥을 쾅쾅 두드리지.\""], ["In looking at me and then laughing heartily, Herbert for the time recovered his usual lively manner.", "나를 바라보다가 호탕하게 웃으면서, 허버트는 잠시나마 평소의 명랑한 태도를 되찾았다."]], [["“Don’t you expect to see him?”", "\"그분을 만나게 될 거라고 기대하지는 않나?\""], ["said I.", "내가 말했다."]], [["“O yes, I constantly expect to see him,”", "\"오, 그럼, 나는 끊임없이 그분을 만나게 되리라 기대하지.\""], ["returned Herbert, “because I never hear him, without expecting him to come tumbling through the ceiling.", "허버트가 대답했다. \"그분 소리를 들을 때마다 천장을 뚫고 와르르 떨어져 내리시지 않을까 하는 기대를 안 한 적이 없으니까."], ["But I don’t know how long the rafters may hold.”", "다만 그 서까래가 얼마나 더 버텨 줄지는 모르겠네만.\""]], [["When he had once more laughed heartily, he became meek again, and told me that the moment he began to realise Capital, it was his intention to marry this young lady.", "또 한 번 호탕하게 웃고 나서 그는 다시 고분고분해지더니, 자본을 좀 모으기 시작하는 순간 이 아가씨와 결혼할 작정이라고 내게 말했다."], ["He added as a self-evident proposition, engendering low spirits, “But you can’t marry, you know, while you’re looking about you.”", "그러고는 풀 죽게 만드는 자명한 명제라도 되는 양 이렇게 덧붙였다. \"하지만 알다시피, 이리저리 기회를 엿보고 있는 동안에는 결혼이란 걸 할 수가 없는 법이지.\""]], [["As we contemplated the fire, and as I thought what a difficult vision to realise this same Capital sometimes was, I put my hands in my pockets.", "우리가 불을 바라보고 있는 동안, 그리고 바로 그 자본이라는 것이 때로는 얼마나 실현하기 어려운 환상인가를 내가 생각하고 있는 동안, 나는 주머니에 손을 찔러 넣었다."], ["A folded piece of paper in one of them attracting my attention, I opened it and found it to be the play-bill I had received from Joe, relative to the celebrated provincial amateur of Roscian renown.", "그중 한쪽 주머니에 든 접힌 종잇조각이 눈길을 끌기에, 나는 그것을 펴 보았고, 그것이 조에게서 받은 연극 광고지, 그러니까 그 명성 자자한 로스키우스풍의 지방 아마추어 배우에 관한 광고지임을 알았다."], ["“And bless my heart,”", "\"아니, 이런 세상에.\""], ["I involuntarily added aloud, “it’s to-night!”", "나는 나도 모르게 소리 내어 덧붙였다. \"그게 바로 오늘 밤이잖아!\""]], [["This changed the subject in an instant, and made us hurriedly resolve to go to the play.", "이 일로 화제가 단숨에 바뀌었고, 우리는 부랴부랴 연극을 보러 가기로 마음먹었다."], ["So, when I had pledged myself to comfort and abet Herbert in the affair of his heart by all practicable and impracticable means, and when Herbert had told me that his affianced already knew me by reputation and that I should be presented to her, and when we had warmly shaken hands upon our mutual confidence, we blew out our candles, made up our fire, locked our door, and issued forth in quest of Mr.", "그리하여 내가 허버트의 연애 문제에 있어 가능한 수단이든 불가능한 수단이든 가리지 않고 그를 위로하고 돕겠노라 다짐하고, 허버트가 자기 약혼녀는 이미 소문으로 나를 알고 있으며 내가 그녀에게 소개될 거라고 말하고, 우리가 서로의 신뢰를 두고 뜨겁게 악수를 나누고 나서, 우리는 촛불을 끄고, 난롯불을 돋우고, 문을 잠근 뒤, 워프슬 씨와 덴마크를 찾아 길을 나섰다. * 로스키우스: 고대 로마의 이름난 희극 배우."], ["Wopsle and Denmark.", "명배우를 가리키는 대명사로 쓰인다."]], [["# Chapter XXXI.", "# Chapter XXXI."]], [["On our arrival in Denmark, we found the king and queen of that country elevated in two arm-chairs on a kitchen-table, holding a Court.", "덴마크에 도착해 보니, 그 나라의 왕과 왕비가 부엌 식탁 위에 놓인 두 개의 안락의자에 높이 올라앉아 어전 회의를 열고 있었다."], ["The whole of the Danish nobility were in attendance; consisting of a noble boy in the wash-leather boots of a gigantic ancestor, a venerable Peer with a dirty face who seemed to have risen from the people late in life, and the Danish chivalry with a comb in its hair and a pair of white silk legs, and presenting on the whole a feminine appearance.", "덴마크 귀족 일동이 시중을 들고 있었는데, 그 면면은 이러했다. 거인 같은 조상에게서 물려받은 무두질 가죽 장화를 신은 고귀한 소년 하나, 인생 후반에야 평민에서 출세한 듯 보이는 더러운 얼굴의 위엄 있는 귀족 하나, 그리고 머리에 빗을 꽂고 흰 비단 다리를 한 채 전체적으로 여성스러운 분위기를 풍기는 덴마크 기사도였다."], ["My gifted townsman stood gloomily apart, with folded arms, and I could have wished that his curls and forehead had been more probable.", "재능을 타고난 나의 고향 사람은 팔짱을 끼고 침울하게 한쪽에 떨어져 서 있었는데, 나는 그의 곱슬머리와 이마가 좀 더 그럴듯해 보였으면 하고 바랐다."]], [["Several curious little circumstances transpired as the action proceeded.", "극이 진행되는 동안 몇 가지 신기하고 자잘한 일들이 벌어졌다."], ["The late king of the country not only appeared to have been troubled with a cough at the time of his decease, but to have taken it with him to the tomb, and to have brought it back.", "이 나라의 선왕은 서거할 무렵 기침에 시달리고 있었던 듯할 뿐 아니라, 그 기침을 무덤까지 가지고 갔다가 다시 가지고 돌아온 듯했다."], ["The royal phantom also carried a ghostly manuscript round its truncheon, to which it had the appearance of occasionally referring, and that too, with an air of anxiety and a tendency to lose the place of reference which were suggestive of a state of mortality.", "또 그 왕의 망령은 지휘봉 둘레에 유령의 원고를 지니고 다녔는데, 이따금 그것을 들여다보는 모양새였고, 그것도 불안한 기색으로 어디를 보고 있었는지 곧잘 잊어버리는 품이, 살아 있는 인간의 상태를 떠오르게 했다."], ["It was this, I conceive, which led to the Shade’s being advised by the gallery to “turn over!”—a recommendation which it took extremely ill.", "내 짐작으로는 바로 이 때문에 망령은 위층 관람석으로부터 \"넘겨!\" 하는 충고를 들었을 텐데 — 그 망령은 이 권고를 몹시 언짢게 받아들였다."], ["It was likewise to be noted of this majestic spirit, that whereas it always appeared with an air of having been out a long time and walked an immense distance, it perceptibly came from a closely contiguous wall.", "또 이 장엄한 혼령에 대해 짚어 둘 점은, 언제나 오랫동안 바깥에 나와 있다가 엄청난 거리를 걸어온 듯한 분위기로 등장하면서도, 정작 바로 옆에 붙은 벽에서 나오는 것이 빤히 보였다는 사실이다."], ["This occasioned its terrors to be received derisively.", "이 탓에 그의 공포는 비웃음으로 받아들여졌다."], ["The Queen of Denmark, a very buxom lady, though no doubt historically brazen, was considered by the public to have too much brass about her; her chin being attached to her diadem by a broad band of that metal (as if she had a gorgeous toothache), her waist being encircled by another, and each of her arms by another, so that she was openly mentioned as “the kettle-drum.”", "덴마크 왕비는 매우 풍만한 부인이었는데, 역사적으로야 의심할 바 없이 뻔뻔한 여자였겠으나, 관객들은 그녀가 몸에 놋쇠를 너무 많이 두르고 있다고 여겼다. 그녀의 턱은 (마치 화려한 치통이라도 앓는 듯이) 넓은 놋쇠 띠로 왕관에 붙들어 매여 있었고, 허리도 또 다른 놋쇠 띠로 둘려 있었으며, 두 팔도 각각 또 다른 띠로 감겨 있었으니, 사람들은 대놓고 그녀를 \"큰북\"이라고 불렀다."], ["The noble boy in the ancestral boots was inconsistent, representing himself, as it were in one breath, as an able seaman, a strolling actor, a grave-digger, a clergyman, and a person of the utmost importance at a Court fencing-match, on the authority of whose practised eye and nice discrimination the finest strokes were judged.", "조상의 장화를 신은 고귀한 소년은 일관성이 없었으니, 말하자면 한숨에 자기 자신을 유능한 뱃사람이자, 떠돌이 배우이자, 무덤 파는 인부이자, 성직자이자, 궁정 검술 시합에서 더없이 중요한 인물 — 그 노련한 눈과 정확한 판별력의 권위로 가장 멋진 칼놀림을 가려내는 인물 — 로 내세웠다."], ["This gradually led to a want of toleration for him, and even—on his being detected in holy orders, and declining to perform the funeral service—to the general indignation taking the form of nuts.", "이 때문에 차츰 그에 대한 관용이 사라졌고, 급기야 — 그가 성직에 몸담은 것이 들통나고도 장례식 집전을 거부하자 — 군중의 분노가 호두알 세례의 형태로 터져 나오기에 이르렀다."], ["Lastly, Ophelia was a prey to such slow musical madness, that when, in course of time, she had taken off her white muslin scarf, folded it up, and buried it, a sulky man who had been long cooling his impatient nose against an iron bar in the front row of the gallery, growled, “Now the baby’s put to bed let’s have supper!”", "끝으로, 오필리아는 어찌나 더디게 음악적인 광기에 사로잡혀 있던지, 시간이 한참 흘러 그녀가 흰 모슬린 스카프를 벗어 개켜서 묻고 있을 때, 위층 관람석 맨 앞줄에서 오래도록 안달 난 코를 철창에 갖다 대며 식히고 있던 한 뚱한 사내가 으르렁대듯 말했다. \"이제 아기도 재웠으니 저녁이나 먹읍시다!\""], ["Which, to say the least of it, was out of keeping.", "아무리 좋게 말한대도 그것은 분위기에 어울리지 않는 소리였다."]], [["Upon my unfortunate townsman all these incidents accumulated with playful effect.", "이 모든 사건은 나의 가엾은 고향 사람 위에 장난스러운 효과를 더하며 차곡차곡 쌓여 갔다."], ["Whenever that undecided Prince had to ask a question or state a doubt, the public helped him out with it.", "그 우유부단한 왕자가 무언가를 묻거나 의문을 토로해야 할 때마다, 관객은 그를 거들어 주었다."], ["As for example; on the question whether ’twas nobler in the mind to suffer, some roared yes, and some no, and some inclining to both opinions said “Toss up for it;”", "예컨대, 마음속으로 견디는 것이 더 고귀한가 어떤가 하는 물음이 나오자, 누구는 그렇다고 고함치고 누구는 아니라고 고함쳤으며, 양쪽 의견에 다 솔깃해진 누구는 \"동전 던져서 정해!\""], ["and quite a Debating Society arose. When he asked what should such fellows as he do crawling between earth and heaven, he was encouraged with loud cries of “Hear, hear!”", "하고 외쳤으니, 흡사 토론회 하나가 생겨난 셈이었다. 그가 자기 같은 작자들이 하늘과 땅 사이를 기어 다니며 무엇을 해야 하느냐고 물었을 때, 그는 \"옳소, 옳소!\""], ["When he appeared with his stocking disordered (its disorder expressed, according to usage, by one very neat fold in the top, which I suppose to be always got up with a flat iron), a conversation took place in the gallery respecting the paleness of his leg, and whether it was occasioned by the turn the ghost had given him.", "하는 우렁찬 외침으로 격려를 받았다. 그가 양말을 흐트러뜨린 채 등장했을 때(관례에 따라 그 흐트러짐은 양말 윗부분의 아주 깔끔한 주름 하나로 표현되었는데, 내 짐작으로는 늘 다리미로 잡은 것이리라), 위층 관람석에서는 그의 다리가 창백한 것을 두고, 그것이 유령이 그에게 안겨 준 충격 탓인지 아닌지에 관해 대화가 오갔다."], ["On his taking the recorders,—very like a little black flute that had just been played in the orchestra and handed out at the door,—he was called upon unanimously for Rule Britannia.", "그가 리코더를 집어 들었을 때 — 그것은 방금 오케스트라석에서 연주하다가 문간으로 건네받은 작고 검은 플루트와 매우 흡사했는데 — 관객들은 한목소리로 그에게 \"지배하라 브리타니아\"를 청했다."], ["When he recommended the player not to saw the air thus, the sulky man said, “And don’t you do it, neither; you’re a deal worse than him!”", "그가 배우에게 그런 식으로 허공을 톱질하듯 휘젓지 말라고 충고하자, 그 뚱한 사내가 말했다. \"당신도 그러지 마쇼, 당신이 저 사람보다 훨씬 더 심하니까!\""], ["And I grieve to add that peals of laughter greeted Mr. Wopsle on every one of these occasions.", "그리고 안타깝게도 덧붙여 말하건대, 이런 일이 있을 때마다 웝슬 씨에게는 와르르 터지는 폭소가 쏟아졌다."]], [["But his greatest trials were in the churchyard, which had the appearance of a primeval forest, with a kind of small ecclesiastical wash-house on one side, and a turnpike gate on the other. Mr.", "그러나 그가 가장 혹독한 시련을 겪은 곳은 교회 묘지였으니, 그곳은 한쪽에 작은 교회 부속 세탁장 같은 것이, 다른 쪽에는 통행료 징수 문이 있어, 흡사 태곳적 원시림 같은 모양새였다."], ["Wopsle in a comprehensive black cloak, being descried entering at the turnpike, the gravedigger was admonished in a friendly way, “Look out!", "통이 넓은 검은 망토를 두른 웝슬 씨가 통행료 징수 문으로 들어서는 것이 눈에 띄자, 무덤 파는 인부에게 친절한 충고가 날아들었다. \"조심해!"], ["Here’s the undertaker a coming, to see how you’re a getting on with your work!”", "저기 장의사가 자네 일이 어떻게 돼 가나 보러 오는구먼!\""], ["I believe it is well known in a constitutional country that Mr. Wopsle could not possibly have returned the skull, after moralizing over it, without dusting his fingers on a white napkin taken from his breast; but even that innocent and indispensable action did not pass without the comment, “Wai-ter!”", "헌정 국가에서라면 웝슬 씨가 해골을 두고 일장 훈계를 늘어놓은 뒤, 품에서 꺼낸 흰 냅킨에 손가락의 먼지를 털지 않고서는 도저히 그것을 돌려놓을 수 없었으리라는 것이 익히 알려진 사실이라고 나는 믿는다. 그러나 그 순결하고도 없어서는 안 될 동작조차 \"웨이터!\""], ["The arrival of the body for interment (in an empty black box with the lid tumbling open), was the signal for a general joy, which was much enhanced by the discovery, among the bearers, of an individual obnoxious to identification. The joy attended Mr.", "하는 외침 없이는 지나가지 못했다. 매장할 시신이 (뚜껑이 덜렁덜렁 열린 빈 검은 상자에 담겨) 도착한 것은 온통 환호를 자아내는 신호였는데, 그 환호는 관을 멘 사람들 가운데서 신원을 들켜 미움을 살 만한 인물 하나가 발견되면서 한층 드높아졌다."], ["Wopsle through his struggle with Laertes on the brink of the orchestra and the grave, and slackened no more until he had tumbled the king off the kitchen-table, and had died by inches from the ankles upward.", "그 환호는 오케스트라석과 무덤의 가장자리에서 웝슬 씨가 레어티즈와 격투를 벌이는 내내 따라다녔고, 그가 부엌 식탁에서 왕을 떨어뜨리고 발목부터 위로 차츰차츰 죽어 갈 때까지 조금도 누그러지지 않았다."]], [["We had made some pale efforts in the beginning to applaud Mr. Wopsle; but they were too hopeless to be persisted in.", "처음에 우리는 웝슬 씨에게 박수를 보내려고 맥없이 몇 번 애를 써 보았으나, 그것은 너무도 가망 없는 일이라 계속할 수가 없었다."], ["Therefore we had sat, feeling keenly for him, but laughing, nevertheless, from ear to ear.", "그래서 우리는 그를 절절히 가엾게 여기면서도, 그럼에도 입이 귀에 걸리도록 웃으며 앉아 있었다."], ["I laughed in spite of myself all the time, the whole thing was so droll; and yet I had a latent impression that there was something decidedly fine in Mr.", "나는 내내 나 자신도 어쩌지 못한 채 웃었다. 그 모든 것이 어찌나 우스꽝스러웠던지."], ["Wopsle’s elocution,—not for old associations’ sake, I am afraid, but because it was very slow, very dreary, very uphill and downhill, and very unlike any way in which any man in any natural circumstances of life or death ever expressed himself about anything.", "그러면서도 나는 웝슬 씨의 낭독에 어딘가 단연 훌륭한 데가 있다는 막연한 인상을 품고 있었다 — 옛정 때문은 아닐까 싶어 두렵지만, 사실은 그것이 몹시 느리고, 몹시 음울하고, 몹시 오르락내리락했으며, 삶이나 죽음의 그 어떤 자연스러운 상황에서도 사람이 무언가에 대해 자신을 표현하는 그 어떤 방식과도 몹시 달랐기 때문이었다."], ["When the tragedy was over, and he had been called for and hooted, I said to Herbert, “Let us go at once, or perhaps we shall meet him.”", "비극이 끝나고, 그가 호명을 받고 야유를 받았을 때, 나는 허버트에게 말했다. \"당장 갑시다, 안 그러면 그와 마주칠지도 모르니까요.\""]], [["We made all the haste we could downstairs, but we were not quick enough either.", "우리는 있는 힘껏 서둘러 아래층으로 내려갔으나, 그것마저도 충분히 빠르지 못했다."], ["Standing at the door was a Jewish man with an unnatural heavy smear of eyebrow, who caught my eyes as we advanced, and said, when we came up with him,—", "문간에 부자연스럽게 짙은 눈썹 칠을 한 유대인 사내가 서 있었는데, 우리가 다가가자 그 눈이 내 시선을 붙잡았고, 우리가 가까이 이르자 이렇게 말했다."]], [["“Mr. Pip and friend?”", "\"핍 씨와 친구분이신가요?\""]], [["Identity of Mr. Pip and friend confessed.", "핍 씨와 친구라는 신원을 우리는 시인했다."]], [["“Mr. Waldengarver,”", "\"월든가버 씨께서,\""], ["said the man, “would be glad to have the honour.”", "사내가 말했다. \"두 분을 모시는 영광을 누리고 싶어 하십니다.\""]], [["“Waldengarver?”", "\"월든가버요?\""], ["I repeated—when Herbert murmured in my ear, “Probably Wopsle.”", "내가 되물었다 — 그때 허버트가 내 귀에 속삭였다. \"아마 웝슬일 겁니다.\""]], [["“Oh!”", "\"아!\""], ["said I. “Yes. Shall we follow you?”", "내가 말했다. \"네. 따라가면 됩니까?\""]], [["“A few steps, please.”", "\"몇 걸음만요.\""], ["When we were in a side alley, he turned and asked, “How did you think he looked?—I dressed him.”", "우리가 옆 골목에 들어서자 사내는 돌아서서 물었다. \"그분이 어때 보이던가요? — 제가 의상을 입혀 드렸지요.\""]], [["I don’t know what he had looked like, except a funeral; with the addition of a large Danish sun or star hanging round his neck by a blue ribbon, that had given him the appearance of being insured in some extraordinary Fire Office.", "나는 그가 어때 보였는지 알 수 없었다, 장례식 같다는 것 말고는. 거기에 파란 리본으로 목에 매단 커다란 덴마크 태양인지 별인지가 더해져, 흡사 무슨 별난 화재 보험에라도 든 사람 같은 꼴이었다."], ["But I said he had looked very nice.", "그러나 나는 그가 아주 근사해 보였다고 말했다."]], [["“When he come to the grave,”", "\"그분이 무덤가에 이르렀을 때,\""], ["said our conductor, “he showed his cloak beautiful.", "우리를 안내하던 사내가 말했다."], ["But, judging from the wing, it looked to me that when he see the ghost in the queen’s apartment, he might have made more of his stockings.”", "\"망토를 아주 멋들어지게 보여 주셨지요. 하지만 무대 옆에서 보자니, 왕비의 침실에서 유령을 봤을 때는 양말을 좀 더 잘 살릴 수도 있지 않았나 싶더군요.\""]], [["I modestly assented, and we all fell through a little dirty swing door, into a sort of hot packing-case immediately behind it. Here Mr.", "나는 겸손하게 동의했고, 우리 모두는 작고 더러운 회전문을 지나 그 바로 뒤에 있는 무슨 뜨거운 포장 상자 같은 곳으로 우르르 떨어지듯 들어섰다."], ["Wopsle was divesting himself of his Danish garments, and here there was just room for us to look at him over one another’s shoulders, by keeping the packing-case door, or lid, wide open.", "여기서 웝슬 씨는 덴마크 의상을 벗고 있었고, 우리는 그 포장 상자의 문, 즉 뚜껑을 활짝 열어 둔 채 서로의 어깨너머로 그를 들여다볼 만한 자리밖에 없었다."]], [["“Gentlemen,” said Mr.", "\"여러분,\" 웝슬 씨가 말했다."], ["Wopsle, “I am proud to see you.", "\"두 분을 뵙게 되어 자랑스럽습니다."], ["I hope, Mr. Pip, you will excuse my sending round.", "핍 씨, 사람을 보내 청한 것을 부디 용서해 주시기 바랍니다."], ["I had the happiness to know you in former times, and the Drama has ever had a claim which has ever been acknowledged, on the noble and the affluent.”", "저는 지난날 당신을 알게 된 행복을 누렸고, 연극이란 늘 고귀한 분들과 부유한 분들에게 권리를 주장해 왔으며, 그 권리는 늘 인정받아 왔지요.\""]], [["Meanwhile, Mr. Waldengarver, in a frightful perspiration, was trying to get himself out of his princely sables.", "그러는 동안 월든가버 씨는 무시무시하게 땀을 뻘뻘 흘리며 왕자의 검은 의상에서 빠져나오려 애쓰고 있었다."]], [["“Skin the stockings off Mr. Waldengarver,”", "\"월든가버 씨한테서 양말 가죽 벗기듯 살살 벗겨 내,\""], ["said the owner of that property, “or you’ll bust ’em.", "그 의상의 주인이 말했다. \"안 그러면 터뜨려 먹어."], ["Bust ’em, and you’ll bust five-and-thirty shillings.", "터뜨렸다 하면 삼십오 실링을 터뜨리는 거다."], ["Shakspeare never was complimented with a finer pair.", "셰익스피어도 이보다 더 좋은 양말을 받쳐 입은 적이 없어."], ["Keep quiet in your chair now, and leave ’em to me.”", "자, 의자에 가만히 앉아 계시고, 나한테 맡기쇼.\""]], [["With that, he went upon his knees, and began to flay his victim; who, on the first stocking coming off, would certainly have fallen over backward with his chair, but for there being no room to fall anyhow.", "그 말과 함께 사내는 무릎을 꿇고 자기 희생자의 가죽을 벗기기 시작했는데, 첫 번째 양말이 벗겨질 때 그 사람은 의자째 뒤로 나자빠질 것이 분명했으나, 도무지 어디로든 넘어질 자리가 없는 덕분에 그러지 않았다."]], [["I had been afraid until then to say a word about the play.", "그때까지 나는 연극에 관해 한마디 꺼내기가 두려웠다."], ["But then, Mr. Waldengarver looked up at us complacently, and said,—", "그러나 그때 월든가버 씨가 흡족한 얼굴로 우리를 올려다보며 말했다."]], [["“Gentlemen, how did it seem to you, to go, in front?”", "\"여러분, 앞에서 보시기에 공연이 어떻게 진행되는 것 같던가요?\""]], [["Herbert said from behind (at the same time poking me), “Capitally.”", "허버트가 뒤에서 (동시에 나를 쿡 찌르며) 말했다. \"훌륭했지요.\" 그래서 나도 \"훌륭했습니다\""], ["So I said “Capitally.”", "하고 말했다."]], [["“How did you like my reading of the character, gentlemen?”", "\"제가 그 배역을 해석한 방식이 마음에 드셨습니까, 여러분?\""], ["said Mr. Waldengarver, almost, if not quite, with patronage.", "월든가버 씨가 거의, 완전히는 아니더라도, 거의 생색을 내는 투로 말했다."]], [["Herbert said from behind (again poking me), “Massive and concrete.”", "허버트가 뒤에서 (다시 나를 쿡 찌르며) 말했다. \"묵직하고 견고했지요.\""], ["So I said boldly, as if I had originated it, and must beg to insist upon it, “Massive and concrete.”", "그래서 나는 마치 그 말을 내가 처음 생각해 내기라도 한 듯, 그리고 그 점을 꼭 강조해야겠다는 듯 대담하게 말했다. \"묵직하고 견고했습니다.\""]], [["“I am glad to have your approbation, gentlemen,”", "\"여러분의 찬사를 받으니 기쁩니다.\""], ["said Mr. Waldengarver, with an air of dignity, in spite of his being ground against the wall at the time, and holding on by the seat of the chair.", "월든가버 씨가 위엄 있는 태도로 말했는데, 그러는 동안에도 그는 벽에 밀쳐져 의자 바닥을 붙들고 있었다."]], [["“But I’ll tell you one thing, Mr. Waldengarver,”", "\"하지만 한 가지 말씀드리지요, 월든가버 씨,\""], ["said the man who was on his knees, “in which you’re out in your reading.", "무릎을 꿇고 있던 사내가 말했다. \"당신 해석에서 빗나간 데가 있어요."], ["Now mind! I don’t care who says contrairy; I tell you so.", "자, 잘 들으쇼! 누가 반대하든 난 상관 안 합니다."], ["You’re out in your reading of Hamlet when you get your legs in profile.", "당신한테 그렇게 말하는 거요. 당신은 다리를 옆모습으로 둘 때 햄릿 해석이 빗나가요."], ["The last Hamlet as I dressed, made the same mistakes in his reading at rehearsal, till I got him to put a large red wafer on each of his shins, and then at that rehearsal (which was the last) I went in front, sir, to the back of the pit, and whenever his reading brought him into profile, I called out “I don’t see no wafers!”", "내가 마지막으로 의상을 입혀 준 햄릿도 연습 때 같은 실수를 했었지요, 그래서 내가 그 사람 정강이마다 큼직한 빨간 봉인지를 붙이게 했는데, 그러고 나서 그 연습 때(그게 마지막 연습이었어요) 내가 객석 앞쪽, 일층 뒤편까지 나가서, 그 사람 해석이 옆모습으로 갈 때마다 '봉인지가 안 보이는데!' 하고 외쳐 댔지요."], ["And at night his reading was lovely.”", "그랬더니 밤 공연 때는 그 사람 해석이 아주 근사하더군요.\""]], [["Mr. Waldengarver smiled at me, as much as to say “a faithful Dependent—I overlook his folly;”", "월든가버 씨는 나를 향해 미소 지었는데, 흡사 \"충직한 아랫것이지요 — 저는 저자의 어리석음을 너그러이 봐줍니다\" 하고 말하는 듯했다."], ["and then said aloud, “My view is a little classic and thoughtful for them here; but they will improve, they will improve.”", "그러더니 소리 내어 말했다. \"제 견해는 여기 사람들이 보기엔 좀 고전적이고 사색적이지요. 하지만 저들도 나아질 겁니다, 나아질 거예요.\""]], [["Herbert and I said together, O, no doubt they would improve.", "허버트와 나는 한목소리로 말했다. 아, 그들도 틀림없이 나아질 거라고."]], [["“Did you observe, gentlemen,”", "\"여러분, 보셨습니까,\""], ["said Mr. Waldengarver, “that there was a man in the gallery who endeavoured to cast derision on the service,—I mean, the representation?”", "월든가버 씨가 말했다. \"위층 관람석에 그 예배를 — 그러니까, 그 공연을 비웃으려 든 자가 하나 있던 것을?\""]], [["We basely replied that we rather thought we had noticed such a man.", "우리는 비굴하게도, 아무래도 그런 자를 본 것 같다고 대답했다. 나는 덧붙였다."], ["I added, “He was drunk, no doubt.”", "\"틀림없이 술에 취했을 겁니다.\""]], [["“O dear no, sir,”", "\"아, 천만에요, 선생,\""], ["said Mr. Wopsle, “not drunk.", "웝슬 씨가 말했다. \"취한 게 아닙니다."], ["His employer would see to that, sir.", "그자의 고용주가 그렇게 두지 않을 테니까요, 선생."], ["His employer would not allow him to be drunk.”", "그자의 고용주가 그를 취하도록 내버려 두지 않을 겁니다.\""]], [["“You know his employer?” said I.", "\"그자의 고용주를 아십니까?\" 내가 말했다."]], [["Mr. Wopsle shut his eyes, and opened them again; performing both ceremonies very slowly.", "웝슬 씨는 눈을 감았다가 다시 떴는데, 그 두 동작을 모두 매우 천천히 해냈다."], ["“You must have observed, gentlemen,”", "\"여러분도 보셨을 겁니다,\" 그가 말했다."], ["said he, “an ignorant and a blatant ass, with a rasping throat and a countenance expressive of low malignity, who went through—I will not say sustained—the rôle (if I may use a French expression) of Claudius, King of Denmark.", "\"긁어 대는 듯한 목청과 저열한 악의가 드러나는 표정을 한, 무지하고 떠들썩한 멍청이가 — 감히 소화해 냈다고는 말하지 않겠습니다만 — 덴마크 왕 클로디어스의 배역(프랑스 말을 빌려 써도 된다면)을 그럭저럭 해 나가는 것을요."], ["That is his employer, gentlemen.", "그자가 바로 그 고용주입니다, 여러분."], ["Such is the profession!”", "이게 바로 이 직업이라는 것이지요!\""]], [["Without distinctly knowing whether I should have been more sorry for Mr. Wopsle if he had been in despair, I was so sorry for him as it was, that I took the opportunity of his turning round to have his braces put on,—which jostled us out at the doorway,—to ask Herbert what he thought of having him home to supper?", "웝슬 씨가 절망에 빠져 있었더라면 그가 더 가여웠을지 어땠을지 분명히 알 수는 없었으나, 나는 지금 이대로의 그가 어찌나 가엾던지, 그가 멜빵을 채우려고 몸을 돌린 틈 — 그 바람에 우리는 문간으로 밀려났는데 — 을 타서, 허버트에게 그를 집으로 데려가 저녁을 대접하면 어떻겠느냐고 물었다."], ["Herbert said he thought it would be kind to do so; therefore I invited him, and he went to Barnard’s with us, wrapped up to the eyes, and we did our best for him, and he sat until two o’clock in the morning, reviewing his success and developing his plans.", "허버트는 그것이 친절한 일일 것 같다고 했다. 그래서 나는 그를 초대했고, 그는 눈까지 푹 싸맨 채 우리와 함께 바너드 여관으로 갔으며, 우리는 그를 위해 최선을 다했다. 그는 새벽 두 시까지 앉아 자신의 성공을 돌이켜 보고 앞으로의 계획을 펼쳐 놓았다."], ["I forget in detail what they were, but I have a general recollection that he was to begin with reviving the Drama, and to end with crushing it; inasmuch as his decease would leave it utterly bereft and without a chance or hope.", "그 계획이 자세히 어떤 것이었는지는 잊어버렸으나, 그가 연극을 부흥시키는 일로 시작해 연극을 짓밟는 일로 끝맺으려 했다는 것만큼은 어렴풋이 기억난다. 그도 그럴 것이, 그가 죽고 나면 연극은 완전히 버려져 일말의 기회도 희망도 없어질 터였으니까."]], [["Miserably I went to bed after all, and miserably thought of Estella, and miserably dreamed that my expectations were all cancelled, and that I had to give my hand in marriage to Herbert’s Clara, or play Hamlet to Miss Havisham’s Ghost, before twenty thousand people, without knowing twenty words of it.", "결국 나는 비참한 심정으로 잠자리에 들었고, 비참하게 에스텔러를 생각했으며, 비참하게도 내 유산이 죄다 취소되어, 허버트의 클래라와 결혼하여 손을 내주거나, 아니면 단 스무 마디도 외우지 못한 채 이만 명의 관객 앞에서 해비셤 양의 유령을 상대로 햄릿을 연기해야 하는 꿈을 꾸었다."]], [["# Chapter XXXII.", "# Chapter XXXII."]], [["One day when I was busy with my books and Mr.", "어느 날, 내가 책과 포켓 씨를 상대로 분주히 공부하고 있을 때, 나는 우편으로 한 통의 쪽지를 받았다."], ["Pocket, I received a note by the post, the mere outside of which threw me into a great flutter; for, though I had never seen the handwriting in which it was addressed, I divined whose hand it was.", "그 겉봉만 보고도 나는 큰 동요에 휩싸였다. 비록 그 주소를 쓴 필체를 한 번도 본 적은 없었으나, 나는 그것이 누구의 손에서 나온 것인지 직감했던 것이다."], ["It had no set beginning, as Dear Mr. Pip, or Dear Pip, or Dear Sir, or Dear Anything, but ran thus:—", "친애하는 핍 씨, 혹은 친애하는 핍, 혹은 친애하는 선생님, 혹은 친애하는 그 무엇이라는 식의 정해진 서두도 없이, 그 글은 이렇게 이어졌다."]], [["“I am to come to London the day after to-morrow by the midday coach. I believe it was settled you should meet me?", "\"모레 정오 마차로 런던에 가게 되었어요. 당신이 저를 마중하기로 정해진 것으로 알고 있는데요?"], ["At all events Miss Havisham has that impression, and I write in obedience to it.", "어쨌든 해비셤 양께서 그렇게 알고 계셔서, 저는 그 뜻에 따라 이 글을 씁니다."], ["She sends you her regard.", "그분이 안부를 전하라 하시네요."]], [["“Yours, ESTELLA.”", "당신의 에스텔러로부터.\""]], [["If there had been time, I should probably have ordered several suits of clothes for this occasion; but as there was not, I was fain to be content with those I had.", "시간이 있었더라면 나는 아마 이번 기회를 위해 옷을 여러 벌 맞추었을 것이다. 그러나 시간이 없었으므로, 나는 가진 옷으로 만족하는 수밖에 없었다. 입맛은 순식간에 사라졌고, 그날이 오기까지 나는 평온도 안식도 알지 못했다."], ["My appetite vanished instantly, and I knew no peace or rest until the day arrived.", "그렇다고 그날이 온다고 해서 평온이나 안식이 찾아온 것도 아니었다."], ["Not that its arrival brought me either; for, then I was worse than ever, and began haunting the coach-office in Wood Street, Cheapside, before the coach had left the Blue Boar in our town.", "그날이 되자 나는 어느 때보다 더 안절부절못했고, 우리 마을의 블루 보어에서 마차가 아직 떠나지도 않았을 무렵부터 이미 칩사이드의 우드 가에 있는 마차 사무소 주변을 서성이기 시작했으니까."], ["For all that I knew this perfectly well, I still felt as if it were not safe to let the coach-office be out of my sight longer than five minutes at a time; and in this condition of unreason I had performed the first half-hour of a watch of four or five hours, when Wemmick ran against me.", "이 사실을 더없이 잘 알고 있었음에도, 나는 여전히 마차 사무소를 한 번에 오 분 이상 시야에서 놓치는 것은 안전하지 않다고 느꼈다. 이런 비이성적인 상태로 나는 네댓 시간에 걸친 감시의 첫 삼십 분을 보내고 있었는데, 그때 웨믹이 나와 마주쳐 부딪쳤다."]], [["“Halloa, Mr. Pip,”", "\"이런, 핍 씨.\""], ["said he; “how do you do?", "그가 말했다. \"안녕하십니까?"], ["I should hardly have thought this was your beat.”", "여기가 당신이 다니실 구역이라고는 도무지 생각지 못했는데요.\""]], [["I explained that I was waiting to meet somebody who was coming up by coach, and I inquired after the Castle and the Aged.", "나는 마차로 올라오는 누군가를 마중하려고 기다리는 중이라고 설명한 뒤, 캐슬과 노인장의 안부를 물었다."]], [["“Both flourishing thankye,”", "\"덕분에 둘 다 잘 지냅니다.\""], ["said Wemmick, “and particularly the Aged.", "웨믹이 말했다. \"특히 노인장께서요."], ["He’s in wonderful feather.", "기력이 아주 좋으시지요."], ["He’ll be eighty-two next birthday.", "다음 생신이면 여든둘이 되십니다."], ["I have a notion of firing eighty-two times, if the neighbourhood shouldn’t complain, and that cannon of mine should prove equal to the pressure. However, this is not London talk.", "이웃들이 불평만 하지 않고, 제 그 대포가 그 압력을 견뎌 준다면, 여든두 발을 쏠까 하는 생각이 있습니다. 하지만 이건 런던에서 할 이야기는 아니지요."], ["Where do you think I am going to?”", "제가 지금 어디로 가는 길인지 아시겠습니까?\""]], [["“To the office?”", "\"사무소로요?\""], ["said I, for he was tending in that direction.", "그가 그쪽 방향으로 향하고 있었기에 내가 말했다."]], [["“Next thing to it,”", "\"거기랑 가까운 데지요.\""], ["returned Wemmick, “I am going to Newgate.", "웨믹이 대답했다. \"뉴게이트로 가는 길입니다."], ["We are in a banker’s-parcel case just at present, and I have been down the road taking a squint at the scene of action, and thereupon must have a word or two with our client.”", "지금 은행 소포 사건을 하나 맡고 있는데, 제가 그 길을 따라 내려가 사건 현장을 한번 살펴보고 왔고, 그러고 나서 우리 의뢰인과 한두 마디 나눠야 하거든요.\""]], [["“Did your client commit the robbery?”", "\"당신 의뢰인이 그 강도질을 저질렀습니까?\""], ["I asked.", "내가 물었다."]], [["“Bless your soul and body, no,”", "\"천만의 말씀, 아닙니다.\""], ["answered Wemmick, very drily.", "웨믹이 아주 무미건조하게 대답했다."], ["“But he is accused of it.", "\"하지만 그 혐의를 받고 있지요."], ["So might you or I be.", "당신이나 나도 그럴 수 있습니다."], ["Either of us might be accused of it, you know.”", "우리 둘 중 누구라도 그런 혐의를 받을 수 있는 거예요, 아시다시피.\""]], [["“Only neither of us is,”", "\"다만 우리 둘 다 그렇지 않을 뿐이죠.\""], ["I remarked.", "내가 말했다."]], [["“Yah!”", "\"하!\""], ["said Wemmick, touching me on the breast with his forefinger; “you’re a deep one, Mr. Pip!", "웨믹이 집게손가락으로 내 가슴을 툭 건드리며 말했다. \"당신은 보통이 아니시군요, 핍 씨!"], ["Would you like to have a look at Newgate?", "뉴게이트를 한번 둘러보시겠습니까?"], ["Have you time to spare?”", "시간 여유가 있으신지요?\""]], [["I had so much time to spare, that the proposal came as a relief, notwithstanding its irreconcilability with my latent desire to keep my eye on the coach-office.", "내게는 시간 여유가 어찌나 많았던지, 그 제안은 마차 사무소에서 눈을 떼지 않으려는 내 은밀한 욕구와는 도무지 양립할 수 없었음에도, 오히려 안도감으로 다가왔다."], ["Muttering that I would make the inquiry whether I had time to walk with him, I went into the office, and ascertained from the clerk with the nicest precision and much to the trying of his temper, the earliest moment at which the coach could be expected,—which I knew beforehand, quite as well as he.", "나는 그와 함께 걸어갈 시간이 있는지 알아보겠다고 중얼거리며 사무소로 들어가, 마차가 도착할 수 있는 가장 이른 시각을 직원에게서 더없이 세밀한 정확성으로, 또한 그의 인내심을 적잖이 시험해 가며 확인했다. 그 시각은 그 직원만큼이나 나도 미리 잘 알고 있던 것이었다."], ["I then rejoined Mr. Wemmick, and affecting to consult my watch, and to be surprised by the information I had received, accepted his offer.", "그러고 나서 나는 웨믹 씨에게로 돌아가, 회중시계를 들여다보는 척하고 방금 들은 정보에 놀란 척하면서, 그의 제안을 받아들였다."]], [["We were at Newgate in a few minutes, and we passed through the lodge where some fetters were hanging up on the bare walls among the prison rules, into the interior of the jail.", "우리는 몇 분 만에 뉴게이트에 이르렀고, 헐벗은 벽에 감옥 규칙들 사이로 족쇄 몇 개가 걸려 있는 수위실을 지나 감옥 안쪽으로 들어갔다."], ["At that time jails were much neglected, and the period of exaggerated reaction consequent on all public wrongdoing—and which is always its heaviest and longest punishment—was still far off.", "그 시절에는 감옥이 몹시 등한시되었고, 모든 공적 비행에 뒤따르는 과장된 반작용의 시기 — 그것이야말로 언제나 가장 무겁고 가장 긴 형벌인데 — 는 아직 한참 멀었다."], ["So, felons were not lodged and fed better than soldiers (to say nothing of paupers), and seldom set fire to their prisons with the excusable object of improving the flavour of their soup.", "그래서 중죄인들이 병사들보다 (빈민들은 말할 것도 없고) 더 나은 숙식을 제공받는 일도 없었고, 수프 맛을 개선한다는 그럴듯한 명목으로 제 감옥에 불을 지르는 일도 좀처럼 없었다."], ["It was visiting time when Wemmick took me in, and a potman was going his rounds with beer; and the prisoners, behind bars in yards, were buying beer, and talking to friends; and a frowzy, ugly, disorderly, depressing scene it was.", "웨믹이 나를 데리고 들어간 때는 면회 시간이었고, 맥줏집 사환 하나가 맥주를 들고 돌아다니고 있었다. 그리고 마당의 창살 뒤편에서 죄수들은 맥주를 사거나 친구들과 이야기를 나누고 있었다. 추레하고 흉하고 무질서하며 사람의 기를 꺾는 광경이었다."]], [["It struck me that Wemmick walked among the prisoners much as a gardener might walk among his plants.", "웨믹이 죄수들 사이를 걸어 다니는 모습은, 마치 정원사가 제 화초들 사이를 거니는 것과 흡사하다는 생각이 내게 들었다."], ["This was first put into my head by his seeing a shoot that had come up in the night, and saying, “What, Captain Tom?", "이런 생각이 처음 떠오른 것은, 그가 밤새 돋아난 새싹 하나를 보고는 \"아니, 톰 선장 아니신가? 자네가 거기 있었어?"], ["Are you there?", "아, 그렇구먼!\""], ["Ah, indeed!” and also, “Is that Black Bill behind the cistern?", "하고 말하는 것을 보았을 때였다. 또한 \"물통 뒤에 있는 게 블랙 빌인가?"], ["Why I didn’t look for you these two months; how do you find yourself?”", "이런, 자네를 두 달 동안이나 못 봤군그래. 어떻게 지내나?\""], ["Equally in his stopping at the bars and attending to anxious whisperers,—always singly,—Wemmick with his post-office in an immovable state, looked at them while in conference, as if he were taking particular notice of the advance they had made, since last observed, towards coming out in full blow at their trial.", "하고 말하는 것을 들었을 때이기도 했다. 창살 앞에 멈춰 서서 — 언제나 한 사람씩 — 근심에 차 속삭이는 자들에게 귀를 기울일 때도 마찬가지로, 웨믹은 그 우체통 같은 입을 꿈쩍도 않는 상태로 둔 채 그들과 의논하며, 마치 지난번에 보았을 때 이후로 그들이 재판에서 활짝 꽃피어 나오기까지 얼마나 진척되었는지를 각별히 주목하는 듯한 눈길로 그들을 바라보았다."]], [["He was highly popular, and I found that he took the familiar department of Mr. Jaggers’s business; though something of the state of Mr.", "그는 대단히 인기가 있었고, 나는 그가 재거스 씨 업무 중에서 친근하게 사람을 대하는 부분을 맡고 있음을 알게 되었다."], ["Jaggers hung about him too, forbidding approach beyond certain limits. His personal recognition of each successive client was comprised in a nod, and in his settling his hat a little easier on his head with both hands, and then tightening the post-office, and putting his hands in his pockets.", "다만 그에게도 재거스 씨 특유의 위엄 같은 것이 얼마간 감돌아, 일정한 선을 넘어 다가오는 것을 허락하지 않았다. 잇따라 찾아오는 의뢰인 하나하나를 그가 알아보았다는 표시는, 고갯짓 한 번과, 두 손으로 모자를 머리 위에 좀 더 편하게 고쳐 쓰고는, 이어 우체통 같은 입을 꽉 다물고 두 손을 주머니에 찔러 넣는 것으로 끝났다."], ["In one or two instances there was a difficulty respecting the raising of fees, and then Mr. Wemmick, backing as far as possible from the insufficient money produced, said, “it’s no use, my boy.", "한두 번은 수임료를 마련하는 문제로 곤란한 일이 있었는데, 그럴 때면 웨믹 씨는 내밀어진 부족한 돈에서 가능한 한 멀찍이 물러서며 이렇게 말했다. \"소용없네, 이 사람아."], ["I’m only a subordinate.", "나는 한낱 부하일 뿐이야."], ["I can’t take it.", "그건 받을 수 없어."], ["Don’t go on in that way with a subordinate.", "부하한테 그런 식으로 굴지 말게."], ["If you are unable to make up your quantum, my boy, you had better address yourself to a principal; there are plenty of principals in the profession, you know, and what is not worth the while of one, may be worth the while of another; that’s my recommendation to you, speaking as a subordinate.", "자네가 정해진 액수를 채울 수 없다면, 이 사람아, 차라리 주인 되는 분께 직접 말씀드리는 게 나아. 이 바닥에 주인 되는 사람은 얼마든지 있고, 한 사람에게는 수고할 가치가 없는 일이 다른 사람에게는 수고할 가치가 있을 수도 있는 법이지. 그게 부하 된 입장에서 자네에게 드리는 권고일세."], ["Don’t try on useless measures.", "쓸데없는 수단은 부리려 들지 말게."], ["Why should you?", "그래 봤자 뭘 하겠나?"], ["Now, who’s next?”", "자, 다음은 누구지?\""]], [["Thus, we walked through Wemmick’s greenhouse, until he turned to me and said, “Notice the man I shall shake hands with.”", "이리하여 우리는 웨믹의 온실을 두루 거닐었고, 마침내 그가 내게 돌아서서 말했다. \"내가 악수를 나눌 사람을 눈여겨보십시오.\""], ["I should have done so, without the preparation, as he had shaken hands with no one yet.", "그가 일러 주지 않았더라도 나는 그렇게 했을 것이다. 그때까지 그는 아무와도 악수를 나누지 않았으니까."]], [["Almost as soon as he had spoken, a portly upright man (whom I can see now, as I write) in a well-worn olive-coloured frock-coat, with a peculiar pallor overspreading the red in his complexion, and eyes that went wandering about when he tried to fix them, came up to a corner of the bars, and put his hand to his hat—which had a greasy and fatty surface like cold broth—with a half-serious and half-jocose military salute.", "그가 말을 마치기가 무섭게, 풍채 좋고 꼿꼿한 한 사내가 (지금 이 글을 쓰는 순간에도 눈에 선하다) 닳고 닳은 올리브색 프록코트 차림으로, 불그레한 안색 위로 묘한 창백함이 번지고, 눈을 한곳에 고정하려 해도 자꾸만 이리저리 헤매는 눈을 한 채, 창살 한 귀퉁이로 다가와 모자에 손을 가져갔다. 차게 식은 묽은 수프처럼 번들거리고 기름진 표면을 한 그 모자에. 그러고는 반쯤은 진지하고 반쯤은 익살스러운 군대식 거수경례를 했다."]], [["“Colonel, to you!”", "\"대령께 인사 올립니다!\""], ["said Wemmick; “how are you, Colonel?”", "웨믹이 말했다. \"어떻게 지내십니까, 대령?\""]], [["“All right, Mr. Wemmick.”", "\"좋습니다, 웨믹 씨.\""]], [["“Everything was done that could be done, but the evidence was too strong for us, Colonel.”", "\"할 수 있는 일은 다 했습니다만, 증거가 우리로서는 너무 강력했지요, 대령.\""]], [["“Yes, it was too strong, sir,—but I don’t care.”", "\"그래요, 너무 강력했지요, 선생. 하지만 나는 개의치 않습니다.\""]], [["“No, no,”", "\"네, 네.\""], ["said Wemmick, coolly, “you don’t care.”", "웨믹이 차분하게 말했다. \"당신은 개의치 않으시지요.\""], ["Then, turning to me, “Served His Majesty this man.", "그러고는 내게로 돌아서며 말했다. \"이 사람은 폐하를 위해 복무했습니다."], ["Was a soldier in the line and bought his discharge.”", "정규 보병으로 군 복무를 하다가 돈을 주고 제대했지요.\""]], [["I said, “Indeed?”", "내가 \"그렇습니까?\""], ["and the man’s eyes looked at me, and then looked over my head, and then looked all round me, and then he drew his hand across his lips and laughed.", "하고 말하자, 사내의 두 눈이 나를 보았다가, 다시 내 머리 위를 보았다가, 다시 내 주위를 빙 둘러보았고, 그러고 나서 그는 손으로 제 입술을 쓱 훔치며 웃었다."]], [["“I think I shall be out of this on Monday, sir,”", "\"저는 월요일이면 여기서 나갈 것 같습니다, 선생.\""], ["he said to Wemmick.", "그가 웨믹에게 말했다."]], [["“Perhaps,”", "\"아마도요.\""], ["returned my friend, “but there’s no knowing.”", "내 친구가 대답했다. \"하지만 알 수 없는 일이지요.\""]], [["“I am glad to have the chance of bidding you good-bye, Mr. Wemmick,”", "\"이렇게 작별 인사를 드릴 기회가 있어 기쁩니다, 웨믹 씨.\""], ["said the man, stretching out his hand between two bars.", "사내가 창살 두 개 사이로 손을 내밀며 말했다."]], [["“Thankye,” said Wemmick, shaking hands with him.", "\"고맙습니다.\" 웨믹이 그와 악수하며 말했다."], ["“Same to you, Colonel.”", "\"당신도 잘 지내시길, 대령.\""]], [["“If what I had upon me when taken had been real, Mr. Wemmick,”", "\"제가 붙잡혔을 때 몸에 지니고 있던 것이 진짜였더라면, 웨믹 씨.\""], ["said the man, unwilling to let his hand go, “I should have asked the favour of your wearing another ring—in acknowledgment of your attentions.”", "사내가 그의 손을 좀처럼 놓으려 하지 않으며 말했다. \"당신이 베풀어 주신 배려에 대한 감사의 표시로, 반지를 하나 더 끼워 주십사 청을 드렸을 텐데요.\""]], [["“I’ll accept the will for the deed,”", "\"그 뜻만으로도 한 것이나 다름없이 받겠습니다.\""], ["said Wemmick.", "웨믹이 말했다."], ["“By the by; you were quite a pigeon-fancier.”", "\"그건 그렇고, 당신은 비둘기 애호가셨다지요.\""], ["The man looked up at the sky.", "사내가 하늘을 올려다보았다."], ["“I am told you had a remarkable breed of tumblers.", "\"공중제비 비둘기로 아주 보기 드문 종을 기르셨다고 들었습니다."], ["Could you commission any friend of yours to bring me a pair, if you’ve no further use for ’em?”", "더 이상 쓸데가 없으시다면, 친구분께 부탁해 제게 한 쌍을 가져다주게 하실 수 있겠습니까?\""]], [["“It shall be done, sir.”", "\"그렇게 하지요, 선생.\""]], [["“All right,”", "\"좋습니다.\""], ["said Wemmick, “they shall be taken care of.", "웨믹이 말했다. \"잘 보살펴 주겠습니다."], ["Good-afternoon, Colonel.", "안녕히 계십시오, 대령."], ["Good-bye!”", "그럼 또!\""], ["They shook hands again, and as we walked away Wemmick said to me, “A Coiner, a very good workman.", "두 사람은 다시 악수했고, 우리가 걸어 나오는 동안 웨믹이 내게 말했다. \"위조 주화범인데, 솜씨가 아주 좋은 자입니다."], ["The Recorder’s report is made to-day, and he is sure to be executed on Monday.", "오늘 재판 기록관의 보고가 올라가니, 월요일이면 틀림없이 처형될 겁니다."], ["Still you see, as far as it goes, a pair of pigeons are portable property all the same.”", "하지만 보시다시피, 어쨌든 그것대로, 비둘기 한 쌍은 그래도 운반할 수 있는 재산이지요.\""], ["With that, he looked back, and nodded at this dead plant, and then cast his eyes about him in walking out of the yard, as if he were considering what other pot would go best in its place.", "그 말과 함께 그는 뒤를 돌아보고 이 죽은 화초를 향해 고갯짓을 하더니, 마당을 빠져나오면서, 마치 그 자리에 어떤 화분이 가장 잘 어울릴까를 궁리하는 사람처럼 주위로 눈길을 둘렀다."]], [["As we came out of the prison through the lodge, I found that the great importance of my guardian was appreciated by the turnkeys, no less than by those whom they held in charge. “Well, Mr.", "우리가 수위실을 거쳐 감옥에서 나올 때, 나는 내 후견인의 그 대단한 위세가 그들이 맡아 가두고 있는 자들 못지않게 간수들에게도 인정받고 있음을 알게 되었다."], ["Wemmick,” said the turnkey, who kept us between the two studded and spiked lodge gates, and who carefully locked one before he unlocked the other, “what’s Mr.", "\"그런데 말입니다, 웨믹 씨.\" 대못 박힌 두 짝의 수위실 문 사이에 우리를 세워 두고, 한쪽을 잠근 뒤에야 다른 쪽을 여는 간수가 말했다."], ["Jaggers going to do with that water-side murder?", "\"그 강가 살인 사건은 재거스 씨가 어떻게 처리하실 작정입니까?"], ["Is he going to make it manslaughter, or what’s he going to make of it?”", "과실치사로 만드실 건가요, 아니면 어떻게 만드실 작정이래요?\""]], [["“Why don’t you ask him?” returned Wemmick.", "\"왜 그분께 직접 여쭤보지 않으시오?\" 웨믹이 대꾸했다."]], [["“O yes, I dare say!” said the turnkey.", "\"오, 그래요, 여쭤보고말고요!\" 간수가 말했다."]], [["“Now, that’s the way with them here, Mr. Pip,”", "\"바로 저런 식이라니까요, 핍 씨.\""], ["remarked Wemmick, turning to me with his post-office elongated.", "웨믹이 그 우체통 같은 입을 길쭉하게 늘이며 내게로 돌아서서 말했다."], ["“They don’t mind what they ask of me, the subordinate; but you’ll never catch ’em asking any questions of my principal.”", "\"부하인 나한테는 뭐든 거리낌 없이 물어 대지만, 내 주인 되는 분께 무얼 묻는 모습은 결코 보지 못하실 겁니다.\""]], [["“Is this young gentleman one of the ’prentices or articled ones of your office?”", "\"이 젊은 신사분은 댁의 사무소에 다니는 견습생이나 수습 서기 가운데 한 사람입니까?\""], ["asked the turnkey, with a grin at Mr. Wemmick’s humour.", "간수가 웨믹의 농담에 씩 웃으며 물었다."]], [["“There he goes again, you see!”", "\"또 저런다니까요, 보십시오!\""], ["cried Wemmick, “I told you so!", "웨믹이 외쳤다. \"제가 말씀드렸지요!"], ["Asks another question of the subordinate before his first is dry!", "첫 번째 질문에 답할 잉크도 마르기 전에 부하한테 또 질문을 던진다니까요!"], ["Well, supposing Mr. Pip is one of them?”", "자, 핍 씨가 그중 한 사람이라고 칩시다. 그러면요?\""]], [["“Why then,”", "\"그렇다면야.\""], ["said the turnkey, grinning again, “he knows what Mr. Jaggers is.”", "간수가 다시 씩 웃으며 말했다. \"재거스 씨가 어떤 분인지 알 거 아니오.\""]], [["“Yah!”", "\"하!\""], ["cried Wemmick, suddenly hitting out at the turnkey in a facetious way, “you’re dumb as one of your own keys when you have to do with my principal, you know you are.", "웨믹이 익살스럽게 별안간 간수를 향해 주먹질하는 시늉을 하며 외쳤다. \"내 주인 되는 분을 상대할 때면 당신도 당신 열쇠 중 하나만큼이나 입을 꾹 다물면서, 잘도 그러시는군."], ["Let us out, you old fox, or I’ll get him to bring an action against you for false imprisonment.”", "어서 내보내 주시오, 이 늙은 여우 양반, 안 그러면 내가 그분께 부탁해서 당신을 불법 감금죄로 고소하게 만들 테니.\""]], [["The turnkey laughed, and gave us good day, and stood laughing at us over the spikes of the wicket when we descended the steps into the street.", "간수는 웃으며 우리에게 잘 가라고 인사했고, 우리가 계단을 내려가 거리로 들어설 때 쪽문의 대못 너머로 우리를 바라보며 서서 웃고 있었다."]], [["“Mind you, Mr. Pip,”", "\"잘 새겨 두십시오, 핍 씨.\""], ["said Wemmick, gravely in my ear, as he took my arm to be more confidential; “I don’t know that Mr.", "웨믹이 좀 더 은밀히 이야기하려 내 팔을 잡으며 진지하게 내 귀에 대고 말했다."], ["Jaggers does a better thing than the way in which he keeps himself so high.", "\"내 보기엔 재거스 씨가 그토록 높은 자리를 지켜 내는 그 방식보다 더 잘하는 일은 없는 것 같습니다."], ["He’s always so high. His constant height is of a piece with his immense abilities.", "그분은 늘 그렇게 높이 계시지요. 그 한결같은 높이가 그분의 엄청난 역량과 한 짝을 이룹니다."], ["That Colonel durst no more take leave of him, than that turnkey durst ask him his intentions respecting a case.", "저 대령이 그분께 작별 인사를 감히 건넬 수 없는 것은, 저 간수가 어느 사건을 두고 그분의 의중을 감히 묻지 못하는 것과 마찬가지지요."], ["Then, between his height and them, he slips in his subordinate,—don’t you see?—and so he has ’em, soul and body.”", "그러고서 그분은 당신의 그 높이와 그들 사이에 부하인 나를 슬쩍 끼워 넣는 겁니다. 아시겠습니까? 그렇게 해서 그들을 송두리째, 몸과 마음을 다 손아귀에 쥐는 거지요.\""]], [["I was very much impressed, and not for the first time, by my guardian’s subtlety.", "나는 내 후견인의 그 교묘함에 깊은 감명을 받았는데, 이번이 처음은 아니었다."], ["To confess the truth, I very heartily wished, and not for the first time, that I had had some other guardian of minor abilities.", "솔직히 고백하자면, 나는 차라리 역량이 그보다 못한 다른 후견인을 두었더라면 좋았을 것이라고 진심으로 바랐는데, 이 또한 처음 있는 일이 아니었다."]], [["Mr. Wemmick and I parted at the office in Little Britain, where suppliants for Mr.", "웨믹 씨와 나는 리틀 브리튼의 사무소에서 헤어졌는데, 그곳에는 재거스 씨의 눈에 들기를 바라는 청원자들이 여느 때처럼 서성이고 있었다."], ["Jaggers’s notice were lingering about as usual, and I returned to my watch in the street of the coach-office, with some three hours on hand.", "그리고 나는 세 시간쯤을 남겨 둔 채, 마차 사무소가 있는 거리에서 다시 감시 자세로 돌아갔다."], ["I consumed the whole time in thinking how strange it was that I should be encompassed by all this taint of prison and crime; that, in my childhood out on our lonely marshes on a winter evening, I should have first encountered it; that, it should have reappeared on two occasions, starting out like a stain that was faded but not gone; that, it should in this new way pervade my fortune and advancement.", "나는 그 시간 내내, 내가 감옥과 범죄의 이 온갖 오욕에 둘러싸여 있다는 것이 얼마나 기이한 일인가를 생각하며 보냈다. 어린 시절 어느 겨울 저녁 우리 고장의 외딴 습지에서 내가 그것과 처음 마주쳤다는 것, 그것이 바랬으되 사라지지는 않은 얼룩처럼 두 차례나 다시 불쑥 나타났다는 것, 그것이 이제 이런 새로운 방식으로 내 재산과 출세에 두루 스며들고 있다는 것을 생각하면서."], ["While my mind was thus engaged, I thought of the beautiful young Estella, proud and refined, coming towards me, and I thought with absolute abhorrence of the contrast between the jail and her.", "이렇게 마음이 어지러운 와중에, 나는 도도하고 기품 있는 아름다운 젊은 에스텔러가 내게로 다가오는 모습을 떠올렸고, 감옥과 그녀 사이의 그 대비를 절대적인 혐오감으로 떠올렸다."], ["I wished that Wemmick had not met me, or that I had not yielded to him and gone with him, so that, of all days in the year on this day, I might not have had Newgate in my breath and on my clothes.", "차라리 웨믹이 나와 마주치지 않았더라면, 아니면 내가 그의 말에 넘어가 그를 따라가지 않았더라면 좋았을 것을, 그리하여 일 년 중 하필 이날만은 내 숨결과 옷에 뉴게이트를 묻혀 두지 않았더라면 좋았을 것을 하고 바랐다."], ["I beat the prison dust off my feet as I sauntered to and fro, and I shook it out of my dress, and I exhaled its air from my lungs.", "나는 이리저리 거니는 동안 발에서 감옥의 먼지를 털어 냈고, 옷에서도 그것을 떨어냈으며, 내 폐에서도 그 공기를 내뿜었다."], ["So contaminated did I feel, remembering who was coming, that the coach came quickly after all, and I was not yet free from the soiling consciousness of Mr. Wemmick’s conservatory, when I saw her face at the coach window and her hand waving to me.", "누가 오는지를 떠올리니 어찌나 오염된 기분이었던지, 마차는 그래도 빨리 도착해 버렸고, 마차 창가에 어른거리는 그녀의 얼굴과 내게 흔드는 그녀의 손을 보았을 때 나는 아직 웨믹 씨의 그 온실에 더럽혀진 의식에서 벗어나지도 못한 상태였다."]], [["What was the nameless shadow which again in that one instant had passed?", "그 한순간에 또다시 스쳐 지나간 그 이름 모를 그림자는 대체 무엇이었을까?"]], [["# Chapter XXXIII.", "# Chapter XXXIII."]], [["In her furred travelling-dress, Estella seemed more delicately beautiful than she had ever seemed yet, even in my eyes.", "털 장식이 달린 여행복을 입은 에스텔라는, 내 눈에조차도, 지금껏 보아 온 그 어느 때보다 한층 섬세하게 아름다워 보였다."], ["Her manner was more winning than she had cared to let it be to me before, and I thought I saw Miss Havisham’s influence in the change.", "그녀의 태도는 전에 내게 보여 주려 하던 것보다 더 사람을 끄는 데가 있었고, 나는 그 변화에서 해비셤 양의 영향을 본 듯한 생각이 들었다."]], [["We stood in the Inn Yard while she pointed out her luggage to me, and when it was all collected I remembered—having forgotten everything but herself in the meanwhile—that I knew nothing of her destination.", "우리는 여관 마당에 서 있었고, 그녀는 내게 자기 짐을 가리켜 주었다. 짐이 모두 모이자, 그동안 그녀 말고는 모든 것을 잊고 있었던 나는, 그녀가 어디로 가는지조차 아무것도 모른다는 사실을 떠올렸다."]], [["“I am going to Richmond,” she told me.", "\"리치먼드로 가요.\" 그녀가 내게 말했다."], ["“Our lesson is, that there are two Richmonds, one in Surrey and one in Yorkshire, and that mine is the Surrey Richmond.", "\"우리가 배운 바로는 리치먼드가 둘인데, 하나는 서리에 있고 하나는 요크셔에 있다지요. 내가 가는 곳은 서리의 리치먼드예요."], ["The distance is ten miles. I am to have a carriage, and you are to take me.", "거리는 십 마일이고요. 나는 마차를 타게 되어 있고, 당신은 나를 데려다주게 되어 있어요."], ["This is my purse, and you are to pay my charges out of it.", "이건 내 지갑이고, 당신은 이 안에서 내 비용을 치러야 해요."], ["O, you must take the purse!", "아, 지갑을 받아야 해요!"], ["We have no choice, you and I, but to obey our instructions.", "당신과 나는, 지시를 따르는 것 말고는 달리 선택의 여지가 없어요."], ["We are not free to follow our own devices, you and I.”", "당신과 나는 우리 뜻대로 할 자유가 없는 거예요.\""]], [["As she looked at me in giving me the purse, I hoped there was an inner meaning in her words.", "지갑을 건네며 그녀가 나를 바라보았을 때, 나는 그 말에 어떤 숨은 뜻이 있기를 바랐다."], ["She said them slightingly, but not with displeasure.", "그녀는 무심하게 그 말을 했지만, 못마땅한 기색은 아니었다."]], [["“A carriage will have to be sent for, Estella.", "\"마차를 불러야겠군요, 에스텔라."], ["Will you rest here a little?”", "여기서 잠깐 쉬겠어요?\""]], [["“Yes, I am to rest here a little, and I am to drink some tea, and you are to take care of me the while.”", "\"네, 여기서 잠깐 쉬고, 차를 좀 마시기로 되어 있어요. 그동안 당신이 나를 돌봐 주기로 되어 있고요.\""]], [["She drew her arm through mine, as if it must be done, and I requested a waiter who had been staring at the coach like a man who had never seen such a thing in his life, to show us a private sitting-room.", "그녀는 마땅히 그래야 한다는 듯 내 팔에 자기 팔을 끼웠고, 나는 평생 그런 물건은 본 적도 없는 사람처럼 마차를 빤히 쳐다보고 있던 웨이터에게 별실 하나를 안내해 달라고 청했다."], ["Upon that, he pulled out a napkin, as if it were a magic clue without which he couldn’t find the way upstairs, and led us to the black hole of the establishment, fitted up with a diminishing mirror (quite a superfluous article, considering the hole’s proportions), an anchovy sauce-cruet, and somebody’s pattens.", "그러자 그는, 그것이 없으면 이층으로 가는 길도 못 찾는 마법의 실마리라도 되는 양 냅킨을 꺼내 들고는, 우리를 그 가게의 검은 굴 속으로 이끌었다. 그곳에는 점점 작아 보이게 하는 거울 하나(굴의 크기를 생각하면 아주 쓸데없는 물건이었다)와 멸치 소스 병 하나, 그리고 누군가의 나막신이 놓여 있었다."], ["On my objecting to this retreat, he took us into another room with a dinner-table for thirty, and in the grate a scorched leaf of a copy-book under a bushel of coal-dust.", "내가 이 은신처에 이의를 제기하자, 그는 우리를 서른 명이 앉을 만찬 탁자가 놓인 다른 방으로 데려갔는데, 벽난로 쇠살대 안에는 한 말은 됨직한 석탄 가루 밑에 그을린 습자책 한 장이 들어 있었다."], ["Having looked at this extinct conflagration and shaken his head, he took my order; which, proving to be merely, “Some tea for the lady,” sent him out of the room in a very low state of mind.", "그는 이 꺼져 버린 화재를 들여다보고는 고개를 절레절레 흔들더니 내 주문을 받았다. 그런데 그 주문이라는 것이 고작 \"이 숙녀분께 차 좀 주시오\"였던 탓에, 그는 몹시 기가 죽은 채로 방을 나갔다."]], [["I was, and I am, sensible that the air of this chamber, in its strong combination of stable with soup-stock, might have led one to infer that the coaching department was not doing well, and that the enterprising proprietor was boiling down the horses for the refreshment department.", "이 방의 공기는, 마구간 냄새와 수프 국물 냄새가 강하게 뒤섞인 탓에, 마차 영업 부문이 신통치 못하고 진취적인 주인이 음식 부문을 위해 말을 고아 내고 있다는 추측을 불러일으킬 만했다. 나는 그때도 그것을 알았고 지금도 안다."], ["Yet the room was all in all to me, Estella being in it.", "그러나 에스텔라가 그 안에 있었으므로, 그 방은 내게 전부였다."], ["I thought that with her I could have been happy there for life.", "나는 그녀와 함께라면 그곳에서 평생 행복할 수 있으리라 생각했다."], ["(I was not at all happy there at the time, observe, and I knew it well.)", "(잘 봐 두시라, 나는 그때 그곳에서 조금도 행복하지 않았고, 그것을 잘 알고 있었다.)"]], [["“Where are you going to, at Richmond?” I asked Estella.", "\"리치먼드에서는 어디로 가나요?\" 나는 에스텔라에게 물었다."]], [["“I am going to live,”", "\"가서 살게 돼요.\" 그녀가 말했다."], ["said she, “at a great expense, with a lady there, who has the power—or says she has—of taking me about, and introducing me, and showing people to me and showing me to people.”", "\"큰돈을 들여, 그곳의 한 부인과 함께요. 그 부인은 나를 데리고 다니며 소개해 주고, 사람들을 내게 보여 주고 나를 사람들에게 보여 줄 힘을 가졌다나요. 아니, 가졌다고 하더군요.\""]], [["“I suppose you will be glad of variety and admiration?”", "\"변화도 있고 사람들의 찬사도 받게 되니 기쁘겠군요?\""]], [["“Yes, I suppose so.”", "\"네, 그럴 것 같네요.\""]], [["She answered so carelessly, that I said, “You speak of yourself as if you were some one else.”", "그녀가 어찌나 무심하게 대답하던지, 나는 이렇게 말했다. \"당신은 자기 얘기를 마치 다른 사람 얘기하듯 하는군요.\""]], [["“Where did you learn how I speak of others?", "\"내가 남들 얘기를 어떻게 하는지는 또 어디서 배웠대요?"], ["Come, come,”", "이봐요, 이봐요.\""], ["said Estella, smiling delightfully, “you must not expect me to go to school to you; I must talk in my own way.", "에스텔라가 매혹적으로 미소 지으며 말했다. \"내가 당신한테 배우러 다닐 거라곤 기대하지 말아요. 나는 내 방식대로 말할 거예요."], ["How do you thrive with Mr. Pocket?”", "포켓 선생님 댁에서는 잘 지내요?\""]], [["“I live quite pleasantly there; at least—”", "\"거기서 아주 즐겁게 지내요. 적어도ㅡ\""], ["It appeared to me that I was losing a chance.", "나는 기회를 놓치고 있는 듯한 느낌이 들었다."]], [["“At least?”", "\"적어도?\""], ["repeated Estella.", "에스텔라가 따라 말했다."]], [["“As pleasantly as I could anywhere, away from you.”", "\"당신을 떠나 어디서든 지낼 수 있는 만큼은 즐겁게요.\""]], [["“You silly boy,”", "\"바보 같은 사람.\""], ["said Estella, quite composedly, “how can you talk such nonsense?", "에스텔라가 아주 침착하게 말했다. \"어떻게 그런 허튼소리를 할 수 있죠?"], ["Your friend Mr. Matthew, I believe, is superior to the rest of his family?”", "당신 친구 매슈 선생님은, 내가 듣기로 그 집안의 다른 사람들보다 훨씬 나으신 분이라던데요?\""]], [["“Very superior indeed.", "\"정말 훨씬 나으신 분이죠."], ["He is nobody’s enemy—”", "그분은 누구의 적도 아니ㅡ\""]], [["“Don’t add but his own,”", "\"자기 자신 말고는, 이라고 덧붙이진 말아요.\""], ["interposed Estella, “for I hate that class of man.", "에스텔라가 끼어들었다. \"나는 그런 부류의 사람을 싫어하니까요."], ["But he really is disinterested, and above small jealousy and spite, I have heard?”", "하지만 그분은 정말로 사심이 없고, 자잘한 시기나 악의 따위를 초월한 분이라던데, 맞나요?\""]], [["“I am sure I have every reason to say so.”", "\"그렇게 말할 만한 충분한 이유가 내겐 있다고 확신해요.\""]], [["“You have not every reason to say so of the rest of his people,”", "\"그 집안의 다른 사람들에 대해서는 그렇게 말할 이유가 도무지 없을걸요.\""], ["said Estella, nodding at me with an expression of face that was at once grave and rallying, “for they beset Miss Havisham with reports and insinuations to your disadvantage.", "에스텔라가, 진지하면서도 놀리는 듯한 표정으로 내게 고개를 끄덕이며 말했다. \"그 사람들은 당신한테 불리한 소문과 빈정거림으로 해비셤 양을 들들 볶고 있으니까요."], ["They watch you, misrepresent you, write letters about you (anonymous sometimes), and you are the torment and the occupation of their lives.", "그들은 당신을 감시하고, 당신을 모함하고, 당신에 관한 편지를 쓰고(때로는 익명으로요), 당신은 그들 삶의 고민거리이자 일거리예요."], ["You can scarcely realise to yourself the hatred those people feel for you.”", "그 사람들이 당신한테 품고 있는 증오가 어느 정도인지는 당신 스스로도 거의 실감하지 못할 거예요.\""]], [["“They do me no harm, I hope?”", "\"그들이 내게 해를 끼치진 못하겠죠?\""]], [["Instead of answering, Estella burst out laughing.", "대답하는 대신 에스텔라는 웃음을 터뜨렸다."], ["This was very singular to me, and I looked at her in considerable perplexity.", "이것은 내게 무척 묘한 일이어서, 나는 적잖이 어리둥절해하며 그녀를 바라보았다."], ["When she left off—and she had not laughed languidly, but with real enjoyment—I said, in my diffident way with her,—", "그녀가 웃음을 그쳤을 때ㅡ그녀는 나른하게가 아니라 진심으로 즐거워하며 웃었던 것인데ㅡ나는 그녀를 대할 때면 늘 그러하듯 머뭇거리며 말했다."]], [["“I hope I may suppose that you would not be amused if they did me any harm.”", "\"그들이 내게 무슨 해라도 끼친다면 당신이 즐거워하지는 않으리라고 생각해도 되겠지요.\""]], [["“No, no you may be sure of that,”", "\"그럼요, 그럼요, 그건 믿어도 돼요.\""], ["said Estella.", "에스텔라가 말했다."], ["“You may be certain that I laugh because they fail.", "\"내가 웃는 건 그들이 실패하기 때문이라는 걸 확실히 믿어도 좋아요."], ["O, those people with Miss Havisham, and the tortures they undergo!”", "아, 해비셤 양 곁에 붙어 있는 그 사람들이라니, 또 그들이 겪는 고통이라니!\""], ["She laughed again, and even now when she had told me why, her laughter was very singular to me, for I could not doubt its being genuine, and yet it seemed too much for the occasion.", "그녀가 다시 웃었고, 이제 이유를 말해 준 뒤에도 그 웃음은 내게 무척 묘하게 느껴졌다. 진심이 아닐 리는 없었지만, 그렇다고 하기엔 이 상황에 비해 너무 지나친 웃음 같았기 때문이다."], ["I thought there must really be something more here than I knew; she saw the thought in my mind, and answered it.", "나는 여기에 내가 아는 것보다 무언가 더 있는 게 분명하다고 생각했다. 그녀는 내 마음속의 그 생각을 알아채고는 거기에 답했다."]], [["“It is not easy for even you.” said Estella, “to know what satisfaction it gives me to see those people thwarted, or what an enjoyable sense of the ridiculous I have when they are made ridiculous.", "\"그 사람들이 좌절하는 꼴을 보는 것이 내게 얼마나 큰 만족을 주는지, 그들이 우스운 꼴이 될 때 내가 얼마나 그 우스꽝스러움을 즐기는지는, 당신조차도 알기 쉽지 않을 거예요.\" 에스텔라가 말했다."], ["For you were not brought up in that strange house from a mere baby. I was.", "\"당신은 갓난아기 때부터 그 이상한 집에서 자라지 않았으니까요. 나는 그랬어요."], ["You had not your little wits sharpened by their intriguing against you, suppressed and defenceless, under the mask of sympathy and pity and what not that is soft and soothing. I had.", "당신은 동정이며 연민이며 그 밖에 부드럽고 다독여 주는 척하는 온갖 가면 아래에서, 억눌리고 무방비인 채, 자기를 노리는 음모들 때문에 어린 분별력을 날카롭게 벼리지 않았으니까요. 나는 그랬어요."], ["You did not gradually open your round childish eyes wider and wider to the discovery of that impostor of a woman who calculates her stores of peace of mind for when she wakes up in the night.", "당신은 그 사기꾼 같은 여자를 발견하면서 동그란 어린아이 눈을 점점 더 크게 뜨지는 않았으니까요. 그 여자는 밤중에 깨어났을 때를 대비해 자기 마음의 평안을 비축해 두며 계산하는 사람이지요."], ["I did.”", "나는 그랬어요.\""]], [["It was no laughing matter with Estella now, nor was she summoning these remembrances from any shallow place.", "이제 에스텔라에게 그것은 웃을 일이 아니었고, 그녀가 이런 기억들을 불러내는 곳도 어떤 얕은 데가 아니었다."], ["I would not have been the cause of that look of hers for all my expectations in a heap.", "내 기대의 전부를 한데 쌓아 준다 해도, 나는 그녀의 그런 표정을 불러일으킨 장본인이 되고 싶지는 않았다."]], [["“Two things I can tell you,”", "\"두 가지를 말해 줄 수 있어요.\""], ["said Estella.", "에스텔라가 말했다."], ["“First, notwithstanding the proverb that constant dropping will wear away a stone, you may set your mind at rest that these people never will—never would in a hundred years—impair your ground with Miss Havisham, in any particular, great or small.", "\"첫째, 낙숫물이 댓돌을 뚫는다는 속담이 있긴 하지만, 그 사람들이 해비셤 양에 대한 당신의 입지를 크든 작든 어떤 점에서도 결코 손상시키지 못하리라는 것ㅡ백 년이 가도 못 하리라는 것은 마음 놓아도 돼요."], ["Second, I am beholden to you as the cause of their being so busy and so mean in vain, and there is my hand upon it.”", "둘째, 그들이 그토록 분주하고 그토록 비열하게 굴어도 헛수고가 되게 만든 원인이 당신이니, 나는 당신에게 빚을 진 셈이에요. 자, 여기 그 증표로 내 손을 내밀게요.\""]], [["As she gave it to me playfully,—for her darker mood had been but momentary—I held it and put it to my lips.", "그녀가 장난스럽게 손을 내밀었기에ㅡ그녀의 어두운 기분은 잠깐뿐이었으니까ㅡ나는 그 손을 잡아 입술에 갖다 댔다. \"우스운 사람.\""], ["“You ridiculous boy,”", "에스텔라가 말했다."], ["said Estella, “will you never take warning?", "\"당신은 끝내 경고를 새겨듣지 않을 건가요?"], ["Or do you kiss my hand in the same spirit in which I once let you kiss my cheek?”", "아니면 예전에 내가 당신더러 내 뺨에 입 맞추게 했을 때와 똑같은 심정으로 내 손에 입 맞추는 건가요?\""]], [["“What spirit was that?”", "\"그게 무슨 심정이었는데요?\""], ["said I.", "내가 말했다."]], [["“I must think a moment.", "\"잠깐 생각해 봐야겠네요."], ["A spirit of contempt for the fawners and plotters.”", "아첨꾼들과 모사꾼들을 향한 경멸의 심정이었죠.\""]], [["“If I say yes, may I kiss the cheek again?”", "\"그렇다고 대답하면, 다시 뺨에 입 맞춰도 될까요?\""]], [["“You should have asked before you touched the hand. But, yes, if you like.”", "\"손을 건드리기 전에 물었어야죠. 하지만, 좋아요, 그러고 싶다면요.\""]], [["I leaned down, and her calm face was like a statue’s.", "나는 몸을 숙였고, 그녀의 차분한 얼굴은 조각상의 그것 같았다. \"자.\""], ["“Now,” said Estella, gliding away the instant I touched her cheek, “you are to take care that I have some tea, and you are to take me to Richmond.”", "내가 뺨에 입을 대는 순간 미끄러지듯 물러나며 에스텔라가 말했다. \"당신은 내가 차를 마실 수 있게 신경 써 주고, 나를 리치먼드까지 데려다주기로 되어 있어요.\""]], [["Her reverting to this tone as if our association were forced upon us, and we were mere puppets, gave me pain; but everything in our intercourse did give me pain.", "마치 우리의 동행이 우리에게 강요된 것이고 우리는 한낱 꼭두각시에 불과하다는 듯, 그녀가 다시 그런 말투로 돌아간 것이 내게는 괴로웠다. 그러나 우리가 주고받는 모든 것이 내게는 괴로움이었다."], ["Whatever her tone with me happened to be, I could put no trust in it, and build no hope on it; and yet I went on against trust and against hope.", "그녀가 내게 어떤 말투를 쓰든, 나는 거기에 아무 신뢰도 둘 수 없었고 어떤 희망도 쌓아 올릴 수 없었다. 그러면서도 나는 신뢰를 거슬러, 희망을 거슬러 계속 나아갔다."], ["Why repeat it a thousand times?", "이것을 천 번이고 되풀이해 말한들 무엇하랴?"], ["So it always was.", "늘 그런 식이었다."]], [["I rang for the tea, and the waiter, reappearing with his magic clue, brought in by degrees some fifty adjuncts to that refreshment, but of tea not a glimpse.", "나는 종을 울려 차를 청했고, 웨이터는 마법의 실마리를 들고 다시 나타나더니, 그 다과에 딸린 부속품 쉰 가지쯤을 차례차례 들여왔으되, 정작 차는 그림자도 비치지 않았다."], ["A teaboard, cups and saucers, plates, knives and forks (including carvers), spoons (various), salt-cellars, a meek little muffin confined with the utmost precaution under a strong iron cover, Moses in the bulrushes typified by a soft bit of butter in a quantity of parsley, a pale loaf with a powdered head, two proof impressions of the bars of the kitchen fireplace on triangular bits of bread, and ultimately a fat family urn; which the waiter staggered in with, expressing in his countenance burden and suffering.", "찻쟁반, 찻잔과 받침, 접시들, 나이프와 포크(고기 써는 큰 것까지 포함해서), 숟가락(여러 가지), 소금 그릇들, 튼튼한 쇠 뚜껑 아래 더없이 조심스레 갇힌 얌전한 작은 머핀 하나, 잔뜩 쌓인 파슬리 속에 놓인 부드러운 버터 한 조각으로 형상화된 갈대밭의 모세, 머리에 가루를 뿌린 빛바랜 빵 한 덩이, 세모난 빵 조각 위에 부엌 벽난로 쇠살대의 모양이 고스란히 찍힌 자국 두 점, 그리고 마침내 뚱뚱한 가족용 주전자 하나ㅡ웨이터는 얼굴에 무게와 고통을 드러내며 그것을 비틀비틀 들고 들어왔다."], ["After a prolonged absence at this stage of the entertainment, he at length came back with a casket of precious appearance containing twigs.", "이 대접의 단계에 이르러 한참을 자리를 비웠다가, 그는 마침내 잔가지가 담긴 값나가 보이는 함 하나를 들고 돌아왔다."], ["These I steeped in hot water, and so from the whole of these appliances extracted one cup of I don’t know what for Estella.", "나는 그 잔가지를 뜨거운 물에 우려, 결국 이 온갖 기구들로부터 에스텔라를 위해 무엇인지 모를 차 한 잔을 우려냈다."]], [["The bill paid, and the waiter remembered, and the ostler not forgotten, and the chambermaid taken into consideration,—in a word, the whole house bribed into a state of contempt and animosity, and Estella’s purse much lightened,—we got into our post-coach and drove away.", "계산을 치르고, 웨이터에게 사례하고, 마부도 잊지 않고, 객실 하녀까지 고려하고ㅡ한마디로, 온 여관을 경멸과 적의의 상태에 빠지도록 매수하고, 에스텔라의 지갑은 한결 가벼워진 다음ㅡ우리는 역마차에 올라 떠났다."], ["Turning into Cheapside and rattling up Newgate Street, we were soon under the walls of which I was so ashamed.", "칩사이드로 접어들어 뉴게이트 거리를 덜컹거리며 올라가자, 우리는 이내 내가 그토록 부끄러워하던 그 담벼락 아래에 이르렀다."]], [["“What place is that?”", "\"저긴 무슨 곳이에요?\""], ["Estella asked me.", "에스텔라가 내게 물었다."]], [["I made a foolish pretence of not at first recognising it, and then told her.", "나는 처음에는 알아보지 못하는 척 어리석은 시늉을 하다가, 이윽고 그녀에게 일러 주었다."], ["As she looked at it, and drew in her head again, murmuring, “Wretches!” I would not have confessed to my visit for any consideration.", "그녀가 그곳을 바라보다가 다시 고개를 들이밀며 \"가엾은 것들!\" 하고 중얼거리는 동안, 나는 무슨 일이 있어도 내가 거기 다녀왔다는 사실은 털어놓지 않았으리라."]], [["“Mr. Jaggers,”", "\"재거스 씨는요.\""], ["said I, by way of putting it neatly on somebody else, “has the reputation of being more in the secrets of that dismal place than any man in London.”", "나는 그것을 누군가 다른 사람에게 깔끔하게 떠넘기는 셈치고 말했다. \"런던의 그 누구보다도 저 음산한 곳의 비밀에 정통하다는 평판이 있죠.\""]], [["“He is more in the secrets of every place, I think,”", "\"내 생각엔, 그분은 어디든 모든 곳의 비밀에 더 정통한 것 같아요.\""], ["said Estella, in a low voice.", "에스텔라가 낮은 목소리로 말했다."]], [["“You have been accustomed to see him often, I suppose?”", "\"당신은 그분을 자주 보는 데 익숙하겠지요?\""]], [["“I have been accustomed to see him at uncertain intervals, ever since I can remember.", "\"기억할 수 있는 때부터 줄곧, 정해지지 않은 간격을 두고 그분을 봐 오는 데 익숙해요."], ["But I know him no better now, than I did before I could speak plainly.", "하지만 지금도 그분을 또렷이 말도 못 하던 시절보다 더 잘 알지는 못해요."], ["What is your own experience of him?", "당신은 그분에 대해 어떤 경험을 했나요?"], ["Do you advance with him?”", "그분과 사이가 진전되나요?\""]], [["“Once habituated to his distrustful manner,”", "\"일단 그분의 의심 많은 태도에 익숙해지고 나서는.\""], ["said I, “I have done very well.”", "내가 말했다. \"아주 잘 지내 왔어요.\""]], [["“Are you intimate?”", "\"친밀한가요?\""]], [["“I have dined with him at his private house.”", "\"그분 자택에서 함께 식사한 적이 있어요.\""]], [["“I fancy,”", "\"내 생각엔.\""], ["said Estella, shrinking “that must be a curious place.”", "에스텔라가 움츠러들며 말했다. \"거긴 분명 묘한 곳일 거예요.\""]], [["“It is a curious place.”", "\"묘한 곳이지요.\""]], [["I should have been chary of discussing my guardian too freely even with her; but I should have gone on with the subject so far as to describe the dinner in Gerrard Street, if we had not then come into a sudden glare of gas.", "나는 그녀에게라도 내 후견인 이야기를 너무 함부로 하는 것은 삼갔어야 했을 것이다. 그러나 그때 우리가 별안간 가스등의 환한 불빛 속으로 들어서지만 않았더라면, 나는 그 화제를 이어 가 제라드 거리의 만찬 이야기까지 묘사했을 것이다."], ["It seemed, while it lasted, to be all alight and alive with that inexplicable feeling I had had before; and when we were out of it, I was as much dazed for a few moments as if I had been in lightning.", "그 불빛은 그것이 지속되는 동안, 전에 내가 느꼈던 그 설명할 수 없는 감정으로 온통 환하게 살아 있는 듯했다. 그리고 우리가 그 불빛을 벗어났을 때, 나는 마치 번개를 맞기라도 한 것처럼 잠깐 동안 멍해 있었다."]], [["So we fell into other talk, and it was principally about the way by which we were travelling, and about what parts of London lay on this side of it, and what on that.", "그래서 우리는 다른 이야기로 빠져들었는데, 주로 우리가 가고 있는 길에 관한 것, 그리고 런던의 어느 지역이 이쪽에 있고 어느 지역이 저쪽에 있는지에 관한 것이었다."], ["The great city was almost new to her, she told me, for she had never left Miss Havisham’s neighbourhood until she had gone to France, and she had merely passed through London then in going and returning.", "그녀가 내게 말하기를, 이 거대한 도시는 자기에게 거의 낯선 곳이라고 했다. 프랑스로 떠나기 전까지는 해비셤 양의 동네를 한 번도 벗어난 적이 없었고, 그때도 오가는 길에 그저 런던을 지나쳤을 뿐이라는 것이었다."], ["I asked her if my guardian had any charge of her while she remained here?", "나는 그녀가 이곳에 머무는 동안 내 후견인이 그녀를 맡고 있는지 물었다."], ["To that she emphatically said “God forbid!” and no more.", "그러자 그녀는 단호하게 \"당치도 않아요!\"라고만 말하고 더는 말하지 않았다."]], [["It was impossible for me to avoid seeing that she cared to attract me; that she made herself winning, and would have won me even if the task had needed pains.", "그녀가 나를 끌어당기려 마음 쓰고 있다는 것, 스스로를 매력적으로 만들고 있으며 설령 그 일에 애를 써야 했더라도 나를 사로잡았으리라는 것을, 나는 도저히 못 본 척할 수 없었다."], ["Yet this made me none the happier, for even if she had not taken that tone of our being disposed of by others, I should have felt that she held my heart in her hand because she wilfully chose to do it, and not because it would have wrung any tenderness in her to crush it and throw it away.", "그러나 이것이 나를 조금도 더 행복하게 만들지는 못했다. 설령 그녀가 우리가 남들에 의해 처분되는 처지라는 그런 말투를 쓰지 않았더라도, 나는 그녀가 내 마음을 손아귀에 쥐고 있는 까닭이, 그 마음을 짓이겨 내버리는 일이 그녀에게 어떤 애틋함이라도 자아내기 때문이 아니라, 단지 그녀가 일부러 그러기로 택했기 때문임을 느꼈을 테니까."]], [["When we passed through Hammersmith, I showed her where Mr. Matthew Pocket lived, and said it was no great way from Richmond, and that I hoped I should see her sometimes.", "해머스미스를 지날 때 나는 그녀에게 매슈 포켓 선생이 사는 곳을 가리켜 보이며, 리치먼드에서 그리 멀지 않으니 이따금 당신을 볼 수 있기를 바란다고 말했다."]], [["“O yes, you are to see me; you are to come when you think proper; you are to be mentioned to the family; indeed you are already mentioned.”", "\"아 그럼요, 나를 보게 될 거예요. 적당하다 싶을 때 오면 돼요. 당신 이야기는 그 댁 식구들에게 일러두기로 되어 있어요. 실은 이미 일러두었고요.\""]], [["I inquired was it a large household she was going to be a member of?", "나는 그녀가 식구가 될 그 집이 큰 집안이냐고 물었다."]], [["“No; there are only two; mother and daughter.", "\"아니요, 둘뿐이에요. 어머니와 딸이지요."], ["The mother is a lady of some station, though not averse to increasing her income.”", "그 어머니는 신분이 좀 있는 부인인데, 그렇다고 수입을 늘리는 일을 마다하지는 않아요.\""]], [["“I wonder Miss Havisham could part with you again so soon.”", "\"해비셤 양이 그렇게 빨리 당신을 또 떠나보낼 수 있다니 의외군요.\""]], [["“It is a part of Miss Havisham’s plans for me, Pip,”", "\"그게 나에 대한 해비셤 양의 계획의 일부예요, 핍.\" 에스텔라가 지친 듯 한숨을 쉬며 말했다."], ["said Estella, with a sigh, as if she were tired; “I am to write to her constantly and see her regularly and report how I go on,—I and the jewels,—for they are nearly all mine now.”", "\"나는 그분께 끊임없이 편지를 쓰고 정기적으로 찾아뵙고 어떻게 지내는지 보고해야 해요. 나도, 그리고 보석들도요. 이제 그 보석들은 거의 다 내 것이거든요.\""]], [["It was the first time she had ever called me by my name. Of course she did so purposely, and knew that I should treasure it up.", "그녀가 내 이름을 부른 것은 그때가 처음이었다. 물론 그녀는 일부러 그렇게 한 것이었고, 내가 그것을 소중히 간직하리라는 것을 알고 있었다."]], [["We came to Richmond all too soon, and our destination there was a house by the green,—a staid old house, where hoops and powder and patches, embroidered coats, rolled stockings, ruffles and swords, had had their court days many a time.", "우리는 너무도 빨리 리치먼드에 이르렀고, 그곳 우리의 목적지는 잔디밭 곁에 있는 한 집이었다. 차분하고 오래된 집으로, 둥근 테 버팀살과 머리분과 애교점, 수놓은 외투, 둘둘 말아 올린 양말, 주름 장식과 검 따위가 여러 번 그 화려한 사교의 나날을 누렸던 곳이었다."], ["Some ancient trees before the house were still cut into fashions as formal and unnatural as the hoops and wigs and stiff skirts; but their own allotted places in the great procession of the dead were not far off, and they would soon drop into them and go the silent way of the rest.", "집 앞의 오래된 나무 몇 그루는 여전히, 그 둥근 테나 가발이나 빳빳한 치마만큼이나 격식 차린 부자연스러운 모양으로 다듬어져 있었다. 그러나 죽은 것들의 그 거대한 행렬에서 그들 자신에게 정해진 자리도 멀지 않았으니, 그들도 곧 거기에 떨어져 나머지 모든 것이 간 그 고요한 길을 따라가게 될 터였다."]], [["A bell with an old voice—which I dare say in its time had often said to the house, Here is the green farthingale, Here is the diamond-hilted sword, Here are the shoes with red heels and the blue solitaire—sounded gravely in the moonlight, and two cherry-coloured maids came fluttering out to receive Estella.", "낡은 목소리를 지닌 종 하나가ㅡ아마도 그 시절에는 이 집을 향해, 초록색 버팀치마가 왔습니다, 다이아몬드 자루 검이 왔습니다, 빨간 굽 구두와 푸른 외알 보석 장식이 왔습니다, 하고 곧잘 알렸을 그 종이ㅡ달빛 속에서 장중하게 울렸고, 버찌 빛 옷을 입은 하녀 둘이 팔랑팔랑 나와 에스텔라를 맞았다."], ["The doorway soon absorbed her boxes, and she gave me her hand and a smile, and said good-night, and was absorbed likewise.", "현관은 이내 그녀의 짐 상자들을 빨아들였고, 그녀는 내게 손과 미소를 건네며 잘 자라는 인사를 하고는, 그녀 또한 그렇게 빨려 들어갔다."], ["And still I stood looking at the house, thinking how happy I should be if I lived there with her, and knowing that I never was happy with her, but always miserable.", "그래도 나는 여전히 그 집을 바라보며 서서, 그녀와 함께 저곳에서 산다면 얼마나 행복할까 생각하면서도, 내가 그녀와 함께 있어 결코 행복했던 적이 없으며 늘 비참했을 뿐임을 알고 있었다."]], [["I got into the carriage to be taken back to Hammersmith, and I got in with a bad heart-ache, and I got out with a worse heart-ache.", "나는 해머스미스로 돌아가기 위해 마차에 올랐고, 가슴앓이를 안고 올랐다가, 더 심한 가슴앓이를 안고 내렸다."], ["At our own door, I found little Jane Pocket coming home from a little party escorted by her little lover; and I envied her little lover, in spite of his being subject to Flopson.", "우리 집 문 앞에서 나는, 어린 제인 포켓이 어린 연인의 호위를 받으며 어느 조촐한 모임에서 집으로 돌아오는 것을 보았다. 그리고 나는 그 어린 연인이 플롭슨에게 휘둘리는 처지인데도 불구하고, 그가 부러웠다."]], [["Mr. Pocket was out lecturing; for, he was a most delightful lecturer on domestic economy, and his treatises on the management of children and servants were considered the very best text-books on those themes.", "포켓 선생은 강연을 하러 나가고 없었다. 그는 가정 경제에 관한 더없이 유쾌한 강연자였으며, 아이들과 하인들을 다루는 법에 관한 그의 논저들은 그 주제에 관한 한 가장 훌륭한 교본으로 여겨졌으니 말이다."], ["But Mrs. Pocket was at home, and was in a little difficulty, on account of the baby’s having been accommodated with a needle-case to keep him quiet during the unaccountable absence (with a relative in the Foot Guards) of Millers.", "그러나 포켓 부인은 집에 있었고, 작은 곤경에 빠져 있었다. 밀러스가 (근위 보병대에 있는 친척에게 가느라) 영문 모를 자리를 비운 사이, 아기를 조용히 시키느라 바늘쌈지를 쥐여 주었던 탓이었다."], ["And more needles were missing than it could be regarded as quite wholesome for a patient of such tender years either to apply externally or to take as a tonic.", "그런데 사라진 바늘의 수가, 그토록 어린 환자가 겉에 바르든 강장제 삼아 먹든 도무지 몸에 이롭다고 볼 수 없을 만큼이나 많았다."]], [["Mr. Pocket being justly celebrated for giving most excellent practical advice, and for having a clear and sound perception of things and a highly judicious mind, I had some notion in my heart-ache of begging him to accept my confidence.", "포켓 선생은 더없이 훌륭한 실질적 조언을 해 주는 것으로, 또 사물에 대한 분명하고 건전한 통찰과 매우 사려 깊은 정신을 지닌 것으로 마땅히 이름이 높았던 터라, 나는 가슴앓이 속에서 그에게 내 비밀을 털어놓아 달라 청해 볼까 하는 생각이 어렴풋이 들었다."], ["But happening to look up at Mrs. Pocket as she sat reading her book of dignities after prescribing Bed as a sovereign remedy for baby, I thought—Well—No, I wouldn’t.", "그러나 아기에게 잠자리를 만병통치약으로 처방해 준 뒤 작위(爵位) 명부를 읽고 앉아 있는 포켓 부인을 마침 올려다보다가, 나는 생각했다ㅡ글쎄ㅡ아니, 그만두자고."]], [["# Chapter XXXIV.", "# Chapter XXXIV."]], [["As I had grown accustomed to my expectations, I had insensibly begun to notice their effect upon myself and those around me.", "내가 내 유산에 익숙해지면서, 나는 어느새 그것이 나 자신과 주위 사람들에게 끼치는 영향을 눈여겨보기 시작했다."], ["Their influence on my own character I disguised from my recognition as much as possible, but I knew very well that it was not all good.", "그것이 내 성격에 미친 영향을 나는 가능한 한 스스로 인정하지 않으려 애썼으나, 그것이 결코 다 좋은 것만은 아니라는 사실은 너무도 잘 알고 있었다."], ["I lived in a state of chronic uneasiness respecting my behaviour to Joe.", "나는 조에게 대하는 내 태도에 관해 만성적인 불안 상태에 빠져 살았다."], ["My conscience was not by any means comfortable about Biddy.", "비디에 대한 내 양심도 결코 편안하지 않았다."], ["When I woke up in the night,—like Camilla,—I used to think, with a weariness on my spirits, that I should have been happier and better if I had never seen Miss Havisham’s face, and had risen to manhood content to be partners with Joe in the honest old forge.", "한밤중에 깨어날 때면 — 카밀라처럼 — 나는 마음에 무거운 권태를 느끼며 이런 생각을 하곤 했다. 미스 해비셤의 얼굴을 한 번도 보지 않았더라면, 그리고 정직한 옛 대장간에서 조와 동업자가 되는 것에 만족하며 어른이 되었더라면, 나는 더 행복하고 더 나은 사람이 되었으리라고."], ["Many a time of an evening, when I sat alone looking at the fire, I thought, after all there was no fire like the forge fire and the kitchen fire at home.", "저녁이면 여러 번, 혼자 앉아 불을 바라보면서, 나는 따지고 보면 집 대장간의 불과 부엌의 불만 한 불은 어디에도 없다고 생각했다."]], [["Yet Estella was so inseparable from all my restlessness and disquiet of mind, that I really fell into confusion as to the limits of my own part in its production.", "그러나 에스텔라는 내 모든 안절부절못함과 마음의 불안과 너무도 떼어 놓을 수 없었으므로, 나는 그 불안을 만들어 내는 데 내 몫이 어디까지인지 그 한계를 두고 정말로 혼란에 빠지고 말았다."], ["That is to say, supposing I had had no expectations, and yet had had Estella to think of, I could not make out to my satisfaction that I should have done much better.", "다시 말해, 만약 내게 유산에 대한 기대가 전혀 없었다 해도 여전히 에스텔라를 생각해야 했다면, 내 처지가 지금보다 훨씬 나았으리라고 만족스럽게 결론 내릴 수가 없었던 것이다."], ["Now, concerning the influence of my position on others, I was in no such difficulty, and so I perceived—though dimly enough perhaps—that it was not beneficial to anybody, and, above all, that it was not beneficial to Herbert.", "그런데 내 처지가 다른 사람들에게 미치는 영향에 관해서는 그런 어려움이 없었으니, 그래서 나는 — 아마 어렴풋이나마 — 그것이 누구에게도 이롭지 않다는 것을, 무엇보다도 허버트에게 이롭지 않다는 것을 알아차렸다."], ["My lavish habits led his easy nature into expenses that he could not afford, corrupted the simplicity of his life, and disturbed his peace with anxieties and regrets.", "내 헤픈 습관은 그의 무던한 천성을 그가 감당할 수 없는 지출로 이끌었고, 그의 소박한 삶을 타락시켰으며, 그의 평온을 근심과 후회로 어지럽혔다."], ["I was not at all remorseful for having unwittingly set those other branches of the Pocket family to the poor arts they practised; because such littlenesses were their natural bent, and would have been evoked by anybody else, if I had left them slumbering.", "나는 포켓 집안의 다른 곁가지들을 무심코 그들이 부리던 그 옹졸한 술수로 몰아넣은 것에 대해서는 조금도 자책하지 않았다. 그러한 자질구레함은 그들의 타고난 기질이었으므로, 내가 그것을 잠들게 내버려 두었더라도 다른 누군가에 의해 불려 나왔을 것이기 때문이다."], ["But Herbert’s was a very different case, and it often caused me a twinge to think that I had done him evil service in crowding his sparely furnished chambers with incongruous upholstery work, and placing the Canary-breasted Avenger at his disposal.", "그러나 허버트의 경우는 사정이 아주 달랐으니, 검소하게 꾸며진 그의 방을 어울리지 않는 가구 장식으로 가득 채우고 카나리아 가슴빛 복수자를 그가 부리도록 안겨 줌으로써 그에게 못쓸 짓을 했다는 생각이 자주 나를 찔러 댔다."]], [["So now, as an infallible way of making little ease great ease, I began to contract a quantity of debt.", "그래서 이제, 작은 안락을 큰 안락으로 만드는 틀림없는 방법으로, 나는 빚을 잔뜩 끌어들이기 시작했다."], ["I could hardly begin but Herbert must begin too, so he soon followed.", "내가 시작하기가 무섭게 허버트도 따라 시작할 수밖에 없었으니, 그도 곧 뒤를 따랐다."], ["At Startop’s suggestion, we put ourselves down for election into a club called The Finches of the Grove: the object of which institution I have never divined, if it were not that the members should dine expensively once a fortnight, to quarrel among themselves as much as possible after dinner, and to cause six waiters to get drunk on the stairs.", "스타톱의 제안으로 우리는 '숲속의 핀치들'이라는 클럽의 회원으로 선출되도록 이름을 올렸다. 그 단체의 목적이 회원들이 보름에 한 번씩 비싼 식사를 하고, 식후에는 가능한 한 자기들끼리 싸우고, 여섯 명의 종업원을 계단에서 취하게 만드는 것이 아니라면, 나는 그 목적을 끝내 짐작조차 할 수 없었다."], ["I know that these gratifying social ends were so invariably accomplished, that Herbert and I understood nothing else to be referred to in the first standing toast of the society: which ran “Gentlemen, may the present promotion of good feeling ever reign predominant among the Finches of the Grove.”", "이 흐뭇한 사교적 목표들이 어찌나 한결같이 달성되었던지, 허버트와 나는 이 단체의 첫 번째 정례 건배사가 다른 무엇을 가리키는 것이라고는 도무지 이해할 수 없었다. 그 건배사는 이러했다. \"신사 여러분, 부디 지금 이 화기애애한 분위기가 숲속의 핀치들 사이에 길이 으뜸으로 군림하기를.\""]], [["The Finches spent their money foolishly (the Hotel we dined at was in Covent Garden), and the first Finch I saw when I had the honour of joining the Grove was Bentley Drummle, at that time floundering about town in a cab of his own, and doing a great deal of damage to the posts at the street corners.", "핀치들은 돈을 어리석게 써 댔으며(우리가 식사한 호텔은 코번트 가든에 있었다), 내가 영광스럽게도 숲에 가입했을 때 처음 본 핀치는 벤틀리 드러믈이었다. 그는 그 무렵 자기 마차를 몰고 시내를 비틀비틀 헤매고 다니면서 길모퉁이 기둥들에 막대한 손해를 입히고 있었다."], ["Occasionally, he shot himself out of his equipage headforemost over the apron; and I saw him on one occasion deliver himself at the door of the Grove in this unintentional way—like coals.", "이따금 그는 자기 마차의 가림막 너머로 머리부터 곤두박질쳐 떨어지곤 했다. 한번은 그가 이런 의도치 않은 방식으로 — 마치 석탄처럼 — 숲의 문 앞에 자기 몸을 부려 놓는 것을 본 적도 있었다."], ["But here I anticipate a little, for I was not a Finch, and could not be, according to the sacred laws of the society, until I came of age.", "그러나 여기서 나는 조금 앞서 가고 있는 셈이니, 나는 핀치가 아니었고, 또 이 단체의 신성한 규약에 따르면 성년이 되기 전까지는 핀치가 될 수도 없었기 때문이다."]], [["In my confidence in my own resources, I would willingly have taken Herbert’s expenses on myself; but Herbert was proud, and I could make no such proposal to him.", "내 재력에 대한 자신감에서, 나는 기꺼이 허버트의 비용을 내가 떠맡고 싶었으나, 허버트는 자존심이 강했으므로 그에게 그런 제안을 할 수가 없었다."], ["So he got into difficulties in every direction, and continued to look about him.", "그래서 그는 사방에서 곤경에 빠진 채, 계속해서 두리번거리며 기회를 살폈다."], ["When we gradually fell into keeping late hours and late company, I noticed that he looked about him with a desponding eye at breakfast-time; that he began to look about him more hopefully about midday; that he drooped when he came into dinner; that he seemed to descry Capital in the distance, rather clearly, after dinner; that he all but realised Capital towards midnight; and that at about two o’clock in the morning, he became so deeply despondent again as to talk of buying a rifle and going to America, with a general purpose of compelling buffaloes to make his fortune.", "우리가 차츰 늦은 시간과 늦은 교제에 빠져들면서, 나는 그가 아침 식사 때면 낙담한 눈으로 주위를 둘러보고, 한낮쯤이면 좀 더 희망에 찬 눈으로 둘러보기 시작하며, 저녁 식사 자리에 들어올 때면 풀이 죽고, 식후에는 멀리 자본이 꽤 또렷이 보이는 듯하고, 자정 무렵에는 그 자본을 거의 손에 넣은 것 같다가, 새벽 두 시쯤이면 다시금 너무도 깊이 낙담해서, 소총을 한 자루 사서 미국으로 건너가, 버펄로 떼를 닦달해 한밑천 잡겠다는 막연한 계획을 이야기하는 것을 눈여겨보았다."]], [["I was usually at Hammersmith about half the week, and when I was at Hammersmith I haunted Richmond, whereof separately by and by.", "나는 보통 일주일의 절반쯤은 해머스미스에 가 있었고, 해머스미스에 있을 때면 리치먼드를 들락거렸는데, 그 일은 따로 차차 이야기하겠다."], ["Herbert would often come to Hammersmith when I was there, and I think at those seasons his father would occasionally have some passing perception that the opening he was looking for, had not appeared yet.", "내가 그곳에 있을 때면 허버트도 자주 해머스미스로 오곤 했는데, 그럴 때면 그의 아버지가 이따금 그가 찾고 있는 기회가 아직 나타나지 않았다는 것을 스쳐 가듯 깨닫기도 했으리라 생각한다."], ["But in the general tumbling up of the family, his tumbling out in life somewhere, was a thing to transact itself somehow.", "그러나 온 식구가 한데 뒤엉켜 굴러가는 와중에, 그가 인생의 어딘가로 굴러 나가는 일은 어떻게든 알아서 처리되어야 할 일이었다."], ["In the meantime Mr. Pocket grew greyer, and tried oftener to lift himself out of his perplexities by the hair. While Mrs.", "그러는 동안 포켓 씨는 머리가 더 희끗희끗해졌고, 자신의 곤혹스러움에서 벗어나려 제 머리카락을 잡아당겨 자신을 들어 올리려는 시도를 더 자주 했다."], ["Pocket tripped up the family with her footstool, read her book of dignities, lost her pocket-handkerchief, told us about her grandpapa, and taught the young idea how to shoot, by shooting it into bed whenever it attracted her notice.", "한편 포켓 부인은 발받침으로 식구들을 걸려 넘어지게 하고, 작위에 관한 책을 읽고, 손수건을 잃어버리고, 우리에게 자기 할아버지 이야기를 늘어놓고, 어린 자식이 눈에 띌 때마다 침대 속으로 던져 넣음으로써 그 어린 새싹이 자라나는 법을 가르쳤다."]], [["As I am now generalising a period of my life with the object of clearing my way before me, I can scarcely do so better than by at once completing the description of our usual manners and customs at Barnard’s Inn.", "이제 나는 앞길을 정리하기 위한 목적에서 내 인생의 한 시기를 통틀어 이야기하고 있으므로, 바너즈 인에서의 우리의 일상적인 생활 방식과 습관에 대한 묘사를 곧장 마무리 짓는 것보다 더 나은 방법은 없을 듯싶다."]], [["We spent as much money as we could, and got as little for it as people could make up their minds to give us.", "우리는 쓸 수 있는 만큼 돈을 썼고, 사람들이 마음먹고 내줄 수 있는 만큼 적은 대가만을 그 돈으로 얻었다."], ["We were always more or less miserable, and most of our acquaintance were in the same condition.", "우리는 늘 다소간 비참했고, 우리가 아는 사람들 대부분도 같은 처지였다."], ["There was a gay fiction among us that we were constantly enjoying ourselves, and a skeleton truth that we never did.", "우리 사이에는 우리가 끊임없이 즐기며 지낸다는 유쾌한 허구와, 결코 그러지 못한다는 앙상한 진실이 함께 있었다."], ["To the best of my belief, our case was in the last aspect a rather common one.", "내가 믿는 바로는, 우리의 경우는 그 마지막 측면에서 꽤나 흔한 사례였다."]], [["Every morning, with an air ever new, Herbert went into the City to look about him.", "매일 아침 허버트는 늘 새로운 기색으로, 기회를 살피러 시티로 나갔다."], ["I often paid him a visit in the dark back-room in which he consorted with an ink-jar, a hat-peg, a coal-box, a string-box, an almanac, a desk and stool, and a ruler; and I do not remember that I ever saw him do anything else but look about him.", "나는 그가 잉크 항아리 하나, 모자걸이 하나, 석탄통 하나, 노끈통 하나, 달력 하나, 책상과 걸상 하나, 그리고 자 하나와 더불어 지내는 그 어두컴컴한 뒷방으로 자주 그를 찾아갔다. 그러나 그가 두리번거리며 기회를 살피는 것 말고 다른 무언가를 하는 모습은 본 기억이 없다."], ["If we all did what we undertake to do, as faithfully as Herbert did, we might live in a Republic of the Virtues.", "만약 우리 모두가 하기로 한 일을 허버트만큼 충실히 해낸다면, 우리는 미덕의 공화국에서 살 수 있을 것이다."], ["He had nothing else to do, poor fellow, except at a certain hour of every afternoon to “go to Lloyd’s”—in observance of a ceremony of seeing his principal, I think.", "가엾은 친구에게는, 매일 오후 일정한 시간에 \"로이즈에 가는\" 것 말고는 — 내 생각엔 자기 윗사람을 알현하는 의식을 치르려고 — 달리 할 일이 없었다."], ["He never did anything else in connection with Lloyd’s that I could find out, except come back again.", "내가 알아낼 수 있는 한, 그가 로이즈와 관련해 한 일이라고는 다시 돌아오는 것 말고는 아무것도 없었다."], ["When he felt his case unusually serious, and that he positively must find an opening, he would go on ’Change at a busy time, and walk in and out, in a kind of gloomy country dance figure, among the assembled magnates.", "자기 사정이 유난히 심각하다고, 그래서 무슨 일이 있어도 기회를 찾아내야 한다고 느낄 때면, 그는 거래소가 붐비는 시간에 그곳에 가서, 모여 있는 거물들 사이를 일종의 음울한 시골 춤사위처럼 이리저리 드나들곤 했다. \"이봐,\""], ["“For,” says Herbert to me, coming home to dinner on one of those special occasions, “I find the truth to be, Handel, that an opening won’t come to one, but one must go to it,—so I have been.”", "그런 특별한 경우의 어느 날 저녁 식사를 하러 집에 돌아오며 허버트가 내게 말했다. \"내가 깨달은 진실은 말이야, 핸들, 기회란 사람한테 찾아오는 게 아니라 사람이 그리로 찾아가야 한다는 거야 — 그래서 다녀왔지.\""]], [["If we had been less attached to one another, I think we must have hated one another regularly every morning.", "만약 우리가 서로에게 덜 정들어 있었다면, 우리는 아마 매일 아침 어김없이 서로를 미워했을 것이다."], ["I detested the chambers beyond expression at that period of repentance, and could not endure the sight of the Avenger’s livery; which had a more expensive and a less remunerative appearance then than at any other time in the four-and-twenty hours.", "그 참회의 시기에 나는 그 방을 이루 말할 수 없이 혐오했고, 복수자의 제복을 보는 것도 견딜 수 없었다. 그 제복은 하루 스물네 시간 중 다른 어느 때보다 그 시각에 더 값비싸고도 덜 본전 뽑는 모양새를 띠었다."], ["As we got more and more into debt, breakfast became a hollower and hollower form, and, being on one occasion at breakfast-time threatened (by letter) with legal proceedings, “not unwholly unconnected,” as my local paper might put it, “with jewelery,” I went so far as to seize the Avenger by his blue collar and shake him off his feet,—so that he was actually in the air, like a booted Cupid,—for presuming to suppose that we wanted a roll.", "우리가 점점 더 빚더미에 빠져들면서, 아침 식사는 점점 더 공허한 형식으로 변해 갔고, 한번은 아침 식사 시간에 (편지로) 법적 절차를 밟겠다는 위협을 받았는데 — 우리 동네 신문이라면 \"보석류와 전혀 무관하지 않은\"이라고 표현했을 — 나는 우리가 빵 한 조각을 원한다고 감히 넘겨짚었다는 이유로, 복수자의 푸른 옷깃을 움켜쥐고 발이 땅에서 떨어지도록 흔들어 대기까지 했다 — 그래서 그는 마치 장화 신은 큐피드처럼 정말로 공중에 떠 있었다."]], [["At certain times—meaning at uncertain times, for they depended on our humour—I would say to Herbert, as if it were a remarkable discovery,—", "어떤 때면 — 정해지지 않은 때를 뜻하는데, 그것은 우리 기분에 달려 있었으니까 — 나는 마치 대단한 발견이라도 한 듯 허버트에게 이렇게 말하곤 했다."]], [["“My dear Herbert, we are getting on badly.”", "\"이보게 허버트, 우리 형편이 영 안 좋아지고 있군.\""]], [["“My dear Handel,”", "\"이보게 핸들,\""], ["Herbert would say to me, in all sincerity, “if you will believe me, those very words were on my lips, by a strange coincidence.”", "허버트는 더없이 진지하게 내게 대꾸하곤 했다. \"내 말을 믿어 준다면, 묘한 우연이지만 바로 그 말이 내 입술에 막 맴돌고 있었다네.\""]], [["“Then, Herbert,”", "\"그러면 허버트,\""], ["I would respond, “let us look into our affairs.”", "나는 응수하곤 했다. \"우리 형편을 한번 따져 보세.\""]], [["We always derived profound satisfaction from making an appointment for this purpose.", "우리는 늘 이 목적을 위한 약속을 정하는 데서 깊은 만족을 얻었다."], ["I always thought this was business, this was the way to confront the thing, this was the way to take the foe by the throat.", "나는 언제나 이것이야말로 일이라고, 이것이 문제를 정면으로 마주하는 방식이라고, 이것이 적의 멱살을 잡는 방식이라고 생각했다."], ["And I know Herbert thought so too.", "그리고 허버트도 그렇게 생각했다는 것을 나는 안다."]], [["We ordered something rather special for dinner, with a bottle of something similarly out of the common way, in order that our minds might be fortified for the occasion, and we might come well up to the mark.", "우리는 이 행사를 위해 마음의 무장을 단단히 하고 제대로 임할 수 있도록, 저녁 식사로 다소 특별한 무언가를 시켰고, 그에 못지않게 평범하지 않은 무언가를 한 병 곁들였다."], ["Dinner over, we produced a bundle of pens, a copious supply of ink, and a goodly show of writing and blotting paper.", "식사가 끝나면, 우리는 펜 한 다발과 넉넉한 양의 잉크, 그리고 그득한 필기지와 압지를 꺼내 놓았다."], ["For there was something very comfortable in having plenty of stationery.", "문방구가 잔뜩 있다는 데에는 무척 마음 든든한 구석이 있었기 때문이다."]], [["I would then take a sheet of paper, and write across the top of it, in a neat hand, the heading, “Memorandum of Pip’s debts”; with Barnard’s Inn and the date very carefully added.", "그런 다음 나는 종이 한 장을 집어 들고, 깔끔한 글씨로 맨 위에 가로질러 \"핍의 빚 명세서\"라는 제목을 적고, 바너즈 인과 날짜를 아주 정성스레 덧붙였다."], ["Herbert would also take a sheet of paper, and write across it with similar formalities, “Memorandum of Herbert’s debts.”", "허버트도 종이 한 장을 집어 들어, 비슷한 격식을 갖추어 가로질러 \"허버트의 빚 명세서\"라고 적었다."]], [["Each of us would then refer to a confused heap of papers at his side, which had been thrown into drawers, worn into holes in pockets, half burnt in lighting candles, stuck for weeks into the looking-glass, and otherwise damaged.", "그러고 나서 우리는 각자 곁에 놓인 어수선한 종이 더미를 들춰 보았는데, 그것들은 서랍 속에 처박혀 있거나, 주머니 속에서 닳아 구멍이 나거나, 촛불을 켜다가 반쯤 타거나, 몇 주씩 거울 틈에 끼워져 있거나, 그 밖의 방식으로 망가진 것들이었다."], ["The sound of our pens going refreshed us exceedingly, insomuch that I sometimes found it difficult to distinguish between this edifying business proceeding and actually paying the money.", "우리의 펜이 사각거리는 소리는 우리를 어찌나 새롭게 북돋아 주던지, 나는 이따금 이 교훈적인 사무 처리와 실제로 돈을 갚는 것을 구별하기가 어렵다고 느낄 정도였다."], ["In point of meritorious character, the two things seemed about equal.", "갸륵한 성격으로 따지자면, 그 두 가지는 거의 막상막하로 보였다."]], [["When we had written a little while, I would ask Herbert how he got on?", "한참 동안 적고 나면, 나는 허버트에게 일이 잘 되어 가느냐고 묻곤 했다."], ["Herbert probably would have been scratching his head in a most rueful manner at the sight of his accumulating figures.", "허버트는 아마 자기 숫자들이 불어나는 것을 보고는 더없이 침통한 태도로 머리를 긁적이고 있었을 것이다."]], [["“They are mounting up, Handel,”", "\"점점 불어나는군, 핸들,\""], ["Herbert would say; “upon my life, they are mounting up.”", "허버트는 말하곤 했다. \"정말이지, 점점 불어나고 있어.\""]], [["“Be firm, Herbert,” I would retort, plying my own pen with great assiduity.", "\"굳세게 마음먹게, 허버트,\" 나는 내 펜을 무척 부지런히 놀리며 받아치곤 했다."], ["“Look the thing in the face.", "\"문제를 정면으로 바라보게."], ["Look into your affairs.", "자네 형편을 들여다보게."], ["Stare them out of countenance.”", "노려봐서 기를 죽여 버리게.\""]], [["“So I would, Handel, only they are staring me out of countenance.”", "\"나도 그러고 싶네만, 핸들, 외려 그것들이 나를 노려봐서 기를 죽이고 있으니 탈일세.\""]], [["However, my determined manner would have its effect, and Herbert would fall to work again.", "그러나 나의 단호한 태도는 효과를 발휘했고, 허버트는 다시 일에 매달리곤 했다."], ["After a time he would give up once more, on the plea that he had not got Cobbs’s bill, or Lobbs’s, or Nobbs’s, as the case might be.", "얼마 후 그는 코브스의 청구서를, 아니면 로브스의, 또는 노브스의 청구서를, 경우에 따라 아직 못 받았다는 핑계로 다시 한 번 손을 놓곤 했다."]], [["“Then, Herbert, estimate; estimate it in round numbers, and put it down.”", "\"그러면 허버트, 어림잡게. 어림수로 헤아려서 적어 두게.\""]], [["“What a fellow of resource you are!”", "\"자네는 어찌 그리 수완이 좋은가!\""], ["my friend would reply, with admiration.", "내 친구는 감탄하며 대꾸하곤 했다."], ["“Really your business powers are very remarkable.”", "\"정말이지 자네의 사무 능력은 무척 놀랍다네.\""]], [["I thought so too.", "나도 그렇게 생각했다."], ["I established with myself, on these occasions, the reputation of a first-rate man of business,—prompt, decisive, energetic, clear, cool-headed.", "나는 이런 경우에 스스로에게 일류 사업가라는 평판을 — 신속하고, 결단력 있고, 정력적이고, 명료하며, 냉철한 사람이라는 평판을 — 세워 두었다."], ["When I had got all my responsibilities down upon my list, I compared each with the bill, and ticked it off.", "내 모든 부채를 내 명세서에 적어 내려간 뒤, 나는 그 하나하나를 청구서와 견주어 보고 표시를 해 나갔다."], ["My self-approval when I ticked an entry was quite a luxurious sensation.", "한 항목에 표시를 할 때마다 느끼는 자기만족은 그야말로 사치스러운 감각이었다."], ["When I had no more ticks to make, I folded all my bills up uniformly, docketed each on the back, and tied the whole into a symmetrical bundle.", "더 이상 표시할 것이 없으면, 나는 청구서들을 모두 가지런히 접어 뒷면에 각각 표제를 달고, 전부를 좌우 균형 잡힌 다발로 묶었다."], ["Then I did the same for Herbert (who modestly said he had not my administrative genius), and felt that I had brought his affairs into a focus for him.", "그런 다음 나는 허버트의 것도 똑같이 해 주었고(그는 겸손하게 자기에겐 나 같은 관리의 재능이 없다고 말했다), 내가 그의 형편을 그를 위해 한데 초점에 모아 주었다고 느꼈다."]], [["My business habits had one other bright feature, which I called “leaving a Margin.”", "내 사무 습관에는 또 하나의 빛나는 특징이 있었으니, 나는 그것을 \"여유분 남기기\"라고 불렀다."], ["For example; supposing Herbert’s debts to be one hundred and sixty-four pounds four-and-twopence, I would say, “Leave a margin, and put them down at two hundred.”", "예를 들어, 허버트의 빚이 백육십사 파운드 사 실링 이 펜스라고 치면, 나는 이렇게 말하곤 했다. \"여유를 남겨서, 이백 파운드로 적어 두게.\""], ["Or, supposing my own to be four times as much, I would leave a margin, and put them down at seven hundred.", "또는 내 빚이 그 네 배라고 치면, 나는 여유를 남겨 칠백 파운드로 적어 두었다."], ["I had the highest opinion of the wisdom of this same Margin, but I am bound to acknowledge that on looking back, I deem it to have been an expensive device.", "나는 이 여유분이라는 것의 지혜를 더없이 높이 평가했으나, 돌이켜 보건대 그것이 값비싼 술책이었음을 인정하지 않을 수 없다."], ["For, we always ran into new debt immediately, to the full extent of the margin, and sometimes, in the sense of freedom and solvency it imparted, got pretty far on into another margin.", "우리는 늘 그 여유분의 한도까지 곧장 새 빚을 끌어들였고, 때로는 그것이 안겨 주는 자유와 지급 능력의 느낌에 취해, 또 다른 여유분 속으로 꽤 멀리 들어가기도 했기 때문이다."]], [["But there was a calm, a rest, a virtuous hush, consequent on these examinations of our affairs that gave me, for the time, an admirable opinion of myself.", "그러나 우리의 형편을 이렇게 점검하고 나면 뒤따르는 어떤 평온함, 어떤 안식, 어떤 미덕에 찬 고요함이 있어, 그것이 한동안 나로 하여금 나 자신에 대해 더없이 흐뭇한 견해를 품게 했다."], ["Soothed by my exertions, my method, and Herbert’s compliments, I would sit with his symmetrical bundle and my own on the table before me among the stationery, and feel like a Bank of some sort, rather than a private individual.", "나의 노력과, 나의 방식과, 허버트의 칭찬에 마음이 누그러진 채, 나는 내 앞 탁자 위의 문방구들 사이에 그의 균형 잡힌 다발과 내 다발을 놓아두고 앉아, 한 개인이라기보다는 일종의 은행이라도 된 듯한 기분을 느끼곤 했다."]], [["We shut our outer door on these solemn occasions, in order that we might not be interrupted.", "우리는 이런 엄숙한 경우에는 방해받지 않도록 바깥문을 닫아걸었다."], ["I had fallen into my serene state one evening, when we heard a letter dropped through the slit in the said door, and fall on the ground.", "어느 날 저녁 내가 그 고요한 상태에 빠져들었을 때, 바로 그 문의 틈으로 편지 한 통이 떨어져 바닥에 닿는 소리가 들렸다."], ["“It’s for you, Handel,”", "\"자네한테 온 거야, 핸들,\""], ["said Herbert, going out and coming back with it, “and I hope there is nothing the matter.”", "허버트가 나가서 그것을 가지고 돌아오며 말했다. \"별일 아니어야 할 텐데.\""], ["This was in allusion to its heavy black seal and border.", "이는 그 편지의 묵직한 검은 봉인과 검은 테두리를 두고 한 말이었다."]], [["The letter was signed Trabb & Co., and its contents were simply, that I was an honoured sir, and that they begged to inform me that Mrs. J. Gargery had departed this life on Monday last at twenty minutes past six in the evening, and that my attendance was requested at the interment on Monday next at three o’clock in the afternoon.", "그 편지에는 트래브 상회의 서명이 있었고, 그 내용인즉 간단히, 내가 존경하는 귀하시라는 것과, 조 가저리 부인이 지난 월요일 저녁 여섯 시 이십 분에 이 세상을 떠나셨음을 삼가 알려 드린다는 것과, 다음 월요일 오후 세 시의 장례식에 참석해 주시기를 바란다는 것이었다."]], [["# Chapter XXXV.", "# Chapter XXXV."]], [["It was the first time that a grave had opened in my road of life, and the gap it made in the smooth ground was wonderful.", "내 인생의 길 위에 무덤이 입을 벌린 것은 그때가 처음이었고, 그것이 평탄하던 땅에 남긴 틈은 놀라운 것이었다."], ["The figure of my sister in her chair by the kitchen fire, haunted me night and day.", "부엌 난롯가 의자에 앉아 있던 누나의 모습이 밤낮으로 나를 따라다녔다."], ["That the place could possibly be, without her, was something my mind seemed unable to compass; and whereas she had seldom or never been in my thoughts of late, I had now the strangest ideas that she was coming towards me in the street, or that she would presently knock at the door.", "그곳이 누나 없이도 존재할 수 있다는 사실은 내 정신으로는 도무지 헤아릴 수 없는 무엇이었다. 그리고 요즈음 누나가 내 생각 속에 떠오른 적이 거의, 아니 전혀 없었음에도, 이제는 누나가 거리에서 나를 향해 걸어오고 있다거나 곧 문을 두드릴 것 같다는 더없이 기이한 상상이 들었다."], ["In my rooms too, with which she had never been at all associated, there was at once the blankness of death and a perpetual suggestion of the sound of her voice or the turn of her face or figure, as if she were still alive and had been often there.", "누나와는 조금도 연관된 적 없는 내 방에서조차, 한순간 죽음의 공허함이 깃들면서 동시에 누나의 목소리가, 그 얼굴이나 몸의 움직임이 끊임없이 떠오르는 것이었다. 마치 누나가 아직 살아 있어 그곳에 자주 와 있었던 것처럼."]], [["Whatever my fortunes might have been, I could scarcely have recalled my sister with much tenderness.", "내 처지가 어떠했든 간에, 나는 누나를 그리 다정한 마음으로 떠올리기는 어려웠을 것이다."], ["But I suppose there is a shock of regret which may exist without much tenderness.", "그러나 다정함은 별로 없으면서도 존재할 수 있는 회한의 충격이라는 것이 있는 모양이다."], ["Under its influence (and perhaps to make up for the want of the softer feeling) I was seized with a violent indignation against the assailant from whom she had suffered so much; and I felt that on sufficient proof I could have revengefully pursued Orlick, or any one else, to the last extremity.", "그 영향 아래(어쩌면 그 부드러운 감정의 결핍을 메우기 위해), 나는 누나에게 그토록 큰 고통을 안긴 그 가해자를 향한 격렬한 분노에 사로잡혔다. 그리고 충분한 증거만 있다면 올릭이든 그 누구든 끝까지 복수의 일념으로 쫓을 수 있으리라고 느꼈다."]], [["Having written to Joe, to offer him consolation, and to assure him that I would come to the funeral, I passed the intermediate days in the curious state of mind I have glanced at.", "조에게 편지를 써서 위로의 말을 전하고 장례식에 가겠다고 약속한 뒤, 나는 앞서 잠깐 언급한 그 묘한 심정으로 그사이의 며칠을 보냈다."], ["I went down early in the morning, and alighted at the Blue Boar in good time to walk over to the forge.", "나는 아침 일찍 내려가, 대장간까지 걸어갈 수 있도록 넉넉한 시각에 블루 보어 여관에 닿았다."]], [["It was fine summer weather again, and, as I walked along, the times when I was a little helpless creature, and my sister did not spare me, vividly returned.", "다시금 화창한 여름 날씨였고, 길을 따라 걷노라니 내가 가냘프고 무력한 어린것이었고 누나가 나를 가차 없이 대하던 시절이 생생하게 되살아났다."], ["But they returned with a gentle tone upon them that softened even the edge of Tickler.", "그러나 그 시절은 부드러운 색조를 띠고 돌아와, 간질이 막대기의 날카로움마저 누그러뜨렸다."], ["For now, the very breath of the beans and clover whispered to my heart that the day must come when it would be well for my memory that others walking in the sunshine should be softened as they thought of me.", "이제 콩과 토끼풀의 바로 그 숨결이, 언젠가 햇살 속을 걷는 다른 이들이 나를 떠올리며 마음을 누그러뜨려 준다면 내 추억에 좋은 일이 될 그런 날이 반드시 오리라고 내 가슴에 속삭였기 때문이다. * 간질이 막대기: 누나가 핍을 매질하던 회초리에 붙인 별명."]], [["At last I came within sight of the house, and saw that Trabb and Co. had put in a funereal execution and taken possession.", "마침내 나는 집이 보이는 곳에 이르렀고, 트랩 상회가 장례 집행에 들어가 그 집을 점령했음을 알았다."], ["Two dismally absurd persons, each ostentatiously exhibiting a crutch done up in a black bandage,—as if that instrument could possibly communicate any comfort to anybody,—were posted at the front door; and in one of them I recognised a postboy discharged from the Boar for turning a young couple into a sawpit on their bridal morning, in consequence of intoxication rendering it necessary for him to ride his horse clasped round the neck with both arms.", "우스꽝스러울 만큼 침울한 두 사람이, 검은 붕대로 감싼 지팡이를 저마다 과시하듯 내보이며—마치 그 도구가 누군가에게 위안을 전해 줄 수 있기라도 한 양—현관 앞에 배치되어 있었는데, 그중 한 사람은 술에 취해 말 목을 두 팔로 끌어안고 타야 할 지경이 되는 바람에 어느 신혼부부를 결혼식 날 아침 톱질 구덩이에 처박은 죄로 보어 여관에서 쫓겨난 마부였음을 나는 알아보았다."], ["All the children of the village, and most of the women, were admiring these sable warders and the closed windows of the house and forge; and as I came up, one of the two warders (the postboy) knocked at the door,—implying that I was far too much exhausted by grief to have strength remaining to knock for myself.", "마을의 아이들 전부와 여자들 대다수가 이 검은 옷의 문지기들과 굳게 닫힌 집과 대장간의 창문들을 감탄하며 바라보고 있었다. 내가 다가가자 두 문지기 중 하나가(그 마부가) 문을 두드렸는데—그것은 내가 슬픔에 너무도 지친 나머지 스스로 두드릴 힘조차 남아 있지 않으리라는 뜻이었다."]], [["Another sable warder (a carpenter, who had once eaten two geese for a wager) opened the door, and showed me into the best parlour. Here, Mr.", "또 다른 검은 옷의 문지기가(한때 내기로 거위 두 마리를 먹어 치운 적이 있는 목수였다) 문을 열고 나를 가장 좋은 응접실로 안내했다."], ["Trabb had taken unto himself the best table, and had got all the leaves up, and was holding a kind of black Bazaar, with the aid of a quantity of black pins.", "그곳에서 트랩 씨는 가장 좋은 탁자를 차지하고 받침판을 죄다 펼쳐 올려 놓은 채, 검은 핀을 잔뜩 동원해 일종의 검은 시장판을 벌이고 있었다."], ["At the moment of my arrival, he had just finished putting somebody’s hat into black long-clothes, like an African baby; so he held out his hand for mine.", "내가 도착한 순간, 그는 막 누군가의 모자를 아프리카 아기처럼 검은 긴 포대기로 감싸는 일을 끝낸 참이었기에, 내 모자를 받으려고 손을 내밀었다."], ["But I, misled by the action, and confused by the occasion, shook hands with him with every testimony of warm affection.", "그러나 나는 그 동작에 잘못 이끌리고 또 상황에 당황한 나머지, 더없이 따뜻한 정을 다해 그와 악수를 나누고 말았다."]], [["Poor dear Joe, entangled in a little black cloak tied in a large bow under his chin, was seated apart at the upper end of the room; where, as chief mourner, he had evidently been stationed by Trabb.", "가엾고 정다운 조는, 턱밑에 커다란 매듭으로 묶은 작은 검은 망토에 휘감긴 채 방의 윗자리에 따로 앉아 있었는데, 상주로서 트랩이 분명 그를 그 자리에 배치해 둔 모양이었다."], ["When I bent down and said to him, “Dear Joe, how are you?”", "내가 몸을 굽혀 \"조 아저씨, 좀 어떠세요?\""], ["he said, “Pip, old chap, you knowed her when she were a fine figure of a—” and clasped my hand and said no more.", "하고 묻자, 그는 \"핍, 이 친구야, 자네는 누나가 한창 좋은 모습이었을 적 그—\" 하고 말하더니, 내 손을 꼭 쥐고는 더는 말을 잇지 못했다."]], [["Biddy, looking very neat and modest in her black dress, went quietly here and there, and was very helpful.", "비디는 검은 옷차림으로 무척 단정하고 얌전해 보였는데, 조용히 이리저리 오가며 큰 도움을 주었다."], ["When I had spoken to Biddy, as I thought it not a time for talking I went and sat down near Joe, and there began to wonder in what part of the house it—she—my sister—was.", "비디에게 말을 건넨 뒤, 나는 지금이 이야기를 나눌 때가 아니라고 여겨 조 곁으로 가 앉았고, 그러고는 그것이—그분이—내 누나가 이 집의 어디쯤 있을까 하고 생각하기 시작했다."], ["The air of the parlour being faint with the smell of sweet-cake, I looked about for the table of refreshments; it was scarcely visible until one had got accustomed to the gloom, but there was a cut-up plum cake upon it, and there were cut-up oranges, and sandwiches, and biscuits, and two decanters that I knew very well as ornaments, but had never seen used in all my life; one full of port, and one of sherry.", "응접실의 공기에 단 과자 냄새가 희미하게 배어 있어, 나는 다과상을 찾아 둘러보았다. 그것은 어둠에 눈이 익기 전에는 거의 보이지 않았으나, 그 위에는 자른 자두 케이크가 있었고, 자른 오렌지와 샌드위치와 비스킷이, 그리고 평생 장식품으로만 잘 알고 있었을 뿐 한 번도 쓰이는 것을 본 적 없는 유리병 두 개가 놓여 있었다. 하나에는 포트와인이, 다른 하나에는 셰리주가 가득 차 있었다."], ["Standing at this table, I became conscious of the servile Pumblechook in a black cloak and several yards of hatband, who was alternately stuffing himself, and making obsequious movements to catch my attention.", "이 탁자 앞에 서 있노라니, 나는 검은 망토를 두르고 몇 야드나 되는 모자 띠를 늘어뜨린 비굴한 펌블추크가 번갈아 가며 음식을 입에 욱여넣다가 내 주의를 끌려고 굽실거리는 동작을 하고 있음을 알아챘다."], ["The moment he succeeded, he came over to me (breathing sherry and crumbs), and said in a subdued voice, “May I, dear sir?”", "마침내 성공하자 그는 (셰리주와 빵 부스러기 냄새를 풍기며) 내게 다가와 나직한 목소리로 \"허락해 주시겠습니까, 도련님?\""], ["and did. I then descried Mr.", "하고 말하더니 그렇게 했다."], ["and Mrs. Hubble; the last-named in a decent speechless paroxysm in a corner.", "그러고 나서 나는 허블 씨 부부를 발견했는데, 부인은 한쪽 구석에서 점잖게 말없이 발작하듯 흐느끼고 있었다."], ["We were all going to “follow,”", "우리 모두는 \"뒤따를\""], ["and were all in course of being tied up separately (by Trabb) into ridiculous bundles.", "참이었고, 모두가 (트랩의 손에) 우스꽝스러운 꾸러미로 하나씩 따로따로 묶이고 있는 중이었다."]], [["“Which I meantersay, Pip,” Joe whispered me, as we were being what Mr.", "\"그러니까 제 말은 말입니다, 핍,\""], ["Trabb called “formed” in the parlour, two and two,—and it was dreadfully like a preparation for some grim kind of dance; “which I meantersay, sir, as I would in preference have carried her to the church myself, along with three or four friendly ones wot come to it with willing harts and arms, but it were considered wot the neighbours would look down on such and would be of opinions as it were wanting in respect.”", "응접실에서 트랩 씨가 \"정렬\"이라고 부른 대로 우리가 둘씩 둘씩 짝지어지던 중에—그것은 무슨 음산한 춤을 위한 준비와 끔찍이도 닮아 있었다—조가 내게 속삭였다. \"그러니까 제 말은 말입니다, 도련님, 저로서야 차라리 흔쾌한 마음과 팔로 와 줄 서너 명의 다정한 친구들과 함께 제가 직접 누나를 교회로 모시고 싶었습니다만, 이웃들이 그런 걸 깔보면서 예의에 어긋난다고 여길 거라는 의견들이 있었던 거지요.\""]], [["“Pocket-handkerchiefs out, all!” cried Mr.", "\"손수건을 꺼내십시오, 모두!\""], ["Trabb at this point, in a depressed business-like voice.", "이때 트랩 씨가 침울하고 사무적인 목소리로 외쳤다."], ["“Pocket-handkerchiefs out!", "\"손수건을 꺼내십시오!"], ["We are ready!”", "준비됐습니다!\""]], [["So we all put our pocket-handkerchiefs to our faces, as if our noses were bleeding, and filed out two and two; Joe and I; Biddy and Pumblechook; Mr. and Mrs.", "그리하여 우리는 모두 코피라도 나는 양 손수건을 얼굴에 갖다 대고 둘씩 둘씩 줄지어 나갔다. 조와 나, 비디와 펌블추크, 허블 씨 부부 순이었다."], ["Hubble. The remains of my poor sister had been brought round by the kitchen door, and, it being a point of Undertaking ceremony that the six bearers must be stifled and blinded under a horrible black velvet housing with a white border, the whole looked like a blind monster with twelve human legs, shuffling and blundering along, under the guidance of two keepers,—the postboy and his comrade.", "가엾은 누나의 유해는 부엌문으로 돌아 운구되어 나왔는데, 흰 가장자리를 두른 무시무시한 검은 벨벳 천 아래 여섯 명의 운구꾼이 숨이 막히고 앞이 가려진 채 가야 하는 것이 장의 의식의 한 관례였던 까닭에, 그 전체 모습은 마치 열두 개의 사람 다리가 달린 눈먼 괴물이 두 명의 관리인—마부와 그 동료—의 인도 아래 발을 질질 끌며 비척비척 나아가는 듯이 보였다."]], [["The neighbourhood, however, highly approved of these arrangements, and we were much admired as we went through the village; the more youthful and vigorous part of the community making dashes now and then to cut us off, and lying in wait to intercept us at points of vantage.", "그러나 이웃들은 이러한 배치를 크게 흡족해했고, 우리가 마을을 지나는 동안 우리는 무척 감탄의 대상이 되었다. 마을 사람들 가운데 젊고 혈기 왕성한 축들은 이따금 앞질러 우리 길을 가로막으려 내달았고, 좋은 길목에 매복해 있다가 우리를 맞으려 했다."], ["At such times the more exuberant among them called out in an excited manner on our emergence round some corner of expectancy, “Here they come!”", "그럴 때면 그중 더 들뜬 자들은, 우리가 예상되는 모퉁이를 돌아 나타나기라도 하면 흥분한 투로 \"온다, 온다!\""], ["“Here they are!”", "\"여기 왔다!\""], ["and we were all but cheered. In this progress I was much annoyed by the abject Pumblechook, who, being behind me, persisted all the way as a delicate attention in arranging my streaming hatband, and smoothing my cloak.", "하고 외쳐 댔고, 우리는 환호를 받기 직전이었다. 이렇게 나아가는 동안 나는 그 비굴한 펌블추크 때문에 무척 짜증이 났는데, 그는 내 뒤에 서서 가는 내내 세심한 배려라며 흩날리는 내 모자 띠를 매만지고 내 망토를 쓸어 주기를 고집했다."], ["My thoughts were further distracted by the excessive pride of Mr. and Mrs. Hubble, who were surpassingly conceited and vainglorious in being members of so distinguished a procession.", "게다가 내 생각은 허블 씨 부부의 지나친 자긍심 때문에 더욱 산만해졌으니, 그들은 이토록 격조 있는 행렬의 일원이 된 것에 대해 더할 나위 없이 우쭐하고 으스대고 있었다."]], [["And now the range of marshes lay clear before us, with the sails of the ships on the river growing out of it; and we went into the churchyard, close to the graves of my unknown parents, Philip Pirrip, late of this parish, and Also Georgiana, Wife of the Above.", "이제 늪지대가 우리 앞에 훤히 펼쳐졌고, 강 위 배들의 돛이 그 위로 솟아오르고 있었다. 우리는 교회 묘지로 들어가, 내가 알지 못하는 부모님—이 교구의 고인이 된 필립 피립과 또한 위 사람의 아내 조지애나—의 무덤 가까이로 갔다."], ["And there, my sister was laid quietly in the earth, while the larks sang high above it, and the light wind strewed it with beautiful shadows of clouds and trees.", "그리고 그곳에서, 종다리들이 그 위 높은 곳에서 노래하고 가벼운 바람이 구름과 나무의 아름다운 그림자를 흩뿌리는 가운데, 누나는 조용히 땅에 묻혔다."]], [["Of the conduct of the worldly minded Pumblechook while this was doing, I desire to say no more than it was all addressed to me; and that even when those noble passages were read which remind humanity how it brought nothing into the world and can take nothing out, and how it fleeth like a shadow and never continueth long in one stay, I heard him cough a reservation of the case of a young gentleman who came unexpectedly into large property.", "이 일이 진행되는 동안 그 속물적인 펌블추크의 행동에 관해서는, 그것이 죄다 나를 향한 것이었다는 점, 그리고 인간이 이 세상에 아무것도 가지고 오지 않았으니 아무것도 가지고 갈 수 없으며 그림자처럼 사라져 한곳에 오래 머무르지 못한다는 것을 일깨우는 그 고귀한 구절들이 낭독될 때조차, 그가 뜻밖에 큰 재산을 물려받은 한 젊은 신사의 경우는 예외라는 단서를 기침으로 덧붙이는 소리를 내가 들었다는 점 외에는 더 말하고 싶지 않다."], ["When we got back, he had the hardihood to tell me that he wished my sister could have known I had done her so much honour, and to hint that she would have considered it reasonably purchased at the price of her death.", "우리가 돌아왔을 때, 그는 뻔뻔하게도 누나가 내가 자신에게 이토록 큰 영광을 안겨 주었음을 알았더라면 좋았을 것이라고 내게 말했고, 누나가 자기 죽음의 대가로 그 영광을 치렀더라도 그것을 합당한 거래로 여겼으리라는 뜻까지 넌지시 내비쳤다."], ["After that, he drank all the rest of the sherry, and Mr. Hubble drank the port, and the two talked (which I have since observed to be customary in such cases) as if they were of quite another race from the deceased, and were notoriously immortal.", "그런 다음 그는 남은 셰리주를 모조리 마셨고, 허블 씨는 포트와인을 마셨으며, 두 사람은 (이후 나는 그런 경우에 으레 그러함을 관찰했다) 마치 자기들이 고인과는 전혀 다른 종족이며 누구나 다 아는 불사신이기라도 한 양 떠들어 댔다."], ["Finally, he went away with Mr. and Mrs. Hubble,—to make an evening of it, I felt sure, and to tell the Jolly Bargemen that he was the founder of my fortunes and my earliest benefactor.", "마침내 그는 허블 씨 부부와 함께 떠나갔는데—저녁 시간을 그렇게 즐기려는 것임을, 그리고 졸리 바지선꾼 술집에 가서 자신이 내 행운의 창시자이자 가장 이른 은인이라고 떠벌리려는 것임을 나는 확신했다."]], [["When they were all gone, and when Trabb and his men—but not his Boy; I looked for him—had crammed their mummery into bags, and were gone too, the house felt wholesomer.", "그들이 모두 떠나고, 트랩과 그 일꾼들이—그러나 그 점원 녀석은 아니었으니, 나는 그를 찾아보았다—제 장식 도구들을 자루에 쑤셔 넣고 그들마저 떠나가자, 집 안은 한결 정갈한 느낌이 들었다."], ["Soon afterwards, Biddy, Joe, and I, had a cold dinner together; but we dined in the best parlour, not in the old kitchen, and Joe was so exceedingly particular what he did with his knife and fork and the saltcellar and what not, that there was great restraint upon us.", "곧이어 비디와 조와 나는 함께 차가운 만찬을 들었다. 그러나 우리는 옛 부엌이 아니라 가장 좋은 응접실에서 식사를 했고, 조는 나이프와 포크와 소금 그릇이며 이런저런 것을 다루는 데 어찌나 지나치게 신경을 쓰던지, 우리 사이에는 큰 어색함이 감돌았다."], ["But after dinner, when I made him take his pipe, and when I had loitered with him about the forge, and when we sat down together on the great block of stone outside it, we got on better.", "그러나 식사가 끝난 뒤, 내가 그에게 파이프를 물게 하고, 그와 함께 대장간 주위를 어슬렁거리고, 그 바깥의 커다란 돌덩이 위에 함께 앉고 나니, 우리는 한결 나아졌다."], ["I noticed that after the funeral Joe changed his clothes so far, as to make a compromise between his Sunday dress and working dress; in which the dear fellow looked natural, and like the Man he was.", "나는 조가 장례식이 끝난 뒤 옷을 갈아입어, 일요일 나들이옷과 작업복 사이의 절충을 이루어 놓은 것을 알아챘는데, 그 차림새 속에서 정다운 친구는 자연스러워 보였고, 본래 그가 어떤 사람인지 그대로 드러나 보였다."]], [["He was very much pleased by my asking if I might sleep in my own little room, and I was pleased too; for I felt that I had done rather a great thing in making the request.", "내가 내 작은 방에서 자도 되겠느냐고 묻자 그는 무척 기뻐했고, 나 또한 기뻤다. 그런 청을 한 것이 다소 대단한 일을 한 셈이라고 느꼈기 때문이다."], ["When the shadows of evening were closing in, I took an opportunity of getting into the garden with Biddy for a little talk.", "저녁 그림자가 짙게 드리워질 무렵, 나는 잠깐 이야기를 나누려고 비디와 함께 정원으로 나갈 기회를 잡았다."]], [["“Biddy,”", "\"비디,\""], ["said I, “I think you might have written to me about these sad matters.”", "내가 말했다. \"이 슬픈 일들에 대해 자네가 내게 편지를 써 줄 수도 있었을 텐데.\""]], [["“Do you, Mr. Pip?” said Biddy.", "\"그렇게 생각하세요, 핍 도련님?\""], ["“I should have written if I had thought that.”", "비디가 말했다. \"그렇게 여겼더라면 편지를 썼을 거예요.\""]], [["“Don’t suppose that I mean to be unkind, Biddy, when I say I consider that you ought to have thought that.”", "\"내가 자네에게 야박하게 굴려는 거라고는 여기지 말게, 비디. 나는 자네가 그렇게 여겼어야 했다고 생각한다는 말일세.\""]], [["“Do you, Mr. Pip?”", "\"그렇게 생각하세요, 핍 도련님?\""]], [["She was so quiet, and had such an orderly, good, and pretty way with her, that I did not like the thought of making her cry again.", "비디는 어찌나 차분하고, 또 어찌나 단정하고 선량하고 어여쁜 태도를 지녔던지, 나는 그녀를 다시 울리고 싶지 않았다."], ["After looking a little at her downcast eyes as she walked beside me, I gave up that point.", "내 곁에서 걷는 동안 내리뜬 그녀의 눈을 잠시 바라본 뒤, 나는 그 문제를 접었다."]], [["“I suppose it will be difficult for you to remain here now, Biddy dear?”", "\"이제 자네가 이곳에 남아 있기는 어렵겠지, 비디?\""]], [["“Oh!", "\"아!"], ["I can’t do so, Mr. Pip,”", "그럴 수는 없어요, 핍 도련님,\""], ["said Biddy, in a tone of regret but still of quiet conviction.", "비디가 아쉬워하면서도 여전히 조용한 확신이 담긴 어조로 말했다."], ["“I have been speaking to Mrs. Hubble, and I am going to her to-morrow.", "\"허블 부인과 이야기를 나누었는데, 내일 그분에게로 갈 거예요."], ["I hope we shall be able to take some care of Mr. Gargery, together, until he settles down.”", "가저리 씨가 자리를 잡으실 때까지 우리가 함께 그분을 좀 보살펴 드릴 수 있으면 좋겠어요.\""]], [["“How are you going to live, Biddy?", "\"자네는 어떻게 살아갈 작정인가, 비디?"], ["If you want any mo—”", "만일 돈이 좀 더 필요하다—\""]], [["“How am I going to live?” repeated Biddy, striking in, with a momentary flush upon her face.", "\"제가 어떻게 살아갈 작정이냐고요?\" 비디가 얼굴에 잠깐 홍조를 띠며 끼어들어 되풀이했다."], ["“I’ll tell you, Mr. Pip.", "\"말씀드릴게요, 핍 도련님."], ["I am going to try to get the place of mistress in the new school nearly finished here.", "저는 여기 거의 다 지어진 새 학교의 여교사 자리를 얻어 보려고 해요."], ["I can be well recommended by all the neighbours, and I hope I can be industrious and patient, and teach myself while I teach others.", "이웃들 모두에게서 좋은 추천을 받을 수 있고, 부지런하고 참을성 있게, 남을 가르치면서 저 자신도 가르칠 수 있기를 바라고 있어요."], ["You know, Mr. Pip,”", "아시잖아요, 핍 도련님,\""], ["pursued Biddy, with a smile, as she raised her eyes to my face, “the new schools are not like the old, but I learnt a good deal from you after that time, and have had time since then to improve.”", "비디가 내 얼굴로 눈을 들며 미소를 띠고 말을 이었다. \"새 학교는 옛날 학교와는 다르지만, 저는 그때 이후로 도련님께 많은 걸 배웠고, 그 뒤로 실력을 갈고닦을 시간도 있었으니까요.\""]], [["“I think you would always improve, Biddy, under any circumstances.”", "\"비디, 자네는 어떤 상황에서든 늘 실력을 갈고닦을 사람이라고 생각하네.\""]], [["“Ah!", "\"아!"], ["Except in my bad side of human nature,”", "제 인간 본성의 못된 쪽만 빼고요,\""], ["murmured Biddy.", "비디가 중얼거렸다."]], [["It was not so much a reproach as an irresistible thinking aloud. Well!", "그것은 책망이라기보다는 억누를 수 없이 새어 나온 혼잣말에 가까웠다."], ["I thought I would give up that point too.", "그래, 나는 그 문제 또한 접는 게 좋겠다고 생각했다."], ["So, I walked a little further with Biddy, looking silently at her downcast eyes.", "그래서 나는 비디와 조금 더 걸으며, 내리뜬 그녀의 눈을 말없이 바라보았다."]], [["“I have not heard the particulars of my sister’s death, Biddy.”", "\"비디, 나는 누나가 어떻게 죽었는지 자세한 이야기를 듣지 못했네.\""]], [["“They are very slight, poor thing.", "\"별것 없어요, 가엾은 분."], ["She had been in one of her bad states—though they had got better of late, rather than worse—for four days, when she came out of it in the evening, just at tea-time, and said quite plainly, ‘Joe.’ As she had never said any word for a long while, I ran and fetched in Mr.", "나흘 동안 발작 상태에 빠져 계셨는데—요즘 들어선 그게 더 나빠지기보다는 오히려 좀 나아지긴 했지만요—저녁에, 마침 차 마실 시간에 그 상태에서 깨어나시더니 또렷하게 '조' 하고 말씀하셨어요. 오랫동안 한마디도 안 하셨던 터라, 저는 달려가서 대장간에 계신 가저리 씨를 모셔 왔지요."], ["Gargery from the forge. She made signs to me that she wanted him to sit down close to her, and wanted me to put her arms round his neck.", "그분은 가저리 씨가 자기 곁에 가까이 앉기를 바라고, 제가 자기 두 팔을 그분 목에 둘러 주기를 바란다는 몸짓을 하셨어요."], ["So I put them round his neck, and she laid her head down on his shoulder quite content and satisfied.", "그래서 제가 그 팔을 그분 목에 둘러 드렸더니, 그분은 아주 흡족하고 만족스러운 듯 머리를 그분 어깨에 기대셨어요. 그러고는 곧 다시 '조'"], ["And so she presently said ‘Joe’ again, and once ‘Pardon,’ and once ‘Pip.’ And so she never lifted her head up any more, and it was just an hour later when we laid it down on her own bed, because we found she was gone.”", "하고 말씀하셨고, 한 번은 '용서를', 또 한 번은 '핍' 하고 말씀하셨어요. 그러고는 다시는 고개를 들지 않으셨고, 한 시간쯤 지나서 우리는 그분이 가셨음을 알았기에 그분의 머리를 그분 침대에 누여 드렸어요.\""]], [["Biddy cried; the darkening garden, and the lane, and the stars that were coming out, were blurred in my own sight.", "비디는 울었다. 어두워지는 정원과 좁은 길, 돋아나기 시작한 별들이 내 눈에도 흐릿하게 번졌다."]], [["“Nothing was ever discovered, Biddy?”", "\"끝내 아무것도 밝혀지지 않았나, 비디?\""]], [["“Nothing.”", "\"아무것도요.\""]], [["“Do you know what is become of Orlick?”", "\"올릭이 어떻게 됐는지 아는가?\""]], [["“I should think from the colour of his clothes that he is working in the quarries.”", "\"옷 색깔로 보아 채석장에서 일하고 있는 것 같아요.\""]], [["“Of course you have seen him then?—Why are you looking at that dark tree in the lane?”", "\"그럼 물론 그를 본 적이 있다는 거로군?—왜 저 길의 어두운 나무를 보고 있나?\""]], [["“I saw him there, on the night she died.”", "\"누나가 돌아가시던 날 밤에 거기서 그를 봤어요.\""]], [["“That was not the last time either, Biddy?”", "\"그게 마지막도 아니었던 거지, 비디?\""]], [["“No; I have seen him there, since we have been walking here.—It is of no use,”", "\"네. 우리가 여기서 걷는 동안에도 거기서 그를 봤어요.—소용없어요,\""], ["said Biddy, laying her hand upon my arm, as I was for running out, “you know I would not deceive you; he was not there a minute, and he is gone.”", "내가 막 달려 나가려 하자 비디가 내 팔에 손을 얹으며 말했다. \"제가 도련님을 속이지 않을 거라는 건 아시잖아요. 그는 거기 채 일 분도 없었고, 이미 가 버렸어요.\""]], [["It revived my utmost indignation to find that she was still pursued by this fellow, and I felt inveterate against him.", "그녀가 여전히 이 사내에게 쫓기고 있음을 알자 내 분노는 극에 달했고, 나는 그에게 사무치는 적의를 느꼈다."], ["I told her so, and told her that I would spend any money or take any pains to drive him out of that country.", "나는 그녀에게 그렇게 말했고, 그를 이 고장에서 쫓아내기 위해서라면 얼마든 돈을 쓰고 어떤 수고든 아끼지 않겠다고 말했다."], ["By degrees she led me into more temperate talk, and she told me how Joe loved me, and how Joe never complained of anything,—she didn’t say, of me; she had no need; I knew what she meant,—but ever did his duty in his way of life, with a strong hand, a quiet tongue, and a gentle heart.", "그녀는 차츰 나를 좀 더 차분한 이야기로 이끌었고, 조가 나를 얼마나 사랑하는지, 그리고 조가 어떤 일에 대해서도—나에 대해서라고는 말하지 않았으나, 그럴 필요도 없었으니, 나는 그녀의 뜻을 알았다—결코 불평하지 않고, 굳센 손과 조용한 혀와 다정한 마음으로 자기 삶의 방식 안에서 늘 제 본분을 다해 왔는지를 들려주었다."]], [["“Indeed, it would be hard to say too much for him,”", "\"정말이지, 그분에 대해서는 아무리 좋게 말해도 지나치지 않을 거야,\""], ["said I; “and Biddy, we must often speak of these things, for of course I shall be often down here now.", "내가 말했다. \"그리고 비디, 우리는 이런 이야기들을 자주 나눠야 하네. 물론 나는 이제 이곳에 자주 내려올 테니까."], ["I am not going to leave poor Joe alone.”", "가엾은 조 아저씨를 외롭게 내버려 두지 않을 거야.\""]], [["Biddy said never a single word.", "비디는 단 한마디도 하지 않았다."]], [["“Biddy, don’t you hear me?”", "\"비디, 내 말 안 들리나?\""]], [["“Yes, Mr. Pip.”", "\"네, 핍 도련님.\""]], [["“Not to mention your calling me Mr. Pip,—which appears to me to be in bad taste, Biddy,—what do you mean?”", "\"나를 핍 도련님이라고 부르는 건 차치하고라도—그것도 내겐 영 못마땅한 취향으로 보입니다만, 비디—자네 무슨 뜻으로 그러는 건가?\""]], [["“What do I mean?”", "\"무슨 뜻이냐고요?\""], ["asked Biddy, timidly.", "비디가 머뭇거리며 물었다."]], [["“Biddy,”", "\"비디,\""], ["said I, in a virtuously self-asserting manner, “I must request to know what you mean by this?”", "내가 점잖게 제 옳음을 내세우는 투로 말했다. \"자네가 이러는 게 무슨 뜻인지 좀 알아야겠네.\""]], [["“By this?”", "\"이러는 거요?\""], ["said Biddy.", "비디가 말했다."]], [["“Now, don’t echo,” I retorted. “You used not to echo, Biddy.”", "\"이봐, 메아리처럼 따라 하지 말게,\""], ["“Used not!”", "내가 쏘아붙였다."], ["said Biddy. “O Mr. Pip! Used!”", "\"예전엔 메아리처럼 따라 하지 않았잖나, 비디.\""]], [["Well!", "\"하지 않았다고요!\""], ["I rather thought I would give up that point too.", "비디가 말했다. \"아, 핍 도련님! 예전엔이라니요!\" 그래!"], ["After another silent turn in the garden, I fell back on the main position.", "나는 그 문제 역시 접는 게 좋겠다고 생각했다. 정원을 또 한 바퀴 말없이 돈 뒤, 나는 본래의 입장으로 돌아갔다."]], [["“Biddy,”", "\"비디,\""], ["said I, “I made a remark respecting my coming down here often, to see Joe, which you received with a marked silence.", "내가 말했다. \"내가 조 아저씨를 뵈러 이곳에 자주 내려올 거라는 말을 했는데, 자네는 그 말을 뚜렷한 침묵으로 받아들였네."], ["Have the goodness, Biddy, to tell me why.”", "비디, 부디 그 까닭을 말해 주게.\""]], [["“Are you quite sure, then, that you WILL come to see him often?”", "\"그럼 도련님은 정말로 그분을 자주 뵈러 오시리라고 확신하세요?\""], ["asked Biddy, stopping in the narrow garden walk, and looking at me under the stars with a clear and honest eye.", "비디가 좁은 정원 길에서 걸음을 멈추고, 별빛 아래 맑고 정직한 눈으로 나를 바라보며 물었다."]], [["“O dear me!”", "\"아, 이런!\""], ["said I, as if I found myself compelled to give up Biddy in despair.", "내가, 절망 속에서 비디를 단념할 수밖에 없다는 듯이 말했다."], ["“This really is a very bad side of human nature!", "\"이거야말로 인간 본성의 참으로 못된 쪽이로군!"], ["Don’t say any more, if you please, Biddy.", "제발, 비디, 더는 아무 말도 하지 말게."], ["This shocks me very much.”", "이건 나를 몹시 충격에 빠뜨리네.\""]], [["For which cogent reason I kept Biddy at a distance during supper, and when I went up to my own old little room, took as stately a leave of her as I could, in my murmuring soul, deem reconcilable with the churchyard and the event of the day.", "이 그럴듯한 이유로 나는 저녁 식사 내내 비디를 멀찍이 두었고, 내 옛 작은 방으로 올라갈 때에는 교회 묘지와 그날의 일에 비추어 내 웅얼거리는 영혼이 어울린다고 여길 만한 한도 내에서 더없이 위엄 있게 그녀에게 작별을 고했다."], ["As often as I was restless in the night, and that was every quarter of an hour, I reflected what an unkindness, what an injury, what an injustice, Biddy had done me.", "밤중에 잠 못 이룰 때마다, 그것도 십오 분마다였는데, 나는 비디가 내게 얼마나 야박하게, 얼마나 큰 해를, 얼마나 큰 부당함을 안겼는지 곱씹었다."]], [["Early in the morning I was to go.", "나는 아침 일찍 떠나기로 되어 있었다."], ["Early in the morning I was out, and looking in, unseen, at one of the wooden windows of the forge.", "아침 일찍 나는 밖으로 나가, 들키지 않은 채 대장간의 나무 창 하나로 안을 들여다보았다."], ["There I stood, for minutes, looking at Joe, already at work with a glow of health and strength upon his face that made it show as if the bright sun of the life in store for him were shining on it.", "거기서 나는 몇 분 동안 서서 조를 바라보았다. 그는 벌써 일을 하고 있었는데, 그의 얼굴에는 건강과 힘의 광채가 어려, 마치 그를 위해 마련된 인생의 밝은 태양이 그 얼굴을 비추고 있는 듯이 보였다."]], [["“Good-bye, dear Joe!—No, don’t wipe it off—for God’s sake, give me your blackened hand!—I shall be down soon and often.”", "\"안녕히 계세요, 정다운 조 아저씨!—아니, 닦지 마세요—제발, 그 검게 그을린 손을 제게 내밀어 주세요!—곧, 또 자주 내려올게요.\""]], [["“Never too soon, sir,”", "\"아무리 일러도 좋고,\""], ["said Joe, “and never too often, Pip!”", "조가 말했다. \"아무리 자주여도 좋네, 핍!\""]], [["Biddy was waiting for me at the kitchen door, with a mug of new milk and a crust of bread.", "비디는 새로 짠 우유 한 잔과 빵 한 조각을 들고 부엌문에서 나를 기다리고 있었다. \"비디,\""], ["“Biddy,” said I, when I gave her my hand at parting, “I am not angry, but I am hurt.”", "작별하며 그녀에게 손을 내밀 때 내가 말했다. \"나는 화가 난 게 아니라, 마음이 상한 거라네.\""]], [["“No, don’t be hurt,”", "\"아니에요, 마음 상해하지 마세요,\""], ["she pleaded quite pathetically; “let only me be hurt, if I have been ungenerous.”", "그녀가 자못 애처롭게 호소했다. \"제가 너그럽지 못했던 거라면, 마음 상해야 할 사람은 저 하나뿐이게 해 주세요.\""]], [["Once more, the mists were rising as I walked away.", "다시 한번, 내가 걸어 나오는 동안 안개가 피어오르고 있었다."], ["If they disclosed to me, as I suspect they did, that I should not come back, and that Biddy was quite right, all I can say is,—they were quite right too.", "만일 그 안개가, 내 짐작대로, 내가 돌아오지 않으리라는 것을, 그리고 비디가 전적으로 옳았다는 것을 내게 드러내 보인 것이라면, 내가 할 수 있는 말은 이것뿐이다—그 안개 또한 전적으로 옳았다고."]], [["# Chapter XXXVI.", "# Chapter XXXVI."]], [["Herbert and I went on from bad to worse, in the way of increasing our debts, looking into our affairs, leaving Margins, and the like exemplary transactions; and Time went on, whether or no, as he has a way of doing; and I came of age,—in fulfilment of Herbert’s prediction, that I should do so before I knew where I was.", "허버트와 나는 빚을 늘리고, 형편을 들여다보고, 여백을 남겨 두는 따위의 모범적인 거래를 거듭하며 점점 더 나빠져 갔다. 그리고 세월은, 늘 그러하듯, 좋든 싫든 흘러갔다. 그리하여 나는 성년이 되었다. 어느새 정신을 차리기도 전에 성년이 될 거라던 허버트의 예언이 그대로 이루어진 셈이었다."]], [["Herbert himself had come of age eight months before me.", "허버트 자신은 나보다 여덟 달 앞서 성년이 되었다."], ["As he had nothing else than his majority to come into, the event did not make a profound sensation in Barnard’s Inn.", "하지만 그에게는 성년이 된다는 것 말고 달리 물려받을 것이 없었으므로, 그 사건은 바너드 여관에 깊은 파문을 일으키지 못했다."], ["But we had looked forward to my one-and-twentieth birthday, with a crowd of speculations and anticipations, for we had both considered that my guardian could hardly help saying something definite on that occasion.", "그러나 우리는 내 스물한 번째 생일을 온갖 추측과 기대를 안고 손꼽아 기다렸다. 그날만은 후견인이 무언가 분명한 말을 하지 않을 수 없으리라고 우리 둘 다 여겼기 때문이다."]], [["I had taken care to have it well understood in Little Britain when my birthday was.", "나는 내 생일이 언제인지 리틀브리튼에 잘 알려지도록 미리 손을 써 두었다."], ["On the day before it, I received an official note from Wemmick, informing me that Mr. Jaggers would be glad if I would call upon him at five in the afternoon of the auspicious day.", "생일 전날, 나는 웨믹에게서 공식적인 쪽지를 받았는데, 그 길한 날 오후 다섯 시에 재거스 씨를 방문해 준다면 그분이 기뻐하시리라는 내용이었다."], ["This convinced us that something great was to happen, and threw me into an unusual flutter when I repaired to my guardian’s office, a model of punctuality.", "이로써 우리는 무언가 대단한 일이 일어나리라 확신했고, 시간을 칼같이 지켜 후견인의 사무실로 향할 때 나는 평소와 달리 가슴이 두근거렸다."]], [["In the outer office Wemmick offered me his congratulations, and incidentally rubbed the side of his nose with a folded piece of tissue-paper that I liked the look of.", "바깥 사무실에서 웨믹은 내게 축하 인사를 건넸고, 그러는 김에 보기 좋게 접은 박엽지로 코 옆을 문질렀는데, 나는 그 모양새가 마음에 들었다."], ["But he said nothing respecting it, and motioned me with a nod into my guardian’s room.", "그러나 그는 그것에 대해 아무 말도 하지 않고, 고갯짓으로 나를 후견인의 방으로 들여보냈다."], ["It was November, and my guardian was standing before his fire leaning his back against the chimney-piece, with his hands under his coattails.", "십일월이었고, 후견인은 두 손을 코트 자락 밑에 넣은 채 등을 벽난로 선반에 기대고 불 앞에 서 있었다."]], [["“Well, Pip,”", "\"이런, 핍.\""], ["said he, “I must call you Mr. Pip to-day.", "그가 말했다. \"오늘은 자네를 핍 씨라고 불러야겠군."], ["Congratulations, Mr. Pip.”", "축하하네, 핍 씨.\""]], [["We shook hands,—he was always a remarkably short shaker,—and I thanked him.", "우리는 악수를 나누었고 — 그는 언제나 유난히 짧게 손을 잡는 사람이었다 — 나는 그에게 고맙다고 했다."]], [["“Take a chair, Mr. Pip,”", "\"의자에 앉게, 핍 씨.\""], ["said my guardian.", "후견인이 말했다."]], [["As I sat down, and he preserved his attitude and bent his brows at his boots, I felt at a disadvantage, which reminded me of that old time when I had been put upon a tombstone.", "내가 앉는 동안에도 그는 그 자세를 그대로 유지한 채 눈썹을 찌푸리며 자기 장화를 내려다보았는데, 나는 불리한 처지에 놓인 듯한 기분이 들었고, 그것은 묘비 위에 올려졌던 그 옛 시절을 떠올리게 했다."], ["The two ghastly casts on the shelf were not far from him, and their expression was as if they were making a stupid apoplectic attempt to attend to the conversation.", "선반 위의 그 무시무시한 석고 두상 두 개가 그에게서 멀지 않은 곳에 있었는데, 그 표정은 마치 대화에 귀를 기울이려고 멍청하게 뇌졸중이라도 일으킬 듯 애쓰는 것 같았다."]], [["“Now my young friend,”", "\"자, 젊은 친구.\""], ["my guardian began, as if I were a witness in the box, “I am going to have a word or two with you.”", "후견인은 마치 내가 증인석에 선 증인이라도 되는 양 입을 열었다. \"자네와 한두 마디 나눠 볼 참이네.\""]], [["“If you please, sir.”", "\"그러시지요, 선생님.\""]], [["“What do you suppose,”", "\"자네는 짐작하나.\""], ["said Mr. Jaggers, bending forward to look at the ground, and then throwing his head back to look at the ceiling,—“what do you suppose you are living at the rate of?”", "재거스 씨가 몸을 앞으로 숙여 바닥을 보더니, 이내 고개를 뒤로 젖혀 천장을 쳐다보며 말했다. \"자네가 어느 정도 규모로 살고 있다고 짐작하나?\""]], [["“At the rate of, sir?”", "\"규모라니요, 선생님?\""]], [["“At,” repeated Mr.", "\"어느,\""], ["Jaggers, still looking at the ceiling, “the—rate—of?”", "재거스 씨가 여전히 천장을 보며 되풀이했다."], ["And then looked all round the room, and paused with his pocket-handkerchief in his hand, half-way to his nose.", "\"정도—규모—로 말이야?\" 그러고는 방 안을 빙 둘러보더니, 손수건을 든 손을 코까지 절반쯤 가져가다 멈추었다."]], [["I had looked into my affairs so often, that I had thoroughly destroyed any slight notion I might ever have had of their bearings.", "나는 내 형편을 하도 자주 들여다본 나머지, 그 형편의 내막에 대해 어렴풋이나마 가졌을 법한 관념마저 깡그리 무너뜨려 버린 터였다."], ["Reluctantly, I confessed myself quite unable to answer the question.", "나는 마지못해 그 질문에 도저히 답할 수 없노라고 실토했다."], ["This reply seemed agreeable to Mr. Jaggers, who said, “I thought so!”", "이 대답이 재거스 씨에게는 흡족했던 모양인지, 그는 \"그럴 줄 알았네!\""], ["and blew his nose with an air of satisfaction.", "하고는 만족스러운 기색으로 코를 풀었다."]], [["“Now, I have asked you a question, my friend,”", "\"자, 내가 자네한테 질문을 했네, 친구.\""], ["said Mr. Jaggers.", "재거스 씨가 말했다."], ["“Have you anything to ask me?”", "\"자네는 나한테 물어볼 게 있나?\""]], [["“Of course it would be a great relief to me to ask you several questions, sir; but I remember your prohibition.”", "\"물론 여러 가지를 여쭤볼 수 있다면 제게는 큰 위안이 되겠지만, 선생님, 저는 선생님의 금지령을 기억하고 있습니다.\""]], [["“Ask one,”", "\"하나만 물어보게.\""], ["said Mr. Jaggers.", "재거스 씨가 말했다."]], [["“Is my benefactor to be made known to me to-day?”", "\"제 은인이 오늘 제게 밝혀지는 겁니까?\""]], [["“No.", "\"아니."], ["Ask another.”", "다른 걸 물어보게.\""]], [["“Is that confidence to be imparted to me soon?”", "\"그 비밀이 곧 제게 전해지는 겁니까?\""]], [["“Waive that, a moment,”", "\"그건 잠깐 접어 두게.\""], ["said Mr. Jaggers, “and ask another.”", "재거스 씨가 말했다. \"그리고 다른 걸 물어보게.\""]], [["I looked about me, but there appeared to be now no possible escape from the inquiry, “Have-I—anything to receive, sir?”", "나는 주위를 둘러보았지만, 이제 이 물음을 피할 길은 도저히 없을 듯했다. \"제가—받을 것이 있습니까, 선생님?\""], ["On that, Mr. Jaggers said, triumphantly, “I thought we should come to it!”", "그러자 재거스 씨는 의기양양하게 말했다. \"그리로 올 줄 알았지!\""], ["and called to Wemmick to give him that piece of paper.", "그러고는 웨믹을 불러 그 종이를 달라고 했다."], ["Wemmick appeared, handed it in, and disappeared.", "웨믹이 나타나 그것을 건네고는 사라졌다."]], [["“Now, Mr. Pip,”", "\"자, 핍 씨.\""], ["said Mr. Jaggers, “attend, if you please.", "재거스 씨가 말했다. \"잘 들어 두게."], ["You have been drawing pretty freely here; your name occurs pretty often in Wemmick’s cash-book; but you are in debt, of course?”", "자네는 이곳에서 꽤 마음껏 돈을 인출해 왔지. 웨믹의 출납부에 자네 이름이 꽤 자주 등장하네. 한데 자네는 물론 빚을 지고 있겠지?\""]], [["“I am afraid I must say yes, sir.”", "\"그렇다고 말씀드려야 할 듯합니다, 선생님.\""]], [["“You know you must say yes; don’t you?”", "\"그렇다고 말할 수밖에 없다는 걸 알지, 안 그런가?\""], ["said Mr. Jaggers.", "재거스 씨가 말했다."]], [["“Yes, sir.”", "\"네, 선생님.\""]], [["“I don’t ask you what you owe, because you don’t know; and if you did know, you wouldn’t tell me; you would say less.", "\"자네가 얼마를 빚졌는지는 묻지 않겠네, 자네도 모를 테니까. 설령 안다 해도 내게 말하지 않을 테고, 실제보다 적게 말하겠지."], ["Yes, yes, my friend,”", "그래, 그래, 친구.\""], ["cried Mr. Jaggers, waving his forefinger to stop me as I made a show of protesting: “it’s likely enough that you think you wouldn’t, but you would.", "내가 항의하는 시늉을 하자 재거스 씨는 집게손가락을 흔들어 나를 막으며 외쳤다. \"자네는 그러지 않으리라 생각하겠지만, 자네는 그럴 걸세."], ["You’ll excuse me, but I know better than you.", "미안하지만, 내가 자네보다 더 잘 알아."], ["Now, take this piece of paper in your hand. You have got it?", "자, 이 종이를 손에 쥐어 보게. 쥐었나? 좋아."], ["Very good. Now, unfold it and tell me what it is.”", "이제 펴서 그게 뭔지 내게 말해 보게.\""]], [["“This is a bank-note,”", "\"이건 은행권입니다.\""], ["said I, “for five hundred pounds.”", "내가 말했다. \"오백 파운드짜리요.\""]], [["“That is a bank-note,” repeated Mr.", "\"그건 오백 파운드짜리 은행권일세.\""], ["Jaggers, “for five hundred pounds.", "재거스 씨가 되받았다."], ["And a very handsome sum of money too, I think.", "\"그리고 내 생각엔 꽤 두둑한 금액이기도 하지."], ["You consider it so?”", "자네도 그렇게 여기나?\""]], [["“How could I do otherwise!”", "\"어찌 달리 생각하겠습니까!\""]], [["“Ah!", "\"아!"], ["But answer the question,”", "하지만 질문에 답하게.\""], ["said Mr. Jaggers.", "재거스 씨가 말했다."]], [["“You consider it, undoubtedly, a handsome sum of money.", "\"의심할 바 없이 그렇습니다.\" \"자네는 의심할 바 없이 그것을 두둑한 금액으로 여긴다는 거지."], ["Now, that handsome sum of money, Pip, is your own.", "자, 그 두둑한 금액은, 핍, 자네 것일세."], ["It is a present to you on this day, in earnest of your expectations.", "자네의 유산을 보증하는 뜻으로, 오늘 자네에게 주는 선물이지."], ["And at the rate of that handsome sum of money per annum, and at no higher rate, you are to live until the donor of the whole appears.", "그리고 자네는 그 두둑한 금액을 연간 한도로 삼아, 그 이상은 넘지 않는 선에서, 그 모든 것을 베푸는 분이 나타날 때까지 살아가야 하네."], ["That is to say, you will now take your money affairs entirely into your own hands, and you will draw from Wemmick one hundred and twenty-five pounds per quarter, until you are in communication with the fountain-head, and no longer with the mere agent.", "다시 말해, 이제 자네는 자네의 금전 문제를 전적으로 자네 손에 맡게 되는 것이고, 자네가 근원지와 직접 연락이 닿을 때까지는, 그러니까 한낱 대리인이 아닌 그분과 닿을 때까지는, 웨믹에게서 분기마다 백이십오 파운드를 인출하게 되네."], ["As I have told you before, I am the mere agent.", "전에도 말했듯, 나는 한낱 대리인일 뿐이야."], ["I execute my instructions, and I am paid for doing so.", "나는 내 지시를 이행하고, 그 대가로 보수를 받지."], ["I think them injudicious, but I am not paid for giving any opinion on their merits.”", "나는 그 지시가 분별없다고 여기지만, 그 옳고 그름에 대해 의견을 내는 일로 보수를 받는 건 아니라네.\""]], [["I was beginning to express my gratitude to my benefactor for the great liberality with which I was treated, when Mr. Jaggers stopped me.", "나는 내가 받은 그 큰 관대함에 대해 은인에게 감사의 뜻을 표하려던 참이었는데, 재거스 씨가 나를 막았다. \"나는 자네 말을 누군가에게 전하라고 보수를 받는 게 아닐세, 핍.\""], ["“I am not paid, Pip,”", "그가 차갑게 말했다."], ["said he, coolly, “to carry your words to any one;” and then gathered up his coat-tails, as he had gathered up the subject, and stood frowning at his boots as if he suspected them of designs against him.", "그러고는, 화제를 거두어들이듯 코트 자락을 그러모으고 서서, 마치 자기 장화가 자기에게 무슨 음모라도 꾸미는 게 아닌가 의심하듯 눈썹을 찌푸린 채 그것을 노려보았다."]], [["After a pause, I hinted,—", "잠시 뜸을 들인 뒤, 나는 넌지시 말을 꺼냈다."]], [["“There was a question just now, Mr. Jaggers, which you desired me to waive for a moment.", "\"방금 전에, 재거스 씨, 잠깐 접어 두라고 하신 질문이 하나 있었지요."], ["I hope I am doing nothing wrong in asking it again?”", "그걸 다시 여쭤도 제가 잘못하는 게 아니길 바랍니다만?\""]], [["“What is it?”", "\"그게 뭔가?\""], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."]], [["I might have known that he would never help me out; but it took me aback to have to shape the question afresh, as if it were quite new. “Is it likely,”", "그가 결코 나를 거들어 주지 않으리라는 것쯤은 미리 알았어야 했다. 하지만 그 질문을 마치 전혀 새것인 양 다시 빚어내야 한다는 사실에 나는 당황했다. \"혹시,\""], ["I said, after hesitating, “that my patron, the fountain-head you have spoken of, Mr. Jaggers, will soon—”", "나는 머뭇거리다 말했다. \"제 후원자, 그러니까 선생님께서 말씀하신 그 근원지가, 재거스 씨, 곧—\""], ["there I delicately stopped.", "거기서 나는 조심스레 말을 멈추었다."]], [["“Will soon what?” asked Mr.", "\"곧 뭐가 어떻다는 건가?\""], ["Jaggers. “That’s no question as it stands, you know.”", "재거스 씨가 물었다. \"그대로는 질문이 안 되네, 알겠나.\""]], [["“Will soon come to London,”", "\"곧 런던에 오실는지요.\""], ["said I, after casting about for a precise form of words, “or summon me anywhere else?”", "나는 정확한 표현을 골똘히 더듬다가 말했다. \"아니면 저를 어딘가 다른 곳으로 부르실는지요?\""]], [["“Now, here,”", "\"자, 여기서,\""], ["replied Mr. Jaggers, fixing me for the first time with his dark deep-set eyes, “we must revert to the evening when we first encountered one another in your village.", "재거스 씨가 그 어둡고 깊숙이 박힌 눈으로 처음으로 나를 똑바로 응시하며 대답했다. \"우리는 자네 마을에서 처음 서로 마주쳤던 그 저녁으로 되돌아가야 하네."], ["What did I tell you then, Pip?”", "그때 내가 자네한테 뭐라고 했지, 핍?\""]], [["“You told me, Mr. Jaggers, that it might be years hence when that person appeared.”", "\"그 사람이 나타나는 건 지금부터 몇 년 뒤가 될 수도 있다고 하셨습니다, 재거스 씨.\""]], [["“Just so,”", "\"바로 그걸세.\""], ["said Mr. Jaggers, “that’s my answer.”", "재거스 씨가 말했다. \"그게 내 대답이야.\""]], [["As we looked full at one another, I felt my breath come quicker in my strong desire to get something out of him.", "우리가 서로를 정면으로 바라볼 때, 그에게서 무언가를 끌어내려는 강렬한 열망에 내 숨이 가빠지는 것을 느꼈다."], ["And as I felt that it came quicker, and as I felt that he saw that it came quicker, I felt that I had less chance than ever of getting anything out of him.", "그리고 내 숨이 가빠지는 것을 느끼고, 또 그가 내 숨이 가빠지는 것을 알아챘음을 느끼면서, 나는 그에게서 무언가를 끌어낼 가망이 그 어느 때보다도 적다는 것을 느꼈다."]], [["“Do you suppose it will still be years hence, Mr. Jaggers?”", "\"그게 여전히 몇 년 뒤일 거라고 보십니까, 재거스 씨?\""]], [["Mr.", "재거스 씨는 고개를 가로저었다."], ["Jaggers shook his head,—not in negativing the question, but in altogether negativing the notion that he could anyhow be got to answer it,—and the two horrible casts of the twitched faces looked, when my eyes strayed up to them, as if they had come to a crisis in their suspended attention, and were going to sneeze.", "그 질문을 부정하는 것이 아니라, 어떤 식으로든 자기가 그 질문에 답하도록 끌려갈 수 있다는 생각 자체를 통째로 부정하는 몸짓이었다. 그리고 내 눈길이 위로 흘러갔을 때, 그 일그러진 얼굴의 무시무시한 두상 두 개는, 마치 멈춰 있던 주의력이 어떤 절정에 다다라 막 재채기라도 하려는 것처럼 보였다."]], [["“Come!” said Mr.", "\"이보게!\""], ["Jaggers, warming the backs of his legs with the backs of his warmed hands, “I’ll be plain with you, my friend Pip.", "재거스 씨가 따뜻하게 데운 손등으로 자기 종아리 뒤를 덥히며 말했다. \"내 솔직하게 말함세, 친구 핍."], ["That’s a question I must not be asked.", "그건 내게 물어서는 안 되는 질문이야."], ["You’ll understand that better, when I tell you it’s a question that might compromise me. Come!", "그게 나를 곤란하게 만들 수 있는 질문이라고 말하면, 자네도 더 잘 이해하겠지. 이보게!"], ["I’ll go a little further with you; I’ll say something more.”", "자네한테 한 걸음 더 나아가 줌세. 무언가 좀 더 말해 주지.\""]], [["He bent down so low to frown at his boots, that he was able to rub the calves of his legs in the pause he made.", "그는 자기 장화를 향해 어찌나 낮게 몸을 숙여 눈썹을 찌푸렸던지, 그렇게 멈춰 있는 동안 자기 종아리를 문지를 수 있을 정도였다."]], [["“When that person discloses,”", "\"그 사람이 정체를 드러내면,\""], ["said Mr. Jaggers, straightening himself, “you and that person will settle your own affairs.", "재거스 씨가 몸을 똑바로 펴며 말했다. \"자네와 그 사람이 직접 자네들의 일을 처리하게 될 걸세."], ["When that person discloses, my part in this business will cease and determine.", "그 사람이 정체를 드러내면, 이 일에서 내가 맡은 몫은 끝나고 마무리되네."], ["When that person discloses, it will not be necessary for me to know anything about it.", "그 사람이 정체를 드러내면, 내가 그것에 대해 무언가를 알 필요는 없어지지."], ["And that’s all I have got to say.”", "그게 내가 할 말의 전부일세.\""]], [["We looked at one another until I withdrew my eyes, and looked thoughtfully at the floor.", "우리는 서로를 바라보다가, 내가 눈길을 거두어 생각에 잠긴 채 바닥을 내려다보았다."], ["From this last speech I derived the notion that Miss Havisham, for some reason or no reason, had not taken him into her confidence as to her designing me for Estella; that he resented this, and felt a jealousy about it; or that he really did object to that scheme, and would have nothing to do with it.", "이 마지막 말에서 나는 이런 생각을 끌어냈다. 즉, 해비셤 양이, 무슨 까닭에서든 혹은 아무 까닭 없이든, 나를 에스텔라의 짝으로 점찍은 자신의 계획에 대해서는 그를 속내까지 끌어들이지 않았다는 것, 그래서 그가 이를 못마땅해하며 일종의 질투를 느끼고 있거나, 아니면 그가 정말로 그 계획에 반대하여 거기에 일절 관여하지 않으려 한다는 것이었다."], ["When I raised my eyes again, I found that he had been shrewdly looking at me all the time, and was doing so still.", "내가 다시 눈을 들었을 때, 나는 그가 그동안 줄곧 나를 빈틈없이 살펴보고 있었으며, 지금도 그러고 있다는 것을 알아차렸다."]], [["“If that is all you have to say, sir,”", "\"그게 하실 말씀의 전부라면, 선생님,\""], ["I remarked, “there can be nothing left for me to say.”", "내가 말했다. \"저로서도 더 할 말이 남아 있을 리 없겠지요.\""]], [["He nodded assent, and pulled out his thief-dreaded watch, and asked me where I was going to dine?", "그는 그렇다는 듯 고개를 끄덕이고는, 도둑들이 두려워하는 그 시계를 꺼내더니, 어디서 저녁을 먹을 거냐고 물었다."], ["I replied at my own chambers, with Herbert.", "나는 내 거처에서 허버트와 함께 먹을 거라고 답했다."], ["As a necessary sequence, I asked him if he would favour us with his company, and he promptly accepted the invitation.", "자연히 뒤따르는 순서로, 나는 그에게 함께해 주시겠느냐고 청했고, 그는 그 초대를 선뜻 받아들였다."], ["But he insisted on walking home with me, in order that I might make no extra preparation for him, and first he had a letter or two to write, and (of course) had his hands to wash.", "다만 그는 나더러 자기 때문에 따로 준비를 하지 못하도록 나와 함께 걸어가겠다고 고집했고, 우선은 편지를 한두 통 써야 하며, (당연하게도) 손을 씻어야 한다고 했다."], ["So I said I would go into the outer office and talk to Wemmick.", "그래서 나는 바깥 사무실로 가서 웨믹과 이야기를 나누겠다고 했다."]], [["The fact was, that when the five hundred pounds had come into my pocket, a thought had come into my head which had been often there before; and it appeared to me that Wemmick was a good person to advise with concerning such thought.", "사실인즉, 그 오백 파운드가 내 주머니에 들어왔을 때, 전부터 종종 떠올랐던 한 가지 생각이 머릿속에 다시 떠올랐던 것이다. 그리고 그런 생각에 관해서라면 웨믹이야말로 의논하기에 좋은 사람으로 보였다."]], [["He had already locked up his safe, and made preparations for going home.", "그는 이미 금고를 잠그고 집에 갈 채비를 마친 뒤였다."], ["He had left his desk, brought out his two greasy office candlesticks and stood them in line with the snuffers on a slab near the door, ready to be extinguished; he had raked his fire low, put his hat and great-coat ready, and was beating himself all over the chest with his safe-key, as an athletic exercise after business.", "그는 책상을 떠나, 기름때 묻은 사무실 촛대 두 개를 꺼내어 곧 끌 수 있도록 문 가까운 석판 위에 심지가위와 나란히 세워 두었고, 불을 낮게 긁어내려 두었으며, 모자와 외투를 준비해 두고는, 업무를 마친 뒤의 운동이라도 하듯 금고 열쇠로 제 가슴팍 여기저기를 두드리고 있었다."]], [["“Mr. Wemmick,”", "\"웨믹 씨.\""], ["said I, “I want to ask your opinion.", "내가 말했다. \"당신 의견을 좀 여쭙고 싶습니다."], ["I am very desirous to serve a friend.”", "저는 친구 하나를 몹시 돕고 싶습니다.\""]], [["Wemmick tightened his post-office and shook his head, as if his opinion were dead against any fatal weakness of that sort.", "웨믹은 자기 우체통을 단단히 다물고 고개를 가로저었는데, 마치 그런 종류의 치명적인 약점이라면 무엇이든 자기 의견이 단호히 반대한다는 듯한 모습이었다."]], [["“This friend,”", "\"이 친구는,\""], ["I pursued, “is trying to get on in commercial life, but has no money, and finds it difficult and disheartening to make a beginning.", "나는 말을 이었다. \"상업계에서 자리를 잡으려 하는데, 돈이 없어서, 시작을 떼기가 어렵고 또 의욕마저 꺾이는 처지입니다."], ["Now I want somehow to help him to a beginning.”", "그래서 저는 어떻게든 그가 첫발을 떼도록 돕고 싶습니다.\""]], [["“With money down?”", "\"현금을 대서 말이오?\""], ["said Wemmick, in a tone drier than any sawdust.", "웨믹이 톱밥보다도 더 메마른 어조로 말했다."]], [["“With some money down,”", "\"얼마간의 현금을 대서요.\""], ["I replied, for an uneasy remembrance shot across me of that symmetrical bundle of papers at home—“with some money down, and perhaps some anticipation of my expectations.”", "나는 대답했다. 집에 있는 그 가지런히 묶인 종이 다발이 불안하게 머릿속을 스쳐 지나갔기 때문이다. \"얼마간의 현금을 대고, 어쩌면 제 유산 일부를 미리 끌어다가요.\""]], [["“Mr. Pip,”", "\"핍 씨.\""], ["said Wemmick, “I should like just to run over with you on my fingers, if you please, the names of the various bridges up as high as Chelsea Reach.", "웨믹이 말했다. \"괜찮으시다면 첼시 리치까지 거슬러 올라가며 여러 다리의 이름을 손가락으로 함께 꼽아 보고 싶군요. 어디 봅시다."], ["Let’s see; there’s London, one; Southwark, two; Blackfriars, three; Waterloo, four; Westminster, five; Vauxhall, six.”", "런던교가 하나, 서더크교가 둘, 블랙프라이어스교가 셋, 워털루교가 넷, 웨스트민스터교가 다섯, 복스홀교가 여섯이오.\""], ["He had checked off each bridge in its turn, with the handle of his safe-key on the palm of his hand.", "그는 금고 열쇠 손잡이로 손바닥을 짚어 가며 다리를 차례차례 헤아렸다."], ["“There’s as many as six, you see, to choose from.”", "\"보다시피 골라잡을 다리가 여섯이나 되지요.\""]], [["“I don’t understand you,”", "\"무슨 말씀이신지 모르겠습니다.\""], ["said I.", "내가 말했다."]], [["“Choose your bridge, Mr. Pip,”", "\"다리를 하나 고르시오, 핍 씨.\""], ["returned Wemmick, “and take a walk upon your bridge, and pitch your money into the Thames over the centre arch of your bridge, and you know the end of it.", "웨믹이 되받았다. \"그러고는 그 다리 위로 산책을 나가서, 그 다리 한가운데 아치 너머로 돈을 템스강에 던져 버리시오. 그 끝이 어찌 될지는 당신도 알 거요."], ["Serve a friend with it, and you may know the end of it too,—but it’s a less pleasant and profitable end.”", "그 돈으로 친구를 돕더라도, 그 끝이 어찌 될지는 마찬가지로 알 수 있을 테지요. 다만 그쪽이 덜 즐겁고 덜 이로운 끝일 뿐이오.\""]], [["I could have posted a newspaper in his mouth, he made it so wide after saying this.", "이 말을 하고 나서 그가 입을 어찌나 크게 벌렸던지, 그 안에 신문이라도 한 장 부쳐 넣을 수 있을 것 같았다."]], [["“This is very discouraging,” said I.", "\"이거 참 맥 빠지는 말씀이군요.\" 내가 말했다."]], [["“Meant to be so,”", "\"그러라고 한 말이오.\""], ["said Wemmick.", "웨믹이 말했다."]], [["“Then is it your opinion,”", "\"그렇다면 당신 의견으로는,\""], ["I inquired, with some little indignation, “that a man should never—”", "나는 다소 분개하며 물었다. \"사람이 결코—\""]], [["“—Invest portable property in a friend?” said Wemmick.", "\"—친구한테 운반 가능한 재산을 투자해서는 안 된다는 거냐고요?\""], ["“Certainly he should not.", "웨믹이 말했다. \"당연히 안 되지요."], ["Unless he wants to get rid of the friend,—and then it becomes a question how much portable property it may be worth to get rid of him.”", "그 친구를 떼어 내고 싶은 게 아니라면 말이오. 그렇다면 이제 그 친구를 떼어 내는 데 운반 가능한 재산이 얼마쯤 들 만한 가치가 있느냐 하는 문제가 되지요.\""]], [["“And that,”", "\"그러면 그게,\""], ["said I, “is your deliberate opinion, Mr. Wemmick?”", "내가 말했다. \"당신의 신중한 의견입니까, 웨믹 씨?\""]], [["“That,”", "\"그게,\""], ["he returned, “is my deliberate opinion in this office.”", "그가 되받았다. \"이 사무실에서의 제 신중한 의견이오.\""]], [["“Ah!”", "\"아!\""], ["said I, pressing him, for I thought I saw him near a loophole here; “but would that be your opinion at Walworth?”", "나는 여기서 그에게 빠져나갈 틈이 보이는 듯하여 다그쳐 물었다. \"하지만 그게 월워스에서도 당신 의견일까요?\""]], [["“Mr. Pip,”", "\"핍 씨.\""], ["he replied, with gravity, “Walworth is one place, and this office is another.", "그가 진지하게 대답했다. \"월워스는 한 곳이고, 이 사무실은 또 다른 곳이오."], ["Much as the Aged is one person, and Mr. Jaggers is another.", "마치 노친께서 한 사람이고, 재거스 씨가 또 다른 사람이듯이 말이오."], ["They must not be confounded together.", "그 둘을 한데 뒤섞어서는 안 되지요."], ["My Walworth sentiments must be taken at Walworth; none but my official sentiments can be taken in this office.”", "제 월워스의 정서는 월워스에서 받아들여야 하고, 이 사무실에서는 제 공적인 정서밖에 받아들일 수 없소.\""]], [["“Very well,”", "\"아주 좋습니다.\""], ["said I, much relieved, “then I shall look you up at Walworth, you may depend upon it.”", "나는 크게 안도하며 말했다. \"그러면 월워스로 당신을 찾아가겠습니다, 틀림없이요.\""]], [["“Mr. Pip,”", "\"핍 씨.\""], ["he returned, “you will be welcome there, in a private and personal capacity.”", "그가 되받았다. \"사적이고 개인적인 자격이라면, 그곳에서 당신을 환영하겠소.\""]], [["We had held this conversation in a low voice, well knowing my guardian’s ears to be the sharpest of the sharp.", "우리는 이 대화를 낮은 목소리로 나누었는데, 후견인의 귀가 둘도 없이 날카롭다는 것을 잘 알고 있었기 때문이다."], ["As he now appeared in his doorway, towelling his hands, Wemmick got on his great-coat and stood by to snuff out the candles.", "이제 그가 손을 닦으며 문간에 나타나자, 웨믹은 외투를 걸치고 촛불을 끄려고 곁에 섰다."], ["We all three went into the street together, and from the door-step Wemmick turned his way, and Mr. Jaggers and I turned ours.", "우리 셋은 함께 거리로 나섰고, 문간 계단에서 웨믹은 자기 길로, 재거스 씨와 나는 우리 길로 갈라졌다."]], [["I could not help wishing more than once that evening, that Mr. Jaggers had had an Aged in Gerrard Street, or a Stinger, or a Something, or a Somebody, to unbend his brows a little.", "그날 저녁 나는 재거스 씨에게도 제라드 가에 노친 같은 이가, 혹은 스팅어든, 혹은 어떤 무엇이든, 혹은 어떤 누구든, 그의 찌푸린 눈썹을 조금이나마 펴 줄 존재가 있었으면 하는 바람을 한 번 이상 품지 않을 수 없었다."], ["It was an uncomfortable consideration on a twenty-first birthday, that coming of age at all seemed hardly worth while in such a guarded and suspicious world as he made of it.", "스물한 번째 생일에, 그가 빚어 놓은 것 같은 그토록 경계심 많고 의심 많은 세상에서는 성년이 된다는 것 자체가 도무지 그럴 만한 가치가 없어 보인다는 것은, 마음을 불편하게 하는 생각이었다."], ["He was a thousand times better informed and cleverer than Wemmick, and yet I would a thousand times rather have had Wemmick to dinner.", "그는 웨믹보다 천 배는 더 박식하고 영리했지만, 그럼에도 나는 천 배는 더 웨믹을 저녁 식사에 모시고 싶었다."], ["And Mr. Jaggers made not me alone intensely melancholy, because, after he was gone, Herbert said of himself, with his eyes fixed on the fire, that he thought he must have committed a felony and forgotten the details of it, he felt so dejected and guilty.", "그리고 재거스 씨가 우울하게 만든 사람은 나 하나만이 아니었으니, 그가 떠난 뒤 허버트는 불을 응시한 채 자기 자신에 대해 이렇게 말했다. 자기가 무슨 중죄라도 저지르고는 그 자세한 내막을 잊어버린 게 틀림없는 것 같다고, 그만큼 풀이 죽고 죄스러운 기분이 든다고."]], [["# Chapter XXXVII.", "# Chapter XXXVII."]], [["Deeming Sunday the best day for taking Mr. Wemmick’s Walworth sentiments, I devoted the next ensuing Sunday afternoon to a pilgrimage to the Castle.", "일요일이 웨믹 씨의 월워스식 정서를 살피기에 가장 좋은 날이라 여긴 나는, 다음에 돌아온 일요일 오후를 성으로의 순례에 바쳤다."], ["On arriving before the battlements, I found the Union Jack flying and the drawbridge up; but undeterred by this show of defiance and resistance, I rang at the gate, and was admitted in a most pacific manner by the Aged.", "성벽 앞에 다다라 보니 유니언 잭이 펄럭이고 도개교가 올려져 있었다. 그러나 이런 항거와 저항의 과시에 굴하지 않고 나는 문에서 종을 울렸고, 노인장의 더없이 평화로운 응대를 받으며 안으로 들어섰다."]], [["“My son, sir,”", "\"제 아들이 말입니다.\""], ["said the old man, after securing the drawbridge, “rather had it in his mind that you might happen to drop in, and he left word that he would soon be home from his afternoon’s walk.", "노인이 도개교를 단단히 고정한 뒤 말했다. \"혹시 손님께서 들르실지도 모른다고 어느 정도 생각하고 있었지요. 오후 산책에서 곧 돌아오겠노라 일러두었습니다."], ["He is very regular in his walks, is my son.", "제 아들은 산책이 아주 규칙적이거든요."], ["Very regular in everything, is my son.”", "매사에 아주 규칙적이지요, 제 아들은.\""]], [["I nodded at the old gentleman as Wemmick himself might have nodded, and we went in and sat down by the fireside.", "나는 웨믹 본인이 끄덕였을 법한 모양새로 노신사에게 고개를 끄덕였고, 우리는 안으로 들어가 난롯가에 앉았다."]], [["“You made acquaintance with my son, sir,”", "\"제 아들과 알게 되신 게 말이지요, 손님.\""], ["said the old man, in his chirping way, while he warmed his hands at the blaze, “at his office, I expect?”", "노인이 불길에 두 손을 쬐며 그 지저귀는 듯한 말투로 말했다. \"그 애 사무소에서였겠지요?\" 나는 끄덕였다. \"허!"], ["I nodded. “Hah! I have heerd that my son is a wonderful hand at his business, sir?”", "제 아들이 제 일에 솜씨가 대단하다고 들었는데요, 손님?\" 나는 힘차게 끄덕였다."], ["I nodded hard. “Yes; so they tell me.", "\"예, 다들 그렇게들 말합디다."], ["His business is the Law?”", "그 애 일이 법률 쪽이지요?\""], ["I nodded harder. “Which makes it more surprising in my son,”", "나는 더 힘차게 끄덕였다. \"그러니 제 아들로선 더 놀라운 일이지요.\""], ["said the old man, “for he was not brought up to the Law, but to the Wine-Coopering.”", "노인이 말했다. \"그 애는 법률을 배운 게 아니라 포도주 통 만드는 일을 배웠으니까요.\""]], [["Curious to know how the old gentleman stood informed concerning the reputation of Mr.", "노신사가 재거스 씨의 평판에 관해 어느 정도 알고 있는지 궁금해진 나는, 그 이름을 그에게 큰 소리로 외쳤다."], ["Jaggers, I roared that name at him. He threw me into the greatest confusion by laughing heartily and replying in a very sprightly manner, “No, to be sure; you’re right.”", "그러자 노인은 진심으로 웃으며 아주 쾌활한 태도로, \"아니지요, 그렇고말고요. 손님 말씀이 옳습니다.\"라고 대답하여 나를 더없는 혼란에 빠뜨렸다."], ["And to this hour I have not the faintest notion what he meant, or what joke he thought I had made.", "그리고 나는 지금 이 순간까지도 그가 무슨 뜻으로 한 말인지, 또 내가 무슨 농담을 했다고 그가 여긴 것인지 전혀 짐작조차 못 하고 있다."]], [["As I could not sit there nodding at him perpetually, without making some other attempt to interest him, I shouted at inquiry whether his own calling in life had been “the Wine-Coopering.”", "그렇다고 그에게 흥미를 끌 다른 시도도 없이 마냥 거기 앉아 끄덕이고만 있을 수는 없었으므로, 나는 그분 자신의 평생 직업이 \"포도주 통 만드는 일\"이었는지를 큰 소리로 물었다."], ["By dint of straining that term out of myself several times and tapping the old gentleman on the chest to associate it with him, I at last succeeded in making my meaning understood.", "그 말을 몇 번이나 애써 짜내고, 그것을 그분과 연결 짓고자 노신사의 가슴을 톡톡 두드린 끝에, 나는 마침내 내 뜻을 알아듣게 하는 데 성공했다."]], [["“No,” said the old gentleman; “the warehousing, the warehousing.", "\"아니지요.\" 노신사가 말했다."], ["First, over yonder;”", "\"창고업, 창고업이었습니다."], ["he appeared to mean up the chimney, but I believe he intended to refer me to Liverpool; “and then in the City of London here.", "처음엔 저 너머에서\"—그는 굴뚝 위를 가리키는 듯했으나, 내 짐작에는 리버풀을 두고 한 말인 듯했다—\"그러고는 여기 런던 시내에서요."], ["However, having an infirmity—for I am hard of hearing, sir—”", "한데, 몸에 탈이 나서 말이지요—제가 귀가 어둡거든요, 손님—\""]], [["I expressed in pantomime the greatest astonishment.", "나는 더없는 경악을 몸짓으로 나타냈다."]], [["“—Yes, hard of hearing; having that infirmity coming upon me, my son he went into the Law, and he took charge of me, and he by little and little made out this elegant and beautiful property.", "\"—예, 귀가 어둡지요. 그런 탈이 제게 닥쳐오니, 제 아들이 법률 쪽으로 들어가 저를 떠맡았고, 조금씩 조금씩 이 우아하고 아름다운 재산을 일구어 냈답니다. 한데 손님께서 하신 말씀으로 돌아가자면 말이지요.\""], ["But returning to what you said, you know,”", "노인이 다시 진심으로 웃으며 말을 이었다."], ["pursued the old man, again laughing heartily, “what I say is, No to be sure; you’re right.”", "\"제가 드리는 말씀은, 아니지요, 그렇고말고요, 손님 말씀이 옳다는 겁니다.\""]], [["I was modestly wondering whether my utmost ingenuity would have enabled me to say anything that would have amused him half as much as this imaginary pleasantry, when I was startled by a sudden click in the wall on one side of the chimney, and the ghostly tumbling open of a little wooden flap with “JOHN”", "나는 내 온갖 재간을 다 짜낸들 이 가상의 농담의 절반만큼이라도 그를 즐겁게 할 만한 말을 할 수 있었을까 겸손히 자문하던 차에, 굴뚝 한쪽 벽에서 느닷없이 딸깍 소리가 나고 \"존\"이라고 쓰인 작은 나무 덮개가 유령처럼 펄렁 열리는 바람에 흠칫 놀랐다."], ["upon it. The old man, following my eyes, cried with great triumph, “My son’s come home!”", "노인은 내 눈길을 좇더니 의기양양하게 외쳤다. \"제 아들이 돌아왔어요!\""], ["and we both went out to the drawbridge.", "그러고는 우리 둘 다 도개교로 나갔다."]], [["It was worth any money to see Wemmick waving a salute to me from the other side of the moat, when we might have shaken hands across it with the greatest ease.", "손쉽게 해자 너머로 악수를 나눌 만한 거리인데도, 웨믹이 저편에서 내게 인사를 보내며 손을 흔드는 광경은 그 어떤 돈을 주고도 볼 만한 것이었다."], ["The Aged was so delighted to work the drawbridge, that I made no offer to assist him, but stood quiet until Wemmick had come across, and had presented me to Miss Skiffins; a lady by whom he was accompanied.", "노인장이 도개교를 작동시키는 일을 어찌나 즐거워하던지, 나는 거들겠다고 나서지 않고 가만히 서 있었고, 마침내 웨믹이 건너와 자신과 동행한 부인인 스키핀스 양에게 나를 소개했다."]], [["Miss Skiffins was of a wooden appearance, and was, like her escort, in the post-office branch of the service.", "스키핀스 양은 목석 같은 외양이었고, 그녀의 동반자와 마찬가지로 우편 업무 분야에 종사하고 있었다."], ["She might have been some two or three years younger than Wemmick, and I judged her to stand possessed of portable property.", "웨믹보다 두세 살쯤 어려 보였고, 동산을 좀 소유하고 있는 듯싶었다."], ["The cut of her dress from the waist upward, both before and behind, made her figure very like a boy’s kite; and I might have pronounced her gown a little too decidedly orange, and her gloves a little too intensely green.", "앞뒤로 허리 위쪽 옷의 재단 모양 때문에 그녀의 몸매는 영락없이 사내아이가 날리는 연 같았다. 그리고 나는 그녀의 드레스가 좀 지나치게 단호히 주황색이고, 장갑이 좀 지나치게 짙은 초록색이라고 단언했을 법하다."], ["But she seemed to be a good sort of fellow, and showed a high regard for the Aged.", "그래도 그녀는 사람 됨됨이가 좋은 축인 듯했고, 노인장을 매우 존중했다."], ["I was not long in discovering that she was a frequent visitor at the Castle; for, on our going in, and my complimenting Wemmick on his ingenious contrivance for announcing himself to the Aged, he begged me to give my attention for a moment to the other side of the chimney, and disappeared.", "그녀가 성을 자주 찾는 손님이라는 것을 알아채는 데는 오래 걸리지 않았다. 우리가 안으로 들어가서, 노인장에게 자신의 귀가를 알리는 그 기발한 장치를 내가 웨믹에게 칭찬하자, 그는 잠시 굴뚝 반대편에 주목해 달라고 청하고는 사라졌기 때문이다."], ["Presently another click came, and another little door tumbled open with “Miss Skiffins”", "곧 또 한 번 딸깍 소리가 나며, \"스키핀스 양\"이라고 쓰인 또 다른 작은 문이 펄렁 열렸다."], ["on it; then Miss Skiffins shut up and John tumbled open; then Miss Skiffins and John both tumbled open together, and finally shut up together.", "이어 스키핀스 양이 닫히고 존이 펄렁 열렸으며, 그러고는 스키핀스 양과 존이 함께 펄렁 열렸다가 마침내 함께 닫혔다."], ["On Wemmick’s return from working these mechanical appliances, I expressed the great admiration with which I regarded them, and he said, “Well, you know, they’re both pleasant and useful to the Aged.", "이 기계 장치들을 작동시키고 돌아온 웨믹에게 나는 그것들에 대해 품은 크나큰 감탄을 표했고, 그는 이렇게 말했다. \"음, 아시다시피 이건 노인장께 즐겁기도 하고 쓸모도 있는 것들이지요."], ["And by George, sir, it’s a thing worth mentioning, that of all the people who come to this gate, the secret of those pulls is only known to the Aged, Miss Skiffins, and me!”", "그리고 정말이지, 손님, 이건 말할 만한 일인데, 이 문으로 찾아오는 모든 사람들 중에 그 줄들의 비밀을 아는 건 노인장과 스키핀스 양과 저뿐이랍니다!\""]], [["“And Mr. Wemmick made them,”", "\"그리고 웨믹 씨가 만드셨어요.\""], ["added Miss Skiffins, “with his own hands out of his own head.”", "스키핀스 양이 덧붙였다. \"당신 머리에서 나온 생각으로, 당신 손수요.\""]], [["While Miss Skiffins was taking off her bonnet (she retained her green gloves during the evening as an outward and visible sign that there was company), Wemmick invited me to take a walk with him round the property, and see how the island looked in wintertime.", "스키핀스 양이 보닛을 벗는 동안(그녀는 손님이 와 있다는 외적이고 가시적인 표시로 저녁 내내 초록 장갑은 끼고 있었다), 웨믹은 함께 재산을 한 바퀴 둘러보며 겨울철에 이 섬이 어떤 모습인지 보자고 나를 청했다."], ["Thinking that he did this to give me an opportunity of taking his Walworth sentiments, I seized the opportunity as soon as we were out of the Castle.", "그가 내게 월워스식 정서를 살필 기회를 주려고 그러는 것이라 여긴 나는, 성을 벗어나자마자 그 기회를 붙잡았다."]], [["Having thought of the matter with care, I approached my subject as if I had never hinted at it before.", "이 문제를 신중히 생각해 둔 터라, 나는 전에 한 번도 그것을 비친 적이 없는 듯이 본론에 다가갔다."], ["I informed Wemmick that I was anxious in behalf of Herbert Pocket, and I told him how we had first met, and how we had fought.", "나는 웨믹에게 허버트 포켓을 위해 마음이 쓰인다고 알리고, 우리가 처음 어떻게 만났는지, 또 어떻게 싸웠는지를 이야기했다."], ["I glanced at Herbert’s home, and at his character, and at his having no means but such as he was dependent on his father for; those, uncertain and unpunctual.", "나는 허버트의 가정과 그의 인품, 그리고 그가 아버지에게 의존하는 것 말고는 다른 수단이 없다는 것—그나마도 불확실하고 들쭉날쭉한 것임을—을 짚었다."], ["I alluded to the advantages I had derived in my first rawness and ignorance from his society, and I confessed that I feared I had but ill repaid them, and that he might have done better without me and my expectations.", "나는 미숙하고 무지하던 시절 그와의 교제에서 얻은 이로움을 언급했고, 그것을 제대로 갚지 못한 것이 두렵다는 것, 그리고 그가 나와 내 유산이 없었더라면 더 잘 지냈을지도 모른다는 것을 고백했다."], ["Keeping Miss Havisham in the background at a great distance, I still hinted at the possibility of my having competed with him in his prospects, and at the certainty of his possessing a generous soul, and being far above any mean distrusts, retaliations, or designs.", "해비셤 양은 멀찍이 뒤편에 두면서도, 나는 내가 그의 앞날에서 그와 경쟁했을 가능성과, 그가 너그러운 영혼을 지녀 그 어떤 치사한 불신이나 보복이나 술책 따위와는 거리가 먼 사람임이 분명하다는 것을 넌지시 비쳤다."], ["For all these reasons (I told Wemmick), and because he was my young companion and friend, and I had a great affection for him, I wished my own good fortune to reflect some rays upon him, and therefore I sought advice from Wemmick’s experience and knowledge of men and affairs, how I could best try with my resources to help Herbert to some present income,—say of a hundred a year, to keep him in good hope and heart,—and gradually to buy him on to some small partnership.", "이 모든 이유들 때문에(나는 웨믹에게 말했다), 그리고 그가 나의 젊은 동무이자 친구이며 내가 그에게 큰 애정을 품고 있기에, 나는 나 자신의 행운이 그에게도 몇 줄기 빛을 비춰 주기를 바란다고, 그래서 내 재력으로 어떻게 하면 가장 잘 허버트를 도와 당장의 얼마간의 수입을—이를테면 그가 좋은 희망과 기운을 유지할 수 있도록 일 년에 백 파운드쯤을—마련해 주고, 차츰 작은 동업의 몫을 사 줄 수 있을지, 사람과 세상사에 관한 웨믹의 경험과 식견에서 조언을 구한다고 말했다."], ["I begged Wemmick, in conclusion, to understand that my help must always be rendered without Herbert’s knowledge or suspicion, and that there was no one else in the world with whom I could advise.", "끝으로 나는 웨믹에게, 내 도움은 언제나 허버트가 알거나 눈치채지 못하게 베풀어져야 한다는 것, 그리고 이 세상에 의논할 사람이 그 말고는 아무도 없다는 것을 헤아려 달라고 청했다."], ["I wound up by laying my hand upon his shoulder, and saying, “I can’t help confiding in you, though I know it must be troublesome to you; but that is your fault, in having ever brought me here.”", "나는 그의 어깨에 손을 얹으며 이렇게 매듭지었다. \"당신에게 이게 성가신 일임을 알면서도 당신을 믿고 털어놓지 않을 수 없군요. 하지만 그건 애초에 나를 이곳으로 데려온 당신 탓입니다.\""]], [["Wemmick was silent for a little while, and then said with a kind of start, “Well you know, Mr. Pip, I must tell you one thing.", "웨믹은 잠시 말이 없더니, 흠칫하는 듯한 태도로 입을 열었다. \"음, 그러니까 핍 씨, 한 가지는 말씀드려야겠군요."], ["This is devilish good of you.”", "이건 지독히도 훌륭한 일입니다.\""]], [["“Say you’ll help me to be good then,”", "\"그럼 내가 훌륭한 사람이 되도록 도와주겠다고 말해 줘요.\""], ["said I.", "내가 말했다."]], [["“Ecod,”", "\"이런.\""], ["replied Wemmick, shaking his head, “that’s not my trade.”", "웨믹이 고개를 저으며 대답했다. \"그건 제 장사가 아니지요.\""]], [["“Nor is this your trading-place,” said I.", "\"여기도 당신의 장사터가 아니잖아요.\" 내가 말했다."]], [["“You are right,”", "\"맞습니다.\""], ["he returned.", "그가 응수했다."], ["“You hit the nail on the head. Mr.", "\"정곡을 찌르셨군요."], ["Pip, I’ll put on my considering-cap, and I think all you want to do may be done by degrees.", "핍 씨, 제가 곰곰이 궁리하는 모자를 한번 써 보겠습니다. 손님께서 하시려는 일은 차츰차츰 다 이루어질 수 있을 거라 봅니다."], ["Skiffins (that’s her brother) is an accountant and agent.", "스키핀스라는 사람이(그게 그녀의 오라비입니다) 회계사이자 대리인이지요."], ["I’ll look him up and go to work for you.”", "그를 찾아가 손님을 위해 일을 추진해 보겠습니다.\""]], [["“I thank you ten thousand times.”", "\"수만 번 감사드립니다.\""]], [["“On the contrary,”", "\"천만에요.\" 그가 말했다."], ["said he, “I thank you, for though we are strictly in our private and personal capacity, still it may be mentioned that there are Newgate cobwebs about, and it brushes them away.”", "\"오히려 제가 손님께 감사드립니다. 우리가 엄밀히 사적이고 개인적인 처지로 하는 일이긴 하나, 그래도 말씀드릴 만한 건, 주위에 뉴게이트의 거미줄이 끼어 있는데, 이런 일이 그것을 쓸어 없애 준다는 거지요.\""]], [["After a little further conversation to the same effect, we returned into the Castle where we found Miss Skiffins preparing tea.", "같은 취지로 조금 더 이야기를 나눈 뒤, 우리는 성으로 돌아왔고, 거기서 스키핀스 양이 차를 준비하는 것을 보았다."], ["The responsible duty of making the toast was delegated to the Aged, and that excellent old gentleman was so intent upon it that he seemed to me in some danger of melting his eyes.", "토스트를 굽는 막중한 임무는 노인장에게 맡겨졌는데, 그 훌륭한 노신사가 어찌나 거기에 골몰하던지 내 눈에는 그가 눈알을 녹여 버릴 위험에 처한 것처럼 보였다."], ["It was no nominal meal that we were going to make, but a vigorous reality.", "우리가 하려는 것은 이름뿐인 식사가 아니라 기운찬 실체였다."], ["The Aged prepared such a hay-stack of buttered toast, that I could scarcely see him over it as it simmered on an iron stand hooked on to the top-bar; while Miss Skiffins brewed such a jorum of tea, that the pig in the back premises became strongly excited, and repeatedly expressed his desire to participate in the entertainment.", "노인장은 버터 바른 토스트를 어찌나 건초 더미처럼 쌓아 올렸던지, 그것이 맨 위 가로대에 걸린 무쇠 받침대 위에서 지글거리는 동안 나는 그 너머로 그의 모습을 보기조차 어려웠다. 한편 스키핀스 양은 차를 어찌나 큰 동이로 끓였던지, 뒤편 마당에 있던 돼지가 몹시 흥분하여 이 잔치에 끼고 싶은 욕구를 거듭 드러냈다."]], [["The flag had been struck, and the gun had been fired, at the right moment of time, and I felt as snugly cut off from the rest of Walworth as if the moat were thirty feet wide by as many deep.", "깃발은 내려졌고 대포는 알맞은 시각에 발사되었으니, 나는 해자가 너비 삼십 피트에 깊이도 그만큼 되기라도 한 듯 월워스의 나머지 세상으로부터 아늑하게 차단된 기분이 들었다."], ["Nothing disturbed the tranquillity of the Castle, but the occasional tumbling open of John and Miss Skiffins: which little doors were a prey to some spasmodic infirmity that made me sympathetically uncomfortable until I got used to it.", "성의 평온을 어지럽히는 것은, 이따금 펄렁 열리는 존과 스키핀스 양뿐이었다. 그 작은 문들은 어떤 경련성 질환에 시달리는 듯하여, 익숙해지기 전까지는 나도 덩달아 마음이 편치 않았다."], ["I inferred from the methodical nature of Miss Skiffins’s arrangements that she made tea there every Sunday night; and I rather suspected that a classic brooch she wore, representing the profile of an undesirable female with a very straight nose and a very new moon, was a piece of portable property that had been given her by Wemmick.", "스키핀스 양의 준비가 짜임새 있는 것으로 미루어, 나는 그녀가 일요일 밤마다 거기서 차를 끓이는 것이라 짐작했다. 그리고 그녀가 단 고전풍의 브로치는—매우 곧은 코와 아주 가느다란 초승달을 가진, 그다지 탐탁잖은 여인의 옆얼굴을 새긴 것이었는데—웨믹이 그녀에게 준 동산의 일부가 아닐까 하는 의심이 들었다."]], [["We ate the whole of the toast, and drank tea in proportion, and it was delightful to see how warm and greasy we all got after it.", "우리는 토스트를 죄다 먹어 치우고 그에 걸맞게 차를 마셨는데, 그러고 나서 우리 모두가 어찌나 따뜻해지고 기름기가 도는지 보는 것이 즐거웠다."], ["The Aged especially, might have passed for some clean old chief of a savage tribe, just oiled.", "특히 노인장은 막 기름칠을 한, 어느 깔끔한 야만족 늙은 추장으로 통할 만했다."], ["After a short pause of repose, Miss Skiffins—in the absence of the little servant who, it seemed, retired to the bosom of her family on Sunday afternoons—washed up the tea-things, in a trifling lady-like amateur manner that compromised none of us.", "잠시 쉬고 난 뒤, 일요일 오후면 가족의 품으로 돌아가는 모양인 어린 하녀가 없는 터라, 스키핀스 양이 우리 가운데 누구의 체면도 손상하지 않는 사소하고 숙녀다운 아마추어 솜씨로 찻그릇을 설거지했다."], ["Then, she put on her gloves again, and we drew round the fire, and Wemmick said, “Now, Aged Parent, tip us the paper.”", "그러고 나서 그녀는 다시 장갑을 끼었고, 우리는 난롯가에 둘러앉았으며, 웨믹이 말했다. \"자, 노친네, 신문 좀 펼쳐 주시지요.\""]], [["Wemmick explained to me while the Aged got his spectacles out, that this was according to custom, and that it gave the old gentleman infinite satisfaction to read the news aloud.", "노인장이 안경을 꺼내는 동안 웨믹이 내게 설명하기를, 이것이 관례에 따른 것이며 노신사는 뉴스를 소리 내어 읽는 일에서 한없는 만족을 얻는다고 했다. \"양해를 구하지는 않겠습니다.\""], ["“I won’t offer an apology,”", "웨믹이 말했다."], ["said Wemmick, “for he isn’t capable of many pleasures—are you, Aged P.?”", "\"이분은 누리실 수 있는 즐거움이 많지 않으시거든요—그렇지요, 노친네?\""]], [["“All right, John, all right,”", "\"좋다, 존, 좋아.\""], ["returned the old man, seeing himself spoken to.", "자기에게 하는 말임을 알아챈 노인이 대답했다."]], [["“Only tip him a nod every now and then when he looks off his paper,”", "\"그저 이따금 이분이 신문에서 눈을 떼실 때 끄덕여만 주시면.\""], ["said Wemmick, “and he’ll be as happy as a king.", "웨믹이 말했다. \"임금님처럼 행복해하실 겁니다."], ["We are all attention, Aged One.”", "자, 다들 경청하고 있습니다, 노친네.\""]], [["“All right, John, all right!”", "\"좋다, 존, 좋아!\""], ["returned the cheerful old man, so busy and so pleased, that it really was quite charming.", "명랑한 노인이 어찌나 분주하고 어찌나 흐뭇해하던지, 정말이지 더없이 사랑스러웠다."]], [["The Aged’s reading reminded me of the classes at Mr. Wopsle’s great-aunt’s, with the pleasanter peculiarity that it seemed to come through a keyhole.", "노인장의 낭독은 워플 씨 종조할머니네 교실을 떠올리게 했는데, 다만 그것이 열쇠 구멍을 통해 새어 나오는 듯하다는 더 유쾌한 특색이 있었다."], ["As he wanted the candles close to him, and as he was always on the verge of putting either his head or the newspaper into them, he required as much watching as a powder-mill. But Wemmick was equally untiring and gentle in his vigilance, and the Aged read on, quite unconscious of his many rescues.", "그는 촛불을 바싹 가까이 두고 싶어 했고, 늘 제 머리든 신문이든 그 속에 처박을 지경이었으므로, 화약 공장만큼이나 지켜봐야 했다. 그러나 웨믹은 그 경계에 있어 한결같이 지칠 줄 모르고 부드러웠으며, 노인장은 자신이 여러 번 구출된 것을 전혀 알아채지 못한 채 계속 읽어 나갔다."], ["Whenever he looked at us, we all expressed the greatest interest and amazement, and nodded until he resumed again.", "그가 우리를 쳐다볼 때마다 우리는 모두 더없는 흥미와 경탄을 표하며, 그가 다시 읽기 시작할 때까지 고개를 끄덕였다."]], [["As Wemmick and Miss Skiffins sat side by side, and as I sat in a shadowy corner, I observed a slow and gradual elongation of Mr. Wemmick’s mouth, powerfully suggestive of his slowly and gradually stealing his arm round Miss Skiffins’s waist.", "웨믹과 스키핀스 양이 나란히 앉아 있고 나는 어둑한 구석에 앉아 있었던 까닭에, 나는 웨믹 씨의 입이 더디고 차차 길어지는 것을 관찰했는데, 그것은 그가 천천히 또 차츰 스키핀스 양의 허리로 팔을 둘러 가고 있음을 강력히 시사했다."], ["In course of time I saw his hand appear on the other side of Miss Skiffins; but at that moment Miss Skiffins neatly stopped him with the green glove, unwound his arm again as if it were an article of dress, and with the greatest deliberation laid it on the table before her.", "이윽고 나는 그의 손이 스키핀스 양의 반대편에 나타나는 것을 보았다. 그러나 바로 그 순간 스키핀스 양은 초록 장갑으로 그를 깔끔하게 저지하고는, 그 팔을 무슨 옷가지라도 되는 양 다시 풀어내어, 더없이 신중하게 자기 앞 탁자 위에 올려놓았다."], ["Miss Skiffins’s composure while she did this was one of the most remarkable sights I have ever seen, and if I could have thought the act consistent with abstraction of mind, I should have deemed that Miss Skiffins performed it mechanically.", "이 일을 하는 동안 스키핀스 양의 침착함은 내가 여태껏 본 가장 놀라운 광경 가운데 하나였으며, 만일 그 행동이 정신이 딴 데 팔린 상태와 양립할 수 있다고 여길 수 있었다면, 나는 스키핀스 양이 그것을 기계적으로 해낸 것이라 판단했을 것이다."]], [["By and by, I noticed Wemmick’s arm beginning to disappear again, and gradually fading out of view.", "얼마 지나지 않아, 나는 웨믹의 팔이 다시 사라지기 시작해 차츰 시야에서 흐릿해져 가는 것을 알아챘다."], ["Shortly afterwards, his mouth began to widen again.", "곧이어 그의 입이 다시 벌어지기 시작했다."], ["After an interval of suspense on my part that was quite enthralling and almost painful, I saw his hand appear on the other side of Miss Skiffins.", "나로서는 자못 사로잡히고 거의 고통스럽기까지 한 한동안의 긴장 끝에, 나는 그의 손이 스키핀스 양의 반대편에 나타나는 것을 보았다."], ["Instantly, Miss Skiffins stopped it with the neatness of a placid boxer, took off that girdle or cestus as before, and laid it on the table.", "즉시 스키핀스 양은 차분한 권투 선수의 솜씨로 그것을 저지하고, 앞서처럼 그 허리띠 혹은 케스투스를 벗겨 내어 탁자 위에 올려놓았다."], ["Taking the table to represent the path of virtue, I am justified in stating that during the whole time of the Aged’s reading, Wemmick’s arm was straying from the path of virtue and being recalled to it by Miss Skiffins.", "탁자를 미덕의 길에 비긴다면, 노인장이 낭독하던 내내 웨믹의 팔이 미덕의 길에서 벗어나 헤매다가 스키핀스 양에 의해 그리로 다시 불려 돌아왔다고 진술해도 무방하리라."]], [["At last, the Aged read himself into a light slumber.", "* 케스투스: 고대 그리스·로마에서 권투 선수가 손에 감던 가죽끈."], ["This was the time for Wemmick to produce a little kettle, a tray of glasses, and a black bottle with a porcelain-topped cork, representing some clerical dignitary of a rubicund and social aspect.", "마침내 노인장은 신문을 읽다가 가벼운 선잠에 빠져들었다. 이제 웨믹이 작은 주전자 하나와 유리잔 쟁반 하나, 그리고 불그레하고 사교적인 인상의 어느 성직 고관을 본뜬, 도자기 마개를 씌운 검은 병 하나를 꺼낼 때였다."], ["With the aid of these appliances we all had something warm to drink, including the Aged, who was soon awake again.", "이 도구들의 도움으로 우리는 모두 따뜻한 마실 것을 한잔씩 들었는데, 어느새 다시 잠을 깬 노인장도 함께였다."], ["Miss Skiffins mixed, and I observed that she and Wemmick drank out of one glass.", "스키핀스 양이 술을 탔고, 나는 그녀와 웨믹이 한 잔으로 나누어 마시는 것을 관찰했다."], ["Of course I knew better than to offer to see Miss Skiffins home, and under the circumstances I thought I had best go first; which I did, taking a cordial leave of the Aged, and having passed a pleasant evening.", "물론 나는 스키핀스 양을 집까지 바래다주겠다고 나설 만큼 눈치 없지는 않았고, 사정이 그러하니 내가 먼저 자리를 뜨는 것이 상책이라 생각했다. 그래서 나는 노인장에게 정답게 작별을 고하고, 즐거운 저녁을 보낸 뒤 먼저 떠났다."]], [["Before a week was out, I received a note from Wemmick, dated Walworth, stating that he hoped he had made some advance in that matter appertaining to our private and personal capacities, and that he would be glad if I could come and see him again upon it.", "일주일이 채 지나기 전에, 나는 웨믹에게서 월워스라는 발신지가 적힌 쪽지 한 통을 받았다. 우리의 사적이고 개인적인 처지에 속하는 그 일에 어느 정도 진전을 보았기를 바라며, 그 일로 내가 다시 한번 찾아와 줄 수 있다면 기쁘겠다는 내용이었다."], ["So, I went out to Walworth again, and yet again, and yet again, and I saw him by appointment in the City several times, but never held any communication with him on the subject in or near Little Britain.", "그리하여 나는 다시 월워스로 나갔고, 또 한 번, 또 한 번 나갔으며, 약속을 잡아 시내에서도 그를 여러 번 만났으나, 리틀 브리튼 안에서나 그 근처에서는 그 일에 관해 그와 아무런 교신도 하지 않았다."], ["The upshot was, that we found a worthy young merchant or shipping-broker, not long established in business, who wanted intelligent help, and who wanted capital, and who in due course of time and receipt would want a partner.", "그 결과, 우리는 사업을 시작한 지 오래되지 않아 영민한 조력과 자본을 필요로 하고, 때가 무르익으면 동업자도 원하게 될 어느 훌륭한 젊은 상인 겸 선박 중개인을 찾아냈다."], ["Between him and me, secret articles were signed of which Herbert was the subject, and I paid him half of my five hundred pounds down, and engaged for sundry other payments: some, to fall due at certain dates out of my income: some, contingent on my coming into my property.", "그와 나 사이에 허버트를 대상으로 한 비밀 계약서가 서명되었고, 나는 내 오백 파운드 중 절반을 즉석에서 치렀으며, 그 밖의 여러 지급도 약정했다. 그중 일부는 내 수입에서 정해진 날짜에 치러질 것이었고, 일부는 내가 유산을 물려받게 되는 데 달린 것이었다."], ["Miss Skiffins’s brother conducted the negotiation.", "스키핀스 양의 오라비가 그 협상을 이끌었다."], ["Wemmick pervaded it throughout, but never appeared in it.", "웨믹은 그 일 전반에 두루 스며 있었으나, 결코 표면에 나서지 않았다."]], [["The whole business was so cleverly managed, that Herbert had not the least suspicion of my hand being in it.", "이 모든 일이 어찌나 교묘하게 처리되었던지, 허버트는 거기에 내 손길이 닿아 있으리라고는 조금도 의심하지 못했다."], ["I never shall forget the radiant face with which he came home one afternoon, and told me, as a mighty piece of news, of his having fallen in with one Clarriker (the young merchant’s name), and of Clarriker’s having shown an extraordinary inclination towards him, and of his belief that the opening had come at last.", "나는 어느 날 오후 그가 환히 빛나는 얼굴로 집에 돌아와, 대단한 소식이랍시고, 클래리커(그 젊은 상인의 이름이다)라는 사람과 우연히 만난 일이며, 클래리커가 그에게 유별난 호의를 보인 일이며, 드디어 기회가 찾아왔다는 자기 믿음을 내게 들려주던 그 모습을 결코 잊지 못할 것이다."], ["Day by day as his hopes grew stronger and his face brighter, he must have thought me a more and more affectionate friend, for I had the greatest difficulty in restraining my tears of triumph when I saw him so happy.", "날이 갈수록 그의 희망이 더 굳어지고 그의 얼굴이 더 밝아짐에 따라, 그는 나를 점점 더 다정한 친구라 여겼음이 분명한데, 그가 그토록 행복해하는 것을 보면서 나는 승리의 눈물을 억누르기가 여간 힘들지 않았기 때문이다."], ["At length, the thing being done, and he having that day entered Clarriker’s House, and he having talked to me for a whole evening in a flush of pleasure and success, I did really cry in good earnest when I went to bed, to think that my expectations had done some good to somebody.", "마침내 일이 다 이루어지고, 그날 그가 클래리커 상회에 들어가게 되어, 기쁨과 성공에 들떠 저녁 내내 내게 이야기를 늘어놓은 뒤, 나는 잠자리에 들면서, 내 유산이 누군가에게 얼마간의 보탬이 되었다는 생각에 정말이지 진심으로 울었다."]], [["A great event in my life, the turning point of my life, now opens on my view.", "내 인생의 크나큰 사건, 내 인생의 전환점이 이제 내 눈앞에 펼쳐진다."], ["But, before I proceed to narrate it, and before I pass on to all the changes it involved, I must give one chapter to Estella.", "그러나 그것을 이야기하기에 앞서, 또 그것이 수반한 온갖 변화로 넘어가기에 앞서, 나는 한 장을 에스텔라에게 바쳐야겠다."], ["It is not much to give to the theme that so long filled my heart.", "그토록 오래 내 마음을 채웠던 그 주제에 바치기에 한 장은 결코 많은 것이 아니다."]], [["# Chapter XXXVIII.", "# Chapter XXXVIII."]], [["If that staid old house near the Green at Richmond should ever come to be haunted when I am dead, it will be haunted, surely, by my ghost.", "리치먼드의 그린 광장 가까이에 있는 그 차분한 옛집이 내가 죽은 뒤 언젠가 유령이 나오는 집이 된다면, 그 집에 출몰하는 것은 분명 내 유령일 것이다."], ["O the many, many nights and days through which the unquiet spirit within me haunted that house when Estella lived there!", "아, 에스텔라가 그곳에 살던 시절, 내 안의 안식 없는 영혼이 그 집을 떠돌던 수많고도 수많은 밤과 낮이여!"], ["Let my body be where it would, my spirit was always wandering, wandering, wandering, about that house.", "내 몸이 어디에 있든, 내 영혼은 언제나 그 집 언저리를 헤매고 또 헤매고 또 헤맸다."]], [["The lady with whom Estella was placed, Mrs. Brandley by name, was a widow, with one daughter several years older than Estella.", "에스텔라가 맡겨진 집의 부인은 브랜들리 부인이라는 이름의 과부였는데, 에스텔라보다 몇 살 위인 딸 하나를 두고 있었다."], ["The mother looked young, and the daughter looked old; the mother’s complexion was pink, and the daughter’s was yellow; the mother set up for frivolity, and the daughter for theology.", "어머니는 젊어 보였고 딸은 늙어 보였으며, 어머니의 안색은 발그레했고 딸의 안색은 누르께했으며, 어머니는 경박함을 자처했고 딸은 신학을 자처했다."], ["They were in what is called a good position, and visited, and were visited by, numbers of people.", "그들은 이른바 좋은 위치에 있었고, 많은 사람들을 방문했으며 많은 사람들의 방문을 받았다."], ["Little, if any, community of feeling subsisted between them and Estella, but the understanding was established that they were necessary to her, and that she was necessary to them. Mrs.", "그들과 에스텔라 사이에는 감정의 공감이라 할 만한 것이 거의, 아니 전혀 없었으나, 그들이 에스텔라에게 필요하고 에스텔라가 그들에게 필요하다는 양해는 성립되어 있었다."], ["Brandley had been a friend of Miss Havisham’s before the time of her seclusion.", "브랜들리 부인은 미스 해비셤이 칩거에 들기 전부터 그분의 친구였다."]], [["In Mrs. Brandley’s house and out of Mrs. Brandley’s house, I suffered every kind and degree of torture that Estella could cause me.", "브랜들리 부인의 집 안에서나 밖에서나, 나는 에스텔라가 내게 안길 수 있는 온갖 종류와 정도의 고문을 겪었다."], ["The nature of my relations with her, which placed me on terms of familiarity without placing me on terms of favour, conduced to my distraction.", "그녀와 나의 관계는 친밀한 처지에 나를 두면서도 총애받는 처지에 두지는 않는 성질의 것이었으니, 그것이 나를 더없이 산란하게 만들었다."], ["She made use of me to tease other admirers, and she turned the very familiarity between herself and me to the account of putting a constant slight on my devotion to her.", "그녀는 나를 이용해 다른 숭배자들을 약 올렸고, 그녀와 나 사이의 그 친밀함 자체를 이용해, 그녀를 향한 내 헌신을 끊임없이 멸시하는 수단으로 삼았다."], ["If I had been her secretary, steward, half-brother, poor relation,—if I had been a younger brother of her appointed husband,—I could not have seemed to myself further from my hopes when I was nearest to her.", "내가 그녀의 비서나 집사나 이복 오라비나 가난한 친척이었더라도 — 그녀가 정혼한 남편의 동생이었더라도 — 그녀에게 가장 가까이 있을 때조차 나는 내 희망에서 그보다 더 멀어진 듯 느껴질 수는 없었을 것이다."], ["The privilege of calling her by her name and hearing her call me by mine became, under the circumstances an aggravation of my trials; and while I think it likely that it almost maddened her other lovers, I know too certainly that it almost maddened me.", "그녀를 이름으로 부르고 그녀가 나를 이름으로 부르는 것을 듣는 그 특권은, 이런 사정 아래서는 내 시련을 가중시키는 것이 되었다. 그리고 그것이 그녀의 다른 연인들을 거의 미치게 했으리라는 짐작이 들지만, 그것이 나를 거의 미치게 했다는 것만큼은 너무도 확실히 안다."]], [["She had admirers without end.", "그녀에게는 숭배자가 끝도 없이 많았다."], ["No doubt my jealousy made an admirer of every one who went near her; but there were more than enough of them without that.", "내 질투가 그녀에게 다가가는 모든 이를 숭배자로 만들어 버렸음은 의심할 바 없으나, 그런 것을 빼고도 그들은 차고 넘칠 만큼 많았다."]], [["I saw her often at Richmond, I heard of her often in town, and I used often to take her and the Brandleys on the water; there were picnics, fête days, plays, operas, concerts, parties, all sorts of pleasures, through which I pursued her,—and they were all miseries to me.", "나는 리치먼드에서 그녀를 자주 보았고, 시내에서 그녀에 관한 소문을 자주 들었으며, 그녀와 브랜들리 모녀를 데리고 자주 뱃놀이를 나가곤 했다. 소풍이며 축제일이며 연극이며 오페라며 음악회며 파티며, 온갖 종류의 즐거운 자리가 있었고, 그 자리들을 통해 나는 그녀를 좇았다 — 그러나 그 모든 것이 내게는 괴로움이었다."], ["I never had one hour’s happiness in her society, and yet my mind all round the four-and-twenty hours was harping on the happiness of having her with me unto death.", "나는 그녀와 함께한 자리에서 단 한 시간의 행복도 누려 본 적이 없었으되, 내 마음은 하루 스물네 시간 내내 죽을 때까지 그녀를 곁에 둔다는 행복을 되뇌고 있었다."]], [["Throughout this part of our intercourse,—and it lasted, as will presently be seen, for what I then thought a long time,—she habitually reverted to that tone which expressed that our association was forced upon us.", "우리가 교제하던 이 시기 내내 — 곧 보게 되겠지만 그것은 그 당시 내가 길다고 여긴 만큼 이어졌다 — 그녀는 우리의 교제가 우리에게 강요된 것임을 드러내는 그 말투로 으레 되돌아가곤 했다."], ["There were other times when she would come to a sudden check in this tone and in all her many tones, and would seem to pity me.", "또 어떤 때는 이 말투와 그녀의 그 숱한 말투들에서 별안간 멈추어 서서, 나를 가엾이 여기는 듯 보이기도 했다."]], [["“Pip, Pip,”", "\"핍, 핍.\""], ["she said one evening, coming to such a check, when we sat apart at a darkening window of the house in Richmond; “will you never take warning?”", "어느 날 저녁, 리치먼드 집의 어둑해 가는 창가에 우리가 떨어져 앉아 있을 때, 그녀가 그렇게 멈추어 서며 말했다. \"당신은 끝내 경고를 새기지 않을 건가요?\" \"무엇에 대한 경고요?\""]], [["“Of what?” “Of me.”", "\"나에 대한 경고요.\""]], [["“Warning not to be attracted by you, do you mean, Estella?”", "\"당신에게 끌리지 말라는 경고를 말하는 건가요, 에스텔라?\""]], [["“Do I mean! If you don’t know what I mean, you are blind.”", "\"말하는 거냐고요! 내 말뜻을 모른다면, 당신은 눈먼 사람이에요.\""]], [["I should have replied that Love was commonly reputed blind, but for the reason that I always was restrained—and this was not the least of my miseries—by a feeling that it was ungenerous to press myself upon her, when she knew that she could not choose but obey Miss Havisham.", "사랑은 흔히 눈멀었다고들 하지 않느냐고 받아쳤어야 마땅했으나, 나는 늘 한 가지 감정에 매여 있었으니 — 이것이 내 괴로움 가운데 결코 작은 것이 아니었다 — 그것은, 그녀가 미스 해비셤에게 복종하는 수밖에 달리 선택할 길이 없음을 스스로 아는 마당에 그녀에게 나를 들이대는 것은 점잖지 못한 일이라는 느낌이었다."], ["My dread always was, that this knowledge on her part laid me under a heavy disadvantage with her pride, and made me the subject of a rebellious struggle in her bosom.", "그녀의 이 자각이, 그녀의 자존심 앞에서 나를 심히 불리한 처지에 놓이게 하고, 그녀의 가슴속에서 일어나는 반항적 갈등의 대상으로 나를 만들지나 않을까 — 그것이 늘 내 두려움이었다."]], [["“At any rate,”", "\"어쨌거나,\""], ["said I, “I have no warning given me just now, for you wrote to me to come to you, this time.”", "내가 말했다. \"지금 막은 내게 아무 경고도 주어지지 않았어요. 이번에는 당신이 내게 와 달라고 편지를 썼으니까요.\""]], [["“That’s true,”", "\"그건 사실이에요.\""], ["said Estella, with a cold careless smile that always chilled me.", "에스텔라가 늘 나를 오싹하게 하던 그 차갑고 무심한 미소를 띠며 말했다."]], [["After looking at the twilight without, for a little while, she went on to say:—", "잠시 바깥의 황혼을 바라보더니, 그녀는 말을 이었다."]], [["“The time has come round when Miss Havisham wishes to have me for a day at Satis.", "\"미스 해비셤이 나를 하루 새티스로 데려가고 싶어 하시는 때가 다시 돌아왔어요."], ["You are to take me there, and bring me back, if you will.", "당신이 괜찮다면, 나를 그곳에 데려갔다가 다시 데려와 주세요."], ["She would rather I did not travel alone, and objects to receiving my maid, for she has a sensitive horror of being talked of by such people.", "그분은 내가 혼자 여행하는 걸 원치 않으시고, 내 하녀를 들이는 것도 마다하세요. 그런 부류의 사람들 입에 오르내리는 데 예민하리만치 진저리를 치시니까요."], ["Can you take me?”", "나를 데려가 줄 수 있나요?\""]], [["“Can I take you, Estella!”", "\"내가 당신을 데려갈 수 있느냐고요, 에스텔라!\""]], [["“You can then? The day after to-morrow, if you please.", "\"그럼 데려가 줄 수 있는 거죠? 괜찮다면 모레요."], ["You are to pay all charges out of my purse.", "비용은 모두 내 지갑에서 치르도록 하세요."], ["You hear the condition of your going?”", "당신이 동행하는 조건을 들었지요?\""]], [["“And must obey,” said I.", "\"따를 수밖에요.\" 내가 말했다."]], [["This was all the preparation I received for that visit, or for others like it; Miss Havisham never wrote to me, nor had I ever so much as seen her handwriting.", "그것이 그 방문에 대해, 혹은 그와 비슷한 다른 방문들에 대해 내가 받은 준비의 전부였다. 미스 해비셤은 내게 한 번도 편지를 쓴 적이 없었고, 나는 그분의 필체조차 본 적이 없었다."], ["We went down on the next day but one, and we found her in the room where I had first beheld her, and it is needless to add that there was no change in Satis House.", "우리는 그다음다음 날 내려갔고, 내가 그분을 처음 보았던 바로 그 방에서 그분을 만났는데, 새티스 하우스에 아무런 변화도 없었다는 것은 굳이 덧붙일 필요가 없으리라."]], [["She was even more dreadfully fond of Estella than she had been when I last saw them together; I repeat the word advisedly, for there was something positively dreadful in the energy of her looks and embraces.", "그분은 내가 두 사람을 함께 본 마지막 때보다도 한층 더 무섭도록 에스텔라를 애지중지했다. 나는 일부러 그 말을 다시 쓴다. 그분의 눈길과 포옹의 열기에는 실로 무서운 무언가가 깃들어 있었으니까."], ["She hung upon Estella’s beauty, hung upon her words, hung upon her gestures, and sat mumbling her own trembling fingers while she looked at her, as though she were devouring the beautiful creature she had reared.", "그분은 에스텔라의 아름다움에 매달리고, 그녀의 말에 매달리고, 그녀의 몸짓에 매달렸으며, 그녀를 바라보면서 자신의 떨리는 손가락을 우물우물 씹고 앉아 있었는데, 마치 자신이 길러 낸 그 아름다운 피조물을 집어삼키기라도 하려는 듯했다."]], [["From Estella she looked at me, with a searching glance that seemed to pry into my heart and probe its wounds.", "에스텔라에게서 눈을 돌려 그분은 나를 바라보았는데, 내 가슴속을 캐고 들어 그 상처를 헤집는 듯한 살피는 눈길이었다."], ["“How does she use you, Pip; how does she use you?”", "\"그 애가 너를 어떻게 대하더냐, 핍, 어떻게 대하더냐?\""], ["she asked me again, with her witch-like eagerness, even in Estella’s hearing.", "에스텔라가 듣고 있는 자리에서까지 그분은 마녀 같은 열의로 내게 거듭 물었다."], ["But, when we sat by her flickering fire at night, she was most weird; for then, keeping Estella’s hand drawn through her arm and clutched in her own hand, she extorted from her, by dint of referring back to what Estella had told her in her regular letters, the names and conditions of the men whom she had fascinated; and as Miss Havisham dwelt upon this roll, with the intensity of a mind mortally hurt and diseased, she sat with her other hand on her crutch stick, and her chin on that, and her wan bright eyes glaring at me, a very spectre.", "그러나 밤에 우리가 그분의 가물거리는 난롯가에 앉아 있을 때, 그분은 더없이 기괴했다. 그때 그분은 에스텔라의 손을 자신의 팔에 꿰어 자기 손으로 움켜쥔 채, 에스텔라가 정기적으로 보낸 편지에 적어 보낸 내용을 거듭 들먹여 가며, 그녀가 호려 놓은 사내들의 이름과 처지를 그녀에게서 끌어냈다. 그리고 미스 해비셤이 치명적으로 상하고 병든 마음의 강렬함으로 그 명단을 곱씹는 동안, 그분은 다른 손을 목발 지팡이에 얹고 그 위에 턱을 괸 채, 핼쑥하게 번득이는 눈으로 나를 노려보고 앉아 있었으니, 영락없는 망령이었다."]], [["I saw in this, wretched though it made me, and bitter the sense of dependence and even of degradation that it awakened,—I saw in this that Estella was set to wreak Miss Havisham’s revenge on men, and that she was not to be given to me until she had gratified it for a term.", "나는 이 광경에서 — 그것이 나를 비참하게 만들고, 그것이 일깨운 의존의 감각, 나아가 굴욕의 감각이 쓰라렸음에도 — 나는 이 광경에서, 에스텔라가 사내들에게 미스 해비셤의 복수를 행하도록 정해져 있다는 것을, 그리고 그녀가 한동안 그 복수를 채워 줄 때까지는 내게 주어지지 않으리라는 것을 보았다."], ["I saw in this, a reason for her being beforehand assigned to me.", "나는 이 광경에서, 그녀가 미리부터 내게 정해져 있던 이유를 보았다."], ["Sending her out to attract and torment and do mischief, Miss Havisham sent her with the malicious assurance that she was beyond the reach of all admirers, and that all who staked upon that cast were secured to lose.", "그녀를 내보내어 사내들을 끌고 괴롭히고 해코지하게 하면서, 미스 해비셤은 그녀가 어떤 숭배자도 손 닿지 못할 곳에 있다는, 그리고 거기에 운을 거는 자는 모조리 잃게끔 정해져 있다는 악의에 찬 확신을 함께 들려 보냈던 것이다."], ["I saw in this that I, too, was tormented by a perversion of ingenuity, even while the prize was reserved for me.", "나는 이 광경에서, 상이 나를 위해 따로 마련되어 있는 동안조차 나 또한 비뚤어진 술책에 의해 괴로움을 당하고 있다는 것을 보았다."], ["I saw in this the reason for my being staved off so long and the reason for my late guardian’s declining to commit himself to the formal knowledge of such a scheme.", "나는 이 광경에서, 내가 그토록 오래 따돌림당해 온 이유를, 그리고 나의 지난날 후견인이 그런 음모를 공식적으로 안다고 자처하기를 마다한 이유를 보았다."], ["In a word, I saw in this Miss Havisham as I had her then and there before my eyes, and always had had her before my eyes; and I saw in this, the distinct shadow of the darkened and unhealthy house in which her life was hidden from the sun.", "한마디로, 나는 이 광경에서, 그때 그 자리에서 내 눈앞에 있던 그대로의, 그리고 늘 내 눈앞에 있어 온 그대로의 미스 해비셤을 보았다. 그리고 나는 이 광경에서, 그분의 삶이 햇빛으로부터 숨겨져 있던 그 어둡고 병든 집의 또렷한 그림자를 보았다."]], [["The candles that lighted that room of hers were placed in sconces on the wall.", "그분의 그 방을 밝히던 촛불들은 벽에 붙은 촛대에 꽂혀 있었다."], ["They were high from the ground, and they burnt with the steady dulness of artificial light in air that is seldom renewed.", "그것들은 바닥에서 높이 떨어져 있었고, 좀처럼 새 공기가 들지 않는 대기 속에서 인공조명 특유의 한결같은 둔탁함으로 타고 있었다."], ["As I looked round at them, and at the pale gloom they made, and at the stopped clock, and at the withered articles of bridal dress upon the table and the ground, and at her own awful figure with its ghostly reflection thrown large by the fire upon the ceiling and the wall, I saw in everything the construction that my mind had come to, repeated and thrown back to me.", "그 촛불들을, 그리고 그것들이 자아내는 희뿌연 어스름을, 그리고 멈춰 선 시계를, 그리고 탁자와 바닥 위에 놓인 시들어 버린 신부 예복 따위를, 그리고 난롯불이 천장과 벽에 던져 크게 비춘 망령 같은 그림자와 더불어 그분 자신의 끔찍한 형체를 둘러보면서, 나는 모든 것 속에서 내 마음이 다다른 그 결론이 거듭 비치고 내게 되던져지는 것을 보았다."], ["My thoughts passed into the great room across the landing where the table was spread, and I saw it written, as it were, in the falls of the cobwebs from the centre-piece, in the crawlings of the spiders on the cloth, in the tracks of the mice as they betook their little quickened hearts behind the panels, and in the gropings and pausings of the beetles on the floor.", "내 생각은 층계참 건너편의, 식탁이 차려져 있는 그 큰 방으로 옮겨 갔고, 거기서 나는 그 결론이 적혀 있는 것을 보았으니 — 가운데 장식에서 늘어진 거미줄에, 식탁보 위를 기어 다니는 거미들에, 작고 다급해진 심장을 안고 벽판 뒤로 내빼는 생쥐들의 발자취에, 그리고 마룻바닥 위에서 더듬거리며 멈칫대는 딱정벌레들에, 그것은 적혀 있었다."]], [["It happened on the occasion of this visit that some sharp words arose between Estella and Miss Havisham.", "이 방문 때에 마침 에스텔라와 미스 해비셤 사이에 날 선 말이 오가는 일이 있었다."], ["It was the first time I had ever seen them opposed.", "두 사람이 맞서는 것을 내가 본 것은 그때가 처음이었다."]], [["We were seated by the fire, as just now described, and Miss Havisham still had Estella’s arm drawn through her own, and still clutched Estella’s hand in hers, when Estella gradually began to detach herself.", "방금 묘사한 대로 우리는 난롯가에 앉아 있었고, 미스 해비셤은 여전히 에스텔라의 팔을 자신의 팔에 꿰고 에스텔라의 손을 자기 손으로 움켜쥐고 있었는데, 그때 에스텔라가 차츰 몸을 빼기 시작했다."], ["She had shown a proud impatience more than once before, and had rather endured that fierce affection than accepted or returned it.", "그녀는 그 전에도 한두 번 거만한 짜증을 내비친 적이 있었고, 그 격렬한 애정을 받아들이거나 되돌려 주기보다는 차라리 견뎌 내고 있던 터였다."]], [["“What!”", "\"뭐라고!\""], ["said Miss Havisham, flashing her eyes upon her, “are you tired of me?”", "미스 해비셤이 그녀에게 두 눈을 번득이며 말했다. \"내가 지겨워졌느냐?\""]], [["“Only a little tired of myself,” replied Estella, disengaging her arm, and moving to the great chimney-piece, where she stood looking down at the fire.", "\"그저 제 자신이 조금 지겨워졌을 뿐이에요.\" 에스텔라가 팔을 빼며 대답하고는, 커다란 벽난로 선반 쪽으로 옮겨 가, 그 앞에 서서 불을 내려다보았다."]], [["“Speak the truth, you ingrate!”", "\"사실대로 말해, 이 배은망덕한 것아!\""], ["cried Miss Havisham, passionately striking her stick upon the floor; “you are tired of me.”", "미스 해비셤이 격정에 차서 지팡이로 바닥을 내리치며 외쳤다. \"내가 지겨워진 게지.\""]], [["Estella looked at her with perfect composure, and again looked down at the fire.", "에스텔라는 더없이 침착하게 그분을 바라보다가, 다시 불을 내려다보았다."], ["Her graceful figure and her beautiful face expressed a self-possessed indifference to the wild heat of the other, that was almost cruel.", "그녀의 우아한 자태와 아름다운 얼굴은 상대의 그 광포한 열기에 대한, 거의 잔인하다 할 만큼 차분한 무관심을 드러내고 있었다."]], [["“You stock and stone!”", "\"이 목석 같은 것!\""], ["exclaimed Miss Havisham. “You cold, cold heart!”", "미스 해비셤이 부르짖었다. \"이 차디찬, 차디찬 마음을 가진 것!\""]], [["“What?”", "\"뭐라고요?\""], ["said Estella, preserving her attitude of indifference as she leaned against the great chimney-piece and only moving her eyes; “do you reproach me for being cold? You?”", "에스텔라가 커다란 벽난로 선반에 기댄 채 무관심한 태도를 그대로 지키며, 눈동자만 움직여 말했다. \"제가 차갑다고 나무라시는 건가요? 당신이요?\""]], [["“Are you not?” was the fierce retort.", "\"네가 차갑지 않다는 게냐?\" 사나운 반박이 돌아왔다."]], [["“You should know,”", "\"그건 당신이 아셔야지요.\""], ["said Estella.", "에스텔라가 말했다."], ["“I am what you have made me.", "\"저는 당신이 만든 그대로의 사람이에요."], ["Take all the praise, take all the blame; take all the success, take all the failure; in short, take me.”", "칭찬도 다 가지시고, 비난도 다 가지세요. 성공도 다 가지시고, 실패도 다 가지세요. 한마디로, 저를 다 가지세요.\""]], [["“O, look at her, look at her!”", "\"오, 저것 좀 봐, 저것 좀 봐!\""], ["cried Miss Havisham, bitterly; “Look at her so hard and thankless, on the hearth where she was reared!", "미스 해비셤이 쓰라리게 외쳤다. \"저렇게 모질고 은혜를 모르는 꼴을, 저 애가 길러진 바로 이 화롯가에서!"], ["Where I took her into this wretched breast when it was first bleeding from its stabs, and where I have lavished years of tenderness upon her!”", "처음으로 찔린 상처에서 피를 흘리던 이 비참한 가슴에 내가 저 애를 받아들였던 곳, 그리고 내가 여러 해의 다정함을 저 애에게 아낌없이 쏟아부었던 이곳에서!\""]], [["“At least I was no party to the compact,”", "\"적어도 저는 그 약조에 끼어든 적이 없어요.\""], ["said Estella, “for if I could walk and speak, when it was made, it was as much as I could do.", "에스텔라가 말했다. \"그 약조가 맺어졌을 때 제가 걷고 말할 수 있었다 한들, 그게 제가 할 수 있는 전부였으니까요."], ["But what would you have?", "하지만 무엇을 원하세요?"], ["You have been very good to me, and I owe everything to you.", "당신은 제게 무척 잘해 주셨고, 저는 모든 것을 당신께 빚지고 있어요."], ["What would you have?”", "무엇을 원하세요?\""]], [["“Love,” replied the other.", "\"사랑이지.\" 상대가 대답했다."]], [["“You have it.”", "\"그건 가지고 계세요.\""]], [["“I have not,”", "\"못 가졌다.\""], ["said Miss Havisham.", "미스 해비셤이 말했다."]], [["“Mother by adoption,”", "\"양어머니,\""], ["retorted Estella, never departing from the easy grace of her attitude, never raising her voice as the other did, never yielding either to anger or tenderness,—“mother by adoption, I have said that I owe everything to you.", "에스텔라가, 그 편안하고 우아한 태도에서 조금도 벗어나지 않은 채, 상대처럼 목소리를 높이는 일도 없이, 노여움에도 다정함에도 굴하는 일 없이 받아쳤다. \"양어머니, 저는 모든 것을 당신께 빚지고 있다고 말했어요."], ["All I possess is freely yours.", "제가 가진 모든 것은 거리낌 없이 당신 것이에요."], ["All that you have given me, is at your command to have again.", "당신이 제게 주신 모든 것은, 도로 거두어 가시겠다면 당신 뜻대로예요."], ["Beyond that, I have nothing.", "그 너머로는 제게 아무것도 없어요."], ["And if you ask me to give you, what you never gave me, my gratitude and duty cannot do impossibilities.”", "그런데 당신이 한 번도 제게 주지 않은 것을 당신께 드리라 하신다면, 제 감사와 도리로도 불가능한 일은 할 수 없는 거예요.\""]], [["“Did I never give her love!”", "\"내가 저 애에게 사랑을 준 적이 없다고!\""], ["cried Miss Havisham, turning wildly to me.", "미스 해비셤이 격하게 내게로 돌아서며 외쳤다."], ["“Did I never give her a burning love, inseparable from jealousy at all times, and from sharp pain, while she speaks thus to me!", "\"저 애가 내게 저렇게 말하는 마당에도, 내가 저 애에게 늘 질투와 떼려야 뗄 수 없고 쓰라린 아픔과도 떼려야 뗄 수 없는 불타는 사랑을 준 적이 없다고!"], ["Let her call me mad, let her call me mad!”", "나더러 미쳤다고 하라지, 미쳤다고 하라지!\""]], [["“Why should I call you mad,”", "\"제가 왜 당신을 미쳤다고 하겠어요.\""], ["returned Estella, “I, of all people?", "에스텔라가 응수했다. \"하필 제가요?"], ["Does any one live, who knows what set purposes you have, half as well as I do?", "당신이 어떤 작정을 품으셨는지, 저만큼 잘 아는 사람이 세상에 살아 있나요?"], ["Does any one live, who knows what a steady memory you have, half as well as I do?", "당신의 기억이 얼마나 한결같은지, 저만큼 잘 아는 사람이 세상에 살아 있나요?"], ["I who have sat on this same hearth on the little stool that is even now beside you there, learning your lessons and looking up into your face, when your face was strange and frightened me!”", "지금도 당신 곁에 놓여 있는 저 작은 걸상에 앉아 바로 이 화롯가에서, 당신의 가르침을 익히며 당신 얼굴을 올려다보던 저인데요. 그 얼굴이 낯설어 저를 겁먹게 하던 때에 말이에요!\""]], [["“Soon forgotten!”", "\"금세 잊었구나!\""], ["moaned Miss Havisham.", "미스 해비셤이 신음했다."], ["“Times soon forgotten!”", "\"그 시절을 금세 잊었어!\""]], [["“No, not forgotten,”", "\"아니에요, 잊지 않았어요.\""], ["retorted Estella,—“not forgotten, but treasured up in my memory.", "에스텔라가 받아쳤다. \"잊지 않고, 제 기억 속에 소중히 간직해 두었어요."], ["When have you found me false to your teaching?", "제가 당신의 가르침을 저버린 적이 언제 있었나요?"], ["When have you found me unmindful of your lessons?", "제가 당신의 교훈을 소홀히 한 적이 언제 있었나요?"], ["When have you found me giving admission here,”", "제가 여기에,\" 그녀가 손으로 자기 가슴을 짚었다."], ["she touched her bosom with her hand, “to anything that you excluded? Be just to me.”", "\"당신이 막아 두신 어떤 것이든 들여놓은 적이 언제 있었나요? 제게 공정하게 대해 주세요.\""]], [["“So proud, so proud!” moaned Miss Havisham, pushing away her grey hair with both her hands.", "\"저토록 거만하구나, 저토록 거만해!\" 미스 해비셤이 두 손으로 자신의 잿빛 머리카락을 밀어 헤치며 신음했다."]], [["“Who taught me to be proud?”", "\"누가 저를 거만하게끔 가르쳤나요?\""], ["returned Estella.", "에스텔라가 응수했다."], ["“Who praised me when I learnt my lesson?”", "\"제가 그 가르침을 익혔을 때 누가 저를 칭찬했나요?\""]], [["“So hard, so hard!” moaned Miss Havisham, with her former action.", "\"저토록 모질구나, 저토록 모질어!\" 미스 해비셤이 앞서와 같은 동작을 하며 신음했다."]], [["“Who taught me to be hard?”", "\"누가 저를 모질게끔 가르쳤나요?\""], ["returned Estella.", "에스텔라가 응수했다."], ["“Who praised me when I learnt my lesson?”", "\"제가 그 가르침을 익혔을 때 누가 저를 칭찬했나요?\""]], [["“But to be proud and hard to me!”", "\"하지만 나에게 거만하고 모질다니!\""], ["Miss Havisham quite shrieked, as she stretched out her arms.", "미스 해비셤이 두 팔을 뻗으며 거의 비명을 질렀다."], ["“Estella, Estella, Estella, to be proud and hard to me!”", "\"에스텔라, 에스텔라, 에스텔라, 나에게 거만하고 모질다니!\""]], [["Estella looked at her for a moment with a kind of calm wonder, but was not otherwise disturbed; when the moment was past, she looked down at the fire again.", "에스텔라는 일종의 차분한 의아함을 담아 잠시 그분을 바라보았으나, 그 밖에 달리 흔들리지는 않았다. 그 순간이 지나자, 그녀는 다시 불을 내려다보았다."]], [["“I cannot think,”", "\"이별 끝에 제가 당신을 뵈러 왔는데,\""], ["said Estella, raising her eyes after a silence “why you should be so unreasonable when I come to see you after a separation.", "잠시 침묵하던 에스텔라가 눈을 들며 말했다. \"당신이 어찌하여 그토록 사리에 맞지 않게 구시는지 저는 모르겠어요."], ["I have never forgotten your wrongs and their causes.", "저는 당신이 입은 부당한 일과 그 까닭을 한 번도 잊은 적이 없어요."], ["I have never been unfaithful to you or your schooling.", "저는 당신께도, 당신의 가르침에도 한 번도 거스른 적이 없어요."], ["I have never shown any weakness that I can charge myself with.”", "저는 스스로 탓할 만한 어떤 나약함도 보인 적이 없어요.\""]], [["“Would it be weakness to return my love?”", "\"내 사랑에 보답하는 것이 나약함이라는 게냐?\""], ["exclaimed Miss Havisham. “But yes, yes, she would call it so!”", "미스 해비셤이 외쳤다. \"그래, 그래, 저 애는 그걸 나약함이라 부르겠지!\""]], [["“I begin to think,” said Estella, in a musing way, after another moment of calm wonder, “that I almost understand how this comes about.", "\"이제 어렴풋이 알 것 같아요.\" 또 한순간 차분한 의아함에 잠겼던 에스텔라가, 곰곰이 생각하는 투로 말했다."], ["If you had brought up your adopted daughter wholly in the dark confinement of these rooms, and had never let her know that there was such a thing as the daylight by which she had never once seen your face,—if you had done that, and then, for a purpose had wanted her to understand the daylight and know all about it, you would have been disappointed and angry?”", "\"어쩌다 이리되었는지 거의 알 것 같아요. 만약 당신이 양딸을 이 방들의 어두운 감금 속에서만 길러 내고, 그 애가 당신 얼굴을 한 번도 본 적 없게 한 그 햇빛이라는 것이 세상에 있다는 걸 끝내 알지 못하게 하셨다면 — 그렇게 해 놓고는, 어떤 목적을 위해 그 애가 햇빛을 이해하고 그것에 관해 다 알기를 바라셨다면, 당신은 실망하고 노여워하셨겠지요?\""]], [["Miss Havisham, with her head in her hands, sat making a low moaning, and swaying herself on her chair, but gave no answer.", "미스 해비셤은 두 손에 머리를 묻은 채 나직이 신음하며 의자에 앉아 몸을 앞뒤로 흔들고 있었으나, 아무 대답도 하지 않았다."]], [["“Or,”", "\"아니면,\""], ["said Estella,—“which is a nearer case,—if you had taught her, from the dawn of her intelligence, with your utmost energy and might, that there was such a thing as daylight, but that it was made to be her enemy and destroyer, and she must always turn against it, for it had blighted you and would else blight her;—if you had done this, and then, for a purpose, had wanted her to take naturally to the daylight and she could not do it, you would have been disappointed and angry?”", "에스텔라가 말했다. \"이쪽이 더 가까운 경우인데요 — 만약 당신이, 그 애의 지각이 트일 때부터 온 힘과 기력을 다해, 햇빛이라는 것이 세상에 있기는 하나 그것은 그 애의 적이요 파괴자가 되게끔 만들어진 것이니 늘 그것에 등을 돌려야 한다고, 그것이 당신을 망쳐 놓았고 그러지 않으면 그 애마저 망쳐 놓으리라고 가르치셨다면 — 그렇게 해 놓고는, 어떤 목적을 위해 그 애가 햇빛에 자연스레 다가가기를 바라셨는데 그 애가 그러지 못한다면, 당신은 실망하고 노여워하셨겠지요?\""]], [["Miss Havisham sat listening (or it seemed so, for I could not see her face), but still made no answer.", "미스 해비셤은 귀 기울여 듣고 앉아 있었으나 (그렇게 보였다, 그분의 얼굴은 볼 수 없었으니까) 여전히 아무 대답도 하지 않았다."]], [["“So,”", "\"그러니,\""], ["said Estella, “I must be taken as I have been made.", "에스텔라가 말했다. \"저는 만들어진 그대로 받아들여져야 해요."], ["The success is not mine, the failure is not mine, but the two together make me.”", "성공도 제 것이 아니고, 실패도 제 것이 아니지만, 그 둘이 합쳐져 저를 이루는 거예요.\""]], [["Miss Havisham had settled down, I hardly knew how, upon the floor, among the faded bridal relics with which it was strewn.", "미스 해비셤은, 어찌 된 영문인지 나도 잘 모르는 사이에, 바닥에 흩어진 빛바랜 신부 예복의 잔해들 가운데로 주저앉아 있었다."], ["I took advantage of the moment—I had sought one from the first—to leave the room, after beseeching Estella’s attention to her, with a movement of my hand.", "나는 그 순간을 틈타 — 처음부터 나는 그런 순간을 노리고 있었다 — 손짓으로 에스텔라에게 그분을 보살펴 달라고 청한 뒤, 방을 나왔다."], ["When I left, Estella was yet standing by the great chimney-piece, just as she had stood throughout.", "내가 나올 때, 에스텔라는 줄곧 서 있던 그대로, 여전히 커다란 벽난로 선반 곁에 서 있었다."], ["Miss Havisham’s grey hair was all adrift upon the ground, among the other bridal wrecks, and was a miserable sight to see.", "미스 해비셤의 잿빛 머리카락은 다른 신부 예복의 잔해들 사이로 온통 흐트러진 채 바닥에 널려 있었으니, 보기에 처량한 광경이었다."]], [["It was with a depressed heart that I walked in the starlight for an hour and more, about the courtyard, and about the brewery, and about the ruined garden.", "나는 침울한 마음으로 한 시간 남짓, 별빛 아래 안뜰을, 양조장을, 그리고 폐허가 된 정원을 거닐었다."], ["When I at last took courage to return to the room, I found Estella sitting at Miss Havisham’s knee, taking up some stitches in one of those old articles of dress that were dropping to pieces, and of which I have often been reminded since by the faded tatters of old banners that I have seen hanging up in cathedrals.", "마침내 용기를 내어 방으로 돌아가 보니, 에스텔라가 미스 해비셤의 무릎 곁에 앉아, 너덜너덜 헤어져 가던 그 낡은 예복 가운데 하나에 몇 땀 바느질을 하고 있었다. 그 예복은, 그 뒤로 내가 대성당에 걸려 있는 것을 본, 빛바랜 누더기 옛 깃발들을 통해 종종 떠올리게 된 바로 그런 물건이었다."], ["Afterwards, Estella and I played at cards, as of yore,—only we were skilful now, and played French games,—and so the evening wore away, and I went to bed.", "그 후 에스텔라와 나는 옛날처럼 카드놀이를 했는데 — 다만 이제 우리는 솜씨가 늘어, 프랑스식 놀이를 했다 — 그렇게 저녁이 저물어 갔고, 나는 잠자리에 들었다."]], [["I lay in that separate building across the courtyard.", "나는 안뜰 건너편의 그 별채에서 잤다."], ["It was the first time I had ever lain down to rest in Satis House, and sleep refused to come near me.", "새티스 하우스에서 내가 쉬려고 누워 본 것은 그때가 처음이었는데, 잠은 좀처럼 내게 다가오려 하지 않았다."], ["A thousand Miss Havishams haunted me.", "천 명의 미스 해비셤이 나를 따라다녔다."], ["She was on this side of my pillow, on that, at the head of the bed, at the foot, behind the half-opened door of the dressing-room, in the dressing-room, in the room overhead, in the room beneath,—everywhere.", "그분은 내 베개 이쪽에, 저쪽에, 침대 머리맡에, 발치에, 반쯤 열린 화장실 문 뒤에, 화장실 안에, 위층 방에, 아래층 방에 — 어디에나 있었다."], ["At last, when the night was slow to creep on towards two o’clock, I felt that I absolutely could no longer bear the place as a place to lie down in, and that I must get up.", "마침내 밤이 느릿느릿 두 시를 향해 기어가던 무렵, 나는 더 이상은 이곳을 누워 쉴 곳으로 도저히 견딜 수 없으며, 일어나야만 한다는 느낌에 사로잡혔다."], ["I therefore got up and put on my clothes, and went out across the yard into the long stone passage, designing to gain the outer courtyard and walk there for the relief of my mind.", "그래서 나는 일어나 옷을 입고, 마음을 달래려고 바깥뜰로 나가 그곳을 거닐 작정으로, 뜰을 가로질러 긴 돌 복도로 나섰다. 그러나 복도에 들어서기가 무섭게 나는 촛불을 꺼 버렸다."], ["But I was no sooner in the passage than I extinguished my candle; for I saw Miss Havisham going along it in a ghostly manner, making a low cry.", "미스 해비셤이 망령처럼 그 복도를 따라 걸어가며 나직이 흐느끼는 소리를 내고 있는 것을 보았기 때문이다."], ["I followed her at a distance, and saw her go up the staircase.", "나는 멀찍이서 그분을 뒤따랐고, 그분이 층계를 올라가는 것을 보았다."], ["She carried a bare candle in her hand, which she had probably taken from one of the sconces in her own room, and was a most unearthly object by its light.", "그분은 손에 촛대도 없는 맨 양초 하나를 들고 있었는데, 아마도 자기 방의 촛대 가운데 하나에서 빼 든 것이었을 테고, 그 불빛 속의 그분은 더없이 섬뜩한 형상이었다."], ["Standing at the bottom of the staircase, I felt the mildewed air of the feast-chamber, without seeing her open the door, and I heard her walking there, and so across into her own room, and so across again into that, never ceasing the low cry.", "층계 밑에 서서, 나는 그분이 문을 여는 것을 보지는 못한 채 그 잔치방의 곰팡내 나는 공기를 느꼈고, 그분이 그 안을 거니는 소리를, 그러고는 자기 방으로 건너갔다가 다시 그 방으로 건너오는 소리를 들었으니, 나직한 흐느낌은 그칠 줄을 몰랐다."], ["After a time, I tried in the dark both to get out, and to go back, but I could do neither until some streaks of day strayed in and showed me where to lay my hands.", "한참 뒤, 나는 어둠 속에서 밖으로 나가 보려고도, 도로 돌아가 보려고도 했으나, 한 줄기 새벽빛이 새어 들어 손 짚을 곳을 일러 줄 때까지는 어느 쪽도 할 수 없었다."], ["During the whole interval, whenever I went to the bottom of the staircase, I heard her footstep, saw her light pass above, and heard her ceaseless low cry.", "그동안 내내, 내가 층계 밑으로 갈 때마다, 나는 그분의 발소리를 듣고, 그분의 불빛이 위로 지나가는 것을 보고, 그분의 그칠 줄 모르는 나직한 흐느낌을 들었다."]], [["Before we left next day, there was no revival of the difference between her and Estella, nor was it ever revived on any similar occasion; and there were four similar occasions, to the best of my remembrance.", "이튿날 우리가 떠나기 전, 그분과 에스텔라 사이의 그 불화가 되살아나는 일은 없었고, 그와 비슷한 어떤 자리에서도 그것이 되살아난 적은 없었다. 그리고 내 기억이 옳다면, 그와 비슷한 자리는 네 번 있었다."], ["Nor, did Miss Havisham’s manner towards Estella in anywise change, except that I believed it to have something like fear infused among its former characteristics.", "또한 미스 해비셤이 에스텔라를 대하는 태도도 조금도 변하지 않았으니, 다만 그 예전의 특징들 가운데 두려움 비슷한 무언가가 스며든 것 같다고 나는 믿었다."]], [["It is impossible to turn this leaf of my life, without putting Bentley Drummle’s name upon it; or I would, very gladly.", "벤틀리 드러믈의 이름을 거기 적어 넣지 않고서는 내 인생의 이 책장을 넘길 수가 없다. 그러지 않을 수만 있다면 나는 기꺼이 그러고 싶지만."]], [["On a certain occasion when the Finches were assembled in force, and when good feeling was being promoted in the usual manner by nobody’s agreeing with anybody else, the presiding Finch called the Grove to order, forasmuch as Mr.", "언젠가 핀치 회원들이 한껏 모여, 아무도 다른 누구의 말에 동의하지 않는 평소의 방식으로 좋은 분위기가 무르익어 가던 자리에서, 사회를 맡은 핀치가 그로브의 정숙을 청했다."], ["Drummle had not yet toasted a lady; which, according to the solemn constitution of the society, it was the brute’s turn to do that day.", "드러믈 씨가 아직 어느 숙녀를 위해 건배를 올리지 않았기 때문이었는데, 이 모임의 엄숙한 규약에 따르자면 그날 그 짐승이 그 일을 할 차례였던 것이다."], ["I thought I saw him leer in an ugly way at me while the decanters were going round, but as there was no love lost between us, that might easily be.", "술병이 돌아가는 동안 그자가 나를 향해 흉하게 곁눈질하며 히죽거리는 것을 본 듯했으나, 우리 사이에 정이라곤 없었으니 그건 능히 그럴 법한 일이었다."], ["What was my indignant surprise when he called upon the company to pledge him to “Estella!”", "그자가 좌중에게 \"에스텔라!\"를 위해 함께 건배하자고 청했을 때, 내가 느낀 분개에 찬 놀라움이란!"]], [["“Estella who?”", "\"어디의 에스텔라요?\""], ["said I.", "내가 말했다."]], [["“Never you mind,”", "\"당신이 알 바 아니오.\""], ["retorted Drummle.", "드러믈이 받아쳤다."]], [["“Estella of where?”", "\"어디 사는 에스텔라요?\""], ["said I.", "내가 말했다."], ["“You are bound to say of where.” Which he was, as a Finch.", "\"어디 사는지를 말해야 하오.\" 핀치 회원으로서 그는 마땅히 그래야 했다."]], [["“Of Richmond, gentlemen,”", "\"리치먼드의 에스텔라요, 여러분.\""], ["said Drummle, putting me out of the question, “and a peerless beauty.”", "드러믈이 나를 무시한 채 말했다. \"비할 데 없는 미인이지요.\""]], [["Much he knew about peerless beauties, a mean, miserable idiot!", "비할 데 없는 미인에 대해 그자가 무얼 안다고, 야비하고 한심한 멍청이 같으니!"], ["I whispered Herbert.", "나는 허버트에게 속삭였다."]], [["“I know that lady,” said Herbert, across the table, when the toast had been honoured.", "\"나는 그 숙녀를 안다네.\" 건배가 끝난 뒤 허버트가 식탁 너머로 말했다."]], [["“Do you?”", "\"그래?\""], ["said Drummle.", "드러믈이 말했다."]], [["“And so do I,”", "\"나도 알지.\""], ["I added, with a scarlet face.", "내가 새빨개진 얼굴로 덧붙였다."]], [["“Do you?”", "\"그래?\""], ["said Drummle.", "드러믈이 말했다."], ["“O, Lord!”", "\"허, 이런!\""]], [["This was the only retort—except glass or crockery—that the heavy creature was capable of making; but, I became as highly incensed by it as if it had been barbed with wit, and I immediately rose in my place and said that I could not but regard it as being like the honourable Finch’s impudence to come down to that Grove,—we always talked about coming down to that Grove, as a neat Parliamentary turn of expression,—down to that Grove, proposing a lady of whom he knew nothing. Mr.", "이 무거운 짐승이 할 수 있는 응수란 — 유리잔이나 사기그릇을 던지는 것 말고는 — 그것뿐이었다. 그러나 나는 그것이 마치 위트로 날을 세운 것이기라도 한 양 잔뜩 분개해, 그 자리에서 즉시 일어나, 자기가 전혀 알지도 못하는 숙녀를 추천하려고 이 그로브로 내려오다니 — 우리는 늘 그 그로브로 \"내려온다\"고 말했으니, 의회식의 말끔한 표현법을 본뜬 것이었다 — 그 그로브로 내려오다니, 명예로운 핀치 회원의 뻔뻔함이라고밖에 볼 수 없다고 말했다."], ["Drummle, upon this, starting up, demanded what I meant by that?", "이에 드러믈 씨가 벌떡 일어나, 그게 무슨 뜻이냐고 따져 물었다."], ["Whereupon I made him the extreme reply that I believed he knew where I was to be found.", "그러자 나는 그에게, 나를 어디서 찾을 수 있는지는 그가 알 거라고 믿는다는 극단적인 대꾸를 했다."]], [["Whether it was possible in a Christian country to get on without blood, after this, was a question on which the Finches were divided.", "이 일이 있은 뒤, 기독교 국가에서 피를 보지 않고 일을 마무리할 수 있느냐 하는 것이 핀치 회원들 사이에서 의견이 갈리는 문제였다."], ["The debate upon it grew so lively, indeed, that at least six more honourable members told six more, during the discussion, that they believed they knew where they were to be found.", "그것을 둘러싼 논쟁이 어찌나 활기를 띠었던지, 논의가 진행되는 동안 적어도 여섯 명의 명예로운 회원이 또 다른 여섯 명에게, 자기들을 어디서 찾을 수 있는지는 자기들도 알 거라고 믿는다고 말했을 정도였다."], ["However, it was decided at last (the Grove being a Court of Honour) that if Mr.", "그러나 마침내 (그로브는 명예 법정이었으므로) 다음과 같이 결정되었다."], ["Drummle would bring never so slight a certificate from the lady, importing that he had the honour of her acquaintance, Mr. Pip must express his regret, as a gentleman and a Finch, for “having been betrayed into a warmth which.”", "만약 드러믈 씨가 그 숙녀와 알고 지내는 영광을 누렸음을 알리는, 아무리 사소한 것이라도 그 숙녀의 증명서를 가져온다면, 핍 씨는 신사로서 또한 핀치 회원으로서, \"어떠한 흥분에 빠졌던\" 것을 유감으로 표해야 한다는 것이었다."], ["Next day was appointed for the production (lest our honour should take cold from delay), and next day Drummle appeared with a polite little avowal in Estella’s hand, that she had had the honour of dancing with him several times.", "증명서 제출은 (지체하다 우리의 명예가 식어 버릴까 하여) 이튿날로 정해졌고, 이튿날 드러믈은, 자기가 그와 여러 차례 춤추는 영광을 누렸다는, 에스텔라의 필체로 된 정중하고 자그마한 인정의 글을 들고 나타났다."], ["This left me no course but to regret that I had been “betrayed into a warmth which,” and on the whole to repudiate, as untenable, the idea that I was to be found anywhere.", "이로써 나는 \"어떠한 흥분에 빠졌던\" 것을 유감으로 표하고, 또 전체적으로는 나를 어디서든 찾을 수 있으리라는 생각을 근거 없는 것으로 철회하는 수밖에 다른 길이 없었다."], ["Drummle and I then sat snorting at one another for an hour, while the Grove engaged in indiscriminate contradiction, and finally the promotion of good feeling was declared to have gone ahead at an amazing rate.", "그리하여 드러믈과 나는 한 시간 동안 서로를 향해 콧방귀를 뀌며 앉아 있었고, 그동안 그로브는 가리지 않는 반박에 몰두했으며, 마침내 좋은 분위기의 증진이 놀라운 속도로 진척되었다고 선언되었다."]], [["I tell this lightly, but it was no light thing to me.", "나는 이 일을 가볍게 이야기하지만, 내게는 결코 가벼운 일이 아니었다."], ["For, I cannot adequately express what pain it gave me to think that Estella should show any favour to a contemptible, clumsy, sulky booby, so very far below the average.", "에스텔라가, 평균에도 한참 못 미치는, 그토록 경멸스럽고 굼뜨고 뚱한 멍청이에게 조금이라도 호의를 보였으리라는 생각이 내게 안긴 고통이 어떤 것이었는지, 나는 도저히 제대로 표현할 수가 없다."], ["To the present moment, I believe it to have been referable to some pure fire of generosity and disinterestedness in my love for her, that I could not endure the thought of her stooping to that hound.", "지금 이 순간까지도 나는, 그녀가 그 개 같은 자에게 몸을 굽히는 것을 차마 견딜 수 없었던 까닭이, 그녀를 향한 내 사랑 속의 어떤 순수한 관대함과 사심 없음의 불꽃에서 비롯된 것이었다고 믿는다."], ["No doubt I should have been miserable whomsoever she had favoured; but a worthier object would have caused me a different kind and degree of distress.", "그녀가 누구에게 호의를 보였든 나는 비참했으리라는 데 의심의 여지가 없으나, 그보다 더 합당한 상대였다면 내게 다른 종류와 정도의 괴로움을 안겼을 것이다."]], [["It was easy for me to find out, and I did soon find out, that Drummle had begun to follow her closely, and that she allowed him to do it.", "드러믈이 그녀를 바짝 따라다니기 시작했다는 것, 그리고 그녀가 그러도록 내버려 두고 있다는 것을 알아내기란 내게 쉬운 일이었고, 나는 곧 그것을 알아냈다."], ["A little while, and he was always in pursuit of her, and he and I crossed one another every day.", "얼마 지나지 않아 그는 늘 그녀를 좇고 있었고, 그와 나는 날마다 마주쳤다."], ["He held on, in a dull persistent way, and Estella held him on; now with encouragement, now with discouragement, now almost flattering him, now openly despising him, now knowing him very well, now scarcely remembering who he was.", "그는 둔하고 끈질긴 태도로 매달렸고, 에스텔라는 그를 붙들어 두었다. 어떤 때는 부추기고, 어떤 때는 낙담시키고, 어떤 때는 거의 그를 추켜세우고, 어떤 때는 대놓고 그를 멸시하고, 어떤 때는 그를 아주 잘 아는 듯하다가, 어떤 때는 그가 누구였는지 거의 기억도 못 하는 듯이."]], [["The Spider, as Mr. Jaggers had called him, was used to lying in wait, however, and had the patience of his tribe.", "그러나 재거스 씨가 거미라 불렀던 그자는 매복에 익숙했고, 제 족속다운 인내심을 지니고 있었다."], ["Added to that, he had a blockhead confidence in his money and in his family greatness, which sometimes did him good service,—almost taking the place of concentration and determined purpose.", "거기에 더해, 그는 제 돈과 제 가문의 위세에 대한 멍청한 자신감을 지니고 있었는데, 그것이 이따금 그에게 큰 도움이 되었으니 — 집중력과 굳은 결의를 거의 대신할 정도였다."], ["So, the Spider, doggedly watching Estella, outwatched many brighter insects, and would often uncoil himself and drop at the right nick of time.", "그리하여 거미는, 끈덕지게 에스텔라를 지켜보면서, 저보다 훨씬 영민한 숱한 벌레들보다 더 오래 지켜보았고, 종종 적시에 몸을 풀어 뚝 떨어지곤 했다."]], [["At a certain Assembly Ball at Richmond (there used to be Assembly Balls at most places then), where Estella had outshone all other beauties, this blundering Drummle so hung about her, and with so much toleration on her part, that I resolved to speak to her concerning him.", "리치먼드의 어느 무도회에서 (그 시절엔 거의 모든 곳에 무도회가 있곤 했다), 에스텔라가 다른 모든 미인들을 압도하던 그 자리에서, 이 얼뜬 드러믈이 그녀 곁을 어찌나 떠나지 못하던지, 게다가 그녀 쪽에서도 어찌나 너그러이 봐주던지, 나는 그에 관해 그녀에게 말해야겠다고 결심했다."], ["I took the next opportunity; which was when she was waiting for Mrs. Blandley to take her home, and was sitting apart among some flowers, ready to go.", "나는 다음 기회를 잡았다. 그녀가 브랜들리 부인이 자기를 집에 데려가 주기를 기다리며, 떠날 채비를 한 채 꽃들 사이에 떨어져 앉아 있을 때였다."], ["I was with her, for I almost always accompanied them to and from such places.", "나는 그녀와 함께 있었으니, 그런 자리를 오갈 때 나는 거의 언제나 그들을 동행했던 것이다."]], [["“Are you tired, Estella?”", "\"피곤한가요, 에스텔라?\""]], [["“Rather, Pip.”", "\"좀 그래요, 핍.\""]], [["“You should be.”", "\"그럴 만도 하지요.\""]], [["“Say rather, I should not be; for I have my letter to Satis House to write, before I go to sleep.”", "\"차라리 피곤하지 말아야 한다고 하는 편이 낫겠어요. 잠들기 전에 새티스 하우스로 보낼 편지를 써야 하니까요.\""]], [["“Recounting to-night’s triumph?” said I.", "\"오늘 밤의 개가를 늘어놓는 건가요?\" 내가 말했다."], ["“Surely a very poor one, Estella.”", "\"참으로 보잘것없는 개가지요, 에스텔라.\""]], [["“What do you mean?", "\"무슨 말이에요?"], ["I didn’t know there had been any.”", "무슨 개가가 있었다는 건지 난 몰랐는데요.\""]], [["“Estella,”", "\"에스텔라,\""], ["said I, “do look at that fellow in the corner yonder, who is looking over here at us.”", "내가 말했다. \"저기 저 구석에서 이쪽의 우리를 건너다보고 있는 저 친구 좀 봐요.\""]], [["“Why should I look at him?”", "\"내가 왜 그를 봐야 하죠?\""], ["returned Estella, with her eyes on me instead.", "에스텔라가 그 대신 나를 바라보며 응수했다."], ["“What is there in that fellow in the corner yonder,—to use your words,—that I need look at?”", "\"저기 저 구석의 저 친구에게 — 당신 말을 빌리자면 — 내가 봐야 할 무엇이 있다는 거죠?\""]], [["“Indeed, that is the very question I want to ask you,” said I.", "\"실은 그게 바로 내가 당신에게 묻고 싶은 거예요.\" 내가 말했다."], ["“For he has been hovering about you all night.”", "\"그가 밤새도록 당신 주위를 맴돌고 있었으니까요.\""]], [["“Moths, and all sorts of ugly creatures,”", "\"나방이며 온갖 흉한 것들은,\""], ["replied Estella, with a glance towards him, “hover about a lighted candle.", "에스텔라가 그를 향해 흘긋 눈길을 던지며 대꾸했다. \"불 켜진 촛불 주위를 맴도는 법이지요."], ["Can the candle help it?”", "그게 촛불 탓이겠어요?\""]], [["“No,”", "\"아니지요.\""], ["I returned; “but cannot the Estella help it?”", "내가 받았다. \"하지만 그 에스텔라는 어쩔 수 없는 걸까요?\""]], [["“Well!”", "\"글쎄요!\""], ["said she, laughing, after a moment, “perhaps. Yes.", "그녀가 잠시 뒤 웃으며 말했다. \"어쩌면요. 그래요."], ["Anything you like.”", "당신 좋을 대로요.\""]], [["“But, Estella, do hear me speak.", "\"하지만, 에스텔라, 부디 내 말 좀 들어 봐요."], ["It makes me wretched that you should encourage a man so generally despised as Drummle. You know he is despised.”", "드러믈처럼 두루 멸시받는 사내를 당신이 부추긴다는 게 나를 비참하게 만들어요. 그가 멸시받는 자라는 걸 당신도 알잖아요.\""]], [["“Well?”", "\"그래서요?\""], ["said she.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["“You know he is as ungainly within as without.", "\"그가 겉모습만큼이나 속도 볼품없다는 걸 당신도 알잖아요."], ["A deficient, ill-tempered, lowering, stupid fellow.”", "모자라고, 성마르고, 음침하고, 멍청한 친구라는 걸요.\""]], [["“Well?”", "\"그래서요?\""], ["said she.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["“You know he has nothing to recommend him but money and a ridiculous roll of addle-headed predecessors; now, don’t you?”", "\"그를 추천할 거라곤 돈과, 머리가 텅 빈 조상들의 우스꽝스러운 명부밖에 없다는 걸 당신도 알잖아요. 자, 그렇지 않나요?\""]], [["“Well?”", "\"그래서요?\""], ["said she again; and each time she said it, she opened her lovely eyes the wider.", "그녀가 다시 말했다. 그리고 그 말을 할 때마다, 그녀는 그 사랑스러운 두 눈을 점점 더 크게 떴다."]], [["To overcome the difficulty of getting past that monosyllable, I took it from her, and said, repeating it with emphasis, “Well!", "그 외마디를 넘어서기가 어려워, 나는 그 말을 그녀에게서 가져다, 힘주어 되풀이하며 말했다. \"그래서라니요!"], ["Then, that is why it makes me wretched.”", "그러니까, 그래서 나를 비참하게 만든다는 거예요.\""]], [["Now, if I could have believed that she favoured Drummle with any idea of making me—me—wretched, I should have been in better heart about it; but in that habitual way of hers, she put me so entirely out of the question, that I could believe nothing of the kind.", "만약 그녀가 나를 — 바로 나를 — 비참하게 만들려는 어떤 생각으로 드러믈에게 호의를 보이는 것이라고 믿을 수 있었다면, 나는 그에 관해 한결 마음이 나았을 것이다. 그러나 그녀는 그 늘 하던 식으로, 나를 너무도 철저히 안중에 두지 않았으니, 나는 그런 것이라곤 도무지 믿을 수 없었다."]], [["“Pip,”", "\"핍,\""], ["said Estella, casting her glance over the room, “don’t be foolish about its effect on you.", "에스텔라가 방 안을 휘 둘러보며 말했다. \"그것이 당신에게 미치는 영향에 대해 어리석게 굴지 말아요."], ["It may have its effect on others, and may be meant to have.", "그것이 다른 이들에게는 영향을 미칠지도 모르고, 또 그러도록 의도된 것인지도 모르지요."], ["It’s not worth discussing.”", "따져 볼 가치도 없는 일이에요.\""]], [["“Yes it is,”", "\"가치가 있어요.\""], ["said I, “because I cannot bear that people should say, ‘she throws away her graces and attractions on a mere boor, the lowest in the crowd.’”", "내가 말했다. \"사람들이 '저 여자는 자신의 우아함과 매력을, 무리 가운데 가장 천한 한낱 촌뜨기에게 내던지고 있군' 하고 말하는 걸 나는 견딜 수가 없으니까요.\""]], [["“I can bear it,”", "\"나는 견딜 수 있어요.\""], ["said Estella.", "에스텔라가 말했다."]], [["“Oh!", "\"오!"], ["don’t be so proud, Estella, and so inflexible.”", "그렇게 거만하게, 그렇게 완고하게 굴지 말아요, 에스텔라.\""]], [["“Calls me proud and inflexible in this breath!”", "\"같은 입으로 나더러 거만하고 완고하다고 하다니요!\""], ["said Estella, opening her hands.", "에스텔라가 두 손을 펼치며 말했다."], ["“And in his last breath reproached me for stooping to a boor!”", "\"그리고 바로 앞서는, 내가 촌뜨기에게 몸을 굽힌다고 나무라고서는!\""]], [["“There is no doubt you do,”", "\"당신이 그러는 건 의심할 여지가 없어요.\""], ["said I, something hurriedly, “for I have seen you give him looks and smiles this very night, such as you never give to—me.”", "나는 다소 다급하게 말했다. \"바로 오늘 밤에도, 당신이 그에게, 한 번도 나에게는 주지 않은 그런 눈길과 미소를 주는 걸 내가 보았으니까요.\""]], [["“Do you want me then,”", "\"그러면 당신은,\""], ["said Estella, turning suddenly with a fixed and serious, if not angry, look, “to deceive and entrap you?”", "에스텔라가 노여운 것까지는 아니더라도 고정되고 진지한 표정으로 별안간 돌아서며 말했다. \"내가 당신을 속이고 옭아매기를 바라는 건가요?\""]], [["“Do you deceive and entrap him, Estella?”", "\"당신은 그를 속이고 옭아매나요, 에스텔라?\""]], [["“Yes, and many others,—all of them but you.", "\"그래요, 그리고 다른 많은 이들도요 — 당신만 빼고 모두를요."], ["Here is Mrs. Brandley.", "저기 브랜들리 부인이 오시네요."], ["I’ll say no more.”", "더는 말하지 않겠어요.\""]], [["And now that I have given the one chapter to the theme that so filled my heart, and so often made it ache and ache again, I pass on unhindered, to the event that had impended over me longer yet; the event that had begun to be prepared for, before I knew that the world held Estella, and in the days when her baby intelligence was receiving its first distortions from Miss Havisham’s wasting hands.", "그리하여 내 마음을 그토록 가득 채웠고, 그토록 자주 내 가슴을 아프게 하고 또 아프게 했던 그 주제에 한 장을 바쳤으니, 나는 거침없이 나아가, 그보다도 더 오래 내 위에 드리워져 있던 그 사건으로 옮겨 간다. 내가 세상에 에스텔라라는 존재가 있다는 것을 알기도 전에, 그리고 그녀의 어린 지각이 미스 해비셤의 메말라 가는 손길에서 그 첫 왜곡을 받고 있던 그 시절에 이미 준비되기 시작했던 그 사건으로."]], [["In the Eastern story, the heavy slab that was to fall on the bed of state in the flush of conquest was slowly wrought out of the quarry, the tunnel for the rope to hold it in its place was slowly carried through the leagues of rock, the slab was slowly raised and fitted in the roof, the rope was rove to it and slowly taken through the miles of hollow to the great iron ring.", "동방의 이야기 속에서, 정복의 환희에 들뜬 옥좌의 침상 위로 떨어지도록 마련된 그 무거운 석판은 채석장에서 더디게 깎여 나왔고, 그것을 제자리에 붙들어 둘 밧줄을 꿸 굴은 몇 리에 걸친 바위를 뚫고 더디게 파였으며, 석판은 더디게 들어 올려져 천장에 끼워졌고, 밧줄이 거기 꿰여 몇 마일에 이르는 빈 통로를 지나 그 커다란 쇠고리까지 더디게 이끌렸다."], ["All being made ready with much labour, and the hour come, the sultan was aroused in the dead of the night, and the sharpened axe that was to sever the rope from the great iron ring was put into his hand, and he struck with it, and the rope parted and rushed away, and the ceiling fell.", "숱한 수고로 모든 것이 채비를 갖추고 그 시각이 다다르자, 술탄은 깊은 밤중에 깨워져, 그 커다란 쇠고리에서 밧줄을 끊어 낼 잘 벼린 도끼가 그의 손에 쥐여졌고, 그가 그것으로 내리치자 밧줄이 끊어져 휙 달아나 버렸으며, 천장이 무너져 내렸다. 그러니, 내 경우도 그러했다."], ["So, in my case; all the work, near and afar, that tended to the end, had been accomplished; and in an instant the blow was struck, and the roof of my stronghold dropped upon me.", "가깝고도 먼 곳에서, 그 종말로 향해 가던 모든 작업이 다 이루어져 있었던 것이다. 그리하여 한순간에 일격이 가해졌고, 내 보루의 지붕이 내 위로 떨어져 내렸다."]], [["# Chapter XXXIX.", "# Chapter XXXIX."]], [["I was three-and-twenty years of age.", "나는 스물세 살이었다."], ["Not another word had I heard to enlighten me on the subject of my expectations, and my twenty-third birthday was a week gone.", "내 유산에 관한 문제를 밝혀 줄 한마디도 더 듣지 못한 채, 내 스물세 번째 생일이 지나간 지 일주일이 되었다."], ["We had left Barnard’s Inn more than a year, and lived in the Temple.", "우리는 바너드 여관을 떠난 지 일 년이 넘었고, 이제 템플에서 살고 있었다."], ["Our chambers were in Garden-court, down by the river.", "우리 거처는 강가 아래쪽 가든코트에 있었다."]], [["Mr. Pocket and I had for some time parted company as to our original relations, though we continued on the best terms.", "포켓 군과 나는 우리의 원래 관계에 관한 한 얼마간 손을 떼었지만, 더없이 좋은 사이는 그대로 이어 가고 있었다."], ["Notwithstanding my inability to settle to anything,—which I hope arose out of the restless and incomplete tenure on which I held my means,—I had a taste for reading, and read regularly so many hours a day.", "무엇 하나에도 진득하게 정착하지 못하는 내 무능함에도 불구하고 — 그것은 불안정하고 미완인 조건 위에 내 재력을 쥐고 있던 데서 비롯된 것이기를 바라거니와 — 나는 독서에 취미가 있어, 하루에 정해진 시간만큼 꾸준히 책을 읽었다."], ["That matter of Herbert’s was still progressing, and everything with me was as I have brought it down to the close of the last preceding chapter.", "허버트의 그 일도 여전히 진척되어 가고 있었고, 나에 관한 모든 사정은 바로 앞 장의 끝에서 내가 적어 내려온 그대로였다."]], [["Business had taken Herbert on a journey to Marseilles.", "볼일이 있어 허버트는 마르세유로 여행을 떠난 참이었다."], ["I was alone, and had a dull sense of being alone.", "나는 혼자였고, 혼자라는 것에 둔한 느낌을 받고 있었다."], ["Dispirited and anxious, long hoping that to-morrow or next week would clear my way, and long disappointed, I sadly missed the cheerful face and ready response of my friend.", "의기소침하고 불안해진 채, 내일이나 다음 주면 내 앞길이 열리리라 오래 기대했다가 오래 실망해 온 터라, 나는 친구의 쾌활한 얼굴과 시원시원한 응답이 사무치게 그리웠다."]], [["It was wretched weather; stormy and wet, stormy and wet; and mud, mud, mud, deep in all the streets.", "날씨는 고약했다. 비바람이 몰아치고 또 몰아쳤다. 그리고 진흙, 진흙, 진흙이 거리란 거리마다 깊이 깔려 있었다."], ["Day after day, a vast heavy veil had been driving over London from the East, and it drove still, as if in the East there were an eternity of cloud and wind.", "날이면 날마다 거대하고 묵직한 장막이 동쪽에서부터 런던 위로 휘몰아쳐 왔고, 마치 동쪽에 구름과 바람의 영원이 자리하고 있기라도 한 듯 여전히 휘몰아쳤다."], ["So furious had been the gusts, that high buildings in town had had the lead stripped off their roofs; and in the country, trees had been torn up, and sails of windmills carried away; and gloomy accounts had come in from the coast, of shipwreck and death.", "돌풍이 어찌나 사나웠던지, 시내의 높은 건물들은 지붕의 납 덮개가 벗겨져 나갔다. 시골에서는 나무가 뿌리째 뽑히고 풍차의 날개가 날아갔으며, 해안에서는 난파와 죽음에 관한 음울한 소식이 들려왔다."], ["Violent blasts of rain had accompanied these rages of wind, and the day just closed as I sat down to read had been the worst of all.", "거센 빗줄기가 이 바람의 광란을 동반했고, 내가 책을 읽으려 앉았을 때 막 저물어 가던 그날은 그 모든 날 가운데 가장 험악한 날이었다."]], [["Alterations have been made in that part of the Temple since that time, and it has not now so lonely a character as it had then, nor is it so exposed to the river.", "그 이후로 템플의 그 일대는 손질이 가해져, 이제는 그때만큼 외진 분위기도 아니고, 강에 그토록 노출되어 있지도 않다."], ["We lived at the top of the last house, and the wind rushing up the river shook the house that night, like discharges of cannon, or breakings of a sea.", "우리는 맨 끝 집 꼭대기에 살고 있었는데, 그날 밤 강을 거슬러 치달아 오는 바람은 대포의 발사나 바다의 부서짐처럼 집을 뒤흔들었다."], ["When the rain came with it and dashed against the windows, I thought, raising my eyes to them as they rocked, that I might have fancied myself in a storm-beaten lighthouse.", "거기에 비까지 실려 와 창문을 후려치자, 나는 흔들리는 창들로 눈을 들어 올리며, 폭풍에 두들겨 맞는 등대 안에 있는 듯한 착각마저 들 법하다고 생각했다."], ["Occasionally, the smoke came rolling down the chimney as though it could not bear to go out into such a night; and when I set the doors open and looked down the staircase, the staircase lamps were blown out; and when I shaded my face with my hands and looked through the black windows (opening them ever so little was out of the question in the teeth of such wind and rain), I saw that the lamps in the court were blown out, and that the lamps on the bridges and the shore were shuddering, and that the coal-fires in barges on the river were being carried away before the wind like red-hot splashes in the rain.", "이따금 연기는 그런 밤 속으로 빠져나가기가 견디기 힘들다는 듯이 굴뚝을 타고 도로 굴러 내려왔다. 문을 열어 계단을 내려다보니, 계단의 등불은 바람에 꺼져 있었다. 두 손으로 얼굴을 가리고 검은 창들을 통해 내다보니(그런 비바람의 기세 앞에서 창문을 손톱만큼이라도 여는 일은 어림도 없었다), 안마당의 등불도 꺼져 있었고, 다리와 강변의 등불은 부들부들 떨고 있었으며, 강 위 거룻배에 피운 석탄불은 빗속에서 벌겋게 단 물보라처럼 바람에 실려 가고 있는 것이 보였다."]], [["I read with my watch upon the table, purposing to close my book at eleven o’clock.", "나는 탁자 위에 시계를 올려놓고, 열한 시에 책을 덮으리라 작정하며 읽었다."], ["As I shut it, Saint Paul’s, and all the many church-clocks in the City—some leading, some accompanying, some following—struck that hour.", "책을 덮는 순간, 세인트폴 대성당을 비롯해 시내의 그 많은 교회 시계들이 — 어떤 것은 앞장서고, 어떤 것은 같이 울리고, 어떤 것은 뒤따르며 — 그 시각을 알렸다."], ["The sound was curiously flawed by the wind; and I was listening, and thinking how the wind assailed and tore it, when I heard a footstep on the stair.", "그 소리는 바람에 묘하게 일그러져 있었다. 나는 그 소리에 귀를 기울이며, 바람이 어떻게 그것을 공격하고 갈가리 찢어 놓는가를 생각하고 있던 차에, 계단에서 발소리를 들었다."]], [["What nervous folly made me start, and awfully connect it with the footstep of my dead sister, matters not.", "어떤 신경질적인 어리석음이 나를 흠칫 놀라게 했고, 그 소리를 죽은 누나의 발소리와 끔찍하게도 연결 짓게 했는지는 중요하지 않다."], ["It was past in a moment, and I listened again, and heard the footstep stumble in coming on.", "그것은 한순간에 지나갔고, 나는 다시 귀를 기울였으며, 발소리가 올라오다 비틀거리는 것을 들었다."], ["Remembering then, that the staircase-lights were blown out, I took up my reading-lamp and went out to the stair-head. Whoever was below had stopped on seeing my lamp, for all was quiet.", "그제야 계단 등불이 꺼졌다는 사실이 떠올라, 나는 독서용 등불을 집어 들고 계단참으로 나갔다. 아래에 있던 사람이 누구든 내 등불을 보고 멈춰 선 모양이었으니, 온통 조용했기 때문이다."]], [["“There is some one down there, is there not?”", "\"거기 아래 누가 있군요, 그렇지요?\""], ["I called out, looking down.", "나는 아래를 내려다보며 소리쳤다."]], [["“Yes,” said a voice from the darkness beneath.", "\"그렇소.\" 아래 어둠 속에서 한 목소리가 대답했다."]], [["“What floor do you want?”", "\"몇 층을 찾으십니까?\""]], [["“The top. Mr. Pip.”", "\"꼭대기요. 핍 씨.\""]], [["“That is my name.—There is nothing the matter?”", "\"그게 제 이름입니다만. 무슨 일이라도 있는 겁니까?\""]], [["“Nothing the matter,”", "\"무슨 일은 없소.\""], ["returned the voice.", "그 목소리가 대답했다."], ["And the man came on.", "그러고는 사내가 올라왔다."]], [["I stood with my lamp held out over the stair-rail, and he came slowly within its light.", "나는 등불을 계단 난간 너머로 내밀고 서 있었고, 사내는 천천히 그 불빛 안으로 들어왔다."], ["It was a shaded lamp, to shine upon a book, and its circle of light was very contracted; so that he was in it for a mere instant, and then out of it.", "그것은 책을 비추도록 갓을 씌운 등불이라 불빛의 둘레가 몹시 좁았으므로, 사내는 그저 한순간 그 안에 들어왔다가 곧 벗어났다."], ["In the instant, I had seen a face that was strange to me, looking up with an incomprehensible air of being touched and pleased by the sight of me.", "그 한순간에 나는 내게 낯선 얼굴 하나를 보았는데, 나를 보는 것만으로 마음이 움직여 흡족해진다는 듯한, 도무지 영문 모를 기색으로 나를 올려다보고 있었다."]], [["Moving the lamp as the man moved, I made out that he was substantially dressed, but roughly, like a voyager by sea.", "사내가 움직이는 대로 등불을 옮겨 가며, 나는 그가 옷차림은 그럴듯하나 거칠어서 바다를 떠도는 여행자 같다는 것을 알아보았다."], ["That he had long iron-grey hair.", "길고 희끗한 잿빛 머리칼을 가진 사내였다."], ["That his age was about sixty.", "나이는 예순쯤 되어 보였다."], ["That he was a muscular man, strong on his legs, and that he was browned and hardened by exposure to weather.", "근육질에 다리가 튼튼했고, 비바람에 노출되어 살갗이 거뭇하게 그을고 단단해진 사내였다."], ["As he ascended the last stair or two, and the light of my lamp included us both, I saw, with a stupid kind of amazement, that he was holding out both his hands to me.", "그가 마지막 한두 계단을 올라와 내 등불 빛이 우리 둘을 함께 감쌌을 때, 나는 멍청한 종류의 경악과 함께, 그가 두 손을 내게로 내밀고 있는 것을 보았다."]], [["“Pray what is your business?” I asked him.", "\"무슨 볼일이신지요?\" 나는 그에게 물었다."]], [["“My business?”", "\"내 볼일?\""], ["he repeated, pausing. “Ah! Yes. I will explain my business, by your leave.”", "그가 잠시 멈추며 되뇌었다. \"아! 그렇지. 괜찮으시다면 내 볼일을 설명하리다.\""]], [["“Do you wish to come in?”", "\"들어오시겠습니까?\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"그렇소.\""], ["he replied; “I wish to come in, master.”", "그가 대답했다. \"들어가고 싶소이다, 나리.\""]], [["I had asked him the question inhospitably enough, for I resented the sort of bright and gratified recognition that still shone in his face.", "나는 그 질문을 꽤나 박정하게 던졌으니, 그의 얼굴에 여전히 빛나고 있던, 환하고 만족스러워하는 그 알아봄의 기색이 나는 거슬렸기 때문이다."], ["I resented it, because it seemed to imply that he expected me to respond to it.", "그것이 거슬린 것은, 내가 거기에 화답하기를 그가 기대하고 있음을 암시하는 듯했기 때문이다."], ["But I took him into the room I had just left, and, having set the lamp on the table, asked him as civilly as I could to explain himself.", "그래도 나는 그를 방금 내가 떠나온 방으로 들였고, 등불을 탁자 위에 올려놓은 뒤, 되도록 정중하게 그에게 사정을 설명해 달라고 청했다."]], [["He looked about him with the strangest air,—an air of wondering pleasure, as if he had some part in the things he admired,—and he pulled off a rough outer coat, and his hat.", "사내는 더없이 묘한 기색으로 — 마치 자기가 감탄하는 그 물건들에 자기 몫이 얼마간 있기라도 하다는 듯, 의아해하면서도 흐뭇해하는 기색으로 — 주위를 둘러보더니, 거친 겉옷과 모자를 벗었다."], ["Then, I saw that his head was furrowed and bald, and that the long iron-grey hair grew only on its sides.", "그제야 나는 그의 머리가 주름지고 벗어졌으며, 길고 희끗한 잿빛 머리칼은 양옆에만 나 있다는 것을 알아보았다."], ["But, I saw nothing that in the least explained him. On the contrary, I saw him next moment, once more holding out both his hands to me.", "그러나 그를 조금이라도 설명해 줄 만한 것은 아무것도 보이지 않았다. 오히려 다음 순간 나는, 그가 또다시 두 손을 내게로 내미는 것을 보았다."]], [["“What do you mean?”", "\"무슨 뜻입니까?\""], ["said I, half suspecting him to be mad.", "나는 그가 미친 게 아닌가 반쯤 의심하며 말했다."]], [["He stopped in his looking at me, and slowly rubbed his right hand over his head.", "사내는 나를 바라보던 것을 멈추고, 오른손으로 천천히 자기 머리를 문질렀다."], ["“It’s disapinting to a man,”", "\"사람한테 실망스러운 일이지.\""], ["he said, in a coarse broken voice, “arter having looked for’ard so distant, and come so fur; but you’re not to blame for that,—neither on us is to blame for that.", "그가 거칠고 갈라진 목소리로 말했다. \"그렇게 멀리서부터 고대해 오고, 그렇게 먼 길을 왔는데 말이야. 하지만 그건 자네 탓이 아냐."], ["I’ll speak in half a minute.", "우리 둘 다 탓할 일이 아니지."], ["Give me half a minute, please.”", "반 분만 있으면 말하리다. 부디 반 분만 주시오.\""]], [["He sat down on a chair that stood before the fire, and covered his forehead with his large brown veinous hands.", "사내는 난롯불 앞에 놓인 의자에 앉아, 굵고 거뭇하고 핏줄이 불거진 두 손으로 이마를 가렸다."], ["I looked at him attentively then, and recoiled a little from him; but I did not know him.", "그때 나는 그를 유심히 바라보다가 그에게서 조금 움찔 물러났다. 그러나 나는 그가 누군지 알지 못했다."]], [["“There’s no one nigh,”", "\"근처에 아무도 없겠지.\""], ["said he, looking over his shoulder; “is there?”", "그가 어깨 너머를 돌아보며 말했다. \"없지?\""]], [["“Why do you, a stranger coming into my rooms at this time of the night, ask that question?” said I.", "\"낯선 사람이 이런 밤중에 내 방에 들어와서, 어째서 그런 걸 묻는 겁니까?\" 내가 말했다."]], [["“You’re a game one,”", "\"자넨 배짱이 있는 친구야.\""], ["he returned, shaking his head at me with a deliberate affection, at once most unintelligible and most exasperating; “I’m glad you’ve grow’d up, a game one!", "그가 신중한 애정을 담아 내게 고개를 절레절레 흔들며 대꾸했는데, 그것은 더없이 알아들을 수 없으면서도 더없이 부아를 돋우는 짓이었다. \"자네가 배짱 있는 친구로 자라나서 기쁘구먼!"], ["But don’t catch hold of me.", "하지만 날 붙잡지는 말게."], ["You’d be sorry arterwards to have done it.”", "나중에 그랬던 걸 후회하게 될 테니.\""]], [["I relinquished the intention he had detected, for I knew him!", "나는 그가 알아챈 그 마음을 거두었다. 나는 그를 알아본 것이다!"], ["Even yet I could not recall a single feature, but I knew him!", "아직도 이목구비 하나조차 떠올릴 수 없었으나, 나는 그를 알았다!"], ["If the wind and the rain had driven away the intervening years, had scattered all the intervening objects, had swept us to the churchyard where we first stood face to face on such different levels, I could not have known my convict more distinctly than I knew him now as he sat in the chair before the fire.", "만일 비바람이 그 사이의 세월을 몰아내고, 그 사이의 모든 것들을 흩뜨려 버리고, 우리가 그토록 다른 처지에서 처음 얼굴을 맞대고 섰던 그 교회 묘지로 우리를 쓸어다 놓았다 한들, 나는 지금 난롯불 앞 의자에 앉아 있는 그를 알아보는 것보다 더 또렷이 내 죄수를 알아보지는 못했을 것이다."], ["No need to take a file from his pocket and show it to me; no need to take the handkerchief from his neck and twist it round his head; no need to hug himself with both his arms, and take a shivering turn across the room, looking back at me for recognition.", "주머니에서 줄칼을 꺼내 내게 보일 필요도 없었다. 목에서 손수건을 풀어 머리에 둘러맬 필요도 없었다. 두 팔로 제 몸을 끌어안고, 알아봐 달라는 듯 나를 돌아보며 부들부들 떨면서 방을 가로질러 한 바퀴 돌 필요도 없었다."], ["I knew him before he gave me one of those aids, though, a moment before, I had not been conscious of remotely suspecting his identity.", "한순간 전만 해도 그의 정체를 어렴풋이나마 의심하고 있다는 의식조차 없었건만, 나는 그가 그런 도움 중 하나라도 베풀기 전에 이미 그를 알아보았던 것이다."]], [["He came back to where I stood, and again held out both his hands.", "사내는 내가 서 있는 데로 돌아와, 또다시 두 손을 내밀었다."], ["Not knowing what to do,—for, in my astonishment I had lost my self-possession,—I reluctantly gave him my hands.", "어찌할 바를 몰라 — 경악한 나머지 평정심을 잃어버린 터라 — 나는 마지못해 그에게 두 손을 내주었다."], ["He grasped them heartily, raised them to his lips, kissed them, and still held them.", "그는 내 손을 진심으로 움켜쥐고, 입술로 들어 올려 입을 맞춘 뒤, 여전히 놓지 않았다."]], [["“You acted noble, my boy,”", "\"자넨 고귀하게 행동했어, 얘야.\""], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."], ["“Noble, Pip!", "\"고귀했지, 핍!"], ["And I have never forgot it!”", "난 그걸 한 번도 잊은 적이 없어!\""]], [["At a change in his manner as if he were even going to embrace me, I laid a hand upon his breast and put him away.", "마치 나를 끌어안기라도 할 것처럼 그의 태도가 바뀌는 것을 보고, 나는 한 손을 그의 가슴에 대고 그를 밀쳐 냈다."]], [["“Stay!” said I.", "\"잠깐만요!\""], ["“Keep off!", "내가 말했다."], ["If you are grateful to me for what I did when I was a little child, I hope you have shown your gratitude by mending your way of life.", "\"물러서요! 내가 어린아이였을 때 한 일에 고마워한다면, 그 고마움은 당신이 삶의 방식을 고쳐 살아온 것으로 보여 줬으면 합니다."], ["If you have come here to thank me, it was not necessary.", "내게 고맙다는 말을 하러 여기 온 거라면, 그럴 필요는 없었어요."], ["Still, however you have found me out, there must be something good in the feeling that has brought you here, and I will not repulse you; but surely you must understand that—I—”", "그래도, 당신이 어떻게 나를 찾아냈든, 당신을 여기까지 이끈 그 마음에는 무언가 선한 것이 있을 테니, 나는 당신을 매정하게 내치지는 않겠습니다. 하지만 분명히 알아 두셔야 할 것이 — 나는 —\""]], [["My attention was so attracted by the singularity of his fixed look at me, that the words died away on my tongue.", "나를 향한 그의 그 고정된 시선이 어찌나 기묘했던지, 거기에 마음을 빼앗겨 말이 혀끝에서 사그라들고 말았다."]], [["“You was a-saying,”", "\"자네가 방금 한 말은,\""], ["he observed, when we had confronted one another in silence, “that surely I must understand.", "우리가 말없이 서로를 마주 보고 있던 끝에 그가 입을 열었다. \"내가 분명히 알아 둬야 할 게 있다는 거였지."], ["What, surely must I understand?”", "대체 뭘 분명히 알아 둬야 한다는 건가?\""]], [["“That I cannot wish to renew that chance intercourse with you of long ago, under these different circumstances.", "\"이렇게 처지가 달라진 마당에, 오래전 우연히 맺은 당신과의 그 인연을 다시 잇고 싶을 수는 없다는 겁니다."], ["I am glad to believe you have repented and recovered yourself. I am glad to tell you so.", "당신이 뉘우치고 자신을 추슬렀다고 믿게 되어 기쁩니다. 그렇게 말씀드릴 수 있어 기쁩니다."], ["I am glad that, thinking I deserve to be thanked, you have come to thank me.", "내가 고마워할 만한 사람이라 여기고 고마움을 전하러 와 주셔서 기쁩니다."], ["But our ways are different ways, none the less.", "하지만 그래도 우리가 가는 길은 서로 다른 길입니다."], ["You are wet, and you look weary.", "당신은 젖었고, 지쳐 보이는군요."], ["Will you drink something before you go?”", "가시기 전에 뭐라도 좀 드시겠습니까?\""]], [["He had replaced his neckerchief loosely, and had stood, keenly observant of me, biting a long end of it. “I think,”", "사내는 목수건을 다시 느슨하게 둘러맨 채, 그 기다란 끝자락을 깨물면서, 예리하게 나를 살피며 서 있었다. \"내 생각에는,\""], ["he answered, still with the end at his mouth and still observant of me, “that I will drink (I thank you) afore I go.”", "그가 여전히 그 끝자락을 입에 문 채, 여전히 나를 살피며 대답했다. \"가기 전에 한잔 마시기는, 마시겠소이다(고맙소).\""]], [["There was a tray ready on a side-table.", "곁탁자 위에 쟁반이 마련되어 있었다."], ["I brought it to the table near the fire, and asked him what he would have?", "나는 그것을 난롯가 탁자로 가져와, 그에게 무엇을 들겠느냐고 물었다."], ["He touched one of the bottles without looking at it or speaking, and I made him some hot rum and water.", "그는 쳐다보지도 말하지도 않은 채 병 하나를 손으로 짚었고, 나는 그에게 뜨거운 럼주에 물을 탄 것을 만들어 주었다."], ["I tried to keep my hand steady while I did so, but his look at me as he leaned back in his chair with the long draggled end of his neckerchief between his teeth—evidently forgotten—made my hand very difficult to master.", "그러는 동안 손을 떨지 않으려 애썼지만, 의자에 기대앉아 목수건의 길게 늘어진 끝자락을 — 그새 잊어버린 것이 분명한 그것을 — 이 사이에 물고 나를 바라보는 그 시선 탓에 손을 다스리기가 여간 어려운 게 아니었다."], ["When at last I put the glass to him, I saw with amazement that his eyes were full of tears.", "마침내 잔을 그에게 내밀었을 때, 나는 그의 두 눈에 눈물이 가득 고여 있는 것을 보고 놀랐다."]], [["Up to this time I had remained standing, not to disguise that I wished him gone.", "이때까지 나는 그가 가 주기를 바란다는 것을 굳이 감추지 않으려 선 채로 있었다."], ["But I was softened by the softened aspect of the man, and felt a touch of reproach. “I hope,”", "그러나 사내의 누그러진 모습에 나도 마음이 누그러졌고, 한 가닥 자책감을 느꼈다. \"바라건대,\""], ["said I, hurriedly putting something into a glass for myself, and drawing a chair to the table, “that you will not think I spoke harshly to you just now.", "나는 서둘러 내 잔에도 뭔가를 따르고, 의자를 탁자 쪽으로 끌어당기며 말했다. \"방금 내가 당신에게 모질게 말했다고는 생각하지 말아 주세요."], ["I had no intention of doing it, and I am sorry for it if I did. I wish you well and happy!”", "그럴 뜻은 없었고, 만일 그랬다면 미안합니다. 당신이 잘되고 행복하기를 빕니다!\""]], [["As I put my glass to my lips, he glanced with surprise at the end of his neckerchief, dropping from his mouth when he opened it, and stretched out his hand.", "내가 잔을 입술에 가져가자, 사내는 입을 열 때 입에서 떨어지는 목수건 끝자락을 놀란 듯 흘끔 보더니, 손을 내밀었다."], ["I gave him mine, and then he drank, and drew his sleeve across his eyes and forehead.", "나는 그에게 손을 내주었고, 그러자 그는 마신 뒤 소매로 눈과 이마를 훔쳤다."]], [["“How are you living?” I asked him.", "\"어떻게 지내십니까?\" 나는 그에게 물었다."]], [["“I’ve been a sheep-farmer, stock-breeder, other trades besides, away in the new world,”", "\"양 치는 농장도 하고, 가축도 길러 봤고, 그 밖에 다른 장사도 했지. 저 멀리 신세계에서 말이야.\""], ["said he; “many a thousand mile of stormy water off from this.”", "그가 말했다. \"여기서부터 사나운 바닷물로 수천 마일 떨어진 데서.\""]], [["“I hope you have done well?”", "\"잘 풀리셨기를 바랍니다.\""]], [["“I’ve done wonderfully well.", "\"놀랄 만큼 잘 풀렸지."], ["There’s others went out alonger me as has done well too, but no man has done nigh as well as me.", "나하고 같이 나간 다른 자들도 잘된 이가 있긴 하지만, 나만큼 잘된 사람은 아무도 없어."], ["I’m famous for it.”", "그걸로 내가 유명하다네.\""]], [["“I am glad to hear it.”", "\"그 말씀을 들으니 기쁩니다.\""]], [["“I hope to hear you say so, my dear boy.”", "\"자네 입에서 그 말을 듣고 싶었네, 얘야.\""]], [["Without stopping to try to understand those words or the tone in which they were spoken, I turned off to a point that had just come into my mind.", "그 말이나 그 말을 하는 어조를 굳이 멈춰 서서 헤아려 보지 않고, 나는 막 머릿속에 떠오른 한 가지로 화제를 돌렸다."]], [["“Have you ever seen a messenger you once sent to me,”", "\"전에 당신이 내게 보냈던 심부름꾼을,\" 나는 물었다."], ["I inquired, “since he undertook that trust?”", "\"그가 그 일을 맡은 뒤로 만난 적이 있습니까?\""]], [["“Never set eyes upon him.", "\"그자를 다시는 본 적 없네."], ["I warn’t likely to it.”", "그럴 일도 없었고.\""]], [["“He came faithfully, and he brought me the two one-pound notes.", "\"그는 충실하게 와서, 1파운드짜리 지폐 두 장을 내게 전해 주었습니다."], ["I was a poor boy then, as you know, and to a poor boy they were a little fortune.", "당신도 알다시피 그때 나는 가난한 아이였고, 가난한 아이에게 그건 작은 횡재였지요."], ["But, like you, I have done well since, and you must let me pay them back.", "하지만 당신처럼 나도 그 뒤로 형편이 폈으니, 그 돈은 돌려드리게 해 주세요."], ["You can put them to some other poor boy’s use.”", "다른 어느 가난한 아이를 위해 쓰시면 될 겁니다.\""], ["I took out my purse.", "나는 지갑을 꺼냈다."]], [["He watched me as I laid my purse upon the table and opened it, and he watched me as I separated two one-pound notes from its contents.", "사내는 내가 지갑을 탁자에 올려놓고 여는 것을 지켜보았고, 내가 그 속에서 1파운드짜리 지폐 두 장을 골라내는 것도 지켜보았다."], ["They were clean and new, and I spread them out and handed them over to him.", "깨끗한 새 지폐였고, 나는 그것을 펴서 그에게 건넸다."], ["Still watching me, he laid them one upon the other, folded them long-wise, gave them a twist, set fire to them at the lamp, and dropped the ashes into the tray.", "여전히 나를 지켜보던 그는, 그것을 한 장 위에 다른 한 장을 포개어 길게 접고는, 한 번 비틀어, 등불에 불을 붙여, 재를 쟁반 위에 떨어뜨렸다."]], [["“May I make so bold,”", "\"한 가지 외람되게 물어봐도 되겠나,\""], ["he said then, with a smile that was like a frown, and with a frown that was like a smile, “as ask you how you have done well, since you and me was out on them lone shivering marshes?”", "그러더니 그는, 찡그림 같은 미소와 미소 같은 찡그림을 띤 채 말했다. \"자네와 내가 그 외롭고 쓸쓸한 습지에 나가 있던 이후로, 자네가 대체 어떻게 형편이 폈는지 말일세.\" \"어떻게라니요?\""]], [["“How?” “Ah!”", "\"어떻게라니요?\" \"아!\""]], [["He emptied his glass, got up, and stood at the side of the fire, with his heavy brown hand on the mantel-shelf.", "사내는 잔을 비우고 일어나, 거뭇하고 묵직한 손을 벽난로 선반에 얹은 채 난롯가에 섰다."], ["He put a foot up to the bars, to dry and warm it, and the wet boot began to steam; but, he neither looked at it, nor at the fire, but steadily looked at me.", "한쪽 발을 말리고 데우려고 쇠살대에 올려놓자, 젖은 장화에서 김이 나기 시작했다. 그러나 그는 장화도 난롯불도 보지 않고, 줄곧 나를 바라보았다."], ["It was only now that I began to tremble.", "내가 떨기 시작한 것은 바로 그제야였다."]], [["When my lips had parted, and had shaped some words that were without sound, I forced myself to tell him (though I could not do it distinctly), that I had been chosen to succeed to some property.", "입술이 벌어져 소리 없는 몇 마디 말을 빚어냈을 때, 나는 (또렷하게는 못 했지만) 억지로 그에게 말했다. 내가 어떤 재산을 물려받을 사람으로 정해졌다고."]], [["“Might a mere warmint ask what property?”", "\"하찮은 미물이 어떤 재산인지 물어봐도 되나?\""], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."]], [["I faltered, “I don’t know.”", "나는 더듬거렸다. \"모릅니다.\""]], [["“Might a mere warmint ask whose property?”", "\"하찮은 미물이 누구 재산인지 물어봐도 되나?\""], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."]], [["I faltered again, “I don’t know.”", "나는 다시 더듬거렸다. \"모릅니다.\""]], [["“Could I make a guess, I wonder,”", "\"내가 한번 알아맞혀 볼까,\""], ["said the Convict, “at your income since you come of age!", "죄수가 말했다. \"자네가 성년이 된 뒤 받은 수입을 말이야!"], ["As to the first figure now. Five?”", "자, 우선 첫 숫자라고 치고. 5인가?\""]], [["With my heart beating like a heavy hammer of disordered action, I rose out of my chair, and stood with my hand upon the back of it, looking wildly at him.", "심장이 마구 어지러이 뛰는 묵직한 망치처럼 두근거리는 가운데, 나는 의자에서 일어나, 등받이에 한 손을 얹은 채, 그를 멍하니 바라보며 섰다."]], [["“Concerning a guardian,”", "\"후견인에 관해서 말인데,\""], ["he went on. “There ought to have been some guardian, or such-like, whiles you was a minor.", "그가 말을 이었다. \"자네가 미성년이던 동안엔 후견인 같은 게, 뭐 그런 누가 있었을 게 분명해."], ["Some lawyer, maybe.", "변호사일지도 모르지."], ["As to the first letter of that lawyer’s name now.", "자, 그 변호사 이름 첫 글자라고 치고."], ["Would it be J?”", "그게 혹시 J인가?\""]], [["All the truth of my position came flashing on me; and its disappointments, dangers, disgraces, consequences of all kinds, rushed in in such a multitude that I was borne down by them and had to struggle for every breath I drew.", "내 처지의 진실 전부가 번개처럼 내게 들이쳤다. 그리고 그 모든 종류의 실망, 위험, 치욕, 결과들이 어찌나 떼 지어 밀려들었던지, 나는 그 무게에 짓눌려 숨 한 번 쉴 때마다 안간힘을 써야 했다."]], [["“Put it,”", "\"이렇게 쳐 보세,\""], ["he resumed, “as the employer of that lawyer whose name begun with a J, and might be Jaggers,—put it as he had come over sea to Portsmouth, and had landed there, and had wanted to come on to you.", "그가 다시 말을 이었다. \"이름이 J로 시작하고, 어쩌면 재거스일지도 모를, 그 변호사를 부리는 고용주가 말이야 — 그자가 바다를 건너 포츠머스로 와서, 거기 상륙해서, 자네한테로 오고 싶어 했다고 쳐 보세."], ["‘However, you have found me out,’ you says just now. Well!", "'당신이 어떻게 나를 찾아냈든' 하고 자네가 방금 말했지. 그런데 말이야!"], ["However, did I find you out?", "내가 어떻게 자네를 찾아냈을까?"], ["Why, I wrote from Portsmouth to a person in London, for particulars of your address.", "글쎄, 나는 포츠머스에서 런던에 있는 어떤 사람한테 편지를 써서 자네 주소를 자세히 알아냈다네."], ["That person’s name?", "그 사람 이름이 뭐냐고?"], ["Why, Wemmick.”", "글쎄, 웨믹이지.\""]], [["I could not have spoken one word, though it had been to save my life.", "목숨을 구하기 위해서라 해도 나는 한마디도 할 수 없었을 것이다."], ["I stood, with a hand on the chair-back and a hand on my breast, where I seemed to be suffocating,—I stood so, looking wildly at him, until I grasped at the chair, when the room began to surge and turn.", "나는 한 손을 의자 등받이에, 다른 한 손을 숨이 막히는 듯한 가슴에 얹고 서 있었다. 그렇게 서서, 의자를 와락 붙들 때까지 그를 멍하니 바라보았고, 그러자 방이 출렁이며 빙빙 돌기 시작했다."], ["He caught me, drew me to the sofa, put me up against the cushions, and bent on one knee before me, bringing the face that I now well remembered, and that I shuddered at, very near to mine.", "사내는 나를 붙잡아 소파로 끌어다, 쿠션에 기대 앉히고는, 내 앞에 한쪽 무릎을 꿇고서, 이제 똑똑히 기억나는, 그래서 내가 몸서리치게 되는 그 얼굴을 내 얼굴 아주 가까이 들이댔다."]], [["“Yes, Pip, dear boy, I’ve made a gentleman on you!", "\"그래, 핍, 얘야, 내가 자넬 신사로 만들었어!"], ["It’s me wot has done it!", "그걸 해낸 게 바로 날세!"], ["I swore that time, sure as ever I earned a guinea, that guinea should go to you.", "그때 나는 맹세했지, 내가 1기니를 벌어들이는 것만큼이나 틀림없이, 그 1기니는 자네한테 가게 하겠다고."], ["I swore arterwards, sure as ever I spec’lated and got rich, you should get rich.", "그 뒤에도 맹세했네, 내가 투기를 해서 부자가 되는 것만큼이나 틀림없이, 자네도 부자가 되게 하겠다고."], ["I lived rough, that you should live smooth; I worked hard, that you should be above work.", "나는 거칠게 살았어, 자네가 편하게 살게 하려고. 나는 고되게 일했지, 자네가 일 따위 안 해도 되게 하려고."], ["What odds, dear boy?", "그게 무슨 대수겠나, 얘야?"], ["Do I tell it, fur you to feel a obligation? Not a bit.", "내가 이걸 자네한테 빚진 마음을 느끼게 하려고 말하는 줄 아나? 천만에."], ["I tell it, fur you to know as that there hunted dunghill dog wot you kep life in, got his head so high that he could make a gentleman,—and, Pip, you’re him!”", "내가 이걸 말하는 건, 자네가 목숨을 부지시켜 준 그 쫓기던 똥개가, 신사 하나 만들 수 있을 만큼 고개를 높이 쳐들었다는 걸 알라는 거야 — 그리고, 핍, 그 신사가 바로 자넬세!\""]], [["The abhorrence in which I held the man, the dread I had of him, the repugnance with which I shrank from him, could not have been exceeded if he had been some terrible beast.", "내가 그 사내에게 품은 혐오, 그에게 느낀 두려움, 그에게서 움찔 물러나게 하던 거부감은, 설령 그가 어떤 무시무시한 짐승이었다 한들 그보다 더하지는 못했을 것이다."]], [["“Look’ee here, Pip.", "\"이걸 보게, 핍."], ["I’m your second father.", "나는 자네의 두 번째 아비일세."], ["You’re my son,—more to me nor any son. I’ve put away money, only for you to spend.", "자네는 내 아들이야 — 그 어떤 아들보다도 내게 더한 아들이지. 나는 돈을 모아 뒀어, 오직 자네가 쓰라고."], ["When I was a hired-out shepherd in a solitary hut, not seeing no faces but faces of sheep till I half forgot wot men’s and women’s faces wos like, I see yourn.", "외딴 오두막에서 품팔이 양치기 노릇을 할 때, 양들 얼굴 말고는 사람 얼굴이라곤 보질 못해서 남자고 여자고 얼굴이 어떻게 생겼는지 반쯤 잊어버릴 지경이었을 때도, 나는 자네 얼굴을 봤네."], ["I drops my knife many a time in that hut when I was a-eating my dinner or my supper, and I says, ‘Here’s the boy again, a looking at me whiles I eats and drinks!’ I see you there a many times, as plain as ever I see you on them misty marshes.", "그 오두막에서 점심이나 저녁을 먹다가 나는 몇 번이고 칼을 떨어뜨리며 말했지. '여기 또 그 애가 있구나, 내가 먹고 마시는 걸 지켜보면서!' 안개 낀 그 습지에서 자넬 보던 것만큼이나 또렷하게, 나는 거기서 자네를 몇 번이고 봤네."], ["‘Lord strike me dead!’ I says each time,—and I goes out in the air to say it under the open heavens,—‘but wot, if I gets liberty and money, I’ll make that boy a gentleman!’ And I done it.", "'하느님, 차라리 날 쳐 죽이시오!' 하고 매번 나는 말했어 — 그리고 그 말을 탁 트인 하늘 아래서 하려고 바깥바람 속으로 나가곤 했지 — '하지만 내가 자유와 돈을 얻게 되면, 저 애를 신사로 만들어 주고 말겠소!'"], ["Why, look at you, dear boy!", "그리고 나는 그걸 해냈어."], ["Look at these here lodgings of yourn, fit for a lord! A lord? Ah!", "자, 자네 좀 보게, 얘야! 영주에게나 어울릴 자네의 이 거처를 좀 보게! 영주라고? 아!"], ["You shall show money with lords for wagers, and beat ’em!”", "자넨 영주들과 돈을 걸고 겨뤄서 이길 수도 있을 게야!\""]], [["In his heat and triumph, and in his knowledge that I had been nearly fainting, he did not remark on my reception of all this.", "자기 열기와 승리감에 휩싸인 데다, 내가 거의 기절할 뻔했다는 것을 안 까닭에, 그는 이 모든 말을 받아들이는 내 태도에 대해서는 토를 달지 않았다."], ["It was the one grain of relief I had.", "그것이 내게는 한 톨의 위안이었다."]], [["“Look’ee here!”", "\"이걸 보게!\""], ["he went on, taking my watch out of my pocket, and turning towards him a ring on my finger, while I recoiled from his touch as if he had been a snake, “a gold ’un and a beauty: that’s a gentleman’s, I hope!", "그가 말을 이으며, 내 주머니에서 시계를 꺼내고, 내 손가락의 반지를 자기 쪽으로 돌려 보았는데, 그동안 나는 마치 그가 뱀이기라도 한 듯 그의 손길에서 움찔 물러났다. \"금붙이에다 일품이로구먼. 이게 바로 신사의 것이렷다!"], ["A diamond all set round with rubies; that’s a gentleman’s, I hope!", "둘레를 온통 루비로 박은 다이아몬드라. 이게 바로 신사의 것이렷다!"], ["Look at your linen; fine and beautiful!", "자네 속옷을 보게. 곱고 아름답지! 자네 옷을 보게."], ["Look at your clothes; better ain’t to be got!", "이보다 더 좋은 건 구할 수도 없을 게야!"], ["And your books too,”", "그리고 자네 책들도,\""], ["turning his eyes round the room, “mounting up, on their shelves, by hundreds!", "그가 방 안을 빙 둘러보며 말했다. \"선반에 수백 권씩 쌓여 올라가는구먼!"], ["And you read ’em; don’t you?", "그리고 자넨 그걸 읽지, 안 그런가?"], ["I see you’d been a reading of ’em when I come in.", "내가 들어왔을 때 자네가 그걸 읽고 있던 걸 봤네."], ["Ha, ha, ha!", "하, 하, 하!"], ["You shall read ’em to me, dear boy!", "자네가 그걸 내게 읽어 주게, 얘야!"], ["And if they’re in foreign languages wot I don’t understand, I shall be just as proud as if I did.”", "그리고 그게 내가 알지 못하는 외국 말로 쓰인 거라 해도, 나는 알아듣는 것만큼이나 자랑스러울 게야.\""]], [["Again he took both my hands and put them to his lips, while my blood ran cold within me.", "또다시 그는 내 두 손을 잡아 입술에 갖다 댔고, 그동안 내 핏줄 속 피는 싸늘하게 식어 갔다."]], [["“Don’t you mind talking, Pip,”", "\"말하려고 애쓰지 말게, 핍.\""], ["said he, after again drawing his sleeve over his eyes and forehead, as the click came in his throat which I well remembered,—and he was all the more horrible to me that he was so much in earnest; “you can’t do better nor keep quiet, dear boy.", "그는 다시 소매로 눈과 이마를 훔치고 나서, 내가 똑똑히 기억하는 그 딸깍하는 소리가 목구멍에서 울리는 가운데 말했다 — 그가 그토록 진심이라는 점이 내게는 그를 한층 더 끔찍하게 만들었다. \"가만히 있는 것보다 더 나은 건 없어, 얘야."], ["You ain’t looked slowly forward to this as I have; you wosn’t prepared for this as I wos.", "자네는 나처럼 이걸 천천히 고대해 온 게 아니지. 나처럼 이걸 마음의 준비를 해 둔 게 아니야."], ["But didn’t you never think it might be me?”", "하지만 그게 나일지도 모른다고는 한 번도 생각 못 했나?\""]], [["“O no, no, no,”", "\"아, 아니요, 아니요, 아니요.\""], ["I returned, “Never, never!”", "나는 대답했다. \"단 한 번도, 단 한 번도요!\""]], [["“Well, you see it wos me, and single-handed.", "\"음, 보다시피 그게 나였어, 나 혼자서 한 일이지."], ["Never a soul in it but my own self and Mr. Jaggers.”", "나 자신하고 재거스 씨 말고는 거기 낀 사람이 아무도 없었네.\""]], [["“Was there no one else?”", "\"다른 사람은 아무도 없었습니까?\""], ["I asked.", "내가 물었다."]], [["“No,”", "\"없네.\""], ["said he, with a glance of surprise: “who else should there be?", "그가 놀란 눈빛으로 말했다. \"달리 누가 또 있겠나?"], ["And, dear boy, how good looking you have growed!", "그런데, 얘야, 자네 참 잘생기게 자랐구먼!"], ["There’s bright eyes somewheres—eh?", "어디 빛나는 두 눈이 있겠지 — 응?"], ["Isn’t there bright eyes somewheres, wot you love the thoughts on?”", "어디 자네가 떠올리기를 사랑하는, 그런 빛나는 두 눈이 있지 않나?\""]], [["O Estella, Estella!", "오, 에스텔라, 에스텔라!"]], [["“They shall be yourn, dear boy, if money can buy ’em.", "\"그 두 눈도 자네 것이 될 걸세, 얘야, 돈으로 살 수만 있다면 말이야."], ["Not that a gentleman like you, so well set up as you, can’t win ’em off of his own game; but money shall back you!", "자네같이 그렇게 잘 차려입은 신사가 제 솜씨로 그 눈을 손에 넣지 못하리라는 게 아니라, 그래도 돈이 자넬 뒷받침해 줄 거란 거지!"], ["Let me finish wot I was a telling you, dear boy.", "내가 자네한테 들려주던 얘기를 마저 끝내게 해 주게, 얘야."], ["From that there hut and that there hiring-out, I got money left me by my master (which died, and had been the same as me), and got my liberty and went for myself.", "그 오두막과 그 품팔이살이에서, 나는 주인이 — 그 사람도 죽었는데, 나하고 똑같은 처지였지 — 남겨 준 돈을 받았고, 자유의 몸이 되어 내 사업을 시작했네."], ["In every single thing I went for, I went for you.", "내가 손댄 모든 일을, 단 하나하나, 다 자네를 위해서 했어."], ["‘Lord strike a blight upon it,’ I says, wotever it was I went for, ‘if it ain’t for him!’ It all prospered wonderful.", "'하느님, 이 일에 천벌을 내리시오.' 하고 나는 내가 손대는 게 뭐든 간에 그렇게 말했지, '이게 저 애를 위한 게 아니라면!'"], ["As I giv’ you to understand just now, I’m famous for it.", "모든 게 놀랄 만큼 잘 풀렸네. 방금 자네한테 알려 줬다시피, 그걸로 내가 유명하지."], ["It was the money left me, and the gains of the first few year wot I sent home to Mr. Jaggers—all for you—when he first come arter you, agreeable to my letter.”", "내가 처음 몇 해 동안 자네를 위해 — 죄다 자네를 위해 — 재거스 씨한테 부쳐 보낸 게 바로 그 물려받은 돈과 그 벌어들인 이문이었어, 그가 내 편지에 따라 자네를 처음 찾아갔을 때 말일세.\""]], [["O that he had never come!", "오, 그가 차라리 오지 않았더라면!"], ["That he had left me at the forge,—far from contented, yet, by comparison happy!", "그가 나를 그 대장간에 — 결코 만족스럽지는 않았으나, 그래도 비교하자면 행복했던 그곳에 — 그냥 내버려 두었더라면!"]], [["“And then, dear boy, it was a recompense to me, look’ee here, to know in secret that I was making a gentleman.", "\"그러고 나서, 얘야, 내겐 보답이었네, 이걸 보게, 내가 신사 하나를 만들어 내고 있다는 걸 남몰래 안다는 것이 말이야."], ["The blood horses of them colonists might fling up the dust over me as I was walking; what do I say?", "내가 길을 걸을 때 그 식민지 사람들의 명마들이 먼지를 일으켜 나한테 끼얹어도, 내가 뭐라고 했겠나? 나는 속으로 말했지."], ["I says to myself, ‘I’m making a better gentleman nor ever you’ll be!’ When one of ’em says to another, ‘He was a convict, a few year ago, and is a ignorant common fellow now, for all he’s lucky,’ what do I say?", "'나는 자네들이 앞으로 될 그 어떤 신사보다도 더 나은 신사를 만들고 있어!' 그자들 중 하나가 다른 자한테 '저 사람은 몇 해 전엔 죄수였고, 운은 좀 텄어도 지금도 무식한 막돼먹은 놈이야.' 하고 말할 때, 내가 뭐라고 했겠나?"], ["I says to myself, ‘If I ain’t a gentleman, nor yet ain’t got no learning, I’m the owner of such.", "나는 속으로 말했어. '내가 신사가 아니라 한들, 또 배움이 없다 한들, 나는 그런 걸 가진 사람의 주인일세."], ["All on you owns stock and land; which on you owns a brought-up London gentleman?’ This way I kep myself a-going.", "자네들은 죄다 가축이며 땅을 가졌지. 그중에 누가 런던에서 길러 낸 신사를 가졌나?' 이렇게 나는 나 자신을 버티게 했네."], ["And this way I held steady afore my mind that I would for certain come one day and see my boy, and make myself known to him, on his own ground.”", "그리고 이렇게 나는 마음속에 굳게 다잡았지, 언젠가 반드시 와서 내 아이를 보고, 그 아이가 사는 그곳에서 나를 그 아이에게 알리겠다고 말이야.\""]], [["He laid his hand on my shoulder.", "그가 내 어깨에 손을 얹었다."], ["I shuddered at the thought that for anything I knew, his hand might be stained with blood.", "나는 내가 알지 못하는 어떤 사정으로든 그 손이 피로 물들어 있을지도 모른다는 생각에 몸서리쳤다."]], [["“It warn’t easy, Pip, for me to leave them parts, nor yet it warn’t safe.", "\"내가 그 고장을 떠나기란 쉽지 않았네, 핍, 그렇다고 안전한 일도 아니었지."], ["But I held to it, and the harder it was, the stronger I held, for I was determined, and my mind firm made up.", "하지만 나는 그 결심을 고수했고, 그게 어려우면 어려울수록 더 단단히 붙들었어. 나는 작정했고, 마음을 굳게 정했으니까."], ["At last I done it.", "마침내 나는 해냈네."], ["Dear boy, I done it!”", "얘야, 내가 해냈어!\""]], [["I tried to collect my thoughts, but I was stunned.", "나는 생각을 추스르려 애썼지만, 멍해 있었다."], ["Throughout, I had seemed to myself to attend more to the wind and the rain than to him; even now, I could not separate his voice from those voices, though those were loud and his was silent.", "처음부터 줄곧, 나는 그보다는 비바람에 더 귀를 기울이고 있는 것만 같았다. 지금조차도, 그 목소리들은 요란하고 그의 목소리는 잠잠했건만, 나는 그의 목소리를 그 목소리들에서 떼어 낼 수가 없었다."]], [["“Where will you put me?”", "\"날 어디 재워 줄 텐가?\""], ["he asked, presently.", "이윽고 그가 물었다."], ["“I must be put somewheres, dear boy.”", "\"어디든 날 재워 줘야지, 얘야.\""]], [["“To sleep?”", "\"주무시게요?\""], ["said I.", "내가 말했다."]], [["“Yes.", "\"그래."], ["And to sleep long and sound,”", "길게 푹 자야지.\" 그가 대답했다."], ["he answered; “for I’ve been sea-tossed and sea-washed, months and months.”", "\"여러 달, 여러 달을 바다에서 시달리고 바닷물에 씻겼으니 말일세.\""]], [["“My friend and companion,”", "\"제 친구이자 동거인은,\""], ["said I, rising from the sofa, “is absent; you must have his room.”", "나는 소파에서 일어나며 말했다. \"지금 자리에 없습니다. 그 사람 방을 쓰시면 됩니다.\""]], [["“He won’t come back to-morrow; will he?”", "\"그 사람, 내일은 안 돌아오겠지?\""]], [["“No,”", "\"네.\""], ["said I, answering almost mechanically, in spite of my utmost efforts; “not to-morrow.”", "나는 안간힘을 다했음에도, 거의 기계적으로 대답했다. \"내일은요.\""]], [["“Because, look’ee here, dear boy,”", "\"왜냐하면, 이걸 보게, 얘야,\""], ["he said, dropping his voice, and laying a long finger on my breast in an impressive manner, “caution is necessary.”", "그가 목소리를 낮추고, 긴 손가락을 인상적으로 내 가슴에 갖다 대며 말했다. \"조심이 필요하거든.\""]], [["“How do you mean? Caution?”", "\"무슨 뜻이야? 조심하라고?\""]], [["“By G——, it’s Death!” “What’s death?”", "\"맙소사, 죽음이잖아!\" \"무엇이 죽음이라는 겁니까?\""]], [["“I was sent for life.", "\"나는 종신형으로 보내졌어."], ["It’s death to come back.", "돌아오는 건 곧 죽음이지."], ["There’s been overmuch coming back of late years, and I should of a certainty be hanged if took.”", "요 몇 해 사이에 돌아오는 일이 너무 많았던 통에, 만약 잡히면 나는 영락없이 교수형이야.\""]], [["Nothing was needed but this; the wretched man, after loading wretched me with his gold and silver chains for years, had risked his life to come to me, and I held it there in my keeping!", "이것 말고는 더 필요한 게 없었다. 그 가엾은 사내는, 여러 해 동안 가엾은 나에게 황금과 은의 사슬을 짊어지운 끝에, 목숨을 걸고 내게로 왔고, 나는 그 목숨을 내 손안에 쥐고 있었던 것이다!"], ["If I had loved him instead of abhorring him; if I had been attracted to him by the strongest admiration and affection, instead of shrinking from him with the strongest repugnance; it could have been no worse.", "만일 내가 그를 혐오하는 대신 사랑했더라면, 만일 더없이 강한 거부감으로 그에게서 움찔 물러나는 대신 더없이 강한 흠모와 애정으로 그에게 끌렸더라면, 사정이 이보다 더 나빠질 수는 없었을 것이다."], ["On the contrary, it would have been better, for his preservation would then have naturally and tenderly addressed my heart.", "오히려 그편이 나았을 테니, 그렇다면 그를 지켜 내는 일이 자연스럽고도 다정하게 내 마음에 호소했을 것이기 때문이다."]], [["My first care was to close the shutters, so that no light might be seen from without, and then to close and make fast the doors.", "내가 가장 먼저 마음 쓴 일은, 밖에서 불빛이 새어 나가지 않도록 덧문을 닫는 것이었고, 그다음으로는 문들을 닫아 단단히 잠그는 것이었다."], ["While I did so, he stood at the table drinking rum and eating biscuit; and when I saw him thus engaged, I saw my convict on the marshes at his meal again.", "그러는 동안 그는 탁자 앞에 서서 럼주를 마시고 비스킷을 먹고 있었다. 그가 그러고 있는 모습을 보자, 나는 습지에서 끼니를 먹던 내 죄수를 다시 보는 듯했다."], ["It almost seemed to me as if he must stoop down presently, to file at his leg.", "그가 이내 몸을 숙여 제 다리에 채운 쇠고랑을 줄칼로 갈기 시작하지나 않을까 싶을 지경이었다."]], [["When I had gone into Herbert’s room, and had shut off any other communication between it and the staircase than through the room in which our conversation had been held, I asked him if he would go to bed?", "내가 허버트의 방으로 들어가, 그 방과 계단 사이에 우리가 이야기를 나눈 방을 거치는 것 말고는 다른 통로를 죄다 막아 버린 뒤, 나는 그에게 잠자리에 들겠느냐고 물었다."], ["He said yes, but asked me for some of my “gentleman’s linen” to put on in the morning.", "그는 그러겠다고 했지만, 아침에 입을 내 \"신사용 속옷\"을 좀 달라고 청했다."], ["I brought it out, and laid it ready for him, and my blood again ran cold when he again took me by both hands to give me good-night.", "나는 그것을 꺼내 그가 쓰도록 챙겨 두었고, 그가 잘 자라는 인사를 하려고 또다시 내 두 손을 맞잡았을 때, 내 핏줄 속 피는 다시금 싸늘하게 식었다."]], [["I got away from him, without knowing how I did it, and mended the fire in the room where we had been together, and sat down by it, afraid to go to bed.", "나는 어떻게 그렇게 했는지도 모르는 채 그에게서 빠져나와, 우리가 함께 있던 방의 난롯불을 돋우고는, 잠자리에 들기가 두려워 그 곁에 앉았다."], ["For an hour or more, I remained too stunned to think; and it was not until I began to think, that I began fully to know how wrecked I was, and how the ship in which I had sailed was gone to pieces.", "한 시간 남짓 동안, 나는 너무 얼이 빠져 아무 생각도 할 수 없었다. 그리고 비로소 생각을 하기 시작하고 나서야, 내가 얼마나 파선했는지를, 내가 타고 항해하던 배가 얼마나 산산조각 났는지를 온전히 알게 되었다."]], [["Miss Havisham’s intentions towards me, all a mere dream; Estella not designed for me; I only suffered in Satis House as a convenience, a sting for the greedy relations, a model with a mechanical heart to practise on when no other practice was at hand; those were the first smarts I had.", "나를 향한 해비셤 양의 의도는 죄다 한낱 꿈에 지나지 않았고, 에스텔라는 나를 위해 점지된 사람이 아니었으며, 나는 그저 하나의 편의로서 새티스 하우스에서 시달림을 당했던 것이다. 탐욕스러운 친척들을 찌르는 가시로서, 그리고 달리 연습할 상대가 없을 때 그 기계 같은 심장을 상대로 연습할 본보기로서 말이다. 그것이 내가 맨 먼저 느낀 쓰라림이었다."], ["But, sharpest and deepest pain of all,—it was for the convict, guilty of I knew not what crimes, and liable to be taken out of those rooms where I sat thinking, and hanged at the Old Bailey door, that I had deserted Joe.", "그러나 그 모든 것 중에서도 가장 날카롭고 깊은 고통은, 내가 알지 못하는 죄를 저질렀고 내가 앉아 생각에 잠겨 있는 이 방에서 끌려 나가 올드베일리 문간에서 교수형을 당할지도 모를 그 죄수를 위하여, 내가 조를 저버렸다는 것이었다."]], [["I would not have gone back to Joe now, I would not have gone back to Biddy now, for any consideration; simply, I suppose, because my sense of my own worthless conduct to them was greater than every consideration.", "지금은 그 무엇을 고려한다 해도 조에게로 돌아가지 않을 것이며, 비디에게로도 돌아가지 않을 것이었다. 짐작건대 그것은 단지, 그들에게 내가 취한 그 보잘것없는 처신에 대한 자각이 그 어떤 고려보다도 컸기 때문이리라."], ["No wisdom on earth could have given me the comfort that I should have derived from their simplicity and fidelity; but I could never, never, undo what I had done.", "그들의 꾸밈없음과 한결같음에서 내가 얻었을 위안을, 세상의 그 어떤 지혜로도 내게 줄 수는 없었을 것이다. 그러나 나는 내가 저지른 일을 결코, 결코 돌이킬 수 없었다."]], [["In every rage of wind and rush of rain, I heard pursuers.", "바람이 미쳐 날뛰고 비가 쏟아질 때마다, 나는 추격자들의 소리를 들었다."], ["Twice, I could have sworn there was a knocking and whispering at the outer door.", "두 번이나, 나는 바깥문을 두드리고 속닥거리는 소리가 있었다고 맹세할 수도 있었다."], ["With these fears upon me, I began either to imagine or recall that I had had mysterious warnings of this man’s approach.", "이런 두려움에 사로잡힌 채, 나는 이 사내가 다가오고 있다는 신비한 경고를 받았었다고 상상하거나 떠올리기 시작했다."], ["That, for weeks gone by, I had passed faces in the streets which I had thought like his.", "지난 몇 주 동안 거리에서 그와 닮았다 싶은 얼굴들을 스쳐 지나왔다고."], ["That these likenesses had grown more numerous, as he, coming over the sea, had drawn nearer.", "그리고 그가 바다를 건너오며 점점 가까워짐에 따라, 그 닮은 얼굴들이 점점 더 많아졌다고."], ["That his wicked spirit had somehow sent these messengers to mine, and that now on this stormy night he was as good as his word, and with me.", "그의 사악한 영혼이 어떻게든 이 전령들을 내 영혼에게로 보냈고, 이제 이 폭풍우 치는 밤에 그가 그 약속대로 나와 함께 있게 되었다고 말이다."]], [["Crowding up with these reflections came the reflection that I had seen him with my childish eyes to be a desperately violent man; that I had heard that other convict reiterate that he had tried to murder him; that I had seen him down in the ditch tearing and fighting like a wild beast.", "이런 상념들과 함께 떼 지어 밀려든 것은, 내가 어린아이의 눈으로 그를 무섭도록 난폭한 사내로 보았다는 상념이었다. 그 다른 죄수가 그가 자기를 죽이려 했다고 거듭 말하는 것을 들었다는 것, 그리고 그가 도랑에 처박혀 야수처럼 물어뜯고 싸우는 것을 보았다는 것이었다."], ["Out of such remembrances I brought into the light of the fire a half-formed terror that it might not be safe to be shut up there with him in the dead of the wild solitary night.", "그런 기억들로부터 나는, 이 거칠고 외로운 밤이 깊었을 때 그와 함께 여기 갇혀 있는 것이 안전하지 않을지도 모른다는 어렴풋한 공포를 난롯불 빛 속으로 끄집어냈다."], ["This dilated until it filled the room, and impelled me to take a candle and go in and look at my dreadful burden.", "이 공포는 점점 부풀어 방 안을 가득 채웠고, 마침내 나는 촛불을 들고 들어가 내 끔찍한 짐을 들여다보지 않을 수 없게 되었다."]], [["He had rolled a handkerchief round his head, and his face was set and lowering in his sleep.", "그는 손수건을 머리에 둘둘 둘렀고, 잠든 그의 얼굴은 굳어 있으며 험상궂게 찌푸려져 있었다."], ["But he was asleep, and quietly too, though he had a pistol lying on the pillow.", "그러나 그는 잠들어 있었고, 베개 위에 권총을 한 자루 올려놓고 있긴 했어도, 조용히 잠들어 있었다."], ["Assured of this, I softly removed the key to the outside of his door, and turned it on him before I again sat down by the fire.", "그것을 확인한 나는, 그의 방문 열쇠를 가만히 바깥쪽으로 옮겨, 그를 가둔 채 잠그고 나서야 다시 난롯가에 앉았다."], ["Gradually I slipped from the chair and lay on the floor.", "차츰 나는 의자에서 미끄러져 내려와 바닥에 누웠다."], ["When I awoke without having parted in my sleep with the perception of my wretchedness, the clocks of the Eastward churches were striking five, the candles were wasted out, the fire was dead, and the wind and rain intensified the thick black darkness.", "잠을 자면서도 내 비참함에 대한 자각을 놓아 버리지 못한 채 깨어났을 때, 동쪽 교회들의 시계는 다섯 시를 치고 있었고, 촛불은 다 타 버렸으며, 난롯불은 꺼졌고, 비바람은 짙은 검은 어둠을 한층 더 깊게 했다. 이로써 핍의 유산 이야기 제2부가 끝난다."]], [["# Chapter XL.", "# Chapter XL."]], [["It was fortunate for me that I had to take precautions to ensure (so far as I could) the safety of my dreaded visitor; for, this thought pressing on me when I awoke, held other thoughts in a confused concourse at a distance.", "무서운 방문객의 안전을(내가 할 수 있는 한) 확실히 지키기 위해 미리 손을 써 두어야 했던 것은, 내게는 다행스러운 일이었다. 잠에서 깨었을 때 그 생각이 나를 짓눌렀으므로, 다른 온갖 생각들은 어수선한 무리를 이룬 채 멀찍이 물러나 있었기 때문이다."]], [["The impossibility of keeping him concealed in the chambers was self-evident.", "그를 거처에 숨겨 둔다는 것이 불가능하다는 사실은 자명했다."], ["It could not be done, and the attempt to do it would inevitably engender suspicion.", "그건 될 일이 아니었고, 그렇게 하려 시도하다가는 필연코 의심을 사게 마련이었다."], ["True, I had no Avenger in my service now, but I was looked after by an inflammatory old female, assisted by an animated rag-bag whom she called her niece, and to keep a room secret from them would be to invite curiosity and exaggeration.", "사실 지금 내게는 어벤저라는 하인이 없었지만, 나는 걸핏하면 흥분하는 늙은 여자에게 시중을 받고 있었고, 그 여자는 자기 조카라고 부르는 살아 움직이는 넝마 자루 같은 여자의 도움을 받았다. 그들에게 방 하나를 비밀로 한다는 것은 곧 호기심과 과장을 불러들이는 짓이었다."], ["They both had weak eyes, which I had long attributed to their chronically looking in at keyholes, and they were always at hand when not wanted; indeed that was their only reliable quality besides larceny.", "두 사람 다 눈이 침침했는데, 나는 오래전부터 그것이 노상 열쇠 구멍을 들여다보는 버릇 탓이라고 여겨 왔다. 또 그들은 필요 없을 때면 어김없이 곁에 있었으니, 사실 그것이야말로 도둑질을 빼면 그들에게서 믿을 수 있는 유일한 자질이었다."], ["Not to get up a mystery with these people, I resolved to announce in the morning that my uncle had unexpectedly come from the country.", "이런 사람들과 수수께끼를 꾸미는 일을 만들지 않으려고, 나는 아침에 시골에서 삼촌이 뜻밖에 찾아왔노라고 알리기로 마음먹었다."]], [["This course I decided on while I was yet groping about in the darkness for the means of getting a light.", "이 방침을 정한 것은, 어둠 속에서 불을 밝힐 방도를 더듬어 찾고 있을 때였다."], ["Not stumbling on the means after all, I was fain to go out to the adjacent Lodge and get the watchman there to come with his lantern.", "끝내 그 방도를 찾아내지 못한 나는, 부득이 가까운 수위실로 나가 그곳 야경꾼에게 등불을 들고 와 달라고 청할 수밖에 없었다."], ["Now, in groping my way down the black staircase I fell over something, and that something was a man crouching in a corner.", "그런데 캄캄한 계단을 더듬어 내려가다가 나는 무언가에 걸려 넘어졌고, 그 무언가는 한쪽 구석에 웅크리고 있던 사내였다."]], [["As the man made no answer when I asked him what he did there, but eluded my touch in silence, I ran to the Lodge and urged the watchman to come quickly; telling him of the incident on the way back.", "그곳에서 뭘 하느냐고 물었으나 사내는 아무 대답도 하지 않고 말없이 내 손길을 피하기에, 나는 수위실로 달려가 야경꾼에게 어서 와 달라고 재촉했다. 돌아오는 길에 나는 그에게 그 일을 이야기해 주었다."], ["The wind being as fierce as ever, we did not care to endanger the light in the lantern by rekindling the extinguished lamps on the staircase, but we examined the staircase from the bottom to the top and found no one there.", "바람이 여전히 사납게 불었으므로, 우리는 계단에서 꺼진 등불을 다시 켜다가 등불의 불을 위태롭게 할 마음은 들지 않았고, 그저 계단을 아래에서 위까지 살펴보았으나 거기에는 아무도 없었다."], ["It then occurred to me as possible that the man might have slipped into my rooms; so, lighting my candle at the watchman’s, and leaving him standing at the door, I examined them carefully, including the room in which my dreaded guest lay asleep.", "그때 문득 그 사내가 내 방으로 몰래 숨어들었을지도 모른다는 생각이 들었다. 그래서 나는 야경꾼의 등불로 내 초에 불을 붙이고, 그를 문간에 세워 둔 채, 내 무서운 손님이 잠들어 있는 방까지 포함하여 방들을 꼼꼼히 살폈다."], ["All was quiet, and assuredly no other man was in those chambers.", "모든 것이 조용했고, 그 거처 안에 다른 사람은 분명 아무도 없었다."]], [["It troubled me that there should have been a lurker on the stairs, on that night of all nights in the year, and I asked the watchman, on the chance of eliciting some hopeful explanation as I handed him a dram at the door, whether he had admitted at his gate any gentleman who had perceptibly been dining out?", "한 해 중에서도 하필이면 그 밤에 계단에 숨어 있던 자가 있었다는 사실이 나를 불안하게 했다. 그래서 나는 문간에서 야경꾼에게 술 한 잔을 건네면서, 혹시 무언가 마음을 놓이게 할 만한 설명을 끌어낼 수 있을까 싶어, 밖에서 만찬을 들고 온 티가 뚜렷이 나는 신사를 그가 문으로 들여보낸 적이 있느냐고 물었다. 그렇다고, 그가 말했다."], ["Yes, he said; at different times of the night, three.", "밤중에 여러 차례, 세 사람이 있었다고 했다."], ["One lived in Fountain Court, and the other two lived in the Lane, and he had seen them all go home.", "한 사람은 파운틴 코트에 살았고, 다른 두 사람은 레인에 살았으며, 그는 그들이 모두 집으로 들어가는 것을 보았다고 했다."], ["Again, the only other man who dwelt in the house of which my chambers formed a part had been in the country for some weeks, and he certainly had not returned in the night, because we had seen his door with his seal on it as we came upstairs.", "또한 내 거처가 속한 그 집에 사는 다른 단 한 사람은 몇 주째 시골에 가 있었고, 그가 밤중에 돌아오지 않은 것은 분명했으니, 우리가 계단을 올라오면서 그의 봉인이 찍힌 문을 보았기 때문이다."]], [["“The night being so bad, sir,”", "\"밤이 워낙 사나워서요, 나리.\""], ["said the watchman, as he gave me back my glass, “uncommon few have come in at my gate.", "야경꾼이 내 잔을 돌려주며 말했다. \"유난히 적은 사람만 제 문으로 들어왔습니다. 제가 말씀드린 그 세 신사 말고는, 열한 시쯤 어떤 낯선 사람이 나리를 찾았던 것 말고는 달리 떠오르는 이가 없습니다.\""], ["Besides them three gentlemen that I have named, I don’t call to mind another since about eleven o’clock, when a stranger asked for you.”", "\"내 삼촌이오.\" 나는 중얼거렸다. \"그렇소.\""]], [["“My uncle,” I muttered. “Yes.”", "\"그분을 보셨습니까, 나리?\""]], [["“You saw him, sir?”", "\"보았소. 아, 그럼.\""]], [["“Yes. Oh yes.”", "\"같이 온 사람도요?\""]], [["“Likewise the person with him?”", "\"같이 온 사람이라니!\" 나는 되뇌었다."]], [["“Person with him!” I repeated. “I judged the person to be with him,”", "\"제가 보기엔 그 사람이 함께 온 것 같았습니다.\""], ["returned the watchman.", "야경꾼이 대답했다."], ["“The person stopped, when he stopped to make inquiry of me, and the person took this way when he took this way.”", "\"그 사람은 그분이 멈춰 서서 제게 묻는 동안 함께 멈춰 섰고, 그분이 이쪽 길로 가니 그 사람도 이쪽 길로 갔거든요.\""]], [["“What sort of person?”", "\"어떤 사람이던가?\""]], [["The watchman had not particularly noticed; he should say a working person; to the best of his belief, he had a dust-coloured kind of clothes on, under a dark coat.", "야경꾼은 딱히 눈여겨보지는 않았다고 했다. 일하는 사람 같았다고나 할까, 그가 보기로는 짙은 외투 안에 먼지 빛깔의 옷 같은 것을 걸치고 있었다고 했다."], ["The watchman made more light of the matter than I did, and naturally; not having my reason for attaching weight to it.", "야경꾼은 나보다 이 일을 훨씬 가볍게 여겼는데, 그도 그럴 것이 거기에 무게를 둘 까닭이 내게는 있었으나 그에게는 없었으니, 당연한 일이었다."]], [["When I had got rid of him, which I thought it well to do without prolonging explanations, my mind was much troubled by these two circumstances taken together.", "설명을 길게 끌지 않고 그를 떼어 보내는 편이 좋겠다 싶어 그렇게 한 뒤, 이 두 가지 정황을 한데 묶어 생각하노라니 내 마음은 몹시 어지러웠다."], ["Whereas they were easy of innocent solution apart,—as, for instance, some diner out or diner at home, who had not gone near this watchman’s gate, might have strayed to my staircase and dropped asleep there,—and my nameless visitor might have brought some one with him to show him the way,—still, joined, they had an ugly look to one as prone to distrust and fear as the changes of a few hours had made me.", "따로 떼어 놓고 보면 죄 없는 해명이 쉽사리 가능한 것이었으니 — 이를테면, 야경꾼의 문 근처에는 얼씬도 하지 않은 채 밖에서든 집에서든 만찬을 든 누군가가 내 계단으로 잘못 들어와 거기서 잠들었을 수도 있고, 이름 모를 내 방문객이 길을 안내하라고 누군가를 데려왔을 수도 있었다 — 그래도 그 둘을 한데 합쳐 놓으면, 불과 몇 시간 사이의 변화로 의심과 두려움에 빠지기 쉬워진 나 같은 자에게는 흉한 낌새로 보였다."]], [["I lighted my fire, which burnt with a raw pale flare at that time of the morning, and fell into a doze before it.", "나는 불을 지폈는데, 그 아침 시각에 불은 설익은 듯 희끄무레한 불꽃을 일으키며 타올랐고, 나는 그 앞에서 꾸벅꾸벅 졸기 시작했다."], ["I seemed to have been dozing a whole night when the clocks struck six.", "시계가 여섯 시를 칠 때, 나는 마치 밤새도록 졸았던 것 같았다."], ["As there was full an hour and a half between me and daylight, I dozed again; now, waking up uneasily, with prolix conversations about nothing, in my ears; now, making thunder of the wind in the chimney; at length, falling off into a profound sleep from which the daylight woke me with a start.", "날이 밝으려면 아직 한 시간 반은 족히 남아 있었으므로, 나는 다시 졸았다. 어느 때는 아무것도 아닌 일에 관한 장황한 대화가 귓가에 맴돌며 불안하게 잠을 깼고, 어느 때는 굴뚝을 지나는 바람 소리를 천둥소리로 만들어 냈으며, 마침내 깊은 잠에 빠져들었다가 날이 밝아 오는 빛에 화들짝 놀라 깨어났다."]], [["All this time I had never been able to consider my own situation, nor could I do so yet.", "이 모든 시간 동안 나는 내 처지를 도무지 헤아릴 수 없었고, 지금도 여전히 그러지 못했다."], ["I had not the power to attend to it.", "거기에 마음을 쏟을 기력이 내게는 없었다."], ["I was greatly dejected and distressed, but in an incoherent wholesale sort of way.", "나는 몹시 풀이 죽고 괴로웠으나, 그것은 갈피를 잡을 수 없이 막연하고 두루뭉술한 괴로움이었다."], ["As to forming any plan for the future, I could as soon have formed an elephant.", "앞날에 대한 계획을 세운다는 것은, 코끼리 한 마리를 만들어 내는 일이나 다름없었다."], ["When I opened the shutters and looked out at the wet wild morning, all of a leaden hue; when I walked from room to room; when I sat down again shivering, before the fire, waiting for my laundress to appear; I thought how miserable I was, but hardly knew why, or how long I had been so, or on what day of the week I made the reflection, or even who I was that made it.", "덧문을 열고 온통 납빛으로 물든 축축하고 거친 아침을 내다볼 때, 이 방 저 방을 거닐 때, 빨래하는 여자가 나타나기를 기다리며 불 앞에서 다시 부들부들 떨며 앉아 있을 때, 나는 내가 얼마나 비참한지를 생각했으나, 왜 그러한지, 언제부터 그러했는지, 무슨 요일에 그런 생각을 하고 있는지, 심지어 그런 생각을 하는 내가 누구인지조차 거의 알지 못했다."]], [["At last, the old woman and the niece came in,—the latter with a head not easily distinguishable from her dusty broom,—and testified surprise at sight of me and the fire.", "마침내 늙은 여자와 그 조카가 들어왔는데 — 조카라는 여자는 머리가 자기 먼지투성이 빗자루와 좀처럼 구별되지 않았다 — 두 사람은 나와 불을 보고는 놀라는 기색을 보였다."], ["To whom I imparted how my uncle had come in the night and was then asleep, and how the breakfast preparations were to be modified accordingly.", "나는 그들에게 삼촌이 밤중에 와서 지금 잠들어 있으며, 그에 맞춰 아침 식사 준비를 달리해야 한다고 일러 주었다."], ["Then I washed and dressed while they knocked the furniture about and made a dust; and so, in a sort of dream or sleep-waking, I found myself sitting by the fire again, waiting for—Him—to come to breakfast.", "그러고 나서 그들이 가구를 이리저리 두드리며 먼지를 일으키는 동안, 나는 씻고 옷을 입었다. 그리하여 일종의 꿈속에서, 혹은 잠과 깨어남 사이에서, 나는 다시 불 앞에 앉아 그가, 그분이, 아침을 들러 나오기를 기다리는 나 자신을 발견했다."]], [["By and by, his door opened and he came out.", "이윽고 그의 방문이 열리며 그가 나왔다."], ["I could not bring myself to bear the sight of him, and I thought he had a worse look by daylight.", "나는 도저히 그를 바로 볼 수가 없었고, 그가 환한 대낮에는 한층 더 험상궂게 보인다고 생각했다."]], [["“I do not even know,” said I, speaking low as he took his seat at the table, “by what name to call you.", "\"나는 당신을 무슨 이름으로 불러야 할지조차 모르겠습니다.\" 나는 그가 식탁에 자리를 잡을 때 나직이 말했다."], ["I have given out that you are my uncle.”", "\"나는 당신이 내 삼촌이라고 둘러대 두었습니다.\""]], [["“That’s it, dear boy! Call me uncle.”", "\"바로 그거다, 얘야! 나를 삼촌이라고 불러라.\""]], [["“You assumed some name, I suppose, on board ship?”", "\"배에서는 무슨 이름을 쓰셨겠지요?\""]], [["“Yes, dear boy.", "\"그래, 얘야."], ["I took the name of Provis.”", "나는 프로비스라는 이름을 썼다.\""]], [["“Do you mean to keep that name?”", "\"그 이름을 계속 쓰실 작정인가요?\""]], [["“Why, yes, dear boy, it’s as good as another,—unless you’d like another.”", "\"왜, 그래, 얘야, 다른 이름이나 매한가지니까 — 네가 다른 이름을 바라지 않는다면 말이다.\""]], [["“What is your real name?”", "\"진짜 이름은 무엇입니까?\""], ["I asked him in a whisper.", "나는 속삭이듯 그에게 물었다."]], [["“Magwitch,”", "\"매그위치다.\""], ["he answered, in the same tone; “chrisen’d Abel.”", "그가 같은 어조로 대답했다. \"세례명은 에이벌이고.\""]], [["“What were you brought up to be?”", "\"무슨 일을 하도록 길러지셨나요?\""]], [["“A warmint, dear boy.”", "\"해충이지, 얘야.\""]], [["He answered quite seriously, and used the word as if it denoted some profession.", "그는 자못 진지하게 대답했고, 마치 그것이 무슨 직업의 이름이기라도 한 듯 그 말을 썼다."]], [["“When you came into the Temple last night—”", "\"어젯밤 당신이 템플에 들어왔을 때 — \""], ["said I, pausing to wonder whether that could really have been last night, which seemed so long ago.", "나는 그것이 정말로 어젯밤 일이었던가 의아해하며 말을 멈추었으니, 그것이 까마득히 오래전 일처럼 여겨졌던 것이다."]], [["“Yes, dear boy?”", "\"그래, 얘야?\""]], [["“When you came in at the gate and asked the watchman the way here, had you any one with you?”", "\"당신이 문으로 들어와 야경꾼에게 여기 오는 길을 물었을 때, 누군가 같이 있었습니까?\""]], [["“With me?", "\"나랑?"], ["No, dear boy.”", "아니다, 얘야.\""]], [["“But there was some one there?”", "\"하지만 거기 누군가 있었습니다.\""]], [["“I didn’t take particular notice,”", "\"딱히 눈여겨보지는 않았다만.\""], ["he said, dubiously, “not knowing the ways of the place.", "그가 미심쩍은 듯 말했다. \"여기 사정을 몰라서 말이다."], ["But I think there was a person, too, come in alonger me.”", "하지만 나하고 같이 들어온 사람이 하나 있긴 했던 것 같다.\""]], [["“Are you known in London?”", "\"런던에 당신을 아는 사람이 있습니까?\""]], [["“I hope not!”", "\"없기를 바라야지!\""], ["said he, giving his neck a jerk with his forefinger that made me turn hot and sick.", "그가 집게손가락으로 제 목을 홱 그어 보이며 말했는데, 그 바람에 나는 얼굴이 화끈 달아오르며 속이 메스꺼워졌다."]], [["“Were you known in London, once?”", "\"예전에 런던에 당신을 아는 사람이 있었나요?\""]], [["“Not over and above, dear boy.", "\"그리 많지는 않았다, 얘야."], ["I was in the provinces mostly.”", "나는 주로 지방에 있었으니까.\""]], [["“Were you—tried—in London?”", "\"런던에서 — 재판을 받으셨나요?\""]], [["“Which time?”", "\"어느 때 말이냐?\""], ["said he, with a sharp look.", "그가 날카로운 눈초리로 물었다."]], [["“The last time.”", "\"마지막 때요.\""]], [["He nodded.", "그가 고개를 끄덕였다."], ["“First knowed Mr. Jaggers that way.", "\"그렇게 해서 처음 재거스 씨를 알게 됐다."], ["Jaggers was for me.”", "재거스가 내 변호를 맡았지.\""]], [["It was on my lips to ask him what he was tried for, but he took up a knife, gave it a flourish, and with the words, “And what I done is worked out and paid for!” fell to at his breakfast.", "그가 무슨 죄로 재판을 받았느냐고 물으려는 말이 입가에 맴돌았으나, 그는 칼을 집어 들고 한 번 휘두르더니, \"그리고 내가 저지른 짓은 다 치르고 갚았다!\"라는 말과 함께 아침 식사에 달려들었다."]], [["He ate in a ravenous way that was very disagreeable, and all his actions were uncouth, noisy, and greedy.", "그는 몹시 게걸스럽게 먹었는데 그 꼴이 여간 거북한 게 아니었고, 그의 모든 동작은 거칠고 시끄럽고 탐욕스러웠다."], ["Some of his teeth had failed him since I saw him eat on the marshes, and as he turned his food in his mouth, and turned his head sideways to bring his strongest fangs to bear upon it, he looked terribly like a hungry old dog.", "내가 습지에서 그가 먹는 것을 본 이후로 이가 몇 개 빠진 듯, 음식을 입안에서 굴리며 가장 튼튼한 어금니로 씹으려고 고개를 옆으로 돌리는 모습은 흡사 굶주린 늙은 개와도 같아 끔찍하기 짝이 없었다."], ["If I had begun with any appetite, he would have taken it away, and I should have sat much as I did,—repelled from him by an insurmountable aversion, and gloomily looking at the cloth.", "내게 처음에 어떤 식욕이 있었더라도 그가 그것을 가셔 놓았을 것이고, 나는 지금처럼 — 도저히 떨칠 수 없는 혐오에 그에게서 밀려난 채 침울하게 식탁보만 바라보며 — 앉아 있었을 것이다."]], [["“I’m a heavy grubber, dear boy,”", "\"나는 대식가다, 얘야.\""], ["he said, as a polite kind of apology when he made an end of his meal, “but I always was.", "그가 식사를 마치면서 일종의 정중한 사과처럼 말했다. \"하지만 늘 그랬다."], ["If it had been in my constitution to be a lighter grubber, I might ha’ got into lighter trouble.", "좀 적게 먹는 체질로 타고났더라면, 어쩌면 좀 가벼운 곤경에 빠졌을지도 모르지."], ["Similarly, I must have my smoke. When I was first hired out as shepherd t’other side the world, it’s my belief I should ha’ turned into a molloncolly-mad sheep myself, if I hadn’t a had my smoke.”", "마찬가지로, 나는 담배를 피워야 한다. 처음 세상 저편에서 양치기로 고용되었을 때, 담배가 없었더라면 나 자신이 우울증에 미친 양으로 변해 버렸을 거라고 나는 믿는다.\""]], [["As he said so, he got up from table, and putting his hand into the breast of the pea-coat he wore, brought out a short black pipe, and a handful of loose tobacco of the kind that is called Negro-head.", "그렇게 말하면서 그는 식탁에서 일어나, 입고 있던 두꺼운 모직 외투의 가슴께에 손을 넣어 짤막한 검은 파이프 하나와, 흑인 머리라 불리는 종류의 부스러기 담배 한 줌을 꺼냈다."], ["Having filled his pipe, he put the surplus tobacco back again, as if his pocket were a drawer.", "파이프를 채우고 나서 그는 남은 담배를 다시 도로 집어넣었는데, 마치 자기 주머니가 서랍이라도 되는 양했다."], ["Then, he took a live coal from the fire with the tongs, and lighted his pipe at it, and then turned round on the hearth-rug with his back to the fire, and went through his favourite action of holding out both his hands for mine.", "그러고는 부젓가락으로 불에서 이글거리는 숯 한 덩이를 집어 파이프에 불을 붙이고, 벽난로 앞 깔개 위에서 불을 등진 채 몸을 돌려, 두 손을 내밀어 내 손을 잡으려는 그 좋아하는 동작을 되풀이했다."]], [["“And this,”", "\"그리고 이게,\""], ["said he, dandling my hands up and down in his, as he puffed at his pipe,—“and this is the gentleman what I made!", "그가 파이프를 뻐끔거리며 제 두 손으로 내 손을 위아래로 어르듯 흔들면서 말했다. \"그리고 이게 내가 만든 신사다!"], ["The real genuine One!", "진짜배기 신사 말이다!"], ["It does me good fur to look at you, Pip.", "너를 보고 있으면 마음이 흐뭇해진다, 핍."], ["All I stip’late, is, to stand by and look at you, dear boy!”", "내가 바라는 건 오직, 곁에 서서 너를 바라보는 것뿐이야, 얘야!\""]], [["I released my hands as soon as I could, and found that I was beginning slowly to settle down to the contemplation of my condition.", "나는 되도록 빨리 손을 빼냈고, 내가 천천히 내 처지를 곰곰이 따져 보는 마음 상태로 가라앉기 시작했음을 깨달았다."], ["What I was chained to, and how heavily, became intelligible to me, as I heard his hoarse voice, and sat looking up at his furrowed bald head with its iron grey hair at the sides.", "내가 무엇에, 그리고 얼마나 무겁게 매여 있는지가, 그의 쉰 목소리를 듣고 깊은 주름이 새겨진 그의 대머리와 양옆의 무쇠 같은 잿빛 머리칼을 올려다보며 앉아 있노라니, 차츰 또렷하게 다가왔다."]], [["“I mustn’t see my gentleman a footing it in the mire of the streets; there mustn’t be no mud on his boots.", "\"내 신사가 거리의 진창을 밟고 다니는 꼴을 봐선 안 되지. 그의 구두에 흙 한 점 묻어선 안 돼."], ["My gentleman must have horses, Pip!", "내 신사는 말을 가져야 한다, 핍!"], ["Horses to ride, and horses to drive, and horses for his servant to ride and drive as well.", "타고 다닐 말, 몰고 다닐 말, 그리고 그의 하인이 타고 몰 말도 말이다."], ["Shall colonists have their horses (and blood ’uns, if you please, good Lord!) and not my London gentleman? No, no.", "식민지 사람들이 자기네 말을(그것도 순종 말을, 천만의 말씀, 맙소사!) 갖는데, 내 런던 신사가 못 가질 게 무어냐? 아니, 안 될 말이지."], ["We’ll show ’em another pair of shoes than that, Pip; won’t us?”", "우리는 그자들한테 그것보다 한 수 위의 솜씨를 보여 줄 거다, 핍, 안 그러냐?\""]], [["He took out of his pocket a great thick pocket-book, bursting with papers, and tossed it on the table.", "그는 주머니에서 서류로 터질 듯한 두툼한 지갑 하나를 꺼내 식탁 위에 휙 던졌다."]], [["“There’s something worth spending in that there book, dear boy.", "\"그 책 속에 쓸 만한 게 들어 있다, 얘야. 그건 네 거다."], ["It’s yourn. All I’ve got ain’t mine; it’s yourn.", "내가 가진 건 전부 내 것이 아니라 네 것이야."], ["Don’t you be afeerd on it.", "그걸 두려워 말아라."], ["There’s more where that come from.", "그게 나온 데서 더 많이 나올 테니까."], ["I’ve come to the old country fur to see my gentleman spend his money like a gentleman.", "나는 내 신사가 신사답게 돈을 쓰는 꼴을 보려고 옛 고향으로 돌아온 거다."], ["That’ll be my pleasure.", "그게 내 기쁨이 될 거다."], ["My pleasure ’ull be fur to see him do it. And blast you all!”", "그가 그렇게 하는 걸 보는 게 내 기쁨이란 말이다. 그리고 너희 모두 망해 버려라!\""], ["he wound up, looking round the room and snapping his fingers once with a loud snap, “blast you every one, from the judge in his wig, to the colonist a stirring up the dust, I’ll show a better gentleman than the whole kit on you put together!”", "그는 방 안을 둘러보며 큰 소리로 한 번 손가락을 딱 튀기고는 이렇게 끝맺었다. \"가발 쓴 판사부터 먼지를 일으키는 식민지 놈에 이르기까지, 너희 한 놈도 빠짐없이 다 망해 버려라. 나는 너희를 죄다 합쳐 놓은 것보다 더 나은 신사를 보여 줄 테니까!\""]], [["“Stop!”", "\"그만!\""], ["said I, almost in a frenzy of fear and dislike, “I want to speak to you.", "나는 거의 두려움과 혐오에 미칠 지경이 되어 말했다. \"당신과 얘기를 좀 해야겠습니다."], ["I want to know what is to be done.", "나는 어떻게 해야 할지 알고 싶습니다."], ["I want to know how you are to be kept out of danger, how long you are going to stay, what projects you have.”", "어떻게 하면 당신을 위험에서 지킬 수 있는지, 얼마나 오래 머무를 작정인지, 무슨 계획을 가지고 있는지 알고 싶습니다.\""]], [["“Look’ee here, Pip,”", "\"이것 봐라, 핍.\""], ["said he, laying his hand on my arm in a suddenly altered and subdued manner; “first of all, look’ee here.", "그가 별안간 달라진 가라앉은 태도로 내 팔에 손을 얹으며 말했다. \"무엇보다 먼저, 이것 봐라."], ["I forgot myself half a minute ago.", "내가 조금 전에 분수를 잊었다."], ["What I said was low; that’s what it was; low.", "내가 한 말은 천박했어. 바로 그거다, 천박했지."], ["Look’ee here, Pip.", "이것 봐라, 핍."], ["Look over it.", "눈감아 다오."], ["I ain’t a-going to be low.”", "나는 천박하게 굴지 않을 거다.\""]], [["“First,”", "\"먼저,\""], ["I resumed, half groaning, “what precautions can be taken against your being recognised and seized?”", "나는 반쯤 신음하듯 말을 이었다. \"당신이 발각되어 붙잡히지 않으려면 어떤 대책을 세울 수 있겠습니까?\""]], [["“No, dear boy,”", "\"아니다, 얘야.\""], ["he said, in the same tone as before, “that don’t go first.", "그가 아까와 같은 어조로 말했다. \"그게 먼저가 아니다."], ["Lowness goes first.", "천박함이 먼저야."], ["I ain’t took so many year to make a gentleman, not without knowing what’s due to him.", "내가 신사 하나 만드는 데 그 숱한 세월을 보낸 게, 그에게 마땅히 갖춰야 할 도리를 모르고서 한 일이 아니란 말이다."], ["Look’ee here, Pip.", "이것 봐라, 핍."], ["I was low; that’s what I was; low.", "나는 천박했다. 바로 그거였어, 천박했지."], ["Look over it, dear boy.”", "눈감아 다오, 얘야.\""]], [["Some sense of the grimly-ludicrous moved me to a fretful laugh, as I replied, “I have looked over it.", "어딘가 음산하면서도 우스꽝스러운 느낌에 나는 짜증 섞인 웃음이 나와, 이렇게 대꾸했다. \"이미 눈감았습니다."], ["In Heaven’s name, don’t harp upon it!”", "제발 부탁이니, 그 얘긴 그만 되풀이하세요!\""]], [["“Yes, but look’ee here,”", "\"그래, 하지만 이것 봐라.\""], ["he persisted.", "그가 고집스레 말했다."], ["“Dear boy, I ain’t come so fur, not fur to be low.", "\"얘야, 내가 이 먼 길을 온 게 천박하게 굴려고 온 게 아니다."], ["Now, go on, dear boy.", "자, 계속해라, 얘야."], ["You was a saying—”", "네가 말하던 게 — \""]], [["“How are you to be guarded from the danger you have incurred?”", "\"당신이 무릅쓴 위험으로부터 어떻게 당신을 지켜야 할까요?\""]], [["“Well, dear boy, the danger ain’t so great.", "\"그게 말이다, 얘야, 위험이 그리 크진 않다."], ["Without I was informed agen, the danger ain’t so much to signify.", "다시 누가 밀고만 안 한다면, 위험이랄 것도 그다지 대단치 않아."], ["There’s Jaggers, and there’s Wemmick, and there’s you. Who else is there to inform?”", "재거스가 있고, 웨믹이 있고, 네가 있지. 그 밖에 밀고할 자가 누가 있겠느냐?\""]], [["“Is there no chance person who might identify you in the street?” said I.", "\"거리에서 우연히 당신을 알아볼 사람은 없을까요?\" 내가 말했다."]], [["“Well,” he returned, “there ain’t many.", "\"글쎄,\" 그가 대꾸했다."], ["Nor yet I don’t intend to advertise myself in the newspapers by the name of A.M. come back from Botany Bay; and years have rolled away, and who’s to gain by it?", "\"그런 사람은 많지 않다. 게다가 나는 보터니 만에서 돌아온 A.M.이라는 이름으로 신문에 내 광고를 낼 생각도 없고, 세월도 많이 흘렀는데, 누가 그걸로 득을 보겠느냐?"], ["Still, look’ee here, Pip.", "그래도 이것 봐라, 핍."], ["If the danger had been fifty times as great, I should ha’ come to see you, mind you, just the same.”", "설령 위험이 쉰 배는 더 컸더라도, 나는 너를 보러 왔을 거다. 명심해라, 똑같이 말이야.\""]], [["“And how long do you remain?”", "\"그럼 얼마나 머무르실 작정인가요?\""]], [["“How long?”", "\"얼마나라고?\""], ["said he, taking his black pipe from his mouth, and dropping his jaw as he stared at me.", "그가 입에서 검은 파이프를 떼고, 나를 빤히 바라보며 턱을 떨어뜨린 채 말했다."], ["“I’m not a-going back.", "\"나는 돌아가지 않는다."], ["I’ve come for good.”", "아주 돌아온 거다.\""]], [["“Where are you to live?”", "\"어디서 사실 겁니까?\""], ["said I. “What is to be done with you?", "내가 말했다. \"당신을 어찌해야 합니까?"], ["Where will you be safe?”", "어디라야 당신이 안전하겠어요?\""]], [["“Dear boy,”", "\"얘야,\""], ["he returned, “there’s disguising wigs can be bought for money, and there’s hair powder, and spectacles, and black clothes,—shorts and what not.", "그가 대답했다. \"돈만 있으면 변장용 가발도 살 수 있고, 머릿가루도, 안경도, 검은 옷도 — 반바지며 뭐며 다 있다."], ["Others has done it safe afore, and what others has done afore, others can do agen.", "전에도 다른 자들이 무사히 해냈으니, 전에 남들이 해낸 일이라면 또 남들도 다시 해낼 수 있는 법이지."], ["As to the where and how of living, dear boy, give me your own opinions on it.”", "어디서 어떻게 사느냐 하는 건, 얘야, 네 의견을 좀 들려다오.\""]], [["“You take it smoothly now,”", "\"지금은 태평하게 말씀하시지만,\""], ["said I, “but you were very serious last night, when you swore it was Death.”", "내가 말했다. \"어젯밤엔 그게 곧 죽음이라고 맹세하며 자못 심각하셨잖습니까.\""]], [["“And so I swear it is Death,”", "\"그래, 나는 그게 죽음이라고 맹세한다.\""], ["said he, putting his pipe back in his mouth, “and Death by the rope, in the open street not fur from this, and it’s serious that you should fully understand it to be so.", "그가 파이프를 도로 입에 물며 말했다. \"그것도 교수형으로, 여기서 멀지 않은 한길 한복판에서 당하는 죽음이지. 네가 그것을 그렇게 똑똑히 이해하는 게 중요한 일이고말고."], ["What then, when that’s once done?", "그렇다면 어쩌겠느냐, 일단 그렇게 된 마당에?"], ["Here I am.", "나는 여기 있다."], ["To go back now ’ud be as bad as to stand ground—worse.", "이제 와서 돌아가는 건 버티고 있는 것만큼이나 나쁜 일이야 — 아니, 더 나쁘지."], ["Besides, Pip, I’m here, because I’ve meant it by you, years and years.", "게다가, 핍, 내가 여기 온 건 여러 해 동안, 오랜 세월 너를 마음에 두고 있었기 때문이다."], ["As to what I dare, I’m a old bird now, as has dared all manner of traps since first he was fledged, and I’m not afeerd to perch upon a scarecrow.", "내가 무엇을 무릅쓰느냐 하면, 나는 이제 늙은 새다. 깃털이 처음 돋은 이래로 온갖 덫을 다 무릅써 온 늙은 새 말이다. 그러니 허수아비 위에 내려앉는 것쯤 두렵지 않아."], ["If there’s Death hid inside of it, there is, and let him come out, and I’ll face him, and then I’ll believe in him and not afore.", "그 안에 죽음이 숨어 있다면, 그렇다면 그렇겠지. 어디 나오라지, 그럼 내가 맞서 줄 테니. 그때 가서야 나는 그것을 믿을 거다, 그 전엔 안 믿어."], ["And now let me have a look at my gentleman agen.”", "자, 이제 내 신사를 다시 한 번 보게 해 다오.\""]], [["Once more, he took me by both hands and surveyed me with an air of admiring proprietorship: smoking with great complacency all the while.", "다시 한 번 그는 내 두 손을 잡고, 흐뭇해하는 소유주의 태도로 나를 찬찬히 뜯어보았다. 그러는 내내 그는 더없이 만족스럽게 담배를 피워 댔다."]], [["It appeared to me that I could do no better than secure him some quiet lodging hard by, of which he might take possession when Herbert returned: whom I expected in two or three days.", "내가 할 수 있는 최선은, 그에게 바로 가까운 곳에 조용한 거처를 마련해 주는 것이리라 여겨졌다. 이삼 일 안에 돌아오리라 예상되던 허버트가 오면 그가 그곳을 차지하면 될 터였다."], ["That the secret must be confided to Herbert as a matter of unavoidable necessity, even if I could have put the immense relief I should derive from sharing it with him out of the question, was plain to me.", "이 비밀을 허버트와 나눔으로써 내가 얻게 될 엄청난 안도감을 차치하더라도, 피할 수 없는 필연으로 그 비밀을 그에게 털어놓아야 한다는 것은 내게 분명한 일이었다."], ["But it was by no means so plain to Mr. Provis (I resolved to call him by that name), who reserved his consent to Herbert’s participation until he should have seen him and formed a favourable judgment of his physiognomy.", "그러나 프로비스 씨에게는(나는 그를 그 이름으로 부르기로 마음먹었다) 그것이 결코 그리 분명한 일이 아니었으니, 그는 허버트를 직접 보고 그 관상에 호의적인 판단을 내릴 때까지는 그를 끼워 주는 데 동의하기를 유보했다."], ["“And even then, dear boy,”", "\"그리고 그때 가서도, 얘야,\""], ["said he, pulling a greasy little clasped black Testament out of his pocket, “we’ll have him on his oath.”", "그는 주머니에서 기름때 묻은 작고 검은, 걸쇠 달린 신약 성서를 꺼내며 말했다. \"그에게 맹세를 시켜야 한다.\""]], [["To state that my terrible patron carried this little black book about the world solely to swear people on in cases of emergency, would be to state what I never quite established; but this I can say, that I never knew him put it to any other use.", "나의 그 무서운 후원자가 이 작은 검은 책을 세상천지에 들고 다닌 것이 오로지 긴급한 경우에 사람들에게 맹세를 시키기 위해서였다고 단언한다면, 내가 끝내 확실히 밝혀내지 못한 사실을 단언하는 셈이 되리라. 그러나 이것만은 말할 수 있다."], ["The book itself had the appearance of having been stolen from some court of justice, and perhaps his knowledge of its antecedents, combined with his own experience in that wise, gave him a reliance on its powers as a sort of legal spell or charm.", "나는 그가 그 책을 다른 어떤 용도로 쓰는 것을 한 번도 본 적이 없다. 그 책 자체는 어느 법정에서 훔쳐 온 듯한 모양새였고, 어쩌면 그 책의 내력에 대한 그의 지식이, 그 방면에서 쌓은 그 자신의 경험과 어우러져, 일종의 법적 주술이나 부적 같은 그 책의 힘에 그가 의지를 두게 했는지도 몰랐다."], ["On this first occasion of his producing it, I recalled how he had made me swear fidelity in the churchyard long ago, and how he had described himself last night as always swearing to his resolutions in his solitude.", "그가 그 책을 꺼내 보인 이 첫 번째 순간에, 나는 오래전 그가 교회 묘지에서 내게 충성을 맹세시켰던 일과, 어젯밤 그가 고독 속에서 늘 자신의 결심을 두고 맹세하곤 했노라고 스스로를 설명하던 일을 떠올렸다."]], [["As he was at present dressed in a seafaring slop suit, in which he looked as if he had some parrots and cigars to dispose of, I next discussed with him what dress he should wear.", "그가 지금은 마치 앵무새나 시가를 팔러 다니는 사람처럼 보이는 뱃사람용 헐렁한 작업복 차림이었으므로, 나는 다음으로 그에게 어떤 옷을 입혀야 할지 의논했다."], ["He cherished an extraordinary belief in the virtues of “shorts” as a disguise, and had in his own mind sketched a dress for himself that would have made him something between a dean and a dentist.", "그는 변장 수단으로서 반바지의 효능에 대해 유별난 믿음을 품고 있었고, 제 머릿속으로는 자기 옷차림을 그려 두었는데, 그대로라면 그는 성당 참사회장과 치과 의사 사이쯤 되는 무엇이 되었을 것이다."], ["It was with considerable difficulty that I won him over to the assumption of a dress more like a prosperous farmer’s; and we arranged that he should cut his hair close, and wear a little powder.", "형편 넉넉한 농부 비슷한 옷차림을 받아들이도록 그를 설득하는 데는 적잖은 애를 먹었다. 그리고 우리는 그가 머리를 짧게 깎고 머릿가루를 약간 바르기로 정했다."], ["Lastly, as he had not yet been seen by the laundress or her niece, he was to keep himself out of their view until his change of dress was made.", "끝으로, 빨래하는 여자나 그 조카가 아직 그를 보지 못했으므로, 옷을 갈아입을 때까지는 그들 눈에 띄지 않도록 몸을 숨기고 있기로 했다."]], [["It would seem a simple matter to decide on these precautions; but in my dazed, not to say distracted, state, it took so long, that I did not get out to further them until two or three in the afternoon.", "이런 대비책을 정하는 일이야 간단해 보일 테지만, 멍하다 못해 정신이 흩어진 내 상태로는 어찌나 오래 걸렸던지, 나는 그것들을 실행에 옮기러 나가지도 못한 채 오후 두세 시가 되어서야 겨우 밖으로 나섰다."], ["He was to remain shut up in the chambers while I was gone, and was on no account to open the door.", "내가 나가 있는 동안 그는 거처에 틀어박혀 있기로 했고, 어떤 일이 있어도 문을 열어선 안 되었다."]], [["There being to my knowledge a respectable lodging-house in Essex Street, the back of which looked into the Temple, and was almost within hail of my windows, I first of all repaired to that house, and was so fortunate as to secure the second floor for my uncle, Mr. Provis.", "내가 알기로 에식스 가에 점잖은 하숙집이 하나 있었는데, 그 뒤편은 템플 쪽을 향해 있어 거의 내 창문에서 부르면 들릴 만한 거리였다. 나는 무엇보다 먼저 그 집으로 가서, 다행히도 삼촌인 프로비스 씨를 위해 삼 층을 구할 수 있었다."], ["I then went from shop to shop, making such purchases as were necessary to the change in his appearance.", "그러고는 이 가게 저 가게를 다니며 그의 외양을 바꾸는 데 필요한 물건들을 사들였다."], ["This business transacted, I turned my face, on my own account, to Little Britain. Mr.", "이 일을 마치고 나서, 나는 내 일을 보러 리틀 브리튼으로 발길을 돌렸다."], ["Jaggers was at his desk, but, seeing me enter, got up immediately and stood before his fire.", "재거스 씨는 책상 앞에 있었으나, 내가 들어서는 것을 보자 곧바로 일어나 벽난로 앞에 섰다."]], [["“Now, Pip,”", "\"자, 핍,\""], ["said he, “be careful.”", "그가 말했다. \"조심하게.\""]], [["“I will, sir,” I returned.", "\"그러겠습니다, 선생님.\""], ["For, coming along I had thought well of what I was going to say.", "나는 대답했다. 오는 길에 무슨 말을 할지 곰곰이 생각해 두었던 것이다."]], [["“Don’t commit yourself,”", "\"자네 자신을 옭아매지 말게.\""], ["said Mr. Jaggers, “and don’t commit any one.", "재거스 씨가 말했다. \"그리고 그 누구도 옭아매지 말게."], ["You understand—any one.", "알겠나 — 그 누구도."], ["Don’t tell me anything: I don’t want to know anything; I am not curious.”", "내게 아무것도 말하지 말게. 나는 아무것도 알고 싶지 않으니까. 나는 궁금하지 않네.\""]], [["Of course I saw that he knew the man was come.", "물론 나는 그가 그 사람이 왔다는 사실을 알고 있음을 알아챘다."]], [["“I merely want, Mr. Jaggers,”", "\"제가 바라는 건 다만, 재거스 씨,\""], ["said I, “to assure myself that what I have been told is true.", "내가 말했다. \"제가 들은 바가 사실인지 확인하려는 것뿐입니다."], ["I have no hope of its being untrue, but at least I may verify it.”", "그게 거짓이리라는 기대는 없습니다만, 적어도 그것만은 확인해 둘 수 있겠지요.\""]], [["Mr. Jaggers nodded.", "재거스 씨가 고개를 끄덕였다."], ["“But did you say ‘told’ or ‘informed’?” he asked me, with his head on one side, and not looking at me, but looking in a listening way at the floor.", "\"그런데 자네 '들었다'고 했나, '통지받았다'고 했나?\" 그가 고개를 한쪽으로 기울인 채, 나를 보지 않고, 귀를 기울이는 듯한 태도로 바닥을 내려다보며 물었다."], ["“Told would seem to imply verbal communication.", "\"들었다고 하면 구두로 전달받은 것을 뜻하는 듯한데."], ["You can’t have verbal communication with a man in New South Wales, you know.”", "자네도 알다시피 뉴사우스웨일스에 있는 사람과는 구두로 의사를 주고받을 수 없네.\""]], [["“I will say, informed, Mr. Jaggers.”", "\"통지받았다고 하겠습니다, 재거스 씨.\""]], [["“Good.”", "\"좋아.\""]], [["“I have been informed by a person named Abel Magwitch, that he is the benefactor so long unknown to me.”", "\"에이벌 매그위치라는 사람에게서, 그가 오래도록 제게 알려지지 않았던 그 후원자라는 통지를 받았습니다.\""]], [["“That is the man,”", "\"그게 바로 그 사람이지.\""], ["said Mr. Jaggers, “in New South Wales.”", "재거스 씨가 말했다. \"뉴사우스웨일스에 있는.\""]], [["“And only he?”", "\"그리고 오직 그 사람뿐인가요?\""], ["said I.", "내가 말했다."], ["“And only he,”", "\"그리고 오직 그 사람뿐이오.\""], ["said Mr. Jaggers.", "재거스 씨가 말했다."]], [["“I am not so unreasonable, sir, as to think you at all responsible for my mistakes and wrong conclusions; but I always supposed it was Miss Havisham.”", "\"제가 제 잘못과 그릇된 결론을 두고 선생님께 책임이 있다고 여길 만큼 분별없는 사람은 아닙니다만, 저는 줄곧 그것이 미스 해비셤이라고 짐작했습니다.\""]], [["“As you say, Pip,” returned Mr.", "\"자네 말마따나, 핍,\""], ["Jaggers, turning his eyes upon me coolly, and taking a bite at his forefinger, “I am not at all responsible for that.”", "재거스 씨가 차갑게 내게 눈길을 돌리고 집게손가락을 한 번 깨물며 대꾸했다. \"그 일에 나는 조금도 책임이 없네.\""]], [["“And yet it looked so like it, sir,”", "\"하지만 그것이 어찌나 그래 보였는지요, 선생님.\""], ["I pleaded with a downcast heart.", "나는 풀이 죽은 가슴으로 하소연했다."]], [["“Not a particle of evidence, Pip,”", "\"증거라곤 티끌만큼도 없었네, 핍.\""], ["said Mr. Jaggers, shaking his head and gathering up his skirts.", "재거스 씨가 고개를 저으며 옷자락을 추슬렀다."], ["“Take nothing on its looks; take everything on evidence.", "\"겉모습으로는 아무것도 받아들이지 말게. 모든 것을 증거로 받아들이게."], ["There’s no better rule.”", "그보다 더 나은 법칙은 없네.\""]], [["“I have no more to say,” said I, with a sigh, after standing silent for a little while.", "\"더는 드릴 말씀이 없습니다.\" 잠시 말없이 서 있다가 나는 한숨을 쉬며 말했다."], ["“I have verified my information, and there’s an end.”", "\"제가 통지받은 바를 확인했으니, 그것으로 끝입니다.\""]], [["“And Magwitch—in New South Wales—having at last disclosed himself,”", "\"그리고 매그위치가 — 뉴사우스웨일스에 있는 — 마침내 제 정체를 드러냈으니,\""], ["said Mr. Jaggers, “you will comprehend, Pip, how rigidly throughout my communication with you, I have always adhered to the strict line of fact.", "재거스 씨가 말했다. \"핍, 자네는 내가 자네와 주고받은 모든 일에서 얼마나 엄정하게 늘 사실의 엄격한 선만을 고수해 왔는지 이해하겠지."], ["There has never been the least departure from the strict line of fact.", "사실의 엄격한 선에서 단 한 번도 벗어난 적이 없네. 자네도 그건 잘 알고 있겠지?\""], ["You are quite aware of that?”", "\"잘 알고 있습니다, 선생님.\""]], [["“Quite, sir.” “I communicated to Magwitch—in New South Wales—when he first wrote to me—from New South Wales—the caution that he must not expect me ever to deviate from the strict line of fact.", "\"나는 매그위치에게 — 뉴사우스웨일스에 있는 — 그가 처음 내게 편지를 보냈을 때 — 뉴사우스웨일스에서 — 내가 사실의 엄격한 선에서 결코 벗어나리라 기대해선 안 된다는 주의를 주었네."], ["I also communicated to him another caution.", "나는 또 그에게 다른 주의도 하나 주었지."], ["He appeared to me to have obscurely hinted in his letter at some distant idea he had of seeing you in England here.", "내가 보기에 그는 편지에서, 여기 영국에서 자네를 만나겠다는 어떤 막연한 생각을 은연중에 내비친 듯했네."], ["I cautioned him that I must hear no more of that; that he was not at all likely to obtain a pardon; that he was expatriated for the term of his natural life; and that his presenting himself in this country would be an act of felony, rendering him liable to the extreme penalty of the law. I gave Magwitch that caution,”", "나는 그 일에 관해서는 더 듣고 싶지 않다고, 그가 사면을 받을 가능성은 전혀 없다고, 그는 평생을 기한으로 국외로 추방된 몸이라고, 그러니 그가 이 나라에 모습을 나타내는 것은 곧 중죄 행위로서 법의 극형에 처해질 수 있는 일이라고 주의를 주었네. 나는 매그위치에게 그렇게 주의를 주었지.\""], ["said Mr. Jaggers, looking hard at me; “I wrote it to New South Wales.", "재거스 씨가 나를 빤히 바라보며 말했다. \"그것을 뉴사우스웨일스로 써 보냈네."], ["He guided himself by it, no doubt.”", "그는 틀림없이 그에 따라 처신했을 걸세.\""]], [["“No doubt,”", "\"틀림없이 그랬겠지요.\""], ["said I.", "내가 말했다."]], [["“I have been informed by Wemmick,”", "\"나는 웨믹에게서 통지받았네.\""], ["pursued Mr. Jaggers, still looking hard at me, “that he has received a letter, under date Portsmouth, from a colonist of the name of Purvis, or—”", "재거스 씨가 여전히 나를 빤히 바라보며 말을 이었다. \"그가 퍼비스인지 뭔지 하는 이름의 식민지 사람에게서 포츠머스 소인이 찍힌 편지 한 통을 받았다고 말일세 — \""]], [["“Or Provis,”", "\"프로비스 말씀이시겠죠.\""], ["I suggested.", "내가 일러 주었다."]], [["“Or Provis—thank you, Pip.", "\"아니면 프로비스 — 고맙네, 핍."], ["Perhaps it is Provis? Perhaps you know it’s Provis?”", "어쩌면 프로비스인가? 어쩌면 자네는 그게 프로비스라는 걸 알고 있나?\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"네.\""], ["said I.", "내가 말했다."]], [["“You know it’s Provis.", "\"자네는 그게 프로비스라는 걸 알고 있군."], ["A letter, under date Portsmouth, from a colonist of the name of Provis, asking for the particulars of your address, on behalf of Magwitch.", "프로비스라는 이름의 식민지 사람에게서, 포츠머스 소인이 찍혀 온 편지 한 통. 매그위치를 대신하여 자네 주소의 상세한 내용을 묻는 편지였지."], ["Wemmick sent him the particulars, I understand, by return of post.", "내가 알기로 웨믹이 그에게 회신으로 그 상세한 내용을 보냈네."], ["Probably it is through Provis that you have received the explanation of Magwitch—in New South Wales?”", "아마도 자네가 매그위치에 관한 — 뉴사우스웨일스에 있는 — 해명을 전해 받은 것도 프로비스를 통해서겠지?\""]], [["“It came through Provis,”", "\"프로비스를 통해 전해 받았습니다.\""], ["I replied.", "나는 대답했다."]], [["“Good day, Pip,” said Mr.", "\"그럼 잘 가게, 핍.\""], ["Jaggers, offering his hand; “glad to have seen you.", "재거스 씨가 손을 내밀며 말했다. \"만나서 반가웠네."], ["In writing by post to Magwitch—in New South Wales—or in communicating with him through Provis, have the goodness to mention that the particulars and vouchers of our long account shall be sent to you, together with the balance; for there is still a balance remaining.", "우편으로 매그위치에게 — 뉴사우스웨일스에 있는 — 편지를 쓰거나, 프로비스를 통해 그와 연락할 때, 우리의 오랜 거래 내역의 상세한 명세와 증빙 서류를 잔액과 함께 자네에게 보내겠다고 부디 전해 주게. 아직 남아 있는 잔액이 있으니 말일세."], ["Good-day, Pip!”", "잘 가게, 핍!\""]], [["We shook hands, and he looked hard at me as long as he could see me.", "우리는 악수를 나누었고, 그는 나를 볼 수 있는 한 끝까지 빤히 바라보았다."], ["I turned at the door, and he was still looking hard at me, while the two vile casts on the shelf seemed to be trying to get their eyelids open, and to force out of their swollen throats, “O, what a man he is!”", "내가 문간에서 돌아보니, 그는 여전히 나를 빤히 바라보고 있었고, 그동안 선반 위의 그 흉측한 두 석고상은 부어오른 눈꺼풀을 애써 떠 보려 하고, 부풀어 오른 목구멍에서 \"오, 정말 대단한 사람이로군!\" 하는 소리를 가까스로 짜내려 애쓰는 것만 같았다."]], [["Wemmick was out, and though he had been at his desk he could have done nothing for me.", "웨믹은 자리에 없었고, 설령 그가 책상 앞에 있었다 한들 나를 위해 해 줄 수 있는 일이라곤 없었을 것이다."], ["I went straight back to the Temple, where I found the terrible Provis drinking rum and water and smoking negro-head, in safety.", "나는 곧장 템플로 돌아왔고, 거기서 그 무서운 프로비스가 럼주에 물을 타 마시며 흑인 머리 담배를 피우면서 무사히 있는 것을 발견했다."]], [["Next day the clothes I had ordered all came home, and he put them on.", "이튿날 내가 주문한 옷이 모두 집으로 배달되어 왔고, 그는 그것들을 입었다."], ["Whatever he put on, became him less (it dismally seemed to me) than what he had worn before.", "그가 무엇을 걸치든, (내게는 침울하게도) 전에 입고 있던 것보다 덜 어울리는 것만 같았다."], ["To my thinking, there was something in him that made it hopeless to attempt to disguise him.", "내 생각에는, 그를 변장시키려는 시도를 가망 없게 만드는 무언가가 그에게 있었다."], ["The more I dressed him and the better I dressed him, the more he looked like the slouching fugitive on the marshes.", "그에게 옷을 입히면 입힐수록, 더 좋은 옷을 입히면 입힐수록, 그는 점점 더 습지에서 어슬렁대던 그 도망자처럼 보였다."], ["This effect on my anxious fancy was partly referable, no doubt, to his old face and manner growing more familiar to me; but I believe too that he dragged one of his legs as if there were still a weight of iron on it, and that from head to foot there was Convict in the very grain of the man.", "내 불안한 상상에 미친 이런 영향은, 두말할 것 없이 그의 옛 얼굴과 거동이 내게 점점 더 낯익어졌기 때문이기도 했을 것이다. 그러나 나는 또한 그가 마치 아직도 쇠고랑의 무게가 매달려 있는 듯 한쪽 다리를 질질 끌고 다니며, 머리끝부터 발끝까지 그 사람의 됨됨이 결마다 죄수가 배어 있다고 믿었다."]], [["The influences of his solitary hut-life were upon him besides, and gave him a savage air that no dress could tame; added to these were the influences of his subsequent branded life among men, and, crowning all, his consciousness that he was dodging and hiding now.", "게다가 그에게는 외로운 오두막 생활의 영향이 배어 있어, 어떤 옷으로도 길들일 수 없는 야성적인 분위기를 풍겼다. 거기에 더해, 그 뒤로 사람들 틈에서 낙인찍힌 채 살아온 삶의 영향이 있었고, 그 모든 것의 정점에는 자신이 지금 몸을 피하고 숨어 다니고 있다는 의식이 있었다."], ["In all his ways of sitting and standing, and eating and drinking,—of brooding about in a high-shouldered reluctant style,—of taking out his great horn-handled jackknife and wiping it on his legs and cutting his food,—of lifting light glasses and cups to his lips, as if they were clumsy pannikins,—of chopping a wedge off his bread, and soaking up with it the last fragments of gravy round and round his plate, as if to make the most of an allowance, and then drying his finger-ends on it, and then swallowing it,—in these ways and a thousand other small nameless instances arising every minute in the day, there was Prisoner, Felon, Bondsman, plain as plain could be.", "앉고 서는 모든 몸짓에서, 먹고 마시는 모든 몸짓에서 — 어깨를 잔뜩 추켜올린 채 내키지 않는 듯한 태도로 곰곰 생각에 잠기는 모습에서, 뿔 손잡이가 달린 큼직한 잭나이프를 꺼내 다리에 쓱쓱 닦고는 음식을 써는 모습에서, 마치 투박한 양철 잔이라도 되는 양 얇은 유리잔과 찻잔을 입가로 들어 올리는 모습에서, 빵에서 한 조각을 베어 내어 그것으로 접시 둘레에 남은 그레이비의 마지막 부스러기까지 정해진 양을 한 톨이라도 더 알뜰히 쓰려는 듯 빙빙 돌려 가며 닦아 내고는, 그 빵으로 손가락 끝까지 훔친 다음 삼켜 버리는 모습에서 — 이런 몸짓들과, 하루 중에도 매 순간 일어나는 이름 없는 천 가지 다른 사소한 일들에서, 죄수가, 중죄인이, 도형수가 더없이 또렷하게 드러났다."]], [["It had been his own idea to wear that touch of powder, and I had conceded the powder after overcoming the shorts.", "머릿가루를 약간 바르자는 것은 그 자신의 생각이었고, 나는 반바지 문제를 잠재운 뒤에 그 머릿가루는 양보해 준 터였다."], ["But I can compare the effect of it, when on, to nothing but the probable effect of rouge upon the dead; so awful was the manner in which everything in him that it was most desirable to repress, started through that thin layer of pretence, and seemed to come blazing out at the crown of his head.", "그러나 그것을 바른 뒤의 효과는, 죽은 사람에게 연지를 발랐을 때 났음 직한 효과 말고는 무엇에도 견줄 수가 없었다. 그토록 끔찍하게도, 그에게서 가장 억눌러야 마땅한 모든 것이 그 얄팍한 위장의 막을 뚫고 솟아올라, 마치 그의 정수리에서 활활 타오르며 터져 나오는 것만 같았다."], ["It was abandoned as soon as tried, and he wore his grizzled hair cut short.", "그것은 시도하자마자 곧 포기되었고, 그는 희끗희끗한 머리를 짧게 깎은 채로 지냈다."]], [["Words cannot tell what a sense I had, at the same time, of the dreadful mystery that he was to me.", "그와 동시에 그가 내게 얼마나 무시무시한 수수께끼였던지, 그 느낌은 말로 다 할 수가 없다."], ["When he fell asleep of an evening, with his knotted hands clenching the sides of the easy-chair, and his bald head tattooed with deep wrinkles falling forward on his breast, I would sit and look at him, wondering what he had done, and loading him with all the crimes in the Calendar, until the impulse was powerful on me to start up and fly from him.", "저녁이면 그가 옹이 진 두 손으로 안락의자의 팔걸이를 움켜쥐고, 깊은 주름이 문신처럼 새겨진 대머리를 앞으로 떨어뜨려 가슴에 묻은 채 잠들 때면, 나는 앉아서 그를 바라보며, 그가 무슨 짓을 저질렀을까 헤아려 보고, 그에게 죄목록에 오른 온갖 죄를 죄다 뒤집어씌우다가, 마침내 벌떡 일어나 그에게서 달아나고픈 충동에 휩싸이곤 했다."], ["Every hour so increased my abhorrence of him, that I even think I might have yielded to this impulse in the first agonies of being so haunted, notwithstanding all he had done for me and the risk he ran, but for the knowledge that Herbert must soon come back.", "시시각각 그에 대한 내 혐오가 어찌나 커져 갔던지, 그가 나를 위해 해 준 그 모든 일과 그가 무릅쓰고 있는 위험에도 불구하고, 그런 시달림에 처음 휩싸여 괴로워하던 무렵에 나는, 허버트가 곧 돌아오리라는 사실을 알지 못했더라면 정말 이 충동에 굴복해 버렸을지도 모른다는 생각마저 든다."], ["Once, I actually did start out of bed in the night, and begin to dress myself in my worst clothes, hurriedly intending to leave him there with everything else I possessed, and enlist for India as a private soldier.", "한번은 실제로 한밤중에 침대에서 벌떡 일어나, 그를 내가 가진 다른 모든 것과 함께 거기 남겨 둔 채 떠나, 사병으로 인도로 떠날 작정으로 황급히 가장 허름한 옷을 주워 입기 시작한 적도 있었다."]], [["I doubt if a ghost could have been more terrible to me, up in those lonely rooms in the long evenings and long nights, with the wind and the rain always rushing by.", "길고 긴 저녁과 길고 긴 밤마다, 바람과 비가 끊임없이 휘몰아쳐 지나가는 그 외딴 방에서, 유령이라 한들 내게 그보다 더 무서웠을까 싶다."], ["A ghost could not have been taken and hanged on my account, and the consideration that he could be, and the dread that he would be, were no small addition to my horrors.", "유령은 나 때문에 붙잡혀 교수형을 당할 리 없었으나, 그는 그럴 수도 있다는 생각과, 그가 그렇게 되리라는 두려움은 내 공포를 적잖이 키워 놓았다."], ["When he was not asleep, or playing a complicated kind of Patience with a ragged pack of cards of his own,—a game that I never saw before or since, and in which he recorded his winnings by sticking his jackknife into the table,—when he was not engaged in either of these pursuits, he would ask me to read to him,—“Foreign language, dear boy!”", "그가 잠들어 있지도, 자기만의 너덜너덜한 카드 한 벌로 — 나는 그 전에도 그 후에도 본 적 없는 놀이로, 그는 잭나이프를 식탁에 푹 꽂아 가며 자기가 딴 점수를 기록했다 — 복잡한 패 맞추기 놀이를 하고 있지도 않을 때면, 그는 내게 책을 읽어 달라고 청하곤 했다. \"외국어로, 얘야!\""], ["While I complied, he, not comprehending a single word, would stand before the fire surveying me with the air of an Exhibitor, and I would see him, between the fingers of the hand with which I shaded my face, appealing in dumb show to the furniture to take notice of my proficiency.", "내가 그 청을 들어주는 동안, 그는 단 한마디도 알아듣지 못하면서, 불 앞에 서서 마치 전시 흥행사 같은 태도로 나를 바라보았고, 나는 손으로 얼굴을 가린 그 손가락 사이로, 그가 내 능숙함을 좀 알아 달라고 가구를 향해 말없는 몸짓으로 호소하는 모습을 보곤 했다."], ["The imaginary student pursued by the misshapen creature he had impiously made, was not more wretched than I, pursued by the creature who had made me, and recoiling from him with a stronger repulsion, the more he admired me and the fonder he was of me.", "자신이 불경하게 만들어 낸 흉측한 괴물에게 쫓기는 그 가상의 학생도, 나보다 더 비참하지는 않았으리라. 나를 만들어 낸 그 괴물에게 쫓기면서, 그가 나를 우러러보면 볼수록, 나를 더 애지중지하면 할수록, 더 강한 거부감으로 그에게서 움츠러드는 나보다는."]], [["This is written of, I am sensible, as if it had lasted a year.", "이 일을 마치 한 해 동안 계속된 것처럼 적고 있다는 것을, 나도 잘 안다."], ["It lasted about five days.", "그것은 닷새쯤 이어졌을 뿐이다."], ["Expecting Herbert all the time, I dared not go out, except when I took Provis for an airing after dark.", "그동안 줄곧 허버트를 기다리느라, 나는 날이 저문 뒤 프로비스를 바람 쐬러 데리고 나갈 때 말고는 감히 밖에 나가지 못했다."], ["At length, one evening when dinner was over and I had dropped into a slumber quite worn out,—for my nights had been agitated and my rest broken by fearful dreams,—I was roused by the welcome footstep on the staircase.", "마침내 어느 저녁, 저녁 식사가 끝나고 내가 완전히 지쳐 선잠에 빠져들었을 때 — 밤이면 마음이 들뜨고 무서운 꿈에 잠을 설쳐 왔던 까닭에 — 나는 계단을 올라오는 반가운 발소리에 잠이 깨었다."], ["Provis, who had been asleep too, staggered up at the noise I made, and in an instant I saw his jackknife shining in his hand.", "마찬가지로 잠들어 있던 프로비스는 내가 내는 소리에 비틀거리며 일어났고, 다음 순간 나는 그의 손에서 잭나이프가 번뜩이는 것을 보았다."]], [["“Quiet!", "\"조용히!"], ["It’s Herbert!”", "허버트입니다!\""], ["I said; and Herbert came bursting in, with the airy freshness of six hundred miles of France upon him.", "나는 말했다. 그러자 허버트가, 프랑스 육백 마일의 상쾌한 생기를 온몸에 두른 채 와락 뛰어들었다."]], [["“Handel, my dear fellow, how are you, and again how are you, and again how are you?", "\"핸들, 이 친구야, 잘 있었나, 또 잘 있었나, 또 잘 있었나?"], ["I seem to have been gone a twelvemonth!", "마치 일 년은 떠나 있었던 것 같군!"], ["Why, so I must have been, for you have grown quite thin and pale!", "아니, 정말 그랬던 게 틀림없어. 자네가 아주 야위고 핼쑥해졌으니 말이야!"], ["Handel, my—Halloa! I beg your pardon.”", "핸들, 내 — 아니! 실례했네.\""]], [["He was stopped in his running on and in his shaking hands with me, by seeing Provis.", "그는 프로비스를 보고는, 신나게 떠들며 내 손을 흔들어 대던 것을 멈추었다."], ["Provis, regarding him with a fixed attention, was slowly putting up his jackknife, and groping in another pocket for something else.", "프로비스는 그를 뚫어지게 주시하며, 천천히 잭나이프를 도로 집어넣고, 다른 주머니에서 무언가를 더듬어 찾고 있었다."]], [["“Herbert, my dear friend,”", "\"허버트, 내 소중한 친구,\""], ["said I, shutting the double doors, while Herbert stood staring and wondering, “something very strange has happened.", "나는 이중문을 닫으며 말했고, 그동안 허버트는 빤히 바라보며 어리둥절해 서 있었다. \"아주 이상한 일이 일어났다네."], ["This is—a visitor of mine.”", "이분은 — 내 손님일세.\""]], [["“It’s all right, dear boy!”", "\"괜찮네, 얘야!\""], ["said Provis coming forward, with his little clasped black book, and then addressing himself to Herbert.", "프로비스가 걸쇠 달린 작고 검은 책을 들고 앞으로 나오며 말하더니, 이내 허버트를 향해 말했다."], ["“Take it in your right hand.", "\"그걸 오른손에 쥐어라."], ["Lord strike you dead on the spot, if ever you split in any way sumever! Kiss it!”", "어떤 식으로든 털어놓는 날에는 하느님이 그 자리에서 자네를 쳐 죽이시리라! 거기 입을 맞춰라!\""]], [["“Do so, as he wishes it,”", "\"그분이 원하시는 대로 하게.\""], ["I said to Herbert.", "나는 허버트에게 말했다."], ["So, Herbert, looking at me with a friendly uneasiness and amazement, complied, and Provis immediately shaking hands with him, said, “Now you’re on your oath, you know.", "그러자 허버트는 다정하면서도 불안하고 놀란 눈빛으로 나를 바라보며 시키는 대로 했고, 프로비스는 곧바로 그와 악수하며 말했다. \"이제 자네는 맹세한 거다, 알겠나."], ["And never believe me on mine, if Pip shan’t make a gentleman on you!”", "핍이 자네를 신사로 만들어 주지 못한다면, 내 맹세도 두 번 다시 믿지 마라!\""]], [["# Chapter XLI.", "# Chapter XLI."]], [["In vain should I attempt to describe the astonishment and disquiet of Herbert, when he and I and Provis sat down before the fire, and I recounted the whole of the secret.", "허버트와 나, 그리고 프로비스가 난롯가에 둘러앉고 내가 그 비밀을 죄다 털어놓았을 때 허버트가 보인 경악과 동요를 묘사하려 한들 부질없는 노릇일 것이다."], ["Enough, that I saw my own feelings reflected in Herbert’s face, and not least among them, my repugnance towards the man who had done so much for me.", "다만 나는 내 자신의 감정이 허버트의 얼굴에 고스란히 비치는 것을 보았고, 그중에서도 나를 위해 그토록 많은 일을 해 준 그 사내에 대한 혐오감이 적지 않게 드러나 있었다는 것만으로 충분하리라."]], [["What would alone have set a division between that man and us, if there had been no other dividing circumstance, was his triumph in my story.", "다른 가르는 사정이 전혀 없었다 하더라도 그 사내와 우리 사이에 홀로 분단을 그어 놓았을 것은, 내 이야기에 대한 그의 의기양양함이었다."], ["Saving his troublesome sense of having been “low” on one occasion since his return,—on which point he began to hold forth to Herbert, the moment my revelation was finished,—he had no perception of the possibility of my finding any fault with my good fortune.", "돌아온 뒤로 한 번 자신이 \"천박했다\"는, 그를 괴롭히는 자각만 빼면 — 내 폭로가 끝나기가 무섭게 그는 그 점에 관해 허버트에게 일장 연설을 늘어놓기 시작했다 — 그에게는 내가 내 행운에서 그 어떤 흠을 잡을 수도 있으리라는 생각이 전혀 없었다."], ["His boast that he had made me a gentleman, and that he had come to see me support the character on his ample resources, was made for me quite as much as for himself.", "자신이 나를 신사로 만들었으며, 자신의 넉넉한 재력으로 내가 그 신분을 떠받치는 것을 보러 왔다는 그의 자랑은 자기 자신을 위한 것인 만큼이나 나를 위한 것이기도 했다."], ["And that it was a highly agreeable boast to both of us, and that we must both be very proud of it, was a conclusion quite established in his own mind.", "그리고 그것이 우리 둘 모두에게 더없이 유쾌한 자랑이며, 우리 둘 다 그것을 몹시 자랑스러워해야 마땅하다는 것이 그의 마음속에서는 확고하게 굳어진 결론이었다."]], [["“Though, look’ee here, Pip’s comrade,”", "\"하지만 이봐, 핍의 동무.\""], ["he said to Herbert, after having discoursed for some time, “I know very well that once since I come back—for half a minute—I’ve been low.", "한동안 떠들어 댄 뒤에 그가 허버트에게 말했다. \"내가 돌아온 뒤로 한 번 — 반 분 동안 — 천박했다는 건 나도 아주 잘 알아."], ["I said to Pip, I knowed as I had been low. But don’t you fret yourself on that score.", "핍한테 말했지, 내가 천박했었다는 걸 알고 있다고. 하지만 그 일로 마음 쓰지 마."], ["I ain’t made Pip a gentleman, and Pip ain’t a-going to make you a gentleman, not fur me not to know what’s due to ye both.", "내가 핍을 신사로 만들었고, 핍이 자네를 신사로 만들 텐데, 내가 자네들 둘에게 마땅히 해야 할 도리를 모를 리가 있나."], ["Dear boy, and Pip’s comrade, you two may count upon me always having a genteel muzzle on.", "얘야, 그리고 핍의 동무, 자네 둘은 내가 언제나 점잖게 입에 재갈을 물고 있으리라 믿어도 좋아."], ["Muzzled I have been since that half a minute when I was betrayed into lowness, muzzled I am at the present time, muzzled I ever will be.”", "천박함에 넘어갔던 그 반 분 이후로 줄곧 재갈을 물어 왔고, 지금 이 순간에도 재갈을 물고 있고, 앞으로도 영원히 재갈을 물고 있을 테니까.\""]], [["Herbert said, “Certainly,”", "허버트는 \"그럼요.\""], ["but looked as if there were no specific consolation in this, and remained perplexed and dismayed.", "하고 대꾸했지만, 그 말에 별다른 위안이 없다는 듯한 표정이었고, 여전히 당혹스럽고 낙담한 채였다."], ["We were anxious for the time when he would go to his lodging and leave us together, but he was evidently jealous of leaving us together, and sat late.", "우리는 그가 자기 숙소로 가서 우리 둘만 남겨 둘 시간을 애타게 기다렸으나, 그는 우리 둘만 남겨 두는 것을 분명 시샘하는지 늦게까지 앉아 있었다."], ["It was midnight before I took him round to Essex Street, and saw him safely in at his own dark door.", "내가 그를 에식스 가까지 데려다주고 그 어두운 자기 집 문 안으로 무사히 들어가는 것을 본 것은 자정이 넘어서였다."], ["When it closed upon him, I experienced the first moment of relief I had known since the night of his arrival.", "문이 그의 등 뒤로 닫혔을 때, 나는 그가 도착하던 밤 이후로 처음으로 안도의 순간을 맛보았다."]], [["Never quite free from an uneasy remembrance of the man on the stairs, I had always looked about me in taking my guest out after dark, and in bringing him back; and I looked about me now.", "계단에 있던 그 사내에 대한 불안한 기억에서 완전히 자유로운 적이 없었던 터라, 나는 날이 저문 뒤 손님을 데리고 나가거나 다시 데려올 때면 늘 주위를 살폈고, 이번에도 주위를 둘러보았다."], ["Difficult as it is in a large city to avoid the suspicion of being watched, when the mind is conscious of danger in that regard, I could not persuade myself that any of the people within sight cared about my movements.", "그런 점에서 위험을 의식하고 있을 때 큰 도시에서 감시당하고 있다는 의심을 떨치기란 어려운 일이지만, 그럼에도 시야에 들어오는 사람들 중 누가 내 행동에 관심을 둔다고는 도무지 믿어지지 않았다."], ["The few who were passing passed on their several ways, and the street was empty when I turned back into the Temple.", "지나가던 몇 안 되는 이들은 저마다의 길로 지나쳐 갔고, 내가 템플로 돌아섰을 때 거리는 텅 비어 있었다."], ["Nobody had come out at the gate with us, nobody went in at the gate with me.", "아무도 우리와 함께 문으로 나오지 않았고, 아무도 나와 함께 문으로 들어가지 않았다."], ["As I crossed by the fountain, I saw his lighted back windows looking bright and quiet, and, when I stood for a few moments in the doorway of the building where I lived, before going up the stairs, Garden Court was as still and lifeless as the staircase was when I ascended it.", "분수대 옆을 지날 때 그의 집 불 켜진 뒤쪽 창문들이 환하고 고요하게 보였고, 계단을 오르기 전 내가 사는 건물 입구에 잠시 서 있을 때, 가든 코트는 내가 올라간 그 계단만큼이나 적막하고 생기가 없었다."]], [["Herbert received me with open arms, and I had never felt before so blessedly what it is to have a friend.", "허버트는 두 팔을 벌려 나를 맞아 주었고, 나는 친구가 있다는 것이 어떤 것인지 그토록 복되게 느껴 본 적이 일찍이 없었다."], ["When he had spoken some sound words of sympathy and encouragement, we sat down to consider the question, What was to be done?", "그가 진심 어린 동정과 격려의 말을 몇 마디 건넨 뒤, 우리는 자리에 앉아 문제를 곰곰이 따져 보기 시작했다. 어떻게 할 것인가, 하는 문제를."]], [["The chair that Provis had occupied still remaining where it had stood,—for he had a barrack way with him of hanging about one spot, in one unsettled manner, and going through one round of observances with his pipe and his negro-head and his jackknife and his pack of cards, and what not, as if it were all put down for him on a slate,—I say his chair remaining where it had stood, Herbert unconsciously took it, but next moment started out of it, pushed it away, and took another.", "프로비스가 앉았던 의자는 여전히 놓여 있던 자리에 그대로 있었는데 — 그에게는 한 자리를 떠나지 못하고 어슬렁대는, 한결같이 안정되지 못한 태도로, 파이프와 흑인 머리 담배와 잭나이프와 카드 한 벌이며 그밖에 이것저것을 가지고 마치 석판에 정해 적혀 있기라도 한 듯 똑같은 일과를 되풀이하는, 병영 같은 버릇이 있었으므로 — 다시 말해 그의 의자가 놓여 있던 자리에 그대로 있었으므로, 허버트는 무심코 거기에 앉았다가 다음 순간 벌떡 일어나 그것을 밀어내고 다른 의자에 앉았다."], ["He had no occasion to say after that that he had conceived an aversion for my patron, neither had I occasion to confess my own.", "그 뒤로 그가 내 후원자에게 혐오감을 품게 되었노라고 말할 필요도 없었거니와, 나 또한 내 혐오감을 고백할 필요가 없었다."], ["We interchanged that confidence without shaping a syllable.", "우리는 한마디 말로 빚어내지 않고도 그 속내를 주고받았다."]], [["“What,”", "\"어떻게.\""], ["said I to Herbert, when he was safe in another chair,—“what is to be done?”", "허버트가 다른 의자에 안전하게 앉았을 때 내가 그에게 말했다. \"어떻게 해야 할까?\""]], [["“My poor dear Handel,”", "\"가엾은 친구 핸들.\""], ["he replied, holding his head, “I am too stunned to think.”", "그가 머리를 감싸 쥐며 대답했다. \"나는 너무 얼떨떨해서 생각이 안 돼.\""]], [["“So was I, Herbert, when the blow first fell.", "\"나도 그랬어, 허버트. 처음 그 일격을 맞았을 때는."], ["Still, something must be done.", "그래도 뭔가는 해야 해."], ["He is intent upon various new expenses,—horses, and carriages, and lavish appearances of all kinds.", "그는 갖가지 새로운 지출에 골몰해 있어 — 말이니, 마차니, 온갖 종류의 호사스러운 겉치레니."], ["He must be stopped somehow.”", "어떻게든 그를 말려야 해.\""]], [["“You mean that you can’t accept—”", "\"네 말은, 네가 받아들일 수 없다는—\""]], [["“How can I?” I interposed, as Herbert paused.", "\"내가 어떻게 받겠어?\" 허버트가 말을 멈추자 내가 끼어들었다."], ["“Think of him!", "\"그를 생각해 봐!"], ["Look at him!”", "그를 봐!\""]], [["An involuntary shudder passed over both of us.", "우리 둘 모두에게 저도 모르게 몸서리가 스쳐 지나갔다."]], [["“Yet I am afraid the dreadful truth is, Herbert, that he is attached to me, strongly attached to me.", "\"하지만 두려운 건, 허버트, 그 끔찍한 진실은 그가 나를 따른다는 거야, 그것도 단단히 따른다는 거지."], ["Was there ever such a fate!”", "이런 운명이 또 어디 있겠어!\""]], [["“My poor dear Handel,”", "\"가엾은 친구 핸들.\""], ["Herbert repeated.", "허버트가 거듭 말했다."]], [["“Then,”", "\"그렇다고.\""], ["said I, “after all, stopping short here, never taking another penny from him, think what I owe him already!", "내가 말했다. \"결국 여기서 딱 멈추고, 그에게서 더는 한 푼도 받지 않는다 쳐도, 내가 이미 그에게 진 빚을 생각해 봐!"], ["Then again: I am heavily in debt,—very heavily for me, who have now no expectations,—and I have been bred to no calling, and I am fit for nothing.”", "게다가 또 이렇잖아. 나는 빚이 잔뜩 있어 — 이제 아무 유산도 바랄 게 없는 나로서는 아주 무거운 빚이지 — 그리고 나는 아무런 직업도 익히지 못했고, 아무짝에도 쓸모가 없어.\""]], [["“Well, well, well!”", "\"이런, 이런, 이런!\""], ["Herbert remonstrated.", "허버트가 나무라듯 말했다."], ["“Don’t say fit for nothing.”", "\"아무짝에도 쓸모없다는 말은 하지 마.\""]], [["“What am I fit for?", "\"내가 뭐에 쓸모가 있겠어?"], ["I know only one thing that I am fit for, and that is, to go for a soldier.", "내가 쓸모 있을 만한 일은 딱 하나밖에 모르겠어. 그건 군인이 되는 거지."], ["And I might have gone, my dear Herbert, but for the prospect of taking counsel with your friendship and affection.”", "그리고 자네의 우정과 애정에 의논을 구할 가망만 아니었다면, 사랑하는 허버트, 나는 그렇게 갔을지도 몰라.\""]], [["Of course I broke down there: and of course Herbert, beyond seizing a warm grip of my hand, pretended not to know it.", "물론 나는 거기서 무너지고 말았다. 그리고 물론 허버트는 내 손을 따뜻하게 꽉 쥐는 것 말고는 그것을 모르는 척했다."]], [["“Anyhow, my dear Handel,”", "\"어쨌든, 사랑하는 핸들.\""], ["said he presently, “soldiering won’t do.", "그가 이윽고 말했다. \"군인 노릇은 안 될 일이야."], ["If you were to renounce this patronage and these favours, I suppose you would do so with some faint hope of one day repaying what you have already had.", "만약 자네가 이 후원과 이 호의를 마다한다면, 짐작건대 자네는 이미 받은 것을 언젠가 갚으리라는 희미한 희망이라도 품고 그렇게 하겠지."], ["Not very strong, that hope, if you went soldiering!", "자네가 군인이 되어 버린다면 그 희망이 그리 든든할 리가 없잖아!"], ["Besides, it’s absurd.", "게다가 그건 터무니없는 짓이야."], ["You would be infinitely better in Clarriker’s house, small as it is.", "자네는 클래리커 상회가 작긴 해도 거기 있는 편이 한없이 나을 거야."], ["I am working up towards a partnership, you know.”", "알다시피 나는 동업자가 되려고 노력하는 중이고 말이야.\""]], [["Poor fellow!", "가엾은 친구!"], ["He little suspected with whose money.", "그는 그게 누구의 돈인지 거의 짐작도 못하고 있었다."]], [["“But there is another question,”", "\"하지만 또 다른 문제가 있어.\""], ["said Herbert.", "허버트가 말했다."], ["“This is an ignorant, determined man, who has long had one fixed idea.", "\"이 사람은 무지하고 고집 센 사람이고, 오랫동안 단 하나의 고정관념을 품어 온 사람이야."], ["More than that, he seems to me (I may misjudge him) to be a man of a desperate and fierce character.”", "그뿐만 아니라, 내가 보기엔 (내가 잘못 판단하는 걸지도 모르지만) 그는 필사적이고 사나운 성격의 사람인 것 같아.\""]], [["“I know he is,”", "\"그래, 맞아.\""], ["I returned.", "내가 대답했다."], ["“Let me tell you what evidence I have seen of it.”", "\"내가 그걸 어떤 증거로 봤는지 말해 줄게.\""], ["And I told him what I had not mentioned in my narrative, of that encounter with the other convict.", "그리고 나는 내 이야기에서 언급하지 않았던 것, 그 다른 죄수와 맞붙었던 일을 그에게 들려주었다."]], [["“See, then,” said Herbert; “think of this!", "\"그렇다면 봐.\" 허버트가 말했다."], ["He comes here at the peril of his life, for the realisation of his fixed idea.", "\"이걸 생각해 봐! 그는 자신의 고정관념을 실현하기 위해 목숨을 걸고 여기 온 거야."], ["In the moment of realisation, after all his toil and waiting, you cut the ground from under his feet, destroy his idea, and make his gains worthless to him.", "그 모든 고생과 기다림 끝에, 막상 실현되는 순간에 자네가 그의 발밑 땅을 무너뜨리고, 그의 관념을 부수고, 그가 얻은 것을 그에게 무가치하게 만들어 버린다면."], ["Do you see nothing that he might do, under the disappointment?”", "그 실망 속에서 그가 무슨 짓을 저지를지 짐작되는 게 없어?\""]], [["“I have seen it, Herbert, and dreamed of it, ever since the fatal night of his arrival.", "\"짐작하고 있어, 허버트, 게다가 그게 도착한 그 불길한 밤 이후로 줄곧 그 꿈까지 꾸고 있어."], ["Nothing has been in my thoughts so distinctly as his putting himself in the way of being taken.”", "그가 스스로 잡힐 길로 뛰어드는 것만큼 내 머릿속에 또렷이 박혀 있는 건 없었어.\""]], [["“Then you may rely upon it,”", "\"그렇다면 믿어도 좋아.\""], ["said Herbert, “that there would be great danger of his doing it.", "허버트가 말했다. \"그가 그런 짓을 저지를 위험이 크다는 걸."], ["That is his power over you as long as he remains in England, and that would be his reckless course if you forsook him.”", "그게 그가 잉글랜드에 머무는 한 자네를 옭아매는 힘이고, 자네가 그를 저버린다면 그가 택할 무모한 행보일 거야.\""]], [["I was so struck by the horror of this idea, which had weighed upon me from the first, and the working out of which would make me regard myself, in some sort, as his murderer, that I could not rest in my chair, but began pacing to and fro.", "처음부터 나를 짓눌러 온 이 생각의 끔찍함에, 그리고 그것이 현실로 이루어진다면 내가 어떤 의미에서는 나 자신을 그의 살인자로 여기게 되리라는 데에 너무도 충격을 받은 나머지, 나는 의자에 가만히 앉아 있을 수가 없어 이리저리 서성이기 시작했다."], ["I said to Herbert, meanwhile, that even if Provis were recognised and taken, in spite of himself, I should be wretched as the cause, however innocently. Yes; even though I was so wretched in having him at large and near me, and even though I would far rather have worked at the forge all the days of my life than I would ever have come to this!", "그러는 동안 나는 허버트에게, 설령 프로비스가 본인의 뜻과 상관없이 신원이 드러나 잡힌다 해도, 내가 그 원인이라는 점에서, 아무리 죄가 없다 해도 나는 비참할 것이라고 말했다. 그렇다. 그를 풀어 둔 채 가까이 두는 것이 그토록 비참하고, 또 이 지경에 이르느니 차라리 평생을 그 대장간에서 일하는 편이 훨씬 나았으리라 여기는데도 말이다!"]], [["But there was no staving off the question, What was to be done?", "그러나 어떻게 할 것인가, 하는 문제를 미뤄 둘 길은 없었다."]], [["“The first and the main thing to be done,”", "\"제일 먼저, 그리고 가장 중요하게 해야 할 일은.\""], ["said Herbert, “is to get him out of England.", "허버트가 말했다. \"그를 잉글랜드 밖으로 빼내는 거야."], ["You will have to go with him, and then he may be induced to go.”", "자네가 그와 함께 가야 할 거고, 그래야 그도 떠나도록 설득될 테니까.\""]], [["“But get him where I will, could I prevent his coming back?”", "\"하지만 그를 어디로 데려가든, 그가 돌아오는 걸 내가 막을 수 있을까?\""]], [["“My good Handel, is it not obvious that with Newgate in the next street, there must be far greater hazard in your breaking your mind to him and making him reckless, here, than elsewhere?", "\"착한 핸들, 바로 옆 거리에 뉴게이트 감옥이 있는데, 자네 속내를 그에게 털어놓아 그를 무모하게 만드는 것이 여기서가 다른 어디서보다 훨씬 더 큰 위험을 안긴다는 게 뻔하지 않나?"], ["If a pretext to get him away could be made out of that other convict, or out of anything else in his life, now.”", "그 다른 죄수 일이든, 아니면 그의 인생에 있었던 그 무엇이든을 구실 삼아 그를 떠나보낼 핑계를 만들어 낼 수 있다면 말이야, 지금.\""]], [["“There, again!”", "\"그것도 또 그래!\""], ["said I, stopping before Herbert, with my open hands held out, as if they contained the desperation of the case. “I know nothing of his life.", "내가 두 손을 펼쳐 마치 그 안에 이 사태의 절망이 담겨 있기라도 한 듯 허버트 앞에 멈춰 서서 말했다. \"나는 그의 인생에 대해 아무것도 몰라."], ["It has almost made me mad to sit here of a night and see him before me, so bound up with my fortunes and misfortunes, and yet so unknown to me, except as the miserable wretch who terrified me two days in my childhood!”", "밤마다 여기 앉아, 내 행운과 불운에 그토록 얽혀 있으면서도, 어린 시절 이틀 동안 나를 공포에 떨게 한 가엾은 비참한 인간이라는 것 말고는 그토록 알려진 게 없는 그를 눈앞에 두고 보고 있노라면, 거의 미칠 지경이었어!\""]], [["Herbert got up, and linked his arm in mine, and we slowly walked to and fro together, studying the carpet.", "허버트는 일어나 내 팔에 자기 팔을 끼웠고, 우리는 카펫을 들여다보며 천천히 함께 이리저리 거닐었다."]], [["“Handel,”", "\"핸들.\""], ["said Herbert, stopping, “you feel convinced that you can take no further benefits from him; do you?”", "허버트가 멈춰 서서 말했다. \"자네는 그에게서 더 이상 아무 도움도 받을 수 없다고 확신하지, 그렇지?\""]], [["“Fully. Surely you would, too, if you were in my place?”", "\"그래, 전적으로. 자네가 내 처지라도 분명 그러지 않겠어?\""]], [["“And you feel convinced that you must break with him?”", "\"그리고 자네는 그와 인연을 끊어야 한다고 확신하지?\""]], [["“Herbert, can you ask me?”", "\"허버트, 그걸 물어볼 게 있어?\""]], [["“And you have, and are bound to have, that tenderness for the life he has risked on your account, that you must save him, if possible, from throwing it away.", "\"그리고 자네는 그가 자네를 위해 걸었던 목숨에 대해, 그 다정함을 품고 있고, 또 품을 수밖에 없으니, 가능하다면 그가 그 목숨을 내던지지 않게 구해야 해."], ["Then you must get him out of England before you stir a finger to extricate yourself.", "그렇다면 자네 자신을 빼내려고 손가락 하나 까딱하기 전에, 먼저 그를 잉글랜드 밖으로 빼내야 해."], ["That done, extricate yourself, in Heaven’s name, and we’ll see it out together, dear old boy.”", "그 일이 끝나거든, 부디 자네 자신을 빼내게. 그러면 우리 함께 끝까지 헤쳐 나가는 거야, 사랑하는 친구.\""]], [["It was a comfort to shake hands upon it, and walk up and down again, with only that done.", "그 일에 대해 악수를 나누고, 그것만이라도 정해 두고 다시 이리저리 거닐 수 있다는 것은 위안이 되었다."]], [["“Now, Herbert,”", "\"자, 허버트.\""], ["said I, “with reference to gaining some knowledge of his history.", "내가 말했다. \"그의 내력을 좀 알아내는 일 말인데."], ["There is but one way that I know of. I must ask him point blank.”", "내가 아는 길은 단 하나뿐이야. 그에게 단도직입적으로 물어보는 수밖에 없어.\""]], [["“Yes. Ask him,”", "\"그래. 물어봐.\""], ["said Herbert, “when we sit at breakfast in the morning.”", "허버트가 말했다. \"내일 아침 식사 자리에 앉았을 때.\""], ["For he had said, on taking leave of Herbert, that he would come to breakfast with us.", "그가 허버트에게 작별을 고하면서 우리와 함께 아침을 먹으러 오겠다고 했던 것이다."]], [["With this project formed, we went to bed.", "이 계획을 세우고 우리는 잠자리에 들었다."], ["I had the wildest dreams concerning him, and woke unrefreshed; I woke, too, to recover the fear which I had lost in the night, of his being found out as a returned transport.", "나는 그에 관한 더없이 어수선한 꿈을 꾸었고, 개운치 않은 채로 잠에서 깼다. 또한 잠에서 깨면서, 밤사이 잊고 있었던 두려움, 그가 돌아온 유형수임이 발각될지 모른다는 두려움이 되살아났다."], ["Waking, I never lost that fear.", "깨어 있는 동안 나는 그 두려움을 결코 떨치지 못했다."]], [["He came round at the appointed time, took out his jackknife, and sat down to his meal.", "그는 약속한 시각에 와서 잭나이프를 꺼내 들고 식사 앞에 앉았다."], ["He was full of plans “for his gentleman’s coming out strong, and like a gentleman,” and urged me to begin speedily upon the pocket-book which he had left in my possession.", "그는 \"자기 신사가 보란 듯이, 신사답게 두각을 나타낼\" 갖가지 계획으로 가득 차 있었고, 자기가 내게 맡겨 둔 그 수첩을 어서 쓰기 시작하라고 재촉했다."], ["He considered the chambers and his own lodging as temporary residences, and advised me to look out at once for a “fashionable crib” near Hyde Park, in which he could have “a shake-down.”", "그는 이 거처와 자기 숙소를 임시 거처로 여겼으며, 하이드 파크 근처에 자기가 \"하룻밤 묵을 자리\"를 둘 수 있는 \"근사한 처소\"를 당장 알아보라고 권했다."], ["When he had made an end of his breakfast, and was wiping his knife on his leg, I said to him, without a word of preface,—", "그가 아침 식사를 끝내고 칼을 자기 다리에 닦고 있을 때, 나는 아무런 서두도 없이 그에게 말을 꺼냈다."]], [["“After you were gone last night, I told my friend of the struggle that the soldiers found you engaged in on the marshes, when we came up.", "\"어젯밤 아저씨가 가신 뒤에, 우리가 다가갔을 때 군인들이 습지에서 아저씨가 맞붙어 있는 걸 발견했던 그 몸싸움 이야기를 친구한테 했어요."], ["You remember?”", "기억하시죠?\""]], [["“Remember!”", "\"기억하느냐고!\""], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."], ["“I think so!”", "\"당연하지!\""]], [["“We want to know something about that man—and about you.", "\"우리는 그 사내에 대해 — 그리고 아저씨에 대해 좀 알고 싶어요."], ["It is strange to know no more about either, and particularly you, than I was able to tell last night.", "어느 쪽에 대해서든, 특히 아저씨에 대해 어젯밤 내가 말할 수 있었던 것보다 더 아는 게 없다는 게 이상해요."], ["Is not this as good a time as another for our knowing more?”", "우리가 더 아는 데 지금만 한 때가 또 있을까요?\""]], [["“Well!”", "\"그래!\""], ["he said, after consideration.", "그가 한참 생각한 끝에 말했다."], ["“You’re on your oath, you know, Pip’s comrade?”", "\"자네는 맹세를 한 몸이지, 그렇지, 핍의 동무?\""]], [["“Assuredly,”", "\"틀림없습니다.\""], ["replied Herbert.", "허버트가 대답했다."]], [["“As to anything I say, you know,”", "\"내가 무슨 말을 하든 말이야, 알겠지.\""], ["he insisted. “The oath applies to all.”", "그가 다짐을 두었다. \"그 맹세는 전부에 적용돼.\""]], [["“I understand it to do so.”", "\"그렇게 알아듣고 있습니다.\""]], [["“And look’ee here! Wotever I done is worked out and paid for,”", "\"그리고 이봐! 내가 무슨 짓을 했든 그건 다 형을 치르고 값을 치른 거야.\""], ["he insisted again.", "그가 다시 다짐을 두었다."]], [["“So be it.”", "\"그렇게 하지요.\""]], [["He took out his black pipe and was going to fill it with negro-head, when, looking at the tangle of tobacco in his hand, he seemed to think it might perplex the thread of his narrative.", "그는 검은 파이프를 꺼내 흑인 머리 담배를 채워 넣으려다가, 손안에 엉킨 담배를 들여다보더니 그것이 자기 이야기의 가닥을 헝클어뜨릴지도 모른다고 여기는 듯했다."], ["He put it back again, stuck his pipe in a button-hole of his coat, spread a hand on each knee, and after turning an angry eye on the fire for a few silent moments, looked round at us and said what follows.", "그는 그것을 도로 집어넣고, 파이프를 외투 단춧구멍에 꽂은 다음, 양 무릎에 한 손씩 펼쳐 얹고는, 잠시 말없이 성난 눈길을 난롯불에 던지고 나서, 우리를 둘러보며 다음과 같이 말했다."]], [["# Chapter XLII.", "# Chapter XLII."]], [["“Dear boy and Pip’s comrade. I am not a-going fur to tell you my life like a song, or a story-book.", "「사랑하는 도련님, 그리고 핍의 친구분. 나는 노래나 이야기책처럼 내 인생을 늘어놓을 생각은 없소."], ["But to give it you short and handy, I’ll put it at once into a mouthful of English.", "다만 짤막하고 손쉽게 일러 드리려고, 단숨에 영어 한 입거리로 줄여 보겠소."], ["In jail and out of jail, in jail and out of jail, in jail and out of jail.", "감옥에 들어갔다 나오고, 들어갔다 나오고, 또 들어갔다 나오고."], ["There, you’ve got it.", "자, 다 들으셨소."], ["That’s my life pretty much, down to such times as I got shipped off, arter Pip stood my friend.", "그게 대체로 내 인생이오, 핍이 내 편이 되어 주고 나서 배에 실려 보내질 때까지 말이오."]], [["“I’ve been done everything to, pretty well—except hanged.", "「나는 거의 별의별 일을 다 당했소. 목 매달리는 것만 빼고."], ["I’ve been locked up as much as a silver tea-kittle.", "은 찻주전자처럼 자주 갇혀 있었지."], ["I’ve been carted here and carted there, and put out of this town, and put out of that town, and stuck in the stocks, and whipped and worried and drove.", "이리 끌려가고 저리 끌려가고, 이 마을에서 쫓겨나고 저 마을에서 쫓겨나고, 차꼬에 채워지고, 매질당하고, 시달리고, 내몰렸소."], ["I’ve no more notion where I was born than you have—if so much.", "내가 어디서 태어났는지는 당신들이 아는 만큼도 모르오 — 그만큼도 못 되지."], ["I first become aware of myself down in Essex, a thieving turnips for my living.", "나는 에식스 지방에서, 먹고살려고 순무를 훔치다가 처음으로 나라는 존재를 깨달았소."], ["Summun had run away from me—a man—a tinker—and he’d took the fire with him, and left me wery cold.", "누군가가 내게서 달아났더군 — 어떤 남자 — 땜장이였는데 — 그자가 불씨를 가지고 가 버려서, 나를 몹시 춥게 내버려 두었지."]], [["“I know’d my name to be Magwitch, chrisen’d Abel.", "「내 이름이 매그위치고, 에이블이라고 세례받았다는 건 알았소."], ["How did I know it?", "그걸 어떻게 알았느냐고?"], ["Much as I know’d the birds’ names in the hedges to be chaffinch, sparrer, thrush.", "산울타리의 새 이름이 되새, 참새, 개똥지빠귀라는 걸 아는 정도로 알았던 거요."], ["I might have thought it was all lies together, only as the birds’ names come out true, I supposed mine did.", "그게 다 거짓말일 수도 있다고 생각할 뻔했지만, 새 이름들이 다 참인 걸 보니 내 이름도 참이려니 했소."]], [["“So fur as I could find, there warn’t a soul that see young Abel Magwitch, with us little on him as in him, but wot caught fright at him, and either drove him off, or took him up.", "「내가 알아낼 수 있는 한, 몸뚱이에 걸친 것이라곤 그 속에 든 것만큼이나 변변찮은 이 어린 에이블 매그위치를 본 사람치고, 그 아이를 보고 겁먹지 않은 영혼은 하나도 없었소. 그래서 그들은 나를 쫓아 버리거나 잡아들였지."], ["I was took up, took up, took up, to that extent that I reg’larly grow’d up took up.", "나는 잡혀가고, 잡혀가고, 또 잡혀가서, 그야말로 잡혀가며 자란 셈이오."]], [["“This is the way it was, that when I was a ragged little creetur as much to be pitied as ever I see (not that I looked in the glass, for there warn’t many insides of furnished houses known to me), I got the name of being hardened.", "「사정은 이러했소. 내가 여태 본 그 누구 못지않게 가엾은, 누더기를 걸친 어린것이었을 적에(거울을 들여다본 건 아니오. 가구가 갖춰진 집 안을 내가 본 적이 별로 없었으니까), 나는 천성이 모진 놈이라는 평판을 얻었소."], ["‘This is a terrible hardened one,’ they says to prison wisitors, picking out me.", "‘이놈은 지독하게 모진 놈입니다.’ 그들은 감옥 방문객들에게 나를 콕 집어 그리 말했지."], ["‘May be said to live in jails, this boy.’ Then they looked at me, and I looked at them, and they measured my head, some on ’em,—they had better a measured my stomach,—and others on ’em giv me tracts what I couldn’t read, and made me speeches what I couldn’t understand.", "‘이 아이는 감옥에서 산다고 해도 될 지경입니다.’ 그러면 그들은 나를 보고, 나는 그들을 보고, 그중 몇몇은 내 머리 크기를 재고 — 차라리 내 뱃속이나 잴 것이지 — 또 몇몇은 내가 읽지도 못하는 전도책자를 주고, 알아듣지도 못하는 연설을 늘어놓았소."], ["They always went on agen me about the Devil. But what the Devil was I to do?", "그들은 늘 악마 이야기로 나를 몰아붙였지. 한데 대체 이 악마 같은 처지에 내가 뭘 어쩌란 말이오?"], ["I must put something into my stomach, mustn’t I?—Howsomever, I’m a getting low, and I know what’s due.", "뱃속에 뭐라도 채워 넣어야 하지 않겠소? — 그건 그렇고, 내 말이 점점 처량해지는구려, 도리는 안다오."], ["Dear boy and Pip’s comrade, don’t you be afeerd of me being low.", "사랑하는 도련님, 그리고 핍의 친구분, 내가 처량해질까 봐 두려워 마시오."]], [["“Tramping, begging, thieving, working sometimes when I could,—though that warn’t as often as you may think, till you put the question whether you would ha’ been over-ready to give me work yourselves,—a bit of a poacher, a bit of a labourer, a bit of a wagoner, a bit of a haymaker, a bit of a hawker, a bit of most things that don’t pay and lead to trouble, I got to be a man.", "「떠돌고, 구걸하고, 훔치고, 할 수 있을 땐 이따금 일도 하면서 — 하긴 당신들이 생각하는 것만큼 자주는 아니었지. 당신들 스스로가 나 같은 놈한테 일거리를 선뜻 내줄 마음이 있었겠는지 한번 따져 보면 알 거요 — 약간은 밀렵꾼, 약간은 막일꾼, 약간은 짐마차꾼, 약간은 건초꾼, 약간은 행상꾼, 돈 안 되고 말썽만 부르는 거의 모든 일을 조금씩 하면서, 나는 어른이 되었소."], ["A deserting soldier in a Traveller’s Rest, what lay hid up to the chin under a lot of taturs, learnt me to read; and a travelling Giant what signed his name at a penny a time learnt me to write.", "‘나그네의 쉼터’라는 데서, 감자 무더기 밑에 턱까지 숨어 있던 탈영병 하나가 내게 글 읽는 법을 가르쳐 주었고, 한 번에 한 푼씩 받고 제 이름을 써 주던 떠돌이 거인 하나가 글 쓰는 법을 가르쳐 주었지."], ["I warn’t locked up as often now as formerly, but I wore out my good share of key-metal still.", "이제 예전만큼 자주 갇히지는 않았지만, 그래도 자물쇠 쇠붙이는 여전히 내 몫만큼 닳게 했소."]], [["“At Epsom races, a matter of over twenty years ago, I got acquainted wi’ a man whose skull I’d crack wi’ this poker, like the claw of a lobster, if I’d got it on this hob.", "「이십 년도 더 전에 엡섬 경마장에서, 나는 한 사내와 알게 되었소. 만약 그자의 머리통이 지금 이 화로 받침대 위에 있다면, 나는 이 부지깽이로 바닷가재 집게발 부수듯 박살 내고 말 거요."], ["His right name was Compeyson; and that’s the man, dear boy, what you see me a pounding in the ditch, according to what you truly told your comrade arter I was gone last night.", "그자의 본명은 콤피슨이었소. 그리고 그자가 바로, 사랑하는 도련님, 어젯밤 내가 떠난 뒤에 당신이 친구분께 사실대로 말한 대로, 내가 도랑에서 두들겨 패던 그 사내요."]], [["“He set up fur a gentleman, this Compeyson, and he’d been to a public boarding-school and had learning.", "「이 콤피슨이라는 자는 신사 행세를 했소. 사립 기숙학교에 다녔고 배운 것도 있었지."], ["He was a smooth one to talk, and was a dab at the ways of gentlefolks.", "말솜씨가 매끄러웠고, 상류층의 몸가짐에도 능했소."], ["He was good-looking too.", "인물도 잘났고."], ["It was the night afore the great race, when I found him on the heath, in a booth that I know’d on.", "큰 경마가 열리기 전날 밤, 나는 황야에서, 내가 잘 아는 어느 천막 술집에서 그자를 만났소."], ["Him and some more was a sitting among the tables when I went in, and the landlord (which had a knowledge of me, and was a sporting one) called him out, and said, ‘I think this is a man that might suit you,’—meaning I was.", "내가 들어갔을 때 그자와 몇몇이 탁자 사이에 앉아 있었는데, 주인이(그자는 나를 알았고, 노름을 즐기는 위인이었지) 그자를 불러내며 말했소. ‘이 사람이 당신한테 쓸모가 있을 것 같소만.’ — 나를 두고 한 말이었지."]], [["“Compeyson, he looks at me very noticing, and I look at him.", "「콤피슨, 그자는 나를 아주 유심히 뜯어보고, 나도 그자를 봤소."], ["He has a watch and a chain and a ring and a breast-pin and a handsome suit of clothes.", "그자는 시계와 시곗줄과 반지와 가슴 핀에다, 말쑥한 옷 한 벌을 갖추고 있었소."]], [["“‘To judge from appearances, you’re out of luck,’ says Compeyson to me.", "「‘겉모습으로 보아하니, 당신은 운이 없는 사람이로군.’ 콤피슨이 내게 말했소."]], [["“‘Yes, master, and I’ve never been in it much.’ (I had come out of Kingston Jail last on a vagrancy committal. Not but what it might have been for something else; but it warn’t.)", "「‘그렇소, 나리, 운이라곤 별로 누려 본 적이 없지요.’(나는 마지막으로 부랑죄 송치로 킹스턴 감옥에서 막 나온 참이었소. 다른 죄목일 수도 있었겠지만, 그건 아니었지.)"]], [["“‘Luck changes,’ says Compeyson; ‘perhaps yours is going to change.’", "「‘운은 바뀌는 법이지.’ 콤피슨이 말했소. ‘어쩌면 당신 운도 바뀌려나 보군.’"]], [["“I says, ‘I hope it may be so.", "「나는 말했소. ‘그랬으면 좋겠소."], ["There’s room.’", "바뀔 여지는 충분하니까.’"]], [["“‘What can you do?’ says Compeyson.", "「‘당신, 뭘 할 줄 아시오?’ 콤피슨이 물었소."]], [["“‘Eat and drink,’ I says; ‘if you’ll find the materials.’", "「‘먹고 마시는 거요.’ 내가 말했소. ‘당신이 그 재료를 대 준다면 말이오.’"]], [["“Compeyson laughed, looked at me again very noticing, giv me five shillings, and appointed me for next night.", "「콤피슨은 웃더니, 다시 나를 아주 유심히 뜯어보고는, 내게 오 실링을 주고 다음 날 밤에 만나자고 약속했소."], ["Same place.", "같은 장소에서."]], [["“I went to Compeyson next night, same place, and Compeyson took me on to be his man and pardner.", "「나는 다음 날 밤 같은 장소로 콤피슨을 찾아갔고, 콤피슨은 나를 제 부하이자 동업자로 거두었소."], ["And what was Compeyson’s business in which we was to go pardners?", "그래, 우리가 동업하기로 한 콤피슨의 사업이란 게 뭐였느냐고?"], ["Compeyson’s business was the swindling, handwriting forging, stolen bank-note passing, and such-like.", "콤피슨의 사업은 사기, 필적 위조, 훔친 지폐 유통, 그런 따위였소."], ["All sorts of traps as Compeyson could set with his head, and keep his own legs out of and get the profits from and let another man in for, was Compeyson’s business.", "콤피슨이 제 머리로 꾸밀 수 있는 온갖 덫, 제 다리는 거기서 빼고, 이익은 챙기고, 다른 놈은 걸려들게 하는 그런 덫이 바로 콤피슨의 사업이었지."], ["He’d no more heart than a iron file, he was as cold as death, and he had the head of the Devil afore mentioned.", "그자는 쇠줄칼만큼도 인정이 없었고, 죽음처럼 차가웠으며, 앞서 말한 악마의 머리를 가진 자였소."]], [["“There was another in with Compeyson, as was called Arthur,—not as being so chrisen’d, but as a surname.", "「콤피슨과 한패인 자가 또 하나 있었는데, 아서라고 불렀소 — 세례명이 아니라 성이었지."], ["He was in a Decline, and was a shadow to look at.", "그자는 폐병을 앓고 있어서, 보기에 그림자나 다름없었소."], ["Him and Compeyson had been in a bad thing with a rich lady some years afore, and they’d made a pot of money by it; but Compeyson betted and gamed, and he’d have run through the king’s taxes.", "그자와 콤피슨은 몇 해 전에 어느 부유한 부인을 상대로 못된 짓을 벌여, 그걸로 큰돈을 한밑천 잡았더랬소. 한데 콤피슨이 노름과 도박에 빠져, 임금의 세금까지도 탕진할 위인이었지."], ["So, Arthur was a dying, and a dying poor and with the horrors on him, and Compeyson’s wife (which Compeyson kicked mostly) was a having pity on him when she could, and Compeyson was a having pity on nothing and nobody.", "그래서 아서는 죽어 가고 있었소. 가난뱅이로, 헛것에 시달리며 죽어 가고 있었지. 콤피슨의 아내는(콤피슨이 걸핏하면 발길질하던 여자였소) 할 수 있을 때면 그자를 가엾이 여겼지만, 콤피슨은 그 무엇도, 그 누구도 가엾이 여기는 법이 없었소."]], [["“I might a took warning by Arthur, but I didn’t; and I won’t pretend I was partick’ler—for where ’ud be the good on it, dear boy and comrade?", "「나는 아서를 보고 경계할 수도 있었으련만, 그러지 않았소. 그렇다고 내가 까다롭게 굴었던 척하진 않겠소 — 그래 봤자 무슨 소용이겠소, 사랑하는 도련님, 그리고 친구분?"], ["So I begun wi’ Compeyson, and a poor tool I was in his hands. Arthur lived at the top of Compeyson’s house (over nigh Brentford it was), and Compeyson kept a careful account agen him for board and lodging, in case he should ever get better to work it out.", "그렇게 나는 콤피슨과 손을 잡았고, 그자 손아귀에서 한낱 변변찮은 도구 노릇을 했지. 아서는 콤피슨네 집 꼭대기 층에 살았는데(브렌트퍼드 근처였소), 콤피슨은 그자가 혹 병이 나아 일해서 갚을 때를 대비해, 숙식비를 빈틈없이 셈해 두었소."], ["But Arthur soon settled the account.", "한데 아서는 곧 그 셈을 다 치러 버렸지."], ["The second or third time as ever I see him, he come a tearing down into Compeyson’s parlour late at night, in only a flannel gown, with his hair all in a sweat, and he says to Compeyson’s wife, ‘Sally, she really is upstairs alonger me, now, and I can’t get rid of her.", "내가 그자를 처음 본 지 두세 번째쯤 되던 때였는데, 그자가 밤늦게 플란넬 잠옷 하나만 걸친 채, 머리는 온통 땀에 젖어, 콤피슨네 거실로 허둥지둥 뛰어 내려와 콤피슨의 아내에게 말했소. ‘샐리, 그 여자가 지금 위층에서 정말로 나랑 같이 있는데, 도무지 떼어 낼 수가 없어."], ["She’s all in white,’ he says, ‘wi’ white flowers in her hair, and she’s awful mad, and she’s got a shroud hanging over her arm, and she says she’ll put it on me at five in the morning.’", "온통 하얀 옷을 입고,’ 그자가 말하기를, ‘머리엔 흰 꽃을 꽂고, 무섭도록 미쳐서, 팔에 수의를 걸치고는, 새벽 다섯 시에 그걸 내게 입히겠다는 거야.’"]], [["“Says Compeyson: ‘Why, you fool, don’t you know she’s got a living body?", "「콤피슨이 말했소. ‘이런 멍청한 놈, 그 여자가 멀쩡히 살아 있는 몸이란 걸 모르나?"], ["And how should she be up there, without coming through the door, or in at the window, and up the stairs?’", "게다가 문으로 들어오지도, 창문으로 들어오지도, 계단을 올라오지도 않고서 어떻게 거기 위에 있단 말이야?’"]], [["“‘I don’t know how she’s there,’ says Arthur, shivering dreadful with the horrors, ‘but she’s standing in the corner at the foot of the bed, awful mad.", "「‘어떻게 거기 있는지는 나도 몰라.’ 아서가 헛것에 무섭게 떨면서 말했소. ‘하지만 그 여자가 침대 발치 구석에 서 있다고, 무섭도록 미친 채로."], ["And over where her heart’s broke—you broke it!—there’s drops of blood.’", "그리고 그 여자 심장이 부서진 데 — 당신이 부쉈잖아! — 거기서 핏방울이 떨어진단 말이야.’"]], [["“Compeyson spoke hardy, but he was always a coward.", "「콤피슨은 짐짓 거칠게 말했지만, 본래 늘 겁쟁이였소."], ["‘Go up alonger this drivelling sick man,’ he says to his wife, ‘and Magwitch, lend her a hand, will you?’ But he never come nigh himself.", "‘이 헛소리하는 병자랑 같이 위로 올라가 봐.’ 그자는 제 아내에게 말했소. ‘그리고 매그위치, 자네도 좀 거들어 주겠나?’ 한데 그자 자신은 결코 가까이 오지 않았지."]], [["“Compeyson’s wife and me took him up to bed agen, and he raved most dreadful.", "「콤피슨의 아내와 나는 그자를 다시 침대로 데려갔는데, 그자는 무섭도록 헛소리를 해 댔소."], ["‘Why look at her!’ he cries out.", "‘아, 저것 좀 봐!’ 그자가 외쳤소."], ["‘She’s a shaking the shroud at me! Don’t you see her?", "‘저 여자가 나한테 수의를 흔들고 있어! 안 보여?"], ["Look at her eyes!", "저 눈 좀 봐!"], ["Ain’t it awful to see her so mad?’ Next he cries, ‘She’ll put it on me, and then I’m done for!", "저렇게 미친 꼴을 보는 게 끔찍하지 않아?’ 그러더니 외치기를, ‘저 여자가 그걸 내게 입힐 거야, 그럼 난 끝장이야!"], ["Take it away from her, take it away!’ And then he catched hold of us, and kep on a talking to her, and answering of her, till I half believed I see her myself.", "저 여자한테서 그걸 치워, 치워 버려!’ 그러고는 우리를 붙들고, 그 여자한테 계속 말을 걸며 대꾸까지 해 대니, 나도 그 여자가 정말 보이는 것만 같았소."]], [["“Compeyson’s wife, being used to him, giv him some liquor to get the horrors off, and by and by he quieted. ‘O, she’s gone!", "「콤피슨의 아내는 그자에게 이골이 난지라, 헛것을 떨쳐 내라고 술을 좀 먹였더니, 그자는 차츰 잠잠해졌소. ‘오, 그 여자가 갔어!"], ["Has her keeper been for her?’ he says.", "그 여자 간수가 데리러 왔던가?’ 그자가 물었소."], ["‘Yes,’ says Compeyson’s wife.", "‘그래요.’ 콤피슨의 아내가 말했지."], ["‘Did you tell him to lock her and bar her in?’ ‘Yes.’ ‘And to take that ugly thing away from her?’ ‘Yes, yes, all right.’ ‘You’re a good creetur,’ he says, ‘don’t leave me, whatever you do, and thank you!’", "‘그 여자를 가두고 빗장 채우라고 일렀어?’ ‘그럼요.’ ‘그리고 그 흉측한 물건도 치우라고?’ ‘그럼요, 그럼, 다 됐어요.’ ‘당신은 참 착한 사람이야.’ 그자가 말했소. ‘무슨 일이 있어도 날 떠나지 마, 그리고 고마워!’"]], [["“He rested pretty quiet till it might want a few minutes of five, and then he starts up with a scream, and screams out, ‘Here she is!", "「그자는 다섯 시가 몇 분쯤 못 미쳤을 무렵까지 꽤 잠잠히 쉬고 있다가, 갑자기 비명을 지르며 벌떡 일어나 외쳤소."], ["She’s got the shroud again.", "‘여기 왔어! 또 수의를 들었어."], ["She’s unfolding it.", "그걸 펼치고 있어."], ["She’s coming out of the corner.", "구석에서 나오고 있어."], ["She’s coming to the bed. Hold me, both on you—one of each side—don’t let her touch me with it. Hah!", "침대로 다가와. 날 붙들어, 둘이서 — 양쪽에서 한 명씩 — 그걸로 날 건드리지 못하게 해. 하!"], ["she missed me that time.", "이번엔 빗나갔어."], ["Don’t let her throw it over my shoulders.", "그걸 내 어깨에 못 걸치게 해."], ["Don’t let her lift me up to get it round me.", "그걸 둘러씌우려고 날 일으키지 못하게 해."], ["She’s lifting me up. Keep me down!’ Then he lifted himself up hard, and was dead.", "저 여자가 날 일으키고 있어. 날 눌러 둬!’ 그러더니 그자는 힘껏 제 몸을 일으키고는, 죽어 버렸소."]], [["“Compeyson took it easy as a good riddance for both sides.", "「콤피슨은 그걸 양쪽에 다 잘된 일이라며 대수롭잖게 받아들였소."], ["Him and me was soon busy, and first he swore me (being ever artful) on my own book,—this here little black book, dear boy, what I swore your comrade on.", "그자와 나는 곧 일에 바빠졌는데, 그자는 먼저(어디까지나 교활한 위인이라) 내게 직접 책에 대고 맹세를 시켰소 — 바로 이 작고 검은 책에 대고 말이오, 사랑하는 도련님, 당신 친구분께 맹세시킨 그 책 말이오."]], [["“Not to go into the things that Compeyson planned, and I done—which ’ud take a week—I’ll simply say to you, dear boy, and Pip’s comrade, that that man got me into such nets as made me his black slave.", "「콤피슨이 꾸미고 내가 해치운 일들을 일일이 늘어놓지는 않겠소 — 그러자면 일주일은 걸릴 테니 — 다만 이것만 말해 두겠소, 사랑하는 도련님, 그리고 핍의 친구분. 그자는 나를 그물에 옭아매어, 제 검은 노예로 부려 먹었소."], ["I was always in debt to him, always under his thumb, always a working, always a getting into danger.", "나는 늘 그자에게 빚을 졌고, 늘 그자 손아귀에 잡혀 있었고, 늘 일에 매여 있었고, 늘 위험에 빠져들었지."], ["He was younger than me, but he’d got craft, and he’d got learning, and he overmatched me five hundred times told and no mercy.", "그자는 나보다 어렸지만, 약삭빨랐고, 배운 것도 있어서, 나보다 오백 배는 더 나았고 인정사정이라곤 없었소."], ["My Missis as I had the hard time wi’—Stop though! I ain’t brought her in—”", "내가 그토록 모진 세월을 함께 보낸 마누라는 — 한데 잠깐! 나는 아직 그 여자 얘기는 꺼내지도 않았구려 — 」"]], [["He looked about him in a confused way, as if he had lost his place in the book of his remembrance; and he turned his face to the fire, and spread his hands broader on his knees, and lifted them off and put them on again.", "그는 마치 제 기억의 책에서 읽던 자리를 잃어버리기라도 한 듯, 어리둥절한 모양으로 주위를 둘러보았다. 그러더니 불 쪽으로 얼굴을 돌리고, 무릎 위에 두 손을 더 넓게 펴 놓았다가, 떼었다가, 다시 얹었다."]], [["“There ain’t no need to go into it,”", "「그 얘기까지 들어갈 건 없소.」"], ["he said, looking round once more.", "그는 다시 한번 둘러보며 말했다."], ["“The time wi’ Compeyson was a’most as hard a time as ever I had; that said, all’s said.", "「콤피슨과 함께한 세월은, 내가 여태 겪은 가장 모진 세월에 거의 맞먹었소. 그 말로 다 한 셈이지."], ["Did I tell you as I was tried, alone, for misdemeanor, while with Compeyson?”", "내가 콤피슨과 함께 있을 적에, 혼자서 경범죄로 재판받았던 일을 도련님께 말했던가요?」"]], [["I answered, No.", "나는 아니라고 대답했다."]], [["“Well!”", "「그래!」"], ["he said, “I was, and got convicted.", "그가 말했다. 「내가 그랬소."], ["As to took up on suspicion, that was twice or three times in the four or five year that it lasted; but evidence was wanting.", "유죄 판결을 받았지. 혐의를 받고 잡혀간 거로 치자면, 그게 사오 년 동안 두세 번은 됐소. 한데 증거가 없었지."], ["At last, me and Compeyson was both committed for felony,—on a charge of putting stolen notes in circulation,—and there was other charges behind.", "마침내 나와 콤피슨은 둘 다 중죄로 송치되었소 — 훔친 지폐를 유통한 혐의로 — 그리고 그 뒤에 다른 혐의들도 줄줄이 있었지."], ["Compeyson says to me, ‘Separate defences, no communication,’ and that was all.", "콤피슨이 내게 말합디다. ‘변론은 따로따로, 서로 연락하지 말 것.’ 그게 다였소."], ["And I was so miserable poor, that I sold all the clothes I had, except what hung on my back, afore I could get Jaggers.", "그리고 나는 어찌나 비참하게 가난했던지, 등에 걸친 것만 빼고 가진 옷을 죄다 팔고 나서야 재거스를 살 수 있었소."]], [["“When we was put in the dock, I noticed first of all what a gentleman Compeyson looked, wi’ his curly hair and his black clothes and his white pocket-handkercher, and what a common sort of a wretch I looked.", "「우리가 피고석에 세워졌을 때, 나는 무엇보다도 먼저, 콤피슨이 곱슬머리에 검은 옷에 흰 손수건을 갖춰, 얼마나 신사처럼 보이는지, 그리고 내가 얼마나 천한 비렁뱅이 꼴로 보이는지 알아챘소."], ["When the prosecution opened and the evidence was put short, aforehand, I noticed how heavy it all bore on me, and how light on him.", "검사 측이 사건을 펼치며 증거를 미리 짤막하게 늘어놓았을 때, 나는 그 모든 게 내게는 얼마나 무겁게, 그자에게는 얼마나 가볍게 걸리는지 알아챘지."], ["When the evidence was giv in the box, I noticed how it was always me that had come for’ard, and could be swore to, how it was always me that the money had been paid to, how it was always me that had seemed to work the thing and get the profit.", "증언대에서 증거가 제시될 때, 나는 늘 앞에 나서서 지목당할 수 있었던 게 나였고, 돈이 늘 건네진 게 나였고, 늘 그 일을 꾸미고 이익을 챙긴 것처럼 보인 게 나였다는 걸 알아챘소. 한데 변론에 들어가니, 그제야 그 수작이 더 또렷이 보입디다. 콤피슨의 변호사가 말하기를, ‘재판장님과 배심원 여러분, 여기 여러분 앞에 나란히 서 있는 두 사람이 있는데, 여러분의 눈에도 그 차이가 훤히 갈릴 것입니다."], ["But when the defence come on, then I see the plan plainer; for, says the counsellor for Compeyson, ‘My lord and gentlemen, here you has afore you, side by side, two persons as your eyes can separate wide; one, the younger, well brought up, who will be spoke to as such; one, the elder, ill brought up, who will be spoke to as such; one, the younger, seldom if ever seen in these here transactions, and only suspected; t’other, the elder, always seen in ’em and always wi’ his guilt brought home.", "한 사람은 더 젊고, 잘 자랐으니, 그에 걸맞게 다뤄질 것이요. 한 사람은 더 늙고, 못되게 자랐으니, 그에 걸맞게 다뤄질 것입니다. 한 사람, 더 젊은 쪽은 이런 거래에 거의 모습을 드러낸 적이 없고, 그저 의심만 받았을 뿐이며, 다른 한 사람, 더 늙은 쪽은 늘 거기 모습을 드러냈고 늘 그 죄가 들통났습니다."], ["Can you doubt, if there is but one in it, which is the one, and, if there is two in it, which is much the worst one?’ And such-like.", "이 일에 한 사람만 끼어 있다면 그게 누구인지, 또 두 사람이 끼어 있다면 어느 쪽이 훨씬 더 악질인지, 의심할 여지가 있겠습니까?’ 뭐 그런 따위였소."], ["And when it come to character, warn’t it Compeyson as had been to the school, and warn’t it his schoolfellows as was in this position and in that, and warn’t it him as had been know’d by witnesses in such clubs and societies, and nowt to his disadvantage?", "그리고 인품 얘기에 이르러서는, 학교에 다닌 게 콤피슨 아니던가? 이런 지위 저런 지위에 오른 옛 동창들이 있는 게 그자 아니던가? 이런저런 클럽이며 단체에서 증인들에게 알려져 있고, 흠잡힐 데라곤 없던 게 그자 아니던가?"], ["And warn’t it me as had been tried afore, and as had been know’d up hill and down dale in Bridewells and Lock-Ups!", "그리고 전에도 재판받은 적이 있고, 감화원이며 유치장에서 안 알려진 데가 없던 게 나 아니던가!"], ["And when it come to speech-making, warn’t it Compeyson as could speak to ’em wi’ his face dropping every now and then into his white pocket-handkercher,—ah!", "그리고 변론하는 데 이르러서는, 흰 손수건에 이따금 얼굴을 파묻어 가며 — 아! 게다가 연설 중에 시구절까지 섞어 가며 — 그자들에게 말할 수 있었던 게 콤피슨 아니던가?"], ["and wi’ verses in his speech, too,—and warn’t it me as could only say, ‘Gentlemen, this man at my side is a most precious rascal’?", "그리고 ‘여러분, 제 옆에 있는 이 사람은 더없이 고약한 악당이올시다’라는 말밖에 못 한 게 나 아니던가?"], ["And when the verdict come, warn’t it Compeyson as was recommended to mercy on account of good character and bad company, and giving up all the information he could agen me, and warn’t it me as got never a word but Guilty?", "그리고 평결이 내려졌을 때, 좋은 인품과 나쁜 친구를 잘못 만난 탓이라는 정상참작에다, 나에 대해 알 수 있는 정보는 죄다 넘긴 공로로 선처가 권고된 게 콤피슨 아니던가? 그리고 ‘유죄’라는 한마디밖에 못 들은 게 나 아니던가?"], ["And when I says to Compeyson, ‘Once out of this court, I’ll smash that face of yourn!’ ain’t it Compeyson as prays the Judge to be protected, and gets two turnkeys stood betwixt us?", "그리고 내가 콤피슨에게 ‘이 법정을 나가기만 해 봐라, 그 낯짝을 박살 내 주마!’라고 했을 때, 판사에게 보호를 청해 우리 사이에 간수 둘을 세우게 한 게 콤피슨 아니던가?"], ["And when we’re sentenced, ain’t it him as gets seven year, and me fourteen, and ain’t it him as the Judge is sorry for, because he might a done so well, and ain’t it me as the Judge perceives to be a old offender of wiolent passion, likely to come to worse?”", "그리고 우리가 선고를 받을 때, 칠 년을 받은 게 그자고, 십사 년을 받은 게 나 아니던가? 그자는 잘될 수도 있었던 자라며 판사가 안타까워한 게 그자고, 더 험한 꼴을 보기 십상인, 난폭한 성정의 상습범이라고 판사가 본 게 나 아니던가?」"]], [["He had worked himself into a state of great excitement, but he checked it, took two or three short breaths, swallowed as often, and stretching out his hand towards me said, in a reassuring manner, “I ain’t a-going to be low, dear boy!”", "그는 격한 흥분 상태로 스스로를 몰아넣었으나, 이내 그것을 억누르고, 두세 번 짧게 숨을 들이쉬고, 그만큼 자주 침을 삼키더니, 내게 손을 뻗으며 안심시키려는 투로 말했다. 「나는 처량해지지 않을 거요, 사랑하는 도련님!」"]], [["He had so heated himself that he took out his handkerchief and wiped his face and head and neck and hands, before he could go on.", "그는 어찌나 몸이 달아올랐던지, 말을 잇기 전에 손수건을 꺼내 얼굴과 머리와 목과 손을 닦아야 했다."]], [["“I had said to Compeyson that I’d smash that face of his, and I swore Lord smash mine!", "「나는 콤피슨에게 그 낯짝을 박살 내 주겠다고 말했고, 내 낯짝을 망할 신이 박살 내도 좋으니 기어이 해내겠다고 맹세했소."], ["to do it. We was in the same prison-ship, but I couldn’t get at him for long, though I tried.", "우리는 같은 죄수선에 실렸지만, 나는 한동안 그자에게 다가가지 못했소. 무던히도 애를 썼는데 말이오."], ["At last I come behind him and hit him on the cheek to turn him round and get a smashing one at him, when I was seen and seized.", "마침내 나는 그자 뒤로 다가가 뺨을 쳐서, 그자를 돌려세워 제대로 한 방 박살 내려 했지. 그 순간 들켜서 붙잡히고 말았소."], ["The black-hole of that ship warn’t a strong one, to a judge of black-holes that could swim and dive.", "헤엄치고 자맥질할 줄 아는, 영창에 도가 튼 자가 보기에 그 배의 영창은 그리 튼튼한 게 아니었소."], ["I escaped to the shore, and I was a hiding among the graves there, envying them as was in ’em and all over, when I first see my boy!”", "나는 뭍으로 달아나, 그 무덤들 사이에 숨어서, 그 안에 누워 모든 게 끝나 버린 자들을 부러워하고 있었지, 바로 그때 내 아들을 처음 봤던 거요!」"]], [["He regarded me with a look of affection that made him almost abhorrent to me again, though I had felt great pity for him.", "그는 다정한 눈길로 나를 바라보았는데, 나는 그를 몹시 가엾이 여겨 왔건만도, 그 눈길에 다시금 그가 거의 혐오스럽게 느껴졌다."]], [["“By my boy, I was giv to understand as Compeyson was out on them marshes too.", "「내 아들 덕분에, 콤피슨도 그 습지에 나와 있다는 걸 알게 됐소."], ["Upon my soul, I half believe he escaped in his terror, to get quit of me, not knowing it was me as had got ashore.", "내 영혼을 걸고 말하건대, 그자는 겁에 질린 나머지, 뭍에 오른 게 나라는 걸 모른 채, 나한테서 벗어나려고 달아난 게 아닌가 싶소."], ["I hunted him down.", "나는 그자를 쫓아가 사냥했소."], ["I smashed his face.", "그 낯짝을 박살 냈지."], ["‘And now,’ says I ‘as the worst thing I can do, caring nothing for myself, I’ll drag you back.’ And I’d have swum off, towing him by the hair, if it had come to that, and I’d a got him aboard without the soldiers.", "‘그리고 이제,’ 내가 말했소, ‘내 한 몸 따위 아랑곳 않고, 내가 할 수 있는 가장 모진 짓으로, 너를 끌고 돌아가 주마.’ 정 그래야 했다면, 나는 그자 머리채를 끌고 헤엄쳐 가기라도 했을 거고, 군인들 없이도 그자를 배에 실어 보냈을 거요."]], [["“Of course he’d much the best of it to the last,—his character was so good.", "「물론 그자는 끝까지 훨씬 유리했지 — 인품이 워낙 좋았으니까."], ["He had escaped when he was made half wild by me and my murderous intentions; and his punishment was light.", "그자는 나와 내 살의에 시달려 반쯤 미쳐 버린 상태에서 달아난 거였고, 그래서 그자가 받은 벌은 가벼웠소."], ["I was put in irons, brought to trial again, and sent for life.", "나는 쇠고랑이 채워져 다시 재판에 부쳐졌고, 종신형을 선고받았소."], ["I didn’t stop for life, dear boy and Pip’s comrade, being here.”", "한데 종신토록 머물러 있진 않았지, 사랑하는 도련님, 그리고 핍의 친구분, 이렇게 여기 와 있으니 말이오.」"]], [["He wiped himself again, as he had done before, and then slowly took his tangle of tobacco from his pocket, and plucked his pipe from his button-hole, and slowly filled it, and began to smoke.", "그는 아까처럼 다시 제 몸을 닦더니, 호주머니에서 엉킨 담배 뭉치를 천천히 꺼내고, 단춧구멍에서 파이프를 뽑아, 천천히 채워 넣고는, 담배를 피우기 시작했다. 「그자는 죽었습니까?」 잠시 침묵이 흐른 뒤 내가 물었다."]], [["“Is he dead?”", "「그가 죽었나요?」"], ["I asked, after a silence.", "나는 잠시 침묵한 뒤에 물었다."]], [["“Is who dead, dear boy?”", "「누가 죽었다는 거요, 도련님?」"]], [["“Compeyson.” “He hopes I am, if he’s alive, you may be sure,”", "「그자가 살아 있다면, 차라리 내가 죽었기를 바라고 있을 거요, 그건 틀림없소.」"], ["with a fierce look.", "사나운 눈빛으로 그가 말했다."], ["“I never heerd no more of him.”", "「그자 소식은 그 뒤로 한 번도 들은 적이 없소.」"]], [["Herbert had been writing with his pencil in the cover of a book. He softly pushed the book over to me, as Provis stood smoking with his eyes on the fire, and I read in it:—", "허버트는 연필로 책 표지에 무언가를 적고 있었다. 프로비스가 두 눈을 불에 둔 채 담배를 피우며 서 있는 동안, 허버트는 그 책을 슬며시 내 쪽으로 밀었고, 나는 거기 적힌 글을 읽었다."]], [["“Young Havisham’s name was Arthur.", "「젊은 해비셤의 이름이 아서였어."], ["Compeyson is the man who professed to be Miss Havisham’s lover.”", "콤피슨은 해비셤 양의 연인 행세를 하던 바로 그 사내야.」"]], [["I shut the book and nodded slightly to Herbert, and put the book by; but we neither of us said anything, and both looked at Provis as he stood smoking by the fire.", "나는 책을 덮고 허버트에게 가볍게 고개를 끄덕인 뒤 책을 옆으로 치워 두었다. 그러나 우리 둘 다 아무 말도 하지 않은 채, 불가에 서서 담배를 피우는 프로비스를 바라보기만 했다."]], [["# Chapter XLIII.", "# Chapter XLIII."]], [["Why should I pause to ask how much of my shrinking from Provis might be traced to Estella? Why should I loiter on my road, to compare the state of mind in which I had tried to rid myself of the stain of the prison before meeting her at the coach-office, with the state of mind in which I now reflected on the abyss between Estella in her pride and beauty, and the returned transport whom I harboured?", "프로비스에게서 내가 본능적으로 움츠러드는 그 감정이 어느 만큼이나 에스텔라에게서 비롯된 것인지, 무엇하러 발걸음을 멈추고 따져 보겠는가? 마차 사무소에서 그녀를 만나기 전에 감옥의 얼룩을 씻어 내려 애쓰던 그때의 심경과, 자긍심과 아름다움을 갖춘 에스텔라와 내가 거두어 들인 돌아온 유형수 사이에 가로놓인 심연을 지금 곱씹는 심경을 비교하려고, 무엇하러 길 위에서 어정거리겠는가?"], ["The road would be none the smoother for it, the end would be none the better for it, he would not be helped, nor I extenuated.", "그런다고 가는 길이 더 평탄해질 것도 아니요, 끝이 더 나아질 것도 아니며, 그가 도움을 받을 것도, 내 처지가 덜어질 것도 아니다."]], [["A new fear had been engendered in my mind by his narrative; or rather, his narrative had given form and purpose to the fear that was already there.", "그의 이야기가 내 마음속에 새로운 두려움을 낳았다. 아니, 정확히 말하자면 그의 이야기는 이미 거기 있던 두려움에 형체와 목적을 부여한 셈이었다."], ["If Compeyson were alive and should discover his return, I could hardly doubt the consequence. That Compeyson stood in mortal fear of him, neither of the two could know much better than I; and that any such man as that man had been described to be would hesitate to release himself for good from a dreaded enemy by the safe means of becoming an informer was scarcely to be imagined.", "만약 컴페이슨이 살아 있어 그가 돌아온 사실을 알아챈다면, 그 결과가 어떠하리라는 것은 내게 거의 의심의 여지가 없었다. 컴페이슨이 그를 죽도록 두려워한다는 사실은 두 사람 가운데 누구도 나만큼 잘 알지는 못했을 것이며, 그자에 대해 묘사된 바와 같은 인간이라면 밀고자가 되는 안전한 수단으로 두려운 원수에게서 영영 벗어나기를 망설일 리는 좀처럼 상상하기 어려웠다."]], [["Never had I breathed, and never would I breathe—or so I resolved—a word of Estella to Provis.", "에스텔라에 관해서는 단 한마디도 프로비스에게 입에 올린 적이 없었고, 앞으로도 결코 올리지 않으리라 — 나는 그렇게 결심했다."], ["But, I said to Herbert that, before I could go abroad, I must see both Estella and Miss Havisham.", "그러나 나는 외국으로 떠나기 전에 에스텔라와 해비셤 양을 둘 다 만나야겠다고 허버트에게 말했다."], ["This was when we were left alone on the night of the day when Provis told us his story.", "프로비스가 우리에게 자기 이야기를 들려준 그날 밤, 둘만 남게 되었을 때였다."], ["I resolved to go out to Richmond next day, and I went.", "나는 이튿날 리치먼드로 나가 보기로 마음먹었고, 실제로 갔다."]], [["On my presenting myself at Mrs. Brandley’s, Estella’s maid was called to tell that Estella had gone into the country.", "브랜들리 부인 댁에 찾아가 내가 왔음을 알리자, 에스텔라가 시골로 떠났다는 말을 전하려고 그녀의 하녀가 불려 나왔다. 어디로 갔느냐?"], ["Where? To Satis House, as usual.", "늘 그렇듯 새티스 하우스로 갔다."], ["Not as usual, I said, for she had never yet gone there without me; when was she coming back?", "늘 그렇지는 않다고 나는 말했다. 그녀가 나 없이 그곳에 간 적은 여태 한 번도 없었으니까."], ["There was an air of reservation in the answer which increased my perplexity, and the answer was, that her maid believed she was only coming back at all for a little while.", "언제 돌아오느냐? 그 대답에는 어딘가 말을 아끼는 기색이 감돌아 내 당혹감을 더욱 키웠는데, 하녀의 대답인즉 자기가 알기로는 그녀가 잠깐 동안만 돌아올 뿐이라는 것이었다."], ["I could make nothing of this, except that it was meant that I should make nothing of it, and I went home again in complete discomfiture.", "나는 이 말에서 아무런 영문도 알아낼 수 없었으니, 다만 내가 아무 영문도 알아내지 못하게 하려는 의도였다는 것 외에는 알 길이 없었고, 나는 완전히 낙담한 채 다시 집으로 돌아왔다."]], [["Another night consultation with Herbert after Provis was gone home (I always took him home, and always looked well about me), led us to the conclusion that nothing should be said about going abroad until I came back from Miss Havisham’s.", "프로비스가 집으로 돌아간 뒤(나는 늘 그를 데려다주었고, 늘 주위를 잘 살폈다) 허버트와 다시 밤에 의논한 끝에, 내가 해비셤 양에게 다녀올 때까지는 외국으로 떠나는 일에 관해 아무 말도 하지 않기로 결론을 내렸다."], ["In the mean time, Herbert and I were to consider separately what it would be best to say; whether we should devise any pretence of being afraid that he was under suspicious observation; or whether I, who had never yet been abroad, should propose an expedition.", "그동안 허버트와 나는 따로따로 어떻게 말을 꺼내는 것이 가장 좋을지 궁리하기로 했다. 그가 의심스러운 감시를 받고 있는 듯해 두렵다는 핑계를 꾸며 댈지, 아니면 여태 외국에 나가 본 적이 없는 내가 여행을 가자고 제안할지 말이다."], ["We both knew that I had but to propose anything, and he would consent.", "우리는 둘 다 내가 무엇을 제안하든 그가 동의하리라는 것을 알고 있었다."], ["We agreed that his remaining many days in his present hazard was not to be thought of.", "우리는 그가 지금의 위태로운 처지에서 여러 날 더 머무는 일은 생각조차 할 수 없다는 데 의견이 일치했다."]], [["Next day I had the meanness to feign that I was under a binding promise to go down to Joe; but I was capable of almost any meanness towards Joe or his name.", "이튿날 나는 조에게 다녀오기로 한 굳은 약속이 있는 척하는 비열한 짓을 저질렀다. 그러나 나는 조나 그의 이름에 대해서라면 거의 어떤 비열한 짓이든 저지를 수 있는 인간이었다."], ["Provis was to be strictly careful while I was gone, and Herbert was to take the charge of him that I had taken.", "내가 떠나 있는 동안 프로비스는 각별히 조심하기로 했고, 내가 맡았던 그를 돌보는 일은 허버트가 맡기로 했다."], ["I was to be absent only one night, and, on my return, the gratification of his impatience for my starting as a gentleman on a greater scale was to be begun.", "나는 하룻밤만 자리를 비울 작정이었고, 돌아오는 대로 내가 더 큰 규모의 신사로 출발하기를 바라는 그의 조바심을 채워 주는 일을 시작하기로 했다."], ["It occurred to me then, and as I afterwards found to Herbert also, that he might be best got away across the water, on that pretence,—as, to make purchases, or the like.", "그때 나는, 또 나중에 알고 보니 허버트 역시, 그 핑계를 빌려 — 이를테면 물건을 사들이러 간다거나 하는 식으로 — 그를 바다 건너로 데려가는 것이 상책이지 않을까 하는 생각이 들었다."]], [["Having thus cleared the way for my expedition to Miss Havisham’s, I set off by the early morning coach before it was yet light, and was out on the open country road when the day came creeping on, halting and whimpering and shivering, and wrapped in patches of cloud and rags of mist, like a beggar.", "이렇게 해비셤 양에게 다녀올 길을 마련해 놓고, 나는 아직 동이 트기도 전인 이른 아침 마차로 길을 나섰는데, 날이 밝아 올 무렵엔 어느덧 탁 트인 시골길 위에 나와 있었다. 그날은 거지처럼 절뚝거리고 흐느끼며 부들부들 떨면서, 군데군데 구름 조각과 안개 누더기를 두른 채 살금살금 기어오고 있었다."], ["When we drove up to the Blue Boar after a drizzly ride, whom should I see come out under the gateway, toothpick in hand, to look at the coach, but Bentley Drummle!", "보슬비 속을 달려 블루 보어 여관에 마차가 다다랐을 때, 이쑤시개를 손에 들고 마차를 구경하러 정문 아래로 나오는 사람이 누구였는가 하면, 바로 벤틀리 드러믈이었다!"]], [["As he pretended not to see me, I pretended not to see him.", "그가 나를 못 본 척했으므로, 나도 그를 못 본 척했다."], ["It was a very lame pretence on both sides; the lamer, because we both went into the coffee-room, where he had just finished his breakfast, and where I ordered mine.", "양쪽 다 어설프기 짝이 없는 시늉이었다. 더욱 어설펐던 것은, 우리 둘 다 그 커피실로 들어갔기 때문인데, 그는 거기서 막 아침 식사를 마친 참이었고 나는 거기서 내 아침을 주문했다."], ["It was poisonous to me to see him in the town, for I very well knew why he had come there.", "그를 그 마을에서 보는 일은 내게 더없이 괴로웠으니, 그가 무엇 때문에 거기 왔는지 나는 너무도 잘 알고 있었기 때문이다."]], [["Pretending to read a smeary newspaper long out of date, which had nothing half so legible in its local news, as the foreign matter of coffee, pickles, fish sauces, gravy, melted butter, and wine with which it was sprinkled all over, as if it had taken the measles in a highly irregular form, I sat at my table while he stood before the fire.", "나는 시기가 한참 지난 얼룩투성이 신문을 읽는 척하며 내 식탁에 앉아 있었고, 그동안 그는 난롯불 앞에 서 있었다. 그 신문에 실린 지역 소식이라고 해 봐야, 마치 몹시 변칙적인 형태의 홍역에라도 걸린 듯 온통 뿌려져 얼룩진 커피, 피클, 생선 소스, 그레이비, 녹인 버터, 포도주 같은 외래의 물질만큼도 알아볼 수 없는 것이었다."], ["By degrees it became an enormous injury to me that he stood before the fire.", "차츰 그가 난롯불 앞에 서 있다는 사실이 내게 엄청난 해코지처럼 느껴졌다."], ["And I got up, determined to have my share of it.", "그래서 나는 내 몫의 불을 차지하기로 마음먹고 일어섰다."], ["I had to put my hand behind his legs for the poker when I went up to the fireplace to stir the fire, but still pretended not to know him.", "불을 쑤시려고 난로 쪽으로 다가갔을 때 나는 부지깽이를 집으려고 그의 두 다리 뒤로 손을 뻗어야 했지만, 그래도 여전히 그를 모르는 척했다."]], [["“Is this a cut?”", "\"이거 일부러 모른 체하는 건가?\""], ["said Mr. Drummle.", "드러믈 씨가 말했다."]], [["“Oh!” said I, poker in hand; “it’s you, is it?", "\"아!\" 나는 부지깽이를 손에 든 채 말했다."], ["How do you do?", "\"당신이었군."], ["I was wondering who it was, who kept the fire off.”", "안녕하시오? 누가 불을 가리고 있나 궁금하던 참이었는데.\""]], [["With that, I poked tremendously, and having done so, planted myself side by side with Mr. Drummle, my shoulders squared and my back to the fire.", "그러고는 나는 사정없이 불을 쑤셔 댔고, 그러고 나서 어깨를 떡 벌리고 불에 등을 댄 채 드러믈 씨와 나란히 자리를 잡았다."]], [["“You have just come down?”", "\"방금 내려온 거요?\""], ["said Mr. Drummle, edging me a little away with his shoulder.", "드러믈 씨가 어깨로 나를 슬쩍 밀어내며 말했다."]], [["“Yes,” said I, edging him a little away with my shoulder.", "\"그렇소.\" 나는 내 어깨로 그를 슬쩍 밀어내며 말했다."]], [["“Beastly place,”", "\"형편없는 곳이군.\""], ["said Drummle. “Your part of the country, I think?”", "드러믈이 말했다. \"당신 고향이라지, 아마?\""]], [["“Yes,” I assented.", "\"그렇소.\" 나는 수긍했다."], ["“I am told it’s very like your Shropshire.”", "\"당신네 슈롭셔하고 아주 비슷하다던데.\""]], [["“Not in the least like it,” said Drummle.", "\"전혀 안 비슷한데.\" 드러믈이 말했다."]], [["Here Mr. Drummle looked at his boots and I looked at mine, and then Mr. Drummle looked at my boots, and I looked at his.", "이때 드러믈 씨는 자기 장화를 내려다보았고 나는 내 장화를 내려다보았으며, 그런 다음 드러믈 씨는 내 장화를 보았고 나는 그의 장화를 보았다."]], [["“Have you been here long?”", "\"여기 온 지 오래됐소?\""], ["I asked, determined not to yield an inch of the fire.", "나는 불자리를 한 치도 양보하지 않기로 작정하고 물었다."]], [["“Long enough to be tired of it,”", "\"지겨워질 만큼은 됐소.\""], ["returned Drummle, pretending to yawn, but equally determined.", "드러믈이 하품하는 척하면서도 똑같이 작정한 태도로 대꾸했다."]], [["“Do you stay here long?”", "\"오래 머물 거요?\""]], [["“Can’t say,”", "\"모르겠소.\""], ["answered Mr. Drummle.", "드러믈 씨가 답했다."], ["“Do you?”", "\"당신은?\""]], [["“Can’t say,” said I.", "\"모르겠소.\" 내가 말했다."]], [["I felt here, through a tingling in my blood, that if Mr. Drummle’s shoulder had claimed another hair’s breadth of room, I should have jerked him into the window; equally, that if my own shoulder had urged a similar claim, Mr. Drummle would have jerked me into the nearest box.", "이때 나는 피가 짜릿하게 솟구치는 것을 느끼며, 만약 드러믈 씨의 어깨가 머리카락 한 올만큼이라도 더 자리를 요구했다면 내가 그를 창문 쪽으로 홱 밀쳐 버렸으리라는 것을, 또한 마찬가지로 내 어깨가 비슷한 요구를 했다면 드러믈 씨가 나를 가장 가까운 칸막이 자리로 홱 밀쳐 버렸으리라는 것을 알았다."], ["He whistled a little. So did I.", "그가 휘파람을 조금 불었다. 나도 그렇게 했다."]], [["“Large tract of marshes about here, I believe?”", "\"이 부근에 넓은 습지가 펼쳐져 있다지, 아마?\""], ["said Drummle.", "드러믈이 말했다."]], [["“Yes.", "\"그렇소."], ["What of that?”", "그게 어쨌다는 거요?\""], ["said I.", "내가 말했다."]], [["Mr. Drummle looked at me, and then at my boots, and then said, “Oh!”", "드러믈 씨는 나를 보았다가 내 장화를 보더니, \"아!\""], ["and laughed.", "하고는 웃었다."]], [["“Are you amused, Mr. Drummle?”", "\"드러믈 씨, 뭐가 그리 우습소?\""]], [["“No,” said he, “not particularly.", "\"아니.\" 그가 말했다."], ["I am going out for a ride in the saddle.", "\"특별히 우스운 건 아니오. 나는 말을 타고 나가 보려는 참이오."], ["I mean to explore those marshes for amusement.", "재미 삼아 그 습지를 탐험할 작정이거든."], ["Out-of-the-way villages there, they tell me.", "그 안에 외진 마을들이 있다더군."], ["Curious little public-houses—and smithies—and that. Waiter!”", "별난 작은 선술집들, 대장간들, 뭐 그런 것들 말이오. 웨이터!\""]], [["“Yes, sir.”", "\"네, 손님.\""]], [["“Is that horse of mine ready?”", "\"내 말 준비됐나?\""]], [["“Brought round to the door, sir.”", "\"문 앞에 끌고 와 두었습니다, 손님.\""]], [["“I say.", "\"이봐."], ["Look here, you sir.", "잘 들어, 자네."], ["The lady won’t ride to-day; the weather won’t do.”", "그 부인은 오늘 말을 타지 않으실 거야. 날씨가 안 좋으니까.\""]], [["“Very good, sir.”", "\"알겠습니다, 손님.\""]], [["“And I don’t dine, because I’m going to dine at the lady’s.”", "\"그리고 나는 식사하지 않겠네. 부인 댁에서 저녁을 들 테니까.\""]], [["“Very good, sir.”", "\"알겠습니다, 손님.\""]], [["Then, Drummle glanced at me, with an insolent triumph on his great-jowled face that cut me to the heart, dull as he was, and so exasperated me, that I felt inclined to take him in my arms (as the robber in the story-book is said to have taken the old lady) and seat him on the fire.", "그러더니 드러믈은, 그 둔한 위인답지 않게, 살이 두툼한 얼굴에 오만한 승리감을 띠고 나를 흘끔 보았는데, 그것이 내 가슴을 후벼 파고 나를 어찌나 격분시켰던지, 나는 (이야기책 속의 도둑이 노부인을 안아 들었다는 식으로) 그를 두 팔로 안아다가 난롯불 위에 앉혀 버리고 싶은 충동이 일었다."]], [["One thing was manifest to both of us, and that was, that until relief came, neither of us could relinquish the fire.", "우리 둘에게 분명한 사실이 하나 있었으니, 그것은 구원이 올 때까지 어느 쪽도 불자리를 내놓을 수 없다는 것이었다."], ["There we stood, well squared up before it, shoulder to shoulder and foot to foot, with our hands behind us, not budging an inch.", "거기서 우리는 불 앞에 어깨를 떡 벌리고 어깨를 맞대고 발을 맞댄 채, 두 손을 등 뒤로 한 채 한 치도 물러서지 않고 버티고 서 있었다."], ["The horse was visible outside in the drizzle at the door, my breakfast was put on the table, Drummle’s was cleared away, the waiter invited me to begin, I nodded, we both stood our ground.", "밖에서는 보슬비 속에 문간에 매인 말이 보였고, 내 아침 식사는 식탁 위에 차려졌으며, 드러믈의 식탁은 치워졌고, 웨이터가 내게 들라고 권했고, 나는 고개를 끄덕였으며, 우리 둘은 자리를 지키고 서 있었다."]], [["“Have you been to the Grove since?”", "\"그 뒤로 그로브에 가 봤소?\""], ["said Drummle.", "드러믈이 말했다."]], [["“No,”", "\"아니.\""], ["said I, “I had quite enough of the Finches the last time I was there.”", "내가 말했다. \"지난번에 갔을 때 핀치 회원들이라면 진절머리가 나도록 겪었으니까.\""]], [["“Was that when we had a difference of opinion?”", "\"그게 우리가 의견이 엇갈렸던 그때였나?\""]], [["“Yes,” I replied, very shortly.", "\"그렇소.\" 나는 아주 짧게 대답했다."]], [["“Come, come!", "\"이런, 이런!"], ["They let you off easily enough,”", "그 사람들이 당신을 어지간히 봐줬지.\""], ["sneered Drummle.", "드러믈이 빈정거렸다."], ["“You shouldn’t have lost your temper.”", "\"당신이 성을 내지 말았어야 했어.\""]], [["“Mr. Drummle,”", "\"드러믈 씨.\""], ["said I, “you are not competent to give advice on that subject.", "내가 말했다. \"당신은 그 문제에 관해 충고할 자격이 없소."], ["When I lose my temper (not that I admit having done so on that occasion), I don’t throw glasses.”", "나는 성이 나도(그때 그랬다고 인정하는 건 아니지만) 술잔을 던지진 않거든.\""]], [["“I do,” said Drummle.", "\"나는 던지지.\" 드러믈이 말했다."]], [["After glancing at him once or twice, in an increased state of smouldering ferocity, I said,—", "부글부글 끓어오르는 사나운 기운이 점점 짙어진 채로 그를 한두 번 흘끔 보고 나서, 나는 말했다."]], [["“Mr. Drummle, I did not seek this conversation, and I don’t think it an agreeable one.”", "\"드러믈 씨, 나는 이 대화를 청한 적이 없고, 이게 유쾌한 대화라고도 생각지 않소.\""]], [["“I am sure it’s not,”", "\"유쾌하지 않은 건 확실하지.\""], ["said he, superciliously over his shoulder; “I don’t think anything about it.”", "그가 어깨 너머로 거만하게 말했다. \"나는 이 대화에 대해 아무 생각도 안 하니까.\""]], [["“And therefore,”", "\"그러니까.\""], ["I went on, “with your leave, I will suggest that we hold no kind of communication in future.”", "나는 말을 이었다. \"당신만 괜찮다면, 앞으로 우리 어떤 식의 교류도 하지 말자고 제안하고 싶소.\""]], [["“Quite my opinion,”", "\"전적으로 내 생각도 그렇소.\""], ["said Drummle, “and what I should have suggested myself, or done—more likely—without suggesting.", "드러믈이 말했다. \"내가 먼저 제안했을 법한, 아니, 더 그럴듯하게는 제안도 없이 그냥 해 버렸을 일이지."], ["But don’t lose your temper.", "하지만 성은 내지 마시오."], ["Haven’t you lost enough without that?”", "그것 말고도 이미 충분히 잃은 게 아니오?\""]], [["“What do you mean, sir?”", "\"무슨 뜻이오?\""]], [["“Waiter!”", "\"웨이터!\""], ["said Drummle, by way of answering me.", "드러믈이 내게 대답하는 대신 그렇게 외쳤다."]], [["The waiter reappeared.", "웨이터가 다시 나타났다."]], [["“Look here, you sir. You quite understand that the young lady don’t ride to-day, and that I dine at the young lady’s?”", "\"이봐, 자네. 그 젊은 아가씨가 오늘 말을 타지 않으신다는 것, 그리고 내가 그 젊은 아가씨 댁에서 저녁을 든다는 것, 잘 알아들었지?\""]], [["“Quite so, sir!”", "\"잘 알겠습니다, 손님!\""]], [["When the waiter had felt my fast-cooling teapot with the palm of his hand, and had looked imploringly at me, and had gone out, Drummle, careful not to move the shoulder next me, took a cigar from his pocket and bit the end off, but showed no sign of stirring.", "웨이터가 빠르게 식어 가는 내 찻주전자를 손바닥으로 만져 보고는 애원하듯 나를 쳐다본 뒤 나가 버리자, 드러믈은 내 쪽 어깨를 움직이지 않으려 조심하면서 주머니에서 시가를 꺼내 끝을 물어뜯었지만, 자리를 뜰 기색은 전혀 보이지 않았다."], ["Choking and boiling as I was, I felt that we could not go a word further, without introducing Estella’s name, which I could not endure to hear him utter; and therefore I looked stonily at the opposite wall, as if there were no one present, and forced myself to silence.", "숨이 막히도록 끓어오르던 나는, 한마디만 더 나아가면 에스텔라의 이름이 나올 수밖에 없다는 것을 느꼈는데, 그의 입에서 그 이름이 흘러나오는 것을 도저히 견딜 수 없었으므로, 마치 그 자리에 아무도 없는 듯 맞은편 벽을 돌처럼 굳은 눈으로 바라보며 억지로 침묵을 지켰다."], ["How long we might have remained in this ridiculous position it is impossible to say, but for the incursion of three thriving farmers—laid on by the waiter, I think—who came into the coffee-room unbuttoning their great-coats and rubbing their hands, and before whom, as they charged at the fire, we were obliged to give way.", "우리가 이 우스꽝스러운 자세로 얼마나 오래 머물렀을지는 알 수 없으나, 잘나가는 농부 셋이 — 내 생각엔 웨이터가 데려온 듯한데 — 외투 단추를 풀고 두 손을 비비며 커피실로 들어오는 통에, 그들이 불을 향해 돌진해 오자 우리는 어쩔 수 없이 자리를 비켜 줘야 했다."]], [["I saw him through the window, seizing his horse’s mane, and mounting in his blundering brutal manner, and sidling and backing away.", "나는 그가 창문 너머로, 말갈기를 움켜쥐고 그 서투르고 거친 솜씨로 올라타서는, 옆걸음질로 뒷걸음질 치며 멀어져 가는 모습을 보았다."], ["I thought he was gone, when he came back, calling for a light for the cigar in his mouth, which he had forgotten.", "그가 떠났구나 싶었는데, 그는 입에 문 시가에 불을 붙일 것을 깜빡 잊었다며 불을 가져오라고 외치며 되돌아왔다."], ["A man in a dust-coloured dress appeared with what was wanted,—I could not have said from where: whether from the inn yard, or the street, or where not,—and as Drummle leaned down from the saddle and lighted his cigar and laughed, with a jerk of his head towards the coffee-room windows, the slouching shoulders and ragged hair of this man whose back was towards me reminded me of Orlick.", "먼지 빛깔 옷을 입은 한 사내가 그가 원하는 것을 들고 나타났는데 — 그가 여관 마당에서 나왔는지, 길에서 나왔는지, 그 밖에 어디서 나왔는지 나로서는 알 수가 없었다 — 드러믈이 안장에서 몸을 굽혀 시가에 불을 붙이고는 커피실 창문 쪽으로 고갯짓을 하며 웃을 때, 내게 등을 돌린 그 사내의 구부정한 어깨와 텁수룩한 머리는 올릭을 떠올리게 했다."]], [["Too heavily out of sorts to care much at the time whether it were he or no, or after all to touch the breakfast, I washed the weather and the journey from my face and hands, and went out to the memorable old house that it would have been so much the better for me never to have entered, never to have seen.", "그자가 올릭이든 아니든 당장은 별로 신경 쓸 마음도 없을 만큼, 또 결국 아침 식사에 손댈 마음도 없을 만큼 몸도 마음도 잔뜩 상해 있던 나는, 얼굴과 두 손에서 날씨와 여행의 흔적을 씻어 내고는, 차라리 한 번도 발을 들이지 않았더라면, 한 번도 보지 않았더라면 내게 그토록 더 나았을 그 잊지 못할 낡은 집을 향해 길을 나섰다."]], [["# Chapter XLIV.", "# Chapter XLIV."]], [["In the room where the dressing-table stood, and where the wax-candles burnt on the wall, I found Miss Havisham and Estella; Miss Havisham seated on a settee near the fire, and Estella on a cushion at her feet.", "화장대가 놓여 있고 벽의 양초들이 타오르던 그 방에서, 나는 해비셤 양과 에스텔러를 발견했다. 해비셤 양은 난롯가 가까운 긴 의자에 앉아 있었고, 에스텔러는 그녀의 발치 방석 위에 앉아 있었다."], ["Estella was knitting, and Miss Havisham was looking on.", "에스텔러는 뜨개질을 하고 있었고, 해비셤 양은 그것을 바라보고 있었다."], ["They both raised their eyes as I went in, and both saw an alteration in me.", "내가 들어서자 두 사람 모두 눈을 들었고, 두 사람 모두 내게 일어난 변화를 알아차렸다."], ["I derived that, from the look they interchanged.", "나는 그것을 두 사람이 주고받은 눈빛에서 짐작했다."]], [["“And what wind,”", "\"무슨 바람이,\""], ["said Miss Havisham, “blows you here, Pip?”", "해비셤 양이 말했다. \"너를 여기로 불어 보냈느냐, 핍?\""]], [["Though she looked steadily at me, I saw that she was rather confused.", "그녀가 나를 똑바로 바라보고는 있었지만, 나는 그녀가 다소 당황해 있음을 알아챘다."], ["Estella, pausing a moment in her knitting with her eyes upon me, and then going on, I fancied that I read in the action of her fingers, as plainly as if she had told me in the dumb alphabet, that she perceived I had discovered my real benefactor.", "에스텔러가 나를 바라보며 뜨개질을 잠깐 멈췄다가 다시 이어 가는 모습을 보면서, 나는 그녀의 손가락 움직임에서 마치 그녀가 수화 알파벳으로 내게 일러 주기라도 한 듯 또렷이 읽어 냈다. 그녀가 내가 진짜 후원자를 알아냈음을 감지했다는 것을."]], [["“Miss Havisham,”", "\"해비셤 양,\""], ["said I, “I went to Richmond yesterday, to speak to Estella; and finding that some wind had blown her here, I followed.”", "내가 말했다. \"저는 어제 에스텔러와 이야기하려고 리치먼드에 갔습니다. 그런데 무슨 바람이 그녀를 이리로 불어 보냈다는 걸 알고, 뒤따라왔습니다.\""]], [["Miss Havisham motioning to me for the third or fourth time to sit down, I took the chair by the dressing-table, which I had often seen her occupy.", "해비셤 양이 세 번째인지 네 번째인지 내게 앉으라고 손짓했으므로, 나는 화장대 옆 의자에 앉았다. 그녀가 거기 앉아 있는 모습을 나는 자주 보아 왔다."], ["With all that ruin at my feet and about me, it seemed a natural place for me, that day.", "그 모든 폐허를 발치에, 그리고 주위에 둔 채, 그날 그곳은 내게 자연스러운 자리처럼 느껴졌다."]], [["“What I had to say to Estella, Miss Havisham, I will say before you, presently—in a few moments.", "\"에스텔러에게 할 말을, 해비셤 양, 곧 당신 앞에서 하겠습니다 — 잠시 후에요."], ["It will not surprise you, it will not displease you.", "놀라실 것도 없고, 언짢으실 것도 없을 겁니다."], ["I am as unhappy as you can ever have meant me to be.”", "저는 당신이 의도하셨을 만큼이나 불행합니다.\""]], [["Miss Havisham continued to look steadily at me.", "해비셤 양은 계속해서 나를 똑바로 바라보았다."], ["I could see in the action of Estella’s fingers as they worked that she attended to what I said; but she did not look up.", "나는 뜨개질을 하는 에스텔러의 손가락 움직임에서 그녀가 내 말에 귀를 기울이고 있음을 알 수 있었다. 그러나 그녀는 눈을 들지 않았다."]], [["“I have found out who my patron is.", "\"저는 제 후원자가 누구인지 알아냈습니다."], ["It is not a fortunate discovery, and is not likely ever to enrich me in reputation, station, fortune, anything.", "그것은 행운의 발견이 아니며, 명성으로든 지위로든 재산으로든 그 무엇으로든 결코 저를 부유하게 해 줄 것 같지도 않습니다."], ["There are reasons why I must say no more of that.", "그에 관해서는 더 말씀드릴 수 없는 이유들이 있습니다."], ["It is not my secret, but another’s.”", "그것은 제 비밀이 아니라 다른 사람의 비밀이니까요.\""]], [["As I was silent for a while, looking at Estella and considering how to go on, Miss Havisham repeated, “It is not your secret, but another’s. Well?”", "내가 에스텔러를 바라보며 어떻게 말을 이어 갈지 궁리하느라 한동안 잠자코 있자, 해비셤 양이 되풀이해 말했다. \"네 비밀이 아니라 다른 사람의 비밀이라. 그래서?\""]], [["“When you first caused me to be brought here, Miss Havisham, when I belonged to the village over yonder, that I wish I had never left, I suppose I did really come here, as any other chance boy might have come,—as a kind of servant, to gratify a want or a whim, and to be paid for it?”", "\"처음에 저를 이곳으로 데려오게 하셨을 때, 해비셤 양, 그러니까 제가 저 너머 마을에 속해 있던 — 차라리 떠나지 말았어야 했다고 생각하는 그 마을에 — 그때 저는 정말로, 다른 어떤 운수 사나운 아이가 왔을 법한 모양으로 이곳에 온 거라고 생각합니다. 일종의 하인으로서, 어떤 욕구나 변덕을 채워 주고 그 대가를 받기 위해서요. 그렇지요?\""]], [["“Ay, Pip,”", "\"그래, 핍.\""], ["replied Miss Havisham, steadily nodding her head; “you did.”", "해비셤 양이 꾸준히 고개를 끄덕이며 대답했다. \"그랬지.\""]], [["“And that Mr. Jaggers—”", "\"그리고 재거스 씨가 —\""]], [["“Mr. Jaggers,”", "\"재거스 씨는,\""], ["said Miss Havisham, taking me up in a firm tone, “had nothing to do with it, and knew nothing of it.", "해비셤 양이 단호한 어조로 내 말을 가로채며 말했다. \"그 일과는 아무 관계가 없고, 그 일에 대해 아무것도 몰랐다."], ["His being my lawyer, and his being the lawyer of your patron is a coincidence.", "그가 내 변호사이고 또 네 후원자의 변호사이기도 한 것은 우연이야."], ["He holds the same relation towards numbers of people, and it might easily arise.", "그는 수많은 사람들과 똑같은 관계를 맺고 있으니, 그런 일은 얼마든지 생길 수 있지."], ["Be that as it may, it did arise, and was not brought about by any one.”", "어찌 됐든 간에, 그렇게 된 거고, 누가 일부러 꾸민 것이 아니다.\""]], [["Any one might have seen in her haggard face that there was no suppression or evasion so far.", "거기까지는 그녀의 초췌한 얼굴에서 어떤 은폐도 회피도 없음을, 누구라도 알아볼 수 있었을 것이다."]], [["“But when I fell into the mistake I have so long remained in, at least you led me on?” said I.", "\"하지만 제가 그토록 오래 빠져 있던 그 오해에 빠졌을 때, 적어도 당신은 저를 그쪽으로 이끄셨지요?\" 내가 말했다."]], [["“Yes,”", "\"그래.\""], ["she returned, again nodding steadily, “I let you go on.” “Was that kind?”", "그녀가 다시 꾸준히 고개를 끄덕이며 대답했다. \"내가 네가 그러도록 내버려 두었지.\""]], [["“Who am I,”", "\"그게 친절한 일이었습니까?\""], ["cried Miss Havisham, striking her stick upon the floor and flashing into wrath so suddenly that Estella glanced up at her in surprise,—“who am I, for God’s sake, that I should be kind?”", "\"내가 누구냐,\" 해비셤 양이 지팡이로 바닥을 내리치며, 너무도 갑작스럽게 분노로 타올라 에스텔러가 놀라 그녀를 올려다볼 정도로 외쳤다. \"내가 도대체 누구이기에, 친절해야 한단 말이냐?\""]], [["It was a weak complaint to have made, and I had not meant to make it. I told her so, as she sat brooding after this outburst.", "그것은 해 봐야 부질없는 항변이었고, 나도 그런 말을 할 작정은 아니었다. 그녀가 이 격발 뒤에 골똘히 생각에 잠겨 앉아 있을 때, 나는 그렇다고 그녀에게 말했다."]], [["“Well, well, well!”", "\"그래, 그래, 그래!\""], ["she said. “What else?”", "그녀가 말했다. \"또 뭐냐?\""]], [["“I was liberally paid for my old attendance here,”", "\"이곳에서 옛날에 시중든 대가는 후하게 받았습니다.\""], ["I said, to soothe her, “in being apprenticed, and I have asked these questions only for my own information.", "나는 그녀를 달래려고 말했다. \"도제 수업을 받게 되었으니까요. 그리고 제가 이런 질문들을 드린 것은 오로지 저 자신이 알고 싶어서였습니다."], ["What follows has another (and I hope more disinterested) purpose.", "다음에 드릴 말씀은 다른 (그리고 좀 더 사심 없는 것이기를 바라는) 목적이 있습니다."], ["In humouring my mistake, Miss Havisham, you punished—practised on—perhaps you will supply whatever term expresses your intention, without offence—your self-seeking relations?”", "제 오해를 받아 주시면서, 해비셤 양, 당신은 — 벌하셨던 거지요. 시험하신 — 어쩌면 당신은 당신의 의도를 표현할 말이라면 무엇이든, 노여워 마시고 채워 주실 테지요 — 사욕을 채우려는 친척들을요?\""]], [["“I did.", "\"그랬지."], ["Why, they would have it so! So would you.", "그야, 그들이 그렇게 되기를 자초했으니까!"], ["What has been my history, that I should be at the pains of entreating either them or you not to have it so!", "너도 마찬가지였고. 내 사연이 어떠했기에, 그들에게든 너에게든 그러지 말아 달라고 애써 사정해야 한단 말이냐!"], ["You made your own snares.", "너는 네 덫을 스스로 놓은 거야."], ["I never made them.”", "나는 결코 그것을 놓지 않았다.\""]], [["Waiting until she was quiet again,—for this, too, flashed out of her in a wild and sudden way,—I went on.", "그녀가 다시 잠잠해질 때까지 기다렸다가 — 이 역시 격렬하고 갑작스럽게 그녀에게서 터져 나온 것이었으므로 — 나는 말을 이었다."]], [["“I have been thrown among one family of your relations, Miss Havisham, and have been constantly among them since I went to London.", "\"저는 당신의 친척 한 가족 사이로 던져졌고, 해비셤 양, 런던에 간 이후로 줄곧 그들과 함께 지냈습니다."], ["I know them to have been as honestly under my delusion as I myself.", "저는 그들이 저 자신만큼이나 솔직하게 그 망상에 빠져 있었음을 알고 있습니다."], ["And I should be false and base if I did not tell you, whether it is acceptable to you or no, and whether you are inclined to give credence to it or no, that you deeply wrong both Mr. Matthew Pocket and his son Herbert, if you suppose them to be otherwise than generous, upright, open, and incapable of anything designing or mean.”", "그리고 이 말이 당신께 받아들여지든 아니든, 당신이 그것을 믿어 줄 마음이 있든 없든, 제가 이 말씀을 드리지 않는다면 저는 거짓되고 비열한 자가 될 겁니다. 만약 당신이 매슈 포켓 씨와 그의 아들 허버트를 너그럽고 올곧고 솔직하며, 음흉하거나 비열한 짓이라곤 할 줄 모르는 사람이 아니라고 여기신다면, 당신은 그 두 사람에게 깊이 부당한 일을 하시는 겁니다.\""]], [["“They are your friends,”", "\"그들은 네 친구들이지.\""], ["said Miss Havisham.", "해비셤 양이 말했다."]], [["“They made themselves my friends,”", "\"그들은 스스로 제 친구가 되었습니다.\""], ["said I, “when they supposed me to have superseded them; and when Sarah Pocket, Miss Georgiana, and Mistress Camilla were not my friends, I think.”", "내가 말했다. \"제가 자기들의 자리를 빼앗았다고 여겼을 때 말입니다. 그리고 새러 포켓과 조지애나 양과 커밀라 부인은 제 친구가 아니었지요.\""]], [["This contrasting of them with the rest seemed, I was glad to see, to do them good with her.", "그들을 나머지 친척들과 대비시킨 것이, 기쁘게도, 그녀에게 그들의 인상을 좋게 만든 듯했다."], ["She looked at me keenly for a little while, and then said quietly,—", "그녀는 잠시 나를 날카롭게 바라보더니 조용히 말했다."]], [["“What do you want for them?”", "\"그들을 위해 무엇을 바라느냐?\""]], [["“Only,”", "\"단지,\""], ["said I, “that you would not confound them with the others.", "내가 말했다. \"당신이 그들을 나머지와 혼동하지 않으시기를 바랄 뿐입니다."], ["They may be of the same blood, but, believe me, they are not of the same nature.”", "그들은 같은 핏줄일지 몰라도, 믿어 주십시오, 같은 본성을 지니지는 않았습니다.\""]], [["Still looking at me keenly, Miss Havisham repeated,—", "여전히 나를 날카롭게 바라보면서, 해비셤 양이 되풀이했다."]], [["“What do you want for them?”", "\"그들을 위해 무엇을 바라느냐?\""]], [["“I am not so cunning, you see,”", "\"보시다시피 저는 그리 교활하지가 못합니다.\""], ["I said, in answer, conscious that I reddened a little, “as that I could hide from you, even if I desired, that I do want something.", "나는 얼굴이 조금 붉어지는 것을 의식하며 대답했다. \"그래서 설령 감추고 싶어도, 제가 무언가를 바라고 있다는 것을 당신께 감출 수가 없군요."], ["Miss Havisham, if you would spare the money to do my friend Herbert a lasting service in life, but which from the nature of the case must be done without his knowledge, I could show you how.”", "해비셤 양, 만약 당신이 제 친구 허버트에게 평생토록 도움이 될 일을 해 줄 돈을 내어 주신다면 — 다만 그 일의 성격상 그가 모르게 이루어져야만 하는데 — 그 방법을 보여 드릴 수 있습니다.\""]], [["“Why must it be done without his knowledge?”", "\"왜 그 사람이 모르게 해야 하느냐?\""], ["she asked, settling her hands upon her stick, that she might regard me the more attentively.", "그녀가 나를 더 주의 깊게 살피려는 듯 두 손을 지팡이 위에 모으며 물었다."]], [["“Because,”", "\"왜냐하면,\""], ["said I, “I began the service myself, more than two years ago, without his knowledge, and I don’t want to be betrayed.", "내가 말했다. \"제가 그 일을 직접 시작했기 때문입니다. 이 년도 더 전에, 그가 모르게요."], ["Why I fail in my ability to finish it, I cannot explain.", "그리고 저는 들통나고 싶지 않습니다. 어째서 그 일을 끝낼 능력이 모자라게 되었는지는 설명드릴 수 없습니다."], ["It is a part of the secret which is another person’s and not mine.”", "그것은 제 것이 아니라 다른 사람의 것인 그 비밀의 일부니까요.\""]], [["She gradually withdrew her eyes from me, and turned them on the fire.", "그녀는 차츰 나에게서 눈을 거두어 난롯불로 돌렸다."], ["After watching it for what appeared in the silence and by the light of the slowly wasting candles to be a long time, she was roused by the collapse of some of the red coals, and looked towards me again—at first, vacantly—then, with a gradually concentrating attention.", "그 침묵 속에서, 그리고 천천히 사위어 가는 촛불에 비추어 보아 긴 시간처럼 여겨지는 동안 불을 지켜보던 그녀는, 붉은 석탄 몇 덩이가 무너지는 소리에 정신이 들어 다시 나를 바라보았다 — 처음에는 멍하니, 그러다가 차츰 주의를 모으면서."], ["All this time Estella knitted on. When Miss Havisham had fixed her attention on me, she said, speaking as if there had been no lapse in our dialogue,—", "그동안 내내 에스텔러는 뜨개질을 계속했다. 해비셤 양은 나에게 주의를 고정시킨 다음, 우리 대화에 아무런 끊김도 없었던 듯이 말했다."]], [["“What else?”", "\"또 뭐냐?\""]], [["“Estella,”", "\"에스텔러.\""], ["said I, turning to her now, and trying to command my trembling voice, “you know I love you.", "나는 이제 그녀에게로 몸을 돌리며, 떨리는 목소리를 다잡으려 애쓰면서 말했다. \"당신은 내가 당신을 사랑한다는 걸 알지요."], ["You know that I have loved you long and dearly.”", "내가 오래도록, 그리고 애틋하게 당신을 사랑해 왔다는 걸 알 거요.\""]], [["She raised her eyes to my face, on being thus addressed, and her fingers plied their work, and she looked at me with an unmoved countenance. I saw that Miss Havisham glanced from me to her, and from her to me.", "이렇게 말을 건네받자 그녀는 내 얼굴로 눈을 들었고, 손가락은 일감을 놀렸으며, 무표정한 얼굴로 나를 바라보았다. 나는 해비셤 양이 나에게서 그녀에게로, 그녀에게서 나에게로 눈길을 옮기는 것을 보았다."]], [["“I should have said this sooner, but for my long mistake.", "\"내 오랜 오해만 아니었다면 이 말을 더 일찍 했어야 했소."], ["It induced me to hope that Miss Havisham meant us for one another.", "그 오해가, 해비셤 양이 우리 둘을 짝지을 작정이라는 희망을 내게 품게 했지요."], ["While I thought you could not help yourself, as it were, I refrained from saying it.", "당신이 말하자면 어쩔 도리가 없다고 생각하는 동안에는, 나는 이 말을 삼갔소."], ["But I must say it now.”", "하지만 이제는 해야겠소.\""]], [["Preserving her unmoved countenance, and with her fingers still going, Estella shook her head.", "무표정한 얼굴을 그대로 유지한 채, 손가락은 여전히 놀리면서, 에스텔러는 고개를 저었다."]], [["“I know,”", "\"알아요.\""], ["said I, in answer to that action,—“I know.", "나는 그 몸짓에 답하여 말했다. \"알아요."], ["I have no hope that I shall ever call you mine, Estella.", "당신을 내 것이라 부를 수 있으리란 희망은 내게 없소, 에스텔러."], ["I am ignorant what may become of me very soon, how poor I may be, or where I may go. Still, I love you.", "나는 머지않아 내가 어찌 될지, 내가 얼마나 가난해질지, 어디로 가게 될지 알지 못하오. 그래도, 나는 당신을 사랑하오."], ["I have loved you ever since I first saw you in this house.”", "이 집에서 당신을 처음 본 그 순간부터 줄곧 당신을 사랑해 왔소.\""]], [["Looking at me perfectly unmoved and with her fingers busy, she shook her head again.", "조금도 동요 없이 손가락을 분주히 놀리며 나를 바라보던 그녀는, 다시 고개를 저었다."]], [["“It would have been cruel in Miss Havisham, horribly cruel, to practise on the susceptibility of a poor boy, and to torture me through all these years with a vain hope and an idle pursuit, if she had reflected on the gravity of what she did.", "\"해비셤 양이 자신이 한 일의 무게를 헤아렸더라면, 가엾은 한 소년의 감수성을 시험거리로 삼고, 이 모든 세월 동안 부질없는 희망과 헛된 추구로 나를 괴롭힌 것은 잔인한 일이었을 거요. 끔찍이도 잔인한 일이었지요. 하지만 그분은 그러지 않으셨다고 생각하오."], ["But I think she did not. I think that, in the endurance of her own trial, she forgot mine, Estella.”", "자신의 시련을 견뎌 내는 가운데, 그분은 내 시련을 잊으셨던 거라고 나는 생각하오, 에스텔러.\""]], [["I saw Miss Havisham put her hand to her heart and hold it there, as she sat looking by turns at Estella and at me.", "나는 해비셤 양이 손을 가슴에 갖다 대고 거기 짚은 채, 에스텔러와 나를 번갈아 바라보며 앉아 있는 것을 보았다."]], [["“It seems,”", "\"아무래도,\""], ["said Estella, very calmly, “that there are sentiments, fancies,—I don’t know how to call them,—which I am not able to comprehend.", "에스텔러가 아주 차분하게 말했다. \"내가 이해할 수 없는 어떤 감정, 어떤 공상 — 뭐라고 불러야 할지 모르겠지만 — 그런 것들이 있나 봐요."], ["When you say you love me, I know what you mean, as a form of words; but nothing more.", "당신이 나를 사랑한다고 말할 때, 나는 그것이 하나의 말 형식으로서 무슨 뜻인지는 알아요. 하지만 그 이상은 모르겠어요."], ["You address nothing in my breast, you touch nothing there.", "당신은 내 가슴속 그 무엇에도 가닿지 못하고, 그 안의 그 무엇도 건드리지 못해요."], ["I don’t care for what you say at all.", "나는 당신이 하는 말에 도무지 관심이 없어요."], ["I have tried to warn you of this; now, have I not?”", "나는 이것을 당신에게 경고하려고 애써 왔지요. 자, 그러지 않았나요?\""]], [["I said in a miserable manner, “Yes.”", "나는 비참한 태도로 말했다. \"그랬소.\""]], [["“Yes.", "\"그래요."], ["But you would not be warned, for you thought I did not mean it.", "하지만 당신은 경고를 받아들이려 하지 않았어요. 내가 진심으로 하는 말이 아니라고 생각했으니까요."], ["Now, did you not think so?”", "자, 그렇게 생각하지 않았나요?\""]], [["“I thought and hoped you could not mean it.", "\"당신이 진심일 리 없다고 생각했고, 또 그러기를 바랐소."], ["You, so young, untried, and beautiful, Estella!", "당신은, 그토록 젊고, 세상을 모르고, 아름다운데, 에스텔러!"], ["Surely it is not in Nature.”", "그게 자연의 이치일 리 없잖소.\""]], [["“It is in my nature,”", "\"그게 내 본성이에요.\""], ["she returned.", "그녀가 대답했다."], ["And then she added, with a stress upon the words, “It is in the nature formed within me.", "그러고는 그 말에 힘을 주어 덧붙였다. \"그게 내 안에서 만들어진 본성이지요."], ["I make a great difference between you and all other people when I say so much. I can do no more.”", "내가 이만큼이나 말하는 건, 당신을 다른 모든 사람들과 크게 구별해서예요. 나는 그 이상은 할 수 없어요.\""]], [["“Is it not true,” said I, “that Bentley Drummle is in town here, and pursuing you?”", "\"벤틀리 드러믈이 여기 시내에 와 있고, 당신을 쫓아다닌다는 게 사실 아니오?\" 내가 말했다."]], [["“It is quite true,”", "\"전적으로 사실이에요.\""], ["she replied, referring to him with the indifference of utter contempt.", "그녀가 더할 나위 없는 경멸이 담긴 무심함으로 그를 언급하며 대답했다."]], [["“That you encourage him, and ride out with him, and that he dines with you this very day?”", "\"당신이 그를 부추기고, 그와 함께 말을 타고 나가며, 바로 오늘 그가 당신과 식사를 한다는 것도요?\""]], [["She seemed a little surprised that I should know it, but again replied, “Quite true.”", "내가 그것을 안다는 데 그녀는 조금 놀란 듯했으나, 다시 대답했다. \"전적으로 사실이에요.\""]], [["“You cannot love him, Estella!”", "\"당신이 그를 사랑할 리 없소, 에스텔러!\""]], [["Her fingers stopped for the first time, as she retorted rather angrily, “What have I told you?", "처음으로 그녀의 손가락이 멈췄고, 그녀는 다소 성난 어조로 받아쳤다. \"내가 당신에게 뭐라고 했지요?"], ["Do you still think, in spite of it, that I do not mean what I say?”", "그런데도 당신은 여전히, 내가 하는 말이 진심이 아니라고 생각하나요?\""]], [["“You would never marry him, Estella?”", "\"당신이 그와 결혼할 리 없잖소, 에스텔러?\""]], [["She looked towards Miss Havisham, and considered for a moment with her work in her hands.", "그녀는 해비셤 양 쪽을 바라보았고, 손에 일감을 든 채 잠시 생각에 잠겼다. 그러더니 말했다."], ["Then she said, “Why not tell you the truth?", "\"사실대로 말하지 않을 까닭이 없겠지요?"], ["I am going to be married to him.”", "나는 그와 결혼할 거예요.\""]], [["I dropped my face into my hands, but was able to control myself better than I could have expected, considering what agony it gave me to hear her say those words.", "나는 얼굴을 두 손에 묻었으나, 그 말을 듣는 것이 내게 얼마나 큰 고통을 주었는지를 생각하면, 예상보다는 훨씬 잘 나 자신을 다스릴 수 있었다."], ["When I raised my face again, there was such a ghastly look upon Miss Havisham’s, that it impressed me, even in my passionate hurry and grief.", "다시 얼굴을 들었을 때, 해비셤 양의 얼굴에는 너무도 섬뜩한 표정이 어려 있어서, 격정에 사로잡힌 황망함과 비탄 속에서도 그것이 내게 강하게 와닿았다."]], [["“Estella, dearest Estella, do not let Miss Havisham lead you into this fatal step.", "\"에스텔러, 사랑하는 에스텔러, 해비셤 양이 당신을 이 치명적인 걸음으로 이끌게 두지 마시오."], ["Put me aside for ever,—you have done so, I well know,—but bestow yourself on some worthier person than Drummle.", "나를 영영 제쳐 두시오 — 당신이 이미 그렇게 했다는 걸 나는 잘 알지만 — 그래도 드러믈보다 더 가치 있는 사람에게 당신 자신을 주시오."], ["Miss Havisham gives you to him, as the greatest slight and injury that could be done to the many far better men who admire you, and to the few who truly love you.", "해비셤 양은 당신을 그에게 줌으로써, 당신을 흠모하는 훨씬 더 훌륭한 수많은 남자들에게, 그리고 진정으로 당신을 사랑하는 몇 안 되는 이들에게, 가할 수 있는 가장 큰 모욕과 상처를 가하는 거요."], ["Among those few there may be one who loves you even as dearly, though he has not loved you as long, as I.", "그 몇 안 되는 이들 가운데에는, 비록 나만큼 오래 사랑하지는 못했어도 나만큼이나 애틋하게 당신을 사랑하는 사람이 하나 있을지도 모르오."], ["Take him, and I can bear it better, for your sake!”", "그 사람을 택하시오, 그러면 나는 당신을 위해 이 일을 더 잘 견딜 수 있을 거요!\""]], [["My earnestness awoke a wonder in her that seemed as if it would have been touched with compassion, if she could have rendered me at all intelligible to her own mind.", "나의 간절함은 그녀 안에 어떤 경이를 불러일으켰는데, 만약 그녀가 자기 마음으로 나를 조금이라도 이해할 수 있었더라면 그것은 연민으로 물들었을 법한 경이였다."]], [["“I am going,” she said again, in a gentler voice, “to be married to him.", "\"나는 그와 결혼할 거예요.\" 그녀가 다시, 한결 부드러운 목소리로 말했다."], ["The preparations for my marriage are making, and I shall be married soon.", "\"내 결혼 준비가 진행되고 있고, 나는 곧 결혼할 거예요."], ["Why do you injuriously introduce the name of my mother by adoption? It is my own act.”", "어째서 당신은 내 양어머니의 이름을 끌어들여 모욕을 주려 하지요? 이건 내 자신의 행동이에요.\""]], [["“Your own act, Estella, to fling yourself away upon a brute?”", "\"당신 자신의 행동이라니, 에스텔러, 한낱 짐승 같은 자에게 당신 자신을 내던지는 게?\""]], [["“On whom should I fling myself away?”", "\"그럼 내가 누구에게 나 자신을 내던져야 하지요?\""], ["she retorted, with a smile.", "그녀가 미소를 띠며 받아쳤다."], ["“Should I fling myself away upon the man who would the soonest feel (if people do feel such things) that I took nothing to him?", "\"내가 그에게 아무것도 주지 않는다는 걸 (사람이 그런 걸 느낀다면) 가장 빨리 느낄 그런 남자에게 나 자신을 내던져야 하나요? 자!"], ["There! It is done.", "이미 끝난 일이에요."], ["I shall do well enough, and so will my husband.", "나는 그럭저럭 잘 지낼 거고, 내 남편도 그럴 거예요."], ["As to leading me into what you call this fatal step, Miss Havisham would have had me wait, and not marry yet; but I am tired of the life I have led, which has very few charms for me, and I am willing enough to change it.", "당신이 말하는 이 치명적인 걸음으로 나를 이끌었다는 것에 관해 말하자면, 해비셤 양은 오히려 내가 기다리며 아직 결혼하지 말기를 바라셨어요. 하지만 나는 내가 살아온 삶에 지쳤어요. 그 삶에는 내게 매력이라곤 거의 없으니, 그것을 바꾸는 데 나는 기꺼이 응할 마음이에요."], ["Say no more.", "더는 말하지 말아요."], ["We shall never understand each other.”", "우리는 결코 서로를 이해하지 못할 거예요.\""]], [["“Such a mean brute, such a stupid brute!”", "\"그런 비열한 짐승, 그런 멍청한 짐승을!\""], ["I urged, in despair.", "나는 절망에 빠져 다그쳤다."]], [["“Don’t be afraid of my being a blessing to him,”", "\"내가 그에게 축복이 될까 봐 걱정하진 말아요.\" 에스텔러가 말했다."], ["said Estella; “I shall not be that. Come!", "\"나는 그런 게 되지 않을 테니까요. 자!"], ["Here is my hand.", "여기 내 손이 있어요."], ["Do we part on this, you visionary boy—or man?”", "우리는 이렇게 헤어지는 건가요, 당신, 몽상에 빠진 소년 — 아니 어른?\""]], [["“O Estella!”", "\"오, 에스텔러!\""], ["I answered, as my bitter tears fell fast on her hand, do what I would to restrain them; “even if I remained in England and could hold my head up with the rest, how could I see you Drummle’s wife?”", "나는 아무리 참으려 해도 그녀의 손 위로 쓰디쓴 눈물이 빠르게 떨어지는 가운데 대답했다. \"설령 내가 영국에 남아 다른 사람들과 어깨를 나란히 하고 고개를 들 수 있다 한들, 당신이 드러믈의 아내가 된 모습을 내가 어찌 볼 수 있겠소?\""]], [["“Nonsense,”", "\"부질없는 소리예요.\""], ["she returned,—“nonsense.", "그녀가 대꾸했다. \"부질없는 소리."], ["This will pass in no time.”", "이런 건 금세 지나갈 거예요.\""]], [["“Never, Estella!”", "\"결코 그렇지 않소, 에스텔러!\""]], [["“You will get me out of your thoughts in a week.”", "\"당신은 일주일이면 나를 생각에서 지워 버릴 거예요.\""]], [["“Out of my thoughts!", "\"생각에서 지운다니!"], ["You are part of my existence, part of myself.", "당신은 내 존재의 일부, 나 자신의 일부요."], ["You have been in every line I have ever read since I first came here, the rough common boy whose poor heart you wounded even then.", "이곳에 처음 온 이후로 — 그토록 당신이 그 가엾은 마음에 상처를 입혔던 그 거칠고 천한 소년이 온 이후로 — 내가 읽은 모든 글줄마다 당신이 있었소."], ["You have been in every prospect I have ever seen since,—on the river, on the sails of the ships, on the marshes, in the clouds, in the light, in the darkness, in the wind, in the woods, in the sea, in the streets.", "그 이후로 내가 본 모든 풍경마다 당신이 있었소 — 강 위에, 배의 돛 위에, 습지에, 구름 속에, 빛 속에, 어둠 속에, 바람 속에, 숲속에, 바다에, 거리에. 당신은 내 마음이 알게 된 모든 우아한 공상의 화신이었소."], ["You have been the embodiment of every graceful fancy that my mind has ever become acquainted with. The stones of which the strongest London buildings are made are not more real, or more impossible to be displaced by your hands, than your presence and influence have been to me, there and everywhere, and will be.", "런던에서 가장 튼튼한 건물들을 이루는 돌덩이들도, 당신의 존재와 영향이 그곳에서나 어디에서나 내게 그러했고 또 앞으로 그러할 만큼 더 실재하지도, 당신의 손으로도 더 옮길 수 없을 만큼 단단하지도 않소. 에스텔러, 내 생의 마지막 순간까지, 당신은 내 인격의 일부, 내 안의 작은 선의 일부, 악의 일부로 남을 수밖에 없소."], ["Estella, to the last hour of my life, you cannot choose but remain part of my character, part of the little good in me, part of the evil.", "그러나 이 이별 속에서, 나는 당신을 오직 그 선과 결부시키겠소. 그리고 나는 언제나 당신을 충실히 그렇게 간직하겠소."], ["But, in this separation, I associate you only with the good; and I will faithfully hold you to that always, for you must have done me far more good than harm, let me feel now what sharp distress I may. O God bless you, God forgive you!”", "지금 내가 아무리 모진 고통을 느낀다 한들, 당신은 분명 내게 해악보다 훨씬 더 큰 선을 베풀어 주었을 테니까. 오, 신의 가호가 당신에게 있기를, 신이 당신을 용서하시기를!\""]], [["In what ecstasy of unhappiness I got these broken words out of myself, I don’t know.", "이 토막 난 말들을 어떤 불행의 황홀경 속에서 나 자신에게서 끄집어냈는지, 나는 모른다."], ["The rhapsody welled up within me, like blood from an inward wound, and gushed out.", "그 격정의 토로는 마치 속으로 난 상처에서 흘러나오는 피처럼 내 안에서 솟구쳐 올라 쏟아져 나왔다."], ["I held her hand to my lips some lingering moments, and so I left her.", "나는 그녀의 손을 입술에 대고 여운 어린 몇 순간을 머물렀고, 그렇게 그녀를 떠났다."], ["But ever afterwards, I remembered,—and soon afterwards with stronger reason,—that while Estella looked at me merely with incredulous wonder, the spectral figure of Miss Havisham, her hand still covering her heart, seemed all resolved into a ghastly stare of pity and remorse.", "그러나 그 후로 줄곧 — 그리고 머지않아 더 강한 까닭으로 — 나는 기억했다. 에스텔러가 그저 믿기지 않는다는 듯한 경이로 나를 바라보는 동안, 손을 여전히 가슴에 얹은 채 유령 같던 해비셤 양의 모습이, 온통 섬뜩한 연민과 회한의 응시로 화한 듯 보였던 것을."]], [["All done, all gone! So much was done and gone, that when I went out at the gate, the light of the day seemed of a darker colour than when I went in.", "다 끝나고, 다 가 버렸다! 너무나 많은 것이 끝나고 가 버려서, 내가 정문을 나설 때 낮의 빛은 들어갈 때보다 더 어두운 빛깔로 보였다."], ["For a while, I hid myself among some lanes and by-paths, and then struck off to walk all the way to London.", "한동안 나는 좁은 길과 샛길 사이로 몸을 숨겼고, 그러다가 런던까지 내내 걸어서 가기로 방향을 잡았다."], ["For, I had by that time come to myself so far as to consider that I could not go back to the inn and see Drummle there; that I could not bear to sit upon the coach and be spoken to; that I could do nothing half so good for myself as tire myself out.", "그 무렵 나는 어느 정도 정신을 차려, 여관으로 돌아가 그곳의 드러믈을 볼 수는 없다는 것, 마차에 앉아 누군가 말을 거는 것을 견딜 수 없다는 것, 나 자신을 위해 할 수 있는 일 가운데 기진맥진하도록 몸을 지치게 하는 것의 절반만큼이라도 좋은 일은 없다는 것을 헤아릴 수 있게 되었다."]], [["It was past midnight when I crossed London Bridge.", "내가 런던교를 건넌 것은 자정이 지나서였다."], ["Pursuing the narrow intricacies of the streets which at that time tended westward near the Middlesex shore of the river, my readiest access to the Temple was close by the river-side, through Whitefriars.", "그 무렵 강의 미들섹스 쪽 기슭 가까이서 서쪽으로 뻗어 있던 거리들의 좁고 뒤얽힌 골목들을 따라가다 보니, 템플로 가는 가장 손쉬운 길은 강가 바로 옆, 화이트프라이어스를 통과하는 것이었다."], ["I was not expected till to-morrow; but I had my keys, and, if Herbert were gone to bed, could get to bed myself without disturbing him.", "나는 내일이나 되어야 올 사람으로 예정되어 있었지만, 열쇠를 가지고 있었으므로, 허버트가 잠자리에 들었다면 그를 깨우지 않고 나도 잠자리에 들 수 있을 터였다."]], [["As it seldom happened that I came in at that Whitefriars gate after the Temple was closed, and as I was very muddy and weary, I did not take it ill that the night-porter examined me with much attention as he held the gate a little way open for me to pass in.", "템플이 닫힌 뒤 그 화이트프라이어스 문으로 내가 들어오는 일은 좀처럼 없었던 데다, 내가 진흙투성이에 몹시 지쳐 있었으므로, 야간 수위가 내가 지나가도록 문을 조금 열어 둔 채 나를 매우 주의 깊게 살피는 것을 나는 언짢게 여기지 않았다."], ["To help his memory I mentioned my name.", "그의 기억을 돕기 위해 나는 내 이름을 댔다."]], [["“I was not quite sure, sir, but I thought so.", "\"확실치는 않았습니다만, 그러시리라 생각했지요."], ["Here’s a note, sir. The messenger that brought it, said would you be so good as read it by my lantern?”", "여기 쪽지가 있습니다, 손님. 이걸 가져온 심부름꾼이 말하길, 제 등불로 이걸 읽어 주시면 고맙겠다더군요.\""]], [["Much surprised by the request, I took the note.", "그 부탁에 몹시 놀라며, 나는 쪽지를 받아 들었다."], ["It was directed to Philip Pip, Esquire, and on the top of the superscription were the words, “PLEASE READ THIS, HERE.”", "그것은 필립 핍 귀하 앞으로 되어 있었고, 수신인 표기 맨 위에는 \"이것을, 여기서, 읽으십시오\"라는 말이 적혀 있었다."], ["I opened it, the watchman holding up his light, and read inside, in Wemmick’s writing,—", "수위가 등불을 들어 비추는 가운데 나는 그것을 펼쳤고, 안에서 웨믹의 필체로 적힌 글을 읽었다. \"집에 가지 마시오.\""]], [["# Chapter XLV.", "# Chapter XLV."]], [["Turning from the Temple gate as soon as I had read the warning, I made the best of my way to Fleet Street, and there got a late hackney chariot and drove to the Hummums in Covent Garden.", "경고문을 읽자마자 나는 템플 정문에서 발길을 돌려 한껏 서둘러 플리트 스트리트로 갔고, 거기서 늦은 시각의 삯마차를 잡아타고 코번트 가든의 허맘스로 향했다."], ["In those times a bed was always to be got there at any hour of the night, and the chamberlain, letting me in at his ready wicket, lighted the candle next in order on his shelf, and showed me straight into the bedroom next in order on his list.", "그 시절에는 한밤중 어느 시각에라도 그곳에서 잠자리를 구할 수 있었으니, 객실 담당자는 늘 열려 있는 쪽문으로 나를 들여보내고, 선반 위에 차례로 놓인 양초 중 다음 것에 불을 붙여, 명부에 적힌 차례대로 다음 침실로 곧장 나를 안내했다."], ["It was a sort of vault on the ground floor at the back, with a despotic monster of a four-post bedstead in it, straddling over the whole place, putting one of his arbitrary legs into the fireplace and another into the doorway, and squeezing the wretched little washing-stand in quite a Divinely Righteous manner.", "그곳은 건물 뒤편 일층에 자리한 일종의 지하실 같은 방이었는데, 그 안에는 사주식 침대라는 폭군 같은 괴물이 방 전체를 가로질러 떡 버티고 서서, 제멋대로 뻗은 다리 하나를 벽난로 속에 들이밀고 또 하나를 출입구에 들이민 채, 가엾고 자그마한 세면대를 자못 거룩하고 정의로운 태도로 짓누르고 있었다."]], [["As I had asked for a night-light, the chamberlain had brought me in, before he left me, the good old constitutional rushlight of those virtuous days—an object like the ghost of a walking-cane, which instantly broke its back if it were touched, which nothing could ever be lighted at, and which was placed in solitary confinement at the bottom of a high tin tower, perforated with round holes that made a staringly wide-awake pattern on the walls.", "내가 야간등을 부탁했던 터라, 객실 담당자는 나를 두고 나가기 전에 그 미덕 넘치던 시절의 유서 깊고 정통적인 골풀 양초를 가져다주었다. 그것은 마치 지팡이의 유령 같은 물건이어서, 건드리기만 하면 곧장 허리가 부러져 버렸고, 어떤 것에도 결코 불을 붙일 수 없었으며, 둥근 구멍이 숭숭 뚫린 높다란 양철 탑 밑바닥에 독방에 갇히듯 놓여 있었으니, 그 구멍들이 벽에 빤히 뜬 눈처럼 잠 못 드는 무늬를 드리웠다."], ["When I had got into bed, and lay there footsore, weary, and wretched, I found that I could no more close my own eyes than I could close the eyes of this foolish Argus.", "침대에 들어가 발이 부르트고 지치고 비참한 몸으로 누워 있노라니, 나는 내 두 눈을 감을 수 없는 것이, 이 어리석은 아르고스의 눈을 감길 수 없는 것과 다를 바 없음을 깨달았다."], ["And thus, in the gloom and death of the night, we stared at one another.", "그리하여 밤의 음울함과 죽음 같은 정적 속에서, 우리는 서로를 빤히 마주 보았다."]], [["What a doleful night!", "얼마나 서글픈 밤이었던가!"], ["How anxious, how dismal, how long!", "얼마나 불안하고, 얼마나 음산하며, 얼마나 길었던가!"], ["There was an inhospitable smell in the room, of cold soot and hot dust; and, as I looked up into the corners of the tester over my head, I thought what a number of blue-bottle flies from the butchers’, and earwigs from the market, and grubs from the country, must be holding on up there, lying by for next summer.", "방 안에는 차가운 검댕과 뜨거운 먼지가 뒤섞인, 손님을 반기지 않는 냄새가 감돌았다. 그리고 머리맡 침대 덮개의 구석구석을 올려다보면서, 나는 푸줏간에서 날아온 쇠파리며, 시장에서 기어든 집게벌레며, 시골에서 들어온 굼벵이가 얼마나 많이 저 위에 매달려 다음 여름을 기다리며 숨죽이고 있을까 생각했다."], ["This led me to speculate whether any of them ever tumbled down, and then I fancied that I felt light falls on my face,—a disagreeable turn of thought, suggesting other and more objectionable approaches up my back.", "그러다 보니 그것들 중 어느 하나라도 굴러떨어지지나 않을까 하는 생각에 이르렀고, 이내 내 얼굴 위로 무언가 가볍게 떨어지는 듯한 느낌이 들었다. 등을 타고 기어오르는 다른, 더욱 거북한 것들의 접근을 떠올리게 하는, 불쾌하기 짝이 없는 상념의 전환이었다."], ["When I had lain awake a little while, those extraordinary voices with which silence teems began to make themselves audible.", "한동안 잠 못 이루고 누워 있자니, 정적이 잔뜩 품고 있는 그 기묘한 목소리들이 차츰 들려오기 시작했다."], ["The closet whispered, the fireplace sighed, the little washing-stand ticked, and one guitar-string played occasionally in the chest of drawers.", "벽장은 속삭이고, 벽난로는 한숨짓고, 자그마한 세면대는 똑딱거렸으며, 서랍장 속에서는 이따금 기타 줄 하나가 울렸다."], ["At about the same time, the eyes on the wall acquired a new expression, and in every one of those staring rounds I saw written, DON’T GO HOME.", "거의 같은 무렵, 벽 위의 그 눈들은 새로운 표정을 띠었고, 빤히 뜬 둥근 눈마다 집에 가지 마라라고 쓰인 것이 보였다."]], [["Whatever night-fancies and night-noises crowded on me, they never warded off this DON’T GO HOME.", "밤의 온갖 망상과 밤의 온갖 소음이 내게 몰려들어도, 그것들은 결코 이 집에 가지 마라를 물리치지 못했다."], ["It plaited itself into whatever I thought of, as a bodily pain would have done.", "그것은 마치 육체의 고통이 그러하듯, 내가 무엇을 생각하든 그 속에 얽혀 들었다."], ["Not long before, I had read in the newspapers, how a gentleman unknown had come to the Hummums in the night, and had gone to bed, and had destroyed himself, and had been found in the morning weltering in blood.", "얼마 전 나는 신문에서, 어느 알려지지 않은 신사가 밤중에 허맘스에 와서 잠자리에 들었다가 스스로 목숨을 끊고는 이튿날 아침 피투성이로 뒹군 채 발견되었다는 기사를 읽은 적이 있었다."], ["It came into my head that he must have occupied this very vault of mine, and I got out of bed to assure myself that there were no red marks about; then opened the door to look out into the passages, and cheer myself with the companionship of a distant light, near which I knew the chamberlain to be dozing.", "그자가 바로 이 나의 지하실 같은 방을 썼던 게 틀림없다는 생각이 머리에 떠올랐고, 나는 침대에서 일어나 핏자국 같은 것이 없는지 직접 확인했다. 그러고는 문을 열고 복도를 내다보며, 객실 담당자가 졸고 있으리라 짐작되는 저 멀리 등불의 동행으로 마음을 달랬다."], ["But all this time, why I was not to go home, and what had happened at home, and when I should go home, and whether Provis was safe at home, were questions occupying my mind so busily, that one might have supposed there could be no more room in it for any other theme.", "그러나 이 모든 와중에도, 어째서 내가 집에 가서는 안 되는지, 집에서 무슨 일이 일어났는지, 언제 집에 가야 하는지, 프로비스는 집에서 무사한지 하는 의문들이 어찌나 분주히 내 머릿속을 차지하고 있던지, 다른 어떤 주제가 들어설 자리가 더는 없으리라 여겨질 정도였다."], ["Even when I thought of Estella, and how we had parted that day forever, and when I recalled all the circumstances of our parting, and all her looks and tones, and the action of her fingers while she knitted,—even then I was pursuing, here and there and everywhere, the caution, Don’t go home.", "에스텔라를 떠올리며, 그날 우리가 영영 작별했음을, 그 작별의 온갖 정황과 그녀의 갖가지 표정과 말투, 뜨개질하던 손가락의 움직임을 되새길 때조차도, 나는 여기서고 저기서고 어디서고 그 경고를, 집에 가지 마라를 좇고 있었다."], ["When at last I dozed, in sheer exhaustion of mind and body, it became a vast shadowy verb which I had to conjugate.", "마침내 심신이 완전히 소진되어 깜박 졸음에 빠져들자, 그것은 내가 활용해야 할 거대하고 어렴풋한 동사가 되었다."], ["Imperative mood, present tense: Do not thou go home, let him not go home, let us not go home, do not ye or you go home, let not them go home.", "명령법 현재 시제로, 너는 집에 가지 마라, 그로 하여금 집에 가지 못하게 하라, 우리 집에 가지 말자, 너희는 집에 가지 마라, 그들로 하여금 집에 가지 못하게 하라."], ["Then potentially: I may not and I cannot go home; and I might not, could not, would not, and should not go home; until I felt that I was going distracted, and rolled over on the pillow, and looked at the staring rounds upon the wall again.", "그다음에는 가능법으로, 나는 집에 가서는 안 되고 갈 수도 없다, 나는 집에 가지 않을지도, 갈 수 없을지도, 가려 하지 않을지도, 가서는 안 될지도 모른다. 그러다 결국 정신이 산란해지는 것을 느끼고는, 베개 위로 몸을 뒤척이며 벽에 빤히 뜬 둥근 눈들을 다시 바라보았다."]], [["I had left directions that I was to be called at seven; for it was plain that I must see Wemmick before seeing any one else, and equally plain that this was a case in which his Walworth sentiments only could be taken.", "나는 일곱 시에 깨워 달라는 당부를 남겨 두었다. 다른 누구를 만나기에 앞서 웨믹부터 만나야 함이 분명했고, 또한 이번 일이 오직 그의 월워스식 감정만을 취할 수 있는 경우라는 것 역시 분명했기 때문이다."], ["It was a relief to get out of the room where the night had been so miserable, and I needed no second knocking at the door to startle me from my uneasy bed.", "그토록 비참한 밤을 보낸 방에서 나오게 된 것은 다행한 일이었고, 불편한 잠자리에서 화들짝 깨어나는 데 문을 두 번 두드릴 필요도 없었다."]], [["The Castle battlements arose upon my view at eight o’clock.", "여덟 시에 그 성의 흉벽이 내 눈앞에 솟아올랐다."], ["The little servant happening to be entering the fortress with two hot rolls, I passed through the postern and crossed the drawbridge in her company, and so came without announcement into the presence of Wemmick as he was making tea for himself and the Aged.", "마침 어린 하녀가 따끈한 빵 두 개를 들고 그 요새로 들어가던 참이라, 나는 그녀와 동행하여 뒷문을 지나 도개교를 건넜고, 그리하여 별다른 기별 없이, 자신과 노친을 위해 차를 끓이고 있던 웨믹 앞에 이르렀다."], ["An open door afforded a perspective view of the Aged in bed.", "열린 문 너머로 침대에 누운 노친의 모습이 멀찍이 보였다."]], [["“Halloa, Mr. Pip!”", "\"이런, 핍 씨!\""], ["said Wemmick.", "웨믹이 말했다."], ["“You did come home, then?”", "\"그래도 시내로 돌아오셨군요?\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"네.\""], ["I returned; “but I didn’t go home.”", "나는 대답했다. \"하지만 집에는 가지 않았습니다.\""]], [["“That’s all right,”", "\"잘하셨습니다.\""], ["said he, rubbing his hands.", "그가 두 손을 비비며 말했다."], ["“I left a note for you at each of the Temple gates, on the chance.", "\"혹시나 해서 템플 정문마다 쪽지를 하나씩 남겨 두었지요. 어느 문으로 오셨습니까?\""], ["Which gate did you come to?”", "나는 어느 문이었는지 말해 주었다."]], [["I told him. “I’ll go round to the others in the course of the day and destroy the notes,”", "\"오늘 중에 나머지 문들을 돌아 그 쪽지들을 없애야겠군요.\" 웨믹이 말했다."], ["said Wemmick; “it’s a good rule never to leave documentary evidence if you can help it, because you don’t know when it may be put in.", "\"되도록이면 문서로 된 증거는 결코 남기지 않는 게 좋은 원칙입니다. 그게 언제 들이밀어질지 모르니까요."], ["I’m going to take a liberty with you.", "한 가지 실례를 무릅쓰겠습니다."], ["Would you mind toasting this sausage for the Aged P.?”", "노친께 드릴 이 소시지를 좀 구워 주시겠습니까?\""]], [["I said I should be delighted to do it.", "나는 기꺼이 그러겠다고 말했다."]], [["“Then you can go about your work, Mary Anne,”", "\"그럼 너는 네 일을 보러 가도 되겠다, 메리 앤.\""], ["said Wemmick to the little servant; “which leaves us to ourselves, don’t you see, Mr. Pip?”", "웨믹이 어린 하녀에게 말했다. \"그러면 우리끼리만 남게 되는 셈이지요, 안 그렇습니까, 핍 씨?\""], ["he added, winking, as she disappeared.", "하녀가 사라지자 그는 눈을 찡긋하며 덧붙였다."]], [["I thanked him for his friendship and caution, and our discourse proceeded in a low tone, while I toasted the Aged’s sausage and he buttered the crumb of the Aged’s roll.", "나는 그의 우정과 신중함에 고마움을 표했고, 내가 노친의 소시지를 굽고 그가 노친의 빵 속살에 버터를 바르는 동안, 우리의 대화는 낮은 목소리로 이어졌다."]], [["“Now, Mr. Pip, you know,”", "\"자, 핍 씨, 아시다시피,\""], ["said Wemmick, “you and I understand one another.", "웨믹이 말했다. \"당신과 나는 서로를 이해하고 있지요."], ["We are in our private and personal capacities, and we have been engaged in a confidential transaction before to-day.", "우리는 지금 사사로운 개인 자격으로 있는 것이고, 또 우리는 오늘 이전에도 은밀한 거래를 함께한 적이 있습니다."], ["Official sentiments are one thing. We are extra official.”", "공적인 감정은 별개의 것이지요. 우리는 공무 바깥에 있는 겁니다.\""]], [["I cordially assented.", "나는 진심으로 동의했다."], ["I was so very nervous, that I had already lighted the Aged’s sausage like a torch, and been obliged to blow it out.", "어찌나 긴장했던지, 나는 이미 노친의 소시지를 횃불처럼 활활 태워 버려 입으로 불어 꺼야 했다."]], [["“I accidentally heard, yesterday morning,”", "\"어제 아침에 우연히 들었습니다.\" 웨믹이 말했다."], ["said Wemmick, “being in a certain place where I once took you,—even between you and me, it’s as well not to mention names when avoidable—”", "\"언젠가 당신을 데려간 적이 있는 어떤 장소에 있다가 말이지요. 당신과 나 사이라 해도, 피할 수 있다면 이름은 거론하지 않는 편이 좋겠습니다만.\""]], [["“Much better not,”", "\"거론하지 않는 편이 훨씬 낫지요.\""], ["said I.", "내가 말했다."], ["“I understand you.”", "\"무슨 말씀인지 알겠습니다.\""]], [["“I heard there by chance, yesterday morning,”", "\"어제 아침에 우연히 거기서 들었는데,\" 웨믹이 말했다."], ["said Wemmick, “that a certain person not altogether of uncolonial pursuits, and not unpossessed of portable property,—I don’t know who it may really be,—we won’t name this person—”", "\"전적으로 식민지와 무관한 일에 종사한다고는 할 수 없고, 또 운반 가능한 재산을 지니지 않았다고도 할 수 없는 어떤 인물이 말입니다. 그게 정말 누구인지는 나도 모릅니다만, 이 인물의 이름은 거론하지 않기로 하지요.\""]], [["“Not necessary,”", "\"그럴 필요 없습니다.\""], ["said I.", "내가 말했다."]], [["“—Had made some little stir in a certain part of the world where a good many people go, not always in gratification of their own inclinations, and not quite irrespective of the government expense—”", "\"꽤 많은 사람들이 가는, 세상 어느 한 지역에서 약간의 소동을 일으켰다는 겁니다. 그곳에 가는 자들이 늘 제 좋아서 가는 것도 아니고, 또 정부의 비용과 전혀 무관하지도 않은 곳이지요.\""]], [["In watching his face, I made quite a firework of the Aged’s sausage, and greatly discomposed both my own attention and Wemmick’s; for which I apologised.", "그의 얼굴을 살피느라, 나는 노친의 소시지를 그야말로 불꽃놀이로 만들어 버려서 나 자신의 주의력은 물론 웨믹의 주의력까지 크게 흩뜨려 놓았으니, 그 점에 대해 사과했다."]], [["“—By disappearing from such place, and being no more heard of thereabouts.", "\"바로 그곳에서 사라져, 그 일대에서 더는 소식이 들리지 않게 됨으로써 말이지요."], ["From which,”", "그로 인해,\""], ["said Wemmick, “conjectures had been raised and theories formed.", "웨믹이 말했다. \"추측이 일고 갖가지 억측이 세워졌다는 겁니다."], ["I also heard that you at your chambers in Garden Court, Temple, had been watched, and might be watched again.”", "또 들으니, 템플 가든 코트에 있는 당신의 거처가 감시당해 왔으며, 앞으로도 다시 감시당할 수 있다더군요.\""]], [["“By whom?”", "\"누구한테요?\""], ["said I.", "내가 말했다."]], [["“I wouldn’t go into that,”", "\"그건 파고들지 않는 게 좋겠습니다.\""], ["said Wemmick, evasively, “it might clash with official responsibilities.", "웨믹이 둘러대며 말했다. \"공적인 책무와 충돌할 수도 있으니까요."], ["I heard it, as I have in my time heard other curious things in the same place.", "나는 그 말을, 같은 장소에서 그동안 다른 기이한 일들을 들어 온 것처럼 들었을 뿐입니다."], ["I don’t tell it you on information received. I heard it.”", "입수한 정보를 근거로 당신에게 말하는 게 아니라, 그저 들은 겁니다.\""]], [["He took the toasting-fork and sausage from me as he spoke, and set forth the Aged’s breakfast neatly on a little tray.", "그는 그렇게 말하면서 내게서 굽는 포크와 소시지를 받아 들고는, 작은 쟁반 위에 노친의 아침 식사를 정갈하게 차렸다."], ["Previous to placing it before him, he went into the Aged’s room with a clean white cloth, and tied the same under the old gentleman’s chin, and propped him up, and put his nightcap on one side, and gave him quite a rakish air.", "그것을 노친 앞에 놓기에 앞서, 그는 깨끗한 흰 천을 들고 노친의 방으로 들어가 그 노신사의 턱 밑에 그것을 매어 주고, 몸을 받쳐 일으켜 세우고, 나이트캡을 한쪽으로 비스듬히 씌워 자못 멋들어진 분위기를 만들어 주었다."], ["Then he placed his breakfast before him with great care, and said, “All right, ain’t you, Aged P.?”", "그러고는 더없이 정성스레 아침 식사를 그 앞에 차려 놓고 말했다. \"이만하면 됐지요, 노친?\""], ["To which the cheerful Aged replied, “All right, John, my boy, all right!”", "그러자 명랑한 노친이 대답했다. \"됐다, 존, 얘야, 됐어!\""], ["As there seemed to be a tacit understanding that the Aged was not in a presentable state, and was therefore to be considered invisible, I made a pretence of being in complete ignorance of these proceedings.", "노친이 남 앞에 내놓을 만한 차림새가 아니어서 보이지 않는 존재로 여겨야 한다는 무언의 양해가 있는 듯했으므로, 나는 이 모든 일을 전혀 모르는 척 꾸몄다."]], [["“This watching of me at my chambers (which I have once had reason to suspect),”", "\"내 거처에서 나를 감시한다는 이 일은(나도 한번 의심할 만한 까닭이 있었습니다만),\""], ["I said to Wemmick when he came back, “is inseparable from the person to whom you have adverted; is it?”", "웨믹이 돌아왔을 때 내가 그에게 말했다. \"방금 당신이 언급한 그 인물과 떼어 놓을 수 없는 일이지요, 그렇습니까?\""]], [["Wemmick looked very serious.", "웨믹은 자못 심각한 표정을 지었다."], ["“I couldn’t undertake to say that, of my own knowledge.", "\"그것까지는 내 직접 아는 바로 단언할 수 없습니다."], ["I mean, I couldn’t undertake to say it was at first.", "그러니까, 처음부터 그랬다고는 단언할 수 없다는 말이지요."], ["But it either is, or it will be, or it’s in great danger of being.”", "하지만 그것은 그렇거나, 그렇게 될 것이거나, 그렇게 될 위험이 다분합니다.\""]], [["As I saw that he was restrained by fealty to Little Britain from saying as much as he could, and as I knew with thankfulness to him how far out of his way he went to say what he did, I could not press him.", "그가 리틀 브리튼에 대한 충심에 매여 자기가 할 수 있는 만큼 다 말하지는 못하고 있음을 알았고, 또 그가 이만큼 말해 주려고 얼마나 자기 본분에서 벗어나 애쓰고 있는지 고맙게 헤아렸던 터라, 나는 그를 다그칠 수 없었다."], ["But I told him, after a little meditation over the fire, that I would like to ask him a question, subject to his answering or not answering, as he deemed right, and sure that his course would be right.", "다만 나는 난롯불 앞에서 잠시 곰곰이 생각한 뒤, 그에게 한 가지 질문을 하고 싶다고 말했다. 답하든 답하지 않든 옳다고 여기는 대로 하시라고, 그리고 그가 택하는 길이 옳으리라 믿는다고 덧붙였다."], ["He paused in his breakfast, and crossing his arms, and pinching his shirt-sleeves (his notion of in-door comfort was to sit without any coat), he nodded to me once, to put my question.", "그는 아침 식사를 멈추고, 두 팔을 포갠 채 셔츠 소매를 집어 올리며(실내에서 편안하게 지내는 그의 방식이란 외투 없이 앉아 있는 것이었다), 내게 한 번 고개를 끄덕여 질문을 해 보라는 뜻을 내비쳤다."]], [["“You have heard of a man of bad character, whose true name is Compeyson?”", "\"본명이 콤피슨인 평판 나쁜 사내에 대해 들어 보셨지요?\" 그는 또 한 번 고개를 끄덕여 답했다."]], [["He answered with one other nod.", "\"그자가 살아 있습니까?\""]], [["“Is he living?” One other nod.", "또 한 번 고개를 끄덕였다."]], [["“Is he in London?”", "\"런던에 있습니까?\""]], [["He gave me one other nod, compressed the post-office exceedingly, gave me one last nod, and went on with his breakfast.", "그는 또 한 번 고개를 끄덕이고는, 우체통 같은 입을 한껏 오므렸다가, 마지막으로 한 번 더 고개를 끄덕이고 다시 아침 식사를 이어 갔다."]], [["“Now,”", "\"자,\""], ["said Wemmick, “questioning being over,”", "웨믹이 말했다. \"질문은 끝났으니\""], ["which he emphasised and repeated for my guidance, “I come to what I did, after hearing what I heard.", "하고, 그는 내가 새겨듣도록 이 말을 힘주어 되풀이했다. \"내가 들은 바를 듣고 나서 한 일에 대해 말하지요."], ["I went to Garden Court to find you; not finding you, I went to Clarriker’s to find Mr. Herbert.”", "나는 당신을 찾으러 가든 코트에 갔습니다. 당신이 없기에, 허버트 씨를 찾으러 클래리커 상회로 갔지요.\""]], [["“And him you found?”", "\"그래서 그를 만나셨습니까?\""], ["said I, with great anxiety.", "나는 몹시 불안해하며 말했다."]], [["“And him I found.", "\"만났지요."], ["Without mentioning any names or going into any details, I gave him to understand that if he was aware of anybody—Tom, Jack, or Richard—being about the chambers, or about the immediate neighbourhood, he had better get Tom, Jack, or Richard out of the way while you were out of the way.”", "이름은 일절 거론하지 않고 세세한 내막도 들추지 않은 채, 그에게 이렇게 일러두었습니다. 만약 누군가가, 톰이든 잭이든 리처드든, 그 거처 근처나 바로 이웃에 어슬렁거리는 걸 그가 알고 있다면, 당신이 자리를 비운 동안 그 톰이든 잭이든 리처드든 어디 멀찍이 치워 두는 게 좋겠다고 말입니다.\""]], [["“He would be greatly puzzled what to do?”", "\"그가 어떻게 해야 할지 몹시 난감해했겠군요?\""]], [["“He was puzzled what to do; not the less, because I gave him my opinion that it was not safe to try to get Tom, Jack, or Richard too far out of the way at present. Mr.", "\"난감해하긴 했지요. 게다가 지금 당장 톰이든 잭이든 리처드든 너무 멀리 치워 두려 드는 건 안전하지 않다고 내가 의견을 밝혔으니 더욱 그러했습니다."], ["Pip, I’ll tell you something.", "핍 씨, 한 가지 말씀드리지요."], ["Under existing circumstances, there is no place like a great city when you are once in it.", "지금 같은 상황에서는, 일단 들어와 있기만 하다면 대도시만 한 곳이 없습니다. 너무 일찍 몸을 드러내지 마십시오."], ["Don’t break cover too soon.", "바짝 엎드려 계세요."], ["Lie close. Wait till things slacken, before you try the open, even for foreign air.”", "사정이 누그러질 때까지 기다렸다가, 바깥으로 나서십시오. 외국 공기를 쐬러 가는 것이라 해도 말이지요.\""]], [["I thanked him for his valuable advice, and asked him what Herbert had done?", "나는 그 값진 충고에 고마움을 표하고, 허버트가 어떻게 했는지 물었다."]], [["“Mr. Herbert,”", "\"허버트 씨는,\""], ["said Wemmick, “after being all of a heap for half an hour, struck out a plan.", "웨믹이 말했다. \"반 시간쯤 넋이 나가 있다가 한 가지 방책을 떠올렸습니다."], ["He mentioned to me as a secret, that he is courting a young lady who has, as no doubt you are aware, a bedridden Pa.", "그가 비밀이라며 내게 일러 주기를, 자기가 어느 젊은 아가씨와 사귀고 있는데, 당신도 분명 알고 계시겠지만, 그 아가씨에게는 자리보전한 아버지가 계시다는 겁니다."], ["Which Pa, having been in the Purser line of life, lies a-bed in a bow-window where he can see the ships sail up and down the river.", "그 아버지는 한때 사무장 일을 하던 분이라, 강을 오르내리는 배들을 내다볼 수 있는 활처럼 굽은 내닫이창 곁 침대에 누워 지낸다더군요."], ["You are acquainted with the young lady, most probably?”", "당신도 그 젊은 아가씨와 아마 아는 사이겠지요?\""]], [["“Not personally,”", "\"직접 아는 사이는 아닙니다.\""], ["said I.", "내가 말했다."]], [["The truth was, that she had objected to me as an expensive companion who did Herbert no good, and that, when Herbert had first proposed to present me to her, she had received the proposal with such very moderate warmth, that Herbert had felt himself obliged to confide the state of the case to me, with a view to the lapse of a little time before I made her acquaintance.", "사실인즉, 그녀는 나를 두고 허버트에게 아무 보탬도 안 되는 돈만 많이 드는 친구라며 못마땅해했고, 허버트가 처음 나를 그녀에게 소개하겠다고 했을 때 그 제안을 어찌나 미적지근하게 받아들였던지, 허버트는 내가 그녀와 인사를 나누기까지 얼마간 시간을 두는 게 좋겠다는 뜻으로 그 사정을 내게 털어놓을 수밖에 없었던 것이다."], ["When I had begun to advance Herbert’s prospects by stealth, I had been able to bear this with cheerful philosophy: he and his affianced, for their part, had naturally not been very anxious to introduce a third person into their interviews; and thus, although I was assured that I had risen in Clara’s esteem, and although the young lady and I had long regularly interchanged messages and remembrances by Herbert, I had never seen her.", "내가 몰래 허버트의 앞날을 돕기 시작한 뒤로, 나는 이 일을 명랑한 달관으로 견딜 수 있었다. 그들 두 사람, 그와 그의 약혼녀로서도 당연히 둘만의 만남에 제삼자를 끼워 넣고 싶어 하지 않았을 터였다. 그리하여 비록 내가 클라라의 마음속에서 평판이 높아졌다는 말을 들었고, 또 그 젊은 아가씨와 내가 오래도록 허버트를 통해 꼬박꼬박 안부와 인사를 주고받기는 했어도, 나는 그녀를 한 번도 본 적이 없었다."], ["However, I did not trouble Wemmick with these particulars.", "어쨌거나 나는 이런 세세한 사정으로 웨믹을 번거롭게 하지는 않았다."]], [["“The house with the bow-window,”", "\"그 내닫이창이 달린 집이,\""], ["said Wemmick, “being by the river-side, down the Pool there between Limehouse and Greenwich, and being kept, it seems, by a very respectable widow who has a furnished upper floor to let, Mr.", "웨믹이 말했다. \"라임하우스와 그리니치 사이의 그 풀 강가에 있는데, 듣자 하니 가구가 갖춰진 위층을 세놓고 있는 아주 점잖은 과부가 꾸려 가는 집이랍니다."], ["Herbert put it to me, what did I think of that as a temporary tenement for Tom, Jack, or Richard?", "그래서 허버트 씨가 내게 묻기를, 그곳을 톰이든 잭이든 리처드든의 임시 거처로 삼는 게 어떻겠느냐는 것이었지요."], ["Now, I thought very well of it, for three reasons I’ll give you. That is to say: Firstly.", "그런데 나는 그곳을 아주 마음에 들어 했습니다. 세 가지 이유에서였는데, 그것을 말씀드리지요."], ["It’s altogether out of all your beats, and is well away from the usual heap of streets great and small. Secondly.", "즉, 첫째. 그곳은 당신이 다니는 길과는 전혀 동떨어진 곳이고, 크고 작은 거리들이 흔히 몰린 데서도 멀찍이 떨어져 있습니다. 둘째."], ["Without going near it yourself, you could always hear of the safety of Tom, Jack, or Richard, through Mr. Herbert.", "당신이 직접 그 근처에 가지 않고도, 허버트 씨를 통해 톰이든 잭이든 리처드든의 안위를 늘 전해 들을 수 있지요. 셋째."], ["Thirdly. After a while and when it might be prudent, if you should want to slip Tom, Jack, or Richard on board a foreign packet-boat, there he is—ready.”", "얼마 뒤 신중을 기하는 게 마땅한 때가 와서, 만약 당신이 톰이든 잭이든 리처드든을 외국으로 가는 우편선에 슬쩍 태우고자 한다면, 거기 바로 — 채비가 되어 있는 셈이지요.\""]], [["Much comforted by these considerations, I thanked Wemmick again and again, and begged him to proceed.", "이런 고려들에 적잖이 마음이 놓인 나는, 웨믹에게 거듭거듭 고마움을 표하고 말을 이어 달라 청했다."]], [["“Well, sir! Mr. Herbert threw himself into the business with a will, and by nine o’clock last night he housed Tom, Jack, or Richard,—whichever it may be,—you and I don’t want to know,—quite successfully.", "\"자, 그래서 말입니다! 허버트 씨는 그 일에 흔쾌히 몸을 던졌고, 어젯밤 아홉 시 무렵에는 톰이든 잭이든 리처드든을 — 그게 누구든 간에, 당신이나 나나 알 필요는 없습니다만 — 아주 성공적으로 거처에 들여놓았습니다."], ["At the old lodgings it was understood that he was summoned to Dover, and, in fact, he was taken down the Dover road and cornered out of it.", "예전 하숙에서는 그가 도버로 불려 갔다고 알려졌고, 실제로 그를 도버 가도로 데리고 내려갔다가 거기서 빠져나오게 한 거지요."], ["Now, another great advantage of all this is, that it was done without you, and when, if any one was concerning himself about your movements, you must be known to be ever so many miles off and quite otherwise engaged.", "그런데 이 모든 일의 또 다른 큰 이점은, 당신 없이 해치워졌다는 데 있습니다. 그래서 만약 누군가가 당신의 동태에 신경을 쓰고 있었다면, 당신은 몇십 마일이나 떨어진 데서 아주 딴 일에 매여 있는 걸로 알려져 있을 게 틀림없거든요."], ["This diverts suspicion and confuses it; and for the same reason I recommended that, even if you came back last night, you should not go home.", "이것이 의심을 딴 데로 돌리고 헷갈리게 만들지요. 같은 이유로 나는, 설령 당신이 어젯밤 돌아온다 하더라도 집에는 가지 말라고 권했던 겁니다."], ["It brings in more confusion, and you want confusion.”", "그래야 혼란이 더 보태지고, 당신에게는 그 혼란이 필요하니까요.\""]], [["Wemmick, having finished his breakfast, here looked at his watch, and began to get his coat on.", "아침 식사를 마친 웨믹은 여기서 시계를 들여다보더니, 외투를 걸치기 시작했다."]], [["“And now, Mr. Pip,”", "\"자 이제, 핍 씨,\""], ["said he, with his hands still in the sleeves, “I have probably done the most I can do; but if I can ever do more,—from a Walworth point of view, and in a strictly private and personal capacity,—I shall be glad to do it.", "그가 두 손을 아직 소매에 꿴 채 말했다. \"나는 아마 내가 할 수 있는 일은 거의 다 한 것 같습니다. 하지만 앞으로 더 할 수 있는 일이 있다면 — 월워스의 관점에서, 또 철저히 사사로운 개인 자격으로 — 기꺼이 하겠습니다."], ["Here’s the address.", "여기 주소가 있습니다."], ["There can be no harm in your going here to-night, and seeing for yourself that all is well with Tom, Jack, or Richard, before you go home,—which is another reason for your not going home last night.", "집에 가기 전에 오늘 밤 이리로 가서, 톰이든 잭이든 리처드든이 무탈한지 직접 확인하셔도 해로울 건 없겠지요. 그러는 게 어젯밤 집에 가지 말아야 했던 또 하나의 이유이기도 합니다."], ["But, after you have gone home, don’t go back here.", "다만, 집에 다녀온 뒤에는 이리로 다시 오지 마십시오."], ["You are very welcome, I am sure, Mr.", "정말이지 언제든 환영입니다, 핍 씨.\""], ["Pip”; his hands were now out of his sleeves, and I was shaking them; “and let me finally impress one important point upon you.”", "이제 그의 두 손이 소매에서 나와 있었고, 나는 그 손을 맞잡고 흔들고 있었다. \"그리고 마지막으로 한 가지 중요한 점을 새겨 두시도록 하겠습니다.\""], ["He laid his hands upon my shoulders, and added in a solemn whisper: “Avail yourself of this evening to lay hold of his portable property.", "그는 두 손을 내 어깨에 얹고는, 자못 엄숙한 속삭임으로 덧붙였다. \"오늘 저녁을 이용해 그의 운반 가능한 재산을 손에 넣으십시오."], ["You don’t know what may happen to him.", "그에게 무슨 일이 닥칠지 모릅니다."], ["Don’t let anything happen to the portable property.”", "그 운반 가능한 재산에는 아무 일도 일어나지 않게 하십시오.\""]], [["Quite despairing of making my mind clear to Wemmick on this point, I forbore to try.", "이 점에 관해서는 웨믹에게 내 뜻을 분명히 이해시키기를 도무지 단념한 터라, 나는 굳이 시도하지 않았다."]], [["“Time’s up,” said Wemmick, “and I must be off.", "\"시간이 다 됐군요.\" 웨믹이 말했다."], ["If you had nothing more pressing to do than to keep here till dark, that’s what I should advise.", "\"나는 가 봐야겠습니다. 만약 어두워질 때까지 여기 머무는 것보다 더 급한 일이 없다면, 나로서는 그러시기를 권하겠습니다."], ["You look very much worried, and it would do you good to have a perfectly quiet day with the Aged,—he’ll be up presently,—and a little bit of—you remember the pig?”", "몹시 근심에 차 보이는군요. 노친과 함께 더없이 조용한 하루를 보내시면 좋을 겁니다 — 그분도 곧 일어나실 거고요 — 그리고 한 점쯤 — 그 돼지 기억하시지요?\""]], [["“Of course,” said I.", "\"물론이지요.\" 내가 말했다."]], [["“Well; and a little bit of him.", "\"네. 그놈을 한 점 드시는 것 말입니다."], ["That sausage you toasted was his, and he was in all respects a first-rater.", "당신이 구운 그 소시지가 그놈이었는데, 그놈은 어느 모로 보나 일등급이었지요."], ["Do try him, if it is only for old acquaintance sake.", "그저 옛정을 봐서라도 한번 맛보십시오."], ["Good-bye, Aged Parent!”", "안녕히, 노친 어른!\""], ["in a cheery shout.", "그가 명랑하게 외쳤다."]], [["“All right, John; all right, my boy!” piped the old man from within.", "\"됐다, 존, 됐어, 얘야!\" 안에서 노인이 가느다란 목소리로 화답했다."]], [["I soon fell asleep before Wemmick’s fire, and the Aged and I enjoyed one another’s society by falling asleep before it more or less all day.", "나는 이내 웨믹의 난롯불 앞에서 잠이 들었고, 노친과 나는 하루 종일 그 앞에서 이래저래 잠에 빠져들며 서로의 벗이 되어 주는 즐거움을 누렸다."], ["We had loin of pork for dinner, and greens grown on the estate; and I nodded at the Aged with a good intention whenever I failed to do it drowsily.", "점심으로는 돼지 허릿살에 그 영지에서 기른 푸성귀를 곁들여 먹었다. 그리고 졸음에 겨워 끄덕이지 못할 때면, 나는 좋은 뜻으로 노친에게 일부러 고개를 끄덕여 보였다."], ["When it was quite dark, I left the Aged preparing the fire for toast; and I inferred from the number of teacups, as well as from his glances at the two little doors in the wall, that Miss Skiffins was expected.", "날이 완전히 어두워지자, 나는 토스트를 구우려 불을 지피는 노친을 남겨 두고 자리를 떴다. 찻잔의 수효로 보나, 벽에 달린 그 자그마한 두 문을 향한 그의 눈길로 보나, 나는 스키핀스 양이 올 예정임을 짐작할 수 있었다."]], [["# Chapter XLVI.", "# Chapter XLVI."]], [["Eight o’clock had struck before I got into the air, that was scented, not disagreeably, by the chips and shavings of the long-shore boat-builders, and mast, oar, and block makers.", "본문에 들어섰을 때는 이미 여덟 시를 알리는 종이 친 뒤였는데, 강가의 보트 건조공들과 돛대, 노, 도르래 만드는 이들이 남긴 나뭇조각과 대팻밥 냄새가 코를 찔렀으나 불쾌하지는 않았다."], ["All that water-side region of the upper and lower Pool below Bridge was unknown ground to me; and when I struck down by the river, I found that the spot I wanted was not where I had supposed it to be, and was anything but easy to find.", "다리 아래쪽, 풀 상류와 하류에 걸친 그 강변 일대는 내게 낯선 땅이었다. 그래서 강을 따라 내려가다 보니, 내가 찾던 곳이 짐작했던 자리에 있지 않았고, 도무지 찾기가 쉽지 않았다."], ["It was called Mill Pond Bank, Chinks’s Basin; and I had no other guide to Chinks’s Basin than the Old Green Copper Rope-walk.", "그곳은 칭크스 만의 밀폰드 둑이라 불렸는데, 칭크스 만을 찾는 데 내가 의지할 길잡이라고는 오래된 푸른 구리 밧줄 제조장밖에 없었다."]], [["It matters not what stranded ships repairing in dry docks I lost myself among, what old hulls of ships in course of being knocked to pieces, what ooze and slime and other dregs of tide, what yards of ship-builders and ship-breakers, what rusty anchors blindly biting into the ground, though for years off duty, what mountainous country of accumulated casks and timber, how many rope-walks that were not the Old Green Copper.", "내가 마른 선거에서 수리 중인 좌초선들 사이에서 얼마나 길을 잃었는지, 산산조각으로 부서지고 있던 낡은 배의 선체들이 얼마나 많았는지, 진창과 점액과 조수가 남긴 온갖 찌꺼기가 어떠했는지, 조선공들과 해체공들의 작업장이 어떠했는지, 몇 해 동안 쓰이지 않았으면서도 맹목적으로 땅을 물어뜯고 있던 녹슨 닻들이 어떠했는지, 쌓아 올린 통과 목재가 산악 지대처럼 이루어진 광경이 어떠했는지, 오래된 푸른 구리 제조장이 아닌 밧줄 제조장이 또 얼마나 많았는지는 따질 필요가 없겠다."], ["After several times falling short of my destination and as often overshooting it, I came unexpectedly round a corner, upon Mill Pond Bank.", "목적지에 몇 번이나 못 미쳤다가 또 그만큼 지나치기를 거듭한 끝에, 나는 뜻밖에도 어느 모퉁이를 돌아서서 밀폰드 둑에 이르렀다."], ["It was a fresh kind of place, all circumstances considered, where the wind from the river had room to turn itself round; and there were two or three trees in it, and there was the stump of a ruined windmill, and there was the Old Green Copper Rope-walk,—whose long and narrow vista I could trace in the moonlight, along a series of wooden frames set in the ground, that looked like superannuated haymaking-rakes which had grown old and lost most of their teeth.", "모든 사정을 헤아려 볼 때 그곳은 산뜻한 편의 장소였으니, 강에서 불어오는 바람이 몸을 돌릴 여유가 있는 곳이었다. 그 안에는 나무가 두세 그루 있었고, 허물어진 풍차의 그루터기가 있었으며, 오래된 푸른 구리 밧줄 제조장이 있었다. 그 길고 좁은 풍경은 달빛 속에서 땅에 박힌 일련의 나무 틀을 따라 더듬어 볼 수 있었는데, 그 틀들은 마치 나이가 들어 이가 거의 다 빠진 낡은 건초 갈퀴처럼 보였다."]], [["Selecting from the few queer houses upon Mill Pond Bank a house with a wooden front and three stories of bow-window (not bay-window, which is another thing), I looked at the plate upon the door, and read there, Mrs. Whimple.", "밀폰드 둑에 늘어선 몇 채의 기묘한 집들 가운데서, 정면이 나무로 되어 있고 삼층에 활꼴 창문(베이 창과는 다른, 별개의 것이다)이 달린 집을 골라, 나는 문에 붙은 명패를 보고 거기서 윔플 부인이라는 이름을 읽었다."], ["That being the name I wanted, I knocked, and an elderly woman of a pleasant and thriving appearance responded.", "그것이 내가 찾던 이름이었으므로, 나는 문을 두드렸고, 유쾌하고 풍채 좋은 노부인이 나왔다."], ["She was immediately deposed, however, by Herbert, who silently led me into the parlour and shut the door.", "그러나 부인은 곧 허버트에게 자리를 내주었으니, 허버트는 말없이 나를 응접실로 이끌어 들이고 문을 닫았다."], ["It was an odd sensation to see his very familiar face established quite at home in that very unfamiliar room and region; and I found myself looking at him, much as I looked at the corner-cupboard with the glass and china, the shells upon the chimney-piece, and the coloured engravings on the wall, representing the death of Captain Cook, a ship-launch, and his Majesty King George the Third in a state coachman’s wig, leather-breeches, and top-boots, on the terrace at Windsor.", "그토록 익숙한 그의 얼굴이 그토록 낯선 방과 동네에 아주 편안하게 자리 잡고 있는 것을 보는 것은 묘한 느낌이었다. 그리고 나는 어느새 그를 바라보고 있었는데, 그것은 마치 유리와 도자기가 든 모퉁이 찬장이며, 벽난로 선반 위의 조개껍데기며, 벽에 걸린 채색 판화들을 바라보는 것과 흡사했다. 그 판화들은 쿡 선장의 죽음과, 진수식 광경과, 윈저궁 테라스에서 궁정 마부의 가발과 가죽 반바지와 장화 차림을 한 국왕 조지 삼세 폐하를 그린 것이었다."]], [["“All is well, Handel,”", "\"다 잘되었네, 핸들.\""], ["said Herbert, “and he is quite satisfied, though eager to see you.", "허버트가 말했다. \"그분은 무척 만족해하시는데, 자네를 몹시 보고 싶어 하시지."], ["My dear girl is with her father; and if you’ll wait till she comes down, I’ll make you known to her, and then we’ll go upstairs.", "내 사랑스러운 아가씨는 지금 아버지 곁에 있어. 그녀가 내려올 때까지 기다려 주면 자네에게 소개해 주고, 그러고 나서 위층으로 올라가세."], ["That’s her father.”", "저게 바로 그녀의 아버지일세.\""]], [["I had become aware of an alarming growling overhead, and had probably expressed the fact in my countenance.", "머리 위에서 들려오는 으르렁대는 소리에 나는 섬뜩해졌고, 아마 그 사실이 내 얼굴에 드러났던 모양이다."]], [["“I am afraid he is a sad old rascal,”", "\"안됐지만 저분은 딱한 늙은 망나니라네.\""], ["said Herbert, smiling, “but I have never seen him.", "허버트가 미소 지으며 말했다. \"그래도 나는 한 번도 그분을 본 적이 없어."], ["Don’t you smell rum?", "럼주 냄새가 나지 않나?"], ["He is always at it.”", "늘 그걸 들이켜고 계시거든.\""]], [["“At rum?”", "\"럼주를요?\""], ["said I.", "내가 말했다."]], [["“Yes,”", "\"그래.\""], ["returned Herbert, “and you may suppose how mild it makes his gout.", "허버트가 대답했다. \"그러니 그분의 통풍이 얼마나 가벼워지겠나 짐작이 가겠지."], ["He persists, too, in keeping all the provisions upstairs in his room, and serving them out.", "게다가 그분은 모든 식료품을 위층 자기 방에 두고 손수 나누어 주겠다고 고집을 부리신다네."], ["He keeps them on shelves over his head, and will weigh them all.", "머리 위 선반에 그것들을 얹어 두고는 일일이 무게를 달아야만 직성이 풀리시지."], ["His room must be like a chandler’s shop.”", "그분 방은 잡화상 가게 같을 게 분명해.\""]], [["While he thus spoke, the growling noise became a prolonged roar, and then died away.", "그가 이렇게 말하는 동안, 으르렁대던 소리가 길게 끄는 포효로 변했다가, 이윽고 잦아들었다."]], [["“What else can be the consequence,”", "\"달리 무슨 결과가 나오겠나.\""], ["said Herbert, in explanation, “if he will cut the cheese?", "허버트가 설명조로 말했다. \"그분이 굳이 치즈를 손수 자르겠다고 하시니 말이야."], ["A man with the gout in his right hand—and everywhere else—can’t expect to get through a Double Gloucester without hurting himself.”", "오른손에—아니, 온몸에—통풍을 앓는 사람이 더블 글로스터 치즈 한 덩이를 자기 몸을 다치지 않고 다 잘라 낼 수 있다고 기대할 수야 없지.\""]], [["He seemed to have hurt himself very much, for he gave another furious roar.", "그는 몹시 다친 모양이었으니, 또 한 번 사납게 포효했기 때문이다."]], [["“To have Provis for an upper lodger is quite a godsend to Mrs.", "\"프로비스 같은 위층 하숙인을 두게 된 건 윔플 부인에게는 정말 횡재일세.\""], ["Whimple,” said Herbert, “for of course people in general won’t stand that noise.", "허버트가 말했다. \"왜냐하면 보통 사람들은 저런 소음을 참아 내지 못할 테니까."], ["A curious place, Handel; isn’t it?”", "묘한 곳이지, 핸들? 그렇지 않나?\""]], [["It was a curious place, indeed; but remarkably well kept and clean.", "과연 묘한 곳이었다. 하지만 놀랄 만큼 잘 정돈되어 있고 깨끗했다."]], [["“Mrs. Whimple,”", "\"윔플 부인은.\""], ["said Herbert, when I told him so, “is the best of housewives, and I really do not know what my Clara would do without her motherly help.", "내가 그렇게 말하자 허버트가 말했다. \"더없이 훌륭한 주부라네. 그녀의 어머니 같은 보살핌이 없었더라면 내 클라라가 어떻게 지냈을지 나는 정말 모르겠어."], ["For, Clara has no mother of her own, Handel, and no relation in the world but old Gruffandgrim.”", "클라라에게는 친어머니가 없거든, 핸들. 그리고 이 세상에 늙은 그러프앤드그림 말고는 아무 친척도 없다네.\""]], [["“Surely that’s not his name, Herbert?”", "\"설마 그게 그분 이름은 아니겠지, 허버트?\""]], [["“No, no,”", "\"아니, 아니야.\""], ["said Herbert, “that’s my name for him. His name is Mr.", "허버트가 말했다. \"그건 내가 그분을 부르는 별명이야."], ["Barley. But what a blessing it is for the son of my father and mother to love a girl who has no relations, and who can never bother herself or anybody else about her family!”", "본명은 발리 씨라네. 하지만 우리 부모님의 아들로서, 친척이 하나도 없고, 자기 집안 문제로 자기 자신이나 남을 결코 성가시게 할 일이 없는 아가씨를 사랑하게 되었으니 이 얼마나 복된 일인가!\""]], [["Herbert had told me on former occasions, and now reminded me, that he first knew Miss Clara Barley when she was completing her education at an establishment at Hammersmith, and that on her being recalled home to nurse her father, he and she had confided their affection to the motherly Mrs. Whimple, by whom it had been fostered and regulated with equal kindness and discretion, ever since.", "허버트는 전에도 내게 들려준 적이 있고 지금 다시 일깨워 주었는데, 그가 처음 클라라 발리 양을 알게 된 것은 그녀가 해머스미스의 한 학원에서 학업을 마무리하던 무렵이었고, 그녀가 아버지를 간병하러 집으로 불려 돌아간 뒤로 그와 그녀는 어머니 같은 윔플 부인에게 그들의 애정을 털어놓았으며, 그 후로 줄곧 부인이 그 애정을 한결같은 친절과 사려로 길러 주고 다스려 주었다는 것이다."], ["It was understood that nothing of a tender nature could possibly be confided to old Barley, by reason of his being totally unequal to the consideration of any subject more psychological than Gout, Rum, and Purser’s stores.", "통풍과 럼주와 선상 물품 관리 외에 그보다 심리적인 어떤 주제도 헤아릴 능력이 도무지 없는 사람인지라, 늙은 발리에게는 그 어떤 애틋한 이야기도 결코 털어놓을 수 없으리라는 것이 자명한 일이었다."]], [["As we were thus conversing in a low tone while Old Barley’s sustained growl vibrated in the beam that crossed the ceiling, the room door opened, and a very pretty, slight, dark-eyed girl of twenty or so came in with a basket in her hand: whom Herbert tenderly relieved of the basket, and presented, blushing, as “Clara.”", "천장을 가로지르는 들보 속에서 늙은 발리의 끊임없는 으르렁거림이 울리는 가운데 우리가 이렇게 낮은 목소리로 이야기를 나누고 있을 때, 방문이 열리더니 스무 살쯤 된, 무척 예쁘고 가냘프며 검은 눈동자를 한 아가씨가 바구니를 손에 들고 들어왔다. 허버트는 다정하게 그녀에게서 바구니를 받아 주고는, 얼굴을 붉히며 \"클라라\"라고 소개했다."], ["She really was a most charming girl, and might have passed for a captive fairy, whom that truculent Ogre, Old Barley, had pressed into his service.", "그녀는 정말이지 더없이 매력적인 아가씨였으니, 저 사나운 식인귀 늙은 발리가 강제로 부려 먹는 사로잡힌 요정으로 보아도 좋을 정도였다."]], [["“Look here,”", "\"여기 보게.\""], ["said Herbert, showing me the basket, with a compassionate and tender smile, after we had talked a little; “here’s poor Clara’s supper, served out every night.", "우리가 잠시 이야기를 나눈 뒤, 허버트가 측은하고 다정한 미소를 지으며 내게 바구니를 보여 주었다. \"이게 가엾은 클라라의 저녁 식사인데, 매일 밤 이렇게 배급되는 거라네."], ["Here’s her allowance of bread, and here’s her slice of cheese, and here’s her rum,—which I drink. This is Mr.", "이게 그녀 몫의 빵이고, 이게 그녀의 치즈 한 조각이고, 이게 그녀의 럼주인데—이건 내가 마셔 버리지."], ["Barley’s breakfast for to-morrow, served out to be cooked.", "이건 발리 씨의 내일 아침 식사로, 조리하라고 내준 거야."], ["Two mutton-chops, three potatoes, some split peas, a little flour, two ounces of butter, a pinch of salt, and all this black pepper.", "양고기 갈빗살 두 점, 감자 세 알, 쪼갠 완두콩 약간, 밀가루 조금, 버터 두 온스, 소금 한 자밤, 그리고 이 검은 후추 전부."], ["It’s stewed up together, and taken hot, and it’s a nice thing for the gout, I should think!”", "이걸 함께 끓여서 뜨겁게 들이시는데, 통풍에는 아주 좋은 것이렷다, 내 생각엔 말이야!\""]], [["There was something so natural and winning in Clara’s resigned way of looking at these stores in detail, as Herbert pointed them out; and something so confiding, loving, and innocent in her modest manner of yielding herself to Herbert’s embracing arm; and something so gentle in her, so much needing protection on Mill Pond Bank, by Chinks’s Basin, and the Old Green Copper Rope-walk, with Old Barley growling in the beam,—that I would not have undone the engagement between her and Herbert for all the money in the pocket-book I had never opened.", "허버트가 하나하나 가리키는 대로, 그 식료품들을 자세히 들여다보는 클라라의 체념 어린 모습에는 무언가 그토록 천연덕스럽고 마음을 끄는 데가 있었고, 허버트의 끌어안는 팔에 다소곳이 몸을 내맡기는 그 겸손한 태도에는 무언가 그토록 미덥고 사랑스럽고 순수한 데가 있었으며, 늙은 발리가 들보 속에서 으르렁거리는 가운데 칭크스 만 곁 오래된 푸른 구리 밧줄 제조장 옆 밀폰드 둑에서 그토록 보호가 필요한, 그녀 안의 무언가 그토록 온화한 데가 있었다. 그리하여 나는 내가 한 번도 열어 보지 않은 그 지갑 속 돈을 전부 준다 해도 그녀와 허버트 사이의 약혼을 깨뜨리고 싶지 않았다."]], [["I was looking at her with pleasure and admiration, when suddenly the growl swelled into a roar again, and a frightful bumping noise was heard above, as if a giant with a wooden leg were trying to bore it through the ceiling to come at us.", "내가 기쁨과 감탄에 차서 그녀를 바라보고 있을 때, 별안간 으르렁거림이 다시 포효로 부풀어 오르더니, 위에서 무시무시하게 쿵쿵대는 소리가 들려왔다. 마치 나무다리를 한 거인이 우리에게 다다르려고 천장을 뚫고 내려오려는 것 같았다."], ["Upon this Clara said to Herbert, “Papa wants me, darling!” and ran away.", "이에 클라라가 허버트에게 \"아빠가 나를 부르세요, 여보!\" 하고는 달려가 버렸다."]], [["“There is an unconscionable old shark for you!”", "\"저런 양심도 없는 늙은 상어가 어디 또 있겠나!\""], ["said Herbert.", "허버트가 말했다."], ["“What do you suppose he wants now, Handel?”", "\"이번엔 또 뭘 원하시는 것 같나, 핸들?\""]], [["“I don’t know,”", "\"모르겠는데.\""], ["said I. “Something to drink?”", "내가 말했다. \"뭐 마실 거라도?\""]], [["“That’s it!”", "\"바로 그거야!\""], ["cried Herbert, as if I had made a guess of extraordinary merit.", "허버트가 마치 내가 대단히 훌륭한 추측이라도 해낸 것처럼 외쳤다."], ["“He keeps his grog ready mixed in a little tub on the table.", "\"그분은 탁자 위 작은 통에 그로그주를 미리 타서 늘 준비해 두신다네."], ["Wait a moment, and you’ll hear Clara lift him up to take some.", "잠깐만 기다려 보게, 클라라가 그분을 일으켜 한 모금 들이게 하는 소리가 들릴 테니."], ["There he goes!”", "자, 시작하시는군!\""], ["Another roar, with a prolonged shake at the end. “Now,”", "또 한 번의 포효가, 끝에 가서 길게 떨리며 들려왔다. \"자.\""], ["said Herbert, as it was succeeded by silence, “he’s drinking. Now,”", "그것에 뒤이어 침묵이 찾아오자 허버트가 말했다. \"이제 마시고 계셔. 자.\""], ["said Herbert, as the growl resounded in the beam once more, “he’s down again on his back!”", "들보 속에서 으르렁거림이 다시금 울려 퍼지자 허버트가 말했다. \"이제 다시 등을 대고 누우셨군!\""]], [["Clara returned soon afterwards, and Herbert accompanied me upstairs to see our charge.", "클라라는 곧 돌아왔고, 허버트는 우리가 맡은 이를 보러 나를 위층으로 데려갔다."], ["As we passed Mr. Barley’s door, he was heard hoarsely muttering within, in a strain that rose and fell like wind, the following Refrain, in which I substitute good wishes for something quite the reverse:—", "우리가 발리 씨의 방문 앞을 지날 때, 그가 안에서 바람처럼 높아졌다 낮아졌다 하는 곡조로 다음과 같은 후렴을 쉰 목소리로 중얼거리는 소리가 들렸는데, 나는 여기서 정반대의 뜻을 가진 말들을 좋은 축원의 말로 바꾸어 옮기겠다."]], [["“Ahoy!", "\"어이!"], ["Bless your eyes, here’s old Bill Barley.", "자네에게 축복을, 여기 늙은 빌 발리가 있도다."], ["Here’s old Bill Barley, bless your eyes.", "여기 늙은 빌 발리가 있도다, 자네에게 축복을."], ["Here’s old Bill Barley on the flat of his back, by the Lord.", "여기 늙은 빌 발리가 등을 대고 누워 있도다, 맹세코."], ["Lying on the flat of his back like a drifting old dead flounder, here’s your old Bill Barley, bless your eyes. Ahoy! Bless you.”", "떠다니는 늙은 죽은 가자미처럼 등을 대고 누워서, 여기 자네의 늙은 빌 발리가 있도다, 자네에게 축복을. 어이! 축복을.\""]], [["In this strain of consolation, Herbert informed me the invisible Barley would commune with himself by the day and night together; Often, while it was light, having, at the same time, one eye at a telescope which was fitted on his bed for the convenience of sweeping the river.", "이런 위안의 곡조로, 보이지 않는 발리는 밤낮을 가리지 않고 혼잣말로 자기 자신과 교감하는 것이라고 허버트가 일러 주었다. 흔히 날이 밝을 때는 강을 훑어보기 편하도록 침대에 장치해 둔 망원경에 한쪽 눈을 댄 채로 그러기도 한다는 것이었다."]], [["In his two cabin rooms at the top of the house, which were fresh and airy, and in which Mr. Barley was less audible than below, I found Provis comfortably settled.", "집 꼭대기에 있는, 산뜻하고 통풍이 잘되며 발리 씨의 소리가 아래층보다 덜 들리는 그의 두 선실 같은 방에서, 나는 프로비스가 편안하게 자리 잡고 있는 것을 보았다."], ["He expressed no alarm, and seemed to feel none that was worth mentioning; but it struck me that he was softened,—indefinably, for I could not have said how, and could never afterwards recall how when I tried, but certainly.", "그는 아무런 불안도 내비치지 않았고, 입에 담을 만한 불안을 느끼는 것 같지도 않았다. 그러나 내게는 그가 부드러워진 듯 여겨졌으니—어떻게 그러한지는 말할 수 없을 만큼 막연했고, 훗날 떠올리려 애써도 도무지 어떻게였는지 되살릴 수 없었지만, 그래도 분명히 그러했다."]], [["The opportunity that the day’s rest had given me for reflection had resulted in my fully determining to say nothing to him respecting Compeyson.", "그날의 휴식이 내게 안겨 준 사색의 기회는, 컴페이슨에 관해서는 그에게 한마디도 하지 않겠다는 결심을 굳히는 것으로 귀결되었다."], ["For anything I knew, his animosity towards the man might otherwise lead to his seeking him out and rushing on his own destruction.", "내가 아는 한, 그러지 않았다가는 그 사내에 대한 그의 적개심이 그를 부추겨 그자를 찾아 나서게 하고 제 손으로 파멸로 뛰어들게 만들지도 몰랐다."], ["Therefore, when Herbert and I sat down with him by his fire, I asked him first of all whether he relied on Wemmick’s judgment and sources of information?", "그래서 허버트와 내가 그의 난롯가에 그와 함께 앉았을 때, 나는 무엇보다 먼저 그가 웨믹의 판단과 정보의 출처를 믿느냐고 물었다."]], [["“Ay, ay, dear boy!”", "\"암, 암, 얘야!\""], ["he answered, with a grave nod, “Jaggers knows.”", "그가 진지하게 고개를 끄덕이며 대답했다. \"재거스라면 알지.\""]], [["“Then, I have talked with Wemmick,”", "\"그래서 제가 웨믹과 이야기를 나누었어요.\""], ["said I, “and have come to tell you what caution he gave me and what advice.”", "내가 말했다. \"그가 제게 어떤 주의를 주었고 어떤 충고를 했는지 말씀드리려고 왔습니다.\""]], [["This I did accurately, with the reservation just mentioned; and I told him how Wemmick had heard, in Newgate prison (whether from officers or prisoners I could not say), that he was under some suspicion, and that my chambers had been watched; how Wemmick had recommended his keeping close for a time, and my keeping away from him; and what Wemmick had said about getting him abroad.", "나는 방금 말한 단서만 빼고는 이것을 정확히 전했다. 그리고 그에게, 웨믹이 뉴게이트 감옥에서(간수에게서인지 죄수에게서인지는 나도 알 수 없었지만) 그가 어느 정도 의심받고 있다는 것과 내 거처가 감시당하고 있다는 것을 들었다는 사실, 그리고 웨믹이 그에게 한동안 몸을 숨기고 있으라고, 또 내게는 그를 멀리하라고 권했다는 사실, 그리고 그를 국외로 빼돌리는 일에 관해 웨믹이 무슨 말을 했는지를 일러 주었다."], ["I added, that of course, when the time came, I should go with him, or should follow close upon him, as might be safest in Wemmick’s judgment.", "나는 덧붙이기를, 물론 때가 오면 웨믹의 판단에 따라 가장 안전한 쪽으로, 내가 그와 함께 가거나 그의 뒤를 바짝 따라가겠다고 했다."], ["What was to follow that I did not touch upon; neither, indeed, was I at all clear or comfortable about it in my own mind, now that I saw him in that softer condition, and in declared peril for my sake.", "그다음에 무엇이 이어질지에 대해서는 건드리지 않았다. 사실 나 또한 그 점에 대해서는 내 마음속에서도 전혀 분명하지도 편안하지도 않았으니, 이제 그가 그토록 부드러워진 상태로, 게다가 나를 위해 명백히 위험에 처해 있는 모습을 본 마당이었다."], ["As to altering my way of living by enlarging my expenses, I put it to him whether in our present unsettled and difficult circumstances, it would not be simply ridiculous, if it were no worse?", "씀씀이를 늘려 내 생활 방식을 바꾸는 일에 관해서는, 지금처럼 불안정하고 어려운 우리 처지에서 그것이 더 나쁜 일까지는 아니더라도 그저 우스꽝스러운 짓이 되지 않겠느냐고 그에게 물었다."]], [["He could not deny this, and indeed was very reasonable throughout.", "그는 이를 부인할 수 없었고, 사실 처음부터 끝까지 매우 사리에 밝았다."], ["His coming back was a venture, he said, and he had always known it to be a venture.", "자기가 돌아온 것은 하나의 모험이며, 자기는 그것이 모험임을 늘 알고 있었다고 그는 말했다."], ["He would do nothing to make it a desperate venture, and he had very little fear of his safety with such good help.", "자기는 그것을 무모한 모험으로 만들 어떤 짓도 하지 않을 것이며, 이토록 든든한 도움을 받고 있으니 제 안전에 대해서는 거의 두려움이 없다고 했다."]], [["Herbert, who had been looking at the fire and pondering, here said that something had come into his thoughts arising out of Wemmick’s suggestion, which it might be worth while to pursue.", "줄곧 난롯불을 바라보며 골똘히 생각하던 허버트가 여기서 입을 열어, 웨믹의 제안에서 떠오른 생각이 하나 있는데 그것을 추진해 볼 만하겠다고 말했다."], ["“We are both good watermen, Handel, and could take him down the river ourselves when the right time comes.", "\"우리 둘 다 노 솜씨가 좋으니, 핸들, 때가 무르익으면 우리 손으로 그분을 강 아래로 모셔 갈 수 있어."], ["No boat would then be hired for the purpose, and no boatmen; that would save at least a chance of suspicion, and any chance is worth saving.", "그러면 그 일을 위해 배를 빌릴 필요도, 뱃사공을 쓸 필요도 없게 되지. 그것만으로도 적어도 의심받을 여지 하나는 덜게 될 텐데, 여지는 어떤 것이든 더는 게 좋아."], ["Never mind the season; don’t you think it might be a good thing if you began at once to keep a boat at the Temple stairs, and were in the habit of rowing up and down the river?", "철은 신경 쓰지 말게. 자네가 지금 당장 템플 선착장에 배 한 척을 갖다 두고, 강을 오르내리며 노 젓는 일을 습관처럼 해 두면 좋지 않겠나?"], ["You fall into that habit, and then who notices or minds?", "자네가 그런 습관에 빠져들면, 그다음엔 누가 눈여겨보거나 마음에 두겠나?"], ["Do it twenty or fifty times, and there is nothing special in your doing it the twenty-first or fifty-first.”", "스무 번이고 쉰 번이고 그렇게 하면, 스물한 번째나 쉰한 번째에 자네가 그러는 것도 별스러울 게 없어지지.\""]], [["I liked this scheme, and Provis was quite elated by it.", "나는 이 계획이 마음에 들었고, 프로비스는 그것에 무척 들떴다."], ["We agreed that it should be carried into execution, and that Provis should never recognise us if we came below Bridge, and rowed past Mill Pond Bank.", "우리는 이를 실행에 옮기기로, 그리고 우리가 다리 아래로 내려가 밀폰드 둑을 지나 노를 저어 갈 때 프로비스는 결코 우리를 아는 체하지 않기로 합의했다."], ["But we further agreed that he should pull down the blind in that part of his window which gave upon the east, whenever he saw us and all was right.", "그러나 우리는 한 걸음 더 나아가, 그가 우리를 보고 모든 것이 무사할 때면 언제든 동쪽으로 난 창문 부분의 블라인드를 내리기로 합의했다."]], [["Our conference being now ended, and everything arranged, I rose to go; remarking to Herbert that he and I had better not go home together, and that I would take half an hour’s start of him.", "우리의 협의가 이제 끝나고 모든 것이 정해졌으므로, 나는 일어나 떠나려 했다. 그러면서 허버트에게, 그와 내가 함께 집으로 가지 않는 편이 낫겠다고, 내가 그보다 삼십 분 먼저 출발하겠다고 일렀다. \"여기 당신을 두고 가는 게 마음에 걸립니다.\""], ["“I don’t like to leave you here,”", "나는 프로비스에게 말했다."], ["I said to Provis, “though I cannot doubt your being safer here than near me.", "\"당신이 제 곁에 있는 것보다 여기 있는 게 더 안전하다는 건 의심할 여지가 없지만요."], ["Good-bye!”", "안녕히 계세요!\""]], [["“Dear boy,”", "\"얘야.\""], ["he answered, clasping my hands, “I don’t know when we may meet again, and I don’t like good-bye.", "그가 내 두 손을 맞잡으며 대답했다. \"우리가 언제 다시 만날 수 있을지 모르겠구나. 나는 작별 인사가 싫다."], ["Say good-night!”", "잘 자라고만 해 다오!\""]], [["“Good-night!", "\"안녕히 주무세요!"], ["Herbert will go regularly between us, and when the time comes you may be certain I shall be ready.", "허버트가 우리 사이를 꼬박꼬박 오갈 거예요. 그리고 때가 오면 제가 채비를 갖추고 있으리라는 걸 믿으셔도 됩니다."], ["Good-night, good-night!”", "안녕히 주무세요, 안녕히 주무세요!\""]], [["We thought it best that he should stay in his own rooms; and we left him on the landing outside his door, holding a light over the stair-rail to light us downstairs.", "그가 자기 방에 머무는 편이 가장 좋겠다고 우리는 생각했고, 그래서 그를 방문 밖 층계참에 남겨 둔 채 떠났다. 그는 우리가 아래층으로 내려가는 길을 밝혀 주려고 계단 난간 너머로 등불을 들고 있었다."], ["Looking back at him, I thought of the first night of his return, when our positions were reversed, and when I little supposed my heart could ever be as heavy and anxious at parting from him as it was now.", "그를 돌아보며, 나는 그가 돌아온 첫날 밤을 떠올렸다. 그때는 우리의 처지가 지금과 뒤바뀌어 있었고, 그와 헤어지는 마당에 내 마음이 지금처럼 이토록 무겁고 불안해지리라고는 그때 나는 거의 짐작조차 하지 못했다."]], [["Old Barley was growling and swearing when we repassed his door, with no appearance of having ceased or of meaning to cease.", "우리가 다시 그의 방문 앞을 지날 때, 늙은 발리는 으르렁대며 욕을 퍼붓고 있었는데, 그칠 기색도, 그칠 마음도 전혀 없어 보였다."], ["When we got to the foot of the stairs, I asked Herbert whether he had preserved the name of Provis.", "계단 아래에 다다랐을 때, 나는 허버트에게 프로비스라는 이름을 그대로 두었느냐고 물었다."], ["He replied, certainly not, and that the lodger was Mr. Campbell.", "그는 절대 그러지 않았으며, 하숙인은 캠벨 씨로 되어 있다고 대답했다."], ["He also explained that the utmost known of Mr. Campbell there was, that he (Herbert) had Mr. Campbell consigned to him, and felt a strong personal interest in his being well cared for, and living a secluded life.", "또한 여기서 캠벨 씨에 관해 알려진 것이라고는 기껏해야, 그(허버트)가 캠벨 씨를 위탁받았으며 그가 잘 보살핌을 받고 은둔 생활을 하는 데 깊은 개인적 관심을 두고 있다는 정도가 전부라고 설명했다."], ["So, when we went into the parlour where Mrs. Whimple and Clara were seated at work, I said nothing of my own interest in Mr. Campbell, but kept it to myself.", "그래서 우리가 윔플 부인과 클라라가 앉아 바느질을 하고 있는 응접실로 들어갔을 때, 나는 캠벨 씨에 대한 나 자신의 관심에 대해서는 한마디도 하지 않고 마음속에 묻어 두었다."]], [["When I had taken leave of the pretty, gentle, dark-eyed girl, and of the motherly woman who had not outlived her honest sympathy with a little affair of true love, I felt as if the Old Green Copper Rope-walk had grown quite a different place.", "예쁘고 온화하며 검은 눈동자를 한 그 아가씨에게, 그리고 진실한 사랑의 자그마한 사연에 대한 진솔한 동정심을 여태 잃지 않은 그 어머니 같은 부인에게 작별을 고하고 나니, 오래된 푸른 구리 밧줄 제조장이 사뭇 다른 곳이 된 듯한 느낌이 들었다."], ["Old Barley might be as old as the hills, and might swear like a whole field of troopers, but there were redeeming youth and trust and hope enough in Chinks’s Basin to fill it to overflowing.", "늙은 발리는 산만큼이나 늙었을지 모르고, 한 부대의 기병들처럼 욕을 퍼부을지 모르지만, 칭크스 만에는 그곳을 차고 넘치도록 채울 만한, 그 모든 것을 만회하는 젊음과 믿음과 희망이 있었다."], ["And then I thought of Estella, and of our parting, and went home very sadly.", "그러고 나서 나는 에스텔라를, 그리고 우리의 이별을 떠올리며, 무척 서글픈 심정으로 집으로 돌아갔다."]], [["All things were as quiet in the Temple as ever I had seen them.", "템플은 내가 여태껏 본 것 못지않게 모든 것이 고요했다."], ["The windows of the rooms on that side, lately occupied by Provis, were dark and still, and there was no lounger in Garden Court.", "얼마 전까지 프로비스가 쓰던 그쪽 방들의 창문은 어둡고 조용했으며, 가든 코트에는 어슬렁거리는 사람 하나 없었다."], ["I walked past the fountain twice or thrice before I descended the steps that were between me and my rooms, but I was quite alone.", "나는 나와 내 방 사이에 놓인 계단을 내려가기에 앞서 분수 곁을 두세 번 지나쳐 걸었으나, 정말로 나 혼자뿐이었다."], ["Herbert, coming to my bedside when he came in,—for I went straight to bed, dispirited and fatigued,—made the same report.", "허버트는 안으로 들어와—나는 풀이 죽고 지쳐 곧장 잠자리에 들었으므로—내 침대 곁으로 와서는 똑같은 보고를 했다."], ["Opening one of the windows after that, he looked out into the moonlight, and told me that the pavement was as solemnly empty as the pavement of any cathedral at that same hour.", "그러고 나서 창문 하나를 열고 달빛 속을 내다보더니, 그 시각 어느 대성당의 포도(鋪道) 못지않게 길바닥이 엄숙하리만치 텅 비어 있다고 내게 일러 주었다."]], [["Next day I set myself to get the boat.", "이튿날 나는 배를 마련하는 일에 착수했다."], ["It was soon done, and the boat was brought round to the Temple stairs, and lay where I could reach her within a minute or two.", "그것은 곧 처리되었고, 배는 템플 선착장으로 옮겨져, 내가 일이 분이면 손이 닿을 수 있는 곳에 매여 있었다."], ["Then, I began to go out as for training and practice: sometimes alone, sometimes with Herbert.", "그러고 나서 나는 훈련과 연습을 한다는 명목으로 나가기 시작했다. 어떤 때는 혼자서, 어떤 때는 허버트와 함께."], ["I was often out in cold, rain, and sleet, but nobody took much note of me after I had been out a few times.", "추위와 비와 진눈깨비 속에 나가는 일이 잦았으나, 몇 번 나가고 나니 아무도 나를 그다지 눈여겨보지 않았다."], ["At first, I kept above Blackfriars Bridge; but as the hours of the tide changed, I took towards London Bridge.", "처음에는 블랙프라이어스 다리 위쪽에만 머물렀으나, 조수의 시각이 바뀌자 런던 다리 쪽으로 향했다."], ["It was Old London Bridge in those days, and at certain states of the tide there was a race and fall of water there which gave it a bad reputation.", "그 시절에는 옛 런던 다리였는데, 조수가 어떤 상태일 때면 거기 거센 물살과 낙차가 있어 평판이 나빴다."], ["But I knew well enough how to ‘shoot’ the bridge after seeing it done, and so began to row about among the shipping in the Pool, and down to Erith.", "그러나 나는 남이 하는 것을 보고 나서 다리를 '쏘듯 통과하는' 법을 충분히 익혔으므로, 풀의 선박들 사이를 누비며, 그리고 에리스까지 내려가며 노를 젓기 시작했다."], ["The first time I passed Mill Pond Bank, Herbert and I were pulling a pair of oars; and, both in going and returning, we saw the blind towards the east come down.", "내가 처음으로 밀폰드 둑을 지날 때 허버트와 나는 노 한 쌍을 저어 가고 있었는데, 갈 때나 돌아올 때나 우리는 동쪽을 향한 블라인드가 내려오는 것을 보았다."], ["Herbert was rarely there less frequently than three times in a week, and he never brought me a single word of intelligence that was at all alarming.", "허버트는 일주일에 세 번보다 드물게 그곳에 가는 일이 거의 없었고, 조금이라도 섬뜩한 소식을 단 한마디도 내게 전해 온 적이 없었다."], ["Still, I knew that there was cause for alarm, and I could not get rid of the notion of being watched.", "그래도 나는 섬뜩해할 까닭이 있음을 알고 있었고, 감시당하고 있다는 생각을 떨쳐 낼 수가 없었다."], ["Once received, it is a haunting idea; how many undesigning persons I suspected of watching me, it would be hard to calculate.", "한번 자리 잡고 나면 그것은 사람을 들볶는 생각이니, 내가 얼마나 많은 무고한 사람들을 나를 감시하는 자로 의심했는지는 헤아리기 어려울 정도였다."]], [["In short, I was always full of fears for the rash man who was in hiding.", "요컨대, 나는 숨어 지내는 그 무모한 사내에 대한 두려움으로 늘 가득 차 있었다."], ["Herbert had sometimes said to me that he found it pleasant to stand at one of our windows after dark, when the tide was running down, and to think that it was flowing, with everything it bore, towards Clara.", "허버트는 가끔 내게, 날이 저문 뒤 조수가 빠져나갈 때 우리 창문 하나에 서서, 그 물결이 거기 실린 온갖 것과 더불어 클라라를 향해 흘러간다고 생각하면 흐뭇하다고 말하곤 했다."], ["But I thought with dread that it was flowing towards Magwitch, and that any black mark on its surface might be his pursuers, going swiftly, silently, and surely, to take him.", "그러나 나는 두려움에 차서, 그 물결이 매그위치를 향해 흘러간다고, 그리고 그 수면 위의 어떤 검은 자국이든 그를 붙잡으러 빠르고 소리 없이 그리고 어김없이 가는 그의 추격자들일지 모른다고 생각했다."]], [["# Chapter XLVII.", "# Chapter XLVII."]], [["Some weeks passed without bringing any change.", "몇 주가 지나갔으나 아무런 변화도 찾아오지 않았다."], ["We waited for Wemmick, and he made no sign.", "우리는 웨믹을 기다렸지만, 그는 아무런 기척도 보이지 않았다."], ["If I had never known him out of Little Britain, and had never enjoyed the privilege of being on a familiar footing at the Castle, I might have doubted him; not so for a moment, knowing him as I did.", "만약 내가 그를 리틀브리튼 바깥에서 한 번도 본 적이 없고, 그 성에서 허물없이 지내는 특권을 누려 본 적이 없었더라면, 나는 그를 의심했을지도 모른다. 그러나 내가 아는 그의 사람됨으로 보아, 단 한순간도 그러지 않았다."]], [["My worldly affairs began to wear a gloomy appearance, and I was pressed for money by more than one creditor.", "내 세속적인 형편이 점차 음울한 빛을 띠기 시작했고, 나는 한둘이 아닌 채권자들로부터 돈 독촉에 시달렸다."], ["Even I myself began to know the want of money (I mean of ready money in my own pocket), and to relieve it by converting some easily spared articles of jewelery into cash.", "나 자신조차 돈이 궁한 처지(내 호주머니 속 현금이 궁하다는 말이다)를 알게 되었고, 그것을 덜어 보려고 어렵잖게 처분할 만한 보석 몇 점을 현금으로 바꾸기에 이르렀다."], ["But I had quite determined that it would be a heartless fraud to take more money from my patron in the existing state of my uncertain thoughts and plans.", "그러나 나는, 내 생각과 계획이 이토록 불확실한 형편에서 후원자에게서 돈을 더 받는 것은 인정머리 없는 사기 행위라고 단단히 마음을 굳혔다."], ["Therefore, I had sent him the unopened pocket-book by Herbert, to hold in his own keeping, and I felt a kind of satisfaction—whether it was a false kind or a true, I hardly know—in not having profited by his generosity since his revelation of himself.", "그래서 나는 뜯지 않은 그 지갑을 허버트 편에 보내 그가 직접 간직하도록 했고, 그가 자기 정체를 밝힌 이후로 그의 후의를 누리지 않았다는 데서 일종의 만족감을 느꼈다. 그것이 거짓된 만족인지 참된 만족인지는 나도 잘 모르겠다."]], [["As the time wore on, an impression settled heavily upon me that Estella was married.", "시간이 흐를수록, 에스텔라가 결혼했다는 인상이 내 마음을 무겁게 짓눌렀다."], ["Fearful of having it confirmed, though it was all but a conviction, I avoided the newspapers, and begged Herbert (to whom I had confided the circumstances of our last interview) never to speak of her to me.", "이미 거의 확신에 가까웠음에도, 나는 그것이 확증되는 것이 두려워 신문을 멀리했고, 허버트에게(나는 그에게 우리의 마지막 대면이 어떠했는지 털어놓았다) 그녀에 관한 말은 절대 내게 꺼내지 말아 달라고 청했다."], ["Why I hoarded up this last wretched little rag of the robe of hope that was rent and given to the winds, how do I know?", "이미 갈가리 찢겨 바람결에 흩어져 버린 희망의 옷자락, 그 마지막 가련한 자투리를 내가 왜 그토록 움켜쥐고 있었는지, 내가 어찌 알겠는가?"], ["Why did you who read this, commit that not dissimilar inconsistency of your own last year, last month, last week?", "이 글을 읽는 당신은 또 어찌하여 작년에, 지난달에, 지난주에, 이와 그리 다르지 않은 모순을 스스로 저질렀는가?"]], [["It was an unhappy life that I lived; and its one dominant anxiety, towering over all its other anxieties, like a high mountain above a range of mountains, never disappeared from my view.", "내가 살던 삶은 불행한 삶이었다. 그리고 그 모든 다른 근심들 위로 우뚝 솟은, 산맥 위로 솟구친 높은 산과도 같은, 한 가지 지배적인 근심이 결코 내 시야에서 사라지지 않았다."], ["Still, no new cause for fear arose.", "그래도 새로운 두려움의 빌미는 생겨나지 않았다."], ["Let me start from my bed as I would, with the terror fresh upon me that he was discovered; let me sit listening, as I would with dread, for Herbert’s returning step at night, lest it should be fleeter than ordinary, and winged with evil news,—for all that, and much more to like purpose, the round of things went on.", "그가 발각되었다는 갓 솟아난 공포에 사로잡혀 잠자리에서 벌떡 일어나든, 밤마다 허버트의 돌아오는 발소리가 여느 때보다 빠르고 불길한 소식의 날개를 단 것은 아닐까 두려워하며 귀를 곤두세우고 앉아 있든 — 그 모든 것에도 불구하고, 또 그와 비슷한 수많은 일들에도 불구하고, 세상사의 쳇바퀴는 그저 굴러갔다."], ["Condemned to inaction and a state of constant restlessness and suspense, I rowed about in my boat, and waited, waited, waited, as I best could.", "아무것도 할 수 없는 처지에, 끝없는 불안과 초조 속에 갇힌 채, 나는 내 보트를 저어 다니며 할 수 있는 한 최선을 다해 기다리고, 또 기다리고, 또 기다렸다."]], [["There were states of the tide when, having been down the river, I could not get back through the eddy-chafed arches and starlings of old London Bridge; then, I left my boat at a wharf near the Custom House, to be brought up afterwards to the Temple stairs.", "강 아래쪽으로 내려갔다가, 물살이 소용돌이치며 부딪치는 옛 런던교의 아치며 교각 보호용 말뚝 사이로 되돌아올 수 없는 밀물 때도 있었다. 그럴 때면 나는 세관 근처 부두에 보트를 매어 두었다가, 나중에 템플 선착장까지 끌어 올리도록 했다."], ["I was not averse to doing this, as it served to make me and my boat a commoner incident among the water-side people there.", "나는 이 일을 마다하지 않았는데, 그렇게 함으로써 나와 내 보트가 그곳 강가 사람들 사이에서 흔히 보는 광경이 되었기 때문이다."], ["From this slight occasion sprang two meetings that I have now to tell of.", "이 사소한 계기에서 이제 내가 이야기하려는 두 차례의 만남이 비롯되었다."]], [["One afternoon, late in the month of February, I came ashore at the wharf at dusk.", "이월 하순 어느 오후, 나는 해 질 무렵 그 부두에 닻을 내렸다."], ["I had pulled down as far as Greenwich with the ebb tide, and had turned with the tide.", "썰물을 타고 그리니치까지 내려갔다가, 물때가 바뀌자 방향을 돌려 돌아온 것이었다."], ["It had been a fine bright day, but had become foggy as the sun dropped, and I had had to feel my way back among the shipping, pretty carefully.", "맑고 화창한 날이었으나, 해가 지면서 안개가 끼기 시작해, 나는 정박해 있는 배들 사이로 꽤 조심스레 길을 더듬어 돌아와야 했다."], ["Both in going and returning, I had seen the signal in his window, All well.", "갈 때나 돌아올 때나, 나는 그의 창문에 걸린 신호, 이상 없음을 보았다."]], [["As it was a raw evening, and I was cold, I thought I would comfort myself with dinner at once; and as I had hours of dejection and solitude before me if I went home to the Temple, I thought I would afterwards go to the play. The theatre where Mr.", "쌀쌀한 저녁이었고 몸도 추웠던 터라, 나는 우선 저녁이라도 들며 몸을 녹이는 게 좋겠다고 생각했다. 그리고 템플의 집으로 돌아가 봤자 우울과 고독의 시간만이 기다리고 있을 터이니, 식사 뒤에는 연극이나 보러 갈까 싶었다."], ["Wopsle had achieved his questionable triumph was in that water-side neighbourhood (it is nowhere now), and to that theatre I resolved to go. I was aware that Mr.", "워플 씨가 그 미심쩍은 승리를 거둔 적이 있는 극장이 바로 그 강가 동네에 있었으므로(지금은 어디에도 남아 있지 않다), 나는 그 극장으로 가기로 마음먹었다."], ["Wopsle had not succeeded in reviving the Drama, but, on the contrary, had rather partaken of its decline.", "워플 씨가 연극을 부흥시키는 데 성공하기는커녕, 오히려 그 쇠락에 한몫 거들었다는 것은 나도 알고 있었다."], ["He had been ominously heard of, through the play-bills, as a faithful Black, in connection with a little girl of noble birth, and a monkey.", "그가 고귀한 가문의 어린 소녀와 원숭이 한 마리와 더불어, 충직한 흑인 노릇을 맡았다는 불길한 소식이 연극 전단을 통해 들려왔다."], ["And Herbert had seen him as a predatory Tartar of comic propensities, with a face like a red brick, and an outrageous hat all over bells.", "또 허버트는 그가 벽돌처럼 붉은 얼굴에 온통 방울이 달린 터무니없는 모자를 쓰고, 익살스러운 성정의 약탈자 타타르인 역을 하는 것을 본 적이 있었다."]], [["I dined at what Herbert and I used to call a geographical chop-house, where there were maps of the world in porter-pot rims on every half-yard of the tablecloths, and charts of gravy on every one of the knives,—to this day there is scarcely a single chop-house within the Lord Mayor’s dominions which is not geographical,—and wore out the time in dozing over crumbs, staring at gas, and baking in a hot blast of dinners.", "나는 허버트와 내가 이른바 지리학적 음식점이라 부르곤 하던 곳에서 저녁을 들었다. 그곳은 식탁보 반 야드마다 흑맥주 잔 자국으로 세계 지도가 그려져 있고, 나이프마다 육즙으로 해도가 그려져 있는 집이었다 — 오늘날까지도 런던 시장의 관할 구역 안에 지리학적이지 않은 음식점은 거의 한 군데도 없다 — 나는 빵 부스러기 위로 꾸벅꾸벅 졸고, 가스등을 멍하니 바라보고, 음식들이 뿜어내는 뜨거운 열기에 몸을 구우며 시간을 죽였다."], ["By and by, I roused myself, and went to the play.", "이윽고 나는 정신을 차리고 연극을 보러 갔다."]], [["There, I found a virtuous boatswain in His Majesty’s service,—a most excellent man, though I could have wished his trousers not quite so tight in some places, and not quite so loose in others,—who knocked all the little men’s hats over their eyes, though he was very generous and brave, and who wouldn’t hear of anybody’s paying taxes, though he was very patriotic.", "거기서 나는 영국 해군에 복무하는 덕망 높은 갑판장 한 사람을 보았는데 — 더없이 훌륭한 사내였으나, 다만 그의 바지가 어떤 데서는 그토록 꽉 끼지 않고 어떤 데서는 그토록 헐렁하지 않았더라면 싶었다 — 그는 더없이 너그럽고 용감했음에도 작은 사내들의 모자를 죄다 눈까지 푹 눌러 내려 버렸고, 더없이 애국적이었음에도 누구든 세금을 내겠다는 소리는 들으려 하지도 않았다."], ["He had a bag of money in his pocket, like a pudding in the cloth, and on that property married a young person in bed-furniture, with great rejoicings; the whole population of Portsmouth (nine in number at the last census) turning out on the beach to rub their own hands and shake everybody else’s, and sing “Fill, fill!”", "그는 호주머니에 보자기에 싼 푸딩 같은 돈주머니를 지니고 있었고, 그 재산을 밑천 삼아 침대보 차림의 한 젊은 여인과 성대한 잔치를 벌이며 결혼했다. 포츠머스의 온 주민(마지막 인구 조사에서 아홉 명이었다)이 해변으로 몰려나와 제 손을 비비고 남들의 손을 잡아 흔들며 \"채워라, 채워라!\" 하고 노래를 불렀다."], ["A certain dark-complexioned Swab, however, who wouldn’t fill, or do anything else that was proposed to him, and whose heart was openly stated (by the boatswain) to be as black as his figure-head, proposed to two other Swabs to get all mankind into difficulties; which was so effectually done (the Swab family having considerable political influence) that it took half the evening to set things right, and then it was only brought about through an honest little grocer with a white hat, black gaiters, and red nose, getting into a clock, with a gridiron, and listening, and coming out, and knocking everybody down from behind with the gridiron whom he couldn’t confute with what he had overheard. This led to Mr.", "그러나 살갗이 거무튀튀한 어떤 갑판 잡것 하나는, 잔을 채우려고도, 자기에게 권하는 다른 무엇도 하려 들지 않았으며, 그 마음이 자기 선수상만큼이나 시커멓다고(갑판장의 입을 통해) 공공연히 밝혀진 자였는데, 다른 두 갑판 잡것에게 온 인류를 곤경에 빠뜨리자고 부추겼다. 그 일이 어찌나 감쪽같이 이루어졌던지(이 갑판 잡것 일가가 상당한 정치적 영향력을 지닌 까닭이었다), 사태를 바로잡는 데 저녁 시간의 절반이 걸렸고, 그나마도 흰 모자에 검은 각반을 차고 빨간 코를 한 정직한 작은 식료품 장수 하나가 석쇠를 들고 괘종시계 안으로 들어가 엿듣다가 나와서는, 엿들은 말로 논박할 수 없는 자는 누구든 뒤에서 그 석쇠로 때려눕힌 덕분에야 겨우 해결되었다."], ["Wopsle’s (who had never been heard of before) coming in with a star and garter on, as a plenipotentiary of great power direct from the Admiralty, to say that the Swabs were all to go to prison on the spot, and that he had brought the boatswain down the Union Jack, as a slight acknowledgment of his public services.", "이 일로 워플 씨가(그때까지 한 번도 언급된 적이 없던 인물이었다) 가슴에 별 훈장을 달고 가터 훈장을 두른 채, 해군성에서 곧장 파견된 막강한 권한의 전권 대사로 등장하여, 갑판 잡것들은 모두 그 자리에서 감옥으로 끌려가야 한다고, 그리고 갑판장의 공로에 대한 작은 사례로 유니언잭을 가져왔다고 선언했다."], ["The boatswain, unmanned for the first time, respectfully dried his eyes on the Jack, and then cheering up, and addressing Mr. Wopsle as Your Honour, solicited permission to take him by the fin. Mr.", "갑판장은 처음으로 마음이 약해져 그 깃발에 공손히 눈물을 닦더니, 이내 기운을 차리고 워플 씨를 각하라 부르며 그의 지느러미를 잡게 해 달라고 청했다."], ["Wopsle, conceding his fin with a gracious dignity, was immediately shoved into a dusty corner, while everybody danced a hornpipe; and from that corner, surveying the public with a discontented eye, became aware of me.", "워플 씨가 우아하고 위엄 있게 자기 지느러미를 내어 주자, 그는 곧장 먼지투성이 구석으로 떠밀려 났고, 그동안 모두가 호른파이프 춤을 추었다. 그리고 그 구석에서 못마땅한 눈초리로 관객들을 둘러보던 그가, 나를 알아보았다."]], [["The second piece was the last new grand comic Christmas pantomime, in the first scene of which, it pained me to suspect that I detected Mr.", "두 번째 작품은 새로 올린 성대한 희극풍 크리스마스 무언극으로, 그 첫 장면에서 나는 워플 씨를 알아본 듯하여 마음이 아팠다."], ["Wopsle with red worsted legs under a highly magnified phosphoric countenance and a shock of red curtain-fringe for his hair, engaged in the manufacture of thunderbolts in a mine, and displaying great cowardice when his gigantic master came home (very hoarse) to dinner.", "그는 빨간 털실로 짠 다리를 하고, 잔뜩 부풀린 인광 빛 얼굴에, 빨간 커튼 술 같은 더부룩한 머리칼을 늘어뜨린 채, 광산 안에서 벼락을 만들어 내는 일에 종사하다가, 그의 거대한 주인이 (몹시 쉰 목소리로) 저녁을 먹으러 집에 돌아오자 엄청난 겁쟁이 짓을 부렸다."], ["But he presently presented himself under worthier circumstances; for, the Genius of Youthful Love being in want of assistance,—on account of the parental brutality of an ignorant farmer who opposed the choice of his daughter’s heart, by purposely falling upon the object, in a flour-sack, out of the first-floor window,—summoned a sententious Enchanter; and he, coming up from the antipodes rather unsteadily, after an apparently violent journey, proved to be Mr. Wopsle in a high-crowned hat, with a necromantic work in one volume under his arm.", "그러나 그는 이내 좀 더 점잖은 사정 아래 모습을 드러냈다. 젊은 사랑의 정령이 도움을 필요로 했기 때문이다 — 무지한 농부 하나가 제 딸의 마음이 택한 상대를 반대하여, 일부러 밀가루 자루를 뒤집어쓴 채 이 층 창문에서 그 상대 위로 떨어지는 식의 부모다운 횡포를 부린 탓이었다 — 그리하여 정령은 격언을 늘어놓는 어느 마법사를 불러냈고, 보아하니 험난한 여정 끝에 지구 반대편에서 비틀비틀 올라온 그 마법사는, 챙 높은 모자를 쓰고 강령술에 관한 한 권짜리 책을 옆구리에 낀 워플 씨로 밝혀졌다."], ["The business of this enchanter on earth being principally to be talked at, sung at, butted at, danced at, and flashed at with fires of various colours, he had a good deal of time on his hands.", "이 마법사가 지상에서 하는 일이란 주로 사람들이 그에게 말을 퍼붓고, 노래를 퍼붓고, 머리로 들이받고, 춤을 춰 대고, 갖가지 빛깔의 불꽃을 터뜨려 대는 것을 받아 내는 데 있었던 까닭에, 그에게는 빈 시간이 꽤 많았다."], ["And I observed, with great surprise, that he devoted it to staring in my direction as if he were lost in amazement.", "그리고 나는 크게 놀라며, 그가 그 시간을 마치 경이에 넋을 잃은 사람처럼 내 쪽을 빤히 바라보는 데 바치고 있음을 알아챘다."]], [["There was something so remarkable in the increasing glare of Mr. Wopsle’s eye, and he seemed to be turning so many things over in his mind and to grow so confused, that I could not make it out.", "워플 씨의 눈빛이 점점 더 형형해지는 데는 무언가 심상치 않은 구석이 있었고, 그가 마음속으로 온갖 생각을 굴리며 어찌나 어리둥절해하는 듯 보였던지, 나는 도무지 영문을 알 수 없었다."], ["I sat thinking of it long after he had ascended to the clouds in a large watch-case, and still I could not make it out.", "그가 커다란 회중시계 갑 속에 담겨 구름 위로 올라간 뒤에도 나는 한참을 그 일을 생각하며 앉아 있었으나, 여전히 영문을 알 수 없었다."], ["I was still thinking of it when I came out of the theatre an hour afterwards, and found him waiting for me near the door.", "한 시간 뒤 극장을 나서며 문 가까이에서 나를 기다리는 그를 발견했을 때에도, 나는 여전히 그 일을 생각하고 있었다."]], [["“How do you do?”", "\"안녕하셨습니까?\""], ["said I, shaking hands with him as we turned down the street together. “I saw that you saw me.”", "함께 거리를 걸어 내려가며 나는 그와 악수하며 말했다. \"선생께서 저를 보신 걸 저도 알았습니다.\""]], [["“Saw you, Mr. Pip!”", "\"보았지요, 핍 씨!\""], ["he returned.", "그가 대답했다."], ["“Yes, of course I saw you.", "\"그럼요, 당연히 보았고말고요."], ["But who else was there?”", "한데 거기 또 누가 있었지요?\""]], [["“Who else?”", "\"또 누구라니요?\""]], [["“It is the strangest thing,”", "\"참으로 이상한 일이오.\""], ["said Mr. Wopsle, drifting into his lost look again; “and yet I could swear to him.”", "다시 넋이 나간 표정으로 빠져들며 워플 씨가 말했다. \"그렇지만 나는 그자라고 맹세할 수 있소.\""]], [["Becoming alarmed, I entreated Mr. Wopsle to explain his meaning.", "나는 불안해져, 무슨 뜻인지 설명해 달라고 워플 씨에게 간청했다."]], [["“Whether I should have noticed him at first but for your being there,” said Mr.", "\"당신이 거기 없었더라면 내가 애초에 그자를 알아챘을지는,\""], ["Wopsle, going on in the same lost way, “I can’t be positive; yet I think I should.”", "여전히 같은 넋 나간 투로 워플 씨가 말을 이었다. \"확언할 수 없소. 그래도 알아챘을 거라 생각하오.\""]], [["Involuntarily I looked round me, as I was accustomed to look round me when I went home; for these mysterious words gave me a chill.", "나는 나도 모르게 주위를 둘러보았다. 집으로 돌아갈 때면 으레 그러던 버릇대로. 이 알 수 없는 말이 내게 오싹한 한기를 안겨 주었던 것이다."]], [["“Oh!", "\"오!"], ["He can’t be in sight,”", "그자가 보일 리는 없소.\""], ["said Mr. Wopsle.", "워플 씨가 말했다."], ["“He went out before I went off.", "\"그자는 내가 무대를 떠나기 전에 나갔으니까."], ["I saw him go.”", "나가는 걸 내가 보았소.\""]], [["Having the reason that I had for being suspicious, I even suspected this poor actor.", "의심을 품을 만한 까닭을 지니고 있던 나는, 이 가련한 배우조차도 의심했다."], ["I mistrusted a design to entrap me into some admission.", "나에게서 무언가 시인을 끌어내려는 계략이 아닌가 미덥지 않았던 것이다."], ["Therefore I glanced at him as we walked on together, but said nothing.", "그래서 나는 함께 걸어가면서 그를 흘끔거렸을 뿐, 아무 말도 하지 않았다."]], [["“I had a ridiculous fancy that he must be with you, Mr. Pip, till I saw that you were quite unconscious of him, sitting behind you there like a ghost.”", "\"나는 그자가 틀림없이 당신과 함께 온 사람이려니 하는 어처구니없는 생각을 했소, 핍 씨, 당신이 그자를 전혀 알아채지 못하고 있다는 걸 알기 전까지는 말이오. 그자는 유령처럼 거기 당신 뒤에 앉아 있었거든.\""]], [["My former chill crept over me again, but I was resolved not to speak yet, for it was quite consistent with his words that he might be set on to induce me to connect these references with Provis.", "아까의 한기가 다시 나를 스멀스멀 덮쳐 왔으나, 나는 아직은 입을 열지 않기로 마음먹었다. 그가 이 언급들을 프로비스와 결부시키도록 나를 꾀어내라고 누군가에게 사주받았을 수도 있다는 것이, 그의 말과 충분히 맞아떨어졌기 때문이다."], ["Of course, I was perfectly sure and safe that Provis had not been there.", "물론 나는 프로비스가 거기 있지 않았다는 것을 완벽히 확신하며 마음이 놓였다."]], [["“I dare say you wonder at me, Mr.", "\"내가 이상해 보이는가 보오, 핍 씨."], ["Pip; indeed, I see you do.", "정말이지, 그렇게 여기는 게 보이는구려."], ["But it is so very strange!", "하지만 너무나도 이상한 일이라서 말이오!"], ["You’ll hardly believe what I am going to tell you.", "내가 지금 하려는 말을 당신은 좀처럼 믿지 못할 거요."], ["I could hardly believe it myself, if you told me.”", "당신이 내게 그런 말을 한다면 나 자신도 좀처럼 믿지 못할 테니까.\""]], [["“Indeed?”", "\"그렇습니까?\""], ["said I.", "내가 말했다."]], [["“No, indeed. Mr.", "\"그렇소, 정말이오."], ["Pip, you remember in old times a certain Christmas Day, when you were quite a child, and I dined at Gargery’s, and some soldiers came to the door to get a pair of handcuffs mended?”", "핍 씨, 당신이 아주 어린아이였던 옛 시절, 어느 크리스마스 날을 기억하오? 내가 가저리네 집에서 저녁을 들고, 군인 몇이 수갑 한 짝을 고치러 문간에 찾아왔던 그날 말이오.\""]], [["“I remember it very well.”", "\"아주 잘 기억합니다.\""]], [["“And you remember that there was a chase after two convicts, and that we joined in it, and that Gargery took you on his back, and that I took the lead, and you kept up with me as well as you could?”", "\"그리고 죄수 둘을 뒤쫓는 추격이 벌어졌고, 우리도 거기 끼어들었고, 가저리가 당신을 등에 업었고, 내가 앞장섰고, 당신이 힘닿는 데까지 나를 따라붙었던 걸 기억하오?\""]], [["“I remember it all very well.”", "\"그 일을 모두 아주 잘 기억합니다.\""], ["Better than he thought,—except the last clause.", "그가 생각하는 것보다 더 잘 — 다만 마지막 대목만은 빼고."]], [["“And you remember that we came up with the two in a ditch, and that there was a scuffle between them, and that one of them had been severely handled and much mauled about the face by the other?”", "\"그리고 우리가 도랑 속에서 그 둘을 따라잡았던 걸, 그 둘 사이에 격투가 벌어졌던 걸, 그중 하나가 다른 하나에게 호되게 당하고 얼굴이 마구 찢긴 걸 기억하오?\""]], [["“I see it all before me.”", "\"눈앞에 그대로 떠오릅니다.\""]], [["“And that the soldiers lighted torches, and put the two in the centre, and that we went on to see the last of them, over the black marshes, with the torchlight shining on their faces,—I am particular about that,—with the torchlight shining on their faces, when there was an outer ring of dark night all about us?”", "\"그리고 군인들이 횃불을 밝혀 그 둘을 가운데 세웠던 걸, 우리가 그들의 마지막 모습을 보려고 검은 습지를 가로질러 따라갔던 걸, 그들의 얼굴 위로 횃불 빛이 비치던 걸 — 나는 이 대목을 분명히 해 두고 싶소 — 우리를 사방으로 에워싼 바깥 둘레는 캄캄한 밤이었는데, 그들의 얼굴 위로 횃불 빛이 비치던 걸 기억하오?\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"네.\""], ["said I.", "내가 말했다."], ["“I remember all that.”", "\"그 일을 모두 기억합니다.\""]], [["“Then, Mr. Pip, one of those two prisoners sat behind you tonight.", "\"그렇다면, 핍 씨, 그 두 죄수 가운데 하나가 오늘 밤 당신 뒤에 앉아 있었소."], ["I saw him over your shoulder.”", "나는 당신 어깨 너머로 그자를 보았소.\""]], [["“Steady!” I thought.", "'침착하자!' 나는 마음속으로 생각했다."], ["I asked him then, “Which of the two do you suppose you saw?”", "그러고는 그에게 물었다. \"그 둘 중 어느 쪽을 보았다고 생각하십니까?\""]], [["“The one who had been mauled,”", "\"호되게 당했던 쪽이오.\""], ["he answered readily, “and I’ll swear I saw him!", "그가 거침없이 대답했다. \"맹세코 내가 그자를 보았소!"], ["The more I think of him, the more certain I am of him.”", "그자를 생각하면 할수록, 그자가 틀림없다는 확신이 더 굳어지오.\""]], [["“This is very curious!”", "\"참으로 묘한 일이군요!\""], ["said I, with the best assumption I could put on of its being nothing more to me.", "나는 그 일이 내게는 그 이상 아무것도 아니라는 시늉을 할 수 있는 최선을 다해 말했다."], ["“Very curious indeed!”", "\"정말이지 참으로 묘한 일입니다!\""]], [["I cannot exaggerate the enhanced disquiet into which this conversation threw me, or the special and peculiar terror I felt at Compeyson’s having been behind me “like a ghost.”", "이 대화가 나를 얼마나 더한 불안 속으로 몰아넣었는지, 또 콤페이슨이 \"유령처럼\" 내 등 뒤에 있었다는 데서 내가 느낀 그 각별하고도 기묘한 공포가 어떠했는지는, 아무리 부풀려 말해도 지나치지 않을 것이다."], ["For if he had ever been out of my thoughts for a few moments together since the hiding had begun, it was in those very moments when he was closest to me; and to think that I should be so unconscious and off my guard after all my care was as if I had shut an avenue of a hundred doors to keep him out, and then had found him at my elbow.", "은신이 시작된 이래로 그가 단 몇 분이라도 한꺼번에 내 머릿속을 떠나 있었던 적이 있다면, 그것은 바로 그가 내게 가장 가까이 다가와 있던 그 순간들이었으니까. 그리고 그 온갖 조심에도 불구하고 내가 그토록 무방비로 넋을 놓고 있었다고 생각하니, 마치 그를 막으려고 백 개의 문이 달린 길목을 닫아걸고서는, 그러고 나서 바로 내 팔꿈치 곁에 그가 있는 것을 발견한 격이었다."], ["I could not doubt, either, that he was there, because I was there, and that, however slight an appearance of danger there might be about us, danger was always near and active.", "또한 그가 거기 있었던 것은 내가 거기 있었기 때문이며, 우리를 둘러싼 위험이 아무리 사소해 보일지라도 위험은 늘 가까이서 살아 움직이고 있다는 것을, 나는 의심할 수 없었다."]], [["I put such questions to Mr.", "나는 워플 씨에게 여러 가지를 물었다."], ["Wopsle as, When did the man come in?", "이를테면, 그 사내가 언제 들어왔느냐고."], ["He could not tell me that; he saw me, and over my shoulder he saw the man.", "그는 그것을 내게 말해 주지 못했다. 그는 나를 보았고, 내 어깨 너머로 그 사내를 보았던 것이다."], ["It was not until he had seen him for some time that he began to identify him; but he had from the first vaguely associated him with me, and known him as somehow belonging to me in the old village time.", "한동안 그를 보고 나서야 비로소 그자가 누구인지 알아보기 시작했으나, 처음부터 그는 어렴풋이 그 사내를 나와 결부시켰고, 옛 마을 시절에 어떻게든 나와 관계있는 자로 알아보았다."], ["How was he dressed? Prosperously, but not noticeably otherwise; he thought, in black.", "그 사내는 어떤 차림이었는가? 형편이 넉넉해 보였으나, 그 밖에 눈에 띌 만한 데는 없었다."], ["Was his face at all disfigured?", "그가 보기에는 검은 옷차림인 듯했다."], ["No, he believed not.", "얼굴에 무슨 흉터라도 있었는가?"], ["I believed not too, for, although in my brooding state I had taken no especial notice of the people behind me, I thought it likely that a face at all disfigured would have attracted my attention.", "아니, 없었던 것 같다고 했다. 나 역시 없었으리라 여겼다. 골똘히 생각에 잠겨 있던 터라 내 뒤의 사람들을 특별히 눈여겨보지는 않았으나, 조금이라도 흉이 있는 얼굴이었다면 내 주의를 끌었을 법하다고 생각했기 때문이다."]], [["When Mr. Wopsle had imparted to me all that he could recall or I extract, and when I had treated him to a little appropriate refreshment, after the fatigues of the evening, we parted.", "워플 씨가 기억해 낼 수 있는 것이든 내가 캐낼 수 있는 것이든 죄다 내게 들려준 뒤, 저녁의 노고를 위로하고자 그에게 알맞은 다과를 조금 대접하고 나서, 우리는 헤어졌다."], ["It was between twelve and one o’clock when I reached the Temple, and the gates were shut. No one was near me when I went in and went home.", "내가 템플에 이르렀을 때는 열두 시와 한 시 사이였고, 대문은 닫혀 있었다. 내가 안으로 들어가 집으로 향할 때 내 가까이에는 아무도 없었다."]], [["Herbert had come in, and we held a very serious council by the fire.", "허버트가 돌아와 있어서, 우리는 난롯가에서 매우 진지하게 의논했다."], ["But there was nothing to be done, saving to communicate to Wemmick what I had that night found out, and to remind him that we waited for his hint.", "그러나 그날 밤 내가 알아낸 것을 웨믹에게 알리고, 우리가 그의 언질을 기다리고 있음을 그에게 상기시키는 것 외에는, 달리 할 수 있는 일이 없었다."], ["As I thought that I might compromise him if I went too often to the Castle, I made this communication by letter.", "내가 너무 자주 그 성에 드나들면 그를 곤란하게 만들 수도 있겠다 싶어, 나는 이 전갈을 편지로 보냈다."], ["I wrote it before I went to bed, and went out and posted it; and again no one was near me.", "잠자리에 들기 전에 편지를 써서 나가 부쳤는데, 이번에도 내 가까이에는 아무도 없었다."], ["Herbert and I agreed that we could do nothing else but be very cautious.", "허버트와 나는 그저 몹시 조심하는 것밖에는 달리 도리가 없다는 데 뜻을 모았다."], ["And we were very cautious indeed,—more cautious than before, if that were possible,—and I for my part never went near Chinks’s Basin, except when I rowed by, and then I only looked at Mill Pond Bank as I looked at anything else.", "그리고 우리는 정말이지 몹시 조심했다 — 가능한 일이라면, 전보다 더 조심했다 — 그리고 나로 말하자면, 보트를 저어 지나갈 때를 빼고는 결코 칭크스 만 근처에 가지 않았으며, 그럴 때에도 다른 무엇을 바라보듯 그저 밀폰드 둑을 바라볼 뿐이었다."]], [["# Chapter XLVIII.", "# Chapter XLVIII."]], [["The second of the two meetings referred to in the last chapter occurred about a week after the first.", "앞 장에서 언급한 두 차례의 만남 가운데 두 번째 것은, 첫 번째 만남이 있고 나서 일주일쯤 지나 일어났다."], ["I had again left my boat at the wharf below Bridge; the time was an hour earlier in the afternoon; and, undecided where to dine, I had strolled up into Cheapside, and was strolling along it, surely the most unsettled person in all the busy concourse, when a large hand was laid upon my shoulder by some one overtaking me.", "나는 다시금 다리 아래 부두에 내 배를 매어 두었는데, 시간은 오후로도 한 시간쯤 이른 때였다. 어디서 저녁을 먹을지 정하지 못한 채 나는 칩사이드 쪽으로 어슬렁어슬렁 올라갔고, 그 분주한 인파 가운데서도 분명 가장 갈피를 못 잡는 사람의 모습으로 그 거리를 따라 거닐고 있었다. 그때 누군가 나를 앞질러 오며 커다란 손을 내 어깨에 얹었다."], ["It was Mr. Jaggers’s hand, and he passed it through my arm.", "그것은 재거스 씨의 손이었고, 그는 그 손을 내 팔에 끼워 넣었다."]], [["“As we are going in the same direction, Pip, we may walk together.", "\"우리가 같은 방향으로 가니, 핍, 함께 걸어도 되겠군."], ["Where are you bound for?”", "어디로 가는 길인가?\""]], [["“For the Temple, I think,” said I.", "\"템플로 갈까 합니다.\" 내가 말했다."]], [["“Don’t you know?”", "\"모르겠다는 건가?\""], ["said Mr. Jaggers.", "재거스 씨가 말했다."]], [["“Well,”", "\"글쎄요.\""], ["I returned, glad for once to get the better of him in cross-examination, “I do not know, for I have not made up my mind.”", "나는 한 번쯤은 그를 반대 신문에서 이겨 보았다는 것이 기뻐서 대꾸했다. \"저는 정말 모릅니다. 아직 마음을 정하지 못했으니까요.\""]], [["“You are going to dine?” said Mr.", "\"저녁을 먹으러 가는 길이지?\""], ["Jaggers. “You don’t mind admitting that, I suppose?”", "재거스 씨가 말했다. \"그건 인정해도 상관없을 테지?\""]], [["“No,”", "\"네.\""], ["I returned, “I don’t mind admitting that.”", "내가 대꾸했다. \"그건 인정해도 상관없습니다.\""]], [["“And are not engaged?”", "\"그리고 선약은 없고?\""]], [["“I don’t mind admitting also that I am not engaged.”", "\"선약이 없다는 것도 인정해도 상관없습니다.\""]], [["“Then,”", "\"그렇다면,\""], ["said Mr. Jaggers, “come and dine with me.”", "재거스 씨가 말했다. \"나와 함께 저녁을 들지.\""]], [["I was going to excuse myself, when he added, “Wemmick’s coming.”", "내가 막 사양하려는데, 그가 덧붙였다. \"웨믹도 올 거야.\""], ["So I changed my excuse into an acceptance,—the few words I had uttered, serving for the beginning of either,—and we went along Cheapside and slanted off to Little Britain, while the lights were springing up brilliantly in the shop windows, and the street lamp-lighters, scarcely finding ground enough to plant their ladders on in the midst of the afternoon’s bustle, were skipping up and down and running in and out, opening more red eyes in the gathering fog than my rushlight tower at the Hummums had opened white eyes in the ghostly wall.", "그래서 나는 사양을 받아들임으로 바꾸었다 — 내가 내뱉은 몇 마디 말은 둘 중 어느 쪽의 시작으로도 쓸 수 있었으니까 — 그리고 우리는 칩사이드를 따라 걷다가 비스듬히 꺾어 리틀브리튼 쪽으로 들어섰다. 그동안 상점 진열창마다 불빛이 화려하게 피어올랐고, 거리의 가로등지기들은 오후의 북새통 한가운데서 사다리를 세울 땅조차 좀체 찾지 못한 채 위아래로 폴짝거리고 이리저리 뛰어다니며, 후멈스 여관에서 내 골풀 양초 탑이 그 으스스한 벽에 흰 눈을 떴던 것보다 더 많은 붉은 눈을, 모여드는 안개 속에 떠 올리고 있었다."]], [["At the office in Little Britain there was the usual letter-writing, hand-washing, candle-snuffing, and safe-locking, that closed the business of the day. As I stood idle by Mr.", "리틀브리튼의 사무소에서는 하루의 업무를 마무리하는 늘 그대로의 편지 쓰기, 손 씻기, 촛불 끄기, 금고 잠그기가 이루어지고 있었다."], ["Jaggers’s fire, its rising and falling flame made the two casts on the shelf look as if they were playing a diabolical game at bo-peep with me; while the pair of coarse, fat office candles that dimly lighted Mr. Jaggers as he wrote in a corner were decorated with dirty winding-sheets, as if in remembrance of a host of hanged clients.", "내가 재거스 씨의 난롯가에 우두커니 서 있자니, 오르락내리락하는 불꽃 때문에 선반 위의 두 석고상은 마치 나를 상대로 까꿍 놀이 같은 흉악한 장난을 치고 있는 것처럼 보였다. 한편 한쪽 구석에서 글을 쓰고 있는 재거스 씨를 침침하게 비추는 거칠고 굵은 사무용 양초 한 쌍은, 마치 교수형당한 의뢰인들의 무리를 추억하기라도 하듯 더러운 수의 같은 촛농을 두르고 있었다."]], [["We went to Gerrard Street, all three together, in a hackney-coach: And, as soon as we got there, dinner was served.", "우리는 셋이 다 함께 삯마차를 타고 제라드 거리로 갔다. 그리고 그곳에 닿자마자 저녁상이 차려졌다."], ["Although I should not have thought of making, in that place, the most distant reference by so much as a look to Wemmick’s Walworth sentiments, yet I should have had no objection to catching his eye now and then in a friendly way. But it was not to be done.", "비록 그곳에서라면 나는 눈짓 하나로라도 웨믹의 월워스식 정서에 대해 아무리 먼 언급조차 떠올리지 않았을 테지만, 그래도 이따금 다정하게 그의 눈길과 마주치는 것쯤은 마다하지 않았을 것이다. 하지만 그것은 가능한 일이 아니었다."], ["He turned his eyes on Mr. Jaggers whenever he raised them from the table, and was as dry and distant to me as if there were twin Wemmicks, and this was the wrong one.", "그는 식탁에서 눈을 들 때마다 그 눈길을 재거스 씨에게로 돌렸고, 마치 웨믹이 쌍둥이라도 되어 이쪽은 엉뚱한 쪽이기라도 한 듯, 나에게는 메마르고 서먹하기만 했다."]], [["“Did you send that note of Miss Havisham’s to Mr.", "\"미스 해비셤의 그 쪽지를 핍 군에게 보냈나, 웨믹?\""], ["Pip, Wemmick?” Mr. Jaggers asked, soon after we began dinner.", "우리가 저녁을 들기 시작한 지 얼마 되지 않아 재거스 씨가 물었다."]], [["“No, sir,”", "\"아닙니다, 선생님.\""], ["returned Wemmick; “it was going by post, when you brought Mr. Pip into the office.", "웨믹이 대답했다. \"막 우편으로 부치려던 참에 선생님께서 핍 군을 사무소로 데리고 오셨지요."], ["Here it is.”", "여기 있습니다.\""], ["He handed it to his principal instead of to me.", "그는 그것을 나에게가 아니라 자기 상관에게 건넸다."]], [["“It’s a note of two lines, Pip,”", "\"두 줄짜리 쪽지일세, 핍.\""], ["said Mr. Jaggers, handing it on, “sent up to me by Miss Havisham on account of her not being sure of your address.", "재거스 씨가 그것을 다시 넘겨주며 말했다. \"미스 해비셤이 자네 주소를 확실히 알지 못해 나한테 보내온 거야."], ["She tells me that she wants to see you on a little matter of business you mentioned to her.", "자네가 그분에게 말했던 어떤 사소한 용무 때문에 자네를 보고 싶다는군."], ["You’ll go down?”", "내려가 볼 텐가?\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"네.\""], ["said I, casting my eyes over the note, which was exactly in those terms.", "내가 그 쪽지에 눈을 던지며 말했는데, 내용은 정확히 그런 말투였다."]], [["“When do you think of going down?”", "\"언제 내려갈 생각인가?\""]], [["“I have an impending engagement,”", "\"임박한 약속이 하나 있어서요.\""], ["said I, glancing at Wemmick, who was putting fish into the post-office, “that renders me rather uncertain of my time.", "나는 생선을 우편함에 집어넣고 있는 웨믹을 흘긋 보며 말했다. \"시간이 좀 불확실합니다만."], ["At once, I think.”", "곧장 가야겠다고 생각합니다.\""]], [["“If Mr. Pip has the intention of going at once,”", "\"핍 군이 곧장 갈 작정이라면,\""], ["said Wemmick to Mr. Jaggers, “he needn’t write an answer, you know.”", "웨믹이 재거스 씨에게 말했다. \"답장은 쓸 필요가 없겠군요, 아시겠지만.\""]], [["Receiving this as an intimation that it was best not to delay, I settled that I would go to-morrow, and said so.", "이 말을 지체하지 않는 편이 낫다는 암시로 받아들인 나는, 내일 가기로 마음을 정하고 그렇게 말했다."], ["Wemmick drank a glass of wine, and looked with a grimly satisfied air at Mr. Jaggers, but not at me.", "웨믹은 포도주를 한 잔 마시고는 음울하게 만족스러운 기색으로 재거스 씨를 바라보았으나, 나는 보지 않았다."]], [["“So, Pip! Our friend the Spider,”", "\"그래서 말이지, 핍! 우리 친구 거미가,\""], ["said Mr. Jaggers, “has played his cards.", "재거스 씨가 말했다. \"패를 다 펼쳤어."], ["He has won the pool.”", "판돈을 따 갔지.\""]], [["It was as much as I could do to assent.", "나로서는 동의하는 것만으로도 벅찼다."]], [["“Hah!", "\"하!"], ["He is a promising fellow—in his way—but he may not have it all his own way.", "그자는 장래성 있는 친구야 — 제 나름으로는 말이야 — 하지만 모든 일이 제 뜻대로 되지만은 않을 거야."], ["The stronger will win in the end, but the stronger has to be found out first. If he should turn to, and beat her—”", "결국엔 더 센 쪽이 이기겠지만, 누가 더 센지는 우선 가려져야 하지. 만약 그자가 마음을 돌려, 그녀를 두들겨 패기라도 한다면 —\""]], [["“Surely,”", "\"설마,\""], ["I interrupted, with a burning face and heart, “you do not seriously think that he is scoundrel enough for that, Mr. Jaggers?”", "내가 얼굴과 가슴이 달아오른 채 말을 가로막았다. \"그자가 그럴 만큼 비열한 놈이라고 진지하게 생각하시는 건 아니겠지요, 재거스 씨?\""]], [["“I didn’t say so, Pip. I am putting a case.", "\"나는 그렇다고 말하지 않았네, 핍. 하나의 경우를 가정하는 거야."], ["If he should turn to and beat her, he may possibly get the strength on his side; if it should be a question of intellect, he certainly will not.", "만약 그자가 마음을 돌려 그녀를 두들겨 팬다면, 어쩌면 힘에서는 자기 쪽이 우위를 점할지도 모르지. 하지만 그것이 지력의 문제라면, 그자는 분명 이기지 못할 거야."], ["It would be chance work to give an opinion how a fellow of that sort will turn out in such circumstances, because it’s a toss-up between two results.”", "그런 종류의 친구가 그런 상황에서 어떻게 될지에 대해 의견을 내놓는 건 요행에 맡기는 일이야. 두 가지 결과 사이의 동전 던지기니까.\""]], [["“May I ask what they are?”", "\"그 두 가지가 무엇인지 여쭤봐도 될까요?\""]], [["“A fellow like our friend the Spider,” answered Mr.", "\"우리 친구 거미 같은 친구는,\" 재거스 씨가 대답했다."], ["Jaggers, “either beats or cringes.", "\"두들겨 패거나 아니면 굽실거리거나, 둘 중 하나야."], ["He may cringe and growl, or cringe and not growl; but he either beats or cringes.", "굽실거리며 으르렁댈 수도 있고, 굽실거리되 으르렁대지 않을 수도 있지. 하지만 그자는 두들겨 패거나 아니면 굽실거려."], ["Ask Wemmick his opinion.”", "웨믹에게 그의 의견을 물어보게.\""]], [["“Either beats or cringes,” said Wemmick, not at all addressing himself to me.", "\"두들겨 패거나 아니면 굽실거리거나죠.\" 웨믹은 조금도 나를 향해 말하지 않은 채 말했다."]], [["“So here’s to Mrs. Bentley Drummle,” said Mr.", "\"그러니, 벤틀리 드러믈 부인을 위하여.\""], ["Jaggers, taking a decanter of choicer wine from his dumb-waiter, and filling for each of us and for himself, “and may the question of supremacy be settled to the lady’s satisfaction!", "재거스 씨가 운반대에서 더 고급한 포도주가 든 디캔터를 집어 우리 각자와 자신의 잔에 따르며 말했다. \"우열의 문제가 그 부인이 만족할 만하게 결판나기를!"], ["To the satisfaction of the lady and the gentleman, it never will be.", "부인과 신사 양쪽이 다 만족할 만하게야 결코 결판나지 않을 테지."], ["Now, Molly, Molly, Molly, Molly, how slow you are to-day!”", "자, 몰리, 몰리, 몰리, 몰리, 오늘은 어찌 그리 굼뜬가!\""]], [["She was at his elbow when he addressed her, putting a dish upon the table.", "그가 그녀에게 말을 건넸을 때 그녀는 식탁 위에 접시를 놓으며 그의 팔꿈치 곁에 있었다."], ["As she withdrew her hands from it, she fell back a step or two, nervously muttering some excuse.", "그녀는 접시에서 손을 떼며 한두 걸음 물러섰고, 무언가 변명을 신경질적으로 중얼거렸다."], ["And a certain action of her fingers, as she spoke, arrested my attention.", "그리고 그녀가 말할 때 그 손가락의 어떤 움직임이 내 주의를 사로잡았다."]], [["“What’s the matter?”", "\"무슨 일인가?\""], ["said Mr. Jaggers.", "재거스 씨가 말했다."]], [["“Nothing. Only the subject we were speaking of,”", "\"아무것도 아닙니다. 다만 우리가 이야기하던 그 화제가,\""], ["said I, “was rather painful to me.”", "내가 말했다. \"저에게는 좀 괴로운 것이라서요.\""]], [["The action of her fingers was like the action of knitting.", "그녀의 손가락 움직임은 뜨개질하는 동작 같았다."], ["She stood looking at her master, not understanding whether she was free to go, or whether he had more to say to her and would call her back if she did go.", "그녀는 자기 주인을 바라보며 서 있었는데, 자기가 가도 되는 것인지, 아니면 그가 더 할 말이 있어 자기가 가면 다시 불러 세울 것인지를 알지 못하는 듯했다."], ["Her look was very intent.", "그녀의 눈길은 무척 골똘했다."], ["Surely, I had seen exactly such eyes and such hands on a memorable occasion very lately!", "분명 나는 바로 이런 눈과 이런 손을, 아주 최근의 어느 잊지 못할 자리에서 본 적이 있었다!"]], [["He dismissed her, and she glided out of the room.", "그가 그녀를 물러가게 하자, 그녀는 미끄러지듯 방을 나갔다."], ["But she remained before me as plainly as if she were still there.", "그러나 그녀는 여전히 거기 있는 것처럼 또렷이 내 앞에 남아 있었다."], ["I looked at those hands, I looked at those eyes, I looked at that flowing hair; and I compared them with other hands, other eyes, other hair, that I knew of, and with what those might be after twenty years of a brutal husband and a stormy life.", "나는 그 손을 보았고, 그 눈을 보았고, 그 흘러내리는 머리카락을 보았다. 그리고 그것들을, 내가 알고 있는 다른 손, 다른 눈, 다른 머리카락과 견주어 보았고, 그것들이 잔인한 남편과 험난한 삶을 이십 년 겪고 나면 어떻게 될지와도 견주어 보았다."], ["I looked again at those hands and eyes of the housekeeper, and thought of the inexplicable feeling that had come over me when I last walked—not alone—in the ruined garden, and through the deserted brewery.", "나는 그 가정부의 손과 눈을 다시 바라보며, 내가 지난번에 — 혼자가 아니라 — 그 폐허가 된 정원을 거닐고 버려진 양조장을 지날 때 나를 덮쳤던 그 설명할 수 없는 느낌을 떠올렸다."], ["I thought how the same feeling had come back when I saw a face looking at me, and a hand waving to me from a stage-coach window; and how it had come back again and had flashed about me like lightning, when I had passed in a carriage—not alone—through a sudden glare of light in a dark street.", "나는 그 똑같은 느낌이, 어느 역마차 창문에서 나를 바라보던 얼굴과 나를 향해 흔들던 손을 보았을 때 다시 돌아왔던 일을 떠올렸다. 그리고 그것이, 내가 마차를 타고 — 혼자가 아니라 — 어두운 거리에서 별안간 쏟아진 불빛 사이를 지날 때, 또다시 돌아와 번개처럼 내 주위에서 번쩍였던 일을 떠올렸다."], ["I thought how one link of association had helped that identification in the theatre, and how such a link, wanting before, had been riveted for me now, when I had passed by a chance swift from Estella’s name to the fingers with their knitting action, and the attentive eyes.", "나는 어떻게 연상의 한 고리가 그 극장에서의 식별을 도왔는지를, 그리고 전에는 빠져 있던 그런 고리가, 내가 우연히 에스텔러라는 이름에서 그 뜨개질하는 동작의 손가락과 그 골똘한 눈으로 잽싸게 옮겨 갔을 때 어떻게 내게 단단히 박혀 버렸는지를 떠올렸다."], ["And I felt absolutely certain that this woman was Estella’s mother.", "그리고 나는 이 여자가 에스텔러의 어머니라는 것을 절대적으로 확신했다."]], [["Mr. Jaggers had seen me with Estella, and was not likely to have missed the sentiments I had been at no pains to conceal.", "재거스 씨는 내가 에스텔러와 함께 있는 것을 본 적이 있었으니, 내가 굳이 감추려 애쓰지 않았던 그 감정을 놓쳤을 리 없었다."], ["He nodded when I said the subject was painful to me, clapped me on the back, put round the wine again, and went on with his dinner.", "그는 내가 그 화제가 괴롭다고 말하자 고개를 끄덕이고는, 내 등을 툭 치고, 다시 포도주를 돌리고, 저녁 식사를 계속했다."]], [["Only twice more did the housekeeper reappear, and then her stay in the room was very short, and Mr. Jaggers was sharp with her.", "가정부가 다시 나타난 것은 단 두 번뿐이었고, 그나마 방에 머무는 시간은 무척 짧았으며, 재거스 씨는 그녀에게 퉁명스러웠다."], ["But her hands were Estella’s hands, and her eyes were Estella’s eyes, and if she had reappeared a hundred times I could have been neither more sure nor less sure that my conviction was the truth.", "그러나 그녀의 손은 에스텔러의 손이었고, 그녀의 눈은 에스텔러의 눈이었으니, 설령 그녀가 백 번을 다시 나타났다 해도 나는 내 확신이 진실이라는 것에 대해 더 확신하지도 덜 확신하지도 못했을 것이다."]], [["It was a dull evening, for Wemmick drew his wine, when it came round, quite as a matter of business,—just as he might have drawn his salary when that came round,—and with his eyes on his chief, sat in a state of perpetual readiness for cross-examination.", "그날 저녁은 따분했다. 웨믹은 포도주가 돌아오면 그것을 순전히 업무처럼 따라 마셨으니 — 마치 봉급날이 돌아오면 봉급을 타듯 — 그러면서 줄곧 상관에게 눈을 둔 채, 반대 신문에 끊임없이 대비하고 있는 상태로 앉아 있었다."], ["As to the quantity of wine, his post-office was as indifferent and ready as any other post-office for its quantity of letters.", "포도주의 양으로 말하자면, 그의 우편함은 다른 어떤 우편함이 편지의 양에 대해 그러하듯 무심하고 또 채비되어 있었다."], ["From my point of view, he was the wrong twin all the time, and only externally like the Wemmick of Walworth.", "내 시각에서 보자면, 그는 내내 엉뚱한 쪽 쌍둥이였고, 겉모습만 월워스의 웨믹을 닮았을 뿐이었다."]], [["We took our leave early, and left together.", "우리는 일찍 작별을 고하고 함께 자리를 떴다."], ["Even when we were groping among Mr. Jaggers’s stock of boots for our hats, I felt that the right twin was on his way back; and we had not gone half a dozen yards down Gerrard Street in the Walworth direction, before I found that I was walking arm in arm with the right twin, and that the wrong twin had evaporated into the evening air.", "재거스 씨의 장화 무더기 속을 더듬어 우리 모자를 찾고 있을 때조차, 나는 진짜 쪽 쌍둥이가 돌아오고 있음을 느꼈다. 그리고 우리가 제라드 거리를 월워스 방향으로 여섯 걸음도 채 가기 전에, 나는 내가 진짜 쪽 쌍둥이와 팔짱을 끼고 걷고 있으며, 엉뚱한 쪽 쌍둥이는 저녁 공기 속으로 증발해 버렸음을 알았다."]], [["“Well!”", "\"자!\""], ["said Wemmick, “that’s over!", "웨믹이 말했다. \"그게 끝났군요!"], ["He’s a wonderful man, without his living likeness; but I feel that I have to screw myself up when I dine with him,—and I dine more comfortably unscrewed.”", "그분은 산 채로 짝을 찾을 수 없는 놀라운 사람이지요. 하지만 저는 그분과 저녁을 들 때면 제 나사를 단단히 죄어야 한다는 느낌이 들어요 — 그리고 나사를 풀고 나면 더 편하게 저녁을 들지요.\""]], [["I felt that this was a good statement of the case, and told him so.", "나는 이것이 사정을 잘 짚은 말이라고 느꼈고, 그에게 그렇게 말했다."]], [["“Wouldn’t say it to anybody but yourself,”", "\"당신 말고 다른 사람한테는 안 할 말이지요.\""], ["he answered.", "그가 대답했다."], ["“I know that what is said between you and me goes no further.”", "\"당신과 나 사이에 오간 말은 더 멀리 가지 않는다는 걸 아니까요.\""]], [["I asked him if he had ever seen Miss Havisham’s adopted daughter, Mrs.", "나는 그에게 미스 해비셤의 양녀인 벤틀리 드러믈 부인을 본 적이 있느냐고 물었다."], ["Bentley Drummle.", "그는 없다고 했다."], ["He said no. To avoid being too abrupt, I then spoke of the Aged and of Miss Skiffins.", "너무 갑작스럽지 않으려고, 나는 이어서 노친네와 미스 스키핀스 이야기를 꺼냈다."], ["He looked rather sly when I mentioned Miss Skiffins, and stopped in the street to blow his nose, with a roll of the head, and a flourish not quite free from latent boastfulness.", "미스 스키핀스를 언급하자 그는 사뭇 음흉한 표정을 짓더니, 거리에 멈춰 서서 고개를 한 번 휘젓고는, 은근한 자랑기가 아주 없지는 않은 손짓으로 코를 풀었다."]], [["“Wemmick,”", "\"웨믹,\""], ["said I, “do you remember telling me, before I first went to Mr. Jaggers’s private house, to notice that housekeeper?”", "내가 말했다. \"내가 처음 재거스 씨의 사택에 가기 전에, 당신이 나더러 저 가정부를 눈여겨보라고 했던 걸 기억합니까?\""]], [["“Did I?”", "\"제가요?\""], ["he replied.", "그가 대답했다."], ["“Ah, I dare say I did.", "\"아, 그랬을 법하군요. 제기랄,\""], ["Deuce take me,”", "그가 불쑥 덧붙였다."], ["he added, suddenly, “I know I did. I find I am not quite unscrewed yet.”", "\"그랬지요, 기억나요. 보아하니 제 나사가 아직 다 풀리지 않았나 봅니다.\""]], [["“A wild beast tamed, you called her.”", "\"길들여진 야수라고, 당신이 그녀를 그렇게 불렀지요.\""]], [["“And what do you call her?”", "\"그럼 당신은 그녀를 뭐라고 부릅니까?\""]], [["“The same.", "\"같은 말로요."], ["How did Mr. Jaggers tame her, Wemmick?”", "재거스 씨가 그녀를 어떻게 길들였습니까, 웨믹?\""]], [["“That’s his secret.", "\"그건 그분의 비밀이지요."], ["She has been with him many a long year.”", "그녀는 그분과 함께한 지 오랜 세월이 되었습니다.\""]], [["“I wish you would tell me her story.", "\"그녀의 사연을 좀 들려주면 좋겠습니다."], ["I feel a particular interest in being acquainted with it.", "나는 그것을 알게 되는 데 특별한 흥미를 느끼거든요."], ["You know that what is said between you and me goes no further.”", "당신과 나 사이에 오간 말은 더 멀리 가지 않는다는 걸 알지 않습니까.\""]], [["“Well!”", "\"글쎄요!\""], ["Wemmick replied, “I don’t know her story,—that is, I don’t know all of it.", "웨믹이 대답했다. \"저도 그녀의 사연을 다 알지는 못합니다 — 그러니까 전부는 모른다는 거지요."], ["But what I do know I’ll tell you.", "하지만 제가 아는 만큼은 말씀드리지요."], ["We are in our private and personal capacities, of course.”", "물론 우리는 지금 사적이고 개인적인 자격으로 있는 거고요.\""]], [["“Of course.”", "\"물론이지요.\""]], [["“A score or so of years ago, that woman was tried at the Old Bailey for murder, and was acquitted.", "\"한 이십 년쯤 전에, 그 여자는 살인죄로 올드베일리에서 재판을 받았고, 무죄로 풀려났습니다."], ["She was a very handsome young woman, and I believe had some gypsy blood in her.", "그녀는 무척 잘생긴 젊은 여자였고, 제 생각에 집시 피가 좀 섞여 있었던 것 같아요."], ["Anyhow, it was hot enough when it was up, as you may suppose.”", "어쨌든, 짐작하시겠지만, 한번 끓어오르면 어지간히 뜨거운 여자였지요.\""]], [["“But she was acquitted.”", "\"하지만 무죄로 풀려났다고요.\""]], [["“Mr. Jaggers was for her,”", "\"재거스 씨가 그녀의 변호를 맡았는데,\""], ["pursued Wemmick, with a look full of meaning, “and worked the case in a way quite astonishing.", "웨믹이 의미심장한 표정을 가득 담아 말을 이었다. \"정말 놀라운 방식으로 그 사건을 다루었지요."], ["It was a desperate case, and it was comparatively early days with him then, and he worked it to general admiration; in fact, it may almost be said to have made him.", "절망적인 사건이었고, 그분으로서는 비교적 초창기였는데도, 그분은 모두의 감탄을 받을 만큼 그 사건을 요리했어요. 사실, 그 사건이 그분을 만들어 주었다고 해도 거의 과언이 아닙니다."], ["He worked it himself at the police-office, day after day for many days, contending against even a committal; and at the trial where he couldn’t work it himself, sat under counsel, and—every one knew—put in all the salt and pepper.", "그분은 경찰서에서 여러 날 날마다 손수 매달려 기소조차 못 하게 맞섰고, 정작 자기가 직접 변론할 수 없는 재판에서는 변호인 밑에 앉아서 — 다들 알고 있었지요 — 모든 소금과 후추를 다 쳐 넣었지요."], ["The murdered person was a woman,—a woman a good ten years older, very much larger, and very much stronger.", "살해된 사람은 여자였습니다 — 나이가 족히 열 살은 더 많고, 훨씬 덩치가 크고, 훨씬 힘이 센 여자였어요."], ["It was a case of jealousy.", "질투로 인한 사건이었습니다."], ["They both led tramping lives, and this woman in Gerrard Street here had been married very young, over the broomstick (as we say), to a tramping man, and was a perfect fury in point of jealousy.", "둘 다 떠돌이 생활을 했는데, 여기 제라드 거리의 이 여자는 아주 젊은 나이에, 우리 식으로 말하면 빗자루를 뛰어넘는 식으로 어느 떠돌이 사내와 혼인했고, 질투심으로 말하자면 완벽한 분노의 화신이었지요."], ["The murdered woman,—more a match for the man, certainly, in point of years—was found dead in a barn near Hounslow Heath.", "살해된 여자는 — 나이로 보면 그 사내에게 분명 더 어울리는 짝이었는데 — 하운슬로 히스 근처의 어느 헛간에서 죽은 채 발견되었습니다."], ["There had been a violent struggle, perhaps a fight.", "격렬한 몸싸움이, 어쩌면 격투가 있었던 모양이에요."], ["She was bruised and scratched and torn, and had been held by the throat, at last, and choked.", "그녀는 멍이 들고 긁히고 찢긴 데다, 끝내는 목을 졸려 질식당했습니다."], ["Now, there was no reasonable evidence to implicate any person but this woman, and on the improbabilities of her having been able to do it Mr. Jaggers principally rested his case.", "그런데, 이 여자 말고 다른 어떤 사람을 연루시킬 만한 합당한 증거가 없었고, 재거스 씨는 주로 그녀가 그 일을 해낼 수 있었을 리 없다는 그 개연성 없음에 사건의 근거를 두었지요."], ["You may be sure,”", "분명히 말씀드리지만,\""], ["said Wemmick, touching me on the sleeve, “that he never dwelt upon the strength of her hands then, though he sometimes does now.”", "웨믹이 내 소맷자락을 건드리며 말했다. \"그분은 그때만큼은 그녀의 손 힘에 대해 결코 늘어놓지 않았답니다. 요즘은 가끔 그러기는 합니다만.\""]], [["I had told Wemmick of his showing us her wrists, that day of the dinner party.", "나는 그 만찬 날에 그분이 우리에게 그녀의 손목을 내보였던 일을 웨믹에게 이야기한 바 있었다."]], [["“Well, sir!” Wemmick went on; “it happened—happened, don’t you see?—that this woman was so very artfully dressed from the time of her apprehension, that she looked much slighter than she really was; in particular, her sleeves are always remembered to have been so skilfully contrived that her arms had quite a delicate look.", "\"그런데 말입니다, 선생!\" 웨믹이 말을 이었다."], ["She had only a bruise or two about her,—nothing for a tramp,—but the backs of her hands were lacerated, and the question was, Was it with finger-nails? Now, Mr.", "\"마침 그렇게 됐어요 — 그렇게 됐다는 거, 아시겠어요? — 이 여자가 체포된 순간부터 어찌나 교묘하게 옷을 차려입었던지, 실제보다 훨씬 가냘파 보였단 말이지요."], ["Jaggers showed that she had struggled through a great lot of brambles which were not as high as her face; but which she could not have got through and kept her hands out of; and bits of those brambles were actually found in her skin and put in evidence, as well as the fact that the brambles in question were found on examination to have been broken through, and to have little shreds of her dress and little spots of blood upon them here and there.", "특히, 그녀의 소매는 어찌나 솜씨 있게 만들어졌던지 팔이 아주 가녀린 모양새로 보였다고 다들 기억하고 있습니다. 그녀 몸에는 멍이 한두 군데 있을 뿐이었어요 — 떠돌이에게는 아무것도 아닌 정도였지요 — 그런데 손등은 찢겨 있었고, 그래서 문제는 이것이었습니다, 그게 손톱으로 그런 것이냐? 그런데 재거스 씨는, 그녀가 제 얼굴 높이만큼 높지는 않으나 손을 빼낸 채로는 도저히 빠져나갈 수 없는 무성한 가시덤불 사이를 헤치고 나왔다는 것을 보여 주었지요."], ["But the boldest point he made was this: it was attempted to be set up, in proof of her jealousy, that she was under strong suspicion of having, at about the time of the murder, frantically destroyed her child by this man—some three years old—to revenge herself upon him. Mr.", "그리고 그 가시덤불 조각이 실제로 그녀의 살갗에서 발견되어 증거로 제출되었고, 게다가 문제의 그 가시덤불을 조사해 보니 누군가 뚫고 지나간 흔적이 있고, 여기저기 그녀의 옷자락 조각과 작은 핏방울이 묻어 있더라는 사실도 함께 제출되었습니다. 하지만 그분이 펼친 가장 대담한 수는 이것이었어요. 그녀의 질투심을 입증하려고, 그녀가 살인이 벌어진 무렵에 그 사내에게 복수하려고 그 사내와의 사이에서 난 자기 아이를 — 세 살쯤 된 — 미친 듯이 죽였다는 강한 혐의를 받고 있다는 점을 세우려는 시도가 있었거든요."], ["Jaggers worked that in this way: “We say these are not marks of finger-nails, but marks of brambles, and we show you the brambles.", "재거스 씨는 그것을 이런 식으로 받아쳤지요. '우리는 이것이 손톱 자국이 아니라 가시덤불 자국이라고 주장하며, 여러분께 그 가시덤불을 보여 드립니다."], ["You say they are marks of finger-nails, and you set up the hypothesis that she destroyed her child.", "여러분은 그것이 손톱 자국이라고 주장하며, 그녀가 자기 아이를 죽였다는 가설을 세웁니다."], ["You must accept all consequences of that hypothesis.", "그렇다면 여러분은 그 가설의 모든 귀결을 받아들여야 합니다."], ["For anything we know, she may have destroyed her child, and the child in clinging to her may have scratched her hands.", "우리가 알 수 없는 일이나, 그녀가 제 아이를 죽였을 수도 있고, 그 아이가 그녀에게 매달리다가 그 손을 할퀴었을 수도 있습니다."], ["What then? You are not trying her for the murder of her child; why don’t you?", "그렇다면 어떻습니까? 여러분은 그녀를 제 아이를 죽인 죄로 재판하고 있지 않습니다."], ["As to this case, if you will have scratches, we say that, for anything we know, you may have accounted for them, assuming for the sake of argument that you have not invented them?”", "어째서 그러지 않습니까? 이 사건으로 말하자면, 여러분이 기어이 할퀸 자국을 들이대겠다면, 우리는 이렇게 말합니다, 여러분이 그것을 지어낸 것이 아니라고 논의를 위해 가정하더라도, 우리가 알 수 없는 일이나, 여러분 스스로가 이미 그것을 설명해 버린 것일 수도 있다고 말이지요.'"], ["“To sum up, sir,” said Wemmick, “Mr.", "요컨대 말입니다, 선생,\" 웨믹이 말했다."], ["Jaggers was altogether too many for the jury, and they gave in.”", "\"재거스 씨는 배심원단으로서는 도저히 당해 낼 수 없는 상대였고, 그들은 손을 들고 말았지요.\""]], [["“Has she been in his service ever since?”", "\"그 뒤로 그녀는 줄곧 그분 밑에서 일해 온 겁니까?\""]], [["“Yes; but not only that,”", "\"네. 하지만 그뿐이 아닙니다.\""], ["said Wemmick, “she went into his service immediately after her acquittal, tamed as she is now.", "웨믹이 말했다. \"그녀는 무죄로 풀려난 직후 곧장 그분 밑으로 들어갔어요, 지금처럼 길들여진 채로 말이지요."], ["She has since been taught one thing and another in the way of her duties, but she was tamed from the beginning.”", "그 이후로 직무에 관련된 이런저런 일을 배우기는 했지만, 그녀는 처음부터 길들여져 있었습니다.\""]], [["“Do you remember the sex of the child?”", "\"그 아이의 성별을 기억합니까?\""]], [["“Said to have been a girl.”", "\"여자아이였다고 합니다.\""]], [["“You have nothing more to say to me to-night?”", "\"오늘 밤 나에게 더 할 말은 없습니까?\""]], [["“Nothing.", "\"없습니다."], ["I got your letter and destroyed it.", "당신 편지는 받아서 없애 버렸어요."], ["Nothing.”", "없습니다.\""]], [["We exchanged a cordial good-night, and I went home, with new matter for my thoughts, though with no relief from the old.", "우리는 다정하게 작별 인사를 나누었고, 나는 새로운 생각거리를 안은 채 집으로 돌아갔다. 다만 묵은 생각으로부터는 아무런 위안도 얻지 못한 채로."]], [["# Chapter XLIX.", "# Chapter XLIX."]], [["Putting Miss Havisham’s note in my pocket, that it might serve as my credentials for so soon reappearing at Satis House, in case her waywardness should lead her to express any surprise at seeing me, I went down again by the coach next day.", "미스 해비셤의 쪽지를 호주머니에 넣어 두었다. 혹시 그녀가 변덕을 부려 내가 이렇게 빨리 다시 새티스 하우스에 나타난 것을 보고 놀라움을 표할 경우, 그것이 내 신분 증명이 되어 줄 것이었기 때문이다. 이튿날 나는 다시 역마차를 타고 내려갔다."], ["But I alighted at the Halfway House, and breakfasted there, and walked the rest of the distance; for I sought to get into the town quietly by the unfrequented ways, and to leave it in the same manner.", "그러나 중간 지점의 여관에서 내려 그곳에서 아침을 먹고, 남은 거리는 걸어서 갔다. 사람들 눈에 띄지 않는 한적한 길로 조용히 읍내에 들어가고, 또 같은 방식으로 떠나려 했기 때문이다."]], [["The best light of the day was gone when I passed along the quiet echoing courts behind the High Street.", "번화가 뒤편의 메아리 울리는 고요한 안뜰들을 지날 무렵, 하루 중 가장 좋은 햇빛은 이미 사라지고 없었다."], ["The nooks of ruin where the old monks had once had their refectories and gardens, and where the strong walls were now pressed into the service of humble sheds and stables, were almost as silent as the old monks in their graves.", "옛날 수도사들이 한때 식당과 정원을 두었던, 그리고 이제는 그 튼튼한 벽들이 초라한 헛간과 마구간 노릇에 떠밀려 쓰이고 있는 폐허의 구석들은, 무덤 속의 그 옛 수도사들만큼이나 적막했다."], ["The cathedral chimes had at once a sadder and a more remote sound to me, as I hurried on avoiding observation, than they had ever had before; so, the swell of the old organ was borne to my ears like funeral music; and the rooks, as they hovered about the grey tower and swung in the bare high trees of the priory garden, seemed to call to me that the place was changed, and that Estella was gone out of it for ever.", "남의 눈을 피해 서둘러 걸어가는 내 귀에, 대성당의 종소리는 전에 없이 한결 더 슬프고 한결 더 아득하게 들려왔다. 그리고 오래된 오르간의 울림은 마치 장송곡처럼 내 귀에 실려 왔다. 또한 까마귀들은 잿빛 탑 주위를 맴돌고 수도원 정원의 헐벗은 높은 나무들 위에서 흔들거리며, 이곳이 변해 버렸노라고, 에스텔라는 영영 이곳을 떠나 버렸노라고 내게 외치는 것만 같았다."]], [["An elderly woman, whom I had seen before as one of the servants who lived in the supplementary house across the back courtyard, opened the gate.", "전에 본 적이 있는 어느 나이 든 여자가 문을 열어 주었다. 뒤뜰 건너편의 별채에 사는 하인들 가운데 하나였다."], ["The lighted candle stood in the dark passage within, as of old, and I took it up and ascended the staircase alone.", "불을 켠 양초가 옛날처럼 어두운 복도 안에 놓여 있었고, 나는 그것을 집어 들고 혼자서 계단을 올라갔다."], ["Miss Havisham was not in her own room, but was in the larger room across the landing.", "미스 해비셤은 자기 방에 있지 않고, 층계참 건너편의 더 큰 방에 있었다."], ["Looking in at the door, after knocking in vain, I saw her sitting on the hearth in a ragged chair, close before, and lost in the contemplation of, the ashy fire.", "문 앞에서 헛되이 두드린 끝에 안을 들여다보니, 그녀는 다 해진 의자에 앉아 잿빛으로 사그라진 난롯불에 바싹 다가앉아, 그 불을 응시하는 데 넋을 잃고 있었다."]], [["Doing as I had often done, I went in, and stood touching the old chimney-piece, where she could see me when she raised her eyes.", "전에 곧잘 그러했듯이, 나는 안으로 들어가, 그녀가 눈을 들면 나를 볼 수 있는 곳에, 낡은 벽난로 선반에 손을 댄 채 섰다."], ["There was an air of utter loneliness upon her, that would have moved me to pity though she had wilfully done me a deeper injury than I could charge her with.", "그녀에게는 더없는 외로움의 기운이 감돌고 있었다. 설령 그녀가 내가 그녀 탓으로 돌릴 수 있는 것보다 더 깊은 상처를 고의로 내게 입혔다 한들, 나는 연민에 사로잡혔을 것이다."], ["As I stood compassionating her, and thinking how, in the progress of time, I too had come to be a part of the wrecked fortunes of that house, her eyes rested on me.", "내가 그녀를 가엾이 여기며 서서, 세월이 흐르는 동안 나 또한 그 집의 몰락한 운명의 한 부분이 되어 버렸구나 하고 생각하고 있을 때, 그녀의 눈이 내게 머물렀다."], ["She stared, and said in a low voice, “Is it real?”", "그녀는 빤히 바라보더니 낮은 목소리로 말했다. \"이게 진짜인가?\""]], [["“It is I, Pip. Mr. Jaggers gave me your note yesterday, and I have lost no time.”", "\"저예요, 핍. 어제 재거스 씨가 부인의 쪽지를 전해 주셨고, 저는 한시도 지체하지 않았습니다.\""]], [["“Thank you. Thank you.”", "\"고맙네. 고마워.\""]], [["As I brought another of the ragged chairs to the hearth and sat down, I remarked a new expression on her face, as if she were afraid of me.", "내가 다 해진 의자를 또 하나 난롯가로 가져와 앉을 때, 나는 그녀의 얼굴에 떠오른 새로운 표정을 알아차렸다. 마치 나를 두려워하는 듯한 표정이었다."]], [["“I want,”", "\"내가 하고 싶은 건,\""], ["she said, “to pursue that subject you mentioned to me when you were last here, and to show you that I am not all stone.", "그녀가 말했다. \"자네가 지난번에 여기 왔을 때 내게 꺼냈던 그 이야기를 매듭짓는 일일세. 그리고 내가 온통 돌덩이만은 아니라는 걸 자네에게 보여 주고 싶네."], ["But perhaps you can never believe, now, that there is anything human in my heart?”", "하지만 어쩌면 자네는, 이제 내 마음속에 인간적인 무언가가 있다는 걸 도무지 믿지 못할지도 모르겠군?\""]], [["When I said some reassuring words, she stretched out her tremulous right hand, as though she was going to touch me; but she recalled it again before I understood the action, or knew how to receive it.", "내가 안심시키는 몇 마디를 건네자, 그녀는 마치 나를 만지려는 듯 떨리는 오른손을 뻗었다. 그러나 내가 그 동작의 의미를 이해하기도 전에, 또 어떻게 받아들여야 할지 알기도 전에, 그녀는 그 손을 도로 거두어들였다."]], [["“You said, speaking for your friend, that you could tell me how to do something useful and good.", "\"자네는 자네 친구를 대신해 말하면서, 유익하고 좋은 일을 어떻게 하면 되는지 내게 일러 줄 수 있다고 했었지."], ["Something that you would like done, is it not?”", "자네가 이루어졌으면 하는 어떤 일이라고, 그렇지 않았나?\""]], [["“Something that I would like done very much.”", "\"제가 무척이나 이루어졌으면 하는 일이지요.\""]], [["“What is it?”", "\"그게 뭔가?\""]], [["I began explaining to her that secret history of the partnership.", "나는 그녀에게 그 동업 관계의 비밀스러운 내막을 설명하기 시작했다."], ["I had not got far into it, when I judged from her looks that she was thinking in a discursive way of me, rather than of what I said.", "그러나 그리 멀리 나아가지 못해, 나는 그녀의 표정으로 미루어 그녀가 내 말의 내용보다는 나에 대해 두서없이 생각하고 있다고 판단했다."], ["It seemed to be so; for, when I stopped speaking, many moments passed before she showed that she was conscious of the fact.", "정말로 그런 것 같았다. 내가 말을 멈추었을 때, 그녀가 그 사실을 의식한다는 기색을 보이기까지 한참이 지났으니까."]], [["“Do you break off,”", "\"자네가 말을 멈춘 건,\""], ["she asked then, with her former air of being afraid of me, “because you hate me too much to bear to speak to me?”", "그녀가 그때, 전처럼 나를 두려워하는 기색으로 물었다. \"내가 너무도 미워서, 나한테 말을 거는 걸 견딜 수 없어서인가?\""]], [["“No, no,”", "\"아닙니다, 아니에요.\""], ["I answered, “how can you think so, Miss Havisham!", "나는 대답했다. \"어떻게 그런 생각을 하실 수 있어요, 미스 해비셤!"], ["I stopped because I thought you were not following what I said.”", "제가 멈춘 건, 부인이 제 말을 따라오지 못하신다고 생각해서였습니다.\""]], [["“Perhaps I was not,”", "\"아마 그랬는지도 모르지.\""], ["she answered, putting a hand to her head.", "그녀가 한 손을 머리에 짚으며 대답했다."], ["“Begin again, and let me look at something else. Stay!", "\"다시 시작해 주게. 나는 다른 무언가를 보고 있을 테니. 잠깐!"], ["Now tell me.”", "자, 이제 말해 보게.\""]], [["She set her hand upon her stick in the resolute way that sometimes was habitual to her, and looked at the fire with a strong expression of forcing herself to attend.", "그녀는 가끔 그녀의 버릇이 되어 있던 그 단호한 태도로 한 손을 지팡이에 얹고, 억지로 정신을 집중하려는 강한 표정을 지으며 불을 바라보았다."], ["I went on with my explanation, and told her how I had hoped to complete the transaction out of my means, but how in this I was disappointed.", "나는 설명을 이어 가, 내 돈으로 그 거래를 완결 짓고 싶었으나 이 점에서 좌절을 겪었다고 그녀에게 말했다."], ["That part of the subject (I reminded her) involved matters which could form no part of my explanation, for they were the weighty secrets of another.", "그 이야기의 그 부분은(나는 그녀에게 일러 두었다) 내 설명의 일부가 될 수 없는 사정들과 얽혀 있는데, 그것이 다른 사람의 묵직한 비밀이기 때문이라고 했다."]], [["“So!” said she, assenting with her head, but not looking at me.", "\"그래서!\" 그녀가 고개를 끄덕여 수긍하면서도 나를 보지는 않은 채 말했다."], ["“And how much money is wanting to complete the purchase?”", "\"그래, 그 매입을 완결 짓는 데 돈이 얼마나 모자라는가?\""]], [["I was rather afraid of stating it, for it sounded a large sum.", "나는 그것을 말하기가 적이 두려웠다. 큰 액수로 들렸기 때문이다."], ["“Nine hundred pounds.”", "\"구백 파운드입니다.\""]], [["“If I give you the money for this purpose, will you keep my secret as you have kept your own?”", "\"내가 이 일에 쓸 돈을 자네에게 준다면, 자네는 자네 자신의 비밀을 지켜 온 만큼 내 비밀도 지켜 주겠나?\""]], [["“Quite as faithfully.”", "\"꼭 그만큼 충실히 지키겠습니다.\""]], [["“And your mind will be more at rest?”", "\"그러면 자네 마음이 더 편안해지겠나?\""]], [["“Much more at rest.”", "\"훨씬 더 편안해지겠지요.\""]], [["“Are you very unhappy now?”", "\"자네는 지금 몹시 불행한가?\""]], [["She asked this question, still without looking at me, but in an unwonted tone of sympathy.", "그녀는 여전히 나를 보지 않은 채, 그러나 평소답지 않은 동정 어린 어조로 이 질문을 던졌다."], ["I could not reply at the moment, for my voice failed me.", "나는 그 순간 대답할 수가 없었다. 목소리가 나오지 않았기 때문이다."], ["She put her left arm across the head of her stick, and softly laid her forehead on it.", "그녀는 왼팔을 지팡이 머리 위에 가로 얹고, 그 위에 가만히 이마를 댔다."]], [["“I am far from happy, Miss Havisham; but I have other causes of disquiet than any you know of.", "\"행복과는 거리가 멉니다, 미스 해비셤. 하지만 제게는 부인이 아시는 그 어떤 것과도 다른 불안의 원인들이 있습니다."], ["They are the secrets I have mentioned.”", "그것이 제가 말씀드렸던 그 비밀들입니다.\""]], [["After a little while, she raised her head, and looked at the fire again.", "잠시 후, 그녀는 고개를 들어 다시 불을 바라보았다."]], [["“It is noble in you to tell me that you have other causes of unhappiness.", "\"자네에게 다른 불행의 원인들이 있다고 내게 말해 주다니, 고결하군. 그게 정말인가?\""], ["Is it true?”", "\"너무도 정말입니다.\""]], [["“Too true.” “Can I only serve you, Pip, by serving your friend?", "\"핍, 내가 자네를 위해 해 줄 수 있는 건 자네 친구를 돕는 일뿐인가?"], ["Regarding that as done, is there nothing I can do for you yourself?”", "그 일이 이루어진 것으로 친다면, 자네 자신을 위해 내가 해 줄 수 있는 일은 아무것도 없는가?\""]], [["“Nothing.", "\"없습니다."], ["I thank you for the question.", "그렇게 물어봐 주셔서 감사합니다."], ["I thank you even more for the tone of the question.", "그렇게 물어봐 주시는 그 어조에는 더욱 감사하고요."], ["But there is nothing.”", "하지만 아무것도 없습니다.\""]], [["She presently rose from her seat, and looked about the blighted room for the means of writing.", "그녀는 이내 자리에서 일어나, 황폐해진 방 안에서 글씨 쓸 도구를 찾아 둘러보았다."], ["There were none there, and she took from her pocket a yellow set of ivory tablets, mounted in tarnished gold, and wrote upon them with a pencil in a case of tarnished gold that hung from her neck.", "그곳에는 아무것도 없었으므로, 그녀는 호주머니에서 빛바랜 금테를 두른 누런 상아 수첩을 꺼내, 목에 걸린 빛바랜 금 케이스에 든 연필로 거기에 글을 썼다."]], [["“You are still on friendly terms with Mr. Jaggers?”", "\"자네는 아직도 재거스 씨와 사이좋게 지내고 있나?\""]], [["“Quite.", "\"그럼요."], ["I dined with him yesterday.”", "어제 그분과 저녁을 함께 했습니다.\""]], [["“This is an authority to him to pay you that money, to lay out at your irresponsible discretion for your friend.", "\"이건 그가 자네에게 그 돈을 지급하라는 위임장일세. 자네 친구를 위해 자네가 책임 없이 마음대로 쓰라고 말이야."], ["I keep no money here; but if you would rather Mr. Jaggers knew nothing of the matter, I will send it to you.”", "나는 이곳에 돈을 두지 않네. 하지만 자네가 차라리 재거스 씨가 이 일을 전혀 모르길 바란다면, 내가 자네에게 돈을 보내 주겠네.\""]], [["“Thank you, Miss Havisham; I have not the least objection to receiving it from him.”", "\"감사합니다, 미스 해비셤. 그분에게서 받는 것에 저는 조금도 거리낌이 없습니다.\""]], [["She read me what she had written; and it was direct and clear, and evidently intended to absolve me from any suspicion of profiting by the receipt of the money.", "그녀는 자기가 쓴 것을 내게 읽어 주었다. 그것은 직설적이고 명료했으며, 분명 내가 그 돈을 받음으로써 사사로운 이익을 챙긴다는 의심에서 나를 벗어나게 하려는 의도였다."], ["I took the tablets from her hand, and it trembled again, and it trembled more as she took off the chain to which the pencil was attached, and put it in mine.", "나는 그녀의 손에서 수첩을 건네받았는데, 그 손은 또다시 떨렸고, 연필이 매달린 사슬을 풀어 내 손에 쥐여 줄 때는 더욱 심하게 떨렸다."], ["All this she did without looking at me.", "이 모든 일을 그녀는 나를 보지 않은 채 했다."]], [["“My name is on the first leaf.", "\"첫 장에 내 이름이 적혀 있네."], ["If you can ever write under my name, “I forgive her,” though ever so long after my broken heart is dust pray do it!”", "혹시 자네가 언젠가 내 이름 아래에 '나는 그녀를 용서한다'라고 써 줄 수 있다면, 설령 내 부서진 심장이 한 줌 먼지가 되고도 한참 뒤일지라도, 부디 그렇게 해 주게!\""]], [["“O Miss Havisham,”", "\"오, 미스 해비셤.\""], ["said I, “I can do it now.", "내가 말했다. \"지금 당장이라도 그렇게 할 수 있습니다."], ["There have been sore mistakes; and my life has been a blind and thankless one; and I want forgiveness and direction far too much, to be bitter with you.”", "쓰라린 잘못들이 있었지요. 제 삶은 눈먼, 감사할 줄 모르는 삶이었습니다. 그리고 저는 용서와 인도가 너무도 절실히 필요한 처지라, 부인을 원망하고 있을 수가 없습니다.\""]], [["She turned her face to me for the first time since she had averted it, and, to my amazement, I may even add to my terror, dropped on her knees at my feet; with her folded hands raised to me in the manner in which, when her poor heart was young and fresh and whole, they must often have been raised to heaven from her mother’s side.", "그녀는 얼굴을 돌린 이래 처음으로 내게로 얼굴을 향하더니, 놀랍게도, 아니 두렵게도, 내 발치에 무릎을 꿇었다. 두 손을 모아 내게로 들어 올렸는데, 그것은 그녀의 가엾은 심장이 어리고 싱싱하고 온전했던 시절, 어머니 곁에서 하늘을 향해 자주 들어 올렸을 그 모습 그대로였다."]], [["To see her with her white hair and her worn face kneeling at my feet gave me a shock through all my frame.", "백발에 초췌한 얼굴을 한 그녀가 내 발치에 무릎 꿇은 것을 보자 온몸에 전율이 일었다."], ["I entreated her to rise, and got my arms about her to help her up; but she only pressed that hand of mine which was nearest to her grasp, and hung her head over it and wept.", "나는 일어나시라고 간청하며 그녀를 일으켜 세우려 두 팔로 감쌌으나, 그녀는 자기 손에 가장 가까이 닿는 내 손을 꼭 쥐고는, 그 위에 고개를 떨군 채 흐느낄 뿐이었다."], ["I had never seen her shed a tear before, and, in the hope that the relief might do her good, I bent over her without speaking.", "나는 그녀가 눈물을 흘리는 것을 그때까지 한 번도 본 적이 없었고, 그렇게 감정을 쏟아 내는 것이 그녀에게 도움이 될지도 모른다는 바람에서, 아무 말 없이 그녀 위로 몸을 굽혔다."], ["She was not kneeling now, but was down upon the ground.", "그녀는 이제 무릎을 꿇은 게 아니라, 바닥에 주저앉아 있었다."]], [["“O!”", "\"오!\""], ["she cried, despairingly.", "그녀가 절망스럽게 부르짖었다."], ["“What have I done!", "\"내가 무슨 짓을 한 거지!"], ["What have I done!”", "내가 무슨 짓을 한 거지!\""]], [["“If you mean, Miss Havisham, what have you done to injure me, let me answer.", "\"미스 해비셤, 부인께서 저를 해친 게 무엇이냐는 뜻이라면, 제가 대답해 드리지요."], ["Very little.", "아주 사소합니다."], ["I should have loved her under any circumstances.", "어떤 상황에서든 저는 그녀를 사랑했을 테니까요."], ["Is she married?”", "그녀가 결혼했습니까?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"그래.\""]], [["It was a needless question, for a new desolation in the desolate house had told me so.", "그것은 굳이 물을 필요도 없는 질문이었다. 황폐한 집 안에 감도는 또 하나의 새로운 황량함이 이미 내게 그렇게 일러 주었으니까."]], [["“What have I done! What have I done!”", "\"내가 무슨 짓을 한 거지! 내가 무슨 짓을 한 거지!\""], ["She wrung her hands, and crushed her white hair, and returned to this cry over and over again. “What have I done!”", "그녀는 두 손을 비틀고 백발을 움켜쥐며, 이 부르짖음으로 거듭거듭 돌아왔다. \"내가 무슨 짓을 한 거지!\""]], [["I knew not how to answer, or how to comfort her. That she had done a grievous thing in taking an impressionable child to mould into the form that her wild resentment, spurned affection, and wounded pride found vengeance in, I knew full well.", "나는 어떻게 대답해야 할지, 어떻게 그녀를 위로해야 할지 알 수 없었다. 감수성이 예민한 아이를 데려다, 그녀의 광적인 원한과 짓밟힌 애정과 상처 입은 자존심이 복수의 길을 찾아낸 그 틀에 맞춰 빚어낸 것이 통탄할 짓이라는 것을 나는 너무도 잘 알았다."], ["But that, in shutting out the light of day, she had shut out infinitely more; that, in seclusion, she had secluded herself from a thousand natural and healing influences; that, her mind, brooding solitary, had grown diseased, as all minds do and must and will that reverse the appointed order of their Maker, I knew equally well.", "그러나 그녀가 햇빛을 차단함으로써 그보다 한없이 더 많은 것을 차단해 버렸다는 것을, 칩거함으로써 천 가지 자연스럽고 치유하는 영향들로부터 스스로를 가두어 버렸다는 것을, 외로이 골똘히 생각에 잠긴 그녀의 마음이 병들어 버렸다는 것을 — 자신을 지으신 분이 정해 놓은 질서를 거스르는 모든 마음이 그러하듯, 그리고 그래야만 하고 또 그러기 마련이듯 — 나는 똑같이 잘 알았다."], ["And could I look upon her without compassion, seeing her punishment in the ruin she was, in her profound unfitness for this earth on which she was placed, in the vanity of sorrow which had become a master mania, like the vanity of penitence, the vanity of remorse, the vanity of unworthiness, and other monstrous vanities that have been curses in this world?", "그러니 내가 어찌 연민 없이 그녀를 바라볼 수 있었겠는가. 그녀가 처한 폐허 속에서, 자신이 놓인 이 땅에 그토록 깊이 어울리지 않는 모습 속에서, 그 형벌을 보면서 말이다. 참회의 헛됨, 회한의 헛됨, 무가치함의 헛됨, 그리고 이 세상의 저주가 되어 온 그 밖의 끔찍한 헛됨들처럼, 하나의 광적인 집념이 되어 버린 슬픔의 헛됨 속에서 말이다."]], [["“Until you spoke to her the other day, and until I saw in you a looking-glass that showed me what I once felt myself, I did not know what I had done.", "\"자네가 며칠 전 그녀에게 말을 거는 걸 보기 전까지, 그리고 한때 내가 직접 느꼈던 것을 비춰 보여 주는 거울을 자네에게서 보기 전까지, 나는 내가 무슨 짓을 했는지 알지 못했네."], ["What have I done!", "내가 무슨 짓을 한 거지!"], ["What have I done!”", "내가 무슨 짓을 한 거지!\""], ["And so again, twenty, fifty times over, What had she done!", "그렇게 그녀는 또다시, 스무 번이고 쉰 번이고, 자신이 무슨 짓을 했느냐고 되뇌었다!"]], [["“Miss Havisham,”", "\"미스 해비셤.\""], ["I said, when her cry had died away, “you may dismiss me from your mind and conscience.", "그녀의 부르짖음이 잦아들었을 때, 내가 말했다. \"부인의 마음과 양심에서 저는 떨쳐 버리셔도 좋습니다."], ["But Estella is a different case, and if you can ever undo any scrap of what you have done amiss in keeping a part of her right nature away from her, it will be better to do that than to bemoan the past through a hundred years.”", "하지만 에스텔라는 다른 경우예요. 만약 부인이 그녀의 올바른 본성의 일부를 그녀에게서 떼어 놓은, 그 그릇되이 저지른 일을 단 한 조각이라도 되돌릴 수 있다면, 그렇게 하는 것이 백 년 동안 과거를 한탄하는 것보다 나을 겁니다.\""]], [["“Yes, yes, I know it.", "\"그래, 그래, 나도 안다네."], ["But, Pip—my dear!”", "하지만, 핍 — 이보게!\""], ["There was an earnest womanly compassion for me in her new affection. “My dear!", "그녀의 새로운 애정 속에는 나를 향한 진심 어린, 여성다운 연민이 깃들어 있었다. \"이보게!"], ["Believe this: when she first came to me, I meant to save her from misery like my own. At first, I meant no more.”", "이것만은 믿어 주게. 그 애가 처음 내게 왔을 때, 나는 그 애를 나 같은 비참함에서 구해 주려 했네."], ["“Well, well!” said I. “I hope so.”", "처음에는 그 이상은 바라지 않았어.\""]], [["“But as she grew, and promised to be very beautiful, I gradually did worse, and with my praises, and with my jewels, and with my teachings, and with this figure of myself always before her, a warning to back and point my lessons, I stole her heart away, and put ice in its place.”", "\"그러셨겠지요, 그러셨겠지요!\" 내가 말했다. \"그러셨기를 바랍니다.\" \"하지만 그 애가 자라면서, 무척 아름다워질 조짐을 보이자, 나는 점점 더 못된 짓을 했네. 칭찬으로, 보석으로, 가르침으로, 그리고 내 교훈을 뒷받침하고 일깨워 주는 경고로서 늘 그 애 앞에 놓여 있던 이 내 꼬락서니로, 나는 그 애의 마음을 훔쳐 내고 그 자리에 얼음을 박아 넣었어.\""]], [["“Better,”", "\"차라리,\""], ["I could not help saying, “to have left her a natural heart, even to be bruised or broken.”", "나는 말하지 않을 수 없었다. \"멍들거나 부서질지언정, 그녀에게 자연스러운 마음을 그대로 남겨 두는 편이 나았을 겁니다.\""]], [["With that, Miss Havisham looked distractedly at me for a while, and then burst out again, What had she done!", "그 말에 미스 해비셤은 한동안 넋이 나간 듯 나를 바라보더니, 다시 터뜨렸다. 자신이 무슨 짓을 했느냐고!"]], [["“If you knew all my story,”", "\"내 이야기를 전부 안다면,\""], ["she pleaded, “you would have some compassion for me and a better understanding of me.”", "그녀가 호소했다. \"자네는 나를 어느 정도 가엾이 여기고, 나를 좀 더 잘 이해하게 될 걸세.\""]], [["“Miss Havisham,”", "\"미스 해비셤.\""], ["I answered, as delicately as I could, “I believe I may say that I do know your story, and have known it ever since I first left this neighbourhood.", "나는 할 수 있는 한 조심스럽게 대답했다. \"저는 부인의 이야기를 정말로 알고 있다고 말씀드릴 수 있을 것 같습니다. 그리고 제가 이 동네를 처음 떠난 이래로 줄곧 알고 있었습니다."], ["It has inspired me with great commiseration, and I hope I understand it and its influences.", "그 이야기는 제게 커다란 연민을 불러일으켰고, 저는 그 이야기와 그것이 미친 영향들을 이해한다고 생각합니다."], ["Does what has passed between us give me any excuse for asking you a question relative to Estella?", "우리 사이에 오간 이야기가, 에스텔라에 관해 제가 부인께 한 가지 여쭐 만한 구실이 될까요?"], ["Not as she is, but as she was when she first came here?”", "지금의 그녀가 아니라, 그녀가 처음 이곳에 왔을 때의 그녀에 관해서요?\""]], [["She was seated on the ground, with her arms on the ragged chair, and her head leaning on them.", "그녀는 다 해진 의자에 두 팔을 얹고, 그 위에 머리를 기댄 채 바닥에 앉아 있었다."], ["She looked full at me when I said this, and replied, “Go on.”", "내가 이렇게 말하자 그녀는 나를 정면으로 바라보며 대답했다. \"계속하게.\""]], [["“Whose child was Estella?”", "\"에스텔라는 누구의 아이였습니까?\""]], [["She shook her head.", "그녀는 고개를 가로저었다."]], [["“You don’t know?”", "\"모르세요?\""]], [["She shook her head again.", "그녀는 다시 고개를 가로저었다."]], [["“But Mr. Jaggers brought her here, or sent her here?”", "\"하지만 재거스 씨가 그녀를 이곳으로 데려왔거나, 이곳으로 보냈겠지요?\""]], [["“Brought her here.”", "\"데려왔지.\""]], [["“Will you tell me how that came about?”", "\"어떻게 그렇게 되었는지 말씀해 주시겠습니까?\""]], [["She answered in a low whisper and with caution: “I had been shut up in these rooms a long time (I don’t know how long; you know what time the clocks keep here), when I told him that I wanted a little girl to rear and love, and save from my fate.", "그녀는 낮은 속삭임으로, 조심스럽게 대답했다. \"나는 오랫동안 이 방들에 갇혀 지내고 있었네(얼마나 오래였는지는 모르겠어. 여기 시계들이 어떤 시간을 가리키는지는 자네도 알 테니)."], ["I had first seen him when I sent for him to lay this place waste for me; having read of him in the newspapers, before I and the world parted.", "그때 나는 그에게, 기르고 사랑하고 내 운명에서 구해 줄 어린 여자아이를 하나 갖고 싶다고 말했지. 그를 처음 본 건, 나를 위해 이곳을 폐허로 만들어 달라고 그를 불렀을 때였네. 나와 세상이 갈라서기 전에, 신문에서 그에 관해 읽었던 거지."], ["He told me that he would look about him for such an orphan child. One night he brought her here asleep, and I called her Estella.”", "그는 그런 고아 아이를 한번 찾아보겠노라고 했어. 어느 날 밤 그가 잠든 그 애를 이곳으로 데려왔고, 나는 그 애를 에스텔라라고 불렀네.\""]], [["“Might I ask her age then?”", "\"그때 그녀가 몇 살이었는지 여쭤도 될까요?\""]], [["“Two or three.", "\"두세 살쯤."], ["She herself knows nothing, but that she was left an orphan and I adopted her.”", "그 애 자신은 아무것도 몰라. 자기가 고아로 남겨졌고 내가 거두었다는 것밖에는.\""]], [["So convinced I was of that woman’s being her mother, that I wanted no evidence to establish the fact in my own mind.", "나는 그 여자가 그녀의 어머니라는 것을 너무도 확신한 나머지, 내 마음속에서 그 사실을 입증할 어떤 증거도 필요하지 않았다."], ["But, to any mind, I thought, the connection here was clear and straight.", "그러나 누구의 마음에라도, 이 둘의 연관은 분명하고 곧바른 것이라고 나는 생각했다."]], [["What more could I hope to do by prolonging the interview?", "대화를 더 길게 끈다고 한들, 내가 더 무엇을 이루기를 바랄 수 있었겠는가?"], ["I had succeeded on behalf of Herbert, Miss Havisham had told me all she knew of Estella, I had said and done what I could to ease her mind.", "나는 허버트를 위한 일에 성공했고, 미스 해비셤은 에스텔라에 관해 자기가 아는 것을 모두 내게 말해 주었으며, 나는 그녀의 마음을 달래 주기 위해 할 수 있는 말과 행동을 다 했다."], ["No matter with what other words we parted; we parted.", "우리가 또 어떤 말로 헤어졌는지는 중요하지 않다. 우리는 헤어졌다."]], [["Twilight was closing in when I went downstairs into the natural air.", "내가 아래층으로 내려가 바깥 공기 속으로 나왔을 때는 땅거미가 내리고 있었다."], ["I called to the woman who had opened the gate when I entered, that I would not trouble her just yet, but would walk round the place before leaving.", "나는 들어올 때 문을 열어 주었던 여자에게, 아직은 수고를 끼치지 않겠지만 떠나기 전에 이곳을 한 바퀴 둘러보겠노라고 일러 두었다."], ["For I had a presentiment that I should never be there again, and I felt that the dying light was suited to my last view of it.", "다시는 이곳에 오지 못하리라는 예감이 들었고, 사그라드는 빛이 이곳을 마지막으로 바라보기에 어울린다고 느꼈기 때문이다."]], [["By the wilderness of casks that I had walked on long ago, and on which the rain of years had fallen since, rotting them in many places, and leaving miniature swamps and pools of water upon those that stood on end, I made my way to the ruined garden.", "오래전에 내가 그 위를 걸었던, 그리고 그 이후로 여러 해 동안 빗물이 떨어져 군데군데 썩어 들고, 세워져 있는 통 위에는 작은 늪과 물웅덩이를 남겨 놓은, 그 어수선하게 널린 술통들 곁을 지나, 나는 폐허가 된 정원으로 걸음을 옮겼다. 나는 정원을 빙 둘러보았다."], ["I went all round it; round by the corner where Herbert and I had fought our battle; round by the paths where Estella and I had walked. So cold, so lonely, so dreary all!", "허버트와 내가 한판 싸움을 벌였던 그 구석을 돌아, 에스텔라와 내가 함께 거닐던 그 오솔길들을 돌아서. 모든 것이 어찌나 차갑고, 어찌나 외롭고, 어찌나 음산하던지!"]], [["Taking the brewery on my way back, I raised the rusty latch of a little door at the garden end of it, and walked through.", "돌아오는 길에 양조장을 거치며, 나는 그 정원 쪽 끝에 난 작은 문의 녹슨 빗장을 들어 올리고 안으로 걸어 들어갔다."], ["I was going out at the opposite door,—not easy to open now, for the damp wood had started and swelled, and the hinges were yielding, and the threshold was encumbered with a growth of fungus,—when I turned my head to look back.", "반대편 문으로 나가려던 참이었는데 — 이제는 열기가 쉽지 않았다. 축축한 나무가 뒤틀려 부풀고, 경첩이 헐거워지고, 문지방에는 곰팡이가 무성하게 자라 가로막고 있었으니까 — 그때 나는 뒤를 돌아보려고 고개를 돌렸다."], ["A childish association revived with wonderful force in the moment of the slight action, and I fancied that I saw Miss Havisham hanging to the beam.", "그 사소한 동작의 순간에 어린 시절의 한 기억이 놀라운 힘으로 되살아났고, 나는 미스 해비셤이 들보에 목매달려 있는 것을 본 것만 같았다."], ["So strong was the impression, that I stood under the beam shuddering from head to foot before I knew it was a fancy,—though to be sure I was there in an instant.", "그 인상이 어찌나 강렬했던지, 나는 그것이 환상임을 깨닫기도 전에 들보 아래 서서 머리끝부터 발끝까지 부들부들 떨었다 — 물론 곧바로 정신을 차리기는 했지만."]], [["The mournfulness of the place and time, and the great terror of this illusion, though it was but momentary, caused me to feel an indescribable awe as I came out between the open wooden gates where I had once wrung my hair after Estella had wrung my heart.", "그곳과 그 시각의 애처로움, 그리고 비록 한순간이었으나 이 환영이 안겨 준 크나큰 공포로 인해, 나는 한때 에스텔라가 내 마음을 쥐어짰던 그 자리에서 내 머리카락을 쥐어뜯었던, 그 활짝 열린 나무문 사이로 나오면서 형언할 수 없는 두려움을 느꼈다."], ["Passing on into the front courtyard, I hesitated whether to call the woman to let me out at the locked gate of which she had the key, or first to go upstairs and assure myself that Miss Havisham was as safe and well as I had left her.", "앞뜰로 걸어 들어가면서, 나는 망설였다. 그 여자가 열쇠를 가지고 있는 잠긴 대문에서 나를 내보내 달라고 그녀를 부를지, 아니면 먼저 위층으로 올라가 미스 해비셤이 내가 두고 온 그대로 안전하고 무사한지 확인할지."], ["I took the latter course and went up.", "나는 후자를 택해 위층으로 올라갔다."]], [["I looked into the room where I had left her, and I saw her seated in the ragged chair upon the hearth close to the fire, with her back towards me.", "나는 그녀를 두고 왔던 방 안을 들여다보았고, 그녀가 난롯불에 바싹 다가앉아 다 해진 의자에 등을 내게로 향한 채 앉아 있는 것을 보았다."], ["In the moment when I was withdrawing my head to go quietly away, I saw a great flaming light spring up.", "조용히 물러가려고 고개를 빼던 그 순간, 나는 커다란 불길이 확 치솟는 것을 보았다."], ["In the same moment I saw her running at me, shrieking, with a whirl of fire blazing all about her, and soaring at least as many feet above her head as she was high.", "같은 순간, 나는 그녀가 비명을 지르며 내게로 달려오는 것을 보았다. 온몸에 휘몰아치는 불길을 두른 채, 적어도 자기 키만큼이나 높이 머리 위로 불꽃을 솟구치며."]], [["I had a double-caped great-coat on, and over my arm another thick coat.", "나는 두 겹 망토가 달린 두꺼운 외투를 입고 있었고, 팔에는 또 다른 두툼한 코트를 걸치고 있었다."], ["That I got them off, closed with her, threw her down, and got them over her; that I dragged the great cloth from the table for the same purpose, and with it dragged down the heap of rottenness in the midst, and all the ugly things that sheltered there; that we were on the ground struggling like desperate enemies, and that the closer I covered her, the more wildly she shrieked and tried to free herself,—that this occurred I knew through the result, but not through anything I felt, or thought, or knew I did.", "내가 그것들을 벗어, 그녀에게 달려들어, 그녀를 쓰러뜨리고, 그것들로 그녀를 뒤덮은 것. 같은 목적으로 식탁에서 그 커다란 천을 끌어내렸고, 그러면서 그 한가운데 쌓여 있던 썩은 더미와 그곳에 깃들어 있던 그 모든 흉측한 것들까지 함께 끌어 내린 것. 우리가 바닥에서 필사적인 원수처럼 몸싸움을 벌였고, 내가 그녀를 바싹 뒤덮을수록 그녀가 더욱 미친 듯이 비명을 지르며 벗어나려 한 것 — 이런 일들이 벌어졌다는 것을 나는 그 결과를 통해 알았지, 내가 느끼거나 생각하거나 내가 무엇을 했는지 안 것을 통해서가 아니었다."], ["I knew nothing until I knew that we were on the floor by the great table, and that patches of tinder yet alight were floating in the smoky air, which, a moment ago, had been her faded bridal dress.", "우리가 그 커다란 식탁 옆 마룻바닥에 있다는 것을, 그리고 한순간 전만 해도 그녀의 빛바랜 신부 드레스였던 것이 아직 타고 있는 불씨 조각들이 되어 연기 자욱한 공기 속을 떠다닌다는 것을 알기 전까지, 나는 아무것도 알지 못했다."]], [["Then, I looked round and saw the disturbed beetles and spiders running away over the floor, and the servants coming in with breathless cries at the door.", "그러고 나서, 나는 둘러보았고, 놀란 딱정벌레들과 거미들이 마룻바닥 위로 달아나는 것을, 그리고 하인들이 숨 가쁜 비명을 지르며 문으로 들어오는 것을 보았다."], ["I still held her forcibly down with all my strength, like a prisoner who might escape; and I doubt if I even knew who she was, or why we had struggled, or that she had been in flames, or that the flames were out, until I saw the patches of tinder that had been her garments no longer alight but falling in a black shower around us.", "나는 여전히, 달아날지도 모르는 죄수를 붙들 듯, 온 힘을 다해 그녀를 억지로 짓누르고 있었다. 그리고 나는 그녀가 누구인지, 우리가 왜 몸싸움을 벌였는지, 그녀가 불길에 휩싸였었는지, 또 그 불길이 꺼졌는지조차 알았을까 싶다. 그녀의 옷이었던 그 불씨 조각들이 더는 타지 않고 우리 주위로 검은 소나기처럼 떨어지는 것을 볼 때까지는 말이다."]], [["She was insensible, and I was afraid to have her moved, or even touched.", "그녀는 의식이 없었고, 나는 그녀를 옮기거나, 심지어 건드리기조차 두려웠다."], ["Assistance was sent for, and I held her until it came, as if I unreasonably fancied (I think I did) that, if I let her go, the fire would break out again and consume her.", "도움을 청해 사람을 보냈고, 나는 도움이 올 때까지 그녀를 붙들고 있었다. 마치, 만약 그녀를 놓아 버리면 불이 다시 일어나 그녀를 삼켜 버리기라도 할 것처럼 터무니없이 상상했던 것처럼(실제로 그랬던 것 같다)."], ["When I got up, on the surgeon’s coming to her with other aid, I was astonished to see that both my hands were burnt; for, I had no knowledge of it through the sense of feeling.", "외과 의사가 다른 구조 인력과 함께 그녀에게 다가왔을 때 내가 일어서고 보니, 놀랍게도 내 두 손이 모두 화상을 입어 있었다. 촉각으로는 그것을 전혀 알아차리지 못했던 것이다."]], [["On examination it was pronounced that she had received serious hurts, but that they of themselves were far from hopeless; the danger lay mainly in the nervous shock.", "진찰 결과, 그녀가 심각한 부상을 입었으나, 그 부상 자체만으로는 결코 가망 없는 것이 아니라는 진단이 내려졌다. 위험은 주로 신경성 충격에 있었다."], ["By the surgeon’s directions, her bed was carried into that room and laid upon the great table, which happened to be well suited to the dressing of her injuries.", "외과 의사의 지시에 따라, 그녀의 침대를 그 방으로 옮겨 와 커다란 식탁 위에 얹었는데, 마침 그 식탁이 그녀의 상처를 처치하기에 안성맞춤이었다."], ["When I saw her again, an hour afterwards, she lay, indeed, where I had seen her strike her stick, and had heard her say that she would lie one day.", "한 시간 후 그녀를 다시 보았을 때, 그녀는 정말로, 일찍이 내가 그녀가 지팡이로 내려치는 것을 보았던 그 자리에, 언젠가 자기가 누워 있게 될 거라고 말하는 것을 들었던 바로 그 자리에 누워 있었다."]], [["Though every vestige of her dress was burnt, as they told me, she still had something of her old ghastly bridal appearance; for, they had covered her to the throat with white cotton-wool, and as she lay with a white sheet loosely overlying that, the phantom air of something that had been and was changed was still upon her.", "사람들 말로는 그녀의 옷은 흔적도 없이 다 타 버렸다는데도, 그녀에게는 여전히 그 옛날의 섬뜩한 신부 같은 모습이 어느 정도 남아 있었다. 사람들이 그녀를 목까지 흰 솜으로 덮어 놓았고, 그 위에 흰 시트를 헐겁게 덮어씌운 채 누워 있었으므로, 한때 존재했다가 변해 버린 어떤 것의 유령 같은 기운이 여전히 그녀에게 감돌고 있었던 것이다."]], [["I found, on questioning the servants, that Estella was in Paris, and I got a promise from the surgeon that he would write to her by the next post.", "하인들에게 물어, 나는 에스텔라가 파리에 있다는 것을 알아냈고, 외과 의사로부터 다음 편 우편으로 그녀에게 편지를 써 보내겠다는 약속을 받아 냈다."], ["Miss Havisham’s family I took upon myself; intending to communicate with Mr. Matthew Pocket only, and leave him to do as he liked about informing the rest.", "미스 해비셤의 가족 문제는 내가 떠맡았다. 매슈 포켓 씨에게만 연락하고, 나머지 사람들에게 알리는 일은 그가 알아서 하도록 맡겨 둘 작정이었다."], ["This I did next day, through Herbert, as soon as I returned to town.", "이 일을 나는 이튿날, 읍내로 돌아오자마자 허버트를 통해 처리했다."]], [["There was a stage, that evening, when she spoke collectedly of what had happened, though with a certain terrible vivacity.", "그날 저녁, 그녀가 일어난 일에 대해 차분하게, 그러나 어딘가 무시무시한 생기를 띠고 이야기하던 한때가 있었다."], ["Towards midnight she began to wander in her speech; and after that it gradually set in that she said innumerable times in a low solemn voice, “What have I done!”", "자정 무렵부터 그녀는 헛소리를 하기 시작했다. 그 후로는 차츰, 낮고 엄숙한 목소리로 \"내가 무슨 짓을 한 거지!\" 하고 수없이 되뇌는 것이 굳어졌다."], ["And then, “When she first came, I meant to save her from misery like mine.”", "그러고는 \"그 애가 처음 왔을 때, 나는 그 애를 나 같은 비참함에서 구해 주려 했어.\""], ["And then, “Take the pencil and write under my name, ‘I forgive her!’”", "그러고는 \"연필을 들고 내 이름 아래에 써 주게, '나는 그녀를 용서한다!'라고.\""], ["She never changed the order of these three sentences, but she sometimes left out a word in one or other of them; never putting in another word, but always leaving a blank and going on to the next word.", "그녀는 이 세 문장의 순서를 결코 바꾸지 않았으나, 가끔 그중 하나에서 한 단어를 빠뜨리곤 했다. 다른 단어를 끼워 넣는 일은 결코 없이, 늘 빈자리를 남겨 둔 채 다음 단어로 넘어갔다."]], [["As I could do no service there, and as I had, nearer home, that pressing reason for anxiety and fear which even her wanderings could not drive out of my mind, I decided, in the course of the night that I would return by the early morning coach, walking on a mile or so, and being taken up clear of the town.", "그곳에서 내가 해 줄 수 있는 일이 없었고, 또 집과 더 가까운 곳에, 그녀의 헛소리조차 내 마음에서 몰아낼 수 없었던 그 절박한 불안과 두려움의 까닭이 있었으므로, 나는 그날 밤 사이에 마음을 정했다. 이른 아침 역마차로 돌아가되, 일 마일쯤 걸어 나가 읍내를 벗어난 곳에서 마차를 타기로."], ["At about six o’clock of the morning, therefore, I leaned over her and touched her lips with mine, just as they said, not stopping for being touched, “Take the pencil and write under my name, ‘I forgive her.’”", "그리하여 아침 여섯 시쯤, 나는 그녀 위로 몸을 굽혀 내 입술을 그녀의 입술에 댔다. 마침 그녀가, 입맞춤을 받는 일 따위로 멈추지 않은 채, \"연필을 들고 내 이름 아래에 써 주게, '나는 그녀를 용서한다'라고.\" 하고 말하던 바로 그때였다."]], [["# Chapter L.", "# Chapter L."]], [["My hands had been dressed twice or thrice in the night, and again in the morning.", "간밤에 두세 번, 그리고 아침에 다시 한 번 내 손은 새로 처치를 받았다."], ["My left arm was a good deal burned to the elbow, and, less severely, as high as the shoulder; it was very painful, but the flames had set in that direction, and I felt thankful it was no worse.", "왼팔은 팔꿈치까지 심하게 데었고, 어깨께까지는 그보다 덜한 화상을 입었다. 몹시 아팠으나 불길이 그쪽으로 번졌으니, 나는 이만하기를 다행으로 여겼다."], ["My right hand was not so badly burnt but that I could move the fingers.", "오른손은 손가락을 움직일 수 있을 만큼 덜 데었다."], ["It was bandaged, of course, but much less inconveniently than my left hand and arm; those I carried in a sling; and I could only wear my coat like a cloak, loose over my shoulders and fastened at the neck.", "물론 붕대를 감았으나, 왼손과 왼팔에 비하면 훨씬 덜 불편했다. 그 둘은 삼각건에 매달아 두었다. 그리고 나는 외투를 망토처럼, 어깨에 헐겁게 걸치고 목께에서 여미는 식으로밖에 걸칠 수가 없었다."], ["My hair had been caught by the fire, but not my head or face.", "불에 머리카락이 그슬리긴 했으나, 머리나 얼굴은 무사했다."]], [["When Herbert had been down to Hammersmith and seen his father, he came back to me at our chambers, and devoted the day to attending on me.", "허버트는 해머스미스에 내려가 자기 아버지를 뵙고 온 뒤, 우리 하숙으로 돌아와 그날 하루를 나를 돌보는 데 바쳤다."], ["He was the kindest of nurses, and at stated times took off the bandages, and steeped them in the cooling liquid that was kept ready, and put them on again, with a patient tenderness that I was deeply grateful for.", "그는 더없이 다정한 간병인이었다. 정해진 시간마다 붕대를 풀어, 미리 준비해 둔 차가운 약물에 적셨다가 다시 감아 주었는데, 그 참을성 있는 부드러움에 나는 깊이 감사했다."]], [["At first, as I lay quiet on the sofa, I found it painfully difficult, I might say impossible, to get rid of the impression of the glare of the flames, their hurry and noise, and the fierce burning smell.", "처음에 소파에 가만히 누워 있을 때, 나는 불길의 번쩍임과 그 황급함과 소음, 그리고 사납게 타오르던 냄새의 인상을 떨쳐 내기가 고통스러우리만치 어려웠다. 아니 불가능했다고 해도 좋을 것이다."], ["If I dozed for a minute, I was awakened by Miss Havisham’s cries, and by her running at me with all that height of fire above her head.", "잠깐이라도 졸면, 미스 해비셤의 비명에, 그리고 머리 위로 그 높다란 불기둥을 인 채 내게로 달려드는 그녀의 모습에 잠을 깼다."], ["This pain of the mind was much harder to strive against than any bodily pain I suffered; and Herbert, seeing that, did his utmost to hold my attention engaged.", "이 마음의 고통은 내가 겪은 그 어떤 육체의 고통보다 맞서 싸우기가 훨씬 힘들었다. 그것을 알아챈 허버트는 내 주의를 딴 데로 붙들어 두려고 온갖 애를 썼다."]], [["Neither of us spoke of the boat, but we both thought of it.", "우리 둘 다 그 배 이야기는 입에 올리지 않았으나, 둘 다 그것을 생각하고 있었다."], ["That was made apparent by our avoidance of the subject, and by our agreeing—without agreement—to make my recovery of the use of my hands a question of so many hours, not of so many weeks.", "그 사실은 우리가 그 화제를 피한다는 점에서, 그리고 내 손이 다시 쓸 수 있게 되는 것을 몇 주가 아니라 몇 시간의 문제로 삼자고 — 무언의 — 합의를 보았다는 점에서 분명히 드러났다."]], [["My first question when I saw Herbert had been of course, whether all was well down the river?", "허버트를 보았을 때 내가 던진 첫 질문은 물론 강 아래쪽이 다 무사한가 하는 것이었다."], ["As he replied in the affirmative, with perfect confidence and cheerfulness, we did not resume the subject until the day was wearing away.", "그가 더없는 확신과 명랑함으로 그렇다고 답했으므로, 우리는 하루가 저물어 갈 때까지 그 화제를 다시 꺼내지 않았다."], ["But then, as Herbert changed the bandages, more by the light of the fire than by the outer light, he went back to it spontaneously.", "그러나 그때, 바깥 빛보다는 난롯불 빛에 기대어 붕대를 갈던 허버트가 스스로 그 이야기로 돌아갔다."]], [["“I sat with Provis last night, Handel, two good hours.”", "\"어젯밤에 프로비스랑 같이 앉아 있었어, 핸들, 꼬박 두 시간을 말이야.\""]], [["“Where was Clara?”", "\"클라라는 어디 있었고?\""]], [["“Dear little thing!”", "\"가엾고 사랑스러운 사람!\""], ["said Herbert.", "허버트가 말했다."], ["“She was up and down with Gruffandgrim all the evening.", "\"저녁 내내 그루팬드그림 영감 곁을 오르락내리락했지."], ["He was perpetually pegging at the floor the moment she left his sight.", "그 양반은 클라라가 눈앞에서 사라지기만 하면 줄곧 바닥을 쿵쿵 두드려 댔어."], ["I doubt if he can hold out long, though.", "하지만 영감이 오래 버틸 것 같진 않아."], ["What with rum and pepper,—and pepper and rum,—I should think his pegging must be nearly over.”", "럼주에 후추를 — 후추에 럼주를 곁들여 대니, 그 쿵쿵거림도 머지않아 끝날 것 같단 말이지.\""]], [["“And then you will be married, Herbert?”", "\"그럼 그때 자네는 결혼하게 되는 건가, 허버트?\""]], [["“How can I take care of the dear child otherwise?—Lay your arm out upon the back of the sofa, my dear boy, and I’ll sit down here, and get the bandage off so gradually that you shall not know when it comes.", "\"안 그러고서야 그 사랑스러운 사람을 어떻게 보살피겠나? — 팔을 소파 등받이에 얹어 봐, 친구. 나는 여기 앉아서, 언제 떨어지는지도 모를 만큼 천천히 붕대를 풀어 줄 테니."], ["I was speaking of Provis.", "프로비스 얘기를 하던 참이지."], ["Do you know, Handel, he improves?”", "그런데 말이야, 핸들, 그 사람이 점점 나아지고 있어.\""]], [["“I said to you I thought he was softened when I last saw him.”", "\"지난번에 마지막으로 봤을 때 그분이 한결 부드러워졌더라고 자네한테 말했잖나.\""]], [["“So you did.", "\"그랬지."], ["And so he is.", "정말 그래."], ["He was very communicative last night, and told me more of his life.", "어젯밤엔 무척 말이 많아져서, 자기 살아온 이야기를 더 들려주더군."], ["You remember his breaking off here about some woman that he had had great trouble with.—Did I hurt you?”", "전에 여기서 어떤 여자 얘기를 하다가, 그 여자 때문에 엄청 고생했다면서 말을 끊었던 거 기억나? — 아팠어?\""]], [["I had started, but not under his touch.", "나는 움찔했으나, 그의 손길 때문은 아니었다."], ["His words had given me a start.", "그의 말이 나를 흠칫하게 했던 것이다."]], [["“I had forgotten that, Herbert, but I remember it now you speak of it.”", "\"그건 잊고 있었는데, 자네가 말하니까 이제 기억이 나는군, 허버트.\""]], [["“Well!", "\"그래!"], ["He went into that part of his life, and a dark wild part it is. Shall I tell you?", "그 사람이 자기 인생의 그 부분으로 들어갔는데, 어둡고 험한 대목이야. 들려줄까?"], ["Or would it worry you just now?”", "아니면 지금은 자네한테 부담이 될까?\""]], [["“Tell me by all means. Every word.”", "\"꼭 들려주게. 한마디도 빼지 말고.\""]], [["Herbert bent forward to look at me more nearly, as if my reply had been rather more hurried or more eager than he could quite account for.", "허버트는 내 대답이 그가 헤아릴 수 있는 것보다 다소 다급하거나 간절했다는 듯, 몸을 앞으로 숙여 나를 더 가까이 들여다보았다."], ["“Your head is cool?”", "\"머리는 시원해?\""], ["he said, touching it.", "그가 내 머리를 만지며 말했다."]], [["“Quite,” said I.", "\"아주 시원해.\""], ["“Tell me what Provis said, my dear Herbert.”", "내가 말했다. \"프로비스가 뭐라고 했는지 말해 주게, 허버트.\""]], [["“It seems,”", "\"그게 말이야,\""], ["said Herbert, “—there’s a bandage off most charmingly, and now comes the cool one,—makes you shrink at first, my poor dear fellow, don’t it?", "허버트가 말했다. \"— 붕대 하나가 아주 멋지게 풀렸군, 이제 차가운 걸 댈 거야 — 처음엔 움츠러들게 되지, 가엾은 친구, 안 그래?"], ["but it will be comfortable presently,—it seems that the woman was a young woman, and a jealous woman, and a revengeful woman; revengeful, Handel, to the last degree.”", "하지만 곧 편안해질 거야 — 그게 말이지, 그 여자는 젊은 여자였고, 질투심이 강한 여자였고, 복수심에 불타는 여자였어. 복수심이라면, 핸들, 더할 나위 없는 정도였지.\""]], [["“To what last degree?”", "\"더할 나위 없다니, 어느 정도였는데?\""]], [["“Murder.—Does it strike too cold on that sensitive place?”", "\"살인까지. — 그 예민한 데가 너무 차갑게 와닿진 않아?\""]], [["“I don’t feel it.", "\"느껴지지 않아."], ["How did she murder?", "어떻게 죽였대?"], ["Whom did she murder?”", "누구를 죽였대?\""]], [["“Why, the deed may not have merited quite so terrible a name,”", "\"뭐, 그 짓이 그렇게 끔찍한 이름값까진 못 했을지도 몰라.\" 허버트가 말했다."], ["said Herbert, “but, she was tried for it, and Mr. Jaggers defended her, and the reputation of that defence first made his name known to Provis.", "\"하지만 그 일로 재판을 받았고, 재거스 씨가 그 여자를 변호했지. 그 변호로 얻은 명성 덕분에 프로비스가 처음 그 양반 이름을 알게 된 거야."], ["It was another and a stronger woman who was the victim, and there had been a struggle—in a barn.", "희생자는 그 여자보다 더 힘센 다른 여자였는데, 둘 사이에 — 헛간에서 — 몸싸움이 있었어."], ["Who began it, or how fair it was, or how unfair, may be doubtful; but how it ended is certainly not doubtful, for the victim was found throttled.”", "누가 먼저 시작했는지, 얼마나 정당했는지 부당했는지는 알 수 없을지 몰라도, 어떻게 끝났는지는 분명히 의심할 여지가 없어. 희생자가 목이 졸려 죽은 채로 발견됐으니까.\""]], [["“Was the woman brought in guilty?”", "\"그 여자는 유죄 판결을 받았고?\""]], [["“No; she was acquitted.—My poor Handel, I hurt you!”", "\"아니, 무죄로 풀려났어. — 가엾은 핸들, 아프게 했군!\""]], [["“It is impossible to be gentler, Herbert. Yes?", "\"이보다 더 부드러울 순 없네, 허버트. 그래서?"], ["What else?”", "또 뭐가 있나?\""]], [["“This acquitted young woman and Provis had a little child; a little child of whom Provis was exceedingly fond.", "\"무죄로 풀려난 이 젊은 여자와 프로비스 사이엔 어린아이가 하나 있었어. 프로비스가 무척이나 아끼던 어린아이였지."], ["On the evening of the very night when the object of her jealousy was strangled as I tell you, the young woman presented herself before Provis for one moment, and swore that she would destroy the child (which was in her possession), and he should never see it again; then she vanished.—There’s the worst arm comfortably in the sling once more, and now there remains but the right hand, which is a far easier job.", "내가 말한 대로 그 질투의 대상이 목 졸려 죽은 바로 그날 밤, 그 젊은 여자가 잠깐 프로비스 앞에 나타나서는, 그 아이를(아이는 자기가 데리고 있었어) 없애 버리겠다고, 다시는 못 보게 하겠다고 맹세하고는 사라져 버렸어. — 자, 제일 안 좋은 팔이 다시 삼각건에 편안히 들어갔고, 이제 오른손만 남았는데, 이건 훨씬 쉬운 일이지."], ["I can do it better by this light than by a stronger, for my hand is steadiest when I don’t see the poor blistered patches too distinctly.—You don’t think your breathing is affected, my dear boy?", "이 빛으로 하는 게 더 밝은 빛으로 하는 것보다 나아. 가엾게 물집 잡힌 자리가 너무 또렷이 보이지 않을 때 손이 제일 안 떨리거든. — 숨쉬기가 불편한 건 아니지, 친구?"], ["You seem to breathe quickly.”", "호흡이 가빠 보이는데.\""]], [["“Perhaps I do, Herbert.", "\"아마 그런가 봐, 허버트."], ["Did the woman keep her oath?”", "그 여자는 맹세를 지켰어?\""]], [["“There comes the darkest part of Provis’s life. She did.”", "\"여기서부터가 프로비스 인생의 가장 어두운 대목이야. 지켰어.\""]], [["“That is, he says she did.”", "\"그러니까, 그 사람이 지켰다고 말하는 거겠지.\""]], [["“Why, of course, my dear boy,”", "\"아니, 당연하지, 친구.\""], ["returned Herbert, in a tone of surprise, and again bending forward to get a nearer look at me. “He says it all.", "허버트가 놀란 어조로 대답하며, 다시 몸을 앞으로 숙여 나를 가까이 들여다보았다. \"그 사람이 전부 말한 거야."], ["I have no other information.”", "내겐 다른 정보가 없어.\""]], [["“No, to be sure.”", "\"그렇지, 그야 당연하지.\""]], [["“Now, whether,” pursued Herbert, “he had used the child’s mother ill, or whether he had used the child’s mother well, Provis doesn’t say; but she had shared some four or five years of the wretched life he described to us at this fireside, and he seems to have felt pity for her, and forbearance towards her.", "\"자, 그가 그 아이 어미를 나쁘게 대했는지, 아니면 잘 대했는지는,\" 허버트가 말을 이었다."], ["Therefore, fearing he should be called upon to depose about this destroyed child, and so be the cause of her death, he hid himself (much as he grieved for the child), kept himself dark, as he says, out of the way and out of the trial, and was only vaguely talked of as a certain man called Abel, out of whom the jealousy arose.", "\"프로비스도 말하지 않아. 하지만 그 여자는 그가 이 난롯가에서 우리한테 들려준 그 비참한 삶을 사오 년쯤 함께했고, 그는 그 여자한테 연민을 느꼈고, 너그럽게 굴었던 모양이야. 그래서 이 죽임당한 아이에 대해 증언하라는 요구를 받게 될까 봐, 그리하여 그 여자의 죽음에 빌미를 줄까 봐, 그는 (아이를 그토록 애통해하면서도) 몸을 숨기고, 그의 표현대로 어둠 속에 자취를 감추어, 사건에서도 재판에서도 비켜나 있었어."], ["After the acquittal she disappeared, and thus he lost the child and the child’s mother.”", "그저 질투가 비롯된 발단이 된 에이블이라는 어떤 사내로 막연히 입에 오를 뿐이었지. 무죄 판결 뒤에 그 여자는 사라졌고, 그렇게 그는 아이도 아이 어미도 잃어버렸어.\""]], [["“I want to ask—”", "\"하나 묻고 싶은데 —\""]], [["“A moment, my dear boy, and I have done.", "\"잠깐만, 친구, 거의 다 됐어."], ["That evil genius, Compeyson, the worst of scoundrels among many scoundrels, knowing of his keeping out of the way at that time and of his reasons for doing so, of course afterwards held the knowledge over his head as a means of keeping him poorer and working him harder.", "그 악령 같은 콤피슨, 숱한 악당들 가운데서도 가장 못된 그 악당이, 그 시절 프로비스가 자취를 감추고 있었다는 것과 그렇게 한 까닭을 알고서, 물론 나중에 그 약점을 빌미 삼아 그를 더 가난하게 옭아매고 더 혹독하게 부려 먹었던 거야. 어젯밤에도 분명히 드러나더군."], ["It was clear last night that this barbed the point of Provis’s animosity.”", "바로 이 점이 프로비스의 적개심에 가시 돋친 날을 세워 주었다는 게.\""]], [["“I want to know,”", "\"내가 알고 싶은 건,\""], ["said I, “and particularly, Herbert, whether he told you when this happened?”", "내가 말했다. \"특히, 허버트, 그 사람이 이 일이 언제 일어났는지 말하던가?\""]], [["“Particularly?", "\"특히 그거 말이지?"], ["Let me remember, then, what he said as to that.", "그럼 그 점에 대해 그가 뭐라 했는지 기억을 더듬어 보지."], ["His expression was, ‘a round score o’ year ago, and a’most directly after I took up wi’ Compeyson.’ How old were you when you came upon him in the little churchyard?”", "그 사람 표현이, '한 이십 년쯤 전에, 콤피슨이란 놈하고 어울리기 시작한 거의 바로 그 직후에' 그랬어. 그 작은 교회 묘지에서 그 사람을 마주쳤을 때 자네 몇 살이었지?\""]], [["“I think in my seventh year.”", "\"일곱 살 무렵이었던 것 같아.\""]], [["“Ay.", "\"그래."], ["It had happened some three or four years then, he said, and you brought into his mind the little girl so tragically lost, who would have been about your age.”", "그러면 그땐 그 일이 일어난 지 삼사 년쯤 됐다고 그러더군. 그리고 자네를 보니, 그렇게 비극적으로 잃어버린 어린 딸이 떠올랐다는 거야. 그 아이가 살아 있었다면 자네 또래쯤 됐을 거라면서.\""]], [["“Herbert,”", "\"허버트,\""], ["said I, after a short silence, in a hurried way, “can you see me best by the light of the window, or the light of the fire?”", "잠시 침묵한 뒤 나는 다급한 투로 말했다. \"내가 창문 빛으로 더 잘 보여, 아니면 난롯불 빛으로 더 잘 보여?\""]], [["“By the firelight,”", "\"난롯불 빛으로 잘 보여.\""], ["answered Herbert, coming close again.", "허버트가 다시 가까이 다가오며 대답했다."]], [["“Look at me.”", "\"나를 봐.\""]], [["“I do look at you, my dear boy.”", "\"보고 있어, 친구.\""]], [["“Touch me.”", "\"나를 만져 봐.\""]], [["“I do touch you, my dear boy.”", "\"만지고 있어, 친구.\""]], [["“You are not afraid that I am in any fever, or that my head is much disordered by the accident of last night?”", "\"내가 무슨 열에 들떠 있다거나, 어젯밤 사고로 머리가 많이 어지러워졌다고 걱정하는 건 아니지?\""]], [["“N-no, my dear boy,”", "\"아-아니, 친구.\""], ["said Herbert, after taking time to examine me.", "허버트가 나를 한참 살펴본 뒤에 말했다."], ["“You are rather excited, but you are quite yourself.”", "\"좀 흥분하긴 했지만, 자네는 멀쩡해.\""]], [["“I know I am quite myself.", "\"내가 멀쩡하다는 건 나도 알아."], ["And the man we have in hiding down the river, is Estella’s Father.”", "그리고 우리가 강 아래쪽에 숨겨 둔 그 사람이, 에스텔라의 아버지야.\""]], [["# Chapter LI.", "# Chapter LI."]], [["What purpose I had in view when I was hot on tracing out and proving Estella’s parentage, I cannot say.", "에스텔라의 혈통을 캐내고 입증하는 일에 열을 올렸을 때 내가 어떤 목적을 마음에 품고 있었는지, 나는 말할 수 없다."], ["It will presently be seen that the question was not before me in a distinct shape until it was put before me by a wiser head than my own.", "그 문제가 나보다 더 현명한 사람의 입을 통해 내 앞에 던져지기 전까지는, 내 머릿속에서 또렷한 형태를 갖추지 못했음이 곧 드러날 것이다."]], [["But when Herbert and I had held our momentous conversation, I was seized with a feverish conviction that I ought to hunt the matter down,—that I ought not to let it rest, but that I ought to see Mr. Jaggers, and come at the bare truth.", "그러나 허버트와 그 중대한 대화를 나누고 나자, 나는 이 일을 끝까지 추적해야 한다는, 그대로 내버려 두어서는 안 되며 재거스 씨를 만나 적나라한 진실에 다다라야 한다는 열병 같은 확신에 사로잡혔다."], ["I really do not know whether I felt that I did this for Estella’s sake, or whether I was glad to transfer to the man in whose preservation I was so much concerned some rays of the romantic interest that had so long surrounded me.", "내가 정말로 이것을 에스텔라를 위해 한다고 느꼈는지, 아니면 그토록 오래 나를 둘러싸고 있던 낭만적 흥미의 몇 가닥 빛줄기를, 그 안위를 내가 그토록 걱정하던 사내에게로 옮겨 놓는 것이 기뻤던 것인지, 나는 정말 알 수가 없다."], ["Perhaps the latter possibility may be the nearer to the truth.", "어쩌면 후자의 가능성이 진실에 더 가까울지도 모른다."]], [["Any way, I could scarcely be withheld from going out to Gerrard Street that night.", "어쨌든 그날 밤 나는 제라드 가로 달려 나가는 것을 좀처럼 자제할 수가 없었다."], ["Herbert’s representations that, if I did, I should probably be laid up and stricken useless, when our fugitive’s safety would depend upon me, alone restrained my impatience.", "만약 그렇게 한다면 십중팔구 몸져누워 아무짝에도 쓸모없게 될 텐데, 그렇게 되면 우리 도망자의 안위가 나에게 달려 있는 마당에 큰일이라는 허버트의 설득만이 내 조바심을 붙들어 매 주었다."], ["On the understanding, again and again reiterated, that, come what would, I was to go to Mr. Jaggers to-morrow, I at length submitted to keep quiet, and to have my hurts looked after, and to stay at home.", "무슨 일이 있어도 내일은 재거스 씨에게 가겠다는 것을 거듭거듭 다짐한 끝에, 나는 마침내 가만히 있기로, 상처를 돌보게 하기로, 집에 머물기로 굴복했다."], ["Early next morning we went out together, and at the corner of Giltspur Street by Smithfield, I left Herbert to go his way into the City, and took my way to Little Britain.", "이튿날 아침 일찍 우리는 함께 나섰고, 스미스필드 옆 길트스퍼 가 모퉁이에서 나는 허버트를 시티 쪽으로 가는 그의 길로 보내고, 나는 리틀브리튼으로 향했다."]], [["There were periodical occasions when Mr. Jaggers and Wemmick went over the office accounts, and checked off the vouchers, and put all things straight.", "재거스 씨와 웨믹이 사무소 장부를 검토하고, 영수증을 대조하고, 모든 것을 정리하는 정기적인 때가 있었다."], ["On these occasions, Wemmick took his books and papers into Mr. Jaggers’s room, and one of the upstairs clerks came down into the outer office.", "그런 때면 웨믹은 자기 장부와 서류를 들고 재거스 씨의 방으로 들어갔고, 위층 사무원 가운데 하나가 바깥 사무실로 내려왔다."], ["Finding such clerk on Wemmick’s post that morning, I knew what was going on; but I was not sorry to have Mr.", "그날 아침 웨믹의 자리에 그런 사무원이 있는 것을 보고 나는 무슨 일이 벌어지고 있는지 알았다. 그러나 재거스 씨와 웨믹이 함께 있는 것이 나로서는 싫지 않았다."], ["Jaggers and Wemmick together, as Wemmick would then hear for himself that I said nothing to compromise him.", "그러면 웨믹이 내가 그를 곤란하게 할 말을 한마디도 하지 않는다는 것을 직접 들을 수 있을 테니까."]], [["My appearance, with my arm bandaged and my coat loose over my shoulders, favoured my object.", "팔에 붕대를 감고 외투를 어깨에 헐겁게 걸친 내 모습은 내 목적에 도움이 되었다."], ["Although I had sent Mr. Jaggers a brief account of the accident as soon as I had arrived in town, yet I had to give him all the details now; and the speciality of the occasion caused our talk to be less dry and hard, and less strictly regulated by the rules of evidence, than it had been before.", "도시에 도착하자마자 재거스 씨에게 사고에 관한 간략한 보고를 보내 두긴 했으나, 이제는 그에게 모든 세부 사항을 들려주어야 했다. 그리고 이 상황의 특수성 덕분에 우리의 대화는 전보다 덜 메마르고 덜 딱딱했으며, 증거의 규칙에 덜 엄격하게 매여 있었다."], ["While I described the disaster, Mr. Jaggers stood, according to his wont, before the fire.", "내가 그 재난을 묘사하는 동안 재거스 씨는 평소 버릇대로 난로 앞에 서 있었다."], ["Wemmick leaned back in his chair, staring at me, with his hands in the pockets of his trousers, and his pen put horizontally into the post.", "웨믹은 의자에 등을 기댄 채, 두 손을 바지 주머니에 넣고, 펜을 우편함 꽂이에 가로질러 꽂아 둔 채 나를 응시했다."], ["The two brutal casts, always inseparable in my mind from the official proceedings, seemed to be congestively considering whether they didn’t smell fire at the present moment.", "내 마음속에서 그 공무적 절차와 떼려야 뗄 수 없는 그 야만스러운 두 개의 석고상은, 지금 이 순간 불 냄새가 나는 것은 아닌지 충혈된 얼굴로 곰곰이 따져 보는 듯했다."]], [["My narrative finished, and their questions exhausted, I then produced Miss Havisham’s authority to receive the nine hundred pounds for Herbert. Mr.", "내 이야기가 끝나고 그들의 질문도 동나자, 나는 허버트를 위해 구백 파운드를 수령할 수 있다는 미스 해비셤의 위임장을 내놓았다."], ["Jaggers’s eyes retired a little deeper into his head when I handed him the tablets, but he presently handed them over to Wemmick, with instructions to draw the check for his signature.", "내가 그 수첩을 건네자 재거스 씨의 두 눈은 머릿속으로 조금 더 깊이 물러났으나, 그는 이내 그것을 웨믹에게 넘기며 자기 서명을 받아 수표를 끊으라고 지시했다."], ["While that was in course of being done, I looked on at Wemmick as he wrote, and Mr. Jaggers, poising and swaying himself on his well-polished boots, looked on at me.", "그 일이 진행되는 동안 나는 글씨를 쓰는 웨믹을 지켜보았고, 재거스 씨는 잘 닦은 장화 위에서 몸을 가누며 좌우로 흔들면서 나를 지켜보았다. \"유감이군, 핍.\""], ["“I am sorry, Pip,” said he, as I put the check in my pocket, when he had signed it, “that we do nothing for you.”", "그가 서명한 수표를 내가 주머니에 넣자 그가 말했다. \"우리가 자네를 위해서는 아무것도 해 주는 게 없으니 말이야.\""]], [["“Miss Havisham was good enough to ask me,” I returned, “whether she could do nothing for me, and I told her No.”", "\"미스 해비셤께서 친절하게도,\" 내가 대답했다, \"저를 위해 뭔가 해 줄 일이 없겠느냐고 물으셨는데, 저는 없다고 말씀드렸습니다.\""]], [["“Everybody should know his own business,”", "\"누구나 제 일은 제가 알아야 하는 법이지.\""], ["said Mr. Jaggers.", "재거스 씨가 말했다."], ["And I saw Wemmick’s lips form the words “portable property.”", "그리고 나는 웨믹의 입술이 \"동산\"이라는 말을 빚어내는 것을 보았다."]], [["“I should not have told her No, if I had been you,”", "\"내가 자네였다면 없다고는 말하지 않았을 거야.\""], ["said Mr Jaggers; “but every man ought to know his own business best.”", "재거스 씨가 말했다. \"하지만 사람은 누구나 제 일을 제일 잘 아는 법이니.\""]], [["“Every man’s business,”", "\"사람의 일이란,\""], ["said Wemmick, rather reproachfully towards me, “is portable property.”", "웨믹이 나를 향해 다소 나무라는 투로 말했다, \"곧 동산이지요.\""]], [["As I thought the time was now come for pursuing the theme I had at heart, I said, turning on Mr. Jaggers:—", "이제야말로 내 마음에 품고 있던 주제를 좇을 때가 왔다고 여긴 나는, 재거스 씨를 향해 돌아서며 말했다."]], [["“I did ask something of Miss Havisham, however, sir.", "\"그렇지만 저는 미스 해비셤께 무언가를 청하긴 했습니다, 선생님."], ["I asked her to give me some information relative to her adopted daughter, and she gave me all she possessed.”", "그분의 양녀에 관해 어떤 정보를 좀 알려 달라고 청했고, 그분은 자신이 가진 것을 모두 제게 주셨습니다.\""]], [["“Did she?”", "\"그랬나?\""], ["said Mr. Jaggers, bending forward to look at his boots and then straightening himself. “Hah!", "재거스 씨가 몸을 앞으로 굽혀 자기 장화를 들여다보더니 다시 몸을 폈다. \"허!"], ["I don’t think I should have done so, if I had been Miss Havisham.", "내가 미스 해비셤이었다면 그렇게는 하지 않았을 것 같군."], ["But she ought to know her own business best.”", "하지만 그분이야 제 일을 제일 잘 아시겠지.\""]], [["“I know more of the history of Miss Havisham’s adopted child than Miss Havisham herself does, sir. I know her mother.”", "\"저는 미스 해비셤의 양녀의 내력을 미스 해비셤 자신보다 더 많이 알고 있습니다, 선생님. 저는 그 애의 어머니를 압니다.\""]], [["Mr. Jaggers looked at me inquiringly, and repeated “Mother?”", "재거스 씨가 캐묻듯 나를 바라보며 \"어머니?\" 하고 되뇌었다."]], [["“I have seen her mother within these three days.”", "\"저는 사흘 안에 그 애의 어머니를 본 적이 있습니다.\""]], [["“Yes?”", "\"그래?\""], ["said Mr. Jaggers.", "재거스 씨가 말했다."]], [["“And so have you, sir.", "\"그리고 선생님께서도 보셨지요."], ["And you have seen her still more recently.”", "게다가 선생님께서는 그보다 훨씬 더 최근에 보셨고요.\""]], [["“Yes?”", "\"그래?\""], ["said Mr. Jaggers.", "재거스 씨가 말했다."]], [["“Perhaps I know more of Estella’s history than even you do,”", "\"어쩌면 저는 에스텔라의 내력을 선생님보다도 더 많이 알고 있는지도 모릅니다.\""], ["said I.", "내가 말했다."], ["“I know her father too.”", "\"저는 그 애의 아버지도 압니다.\""]], [["A certain stop that Mr. Jaggers came to in his manner—he was too self-possessed to change his manner, but he could not help its being brought to an indefinably attentive stop—assured me that he did not know who her father was.", "재거스 씨의 태도에 어떤 멈춤이 일었는데 — 그는 너무도 침착해 태도를 바꾸지는 않았으나, 그것이 형언할 수 없을 만큼 주의 깊은 멈춤에 이르는 것까지는 어쩌지 못했다 — 그 멈춤은 그가 그 애의 아버지가 누구인지 모른다는 것을 내게 확신시켜 주었다."], ["This I had strongly suspected from Provis’s account (as Herbert had repeated it) of his having kept himself dark; which I pieced on to the fact that he himself was not Mr. Jaggers’s client until some four years later, and when he could have no reason for claiming his identity.", "이는 프로비스가 자신을 어둠 속에 숨겨 왔다는 그의 이야기(허버트가 되풀이해 들려준 대로)에서 내가 강하게 의심하던 바였다. 나는 그것을, 프로비스 자신이 사 년쯤 뒤에야, 더는 자기 신원을 주장할 이유가 없어졌을 때에야 재거스 씨의 의뢰인이 되었다는 사실에 짜 맞추었다."], ["But, I could not be sure of this unconsciousness on Mr. Jaggers’s part before, though I was quite sure of it now.", "그러나 전에는 재거스 씨 쪽의 이런 무지를 확신할 수 없었지만, 지금은 제법 확신할 수 있었다."]], [["“So! You know the young lady’s father, Pip?”", "\"그래! 자네가 그 아가씨의 아버지를 안다고, 핍?\""], ["said Mr. Jaggers.", "재거스 씨가 말했다."]], [["“Yes,”", "\"네.\""], ["I replied, “and his name is Provis—from New South Wales.”", "내가 대답했다. \"그리고 그의 이름은 프로비스입니다. 뉴사우스웨일스에서 온.\""]], [["Even Mr. Jaggers started when I said those words.", "내가 그 말을 하자 재거스 씨조차 흠칫했다."], ["It was the slightest start that could escape a man, the most carefully repressed and the sooner checked, but he did start, though he made it a part of the action of taking out his pocket-handkerchief.", "그것은 사람이 보일 수 있는 가장 미미한 흠칫함이었고, 가장 조심스럽게 억눌리고 가장 빨리 제압된 것이었으나, 그래도 그는 흠칫했다. 비록 그것을 손수건을 꺼내는 동작의 일부로 만들어 버리긴 했지만."], ["How Wemmick received the announcement I am unable to say; for I was afraid to look at him just then, lest Mr.", "웨믹이 그 발표를 어떻게 받아들였는지 나는 말할 수 없다. 그때는 그를 차마 쳐다볼 수 없었으니까."], ["Jaggers’s sharpness should detect that there had been some communication unknown to him between us.", "재거스 씨의 예리함이, 그와 나 사이에 그가 모르는 어떤 교감이 있었음을 알아챌까 두려웠기 때문이다."]], [["“And on what evidence, Pip,”", "\"그래, 핍, 무슨 증거로,\""], ["asked Mr. Jaggers, very coolly, as he paused with his handkerchief half way to his nose, “does Provis make this claim?”", "손수건을 코까지 절반쯤 가져가다 멈춘 채 재거스 씨가 아주 냉정하게 물었다, \"프로비스가 이런 주장을 하는 건가?\""]], [["“He does not make it,”", "\"그는 그런 주장을 하지 않습니다.\""], ["said I, “and has never made it, and has no knowledge or belief that his daughter is in existence.”", "내가 말했다. \"한 번도 한 적이 없고, 자기 딸이 살아 있다는 것을 알지도 믿지도 못하고 있습니다.\""]], [["For once, the powerful pocket-handkerchief failed.", "이번만큼은 그 강력한 손수건도 제구실을 못했다."], ["My reply was so unexpected, that Mr. Jaggers put the handkerchief back into his pocket without completing the usual performance, folded his arms, and looked with stern attention at me, though with an immovable face.", "내 대답이 너무도 뜻밖이어서, 재거스 씨는 늘 하던 동작을 마무리하지 못한 채 손수건을 도로 주머니에 넣고, 팔짱을 끼고는, 미동도 없는 얼굴로, 그러나 준엄하게 주의를 기울여 나를 바라보았다."]], [["Then I told him all I knew, and how I knew it; with the one reservation that I left him to infer that I knew from Miss Havisham what I in fact knew from Wemmick. I was very careful indeed as to that.", "그리하여 나는 그에게 내가 아는 모든 것을, 그리고 그것을 어떻게 알게 되었는지를 들려주었다. 다만 한 가지만은 유보하여, 실은 웨믹에게서 알게 된 것을 미스 해비셤에게서 알게 된 것으로 그가 추측하도록 내버려 두었다."], ["Nor did I look towards Wemmick until I had finished all I had to tell, and had been for some time silently meeting Mr. Jaggers’s look.", "나는 정말이지 그 점에 대해서는 무척 조심했다. 또한 내가 들려줄 이야기를 모두 마치고, 한동안 말없이 재거스 씨의 시선을 마주하고 난 뒤에야 비로소 나는 웨믹 쪽으로 시선을 돌렸다."], ["When I did at last turn my eyes in Wemmick’s direction, I found that he had unposted his pen, and was intent upon the table before him.", "마침내 웨믹 쪽으로 눈을 돌렸을 때, 나는 그가 펜을 꽂이에서 빼 들고 자기 앞의 탁자에 몰두해 있는 것을 보았다."]], [["“Hah!”", "\"허!\""], ["said Mr. Jaggers at last, as he moved towards the papers on the table.", "재거스 씨가 마침내 탁자 위의 서류 쪽으로 움직이며 말했다."], ["“What item was it you were at, Wemmick, when Mr. Pip came in?”", "\"웨믹, 핍 군이 들어왔을 때 자네가 어느 항목을 보고 있었지?\""]], [["But I could not submit to be thrown off in that way, and I made a passionate, almost an indignant appeal, to him to be more frank and manly with me.", "그러나 나는 그런 식으로 떨쳐지는 것을 받아들일 수 없어, 그에게 나를 좀 더 솔직하고 사내답게 대해 달라고 격정적인, 거의 분개에 가까운 호소를 했다."], ["I reminded him of the false hopes into which I had lapsed, the length of time they had lasted, and the discovery I had made: and I hinted at the danger that weighed upon my spirits.", "나는 그에게 내가 빠져들었던 헛된 희망과, 그것이 지속된 시간의 길이와, 내가 해낸 발견을 일깨워 주었다. 그리고 내 마음을 짓누르는 위험을 넌지시 비쳤다."], ["I represented myself as being surely worthy of some little confidence from him, in return for the confidence I had just now imparted.", "나는 방금 내가 전한 신뢰의 보답으로, 그에게서 약간의 신뢰를 받을 만한 자격이 분명히 있다고 내 처지를 표명했다."], ["I said that I did not blame him, or suspect him, or mistrust him, but I wanted assurance of the truth from him.", "나는 그를 탓하지도, 의심하지도, 불신하지도 않으나, 다만 그에게서 진실에 대한 확언을 듣고 싶을 뿐이라고 말했다."], ["And if he asked me why I wanted it, and why I thought I had any right to it, I would tell him, little as he cared for such poor dreams, that I had loved Estella dearly and long, and that although I had lost her, and must live a bereaved life, whatever concerned her was still nearer and dearer to me than anything else in the world.", "그리고 만약 그가 어째서 그것을 원하는지, 어째서 내게 그럴 권리가 있다고 여기는지 묻는다면, 그가 그런 가련한 꿈 따위에 아무리 관심이 없다 해도, 나는 에스텔라를 오래도록 진심으로 사랑해 왔으며, 비록 그 애를 잃고 빼앗긴 삶을 살아가야 할지언정, 그 애와 관련된 것은 무엇이든 이 세상 그 무엇보다 여전히 더 가깝고 소중하다고 그에게 말하겠노라고 했다."], ["And seeing that Mr. Jaggers stood quite still and silent, and apparently quite obdurate, under this appeal, I turned to Wemmick, and said, “Wemmick, I know you to be a man with a gentle heart.", "그리고 이 호소에도 재거스 씨가 미동도 없이 침묵한 채, 보아하니 도무지 완강하게 서 있는 것을 보고, 나는 웨믹을 향해 돌아서서 말했다. \"웨믹, 저는 당신이 다정한 마음을 가진 사람이라는 걸 압니다."], ["I have seen your pleasant home, and your old father, and all the innocent, cheerful playful ways with which you refresh your business life.", "저는 당신의 즐거운 집과, 당신의 노부친과, 당신이 자기 직업 생활을 새롭게 해 주는 그 모든 순박하고 명랑하고 장난기 어린 방식들을 보았습니다."], ["And I entreat you to say a word for me to Mr. Jaggers, and to represent to him that, all circumstances considered, he ought to be more open with me!”", "그러니 부디 저를 위해 재거스 씨에게 한마디 해 주시고, 모든 정황을 고려할 때 그가 제게 좀 더 터놓고 대해야 한다고 그분께 말씀해 주시기를 간청합니다!\""]], [["I have never seen two men look more oddly at one another than Mr. Jaggers and Wemmick did after this apostrophe.", "이 외침이 있은 뒤 재거스 씨와 웨믹이 서로를 바라본 것보다 더 기묘하게 서로를 바라보는 두 사람을 나는 본 적이 없다."], ["At first, a misgiving crossed me that Wemmick would be instantly dismissed from his employment; but it melted as I saw Mr. Jaggers relax into something like a smile, and Wemmick become bolder.", "처음에는 웨믹이 당장 일자리에서 해고되는 것은 아닐까 하는 불안이 스쳤으나, 재거스 씨가 미소 비슷한 것으로 누그러지고 웨믹이 더 대담해지는 것을 보자 그 불안은 녹아 사라졌다."]], [["“What’s all this?”", "\"이게 다 무슨 소린가?\""], ["said Mr. Jaggers.", "재거스 씨가 말했다."], ["“You with an old father, and you with pleasant and playful ways?”", "\"자네한테 노부친이 있다니, 게다가 자네한테 즐겁고 장난기 어린 방식이라니?\""]], [["“Well!” returned Wemmick.", "\"뭐 어떻습니까!\" 웨믹이 응수했다."], ["“If I don’t bring ’em here, what does it matter?”", "\"그것들을 여기로 데려오지만 않으면, 무슨 상관입니까?\""]], [["“Pip,”", "\"핍.\""], ["said Mr. Jaggers, laying his hand upon my arm, and smiling openly, “this man must be the most cunning impostor in all London.”", "재거스 씨가 내 팔에 손을 얹고 드러내 놓고 미소 지으며 말했다. \"이자는 분명 온 런던에서 가장 교활한 사기꾼일 걸세.\""]], [["“Not a bit of it,”", "\"천만의 말씀입니다.\""], ["returned Wemmick, growing bolder and bolder. “I think you’re another.”", "웨믹이 점점 더 대담해지며 응수했다. \"제가 보기엔 선생님이야말로 또 한 사람이시지요.\""]], [["Again they exchanged their former odd looks, each apparently still distrustful that the other was taking him in.", "다시 두 사람은 앞서의 그 기묘한 눈길을 주고받았는데, 저마다 여전히 상대가 자신을 속이고 있는 것은 아닌지 미심쩍어하는 기색이었다."]], [["“You with a pleasant home?”", "\"자네한테 즐거운 집이 있다고?\""], ["said Mr. Jaggers.", "재거스 씨가 말했다."]], [["“Since it don’t interfere with business,” returned Wemmick, “let it be so.", "\"업무에 지장만 안 준다면야,\" 웨믹이 응수했다, \"그렇다고 해 두지요."], ["Now, I look at you, sir, I shouldn’t wonder if you might be planning and contriving to have a pleasant home of your own one of these days, when you’re tired of all this work.”", "자, 이렇게 선생님을 보고 있자니, 선생님께서도 이 모든 일에 싫증이 날 즈음이면 언젠가 선생님만의 즐거운 집을 가지려고 계획하고 궁리하고 계신다 해도 저는 놀라지 않겠습니다.\""]], [["Mr. Jaggers nodded his head retrospectively two or three times, and actually drew a sigh.", "재거스 씨는 지난날을 돌이키듯 두세 번 고개를 끄덕이더니, 실제로 한숨까지 내쉬었다. \"핍.\" 그가 말했다."], ["“Pip,”", "\"'가련한 꿈'"], ["said he, “we won’t talk about ‘poor dreams;’ you know more about such things than I, having much fresher experience of that kind.", "따위는 이야기하지 마세나. 그런 일이라면 자네가 나보다 더 잘 알 테니까. 그런 종류의 경험이 자네 쪽이 훨씬 더 생생하지 않은가."], ["But now about this other matter.", "하지만 이제 이 다른 문제로 가 보세."], ["I’ll put a case to you. Mind! I admit nothing.”", "자네에게 한 가지 경우를 들어 보겠네. 명심하게! 나는 아무것도 인정하지 않네.\""]], [["He waited for me to declare that I quite understood that he expressly said that he admitted nothing.", "그는 자신이 아무것도 인정하지 않는다고 명시적으로 말했음을 내가 충분히 이해했다고 선언하기를 기다렸다."]], [["“Now, Pip,” said Mr.", "\"자, 핍.\""], ["Jaggers, “put this case.", "재거스 씨가 말했다."], ["Put the case that a woman, under such circumstances as you have mentioned, held her child concealed, and was obliged to communicate the fact to her legal adviser, on his representing to her that he must know, with an eye to the latitude of his defence, how the fact stood about that child.", "\"이런 경우를 가정해 보세. 어떤 여자가 자네가 말한 것과 같은 상황에서 제 아이를 숨겨 두었는데, 그녀의 법률 고문이, 자기 변호의 폭을 고려할 때 그 아이에 관한 사정이 어떤지 반드시 알아야겠다고 그녀에게 밝히는 바람에, 그 사실을 그에게 알려야만 했다고 가정해 보세."], ["Put the case that, at the same time he held a trust to find a child for an eccentric rich lady to adopt and bring up.”", "그리고 같은 시기에 그가, 어느 별난 부유한 부인이 입양해서 길러 줄 아이를 하나 찾아 주는 임무를 맡고 있었다고 가정해 보세.\""]], [["“I follow you, sir.”", "\"따라가고 있습니다, 선생님.\""]], [["“Put the case that he lived in an atmosphere of evil, and that all he saw of children was their being generated in great numbers for certain destruction.", "\"그가 악의 분위기 속에서 살았고, 그가 아이들에 대해 본 것이라곤 아이들이 정해진 파멸을 위해 엄청난 수로 태어나는 것뿐이었다고 가정해 보세."], ["Put the case that he often saw children solemnly tried at a criminal bar, where they were held up to be seen; put the case that he habitually knew of their being imprisoned, whipped, transported, neglected, cast out, qualified in all ways for the hangman, and growing up to be hanged.", "그가 아이들이 형사 법정에서 엄숙하게 재판받으며 사람들 앞에 내세워지는 것을 자주 보았다고 가정해 보세. 그가 아이들이 투옥되고, 매질당하고, 유형 보내지고, 방치되고, 내쫓기고, 온갖 식으로 교수형 집행인에게 어울리는 자격을 갖추고, 자라나서 교수형에 처해지는 것을 늘 알고 지냈다고 가정해 보세."], ["Put the case that pretty nigh all the children he saw in his daily business life he had reason to look upon as so much spawn, to develop into the fish that were to come to his net,—to be prosecuted, defended, forsworn, made orphans, bedevilled somehow.”", "그가 매일의 업무 생활에서 본 거의 모든 아이를, 그의 그물로 들어올 물고기로 자라날 알 무더기로 — 기소되고, 변호받고, 위증당하고, 고아가 되고, 어떤 식으로든 괴롭힘당할 알 무더기로 — 바라볼 만한 이유가 있었다고 가정해 보세.\""]], [["“I follow you, sir.”", "\"따라가고 있습니다, 선생님.\""]], [["“Put the case, Pip, that here was one pretty little child out of the heap who could be saved; whom the father believed dead, and dared make no stir about; as to whom, over the mother, the legal adviser had this power: “I know what you did, and how you did it.", "\"핍, 이렇게 가정해 보세. 그 무더기 가운데 구해 낼 수 있는 예쁘고 어린 아이가 하나 있었는데, 아버지는 그 애가 죽었다고 믿어 감히 아무런 소동도 일으키지 못했고, 그 어머니에 관해서는 법률 고문이 이런 힘을 쥐고 있었다고 말이야. '나는 당신이 무슨 짓을 했고 어떻게 했는지 알고 있소."], ["You came so and so, you did such and such things to divert suspicion. I have tracked you through it all, and I tell it you all.", "당신은 이러저러하게 왔고, 혐의를 돌리려고 이러저러한 짓을 했소. 나는 그 전부를 통해 당신을 추적해 왔고, 그것을 당신에게 모두 말해 주는 거요."], ["Part with the child, unless it should be necessary to produce it to clear you, and then it shall be produced.", "당신의 혐의를 벗기기 위해 그 아이를 내세워야 할 필요가 생기지 않는 한 그 아이와 헤어지시오. 그리고 그럴 필요가 생기면 그 아이는 내세워질 거요."], ["Give the child into my hands, and I will do my best to bring you off.", "그 아이를 내 손에 맡기시오, 그러면 나는 당신을 빼내기 위해 최선을 다하겠소."], ["If you are saved, your child is saved too; if you are lost, your child is still saved.”", "당신이 구원받으면 당신 아이도 구원받는 것이고, 당신이 파멸한다 해도 당신 아이는 여전히 구원받는 거요.'"], ["Put the case that this was done, and that the woman was cleared.”", "이 일이 행해졌고, 그 여자는 혐의를 벗었다고 가정해 보세.\""]], [["“I understand you perfectly.”", "\"완벽하게 이해했습니다.\""]], [["“But that I make no admissions?”", "\"하지만 나는 아무것도 인정하지 않는 거지?\""]], [["“That you make no admissions.”", "\"선생님께서는 아무것도 인정하지 않으시는 거지요.\""], ["And Wemmick repeated, “No admissions.”", "그러자 웨믹이 되풀이했다. \"인정 없음.\""]], [["“Put the case, Pip, that passion and the terror of death had a little shaken the woman’s intellects, and that when she was set at liberty, she was scared out of the ways of the world, and went to him to be sheltered.", "\"핍, 이렇게 가정해 보세. 격정과 죽음의 공포가 그 여자의 지성을 조금 흔들어 놓아, 그녀가 풀려났을 때 세상의 방식에서 겁을 집어먹고, 보호를 구하러 그에게 갔다고 말이야."], ["Put the case that he took her in, and that he kept down the old, wild, violent nature whenever he saw an inkling of its breaking out, by asserting his power over her in the old way.", "그가 그녀를 거두어 주었고, 그 옛날의 거칠고 사나운 본성이 터져 나오려는 낌새를 볼 때마다 옛날 방식대로 그녀에 대한 자신의 힘을 행사하여 그것을 억눌렀다고 가정해 보세."], ["Do you comprehend the imaginary case?”", "이 가상의 경우를 이해하겠나?\""]], [["“Quite.”", "\"충분히요.\""]], [["“Put the case that the child grew up, and was married for money.", "\"그 아이가 자라나서 돈을 보고 시집갔다고 가정해 보세."], ["That the mother was still living.", "어머니가 아직 살아 있다고."], ["That the father was still living.", "아버지가 아직 살아 있다고."], ["That the mother and father, unknown to one another, were dwelling within so many miles, furlongs, yards if you like, of one another.", "어머니와 아버지가 서로를 모른 채, 서로에게서 몇 마일, 몇 펄롱, 굳이 말하자면 몇 야드 안쪽에 살고 있다고."], ["That the secret was still a secret, except that you had got wind of it.", "그리고 그 비밀이, 자네가 그 낌새를 챈 것을 빼면, 여전히 비밀이라고 가정해 보세."], ["Put that last case to yourself very carefully.”", "그 마지막 경우를 자네 자신에게 아주 신중하게 적용해 보게.\" \"그러겠습니다.\""]], [["“I do.” “I ask Wemmick to put it to himself very carefully.”", "\"웨믹에게도 그것을 자기 자신에게 아주 신중하게 적용해 보라고 청하겠네.\""]], [["And Wemmick said, “I do.”", "그러자 웨믹이 말했다. \"그러겠습니다.\""]], [["“For whose sake would you reveal the secret?", "\"누구를 위해 자네는 그 비밀을 밝히겠나?"], ["For the father’s?", "아버지를 위해?"], ["I think he would not be much the better for the mother.", "어머니를 찾는다고 해서 그가 별로 나아질 것 같지는 않은데."], ["For the mother’s?", "어머니를 위해?"], ["I think if she had done such a deed she would be safer where she was. For the daughter’s?", "그녀가 그런 짓을 저질렀다면, 지금 있는 곳에 있는 편이 더 안전할 것 같은데. 딸을 위해?"], ["I think it would hardly serve her to establish her parentage for the information of her husband, and to drag her back to disgrace, after an escape of twenty years, pretty secure to last for life.", "남편에게 알려 주려고 그녀의 혈통을 밝혀내고, 이십 년이나 도망쳐 나와 거의 평생 안전하게 지속될 그 도피를 깨뜨려 그녀를 다시 치욕 속으로 끌고 들어가는 일이, 그녀에게 도움이 될 것 같지는 않은데."], ["But add the case that you had loved her, Pip, and had made her the subject of those ‘poor dreams’ which have, at one time or another, been in the heads of more men than you think likely, then I tell you that you had better—and would much sooner when you had thought well of it—chop off that bandaged left hand of yours with your bandaged right hand, and then pass the chopper on to Wemmick there, to cut that off too.”", "하지만 핍, 자네가 그녀를 사랑했고, 자네가 생각하는 것보다 더 많은 사내들의 머릿속에 이런저런 때에 들어앉았던 그 '가련한 꿈'의 대상으로 그녀를 삼았다는 경우를 보태 보세. 그렇다면 자네에게 말하겠는데, 자네는 — 그리고 곰곰이 생각해 본다면 차라리 그편을 훨씬 빨리 택할 텐데 — 그 붕대 감은 왼손을 그 붕대 감은 오른손으로 잘라 내고, 그러고 나서 그 도끼를 저기 저 웨믹에게 넘겨 그것마저 잘라 내게 하는 편이 나을 걸세.\""]], [["I looked at Wemmick, whose face was very grave.", "나는 웨믹을 바라보았는데, 그의 얼굴은 무척 엄숙했다."], ["He gravely touched his lips with his forefinger.", "그는 엄숙하게 집게손가락으로 자기 입술을 건드렸다."], ["I did the same. Mr.", "나도 똑같이 했다."], ["Jaggers did the same.", "재거스 씨도 똑같이 했다."], ["“Now, Wemmick,”", "\"자, 웨믹.\""], ["said the latter then, resuming his usual manner, “what item was it you were at when Mr. Pip came in?”", "그러더니 재거스 씨가 평소의 태도로 돌아가며 말했다. \"핍 군이 들어왔을 때 자네가 어느 항목을 보고 있었지?\""]], [["Standing by for a little, while they were at work, I observed that the odd looks they had cast at one another were repeated several times: with this difference now, that each of them seemed suspicious, not to say conscious, of having shown himself in a weak and unprofessional light to the other.", "그들이 일하는 동안 잠시 곁에 서 있으면서, 나는 그들이 서로에게 던졌던 그 기묘한 눈길이 여러 번 되풀이되는 것을 보았다. 다만 이번에는 다른 점이 있었으니, 저마다 자신이 상대에게 약하고 직업적이지 못한 모습을 드러냈다는 사실을 의식까지는 아니더라도 미심쩍어하는 듯했다."], ["For this reason, I suppose, they were now inflexible with one another; Mr.", "짐작건대 그런 이유로, 그들은 이제 서로에게 완강했다."], ["Jaggers being highly dictatorial, and Wemmick obstinately justifying himself whenever there was the smallest point in abeyance for a moment.", "재거스 씨는 몹시 위압적이었고, 웨믹은 잠시라도 미결로 남은 사소한 점이 하나라도 있으면 고집스럽게 자기를 정당화했다."], ["I had never seen them on such ill terms; for generally they got on very well indeed together.", "나는 그들이 그토록 사이가 나쁜 것을 본 적이 없었다. 보통 때 그들은 정말이지 무척 잘 지냈으니까."]], [["But they were both happily relieved by the opportune appearance of Mike, the client with the fur cap and the habit of wiping his nose on his sleeve, whom I had seen on the very first day of my appearance within those walls.", "그러나 다행히도 두 사람은 마이크가 때맞춰 나타나 준 덕에 한시름 놓았다. 마이크는 모피 모자를 쓰고 소매로 코를 닦는 버릇이 있는 그 의뢰인으로, 내가 그 벽 안에 처음 발을 들인 바로 그날 본 적이 있는 자였다."], ["This individual, who, either in his own person or in that of some member of his family, seemed to be always in trouble (which in that place meant Newgate), called to announce that his eldest daughter was taken up on suspicion of shoplifting.", "자신이든 가족 중 누군가든 늘 곤경에 처해 있는 듯한(그곳에서 그것은 곧 뉴게이트를 뜻했다) 이 사내는, 자기 맏딸이 가게 좀도둑질 혐의로 붙잡혔다는 것을 알리러 들렀다."], ["As he imparted this melancholy circumstance to Wemmick, Mr. Jaggers standing magisterially before the fire and taking no share in the proceedings, Mike’s eye happened to twinkle with a tear.", "재거스 씨가 난로 앞에 위엄 있게 선 채 그 일에 끼어들지 않는 가운데, 마이크가 이 우울한 사정을 웨믹에게 전하다가, 그의 눈에 그만 눈물이 한 방울 반짝였다."]], [["“What are you about?” demanded Wemmick, with the utmost indignation.", "\"자네 지금 뭐 하는 건가?\" 웨믹이 더없이 분개하여 따져 물었다."], ["“What do you come snivelling here for?”", "\"여기 와서 뭣 하러 훌쩍거리는 거야?\""]], [["“I didn’t go to do it, Mr. Wemmick.”", "\"일부러 그런 게 아닙니다, 웨믹 씨.\""]], [["“You did,” said Wemmick.", "\"일부러 그런 거잖아.\""], ["“How dare you?", "웨믹이 말했다."], ["You’re not in a fit state to come here, if you can’t come here without spluttering like a bad pen.", "\"어떻게 감히? 망가진 펜처럼 그렇게 부적부적 흘리지 않고는 여기 올 수 없다면, 자네는 여기 올 만한 상태가 아니야."], ["What do you mean by it?”", "대체 무슨 셈으로 그러는 건가?\""]], [["“A man can’t help his feelings, Mr. Wemmick,”", "\"사람이 제 감정을 어쩌겠습니까, 웨믹 씨.\""], ["pleaded Mike.", "마이크가 간청했다."]], [["“His what?”", "\"제 뭐라고?\""], ["demanded Wemmick, quite savagely.", "웨믹이 아주 사납게 따져 물었다."], ["“Say that again!”", "\"다시 말해 봐!\""]], [["“Now look here my man,”", "\"이보게, 자네.\""], ["said Mr. Jaggers, advancing a step, and pointing to the door.", "재거스 씨가 한 걸음 다가서며 문을 가리키고 말했다."], ["“Get out of this office.", "\"이 사무실에서 나가게."], ["I’ll have no feelings here. Get out.”", "여기서 감정 따위는 용납하지 않아. 나가.\""]], [["“It serves you right,” said Wemmick, “Get out.”", "\"꼴좋군.\" 웨믹이 말했다. \"나가.\""]], [["So, the unfortunate Mike very humbly withdrew, and Mr. Jaggers and Wemmick appeared to have re-established their good understanding, and went to work again with an air of refreshment upon them as if they had just had lunch.", "그리하여 가엾은 마이크는 아주 공손하게 물러났고, 재거스 씨와 웨믹은 좋은 관계를 다시 회복한 듯, 마치 방금 점심이라도 먹고 온 것처럼 산뜻한 기색으로 다시 일에 착수했다."]], [["# Chapter LII.", "# Chapter LII."]], [["From Little Britain I went, with my check in my pocket, to Miss Skiffins’s brother, the accountant; and Miss Skiffins’s brother, the accountant, going straight to Clarriker’s and bringing Clarriker to me, I had the great satisfaction of concluding that arrangement.", "리틀 브리튼을 나선 나는, 수표를 주머니에 넣고서 스키핀스 양의 오라버니인 회계사를 찾아갔다. 그리고 스키핀스 양의 오라버니인 회계사가 곧장 클래리커에게 가서 그를 내게 데려왔으므로, 나는 그 일을 마무리 짓는 크나큰 만족을 누렸다."], ["It was the only good thing I had done, and the only completed thing I had done, since I was first apprised of my great expectations.", "그것은 내가 막대한 유산에 관한 소식을 처음 들은 이래, 내가 한 유일하게 좋은 일이자 유일하게 끝까지 해낸 일이었다."]], [["Clarriker informing me on that occasion that the affairs of the House were steadily progressing, that he would now be able to establish a small branch-house in the East which was much wanted for the extension of the business, and that Herbert in his new partnership capacity would go out and take charge of it, I found that I must have prepared for a separation from my friend, even though my own affairs had been more settled.", "그때 클래리커가 내게 알려 주기를, 회사의 사정이 꾸준히 나아가고 있어, 이제 사업 확장을 위해 절실히 필요했던 작은 동방 지점을 세울 수 있게 되었고, 새로이 동업자가 된 허버트가 그곳으로 나가 그 일을 맡으리라는 것이었다. 나는 설령 내 처지가 더 안정되어 있었다 하더라도, 친구와의 이별을 미리 각오해 두어야 했으리라는 것을 깨달았다."], ["And now, indeed, I felt as if my last anchor were loosening its hold, and I should soon be driving with the winds and waves.", "그리고 지금이야말로 정말이지, 나의 마지막 닻이 그 잡은 자리를 놓아 가는 듯했고, 머지않아 나는 바람과 파도에 떠밀려 가게 될 것만 같았다."]], [["But there was recompense in the joy with which Herbert would come home of a night and tell me of these changes, little imagining that he told me no news, and would sketch airy pictures of himself conducting Clara Barley to the land of the Arabian Nights, and of me going out to join them (with a caravan of camels, I believe), and of our all going up the Nile and seeing wonders.", "그러나 그에 대한 보상이라면, 허버트가 밤이면 집에 돌아와 이런 변화들을 내게 이야기해 줄 때의 그 기쁨이었다. 그는 자기가 내게 들려주는 것이 전혀 새로운 소식이 아니라는 것은 조금도 짐작하지 못한 채, 자신이 클래라 발리를 데리고 『아라비안 나이트』의 나라로 가는 그림을, 그리고 내가 (낙타 대상隊商을 거느리고서, 라고 했던 것 같다) 그들과 합류하러 떠나는 그림을, 그리고 우리 모두가 나일강을 거슬러 올라가며 온갖 경이를 구경하는 그림을 허공에 즐겨 그려 보이곤 했다."], ["Without being sanguine as to my own part in those bright plans, I felt that Herbert’s way was clearing fast, and that old Bill Barley had but to stick to his pepper and rum, and his daughter would soon be happily provided for.", "그 빛나는 계획들 속의 내 몫에 대해서는 그리 낙관하지 않았지만, 나는 허버트의 앞길이 빠르게 트이고 있다는 것, 그리고 늙은 빌 발리가 후추와 럼주만 붙잡고 있어 주면 그의 딸은 머지않아 행복하게 자리를 잡으리라는 것을 느꼈다."]], [["We had now got into the month of March.", "이제 우리는 삼월에 접어들어 있었다."], ["My left arm, though it presented no bad symptoms, took, in the natural course, so long to heal that I was still unable to get a coat on.", "내 왼팔은 나쁜 징후를 보이지는 않았으나, 자연스러운 경과에 따라 아무는 데 워낙 오래 걸려서 나는 아직도 윗도리를 걸칠 수가 없었다."], ["My right arm was tolerably restored; disfigured, but fairly serviceable.", "오른팔은 그런대로 회복되어, 보기 흉하긴 해도 제법 쓸 만했다."]], [["On a Monday morning, when Herbert and I were at breakfast, I received the following letter from Wemmick by the post.", "어느 월요일 아침, 허버트와 내가 식사를 하고 있을 때, 나는 우편으로 웨믹에게서 다음과 같은 편지를 받았다."]], [["“Walworth.", "\"월워스에서."], ["Burn this as soon as read.", "읽는 즉시 태워 버릴 것."], ["Early in the week, or say Wednesday, you might do what you know of, if you felt disposed to try it. Now burn.”", "이번 주 초, 이를테면 수요일쯤에, 마음이 내키거든 자네가 아는 그 일을 해도 좋을 것이네. 자, 태우게.\""]], [["When I had shown this to Herbert and had put it in the fire—but not before we had both got it by heart—we considered what to do.", "내가 이것을 허버트에게 보여 주고 불 속에 넣은 뒤 — 그러나 우리 둘 다 그것을 외워 두기 전에는 아니었다 — 우리는 어찌할지를 궁리했다."], ["For, of course my being disabled could now be no longer kept out of view.", "왜냐하면 물론, 이제 내가 몸을 쓰지 못한다는 사실을 더는 못 본 체할 수가 없었기 때문이다."]], [["“I have thought it over again and again,”", "\"몇 번이고 거듭 생각해 봤는데,\""], ["said Herbert, “and I think I know a better course than taking a Thames waterman.", "허버트가 말했다. \"템스강 뱃사공을 쓰는 것보다 나은 방도를 알 것 같아."], ["Take Startop.", "스타톱을 쓰자고."], ["A good fellow, a skilled hand, fond of us, and enthusiastic and honourable.”", "좋은 친구에다, 노 솜씨도 능숙하고, 우리를 아끼는 데다, 열의도 있고 의리도 있는 사람이야.\""]], [["I had thought of him more than once.", "나도 그를 한 번 이상 떠올린 적이 있었다."]], [["“But how much would you tell him, Herbert?”", "\"하지만 그에게 어디까지 말할 셈인가, 허버트?\""]], [["“It is necessary to tell him very little.", "\"아주 조금만 말하면 돼."], ["Let him suppose it a mere freak, but a secret one, until the morning comes: then let him know that there is urgent reason for your getting Provis aboard and away.", "아침이 올 때까지는 그저 한낱 변덕이려니, 다만 비밀스러운 변덕이려니 여기게 해 두는 거지. 그러다 막상 그날 아침이 되면, 프로비스를 배에 태워 떠나보내야 할 절박한 사정이 있다는 걸 알려 주는 거야."], ["You go with him?”", "자네도 그와 함께 가는 거지?\""]], [["“No doubt.”", "\"물론이지.\""]], [["“Where?”", "\"어디로?\""]], [["It had seemed to me, in the many anxious considerations I had given the point, almost indifferent what port we made for,—Hamburg, Rotterdam, Antwerp,—the place signified little, so that he was out of England.", "이 문제를 두고 여러 차례 노심초사하며 궁리하는 동안, 우리가 어느 항구를 향하든 거의 상관없는 일처럼 여겨졌다. 함부르크든, 로테르담이든, 안트베르펜이든 — 그가 영국 밖으로만 나가면 그곳이 어디인지는 별로 중요치 않았다."], ["Any foreign steamer that fell in our way and would take us up would do.", "우리 앞길에 나타나 우리를 태워 줄 외국 기선이라면 어느 것이든 좋았다."], ["I had always proposed to myself to get him well down the river in the boat; certainly well beyond Gravesend, which was a critical place for search or inquiry if suspicion were afoot.", "나는 늘 그를 배에 태워 강 하류로 멀찍이 데려가리라 마음먹고 있었다. 적어도 그레이브젠드를 훌쩍 지나서까지는. 혹시라도 의심이 일고 있다면 그곳이야말로 수색이나 심문이 벌어지기 쉬운 위험한 길목이었으니까."], ["As foreign steamers would leave London at about the time of high-water, our plan would be to get down the river by a previous ebb-tide, and lie by in some quiet spot until we could pull off to one.", "외국 기선들은 만조 무렵에 런던을 떠날 터이므로, 우리의 계획은 그에 앞선 썰물을 타고 강 하류로 내려가, 한 척에 다가갈 수 있을 때까지 어느 한적한 곳에 숨어 기다리는 것이었다."], ["The time when one would be due where we lay, wherever that might be, could be calculated pretty nearly, if we made inquiries beforehand.", "우리가 숨어 있는 곳이 어디가 되었든, 그곳에 배가 당도할 시각은 미리 알아보아 두면 제법 정확히 가늠할 수 있었다."]], [["Herbert assented to all this, and we went out immediately after breakfast to pursue our investigations.", "허버트는 이 모든 것에 동의했고, 우리는 아침 식사를 마치자마자 곧장 밖으로 나가 조사에 착수했다."], ["We found that a steamer for Hamburg was likely to suit our purpose best, and we directed our thoughts chiefly to that vessel.", "우리는 함부르크행 기선이 우리 목적에 가장 잘 맞을 듯하다는 것을 알아내고, 주로 그 배에 생각을 모았다."], ["But we noted down what other foreign steamers would leave London with the same tide, and we satisfied ourselves that we knew the build and colour of each.", "그러나 같은 물때에 런던을 떠날 다른 외국 기선들도 적어 두었고, 그 배들 각각의 선체 모양과 빛깔을 익혀 두었음을 스스로 확인했다."], ["We then separated for a few hours: I, to get at once such passports as were necessary; Herbert, to see Startop at his lodgings.", "그런 다음 우리는 몇 시간 동안 헤어졌다. 나는 당장 필요한 여권들을 마련하러, 허버트는 스타톱을 그의 하숙으로 찾아가 만나러."], ["We both did what we had to do without any hindrance, and when we met again at one o’clock reported it done.", "우리 둘 다 별다른 방해 없이 해야 할 일을 해냈고, 한 시에 다시 만났을 때 일이 끝났음을 서로 알렸다."], ["I, for my part, was prepared with passports; Herbert had seen Startop, and he was more than ready to join.", "내 쪽에서는 여권이 준비되어 있었고, 허버트는 스타톱을 만났는데, 그는 기꺼이 합류하겠다고 했다."]], [["Those two should pull a pair of oars, we settled, and I would steer; our charge would be sitter, and keep quiet; as speed was not our object, we should make way enough.", "우리는 그 두 사람이 한 쌍의 노를 젓고, 내가 키를 잡기로 정했다. 우리가 데려갈 사람은 앉은 자리에 가만히 있기로 했다. 속도가 우리의 목적은 아니었으니, 그 정도로도 충분히 나아갈 수 있을 터였다."], ["We arranged that Herbert should not come home to dinner before going to Mill Pond Bank that evening; that he should not go there at all to-morrow evening, Tuesday; that he should prepare Provis to come down to some stairs hard by the house, on Wednesday, when he saw us approach, and not sooner; that all the arrangements with him should be concluded that Monday night; and that he should be communicated with no more in any way, until we took him on board.", "우리는 이렇게 짜 두었다. 허버트가 그날 저녁 밀 폰드 뱅크로 가기 전에 집에 와서 저녁을 먹지 않을 것, 내일 저녁인 화요일에는 그곳에 아예 가지 않을 것, 수요일에 우리가 다가오는 것을 보거든, 그러나 그 전에는 아니되, 프로비스가 집 가까이에 있는 어느 층계로 내려오도록 그를 채비시켜 둘 것, 그와의 모든 약속은 그 월요일 밤에 마무리 지을 것, 그리고 우리가 그를 배에 태울 때까지는 더 이상 어떤 식으로도 그에게 연락하지 않을 것."]], [["These precautions well understood by both of us, I went home.", "이 모든 조심스러운 절차를 우리 둘 다 잘 새겨 둔 뒤, 나는 집으로 돌아갔다."]], [["On opening the outer door of our chambers with my key, I found a letter in the box, directed to me; a very dirty letter, though not ill-written.", "내 열쇠로 우리 거처의 바깥문을 여는데, 함 속에 내게 온 편지 한 통이 들어 있었다. 몹시 더러운 편지였으나, 글씨가 서투르지는 않았다."], ["It had been delivered by hand (of course, since I left home), and its contents were these:—", "그것은 (물론 내가 집을 비운 사이에) 인편으로 전해진 것이었고, 그 내용은 이러했다."]], [["“If you are not afraid to come to the old marshes to-night or to-morrow night at nine, and to come to the little sluice-house by the limekiln, you had better come.", "\"오늘 밤이나 내일 밤 아홉 시에 옛 습지로, 석회 굽는 가마 곁의 작은 수문 막사로 오기가 두렵지 않거든, 오는 편이 좋을 것이다."], ["If you want information regarding your uncle Provis, you had much better come and tell no one, and lose no time.", "네 외숙 프로비스에 관한 정보를 원한다면, 와서 아무에게도 말하지 말고, 한시도 지체하지 않는 편이 훨씬 좋을 것이다."], ["You must come alone.", "반드시 혼자 와야 한다."], ["Bring this with you.”", "이것을 가지고 와라.\""]], [["I had had load enough upon my mind before the receipt of this strange letter.", "이 기이한 편지를 받기 전부터도 내 마음에는 짐이 충분히 얹혀 있었다."], ["What to do now, I could not tell.", "이제 어찌해야 할지 알 수가 없었다."], ["And the worst was, that I must decide quickly, or I should miss the afternoon coach, which would take me down in time for to-night.", "그리고 가장 곤란한 것은, 빨리 결정을 내리지 않으면 오늘 밤에 맞춰 나를 그곳으로 데려다줄 오후 마차를 놓치게 된다는 점이었다."], ["To-morrow night I could not think of going, for it would be too close upon the time of the flight.", "내일 밤에 가는 것은 생각조차 할 수 없었으니, 도주 시각과 너무 가까웠기 때문이다."], ["And again, for anything I knew, the proffered information might have some important bearing on the flight itself.", "게다가 내가 아는 한, 그 제안된 정보가 도주 그 자체와 어떤 중요한 관련이 있을지도 모를 일이었다."]], [["If I had had ample time for consideration, I believe I should still have gone.", "만약 내게 곰곰이 생각할 넉넉한 시간이 있었더라도, 나는 그래도 갔으리라고 믿는다."], ["Having hardly any time for consideration,—my watch showing me that the coach started within half an hour,—I resolved to go.", "생각할 겨를이 거의 없는 터에 — 시계를 보니 마차가 삼십 분 안에 떠나게 되어 있었으므로 — 나는 가기로 마음을 정했다."], ["I should certainly not have gone, but for the reference to my Uncle Provis.", "프로비스 외숙에 대한 언급만 아니었다면 나는 분명 가지 않았을 것이다."], ["That, coming on Wemmick’s letter and the morning’s busy preparation, turned the scale.", "그 한마디가, 웨믹의 편지와 아침나절의 분주한 채비에 더해져, 저울추를 기울게 했다."]], [["It is so difficult to become clearly possessed of the contents of almost any letter, in a violent hurry, that I had to read this mysterious epistle again twice, before its injunction to me to be secret got mechanically into my mind.", "극심한 황망함 속에서는 거의 어떤 편지든 그 내용을 또렷이 머릿속에 담기가 워낙 어려운지라, 나는 이 수상쩍은 서한을 다시 두 번이나 읽고 나서야 비밀을 지키라는 그 당부가 기계적으로 머리에 들어왔다."], ["Yielding to it in the same mechanical kind of way, I left a note in pencil for Herbert, telling him that as I should be so soon going away, I knew not for how long, I had decided to hurry down and back, to ascertain for myself how Miss Havisham was faring.", "똑같이 기계적인 식으로 그에 따르며, 나는 허버트에게 연필로 쪽지 한 장을 남겼다. 머지않아, 그것도 얼마나 오래일지 모를 채로 떠나게 될 터이니, 미스 해비셤이 어떻게 지내는지 내 눈으로 확인하러 서둘러 내려갔다 오기로 했다는 내용이었다."], ["I had then barely time to get my great-coat, lock up the chambers, and make for the coach-office by the short by-ways.", "그러고 나니 외투를 챙기고, 거처를 잠그고, 지름길로 마차 사무소를 향해 갈 시간이 가까스로 남았을 뿐이었다."], ["If I had taken a hackney-chariot and gone by the streets, I should have missed my aim; going as I did, I caught the coach just as it came out of the yard.", "만약 내가 삯마차를 잡아 큰길로 갔더라면 시간을 맞추지 못했을 것이다. 그러나 그렇게 간 덕분에, 나는 마차가 막 마당을 빠져나오는 순간에 그것을 붙잡았다."], ["I was the only inside passenger, jolting away knee-deep in straw, when I came to myself.", "정신을 차리고 보니, 나는 무릎까지 차오른 짚더미 속에서 덜컹거리며 가는 유일한 객실 안 승객이었다."]], [["For I really had not been myself since the receipt of the letter; it had so bewildered me, ensuing on the hurry of the morning.", "사실 나는 그 편지를 받은 뒤로 줄곧 제정신이 아니었다. 아침의 그 황망함에 이어진 터라, 그것이 나를 어찌나 어지럽혀 놓았던지."], ["The morning hurry and flutter had been great; for, long and anxiously as I had waited for Wemmick, his hint had come like a surprise at last.", "아침의 그 분주함과 안절부절못함은 대단한 것이었다. 내가 웨믹을 그토록 오래, 그토록 애타게 기다려 왔으면서도, 막상 그의 기별은 결국 뜻밖의 일처럼 닥쳐왔던 것이다."], ["And now I began to wonder at myself for being in the coach, and to doubt whether I had sufficient reason for being there, and to consider whether I should get out presently and go back, and to argue against ever heeding an anonymous communication, and, in short, to pass through all those phases of contradiction and indecision to which I suppose very few hurried people are strangers.", "그리고 이제 나는 내가 마차 안에 있다는 것이 새삼 의아해지기 시작했고, 내게 거기 있을 만한 충분한 이유가 있는지 의심스러워졌고, 이내 내려서 되돌아가야 하지 않을까 따져 보게 되었고, 익명의 편지 따위에 귀를 기울이는 짓에 대해 스스로 반박하게 되었다. 한마디로, 급히 서두르는 사람치고 모르는 이가 거의 없으리라 싶은 그 온갖 모순과 망설임의 단계를 두루 거쳤다."], ["Still, the reference to Provis by name mastered everything.", "그래도 프로비스라는 이름의 언급이 모든 것을 압도했다."], ["I reasoned as I had reasoned already without knowing it,—if that be reasoning,—in case any harm should befall him through my not going, how could I ever forgive myself!", "나는, 그것이 추론이라 할 수 있다면, 이미 나도 모르게 했던 그 추론을 다시 되풀이했다. 만약 내가 가지 않은 탓에 그에게 어떤 해라도 닥친다면, 내가 어찌 나 자신을 용서할 수 있겠는가!"]], [["It was dark before we got down, and the journey seemed long and dreary to me, who could see little of it inside, and who could not go outside in my disabled state.", "우리가 도착하기 전에 날이 저물었고, 객실 안에 있어 바깥을 거의 볼 수 없었던 데다 몸을 쓰지 못하는 처지라 바깥자리에 나가 앉을 수도 없었던 나에게 그 여정은 길고도 음울하게만 느껴졌다."], ["Avoiding the Blue Boar, I put up at an inn of minor reputation down the town, and ordered some dinner.", "나는 블루 보어를 피해, 읍내 아래쪽의 그리 이름나지 않은 어느 여관에 들어 저녁을 시켰다."], ["While it was preparing, I went to Satis House and inquired for Miss Havisham; she was still very ill, though considered something better.", "음식이 준비되는 동안 나는 새티스 하우스로 가서 미스 해비셤의 안부를 물었다. 그녀는 여전히 몹시 앓고 있었으나, 다소 차도가 있다고 여겨지고 있었다."]], [["My inn had once been a part of an ancient ecclesiastical house, and I dined in a little octagonal common-room, like a font.", "내가 든 여관은 한때 어느 오래된 교회 부속 건물의 일부였던 곳으로, 나는 세례반처럼 생긴 작은 팔각형 공동 식당에서 식사를 했다."], ["As I was not able to cut my dinner, the old landlord with a shining bald head did it for me.", "내가 음식을 썰 수 없었으므로, 번들번들 빛나는 대머리의 늙은 주인이 나를 대신해 썰어 주었다."], ["This bringing us into conversation, he was so good as to entertain me with my own story,—of course with the popular feature that Pumblechook was my earliest benefactor and the founder of my fortunes.", "이 일로 우리가 이야기를 나누게 되자, 그는 친절하게도 내 이야기를 들려주며 나를 즐겁게 해 주었다 — 물론 펌블추크가 나의 최초의 은인이요 내 재산의 토대를 닦은 사람이라는, 세간에 널리 퍼진 대목을 곁들여서."]], [["“Do you know the young man?” said I.", "\"그 젊은이를 아십니까?\" 내가 물었다."]], [["“Know him!”", "\"알다마다요!\""], ["repeated the landlord. “Ever since he was—no height at all.”", "주인이 되받았다. \"그 사람이 — 키도 채 자라기 전부터 알았지요.\""]], [["“Does he ever come back to this neighbourhood?”", "\"그 사람이 이 동네로 돌아오는 일이 있습니까?\""]], [["“Ay, he comes back,”", "\"예, 돌아오지요,\""], ["said the landlord, “to his great friends, now and again, and gives the cold shoulder to the man that made him.”", "주인이 말했다. \"이따금씩 자기 잘난 친구들한테로 돌아와서는, 자기를 사람 만들어 준 이한테는 쌀쌀맞게 등을 돌리지요.\""]], [["“What man is that?”", "\"그게 누구 말씀입니까?\""]], [["“Him that I speak of,”", "\"제가 말씀드린 그 사람이지요,\""], ["said the landlord. “Mr. Pumblechook.”", "주인이 말했다. \"펌블추크 씨 말입니다.\""]], [["“Is he ungrateful to no one else?”", "\"다른 누구에게도 배은망덕하게 굽니까?\""]], [["“No doubt he would be, if he could,”", "\"할 수만 있다면야 그러고도 남겠지요,\""], ["returned the landlord, “but he can’t. And why?", "주인이 대답했다. \"하지만 그러질 못해요. 왜냐?"], ["Because Pumblechook done everything for him.”", "펌블추크가 그 사람을 위해 모든 걸 다 해 주었으니까요.\""]], [["“Does Pumblechook say so?”", "\"펌블추크가 그렇게 말합니까?\""]], [["“Say so!” replied the landlord.", "\"말이라뇨!\" 주인이 대꾸했다."], ["“He han’t no call to say so.”", "\"굳이 입에 올릴 것도 없지요.\""]], [["“But does he say so?”", "\"하지만 그렇게 말하긴 합니까?\""]], [["“It would turn a man’s blood to white wine winegar to hear him tell of it, sir,” said the landlord.", "\"그 사람이 그 얘기를 늘어놓는 걸 듣고 있노라면, 멀쩡한 사람 피가 백포도주 식초로 변할 지경입니다, 손님.\" 주인이 말했다."]], [["I thought, “Yet Joe, dear Joe, you never tell of it.", "나는 생각했다. \"그런데도 조, 사랑하는 조, 당신은 결코 그런 얘기를 입에 올리지 않지."], ["Long-suffering and loving Joe, you never complain.", "묵묵히 견디며 사랑이 넘치는 조, 당신은 결코 불평하는 법이 없어."], ["Nor you, sweet-tempered Biddy!”", "너도 그렇지, 마음씨 고운 비디!\""]], [["“Your appetite’s been touched like by your accident,”", "\"그 사고 때문에 입맛이 좀 상하셨나 봅니다.\""], ["said the landlord, glancing at the bandaged arm under my coat. “Try a tenderer bit.”", "주인이 내 윗도리 밑으로 붕대를 감은 팔을 흘끔 보며 말했다. \"좀 더 연한 부위로 들어 보시지요.\""]], [["“No, thank you,”", "\"아니요, 괜찮습니다.\""], ["I replied, turning from the table to brood over the fire.", "나는 식탁에서 몸을 돌려 난롯불을 바라보며 골똘히 잠긴 채 대꾸했다."], ["“I can eat no more.", "\"더는 못 먹겠어요."], ["Please take it away.”", "좀 치워 주세요.\""]], [["I had never been struck at so keenly, for my thanklessness to Joe, as through the brazen impostor Pumblechook.", "조에 대한 나의 배은망덕함이, 저 뻔뻔스러운 사기꾼 펌블추크를 통해서만큼 그토록 날카롭게 가슴을 후벼 든 적은 없었다."], ["The falser he, the truer Joe; the meaner he, the nobler Joe.", "그가 거짓될수록 조는 더 참되었고, 그가 비열할수록 조는 더 고결했다."]], [["My heart was deeply and most deservedly humbled as I mused over the fire for an hour or more.", "한 시간 남짓 난롯불을 바라보며 깊은 생각에 잠겨 있는 동안, 내 마음은 깊이, 그리고 마땅한 만큼 겸허해졌다."], ["The striking of the clock aroused me, but not from my dejection or remorse, and I got up and had my coat fastened round my neck, and went out.", "시계 치는 소리에 정신이 들었으나, 내 의기소침이나 회한에서 깨어난 것은 아니었다. 나는 일어나 외투를 목에 둘러 여미고 밖으로 나섰다."], ["I had previously sought in my pockets for the letter, that I might refer to it again; but I could not find it, and was uneasy to think that it must have been dropped in the straw of the coach.", "그에 앞서 나는 그 편지를 다시 들여다보려고 주머니를 뒤졌으나 찾을 수가 없었다. 마차의 짚더미 속에 떨어뜨린 게 틀림없으리라는 생각에 마음이 편치 않았다."], ["I knew very well, however, that the appointed place was the little sluice-house by the limekiln on the marshes, and the hour nine.", "그러나 약속된 장소가 습지의 석회 가마 곁 작은 수문 막사이며, 시각은 아홉 시라는 것은 잘 알고 있었다."], ["Towards the marshes I now went straight, having no time to spare.", "한시도 지체할 틈이 없었으므로, 나는 이제 곧장 습지를 향해 나아갔다."]], [["# Chapter LIII.", "# Chapter LIII."]], [["It was a dark night, though the full moon rose as I left the enclosed lands, and passed out upon the marshes.", "어두운 밤이었다. 내가 울타리 쳐진 농지를 벗어나 습지로 나설 무렵 보름달이 떠오르기는 했지만 말이다."], ["Beyond their dark line there was a ribbon of clear sky, hardly broad enough to hold the red large moon.", "습지의 검은 윤곽선 너머로는 맑은 하늘이 한 줄기 띠처럼 펼쳐져 있었으나, 크고 붉은 달을 담기에는 너무 좁았다."], ["In a few minutes she had ascended out of that clear field, in among the piled mountains of cloud.", "몇 분도 지나지 않아 달은 그 맑은 들판에서 솟아올라, 겹겹이 쌓인 구름의 산봉우리들 사이로 사라졌다."]], [["There was a melancholy wind, and the marshes were very dismal.", "구슬픈 바람이 불었고, 습지는 몹시 음산했다."], ["A stranger would have found them insupportable, and even to me they were so oppressive that I hesitated, half inclined to go back.", "낯선 사람이라면 견디기 힘들었을 것이며, 나에게조차 그 풍경은 어찌나 짓누르는 듯했던지, 절반쯤은 돌아갈 마음이 들어 망설였다."], ["But I knew them well, and could have found my way on a far darker night, and had no excuse for returning, being there.", "그러나 나는 그곳을 잘 알았고, 이보다 훨씬 더 어두운 밤이라도 길을 찾을 수 있었으며, 이왕 거기까지 온 마당에 돌아갈 핑계가 없었다."], ["So, having come there against my inclination, I went on against it.", "그래서 마음에 거슬리면서도 그곳에 왔으니, 마음에 거슬리면서도 계속 나아갔다."]], [["The direction that I took was not that in which my old home lay, nor that in which we had pursued the convicts.", "내가 잡은 방향은 옛집이 있는 쪽도, 우리가 죄수들을 쫓던 쪽도 아니었다."], ["My back was turned towards the distant Hulks as I walked on, and, though I could see the old lights away on the spits of sand, I saw them over my shoulder.", "걸어가는 동안 멀리 떨어진 감옥선들은 내 등 뒤에 두었고, 모래톱 위로 멀찍이 떠 있는 옛 불빛들이 보이기는 했으나 어깨 너머로 보았을 뿐이다."], ["I knew the limekiln as well as I knew the old Battery, but they were miles apart; so that, if a light had been burning at each point that night, there would have been a long strip of the blank horizon between the two bright specks.", "나는 옛 포대를 알듯이 석회 가마를 잘 알았으나, 둘은 몇 마일이나 떨어져 있었다. 그러니 그날 밤 두 지점에 각각 불이 켜져 있었다 한들, 두 개의 밝은 점 사이에는 텅 빈 지평선이 길게 한 줄 가로놓여 있었을 것이다."]], [["At first, I had to shut some gates after me, and now and then to stand still while the cattle that were lying in the banked-up pathway arose and blundered down among the grass and reeds.", "처음에는 지나면서 몇 개의 문을 닫아야 했고, 이따금 둑길에 누워 있던 소들이 일어나 풀과 갈대 사이로 비틀거리며 내려갈 때까지 가만히 서 있어야 했다."], ["But after a little while I seemed to have the whole flats to myself.", "그러나 얼마 지나지 않아 그 너른 평지가 온통 나 혼자만의 것이 된 듯싶었다."]], [["It was another half-hour before I drew near to the kiln.", "가마에 다가가기까지는 다시 반 시간이 걸렸다."], ["The lime was burning with a sluggish stifling smell, but the fires were made up and left, and no workmen were visible.", "석회는 굼뜨고 숨 막히는 냄새를 풍기며 타고 있었으나, 불은 지펴 놓은 채 버려져 있었고 일꾼은 하나도 보이지 않았다."], ["Hard by was a small stone-quarry.", "바로 곁에는 작은 채석장이 있었다."], ["It lay directly in my way, and had been worked that day, as I saw by the tools and barrows that were lying about.", "그것은 내 길목에 곧장 놓여 있었으며, 여기저기 흩어진 연장과 손수레로 보아 그날 작업이 있었던 것 같았다."]], [["Coming up again to the marsh level out of this excavation,—for the rude path lay through it,—I saw a light in the old sluice-house.", "이 굴토장에서 다시 습지 높이로 올라오면서 — 거친 길이 그곳을 가로질러 나 있었으므로 — 나는 낡은 수문집에서 새어 나오는 불빛을 보았다."], ["I quickened my pace, and knocked at the door with my hand.", "나는 걸음을 재촉해 손으로 문을 두드렸다."], ["Waiting for some reply, I looked about me, noticing how the sluice was abandoned and broken, and how the house—of wood with a tiled roof—would not be proof against the weather much longer, if it were so even now, and how the mud and ooze were coated with lime, and how the choking vapour of the kiln crept in a ghostly way towards me.", "무슨 대답을 기다리며 나는 주위를 둘러보았다. 수문이 얼마나 버려지고 부서져 있는지, 그리고 기와지붕을 인 그 나무집이 — 지금도 이미 그러하다면 — 머지않아 비바람을 막아 내지 못하리라는 것을, 그리고 진흙과 진창이 석회로 덮여 있는 것을, 그리고 가마의 숨 막히는 증기가 유령처럼 내 쪽으로 기어오는 것을 눈여겨보았다."], ["Still there was no answer, and I knocked again.", "여전히 대답이 없어 나는 다시 두드렸다."], ["No answer still, and I tried the latch.", "그래도 대답이 없자, 나는 빗장을 밀어 보았다."]], [["It rose under my hand, and the door yielded.", "빗장은 내 손에 들려 올라갔고, 문이 열렸다."], ["Looking in, I saw a lighted candle on a table, a bench, and a mattress on a truckle bedstead.", "안을 들여다보니 탁자 위에 불 켜진 양초 하나와 긴 의자 하나, 그리고 바퀴 달린 나직한 침대 위에 매트리스 하나가 보였다."], ["As there was a loft above, I called, “Is there any one here?” but no voice answered.", "위에 다락이 있기에 나는 \"거기 누구 있습니까?\" 하고 불렀으나, 대답하는 목소리는 없었다."], ["Then I looked at my watch, and, finding that it was past nine, called again, “Is there any one here?”", "이어 나는 시계를 보았고, 아홉 시가 지났음을 확인하고는 다시 \"거기 누구 있습니까?\" 하고 불렀다."], ["There being still no answer, I went out at the door, irresolute what to do.", "그래도 대답이 없자, 나는 어찌해야 할지 갈피를 잡지 못한 채 문밖으로 나갔다."]], [["It was beginning to rain fast.", "비가 거세게 내리기 시작했다."], ["Seeing nothing save what I had seen already, I turned back into the house, and stood just within the shelter of the doorway, looking out into the night.", "이미 본 것 말고는 아무것도 보이지 않아, 나는 다시 집 안으로 돌아와 문간의 그늘진 자리에 막 들어선 채 서서 밤을 내다보았다."], ["While I was considering that some one must have been there lately and must soon be coming back, or the candle would not be burning, it came into my head to look if the wick were long.", "누군가 최근에 여기 있었던 게 분명하고 곧 돌아올 게 틀림없다고, 그렇지 않다면 촛불이 타고 있을 리 없다고 생각하던 차에, 심지가 긴지 살펴봐야겠다는 생각이 머리에 떠올랐다."], ["I turned round to do so, and had taken up the candle in my hand, when it was extinguished by some violent shock; and the next thing I comprehended was, that I had been caught in a strong running noose, thrown over my head from behind.", "그러려고 몸을 돌려 손에 촛불을 막 집어 든 순간, 촛불이 어떤 거센 충격에 꺼져 버렸다. 그리고 다음으로 내가 깨달은 것은, 뒤에서 내 머리 위로 던져진 단단한 올가미에 내가 옭아매였다는 사실이었다."]], [["“Now,”", "\"자.\""], ["said a suppressed voice with an oath, “I’ve got you!”", "욕설과 함께 억눌린 목소리가 말했다. \"이제 너를 잡았다!\""]], [["“What is this?”", "\"이게 무슨 짓이야?\""], ["I cried, struggling. “Who is it?", "나는 발버둥치며 외쳤다. \"누구냐?"], ["Help, help, help!”", "사람 살려, 살려, 살려!\""]], [["Not only were my arms pulled close to my sides, but the pressure on my bad arm caused me exquisite pain.", "내 두 팔이 옆구리에 바싹 죄어졌을 뿐 아니라, 다친 팔에 가해진 압박이 견딜 수 없는 고통을 안겼다."], ["Sometimes, a strong man’s hand, sometimes a strong man’s breast, was set against my mouth to deaden my cries, and with a hot breath always close to me, I struggled ineffectually in the dark, while I was fastened tight to the wall.", "어떤 때는 억센 사내의 손이, 어떤 때는 억센 사내의 가슴이 내 입을 틀어막아 비명을 잠재웠고, 뜨거운 숨결이 늘 가까이 닿는 가운데, 나는 벽에 단단히 붙들린 채 어둠 속에서 헛되이 발버둥쳤다. \"자, 이제.\""], ["“And now,” said the suppressed voice with another oath, “call out again, and I’ll make short work of you!”", "억눌린 목소리가 또 한 번 욕설과 함께 말했다. \"다시 한번 소리 질러 봐, 그럼 단번에 끝장내 주마!\""]], [["Faint and sick with the pain of my injured arm, bewildered by the surprise, and yet conscious how easily this threat could be put in execution, I desisted, and tried to ease my arm were it ever so little.", "다친 팔의 고통으로 정신이 아득하고 속이 메스꺼웠으며, 갑작스러운 일에 어리둥절했으나, 그러면서도 이 협박이 얼마나 쉽게 실행될 수 있는지 의식하고 있던 나는 몸부림을 멈추고, 단 조금이라도 팔을 편하게 해 보려 했다."], ["But, it was bound too tight for that. I felt as if, having been burnt before, it were now being boiled.", "그러나 팔은 그러기에는 너무 단단히 묶여 있었다. 전에 데었던 그 팔이 이제는 삶기고 있는 것만 같았다."]], [["The sudden exclusion of the night, and the substitution of black darkness in its place, warned me that the man had closed a shutter.", "밤이 별안간 차단되고 그 자리를 새카만 어둠이 대신한 것으로 보아, 사내가 덧문을 닫았음을 나는 알아차렸다."], ["After groping about for a little, he found the flint and steel he wanted, and began to strike a light.", "잠시 더듬거린 끝에 사내는 원하던 부싯돌과 쇠를 찾아내, 불을 켜기 시작했다."], ["I strained my sight upon the sparks that fell among the tinder, and upon which he breathed and breathed, match in hand, but I could only see his lips, and the blue point of the match; even those but fitfully.", "나는 부싯깃 사이로 떨어지는 불꽃에 시선을 모았다. 사내는 성냥을 손에 든 채 그 위로 거듭거듭 입김을 불었으나, 나는 그의 입술과 성냥의 푸른 끝밖에 볼 수 없었으며, 그것조차 띄엄띄엄 보였을 뿐이다."], ["The tinder was damp,—no wonder there,—and one after another the sparks died out.", "부싯깃은 축축했으니 — 그곳에서야 당연한 일이었다 — 불꽃은 하나둘 잇따라 사그라들었다."]], [["The man was in no hurry, and struck again with the flint and steel.", "사내는 서두르지 않고 다시 부싯돌과 쇠를 쳤다."], ["As the sparks fell thick and bright about him, I could see his hands, and touches of his face, and could make out that he was seated and bending over the table; but nothing more.", "불꽃이 그의 주위로 빽빽하고 밝게 떨어지자, 나는 그의 두 손과 얼굴의 한두 군데를 볼 수 있었으며, 그가 앉아서 탁자 위로 몸을 숙이고 있다는 것을 알아볼 수 있었다. 그러나 그 이상은 보이지 않았다."], ["Presently I saw his blue lips again, breathing on the tinder, and then a flare of light flashed up, and showed me Orlick.", "이윽고 부싯깃에 입김을 부는 그의 푸른 입술이 다시 보이는가 싶더니, 불꽃이 확 타올라 내게 올릭의 모습을 비추어 주었다."]], [["Whom I had looked for, I don’t know.", "내가 누구를 예상했는지는 모르겠다."], ["I had not looked for him.", "그를 예상한 것은 아니었다."], ["Seeing him, I felt that I was in a dangerous strait indeed, and I kept my eyes upon him.", "그를 보는 순간 나는 내가 정말로 위험한 궁지에 처했음을 느꼈고, 그에게서 눈을 떼지 않았다."]], [["He lighted the candle from the flaring match with great deliberation, and dropped the match, and trod it out.", "그는 활활 타는 성냥불로 아주 신중하게 양초에 불을 붙였고, 성냥을 떨어뜨려 발로 비벼 껐다."], ["Then he put the candle away from him on the table, so that he could see me, and sat with his arms folded on the table and looked at me.", "그런 다음 나를 볼 수 있도록 양초를 탁자 위 자기에게서 떨어진 자리에 놓고는, 탁자 위에 팔짱을 낀 채 앉아 나를 바라보았다."], ["I made out that I was fastened to a stout perpendicular ladder a few inches from the wall,—a fixture there,—the means of ascent to the loft above.", "나는 내가 벽에서 몇 인치 떨어진 곳에 세워진 튼튼한 수직 사다리에 — 그곳에 붙박이로 고정된 것이었으며, 위쪽 다락으로 오르는 수단이었다 — 묶여 있음을 알아차렸다."]], [["“Now,”", "\"자.\""], ["said he, when we had surveyed one another for some time, “I’ve got you.”", "우리가 한동안 서로를 살핀 뒤에 그가 말했다. \"이제 너를 잡았다.\""]], [["“Unbind me.", "\"풀어 줘."], ["Let me go!”", "나를 보내 줘!\""]], [["“Ah!” he returned, “I’ll let you go.", "\"하!\" 그가 대꾸했다."], ["I’ll let you go to the moon, I’ll let you go to the stars.", "\"내가 너를 보내 주지. 달나라로 보내 주고, 별나라로 보내 주마."], ["All in good time.”", "다 때가 되면 말이야.\""]], [["“Why have you lured me here?”", "\"왜 나를 여기로 꾀어냈지?\""]], [["“Don’t you know?” said he, with a deadly look.", "\"모르겠냐?\" 그가 살기 어린 눈빛으로 말했다."]], [["“Why have you set upon me in the dark?”", "\"왜 어둠 속에서 나를 덮친 거지?\""]], [["“Because I mean to do it all myself.", "\"이 일을 죄다 나 혼자 해치울 작정이니까."], ["One keeps a secret better than two.", "비밀은 둘보다 하나가 더 잘 지켜지는 법이지."], ["O you enemy, you enemy!”", "오, 이 원수 놈, 이 원수 놈아!\""]], [["His enjoyment of the spectacle I furnished, as he sat with his arms folded on the table, shaking his head at me and hugging himself, had a malignity in it that made me tremble.", "탁자 위에 팔짱을 낀 채 앉아, 내게 고개를 절레절레 흔들며 제 몸을 끌어안고서 내가 보여 주는 광경을 즐기는 그의 모습에는, 나를 떨게 만드는 악의가 서려 있었다."], ["As I watched him in silence, he put his hand into the corner at his side, and took up a gun with a brass-bound stock.", "내가 말없이 그를 지켜보는 동안, 그는 옆쪽 구석에 손을 넣어 놋쇠로 개머리판을 두른 총 한 자루를 집어 들었다."]], [["“Do you know this?”", "\"이걸 알아보겠냐?\""], ["said he, making as if he would take aim at me.", "그가 마치 나를 겨누려는 듯한 시늉을 하며 말했다."], ["“Do you know where you saw it afore? Speak, wolf!”", "\"이걸 전에 어디서 봤는지 알겠냐? 말해 봐, 이 이리 새끼야!\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"그래.\""], ["I answered.", "나는 대답했다."]], [["“You cost me that place.", "\"네놈이 그 일자리를 잃게 만들었지."], ["You did.", "네가 그랬어."], ["Speak!”", "말해 봐!\""]], [["“What else could I do?”", "\"그럼 내가 달리 뭘 할 수 있었겠어?\""]], [["“You did that, and that would be enough, without more.", "\"네가 그랬어, 그것만으로도 충분하지, 더 따질 것도 없이."], ["How dared you to come betwixt me and a young woman I liked?”", "어디 감히 나와, 내가 좋아하던 처녀 사이에 끼어든단 말이냐?\" \"내가 언제 그랬다는 거지?\""]], [["“When did I?” “When didn’t you?", "\"안 그런 적이 언제 있었냐?"], ["It was you as always give Old Orlick a bad name to her.”", "그 처녀한테 늙은 올릭의 험담을 늘어놓은 게 늘 너였잖아.\""]], [["“You gave it to yourself; you gained it for yourself.", "\"그 험담은 네가 스스로 자초한 거야. 네가 스스로 벌어들인 거지."], ["I could have done you no harm, if you had done yourself none.”", "네가 너 자신에게 해를 끼치지 않았다면, 나도 너한테 아무런 해를 끼치지 않았을 거다.\""]], [["“You’re a liar.", "\"거짓말쟁이 같으니."], ["And you’ll take any pains, and spend any money, to drive me out of this country, will you?”", "그래서 무슨 수고를 들여서라도, 돈을 얼마든지 써서라도 나를 이 고장에서 쫓아내겠다, 그거였냐?\""], ["said he, repeating my words to Biddy in the last interview I had with her.", "그가 비디와 마지막으로 만났을 때 내가 했던 말을 그대로 되뇌며 말했다."], ["“Now, I’ll tell you a piece of information.", "\"자, 이제 한 가지 알려 주마."], ["It was never so well worth your while to get me out of this country as it is to-night. Ah!", "나를 이 고장에서 쫓아내는 게 오늘 밤만큼 너한테 보람 있는 적은 없었을 거다. 하!"], ["If it was all your money twenty times told, to the last brass farden!”", "그게 네 돈을 스무 배로 불려서 마지막 한 푼까지 다 들이부어도 그렇단 말이야!\""], ["As he shook his heavy hand at me, with his mouth snarling like a tiger’s, I felt that it was true.", "그가 호랑이처럼 으르렁대는 입으로 묵직한 손을 내게 흔들어 보였을 때, 나는 그 말이 사실임을 느꼈다."]], [["“What are you going to do to me?”", "\"나한테 무슨 짓을 하려는 거지?\""]], [["“I’m a-going,”", "\"내가 하려는 건.\""], ["said he, bringing his fist down upon the table with a heavy blow, and rising as the blow fell to give it greater force,—“I’m a-going to have your life!”", "그가 주먹을 묵직하게 탁자에 내리치며, 그 일격에 더 큰 힘을 싣고자 내리치는 동시에 몸을 일으키며 말했다. \"네 목숨을 가지려는 거다!\""]], [["He leaned forward staring at me, slowly unclenched his hand and drew it across his mouth as if his mouth watered for me, and sat down again.", "그는 몸을 앞으로 기울여 나를 노려보더니, 마치 내 생각에 입에 침이 고이기라도 한 듯 천천히 주먹을 펴서 입가를 훔치고는, 다시 자리에 앉았다."]], [["“You was always in Old Orlick’s way since ever you was a child.", "\"넌 어릴 적부터 늘 늙은 올릭의 앞길을 가로막았지."], ["You goes out of his way this present night.", "오늘 밤 이걸로 그 앞길에서 비켜나게 되는 거야."], ["He’ll have no more on you. You’re dead.”", "그가 더는 너 때문에 골치 썩을 일이 없게 말이지. 넌 이제 죽은 목숨이다.\""]], [["I felt that I had come to the brink of my grave.", "나는 내가 무덤 가장자리에 이르렀음을 느꼈다."], ["For a moment I looked wildly round my trap for any chance of escape; but there was none.", "한순간 나는 갇힌 그 자리를 둘러보며 빠져나갈 기회를 미친 듯이 찾았으나, 그런 것은 없었다."]], [["“More than that,”", "\"게다가 말이야.\""], ["said he, folding his arms on the table again, “I won’t have a rag of you, I won’t have a bone of you, left on earth.", "그가 다시 탁자 위에 팔짱을 끼며 말했다. \"네 살점 한 조각, 뼈 한 토막도 이 땅에 남겨 두지 않을 거다."], ["I’ll put your body in the kiln,—I’d carry two such to it, on my shoulders—and, let people suppose what they may of you, they shall never know nothing.”", "네 시체를 가마에 처넣을 거야 — 너 같은 놈 둘쯤은 어깨에 메고 가지 — 그러면 사람들이 너에 대해 무슨 짐작을 하든, 끝내 아무것도 알아내지 못할 거다.\""]], [["My mind, with inconceivable rapidity followed out all the consequences of such a death.", "내 마음은 헤아릴 수 없을 만큼 빠르게 그런 죽음의 온갖 결과를 좇았다."], ["Estella’s father would believe I had deserted him, would be taken, would die accusing me; even Herbert would doubt me, when he compared the letter I had left for him with the fact that I had called at Miss Havisham’s gate for only a moment; Joe and Biddy would never know how sorry I had been that night, none would ever know what I had suffered, how true I had meant to be, what an agony I had passed through.", "에스텔라의 아버지는 내가 자기를 저버렸다고 믿을 것이며, 붙잡혀, 나를 원망하며 죽어 갈 것이다. 허버트조차 내가 그에게 남긴 편지를, 내가 미스 해비셤의 대문에 잠깐만 들렀다는 사실과 견주어 보고는 나를 의심할 것이다. 조와 비디는 그날 밤 내가 얼마나 미안해했는지 결코 알지 못할 것이며, 내가 무엇을 견뎌 냈는지, 얼마나 진실하고자 했는지, 어떤 고뇌를 거쳤는지 아무도 알지 못할 것이다."], ["The death close before me was terrible, but far more terrible than death was the dread of being misremembered after death.", "코앞에 닥친 죽음은 끔찍했으나, 죽음보다 훨씬 더 끔찍한 것은 죽은 뒤에 잘못 기억되리라는 두려움이었다."], ["And so quick were my thoughts, that I saw myself despised by unborn generations,—Estella’s children, and their children,—while the wretch’s words were yet on his lips.", "그리고 내 생각이 어찌나 빨랐던지, 그 비열한 자의 말이 아직 입가에 남아 있는 동안, 나는 아직 태어나지도 않은 세대들에게 — 에스텔라의 자식들과, 그 자식들의 자식들에게 — 멸시당하는 내 모습을 보았다."]], [["“Now, wolf,”", "\"자, 이리 새끼야.\""], ["said he, “afore I kill you like any other beast,—which is wot I mean to do and wot I have tied you up for,—I’ll have a good look at you and a good goad at you.", "그가 말했다. \"여느 짐승처럼 너를 죽이기 전에 — 그게 내가 하려는 일이고, 그러려고 너를 묶어 놓은 거니까 — 너를 실컷 들여다보고, 실컷 찔러 대 주마."], ["O you enemy!”", "오, 이 원수 놈!\""]], [["It had passed through my thoughts to cry out for help again; though few could know better than I, the solitary nature of the spot, and the hopelessness of aid.", "다시 한번 소리쳐 도움을 청할까 하는 생각이 머릿속을 스쳤다. 그 장소가 얼마나 외진 곳인지, 도움을 바랄 수 없다는 것이 얼마나 가망 없는 일인지를 나만큼 잘 아는 사람은 거의 없었지만 말이다."], ["But as he sat gloating over me, I was supported by a scornful detestation of him that sealed my lips.", "그러나 그가 앉아 나를 흡족하게 바라보는 동안, 나는 그에 대한 경멸과 혐오에 떠받쳐 입을 다물었다."], ["Above all things, I resolved that I would not entreat him, and that I would die making some last poor resistance to him.", "무엇보다도 나는 그에게 애원하지 않으리라, 그리고 그에게 보잘것없으나마 마지막 저항이라도 하면서 죽으리라 결심했다."], ["Softened as my thoughts of all the rest of men were in that dire extremity; humbly beseeching pardon, as I did, of Heaven; melted at heart, as I was, by the thought that I had taken no farewell, and never now could take farewell of those who were dear to me, or could explain myself to them, or ask for their compassion on my miserable errors,—still, if I could have killed him, even in dying, I would have done it.", "그 끔찍한 절체절명의 순간, 다른 모든 사람을 향한 내 마음이 한껏 누그러졌고, 하늘을 향해 겸허히 용서를 빌었으며, 내가 사랑하는 이들에게 작별 인사 한마디 건네지 못했고 이제 영영 작별을 고할 수도, 그들에게 나 자신을 해명할 수도, 내 비참한 과오에 대한 그들의 동정을 구할 수도 없으리라는 생각에 가슴이 미어졌으나 — 그래도 만약 그를 죽일 수만 있다면, 비록 죽어 가면서라도 나는 그를 죽였을 것이다."]], [["He had been drinking, and his eyes were red and bloodshot.", "그는 술을 마신 터라 두 눈이 벌겋게 충혈되어 있었다."], ["Around his neck was slung a tin bottle, as I had often seen his meat and drink slung about him in other days.", "목에는 양철 병이 매달려 있었는데, 지난 시절 그가 먹을 것과 마실 것을 몸에 매달고 다니던 모습을 나는 자주 보았었다."], ["He brought the bottle to his lips, and took a fiery drink from it; and I smelt the strong spirits that I saw flash into his face.", "그는 병을 입가로 가져가 독한 술을 벌컥 들이켰다. 그리고 나는 그의 얼굴로 확 끼쳐 오르는, 내 눈에도 보이던 독주의 냄새를 맡았다."]], [["“Wolf!”", "\"이리 새끼야!\""], ["said he, folding his arms again, “Old Orlick’s a-going to tell you somethink.", "그가 다시 팔짱을 끼며 말했다. \"늙은 올릭이 너한테 한 가지 일러 줄 게 있다."], ["It was you as did for your shrew sister.”", "네 그 표독스러운 누나를 결딴낸 게 바로 너였어.\""]], [["Again my mind, with its former inconceivable rapidity, had exhausted the whole subject of the attack upon my sister, her illness, and her death, before his slow and hesitating speech had formed these words.", "다시금 내 마음은 그 예전의 헤아릴 수 없는 빠르기로, 그의 느리고 머뭇거리는 말이 이런 문장을 이루기도 전에, 누나에 대한 습격과 그녀의 병환과 죽음에 관한 모든 주제를 남김없이 다 훑어 냈다."]], [["“It was you, villain,”", "\"그건 너였어, 이 악당아.\""], ["said I.", "내가 말했다."]], [["“I tell you it was your doing,—I tell you it was done through you,”", "\"네가 한 짓이라니까 — 너 때문에 벌어진 일이라니까.\""], ["he retorted, catching up the gun, and making a blow with the stock at the vacant air between us.", "그가 총을 집어 들어, 우리 사이의 빈 허공에 개머리판을 한 차례 휘두르며 받아쳤다."], ["“I come upon her from behind, as I come upon you to-night.", "\"오늘 밤 너한테 다가가듯, 난 그 여자한테 뒤에서 다가갔지."], ["I giv’ it her!", "내가 후려쳤어!"], ["I left her for dead, and if there had been a limekiln as nigh her as there is now nigh you, she shouldn’t have come to life again.", "죽은 줄 알고 내버려 뒀지. 그리고 지금 너 곁에 석회 가마가 있는 것처럼 그때 그 여자 곁에 가마가 있었다면, 그 여자는 다시 살아나지 못했을 거다."], ["But it warn’t Old Orlick as did it; it was you.", "하지만 그 짓을 한 건 늙은 올릭이 아니었어. 너였지."], ["You was favoured, and he was bullied and beat.", "넌 귀염을 받았고, 그는 괄시받고 두들겨 맞았어."], ["Old Orlick bullied and beat, eh? Now you pays for it.", "늙은 올릭이 괄시받고 두들겨 맞았다, 그거지? 이제 네가 그 값을 치르는 거야."], ["You done it; now you pays for it.”", "네가 저지른 일이니, 이제 네가 값을 치러.\""]], [["He drank again, and became more ferocious.", "그가 다시 술을 마시고는 더욱 사나워졌다."], ["I saw by his tilting of the bottle that there was no great quantity left in it.", "병을 기울이는 모양으로 보아 안에 술이 많이 남지 않은 것을 알 수 있었다."], ["I distinctly understood that he was working himself up with its contents to make an end of me.", "그가 그 안의 술기운으로 자신을 부추겨 나를 끝장내려 한다는 것을 나는 분명히 알아챘다."], ["I knew that every drop it held was a drop of my life. I knew that when I was changed into a part of the vapour that had crept towards me but a little while before, like my own warning ghost, he would do as he had done in my sister’s case,—make all haste to the town, and be seen slouching about there drinking at the alehouses.", "그 병에 든 한 방울 한 방울이 곧 내 목숨의 한 방울임을 나는 알았다. 내가, 조금 전 마치 나 자신을 경고하는 유령처럼 내 쪽으로 기어오던 그 증기의 일부로 변하고 나면, 그는 누나의 경우에 했던 그대로 — 부랴부랴 읍내로 가서 술집들을 어슬렁거리며 술을 마시는 모습을 사람들 눈에 보일 — 짓을 하리라는 것을 나는 알았다."], ["My rapid mind pursued him to the town, made a picture of the street with him in it, and contrasted its lights and life with the lonely marsh and the white vapour creeping over it, into which I should have dissolved.", "빠르게 돌아가는 내 마음은 그를 좇아 읍내까지 가서, 그가 있는 거리의 풍경을 그려 냈고, 그 불빛과 활기를, 외로운 습지와 그 위로 기어오는 흰 증기 — 내가 녹아들고 말 그 증기 — 와 대비시켰다."]], [["It was not only that I could have summed up years and years and years while he said a dozen words, but that what he did say presented pictures to me, and not mere words.", "그가 열두 마디를 말하는 동안 내가 몇 년이고 몇 년이고 또 몇 년을 다 헤아릴 수 있었을 뿐 아니라, 그가 실제로 한 말이 내게 한낱 말이 아니라 그림으로 떠올랐다."], ["In the excited and exalted state of my brain, I could not think of a place without seeing it, or of persons without seeing them.", "흥분하고 고조된 내 머릿속에서는, 어떤 장소를 떠올리면 곧 그곳이 보였고, 어떤 사람을 떠올리면 곧 그 사람이 보였다."], ["It is impossible to overstate the vividness of these images, and yet I was so intent, all the time, upon him himself,—who would not be intent on the tiger crouching to spring!—that I knew of the slightest action of his fingers.", "이 영상들의 생생함은 아무리 과장해도 지나치지 않으련만, 그러면서도 나는 줄곧 그 사람 자신에게 — 막 덮치려 웅크린 호랑이에게 누군들 정신을 쏟지 않으랴! — 어찌나 골똘했던지, 그의 손가락이 미세하게 움직이는 것까지 다 알아챌 수 있었다."]], [["When he had drunk this second time, he rose from the bench on which he sat, and pushed the table aside.", "이 두 번째 술을 들이켜고 나서, 그는 앉아 있던 긴 의자에서 일어나 탁자를 옆으로 밀쳤다."], ["Then, he took up the candle, and, shading it with his murderous hand so as to throw its light on me, stood before me, looking at me and enjoying the sight.", "그런 다음 양초를 집어 들어, 그 불빛이 내게 비치도록 살기 어린 손으로 가린 채 내 앞에 서서, 나를 바라보며 그 광경을 즐겼다."]], [["“Wolf, I’ll tell you something more.", "\"이리 새끼야, 한 가지 더 일러 주마."], ["It was Old Orlick as you tumbled over on your stairs that night.”", "그날 밤 네가 계단에서 걸려 넘어진 게 바로 늙은 올릭이었어.\""]], [["I saw the staircase with its extinguished lamps.", "나는 불이 꺼진 등이 달린 그 계단을 보았다."], ["I saw the shadows of the heavy stair-rails, thrown by the watchman’s lantern on the wall.", "야경꾼의 등불이 벽에 드리운 묵직한 계단 난간의 그림자들을 보았다."], ["I saw the rooms that I was never to see again; here, a door half open; there, a door closed; all the articles of furniture around.", "다시는 보지 못할 방들을 보았다. 이쪽에는 반쯤 열린 문 하나, 저쪽에는 닫힌 문 하나, 그리고 주위의 온갖 가구들을."]], [["“And why was Old Orlick there?", "\"그런데 늙은 올릭이 거기 왜 있었느냐?"], ["I’ll tell you something more, wolf.", "한 가지 더 일러 주마, 이리 새끼야."], ["You and her have pretty well hunted me out of this country, so far as getting a easy living in it goes, and I’ve took up with new companions, and new masters.", "너랑 그 여자가 이 고장에서, 적어도 편히 먹고사는 문제에 관한 한 나를 거의 다 몰아냈지. 그래서 나는 새 동료들, 새 주인들과 어울리게 됐어."], ["Some of ’em writes my letters when I wants ’em wrote,—do you mind?—writes my letters, wolf!", "그중 몇은 내가 편지를 쓰고 싶을 때 대신 써 준다 — 알겠냐? — 내 편지를 써 준단 말이야, 이리 새끼야!"], ["They writes fifty hands; they’re not like sneaking you, as writes but one.", "그자들은 쉰 가지 필체로 쓰지. 한 가지 필체밖에 못 쓰는 너 같은 비열한 놈과는 다르단 말이야."], ["I’ve had a firm mind and a firm will to have your life, since you was down here at your sister’s burying.", "네가 여기 네 누나 장례에 내려왔을 때부터, 나는 네 목숨을 가지겠다는 굳은 마음과 굳은 의지를 품어 왔다."], ["I han’t seen a way to get you safe, and I’ve looked arter you to know your ins and outs.", "너를 감쪽같이 처리할 방도가 보이지 않아서, 네 사정을 속속들이 알아내려고 너를 줄곧 지켜봤지."], ["For, says Old Orlick to himself, ‘Somehow or another I’ll have him!’ What!", "늙은 올릭은 속으로 이렇게 말했거든. '어떻게든 저놈을 손에 넣고 말겠다!' 그런데 이게 웬일이냐!"], ["When I looks for you, I finds your uncle Provis, eh?”", "너를 노리다 보니, 네 삼촌 프로비스를 찾아낸 게 아니냐, 응?\""]], [["Mill Pond Bank, and Chinks’s Basin, and the Old Green Copper Rope-walk, all so clear and plain!", "밀폰드 뱅크와 칭크스 만, 그리고 옛 그린 코퍼 밧줄 공장이, 모두 어쩌면 그리도 또렷하고 선명하게 떠올랐던지!"], ["Provis in his rooms, the signal whose use was over, pretty Clara, the good motherly woman, old Bill Barley on his back, all drifting by, as on the swift stream of my life fast running out to sea!", "자기 방에 있는 프로비스, 이제 쓸모를 다한 신호, 어여쁜 클라라, 그 인자한 어머니 같은 여인, 등을 대고 누운 늙은 빌 발리, 그 모든 것이, 마치 바다로 빠르게 빠져나가는 내 생명의 급류 위를 흘러가듯 스쳐 지나갔다!"]], [["“You with a uncle too!", "\"너한테 삼촌도 다 있다고!"], ["Why, I know’d you at Gargery’s when you was so small a wolf that I could have took your weazen betwixt this finger and thumb and chucked you away dead (as I’d thoughts o’ doing, odd times, when I see you loitering amongst the pollards on a Sunday), and you hadn’t found no uncles then.", "흥, 난 네가 가저리네에 있던 시절부터 너를 알았어. 그때 넌 어찌나 조그만 이리 새끼였던지, 이 손가락하고 엄지 사이에 네 모가지를 끼워서 죽은 채로 내던질 수도 있었지(일요일에 네가 가지치기한 나무들 사이를 어슬렁대는 걸 볼 때면, 가끔 그러고 싶은 마음이 들곤 했거든). 그때 넌 무슨 삼촌 따위도 못 찾아냈잖아."], ["No, not you!", "아무렴, 네까짓 게!"], ["But when Old Orlick come for to hear that your uncle Provis had most like wore the leg-iron wot Old Orlick had picked up, filed asunder, on these meshes ever so many year ago, and wot he kep by him till he dropped your sister with it, like a bullock, as he means to drop you—hey?—when he come for to hear that—hey?”", "그런데 말이야, 늙은 올릭이, 네 삼촌 프로비스가 차고 있던 그 다리 쇠고랑이 — 늙은 올릭이 여러 해 전에 이 습지에서 주워다가 줄로 끊어 놓은 그 쇠고랑, 그리고 곁에 간직해 두었다가 황소 잡듯 네 누나를 그걸로 후려쳐 쓰러뜨린, 그래서 너도 그렇게 쓰러뜨리려는 — 바로 그 쇠고랑이 거의 틀림없다는 소식을 듣게 됐을 때 — 응? — 그 소식을 듣게 됐을 때 말이야 — 응?\""]], [["In his savage taunting, he flared the candle so close at me that I turned my face aside to save it from the flame.", "그는 사납게 빈정대면서 양초를 내게 어찌나 바싹 들이댔던지, 나는 불꽃을 피하려고 얼굴을 옆으로 돌렸다."]], [["“Ah!”", "\"하!\""], ["he cried, laughing, after doing it again, “the burnt child dreads the fire!", "그가 한 번 더 그러고는 웃으며 외쳤다. \"불에 덴 아이가 불을 무서워하는 법이지!"], ["Old Orlick knowed you was burnt, Old Orlick knowed you was smuggling your uncle Provis away, Old Orlick’s a match for you and know’d you’d come to-night!", "늙은 올릭은 네가 데었다는 걸 알았어. 늙은 올릭은 네가 네 삼촌 프로비스를 몰래 빼돌리고 있다는 걸 알았지. 늙은 올릭은 네 적수가 되고도 남아서, 네가 오늘 밤 올 줄 알았던 거야!"], ["Now I’ll tell you something more, wolf, and this ends it.", "자, 한 가지 더 일러 주마, 이리 새끼야. 이걸로 끝이다."], ["There’s them that’s as good a match for your uncle Provis as Old Orlick has been for you.", "늙은 올릭이 너한테 그랬던 것만큼이나 네 삼촌 프로비스한테 만만찮은 적수가 되는 자들이 있어."], ["Let him ’ware them, when he’s lost his nevvy!", "조카를 잃거든 그자들을 조심하라고 해라!"], ["Let him ’ware them, when no man can’t find a rag of his dear relation’s clothes, nor yet a bone of his body.", "그 소중한 친척의 옷 한 조각도, 몸의 뼈 한 토막도 아무도 못 찾게 되거든 그자들을 조심하라고 해!"], ["There’s them that can’t and that won’t have Magwitch,—yes, I know the name!—alive in the same land with them, and that’s had such sure information of him when he was alive in another land, as that he couldn’t and shouldn’t leave it unbeknown and put them in danger.", "매그위치를 — 그래, 나는 그 이름을 안다! — 같은 땅에서 살아 숨 쉬게 둘 수 없고 둘 마음도 없는 자들이 있어. 그자가 다른 땅에서 살아 있을 때에도, 그자가 자기들 모르게 그 땅을 떠나 자기들을 위험에 빠뜨리는 일이 없도록, 그자에 관해 어찌나 확실한 정보를 쥐고 있던지 말이야."], ["P’raps it’s them that writes fifty hands, and that’s not like sneaking you as writes but one.", "어쩌면 그게 바로 쉰 가지 필체로 쓰는 자들, 한 가지밖에 못 쓰는 너 같은 비열한 놈과는 다른 자들이겠지."], ["’Ware Compeyson, Magwitch, and the gallows!”", "콤피슨과, 매그위치와, 교수대를 조심하라고 해!\""]], [["He flared the candle at me again, smoking my face and hair, and for an instant blinding me, and turned his powerful back as he replaced the light on the table.", "그가 다시 양초를 내게 들이대 내 얼굴과 머리카락을 그을리고는, 한순간 내 눈을 멀게 하더니, 양초를 탁자에 도로 놓으며 그 우람한 등을 돌렸다."], ["I had thought a prayer, and had been with Joe and Biddy and Herbert, before he turned towards me again.", "그가 다시 내 쪽으로 돌아서기 전에, 나는 기도를 한 자락 떠올렸고, 조와 비디와 허버트와 함께 있었다."]], [["There was a clear space of a few feet between the table and the opposite wall.", "탁자와 맞은편 벽 사이에는 몇 피트의 빈 공간이 있었다."], ["Within this space, he now slouched backwards and forwards.", "이제 그는 그 공간 안에서 어슬렁어슬렁 앞뒤로 오갔다."], ["His great strength seemed to sit stronger upon him than ever before, as he did this with his hands hanging loose and heavy at his sides, and with his eyes scowling at me. I had no grain of hope left.", "두 손을 옆구리에 늘어뜨려 묵직하게 떨군 채, 나를 사납게 노려보며 그렇게 거니는 그의 어마어마한 힘은, 전에 없이 더 단단하게 그의 몸에 자리 잡은 듯했다. 내게는 한 톨의 희망도 남아 있지 않았다."], ["Wild as my inward hurry was, and wonderful the force of the pictures that rushed by me instead of thoughts, I could yet clearly understand that, unless he had resolved that I was within a few moments of surely perishing out of all human knowledge, he would never have told me what he had told.", "내 속의 다급함이 아무리 미쳐 날뛰고, 생각 대신 스쳐 가는 영상들의 힘이 아무리 놀라웠다 한들, 나는 그래도 또렷이 이해할 수 있었다. 즉, 그가 내가 몇 순간 안에 모든 인간의 기억에서 영영 사라지리라 단정하지 않았다면, 그는 결코 자기가 들려준 그 모든 것을 내게 들려주지 않았으리라는 사실을."]], [["Of a sudden, he stopped, took the cork out of his bottle, and tossed it away. Light as it was, I heard it fall like a plummet.", "별안간 그가 멈춰 서더니, 병의 마개를 뽑아 내던졌다. 그토록 가벼운 것인데도, 나는 그것이 추처럼 떨어지는 소리를 들었다."], ["He swallowed slowly, tilting up the bottle by little and little, and now he looked at me no more.", "그는 병을 조금씩 조금씩 기울여 가며 천천히 삼켰고, 이제 더는 나를 보지 않았다."], ["The last few drops of liquor he poured into the palm of his hand, and licked up.", "마지막 몇 방울의 술을 그는 손바닥에 부어 핥아 먹었다."], ["Then, with a sudden hurry of violence and swearing horribly, he threw the bottle from him, and stooped; and I saw in his hand a stone-hammer with a long heavy handle.", "그런 다음, 갑작스레 폭력적으로 서두르며 끔찍하게 욕설을 퍼붓더니, 병을 내던지고 몸을 굽혔다. 그리고 그의 손에는 길고 묵직한 자루가 달린 돌 망치가 들려 있었다."]], [["The resolution I had made did not desert me, for, without uttering one vain word of appeal to him, I shouted out with all my might, and struggled with all my might.", "내가 세운 결심은 나를 저버리지 않았으니, 나는 그에게 애원하는 헛된 말 한마디 내뱉지 않고, 있는 힘껏 소리를 질러 대고 있는 힘껏 몸부림쳤다."], ["It was only my head and my legs that I could move, but to that extent I struggled with all the force, until then unknown, that was within me.", "움직일 수 있는 것은 머리와 다리뿐이었으나, 그 정도로나마 나는 그때까지 미처 몰랐던 내 안의 온 힘을 다해 버둥거렸다."], ["In the same instant I heard responsive shouts, saw figures and a gleam of light dash in at the door, heard voices and tumult, and saw Orlick emerge from a struggle of men, as if it were tumbling water, clear the table at a leap, and fly out into the night.", "바로 그 순간 나는 화답하는 외침 소리를 들었고, 사람 형체들과 한 줄기 불빛이 문으로 뛰어드는 것을 보았으며, 목소리들과 소란을 듣고는, 올릭이 사람들의 격투 속에서 — 마치 그것이 솟구치는 물결이라도 되는 듯 — 빠져나와, 단숨에 탁자를 뛰어넘어 밤 속으로 달아나는 것을 보았다."]], [["After a blank, I found that I was lying unbound, on the floor, in the same place, with my head on some one’s knee.", "한바탕 정신을 잃은 뒤, 나는 같은 자리 바닥에 묶이지 않은 채 누워 있고, 머리는 누군가의 무릎에 얹혀 있음을 알게 되었다."], ["My eyes were fixed on the ladder against the wall, when I came to myself,—had opened on it before my mind saw it,—and thus as I recovered consciousness, I knew that I was in the place where I had lost it.", "정신이 들었을 때 — 내 마음이 그것을 알아보기도 전에 그쪽으로 눈이 떠져 있었으니 — 내 두 눈은 벽에 세워진 그 사다리에 고정되어 있었다. 그리하여 의식을 되찾으면서, 나는 내가 의식을 잃었던 바로 그 자리에 있음을 알았다."]], [["Too indifferent at first, even to look round and ascertain who supported me, I was lying looking at the ladder, when there came between me and it a face.", "처음에는 나를 떠받치고 있는 이가 누군지 둘러보아 확인할 마음조차 없을 만큼 무기력했던 나는, 사다리를 바라보며 누워 있었는데, 그때 나와 사다리 사이로 얼굴 하나가 들어왔다."], ["The face of Trabb’s boy!", "트래브 점원 아이의 얼굴이었다!"]], [["“I think he’s all right!”", "\"이만하면 괜찮은 것 같아요!\""], ["said Trabb’s boy, in a sober voice; “but ain’t he just pale though!”", "트래브 점원 아이가 차분한 목소리로 말했다. \"근데 어찌 이리 핼쑥하담!\""]], [["At these words, the face of him who supported me looked over into mine, and I saw my supporter to be—", "이 말에, 나를 떠받치고 있던 이의 얼굴이 내 얼굴을 들여다보았고, 나는 나를 떠받친 사람이 누구인지 알아보았다 —"]], [["“Herbert! Great Heaven!”", "\"허버트! 맙소사!\""]], [["“Softly,”", "\"가만히.\""], ["said Herbert.", "허버트가 말했다."], ["“Gently, Handel.", "\"찬찬히, 핸들."], ["Don’t be too eager.”", "너무 서두르지 마.\""]], [["“And our old comrade, Startop!”", "\"그리고 우리 옛 동지, 스타톱!\""], ["I cried, as he too bent over me.", "그도 내 위로 몸을 숙이자 나는 외쳤다."]], [["“Remember what he is going to assist us in,”", "\"그가 우리를 도와 할 일이 무엇인지 기억하고.\""], ["said Herbert, “and be calm.”", "허버트가 말했다. \"마음을 가라앉혀.\""]], [["The allusion made me spring up; though I dropped again from the pain in my arm.", "그 말에 나는 벌떡 몸을 일으켰으나, 팔의 통증으로 다시 주저앉고 말았다."], ["“The time has not gone by, Herbert, has it?", "\"아직 시간이 다 가지 않았지, 허버트, 그렇지?"], ["What night is to-night?", "오늘이 무슨 요일 밤이야?"], ["How long have I been here?”", "내가 여기 얼마나 있었던 거지?\""], ["For, I had a strange and strong misgiving that I had been lying there a long time—a day and a night,—two days and nights,—more.", "내가 거기 오랫동안 — 하루 낮 하루 밤이고, 이틀 낮 이틀 밤이고, 그보다 더 오래 — 누워 있었던 게 아닐까 하는 이상하고도 강한 불안이 들었기 때문이다."]], [["“The time has not gone by.", "\"시간은 다 가지 않았어."], ["It is still Monday night.”", "아직 월요일 밤이야.\""]], [["“Thank God!”", "\"고마우신 하느님!\""]], [["“And you have all to-morrow, Tuesday, to rest in,”", "\"그리고 너한테는 내일 화요일 하루가 통째로 남아 있으니, 그때 쉬면 돼.\""], ["said Herbert.", "허버트가 말했다."], ["“But you can’t help groaning, my dear Handel.", "\"하지만 신음 소리가 절로 나오는구나, 핸들."], ["What hurt have you got?", "어디를 다친 거야?"], ["Can you stand?”", "일어설 수 있겠어?\""]], [["“Yes, yes,”", "\"응, 응.\""], ["said I, “I can walk.", "내가 말했다. \"걸을 수 있어."], ["I have no hurt but in this throbbing arm.”", "이 욱신거리는 팔 말고는 다친 데가 없어.\""]], [["They laid it bare, and did what they could.", "그들은 팔을 드러내 놓고 할 수 있는 처치를 했다."], ["It was violently swollen and inflamed, and I could scarcely endure to have it touched.", "팔은 심하게 부어올라 벌겋게 달아 있었고, 나는 손이 닿는 것조차 거의 견디지 못했다."], ["But, they tore up their handkerchiefs to make fresh bandages, and carefully replaced it in the sling, until we could get to the town and obtain some cooling lotion to put upon it.", "그러나 그들은 손수건을 찢어 새 붕대를 만들었고, 우리가 읍내에 이르러 바를 만한 시원한 물약을 구할 때까지, 조심스레 팔을 다시 삼각건에 걸어 주었다."], ["In a little while we had shut the door of the dark and empty sluice-house, and were passing through the quarry on our way back. Trabb’s boy—Trabb’s overgrown young man now—went before us with a lantern, which was the light I had seen come in at the door.", "이윽고 우리는 어둡고 텅 빈 수문집의 문을 닫고, 돌아가는 길에 채석장을 가로질러 가고 있었다. 트래브 점원 아이가 — 이제는 트래브의 덩치 큰 젊은이가 — 등불을 들고 우리 앞에 섰는데, 그것이 바로 아까 문으로 들어오는 것을 내가 보았던 그 불빛이었다."], ["But, the moon was a good two hours higher than when I had last seen the sky, and the night, though rainy, was much lighter.", "그러나 달은 내가 마지막으로 하늘을 보았을 때보다 족히 두 시간은 더 높이 떠 있었고, 밤은 비가 오는데도 한결 밝았다."], ["The white vapour of the kiln was passing from us as we went by, and as I had thought a prayer before, I thought a thanksgiving now.", "우리가 지나는 동안 가마의 흰 증기는 우리에게서 멀어져 갔고, 앞서 기도를 떠올렸던 나는 이제 감사 기도를 떠올렸다."]], [["Entreating Herbert to tell me how he had come to my rescue,—which at first he had flatly refused to do, but had insisted on my remaining quiet,—I learnt that I had in my hurry dropped the letter, open, in our chambers, where he, coming home to bring with him Startop whom he had met in the street on his way to me, found it, very soon after I was gone.", "내가 어떻게 나를 구하러 오게 되었는지 들려 달라고 허버트에게 간청하니 — 처음에 그는 딱 잘라 거절하며 내가 가만히 있어야 한다고 우겼으나 — 나는 다음과 같은 사정을 알게 되었다. 다급한 나머지 나는 우리 숙소에 그 편지를 펼친 채로 떨어뜨려 두었던 것이다. 허버트는 내게 오는 길에 거리에서 스타톱을 만나 그를 데리고 집으로 돌아왔다가, 내가 떠난 지 얼마 되지 않아 그 편지를 발견했다."], ["Its tone made him uneasy, and the more so because of the inconsistency between it and the hasty letter I had left for him.", "편지의 어조가 그를 불안하게 했고, 내가 그에게 남긴 황급한 편지와 그 내용이 어긋나는 탓에 더더욱 그러했다."], ["His uneasiness increasing instead of subsiding, after a quarter of an hour’s consideration, he set off for the coach-office with Startop, who volunteered his company, to make inquiry when the next coach went down.", "그의 불안은 가라앉기는커녕 점점 커졌고, 십오 분쯤 곰곰이 생각한 끝에, 동행을 자청한 스타톱과 함께 역마차 사무소로 가서 다음 마차가 언제 내려가는지 알아보았다."], ["Finding that the afternoon coach was gone, and finding that his uneasiness grew into positive alarm, as obstacles came in his way, he resolved to follow in a post-chaise.", "오후 마차는 이미 떠난 것을 알게 되었고, 또 일이 막힐 때마다 그의 불안이 분명한 경악으로 자라나자, 그는 역마차를 빌려 뒤따르기로 했다."], ["So he and Startop arrived at the Blue Boar, fully expecting there to find me, or tidings of me; but, finding neither, went on to Miss Havisham’s, where they lost me.", "그리하여 그와 스타톱은 블루 보어에 도착했는데, 거기서 나를, 혹은 내 소식을 찾으리라 잔뜩 기대했으나, 둘 다 찾지 못하자 미스 해비셤의 집으로 갔고, 거기서 내 행적을 놓쳐 버렸다."], ["Hereupon they went back to the hotel (doubtless at about the time when I was hearing the popular local version of my own story) to refresh themselves and to get some one to guide them out upon the marshes.", "그래서 그들은 (필시 내가 내 이야기에 관한 그 고장의 떠도는 소문을 듣고 있던 무렵에) 요기도 하고 습지로 안내해 줄 사람도 구하러 호텔로 돌아왔다."], ["Among the loungers under the Boar’s archway happened to be Trabb’s Boy,—true to his ancient habit of happening to be everywhere where he had no business,—and Trabb’s boy had seen me passing from Miss Havisham’s in the direction of my dining-place.", "보어 여관 아치문 아래 빈둥거리던 자들 가운데 마침 트래브 점원 아이가 — 제 볼일도 없는 데마다 으레 나타나곤 하는 그 오랜 버릇에 충실하게 — 끼어 있었고, 트래브 점원 아이는 내가 미스 해비셤의 집에서 식사하기로 한 곳 쪽으로 지나가는 것을 보았던 터였다."], ["Thus Trabb’s boy became their guide, and with him they went out to the sluice-house, though by the town way to the marshes, which I had avoided.", "그리하여 트래브 점원 아이가 그들의 길잡이가 되었고, 그들은 그를 데리고 수문집으로 나섰다 — 다만 내가 피했던, 읍내를 거쳐 습지로 가는 길로 갔다."], ["Now, as they went along, Herbert reflected, that I might, after all, have been brought there on some genuine and serviceable errand tending to Provis’s safety, and, bethinking himself that in that case interruption must be mischievous, left his guide and Startop on the edge of the quarry, and went on by himself, and stole round the house two or three times, endeavouring to ascertain whether all was right within. As he could hear nothing but indistinct sounds of one deep rough voice (this was while my mind was so busy), he even at last began to doubt whether I was there, when suddenly I cried out loudly, and he answered the cries, and rushed in, closely followed by the other two.", "그런데 길을 가는 동안, 허버트는 어쩌면 내가 결국 프로비스의 안전과 관련된 어떤 진짜 긴요한 용무로 거기 불려 왔을지도 모른다는 데 생각이 미쳤고, 만약 그렇다면 끼어드는 것이 해가 되리라 헤아려, 길잡이와 스타톱을 채석장 가장자리에 남겨 두고 혼자 나아가, 안에서 모든 일이 무탈한지 알아보고자 두세 차례 집 둘레를 살그머니 돌았다. 그가 들을 수 있는 것이라곤 거칠고 굵은 목소리 하나의 어렴풋한 소리뿐이었기에(이것은 내 마음이 그토록 분주히 돌아가던 동안의 일이었다), 그는 마침내 내가 거기 있기는 한 건지 의심하기 시작하던 차에, 별안간 내가 큰 소리로 외쳤고, 그가 그 외침에 화답하며 안으로 뛰어들었으며, 나머지 두 사람이 그 뒤를 바짝 따랐던 것이다."]], [["When I told Herbert what had passed within the house, he was for our immediately going before a magistrate in the town, late at night as it was, and getting out a warrant. But, I had already considered that such a course, by detaining us there, or binding us to come back, might be fatal to Provis.", "내가 집 안에서 벌어진 일을 허버트에게 들려주자, 그는 밤이 늦었음에도 당장 읍내의 치안판사에게 가서 영장을 발부받자고 했다. 그러나 나는 이미, 그렇게 하면 우리가 거기 발이 묶이거나 다시 돌아와야 할 처지가 되어, 프로비스에게 치명적일 수 있다는 점을 헤아려 둔 터였다."], ["There was no gainsaying this difficulty, and we relinquished all thoughts of pursuing Orlick at that time.", "이 난점은 부인할 도리가 없었고, 그래서 우리는 그때로서는 올릭을 추적할 생각을 모두 단념했다."], ["For the present, under the circumstances, we deemed it prudent to make rather light of the matter to Trabb’s boy; who, I am convinced, would have been much affected by disappointment, if he had known that his intervention saved me from the limekiln.", "당장은 사정이 사정인 만큼, 우리는 그 일을 트래브 점원 아이에게 가벼운 일인 양 둘러대는 편이 현명하다고 여겼다. 만약 자기가 끼어든 덕에 내가 석회 가마에서 구해졌다는 것을 그 아이가 알았더라면, 분명 크게 실망해 어지간히 마음을 다쳤을 것이다."], ["Not that Trabb’s boy was of a malignant nature, but that he had too much spare vivacity, and that it was in his constitution to want variety and excitement at anybody’s expense.", "트래브 점원 아이가 악의 있는 성품이어서가 아니라, 남아도는 활기가 너무 많아서, 누구를 희생시켜서라도 변화와 자극을 갈구하는 것이 그 천성이었기 때문이다."], ["When we parted, I presented him with two guineas (which seemed to meet his views), and told him that I was sorry ever to have had an ill opinion of him (which made no impression on him at all).", "헤어질 때 나는 그에게 2기니를 건넸고(그것은 그의 바람에 들어맞는 듯했다), 한때 너를 나쁘게 여겼던 것이 미안하다고 말했다(이 말은 그에게 아무런 감흥도 주지 못했다)."]], [["Wednesday being so close upon us, we determined to go back to London that night, three in the post-chaise; the rather, as we should then be clear away before the night’s adventure began to be talked of.", "수요일이 이토록 코앞에 닥쳤으므로, 우리는 셋이 역마차를 타고 그날 밤 런던으로 돌아가기로 마음먹었다. 그래야 그날 밤의 소동이 입에 오르내리기 전에 빠져나갈 수 있으리라는 이유도 있었다."], ["Herbert got a large bottle of stuff for my arm; and by dint of having this stuff dropped over it all the night through, I was just able to bear its pain on the journey.", "허버트는 내 팔에 바를 큰 약병 하나를 구해 왔고, 밤새도록 그 약을 팔에 떨어뜨려 준 덕에, 나는 여정 동안 그 고통을 가까스로 견딜 수 있었다."], ["It was daylight when we reached the Temple, and I went at once to bed, and lay in bed all day.", "우리가 템플에 다다랐을 때는 날이 밝아 있었고, 나는 곧장 잠자리에 들어 하루 종일 누워 있었다."]], [["My terror, as I lay there, of falling ill, and being unfitted for to-morrow, was so besetting, that I wonder it did not disable me of itself.", "거기 누워 있는 동안, 병이 나서 내일 일을 감당하지 못하게 되리라는 두려움이 어찌나 끈질기게 나를 따라다녔던지, 그것이 그 자체만으로도 나를 쓰러뜨리지 않은 게 신기할 정도다."], ["It would have done so, pretty surely, in conjunction with the mental wear and tear I had suffered, but for the unnatural strain upon me that to-morrow was.", "내가 겪은 정신적 소모와 맞물렸다면, 그 두려움은 거의 틀림없이 나를 쓰러뜨렸을 것이다. 그러나 내일이라는 것이 내게 가한 그 비상한 긴장이 그것을 막아 주었다."], ["So anxiously looked forward to, charged with such consequences, its results so impenetrably hidden, though so near.", "그토록 애타게 기다려 온 내일, 그토록 중대한 결과를 품은 내일, 그토록 가까이 다가왔으면서도 그 결말은 그토록 헤아릴 수 없게 감추어진 내일이."]], [["No precaution could have been more obvious than our refraining from communication with him that day; yet this again increased my restlessness.", "그날 그와 연락하기를 삼가는 것보다 더 당연한 조처는 없었다. 그러나 이것이 또 내 불안을 더했다."], ["I started at every footstep and every sound, believing that he was discovered and taken, and this was the messenger to tell me so.", "나는 발소리 하나, 소리 하나에도 깜짝깜짝 놀라며, 그가 발각되어 잡혔고 이것이 내게 그 소식을 전하러 온 전령이라고 믿었다."], ["I persuaded myself that I knew he was taken; that there was something more upon my mind than a fear or a presentiment; that the fact had occurred, and I had a mysterious knowledge of it.", "나는 그가 잡혔음을 내가 안다고, 두려움이나 예감 이상의 무언가가 내 마음을 짓누르고 있다고, 그 일이 실제로 일어났고 내게 그것에 대한 알 수 없는 앎이 있다고 스스로를 다그쳐 믿었다."], ["As the days wore on, and no ill news came, as the day closed in and darkness fell, my overshadowing dread of being disabled by illness before to-morrow morning altogether mastered me.", "날이 저물어 가도 흉한 소식이 오지 않고, 하루해가 기울어 어둠이 내리자, 내일 아침이 오기 전에 병으로 쓰러지고 말리라는 그 무겁게 짓누르는 두려움이 나를 완전히 사로잡았다."], ["My burning arm throbbed, and my burning head throbbed, and I fancied I was beginning to wander.", "화끈거리는 팔이 욱신거리고, 화끈거리는 머리가 욱신거렸으며, 나는 내가 헛소리를 하기 시작하는 것이 아닌가 싶었다."], ["I counted up to high numbers, to make sure of myself, and repeated passages that I knew in prose and verse.", "나는 정신을 다잡으려고 큰 수까지 헤아렸고, 산문이며 운문이며 외고 있던 구절들을 되뇌었다."], ["It happened sometimes that in the mere escape of a fatigued mind, I dozed for some moments or forgot; then I would say to myself with a start, “Now it has come, and I am turning delirious!”", "더러는 지친 마음이 잠시 빠져나갈 틈을 타, 몇 순간 깜빡 졸거나 깜빡 잊기도 했다. 그러면 나는 흠칫 놀라며 스스로에게 말하곤 했다. \"이제 그게 닥쳐온 거야, 나는 헛소리에 빠져들고 있어!\""]], [["They kept me very quiet all day, and kept my arm constantly dressed, and gave me cooling drinks.", "그들은 온종일 나를 아주 조용히 있게 했고, 끊임없이 내 팔에 새 처치를 해 주었으며, 시원한 마실 것을 주었다."], ["Whenever I fell asleep, I awoke with the notion I had had in the sluice-house, that a long time had elapsed and the opportunity to save him was gone.", "잠이 들 때마다 나는 수문집에서 가졌던 그 생각, 즉 오랜 시간이 흘러 그를 구할 기회가 사라져 버렸다는 생각과 함께 깨어났다."], ["About midnight I got out of bed and went to Herbert, with the conviction that I had been asleep for four-and-twenty hours, and that Wednesday was past.", "자정 무렵 나는 침대에서 나와, 내가 스물네 시간이나 잠들어 있었고 수요일이 지나가 버렸다고 굳게 믿은 채 허버트에게로 갔다."], ["It was the last self-exhausting effort of my fretfulness, for after that I slept soundly.", "그것이 내 안달이 스스로를 소진시킨 마지막 발버둥이었으니, 그 뒤로 나는 깊이 잠들었다."]], [["Wednesday morning was dawning when I looked out of window.", "창밖을 내다보았을 때 수요일 아침이 밝아 오고 있었다."], ["The winking lights upon the bridges were already pale, the coming sun was like a marsh of fire on the horizon.", "다리 위에서 깜박이던 불빛들은 이미 희미해졌고, 떠오르는 해는 지평선 위의 불타는 습지 같았다."], ["The river, still dark and mysterious, was spanned by bridges that were turning coldly grey, with here and there at top a warm touch from the burning in the sky.", "강은 여전히 어둡고 신비로웠으며, 차갑게 잿빛으로 물들어 가는 다리들이 강을 가로질렀는데, 그 꼭대기에는 여기저기 하늘에서 타오르는 불의 따스한 빛이 한 점씩 닿아 있었다."], ["As I looked along the clustered roofs, with church-towers and spires shooting into the unusually clear air, the sun rose up, and a veil seemed to be drawn from the river, and millions of sparkles burst out upon its waters.", "유난히 맑은 대기 속으로 교회 탑들과 첨탑들이 솟아오른, 다닥다닥 모인 지붕들을 따라 내가 바라보고 있노라니, 해가 떠올랐고, 강에서 베일이 한 겹 벗겨지는 듯하더니 수백만 개의 반짝임이 그 물 위로 한꺼번에 터져 나왔다."], ["From me too, a veil seemed to be drawn, and I felt strong and well.", "나에게서도 베일이 한 겹 벗겨지는 듯했고, 나는 몸이 튼튼하고 거뜬해진 것을 느꼈다."]], [["Herbert lay asleep in his bed, and our old fellow-student lay asleep on the sofa.", "허버트는 침대에서 잠들어 있었고, 우리의 옛 동창은 소파에서 잠들어 있었다."], ["I could not dress myself without help; but I made up the fire, which was still burning, and got some coffee ready for them.", "나는 누구의 도움 없이는 옷을 입을 수 없었으나, 아직 타고 있던 불을 지피고 그들을 위해 커피를 좀 끓여 두었다."], ["In good time they too started up strong and well, and we admitted the sharp morning air at the windows, and looked at the tide that was still flowing towards us.", "때맞춰 그들도 튼튼하고 거뜬한 몸으로 벌떡 일어났고, 우리는 창문으로 상쾌한 아침 공기를 들이고는, 여전히 우리 쪽으로 밀려오고 있는 밀물을 바라보았다."]], [["“When it turns at nine o’clock,”", "\"아홉 시에 물이 바뀌거든.\""], ["said Herbert, cheerfully, “look out for us, and stand ready, you over there at Mill Pond Bank!”", "허버트가 쾌활하게 말했다. \"우리를 잘 살펴보고, 채비를 갖추고 있게나, 저기 밀폰드 뱅크에 있는 자네 말일세!\""]], [["# Chapter LIV.", "# Chapter LIV."]], [["It was one of those March days when the sun shines hot and the wind blows cold: when it is summer in the light, and winter in the shade.", "삼월의 어느 날이었다. 햇볕은 뜨겁게 내리쬐는데 바람은 차갑게 불어, 빛 속은 여름이요 그늘 속은 겨울인 그런 날 가운데 하나였다."], ["We had our pea-coats with us, and I took a bag.", "우리는 두꺼운 모직 외투를 챙겼고, 나는 가방 하나를 들었다."], ["Of all my worldly possessions I took no more than the few necessaries that filled the bag.", "내가 가진 세간 가운데 그 가방을 채울 몇 가지 필수품 외에는 아무것도 가져가지 않았다."], ["Where I might go, what I might do, or when I might return, were questions utterly unknown to me; nor did I vex my mind with them, for it was wholly set on Provis’s safety.", "내가 어디로 갈지, 무엇을 할지, 또 언제 돌아올지는 내게 전혀 알 수 없는 물음이었다. 그러나 나는 그런 물음으로 마음을 어지럽히지 않았으니, 내 마음은 온전히 프로비스의 안전에만 쏠려 있었던 것이다."], ["I only wondered for the passing moment, as I stopped at the door and looked back, under what altered circumstances I should next see those rooms, if ever.", "다만 나는 문 앞에 멈춰 서서 뒤를 돌아보며, 다음번에 — 만약 다시 보게 된다면 — 어떤 달라진 처지에서 이 방들을 보게 될까 하고 스쳐 가는 순간 동안만 궁금해했을 뿐이다."]], [["We loitered down to the Temple stairs, and stood loitering there, as if we were not quite decided to go upon the water at all.", "우리는 어슬렁어슬렁 템플 선착장으로 내려가, 아예 물 위로 나설 결심이 서지 않은 사람들처럼 그곳에서 한참을 서성였다."], ["Of course, I had taken care that the boat should be ready and everything in order.", "물론 나는 배가 준비되어 있고 모든 것이 제자리에 갖추어져 있도록 미리 손을 써 두었다."], ["After a little show of indecision, which there were none to see but the two or three amphibious creatures belonging to our Temple stairs, we went on board and cast off; Herbert in the bow, I steering.", "우리 템플 선착장에 딸린 두세 명의 물뭍 양서류 같은 사내들 말고는 보는 이도 없는데, 잠시 결심이 서지 않는 척 시늉을 한 뒤, 우리는 배에 올라 줄을 풀었다. 허버트가 뱃머리에, 내가 키를 잡았다."], ["It was then about high-water,—half-past eight.", "그때가 만조 무렵, 여덟 시 반쯤이었다."]], [["Our plan was this.", "우리 계획은 이러했다."], ["The tide, beginning to run down at nine, and being with us until three, we intended still to creep on after it had turned, and row against it until dark.", "아홉 시에 썰물이 시작되어 세 시까지 우리 쪽으로 흘러가니, 물때가 바뀐 뒤에도 계속 슬금슬금 나아가다가 어두워질 때까지 물살을 거슬러 노를 저을 작정이었다."], ["We should then be well in those long reaches below Gravesend, between Kent and Essex, where the river is broad and solitary, where the water-side inhabitants are very few, and where lone public-houses are scattered here and there, of which we could choose one for a resting-place.", "그렇게 하면 그레이브젠드 아래쪽, 켄트와 에식스 사이로 길게 뻗은 그 물줄기에 이를 터였다. 그곳은 강이 넓고 한적하며, 강가에 사는 이도 매우 드물고, 외딴 선술집들이 여기저기 흩어져 있어 그중 하나를 골라 쉴 곳으로 삼을 수 있었다."], ["There, we meant to lie by all night.", "거기서 우리는 밤새 머물 작정이었다."], ["The steamer for Hamburg and the steamer for Rotterdam would start from London at about nine on Thursday morning.", "함부르크행 증기선과 로테르담행 증기선은 목요일 아침 아홉 시쯤 런던에서 출발할 예정이었다."], ["We should know at what time to expect them, according to where we were, and would hail the first; so that, if by any accident we were not taken abroad, we should have another chance.", "우리가 어디쯤 있는가에 따라 그 배들을 몇 시쯤 만나리라는 것을 알 수 있을 터였고, 먼저 오는 배에 손짓해 부를 작정이었다. 그래서 혹여 무슨 일이 생겨 먼저 배에 오르지 못하더라도 또 한 번의 기회가 남는 셈이었다."], ["We knew the distinguishing marks of each vessel.", "우리는 두 배를 구별하는 표지를 알고 있었다."]], [["The relief of being at last engaged in the execution of the purpose was so great to me that I felt it difficult to realise the condition in which I had been a few hours before.", "마침내 그 목적을 실행에 옮기는 일에 착수했다는 안도감이 어찌나 컸던지, 나는 불과 몇 시간 전에 내가 어떤 상태에 있었는지조차 실감하기 어려웠다."], ["The crisp air, the sunlight, the movement on the river, and the moving river itself,—the road that ran with us, seeming to sympathise with us, animate us, and encourage us on,—freshened me with new hope.", "상쾌한 공기, 햇빛, 강 위의 움직임, 그리고 흐르는 강물 그 자체 — 우리와 나란히 달리며 우리에게 공감하고, 우리를 북돋우고, 앞으로 나아가라 격려해 주는 듯한 그 길 — 그것들이 나를 새로운 희망으로 새롭게 했다."], ["I felt mortified to be of so little use in the boat; but, there were few better oarsmen than my two friends, and they rowed with a steady stroke that was to last all day.", "배 안에서 내가 거의 쓸모가 없다는 사실에 부끄러움을 느꼈지만, 내 두 친구보다 나은 노잡이는 드물었고, 그들은 하루 종일 이어 갈 만한 한결같은 노질로 배를 저었다."]], [["At that time, the steam-traffic on the Thames was far below its present extent, and watermen’s boats were far more numerous.", "그 시절 템스강의 증기선 통행량은 지금에 비해 훨씬 적었고, 뱃사공의 거룻배는 훨씬 많았다."], ["Of barges, sailing colliers, and coasting-traders, there were perhaps, as many as now; but of steam-ships, great and small, not a tithe or a twentieth part so many.", "바지선이며 석탄 운반 범선이며 연안 무역선은 아마 지금만큼은 있었을 것이다. 그러나 크고 작은 증기선은 지금의 십분의 일도, 이십분의 일도 되지 않았다."], ["Early as it was, there were plenty of scullers going here and there that morning, and plenty of barges dropping down with the tide; the navigation of the river between bridges, in an open boat, was a much easier and commoner matter in those days than it is in these; and we went ahead among many skiffs and wherries briskly.", "이른 시각이었음에도 그날 아침 여기저기 노 젓는 작은 배들이 잔뜩 오갔고, 물때를 따라 내려가는 바지선도 수두룩했다. 다리와 다리 사이를 트인 배로 오가는 일은 요즘보다 그 시절에 훨씬 수월하고 흔한 일이었으니, 우리는 수많은 작은 배와 나룻배 사이를 활기차게 헤치고 나아갔다."]], [["Old London Bridge was soon passed, and old Billingsgate Market with its oyster-boats and Dutchmen, and the White Tower and Traitor’s Gate, and we were in among the tiers of shipping.", "이내 옛 런던 다리를 지났고, 굴 까는 배들과 네덜란드인들로 북적이는 옛 빌링스게이트 시장과 화이트 타워와 반역자의 문을 지나, 우리는 줄지어 늘어선 배들 사이로 들어섰다."], ["Here were the Leith, Aberdeen, and Glasgow steamers, loading and unloading goods, and looking immensely high out of the water as we passed alongside; here, were colliers by the score and score, with the coal-whippers plunging off stages on deck, as counterweights to measures of coal swinging up, which were then rattled over the side into barges; here, at her moorings was to-morrow’s steamer for Rotterdam, of which we took good notice; and here to-morrow’s for Hamburg, under whose bowsprit we crossed.", "거기에는 리스, 애버딘, 글래스고로 가는 증기선들이 화물을 싣고 부리고 있었는데, 우리가 옆을 지날 때 보니 물 위로 어마어마하게 높이 솟아 있었다. 거기에는 석탄선이 수십 척, 또 수십 척이나 있었고, 갑판의 발판에서 석탄 운반꾼들이 뛰어내리며 위로 끌어올려지는 석탄 무게의 평형추 노릇을 했는데, 그 석탄은 곧 뱃전 너머 바지선으로 덜커덕덜커덕 쏟아졌다. 거기, 정박줄에 매여 있는 것이 내일 로테르담으로 떠날 증기선이라, 우리는 그것을 눈여겨보았다."], ["And now I, sitting in the stern, could see, with a faster beating heart, Mill Pond Bank and Mill Pond stairs.", "그리고 거기, 우리가 그 제1사장(斜檣) 아래로 지나간 것이 내일 함부르크로 떠날 배였다. 이제 고물에 앉은 나는, 한결 빨라진 가슴의 고동과 함께 밀폰드 둑과 밀폰드 선착장을 볼 수 있었다."]], [["“Is he there?” said Herbert.", "\"그분이 거기 계셔?\""], ["“Not yet.”", "허버트가 말했다."]], [["“Right! He was not to come down till he saw us.", "\"아직 안 보여.\" \"좋아! 우리를 보기 전에는 내려오지 않기로 했으니까."], ["Can you see his signal?”", "신호가 보여?\""]], [["“Not well from here; but I think I see it.—Now I see him!", "\"여기서는 잘 안 보이는데, 보이는 것 같기도 해. — 이제 보인다!"], ["Pull both.", "자, 둘 다 저어."], ["Easy, Herbert. Oars!”", "천천히, 허버트. 노 멈춰!\""]], [["We touched the stairs lightly for a single moment, and he was on board, and we were off again.", "우리는 한순간 선착장에 가볍게 닿았고, 그분이 배에 올랐으며, 우리는 다시 출발했다."], ["He had a boat-cloak with him, and a black canvas bag; and he looked as like a river-pilot as my heart could have wished.", "그분은 뱃사람용 망토와 검은 캔버스 가방을 지니고 있었고, 내 마음이 바랄 수 있는 만큼 영락없는 강물 도선사처럼 보였다."]], [["“Dear boy!”", "\"얘야!\""], ["he said, putting his arm on my shoulder, as he took his seat.", "그분이 자리에 앉으며 내 어깨에 팔을 올리고 말했다."], ["“Faithful dear boy, well done.", "\"충실한 내 아들아, 잘했다."], ["Thankye, thankye!”", "고맙다, 고마워!\""]], [["Again among the tiers of shipping, in and out, avoiding rusty chain-cables frayed hempen hawsers and bobbing buoys, sinking for the moment floating broken baskets, scattering floating chips of wood and shaving, cleaving floating scum of coal, in and out, under the figure-head of the John of Sunderland making a speech to the winds (as is done by many Johns), and the Betsy of Yarmouth with a firm formality of bosom and her knobby eyes starting two inches out of her head; in and out, hammers going in ship-builders’ yards, saws going at timber, clashing engines going at things unknown, pumps going in leaky ships, capstans going, ships going out to sea, and unintelligible sea-creatures roaring curses over the bulwarks at respondent lightermen, in and out,—out at last upon the clearer river, where the ships’ boys might take their fenders in, no longer fishing in troubled waters with them over the side, and where the festooned sails might fly out to the wind.", "다시 줄지어 늘어선 배들 사이로, 들어갔다 나오며, 녹슨 사슬 닻줄과 닳아 해진 삼밧줄과 까딱거리는 부표를 피하고, 떠다니는 부서진 바구니를 잠깐씩 가라앉히고, 떠다니는 나뭇조각과 대팻밥을 흩뜨리고, 떠다니는 석탄 찌끼를 가르며, 들어갔다 나오며, 바람을 향해 일장 연설을 하는 (수많은 존이 그러하듯) 선더랜드의 존호의 선수상 아래로, 그리고 가슴팍을 의젓하고 위엄 있게 내밀고 두 눈이 머리에서 두 치나 튀어나온 듯 불거진 야머스의 벳시호 아래로, 들어갔다 나오며, 조선소마다 망치 소리가 울리고, 톱이 목재를 켜고, 알 수 없는 무언가를 두드리는 기계들이 쟁그랑거리고, 물 새는 배에서 펌프가 돌아가고, 캡스턴이 돌아가고, 배들이 바다로 나아가고, 알아들을 수 없는 바다 짐승 같은 사내들이 뱃전 너머로 거룻배꾼들에게 저주를 퍼붓고 거룻배꾼들이 맞받아치는 가운데, 들어갔다 나오며, — 마침내 더 맑은 강 위로 나왔다. 거기서는 갑판 소년들이 뱃전 너머에 늘어뜨려 흙탕물 속에서 고기를 낚는 짓을 그만두고 방현재를 거두어들일 수 있었고, 꽃줄처럼 늘어진 돛들이 바람을 받아 활짝 펼쳐질 수 있었다."]], [["At the stairs where we had taken him abroad, and ever since, I had looked warily for any token of our being suspected.", "우리가 그분을 태운 선착장에서부터, 그리고 그 후로 줄곧, 나는 우리가 의심받고 있다는 어떤 낌새라도 있는지 조심스레 살폈다."], ["I had seen none. We certainly had not been, and at that time as certainly we were not either attended or followed by any boat.", "아무것도 보이지 않았다. 우리는 분명 미행당하지 않았으며, 그때 역시 분명 어떤 배에 따라붙임을 당하거나 미행당하지 않았다."], ["If we had been waited on by any boat, I should have run in to shore, and have obliged her to go on, or to make her purpose evident.", "만약 어떤 배가 우리를 노리고 있었다면, 나는 배를 기슭에 댄 뒤 그 배가 먼저 지나가게 하거나 그 의도를 분명히 드러내도록 했을 것이다."], ["But we held our own without any appearance of molestation.", "그러나 우리는 어떤 방해의 기색도 없이 우리 길을 지켰다."]], [["He had his boat-cloak on him, and looked, as I have said, a natural part of the scene.", "그분은 뱃사람용 망토를 두르고 있어, 앞서 말했듯 풍경 속에 자연스레 녹아드는 한 부분처럼 보였다."], ["It was remarkable (but perhaps the wretched life he had led accounted for it) that he was the least anxious of any of us.", "(어쩌면 그분이 살아온 비참한 삶이 그 까닭일지도 모르나) 그분이 우리 가운데 가장 덜 불안해한다는 것은 주목할 만한 일이었다."], ["He was not indifferent, for he told me that he hoped to live to see his gentleman one of the best of gentlemen in a foreign country; he was not disposed to be passive or resigned, as I understood it; but he had no notion of meeting danger half way.", "그분이 무심한 것은 아니었으니, 자기 신사가 머나먼 나라에서 가장 훌륭한 신사 가운데 하나가 되는 것을 살아서 보고 싶다고 내게 말했기 때문이다. 또 내가 보기에 그분은 수동적이거나 체념하려는 사람도 아니었다. 다만 위험을 미리 마중하러 나갈 생각이 없을 뿐이었다."], ["When it came upon him, he confronted it, but it must come before he troubled himself.", "위험이 닥쳐오면 그분은 그것에 맞섰으나, 위험은 그분이 스스로를 괴롭히기에 앞서 먼저 와야만 하는 것이었다."]], [["“If you knowed, dear boy,”", "\"얘야, 네가 안다면 말이다.\""], ["he said to me, “what it is to sit here alonger my dear boy and have my smoke, arter having been day by day betwixt four walls, you’d envy me.", "그분이 내게 말했다. \"날이면 날마다 사면이 벽으로 둘러싸인 곳에 갇혀 있다가, 이렇게 내 사랑하는 아들 곁에 앉아 담배를 피운다는 게 어떤 건지 안다면, 너는 나를 부러워할 거다."], ["But you don’t know what it is.”", "하지만 너는 그게 어떤 건지 모른다.\""]], [["“I think I know the delights of freedom,”", "\"저도 자유의 기쁨은 좀 안다고 생각해요.\""], ["I answered.", "내가 대답했다."]], [["“Ah,” said he, shaking his head gravely.", "\"아.\" 그분이 진지하게 고개를 저으며 말했다."], ["“But you don’t know it equal to me.", "\"하지만 너는 나만큼은 모른다."], ["You must have been under lock and key, dear boy, to know it equal to me,—but I ain’t a-going to be low.”", "나만큼 알려면, 얘야, 자물쇠와 열쇠에 갇혀 봤어야 한다. — 하지만 처량한 소리는 하지 않으마.\""]], [["It occurred to me as inconsistent, that, for any mastering idea, he should have endangered his freedom, and even his life.", "어떤 사로잡힌 생각 하나 때문에 그분이 제 자유와, 심지어 제 목숨까지 위태롭게 했다는 것은 앞뒤가 맞지 않는 일처럼 느껴졌다."], ["But I reflected that perhaps freedom without danger was too much apart from all the habit of his existence to be to him what it would be to another man.", "그러나 어쩌면 위험 없는 자유라는 것은, 그분이 살아온 모든 습성과 너무나 동떨어진 것이어서, 다른 사람에게라면 의미를 지닐 그것이 그분에게는 그렇지 않았으리라고 나는 곰곰이 생각했다."], ["I was not far out, since he said, after smoking a little:—", "내 짐작은 크게 빗나가지 않았으니, 그분이 잠시 담배를 피운 뒤 이렇게 말했기 때문이다. —"]], [["“You see, dear boy, when I was over yonder, t’other side the world, I was always a looking to this side; and it come flat to be there, for all I was a growing rich.", "\"있잖니, 얘야, 내가 저 너머, 세상 반대편에 있을 때, 나는 늘 이쪽을 바라보고 있었다. 그리고 부자가 되어 가는데도 거기 있는 게 영 시들했지."], ["Everybody knowed Magwitch, and Magwitch could come, and Magwitch could go, and nobody’s head would be troubled about him.", "누구나 매그위치를 알았고, 매그위치는 오든 가든, 누구도 그자에 대해 머리를 썩이지 않았다."], ["They ain’t so easy concerning me here, dear boy,—wouldn’t be, leastwise, if they knowed where I was.”", "그런데 여기서는 나를 그렇게 마음 편히 내버려 두지 않아, 얘야. — 적어도 내가 어디 있는지 알면 그렇지 않을 거란 말이다.\""]], [["“If all goes well,”", "\"모든 일이 잘 풀리면,\""], ["said I, “you will be perfectly free and safe again within a few hours.”", "내가 말했다. \"몇 시간 안에 다시 완전히 자유롭고 안전해지실 거예요.\""]], [["“Well,”", "\"그래.\""], ["he returned, drawing a long breath, “I hope so.”", "그분이 긴 숨을 내쉬며 대꾸했다. \"그러길 바란다.\""]], [["“And think so?”", "\"그렇게 생각도 하시고요?\""]], [["He dipped his hand in the water over the boat’s gunwale, and said, smiling with that softened air upon him which was not new to me:—", "그분이 뱃전 너머 물에 손을 담그더니, 내게 낯설지 않은 그 부드러워진 기색을 띠고 미소 지으며 말했다. —"]], [["“Ay, I s’pose I think so, dear boy.", "\"그래, 그렇게 생각하는가 보다, 얘야."], ["We’d be puzzled to be more quiet and easy-going than we are at present.", "지금 우리보다 더 조용하고 느긋하기도 어렵겠지."], ["But—it’s a flowing so soft and pleasant through the water, p’raps, as makes me think it—I was a thinking through my smoke just then, that we can no more see to the bottom of the next few hours than we can see to the bottom of this river what I catches hold of.", "하지만 — 어쩌면 물 위로 이리 부드럽고 기분 좋게 흘러가는 탓에 그런 생각이 드는지도 모르겠다만 — 방금 담배를 피우면서 이런 생각을 했다. 우리가 앞으로 닥칠 몇 시간의 바닥을 들여다볼 수 없는 게, 내가 손으로 움켜쥐는 이 강물의 바닥을 들여다볼 수 없는 거나 매한가지라고 말이다."], ["Nor yet we can’t no more hold their tide than I can hold this.", "게다가 그 시간의 물때를 붙잡아 둘 수 없는 게, 내가 이 물을 붙잡아 둘 수 없는 거나 똑같지."], ["And it’s run through my fingers and gone, you see!” holding up his dripping hand.", "봐라, 손가락 사이로 다 흘러가 버렸잖니!\" 그분이 물방울 듣는 손을 들어 보이며 말했다."]], [["“But for your face I should think you were a little despondent,” said I.", "\"그 얼굴만 아니라면 저는 아저씨가 좀 풀이 죽으셨나 생각했을 거예요.\" 내가 말했다."]], [["“Not a bit on it, dear boy!", "\"천만에, 얘야!"], ["It comes of flowing on so quiet, and of that there rippling at the boat’s head making a sort of a Sunday tune.", "이렇게 조용히 흘러가는 데다, 저 뱃머리에서 찰랑거리는 소리가 무슨 주일 찬송가 가락 같아서 그런 거다."], ["Maybe I’m a growing a trifle old besides.”", "어쩌면 내가 조금 늙어 가는 탓도 있겠지.\""]], [["He put his pipe back in his mouth with an undisturbed expression of face, and sat as composed and contented as if we were already out of England.", "그분은 흐트러짐 없는 표정으로 다시 파이프를 입에 물고, 우리가 이미 영국을 벗어나기라도 한 양 침착하고 흡족하게 앉아 있었다."], ["Yet he was as submissive to a word of advice as if he had been in constant terror; for, when we ran ashore to get some bottles of beer into the boat, and he was stepping out, I hinted that I thought he would be safest where he was, and he said. “Do you, dear boy?”", "그러면서도 그분은 끊임없이 두려움에 떨고 있는 사람처럼 한마디 충고에도 고분고분했다. 우리가 맥주 몇 병을 배에 실으려고 기슭에 댔을 때, 그분이 내리려 하기에 나는 그분이 있던 자리에 그대로 있는 편이 가장 안전할 것 같다고 넌지시 말했고, 그러자 그분은 \"그러냐, 얘야?\""], ["and quietly sat down again.", "하더니 조용히 다시 앉았다."]], [["The air felt cold upon the river, but it was a bright day, and the sunshine was very cheering.", "강 위의 공기는 차갑게 느껴졌지만 화창한 날이었고, 햇볕은 무척 기운을 북돋아 주었다."], ["The tide ran strong, I took care to lose none of it, and our steady stroke carried us on thoroughly well.", "물때가 거세게 흘러, 나는 그 한 자락도 놓치지 않도록 주의했고, 우리의 한결같은 노질은 우리를 더없이 잘 실어 날랐다."], ["By imperceptible degrees, as the tide ran out, we lost more and more of the nearer woods and hills, and dropped lower and lower between the muddy banks, but the tide was yet with us when we were off Gravesend.", "썰물이 빠져나가면서, 알아챌 수 없을 만큼 조금씩, 우리는 가까운 숲과 언덕을 점점 더 잃어 가며 진흙 둑 사이로 점점 더 낮게 내려갔지만, 그레이브젠드를 벗어날 때까지도 물때는 아직 우리 편이었다."], ["As our charge was wrapped in his cloak, I purposely passed within a boat or two’s length of the floating Custom House, and so out to catch the stream, alongside of two emigrant ships, and under the bows of a large transport with troops on the forecastle looking down at us.", "우리의 화물 같은 그분이 망토에 싸여 있었으므로, 나는 일부러 떠 있는 세관 배에서 한두 척 길이 안쪽으로 지나, 두 척의 이민선 옆을 따라 본류로 빠져나갔는데, 앞갑판에 병사들을 태우고 우리를 내려다보는 큰 수송선의 뱃머리 밑을 지났다."], ["And soon the tide began to slacken, and the craft lying at anchor to swing, and presently they had all swung round, and the ships that were taking advantage of the new tide to get up to the Pool began to crowd upon us in a fleet, and we kept under the shore, as much out of the strength of the tide now as we could, standing carefully off from low shallows and mudbanks.", "이윽고 물때가 느슨해지기 시작했고, 닻을 내리고 있던 배들이 빙 돌기 시작했으며, 곧 그것들이 모두 한 방향으로 돌아서더니, 새 물때를 타고 풀로 거슬러 오르려는 배들이 한 무리를 이루어 우리에게 밀려들기 시작했다. 우리는 이제 물살의 거센 곳을 되도록 피해 기슭에 바싹 붙어, 얕은 여울과 진흙 둑을 조심스레 비켜 가며 나아갔다."]], [["Our oarsmen were so fresh, by dint of having occasionally let her drive with the tide for a minute or two, that a quarter of an hour’s rest proved full as much as they wanted.", "우리 노잡이들은 이따금 일이 분씩 배를 물때에 맡겨 흘러가게 한 덕에 워낙 기운이 팔팔해서, 십오 분만 쉬어도 충분하고도 남았다."], ["We got ashore among some slippery stones while we ate and drank what we had with us, and looked about.", "우리는 미끄러운 돌들 사이로 기슭에 올라 가져온 것을 먹고 마시며 주위를 둘러보았다."], ["It was like my own marsh country, flat and monotonous, and with a dim horizon; while the winding river turned and turned, and the great floating buoys upon it turned and turned, and everything else seemed stranded and still.", "그곳은 평평하고 단조로우며 지평선이 흐릿한 것이, 꼭 내 고향 습지 같았다. 구불구불한 강은 굽이굽이 돌고, 그 위에 떠 있는 커다란 부표들도 빙글빙글 돌았지만, 그 밖의 모든 것은 좌초된 듯 멈춰 있는 것 같았다."], ["For now the last of the fleet of ships was round the last low point we had headed; and the last green barge, straw-laden, with a brown sail, had followed; and some ballast-lighters, shaped like a child’s first rude imitation of a boat, lay low in the mud; and a little squat shoal-lighthouse on open piles stood crippled in the mud on stilts and crutches; and slimy stakes stuck out of the mud, and slimy stones stuck out of the mud, and red landmarks and tidemarks stuck out of the mud, and an old landing-stage and an old roofless building slipped into the mud, and all about us was stagnation and mud.", "이제 그 배의 무리 가운데 마지막 배가 우리가 돌아 나온 마지막 낮은 곶을 돌아갔고, 갈색 돛을 단 채 짚을 가득 실은 마지막 초록 바지선이 그 뒤를 따랐다. 어린아이가 처음으로 서툴게 흉내 낸 배 모양 같은 바닥짐 거룻배 몇 척이 진흙 속에 나지막이 누워 있었고, 트인 말뚝 위에 얹힌 작고 땅딸막한 여울 등대 하나가 진흙 속에서 죽마와 목발에 의지한 듯 절뚝거리며 서 있었다. 진흙에서는 미끈미끈한 말뚝이 삐죽 솟아 있고, 미끈미끈한 돌이 삐죽 솟아 있고, 붉은 표지와 조수 표지가 삐죽 솟아 있었으며, 낡은 선착장과 지붕 없는 낡은 건물이 진흙 속으로 미끄러져 들어가고 있었으니, 우리 주위는 온통 정체와 진흙뿐이었다."]], [["We pushed off again, and made what way we could.", "우리는 다시 배를 밀어내고 갈 수 있는 만큼 나아갔다."], ["It was much harder work now, but Herbert and Startop persevered, and rowed and rowed and rowed until the sun went down.", "이제는 훨씬 힘든 일이었지만, 허버트와 스타톱은 끈질기게 버티며 해가 질 때까지 젓고 또 젓고 또 저었다."], ["By that time the river had lifted us a little, so that we could see above the bank.", "그 무렵 강물이 우리를 조금 들어 올려, 우리는 둑 위로 시야를 둘 수 있었다."], ["There was the red sun, on the low level of the shore, in a purple haze, fast deepening into black; and there was the solitary flat marsh; and far away there were the rising grounds, between which and us there seemed to be no life, save here and there in the foreground a melancholy gull.", "거기, 기슭의 나지막한 수평선 위로 붉은 해가, 보랏빛 안개 속에 빠르게 검은빛으로 짙어 가며 걸려 있었다. 그리고 거기 외로운 평평한 습지가 있었고, 저 멀리 솟아오른 땅들이 있었는데, 그 사이와 우리 사이에는 앞쪽 군데군데 처량한 갈매기 한 마리 말고는 아무 생명도 없는 것 같았다."]], [["As the night was fast falling, and as the moon, being past the full, would not rise early, we held a little council; a short one, for clearly our course was to lie by at the first lonely tavern we could find.", "밤이 빠르게 내리고, 달은 만월을 지난지라 일찍 뜨지 않을 터였으므로, 우리는 잠깐 의논을 했다. 짧은 의논이었으니, 우리가 갈 길은 분명 찾을 수 있는 첫 외딴 선술집에서 밤을 보내는 것이었기 때문이다."], ["So, they plied their oars once more, and I looked out for anything like a house.", "그래서 그들은 다시 한 번 노를 저었고, 나는 집처럼 보이는 무언가가 없는지 살폈다."], ["Thus we held on, speaking little, for four or five dull miles.", "그렇게 우리는 거의 말을 않고 따분한 사오 마일을 나아갔다."], ["It was very cold, and, a collier coming by us, with her galley-fire smoking and flaring, looked like a comfortable home.", "무척 추웠고, 조리용 화덕에서 연기와 불길을 내며 석탄선 한 척이 우리 곁을 지나가는데, 그것이 마치 아늑한 보금자리처럼 보였다."], ["The night was as dark by this time as it would be until morning; and what light we had, seemed to come more from the river than the sky, as the oars in their dipping struck at a few reflected stars.", "이때쯤 밤은 아침까지 그러할 만큼 캄캄했고, 우리가 가진 빛이라곤 하늘보다 강에서 더 많이 비쳐 나오는 듯했으니, 물에 잠겼다 나오는 노가 몇 개의 반사된 별들을 두드렸기 때문이다."]], [["At this dismal time we were evidently all possessed by the idea that we were followed.", "이 음산한 시각, 우리는 모두가 누군가에게 미행당하고 있다는 생각에 사로잡혀 있는 것이 분명했다."], ["As the tide made, it flapped heavily at irregular intervals against the shore; and whenever such a sound came, one or other of us was sure to start, and look in that direction.", "밀물이 들면서, 불규칙한 간격으로 기슭을 무겁게 철썩철썩 때렸다. 그런 소리가 날 때마다 우리 가운데 누군가는 어김없이 흠칫 놀라 그쪽을 바라보았다."], ["Here and there, the set of the current had worn down the bank into a little creek, and we were all suspicious of such places, and eyed them nervously.", "군데군데 물살이 둑을 깎아 작은 후미를 이루어 놓았는데, 우리는 그런 곳을 모두 의심하며 신경질적으로 살폈다."], ["Sometimes, “What was that ripple?”", "이따금 \"저 잔물결은 뭐지?\""], ["one of us would say in a low voice.", "하고 누군가 낮은 목소리로 말했다."], ["Or another, “Is that a boat yonder?” And afterwards we would fall into a dead silence, and I would sit impatiently thinking with what an unusual amount of noise the oars worked in the thowels.", "또 다른 누군가는 \"저기 저게 배 아냐?\" 했다. 그러고 나면 우리는 죽은 듯한 침묵에 빠졌고, 나는 노가 노받이에서 어찌 그리 유난스레 큰 소리를 내며 움직이나 하고 안달하며 앉아 있었다."]], [["At length we descried a light and a roof, and presently afterwards ran alongside a little causeway made of stones that had been picked up hard by.", "마침내 우리는 불빛 하나와 지붕 하나를 알아보았고, 곧이어 가까이서 주워 모은 돌로 쌓은 작은 둑길에 배를 댔다."], ["Leaving the rest in the boat, I stepped ashore, and found the light to be in a window of a public-house.", "나머지를 배에 남겨 둔 채 나는 기슭에 올랐고, 그 불빛이 어느 선술집 창문에서 나오는 것임을 알았다."], ["It was a dirty place enough, and I dare say not unknown to smuggling adventurers; but there was a good fire in the kitchen, and there were eggs and bacon to eat, and various liquors to drink.", "꽤나 지저분한 곳으로, 밀수꾼들에게도 낯설지 않을 성싶었다. 그러나 부엌에는 불이 잘 피워져 있었고, 먹을 달걀과 베이컨이 있었으며, 마실 술도 여러 가지 있었다."], ["Also, there were two double-bedded rooms,—“such as they were,” the landlord said.", "또 침대가 둘씩 든 방이 두 개 있었는데 — \"그래 봤자 그 모양이지만요\""], ["No other company was in the house than the landlord, his wife, and a grizzled male creature, the “Jack” of the little causeway, who was as slimy and smeary as if he had been low-water mark too.", "하고 주인이 말했다. 집 안에 있는 사람이라곤 주인과 그 아내, 그리고 그 작은 둑길의 \"잭\"이라는, 마치 자기도 간조선이기라도 한 양 미끈거리고 꾀죄죄한 희끗희끗한 사내 하나뿐이었다."]], [["With this assistant, I went down to the boat again, and we all came ashore, and brought out the oars, and rudder and boat-hook, and all else, and hauled her up for the night.", "이 일꾼의 도움을 받아 나는 다시 배로 내려갔고, 우리 모두 기슭에 올라 노와 키와 보트 갈고리며 그 밖의 모든 것을 꺼내고, 배를 끌어 올려 밤을 대비했다."], ["We made a very good meal by the kitchen fire, and then apportioned the bedrooms: Herbert and Startop were to occupy one; I and our charge the other.", "우리는 부엌 불가에서 아주 잘 먹은 뒤 침실을 나누었다. 허버트와 스타톱이 한 방을, 나와 우리의 화물 같은 그분이 다른 방을 쓰기로 했다."], ["We found the air as carefully excluded from both, as if air were fatal to life; and there were more dirty clothes and bandboxes under the beds than I should have thought the family possessed.", "두 방 모두 마치 공기가 생명에 치명적이기라도 한 양 공기가 어찌나 정성껏 차단되어 있던지, 침대 밑에는 그 집이 가졌으리라 짐작되는 것보다 더 많은 더러운 옷가지와 모자 상자가 들어 있었다."], ["But we considered ourselves well off, notwithstanding, for a more solitary place we could not have found.", "그래도 우리는 형편이 좋은 편이라 여겼으니, 이보다 더 외진 곳은 찾을 수 없었을 것이기 때문이다."]], [["While we were comforting ourselves by the fire after our meal, the Jack—who was sitting in a corner, and who had a bloated pair of shoes on, which he had exhibited while we were eating our eggs and bacon, as interesting relics that he had taken a few days ago from the feet of a drowned seaman washed ashore—asked me if we had seen a four-oared galley going up with the tide? When I told him No, he said she must have gone down then, and yet she “took up too,”", "식사 후 불가에서 몸을 녹이고 있는데, 잭이 — 그는 한구석에 앉아 있었고, 우리가 달걀과 베이컨을 먹는 동안 며칠 전 기슭으로 떠밀려 온 익사한 뱃사람의 발에서 벗겨 낸 흥미로운 유물이라며 퉁퉁 부어오른 신발 한 켤레를 신어 보였었다 — 나에게 네 사람이 노 젓는 갤리선 한 척이 물때를 따라 거슬러 올라가는 것을 보았느냐고 물었다. 못 봤다고 했더니, 그렇다면 그 배는 도로 내려갔을 거라며, 그래도 거기를 떠날 때는 분명 \"거슬러도 올라갔는데\""], ["when she left there.", "하는 것이었다."]], [["“They must ha’ thought better on’t for some reason or another,”", "\"무슨 까닭에선지 마음을 고쳐먹고,\" 잭이 말했다."], ["said the Jack, “and gone down.”", "\"다시 내려간 게 틀림없어요.\""]], [["“A four-oared galley, did you say?” said I.", "\"네 사람이 노 젓는 갤리선이라고 했소?\" 내가 말했다."]], [["“A four,”", "\"네 명이,\""], ["said the Jack, “and two sitters.”", "잭이 말했다. \"거기다 앉은 사람이 둘.\""]], [["“Did they come ashore here?”", "\"여기 기슭에 댔소?\""]], [["“They put in with a stone two-gallon jar for some beer.", "\"2갤런짜리 돌 단지를 가지고 맥주를 좀 받으러 들어왔지요."], ["I’d ha’ been glad to pison the beer myself,”", "나 같으면 그 맥주에 독이라도 타고 싶었어요.\""], ["said the Jack, “or put some rattling physic in it.”", "잭이 말했다. \"아니면 무슨 지독한 설사약이라도 넣든지.\""]], [["“Why?”", "\"왜?\""], ["“I know why,” said the Jack.", "\"내가 왜 그러는지는 나도 알지요.\""], ["He spoke in a slushy voice, as if much mud had washed into his throat.", "잭이 말했다. 그는 목구멍에 진흙이 잔뜩 씻겨 들어가기라도 한 양 질척한 목소리로 말했다."]], [["“He thinks,”", "\"저놈은 생각하지요.\""], ["said the landlord, a weakly meditative man with a pale eye, who seemed to rely greatly on his Jack,—“he thinks they was, what they wasn’t.”", "자기 잭에게 크게 의지하는 듯한, 핏기 없는 눈을 한 병약하고 골똘한 사내인 주인이 말했다. \"저들이 아닌 것을 두고 그것이라고 생각하는 거예요.\""]], [["“I knows what I thinks,” observed the Jack.", "\"내가 뭘 생각하는지는 내가 알아요.\" 잭이 한마디 했다."]], [["“You thinks Custom ’Us, Jack?”", "\"세관이라고 생각하나, 잭?\""], ["said the landlord.", "주인이 말했다."]], [["“I do,” said the Jack.", "\"그렇소.\" 잭이 말했다."]], [["“Then you’re wrong, Jack.”", "\"그럼 자네가 틀렸어, 잭.\""]], [["In the infinite meaning of his reply and his boundless confidence in his views, the Jack took one of his bloated shoes off, looked into it, knocked a few stones out of it on the kitchen floor, and put it on again.", "\"내가 틀렸다고요!\" 그 대답에 담긴 무한한 의미와 제 견해에 대한 끝없는 자신감을 보이며, 잭은 퉁퉁 부은 신발 한 짝을 벗어 안을 들여다보더니, 부엌 바닥에 돌 몇 개를 털어 내고 다시 신었다."], ["He did this with the air of a Jack who was so right that he could afford to do anything.", "그는 자기가 너무나 옳아서 무슨 짓이든 해도 괜찮은 잭이라는 듯한 태도로 그리했다."]], [["“Why, what do you make out that they done with their buttons then, Jack?”", "\"그럼 잭, 자네는 저들이 단추를 어떻게 했다고 보는 거야?\""], ["asked the landlord, vacillating weakly.", "주인이 미적지근하게 흔들리며 물었다."]], [["“Done with their buttons?”", "\"단추를 어떻게 했느냐고요?\""], ["returned the Jack. “Chucked ’em overboard.", "잭이 되받았다. \"뱃전 너머로 던져 버렸겠지요."], ["Swallered ’em.", "삼켜 버렸든지."], ["Sowed ’em, to come up small salad.", "새싹 샐러드처럼 돋아나라고 심었든지."], ["Done with their buttons!”", "단추를 어떻게 했느냐니!\""]], [["“Don’t be cheeky, Jack,” remonstrated the landlord, in a melancholy and pathetic way.", "\"건방 떨지 말게, 잭.\" 주인이 우울하고 가련한 투로 나무랐다."]], [["“A Custom ’Us officer knows what to do with his Buttons,”", "\"세관 관리는 제 단추를 어떻게 해야 할지 다 알지요.\""], ["said the Jack, repeating the obnoxious word with the greatest contempt, “when they comes betwixt him and his own light.", "잭이 그 역겨운 낱말을 더없는 경멸을 담아 되풀이하며 말했다. \"그게 자기와 제 정체 사이에 끼어들 때 말이오."], ["A four and two sitters don’t go hanging and hovering, up with one tide and down with another, and both with and against another, without there being Custom ’Us at the bottom of it.”", "네 사람에 앉은 사람 둘짜리 배가, 한 물때엔 올라갔다 다른 물때엔 내려갔다, 또 어떤 물때엔 거슬렀다 따라갔다 하면서 어슬렁어슬렁 맴도는 데에는, 그 밑바닥에 세관이 깔려 있지 않고서야 그럴 리가 없는 법이오.\""], ["Saying which he went out in disdain; and the landlord, having no one to reply upon, found it impracticable to pursue the subject.", "그러고는 경멸하듯 밖으로 나가 버렸다. 받아 줄 사람이 없어진 주인은 그 이야기를 더 끌고 갈 수가 없게 되었다."]], [["This dialogue made us all uneasy, and me very uneasy.", "이 대화는 우리 모두를 불안하게 했고, 특히 나를 몹시 불안하게 했다."], ["The dismal wind was muttering round the house, the tide was flapping at the shore, and I had a feeling that we were caged and threatened.", "음산한 바람이 집 둘레에서 웅얼거렸고, 밀물은 기슭을 철썩였으며, 나는 우리가 갇힌 채 위협받고 있는 듯한 느낌이 들었다."], ["A four-oared galley hovering about in so unusual a way as to attract this notice was an ugly circumstance that I could not get rid of.", "이토록 눈에 띌 만큼 유별나게 맴돌아 사람들의 주목을 끄는 네 명짜리 갤리선이라니, 그것은 내가 떨쳐 버릴 수 없는 흉한 정황이었다."], ["When I had induced Provis to go up to bed, I went outside with my two companions (Startop by this time knew the state of the case), and held another council.", "프로비스를 설득해 잠자리에 들게 한 뒤, 나는 두 친구와 함께 (이때쯤 스타톱도 사정을 알고 있었다) 밖으로 나가 다시 한 번 의논을 했다."], ["Whether we should remain at the house until near the steamer’s time, which would be about one in the afternoon, or whether we should put off early in the morning, was the question we discussed.", "오후 한 시쯤일 증기선이 올 시각이 가까워질 때까지 이 집에 머물 것인가, 아니면 아침 일찍 떠날 것인가가 우리가 논의한 문제였다."], ["On the whole we deemed it the better course to lie where we were, until within an hour or so of the steamer’s time, and then to get out in her track, and drift easily with the tide.", "결국 우리는, 증기선이 올 시각의 한 시간쯤 전까지 있던 자리에 머물러 있다가 그 항로로 나가 물때를 타고 느긋하게 흘러가는 편이 더 나은 방책이라 여겼다."], ["Having settled to do this, we returned into the house and went to bed.", "그렇게 하기로 정한 뒤, 우리는 집 안으로 돌아가 잠자리에 들었다."]], [["I lay down with the greater part of my clothes on, and slept well for a few hours.", "나는 옷을 거의 다 입은 채 누워, 몇 시간 동안 잘 잤다."], ["When I awoke, the wind had risen, and the sign of the house (the Ship) was creaking and banging about, with noises that startled me.", "깨어 보니 바람이 거세져, 집의 간판이 (그 배 모양 간판이) 삐걱거리고 쾅쾅거리며 나를 놀라게 하는 소리를 내고 있었다."], ["Rising softly, for my charge lay fast asleep, I looked out of the window.", "내 화물 같은 그분이 깊이 잠들어 있었으므로, 나는 가만히 일어나 창밖을 내다보았다."], ["It commanded the causeway where we had hauled up our boat, and, as my eyes adapted themselves to the light of the clouded moon, I saw two men looking into her.", "창은 우리가 배를 끌어 올려 둔 둑길을 향해 나 있었는데, 눈이 흐린 달빛에 익숙해지자, 나는 두 사내가 그 배를 들여다보고 있는 것을 보았다."], ["They passed by under the window, looking at nothing else, and they did not go down to the landing-place which I could discern to be empty, but struck across the marsh in the direction of the Nore.", "그들은 다른 것은 아무것도 보지 않은 채 창 밑을 지나갔고, 내가 보기에 텅 비어 있는 선착장으로는 내려가지 않고, 노어 방향으로 습지를 가로질러 갔다."]], [["My first impulse was to call up Herbert, and show him the two men going away.", "내 첫 충동은 허버트를 깨워 그 두 사내가 가는 것을 보여 주는 것이었다."], ["But reflecting, before I got into his room, which was at the back of the house and adjoined mine, that he and Startop had had a harder day than I, and were fatigued, I forbore.", "그러나 집 뒤편에 있어 내 방과 붙은 그의 방으로 들어가기 전에, 그와 스타톱이 나보다 더 고된 하루를 보내 지쳐 있다는 데 생각이 미쳐, 나는 참았다."], ["Going back to my window, I could see the two men moving over the marsh.", "다시 창가로 돌아가 보니, 두 사내가 습지를 가로질러 움직이는 것이 보였다."], ["In that light, however, I soon lost them, and, feeling very cold, lay down to think of the matter, and fell asleep again.", "그러나 그 빛 속에서 나는 곧 그들을 놓쳤고, 몹시 추워져, 그 일을 곰곰이 생각하려고 누웠다가 다시 잠이 들었다."]], [["We were up early.", "우리는 일찍 일어났다."], ["As we walked to and fro, all four together, before breakfast, I deemed it right to recount what I had seen.", "아침 식사 전에 넷이 함께 이리저리 거닐며, 나는 내가 본 것을 이야기하는 것이 옳다고 여겼다."], ["Again our charge was the least anxious of the party.", "또다시 우리의 화물 같은 그분이 일행 가운데 가장 덜 불안해했다."], ["It was very likely that the men belonged to the Custom House, he said quietly, and that they had no thought of us.", "그 사내들은 십중팔구 세관 사람들일 테고, 우리 따위는 안중에도 없을 거라고 그분이 차분히 말했다."], ["I tried to persuade myself that it was so,—as, indeed, it might easily be.", "나도 그러려니 — 사실 충분히 그럴 수도 있는 일이었다 — 스스로를 설득해 보려 했다."], ["However, I proposed that he and I should walk away together to a distant point we could see, and that the boat should take us aboard there, or as near there as might prove feasible, at about noon.", "그렇지만 나는, 우리 눈에 보이는 먼 곳까지 그분과 내가 함께 걸어가고, 정오쯤 배가 거기서, 혹은 그 근처 형편 닿는 데서 우리를 태우자고 제안했다."], ["This being considered a good precaution, soon after breakfast he and I set forth, without saying anything at the tavern.", "이것이 좋은 예방책이라 여겨져, 아침 식사 후 얼마 지나지 않아 그분과 나는 선술집에는 아무 말 없이 길을 나섰다."]], [["He smoked his pipe as we went along, and sometimes stopped to clap me on the shoulder.", "그분은 걸어가며 파이프를 피웠고, 이따금 멈춰 서서 내 어깨를 토닥였다."], ["One would have supposed that it was I who was in danger, not he, and that he was reassuring me. We spoke very little.", "누가 봐도 위험에 처한 것은 그분이 아니라 나인 듯, 또 그분이 나를 안심시키는 듯이 보였을 것이다. 우리는 거의 말을 하지 않았다."], ["As we approached the point, I begged him to remain in a sheltered place, while I went on to reconnoitre; for it was towards it that the men had passed in the night.", "그 곶에 가까워지자, 나는 그분에게 가려진 곳에 머물러 달라고 청한 뒤 나 혼자 정찰을 나갔다. 간밤에 사내들이 지나간 것이 바로 그쪽 방향이었기 때문이다."], ["He complied, and I went on alone.", "그분이 따랐고, 나는 혼자 나아갔다."], ["There was no boat off the point, nor any boat drawn up anywhere near it, nor were there any signs of the men having embarked there.", "곶 앞에는 배가 한 척도 없었고, 그 근처 어디에도 끌어 올려 둔 배가 없었으며, 사내들이 거기서 배에 올랐다는 어떤 흔적도 없었다."], ["But, to be sure, the tide was high, and there might have been some footprints under water.", "하지만 물론, 물때가 높아 물밑에 발자국이 남아 있었을지도 모를 일이었다."]], [["When he looked out from his shelter in the distance, and saw that I waved my hat to him to come up, he rejoined me, and there we waited; sometimes lying on the bank, wrapped in our coats, and sometimes moving about to warm ourselves, until we saw our boat coming round.", "그분이 멀리 자기가 숨은 곳에서 내다보다가 내가 모자를 흔들어 올라오라는 신호를 보내는 것을 보고는 내게 다시 합류했고, 우리는 거기서 기다렸다. 때로는 외투를 두르고 둑에 누웠다가, 때로는 몸을 녹이려 이리저리 움직이며, 우리 배가 돌아 나오는 것을 볼 때까지."], ["We got aboard easily, and rowed out into the track of the steamer.", "우리는 수월하게 배에 올라, 증기선의 항로로 노 저어 나갔다."], ["By that time it wanted but ten minutes of one o’clock, and we began to look out for her smoke.", "그 무렵엔 한 시까지 십 분밖에 남지 않았고, 우리는 그 배의 연기를 살피기 시작했다."]], [["But, it was half-past one before we saw her smoke, and soon afterwards we saw behind it the smoke of another steamer.", "그러나 우리가 그 연기를 본 것은 한 시 반이 되어서였고, 곧이어 그 뒤로 또 다른 증기선의 연기가 보였다."], ["As they were coming on at full speed, we got the two bags ready, and took that opportunity of saying good-bye to Herbert and Startop.", "두 배가 전속력으로 다가오고 있었으므로, 우리는 가방 두 개를 챙기고, 그 틈을 타 허버트와 스타톱에게 작별을 고했다."], ["We had all shaken hands cordially, and neither Herbert’s eyes nor mine were quite dry, when I saw a four-oared galley shoot out from under the bank but a little way ahead of us, and row out into the same track.", "우리는 모두 다정하게 악수를 나누었고, 허버트의 눈도 내 눈도 채 마르지 않았을 때, 나는 우리 바로 앞쪽으로 네 명짜리 갤리선 한 척이 둑 밑에서 쏜살같이 튀어나와 같은 항로로 노 저어 나오는 것을 보았다."]], [["A stretch of shore had been as yet between us and the steamer’s smoke, by reason of the bend and wind of the river; but now she was visible, coming head on.", "강의 굽이와 휘어짐 탓에 우리와 증기선의 연기 사이에는 아직 한 자락 기슭이 가로놓여 있었지만, 이제 그 배가 정면으로 다가오는 것이 보였다."], ["I called to Herbert and Startop to keep before the tide, that she might see us lying by for her, and I adjured Provis to sit quite still, wrapped in his cloak.", "나는 허버트와 스타톱에게 물때 앞쪽을 지키라고, 그래야 우리가 그 배를 기다리고 있다는 것을 알아볼 수 있다고 외쳤고, 프로비스에게는 망토에 싸인 채 꼼짝 말고 앉아 있으라고 간곡히 일렀다."], ["He answered cheerily, “Trust to me, dear boy,” and sat like a statue.", "그분은 \"나만 믿어라, 얘야\" 하고 명랑하게 대답하더니, 조각상처럼 앉아 있었다."], ["Meantime the galley, which was very skilfully handled, had crossed us, let us come up with her, and fallen alongside.", "그러는 사이, 매우 능숙하게 다루어진 그 갤리선이 우리를 가로질러, 우리가 따라잡게 한 뒤 옆에 바싹 붙었다."], ["Leaving just room enough for the play of the oars, she kept alongside, drifting when we drifted, and pulling a stroke or two when we pulled.", "노가 움직일 만큼의 틈만 남긴 채, 그 배는 우리 곁에 붙어, 우리가 흘러가면 함께 흘러가고, 우리가 노를 저으면 한두 번 노를 당겼다."], ["Of the two sitters one held the rudder-lines, and looked at us attentively,—as did all the rowers; the other sitter was wrapped up, much as Provis was, and seemed to shrink, and whisper some instruction to the steerer as he looked at us.", "앉아 있는 두 사람 가운데 하나는 키 줄을 잡고 우리를 주의 깊게 바라보았으니 — 노잡이들 모두가 그러했다 — 다른 한 사람은 프로비스와 꼭 마찬가지로 망토에 싸여, 몸을 움츠린 채 우리를 바라보며 키잡이에게 무어라 지시를 속삭이는 듯했다."], ["Not a word was spoken in either boat.", "어느 배에서도 한마디 말이 없었다."]], [["Startop could make out, after a few minutes, which steamer was first, and gave me the word “Hamburg,” in a low voice, as we sat face to face.", "스타톱이 몇 분 뒤 어느 증기선이 먼저인지 알아보고는, 우리가 마주 앉아 있던 터라 낮은 목소리로 \"함부르크\" 하고 일러 주었다."], ["She was nearing us very fast, and the beating of her peddles grew louder and louder.", "그 배는 우리를 향해 무서운 속도로 다가왔고, 외륜이 물을 때리는 소리가 점점 더 커졌다."], ["I felt as if her shadow were absolutely upon us, when the galley hailed us.", "그 그림자가 영락없이 우리 위에 드리워진 것처럼 느껴졌을 때, 갤리선에서 우리를 불렀다."], ["I answered.", "내가 대답했다."]], [["“You have a returned Transport there,”", "\"당신네 배에 귀환한 유형수가 타고 있소.\""], ["said the man who held the lines.", "키 줄을 잡은 사내가 말했다."], ["“That’s the man, wrapped in the cloak.", "\"저 사람이오, 망토에 싸인 자."], ["His name is Abel Magwitch, otherwise Provis.", "이름은 에이블 매그위치, 다른 이름으로는 프로비스."], ["I apprehend that man, and call upon him to surrender, and you to assist.”", "나는 저 사람을 체포하니, 저자에게는 투항을, 당신들에게는 협조를 요구하오.\""]], [["At the same moment, without giving any audible direction to his crew, he ran the galley abroad of us.", "바로 그 순간, 자기 선원들에게 들리는 어떤 지시도 내리지 않은 채, 그는 갤리선을 우리에게 바짝 들이댔다."], ["They had pulled one sudden stroke ahead, had got their oars in, had run athwart us, and were holding on to our gunwale, before we knew what they were doing.", "우리가 그들이 무엇을 하는지 알아차리기도 전에, 그들은 갑자기 노를 한 번 힘껏 저어 앞으로 나아가더니, 노를 거두고, 우리를 가로질러 들이박고는, 우리 뱃전을 붙들었다."], ["This caused great confusion on board the steamer, and I heard them calling to us, and heard the order given to stop the paddles, and heard them stop, but felt her driving down upon us irresistibly.", "이 때문에 증기선에서는 큰 혼란이 일어났고, 나는 그들이 우리에게 외치는 소리와, 외륜을 멈추라는 명령이 내려지는 소리, 그리고 외륜이 멈추는 소리를 들었으나, 그 배가 거역할 수 없는 기세로 우리를 향해 내리밀려 오는 것을 느꼈다."], ["In the same moment, I saw the steersman of the galley lay his hand on his prisoner’s shoulder, and saw that both boats were swinging round with the force of the tide, and saw that all hands on board the steamer were running forward quite frantically.", "같은 순간, 나는 갤리선의 키잡이가 제 죄수의 어깨에 손을 얹는 것을 보았고, 두 배가 물때의 힘에 떠밀려 빙 돌아가는 것을 보았으며, 증기선 위의 선원들이 모두 미친 듯이 앞으로 달려 나오는 것을 보았다."], ["Still, in the same moment, I saw the prisoner start up, lean across his captor, and pull the cloak from the neck of the shrinking sitter in the galley.", "여전히 같은 순간, 나는 죄수가 벌떡 일어나, 자기를 붙잡은 자를 가로질러 몸을 기울여, 갤리선 안에서 움츠리고 있던 사람의 목에서 망토를 잡아채는 것을 보았다."], ["Still in the same moment, I saw that the face disclosed, was the face of the other convict of long ago.", "여전히 같은 순간, 나는 그렇게 드러난 얼굴이 오래전 그 또 다른 죄수의 얼굴임을 보았다."], ["Still, in the same moment, I saw the face tilt backward with a white terror on it that I shall never forget, and heard a great cry on board the steamer, and a loud splash in the water, and felt the boat sink from under me.", "여전히 같은 순간, 나는 그 얼굴이 내가 결코 잊지 못할 새하얀 공포를 띤 채 뒤로 젖혀지는 것을 보았고, 증기선에서 터져 나오는 큰 비명과 물속의 요란한 첨벙 소리를 들었으며, 발밑에서 배가 가라앉는 것을 느꼈다."]], [["It was but for an instant that I seemed to struggle with a thousand mill-weirs and a thousand flashes of light; that instant past, I was taken on board the galley.", "내가 천 개의 물방아 둑과 천 개의 번뜩이는 빛과 씨름하는 듯했던 것은 한순간뿐이었다. 그 한순간이 지나자, 나는 갤리선에 끌어 올려져 있었다."], ["Herbert was there, and Startop was there; but our boat was gone, and the two convicts were gone.", "허버트도 거기 있었고, 스타톱도 거기 있었다. 그러나 우리 배는 사라졌고, 두 죄수도 사라졌다."]], [["What with the cries aboard the steamer, and the furious blowing off of her steam, and her driving on, and our driving on, I could not at first distinguish sky from water or shore from shore; but the crew of the galley righted her with great speed, and, pulling certain swift strong strokes ahead, lay upon their oars, every man looking silently and eagerly at the water astern.", "증기선에서 터지는 비명 소리에, 사납게 뿜어내는 증기 소리에, 그 배가 밀려오고 우리가 떠밀려 가는 통에, 나는 처음에는 하늘과 물을, 이 기슭과 저 기슭을 분간할 수 없었다. 그러나 갤리선의 선원들이 재빠른 솜씨로 배를 바로 세웠고, 빠르고 힘찬 노질로 앞으로 몇 번 나아간 뒤 노를 멈추고, 저마다 말없이 간절하게 고물 쪽 물을 바라보았다."], ["Presently a dark object was seen in it, bearing towards us on the tide.", "이윽고 물속에 검은 물체 하나가 보였는데, 물때를 타고 우리 쪽으로 떠내려오고 있었다."], ["No man spoke, but the steersman held up his hand, and all softly backed water, and kept the boat straight and true before it.", "아무도 말하지 않았으나, 키잡이가 손을 들자 모두가 가만히 노를 거꾸로 저어, 그것을 향해 배를 똑바로 곧게 두었다."], ["As it came nearer, I saw it to be Magwitch, swimming, but not swimming freely.", "가까이 다가오자, 나는 그것이 헤엄치고 있는 매그위치임을 알아보았다. 그러나 자유로이 헤엄치는 것은 아니었다."], ["He was taken on board, and instantly manacled at the wrists and ankles.", "그분은 배에 끌어 올려졌고, 곧바로 양 손목과 발목에 쇠고랑이 채워졌다."]], [["The galley was kept steady, and the silent, eager look-out at the water was resumed.", "갤리선은 흔들림 없이 유지되었고, 말없이 간절한 물의 감시가 다시 시작되었다."], ["But, the Rotterdam steamer now came up, and apparently not understanding what had happened, came on at speed.", "그러나 이제 로테르담행 증기선이 다가왔는데, 무슨 일이 벌어졌는지 모르는 듯 빠른 속도로 다가왔다."], ["By the time she had been hailed and stopped, both steamers were drifting away from us, and we were rising and falling in a troubled wake of water.", "그 배를 불러 세웠을 때쯤에는 두 증기선이 모두 우리에게서 멀어져 가고 있었고, 우리는 어지러이 일렁이는 물결의 항적 속에서 오르내리고 있었다."], ["The look-out was kept, long after all was still again and the two steamers were gone; but everybody knew that it was hopeless now.", "모든 것이 다시 잠잠해지고 두 증기선이 사라진 뒤로도 한참 동안 감시는 계속되었다. 그러나 이제는 가망이 없다는 것을 모두가 알고 있었다."]], [["At length we gave it up, and pulled under the shore towards the tavern we had lately left, where we were received with no little surprise.", "마침내 우리는 그것을 단념하고, 조금 전 떠나온 선술집을 향해 기슭 가까이로 노 저어 갔다. 거기서 우리는 적잖은 놀라움 속에 맞아들여졌다."], ["Here I was able to get some comforts for Magwitch,—Provis no longer,—who had received some very severe injury in the chest, and a deep cut in the head.", "여기서 나는 매그위치를 — 이제 더는 프로비스가 아니었다 — 위해 몇 가지 편의를 마련할 수 있었다. 그분은 가슴에 매우 심한 부상을 입었고, 머리에는 깊은 상처가 나 있었다."]], [["He told me that he believed himself to have gone under the keel of the steamer, and to have been struck on the head in rising.", "그분은 자기가 증기선의 용골 밑으로 들어갔다가 떠오르면서 머리를 부딪친 것 같다고 내게 말했다."], ["The injury to his chest (which rendered his breathing extremely painful) he thought he had received against the side of the galley.", "가슴의 부상은 (그 때문에 숨쉬기가 몹시 고통스러웠다) 갤리선 뱃전에 부딪혀 입은 것 같다고 했다."], ["He added that he did not pretend to say what he might or might not have done to Compeyson, but that, in the moment of his laying his hand on his cloak to identify him, that villain had staggered up and staggered back, and they had both gone overboard together, when the sudden wrenching of him (Magwitch) out of our boat, and the endeavour of his captor to keep him in it, had capsized us.", "그분은 자기가 콤피슨에게 무엇을 했는지 안 했는지 굳이 말하지는 않겠다고 덧붙였지만, 그자를 알아보려고 망토에 손을 댄 순간, 그 악당이 비틀거리며 일어나 비틀거리며 뒤로 물러났고, 둘이 함께 뱃전 너머로 떨어졌다고 했다. 바로 그때 그분(매그위치)이 우리 배에서 갑작스레 잡아채여 나가고, 그분을 붙잡은 자가 그분을 배 안에 붙들어 두려 한 것이 우리 배를 뒤집어 버린 것이었다."], ["He told me in a whisper that they had gone down fiercely locked in each other’s arms, and that there had been a struggle under water, and that he had disengaged himself, struck out, and swum away.", "그분은 둘이 서로의 팔에 사납게 얽혀 가라앉았고, 물속에서 몸싸움이 있었으며, 자기가 몸을 떼어 내 헤엄쳐 빠져나왔노라고 내게 속삭였다."]], [["I never had any reason to doubt the exact truth of what he thus told me.", "나는 그분이 이렇게 들려준 이야기가 한 치도 틀림없는 사실임을 의심할 까닭이 전혀 없었다."], ["The officer who steered the galley gave the same account of their going overboard.", "갤리선의 키를 잡았던 관리도 그들이 뱃전 너머로 떨어진 일에 대해 똑같이 진술했다."]], [["When I asked this officer’s permission to change the prisoner’s wet clothes by purchasing any spare garments I could get at the public-house, he gave it readily: merely observing that he must take charge of everything his prisoner had about him.", "내가 이 관리에게, 선술집에서 구할 수 있는 여분의 옷을 사서 죄수의 젖은 옷을 갈아입히도록 허락해 달라고 청하자, 그는 흔쾌히 허락했다. 다만 죄수가 몸에 지닌 것은 전부 자기가 맡아야 한다고만 일러 두었다."], ["So the pocket-book which had once been in my hands passed into the officer’s.", "그리하여 한때 내 손에 있던 수첩이 그 관리의 손으로 넘어갔다."], ["He further gave me leave to accompany the prisoner to London; but declined to accord that grace to my two friends.", "그는 또 죄수를 따라 런던까지 동행하도록 허락해 주었으나, 내 두 친구에게는 그 호의를 베풀기를 거절했다."]], [["The Jack at the Ship was instructed where the drowned man had gone down, and undertook to search for the body in the places where it was likeliest to come ashore.", "배 간판을 단 선술집의 잭은 익사한 사내가 가라앉은 곳을 전해 듣고, 시신이 기슭으로 떠밀려 올라올 가능성이 가장 높은 곳들에서 그것을 찾아보기로 했다."], ["His interest in its recovery seemed to me to be much heightened when he heard that it had stockings on.", "그것을 거두는 일에 대한 그의 관심은, 그 시신이 양말을 신고 있다는 말을 듣자 내가 보기에 한층 높아진 듯했다."], ["Probably, it took about a dozen drowned men to fit him out completely; and that may have been the reason why the different articles of his dress were in various stages of decay.", "아마 그를 완전히 차려입히는 데에는 익사한 사내 열두 명쯤이 들었을 터이고, 그 때문에 그의 옷가지가 저마다 다른 정도로 썩어 있었던 것인지도 모른다."]], [["We remained at the public-house until the tide turned, and then Magwitch was carried down to the galley and put on board.", "우리는 물때가 바뀔 때까지 선술집에 머물렀고, 그런 뒤 매그위치를 갤리선으로 옮겨 태웠다."], ["Herbert and Startop were to get to London by land, as soon as they could.", "허버트와 스타톱은 되도록 빨리 육로로 런던에 가기로 했다."], ["We had a doleful parting, and when I took my place by Magwitch’s side, I felt that that was my place henceforth while he lived.", "우리는 서글픈 작별을 나누었고, 내가 매그위치 곁에 자리를 잡았을 때, 나는 그것이 그분이 살아 있는 한 앞으로 내 자리이리라는 것을 느꼈다."]], [["For now, my repugnance to him had all melted away; and in the hunted, wounded, shackled creature who held my hand in his, I only saw a man who had meant to be my benefactor, and who had felt affectionately, gratefully, and generously, towards me with great constancy through a series of years.", "이제 그분에 대한 내 혐오감은 모두 녹아 사라졌으니, 내 손을 제 손에 쥔 그 쫓기고 다치고 사슬에 묶인 사람에게서, 나는 그저 나의 은인이 되고자 했던 한 사내, 그리고 여러 해에 걸쳐 한결같이 나를 향해 애정 어리고 고마워하며 너그러운 마음을 품어 온 한 사내를 볼 뿐이었다."], ["I only saw in him a much better man than I had been to Joe.", "나는 그분에게서, 내가 조에게 그래 왔던 것보다 훨씬 나은 사람을 볼 뿐이었다."]], [["His breathing became more difficult and painful as the night drew on, and often he could not repress a groan.", "밤이 깊어 갈수록 그분의 숨은 더욱 힘들고 고통스러워졌으며, 종종 신음을 억누르지 못했다."], ["I tried to rest him on the arm I could use, in any easy position; but it was dreadful to think that I could not be sorry at heart for his being badly hurt, since it was unquestionably best that he should die.", "나는 쓸 수 있는 한쪽 팔로 그분을 편한 자세로 받쳐 주려 애썼다. 그러나 그분이 죽는 것이 의심할 여지 없이 가장 나은 일인 까닭에, 그분이 심하게 다친 것을 마음속으로 슬퍼할 수 없다는 것을 생각하니 끔찍한 노릇이었다."], ["That there were, still living, people enough who were able and willing to identify him, I could not doubt.", "그분의 신원을 확인할 능력과 의향을 가진 사람들이 아직 충분히 살아 있다는 것을, 나는 의심할 수 없었다."], ["That he would be leniently treated, I could not hope.", "그분이 관대한 처분을 받으리라고는, 나는 바랄 수 없었다."], ["He who had been presented in the worst light at his trial, who had since broken prison and had been tried again, who had returned from transportation under a life sentence, and who had occasioned the death of the man who was the cause of his arrest.", "재판에서 가장 나쁜 모습으로 비추어졌고, 그 후 탈옥하여 다시 재판을 받았으며, 종신형을 받은 유형지에서 돌아왔고, 자기를 체포케 한 원인인 사내의 죽음을 초래한 그분이었으니."]], [["As we returned towards the setting sun we had yesterday left behind us, and as the stream of our hopes seemed all running back, I told him how grieved I was to think that he had come home for my sake.", "우리가 어제 뒤로하고 떠났던 그 지는 해를 향해 돌아가는 동안, 그리고 우리 희망의 물줄기가 모두 거꾸로 흘러가는 듯한 동안, 나는 그분에게 나를 위해 고국으로 돌아오셨다는 생각이 얼마나 가슴 아픈지 모르겠다고 말했다."]], [["“Dear boy,”", "\"얘야,\""], ["he answered, “I’m quite content to take my chance.", "그분이 대답했다. \"나는 내 운수를 받아들이는 게 아주 만족스럽다."], ["I’ve seen my boy, and he can be a gentleman without me.”", "내 아들을 봤으니, 그 애는 나 없이도 신사가 될 수 있어.\""]], [["No. I had thought about that, while we had been there side by side. No.", "아니었다. 나는 우리가 거기 나란히 앉아 있는 동안 그 일을 생각해 보았다. 아니었다."], ["Apart from any inclinations of my own, I understood Wemmick’s hint now.", "내 자신의 어떤 마음과는 별개로, 나는 이제 웨믹의 암시를 이해했다."], ["I foresaw that, being convicted, his possessions would be forfeited to the Crown.", "그분이 유죄 판결을 받으면, 그분의 재산은 국왕에게 몰수되리라는 것을 나는 내다보았다."]], [["“Lookee here, dear boy,”", "\"잘 들어라, 얘야.\""], ["said he “It’s best as a gentleman should not be knowed to belong to me now.", "그분이 말했다. \"이제 어떤 신사가 나한테 딸린 사람으로 알려지지 않는 게 상책이다."], ["Only come to see me as if you come by chance alonger Wemmick.", "그저 웨믹과 함께 우연히 들른 것처럼 나를 보러 와 주려무나."], ["Sit where I can see you when I am swore to, for the last o’ many times, and I don’t ask no more.”", "내가 여러 번 가운데 마지막으로 선서를 할 때, 너를 볼 수 있는 자리에 앉아 다오. 그 이상은 바라지 않으마.\""]], [["“I will never stir from your side,”", "\"제가 곁에 있도록 허락받는 한,\" 내가 말했다."], ["said I, “when I am suffered to be near you.", "\"저는 결코 아저씨 곁을 떠나지 않을 거예요."], ["Please God, I will be as true to you as you have been to me!”", "부디 하느님의 뜻이라면, 아저씨가 제게 그러셨던 만큼 저도 아저씨께 진실하겠어요!\""]], [["I felt his hand tremble as it held mine, and he turned his face away as he lay in the bottom of the boat, and I heard that old sound in his throat,—softened now, like all the rest of him.", "내 손을 쥔 그분의 손이 떨리는 것이 느껴졌고, 그분은 배 바닥에 누운 채 얼굴을 돌렸으며, 나는 그분의 목구멍에서 그 옛 소리가 — 이제는 그분의 다른 모든 것과 마찬가지로 부드러워진 그 소리가 — 나는 것을 들었다."], ["It was a good thing that he had touched this point, for it put into my mind what I might not otherwise have thought of until too late,—that he need never know how his hopes of enriching me had perished.", "그분이 이 점을 꺼낸 것은 다행한 일이었다. 그 덕분에, 그러지 않았더라면 너무 늦을 때까지 내가 미처 생각지 못했을 것 하나가 내 마음에 떠올랐기 때문이다. 그것은, 나를 부유하게 해 주려던 그분의 희망이 어떻게 스러졌는지를 그분이 결코 알 필요가 없다는 것이었다."]], [["# Chapter LV.", "# Chapter LV."]], [["He was taken to the Police Court next day, and would have been immediately committed for trial, but that it was necessary to send down for an old officer of the prison-ship from which he had once escaped, to speak to his identity.", "그는 이튿날 즉결 재판소로 끌려갔고, 곧바로 정식 재판에 회부될 뻔했다. 다만 그가 한때 탈출했던 죄수 호송선의 늙은 간수를 불러 내려와 그의 신원을 증언하게 해야 했기 때문에, 일은 미뤄졌다."], ["Nobody doubted it; but Compeyson, who had meant to depose to it, was tumbling on the tides, dead, and it happened that there was not at that time any prison officer in London who could give the required evidence. I had gone direct to Mr.", "신원을 의심하는 이는 아무도 없었다. 그러나 그것을 증언하려 했던 컴피슨은 죽어서 조수에 떠밀려 나뒹굴고 있었고, 마침 그때 런던에는 필요한 증언을 해 줄 만한 교도관이 한 사람도 없었다."], ["Jaggers at his private house, on my arrival over night, to retain his assistance, and Mr. Jaggers on the prisoner’s behalf would admit nothing.", "나는 간밤에 도착하자마자 곧장 재거스 씨의 자택으로 찾아가 그의 도움을 청해 두었으나, 재거스 씨는 죄수를 위해서는 아무것도 인정하려 들지 않았다. 그것이 유일한 방책이었다."], ["It was the sole resource; for he told me that the case must be over in five minutes when the witness was there, and that no power on earth could prevent its going against us.", "그는 증인이 나타나면 재판은 오 분 안에 끝나 버릴 것이며, 이 세상 그 어떤 힘으로도 우리에게 불리하게 흘러가는 것을 막을 수 없으리라고 내게 말했기 때문이다."]], [["I imparted to Mr. Jaggers my design of keeping him in ignorance of the fate of his wealth. Mr.", "나는 그가 자기 재산의 운명에 대해 모르도록 해 두려는 내 계획을 재거스 씨에게 전했다."], ["Jaggers was querulous and angry with me for having “let it slip through my fingers,”", "재거스 씨는 내가 \"그것을 손가락 사이로 흘려보냈다\"며 투덜대고 화를 냈다."], ["and said we must memorialise by and by, and try at all events for some of it.", "그러면서 머지않아 진정서를 올려 어떻게든 그 일부만이라도 건져 보아야 한다고 말했다."], ["But he did not conceal from me that, although there might be many cases in which the forfeiture would not be exacted, there were no circumstances in this case to make it one of them.", "그러나 재산 몰수가 강행되지 않는 경우도 많기는 하나, 이 사건에는 그런 예외에 해당될 만한 사정이 전혀 없다는 사실도 그는 내게 숨기지 않았다. 나는 그것을 아주 잘 이해하고 있었다."], ["I understood that very well. I was not related to the outlaw, or connected with him by any recognisable tie; he had put his hand to no writing or settlement in my favour before his apprehension, and to do so now would be idle.", "나는 그 무법자와 혈연도 아니었고, 인정받을 만한 어떤 유대로 그와 얽혀 있지도 않았다. 그는 체포되기 전에 나를 위한 어떤 증서나 양도 문서에도 손을 대 두지 않았으며, 이제 와서 그렇게 하는 것도 부질없는 일이었다."], ["I had no claim, and I finally resolved, and ever afterwards abided by the resolution, that my heart should never be sickened with the hopeless task of attempting to establish one.", "내게는 아무런 권리도 없었으니, 나는 마침내 결심했고, 그 후로도 줄곧 그 결심을 지켰다. 권리를 세워 보려는 가망 없는 일로 내 마음을 병들게 하지는 않으리라고."]], [["There appeared to be reason for supposing that the drowned informer had hoped for a reward out of this forfeiture, and had obtained some accurate knowledge of Magwitch’s affairs.", "익사한 밀고자가 이 몰수에서 보상을 바랐으며, 매그위치의 재산 사정에 대해 어느 정도 정확한 정보를 얻어 두었으리라고 추측할 만한 근거가 있는 듯했다."], ["When his body was found, many miles from the scene of his death, and so horribly disfigured that he was only recognisable by the contents of his pockets, notes were still legible, folded in a case he carried.", "그의 시신이 죽은 자리에서 수 마일이나 떨어진 곳에서 발견되었을 때, 그리고 어찌나 끔찍하게 훼손되었던지 주머니에 든 물건으로만 신원을 알아볼 수 있을 정도였을 때, 그가 지니고 다니던 지갑 속에 접혀 있던 쪽지들은 여전히 읽을 수 있었다."], ["Among these were the name of a banking-house in New South Wales, where a sum of money was, and the designation of certain lands of considerable value. Both these heads of information were in a list that Magwitch, while in prison, gave to Mr.", "그 가운데에는 어느 정도의 돈이 예치된 뉴사우스웨일스의 한 은행 이름과, 상당한 가치가 있는 어떤 토지의 지명이 적혀 있었다. 이 두 항목의 정보는 매그위치가 옥중에서 재거스 씨에게 건넨, 내가 상속받으리라 여긴 재산의 목록에 들어 있던 것이었다."], ["Jaggers, of the possessions he supposed I should inherit.", "그 가엾은 사람은 결국 자신의 무지 덕을 본 셈이었다."], ["His ignorance, poor fellow, at last served him; he never mistrusted but that my inheritance was quite safe, with Mr. Jaggers’s aid.", "그는 재거스 씨의 도움이 있으면 내 상속이 완전히 안전하리라는 것을 단 한 번도 의심하지 않았다."]], [["After three days’ delay, during which the crown prosecution stood over for the production of the witness from the prison-ship, the witness came, and completed the easy case. He was committed to take his trial at the next Sessions, which would come on in a month.", "호송선에서 증인을 불러오느라 검찰 측 기소가 보류된 채 사흘이 지연된 뒤, 마침내 증인이 도착해 그 손쉬운 사건을 매듭지었다. 그는 다음 분기 형사 회기에서 재판을 받도록 회부되었고, 그 회기는 한 달 뒤에 열릴 예정이었다."]], [["It was at this dark time of my life that Herbert returned home one evening, a good deal cast down, and said,—", "내 인생의 그 어두운 시기에, 어느 날 저녁 허버트가 잔뜩 풀이 죽은 채 집으로 돌아와 말했다."]], [["“My dear Handel, I fear I shall soon have to leave you.”", "\"이보게 헨델, 아무래도 머지않아 자네 곁을 떠나야 할 것 같네.\""]], [["His partner having prepared me for that, I was less surprised than he thought.", "그의 동업자가 이미 그 일에 대해 내게 언질을 주었던 터라, 나는 그가 생각한 것만큼 놀라지 않았다."]], [["“We shall lose a fine opportunity if I put off going to Cairo, and I am very much afraid I must go, Handel, when you most need me.”", "\"카이로 가는 일을 미루면 좋은 기회를 놓치게 될 걸세. 그런데 자네가 나를 가장 필요로 하는 이때에, 아무래도 떠나야만 할 것 같아서 정말 두렵네, 헨델.\""]], [["“Herbert, I shall always need you, because I shall always love you; but my need is no greater now than at another time.”", "\"허버트, 나는 자네를 언제까지나 필요로 할 걸세. 자네를 언제까지나 사랑할 테니까. 하지만 지금 내가 자네를 필요로 하는 정도가 다른 때보다 더한 것은 아니라네.\""]], [["“You will be so lonely.”", "\"자네가 너무 외로울 텐데.\""]], [["“I have not leisure to think of that,” said I.", "\"그런 걸 생각할 겨를이 없네.\" 내가 말했다."], ["“You know that I am always with him to the full extent of the time allowed, and that I should be with him all day long, if I could.", "\"자네도 알다시피 나는 허락된 시간을 한껏 다 채워 늘 그분 곁에 있고, 할 수만 있다면 온종일 그분 곁에 있을 걸세."], ["And when I come away from him, you know that my thoughts are with him.”", "그리고 그분에게서 떠나와도, 자네도 알다시피 내 생각은 그분과 함께 있다네.\""]], [["The dreadful condition to which he was brought, was so appalling to both of us, that we could not refer to it in plainer words.", "그가 처하게 된 그 끔찍한 처지는 우리 둘 모두에게 너무도 참담한 것이어서, 우리는 그것을 더 노골적인 말로 입에 올릴 수 없었다."]], [["“My dear fellow,”", "\"이보게 친구.\""], ["said Herbert, “let the near prospect of our separation—for, it is very near—be my justification for troubling you about yourself.", "허버트가 말했다. \"우리가 헤어질 날이 가까웠으니 — 정말로 무척 가까웠으니 — 그것을 핑계 삼아 자네 자신에 관해 자네를 좀 성가시게 해도 되겠지."], ["Have you thought of your future?”", "자네의 앞날을 생각해 본 적 있나?\""]], [["“No, for I have been afraid to think of any future.”", "\"아니, 어떤 앞날이든 생각하기가 두려웠으니까.\""]], [["“But yours cannot be dismissed; indeed, my dear dear Handel, it must not be dismissed.", "\"하지만 자네의 앞날은 그냥 묻어 둘 수 없네. 정말이지, 이보게 헨델, 묻어 두어서는 안 되네."], ["I wish you would enter on it now, as far as a few friendly words go, with me.”", "지금 나와 함께 몇 마디 다정한 말을 나누는 정도로라도, 그 문제에 들어서 보았으면 하네.\""]], [["“I will,” said I.", "\"그러지.\" 내가 말했다."]], [["“In this branch house of ours, Handel, we must have a—”", "\"우리 그 지점에는 말일세, 헨델, 한 사람의 — 가 필요하다네.\""]], [["I saw that his delicacy was avoiding the right word, so I said, “A clerk.”", "나는 그의 조심스러움이 정확한 단어를 피하고 있음을 알아채고서 말했다. \"사무원 말이지.\""]], [["“A clerk. And I hope it is not at all unlikely that he may expand (as a clerk of your acquaintance has expanded) into a partner.", "\"사무원이지. 그리고 그가 (자네가 아는 어느 사무원이 그러했듯이) 동업자로 성장할 가능성이 결코 없지 않으리라 생각하네."], ["Now, Handel,—in short, my dear boy, will you come to me?”", "그러니 헨델 — 한마디로, 이보게, 자네 나에게로 오지 않겠나?\""]], [["There was something charmingly cordial and engaging in the manner in which after saying “Now, Handel,” as if it were the grave beginning of a portentous business exordium, he had suddenly given up that tone, stretched out his honest hand, and spoken like a schoolboy.", "\"그러니 헨델\"이라고, 마치 대단한 사업 연설의 엄숙한 서두라도 되는 양 말해 놓고는, 별안간 그 어조를 거두고 정직한 손을 내밀며 학생처럼 말하는 그의 태도에는, 매혹적일 만큼 진심 어리고 사람을 끄는 무언가가 있었다."]], [["“Clara and I have talked about it again and again,”", "\"클래라와 나는 그 얘기를 몇 번이고 거듭 나누었네.\""], ["Herbert pursued, “and the dear little thing begged me only this evening, with tears in her eyes, to say to you that, if you will live with us when we come together, she will do her best to make you happy, and to convince her husband’s friend that he is her friend too.", "허버트가 말을 이었다. \"그리고 그 사랑스러운 사람이 바로 오늘 저녁에도 눈물을 글썽이며 자네에게 이렇게 전해 달라고 내게 간청했네. 우리가 함께 살게 될 때 자네도 우리와 같이 산다면, 자네를 행복하게 해 주려고, 그리고 남편의 친구가 자기 친구이기도 하다는 것을 자기 자신에게 납득시키려고 최선을 다하겠다고 말일세."], ["We should get on so well, Handel!”", "우리 정말 잘 지낼 수 있을 걸세, 헨델!\""]], [["I thanked her heartily, and I thanked him heartily, but said I could not yet make sure of joining him as he so kindly offered.", "나는 그녀에게 진심으로 고마워했고, 그에게도 진심으로 고마워했다. 그러나 그가 그토록 친절히 제안해 주었음에도 아직은 그와 합류하리라 확언할 수 없다고 말했다."], ["Firstly, my mind was too preoccupied to be able to take in the subject clearly.", "첫째로, 내 마음이 너무 사로잡혀 있어 그 문제를 또렷이 받아들일 수가 없었다."], ["Secondly,—Yes!", "둘째로 — 그렇다!"], ["Secondly, there was a vague something lingering in my thoughts that will come out very near the end of this slight narrative.", "둘째로, 이 짤막한 이야기의 끝자락에 가서야 드러날, 어떤 막연한 무언가가 내 생각 속에 어른거리고 있었다."]], [["“But if you thought, Herbert, that you could, without doing any injury to your business, leave the question open for a little while—”", "\"하지만 허버트, 자네 사업에 아무 해를 끼치지 않으면서 이 문제를 잠시 동안 미결로 남겨 둘 수 있겠다고 여겨진다면 —\""]], [["“For any while,”", "\"얼마든지 그래도 되네.\""], ["cried Herbert.", "허버트가 외쳤다."], ["“Six months, a year!”", "\"여섯 달이든, 일 년이든!\""]], [["“Not so long as that,”", "\"그렇게까지 길게는 아닐세.\""], ["said I. “Two or three months at most.”", "내가 말했다. \"기껏해야 두세 달이지.\""]], [["Herbert was highly delighted when we shook hands on this arrangement, and said he could now take courage to tell me that he believed he must go away at the end of the week.", "이 합의에 우리가 악수를 나누자 허버트는 몹시 기뻐하며, 이제 용기를 내어 말할 수 있겠다면서 아무래도 이번 주말에는 떠나야 할 것 같다고 했다."]], [["“And Clara?”", "\"그럼 클래라는?\""], ["said I.", "내가 말했다."]], [["“The dear little thing,”", "\"그 사랑스러운 사람은.\""], ["returned Herbert, “holds dutifully to her father as long as he lasts; but he won’t last long. Mrs.", "허버트가 대답했다. \"아버지가 살아 계시는 한 도리를 다해 곁을 지킬 거라네. 하지만 그분도 오래 못 버티실 걸세."], ["Whimple confides to me that he is certainly going.”", "윔플 부인이 내게 귀띔하기를, 분명 가실 날이 멀지 않았다더군.\""]], [["“Not to say an unfeeling thing,”", "\"매정한 말이 되겠네만.\" 내가 말했다."], ["said I, “he cannot do better than go.”", "\"그분으로서는 가시는 것보다 나은 일이 없겠지.\""]], [["“I am afraid that must be admitted,”", "\"안타깝지만 그건 인정해야겠지.\" 허버트가 말했다."], ["said Herbert; “and then I shall come back for the dear little thing, and the dear little thing and I will walk quietly into the nearest church.", "\"그러면 나는 그 사랑스러운 사람을 데리러 돌아올 걸세. 그리고 그 사랑스러운 사람과 나는 가장 가까운 교회로 조용히 걸어 들어가는 거지."], ["Remember!", "잊지 말게!"], ["The blessed darling comes of no family, my dear Handel, and never looked into the red book, and hasn’t a notion about her grandpapa.", "그 복받은 사람은 이렇다 할 가문 출신도 아니고, 이보게 헨델, 귀족 명부 따위 들여다본 적도 없으며, 자기 할아버지가 누구인지도 전혀 모른다네. 우리 어머니 아들에게 이 얼마나 큰 행운인가!\""], ["What a fortune for the son of my mother!”", "* 귀족 명부: 영국 귀족·상류층의 가계를 기록한 명부."]], [["On the Saturday in that same week, I took my leave of Herbert,—full of bright hope, but sad and sorry to leave me,—as he sat on one of the seaport mail coaches.", "바로 그 주 토요일에 나는 허버트와 작별했다. 항구로 가는 우편 마차 좌석 하나에 앉은 그는 밝은 희망에 부풀어 있으면서도, 나를 두고 떠나는 것을 슬퍼하고 안타까워했다. 나는 어느 커피점에 들어가 클래라에게 짤막한 편지를 썼다."], ["I went into a coffee-house to write a little note to Clara, telling her he had gone off, sending his love to her over and over again, and then went to my lonely home,—if it deserved the name; for it was now no home to me, and I had no home anywhere.", "그가 떠났다는 것과, 그녀에게 거듭거듭 사랑을 전한다는 말을 적었다. 그러고 나서 나는 외로운 내 집으로 — 그것을 집이라 부를 자격이 있다면 — 돌아갔다. 이제 그곳은 내게 더 이상 집이 아니었고, 나는 어디에도 집이라 할 곳이 없었으니까."]], [["On the stairs I encountered Wemmick, who was coming down, after an unsuccessful application of his knuckles to my door.", "계단에서 나는 웨믹과 마주쳤다. 그는 내 문을 두드려 보았으나 헛수고로 끝난 뒤 내려오는 길이었다."], ["I had not seen him alone since the disastrous issue of the attempted flight; and he had come, in his private and personal capacity, to say a few words of explanation in reference to that failure.", "탈주 시도가 처참하게 끝난 이래로 그를 단둘이 만난 것은 처음이었다. 그는 사사로운 개인 자격으로, 그 실패에 관해 몇 마디 해명을 하러 온 것이었다."]], [["“The late Compeyson,”", "\"고인이 된 컴피슨은 말입니다.\""], ["said Wemmick, “had by little and little got at the bottom of half of the regular business now transacted; and it was from the talk of some of his people in trouble (some of his people being always in trouble) that I heard what I did.", "웨믹이 말했다. \"지금 처리되는 정규 업무의 절반쯤은 조금씩조금씩 그 속내를 캐내 두었더군요. 그리고 곤경에 빠진 그자의 패거리 몇몇이 (그자의 패거리 중에는 늘 곤경에 빠진 자가 있게 마련이지요) 떠드는 얘기를 통해 제가 그 정보를 들은 겁니다."], ["I kept my ears open, seeming to have them shut, until I heard that he was absent, and I thought that would be the best time for making the attempt.", "저는 귀를 닫은 척하면서 줄곧 귀를 열어 두었지요. 그러다 그자가 자리를 비웠다는 말을 듣고는, 그때가 결행하기에 가장 좋은 때라고 생각했던 겁니다."], ["I can only suppose now, that it was a part of his policy, as a very clever man, habitually to deceive his own instruments.", "이제 와 짐작건대, 매우 영리한 자였던 그자가 제 수족까지 늘 속여 두는 것이 그자의 술책의 일부였던 모양입니다."], ["You don’t blame me, I hope, Mr. Pip?", "저를 탓하지는 않으시겠지요, 핍 씨?"], ["I am sure I tried to serve you, with all my heart.”", "저는 진심을 다해 당신을 도우려 애썼노라 분명히 말씀드릴 수 있습니다.\""]], [["“I am as sure of that, Wemmick, as you can be, and I thank you most earnestly for all your interest and friendship.”", "\"그 점은 웨믹 당신만큼이나 저도 확신합니다. 그리고 당신의 온갖 관심과 우정에 더없이 진심으로 감사드립니다.\""]], [["“Thank you, thank you very much.", "\"고맙습니다, 정말 고맙습니다."], ["It’s a bad job,”", "안된 일이지요.\""], ["said Wemmick, scratching his head, “and I assure you I haven’t been so cut up for a long time.", "웨믹이 머리를 긁적이며 말했다. \"정말이지 이토록 속이 상해 본 게 오랜만입니다."], ["What I look at is the sacrifice of so much portable property. Dear me!”", "제가 안타까워하는 건 그 많은 동산이 날아가 버렸다는 겁니다. 저런, 저런!\""]], [["“What I think of, Wemmick, is the poor owner of the property.”", "\"제가 마음 쓰는 건 말입니다, 웨믹, 그 재산의 가엾은 주인이지요.\""]], [["“Yes, to be sure,”", "\"네, 그야 물론이지요.\""], ["said Wemmick.", "웨믹이 말했다."], ["“Of course, there can be no objection to your being sorry for him, and I’d put down a five-pound note myself to get him out of it.", "\"당연히 당신이 그를 가엾게 여기는 데야 이의가 있을 수 없지요. 그를 거기서 빼내 줄 수만 있다면 저라도 5파운드짜리 지폐 한 장쯤은 내놓겠습니다."], ["But what I look at is this.", "하지만 제가 보는 건 이렇습니다."], ["The late Compeyson having been beforehand with him in intelligence of his return, and being so determined to bring him to book, I do not think he could have been saved.", "고인이 된 컴피슨이 그의 귀환을 미리 알아채고 있었고, 또 그토록 단단히 그를 잡아넣겠다고 작정한 마당이었으니, 저는 그가 구제될 수 없었으리라고 봅니다."], ["Whereas, the portable property certainly could have been saved.", "반면에 동산은 분명히 구해 낼 수 있었지요."], ["That’s the difference between the property and the owner, don’t you see?”", "그게 바로 재산과 주인의 차이입니다, 아시겠지요?\""]], [["I invited Wemmick to come upstairs, and refresh himself with a glass of grog before walking to Walworth.", "나는 웨믹에게 위층으로 올라와 월워스로 걸어가기 전에 그로그 한 잔으로 몸을 풀라고 청했다."], ["He accepted the invitation.", "그는 그 청을 받아들였다."], ["While he was drinking his moderate allowance, he said, with nothing to lead up to it, and after having appeared rather fidgety,—", "그가 적당한 양을 마시는 동안, 그는 다소 안절부절못하는 기색을 보이더니, 별다른 운도 떼지 않고 불쑥 말했다."]], [["“What do you think of my meaning to take a holiday on Monday, Mr. Pip?”", "\"월요일에 제가 휴가를 낼까 하는데, 핍 씨, 어떻게 생각하십니까?\""]], [["“Why, I suppose you have not done such a thing these twelve months.”", "\"글쎄요, 지난 열두 달 동안 그런 일을 해 본 적이 없으시잖습니까.\""]], [["“These twelve years, more likely,”", "\"열두 해라 하는 게 맞겠지요.\""], ["said Wemmick. “Yes.", "웨믹이 말했다. \"네."], ["I’m going to take a holiday.", "저는 휴가를 낼 작정입니다."], ["More than that; I’m going to take a walk.", "그뿐만이 아니라, 산책을 나갈 작정이지요."], ["More than that; I’m going to ask you to take a walk with me.”", "게다가, 당신께 함께 산책 나가시자고 청할 작정입니다.\""]], [["I was about to excuse myself, as being but a bad companion just then, when Wemmick anticipated me.", "나는 지금으로서는 영 변변찮은 길동무일 뿐이라며 사양하려던 참이었으나, 웨믹이 먼저 내 말을 막았다."]], [["“I know your engagements,”", "\"당신의 사정은 압니다.\" 그가 말했다."], ["said he, “and I know you are out of sorts, Mr. Pip.", "\"그리고 당신이 마음이 편치 않으시다는 것도 알지요, 핍 씨."], ["But if you could oblige me, I should take it as a kindness. It ain’t a long walk, and it’s an early one.", "하지만 만일 저를 위해 시간을 내 주실 수 있다면, 그것을 호의로 여기겠습니다. 긴 산책도 아니고, 이른 산책이지요."], ["Say it might occupy you (including breakfast on the walk) from eight to twelve.", "이를테면 (도중에 아침 식사를 하는 것까지 쳐서) 여덟 시부터 열두 시까지면 될 겁니다."], ["Couldn’t you stretch a point and manage it?”", "한 번만 무리해서 시간을 내 주실 순 없겠습니까?\""]], [["He had done so much for me at various times, that this was very little to do for him.", "그는 여러 차례 나를 위해 너무도 많은 일을 해 주었던 터라, 이쯤은 그를 위해 해 줄 일도 못 되었다."], ["I said I could manage it,—would manage it,—and he was so very much pleased by my acquiescence, that I was pleased too.", "나는 그럴 수 있다고, 그렇게 하겠다고 말했고, 그는 내 승낙에 어찌나 기뻐하던지 나도 덩달아 기뻤다."], ["At his particular request, I appointed to call for him at the Castle at half past eight on Monday morning, and so we parted for the time.", "그의 각별한 부탁에 따라, 나는 월요일 아침 여덟 시 반에 캐슬로 그를 데리러 가기로 약속했고, 그렇게 우리는 일단 헤어졌다."]], [["Punctual to my appointment, I rang at the Castle gate on the Monday morning, and was received by Wemmick himself, who struck me as looking tighter than usual, and having a sleeker hat on.", "월요일 아침, 나는 약속 시간에 어김없이 캐슬 문 앞에서 종을 울렸고, 웨믹이 몸소 나를 맞았다. 그는 평소보다 한결 말쑥해 보였고, 더 윤기 나는 모자를 쓰고 있었다."], ["Within, there were two glasses of rum and milk prepared, and two biscuits.", "안에는 럼주를 탄 우유 두 잔과 비스킷 두 개가 준비되어 있었다."], ["The Aged must have been stirring with the lark, for, glancing into the perspective of his bedroom, I observed that his bed was empty.", "노인장은 종달새와 함께 일어난 모양이었으니, 그의 침실 안쪽을 흘끗 들여다보니 침대가 비어 있는 것이 눈에 들어왔다."]], [["When we had fortified ourselves with the rum and milk and biscuits, and were going out for the walk with that training preparation on us, I was considerably surprised to see Wemmick take up a fishing-rod, and put it over his shoulder.", "럼주를 탄 우유와 비스킷으로 든든히 배를 채우고, 그렇게 채비를 갖춘 채 산책을 나서려는데, 웨믹이 낚싯대를 집어 어깨에 둘러메는 것을 보고 나는 적잖이 놀랐다. \"아니, 우리 낚시하러 가는 게 아니잖습니까!\""], ["“Why, we are not going fishing!”", "내가 말했다. \"아니지요.\""], ["said I. “No,”", "웨믹이 대답했다."], ["returned Wemmick, “but I like to walk with one.”", "\"하지만 저는 낚싯대를 들고 걷는 걸 좋아합니다.\""]], [["I thought this odd; however, I said nothing, and we set off. We went towards Camberwell Green, and when we were thereabouts, Wemmick said suddenly,—", "나는 이것이 이상하다고 여겼으나, 아무 말도 하지 않고 우리는 길을 나섰다. 우리는 캠버웰 그린 쪽으로 갔고, 그 근방에 이르자 웨믹이 불쑥 말했다."]], [["“Halloa!", "\"어이쿠!"], ["Here’s a church!”", "여기 교회가 있네요!\""]], [["There was nothing very surprising in that; but again, I was rather surprised, when he said, as if he were animated by a brilliant idea,—", "거기에 별로 놀랄 만한 것은 없었다. 그러나 그가 마치 기막힌 생각이 떠올라 들뜬 사람처럼 이렇게 말했을 때, 나는 또 한 번 다소 놀랐다."]], [["“Let’s go in!”", "\"들어가 봅시다!\""]], [["We went in, Wemmick leaving his fishing-rod in the porch, and looked all round.", "우리는 안으로 들어갔다. 웨믹은 낚싯대를 현관에 세워 두고는, 사방을 두루 둘러보았다."], ["In the mean time, Wemmick was diving into his coat-pockets, and getting something out of paper there.", "그러는 사이 웨믹은 자기 외투 주머니를 뒤지더니, 거기서 종이에 싼 무언가를 꺼냈다."]], [["“Halloa!”", "\"어이쿠!\""], ["said he. “Here’s a couple of pair of gloves!", "그가 말했다. \"여기 장갑이 두 켤레 있네요!"], ["Let’s put ’em on!”", "끼워 봅시다!\""]], [["As the gloves were white kid gloves, and as the post-office was widened to its utmost extent, I now began to have my strong suspicions.", "장갑이 흰 새끼 염소 가죽 장갑이었고, 게다가 그의 우체통 같은 입이 한껏 옆으로 벌어졌으므로, 나는 이제 강한 의심이 들기 시작했다."], ["They were strengthened into certainty when I beheld the Aged enter at a side door, escorting a lady.", "그리고 노인장이 한 부인을 모시고 옆문으로 들어서는 것을 보자, 그 의심은 확신으로 굳어졌다."]], [["“Halloa!”", "\"어이쿠!\""], ["said Wemmick.", "웨믹이 말했다."], ["“Here’s Miss Skiffins!", "\"스키핀스 양이 오셨네요!"], ["Let’s have a wedding.”", "결혼식이나 올립시다.\""]], [["That discreet damsel was attired as usual, except that she was now engaged in substituting for her green kid gloves a pair of white.", "그 신중한 처녀는 평소처럼 차려입고 있었는데, 다만 이제 자기 초록색 새끼 염소 가죽 장갑을 흰 것으로 바꿔 끼는 일에 골몰해 있다는 점만은 달랐다."], ["The Aged was likewise occupied in preparing a similar sacrifice for the altar of Hymen.", "노인장도 마찬가지로, 히멘의 제단에 바칠 비슷한 제물을 준비하느라 여념이 없었다."], ["The old gentleman, however, experienced so much difficulty in getting his gloves on, that Wemmick found it necessary to put him with his back against a pillar, and then to get behind the pillar himself and pull away at them, while I for my part held the old gentleman round the waist, that he might present an equal and safe resistance.", "그러나 노신사는 장갑을 끼는 데 어찌나 애를 먹던지, 웨믹은 부득이 그를 기둥에 등을 대고 세운 다음, 자신은 기둥 뒤로 돌아가 장갑을 잡아당겨야 했고, 나는 나대로 노신사가 한결같이 안정된 저항을 보일 수 있도록 그의 허리를 끌어안고 있어야 했다. 이 기발한 방책 덕분에, 그의 장갑은 더할 나위 없이 완벽하게 끼워졌다."], ["By dint of this ingenious scheme, his gloves were got on to perfection.", "* 히멘: 그리스 신화의 결혼의 신."]], [["The clerk and clergyman then appearing, we were ranged in order at those fatal rails.", "이윽고 서기와 목사가 나타나자, 우리는 그 운명의 난간 앞에 차례로 늘어섰다."], ["True to his notion of seeming to do it all without preparation, I heard Wemmick say to himself, as he took something out of his waistcoat-pocket before the service began, “Halloa!", "무엇이든 미리 준비하지 않고 그 자리에서 해치우는 것처럼 보이려는 그의 지론에 충실하게도, 예식이 시작되기 전 그가 조끼 주머니에서 무언가를 꺼내며 혼잣말로 이렇게 중얼거리는 것이 들렸다. \"어이쿠!"], ["Here’s a ring!”", "여기 반지가 있네!\""]], [["I acted in the capacity of backer, or best-man, to the bridegroom; while a little limp pew-opener in a soft bonnet like a baby’s, made a feint of being the bosom friend of Miss Skiffins.", "나는 신랑의 후원자, 즉 들러리 역할을 맡았다. 한편 갓난아기 모자 같은 부드러운 보닛을 쓴, 자그마하고 맥없어 보이는 좌석 안내인 여자가 스키핀스 양의 절친한 벗인 척하는 시늉을 했다."], ["The responsibility of giving the lady away devolved upon the Aged, which led to the clergyman’s being unintentionally scandalised, and it happened thus.", "신부를 인도하는 책임은 노인장에게 돌아갔는데, 이 때문에 목사가 본의 아니게 망신을 당하는 일이 벌어졌다. 사연인즉 이러했다."], ["When he said, “Who giveth this woman to be married to this man?”", "목사가 \"누가 이 여자를 이 남자에게 시집보내려 데려왔습니까?\""], ["the old gentleman, not in the least knowing what point of the ceremony we had arrived at, stood most amiably beaming at the ten commandments.", "하고 물었을 때, 노신사는 예식이 어느 대목에 이르렀는지 전혀 모른 채, 더없이 흐뭇한 얼굴로 십계명 판을 향해 환히 미소 지으며 서 있었다."], ["Upon which, the clergyman said again, “WHO giveth this woman to be married to this man?”", "이에 목사는 다시 \"누가 이 여자를 이 남자에게 시집보내려 데려왔습니까?\" 하고 물었다."], ["The old gentleman being still in a state of most estimable unconsciousness, the bridegroom cried out in his accustomed voice, “Now Aged P.", "노신사는 여전히 더없이 갸륵한 무심 상태에 빠져 있었으므로, 신랑이 평소의 그 목소리로 외쳤다. \"자 노인장 피 씨, 아시잖아요."], ["you know; who giveth?”", "누가 데려왔냐고요?\""], ["To which the Aged replied with great briskness, before saying that he gave, “All right, John, all right, my boy!”", "이에 노인장은 자기가 데려왔노라 말하기에 앞서 아주 활기차게 대답했다. \"좋아, 존, 잘했다, 얘야!\""], ["And the clergyman came to so gloomy a pause upon it, that I had doubts for the moment whether we should get completely married that day.", "그러자 목사가 그 말에 어찌나 침울하게 말문이 막혀 멈추던지, 나는 순간 우리가 그날 결혼식을 온전히 마칠 수 있을까 하는 의구심마저 들었다."]], [["It was completely done, however, and when we were going out of church Wemmick took the cover off the font, and put his white gloves in it, and put the cover on again. Mrs.", "그러나 결혼식은 온전히 끝났고, 우리가 교회를 나설 때 웨믹은 세례반 뚜껑을 열어 그 안에 자기 흰 장갑을 넣고는 다시 뚜껑을 덮었다."], ["Wemmick, more heedful of the future, put her white gloves in her pocket and assumed her green.", "앞날을 더 헤아릴 줄 아는 웨믹 부인은 자기 흰 장갑을 주머니에 넣고 초록색 장갑을 끼었다."], ["“Now, Mr. Pip,”", "\"자, 핍 씨.\""], ["said Wemmick, triumphantly shouldering the fishing-rod as we came out, “let me ask you whether anybody would suppose this to be a wedding-party!”", "우리가 밖으로 나올 때 웨믹이 낚싯대를 의기양양하게 어깨에 둘러메며 말했다. \"누가 이걸 보고 결혼식 일행이라 짐작이나 하겠는지 한번 여쭤봅시다!\""]], [["Breakfast had been ordered at a pleasant little tavern, a mile or so away upon the rising ground beyond the green; and there was a bagatelle board in the room, in case we should desire to unbend our minds after the solemnity.", "아침 식사는 그린 너머 언덕 위로 일 마일쯤 떨어진, 아담하고 정겨운 선술집에 주문해 두었다. 그리고 그 방에는, 우리가 예식의 엄숙함을 떨치고 마음을 풀고 싶어질 경우를 대비해 바가텔 판이 놓여 있었다."], ["It was pleasant to observe that Mrs. Wemmick no longer unwound Wemmick’s arm when it adapted itself to her figure, but sat in a high-backed chair against the wall, like a violoncello in its case, and submitted to be embraced as that melodious instrument might have done.", "웨믹 부인이 웨믹의 팔이 자기 몸매에 맞춰 둘려도 더 이상 그것을 풀어내지 않고, 등받이가 높은 의자에 마치 케이스에 담긴 첼로처럼 벽에 기대 앉아, 그 가락 고운 악기가 그러하듯 얌전히 안기는 모습을 지켜보는 것은 흐뭇한 일이었다."]], [["We had an excellent breakfast, and when any one declined anything on table, Wemmick said, “Provided by contract, you know; don’t be afraid of it!”", "우리는 더할 나위 없이 훌륭한 아침을 먹었고, 누군가가 식탁 위의 무언가를 사양할 때면 웨믹이 말했다. \"정해진 값에 다 받아 둔 거니까, 사양 말고 드세요!\""], ["I drank to the new couple, drank to the Aged, drank to the Castle, saluted the bride at parting, and made myself as agreeable as I could.", "나는 새 부부를 위해 잔을 들었고, 노인장을 위해 잔을 들었으며, 캐슬을 위해 잔을 들었고, 헤어질 때 신부에게 인사를 건넸으며, 할 수 있는 한 상냥하게 처신했다."]], [["Wemmick came down to the door with me, and I again shook hands with him, and wished him joy.", "웨믹이 나를 배웅하러 문간까지 내려왔고, 나는 다시 그와 악수하며 그의 행복을 빌었다."]], [["“Thankee!”", "\"고맙습니다!\""], ["said Wemmick, rubbing his hands.", "웨믹이 두 손을 비비며 말했다."], ["“She’s such a manager of fowls, you have no idea.", "\"그 사람이 닭을 어찌나 잘 치는지 당신은 모르실 겁니다."], ["You shall have some eggs, and judge for yourself.", "달걀을 좀 보내 드릴 테니, 직접 판단해 보시지요."], ["I say, Mr. Pip!”", "그런데 핍 씨!\""], ["calling me back, and speaking low.", "그가 나를 다시 불러 세우더니 목소리를 낮추어 말했다."], ["“This is altogether a Walworth sentiment, please.”", "\"이건 어디까지나 월워스의 정서이니, 부디 그리 알아주십시오.\""]], [["“I understand.", "\"알겠습니다."], ["Not to be mentioned in Little Britain,”", "리틀 브리튼에서는 입에 올리지 말라는 말씀이지요.\""], ["said I.", "내가 말했다."]], [["Wemmick nodded.", "웨믹이 고개를 끄덕였다."], ["“After what you let out the other day, Mr. Jaggers may as well not know of it.", "\"요전 날 당신이 무심코 흘리신 일도 있으니, 재거스 씨는 이 일을 모르는 편이 낫겠습니다."], ["He might think my brain was softening, or something of the kind.”", "그분은 제 머리가 물러진다거나, 뭐 그런 식으로 여기실지도 모르니까요.\""]], [["# Chapter LVI.", "# Chapter LVI."]], [["He lay in prison very ill, during the whole interval between his committal for trial and the coming round of the Sessions.", "그는 재판에 회부된 뒤 개정기가 돌아올 때까지의 그 기간 내내 감옥에 누워 몹시 앓았다."], ["He had broken two ribs, they had wounded one of his lungs, and he breathed with great pain and difficulty, which increased daily.", "그는 갈비뼈가 두 대 부러졌고, 한쪽 폐가 다쳤으며, 큰 고통과 곤란 속에서 숨을 쉬었는데, 그 고통은 날로 더해 갔다."], ["It was a consequence of his hurt that he spoke so low as to be scarcely audible; therefore he spoke very little.", "부상의 여파로 그는 목소리가 어찌나 낮은지 거의 알아들을 수 없을 지경이었다. 그래서 그는 말을 아주 적게 했다."], ["But he was ever ready to listen to me; and it became the first duty of my life to say to him, and read to him, what I knew he ought to hear.", "그러나 그는 내 말을 들으려는 마음만큼은 언제나 준비되어 있었다. 그가 마땅히 들어야 한다고 내가 여기는 것을 그에게 말해 주고 읽어 주는 일이, 내 삶의 첫째 의무가 되었다."]], [["Being far too ill to remain in the common prison, he was removed, after the first day or so, into the infirmary.", "그는 일반 감방에 머무르기엔 너무도 위중했으므로, 하루 이틀이 지난 뒤 의무실로 옮겨졌다."], ["This gave me opportunities of being with him that I could not otherwise have had.", "덕분에 나는 달리는 결코 가질 수 없었을, 그와 함께할 기회를 얻었다."], ["And but for his illness he would have been put in irons, for he was regarded as a determined prison-breaker, and I know not what else.", "그리고 그의 병이 아니었다면 그는 쇠고랑이 채워졌을 것이다. 그는 작정하고 탈옥을 시도한 자로, 그 밖에 무엇으로 또 여겨졌는지 나로서는 알 수 없지만, 어쨌든 그렇게 간주되었기 때문이다."]], [["Although I saw him every day, it was for only a short time; hence, the regularly recurring spaces of our separation were long enough to record on his face any slight changes that occurred in his physical state.", "나는 그를 날마다 보았으나, 그저 짧은 시간 동안이었다. 그러므로 규칙적으로 되풀이되는 우리의 헤어짐의 간격은, 그의 신체 상태에 일어난 사소한 변화라도 그의 얼굴에 새겨 보여 줄 만큼 충분히 길었다."], ["I do not recollect that I once saw any change in it for the better; he wasted, and became slowly weaker and worse, day by day, from the day when the prison door closed upon him.", "나는 그 얼굴에서 단 한 번이라도 나은 쪽으로의 변화를 본 기억이 없다. 그는 감방 문이 그를 가둔 그날부터 날이 갈수록 야위어 갔고, 서서히 더 약해지고 더 나빠져 갔다."]], [["The kind of submission or resignation that he showed was that of a man who was tired out.", "그가 보인 그런 종류의 순종 혹은 체념은, 기진맥진한 사람의 그것이었다."], ["I sometimes derived an impression, from his manner or from a whispered word or two which escaped him, that he pondered over the question whether he might have been a better man under better circumstances.", "나는 이따금 그의 태도에서, 또는 그의 입에서 새어 나온 한두 마디 속삭임에서, 그가 더 나은 환경에서라면 더 나은 사람이 되었을지를 곰곰이 헤아리고 있다는 인상을 받았다."], ["But he never justified himself by a hint tending that way, or tried to bend the past out of its eternal shape.", "그러나 그는 그런 방향의 암시로 결코 자신을 변호하지 않았고, 영원히 굳어 버린 과거를 그 형태에서 비틀어 내려고도 하지 않았다."]], [["It happened on two or three occasions in my presence, that his desperate reputation was alluded to by one or other of the people in attendance on him.", "내가 있는 자리에서 두세 번, 그를 돌보던 사람들 가운데 누군가가 그의 무시무시한 평판을 넌지시 입에 올린 일이 있었다."], ["A smile crossed his face then, and he turned his eyes on me with a trustful look, as if he were confident that I had seen some small redeeming touch in him, even so long ago as when I was a little child.", "그러면 그의 얼굴에 미소가 스쳤고, 그는 마치 내가 그에게서 작으나마 그를 구원해 줄 어떤 면모를 보았다고, 그것도 내가 어린아이였던 그 까마득한 시절에 이미 보았다고 믿어 의심치 않는 듯, 신뢰에 찬 눈빛으로 나에게 눈을 돌리는 것이었다."], ["As to all the rest, he was humble and contrite, and I never knew him complain.", "그 밖의 모든 것에 대해 그는 겸허하고 회개하는 마음이었고, 나는 그가 불평하는 것을 한 번도 본 적이 없다."]], [["When the Sessions came round, Mr. Jaggers caused an application to be made for the postponement of his trial until the following Sessions.", "개정기가 돌아오자, 재거스 씨는 그의 재판을 다음 개정기까지 연기해 달라는 신청을 제출하게 했다."], ["It was obviously made with the assurance that he could not live so long, and was refused.", "그것은 분명 그가 그렇게 오래 살지 못하리라는 확신에서 낸 신청이었으나, 기각되었다."], ["The trial came on at once, and, when he was put to the bar, he was seated in a chair.", "재판은 즉시 시작되었고, 그가 피고석에 세워졌을 때, 그는 의자에 앉혀졌다."], ["No objection was made to my getting close to the dock, on the outside of it, and holding the hand that he stretched forth to me.", "내가 피고석 바깥쪽으로 바싹 다가가, 그가 나에게 내미는 손을 잡는 일에는 아무런 이의도 제기되지 않았다."]], [["The trial was very short and very clear.", "재판은 아주 짧고 아주 명료했다."], ["Such things as could be said for him were said,—how he had taken to industrious habits, and had thriven lawfully and reputably.", "그를 위해 할 수 있는 말은 다 나왔다. 그가 어떻게 근면한 습관을 들였고, 합법적이고 떳떳하게 번창했는지에 대해서."], ["But nothing could unsay the fact that he had returned, and was there in presence of the Judge and Jury.", "그러나 그가 돌아왔다는 사실, 그리고 지금 판사와 배심원 앞에 와 있다는 사실은 그 무엇으로도 없던 일로 만들 수 없었다."], ["It was impossible to try him for that, and do otherwise than find him guilty.", "그 일로 그를 재판하면서 유죄가 아닌 다른 결론을 내리는 것은 불가능했다."]], [["At that time, it was the custom (as I learnt from my terrible experience of that Sessions) to devote a concluding day to the passing of Sentences, and to make a finishing effect with the Sentence of Death.", "당시에는(나는 그 개정기의 끔찍한 경험을 통해 알게 되었거니와) 마지막 하루를 선고에 바치고, 사형 선고로써 절정의 효과를 내는 것이 관례였다."], ["But for the indelible picture that my remembrance now holds before me, I could scarcely believe, even as I write these words, that I saw two-and-thirty men and women put before the Judge to receive that sentence together.", "내 기억이 지금 내 앞에 들이미는 이 지워지지 않는 그림이 아니라면, 나는 이 글을 쓰는 지금조차도, 서른두 명의 남녀가 판사 앞에 세워져 그 선고를 함께 받는 것을 내가 보았다는 사실을 도무지 믿기 어려울 것이다."], ["Foremost among the two-and-thirty was he; seated, that he might get breath enough to keep life in him.", "그 서른두 명 가운데 맨 앞에 그가 있었다. 목숨을 부지할 만큼의 숨을 들이쉬기 위해 그는 앉아 있었다."]], [["The whole scene starts out again in the vivid colours of the moment, down to the drops of April rain on the windows of the court, glittering in the rays of April sun.", "그 장면 전체가 다시금 그 순간의 생생한 색채로 떠오른다. 법정 창문에 맺힌 사월의 빗방울이 사월의 햇살을 받아 반짝이던 것까지도."], ["Penned in the dock, as I again stood outside it at the corner with his hand in mine, were the two-and-thirty men and women; some defiant, some stricken with terror, some sobbing and weeping, some covering their faces, some staring gloomily about.", "내가 다시 피고석 모퉁이 바깥에 서서 그의 손을 내 손에 쥐고 있는 동안, 그 우리 안에 갇힌 것은 서른두 명의 남녀였다. 더러는 반항적이었고, 더러는 공포에 사로잡혀 있었으며, 더러는 흐느끼며 울고 있었고, 더러는 얼굴을 가리고 있었으며, 더러는 침울하게 사방을 노려보고 있었다."], ["There had been shrieks from among the women convicts; but they had been stilled, and a hush had succeeded.", "여죄수들 사이에서 비명이 터져 나왔으나, 이내 가라앉았고, 정적이 뒤따랐다."], ["The sheriffs with their great chains and nosegays, other civic gewgaws and monsters, criers, ushers, a great gallery full of people,—a large theatrical audience,—looked on, as the two-and-thirty and the Judge were solemnly confronted. Then the Judge addressed them.", "거창한 사슬과 꽃다발을 두른 보안관들, 그 밖의 갖가지 시정의 장식물과 괴물 같은 물건들, 정리들, 안내인들, 사람으로 가득 찬 거대한 방청석 — 연극을 보러 온 듯한 수많은 관중 — 이 모두가, 서른두 명과 판사가 엄숙하게 마주 서는 것을 지켜보고 있었다."], ["Among the wretched creatures before him whom he must single out for special address was one who almost from his infancy had been an offender against the laws; who, after repeated imprisonments and punishments, had been at length sentenced to exile for a term of years; and who, under circumstances of great violence and daring, had made his escape and been re-sentenced to exile for life.", "이윽고 판사가 그들에게 말을 건넸다. 그가 특별히 따로 골라 말을 건네야 할, 그의 앞에 선 비참한 자들 가운데 하나는, 거의 갓난아기 시절부터 법을 어겨 온 자였다. 거듭된 투옥과 형벌 끝에 마침내 몇 년간의 유형을 선고받았고, 엄청난 폭력과 대담함을 동원하여 탈출했다가 다시 종신 유형을 선고받은 자였다."], ["That miserable man would seem for a time to have become convinced of his errors, when far removed from the scenes of his old offences, and to have lived a peaceable and honest life.", "그 가련한 사내는, 옛 죄를 저지른 무대에서 멀리 떨어져 있는 동안에는 한때 자신의 과오를 깨달은 듯, 평화롭고 정직한 삶을 산 듯도 보였다."], ["But in a fatal moment, yielding to those propensities and passions, the indulgence of which had so long rendered him a scourge to society, he had quitted his haven of rest and repentance, and had come back to the country where he was proscribed.", "그러나 어느 운명적인 순간, 그토록 오랫동안 그를 사회의 재앙으로 만들어 온 그 성향과 정념에 굴복하여, 그는 안식과 회개의 안식처를 떠나, 자신에게 추방령이 내려진 나라로 되돌아왔다."], ["Being here presently denounced, he had for a time succeeded in evading the officers of Justice, but being at length seized while in the act of flight, he had resisted them, and had—he best knew whether by express design, or in the blindness of his hardihood—caused the death of his denouncer, to whom his whole career was known.", "이곳에서 이내 고발당한 그는, 한동안 사법 당국의 추적을 따돌리는 데 성공했으나, 마침내 도주하던 중에 붙잡혔을 때 그들에게 저항하였고, 그리고 — 명백한 의도에서였는지, 아니면 무모함의 맹목 속에서였는지는 그 자신이 가장 잘 알겠지만 — 자신의 모든 행적을 알고 있던 고발자를 죽음에 이르게 하였다."], ["The appointed punishment for his return to the land that had cast him out, being Death, and his case being this aggravated case, he must prepare himself to Die.", "자신을 내쫓은 땅으로 되돌아온 데 대해 정해진 형벌이 사형이며, 그의 경우가 이처럼 가중된 사례이므로, 그는 죽을 각오를 해야 한다는 것이었다."]], [["The sun was striking in at the great windows of the court, through the glittering drops of rain upon the glass, and it made a broad shaft of light between the two-and-thirty and the Judge, linking both together, and perhaps reminding some among the audience how both were passing on, with absolute equality, to the greater Judgment that knoweth all things, and cannot err.", "햇빛이 유리에 맺힌 반짝이는 빗방울을 뚫고 법정의 커다란 창문으로 비쳐 들었고, 그것은 서른두 명과 판사 사이에 한 줄기 넓은 빛의 기둥을 만들어 양쪽을 하나로 잇고 있었다. 어쩌면 그것은 방청객 가운데 몇몇에게, 양쪽 모두가 절대적인 평등 속에서, 모든 것을 알며 결코 그르치지 않는 더 큰 심판을 향해 나아가고 있음을 일깨워 주었는지도 모른다."], ["Rising for a moment, a distinct speck of face in this way of light, the prisoner said, “My Lord, I have received my sentence of Death from the Almighty, but I bow to yours,”", "이 빛줄기 속에서 또렷한 얼굴 한 점으로, 잠시 몸을 일으키며, 그 죄수는 이렇게 말했다. \"재판장님, 저는 전능하신 분으로부터 사형 선고를 받았습니다만, 재판장님의 선고에도 머리 숙입니다.\""], ["and sat down again.", "그러고는 다시 앉았다."], ["There was some hushing, and the Judge went on with what he had to say to the rest.", "잠시 조용히 하라는 소리가 일었고, 판사는 나머지 사람들에게 할 말을 이어 갔다."], ["Then they were all formally doomed, and some of them were supported out, and some of them sauntered out with a haggard look of bravery, and a few nodded to the gallery, and two or three shook hands, and others went out chewing the fragments of herb they had taken from the sweet herbs lying about.", "그리하여 그들은 모두 정식으로 운명이 결정되었고, 더러는 부축을 받아 끌려 나갔으며, 더러는 초췌하면서도 용감한 척하는 얼굴로 어슬렁어슬렁 걸어 나갔고, 몇몇은 방청석을 향해 고개를 끄덕였으며, 두세 명은 악수를 나누었고, 또 더러는 여기저기 놓인 향초 더미에서 집어 든 풀잎 조각을 씹으며 나갔다."], ["He went last of all, because of having to be helped from his chair, and to go very slowly; and he held my hand while all the others were removed, and while the audience got up (putting their dresses right, as they might at church or elsewhere), and pointed down at this criminal or at that, and most of all at him and me.", "그는 맨 마지막에 나갔는데, 의자에서 부축을 받아야 했고, 또 아주 천천히 걸어야 했기 때문이다. 다른 사람들이 모두 끌려 나가는 동안, 그리고 방청객들이 (교회에서나 다른 데서나 그러듯 옷매무새를 바로잡으며) 일어나, 이 죄인 저 죄인을 손가락질하고, 무엇보다도 그와 나를 손가락질하는 동안에도, 그는 내 손을 잡고 있었다."]], [["I earnestly hoped and prayed that he might die before the Recorder’s Report was made; but, in the dread of his lingering on, I began that night to write out a petition to the Home Secretary of State, setting forth my knowledge of him, and how it was that he had come back for my sake.", "나는 그가 형 집행 권고 보고서가 작성되기 전에 죽기를 간절히 바라고 기도했다. 그러나 그가 질질 끌며 살아남을까 두려워, 나는 그날 밤 내무 장관에게 보낼 청원서를 쓰기 시작했다. 그에 대해 내가 아는 바와, 그가 나를 위해 어떻게 돌아오게 되었는지를 적었다."], ["I wrote it as fervently and pathetically as I could; and when I had finished it and sent it in, I wrote out other petitions to such men in authority as I hoped were the most merciful, and drew up one to the Crown itself.", "나는 할 수 있는 한 열렬하고 애절하게 그것을 썼다. 그리고 다 써서 제출한 뒤에는, 가장 자비로우리라고 내가 기대하는 권력자들에게 보낼 또 다른 청원서들을 썼고, 왕실 자체에 올릴 청원서까지 한 통 작성했다."], ["For several days and nights after he was sentenced I took no rest except when I fell asleep in my chair, but was wholly absorbed in these appeals.", "그가 선고를 받은 뒤로 며칠 밤낮 동안 나는 의자에서 잠들 때를 빼고는 쉬지 않았으며, 오로지 이 호소에만 온전히 몰두했다."], ["And after I had sent them in, I could not keep away from the places where they were, but felt as if they were more hopeful and less desperate when I was near them.", "그리고 그것들을 제출한 뒤에는, 그것들이 놓여 있는 장소에서 도무지 멀리 떨어져 있을 수가 없었고, 내가 그 가까이에 있을 때면 그것들이 더 희망차고 덜 절망적으로 느껴지는 듯했다."], ["In this unreasonable restlessness and pain of mind I would roam the streets of an evening, wandering by those offices and houses where I had left the petitions.", "이 까닭 모를 안절부절못함과 마음의 고통 속에서, 나는 저녁이면 거리를 헤매며, 청원서를 두고 온 그 관청과 저택들 곁을 배회하곤 했다."], ["To the present hour, the weary western streets of London on a cold, dusty spring night, with their ranges of stern, shut-up mansions, and their long rows of lamps, are melancholy to me from this association.", "지금 이 순간까지도, 춥고 먼지 자욱한 봄밤의 런던 서쪽의 그 황량한 거리들은, 줄지어 늘어선 근엄하게 닫힌 저택들과 길게 이어진 가로등의 행렬과 더불어, 이 기억의 연상으로 인해 내게 우수에 찬 곳으로 남아 있다."]], [["The daily visits I could make him were shortened now, and he was more strictly kept.", "내가 그를 찾아갈 수 있는 매일의 면회는 이제 더 짧아졌고, 그에 대한 감시는 더욱 엄해졌다."], ["Seeing, or fancying, that I was suspected of an intention of carrying poison to him, I asked to be searched before I sat down at his bedside, and told the officer who was always there, that I was willing to do anything that would assure him of the singleness of my designs.", "내가 그에게 독약을 가져다주려는 의도를 의심받고 있다는 것을 알아차렸거나, 혹은 그렇게 짐작한 나는, 그의 침상 곁에 앉기 전에 몸수색을 해 달라고 청했고, 늘 그곳에 있던 관리에게, 내 의도가 오직 하나뿐임을 그가 확신할 수 있도록 무엇이든 기꺼이 하겠다고 말했다."], ["Nobody was hard with him or with me.", "그에게도 나에게도 모질게 구는 사람은 없었다."], ["There was duty to be done, and it was done, but not harshly.", "해야 할 의무가 있었고, 그것은 수행되었으나, 가혹하지는 않았다."], ["The officer always gave me the assurance that he was worse, and some other sick prisoners in the room, and some other prisoners who attended on them as sick nurses, (malefactors, but not incapable of kindness, God be thanked!) always joined in the same report.", "관리는 늘 그가 더 나빠졌다고 일러 주었고, 그 방에 있던 다른 병든 죄수들과, 그들을 병자 간병인으로서 돌보던 다른 죄수들(죄인들이긴 했으나, 친절을 베풀 줄 모르는 자들은 아니었으니, 하느님께 감사할 일이다!)도 늘 같은 보고에 입을 모았다."]], [["As the days went on, I noticed more and more that he would lie placidly looking at the white ceiling, with an absence of light in his face until some word of mine brightened it for an instant, and then it would subside again.", "날이 갈수록 나는, 그가 흰 천장을 평온하게 바라보며 누워 있는 것을 점점 더 자주 보게 되었는데, 그의 얼굴에는 빛이 없다가, 내 어떤 말이 한순간 그것을 환하게 밝히면, 그 빛은 이내 다시 잦아들곤 했다."], ["Sometimes he was almost or quite unable to speak, then he would answer me with slight pressures on my hand, and I grew to understand his meaning very well.", "때로 그는 거의, 혹은 전혀 말을 할 수 없었고, 그러면 내 손을 가볍게 눌러 대답했는데, 나는 그의 뜻을 아주 잘 헤아리게 되었다."]], [["The number of the days had risen to ten, when I saw a greater change in him than I had seen yet.", "날수가 열흘에 이르렀을 때, 나는 지금까지 본 것보다 더 큰 변화를 그에게서 보았다."], ["His eyes were turned towards the door, and lighted up as I entered.", "그의 두 눈은 문 쪽을 향해 있었고, 내가 들어서자 환해졌다."]], [["“Dear boy,”", "\"얘야,\""], ["he said, as I sat down by his bed: “I thought you was late.", "내가 그의 침상 곁에 앉자 그가 말했다. \"네가 늦은 줄 알았다."], ["But I knowed you couldn’t be that.”", "하지만 네가 그럴 리 없다는 것도 알았지.\""]], [["“It is just the time,”", "\"꼭 시간에 맞춰 왔어요.\""], ["said I. “I waited for it at the gate.”", "내가 말했다. \"문 앞에서 기다리고 있었거든요.\""]], [["“You always waits at the gate; don’t you, dear boy?”", "\"넌 늘 문 앞에서 기다리는구나. 그렇지, 얘야?\""]], [["“Yes.", "\"네."], ["Not to lose a moment of the time.”", "한순간도 놓치지 않으려고요.\""]], [["“Thank’ee dear boy, thank’ee. God bless you!", "\"고맙다, 얘야, 고마워. 하느님의 축복이 있기를!"], ["You’ve never deserted me, dear boy.”", "넌 한 번도 나를 저버리지 않았어, 얘야.\""]], [["I pressed his hand in silence, for I could not forget that I had once meant to desert him.", "나는 말없이 그의 손을 꼭 쥐었다. 한때 내가 그를 저버리려 했던 일을 잊을 수가 없었기 때문이다."]], [["“And what’s the best of all,”", "\"그리고 무엇보다 좋은 건 말이다,\""], ["he said, “you’ve been more comfortable alonger me, since I was under a dark cloud, than when the sun shone.", "그가 말했다. \"내가 어두운 먹구름 아래 놓인 뒤로, 너는 햇빛이 비치던 때보다 나와 함께 있는 걸 더 편하게 여겼다는 거야."], ["That’s best of all.”", "그게 무엇보다 좋은 일이지.\""]], [["He lay on his back, breathing with great difficulty.", "그는 등을 대고 누운 채 몹시 힘겹게 숨을 쉬고 있었다."], ["Do what he would, and love me though he did, the light left his face ever and again, and a film came over the placid look at the white ceiling.", "무슨 짓을 하든, 또 나를 그토록 사랑하든, 그의 얼굴에서는 이따금 빛이 사라졌고, 흰 천장을 향한 그 평온한 눈빛 위로 막이 끼었다."]], [["“Are you in much pain to-day?”", "\"오늘은 많이 아프세요?\""]], [["“I don’t complain of none, dear boy.”", "\"아무것도 불평할 게 없다, 얘야.\""]], [["“You never do complain.”", "\"한 번도 불평하시는 법이 없네요.\""]], [["He had spoken his last words.", "그것이 그가 말한 마지막 말이었다."], ["He smiled, and I understood his touch to mean that he wished to lift my hand, and lay it on his breast.", "그는 미소를 지었고, 나는 그가 내 손을 들어 올려 자기 가슴에 얹어 주기를 바란다는 뜻으로 그의 손길을 알아들었다."], ["I laid it there, and he smiled again, and put both his hands upon it.", "나는 손을 거기에 얹었고, 그는 다시 미소를 지으며 두 손을 그 위에 포갰다."]], [["The allotted time ran out, while we were thus; but, looking round, I found the governor of the prison standing near me, and he whispered, “You needn’t go yet.”", "우리가 그렇게 있는 동안 허락된 면회 시간이 다 되었다. 그러나 둘러보니, 감옥의 소장이 내 곁에 서 있었고, 그가 속삭였다. \"아직 가지 않아도 됩니다.\""], ["I thanked him gratefully, and asked, “Might I speak to him, if he can hear me?”", "나는 고마운 마음으로 그에게 감사를 표하고는 물었다. \"그가 제 말을 들을 수 있다면, 그에게 말을 해도 되겠습니까?\""]], [["The governor stepped aside, and beckoned the officer away.", "소장은 옆으로 비켜서며 관리에게 물러가라고 손짓했다."], ["The change, though it was made without noise, drew back the film from the placid look at the white ceiling, and he looked most affectionately at me.", "그 변화는 소리 없이 이루어졌으나, 흰 천장을 향한 그 평온한 눈빛에서 막을 걷어 냈고, 그는 더없이 다정하게 나를 바라보았다."]], [["“Dear Magwitch, I must tell you now, at last.", "\"매그위치 아저씨, 이제 마침내 말씀드려야겠어요."], ["You understand what I say?”", "제 말을 알아들으시겠어요?\""]], [["A gentle pressure on my hand.", "내 손을 가볍게 누르는 손길."]], [["“You had a child once, whom you loved and lost.”", "\"아저씨에겐 한때 사랑했다가 잃은 아이가 있었지요.\""]], [["A stronger pressure on my hand.", "내 손을 더 힘주어 누르는 손길."]], [["“She lived, and found powerful friends.", "\"그 아이는 살아 있었고, 힘 있는 후견인들을 만났어요."], ["She is living now.", "지금도 살아 있고요."], ["She is a lady and very beautiful.", "그 아이는 숙녀가 되었고, 무척 아름다워요."], ["And I love her!”", "그리고 저는 그녀를 사랑해요!\""]], [["With a last faint effort, which would have been powerless but for my yielding to it and assisting it, he raised my hand to his lips.", "마지막 남은 희미한 힘으로 — 내가 그것에 응해 거들지 않았다면 아무 소용없었을 그 힘으로 — 그는 내 손을 자기 입술로 들어 올렸다."], ["Then, he gently let it sink upon his breast again, with his own hands lying on it.", "그러고는 자기 두 손을 그 위에 얹은 채, 다시 부드럽게 그것을 자기 가슴 위에 내려놓았다."], ["The placid look at the white ceiling came back, and passed away, and his head dropped quietly on his breast.", "흰 천장을 향한 그 평온한 눈빛이 되돌아왔다가, 사라져 갔고, 그의 머리가 조용히 가슴 위로 떨어졌다."]], [["Mindful, then, of what we had read together, I thought of the two men who went up into the Temple to pray, and I knew there were no better words that I could say beside his bed, than “O Lord, be merciful to him a sinner!”", "그때, 우리가 함께 읽었던 것을 떠올리며, 나는 기도하러 성전에 올라갔던 두 사람을 생각했다. 그리고 나는 그의 침상 곁에서 할 수 있는 말 가운데, \"오 주여, 죄인인 그에게 자비를 베푸소서!\"보다 더 나은 말은 없음을 알았다."]], [["# Chapter LVII.", "# Chapter LVII."]], [["Now that I was left wholly to myself, I gave notice of my intention to quit the chambers in the Temple as soon as my tenancy could legally determine, and in the meanwhile to underlet them.", "이제 완전히 홀로 남겨진 나는, 법적으로 임차 기간이 끝나는 대로 템플의 거처를 비우겠다는 뜻을 알리고, 그동안에는 그곳을 다시 세놓기로 했다."], ["At once I put bills up in the windows; for, I was in debt, and had scarcely any money, and began to be seriously alarmed by the state of my affairs.", "곧바로 창문에 임대 광고지를 내붙였다. 빚이 있었고 가진 돈이 거의 없어서, 내 살림 형편에 심각한 두려움을 느끼기 시작했기 때문이다."], ["I ought rather to write that I should have been alarmed if I had had energy and concentration enough to help me to the clear perception of any truth beyond the fact that I was falling very ill.", "차라리 이렇게 적어야 옳겠다. 내가 몹시 앓아 가고 있다는 사실 너머로 그 어떤 진실이든 또렷이 헤아릴 만한 기력과 집중력이 내게 있었더라면 두려움을 느꼈을 거라고."], ["The late stress upon me had enabled me to put off illness, but not to put it away; I knew that it was coming on me now, and I knew very little else, and was even careless as to that.", "근래의 긴장은 내가 병을 미뤄 두게는 해 주었으나, 떨쳐 내게 해 주지는 못했다. 나는 이제 그 병이 내게 닥쳐오고 있다는 것을 알았고, 그 외에는 거의 아무것도 알지 못했으며, 심지어 그것조차도 개의치 않았다."]], [["For a day or two, I lay on the sofa, or on the floor,—anywhere, according as I happened to sink down,—with a heavy head and aching limbs, and no purpose, and no power.", "하루 이틀 동안, 나는 소파 위에, 또는 바닥에 — 그러니까 마침 쓰러진 데가 어디든 그곳에 — 무거운 머리와 쑤시는 사지를 늘어뜨린 채, 아무런 목적도 없고 아무런 힘도 없이 누워 있었다."], ["Then there came, one night which appeared of great duration, and which teemed with anxiety and horror; and when in the morning I tried to sit up in my bed and think of it, I found I could not do so.", "그러던 어느 밤, 한없이 길게 느껴지고 불안과 공포로 가득한 밤이 찾아왔다. 그리고 아침이 되어 침대에서 몸을 일으켜 그 일을 곱씹어 보려 했을 때, 나는 그럴 수 없음을 깨달았다."]], [["Whether I really had been down in Garden Court in the dead of the night, groping about for the boat that I supposed to be there; whether I had two or three times come to myself on the staircase with great terror, not knowing how I had got out of bed; whether I had found myself lighting the lamp, possessed by the idea that he was coming up the stairs, and that the lights were blown out; whether I had been inexpressibly harassed by the distracted talking, laughing, and groaning of some one, and had half suspected those sounds to be of my own making; whether there had been a closed iron furnace in a dark corner of the room, and a voice had called out, over and over again, that Miss Havisham was consuming within it,—these were things that I tried to settle with myself and get into some order, as I lay that morning on my bed.", "내가 정말로 한밤중에 가든 코트로 내려가, 거기 있으리라고 여긴 보트를 더듬어 찾았던 것인지, 내가 어떻게 침대에서 빠져나왔는지도 모른 채 두세 번이나 큰 공포에 휩싸여 계단에서 제정신을 차렸던 것인지, 그가 계단을 올라오고 있으며 등불이 다 꺼졌다는 생각에 사로잡혀 등불을 켜고 있는 나 자신을 발견했던 것인지, 누군가가 정신없이 떠들고 웃고 신음하는 소리에 형언할 수 없을 만큼 시달렸으며 그 소리가 실은 내가 내는 소리가 아닐까 반쯤 의심했던 것인지, 방 어두운 구석에 닫힌 쇠 화로가 하나 있어 어떤 목소리가 미스 해비셤이 그 안에서 타고 있다고 거듭거듭 외쳐 댔던 것인지 — 이런 것들이, 그 아침 침대에 누운 채 내가 스스로 정리하여 어떤 질서를 잡아 보려 애쓰던 일들이었다."], ["But the vapour of a limekiln would come between me and them, disordering them all, and it was through the vapour at last that I saw two men looking at me.", "그러나 석회 가마의 수증기가 나와 그것들 사이로 끼어들어 모조리 흐트러뜨리곤 했고, 마침내 그 수증기 너머로 나는 두 사내가 나를 바라보고 있는 것을 보았다."]], [["“What do you want?”", "\"무슨 일이오?\""], ["I asked, starting; “I don’t know you.”", "나는 흠칫 놀라 물었다. \"당신들을 모르는데.\""]], [["“Well, sir,”", "\"저, 선생님.\""], ["returned one of them, bending down and touching me on the shoulder, “this is a matter that you’ll soon arrange, I dare say, but you’re arrested.”", "그중 하나가 몸을 굽혀 내 어깨를 짚으며 대답했다. \"이건 곧 정리하실 수 있는 일일 겁니다, 아마도. 하지만 선생님은 체포되셨습니다.\""]], [["“What is the debt?”", "\"빚이 얼마요?\""]], [["“Hundred and twenty-three pound, fifteen, six.", "\"백이십삼 파운드 십오 실링 육 펜스입니다."], ["Jeweller’s account, I think.”", "제 생각엔 보석상 외상값인 것 같군요.\""]], [["“What is to be done?”", "\"어떻게 해야 하오?\""]], [["“You had better come to my house,”", "\"제 집으로 가시는 게 좋겠습니다.\""], ["said the man.", "사내가 말했다."], ["“I keep a very nice house.”", "\"아주 괜찮은 집을 운영하고 있지요.\""]], [["I made some attempt to get up and dress myself.", "나는 일어나 옷을 입어 보려고 얼마간 애썼다."], ["When I next attended to them, they were standing a little off from the bed, looking at me. I still lay there.", "그러나 다음번에 그들에게 주의를 돌렸을 때, 그들은 침대에서 조금 떨어져 서서 나를 바라보고 있었다. 나는 여전히 그곳에 누워 있었다."]], [["“You see my state,”", "\"내 상태를 보시오.\""], ["said I. “I would come with you if I could; but indeed I am quite unable.", "내가 말했다. \"갈 수 있다면 당신들과 가겠소만, 정말로 도저히 갈 수가 없소."], ["If you take me from here, I think I shall die by the way.”", "여기서 나를 데려간다면, 나는 가는 길에 죽고 말 거요.\""]], [["Perhaps they replied, or argued the point, or tried to encourage me to believe that I was better than I thought.", "아마도 그들은 대답을 했거나, 그 점을 두고 따졌거나, 내가 생각하는 것보다 상태가 낫다고 믿도록 나를 격려하려 했을 것이다."], ["Forasmuch as they hang in my memory by only this one slender thread, I don’t know what they did, except that they forbore to remove me.", "내 기억 속에 오직 이 한 가닥 가느다란 실로만 매달려 있는 까닭에, 그들이 나를 옮기기를 보류했다는 것 말고는 무엇을 했는지 나는 알지 못한다."]], [["That I had a fever and was avoided, that I suffered greatly, that I often lost my reason, that the time seemed interminable, that I confounded impossible existences with my own identity; that I was a brick in the house-wall, and yet entreating to be released from the giddy place where the builders had set me; that I was a steel beam of a vast engine, clashing and whirling over a gulf, and yet that I implored in my own person to have the engine stopped, and my part in it hammered off; that I passed through these phases of disease, I know of my own remembrance, and did in some sort know at the time.", "내가 열병에 걸려 사람들이 나를 피했다는 것, 내가 몹시 고통받았다는 것, 내가 자주 이성을 잃었다는 것, 시간이 끝없이 느껴졌다는 것, 내가 있을 수 없는 존재들과 내 정체성을 뒤섞었다는 것, 내가 집의 벽에 박힌 벽돌이 되어 건축공들이 나를 박아 놓은 그 아찔한 자리에서 빼내 달라고 애원했다는 것, 내가 거대한 기관의 강철 들보가 되어 심연 위에서 부딪치며 빙빙 돌면서도 내 자신의 몸으로 그 기관을 멈추고 거기 박힌 내 부분을 두드려 떼어 달라고 간청했다는 것, 내가 병의 이런 국면들을 거쳤다는 것을, 나는 내 기억으로 알고 있으며, 당시에도 어느 정도는 알고 있었다."], ["That I sometimes struggled with real people, in the belief that they were murderers, and that I would all at once comprehend that they meant to do me good, and would then sink exhausted in their arms, and suffer them to lay me down, I also knew at the time.", "내가 때때로 진짜 사람들을 살인자라 믿고서 그들과 몸싸움을 벌이다가, 별안간 그들이 내게 좋은 일을 하려 한다는 것을 깨닫고는 지쳐 그들의 품에 쓰러져 그들이 나를 눕히도록 내맡겼다는 것 또한 당시에도 알고 있었다."], ["But, above all, I knew that there was a constant tendency in all these people,—who, when I was very ill, would present all kinds of extraordinary transformations of the human face, and would be much dilated in size,—above all, I say, I knew that there was an extraordinary tendency in all these people, sooner or later, to settle down into the likeness of Joe.", "그러나 무엇보다도 나는, 이 모든 사람들에게 — 내가 몹시 앓을 때면 온갖 기이한 모습으로 인간의 얼굴을 변형시키고 몸집이 한껏 부풀어 오르던 이 사람들에게 — 무엇보다도, 다시 말하건대 이 모든 사람들에게, 머잖아 조의 모습으로 가라앉아 정착하려는 기이한 경향이 있다는 것을 알고 있었다."]], [["After I had turned the worst point of my illness, I began to notice that while all its other features changed, this one consistent feature did not change.", "병의 고비를 넘긴 뒤, 나는 병의 다른 온갖 양상은 변하면서도 이 한 가지 일관된 양상만은 변하지 않는다는 것을 알아채기 시작했다."], ["Whoever came about me, still settled down into Joe. I opened my eyes in the night, and I saw, in the great chair at the bedside, Joe.", "누가 내 곁에 오든 여전히 조의 모습으로 가라앉아 정착했다. 밤에 눈을 뜨면, 침대 곁 큰 안락의자에 앉은 조가 보였다."], ["I opened my eyes in the day, and, sitting on the window-seat, smoking his pipe in the shaded open window, still I saw Joe.", "낮에 눈을 뜨면, 차양을 드리운 열린 창가 자리에 앉아 파이프를 피우는 조가 여전히 보였다."], ["I asked for cooling drink, and the dear hand that gave it me was Joe’s.", "시원한 마실 것을 청하면, 그것을 건네주는 그 정다운 손이 조의 손이었다."], ["I sank back on my pillow after drinking, and the face that looked so hopefully and tenderly upon me was the face of Joe.", "마시고 나서 다시 베개에 몸을 누이면, 그토록 희망에 차고 다정하게 나를 굽어보는 얼굴이 조의 얼굴이었다."]], [["At last, one day, I took courage, and said, “Is it Joe?”", "마침내 어느 날, 나는 용기를 내어 말했다. \"이게 정말 조예요?\""]], [["And the dear old home-voice answered, “Which it air, old chap.”", "그러자 그 정다운 옛 고향의 목소리가 대답했다. \"그래 나다, 우리 친구야.\""]], [["“O Joe, you break my heart!", "\"아, 조, 가슴이 미어져요!"], ["Look angry at me, Joe.", "나한테 화를 내요, 조."], ["Strike me, Joe.", "나를 때려요, 조."], ["Tell me of my ingratitude. Don’t be so good to me!”", "내 배은망덕을 꾸짖어요. 나한테 그렇게 잘해 주지 말아요!\""]], [["For Joe had actually laid his head down on the pillow at my side, and put his arm round my neck, in his joy that I knew him.", "내가 자기를 알아본 기쁨에, 조는 정말로 내 곁 베개에 머리를 누이고 한 팔로 내 목을 감싸 안았던 것이다."]], [["“Which dear old Pip, old chap,”", "\"우리 정다운 핍, 친구야.\""], ["said Joe, “you and me was ever friends.", "조가 말했다. \"너랑 나랑은 언제나 친구였잖니."], ["And when you’re well enough to go out for a ride—what larks!”", "그리고 네가 마차 타고 바깥바람 쐴 만큼 나으면 — 얼마나 신날까!\""]], [["After which, Joe withdrew to the window, and stood with his back towards me, wiping his eyes.", "그러고 나서 조는 창가로 물러나, 내게 등을 돌린 채 서서 눈을 닦았다."], ["And as my extreme weakness prevented me from getting up and going to him, I lay there, penitently whispering, “O God bless him!", "그리고 극심한 쇠약 탓에 나는 일어나 그에게 다가갈 수가 없었으므로, 그저 거기 누워 참회하듯 속삭였다. \"오, 신이여 그를 축복하소서!"], ["O God bless this gentle Christian man!”", "오, 신이여 이 온화한 기독교인을 축복하소서!\""]], [["Joe’s eyes were red when I next found him beside me; but I was holding his hand, and we both felt happy.", "다음번에 내 곁에서 조를 보았을 때 그의 눈은 붉어져 있었다. 그러나 나는 그의 손을 잡고 있었고, 우리 둘 다 행복했다."]], [["“How long, dear Joe?”", "\"얼마나 됐어요, 조?\""]], [["“Which you meantersay, Pip, how long have your illness lasted, dear old chap?”", "\"네 말은, 핍, 네 병이 얼마나 오래갔느냐는 거지, 우리 친구야?\""]], [["“Yes, Joe.”", "\"네, 조.\""]], [["“It’s the end of May, Pip.", "\"오월 말이다, 핍."], ["To-morrow is the first of June.”", "내일이 유월 초하루지.\""]], [["“And have you been here all that time, dear Joe?”", "\"그럼 그 내내 여기 있었던 거예요, 조?\""]], [["“Pretty nigh, old chap.", "\"거의 그랬지, 친구야."], ["For, as I says to Biddy when the news of your being ill were brought by letter, which it were brought by the post, and being formerly single he is now married though underpaid for a deal of walking and shoe-leather, but wealth were not a object on his part, and marriage were the great wish of his hart—”", "네가 아프다는 소식이 편지로 전해졌을 때, 그러니까 우편으로 왔을 때 비디한테 내가 말했지, 그 사람이 전엔 홀몸이었는데 이젠 결혼을 했지만 많이 걷고 구두 가죽 닳리는 거에 비하면 박봉이긴 해도, 재산은 그 사람 쪽에서 따질 게 못 됐고, 결혼이야말로 그 사람 가슴속의 큰 소원이었으니—\""]], [["“It is so delightful to hear you, Joe!", "\"당신 말을 듣는 게 너무 즐거워요, 조!"], ["But I interrupt you in what you said to Biddy.”", "그런데 비디한테 무슨 말을 했는지 도중에 끊었네요.\""]], [["“Which it were,”", "\"그러니까 말이지.\""], ["said Joe, “that how you might be amongst strangers, and that how you and me having been ever friends, a wisit at such a moment might not prove unacceptabobble.", "조가 말했다. \"네가 낯선 사람들 틈에 있을지도 모르겠고, 너랑 나랑은 언제나 친구였으니, 이런 때에 한 번 들여다보는 게 영 달갑잖은 일은 아닐 거라고 했지."], ["And Biddy, her word were, ‘Go to him, without loss of time.’ That,”", "그랬더니 비디 말이, '한시도 지체 말고 그에게 가세요.' 그게 말이다.\""], ["said Joe, summing up with his judicial air, “were the word of Biddy.", "조가 판관 같은 태도로 매듭지으며 말했다. \"비디의 말이었어. '그에게 가세요.'"], ["‘Go to him,’ Biddy say, ‘without loss of time.’ In short, I shouldn’t greatly deceive you,”", "비디가 그러더라, '한시도 지체 말고.' 한마디로.\" 조가 잠시 진지하게 곱씹은 뒤 덧붙였다."], ["Joe added, after a little grave reflection, “if I represented to you that the word of that young woman were, ‘without a minute’s loss of time.’”", "\"그 젊은 여자의 말이 '일 분도 지체 말고'였다고 네게 전한다 해도, 나는 너를 크게 속이는 게 아닐 거다.\""]], [["There Joe cut himself short, and informed me that I was to be talked to in great moderation, and that I was to take a little nourishment at stated frequent times, whether I felt inclined for it or not, and that I was to submit myself to all his orders. So I kissed his hand, and lay quiet, while he proceeded to indite a note to Biddy, with my love in it.", "거기서 조는 말을 뚝 끊고, 내게 아주 적당히만 말을 시켜야 한다고, 그리고 내가 내키든 내키지 않든 정해진 시간마다 자주 영양분을 조금씩 섭취해야 한다고, 그리고 내가 그의 모든 지시에 따라야 한다고 일러 주었다. 그래서 나는 그의 손에 입을 맞추고 조용히 누워 있었고, 그동안 그는 내 사랑을 담아 비디에게 보내는 짧은 편지를 쓰기 시작했다."]], [["Evidently Biddy had taught Joe to write.", "비디가 조에게 글쓰기를 가르친 게 분명했다."], ["As I lay in bed looking at him, it made me, in my weak state, cry again with pleasure to see the pride with which he set about his letter.", "침대에 누워 그를 바라보고 있자니, 그가 편지에 착수하는 모습에 깃든 그 자부심을 보고서 쇠약해진 나는 기쁨에 또다시 눈물이 났다."], ["My bedstead, divested of its curtains, had been removed, with me upon it, into the sitting-room, as the airiest and largest, and the carpet had been taken away, and the room kept always fresh and wholesome night and day.", "커튼을 떼어 낸 내 침대는, 나를 그 위에 누인 채로, 가장 바람이 잘 통하고 가장 넓은 거실로 옮겨져 있었으며, 양탄자는 걷어 냈고, 방은 밤낮으로 늘 신선하고 청결하게 유지되었다."], ["At my own writing-table, pushed into a corner and cumbered with little bottles, Joe now sat down to his great work, first choosing a pen from the pen-tray as if it were a chest of large tools, and tucking up his sleeves as if he were going to wield a crow-bar or sledgehammer.", "한쪽 구석으로 밀어 두어 작은 약병들이 어수선하게 놓인 내 책상에, 조는 이제 그 위대한 작업을 위해 앉았다. 먼저 펜꽂이에서 펜을 하나 고르는데 마치 커다란 연장이 든 상자에서 고르는 듯했고, 마치 쇠지레나 큰 망치를 휘두를 것처럼 소매를 걷어붙였다."], ["It was necessary for Joe to hold on heavily to the table with his left elbow, and to get his right leg well out behind him, before he could begin; and when he did begin he made every downstroke so slowly that it might have been six feet long, while at every upstroke I could hear his pen spluttering extensively.", "조는 시작하기에 앞서 왼쪽 팔꿈치로 책상을 묵직하게 짚고 오른쪽 다리를 뒤로 한껏 빼야만 했다. 그리고 막상 시작하자, 내려긋는 획마다 어찌나 천천히 그었던지 그 길이가 육 피트는 됨 직했고, 올려긋는 획마다 그의 펜이 사방으로 잉크를 튀기며 긁어 대는 소리가 들렸다."], ["He had a curious idea that the inkstand was on the side of him where it was not, and constantly dipped his pen into space, and seemed quite satisfied with the result.", "그는 잉크병이 실제 놓인 쪽이 아닌 반대쪽에 있다는 묘한 생각을 품고서, 끊임없이 펜을 허공에 적셔 댔고, 그 결과에 자못 만족한 듯 보였다."], ["Occasionally, he was tripped up by some orthographical stumbling-block; but on the whole he got on very well indeed; and when he had signed his name, and had removed a finishing blot from the paper to the crown of his head with his two forefingers, he got up and hovered about the table, trying the effect of his performance from various points of view, as it lay there, with unbounded satisfaction.", "이따금 어떤 철자상의 걸림돌에 발이 걸리기도 했으나, 전체적으로는 정말이지 아주 잘해 나갔다. 그리고 자기 이름을 서명하고, 마무리로 종이에 묻은 얼룩 한 점을 두 검지로 떠서 자기 정수리에 옮겨 붙인 뒤, 그는 일어나 책상 주위를 맴돌며, 거기 놓인 자기 작품의 효과를 이런저런 각도에서 한없는 만족감으로 가늠해 보았다."]], [["Not to make Joe uneasy by talking too much, even if I had been able to talk much, I deferred asking him about Miss Havisham until next day.", "말을 너무 많이 해서 조를 불편하게 하지 않으려고 — 설령 내가 많이 말할 수 있었다 하더라도 — 나는 미스 해비셤에 대해 묻는 일을 다음 날로 미뤘다."], ["He shook his head when I then asked him if she had recovered.", "그래서 그녀가 회복되었는지 물었을 때, 조는 고개를 가로저었다."]], [["“Is she dead, Joe?”", "\"돌아가셨어요, 조?\""]], [["“Why you see, old chap,”", "\"저, 그게 말이다, 친구야.\" 조가 만류하는 듯한 어조로, 차근차근 본론에 다가갈 양으로 말했다."], ["said Joe, in a tone of remonstrance, and by way of getting at it by degrees, “I wouldn’t go so far as to say that, for that’s a deal to say; but she ain’t—”", "\"거기까지 말하진 않으련다, 그건 너무 큰 말이니까. 하지만 그분은—\" \"살아 계시지 않다는 거죠, 조?\""]], [["“Living, Joe?” “That’s nigher where it is,”", "\"그게 더 가까운 말이지.\" 조가 말했다."], ["said Joe; “she ain’t living.”", "\"그분은 살아 계시지 않아.\""]], [["“Did she linger long, Joe?”", "\"오래 버티셨어요, 조?\""]], [["“Arter you was took ill, pretty much about what you might call (if you was put to it) a week,”", "\"네가 앓아눕고 나서, 굳이 따지자면 한 일주일쯤이라 할 만했지.\" 조가 말했다."], ["said Joe; still determined, on my account, to come at everything by degrees.", "여전히 나를 위해 모든 것에 차근차근 다가가기로 작정한 듯했다."]], [["“Dear Joe, have you heard what becomes of her property?”", "\"정다운 조, 그분 재산이 어떻게 됐는지 들었어요?\""]], [["“Well, old chap,”", "\"저, 친구야.\""], ["said Joe, “it do appear that she had settled the most of it, which I meantersay tied it up, on Miss Estella.", "조가 말했다. \"그분이 그 대부분을 미스 에스텔라 앞으로 정리해 두신 것 같더라, 그러니까 묶어 두셨다는 말이지."], ["But she had wrote out a little coddleshell in her own hand a day or two afore the accident, leaving a cool four thousand to Mr. Matthew Pocket.", "그런데 사고 나기 하루 이틀 전에 직접 손으로 작은 추가 유언장을 써 두셔서, 매슈 포켓 씨한테 사천 파운드를 거뜬히 남기셨다는구나."], ["And why, do you suppose, above all things, Pip, she left that cool four thousand unto him?", "그런데 핍, 무엇보다도 그분이 왜 그 거뜬한 사천 파운드를 그에게 남기셨는지 짐작이 가니?"], ["‘Because of Pip’s account of him, the said Matthew.’ I am told by Biddy, that air the writing,”", "'핍이 그에 대해, 그러니까 그 매슈에 대해 한 이야기 덕분에.' 비디한테 들었는데, 그게 그 글귀였다더라.\""], ["said Joe, repeating the legal turn as if it did him infinite good, “‘account of him the said Matthew.’ And a cool four thousand, Pip!”", "조가 그 법률적 어투를 한없이 흐뭇해하며 되풀이했다. \"'그에 대해, 그러니까 그 매슈에 대한 이야기.' 거기다 거뜬한 사천 파운드라니, 핍!\""]], [["I never discovered from whom Joe derived the conventional temperature of the four thousand pounds; but it appeared to make the sum of money more to him, and he had a manifest relish in insisting on its being cool.", "조가 그 사천 파운드에 으레 붙이던 '거뜬한'이라는 표현을 누구에게서 따왔는지 나는 끝내 알아내지 못했다. 그러나 그것이 그에게는 그 금액을 더욱 대단하게 만들어 주는 듯했고, 그는 그것이 거뜬하다고 우기는 데서 역력한 즐거움을 느꼈다."]], [["This account gave me great joy, as it perfected the only good thing I had done.", "이 이야기는 내게 큰 기쁨을 주었으니, 내가 한 유일한 선한 일을 완성해 준 셈이었기 때문이다."], ["I asked Joe whether he had heard if any of the other relations had any legacies?", "나는 조에게 다른 친척들 가운데 유산을 받은 이가 있는지 들었느냐고 물었다."]], [["“Miss Sarah,”", "\"미스 새러 말이다.\""], ["said Joe, “she have twenty-five pound perannium fur to buy pills, on account of being bilious.", "조가 말했다. \"담즙 과다라서 알약 사 먹으라고 매년 이십오 파운드를 받는다는구나."], ["Miss Georgiana, she have twenty pound down.", "미스 조지애나는 이십 파운드를 일시불로 받고."], ["Mrs.—what’s the name of them wild beasts with humps, old chap?”", "그리고 어, 혹 달린 그 야수들 이름이 뭐였더라, 친구야?\""]], [["“Camels?”", "\"낙타요?\""], ["said I, wondering why he could possibly want to know.", "나는 그가 그걸 왜 알고 싶어 하는지 의아해하며 말했다."]], [["Joe nodded. “Mrs. Camels,”", "조는 고개를 끄덕였다."], ["by which I presently understood he meant Camilla, “she have five pound fur to buy rushlights to put her in spirits when she wake up in the night.”", "\"낙타 부인 말이다.\" 잠시 후 나는 그가 커밀라를 두고 하는 말임을 알아들었다. \"그분은 밤중에 잠 깰 때 기운 차리게 골풀 양초 사 두라고 오 파운드를 받는다는구나.\""]], [["The accuracy of these recitals was sufficiently obvious to me, to give me great confidence in Joe’s information.", "이 이야기들의 정확성은 내게 충분히 명백했으므로, 조의 정보에 큰 신뢰가 갔다. \"그리고 이제.\""], ["“And now,” said Joe, “you ain’t that strong yet, old chap, that you can take in more nor one additional shovelful to-day.", "조가 말했다. \"넌 아직 그렇게 튼튼하진 않아서, 오늘 한 삽 이상 더 떠넣을 형편은 못 된다, 친구야."], ["Old Orlick he’s been a bustin’ open a dwelling-ouse.”", "늙은 올릭이 어느 집을 부수고 들어갔단다.\""]], [["“Whose?”", "\"누구 집을요?\""], ["said I.", "내가 물었다."]], [["“Not, I grant you, but what his manners is given to blusterous,”", "\"하기야 그자 행실이 떠들썩하게 굴기 일쑤인 건 인정하다만.\""], ["said Joe, apologetically; “still, a Englishman’s ouse is his Castle, and castles must not be busted ’cept when done in war time.", "조가 변명하듯 말했다. \"그래도 영국인의 집은 그 사람의 성채이고, 성채는 전시가 아니면 부숴서는 안 되는 법이지."], ["And wotsume’er the failings on his part, he were a corn and seedsman in his hart.”", "그리고 그 사람 쪽의 결점이 무엇이든 간에, 그는 가슴속으로는 곡물상이자 종묘상이었어.\""]], [["“Is it Pumblechook’s house that has been broken into, then?”", "\"그럼 펌블추크의 집이 털린 거예요?\""]], [["“That’s it, Pip,”", "\"바로 그거다, 핍.\""], ["said Joe; “and they took his till, and they took his cash-box, and they drinked his wine, and they partook of his wittles, and they slapped his face, and they pulled his nose, and they tied him up to his bedpust, and they giv’ him a dozen, and they stuffed his mouth full of flowering annuals to prewent his crying out.", "조가 말했다. \"놈들이 그의 돈궤를 가져갔고, 금고를 가져갔고, 그의 포도주를 마셨고, 그의 음식을 나눠 먹었고, 그의 뺨을 갈겼고, 그의 코를 잡아당겼고, 그를 침대 기둥에 묶었고, 매를 열두 대 안겼고, 소리치지 못하게 그의 입에 일년생 꽃을 잔뜩 쑤셔 넣었지."], ["But he knowed Orlick, and Orlick’s in the county jail.”", "하지만 그가 올릭을 알아봤고, 올릭은 지금 군 감옥에 있다.\""]], [["By these approaches we arrived at unrestricted conversation.", "이런 식으로 다가가다 보니 우리는 거리낌 없는 대화에 이르렀다."], ["I was slow to gain strength, but I did slowly and surely become less weak, and Joe stayed with me, and I fancied I was little Pip again.", "나는 더디게 기력을 회복했으나, 더디지만 분명하게 점점 덜 쇠약해졌고, 조는 내 곁에 머물렀으며, 나는 내가 다시 어린 핍이 된 듯한 기분이 들었다."]], [["For the tenderness of Joe was so beautifully proportioned to my need, that I was like a child in his hands.", "조의 다정함이 내 필요에 어찌나 아름답게 들어맞았던지, 나는 그의 손안에서 어린아이 같았다."], ["He would sit and talk to me in the old confidence, and with the old simplicity, and in the old unassertive protecting way, so that I would half believe that all my life since the days of the old kitchen was one of the mental troubles of the fever that was gone.", "그는 앉아서 옛 그대로의 격의 없음으로, 옛 그대로의 소박함으로, 옛 그대로의 나서지 않으면서 감싸 주는 태도로 내게 말을 건넸고, 그래서 나는 옛 부엌 시절 이후의 내 모든 삶이 이제는 지나가 버린 그 열병이 빚어낸 정신적 착란의 하나였던 게 아닐까 반쯤 믿을 지경이었다."], ["He did everything for me except the household work, for which he had engaged a very decent woman, after paying off the laundress on his first arrival.", "그는 집안일만 빼고 나를 위해 모든 것을 해 주었는데, 그 집안일을 위해 그는 처음 도착했을 때 세탁부를 내보낸 뒤 아주 점잖은 여자를 한 사람 고용해 두었다."], ["“Which I do assure you, Pip,”", "\"정말이지 단언하건대, 핍.\""], ["he would often say, in explanation of that liberty; “I found her a tapping the spare bed, like a cask of beer, and drawing off the feathers in a bucket, for sale.", "그는 그 멋대로 한 일을 해명하느라 곧잘 이렇게 말하곤 했다. \"그 여편네가 손님용 침대를 맥주통처럼 콕콕 찔러서, 깃털을 양동이에 뽑아내 팔아먹고 있더라니까."], ["Which she would have tapped yourn next, and draw’d it off with you a laying on it, and was then a carrying away the coals gradiwally in the soup-tureen and wegetable-dishes, and the wine and spirits in your Wellington boots.”", "다음번엔 네가 쓰는 침대도 찔러서, 네가 그 위에 누워 있는 채로 뽑아낼 판이었고, 그러고는 석탄을 수프 그릇이랑 야채 접시에 담아 조금씩 빼돌리고, 포도주랑 독한 술은 네 웰링턴 부츠에 담아 날라 가고 있었지.\""]], [["We looked forward to the day when I should go out for a ride, as we had once looked forward to the day of my apprenticeship.", "우리는 내가 마차를 타고 바깥바람을 쐬러 나갈 날을, 한때 내 도제 입문 날을 손꼽아 기다렸던 것처럼 손꼽아 기다렸다."], ["And when the day came, and an open carriage was got into the Lane, Joe wrapped me up, took me in his arms, carried me down to it, and put me in, as if I were still the small helpless creature to whom he had so abundantly given of the wealth of his great nature.", "그리고 그날이 와서 골목에 포장 없는 마차가 대기되자, 조는 나를 둘둘 싸매고 두 팔로 안아 그것까지 데려가 태웠다. 마치 내가 여전히, 그가 자기의 위대한 천성의 풍요로움을 그토록 아낌없이 나누어 주었던 그 작고 무력한 존재인 것처럼."]], [["And Joe got in beside me, and we drove away together into the country, where the rich summer growth was already on the trees and on the grass, and sweet summer scents filled all the air.", "그리고 조는 내 곁에 올라탔고, 우리는 함께 시골로 마차를 몰아 나갔다. 그곳에서는 풍성한 여름의 생장이 이미 나무와 풀에 깃들어 있었고, 달콤한 여름 향내가 온 대기를 가득 채웠다."], ["The day happened to be Sunday, and when I looked on the loveliness around me, and thought how it had grown and changed, and how the little wild-flowers had been forming, and the voices of the birds had been strengthening, by day and by night, under the sun and under the stars, while poor I lay burning and tossing on my bed, the mere remembrance of having burned and tossed there came like a check upon my peace.", "마침 그날은 일요일이었는데, 내 주위의 사랑스러움을 바라보며, 그것이 어떻게 자라고 변해 왔는지를, 그리고 가엾은 내가 침대 위에서 불덩이가 되어 뒤척이는 동안에도 작은 들꽃들이 어떻게 피어났고 새들의 노랫소리가 어떻게 낮이고 밤이고, 태양 아래서 또 별빛 아래서 어떻게 우렁차졌는지를 생각하니, 거기서 불덩이가 되어 뒤척였다는 기억 그 자체가 내 평온함에 제동을 거는 듯이 다가왔다."], ["But when I heard the Sunday bells, and looked around a little more upon the outspread beauty, I felt that I was not nearly thankful enough,—that I was too weak yet to be even that,—and I laid my head on Joe’s shoulder, as I had laid it long ago when he had taken me to the Fair or where not, and it was too much for my young senses.", "그러나 일요일의 종소리를 듣고, 펼쳐진 아름다움을 좀 더 둘러보았을 때, 나는 내가 충분히 감사하기에는 한참 모자란다는 것을 — 아직 그러기에도 너무 쇠약하다는 것을 — 느꼈고, 오래전에 조가 나를 장터며 이런저런 곳에 데려갔을 때 그랬듯이 그의 어깨에 머리를 기댔는데, 그것은 어린 내 감각에 벅찬 일이었다."]], [["More composure came to me after a while, and we talked as we used to talk, lying on the grass at the old Battery.", "얼마 후 나는 좀 더 평정을 되찾았고, 우리는 옛 포대의 풀밭에 누워 그러곤 했듯이 이야기를 나누었다."], ["There was no change whatever in Joe.", "조에게는 아무런 변화도 없었다."], ["Exactly what he had been in my eyes then, he was in my eyes still; just as simply faithful, and as simply right.", "그때 내 눈에 비쳤던 모습 그대로, 그는 여전히 내 눈에 비쳤다. 그저 한결같이 충실했고, 그저 한결같이 옳았다."]], [["When we got back again, and he lifted me out, and carried me—so easily!—across the court and up the stairs, I thought of that eventful Christmas Day when he had carried me over the marshes.", "다시 돌아와 그가 나를 안아 내려, 어찌나 수월하게! 안마당을 가로질러 계단을 올라 데려갈 때, 나는 그가 나를 안고 습지를 가로질러 데려갔던 그 잊지 못할 크리스마스 날을 떠올렸다."], ["We had not yet made any allusion to my change of fortune, nor did I know how much of my late history he was acquainted with.", "우리는 내 운명의 변화에 대해서는 아직 아무 언급도 한 적이 없었고, 그가 내 근래의 내력을 얼마나 알고 있는지도 나는 알지 못했다."], ["I was so doubtful of myself now, and put so much trust in him, that I could not satisfy myself whether I ought to refer to it when he did not.", "나는 이제 나 자신에 대해 너무도 미덥지 못했고 그를 너무도 신뢰했던 까닭에, 그가 그것을 들먹이지 않는데도 내가 거기 대해 말을 꺼내야 옳은지 어떤지 스스로 판단을 내릴 수가 없었다."]], [["“Have you heard, Joe,”", "\"들었어요, 조.\""], ["I asked him that evening, upon further consideration, as he smoked his pipe at the window, “who my patron was?”", "그날 저녁, 그가 창가에서 파이프를 피울 때, 나는 곰곰이 생각한 끝에 그에게 물었다. \"내 후원자가 누구였는지?\""]], [["“I heerd,”", "\"내가 듣기론.\""], ["returned Joe, “as it were not Miss Havisham, old chap.”", "조가 대답했다. \"미스 해비셤은 아니었다더라, 친구야.\""]], [["“Did you hear who it was, Joe?”", "\"누구였는지 들었어요, 조?\""]], [["“Well! I heerd as it were a person what sent the person what giv’ you the bank-notes at the Jolly Bargemen, Pip.”", "\"글쎄! 졸리 바지맨에서 너한테 그 지폐를 건넨 사람을 보낸 그 사람이었다고 듣긴 했다, 핍.\""]], [["“So it was.”", "\"그래요, 그랬죠.\""]], [["“Astonishing!”", "\"놀라운 일이지!\""], ["said Joe, in the placidest way.", "조가 더없이 차분하게 말했다."]], [["“Did you hear that he was dead, Joe?”", "\"그 사람이 죽었다는 것도 들었어요, 조?\""], ["I presently asked, with increasing diffidence.", "나는 이내 점점 더 머뭇거리며 물었다."]], [["“Which?", "\"누구?"], ["Him as sent the bank-notes, Pip?”", "그 지폐를 보낸 사람 말이냐, 핍?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"네.\""]], [["“I think,”", "\"내 생각엔 말이다.\""], ["said Joe, after meditating a long time, and looking rather evasively at the window-seat, “as I did hear tell that how he were something or another in a general way in that direction.”", "조가 한참 곰곰이 생각하고 나서, 창가 자리를 다소 피하는 듯한 눈길로 보며 말했다. \"그가 그쪽 방면에서 대충 무슨 일인가에 관련돼 있다는 얘긴 듣긴 들은 것 같다.\""]], [["“Did you hear anything of his circumstances, Joe?”", "\"그의 처지에 대해 뭔가 들은 게 있어요, 조?\""]], [["“Not partickler, Pip.”", "\"특별한 건 없다, 핍.\""]], [["“If you would like to hear, Joe—”", "\"혹시 듣고 싶으면, 조—\""], ["I was beginning, when Joe got up and came to my sofa.", "내가 말을 꺼내려는데, 조가 일어나 내 소파로 다가왔다."]], [["“Lookee here, old chap,”", "\"이봐라, 친구야.\""], ["said Joe, bending over me.", "조가 내 위로 몸을 굽히며 말했다."], ["“Ever the best of friends; ain’t us, Pip?”", "\"우린 늘 둘도 없는 친구잖니, 안 그러냐, 핍?\""]], [["I was ashamed to answer him.", "나는 부끄러워 그에게 대답할 수가 없었다."]], [["“Wery good, then,”", "\"그럼 됐다.\""], ["said Joe, as if I had answered; “that’s all right; that’s agreed upon.", "마치 내가 대답이라도 한 것처럼 조가 말했다. \"그럼 괜찮아. 그렇게 합의된 거다."], ["Then why go into subjects, old chap, which as betwixt two sech must be for ever onnecessary?", "그럼 우리 같은 두 사람 사이에 영영 불필요할 수밖에 없는 그런 주제는 왜 파고들겠니?"], ["There’s subjects enough as betwixt two sech, without onnecessary ones. Lord!", "우리 같은 두 사람 사이엔 불필요한 것들 말고도 이야깃거리가 충분하잖아. 원, 세상에!"], ["To think of your poor sister and her Rampages!", "네 가엾은 누나랑 그 누나의 발작을 생각해 봐라!"], ["And don’t you remember Tickler?”", "그리고 티클러 기억 안 나니?\""]], [["“I do indeed, Joe.”", "\"정말 기억나요, 조.\""]], [["“Lookee here, old chap,” said Joe.", "\"이봐라, 친구야.\" 조가 말했다."], ["“I done what I could to keep you and Tickler in sunders, but my power were not always fully equal to my inclinations.", "\"나는 너랑 티클러를 떼어 놓으려고 할 수 있는 건 다 했다만, 내 힘이 늘 내 마음만큼 충분치는 못했지."], ["For when your poor sister had a mind to drop into you, it were not so much,”", "네 가엾은 누나가 너를 한바탕 두들겨 패려고 마음먹으면 말이다.\""], ["said Joe, in his favourite argumentative way, “that she dropped into me too, if I put myself in opposition to her, but that she dropped into you always heavier for it.", "조가 즐겨 쓰는 따지는 투로 말했다. \"내가 누나한테 맞서면 누나가 나까지 두들겨 팼다는 게 문제가 아니라, 그 때문에 너를 늘 더 호되게 두들겨 팼다는 게 문제였지."], ["I noticed that. It ain’t a grab at a man’s whisker, not yet a shake or two of a man (to which your sister was quite welcome), that ’ud put a man off from getting a little child out of punishment.", "나는 그걸 알아챘다. 사내 수염을 한번 잡아채거나, 사내를 한두 번 흔들어 댄다고 해서 (네 누나야 얼마든지 그래도 좋았다만) 어린아이를 매에서 구해 내려는 마음이 꺾이는 건 아니야."], ["But when that little child is dropped into heavier for that grab of whisker or shaking, then that man naterally up and says to himself, ‘Where is the good as you are a-doing?", "하지만 그 수염 잡아채기나 흔들어 대기 때문에 그 어린아이가 더 호되게 두들겨 맞는다면, 그러면 그 사내는 자연히 떨치고 일어나 스스로에게 말하지, '네가 행하는 선이 어디 있느냐?"], ["I grant you I see the ’arm,’ says the man, ‘but I don’t see the good.", "해악은 내 보고 있다만,' 그 사내가 말하길, '선은 안 보인단 말이다."], ["I call upon you, sir, therefore, to pint out the good.’”", "그러니 선생, 그 선이 어디 있는지 짚어 보시오.'\""]], [["“The man says?” I observed, as Joe waited for me to speak.", "\"그 사내가 그렇게 말한다고요?\" 조가 내 말을 기다리기에 내가 끼어들었다."]], [["“The man says,” Joe assented.", "\"그 사내가 그렇게 말하지.\" 조가 수긍했다."], ["“Is he right, that man?”", "\"그 사내 말이 옳으냐?\""]], [["“Dear Joe, he is always right.”", "\"정다운 조, 그 사람은 늘 옳지요.\""]], [["“Well, old chap,” said Joe, “then abide by your words.", "\"그럼 말이다, 친구야.\" 조가 말했다."], ["If he’s always right (which in general he’s more likely wrong), he’s right when he says this: Supposing ever you kep any little matter to yourself, when you was a little child, you kep it mostly because you know’d as J.", "\"네가 한 말을 지켜야지. 그가 늘 옳다면 (하기야 대체로는 틀릴 가능성이 더 많지만), 이 말을 할 때도 그가 옳은 거다. 네가 어린아이였을 때 혹 어떤 사소한 일을 혼자만 간직했다면, 너는 주로 J."], ["Gargery’s power to part you and Tickler in sunders were not fully equal to his inclinations.", "가저리가 너랑 티클러를 떼어 놓을 힘이 그 마음만큼 충분치 못하다는 걸 알았기 때문에 그랬던 거다."], ["Theerfore, think no more of it as betwixt two sech, and do not let us pass remarks upon onnecessary subjects.", "그러니 우리 같은 두 사람 사이의 일로 더는 그 일을 생각지 말고, 불필요한 주제에 대해선 우리 왈가왈부하지 말자꾸나."], ["Biddy giv’ herself a deal o’ trouble with me afore I left (for I am almost awful dull), as I should view it in this light, and, viewing it in this light, as I should so put it.", "내가 떠나기 전에 비디가 나 때문에 어지간히 애를 먹었지 (내가 워낙 끔찍하게 둔하니까), 내가 그걸 이런 식으로 봐야 한다고, 그리고 이런 식으로 보고서 이렇게 말해야 한다고 말이다."], ["Both of which,”", "그 둘 다.\""], ["said Joe, quite charmed with his logical arrangement, “being done, now this to you a true friend, say. Namely.", "조가 자기의 논리적 정리에 자못 흐뭇해하며 말했다. \"다 마쳤으니, 이제 진정한 친구로서 네게 이 말을 하마. 그러니까."], ["You mustn’t go a overdoing on it, but you must have your supper and your wine and water, and you must be put betwixt the sheets.”", "너는 무리해선 안 되고, 저녁을 먹고 포도주에 물 탄 걸 마시고, 이불 사이에 눕혀져야 한다.\""]], [["The delicacy with which Joe dismissed this theme, and the sweet tact and kindness with which Biddy—who with her woman’s wit had found me out so soon—had prepared him for it, made a deep impression on my mind.", "조가 이 주제를 거두어들이는 그 섬세함, 그리고 — 여자 특유의 기지로 나를 그토록 일찌감치 꿰뚫어 본 — 비디가 그를 그 일에 미리 대비시킨 그 다정한 재치와 친절함은, 내 마음에 깊은 인상을 남겼다."], ["But whether Joe knew how poor I was, and how my great expectations had all dissolved, like our own marsh mists before the sun, I could not understand.", "그러나 조가 내가 얼마나 가난해졌는지, 그리고 내 위대한 유산이 — 태양 앞의 우리 습지 안개처럼 — 어떻게 죄다 녹아 사라졌는지를 알고 있는지 어떤지는, 나로서는 헤아릴 수 없었다."]], [["Another thing in Joe that I could not understand when it first began to develop itself, but which I soon arrived at a sorrowful comprehension of, was this: As I became stronger and better, Joe became a little less easy with me.", "조에게서 처음 드러나기 시작했을 때는 이해할 수 없었으나 이내 서글픈 깨달음에 이르게 된 또 한 가지는 이것이었다. 내가 점점 튼튼해지고 나아지면서, 조가 나를 대하는 것이 조금씩 덜 편안해졌다는 것."], ["In my weakness and entire dependence on him, the dear fellow had fallen into the old tone, and called me by the old names, the dear “old Pip, old chap,” that now were music in my ears.", "내가 쇠약하여 그에게 전적으로 의지하던 동안, 그 정다운 사람은 옛 어조로 돌아가, 옛 이름으로, 그러니까 이제는 내 귀에 음악이던 그 정다운 \"정다운 핍, 친구야\"라고 나를 불렀다."], ["I too had fallen into the old ways, only happy and thankful that he let me.", "나 역시 옛 방식으로 돌아가, 그가 그렇게 하도록 내버려 두는 것만으로 그저 행복하고 감사했다."], ["But, imperceptibly, though I held by them fast, Joe’s hold upon them began to slacken; and whereas I wondered at this, at first, I soon began to understand that the cause of it was in me, and that the fault of it was all mine.", "그러나 내가 그것을 단단히 붙들고 있는데도, 알아차릴 수 없을 만큼 슬그머니 조가 그것을 붙드는 힘이 느슨해지기 시작했다. 그리고 처음엔 이를 의아해했으나, 나는 이내 그 원인이 내게 있다는 것을, 그리고 그 잘못이 온전히 내 것이라는 것을 깨닫기 시작했다."]], [["Ah!", "아!"], ["Had I given Joe no reason to doubt my constancy, and to think that in prosperity I should grow cold to him and cast him off?", "내가 조에게, 내 한결같음을 의심할 빌미를, 그리고 형편이 피면 내가 그에게 냉랭해져 그를 내쳐 버리리라 여길 빌미를 주었던 것은 아닐까?"], ["Had I given Joe’s innocent heart no cause to feel instinctively that as I got stronger, his hold upon me would be weaker, and that he had better loosen it in time and let me go, before I plucked myself away?", "내가 조의 순진한 마음에, 내가 튼튼해질수록 나에 대한 그의 손길이 약해지리라는 것을, 그래서 내가 스스로 몸을 빼내기 전에 제때에 그 손길을 늦추어 나를 놓아 보내는 편이 낫겠다는 것을, 본능적으로 느낄 까닭을 주었던 것은 아닐까?"]], [["It was on the third or fourth occasion of my going out walking in the Temple Gardens leaning on Joe’s arm, that I saw this change in him very plainly.", "조의 팔에 기대어 템플 정원으로 산책을 나간 세 번째인가 네 번째 날에, 나는 그의 이 변화를 아주 또렷이 보았다."], ["We had been sitting in the bright warm sunlight, looking at the river, and I chanced to say as we got up,—", "우리는 밝고 따뜻한 햇살 속에 앉아 강을 바라보고 있었는데, 일어서며 나는 마침 이렇게 말했다."]], [["“See, Joe!", "\"보세요, 조!"], ["I can walk quite strongly.", "나 아주 튼튼하게 걸을 수 있어요."], ["Now, you shall see me walk back by myself.”", "자, 내가 혼자서 걸어 돌아가는 걸 보여 드릴게요.\""]], [["“Which do not overdo it, Pip,”", "\"무리하진 말아라, 핍.\" 조가 말했다."], ["said Joe; “but I shall be happy fur to see you able, sir.”", "\"하지만 선생님이 그럴 수 있게 된 걸 보니 기쁩니다.\""]], [["The last word grated on me; but how could I remonstrate!", "마지막 그 호칭이 내 마음을 거슬렀다. 그러나 어찌 따지겠는가!"], ["I walked no further than the gate of the gardens, and then pretended to be weaker than I was, and asked Joe for his arm. Joe gave it me, but was thoughtful.", "나는 정원 문 앞까지밖에 걷지 못했고, 그러고는 실제보다 더 쇠약한 척하며 조에게 팔을 청했다. 조는 팔을 내주었으나, 생각에 잠겨 있었다."]], [["I, for my part, was thoughtful too; for, how best to check this growing change in Joe was a great perplexity to my remorseful thoughts.", "나 또한 나대로 생각에 잠겨 있었다. 조에게서 점점 자라나는 이 변화를 어떻게 하면 가장 잘 막을까 하는 것이, 회한에 찬 내 생각에 큰 골칫거리였기 때문이다."], ["That I was ashamed to tell him exactly how I was placed, and what I had come down to, I do not seek to conceal; but I hope my reluctance was not quite an unworthy one.", "내가 정확히 어떤 처지에 놓였는지, 그리고 내가 어디까지 영락했는지를 그에게 말하기가 부끄러웠다는 것을, 나는 감추려 하지 않는다. 그러나 내 망설임이 그리 비루하기만 한 것은 아니었기를 바란다."], ["He would want to help me out of his little savings, I knew, and I knew that he ought not to help me, and that I must not suffer him to do it.", "그가 자기의 변변찮은 저축에서 나를 도우려 들 것임을 나는 알았고, 또 그가 나를 도와서는 안 된다는 것을, 그리고 내가 그렇게 하도록 그를 내버려 두어서는 안 된다는 것을 알고 있었다."]], [["It was a thoughtful evening with both of us.", "우리 둘 다에게 생각이 많은 저녁이었다."], ["But, before we went to bed, I had resolved that I would wait over to-morrow,—to-morrow being Sunday,—and would begin my new course with the new week.", "그러나 잠자리에 들기 전, 나는 내일까지 기다리기로 — 내일이 일요일이었으므로 — 그리고 새로운 한 주와 더불어 나의 새로운 행로를 시작하기로 결심했다."], ["On Monday morning I would speak to Joe about this change, I would lay aside this last vestige of reserve, I would tell him what I had in my thoughts (that Secondly, not yet arrived at), and why I had not decided to go out to Herbert, and then the change would be conquered for ever.", "월요일 아침에 나는 조에게 이 변화에 대해 말하리라, 이 마지막 남은 거리낌의 자취를 떨쳐 버리리라, 내가 마음에 품은 것을 (아직 다다르지 못한 그 '둘째'를) 그에게 말하리라, 그리고 어째서 허버트에게로 가기로 결정하지 못했는지를 말하리라, 그러면 이 변화는 영영 정복되리라."], ["As I cleared, Joe cleared, and it seemed as though he had sympathetically arrived at a resolution too.", "내 마음이 개자, 조의 마음도 갰고, 마치 그도 공감하여 어떤 결심에 다다른 듯이 보였다."]], [["We had a quiet day on the Sunday, and we rode out into the country, and then walked in the fields.", "우리는 일요일을 조용히 보냈고, 마차를 타고 시골로 나가, 그러고는 들판을 거닐었다."]], [["“I feel thankful that I have been ill, Joe,” I said.", "\"내가 앓았던 게 감사하게 느껴져요, 조.\" 내가 말했다."]], [["“Dear old Pip, old chap, you’re a’most come round, sir.”", "\"정다운 핍, 친구야, 너 이제 거의 다 나았구나.\""]], [["“It has been a memorable time for me, Joe.”", "\"내겐 잊지 못할 시간이었어요, 조.\""]], [["“Likeways for myself, sir,”", "\"저도 마찬가지지요, 선생님.\""], ["Joe returned.", "조가 대답했다."]], [["“We have had a time together, Joe, that I can never forget.", "\"우린 함께 보낸 시간이 있었지요, 조, 내가 결코 잊을 수 없는 시간이요."], ["There were days once, I know, that I did for a while forget; but I never shall forget these.”", "한때, 한동안 잊어버렸던 날들이 있었다는 걸 나도 알아요. 하지만 이 시간만은 결코 잊지 않을 거예요.\""]], [["“Pip,”", "\"핍.\""], ["said Joe, appearing a little hurried and troubled, “there has been larks.", "조가 다소 황급하고 곤혹스러워하는 듯이 말했다. \"신나는 일들이 있었지."], ["And, dear sir, what have been betwixt us—have been.”", "그리고, 친애하는 선생님, 우리 사이에 있었던 일은 — 그저 있었던 일이고요.\""]], [["At night, when I had gone to bed, Joe came into my room, as he had done all through my recovery.", "밤이 되어 내가 잠자리에 들자, 조는 내 회복 기간 내내 그래 왔듯이 내 방으로 들어왔다."], ["He asked me if I felt sure that I was as well as in the morning?", "그는 내가 아침과 다름없이 잘 지내고 있다고 확신하느냐고 물었다."]], [["“Yes, dear Joe, quite.”", "\"네, 정다운 조, 아주요.\""]], [["“And are always a getting stronger, old chap?”", "\"그리고 늘 점점 튼튼해지고 있고, 친구야?\""]], [["“Yes, dear Joe, steadily.”", "\"네, 정다운 조, 꾸준히요.\""]], [["Joe patted the coverlet on my shoulder with his great good hand, and said, in what I thought a husky voice, “Good night!”", "조는 그 크고 선량한 손으로 내 어깨께 이불을 토닥이고는, 내가 듣기에 잠긴 듯한 목소리로 말했다. \"잘 자라!\""]], [["When I got up in the morning, refreshed and stronger yet, I was full of my resolution to tell Joe all, without delay.", "아침에 일어났을 때, 한결 상쾌하고 더욱 튼튼해진 나는, 지체 없이 조에게 모든 것을 말하리라는 결심으로 가득 차 있었다."], ["I would tell him before breakfast.", "아침 식사 전에 그에게 말하리라."], ["I would dress at once and go to his room and surprise him; for, it was the first day I had been up early.", "당장 옷을 입고 그의 방으로 가서 그를 놀래 주리라. 일찍 일어난 것은 그날이 처음이었으니까."], ["I went to his room, and he was not there.", "나는 그의 방으로 갔으나, 그는 거기 없었다."], ["Not only was he not there, but his box was gone.", "그가 없을 뿐만 아니라, 그의 짐 상자도 사라지고 없었다."]], [["I hurried then to the breakfast-table, and on it found a letter.", "나는 서둘러 아침 식탁으로 갔고, 그 위에서 편지 한 통을 발견했다."], ["These were its brief contents:—", "그 짤막한 내용은 이러했다."]], [["“Not wishful to intrude I have departured fur you are well again dear Pip and will do better without", "\"폐를 끼치고 싶지 않아 나는 떠난다 정다운 핍 네가 다시 건강해졌으니 내가 없는 편이 더 나을 게다\n조."]], [["“P.S.", "추신."], ["Ever the best of friends.”", "우린 늘 둘도 없는 친구다.\""]], [["Enclosed in the letter was a receipt for the debt and costs on which I had been arrested.", "편지 안에는 내가 체포되었던 그 빚과 비용에 대한 영수증이 동봉되어 있었다."], ["Down to that moment, I had vainly supposed that my creditor had withdrawn, or suspended proceedings until I should be quite recovered.", "그 순간까지도 나는, 내 채권자가 절차를 철회했거나, 내가 완전히 회복될 때까지 절차를 미뤄 둔 것이리라 부질없이 짐작하고 있었다."], ["I had never dreamed of Joe’s having paid the money; but Joe had paid it, and the receipt was in his name.", "조가 그 돈을 갚았으리라고는 꿈에도 생각지 못했다. 그러나 조가 그것을 갚았고, 영수증은 그의 이름으로 되어 있었다."]], [["What remained for me now, but to follow him to the dear old forge, and there to have out my disclosure to him, and my penitent remonstrance with him, and there to relieve my mind and heart of that reserved Secondly, which had begun as a vague something lingering in my thoughts, and had formed into a settled purpose?", "이제 내게 남은 일이라곤, 그를 따라 정다운 옛 대장간으로 내려가, 거기서 그에게 내 고백을 털어놓고, 참회에 찬 항변을 그에게 하고, 그리고 처음엔 내 생각 속에 어렴풋이 머물던 무언가로 시작했다가 굳은 결심으로 자리 잡은 그 미뤄 둔 '둘째'로부터 내 마음과 가슴을 풀어 주는 것 말고 무엇이 있겠는가?"]], [["The purpose was, that I would go to Biddy, that I would show her how humbled and repentant I came back, that I would tell her how I had lost all I once hoped for, that I would remind her of our old confidences in my first unhappy time.", "그 결심이란, 내가 비디에게로 가서, 내가 얼마나 겸허해지고 뉘우치며 돌아왔는지를 그녀에게 보여 주고, 내가 한때 바라던 모든 것을 어떻게 잃었는지를 그녀에게 말하고, 내가 처음 불행했던 시절 우리가 나누었던 옛 흉금을 그녀에게 일깨우겠다는 것이었다."], ["Then I would say to her, “Biddy, I think you once liked me very well, when my errant heart, even while it strayed away from you, was quieter and better with you than it ever has been since.", "그러고 나서 나는 그녀에게 말하리라. \"비디, 내 마음이 한때 당신을 무척 좋아했던 것 같아요. 내 방황하는 마음이 당신에게서 멀리 헤매던 그때조차도, 그 후 어느 때보다 당신 곁에서 더 잔잔하고 더 선했으니까요."], ["If you can like me only half as well once more, if you can take me with all my faults and disappointments on my head, if you can receive me like a forgiven child (and indeed I am as sorry, Biddy, and have as much need of a hushing voice and a soothing hand), I hope I am a little worthier of you that I was,—not much, but a little.", "만약 당신이 단 절반만큼이라도 다시 한번 나를 좋아해 줄 수 있다면, 만약 내 머리 위에 짊어진 온갖 결함과 좌절과 더불어 나를 받아들여 줄 수 있다면, 만약 용서받은 아이처럼 나를 맞아 줄 수 있다면 (정말이지 나는 그만큼 미안하고, 비디, 달래 주는 목소리와 어루만지는 손길이 그만큼 절실해요), 나는 내가 예전의 나보다 당신에게 조금은 더 어울리는 사람이기를 바라요."], ["And, Biddy, it shall rest with you to say whether I shall work at the forge with Joe, or whether I shall try for any different occupation down in this country, or whether we shall go away to a distant place where an opportunity awaits me which I set aside, when it was offered, until I knew your answer.", "많이는 아니어도, 조금은요. 그리고, 비디, 내가 조와 함께 대장간에서 일할지, 아니면 이 시골에서 다른 일자리를 찾아볼지, 아니면 우리가 멀리 어딘가로 떠날지는 당신이 정하기에 달려 있어요. 그 먼 곳에는, 제안받았을 때 내가 당신의 대답을 알게 될 때까지 미뤄 둔 어떤 기회가 나를 기다리고 있거든요."], ["And now, dear Biddy, if you can tell me that you will go through the world with me, you will surely make it a better world for me, and me a better man for it, and I will try hard to make it a better world for you.”", "그러니 이제, 정다운 비디, 만약 당신이 나와 함께 이 세상을 헤쳐 가겠다고 말해 준다면, 당신은 분명 내게 이 세상을 더 나은 곳으로 만들어 줄 것이고, 나를 그로 인해 더 나은 사람으로 만들어 줄 것이며, 나 또한 당신을 위해 이 세상을 더 나은 곳으로 만들고자 힘껏 애쓰겠어요.\""]], [["Such was my purpose.", "이것이 나의 결심이었다."], ["After three days more of recovery, I went down to the old place to put it in execution. And how I sped in it is all I have left to tell.", "사흘을 더 회복한 뒤, 나는 그것을 실행에 옮기러 옛 고장으로 내려갔다. 그리고 내가 그 일에서 어떻게 되었는지가, 내게 남은 이야기의 전부다."]], [["# Chapter LVIII.", "# Chapter LVIII."]], [["The tidings of my high fortunes having had a heavy fall had got down to my native place and its neighbourhood before I got there.", "내 드높은 재산이 무겁게 추락했다는 소식은 내가 도착하기도 전에 이미 고향과 그 인근까지 전해져 있었다."], ["I found the Blue Boar in possession of the intelligence, and I found that it made a great change in the Boar’s demeanour.", "블루 보어 여관도 그 정보를 알고 있었고, 그것이 보어의 태도에 큰 변화를 가져왔음을 나는 알아차렸다."], ["Whereas the Boar had cultivated my good opinion with warm assiduity when I was coming into property, the Boar was exceedingly cool on the subject now that I was going out of property.", "내가 재산을 손에 넣어 가던 무렵 보어는 따뜻한 열성으로 내 호감을 사려 애썼으나, 이제 내가 재산을 잃어 가는 처지가 되자 보어는 그 화제에 대해 몹시 냉랭했다."]], [["It was evening when I arrived, much fatigued by the journey I had so often made so easily.", "내가 도착한 것은 저녁이었고, 그토록 자주 그토록 수월하게 다니던 그 여정에 나는 몹시 지쳐 있었다."], ["The Boar could not put me into my usual bedroom, which was engaged (probably by some one who had expectations), and could only assign me a very indifferent chamber among the pigeons and post-chaises up the yard.", "보어는 내가 늘 쓰던 침실을 내주지 못했는데, 그 방은 (아마도 무슨 유산을 기대하는 누군가에게) 이미 예약되어 있었기에, 안뜰 위쪽 비둘기들과 역마차들 사이의 지극히 변변찮은 방 하나밖에 내게 배정해 주지 못했다."], ["But I had as sound a sleep in that lodging as in the most superior accommodation the Boar could have given me, and the quality of my dreams was about the same as in the best bedroom.", "그러나 나는 그 숙소에서, 보어가 내줄 수 있었을 가장 고급스러운 방에서만큼이나 깊은 잠을 잤고, 내 꿈의 질도 가장 좋은 침실에서와 별반 다르지 않았다."]], [["Early in the morning, while my breakfast was getting ready, I strolled round by Satis House.", "이른 아침, 아침 식사가 준비되는 동안 나는 새티스 하우스 쪽으로 어슬렁거리며 돌아보았다."], ["There were printed bills on the gate and on bits of carpet hanging out of the windows, announcing a sale by auction of the Household Furniture and Effects, next week.", "대문에도, 창밖으로 늘어뜨린 카펫 조각에도 인쇄된 벽보가 붙어 있었는데, 다음 주에 가재도구와 동산을 경매로 매각한다는 공고였다."], ["The House itself was to be sold as old building materials, and pulled down. LOT 1 was marked in whitewashed knock-knee letters on the brew house; LOT 2 on that part of the main building which had been so long shut up.", "저택 자체도 낡은 건축 자재로 팔려 헐릴 예정이었다. 양조장 건물에는 \"제1호\"라는 글씨가 휘청거리는 안짱다리 같은 흰 칠로 새겨져 있었고, 그토록 오래 닫혀 있던 본관의 한 부분에는 \"제2호\"가 적혀 있었다."], ["Other lots were marked off on other parts of the structure, and the ivy had been torn down to make room for the inscriptions, and much of it trailed low in the dust and was withered already.", "구조물의 다른 부분들에도 다른 번호가 매겨져 있었고, 그 글씨를 적을 자리를 마련하느라 담쟁이덩굴은 뜯겨 나가, 그 상당 부분이 흙먼지 속에 낮게 늘어진 채 벌써 시들어 있었다."], ["Stepping in for a moment at the open gate, and looking around me with the uncomfortable air of a stranger who had no business there, I saw the auctioneer’s clerk walking on the casks and telling them off for the information of a catalogue-compiler, pen in hand, who made a temporary desk of the wheeled chair I had so often pushed along to the tune of Old Clem.", "열린 대문 안으로 잠시 발을 들여, 거기 볼일이 없는 낯선 자의 거북한 태도로 사방을 둘러보다가, 나는 경매인의 서기가 술통 위를 걸어 다니며 목록을 작성하는 자에게 그것들을 일러 주는 모습을 보았는데, 그 목록 작성자는 펜을 손에 쥔 채, 내가 그토록 자주 \"올드 클렘\" 가락에 맞추어 밀고 다니던 그 바퀴 달린 의자를 임시 책상 삼아 쓰고 있었다."]], [["When I got back to my breakfast in the Boar’s coffee-room, I found Mr. Pumblechook conversing with the landlord. Mr.", "보어의 커피실로 돌아와 아침 식사 앞에 앉으니, 펌블추크 씨가 주인과 이야기를 나누고 있는 것이 보였다."], ["Pumblechook (not improved in appearance by his late nocturnal adventure) was waiting for me, and addressed me in the following terms:—", "펌블추크 씨는 (지난번 야밤의 모험으로 외모가 나아진 바는 없었다) 나를 기다리고 있었고, 다음과 같은 말로 내게 말을 걸었다."]], [["“Young man, I am sorry to see you brought low.", "\"젊은이, 자네가 영락한 꼴을 보게 되어 유감일세."], ["But what else could be expected!", "하지만 달리 무엇을 기대할 수 있었겠나!"], ["what else could be expected!”", "달리 무엇을 기대할 수 있었겠어!\""]], [["As he extended his hand with a magnificently forgiving air, and as I was broken by illness and unfit to quarrel, I took it.", "그가 더없이 관대하게 용서하는 태도로 손을 내미는데, 나는 병으로 쇠약해져 다툴 형편이 못 되었으므로, 그 손을 잡았다."]], [["“William,”", "\"윌리엄,\""], ["said Mr. Pumblechook to the waiter, “put a muffin on table.", "펌블추크 씨가 웨이터에게 말했다. \"탁자에 머핀을 올리게나."], ["And has it come to this!", "일이 이 지경에 이르다니!"], ["Has it come to this!”", "일이 이 지경에 이르다니!\""]], [["I frowningly sat down to my breakfast. Mr.", "나는 얼굴을 찌푸린 채 아침 식사 앞에 앉았다."], ["Pumblechook stood over me and poured out my tea—before I could touch the teapot—with the air of a benefactor who was resolved to be true to the last.", "펌블추크 씨는 내 위로 우뚝 서서, 내가 찻주전자에 손을 대기도 전에, 끝까지 진실하기로 작정한 은인의 태도로 내 차를 따라 주었다."]], [["“William,”", "\"윌리엄,\""], ["said Mr. Pumblechook, mournfully, “put the salt on.", "펌블추크 씨가 구슬프게 말했다. \"소금을 놓아 주게."], ["In happier times,”", "더 행복하던 시절에는,\""], ["addressing me, “I think you took sugar?", "그가 내게 말을 돌렸다. \"설탕을 넣으셨던 것 같은데?"], ["And did you take milk?", "그리고 우유도 넣으셨지요?"], ["You did. Sugar and milk.", "그러셨고말고. 설탕과 우유라."], ["William, bring a watercress.”", "윌리엄, 물냉이를 좀 가져오게.\""]], [["“Thank you,”", "\"고맙습니다만,\""], ["said I, shortly, “but I don’t eat watercresses.”", "나는 짧게 말했다. \"저는 물냉이는 먹지 않습니다.\""]], [["“You don’t eat ’em,”", "\"안 드신다고요,\""], ["returned Mr. Pumblechook, sighing and nodding his head several times, as if he might have expected that, and as if abstinence from watercresses were consistent with my downfall.", "펌블추크 씨가 마치 그럴 줄 알았다는 듯, 그리고 물냉이를 멀리하는 것이 내 몰락과 부합한다는 듯, 한숨을 내쉬며 여러 번 고개를 끄덕이고는 대꾸했다. \"옳거니."], ["“True. The simple fruits of the earth. No.", "땅이 내어 주는 소박한 결실인데. 그렇지요."], ["You needn’t bring any, William.”", "가져올 것 없네, 윌리엄.\""]], [["I went on with my breakfast, and Mr. Pumblechook continued to stand over me, staring fishily and breathing noisily, as he always did.", "나는 아침 식사를 계속했고, 펌블추크 씨는 늘 그러하듯 물고기 같은 눈으로 빤히 쳐다보고 시끄럽게 숨을 몰아쉬며 내 위에 우뚝 서 있었다."]], [["“Little more than skin and bone!”", "\"가죽과 뼈밖에 안 남았구먼!\""], ["mused Mr. Pumblechook, aloud.", "펌블추크 씨가 소리 내어 곰곰이 읊조렸다."], ["“And yet when he went from here (I may say with my blessing), and I spread afore him my humble store, like the Bee, he was as plump as a Peach!”", "\"그런데도 이 사람이 여기를 떠날 적에는 (감히 내 축복을 받으며 떠났다고 말할 수 있겠지), 그리고 내가 꿀벌처럼 변변찮은 내 곳간을 그 앞에 펼쳐 놓았을 적에는, 복숭아처럼 통통했단 말이야!\""]], [["This reminded me of the wonderful difference between the servile manner in which he had offered his hand in my new prosperity, saying, “May I?” and the ostentatious clemency with which he had just now exhibited the same fat five fingers.", "이 말은, 내가 새로이 번창하던 무렵에 그가 \"그래도 되겠습니까?\" 하며 비굴하게 손을 내밀던 태도와, 방금 그 똑같은 살진 다섯 손가락을 과시하듯 관대하게 내밀던 태도 사이의 놀라운 차이를 내게 일깨워 주었다."]], [["“Hah!”", "\"하!\""], ["he went on, handing me the bread and butter.", "그가 내게 버터 바른 빵을 건네며 말을 이었다."], ["“And air you a-going to Joseph?”", "\"그래, 조지프한테 가는 길인가?\""]], [["“In heaven’s name,”", "\"맙소사,\""], ["said I, firing in spite of myself, “what does it matter to you where I am going?", "나는 나도 모르게 발끈하여 말했다. \"제가 어디를 가든 그게 당신과 무슨 상관입니까?"], ["Leave that teapot alone.”", "그 찻주전자 좀 가만 놔두세요.\""]], [["It was the worst course I could have taken, because it gave Pumblechook the opportunity he wanted.", "그것은 내가 취할 수 있는 최악의 처사였으니, 펌블추크에게 그가 바라던 기회를 주고 말았기 때문이다."]], [["“Yes, young man,”", "\"그렇지, 젊은이,\""], ["said he, releasing the handle of the article in question, retiring a step or two from my table, and speaking for the behoof of the landlord and waiter at the door, “I will leave that teapot alone.", "그가 문제의 그 물건 손잡이를 놓고, 내 탁자에서 한두 걸음 물러서며, 문간의 주인과 웨이터가 들으라는 듯 말했다. \"그 찻주전자, 가만 놔두지."], ["You are right, young man.", "자네 말이 옳네, 젊은이."], ["For once you are right.", "모처럼 자네 말이 옳아."], ["I forgit myself when I take such an interest in your breakfast, as to wish your frame, exhausted by the debilitating effects of prodigygality, to be stimilated by the ’olesome nourishment of your forefathers. And yet,”", "방종이 가져온 쇠약의 폐해로 기진맥진한 자네 몸뚱이가 조상 대대로 내려온 건강한 자양분으로 기운을 차리기를 바라는 마음에서, 자네 아침 식사에 이토록 관심을 쏟다가 그만 내가 분수를 잊었네그려. 하지만 말이야,\""], ["said Pumblechook, turning to the landlord and waiter, and pointing me out at arm’s length, “this is him as I ever sported with in his days of happy infancy!", "펌블추크가 주인과 웨이터 쪽으로 돌아서며, 팔을 쭉 뻗어 나를 가리켜 보였다. \"이 사람이 바로, 행복한 어린 시절에 내가 늘 데리고 놀아 주던 그 아이라네! 그럴 리 없다고는 말하지 말게."], ["Tell me not it cannot be; I tell you this is him!”", "내가 말하지 않나, 이 사람이 바로 그 아이라고!\""]], [["A low murmur from the two replied. The waiter appeared to be particularly affected.", "두 사람에게서 나직한 웅성거림이 답으로 돌아왔다. 웨이터는 특히 감동한 듯이 보였다."]], [["“This is him,”", "\"이 사람이 바로,\""], ["said Pumblechook, “as I have rode in my shay-cart.", "펌블추크가 말했다. \"내가 내 이륜마차에 태우고 다니던 그 아이라네."], ["This is him as I have seen brought up by hand.", "이 사람이 바로 손수 길러지는 것을 내가 지켜본 그 아이야."], ["This is him untoe the sister of which I was uncle by marriage, as her name was Georgiana M’ria from her own mother, let him deny it if he can!”", "이 사람이 바로, 친정어머니에게서 물려받은 이름이 조지애나 마리아였던, 내가 혼인으로 외삼촌이 된 그 누나의 동생이라네. 아니라고 부인할 수 있거든 어디 해 보라지!\""]], [["The waiter seemed convinced that I could not deny it, and that it gave the case a black look.", "웨이터는 내가 그것을 부인할 수 없으리라는 것을, 그리고 그것이 이 사건을 음험하게 보이게 만든다는 것을 확신하는 듯했다."]], [["“Young man,”", "\"젊은이,\""], ["said Pumblechook, screwing his head at me in the old fashion, “you air a-going to Joseph.", "펌블추크가 예전 그 버릇대로 내 쪽으로 고개를 비틀며 말했다. \"자네는 조지프한테 가는 길이지."], ["What does it matter to me, you ask me, where you air a-going?", "자네가 어디를 가든 그게 나와 무슨 상관이냐고, 자네는 내게 묻지."], ["I say to you, Sir, you air a-going to Joseph.”", "내 자네에게 말하지, 이보게, 자네는 조지프한테 가는 길이야.\""]], [["The waiter coughed, as if he modestly invited me to get over that.", "웨이터는 마치 그쯤은 넘어가 달라고 점잖게 청하기라도 하듯 기침을 했다."]], [["“Now,”", "\"자,\""], ["said Pumblechook, and all this with a most exasperating air of saying in the cause of virtue what was perfectly convincing and conclusive, “I will tell you what to say to Joseph.", "펌블추크가 말했는데, 이 모든 것을 더없이 약을 올리는 태도로, 더할 나위 없이 설득력 있고 결정적인 말을 미덕을 위하여 베푸는 듯이 늘어놓았다. \"조지프한테 무어라 말해야 할지 일러 주지."], ["Here is Squires of the Boar present, known and respected in this town, and here is William, which his father’s name was Potkins if I do not deceive myself.”", "여기 이 고장에서 알려지고 존경받는 보어의 주인장 스콰이어스 씨가 계시고, 또 여기 윌리엄이 있는데, 내가 잘못 안 게 아니라면 그 아버지 이름이 포트킨스였지.\""]], [["“You do not, sir,”", "\"맞습니다, 어르신,\""], ["said William.", "윌리엄이 말했다."]], [["“In their presence,”", "\"이분들 앞에서,\""], ["pursued Pumblechook, “I will tell you, young man, what to say to Joseph.", "펌블추크가 말을 이었다. \"내 자네에게, 젊은이, 조지프한테 무어라 말해야 할지 일러 주겠네."], ["Says you, “Joseph, I have this day seen my earliest benefactor and the founder of my fortun’s.", "자네는 이렇게 말하는 거야. '조지프, 나는 오늘 내 가장 이른 은인이자 내 재산의 창시자를 만났소."], ["I will name no names, Joseph, but so they are pleased to call him up town, and I have seen that man.”", "이름은 대지 않겠소, 조지프, 하지만 시내에서들 그렇게 기꺼이 부르고 있고, 나는 그분을 만났단 말이오.'\""]], [["“I swear I don’t see him here,”", "\"맹세컨대 저는 여기서 그런 분을 못 보겠는데요,\""], ["said I.", "나는 말했다."]], [["“Say that likewise,”", "\"그것도 같이 말하게,\""], ["retorted Pumblechook.", "펌블추크가 받아쳤다."], ["“Say you said that, and even Joseph will probably betray surprise.”", "\"자네가 그렇게 말했다고 전하게. 그러면 조지프조차도 아마 놀라움을 드러낼 걸세.\""]], [["“There you quite mistake him,”", "\"그 점에서는 당신이 그 사람을 완전히 잘못 봤습니다,\""], ["said I.", "나는 말했다."], ["“I know better.”", "\"제가 더 잘 압니다.\""]], [["“Says you,”", "\"자네는 이렇게 말하는 거야,\""], ["Pumblechook went on, “‘Joseph, I have seen that man, and that man bears you no malice and bears me no malice.", "펌블추크가 말을 이었다. \"'조지프, 나는 그분을 만났는데, 그분은 당신에게도 악의가 없고 내게도 악의가 없소."], ["He knows your character, Joseph, and is well acquainted with your pig-headedness and ignorance; and he knows my character, Joseph, and he knows my want of gratitoode.", "그분은 당신의 성품을 아오, 조지프, 그리고 당신의 옹고집과 무지를 잘 알고 있소. 그리고 그분은 내 성품도 아오, 조지프, 또 내가 감사할 줄 모른다는 것도 아오. 그렇소, 조지프,'"], ["Yes, Joseph,’ says you,”", "자네는 이렇게 말하는 거야,\""], ["here Pumblechook shook his head and hand at me, “‘he knows my total deficiency of common human gratitoode.", "여기서 펌블추크는 내게 고개와 손을 흔들어 보였다. \"'그분은 내게 사람으로서 마땅히 갖춰야 할 보통의 감사하는 마음이 완전히 결여되어 있다는 것을 아오."], ["He knows it, Joseph, as none can. You do not know it, Joseph, having no call to know it, but that man do.’”", "그분은 그것을 아오, 조지프, 누구도 알 수 없을 만큼 아오. 당신은 그것을 모르오, 조지프, 알 까닭이 없으니까, 하지만 그분은 안단 말이오.'\""]], [["Windy donkey as he was, it really amazed me that he could have the face to talk thus to mine.", "허풍선이 멍청이 주제에, 감히 그런 말을 내 면전에 늘어놓을 낯짝이 있다는 것이 나는 정말이지 어처구니없었다."]], [["“Says you, ‘Joseph, he gave me a little message, which I will now repeat.", "\"자네는 이렇게 말하는 거야. '조지프, 그분이 내게 짤막한 전갈을 주었는데, 그것을 지금 그대로 옮기겠소."], ["It was that, in my being brought low, he saw the finger of Providence.", "그것은, 내가 영락한 데서 그분은 섭리의 손가락을 보았다는 것이오."], ["He knowed that finger when he saw Joseph, and he saw it plain.", "그분은 조지프를 보았을 때 그 손가락을 알아보았고, 그것을 똑똑히 보았소."], ["It pinted out this writing, Joseph.", "그것은 이런 글귀를 가리키고 있었소, 조지프."], ["Reward of ingratitoode to his earliest benefactor, and founder of fortun’s.", "자신의 가장 이른 은인이자 재산의 창시자에 대한 배은망덕의 대가."], ["But that man said he did not repent of what he had done, Joseph. Not at all.", "하지만 그분은 자신이 한 일을 후회하지 않는다고 말했소, 조지프. 조금도."], ["It was right to do it, it was kind to do it, it was benevolent to do it, and he would do it again.’”", "그렇게 한 것은 옳은 일이었고, 친절한 일이었고, 자비로운 일이었으며, 다시라도 그렇게 하겠노라고 했소.'\""]], [["“It’s pity,”", "\"딱하기도 하지,\""], ["said I, scornfully, as I finished my interrupted breakfast, “that the man did not say what he had done and would do again.”", "나는 중단되었던 아침 식사를 마치며 비웃듯이 말했다. \"그분이 대체 무슨 일을 했고 다시라도 무슨 일을 하겠다는 건지는 말하지 않았다니 말입니다.\""]], [["“Squires of the Boar!”", "\"보어의 스콰이어스 씨!\""], ["Pumblechook was now addressing the landlord, “and William!", "펌블추크는 이제 주인에게 말을 걸고 있었다. \"그리고 윌리엄!"], ["I have no objections to your mentioning, either up town or down town, if such should be your wishes, that it was right to do it, kind to do it, benevolent to do it, and that I would do it again.”", "자네들이 원한다면, 시내에서든 변두리에서든, 그렇게 한 것이 옳은 일이었고, 친절한 일이었고, 자비로운 일이었으며, 내가 다시라도 그렇게 하겠노라고 했다는 걸 언급해도 나는 이의가 없네.\""]], [["With those words the Impostor shook them both by the hand, with an air, and left the house; leaving me much more astonished than delighted by the virtues of that same indefinite “it.”", "그 말과 함께 그 사기꾼은 거드름을 피우며 두 사람과 악수를 나눈 뒤 여관을 떠났는데, 나는 그 막연한 \"그 일\"이라는 것의 미덕에 기뻐했다기보다는 훨씬 더 어리둥절해질 뿐이었다."], ["I was not long after him in leaving the house too, and when I went down the High Street I saw him holding forth (no doubt to the same effect) at his shop door to a select group, who honoured me with very unfavourable glances as I passed on the opposite side of the way.", "나도 그의 뒤를 이어 곧 여관을 나섰고, 큰길을 따라 내려가다 보니 그가 자기 가게 문 앞에서 한 무리의 선택된 사람들을 향해 (분명 같은 취지로) 일장 연설을 늘어놓고 있는 것이 보였는데, 내가 길 반대편으로 지나가자 그들은 내게 자못 곱지 않은 눈길을 보내는 영광을 베풀어 주었다."]], [["But, it was only the pleasanter to turn to Biddy and to Joe, whose great forbearance shone more brightly than before, if that could be, contrasted with this brazen pretender.", "그러나 이 뻔뻔한 사기꾼과 대비될 때, 그 큰 아량이 (그럴 수 있다면) 전보다 한층 더 밝게 빛나는 비디와 조에게로 돌아서니 그저 더없이 기쁠 뿐이었다."], ["I went towards them slowly, for my limbs were weak, but with a sense of increasing relief as I drew nearer to them, and a sense of leaving arrogance and untruthfulness further and further behind.", "다리가 약했던 까닭에 나는 천천히 그들에게로 다가갔으나, 가까이 갈수록 점점 커지는 안도감과, 오만과 거짓을 점점 더 멀리 뒤로하고 떠나오는 듯한 느낌을 안고 걸었다."]], [["The June weather was delicious.", "6월의 날씨는 더없이 상쾌했다."], ["The sky was blue, the larks were soaring high over the green corn, I thought all that countryside more beautiful and peaceful by far than I had ever known it to be yet.", "하늘은 푸르렀고, 종달새들은 푸른 보리밭 위 높이 날아오르고 있었으니, 나는 그 온 시골 풍경이 여태껏 알아 온 그 어느 때보다도 한결 더 아름답고 평화롭다고 생각했다."], ["Many pleasant pictures of the life that I would lead there, and of the change for the better that would come over my character when I had a guiding spirit at my side whose simple faith and clear home wisdom I had proved, beguiled my way.", "내가 그곳에서 누리게 될 삶의 즐거운 그림들이, 그리고 그 소박한 믿음과 맑은 가정적 지혜를 내가 몸소 겪어 알게 된 길잡이가 곁에 있을 때 내 성품에 찾아올 더 나은 방향으로의 변화가, 가는 길을 즐겁게 해 주었다."], ["They awakened a tender emotion in me; for my heart was softened by my return, and such a change had come to pass, that I felt like one who was toiling home barefoot from distant travel, and whose wanderings had lasted many years.", "그것들은 내 안에 다정한 감정을 불러일으켰으니, 돌아왔다는 것만으로도 내 마음은 누그러졌고, 또한 이런 변화가 이미 일어나 있었던지라, 나는 마치 먼 여행에서 맨발로 고생스레 집으로 돌아오는, 그 방랑이 여러 해나 이어진 사람처럼 느껴졌다."]], [["The schoolhouse where Biddy was mistress I had never seen; but, the little roundabout lane by which I entered the village, for quietness’ sake, took me past it.", "비디가 선생으로 있는 학교는 한 번도 본 적이 없었다. 그러나 조용히 가려고 마을로 들어선 그 작고 구불구불한 샛길이 나를 그 앞으로 지나가게 했다."], ["I was disappointed to find that the day was a holiday; no children were there, and Biddy’s house was closed.", "그날이 휴일이라는 것을 알고 나는 실망했다. 아이들도 없었고, 비디의 집도 닫혀 있었다."], ["Some hopeful notion of seeing her, busily engaged in her daily duties, before she saw me, had been in my mind and was defeated.", "비디가 나를 보기 전에 그녀가 날마다의 일에 바쁘게 매달려 있는 모습을 보았으면 하는 어떤 기대 어린 마음이 내 안에 있었으나, 그것은 무산되고 말았다."]], [["But the forge was a very short distance off, and I went towards it under the sweet green limes, listening for the clink of Joe’s hammer.", "그러나 대장간은 아주 가까운 거리에 있었으므로, 나는 향긋한 푸른 라임나무 아래로 조의 망치 소리가 들리려나 귀를 기울이며 그쪽으로 걸어갔다."], ["Long after I ought to have heard it, and long after I had fancied I heard it and found it but a fancy, all was still. The limes were there, and the white thorns were there, and the chestnut-trees were there, and their leaves rustled harmoniously when I stopped to listen; but, the clink of Joe’s hammer was not in the midsummer wind.", "진작 그 소리를 들었어야 할 때가 한참 지나도록, 그리고 그 소리를 들은 듯싶었으나 그저 착각이었음을 알게 된 때가 한참 지나도록, 사방은 고요하기만 했다. 라임나무도 거기 있었고, 흰 가시나무도 거기 있었고, 밤나무도 거기 있어, 내가 걸음을 멈추고 귀 기울일 때 그 잎들이 어우러져 사각거렸으나, 조의 망치 소리는 한여름의 바람 속에 없었다."]], [["Almost fearing, without knowing why, to come in view of the forge, I saw it at last, and saw that it was closed.", "까닭도 모른 채 대장간이 시야에 들어오는 것이 거의 두려워질 무렵, 나는 마침내 그것을 보았고, 그것이 닫혀 있음을 보았다."], ["No gleam of fire, no glittering shower of sparks, no roar of bellows; all shut up, and still.", "불빛 한 점, 반짝이며 쏟아지는 불똥 한 줄기, 풀무의 울부짖음 하나 없이, 죄다 닫힌 채 고요했다."]], [["But the house was not deserted, and the best parlour seemed to be in use, for there were white curtains fluttering in its window, and the window was open and gay with flowers.", "그러나 집은 버려진 것이 아니었고, 제일 좋은 응접실은 쓰이고 있는 듯했으니, 그 창문에는 흰 커튼이 나부끼고 있었고, 창문은 열려 꽃으로 화사했기 때문이다."], ["I went softly towards it, meaning to peep over the flowers, when Joe and Biddy stood before me, arm in arm.", "나는 꽃 너머로 안을 들여다볼 작정으로 살그머니 그쪽으로 다가갔는데, 그때 조와 비디가 팔짱을 낀 채 내 앞에 나타났다."]], [["At first Biddy gave a cry, as if she thought it was my apparition, but in another moment she was in my embrace.", "처음에 비디는 내 유령이라도 본 듯 비명을 질렀으나, 다음 순간 그녀는 내 품에 안겨 있었다."], ["I wept to see her, and she wept to see me; I, because she looked so fresh and pleasant; she, because I looked so worn and white.", "나는 그녀를 보고 울었고, 그녀도 나를 보고 울었다. 나는 그녀가 그토록 싱그럽고 정겨워 보였기 때문이었고, 그녀는 내가 그토록 수척하고 핼쑥해 보였기 때문이었다."]], [["“But dear Biddy, how smart you are!”", "\"하지만 비디, 이렇게 말끔하게 차려입었구나!\""]], [["“Yes, dear Pip.”", "\"응, 핍.\""]], [["“And Joe, how smart you are!”", "\"그리고 조, 형도 이렇게 말끔하게 차려입었네요!\""]], [["“Yes, dear old Pip, old chap.”", "\"그래, 그래 우리 핍, 친구.\""]], [["I looked at both of them, from one to the other, and then—", "나는 두 사람을 번갈아 바라보다가, 그러고 나서—"]], [["“It’s my wedding-day!”", "\"오늘이 내 결혼식 날이야!\""], ["cried Biddy, in a burst of happiness, “and I am married to Joe!”", "비디가 행복에 겨워 터뜨리듯 외쳤다. \"나, 조랑 결혼했어!\""]], [["They had taken me into the kitchen, and I had laid my head down on the old deal table.", "그들은 나를 부엌으로 데려갔고, 나는 낡은 전나무 탁자에 머리를 묻었다."], ["Biddy held one of my hands to her lips, and Joe’s restoring touch was on my shoulder.", "비디는 내 한 손을 잡아 제 입술에 갖다 댔고, 조의 위로해 주는 손길이 내 어깨에 놓여 있었다."], ["“Which he warn’t strong enough, my dear, fur to be surprised,”", "\"이 애가 놀라기에는 충분히 튼튼하지가 못했지 뭐야, 여보,\""], ["said Joe.", "조가 말했다."], ["And Biddy said, “I ought to have thought of it, dear Joe, but I was too happy.”", "그러자 비디가 말했다. \"내가 미처 그 생각을 했어야 했는데, 여보, 너무 행복해서 그만.\""], ["They were both so overjoyed to see me, so proud to see me, so touched by my coming to them, so delighted that I should have come by accident to make their day complete!", "두 사람은 나를 보고 너무도 기뻐했고, 나를 보고 너무도 자랑스러워했고, 내가 그들을 찾아온 것에 너무도 감동했고, 내가 우연히 찾아와 그들의 날을 온전하게 채워 준 것을 너무도 흐뭇해했다!"]], [["My first thought was one of great thankfulness that I had never breathed this last baffled hope to Joe.", "내 첫 생각은, 마지막으로 좌절된 이 희망을 조에게 결코 입 밖에 내지 않았다는 데 대한 크나큰 감사였다."], ["How often, while he was with me in my illness, had it risen to my lips!", "그가 내 병상을 지키는 동안 그 말이 얼마나 자주 내 입술까지 치밀어 올랐던가!"], ["How irrevocable would have been his knowledge of it, if he had remained with me but another hour!", "만일 그가 단 한 시간만 더 내 곁에 머물렀더라면, 그가 그것을 알게 되는 일이 얼마나 돌이킬 수 없는 것이 되었겠는가!"]], [["“Dear Biddy,”", "\"비디,\""], ["said I, “you have the best husband in the whole world, and if you could have seen him by my bed you would have—But no, you couldn’t love him better than you do.”", "나는 말했다. \"너는 온 세상에서 가장 좋은 남편을 얻은 거야. 그가 내 침상 곁에서 어떻게 했는지 네가 보았더라면 너는—아니, 그래도 지금보다 더 그를 사랑할 수는 없겠지.\""]], [["“No, I couldn’t indeed,”", "\"응, 정말 더는 못 할 거야,\""], ["said Biddy.", "비디가 말했다."]], [["“And, dear Joe, you have the best wife in the whole world, and she will make you as happy as even you deserve to be, you dear, good, noble Joe!”", "\"그리고 조, 형은 온 세상에서 가장 좋은 아내를 얻은 거예요. 비디는 형이 누릴 자격이 있는 만큼 형을 행복하게 해 줄 거예요. 친절하고, 선량하고, 고귀한 우리 조!\""]], [["Joe looked at me with a quivering lip, and fairly put his sleeve before his eyes.", "조는 입술을 떨며 나를 바라보다가, 급기야 소매로 눈을 가렸다."]], [["“And Joe and Biddy both, as you have been to church to-day, and are in charity and love with all mankind, receive my humble thanks for all you have done for me, and all I have so ill repaid!", "\"그리고 조도 비디도, 두 사람 오늘 교회에 다녀와 온 인류를 향한 자비와 사랑에 차 있으니, 두 사람이 내게 베풀어 준 모든 것에 대해, 그리고 내가 그토록 형편없이 갚은 그 모든 것에 대해, 내 변변찮은 감사를 받아 줘요!"], ["And when I say that I am going away within the hour, for I am soon going abroad, and that I shall never rest until I have worked for the money with which you have kept me out of prison, and have sent it to you, don’t think, dear Joe and Biddy, that if I could repay it a thousand times over, I suppose I could cancel a farthing of the debt I owe you, or that I would do so if I could!”", "그리고 내가 한 시간 안에 떠난다고, 머지않아 외국으로 나갈 거라고, 그리고 두 사람이 나를 감옥에서 벗어나게 해 준 그 돈을 벌어 갚아 두 사람에게 보낼 때까지 결코 마음 편히 쉬지 않을 거라고 말한다 해서, 부디 생각하지는 말아 줘요, 조, 비디, 내가 그 돈을 천 배로 갚는다 한들 두 사람에게 진 빚을 단 한 푼이라도 청산할 수 있으리라고, 혹은 그럴 수 있다 한들 내가 그렇게 청산해 버리려 한다고는!\""]], [["They were both melted by these words, and both entreated me to say no more.", "두 사람은 이 말에 마음이 녹아내려, 둘 다 내게 더는 말하지 말라고 간청했다."]], [["“But I must say more.", "\"하지만 더 말해야겠어요."], ["Dear Joe, I hope you will have children to love, and that some little fellow will sit in this chimney-corner of a winter night, who may remind you of another little fellow gone out of it for ever.", "조, 형이 사랑할 자식들을 두기를 바라요. 그리고 어느 겨울밤 이 굴뚝 모퉁이에 한 어린 녀석이 앉아, 영영 그곳을 떠나 버린 또 다른 어린 녀석을 형에게 떠올리게 해 주기를 바라요. 그 아이에게 말하지 말아요, 조, 내가 은혜를 모르는 놈이었다고는."], ["Don’t tell him, Joe, that I was thankless; don’t tell him, Biddy, that I was ungenerous and unjust; only tell him that I honoured you both, because you were both so good and true, and that, as your child, I said it would be natural to him to grow up a much better man than I did.”", "그 아이에게 말하지 말아줘, 비디, 내가 야박하고 부당한 놈이었다고는. 다만 그 아이에게, 내가 두 사람을 다 존경했다고만 말해 줘요, 두 사람이 다 그토록 선량하고 진실했기 때문에. 그리고 두 사람의 자식이니, 나보다 훨씬 더 나은 사람으로 자라나는 것이 그 아이에게는 당연한 일이리라고 내가 말했다고만.\""]], [["“I ain’t a-going,”", "\"난 말하지 않을 거다,\""], ["said Joe, from behind his sleeve, “to tell him nothink o’ that natur, Pip.", "조가 소매 뒤에서 말했다. \"그 아이한테 그런 종류의 말은 아무것도 안 할 거야, 핍."], ["Nor Biddy ain’t.", "비디도 안 할 거고."], ["Nor yet no one ain’t.”", "그 누구도 안 할 거다.\""]], [["“And now, though I know you have already done it in your own kind hearts, pray tell me, both, that you forgive me!", "\"그리고 이제, 두 사람의 그 너그러운 마음속으로는 이미 그렇게 해 주었다는 걸 알지만, 부디 둘 다 내게 말해 줘요, 나를 용서한다고!"], ["Pray let me hear you say the words, that I may carry the sound of them away with me, and then I shall be able to believe that you can trust me, and think better of me, in the time to come!”", "부디 그 말을 들려줘요, 그 소리를 내가 지니고 떠날 수 있도록. 그러면 나는 앞으로 두 사람이 나를 믿어 주고, 나를 더 좋게 여겨 주리라고 믿을 수 있을 거예요!\""]], [["“O dear old Pip, old chap,”", "\"오, 우리 핍, 친구,\""], ["said Joe.", "조가 말했다."], ["“God knows as I forgive you, if I have anythink to forgive!”", "\"내가 용서할 게 뭐라도 있다면, 하느님은 아실 거다, 내가 너를 용서한다는 걸!\""]], [["“Amen! And God knows I do!”", "\"아멘! 그리고 하느님은 아셔, 나도 그렇다는 걸!\""], ["echoed Biddy.", "비디가 그 말을 따라 했다."]], [["“Now let me go up and look at my old little room, and rest there a few minutes by myself.", "\"이제 위로 올라가 옛날 내 작은 방을 좀 보고, 거기서 혼자 몇 분만 쉬게 해 줘요."], ["And then, when I have eaten and drunk with you, go with me as far as the finger-post, dear Joe and Biddy, before we say good-bye!”", "그러고 나서 두 사람과 함께 먹고 마신 뒤에, 작별 인사를 하기 전에 이정표가 있는 데까지만 나와 함께 가 줘요, 조, 비디!\""]], [["I sold all I had, and put aside as much as I could, for a composition with my creditors,—who gave me ample time to pay them in full,—and I went out and joined Herbert.", "나는 가진 것을 모두 팔고, 채권자들과의 화해를 위해 — 그들은 내게 전액을 갚도록 넉넉한 시간을 주었다 — 가능한 한 많은 돈을 따로 떼어 두고서, 길을 나서 허버트와 합류했다."], ["Within a month, I had quitted England, and within two months I was clerk to Clarriker and Co., and within four months I assumed my first undivided responsibility.", "한 달 안에 나는 영국을 떠났고, 두 달 안에 클라리커 상회의 서기가 되었으며, 넉 달 안에 처음으로 온전히 내 몫의 책임을 맡게 되었다."], ["For the beam across the parlour ceiling at Mill Pond Bank had then ceased to tremble under old Bill Barley’s growls and was at peace, and Herbert had gone away to marry Clara, and I was left in sole charge of the Eastern Branch until he brought her back.", "그 무렵에는 밀 폰드 뱅크의 응접실 천장을 가로지른 들보가 늙은 빌 발리의 으르렁거림 아래 떨기를 그치고 평온에 들었고, 허버트는 클라라와 결혼하러 떠났으며, 나는 그가 그녀를 데리고 돌아올 때까지 동부 지점을 혼자서 도맡게 되었다."]], [["Many a year went round before I was a partner in the House; but I lived happily with Herbert and his wife, and lived frugally, and paid my debts, and maintained a constant correspondence with Biddy and Joe.", "내가 그 상회의 동업자가 되기까지는 여러 해가 흘렀다. 그러나 나는 허버트와 그의 아내와 더불어 행복하게 살았고, 검소하게 살았고, 빚을 갚았으며, 비디와 조와 끊임없이 서신을 주고받았다."], ["It was not until I became third in the Firm, that Clarriker betrayed me to Herbert; but he then declared that the secret of Herbert’s partnership had been long enough upon his conscience, and he must tell it.", "내가 상회의 세 번째 인물이 되고 나서야 비로소 클라리커가 허버트에게 내 비밀을 털어놓았는데, 그때 그는 허버트의 동업 자격에 얽힌 그 비밀이 오랫동안 자기 양심을 짓눌러 왔으니 이제는 말하지 않을 수 없다고 했다."], ["So he told it, and Herbert was as much moved as amazed, and the dear fellow and I were not the worse friends for the long concealment.", "그래서 그는 그것을 털어놓았고, 허버트는 놀란 만큼이나 깊이 감동했으며, 그 다정한 친구와 나는 오랜 비밀로 인해 사이가 나빠지기는커녕 조금도 변함이 없었다."], ["I must not leave it to be supposed that we were ever a great House, or that we made mints of money.", "우리가 대단한 상회였다거나, 우리가 어마어마한 돈을 벌었다고 짐작하게 두어서는 안 되겠다."], ["We were not in a grand way of business, but we had a good name, and worked for our profits, and did very well.", "우리는 대규모로 사업을 하지는 않았으나, 평판이 좋았고, 이익을 위해 부지런히 일했으며, 아주 잘해 나갔다."], ["We owed so much to Herbert’s ever cheerful industry and readiness, that I often wondered how I had conceived that old idea of his inaptitude, until I was one day enlightened by the reflection, that perhaps the inaptitude had never been in him at all, but had been in me.", "우리는 허버트의 한결같이 쾌활한 근면과 민첩함에 많은 것을 빚지고 있었으니, 나는 어쩌다 그가 무능하다는 그 옛 생각을 품게 되었던가 자주 의아해하다가, 어느 날 문득 깨달음을 얻었다. 어쩌면 그 무능함은 애당초 그에게 있었던 것이 아니라, 내게 있었던 것인지도 모른다는 깨달음을."]], [["# Chapter LIX.", "# Chapter LIX."]], [["For eleven years, I had not seen Joe nor Biddy with my bodily eyes,—though they had both been often before my fancy in the East,—when, upon an evening in December, an hour or two after dark, I laid my hand softly on the latch of the old kitchen door.", "십일 년 동안 나는 조도 비디도 이 두 눈으로 보지 못했다. 비록 동방에 있는 동안 두 사람의 모습이 종종 내 머릿속에 떠오르긴 했지만 말이다. 그러던 어느 십이월 저녁, 날이 저물고 한두 시간이 지났을 무렵, 나는 그 낡은 부엌문 빗장 위에 살그머니 손을 얹었다."], ["I touched it so softly that I was not heard, and looked in unseen.", "어찌나 살며시 닿았던지 아무도 내 기척을 듣지 못했고, 나는 들키지 않은 채 안을 들여다보았다."], ["There, smoking his pipe in the old place by the kitchen firelight, as hale and as strong as ever, though a little grey, sat Joe; and there, fenced into the corner with Joe’s leg, and sitting on my own little stool looking at the fire, was—I again!", "거기, 부엌 난롯불 가의 그 옛 자리에서 여전히 정정하고 건장한 모습으로 — 머리가 약간 희끗해지긴 했어도 — 파이프 담배를 피우며 앉아 있는 이는 조였다. 그리고 거기, 조의 다리에 둘러싸여 구석에 갇히듯이, 내 작은 의자에 앉아 불을 바라보고 있는 이는 — 또 한 명의 나였다!"]], [["“We giv’ him the name of Pip for your sake, dear old chap,”", "\"우리는 이 녀석한테 자네를 생각해서 핍이라는 이름을 붙여 줬다네, 정다운 친구.\""], ["said Joe, delighted, when I took another stool by the child’s side (but I did not rumple his hair), “and we hoped he might grow a little bit like you, and we think he do.”", "내가 아이 곁에 또 다른 의자를 가져다 앉았을 때(그러나 나는 아이의 머리를 헝클어뜨리지는 않았다) 조가 기뻐하며 말했다. \"그리고 우린 이 녀석이 자네를 조금이라도 닮아 가길 바랐는데, 우리가 보기엔 정말 그런 것 같아.\""]], [["I thought so too, and I took him out for a walk next morning, and we talked immensely, understanding one another to perfection.", "나도 그렇게 생각했고, 이튿날 아침 아이를 데리고 산책을 나갔다. 우리는 엄청나게 많은 이야기를 나누었고, 서로를 더없이 완벽하게 이해했다."], ["And I took him down to the churchyard, and set him on a certain tombstone there, and he showed me from that elevation which stone was sacred to the memory of Philip Pirrip, late of this Parish, and Also Georgiana, Wife of the Above.", "나는 아이를 교회 묘지로 데려가, 거기 어느 묘비 위에 앉혀 주었다. 아이는 그 높다란 자리에서 어느 돌이 이 교구의 고인이 된 필립 피립과, 또한 위 사람의 아내 조지애나를 기리는 것인지 내게 가리켜 보였다."]], [["“Biddy,”", "\"비디.\""], ["said I, when I talked with her after dinner, as her little girl lay sleeping in her lap, “you must give Pip to me one of these days; or lend him, at all events.”", "저녁 식사 후, 비디의 어린 딸아이가 그녀의 무릎에서 잠들어 있을 때 나는 그녀와 이야기를 나누며 말했다. \"언젠가 핍을 나한테 줘야 해. 아니, 적어도 빌려주기라도 해야 한다고.\""]], [["“No, no,” said Biddy, gently.", "\"안 돼요, 안 돼요.\" 비디가 부드럽게 말했다."], ["“You must marry.”", "\"당신은 결혼을 하셔야지요.\""]], [["“So Herbert and Clara say, but I don’t think I shall, Biddy.", "\"허버트와 클라라도 그렇게 말하지만, 난 그러지 않을 것 같아, 비디."], ["I have so settled down in their home, that it’s not at all likely.", "난 그 사람들 집에 아주 자리를 잡고 정착해서, 그럴 가능성은 전혀 없어."], ["I am already quite an old bachelor.”", "난 이미 어엿한 노총각이라고.\""]], [["Biddy looked down at her child, and put its little hand to her lips, and then put the good matronly hand with which she had touched it into mine.", "비디는 제 아이를 내려다보며 그 작은 손을 입술에 가져다 댔고, 그러고는 그 아이를 어루만지던 후덕한 손을 내 손 안에 쥐여 주었다."], ["There was something in the action, and in the light pressure of Biddy’s wedding-ring, that had a very pretty eloquence in it.", "그 동작에는, 그리고 비디의 결혼반지가 살짝 눌러 오는 감촉에는, 무언가 더없이 아름다운 웅변이 담겨 있었다."]], [["“Dear Pip,”", "\"가엾은 핍.\""], ["said Biddy, “you are sure you don’t fret for her?”", "비디가 말했다. \"당신, 그 사람 때문에 마음 아파하지 않는 거 정말 맞아요?\""]], [["“O no,—I think not, Biddy.”", "\"오, 아니야 — 그렇지 않은 것 같아, 비디.\""]], [["“Tell me as an old, old friend.", "\"아주아주 오래된 벗으로서 말해 줘요."], ["Have you quite forgotten her?", "그 사람을 완전히 잊었어요?\""]], [["“My dear Biddy, I have forgotten nothing in my life that ever had a foremost place there, and little that ever had any place there.", "\"비디, 내 평생 한때 가장 앞자리를 차지했던 것이라면 그 무엇도 잊은 적이 없고, 어디든 한자리를 차지했던 것조차 거의 잊지 않았어."], ["But that poor dream, as I once used to call it, has all gone by, Biddy,—all gone by!”", "하지만 내가 한때 즐겨 부르던 그 가엾은 꿈은, 모두 지나가 버렸어, 비디 — 다 지나가 버렸다고!\""]], [["Nevertheless, I knew, while I said those words, that I secretly intended to revisit the site of the old house that evening, alone, for her sake.", "그렇지만 그 말을 하면서도 나는 알고 있었다. 그날 저녁 그 옛집 자리를 혼자서, 그녀를 생각하며 다시 찾아가 보려고 남몰래 마음먹고 있었다는 것을."], ["Yes, even so.", "그렇다, 정말로 그랬다."], ["For Estella’s sake.", "에스텔라를 위해서였다."]], [["I had heard of her as leading a most unhappy life, and as being separated from her husband, who had used her with great cruelty, and who had become quite renowned as a compound of pride, avarice, brutality, and meanness.", "나는 그녀가 더없이 불행한 삶을 살고 있다는 소식을, 그리고 자기를 몹시 잔인하게 다루던 남편과 헤어졌다는 소식을 들은 바 있었다. 그 남편이라는 자는 오만과 탐욕과 야만과 비열함을 한데 뭉쳐 놓은 인간으로 제법 악명을 떨치게 되었다."], ["And I had heard of the death of her husband, from an accident consequent on his ill-treatment of a horse.", "그리고 나는 그 남편이 말을 학대하다가 일어난 사고로 죽었다는 소식도 들었다."], ["This release had befallen her some two years before; for anything I knew, she was married again.", "그녀가 이렇게 풀려난 것은 이 년쯤 전의 일이었으니, 내가 아는 바로는 그녀가 다시 결혼했을지도 모를 일이었다."]], [["The early dinner hour at Joe’s, left me abundance of time, without hurrying my talk with Biddy, to walk over to the old spot before dark.", "조의 집에서는 저녁 식사 시간이 일렀던 덕분에, 나는 비디와의 이야기를 서두르지 않고도 어두워지기 전에 그 옛 자리로 걸어갈 시간이 넉넉했다."], ["But, what with loitering on the way to look at old objects and to think of old times, the day had quite declined when I came to the place.", "그러나 가는 길에 낡은 것들을 바라보고 옛 시절을 떠올리느라 어슬렁거린 탓에, 그곳에 이르렀을 때는 날이 이미 저물어 있었다."]], [["There was no house now, no brewery, no building whatever left, but the wall of the old garden.", "이제 집도, 양조장도, 그 어떤 건물도 남아 있지 않았고, 오직 옛 정원의 담장만이 남아 있었다."], ["The cleared space had been enclosed with a rough fence, and looking over it, I saw that some of the old ivy had struck root anew, and was growing green on low quiet mounds of ruin.", "말끔히 치워진 공터는 거친 울타리로 둘러쳐져 있었는데, 그 너머를 들여다보니 옛 담쟁이덩굴 일부가 다시 뿌리를 내려, 폐허의 나지막하고 고요한 흙더미 위에서 푸르게 자라고 있었다."], ["A gate in the fence standing ajar, I pushed it open, and went in.", "울타리의 문이 빠끔히 열려 있어, 나는 그것을 밀어 열고 안으로 들어갔다."]], [["A cold silvery mist had veiled the afternoon, and the moon was not yet up to scatter it.", "차갑고 은빛 어린 안개가 오후를 베일처럼 덮고 있었으나, 그것을 흩어 줄 달은 아직 떠오르지 않았다."], ["But, the stars were shining beyond the mist, and the moon was coming, and the evening was not dark.", "그러나 안개 너머로 별들이 빛나고 있었고, 달이 떠오르고 있었으며, 저녁은 어둡지 않았다."], ["I could trace out where every part of the old house had been, and where the brewery had been, and where the gates, and where the casks.", "나는 옛집의 어느 부분이 어디에 있었는지, 양조장이 어디에 있었는지, 대문이 어디였는지, 술통이 어디에 있었는지 하나하나 더듬어 짚어 갈 수 있었다."], ["I had done so, and was looking along the desolate garden walk, when I beheld a solitary figure in it.", "그렇게 다 짚어 보고 나서 황량한 정원 산책로를 따라 시선을 옮기고 있을 때, 나는 거기 외로이 서 있는 한 사람의 모습을 보았다."]], [["The figure showed itself aware of me, as I advanced.", "내가 다가가자 그 모습은 나를 알아챈 기색을 보였다."], ["It had been moving towards me, but it stood still.", "그것은 나를 향해 다가오고 있었으나, 이내 멈춰 섰다."], ["As I drew nearer, I saw it to be the figure of a woman.", "더 가까이 다가가 보니 그것은 한 여인의 모습이었다."], ["As I drew nearer yet, it was about to turn away, when it stopped, and let me come up with it.", "더욱 가까이 다가가자 그 여인은 막 돌아서려다가 멈춰 서서, 내가 다가오도록 그대로 두었다."], ["Then, it faltered, as if much surprised, and uttered my name, and I cried out,—", "그러더니 몹시 놀란 듯 머뭇거리며 내 이름을 불렀고, 나는 외쳤다 —"]], [["“Estella!”", "\"에스텔라!\""]], [["“I am greatly changed. I wonder you know me.”", "\"저는 많이 변했어요. 당신이 저를 알아보다니 놀랍군요.\""]], [["The freshness of her beauty was indeed gone, but its indescribable majesty and its indescribable charm remained.", "그녀의 아름다움이 지녔던 싱그러움은 정말로 사라지고 없었으나, 그 형언할 수 없는 위엄과 형언할 수 없는 매력만은 그대로 남아 있었다."], ["Those attractions in it, I had seen before; what I had never seen before, was the saddened, softened light of the once proud eyes; what I had never felt before was the friendly touch of the once insensible hand.", "그 안에 깃든 그러한 매혹은 나도 전에 본 적이 있었다. 그러나 내가 전에 한 번도 본 적 없는 것은, 한때 그토록 오만하던 두 눈에 어린 슬프고 부드러운 빛이었다. 그리고 내가 전에 한 번도 느껴 본 적 없는 것은, 한때 그토록 무감하던 손길의 다정한 감촉이었다."]], [["We sat down on a bench that was near, and I said, “After so many years, it is strange that we should thus meet again, Estella, here where our first meeting was!", "우리는 가까이 있는 벤치에 앉았고, 나는 말했다. \"이렇게 오랜 세월이 흐른 뒤에, 우리가 처음 만났던 바로 이곳에서 이렇게 다시 만나다니 이상한 일이군요, 에스텔라!"], ["Do you often come back?”", "자주 이곳에 돌아오나요?\""]], [["“I have never been here since.” “Nor I.”", "\"그 후로 한 번도 여기 온 적이 없어요.\""]], [["The moon began to rise, and I thought of the placid look at the white ceiling, which had passed away.", "\"나도 그래요.\" 달이 떠오르기 시작했고, 나는 사라져 버린, 흰 천장을 향하던 그 평온한 눈길을 떠올렸다."], ["The moon began to rise, and I thought of the pressure on my hand when I had spoken the last words he had heard on earth.", "달이 떠오르기 시작했고, 나는 그가 이 세상에서 들은 마지막 말을 내가 건넸을 때 내 손을 눌러 오던 그 감촉을 떠올렸다."]], [["Estella was the next to break the silence that ensued between us.", "우리 사이에 뒤이은 침묵을 먼저 깨뜨린 것은 에스텔라였다."]], [["“I have very often hoped and intended to come back, but have been prevented by many circumstances.", "\"저는 아주 여러 번 이곳에 돌아오기를 바라고 또 그러려고 마음먹었지만, 여러 사정에 가로막혔어요."], ["Poor, poor old place!”", "가엾고 가엾은 옛 자리!\""]], [["The silvery mist was touched with the first rays of the moonlight, and the same rays touched the tears that dropped from her eyes.", "은빛 안개가 달빛의 첫 줄기에 어렴풋이 물들었고, 그 같은 빛줄기가 그녀의 두 눈에서 떨어지는 눈물을 어루만졌다."], ["Not knowing that I saw them, and setting herself to get the better of them, she said quietly,—", "내가 그것을 보았다는 사실을 모른 채, 마음을 다잡아 눈물을 추스르려 애쓰면서 그녀는 조용히 말했다 —"]], [["“Were you wondering, as you walked along, how it came to be left in this condition?”", "\"걸어오면서, 이곳이 어쩌다 이런 모습으로 버려지게 되었을까 궁금해하셨나요?\""]], [["“Yes, Estella.”", "\"그래요, 에스텔라.\""]], [["“The ground belongs to me.", "\"이 땅은 제 것이에요."], ["It is the only possession I have not relinquished.", "제가 포기하지 않은 유일한 재산이지요."], ["Everything else has gone from me, little by little, but I have kept this.", "다른 모든 것은 조금씩 제게서 떠나갔지만, 저는 이것만은 지켰어요."], ["It was the subject of the only determined resistance I made in all the wretched years.”", "그 비참한 세월 내내 제가 단호하게 저항했던 단 하나의 대상이 바로 이것이었어요.\""]], [["“Is it to be built on?”", "\"여기에 무언가를 지을 건가요?\""]], [["“At last, it is.", "\"마침내, 그렇게 됐어요."], ["I came here to take leave of it before its change. And you,”", "그것이 바뀌기 전에 작별을 고하러 여기 온 거예요. 그런데 당신은.\""], ["she said, in a voice of touching interest to a wanderer,—“you live abroad still?”", "그녀가 한 방랑자에게 건네는 듯한, 가슴에 와닿는 관심 어린 목소리로 말했다. \"아직도 외국에 사세요?\" \"여전히 그래요.\""]], [["“Still.” “And do well, I am sure?”", "\"그리고 잘 지내시겠지요, 분명히?\""]], [["“I work pretty hard for a sufficient living, and therefore—yes, I do well.”", "\"충분히 먹고살 만큼은 꽤 열심히 일하니까, 그래서 — 네, 잘 지내요.\""]], [["“I have often thought of you,”", "\"저는 당신을 자주 생각했어요.\""], ["said Estella.", "에스텔라가 말했다."]], [["“Have you?”", "\"그랬나요?\""]], [["“Of late, very often.", "\"요즘 들어, 아주 자주요."], ["There was a long hard time when I kept far from me the remembrance of what I had thrown away when I was quite ignorant of its worth.", "그 가치를 전혀 모른 채 제가 내던져 버린 것에 대한 기억을, 오랫동안 모질게 제게서 멀리 떼어 놓았던 시절이 있었어요."], ["But since my duty has not been incompatible with the admission of that remembrance, I have given it a place in my heart.”", "하지만 그 기억을 받아들이는 것이 제 본분과 어긋나지 않게 된 뒤로는, 저는 그것에 제 마음속 한자리를 내주었어요.\""]], [["“You have always held your place in my heart,”", "\"당신은 언제나 내 마음속 한자리를 차지하고 있었어요.\""], ["I answered.", "나는 대답했다."]], [["And we were silent again until she spoke.", "그리고 우리는 그녀가 다시 입을 열 때까지 또다시 침묵했다."]], [["“I little thought,”", "\"이 자리에 작별을 고하면서,\""], ["said Estella, “that I should take leave of you in taking leave of this spot.", "에스텔라가 말했다. \"당신에게도 작별을 고하게 되리라고는 거의 생각지 못했어요."], ["I am very glad to do so.”", "그렇게 되어 정말 기뻐요.\""]], [["“Glad to part again, Estella? To me, parting is a painful thing.", "\"다시 헤어지게 되어 기쁘다는 건가요, 에스텔라? 내게 이별은 고통스러운 일이에요."], ["To me, the remembrance of our last parting has been ever mournful and painful.”", "내게는 우리의 지난 이별에 대한 기억이 늘 슬프고 고통스러운 것이었어요.\""]], [["“But you said to me,”", "\"하지만 당신은 제게 말했잖아요.\""], ["returned Estella, very earnestly, “‘God bless you, God forgive you!’ And if you could say that to me then, you will not hesitate to say that to me now,—now, when suffering has been stronger than all other teaching, and has taught me to understand what your heart used to be.", "에스텔라가 아주 진지하게 대답했다. \"'신의 가호가 있기를, 신께서 당신을 용서하시기를!' 하고요. 그때 제게 그렇게 말할 수 있었다면, 지금도 주저 없이 제게 그렇게 말해 주시겠지요 — 지금, 고통이 그 어떤 가르침보다도 더 강력했고, 그 고통이 당신의 마음이 어떠했는지를 제게 깨닫게 해 준 지금에 와서는요."], ["I have been bent and broken, but—I hope—into a better shape.", "저는 구부러지고 부서졌어요, 하지만 — 부디 바라건대 — 더 나은 모습으로 그리되었기를요."], ["Be as considerate and good to me as you were, and tell me we are friends.”", "예전처럼 제게 사려 깊고 다정하게 대해 주세요. 그리고 우리가 친구라고 말해 주세요.\""]], [["“We are friends,”", "\"우리는 친구예요.\""], ["said I, rising and bending over her, as she rose from the bench.", "그녀가 벤치에서 일어서자 나도 일어나 그녀에게로 몸을 굽히며 말했다."]], [["“And will continue friends apart,”", "\"그리고 떨어져 있어도 계속 친구일 거예요.\""], ["said Estella.", "에스텔라가 말했다."]], [["I took her hand in mine, and we went out of the ruined place; and, as the morning mists had risen long ago when I first left the forge, so the evening mists were rising now, and in all the broad expanse of tranquil light they showed to me, I saw no shadow of another parting from her.", "나는 그녀의 손을 내 손에 잡았고, 우리는 그 폐허가 된 곳을 빠져나왔다. 그리고 내가 처음 대장간을 떠날 때 아침 안개가 이미 오래전에 걷혀 올랐던 것처럼, 이제 저녁 안개가 피어오르고 있었으며, 그 안개가 내게 보여 주는 드넓게 펼쳐진 고요한 빛 속에서, 나는 그녀와 또다시 헤어지게 되리라는 그 어떤 그림자도 보지 못했다."]]];
